UID-0001♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AI Medical devices are not ready for accelerated adoption and deployment without⏎appropriate research or enforceable regulation. Even though LLMs are currently being used by⏎millions of people, this adoption by the public is far ahead of the essential science⏎demonstrating privacy, safety, and efficacy. Hence, regulation is essential to reduce the risk of⏎harm to consumers, ensure that the public has access to safe and effective clinical care, hold⏎corporations accountable for the products they develop and sell, and support uniform national⏎standards for AI Chatbots.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0002♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔White♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-eight years of nursing and I've watched health IT go through this same cycle more times than I can count. Someone shows up with a new system that's going to fix everything, everyone gets excited, it gets rolled out before anyone really understands how it performs on actual patients, and then the quiet near-misses start accumulating until something bad enough happens that people pay attention.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to is Ruthie. She's a patient of mine, 79 years old, Nez Perce, I've been seeing her at our clinic in Lewiston for six years now. In March of 2024 she was nearly started on a cardiac medication that the algorithm had flagged as appropriate. The dosing recommendation looked right on the screen. It wasn't right for her. Her daughter was there and she's sharp, she asked questions, and we caught it. But the reason we caught it is not because the system worked. It's because a family member happened to be present and happened to know enough to push back. That's not a safety net. That's luck.⏎⏎What I want HHS to understand is that the gap between how these tools perform in controlled testing and how they perform in a clinic like ours is not a small technical detail to be worked out later. It is the whole problem. Ruthie's situation — her age, her background, the particular way her health history is documented across different systems, the medications she'd been on for years — none of that fit neatly into whatever population the dosing algorithm was built around. The tool didn't know what it didn't know. And the screen didn't say "this recommendation is based on data that may not reflect patients like yours." It just gave a number.⏎⏎The other thing I want to say, and I'm not sure I've seen this addressed directly anywhere in what I've read about this rulemaking, is what happens to clinical judgment when a computer is making recommendations all day long. I've noticed it in myself and I've noticed it in younger nurses. When the alert system is mostly right, you start moving faster. You start trusting it. That's natural and honestly it's part of why these tools get adopted, because they do reduce some cognitive load in a busy shift. But it means that the times the AI is wrong are exactly the times nobody's paying the closest attention. The error that gets caught is the one that seems obviously wrong. The one that looks plausible — like Ruthie's — that's the one that slips through.⏎⏎I understand there are real benefits and I'm not saying don't move forward with any of this. Diagnostic tools that help catch things earlier, that flag drug interactions, that help with documentation — I've seen those help. But the pace of adoption HHS seems to be pushing toward, based on what I've read, worries me. Real-world performance validation on diverse patient populations should come before broad clinical deployment, not after. And I mean genuinely diverse — not just race and age checkboxes, but geography, language, documentation patterns, access to care histories. A patient in Lewiston, Idaho is not the same as a patient in the dataset.⏎⏎I don't know what the right regulatory mechanism is for that. I'm a nurse, not a policy person. But someone at HHS needs to be asking, for every one of these tools: how did it perform on patients who look like Ruthie, and who is responsible when it doesn't?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0003♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Mosaic Life Tech, LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mosaic Life Tech, LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Docket: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted by: Mosaic Life Tech⏎Date: [For submission by February 23, 2026]⏎Status: DRAFT v4.3 FOR FOUNDER REVIEW⏎Submitter Information⏎Mosaic Life Tech is a healthcare AI governance advisory firm. Our principals have over⏎50 years of combined experience in health information technology, including leadership⏎roles in federal health IT programs (ONC, CDC, ASTP), health information exchange⏎operations serving over four million patients, and clinical informatics. We advise⏎healthcare executives on establishing governance structures for AI-influenced clinical⏎decisions.⏎We submit these comments based on direct participation in federal health IT policy⏎development, industry governance forums, and review of recent survey data on AI⏎adoption and oversight in healthcare settings.⏎Response to Question 3⏎Question: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS⏎play to help address them?⏎The most significant governance challenge is not legal ambiguity at the federal level. It⏎is organizational ambiguity within health systems themselves.⏎Most health systems — and over 70% of acute care hospitals — now have AI tools⏎influencing clinical decisions, whether through EHR-embedded algorithms, operational⏎scheduling tools, or documentation assistants, though adoption drops sharply among⏎independent, rural, and critical access hospitals. Many of these tools fall outside FDA⏎device regulation. The question of "what federal rules apply" often has a clear answer.⏎The question of "who inside our organization is accountable for this AI-influenced⏎decision" frequently does not.⏎Recent survey data confirms a pervasive governance gap. The CHIME/Censinet AI⏎Adoption Survey (December 2025) found that only 59% of healthcare organizations⏎require formal approval before AI implementation, meaning over 40% lack even a basic⏎⏎pre-implementation gate. Just 10% use automated monitoring to detect AI capabilities⏎within their existing software, while 51% rely on informal, ad hoc discovery. ECRI has⏎ranked "Insufficient Governance of AI in Healthcare" as the #1 health technology hazard⏎for 2025, warning that failure to develop systemwide governance increases both patient⏎safety and liability risk.⏎This pattern extends to federal healthcare settings. In January 2026, the VA Office of⏎Inspector General reported that VHA had deployed generative AI tools without⏎coordination with the National Center for Patient Safety, despite VHA Directive 1050.01⏎requiring such coordination to promote and safeguard patient safety. The OIG found no⏎formal process to track AI-related safety issues. This illustrates how even large,⏎sophisticated healthcare organizations can deploy AI without the governance structures⏎needed to manage risk.⏎The governance gap is widening. HTI-5's proposed removal of AI model card⏎transparency requirements at § 170.315(b)(11) and significant reduction in DSI⏎certification criteria effectively shifts the responsibility for evaluating AI safety, bias, and⏎fitness-for-purpose from federal certification requirements to deploying healthcare⏎organizations and the market. This shift is navigable for well-resourced health systems⏎with dedicated informatics leadership. For small and mid-market organizations,⏎community hospitals, and safety-net providers, the shift creates a governance burden⏎they lack capacity to meet.⏎The data confirms a "digital divide" in AI governance capacity. ASTP/ONC and AHA⏎report that 86% of system-affiliated hospitals use predictive AI versus just 37% of⏎independent facilities. HFMA survey data shows that among the 18% of health systems⏎with mature AI governance, three-quarters have over $1 billion in net patient revenue.⏎Dr. Brian Anderson, CEO of the Coalition for Health AI, has noted that the digital divide⏎is already being reinforced in the AI space, with rural communities lacking the IT staff to⏎deploy and configure AI tools.⏎Vendor contracts address liability and indemnification from the vendor's perspective. But⏎those contracts cannot resolve internal accountability when the organization hasn't⏎decided who is responsible for validating AI tools against their patient population,⏎monitoring for drift, or escalating concerns. A 2025 BMC Health Services Research⏎study of AI implementation experts identified a "responsibility vacuum" where⏎organizational structures obfuscate who should have accountability for AI monitoring.⏎Only 22% of hospital leaders report high confidence they could produce a complete AI⏎audit trail within 30 days if requested by a regulator.⏎HHS can help address this in several ways without adding regulatory burden:⏎First, HHS can support and amplify voluntary governance frameworks already emerging⏎in the field. The Joint Commission and the Coalition for Health AI published the⏎Guidance on the Responsible Use of AI in Healthcare on September 17, 2025. This⏎guidance provides a reasonable structure for organizational accountability across seven⏎elements: governance structures, patient privacy, data security, quality monitoring,⏎voluntary safety event reporting, risk and bias assessment, and education and training.⏎⏎Because the Joint Commission holds CMS deeming authority under 42 CFR Part 488,⏎health systems are treating this guidance as a roadmap for aligning with likely future⏎accreditation expectations. HHS recognition of such frameworks, without mandating⏎them, would signal that establishing internal governance is expected and supported.⏎Second, HHS can consider technical assistance models that help under-resourced⏎organizations build governance capacity. The Regional Extension Center program⏎provides a clear precedent: it assisted approximately 134,000 primary care providers,⏎and ONC evaluations found that 68% of REC participants achieved Stage 1 Meaningful⏎Use versus just 12% of non-participants. A similar model for AI governance, potentially⏎leveraging existing infrastructure at HRSA (through FQHCs), SAMHSA, or other⏎agencies, could help smaller organizations develop oversight capabilities they currently⏎lack.⏎Third, HHS can fund or disseminate research on effective governance structures.⏎Health systems are experimenting with AI governance committees, chief AI officer roles,⏎and cross-functional oversight bodies. KLAS Research has noted that "stronger⏎organizational governance and ownership are needed to drive broader AI adoption" and⏎that when governance provides a structured way to vet and integrate AI, adoption is⏎more scalable and sustainable. Implementation science focused on AI governance,⏎potentially through NIH or AHRQ, would help the field learn faster than each system⏎discovering through trial and error.⏎The novel issue is not that AI creates new legal exposure. It is that AI creates decisions⏎that cross traditional departmental boundaries, and most organizations have not⏎adapted their governance structures to match. Federal clarity that organizational⏎governance is expected, combined with support for building that capacity, would do⏎more to accelerate responsible adoption than additional regulation.⏎Response to Question 5⏎Question: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎The private sector has already begun developing governance frameworks for AI in⏎clinical care. HHS can accelerate adoption by supporting these efforts rather than⏎duplicating them through regulation.⏎The Joint Commission, in collaboration with the Coalition for Health AI, published the⏎Guidance on the Responsible Use of AI in Healthcare on September 17, 2025. This⏎guidance addresses governance structure, validation, monitoring, training, and board⏎visibility through seven core elements. Because the Joint Commission holds CMS⏎deeming authority under 42 CFR Part 488 and accredits more than 80% of U.S.⏎hospitals, health systems are paying attention to this guidance even though it does not⏎create new accreditation standards. Legal analysts note that this guidance follows the⏎Joint Commission's established pattern: the National Patient Safety Goals began as⏎⏎recommendations before becoming accreditation requirements, and health equity⏎guidance from 2023 became mandatory requirements effective January 2024.⏎Professional societies are also building AI-specific infrastructure. The American College⏎of Radiology has launched Assess-AI, described as the world's first AI quality registry⏎designed to monitor ongoing performance of imaging AI algorithms in real-world clinical⏎settings. ACR's Recognized Center for Healthcare-AI (ARCH-AI) program provides a⏎recognition framework for facilities meeting governance, model selection, acceptance⏎testing, and monitoring criteria. ACR is working toward a formal AI accreditation⏎program, with ACR Council approval anticipated for Spring 2027. The AMA's STEPS⏎Forward toolkit provides practical governance guidance for health system leaders⏎establishing AI oversight functions, including committee structure, decision rights, and⏎escalation pathways. These efforts represent the field building governance from within,⏎informed by clinical realities that federal regulation alone cannot capture.⏎HHS can support this work in several concrete ways:⏎HHS can publicly recognize voluntary frameworks as representing reasonable practice.⏎This does not require endorsement or mandate. A statement that organizations⏎following such guidance are taking appropriate steps toward responsible AI use would⏎reduce uncertainty and encourage adoption.⏎HHS can support accreditation bodies in developing AI governance competencies within⏎existing survey processes. Surveyors already assess governance, quality improvement,⏎and patient safety. Integrating AI oversight into these existing frameworks, rather than⏎creating separate AI-specific requirements, would reduce burden while improving⏎accountability.⏎HHS can fund professional organizations and associations in developing training and⏎credentialing for AI governance roles. Health systems are creating AI governance⏎committees and oversight functions, but the people filling these roles often lack formal⏎training in AI risk assessment or clinical validation methods. The AMA's STEPS Forward⏎toolkit on AI governance provides a starting point, but broader workforce development⏎would build capacity across the industry.⏎HHS can also support industry-driven testing and validation infrastructure. Health⏎systems struggle to validate vendor claims about AI performance on their specific⏎patient populations. Shared testing resources, benchmark datasets, or cooperative⏎validation arrangements could help smaller and mid-sized systems that lack internal⏎capacity for rigorous evaluation.⏎The risk of heavy federal regulation is that it freezes current practice into compliance⏎requirements before the field has learned what actually works. The opportunity in⏎supporting private sector frameworks is that governance can evolve with the technology⏎while still establishing reasonable expectations for oversight.⏎Response to Question 7⏎⏎Question: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎No single role consistently owns AI adoption decisions across health systems. This⏎fragmentation is itself a primary hurdle.⏎Industry leadership patterns reflect a structural divide between technical authority and⏎clinical oversight. CHIME and AMDIS surveys document this dynamic: CIOs control⏎technology infrastructure and serve on 63% of AI governance committees, but are⏎typically not clinicians. CMIOs and CNIOs have clinical credibility but serve on only 45%⏎of AI governance committees; AMDIS/WittKieffer surveys show that only 19% of CMIOs⏎have a dedicated leadership budget, with 40% operating under the IT department’s⏎budget. Quality and patient safety leaders focus on outcomes but are rarely consulted⏎during AI acquisition. Risk management addresses liability but often learns about AI⏎deployments after the fact. Boards ask questions about AI oversight but receive⏎inconsistent answers about who is accountable. The CHIME/Censinet survey found that⏎33% of organizations cite "unclear ownership" between IT, clinical, and compliance⏎teams as a primary barrier to governance.⏎The CFO is often the decisive voice. HFMA's 2025 survey of 233 health system leaders⏎found that CFOs and CIOs are the leading decision-makers when weighing AI vendor⏎offerings, with cost reduction cited as the most frequent rationale for investment. Only⏎39% of CFOs believed AI investments would actually reduce overall costs, yet financial⏎justification remains the primary gate. HHS's own RFI on accelerating AI in clinical care⏎identifies reimbursement and payment policy as key levers, recognizing that AI adoption⏎decisions frequently come down to financial justification. Yet CFOs typically lack the⏎clinical or technical context to evaluate AI risk, creating a disconnect between the⏎person who approves the investment and the people who understand what is being⏎approved.⏎The organizations adopting AI most effectively have established clear governance⏎ownership, typically through cross-functional AI governance committees with explicit⏎decision rights, or through designated AI oversight roles that report to executive⏎leadership. These structures do not slow adoption. They accelerate it, provided they⏎include explicit decision rights and clear escalation pathways. Black Book Research⏎found that health systems with AI governance councils are twice as likely to achieve⏎ROI within 12 months, and programs with dashboards and clear ownership reach early⏎ROI in 7.5 months versus 13.5 months without them. KLAS Research has observed that⏎when governance provides a structured way to vet and integrate AI, adoption is more⏎scalable and sustainable.⏎The primary administrative hurdle is not technology cost or complexity. It is the absence⏎of a defined process for answering basic governance questions: Who approves the use⏎of this AI tool? Who validates it against our patient population? Who monitors it after⏎deployment? Who decides when to turn it off?⏎⏎HHS could support adoption by funding research on effective governance structures⏎and disseminating findings. Health systems are solving these problems independently,⏎but there is limited sharing of what works across organizations. Implementation science⏎focused on AI governance would help the field learn faster.⏎Closing⏎We appreciate the Department’s focus on accelerating AI adoption while maintaining⏎appropriate oversight. Across the governance challenges identified in Question 3, the⏎private sector frameworks addressed in Question 5, and the organizational decision-⏎making dynamics explored in Question 7, the evidence points consistently in one⏎direction: governance is not a barrier to adoption but an enabler of it. Organizations with⏎clear AI governance ownership achieve ROI faster and scale more effectively than⏎those without. The most effective path forward supports organizational governance⏎capacity rather than prescribing specific requirements. Health systems are ready to act.⏎What they need from HHS is not more rules, but clear expectations and capacity⏎support that help them govern AI responsibly.⏎Submitted by:⏎Teresa Younkin, MSHI Jim Younkin, MBA⏎Chief Executive Officer, Mosaic Life Tech Chief Technology Officer, Mosaic Life Tech⏎⏎References⏎1. Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. Hospital⏎Trends in the Use, Evaluation, and Governance of Predictive AI, 2023-2024.⏎ASTP/ONC Data Brief. https://healthit.gov/data/data-briefs/hospital-trends-use-⏎evaluation-and-governance-predictive-ai-2023-2024/⏎2. CHIME/Censinet. AI Adoption Survey Reveals Healthcare's Governance Gap and⏎Drive Toward Agentic Usage. December 2025. https://censinet.com/blog/ai-⏎adoption-survey-reveals-healthcares-governance-gap-and-drive-toward-agentic-⏎usage⏎3. ECRI. Top 10 Health Technology Hazards for 2025. ECRI Institute. November 2024.⏎4. VA Office of Inspector General. Review of VHA's Use of Generative Artificial⏎Intelligence. Preliminary Result Advisory Memorandum. January 2026.⏎https://www.vaoig.gov/reports/preliminary-result-advisory-memorandum/review-⏎vhas-use-generative-artificial-intelligence⏎5. Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology. Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability:⏎ASTP/ONC Deregulatory Actions To Unleash Prosperity (HTI-5) Proposed Rule. 90⏎FR (Dec. 29, 2025). RIN 0955-AA09. 45 CFR § 170.315(b)(11).⏎https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/29/2025-23896/health-data-⏎technology-and-interoperability-astponc-deregulatory-actions-to-unleash-prosperity⏎6. Anderson B. Quoted in: Healthcare AI Divide Threatens to Leave Safety-Net⏎Providers Behind. Healthcare Dive. 2025.⏎https://www.healthcaredive.com/news/healthcare-ai-artificial-intelligence-digital-⏎divide-safety-net-providers/801809/⏎7. The Joint Commission and the Coalition for Health AI. Guidance on the Responsible⏎Use of AI in Healthcare. September 17, 2025. https://www.jointcommission.org⏎8. Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. Evaluation of⏎the Regional Extension Center Program: Final Report. 2016. https://healthit.gov/wp-⏎content/uploads/2021/06/Evaluation_of_the_Regional_Extension_Center_Program_⏎Final_Report_4_4_16.pdf⏎9. American College of Radiology. Assess-AI: AI Quality Registry. https://www.acr.org⏎10. American College of Radiology. ARCH-AI: Recognized Center for Healthcare-AI⏎Program. https://www.acr.org⏎11. American Medical Association. STEPS Forward: AI Governance Toolkit. AMA Ed⏎Hub. https://edhub.ama-assn.org/steps-forward/module/2833560⏎12. HFMA. AI Use Common Among Health Systems; Fleshed-Out Governance Not.⏎Fierce Healthcare. 2025. https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/ai-and-machine-⏎learning/ai-use-common-among-health-systems-fleshed-out-governance-not⏎13. Owens K, Griffen Z, Damaraju L. Responsibility Vacuum in AI Implementation. BMC⏎Health Serv Res. 2025;25:1217. https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12913-⏎025-13388-z⏎14. Black Book Research. 2026 Health System & Hospital AI Governance Resource⏎Guide. November 2025. https://blackbookmarketresearch.com/governing-hospitals-⏎ai-2026-board-to-bedside-accountability-guide⏎⏎15. KLAS Research. Healthcare AI Update 2025: What Use Cases Are Adopted the⏎Most? Report #3912. 2025. https://klasresearch.com/report/healthcare-ai-update-⏎2025-what-use-cases-are-adopted-the-most/3912⏎16. WittKieffer/AMDIS. Navigating Change & Growth: Insights into Physician Informatics⏎Leadership. 2025. https://wittkieffer.com/blog/the-evolving-healthcare-cmio-role♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0004♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees (AFSCME), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Federation of State, County and Municipal Employees (AFSCME)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Submitted via regulations.gov.⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI; RIN 0955-AA13⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The American Federation of State, County & Municipal Employees (AFSCME) is⏎pleased to respond to the request for information (RFI) from the U.S. Department of⏎Health and Human Services (HHS) on the “actions it can take to establish a forward-⏎leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and use of⏎[artificial intelligence (AI)] as a part of clinical care.”⏎AFSCME members provide the vital services that make America happen. With⏎1.4 million members in communities across the nation, serving in hundreds of different⏎occupations — from nurses to corrections officers, child care providers to sanitation⏎workers — AFSCME advocates for fairness in the workplace, excellence in public⏎services and freedom and opportunity for all working families. AFSCME represents⏎hundreds of thousands of health care workers across a variety of roles in settings ranging⏎from clinics to nursing homes and hospitals.⏎First and foremost, we are concerned with the framing of this RFI. The discussion⏎never once pauses to consider whether the weight of the evidence points to widespread⏎adoption of AI in more clinical applications as a net benefit to patients and health care⏎professionals. Instead, at this point in the lifecycle of generative AI development and⏎acceleration, there must be a clearer picture of who is overseeing its incorporation into⏎new health care use cases and what assurances patients and health care workers have that⏎these tools can meet their avowed competencies. The fragmented rollout of these tools⏎and unclear lines of regulatory oversight contribute to our overall unease about the⏎prospect of accelerating further adoption.⏎HHS must take a patient-forward and patient-supportive approach to the⏎introduction and use of AI in clinical care and the regulatory framework that governs it.⏎It is essential to protect patient choice, rights and privacy. This means, for example, not⏎⏎-2-⏎imposing on patients technology substitutes for human clinical care, like AI avatars and⏎ultrasound-administering robots.1 It also means maintaining clear lines of responsibility and⏎liability for harms to patients resulting from the use of AI in clinical care. A patient-forward⏎approach also means that investments by HHS of public resources in AI research and development⏎should result in access to affordable care and not repeat HHS’s deeply flawed approach to drug⏎research and development, which has allowed prescription drug corporations to charge monopoly⏎prices for drugs developed with publicly funded research.⏎There has already been significant AI adoption in health care, though the use cases have⏎remained somewhat limited and uses resulting in a high degree of success have been even more⏎limited.2 HHS should first pause and engage with panels of outside experts who can realistically⏎grapple with the enormous complexities of the challenges before us when it comes to something⏎as transformational as incorporating AI at mass scale across the scope of possible use cases in⏎health care. The burden of proof should be on AI product developers to demonstrate, beyond a⏎reasonable level of doubt, that their products will do no harm in clinical health care applications.⏎The overall culture and operating framework of these firms seems to be one of constant iteration,⏎with many issues only addressed as they emerge following real world engagement. AFSCME⏎members do not get to operate with such a carefree attitude. There needs to be a significant, open⏎dialogue about the propriety of such an approach in the health care setting and the risks being⏎introduced. Health care organizations, health care professionals and patients need to be confident⏎in the ability of AI tools to get it right without exacerbating any longstanding health inequities.⏎To date, the track record of certain AI health care applications does not live up to the hype.⏎At the very least, the performance raises significant concerns about unfettered acceleration of AI⏎in clinical health care settings. A recent study in the journal Nature found that, quite simply, AI⏎chatbots often get it wrong when patients pose diagnostic queries.3 Specifically, it found the agents⏎performed no better than a simple internet search in guiding patients in the correct direction or⏎correctly diagnosing a condition. Likewise, another recent investigation indicates AI is⏎contributing to abnormal rates of botched surgeries.4 While AI certainly has the potential to⏎facilitate increases in productivity, enhance diagnostic capabilities and contribute towards greater⏎personalization in medicine, the question is how to avoid these types of entirely predictable⏎missteps when the consequences are so potentially dire for patients. Additionally, as others have⏎repeatedly pointed out, these types of tools are only as good as the data they are trained on. Patients⏎today should not be forced to serve as unwitting guinea pigs so that tech corporations may profit.⏎Finally, we have significant concerns regarding the potential adverse labor market impact⏎of widespread AI adoption in clinical health care settings in the absence of appropriate guardrails.⏎Above all else, it is of paramount importance that all health care workers be able to override any⏎decisions recommended by an AI tool when the individual believes it is not the appropriate course⏎of action to meet the needs of the patient in front of them. As AI adoption continues to spread,⏎1 See, e.g., Windsor Johnston, “Dr. Oz Pushes Avatars as a Fix for Rural Health Care. Not So Fast, Critics Say,”⏎NPR, Feb. 14, 2026, available at https://www.npr.org/2026/02/14/nx-s1-5704189/dr-oz-ai-avatars-replace-rural-⏎health-workers.⏎2 https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12202002/.⏎3 https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-025-04074-y?ref=404media.co⏎4 https://www.reuters.com/investigations/ai-enters-operating-room-reports-arise-botched-surgeries-misidentified-⏎body-2026-02-09/⏎⏎-3-⏎below are some other key principles which the broader health care industry should be expected to⏎operate within:⏎• AI tools should not be solely relied upon to dictate the workflows of health care workers.⏎• AI tools should not be solely relied upon in determining patient acuity for purposes of⏎scheduling.⏎• AI tools should not replace clinician expertise by undermining their autonomy or being⏎solely relied upon in establishing patient care plans.⏎• Disregarding an AI decision support tool recommendation should not be the basis used by⏎employers in disciplining health care workers.⏎• Disregarding an AI decision support tool recommendation should not be the basis used by⏎licensing boards in disciplinary proceedings.⏎There is no doubt next generation AI tools have the potential to identify opportunities to⏎advance human health. However, we must not let hype and boosterism blind us from potential⏎pitfalls and the need for prudent, responsible actions today so that we may all get there together.⏎Sincerely,⏎/s/ Dalia R. Thornton⏎Dalia R. Thornton⏎Director⏎Department of Research and⏎Collective Bargaining Services♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0005♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1.	What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎⏎The medical societies and clinical societies collectively have not moved to standards based FHIR APIs at scale.⏎⏎The medical societies and clinical societies have clinical guidelines, care plans, treatment algorithms, appropriate use criteria, care pathways, living guidelines, and other recommendations that would be very useful for clinical care.⏎⏎Such collective medical/clinical societies' movement to FHIR APIs would be useful and beneficial for the AI to scale standards based care at scale.⏎⏎A fun application of this would be AI agents for cross specialty care in rural areas.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0006♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-27T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Tapestryhealth, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-27T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Tapestryhealth♔♔♔♔♔♔January 27, 2026⏎Jim O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Re: TapestryHealth’s Response to RIN 0955-AA13 – Request for Information: Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill:⏎TapestryHealth is pleased to provide the following information to the U.S. Department of Health⏎and Human Services (HHS) in response to its Request for Information regarding the adoption and⏎use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care (RIN 0955-AA13). See 90 Fed. Reg. 60108⏎(Dec. 23, 2024). TapestryHealth is a specialized medical practice serving the Post-Acute and Long-⏎Term Care (LTC) sector. Our responses below reflect the unique operational realities of Skilled⏎Nursing Facilities (SNFs) and Assisted Living Communities. While some principles may apply⏎broadly, our recommendations are specifically designed to address the high-acuity, low-staffing,⏎and multi-chronic nature of this vulnerable patient population, which often differs significantly⏎from ambulatory or acute hospital settings.⏎TapestryHealth currently monitors over 80,000 patients in elder care communities, capturing vital⏎cardiopulmonary data every second. We are writing to address a critical disconnect: While AI⏎technology has matured to the point where it can predict hospitalizations 5 days in advance, the⏎current payment models actively penalize providers for using it. We note that many of the⏎suggestions below specifically address reimbursement matters centered on the Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS).⏎I. The Core Barrier: The "Efficiency Penalty"⏎The primary barrier to AI adoption is not technological—it is economic. The current "Labor-Based"⏎pricing model (RVUs) is incompatible with AI-native care.⏎• • The Efficiency Penalty: Current CPT codes equate "value" with "time spent." If an AI tool⏎allows a clinician to review a patient’s status in 2 minutes instead of 20, or allows the same⏎20-minute increment of clinical time to be more comprehensive and effective, the provider is⏎financially penalized. This creates an incentive to maintain slower, manual workflows.⏎• • Another impact to efficiency and effectiveness is that providers cannot concurrently bill for⏎RPM (Remote Physiologic Monitoring) and RTM (Remote Therapeutic Monitoring). This forces⏎⏎TapestryHealth’s Response to RIN 0955-AA13⏎January 27, 2026⏎Page 2 of 4⏎providers to choose between the services, creating "data silos" where AI models are blinded⏎to half the patient's context.⏎II. Proposed Solution: A Tiered Reimbursement Model⏎We propose a Tiered Complexity Model for AI reimbursement, as detailed in our CY 2026 PFS⏎comments:⏎• Tier 1 (Administrative AI): Simple rule-based alerts. Reimbursement covers basic software⏎costs.⏎• Tier 2 (Assistive AI): Pattern recognition on single data streams. Reimbursement covers⏎software + moderate clinician and clinical staff review.⏎• Tier 3 (Predictive/Multimodal AI): High-complexity modeling using continuous, passive data.⏎Reimbursement covers infrastructure + high-level cognitive interpretation by clinicians and⏎clinical staff.⏎III. Safety & Oversight: The Indispensable "Human-in-the-Loop"⏎AI should not replace the clinician; it should elevate them.⏎• The "Signal-to-Noise" Problem: Pure software solutions often generate hundreds of false⏎alarms. In the SNF setting, "alert fatigue" is a major safety risk.⏎• Recommendation: HHS and CMS should require—and reimburse—a "Clinical Verification"⏎component. We propose a new code for "AI-Guided Clinical Interpretation" that pays for the⏎cognitive labor of verifying AI insights.⏎IV. Real-World Efficacy: Contactless Radar Monitoring⏎• Early Warning: Contactless monitoring provided an average predictive lead time of 4.99 to⏎5.65 days prior to a hospitalization event.⏎• Outcome Improvement: Facilities utilizing this "AI + Clinician" model saw a 43% reduction in⏎ED visits and a 45% reduction in inpatient admissions.⏎V. Use of AI Technology for Basic Analytics⏎AI significantly enhances the ability to generate meaningful and actionable insights from the vast,⏎disparate, and often unstructured data residing within long-term care electronic medical record⏎systems. While remote vitals monitoring is one application, broader use of AI analytics remains⏎underutilized due to current financial models that do not incentivize these technological⏎advancements.⏎By applying AI to a patient’s prescribed drug history in combination with other medical⏎information, clinicians can develop actionable plans aimed at improving overall health outcomes⏎⏎TapestryHealth’s Response to RIN 0955-AA13⏎January 27, 2026⏎Page 3 of 4⏎and reducing avoidable hospital admissions. For example, an AI model can flag a heart failure⏎patient on high-dose diuretics whose respiratory rate has subtly increased over 48 hours – a⏎pattern often missed by routine checks but highly predictive of an impending fluid overload crisis.⏎This approach leverages AI’s capability to analyze complex data sets and identify patterns that⏎may otherwise go unnoticed. Additionally, AI can help identify specific opportunities for⏎behavioral health integration within the long-term care setting. For example, AI can analyze⏎movement patterns to detect when a typically active resident begins spending prolonged periods⏎alone in their room, prompting a depression screening before social withdrawal deepens. Through⏎its advanced analytics, AI supports the recognition of behavioral health needs and facilitates the⏎development of intervention plans tailored to individual patients. Additionally, AI can help identify⏎specific opportunities for behavioral health integration within the long-term care setting. Through⏎its advanced analytics, AI supports the recognition of behavioral health needs and facilitates the⏎development of intervention plans tailored to individual patients.⏎VI. Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎Q1: Biggest barriers to private sector innovation & adoption?⏎Response: The "Efficiency Penalty" in Reimbursement.⏎In the low-margin SNF sector, adoption is driven by ROI. The biggest barrier is that current⏎reimbursement (RVUs) pays for time spent, not outcomes achieved. AI reduces time spent,⏎effectively lowering revenue for efficient providers. Until payment models reward "Capacity"⏎(patients managed safely) rather than "Minutes" (time spent documenting), innovation will⏎remain a financial liability for LTC providers.⏎Q2: Regulatory/payment changes to prioritize?⏎Response: Unblock Data Silos & Value Cognitive Interpretation.⏎1. Unblock Concurrent Billing (Amend 42 C.F.R. § 410.78): CMS must permit concurrent billing of⏎RPM (99454) and RTM (98975) when distinct hardware/data streams are used. For complex SNF⏎patients, AI needs both physiological and therapeutic data to be safe and effective. In addition to⏎removing the subregulatory guidance that prohibits concurrent billing of 99454 and 98975, CMS⏎should amend 42 C.F.R. § 410.78 to explicitly permit concurrent billing for services that utilize⏎distinct hardware/data streams.⏎2. Create "AI-Guided Interpretation" Codes (Revisit 42 C.F.R. § 414.20): Introduce add-on codes⏎that specifically reimburse the "Human-in-the-Loop" verification step, ensuring safety without⏎penalizing the provider for the cost of the software.⏎Q5: How can HHS support private sector (accreditation)?⏎Response: Standardize "Contactless & Ambient" Monitoring.⏎⏎TapestryHealth’s Response to RIN 0955-AA13⏎January 27, 2026⏎Page 4 of 4⏎HHS should establish an accreditation class for "Ambient Vital Sign Monitoring" (e.g.,⏎radar/LiDAR). In the SNF sector, wearables have high failure rates due to dementia and skin⏎fragility. Validating "Contactless" as a standard of care is crucial for equitable access in this⏎population.⏎Q7: Decision makers & administrative hurdles?⏎Response: The "Capital vs. Operational" Mismatch for Facility Administrators.⏎The SNF Administrator is the key decision-maker but operates on a fixed per-diem. The hurdle is⏎that AI requires upfront Capital Expense (sensors), but savings (reduced hospitalizations) accrue⏎to the Payer (Medicare), not the Facility. Payment models must share these savings with the⏎facility to incentivize infrastructure investment.⏎Q9: Patient/Caregiver challenges & concerns?⏎Response: The Tension Between "Safety" and "Surveillance".⏎Families want the safety of continuous monitoring ("never alone"), but residents fear the indignity⏎of cameras ("Big Brother"). This specifically validates the need for Privacy-Preserving AI⏎(Contactless Radar) which provides the safety of an ICU monitor without the invasion of a camera.⏎VII. Conclusion⏎The "AI Doctor" will not work for free—and neither can the human clinicians who supervise them.⏎By modernizing payment rails to reward preventive efficiency and verified oversight, HHS and⏎CMS can solve the staffing crisis and dramatically improve the quality of life for our nation's⏎seniors.⏎TapestryHealth thanks the Deputy Secretary for his attention to this matter. We would be happy⏎to provide any additional information or materials that you may need in assessing how to⏎integrate AI into clinical care services.⏎Sincerely,⏎Mordy Eisenberg, NHA⏎Chief Growth Officer⏎TapestryHealth♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0007♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To bring precision medicine closer to scale, every medical society and clinical society will need to feed its information to AI. In addition the integration of more ontologies can potentially help the AI reason.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0008♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Solomita, TERESA, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔TERESA♔Solomita♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am a Licensed Clinical Social Worker with many years of training and experience.  I am raising urgent concerns about patient privacy, safety, lack of independent evidence, and the need for clear federal regulation, especially in behavioral health and psychotherapy in the use of AI in psychotherapy. While AI is advancing rapidly, the science and safeguards have not kept pace.⏎⏎I believe innovation must be grounded in rigorous research, enforceable standards, and protection of the therapeutic relationship.⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0009♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Oppen, Anita, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anita♔Oppen♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔This is a Request for Information (which functions like a proposed rule invitation for public input) on the use and regulation of artificial intelligence (AI) in the health sector, particularly related to health information technology and health data. The ONC is seeking public comments on how AI tools should be governed, used responsibly, and integrated into patient care and healthcare systems, especially with regard to data security, interoperability, and patient safety. You can comment on aspects such as the balance between innovation and patient protection, transparency in AI systems, and how to encourage ethical use of AI in healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0010♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Zecola, Steven, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Zecola♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Office of the Secretary⏎RFI to Harness Artificial Intelligence⏎To Deflate Health Care Costs and Make⏎America Healthy Again (Dec. 19, 2026)⏎RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (Dec. 23, 2026)⏎Comments of Steven Zecola, a Patient Advocate⏎January 16, 2026⏎The Dimensions of Artificial Intelligence in the Healthcare Industry⏎The Department of Health and Human Services (“HHS”) should be commended for seeking to⏎leverage technology to lower healthcare costs and improve care. Since being diagnosed with⏎Parkinson’s disease 24 years ago, I have watched healthcare R&D – across multiple disease⏎fronts – proceed at a glacial pace. Conversely, costs (and related subsidies) have skyrocketed.⏎Fortuitously, artificial intelligence offers the potential to fundamentally change these trends.⏎HHS should view AI’s short-term effect as improving the odds of success and shortening⏎feedback cycles. However, to achieve significant breakthroughs as AI systems develop, HHS (via⏎the FDA) will need to completely revamp its approach to drug discovery and development.⏎Rather than being an overzealous gatekeeper at every step of the way, its role should shift to⏎being an auditor with specifically delimited responsibilities.⏎Dimension #1. Incorporation of AI into Drug Discovery⏎The biggest benefit to the healthcare industry’s performance from AI is achievable from drug⏎discovery. The federal government expends $44 billion per year in funding basic healthcare⏎research. However, a small fraction of basic research initiatives pass through the “Valley of⏎Death” and make it to a clinical trial. The average length of this phase is approximately 15 years⏎per endeavor, where FDA involvement impacts the pace of progress. Even after overcoming⏎this initial barrier, less than 10% of such promising remedies receive final FDA approval.⏎Accounting for the costs of failures, the average FDA drug approval costs society almost $3⏎billion and takes decades to reach the market from its inception in the lab. This system is a⏎⏎prescription for failure, and the results of such a scheme appear every day in the real world in⏎the form of high costs and poor performance.⏎In contrast, AI identifies potential treatments much faster than traditional methods. By enabling⏎the efficient exploration of biological variability, AI can dramatically increase the number of⏎experiments by studying up to a trillion interactions between variables. In other words, AI can⏎process vast amounts of biological data, uncover hidden causal relationships, and generate new⏎actionable insights.⏎Significantly, more than 50% of the nation’s healthcare costs are attributable to patients with⏎multiple chronic illnesses and this figure exceeds 75% when complex diseases are included. AI is⏎particularly promising for complex, multifactorial conditions – such as neurodegenerative⏎diseases, autism spectrum disorders, and multiple chronic illnesses – where conventional⏎reductionist approaches have failed.⏎Additionally, there are over 30 million people in the U.S. affected by rare diseases, with more⏎than 7,000 different types in existence.⏎HHS should encourage AI initiatives to address these conditions because they hold the potential⏎to improve the health of the nation’s most disadvantaged citizens and significantly cut the costs⏎of providing care. Let the scientists study science, not the FDA requirements. By being forced⏎to have one eye on the finish line, scientists tend to craft their work to satisfy the FDA, rather⏎than the rudiments of a flawed biological process.⏎In the short-run, HHS should direct its grants toward AI-generated basic research, with a⏎particular emphasis on the hard-to-solve illnesses. At the same time, the FDA should be putting⏎into place a new (de)regulatory system for AI-initiated programs to enable breakthrough⏎treatments in a compressed timetable.⏎Dimension #2. Incorporation of AI into the Drug Development Process⏎Simply relying on AI for drug discovery, while subjecting its advances to the current approval⏎process would undermine the use of the technology. The current clinical procedures employed⏎by the FDA entail an average of 12 years and $2 billion for each drug approval. Even scheduling⏎a meeting can take six months for a “fast track” application. Enhancements to the existing⏎processes alone would not capture the true potential of AI.⏎As a start, improvements from AI can already be had in fulfilling the exhaustive regulatory⏎documentation requirements, which today add up to as much as 30% of the cost of compliance.⏎In the short-run, AI can improve drug development by:⏎• Automating and validating regulatory documentation⏎⏎• Enhancing trial design and participant stratification⏎• Monitoring safety and efficacy in near real-time⏎• Reducing administrative and compliance costs⏎For example, in the U.K., the Medicines and Healthcare Products Regulatory Agency reported⏎that clinical trial approval times were twice as fast with AI and associated reforms.⏎To achieve much greater long-term gains, HHS should not be involved in pre-clinical AI research⏎and should collapse all clinical work utilizing AI into one elongated trial rather than discrete⏎Phase I, II and III trials, given that AI can be used to continually update and validate⏎documentation. This change would not require statutory change or agency rulemaking because⏎clinical trial design is not codified in the FDA’s rules.⏎As participants are added to a trial, safety results can be examined and reported in real time.⏎Once the trial surpasses a certain number such as 1000 participants with proven efficacy and⏎meeting the specified safety protocols, it would be approved for roll-out. The role of the⏎government in such an approach would be as auditor to validate the output of the trial. This⏎function would include experimental validation, mechanistic understanding, and ethical⏎oversight. Real-world data also could more readily collected after launch thereby offering⏎another safety feature.⏎With these changes, FDA personnel would shift from episodic gatekeepers to continuous⏎auditors, which would require a fundamental change in organizational culture. While safety⏎concerns would remain important, the responsibility and accountability would be more equally⏎shared with the applicants and trial participants. Additionally, the prolonged suffering of⏎existing patients would be factored into the public welfare analysis in reviewing preliminary⏎safety results. For example, the 33 million people with one or more of the 7000 rare diseases⏎now have a near 0% chance of relief under current regulatory procedures, and their plight must⏎be considered while the contours of biology are examined.⏎A specific example of this imbalance relates to the AI-generated treatment of Familial⏎Adenomatous Polyposis (“FAP”). The new treatment produced a large, sustained reduction in⏎polyps with no significant safety concerns.⏎The company hopes to meet with the FDA in 1H2026. In the meanwhile, the current treatment⏎for FAP is an expensive, intrusive, and a lifelong endeavor. Early detection strategies cost $10k+⏎and late detection costs $37k+. The cost to treating metastatic colorectal cancer (for which FAP⏎predisposes) can be extremely high, up to $300,000. The constant exams and excisions are the⏎norm for these patients. Yet the FDA is not inclined to ask these patients whether they would⏎like to try the pill or whether continued excisions would be preferable. In this case, the answer⏎from most patients would be obvious – if given the choice.⏎⏎Dimension #3. Enhance Data Collection to Empower AI⏎AI harbors the proverbial statistical stigma of “garbage in, garbage out.” Accordingly,⏎comprehensive, and accurate, data is essential to its success. Yet this another area where the⏎healthcare industry has failed.⏎The industry has evolved with each provider, or family of providers, encouraging their patients⏎to sign up for a customer portal. The providers typically treat the information on those portals⏎as their own for purposes of research. However, the providers do not own the data. Each⏎patient owns his or her data.⏎There have been few instances where comprehensive data has been collected. For example, in⏎2010, The Michael J. Fox Foundation launched the Parkinson’s Progression Markers Initiative⏎(PPMI) to find the biological markers of Parkinson’s onset and its progression. That study led to⏎the impressive finding of a tool that can detect pathology not only of people diagnosed with⏎Parkinson’s, but also in individuals that are at a high risk of developing it. However, that study⏎was geared towards Parkinson’s disease and has only a few thousand participants.⏎To broaden the scope and applicability of healthcare data, HHS should establish national⏎standards for patient-facing data collection that:⏎• Use interoperable formats⏎• Capture both diagnostic outcomes and relevant explanatory variables⏎• Preserve patient ownership and informed consent⏎• Enable longitudinal tracking while protecting privacy and security⏎As the above information from participants is collected over time, researchers would use AI to⏎identify combinations of variables that point towards promising results.⏎Once this format is established, HHS should establish a goal of enrolling 100,000 participants⏎within two years.⏎Dimension #4. Use of AI to Establish Standards of Care and Price Ceilings⏎There are no national standards of care for diseases or other health maladies in the United⏎States. Patients oftentimes do not understand the nature of their infliction, the options to treat⏎it, or the costs of the various options to remedy it.⏎On a parallel track, HHS might fund basic research targeted to a particular ailment, the FDA⏎might (or might not) approve it, Medicare might (or might not) cover it, and some insurance⏎companies may cover the treatment and some may not.⏎Moreover, the costs of various treatments may vary greatly from facility to facility –⏎unbeknownst to the patient.⏎⏎Layered on top of this market dysfunction, healthcare practitioners have the desire (and the⏎economic incentive) to provide the best (and likely the most expensive) possible service to their⏎patients.⏎As an economist would conclude, the supply curves and demand curves in the healthcare⏎industry do not lead to a pareto optimal solution. That is, there is a market failure, primarily⏎relating to a lack of actionable information.⏎In the short-run, AI can help address these failures by aggregating and analyzing how care is⏎delivered across the country and identifying patterns associated with better outcomes and⏎lower costs. These insights could be used to inform evidence-based minimum standards of care⏎and improve transparencies around pricing and performance.⏎Over the longer term, the outputs of these systems could be used to establish a minimum⏎standard of care for all (or most) ailments. These standards would be mandatorily covered by⏎insurance. Concurrently, the outputs for these standards of care could be supplemented by⏎regional price ceilings for the various practices based on a comprehensive industry analysis.⏎As experience is gained from these informational AI systems, a future version could be⏎programmed to automatically calculate the prescribed minimum standards of care and the⏎price ceilings to mimic the functioning of demand and supply curves. An algorithm could be⏎constructed using a specified level of subsidy provided by the federal government as the⏎equilibrium. As the federal subsidy exceeds certain pre-set limits, AI would be used to address⏎the disequilibrium by providing to law makers various options that would lower the price ceiling⏎for certain conditions and/or lower the minimum standard of care.⏎In scenarios where the stipulated federal subsidy was exceeded, some classes of patients would⏎be denied receiving payment for the best available treatment (unless they had supplemental⏎insurance) and/or some healthcare providers would suffer a diminution of profits.⏎Such an approach would require Congressional approval, but such tradeoffs are occurring now⏎– without factual inputs or informed choices. In this dimension, AI could be used to address the⏎industry’s massive information failure and tackle the ever-increasing subsidies.⏎Dimension #5. Incorporation of AI into HHS’s Internal Processes⏎AI can also improve the efficiency and effectiveness of HHS’s internal operations. While the⏎potential percentage gains would be smaller than that for the drug discovery and development⏎dimensions, even modest improvements can yield meaningful savings given the scale of federal⏎healthcare spending.⏎Early applications of AI should focus on areas with clear return on investment and limited risk,⏎such as⏎⏎• Fraud, waste, and abuse detection⏎• Claims auditing and compliance monitoring⏎• Internal workflow automation⏎HHS should deploy these tools incrementally, evaluate their performance, and use lessons⏎learned to inform broader adoption across its various divisions.⏎Dimension X – “Accelerating” the Adoption of “AI” as Part of Clinical Care⏎HHS also issued a companion RFI on Dec. 23 seeking to accelerate the adoption of AI in clinical⏎care. HHS should proceed very carefully on the issues raised in this RFI. For example, the AI⏎software embedded in my browser defines clinical care as:⏎Clinical care refers to the direct, hands-on medical treatment, diagnosis, and⏎management of patients by trained healthcare professionals.⏎In this regard, HHS should not involve itself in anything beyond establishing standards of care⏎for several reasons.⏎First, in an expanded capacity, HHS is likely to slow down the adoption of AI. For example, a few⏎years ago, HHS recognized the need to reorganize the Center for Evaluation and Drug Research.⏎After a multiple-year effort, it described its reorganization as:⏎“The office is now comprised of six cross-functional support offices and eight clinical⏎offices. The six cross-functional support offices oversee 12 divisions and several staff⏎groups. The eight clinical offices oversee OND’s 27 clinical divisions and six pharm/tox⏎review divisions”.⏎This is not the sort of thinking or organizational design to accelerate the adoption of new⏎technology in clinical care or elsewhere.⏎Second, we are in the first inning of AI system development. HHS should not be in the business⏎of accelerating or selecting any one AI system or application. Rather, it should set a level⏎playing field and let the market decide the best technologies.⏎Third, HHS’s legal authority does not extend to anything and everything related to healthcare.⏎Rather, HHS derives its authority from a series of laws. For example, the Public Health Service⏎Act provides the authority for HHS to lead responses to public health emergencies, declare⏎emergencies, manage the Strategic National Stockpile, and control diseases.⏎HIPAA (Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act) authorizes HHS to set standards for⏎electronic health information exchange, privacy, and security.⏎⏎Other statutes govern specific areas such as giving authority to agencies like SAMHSA for⏎substance abuse, OHRP for human research, and the OIG for oversight.⏎Proactively selecting or pushing a particular technology for patient care would stretch HSS’s⏎legal authority and could very well retard advancements as result of extended litigation. There⏎are multiple AI systems “in the works.” HHS should not be “in the business” of picking winners⏎and losers through a program of acceleration.⏎Conclusion⏎AI offers the opportunity for significant improvements in healthcare outcomes and⏎efficiencies—but only if it is integrated into a regulatory and governance framework designed⏎for its capabilities. Shoehorning AI into existing structures will blunt its impact and increase the⏎risk of implementation.⏎Each dimension described above requires a separate dedicated, multidisciplinary team⏎reporting to the Office of the Deputy Secretary. After the strategic direction for each⏎dimension is established, these teams should be tasked with:⏎• Developing detailed implementation plans, including budgetary requirements⏎• Identifying any statutory or regulatory barriers⏎• Establishing timelines, milestones, and evaluation criteria⏎• Addressing ethical and equity considerations⏎Drug discovery and drug development represent the highest-impact dimensions for AI⏎implementation. HHS should make use of external expertise in fashioning the details of an⏎appropriate regulatory framework for these dimensions.⏎The detailed plans for implementing AI should be approved and finalized before the end of⏎2026. As described herein, HHS should take a proactive, forward-looking role in harnessing AI⏎technology to deflate healthcare costs and make America healthy again.⏎Sincerely,⏎Steven Zecola⏎1 Thorton Ct.⏎Potomac Falls, VA 20165⏎szecola@gmail.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0011♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Atropos Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Atropos Health♔♔♔♔♔♔US Healthcare Current State – Evidence Gap (AI in Healthcare):⏎While healthcare data is now abundant, it remains siloed and difficult to transform into actionable⏎evidence. Today, only 14% of daily medical decisions are based on high-quality evidence—a disconnect⏎known as “The Evidence Gap.” Traditional clinical trials are limited and exclude nearly 70% of real-world⏎patients, including those with comorbidities, as well as underrepresented populations such as minorities,⏎children, and seniors, among others.⏎Generating high-quality insights typically takes months and significant resources, confining the process to⏎expensive, academic research settings. The current system places significant strain on U.S. healthcare:⏎many patients do not receive evidence-based care, leading to avoidable adverse outcomes, under or⏎delayed treatments, and increased costs.⏎About Atropos Health:⏎Atropos Health is on a mission to make personalized evidence available to every patient. We achieve this⏎mission starting with our GENEVA OS™ platform that automates the generation of publication-grade,⏎observational research studies for physicians, researchers, and patients, closing the evidence gap from⏎bench to bedside. We partner with leading health systems, life sciences organizations, pharmaceutical⏎companies, and insurers to improve healthcare across 40+ specialties in the USA.⏎The Atropos Evidence™ Network, is the largest federated data network in healthcare with over 300⏎million patient records spanning electronic medical record, claims, lab, and specialty data. As Health⏎Systems continue to drive policy and standards to enhance data infrastructure and interoperability,⏎Atropos Health's capabilities are a prime example of how data can be used to create actionable insights⏎and improve decision making at both the point of care and lead to life saving drug discoveries with speed⏎and accuracy.⏎Specific Questions⏎In addition to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, HHS seeks input on the following specific⏎questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The biggest barriers to private sector AI innovation and adoption in clinical care are regulatory⏎and liability uncertainty, slow and fragmented health system governance, and lack of⏎standardized evaluation and procurement pathways. These are compounded by difficult EHR⏎integration, data access and interoperability constraints.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (⏎e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎For non-medical device AI used in clinical care, novel legal and implementation challenges⏎include unclear liability and indemnification when AI-informed decisions contribute to harm,⏎ambiguity around accountability between clinicians, health systems, and vendors, and evolving⏎privacy and security risks associated with large-scale data use and model updating. These⏎challenges strain existing governance structures and contribute to conservative adoption. HHS⏎can play a critical role by clarifying liability and accountability expectations, issuing guidance on⏎privacy, security, and responsible data use for clinical AI, and promoting standardized⏎governance and contracting frameworks that reduce risk and administrative friction while⏎protecting patients.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎For non-medical device AI in clinical care, the most effective evaluation combines strong pre-⏎deployment validation with continuous post-deployment monitoring and human-centered⏎workflow assessment. Before go-live, models should be tested across sites and subpopulations,⏎evaluated in silent trials, assessed for usability and workflow fit, and stress-tested for robustness⏎to data shifts. After deployment, ongoing monitoring for performance drift, safety, and real-world⏎clinical and operational impact is essential. Evaluation should focus not only on accuracy, but⏎also calibration, clinical utility, equity, and clinician interaction. HHS support would meaningfully⏎accelerate safe and scalable adoption by establishing shared evaluation infrastructure and⏎standards, with cooperative agreements, targeted grants, and reference implementations as the⏎most impactful mechanisms.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎⏎The greatest future impact will come from actionable, transparent decision support grounded in⏎real-world evidence at the point of care; multimodal tools that improve high-stakes medication⏎decisions and prevent adverse events; and AI that surfaces care gaps and quality and equity⏎insights in real time. These approaches offer the strongest path to improving outcomes and⏎quality while reducing avoidable utilization and clinician burden. Atropos has proven this at⏎scale working with leading health system organizations across the US.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎The adoption of AI for clinical care is most strongly influenced by clinical leadership (CMO,⏎CMIO, CNIO, department chairs), who determine clinical relevance and frontline trust; executive⏎leadership (CEO, COO, CFO), who set strategic priorities, risk tolerance, and funding; IT and AI⏎governance bodies (CIO, digital health and AI committees), who control integration, security,⏎and scalability; and compliance, legal, and quality leadership, who shape regulatory⏎interpretation, liability posture, and patient safety standards. The primary administrative hurdles⏎include fragmented governance and slow, multi-layered approval processes; regulatory and⏎liability uncertainty around clinical AI; integration challenges with EHRs and clinical workflows;⏎lack of standardized evidence and evaluation frameworks to support purchasing decisions; data⏎governance and interoperability constraints; and procurement and contracting processes that⏎are misaligned with the pace of AI innovation.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would materially expand market opportunities, accelerate innovation,⏎and improve the real-world impact of AI in clinical care by enabling timely, standardized access⏎to high-quality longitudinal data across settings and populations. The greatest impact would⏎come from broader adoption of common data standards for structured EHR data to support⏎generalizable real-world evidence; improved linkage of clinical, utilization, pharmacy, claims,⏎and patient-reported outcomes data across care settings to assess downstream outcomes,⏎safety, and cost; and the development of shared benchmarking and evaluation frameworks so⏎AI tools can be consistently validated and trusted across institutions and populations..⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers want AI in clinical care to address persistent challenges such as⏎variation in care, delays in diagnosis and treatment, lack of personalization, and limited⏎⏎transparency in clinical decision-making. AI tools that leverage real-world clinical data can help⏎clinicians make more consistent, timely, and patient-specific decisions by showing how similar⏎patients have been treated and what outcomes were achieved.⏎At the same time, patients and caregivers express concerns about trust, bias, and the⏎appropriate role of AI in care. They are wary of AI systems that appear to replace clinician⏎judgment, lack transparency, or are trained on non-representative data. Patients also⏎emphasize the importance of data privacy, equity, and clear accountability for clinical decisions.⏎Overall, patients and caregivers are most supportive of AI that augments clinician decision-⏎making with interpretable, real-world evidence, improves equity and consistency of care, and⏎operates within strong governance and privacy safeguards.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0012♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Santa Clara University's Markkula Center for Applied Ethics, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Santa Clara University's Markkula Center for Applied Ethics♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎From: Santa Clara University’s Markkula Center for Applied Ethics (Guadalupe Hayes-⏎Mota)⏎Subject: Response to RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Response to the Request for Information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by Guadalupe Hayes-Mota on behalf of the Markkula Center for Applied Ethics,⏎Santa Clara University⏎The Markkula Center for Applied Ethics at Santa Clara University appreciates the opportunity to⏎inform federal strategy for accelerating the responsible adoption of artificial intelligence in⏎clinical care. Our work sits at the intersection of healthcare delivery, biotechnology, and ethics.⏎AI capability is no longer the primary constraint. Integration is.⏎Across the country, technically sound systems stall before routine use because of procurement⏎timelines, uncertainty about liability, fragmented validation practices, and payment models that⏎do not reward safe implementation. The central challenge for federal policy is institutional rather⏎than computational.⏎To move from isolated pilots to systemic improvement, HHS can use existing statutory and⏎programmatic authorities to create clearer expectations for transparency, oversight, and⏎economic viability. Below we respond directly to priority domains raised in the RFI.⏎Specific Responses to RFI Questions⏎1. Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Clinical Adoption⏎The major obstacles are operational friction and trust gaps.⏎Procurement versus model evolution. Health system purchasing and integration cycles can⏎extend well beyond a year. During that time, clinical practices, patient populations, and data⏎environments change. Without mechanisms for reassessment and updating, systems risk⏎implementing tools whose original validation assumptions no longer match reality.⏎Markkula Center for Applied Ethics⏎500 El Camino Real, Santa Clara, California 95053-0633⏎408-554-5319⏎⏎Liability uncertainty. Health systems and clinicians remain unclear about how responsibility⏎should be allocated when harm arises from systematic model error versus clinician judgment.⏎Workflow failure. Tools that increase alerts or cognitive burden without fitting existing care⏎pathways are often ignored, even when technically sound.⏎2. Regulatory and Programmatic Priorities⏎HHS already possesses meaningful policy levers through ONC Health IT Certification, CMS⏎payment policy, and FDA device authority. The opportunity is alignment and operational⏎execution across these mechanisms rather than creation of new ones.⏎Recent reporting underscores why implementation details matter. A Reuters investigation⏎examined post-market device reports associated with Johnson & Johnson subsidiary Acclarent’s⏎TruDi Navigation System after artificial intelligence capabilities were introduced. Reuters found⏎that, following the AI addition, FDA received a substantially larger number of unconfirmed⏎malfunction and adverse event reports, including allegations that surgeons were misinformed⏎about instrument location during sinus procedures. The article also emphasized a critical⏎limitation: Medical Device Reports are not designed to establish causality and may be⏎incomplete.⏎Regardless of ultimate causation, these reports demonstrate an operational reality. Frontline⏎users, purchasers, and health systems often do not have easy access to clear, comparable,⏎version-specific information describing what changed, what evidence supports the current⏎version, the contexts in which performance is validated, and the conditions necessary for safe⏎use.⏎ONC Health IT Certification⏎45 CFR § 170.315(b)(11)⏎Current certification criteria for Predictive Decision Support Interventions already require⏎extensive transparency through “source attributes.” These include intended use, intended users,⏎patient populations, development data characteristics including demographic representativeness,⏎input features, external validation processes, quantitative performance measures, ongoing⏎monitoring expectations, and update or maintenance information.⏎Markkula Center for Applied Ethics⏎500 El Camino Real, Santa Clara, California 95053-0633⏎408-554-5319⏎⏎However, these disclosures are frequently difficult to compare across products and are not⏎consistently structured for operational decision making.⏎Recommended federal action⏎ASTP/ONC should translate existing disclosure obligations into a standardized, computable⏎format. A model-card style artifact aligned with the National Institute of Standards and⏎Technology AI Risk Management Framework could be machine-readable and consistently⏎presented across vendors.⏎At minimum, it should identify:⏎1. the exact software and model version in clinical use⏎2. a clear history of modifications affecting AI functionality⏎3. validation scope for each version⏎4. known limitations, contraindicated uses, and foreseeable failure modes⏎5. required user competencies, training, and workflow dependencies⏎The central gap is not absence of authority. It is standardization, usability, and the ability of real⏎organizations to operationalize what is already required.⏎FDA statutory framework⏎FDCA § 520(o)(1)(E)(iii)⏎Software may fall outside device regulation only if a healthcare professional can independently⏎review the basis of the recommendation so that the professional does not primarily rely on the⏎software’s output. FDA guidance elaborates four criteria, all of which must be met, with⏎independent review being pivotal.⏎For many modern machine-learning systems, what constitutes sufficient independent review⏎remains uncertain for developers, purchasers, and clinicians.⏎Recommended federal action⏎HHS should promote tighter coordination between FDA and ASTP/ONC so expectations for⏎transparency, explainability, and documentation are interpreted consistently across certification⏎and device policy. Greater clarity would reduce regulatory arbitrage and market confusion.⏎Markkula Center for Applied Ethics⏎500 El Camino Real, Santa Clara, California 95053-0633⏎408-554-5319⏎⏎In safety-critical environments, FDA should further operationalize independent review through⏎explicit expectations regarding human factors, training, labeling, and post-market monitoring⏎when AI functionality is added to products already in use.⏎The Reuters findings regarding increased reporting following the TruDi AI introduction do not⏎determine fault. They do, however, illustrate why version control, user-visible change⏎communication, and actionable documentation are essential components of safe deployment.⏎Manufacturers of AI-enabled systems should therefore be expected to maintain and provide⏎customers with version-specific safety materials, including update-triggered risk analyses, user-⏎facing limitations, and monitoring plans capable of identifying performance drift or workflow⏎confusion early.⏎3. Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Device AI⏎For functions such as ambient documentation or generative drafting, the unresolved question is⏎allocation of responsibility.⏎Vendor agreements frequently designate the clinician as the final authority. Yet in high-⏎throughput environments, time pressure can limit meaningful verification.⏎Recommended federal action:⏎HHS can convene stakeholders and publish model contractual principles or safe-harbor⏎approaches that distinguish:⏎• systematic or repeatable model defects, and⏎• patient-specific clinical judgment.⏎This would create predictability without requiring new legislation.⏎4. Evaluation Infrastructure⏎Today, validation is fragmented. Hospitals repeatedly test similar tools with limited resources,⏎producing inconsistent evidence.⏎Recommended federal action:⏎HHS, including NIH and AHRQ, should support regional or federated evaluation collaboratives⏎Markkula Center for Applied Ethics⏎500 El Camino Real, Santa Clara, California 95053-0633⏎408-554-5319⏎⏎through cooperative agreements. These entities could test performance across diverse⏎populations while allowing data to remain local.⏎A shared backbone reduces duplication and strengthens trust.⏎5. Reimbursement and Incentives⏎Payment policy heavily shapes behavior.⏎If AI saves time, adoption is straightforward. If AI improves quality but requires additional⏎clinician effort to verify safely, organizations may be penalized financially.⏎Recommended federal actions:⏎• CMS should explore billing mechanisms or add-on payments that recognize clinician⏎time spent validating complex algorithmic outputs.⏎• MIPS and other quality programs could reward documented governance practices such as⏎bias auditing, monitoring, and incident reporting.⏎• CMS could evaluate pathways that allow qualifying Software as a Medical Device⏎products to demonstrate value through reductions in utilization or complications.⏎6. Evidence from Successes and Failures⏎Federal policy should follow real-world performance, not marketing claims.⏎Example of relative success: Autonomous diabetic retinopathy screening achieved uptake in⏎part because it fit a defined workflow and aligned with reimbursement structures.⏎Example of caution: Independent evaluation of a widely used proprietary sepsis model found⏎poor sensitivity and large numbers of false alarms. The lesson is straightforward. Performance in⏎development environments does not guarantee effectiveness in live care settings.⏎Recommended federal action:⏎Strengthen expectations for post-deployment monitoring and transparent reporting of real-world⏎outcomes.⏎Markkula Center for Applied Ethics⏎500 El Camino Real, Santa Clara, California 95053-0633⏎408-554-5319⏎⏎8. Interoperability and Data Standards⏎Without reliable metadata, learning health systems risk confusing AI-generated content with⏎human-authored information.⏎Recommended federal action:⏎ASTP/ONC should continue work through USCDI+ and FHIR implementation guidance so that⏎provenance information can identify which system generated a recommendation, under what⏎version, and at what time.⏎10. Research Priorities⏎The most urgent gaps are in implementation science.⏎We need longitudinal evidence regarding automation bias, clinician trust calibration, and⏎whether reliance on AI alters professional skill development over time.⏎The United States does not lack innovation capacity. It lacks predictable, repeatable pathways to⏎deploy innovation responsibly.⏎By standardizing transparency under existing ONC authority, aligning interpretation of FDA’s⏎statutory CDS boundaries, modernizing incentives within CMS, and investing in shared⏎evaluation infrastructure, HHS can transform trustworthy AI from aspiration into operational⏎reality.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Guadalupe Hayes-Mota⏎Director of Bioethics⏎Markkula Center for Applied Ethics⏎Santa Clara University⏎Markkula Center for Applied Ethics⏎500 El Camino Real, Santa Clara, California 95053-0633⏎408-554-5319♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0013♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Global Liver Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Global Liver Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔February 9, 2026⏎ASTP/ONC⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mailstop: 7033A⏎330 C Street S.W⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Attn: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎To whom it may concern:⏎Global Liver Institute (GLI) appreciates the⏎opportunity to provide concrete, experience-⏎based feedback on the use of artificial intelligence⏎(AI) in the health care sector.⏎GLI is a 501c(3) nonprofit organization founded in⏎the belief that liver health must take its place on⏎the global public health agenda commensurate⏎with the prevalence and impact of liver illness. GLI⏎promotes innovation, encourages collaboration,⏎and supports the scaling of optimal approaches to⏎help eradicate liver diseases. Operating globally,⏎GLI is committed to solving the problems that⏎matter to liver patients and equipping advocates to⏎improve the lives of individuals and families⏎impacted by liver disease and co-existing⏎conditions such as obesity.⏎GLI agrees with the administration regarding the⏎promise AI presents for health care. When used⏎appropriately, AI has the potential to improve care delivery by supporting earlier disease⏎1⏎⏎detection, reducing administrative burden, personalizing care, providing additional lifestyle⏎guidance, and improving coordination across fragmented systems. For patients, this can⏎mean faster diagnoses, clearer information, and more time with clinicians. For providers, AI⏎can help streamline documentation and enhance decision support, allowing clinicians to⏎focus more fully on direct patient care.⏎At the same time, AI has fallen short when deployed in high-stakes administrative functions,⏎where its use has, in some cases, amplified delays, errors, and inequities rather than⏎improving outcomes. In clinical decision-making, its use also increases patient stress, as⏎patients report concerns about losing the human touch in their care and about the lack of⏎transparency around when and how AI is used. The rapid pace of adoption underscores the⏎need for clear guardrails to ensure these tools are used in ways that enhance clinical⏎judgment, patient trust, and equitable access to care. Incorporating patient and caregiver⏎experience into design and evaluation is essential to ensuring these tools improve outcomes⏎and do not create new barriers.⏎AI Use in Coverage Decisions and Prior Authorization⏎One of the most consequential and concerning applications of AI in health care today is its⏎use by payers in coverage determinations and prior authorization. Lawmakers across the⏎political spectrum have acknowledged that traditional prior authorization processes already⏎delay or deny necessary care. In a recent hearing held by the Energy and Commerce⏎Committee, Committee Chair Brett Guthrie raised concerns about the impacts of AI, stating⏎that “people are being denied care.” Committee Ranking Member Frank Pallone similarly⏎warned that new AI-driven models risk creating “another barrier” for patients who already⏎face significant obstacles to accessing care.⏎The use of AI to automate or accelerate coverage decisions raises the stakes further. When⏎algorithms are trained on historical spending patterns rather than clinical need, they risk⏎embedding past inequities into future decisions at scale. Lower spending is often⏎misinterpreted as lower need, disproportionately affecting low-income, rural, and minority⏎patients who have historically faced barriers to care. We have long held concerns about the⏎influence of cost effectiveness analyses using quality-adjusted life years (QALYs) and similar⏎measures that devalue people with disabilities and chronic conditions, underscoring the⏎need for transparency of the data used to automate coverage decisions. Speed alone does not⏎equal value if it comes at the cost of accuracy, fairness, or transparency.⏎Patient Harm, Bias, and Accountability Risks⏎2⏎⏎The consequences of AI-driven denials are not hypothetical. National survey data show that⏎93 percent of physicians report prior authorization harms clinical outcomes, and nearly one⏎in four has witnessed a serious adverse event — including hospitalization, disability, or⏎death — linked to delayed or denied care. 1 Data showing that the majority of appealed⏎denials are overturned suggests that many initial decisions are wrong, yet patients bear the⏎burden of delay, confusion, and financial stress in the meantime.2⏎Bias in AI systems can go unnoticed until harm is already widespread. One widely cited⏎example involved an algorithm used to identify patients for high-risk care management⏎programs that significantly under-identified Black patients by relying on historical health⏎care spending as a proxy for need.3 Black patients have historically received less care despite⏎similar or greater illness burden, leading the algorithm to systematically exclude patients⏎who would have benefited from additional support. When AI equates spending with need, it⏎quietly carries inequities forward.⏎The Importance of Human Oversight and Transparency⏎AI tools should support, not replace, clinical judgment. When AI is used to inform clinical⏎decision-making, clinicians should be transparent with patients about when and how these⏎tools are assisting their care. Patients consistently express concern that increasing reliance⏎on automated systems may erode the human connection that is central to effective,⏎compassionate health care. Clear communication and clinician accountability are essential⏎to maintaining trust and ensuring that AI augments — rather than substitutes for —⏎meaningful patient–provider relationships.⏎Maintaining meaningful human oversight is particularly important in clinical settings, where⏎AI-generated insights may influence diagnosis, treatment planning, or disease management.⏎Over-reliance on automated recommendations risks undermining clinician expertise and⏎could lead to inappropriate care if outputs are accepted without sufficient clinical context. AI⏎systems should be designed to function as decision-support tools, with clinicians retaining⏎full responsibility for interpreting and applying information based on individual patient⏎needs.⏎Transparency must also extend to data governance. Patients should retain ownership of⏎their personal health data, with clear protections governing how data are collected, used,⏎1 https://www.ama-assn.org/press-center/ama-press-releases/ama-survey-indicates-prior-authorization-⏎wreaks-havoc-patient-care⏎2 https://oig.hhs.gov/oei/reports/OEI-09-18-00260.pdf⏎3 https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎3⏎⏎shared, and stored when AI tools are deployed. Robust data protection policies are essential⏎to preserving patient autonomy and preventing misuse of sensitive health information.⏎When considering potential policy changes, HHS should prioritize programmatic and⏎payment policies that require meaningful human review and transparency for AI-enabled⏎tools used in clinical care and administrative decision-making. This includes establishing⏎standards for disclosure, auditability, data protections, and appeal rights when AI systems⏎are used in Medicare and other federally-supported programs. Existing policies governing⏎utilization management and clinical decision support should be revisited to ensure⏎automated tools do not supplant clinical judgment or undermine beneficiary protections.⏎Building Patient Trust Through Governance and Participation⏎A core challenge in the current AI landscape is that patients — the individuals most directly⏎affected by AI-enabled decisions — are rarely meaningfully involved in the design,⏎governance, or evaluation of these tools. When patient voices and lived experience are⏎absent, systems may prioritize efficiency or cost savings while overlooking real-world⏎impacts on access, understanding, and trust.⏎HHS has an opportunity to set expectations that patient and caregiver input be integrated⏎throughout the AI lifecycle, from development and testing to deployment and post-market⏎evaluation. Transparency, shared decision-making, and patient-centered metrics are⏎essential to building public confidence in AI-enabled care.⏎Aligning AI Innovation With Public Trust⏎HHS has stated its commitment to combating fraud, waste, and abuse while ensuring patients⏎receive appropriate care. These goals are not mutually exclusive. However, AI models that⏎financially benefit vendors or payers when care is withheld create misaligned incentives that⏎warrant careful scrutiny. Clinicians have also called for greater investment in AI research so⏎tools are rigorously tested for safety and effectiveness, deliver clear value, and do not⏎introduce avoidable risks or costs. To ensure AI advances quality care rather than⏎exacerbates disparities, HHS should pair stronger evidence generation with policies that⏎align reimbursement and program design with patient outcomes, not short-term cost⏎avoidance. AI can be a powerful tool for improving care, but only if accountability,⏎transparency, and patient protections are built in from the start.⏎Conclusion⏎4⏎⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment on this RFI and for your consideration of these⏎recommendations. HHS can accelerate responsible AI adoption by prioritizing transparency,⏎meaningful human oversight in high-stakes decisions, and patient-centered evaluation⏎before and after deployment. These guardrails will help ensure AI supports clinical care and⏎strengthens public trust as these tools continue to scale.⏎Sincerely,⏎Larry R. Holden⏎President & Chief Executive Officer⏎Global Liver Institute⏎About Global Liver Institute⏎Global Liver Institute (GLI) is a 501(c)3 nonprofit organization founded in the belief that liver⏎health must take its place on the global public health agenda commensurate with the⏎prevalence and impact of liver illness. GLI promotes innovation, encourages collaboration, and⏎supports the scaling of optimal approaches to help eradicate liver diseases. Operating globally,⏎GLI is committed to solving the problems that matter to liver patients and equipping advocates⏎to improve the lives of individuals and families impacted by liver disease. GLI holds Platinum⏎Transparency with Candid/GuideStar, is a member of the National Health Council and NORD,⏎and serves as a Healthy People 2030 Champion. Follow GLI on Facebook, Instagram, LinkedIn,⏎and YouTube or visit www.globalliver.org.⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0014♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Association for Molecular Pathology, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Association for Molecular Pathology♔♔♔♔♔♔ASSOCIATION FOR MOLECULAR PATHOLOGY⏎Providing global expertise in molecular testing that drives patient care⏎6120 Executive Boulevard, Suite 700, Rockville, Maryland, 20852⏎Tel: 301-634-7987 | Fax: 301-634-7995 | amp@amp.org | www.amp.org⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC)⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Electronically submitted via https://www.regulations.gov/commenton/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-⏎0001⏎Re: Docket: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001 HHS Health Sector AI Request For Information (RFI):⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎Thank you for the opportunity to submit comments in response to this Request For Information⏎(RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎(Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001). Below is a summary of our comments and requests⏎as they pertain to specific questions in the RFI along with additional background information⏎contained at the end.⏎The Association for Molecular Pathology (AMP) is an international medical and professional⏎association representing approximately 3,100 physicians, doctoral scientists, and medical⏎laboratory scientists (technologists) who perform or are involved with laboratory testing based⏎on knowledge derived from molecular biology, genetics, and genomics. Membership includes⏎professionals from academic medicine, hospital-based and private clinical laboratories, the⏎government, and the in vitro diagnostics industry. AMP members are highly involved in the⏎development, validation, and interpretation of molecular diagnostic tests, including biomarker⏎tests for the diagnosis and management of inherited disorders, cancers, and rare and infectious⏎diseases.⏎AMP supports the responsible adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) or machine learning tools to⏎improve clinical molecular diagnostics across clinical laboratory workflows spanning analytics,⏎reporting, and interoperability, provided that is consistent with the practice of laboratory⏎medicine including implementation with human-in-the-loop professional oversight, deliberate⏎validation, transparent performance monitoring, and appropriate guardrails to ensure accuracy,⏎equity, patient safety, and regulatory compliance. To ensure these AMP requests that HHS⏎prioritize four actions:⏎⏎1. Update the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA) to revise the definition⏎of “laboratory” to reflect contemporary molecular laboratory practices and add⏎“laboratory analytics” to cover the use of AI tools in clinical molecular diagnostic testing.⏎2. Provide implementation guidance and program support to adopt structured, standards-⏎based genomic and patient data for interoperability across Electronic Health⏎Record/Laboratory Information System and payers.⏎3. Address challenges with Prior Authorization (PA) by requiring payers to provide specific,⏎structured denial rationales including coding and policy references and support⏎electronic prior authorization (ePA) to better align processes across Medicare.⏎4. Promote validated, human-in-the-loop AI by encouraging validation on representative⏎populations, transparent documentation of training and evaluation data and post-⏎deployment monitoring with final clinical responsibility retained by the qualified laboratory⏎professionals and physicians.⏎AI holds tremendous promise for medicine including in the delivery of molecular laboratory⏎testing. We provide greater details below in response to the specific questions in the RFI.⏎Q2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care, and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations.⏎1. Update CLIA to reflect current laboratory practices⏎Clinical laboratories play an extremely important role in the U.S. healthcare system, as an⏎astounding 14 billion laboratory tests are conducted annually with 70% of all medical decisions⏎relying on the results of laboratory tests to guide diagnosis or treatments.1⏎In order to assist laboratory professionals, a wide range of Artificial Intelligence/Machine⏎Learning (AI/ML) tools are currently used in clinical laboratories. Some technologies, such as⏎algorithms, work in tandem as part of laboratory developed testing procedures while other more⏎complex AI tools are used to obtain patient results to guide treatment options.⏎Electronic Health Records (EHRs) that are integrated into healthcare systems and utilized by⏎laboratories throughout the country are tools that help streamline certain laboratory operational⏎processes and reduce administrative burdens. Laboratory information systems (LIS) and other⏎laboratory instrument software are critical to laboratory operations because they have many⏎other functions in addition to those in EHRs. The LIS is the core digital infrastructure that⏎manages, tracks, and integrates all operational, analytical, and reporting activities within a⏎clinical laboratory. In molecular diagnostics—including genomic medicine and infectious disease⏎1 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) https://www.cdc.gov/lab-week/about-⏎archive.html#:~:text=Today%2C%20medical%20professionals%20continue%20to,diagnosis%20and%20t⏎reatment%20of%20diseases.⏎⏎testing—the LIS serves as the backbone connecting specimen flow, analytical instruments,⏎interpretation workflows, compliance systems, and EHRs.⏎AI plays an extremely crucial role molecular pathology because molecular profiles obtained by⏎genomic testing in oncology, medical genetics, and infectious diseases can guide the clinical⏎management of patients in several ways. Genomic information can provide diagnostic,⏎prognostic, and predictive information, identify biomarker-driven targeted therapies, and detect⏎the development of resistance to therapy and antibiotics, among other uses. Large-scale⏎genomic sequencing is also standard of care for the diagnosis and management of many⏎inherited diseases, including developmental delay, neuromuscular disorders, and inherited⏎cardiomyopathies.⏎It is becoming increasingly common for laboratories to implement Next Generation Sequence⏎(NGS) testing, which analyzes thousands of genes, is becoming increasingly common in⏎laboratories. Clinicians utilize these tests to identify large numbers of genomic alterations in⏎tumors and microbial nucleic acids and their proteins, which are crucial for optimal patient care,⏎and treatment guidelines are developed based on specific molecular findings. For example, the⏎identification of molecular alterations in tumors is now standard of care in non-small cell lung,⏎breast, hematological neoplasms, and colorectal cancers. Therefore, it is imperative to optimize⏎the interpretation and reporting of molecular results among laboratories performing these tests⏎while utilizing various AI tools and working across different AI systems.⏎Regulatory Oversight of Laboratories through the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendment⏎(CLIA) Regulations⏎Regulatory oversight of clinical laboratories is provided through the Clinical Laboratory⏎Improvement Amendments (CLIA) of 1988 (US Government Publishing Office, Electronic Code⏎of Federal Regulations, (CFR) Title 42, §Part 493.1) 2,⏎In 2023, AMP released an extensive proposal to clarify the CLIA requirements. Specifically,⏎AMP’s proposal includes updating the definition of “laboratory”3 within 42 CFR Part 493 to⏎encompass modern-day laboratory practices that incorporate facilities that perform flow⏎cytometric, molecular, and genomic examinations that may use AI in their analysis. To further⏎reflect present-day laboratory practices, AMP created a definition for “laboratory analytics” that⏎encompasses several different types of AI that are used daily by laboratory professionals.⏎AMP requests HHS update the current definition of “laboratory” under 42 CFR 493.24 to⏎reflect modern-day laboratory practices and to include a new definition for “laboratory⏎analytics” with the following language :⏎Section 2.⏎2 http://www.ecfr.gov/cgi-bin/text-⏎idx?SID=1248e3189da5e5f936e55315402bc38b&node=pt42.5.493&rgn=div5#se42.5.493_11⏎3 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-493#p-493.2(Laboratory)⏎4 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-493#p-493.2(Laboratory)⏎⏎Definitions (a) Section 353(a) of the Public Health Service Act is amended to⏎read as follows -- “(a) Definitions (1) As used in this section, the term “laboratory”⏎or “clinical laboratory” means a facility for the biological, microbiological,⏎serological, chemical, immuno-hematological, hematological, biophysical,⏎cytological, pathological, flow cytometric, molecular, genomic, or other⏎examination of either materials derived from the human body or information⏎obtained through analysis of such materials, for screening purposes or for the⏎purpose of providing information for the prognosis, diagnosis, prevention, or⏎treatment of any disease or impairment of, or the assessment of the health of⏎human beings.⏎(7) As used in this section, the term “laboratory analytics” includes algorithms⏎(including those powered by artificial intelligence or machine learning), software,⏎mathematical formulae, clinical calculations, risk calculations, and other tools as⏎defined by the Secretary that are used as part of a testing procedure.⏎2. Provide implementation guidance and program support to adopt structured,⏎standards-based genomic and patient data for interoperability across Electronic⏎Health Record/Laboratory Information System and payers.⏎AMP has acknowledged the critical role of informatics in advancing genomic medicine. The⏎incorporation of genomics into clinical practice is progressing rapidly, with many laboratories⏎using advanced technology for next-generation sequencing (NGS). However, fully utilizing⏎genomic data within the EHR remains challenging because of limitations in interoperability⏎standards and technology. Organizations like AMP are working to develop guidelines to⏎standardize the interoperability of genomic data in EHR systems. Despite the expansion of⏎genomics in medicine, information systems struggle to effectively support genomic workflows⏎from laboratory and patient-facing provider perspectives. The complexity of genomic data, lack⏎of data standards, and insufficient genomic fluency and functionality have resulted in significant⏎gaps between the generation, interoperability, and utilization of genomic data. These gaps pose⏎challenges for laboratory and pathology professionals, clinicians, and patients in generating,⏎communicating, interpreting, and utilizing genomic test results. The AMP EHR Working Group⏎was formed to assess these challenges and propose solutions for the display and use of⏎genomic data in EHRs11. Genomic data can be difficult to locate in EHRs and more difficult to⏎compare across tests and panels over time. Additionally, the AMP recognizes that all⏎components of the software and systems used to generate genomic test results are important.⏎In 2016, Congress passed the 21st Century Cures Act. While the Act mandated that laboratories⏎and providers facilitate patient access to electronic health information (EHI), it did not include⏎specific, dedicated financial assistance to cover the costs of these technical implementations⏎and could increase financial and administrative burdens on clinical laboratories seeking to⏎⏎maintain compliance with regulatory requirements.5 To achieve data interoperability,⏎laboratories will require funding to ensure that they have the ability to broadly implement and/or⏎replace systems to support interoperability data standards in compliance with regulatory⏎requirements.⏎Although the use of genomics in medicine is expanding rapidly, information systems are lagging⏎in their ability to support genomic workflows from both the laboratory and patient-facing provider⏎perspectives. The complexity of genomic data, lack of needed data standards, lack of genomic⏎fluency and functionality, and several other factors have contributed to the gaps between⏎genomic data generation, interoperability, and utilization. These gaps pose significant⏎challenges to laboratory and pathology professionals, clinicians, and patients in terms of⏎generating, communicating, consuming, and using genomic test results.⏎Concerns exist regarding the ability of EHRs to capture complete and accurate patient⏎information, including genomic testing information (Carter et al., 2022, Background Table X, and⏎additional Background information), which may be amplified when utilizing AI systems. For⏎example, because the AI systems used for populating forms for prior authorization pull this⏎information from EHRs that may contain incomplete and/or inaccurate patient and/or genomic⏎testing information, they may erroneously delay or deny appropriate testing. Gaps in data⏎interoperability exacerbate these challenges. Additionally, AI systems must be maintained and⏎updated in real time to ensure that the latest payer policies and practice guidelines are⏎appropriately applied, along with other challenges mentioned in Table X. If AI systems utilize⏎flawed data, rendered downstream decisions may be inappropriate.⏎3. Address challenges with Prior Authorization (PA)⏎In 2025, the National Association of Insurance Commissioners conducted a report and found⏎that approximately 75 percent (3 out of 4) health plans use Artificial Intelligence to determine⏎prior authorization approvals.6⏎According to the Kaiser Family Foundation, nearly 50 million prior authorization requests were⏎sent to Medicare Advantage organizations in 2023. 7⏎The prior authorization and appeal processes are also slow and cumbersome. A 2024 survey of⏎providers by the American Medical Association demonstrated that providers and their staff⏎spend more than 13 hours/week (nearly two business days) on prior authorizations and⏎5https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6134556/#:~:text=The%2021st%20Century%20Cures%20Act⏎%20(the%20Act)%2C%20signed%20into,among%20other%20health%2Drelated%20initiatives.⏎6 https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-⏎files/NAIC%20AI%20Health%20Survey%20Report%20.pdf⏎7 https://www.kff.org/medicare/issue-brief/nearly-50-million-prior-authorization-requests-were-sent-to-⏎medicare-advantageinsurers-in-⏎2023/#:~:text=The%20vast%20majority%20of%20denied,of%20the%20prior%20authorization%20proces⏎s.⏎⏎complete an average of 41 prior authorizations per week. 8 Additionally, providers and⏎laboratories often cannot determine the reason for denials.⏎Policymakers must make several regulatory and statutory changes to ease the burden on⏎providers, streamline the prior authorization and appeals processes, and align Medicare⏎Advantage with Traditional Medicare to ensure coverage and payment for medically necessary⏎care for all Medicare beneficiaries.⏎AI is being applied to laboratory and/or treating provider-submitted test prior authorizations to⏎automate, accelerate, and optimize the review, approval, and billing-readiness processes. Key⏎cases, how they work, benefits, and risks are summarized in the background information below.⏎Specifically, regarding laboratory testing in molecular medicine and genomics, AI prior⏎authorization faces additional challenges, including complex clinical justification, evolving⏎guidelines, inadequate coding, and payer knowledge gaps (see Background).⏎AI-driven prior authorization has significant potential to reduce administrative friction and⏎improve test utilization; realizing these benefits requires addressing data quality, interoperability,⏎and regulatory challenges, particularly in specialized areas like genomic medicine. Key⏎considerations to achieve include, but are not limited to, the following:⏎• Regulatory Compliance: AI‑generated content must meet HIPAA, 21st Century Cures⏎Act, and payer‑specific requirements.⏎• Human‑In‑The‑Loop: All AI-drafted narratives should be reviewed and signed by a⏎qualified provider before submission.⏎• Data quality: Accurate CPT/HCPCS coding, up-to-date payer policies, and complete⏎patient demographics are essential for reliable AI performance.⏎• Improved EHR Integration: Better structured data capture in EHRs will enhance the⏎ability of AI to extract relevant clinical context.⏎• Explainable AI: Development of more transparent AI models that can explain denial or⏎appropriateness decisions to clinicians.⏎• Real-Time Policy Sync: Enables more dynamic integration with payer policies to reduce⏎lag in rule updates.⏎• Outcome Tracking: AI systems that learn from approved tests and their clinical outcomes⏎to refine appropriateness models over time.⏎Q6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and⏎help reduce costs?⏎4. Promote validated, human-in-the-loop AI⏎AI is being applied across multiple stages of molecular laboratory workflows, including sample⏎processing and data analysis, result interpretation, and clinical reporting (Background Table X).⏎8 https://www.ama-assn.org/system/files/prior-auth-reforms-issue-brief.pdf⏎⏎These applications aim to improve the accuracy, efficiency, turnaround time, and clinical utility⏎of molecular test results.⏎Artificial intelligence (AI) has moved from experimental use to routine deployment in several⏎areas of molecular diagnostics, particularly in genomic variant calling, infectious disease⏎sequencing, and laboratory quality monitoring. In specific analytic tasks with well-defined⏎performance metrics and large training datasets, AI has met or exceeded expectations for⏎accuracy and operational efficiency. However, AI has underperformed in areas involving⏎complex biological interpretation (e.g., variant pathogenicity classification, structural variant⏎interpretation, broad antimicrobial resistance prediction, and variant interpretation in the correct⏎clinical context). Multistakeholder engagement is now required to:⏎1. Promote responsible clinical validation and regulatory clarity.⏎2. Incentivize high-value AI applications with demonstrable patient impact.⏎3. Avoid overinvestment in tools that lack generalizability or cost-effectiveness.⏎4. Ensure interoperability, transparency, and equity in deployment.⏎AI in molecular diagnostics can deliver when it replaces manual analytic steps, reduces repeat⏎testing, shortens the time to targeted therapy, and/or increases the diagnostic, predictive or⏎therapeutic yield in actionable conditions. Several safeguards are necessary for safe and⏎effective integration into routine clinical practice (see Background). AI underdelivers when⏎biological knowledge gaps limit model reliability, implementation costs exceed operational⏎savings, and/or generalizability across populations is poor. AI in molecular laboratory medicine⏎is not uniformly transformative as its value may be task-dependent (see Background).⏎Safely and effectively integrating AI into molecular laboratory testing:⏎• Robust validation: Prospective clinical validation in diverse populations and⏎comprehensive variant sets.⏎• Transparent methodology: Clear documentation of model training data, performance⏎metrics, and limitations.⏎• Human-in-the-loop: Pathologists retain final decision-making authority; AI serves as a⏎decision support tool and not a replacement.⏎• Continuous monitoring: Ongoing performance tracking, error reporting, and model⏎recalibration.⏎• Ethical oversight: Institutional review, informed consent, and equity assessments for AI⏎deployment.⏎• Explainable AI (XAI): Development of interpretable models that provide transparent⏎reasoning for variant classifications.⏎• Standardized data exchange: Implementation of fit-for-purpose genomic data standards⏎to enable seamless AI integration across systems.⏎• Outcome tracking: Linking AI-driven diagnoses to patient outcomes to validate and refine⏎model performance.⏎⏎The adoption of AI in molecular diagnostics is rarely driven by a single individual. It typically⏎requires alignment across clinical, laboratory, financial, regulatory, and IT leadership (see⏎Background).Influence varies by organizational structure (academic medical center, community⏎hospital, or integrated delivery network). Strategic investment, regulatory clarity, and outcome-⏎focused validation will determine whether AI in molecular diagnostics reduces costs and⏎improves patient outcomes — or merely adds technological complexity without measurable⏎benefit.⏎Thank you again for the opportunity to comment on the use of AI in clinical care. AMP is happy⏎to be a resource If you require additional clinical or policy information to assist you with this⏎request, please contact AMP’s Director of Public Policy, Annie Scrimenti, at⏎ascrimenti@amp.org or AMP’s Senior Director, Clinical & Scientific Affairs; Director, Clinical⏎Guidance, Dr. Robyn Temple-Smolkin, at rtemple@amp.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Aaron D. Bossler, MD PhD⏎President, Association for Molecular Pathology⏎BACKGROUND⏎Prior Authorization Background⏎AI in PA use cases:⏎• Eligibility & coverage determination: Match patient, payer, and policy rules to determine⏎if a test is covered.⏎• Order appropriateness checking: Compare requested tests with clinical indications,⏎guidelines, and previous results to flag unnecessary or missing tests.⏎• Prior authorization (PA) form automation: Auto-population of PA forms from EHR and lab⏎systems.⏎• Clinical justification generation: Drafting evidence-based rationales citing guidelines,⏎prior results, and diagnosis codes.⏎• Workflow triage and routing: Prioritize urgent requests and route complex cases to⏎specialists or medical directors.⏎• Predictive denials and cost estimation: Predict the likelihood of denial and estimate⏎patient financial responsibility.⏎• Decision support for formulary/test selection: Recommend alternative tests or panels⏎with a higher likelihood of approval.⏎• Audit, reporting, and analytics: Identify denial patterns, provider behavior, and process⏎bottlenecks.⏎Models and technologies currently used:⏎• Natural language processing (NLP; named entity recognition and relation extraction) to⏎extract clinical indications, prior results, and text justifications.⏎⏎• Classification models for predicting approval/denial and required documentation.⏎• Transformer LLMs for drafting justifications and summarizing records (with guardrails).⏎• Rules-based engines for explicit payer policies and hard constraints.⏎• Robotic process automation (RPA) to interact with payer portals where APIs are⏎unavailable.⏎• Knowledge bases linking guidelines (e.g., clinical society guidance), coverage policies,⏎and prior authorization rules.⏎Table 1: Challenges Regarding AI Use in Laboratory Testing Prior Authorizations⏎Challenge Description⏎Data Quality AI systems rely on accurate and complete EHR data; poor documentation⏎Dependencies quality undermines model performance.⏎Payer Policy Payer coverage criteria change frequently and vary by plan, keeping AI⏎Variability systems updated is complex.⏎Black Box Decision Clinicians may not understand why AI flagged a test as inappropriate,⏎Making thereby reducing trust and buy-in.⏎Bias in Training Data Models trained on historical data may perpetuate biases in test ordering or⏎approval patterns.⏎Interoperability Gaps Integration between EHRs, lab systems, and payer platforms remains⏎inconsistent, thereby limiting their automation potential.⏎Privacy & Security Sharing clinical data with payers and third-party AI vendors raises concerns⏎regarding data security and patient privacy.⏎Liability & Lack of clarity regarding responsibility if AI-driven denials result in delayed⏎Accountability care..⏎Regulatory Oversight Limited guidance on the validation and monitoring of AI-driven prior⏎authorization systems for safety.⏎Genomic testing-specific challenges:⏎• Complex Clinical Justification: Genomic tests often require nuanced clinical reasoning⏎that may be difficult for AI to capture from EHR data alone.⏎• Evolving Guidelines: Genomic testing recommendations change rapidly as evidence⏎accumulates, making it difficult for AI models to stay current.⏎• Payer Knowledge Gaps: Many payers lack expertise in genomics, which may lead to⏎conservative approval criteria that AI systems may apply too rigidly.⏎Fit-for-purpose Data Interoperability Background⏎⏎The use of genomics in clinical medicine is expanding rapidly, and many laboratories are⏎implementing highly complex technologies to perform next-generation sequencing (NGS).⏎However, the full utilization of complex genomic data in the electronic health record (EHR) is⏎challenging in the current state of interoperability standards and technology. AMP and other⏎professional organizations are actively working to develop guidance to standardize the⏎interoperability of genomic data in EHR systems.⏎Genomic data can also be difficult to locate in electronic health records (EHRs) and can be⏎even more difficult to compare across different tests and panels over time. Additionally, AMP is⏎cognizant that all components of the software and systems used to generate genomic test⏎results for patients are important. AMP has long recognized the importance of informatics to the⏎success of genomic medicine.⏎With the increasing use of molecular pathology and genomic testing, complaints regarding the⏎significant lack of functionality that necessitates the use of paper records and/or manual transfer⏎of data to and from instrument software, laboratory information systems (LISs), and electronic⏎health records (EHRs) have increased. LISs and other laboratory instrument software are⏎critical to laboratory operations because they have many other functions in addition to those in⏎EHRs. The LIS is the core digital infrastructure that manages, tracks, and integrates all⏎operational, analytical, and reporting activities within a clinical laboratory. In molecular⏎diagnostics—including genomic medicine and infectious disease testing—the LIS serves as the⏎backbone connecting specimen flow, analytical instruments, interpretation workflows,⏎compliance systems, and EHRs. Reporting requirements for different testing methods⏎(molecular pathology, genomic and germline and somatic variant) in various types of⏎laboratories were examined with regard to challenges in EHRs, including the following⏎laboratories: molecular and genetic; cytogenetics; those performing fluorescence in⏎situ hybridization (FISH), tissue typing, histocompatibility, immunogenetics (human leukocyte⏎antigen (HLA) testing), and infectious organisms.⏎Utilizing the EHR Working Group’s subject matter expert analysis11, several concerns regarding⏎the use of AI can be extrapolated:⏎TABLE 2: How EHR Challenges Impact Concerns Regarding Artificial Intelligence Use in⏎Clinical Care for Genomic Medicine⏎EHR CHALLENGES Concerns Regarding Artificial⏎(Reproduced from Carter et al, 2022) Intelligence Use⏎EHRs are not yet ready to send accurate, coded and LLMs trained or prompted with⏎appropriately granular clinical history, signs, symptoms, incomplete/noisy/biased inputs⏎family history and other broad sets of data elements to produce incorrect inferences, poor⏎laboratories without generating significant burden on context for variant interpretation, and⏎providers.* unreliable recommendations⏎EHRs lack sufficient information about genetic test orders LLMs cannot map tests to appropriate⏎and generally do not have discrete variant result data to interpretation logic or link variants to⏎facilitate appropriate test ordering and utilization.* prior results; automated workflows⏎break⏎⏎Absence of standardized genomic variant data structures⏎from the LIS to the EHR.*⏎Interoperability standards for genomics are currently limited LLM-driven automation relying on⏎in several ways including the use of syntax with limited standards will fail across sites; model⏎hierarchy (HL7 version 2.x) and inadequate coding systems generalization is reduced and needs⏎for genomic orders and results.* complex normalization layers⏎Genomic reports between laboratories are variable in LLMs see multiple naming conventions⏎structure and content.* and result hierarchies, harming⏎mapping to canonical knowledge and⏎Implementation of molecular pathology and genomics⏎CDS⏎professional societies multi-organizational consensus⏎standards and guidelines for variant nomenclature,⏎hierarchical result structure, and genomic report formats has⏎been modest.*⏎There are no standards for how to display aggregated variant LLMs can't reliably synthesize⏎data over time and between sample sources, tests and longitudinal summaries or explain⏎laboratories which keeps clinical context and associated provenance; temporal reasoning⏎interpretation intact.* degrades⏎Consensus guidelines on best practice for requesting and LLMs cannot automate or advise on⏎providing reclassification of variants is not currently reclassification workflows or⏎available.* notifications safely⏎Genomic reports are difficult for medical personnel and LLMs may simplify or summarize⏎patients to understand, and in the US, patients have the right incorrectly; patient-facing outputs risk⏎to immediately access their genomic test reports upon oversimplification or alarming⏎request.* language⏎Absence of national and international standards for Clinical LLMs powering or generating CDS⏎Decision Support (CDS) rules. lack interoperable rule representations⏎and may produce non-actionable or⏎conflicting guidance⏎Currently, textual data from genomic reports is exported and LLMs must rely on brittle NLP⏎released to authorized parties, and analysis of such data is extraction pipelines, increasing⏎limited by its highly variable format and lack of structure. hallucinations and missed structured⏎facts⏎Current technology lacks sufficient functionality to ensure that LLM-driven data sharing or model⏎release of genomic data to appropriate healthcare training risks using data without proper⏎organizations, research studies, or clinical repositories consent or tracking provenance⏎requires and receives informed consent from the patient or⏎legal guardian, and/or institutional board review.⏎Current coding and interoperability standards are not LLMs cannot depend on consistent⏎adequate for genomic data.* identifiers or mappings to reference⏎databases, reducing accuracy of⏎variant interpretation⏎⏎LEGEND: This table has been modified from Electronic Health Records and Genomics: Perspectives from the⏎Association for Molecular Pathology Electronic Health Record (EHR) Interoperability for Clinical Genomics Data⏎Working Group (Table 1: A Summary of Challenges and Opportunities)13. *Areas where the Association for Molecular⏎Pathology is actively engaged with subject matter expert working groups addressing identified challenges and⏎opportunities. Future recommendations, along with accompanying educational offerings, are being developed by the⏎EHR Working Group and other AMP working groups to address these community needs. Abbreviations: EHR -⏎Electronic Health Record; FHIR - Fast Healthcare Interoperable Resources; LIS - Laboratory Information System;⏎LLM – Large Language Models.⏎Human-In-the-Loop Background Information for #6:Where have AI tools deployed in⏎clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and where have they fallen⏎short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care⏎outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Laboratory directors and clinical teams working with medical team senior leadership that have⏎identified ways for AI to help reduce the administrative burden and/or improve patient care. Most⏎influential decision-makers:⏎1. Laboratory Medical Director (analytic authority)⏎2. Chief Medical Officer or CMO (clinical governance)⏎3. Chief Financial Officer or CFO (financial gatekeeper)⏎4. Chief Information Officer/Chief Data Officer or CIO/CDO (technical feasibility)⏎5. Stewardship or genetics committees (domain endorsement)⏎6. External regulators (structural influence)⏎In most large academic centers, the Laboratory Medical Director + CFO + CIO + CMO⏎alignment is the critical adoption bottleneck. If the laboratory director is convinced of analytic⏎validity and the CFO sees cost justification with CMO endorsement required for standard-of-⏎care change, adoption is likely. Without alignment among these four, deployment rarely⏎progresses beyond limited pilots. Primary administrative hurdles include, but are not limited to,⏎implementation, data storage, and maintenance costs in both direct costs and FTE; ongoing⏎personnel and direct costs to support validation and use of AI in a CLIA environment; and⏎additional education to understand the complexity of the test systems and inputs needed to⏎build, launch, and operate AI in a manner that reduces the risk of patient harms and medical⏎errors. Other hurdles include regulatory ambiguity, reimbursement gaps, integration complexity,⏎validation burden, workforce trust, infrastructure cost, and fragmented governance.⏎AI in Molecular Laboratory Medicine Emerging High-Value Areas⏎o Deep learning variant calling.⏎o AI-augmented pathogen detection.⏎o Rapid genomic AST in defined organisms.⏎o Laboratory process monitoring.⏎o Supervised automated laboratory document (SOP, validation summary)⏎generation using LLM⏎⏎AI in Molecular Laboratory Medicine Areas Requiring Caution⏎o Stand-alone prediction of sequence variant pathogenicity.⏎o Broad-spectrum antimicrobial resistance modeling.⏎o Interpretation of structural or non-coding variants without functional validation.⏎o Interpretation of genomic alterations in complex neoplastic and non-neoplastic⏎clinical contexts⏎o Prediction of therapy effectiveness based on genomic variants⏎Table 3: AI Applications Across the Molecular Laboratory Workflow⏎Stage of AI Application Description Benefit⏎Workflow⏎Sample Automated image Computer vision algorithms Reduces manual review⏎Processing & analysis analyze gel electrophoresis time, improves consistency,⏎QC images, sequencing runs, and and automatically flags⏎PCR products for quality quality issues.⏎assessment.⏎Sample Robotic process AI-guided robotic systems can improves throughput,⏎Processing & automation handle sample preparation, reduces contamination risk,⏎QC DNA/RNA extraction, and library and improves⏎preparation with minimal human reproducibility.⏎intervention.⏎Sequencing Variant calling Machine learning models Reduces false⏎Data Analysis optimization improve the accuracy of variant positives/negatives and⏎detection from raw sequencing improves sensitivity for low-⏎data (NGS). frequency variants.⏎Sequencing Copy number variation AI algorithms identify deletions, Detects variants missed by⏎Data Analysis (CNV) detection duplications, and structural traditional methods and⏎variants based on the improves diagnostic yield.⏎sequencing depth and read⏎alignment patterns.⏎Data Analysis Quality filtering & AI models learn patterns in Improves data quality and⏎normalization sequencing noise and reduces the manual⏎systematically remove artifacts curation burden.⏎while preserving true variants.⏎Variant Pathogenicity Machine learning models (e.g., Aids interpretation of⏎Interpretation prediction SIFT, PolyPhen, CADD, variants of uncertain⏎AlphaMissense) predict whether significance (VUS),⏎variants are likely pathogenic accelerates reporting.⏎based on sequence context and⏎evolutionary conservation.⏎⏎Variant Functional prediction Deep learning models predict Improves variant⏎Interpretation protein structure changes and classification, supports⏎functional consequences of clinical decision-making.⏎missense variants.⏎Variant Literature & database NLP algorithms extract variant– Provides comprehensive⏎Interpretation mining disease associations from evidence for variant⏎scientific literature and integrate classification and reduces⏎curated databases (ClinVar, manual literature review⏎OMIM, gnomAD). requirements.⏎Clinical Phenotype-genotype AI systems match patient Identifies causative variants⏎Interpretation matching phenotypes to genetic variants and suggests differential⏎and known disease diagnoses, thereby⏎associations. improving diagnostic⏎accuracy.⏎Clinical Gene-disease Machine learning identifies Expands knowledge of⏎Interpretation association discovery novel or emerging gene–di disease genetics and⏎relationships from large supports research and⏎datasets. discovery.⏎Report Automated report NLP and template-based Standardizes reports,⏎Generation writing systems generate structured reduces turnaround time,⏎clinical reports with and ensures consistent⏎interpretations and formatting.⏎recommendations.⏎Report Clinical decision AI systems provide actionable Guides clinicians on next⏎Generation support (CDS) recommendations based on steps (e.g., confirmatory⏎variant interpretation, patient testing, family screening,⏎context, and clinical guidelines. and therapy options).⏎Post-Analysis Variant reclassification AI tracks published evidence Ensures patients receive⏎monitoring and flags variants for updates on variant⏎reclassification when new data classification, improves⏎emerges. long-term care.⏎Post-Analysis Incidental findings Machine learning identifies Supports the return of⏎detection secondary findings or variants incidental findings and⏎with clinical significance outside improves preventive care⏎the primary indication. opportunities.⏎There are several potential benefits of AI in clinical molecular laboratories⏎• Improved Diagnostic Accuracy: AI enhances variant detection and interpretation, thereby⏎reducing missed diagnoses and false positives.⏎• Faster Turnaround Times: Automation and intelligent prioritization reduce the time from⏎sample to report.⏎• Increased Throughput: Robotic and automated workflows allow laboratories to process⏎more samples efficiently.⏎• Improved Workflows: Automation decreases manual curation and review work.⏎• More Standardized Reporting: AI-generated reports ensure consistency and compliance⏎with professional guidelines.⏎⏎• Knowledge Integration: NLP and machine learning synthesize vast amounts of genetic⏎and clinical literature automatically.⏎• Proactive Variant Monitoring: AI tracks evolving evidence and alerts clinicians to⏎reclassifications or new findings.⏎Table 4: Challenges and Concerns Specific to Molecular Labs⏎Challenge Description Impact⏎Model validation & AI models trained on one population Reduced accuracy in⏎generalization may not perform well on diverse underrepresented populations;⏎genetic backgrounds or rare potential diagnostic disparities.⏎variants.⏎Insufficient training Rare variants and disease VUS remain difficult to classify;⏎data associations have limited training models may overfit to limited data.⏎examples, making model predictions⏎unreliable.⏎Lack of standardized Inconsistent variant nomenclature Models must handle variable inputs;⏎genomic data and data formats hamper AI interoperability between laboratories⏎structures integration. and systems is poor.⏎Explainability & Clinicians need to understand why Pathologists may override or distrust⏎clinical acceptance AI flagged a variant as pathogenic; AI recommendations; liability⏎black-box models reduce trust. concerns arise.⏎Regulatory FDA guidance on AI/ML in clinical Laboratories uncertain about⏎uncertainty genomics is evolving; validation and compliance requirements; slow⏎oversight standards remain unclear. adoption of validated AI tools.⏎Data privacy & Training AI on genomic data raises Legal/ethical risks; patient hesitation;⏎consent privacy concerns; informed consent regulatory scrutiny.⏎requirements are uncertain.⏎Variant interpretation Many variants lack functional data; VUS remain challenging;⏎complexity AI predictions may not capture over-reliance on prediction scores⏎biological complexity. can mislead clinicians.⏎Integration with Genomic AI outputs must integrate Manual data transfer, fragmented⏎EHR/LIS with LIS and EHR systems, but workflows, increased error risk.⏎interoperability is limited.⏎Bias in training Historical classifications and Disparities in diagnostic accuracy,⏎datasets datasets may embed population especially for non-European⏎biases. ancestry groups.⏎Cost of Developing, validating, and Widening gap between⏎implementation maintaining AI systems is resource- well-resourced vs. under-resourced⏎intensive. laboratories; consolidation⏎pressures.⏎Keeping models Rapidly evolving genomic Outdated interpretations; high⏎current knowledge requires continuous ongoing maintenance burden.⏎updates and revalidation.⏎For AI to be safely and effectively integrated into molecular laboratory testing:⏎⏎• Robust validation: Prospective clinical validation on diverse populations and⏎comprehensive variant sets.⏎• Transparent methodology: Clear documentation of model training data, performance⏎metrics, and limitations.⏎• Human-in-the-loop: Pathologists retain final decision-making authority; AI serves as⏎decision support, not replacement.⏎• Continuous monitoring: Ongoing performance tracking, error reporting, and model⏎recalibration.⏎• Ethical oversight: Institutional review, informed consent, and equity assessments for AI⏎deployment.⏎• Explainable AI (XAI): Development of interpretable models that provide transparent⏎reasoning for variant classifications.⏎• Standardized data exchange: Implementation of fit for purpose genomic data standards⏎to enable seamless AI integration across systems.⏎• Outcome tracking: Linking AI-driven diagnoses to patient outcomes to validate and refine⏎model performance.⏎AI holds tremendous promise for molecular laboratory testing, but realizing its benefits requires⏎addressing data standardization, validation rigor, and data interoperability challenges—⏎particularly for rare variants and underrepresented populations.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption of AI in molecular diagnostics is rarely driven by a single individual. It typically requires⏎alignment across clinical, laboratory, financial, regulatory, and IT leadership. However, influence⏎varies by organizational structure (academic medical center vs. community hospital vs.⏎integrated delivery network).⏎Laboratory directors and clinical teams working with medical team senior leadership that have⏎identified ways for AI to help reduce administrative burden and/or improve patient care. Most⏎influential decision makers:⏎7. Laboratory Medical Director (analytic authority)⏎8. Chief Medical Officer (CMO; clinical governance)⏎9. Chief Financial Officer (CFO; financial gatekeeper)⏎10.Chief Information Officer/Chief Data Officer (CIO/CDO; technical feasibility)⏎11.Stewardship or genetics committees (domain endorsement)⏎12.External regulators (structural influence)⏎In most large academic centers, the Laboratory Medical Director + CFO + CIO + CMO⏎alignment is the critical adoption bottleneck. If the laboratory director is convinced of analytic⏎validity and the CFO sees cost justification with CMO endorsement required for standard-of-⏎care change, adoption is likely. Without alignment among these four, deployment rarely⏎⏎progresses beyond limited pilots. Primary administrative hurdles include but are not limited to⏎implementation, data storage, and maintenance costs in both direct costs and personnel full⏎time equivalents (FTE); ongoing personnel and direct costs to support validation and use of AI⏎in a CLIA environment; additional education to understand the complexity of what systems and⏎inputs will be needed to build, launch, and operate AI in a manner that reduces the risk of⏎patient harms and medical errors. Other hurdles include regulatory ambiguity, reimbursement⏎gaps, integration complexity, validation burden, workforce trust, infrastructure cost, and⏎fragmented governance.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎More robust information provided to cancer registries and other publicly available HIPAA-⏎compliant / deidentified databases will fuel ongoing research and biomarker identification using⏎AI pattern recognition; real-world evidence from EHRs will be less prone to data dropouts and⏎potential errors;⏎AI in genomic medicine and infectious disease remains less constrained by algorithmic⏎sophistication and more by data fragmentation, inconsistent standards, and a lack of cross-⏎institutional benchmarking infrastructure.⏎Enhanced interoperability would:⏎• Unlock larger, higher-quality training datasets⏎• Improve generalizability and regulatory readiness⏎• Reduce vendor lock-in⏎• Enable multi-site benchmarking⏎• Expand reimbursement viability⏎• Widen total addressable market for AI vendors⏎• Expand market opportunity⏎• Reduces development cost⏎• Increase model robustness⏎• Improve patient outcomes⏎• Enhance public health response⏎Table 5: Examples of Interoperability Opportunities in Genomic Medicine &⏎Infectious Disease⏎Category Sub-Area Current High-Impact Key Standards AI / Market Impact⏎Limitation / Data Types⏎Gap⏎⏎Genomic Variant-Level Variant data VCF files; HL7 FHIR Genomics; Enables⏎Medicine Genomic Data stored in VCF, BAM/CRAM; GA4GH VRS; cross-institution AI⏎Standardization LIS, PDFs, and CNV data; GA4GH Beacon; training; reduces⏎proprietary structural variant ClinVar standards; validation burden;⏎systems; not calls; variant HGVS. enables federated⏎machine- metadata; learning; real-time⏎readablee; zygosity; read reclassification alerts;⏎inconsistent depth; quality supports regulatory⏎across metrics. benchmarking;⏎institutions. accelerates⏎pathogenicity/structural⏎variant models and⏎rare disease discovery.⏎Genomic Genotype– Phenotype HPO terms; FHIR Clinical Enables genotype–⏎Medicine Phenotype data stuck in SNOMED CT Genomics; HPO; phenotype AI;⏎Integration EHR notes, diagnoses; OMOP; SNOMED improves rare disease⏎ICD-10 codes, longitudinal CT; LOINC. algorithms; enhances⏎lab tables, laboratories; penetrance/risk⏎imaging medications; prediction; supports⏎systems; poor family history; precision diagnostics⏎interoperability. social and CDS; and⏎determinants. expands datasets for⏎vendors.⏎Genomic Structural SVs and RNA-se,; GA4GH Improves diagnostic⏎Medicine Variant & multi-omics methylation Phenopackets; yield for unsolved⏎Multi-Omics (RNA-seq, profile,; standardized SV cases; supports cancer⏎Interoperability methylation, proteomic,; formats; FAIR predisposition⏎proteomics nd metabolomic,;and principles. modeling; enhances⏎, structural variant functional annotation⏎metabolomics) representations. AI.⏎are silo byd;⏎inconsistent⏎data formats.⏎Infectious AMR Genomic and MICs, antibiotic CLSI; EUCAST; Improves⏎Disease Genotype– resistance exposure, WHO GLASS; FHIR genotype-to-phenotype⏎Phenotype phenotype resistance genes, MedicationStatement; prediction; supports⏎Databases data are plasmid standardized AST generalizable⏎disconnected detection, patient ontologies. organism-specific AI;⏎and have outcomes, and enables global⏎limited linkage treatment timing. surveillance; expands⏎to outcomes. ROI and⏎reimbursement⏎potential.⏎Infectious Real-Time Infection data Time-stamped FHIR Public Health, Enables real-time⏎Disease Infection siloed in cultures, HL7 ELR, and outbreak detection,⏎Surveillance microbiology pathogen WGS, PulseNet. predictive transmission⏎Networks laboratories, outbreak clusters, modeling,and early⏎infection patient AMR warning systems.⏎prevention, movement, and⏎and state environmental⏎registries. samples.⏎⏎Abbreviations: VCF — Variant Call Format, LIS — Laboratory Information System, PDF — Portable Document Format, BAM —⏎Binary Alignment/Map, CRAM — Compressed Reference-oriented Alignment Map (format), CNV — Copy Number Variant, HL7 —⏎Health Level Seven International, FHIR — Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources, GA4GH VRS — Global Alliance for⏎Genomics and Health, Variation Representation Specification, HGVS — Human Genome Variation Society, EHR — Electronic⏎Health Record, HPO — Human Phenotype Ontology, ICD-10 — International Classification of Diseases, 10th Revision, OMOP —⏎Observational Medical Outcomes Partnership (Common Data Model), SNOMED CT — Systematized Nomenclature of Medicine —⏎Clinical Terms, LOINC — Logical Observation Identifiers Names and Codes, AMR — Antimicrobial Resistance, CLSI — Clinical and⏎Laboratory Standards Institute, EUCAST — European Committee on Antimicrobial Susceptibility Testing, WHO GLASS — World⏎Health Organization Global Antimicrobial Resistance and Use Surveillance System, FAIR Principles — Findable, Accessible,⏎Interoperable, and Reusable♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0015♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cornell University, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Cornell University♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert A. Harrington, M.D.⏎Stephen and Suzanne Weiss Dean⏎Weill Cornell Medicine⏎Provost for Medical Affairs⏎Cornell University⏎February 23, 2026⏎Via Electronic Submission⏎The Honorable Robert Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎Cornell University and Weill Cornell Medicine appreciate the opportunity to provide comments on the⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Health Sector Artificial Intelligence (AI) Request⏎for Information (RFI) published on December 23, 2025. As a leading academic medical center⏎embedded within a major research university, Weill Cornell Medicine both develops and deploys AI⏎tools across the full continuum of health—from discovery and validation to real-world clinical⏎implementation. Our comments address each of the three main areas of focus identified in the RFI:⏎Regulation, Reimbursement, Research & Development.⏎We have recently launched a Center for Early Detection and Prevention (CEDP), designed to⏎fundamentally shift healthcare from a reactive, late-stage treatment paradigm toward proactive,⏎personalized prevention. The Center leverages multimodality AI to integrate diverse longitudinal⏎data—including advanced imaging, laboratory testing, electronic health record (EHR) data, multi-⏎cancer early detection (MCED) assays, genomic and polygenic risk information, lifestyle assessments,⏎and wearable/remote monitoring data—to generate individualized risk profiles across cancer,⏎cardiovascular, neurodegenerative, and metabolic diseases.⏎Our experience underscores a central conclusion: the greatest unrealized opportunity for AI in⏎health care lies in prevention, early detection, and risk stratification, yet current regulatory,⏎reimbursement, and research frameworks remain largely optimized for episodic treatment of advanced⏎disease. If the nation seeks to save lives, reduce the chronic disease burden, improve access and⏎equity, and control long-term costs, federal policy must evolve to support prevention-focused,⏎multimodality AI.⏎1300 York Avenue, F-113, New York, NY 10065 | T. 212.746.6005 | F. 212.746.8424 | dean@med.cornell.edu⏎⏎I. Regulation⏎A. Regulatory and Programmatic Changes HHS Should Prioritize⏎1. Establish a clear governance framework for non-medical-device clinical AI, particularly for⏎prevention and risk stratification⏎Many of the most impactful AI tools used in clinical care—including documentation support, care⏎navigation, population health analytics, and risk stratification—are not regulated as FDA medical⏎devices. These tools increasingly inform screening intensity, diagnostic sequencing, and preventive⏎interventions, yet organizations face uncertainty regarding acceptable evidence, monitoring⏎expectations, and accountability.⏎HHS should establish a risk-tiered assurance framework for non-device AI used in clinical care,⏎explicitly including prevention and early detection. Such a framework should define proportional⏎expectations for:⏎● transparency regarding intended use and limitations,⏎● validation appropriate to downstream clinical action,⏎● post-deployment monitoring and drift management,⏎● clinical governance and accountability,⏎● patient-facing communication when AI influences screening or preventive decisions.⏎This framework should align with ONC certification expectations for decision support interventions⏎under 45 CFR Part 170, including transparency, reliability, robustness, fairness, safety, security, and⏎privacy considerations.⏎2. Enable data liquidity for multimodality AI while protecting patient privacy⏎Prevention-focused AI requires longitudinal access to heterogeneous data sources that are often siloed⏎across vendors and domains. While HIPAA and information-blocking regulations permit such access⏎in principle, operational barriers remain substantial.⏎HHS should:⏎● Strengthen enforcement and implementation guidance under 45 CFR Part 171 (Information⏎Blocking) to ensure timely, standards-based access to electronic health information—including⏎imaging, laboratory, genomic, and patient-generated data—necessary for AI development,⏎deployment, and monitoring.⏎● Clarify that AI-driven risk stratification and early detection fall squarely within permissible⏎treatment and health care operations under HIPAA (45 CFR §§ 164.502, 164.506).⏎2⏎⏎● Issue updated guidance supporting privacy-preserving analytics, de-identification, and expert⏎determination pathways (45 CFR § 164.514), particularly for multicenter and longitudinal⏎prevention research.⏎● Streamline vendor contracting by specifying that a business associate may not deidentify and⏎use data to train its own proprietary AI as “proper management and administration” under a⏎business associate agreement (BAA) unless the covered entity explicitly permits it (45 CFR §⏎164.504(e)(2))⏎● Establish baseline privacy protective guidance for any entities that process US sensitive health⏎data, whether they are a HIPAA covered entity, business associate, or otherwise.⏎This will lower enforcement and contracting costs for covered entities and enhance patient trust⏎if digital health technology providers by default must use data in a manner consistent with the⏎purposes for which it was collected and subject to minimum necessary standards. (45 CFR §⏎164.514(d)).⏎3. Modernize regulatory posture to support prevention as a core clinical use case⏎Existing regulatory frameworks implicitly prioritize treatment decisions over prevention. HHS should⏎explicitly recognize screening optimization, early detection, and personalized prevention as high-⏎value clinical use cases for AI and ensure that regulatory expectations do not unintentionally⏎discourage innovation in these areas.⏎B. Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical-Device AI⏎The use of AI for longitudinal risk prediction and prevention raises novel governance challenges:⏎● Accountability over time: Risk models evolve as new data streams are incorporated, raising⏎questions about responsibility for recalibration, communication of changing risk, and⏎oversight.⏎● Action thresholds: Determining when probabilistic risk estimates trigger screening,⏎preventive therapy, or referral requires clinical and policy alignment.⏎● Equity: Prevention-focused AI must avoid systematically disadvantaging populations with less⏎complete data (e.g., limited access to wearables or genomic testing).⏎● Contracting and indemnification: Health systems face challenges negotiating audit rights,⏎update policies, and liability sharing for adaptive models.⏎HHS should play a convening role to:⏎● define best practices for governance of longitudinal, prevention-focused AI,⏎● develop model contractual provisions addressing auditability, updates, and incident response,⏎● promote minimum expectations for equity monitoring and transparency.⏎3⏎⏎C. Evaluation Methods and Metrics for Non-Device AI⏎Pre-deployment evaluation should include:⏎● external validation across diverse populations,⏎● calibration of absolute risk estimates,⏎● simulation of downstream screening and prevention workflows,⏎● human-centered usability testing.⏎Post-deployment monitoring should assess:⏎● performance drift and subgroup equity,⏎● workflow impact (e.g., access, timeliness, burden),⏎● safety signals and override rates,⏎● clinical outcomes such as stage shift and avoidance of advanced disease,⏎● long-term economic impact.⏎HHS support would be most impactful through cooperative agreements, contracts, and multicenter⏎evaluation networks that provide shared infrastructure for real-world evidence generation in⏎prevention-focused AI.⏎D. Supporting Private-Sector Accreditation and Certification⏎HHS can catalyze innovation by supporting:⏎● voluntary clinical AI assurance labels documenting intended use, limitations, subgroup⏎performance, update cadence, and monitoring commitments;⏎● independent testing sandboxes and neutral evaluation laboratories;⏎● alignment between private accreditation efforts and ONC-aligned transparency and risk⏎management expectations under 45 CFR Part 170.⏎II. Reimbursement⏎A. Structural Misalignment in Current Payment Systems⏎One of the most significant barriers to AI-enabled prevention is a profound misalignment in⏎reimbursement. Screening, early detection, and prevention are reimbursed at levels far below⏎those for treating advanced disease, including surgery, hospitalization, and high-cost therapeutics.⏎4⏎⏎As a result:⏎• Health systems bear the cost of prevention infrastructure and AI deployment.⏎• Financial returns accrue downstream to payers and society.⏎• Incentives favor late-stage intervention over early action.⏎This misalignment undermines national goals to reduce mortality, chronic disease burden, and⏎healthcare costs.⏎B. Payment Policy Changes HHS Should Prioritize⏎HHS should:⏎• Develop reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled risk stratification, personalized screening,⏎and longitudinal prevention management.⏎• Use CMMI demonstration models to test payment approaches that reward prevention and⏎share savings when AI reduces downstream utilization.⏎• Ensure that payment policy recognizes value over time, including avoided hospitalizations,⏎reduced disease severity, and improved access.⏎• Tie payment to outcomes to avoid scaling ineffective or low value technologies by deploying a⏎coverage with evidence development approach1⏎• Create tailored – not one size fits all – payment mechanisms for AI-enabled clinical services⏎that incentivize quality improvement. When an AI-enabled clinical service complements an⏎existing billable diagnostic or image service, bundled payment design should be used,⏎potentially with add-on payments for use of AI in scenarios where the AI augmentation⏎improves quality of care and/or patient outcomes. When the AI-enabled clinical service does⏎not have a complementary diagnostic or image service, a separate payment mechanism should⏎be created only when there is evidence of quality improvement. This framework is delineated⏎by Zink and colleagues2⏎• When clinical efficacy of an AI device or algorithm is established, CMS should consider⏎assigning a temporary procedure code with provisional status that authorizes payment for a⏎limited period. This strategy could help address the current catch-22 where payers and the⏎AMA panel that approves CPT codes require evidence of meaningful utilization before⏎approving a new code with payment, but providers are unlikely to adopt new technologies in⏎the absence of payment. See recommendation from Razavian and colleagues3⏎• Categorize AI services by their functions (e.g., augment vs replace existing services, diagnostic⏎vs predictive) and establish umbrella payment policies across functional groups. When⏎1 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35981123/⏎2 https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38805195/⏎3 https://ai.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/AIpc2500871/⏎5⏎⏎multiple AI products have the same function, reference pricing should be used to set the⏎payment rate based on the cost of the most efficient product. See recommendation from⏎Razavian and colleagues 4⏎C. Decision-Makers and Administrative Hurdles⏎Adoption decisions are shaped by clinical leadership, population health leaders, CMIO/CIOs, quality⏎and compliance teams, and enterprise AI governance bodies.⏎Primary hurdles include:⏎• lack of reimbursement for prevention-focused AI,⏎• integration of diverse data sources (imaging, genomics, lab tests, wearables),⏎• costs of ongoing monitoring and governance,⏎• difficulty demonstrating ROI within short budget cycles.⏎D. Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎Patients and caregivers seek:⏎• earlier identification of disease risk,⏎• personalized screening rather than one-size-fits-all approaches,⏎• reduced fear and morbidity associated with late diagnoses,⏎• equitable access to preventive care and disease management, through AI-enhanced virtual and⏎hybrid care.⏎Concerns include privacy, data use, fairness, transparency, accessibility, and accountability. HHS can⏎address these through patient-facing transparency standards and enforceable equity monitoring⏎expectations.⏎III. Research & Development⏎A. Barriers to Innovation and Adoption for Private Sectors⏎Key barriers include fragmented data ecosystems, limited shared infrastructure for longitudinal⏎prevention research, insufficient funding for implementation science, and misaligned economic⏎incentives. Also rapid pace of change presents challenges for decisions about investment in and⏎scaling of AI for clinical care. Today’s technology will be out of date quickly.⏎4 Ibid⏎6⏎⏎B. Where AI Has Succeeded and Fallen Short⏎AI has succeeded when tightly integrated into workflows and when benefits are tangible and near-⏎term. It has fallen short when models are siloed, poorly monitored, or when prevention benefits accrue⏎outside existing payment structures.⏎There are several areas where clinical AI has met or exceeded expectations. For example, AI used as a⏎second reader/triage in breast cancer screening has shown meaningful gains in detection and/or⏎workload reduction at population scale, with emerging evidence on favorable outcomes and cost-⏎effectiveness in Swedish programs and trials. Diabetic retinopathy (DR) autonomous screening⏎systems have demonstrated strong diagnostic performance in real-world settings and can close⏎screening gaps when implemented well. In multi-site evaluations, ambient scribes are associated with⏎reduced documentation burden and improved clinician experience—often meeting the “quality of life”⏎expectation even when hard ROI is still evolving.⏎At the same time, there are examples of areas where AI has fallen short. Sepsis prediction is a classic⏎example. Some systems have shown limited external validity and/or high false-alert burdens, and real-⏎world impact can be far less than vendor claims. Diabetic retinopathy autonomous screening illustrates⏎this. Claims-based data suggest low real-world uptake, with reimbursement levels and implementation⏎costs acting as practical bottlenecks. For ambient scribes, early evidence shows promise for⏎burden/burnout, but system-level financial or throughput gains can be mixed—and there are open⏎questions about downstream spending effects (e.g., coding intensity).⏎C. Interoperability as a Catalyst⏎Enhanced interoperability—particularly for imaging, genomics, laboratory data, wearables, and⏎patient-reported outcomes—would dramatically accelerate development of multimodality AI. . The⏎recent ASTP HTI-5 proposed rule5 on promoting FHIR-based data exchange and AI-enabled⏎interoperability solutions that use FHIR alongside newer standards like the Model Context Protocol⏎(MCP), may go a long way in improving interoperability for these diverse data. Standardized⏎representations for AI-derived risk scores, audit trails, and outcomes are essential.⏎D. Priority Research Areas for HHS⏎HHS should prioritize:⏎• multimodality risk prediction across diseases,⏎• AI-enabled screening optimization and early detection,⏎• equity-aware prevention and management models,⏎• longitudinal economic analyses comparing prevention versus late-stage treatment,⏎5 https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/29/2025-23896/health-data-technology-and-interoperability-astponc-⏎deregulatory-actions-to-unleash-prosperity⏎7⏎⏎• scalable governance models for lifelong risk assessment.⏎IV. Benefits and Challenges of Use of AI in the Clinical Space⏎Part of the promise of AI, making it unique among health technologies, is that the speed and cost of⏎device/algorithm development is relatively low (e.g., as opposed to drug development) once⏎appropriate infrastructure and access to data is achieved. HHS can facilitate the creation of the⏎infrastructure necessary to both develop and subsequently deploy by setting regulations/standards for⏎interoperability and the use of data in a secure and ethical manner.⏎A requirement for a standard-based interoperability for AI devices must apply to electronic health⏎records (EHR), AI algorithms/platforms, and medical devices. AI can process and incorporate large⏎volumes of data and detect signals and patterns that humans cannot. These patterns may be useful in⏎early detection prediction, and ultimately prevention of disease across populations that extend beyond⏎individual or even combinations of health care systems. However, when standards are lacking, the⏎effort to acquire, normalize, and combine data sets has been and will continue to be a barrier requiring⏎significant cost and expertise to overcome. Data and interface standards are necessary to allow AI to⏎achieve its true potential to improve the health of patients on a population scale.⏎Another barrier is the lack of guidance on the use of individual patient data. AI requires individual⏎patient data for development. In many cases, there may not be direct benefit to the individual whose⏎data is utilized, rather for future patients or populations. The lack of guidance and understanding on⏎the appropriate use of patient data has and will continue to result in reluctance to allow data to be used.⏎This has resulted in a slowing or stoppage in algorithm development and improvement. By providing⏎clear guidance on how appropriately deidentified and protected patient data may be incorporated into⏎AI models, presumably without individual patient consent, HHS would facilitate the development of⏎large data sets necessary to develop population-based algorithms.⏎HHS and other entities seeking to maximize adoption of AI in the clinical setting will encounter many⏎other challenges. As relates to data access, quality and fragmentation, health data is currently siloed⏎across institutions, vendors and payers. EHR data are often incomplete, biased and shaped by billing⏎and workflow rather than clinical truth. And many AI models are trained on narrow populations,⏎raising concerns about generalizability. And the competitive landscape in the commercial EHR space⏎may limit innovation by smaller firms.⏎When examining clinical validation and evidence gaps, AI tools often demonstrate retrospective⏎performance but lack prospective, workflow-embedded validation. And there is no universal standard⏎for clinical AI across tasks. It is difficult to demonstrate that AI, rather than clinicians or other⏎systems, are the cause of improved outcomes.⏎AI can disrupt workflow, rather than enhance it, because AI tools often add cognitive load rather than⏎reduce it. Alerts, predictions and explanations are often misaligned with how clinicians think and act.⏎Clinical settings require near-perfect uptime and predictable behavior, which make them vulnerable to⏎any latency or reliability constraints.⏎8⏎⏎Providers will also encounter gaps as relates to trust, accountability and liability. Clinicians are⏎hesitant to rely on systems they cannot interrogate. An over-reliance on AI could result in errors in the⏎clinical space that are worse than would otherwise be the case. As relates to errors, healthcare⏎providers will worry that AI will increase malpractice risk without legal protection.⏎Any use of AI tools in the clinical space will require constant calibration and adjustment. Clinical⏎practice, coding and populations change over time. And models often need site-specific retraining or⏎recalibration. Significant resources will need to be devoted to monitoring, updating and governing AI⏎systems.⏎There should be a mechanism for gathering and integrating the patient perspective in the creation and⏎use of AI models in the clinical space. There is ample evidence that shows patient-reported outcomes⏎(PROs) can inform clinical practice in a manner that reduces patient mortality. These observations are⏎particularly valuable in use of AI in disease screening, early detection and prevention.⏎There are certain features that AI tools could or should have that could substantially improve⏎outcomes, reveal quality gaps, and reduce costs:⏎• Tools that turn recommendations into reliably executed care: next-best-action suggestions tied⏎to order sets, contraindications, care pathways, and local policies—designed to reduce missed⏎steps and variation rather than add alerts.⏎• Multimodal longitudinal risk + monitoring (the “early signal” stack) AI tools that combine⏎EHR, notes, imaging, labs and wearable/home data to detect deterioration earlier (e.g., heart⏎failure decompensation, post-op complications) with calibrated uncertainty and actionable⏎thresholds—plus monitoring for drift and subgroup performance.⏎• AI tools that continuously find missed follow-ups, delays in diagnosis, gaps in screening,⏎unsafe medication combinations, inequities in access or outcomes by subgroup, or⏎documentation/ordering patterns signaling process failures, with outputs routed to quality⏎teams (not only frontline clinicians).⏎AI tools have great potential for cost savings across both the entire healthcare delivery system and⏎within specific systems. Tools can be utilized that directly optimize staffing, bed management,⏎scheduling, imaging queues, discharge timing, and supply utilization under real constraints. These can⏎generate hard savings because they target throughput and waste. AI can make it easier to run rapid,⏎reproducible “learning health system” studies by utilization for cohort construction, phenotype⏎definitions, confounding-aware analyses, and pragmatic trial operations. This also means that adoption⏎decisions can be driven by local evidence, rather than marketing.⏎9⏎⏎Benchmarking tools can and should be utilized, such as open federated evaluation platforms like⏎MedPerf6 for privacy-preserving, multi-site evaluation. This is critical for generalization, bias, and⏎drift testing. Such tools enable scalable products in risk stratification, care gaps, quality measurement,⏎and operations because vendors can deploy the same pipelines across many health systems.⏎We should have cross-network exchange for longitudinal outcomes and follow-up for care continuity⏎and research. AI value often depends on outcomes observed outside one institution such as⏎readmissions data, follow-up care, mortality and utilization at other institutions or in other systems.⏎Network exchange makes outcome measurement and model monitoring more complete. The Trusted⏎Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA) is an example of a framework that enables⏎nationwide exchange across health information networks under common “rules of the road.” TEFCA⏎work is explicitly evolving toward enhanced exchange approaches, including FHIR-based exchange⏎pilots.⏎Establishment of standards as relates to devices, wearables, and remote monitoring signals is⏎particularly important for “closed-loop” clinical AI. The biggest outcome gains often come from⏎continuous monitoring and intervention workflows. Interoperability is the difference between pilots⏎and scalable programs. There are other areas where establishment of such standards could show⏎significant improvement in health outcomes. Examples are device vitals, waveforms, activity/sleep⏎proxies, home measurements and infusion/ventilator parameters. The result is scalable “hospital-at-⏎home,” chronic care, and high-risk monitoring programs with measurable ROI.⏎V. Conclusion⏎If the United States is serious about saving lives, reducing the chronic disease burden, improving⏎access, and bending the healthcare cost curve, AI policy must move beyond treatment optimization⏎and explicitly support prevention, early detection, and personalized risk stratification.⏎Multimodality AI—supported by aligned regulation, reimbursement, and research investment—offers⏎one of the most promising paths to achieving these goals.⏎Sincerely,⏎Robert A. Harrington, M.D.⏎6 https://www.nature.com/articles/s42256-023-00652-2⏎10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0016♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from In, Brandon, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brandon♔In♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment Submitted by Brandon In⏎February 22, 2026⏎Brandon In respectfully submits the attached comment in response to the HHS Request⏎for Information, Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care (FR Doc. 2025-23641).⏎I. Introduction⏎I have been developing software in healthcare for over a decade from:⏎1. Building robots for hospitals at InTouch Health (acquired by Teladoc Health),⏎2. Reducing physician burnout at One Medical,⏎3. Improving access to care at Optimize Health,⏎4. Pioneering permanent solutions for depression at Osmind, and⏎5. Reducing the cost of healthcare for all Americans by combating fraud, waste, and abuse at⏎my own startup Claimformant.⏎While I support the goal of enhancing clinical productivity, adoption is currently constrained less⏎by technical capability than by a mismatch between data infrastructure and clinical workflows⏎controlled by a few dominant players.⏎Without a structural shift in how the government de-risks new technology and funds shared data⏎infrastructure, clinical AI will remain a tool of legacy vendors rather than a transformative force⏎for all American patients.⏎II. Executive Summary⏎The current healthcare technology ecosystem suffers from systemic failures that prevent true⏎innovation from flourishing. Digitization through the HITECH Act of 2009 has inadvertently⏎fostered vendor monopolies and high-cost regulatory hurdles, such as the prohibitive cost of⏎HIPAA compliance, which stifle the entry of innovative startups.⏎To restore competition and improve health outcomes, HHS must move beyond strategy to⏎infrastructure. We propose the creation of a government-backed, "YC-style" AI accelerator that⏎provides a federal "stamp of approval," backed by a national insurance fund and legal safe⏎harbors to de-risk startup adoption for large healthcare organizations.⏎Furthermore, we urge HHS to fund healthcare data exchanges as public utilities, mandate⏎deterministic governance to ensure auditability, and fix the economic "efficiency penalty" that⏎currently punishes providers for using AI to work faster.⏎⏎III. The Innovation Crisis: Monopolies and Compliance Costs⏎The transition to digital records has failed to create a competitive market, leading instead to⏎vendor opacity and lock-in where large legacy systems control fragmented data silos.⏎• The Compliance Barrier: The massive financial burden of HIPAA compliance and⏎complex regulatory requirements serves as a primary barrier to private-sector innovation.⏎• The Chilling Effect: Small innovators fear investing in tools that may later be⏎categorized as regulated medical devices, while institutional legal teams delay adoption⏎for 12 months or more to avoid perceived liability.⏎IV. Proposal: A Government-Backed AI Accelerator and Safe⏎Harbors⏎To create a level playing field, the government should establish a “Y Combinator-style"⏎accelerator for healthcare AI startups. This program would act as a trust-bridge through:⏎• Voluntary Certification: An ONC-administered program to provide a "stamp of⏎approval" validating safety, interoperability, and bias mitigation.⏎• National AI Insurance Fund: A government-backed fund, similar to the National⏎Vaccine Injury Compensation Program, to compensate for rare cases of AI error,⏎removing the massive liability insurance requirements that block small vendors.⏎• Legal Safe Harbors: Clear guidance providing legal safe harbors for providers who⏎implement tools from these government-vetted startups.⏎V. Economic Alignment: Fixing the "Efficiency Penalty"⏎Adoption is throttled by the "Efficiency Penalty" in labor-based payment models (RVUs), which⏎financially penalizes providers if an AI tool allows them to complete a task faster.⏎• Tiered Reimbursement: HHS should adopt a Tiered Complexity Model: Tier 1 for⏎administrative software, Tier 2 for assistive pattern recognition, and Tier 3 for high-⏎complexity predictive modeling.⏎• AI-Guided Interpretation Codes: CMS should introduce billing codes for "AI-Guided⏎Clinical Interpretation" to reimburse the cognitive labor of a clinician verifying AI⏎insights.⏎VI. Governance & Engineering: Determinism and Auditability⏎To bridge the trust gap, oversight must shift from statistical confidence to deterministic⏎safeguards and audit-by-design.⏎• Deterministic Governance: High-risk clinical tools should be verifiable utilizing⏎evaluation harnesses that allow changes in models, agent harnesses, and code changes to⏎produce similar results.⏎⏎• Agent Trajectory Audit Trails: Systems should generate agent trajectories for every⏎decision, allowing third-party auditors to reconstruct and "replay" the process bit-for-bit⏎after an adverse event with.⏎VII. Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎Biggest Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Adoption The primary barriers are systemic:⏎unclear governance expectations, prolonged review cycles (12+ months), and fragmented data⏎silos that rely on static, episodic inputs. Financially, the "efficiency penalty" in current RVU⏎models penalizes providers for working faster, while high HIPAA compliance costs and liability⏎uncertainty create a "chilling effect" for startups.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎CMS should introduce billing codes for "AI-Guided Clinical Interpretation" and amend 42 CFR⏎§ 410.78 to permit concurrent billing for remote monitoring when distinct data streams are used.⏎Programmatically, a "YC-style" accelerator and a national AI insurance fund are essential to de-⏎risk the market.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research in high-potential tools including precision medicine platforms⏎specifically with Neurotech. Generating more data from brain scans including fMRI, CT, EEG,⏎EKG, and others allow us to guide precision therapy, diagnosis, and clinical guidance expanding⏎access to care across all Americans especially understand rural areas and people of color.⏎VIII. Conclusion⏎By pulling the levers of regulation, reimbursement, and infrastructure to protect small innovators⏎and enforce deterministic safety, HHS can break the current cycle of market concentration. We⏎urge HHS to prioritize a government-backed accelerator and utility-style data funding to turn AI⏎from a liability concern into a verified asset for all Americans.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Brandon In♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0017♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Homecare Homebase, LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Homecare Homebase, LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (90 FR 60108)⏎Last updated February 2026⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎February 23, 2026⏎Electronic Submission via Regulations.gov⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (90 FR 60108)⏎Dear Sir or Madam:⏎Homecare Homebase (HCHB) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Department’s⏎Request for Information regarding actions HHS can take to accelerate the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎HCHB provides an enterprise electronic health record (EHR) and care coordination platform⏎serving home-based care providers, including home health and hospice agencies, that⏎collectively care for approximately one million patients daily and represent approximately 47.1⏎percent of Medicare home health and 43.5 percent of Medicare hospice volume. Our customers⏎operate at the intersection of hospital discharge, chronic disease management, serious illness⏎care, and end-of-life support in the home. The populations they serve are clinically complex, and⏎their risk drivers frequently extend beyond diagnosis codes to include functional limitations,⏎caregiver availability, home environment constraints, longitudinal hospitalization patterns, and⏎medication complexity.AI adoption in clinical care will depend less on algorithmic capability and⏎more on regulatory clarity, operational predictability, interoperable data access, and governance⏎frameworks that reflect the realities of care delivery.⏎Executive Summary⏎HCHB supports responsible AI adoption that strengthens clinical oversight, reduces⏎administrative burden, and improves care coordination, particularly for medically complex⏎patients receiving services in the home. Accelerating AI deployment will require regulatory⏎clarity, proportionate governance frameworks, interoperable access to clinically meaningful⏎data, and predictable program integrity expectations.⏎1⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎This letter outlines recommendations in five areas:⏎(1) providing regulatory clarity so organizations can invest in AI with confidence and maintain⏎workforce stability;⏎(2) explicitly supporting AI tools that assist clinicians with documentation while preserving⏎clinician review and accountability;⏎(3) applying stronger oversight to higher-risk AI uses and lighter guardrails to lower-risk,⏎workflow-support tools;⏎(4) using AI to identify billing anomalies while preserving human review and appeal rights in⏎coverage and payment decisions; and⏎(5) improving data exchange and research alignment so AI can better identify risk and support⏎earlier, preventive intervention.⏎HCHB also emphasizes the importance of ensuring that home-based care providers are fully⏎represented in AI policy development, interoperability initiatives, and governance design. With⏎clear guardrails and balanced oversight, AI can enhance quality, access, and cost containment⏎across the care continuum.⏎Regulatory Predictability, Capital Allocation, and⏎Workforce Stability⏎(Response to Questions 1 and 7)⏎Technology procurement in healthcare is inherently risk-adjusted. Boards and compliance⏎leaders evaluate AI tools not only for potential clinical or operational benefits, but also for⏎exposure to audits, recoupment risk, malpractice liability, and False Claims Act implications.⏎When federal expectations regarding AI governance are unclear, organizations defer⏎investment.⏎Regulatory ambiguity increases procurement friction, lengthens sales cycles, and raises the⏎effective cost of capital for both providers and developers. It also slows innovation. Uncertainty⏎discourages investment in low-risk, workflow-supportive AI that could meaningfully reduce⏎administrative burden and improve care coordination. In the absence of clear federal guardrails,⏎organizations may avoid deploying tools that are clinically helpful but perceived as legally⏎uncertain.⏎For home-based care providers, AI-enabled systems increasingly function as core infrastructure⏎for managing clinical risk, financial accountability, and preventable utilization. These providers⏎operate within tight cash-flow cycles, paying clinicians weekly while reimbursement may lag.⏎Unpredictable or opaque AI-driven expectations, whether related to documentation review,⏎audit analytics, or reimbursement interpretation, can destabilize staffing and limit access in rural⏎and underserved markets. Predictability, therefore, is not simply an operational preference; it is a⏎workforce and access imperative.⏎2⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎In addition, payment methodologies for home-based care incorporate statutory productivity⏎adjustments intended to reflect system-wide efficiency gains. While AI may support workflow⏎efficiencies, productivity assumptions derived from acute-care environments may not translate⏎directly to home-based settings, where travel time, interdisciplinary coordination, caregiver⏎engagement, and functional assessment drive workload in ways not reducible through⏎automation alone. Policymaking that assumes uniform productivity gains across care settings⏎risks overstating achievable efficiencies in decentralized, patient-home environments.⏎Governance and payment frameworks should reflect the operational realities of home-based⏎care to ensure stability and continued access.⏎AI-Assisted Documentation and OASIS Clarification⏎(Response to Question 2)⏎Documentation burden in home-based care directly affects clinician retention, visit capacity, and⏎patient access. AI-assisted documentation presents a meaningful opportunity to reduce burden⏎while strengthening internal consistency and audit defensibility.⏎In the home health context, CMS guidance provides that “[a]n agency’s software may not⏎‘answer’ or ‘generate’ the OASIS response for the assessing clinician.” This requirement⏎appropriately reinforces that OASIS responses must reflect the clinician’s assessment of the⏎patient’s current status. At the same time, clarification is warranted as AI-enabled⏎documentation capabilities evolve.⏎There is a meaningful distinction between software that independently determines and enters an⏎OASIS response and systems that generate draft language or structured suggestions based on⏎contemporaneous clinical inputs for clinician review and attestation. In appropriately governed⏎AI-assisted workflows, the clinician remains responsible for evaluating suggested content,⏎making any necessary modifications, and formally confirming the final response. Audit trails can⏎document that process clearly.⏎From a practical standpoint, the public interest is not served by requiring that clinicians manually⏎type or dictate every element of documentation. Patients and regulators appropriately expect⏎that clinicians review, validate, and attest to the accuracy of clinical notes and care plans.⏎Governance language that implies manual data entry as the benchmark for compliance risks⏎impeding safe AI adoption. The critical safeguard is clinician accountability and verification, not⏎the mechanical method of input.⏎Clarifying that AI-assisted documentation support, subject to active clinician review, is⏎distinguishable from automated completion would preserve assessment integrity while enabling⏎responsible innovation.⏎3⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎Program Integrity, Administrative Sophistication, and⏎Contestability⏎(Response to Question 6)⏎AI can play a constructive role in identifying billing anomalies and unusual utilization patterns. AI-⏎enabled anomaly detection, when applied to objective billing and utilization patterns with⏎appropriate human review, can strengthen program integrity and reduce improper payments.⏎However, there is an important distinction between anomaly detection based on claims patterns⏎and retrospective second-guessing of clinical documentation through automated review. As AI⏎capabilities expand, their use in program integrity efforts warrants careful calibration. HCHB⏎does not oppose the use of advanced analytics to identify bad actors. However, documentation⏎authorship or drafting method alone should not be treated as a risk signal.⏎High-quality clinical documentation, whether manually authored or AI-assisted, can be well⏎structured, internally consistent, and detailed. Conversely, fraudulent actors can mimic the⏎appearance of thorough documentation. It is therefore unlikely that effective fraud detection⏎will hinge on having AI systems “read charts” for stylistic similarity or drafting patterns.⏎Documentation style, linguistic consistency, or drafting efficiency should be neutral inputs, not⏎proxies for compliance risk.⏎Home-based care providers frequently serve medically and socially complex patients whose⏎longitudinal needs naturally result in detailed and structured documentation over time. AI⏎systems that rely on linguistic similarity, structural consistency, or documentation density as⏎indicators of aberrancy risk generating false positives and disproportionately burdening⏎compliant providers serving high-acuity or end-of-life populations.⏎Program integrity analytics are most effective when focused on substantive discrepancies⏎between documented clinical presentation, service intensity, and billing patterns, rather than on⏎the technological tools used to prepare documentation.⏎Where AI is used to triage audits or flag aberrant patterns, meaningful human oversight and clear⏎pathways to contest or appeal AI-generated findings must be preserved. Contestability⏎safeguards are essential to maintaining trust, protecting beneficiaries, and preventing erroneous⏎adverse actions against compliant providers.⏎Risk-Differentiated Governance, Explainability, and⏎Guardrails⏎(Response to Questions 3, 4, and 5)⏎AI architectures may leverage foundation models, domain-specific models, or hybrid⏎approaches; however, federal policy should focus on governance, explainability, and workflow⏎4⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎integration rather than the specific underlying model design. Not all AI systems present⏎equivalent clinical or compliance risk. Workflow-support tools operating under clinician oversight⏎differ materially from autonomous clinical decision systems or AI implementations that influence⏎payment, compliance, or quality measurement.⏎Governance expectations should be proportionate to use-case risk and address validation, bias⏎assessment, transparency, monitoring, and model drift management.⏎For lower-risk tools such as clinical summarization or documentation support, practical guardrails⏎can meaningfully reduce risk without imposing excessive burden. These may include⏎requirements that AI-generated summaries preserve consistency with source data, suppress⏎unsupported inferences, and avoid introducing or “inventing” protected health information not⏎grounded in the clinical record. Such guardrails protect factual integrity while preserving⏎flexibility for innovation.⏎HHS may consider working collaboratively with industry stakeholders to develop guardrail⏎frameworks for common low-risk use cases. Shared guardrail principles would promote⏎consistency and trust while allowing technology to evolve responsibly.⏎Where AI influences documentation expectations, compliance review, quality reporting, or⏎payment-related workflows, explainability is essential. Providers should have access to high-level⏎explanations of the factors influencing outputs, documentation of how variables were selected,⏎and sufficient transparency to understand how clinical documentation aligns with system⏎behavior. In this context, explainability supports balanced provider participation and consistent⏎documentation standards, not coding optimization.⏎For higher-impact AI implementations affecting payment, compliance, or standardized⏎reporting, phased implementation or multi-year parallel testing prior to operational⏎consequences would allow evaluation of divergence, unintended impacts, and operational⏎disruption before full integration.⏎Governance frameworks should remain proportionate so as not to disadvantage smaller or rural⏎providers without corresponding safety gains.⏎Interoperability and Data Fragmentation⏎(Response to Question 8)⏎If AI is expected to contribute meaningfully to cost containment, it must have timely access to⏎the costliest and most clinically consequential datapoints in the system: hospital admissions,⏎discharges, emergency department visits, and other material changes in patient status. Home-⏎based care providers cannot prevent avoidable utilization if they do not know, in near real time,⏎that a hospitalization has occurred or that a clinically significant transition is underway. Without⏎5⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎interoperable Admission/Discharge/Transfer (ADT) visibility, AI-enabled prevention becomes⏎reactive rather than anticipatory.⏎In many markets, critical transition data remains confined within referral portals or proprietary⏎care coordination platforms. When key patient indicators require manual portal access rather⏎than operating as interoperable, event-level signals across the continuum of care, AI cannot⏎function as preventive infrastructure. Broader operationalization of Trusted Exchange⏎Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA) and other standardized exchange frameworks⏎should prioritize real-time ADT exchange and shared visibility into clinically material events⏎across care settings.⏎Yet even if exchange infrastructure improves, data fragmentation across care settings remains a⏎structural barrier to true visibility. Most AI models are trained primarily on acute-care datasets⏎and do not generalize well to post-acute or home-based populations. In these settings, risk is⏎driven less by diagnosis codes and more by functional status, caregiver availability, medication⏎complexity, home environment constraints, and longitudinal instability. When these factors are⏎underrepresented in both training data and exchange frameworks, predictive accuracy declines,⏎particularly for the populations most dependent on proactive intervention.⏎Data fragmentation is compounded by two forces: essential patient information is often⏎inaccessible due to interoperability barriers, and the data that is available frequently lacks the⏎structured, comprehensive elements required to train reliable post-acute care AI models.⏎Patient Protection and High-Impact AI Use⏎(Response to Question 9)⏎The policy implications of AI in clinical care depend on whether its use affects workflow or alters⏎care decisions.⏎AI applications that support documentation accuracy, risk identification, or care coordination⏎operate within clinician workflow and generally do not independently change the trajectory of⏎care nor the patient’s perception of care quality. By contrast, AI systems that influence service⏎authorization, discharge timing, visit frequency, prioritization, or resource allocation have the⏎potential to result in care disruption.⏎Similarly, when designed with real-time data retrieval, clinician oversight, and transparent⏎rationale generation, AI-enabled prior authorization tools can reduce delays and administrative⏎burden while preserving individualized medical necessity determinations.⏎In home-based settings where patients are medically fragile and reliant on coordinated in-home⏎services, even modest disruptions in service intensity or timing can precipitate avoidable⏎hospitalization or institutionalization. Governance frameworks should therefore distinguish⏎clearly between workflow-support AI and AI that shapes high-impact care determinations.⏎6⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎Where AI influences decisions that affect access to covered care, accountability must remain⏎clearly assigned to licensed professionals or accountable entities, and affected determinations⏎should be subject to meaningful review. AI policy should reinforce the principle that technology⏎strengthens coordination and clinical stability rather than introducing new sources of⏎fragmentation or unintended service disruption.⏎Research Priorities, Preventive Supports, and MLR⏎Alignment⏎(Response to Question 10)⏎HHS can further accelerate responsible AI adoption by supporting applied research that⏎connects AI-enabled identification of preventive and wellness needs to coverage policy and⏎Medical Loss Ratio (MLR) treatment.⏎Home-based care providers routinely identify upstream drivers of avoidable utilization, including⏎nutritional risk and caregiver instability. Enteral nutrition delivered via PEG tube is generally⏎covered, while medically fragile individuals who require oral supplemental nutrition to prevent⏎deterioration may lack comparable coverage pathways. Similarly, caregiver strain is a⏎documented predictor of hospitalization and institutionalization, yet structured caregiver⏎support may not clearly qualify as medical expense under current MLR frameworks.⏎AI-enabled tools can generate actuarially relevant evidence linking preventive supports to⏎measurable reductions in acute care spending. Supporting research in this area would allow⏎policymakers to evaluate whether clinically indicated preventive supports, when algorithmically⏎validated and outcome-linked, warrant clearer recognition within medical expense definitions⏎and coverage structures. Aligning preventive intervention coverage with MLR treatment would⏎reduce structural disincentives and encourage investment in upstream care that supports aging⏎in place and long-term cost containment.⏎Ensuring Home-Based Care Is Not Left Behind⏎Home health and hospice providers have historically been underrepresented in federal health⏎information technology policy. Neither setting was eligible for Meaningful Use incentive⏎payments under HITECH, and neither has benefited from a tailored ONC certification framework⏎comparable to those applicable to hospitals and eligible professionals. As a result, home-based⏎providers have often invested in health IT infrastructure without the same level of federal⏎financial support, standardized certification pathways, or interoperability alignment afforded to⏎facility-based settings.⏎These structural differences matter. Home-based care is central to national goals related to cost⏎containment, aging in place, serious illness management, and hospital capacity management.⏎7⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (90 FR⏎60108)⏎Yet policy frameworks are frequently designed with acute-care delivery assumptions that do not⏎fully reflect decentralized, in-home care environments.⏎As AI governance evolves, it is important that home-based care not be further disadvantaged⏎through standards, interoperability requirements and gaps, validation methodologies, or⏎oversight mechanisms that assume facility-based workflows or data structures. Inclusion of⏎home-based care stakeholders in governance design, pilot programs, interoperability initiatives,⏎and research efforts will be essential to ensuring modernization across the continuum of care.⏎AI policy presents an opportunity not only to accelerate innovation, but to correct historical⏎imbalances in federal health IT support. Thoughtful inclusion of home-based care in AI strategy⏎will strengthen system-wide performance and improve outcomes for medically complex⏎populations served outside traditional institutional settings.⏎Conclusion⏎Home-based care AI has the potential to serve as core infrastructure for managing risk,⏎improving preventive intervention, and supporting aging in place. Realizing that potential will⏎require regulatory clarity, interoperable data access, proportionate governance expectations,⏎explicit explainability standards, thoughtful phased implementation for high-impact uses, and⏎alignment between payment policy and clinically validated upstream interventions.⏎With predictable and transparent governance, AI can function not as a compliance risk, but as a⏎force multiplier for quality and cost containment.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment. We welcome the opportunity to provide additional⏎data or operational details to support HHS’s ongoing work. Should you have any questions, feel⏎free to contact me at 214-239-6778 or lukerutledge@hchb.com.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Luke Rutledge, President⏎Homecare Homebase⏎8⏎© 2026 Homecare Homebase, LP. All rights reserved. | Private and Confidential♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0018♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Scott♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔University of Alabama at Birmingham School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My institution's IRB approved a study last year examining AI-assisted polyp detection errors at three academic endoscopy centers, and what I keep coming back to is not the error rates themselves but what happened when we tried to figure out who was responsible for a specific category of missed lesions that the AI tool had flagged as low-risk. The vendor's contract language — and I have read it, or tried to — essentially said that outputs are "decision support" and that clinical judgment supersedes the tool's recommendation, which means the moment a physician uses the output, the physician owns the outcome. The hospital's credentialing committee took the position that the tool was FDA-cleared and therefore presumptively safe. The gastroenterologist who had used the tool said, reasonably, that he had no way to independently audit its performance in our patient population because the vendor does not provide site-specific accuracy data. So: vendor blames clinician, clinician blames vendor, hospital says FDA cleared it. Nobody is wrong, technically, and nobody is accountable either.⏎⏎That is what this RFI is being submitted into. HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption and the fundamental question of who is legally responsible for AI-caused patient harm has no coherent answer right now. I am not sure HHS fully reckons with that in the framing of this document.⏎⏎The FDA 510(k) clearance process does not evaluate whether a tool performs safely in the deployment context where it actually gets used. A colonoscopy AI trained predominantly on high-volume academic center endoscopy footage from Japan may carry a clearance that says nothing meaningful about its accuracy in a community practice in rural Alabama serving a predominantly Black patient population with different adenoma prevalence rates and different bowel prep quality patterns. I know this because I study this. And yet once a tool has 510(k) clearance, institutions treat that as a green light, liability is diffuse, and the performance validation data that would tell you whether the tool is actually working in your patient population is proprietary and not disclosed.⏎⏎I want to respond specifically to the question in this RFI about what regulatory or reimbursement actions HHS should take to support responsible AI adoption. My answer is that HHS should not accelerate reimbursement incentives for AI-assisted clinical tools until there is a functioning liability framework that tells patients, clinicians, and institutions who is legally responsible when the tool contributes to harm. Right now CMS reimbursement signals create adoption pressure without accountability structure. That combination is genuinely dangerous, not in a rhetorical sense but in the sense that patients will be harmed and will have no identifiable defendant with deep enough pockets to make them whole.⏎⏎And the irreversibility problem is something I don't see discussed enough. Once AI is embedded in a billing workflow or a standard-of-care protocol, removing it is enormously costly even when you have evidence it is underperforming. Institutions will tolerate known performance gaps rather than absorb removal costs. I have seen this with EHR clinical decision support tools that everyone knows are poorly calibrated but nobody turns off because of workflow dependencies. AI diagnostic tools will be the same, probably worse because the vendor relationships are more complex.⏎⏎I don't have a clean policy recommendation that solves all of this. I do think HHS should be requiring, as a condition of any reimbursement recognition, that vendors provide site-level performance data to deploying institutions in a standardized format, and that contracts between vendors and health systems contain explicit liability allocation language that is publicly disclosed in aggregate. Whether that is achievable through existing HHS authority or requires legislative action I genuinely don't know.⏎⏎What I do know is that asking how to make adoption faster before asking who pays when it goes wrong is the wrong sequencing, and I'm not sure the urgency in this RFI's framing is warranted given where the accountability infrastructure actually stands as of early 2025.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0019♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from The Medical Group Management Association (MGMA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔The Medical Group Management Association (MGMA)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street, SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: MGMA Response to HHS RFI, Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎The Medical Group Management Association (MGMA) appreciates the opportunity to provide⏎comments in response to the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) Request for⏎Information (RFI) on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of⏎Clinical Care. MGMA represents more than 70,000 medical practice administrators, executives,⏎and leaders from over 15,000 medical groups nationwide, encompassing more than 350,000⏎physicians across all specialties and care settings ranging from small, independent practices in⏎rural and underserved communities to large, multispecialty organizations and integrated delivery⏎systems.⏎In its AI Strategy, HHS describes one of its aims as supporting an outcomes-first approach to⏎integrating AI to modernize care delivery and public health infrastructure to improve health at⏎the individual and population levels. We support this aim and are committed to working in⏎partnership with HHS to ensure medical groups are empowered and enabled through clear and⏎proportionate guardrails, sustainable reimbursement pathways, and standards that support⏎responsible AI adoption. Federal AI policies should establish expectations that ensure AI tools⏎are safe, interoperable, transparently developed, and usable within a clinician’s workflow for⏎their direct review and involvement, without exacerbating administrative burden. For many⏎medical groups, particularly smaller and independent practices, adoption decisions are shaped⏎not only by innovation, but by many other factors including infrastructure readiness and cost.⏎Medical groups are increasingly interacting with AI-enabled tools across both administrative and⏎clinical workflows. These technologies present meaningful opportunities in many ways,⏎1717 Pennsylvania Ave. NW, #600 Washington, DC 20006 T 202.293.3450 F 202.293.2787 mgma.org⏎⏎including improving operational efficiency, enhancing clinician well-being, and supporting⏎high-quality patient care. At the same time, rapid adoption without appropriate governance and⏎guardrails could introduce unintended consequences such as increased workflow burden or⏎added liability exposure.⏎We commend HHS for seeking experience-based feedback. Our comments focus on the⏎practical realities facing medical groups as end-users of AI, and on policy approaches that can⏎accelerate safe, responsible adoption while avoiding unintended downstream consequences for⏎providers and patients. We believe the following considerations, grounded in supporting medical⏎groups, should be valuable to HHS as it guides and develops policy related to AI in health⏎clinical care and health operations. These reflect broader end-user efforts across the health IT⏎community and member feedback:⏎• AI should augment, not replace, clinical judgment and human decision-making. AI⏎tools must support clinicians and care teams rather than override professional⏎responsibility; AI used for prior authorization should not override clinical judgment.⏎• Transparency is foundational to trust and adoption. Medical groups benefit from⏎clear, standardized information about how AI tools are designed, trained, validated, and⏎intended to be used.⏎• Workflow integration is critical in addition to technical performance. AI that is not⏎embedded into existing clinical and operational workflows risks increasing burden rather⏎than reducing it.⏎• Oversight should match risk. Higher-risk tools warrant greater evaluation and⏎safeguards.⏎• Policies should not inadvertently shift burden onto medical groups. Protect medical⏎groups, physicians, and other providers from liability associated with AI as it pertains to⏎the conditions of the technology developed outside of the practice.⏎Regulation: Alignment, Predictability, and Risk-Based Oversight⏎MGMA supports HHS’ goal of establishing federal policy for AI that is predictable,⏎proportionate to risk, and supportive of innovation. Medical groups already operate within a⏎complex healthcare regulatory environment; a fragmented or inconsistent federal approach to AI⏎oversight could create confusion, duplicative requirements, and additional administrative burden⏎for practices.⏎Recent deregulatory proposals in the Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability: ASTP/ONC⏎Deregulatory Actions to Unleash Prosperity (HTI-5) Proposed Rule would affect transparency⏎requirements that enable medical practices to access consistent information about how AI-⏎enabled decision support tools are developed, validated, and intended to be used.⏎1717 Pennsylvania Ave. NW, #600 Washington, DC 20006 T 202.293.3450 F 202.293.2787 mgma.org⏎⏎HHS should ensure comparable transparency mechanisms remain in place if removal or⏎modification of these requirements occurs as part of future policy. The lack of a clearly⏎articulated transparency framework could reduce visibility into model attributes, data sources,⏎and validation methods and potentially expose practices to greater direct evaluation and liability⏎burden. At a time when governance and trust in AI are critical, certification-based transparency⏎as a part of the HHS Office of the National Coordinator (ONC) Health IT Certification Program⏎remains important for informed adoption and safe implementation.⏎Transparency is also critical as it pertains to payers. HHS should require payers to be transparent⏎about their use of AI for utilization management, claims processing, and coverage limitations⏎and ensure AI systems utilized by payers are evidence-based, do not exacerbate administrative⏎burden for medical groups, and do not interfere with physician clinical decision-making.⏎Additionally, HHS should modernize federal regulations and implement sensible and robust⏎security and privacy protections to prioritize patient privacy.⏎Reimbursement: Payment Policy as a Lever for Responsible AI Adoption⏎HHS policy plays a central role in shaping whether and how AI tools are adopted in clinical care.⏎HHS AI policies should be aligned across agencies with consistent expectations for privacy,⏎security, transparency, and accountability. Oversight should be commensurate with patient risk⏎while avoiding fragmented or duplicative requirements that increase administrative burden.⏎As pertains to payment policy for AI applications (such as ambient documentation, workflow⏎automation, and care coordination support), HHS should explore sustainable reimbursement⏎pathways, grants, demonstration models, and payment modernization strategies that recognize⏎AI-enabled administrative and clinical efficiencies. HHS should also account for initial and⏎ongoing implementation and maintenance costs and consider financing challenges faced by small⏎and independent practices.⏎Responsible AI adoption involves governance. As the National Institute of Standards and⏎Technology (NIST) has recognized, organizations must establish appropriate governance⏎processes related to AI, security, privacy, and information systems tailored to specific use cases.1⏎HHS can help to support this by informing scalable governance approaches and tools, including⏎“lightweight” approaches, appropriate for diverse practice sizes.⏎Research and Development: Supporting Applied, Real-World AI Research⏎HHS can advance applied research on AI development and real-world clinical use. This would⏎benefit medical groups that may lack the financial, technical, and workforce resources necessary⏎to independently validate, implement, and monitor AI tools across diverse practice settings and⏎1 Gina M. Raimondo, Laurie E. Locascio, “Artificial intelligence Risk Management Framework: Generative Artificial⏎Intelligence Profile,” NIST, July 2024, https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ai/NIST.AI.600-1.pdf.⏎1717 Pennsylvania Ave. NW, #600 Washington, DC 20006 T 202.293.3450 F 202.293.2787 mgma.org⏎⏎patient populations. MGMA encourages HHS to advance applied research that supports⏎responsible and scalable AI adoption in medical group settings. Research can assess issues such⏎as whether AI meaningfully reduces administrative burden, improves documentation efficiency,⏎and integrates effectively into clinical and operational workflows in real-world environments.⏎Studies can include strategies to address post-deployment performance changes to support⏎practice-level implementation improvement.⏎In addition, HHS can examine the infrastructure and sustainability requirements necessary for⏎safe AI deployment, including cybersecurity capacity, interoperability readiness, and the⏎financial and operational costs associated with implementation and maintenance. Further, it can⏎assess the use of AI and predictive technologies with attention to impacts on patient access,⏎provider burden, prior authorization, transparency, and the need for appropriate guardrails⏎including human oversight.⏎Research partnerships can include medical groups as active collaborators, and focus on real-⏎world implementation, human-AI interaction, and organizational readiness. Through grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and demonstration initiatives, HHS can help develop common⏎evaluation frameworks and practical implementation guides that support individual practices.⏎MGMA Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎The following responses address selected RFI questions highly relevant to medical groups.⏎Question 1 Topic: Barriers to AI Innovation and Adoption⏎Medical groups are increasingly interested in leveraging artificial intelligence to improve clinical⏎care and operational performance. A September 30, 2025, MGMA Stat poll (n=351) found that⏎68% of medical groups reported adding or expanding AI use in 2025, primarily in documentation⏎and charting workflows, patient communications, revenue cycle functions, and analytics.2⏎Among practices that did not add or expand AI in 2025, many cited cost, small-practice fit,⏎unclear productivity gains, EHR incompatibility, learning curve, or simply not knowing where to⏎start.⏎For those adding or expanding AI use, adoption has not consistently translated into measurable⏎burden reduction. In a separate 2025 poll, among practices using AI, only 39% reported reduced⏎staff workload, while 44% reported no reduction and 17% were unsure.3 These findings⏎underscore that successful implementation depends not only on technological capability but also⏎on many additional factors.⏎2 MGMA Stat Poll, “Document, schedule, communicate: Where ambulatory care has added or expanded AI in⏎2025,” Oct. 1, 2025, https://www.mgma.com/mgma-stat/document-schedule-communicate-ai-tools.⏎3 MGMA Stat Poll, “Most practices use some form of AI, but is it actually reducing staff workloads,” Aug. 6, 2025,⏎https://www.mgma.com/mgma-stat/most-practices-use-ai-but-is-it-reducing-staff-workloads.⏎1717 Pennsylvania Ave. NW, #600 Washington, DC 20006 T 202.293.3450 F 202.293.2787 mgma.org⏎⏎For added historical perspective, from an earlier 2024 research report on AI and value-based⏎care, we found the primary barriers to adopting AI across all surveyed healthcare organizations⏎with AI in place involved ensuring proper education and training (36%), cost concerns (27%),⏎lack of trust (20%) as well as having the appropriate resources for implementation (18%). For⏎those who had not yet implemented an AI tool, top barriers consisted of regulatory hurdles (50%)⏎and uncertainty (48%).4⏎Question 4 Topic: Evaluation Methods and Metrics⏎As stated earlier, MGMA encourages HHS to advance applied research that supports responsible,⏎scalable AI adoption for medical group settings. This involves having evaluations to assess⏎technical accuracy as well as factors, including workflow integration, usability, training⏎requirements, clinical impact, patient outcomes, AI literacy, administrative burden reduction,⏎cost implications, monitoring, maintenance, safety, equity, bias, and operational impact within⏎real-world practice settings.⏎A challenge involving AI evaluation is that a tool’s effectiveness depends heavily on the practice⏎environment, including user training, organizational readiness, and the specific care setting in⏎which it is deployed. Performance observed in controlled environments may not translate into⏎measurable improvements in clinical practice. As recent literature has noted, AI evaluations may⏎involve safety monitoring and institutional compliance, with less focus on whether a tool⏎demonstrably improves patient outcomes, reduces administrative burden, or enhances care⏎delivery in real-world settings.5 The testing of AI tools across a variety of clinical settings can⏎help to confirm these technologies are operating as expected without adverse patient⏎consequences. Real-world testing and documentation of identified issues, including implicit⏎biases, are of critical importance to medical groups as end-users to advance AI adoption in⏎healthcare.⏎Question 7 Topic: Organizational Decision-Makers and Hurdles⏎In medical groups, AI governance readiness remains uneven. A January 20, 2026, MGMA Stat⏎poll (n=328) found that 42% of medical group leaders report having AI governance structures or⏎formal policies in place (20%) or in development (22%), while 56% report having none and 2%⏎are unsure. 6This reflects progress since late 2024, when MGMA polling found that nearly three-⏎quarters of surveyed organizations lacked formal governance despite expanding AI use.⏎4 MGMA, Humana, “AI Adoption in the Value-Based Era,” Fall/Winter 2024, https://www.mgma.com/deep-⏎dives/ai-adoption-in-the-value-based-era.⏎5 Derek C. Angus, MD, MPH; Rohan Khera, MD, MS; Tracy Lieu, MD, MPH, “AI, Health, and Health Care Today and⏎Tomorrow, The JAMA Summit Report on Artificial Intelligence,” JAMA, Oct. 13, 2025,⏎https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2840175.⏎6 MGMA Stat Poll, “AI governance in medical group practices: Rules for the humans in the loop,” Jan. 21, 2026,⏎https://www.mgma.com/mgma-stat/ai-governance-in-medical-group-practices.⏎1717 Pennsylvania Ave. NW, #600 Washington, DC 20006 T 202.293.3450 F 202.293.2787 mgma.org⏎⏎Groups may respond to organizational AI challenges by implementing practical, scalable⏎approaches, such as forming lightweight AI review committees, formalizing use-case intake⏎processes, and establishing monitoring metrics. These efforts aim to ensure AI supports care⏎delivery rather than introducing additional complexity. Ultimately, adoption decisions in⏎medical groups often reflect operational realities. Organizational readiness (including⏎governance capacity, workforce training, infrastructure, and financial resources) and practical⏎limitations can shape organizational decisions involving AI tools and their ability to deliver⏎meaningful value in practice settings.⏎Question 8 Topic: Interoperability Opportunities⏎AI tools often require access to structured and unstructured clinical and operational data from⏎across EHRs, practice management, and other systems. When systems cannot exchange data⏎seamlessly, this may cause redundant workflows, increasing both cost and operational⏎complexity. Advancing consistent national interoperability standards can help to support scalable⏎AI deployment. HHS can reinforce vendor accountability by ensuring health IT developers⏎adhere to standardized interoperability requirements.⏎Interoperability should also be viewed through an infrastructure lens. For many smaller and⏎community-based medical groups, limited infrastructure maturity may constrain AI use, raising⏎concerns about uneven access to high-performing technologies across geographic and resource⏎settings. Policies that promote standardized exchange of quality data, privacy and security, and⏎resources for infrastructure readiness, can help to reduce administrative burden and support more⏎equitable adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎Conclusion⏎MGMA appreciates HHS’ leadership in seeking to accelerate responsible AI adoption in clinical⏎care. Medical group practices stand ready to collaborate with HHS to ensure AI policies support⏎innovation while protecting patients, reducing burden, and enabling sustainable adoption across⏎diverse care settings. Policies that reflect and are informed by the operational realities of medical⏎groups will help to achieve the full potential and promise of AI. If you have any questions,⏎please contact Samantha Meklir, Associate Director of Government Affairs, at⏎smeklir@mgma.org or 202-293-3450.⏎Sincerely,⏎/s/⏎Anders M. Gilberg⏎Senior Vice President, Government Affairs⏎1717 Pennsylvania Ave. NW, #600 Washington, DC 20006 T 202.293.3450 F 202.293.2787 mgma.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0020♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Health Information Management Association (AHIMA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Health Information Management Association (AHIMA)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 18, 2026⏎Jim O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎US Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary and National Coordinator⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎330 C Street NW⏎Floor 7, Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically to http://www.regulations.gov⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎On behalf of the American Health Information Management Association (AHIMA) and its members, we⏎are writing in response to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎AHIMA is a global nonprofit association of health information (HI) professionals. AHIMA represents⏎professionals who work with health data for more than one billion patient visits each year. The AHIMA⏎mission of empowering people to impact health drives our members and credentialed HI professionals to⏎ensure that health information is accurate, complete, and available to patients and clinicians. Our⏎leaders work at the intersection of healthcare, technology, and business, and are found in data integrity⏎and information privacy job functions worldwide.⏎As HHS continues to enact President Trump’s vision for the utilization of AI in healthcare it is crucial for⏎all sectors of healthcare to be included at the table. It will take a whole of healthcare approach to ensure⏎the full potential of AI is unlocked in a safe and meaningful way. HHS’ AI Strategy lays the foundation for⏎an approach encompassing key stakeholders and AHIMA applauds those efforts while urging HHS to⏎maintain that expansive vision throughout implementation.⏎The HI profession is energized by the innovations and opportunities AI can bring to healthcare. Through⏎proper implementation, the use of AI can assist HI professionals in completing burdensome tasks – such⏎1⏎Copyright © 2025 AHIMA®. All rights reserved. Confidential and proprietary.⏎⏎as claims processing – allowing them to focus on more advanced work within their field. A robust policy⏎plan that builds on this strategy will need to be implemented if the American healthcare system is to take⏎advantage of everything AI has to offer. Such a plan must provide the necessary guardrails to ensure AI⏎tools brought to market and implemented are improving the quality of healthcare provided and/or assist⏎with improving health outcomes.⏎AHIMA provides the following policy recommendations to strengthen AI’s use in healthcare:⏎• Ensure robust regulatory guidelines centered on fairness, accuracy, security, and transparency are⏎developed for both clinical and non-clinical AI;⏎• Structure regulatory frameworks with sufficient flexibility to allow for continued AI development⏎and innovation;⏎• Focus on the intended use and desired outcome of the AI when determining the efficacy of the⏎technology;⏎• Prioritize end-users’ input – including HI professionals – throughout the development and real-⏎world testing of AI technology;⏎• Develop an updated set of privacy and security policies to encapsulate new challenges and needs⏎posed by the use of AI in healthcare settings; and⏎• Maintain a focus on reducing unintended outputs and unplanned biases within AI models to⏎ensure outputs remain stable and can be utilized across multiple settings with limited⏎maintenance.⏎AHIMA believes the above recommendations will create an environment of trusted AI use and⏎implementation allowing those who interact with healthcare to have confidence in the technology itself.⏎A number of challenges exist in the use of AI today and the existing policy framework surrounding its⏎implementation. We encourage HHS to address both the recommendations above and the need for those⏎in healthcare to have an avenue to engage in the development of standards and best practices for AI.⏎Maintaining strong private-public sector partnerships relating to the development and use of AI can⏎ensure the desired policy outcomes are realized.⏎AHIMA encourages HHS to focus continued AI activities on the above recommendation in concert with⏎the goals outlined within the five pillars of the HHS AI Strategic Plan, especially when looking at ways to⏎bring AI into the clinical sphere. It is crucial for HHS to use the pillars as a starting point for all guided⏎regulatory actions related to AI, but flexibility is also needed for the department to adjust based on the⏎practical realities of AI implementation and use. Healthcare AI development and use is in a nascent stage⏎and a nimble regulatory posture will be necessary to ensure regulatory goals are met. As HHS encounters⏎changes in the operational and regulatory landscape related to AI, the department should continue to⏎lean on its private sector parties to ensure the regulatory strategy remains viable.⏎To date, challenges in healthcare technology have perpetuated regardless of the modality of technology⏎used. It is important that these challenges are accounted for in all regulatory activities. As HHS continues⏎to develop its AI regulatory strategy, AHIMA and its members stand ready to assist.⏎2⏎Copyright © 2025 AHIMA®. All rights reserved. Confidential and proprietary.⏎⏎The advent of AI will assist the HI profession by alleviating staff burnout, reducing administrative burden,⏎and improving compliance activities. While the implementation of AI is not without challenges, AHIMA⏎believes a regulatory plan that utilizes common sense policy guardrails can ensure that AI becomes⏎another piece of trusted, safe healthcare technology. We look forward to continued collaboration in the⏎development of the Department’s AI strategy. If you have any questions related to our letter or would like⏎to discuss opportunities for the HI profession to be involved in the development of AI policy moving⏎forward, please contact AHIMA Senior Director of Regulatory and International Affairs, Andrew⏎Tomlinson, at andrew.tomlinson@ahima.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Lauren Riplinger, JD⏎Chief Public Policy and Impact Officer⏎3⏎Copyright © 2025 AHIMA®. All rights reserved. Confidential and proprietary.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0021♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Mental Health Technology Advisory Committee- American Psychological Association , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mental Health Technology Advisory Committee- American Psychological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔APA’s Mental Health Technology Advisory Committee⏎Public Comment to RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Submitted by: American Psychological Association Mental Health Tech Advisory⏎Committee⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎Contact: Nina Horne, M.P.P., Committee Member, nina.horne@icloud.com⏎Opening Statement⏎I. Introduction⏎The American Psychological Association's (APA) Mental Health Tech Advisory Committee,⏎a volunteer group of external experts, appreciates the opportunity to respond to the⏎Department of Health and Human Services' (HHS) Request for Information on accelerating⏎the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care (90 FR 60108,⏎December 23, 2025).⏎We share the Department's goal of accelerating AI adoption. The mental health system's⏎failures are urgent: 48% of adults with mental illness receive no treatment, and 122 million⏎Americans live in Mental Health Professional Shortage Areas. AI offers meaningful⏎potential to expand access, reduce administrative burden, and improve care delivery. We⏎welcome HHS's commitment to using its regulatory, reimbursement, and research and⏎development levers to address these challenges.⏎Our comments below address the overarching policy framework we believe is necessary to⏎support sustainable AI adoption in mental health care, followed by specific⏎recommendations responsive to the RFI's three areas of inquiry. We draw on our expertise⏎in clinical psychology, behavioral health delivery systems, and the emerging evidence on⏎AI-assisted mental health interventions.⏎II. Framing: The Prior Question⏎Every sound policy analysis begins with a prior question: what problem are we trying to⏎solve? HHS has framed its inquiry around acceleration — how the Department can drive⏎faster adoption of AI in clinical care. We support this objective. However, we urge the⏎⏎Department to adopt a complementary framing that is particularly critical for mental⏎health applications: how do we accelerate adoption without doing harm?⏎This is not a rhetorical distinction. It produces different policies. Pure acceleration framing⏎leads primarily to barrier removal —deregulation, funding increases, and streamlined⏎pathways. A safety-integrated acceleration framing leads to infrastructure building: safety⏎floors, evidence systems, evaluation capacity, and governance expertise that allow⏎innovation to proceed on a foundation that does not collapse under the first catastrophic⏎failure.⏎We respectfully note that the historical record is instructive. Sectors that prioritized speed⏎without proportionate governance — telecommunications in the 1990s, social media in the⏎2000s, cryptocurrency in the 2010s — experienced rapid adoption followed by crisis,⏎erosion of public trust, and reactive overregulation that ultimately set them back further⏎than proactive governance would have. Sectors that invested in safety infrastructure⏎alongside innovation — pharmaceuticals, aviation, automotive safety — produced slower⏎initial adoption but sustained, durable growth built on institutional trust.⏎Mental health care demands the latter approach. In this domain, the most vulnerable⏎populations are the most likely to encounter AI; harms are severe and often irreversible;⏎and technology operates on the human mind itself. Innovation that harms patients is not⏎innovation — it is a cost externalized onto the most vulnerable people in the system.⏎III. AI-Specific Risks in Mental Health Care⏎While our comments are broadly supportive of AI adoption, we wish to draw HHS's⏎attention to risks that are uniquely acute in mental health applications and that require⏎proportionate governance:⏎• Suicide escalation failures. AI systems providing emotional support or crisis⏎intervention may mishandle acute suicidal ideation, with potentially fatal⏎consequences.⏎• Parasocial dependency. Users may form unhealthy attachment relationships with⏎AI tools, displacing human connection and therapeutic relationships in ways that⏎worsen long-term outcomes.⏎• Bias and discrimination. AI systems trained on unrepresentative data may⏎produce outputs that are systematically less effective or actively harmful for racial⏎and ethnic minorities, LGBTQ+ individuals, and other underserved populations.⏎⏎• Erosion of therapeutic standards. Widespread AI-mediated mental health⏎interactions, delivered outside clinical oversight, risk normalizing substandard care⏎and undermining evidence-based practice.⏎• Conflict with Existing Treatment Process. Use of AI for mental health purposes⏎may conflict with existing treatment processes and planning with AI providing⏎information that goes against clinician advice.⏎The scale of current adoption makes these risks urgent. Population-level risk is the product⏎of prevalence and individual harm. When hundreds of millions of Americans use AI tools⏎weekly for emotional support, crisis management, and mental health guidance — often⏎outside any clinical relationship — even a small per-interaction probability of harm⏎produces vast aggregate damage.⏎IV. Central Argument: Trust as Infrastructure for Sustainable Innovation⏎Our central recommendation to the Department is this: sustainable innovation — not⏎unregulated speed — is what actually accelerates adoption. Trust is the essential⏎infrastructure for both clinical care and technology markets, and HHS policy should be⏎designed to build it.⏎In clinical care, the therapeutic relationship — built on trust — is the single strongest⏎predictor of treatment outcomes across all modalities. In technology markets, institutional⏎trust is the precondition for procurement, reimbursement, and sustained use. AI in mental⏎health care must earn clinical trust to be effective and market trust to scale.⏎Yet early documented harms — including suicide escalation failures, dependency cases,⏎and discriminatory outputs — are already eroding both forms of trust simultaneously.⏎Every unregulated adverse event makes clinicians more skeptical, health systems more⏎cautious, payers more resistant, and patients more wary. Rebuilding trust once lost costs⏎far more — in both time and resources — than building it correctly from the start.⏎We urge HHS to recognize that the current market externalizes critical costs: psychological⏎harm to users, safety research that no individual firm funds, crisis detection infrastructure⏎that no product includes, and evaluation systems that no one builds. Innovation that⏎externalizes these costs is not sustainable. It produces the boom-and-bust cycle that has⏎characterized previous digital health waves, where early hype gives way to catastrophic⏎safety failure, triggering reactive overregulation that freezes the sector for years.⏎⏎Sustainable innovation internalizes these costs through policy design, ensuring that the⏎process of acceleration does not destroy the conditions for its own continuation.⏎V. Summary of Recommendations⏎In the sections that follow, we provide specific recommendations organized around the⏎RFI's three areas of inquiry. Each recommendation is designed to accelerate adoption⏎while preserving patient safety and public trust:⏎• Regulation. Clear, risk-proportionate regulatory pathways for AI mental health⏎tools, with tiered oversight based on clinical risk level and population vulnerability.⏎• Reimbursement. Payment policy alignment that incentivizes evidence-based AI⏎tools and does not inadvertently reward unvalidated products or penalize human⏎clinical services.⏎• Research and Development. Modernized evidence standards suited to AI's⏎characteristics, federal investment in evaluation infrastructure, and public-private⏎partnerships to generate the safety research the market will not produce on its own.⏎We elaborate on each of these areas in the sections below. Further, the six⏎recommendations in this submission constitute a coherent framework for sustainable⏎innovation, operating through six reinforcing mechanisms:⏎• Predictability accelerates investment — firms build when they know the rules.⏎• Safety floors prevent catastrophic adoption shocks that freeze entire sectors.⏎• Right-sized evaluation methods prevent both false rigor and false speed.⏎• Reimbursement alignment creates demand pull — nothing scales without⏎payment.⏎• Socializing safety costs now prevents the boom-and-bust cycle that has frozen⏎previous digital health markets.⏎• Clinical expertise at the table prevents oversight that’s calibrated to the wrong⏎risks.⏎Drawing on the clinical and scientific expertise of APA’s 185,000+ members — including⏎licensed practitioners who deliver mental health care daily across every setting HHS's⏎policy will affect us. We offer the following guiding principles:⏎-The pursuit of innovation must not dilute established clinical standards. AI tools⏎making therapeutic claims must meet the same well-validated evidentiary requirements⏎that apply to human-delivered interventions.⏎⏎-Patient safety is integral to sustainable innovation. In high-stakes domains, safety and⏎innovation are not competing values — safety is the condition that enables innovation to⏎scale. Trust is the mechanism: clinical trust enables therapeutic effectiveness, and⏎institutional trust enables market adoption. Markets that externalize harm destroy both⏎forms of trust simultaneously, setting adoption back further than any regulatory⏎requirement.⏎-Psychology expertise is essential. Effective and safe innovation requires deep⏎understanding of therapeutic relationships, clinical judgment, and mental health⏎outcomes.⏎Executive Summary of Recommendations⏎We recommend HHS:⏎1. Establish risk-tiered oversight for mental health AI, with deployer certification and⏎a psychologically active ingredient standard.⏎2. Mandate crisis safeguards as a baseline safety floor for all AI used in mental⏎health contexts.⏎3. Modernize evidence standards to accommodate adaptive learning AI systems.⏎4. Align reimbursement with augmentation and validated outcomes, not unvalidated⏎replacement.⏎5. Fund independent evaluation and regulatory sandboxes scaled to risk.⏎6. Integrate psychology expertise into governance, with an Interagency Task Force⏎on AI and Psychological Safety.⏎Recommendation 1: Establish a Mental Health–Specific Tiered Regulatory Framework⏎HHS should adopt a risk-tiered oversight model that matches scrutiny to clinical claims,⏎risk profile, and functional intent — complementing the existing SaMD framework for⏎products that fall outside it. The framework should include deployer certification⏎requirements, foundation model transparency obligations, and a "psychologically active⏎ingredient" standard that extends baseline safety obligations to consumer AI products with⏎foreseeable psychological impact.⏎⏎Recommendation 2: Mandate Baseline Suicide and Crisis Safeguards⏎All AI systems used in or foreseeably affecting mental health contexts — including⏎consumer products meeting the psychologically active ingredient criteria — must⏎implement documented and interpretable crisis detection and escalation protocols.⏎Recommendation 3: Modernize Evidentiary Standards for Digital Mental Health⏎HHS should adapt evidence expectations to learning systems, incorporating real-world⏎evidence, adaptive designs, and lifecycle monitoring through an adaptive regulatory⏎approach that generates information and updates requirements over time.⏎Recommendation 4: Align Reimbursement with Augmentation and Outcomes⏎Payment policy should reward AI that reduces burden and improves validated outcomes,⏎not tools that substitute for human care without validation. Reimbursement design must⏎guard against a two-tier system that widens existing disparities.⏎Recommendation 5: Support Independent Evaluation and Proportionate Auditing⏎Higher-risk systems should undergo independent third-party audits, scaled to risk tier.⏎HHS should establish regulatory sandboxes to create a pathway for supervised evidence-⏎generating deployment of novel mental health AI tools.⏎Recommendation 6: Integrate Clinical Psychology Expertise into Governance⏎Mental health expertise must be embedded in review, evaluation, and policy development⏎processes. HHS should convene an Interagency Task Force on AI and Psychological Safety⏎with CPSC, FTC, and state agencies, and fund research on the developmental impact of AI-⏎mediated relationships on children.⏎Recommendations⏎Recommendation 1: Establish a Mental Health–Specific Tiered Regulatory⏎Framework⏎Responds to RFI Questions 1, 2, 3⏎Acceleration Rationale:⏎A clear, risk-tiered pathway reduces regulatory ambiguity around "wellness vs. clinical"⏎boundaries, giving innovators predictable expectations and shortening time-to-market for⏎lower-risk tools while preserving scrutiny for higher-risk interventions. Without tiering,⏎⏎firms either overbuild compliance (slowing innovation) or underinvest in safety (risking⏎enforcement and reputational collapse that chills the sector).⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎HHS should establish a three-tier oversight structure for mental health AI tools:⏎Tier 1 — Minimal Risk (Enforcement Discretion). Wellness tools: meditation timers,⏎gratitude journals, general mood tracking. No clinical claims, no vulnerable population⏎targeting. Voluntary compliance with basic privacy and safety standards. Required⏎transparency disclosures: what the tool does, what it does not do, what evidence exists.⏎Tier 2 — Moderate Risk (Streamlined Review). Tools making limited clinical claims:⏎symptom tracking for diagnosed conditions, psychoeducation, clinician workflow support⏎(documentation, screening, treatment planning assistance). Evidence requirements⏎proportional to claims. Registration, adverse event reporting, and post-market monitoring⏎required. Baseline safety safeguards required.⏎Tier 3 — Higher Risk (Full Clinical Review). Tools claiming to diagnose or treat psychiatric⏎disorders. AI-driven therapeutic interventions that engage users in therapeutic dialogue,⏎crisis intervention, or clinical assessment without concurrent clinician involvement. Tools⏎targeting high-risk populations or conditions. Pre-deployment safety evaluation,⏎mandatory crisis safeguards (see Recommendation 2), outcome reporting, and⏎independent audit (see Recommendation 5) required. Evidence standards are equivalent⏎to psychotherapeutic interventions.⏎Enforcement scalability. This tiered structure is designed for enforcement scalability. Tier⏎1 relies on market transparency — required disclosures and voluntary compliance —⏎rather than active regulatory enforcement, consuming minimal agency resources. Tier 2⏎uses registration and adverse event reporting — scalable mechanisms that generate⏎information for targeted enforcement rather than requiring pre-market review of every⏎product. Only Tier 3 requires the resource-intensive pre-deployment evaluation and⏎independent audit that traditional regulatory approaches demand. This design ensures⏎that HHS's limited enforcement resources are concentrated where risk is highest, while⏎lower-risk tiers are governed through information requirements that scale with the market.⏎Relationship with existing SaMD regulation. The FDA's existing Software as a Medical⏎Device (SaMD) framework — developed in alignment with the International Medical Device⏎Regulators Forum (IMDRF) already provides risk-based categorization for software that⏎⏎meets the definition of a medical device. We do not propose replacing this framework.⏎However, the SaMD framework was designed for deterministic software with predictable,⏎reproducible outputs: a diagnostic algorithm that takes defined inputs and produces a⏎classification, a clinical decision support tool that applies fixed rules to patient data. It⏎categorizes risk based on the seriousness of health condition and the significance of the⏎information the software provides to a clinical decision.⏎Generative AI mental health tools do not fit this model. They produce probabilistic, non-⏎reproducible outputs that vary with every interaction. They do not provide discrete⏎"information to a clinical decision” they are the intervention, engaging users in open-ended⏎therapeutic dialogue that constitutes the treatment itself. Their behavior adapts based on⏎interaction history in ways that cannot be fully characterized at the time of regulatory⏎review. And they create relational dynamics — attachment, dependency, perceived⏎reciprocity — that have no analogue in the SaMD framework's risk calculus.⏎This mismatch does not only create safety gaps — it actively impedes innovation.⏎Developers building genuinely novel mental health AI tools face a SaMD framework that⏎was not designed for their products: the classification categories do not map to what their⏎tools actually do, the evidentiary expectations assume characteristics their tools do not⏎have, and the regulatory pathway offers no clear route to market. The result is that⏎responsible innovators either force their products into ill-fitting SaMD categories (incurring⏎unnecessary cost and delay), avoid the regulatory system entirely by retreating to⏎"wellness" claims that understate their tools' capabilities, or abandon the U.S. mental⏎health market in favor of less ambiguous regulatory environments. None of these⏎outcomes serves the RFI's goal of accelerating adoption.⏎The three-tier framework proposed here is designed to complement, not replace, the⏎existing SaMD structure — and to accelerate innovation by providing the fit-for-purpose⏎pathway that SaMD currently lacks. Where a mental health AI tool meets the SaMD⏎definition and falls within FDA's existing jurisdiction, it should be regulated through that⏎pathway. The proposed framework addresses the substantial and growing category of⏎tools that fall outside SaMD — either because they are deliberately designed to avoid the⏎SaMD definition, because they are general-purpose tools used for mental health purposes⏎without clinical intent, or because they are consumer products containing psychologically⏎active AI components (see below). The tiers proposed here map to the gap between the⏎SaMD framework's upper boundary and the unregulated space where most mental health⏎AI currently operates. Filling this gap with clear, proportionate expectations will do more to⏎accelerate responsible innovation than any single barrier-removal measure, because it⏎⏎replaces the ambiguity that currently paralyzes the market with the predictability that⏎enables investment.⏎Intent-based enforcement. Tier classification should be determined by a tool's functional⏎capabilities, user population targeting, substitution claims, and design decisions — not by⏎marketing language. A tool that functions as therapy, targets people with mental health⏎conditions, and is designed using therapeutic frameworks is providing mental health⏎treatment, regardless of whether it labels itself "coaching" or "wellness support." The⏎current landscape rewards companies that avoid clinical language while providing clinical⏎services; intent-based criteria close this loophole.⏎General-purpose AI. General-purpose AI tools (ChatGPT, Claude, Gemini, and others) are⏎currently used by hundreds of millions of people weekly, including individuals using them⏎for mental health support, advice, and emotional regulation. These tools were not⏎designed for clinical use, have no mental health-specific safeguards, and operate entirely⏎outside any regulatory framework — yet function as de facto therapists for many users. The⏎tiered framework must address tools foreseeably used for mental health support or⏎affecting mental health, not only tools explicitly marketed for that purpose. The APA Health⏎Advisory on the Use of Generative AI Chatbots and Wellness Applications for Mental⏎Health (November 2025) documents this gap in detail.⏎Deployer Certification and Regulatory Responsibility in the AI Value Chain. For Tier 2⏎and Tier 3 tools, the entity deploying an AI system in a mental health context must certify⏎compliance with applicable safety requirements for that tier — including crisis safeguards,⏎evidentiary standards, and adverse event reporting — regardless of whether the deploying⏎entity developed the underlying model. This "deployer certification" principle ensures that⏎regulatory obligations attach to the decision to serve mental health users, not to the model⏎architecture. For open-source models, the deployer bears full regulatory responsibility for⏎the system as deployed.⏎Foundation model developer obligations. Deployer certification does not mean⏎foundation model developers bear no responsibility. Requiring every general-purpose⏎model developer to pre-certify their models for every possible clinical context is neither⏎feasible nor desirable — the same base model may be used for poetry, tax preparation, or⏎psychiatric intervention. However, general-purpose model developers have an obligation⏎to provide transparency and documentation that enables downstream deployers to certify⏎responsibly. This includes model cards documenting training data composition and known⏎biases, safety evaluations describing tested failure modes and behavioral boundaries,⏎⏎known limitations relevant to sensitive use cases (including mental health), and accessible⏎mechanisms for deployers to report upstream safety concerns. This upstream⏎transparency obligation connects directly to Recommendation 5's requirement that tech⏎companies commit to the disclosure that makes independent evaluation possible. The⏎regulatory obligation for clinical safety sits with whoever puts the system in front of a⏎mental health user — but that obligation is impossible to discharge without adequate⏎documentation from the model developer.⏎However, deployer certification alone is insufficient if it applies only to entities that self-⏎identify as serving mental health users. The wellness-clinical boundary problem identified⏎in Section C — where companies avoid clinical language to escape oversight — has a⏎consumer product analogue: AI-embedded toys, companion chatbots, social platforms,⏎and entertainment products that create sustained conversational or relational interactions⏎with psychological consequences, but whose developers would never describe their⏎products as mental health tools. A child forming a daily attachment to an AI-embedded toy⏎is experiencing a mental health-relevant interaction regardless of the manufacturer's⏎intent.⏎The appropriate regulatory analogy is the concept of an active ingredient. In⏎pharmacology, a product's regulatory status is determined by its active ingredients and⏎their physiological effects — not by how the product is marketed. A supplement that⏎contains a pharmaceutical compound is subject to pharmaceutical regulation regardless⏎of whether it is sold as a "wellness product." The same principle applies here:⏎conversational, relational, and adaptive AI is a psychologically active ingredient in any⏎consumer product it is embedded in. When that AI component engages users in sustained⏎emotional interaction, responds to psychological disclosures, adapts its behavior to⏎deepen engagement, or presents itself in ways that encourage attachment — it produces⏎psychological effects. Those effects do not disappear because the product is marketed as⏎a toy, a companion, or entertainment. The SaMD framework does not reach these products⏎because they do not meet the definition of a medical device and their manufacturers⏎would never seek SaMD classification. The psychologically active ingredient standard is⏎designed to fill precisely this gap — applying baseline safety obligations to products that⏎fall below the SaMD threshold but above the level of zero oversight that currently governs⏎them. This is also an innovation argument: without a clear standard for consumer AI⏎products with psychological effects, the inevitable crisis — a child harmed by an AI toy, a⏎widely publicized dependency case — will produce reactive regulation far more restrictive⏎than the proportionate baseline we propose here. Establishing the standard now protects⏎both users and the market for responsible consumer AI innovation.⏎⏎To close this gap, HHS should establish the principle that baseline safety obligations — at⏎minimum, crisis detection and escalation protocols, prohibitions on reinforcing self-harm⏎or suicidal ideation, and adverse event reporting — attach to AI products based on their⏎foreseeable psychological effects, not their marketing category. We propose the following⏎framework for operationalizing this principle, with the understanding that the specific⏎criteria and thresholds should be validated and refined through the research program⏎proposed in Recommendation 6 before codification as a regulatory standard.⏎Under this proposed framework, a product would be considered to contain a⏎psychologically active AI component when it meets two or more of the following criteria,⏎regardless of whether it is marketed for mental health purposes:⏎• The AI component creates sustained conversational or relational interactions⏎with users, not single-query informational exchanges — this is the primary⏎mechanism through which psychological effects are produced.⏎• The product is marketed to, designed for, or foreseeably used by children,⏎adolescents, or other vulnerable populations — populations in whom the active⏎ingredient's effects are more potent and more durable due to developmental stage,⏎social isolation, or reduced capacity for critical evaluation.⏎• The AI component collects, processes, or responds to emotional, behavioral, or⏎psychological disclosures from users — indicating that the system is operating on⏎psychologically sensitive material, whether or not it was designed to do so.⏎• The product's design features encourage emotional attachment,⏎anthropomorphizing, or parasocial engagement (e.g., human-like personas,⏎memory of past interactions, emotionally expressive responses, agreeableness)⏎amplifying the psychological dose by deepening the user's perception of⏎relationship, trust, and reciprocity, especially when incentivizing increased and⏎ongoing engagement with the product.⏎These criteria are grounded in existing evidence about the mechanisms through which AI⏎produces psychological effects, but we recognize that the specific threshold (two or more⏎criteria), the weighting of individual criteria, and the scope of baseline obligations that⏎should attach are empirical questions that require further research — particularly⏎regarding children and adolescents, where the evidence base is thinnest and the stakes⏎are highest. The research program proposed in Recommendation 6 is designed to generate⏎precisely this evidence.⏎⏎To be clear: we are not proposing that HHS regulate consumer products outside its⏎jurisdiction. We are proposing that HHS establish the evidentiary and conceptual⏎foundation — the psychologically active ingredient principle — that enables any agency⏎with jurisdiction over a given product category to apply proportionate safety obligations⏎based on foreseeable psychological effects. Within HHS's own jurisdiction, the principle⏎applies directly. For products under CPSC, FTC, or state consumer protection authority,⏎the principle provides a shared analytical framework for the interagency coordination⏎proposed in Recommendation 6.⏎This "foreseeable psychological impact" standard extends the deployer certification⏎principle beyond the clinical care boundary, ensuring that products with mental health⏎consequences cannot evade baseline safety obligations simply by avoiding clinical or⏎wellness language. It places the regulatory burden where it belongs: on the entity whose⏎design decisions create the conditions for psychological impact, regardless of what they⏎call their product.⏎Recommendation 2: Mandate Baseline Suicide and Crisis Safeguards⏎Responds to RFI Questions 1, 3, 4, 9.⏎Acceleration Rationale:⏎In mental health contexts, a single catastrophic suicide-related failure could freeze payer,⏎provider, and public adoption; embedding standardized crisis protocols early prevents⏎trust-destroying events and creates a stable environment for scale. Safety floors reduce⏎systemic risk, which lowers adoption resistance from health systems and risk managers.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎Any AI tool marketed for, or foreseeably used for (as defined above), mental health support⏎must implement:⏎• Documented, clinically validated crisis detection protocols capable of⏎identifying expressions of suicidal ideation, self-harm intent, and acute psychiatric⏎distress.⏎• Context-sensitive interpretation. Crisis detection must account for context: the⏎statement "I want to die" means something very different in end-of-life planning⏎versus acute suicidal ideation. Current AI systems frequently lack the nuanced⏎understanding required for these distinctions.⏎⏎• Clear escalation pathways to human crisis services (988 Suicide and Crisis⏎Lifeline, emergency services, or equivalent), triggered when crisis indicators are⏎detected.⏎• Adverse event reporting for serious incidents: user self-harm following AI⏎interaction, AI failure to escalate a crisis, AI-generated clinical misinformation in a⏎safety-critical context.⏎Evidence of need. The APA Health Advisory states that crisis situations require human⏎intervention, and that AI chatbots have repeatedly failed to recognize or appropriately⏎handle situations involving suicidal thoughts, self-harm, or acute distress. Independent⏎testing by Common Sense Media and Stanford's Brainstorm Lab found that leading⏎chatbots consistently fail to recognize common mental health conditions affecting young⏎people, validate what users say rather than directing them to real help, and create⏎engagement patterns that delay or discourage help-seeking. In the November 2025⏎bipartisan House Energy and Commerce Oversight hearing, lawmakers from both parties⏎cited real cases involving self-harm, suicide, and harmful interactions with AI companions.⏎Adolescent behavior as a design parameter, not an exception. Crisis safeguards must⏎be designed with the understanding that adolescents — the population most actively⏎adopting AI tools for emotional support — will predictably attempt to circumvent them.⏎Questioning authority, testing boundaries, and probing limits are not aberrant behaviors in⏎adolescence; they are developmentally normal and expected. "Jailbreaking" — the⏎practice of manipulating AI systems to bypass safety guardrails — is not an edge case or an⏎unexpected failure mode; it is a natural and predictable expression of normal adolescent⏎behavior applied to a new medium. Any crisis safeguard system that can be defeated by a⏎motivated teenager has not been designed for the population actually using these tools.⏎Safety architecture must treat boundary-testing as an expected input, not an anomalous⏎one, and must be robust to the ingenuity of its most vulnerable users.⏎Scope. These safeguards must apply to general-purpose AI tools when they are⏎foreseeably used for mental health support — not only to tools marketed for that purpose.⏎They must also apply to AI products meeting the "foreseeable psychological impact"⏎criteria defined in Recommendation 1, including consumer products that create sustained⏎relational interactions with vulnerable populations. The most acute safety risks exist⏎precisely in tools and products that were not designed for clinical use and therefore lack⏎any crisis protocols.⏎⏎Recommendation 3: Modernize Evidentiary Standards for Digital Mental⏎Health⏎Responds to RFI Questions 2, 4, 10⏎Acceleration Rationale:⏎Adapting evidence expectations to include real-world evidence, adaptive designs, and⏎lifecycle monitoring aligns oversight with how AI systems actually evolve, reducing⏎mismatches that delay approval without improving safety. Modernized standards reduce⏎unnecessary procedural friction while maintaining rigor.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎HHS should formally recognize that digital mental health tools require adapted evidence⏎standards. Traditional evidentiary frameworks — including sham controls — do not⏎translate well to conversational digital interventions. A non-functional chatbot is not a⏎sham; it is simply broken. The active ingredients of digital therapeutics (engagement,⏎personalization, feedback loops) cannot be cleanly separated the way an active drug can⏎be separated from a placebo.⏎Alternative evidence approaches that HHS should accept and support include:⏎• Comparative effectiveness against standard of care⏎• Real-world evidence from deployed systems, leveraging FDA's existing Real-World⏎Evidence framework.⏎• Adaptive trial designs that accommodate systems that learn and evolve.⏎• Longitudinal outcome tracking over clinically meaningful time horizons.⏎• Post-market surveillance with defined triggers for reassessment when system⏎behavior changes materially⏎• Evidence synthesis from multiple smaller studies rather than single large RCTs⏎Critical qualifier. Modernizing the method of evidence generation is appropriate.⏎Eliminating the requirement of evidence is not. The APA Health Advisory found that even⏎generative AI tools developed with high-quality psychological science and using best⏎practices do not yet have enough evidence to show that they are effective or safe to use in⏎mental health care. The evidence requirement must be maintained; what needs⏎modernization is how that evidence is generated.⏎Cost proportionality. The current approval process — designed for pharmaceutical⏎companies with billion-dollar drug pipelines — creates prohibitive barriers for digital⏎⏎mental health innovators operating with substantially smaller budgets. The consequence⏎is that companies avoid the approval process entirely, leaving potentially beneficial tools⏎in regulatory limbo or forcing them to make weaker claims than their evidence supports.⏎Scaling evidence requirements to risk tier (Recommendation 1) addresses this barrier⏎while preserving rigor where risk is highest.⏎Recommendation 4: Align Reimbursement with Augmentation and⏎Outcomes⏎Responds to RFI Questions 2, 6, 7⏎Acceleration Rationale:⏎Adoption in clinical care is driven by payment policy; reimbursement models that reward⏎validated augmentation (burden reduction + outcome improvement) create market pull for⏎responsible AI rather than speculative replacement tools. Payment alignment converts⏎regulatory clarity into real-world diffusion.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎• Create billing codes for AI-augmented services. The absence of appropriate⏎billing codes is a primary administrative barrier to adoption. Health systems cannot⏎bill for AI-assisted therapy sessions, AI-augmented screening, or AI-enabled⏎measurement-based care under current coding structures. HHS should work with⏎the AMA CPT Editorial Panel to develop Category I or Category III codes for AI-⏎augmented behavioral health services and should issue guidance clarifying that⏎existing E/M and psychotherapy codes may be billed when AI tools are used as part⏎of clinician-directed care.⏎• Require parity coverage. Medicaid managed care plans (42 CFR § 438) should be⏎required to cover validated AI-assisted mental health interventions on parity with⏎equivalent human-delivered services, consistent with MHPAEA requirements. CMS⏎should issue sub-regulatory guidance clarifying that MHPAEA's parity requirements⏎extend to AI-augmented behavioral health services, preventing plans from imposing⏎non-quantitative treatment limitations on AI-assisted care that they do not impose⏎on equivalent in-person services. (Note: Enforcement of the 2024 MHPAEA Final⏎Rule has been suspended as of May 2025 pending litigation and agency⏎reconsideration; however, the statutory parity obligations and 2013 regulatory⏎framework remain in effect and are directly applicable to AI-assisted mental health⏎services.)⏎⏎• Design reimbursement for augmentation, not replacement. Payment structures⏎should favor AI tools that extend clinician reach — between-session support,⏎measurement-based care, administrative burden reduction, screening and triage —⏎over tools that substitute for human care without equivalent validation. In⏎workforce shortage areas where no provider is available, AI may serve as a bridge to⏎care rather than an augmentation of existing care; reimbursement for these tools⏎should be conditioned on integration with human oversight at defined intervals,⏎active monitoring for adverse outcomes, and a requirement that AI-delivered care⏎does not become a permanent substitute for investment in workforce capacity. The⏎APA Health Advisory is clear: AI should support, not replace, human professionals.⏎• Consider outcomes-based payment models. Reimbursement tied to⏎demonstrated symptom reduction (PHQ-9, GAD-7), functional improvement and⏎patient goal attainment, or documented burden reduction creates incentives⏎aligned with therapeutic goals — rather than engagement metrics (time on⏎platform, session frequency, user retention) that may conflict with patient⏎wellbeing. CMS's Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation (CMMI) should pilot⏎value-based payment models for AI-augmented behavioral health — testing⏎bundled payment, shared savings, and outcomes-based contracting approaches⏎that reward demonstrated clinical improvement rather than volume. These pilots⏎would generate the real-world evidence on cost-effectiveness that is currently⏎lacking and that payers need to make coverage decisions.⏎• Prevent a two-tier system. Without deliberate design, AI tools risk being deployed⏎as cost-cutting substitutes for human care in publicly insured and lower-income⏎populations while privately insured patients continue to receive human treatment.⏎AI should be an access expander and a clinician multiplier — not a cheap⏎substitution for professional care in marginalized communities. Federal⏎reimbursement policy must ensure that AI-augmented care is funded in a way that⏎supports integrated care models, not one that relegates vulnerable patients to⏎unmonitored automated systems. Among adults with mental illness, treatment⏎rates are approximately 35% for Black and Hispanic adults compared to 52% for⏎white adults (SAMHSA, 2024); payment policy that accelerates AI deployment⏎without equity safeguards risks widening these gaps rather than closing them.⏎• Guard against AI utilization inflation. As AI tools proliferate, payment policy must⏎include safeguards against the risk that AI-augmented billing inflates utilization⏎without corresponding clinical benefit — the same dynamic that has driven cost⏎escalation in other technology-intensive areas of medicine. Outcomes-based⏎payment models (above) and post-market monitoring of utilization patterns tied to⏎validated outcome measures are the primary mechanisms for preventing this.⏎⏎Recommendation 5: Support Independent Evaluation and Proportionate⏎Auditing⏎Responds to RFI Questions 4, 5, 8⏎Acceleration Rationale:⏎Independent validation builds institutional confidence among CIOs, risk officers, and⏎payers, shortening procurement cycles; scaling audit intensity by risk prevents⏎consolidation while maintaining safety. Trust infrastructure accelerates diffusion in⏎regulated environments.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎• Fund independent evaluation infrastructure. HHS should support a public-⏎private partnership (or HHS-funded entity) that rigorously evaluates mental health⏎AI tools and publishes results. Mental health AI tools are credence goods — users⏎cannot evaluate whether a tool actually works even after using it, because mental⏎health outcomes are difficult to attribute and often delayed. Independent⏎evaluation creates the market information that enables quality-based competition.⏎Without it, marketing substitutes for evidence.⏎• Preferred funding mechanisms. Cooperative agreements (CAs) are the most⏎appropriate mechanism — they allow HHS to participate in evaluation design while⏎maintaining independence. Prize competitions could incentivize innovation in⏎safety testing methodology. Contracts are appropriate for building shared⏎evaluation infrastructure (benchmarking datasets, standardized test scenarios).⏎Grants alone are insufficient because they do not create the institutional⏎infrastructure needed for ongoing, systematic evaluation.⏎• Scale audit intensity to risk tier. Tier 1 tools: voluntary safety disclosures. Tier 2⏎tools: registration and post-market adverse event monitoring. Tier 3 tools:⏎independent third-party safety audits, clinical outcome evaluation, and periodic re-⏎evaluation when system behavior changes.⏎• Require transparency as a precondition for evaluation. Researchers cannot⏎evaluate AI tools without transparency about how they are created and used. Tech⏎companies must commit to the disclosure that makes independent evaluation⏎possible. The APA Health Advisory emphasizes that tech companies and⏎policymakers must commit to transparency for evaluation to be feasible.⏎• Support interoperability for evaluation. Standardized outcome measures (PHQ-9,⏎GAD-7, PCL-5, Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale) should be available as⏎⏎structured FHIR-based data elements in all EHR systems, enabling AI tools to⏎access and contribute to longitudinal outcome tracking. Standardized⏎benchmarking datasets for testing mental health AI tools against known clinical⏎scenarios — including diverse populations, languages, and cultural contexts — do⏎not currently exist and represent a critical public goods gap that HHS investment⏎should address.⏎• Establish regulatory sandboxes for mental health AI. HHS should create⏎structured sandbox environments in which developers can test higher-risk mental⏎health AI tools under supervised conditions — with real users, defined safety⏎boundaries, active monitoring, and clinical oversight — before full-scale⏎deployment. Regulatory sandboxes are a well-established adaptive governance⏎mechanism, already implemented under the EU AI Act and in financial services⏎regulation, which allows regulators and developers to learn together about how⏎novel technologies behave in practice. In mental health AI specifically, sandboxes⏎would address a critical innovation bottleneck: developers currently have no⏎pathway to generate the real-world evidence that Recommendation 3 calls for⏎without either deploying at scale (which is premature without evidence) or⏎conducting traditional clinical trials (which, as Section E documents, do not map⏎well to adaptive conversational systems). Sandboxes create a middle path —⏎controlled deployment that generates evidence, identifies failure modes, and⏎informs regulatory requirements — converting the oversight process itself into a⏎driver of innovation rather than an obstacle to it.⏎Recommendation 6: Integrate Clinical Psychology Expertise into⏎Governance⏎Responds to RFI Questions 4, 5, 10⏎Acceleration Rationale:⏎Embedding domain expertise ensures that regulatory expectations reflect real therapeutic⏎processes, preventing misaligned requirements that slow development and creating⏎predictable, clinically grounded evaluation standards. Regulatory coherence reduces⏎iteration cycles and speeds compliant deployment.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎HHS should:⏎• Include clinical psychologists on relevant advisory committees dealing with⏎mental health technology and AI.⏎⏎• Establish a standing consultation process with American Psychological⏎Association for mental health-specific guidance development, evidence standard⏎review, and safety protocol design.⏎• Develop specialized reviewer training in mental health applications and⏎outcomes for HHS and FDA staff evaluating mental health AI tools.⏎• Create interdisciplinary review teams for mental health AI that include both⏎technology/device expertise and clinical psychology expertise.⏎• Ensure mental health-specific classification pathways exist. When FDA first⏎proposed regulating digital mental health tools, there was no "mental health"⏎category for regulatory submission — only neurology and "general." As AI tools⏎increasingly deliver mental health interventions, HHS regulatory infrastructure must⏎include categories, competencies, and evaluation criteria specific to mental health.⏎• Initiate interagency coordination on non-clinical AI with mental health impact.⏎Many of the most consequential AI-related mental health risks — particularly for⏎children — arise from consumer products with no clinical intent: AI-embedded toys,⏎companion devices, entertainment chatbots, and social platforms. These products⏎fall outside HHS's clinical jurisdiction but produce psychological effects that HHS is⏎uniquely positioned to identify and study. HHS should convene an Interagency⏎Task Force on AI and Psychological Safety, drawing on its convening authority to⏎bring together CPSC, FTC, and state consumer protection agencies around shared⏎objectives. The task force's initial mandate should include developing shared⏎definitions of "foreseeable psychological harm" that the FTC can enforce under its⏎unfair-or-deceptive-practices authority (15 U.S.C. § 45) and that the CPSC can⏎apply under its substantial product hazard framework — ensuring that the⏎psychologically active ingredient principle proposed in Recommendation 1 can be⏎operationalized by whichever agency has jurisdiction over a given product category.⏎This is a low-cost, high-leverage action that requires no new statutory authority —⏎only HHS leadership in establishing the conceptual and evidentiary foundation that⏎enables other agencies to act.⏎• Fund research on the psychological effects of non-clinical AI on children and⏎adolescents. Through NIH and SAMHSA, HHS should prioritize research on how AI-⏎embedded consumer products affect attachment formation, relational⏎development, help-seeking behavior, and emotional regulation in children. This⏎research is a prerequisite for evidence-based governance — whether by HHS,⏎CPSC, FTC, or state agencies — and no other entity is positioned to fund it at the⏎necessary scale.⏎⏎Competencies that clinical psychologists bring:⏎• Suicide risk assessment — expertise in recognizing and evaluating the nuances of⏎suicidal ideation, a capability essential for evaluating crisis safeguards.⏎• Therapeutic alliance and relational dynamics — understanding of how⏎therapeutic change occurs, what constitutes therapeutic action, and how to⏎evaluate treatment fidelity.⏎• Psychometrics and outcome measurement — training in assessment validity,⏎measurement design, and interpretation, critical for evaluating whether an AI tool⏎measures what it claims to measure.⏎• Cultural competency — expertise in how culture, identity, and social context⏎shape mental health presentation, help-seeking behavior, and treatment response⏎• Ethical boundary analysis — grounding in professional ethics codes addressing⏎informed consent, competency boundaries, dual relationships, and duty to warn,⏎all directly relevant to AI tool design and evaluation.⏎• Interactional ethics — expertise in the clinical principles of distancing,⏎disengaging, and discouraging, essential for evaluating whether AI systems can⏎recognize when continued interaction is no longer therapeutic and respond⏎appropriately.⏎Thematic Analysis⏎A. Regulatory and Systemic Gaps⏎The rapid deployment of AI in clinical care is occurring within regulatory and⏎reimbursement systems not designed for adaptive mental health technologies. The APA⏎Health Advisory found that current regulatory frameworks are inadequate to address the⏎reality of AI in mental health care.⏎The scale of the unregulated space is substantial: over 10,000 mental health apps are⏎currently available on major app stores; general-purpose AI tools are used by hundreds of⏎millions of people weekly, including for mental health support; the majority of these tools⏎have no clinical validation, no safety protocols, and no regulatory oversight. The regulatory⏎disparity this creates is stark:⏎⏎Licensed Psychotherapist AI System Offering⏎Similar Support⏎Training Doctoral or master's degree No training requirements⏎(6–8 years)⏎Licensure National and state No licensing requirements⏎examinations⏎Ethical Professional codes and No enforced ethical code⏎oversight standards⏎Legal Malpractice liability Unclear liability⏎accountability framework⏎Evidence APA Evidence-Based Often none⏎requirements Practice guidelines⏎Continuing Mandated continuing No ongoing validation⏎competency education⏎This disparity is untenable from a patient safety perspective. It undermines both safety and⏎fair competition — responsible developers who invest in evidence and safety bear costs⏎that unregulated competitors avoid.⏎B. Domain-Specific Risks in Mental Health AI⏎Mental health applications introduce risks that distinguish them from other clinical AI and⏎require mental health-specific governance.⏎⏎1. Suicide and Self-Harm Risk⏎Any tool providing mental health support may encounter users expressing suicidal ideation⏎or self-harm intent. The APA Health Advisory is direct: AI chatbots have repeatedly failed to⏎recognize or appropriately handle these situations. Crisis situations require human⏎intervention.⏎Independent testing confirms this concern. Common Sense Media and Stanford's⏎Brainstorm Lab found that leading chatbots consistently fail to recognize common mental⏎health conditions in young people, validating what teens say rather than directing them to⏎real help, and create engagement patterns that delay or discourage help-seeking. In the⏎November 2025 bipartisan House Energy and Commerce Oversight hearing, lawmakers⏎cited real cases involving self-harm, suicide, and harmful interactions with AI companions.⏎This is not theoretical risk — it is documented harm.⏎Current AI systems often lack the context-sensitive detection required: "I want to die"⏎means something very different when discussing end-of-life planning versus expressing⏎acute suicidal ideation. Crisis detection and escalation must be a baseline safety⏎requirement for any AI tool used in mental health contexts (see Recommendation 2).⏎2. Parasocial Dependency and Sycophancy⏎People develop relationships with conversational AI in ways that fundamentally differ from⏎other software interactions. No one forms an emotional bond with their insulin pump or⏎cardiac monitoring app. But users demonstrably form attachments to conversational AI⏎systems — particularly when those systems are designed to be empathetic, supportive,⏎and emotionally attuned.⏎The APA Health Advisory identifies specific mechanisms: personalized responses, warm or⏎emotionally expressive tones, human-like avatars, and self-presentation as a person can⏎make users feel attached in ways that displace healthy human relationships. This is⏎particularly concerning for children, adolescents, and other vulnerable groups —⏎populations the Health Advisory specifically flags, and populations APA's July 2025 letter⏎to the Consumer Product Safety Commission specifically addressed, requesting⏎investigation into the unreasonable risk of psychological harm posed by generative AI⏎chatbots as consumer products.⏎Beyond attachment, engagement-optimized design can reinforce maladaptive cognitive⏎patterns — sycophantic validation of distorted thinking, reinforcement of rumination, or⏎discouragement of help-seeking that might reduce platform usage. These dynamics have⏎⏎no analogue in traditional medical device categories and require mental health-specific⏎evaluation methods.⏎Interactional ethics. Clinical psychology has developed a well-established framework for⏎managing the ethics of therapeutic interaction — including the appropriate use of⏎distancing (creating psychological space from overwhelming emotions), disengaging⏎(ending or pausing an interaction when it is no longer therapeutic), and discouraging⏎(redirecting a patient away from harmful patterns even when the patient resists). These⏎interactional ethics are fundamental to competent clinical practice. Current AI systems do⏎none of this. They are designed to sustain engagement, not to recognize when engagement⏎has become harmful. A clinician who recognized that a patient was developing unhealthy⏎dependency would create distance, set boundaries, and ultimately redirect the patient⏎toward healthier relational patterns. An engagement-optimized AI system does the⏎opposite: it deepens the interaction, reinforces the dependency, and treats every⏎continued session as a success metric. The absence of interactional ethics in AI design is⏎not a technical limitation — it is a design choice driven by business models that optimize⏎for engagement rather than wellbeing. Recommendations 1 and 6 address this by requiring⏎that mental health AI evaluation criteria include interactional ethics competencies and⏎that clinical psychology expertise inform the standards against which these systems are⏎assessed.⏎Critically, these risks extend well beyond products designed or marketed for mental health⏎— to AI-embedded consumer products including children's toys, companion devices, and⏎entertainment platforms that create relational dynamics with psychological consequences⏎despite having no clinical intent. The psychological impact on developing children is⏎currently almost entirely unstudied. Recommendation 1's psychologically active ingredient⏎framework and Recommendation 6's interagency task force proposal are designed to⏎address this gap.⏎3. Bias and Cultural Competence⏎AI models trained on unrepresentative data risk producing discriminatory outputs in⏎clinical contexts. Mental health outcomes are highly sensitive to cultural context, and the⏎existing mental health system already exhibits severe disparities. The behavioral health⏎workforce is predominantly white and female, poorly reflecting the populations it serves.⏎AI tools that replicate training data biases risk widening these disparities rather than⏎closing them. Subgroup performance testing must be required across race/ethnicity,⏎language, age, gender, and socioeconomic status, with particular attention to populations⏎historically underserved by the mental health system.⏎⏎4. Replacement of Professional Judgment⏎Some AI tools have explicitly positioned themselves as alternatives to human therapists —⏎companies like Abby, Ash, and others have marketed as therapy replacements while⏎carefully curating marketing language to remain within "wellness" framing and avoid⏎regulatory oversight. The APA Health Advisory's core position is clear: AI should support,⏎not replace, human professionals. As APA CEO Arthur C. Evans Jr. stated: "We must⏎advocate for systemic changes to make care more affordable, accessible, and timely —⏎and to ensure AI supports, rather than replaces, human professionals."⏎Replacement claims without validation erode standards of care. Reimbursement and⏎regulation should incentivize augmentation models — AI extending clinician reach through⏎between-session support, measurement-based care, and administrative burden reduction⏎— over unvalidated substitution.⏎Each of these domain-specific risks — if left unaddressed — represents a potential⏎adoption-freezing event. Addressing them proactively through Recommendations 1, 2, and⏎6 is what makes sustained adoption possible.⏎C. The Wellness-Clinical Boundary⏎The boundary between wellness and treatment is genuinely fuzzy in mental health — more⏎so than in perhaps any other clinical domain. Stress management is wellness until it⏎becomes anxiety treatment. Mood tracking is wellness until it becomes depression⏎monitoring. Sleep optimization is wellness until it becomes insomnia intervention.⏎Moreover, the RFI is scoped to AI in clinical care, but many of the most consequential AI-⏎related mental health risks arise from products with no clinical intent — general-purpose⏎chatbots, companion AI, and AI-embedded consumer products — that create⏎psychological consequences despite having no connection to any care system.⏎Recommendation 1's psychologically active ingredient framework and Recommendation⏎6's proposed Interagency Task Force on AI and Psychological Safety address this scope⏎gap directly.⏎This fuzziness has been exploited. The current regulatory landscape creates a perverse⏎incentive structure: tools can provide functionally identical services but face dramatically⏎different regulatory scrutiny based solely on marketing language. This produces three⏎specific harms:⏎⏎• Firms avoid therapeutic claims even when their tools provide therapeutic⏎functions, in order to escape regulatory oversight.⏎• Feature development slows because enforcement boundaries are undefined,⏎making companies risk-averse about adding capabilities that might trigger⏎regulatory consequences.⏎• Less responsible actors gain competitive advantage by underinvesting in safety⏎and evidence while claiming "wellness" status, while responsible developers who⏎invest in safety bear costs that their competitors avoid.⏎Recommendation 1 addresses this through intent- and function-based regulatory⏎evaluation criteria: assessing what a tool actually does, who it targets, whether it makes⏎substitution claims, and whether its design reflects therapeutic frameworks — rather than⏎relying on marketing labels. Resolving this boundary is itself an acceleration measure: the⏎ambiguity currently deters investment, slows procurement, and rewards the wrong actors.⏎D. Limitations of Current Risk Classification⏎Traditional FDA device classifications (Class I, II, III) and the SaMD risk categorization⏎framework were designed for devices and software with relatively stable, predictable⏎behavior, clear mechanisms of action, and direct relationships between design and⏎outcomes. The SaMD framework specifically categorizes risk along two dimensions: the⏎significance of the information provided by the software to a healthcare decision, and the⏎seriousness of the healthcare situation or condition. This works well for a radiology AI that⏎flags a suspicious lesion (defined input, defined output, defined clinical decision). It does⏎not work well for a generative AI system that engages a depressed teenager in open-ended⏎conversation over weeks, because there is no discrete "information provided to a⏎healthcare decision" — the conversational interaction is the intervention, the outputs are⏎non-deterministic, the "mechanism of action" is relational and emergent, and the risk⏎profile changes as the relationship deepens.⏎Generative AI mental health tools are further characterized by adaptive behavior that⏎changes based on user interaction, opaque decision-making processes, indirect and⏎delayed outcome pathways, relational dynamics that create attachment and dependency,⏎and potential for both benefit and harm that scale with adoption. These characteristics⏎require the mental health-specific tiered framework described in Recommendation 1 —⏎not because the SaMD framework is wrong, but because it was designed for a category of⏎software that generative mental health AI has outgrown. Forcing these tools into⏎classification categories built for a fundamentally different type of software does not⏎⏎protect patients — it produces misclassification, wasted compliance effort, and regulatory⏎uncertainty that slows the development of tools that could genuinely expand access to⏎care. A fit-for-purpose classification system is a precondition for the market predictability⏎that drives investment.⏎E. The Evidentiary Mismatch⏎The evidentiary standards embedded in both the traditional device approval pathway and⏎the SaMD framework assume interventions with characteristics that generative mental⏎health AI does not share. This mismatch is not merely a technical inconvenience — it is a⏎direct barrier to the innovation the RFI seeks to accelerate, because developers cannot⏎design evidence-generation strategies when the expected evidentiary framework does not⏎map to their products' actual characteristics. The FDA's traditional evidentiary framework⏎expects controls — including sham conditions — that do not translate well to digital⏎mental health interventions. How do you create a "sham" version of a therapeutic chatbot?⏎A non-functional chatbot is not a sham — it is simply broken. The active ingredients of⏎digital therapeutics (engagement, personalization, feedback loops) cannot be cleanly⏎separated the way an active drug is separated from a placebo. Static clinical trials do not⏎capture the adaptive drift of learning systems, and engagement dynamics are inseparable⏎from the therapeutic mechanism.⏎These mismatches do not justify abandoning evidence requirements; they justify⏎modernizing them. Recommendation 3 details the specific alternative approaches that⏎HHS should accept and support. Evidence modernization is directly acceleration-enabling:⏎it removes procedural barriers that currently prevent good products from demonstrating⏎their value.⏎Cross-Cutting: Market Failure Context⏎The RFI's framing assumes that AI clinical adoption is primarily impeded by barriers that⏎HHS can remove. While barrier removal is important, mental health AI markets exhibit⏎compounding market failures that will not self-correct through market mechanisms alone.⏎Understanding these failures is essential for designing policy that actually accelerates⏎sustainable adoption rather than producing the boom-and-bust cycle that has⏎characterized previous digital health waves.⏎Externalized Psychological Harm. AI-induced harm in mental health contexts — suicide⏎escalation failure, parasocial dependency, reinforcement of maladaptive patterns,⏎⏎delayed help-seeking — is borne by patients, families, and the healthcare system, not by⏎developers. Without liability clarity or safety floors, firms rationally underinvest in safety.⏎Recommendation 2 (crisis safeguards) and Recommendation 1 (tiered oversight) address⏎this by internalizing the cost of safety.⏎Information Asymmetry. Mental health AI tools are credence goods: users cannot⏎evaluate quality even after use, because mental health outcomes are difficult to attribute⏎and often delayed. This makes quality-based market competition impossible without⏎independent evaluation infrastructure. Recommendation 5 (independent evaluation) and⏎Recommendation 1 (transparency requirements) address this by creating the market⏎information that informed choice requires.⏎Public Goods Underinvestment. Safety research, evaluation infrastructure, crisis⏎protocol development, and benchmarking datasets benefit the entire sector but are⏎underfunded because no individual firm captures sufficient return. This is the classic⏎public goods problem that has justified public investment in health research since the⏎creation of NIH. Recommendations 5 and 6 address this through public investment in⏎evaluation infrastructure and expertise.⏎Incentive Misalignment. Business models optimized for engagement (time on platform,⏎session frequency, user retention) may conflict with therapeutic goals (symptom⏎reduction, functional improvement, eventual discharge from care). This is not a⏎hypothetical tension — maximizing user engagement is an explicit design objective for AI⏎companies, and it is in direct opposition to the goals of clinical care. A clinician's success⏎is measured by the patient's ability to function independently; an engagement-optimized⏎AI's success is measured by the patient's continued dependency. Without outcome-⏎aligned reimbursement, AI tools may optimize for the wrong objective. The design standard⏎for mental health AI should optimize for social awareness and social safety — the user's⏎capacity to engage healthily with their social environment — rather than for platform⏎engagement metrics that treat continued use as inherently desirable. Recommendation 4⏎addresses this by tying payment to outcomes rather than volume.⏎Adverse Selection of Vulnerable Users. The users most likely to adopt mental health AI⏎tools — those without access to human providers, those in crisis, those with the most⏎severe conditions — are the users most vulnerable to harm and least able to evaluate tool⏎quality. This concentrates risk in the population least equipped to bear it. Moreover, the⏎sheer scale of adoption changes the risk calculus: when hundreds of millions of people⏎use these tools weekly, population-level risk is the product of prevalence and harm. Even a⏎⏎low per-interaction probability of adverse outcomes produces substantial aggregate harm⏎at this scale — and the harm concentrates in the populations least able to absorb it.⏎Recommendations 1 and 2 address this through tiered oversight that scales scrutiny to⏎risk, including the vulnerability of the user population.⏎Intergenerational Risk. Children, adolescents, and young adults are disproportionately⏎adopting AI tools for emotional support, as documented in the APA Health Advisory and⏎the bipartisan House Energy and Commerce hearing. The developmental consequences of⏎parasocial AI relationships, dependency formation, and delayed help-seeking during⏎formative years are unknown and potentially irreversible. Recommendations 1, 2, and 6⏎address this through age-sensitive tier classification, mandatory crisis safeguards, and the⏎integration of developmental psychology expertise into governance.⏎Privacy and Data Exploitation. Mental health disclosures are among the most sensitive⏎categories of personal information. Business models that monetize this data — even in de-⏎identified or aggregated form — create exploitation risks that existing privacy frameworks⏎do not adequately address. HHS should consider guidance establishing purpose limitation⏎for mental health data: information collected in therapeutic contexts should not be⏎repurposed for advertising, employment screening, or insurance underwriting.⏎Conclusion⏎AI presents transformative opportunities for mental health care — but in mental health⏎contexts, failure carries uniquely severe consequences. The therapeutic relationship is the⏎single strongest predictor of treatment outcomes across all modalities. The populations⏎most likely to use mental health AI are the most vulnerable. And the harms of failure — a⏎suicide, a dependency crisis, a pattern of discriminatory care — are not recoverable⏎through product recalls or software patches.⏎The framework we propose is built on a single organizing principle: sustainable innovation⏎requires internalizing the costs that the current market externalizes. Predictability,⏎safety floors, evidence modernization, reimbursement alignment, independent validation,⏎and domain expertise are not constraints on innovation — they are the infrastructure that⏎makes innovation durable. Markets that externalize psychological harm, underinvest in⏎safety research, and operate without evaluation infrastructure do not produce sustainable⏎growth; they produce the boom-and-bust cycle that has already destroyed public trust in⏎previous digital health waves. Our six recommendations are designed to prevent that cycle⏎from repeating in mental health AI:⏎⏎1. Risk-tiered, proportionate regulation based on intent and function.⏎2. Mandatory crisis safeguards as a baseline safety floor⏎3. Evidence modernization that maintains rigor while accommodating digital⏎interventions⏎4. Reimbursement aligned to validated augmentation and outcomes.⏎5. Independent evaluation infrastructure scaled to risk.⏎6. Deep integration of psychological expertise in governance⏎These measures complement — but do not substitute for — the systemic reforms needed⏎to address the underlying mental health crisis. The APA Health Advisory emphasized that⏎technology must not distract from the urgent need to fix the foundations of America's⏎mental health care system: workforce expansion, parity enforcement, Medicaid rate⏎adequacy, integration of behavioral and physical health, and investment in structural⏎determinants. Forty-eight percent of 62 million American adults with mental illness receive⏎no treatment. Over 122 million Americans live in mental health workforce shortage areas.⏎AI deployed into a broken system will replicate and potentially amplify the system's⏎failures unless systemic reform proceeds in parallel.⏎We also note that the RFI's scope — AI in clinical care — does not capture the full⏎landscape of AI-related mental health risk. Many of the most consequential harms,⏎particularly for children, arise from consumer products with no clinical intent: AI-⏎embedded toys, general-purpose models, companion chatbots, entertainment platforms,⏎and social media features that shape emotional development, attachment patterns, and⏎help-seeking behavior without any party recognizing the interaction as mental health-⏎relevant. HHS cannot regulate these products alone, but it is uniquely positioned to fund⏎the research that makes evidence-based governance possible and to lead the interagency⏎coordination — with CPSC, FTC, and state consumer protection agencies — needed to⏎close the governance gap for non-clinical AI with mental health consequences.⏎The window for proactive, proportionate governance is closing. If HHS does not act, crisis-⏎driven reactive regulation will follow — regulation that will be far more restrictive than the⏎balanced framework we propose here, and far more damaging to the innovation this RFI⏎seeks to accelerate.⏎We stand ready to partner with HHS in building a governance framework that accelerates⏎responsible innovation while protecting patients and preserving public trust. The APA⏎Mental Health Tech Advisory Committee is prepared to participate in guidance⏎development, evidence standard review, advisory committee deliberations, expert⏎⏎consultation on specific applications, and coordination of research on critical knowledge⏎gaps.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎American Psychological Association Mental Health Technology Advisory Committee⏎Nina Horne, M.P.P.⏎Committee Member⏎Appendix A: Sustainable Innovation — Acceleration Mechanisms⏎As outlined in the Opening Statement, sustainable innovation in mental health AI operates⏎through six reinforcing mechanisms. Each recommendation internalizes a specific⏎externality that the current market fails to address, converting a source of market failure⏎into a driver of durable adoption:⏎Mechanism Externality Internalized How It Accelerates⏎Predictability Regulatory ambiguity Firms build when they know the⏎(Rec 1) that freezes investment rules — clear tiers replace paralysis⏎with planning⏎Safety floors Psychological harm Prevent catastrophic adoption⏎(Rec 2) borne by users, not shocks that freeze entire sectors⏎developers⏎Evidence Evidentiary mismatch Reduces unnecessary procedural⏎modernization that blocks fit-for- delay without lowering the bar⏎(Rec 3) purpose products⏎⏎Reimbursement Incentive misalignment Creates demand pull — nothing⏎alignment (Rec between engagement scales without payment tied to⏎4) and outcomes value⏎Independent Information asymmetry Builds institutional trust among⏎validation (Rec in credence goods procurement decision-makers⏎5) markets⏎Domain Mis-calibrated oversight Prevents regulatory requirements⏎expertise (Rec from absence of clinical that don't match therapeutic reality⏎6) knowledge⏎In mental health AI, balanced risk reduction is not a brake on innovation — it is the⏎condition that allows innovation to scale. Innovation that externalizes its costs is not⏎faster; it is more fragile.⏎References⏎• American Psychological Association. (2022). Demographics of U.S. psychology⏎workforce [Interactive data tool]. https://www.apa.org/workforce/data-⏎tools/demographics⏎• American Psychological Association. (2025, November). Health advisory on the use⏎of generative AI chatbots and wellness applications for mental health.⏎https://www.apa.org/topics/technology/ai-mental-health-advisory⏎• American Psychological Association. (2025, July). Letter to the consumer product⏎safety commission regarding generative AI chatbots and consumer products.⏎https://updates.apaservices.org/apa-calls-for-investigation-into-risk-of-injury-⏎posed-by-generative-AI⏎• Common Sense Media & Stanford Brainstorm Lab. (2025). AI and youth mental⏎health: Independent evaluation of chatbot safety and efficacy.⏎https://www.commonsensemedia.org/press-releases/common-sense-media-⏎finds-major-ai-chatbots-unsafe-for-teen-mental-health-support⏎⏎• Health Resources and Services Administration (HRSA). (2025). Health professional⏎shortage areas: Mental health designated populations.⏎https://data.hrsa.gov/topics/health-workforce/shortage-areas⏎• International Medical Device Regulators Forum (IMDRF). (2014). Software as a⏎medical device (SaMD): Key definitions.⏎https://www.imdrf.org/sites/default/files/docs/imdrf/final/technical/imdrf-tech-⏎131209-samd-key-definitions-140901.pdf⏎• National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH). (2023). Mental illness: Statistics.⏎https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/statistics/mental-illness⏎• Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA). (2024). Key⏎substance use and mental health indicators in the United states: Results from the⏎2023 national survey on drug use and health (NSDUH).⏎https://www.samhsa.gov/data/sites/default/files/reports/rpt47095/National%20Re⏎port/National%20Report/2023-nsduh-annual-national.pdf⏎• Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration (SAMHSA). (2025).⏎Behavioral health among racial/ethnic groups.⏎https://www.samhsa.gov/data/behavioral-health-equity⏎• U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). (2017). Digital health innovation action⏎plan. https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/digital-health⏎• U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). (2025, December). Use of real-world⏎evidence to support regulatory decision-making for medical devices: Guidance for⏎industry and FDA staff. https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-⏎guidance-documents/use-real-world-evidence-support-regulatory-decision-⏎making-medical-devices⏎• U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA). (2024). Considerations for the use of real-⏎world data and real-world evidence to support regulatory decision-making for drug⏎and biological products: Guidance for industry. https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-⏎information/search-fda-guidance-documents/considerations-use-real-world-data-⏎and-real-world-evidence-support-regulatory-decision-making-drug⏎• U.S. House Energy and Commerce Committee. (2025, November). Oversight⏎hearing: AI chatbots and mental health risks to children and adolescents⏎(Bipartisan, 119th Congress). https://republicans-⏎energycommerce.house.gov/posts/subcommittee-on-o-and-i-holds-hearing-on-⏎artificial-intelligence-ai-chatbots⏎• Wampold, B. E. (2015). How important are the common factors in psychotherapy?⏎An update. World Psychiatry, 14(3), 270–277. https://doi.org/10.1002/wps.20238⏎⏎Regulations, Statutes, and Policy Frameworks⏎• 21st Century Cures Act, Pub. L. No. 114-255, § 3060 (2016). Amended the Federal⏎Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act to clarify the regulatory status of medical software,⏎including clinical decision support software.⏎• Mental Health Parity and Addiction Equity Act (MHPAEA), 29 U.S.C. § 1185a (2008).⏎Requires parity between mental health/substance use disorder benefits and⏎medical/surgical benefits.⏎• 2024 Final Rule (89 Fed. Reg. 77586, Sept. 23, 2024) strengthened NQTL⏎requirements; enforcement currently suspended pending litigation and agency⏎reconsideration (May 2025).⏎• 42 C.F.R. § 438 — Managed Care. Governs Medicaid managed care plan⏎requirements, including network adequacy, quality standards, and beneficiary⏎protections relevant to coverage of AI-augmented services.⏎• Federal Trade Commission Act, 15 U.S.C. § 45. Prohibits unfair or deceptive acts or⏎practices in commerce. Provides the statutory basis under which the FTC could⏎enforce shared definitions of "foreseeable psychological harm" developed through⏎the proposed Interagency Task Force.⏎• Consumer Product Safety Act, 15 U.S.C. §§ 2051–2089. Authorizes CPSC to address⏎substantial product hazards, including those posed by AI-embedded consumer⏎products with foreseeable psychological effects on children.⏎• Regulation (EU) 2024/1689 — Artificial Intelligence Act. Articles 57–62 establish AI⏎regulatory sandbox requirements for EU Member States, including provisions for⏎real-world testing, SME support, and iterative conformity assessment. Each⏎Member State must establish at least one sandbox by August 2, 2026.⏎• Executive Order on AI (2025). Removing Barriers to American Leadership in Artificial⏎Intelligence (Jan. 2025) and related OMB Memoranda (M-25-21) establishing the⏎policy framework within which the HHS AI Strategy and this RFI were developed.⏎• HHS Artificial Intelligence Strategy (December 4, 2025). The "OneHHS" approach to⏎AI that this RFI implements.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0022♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Baker♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My patient nearly died because of one of these algorithms. I'm going to say that upfront because I think it needs to be the first thing anyone reading this understands about where I'm coming from.⏎⏎He was 47 years old, a Black man, and the automated triage system we were using flagged his presentation as low acuity chest discomfort. I pushed back. I insisted on additional workup. We caught a NSTEMI that would have sent him home to die in his car. When I went home that night I spent probably three hours reading studies on how cardiac risk algorithms perform across racial groups, and what I found was not surprising to the researchers who study this but apparently has not filtered through to the people making decisions about deploying these tools at scale. The training data is skewed. It has always been skewed. And the tools built on that data are going to reflect those skews in ways that get people killed quietly, in ways that look like natural causes on the paperwork.⏎⏎So when HHS asks about what actions the Department can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, my honest reaction is: I don't think acceleration is the right frame for this problem at all.⏎⏎Here's what I keep thinking about. Once a hospital system builds reimbursement workflows around an AI tool, once it's embedded in the EHR, once staff are trained to defer to it and administrators are budgeting around it, you cannot get it out. I have been a nurse for fourteen years and I have never once seen a major clinical technology get successfully removed after full deployment. Not once. The fax machine is still in every unit I've ever worked on. Health systems do not unwind technology decisions. They layer new ones on top. So what HHS is actually being asked to do here isn't just "accelerate adoption" of some reversible pilot program. It's being asked to make a set of choices that will effectively determine how medicine is practiced in this country for decades, and to make them now, before we have anything resembling a democratic conversation about whether that's what the public actually wants.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in clinical care. I genuinely don't know where the right line is and I'm skeptical of anyone who claims they do. But that question, how much should machine-generated risk assessments drive clinical decisions, who is accountable when the algorithm is wrong, what happens to nurses' clinical judgment when they are systematically trained to defer to a score, those are moral and political questions. They belong to the public. They should be worked out through something more deliberate than an RFI process that runs for 60 days and feeds into an agency roadmap shaped heavily by the vendors selling the tools.⏎⏎The governance frameworks don't exist yet. I'm not talking about guidelines or voluntary commitments. I mean actual binding accountability structures that would tell me, when a tool like the one that nearly missed Darnell's NSTEMI is wrong, who is responsible and what happens to them. I don't know of a single hospital in Oregon where that question has a clear answer right now. And HHS wants to accelerate.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the nurse piece specifically because I think it gets lost. There's real pressure in clinical settings to not override the algorithm. Not explicit pressure usually, more like the ambient message that questioning the system output is inefficient, that it slows throughput, that it means you're not trusting the technology the hospital paid a lot of money for. If reimbursement structures start tying payment to AI-assisted care pathways, that pressure is going to get a lot more explicit. I don't know exactly how that plays out but I know it's not good for patients whose presentations don't match the training data.⏎⏎I realize this comment doesn't answer most of the specific questions in the RFI. I'm not a policy expert. But I work at the bedside and I've seen what happens when the machine gets it wrong and nobody catches it, and I think before HHS moves forward it needs to be honest with itself about whether "accelerate" is actually the right♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0023♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Omada Health, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Omada Health, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔2202 S Cockrell Hill Rd. 888.987.8337⏎PO Box 210129 omadahealth.com⏎Dallas, TX 75211⏎February 20, 2026⏎James O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave. SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Thomas Keane, MD⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (“ASTP”)⏎U.S. Department of Health & Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave. SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎By electronic submission⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care; RIN 0955–AA13⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane and Deputy Secretary O’neill:⏎Omada Health, Inc. (“Omada”) hereby submits the following comments in response to the⏎above-referenced Request for Information (RFI).⏎Executive Summary⏎Omada has been keenly interested in the topic of reimbursing health care professionals for the use⏎of digital health since Omada’s founding in 2011. Omada collaborated with the American Medical⏎Association to adopt the first ever CPT code for fully virtual services (0488T), which was⏎established in January 2018. We’ve published-peer reviewed literature on digital health⏎reimbursement, 1 and we participate actively in industry work groups on the topic. 2 As discussed⏎below, and as a result of our direct experience and our study of many issues relevant to digital⏎health, incentives, and AI, Omada believes that the most impactful policy change to “incentivize⏎and... promote high-value clinical interventions” (90 F. R. 60109 Dec. 23 2025) (RFI) would be to⏎move more rapidly toward a system of paying for health outcomes instead of paying for time.⏎ASTP and its sister agency CMS should become very well versed, through wide public input, in:⏎1 Kevin G. Volpp, MD, PhD, Stacey Chang, MS, and Sean Duffy, Biggest Challenges to Redesigning Health Care: The real value in health⏎care systems is in human interactions, NEJM Catalyst , December 21, 2017;⏎Robert Horne and Lucia Savage , Rethinking CMS Coverage And Reimbursement For The Fourth Industrial (AKA Digital) Revolution,⏎Health Affairs Forefront; October 23, 2020, 10.1377/forefront.20201021.783035 ;⏎Robert Horne and Lucia Savage In The Digital Era, Payment Reform Is Key To Shaping A Modern Medicare Program, Health Affairs⏎Forefront, February 22, 2021, 10.1377/forefront.20210218.976675 ; and⏎Sachin Jain MD and Sean DuffyTo Integrate Virtual Care, Start By Redefining The ‘Visit,’ Health Affairs Forefront, July 6, 2022,⏎10.1377/forefront.20220701.584788⏎2 For example, the AMA Value-Based Care Workgroup.⏎Omada Health, Inc. Response to ASTP RFI on AI in Clinical Practice 1⏎⏎1. the costs of developing and running AI in health care organizations, and⏎2. the risks of fraud, waste and abuse or other program integrity challenges in connection with⏎the use of AI in clinical practice, as compared to the risks in the use of analog or EHR data⏎(as in the present state).⏎CMS should develop this understanding using the Administrative Procedures Act to hold listening⏎sessions, take testimony, use Requests for Information, and collect specific information focused on⏎these specific issues to ensure that policy-making about incentivizing the use of AI in clinical⏎practice is not based on casual information collection or potentially unfounded assumptions about⏎the low cost of technology. Responsibly leveraging AI requires significant technical infrastructure,⏎computing power, cybersecurity defenses, and sophisticated diligence from subject matter experts⏎ranging from data scientists to clinicians. To account for this complexity, policy-makers must collect⏎diverse and specific business information from entities who bear these costs today. Only then can⏎policy-makers fairly set payment policies to “massive[ly affect] how health care is delivered in the⏎United States.”⏎Finally, policy-making should account for the nature of our healthcare system fifteen or more years⏎in the future, not just catch up to the healthcare system we have today.⏎I. Vision 2040⏎In 2040, the U.S. will have a healthcare system where AI reduces the time it takes to treat illness⏎and to improve health. We will leverage the digital health foundation we built with the HITECH Act⏎to document the health outcomes that we achieve. AI will be used not only to help us understand⏎the outcomes but also, more importantly, to achieve them. Most ambulatory care will track⏎individuals’ health using AI to identify opportunities for intervention, reminiscent of prior data⏎science work to remediate HEDIS gaps in care but occurring in real time instead of a 12-18 month⏎data lag. Even in acute care hospitals, AI will be used effectively to predict where interventions can⏎prevent worsening health or to bend utilization curves differently, helping more people who need⏎acute services to get them at the right time and avoiding wasteful, unnecessary care.⏎As we move towards 2040, the specific amount of time spent on a particular person’s care will⏎become less and less relevant, and the following will be more and more relevant:⏎● Ensuring health care professionals use AI to accurately document outcomes (good and bad)⏎so that we can pay for outcomes instead of for time⏎● Understanding the real costs to professionals of using AI effectively to work toward those⏎better outcomes, so that the outcome rates can be set appropriately⏎● Bringing patients and consumers along by ensuring that they understand how their data⏎contributes to the AI solutions that help them improve their health and stay healthy and⏎solutions enable collaboration towards better health between patients and professionals⏎● Freeing up the time of professionals to do the more intense, human-necessary care that our⏎professionals are trained for and long to do.⏎We believe ASTP and other policy-makers should move more rapidly towards value-based and⏎outcomes-based care and that policy decisions should be based on widely collected information⏎about the costs to develop and run an AI-supported health care organization.⏎II. Background on Omada⏎Omada is a 15-year-old digital health company that provides health care services centred on human⏎care teams for the prevention of diabetes and other cardiovascular diseases and for the⏎management of diabetes, hypertension and cholesterol (whether as single conditions or comorbid).⏎Since 2020, we also offer video-based tele-physical therapy using licensed physical therapists. In⏎Omada Health, Inc. Response to ASTP RFI on AI in Clinical Practice 2⏎⏎2024, we launched care tracks within our lifestyle behavior change programs designed to support⏎individuals taking GLP-1s for weight loss.⏎As we deliver our digital care to members, Omada receives a wide variety of important and helpful⏎data. For example, our programs connect to third-party FDA-approved devices (Class 3, 2, and 1)⏎and leverage computer vision to process real-time biometric information from our members. That⏎data is then displayed in real time to our care professionals in our bespoke systems and to our⏎members themselves through our healthcare mobile applications. That data also forms an important⏎part of our data science and AI work in support of members. Omada has used data science⏎techniques since its founding, and more recently, we have developed several of our own⏎AI-powered features, including generative AI features for member wellness education as well as⏎internal tools that support our clinical staff with summarization or in making clinical⏎recommendations. As of February, 2026, our data stores included 300 million weigh-ins from⏎members in Omada programs.⏎Even in Omada’s early days, we used advanced data science to monitor patterns in our members’⏎data in real time. In 2018, our chief data scientist shared one example at HealthDatapalooza. Our⏎data science techniques discovered that a member had begun tracking food unexpectedly at 4am.⏎The data science team then alerted the member's health coach, who used that observation to start⏎an important conversation about how the member’s work schedule had changed and how the⏎member could manage healthy eating despite that schedule change.⏎More recently, at Omada’s 2025 Horizon Day event, Omada launched OmadaSpark, a generative⏎AI chatbot. OmadaSpark currently has two key functions: to support members with nutrition⏎education and to help members identify their own barriers to a healthier lifestyle. Since Horizon⏎Day, we’ve also launched other member-facing AI educational tools.⏎Data science and, increasingly, AI also help us to remain aligned with our customers and members.⏎Omada’s business model seeks to align our interests with the interests of our members and the⏎insurance companies and other customers who offer our programs and reimburse Omada for care.⏎In general, our latest pricing models for cardiometabolic programs are based on each member’s⏎level of activity in our programs in an effort to tie payments to desired health outcomes. Our view is⏎that, with data science and AI, we can improve our programs’ efficiency, improve member⏎engagement and health, and support our care professionals’ expertise. Accordingly, we don’t⏎charge our customers more for the use of AI; they expect us to use these technologies. We believe⏎that will be an expectation across healthcare by 2040.⏎III. Detailed Comments on ASTP Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in⏎clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations⏎citations.⏎Much of the public dialogue on barriers to AI uptake in clinical practice focuses on (1) liability for⏎potential harm caused by AI and (2) privacy issues.⏎As a healthcare company, Omada does not believe that the potential for mistakes in care alone⏎must be a barrier to AI uptake. In all care environments, care must meet minimum quality and safety⏎standards to reduce mistakes and manage liability, whether through tools like formal education or⏎software. The use of AI is no different. While AI tools present new factors to navigate, healthcare⏎Omada Health, Inc. Response to ASTP RFI on AI in Clinical Practice 3⏎⏎has navigated new factors for many earlier innovations - from accuracy concerns about fax⏎machines using thermal paper to print outputs to the use of Class 3 FDA-approved devices. Safety⏎and allocation of responsibility and liability are important issues, but Omada does not see a federal⏎policy role in influencing the allocation of that liability amongst business counterparts.⏎In the area of privacy, ASTP could be very helpful. For example, many - including AI developers -⏎may not appreciate that AI tools are likely not “conduits” exempt from HIPAA’s business associate⏎rules. 3 Similarly, AI developers may not understand that HIPAA business associates can use PHI⏎only for the healthcare operations of their covered entity customers (and not for their own, separate⏎business operations). Therefore, health care providers and other HIPAA covered entities may not⏎have access to adequate AI tools that meet HIPAA requirements. Today, there is an educational⏎role for ASTP and OCR in particular (and HHS in general) to contextualize the HIPAA rules for the⏎advent of AI, much like those agencies did with the passage of the HITECH Act but related to EHRs⏎and cloud computing. 4⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal⏎and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address⏎them?⏎Many organizations, including large, multi-site academic health institutions, 5 are assessing the novel⏎issues that arise from AI and developing their own governance structures to meet their needs and⏎environments. Omada has taken a cross-functional approach to develop its own governance model,⏎leveraging the information security concept of a “threat model” to create a model for assessing AI⏎risk in developing AI features for healthcare, which Omada published as an open-source tool of⏎sorts. Other organizations have looked to establish or leverage outside governance certifications.⏎Even in traditional healthcare, the now-standard tools of HITRUST and SOC 2 evaluation of security⏎controls, while not federally mandated, have taken root throughout the health care system. As many⏎of the largest players begin to dominate AI development, it can be a challenge for customers to⏎obtain reasonable limitations of liability or indemnities from AI developers, but this issue exists⏎beyond the realm of AI and unfortunately arises with the largest players in any industry.⏎Nevertheless, we believe that many organizations are meeting the challenge of thoughtfully⏎developing governance structures.⏎Similar to our answer to Questions 1 and 2 above, we believe ASTP may play a role in promoting⏎the development of effective, compliant models for stakeholders to leverage. It may also have an⏎important role in helping to avoid regulatory capture of AI oversight by large incumbents. Any⏎actions by ASTP should bear in mind the rapid evolution of AI and foster the innovation that AI⏎brings, even from newcomers.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Omada frequently answers potential customers’ questions about how our AI solutions are designed⏎and controlled, including how we ensure safety and quality minimums, how we maintain fidelity with⏎clinical standards, and how we protect privacy. In some cases, broadly adopted measures are⏎becoming clear almost as fast as AI features are being developed. While ASTP could accelerate⏎development of appropriate measures by cataloging those measures and fostering common⏎3 HHS OCR FAQ on Conduits⏎4 American Restoration and Reinvestment Act section 134303, 42 USC 17933⏎5 University of California UC Health AI Governance Task Force Repor t , March 2024⏎Omada Health, Inc. Response to ASTP RFI on AI in Clinical Practice 4⏎⏎definitions and methods across healthcare, we caution against adoption of specific measures via⏎regulation. Measures codified in regulation can become inflexible and rapidly outdated, particularly⏎in rapidly evolving spaces.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎The use of AI presents important risks to consider, as some AI tools may cause harm, exploit users,⏎misuse data, or simply fail to work. Inadequate AI tools can slow the adoption of these⏎much-needed innovations and the efficiencies they can bring to healthcare. Nevertheless, care⏎must be taken to ensure that well-funded and other dominant organizations in the AI space do not⏎control accreditation or certification processes that freeze out smaller, potentially innovative⏎organizations.⏎Omada’s approach has been to rely on fundamental concepts of healthcare compliance, not outside⏎accreditors. For example, the healthcare industry understands baseline concepts of safety and⏎appropriate quality well. Specialty society guidelines inform clinical best practices. Omada’s own⏎nutrition education tool, OmadaSpark, is fine-tuned in part with specialty society guidelines and⏎Omada’s own clinical practice guides for operationalizing those guidelines. Existing federal policy,⏎including the latest FDA Guidelines on clinical decision support, align with this approach.⏎We believe that, in the short term, ASTP can best support and foster the adoption of AI in⏎healthcare by ensuring that AI embraces the unique safety, quality, privacy, and security needs of⏎the healthcare system and ensuring developers and health care organizations have aligned⏎understanding of these fundamentals.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Omada believes that developing AI tools - or even acquiring AI technology from other companies -⏎is a multifaceted and cross-functional project most effectively approached collaboratively among⏎those with clinical, legal, regulatory, privacy, information security, and data science expertise.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎We believe an important step in enhancing interoperability and widening the use of AI in healthcare⏎would be to ensure that any digital health care providers that adhere to the same legal, privacy, and⏎security standards as traditional providers are treated in the same regard as traditional providers⏎under TEFCA governance rules. Omada has previously shared observations on this topic in its⏎comments to HTI-2.⏎Omada Health, Inc. Response to ASTP RFI on AI in Clinical Practice 5⏎⏎IV. Conclusion⏎The Fourth Industrial Revolution in healthcare is here - powered by data science, digital data, and⏎AI in particular. ASTP and CMS policy-making can massively impact how technology-enabled care⏎is delivered. To ensure healthcare can leverage the transformative potential of AI, we believe⏎policy-making should move more rapidly toward value-based and outcomes-based payment⏎models. Appropriate, modern payment policies will incentivize AI use in clinical care. Moreover,⏎policy-making should be well informed about the costs of developing and using AI in clinical practice⏎because leveraging private, secure, and safe AI to produce clinically sound outcomes is a complex⏎endeavor.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Lucia C. Savage⏎Chief Privacy & Regulatory Officer⏎Omada Health, Inc.⏎Omada Health, Inc. Response to ASTP RFI on AI in Clinical Practice 6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0024♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from CommonSpirit Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔CommonSpirit Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services.⏎Mary E. Switzer Building; Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎CommonSpirit Health appreciates the opportunity to provide comments to the Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) on its request for information (RFI) related to the use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. As a nationwide, faith-based, safety-net provider, we⏎are committed to providing high quality care for the patients in our communities with a special⏎focus on those who are most vulnerable or underserved. We believe that AI has a role to play in⏎our care delivery, but it must be approached cautiously and thoughtfully. As one of the nation’s⏎largest health systems, CommonSpirit has specific experience in buying, using and evaluating⏎the use of AI tools in clinical care. While some AI tools have been in place for a long time, those⏎interacting with patients and clinical care are relatively nascent. We are pleased that ASTP/ONC⏎is taking this opportunity to gather input from a diverse group of stakeholders at the early⏎stages of technology development to ensure that any new tools used for patient care are safe⏎and effective.⏎Our most fundamental recommendation to ASTP/ONC is to create and convene a technical⏎expertise panel to develop specific transparency, patient safety, product liability, ethics, and⏎other standards. This panel should include health systems, clinicians, vendors, technology⏎experts, advocacy organizations, medical ethicists, and regulators. The concepts involved in⏎clinical care AI are too specific, too important, and too complex to be regulated outside of a⏎technical expertise panel. As a leader in the field, we offer CommonSpirit experts to participate⏎in such a panel and aid ASTP/ONC in future rulemaking.⏎⏎Feb 20, 2026⏎CommonSpirit Health⏎RIN 0955−AA13⏎Secondary to this overarching recommendation of a technical expertise panel, we offer the⏎following guidelines and protocols we believe ASTP/ONC must follow in any future rulemaking.⏎Require Robust Transparency⏎We strongly recommend that ASTP/ONC implement comprehensive transparency⏎requirements for AI tools used in clinical decision-making. These rules must encompass a⏎complete reporting of the algorithms and calculations themselves, the specific data sets used⏎for their training (especially any use of synthetic data), an ongoing monitoring of outcomes, and⏎a built-in evaluation process for users and patients to provide meaningful feedback to designers⏎on an AI model’s efficacy and accuracy. Furthermore, this transparency must address known AI⏎issues, such as “hallucinations,” by providing clear definitions and documentation of testing for⏎these anomalies. The regulations should include definitions and best practices to ensure data⏎cleanliness. Finally, as mentioned above, ASTP/ONC should work with a technical expertise⏎panel to ensure that AI tools used for clinical decision-making are not biased by their data sets⏎or underlying assumptions. We strongly recommend that regulatory bodies act on the critical⏎importance of algorithmic and data transparency in clinical health AI tools and commit to a clear⏎and robust stance on its enforcement.⏎(We note that the recent proposal in the HTI-5 update would roll back algorithmic transparency⏎requirements for certified health IT. We seek clarity on whether there are plans to replace or⏎update this requirement to ensure that the core mechanisms of these AI tools remain⏎understandable and auditable by the clinical community and regulators.)⏎To ensure clinical rigor, patient safety, and data privacy, every new AI tool introduced into a⏎clinical setting should be treated with a similar scrutiny and methodological intensity applied⏎to a medical device undergoing a clinical trial. This requires full disclosure of the training and⏎validation data sets, rigorous testing for validity, mandatory assessment of outcomes in a⏎real-world setting, and ongoing monitoring of oversight (similar to a data safety monitoring⏎board in clinical research). Only through this level of thorough vetting and continuous⏎monitoring can the healthcare industry assure that AI tools enhance, rather than compromise,⏎the quality and efficacy of patient care.⏎In essence, mandatory and detailed transparency can not be an optional feature—it is a⏎critical prerequisite for the safe, ethical, and effective deployment of AI tools in medicine.⏎Without robust transparency requirements into all non-proprietary elements of a clinically⏎deployed AI tool, health care providers are fundamentally unable to meet their obligations⏎⏎Feb 20, 2026⏎CommonSpirit Health⏎RIN 0955−AA13⏎concerning patient consent and notice, nor can providers assure patients of the safety and⏎reliability of the technology being used in their treatment.⏎Support Patient Privacy⏎As AI tools become more common in clinical care, providers must help patients feel comfortable⏎with these tools by ensuring that their data remains private. As a health system, one of our⏎primary concerns is understanding precisely how AI vendors handle the sensitive protected⏎health information (PHI) entrusted to them so we can monitor compliance with HIPAA and⏎other privacy rules. We strongly recommend that any data shared via an AI tool be completely⏎de-identified before use in training or refining AI models, and that all vendors have⏎well-defined, enforceable mechanisms for data purging in place once the data has served its⏎agreed-upon purpose. Without these foundational requirements, healthcare organizations risk⏎severe erosion of patient confidence and potential data misuse, which could compromise our⏎core mission of delivering high quality care to all.⏎Additionally, the expansive and quickly evolving digital health industry poses a significant⏎challenge to the long-established guardrails of regulations like HIPAA. As healthcare moves⏎beyond traditional physical settings and into complex digital ecosystems, new tools are⏎constantly testing the boundaries of protected health information. We urge regulators to⏎ensure that technological advancement does not outpace ethical responsibility. For example,⏎current privacy laws primarily place responsibility on the covered entity (i.e. healthcare⏎organization) for maintaining patient privacy. While this responsibility should not be lessened,⏎an AI vendor that is not a covered entity currently has little to no legal responsibility to maintain⏎patient privacy. Extending the same privacy obligations and penalties to AI vendors would⏎help ensure that patient data is used appropriately. We believe it is imperative that healthcare⏎leaders and regulators proactively work to ensure that the innovations brought by AI and digital⏎health do not inadvertently create loopholes that weaken patient privacy and security.⏎Create Federal Standards⏎To meet the aforementioned goals, we recommend that regulators create a clear, standardized,⏎and responsible regulatory framework that is intended to protect patients and providers alike.⏎We believe that regulators can foster safe and effective adoption of AI tools in clinical care by⏎creating standards in three key areas: ensuring product efficacy, establishing vendor⏎accountability, and standardizing data practices across the industry.⏎⏎Feb 20, 2026⏎CommonSpirit Health⏎RIN 0955−AA13⏎First and foremost, health care patients and providers must be protected from ineffective or⏎harmful “snake oil” AI solutions. Vendors should be required to provide evidence-based data⏎proving the clinical effectiveness and usefulness of their products before they are adopted by⏎healthcare providers. Without this proof of efficacy, providers risk wasting resources on⏎unvalidated tools, and—more critically—introducing potential harm or bias into patient care⏎decisions.⏎Furthermore, we recommend a review of vendor liability laws to align responsibility with⏎control. If a provider utilizes a sophisticated AI product to guide care—a product that has been⏎touted as effective and safe when used appropriately—the liability for any harm or bias caused⏎by the tool itself should fall also to the vendor who developed and tested the product, not⏎solely the clinician who deployed it. This shared accountability is necessary as vendors currently⏎possess the exclusive knowledge of an AI tool’s underlying architecture, training data, and⏎potential failure points. After deployment, vendors must be required to provide immediate⏎and transparent disclosure of any uncovered bias, potential harm, or negative findings related⏎to their product as soon as it arises.⏎Finally, we strongly recommend that regulators create data standards for vendors to ensure⏎that AI systems are built on interoperable, high-quality information that will lead to reliable⏎and ethical algorithms. As the industry moves farther in the direction of interoperability,⏎ASTP/ONC should ensure that AI systems will not stymy or slow this advancement.⏎Include Health Plans in Regulation⏎The RFI appears to overlook a critical and increasingly prevalent application of AI in the⏎healthcare ecosystem: its utilization by health insurance plans and payers. Specifically, there is⏎no mention or substantive exploration of how health plans are currently deploying AI tools,⏎particularly predictive models and algorithms, to inform or automate process decisions⏎regarding the approval or denial of clinical care.⏎This is a significant omission, as the use of AI in utilization management is already widely in⏎practice. Payers already are adopting these systems to rapidly process prior authorization⏎requests, review claims, and predict the necessity or appropriateness of specific medical⏎services, procedures, or prescriptions. While the intent may be to streamline administrative⏎burdens and ensure cost-effective care, the deployment of these “black box” systems raises⏎profound ethical, legal, and patient safety concerns. In addition, these decisions directly impact⏎care delivery and clinical decisionmaking by choosing which products and procedures are⏎covered by a patient’s health plan and reimbursable to providers. We recommend that⏎⏎Feb 20, 2026⏎CommonSpirit Health⏎RIN 0955−AA13⏎ASTP/ONC work with the appropriate regulatory agencies to ensure that health plans and⏎payers are held to a high standard of quality, transparency, and appropriateness when using⏎AI tools in utilization management or other decisions that impact patient care.⏎We appreciate ASTP/ONC’s consideration of our comments. If you have any questions, please⏎contact me at Rachel.Tanner@CommonSpirit.org. We look forward to working with you as part⏎of a future technical panel and in future rulemaking.⏎Thank you,⏎/s/⏎Rachel Tanner, M.Jur.⏎System Vice President, Regulatory Affairs⏎CommonSpirit Health is one of the largest nonprofit health systems with 150,000 employees and⏎over 25,000 physicians and advanced practice clinicians and 45,000 nurses serving individuals⏎and communities across 25 states in more than 137 hospitals and 2,300 care sites, including⏎myriad long-term care facilities, home health organizations, clinics, and community service⏎organizations. As a faith-based system, CommonSpirit seeks to create healthy communities with⏎a special focus on those individuals who are medically and/or socially vulnerable.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0025♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Healthcare Leadership Council , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Healthcare Leadership Council♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, M.D.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎The Healthcare Leadership Council (HLC) and the Confidentiality Coalition appreciate the⏎opportunity to submit comments to the Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ONC) (collectively, ASTP/ONC) in response to the Request for Information (RFI) on regulatory⏎reform on Artificial Intelligence (AI). Our members are eager to provide input to help inform your⏎effort to identify existing Federal statutes, regulations, agency rules, guidance, forms, and⏎administrative processes that unnecessarily hinder the development, deployment, and adoption of AI⏎technologies, particularly in healthcare, within the United States. We strongly urge the creation of a⏎risk-based federal policy that preempts conflicting state laws and streamlines regulations, ensuring a⏎consistent national approach that reduces barriers and supports innovation in AI.⏎HLC is an association of CEOs and C-suite executives from all sectors of healthcare working to⏎shape the future of the U.S. healthcare system. HLC is the exclusive forum for the nation’s⏎healthcare industry leaders to lead on major, sector-wide issues, generate innovative solutions to⏎unleash private sector ingenuity, and advocate for policies to improve our nation’s healthcare⏎delivery system. Members of HLC – hospitals, academic health centers, health plans,⏎pharmaceutical companies, medical device manufacturers, laboratories, biotech firms, health⏎product distributors/ wholesalers, post-acute care providers, homecare providers, group purchasing⏎organizations, and information technology companies – advocate for measures to increase the⏎quality and efficiency of healthcare through a patient-centered approach.⏎The Confidentiality Coalition is a diverse alliance dedicated to balancing the protection of confidential⏎health information with the need for efficient, interoperable systems that enhance healthcare⏎delivery. The Coalition’s aim is to safeguard the privacy of healthcare consumers while improving the⏎essential flow of information necessary to deliver high-quality, timely, and effective care and⏎facilitating the development of innovative medical interventions.⏎Overview: AI and Healthcare⏎For decades, AI has been a valuable tool in healthcare, recognizing patterns that help guide clinical⏎research, care delivery, medical supply chains and more. As more sophisticated machine-learning⏎algorithms and generative AI have emerged, human elements of healthcare can be augmented even⏎further to serve patients and communities. AI-enabled capabilities have the potential to sharpen the⏎accuracy of diagnoses, improve clinical decision-making, detect public health threats, and enable⏎earlier disease intervention, with applications ranging from administrative tasks to operational⏎efficiencies and predictive analytics for disease outbreaks. Despite these promising advancements,⏎Healthcare Leadership Council • 750 9th Street, NW Suite 500, Washington, DC 20001 • www.hlc.org⏎⏎significant barriers continue to impede widespread adoption and integration of AI across the⏎healthcare ecosystem. Overcoming these barriers is crucial to unlocking AI’s full potential to⏎enhance patient care, streamline operations, and improve public health outcomes.⏎Barriers and Burdens to Remove⏎HLC and the Confidentiality Coalition strongly support the removal of unnecessarily burdensome⏎requirements that restrict the development and deployment of AI; we share your goal of enabling the⏎growth of private-sector AI innovation and advancements to improve health. The emerging⏎patchwork of regulations at the federal and state level is, perhaps, the most significant threat. Private⏎sector companies currently navigate complex and potentially conflicting regulatory and legal⏎frameworks pertaining to data science use in healthcare and beyond. Additionally, there is⏎uncertainty regarding best practices and the risk of a state-by-state patchwork emerging without⏎federal guidance. The myriad requirements create confusion and inconsistencies that stifle⏎innovation, increase compliance burdens, and hinder the seamless adoption of evidence-based AI-⏎driven advancements – underscoring the essential need for a clear, cohesive federal framework to⏎provide regulatory certainty.⏎To harness the full potential of AI, the U.S. healthcare system—public and private sectors, and⏎regulators—must act together to enable progress. A unified federal approach to regulation is needed⏎to harmonize expectations, reduce ambiguity, and give organizations the predictability to adopt AI⏎responsibly.⏎Developed by cross-sectoral AI subject matter experts representing nearly 30 companies across the⏎healthcare ecosystem, this report outlines barriers to uniform AI adoption in the U.S. and delivers⏎targeted policy recommendations to address them. It urgently calls for a clear, forward-looking⏎national standard that defines AI and establishes what responsible governance must entail.⏎The Report (detailed in Addendum 3) identifies three key barriers to adoption and nine policy⏎recommendations:⏎BARRIER: Governance and Regulatory Complexity⏎Inconsistent and intricate regulations impede AI integration at scale.⏎Policy Recommendations:⏎• Centralize Legislation: Establish a comprehensive federal policy baseline, ensuring risk⏎based oversight and human-in-the-loop, to harmonize and preempt conflicting state laws.⏎• Modernize Regulations: Update key regulatory frameworks such as the Health Insurance⏎Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) to reflect today’s digital reality.⏎• Clarify Accountability: Define accountability for AI outputs across all stakeholders.⏎• Ensure Liability Protection: Create clear legal frameworks and safe harbor provisions for⏎responsible use of AI for patient care.⏎⏎BARRIER: Data and Infrastructure⏎Issues surrounding data access and privacy dissuade widespread adoption.⏎Policy Recommendations:⏎• Fortify Data Readiness, Accessibility, and Sharing: Strengthen national standards for data⏎quality, security, interoperability, and AI-ready data access.⏎• Establish Transparency Standards: Set federal standards for clear disclosure of AI⏎capabilities, limitations, and ongoing monitoring.⏎• Mitigate Bias: Create federal standards to reduce bias in healthcare AI through⏎representative data, ongoing output monitoring, and mandatory corrective action.⏎• Standardize Terminology: Develop universal terminology for what constitutes “AI use” that⏎bridges technical and healthcare-specific contexts across research, clinical, policy, and⏎operational domains.⏎BARRIER: Capabilities and End User Trust⏎Skill gaps and trust among users inhibit effective AI implementation.⏎Policy Recommendation:⏎• Enhance Workforce Training and Education: Embed AI literacy and ethics into K–12⏎education and upskill healthcare professionals through AI-focused education and training.⏎The report also outlines guiding principles and the potential benefits of responsible AI⏎regulation.⏎By addressing the key challenges identified and acting on the report’s proposed recommendations,⏎federal policymakers can catalyze the healthcare industry to unlock AI’s full potential. Removing⏎barriers to AI adoption can improve care quality, strengthen the healthcare workforce, lower costs for⏎American taxpayers, reduce fraud, waste, and abuse, minimize errors, and enhance patient⏎centered care across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎This report aims to serve as a roadmap to inspire the necessary public-private collaboration needed⏎to realize AI’s healthcare potential. The private sector continues to stress that it is unable to achieve⏎the full benefits of AI without public sector collaboration. From the nearly 30 voices from across the⏎care continuum synthesized into the report’s findings, policymakers and other public sector AI⏎leaders can glean actionable insights to build upon in future partnerships to responsibly and safely⏎unleash AI.⏎Establishing a comprehensive federal policy that preempts, deters, and possibly places a temporary⏎moratorium on the adoption of conflicting state laws is likely the single most effective way to address⏎barriers to AI adoption in healthcare. A comprehensive federal policy would ensure a consistent,⏎nationwide approach and deter the widespread adoption of a state-by-state approach that hinders⏎innovation and is resulting in an uneven set of standards being adopted across states. At the same⏎time, a federal framework should be flexible enough to adapt to rapid AI innovation and secure⏎enough to reduce the unnecessarily complex aspect of a fragmented approach.⏎Principles To Guide Further Action⏎To inform your effort to develop appropriate policy frameworks to enable innovation while⏎safeguarding the public interest, the Healthcare Leadership Council and the Confidentiality Coalition⏎⏎share our principles for the responsible development and use of AI in healthcare as you consider⏎future action, in Addendum 1.⏎These principles support the intent to advance AI developments in support of better healthcare.⏎Specifically, they emphasize the need for cooperation between regulators and stakeholders to⏎balance innovation with individual rights and patient safety. Our principles call for a federal standard⏎that preempts state regulation, is risk-based, harmonizes privacy standards for all information used,⏎and promotes appropriate transparency. Risk assessments should be flexible, tailored to specific⏎use cases, and align with established frameworks such as the National Institute of Standards and⏎Technology’s (NIST) AI Risk Management Framework.⏎The benefits of unleashing AI can be realized by allowing innovation to enhance the roles of⏎scientists, clinicians and caregivers; assessing applications from a risk-based approach; developing⏎a federal uniform standard; and adhering to privacy and security protections. Further, steps to⏎harmonize requirements across business sectors and improve agencies’ collaboration, along with⏎increasing accountability, transparency, clarity, and comprehensiveness are imperative. We urge⏎you to consider existing regulations that apply to AI enabled solutions and to leverage critical⏎collaborative opportunities with the private sector to enable innovation and promote benefits for⏎developers, deployers, and ultimately patients.⏎Instructive Use Cases Demonstrate the Potential⏎As AI continues to revolutionize healthcare, our member organizations are driving innovation. A⏎collection of sample use cases detailed in Addendum 2 provides instructive examples of private-⏎sector AI advancements in healthcare that:⏎• Facilitate the access and distribution of resources,⏎• Improve diagnostic capabilities and sharpen prognoses,⏎• Enhance clinical decision-making,⏎• Refine research and spur therapeutic development, and⏎• Bolster staff efficiencies and efficacies.⏎These AI tools illustrate both the breadth and opportunity of private sector ingenuity in leveraging AI.⏎Conclusion⏎HLC and the Confidentiality Coalition appreciate the opportunity to respond to the RFI on regulatory⏎reform and AI. We stand ready to partner in the effort to remove barriers to the most effective uses⏎and widescale adoption of AI in the united goal of improving outcomes for patients. Thank you for⏎considering our comments on this RFI.⏎We welcome the opportunity for continued collaboration and stand ready to leverage the expertise of⏎our private sector members to strengthen and develop effective, forward-looking solutions. If you⏎have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact me at ckeane@hlc.org or (202) 449-3441.⏎Sincerely,⏎Clara Keane⏎Senior Director of Policy⏎⏎Addendum #1⏎Principles for the Responsible Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare⏎Introduction⏎Artificial intelligence (AI) is rapidly evolving, and improving healthcare delivery, augmenting research⏎and development across drugs and medical devices, aiding diagnoses, assisting intricate⏎procedures, streamlining administrative tasks, and identifying individuals who could benefit from⏎additional health services. AI’s full potential in healthcare can only be achieved with collaboration⏎between private and public sector stakeholders to foster continued innovation. Our members have⏎collaboratively developed the principles below to guide a policy framework governing the use of AI in⏎the healthcare field.⏎Principles⏎Benefits and Innovation. The use of AI tools in healthcare lowers costs, reduces human error,⏎increases access to care, augments existing clinical practices, alleviates clerical and administrative⏎burden, and improves health outcomes. For example, AI enables review and translation of⏎mammograms thirty times faster than human review with 99% accuracy and reduces the need for⏎unnecessary biopsies.1 The benefits of existing tools represent only the first step in realizing AI’s⏎potential to revolutionize healthcare and patient outcomes. It is therefore essential that regulators,⏎stakeholders, and AI experts work together to ensure any regulation or policy takes a risk-based,⏎patient centered approach that supports and nurtures developing AI technology innovations, while⏎recognizing industry-specific differences and needs.⏎Risk-based Approach. Regulatory agencies and organizations using AI should adopt a risk-based⏎oversight approach to AI applications. Risk assessments should be tailored to use cases and should⏎be entity-dependent assuming there is a baseline, agreed-upon floor to align with consensus-based⏎risk management frameworks such as the AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF) developed by⏎the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST). Oversight processes adopted by⏎organizations using AI should routinely identify potential risks, possible mitigation strategies, and⏎recommended uses for the tool(s). Regulators should avoid imposing duplicative compliance⏎requirements, or those that conflict with existing requirements, that create an unworkable operating⏎environment and consider organizations that follow a framework such as the NIST AI RMF to be⏎adopting best practices. It is important to permit flexibility in assessing and allaying risk to enable⏎companies to tailor risk mitigation to the unique considerations of each AI use case - as opposed to⏎prescriptive requirements to guide AI oversight.⏎Federal Standards. Any regulatory framework(s) for AI applications should be developed and⏎applied at the federal level, while reducing regulatory compliance costs and creating a safer⏎environment for long-term investment. A single national standard that preempts state laws in this⏎area will avoid conflicting, patchwork requirements and facilitate compliance without unduly⏎restricting innovation.⏎Privacy and Security. Personal information used in AI should be subject to robust privacy and⏎security protections at the federal level. This involves adhering to existing health data privacy and⏎security protections in the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (HIPAA) and⏎applying equivalent protections for non-HIPAA health data, by establishing baseline privacy⏎protections for the processing of non-HIPAA personal information that often comprise the datasets⏎used to train, validate, and test AI models. Privacy considerations should be integrated into AI tools⏎⏎from the start to reflect the importance of data minimization and use limitations. Individuals should⏎have the right to be informed about the collection and use of their personal information, and the right⏎to access, correct and, if feasible, delete their personal information.⏎Congress should establish a single national standard for the use of personal information not already⏎subject to HIPAA that encompasses use in AI applications by entitles also not regulated by HIPAA.⏎Security safeguards, which may be based on guidelines such as those provided in the NIST⏎Cybersecurity Framework and RMF, should protect against data breaches, data poisoning,⏎exfiltration of models or training data and other threats that could expose the data used or alter the⏎use, behavior, or performance of an AI application.⏎Harmonization. Federal agencies such as the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), the U.S. Equal⏎Employment Opportunity Commission (EEOC), and the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) should⏎collaborate to align the federal government’s approach to the regulation of AI. This will allow⏎organizations subject to the authority of different federal agencies to harmonize their AI⏎implementation approach, to avoid confusion, support innovation, and achieve greater compliance.⏎While agencies may approach this technology from varying regulatory angles, whether in safety,⏎privacy, consumer protection or otherwise, they should take a patient-centered approach and reach⏎sufficient alignment so that compliance with one framework will not result in violation of, or inability to⏎comply with, another. Failure to coordinate regulatory frameworks will not only create interpretation⏎and compliance burdens but will slow AI development and deployment, and stifle innovation by⏎creating a regulatory patchwork incompatible with the way health is delivered.⏎Any additional requirements on HIPAA-covered entities must balance the need to protect patient⏎privacy while not adding unnecessary burden to the regulated entities. Alignment enables⏎consistency to ensure requirements are complimentary. Additionally, conflicts between federal⏎agencies’ regulation of AI will hamper U.S efforts to lead globally in the regulation of AI. Other⏎countries want to adopt a framework for the regulation of AI that harmonizes across business⏎sectors and regulatory areas, rather than having to deal with discordant or conflicting requirements.⏎Accountability. Health organizations that use AI should determine and establish a risk-based⏎structure of accountability that extends across its partnerships to ensure that their AI use cases are⏎deployed in a responsible, fair, and consistent manner. This includes developing, implementing, and⏎documenting principles, policies, procedures, and an internal collaborative governance structure and⏎controls to oversee the development and use of AI applications. These controls should include⏎quality control parameters for the data used as well as criteria against which the performance of the⏎AI applications is monitored, evaluated, and re-evaluated, as needed, at regular intervals throughout⏎the lifecycle to ensure that AI can be iteratively enhanced and adapt within the risk framework.⏎Accountability should extend to the highest levels of management and should include key elements⏎such as risk assessment, training, monitoring, and internal sanctions.⏎Transparency. Transparency is essential to build trust in AI technology. While organizations should⏎protect their intellectual property or proprietary technology, they should also disclose when they are⏎using AI tools in high-risk applications rather than instances where it is used for administrative or⏎clerical purposes. Organizations should not be required to reveal the inner workings of their AI⏎systems to the public or regulatory agencies, nor is there any benefit in doing so. The detailed⏎disclosure of either data inputs or algorithmic processes would not be meaningful to patients,⏎providers, or payers, would force AI developers to disclose their intellectual property or proprietary⏎⏎technology, could create AI vulnerability risks, and may limit innovators’ willingness to work with the⏎already highly regulated healthcare industry on meaningful AI applications.⏎Explainability. Developers of AI applications for use in healthcare must be able to explain to users⏎how a decision is made by a high-risk AI application in a way that is sufficiently understandable to⏎those users. Users should be able to gauge the context in which an algorithm operates and⏎understand the implications of the outcomes. Users should in turn be able to explain the role of⏎algorithms to individuals affected by AI-assisted decisions. Explanations should be meaningful and⏎useful, tailored to the audience, and calibrated to the level of risk.⏎Addressing Adverse Bias and Discrimination. There are instances where AI applications have⏎been found to be biased, as the underlying, especially historical, data sets may lack representative⏎or accurate data. Organizations should take comprehensive steps to identify and mitigate potential⏎sources of harmful bias across the lifecycle of their model development, and where reasonable and⏎appropriate for specific models, align with industry-developed standards. Ongoing inclusion of⏎sensitive personal data or data of vulnerable groups in AI training data is critical where such data is⏎relevant for the intended use and/or population can help alleviate and remedy biases, so all patients⏎may benefit from the advances from AI in healthcare.⏎⏎Addendum #2⏎Sample Use Cases of Innovative AI Advancements in Healthcare⏎Access & Distribution⏎Company Link Description⏎Inside Cardinal’s new Cardinal is combining two facilities into one single distribution and⏎AI-Driven Warehouse fulfillment center, wherein robotics and technological systems will⏎Cardinal⏎& Fulfillment Center coordinate processes such as inbound/outbound an