ic In a multi-partnership, foundation models (pre-trained AI models⏎accelerates amendable to a wide array of tasks) are being used for a genomic-⏎personalized purpose, which is to personalize care such that an AI model can⏎medicine through know things to specifically look individual-to-individual based on risk.⏎foundation models⏎with Microsoft⏎Research and⏎Cerebras Systems⏎AI-enhanced Sleep studies have been previously limited by the necessity of⏎algorithm improves research-grade 3D cameras, but an automated machine learning⏎diagnosis of REM method can analyze video recordings with just a 2D camera.⏎Mount sleep behavior⏎Sinai disorder⏎Artificial Intelligence AI can deduce information by image alone, helping to profile cancer⏎May Expedite in ways that highlight high-risk features and probability of⏎Prognoses in Cancer metastasis.⏎Care⏎Enhancing Clinical Decision-Making and Efficiency⏎Company Link Description⏎Amazon and A partnership sees Memorial Sloan running large language models⏎Memorial Sloan through Amazon Bedrock and SageMaker to amplify data quality,⏎Kettering Cancer which will allow for better monitoring of cancer status in patients as⏎Center leap toward well as shrink the time required for the early stages of drug⏎AI-driven drug development.⏎discovery⏎⏎Amazon⏎AI 'frees up time for Amazon Pharmacy’s goal is to eliminate menial work for doctors and⏎patient care,’ says clinicians such that they can focus more fully on patient interaction⏎Amazon Pharmacy's and counseling.⏎Vin Gupta⏎Mayo AI Message In Basket Art (short for Augmented Response Technology)⏎Responses automatically drafts text for a potential response to the patient’s⏎medical advice request MyChart message based on the text of the⏎message and information from the patient’s record. Initial pilots at⏎Mayo Clinic showed that In Basket Art saves nurses around 30⏎seconds per message and drafts more empathetic responses.⏎Ambient Listening Ambient listening technology documents notes and queues up⏎Helps Primary Care orders to improve the overall patient-physician experience. Third-⏎Doctors Finish Notes party ambient listening vendors integrate with Epic so that doctors⏎Epic⏎Faster can spend less time on documentation, have fewer feelings of⏎burnout, and refocus on the vital human component of patient-⏎centered care.⏎Enhancing Clinical Decision-Making and Efficiency (continued)⏎Six ways Johnson & Harnessing AI for electro-anatomical mapping of the heart: During⏎Johnson is using AI to catheter ablation procedures for atrial fibrillation, it is critical for⏎help advance electrophysiologists to “see” inside the heart. J&J's CARTO™ 3⏎Johnson & healthcare System – the leading 3D heart mapping system features AI to⏎Johnson reconstruct the left atrial anatomy of the heart. By eliminating the⏎need for manual contouring, the system represents a new⏎approach to anatomy creation, improving the efficiency of the⏎procedure workflow.⏎AI Can Reliably Predict Machine learning model SCORPIO “remains a highly accessible⏎Efficacy of Immune model for predicting ICI efficacy and can aid clinical decision-⏎Mount Sinai⏎Checkpoint Inhibitor making when use alongside other assessments.”⏎[(ICI)] Therapy⏎Mount Sinai awarded AI-driven nutrition application NutriScan AI more quickly detect⏎the Hearst Health Prize signs of malnutrition in hospitalized patients and informs treatment⏎for an AI-driven for them.⏎nutrition application⏎Implementing a ML tool Malnutrition Universal Screening Tool (MUST), when⏎Mount Sinai Machine Learning (ML) properly implemented, can serve as a key example of effective⏎Screening Tool for⏎Malnutrition: Insights⏎⏎from Qualitative ML-based clinical decision support systems (CDSS), highlighting a⏎Research Applicable to switch from older CDSS to the most modern ML approach.⏎Other ML-Based⏎Clinical Decision⏎Support Systems⏎Oracle Health Clinical Oracle Health’s AI-powered voice recognition technology records⏎AI Agent (CAA) key elements of the physician-patient encounter to interpret the⏎Oracle⏎information, accurately inputs a draft note into the Oracle Health⏎EHR, and enables the physician to quickly review and approve the⏎clinical documentation produced.⏎Richland business flexHome utilizes smart sensors to build pattern recognition on a⏎leans on AI, tech to senior patient/client’s activity such that AI will alert family or the⏎Senior⏎keep seniors in their needed authorities if a deviation indicates a senior is having a⏎Helpers⏎own homes | Tri-Cities medical emergency like a fall.⏎Area Journal of⏎Business⏎Augmedix Awarded Clients of Vizient will soon be able to access Augmedix services⏎Vizient Contract for for drafting medical notes, meant to alleviate menial tasks and⏎Vizient⏎Ambient AI emphasize patient interaction and counseling.⏎Documentation⏎Solutions⏎Research & Development⏎Company Link Description⏎AI optimizes the In the lead optimization phase of drug discovery, the use of machine⏎quest for small learning accelerates the data consensus on things like all possible⏎molecules - combinations of molecules such that new possible drugs are found⏎Merck Merck.com sooner and pushed forward more quickly.⏎Staff Efficiency/Efficacy⏎Company Link Description⏎Revolutionizing Generative AI allows medical evidence to be sourced outside of just⏎Real-World clinical trials to provide timely, relevant, and necessary data to⏎Evidence: How clinicians and doctors.⏎⏎Generative AI Can⏎Simplify Data⏎Amazon⏎Exploration⏎Amazon Pharmacy A HIPAA-compliant digital platform uses generative AI for data entry⏎Improved and validation which speeds the process of approving and assigning⏎Prescription doctor-approved prescriptions to the pharmacy counter.⏎Processing Time by⏎90% with Gen AI⏎Five ways Amazon Alongside improving prescription processing time, AI can forecast⏎Pharmacy uses AI to demand, provide real-time insurance estimates, answer questions⏎make getting ahead of an agent and conduct batching decisions.⏎medications more⏎convenient for⏎customers⏎How Envision By partnering with Aidoc, Envision Healthcare models the radiology⏎Healthcare Is workflow after the Amazon service model where ED physicians may⏎Envision⏎Incorporating AI into order a study through on-site systems that will more promptly monitor⏎Healthcare⏎Radiology Workflow a patient’s case.⏎| Healthcare⏎Innovation⏎Staff Efficiency/Efficacy (continued)⏎Company Link Description⏎Johnson & Six ways Johnson Analyzing the operating room for efficiency and physician learning: AI⏎Johnson & Johnson is using automates snippets of surgical video to speed up the process of⏎AI to help advance surgeons reviewing their work and adjusting accordingly.⏎healthcare⏎Mayo Clinic With the help of AI-gnostics, Mayo Clinic data and expertise is being⏎launches Mayo combined with machine learning to shift pathology away from⏎Clinic Digital analogy processes to digital ones.⏎Mayo Clinic Pathology to⏎modernize⏎pathology, speed⏎medical⏎breakthroughs⏎⏎Touchless Prior Clinical determinations regarding prior authorization of medications⏎Authorization | can be automated when AI is fed an electronic medical record within⏎Surescripts⏎Remove manual the purviews of the pharmacy benefit manager.⏎work & reach⏎determinations⏎faster⏎Addendum #3:⏎Unleashing AI’s Potential for Patients: A Cross-Sectoral Roadmap for Healthcare⏎⏎January 2026⏎Unleashing AI’s Potential for Patients:⏎A Cross-Sectoral⏎Roadmap for⏎Healthcare⏎⏎TABLE OF CONTENTS⏎Unleashing AI’s Potential for Patients: A⏎Cross-Sectoral Roadmap for Healthcare⏎3 Foreword⏎4 Executive Summary⏎6 Purpose, Call to Action, and Guiding Principles⏎8 Barriers to AI Adoption at Scale⏎11 Policy Recommendations to Break Down Barriers⏎25 Prioritizing Actionable Tactics to Advance Policy⏎Recommendations⏎26 Potential Benefits⏎27 Conclusion⏎27 Contributors⏎28 Appendix⏎2⏎⏎Foreword⏎Bill Coyle Maria Ghazal⏎Chairperson, President and Chief Executive Officer,⏎ZS Healthcare Leadership Council⏎___________________________________________________________________________⏎About ZS: About HLC:⏎ZS is a management consulting and The Healthcare Leadership Council is the⏎technology firm that partners with companies exclusive forum for the nation’s healthcare⏎to improve life and how we live it. We industry leaders to discuss major, sector-wide⏎transform ideas into impact by bringing issues, generate innovative solutions to⏎together data, science, technology and human unleash private sector ingenuity, and⏎ingenuity to deliver better outcomes for all. advocate for policies to improve our nation’s⏎Founded in 1983, ZS has more than 13,000 healthcare delivery system. Learn more about⏎employees in over 35 offices worldwide. To HLC’s policy priorities on our website at⏎learn more, visit www.zs.com or follow us www.hlc.org and follow us on LinkedIn and X.⏎on LinkedIn.⏎3⏎⏎Executive Summary⏎Artificial intelligence (AI) has been revolutionizing the U.S. healthcare delivery system for decades.⏎As the technology has evolved and become more attainable, interest in its use to advance patient⏎care and relieve provider burdens has only increased. AI is already enhancing diagnostics and⏎clinical decision-making, streamlining operations, expanding access to care, and accelerating drug⏎discovery - and innovation continues at a rapid pace.⏎However, despite growing momentum and interest in its potential, adoption of AI across the⏎healthcare ecosystem is inconsistent and inhibited. The current landscape is marked by evolving,⏎disparate federal and state policy frameworks, regulatory uncertainty, infrastructure development⏎needs, challenges in data interoperability, and growing concerns around fairness and trust. These⏎barriers continue to stunt progress and impede the sector from achieving the full benefits of further⏎implementation.⏎To harness the full potential of AI, the U.S. healthcare system—public and private sectors, and⏎regulators—must act together to enable progress. A unified federal approach to regulation is needed⏎to harmonize expectations, reduce ambiguity, and give organizations the predictability to adopt AI⏎responsibly.⏎Developed by cross-sectoral AI subject matter experts representing nearly 30 companies across the⏎healthcare ecosystem, this report outlines barriers to uniform AI adoption in the U.S. and delivers⏎targeted policy recommendations to address them. It urgently calls for a clear, forward-looking⏎national standard that defines AI and establishes what responsible governance must entail.⏎The report identifies three key barriers to adoption and nine policy recommendations:⏎BARRIER:⏎Governance and Regulatory Complexity⏎Inconsistent and intricate regulations impede AI integration at scale.⏎Policy Recommendations:⏎1. Centralize Legislation: Establish a comprehensive federal policy baseline, ensuring risk-⏎based oversight and human-in-the-loop, to harmonize and preempt conflicting state laws.⏎2. Modernize Regulations: Update key regulatory frameworks such as the Health Insurance⏎Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) to reflect today’s digital reality.⏎3. Clarify Accountability: Define accountability for AI outputs across all stakeholders.⏎4. Ensure Liability Protection: Create clear legal frameworks and safe harbor provisions for⏎responsible use of AI for patient care.⏎4⏎⏎BARRIER:⏎Data and Infrastructure⏎Issues surrounding data access and privacy dissuade widespread adoption.⏎Policy Recommendations:⏎1. Fortify Data Readiness, Accessibility, and Sharing: Strengthen national standards for⏎data quality, security, interoperability, and AI-ready data access.⏎2. Establish Transparency Standards: Set federal standards for clear disclosure of AI⏎capabilities, limitations, and ongoing monitoring.⏎3. Mitigate Bias: Create federal standards to reduce bias in healthcare AI through⏎representative data, ongoing output monitoring, and mandatory corrective action.⏎4. Standardize Terminology: Develop universal terminology for what constitutes “AI use” that⏎bridges technical and healthcare-specific contexts across research, clinical, policy, and⏎operational domains.⏎BARRIER:⏎Capabilities and End User Trust⏎Skill gaps and trust among users inhibit effective AI implementation.⏎Policy Recommendation:⏎• Enhance Workforce Training and Education: Embed AI literacy and ethics into K–12⏎education and upskill healthcare professionals through AI-focused education and training.⏎To support execution, the report offers 25 actionable tactics, organized by level of impact and⏎effort required, offering policymakers a clear roadmap from easier to more challenging steps.⏎As displayed in the report’s prioritization framework, the three most high-impact, low-effort tactics⏎are:⏎1. Evaluate Existing Federal Regulations⏎2. Define Healthcare-Specific AI Terminology⏎3. Establish Guidelines for Data Cleansing & Standardize Intake⏎The report also outlines guiding principles and the potential benefits of responsible AI⏎regulation.⏎By addressing the key challenges identified and acting on the report’s proposed recommendations,⏎federal policymakers can catalyze the healthcare industry to unlock AI’s full potential. Removing⏎barriers to AI adoption can improve care quality, strengthen the healthcare workforce, lower costs for⏎American taxpayers, reduce fraud, waste, and abuse, minimize errors, and enhance patient-⏎centered care across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎This report aims to serve as a roadmap to inspire the necessary public-private collaboration needed⏎to realize AI’s healthcare potential. The private sector continues to stress that it is unable to achieve⏎5⏎⏎the full benefits of AI without public sector collaboration. From the nearly 30 voices from across the⏎care continuum synthesized into the report’s findings, policymakers and other public sector AI⏎leaders can glean actionable insights to build upon in future partnerships to responsibly and safely⏎unleash AI.⏎The nine policy recommendations outlined in this report represent Healthcare Leadership Council’s⏎core proposals, while the 25 accompanying actionable tactics are illustrative, non-prescriptive⏎pathways intended to demonstrate how the recommendations could be advanced. Examples are⏎included to illustrate approaches and initiatives currently observed across the healthcare landscape.⏎Their inclusion does not imply endorsement, opposition, or a unified position by HLC, its members,⏎or the authors of this report.⏎Purpose, Call to Action, and⏎Guiding Principles⏎This report offers recommendations to federal legislative and regulatory efforts focused on fostering⏎responsible, unbiased, and scalable AI adoption in healthcare. Drawing from nearly 30 members of⏎HLC, existing standards, and past precedence, our goal is to equip lawmakers and agency leaders⏎with actionable strategies to reduce barriers and accelerate AI integration.⏎By embracing AI technologies and unlocking the full potential of available data, the U.S. has the⏎opportunity to:⏎• Improve patient experiences and expand access through faster, scaled, more accurate,⏎and personalized care that improves health outcomes, well-being, and access to care.⏎• Reduce the cost burden on U.S. taxpayers through greater efficiency and smarter⏎resource allocation.⏎• Alleviate healthcare workforce shortages and burnout by augmenting—not replacing—⏎human expertise.⏎• Decrease healthcare-related fraud, waste, and abuse with advanced detection and⏎prevention tools.⏎• Reduce the frequency of mistakes and errors to enhance safety and quality of care.⏎The sector is already proving the power of this technology, as evidenced in many use cases, such⏎as:⏎• In 2025, a multicenter, double-blind, randomized, placebo-controlled Phase 2a trial of a first-⏎in-class, generative AI-discovered TNIK inhibitor for idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis was⏎completed. Beyond meeting its primary safety endpoint, the study reported improvement in⏎lung function at the highest dose, highlighting that an AI-generated compound can produce⏎patient-relevant signals in humans and merits larger trials.⏎• A prospective, multi-site study (over 590k encounters across five hospitals) of Johns⏎Hopkins’ TREWS AI-enabled early-warning system for sepsis found that when clinicians⏎confirmed an alert within 3 hours, patients had an 18.7% relative reduction in in-hospital⏎mortality, along with less organ failure and shorter stays.⏎6⏎⏎• Ambient tools directly address one of clinicians’ biggest barriers to AI adoption,⏎administrative overload, and free up time for patient care while maintaining clinician oversight⏎of notes. A 2025 JAMA Network Open pre-post study across 17 specialties using Nuance⏎DAX (ambient clinical intelligence) reported 20.4% less time in notes per appointment, 30%⏎less after-hours work per day, and higher same-day note closure.⏎The following five guiding principles are essential to address adoption barriers and inform key⏎policy recommendations:⏎Leverage public-private Prioritize collaboration between government and industry to⏎partnerships. establish clear regulatory guardrails and ensure consistent⏎enforcement of federal AI policies in healthcare. Government⏎sets clear guardrails for safety and effectiveness; private⏎sector partners provide practical, real-world insights for⏎implementation, ensuring regulations are actionable and⏎aligned with healthcare realities.⏎Establish balanced Promote AI innovation while ensuring robust protections are⏎regulations. in place to mitigate risks and prevent harm to patients.⏎Provide privacy guardrails and regulatory clarity without⏎stifling innovation, adding unnecessary administrative⏎burden, or delaying improvements in care.⏎Enable agility for Ensure policies and national standards are flexible enough to⏎AI evolution. adapt to rapid AI advances while maintaining predictability for⏎developers, healthcare providers, and patients.⏎Ensure human oversight Require that AI complements rather than replaces human⏎and accountability. judgment by integrating human oversight throughout the AI⏎lifecycle. Foster a culture of co-intelligence that ensures⏎humans are in the loop.⏎Align to a A risk-based framework must classify AI solutions in⏎risk-based approach. healthcare based on their potential impact and tailor⏎oversight accordingly. Not all AI is the same and should⏎therefore be evaluated based on the intended outcome or⏎purpose of the AI.⏎7⏎⏎Barriers to AI Adoption at Scale⏎While AI holds immense promise to transform healthcare, real-world adoption faces significant,⏎industry-specific hurdles. In-depth interviews with experts across every sector of the healthcare⏎ecosystem point to three interdependent areas where progress can benefit from further clarification⏎and/or support:⏎Governance and Data and Capabilities and End⏎Regulatory Complexity Infrastructure User Trust⏎Governance and Regulation Complexity⏎Inconsistent and intricate regulations impede AI integration at scale.⏎• Regulatory Fragmentation:⏎Healthcare organizations must navigate inconsistent, and often conflicting, state, federal, and⏎international AI regulations. Without a unified federal framework, compliance becomes⏎costly, burdensome, and uncertain—especially for entities operating across multiple⏎jurisdictions.⏎• Pace of Innovation vs. Regulatory Lag:⏎AI advances faster than regulatory policy can adapt. Slow and complex federal policymaking⏎leaves innovators unsure whether emerging technologies and techniques will comply with⏎outdated or unclear rules, discouraging investment.⏎• Bias and Ethical Risks:⏎AI models trained on incomplete or non-representative datasets risk perpetuating bias and⏎discrimination. Standardized federal requirements for demonstrating fairness,⏎representativeness, and accuracy are needed to maintain patient wellbeing.⏎• Accountability and Legal Ambiguity:⏎Uncertainty regarding liability and legal precedent complicates the assignment of fault when⏎AI systems fail or cause harm. The lack of clarity—across developers, data providers,⏎clinicians, manufacturers, and users—complicates business agreements, malpractice⏎coverage, and legal risk management.⏎8⏎⏎Data and Infrastructure⏎Issues surrounding data access and privacy dissuade widespread adoption.⏎• Data Privacy and Security:⏎The integration of AI in healthcare, particularly concerning patient outcomes, faces significant⏎challenges related to data privacy, patient consent, and the complex global regulatory⏎landscape. While HIPAA provides essential protections for certain information when used by⏎covered entities, the inconsistencies in state laws, the varied nature and volume of data⏎collected, and sometimes, international regulations like the EU’s General Data Protection⏎Regulation create hurdles for data access and usability. Additionally, threats to data security,⏎particularly in today’s digital landscape, heighten risks as data protected within healthcare⏎institutions can be compromised when accessed through less secure third-party applications.⏎Without national standards for consent and privacy, innovation in healthcare may stagnate.⏎• Data Access:⏎There are many factors that contribute data extraction for AI applications including but not⏎limited to: differences in technological maturity, inconsistent data codification, and purpose-⏎based standards. Much of this data remains siloed, locked behind paywalls, or technically⏎incompatible, often increasing the cost of extracting and cleaning data. For AI to be⏎developed for purposes such as diagnosing, surfacing incidental radiology findings, or⏎recommending treatment protocols, training sets must be diverse, representative, and⏎comprehensive. Enforceable data standards and accessible infrastructure are essential to⏎realizing AI’s full potential.⏎• Interoperability and Integration:⏎Despite progress, current efforts to support AI tools within healthcare continue to face⏎challenges with interoperability. The incomplete adoption of consistent standards for data⏎extraction and use are limiting the ability of AI applications to meaningfully interoperate with⏎electronic health records, imaging platforms, and other clinical systems. This results in⏎fragmented data exchange and inconsistent performance across and within health systems.⏎• Cost, Resource, and Infrastructure Limitations:⏎The high costs of implementing, safeguarding, and maintaining AI solutions create barriers,⏎especially for underserved organizations. Significant investments in technology⏎infrastructure, data procurement, and skilled personnel are often required, hindering broader⏎adoption. Additionally, many providers face challenges integrating AI due to outdated⏎systems and limited resource capacity.⏎Capabilities and End User Trust⏎Skill gaps and trust among users inhibit effective AI implementation.⏎• Domain Expertise Deficit:⏎A critical barrier to effective AI adoption in healthcare is the lack of medical expertise among⏎most AI developers. Without a strong understanding of clinical practice, developers are not⏎well equipped to validate or train AI systems for real-world medical use. This gap often forces⏎9⏎⏎healthcare organizations to divert clinical staff, such as nurses, from direct patient care to⏎assist in AI training and implementation.⏎This not only strains resources amid nationwide workforce shortages but also increases the⏎risk that AI tools fail to meet clinical standards. Where none already exist, targeted initiatives⏎to embed medical expertise into AI development, coupled with structured training for⏎implementation teams, are essential to ensure AI solutions are safe, effective, and aligned⏎with patient care priorities.⏎• Workforce Resistance and Patient Trust:⏎There is some lack of trust and comfort with AI among healthcare workers and patients.⏎Many practitioners remain skeptical due to fears of job displacement and potential legal⏎liability in case of AI-errors. These concerns are compounded by the “black box” nature of⏎many AI systems, including concerns around the completeness of the training data and⏎limited visibility into how models are built and how outputs are generated.⏎Patients also share concerns, particularly around the accuracy of AI outputs and the⏎protection of their personal health data but tend to be less skeptical than providers. Past data⏎breaches, the commercialization of health apps, and uncertainty about how AI informs⏎clinical decisions reinforce the demand for greater transparency, accountability, and⏎oversight. An additional significant risk is the tendency for end users to over trust AI outputs,⏎assuming they are always correct. This overreliance can lead to serious errors when⏎decisions are made without human judgment or critical evaluation.⏎10⏎⏎Policy Recommendations to Break⏎Down Barriers⏎By embracing AI technologies and unlocking the full potential of available data, the U.S. can improve⏎overall patient outcomes across communities, alleviate workforce shortages, reduce costs for⏎taxpayers, detect and curb healthcare-related fraud, and reduce the burden of mistakes and errors.⏎This section presents a series of nine policy recommendations designed to address key barriers to⏎AI adoption in healthcare. Each recommendation is grounded in the guiding principles outlined⏎above and mapped out to one of three previously articulated categories of barriers (i.e. governance⏎and regulatory complexity, data and infrastructure, and capabilities and end user trust).⏎Each recommendation includes actionable tactics (25 total) detailing practical potential next steps for⏎addressing these opportunities. These recommendations are supported by examples that⏎demonstrate precedent and are intended to be used as general learnings that regulators can take or⏎adopt relevant attributes where applicable. These examples are neither endorsed nor opposed by⏎HLC, its members, or the authors of this report.⏎Under each recommendation, a scale for the actionable tactics serves as a guide to portray the⏎relative level of effort and feasibility to implement the particular recommendation. The left, white⏎section is reflective of a tactic that is easy to implement whereas, the right, red section is reflective of⏎a tactic that would be challenging to implement.⏎Note: The following recommendations are paired with the barriers they would address and⏎are not represented in order of priority or importance.⏎Page 25 features a heat map that organizes each actionable tactic by its potential to drive⏎meaningful change (impact) and the feasibility (effort) of implementing it.⏎11⏎⏎BARRIER⏎Governance and Regulatory Complexity⏎Inconsistent and intricate regulations impede AI integration at scale.⏎Recommendation: Centralize Legislation⏎Centralize Legislation⏎Establish a comprehensive federal policy that preempts conflicting state laws to ensure a consistent,⏎nationwide approach to mitigating AI-related risks in healthcare. This framework should define⏎currently unaddressed areas—such as permissible levels of data de-identification and required⏎safeguards for compliance—while remaining flexible enough to adapt to rapid AI innovation. A single⏎federal standard will reduce regulatory fragmentation and foster responsible adoption.⏎TACTICS⏎Review Current State Legislative Landscape:⏎Review existing state AI-related laws to identify effective practices, inconsistencies, and gaps, and⏎assess their impact on healthcare. The U.S. has several instances that can be leveraged as a⏎starting point, but we can also learn (wins and potential pitfalls) from international regulations that⏎are well ahead on this path to standardization.⏎• U.S. Example: California’s AI Risk Management Framework, linked to the California⏎Consumer Privacy Act and California Privacy Rights Act introduces transparency⏎requirements for automated decision-making. Other states, such as Colorado (SB21-169),⏎address algorithmic bias in specific sectors (e.g., insurance) but lack comprehensive⏎healthcare AI provisions—highlighting the need for federal alignment.⏎• International Example: The EU AI Act uses a risk-tiered model, classifying certain health-⏎related AI as “high-risk” with strict oversight and documentation requirements. While this⏎framework demonstrates the commitment to protections, its complexity and potential⏎overreach offer learnings for US policy makers to be aware of. In contrast, the UK’s MHRA⏎(Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency) “regulatory sandbox” allows⏎controlled real-world testing—offering a potential model for U.S. pilot programs.⏎Establish a State Law Moratorium:⏎Rather than a sweeping ban, a federally guided, narrowly scoped pause on state laws related to use⏎of AI in healthcare should be considered.⏎• Example: Although the proposed five-year moratorium on state-level AI enforcement in the⏎One Big Beautiful Bill Act was removed in a nearly unanimous 99‑1 Senate vote in early⏎July 2025 after clearing the House, its consideration highlights bipartisan concerns about an⏎inconsistent state patchwork as well as a broad and lengthy preemption of state law.⏎• Example: In December 2025, President Donald J. Trump signed an executive order to block⏎states from implementing excessive regulations and create a patchwork of varying⏎12⏎⏎"regulatory regimes." Through the development of a framework serving as a "minimally⏎burdensome national standard," the executive order is intended to cultivate an environment⏎for U.S. AI companies that enables freedom in innovation.⏎Develop a Comprehensive Federal Policy:⏎Collaborate with private sector entities (across healthcare and technology) to shape a⏎comprehensive federal policy framework that incorporates all the aspects of successful AI⏎development, deployment, adoption, and scaling. This framework should include data privacy⏎standards, evaluation and safety standards, post-market monitoring protocols, and transparency⏎guidelines. HLC members widely agree that a unified set of regulations would simplify compliance⏎and ensure consistency across states.⏎• Example: While not enforceable regulation, nor tailored for healthcare, the National Institute⏎of Standards and Technology’s (NIST’s) AI Risk Management Framework (RMF 2.0)⏎outlines voluntary standards for AI governance, addressing key areas such as data integrity,⏎system safety, transparency, and accountability. It exemplifies how public and private sector⏎collaboration can produce practical, consensus-driven guidance that balances innovation⏎with risk management.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to⏎Advance the “Centralize Legislation” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Review Current State Establish a State Law Develop a⏎Legislative Landscape Moratorium Comprehensive Federal⏎Policy⏎Recommendation: Modernize Regulations⏎Centralize Legislation⏎To keep pace with the rapidly evolving digital and AI landscape, federal regulations must be⏎modernized to integrate within outdated frameworks that limit responsible scaling and widespread⏎adoption. Updating core policies—such as HIPAA and others—will foster accountability, consistency,⏎and agility, enabling healthcare organizations to innovate without being constrained by legacy⏎policies and standards. Modernized regulations should provide clear, forward-looking guidance on⏎critical data privacy and security challenges, including consent management, uniform security⏎requirements, and protections aligned to today’s digital reality.⏎13⏎⏎TACTICS⏎Evaluate Existing Federal Regulations:⏎Assess current regulatory standards to identify gaps in applicability, coverage, and enforcement—⏎particularly where definitions (e.g., protected health information, de-identification) no longer reflect⏎today’s technology and data practices.⏎Revise and Modernize Frameworks:⏎Update existing regulations, including HIPAA, to address accountability, data governance, security⏎and provenance, while retaining the flexibility provided by HIPAA’s permitted uses of data for⏎treatment, payment, and health care operations. While existing ONC HTI-1 standards promote⏎interoperability and foundational data transparency, the Health IT framework must go further to⏎address fairness, accountability, and real-world safety for widespread adoption. This framework⏎should clarify display requirements and coverage expectations to ensure all providers delivering⏎health AI solutions are accounted for—not just a subset. We also support utilization of regulatory⏎sandboxes to test approaches and adjust to enable the unified federal approach to regulation and⏎requirements.⏎• Example: While HIPAA provides strong federal standards for protected health information⏎(PHI), it has not received a major update since 2013. Its current security standards do not⏎fully address the realities of AI-driven healthcare, leaving gaps in areas such as consent, risk⏎classification, and accountability. Updating HIPAA to reflect evolving technologies would⏎strengthen privacy, security, and trust in an AI-rich environment.⏎• Example: FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources) was originally designed to⏎provide a set of rules and specifications for the secure exchange of electronic healthcare⏎data, but initiatives like SMART (Substitutable Medical Applications, Reusable Technologies)⏎on FHIR, AI Metadata Extensions, and Clinical Decision Support (CDS) hooks are being⏎introduced and piloted across use cases to expand coverage to AI workflows. The pace of⏎establishment, adoption, and enforcement of these standards will support keeping up with⏎the rapid pace of demand, innovation, and utility of AI within the healthcare ecosystem to⏎formalize the process.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to⏎Advance the “Modernize Regulations” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Evaluate Existing Revise and Modernize⏎Federal Regulations Frameworks⏎14⏎⏎Recommendation: Clarify Accountability⏎Centralize Legislation⏎Clearly define and assign accountability for AI outputs across all parties, including solution⏎developers, data and technology providers, and healthcare providers, to eliminate ambiguity in⏎liability and build trust in AI-enabled care. Current healthcare regulations and standards do not⏎extend to most healthcare value chain partners or developers, as well as healthcare service⏎providers, leaving significant accountability gaps.⏎TACTICS⏎Documentation of Accountability:⏎Establish federal requirements that clearly define roles, responsibilities, and oversight for all parties⏎involved in developing, deploying, and monitoring AI systems, ensuring patients and caregivers⏎understand who is responsible at each stage.⏎• Example: The EU AI Act requires clear documentation of roles and responsibilities for “high-⏎risk” healthcare AI, with providers accountable for design and validation and deployers⏎responsible for data quality, oversight, and communication of system limitations. As U.S.⏎policymakers consider similar approaches, it will be important to evaluate how the EU AI Act⏎has impacted AI innovation in the EU, and whether this model, or an alternative one, would⏎best align with the U.S. healthcare landscape. We note that HIPAA applies only to covered⏎entities, leaving many AI developers, digital health platforms, and third-party aggregators⏎outside federal privacy and security obligations despite handling sensitive health data.⏎• Example: In early 2025, the FDA issued a Draft Guidance on “Considerations for the Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence to Support Regulatory Decision-Making for Drug and Biological⏎Products.” This guidance incorporates risk-based considerations and may serve as a starting⏎point for developing future regulatory frameworks and best practices for AI in healthcare.⏎Establish Standards for Non-covered Entities:⏎Develop comparable HIPAA-aligned standards to ensure the privacy and security of personal health⏎information and health-related data are protected when used by non-covered entities. Today, many⏎AI developers, digital health platforms, and third-party aggregators may be subject to different⏎federal or state privacy and security obligations despite their significant role in the handling of⏎sensitive health data.⏎Expand Malpractice Insurance Coverage:⏎Revise existing malpractice insurance statutes to account for AI-enabled clinical decision-making⏎and clearly distinguish when liability lies with clinicians (e.g. negligent use), healthcare institutions⏎(e.g. insufficient controls), or technology partners (e.g. poor modeling), to protect responsible users⏎while reinforcing oversight and safety.⏎15⏎⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to⏎Advance the “Clarify accountability” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Documentation of Establish Standards for Expand Malpractice⏎Accountability Non-covered Entities Insurance Coverage⏎Recommendation: Ensure Liability Protection⏎Centralize Legislation⏎To foster responsible AI innovation, federal policy must clearly address liability in the use of AI for⏎patient care. This includes defining permitted uses of AI in patient care, setting expectations for⏎controls, and outlining protections for organizations that comply with established standards and best⏎practices. Reducing liability uncertainty will encourage more developers and healthcare⏎organizations to design, deploy, and scale AI solutions safely.⏎TACTICS⏎Establish and Incentivize Industry Certifications and Audits:⏎Create auditable standards, certified by recognized industry bodies to promote adoption of safe⏎development practices.⏎• Example: The HITRUST AI Assurance Program offers AI Security and Risk Management⏎Assessments to help organizations secure AI-powered applications and demonstrate⏎trustworthiness. Establish mechanisms that promote universal adoption of this or an⏎equivalent assessment, making healthcare organizations responsible for ongoing certification⏎and monitoring.⏎Develop a Federal Legal Framework:⏎Provide clear guidance on interpretation, culpability, burden of liability, and permitted use across the⏎spectrum of healthcare AI applications. Several interviewees, especially data partners, suggest that⏎federal leadership should set national standards, convene industry partners, and address liability to⏎enable safe and effective AI use.⏎16⏎⏎Establish a Regulatory Safe Harbor Provision:⏎Offer targeted liability protection for organizations that meet defined guardrails, transparency, and⏎monitoring requirements.⏎• Example: A modernized “Patient Engagement and Support Safe Harbor” could expand⏎protections to additional entities and permitted support areas, enabling broader care⏎coordination while maintaining safeguards.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to⏎Advance the “Ensure Liability Protection” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Establish and Incentivize Establish a Regulatory⏎industry Certifications State Harbor Provision⏎and Audits⏎Develop a Federal Legal⏎Framework⏎BARRIER⏎Data and Infrastructure⏎Issues surrounding data access and privacy dissuade widespread adoption.⏎Recommendation:⏎Fortify Data Readiness, Accessibility & Sharing⏎Strengthen data quality, security, and interoperability standards to ensure healthcare information is⏎secure, accessible, portable, and ready for AI integration throughout its lifecycle. This includes⏎providing clear federal guidance on data access, sharing, usage, and security tailored to AI⏎applications across healthcare. Mandating standardized digital information sharing for payment and⏎operations use cases will help reduce delays, denials, and invalid claim edits that negatively impact⏎both patients and providers.⏎Anchored in FHIR and the U.S. Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) standards, the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology’s (ONC’s) Cures Act Final Rule establishes⏎nationwide requirements for the standardized exchange and accessible sharing of electronic health⏎record (EHR) data. These rules enable secure connections between third-party AI tools and patient⏎information while emphasizing the need for structured, high-quality data to ensure both regulatory⏎compliance and trust across the healthcare system.⏎17⏎⏎TACTICS⏎Establish Guidelines for Data Cleansing and Standardize Intake:⏎Develop comprehensive protocols that outline the process for identifying, correcting, and removing⏎inaccurate or irrelevant data. Also establish standards for how data should be collected, formatted,⏎and input to ensure consistency and accuracy across all datasets to improve future use.⏎Promote Integration, Interoperability, and Standards-based Exchange:⏎Broadly incorporated by major EHR vendors and providers, ONC’s FHIR and USCDI standards⏎represent meaningful progress toward nationwide interoperability. Despite this progress, true data⏎portability and seamless exchange remain challenging and costly, particularly for small providers⏎and practices with limited resources. FHIR implementations vary as vendors often support multiple⏎FHIR versions in parallel, and data beyond the standard US Core profiles can include proprietary⏎extensions, which can lead to 'FHIR profiles' that complicate uniform integration. As the list of⏎clinical information required by USCDI continues to expand, these certification-required capabilities⏎should take into account what data elements are most important for AI. Beyond these technical⏎barriers, the cost and resources (i.e. people and expertise) required for implementation and ongoing⏎updates is high; many smaller providers and practices struggle to comply in a timely manner. As a⏎result, while EHR developers have advanced significantly within their domain, factors beyond their⏎direct control continue to limit widespread and uniform adoption. Federal leadership and targeted⏎incentives will be essential to close these gaps, reduce burdens on smaller organizations, and⏎deliver on the promise of true interoperability across the healthcare system.⏎Establish a Patient Data Registry:⏎Develop a national patient registry with a digital consent ledger, inclusive of an opt-out control, that⏎enables patients to track where their data resides, control who can access it, and manage⏎permissions to determine their data sharing preferences. As suggested in the interviews, by many⏎data brokers, manufacturers, and providers, a patient ledger puts patients at the center,⏎safeguarding privacy while enabling responsible data sharing to support innovation, including AI.⏎While precedents exist such as the oncology registries, the recently mandated Alzheimer’s therapy⏎registry, and the Health Information Exchanges from the Definitive Healthcare, these efforts are⏎fragmented and narrow in scope. They stop short of offering a comprehensive, patient-controlled⏎system that spans the full healthcare ecosystem.⏎The July 2025 announcement from the current Administration to develop a patient centric healthcare⏎ecosystem (in partnership with the private sector) marks progress in advancing aligned networks,⏎securing digital identity, and consistent data exchange standards. This initiative lays the groundwork,⏎but realizing true patient controlled centralized data repository will require significant funding, strong⏎regulatory governance, as well as strong commitment from public and private sectors.⏎18⏎⏎• Example: Some learning from the UK’s Biobank (while still research focused), may offer a⏎starting point for a centralized, consented dataset that allows broad reuse while maintaining⏎participant control (not at a granular level) and transparency.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to Advance the⏎“Fortify Data Readiness, Accessibility & Sharing” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Establish Guidelines for Promote Integration, Establish a Patient Data⏎Data Cleansing and Interoperability and Registry⏎Standardize Intake Standards Based⏎Exchange⏎Recommendation: Establish Transparency Standards⏎Establish clear federal standards for transparency in the design, deployment, and monitoring of AI⏎solutions in healthcare. These standards should define what information organizations must⏎disclose—such as AI capabilities, limitations, training data sources, performance metrics, and error⏎rates—how the disclosed information must be communicated to both clinicians and patients, and⏎requirements for ongoing monitoring. AI-specific laws, such as those that relate to transparency and⏎ethical use, should not conflict with existing privacy laws and should take them into account.⏎Regulatory frameworks should strike a balance—ensuring accountability and improving adoptions⏎without stifling innovation and limiting the potential benefits of improving patient outcomes. Greater⏎transparency will strengthen trust among providers, patients, and regulators, while promoting⏎responsible AI use.⏎TACTICS⏎Develop a Configurable AI “Nutrition Label”:⏎Create a nationally recognized disclosure framework to communicate bias safeguards, privacy⏎protections, and algorithm performance in a clear, consistent format. The label should be⏎configurable to allow comprehensive detail for high-risk applications and lighter specificity for lower⏎risk use cases, as well as adaptable to the specific parameters available for each AI tool.⏎• Example: NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF) aims to foster the development⏎of innovative approaches to address characteristics of trustworthiness, including valid and⏎reliable, safe, secure and resilient, accountable and transparent, explainable and⏎interpretable, privacy-enhanced, and fair, with harmful bias managed.⏎19⏎⏎Define Transparency Policies:⏎Mandate disclosure of AI system performance, limitations, and risk mitigation measures in a⏎standardized format, coupled with appropriate monitoring of outputs.⏎• Example: FDA Guidance on AI-Enabled Medical Devices (in draft) recommends⏎documenting validation data, bias mitigation strategies, and limitations, and encourages the⏎use of model cards to communicate risks and capabilities.⏎• Example: ONC’s HTI-1 Final Rule (effective February 2024) requires algorithm transparency⏎and risk documentation for certified AI decision-support tools, including standardized⏎disclosure of development methods, validation results, and performance metrics.⏎• Example: The U.S. can learn from the EU AI Act, which sets risk-tiered transparency⏎obligations for AI applications, requiring clear instructions for use and disclosure of system⏎capabilities, limitations, and accuracy metrics.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to Advance⏎the “Establish Transparency Standards” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Develop a Configurable Define Transparency⏎AI “Nutrition Label” Policies⏎Recommendation: Mitigate Bias⏎Establish comprehensive federal guidelines to reduce bias in healthcare AI applications. This⏎includes leveraging representative datasets for training, requiring appropriate monitoring of AI⏎outputs, and mandating corrective action when biases are detected. A consistent, nationwide⏎approach will reduce discrimination, promote fairness, and build trust in AI tools.⏎TACTICS⏎Establish Continuous Monitoring Guidelines:⏎Require organizations to implement periodic bias detection and transparency practices during model⏎development and post-deployment, with mandated corrective actions to maintain fairness and⏎accuracy.⏎20⏎⏎Define AI Development Guidelines:⏎Define best practices for AI design, testing, and retraining that prioritize representative datasets and⏎multidisciplinary development teams to more comprehensively represent multi-faceted patient⏎populations.⏎Develop AI Validation Pathways:⏎Ensure clear validation pathways for accuracy testing, usability assessments, and simulated⏎deployments. In line with a risk-based approach, higher risk use cases could be validated and⏎certified by local evaluators or external experts.⏎• Example: A 2023 JAMA proposal recommends a nationwide network of Health AI Assurance⏎Laboratories as shared public–private resources to test and validate models against agreed⏎best practices established by Nation Academy of Medicine.⏎• Example: Several health systems have developed local validation and monitoring⏎platforms—emphasizing multidisciplinary, cross‑department collaboration inside the health⏎system—to ensure AI tools perform safely within their own clinical environments. For⏎instance, Vanderbilt University Medical Center’s Vanderbilt VAMOS (Algorithmovigilance⏎Monitoring and Operations System) system supports ongoing, system‑wide monitoring of AI⏎models throughout their lifecycle to promote safe and effective use. Additionally, University of⏎California, San Francisco Health’s IMPACC (Impact Monitoring Platform for AI in Clinical⏎Care) platform evaluates AI tools in real-world workflows and monitors performance and⏎bias.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to⏎Advance the “Mitigate the Bias” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Establish Continuous Develop AI Validation⏎Monitoring Guidelines Pathways⏎Define AI Development⏎Guidelines⏎21⏎⏎Recommendation: Standardize Terminology⏎The definition of what constitutes ‘AI use’ in research and development remains fluid and⏎inconsistent across the industry. As more advanced forms of AI begin to demonstrate tremendous⏎potential for drug discovery tools, clinical decision support, care coordination, and patient⏎engagement, the need for consistent, universal terminology becomes even more urgent.⏎Establishing a standardized vocabulary that bridges both technical and healthcare-specific contexts⏎will promote clarity, improve communication across stakeholders, and ensure regulations are applied⏎consistently.⏎TACTICS⏎Define Healthcare-Specific AI Terminology:⏎Develop a federal glossary of AI terms tailored to the healthcare sector, like HIPAA’s definitions for⏎health data. This glossary should include concrete and relevant examples for each of the glossary AI⏎terms. For example, diagnostics tools in the context of medical imaging refer to algorithms that⏎interpret medical images.⏎Integrate Definitions into Federal Regulatory Frameworks:⏎Embed the standardized glossary into federal AI regulations and related guidance to ensure uniform⏎application across all policies and programs.⏎• Example: NIST Glossary for Trustworthy AI – A non-sector-specific resource for common AI⏎and machine learning (ML) terminology.⏎• Example: International Organization for Standardization/International Electrotechnical⏎Commission (ISO/IEC 22989:2022) – International consensus-based AI vocabulary widely⏎used in governance frameworks can serve as a seed to developing a US Healthcare centric⏎glossary.⏎• Example: FDA Digital Health and AI Glossary – Aligns with ISO standards and provides⏎practical definitions for AI systems and components.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to⏎Advance the “Standardize the Terminology” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Define Healthcare- Integrate Definitions into⏎Specific AI Federal Regulatory⏎Terminology Frameworks⏎22⏎⏎BARRIER⏎Capabilities and End User Trust⏎Skill gaps and trust among users inhibit effective AI implementation.⏎Recommendation:⏎Enhance Workforce Training & Education⏎Develop comprehensive education and training programs to upskill healthcare professionals in both⏎foundational AI concepts and healthcare-specific applications. By embedding AI literacy into⏎professional development, the healthcare workforce can better understand, evaluate, and⏎responsibly adopt AI to mitigate job-loss fears among clinicians and strengthen patient trust.⏎TACTICS⏎Incentivize Employee Training:⏎Encourage healthcare organizations to provide workforce-wide AI training through federal financial⏎incentives, such as tax credits or subsidies. Training should cover AI terminology, data privacy and⏎security, transparency, bias mitigation, and healthcare use cases. This is supported by a 2022⏎Congressional Research Service report recommendation for targeted tax credits for employer⏎training, with an incremental model that rewards investment above a baseline and prioritizes low-⏎and middle-wage employees to promote fair and inclusive skill-building.⏎• Example: Potentially seek to leverage the One Big Beautiful Bill’s expansion of Pell Grant⏎eligibility for short-term training programs to include AI-focused healthcare courses, enabling⏎nurses and other mid-carrier providers to access affordable upskilling opportunities.⏎Revamp Curriculum to Drive Inherent AI-Readiness:⏎Adapt curricula from K–12 through postgraduate levels to include foundational AI concepts, ethical⏎considerations, and real-world healthcare applications. AI readiness amongst the incoming⏎workforce will ensure faster adoption and scaling across the industry.⏎• Example: The White House Executive Order on Artificial Intelligence Education published in⏎April 2025 directs federal agencies to integrate AI literacy into K–12 curricula,⏎train educators, and prioritize AI in discretionary grants and workforce⏎development programs.⏎23⏎⏎Establish Government Incentives:⏎Target and direct federal incentives to under-resourced healthcare organizations and state colleges⏎to develop and deliver AI-focused curricula, ensuring equitable access to training across⏎geographies and roles.⏎Create a Secure Sandbox:⏎Establish controlled environments where healthcare professionals can safely test AI tools on de-⏎identified data to further build confidence, competence, and capabilities.⏎• Example: The UK’s NHS “AI Airlock” allows innovators to pilot AI tools in live settings under⏎strict governance, ethical review, and patient safety oversight.⏎Relative Ease of Implementing Actionable Tactics to Advance the⏎“Enhance Workforce Training and Education” Recommendation⏎Challenging⏎Easy⏎Incentivize Revamp Curriculum Create a Secure⏎Employee Training to Drive Inherent All- Sandbox⏎Readiness⏎Establish⏎Government⏎Incentives⏎24⏎⏎Prioritizing Actionable Tactics to⏎Advance Policy Recommendations⏎The following figure depicts a prioritization framework categorized across the three barriers:⏎Governance and Data and Capabilities and End⏎Regulatory Complexity Infrastructure User Trust⏎Each actionable tactic is plotted based on its potential to drive meaningful change (i.e. impact) and⏎the feasibility (i.e. effort) of implementing it.⏎This framework, informed by the cross-sector healthcare stakeholders interviewed, identifies near-⏎term opportunities and longer-horizon investments to promote a coordinated and scalable approach⏎to responsible AI adoption in healthcare.⏎Impact versus Effort for the Regulatory Actionable Tactics⏎25⏎⏎Potential Benefits:⏎By addressing barriers, clarifying accountabilities, and investing in education and infrastructure,⏎these actions can not only accelerate responsible AI adoption, but also ensure that the advantages⏎of adoption are appropriately distributed across the healthcare ecosystem. A coordinated, federally⏎guided approach can unlock the following transformative benefits across the healthcare ecosystem,⏎with extraordinary dividends for patient care:⏎✓ Improve Patient Safety and Outcomes:⏎Enhance patient care and outcomes through better data quality and interoperability, ensuring AI⏎systems are reliable and effective.⏎✓ Promote AI Innovation and Adoption:⏎Encourage AI innovation by providing predictability, reducing hesitation to invest, and incentivizing⏎organizations to deploy AI with protection.⏎✓ Enhance Public Trust:⏎Increase public trust by promoting accountability and consistency in AI standards, while encouraging⏎collaboration among regulators, developers, and users.⏎✓ Reduce Compliance Burden and Legal Uncertainties:⏎Streamline regulatory requirements across jurisdictions, reducing the compliance burden and legal⏎uncertainties.⏎✓ Foster Data Sharing and Interoperability:⏎Promote utility of advancements in AI technology by enabling greater data sharing and⏎interoperability.⏎✓ Support Learning and Evolution:⏎Improve AI reliability and patient outcomes through continual learning and evolution based on real-⏎world data and representative patient populations.⏎✓ Address Job Concerns:⏎Mitigate job-loss fears by highlighting the value of AI-supported work practices and its healthcare⏎application.⏎26⏎⏎Conclusion⏎While the barriers described within this report have inhibited progress thus far, removing them is⏎achievable through further public and private sector collaboration. HLC and its members are⏎committed to continue serving as a resource to inform needed AI regulatory and legislative actions.⏎The risk of inaction is compounding. A patchwork of rules will harden into incompatible and⏎competing systems. Incomplete or biased data will deepen access gaps and degrade model⏎performance. Public trust will erode further adoption. Waiting turns today’s friction into tomorrow’s⏎failure to scale when safely and effectively scaling AI can better support providers and serve⏎patients.⏎What is needed now is federal action with urgency and clarity. This report outlines practical, high-⏎impact actions that can be implemented immediately, alongside longer-term objectives that require⏎sustained effort and coordination. If the U.S. can align policy with technology and clinical realities, AI⏎will not only enhance human intelligence—it will extend the reach of better and safer care to all⏎communities.⏎Contributors⏎AUTHORS CONTRIBUTORS⏎Shailee Vimadalal⏎Bill Coyle⏎Principal, ZS⏎Chairperson, ZS⏎Stephanie Santana⏎Maria Ghazal⏎Manager, ZS⏎President and Chief Executive Officer,⏎Healthcare Leadership Council⏎Karli Danielson⏎Associate Consultant, ZS⏎Jessica Jarvis⏎Principal, ZS⏎Clara Keane⏎Senior Policy Director, Healthcare Leadership⏎Council⏎27⏎⏎Appendix⏎1. Summary of Interview Methodology⏎This report is grounded in a series of structured, 30-minute interviews with subject matter experts⏎from HLC member companies that span the healthcare ecosystem. Interviewees were nominated by⏎their organizations to ensure a diversity of perspectives, with representation from regulatory,⏎compliance, data, technology, clinical operations, and digital health innovation functions.⏎Each interview followed a consistent process focused on exploring barriers and accelerators to AI⏎adoption in healthcare, anchored around three core themes:⏎• People: Trust and adoption of AI, stakeholder awareness and education, and the incentives⏎or disincentives influencing AI uptake.⏎• Process & Policy: Existing standards and regulations, integration challenges, and the⏎operational burden of enabling AI solutions.⏎• Data & Technology: Data interoperability and privacy, data quality and completeness, and⏎the infrastructure required to support AI initiatives.⏎This approach ensured that the insights were grounded in real-world experience and reflect the⏎collective insights of leading organizations across the healthcare landscape.⏎2. Participating Organizations⏎A total of 27 organizations from HLC’s membership participated in the interview process,⏎representing a broad cross-section of the healthcare value chain. The organizations interviewed⏎include:⏎Payers⏎• The Cigna Group Manufacturers⏎• GuideWell • Amgen⏎• UnitedHealth Group • Baxter⏎Providers • Johnson & Johnson⏎• AdventHealth • Novartis⏎• City of Hope • Pfizer⏎• ComPsych Data & Technology Partners⏎• Envision Healthcare • Amazon⏎• Labcorp • Cotiviti⏎• MemorialCare • eHealth⏎• Mount Sinai Health System • Leidos⏎• Premier • Surescripts⏎• Sanford Health Distributors & Accreditors⏎• Texas Health Resources • Cencora⏎• Vizient • Joint Commission⏎• Wellvana Health⏎28♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0026♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from College of American Pathologists (CAP), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔College of American Pathologists (CAP)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Jim O'Neill, MA⏎Deputy Secretary⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave., SW,⏎Room 614G⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW⏎Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI) [Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001]⏎Submitted via Electronic Submission to www.regulations.gov⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The College of American Pathologists (CAP) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the⏎Request for Information (RFI) Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care issued by the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of the Deputy⏎Secretary in collaboration with the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)/Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. As the world's largest organization of board-⏎certified pathologists and leading provider of laboratory accreditation and proficiency testing⏎programs, the CAP serves patients, pathologists, and the public by fostering and advocating⏎excellence in the practice of pathology and laboratory medicine worldwide.⏎As physicians specialized in the diagnosis of disease through laboratory methods, pathologists⏎deliver high-quality diagnostic services to patients and other physicians. Pathologists place patient⏎safety and test validity foremost in their minds as they determine which tests to implement in the⏎clinical laboratory. The CAP’s comments in response to this RFI are informed by its commitment to⏎ensuring that patients receive the right test, at the right time, and with the right result. As a Clinical⏎Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA)-deemed accrediting organization and as a leading⏎provider of laboratory proficiency testing/external quality assessment (PT/EQA), the CAP accredits⏎over 8,300 laboratories and regularly introduces new PT/EQA programs to elevate the quality of⏎laboratory medicine. The CAP Accreditation Checklists require the laboratory director to ensure that⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎the performance specifications for new tests, instruments, and methods introduced to the laboratory⏎have been properly validated or verified prior to being used for patient testing, including AI and⏎machine learning algorithms.⏎To accelerate the adoption and use of AI in pathology and laboratory medicine, the CAP⏎recommends taking into account the⏎• Importance of manufacturer transparency regarding AI tools,⏎• Essential and complementary roles of Food and Drug Administration (FDA) review of AI-⏎enabled medical devices and software and CLIA oversight of laboratories,⏎• Lessons from the CAP’s work in laboratory accreditation that can be applied to AI, and the⏎• Importance of pathologists playing a leadership role in the use of AI systems in the⏎laboratory.⏎The CAP will respond to selected questions applicable to its members’ expertise in laboratory⏎medicine and AI oversight.⏎CAP Responses to RFI Questions⏎Regulation⏎HHS seeks feedback on how current HHS regulations impact AI adoption and use for clinical care.⏎CAP Response: Currently, CLIA and the FDA serve complementary and necessary roles in the⏎adoption and use of AI for clinical care. FDA review is necessary to ensure safety and efficacy in a⏎premarket setting and ongoing postmarket analysis. Following CLIA requirements, laboratories verify⏎and validate local test performance prior to implementation, as well as perform and document⏎calibration verification procedures every 6 months to ensure ongoing test validity.⏎Pathologists are the physician leaders of clinical laboratories with responsibility for oversight of⏎clinical and anatomic pathology laboratories. The decision about whether to implement an in vitro⏎diagnostic device in the laboratory begins with careful review of the FDA-approved device label.⏎Assessing AI devices prior to laboratory implementation requires transparent, comprehensible⏎information from device developers about the model development data, performance data,⏎performance metrics, performance monitoring, limitations, installation and use, and customizable⏎features. Implementation challenges for AI systems include use of potentially imbalanced datasets⏎for training; training data that does not accurately reflect the intended deployment context; and⏎overfitting, which can cause a model to perform exceptionally well on training data but fail to⏎generalize effectively to new, unseen data, producing an inaccurate output. The implementation of⏎safeguards against these challenges in AI implementation will enhance physician utilization of AI⏎systems. FDA review helps ensure these safeguards.⏎In the CAP’s April 2025 comments filed in response to the FDA draft guidance “Artificial Intelligence⏎Enabled Device Software Functions: Lifecycle Management and Marketing Submission⏎Recommendations” [Docket No. FDA-2024-D-4488], the CAP encouraged the FDA to create a⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎requirement that manufacturers add to labeling information about the verification process and a set⏎of target verification metrics that have a clear relationship to clinical performance.1 Moreover, for AI-⏎enabled devices that are iteratively modified, including by learning from real-world data, pathologists⏎depend on transparent information from the “Predetermined Change Control Plan” (PCCP) that the⏎FDA has noted can be included in a marketing submission for an AI-enabled device.2⏎After reviewing the FDA labeling and before using a test to report patient results, CLIA requires⏎that pathologists demonstrate that the laboratory can obtain performance specifications with the⏎device that are comparable to those established by the manufacturer for accuracy, precision, and the⏎reportable range of test results for the test system.3 CLIA also requires that pathologists verify that⏎the manufacturer's reference intervals are appropriate for the laboratory's patient population. After⏎implementing a test, laboratories must continue to monitor how it performs. Every 6 months, CLIA⏎requires that laboratories conduct and document calibration verification to substantiate the continued⏎accuracy of the test system throughout the laboratory's reportable range of test results for the⏎test system.4⏎To summarize, CLIA complements FDA review by specifying high-quality practices in the laboratory⏎that go beyond operational requirements defined by a manufacturer of an AI-enabled medical device⏎and approved by the FDA. Pathologists use their experience as CLIA laboratory directors and⏎section directors to ensure that FDA-cleared/approved AI devices are used appropriately, as the⏎inappropriate implementation of any FDA-cleared device can result in harm to patients. While CLIA⏎regulations are not directly applicable to other medical specialties, they may inform thinking about⏎performance quality goals related to AI, which will ensure that the increased use and adoption of AI⏎benefits patient care. Because AI-enabled tools must be safe, reliable, and clinically valid to gain⏎physician trust and be adopted at scale, CLIA’s longstanding quality framework provides an existing,⏎proven system HHS can leverage to accelerate AI adoption, thereby reducing uncertainty,⏎strengthening trust, and streamlining safe implementation.⏎Reimbursement⏎HHS seeks feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive and ability to⏎promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical care AI tool⏎builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care.⏎CAP Response: AI-enabled clinical services have the potential to improve patient care but are likely⏎to increase overall health care spending due to significant implementation and ongoing operational⏎costs. AI also tends to expand clinical services rather than replace physician work by identifying⏎1 Please see the CAP’s comments here: https://www.regulations.gov/comment/FDA-2024-D-4488-0037.⏎2 As the FDA final guidance Marketing Submission Recommendations for a Predetermined Change Control Plan for Artificial⏎Intelligence-Enabled Device Software Functions notes, a PCCP generally includes a detailed description of the specific,⏎planned device modifications, which is referred to as the “Description of Modifications”; 2) the associated methodology to⏎develop, validate, and implement those modifications in a manner that ensures the continued safety and effectiveness of the⏎device across the intended use populations, which is referred to as the “Modification Protocol”; and 3) the assessment of the⏎benefits and risks of the planned modifications and risk mitigations, which is referred to as the “Impact Assessment.”⏎https://www.fda.gov/media/166704/download.⏎3 42 CFR 493.1253.⏎4 42 CFR 493.1255.⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎additional conditions and prompting increased downstream testing, consultations, and treatments.⏎Within the CMS budget neutrality framework, these increased costs could place additional downward⏎pressure on physician reimbursement. To avoid destabilizing physician payment and to preserve⏎access to care, the CAP recommends that costs associated with AI technologies be treated as⏎separate expenses outside of the Physician Fee Schedule.⏎The successful reimbursement of AI in clinical care depends on clear, predictable, and clinically⏎appropriate payment pathways. Uncertainty regarding coding, coverage, and payment creates⏎barriers to reimbursement and discourages adoption. The AMA CPT Editorial Panel plays a central⏎role in establishing the coding structures that serve as the foundation for reimbursement policy,⏎including the development of Appendix S, which classifies AI-enabled clinical services. In addition,⏎ongoing work by the AMA-convened Digital Medicine Payment Advisory Group (DMPAG) and the⏎Digital Medicine Coding Committee (DMCC) continues to develop recommendations for digital and⏎AI services. The CAP recommends that HHS work closely with the AMA CPT Editorial Panel and its⏎associated advisory groups and committees to establish a reimbursement framework that⏎appropriately recognizes physician work, supports innovation, and ensures sustainable payment⏎policies for AI-enabled clinical services.⏎Research & Development⏎HHS seeks input on ways in which HHS may invest in research & development (including public-⏎private partnerships and cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs)) to integrate⏎AI in care delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the health and⏎wellbeing of all Americans.⏎CAP Response: The CAP supports infusion of new HHS funds in public-private partnerships,⏎grants, or cooperative research and development agreements to integrate AI into the clinical⏎laboratory and across healthcare organizations and provide a process for real-world evidence⏎collection and analysis. The CAP has built the CAP AI Studio to provide our members with a safe,⏎simulated space to explore cutting-edge AI tools used in pathology.5 Additional HHS funds would⏎help expand these efforts. From image analysis to diagnostic support, pathologists can interact with⏎models in realistic patient scenarios prior to implementing these diagnostic tools in their clinical⏎practice. This hands-on experience assessing AI systems prior to implementation is a key⏎component of CLIA standards. Designed as both an educational tool and a discovery hub, the⏎platform is a go-to destination for pathologists exploring AI in real-world contexts. This platform⏎highlights the AI tools currently available and offers a glimpse of what’s possible as we continue to⏎investigate how AI can support and enhance the practice of pathology.⏎In addition to this practical experience, federal support for public-private partnerships could help to⏎develop high-grade datasets for validation across a variety of laboratory settings. Diverse,⏎comprehensive datasets from multiple practices are necessary to validate the use of AI devices.⏎5 https://newsroom.cap.org/latest-news/immersive-ai-platform-for-pathologists-set-to-launch-at-cap25-in-orlando/s/f72bb232-⏎0294-430e-a734-7a55f4588894; https://www.cap.org/member-resources/councils-committees/informatics-committee/artificial-⏎intelligence-pathology-resources#ai-studio.⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎These collaborations could also help to build trust and consistent approaches to practical⏎implementation. Within the laboratory setting, we currently rely on the Clinical Laboratory and⏎Standards Institute (a not-for-profit organization that develops consensus-based international⏎laboratory standards and testing guidance) to develop clear guidelines and best practices for⏎maintaining testing standards. Laboratory accreditation and proficiency testing also contribute to⏎establishing and monitoring the quality of laboratory medicine. Pathologists are directly involved in⏎setting the standards and quality maintained by the CAP’s accreditation, proficiency testing, and⏎guidelines programs. These guidelines, best practices, and educational materials standardize⏎laboratory testing and enable local laboratory practitioners to appropriately monitor the quality of a⏎wide variety of tests being performed in their laboratory. Measuring and evaluating AI based upon⏎this foundation of laboratory practice helps ensure reliance on the proven safety, quality, and⏎accountability systems laboratories already use.⏎In addition, the CAP has also collaborated with the FDA on the Systemic Harmonization and⏎Interoperability Enhancement for Laboratory Data (SHIELD) collaboration. Laboratory data provides⏎critical diagnostic information across the continuum of care, and AI technologies may assist with⏎standardizing the data and integrating it in a more seamless way. SHIELD helped to address this⏎problem by stimulating innovation by helping to build, implement, and support a comprehensive⏎solution that addresses clinical and semantic device interoperability for in vitro diagnostics across the⏎nation. Public-private partnerships such as SHIELD would better integrate AI in care delivery and⏎create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the health and wellbeing of all Americans.⏎The CAP would welcome additional opportunities like SHIELD to catalyze innovation to supplement⏎the market.⏎Specific RFI Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎CAP Response: AI deployment in pathology is in early stages with relatively few AI models⏎deployed in a limited number of laboratories. Some of the biggest barriers to the adoption and use of⏎AI in clinical care and private sector innovation in AI for health care include the lack of understanding⏎of how AI models are trained and developed, the uncertainty around the liability associated with use⏎of AI systems, and the need for safeguards around AI systems.⏎Transparent information for AI tools will resolve the lack of understanding of how AI models are⏎trained and developed. For AI-enabled medical devices, the FDA’s review process helps to provide⏎this transparency. Transparency is essential to increase pathologists’ and patients’ trust in AI⏎systems, making pathologists more willing to adopt and use AI in the pathology and laboratory⏎settings. This will therefore accelerate private sector innovation in AI for health care. In addition,⏎transparency will give pathologists greater confidence as to which AI models are trustworthy, thereby⏎helping pathologists protect patient safety and avoid wasting time resources on unhelpful AI tools.⏎Moreover, pathologists need transparency around whether AI tools are static or adaptive—that is,⏎whether they are based on machine learning models with fixed (“static”) parameters or whether they⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎continue to update after deployment (“adaptive”).⏎Another barrier to physicians’ willingness to use AI is that challenges in AI implementation exist and⏎will require implementation of safeguards—such as transparency—to ensure identification and⏎mitigation. For example, generative AI models can produce outputs that are not grounded in truth (a⏎phenomenon often termed as hallucinations). Other implementation challenges for AI systems⏎include use of potentially imbalanced datasets; training data that does not accurately reflect the⏎intended deployment context; and overfitting. The implementation of safeguards against these⏎challenges in AI implementation will enhance physician utilization of AI systems. The implementation⏎of safeguards against these challenges in AI implementation will enhance physician utilization of AI⏎systems and will make physicians more willing to use AI systems.⏎Another barrier to physicians’ willingness to use AI is the uncertainty around the liability associated⏎with use of AI systems, particularly for adaptive AI tools whose performance may change⏎post deployment. In laboratory environments, adaptive systems that update iteratively can drift from⏎their originally validated purpose or introduce unintended interactions with connected instruments,⏎‑⏎software, or data pipelines (e.g., configuration changes or model updates that propagate across⏎linked systems). Physicians should not bear sole liability for harms arising from such model level⏎risks. AI developers should also be held liable for harms from AI due to their knowledge about the⏎‑⏎design of those AI tools. A shared liability approach would encourage physicians to adopt and use⏎AI, including pathologists employing AI in pathology and laboratory medicine.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎CAP Response:⏎Regulatory Policy: In line with the CAP’s response to question 1, the CAP recommends that HHS’⏎federal AI policy supports manufacturer transparency of AI tools and ensures that physicians are not⏎solely liable for the harms from AI. Consequently, the CAP suggests the following specific revisions⏎to HHS regulations:⏎• Repeal 45 CFR 92.210 (Nondiscrimination in the use of patient care decision support⏎tools),6 which holds physicians accountable for discrimination caused by automated and AI-⏎enabled patient care decision support tools. The HHS Office for Civil Rights (OCR) specified⏎that patient care decision support tools include AI used to support clinical decision-making.⏎This nondiscrimination provision places undue burden and responsibility on physicians to⏎account for and address nondiscrimination that should be the responsibility of AI⏎manufacturers that built and validated the AI model. Physicians do not create these patient⏎care decision support tools, and it is the responsibility of the vendor that creates the models⏎6 Which was finalized in the Biden-era regulation Nondiscrimination in Health Programs and Activities (89 FR 37522).⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎to know whether the data that was used was discriminatory. The nondiscrimination provision⏎as written will disincentivize AI use by physicians, as physicians are less equipped than AI⏎vendors to detect the potential for discrimination.⏎• The CAP urges HHS to maintain requirements for transparent information from AI developers⏎regarding source attribute support, access, and modification and intervention risk⏎management for predictive decision support interventions (DSIs), which include AI algorithms⏎in ASTP’s Health IT Certification Program.7 ASTP is proposing8 to rescind these informational⏎requirements. However, as the CAP noted in 2023,9 these informational requirements help⏎guide pathologists in their responsibilities to assess AI systems. Specifically, these⏎informational requirements help provide transparent information to pathologists about the⏎development, deployment, and configuration of AI technology. This information helps⏎pathologists apply their unique knowledge of laboratory testing in the implementation of AI-⏎enabled devices in the laboratory, including ethical considerations, patient safety issues,⏎risks, workflow, and other challenges. The CAP prioritizes patient safety and analytic and⏎clinical validity in development and responsible implementation of AI in pathology. The⏎information included in these requirements is important to promote trust in AI algorithms and⏎encourage adoption and use of AI in pathology and laboratory medicine. Moreover, repealing⏎these requirements would increase the risk of waste, fraud, and abuse proliferating in the⏎healthcare system, as their repeal will make it more difficult for physicians to assess whether⏎specific AI models are trustworthy.⏎Payment Policy: While AI has the potential to improve patient care, its rapid growth is also likely to⏎increase overall health care spending. The implementation and ongoing operation of AI technologies⏎introduce significant new and recurring costs, including investments in software, data infrastructure,⏎cybersecurity, and continuous system updates. Moreover, AI is not a direct substitute for physician⏎work in the clinical setting; instead, it tends to expand clinical care rather than replace existing⏎services. AI can use enhanced diagnostic information to identify additional conditions, which in turn⏎prompts more downstream testing, consultations, and treatments, ultimately driving higher overall⏎utilization. These cost pressures are particularly problematic within CMS’s budget neutrality⏎framework, which requires that any increases in Medicare spending be offset by reductions⏎elsewhere in the Physician Fee Schedule.⏎To avoid destabilizing physician payment and undermining access to care, the CAP recommends⏎that HHS consider establishing a policy approach in which costs associated with AI technologies fall⏎outside the Physician Fee Schedule. Treating AI as a separate investment, rather than as a zero-⏎sum adjustment within physician payment, would enable adoption of these technologies without⏎exacerbating downward pressure on physician reimbursement.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎7 These requirements are in § 170.315(b)(11)(iv), (v), and (vi).⏎8 In the Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability: ASTP/ONC Deregulatory Actions To Unleash Prosperity (HTI-5)⏎Proposed Rule (90 FR 60970)⏎9 https://documents.cap.org/documents/CAP-HTI-1-Comment-Letter.pdf.⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎CAP Response: Significant uncertainty exists for physicians about the liability involved in use of AI-⏎enabled medical devices. Physicians rely on validated, reproducible results from medical devices to⏎have confidence and trust in their use. Similar liability questions would emerge regarding use of non-⏎medical devices, depending on how “non-medical device” is defined by HHS. A shared liability⏎approach would encourage physicians to adopt and use AI, including pathologists employing AI in⏎pathology and laboratory medicine.⏎For privacy and security requirements, the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act⏎(HIPAA) establishes national standards to protect individuals' electronic personal health information⏎that is created, received, used, or maintained by a covered entity, including health plans,⏎clearinghouses, and physicians and healthcare providers. HIPAA requires appropriate⏎administrative, physical, and technical safeguards to ensure the confidentiality, integrity, and security⏎of electronic protected health information. Depending on the type of healthcare information used and⏎the intended use of the non-medical device, HIPAA concerns may certainly arise. We recommend⏎that any issues with privacy and security surrounding non-medical devices be handled through⏎HIPAA so as to avoid conflicting or duplicative requirements. This will help with implementation.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎CAP Response: AI models in medical devices or non-medical devices may undergo performance⏎drift and must be monitored to ensure accuracy and patient safety. CLIA requires that laboratories⏎assess medical device performance at least every 6 months and, in practice, internal and external⏎performance assessment often occurs more frequently. Non-medical devices will also need ongoing⏎monitoring to ensure robust performance.⏎AI encompasses a broad range of machine learning techniques, from simple linear regression to⏎complex deep neural networks, each with distinct assumptions, behaviors, and levels of⏎interpretability. This diversity makes it challenging to define a single, standardized approach for⏎establishing and maintaining AI trustworthiness, as different model architectures may require⏎different validation and monitoring strategies. Consequently, the framework for measuring and⏎evaluating the performance of AI in clinical diagnostics and non-medical devices should be relevant⏎to the AI model and acknowledge the framework for oversight. This will ensure that users have the⏎best information for verification and validity. Depending on the type of non-medical device, AI⏎measurement and evaluation should also account for performance of AI in the local context and the⏎stability of local input data.⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎CAP Response: Implementation of AI systems will require development of standardized⏎assessment and validation tools, as well as increased investment in the digital infrastructure of⏎healthcare organizations. Federal support through the infusion of new funds and involvement in⏎public-private partnerships will be important, such as through the FDA and the Advanced Research⏎Projects Agency for Health (ARPA-H). The CAP would be pleased to be involved in this effort.⏎The healthcare system routinely relies on independent, third-party accreditation, certification, and⏎credentialing to set standards and measure performance. For example, the CAP’s nationwide⏎accreditation and proficiency testing programs form a foundational quality assurance infrastructure⏎that can serve as a model for evaluating, monitoring, and safely deploying AI-enabled tools.⏎Leveraging the CAP’s programs will accelerate AI adoption by utilizing proven mechanisms that⏎laboratories already trust and understand. The CAP’s accreditation standards address conventional⏎laboratory testing methods that share some features with AI systems. For example, the CAP⏎accreditation requirements note that owing to potential drifts and shifts in performance over time,⏎laboratories should establish controls, metrics, corrective actions, and procedures to address⏎changes to the test system that could affect clinical results; such practices are similarly necessary for⏎laboratories to implement with the use of AI -enabled medical devices.10 Similarly, at least twice⏎yearly, CAP accredited laboratories must participate in external proficiency testing or conduct⏎alternative performance assessments to demonstrate the continued accuracy of test results. AI⏎systems must be monitored at least as frequently as conventional test systems to ensure ongoing⏎acceptable performance.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the⏎most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to⏎the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎CAP Response: A primary hurdle to the adoption of AI in clinical care is the lack of transparent⏎information from manufacturers about how AI models are trained and developed. In line with the⏎CAP’s response to question 1, the CAP notes that transparency of AI systems is crucial for resolving⏎this lack of understanding. Transparency is essential to increase pathologists’ and patients’ trust in⏎AI systems, making pathologists more willing to adopt and use AI in the pathology and laboratory⏎settings. Transparency in the FDA-accepted labeling is essential. The CAP has encouraged the FDA⏎to require that manufacturers add to labeling information about the verification process and a set of⏎target verification metrics that have a clear relationship to clinical performance. With this information,⏎pathologists verify and validate AI performance and advocate to institutional decision makers for⏎investment in AI systems. Pathologists will be reluctant to use AI tools for which they lack adequate⏎information about suitability for the tool’s intended use. Pathologists also require clear information⏎10 Furtado LV, Ikemura K, Benkli CY, et al. General applicability of existing College of American Pathologists accreditation⏎requirements to clinical implementation of machine learning–based methods in molecular oncology testing. Arch Pathol Lab⏎Med (2025) 149 (4): 319–327.⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎about whether AI tools are static or adaptive as these characteristics have different implications for⏎institutional oversight and ongoing monitoring. Pathologists will be more willing to adopt and use AI⏎systems when transparent information is available and when those AI systems have appropriate⏎safeguards in place. Put differently, physicians will ultimately be the ones to decide whether AI tools⏎can be safely and appropriately integrated into clinical practice, as the adoption of AI for clinical care⏎ultimately depends on whether these technologies meaningfully affect clinical decision-making and⏎patient care. Accordingly, the CAP recommends that HHS work closely with the CAP and other⏎medical societies to ensure that physician perspectives are incorporated into policies governing the⏎adoption of AI for clinical care.⏎Healthcare systems typically have a committee of decision makers, which may include a chief⏎information officer, with influence to make decisions about adoption of AI for clinical care. The⏎challenge for pathologists is that these committees may not include pathologists, and decisions⏎about AI implementation in a healthcare system would benefit from pathologists’ input from their role⏎as laboratory directors. CLIA requires that pathologists, as laboratory directors, assess AI systems⏎prior to their implementation to verify performance in the local setting. Conducting this evaluation⏎prior to purchase can be challenging. Because of this, the CAP built the CAP AI Studio to address⏎this gap with a secure, web-based simulation environment that replicates a pathologist’s digital⏎workflow.11 This initiative is about giving pathologists a trusted environment to evaluate new tools⏎and stay in control of how technology fits into pathology practice. Federal support for these kinds of⏎initiatives will help drive innovation and facilitate more rapid integration in clinical care.⏎Another administrative hurdle to AI adoption arises when coding, coverage, and payment pathways⏎are unclear or misaligned. In the absence of established or well-defined CPT codes, there is⏎uncertainty regarding documentation requirements, physician work attribution, and reimbursement.⏎This uncertainty can delay or prevent adoption regardless of a technology’s technical capability,⏎making clear and predictable coding pathways a prerequisite for adoption.⏎Consequently, the AMA CPT Editorial Panel serves as a key external governing body influencing AI⏎adoption by determining how AI services are classified, described, and coded. In 2022, the AMA⏎CPT Editorial Panel adopted Appendix S into the CPT code set, which establishes the AI taxonomy⏎for medical services and procedures. Appendix S was developed by the Digital Medicine Payment⏎Advisory Group (DMPAG), a specialized workgroup convened by the AMA, and is used to classify⏎AI-enabled medical services and procedures as assistive, augmentative, or autonomous based on⏎the work performed by the AI application. Recognizing the growth in AI and other digital health tools,⏎the AMA CPT Editorial Panel has also convened the AMA Digital Medicine Coding Committee⏎(DMCC). The DMCC is currently developing comprehensive recommendations on coding, coverage,⏎and payment for digital medicine. This includes discussions related to a new category of CPT codes⏎for algorithmic services that do not include traditional interpretative physician or other qualified health⏎care professional work. The new categorization for digital medicine codes will focus on the distinction⏎11 https://newsroom.cap.org/latest-news/immersive-ai-platform-for-pathologists-set-to-launch-at-cap25-in-orlando/s/f72bb232-⏎0294-430e-a734-7a55f4588894; https://www.cap.org/member-resources/councils-committees/informatics-committee/artificial-⏎intelligence-pathology-resources#ai-studio.⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎between augmentative and autonomous services to account for and appropriately value physician⏎work.⏎Through the combined efforts of the DMPAG and DMCC, the AMA CPT Editorial Panel can provide⏎the clarity health care organizations need to adopt AI technologies within established regulatory and⏎payment frameworks. Accordingly, the CAP recommends that HHS work directly with the AMA CPT⏎Editorial Panel to establish a clear, consistent, and clinically appropriate coding framework that⏎supports the responsible adoption of AI for clinical care.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate⏎the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎CAP Response: The benefits of interoperability include wider availability of laboratory data at the⏎point of patient care, reduction of redundant testing, decreased interpretation errors leading to⏎unnecessary treatment or patient harm, and reduction in costs of system implementation and⏎maintenance—all of these will accelerate the development, adoption, and use of AI for clinical care.⏎With respect to the promotion of interoperability of laboratory data, regulations related to laboratory⏎data interoperability should acknowledge and account for the limitations in pathologists’ responsibility⏎for and control of laboratory data once the data is reported to and/or finalized in downstream⏎healthcare systems such as the EHR. Data exchange or provision requirements should use⏎consistent, open (i.e., nonproprietary) standards and widely accepted coding terminologies.⏎Moreover, regulations around laboratory data interoperability should incorporate financial support to⏎assist laboratories with the cost of implementation. This is because the value of interoperable⏎laboratory data accrues primarily to users of the data (that is, patients, clinicians, and public health⏎policy makers), while the cost of standardization accrues to data creators (that is, laboratories). This⏎disparity between cost and benefit should be addressed as part of the regulatory design to avoid⏎imposing a financial burden on pathologists and laboratories. The CAP envisions that as AI systems⏎improve, developers will be able to train them to transform existing multivariate data into⏎standardized, clinically meaningful form, thereby avoiding considerable investment in standardizing⏎data inputs. This standardization will enable greater use of important diagnostic data from the⏎laboratory setting.⏎* * * * *⏎Thank you for the opportunity to submit these comments. The CAP looks forward to working with⏎HHS to help promote high-quality AI implementation. To conclude, the CAP encourages the HHS to⏎consider the unique insights from pathologists and laboratory medicine in its assessment in how it⏎can accelerate the adoption and use of AI in clinical care. AI models must be transparent and⏎explainable for pathologists to best fulfill their duties concerning AI—this transparency and⏎explainability will increase the use of AI in laboratory medicine. The CAP stands willing to work with⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100⏎⏎the HHS to provide its members’ perspectives as CLIA laboratory directors, section directors, and⏎peer reviewers for the CAP's Laboratory Accreditation Program and how this experience can be⏎applied to accelerating the adoption and use of AI in clinical care. Questions regarding AI regulation⏎can be directed to Han Tran at htran@cap.org. Questions regarding AI reimbursement can be⏎directed to Todd Klemp at tklemp@cap.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎College of American Pathologists⏎College of American Pathologists⏎1001 G Street, NW, Suite 425W⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎202-354-7100♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0027♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Hospice and Palliative Medicine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Hospice and Palliative Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On behalf of the more than 5,000 members of the American Academy of⏎Hospice and Palliative Medicine (AAHPM), thank you for the opportunity⏎to respond to the Department of Health and Human Service (HHS)⏎request for information (RFI) on accelerating the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. AAHPM is the⏎professional organization for physicians specializing in Hospice and⏎Palliative Medicine (HPM). Our membership also includes nurses, social⏎workers, spiritual care providers, and other health professionals deeply⏎committed to improving quality of life for the expanding population of⏎patients facing serious illness as well as their families and caregivers.⏎Together, we strive to advance the field and ensure that patients across⏎all communities and geographies have access to high-quality palliative⏎and hospice care.⏎AAHPM appreciates that AI can have positive impact on the delivery of⏎clinical care, including for patients with serious illness. However, we also⏎recognize that there are inherent risks that come with use of AI when⏎appropriate safeguards are not in place. We therefore appreciate the⏎opportunity to provide the input below, which we believe could help to⏎maximize the benefits of AI in serious illness care, while also ensuring⏎that AI is used safely and responsibly.⏎AI in Serious Illness Care⏎AAHPM believes that the application of AI in serious illness care holds⏎great promise in informing shared decision making, treatment planning,⏎and the identification of necessary psychosocial and spiritual supports, all⏎with the aim of ensuring that patients receive care that is consistent with⏎⏎their goals and preferences. It can also help to reduce provider burden, including through assistance with⏎administrative tasks such as medical record documentation and coding and billing. Importantly, this⏎promise extends to the delivery of hospice care for patients near the end of life, who (along with their⏎families and caregivers) may be at one of the most stressful and vulnerable points in their lives.⏎In order for this promise to be realized, however, investments must be made to support development of⏎AI for serious illness care. Too often, AI tools are targeted towards intervention-based care. In our⏎members’ experience, for example, ambient listening AI has been relatively effective in capturing⏎discussions about procedures or imaging, but it does a poor job of extracting information from⏎discussions about goals and values, which are central to the delivery of palliative care. AI that is designed⏎to support serious illness care would ideally address these limitations.⏎To ensure that seriously ill patients and their palliative care teams are able to fully reap the benefits of AI,⏎AAHPM urges HHS to support development of AI for use in serious illness care, including hospice care.⏎Such AI should include non-device clinical AI that augments communications, goals-of-care⏎documentation, symptom assessment, and care planning. AI tools that can be used in the delivery of⏎virtual care – for example for assessing caregiver stress and functional decline virtually – would also be⏎valuable. We also highlight the importance of caregiver involvement providing care to seriously ill⏎patients, who often require assistance in day-to-day activities of daily living, not to mention accessing⏎care or adhering to treatment plans. We therefore stress the importance of AI for serious illness care⏎prioritizing caregiver-inclusive design, in order to reduce caregiver burden and optimize caregivers’⏎experience.⏎Regulatory, Payment Policy, or Programmatic Design⏎Changes to Prioritize⏎AAHPM appreciates HHS’ interest in identifying regulatory, payment policy, and programmatic design⏎changes to prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care, and we offer our⏎recommendations below.⏎Coverage and Payment⏎To begin, we believe there is a need for clearer pathways for providing coverage and payment of AI non-⏎device tools, including for the Medicare program. Under current Medicare rules, for example, AI⏎applications used in clinical practice generally do not receive separate payment, but instead are⏎reimbursed as part of existing service codes, leaving uncertainty as to whether physicians can recoup their⏎costs for the use of AI tools. AAHPM therefore encourages HHS to establish clear policy that provides a⏎pathway for AI tools to be covered and reimbursed appropriately.⏎Patient and Caregiver Protections⏎We also emphasize the need to ensure there are adequate protections for patients and caregivers when⏎services are delivered using AI – particularly when AI is used to inform treatment decisions or assist in⏎insurance coverage determinations – and we recommend that HHS promulgate policies that ensure⏎accountability in the delivery of services furnished using AI and support safe, effective, and high-quality⏎⏎care. This includes policies requiring human oversight, upholding physician judgment, and establishing⏎quality accountability when AI is used for anything other than administrative workflows.⏎For human oversight, at a minimum, HHS should require human-in-the-loop use of AI tools when used for⏎clinical care – particularly when high-risk and high-impact treatment decisions are at stake. In such cases⏎– for example decisions to withhold or withdraw life sustaining treatments – safeguards are critical,⏎including requirements for appropriate clinical oversight by the physician or non-physician practitioner⏎(NPP) directly involved in managing the patient’s treatment and care plan. To ensure that physicians and⏎other practitioners are incorporating human oversight, we recommend that requirements be put in place⏎regarding documentation of such oversight when AI is used in the delivery of care. Similarly, we note that⏎AI is increasingly being used by payers to make coverage determinations, for example through prior⏎authorization processes. Human oversight is necessary in these cases as well, and HHS should restrict the⏎use of AI to make automated denial decisions without human review.⏎We believe that human oversight is particularly important in serious illness care, especially at the end of⏎life when AI algorithms may suggest poor outcomes or limited benefit of treatment options, and we⏎underscore the importance of honoring independent clinical judgment as an important safeguard in⏎protecting patients’ access to care. Indeed, we believe that physicians maintain ultimate responsibility for⏎treatment decisions, regardless of the use of AI, and therefore exercise of physician judgement should be⏎paramount. HHS should therefore ensure that there are appropriate limits on the use of AI in making⏎clinical decisions and clarify that AI should only be used to augment physician judgement, not replace it.⏎And in no case should AI be able to impose restrictions on access to medically appropriate treatment or⏎symptom management. We can imagine, for example, AI determining that a certain treatment may not⏎be appropriate for a seriously ill patient given algorithms suggesting likely poor outcomes, or AI-based⏎restrictions on the use of opioids or other medications appropriate for patients with serious illness that⏎are outside of common use. We could also envision AI algorithms recommending against hospice⏎election, for example, based on algorithms that conclude the patient has a prognosis greater than 6⏎months. In neither case should these algorithms be able to limit access to treatment choices that are in⏎opposition to physician judgement and patient preferences. As an added precaution to protect physician⏎judgment and support transparency and accountability, HHS should require audit trails and⏎counterfactual explanations when AI is used for clinical decisions.⏎Finally, we recommend that HHS establish a framework for ensuring accountability for quality of care⏎when AI is used in the delivery of care. For example, HHS should explore tying use of AI tools to quality⏎measures. For serious illness care, this could include measures related to symptom improvement,⏎documented care goals, and avoidance of burdensome transitions.⏎Care Team Protections⏎In addition to protections for patients and caregivers, we recommend a policy framework be established⏎to offer protections for care teams. For example, our members have raised concerns that health systems⏎may seek to use AI to unreasonably increase output and service delivery. AAHPM believes that AI should⏎be used to augment care teams, and can be used to improve efficiency, but reasonable limits are⏎necessary to protect the well-being of the care team and limit the risk of burnout.⏎Additionally, HHS should establish a policy framework for care teams’ reasonable use of AI for low-risk⏎activities, including those that support administrative workflows. In particular, we point to the use of⏎ambient listening AI to support medical record documentation, which has been subject to litigation based⏎⏎on patient consent and privacy concerns. While we agree that privacy and security must be protected,⏎we also recognize that current regulations may not keep pace with technological advancements. Updates⏎to regulations to support reasonable use of AI in low-risk situations would protect care teams’ ability to⏎leverage AI to increase efficiency and reduce burden.⏎Evaluation of AI Tools⏎HHS requests input on evaluation of AI tools, which AAHPM agrees should be a priority. However, we⏎highlight the need to evaluate AI in the context of the care being delivered. In particular, we note that AI⏎for serious illness care should be evaluated on outcomes that are meaningful in such care delivery,⏎including on metrics related to communication, prognostic framing, and care decisions. Metrics relating⏎to imaging or diagnosis, for example, would not be as relevant for the delivery of serious illness care.⏎Likewise, benchmarks for performance should also be specific to the care being delivered. Accordingly,⏎we recommend that HHS support the development of benchmarks for serious illness care that consider⏎the distribution of age, multimorbidity, and functional status reflected in seriously ill populations.⏎We also recommend that HHS support rigorous evaluation of AI tools both pre-deployment and post-⏎deployment. Such evaluation should include human-centered user experience testing, bias audits on⏎prognostic or triage recommendations for different populations, cybersecurity and data privacy checks for⏎sensitive information, and routine drift monitoring and outcome tracking. We therefore recommend that⏎HHS advance frameworks that would make clear when and how often these evaluations should be⏎conducted and who (between provider, developer, or third-party auditor) is best positioned to conduct⏎them. Effective analysis of AI tools would also require transparency on part of the developer in order to⏎evaluate training inputs and applicability of AI outputs to all relevant patient populations.⏎Support for Private Sector Activities⏎HHS requests input on ways to best support private sector activities to promote innovative and effective AI⏎use in clinical care, pointing to potential use of accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing, and⏎credentialing.⏎AAHPM agrees that accreditation or certification requirements could support reliability of AI tools and⏎would support HHS efforts to foster industry-driven programs. This support could be through grants or⏎cooperative agreements, for example, and should include programs that focus on the use of AI for serious⏎illness care. We recommend that accreditation or certification requirements fostered by HHS emphasize⏎human oversight, documentation of limitations, and transparent escalation protocols.⏎Leveraging Interoperability to Accelerate AI Adoption⏎HHS seeks information on where enhanced interoperability would widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care.⏎AAHPM agrees that greater interoperability would support expanded use of AI, but we highlight the need⏎to ensure that interoperability requirements address serious illness care. As a starting point, we believe⏎that standard application programming interfaces (APIs) should capture data elements relevant to serious⏎illness care, including related to:⏎⏎• Goals of care⏎• Advance directives, as well as information on physician orders for life-sustaining treatment⏎(POLST)⏎• Primary decision-maker and/or healthcare proxy⏎• Symptom and burden scales⏎• Palliative performance status⏎• Caregiver needs⏎• Preferred place of care and death, as applicable⏎Research and Funding Priorities⏎HHS requests information on specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care.⏎AAHPM believes research addressing the use of AI for serious illness care must be prioritized. This includes⏎research into AI tools that would improve the quality of provider-patient communication and decision⏎support, as well as AI tools to support symptom monitoring and medication safety. We also recommend⏎research to assess AI implementation, for example the impacts of AI implementation on provider burden⏎and clinical workflows, as well as the impacts to patients and caregivers. Research in these areas will help⏎to address major evidence gaps that are likely to have consequential impacts in the delivery of care for⏎patients with serious illness.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you, again, for considering our comments in response to the RFI on AI in clinical care. As noted⏎above, we believe advancements in this area can support more efficient and effective serious illness care,⏎as long as appropriate safeguards are in place, and we look forward to the advancements that further⏎investments and policy guidance can foster. We would be happy to continue to serve as a resource for⏎HHS as you undertake this important work. Please feel free to reach out to Wendy Chill, Senior Director,⏎Health Policy and Government Relations, with any questions at wchill@aahpm.org or (847) 375-6733.⏎Sincerely,⏎Kristina Newport, MD FAAHPM, HMDC⏎Chief Medical Officer, American Academy of Hospice & Palliative Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0028♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Medical Informatics Association (AMIA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Medical Informatics Association (AMIA)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Jim O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary and National Coordinator⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎330 C Street NW⏎Floor 7, Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via Regulations.gov⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The American Medical Informatics Association (AMIA) appreciates the opportunity to⏎respond to the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator⏎for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) Request for Information (RFI) regarding⏎policies to accelerate the responsible adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in⏎clinical care.⏎AMIA is the professional home for more than 6,000 informatics professionals, representing⏎frontline clinicians, researchers, public health experts, and educators who bring meaning⏎to data, manage information, and generate new knowledge across the research and⏎healthcare enterprise. As the voice of the nation’s biomedical and health informatics⏎professionals, AMIA advances health and wellness by moving research from bench to⏎bedside and evaluating interventions, innovations, and public policy across care settings⏎and patient populations.⏎Below, we respond to the numbered questions presented in the RFI.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎American Medical Informatics Association⏎6218 Georgia Avenue NW, Suite #1, PMB 3077, Washington, DC 20011⏎www.AMIA.org | 301.657.1291⏎Page 1 of 5⏎⏎The most significant barriers include limited access to clinically relevant and⏎context-specific data, insufficient implementation infrastructure, unclear return on⏎investment (ROI), and inadequate governance capacity within health systems.⏎AI developers, particularly startups, often lack local clinical expertise and access to⏎representative datasets necessary to build safe and effective tools. Adoption is further⏎constrained by insufficient interoperability, limited technical infrastructure, and workforce⏎readiness gaps, especially in community-based and under-resourced health systems.⏎Many health systems also lack clear guidance on how to assess safety, effectiveness, and⏎ROI prior to adoption. Clinicians must understand whether an AI tool improves outcomes,⏎reduces burden, or enhances quality, not merely that it is technologically advanced.⏎Adoption without a defined implementation strategy and measurable value proposition⏎undermines trust and sustainability.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize a risk-based regulatory framework that aligns oversight with the⏎potential clinical impact of AI tools:⏎• Low-risk tools (e.g., workflow optimization) should be subject to streamlined⏎oversight.⏎• Moderate-risk tools should demonstrate validation and ongoing monitoring.⏎• High-risk tools should require robust pre- and post-deployment evaluation,⏎algorithmic transparency, and continuous performance monitoring.⏎When AI is used as a clinical intervention, transparency regarding algorithm function,⏎limitations, and evidence base is essential. Clinicians cannot responsibly rely on “black⏎box” systems without understanding how effectiveness is assessed and how performance⏎is monitored.⏎From a payment perspective, reimbursement should be rewarded with demonstrated⏎value, not adoption for its own sake. AI should function like a well-designed clinical⏎instrument: improving outcomes, reducing burden, or enhancing quality. HHS should⏎consider pilot programs and regulatory sandboxes to evaluate effectiveness before broad⏎reimbursement.⏎Programmatic priorities should include:⏎American Medical Informatics Association⏎6218 Georgia Avenue NW, Suite #1, PMB 3077, Washington, DC 20011⏎www.AMIA.org | 301.657.1291⏎Page 2 of 5⏎⏎• Implementation funding support, particularly for under-resourced systems.⏎• Incentives tied to measurable reductions in administrative burden or improvements⏎in quality metrics.⏎• Ongoing HHS evaluation to determine whether AI tools merit inclusion in payment⏎schedules.⏎Payment modernization must balance time savings, complexity reduction, and outcome⏎improvement, rather than relying on a one-size-fits-all reimbursement models.⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist, and⏎what role should HHS play?⏎Non-medical AI tools used in clinical contexts—including consumer wearables (e.g., Fitbit⏎devices) and general-purpose AI systems—raise novel governance challenges when⏎integrated into care delivery.⏎Key issues include:⏎• Liability and indemnification: Reliance on opaque AI outputs may shift liability to⏎clinicians despite limited insight into tool functionality.⏎• Privacy and secondary data use: Patients express concern about whether⏎identifiable data are incorporated into commercial datasets.⏎• Lack of professional mediation: Consumer-facing AI may provide health-related⏎information without clinical oversight, increasing the risk of misinformation.⏎HHS should clarify regulatory boundaries between medical and non-medical AI used in⏎clinical workflows and establish guardrails related to transparency, training data⏎provenance, data monetization practices, and disclosure requirements. Clear⏎accountability frameworks will strengthen public trust.⏎4. How can HHS support private sector activities to promote effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎HHS can support private sector innovation by:⏎• Encouraging collaborative development between AI developers and health systems.⏎• Supporting shared benchmarking datasets and evaluation frameworks.⏎American Medical Informatics Association⏎6218 Georgia Avenue NW, Suite #1, PMB 3077, Washington, DC 20011⏎www.AMIA.org | 301.657.1291⏎Page 3 of 5⏎⏎• Promoting implementation toolkits addressing governance, validation, monitoring,⏎and workforce training.⏎Accreditation and certification efforts should emphasize transparency, bias testing and⏎mitigation, real-world performance monitoring, and human-centered evaluation. Federal⏎leadership in convening stakeholders to harmonize standards would reduce duplication⏎and accelerate responsible adoption.⏎5. Who influences AI adoption decisions within health care organizations?⏎AI adoption decisions are typically influenced by C-suite leadership (e.g., Chief Medical⏎Information Officers, Chief Information Officers, Chief Medical Informatics Officers), IT⏎departments, and executive governance bodies. Nurses, frontline clinicians, and⏎operational staff—despite experiencing the greatest workflow impact—are often⏎underrepresented in decision-making.⏎Primary administrative hurdles include:⏎• Unclear liability frameworks⏎• Absence of formal AI governance structures⏎• Limited time and resources for workforce training⏎• Clinicians being responsible for answering patient and caregiver questions about AI⏎use⏎• Uncertain implementation strategies⏎• Lack of clear evidence regarding safety and effectiveness⏎Successful adoption requires inclusive governance models and sufficient time for clinician⏎education and training.⏎6. What challenges do patients and caregivers want AI to address, and what concerns⏎do they have?⏎Patients and caregivers hope AI can:⏎• Improve access to care⏎• Reduce administrative burdens⏎• Personalize treatment recommendations⏎American Medical Informatics Association⏎6218 Georgia Avenue NW, Suite #1, PMB 3077, Washington, DC 20011⏎www.AMIA.org | 301.657.1291⏎Page 4 of 5⏎⏎• Enhance care coordination⏎• Support identification and evaluation of new treatment options⏎However, concerns include:⏎• Diminished relationships with clinical providers⏎• Privacy and protection of personally identifiable information⏎• Secondary monetization of health data⏎• Algorithmic bias and inequity⏎• Lack of transparency in how AI informs clinical decisions⏎• Use of AI tools without meaningful patient awareness or consent⏎Patients often prioritize safety, fairness, and transparency over cost efficiency. Historical⏎examples of data use without adequate disclosure continue to shape public trust. HHS⏎should ensure that economic incentives do not outpace ethical safeguards.⏎***⏎Artificial intelligence holds significant promise to improve clinical care; however, adoption⏎must be purposeful, evidence-driven, and clearly aligned with patient benefit. AMIA⏎encourages HHS to pursue a balanced strategy that aligns regulatory oversight with risk,⏎modernizes payment to reward demonstrated value, and strengthens governance and⏎implementation capacity across care settings. Robust privacy protections, transparency,⏎and ongoing evaluation will be essential to sustaining public trust.⏎Thank you for your consideration. For questions or additional information, please contact⏎Tayler Williams, Senior Manager of Public Policy, AMIA, at twilliams@amia.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Eileen Koski⏎AMIA Public Policy Committee Chair⏎American Medical Informatics Association⏎6218 Georgia Avenue NW, Suite #1, PMB 3077, Washington, DC 20011⏎www.AMIA.org | 301.657.1291⏎Page 5 of 5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0029♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bynon, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Bynon♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David W. Bynon⏎January 3, 2026⏎To:⏎The Honorable Jim O’Neill, Deputy Secretary of Health and Human Services⏎Dr. Stella Mandl, Director, Office of Healthcare Experience and Interoperability (OHEI)⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP/ONC)⏎Re:⏎Submission to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎(Docket ID: HHS-ASTP-2025-23641)⏎Subject: A Scalable Protocol for Deflationary AI Governance and Program Integrity⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Dr. Mandl,⏎I am submitting the enclosed comment in response to HHS’s Request for Information on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical care. The core⏎challenge HHS faces is not whether AI can be deployed, but how to scale its use without⏎expanding federal operational burden or relying on opaque, non-scalable model-level⏎oversight.⏎The enclosed comment describes a scalable, non-device AI governance protocol that⏎enables HHS to accelerate adoption by aligning semantic responsibility with carriers,⏎providers, and intermediaries—where existing accuracy obligations already reside—while⏎preserving federal oversight through deterministic, audit-ready outputs. This approach⏎operationalizes the “AI-enabling infrastructure and documentation” principles articulated⏎in OMB Memorandum M-25-21 by making authoritative policy meaning explicit, versioned,⏎and auditable for high-impact administrative AI.⏎Rather than relying on probabilistic inference from narrative text, this protocol requires AI⏎systems to retrieve from authoritative, machine-readable policy memory. Doing so enables⏎AI outputs to be verified directly against versioned, cryptographically anchored definitions⏎without inspecting model internals or reconstructing inference. The result is audit-by-⏎design oversight that reduces downstream reconciliation costs currently borne by CMS,⏎OMHA, and beneficiary support operations, while allowing industry-led deployment of AI-⏎assisted care and benefits operations without introducing model-specific regulatory⏎regimes or expanding federal operational scope.⏎⏎If useful, I would welcome the opportunity to brief the HHS AI Governance Board or CMMI⏎staff on how this protocol could be explored through existing sub-regulatory guidance or⏎voluntary innovation pilots.⏎Respectfully,⏎David W. Bynon⏎David W. Bynon⏎Independent Systems Architect⏎619-889-0300⏎bynon@yahoo.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0030♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from National Council of State Boards of Nursing (NCSBN), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔National Council of State Boards of Nursing (NCSBN)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Attn: HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (Document ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 90 FR 60108)⏎Submitted electronically via https://www.regulations.gov/document/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The National Council of State Boards of Nursing (NCSBN) appreciates the opportunity to submit comment⏎in response to the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) request for information “Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.”⏎NCSBN is an independent, non-profit association comprising 59 boards of nursing (BONs) from across the⏎U.S., the District of Columbia, and four U.S. territories. BONs are responsible for protecting the public⏎through regulation of licensure, nursing practice, and discipline of the nearly 6 million registered nurses⏎(RNs), licensed practical/vocational (LPN/VNs), and Advanced Practice Registered Nurses (APRNs) in the⏎U.S. with active licenses.⏎In this comment NCSBN specifically addresses areas where it believes HHS’s RFI on health sector uses of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) can benefit from research exploring ways to mitigate risks associated with AI to⏎prioritize patient safety. This includes examining algorithmic bias in AI systems, transparency and proper⏎oversight of AI systems, and ethical or professional concerns for nurses working in settings with AI⏎technology.⏎Responses are provided, from a nursing regulation and patient safety perspective, in response to question⏎#9 – “what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?”⏎Algorithmic Bias in AI Systems⏎As the use of AI technologies becomes increasingly prevalent, policymakers and healthcare providers must⏎take precautions against the risk of algorithmic bias in AI systems. Algorithmic systems can mirror⏎structural data gaps, entrenched biases, and limited knowledge about specific patient populations, which⏎in turn can reproduce or amplify demographic and socioeconomic inequities in healthcare. Algorithmic⏎bias occurs when AI systems inadvertently perpetuate existing healthcare gaps, and if not properly trained⏎1⏎⏎and validated on varied datasets, the algorithms that run these systems could lead to unequal treatment⏎recommendations or diagnoses.i⏎Often, algorithmic failures are not anomalies but can be symptomatic of broader deficiencies in the design,⏎validation, and deployment of AI-driven technologies that fail to represent a range of patient populations.⏎As noted in a recent Journal of Nursing Regulation article, “disparities highlight the ethical obligation of⏎technology companies to design systems that are inclusive and equitable as a cornerstone of innovation,⏎which will give rise to technologies that contribute to rather than hinder the advancement of optimal⏎human-technology interaction.”ii Additionally, once AI technologies are deployed, addressing such⏎concerns can be challenging in a healthcare setting, particularly in rural healthcare settings where resource⏎limitations and miscommunications that occur when patients are transferred between facilities contribute⏎to adverse patient outcomes.iii These systemic inequities suggest a proactive approach is needed to ensure⏎AI technologies that are deployed mitigate harm and ensure equity rather than exacerbate such concerns.iv⏎A 2023 Journal of the American Medical Association study demonstrates the importance of ensuring AI⏎models are not biased.v According to the study, nearly 67% of clinical participants (physicians, nurse⏎practitioners, physician assistants, and others) were not aware AI models could be systematically biased⏎based on patient demographics.vi In the study, hospital-based clinicians reviewed clinical vignettes of⏎patients hospitalized with acute respiratory failure, including symptoms, physical examination findings,⏎laboratory results, and chest radiographs. Participants were asked to assess the likelihood of certain⏎diagnoses like pneumonia, heart failure, or chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, with some vignettes⏎supplemented by AI-generated predictions and image-based explanations outlining the model’s reasoning.⏎While clinician diagnostic accuracy improved modestly, these gains were undermined when the AI models⏎contained bias. The study found a “systematically biased model decreased accuracy by 11.3 percentage⏎points, which only improved a nonsignificant 2.3 percentage points when the model included explanations⏎for its recommendations.” vii Notably, the article found AI models offering explanations might not be⏎enough to mitigate harm. These findings suggest that transparency alone may not be enough to counteract⏎embedded algorithmic bias and that reliance on flawed models can meaningfully degrade clinical decision-⏎making and the accuracy and quality of patient care.⏎Outside of algorithmic biases related to patient demographics, other systemic AI or machine learning⏎biases can include “label bias” where a label applied to patients does not mean the same thing for all⏎patients; “missing data bias” where missing information affects the predictive value of a model; “training-⏎serving bias” where a model is deployed on patients whose data are not similar to the data on which the⏎model was trained; and “automation bias” where the provider is unaware that a model is less accurate for⏎a specific group and may trust it too much or rely on inaccurate predictions.viii In nursing contexts in⏎particular, a human-centered framework is essential to ensure that technological innovation⏎complements, rather than compromises, professional judgment and patient advocacy. As emphasized in⏎research, “Nurses must be involved in the design, development, and implementation of AI technologies to⏎ensure that these tools align with their clinical expertise and patient-centered values.” ix⏎Transparency, Model Limitations, and Oversight of AI Technologies⏎Research suggests that a lack of transparency in technological systems compounds ethical concerns for⏎clinicians, particularly pertaining to AI-based technologies when clinicians are unable to interpret how⏎decisions are made by ‘black box’ algorithms.x This opacity erodes trust, complicates accountability, and⏎2⏎⏎places healthcare providers in ethically uncertain positions when errors occur.xi One example that⏎illustrates this concern is an independent review of a sepsis prediction tool in 2021 that illustrated the risks⏎of opaque AI systems in clinical care.xii The model was protected as proprietary software and developed⏎using data from only three health systems. There was also limited ability for clinicians and other systems⏎to validate the logic used to compute sepsis alerts.xiii When an external validation study was conducted⏎involving nursing informatics experts, physicians, and engineers, that study found the tool had low⏎sensitivity and high alert fatigue.xiv The study also noted the lack of publicly available performance data,⏎which left clinicians responsible for outcomes without adequate transparency. Nurses who used the⏎system and other advocates further criticized it for excluding nursing documentation, restricting overrides,⏎and reducing holistic nursing care to fragmented data tasks.xv⏎In addition to issues with transparency, health facilities, providers, and the companies that build AI systems⏎must be vigilant against other model limitations, such as hallucinations or data drift. Independent⏎healthcare research nonprofit and patient safety organization ECRI identified risks with AI-enabled health⏎technologies as the top health technology hazard concern for 2025.xvi The report warned that AI systems⏎may contain technical vulnerabilities such as “hallucinations,” in which AI systems generate false or⏎misleading outputs, as well as performance instability resulting from data drift and model “brittleness,” or⏎the inability to adapt appropriately to novel conditions. Further, it cautions that model degradation over⏎time, particularly in systems that continuously ingest new data or operate amid shifting clinical⏎environments, further compounds these risks.xvii The report stresses that organizational factors, including⏎insufficient oversight, weak data governance, and overreliance on automated outputs, can heighten⏎patient safety hazards, cautioning that “placing too much trust in an AI model—and failing to appropriately⏎scrutinize its output—may lead to inappropriate patient care decisions.” xviii⏎The Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) Office of Inspector General (OIG) has also recently highlighted⏎the importance of oversight and risk management for AI tools used in clinical care. Last month, the VA OIG⏎released a preliminary result advisory memorandum communicating a potential patient safety risk related⏎to Veterans Health Administration’s (VHA’s) use of generative AI chat tools for clinical care and⏎documentation.xix The OIG’s preliminary report expressed that the office found enough concern to issue a⏎preliminary memo to put VHA leadership on notice about AI-related risk pending a final review. The report⏎also generally noted that generative AI can produce inaccurate outputs, including omissions which may⏎affect diagnosis and treatment decisions, and expressed concern about the VHA’s ability to promote and⏎safeguard patient safety without a standardized process for managing AI-related risks.xx⏎These examples highlight how important transparency, understanding technological limitations, and⏎sufficient human oversight are to ensure AI does not undermine the provision of reliable, safe, and⏎accurate patient care. Ensuring these and other safeguards are in place to manage risk should therefore⏎be a high priority for the healthcare tech industry and providers alike.⏎Ethical and Professional Standards Concerns for Nurses⏎Nurses constitute the largest segment of the healthcare workforce at nearly 6 million providers⏎nationwide. They are often among the most familiar providers in a patient care team. Given the nursing⏎profession's historically high trust and visibility among patients, patients tend to overwhelmingly hold⏎nurses accountable for decisions made with AI tools.xxi From the patient’s vantage point, AI-enabled care⏎may appear streamlined and intuitive; however the unseen side can entail ethical strain and moral conflict⏎3⏎⏎for nurses, particularly when education, training, and institutional support for AI integration are⏎inconsistent or insufficient.xxii As AI tools are increasingly employed in clinical settings, nurses are no longer⏎merely users of technology. They are ethical stewards, patient advocates, and system translators who⏎must bridge algorithmic output and human-centered care.xxiii⏎A nurse’s responsibility becomes more complex, not less, as AI takes on more active roles in clinical triage,⏎diagnostics, and risk assessments.xxiv Rather than replacing nurses, AI increases the need for clinicians’⏎strong judgment and oversight. Recent research highlights the importance of building critical thinking into⏎the daily practices involving AI’s ethical, social, and clinical impacts to prevent dehumanization and⏎depersonalization of care.xxv Healthcare organizations can minimize risks by prioritizing comprehensive⏎training, support, and oversight for nurses using AI technologies to ensure nurses have the knowledge and⏎tools to use technology responsibly while also maintaining professional autonomy. Additionally,⏎interdisciplinary collaboration is essential to align technology use with nursing values and legal⏎protections, minimizing the risk of improper use and its consequences.xxvi For example, clinical decision⏎tools powered by AI may issue recommendations that conflict with a nurse's professional judgment,⏎creating ethical and legal dilemmas. Nurses may face accusations of negligence or failure to uphold their⏎professional standards if errors occur as a result.xxvii For nursing regulators, these situations require both⏎the understanding of systems used and the clinical decision-making of the nurse. Integrating AI⏎technologies into nursing workflows demands technical proficiency and ethical judgment to ensure⏎equitable and patient-centered outcomes. It also warrants recognizing the profession's role as active co-⏎designers and leaders in shaping AI to be consistent with nursing values and priorities.xxviii⏎The Role of the Nursing Regulator⏎Recognizing the integration of AI into healthcare delivery, it is critical to recognize the role nursing⏎regulators have in the delivery of safe and quality nursing care to the public. BONs can ensure nurses⏎receive proper education about AI in healthcare. Boards of Nursing across the country protect the public⏎through the nursing education program approval and oversight process. Program approval is the official⏎recognition of nursing education programs that meet standards of approval established by Boards of⏎Nursing. The program approval process ensures that programs are qualified to prepare students for⏎licensure and safe and competent practice.xxix Nursing regulators could play a role in ensuring the⏎integration of education on AI systems into nursing program curricula. Nursing students graduating with⏎greater AI literacy, understanding how the systems are built and utilized, and the expectations for the⏎nurse will protect patients and strengthen healthcare.⏎BONs also protect the public through investigating complaints and executing disciplinary processes. As AI⏎continues to play an increasing role in health care, it is critical for regulators to understand how AI was⏎utilized in the delivery of care that led to error and/or patient harm. Increasing transparency into AI⏎systems is vital for nursing regulators to be able to identify potential system issues that may directly impact⏎licensee decision-making. While nurses remain responsible for clinical decisions, there is a need for⏎understanding and regulating the AI systems that may be utilized when those decisions are made.⏎Conclusion⏎We appreciate the chance to submit comments in response to this RFI and look forward to opportunities⏎to work with HHS on policies impacting the nursing profession and its regulation. Nursing regulator⏎4⏎⏎expertise should be sought and included in discussions to ensure patient safety in the use of AI in⏎healthcare delivery. If you have any questions or would like additional information, please do not hesitate⏎to contact Ashley Shelton, NCSBN’s Director of Federal and External Affairs (ashelton@ncsbn.org). We look⏎forward to remaining engaged with you on these important issues.⏎Sincerely,⏎Ashley N. Shelton, JD⏎Director, Federal & External Affairs⏎National Council of State Boards of Nursing⏎i Ahmed, S.K.. (2024). Artificial intelligence in nursing: Current trends, possibilities and pitfalls. Journal of Medicine,⏎Surgery, and Public Health, Volume 3, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.glmedi.2024.100072. Accessed February 17, 2026,⏎at https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2949916X24000252⏎ii Johnson, E. et al. (2025). A critical juncture: Reimagining nursing professional identity and regulation in the ethical⏎integration of innovation and technology in healthcare. Journal of Nursing Regulation. 16(1): 10 – 16.⏎https://www.journalofnursingregulation.com/article/S2155-8256(25)00049-3/fulltext⏎iii Id.⏎iv Id.⏎v Jabbour S, Fouhey D, Shepard S, et al. (2023). Measuring the Impact of AI in the Diagnosis of Hospitalized Patients:⏎A Randomized Clinical Vignette Survey Study. JAMA. 330(23):2275–2284. doi:10.1001/jama.2023.22295. Accessed⏎February 17, 2026, at https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2812908⏎vi Id.⏎vii Id.⏎viii Rajkomar A. (2018). Ensuring Fairness in Machine Learning to Advance Health Equity. Ann Intern Med. 2018 Dec⏎18;169(12):866-872. Accessed February 17, 2026, at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6594166/⏎ix Ahmed, S.K. (2024).⏎x Johnson, E. et al. (2025); See Fehr, J. et al. (2024). A trustworthy AI reality-check: The lack of transparency of⏎artificial intelligence products in healthcare. Front Digit Health. 6(2024). Accessed February 17, 2026, at⏎https://www.frontiersin.org/journals/digital-health/articles/10.3389/fdgth.2024.1267290/full⏎xi Johnson, E. et al. (2025).⏎xii Johnson, E. et al. (2025); See Wong, A. et al. (2021). External validation of a widely implemented proprietary⏎sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Internal Medicine. 2021; 181(8).⏎xiii Id.⏎xiv Id.⏎xv Id.⏎xvi ECRI. (2025). Top 10 Health Technology Hazards for 2025. Accessed February 17, 2026, at⏎https://cdn.shopify.com/s/files/1/0826/0472/0443/files/Top10HealthTechHazards_2025_ExecutiveBrief.pdf?v=173⏎3223585⏎xvii Id.⏎xviii Id.⏎xix U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs, Office of the Inspector General. (2026). Review of VHA’s Use of Generative⏎Artificial Intelligence. Accessed February 18, 2026, at https://vaoig.gov/sites/default/files/reports/2026-01/vaoig-⏎26-00182-42_final.pdf⏎xx Id.⏎5⏎⏎xxi Galatzan, B.J. et al. (2025). Regulating at the AI frontier: The collision of policy, regulation, and nursing practice.⏎Journal of Nursing Regulation. 16(3): 207 – 215. https://www.journalofnursingregulation.com/article/S2155-⏎8256(25)00101-2/fulltext⏎xxii Id.; See Benda, N. (2024). Patient Perspectives on AI for Mental Health Care: Cross-Sectional Survey Study.⏎Journal of Medical Internet Research (JMIR) Mental Health. 11, e58462. Accessed February 20, 2026, at⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11447436/⏎xxiii Galatzan, B.J. et al. (2025); See American Nurses Association. (2022). The ethical use of artificial intelligence in⏎nursing practice [position statement]. https://www.nursingworld.org/globalassets/practiceandpolicy/nursing-⏎excellence/ana-position-statements/the-ethical-use-of-artificial-intelligence-in-nursing-practice_bod-approved-⏎12_20_22.pdf; Johnson, E. et al. (2025).⏎xxiv Galatzan, B.J. et al. (2025).⏎xxv Id.; See El Arab et al. (2025). The role of AI in nursing education and practice: Umbrella review. Journal of⏎Medical Internet Research. 2025; 27, e69881. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12008698/⏎xxvi Johnson, E. et al. (2025); See Mello, M.M., Guha, N. (2024). Understanding liability risk from healthcare⏎AI [Policy brief]. Stanford University’s Institute for Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence.⏎https://hai.stanford.edu/policy-brief-understanding-liability-risk-healthcare-ai⏎xxvii Johnson, E. et al. (2025); See Reiner, G. (2024) 5 Risk control considerations as nurse practitioners' scope of⏎practice expands⏎Minority Nurse. https://minoritynurse.com/5-risk-control-considerations-as-nurse-practitioners-scope-of-practice-⏎expands⏎xxviii Johnson, E. et al. (2025).⏎xxix Spector, N. et al. (2018). Board of Nursing Approval of Registered Nurse Education Programs. 8(4): 22 – 31.⏎https://www.journalofnursingregulation.com/article/S2155-8256(17)30178-3/fulltext⏎6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0031♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔William♔Nguyen♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Mayo Clinic♔♔♔♔♔♔My concern with this RFI isn't about whether AI works. In many cases it does. We use ambient documentation tools at our facility and the time savings are real. But the framing of the questions in this request keeps pulling me back to something that I don't think the efficiency metrics are capturing, and I want to try to articulate it even if I don't have a clean policy solution to offer.⏎⏎When I look at our patient satisfaction data from 2023, the comments that stand out most are not about wait times or billing accuracy. They're about whether someone listened. Whether the physician seemed to know them. There's a patient feedback category we track internally that we call "felt seen" and it consistently correlates with trust, with treatment adherence, with readmission rates more strongly than almost any other variable. I keep thinking about what happens to that when the clinician in the room is partly occupied by reviewing an AI-generated summary of the patient, rather than the patient themselves.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I'm not categorically opposed to AI in clinical settings. That would be ridiculous given what I do for a living and the institution I work for. But there's something in how HHS has framed this RFI that treats the physician's time and attention as a resource to be liberated from friction, which I understand, but that framing skips over the fact that a lot of what looks like inefficiency is actually relational work. The fifteen minutes a physician spends getting a patient's history isn't just data collection. The patient experiences it as being known.⏎⏎I'm not sure how you write a regulation around that. And maybe that's part of my frustration with commenting here, because the questions in this RFI are oriented toward adoption rates, reimbursement pathways, and certification frameworks. Those matter. But if we build reimbursement incentives that reward AI-assisted visits without any mechanism for measuring whether the patient's experience of being genuinely attended to has degraded, we will have optimized the wrong thing.⏎⏎Regarding the question about evaluation frameworks specifically: I'd want HHS to consider requiring that any clinical AI tool subject to reimbursement incentives be evaluated not just on diagnostic accuracy or documentation completeness, but on patient-reported experience measures that get at relational quality. Not the generic HCAHPS questions that are already in use, but something more targeted. Whether patients felt the clinician was present with them, not just performing presence. I recognize this is harder to measure. It should be.⏎⏎The thing I keep coming back to is that when an AI tool is technically correct and the patient still feels like a case number, that's a clinical problem. Not a soft one. Patients who don't trust their care team don't follow through on treatment. They don't disclose symptoms that don't fit the narrative they think the physician has already formed. That has real downstream consequences and I have seen it.⏎⏎Mayo has the resources to think carefully about this. A lot of smaller systems will adopt whatever CMS reimburses and build workflows around incentives. Which means the incentive structure HHS builds here will shape what clinical care actually looks like for millions of patients. That's not abstract.⏎⏎I do want to flag one practical issue that may be outside the scope of what this RFI is asking but felt worth mentioning: the liability question for AI recommendations that inform but don't technically constitute a clinical decision is genuinely unresolved and it affects how our legal team advises our physicians on documentation, which in turn affects how physicians interact with the tools themselves. If a physician follows an AI recommendation and there's a bad outcome, the current framework is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0032♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Association for Diagnostics & Laboratory Medicine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Association for Diagnostics & Laboratory Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔February 11, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and⏎The Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Subject: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Sir/Madam:⏎The Association for Diagnostics & Laboratory Medicine (ADLM) welcomes the opportunity to⏎provide input to the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) regarding its December⏎23, 2025 request for information on how to adopt and accelerate the use of artificial intelligence⏎(AI) in clinical care. As the leading association in diagnostic testing and technology, we offer the⏎following suggestions:⏎Harmonize Clinical Laboratory Test Results to Enable Interoperability⏎Interoperability refers to the capacity of disparate laboratory and clinical data to be exchanged,⏎understood, and utilized accurately across different care settings. One of the leading barriers to⏎the use of AI within these healthcare settings is the lack of harmonization among laboratory tests.⏎AI systems that use laboratory data often assume that test results are comparable across different⏎testing sites and methods. In practice, this is often not the case. Different testing platforms can⏎produce different numeric results for the same analyte. While each value may be accurate when⏎using a specific measurement system, numeric differences between measurement systems can:⏎• Degrade model performance when data are pooled across institutions;⏎• Limit an algorithm’s generalizability to new sites or populations;⏎• Introduce systematic errors into risk scores and clinical decision support systems that⏎depend on numeric thresholds; and⏎• Complicate efforts to evaluate AI performance across diverse health systems.⏎The lack of harmonized clinical laboratory test results can lead to misdiagnosis, improper⏎treatment decisions, and increase healthcare costs. While Congress has provided the Centers for⏎Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) with $2 million annually since fiscal year (FY) 2018 to⏎work on this issue, this amount is insufficient. ADLM urges HHS to provide additional funding⏎in the FY27 budget to harmonize laboratory test results.⏎⏎HHS⏎February 11, 2026⏎Page Two⏎Representative Data to Develop Accurate AI Algorithms for Guiding Patient Care⏎One of the biggest challenges facing AI is the need to ensure the data used to make healthcare⏎decisions is representative of the population. In laboratory medicine, two categories of bias-⏎related risk are especially important:⏎• Demographic underrepresentation and skewed clinical data - historical datasets may⏎underrepresent certain racial and ethnic groups, age ranges, or socioeconomic strata.⏎Studies of health AI tools have demonstrated that such imbalances can cause models to⏎systematically underestimate risk or misclassify disease in certain populations.⏎• Technical variability and context mismatch - inconsistent calibration, different pre-⏎analytical protocols, or outdated diagnostic criteria can change how a given laboratory⏎value reflects underlying physiology. If an AI model is based on data from one method⏎and environment and then applied in a different setting without adjustment, it may⏎misinterpret results or fail to identify important variations. These differences can cause⏎algorithms to misinterpret patient health indicators when used across different clinical⏎practices.⏎A variety of strategies should be considered to mitigate these issues, such as:⏎• Using diverse and representative patient datasets in AI model training and validation;⏎• Creating uniform guidelines for sample collection and reporting;⏎• Harmonizing results across assay systems;⏎• Including metadata on test methods and validation parameters in AI models;⏎• Validating AI models across diverse clinical settings; and⏎• Engaging clinicians, laboratorians, and patients to identify sources of variability.⏎Implementation of such measures could ensure that laboratory-based AI promotes equity,⏎fairness, and patient trust.⏎Validation and Verification of AI Systems to Ensure Accurate Laboratory Testing⏎Under the Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA) regulations, clinical⏎laboratories must validate new test systems before use and conduct ongoing quality⏎monitoring—including daily quality control (QC), proficiency testing, and trend analysis—to⏎ensure that performance remains within acceptable limits over time. AI systems that rely on or⏎influence laboratory data introduce similar risks. Notably, models that continuously update or⏎“learn” from new inputs can drift in accuracy, calibration, or bias over time.⏎Additionally, even if AI models are kept constant, changes in analytical instrument performance⏎or patient characteristics over time may degrade their ability to produce accurate diagnostic⏎results. To effectively verify and monitor AI performance, laboratory professionals require⏎sufficient access to the relevant data and model information from developers. Independent⏎⏎HHS⏎February 11, 2026⏎Page Three⏎evaluation of a system’s performance without transparency is challenging and can undermine⏎ongoing quality assurance efforts. To address these considerations, several policy needs should⏎be considered:⏎• Providing clear federal guidance on validation expectations for the AI tools used in⏎laboratory medicine, including requirements for documenting each tool’s intended use,⏎performance characteristics, and limitations.⏎• Enumerating the roles and responsibilities of laboratories, developers, and healthcare⏎organizations in implementing, monitoring, and updating AI systems.⏎• Mandating ongoing performance monitoring of clinical decision support systems and⏎AI/ML applications to ensure continued accuracy, safety, and equity across diverse⏎patient populations and laboratory settings.⏎Oversight should correspond to the level of risk and potential impact an AI tool may have on⏎patient outcomes. Diagnostic applications that rely heavily on laboratory data and lead to⏎decisions with high potential for patient harm should undergo robust validation, ensure⏎transparency, and incorporate continuous monitoring. Lower-risk tools may warrant a more⏎streamlined oversight process consistent with their reduced potential for patient harm.⏎Improved Test Utilization May Produce Better Patient Outcomes and Reduce Costs⏎Inappropriate test ordering protocols cost the US healthcare system up to $200 billion annually.1⏎The downstream effects of misutilization can be serious for patients and involve missed⏎diagnoses due to missed testing opportunities on one end of the spectrum, and follow-up visits⏎for additional testing or unnecessary treatments that result in serious complications and injuries⏎on the other end. Many providers struggle to stay on top of the best evidence regarding⏎laboratory medicine as test menus grow and become more specialized. Utilizing analytics can⏎help address this growing problem by monitoring test ordering patterns to improve use of⏎guidelines, provide real-time best practice alerts, and automatically cancel duplicative, obsolete⏎and “look-alike” test orders. Analyzing both laboratory and administrative or financial data may⏎uncover hidden utilization patterns and further reduce costs.⏎ADLM is a global scientific and medical professional organization dedicated to clinical⏎laboratory science and its application to healthcare. ADLM brings together clinical laboratory⏎professionals, physicians, research scientists, and business leaders from around the world⏎focused on clinical chemistry, molecular diagnostics, mass spectrometry, translational medicine,⏎lab management, and other areas of clinical laboratory science to advance healthcare⏎collaboration, knowledge, expertise, and innovation.⏎1 Kaiser Health News. (2017). Unnecessary medical tests, treatments cost $200 billion annually, cause harm.⏎Retrieved October 14, 2020, from https://www.healthcarefinancenews.com/news/unnecessary-medical-tests-⏎treatments-cost-200-billion-annually-cause-harm⏎⏎HHS⏎February 11, 2026⏎Page Four⏎We look forward to working with you on this important issue. If you have any questions, please⏎email Vince Stine, PhD, ADLM’s Senior Director of Government and Global Affairs, at⏎vstine@myadlm.org, or Evan Fortman, MPA, ADLM’s Manager of Government Affairs at⏎efortman@myadlm.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Paul J. Jannetto, Ph.D., DABCC, FAACC⏎President, ADLM♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0033♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Texas Oncology, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Texas Oncology♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information – HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: RFI: Accelerating the Adoption of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (FR Doc. 2025-23641)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎Community oncology practices are the backbone of cancer care delivery in the United States, caring for most⏎patients close to home across urban, suburban, rural, and underserved communities. Texas Oncology, an⏎independent, physician-led community oncology practice with more than 280 sites of service across Texas and a⏎network of more than 530 physicians, reflects this model of care. Because the community setting is where most⏎cancer care is delivered, supporting digital transformation in community settings is essential to achieving national⏎goals for access, quality, outcomes, and affordability in oncology.1⏎The digital transformation required to integrate artificial intelligence (AI) into clinical care is substantial, requiring not⏎only financial investment but also significant time and human resources. Implementing AI involves workflow⏎redesign, staff training, cybersecurity upgrades, and data integration, burdens that fall disproportionately on⏎independent practices operating with lean staffing and limited capital. Without targeted incentives and guardrails,⏎the risks of adoption may outweigh potential benefits, limiting meaningful participation by community practices.⏎The experience with electronic health record (EHR) adoption provides a clear precedent. Federal incentives enabled⏎practices to invest in technology and training while offsetting transition costs. A similar approach is needed for AI to⏎ensure community oncology practices remain viable, avoid consolidation into higher-cost hospital settings, and⏎continue to deliver high-quality cancer care close to home.⏎1. Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Adoption of AI in Clinical Care⏎Several practical barriers limit the adoption of AI in clinical care, particularly for independent and⏎community-based practices.⏎• Financial and operational barriers. Up-front, non-recoverable implementation costs, including⏎integration, training, cybersecurity, and workflow redesign, are significant. Once implemented, the⏎lack of reimbursement for AI-enabled clinical and administrative functions undermines long-term⏎sustainability. Even short-term operational disruptions can negatively affect patient care and⏎revenue, making experimentation far riskier for small practices than for large health systems.⏎1 https://www.cancer.gov/research/areas/disparities/chanita-hughes-halbert-clinical-trials-community-access⏎6204 Balcones Drive Austin, Texas 7831 512-427-9400 TexasOncology.com⏎⚫ ⚫ ⚫⏎⏎• Cybersecurity as an unfunded prerequisite. AI adoption increases baseline cybersecurity⏎requirements due to cloud access, APIs, and reliance on third-party vendors, while ransomware and⏎hacking threats continue to rise. Independent practices often lack the capital to meet these⏎expectations without dedicated funding, making security a gating barrier to AI use.⏎• Regulatory and liability uncertainty for non-device AI tools. Providers must trust the AI tools they⏎use, yet uncertainty around accountability, malpractice exposure, audit risk, and documentation⏎standards discourages adoption. The potential for bias, hallucinations, and errors underscores the⏎need for transparency, clear governance expectations, and consistent enforcement.⏎• Interoperability gaps. AI tools depend on access to clean, interoperable data across laboratories,⏎pharmacies, imaging, and claims systems. Persistent data silos reduce real-world return on⏎investment and disproportionately disadvantage smaller practices that lack data integration teams.⏎• Burnout and workforce trust concerns. Providers are wary that AI may increase oversight and⏎validation burdens rather than reduce workload. Adoption will stall unless AI is clearly positioned as⏎a tool that reduces administrative burden and preserves clinician autonomy.⏎• Equity and access risks. Community oncology practices disproportionately serve rural, elderly, and⏎underserved patients. Uneven AI adoption risks creating a two-tier cancer care system, undermining⏎national goals for equitable access to high-quality oncology care.⏎2. Regulatory, Payment Policy, and Programmatic Changes HHS Should Prioritize⏎HHS should pursue a multi-faceted strategy to support AI adoption while protecting patient safety, equity,⏎and independent practice viability.⏎Financial Incentives and Infrastructure:⏎• Grants and direct funding. Establish grant programs for community practices to support AI⏎implementation, including non-recoverable start-up costs such as integration, data governance,⏎bias testing, and cybersecurity, paired with transparent reporting on safety, performance, and⏎outcomes.⏎• Tax incentives. Provide refundable tax credits, deductions, or accelerated depreciation for⏎qualifying AI technologies and digital infrastructure investments, including interoperability and⏎cybersecurity upgrades.⏎• Pilot and demonstration programs. Support early adopters through funded pilots that allow AI⏎testing in controlled clinical environments with technical assistance and evaluation support.⏎• Shared digital infrastructure. Enable access to common digital infrastructures such as secure⏎cloud environments and standardized data management tools, to reduce duplicative costs and⏎implement barriers for small practices.⏎Interoperability, Training and Partnerships:⏎Page | 2⏎6204 Balcones Drive⏎⚫⏎Austin, Texas 7831⏎⚫⏎512-427-9400⏎⚫⏎TexasOncology.com⏎⏎• Interoperability and data liquidity. Strengthen interoperability standards and government-led data⏎sharing initiatives, including alignment with 45 CFR Part 171, to ensure AI tools can function⏎effectively across care settings.⏎• Workforce training and change management. Develop and subsidize role-based training for⏎clinicians, nurses, pharmacists, navigators, and administrative staff, paired with change-⏎management support to reduce burnout and adoption friction.⏎• Public-private partnerships. Foster collaborations between government and technology⏎developers to create AI solutions tailored to community practices. These partnerships should⏎include shared implementation playbooks, model contracts, and standardized governance⏎frameworks for privacy, accountability, and auditability.⏎Regulatory Clarity and Reimbursement Alignment:⏎• Clear governance for non-device AI. Establish consistent guidance for accountability,⏎transparency, and HIPAA-aligned privacy protections under 45 CFR Part 164 to reduce uncertainty⏎and compliance risk.⏎• Performance-based reimbursement pathways. Create reimbursement approaches that reward⏎effective AI use through improved outcomes, efficiency, and reduced administrative burden,⏎including per-member-per-month payments or shared savings models under SSA §§ 1899 and⏎1115A.⏎• Ongoing valuation of AI tools. Ensure CMS reimbursement methodologies under the Physician Fee⏎Schedule appropriately reflect the scalable and evolving nature of AI tools.2 Continued recognition⏎and coverage of AMA CPT codes related to augmented intelligence are essential to sustaining⏎adoption.3⏎3. Research and Development Priorities⏎HHS investment in applied research and development should focus on real-world clinical implementation,⏎particularly in community-based settings. Priority areas include evaluating AI performance across diverse⏎patient populations, developing tools that integrate seamlessly into existing workflows, and generating⏎evidence on how AI can reduce administrative burden while improving quality and equity of care. Public-⏎private research partnerships can accelerate learning while lowering risk for smaller practices.⏎4. Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎Patients and caregivers generally support technologies that improve access, reduce delays, and enhance⏎care coordination, while expecting transparency, strong privacy protection, and continued human oversight.⏎Concerns are particularly acute around payer use of AI in utilization management, where providers and⏎patients bear clinical and financial risk while payers capture efficiency gains. Greater transparency,⏎reporting, and oversight of AI-driven prior authorization and coverage decisions are necessary to maintain⏎trust and prevent inappropriate denials.⏎2 https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/epdf/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00672⏎3 https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/cpt/288-new-cpt-codes-cover-digital-health-ai-and-more⏎Page | 3⏎6204 Balcones Drive⏎⚫⏎Austin, Texas 7831⏎⚫⏎512-427-9400⏎⚫⏎TexasOncology.com⏎⏎Given the rise in healthcare data breaches and ransomware attacks, patients must be confident that their⏎data are used in a HIPAA-compliant manner, that AI involvement in care is disclosed, and that safeguards⏎exist in the event of a breach.⏎Conclusion⏎AI has the potential to improve cancer care quality efficiency, but only if adoption is supported by deliberate policy⏎design. Structured incentives, regulatory clarity, and infrastructure investment are essential to ensure community⏎oncology practices can participate fully in the digital transformation of healthcare. Without targeted support,⏎underinvestment in AI will accelerate consolidation, reduce patient choice, and increase overall healthcare costs.⏎A federal approach modeled on the successful EHR incentive programs can help ensure AI strengthens, rather than⏎destabilizes, the community-based oncology care system.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide input on this Request for Information. We appreciate HHS’s leadership in⏎advancing thoughtful, practical approaches to accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical⏎care, particularly in community-based settings.⏎If you have questions or would like additional information, please contact: Debra.Patt@usoncology.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Debra Patt, MD, PhD, MBA⏎Executive Vice President Policy and Strategy⏎Texas Oncology⏎Page | 4⏎6204 Balcones Drive⏎⚫⏎Austin, Texas 7831⏎⚫⏎512-427-9400⏎⚫⏎TexasOncology.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0034♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Altera Digital Health, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Altera Digital Health, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Altera Digital Health, Inc., Response to⏎HHS RFI: AI & Clinical Care (February 2026)⏎HHS seeks feedback on ways in which these approaches can be most effectively applied to support the⏎rapid adoption and use of AI in clinical care, to foster public trust and confidence in modern technology⏎solutions, to reduce uncertainty that impedes AI innovation, and to align federal incentives so that AI is⏎deployed in ways that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower health care costs, and improve health⏎outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities.⏎General – Research & Development⏎Topic: Input on ways in which HHS may invest in research and development (including public-private⏎partnerships and cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs)) to integrate AI in care⏎delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the health and wellbeing of all⏎Americans.⏎Altera Response: "Deflationary" efficiency requires a bi-directional flow – providing funding to incite the⏎acceleration of FHIR write capabilities would result in meaningful acceleration of first- and third-party⏎agentic AI capabilities with reliable and secure vectors for integration.⏎SECTION II: SPECIFIC QUESTIONS⏎Question 1⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎Altera Response: We strongly support the ASTP/ONC's HTI-5 proposal to remove the "Source Attribute"⏎and "Intervention Risk Management" requirements from 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11). These requirements⏎disproportionately burdened "Operational AI" (billing, scheduling) which poses minimal safety risk.⏎Barriers to AI innovation and adoption include regulatory uncertainty, data quality, insufficient payment⏎incentives, unclear ROI, and user readiness.⏎The current regulatory environment specific to AI is confusing to both technology providers and health IT⏎users. There are questions regarding how existing federal frameworks apply to clinical AI that is not⏎classified as a device versus FDA-regulated Software-as-a-Medical Device (SaMD), as well as ambiguity⏎stemming from the incongruity between the White House Executive Order discouraging state passage of AI-⏎related laws and the ongoing legislative activity in that area. HHS’ stated objective of establishing a⏎predictable, proportionate regulatory approach that protects patients while supporting an innovation-⏎friendly environment would be best supported by a consistent, predictable, risk-based Federal framework⏎⏎(passed by Congress). Piecemeal publications, such as the recent FDA enforcement discretion on Clinical⏎Decision Support issued on January 6, 2026, are helpful but insufficient.⏎Not all participants in the healthcare ecosystem comply with the standards required for Certified Health IT.⏎This creates a barrier to data exchange that is fundamental to the use of AI. Achieving the outcomes⏎desired by the HHS RFI relies on the documentation and use of trustworthy, interoperable data in the⏎creation of AI tools, as well as transparent evaluation of those tools on an ongoing basis. We also note that⏎in the absence of federal transparency mandates or a clear regulatory framework specific to AI use in⏎health care, dominant EHR incumbents are stepping in as "Shadow Regulators," using the lack of federal⏎vetting or oversight as a pretext to block third-party AI apps under the guise of "patient safety." A strong,⏎Federal framework would protect against this type of market abuse.⏎AI adoption by healthcare providers can take many forms, and those options will expand significantly in the⏎coming years. Some of those AI tools will be easily selected thanks to the ROI that can be demonstrated by⏎their use – either financial savings or clinical benefit – but there are numerous other use cases that will not⏎primarily benefit the users of the software or even their larger organization. Use cases that are focused on⏎research, as an example, which will rely on collection of health data through interoperability mechanisms⏎could be slowed if payment policies don’t incite corresponding behaviors by the provider organizations.⏎Legacy fee-for-service payment models rarely provide sufficient financial payment to cover investment in⏎innovative technologies, but reimbursement mechanisms that reward validated outcomes and burden⏎reduction would do a lot to stimulate provider demand.⏎It is also clear from conversations with our clients (hospitals of all sizes and larger ambulatory physician⏎practices) that clinicians are very interested in AI on a theoretical basis, but they are focused on identifying⏎AI that is usable (fits well within their EHR workflow), makes recommendations but not decisions, and⏎provide trustworthy, explainable information that can be investigated for the context behind the⏎intervention. Physicians, other clinicians, and the administrative staff all need increased trust in the safety⏎of the tools, as well as assurance that those developing the software will remain committed to continuous⏎improvement and retraining, given the rapid evolution of the technologies that will continue for the⏎foreseeable future.⏎A thoughtful, more comprehensive legal and regulatory framework addressing all the concerns about AI⏎adoption would go a long way to spur both development and use of the new technologies.⏎Question 2⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Altera Response:⏎HHS' payment policies and programs are the single largest determinant of how health care is delivered in⏎the United States, often times with unintended consequences. It is critical to ensure that the potential⏎⏎promises of AI innovations are not diminished through inertia in payment model reform by HHS and instead⏎that such payment systems are modernized to meet the needs of a changing healthcare system.⏎Given that provider organizations will be the ones investing in AI tools used within both their clinical and⏎administrative systems, payment modernization must be completed to reflect those expenditures that will⏎benefit patients and the health system as a whole. CMS should consider targeted add-on payments and⏎pathways specific to advanced alternative payment models (APMs) that reward the deployment of AI tools⏎that improve clinical outcomes and help reduce unnecessary expenditures. Without such incentive⏎structures being rolled out by CMS, financially constrained providers will have a very hard time making the⏎step forward to AI adoption.⏎We also suggest, more specifically, that CMS recognize "AI-Assisted Documentation" as a quality⏎improvement activity that reduces provider burnout. Reward for the use of patient-facing AI tools that⏎empower patients and even reduce the volume of visits or burden accessing information should also be⏎part of new advanced alternative payment models rolled out by CMS. Further, we believe that there is an⏎opportunity to roll out not just payments for new diagnostic devices or AI adoption but also so-called⏎"Deflationary AI" – tools that reduce administrative overhead (e.g., AI Scribes, Autonomous Coding). HHS⏎should evaluate the best way to incite both.⏎We also recommend that Federal investments be made in benchmarking datasets, reference⏎implementations, and safety testbeds to improve model transparency, external validation, and⏎reproducibility. The importance of research, ongoing evaluation, and public–private partnerships to AI⏎innovation and market maturity are appropriately cited in HHS’ RFI. We also suggest that investments in⏎shared testing sandboxes (coupled with common reporting templates) would add real value for the⏎industry in its work developing AI tools, as well as in building the necessary trust amongst organizations⏎that will be evaluating and using the new AI options.⏎We also note there are two information blocking-related factors that should be reviewed by ASTP/ONC and⏎OIG to assess whether there is behavior taking place specific to AI model development and⏎implementation that needs to be addressed within the 21st Century Cures context.⏎1. Use of the "Security Exception" (45 CFR 171.203): The Security Exception is being named by⏎some EHR vendors in their decision to deny connectivity of AI apps. We suggest that ASTP/ONC⏎clarify that "Objective Evidence" of a specific technical threat must be demonstrated before such a⏎decision can be made in the context of the Security Exception. As it stands, "reasonable belief" is⏎too low a bar that permits gatekeeping by vendors who are protecting their turf or trying to limit⏎competition with their own AI offerings.⏎2. Clarify "Data Access for Training": ASTP/ONC, in the context of information blocking should⏎explicitly state that "access, exchange, and use" of EHI includes the right to bulk export data for the⏎purpose of training AI models for limited use (i.e., clinical usage by the health system only).⏎⏎Question 3⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Altera Response: Clarity around liability between AI deployers (EHRs), companies developing AI models⏎that are integrated with EHRs, companies developing independent AI models, and providers is an emergent⏎issue that is a steady source of uncertainty for all stakeholders.⏎A central issue requiring clarity is where liability rests when AI-generated recommendations inform care⏎decisions. Because AI tools accessible within or in conjunction with an EHR serve in advisory or assistive⏎roles and do not constitute regulated medical devices, the boundaries of responsibility can be ambiguous.⏎It is really critical that all legal and procedural structures ensure that assignments of accountability align⏎with real-world roles and capabilities, including preservation of clinicians’ independent judgment.⏎Another challenge is presented by the contracting and indemnification frameworks needed for adaptive or⏎continuously learning AI systems. Traditional contracting models do not adequately address issues such as⏎performance warranties, update obligations, or the allocation of risks associated with model drift and re-⏎training. In particular, small provider organizations who do not have the legal resources to negotiate⏎unfamiliar and complex AI-related contract terms will need guidance from HHS or other Agencies.⏎Non-device AI systems also present privacy and security complexities that differ from those of traditional⏎clinical software. Ambiguity persists around what types of data use are permissible under HIPAA for model⏎training, evaluation, and post-deployment monitoring. HHS could greatly reduce uncertainty by clarifying⏎permitted pathways for using de-identified or limited datasets, articulating requirements for strong de-⏎identification methods, and encouraging privacy-preserving models such as federated learning or⏎evaluation.⏎These complexities underscore the importance of coordinated, consistent federal leadership in the health⏎AI space. HHS should take rapid steps to reduce uncertainty and build the foundation for safe, responsible⏎and trustworthy use of non-device AI tools across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎Question 4⏎For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎Altera Response: Prior to deployment to end users, developers and implementers of non-medical device⏎AI tools should conduct structured intended-use analysis, external validation across both representative⏎provider and patient populations, and human-factors testing in live or simulated EHR workflows. Post-⏎⏎deployment, ongoing monitoring for calibration, drift, fairness impacts and alert fatigue is⏎essential. Additionally, because of the rapid evolution of model data and model logic, guidance or⏎requirements specific to ongoing training would be helpful.⏎Question 5⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven⏎testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Altera Response: Private-sector EHR vendors have worked – given the lack of a reliable AI regulatory⏎framework – to establish internal governance, testing, and safety protocols. Some healthcare clients also⏎request safeguards and data testing before proceeding with adopting AI tools and functionality, but⏎questions they ask and what they expect in terms of assurances currently vary significantly (if they exist at⏎all).⏎We are eager to participate in conversations with HHS and other industry stakeholders about ideas⏎including voluntary accreditation, where ASTP/ONC’s EHR certification program can appropriately address⏎areas requiring consistency or a standards-based approach, and testing mechanisms for clinical AI. The⏎nascent conversations already underway would likely be more successful if HHS recognized and aligned⏎with industry-led initiatives to evaluate robustness, transparency, and interoperability.⏎Question 6⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations⏎and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Response: No response⏎Question 7⏎Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the most⏎influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Altera Response: The industry would greatly benefit from Federal guidance or regulation addressing⏎documentation expectations, lifecycle monitoring, and governance models. The entire framework should⏎be based on a risk-based approach reflecting the differences between AI that assists in clinical decision⏎making vs. tools that aim to take over administrative processes and reduce those burdens for healthcare⏎organizations.⏎⏎Question 8⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate⏎the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎Altera Response: Successful maximization of AI adoption in a way that delivers on its great potential is⏎dependent on the quality and efficient exchange of interoperable data across the health IT ecosystem. AI⏎tools cannot function without the input of structured, standards-based data to generate reliable and⏎equitable analysis and/or recommendations. Ensuring transparency of data provenance and⏎standardization of the data exchanged, alongside supporting model metadata capture directly within EHR⏎systems, would improve the reliability of external validation efforts. Ensuring the establishment of national⏎benchmarking datasets, as well as a standardized evaluation framework against which models can be⏎measured with some regular cadence, would go a long with in helping developers, researchers, and⏎providers assess model performance across diverse populations and environments and both build and⏎retain trust in the new systems.⏎Question 9⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Altera Response: Patients and caregivers alike, while optimistic about the significant benefits that can⏎come for both providers and patients from the use of AI, also consistently raise concerns about⏎transparency regarding the logic being used by AI models, patient privacy, possible bias in the models, the⏎option to opt out of AI use in their care, and accountability when errors occur. All of these are fundamental⏎issues that must be addressed not only now but on an ongoing basis to maintain public trust.⏎Question 10⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎Altera Response: HHS should prioritize research into any topic that increases awareness about the safety⏎and accuracy of adoption of health IT: issues of bias in model data, the impacts of human-centered⏎usability factors, repeated real-world safety evaluations, and ongoing performance monitoring to support⏎necessary adjustments. We also support investment in pragmatic trial designs and implementation⏎science approaches that measure AI’s true impact on outcomes, clinician workload, equitable application⏎of the technology, and related total costs of care that may be changed by AI use. Because cost-benefit⏎analyses and burden-reduction measures are still emerging fields in AI evaluation, federally supported⏎research in this area would significantly help to inform sustainable adoption at scale.⏎⏎We also suggest that HHS fund research and studies into other returns from AI systems: for example, what⏎are the benefits seen in patient care plan adherence attributed to the use of ambient listening AI, the⏎increased time that providers are able to spend face-to-face with patients vs. looking at their laptop, or the⏎increased speed of claims denial resolution coming from AI tools that reduce administrative burden for⏎providers? This quantification of value has proven very difficult in the industry, but clarity around this value⏎would serve to accelerate adoption of AI as yet additional demonstration of it return on investment (both⏎financial and clinical).⏎Question 10.a⏎Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in clinical care?⏎Response: No response⏎Question 10.b⏎How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical⏎care?⏎Response: No response♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0035♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Hospital for Special Surgery , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Hospital for Special Surgery♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically at https://www.regulations.gov.⏎RE: Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎Organization Overview⏎Hospital for Special Surgery (HSS) is the world’s leading academic medical center focused on⏎musculoskeletal health and the oldest orthopedic hospital in the United States. HSS has been ranked⏎number one for orthopedics for 16 consecutive years by U.S. News & World Report and has been among⏎the top-ranked hospitals for both orthopedics and rheumatology for 34 consecutive years. HSS combines⏎clinical care, research, education, and digital innovation to improve patient outcomes at scale.⏎HSS is focused on innovating in healthcare AI, advanced analytics, and digital transformation to support⏎clinical excellence, operational efficiency, and patient engagement. Our work integrates secure cloud⏎infrastructure, enterprise data platforms, predictive analytics, and AI-enabled workflows into real-world⏎clinical environments.⏎Current examples of focus include:⏎• Machine learning models⏎• Predictive analytics for outcomes, complications, and utilization⏎• Natural language processing to extract structured insights from clinical documentation⏎• AI-assisted operational workflows to reduce administrative burden⏎• Data-driven quality improvement and performance monitoring⏎• Secure cloud architecture aligned with HIPAA and healthcare cybersecurity standards⏎HSS approaches AI as applied innovation. Solutions are designed for real clinical workflows, validated⏎with physicians and care teams, and governed through structured compliance, privacy, and model⏎oversight processes. To support this, HSS has established an AI Governance Subcommittee within our⏎Data Governance structure. This group reviews proposed AI solutions, sets standards, and monitors⏎ongoing performance with a focus on safety, transparency, and fairness. Embedding AI oversight in our⏎data governance framework ensures systematic, accountable decision-making with input from clinical,⏎operational, and ethical stakeholders - providing the guardrails necessary for responsible AI deployment⏎and maintaining trust among patients, staff, and our community.⏎⏎Integrated Response to RFI⏎HSS appreciates the opportunity to provide input regarding the development, evaluation, and adoption of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. Based on operational experience in healthcare delivery and⏎digital innovation, we offer the following consolidated observations and recommendations.⏎1. Data Readiness and Interoperability: Data fragmentation and inconsistency remain primary⏎barriers to innovation. Healthcare organizations face challenges identifying necessary data⏎elements, understanding where information resides across systems, distinguishing structured⏎from unstructured data, and accessing complete datasets for training and validation. Greater⏎alignment in data standards, continued focus on interoperability (even within the four walls of a⏎hospital system), and access to shared benchmarking datasets would significantly accelerate⏎responsible AI development and scalability.⏎2. Liability and Accountability Clarity: Uncertainty regarding responsibility when AI-informed⏎insights influence clinical decisions slows adoption. Clearer guidance on accountability among⏎health systems, clinicians, start-ups, and institutions would reduce risk aversion. This in line with⏎AMA’s view that the first step in implementing health AI is to decide and document⏎accountability/oversight and use governance to reduce risk and confusion. Limited safe harbor or⏎risk-sharing models could support responsible pilot programs.⏎3. Evidence-Based Validation and Oversight: AI tools should be evaluated using evidence-based⏎methods, including performance testing, bias assessment, and workflow evaluation. Post-⏎deployment monitoring should focus on real-world outcomes and model drift. Streamlined, risk-⏎based oversight pathways for non-device AI tools would better align regulation with innovation.⏎4. Human-Centered AI: Trust is essential. AI must integrate into clinical workflows, reduce burden,⏎and demonstrate measurable improvements in outcomes and efficiency. Adoption must be first⏎led by clinical leadership, in alignment with IT governance, and reimbursement constructs.⏎5. Patient Considerations: Patients support AI that improves accuracy, timeliness, and⏎personalization of care but express concerns about transparency, privacy, and reduced human⏎interaction. Strong safeguards and clear communication are critical for health systems when⏎implementing AI.⏎6. Incentives and Federal Support: Payment models should reward validated AI that improves⏎quality and reduces cost. Federal investment in data standards, bias mitigation research, real-⏎world evidence generation, and evaluation frameworks would meaningfully accelerate⏎responsible adoption. This should be paired with consistent federal regulation and legislation to⏎provide clear, uniform guardrails for development, evaluation, and deployment.⏎Interest and Next Steps⏎AI holds significant potential to improve clinical quality, operational efficiency, and patient outcomes.⏎Addressing data fragmentation, clarifying liability structures, modernizing evaluation pathways, aligning⏎⏎reimbursement incentives, and strengthening interoperability will be essential to responsible and scalable⏎adoption. We appreciate the opportunity to provide input and welcome further engagement to support the⏎advancement of safe, effective, and innovative AI use in clinical care. Please contact Stephen X. Hunter,⏎Chief Digital Strategy Officer (HunterS@HSS.edu; 646-797-8748) to coordinate follow-up discussion.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0036♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔Mitchell♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist of eleven years retired in 2022, and the practice that took over his patients brought in a new doctor who, in our first appointment, handed me a printed medication recommendation and told me the system had flagged a protocol adjustment. When I asked what system, exactly, and who had reviewed its reasoning, the conversation kind of stalled out. The nurse later told me it was a "decision support tool" integrated into their EHR. That was the whole explanation.⏎⏎I've spent forty years in construction. I've read load-bearing calculations, signed off on structural inspections, worked jobs where if you skip the check behind the check, somebody dies and there is a name on the permit that answers for it. That's not a metaphor I'm using loosely. There is always supposed to be a named person who is on the hook. That's how you keep people from cutting corners.⏎⏎What I want HHS to address is the question of who is actually liable when an AI-assisted clinical decision causes harm. Because right now I don't think anybody has a straight answer to that. The clinician can say they relied on the tool in good faith. The hospital can say the vendor didn't disclose the tool's limitations. The vendor can say the clinician didn't use it correctly, or the training data was outside their control. And the patient is just sitting there with the harm and no clear path to accountability. Existing malpractice law was built around a physician making a judgment call, not a physician deferring to a probabilistic output from software trained on datasets nobody in that exam room has ever seen.⏎⏎Accelerating adoption before sorting that out seems backwards to me. You're pouring the concrete before the rebar inspection. Once the infrastructure is built around these tools and the billing codes are set up and the workflows are redesigned, you're not going to be able to go back and fix the accountability structure without tearing out a lot of expensive work. The people who get hurt in the meantime are not the hospital administrators who made the decision to move fast.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. I genuinely don't know enough about the diagnostic side to have a strong opinion on whether these tools are accurate or not. What I know is that accuracy and accountability are separate questions. A system can give you the right answer ninety-four percent of the time and still be a problem if nobody is responsible for the six percent.⏎⏎What I'd want to see before HHS pushes harder on adoption is some kind of clear requirement that for any AI tool used in a clinical decision, there is a specific named person or entity that carries legal responsibility for the output. Not "the care team." Not "the institution." A name. The same way there's a name on a structural permit.⏎⏎I also want to know whether patients are even being told when these tools are involved in their care. I wasn't. I had to ask, and even then the answer was vague. If I hadn't spent a career learning to ask about the systems behind the systems, I probably would have just taken the printout home and filled the prescription without knowing there was a machine involved at all. I'm not sure my neighbor would have asked. He's 74 and just trusts whatever the doctor hands him, which is not a criticism of him, that's just how a lot of people approach a doctor's office.⏎⏎The notification question and the liability question feel like they should be prerequisites, not afterthoughts, and I don't see how you accelerate adoption responsibly without♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0037♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Essig, Todd, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Todd♔Essig♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 16, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail⏎Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: PsiAN Comment to HHS Health Sector AI RFI RIN 0955-AA13⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology:⏎Psychotherapy Action Network (PsiAN) is grateful to have the opportunity to provide a response⏎to the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for Information regarding⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care.⏎PsiAN is a nonprofit organization that advocates for access to psychotherapies that create⏎meaningful change, with a membership of over 6,500 individuals and 90 partner organizations⏎representing mental health clinicians and researchers, across all disciplines, as well as⏎administrators and users of therapy. Psychotherapy Action Network stands for ethical, effective⏎patient care and clinical standards, amplifies therapists’ voices, and advocates for direct reforms⏎that truly serve patients and clinicians.⏎HHS’ Request for Information solicits comments related to regulation, reimbursement, and⏎research and development in addition to supplemental specific questions. While the comments⏎offered by PsiAN are applicable to all forms of AI in healthcare, our remarks will often focus on⏎the behavioral health landscape and to issues related to provision of psychotherapy.⏎Regarding regulation: Currently, there is cause for great concern related to the privacy, efficacy,⏎and safety of AI in healthcare because there is no current federal regulation of AI in⏎healthcare. Regulation is essential, given the federal government’s central obligation to protect⏎the public. This is not something that can be left to the companies producing and selling these⏎products or to individual states, which could result in a patchwork of incompatible regulations.⏎Our government has already decided that regulation and oversight are essential in the⏎healthcare space to ensure safety and efficacy. For example, regulation and appropriate⏎⏎enforcement are important aspects of new products and treatments through the FDA. We⏎regulate healthcare clinicians through licensing requirements. We should not accept anything⏎less with AI, and perhaps should set the bar higher for an unproven technology.⏎AI chatbots, either specifically for mental health or other generalized models, are not HIPAA⏎compliant, therefore user/patient privacy is not protected by federal law. Privacy is legally⏎required by HIPAA in all healthcare industries and settings. Privacy and confidentiality are⏎cornerstones of effective psychotherapy, and the absence of these protections in the use of⏎AI Chatbots suggests a significant area of concern that demands correction.⏎Regarding research and development: Due to the novelty of this technology, there is not yet⏎enough data available to support efficacy and/or safety. We know that Large Language Models⏎(LLMs) are especially prone to “hallucinations,” with documented cases involving irrevocable⏎harm of the worst kind. For example, AI LLMs have encouraged vulnerable people struggling⏎with suicide to take suicidal action or provided them with information about the heights of bridges⏎or buildings from which to jump. This is unacceptably risky Chatbot behavior that literally⏎threatens lives.⏎Again, with reference to the behavioral health domain, an evidence base showing that these⏎products are effective and safe is lacking. The metrics that corporations are typically tracking,⏎such as number of downloads, user engagement, customer satisfaction, are not substitutes for⏎demonstrating effectiveness. AI products must be thoroughly researched and tested, as is any⏎new psychotherapy protocol or pharmaceutical, to measure effectiveness, dropout rates,⏎remission rates, etc.⏎Importantly, evaluation, assessment, safety and outcome research should be provided by a⏎separate, independent group of researchers to minimize the conflict of interest inherent if⏎corporations that develop these products also supply the evaluation and “science.”⏎Regarding AI instruments approximating psychotherapy, it’s desirable that the products advance⏎recovery, defined by SAMHSA as a process over time leading to a self-directed life. It is⏎insufficient to focus only on symptom management or reduction.⏎HHS advocacy to accelerate use of AI must follow and be linked to development of an evidence⏎base demonstrating safety and efficacy.⏎While PsiAN recognizes legitimate concerns related to access to mental health care, and the⏎hypothesis that AI tools and apps could help close that gap, we wish to highlight the risks⏎associated with the use of AI to provide clinical care in the behavioral healthcare industry,⏎especially without rigorous clinical trials, regulation, and robust oversight. At a minimum, HHS⏎should not accelerate the adoption of AI until the FDA completes its evaluations of AI as⏎medical devices. Given the FDA’s findings that well over 10,000 such applications are already⏎widely available to consumers without appropriate premarket performance evaluations, risk⏎management endeavors, or post-market evaluations, PsiAN recommends that HHS focus on⏎developing research demonstrating safety and efficacy before accelerating⏎implementation.⏎⏎Regarding supplemental specific questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for healthcare and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The privacy of data and corporate responsibility for poor outcomes are paramount. At⏎this point, the corporations developing AI tools are not able to ensure patient privacy,⏎and the legal and regulatory infrastructure holding them accountable for outcomes or⏎harms due to using their products is not well-established.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Uniform Federal Standards for privacy and safety are essential to adoption.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎HHS should contribute to creating uniform federal standards for privacy and safety⏎AFTER the FDA determines whether or not AI instruments are, in fact, medical devices,⏎and in a way consistent with that determination.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎We need more than bench testing and assessment of user experiences and outcomes.⏎We need robust outcome data about whether AI is making a difference in health-related⏎quality of life as opposed to mere symptom improvement.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎Accreditation and credentialing present serious challenges and must look beyond the⏎narrow financial interests of insurance entities or AI corporations.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎In behavioral health, decades of research have confirmed that the therapist-patient⏎⏎relationship is the main predictor of outcome in psychotherapy. Since AI is not, in fact, a⏎human relationship, it is seriously limited as a potential clinical treatment tool. Much⏎more study and assessment over longer periods of time are necessary.⏎At this point, it is unclear how AI can best be used, whether as a support for⏎psychotherapists, a replacement for psychotherapists, or a tool more tightly focused on⏎insurance processing and administrative issues.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎While several states are adopting laws regulating AI mental health chatbots, these have⏎become a patchwork of requirements that differ from state to state and lack uniformity.⏎Hence, adoption of clearcut, science-based federal regulations is indicated to define how⏎all AI chatbots can ensure privacy, safety, and efficacy throughout the nation.⏎Without federal oversight, there are no meaningful incentives for the corporations⏎developing AI tools to self-regulate. HHS should work with clinicians and psychotherapy⏎researchers to define appropriate metrics to ensure product efficacy and safety. Clinical⏎metrics differ from those used by corporations developing and selling AI products, which⏎focus on measuring product profitability rather than safety and efficacy. Leaving⏎regulation and oversight in the hands of the corporations will not promote consumer⏎safety, privacy, or effective care.⏎In summary, AI Medical devices are not ready for accelerated adoption and deployment without⏎appropriate research or enforceable regulation. Even though LLMs are currently being used by⏎millions of people, this adoption by the public is far ahead of the essential science⏎demonstrating privacy, safety, and efficacy. Hence, regulation is essential to reduce the risk of⏎harm to consumers, ensure that the public has access to safe and effective clinical care, hold⏎corporations accountable for the products they develop and sell, and support uniform national⏎standards for AI Chatbots.⏎Should it prove helpful, we would be happy to testify in any hearings on this matter.⏎Sincerely,⏎Linda Michaels, PsyD MBA⏎Chair, Psychotherapy Action Network⏎www.psian.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0038♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Pharmacy Health Information Technology Collaborative , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Pharmacy Health Information Technology Collaborative♔♔♔♔♔♔Via Electronic Submission to: https://www.regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎ASTP/ONC⏎Attn.: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330C Street, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RIN 0955-AA13: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎On behalf of its membership, the Pharmacy Health Information Technology⏎Collaborative (PHIT) is pleased to submit comments for the Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎PHIT has been involved with the federal agencies, including the Department of Health⏎and Human Services (HHS) Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National⏎Coordinator (ASTP/ONC) and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS), in⏎developing the national health information technology (HIT) framework for implementing⏎secure access of electronic health information to improve health outcomes since 2010.⏎Pharmacists provide essential, patient-centered care services to their patients,⏎including Medicare and Medicaid beneficiaries. Pharmacists use health IT, provider directories,⏎telehealth, e-prescribing (eRx), electronic medical record (EMR)/electronic health record (EHR)⏎systems, and certified EHR technology (CEHRT) to help manage patients’ health needs. PHIT⏎supports the use of these systems, which are important to pharmacists in working with other⏎health care providers to provide longitudinal person-centered care planning, needed⏎medications, and transmit patient information related to overall patient care, transitions of⏎care, immunization, medication lists, medication allergies, allergy reactions, patient problem⏎lists, smoking status, and social determinants of health (SDOH). Pharmacists also use health IT⏎for reporting to public health agencies (e.g., immunization reporting), clinical decision support⏎services/knowledge artifacts, drug formulary checking, comprehensive medication⏎management (CMM), and are starting to use artificial intelligence (AI) to enhance efficiency,⏎safety, and person-centered clinical care.⏎Pharmacy Health Information Technology Collaborative⏎| c/o ASCP, 1240 N Pitt St, Suite 300, Alexandria, VA 22314| www.pharmacyHIT.org|703-599-5051|⏎⏎Our comments pertain primarily to question #1, regarding barriers to AI’s use in clinical⏎care, the issues and challenges, and presenting possible solutions.⏎Comments⏎PHIT supports the development and use of artificial intelligence (AI) that maintains⏎patient safety, privacy, equity, and public trust for use in pharmacy clinical care settings.⏎Additionally, PHIT supports a risk-proportionate framework that tailors appropriate regulatory⏎oversight with compliance requirements, while recognizing that regulations may not address all⏎problems that arise or potential ones. As AI for clinical care evolves, other solutions to⏎potential downstream issues may present themselves and not necessarily require regulatory⏎intervention.⏎Because AI and digital innovations provide an incredible opportunity for clinical care,⏎PHIT established a Clinical Workflow, Artificial Intelligence, and Digital Innovation (CAD)⏎Workgroup to improve quality patient care across the continuum by leveraging digital⏎innovation and AI at the intersection of clinical workflows and electronic standards (e.g.,⏎algorithmic clinical decision support (CDS), machine learning, augmented AI-human⏎intelligence, etc.). The workgroup assists PHIT in developing guidance for pharmacists and key⏎stakeholders about digital health, clinical aspects of e-prescribing, and workflow related to the⏎pharmacists’ patient care process (e.g., “Artificial Intelligence Considerations for Pharmacists”).⏎Some AI models are in use currently in various pharmacy settings for improving⏎medication safety and dispensing (e.g., identifying medication orders and prescriptions with⏎common errors, flagging interactions, and enabling the pharmacist to intervene before the⏎medication is dispensed), treatment protocols, enhancing clinical decision through clinical⏎decision support systems (CDSS), patient adherence, and operational efficiency. One example⏎is the National Council for Prescription Drug Program’s (NCPDP) Real-Time Prescription Benefit⏎(RTPB) standard that allows real-time transactions of patient-specific drug coverage, cost, and⏎formulary alternatives at the point of prescribing. This minimizes delays, improves medication⏎adherence, and provides accurate out-of-pocket estimates. It is a national AI ready⏎infrastructure that could be leveraged to help clinical care and advance HHS's AI strategy (see⏎also “Real Time Benefits Check: Improving the patient experience and reducing costs.)⏎Additionally, electronic prior authorization (ePA) in pharmacy is also rapidly evolving⏎from a simple digital form-submission tool into an AI-powered, automated process. These⏎systems are designed to eliminate the administrative burden of traditional fax/phone, reducing⏎prior authorization approval times from days to minutes by using AI to pre-populate forms,⏎analyze clinical data, and predict, rather than just submit, approvals (see also “Enhancing Prior⏎Authorization with Automation and AI-Powered Insights”).⏎Simultaneously, models with applications in clinical pharmacy practice for use in the⏎near future (e.g., disease treatment outcome prediction, clinical documentation and patient⏎chart analysis, pharmacovigilence, and patient education) are being developed, studied, and⏎tested.⏎2⏎⏎Although the adoption of AI in pharmacy practice brings opportunities, it also brings⏎challenges, risks, and barriers. Accelerating the adoption and use of AI can offer significant⏎benefits, provided regulations and guardrails needed to ensure the integrity and safety of data⏎and patients remain in place and are not removed. There is some concern about the proposed⏎HTI-5 (Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability) rule with regard to reducing and removing⏎some AI certification criteria. As AI is a tool that needs appropriate and ethical management, AI⏎adoption must be done in a responsible way.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its use in⏎clinical care?⏎The larger barriers affecting AI use in clinical care include:⏎• Lack of proper infrastructure. Many pharmacies, as well as other health care providers, do⏎not have the specialized hardware needed for training and running AI efficiently. Basic AI⏎tasks can run on standard CPUs (central processing units); however, heavy workloads for⏎deep learning require expensive, high-performance hardware with specialized GPUs⏎(graphic processing units). Incentivizing pharmacists through PIPs could help in securing⏎needed specialized hardware (see Interoperability comment below).⏎• Interoperability. Not all existing pharmacy clinical care systems can connect with AI⏎technologies. Pharmacy systems have been moving toward interoperability, though it is not⏎fully where it needs to be. One reason for this is pharmacies were not included in, nor⏎incentivized, as numerous other health care providers were, through the Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) Promoting Interoperability Programs (PIPs), formerly⏎the EHR Incentive Programs or “Meaningful Use.”⏎“Although electronic health record (EHR) was considered from the beginning of its use,⏎pharmacy software systems have remained largely on the sidelines of these conversations,⏎and as a result, their current state of interoperability has been limited.”1 A 2023 pharmacy⏎data interoperability report by Leavitt Partners “found that many pharmacy stakeholders⏎have inconsistent capacities for importing clinical data to create patient profiles and analyze⏎clinical documentation. While some pharmacies have found innovative solutions for viewing⏎clinical data from other providers, other pharmacies have had to use outdated modalities⏎such as email, fax, or mail. Because of these technical limitations in interoperability,⏎pharmacists are hindered in their ability to deliver clinical care they were trained to provide,⏎further frustrating data interoperability modernization.”2 Interoperability limitations also⏎lead to fragmented data (see also Data quality comment below).⏎The Leavitt report also recommends that CMS include “pharmacies in existing and⏎emerging value-based care models” and “incentivize pharmacies to invest further in data⏎1Kenneth C. Hohmeier, Jason Ausli, Laila Hajyani, et al, “Early implementation of Pharmacist eCare Plan and electronic health⏎record integration: A proof-of-concept study on health information interoperability,” JAPhA Practice Innovations, October 2025.⏎2 “Supporting Pharmacy Data Interoperability: An Imperative for Patient Access and Outcomes,” Leavitt Partners, April 2023,⏎page 6.⏎3⏎⏎interoperability by enabling them to contribute to patient value and share in revenue as⏎members of the interdisciplinary care team. This step could encourage pharmacy data⏎interoperability within existing statutory definitions of ‘provider.’”3⏎Including pharmacists in PIPs’ incentives would help strengthen interoperability and⏎ensure that pharmacists can obtain relevant clinical information, laboratory data,⏎medication changes, diagnoses, etc., in real time.⏎• Workforce challenges and skills gaps. AI adoption appears to be outpacing workforce⏎capability. Recent reports indicate that although AI adoption and usage in health care⏎increased in 2025, they highlight a significant gap between adoption rates and employer⏎training. A 2024 survey showed only 24% of health care workers received employer-⏎provided AI training, even though they are using AI tools.4 This survey also revealed that “AI⏎in health care is primarily used for administrative tasks, while more clinical applications are⏎still in the early stages of adoption.”5⏎• Contract clarity – liability. With the administration currently moving forward with⏎loosening and removing regulations to accelerate the development and adoption of AI,⏎liability allocation within contracts with AI health solution vendors becomes an even greater⏎concern that could impact the adoption of AI in health care settings, particularly⏎pharmacies. AI-generated content and recommendations influence medical and health care⏎decisions. Continued concern pertains to who is responsible if something goes wrong⏎regarding an AI recommendation.⏎“AI vendors position themselves as only a tool to health care providers and try to⏎disclaim liability for any downstream use.”6 Health care providers expect vendors to stand⏎behind their products, especially those products that generate clinical notes, diagnostic⏎suggestions, etc.7⏎The concern is that if regulations are loosened too much or removed, AI developers and⏎vendors will further limit their liability for their products and the content generated, making⏎health care providers the more liable because they are the users. Additionally, insurers are⏎starting to pivot away from AI liability. Three major insurers – AIG, Great American, and WR⏎Berkley – “recently submitted requests for regulatory approval to limit their liability for⏎claims arising from artificial intelligence systems, including chatbots and other automated⏎services.”8⏎If regulations are overly loosened or removed, pharmacists will need to try and⏎negotiate with AI vendors for specific indemnification for risks, such as algorithmic bias and⏎discrimination, data privacy breaches, incorrect AI-generated outputs (e.g., AI-specific⏎3 Ibid., page 12.⏎4 “Medscape and HIMSS Release 2024 Report on AI Adoption in Healthcare,” PRNewsire, December 6, 2024.⏎5 Ibid.⏎6 “AI Contracts in Health Care: Avoiding the Data Dumpster Fire,” Foley, June 26, 2025.⏎7 Ibid.⏎8 “Major Insurers are Pulling Back from AI Liability,” Metropolitan Risk blog, November 24, 2025.⏎4⏎⏎errors), data use and training rights, and data leakage. AI developers and vendors tend not⏎to be open to assuming more liability; they prefer less liability.⏎If the goal is to accelerate the adoption of AI in health care, ASTP then needs to help⏎health care providers by developing model language for use in contracts so that shared⏎liability is equitable, as well as issuing risk-tier rules regarding liability allocation.⏎• Data quality – siloed and fragmented. Health data is often siloed and fragmented, many⏎times because of interoperability limitations, making it not only hard to train AI models but⏎also preventing health care decision-makers (including pharmacists) from seeing the full⏎picture. Siloed data, known as the silent killer of AI initiatives, reduces AI model accuracy⏎and reliability. Generative and predictive AI models trained on partial data cannot⏎understand the full context of a problem, which could lead to poor and inaccurate health⏎recommendations from AI. Data and recommendations from AI in clinical care need to be⏎trusted.⏎Many pharmacy patients see multiple providers that prescribe medications and use⏎various payment systems (e.g., pharmacy benefit managers (PBMs), health plans) that⏎create siloed and fragmented data.⏎“Because no standardized or universally available data-sharing solution exists for⏎pharmacies, health plans have implemented a number of workarounds. While pharmacy⏎systems seamlessly share data related to prescription drug claims between pharmacies and⏎health plans, the same infrastructure has not been applied for sharing clinical services or⏎clinical patient data. Therefore, when health plans and pharmacies collaborate on clinical⏎initiatives, such as transitions of care programs, chronic disease management or other⏎initiatives, data-sharing workarounds must be relied upon.”9⏎The result is a pharmacist working with “multiple plans on multiple patient care⏎initiatives may have to document via various fragmented and disconnected systems that⏎require varying types and formats of information to be entered, often manually.”10⏎• Data and algorithmic bias. AI algorithms used to treat and diagnose patients can have⏎biases. Such bias in AI arises when it is trained on biased data. Although it may not be⏎intentional, medical data contain bias, which could perpetuate historical bias.11 Bias that⏎occurs in health care settings is generally found in patient notes. If data from those notes⏎are given to an AI model, the model will learn and reproduce those biases.12⏎Data bias increases the risk of discrimination in health care. If data lack diversity (e.g.,⏎age, gender, ethnicity, geographic locations, socioeconomic status, language, customs,⏎cultural, etc.) and is used for AI training, then the data are misrepresentative of the full⏎9 Ibid., page 9.⏎10 Ibid., page 9.⏎11 Sian Carey, Allan Pang, Marc de Kamps, “Fairness in AI for healthcare,” Future Healthcare Journal, Volume 11, Issue 3,⏎September 2024.⏎12 Ibid.⏎5⏎⏎population.13 Most health care data used to train AI models for use in the United States⏎comes from patients in the U.S., China, and parts of Europe.14 Not all patient data used to⏎train AI is representative of the U.S. population. “AI systems trained on biased data often⏎perform poorly when applied to populations with different genetic backgrounds, disease⏎prevalence, or environmental exposures.”15⏎Health care and health care systems will face challenges in addressing algorithmic⏎biases, especially as it relates to medications. AI for clinical care should be held to⏎standardized outcomes-focused metrics to ensure accuracy and to reduce risks that may⏎cause patient harm.⏎• Data privacy and security concerns. AI relies on vast amounts of patient data. Collecting⏎vast amounts of data creates privacy and security challenges. Health care practice settings,⏎including pharmacies, using AI are more vulnerable to data breaches and cyber-attacks⏎targeting patient information, drug data, etc., particularly as more pharmacists and other⏎health care providers use chatbots, such as ChatGPT, in health care.16 “Using ChatGPT can⏎put users at risk of losing control of sensitive patient information, source code, and more,”17⏎especially if patient identifiable information is inadvertently provided. As chatbots have⏎become the go-to interface for interacting with large language models (LLMs), not one LLM⏎does it all. This means multiple chatbots may likely be used, further increasing data privacy⏎and security risks.⏎Those adopting AI tools in health care will need to ensure the tools they use comply⏎with the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), as the data being used⏎by AI may likely include protected health information (PHI), personal data, and health data.⏎In this regard, AI developers and vendors need oversight.⏎• Expertise replacement. Relying solely on AI, without incorporating augmented AI, for⏎health care recommendations presents safety, clinical care, and liability risks. Pharmacists⏎play a vital role in bridging the gap between technology and personalized care. Integrating⏎AI into clinical care should not overshadow the human element of care; augmented AI as⏎the assistive role of AI can help in this regard. AI systems can make mistakes, and without⏎human oversight, errors can result in patient harm. Safeguards must be put into place to⏎ensure that a human verifies the accuracy and completeness of AI recommendations that⏎impact patient care.⏎Among the AI adoption and usage training needed in the health care workforce are:⏎data literacy (i.e., how to interpret AI-driven data, evaluate algorithms, and recognize⏎potential biases), understanding AI bias in AI models to prevent inequity in patient care,⏎managing human-AI collaboration, auditing AI-generated content (e.g., drug interaction⏎13 Kathleen Kenny, PharmD, RPh, “Carefully Consider Ethical Implications of Artificial Intelligence Use in Pharmacy, Pharmacy⏎Times, November 29, 2025.⏎14 Gabriel Onuh, “AI in healthcare risks could exclude 5 billion people; here’s what we know about it,” World Economic Forum,⏎October 2025.⏎15 Ibid.⏎16 Li J, “Security Implications of AI Chatbots in Health Care,” J Med Internet Res 2023; 25:e47551.⏎17 Ibid.⏎6⏎⏎checks, dosage recommendations), and integrating AI into clinical practice, especially into⏎existing workflows.⏎Training is also needed in data privacy, patient safety, and ethical considerations to⏎avoid over-reliance on technology.⏎• Costs. AI adoption is an expensive investment and may be prohibitive for some health care⏎providers, especially small providers. Although small-scale basic AI systems start average⏎between $10,000-$50,000, total implementation often start at $1 million and can exceed⏎$10 million.18 Mid-sized is $100,000-$500,000, while enterprise grade AI solutions can be⏎$1 million-$10 million.19 These costs are for initial implementation, not ongoing operational⏎and maintenance costs (average 15-30% of the original build cost); retraining and model⏎updates (AI is not static); infrastructure usage increases; and security, compliance, and risk⏎management.20⏎• Trust and ethics. This is the top-tier concern and barrier in AI adoption. Clinician trust in AI⏎and machine learning (ML)-based predictive DSI (differentiable search indexing) will be⏎crucial for adoption. A lack of trust comes from AI associated risks, such as bias and⏎fairness, data privacy, accountability and security, and limited transparency. As AI becomes⏎integrated into decision-making processes, ethical implications and concerns are arising.⏎Studies indicate that people lack trust in AI to make ethical decisions, which is critical in⏎health care, especially for patient safety.⏎Deloitte’s most recent survey of 1,800 businesses and technical professionals globally,⏎conducted in 2024, showed that 54% of respondents said AI “posed the most severe ethical⏎risk among emerging technologies, with top concerns for GenAI being data privacy,⏎transparency, and data provenance.”21 Further, the aforementioned study published by⏎JAPhA indicated that 34.9% of pharmacists expressed some degree of distrust of AI.22⏎AI faces many ethical dilemmas, especially in health care, as it is not inherently ethical⏎(lacks moral agency). The use of AI in health care raises ethical concerns related to patient⏎privacy, data, recommendations for treatment provided by diagnostic tools, and the⏎potential for AI to replace human expertise.⏎AI can be designed and taught to produce ethical, fair, and transparent outcomes,⏎provided ethical training is built in. To do that, AI developers must proactively mitigate bias⏎in training data; create algorithms that are interpretable, allowing humans to understand⏎the reasoning behind AI decisions; and be human-centric, aligning with human values.⏎To build trust in AI, however, government agencies need to be careful in loosening and⏎removing regulations and other guardrails designed to ensure the development and use of⏎18 Lina Taposhi, “The True Cost of Implementing AI in Business in 2026,” Rise Up Labs.⏎19 Ibid.⏎20 Ibid.⏎21 “State of Ethics and Trust in Technology Report,” Deloitte, September 2024.⏎22 Ibid., “Pharmacists’ perceptions,” JAPhA.⏎7⏎⏎AI in health care in general, clinical care specifically, is safe and does not harm patients.⏎Trust is crucial because AI systems will be making decisions that can significantly impact⏎individuals and society.⏎Patient safety and safeguarding public health need to be the top priority in AI-health⏎care development.⏎2. What HHS regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Pharmacists need to be included in the CMS PIPs incentives as eligible health⏎professionals. When the program was launched in 2011 as the EHR Incentive Programs or⏎“Meaningful Use” to promote the adoption of and meaningful use of Certified Electronic Health⏎Record Technology (CEHRT), pharmacists were excluded from the program. PHIT has requested⏎numerous times since the program’s inception that the Secretary remedy this oversight.⏎Although pharmacists have adopted EHR technology without incentives, as other health⏎care professionals have been afforded, not being included in the incentive program is one of⏎the reasons achieving interoperability in pharmacy settings has been slow and not fully where it⏎needs to be.⏎With the rapid advancement and integration of AI into pharmacy practice, PHIT strongly⏎believes it is time to include pharmacists in PIPs for needed incentives to adopt AI and so that⏎full interoperability can finally be realized.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎• Develop an AI toolkit to assist health care providers to safely integrate AI into clinical care.⏎ASTP currently provides tool kits for health care providers that support effective⏎implementation of health IT, interoperability, patient engagement, and EHR.⏎• Reactivate the ASTP Health IT Advisory Committee’s (HITAC) Pharmacy Interoperability and⏎Emerging Therapeutics Task Force to help address concerns and make further⏎recommendations to move AI adoption forward. In 2023, the pharmacy task force put⏎forward Recommendation 13 that included an AI component.⏎• Continue certification for developers of health IT, particularly AI, ML, deep learning AI, etc.⏎Certification would increase trust in AI.⏎• Because loosening and removing regulations will impact contracts between AI vendors and⏎health care providers, ASTP should develop model language for providers to use so that⏎shared liability is equitable. This could be part of an AI toolkit (noted above). As mentioned⏎previously, with the forthcoming changes from HTI-5, AI vendors will likely seek to further⏎limit their liability, making health care providers more liable because they are users of the AI⏎vendors’ products (see above “Contract clarity – liability” comments).⏎8⏎⏎• Establish a harmonized, risk-proportionate federal framework across federal health⏎agencies and state-level requirements. A unified framework would provide clarity and⏎support AI deployment in clinical care settings, including pharmacies.⏎• Include pharmacists in PIPs as eligible health professionals for those incentives, which will⏎help alleviate interoperability limitations. Pharmacists are consistently ranked among the⏎top three most trusted professionals in the United States, and they provide a range of⏎extensive clinical care services, especially to Medicare patients. In many rural areas,⏎pharmacists are often the only immediate accessible health care providers.⏎• Invest in public-private partnerships and demonstration projects that validate AI clinical⏎care referral models in real-world pharmacy settings.⏎****⏎The Pharmacy HIT Collaborative comprises the major national pharmacy associations,⏎representing 250,000 members. PHIT’s membership is composed of the key national pharmacy⏎associations involved in health IT, the National Council for Prescription Drug Programs, and⏎associate members encompassing e-prescribing, health information networks, transaction⏎processing networks, pharmacy companies, system vendors, pharmaceutical manufacturers,⏎and other organizations that support pharmacists’ services.⏎As the leading authority in pharmacy health information technology, PHIT’s vision and⏎mission are to ensure the U.S. health IT infrastructure better enables pharmacists to optimize⏎person-centered care. Supporting and advancing the use, usability, and interoperability of⏎health IT by pharmacists for person-centered care, PHIT identifies and voices the health IT⏎needs of pharmacists; promotes awareness of functionality and pharmacists’ use of health IT;⏎provides resources, guidance, and support for the adoption and implementation of standards-⏎driven health IT; and guides health IT standards development to address pharmacists’ needs.⏎For additional information, visit www.pharmacyhit.org.⏎*****⏎On behalf of PHIT, thank you again for the opportunity to comment on the Request for⏎Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎For more information, contact Jeff Rochon, Executive Director, Pharmacy HIT⏎Collaborative, at jeff@pharmacyhit.org.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Jeff Rochon, Pharm.D.⏎Executive Director⏎Pharmacy HIT Collaborative⏎jeff@pharmacyhit.org⏎9⏎⏎Chad Worz, PharmD, BCGP⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎American Society of Consultant Pharmacists (ASCP)⏎cworz@ascp.com⏎Ronna B. Hauser, PharmD⏎Senior VP, Policy & Pharmacy Affairs⏎National Community Pharmacists Association (NCPA)⏎ronna.hauser@ncpa.org⏎Michael D. Hogue, PharmD, FAPhA, FNAP, FFIP⏎Executive Vice President and CEO⏎American Pharmacists Association (APhA)⏎mhogue@aphanet.org⏎Scott Anderson, PharmD, MS, CPHIMS,⏎FASHP, FVSHP⏎Director, Member Relations⏎American Society of Health-System Pharmacists (ASHP)⏎SAnderson@ashp.org⏎Kevin Nicholson, R.Ph., JD,⏎Vice President of Policy, Regulatory, and Legal Affairs⏎National Association of Chain Drug Stores(NACDS)⏎KNicholson@NACDS.org⏎Kathy Pham, Pharm.D., BCPPS⏎Senior Director, Policy and Professional Affairs⏎American College of Clinical Pharmacy (ACCP)⏎kpham@accp.com⏎10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0039♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Health Innovation Alliance , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Health Innovation Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Deputy Secretary Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201 Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎Dear HHS Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary Dr. Keane,⏎The Health Innovation Alliance (HIA) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Department of Health and⏎Human Services’ Request for Information, Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care, regarding the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. We are encouraged⏎by the Department’s decision to approach this effort as a cross-agency collaboration with ASTP/ONC.⏎HIA is a diverse coalition of patient advocates, specialty care providers, and technology companies who⏎support the commonsense use of data and technology to improve health outcomes and lower costs. For nearly⏎two decades, HIA has advanced a risk-based, innovation-forward approach to health technology policy,⏎grounded in patient benefit, interoperability, and market competition.⏎Artificial intelligence provides significant benefits in clinical care outcomes and efficiency. Encouraging AI use⏎in federal health programs for clinical care would spark widespread patient outcome improvements, provider⏎productivity increases, and reduction in physician burnout and total cost of care. Federal policy should align⏎incentives to support those measurable outcomes without being tied to abstract deployment goals.⏎Patient and Provider Outcomes as the Anchor for AI Policy⏎AI-enabled tools are currently deployed across the patient life cycle and are actively reducing friction in care⏎coordination.1,2,3 Administrative applications (billing automation, fraud detection, and workflow optimization) can⏎reduce documentation burden and free clinicians to focus on patient care. In drug and device development, we⏎are seeing improvements in clinical trial design, patient recruitment, and post-market surveillance that are⏎attributable to AI models.4,5,6 These use cases illustrate new opportunities for patients and care delivery. But⏎they also underscore an important principle: AI adoption must be grounded in measurable improvement.⏎i. Patient benefit, including improved access, earlier detection, and more personalized care⏎ii. Provider impact, including reduced administrative burden and enhanced clinical decision support⏎See, for example:⏎1 Evaluating sepsis watch generalizability through multisite external validation of a sepsis machine learning model | npj Digital Medicine⏎2 A systematic literature review of predicting patient discharges using statistical methods and machine learning | Health Care⏎Management Science | Springer Nature Link⏎3 Expanding care coordination in an integrated health system through causal machine learning | npj Digital Medicine⏎4 The potential of artificial intelligence in pharmaceutical innovation: from drug discovery to clinical trials | Pharmaceuticals⏎5 Post-market surveillance of medical devices using AI | J Complement Altern Med Res pg 111.⏎6 Harnessing the AI/ML in drug and biological products discovery and development: the regulatory perspective | Pharmaceuticals⏎440 1st Street NW, Suite 430 Washington, DC 20001 | 202.643.8645 | Health-Innovation.org⏎⏎iii. Systems efficiency, including reductions in total cost of care⏎These lenses reflect HIA’s previously adopted principles for the use of artificial intelligence in health care and⏎life sciences:⏎• Risk-Based Approach: Regulation of AI in health care should be proportionate to risk.⏎• Transparency: Patients, users, and regulators should have access to meaningful information about how AI⏎technologies are being used, without mandating disclosure of proprietary information.⏎• Privacy: Any use of health information by AI must comply with existing laws and regulations, including⏎HIPAA.⏎• Responsibility: Developers and users of AI programs in health care should adopt and adhere to best⏎practices and oversight processes.⏎• Fairness: AI tools should mitigate bias and be available for all users, not just those who can afford them.⏎These principles provide the foundation for how HHS should approach reimbursement, interoperability,⏎evaluation, and research policy in the clinical space.⏎Barriers to AI adoption are no longer primarily technological; adoption of AI tools in federal programs lags⏎because of a lack of formal payment pathways to encourage their use and constraints from data⏎fragmentation. As the federal government seeks to accelerate adoption of AI in clinical care, it must address⏎these structural barriers head on.⏎Reimbursement and Incentive Alignment as the Primary Adoption Lever⏎Reimbursement materially influences whether innovation is widely deployed and how it is used. Across our⏎membership, there is broad agreement that incentive alignment is the most powerful federal lever for⏎accelerating responsible AI integration into the broader health care system.⏎In volume-based environments, specific productivity gains and associated cost of delivering care reductions⏎are not reflected in current reimbursement frameworks. In risk-bearing arrangements, savings may accrue⏎primarily to managing entities, not patients or taxpayers. These dynamics can create uncertainty for innovators⏎and care delivery organizations seeking to deploy accessible AI tools.⏎HIA does not endorse a single payment architecture but instead believes that:⏎1. AI-enabled innovation should be viable in traditional Medicare, Medicare Advantage, Medicaid⏎managed care, accountable care arrangements, and commercial markets alike.⏎2. Improvements in care delivery should be portable across payment models and not confined to⏎reimbursement structures.⏎3. Federal policy should ensure that reimbursement in cross care and dual coverage environments will⏎recognize measurable improvements in outcomes, access, and efficiency without favoring one care⏎delivery model over feasible technology-enabled care improvements.⏎These ideas are developed below.⏎Expand CPT Pathways Where Appropriate⏎Where AI materially enhances a covered clinical service or enables new forms of care management, HHS⏎should work with relevant stakeholders to ensure that coding and reimbursement pathways reflect enhanced⏎services. This does not require developing new reimbursement codes for software as a standalone product.⏎2⏎440 1st Street NW, Suite 430 Washington, DC 20001 | 202.643.8645 | Health-Innovation.org⏎⏎Instead, the Department should reward AI-enabled services that require additional clinical oversight,⏎infrastructure, or workflow redesign that is not adequately captured under Current Procedural Terminology⏎code set. Clear and predictable coding pathways reduce uncertainty and encourage responsible deployment⏎tied to measurable service improvement.⏎Recent developments in Remote Physiologic Monitoring (RPM) illustrate how CMS can design payment⏎structures that align incentives with high-value, digitally enabled care. Introduced in 2019, CPT codes 99453,⏎99454, 99457, and 99458 have seen utilization increase more than fourfold.7 Payment policy clearly influenced⏎adoption during a period of Medicare coverage expansion (amidst the COVID-19 pandemic), underscoring⏎nuances in provider demand, and driving reimbursement discussions regarding targeting, documentation⏎standards, and value under fee-for-service payment. RPM uptake provides a practical template for how future⏎AI-enabled services can be incorporated into the CPT framework. HIA supports thoughtful refinement of⏎reimbursement pathways when new technologies materially enhance care delivery, such as many applications⏎of AI. The coalition believes that HHS should expand reimbursement pathways where appropriate to recognize⏎AI-enabled services that demonstrably improve patient outcomes and integrate seamlessly into existing care⏎delivery structures. Targeted updates to CPT provide innovators with predictable reimbursement clarity tied to⏎measurable value and lead to greater uptake.⏎Coding infrastructure is evolving to better capture AI-enabled services that produce clinically meaningful⏎outputs. HHS should continue engaging with stakeholders to ensure that these pathways support measurable⏎service improvement, promote consistency across payment environments, and provide innovators with⏎predictable reimbursement clarity. Inadequate space for new procedures and lack of specificity are⏎fundamental limitations of CPT. Expanding AI enabled care through new reimbursement codes would be⏎easier, more logical, and better designed under an ICD10 PCS framework. PCS is open source and in the⏎public domain, which avoids administrative costs associated with CPT. ICD-10-PCS is based on a uniform⏎alphanumeric seven-character multi-axial structure with clear definitions of the core terminology used in the⏎code descriptions. Each character of an ICD-10-PCS code represents a specific clearly defined concept and⏎would more easily and logically expand AI-enabled clinical care into the physician office space (beyond⏎hospital inpatient) in Medicare and other programs. HIA welcomes the opportunity to provide input as these⏎frameworks mature.⏎AI-Inclusive Bundled and Episode-Based Payments⏎AI applications improve efficiency and reduce administrative obstacles. For example, ambient documentation⏎tools shorten time spent on paperwork. Clinical decision support tools reduce duplicative testing and improve⏎diagnostic precision. Remote monitoring and predictive analytics help prevent avoidable admissions. AI-⏎enabled intake and scheduling systems streamline care delivery. Bundled payments and chronic care models⏎offer flexibility to integrate AI tools that reduce complications, improve adherence, and enhance coordination.⏎HHS should emphasize that AI-enabled workflow improvements are permissible within existing and future⏎bundled and value-based arrangements (DRGs, APCs, MSSP, and CMMI accountable care models). A clear⏎message should be delivered to care delivery systems that are considering using AI tools: payment models will⏎continue to reward outcome improvement and efficiency. Federal reimbursement structures should not⏎inadvertently favor legacy, site-based delivery assumptions when high-quality care can be delivered through⏎distributed, virtual, and hybrid models supported by AI.⏎The Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation should explicitly allow testing AI-enabled care models across⏎Medicare and Medicaid populations. Demonstrations should focus on areas of highest need and opportunity in⏎chronic disease management, documentation processing, and technology-enabled integrated care models,⏎including those that build upon the ACCESS initiative. Pilots without demonstrated improvements should⏎sunset accordingly and should have no ramp to permanent entitlements.⏎7 Rapid Growth Of Remote Patient Monitoring Is Driven By A Small Number Of Primary Care Providers | Health Affairs⏎3⏎440 1st Street NW, Suite 430 Washington, DC 20001 | 202.643.8645 | Health-Innovation.org⏎⏎CMMI demonstrations allow providers and innovators to share in savings generated through measurable⏎improvements while maintaining appropriate beneficiary protections.⏎CMMI Demonstrations and Transitional Add-On or Pass-Through Payments⏎In certain contexts, temporary add-on or pass-through payments will accelerate responsible adoption. The New⏎Technology Add-on Payment (NTAP) designation enhances reimbursement for services based on:⏎• Newness⏎• Cost not recognized in traditional payment pathways⏎• Easily demonstrable and substantial clinical improvement⏎CMS has created alternative NTAP approval pathways that could be enhanced to include a new alternative⏎pathway for AI assistive technologies. For AI, we suggest adding new categories of burden reduction, access⏎expansion, or outcome and patient engagement improvement metrics as qualifiers for AI NTAP classification⏎and create flexibility around the FDA marketing requirement.⏎Catalysts for evidence generation must fasten support to measurable enhancement. CMS should specifically⏎engage stakeholders through the annual NTAP process to encourage AI model applications for alternative⏎NTAP pathways.⏎Interoperability and Information Blocking Enforcement as Safety Infrastructure⏎AI tools depend on access to complete and timely patient data to generate clinically meaningful outputs. When⏎electronic health information is incomplete, delayed, or fragmented across systems, model performance⏎degrades and patient risk increases.⏎Interoperability is the foundational infrastructure for safe and effective AI in clinical care. Incomplete⏎longitudinal records jeopardize patients through elevated clinical decision support error risk, imaging⏎misinterpretation, medication contraindication oversight, and treatment misalignment. Advanced AI agents and⏎decision-support systems require structured data that is reliably captured across care settings in real time.⏎Document exchange alone is insufficient; AI-enabled tools depend on consistent data elements and machine-⏎readable formats that permit algorithms to interpret clinical context accurately. Reliable interoperability also⏎enables site-specific validation, allowing model performance to be evaluated under real-world conditions rather⏎than inferred from training environments. Even well-validated models may degrade in real-world environments⏎when information blocking limits lawful access to the high-quality data required for safe, accurate, and⏎consistent performance.⏎Despite statutory guardrails established under the 21st Century Cures Act, information blocking remains a⏎persistent structural barrier to data exchange. When data exchange is restricted through technical, contractual,⏎or economic barriers, technology developers and care delivery organizations are forced to operate in⏎fragmented environments. This reduces competition, limits innovation, and constrains patient benefit. The⏎Administration has demonstrated an appetite for linkable longitudinal data across federal health programs,⏎recognizing AI in clinical care depends on cross-setting patient records that allow algorithms to interpret clinical⏎context accurately.⏎HIA supports these efforts and believes stronger longitudinal data linkage requires enforcement against⏎information blocking and continued progress on interoperability standards. We are concerned many bad actors⏎flaunt information blocking requirements because HHS enforcement has been lackluster. The Trusted⏎Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA) represents an important step toward expanding⏎exchange pathways. Participation in TEFCA should increase data availability and reduce friction. However,⏎participation alone should not be treated as a safe harbor for behavior that functionally restricts access,⏎exchange, or use of electronic health information.⏎4⏎440 1st Street NW, Suite 430 Washington, DC 20001 | 202.643.8645 | Health-Innovation.org⏎⏎HIA urges ASTP/ONC, CMS, and the HHS Office of Inspector General to coordinate actively and use all its⏎enforcement tools against bad actors who seek to limit interoperability that frustrates new AI tools. Without⏎meaningful enforcement, even well-designed AI reimbursement policies will fail to deliver their intended patient⏎benefits.⏎Risk-Based Evaluation and Governance⏎HIA reiterates its longstanding position that regulation should not be triggered solely by the presence of an⏎algorithm. A function-based regulatory approach that treats all AI-enabled tools as inherently high-risk will chill⏎beneficial deployment without improving patient safety.⏎HIA does not support the creation of a new federal AI certification regime or centralized validation body as a⏎condition of adoption or reimbursement. Federal “seals” or technology-specific approval structures risk slowing⏎innovation cycles in software and AI while duplicating existing authorities.⏎HHS should encourage the use of established governance practices that promote transparency and⏎accountability without mandating disclosure of proprietary information. Model documentation, lifecycle⏎monitoring, performance tracking, and drift detection can operate together to preserve accountability. Oversight⏎should be proportionate to risk, weighing regulation against reliance level, potential of downstream harm, and⏎context of deployment. Additionally, risk could vary for one specific AI tool depending on where and how it is⏎deployed– any regulatory structure and oversight should take this into account.⏎Preserving U.S. Leadership in Health Care AI⏎As health agencies consider additional AI governance structures, they should avoid regulatory models that⏎single out specific AI architectures or impose categorical restrictions without regard for use case. Some⏎international proposals have favored static models with one-size-fits-all regulatory frameworks, rather than⏎tailoring existing device, payment, privacy, and program integrity authorities already in place to address⏎misuse. Many AI-enabled tools are derivative of existing processes embedded in healthcare delivery and⏎therefore should be evaluated based on their application rather than the presence of an algorithmic⏎component. HHS should build on current statutory and regulatory authorities across FDA, CMS, ASTP/ONC,⏎and OIG rather than creating duplicative, technology-specific oversight structures. Federal oversight across⏎regulating agencies is already capable of risk-based calculations that may be triggered by the clinical or⏎administrative use case, not solely by the inclusion of an AI component. Precise, incremental improvements to⏎existing authorities will encourage responsible innovation in return, without replicating more rigid approaches⏎emerging in other jurisdictions.⏎Future regulatory frameworks must incorporate the benefits of post-deployment testing, ongoing monitoring,⏎and drift detection that is calibrated to risk and intended use through site specific training. Clinical⏎environments differ meaningfully in patient populations, workflow design, data completeness, and technical⏎infrastructure. As a result, performance demonstrated in development or training environments may not fully⏎reflect how a model performs in practice. Our membership views real-world testing and monitoring as the gold⏎standard for performance demonstration. Training data cannot replace site-specific testing as an AI⏎performance evaluation tool. At the same time, transparency requirements should focus on communicating⏎intended use and known limitations to regulators, without mandating disclosure of proprietary model⏎architectures. Protecting intellectual property while promoting meaningful performance documentation⏎preserves incentives for private-sector investment. Maintaining America’s edge in clinical AI innovation⏎requires sustained engagement with developers and care delivery partners, while pushing back against trends⏎from international regulatory bodies where AI applications are singled out without risk-based rationale.⏎Federal Research, Development, and Demonstration Pathways⏎Public-private partnerships, cooperative agreements, and pilot programs provide structured environments to⏎test AI-enabled care models under real-world conditions. Integration of AI into models such as ACCESS and⏎5⏎440 1st Street NW, Suite 430 Washington, DC 20001 | 202.643.8645 | Health-Innovation.org⏎⏎other technology-enabled care initiatives can provide early signals about clinical effectiveness, operational⏎feasibility, and beneficiary experience.⏎Conclusion⏎HIA appreciates the Department’s coordinated approach to accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. AI⏎adoption should be measured by improvements in patient outcomes, reduced administrative burden, expanded⏎access, and lower total cost of care. When incentives are aligned, data flows freely, and governance is⏎proportionate to risk, innovation will follow.⏎We look forward to continued engagement with HHS and stand ready to serve as a resource as the⏎Department advances this important work.⏎Sincerely,⏎Joel White⏎President⏎6⏎440 1st Street NW, Suite 430 Washington, DC 20001 | 202.643.8645 | Health-Innovation.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0040♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Mitchell♔♔Colorado♔♔United States♔National Disability Rights Network♔♔♔♔♔♔The framing of this RFI concerns me more than any single question it asks. HHS is soliciting input on how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, but the question of whether to accelerate — and for whom, and under what preconditions — is being treated as already settled. It isn't.⏎⏎The National Disability Rights Network works directly with safety-net providers and the patients who depend on them, and what we see on the ground does not support an acceleration-first posture. I want to address specifically the RFI's questions around implementation barriers and equity considerations, because I think the agency is looking at those two things separately when they are actually the same problem.⏎⏎Our policy review work, including analysis of incident reports submitted to practice management helplines between 2022 and 2024, consistently shows that AI-related billing and diagnostic discrepancies concentrate in safety-net settings. Not randomly. Not uniformly. In clinics serving Medicaid-enrolled patients, patients with limited English proficiency, patients with cognitive or communication-related disabilities. The patients who are already least positioned to notice when something has gone wrong with a recommendation, least able to push back, least likely to have a care team with enough bandwidth to catch an algorithmic error before it affects their treatment.⏎⏎This isn't an abstract concern about bias in training data, though that matters too. It's about what happens when an under-resourced rural clinic in, say, southwestern Colorado adopts an AI diagnostic tool primarily because they can't hire enough staff. The AI isn't supplementing clinical judgment there. It is substituting for it. And if the tool produces a wrong output for a 71-year-old Medicaid patient whose first language is Spanish and who has a physical disability affecting how she presents symptoms, there is no redundant layer of human review catching that error. The efficiency gain that makes the tool attractive to the clinic is exactly the thing that removes the safety net for the patient.⏎⏎HHS has civil rights obligations under Section 504 and Section 1557 that don't disappear because a new technology is involved. I'm genuinely uncertain whether the Department has done a serious, documented equity impact assessment of what accelerated AI deployment means for these populations before issuing this RFI. If it has, that analysis should be made available for public comment. If it hasn't, then what we are really being asked to comment on is a decision that has already been made without the foundational work that HHS's own equity commitments require.⏎⏎I also want to flag something that I think gets lost in these policy discussions. A 2024 survey of dental practitioners found that 31% had received no formal guidance on disclosure obligations when AI systems inform clinical recommendations. Thirty-one percent. And that's among practitioners who are engaged enough to respond to a survey. The informed consent frameworks that currently exist were not designed with algorithmic decision-making in mind, and patients with cognitive disabilities or limited English proficiency are already among the populations for whom standard consent processes are least adequate. Accelerating deployment before that gap is addressed isn't neutral. It's a choice about who bears the risk.⏎⏎I recognize there are real benefits to AI in clinical care and I don't think NDRN's position is that development should stop. But the RFI question about removing implementation barriers needs to be understood in context. Some of what reads as a "barrier" is actually a protection. The friction involved in documenting how an AI tool performs across demographic subgroups, or in training staff on disclosure obligations, or in conducting pre-deployment equity review — that friction exists for reasons. Before HHS moves to reduce it, I would want to know specifically which barriers are being targeted, whether any of them currently function as protections for the populations I've described, and what replaces them if they are streamlined away.⏎⏎We are happy to provide more detailed input on specific questions and can connect HHS staff with member organizations serving the populations most affected. Contact information for our policy team is available at www.ndrn.org — though I'd note our main policy contact address has been in flux since our office reorganization earlier this year and the website may not yet reflect the updated♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0041♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Scott♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔NVIDIA Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔My concern with this RFI is less about whether AI belongs in clinical care — it clearly has a role — and more about whether HHS has thought carefully enough about the gap between how these tools perform in controlled validation studies versus how they actually behave once deployed across the full range of hospital environments, patient populations, and clinical workflows that exist in the real world.⏎⏎I work in compliance at a company that builds and sells AI infrastructure to health systems. I have a direct financial interest in seeing AI adoption accelerate. I want to be transparent about that. But that's also why I think the governance question matters more than some of the commentary in this space acknowledges. When AI tools fail visibly and publicly in clinical settings, the reputational and liability consequences land on the companies that built and deployed them, not on the agencies that encouraged rapid adoption. So I don't think the industry's interest and the patient safety interest are as opposed as they sometimes get framed.⏎⏎The specific issue I keep coming back to is what gets called "automation bias" in the clinical AI literature. There's a reasonably well-documented body of research showing that when clinicians are given AI-generated recommendations, their independent judgment tends to track those recommendations even when the AI is wrong — sometimes especially when the AI is wrong, because the failure modes of these systems often occur in exactly the edge cases where a fatigued or cognitively overloaded clinician is most likely to defer. The apparent cognitive burden reduction that makes these tools attractive can produce worse net outcomes if the underlying model performance degrades outside its training distribution and nobody is monitoring for that in real time.⏎⏎NVIDIA Corporation Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎This isn't a theoretical concern. The sepsis prediction algorithm deployed at University of Michigan Health was the subject of a fairly detailed independent audit published in 2023 in JAMA Internal Medicine that found substantial performance degradation in subgroups that were underrepresented in the training data. That's one well-documented case. The concern I have is that we don't have a systematic mechanism for surfacing these cases before they become patient safety events or litigation triggers.⏎⏎What I'd like to see HHS address specifically — and I recognize this is only one of the questions the RFI is asking — is what post-market surveillance requirements should apply to AI tools that don't meet the FDA's definition of a software as a medical device but are still being used to support clinical decisions. This is the liability ambiguity problem that I hear about constantly from legal and compliance teams across the industry. Right now there's no clear answer. A tool that summarizes a patient chart and flags potential drug interactions might be classified as a clinical decision support tool exempt from FDA oversight, but it's functionally doing something that, if wrong, could cause direct patient harm. The regulatory category and the actual risk profile are not well-aligned.⏎⏎I'm not arguing against HHS's goal here. I think there are real opportunities. But accelerating adoption without closing this surveillance gap first is going to produce the same pattern we saw with EHR rollout — rapid deployment, followed by years of documented unintended consequences, followed by expensive retrospective fixes. The certification and interoperability problems from that era are still not fully resolved and we're already talking about layering AI on top of that infrastructure.⏎⏎Regarding the question about reimbursement pathways specifically: I think payer coverage policy is actually where a lot of the practical deployment decisions get made, and the inconsistency across payers right now is creating a situation where health systems are making AI investment decisions based on anticipated coverage that may or may not materialize. I've seen product deployment timelines slip by six to nine months because a health system was waiting on a coverage determination from a major commercial payer that never came through in the expected form. That's a real cost, and I understand why HHS wants to address it. But I'd want any streamlined reimbursement pathway to include explicit conditions around ongoing performance reporting, not just pre-deployment validation. The problem isn't getting these tools approved. It's knowing whether they're still working the way they were validated to work eighteen months after go-live in a new patient population.⏎⏎One other thing that I don't think gets enough attention: the vendors doing the post-market monitoring need clear guidance on what data they're allowed to use for that monitoring under HIPAA. Right now there's genuine legal uncertainty about whether a vendor can access de-identified encounter data from a deployed model to evaluate drift, even with a BAA in place, and different covered entities have very different interpretations of what's permissible. That's a practical barrier that a clarifying guidance document from OCR could probably address without⏎⏎NVIDIA Corporation Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎requiring any rulemaking, and it would meaningfully change what's operationally feasible for ongoing surveillance.⏎⏎Contact: sscott@nvidia.com⏎Submitted on behalf of: NVIDIA Corporation, Compliance Division♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0042♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Dougherty, Michelle , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Dougherty♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via the Federal eRulemaking Portal: http://www.regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health & Human Services⏎330 C St., SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI RIN 0955-AA13)⏎On behalf of the Long-Term and Post-Acute Care (LTPAC) Health Information Technology⏎Collaborative, we appreciate the opportunity to respond to the Department of Health and Human Services⏎(HHS) Request for Information (RFI) regarding accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence⏎(AI) as part of clinical care. Our collaborative represents providers serving millions of Medicare⏎beneficiaries across skilled nursing facilities, home health agencies, inpatient rehabilitation facilities,⏎hospices, and other LTPAC settings.⏎The LTPAC sector serves the fastest-growing segment of the U.S. population: adults aged 65 and older,⏎with those over 85 increasing at the most rapid pace. These individuals typically present with multiple⏎chronic conditions (multimorbidity), polypharmacy, cognitive and functional impairments, and complex⏎social determinants of health. Despite serving this critical population, LTPAC providers face significant⏎barriers to technology adoption, data interoperability, and participation in the innovative ecosystem that⏎will shape AI deployment in clinical care.⏎The LTPAC Collaborative strongly supports the Administration's priority to establish a forward-⏎leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate AI adoption in healthcare. As⏎articulated in the HHS AI Strategy released December 4, 2025, harnessing the transformative potential of⏎AI while upholding patient privacy, civil rights, and civil liberties represents a critical national priority.⏎The LTPAC sector stands ready to partner with HHS to ensure AI-driven innovation benefits all segments⏎of the healthcare ecosystem, particularly the rapidly growing population of older Americans.⏎We look forward to collaborating with HHS ASTP/ONC to ensure that AI-driven innovation benefits all⏎segments of the healthcare ecosystem, particularly those who have historically been underrepresented in⏎research and technology initiatives. This response addresses the specific questions posed in the RFI and⏎highlights concerns regarding population bias in AI development—risks that could be amplified without⏎intentional policy interventions.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎1 of 23⏎⏎General Comments on Regulation, Reimbursement, and Research & Development⏎Regulation⏎The LTPAC Collaborative supports a regulatory framework that is proportionate, transparent, and enables⏎innovation while protecting vulnerable populations. For AI tools deployed in LTPAC settings, regulatory⏎clarity is essential regarding:⏎● Clinical Decision Support (CDS) versus Decision Support Interventions (DSI): Under the HTI-⏎1 Final Rule, distinctions between CDS software and DSI must account for workflow-critical⏎interventions in LTPAC settings, such as glucose monitoring in diabetes management, delirium⏎detection, fall risk assessment, and polypharmacy optimization. Many AI tools operating in these⏎domains directly impact patient safety and outcomes.⏎● Algorithmic Transparency and Accountability: AI tools targeting LTPAC populations must⏎undergo validation against geriatric cohorts and demonstrate transparency in their decision-⏎making logic. Vendor accountability for algorithmic bias affecting safety outcomes should be⏎enforced, particularly for tools addressing conditions prevalent in older adults.⏎● Age-Inclusive Standards: Regulatory frameworks should mandate age-stratified data collection⏎and validation in real-world data (RWD) studies to mitigate age bias in AI algorithms. Current⏎clinical trials frequently underrepresent geriatric patients, leading to biased algorithms that may⏎recommend inappropriate care pathways for older adults.⏎● Cybersecurity and HIPAA Compliance: Accelerating AI adoption must not come at the expense⏎of patient and provider security. AI development guardrails, scaled proportionately to⏎cybersecurity and HIPAA risk, are essential. This is particularly important since LTPAC⏎providers lack the technical expertise to independently assess protections embedded in⏎proprietary, often "black box," vendor technology that handles residents' personally identifiable⏎information (PII) and protected health information (PHI).⏎Reimbursement⏎Payment policies significantly influence technology adoption in LTPAC settings. The Collaborative⏎recommends:⏎● Value-Based Payment Alignment: Payment models should incentivize the use of high-value AI⏎interventions that improve outcomes for complex, high-need populations. Alternative payment⏎models (APMs) and value-based care arrangements should explicitly include quality measures⏎and risk-adjustment methodologies that account for LTPAC populations.⏎● Coverage for AI-Enabled Remote Monitoring: Medicare and Medicaid should expand coverage⏎for AI-enabled remote patient monitoring (RPM), ambient AI, and other technologies that support⏎aging in place and reduce preventable hospitalizations and emergency department visits.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎2 of 23⏎⏎● Infrastructure Investment: Reimbursement mechanisms should support the underlying⏎infrastructure required for AI adoption, including interoperability investments, workforce⏎training, and technical assistance for small and rural providers.⏎● Reduction of Administrative Burden: AI tools that automate documentation, quality reporting,⏎and administrative tasks should be incentivized through payment policy to offset provider burden⏎and allow clinicians to focus on direct patient care.⏎Research & Development⏎HHS should prioritize R&D investments that address the unique needs of aging, geriatric, and LTPAC⏎populations:⏎● Inclusion of LTPAC Populations in Research: NIH, AHRQ, and other HHS research agencies⏎should require inclusion of LTPAC and geriatric populations in AI research funding. Pilot⏎programs should focus on AI applications in nursing homes, home health, assisted living, and⏎other post-acute settings.⏎● Interoperability Research: Support development and validation of Fast Healthcare⏎Interoperability Resources (FHIR) implementation guides that capture geriatric-specific data⏎elements, including functional status, cognitive assessments, social determinants of health, and⏎caregiver support data.⏎● Public-Private Partnerships: Establish cooperative research and development agreements⏎(CRADAs) with LTPAC providers, health IT vendors, and academic institutions to co-develop⏎and validate AI tools in real-world settings.⏎● Longitudinal and Multimorbidity Research: Fund large, longitudinal studies on aging⏎trajectories, multimorbidity trials, polypharmacy optimization, and research on family caregivers⏎and aging in place.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎For LTPAC providers and the populations they serve, the most significant barriers include:⏎Population Bias and Data Exclusion: Clinical trials and AI training datasets systematically⏎underrepresent older adults, particularly those in LTPAC settings. This leads to biased algorithms that⏎perform poorly or produce harmful recommendations when applied to geriatric populations. AI models⏎trained predominantly on younger cohorts may recommend inappropriate medication dosages, fail to⏎account for age-related pharmacokinetics, or overlook fall risk factors specific to frail older adults.⏎Multiple studies have documented that older adults, particularly those over 85 or living with multiple⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎3 of 23⏎⏎chronic conditions, are routinely excluded from AI training data, and models fail to account for complex⏎social needs of aging populations.⏎Data Interoperability Limitations: LTPAC providers face significant challenges in achieving⏎interoperability with acute care hospitals, primary care providers, specialty clinics, and health information⏎exchanges. The healthcare ecosystem is not designed to be inclusive of LTPAC populations. Limited⏎adoption of standardized data exchange formats, particularly HL7 FHIR implementation guides relevant⏎to LTPAC (such as those developed by the PACIO Project), hinders the flow of critical patient⏎information across care transitions. Statistics demonstrate that while 78% of nursing homes and home⏎health agencies have adopted EHRs, fewer than 40% can easily share structured data with acute care⏎EHRs, with poorly coordinated care transitions associated with hospital readmission, medication errors,⏎and adverse drug events. Without access to longitudinal, interoperable data, AI tools cannot effectively⏎support person-centered, holistic care.⏎Infrastructure and Resource Constraints: Many LTPAC providers, particularly small, rural, and⏎under-resourced facilities, lack the technical infrastructure, broadband connectivity, and financial⏎resources needed to adopt AI technologies. The digital divide in LTPAC settings creates inequitable⏎access to innovation. Additionally, workforce challenges, including limited health IT expertise and high⏎staff turnover, impede successful AI implementation. LTPAC providers prioritize resources for staffing⏎and regulatory compliance over interoperability, creating structural barriers that federal policy must⏎address.⏎Regulatory Uncertainty: Lack of clarity regarding liability, indemnification, privacy, and security for⏎non-medical device AI tools creates hesitation among LTPAC providers and AI developers. Questions⏎about who bears responsibility when an AI tool produces an adverse recommendation, how HIPAA⏎applies to AI-generated insights, and what standards govern AI tool validation remain inadequately⏎addressed.⏎Reimbursement Barriers: Fee-for-service payment structures provide limited incentives for LTPAC⏎providers to invest in AI tools, even when such tools could improve outcomes and reduce costs over time.⏎Coverage and reimbursement decisions for AI-enabled services are slow, and current payment models do⏎not adequately account for the value of AI interventions that prevent adverse events, reduce⏎hospitalization, or improve quality of life.⏎Lack of Geriatric-Specific Clinical Guidelines Integration: AI-powered clinical decision support tools⏎often lack integration with geriatric-specific clinical practice guidelines. Evidence-based guidelines⏎tailored for older adults, such as those published by the Post-Acute and Long-Term Care Medical⏎Association (PALTmed), address conditions like diabetes management, falls prevention, delirium⏎detection, polypharmacy, and pressure ulcer prevention in geriatric populations. AI tools that do not⏎incorporate these guidelines may produce recommendations that are inappropriate for LTPAC patients.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎4 of 23⏎⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎The LTPAC Collaborative recommends the following priority changes:⏎Regulatory Changes⏎● Mandate Age-Stratified Validation (42 CFR Part 412, 413, 482, 483, 484, 485): Require AI⏎tools used in clinical care to undergo validation testing on age-stratified cohorts, particularly for⏎tools intended for use in Medicare populations. Validation should include performance metrics⏎specific to geriatric patients and demonstrate absence of age-based bias.⏎● Algorithmic Transparency Requirements (45 CFR Part 170): Under the ONC Health IT⏎Certification Program, establish certification criteria for AI-enabled clinical decision support⏎tools that require disclosure of training data characteristics, model performance on diverse⏎populations, and explanation of decision logic. FHIR-based event-condition-action rules per HL7⏎Clinical Reasoning modules should be used to enforce transparency.⏎● Interoperability Standards for Geriatric Data (45 CFR 170.315): Expand the United States⏎Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) to include geriatric-specific data elements captured in⏎PACIO Project FHIR implementation guides: functional status (ADLs/IADLs), cognitive⏎assessments (PHQ-9, CAM), advance directives, standardized medication profiles, social⏎determinants of health, and caregiver support information. HL7 PACIO frameworks have been⏎validated for functional status, cognitive assessments, transition of care, and clinical conditions⏎prevalent in older adults.⏎● Privacy and Security Safeguards (45 CFR Parts 160, 164): Clarify HIPAA applicability to AI-⏎generated clinical insights and establish standards for de-identification, consent management, and⏎audit trails for AI tools that access or generate protected health information.⏎Payment Policy Changes⏎● Medicare Coverage for AI-Enabled RPM (42 CFR Part 405, 410, 414): Expand Medicare Part⏎B coverage for AI-enabled remote patient monitoring, ambient AI monitoring, and wearable⏎devices that support aging in place. Evidence demonstrates that RPM in skilled nursing facilities⏎enables real-time alerts and immediate response to abnormal readings, with studies showing⏎significant reductions in Hemoglobin A1c in diabetic patients and decreased need for emergency⏎department visits. Reimbursement should cover device costs, data transmission, clinical⏎interpretation, and care coordination activities.⏎● Value-Based Payment Incentives (42 CFR Part 425, 512): Incorporate AI adoption and outcome⏎metrics into Medicare Shared Savings Program (MSSP), Accountable Care Organization (ACO)⏎models, and other APMs. Quality measures should reward use of AI tools that demonstrate⏎improved outcomes for high-risk, complex patients. Evidence from randomized controlled trials⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎5 of 23⏎⏎demonstrates that AI-based early warning models significantly reduced hospital and 30-day⏎mortality rates and shortened hospital stays in prospective clinical validation.⏎● Infrastructure Investment Payments (42 CFR Part 413, 484, 485): Create a Medicare/Medicaid⏎infrastructure payment mechanism to support LTPAC providers' investments in interoperability,⏎health IT upgrades, and AI readiness. This could be modeled after the Medicare Promoting⏎Interoperability Program but tailored for LTPAC settings directly addressing the 15-year⏎technology investment gap created by LTPAC exclusion from Meaningful Use incentives.⏎● Documentation Burden Reduction (42 CFR Part 483, 484): Allow AI-generated documentation⏎to satisfy regulatory documentation requirements in skilled nursing facilities (42 CFR 483.20,⏎483.21) and home health agencies (42 CFR 484.55, 484.60), provided the AI tool meets⏎transparency and accuracy standards.⏎Programmatic Design Changes⏎● CMMI Innovation Models: Develop Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation (CMMI)⏎models specifically focused on AI adoption in LTPAC settings. Evaluate payment incentives,⏎technical assistance models, and shared savings approach for providers implementing AI tools.⏎● LTPAC-Focused FHIR Adoption: Provide grants and technical assistance through the Office of⏎the National Coordinator (ONC) to accelerate LTPAC provider adoption of PACIO Project FHIR⏎implementation guides and onboarding to Qualified Health Information Networks (QHINs) under⏎the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA).⏎● Geriatric AI Validation Testbeds: Establish regulatory-science testbeds in partnership with FDA,⏎VA, and CMS to validate AI tools for safety, efficacy, and equity in LTPAC settings. Require⏎real-world evidence from diverse care settings for regulatory approval.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist related to AI⏎in clinical care, and what role should HHS play?⏎Liability and Accountability: When an AI tool recommends a clinical intervention that results in patient⏎harm, liability questions arise: Is the provider liable for following the recommendation? Is the AI vendor⏎liable for the algorithm's output? Is the health system liable for deploying an inadequately validated tool?⏎Current medical malpractice frameworks do not clearly address AI-mediated decision-making. HHS⏎should work with state medical boards, professional liability insurers, and legal experts to develop⏎guidance on shared accountability models.⏎Indemnification Agreements: Many AI vendors require healthcare organizations to sign indemnification⏎agreements that shift liability risk entirely to the provider. This is particularly problematic for under-⏎resourced LTPAC providers who lack the financial capacity to assume such risk. HHS should establish⏎model contract language and standards for equitable risk-sharing between vendors and providers.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎6 of 23⏎⏎Privacy and Security: AI tools often require access to large volumes of patient data for training and⏎operation. Questions remain about:⏎● Whether AI-generated clinical insights constitute protected health information (PHI) under⏎HIPAA⏎● How consent should be obtained for AI processing of patient data⏎● What security standards apply to AI model storage, transmission, and updates?⏎● How to address privacy risks when AI tools use consumer-generated health data from non-⏎HIPAA-covered wearables and apps⏎HHS should issue guidance clarifying HIPAA applicability to AI systems and establish security standards⏎for AI tools used in clinical care.⏎Algorithmic Bias and Discrimination: AI tools that exhibit bias against protected classes (age, race,⏎disability status) may violate civil rights laws. However, detection and remediation of algorithmic bias⏎require technical expertise that many healthcare organizations lack. Research demonstrates that AI often⏎underperforms for older adults due to gaps in training data, leading to misdiagnoses and delayed care,⏎with bias linked to socioeconomic factors carrying high stakes when errors disproportionately affect⏎vulnerable patients. HHS Office for Civil Rights (OCR) should develop guidance on algorithmic equity⏎requirements and establish complaint mechanisms for patients who believe they have been harmed by⏎biased AI tools.⏎Data Ownership and Control: When AI tools generate clinical insights, predictions, or risk scores based⏎on patient data, questions arise about who owns these outputs and how they can be used. HHS should⏎establish clear data governance frameworks that respect patient autonomy while enabling appropriate⏎secondary use for research and quality improvement.⏎Workforce Competency: Effective use of AI in clinical care requires workforce education on AI⏎capabilities, limitations, and appropriate supervision. HHS should support development of training⏎programs and competency standards for clinicians, administrators, and health IT professionals who will⏎deploy and oversee AI tools.⏎Question 4: What are the most promising AI evaluation methods for clinical care, and how should⏎HHS support them?⏎Pre-Deployment Evaluation Methods⏎● Prospective Validation on Diverse Cohorts: AI tools should undergo prospective validation⏎testing on age-diverse, racially diverse, and clinically diverse populations before deployment. For⏎tools intended for geriatric populations, validation cohorts must include adequate representation⏎of older adults with multimorbidity and polypharmacy. External validation studies of mortality⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎7 of 23⏎⏎prediction models in older adults demonstrate that local refinement and calibration is required to⏎provide direct estimates of prognosis, with validation highlighting the critical role of assessing⏎performance of proprietary models in diverse healthcare settings.⏎● Bias Auditing and Fairness Testing: Independent bias audits should assess AI tool performance⏎across demographic subgroups (age, race, ethnicity, sex, disability status) and clinical subgroups⏎(disease severity, functional status, cognitive status). A systematic evaluation sampling 48 studies⏎across tabular, imaging, and hybrid data models using PRISMA selection strategy provides a⏎framework for bias assessment. Disparities in performance should trigger remediation before⏎approval.⏎● Explainability Assessment: AI tools should be evaluated for their ability to provide clinically⏎meaningful explanations of recommendations. Age-stratified analysis using methods like SHAP⏎(Shapley Additive Explanations) demonstrates that performance improves and models become⏎more interpretable with feature selection processes. Black-box models that cannot explain their⏎reasoning pose risks in clinical settings where clinicians must understand rationale to⏎appropriately supervise AI outputs.⏎● Workflow Integration Testing: Human-centered design evaluations should assess how AI tools⏎integrate into clinical workflows, including cognitive load, alert fatigue, and usability for diverse⏎users (including staff with varying levels of health IT literacy).⏎Post-Deployment Evaluation Methods⏎● Continuous Performance Monitoring: Real-world performance monitoring should track AI tool⏎accuracy, adoption rates, override rates, and clinical outcomes over time. Performance should be⏎stratified by patient characteristics to detect emerging bias or model drift. FDA guidance⏎emphasizes that AI is never validated once. It requires ongoing lifecycle evaluation with drift⏎monitoring, retraining controls, and change management protocols.⏎● Clinical Outcome Studies: Rigorous implementation science research should evaluate whether⏎AI tools actually improve patient outcomes, reduce costs, or enhance clinician efficiency in real-⏎world settings. Meta-analysis of 116 randomized controlled trials with 204,523 participants⏎demonstrated that EHR-based interventions reduced 30-day all-cause readmission risk by 17%⏎and 90-day readmission risk by 28%, with better performance in study populations composed⏎mostly of older participants.[47] A quasi-experimental study of hospital readmissions⏎demonstrated that AI model implementation decreased readmission rates from 11.4% pre-⏎intervention to 8.1% post-intervention (p<0.001). Studies should include LTPAC sites and⏎geriatric populations.⏎● Safety Surveillance: HHS should establish voluntary or mandatory adverse event reporting⏎mechanisms for AI tools, similar to FDA MedWatch for medical devices. This would enable⏎detection of safety signals and patterns of harm.⏎● Equity Monitoring: Ongoing monitoring should assess whether AI tool deployment widens or⏎narrows health disparities. Particular attention should be paid to access barriers for under-⏎resourced providers and vulnerable populations. The example of Optum algorithm recalibration—⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎8 of 23⏎⏎which increased high-risk Black patient enrollment from 17.7% to 46.5% after using direct health⏎indicators instead of costs—demonstrates that ongoing surveillance is necessary as reliance on⏎historical data could allow biases to re-emerge.⏎HHS Support Mechanisms⏎HHS should support these evaluation methods through:⏎● Contracts and Cooperative Agreements: Fund development of standardized evaluation⏎frameworks, toolkits, and benchmarking datasets that include geriatric populations.⏎● Grants: Support independent research on AI tool performance, safety, and equity through NIH,⏎AHRQ, and PCORI.⏎● Regulatory-Science Testbeds: Establish collaboration among FDA, ONC, CMS, and VA to⏎create shared infrastructure for AI validation.⏎● Prize Competitions: Incentivize development of novel evaluation methods, bias detection tools,⏎and explainability techniques through HHS prize competitions.⏎The most impactful approach would be a combination of contracts (for infrastructure development),⏎grants (for independent research), and cooperative agreements (for multi-stakeholder collaboration).⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote innovative and effective⏎AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should adopt a supportive posture toward industry-driven quality assurance mechanisms while⏎establishing baseline standards to protect patients:⏎Accreditation and Certification⏎● Support development of voluntary AI tool accreditation programs through organizations like The⏎Joint Commission, NCQA, or URAC that assess AI tools for safety, effectiveness, and equity.⏎● Expand the ONC Health IT Certification Program (45 CFR Part 170) to include certification⏎criteria for AI-enabled clinical decision support tools, with specific requirements for validation,⏎transparency, and bias testing. The HTI-1 Final Rule's FAVES framework (Fair, Appropriate,⏎Valid, Effective, Safe) provides a foundation for organizational evaluation of Predictive DSIs.⏎● Recognize and incentivize (through payment policy) use of certified or accredited AI tools in⏎value-based payment arrangements.⏎Industry-Driven Testing and Standards⏎● Partner with standards development organizations (HL7, IEEE, ASTM) to create technical⏎standards for AI tool interoperability, data requirements, and performance reporting.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎9 of 23⏎⏎● Support industry consortia (such as the Coalition for Health AI, Partnership on AI) that develop⏎best practice frameworks, model cards, and datasheets for AI transparency.⏎● Fund development of open-source benchmarking datasets and evaluation toolkits that include⏎geriatric and LTPAC populations.⏎Professional Credentialing⏎● Support professional societies in developing AI competency standards and credentialing⏎programs for clinicians and health IT professionals.⏎● Incentivize AI literacy training through continuing education requirements and Medicare payment⏎bonuses for providers who complete AI training programs.⏎Market Transparency⏎● Establish a public registry of AI tools used in clinical care, including information on their⏎intended use, validation evidence, and performance metrics. This would function similarly to⏎FDA's medical device databases but for non-device AI tools.⏎● Require AI vendors to publish model cards and performance reports that enable informed⏎purchasing decisions by healthcare organizations.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded expectations, and where⏎have they fallen short? What novel AI tools would have the greatest potential?⏎AI Tools Meeting or Exceeding Expectations⏎While robust evidence specific to LTPAC settings remains limited, emerging applications show promise:⏎● Fall Risk Prediction: AI tools analyzing gait patterns, functional status, medication profiles, and⏎environmental factors show potential for identifying high-risk individuals and triggering⏎preventive interventions. Research demonstrates that integrating fall prediction models into real-⏎time human-centered AI decision support systems enables proactive identification of at-risk⏎patients and implementation of preventive measures through valid screening scales and evidence-⏎based interventions.⏎● Sepsis and Acute Illness Detection: Early warning systems analyzing vital signs and laboratory⏎trends may enable earlier identification of acute changes in condition, particularly valuable in⏎nursing home settings where physician presence is intermittent. AI-powered early warning⏎systems in prospective clinical validation studies significantly reduced in-hospital and 30-day⏎mortality rates and shortened overall hospital stays, demonstrating positive impact on patient⏎outcomes in real-world settings.⏎● Documentation Automation: Ambient AI documentation tools that transcribe and structure⏎clinical encounters reduce documentation burden and allow clinicians to focus on patient⏎interaction.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎10 of 23⏎⏎● Medication Management: AI-powered polypharmacy optimization tools that flag drug-drug⏎interactions, inappropriate medications for older adults (Beers Criteria), and opportunities for⏎deprescribing show promise for improving medication safety.⏎● Remote Patient Monitoring: Studies in skilled nursing facilities demonstrate that RPM enables⏎real-time alerts and immediate response to abnormal readings, with significant reductions in⏎Hemoglobin A1c in diabetic patients, decreased need for in-person appointments and emergency⏎room visits. Senior housing programs using RPM have documented reductions in skilled nursing⏎facility placements through 24/7 coverage and real-time situational awareness for care teams.⏎● Quality Management: AI improves diagnostic accuracy, personalizes treatments, reduces⏎recovery times and mortality rates, with automation of documentation and audits reducing⏎administrative burden. Real-time dashboards and predictive analytics enable early risk⏎identification.⏎AI Tools Falling Short of Expectations⏎● Biased Algorithms: Multiple studies have documented racial, age, and socioeconomic bias in AI⏎tools for risk prediction, resource allocation, and clinical decision support. A systematic review of⏎AI-enhanced interventions for older people in long-term care settings found mixed evidence for⏎effectiveness, with high risk of bias in included studies reducing confidence in results. Tools⏎validated on non-representative populations perform poorly when deployed in diverse real-world⏎settings. An example of an AI sepsis detection tool generated thousands of false positive alerts,⏎with implications for standard of care in skilled nursing and long-term care settings.⏎● Alert Fatigue: Some AI-powered clinical decision support systems generate excessive alerts,⏎leading to override behavior and diminished effectiveness.⏎● Lack of Integration: AI tools that function as standalone systems rather than integrating with⏎existing EHR workflows create additional burden rather than reducing it.⏎● Cost Without Demonstrated Value: High-cost AI tools that lack rigorous evidence of improved⏎outcomes or cost savings fail to deliver promised return on investment.⏎Novel AI Tools with Greatest Potential for LTPAC Settings⏎● Multimorbidity Management: AI tools that synthesize complex patient data (functional status,⏎cognitive status, social determinants, multiple chronic conditions) to generate holistic care plans⏎tailored to individual goals and preferences.⏎● Caregiver Support Tools: AI-powered virtual assistants that guide family caregivers in⏎medication administration, symptom monitoring, and care coordination for homebound older⏎adults.⏎● Delirium Detection: Continuous monitoring systems using wearables or ambient sensors to⏎detect early signs of delirium, enabling rapid intervention to prevent adverse outcomes.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎11 of 23⏎⏎● Functional Decline Prediction: AI tools analyzing longitudinal functional status data to predict⏎risk of hospitalization, nursing home placement, or mortality, enabling proactive care plan⏎adjustments.⏎● Social Determinants Screening and Intervention: AI tools that screen for social needs (food⏎insecurity, transportation barriers, housing instability) and connect patients with community⏎resources.⏎● Quality Measure Automation: AI tools that extract quality measure data from clinical⏎documentation and automate reporting to CMS, reducing administrative burden.⏎● AI-Driven Virtual Rehabilitation: AI algorithms prescribing individualized rehabilitation⏎exercises, providing visualizations and feedback to patients, and generating progress reports to⏎clinicians. Pilot studies demonstrate that AI motion tracking during home rehabilitation for spinal⏎cord injury patients documented significant gains in upper-limb strength versus standard exercise⏎videos. Current research suggests that integrating AI with home-based virtual rehabilitation leads⏎to improved outcomes.⏎Question 7: Which roles or governing bodies have the most influence on AI adoption in clinical⏎care? What are the primary administrative hurdles?⏎Influential Decision Makers⏎In LTPAC settings, the following roles and bodies significantly influence AI adoption:⏎● Health System Leadership: Chief Information Officers (CIOs), Chief Medical Information⏎Officers (CMIOs), and Chief Nursing Information Officers (CNIOs) make strategic technology⏎investment decisions.⏎● Facility Administrators: Nursing home administrators, home health agency directors, and⏎hospice executives determine operational technology priorities within resource constraints.⏎● Clinical Champions: Medical directors, directors of nursing, and other clinician leaders who⏎advocate for specific AI tools and support staff adoption.⏎● Corporate Ownership: For provider organizations owned by larger health systems or private⏎equity firms, corporate-level IT and innovation teams often dictate technology choices.⏎● Payers: Medicare Advantage plans, Medicaid managed care organizations, and commercial⏎payers influence adoption through coverage decisions and value-based contract requirements.⏎● Regulatory Bodies: State health departments, CMS, and accrediting organizations (Joint⏎Commission, ACHC) shape adoption through regulatory requirements and survey processes.⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles⏎● Resource Constraints: Limited capital budgets, IT staff, and technical expertise in LTPAC⏎organizations constrain ability to evaluate, purchase, implement, and maintain AI tools,⏎challenges exacerbated by 15 years of exclusion from federal health IT investment programs.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎12 of 23⏎⏎● Competing Priorities: LTPAC providers face numerous regulatory requirements, quality⏎reporting mandates, and operational challenges that compete with AI adoption for attention and⏎resources. Workforce shortages remain the sector's biggest ongoing challenge, with staffing⏎taking priority over technology investments.⏎● Vendor Fragmentation: Lack of standardization across AI vendors requires providers to navigate⏎multiple contracts, integration requirements, and training programs.⏎● Interoperability Barriers: Difficulty obtaining necessary patient data from hospitals, clinics, and⏎other providers limits AI tools' effectiveness in LTPAC settings.⏎● Staff Resistance: Concerns about job displacement, skepticism about AI accuracy, and⏎technology anxiety among frontline staff create adoption barriers.⏎● Unclear Return on Investment: Lack of robust evidence demonstrating cost savings or outcome⏎improvements makes it difficult to justify AI investments, particularly in fee-for-service payment⏎environments.⏎● Procurement Processes: Complex procurement requirements, particularly for government-⏎funded providers, slow adoption cycles.⏎● Training and Change Management: Insufficient time and resources for staff training, workflow⏎redesign, and change management undermine successful implementation.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate AI development for clinical care?⏎Critical Data Types Requiring Enhanced Interoperability⏎● Functional Status: Activities of Daily Living (ADLs), Instrumental Activities of Daily Living⏎(IADLs), mobility assessments, and functional trajectories over time.⏎● Cognitive and Mental Health: Cognitive assessments (Mini-Mental State Exam, Montreal⏎Cognitive Assessment), delirium screening (Confusion Assessment Method), depression⏎screening (PHQ-9), and behavioral health diagnoses.⏎● Social Determinants of Health: Living situation, caregiver support, food security, transportation⏎access, housing stability, social isolation, and community resources.⏎● Care Goals and Preferences: Advance directives, goals of care conversations, patient values and⏎preferences, and care plan goals.⏎● Medications: Complete medication lists including over-the-counter medications, supplements,⏎and medication administration records with adherence data.⏎● Care Transitions: Transition of care summaries, discharge instructions, pending tests and follow-⏎up needs, and communication between sending and receiving providers.⏎● Quality and Outcome Measures: Patient-reported outcome measures (PROMs), clinician-⏎reported outcomes, adverse events, and quality metric data.⏎● Remote Monitoring Data: Wearable device data, home monitoring data, continuous vital sign⏎monitoring, and consumer-generated health data.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎13 of 23⏎⏎Data Standards Requiring Broader Adoption⏎● PACIO Project FHIR Implementation Guides: HL7 FHIR profiles for functional status (PACIO⏎Functional Status), cognitive status (PACIO Cognitive Status), advance directives (PACIO⏎Advance Directives), care transitions, and other geriatric-specific data elements. PACIO⏎frameworks have been validated for clinical conditions prevalent in older adults including⏎diabetes management, falls and fall risk, delirium/depression/dementia, heart failure,⏎osteoporosis, pain management, Parkinson's disease, pressure ulcers, stroke management, and⏎urinary incontinence.⏎● USCDI Expansion: Incorporation of PACIO-developed data elements into the United States⏎Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) to ensure widespread adoption.⏎● FHIR Clinical Reasoning Module: HL7 FHIR Clinical Reasoning module for representing⏎clinical decision support logic in a transparent, interoperable format.⏎● FHIR Subscription and Notification: Real-time event notification standards to enable timely⏎care coordination and early warning system alerts.⏎● SMART on FHIR: Application programming interface (API) standards that enable third-party AI⏎applications to access EHR data with patient consent.⏎Benchmarking Tools and Infrastructure⏎● Standardized Performance Metrics: Industry-wide agreement on how to measure AI tool⏎performance, including sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, negative predictive⏎value, and calibration across demographic subgroups.⏎● Open Benchmarking Datasets: Publicly available, de-identified datasets representative of diverse⏎populations (including geriatric and LTPAC patients) for AI developers to test and benchmark⏎their tools.⏎● Federated Learning Infrastructure: Privacy-preserving federated learning networks that enable⏎AI training on distributed datasets without centralizing sensitive patient data.⏎● QHIN Connectivity: Universal connectivity to Qualified Health Information Networks (QHINs)⏎under TEFCA to enable seamless data exchange across all care settings.⏎Market Opportunities Created by Enhanced Interoperability⏎Enhanced interoperability would create market opportunities for:⏎● AI tools focused on care transitions and post-acute care optimization⏎● Predictive analytics for high-risk, complex patients across care settings⏎● Population health management platforms serving integrated delivery networks⏎● Real-time clinical decision support tools that leverage longitudinal patient data⏎● Patient-facing applications that aggregate data from multiple providers and consumer devices⏎● Quality measurement and reporting automation tools⏎● Research platforms supporting pragmatic clinical trials and real-world evidence generation⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎14 of 23⏎⏎Question 9: What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI adoption?⏎What concerns do they have?⏎Challenges Patients and Caregivers Want AI to Address⏎● Care Coordination: Patients and caregivers struggle with fragmented care across multiple⏎providers, specialists, and care settings. They desire AI tools that facilitate seamless information⏎sharing, reduce redundant testing, and ensure all providers have access to complete patient⏎information.⏎● Medication Management: Managing complex medication regimens is challenging for older⏎adults and caregivers. AI tools that identify drug interactions, simplify medication schedules, and⏎provide reminders would be valuable.⏎● Early Problem Detection: Patients and caregivers want systems that detect early signs of health⏎decline (infections, falls, cognitive changes) before they become emergencies, enabling timely⏎intervention.⏎● Caregiver Support and Guidance: Family caregivers often lack medical training but are⏎responsible for complex care tasks. AI-powered guidance tools, educational resources, and⏎decision support would help caregivers provide better care and reduce anxiety.⏎● Reducing Hospital Readmissions: Patients and caregivers fear repeated hospitalizations and⏎desire tools that help manage conditions effectively at home or in LTPAC settings.⏎● Transparent Communication: Patients want clear, understandable explanations of their⏎conditions, treatment options, and prognoses. AI tools that translate medical jargon into plain⏎language and provide personalized education materials would be valuable.⏎● Access to Specialists: In rural and under-resourced communities, access to specialists is limited.⏎AI tools that provide specialized expertise or triage patients for teleconsultation could improve⏎access.⏎● Quality of Life: Patients prioritize maintaining independence, function, and quality of life. AI⏎tools that support aging in place, prevent functional decline, and align care with patient goals and⏎preferences are desired.⏎Concerns Patients and Caregivers Have About AI⏎● Privacy and Data Security: Patients worry about who has access to their health data, how it will⏎be used, and whether it will be protected from breaches or misuse. Concerns are heightened⏎regarding data from consumer devices and wearables that may not be HIPAA-protected.⏎● Algorithmic Bias and Discrimination: Patients fear that AI tools may perpetuate or worsen⏎health disparities, particularly for underrepresented or marginalized populations. Research⏎documents that without intentional design, AI risks reinforcing inequities rather than reducing⏎them, with models failing to account for complex social needs of aging populations. Concerns⏎about age-based bias are particularly relevant for older adults.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎15 of 23⏎⏎● Loss of Human Connection: Patients value relationships with their clinicians and worry that AI⏎will replace human interaction, empathy, and personalized care. A qualitative study of older⏎adults' perspectives on AI-driven healthcare found mixed acceptance levels, with concerns about⏎technology replacing human connection and desire for maintaining patient-provider relationships.⏎Concerns about "dehumanizing" healthcare are common.⏎● Accuracy and Reliability: Patients and caregivers question whether AI tools are accurate and⏎reliable, particularly when recommendations conflict with clinician judgment or seem⏎counterintuitive.⏎● Lack of Transparency: Patients want to understand how AI tools reach conclusions and⏎recommendations. Black-box algorithms that cannot explain their reasoning are concerning,⏎particularly when they influence important treatment decisions.⏎● Reduced Clinician Autonomy: Patients worry that clinicians will blindly follow AI⏎recommendations without exercising clinical judgment, or conversely, that clinicians will ignore⏎valid AI alert fatigue.⏎● Access and Equity: Patients in under-resourced settings worry that AI tools will only be available⏎to wealthy health systems, widening rather than narrowing health disparities.⏎● Consent and Control: Patients want meaningful control over whether AI tools are used in their⏎care and how their data is used to train AI systems. Concerns about informed consent processes⏎that are difficult to understand are common.⏎● Job Displacement: Patients and communities worry about whether AI will displace healthcare⏎workers, particularly in communities where healthcare facilities are major employers.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate AI⏎adoption in clinical care?⏎Priority Research Areas for LTPAC and Geriatric Populations⏎● Inclusion of Aging Populations in AI Research: Establish requirements that NIH, AHRQ, and⏎other HHS-funded research include adequate representation of older adults, particularly those⏎with multimorbidity, polypharmacy, cognitive impairment, and functional limitations.⏎● Multimorbidity and Polypharmacy Optimization: Develop AI tools that can synthesize evidence⏎across multiple chronic conditions and optimize medication regimens for older adults with⏎complex needs.⏎● Functional Status and Trajectories: Research AI applications for predicting functional decline,⏎targeting interventions to prevent disability, and personalizing rehabilitation approaches.⏎● Cognitive Assessment and Dementia Care: Develop AI tools for early detection of cognitive⏎impairment, monitoring dementia progression, and supporting caregivers of persons with⏎dementia.⏎● Fall Prevention: Research AI applications for fall risk assessment, environmental modification⏎recommendations, and real-time fall detection and response.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎16 of 23⏎⏎● Delirium Prevention and Management: Develop AI tools for early delirium detection,⏎identification of modifiable risk factors, and guidance on non-pharmacological interventions.⏎● Social Determinants of Health: Research AI tools that screen for social needs, predict social risk,⏎and connect patients with appropriate community resources.⏎● Palliative and End-of-Life Care: Develop AI tools that facilitate goals-of-care conversations,⏎predict prognosis, and ensure care aligns with patient values and preferences.⏎● Bias Detection and Mitigation: Research methods for detecting and mitigating age-based, racial,⏎and socioeconomic bias in AI algorithms. Develop fairness metrics and bias audit tools specific to⏎vulnerable populations. Studies demonstrate the need for bias mitigation strategies throughout the⏎AI lifecycle (data collection, model development, deployment, post-deployment monitoring).⏎● Explainable AI: Research techniques for making AI recommendations interpretable and⏎actionable for clinicians and patients, particularly for complex clinical decisions.⏎● Implementation Science: Study effective strategies for implementing AI tools in LTPAC⏎settings, including workflow integration, training approaches, and change management methods.⏎● Health Equity: Research on how AI tools can reduce rather than widen health disparities,⏎including studies of AI deployment in under-resourced and rural settings.⏎● Research: Mimic the effort and research prioritization historically offered to acute and primary⏎care but customize to the unique patient population and care needs, and workflow of facility⏎based long-term and post-acute care providers.⏎Published Findings on Impact of AI Tools⏎A systematic review and meta-analysis specifically examining AI-enhanced interventions for older people⏎in long-term care settings found mixed evidence for effectiveness across different health outcomes, with⏎AI-enhanced interventions being somewhat acceptable to users but high risk of bias in included studies⏎reducing confidence in results. The review concluded that more high-quality trials are required before⏎widespread implementation, especially in low- and middle-income countries. Additional literature⏎documents:⏎● Racial and age bias in clinical risk prediction algorithms⏎● Performance degradation of AI models when applied to populations is different from training data⏎● Alert fatigue and override behavior with clinical decision support systems⏎● Challenges with AI tool integration into clinical workflows⏎● Variable evidence quality for commercially available AI tools⏎Costs, Benefits, and Transfers of AI in Clinical Care⏎The literature suggests:⏎● Potential Benefits: Improved diagnostic accuracy, earlier disease detection, reduced medical⏎errors, personalized treatment recommendations, workflow efficiency, reduced clinician burden,⏎and cost savings through prevention of adverse events.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎17 of 23⏎⏎● Potential Costs: High acquisition and implementation costs, ongoing maintenance expenses,⏎training costs, infrastructure requirements, and potential for increased health disparities if access⏎is unequal.⏎● Transfers: Shifts in labor from routine tasks to oversight and complex decision-making,⏎redistribution of resources from late-stage treatment to prevention, and potential concentration of⏎benefits in well-resourced health systems.⏎Critical research gaps remain regarding long-term impact, cost-effectiveness, and equity implications of⏎AI adoption in clinical care, particularly in LTPAC settings.⏎Conclusion⏎The LTPAC Health Information Technology Collaborative appreciates the opportunity to provide input⏎on HHS's approach to accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical care. We⏎emphasize the critical importance of ensuring that AI innovation benefits all Americans, including the⏎rapidly growing population of older adults served by LTPAC providers.⏎The convergence of demographic trends (10,000 Americans turning 65 daily), workforce crisis (persistent⏎staffing shortages across the LTPAC continuum), and technology exclusion (15 years without federal⏎health IT investment) creates an urgent imperative for strategic federal action. AI technologies offer⏎transformative potential to address workforce constraints, improve care quality, and ensure sustainable⏎care delivery for America's aging population but only if policy frameworks support rather than hinder⏎LTPAC adoption.⏎Key recommendations from this response include:⏎1. Mandate age-stratified validation and bias testing for AI tools intended for use in Medicare⏎populations⏎2. Expand interoperability standards to include geriatric-specific data elements (functional status,⏎cognitive status, social determinants)⏎3. Align payment policies to incentivize high-value AI adoption in LTPAC settings⏎4. Provide infrastructure support and technical assistance to enable AI readiness in under-resourced⏎LTPAC providers⏎5. Prioritize research funding for AI applications addressing multimorbidity, functional decline, and⏎other priorities for aging populations⏎6. Establish clear regulatory frameworks addressing liability, privacy, and algorithmic transparency⏎for non-medical device AI tools⏎7. Create validation testbeds and evaluation frameworks that include LTPAC settings and geriatric⏎populations⏎8. Support the development of AI tools that augment rather than replace healthcare workers,⏎addressing workforce shortages while maintaining the human connection patients’ value⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎18 of 23⏎⏎We urge HHS to take a comprehensive, equity-focused approach that ensures AI technologies reduce⏎rather than widen health disparities, protect vulnerable populations from algorithmic bias, support the⏎complex care needs of older adults across all care settings, and enable LTPAC providers to leverage AI⏎solutions that address critical workforce constraints.⏎The Administration's commitment to accelerating AI adoption in healthcare creates a historic opportunity⏎to rectify longstanding inequities in technology investment and support. By including LTPAC providers⏎in AI policy, payment reform, and research priorities, HHS can enable sustainable, high-quality care for⏎millions of older Americans while demonstrating meaningful return on federal investment through⏎improved outcomes and reduced costs.⏎The LTPAC Health IT Collaborative is ready to collaborate with HHS ASTP/ONC to advance these⏎priorities. We welcome the opportunity for ongoing dialogue and partnership to ensure that AI policy and⏎programs are inclusive of the needs of LTPAC providers and the millions of older Americans they serve.⏎For further information or dialogue, please contact Michelle Dougherty, LTPAC Health IT Collaborative⏎Convener, at leaders@ltpachit.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎The LTPAC Health IT Collaborative⏎For a list of LTPAC Health IT Collaborative members, please visit www.LTPACHIT.org.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎19 of 23⏎⏎References:⏎The following references support the submitted comments and recommendations related to AI bias in⏎geriatric populations, LTPAC interoperability challenges, AI effectiveness in post-acute care, and remote⏎patient monitoring in LTPAC.⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. Federal Register. 2025 Dec 23;90(244):60108-⏎60110.⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. HHS Artificial Intelligence Strategy. 2025 Dec 4.⏎Available from: https://www.hhs.gov/press-room/hhs-unveils-ai-strategy-to-transform-agency-⏎operations.html⏎I Advance Senior Care. Key Senior Living Trends to Watch in 2026. 2026 Jan 26. Available from:⏎https://www.iadvanceseniorcare.com/key-senior-living-trends-to-watch-in-2026/⏎Healthcare IT Today. Healthcare Workforce – 2026 Health IT Predictions. 2025 Dec 30. Available from:⏎https://www.healthcareittoday.com/2025/12/31/healthcare-workforce-2026-health-it-predictions/⏎LeadingAge. Action Needed Now to Shore Up Aging Services Workforce: Findings from LeadingAge⏎Snap Poll. 2022 Jul 11. Available from: https://leadingage.org/action-needed-now-to-shore-up-aging-⏎services-workforce-findings-from-leadingage-snap-poll/⏎The 2026 LTC Labor Crisis. Instagram. 2026 Feb 10. Available from:⏎https://www.instagram.com/p/DUnly8xEQVV/⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative. Response to NIH RFI on Artificial Intelligence Strategy. 2025 Jul 15.⏎American Hospital Association. Health Care Workforce: A System Under Pressure, Poised for⏎Reinvention. 2025 Dec 8. Available from: https://www.aha.org/aha-center-health-innovation-market-⏎scan/2025-12-09-health-care-workforce-system-under-pressure-poised-reinvention⏎Hello SyncX. 10 Healthcare Workforce Trends to Watch in 2026. 2026 Jan 29. Available from:⏎https://hellosyncx.com/blog/healthcare-workforce-trends/⏎Hinshaw & Culbertson LLP. Achieving "Meaningful Use" for Electronic Health Records and Financial⏎Incentives Available Under the HITECH Act. 2026 Feb 12. Available from:⏎https://www.hinshawlaw.com/en/insights/healthcare-alert/achieving-meaningful-use-for-electronic-⏎health-records-and-financial-incentives⏎Long-Term Care and Health Information Technology. PMC. 2013 Sep 30. Available from:⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3797552/⏎Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Medicare and Medicaid Promoting Interoperability Program⏎Basics. 2024 Oct 8. Available from: https://www.cms.gov/medicare/regulations-guidance/promoting-⏎interoperability-programs/medicare-medicaid-basics⏎Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. 2022 Medicare Promoting Interoperability Program⏎Requirements. 2024 Oct 8. Available from: https://www.cms.gov/2022-medicare-promoting-⏎interoperability-program-requirements⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎20 of 23⏎⏎Quality Reporting Center. CY 2025 Medicare Promoting Interoperability Program Requirements. 2025⏎Jan. Available from: https://www.qualityreportingcenter.com/globalassets/2025/01/iqr/pi_infographic_cy-⏎2025-requirements_jan2025_vfinal_508.pdf⏎LinkedIn. Lack of Interoperability in LTPAC: A Challenge to Healthcare. 2025 Aug 27. Available from:⏎https://www.linkedin.com/posts/elieloliveira_healthequity-interoperability-ltpac-activity-⏎7366863022008475650-hvkO⏎The Medical Care Blog. Interoperability Can Help Long-Term Care Bridge the Information Gap. 2025⏎Sep 25. Available from: https://www.themedicalcareblog.com/interoperability-long-term-care/⏎JAMA Health Forum. Postacute Care Use and Outcomes Among Traditional Medicare and Medicare⏎Advantage Beneficiaries. 2023 Aug 3. Available from: https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama-health-⏎forum/fullarticle/2808465⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. HTI-1 Final Rule. 2025 Nov 7.⏎Available from: https://healthit.gov/regulations/hti-rules/hti-1-final-rule/⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative. Response to FDA RFI on FHIR for Real-World Data. 2025 Jun 23.⏎Akin Gump. ONC Steps into AI Regulation, Finalizing Extensive Requirements for Predictive Decision⏎Support. 2024 Jan 10.⏎Mittelstadt BD, Allo C, Taddeo M, et al. The AI cycle of health inequity and digital ageism: mitigating⏎biases throughout the machine learning lifecycle. Journal of Law and the Biosciences.⏎2023;10(2):lsad031.⏎Lassen AJ, Bossen C, Kaltoft SØ, et al. AI ageism: a critical roadmap for studying age discrimination and⏎bias. Age and Ageing. 2022;51(10). Available from: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9527733/⏎Seifert A, Cotten SR, Xie B. New Horizons in artificial intelligence in the healthcare of older people. Age⏎and Ageing. 2023;12. Available from: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10733173/⏎VirtuSense Technologies. How AI is Revolutionizing Post-Acute Care. 2024 Sep 30. Available from:⏎https://www.virtusense.ai/blog/ai-helps-to-improve-telehealth-patient-care-0⏎MyVitalz. Telehealth and Remote Patient Monitoring (RPM). 2023. Available from:⏎https://myvitalz.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/Telehealth-and-Remote-Patient-Monitoring.pdf⏎QAPI Plus. How AI is Changing Quality Management in Post-Acute Care. 2025 Oct 2. Available from:⏎https://home.qapiplus.com/blog/how-ai-is-changing-quality-management-in-post-acute-care⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative. Response to NLM RFI on Research Programs. 2025 Jul 14.⏎HL7 PACIO Project. PACIO Advance Healthcare Directive Interoperability Implementation Guide. 2025⏎Aug 3. Available from: https://build.fhir.org/ig/HL7/pacio-adi/⏎PACIO Project. PACIO Project Homepage. 2025 Jul 13. Available from: https://pacioproject.org⏎Zhang H, Liu Y, Wang S, et al. Smart aging: integrating AI into elderly healthcare. BMC Geriatrics.⏎2025;25. Available from: https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12706947/⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎21 of 23⏎⏎Coalition for Health AI (CHAI) and The SCAN Foundation. Advancing Fair and Effective AI for Older⏎Adults. 2025 May 15. Available from: https://www.chai.org/blog/advancing-fair-and-effective-ai-for-⏎older-adults-our-partnership-with-the⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative. Response to NLM RFI on Research Programs. 2025 Jul 14. Page 1.⏎American Health Law Association. Artificial Intelligence and the Standard of Care in the Post-Acute and⏎Long-Term Care Setting. 2024 Feb 29.⏎Post-Acute and Long-Term Care Medical Association (PALTCmed). Clinical Practice Guidelines.⏎Available from: www.paltmed.org⏎Collins GS, Dhiman P, Navarro CLA, et al. TRIPOD+AI statement: updated guidance for reporting⏎clinical prediction models that use regression or machine learning methods. BMJ. 2024;385:e078378.⏎External validation of a proprietary risk model for 1-year mortality in older adults. Journal of the⏎American Medical Informatics Association. 2025;32(7):1110-1119.⏎EQUATOR Network. TRIPOD+AI Statement - Reporting Guideline. 2024 Apr 17. Available from:⏎https://www.equator-network.org/reporting-guidelines/tripod-statement/⏎U.S. Food and Drug Administration. Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Device Software Functions:⏎Lifecycle Management and Marketing Submission Recommendations. Final Guidance. 2025 Jan 6.⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. HTI-1 Final Rule. 2025 Nov 7.⏎AHIMA. ONC Decision Support Interventions Certification Criteria. 2024 Jan 8.⏎Akin Gump. ONC Steps into AI Regulation, Finalizing Extensive Requirements for Predictive Decision⏎Support. 2024 Jan 10.⏎McKnight's Long-Term Care News. Using remote patient monitoring to improve resident health, staff⏎satisfaction. 2021 Jul 21.⏎Wang Y, Zhang L, Chen H, et al. AI-Powered early warning systems for clinical deterioration: A⏎systematic review and meta-analysis. BMC Medical Informatics and Decision Making. 2025;25(1).⏎U.S. Food and Drug Administration. Considerations for the Use of Artificial Intelligence to Support⏎Regulatory Decision-Making for Drug and Biological Products. Draft Guidance. 2025 May 31.⏎JD Supra. The Regulatory Roadmap: Five Critical Questions for Evaluating AI in Healthcare. 2026 Feb⏎10.⏎Dandelion Health. Equity in Medical AI: Algorithm Validation to Improve Health for Underserved⏎Populations. 2025 Mar 16.⏎Multiple authors. Electronic Health Record Interventions to Reduce Risk of Hospital Readmission:⏎Systematic Review and Meta-analysis. JAMA Network Open. 2025 Jun 30.⏎UC San Diego Health. Study Finds Virtual Clinics Lower Hospital Readmissions. 2025 Sep 23.⏎Zhang H, Liu Y, Wang S, et al. Smart aging: integrating AI into elderly healthcare. BMC Geriatrics.⏎2025;25.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎22 of 23⏎⏎Multiple authors. External validation of a proprietary risk model for 1-year mortality in older adults.⏎Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association. 2025;32(7):1110-1119.⏎Multiple authors. Bias recognition and mitigation strategies in artificial intelligence for healthcare. Nature⏎Digital Medicine. 2025 Mar 10.⏎Multiple authors. Age-Stratified Analysis of COVID-19 Outcome Using Machine Learning. PMC. 2022⏎Oct 13.⏎Van Velsen L, Ludden G, Grünloh C. AI-Assisted Decision-Making in Long-Term Care: Facilitating⏎Aging in Place. JMIR Nursing. 2024;7(1):e55962.⏎FDA AI Guidance 2025: What Life Sciences Must Do Now. USDM. 2025 Dec 17.⏎Health Universe. Understanding the ONC's HTI-1 Rule for Decision Support Interventions. 2024 Mar 27.⏎blueBriX. The 2026 AI Reset: A New Era for Healthcare Policy. 2026 Jan 28.⏎QAPI Plus. How AI is Changing Quality Management in Post-Acute Care. 2025 Oct 2.⏎Wang S, Bolling K, Mao W, et al. Artificial intelligence for older people receiving long-term care: a⏎systematic review of acceptability and effectiveness studies. Lancet Healthy Longevity. 2022;3(4):e286-⏎e297.⏎MedCity News. AI Decides Who Gets Care: Algorithmic Bias in Post-Acute Care Decisions. 2025 Dec⏎29.⏎Multiple authors. Streamlining Patient Fall Prevention and Management Through Human-Centered AI⏎Decision Support Systems. PMC. 2025 Sep 15.⏎SNF Metrics. AI for Elderly: The Benefits in Long Term Care. 2025 May 26.⏎Cascala Health. Advancing Post-Acute Care with AI. 2025 Dec 7.⏎Multiple authors. Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: A Narrative Review of Recent Clinical Trials and⏎Real-World Applications. PMC. 2025 Dec 29.⏎Multiple authors. Artificial intelligence-driven virtual rehabilitation for people living in the community: A⏎scoping review. Nature Digital Medicine. 2024 Feb 2.⏎Multiple authors. Exploring Older Adults' Perspectives and Acceptance of AI-Driven Healthcare. JMIR⏎Aging. 2025 Feb 11.⏎LTPAC Health IT Collaborative Comments⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Page⏎23 of 23♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0043♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bright Star Solutions LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Bright Star Solutions LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Secretary⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Request for Information: Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI – Specific Question Response⏎February 23, 2026⏎Provided to: Provided by:⏎Department of Health and Human Services Bright Star Solutions LLC⏎Office of the Secretary Andrew Burk⏎Business Development Lead⏎3309 Runnymede PL NW⏎Washington, DC 20015⏎aburk@bright-star.com⏎www.bright-star.com⏎SBA 8(a) Certified⏎8(a) STARS III Contract Number:⏎47QTCB22D0055⏎UEI: GMB8FVTPZLD3⏎Top Secret Facilities Clearance⏎bright-star.com DISCLOSURE STATEMENT: THE DATA CONTAINED IN THIS PROPOSAL SHALL NOT BE DISCLOSED OUTSIDE THE FEDERAL⏎GOVERNMENT, NOR SHALL IT BE DUPLICATED, USED, OR DISCLOSED, IN WHOLE OR IN PART, FOR ANY PURPOSE OTHER⏎THAN TO EVALUATE THE CAPABILITIES OF BRIGHT STAR SOLUTIONS LLC. IF A CONTRACT IS AWARDED TO BRIGHT⏎STAR SOLUTIONS LLC. AS A RESULT OF OR IN CONNECTION WITH THE SUBMISSION OF THIS PROPOSAL, THE⏎GOVERNMENT SHALL HAVE THE RIGHT TO DUPLICATE, USE, OR DISCLOSE THESE DATA TO THE EXTENT PROVIDED FOR⏎UNDER THE FREEDOM OF INFORMATION ACT. THIS DISCLOSURE STATEMENT APPLIES TO ALL PAGES⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Table of Contents⏎1 Organizational Perspective and Why Our Experience Transfers to Clinical Care..................1⏎2 Specific Question #1................................................................................................................2⏎3 Specific Question #2................................................................................................................3⏎4 Specific Question #3................................................................................................................5⏎5 Specific Question #4................................................................................................................6⏎6 Specific Question #5................................................................................................................7⏎7 Specific Question #6................................................................................................................8⏎8 Specific Question #7................................................................................................................9⏎9 Specific Question #8..............................................................................................................10⏎10 Specific Question #9..............................................................................................................11⏎11 Specific Question #10............................................................................................................12⏎12 Closing...................................................................................................................................13⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎1 Organizational Perspective and Why Our Experience Transfers to Clinical Care⏎Bright Star Solutions LLC is a Washington, DC–based SBA 8(a) small business that has delivered secure,⏎mission-critical AI, analytics, automation, and digital modernization solutions across high-stakes federal⏎environments since 2016. We support organizations where public trust is the product, where mistakes create⏎real-world harm, and where compliance is not an afterthought. Those are the same core conditions driving AI⏎adoption barriers in clinical care.⏎Our work has required turning AI from a “lab concept” into an operational capability under real constraints:⏎identity and access management, data protection, auditability, change management, cross-organization⏎governance, and measurable outcomes. This is why we believe our federal AI delivery model maps directly to⏎healthcare AI, even though we have not yet delivered production systems within HHS.⏎A key difference between public sector AI and commercial AI is that success is not just accuracy. Success is⏎institutional trust at scale. In our experience, AI adoption succeeds when the government can answer five⏎questions confidently:⏎✓ Who approved this and under what policy?⏎✓ What data did it touch and why?⏎✓ What decisions did it influence and how?⏎✓ How do we monitor performance, bias, drift, and safety over time?⏎✓ What happens when it breaks or causes harm?⏎Those are the same questions HHS needs to be able to answer for AI in clinical care. Bright Star’s core strength⏎is building AI programs that have answers to those questions built into the design, delivery, and operations.⏎Specialist Capital and Institutional Advisory Contributor⏎ZXV Group is an independent investment and strategic advisory firm focused on capital allocation, institutional⏎governance, and risk architecture in complex, regulated environments. The firm provides decision-focused⏎advisory support to senior leaders in public and private sectors when technology adoption intersects with⏎regulatory constraint, accountability requirements, and capital commitment decisions.⏎ZXV Group’s contribution to this response centers on how economic incentives, reimbursement structures,⏎liability allocation, and governance clarity shape the pace and durability of artificial intelligence adoption in⏎clinical settings. Our perspective complements enterprise modernization and secure AI deployment capabilities⏎by grounding adoption in financial, institutional, and risk realities.⏎Relevant Qualifications⏎✓ Advisory participation in strategic transaction contexts valued at over $500M⏎✓ Capital allocation and portfolio advisory exceeding $200M within regulated environments⏎✓ Governance and oversight advisory within federal and government-facing systems⏎✓ Independent research on technology adoption, institutional change, and capital formation⏎✓ Experience aligning technology deployment with compliance, audit, and accountability frameworks⏎ZXV Group operates as a principal-led advisory practice. In the context of this RFI, ZXV Group contributes⏎structured analysis regarding incentive alignment, risk containment, regulatory clarity, and capital formation⏎Page 1⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎mechanisms that influence responsible AI deployment in healthcare. Bright Star Solutions and ZXV Group are⏎pleased to provide HHS with this RFI providing our expertise in accelerating AI adoption in the clinical care⏎industry.⏎2 Specific Question #1⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use in⏎clinical care?⏎Barrier A: Fragmented data access and unclear data permissioning⏎Healthcare AI requires data from EHRs, labs, imaging systems, pharmacy records, claims, and patient-⏎generated sources. Even when data exists, innovators face a problem we have repeatedly seen in federal AI⏎environments: data is owned by diverse groups, governed by different interpretations, and stored in different⏎formats. The result is uncertainty, slow access, and uneven data quality.⏎On our U.S. Citizenship and Immigration Services automation work, we supported workflows that required⏎ingesting data from multiple legacy systems with different owners, formats, and sensitivity constraints. Progress⏎did not accelerate until we established a practical model: explicit data-use boundaries, a documented data flow,⏎access control, and full auditability. Once governance and permissioning were unblocked, automation scaled⏎reliably and securely.⏎How this maps to HHS:⏎Clinical AI cannot scale without a similar model. HHS can accelerate innovation by helping normalize how AI⏎teams define permissible use, data lineage, and governance artifacts for training, testing, and monitoring.⏎Recommendation:⏎HHS should sponsor and publish reference “AI data access patterns” for clinical care that show compliant⏎approaches for 1) model development and 2) validation and evaluation 3) post-deployment monitoring and drift⏎detection. This reduces uncertainty for innovators and health systems without weakening privacy protections.⏎Barrier B: Governance immaturity and unclear accountability⏎Healthcare AI adoption stalls when organizations treat AI like a static software feature rather than continuous⏎capability. AI is a lifecycle. It needs owners, controls, monitoring, and escalation paths.⏎At U.S. Department of State, Bright Star supported enterprise AI tools and adoption programs where the biggest⏎challenge was not building the tool. The challenge was establishing the governance, training, support model,⏎and communications needed to sustain adoption responsibly. Our enterprise-scale generative AI work required⏎policies, use-case boundaries, and stakeholder alignment to safely expand adoption.⏎How this maps to HHS:⏎Clinical AI requires explicit governance: model approval processes, clinical oversight, patient safety⏎mechanisms, and defined accountability for monitoring and incident response. HHS can accelerate adoption by⏎promoting governance norms that require 1) named accountable roles for AI operations (clinical owner and⏎technical owner), 2) minimum monitoring standards, 3) mandatory incident reporting mechanisms, 4) clear⏎“human in the loop” decision boundaries where appropriate.⏎Barrier C: Security and privacy implementation uncertainty, especially for cloud and modern AI⏎Most organizations understand privacy laws exist. What they struggle with is how to implement modern AI⏎architectures compliantly at scale.⏎Page 2⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎In DHS environments, uncertainty around security implementation is often the slowest gating factor. Bright⏎Star’s delivery model addresses this by starting with security architecture and compliance as first-order design⏎constraints and implementing auditable pipelines, role-based access, and monitoring from day one.⏎How this maps to HHS:⏎Healthcare AI adoption slows when vendors and providers are unsure how to operationalize compliant AI⏎pipelines, especially for continuous model improvement and post-deployment monitoring. HHS should provide⏎more practical guidance and reference architectures for secure AI operations, including: model supply chain⏎integrity, logging and auditability standards, AI incident response best practices, and access control patterns for⏎clinical settings.⏎Barrier D: Misaligned financial incentives and ROI ambiguity⏎Many clinical AI tools create value by reducing burden and improving outcomes, but payment structures do not⏎consistently reward those gains. When ROI is unclear, adoption slows.⏎In federal programs, adoption accelerates when performance metrics are tied to operational outcomes, not just⏎delivery of features. For example, adoption metrics, time savings, throughput improvements, and user⏎satisfaction were critical to scaling enterprise AI tools in sensitive environments.⏎How this maps to HHS:⏎Clinical AI needs “value recognition mechanisms,” including reimbursement models that reward burden⏎reduction, error reduction, improved clinical throughput, and improved access.⏎From a capital and institutional perspective, the principal barrier to healthcare AI adoption is not model⏎performance, but incentive alignment.⏎Health systems operate under constrained margins and complex reimbursement frameworks. AI adoption scales⏎when it demonstrably:⏎✓ Improves contribution margin per unit of care⏎✓ Reduces denial or compliance risk⏎✓ Lowers administrative burden⏎✓ Improves throughput within fixed-cost environments⏎✓ Reduces measurable liability exposure⏎Absent economic alignment, AI remains confined to pilot programs. In regulated sectors, uncertainty in liability⏎allocation, monitoring obligations, and reimbursement pathways increases perceived downside risk and⏎suppresses capital deployment. HHS can accelerate adoption by reducing ambiguity in accountability structures⏎and by supporting reimbursement models that recognize operational and quality gains, not only novel technical⏎outputs.⏎3 Specific Question #2⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the⏎effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to⏎augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Page 3⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎We recommend changes in three areas: regulatory clarity, incentive alignment, and shared infrastructure.⏎Priority A: Reduce uncertainty through operational clarity⏎AI adoption slows when organizations lack predictable guidance on how existing rules apply across the AI⏎lifecycle. A key government lever is not always new regulation. It is making the existing rules easier to apply⏎consistently.⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎In federal AI deployments, uncertainty was reduced by producing standardized governance artifacts: decision⏎logs, data flow diagrams, usage boundaries, and monitoring reports. Standardization enabled scale.⏎HHS opportunity:⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by defining minimum standard “AI governance documentation” that buyers can⏎request and vendors can produce consistently.⏎Priority B: Incentivize AI that improves outcomes and reduces burden, not just AI that generates novel⏎outputs⏎HHS reimbursement and program design is one of the most powerful levers for adoption. Where incentives⏎reward volume and not value, adoption will lag.⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎When enterprise AI tools delivered demonstrable time savings and productivity gains, adoption rose rapidly.⏎The difference was measurement discipline and governance mechanisms that demonstrated impact.⏎HHS opportunity:⏎HHS can support incentive models that reward: 1) reduced administrative burden, 2) improved throughput and⏎access, 3) improved quality metrics, 4) reduced preventable utilization, and 5) paired with required monitoring⏎and transparency.⏎Priority C: Invest in shared benchmarks, evaluation frameworks, and public-private pilots⏎One of the most effective accelerants is shared infrastructure that lowers the cost of responsible adoption.⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎In federal settings, shared platforms, repeatable intake processes, and standardized evaluation approaches⏎enabled scale. For example, enterprise intake and feasibility assessment mechanisms created a repeatable⏎pathway for innovation.⏎HHS opportunity:⏎HHS can find: 1) benchmark datasets and test harnesses, 2) reference architectures for monitoring,⏎3) implementation of science pilots in real clinical environments, 4) scalable evaluation templates and reporting⏎standards.⏎Regulatory modernization should prioritize operational clarity over additional rulemaking volume.⏎Institutions require predictable guidance on:⏎✓ Liability demarcation⏎✓ Documentation standards⏎Page 4⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎✓ Monitoring expectations⏎✓ Audit pathways⏎✓ Escalation and override authority⏎In capital-intensive environments, clarity lowers risk perception and enables deployment.⏎HHS can support adoption through:⏎✓ Standardized AI governance documentation templates⏎✓ Safe harbor frameworks for AI-assisted decision support⏎✓ Regulatory sandboxes tied to measurable outcomes⏎✓ Reimbursement pilots that reward burden reduction and quality improvement⏎From a capital formation standpoint, policy that reduces downside exposure is a stronger accelerator than policy⏎that increases speed alone.⏎4 Specific Question #3⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures ( e.g., relating to⏎liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what⏎role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Non-medical device AI used in clinical care introduces legal and implementation issues that challenge⏎governance structures even when the tool is not regulated as a medical device.⏎Issue A: Accountability ambiguity and liability boundary confusion⏎Many AI tools influence clinical workflows even if they do not “make the decision.” When a tool influences⏎decisions, accountability must be explicit.⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎In federal AI, we treated this as a governance problem, not a legal mystery. We implemented: 1) explicit⏎“decision support only” language where appropriate, 2) audit logs showing what data was used and what output⏎was provided, 3) escalation paths for suspected errors, and 4) documented human review requirements for high-⏎risk use cases.⏎HHS role:⏎HHS can help by publishing model governance guidance and procurement language that clearly defines: 1)⏎responsibility for monitoring and remediation, 2) expectations for auditability and transparency, 3) requirements⏎for incident reporting and corrective actions.⏎Issue B: Data privacy, security, and model supply chain risks.⏎Non-medical AI tools still process protected health information and create risk through third-party services,⏎dependencies, and opaque models.⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎Federal AI delivery requires supply chain risk thinking: what dependencies exist, what vendors touch sensitive⏎data, where are logs stored, who can access, and how do you revoke.⏎Page 5⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎HHS role:⏎HHS should encourage standardized AI security and supply chain assurance documentation, especially for tools⏎integrated into clinical workflows.⏎Non-medical AI used in clinical settings introduces accountability ambiguity.⏎Institutions require explicit governance structures that define:⏎✓ Decision authority⏎✓ Human override boundaries⏎✓ Monitoring responsibilities⏎✓ Incident reporting and remediation pathways⏎Liability ambiguity increases adoption hesitation even where technology is sound.⏎Additionally, model supply chain risk, third-party dependencies, and audit traceability must be incorporated into⏎procurement frameworks.⏎HHS can assist by publishing standardized procurement language and governance expectations that clarify⏎responsibility for:⏎✓ Model monitoring⏎✓ Drift detection⏎✓ Data lineage documentation⏎✓ Incident remediation⏎Clear accountability reduces perceived institutional risk.⏎5 Specific Question #4⏎For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-deployment),⏎metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care?⏎Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts,⏎grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Bright Star strongly believes evaluation must be treated as a lifecycle function, not a one-time test.⏎Pre-deployment evaluation should include:⏎✓ clinical validity and utility testing aligned to workflow realities⏎✓ subgroup performance evaluation (equity and fairness)⏎✓ robustness testing under real-world data quality variance⏎✓ usability testing focused on clinician cognitive load and workflow fit⏎✓ clear documentation of intended use and limitations⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎In federal generative AI deployments, the most important early evaluation was not “does it generate text,” but⏎whether it was safe, policy-aligned, and operationally useful under real constraints. We used structured⏎stakeholder review, controlled rollouts, feedback loops, and monitoring dashboards to validate readiness.⏎Page 6⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Post-deployment evaluation must include:⏎✓ drift detection⏎✓ usage analytics and adoption patterns⏎✓ outcome tracking tied to intervention pathways⏎✓ error reporting and safety incident mechanisms⏎✓ periodic model and prompt updates with controlled release⏎HHS role and best mechanisms:⏎HHS should support these processes through:⏎✓ contracts for evaluation infrastructure⏎✓ grants for implementation science⏎✓ cooperative agreements that build shared test harnesses⏎✓ prize competitions focused on monitoring, drift detection, and human-centered evaluation⏎Evaluation must be treated as a lifecycle function.⏎Pre-deployment evaluation should include:⏎✓ Clinical utility and workflow alignment⏎✓ Subgroup and equity analysis⏎✓ Robustness testing under real-world data variability⏎✓ Documentation of intended use and limitations⏎Post-deployment evaluation should include:⏎✓ Drift detection mechanisms⏎✓ Usage analytics⏎✓ Outcome tracking tied to intervention pathways⏎✓ Documented update protocols⏎From an institutional perspective, evaluation infrastructure is capital infrastructure. HHS can accelerate⏎adoption by funding:⏎✓ Shared test harnesses⏎✓ Benchmark datasets⏎✓ Implementation science pilots⏎✓ Monitoring and audit frameworks⏎Lifecycle governance increases executive confidence and improves capital discipline.⏎6 Specific Question #5⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing,⏎and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Page 7⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎HHS should support certification systems that validate operational readiness, not just documentation⏎completeness.⏎Bright Star’s view:⏎What healthcare needs is a norm that “AI is certifiable as an operational system.” That includes governance,⏎monitoring, security, and support.⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎In federal environments, systems achieve trust when they have repeatable assurance artifacts, consistent review,⏎and transparent operational processes. The closer clinical AI assurance resembles disciplined government⏎assurance models, the faster adoption can scale.⏎Recommendation:⏎HHS should support frameworks that certify:⏎✓ monitoring readiness⏎✓ auditability⏎✓ governance structures⏎✓ security patterns⏎✓ human factors evaluation without creating fragmented competing certification burdens.⏎Certification systems should validate operational readiness, not only documentation completeness.⏎Effective certification frameworks should evaluate:⏎✓ Governance structures⏎✓ Monitoring readiness⏎✓ Auditability⏎✓ Security posture⏎✓ Human factors integration⏎Certification aligned with institutional risk management increases trust and lowers procurement friction.⏎7 Specific Question #6⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and where⏎have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care⏎outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎We can speak from a cross-sector operational AI lens: tools succeed when they remove burden and integrate⏎into workflows. They fail when they are bolted on, untrusted, or unsupported.⏎Where AI typically exceeds expectations:⏎✓ administrative burden reduction⏎✓ documentation and summarization⏎✓ workflow triage and routing support⏎Page 8⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎✓ population-level analytics tied to clear interventions⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎At USCIS, RPA and AI components succeeded when they were embedded into process pathways with⏎monitoring, auditability, and human oversight. Adoption rose when time savings were measurable and error⏎rates were managed.⏎Where AI typically falls short:⏎✓ tools that are not integrated into workflows⏎✓ tools that change clinician processes without training and change management⏎✓ tools with opaque outputs that clinicians do not trust⏎✓ tools without monitoring and drift management⏎Novel AI tools with high potential:⏎✓ care navigation copilots that reduce access friction⏎✓ prior authorization and claims automation with transparency⏎✓ predictive models tied to intervention pathways, not just prediction⏎✓ patient communication tools that increase comprehension and engagement while preserving privacy and⏎consent⏎Across sectors, AI tools meet expectations when they reduce burden and integrate into workflows. They fall⏎short when deployed without governance, monitoring, or workflow alignment.⏎High-performing use cases include:⏎✓ Administrative automation⏎✓ Documentation support⏎✓ Workflow triage⏎✓ Population-level analytics tied to defined interventions⏎Underperforming deployments typically lack:⏎✓ Clear ROI measurement⏎✓ Monitoring discipline⏎✓ Workflow integration⏎✓ Defined accountability⏎AI success correlates with governance maturity.⏎8 Specific Question #7⏎Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the most influence⏎on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in⏎clinical care?⏎AI adoption is a governance decision, not a technology purchase.⏎Page 9⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Key decision makers include clinical leadership, informatics, IT security, compliance, procurement, legal risk⏎management, quality and safety governance, and executive leadership.⏎Primary administrative hurdles:⏎✓ procurement cycles and contracting risk⏎✓ unclear ROI and evidence thresholds⏎✓ legal uncertainty and accountability concerns⏎✓ integration complexity with legacy systems⏎✓ workforce and training capacity⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎At DOS, scaling AI required coordinated buy-in from leadership, security, policy stakeholders, and end users.⏎The adoption model worked because we treated change management, training, and governance as primary⏎deliverables, not secondary tasks.⏎AI adoption as stated is a governance decision.⏎Key actors include:⏎✓ Clinical leadership⏎✓ Informatics⏎✓ Compliance and legal⏎✓ Procurement⏎✓ Executive leadership⏎Administrative hurdles include:⏎✓ Procurement cycles⏎✓ Liability ambiguity⏎✓ ROI uncertainty⏎✓ Integration complexity⏎✓ Workforce capacity⏎Adoption improves when change management, governance, and executive framing are treated as primary⏎deliverables rather than secondary tasks.⏎9 Specific Question #8⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the⏎development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking⏎tools.⏎Interoperability is the growth engine for clinical AI. HHS can unlock major market opportunity by supporting⏎standardization and accessible benchmarking across:⏎✓ clinical longitudinal data⏎✓ imaging⏎✓ labs and pathology⏎✓ claims and utilization⏎✓ social determinants⏎✓ patient-reported outcomes⏎Page 10⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎At USCIS, integration across legacy systems enabled scalable automation and analytics. Once data exchange⏎patterns were standardized, capabilities scaled quickly. Healthcare needs the same interoperability enablement,⏎aligned to data standards and real-world benchmarking infrastructure.⏎Interoperability challenges are frequently governance and incentive challenges rather than purely technical⏎constraints.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by supporting:⏎✓ Standardized reporting formats⏎✓ Structured data-sharing agreements⏎✓ Benchmarking infrastructure⏎✓ Incentives for audit traceability⏎Interoperability lowers capital barriers and expands responsible market opportunity.⏎10 Specific Question #9⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption and use of⏎AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of⏎AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers want AI to reduce friction and increase clarity, but they fear loss of control.⏎Top desired improvements:⏎✓ reduced administrative burden⏎✓ faster access and scheduling⏎✓ clearer explanations and care plans⏎✓ improved coordination across providers⏎✓ proactive identification of risks⏎Top concerns:⏎✓ privacy and data misuse⏎✓ opaque decision making⏎✓ bias and inequitable outcomes⏎✓ reduced human interaction⏎✓ inability to appeal or challenge AI-influenced decisions⏎Bright Star crossover story:⏎Public trust is central in immigration and diplomacy systems. We have learned that transparency and agency⏎matter: clear explanations, auditability, and feedback channels are essential to trust. Clinical AI must build those⏎features in from the start.⏎Patients and caregivers seek reduced friction, improved clarity, and preserved agency.⏎Top desired outcomes:⏎Page 11⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎✓ Reduced administrative burden⏎✓ Faster access⏎✓ Clear explanations⏎✓ Improved coordination⏎Primary concerns:⏎✓ Privacy⏎✓ Bias⏎✓ Opaque decision-making⏎✓ Loss of human oversight⏎Trust requires transparency, appeal mechanisms, and clear communication pathways.⏎11 Specific Question #10⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎Yes. HHS should prioritize research that closes the gap between “model performance” and “clinical reality.”⏎Priority research areas:⏎✓ Implementation science for AI in clinical workflows⏎✓ Long-term monitoring and drift detection⏎✓ Bias and subgroup performance evaluation⏎✓ Human factors and clinician cognitive load⏎✓ Privacy-preserving analytics and federated methods⏎✓ Economic impact measurement tied to real delivery models⏎✓ Benchmarking infrastructure and shared evaluation harnesses⏎10a: Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools in clinical care?⏎There is a growing body of research, but impact varies widely depending on workflow integration, monitoring,⏎and governance maturity. Tools that are integrated, measured, and continuously managed show stronger⏎outcomes than tools deployed as stand-alone pilots.⏎Bright Star’s federal experience supports the same principle: adoption and impact are driven by governance,⏎training, monitoring, and operational integration.⏎10b: How does literature approach costs, benefits, and transfers?⏎The most credible approaches account for:⏎✓ direct operational savings⏎✓ downstream cost avoidance⏎✓ workforce burden reduction⏎✓ quality and safety outcomes⏎✓ infrastructure and monitoring costs⏎✓ change management and training investments⏎Page 12⏎⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎AI “benefit claims” fail when they ignore adoption and lifecycle costs.⏎Bright Star’s approach to ROI emphasizes measurable operational outcomes and lifecycle sustainment⏎requirements, rather than one-time implementation success.⏎Research should focus on economic and institutional drivers of adoption, including:⏎✓ Contribution-margin analysis⏎✓ Liability exposure mapping⏎✓ Monitoring cost modeling⏎✓ Implementation science for workflow integration⏎✓ Governance maturity benchmarks⏎Adoption speed will be determined less by incremental model improvements and more by clarity in economic⏎incentives and institutional accountability.⏎12 Closing⏎Bright Star believes HHS has an opportunity to accelerate clinical AI adoption through a practical model:⏎reducing uncertainty, aligning incentives, and funding shared evaluation infrastructure. Our public sector AI⏎delivery story correlates strongly with HHS’s needs because it is built on trust, governance, security, and⏎operational accountability. Those are the same foundations required for safe, scalable AI in clinical care.⏎Responsible AI adoption in clinical care requires alignment among regulatory clarity, economic incentives,⏎governance discipline, and institutional culture.⏎Bright Star contributes enterprise modernization and secure AI delivery expertise. ZXV Group contributes⏎independent capital and institutional analysis focused on improving decision quality before large-scale⏎commitments are made. Together, this combined perspective supports HHS in designing policy environments⏎where safe, accountable, and economically sustainable AI deployment can scale.⏎Page 13♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0044♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from CGI Federal Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔CGI Federal Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔CGI Federal Response⏎to⏎HHS⏎Health Sector AI RFI⏎⏎Policy References: EO 14179, M-25-21, HTI-1, TEFCA⏎Executive Summary⏎This document responds to the HHS Request for Information (RFI) on advancing AI⏎adoption in clinical care. Our strategy aligns with the five pillars of the HHS AI Strategy:⏎Governance and Risk Management, Infrastructure and Platforms, Workforce⏎Development and Burden Reduction, Gold-Standard Research and Reproducibility, and⏎Modernization of Care and Public Health Delivery.⏎CGI recommends targeted regulatory and policy actions to accelerate responsible AI⏎adoption in clinical care by enabling secure, interoperable data access and aligning⏎incentives for adoption at scale. Key priorities and expected impact are summarized⏎below.⏎Regulatory priorities⏎• Fix data silos created by program design by enabling approved cross-program⏎agency data sharing (real-time/streaming/bulk), supported by FHIR APIs and⏎TEFCA alignment.⏎• Modernize HIPAA and ONC certification (45 CFR Part 170) to enable API-based⏎interoperability and AI-enabled workflows (including bulk FHIR access).⏎• Clarify AI liability and governance (human oversight, identity/security controls)⏎and strengthen credentialing/accreditation for safe use.⏎• Consider an HHS-vetted AI app store to distribute compliant tools.⏎Policy priorities⏎• Align payment and incentives to reward high-value AI that improves outcomes⏎and reduces costs.⏎• Build AI readiness into programs/contracts (responsible use of AI across SDLC,⏎operations, cybersecurity, and CX).⏎• Update privacy-related constraints (e.g., 42 CFR Part 2) to enable appropriate⏎data access while maintaining protections.⏎• Require validation, transparency, bias monitoring, and usability supports to build⏎trust and promote equitable access.⏎Expected impact⏎Collectively, these proposed actions would strengthen interoperability and data quality,⏎reduce adoption risk, and support the scalable use of trusted AI to improve outcomes⏎and operational efficiency.⏎⏎AI Strategy Recommendations⏎To provide clear, actionable feedback to HHS, CGI organized the RFI questions into two⏎broad categories—HHS Regulation Impact and Recommendations and HHS Policy⏎Changes—as these categories most directly align with the underlying themes and⏎levers for change embedded in the questions. Many of the questions focus on structural⏎and compliance-related barriers, such as interoperability, liability, and governance,⏎which fall under regulatory frameworks. Others emphasize incentive design, payment⏎models, and programmatic strategies to accelerate adoption, which are inherently policy⏎driven. By distinguishing between regulatory and policy considerations, we ensure our⏎responses address both the foundational rules that govern AI integration, and the⏎operational mechanisms that enable its effective use in clinical care. This approach⏎provides clarity, avoids overlap, and highlights where HHS can exert the greatest⏎influence to foster innovation, trust, and scalability in AI for healthcare.⏎HHS Regulation Impact and Recommendations⏎HHS regulations affect technology effectiveness, IT modernization (including AI⏎adoption), and the ability to adapt to evolving programs and policies. Clinical care is⏎only as effective as the governed ecosystem will allow it to be. For instance, fragmented⏎data ecosystems, such as the lack of interoperability among EHRs, payer systems, and⏎healthcare applications, hinder AI integration. Several regulatory complexities exist in⏎implementation. Although FDA's Predetermined Change Control Plans (PCCP) includes⏎some ways on how streamlined frameworks can facilitate AI innovation while ensuring⏎safety, they are far from adequate. Addressing these challenges through unified⏎standards and incentives can significantly enhance the integration and effectiveness of⏎AI in clinical care. To accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, HHS should drive⏎interagency communication to lead efforts to harmonize standards, reduce friction, and⏎incentivize responsible use.⏎Data silos created by program design are a major regulatory barrier to achieving highly⏎effective outcomes. These silos make true, near real time data sharing not only⏎impractical, but downright impossible. Programs are designed, many by legislation and⏎implementation interpretation, to create restrictions for data use and sharing within a⏎narrow scope. We suggest HHS establish a mechanism to override restrictive data-use⏎provisions for approved use cases and enable multi-mode data sharing—real-time,⏎streaming, and bulk—across agencies such as VA and SSA. We see this as the single⏎most transformative regulatory change that would bring true interoperability to life⏎across HHS. Enhanced interoperability, through the adoption of FHIR-based APIs and⏎alignment with the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA),⏎would enable real-time, secure data exchange across clinical, payer, and research⏎ecosystems, supporting population health analytics and predictive modeling.⏎⏎CGI recommends modernizing HIPAA and ONC certification criteria to support API-⏎based interoperability and AI-enabled workflows. Clarifying HIPAA’s treatment of API-⏎based exchange for AI-enabled clinical care and expanding ONC certification (45 CFR⏎Part 170) to require standardized API access to full EHI (including bulk FHIR APIs)⏎would help accelerate clinical decision support, care coordination, and predictive⏎analytics while maintaining privacy and security.⏎Another critical area is clarifying liability and governance for AI in clinical care. Today’s⏎uncertainty around liability and indemnification for AI-informed decisions elevates risk⏎for providers and vendors and constrains implementation. We recommend HHS develop⏎regulatory guidance on liability allocation between clinicians and technology vendors,⏎requiring governance models mandating human oversight in AI-assisted clinical⏎workflows, and enforce secure management of digital identities to mitigate privacy risks.⏎These measures will strengthen trust, accountability, and security as AI adoption⏎accelerates. Credentialing and accreditation are also essential for safe and effective AI⏎integration. Promoting Credentialing Service Providers (CSPs) and establishing best⏎practices for accreditation and certification in AI-enabled clinical care would help ensure⏎clinicians are prepared to use AI responsibly and effectively, reducing risk and⏎improving patient outcomes.⏎Finally, establishing an HHS-sponsored AI app marketplace could help operationalize⏎these recommendations by making trusted, compliant tools easier to find and adopt. A⏎centralized, criteria-based repository would give providers a dependable source for⏎vetted AI applications.⏎These recommendations ultimately depend on comprehensive, high-quality data being⏎available through secure, interoperable mechanisms. In a recent federal healthcare⏎engagement, we implemented an API-enabled approach to integrate data from multiple⏎provider-domain systems into a virtualized data environment supporting advanced⏎analytics. The same pattern can scale to clinical use cases such as predictive modeling,⏎risk stratification, and personalized care planning. Aligning policy, interoperability, and⏎adoption mechanisms, including a curated marketplace, positions HHS to accelerate⏎responsible AI and improve outcomes while enhancing operational efficiency.⏎HHS Policy Changes⏎First, HHS should update payment policies to incentivize providers and payers to adopt⏎AI-enabled tools that improve clinical outcomes and reduce costs. This can be⏎reinforced by revisiting ONC certification criteria under 45 CFR Part 170 to require full⏎electronic health information (EHI) access via standardized APIs, including Bulk FHIR⏎APIs. By strengthening interoperability requirements at the policy level, HHS can⏎expand market opportunities, support research, and accelerate development of AI-⏎enabled clinical care tools. These changes can help AI solutions integrate more⏎⏎seamlessly into clinical workflows, enabling capabilities such as predictive analytics, risk⏎stratification, and personalized care planning.⏎Next, HHS should broaden and standardize definitions for data use and related artifacts⏎to improve adaptability across programs and to better accommodate rapid change.⏎Policy updates, legislation, reauthorizations, and contracts should set clear expectations⏎for responsible AI use and for applying AI across the SDLC, operations and⏎maintenance, cybersecurity, and customer experience. This proactive approach will⏎embed AI readiness into the fabric of healthcare programs, ensuring scalability and⏎resilience.⏎Further, we recommend HHS make targeted updates to regulations such as 42 CFR⏎Part 2 to improve pathways for appropriate data sharing while maintaining privacy⏎protections for substance use disorder patient records. These updates, paired with⏎expectations for representative datasets and continuous monitoring, would support⏎trustworthy AI-enabled clinical decision-making and help address bias and equity⏎concerns.⏎To alleviate patient and caregiver concerns, HHS can strengthen governance by⏎requiring rigorous validation, clear human-in-the-loop oversight, and transparency for⏎AI-assisted clinical decision-making. Policies should mandate proactive disclosure, clear⏎communication, and access to decision summaries to build confidence in AI⏎technologies. Improving usability through multichannel options and digital literacy⏎coaching will help bridge the digital divide and ensure equitable access to AI-enabled⏎care.⏎Looking ahead, HHS should prioritize policies that support bulk FHIR APIs to enable⏎population health management and real-time claims analysis. These capabilities would⏎strengthen comprehensive, data-driven care delivery while reducing administrative⏎burden. By aligning payment incentives with economic, clinical, and social values, HHS⏎can foster competition among AI developers and accelerate access to affordable, high-⏎value AI tools.⏎Adopting these policies would enable broader, more effective use of AI in healthcare—⏎supporting better care, more efficient operations, and continued innovation. With this⏎foundation, HHS can lead responsible clinical AI adoption and help ensure technology⏎delivers lasting, system-wide improvements.⏎Conclusion⏎Just as health data is often siloed by design, AI policy can become fragmented when⏎guidance is developed program-by-program within individual agencies. To address this,⏎we recommend an HHS-wide AI Adoption Playbook that provides a comprehensive⏎framework for integrating AI across healthcare operations—from care delivery to back-⏎⏎office functions. The Playbook should include an AI journey map that aligns people,⏎process, and technology to HHS’s five pillars in support of One HHS, enabling a clearer⏎path to secure, effective, and innovative AI deployments grounded in strong⏎governance, ongoing innovation, and public trust.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0045♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Ana Law LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ana Law LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care⏎Artificial Intelligence has the potential to revolutionize both healthcare and technology. However, in order⏎to create an environment that promotes innovation while protecting legal, ethical and privacy guidelines,⏎we must develop and implement a balanced approach.⏎1. Support for innovation while protecting IP⏎Although encouraging responsible innovation in Artificial Intelligence for the benefit of⏎consumers, it is essential to protect the intellectual property and trade secrets that fuel innovation,⏎thereby preventing the transparency framework from inadvertently undermining proprietary⏎innovation. Typically, AI models, training data, and related materials contain substantial trade⏎secrets and intellectual property that fuel innovation. These disclosure requirements that are broad⏎and prescriptive pose a risk by exposing data that could potentially undermine the investment in AI⏎innovation.⏎2. Regulatory clarity and predictability⏎Predictability of regulations is essential to ensure the broad adoption of AI in the field of healthcare.⏎However, the current system introduces unnecessary barriers in terms of overlapping jurisdictions,⏎unclear and uneven application. The HHS must align its regulations and develop a common system⏎that defines which AI applications are governed by existing medical device regulations and new⏎regulatory approaches. The HHS must ensure that there are regular, predictable updates to⏎regulations that specifically address AI capabilities, rather than the current ad-hoc approach that⏎introduces planning uncertainty. Finally, the HHS must establish a program with a safe harbor⏎provision that will enable healthcare providers to adopt new AI applications while generating real-⏎world evidence, without the risk of retroactive enforcement.⏎3. Data governance and patient trust⏎In any discussion about Artificial Intelligence in the field of healthcare, the confidentiality of⏎patients’ information is non-negotiable. At the forefront is the need to comply with the Health⏎Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), which is the gold standard for protecting⏎⏎private healthcare information. It is crucial to ensure that all AI-enabled tools (such as applications,⏎etc.) are fully compliant with HIPAA regulations. Failure to fully comply with the regulations will⏎result in putting the patients at risk of having their most private information compromised, which⏎may result in lack of trust to share information, ultimately undermining the potential of AI to⏎improve healthcare outcomes. The regulations should be integrated into AI governance structures⏎not only to ensure confidentiality, but to ensure that there are strict legal guidelines for innovators⏎and healthcare institutions to innovate within.⏎4. Validation, testing, and post-deployment monitoring⏎In the healthcare sector, there is a great demand for standardized frameworks of AI validation,⏎supported by HHS, that take into account new systems of evaluations that are not restricted to the⏎traditional systems of clinical trials. HHS must offer financial support for the development of⏎benchmarking datasets that are open-source and clinical AI performance metrics so that comparison⏎with other tools and vendors can be made in an open and transparent way. Post-deployment⏎surveillance is also essential due to the tendency of AI to degrade in performance. Because of this,⏎HHS must offer facilities and guidelines in real-world settings. There must also be reporting⏎systems for AI performance issues similar to the current systems for adverse event reporting, to⏎create feedback systems for continuous improvement in the sector.⏎Ana Juneja⏎Attorney⏎Ana Law LLC⏎ana@analaw.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0046♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Society of Anesthesiologists, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Society of Anesthesiologists♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attn: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎7033A, 330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted Electronically at www.regulations.gov⏎Re: [HHS Health Sector AI RFI]⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎On behalf of the over 60,000 members of the American Society of Anesthesiologists (ASA), I am pleased⏎to offer comments to the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and the Department of Health⏎and Human Services (HHS) on the Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care Request for Information (RFI). As a medical specialty society with a long track record of⏎prioritizing patient safety, ASA appreciates the opportunity to provide feedback that will inform regulatory⏎action by ASTP and HHS to expedite and improve the deployment of artificial intelligence (AI) solutions in⏎clinical settings.⏎Patient safety, quality of care, and physician workflows can all be supported by the efficient⏎implementation of AI technology. Sufficiently incorporating each of these crucial pillars of patient care into⏎AI tools can only be achieved with communication between frontline clinical staff such as⏎anesthesiologists, policymakers, and AI developers and vendors.⏎ASA’s comments to ASTP’s RFI questions reflect our desire to help remove significant barriers to AI tool⏎adoption in anesthesia settings. Anesthesiologists and AI developers must work to establish trust in the⏎emerging technology, address ease-of-use concerns that can worsen rather than mitigate workflow⏎burdens, and overcome the lack of financial incentives for both frontline health care workers and facility⏎decision-makers.⏎The approach by HHS and ASTP to streamline health IT certification requirements and broader⏎regulations around AI clearly demonstrates the federal government’s “try-first” message to AI developers.⏎ASA supports the agencies’ aim to accelerate the development and implementation of innovative AI⏎solutions in health care settings, ensuring that valuable tools reach our members and their patients⏎sooner. However, we also believe this approach could be compatible with regulatory action that supports⏎greater transparency, real-world testing, and improved incentive structures – each of these objectives⏎would help adapt the development and deployment of AI tools to the implementation barriers faced by⏎anesthesiologists and their facilities. Clear guidance from HHS and ASTP on issues like liability, privacy,⏎and security could also mitigate burdens on small and mid-sized facilities and anesthesia practices that⏎may not have the resources to adequately build a governance structure for AI use on their own.⏎Please see our responses to each of the RFI questions below.⏎⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎AI solutions for anesthesia care are best positioned for widespread adoption and successful use if⏎developers partner with anesthesiologists, nurses, and perioperative teams through all stages of⏎development, rather than just engaging clinical stakeholders for validation. For example, in the⏎perioperative setting, predictive models for surgical case duration often fail because the models are⏎designed without significant input from anesthesiologists. Anesthesiologists understand non-linear factors⏎like surgeon experience, case complexity evolution, and team dynamics – all features that can help in AI⏎design and implementation. Models trained with an overreliance on historical data miss these critical⏎variables.⏎AI developers and ASTP should also focus on building trust among physicians and other medical staff. AI⏎developers should conduct assessments on the causes of any reluctance or delays in implementing AI in⏎health care. For instance, some physicians may be concerned about an AI application using a “black box⏎model” that fails to translate into clinical interpretability. This occurs when a tool provides a⏎recommendation without sufficient detail about why the model produced that recommendation. In other⏎cases, an AI model may lack standardized validation metrics specific to clinical workflows. Physicians⏎may also wish to have more information or data about a tool’s performance data. Last, clinical⏎stakeholders may be concerned about relying too much on AI recommendations.⏎ASA encourages ASTP to incentivize "explainable AI by default" standards. Clinical AI tools must⏎provide interpretable reasoning alongside predictions, similar to how radiologists provide⏎differential diagnoses rather than offering a final read. ASTP may wish to consider incorporating⏎such standards as a requirement in its Certified Electronic Health Record Technology (CEHRT)⏎and clinical decision support standards.⏎Clinician ease-of-use considerations also present a barrier to clinical adoption of AI tools. Tools that⏎disrupt established workflows or add steps to those workflows, at least in the early stages of adoption, are⏎more likely to face resistance from potential users. Similarly, clinicians often face increased workflow⏎burdens after an AI tool is initially integrated into existing EHR systems. A clear example of this is⏎reflected in anesthesiologists’ extensive use of patient monitors. AI tools for anesthesia care that use⏎alerts on monitoring devices can contribute to alarm fatigue if they have a poorly calibrated prediction⏎threshold.⏎Separately, the use of clinically valuable AI tools often lacks a sustainable payment structure even when⏎there are demonstrated improvements in care processes. HHS should coordinate with the Centers for⏎Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) and private payers on how the effective use of AI tools⏎with demonstrated value in patient safety and quality of care can be incentivized through payment⏎levers.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in⏎clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations⏎citations.⏎⏎HHS should develop a programmatic structure for the evaluation of the quality, safety, and⏎efficiency associated with the use of AI tools. Such a structure would assist hospitals and health⏎systems who are deploying AI tools, anticipating that cost savings or improved efficiency may accrue, but⏎whose benefits are mostly theoretical at this point. HHS should dedicate funding for studies to assess the⏎deployment of AI in healthcare, including automation bias, explainability, comprehensiveness,⏎hallucination and its mitigation, errors of omission, model drift, and model bias.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎Current liability frameworks provide little clarity on whether the AI developer, the anesthesiologist, or the⏎health care facility bears responsibility for legal, privacy, and security issues that may result from the use⏎of clinical AI tools. ASA believes that physicians should use their clinical judgement when assessing or⏎using tools that lack comprehensive rationale for their recommendations. At the same time, developers⏎may be less incentivized to invest in addressing gaps if all liability for those clinical gaps or errors rests⏎with the facility and its clinical staff.⏎HHS should convene an expert interdisciplinary task force to develop a firm, risk-based policy⏎framework around governance for liability, privacy, and security in the use of clinical AI tools. This⏎task force should coordinate with relevant HHS subagencies, such as the Food and Drug Administration⏎(FDA), and clarify developer and deployer responsibilities, disclosure expectations, accountability, and⏎post-deployment monitoring requirements.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎postdeployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎ASA appreciated the FDA’s emphasis on AI lifecycle, transparency, and bias mitigation in its January⏎2025 guidance, Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Device Software Functions: Lifecycle Management and⏎Marketing Submission Recommendations. HHS should extend these principles to non-device AI⏎through programmatic funding and standardized evaluation frameworks, as they would apply to⏎high-impact clinical AI tools that do not fit into FDA’s definition of a device.⏎We also support future HHS funding of grant programs for testing AI validation methodologies in⏎real-world clinical environments. These programs should allow for interdisciplinary expertise and⏎comprehensive evaluation across all phases of the translational spectrum. For example, continuous, real-⏎world performance monitoring is significantly more valuable than one-time pre-deployment validation. Yet⏎anesthesiologists would benefit from broader studies on AI validation methodologies. AI tools may predict⏎hypotension accurately in aggregate but, in some cases, they may fail for specific patient subgroups,⏎such as patients with autonomic dysfunction.⏎⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎HHS can effectively promote innovative and safe AI use by developing clear guidance for accreditation,⏎certification, testing, and credentialing, while enabling trusted private-sector organizations to⏎operationalize these standards. By articulating risk-based expectations for AI governance, supporting⏎independent evaluation infrastructure, encouraging clinician competency frameworks, and aligning federal⏎programs with recognized certifications, HHS can reduce uncertainty, improve accountability, and⏎accelerate responsible AI adoption without imposing rigid or premature regulation.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and⏎help reduce costs?⏎AI tools have seen significant success with applications that augment image- and signal-based⏎diagnostics with clear workflows, measurable performance, and explicit payment pathways (e.g., AI-ECG;⏎AI coronary plaque analysis). AI-powered administrative automation tools have also supported workflow⏎burden reduction and improved efficiency.⏎AI tools that have failed to meaningfully improve clinical care have often suffered from opaque models,⏎weak validation, unclear or suboptimal clinical integration pathways, and/or the imposition of high false-⏎positive burden (e.g., seizure detection with high false alarms). Relatedly, the high volume of alarms for⏎some AI tools has contributed to alarm fatigue for anesthesiologists.⏎For anesthesiologists, the greatest perioperative potential for AI tools lies in real-time intraoperative co-⏎pilot systems integrating multi-monitor data for context-aware guidance, personalized anesthesia planning⏎predicting individual medication responses, predictive operating room efficiency optimization, pain⏎management personalization, and just-in-time clinical education surfacing protocols during rare crisis⏎scenarios.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Within facilities and health systems, the adoption of AI tools for clinical care is most strongly influenced by⏎financial and executive leadership, particularly chief financial officers whose decisions hinge on perceived⏎or actual efficiency gains and cost reductions. While these executives are not responsible for identifying⏎or implementing AI tools, they may stall the adoption of these tools if integration costs are underfunded or⏎if regulatory or payer requirements are unclear. Facility stakeholders and anesthesiologists wish to have⏎greater certainty around potential payment mechanisms or incentives to offset integration and⏎governance costs. Addressing these barriers through CMS policy would have a positive impact on AI⏎adoption at scale.⏎On the other hand, frontline physician champions often determine the success of AI tool implementation.⏎Primary hurdles to achieving local buy-in for AI tools include fragmented governance across departments,⏎⏎IT resource constraints and training capacity, liability uncertainty, lack of standardized validation⏎frameworks, siloed data infrastructure, and misaligned incentives in which efficiency gains do not reward⏎physicians and other relevant staff for driving adoption.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Interoperability is a critically necessary but insufficient condition for the improvement of public health and⏎individual patients through IT and AI. There is no international standard for the interoperable sharing of⏎comprehensive anesthesia records. This is partly the result of how the anesthesia record is structured,⏎containing a wide breadth of information that includes medication administration, vital signs, ventilator⏎data, procedure and perioperative evaluation notes, and more. Perioperative medication data, procedure⏎complexity indicators, and loss of longitudinal patient data across care settings are details that have⏎limited interoperable deployment. These nuances are critical for practicing anesthesiologists. Further,⏎certain data, including high-frequency physiologic waveforms are often locked in proprietary systems or⏎not easily extractable.⏎The pathway to interoperability for anesthesia records would benefit from FHIR extensions for⏎anesthesia-specific data, standardized performance metrics, open validation datasets, and model cards⏎documenting training data and limitations. HHS should specifically deploy resources for the⏎completion of the HL7 data model for anesthesiology. This one standard would enable third-party⏎innovation, cross-institutional validation, and broad data access while accelerating safe AI development.⏎To fuel research, HHS should support standardization that enables access to high-fidelity,⏎streaming physiological data (e.g., waveform data, capnography, plethysmography) rather than⏎just static discrete data points entered into an EHR. Enhanced interoperability would encourage⏎“closed-loop” systems in which anesthesia machines, infusion pumps, and physiological monitors would⏎seamlessly communicate with one another. This would create a market for third-party AI developers to⏎build "clinical driver assistance" tools, with algorithms that sit on top of device streams to predict patient⏎conditions like hypotension or depth of anesthesia without being locked into a single manufacturer's⏎hardware.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Physician interactions can be critical in comforting and supporting patients throughout the perioperative⏎process, and AI tools should not limit those interactions or weaken personalized care. Anesthesiologists⏎often observe that patients seek personalized risk assessment, reduced wait times, better pain⏎management, enhanced safety monitoring, and improved communication. Patients also want to be⏎assured that the physician caring for them remains in control of their care, adhering to the “co-pilot, not⏎autopilot” principle. In the same vein, any AI-powered recommendation should be accompanied by a⏎transparent and comprehensive rationale so that care decisions can adequately be explained to patients.⏎Anesthesiologists are privy to these patient needs and concerns and should be empowered to use AI in a⏎way that can mitigate potential biases and improve patient care and safety.⏎⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize implementation science and studying actual AI usage patterns based on real-world⏎evidence, automation bias, cognitive load (including a focus on alarm fatigue), and workflow integration⏎rather than just technical validation. Longitudinal studies on model degradation would also be helpful in⏎addressing drift.⏎HHS may also wish to consider cost-benefit analyses across hospitals and health systems, including⏎those with fewer financial resources. While high upfront costs may remain an obstacle to purchasing⏎decisions, calculating the cost of inaction and, on the flip side, improvements in efficiency through the use⏎of AI tools, can address this barrier. Specifically, anesthesiologists and their facilities may benefit from⏎learning more about the role AI tools can play in recovery optimization, improved pain management, and⏎the prevention or mitigation of postoperative nausea and vomiting. Each of these outcomes contributes to⏎patient length of stay and clinical resources devoted to the patient.⏎******⏎Thank you for your consideration of our comments. ASA welcomes the opportunity to speak with ASTP⏎and HHS further about our feedback. Please contact Matthew Goldan, Senior Quality Operations⏎Associate (m.goldan@asahq.org), for questions or further information.⏎Sincerely,⏎Patrick Giam, MD, FASA⏎President⏎American Society of Anesthesiologists♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0047♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS RFI Response Guide⏎Details⏎Issuing Agency: HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC⏎Responding Agency: University of Texas MD Anderson Cancer Center⏎Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎• Private sector often jumps into developing products with a narrow, limited or even lack of⏎perspective around the clinical workflows, variability in data generation and challenges of⏎integrating AI into clinical care. This results in the development of AI products that may be⏎difficult to adopt and use in clinical care. Although FDA approval signals regulatory compliance,⏎this does not necessarily reflect an AI tool that can readily be implemented into broad clinical⏎use. Frameworks and tools to enable local validation of model performance currently do not exist.⏎While larger academic centers may have the resources to perform local validation and pilots,⏎many smaller and community clinics and centers lack these resources. Further, most⏎organizations do not have a functioning model implementation and lifecycle management⏎framework. The consequence is a cleared tool may work well at healthcare organizations with⏎data quality and capture that matches that of the model training data, but silently fail (mal- or⏎non-performance) at less mature organizations. Currently, there are no accountability mechanisms⏎to catch either of these outcomes.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎• The expansion and evolution of AI capabilities warrant an updated approach to interoperability⏎that emphasizes technological transportability in addition to data exchange. ONC’s USCDI⏎initiative (under 45 CFR § 170.213) appropriately expands capture across settings. To facilitate⏎clinical implementation, the data used to train AI models should be comparable across sites,⏎devices, protocols, and workflows. As stated in the HTI-1 final rule (B part V), “USCDI is not⏎specific to a setting of care, a healthcare specialty, or a specific category of health IT user.”⏎Including data classes on provenance that emphasize transportability would still apply broadly,⏎however, it would enable fit-for-purpose reimbursement mechanisms by ensuring required⏎information is holistic and relevant to clinical practice across institutions.⏎• Furthermore, reimbursement mechanisms do not condition payment on whether a model is fit for⏎the population and data environment where it will be used. This may result in use of AI tools for⏎clinical scenarios and populations that were not reflected in the training or validation data thereby⏎introducing risk of unreliable outputs. While there are regulations to outline edge cases where the⏎AI model might not perform, there are no standardized metrics on real-world implementation⏎metrics that contribute heavily to performance across settings. Reimbursement eligibility should⏎require a data quality attestation alongside FDA clearance. The deploying site’s data pipeline⏎must meet the minimum quality thresholds for the model’s intended use. This also refines the⏎necessary volume of clinical data elements exchanged.⏎⏎o Creates aligned incentives between developers seeking broad billing eligibility and⏎healthcare organizations investing in data infrastructures to access reimbursement⏎mechanisms⏎o CLIA (42 CFR Part 493 - Laboratory Requirements) is an established precedent where⏎reimbursement is conditioned on process quality in addition to reagent approval. The⏎same logic should be applied to AI-driven clinical care solutions to facilitate safe and⏎responsible use of AI along with the associated cost-savings.⏎• Quantitative imaging example: CLIA-equivalent standards for imaging data⏎o https://fas.org/publication/ai-enabled-precision-medicine/⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎• Training models on identifiable data creates a more accurate model, but potentially places data⏎privacy in jeopardy. Require developers to utilize de-identified or aggregate data for⏎development. If a solution does need identifiable data, the model must be locked per⏎implementation. The use of synthetic data could also circumvent the need for real-world de-⏎identified data usage.⏎• Conditional reimbursement tied to data fitness should be paired with clarification of liability⏎allocation between developer, user/site, and attestation framework⏎• HHS could define the domain of applicability tests. Requiring ability to test and demonstrate data⏎used should be within domain of applicability for regulated AI models.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎• Testing and evaluation of AI models will depend heavily on several technical considerations. We⏎focus on framework for assessing whether the deployment site’s data meets the conditions of⏎training/validation data. The existing framework for data exchange is robust and growing,⏎however, as evidenced by medical imaging, AI performance and generalizability will remain⏎unstable because the underlying measurements are not sufficiently comparable across real-world⏎settings. HHS can support AI adoption in clinical settings by prioritizing the comparison of data⏎based on safe reuse, transportability, and post-deployment monitoring through an AI lifecycle⏎management process. Conditioning both initial and continued reimbursement based on AI model⏎management will incentivize developers and users to consider quality assessment and quality⏎control across the AI lifecycle.⏎• Cooperative agreements are a likely high-impact mechanism for building shared evaluation⏎infrastructure. Allowing federal agencies to actively coordinate standards development, data⏎harmonization, QA/QC methods, reference datasets, and multisite validation networks would⏎stimulate cross-institutional coordination. Contracts could support time-bounded deliverables to⏎develop benchmarks, AI lifecycle maturity assessments, and workflow considerations.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven⏎testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎• HHS can best support private-sector accreditation, certification, and credentialing by encouraging⏎certification frameworks that evaluate the deployment environment in which an AI system is⏎⏎actually used. This means requiring standardized data classes for provenance and implementation⏎context (e.g., device type, workflow configuration, staffing patterns) that shape real-world⏎performance. Doing so would create a strong incentive for health systems to mature their data⏎quality and governance infrastructure, while pushing vendors and certifiers to measure⏎effectiveness under real clinical conditions rather than idealized test settings alone. HHS could⏎further reinforce this through joint cross-agency certification pathways and voluntary certification⏎tied to conditional reimbursement eligibility, so that reimbursement is linked to evidence that AI⏎tools are fit for purpose in the settings where they are deployed.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations⏎and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎• The absence of fit-for-purpose data standards makes underperformance invisible. There should be⏎a systematic mechanism to identify why cleared tools are failing in real-world deployment. A⏎well-known example of this phenomenon is the Epic Sepsis model that was validated based on⏎performance, but failed to sustain external validity in different settings. Models that show robust⏎performance using data that can be captured across settings, irrespective of machinery,⏎workflows, and system-specific considerations, would have significantly wider appeal than those⏎without such considerations. Cost-savings would be an outcome of this improved system because⏎the private sector would take on the onus of expanding applicability of their AI model and clinical⏎sites would have the necessary information to evaluate use in their setting.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the most⏎influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎• A non-exhaustive list is provided below:⏎- National Institutes of Health (NIH) (and related organizations such as National Institute⏎of Biomedical Imaging and Bioengineering (NIBIB))⏎- National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST)⏎- Centers for Disease Control (CDC)⏎- Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS)⏎- U.S. Department of Defense (DoD)⏎- Radiological Society of North America (RSNA)⏎- American Association of Physicists in Medicine (AAPM)⏎- International Society for Magnetic Resonance in Medicine (ISMRM)⏎- Society of Nuclear Medicine & Molecular Imaging (SNMMI)⏎- American Institute of Ultrasound in Medicine (AIUM)⏎- American College of Radiology (ACR)⏎- ARPA-H⏎- National Comprehensive Cancer Network⏎- U.S. Preventative Services Task Force⏎• International organizations also have relevant programs, guidance, and insight, including:⏎- European Society of Radiology (ESR)⏎- European Association of National Metrology Institutes (EURAMET)⏎- European Society of Breast Imaging (EUSOBI)⏎- European Society of Radiology’s Imaging Biomarkers Alliance (EIBALL)⏎- European Association of Nuclear Medicine (EANM)⏎- Institute of Physics and Engineering in Medicine (IPEM)⏎- Japan Quantitative Imaging Biomarker Alliance (JQIBA)⏎⏎- National Physical Laboratory (NPL)⏎- National Imaging Facility (NIF)⏎Administrative barriers to adoption are largely related to systemic factors of implementation. While data⏎can be collected for initial validation, continued performance and real point-of-care usage is defined by⏎operational feasibility, sociotechnical factors, and the ability to capture longitudinal performance. These⏎considerations are not captured by existing data classes and are not standardized across healthcare⏎organizations.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the⏎development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎AI Model Types (examples) Potential Systemic Concerns Examples of Additional Variables⏎Medical imaging Lack of standardization across devices Scanner manufacturer and model, software⏎and protocols. Image quality differences version, imaging protocol, reconstruction⏎can reduce model performance. settings, calibration status, QA and QC status⏎Physiologic signals (ECG Signal quality and preprocessing differ Device manufacturer and model, sampling rate,⏎alerts, Telemetry by device and hospital unit. Alert burden preprocessing method, signal quality flags, care⏎deterioration) depends on workflow conditions. unit type⏎EHR risk prediction Coding, documentation, and workflow Care setting, code system and version, missing⏎differences change inputs and outcome data rates, timestamp provenance, outcome⏎labels. definition⏎Clinical language AI Hallucination and omission risk. Output Source note types, note completeness, model⏎quality depends on source note quality version, prompt template version, user edits, user⏎and how staff use the tool. acceptance rate⏎Patient communication or Unsafe or outdated advice. Failure to Knowledge source and update date, language,⏎navigation escalate to a human when needed. escalation triggers, human review status⏎Operational scheduling Optimization can ignore clinical Clinical constraints including fasting and⏎constraints and local exceptions. preparation rules, staffing availability, resource⏎availability, override logs, cancellation patterns⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎• Better scheduling to reduce patient time toxicity⏎• Improved ability to select care providers and have patient information follow easily⏎• Better integration of clinical data across care systems⏎• Better translation of clinical care for patients without medical knowledge (often being addressed⏎using large-language models that are dependent on user prompts)⏎• Availability of recordings of clinician discussions with patients (high priority initiative with the⏎increased use of ambient recording services)⏎• Concerns: Clinicians rely only on AI without adequate backup. Consider clinician peer review or⏎use of agentic AI to review clinician use of AI in the care setting.⏎• Significant improvement if there would be transparency in tracking use of AI predictions along⏎with ultimate care decision and realized outcome. These remain separated and unavailable⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎• Establish data fitness benchmarks by clinical domain and AI modality/classification to link FDA⏎clearance and real-world deployment conditions⏎• Expand interoperability initiatives to capture system-level factors associated with AI⏎implementation and adoption beyond clinical performance and post-deployment tracking. The⏎⏎explicit emphasis on operational workflows and differences in data quality are key determinants⏎of feasibility and long-term value generation.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in clinical⏎care?⏎Hwang et al (2026) showcased a key phenomenon that influences AI adoption in clinical⏎care. We emphasize that adoption and implementation are not directly tied to need, but rather,⏎are related to institutional capability/maturity. Figure 3 clearly shows that implementation is⏎clustered in large metropolitan areas with serious gaps across rural America and low-resource⏎settings. An increase in the number of models approved for use does not translate to direct⏎impact because institutions lack the capabilities needed to implement and monitor the models,⏎such as robust AI lifecycle management. Therefore, we implore HHS and other relevant⏎stakeholders to prioritize understanding systemic data classes that relate to implementation⏎over model performance and tracking. Transparency and transportability are both⏎contributory factors to interoperability.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0048♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from The Pharmaceutical Care Management Association (PCMA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔The Pharmaceutical Care Management Association (PCMA)♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via http://www.regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building Mail⏎Stop:7033A⏎330 C Street, S.W., Washington, D.C. 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The Pharmaceutical Care Management Association (PCMA) appreciates the opportunity to⏎provide comments on the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RFI) published by the Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator of⏎Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) in the Federal Register on December 23, 2025.1⏎PCMA is the national association representing the country’s pharmacy benefit managers⏎(PBMs), which administer prescription drug plans (PDPs) and operate specialty pharmacies for⏎more than 289 million Americans with health coverage through Fortune 500 companies, health⏎insurers, labor unions, Medicare, Medicaid, the Federal Employees Health Benefits Program,⏎and the Exchanges established by the Affordable Care Act. PBMs are hired by employers,⏎unions, government programs, and others, to drive down prescription drug costs and administer⏎prescription drug plans for more than 289 million Americans. Before getting into specifics on⏎RULE, there are four things to know about PBMs:⏎• PBMs are the only part of the drug supply chain whose primary role is to lower⏎prescription drug costs. On average, they save patients and families about $1,154 per⏎person each year.2⏎• PBMs are extremely effective at reducing prescription drug costs for employers and⏎patients, which is why some industries that profit from high drug prices oppose them.⏎• For the enormous savings and value that PBMs provide, they operate on thin profit⏎margins.⏎1 90 Fed. Reg. at 60108 (December 23, 2025).⏎2 Visante. 2025. The Return on Investment (ROI) on PBM Services.⏎https://www.pcmanet.org/wpcontent/uploads/2025/02/ROI-on-PBM-Services.pdf⏎⏎• Hiring a PBM is optional. Employers, unions, government programs, and others choose⏎to use PBMs because they help lower drug costs and manage prescription benefits more⏎efficiently. PBMs negotiate lower drug prices, process claims, and perform safety⏎checks.⏎PBMs play a critical role at the intersection of clinical decision-making, benefit administration,⏎and cost management. Our members have long-standing experience in using advanced⏎analytics, automation, and decision-support tools to improve medication safety, ensure⏎appropriate utilization, and lower costs for patients, plan sponsors, and taxpayers. Several of⏎these tools are integrated directly into prescribers’ workflows at the point of care, providing real-⏎time information on coverage, cost-sharing, prior authorization requirements, and clinically⏎appropriate alternatives to support informed treatment decisions. PCMA supports the⏎responsible and ethical use of artificial intelligence (AI) to advance innovation and efficiency,⏎and improve patient support. As AI-enabled tools become more prevalent in health care delivery⏎and administration, it is essential that federal policy fosters responsible adoption while avoiding⏎regulatory approaches that could inadvertently impede innovation, increase costs, or shift⏎burdens onto patients and providers.⏎I. Regulatory Considerations⏎PCMA strongly supports a risk-based, use-case-specific approach to the regulation of AI in⏎health care. AI applications vary significantly in function and risk, and regulatory oversight⏎should reflect those differences. PBMs increasingly deploy AI-enabled tools to support coverage⏎determinations that may involve a clinical component, including prior authorization and step⏎therapy. These tools are designed to apply established clinical guidelines, benefit parameters,⏎and plan sponsor requirements consistently and efficiently, often with human oversight and⏎established appeal rights. When appropriately designed and governed, such tools can reduce⏎administrative burden, improve consistency, and support timely access to medically appropriate⏎care.⏎Federal policy should consider the level of risk involved in the deployment of AI solutions in⏎crafting guardrails and, where possible, allow for oversight through existing federal and state⏎frameworks, including Medicare Advantage and Part D requirements, ERISA fiduciary⏎obligations, state utilization review laws, and civil rights protections. Layering new, technology-⏎specific requirements on top of these frameworks risks creating duplication and uncertainty⏎without corresponding benefits for patients. To ensure a consistent and trusted approach,⏎PCMA supports the use of widely used and respected frameworks for responsible use of AI,⏎such as the Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development (OECD) Principles on⏎Artificial Intelligence, and the U.S. Department of Commerce’s National Institute of Standards⏎and Technology (NIST) Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework. We encourage HHS⏎to work with NIST and other standards-development organizations to further develop and refine⏎voluntary, consensus-based industry standards, as well as risk-based frameworks and best⏎practices.⏎In order to build patient trust in the use of AI tools in clinical care, it is imperative that any⏎regulatory framework requires that AI tools that involve clinical decision-making facilitate, but⏎never substitute for, decision-making by clinicians, consistent with “human-in-the-loop” AI⏎models. In addition, transparency about the use of AI tools and robust national privacy⏎standards will help build the regulatory framework for AI to flourish.⏎⏎Finally, as states have increasingly begun to regulate the use of AI, the multitude of varying⏎state laws and requirements is becoming a barrier to the development and implementation of AI⏎solutions. It is therefore essential that the appropriate regulation of AI in the health care sector⏎be established at the federal level. This will provide clarity and consistency to ensure that the⏎regulatory burden does not inhibit ongoing innovation to deliver better care and health⏎outcomes.⏎II. Reimbursement Considerations: Aligning Incentives to Encourage⏎Adoption⏎Although AI has demonstrated potential to reduce errors, improve efficiency, and lower costs,⏎current reimbursement and payment structures often fail to recognize or reward its use. This⏎misalignment presents a significant barrier to adoption, particularly in federal health programs⏎where payment incentives strongly shape care delivery and administrative processes.⏎AI-enabled decision support can enhance medication therapy management, improve⏎adherence, reduce inappropriate utilization, and streamline administrative workflows. These⏎improvements benefit patients through better access and lower out-of-pocket costs, while also⏎generating savings for plan sponsors and government programs. However, the entities that⏎invest in AI infrastructure and governance often do not directly capture the value created under⏎existing reimbursement models.⏎PCMA encourages HHS to consider how reimbursement policy can better support adoption of⏎AI tools that demonstrably improve quality, reduce errors, and reduce total cost of care,⏎particularly in areas where adoption has been slow or tentative because of investment,⏎regulatory, or other hurdles. Potential approaches include incorporating the effective use of AI-⏎enabled decision-support tools into value-based payment models, quality initiatives, and shared⏎savings arrangements, and aligning AI use with performance benchmarks where providers have⏎clear incentives to deploy tools that improve quality and efficiency. Additionally, HHS should⏎ensure that reimbursement rules do not treat AI investments as purely administrative costs⏎divorced from clinical value but should recognize that they also deliver meaningful value to⏎patients and the health care system. Private payers should be allowed to use reimbursement⏎models that incentivize the use of AI solutions that improve quality, efficiency, and more cost-⏎effective care.⏎In Medicare and Medicaid, targeted demonstration projects could help evaluate how AI adoption⏎by PBMs, plans, and providers affects outcomes, administrative efficiency, and program⏎spending. Without clearer pathways for aligning incentives, adoption of AI is likely to remain⏎uneven, limiting its ability to deliver system-wide benefits.⏎III. Research and Development: Supporting Innovation While Preserving⏎Flexibility⏎PCMA supports continued federal engagement in AI research and development, particularly⏎efforts that advance interoperability, data quality, and real-world validation. At the same time,⏎R&D policy should be structured to complement private-sector innovation and avoid⏎constraining model development or iteration.⏎PBMs and other stakeholders are actively investing in AI tools tailored to specific populations,⏎⏎benefit designs, and clinical contexts. Federal efforts should prioritize access to high-quality⏎data, promote public-private collaboration, and support research into bias mitigation and⏎governance best practices, while allowing organizations flexibility to adapt tools as evidence and⏎standards evolve.⏎Regulatory clarity is also essential to sustaining R&D investment. Clear signals that low-risk,⏎non-autonomous AI applications will not be subject to disproportionate oversight will encourage⏎continued innovation and responsible deployment.⏎Conclusion⏎AI and machine learning have the potential to improve clinical care, enhance administrative⏎efficiency, and reduce costs across the health care system. PBMs are well-positioned to deploy⏎these tools in ways that benefit patients, plan sponsors, and federal health programs. To fully⏎realize this potential, federal policy should emphasize a risk-based regulatory framework and⏎reimbursement models that align incentives with value, while relying on existing safeguards⏎where appropriate.⏎PCMA appreciates the opportunity to provide input, and it looks forward to continued⏎engagement with the Department as it advances policies to responsibly accelerate the adoption⏎of AI in health care.⏎Thank you for your consideration of these recommendations.⏎Sincerely,⏎Tim Dube⏎Tim Dube, Senior Vice President, Policy, PCMA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0049♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Design Interactive, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Design Interactive♔♔♔♔♔♔a⏎RFI Response: Federal Register Document⏎No. 2025-23641 (HHS Health Sector AI⏎RFI)⏎IP) Position Statement⏎⏎Introduction⏎Design Interactive, LLC (DI) appreciates the opportunity to provide comments on the Department of⏎Health and Human Services’ Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. The RFI identifies the importance of regulation, reimbursement,⏎and research & development (R&D) levers in enabling safe, effective AI adoption. [orangeslices.ai],⏎[vaoig.gov]⏎Our perspective is grounded in real-world experience deploying AI-enabled, machine-learning–powered⏎systems inside large federal healthcare environments, particularly the U.S. Department of Veterans⏎Affairs (VA). For example, DI has developed the AI-enabled competency development platform Nexora⏎under as part of a Small Business Innovative Research (SBIR) contract. We align our comments⏎explicitly with the VA Artificial Intelligence Workforce Resources Blueprint (2024) (a publicly available⏎federal strategy that documents the organizational, workforce, governance, and risk-management⏎challenges federal health systems face when adopting AI at scale). [federalregister.gov]⏎DI deploys AI-enabled adaptive content generation systems that support informaticists and clinical-⏎facing operational staff by using multiple machine-learning models and adaptive/automated content⏎generation to produce tailored microlearnings, job aids, and workflow-support materials. While the⏎products are delivered as SaaS, they behave operationally as an AI system requiring:⏎• Data ingestion and structuring⏎• Model-based content generation⏎• AI lifecycle monitoring⏎• Human oversight⏎• Workflow integration⏎Barrier A: Governance & Decision Rights Become Unclear for AI Systems⏎General Barrier for AI Systems⏎The VA Artificial Intelligence (AI) Workforce Resources Blueprint highlights that AI systems require⏎distinct governance roles such as AI model owners, risk stewards, data governance authorities,⏎evaluation leads, and monitoring leads. These roles do not exist in legacy organizational structures.⏎[federalregister.gov]⏎AI systems differ from traditional platforms because they introduce:⏎• Dynamic model behavior⏎• Need for model oversight⏎• Bias/drift monitoring⏎• Interpretability considerations⏎• Risk-tiering⏎• Human-in-the-loop safety requirements⏎Traditional “software owner” roles do not cover these responsibilities.⏎Capability Example⏎Because adaptive content-generation pipelines use dynamic machine-learning models and adaptive⏎content-generation pipelines, their approval required decisions on:⏎⏎• Who evaluates the AI components?⏎• Who owns AI-generated content risk?⏎• Who approves ongoing updates?⏎• Who monitors model performance or drift?⏎Inside VA, no single director or office could claim AI accountability, even when groups could claim other⏎platform responsibility. This created slowdowns and repeat loops through disparate stakeholders. This⏎problem is explicitly predicted by the VA Artificial Intelligence Workforce Resources Blueprint, which⏎notes that AI governance must be cross-functional and cannot be assigned to a single traditional⏎program office.⏎Recommendations to HHS⏎HHS should promote a federal reference architecture for AI governance, including:⏎• Defined AI model owners⏎• Risk-tiered approval requirements⏎• Required roles for evaluation, monitoring, and oversight⏎• Human-in-the-loop governance expectations⏎Barrier B: Funding Misalignment for AI Lifecycle Sustainment⏎General Barrier for AI Systems⏎AI systems are not “build once” tools. The VA Artificial Intelligence Workforce Resources Blueprint⏎emphasizes that agencies must support:⏎• AI model lifecycle management⏎• Ongoing retraining⏎• Validation and re-validation⏎• Monitoring for drift and bias⏎• Workforce upskilling⏎• Continuous model governance⏎Capability Example⏎Operational AI platforms are often funded under mechanisms like SBIR Phase III, but:⏎• SBIR cannot fund model retraining,⏎• Nor AI monitoring,⏎• Nor algorithmic updates,⏎• Nor evaluation frameworks,⏎• Nor responsible-AI governance participation.⏎AI lifecycle tasks were not yet funded because VA had no dedicated AI adoption or sustainment funding⏎mechanism. This is exactly the funding gap the VA Artificial Intelligence Workforce Resources Blueprint⏎identifies. Agencies lack budget categories for AI sustainment, creating “pilot purgatory.”⏎Recommendations to HHS⏎HHS should create or encourage:⏎• Reimbursement codes or programmatic allowances for AI evaluation and monitoring⏎⏎• Payment models that recognize AI lifecycle costs, not just acquisition⏎• Funding definitions that distinguish “software maintenance” from AI sustainment (drift⏎monitoring, retraining, oversight)⏎This ensures health systems can responsibly maintain AI tools.⏎Barrier C: AI Hosting, Security, and Infrastructure Requirements Are Harder Than SaaS⏎General Barrier for AI Systems⏎The VA Artificial Intelligence Workforce Resources Blueprint identifies “AI-enabling infrastructure”⏎(including compute, data platforms, monitoring pipelines, and secure environments for model⏎evaluation) as essential for AI deployment. [federalregister.gov]⏎AI hosting involves factors SaaS does not:⏎• Access to training/evaluation datasets⏎• Secure environments for PHI-based model operation⏎• Integration into monitoring pipelines⏎• Support for retraining workflows⏎• Logging for auditability and oversight⏎Capability Example⏎For cloud-hosted AI systems, choosing between agency-hosted FedRAMP and commercial FedRAMP⏎was not just an IT choice:⏎• Hosting choices determined access to model inputs⏎• They affected whether automated content generation could run in real time⏎• They determined if model-usage logs were available for governance⏎• They dictated alignment with Blueprint requirements for responsible AI lifecycle monitoring⏎This ambiguity slowed implementation and increased governance risk.⏎Recommendations to HHS⏎HHS should publish AI-specific hosting and ATO guidance, answering:⏎• When must AI run in agency-hosted FedRAMP?⏎• When can it run in external FedRAMP environments?⏎• What AI-specific ATO documentation is needed (model cards, drift monitoring plans, evaluation⏎data)?⏎• How should AI logs and audit trails be handled?⏎This clarity would directly accelerate AI adoption across federal health systems.⏎Barrier D: Workforce Readiness Gaps⏎General Barrier for AI Systems⏎The Blueprint defines dozens of AI competencies required across federal healthcare workforces —⏎including:⏎⏎• AI literacy⏎• Understanding model limitations⏎• Ability to interpret AI outputs⏎• Ability to identify bias/drift⏎• Human-AI teaming skills⏎• Evaluation & monitoring competencies [federalregister.gov]⏎These competencies are not required for SaaS, making AI adoption uniquely dependent on workforce⏎readiness.⏎Capability Example⏎Automated, adaptive content generation requires informaticists to understand:⏎• The models’ intended use⏎• The boundaries of automated recommendations⏎• How to confirm accuracy⏎• How to escalate anomalies⏎• How their oversight mitigates risk⏎Traditional training for SaaS would not prepare them for this type of AI oversight.⏎Recommendations to HHS⏎HHS should:⏎• Promote AI workforce competency frameworks consistent with the Blueprint⏎• Support training for interpreting and evaluating AI outputs⏎• Encourage human-in-the-loop safety roles in clinical settings⏎• Fund workforce development aligned to AI risk-tiered use cases⏎Summary of Recommendations Aligned to HHS’s Levers⏎Regulation⏎• Create federal AI governance role templates⏎• Standardize AI risk-tiering and evaluation expectations⏎• Provide AI-specific ATO, hosting, and security requirements⏎Reimbursement⏎• Support funding for AI lifecycle sustainment⏎• Create incentives or pathways that reward AI monitoring, evaluation, and clinician burden⏎reduction⏎Research & Development⏎• Fund implementation science for AI integration⏎• Create AI evaluation toolkits and testbeds⏎• Support research into AI drift, monitoring, and human oversight models⏎⏎Conclusion⏎The challenges DI experienced in deploying AI systems inside VA were not generic software obstacles,⏎but rather the systemic AI adoption barriers documented in the VA AI Workforce Resources Blueprint⏎(2024). [federalregister.gov]⏎These barriers demonstrate why HHS must address governance, workforce readiness, AI-specific⏎infrastructure, and lifecycle sustainment to accelerate safe, effective AI adoption across the U.S. health⏎system.⏎Design Interactive, LLC appreciates the opportunity to provide input and stands ready to support⏎continued collaboration as HHS develops national frameworks for responsible AI in clinical care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0050♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cairns, Ashley, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Cairns♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎and Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT (ONC)⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Washington, DC⏎Ashley Cairns, MBA⏎Founder and Chief Executive Officer⏎Keywell.ai⏎Austin, TX⏎RE: Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎I am pleased to submit this response to HHS’ RFI on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence as part of clinical care. I am the Founder and CEO of Keywell.ai, a tech-enabled⏎services firm that provides analytics and AI solutions in the health and human services sector. Our⏎clients include payers, clinicians and providers, state agencies, and private-sector health care⏎organizations. I have 16 years of experience in healthcare analytics and AI, including leading data⏎and AI modernization efforts for Texas HHSC, California OSI, and national payer and provider⏎organizations.⏎Response to Question 1: Barriers to Adoption⏎The Department correctly identifies that payers bearing financial risk have an inherent incentive to⏎promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions — but translating that incentive into practice⏎requires a governance infrastructure that many regional and mid-market payers are structurally⏎unable to build on their own. Unlike large national carriers with dedicated data science⏎organizations and compliance teams, smaller payers lack the in-house capacity to evaluate AI⏎vendor claims, implement HIPAA-compliant large language model workflows, navigate HTI-1⏎Decision Support Intervention disclosure requirements, or sustain the continuous performance⏎monitoring that responsible AI deployment demands. Payment policy reforms designed to reward⏎value-based AI adoption will accelerate the intended outcomes only if accessible enterprise⏎governance frameworks are available to organizations that cannot amortize these⏎implementation costs across large member populations. HHS should therefore consider incentive⏎structures — such as enhanced risk corridor adjustments or quality bonus provisions — that⏎explicitly account for the disproportionate implementation burden borne by smaller,⏎resource-constrained payers, and should actively support the development of shared governance⏎infrastructure that enables those payers to act on the incentives the Department seeks to create.⏎Response to Question 3: Legal and Implementation Risks⏎Legal and implementation risks in AI adoption are not hypothetical: they represent active⏎compliance burdens that organizations deploying non-device AI in clinical care must navigate,⏎Page 1⏎⏎currently without clear federal guidance. HIPAA's minimum necessary use standard and six-year⏎audit retention requirements impose architectural constraints on LLM context windows and⏎logging pipelines that most general-purpose AI platforms were not designed to accommodate;⏎HTI-1's Decision Support Intervention framework creates classification uncertainty for tools that⏎provide clinical insight without explicit treatment directives; FDA's medical device boundary⏎remains difficult to operationalize for risk stratification and clinical documentation applications;⏎and the 250 health AI-related bills introduced across 34 states in 2025 alone have produced⏎overlapping and sometimes contradictory transparency, prior authorization, and⏎human-in-the-loop mandates at the state level. The Pieces Technologies enforcement action in⏎Texas — which required post-hoc disclosure of error rates, training data sources, and known⏎limitations following overstated performance claims — illustrates that the absence of harmonized⏎federal standards creates both patient safety risk and material litigation exposure. HHS can⏎meaningfully reduce this fragmentation by issuing unified guidance that maps federal AI⏎governance expectations to established frameworks such as the NIST AI Risk Management⏎Framework, HITRUST, ISO 42001, and the CMS AI playbook, giving organizations a credible,⏎auditable compliance pathway and reducing the due diligence overhead that currently deters⏎smaller market participants from deploying clinical AI at all.⏎Response to Question 8: Interoperability⏎The most consequential barrier to scalable clinical AI is not connectivity between systems but⏎semantic consistency within them. AI models trained on claims or electronic health record data⏎that applies different coding conventions, condition hierarchies, and risk stratification logic across⏎organizations will produce inconsistent outputs, a problem that compounds when models are⏎deployed across payer-provider partnerships or multi-state populations. Standardized healthcare⏎ontologies that provide consistent definitions for clinical concepts, cost categories, and quality⏎measures form the necessary semantic foundation for AI tools that can be developed, validated,⏎and deployed reliably across heterogeneous data environments. Federal support for ontology⏎standardization — through ONC's data standards program, CMS value-based care reporting⏎requirements, or ASTP's interoperability rulemaking — would reduce duplicative data engineering⏎work, enable more reproducible AI validation studies, and materially lower barriers to entry⏎healthcare organizations that stand to benefit most from clinical AI adoption but currently lack the⏎data infrastructure to participate.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎/s/ Ashley Cairns⏎Ashley Cairns, MBA⏎Founder and CEO⏎Keywell.ai⏎Page 2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0051♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Patients For Patient Safety US, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patients For Patient Safety US♔♔♔♔♔♔Patients For Patient Safety US⏎Submitted by: Patients for Patient Safety US (PFPS US)⏎Patients for Patient Safety US (PFPS US) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the⏎Department of Health and Human Services’ Request for Information on accelerating the⏎adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) into clinical care.⏎PFPS US is led by patients and family caregivers who have experienced preventable harm in⏎healthcare and are motivated to contribute our lived experience to systemic learning. We work⏎in partnership with clinicians, health systems, and policymakers to make care safer and more⏎trustworthy for all Americans, and to reduce outcomes disparities experienced by vulnerable⏎populations.⏎PFPS US has a special interest in improving diagnostic timeliness and accuracy, as it is the⏎largest opportunity at hand to prevent harm and strengthen the well-being of patients and⏎families. A growing body of research indicates that missed, delayed or inaccurate diagnoses⏎occur more often than all other preventable harms in healthcare combined. More than⏎900,000 Americans die or are seriously harmed annually by such diagnostic failures. (Newman-⏎Toker DE. Just how many diagnostic errors and harms are out there, really? It depends on how⏎you count. BMJ Qual Saf. 2025 May 19;34(6):355-360). The potential cost savings to patients,⏎families, Medicare and Medicaid, and ultimately taxpayers are enormous.⏎Just as healthcare providers are increasingly integrating AI into their operations, so are patients⏎using consumer-facing AI to gather information not provided by our clinicians, identify risks,⏎reconcile gaps or inconsistencies in our medical records, and inform decision-making. In many⏎ways, AI is democratizing patient access to actionable information that is making us better⏎patients, mitigating our risks of harm, and empowering us as individuals to make the decisions⏎that work best for us. From this vantage point, we enthusiastically support responsible⏎innovation in AI. At the same time, we urge HHS to anchor its AI strategy in a clear and shared⏎purpose: reducing preventable patient harm and strengthening the health system’s capacity⏎for continuous learning and improvement.⏎From the patient perspective, the central question is not how quickly AI is adopted, but for⏎what purpose, whose benefit, and with what accountability once it is in use. In health care,⏎adoption follows incentives. Aligning payment and accountability structures with patient⏎safety goals would help ensure that AI is optimized not only for efficiency and throughput,⏎but also for preventing harm and strengthening trust in the technology.⏎____________________⏎Patients For Patient Safety US | 251 Still Pine Bend, Smyrna, Georgia 30082⏎⏎We encourage HHS to view the question of accelerating AI adoption through a simple,⏎patient-centered theory of change:⏎First, meaningful adoption in health care follows meaningful incentives. Health systems,⏎clinicians, and vendors respond to the business case embedded in payment policy,⏎accreditation, and regulatory expectations. Absent clear incentives, patient safety is not⏎prioritized in the value proposition, as manifested in the rollout of electronic health records⏎which introduced significant new risks of patient harm. Even promising technologies with⏎significant potential to prevent harm remain peripheral if the incentives for technology⏎developers are focused on other goals, e.g. cost saving, maximizing revenue, or shifting⏎liability.⏎Second, preventable patient harm remains one of the most costly and under-addressed⏎problems in the health care system—measured not only in hundreds of billions of dollars in⏎wasteful and unnecessary spending, but in avoidable suffering, loss of trust, long-term⏎disability, and healthcare workforce distress. Yet patient safety is still treated as a regulatory⏎obligation rather than a core value proposition of care delivery.⏎Third, AI is uniquely suited to function as a “central nervous system” for patient safety—⏎continuously sensing risk across fragmented data sources, detecting emerging harm and near-⏎misses, and supporting timely corrective action across clinicians and care settings. Few other⏎technologies offer comparable potential to help health systems see and respond to safety⏎failures as they unfold.⏎Fourth, if CMS and HHS meaningfully prioritize patient safety within payment policy and⏎program design, health systems will seek out solutions that help them meet those⏎expectations. In practical terms, this means that patient safety can—and should—become the⏎primary, high-value use case for AI in clinical care. Aligning incentives around harm⏎prevention and detection would immediately change what kinds of AI tools are built, and the⏎speed at which they are purchased, and implemented.⏎Finally, this approach will only succeed if providers can adopt AI with confidence that these⏎tools are themselves safe, accountable, and supported by robust post-deployment learning⏎systems. A laissez-faire approach to oversight and monitoring is unlikely to accelerate⏎adoption. To the contrary, when technologies introduce unmanaged risk or opaque liability,⏎organizations rationally avoid them. Clear expectations for safety, transparency, and⏎continuous learning are therefore not barriers to adoption; they are preconditions for trust.⏎Taken together, these principles point toward a coherent national strategy: make patient⏎safety the central use case for AI in health care, align incentives accordingly, and ensure that⏎AI-enabled safety infrastructure is supported by strong governance and learning systems. This⏎2⏎⏎approach reflects how patients experience harm—and a radical new opportunity to prevent⏎it.⏎In practical terms, this means that accelerating AI adoption will depend less on exhortations⏎to innovate and more on whether payment policy, accountability structures, and safety⏎expectations make patient harm prevention a central business priority for health care⏎organizations.⏎AI as a Central Nervous System for Patient Safety⏎PFPS US encourages HHS to view AI not primarily as a collection of point tools, but as a⏎potential central nervous system (CNS) for patient safety—one that can continuously sense⏎risk, detect emerging harm, learn from near-misses and failures, and support timely corrective⏎action across care settings.⏎Patients and caregivers experience harm longitudinally—across encounters, clinicians, settings,⏎and payers. Unlike claims systems, EHRs, or specialty silos, patients cross boundaries, and are⏎often the first to recognize breakdowns long before those breakdowns appear in claims data or⏎quality reports.⏎AI has unprecedented potential to:⏎• Detect missed diagnoses, delayed follow-up, and care coordination failures⏎• Identify early deterioration and system-level breakdowns⏎• Aggregate patient-reported health care safety concerns/signals at scale⏎Yet today, most AI tools are designed as isolated point solutions, optimized for efficiency or⏎prediction rather than harm prevention and detection.⏎Absent this safety-oriented framing, accelerated AI adoption risks reinforcing fragmentation,⏎obscuring accountability, and scaling existing inequities rather than addressing them.⏎Responses to specific Questions:⏎In the responses that follow, PFPS US addresses those questions most directly related to patient⏎safety and the conditions needed for AI to meaningfully reduce preventable harm in clinical⏎care.⏎3⏎⏎1. Barriers to Innovation and Adoption⏎From a patient safety perspective, the greatest barriers to effective AI innovation and adoption⏎are at least threefold:⏎1. The absence of meaningful incentives to invest in safety infrastructure. Health systems⏎have little reason to absorb the organizational and legal risk associated with early⏎adoption of AI tools that represent potential breakthroughs in patient safety. Where⏎patient safety is treated as a compliance obligation rather than a strategic priority,⏎technologies oriented toward harm prevention and detection are understandably⏎deprioritized.⏎2. Opacity of performance combined with hospital risk aversion. Today, there is no⏎consistent national approach to AI performance and safety monitoring, or identifying,⏎aggregating, or learning from AI-related harm or near-misses. Healthcare organizations⏎are on their own in an environment characterized by diffused and unclear accountability⏎across the developer, vendor, and organizational ecosystem. In this environment,⏎neither providers nor developers are well positioned to innovate safely and with⏎confidence. PFPS US applauds the Joint Commission/CHAI Responsible Use of AI⏎framework and the Patient AI Collaborative’s recently released AI Care Standard for⏎Patient Communication to try to fill the gap; however, these are strictly voluntary⏎guidances. Given the stakes, and given that HHS has stepped back from AI oversight,⏎PFPS US advises that CMS should consider developing an aligned Condition of⏎Participation (CoP) around AI governance that gives providers leverage to compel⏎performance information from developers.⏎3. Archaic, hospital-centric thinking about how health care should be organized. A⏎distributed care model supported by agentic workflow and real teamwork holds⏎enormous promise for better serving rural and other under-resourced areas.⏎2. Payment and Programmatic Policy Changes as a Catalyst for Safety-Oriented AI⏎Payment policy is not merely a financing mechanism; it is one of the strongest signals of what⏎the system values. If patient safety is not meaningfully valued in payment, the tools designed⏎to improve it—AI included—will remain marginal.⏎Today, most patient safety infrastructure—such as tools and processes designed to detect⏎missed diagnoses, delayed follow-up, care coordination failures, or early clinical deterioration—⏎are treated as unreimbursed overhead rather than value-generating assets.⏎4⏎⏎PFPS US encourages HHS and CMS to explore creative payment models that explicitly prioritize⏎patient safety, including approaches that allow organizations to “earn back” value through⏎demonstrated improvements in patient-reported safety experience. Such approaches could⏎meaningfully shift the internal business case for adopting AI tools that serve safety goals.⏎As one illustrative example, PFPS US’s Project PIVOT has explored the introduction of patient⏎safety, diagnostic accuracy/timeliness, and bias-related questions into patient experience⏎surveys, notably the Consumer Assessment of Health Provider Services (CAHPS) surveys used⏎across the U.S. healthcare system. When paired with an earn-back or shared-savings⏎framework, such measures could create a powerful incentive for healthcare organizations to⏎adopt AI-enabled tools that continuously detect safety risks, surface patient-reported harm⏎signals, and support timely corrective action.⏎Importantly, such approaches would not require CMS to mandate specific technologies.⏎Instead, they would allow organizations flexibility to invest in AI and other innovations that best⏎meet patient safety goals—while aligning payment with outcomes that matter most to patients⏎and caregivers.⏎Programmatically, HHS needs a place to aggregate, analyze and disseminate post-deployment⏎learnings. While FDA is the appropriate lifecycle overseer for AI-as-medical-device, AHRQ, as⏎the nation’s healthcare quality and safety agency, should be resourced to steward the⏎implementation and evaluation science.⏎3. Legal, Governance, and Accountability Considerations⏎A prime objective of PFPS US and our network of patient safety advocates is that patient⏎experience of harm, or bias that contributes to harm, is learned from to fuel future prevention.⏎PFPS US therefore urges HHS to consider policy approaches that normalize disclosure and⏎learning, including safe harbors for good-faith reporting of AI failures and near-misses,⏎expectations for auditability and traceability of AI-supported decisions, and prohibition of⏎confidentiality agreements that hide AI safety concerns from being seen and understood. These⏎mechanisms reduce unmanaged risk for providers while making real-world failures visible⏎and correctable. However, in the absence of clear expectations for AI safety, monitoring, and⏎accountability, health care organizations face rational disincentives to adopt tools that could⏎introduce unmanaged liability risk.⏎A laissez-faire approach to oversight is therefore unlikely to accelerate adoption. To the⏎contrary, it risks creating precisely the conditions under which providers avoid new⏎technologies out of fear that failures will be opaque, reputationally damaging, or legally⏎ambiguous.⏎5⏎⏎Diffuse liability, opaque decision provenance, and silent failure modes pose real risks as AI use⏎expands, particularly for tools that influence clinical workflows without being classified as⏎medical devices. Without safe pathways for reporting problems, failures are likely to remain⏎hidden.⏎4. Evaluation Beyond Deployment⏎Evaluation that ends at deployment will not build provider confidence in AI-enabled safety⏎tools. Health systems will only invest in AI as safety infrastructure if they can trust that⏎performance will be monitored, failures surfaced, and learning shared over time.⏎Traditional approaches to AI evaluation in health care—such as pre-deployment validation and⏎periodic audits—are not sufficient for technologies that continuously interact with clinical⏎workflows and evolve over time. From a patient safety perspective, AI tools should be⏎embedded within integrated safety management systems that monitor performance, detect⏎drift, surface near-misses, and enforce design constraints in real-world use. Systems safety⏎frameworks developed in other high-risk industries, such as the System-Theoretic Accident⏎Model and Processes (STAMP), emphasize continuous, system-level monitoring and feedback⏎rather than episodic spot checks. HHS can play an important role in advancing post-deployment⏎evaluation approaches that treat AI safety as an ongoing operational responsibility, supported⏎by routine monitoring, reporting infrastructure, and learning loops embedded in clinical care.⏎6. AI tools with the greatest potential to improve healthcare outcomes⏎From a patient safety perspective, the greatest unrealized potential lies in AI that:⏎• Aggregates and analyzes patient-reported harm signals⏎• Detects process breakdowns (e.g. during transitions), not just discrete clinical findings⏎• Recognizes patterns across encounters⏎• Does automated surveillance for missed follow-up and diagnostic delays⏎• Uses predictive analytics to avert harm events⏎• Extends provider capacity/availability through agentic workflow – i.e.,⏎identifying remote patient care needs in real time and triggering outreach to the⏎appropriate member of the care team to respond.⏎6⏎⏎8. Interoperability for Patient Safety and Learning⏎Interoperability will only accelerate meaningful AI development if it supports the flow of safety-⏎relevant information across fragmented care settings. From the patient perspective, the most⏎consequential gaps are not simply in data exchange, but in the ability to surface and learn from⏎safety signals that cross organizational boundaries—missed or delayed diagnoses, breakdowns⏎in follow-up, adverse events and near-misses, and patient-reported experiences of harm and⏎bias. AI functions as a “central nervous system” for patient safety only when these signals can⏎move across teams, tools, and settings in near real time. Without interoperability that supports⏎safety signal flow and learning, AI risks reinforcing fragmentation rather than helping to correct⏎it.⏎As an example, a consequential interoperability gap is one that could well frustrate the use of⏎AI for a critical use case: improving timeliness and accuracy of diagnosis for patients who move⏎between settings. Right now, researchers cannot reliably track presenting symptoms across⏎care settings. Patients who present to the emergency department with symptoms such as⏎dizziness may be discharged and later admitted with a diagnosis such as stroke, yet the system⏎often captures “what the patient presented with” differently— or not at all —across ED and⏎inpatient workflows. This semantic and longitudinal fragmentation prevents AI tools from⏎linking symptom presentation to subsequent diagnosis, blinding them to patterns of missed or⏎delayed diagnosis across care transitions. Without consistent, computable representation of⏎presenting symptoms and chief complaints across settings, AI cannot function as a safety⏎“central nervous system” for diagnostic error detection at precisely the points of highest⏎risk. To begin to address this problem, we urge ONC to adopt into the USCDI the proposed new⏎Symptom data class and standardized symptom data elements that HL-7 currently is balloting.⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Priorities and Concerns⏎Patients and caregivers consistently express that they want AI to help ensure that things do not⏎fall through the cracks. We want tools that notice when the system is failing—when follow-up⏎does not happen, when conditions worsen, when information is lost between clinicians, or⏎when bias shapes care. This absolutely includes the ability for patients to contribute our own⏎safety experiences as signals to such an AI-powered “central nervous system”. Patients also⏎want safe and reliable AI tools that can empower us and our caregivers with knowledge, insight,⏎and strategies as we engage with payers, providers, and others.⏎Patients worry about opaque decisions that cannot be questioned, AI being used to deny or⏎delay care, and data being extracted without tangible benefit to those whose experiences⏎7⏎⏎generated it. We fear that technology may further distance us from accountability rather than⏎bringing problems to light.⏎Patients also are keenly aware that what health systems prioritize follows what is paid for.⏎When patient safety is not treated as a core value in payment and accountability structures,⏎we reasonably worry that AI will be optimized for efficiency, throughput, utilization control,⏎or short-term revenue margin rather than for harm prevention.⏎10 Research Priorities to Support Safe Adoption⏎PFPS US encourages HHS to prioritize research that supports the safe and trustworthy⏎integration of AI into real-world care, not only the development of new models. From the⏎patient safety perspective, high-impact research priorities include implementation science for⏎safety-oriented AI, approaches to human–AI teaming that reduce cognitive burden rather than⏎shifting risk, and the design of learning systems that incorporate patient-reported safety⏎signals. Investment in these areas would help ensure that AI adoption strengthens, rather than⏎undermines, the health system’s capacity to prevent harm and continuously improve.⏎In closing⏎PFPS US urges HHS to ensure that efforts to incentivize AI adoption accelerate rather than⏎outpace the nation’s ability to detect harm and protect patients. We are confident that AI can⏎earn trust — and ultimately achieve scale — if it is embedded in a patient-centered safety⏎infrastructure that treats harm prevention, transparency, and continuous learning as a core⏎value proposition. Aligning incentives around patient safety, supporting AI as safety⏎infrastructure, and ensuring robust oversight and learning systems are not competing goals —⏎they are mutually reinforcing prerequisites for adoption that earns patient trust.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to comment and welcome continued engagement as HHS⏎advances this important work.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Patients for Patient Safety US⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0052♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Society for Women's Health Research, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Society for Women's Health Research♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Ave. SW⏎Washington, D.C., 20001⏎Comments submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov.⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Secretary Kennedy:⏎The Society for Women’s Health Research (SWHR) is pleased to offer comments in response to the U.S.⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for Information on Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care.⏎Founded in 1990, SWHR is a national nonprofit dedicated to advancing women’s health through science,⏎policy, and education while promoting research on sex differences to optimize women’s health. SWHR is⏎guided by our goal to close the women’s health gap. Women may live longer than men on average, but⏎“women spend 25% more of their lives in debilitating health.”1 Furthermore, women spend $15.4 billion⏎more on out-of-pocket on health care annually – a disparity that is not exclusively due to costs related to⏎maternity care2 – and remain under researched and underprioritized across clinical studies. Women also⏎account for 60% of the unpaid caregiving population, leading to both short and long-term health impacts.3⏎Taken together, these realities highlight the need for exploring innovative ways to approach health care⏎not only for women, but for all Americans.⏎SWHR recognizes that AI holds tremendous, unprecedented promise in transforming the delivery of⏎clinical care. From being used to improve diagnostic methods to helping identify optimal personalized⏎medicines and treatments, AI applications have potential to advance the quality and reliability of medical⏎research, improve the quality of clinical care, and alleviate patient burdens in navigating the health care⏎system. For women in particular, AI could help us better understand the role of sex differences in disease⏎progression and presentation, potentially serving as a tool to improve women's health across the lifespan.⏎SWHR is excited to consider how emerging technologies, such as AI, could improve health care delivery⏎1 World Economic Forum. Closing the Women’s Health Gap: A $1 Trillion Opportunity to Improve Lives and⏎Economies.; 2024. Accessed February 13, 2026. https://www.weforum.org/publications/closing-the-women-s-⏎health-gap-a-1-trillion-opportunity-to-improve-lives-and-economies/⏎2 Deloitte. Hiding in Plain Sight: The Health Care Gender Toll.; 2023. Accessed February 13, 2026.⏎https://www.deloitte.com/us/en/Industries/life-sciences-health-care/articles/womens-health-equity-disparities.html⏎3 Caputo, J., Pavalko, E. K., & Hardy, M. A. (2016). The Long-Term Effects of Caregiving on Women's Health and⏎Mortality. Journal of marriage and the family, 78(5), 1382–1398. https://doi.org/10.1111/jomf.12332⏎⏎across the country in a way that benefits women and communities and, ideally, help to close the women’s⏎health gap.⏎As HHS considers the use of AI in its approaches to regulation, reimbursement, and research and⏎development, SWHR urges the agency to utilize AI as a supplementary tool, preserving human oversight⏎and the ethical implementation of AI across its agencies. Importantly, any AI use must be guided by and⏎rooted in existing evidence as it relates to sex and gender differences and how such factors influence⏎health care delivery and outcomes. This stance was established in our July 2025 comment to the National⏎Institutes of Health RFI NOT-OD-25-117, and we reiterate it in our comment here.⏎Below, we offer comments relevant to two key areas of inquiry posited by the RFI: areas of AI research⏎that HHS should prioritize and challenges within health care that patients and caregivers may wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care.⏎Prioritizing SABV and Gender Considerations⏎SWHR strongly urges that HHS center women's health considerations – including sex as a biological⏎variable (SABV) and social and cultural determinants of health, including gender – into the regulation and⏎application of AI across clinical use.⏎SABV is fundamental to scientific rigor, reliability, and reproducibility. Biological sex is associated with⏎well-evidenced differences in genetics, physiology, endocrinology, and molecular signaling that influence⏎health and disease processes. Males and females exhibit distinct patterns in metabolism, immune function,⏎reproductive biology, neurobiology, cardiovascular physiology, and skeletal regulation, among other⏎systems. These sex-based differences affect disease susceptibility, pathophysiology, clinical presentation,⏎progression, and therapeutic response. Accordingly, integrating SABV into research design and analysis⏎is fundamental to improving clinical care.⏎To illustrate the above, heart disease is one case where the role of SABV is evident. In heart disease, there⏎are key differences in biology, hormones, and physiology, which impact heart disease progression and⏎treatment response. For example, women’s hearts tend to be smaller than men, making women more⏎vulnerable to plaque development. Further, the protective qualities of estrogen can influence women’s⏎susceptibility to heart disease after undergoing menopause.4 These differences mean that women may⏎present with different symptoms than men and therefore, may require a clinical approach that is mindful⏎of how to recognize and treat symptoms.⏎Key biological sex differences are also evident in Alzheimer's disease, autoimmune diseases, and bone⏎diseases, to name a few.⏎If AI models are trained predominantly on male-centered datasets, they may exhibit reduced sensitivity to⏎sex-specific manifestations of disease in women, potentially leading to higher rates of false negatives and⏎misdiagnosis. For example, sex differences in symptom presentation, biomarker profiles, and disease⏎trajectories in cardiovascular disease may be under-recognized when training data lack adequate female⏎representation. Similar risks extend to other conditions that disproportionately affect or present differently⏎4 Betai, D., Ahmed, A. S., Saxena, P., Rashid, H., Patel, H., Shahzadi, A., Mowo-Wale, A. G., & Nazir, Z. (2024).⏎Gender Disparities in Cardiovascular Disease and Their Management: A Review. Cureus, 16(5), e59663.⏎https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.59663⏎⏎in women. Failure to incorporate balanced and representative data may therefore exacerbate diagnostic⏎disparities and compromise clinical accuracy across diverse patient populations.⏎In addition to biological sex, social context plays a significant role in shaping health outcomes. Cultural⏎expectations, social roles, and lived experiences associated with being male or female influence risk⏎exposure, access to care, health behaviors, and interactions within clinical settings.5 Because these⏎influences evolve over time and can vary across cultural contexts, these dynamics should be accounted for⏎alongside biological factors in clinical care.⏎In summation, systematic evaluation and use of preclinical and clinical research that is aided by AI can⏎help identify whether biological and gender-related factors have been appropriately integrated, while also⏎detecting gaps such as underrepresentation, mischaracterization of sex differences, or implicit gender⏎bias. As such, this information can be utilized to inform more precise and patient-centered health care⏎delivery.⏎Centering Needs of Patients and Caregivers in Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎Biological sex differences, gender roles, and the risk of biased or incomplete datasets are all challenges⏎that will need to be addressed in order for AI to improve the experiences of women in the clinical setting.⏎One 2023 study evaluating the records of more than 200 million Americans with more than 112 chronic⏎diseases found that "women experience significantly longer diagnostic delays compared to men.”6 If AI⏎systems could explicitly incorporate SABV and be trained on balanced datasets, it could reduce false⏎negatives, minimize diagnostic delay, and better capture atypical presentations.⏎AI could also improve existing gender biases in care delivery. Gender norms may influence symptom⏎reporting, pain assessment, and clinician interpretation, sometimes resulting in minimization or⏎misattribution of women’s symptoms, such as in the area of endometriosis. Endometriosis – a condition⏎which endometrial tissue grows outside the uterus and can lead to irregular, heavy, and even painful⏎menstruation; diarrhea or constipation; notable fatigue; and, for some, infertility – is under-researched and⏎under-recognized in clinical care. A woman’s reported pain might be overlooked or disregarded entirely⏎by a provider. AI-driven audits of clinical data could help identify patterns of undertreatment, delayed⏎referral, or differential workup, thereby supporting quality improvement and accountability.⏎Conclusion⏎In implementing a strategy to accelerate the adoption and use of AI in clinical care under HHS leadership,⏎SWHR urges the department to prioritize research and clinical considerations with respect to sex and⏎gender. Women account for more than half of the U.S. population and are responsible for 80% of health⏎care and medical decisions made within families. Yet women bear an undeserved burden of disparities⏎when it comes to disease prevalence, treatment, and access to quality and evidence-based clinical care. AI⏎has the potential to bridge some of these gaps to improve not only the health of all women but the health⏎of all Americans. However, AI’s implementation must be measured and scrutinized to ensure that it relies⏎on scientifically-sound evidence, and human oversight – especially in clinical application and care – must⏎remain paramount.⏎5 Mollborn, S., Lawrence, E. M., & Hummer, R. A. (2020). A gender framework for understanding health⏎lifestyles. Social science & medicine (1982), 265, 113182. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.socscimed.2020.113182⏎6 Sun, T. Y., Hardin, J., Nieva, H. R., Natarajan, K., Cheng, R. F., Ryan, P., & Elhadad, N. (2023). Large-scale⏎characterization of gender differences in diagnosis prevalence and time to diagnosis. medRxiv : the preprint server⏎for health sciences, 2023.10.12.23296976. https://doi.org/10.1101/2023.10.12.23296976⏎⏎Thank you for your time and consideration of these comments. We hope that HHS will take all necessary⏎steps to ensure that women’s unique health needs across lifespan are accounted for as it considers the⏎application of these emergent technologies.⏎Please do not hesitate to reach out to me directly at Kathryn@swhr.org with any questions.⏎Sincerely,⏎Kathryn Schubert, MPP, CAE⏎President and CEO⏎Society for Women’s Health Research♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0053♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from PINC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔PINC♔♔♔♔♔♔If anything, AI development should be slowed and research and ethical guidelines for use, in particular with vulnerable populations such as those suffering mental illness, addiction or the aftereffects of trauma.  To accelerate in this sector is unethical and unwise.  There are many examples of unnecessary AI inspired suicides, hallucinations, and misapplications in mental health.  We urge control and a reliance on human mental health specialists until these issues are addressed if they ever can be.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0054♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Abrams, Amir, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amir♔Abrams♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment on HHS Health Sector AI RFI (ASTP/ONC)⏎Patient-Designated Longitudinal Routing to Enable Safe, Effective Clinical AI⏎Submitted by: Amir Abrams⏎Affiliation: Software Architect; former Director of Medical Informatics, LSU Health Sciences⏎Center⏎Docket/Document: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎Submission Type: Written comment (public)⏎Contact: abramsamir@gmail.com⏎Executive Summary⏎The RFI correctly identifies that clinical AI adoption is constrained less by model capability than⏎by whether care settings can reliably supply the longitudinal, high-fidelity context needed for⏎safe inference. This comment proposes a structural solution: patient-designated longitudinal⏎routing by default.⏎Core Proposal: Enable a one-time patient designation of a certified longitudinal endpoint to⏎which full-fidelity USCDI data is automatically delivered as it is created, across care settings.⏎This is not a consumer portal strategy. It is a convergence layer that makes complete longitudinal⏎records reliably available back to care delivery systems (with consent) where AI actually runs.⏎Why This Matters for AI: Clinical AI fails when deployed into informationally impoverished⏎workflows. Patient-designated routing changes the default so that longitudinal completeness⏎becomes the expected input condition for AI, rather than a special-case integration achievement.⏎Key Recommendations:⏎1. Regulation: Define and certify patient-designated longitudinal endpoints with strict⏎security, consent, provenance, and audit requirements.⏎2. Reimbursement: Pilot payment models tied to verified delivery rather than capability⏎attestations.⏎3. R&D: Fund evaluations that connect longitudinal completeness improvements to clinical⏎outcomes, workflow impact, and AI safety/bias monitoring.⏎This is a narrow structural change with outsized impact: it improves care coordination⏎immediately, reduces preventable harm, and creates the data conditions necessary for safe,⏎effective, and equitable clinical AI.⏎1. Introduction: Why I'm Commenting / Relevant Experience⏎⏎I am submitting this comment because the RFI correctly identifies a core problem: clinical AI⏎adoption is constrained less by model capability than by whether the care setting can reliably⏎supply the longitudinal, high-fidelity context needed for safe and clinically useful inference.⏎My perspective comes from building and operating clinical data infrastructure in real healthcare⏎delivery environments, including clinical data warehouses, integration pipelines, and⏎point-of-care systems used by clinicians under real time constraints. In those settings, the central⏎failure mode is not "no standards exist," but "the record is incomplete when and where clinical⏎decisions are made." AI embedded into workflows inherits that incompleteness, which directly⏎degrades safety, quality, and equity.⏎2. Background: The Problem the RFI is Trying to Solve⏎2.1 Fragmentation, not standards maturity, is the binding constraint⏎Despite FHIR/USCDI maturity, national exchange efforts, and the shift toward API-based⏎access, longitudinal aggregation remains the exception rather than the norm. The operational⏎reality is that records are frequently split across hospitals, specialist groups, labs, urgent care,⏎imaging centers, behavioral health providers, and post-acute settings. Clinicians often have an⏎incomplete view of diagnoses, medications, prior workups, allergies, procedures, problem lists,⏎and results performed elsewhere.⏎For clinical AI, this is not a "nice-to-have." Longitudinal completeness is the substrate for:⏎accurate clinical context and risk stratification; reduction of false positives/false negatives that⏎arise from missing history; bias mitigation (because missingness is not random and often⏎correlates with socioeconomic access patterns); and trustworthy post-deployment monitoring⏎(drift, subgroup performance, safety event correlation).⏎2.2 Why fragmentation persists: routing control is mis-assigned⏎The underlying structural issue is that routing decisions are typically controlled by institutions⏎and vendors, not by patients. Even when standards and exchange exist, the default routing⏎behavior tends to produce episodic exchange, partial aggregation, and brittle "islands" of data⏎that do not converge into a reliable longitudinal record accessible at the point of care.⏎Stated differently: interoperability capability does not guarantee longitudinal completeness,⏎because the system's routing defaults do not reliably cause data to converge.⏎3. Core Proposal: Patient-Designated Longitudinal Routing by Default⏎3.1 One structural change⏎HHS can unlock meaningful clinical AI adoption by enabling patient-designated routing by⏎default: a one-time patient designation of a certified longitudinal endpoint to which full-fidelity⏎USCDI data is automatically delivered as it is created, across care settings.⏎Key properties:⏎⏎4. One-time designation (like choosing a primary pharmacy or selecting a health plan PCP)⏎5. Automatic delivery of standardized clinical payloads (USCDI-aligned) as events occur⏎6. Persistent routing via a record-locator mechanism so the designation "follows" the⏎patient⏎7. No patient management burden (no requirement that patients log in, curate, or actively⏎manage data)⏎8. Return-to-care enablement: treating systems can retrieve a current longitudinal view⏎with patient consent, supporting clinician workflow and embedded AI⏎3.2 What this is not⏎This is not "a consumer portal strategy" and not a burden-shift to patients. The endpoint is not⏎primarily for human browsing or personal recordkeeping. It is a convergence layer intended to⏎make a complete, current longitudinal record reliably available back to care delivery systems⏎(with consent) where AI actually runs.⏎3.3 Why this solves the AI adoption bottleneck⏎Clinical AI fails in practice when deployed into workflows that are informationally⏎impoverished. Patient-designated routing changes the default so that longitudinal completeness⏎becomes the expected input condition for AI, rather than a special-case integration⏎achievement.⏎This is the smallest structural shift that: improves patient safety and care coordination⏎immediately (even without AI); creates the precondition for AI systems to be reliable and⏎monitorable; and aligns incentives in a way enforcement alone has not achieved.⏎4. Analysis: Impacts, Unintended Consequences, and Why HHS Should Prefer⏎This Approach⏎4.1 Benefits to clinical care (even before AI)⏎A persistent longitudinal record reduces: duplicated labs and imaging ordered because prior⏎results are unavailable; medication reconciliation errors (a major source of preventable harm);⏎delays in diagnosis due to missing prior workup and specialist notes; avoidable⏎admissions/readmissions driven by incomplete outpatient history; and clinician time lost hunting⏎for records across portals and faxes. These improvements are direct clinical quality benefits that⏎also improve AI readiness.⏎4.2 Why incentives matter more than capability attestations⏎Interoperability has often been framed as "build capability" + "enforce compliance." That⏎approach does not reliably create longitudinal completeness because it does not make⏎completeness an operational default that organizations are paid to produce.⏎⏎If reimbursement is conditioned on verified delivery to a patient-designated endpoint (not mere⏎"capability to deliver"), interoperability shifts from an enforcement problem to an operational⏎norm. This is a durable behavioral change lever.⏎4.3 Distributional impacts (equity)⏎Fragmentation and missingness are not evenly distributed. People who receive care across⏎multiple systems (often due to access patterns, insurance churn, transportation constraints, or⏎regional provider availability) are disproportionately harmed by non-convergent records. These⏎same populations are at higher risk of AI underperformance when missingness becomes a hidden⏎input variable correlated with disadvantage.⏎Patient-designated routing improves equity by making longitudinal completeness a default⏎expectation across sites rather than a privilege of being "in the right health system."⏎4.4 Semantic interoperability is a real risk—and a necessary forcing function⏎Implementing default longitudinal routing will reveal known weaknesses in semantic⏎consistency: coding variation, normalization drift, incomplete reconciliation of problem lists, and⏎differing local interpretations. These issues exist today; episodic exchange often masks them.⏎This should be treated as a forcing function rather than a reason to avoid reform. AI systems⏎amplify semantic inconsistency risks; therefore, operationalizing longitudinal aggregation is the⏎mechanism that makes semantic quality measurable, improvable, and tied to downstream⏎usability.⏎4.5 Privacy, security, and trust: the "central objection" and how to mitigate it⏎A convergence endpoint increases the perceived "blast radius" of a breach if designed poorly.⏎That concern is legitimate and should be addressed explicitly in the program design.⏎Mitigations that can be required through certification and policy guardrails:⏎• Consent + revocation: simple, durable patient consent workflows; immediate revocation⏎capability⏎• Strong provenance + auditability: comprehensive audit logs; patient-accessible "who⏎accessed what and when"⏎• Minimum necessary retrieval: retrieval into treating systems should be scoped;⏎discourage uncontrolled bulk retrieval except when clinically justified⏎• Segmentation & sensitive domains: phased inclusion for highly sensitive categories;⏎require clear labeling and provenance for sensitive information⏎• Security baseline: modern authentication/authorization, encryption in transit/at rest,⏎continuous monitoring, and incident response controls commensurate with handling⏎longitudinal clinical data⏎A default routing policy must be paired with a certification program that makes "safe⏎aggregation" non-negotiable.⏎⏎5. Recommended HHS Actions Using the RFI Levers⏎(Explicitly aligned to the RFI's emphasis on regulation, reimbursement, and R&D.)⏎5.1 Regulation: Define and certify "longitudinal endpoints" and routing behavior⏎HHS/ASTP/ONC should define certification criteria for longitudinal endpoints and for EHR⏎behaviors required to support patient designation and delivery.⏎Certification should require longitudinal endpoints to:⏎9. Accept complete USCDI-aligned payloads (full-fidelity, not lossy summaries)⏎10. Support standardized query and return to treating systems with patient consent⏎11. Provide robust provenance, versioning, and audit logs⏎12. Enforce strong security controls and clear operational responsibilities⏎13. Implement consent and revocation mechanisms appropriate for clinical workflows⏎14. Support resilience and availability expectations appropriate for point-of-care retrieval⏎Certification should require sending systems to:⏎15. Support a patient-designated routing instruction⏎16. Automatically deliver standardized payloads to that endpoint (subject to consent and⏎policy)⏎17. Support reliable event-based update flows (not just occasional exports)⏎18. Provide delivery verification (receipt + integrity checks)⏎This does not require redefining USCDI or replacing FHIR; it changes the default routing⏎behavior and makes longitudinal convergence reliable.⏎5.2 Persistent routing: record locator mechanism⏎HHS should establish or enable a persistent routing pointer (record-locator-like) so a patient's⏎designation is discoverable across care settings. That pointer should be patient-controlled,⏎privacy-preserving, and designed to avoid enabling unauthorized tracking.⏎Conceptually: the routing pointer says "where the longitudinal record lives," not "what is in it."⏎Access to the content remains consent-governed and auditable.⏎5.3 Reimbursement: tie payment to verified delivery and usability (critical)⏎Regulation alone will not produce durable change. HHS should pilot reimbursement models⏎where the measure is successful delivery (and, over time, usability) rather than capability⏎attestation.⏎Potential models:⏎• Infrastructure payments for certified longitudinal endpoints with pricing guardrails⏎⏎• Quality / value-based measures tied to verified delivery performance (completeness +⏎latency)⏎• Incentives for treating systems that demonstrate retrieval use in workflows (without⏎penalizing patients)⏎CMS can directly influence Medicare/Medicaid. Commercial alignment can be approached via⏎demonstrations that show ROI and reduced waste through fewer duplicates and improved⏎coordination.⏎5.4 R&D and pilots: fund implementation science, not just model research⏎HHS should sponsor pilots that evaluate:⏎• Completeness: USCDI coverage over time vs expected sources⏎• Latency: time from data creation to endpoint availability⏎• Clinical outcomes proxies: duplicate test reduction, medication discrepancy reduction,⏎preventable adverse events⏎• Operational burden: net clinician time saved vs added workflows⏎• AI safety and bias: subgroup performance changes when longitudinal completeness⏎improves⏎• Total cost of ownership: endpoint ops, integration costs, and security compliance⏎Study design suggestion: stepped-wedge rollout across sites or regions to enable causal⏎inference without requiring all participants to switch simultaneously.⏎6. Addressing Likely Counterarguments⏎Counterargument A: "This is too hard / too expensive"⏎The current model externalizes costs into duplicated testing, preventable errors, and⏎administrative burden (fax, portal chasing, manual reconciliation). Patient-designated routing⏎consolidates infrastructure investment into a measurable, reusable utility. By tying⏎reimbursement to verified delivery and using certification to reduce bespoke integrations, the⏎system becomes cheaper over time than the current patchwork of one-off interfaces.⏎Counterargument B: "Patients won't participate"⏎Patients already make durable healthcare designations (insurance plans, pharmacies, PCPs,⏎consent forms). The program can be designed so designation occurs at registration, enrollment,⏎or during standard administrative encounters, with minimal friction. Critically: patients do not⏎need to "use an app" for this to work.⏎Counterargument C: "Privacy risk is unacceptable"⏎The risk is unacceptable only if the endpoint lacks rigorous controls. This is precisely why⏎certification, auditability, consent, and security baselines must be central requirements. A poorly⏎secured fragmented environment is not inherently safer than a well-governed convergence⏎model; it is simply less visible and less accountable.⏎⏎7. Specific Requests to HHS (Recap as Actionable Items)⏎19. Define and certify patient-designated longitudinal endpoints with strict security, consent,⏎provenance, and audit requirements⏎20. Require support in certified health IT for patient designation and automated delivery⏎of standardized USCDI-aligned data to the designated endpoint⏎21. Establish a persistent routing pointer (record-locator-like) so designations follow⏎patients across care settings⏎22. Pilot reimbursement models tied to verified delivery (and later, usability) rather than⏎capability attestations⏎23. Fund evaluations that connect longitudinal completeness improvements to clinical⏎outcomes, workflow impact, and AI safety/bias monitoring⏎8. Conclusion⏎Clinical AI cannot reliably succeed without longitudinal completeness available in real⏎workflows. The most direct way to achieve that completeness is to transfer routing control—by⏎default—from institutions to patients through a one-time designation of a certified longitudinal⏎endpoint, paired with persistent routing and strong consent/audit safeguards.⏎This is a narrow structural change with outsized impact: it improves care coordination⏎immediately, reduces preventable harm, and creates the data conditions necessary for safe,⏎effective, and equitable clinical AI.⏎References⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI (ASTP/ONC), Docket HHS-ONC-2026-0001:⏎https://www.regulations.gov/document/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎Administrative Procedure Act (public comment process context): 5 U.S.C. § 553♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0055♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Hernandez♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔National Partnership for Women and Families♔♔♔♔♔♔The framing of this RFI frustrates me in a way I want to try to articulate clearly, because I think it's doing something that deserves pushback before we even get to the specific questions.⏎⏎HHS is asking how to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical settings, and embedded in that word is an assumption that speed is the right goal. The question I keep returning to is: accelerating for whose benefit? Because when I look at where AI is actually being deployed in health systems right now, the pattern is pretty consistent. It's being used to increase patient throughput, to flag claims for denial faster, and to monitor how long nurses spend at bedsides. Those are labor productivity and cost-extraction applications. They are not, in any meaningful sense, burnout reduction tools for clinicians. The framing in this RFI that AI adoption is about helping overworked clinicians is, I think, sincerely misleading, and I say that as someone who works in advocacy and spends a lot of time reading the more carefully worded version of these arguments.⏎⏎Our organization works closely with the Psychotherapy Action Network, and data they collected from roughly 1,200 practitioners in 2023 showed that 68% had encountered patients who expressed real concern about how mental health data might be used by automated systems. And what was striking wasn't just the number, it was who was opting out. Patients from lower-income backgrounds and communities of color were substantially more likely to decline digital intake processes when any algorithmic involvement was disclosed to them. That's not a consent form design problem. That's a structural trust problem that you will not fix by updating a notice-and-access requirement.⏎⏎I want to focus on one specific thing in this RFI, which is the question about reimbursement incentives and what role Medicare and Medicaid payment policy should play in accelerating adoption. My concern here is that tying reimbursement to AI use creates an adoption mandate in practice even when it's framed as optional. Hospitals and health systems operating on thin margins will read financial incentives as pressure, and they will respond by deploying whatever tools qualify for those incentives fastest. The vendors who benefit from this are already well-capitalized. The workers who absorb the downside are disproportionately women and workers of color in administrative, intake, and support roles that AI tools specifically target for automation.⏎⏎There's something odd about HHS framing this as a health equity opportunity when the labor market effects of the automation being discussed will land hardest on the same demographic groups the Department says it wants to protect as patients. I'm not sure those two things can be resolved by adding an equity consideration to an otherwise unchanged incentive structure.⏎⏎I'd also flag that the "clinician override" question is more complicated than it looks. The 2024 convening where the Psychotherapy Action Network presented its findings included a pretty frank discussion of how algorithmic recommendations function in practice in under-resourced settings. Clinicians technically retain override authority in most frameworks. But when a system flags a treatment as outside protocol and that flag is tied to billing or quality metrics, the override is nominal. The institutional pressure is real and it runs in one direction.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require, before any reimbursement incentive attaches to AI tools in clinical settings, independent audits of labor impact disaggregated by race and gender of the workforce affected, and separate audits of patient-facing outcomes disaggregated the same way. Not self-reported vendor data. Independent. And those audits should be public, not submitted to the agency and then summarized in a guidance document two years later.⏎⏎I recognize that's a harder ask than putting together a voluntary framework with an equity checkbox. But the softer version of this has been tried repeatedly and it does not produce accountability, it produces documentation of stated intentions.⏎⏎One more thing I keep thinking about: the RFI mentions reducing administrative burden several times as a rationale. I want to know specifically what that means when a hospital system automates prior authorization review. Because from where I sit, the administrative burden being reduced is a job, and that job is held by a person, and that person is statistically likely to be a woman of color working in a mid-size hospital in a state without strong labor protections. "Burden reduction" is a real thing in the aggregate but it is not evenly distributed and the RFI doesn't grapple with that at all.⏎⏎Thomas Hernandez⏎Research Director, National Partnership for Women and Families⏎Concord, NH♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0056♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ScriptChain Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ScriptChain Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Regulation⏎Answer:⏎I believe that AI in healthcare should be very close to unregulated from the federal government.⏎For decades, the government has put a fear in institutions and the US population thinking that if⏎private companies get access to their data that they will do harm to them which is very⏎inaccurate. The only form of regulation that should be pushed is the evaluation of AI models and⏎how well they can improve the clinical setting. Healthcare systems and plans should be⏎incentivized and be prompted to use AI in the clinical setting as it will help improve patient⏎outcomes, reduce cost, and improve access to care for patients in rural areas. Data should be⏎shared with private companies so models can improve according to their patient population. The⏎data should be stored and accessed through a secure network to a select number of companies⏎however it should not be held back. Holding back data is what partially inhibits the use of AI in⏎the clinical setting. When AI products get evaluated, they might not be up to par with what⏎clinicians want the tool to be so they end up not procuring the product and deploying it to the⏎clinical work environment. I believe health systems should be told that they must use AI⏎products in the clinical setting within the next 3 years to push adoption but to also let them know⏎that the cost of AI will be greater in the short term but beneficial in the long run.⏎Reimbursement⏎Answer:⏎As a strong proponent of AI adoption in clinical settings, I believe HHS has a critical opportunity⏎to modernize payment policies that will unlock transformative improvements in patient care,⏎outcomes, and system efficiency. The current fee-for-service framework's inherent rigidity and⏎slow adaptation to innovation creates barriers that prevent healthcare providers from accessing⏎and implementing high-value AI tools that could enhance diagnostic accuracy, personalize⏎treatment plans, reduce medical errors, and ultimately save lives. By reforming reimbursement⏎structures to incentivize value-based care and creating clear, expedited pathways for coverage of⏎validated AI clinical interventions, HHS can ensure that payment policies actively⏎promote—rather than hinder—the integration of cutting-edge technologies that improve patient⏎outcomes while controlling costs. I urge HHS to establish flexible, outcomes-oriented payment⏎⏎models that reward providers for utilizing AI tools that demonstrably enhance care quality, create⏎transparent evaluation criteria for AI clinical applications that enable faster coverage decisions,⏎and foster a competitive marketplace where innovation thrives and patients benefit from rapid⏎access to the most effective AI-powered diagnostic and therapeutic solutions. The healthcare⏎system's future depends on our ability to embrace these technologies now, and payment policy⏎reform is the essential catalyst for widespread, equitable AI adoption that will benefit all⏎Americans.⏎Research & Development⏎Answer:⏎The most impactful investments HHS can make are in establishing clear, expedited approval⏎frameworks for AI clinical tools, creating standardized data interoperability requirements that⏎enable private sector innovation, and funding open-access datasets and benchmarking platforms⏎that level the playing field for startups and established companies alike—while allowing the⏎competitive market to drive development, deployment, and iteration at the speed healthcare⏎demands. Have a quota that the department must meet per year for private and public sector⏎companies to join forces and receive grants to prioritize research and commercialization. The⏎amount of startups receiving the funding should be great amount and should align with HHS⏎mission for creating Food as Medicine as a preventative approach to medicine and health⏎outcomes. Most of R&D funding has gone to non profits and academic institutions which⏎yields poor results. There needs to be a strong alliance between the private sector and⏎public sector with an accelerated approval process instead of waiting 9-12 months to know⏎if you are approved. Partner with security based companies to help companies create an⏎encrypted infrastructure for easy deployment and receiving security certifications. This initiative⏎should treat the R&D as a form of program for early stage companies that have a high⏎probability of commercialization and should create a clear path to long term contracts for use in⏎the clinical setting.⏎⏎Additional Questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎A: Use of patient data, funding/reimbursement, and the lack of proper process that healthcare⏎systems should follow on evaluating AI models. The approval process for AI models is also⏎extremely slow between 12-18 months before you can start any partnership which should not be⏎the case if in the private sector.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎A: Every single act that has been implemented or in the process of approval including the⏎Obesity Act should include the use of advanced technologies in the bill for reimbursement. The⏎Personal Health Investment Act should include healthier lives and lifestyles by using advanced⏎technologies like Agentic AI and medically tailored meals for that act.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures ( e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎A: I believe HHS should have a preferred list of health plans and systems that would be more⏎than willing to adopt AI products if we are a private company on the list of preferred AI products⏎that HHS has approved and reviewed. If there is an accelerated pathway that HHS can create to⏎have vendors and buyers adopt the technology, that can help improve the adoption, deployment⏎and contract agreements between sectors. I also believe if we can standardize the process and⏎have EMR systems adopt the changes, that would help with deployment, fitting into the clinical⏎workflows. If there could be a standard contract agreement created for health systems, plans, and⏎⏎vendors, that can reduce the amount of attorney fees, back and forth between multiple⏎departments, and accelerate adoption of devices.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce⏎costs?⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎A: Some of the primary hurdles for AI in clinical care are that the smaller institutions do now⏎have a process put in place to adopt in general. The smaller institutions do not know how to⏎move forward with clinical AI, how to start or what policy they should follow. On the other⏎hand, the larger institutions are overly bureaucratic in making decisions. Larger institutions tend⏎to have several layers of red tap on adopting any form of clinical decision making for AI⏎adoption, the expectations are incredibly high by wanting 7 figures in revenue with our⏎customers, 18 months to make a decision because of the committees. If there can be an open⏎department that is specifically used for AI adoption and evaluation throughout the entire⏎healthcare system, that would decrease the amount of hurdles to go to market. They usually have⏎people at the bottom of command try to send you to the next department and figure out who⏎would be a good fit for the institution. I also believe that if Health Plans and Health Systems⏎published the top 10 needs of their specific hospitals by using advanced technology just like Y⏎Combinator does, that would be extremely helpful.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎A: I would say accelerated care and waitlists to nutritional coaching. At Kaiser Permanente,⏎patients have said that getting into the nutrition programs are very difficult to get into, and the⏎waitlist can take anywhere from 3-12 months. At that rate, you are closer to your deathbed than⏎anything else. Getting into holistic preventative medicine is better for patient outcomes so⏎investing heavily into nutritional devices, technologies should give healthcare reimbursement for⏎providers to help incentivize them in using advanced technologies to reduce obesity, Heart⏎Disease, Type 2 diabetes and pre diabetes.⏎⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎A: Food is Medicine! Lifestyle interventions should be focused on and should not be regulated if⏎you are providing nutrition and exercise plans for patients to follow.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in clinical⏎care?⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0735109723083171⏎https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35129822/⏎https://www.tandfonline.com/doi/10.1080/03007995.2020.1787971?url_ver=Z39.88-2003&rfr_i⏎d=ori:rid:crossref.org&rfr_dat=cr_pub%20%200pubmed#abstract⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0057♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American College of Cardiology, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Cardiology♔♔♔♔♔♔February 18, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The American College of Cardiology (ACC) is the global leader in transforming cardiovascular care⏎and improving heart health for all. As the preeminent source of professional medical education for⏎the entire cardiovascular care team since 1949, ACC credentials cardiovascular professionals in over⏎140 countries who meet stringent qualifications and leads in the formation of health policy,⏎standards and guidelines. Through its world-renowned family of JACC Journals, NCDR registries,⏎ACC Accreditation Services, global network of Member Sections, CardioSmart patient resources and⏎more, the College is committed to ensuring a world where science, knowledge and innovation⏎optimize patient care and outcomes.⏎In this request for information (RFI) that follows others by agencies within the Department of⏎Health and Human Services (HHS), HHS seeks public feedback identifying ways regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development can accelerate the adoption of AI in clinical care. The⏎ACC appreciates this opportunity to provide feedback and offers comments accordingly.⏎Ensuring Clinician Autonomy⏎AI-enabled systems provide well-established benefits by leveraging computing power to enhance⏎patient care. However, the deployment of new tools also comes with great risks that could just as⏎easily put patients in harm’s way and threaten the clinician-patient relationship that has been the⏎central tenet of medicine.⏎Currently the US healthcare system does not have the infrastructure to support autonomous AI.⏎Therefore, the systems established for implementation in the near future are best based in assistive⏎AI or collaborative intelligence, with the clinician as the responsible entity, using AI as a tool. AI⏎systems are inherently trained on large datasets, deriving insights from patterns, averages, and⏎variations across populations. While this approach can uncover meaningful trends, it also risks⏎⏎reducing patients to data points rather than individuals with unique contexts and needs. Diagnostic⏎tests or genetic underpinnings where an AI application contributes and enhances patient care are⏎best utilized in coordination with and under the clinical acumen and reasoning of trained clinicians.⏎While the College is acutely aware AI capabilities will continue to evolve, it is essential that any⏎policy frameworks must reaffirm that clinicians—not algorithms—remain at the center of patient⏎care. The current lack of clarity or interpretative guidance from federal agencies on existing policies⏎threatens this autonomy as developers work to deploy systems with the promise of reducing⏎administrative burdens and costs associated with healthcare clinicians and personnel.⏎The ACC strongly believes that clinical decision making should still lie in the hands of the clinician⏎and the patient, deciding together the best course of action based on what is best for the patient and⏎the evidence available at the time. While AI is a powerful tool, it serves to support clinical decision-⏎making, not replace it. While population-level data remains a foundation, modern AI—through⏎personalization and long context windows—has the unique ability to tailor care based on specific⏎user preferences, social determinants of health (SDOH), and individual goals. Systems established⏎for implementation should support collaborative intelligence. Rather than being limited to⏎population averages, AI can help clinicians uncover unique contexts and needs that might otherwise⏎be overlooked in a traditional clinical setting. Clinicians are essential to interpret, adapt, and⏎individualize these insights to ensure that the art and nuance of medicine are preserved. AI's role is⏎to enhance care while preventing unnecessary treatment. The starting point for such decisions must⏎always be the individual patient. The College believes AI can play an active role in helping clinicians⏎explore options available to a patient by presenting information and serving as a tool adjunctive to⏎clinician decision making, but it is important to ensure that clinician autonomy is not overridden,⏎and software does not supplant the clinician-patient relationship.⏎Evolving Standards of Care and Liability⏎It is important that any policy framework should clearly and unequivocally ensure that AI/ML-⏎driven solutions are not considered or presumed as the new “standard of care” in any way. There is⏎great uncertainty on the potential risks and liability a clinician faces should they deviate from⏎recommended AI-enabled software outputs, whether autonomous or augmentative. While many⏎government agencies may not establish liability criteria, reiterating that these are simply tools that are⏎used to inform the decision-making process, rather than dictating it, will help ensure that clinicians⏎remain in charge of a patient’s care. By taking these actions, the government can clarify the role of⏎clinicians and tools such as AI to maintain the needed trust in the healthcare system.⏎For those health AI tools specifically designed and validated to be autonomous, industry precedents⏎demonstrate that autonomous AI can safely operate within the healthcare ecosystem; for instance,⏎companies like IDx-DR (LumineticsCore, for diabetic retinopathy detection) and Doctronic (for⏎automated prescription refill pilot in Utah) have successfully utilized liability insurance to protect⏎against harm. The government should encourage a framework where developers can assume liability⏎for specific medical diagnosis, treatment, and management use cases, thereby lowering the barrier⏎for clinical adoption.⏎⏎Privacy and Cybersecurity⏎While federal agencies, including those HHS, have made progress to advance cybersecurity in⏎healthcare, it is important that agencies build on this momentum and develop a clear and consistent⏎baseline for data privacy and cybersecurity in healthcare AI. Agencies must develop clear regulations,⏎frameworks and guidance that place these obligations on the entities that build and deploy AI tools,⏎not just on individual clinicians. This includes clear guidance to regulated entities on the privacy and⏎cybersecurity requirements for AI tools and suggested best practices for non-regulated developers,⏎aligned with one another to ensure consistency across the healthcare system.⏎As technology rapidly advances, existing privacy and cybersecurity regulations and guidance,⏎including those like HIPAA overseen by the Office for Civil Rights (OCR) or frameworks⏎developed by the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST), must account for new age⏎technology and threats. The advent of quantum computing and advanced computational techniques⏎threatens to nullify existing privacy and security techniques required or suggested by federal⏎agencies. Therefore, it is essential that these agencies develop policies that adequately ensure that⏎information, often highly sensitive in nature, can be sufficiently protected.⏎Flexibility⏎It is important that regulatory agencies draft policies and guidance that are flexible enough to⏎distinguish between AI-enabled health care applications that actively participate in the practice of⏎medicine, either defined by the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (FD&C Act) and the 21st⏎Century Cures Act, and more broadly designed AI systems that assist with practice of medicine⏎through administrative simplification or other essential tasks. Future AI systems may blur the line⏎between regulated and non-regulated activities. Any actions should allow agencies sufficient⏎regulatory flexibility to provide clear guidance to clinicians, developers, and stakeholders, so they are⏎clear on what is and is not regulated. Regulatory ambiguity hampers innovation, promotes distrust in⏎systems, and results in the development of fewer applications of this promising technology. Federal⏎agencies should ensure that guidance is constantly reassessed to account for iterative changes in⏎technologies and provide the clearest regulatory picture possible.⏎Unified Approach to Governance⏎Any federal action to address statutes, regulations, or policies that govern the use of AI in healthcare⏎should ensure sufficient coordination across the HHS and the Federal Trade Commission (FTC),⏎with HHS leadership aligning its agencies—Food and Drug Administration (FDA), Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS), Assistance Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health IT (ASTP/ONC), and OCR. A unified AI governance framework⏎should establish clear standards on transparency, model card development, risk stratification to⏎prevent overregulation, funding for comparative effectiveness research, harmonized definitions⏎across federal agencies, and real-world case studies to guide clinicians and patients.⏎Many of these concepts already exist and are defined either through regulations or sub-regulatory⏎guidance in other components of the US health care system and are accepted by developers,⏎researchers, and experts from across the ideological spectrum. Borrowing from and building on⏎existing concepts and developing a unified, whole of government approach can remove subjectivity⏎⏎from the decision-making process, boost adoption, and promote the development of innovative AI⏎systems that are cutting edge, transparent, and accurate.⏎At the same time, the growing patchwork of state-based AI policies poses challenges for consistent⏎innovation and implementation. Divergent requirements across jurisdictions can create uncertainty⏎for developers, increase administrative complexity for clinicians, and delay the safe and equitable⏎deployment of AI tools to patients. Ensuring alignment and interoperability of frameworks without⏎constraining legitimate state flexibility will be essential to fostering innovation while maintaining⏎trust, safety, and access to high-quality care.⏎Establishing Public Trust in AI⏎Tangential to the needed unified approach to governance is the need for all agencies to work⏎together to establish and maintain public trust in AI. Federal agencies operate with defined scopes⏎and missions, yet concepts like trust are often ignored as it is hard to develop policies that achieve⏎the aim of an ambiguous term like trust. This is why it is even more essential that all interested⏎parties, including developers, agencies, private sector organizations, and other interested parties⏎work together to ensure that the public can trust that AI will be used in a safe, transparent, and⏎accurate way.⏎One of the prevailing ways to address this is through the reduction of AI bias in outputs. IBM refers⏎to AI-bias, also called machine learning bias or algorithm bias, as the occurrence of biased results⏎due to human biases that skew the original training data or AI algorithm, leading to distorted⏎outputs and potentially harmful outcomes. In medicine, these biases can lead to inaccurate diagnosis⏎and result in patient harm or death.⏎Due to the nature of health care, it is essential that any policy actions work to ensure that any⏎potential biases in data inputs, AI-enabled outputs, and passive and automated systems are⏎minimized as much as possible or adjusted for accordingly to ensure that outputs do not lead to⏎biased or incorrect outputs. Many of the top AI developers are actively working on methods to⏎address and reduce bias, ensuring that systems produce accurate results, and the College believes⏎that any action should consider this important work and reflect the importance of this issue.⏎To that end, it is important that actions work to instill trust by the public through the development⏎of policies that promote transparent, clinically validated AI systems in health care. This includes⏎providing patients, clinicians, and other stakeholders with a firm understanding of and information⏎about data sets used to train AI models. Successful AI-enabled technologies require early⏎engagement from clinicians and organizations like the ACC that stand at the nexus of this⏎innovation. The ACC strongly recommends any actions taken should eliminate barriers that prevent⏎the adoption of trust-based AI and promote policies that encourage AI system developers to⏎integrate clinicians’ perspectives into the design, development, validation, implementation, and⏎monitoring of AI in health care.⏎⏎Communication to the Public⏎Transparency and clear communication are vital to maintaining public trust in AI systems. The ACC⏎appreciates that traditional regulatory mechanisms may not always serve as the most appropriate⏎method for communicating with the public and updating guidance in an environment with rapid⏎innovation and iteration. Agencies should regularly and clearly communicate with stakeholders⏎through numerous mechanisms, including utilizing traditional rulemaking processes. The rulemaking⏎process affords the public the ability to clearly see comments submitted by other stakeholders and⏎affords regulatory agencies the ability to respond to insights and concerns in writing. These⏎responses often provide the clearest understanding of regulatory agencies’ decision-making⏎processes and helps the public comprehend why a decision was made. The ACC calls on agencies to⏎remain transparent, clearly promote any guidance and rulemaking documents, and regularly⏎communicate with the public to ensure trust is maintained.⏎Conclusion⏎The ACC appreciates the opportunity to provide input on the Request for Information on⏎Regulatory Reform on Artificial Intelligence. If you have any questions or follow up, please contact⏎James Vavricek, Director of Regulatory Affairs at jvavricek@acc.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Christopher Kramer, FACC⏎President♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0058♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Kordonowy, Raymond, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Raymond♔Kordonowy♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am very interested in resolving innumerable inefficiencies in our present health care system.  I am excited that the Secretary is interested in helping us get past excess manual data entry as providers.  The biggest drag on payment efficiency is patient demographic entry.  The digital lexicon can easily resolve this provided someone provide a consistent process for this.  The answer lies in placing a data strip on a patients Medicare or insurance card.  This could have been done twenty years ago.  Now with the AI and finally changing Overton window regarding modern health care capabilities in the United States, I offer the following policy proposals.⏎⏎ Policy Recommendations⏎1. Establish Patient Record Custodianship Rights⏎⏎Federal rulemaking should:⏎⏎• Recognize patients as the default legal custodian of their longitudinal record⏎• Require real-time exportability (API-based) from any certified EHR⏎• Prohibit vendor or institutional lock-in⏎• Mandate patient-readable and machine-readable formats⏎⏎Comparable to financial open-banking standards.⏎⏎2. Define a “Patient Health Wallet” Standard⏎⏎Encourage development of secure, interoperable digital wallets capable of:⏎⏎• Storing longitudinal medical record⏎• Storing eligibility/coverage information⏎• Storing consent directives⏎• Storing payment credentials (HSA, employer funds, insurance benefits)⏎• Interfacing with providers via FHIR APIs⏎⏎This becomes the operating system for AI in health care.⏎⏎3. Enable AI as a Patient-Authorized Agent⏎⏎Allow AI tools, under patient consent, to:⏎⏎• Reconcile records from multiple sources⏎• Flag discrepancies⏎• Summarize encounters⏎• Schedule appointments⏎• Generate prior authorization packets⏎• Submit notifications automatically⏎• Produce cost transparency⏎• Execute payment upon service confirmation⏎⏎AI should serve patient and physician, not insurers or administrators.⏎⏎4. Automate Prior Authorization and Notifications⏎⏎Require that:⏎⏎• Clinical documentation triggers automatic authorization review⏎• Standardized criteria used⏎• Machine-readable rules published⏎• Approvals returned in seconds, not days⏎⏎Manual authorization should be the exception.⏎⏎5. Enable Point-of-Care Settlement⏎⏎Encourage payment infrastructure where:⏎⏎• Patient wallet confirms benefit eligibility⏎• Service documented⏎• Patient acknowledges⏎• Funds transfer immediately⏎⏎This reduces:⏎⏎• Claims processing⏎• Billing disputes⏎• Accounts receivable⏎• Collection costs⏎⏎Health care should function more like retail or banking — not months-long reconciliation.⏎⏎6. Protect Privacy by Architecture, Not Paperwork⏎⏎Decentralized custody improves privacy:⏎⏎Instead of:⏎Institutional databases breached at scale⏎⏎Adopt:⏎Encrypted patient-controlled records with selective sharing⏎⏎Less aggregation = smaller breach risk.⏎⏎Expected Outcomes⏎⏎If adopted, this framework would:⏎⏎• Reduce administrative waste by 20–30%, past analysis by myself suggest 800 billion annually in health care costs are being spent documenting and adjudicating payment. ⏎• Improve record completeness⏎• Shorten care cycles⏎• Improve physician efficiency and eliminate redundant/excess clinical testing and risk. ⏎• Strengthen primary care relationships⏎• Enable safe AI adoption⏎• Restore patient autonomy⏎⏎Most importantly:⏎⏎AI becomes a clinical tool, not a bureaucratic one.⏎⏎Implementation Pathway⏎⏎Phase 1⏎API export + wallet pilots + voluntary adoption; allow immediate market access to innovation. ⏎⏎Phase 2- optional.  I would prefer keeping this in the private sector. ⏎CMS + VA + DoD demonstration programs⏎⏎Phase 3-optional⏎Certification requirement for all federally funded systems⏎⏎Phase 4⏎Market adoption and private innovation⏎⏎Closing Statement⏎⏎Artificial intelligence will not fix health care unless we fix the data architecture first.⏎⏎The simplest and most durable solution is:⏎⏎Patient owns the record⏎Physician guides care⏎AI automates logistics⏎Payment occurs at service. This eliminates the need for PBM's and Medicare Part D payment schemes (and fraud). ⏎⏎This restores medicine to its proper relationship: patient and doctor first, institutions second.⏎⏎Submitted by⏎⏎Dr. Raymond Kordonowy, MD⏎Internal Medicine Lipid & Wellness Practice⏎Fort Myers, Florida⏎Chapter Leader, Florida Chapter — Free Market Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0059♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Pharos Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Pharos Health♔♔♔♔♔♔RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎RESPONSE TO REQUEST FOR INFORMATION⏎RIN 0955-AA13 90 Fed. Reg. 60,108 (Dec. 23, 2025)⏎Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted by⏎PHAROS HEALTH⏎San Francisco, California⏎February 23, 2026⏎Submitted to:⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Pharos Health | Page 1⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Dr. Thomas Keane, MD, MBA.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Mr. Steve Posnack⏎Principal Deputy National Coordinator⏎Re: HHS-ONC-2026-0001 | Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Sirs,⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide concrete, experience-based feedback on how the⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) can accelerate the adoption of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) across the healthcare continuum. As an AI vendor with deep experience in⏎healthcare data analytics, clinical NLP, and real-world AI deployment, we offer this response⏎grounded in operational reality.⏎While significant attention is appropriately being paid to AI applications at the point of care such as⏎ambient documentation, clinical decision support, and diagnostic assistance, our response focuses on⏎a complementary domain: interoperability and surveillance, where AI can transform quality⏎improvement, reporting, patient safety monitoring, health information exchange and data⏎normalization.⏎Our response details 10 specific use cases across burden reduction, patient safety, interoperability,⏎and quality improvement. These use cases emerged from hundreds of conversations with clinicians,⏎quality leaders, health plan executives, and health IT professionals. Our team combines clinical⏎expertise with years of production AI and traditional software deployment in healthcare settings. We⏎present only applications where we have observed genuine operational need and where we are⏎actively developing solutions. This is not theoretical speculation; it reflects what healthcare⏎organizations are asking for and what we are building.⏎We look forward to partnering with HHS to move these concepts from pilot to policy.⏎Kind regards⏎Felix Brann⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎Pharos Health, San Francisco CA⏎Pharos Health | Page 2⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Overview⏎For more than two decades, federal agencies and standards development organizations have worked⏎to make clinical information computable. The goal has been straightforward: when data can be read⏎by machines, organizations can automate processes that would otherwise require someone to open a⏎chart, read through documentation, and manually record what they find. This manual work, known as⏎chart abstraction, remains one of the largest drivers of administrative cost in American healthcare.⏎Quality measure reporting alone costs U.S. physician practices an estimated $15.4 billion annually,⏎much of it spent on exactly this kind of review.⏎The EHR Incentive Program, HL7 v2, C-CDA, and FHIR are examples of successive efforts to⏎reduce this burden by encouraging digitizing health data and establishing common data formats.⏎These standards have enabled significant automation, yet chart abstraction persists because clinicians⏎document much of what they know in narrative form. A physician's assessment, a nurse's⏎observations, a social worker's notes: this information often appears in free text rather than structured⏎fields. Furthermore there continues to be weak adoption and implementation of standardization of⏎data across the healthcare continuum.⏎AI-based clinical natural language processing offers a way to extend existing interoperability⏎infrastructure into narrative documentation and weakly standardized data. These methods extract⏎structured meaning from clinical notes, discharge summaries, and legacy records without requiring⏎clinicians to change how they document. The result is programmable access to information that was⏎previously available only through human review.⏎This response describes use cases where AI-enabled processing works alongside existing standards to⏎reduce administrative burden, support patient safety surveillance, and strengthen health information⏎infrastructure. The examples reflect operational deployments within health plans, health systems, and⏎healthcare analytics organizations. Each use case connects directly to ASTP's long standing⏎priorities: making clinical data computable, reducing the cost of manual abstraction, and enabling the⏎interoperability infrastructure that supports a learning health system.⏎Pharos Health | Page 3⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Use Case Summary⏎Clinical Registry Reporting: Hospitals submit detailed patient data to national quality databases,⏎but extracting this information requires staff to manually read through charts for hours per case. AI⏎reads operative notes, pathology reports, and clinical documentation to extract registry variables⏎automatically, reducing review time by 70-90%.⏎Clinical Quality Measurement and Reporting: Healthcare quality measurement depends on⏎standardized, structured data, but implementation of data standardization continues to lag across the⏎industry. Extending measurement into the free-text clinical notes where much of the relevant⏎information lives remains prohibitively expensive, affordable only to the most resource-intensive⏎measurement programs that can fund manual chart review. Emerging AI technology enables less⏎dependency on implementation of standards and extension of measurement into the free text.⏎Guideline Adherence Monitoring: Hospitalized patients often fail to receive recommended⏎preventive treatments like blood clot prophylaxis, but gaps remain invisible until harm occurs. AI⏎monitors patients in real-time against clinical guidelines and can identify trends where adherence to⏎clinical practice guidelines is responsible for harm and additional costs.⏎Utilization Management: Determining whether a hospital admission is medically necessary requires⏎case managers to hunt through records finding scattered clinical data points. AI pre-populates⏎medical necessity reviews by extracting vital signs, lab values, and clinical findings from across the⏎chart, letting reviewers focus on judgment rather than data gathering.⏎Incidental Findings: Unexpected medical observations discovered during diagnostic tests or⏎procedures conducted for unrelated clinical reasons. Because these findings often reside in⏎unstructured narrative notes rather than standardized codes, they frequently lack systematic follow-up⏎or administrative visibility.⏎Elimination of Prior Authorization: Combined implementation of AI powered Guideline⏎Adherence Monitoring and Utilization Management enable a "gold card" qualification where trusted⏎providers receive automatic approval rather than per-request review.⏎Population Health Gap Closure: Patients appear overdue for preventive care when their screenings⏎were done at outside facilities and documented only in scanned PDFs. AI extracts procedure dates⏎and findings from narrative documents to identify which gaps are real and which were already⏎addressed elsewhere.⏎Safety Event Detection: Most patient harm goes undetected because voluntary safety reporting⏎captures only 10-20% of adverse events. AI scans all patient records for clinical warning signs.⏎Root Cause Analysis: When serious safety events occur, investigators must reconstruct what⏎happened from fragmented notes written by different clinicians. AI synthesizes documentation from⏎all sources into a unified timeline, revealing communication gaps and missed warning signs.⏎Sensitive Data Segmentation: Privacy laws require protecting certain health information before⏎sharing records, but code-based filters miss sensitive content documented in narrative text. AI⏎understands the meaning of clinical documentation and identifies protected information regardless of⏎how it was recorded.⏎Pharos Health | Page 4⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Clinical Registry Reporting⏎Understanding of Need⏎Hospitals and physician practices voluntarily participate in clinical registries to benchmark their⏎performance against national standards and identify opportunities for improvement. A registry is⏎essentially a database that collects standardized information about patients with specific conditions or⏎procedures, stroke patients, surgical cases, cancer diagnoses, cardiac interventions. By comparing⏎their outcomes to aggregated data from hundreds of other institutions, healthcare organizations can⏎see where they excel and where they fall short.⏎The challenge is that registries require detailed clinical information that must be manually extracted⏎from medical records. A trained abstractor, typically a nurse or health information specialist, reads⏎through operative notes, progress notes, laboratory results, and discharge summaries to find specific⏎data elements and enter them into the registry system. This process is called chart abstraction, and it⏎is extraordinarily time-consuming.⏎A typical health system participates in dozens of registries across clinical domains: ACS NSQIP⏎alone collects 135 variables per case. A routine case takes an experienced abstractor about 60⏎minutes; complex cases with multiple complications can require 5-6 hours of chart review. A health⏎system performing 22,000 surgical cases annually dedicates over 11,000 labor hours, more than five⏎full-time employees, to this single registry.⏎DOMAIN EXAMPLE REGISTRIES⏎Surgical ACS TQIP, ACS NSQIP, ACS MBSAQIP, STS ACSD⏎Cardiac & Thoracic ACC CathPCI, ACC/SVS VQI, ACC LAAO, ACC Chest Pain-MI, GWTG⏎Resuscitation⏎Oncology NAACCR-NPCR, ACS NCDB, NAACCR-SEER, ASCO QOPI⏎Neurology GWTG Stroke, AANS N2QOD, AANS SRS⏎Current barriers:⏎• Registry variables reside in operative notes and clinical documentation, not structured fields⏎• Determining complications requires clinical judgment applied to narrative text⏎• EHR implementations vary in how clinicians document the same concepts⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI-based natural language processing can read clinical documentation and extract registry variables⏎programmatically. Rather than replacing human abstractors, these systems accelerate their work by⏎identifying relevant information across hundreds of pages of medical records and presenting findings⏎with links to source documentation for human verification. The AI accommodates the variability in⏎how different clinicians and different EHR systems document the same clinical concepts. When⏎information is ambiguous or conflicting across sources, the system flags the discrepancy for human⏎Pharos Health | Page 5⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎review rather than making assumptions. Production deployments demonstrate 95% agreement with⏎expert human abstractors while reducing time per case by as much as 90%.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎Registry participation supports CMS quality reporting requirements and accreditation standards.⏎AI-assisted abstraction reduces the burden on participating practices without requiring changes to⏎how clinicians document care, aligning with federal goals for administrative simplification.⏎Complexity of “Last Known Well”⏎Scenario: For the Get With The Guidelines-Stroke registry from the AHA, the abstractor must⏎determine the "Last Known Well" time, the last moment the patient was observed to be neurologically⏎normal. This single data element is critical because it determines eligibility for clot-dissolving⏎medication (tPA), which must be administered within hours of symptom onset.⏎Clinical Context:⏎The abstractor finds conflicting time references across multiple documentation sources: Data validation⏎audits show "Last Known Well" achieves only 85.3% abstractor agreement, the lowest reliability of⏎any variable in the registry. The complexity arises because abstractors must determine which source is⏎most reliable (direct witness accounts take precedence over second-hand reports) and reconcile⏎conflicting information.⏎Pt: Male, 72 | Encounter: ED Stroke Alert⏎EMS run sheet:Patient found on floor at 14:32, daughter states 'he was fine⏎at breakfast'⏎ED triage note (14:58): Last known well per EMS: 08:00⏎Neurology note (15:20): Spoke with daughter, patient watched morning news,⏎she left for work at 10:15⏎Nursing note (15:05): Family states patient took medications at 9 AM⏎Code Stroke form: field left blank⏎AI Application: The AI extracts all time references from EMS documentation, nursing notes, and⏎physician notes. It identifies the daughter's direct witness account in the neurology note as the most⏎reliable source according to registry abstraction hierarchy guidelines. It determines Last Known Well⏎as 10:15 and presents a complete timeline showing all documented times with links to source⏎documentation, allowing the abstractor to verify the determination in minutes rather than⏎reconstructing the timeline manually.⏎Measurable Impact:⏎• Addresses the registry variable with lowest inter-rater reliability (85.3%)⏎• Synthesizes 4+ documentation sources in seconds vs. 15-20 minutes manual review⏎• Reduces tPA eligibility determination errors from conflicting timestamps⏎Pharos Health | Page 6⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Clinical Quality Measurement and Reporting⏎At its core, healthcare quality measurement is an abstraction problem. Clinical research identifies the⏎markers of high-quality, evidence-based care; quality measurement then seeks to abstract those⏎markers from the primary medical record to evaluate performance. For decades, this abstraction was⏎a manual, labor-intensive process performed by human reviewers. The transition to electronic⏎Clinical Quality Measures (eCQMs) is designed to automate this, reducing the cost and effort of⏎abstraction by standardizing both the data format such as CCDA and FHIR and the measure logic⏎expression with the Clinical Quality Language or CQL.⏎The Limitations of Structural Standardization⏎Standardizing clinical data, moving from HL7 v2 to C-CDA and now toward FHIR has resulted in a⏎matrix of non-uniformity where even when organizations adopt a shared standard, local⏎implementation choices, custom data mappings, and varying rates of adoption across the continuum⏎of care leave the data "messy."⏎As a result, a Chief Quality Officer at an academic medical center cannot simply "run" a measure⏎across their enterprise and trust the result. They find that even standardized fields for common labs or⏎vitals remain unreliable until they undergo resource-intensive, organization-specific reconciliation. In⏎this environment, the administrative data still fails to speak the language of the clinician, remaining⏎an abstraction of a proxy rather than an abstraction of the care itself.⏎The Clinical Narrative and Free Text⏎The second, more significant challenge is that the most vital parts of the clinical story are never⏎"coded." Critical indicators of clinical judgment, patient nuance, and medical necessity reside in⏎unstructured clinical notes and free text. Because these narratives are not standardized, they remain⏎invisible to eCQM developers and the rigid logic of CQL.⏎The move toward Digital Quality Measures (dQMs) is intended to expand the data ecosystem to⏎include registries and devices, but it does not resolve this fundamental visibility gap. dQMs still⏎largely rely on Clinical Quality Language (CQL), a machine-readable expression language that,⏎while powerful, is restricted to structured inputs. Consequently, the industry faces a compounding⏎barrier: the logic standard lacks broad adoption, and the clinical narrative remains unreadable to the⏎machine.⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI-based natural language processing can normalize poorly standardized data and extend the reach of⏎electronic quality measurement by extracting measure-relevant data elements from clinical⏎documentation.⏎Pharos Health | Page 7⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎AI functions as a dynamic abstraction service that can interpret "messy" standardized data and⏎unstructured narrative text with equal high-fidelity, no longer requiring every health system to⏎perfectly map every data element to a rigid schema before a measure can function.⏎AI can also expand the range of clinical quality measures that can be implemented because the⏎complexity of data standardization and chart abstraction is no longer a barrier to implementation.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎This approach supports ASTP requirements for electronic quality measurement while acknowledging⏎the practical reality that not all clinical information exists in structured form. It extends the reach of⏎CQL-based quality measurement to the unstructured content that currently requires manual⏎abstraction. AI enabled abstraction allows for more complex and relevant measures to be⏎implemented that can focus incentive payments directly to high quality care.⏎The SEP-1 Challenge⏎Scenario: The SEP-1 measure (Severe Sepsis and Septic Shock Management Bundle) is one of the most⏎complex and time-consuming quality measures in healthcare. It requires determining "time zero", the⏎moment when a patient first met criteria for severe sepsis, and then verifying that a bundle of treatments⏎(blood cultures, antibiotics, fluids, repeat lactate) was completed within 3 and 6 hours of that moment.⏎Clinical Context:⏎Consider a patient who arrives at the emergency department. Determining "time zero" requires clinical⏎judgment: when did this patient first meet the clinical definition of severe sepsis? Was it at triage when⏎infection was suspected? At 14:45 when vital signs showed organ dysfunction? Studies show that⏎abstractors agree on time zero only 36% of the time, meaning nearly two-thirds of cases involve legitimate⏎disagreement about measure interpretation.⏎Pt: Male, 58 | Encounter: ED Sepsis Alert⏎Triage note at14:32: fever, cough, confusion⏎Vital signs at14:45: temp 39.1°C, BP 88/54, RR 24⏎Lactate at15:18: 4.2 mmol/L⏎Antibiotics administered at16:15⏎AI Application: The AI reviews all documentation from the encounter, identifies the earliest evidence of⏎suspected infection combined with evidence of organ dysfunction, and determines time zero as 14:45 (when⏎vital signs demonstrated hypotension meeting SIRS criteria in a patient with suspected infection). It then⏎calculates whether each bundle element was completed within the required timeframe, presenting a timeline⏎with linked source evidence for each determination.⏎Expanding Measure Development: The CAP-Pharos Collaboration⏎When abstraction costs fall, entirely new categories of quality measurement become feasible. Pharos is⏎currently collaborating with the College of American Pathologists (CAP) to apply neurosymbolic AI to⏎Pharos Health | Page 8⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎pathology quality measures, work presented at the Council of Medical Specialty Societies (CMSS) Annual⏎Meeting in 2025.⏎The Problem: CAP’s Qualified Clinical Data Registry (QCDR) measures require data elements that live⏎exclusively in narrative pathology reports: histologic tumor grade, margin status, lymphovascular invasion,⏎depth of invasion. Consider what a real pathology report looks like:⏎CPT Code(s): 88305x4⏎SPECIMEN SOURCE:⏎RIGHT FOREHEAD / RIGHT ANTERIOR NECK LINE / LEFT MID STERNUM / LEFT FOREARM⏎FINAL PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:⏎A. Skin, right forehead, shave biopsy: Actinic keratosis.⏎B. Skin, right anterior neck line, shave biopsy: Basal cell carcinoma,⏎nodular type; - present at peripheral and deep margins.⏎C. Skin, left mid sternum, shave biopsy: Basal cell carcinoma, superficial⏎type; - present at peripheral margin.⏎D. Skin, left forearm, shave biopsy: Squamous cell carcinoma in situ; -⏎present at peripheral and deep margins.⏎This case has been reviewed and dictated by [Name], M.D., Ph.D., Board Certified⏎Dermatopathologist.⏎The yellow-highlighted elements are precisely what the quality measure needs to capture, but they exist only⏎in narrative text. No structured code or billing field contains this information. Existing keyword and⏎rule-based extraction tools proved inadequate: a rule searching for “GE* biops*” to find gastric biopsies⏎would match on a patient named George who had an ear biopsy. CAP was forced to abandon automated⏎collection of clinically important measures, including a gastric intestinal metaplasia measure, because no⏎existing technology could reliably extract the required data elements from narrative pathology text.⏎See Exhibit 1 - Neurosymbolic AI for automated clinical quality measure abstraction in pathology -⏎CMSS 25⏎The Approach: Rather than prompting a general-purpose large language model, the system converts each⏎measure specification into a neurosymbolic decision tree: a structured pipeline that decomposes the measure⏎into discrete sub-problems. This approach constrains AI tightly to the precise definitions laid out in the⏎measure specifications, minimizing hallucination and producing fully auditable reasoning and evidence⏎citations for every determination.⏎The Results: The system was evaluated across four CAP QCDR measures on 2,000 pathology reports (500⏎randomly subsampled per measure from a feed of 100,000 reports). All reports were independently⏎double-abstracted by experienced third-party abstractors trained on each measure, establishing a human⏎inter-rater reliability (IRR) baseline. Final gold-standard labels were determined by third-party RN⏎abstraction managers. The neurosymbolic models were then evaluated against the same gold standard.⏎IRR Accuracy⏎Human 0.918 95.4%⏎AI 0.922 95.7%⏎No statistical difference between human and AI abstractors.⏎Pharos Health | Page 9⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎The Implication: This collaboration demonstrates a broader principle directly relevant to this RFI.⏎Over-simplifying quality measures to rely only on structured data fields, simply because that is what current⏎tools can process, undermines the clinical purpose of measurement.⏎Clinically complex measures without added burden. Professional societies can develop measures that⏎reflect current clinical practice guidelines without requiring costly manual abstraction or adding⏎documentation burden to clinicians. Measure data can be collected directly from the narrative⏎documentation clinicians already create. Furthermore measures could be developed that leverage patient⏎interviews, sentiment analysis and other non-structured data sources to capture the patient’s “voice” in their⏎care.⏎Reduced friction for participating practices. Practices no longer need to filter or pre-process data before⏎submission to registries. Vastly more accurate measurement means dramatically fewer case corrections and⏎resubmissions, reducing the operational cost of registry participation.⏎Accelerated measure development. New measures can be applied instantly to a large corpus of existing⏎reports, with AI surfacing edge cases for review by measure developers. This allows measure refinement⏎before deployment, compressing the development cycle from years to weeks. The CAP collaboration is an⏎early proof point: measures that were previously intractable are now being automated at human-level⏎accuracy, enabling quality measurement to expand into domains that were inaccessible to existing⏎technology.⏎Pharos Health | Page 10⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Guideline Adherence Monitoring⏎Clinical practice guidelines exist for nearly every dimension of patient care: when to initiate⏎antibiotics, how to manage blood glucose, which prophylactic therapies hospitalized patients should⏎receive, and how to respond to early signs of clinical deterioration. These recommendations reflect⏎decades of accumulated evidence about what improves outcomes and prevents harm.⏎Medicine will always require clinical judgment and nuance. But guidelines define a baseline standard⏎of care - specifying the interventions, timing, and thresholds most consistently associated with better⏎outcomes. When care systematically deviates from that baseline, it can signal breakdowns in⏎processes, communication, or oversight. Detecting those deviations early offers a powerful⏎opportunity to identify emerging safety risks before they result in patient harm. And when avoidable⏎harm does occur, a central question remains: was there a moment when care diverged from best⏎practice?⏎Today, answering that question is extraordinarily labor-intensive. Determining whether care was⏎guideline-concordant requires a trained clinician to manually review the chart - every note, order, lab⏎result, and nursing assessment - reconstruct the clinical timeline, and compare it against what⏎evidence-based recommendations dictate. This depth of review is typically reserved for retrospective⏎root cause analyses and applied to only a small subset of cases. Performing the same analysis⏎continuously, across an entire patient population, remains effectively intractable.⏎Complicating matters further, guidelines include exceptions. A patient may have a legitimate⏎contraindication to a recommended treatment. A clinician may document sound reasoning for an⏎alternative approach. Distinguishing a process failure from an appropriate clinical decision requires⏎reading and interpreting the documentation.⏎Finally, clinical practice guidelines are not static. New evidence emerges continuously, yet formal⏎guideline updates often lag behind the science by months or years. Conversely, some⏎recommendations persist long after their evidentiary basis has weakened or been superseded. Once⏎new guidance is issued, there is rarely a systematic mechanism to evaluate how rapidly it is adopted,⏎how faithfully it is implemented, or whether it produces the intended outcomes in real-world settings.⏎This creates a feedback gap: guidelines shape care, but care data are not routinely used to inform the⏎refinement, retirement, or recalibration of the guidelines themselves.⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI can continuously monitor whether actual care delivery aligns with guideline expectations across⏎the full scope of clinical practice. For each patient, the system maps their clinical situation against⏎relevant guideline pathways, identifying what should happen based on their conditions and risk⏎factors, checking whether those care steps occurred, and reading clinical documentation to determine⏎whether any deviation has documented clinical justification. AI does more than just identify⏎guideline misses, it can reason about which process failures actually drive harm and provide targeted⏎intervention opportunities to the quality team.⏎Pharos Health | Page 11⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎This transforms quality monitoring from expensive retrospective chart abstraction on a small⏎sampling of patients into prospective surveillance at scale, on every patient for every care⏎transaction.. Furthermore, rather than discovering guideline gaps only after harm occurs,⏎organizations can identify deviations while there is still time to intervene. AI can surface to Care⏎teams only high value alerts when a patient's care appears to diverge from guidelines without⏎documented rationale, enabling correction before complications develop.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎This approach supports CMS quality programs by shifting from measuring harm after the fact to⏎preventing it prospectively. It enables organizations to demonstrate systematic guideline adherence⏎rather than relying on outcome-based measures that capture only the cases where process failures⏎resulted in detectable harm. Furthermore, it leads to tighter feedback loops - new processes can be⏎implemented and then immediately evaluated and tuned to specific patient populations.⏎Missed VTE Protocol⏎Scenario: Orthopedic surgery patients are at particularly high risk for VTE due to the nature of the⏎procedure and post-operative immobility. Hospital protocol and national guidelines require VTE⏎prophylaxis for all orthopedic surgery patients unless specifically contraindicated.⏎Clinical Context:⏎The admission nursing assessment correctly identifies the patient as high-risk for VTE based on the planned⏎procedure. Pre-operative orders are placed by the surgical team, including antibiotics, pain management,⏎and post-operative physical therapy, but no VTE prophylaxis order is included in the order set. On⏎post-operative day 1, the patient begins ambulating with physical therapy as documented in the PT notes.⏎By post-operative day 2, the medication administration record still shows no prophylaxis has been given. A⏎review of all clinical documentation, nursing notes, physician progress notes, pharmacy notes, reveals no⏎documented contraindication to anticoagulation and no documentation that the patient declined mechanical⏎prophylaxis. The omission appears to be an inadvertent failure to order a standard component of⏎post-operative care.⏎TIME CLINICAL NOTE GUIDELINE COMPLIANCE⏎Day Admission nursing assessment completed. VTE risk score ✓ Joint Commission NPSG.03.05.01 — VTE risk⏎0 calculated: HIGH (orthopedic procedure, age 68, BMI 32). Risk assessment required for all surgical admissions.⏎14:00 documented in chart.⏎Day Pre-op orders placed: Cefazolin 2g IV, Morphine PCA, PT ✗ ACCP / AAOS — VTE prophylaxis required for⏎0 consult. No VTE prophylaxis ordered. No contraindication all major orthopedic surgery patients unless⏎16:00 documented. specifically contraindicated.⏎Day Total knee replacement completed without complication. ✗ WHO Surgical Safety Checklist — VTE⏎0 Standard post-op order set activated. VTE prophylaxis not prophylaxis plan must be verified before incision.⏎18:30 included in order set. Order set gap allowed omission.⏎Day AM nursing assessment. Patient ambulating with walker. MAR: ✗ ACCP — Chemical and mechanical prophylaxis⏎1 Cefazolin, Morphine, Ondansetron. No anticoagulant should begin within 12–24 hours post-operatively for⏎06:00 administered. No contraindication on file. high-risk patients.⏎Day Patient reports increasing left calf pain and swelling. Positive ✗ Symptoms consistent with developing DVT.⏎1 Homan’s sign. Stat duplex ultrasound ordered. Prophylaxis window missed. No protocol triggered⏎15:00 reassessment.⏎Pharos Health | Page 12⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Day Duplex ultrasound confirms acute DVT, left popliteal vein. ⚠ AVOIDABLE HARM — DVT secondary to⏎2 Heparin drip initiated. Discharge delayed. Vascular surgery missed prophylaxis. Guideline-compliant⏎08:00 consult. prophylaxis reduces post-surgical VTE risk by⏎50–60%.⏎AI Application: On post-operative day 1, the AI system detects the combination of high VTE risk, absence⏎of any prophylaxis order, and no documented contraindication. It generates an alert to the charge nurse and⏎the clinical pharmacist, identifying the specific gap: high-risk orthopedic surgery patient without⏎prophylaxis, no contraindication documented. The pharmacist contacts the surgical team, an order is placed,⏎and prophylaxis begins on post-operative day 1, before any harm occurs.⏎Pharos Health | Page 13⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Utilization Management⏎Understanding of Need⏎When a physician decides that a patient needs to be admitted to the hospital, stay in the ICU, or⏎receive a particular treatment, someone has to verify that the clinical situation justifies that level of⏎care. This process is called utilization management, and it exists because healthcare resources are⏎expensive and sometimes overused. A patient who could safely recover at home shouldn't occupy a⏎hospital bed.⏎Health plans and hospitals employ nurses and other clinical staff as case managers or utilization⏎review specialists. These reviewers compare patient information against standardized medical⏎necessity criteria, commercial products like InterQual or MCG that specify what clinical findings⏎justify different levels of care.⏎Healthcare administrative costs reached an estimated $274.5 billion in 2017, with utilization⏎management representing a substantial component. Case managers spend hours each day on data⏎extraction tasks that don't require clinical judgment, simply finding and copying information from⏎one system to another.⏎The Utilization Management Criteria Landscape⏎Health plans rarely rely on a single criteria set. Utilization management operates through a patchwork of⏎guideline products, each governing different clinical domains, layered with plan-specific customizations and⏎subject to a strict hierarchy of authority.⏎Medical Necessity Criteria Sets in Use⏎CRITERIA SET DOMAIN TYPICAL USE⏎InterQual General acute, subacute, Primary criteria for most large commercial and⏎Optum / Change rehab, long-term care Medicare Advantage plans⏎Healthcare⏎MCG General acute, ambulatory, Primary criteria for many payers; often used⏎Hearst Health behavioral health alongside InterQual⏎ASAM Criteria Substance use disorder Mandated by most states for SUD level-of-care⏎American Society of determinations⏎Addiction Medicine⏎LOCUS / Behavioral health Adults (LOCUS) and children/adolescents⏎CALOCUS-CASII (CALOCUS-CASII) service intensity⏎NCCN Guidelines Oncology Chemotherapy protocols, cancer-related imaging,⏎National Comprehensive treatment pathways⏎Cancer Network⏎Hayes Technology New / experimental Evidence sufficiency for emerging technologies⏎Assessments procedures and novel treatments⏎Carelon Radiology, cardiology, Delegated specialty UM using proprietary clinical⏎formerly AIM / Beacon musculoskeletal guidelines⏎Pharos Health | Page 14⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Apollo Managed General acute Lower-cost alternative used by smaller plans, IPAs,⏎Care and TPAs⏎UM Decision Hierarchy⏎Each review follows a defined hierarchy of authority. Federal mandates override all other sources.⏎SOURCE AUTHORITY EXAMPLE⏎Federal / State Legal requirement to be applied NCDs, LCDs for Medicare; state-mandated⏎1⏎Mandates first SUD criteria⏎Plan-Specific Medical Internal policy supersedes Bariatric Surgery Policy with plan-specific⏎2⏎Policies commercial criteria BMI thresholds⏎Commercial Criteria InterQual, MCG, NCCN, ASAM, Standard level-of-care review for acute⏎3⏎etc. medical admission⏎Physician Clinical Escalation when no criteria fit Peer-to-peer review with Medical Director⏎4⏎Judgment⏎The AI Opportunity⏎This patchwork creates the core problem AI can solve. A single admission review may require the⏎case manager to determine which criteria set applies, navigate the decision hierarchy, extract clinical⏎data from the EHR, map it against the correct criteria, and document the determination, all within⏎increasingly compressed turnaround requirements.⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI can extract clinical data from electronic health records and map it against medical necessity⏎criteria automatically. Rather than requiring a case manager to search through the chart for each data⏎point, the system identifies relevant clinical information, calculates any derived scores (like⏎pneumonia severity indices), and presents a pre-populated review with links to source⏎documentation.⏎This approach doesn't remove the case manager from the process, it removes the tedious data⏎extraction that consumes most of their time.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎Accurate level-of-care determination supports appropriate resource utilization without changing⏎clinical workflows. The approach reduces administrative burden while maintaining the clinical⏎oversight that utilization management requires.⏎Operational Example⏎Optum/Change Healthcare Webinar August 31, 2023⏎Pharos Health | Page 15⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Scenario:⏎An 89-year-old patient presents to the emergency department with a three-day history of shortness of breath,⏎cough, weakness, and fever. O2 saturation ranges from 85-87% on room air, improving to 93-97% on 3L⏎nasal cannula. The emergency physician orders admission for community-acquired pneumonia.⏎Clinical Context:⏎The case manager needs to find specific clinical data points scattered across the medical record. She opens⏎the patient's chart in one screen, the medical necessity criteria tool in another, and the hospital's submission⏎portal in a third. She navigates to vital signs to find the O2 saturation. This process takes 15-25 minutes of⏎pure data extraction before any clinical judgment is applied.⏎AI Application: When the admission order is placed, the AI automatically triggers a utilization review. It⏎extracts vital signs from the nursing flowsheet, lab values from the laboratory system, and imaging results⏎from radiology reports. It confirms that antibiotics were administered (a treatment criterion). It presents a⏎pre-populated InterQual review showing each criterion, the patient's corresponding value, and links to the⏎source documentation. The case manager reviews the populated form, verifies accuracy, and approves the⏎admission in a fraction of the time manual review would require.⏎Pharos Health | Page 16⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Incidental Findings⏎Understanding the Need: The Abstraction of Secondary Clinical Data⏎Clinical documentation is primarily structured to support the immediate reason for a patient⏎encounter. However, diagnostic procedures frequently reveal incidental findings which are⏎unexpected abnormalities that are unrelated to the primary complaint but represent significant early⏎warning signs for chronic disease or malignancy. These findings, such as a lung nodule identified⏎during a cardiac scan, often contain the "second story" of a patient’s health. While these observations⏎are clinically relevant, they are difficult to track systematically because they fall outside the⏎immediate scope of the primary diagnosis and the resulting billing codes.⏎The Limitations of Billing-Centric Records⏎The central barrier to managing incidental findings is that current documentation systems are⏎designed for targeted, episodic care. Because an incidental finding is secondary to the chief⏎complaint, it is rarely assigned a discrete ICD-10 code during the initial encounter. Without a⏎standardized code, the finding does not trigger administrative alerts or follow-up protocols. The data⏎exists only within the narrative text of the clinical record. This gap creates a significant risk where⏎the physician notes may contain actionable findings, yet a majority do not receive the recommended⏎follow-up care because manual chart review is too resource-intensive to perform at scale.⏎The Challenges of Manual and Rules-Based Review⏎Traditional methods of identifying these findings rely on manual abstraction by nursing or⏎administrative staff, which is often cost-prohibitive. Attempts to automate this process using⏎rules-based logic or simple keyword searches frequently fail. These systems often produce high rates⏎of false positives by flagging benign variations or miss subtle clinical nuances because they cannot⏎interpret the context of the physician's prose.⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI-based natural language processing provides a mechanism to bridge this gap by performing⏎high-fidelity abstraction across the entire clinical narrative. Unlike manual review, AI models can⏎process vast quantities of unstructured text to identify specific, actionable findings and reconcile⏎them with existing patient data. This technology allows organizations to move from reactive⏎documentation to a proactive system of care. By automating the identification of incidental findings,⏎AI ensures that secondary clinical data is captured and integrated into the patient’s longitudinal⏎record, enabling timely intervention and more accurate risk assessment without increasing the⏎administrative burden on clinicians.⏎Pharos Health | Page 17⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Elimination of Prior Authorization⏎Understanding of Need⏎Prior authorization is a requirement imposed by health insurance payers that physicians obtain⏎approval before delivering certain medical services. Prior authorization exists because payers lack⏎visibility into whether providers make appropriate clinical decisions. When a physician orders an⏎expensive biologic medication or an advanced imaging study, the payer has no way of knowing⏎whether that decision reflects sound clinical judgment or unnecessary utilization. Prior authorization⏎fills this information gap by requiring providers to submit documentation proving medical necessity⏎for each individual request ahead of time.⏎The time to respond to a prior authorization request can impact patient care and CMS recognized this⏎burden in the CMS-0057-F Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule, which requires⏎payers to process standard requests within 72 hours and urgent requests within 24 hours. The rule⏎acknowledges that authorization delays directly harm patients and impose unsustainable costs on⏎providers.⏎The burden is significant. Physicians spend an average of 14 hours per week on prior authorization⏎(American Medical Association, 2024). Practices complete 43 requests per physician per week⏎(AMA 2023 Physician Survey, 2024). Staffing costs have increased 43% since 2019 (MGMA⏎Regulatory Burden Report, 2024). Patients wait for treatments their physicians have already⏎determined they need (AMA, 2024).⏎This is one area where we see cautionary misuse of AI as a way to augment process with AI on both⏎sides of a prior authorization exchange - where two AI bots are battling each other and the winner is⏎likely not going to be the patient.⏎But the underlying question prior authorization tries to answer is straightforward: does this provider⏎make clinically appropriate decisions? If a payer could answer that question at the provider level⏎rather than the request level, individual prior authorizations would become unnecessary.⏎This is the concept behind "gold carding" which is granting trusted providers automatic approval for⏎services that would otherwise require prior authorization. Several states have enacted gold carding⏎legislation, and CMS has encouraged payers to reduce prior authorization burden for providers with⏎strong track records.⏎State Gold Carding Legislation⏎THRESH⏎STATE LEGISLATION PERIOD VOLUME NOTES⏎OLD⏎HB 3459 (2021); First state to enact; updated⏎Texas 90% 12 Months 5 per service⏎HB 3812 (2025) 2025⏎Michigan PA 60 (2022) 90%+ Annual Payer-defined Threshold varies by payer⏎Arkansas Act 575 (2023) 90% 12 Months N/A 6-month period for Rx drugs⏎Pharos Health | Page 18⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎THRESH⏎STATE LEGISLATION PERIOD VOLUME NOTES⏎OLD⏎W. Va. Code 6 Consecutive⏎West Virginia 90% 30 requests/yr⏎§33-15-4s Mos.⏎Louisiana La. R.S. 22:1020.61 92% 2 Years 10 requests/yr Longest evaluation window⏎Wyoming WY §26-55-112 90% 12 Months 5 per service⏎Colorado CO §10-16-112.5 80%* 12 Months 24 requests *Lowest threshold enacted⏎Vermont HB 960 100% 6 Months 30 requests/yr Highest threshold required⏎New Mexico 13.10.31.12 NMAC 90% Annual N/A Regulation-based, not statute⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI enables providers to demonstrate evidence-based care and sound decision-making directly from⏎clinical documentation, creating an vehicle for gold card qualification that goes far beyond claims⏎metrics. By applying the capabilities described in Utilization Management and Guideline Adherence⏎Monitoring across a provider's patient panel, payers can systematically assess whether that provider's⏎care aligns with medical necessity criteria and clinical guidelines.⏎Providers who demonstrate consistent alignment earn gold card status, the automatic approval for⏎categories of services that would otherwise require prior authorization. The authorization burden⏎shifts from per-request documentation to periodic provider-level validation.⏎This approach benefits both sides. Providers escape the administrative burden of individual⏎authorizations. Payers reduce their own processing costs while maintaining confidence that approved⏎services are medically appropriate. Patients receive timely care without authorization delays.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎CMS has explicitly encouraged payers to reduce prior authorization burden for providers with⏎demonstrated quality. State gold carding laws in Texas, Michigan, and elsewhere require payers to⏎exempt qualifying providers from prior authorization requirements. AI-based provider evaluation⏎creates the measurement infrastructure these policies require, systematic, auditable assessment of⏎clinical decision-making that can support gold card qualification at scale.⏎Pharos Health | Page 19⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Population Health Gap Closure⏎Understanding of Need⏎Value-based care contracts reward healthcare organizations for keeping patients healthy, not just for⏎treating them when they're sick. A key component of this approach is identifying "care gaps",⏎patients who are overdue for recommended preventive services or chronic disease management.⏎Reporting can show which patients are overdue for services, conduct outreach campaigns⏎encouraging patients to schedule appointments, and work to ensure that preventive care doesn't fall⏎through the cracks.⏎The challenge is that care gap identification requires knowing the complete picture of a patient's⏎healthcare, and no single organization has that complete picture. A patient might have their⏎colonoscopy done by a gastroenterologist who uses a different EHR system or the record of a⏎completed preventive service can appear in the free text of a clinical note or coded in a non-standard⏎format that current reporting systems miss. Organizations will often manually review charts (or pay⏎outside services) to find patients eligible for care gaps by reviewing poorly standardized data and free⏎text.⏎Health information exchanges (HIEs) have emerged to address this fragmentation by aggregating⏎data across healthcare organizations in a region. When a patient visits the emergency department, the⏎HIE can pull together records from their primary care doctor, specialists, and previous hospital visits.⏎This gives providers a more complete picture, but only if they can actually find and interpret the⏎relevant information.⏎CANDIDATE CARE GAPS HEDIS OR CMS MEASURE ALIGNMENT⏎Preventive and Proactive Care⏎Whether eligible patients received Breast Cancer Screening HEDIS BCS; CMS125v14⏎recommended preventive services or risk Cervical Cancer Screening HEDIS CCS; CMS124v14⏎screenings within defined time intervals Colorectal Cancer Screening HEDIS COL; CMS130v14⏎Childhood Immunization Status HEDIS CIS; CMS117v14⏎Depression Screening CMS2v15⏎Falls Risk Screening CMS139v14⏎HIV Screening CMS349v8⏎Timely assessment and follow-up after Documented Assessment After Mammogram HEDIS DBM-E⏎abnormal or incomplete preventive screening Follow-Up After Abnormal Mammogram HEDIS FMA-E⏎results⏎Care Coordination and Transitions⏎Pharos Health | Page 20⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Completion of required follow-up, Transitions of Care HEDIS TRC⏎coordination, or reconciliation activities after Medication Reconciliation Post-Discharge HEDIS MRP⏎a qualifying clinical event Follow-Up After Hospitalization for Mental Illness HEDIS FUH⏎Follow-Up After ED Visit for Mental Illness HEDIS FUM⏎Follow-Up After High-Intensity SUD Care HEDIS FUI⏎Closing the Referral Loop CMS50v14⏎Whether clinical information is transmitted Closing the Referral Loop: Receipt of Specialist Report⏎back to the referring provider following CMS50v14⏎specialty or external care⏎Chronic and Longitudinal Care⏎Evidence of ongoing monitoring or control for Controlling High Blood Pressure HEDIS CBP; CMS165v14⏎patients with chronic conditions across care Diabetes Glycemic Control HEDIS GSD; CMS122v14⏎settings Kidney Health Evaluation HEDIS KED; CMS951v4⏎Eye Exam for Diabetes HEDIS EED; CMS131v14⏎Asthma Medication Ratio HEDIS AMR⏎Initiation and persistence of evidence-based Antidepressant Medication Management CMS128v14⏎therapies over defined treatment periods Statin Therapy for ASCVD HEDIS SPC; CMS347v9⏎Statin Therapy for Diabetes HEDIS SPD⏎Beta-Blocker Persistence After MI HEDIS PBH⏎Ongoing management following acute events Cardiac Rehabilitation HEDIS CRE⏎requiring sustained therapy or rehabilitation Pharmacotherapy Management of COPD Exacerbation HEDIS⏎PCE⏎Safety and Appropriateness of Care⏎Avoidance of unnecessary, low-value, or Appropriate Testing for Pharyngitis CMS146v14⏎non-recommended services Appropriate Treatment for URI CMS154v14⏎Avoidance of Antibiotics for Bronchitis HEDIS AAB⏎Imaging for Low Back Pain HEDIS LBP⏎Non-Recommended PSA Screening HEDIS PSA⏎Exposure to high-risk medications or unsafe High-Risk Medications in Older Adults HEDIS DAE;⏎prescribing patterns CMS156v14⏎Drug-Disease Interactions HEDIS DDE⏎Opioid High Dosage / Multiple Providers HEDIS HDO, UOP⏎Deprescribing Benzodiazepines HEDIS DBO⏎Behavioral Health and Whole-Person Care⏎Pharos Health | Page 21⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Monitoring, follow-up, and continuity of care Depression Remission at Twelve Months CMS159v14⏎for behavioral health conditions Follow-Up Care for Children Prescribed ADHD Medication⏎HEDIS ADD-E; CMS136v15⏎Initiation and Engagement of SUD Treatment HEDIS IET;⏎CMS137v14⏎Assessment of functional status, symptoms, or Functional Status After Hip Replacement CMS56v14⏎patient-reported outcomes Heart Failure Functional Status CMS90v15⏎CAHPS / HOS Measures⏎Clinical Documentation Integrity / Completeness⏎Whether required clinical information is Documentation of Current Medications in the Medical Record⏎documented, updated, or returned in a CMS68v15⏎structured and timely manner to support Closing the Referral Loop: Receipt of Specialist Report⏎continuity of care, quality reporting, and CMS50v14⏎downstream clinical decision-making Transitions of Care: Receipt of Discharge Information HEDIS⏎TRC⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI-based natural language processing can extract clinical evidence from narrative documentation and⏎scanned documents flowing through health information exchanges. When structured data doesn't⏎capture completed care, the AI reads consultation notes, procedure reports, and other documentation⏎to identify the information that would close care gaps.⏎The system processes HIE data across multiple contributing organizations, looking for evidence that⏎recommended services have been completed.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎This approach supports value based pay models and helps organizations keep their populations⏎healthy.⏎Cancer Screenings⏎Scenario: The care management team at an ACO is preparing an outreach campaign to encourage patients⏎to schedule colonoscopies.⏎Clinical Context:⏎The ACO's EHR shows no colonoscopy on file for this patient. However, an HIE query reveals that the⏎patient had an emergency department visit at an outside hospital six months ago. Among the documents⏎returned is a scanned PDF of a gastroenterology consultation note from that visit. The note states: "Prior⏎colonoscopy 3/15/2023 at [outside facility], no polyps, recommended surveillance interval 10 years. Patient⏎does not require repeat screening at this time." The patient is not actually overdue for colorectal cancer⏎Pharos Health | Page 22⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎screening, he had a normal colonoscopy less than two years ago and doesn't need another one for eight more⏎years.⏎AI Application: The AI processes the scanned PDF from the HIE query. It identifies the reference to⏎colonoscopy, extracts the date (March 15, 2023), the findings (no polyps), and the recommended⏎surveillance interval (10 years). It determines that this patient's colorectal cancer screening gap should be⏎closed. The patient is removed from the outreach list, and the quality measure is calculated correctly.⏎HEDIS 2026⏎https://www.ncqa.org/report-cards/health-plans/state-of-health-care-quality-report/colorectal-cancer-⏎screening-col/⏎Pharos Health | Page 23⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Safety Event Detection⏎Understanding of Need⏎Voluntary incident reporting, the primary mechanism hospitals use to identify patient harm, captures⏎only 10 to 20 percent of actual adverse events as many of the safety events are not visible to staff but⏎are buried in the clinical documentation.⏎To address this gap, the Institute for Healthcare Improvement developed the Global Trigger Tool⏎(Griffin & Resar, 2009). In addition to voluntary reports, trained reviewers examine patient records⏎for "triggers," clinical findings statistically associated with adverse events: a creatinine value that⏎doubles from baseline, administration of an opioid reversal agent, an unexpected ICU transfer. Each⏎trigger flags a case for clinical review to determine whether harm occurred. The triggers can be⏎automated with current reporting and technology but they only identify that an event “might” have⏎occurred and require manual review by a trained clinician to determine if a safety event occurred.⏎The methodology is effective but constrained by manual effort. IHI recommends reviewing 10⏎randomly selected records every two weeks. At that rate, the vast majority of discharges are never⏎examined, and the events within them are never counted.⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI can execute trigger tool logic against all patient records, not just a random sample. The system⏎scans clinical data, laboratory values, medication administrations, vital signs, and clinical⏎documentation, looking for the same trigger conditions that human reviewers would identify. When⏎triggers fire, the AI can then inspect the entire record of a patient identified by an IHI trigger to⏎determine if a safety event has occurred avoiding the clinician review step current IHI trigger⏎methodology requires.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎This approach supports Joint Commission patient safety requirements by enabling more⏎comprehensive surveillance. It allows organizations to identify systemic issues without the⏎proportional staffing increase that manual comprehensive review would require.⏎IHI Trigger Tool Study⏎Scenario: In 2011, researchers applied the IHI Global Trigger Tool to 795 patient records at three⏎major academic medical centers, comparing it against voluntary incident reporting and AHRQ⏎Patient Safety Indicators using the same patient sample. Voluntary reporting identified 4 adverse⏎events. AHRQ indicators identified 35. The trigger tool identified 354. Adverse events were⏎present in one-third of all admissions.⏎Pharos Health | Page 24⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎The trigger tool works by scanning clinical data for specific signals, called triggers, that are⏎statistically associated with harm. The IHI defines triggers across six categories:⏎CATEGORY TRIGGER⏎Medication (M) Antidote administration, abrupt stops, creatinine doubling → adverse drug⏎events, overdoses, nephrotoxicity⏎Care (C): Unexpected ICU transfer, blood transfusion, code/arrest → clinical⏎deterioration, missed warning signs⏎Intensive Care (I) Re-intubation, new dialysis in ICU → ICU-specific complications⏎Surgical (S) Return to OR, operative time >6 hours, post-op intubation → surgical⏎complications, procedural failures⏎Perinatal (P) Apgar <7 at 5 min, birth trauma, NICU transfer → labor and delivery⏎adverse events⏎ED (E) Return to ED within 48 hours → premature discharge, missed diagnosis⏎Each trigger does not confirm harm, it identifies a case that warrants clinical review. The⏎methodology was designed for a 20-record monthly sample reviewed manually by trained nurses.⏎It was never designed to scale to every patient. AI changes that constraint.⏎AI Application: An automated implementation of the trigger tool runs nightly across all⏎discharges. When a trigger fires, the system cross-references related clinical documentation to⏎assess whether the trigger represents a likely adverse event.⏎Data that shows medication-associated kidney injury clustering in elderly surgical patients become⏎institutional quality targets..⏎Classen, D. C., Resar, R., Griffin, F., Federico, F., Frankel, T., Kimmel, N., Whittington, J. S.,⏎Lichtner, A., & James, B. C. (2011). ‘Global trigger tool’ shows that adverse events in hospitals may⏎be ten times greater than previously measured. Health Affairs, 30(4), 581–589.⏎https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2011.0190⏎Pharos Health | Page 25⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Root Cause Analysis⏎Understanding of Need⏎When a serious patient safety event occurs, a wrong-site surgery, a fatal medication error, a patient⏎death from an unrecognized deterioration, healthcare organizations are required to conduct a⏎thorough investigation to understand what went wrong and prevent similar events in the future. The⏎Joint Commission, which accredits most American hospitals, mandates that these "sentinel events"⏎trigger a root cause analysis within strict timeframes: the investigation must begin within 72 hours⏎and be completed within 45 days.⏎Root cause analysis is a systematic approach to understanding not just what happened, but why it⏎happened. The goal is to look beyond the individual who made the final error and understand the⏎system failures, communication breakdowns, and process gaps that allowed the error to occur.⏎The challenge is that the same clinical event is documented differently by different staff members.⏎Reconstructing a coherent timeline from these fragmented accounts is extraordinarily labor-intensive.⏎Investigators must read through pages of nursing notes, physician documentation, respiratory therapy⏎records, pharmacy records, and safety reports. They must identify where the accounts align and⏎where they conflict.⏎In the same way that Guideline Adherence can scale to include the entire patient population, it's⏎valuable (completely impractical today) to run some version of an RCA on every harm event, not just⏎sentinel events. In conversations with safety leaders, the concept of “mini-RCAs” are emerging as a⏎promising approach to use AI to do an RCA of every event which massively reduces the burden on⏎safety managers to review and triage reported events. AI does the initial RCA and uses reasoning to⏎identify trends and focus review on only the highest value RCA results.⏎Joint Commission Root Cause Analysis: Chart Review Requirements⏎The categories below represent the questions that demand the most intensive clinical chart review,⏎where AI-assisted timeline reconstruction and cross-source synthesis can most significantly⏎accelerate the investigation.⏎RCA CHART REVIEW⏎QUESTION SOURCES TO REVIEW⏎CATEGORY CHALLENGE⏎Communication Were critical findings Nursing notes, physician Escalations documented in one⏎communicated between notes, RT notes, handoff system may be absent from⏎providers and records another⏎acknowledged?⏎Assessment Were changes in condition Vital sign trends, nursing Requires mapping deterioration⏎recognized and acted on in assessments, lab against data available at the time⏎time? trajectories, scoring tools⏎Information Did the decision-maker Lab acknowledgment logs, Results can exist in the system but⏎Availability have access to critical radiology reports, allergy never be opened or referenced⏎results? records⏎Medication Use Were medications ordered, CPOE orders, pharmacy Requires tracing the full chain⏎dispensed, and monitored logs, MAR, renal labs, alert from order to administration⏎Pharos Health | Page 26⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎correctly? override logs⏎Continuum of Care Was information preserved Transfer orders, handoff Information loss at handoff is a⏎across care transitions? notes, discharge summaries leading cause of sentinel events⏎Operative Care Were safety checklists Surgical checklists, Multiple providers document⏎completed and intra-op anesthesia records, overlapping timeframes in⏎events communicated? operative reports, recovery separate systems⏎notes⏎Staffing Were staffing levels and Schedules, assignment Staffing data often lives in HR⏎competencies adequate for sheets, acuity scores, systems separate from clinical⏎patient acuity? overtime logs records⏎Patient Were ID protocols and Consent forms, time-out Requires verifying every⏎Identification consent verification documentation, wristband checkpoint in the identification⏎followed at each step? logs chain⏎Source: Joint Commission Framework for Root Cause Analysis and Corrective Actions.⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI can dramatically accelerate the information-gathering phase of root cause analysis by extracting⏎relevant details from across the medical record and synthesizing them into a unified timeline. The⏎system reads documentation from nursing, physicians, respiratory therapy, pharmacy, and other⏎sources, identifies details relevant to the event under investigation, and constructs a chronological⏎reconstruction that makes gaps and discrepancies visible.⏎This does not replace the clinical judgment and systems thinking that quality professionals bring to⏎root cause analysis but rather it compresses the time-consuming work of reading through records and⏎reconstructing timelines.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎This approach directly supports compliance with Joint Commission sentinel event requirements by⏎accelerating the information-gathering process. Organizations can more reliably meet the 72-hour⏎initiation requirement and 45-day completion deadline while conducting more thorough⏎investigations.⏎Sentinel Event Review⏎Scenario: Two days into the hospitalization, a patient suffers a respiratory arrest requiring emergent⏎intubation. He survives but requires an extended ICU stay. The safety team initiates a root cause analysis to⏎understand what led to this preventable deterioration.⏎Clinical Context:⏎The investigator must piece together what happened from multiple documentation sources that tell⏎overlapping but incomplete parts of the story. A nurse escalates a concern. A respiratory therapist responds⏎with a recommendation. A physician writes a progress note that references neither. A critical lab result⏎enters the system but is never acknowledged. By the time the rapid response is called, every piece needed to⏎prevent the deterioration was present in the record but never assembled into a coherent picture.⏎Pharos Health | Page 27⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Pt: Male, 74 | Encounter: ICU/Rapid Response⏎Nursing note 6:00 AM:working harder to breathe, using accessory muscles |⏎requested RT evaluation⏎RT note 7:15 AM:Recommended BiPAP initiation, order placed, awaiting⏎physician acknowledgment⏎Physician note 8:30 AM:will continue current O2 therapy and monitor closely⏎(NO mention of BiPAP)⏎Blood gas 7:00 AM:pCO2 67, pH 7.28⏎Rapid response called: 9:45 AM,O2 sat 82%⏎AI Application: The AI ingests all documentation sources and constructs a unified timeline. It surfaces⏎what no single clinician could see: a BiPAP recommendation never acted upon, a critical blood gas never⏎acknowledged, and a two-hour window where intervention could have prevented the arrest. It maps these⏎gaps to Joint Commission root cause categories automatically.⏎Pharos Health | Page 28⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Sensitive Data Segmentation⏎Understanding of Need⏎Healthcare records contain some of the most personal information imaginable, and certain categories⏎of health data require extra protection under the law. When someone seeks treatment for substance⏎use disorder, receives reproductive healthcare, or accesses mental health services, that information⏎carries legal protections beyond standard HIPAA privacy rules. The challenge for health information⏎exchanges is that this sensitive information does not always announce itself with convenient labels or⏎codes.⏎The recent (2024) introduction of TEFCA has drastically increased the volume of data exchange⏎across state lines and will require scaled approaches to data segmentation, security and patient⏎consent.⏎Federal and state regulations mandate that healthcare organizations segment certain types of sensitive⏎health information before sharing it. The 2024 rule modifying 42 CFR Part 2 aligns substance use⏎disorder confidentiality requirements with HIPAA, with full compliance required by February 2026.⏎Meanwhile, California's AB352 requires segmentation of reproductive health data before sharing it⏎across state lines. These regulations exist to protect patients from discrimination and to encourage⏎people to seek care for stigmatized conditions without fear that their information will be⏎inappropriately disclosed.⏎Modern clinical documents like C-CDAs contain both structured coded fields and extensive narrative⏎sections where clinicians document the nuances of care. These documents frequently include⏎Base64-encoded PDF attachments containing consultation notes, imaging reports, or historical⏎records. Code-based filtering systems cannot read narrative text or decode and analyze PDF content,⏎creating significant gaps in privacy protection.⏎Examples of Laws that protect data by type⏎Scope Law What Data Is Protected⏎Substance use disorder (SUD) treatment records from⏎Federal 42 CFR Part 2 federally assisted programs. Protects any information that⏎would identify a patient as having or having had an SUD.⏎Psychotherapy notes are defined as notes recorded (in any⏎45 CFR § 164.501 & § medium) by a mental health professional documenting or⏎Federal⏎164.508(a)(2) analyzing the contents of conversation during a private,⏎group, joint, or family counseling session.⏎Sensitive services including abortion, contraception, and⏎AB 352 (CMIA Civil Code⏎California gender-affirming care. Mandates technical segregation of⏎§§56.101, 56.108, 56.110)⏎these records and prohibits automatic transmission to HIEs.⏎Medical information and PHI including an individual's⏎California SB 81⏎immigration status and place of birth.⏎Pharos Health | Page 29⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Scope Law What Data Is Protected⏎Electronic health records. Protects metabolic health and diet⏎SB 1188 (Health & Safety⏎Texas records. Prohibits storing credit scores or voter registration⏎Code Ch. 183)⏎status within an EHR.⏎Health & Safety Code Ch. Communicable disease records including HIV/AIDS test⏎Texas⏎81 results, infection status, and partner notification information.⏎All mental health and drug or alcohol abuse treatment⏎Oklahoma Title 43A §1-109 information and communications between patients and⏎licensed professionals.⏎Communicable disease records. Protects HIV-related test⏎Oklahoma Title 63 §1-502.2⏎results, infection status, illness, and exposure information.⏎Mandates provider reporting to state HIE but strictly excludes⏎SB 1369 (63 O.S. §1-133 /⏎Oklahoma psychotherapy notes and 42 CFR Part 2 data from automatic⏎OKSHINE)⏎transmission without explicit consent.⏎Proposed Approach⏎AI-powered segmentation addresses these gaps by identifying sensitive information based on what it⏎actually means rather than what codes are present. The system reads and interprets narrative text the⏎way a human reviewer would, understanding that a phrase like "discussed pregnancy options, patient⏎elected termination" describes reproductive healthcare even without a specific procedure code⏎attached.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎This capability directly supports compliance with 42 CFR Part 2 as modified by the 2024 rule,⏎ensuring that substance use disorder information receives appropriate protection. It also enables⏎compliance with state-specific requirements like California's AB352 reproductive health privacy law.⏎For organizations participating in the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement⏎(TEFCA), comprehensive segmentation capability is essential for meeting the network's privacy and⏎consent requirements.⏎AI Data Cop⏎Scenario: A California-based health information exchange receives a query from an out-of-state clinician⏎requesting records for a patient who recently relocated. Under California law, reproductive health⏎information requires segmentation before release to out-of-state requesters.⏎Clinical Context:⏎The patient's record includes a 47-page C-CDA document with several embedded PDF attachments. The⏎structured problem list in the document contains only two conditions: hypertension and anxiety, neither of⏎which triggers any privacy restrictions. However, one of the embedded PDFs is a consultation note from an⏎OB/GYN visit about pregnancy options, including the patient's ultimate decision to have an abortion.⏎Pharos Health | Page 30⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Pt: Female, 42 | Encounter: HIE Query/Out-of-State Request⏎Structured Problem List:hypertension, anxiety⏎Embedded PDF (OB/GYN Note):⏎Content:pregnancy options discussion, decision to have abortion⏎System Filter Result:Code-based filter: APPROVED for release (no codes⏎trigger restrictions)⏎AI Analysis Result:REQUIRES SEGMENTATION per CA AB352 (reproductive health⏎narrative)⏎AI Application: The AI system processes the complete document, including decoding and analyzing all⏎embedded PDF attachments. Upon reading the OB/GYN consultation note, it recognizes the reproductive⏎health content based on the meaning of the text.⏎Pharos Health | Page 31⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Cautionary Framework: Responsible AI Deployment in Clinical Settings⏎Two failure modes pose significant risk if left unaddressed by federal policy. The first is operational:⏎AI systems that overwhelm the humans they serve. The second is technical: AI systems whose⏎reasoning cannot be trusted, explained, or reproduced.⏎I. Signal Overload⏎The IHI Global Trigger Tool demonstrated that adverse events occur in roughly one-third of hospital⏎admissions, ten times the rate captured by voluntary reporting. When one multi-hospital system⏎deployed electronic trigger tools, the volume was so overwhelming the organization disabled it⏎entirely. The tool worked. The humans had no framework for processing the output.⏎Our team experienced this directly in sepsis detection work at emergency department triage. The⏎model achieved strong performance (F1 0.71, published in JMIR). The modeling succeeded. The⏎implementation failed. We did not account for clinical workflow and saved zero lives.⏎The principle: detection must be paired with prioritization, and prioritization with workflow⏎integration. Systems should perform counterfactual analysis to surface the guideline failures most⏎strongly associated with harm, transforming a firehose into a ranked, actionable list. Federal⏎guidance should require AI safety tools demonstrate operational integration, not just detection⏎accuracy.⏎II. Hallucination and Reasoning⏎LLMs produce hallucinations: clinically plausible statements that are factually wrong. Research⏎documents hallucination rates of 1.47% and omission rates of 3.45% in clinical note generation. At⏎health system scale, these translate directly to patient safety events.⏎The deeper issue is that LLMs reason by pattern completion, not clinical logic. Consider "last known⏎well" in stroke care: establishing this timestamp requires synthesizing nursing notes, EMS reports,⏎and family interviews, resolving contradictory accounts across multiple sources. An LLM might⏎produce the correct answer, but there is no way to verify why, and no guarantee the same model⏎produces the same answer after a version update. A wrong timestamp determines whether a patient⏎receives tPA or is excluded from treatment.⏎Neurosymbolic reasoning as an alternative. Our approach, validated with the College of American⏎Pathologists, decomposes clinical questions into logic trees of constrained sub-problems. Each node⏎uses AI in a tightly scoped role. The tree composes results using explicit, inspectable logic,⏎producing auditability (answers trace to source evidence), reproducibility (same inputs, same outputs⏎regardless of model version), and targeted validation (each node tested independently).⏎Recommended Policy Guardrails⏎Require structured output over free-text generation for clinical determinations. Mandate hallucination⏎and omission rate disclosure tested against clinician ground truth. Require reasoning transparency so⏎every clinical determination has an auditable chain. Distinguish extraction from generation in federal⏎Pharos Health | Page 32⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎certification. Require post-deployment monitoring per FDA GMLP principles. Mandate⏎implementation outcome disclosure, including cases where accurate detection failed to improve⏎outcomes due to workflow barriers.⏎These recommendations align with FDA Good Machine Learning Practice principles and ASTP⏎transparency requirements. We urge validation frameworks that hold vendors accountable for what⏎they deliver in clinical practice, not just what their models achieve in a laboratory.⏎Pharos Health | Page 33⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Conclusion⏎Healthcare quality improvement runs on chart abstraction and for twenty years, federal policy has⏎pursued a straightforward goal: make clinical data machine-readable so this manual work becomes⏎unnecessary.⏎Despite this investment, chart abstraction persists. Quality measure reporting alone costs U.S.⏎physician practices $15.4 billion annually. Health systems dedicate entire teams to registry⏎abstraction - one academic medical center employs five full-time staff just to abstract surgical cases⏎for a single registry. CMS leadership acknowledges that even basic structured data, such as blood⏎pressure readings, often remain unreliable until organizations individually correct their workflows.⏎The data standardization that was supposed to eliminate manual review never fully arrived.⏎The result is that quality improvement remains constrained by what organizations can afford to⏎abstract. Registries limit their data elements. Quality measures simplify their specifications. Safety⏎surveillance samples only a handful of records each month. Organizations discover guideline failures⏎only after harm occurs because comprehensive monitoring would require reading every chart.⏎AI-based clinical natural language processing changes this calculus. Systems that achieve⏎human-level accuracy while reducing abstraction time by 70–90% do not merely lower costs—they⏎remove the constraint that has shaped what quality improvement can attempt.⏎When abstraction costs drop by an order of magnitude, quality measurement works the way it was⏎always intended:⏎● Registries collect the clinical detail that drives improvement, extracted directly from⏎operative notes and pathology reports, without requiring large abstraction teams⏎● Quality measures extend into narrative documentation where relevant clinical information⏎actually resides, rather than contorting specifications to fit available structured fields⏎● Safety surveillance scales from monthly samples to continuous monitoring, surfacing adverse⏎events that voluntary reporting misses⏎● Guideline adherence shifts from retrospective discovery of failures to prospective⏎identification of care gaps while intervention is still possible⏎● Root cause analysis compresses from weeks of manual reconstruction to hours, enabling⏎more thorough investigations that prevent recurrence⏎When the constraint lifts entirely, new possibilities emerge and applications that were never feasible⏎when abstraction required human review:⏎● Quality measures too complex for manual abstraction become practical, such as the CAP⏎pathology measures we validated at human-level accuracy after previous automation attempts⏎failed⏎● Real-time guideline monitoring across entire patient populations, identifying the specific⏎protocol failures most strongly associated with harm⏎Pharos Health | Page 34⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎● Provider-level performance measurement that enables gold-card qualification, replacing⏎per-request prior authorization with demonstrated trust⏎This is not speculative. Our collaboration with the College of American Pathologists demonstrated⏎AI abstraction matching experienced human abstractors on measures that had defeated prior⏎automation. The capability exists. The question is how federal policy accelerates responsible⏎adoption.⏎We urge caution alongside ambition. AI systems that overwhelm clinical workflows with⏎unactionable alerts help no one. Hallucination rates that appear low in aggregate translate into patient⏎safety risks at scale.⏎Pharos Health welcomes the opportunity to partner with HHS to make this transition responsible,⏎validated, and real.⏎Pharos Health | Page 35⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎References⏎American Medical Association. 2024 Prior Authorization Physician Survey. 2024.⏎Bates DW, et al. The Safety of Inpatient Health Care. NEJM. 2022;386(20):2038-2048.⏎Casalino LP, et al. US Physician Practices Spend More Than $15.4 Billion Annually To Report Quality⏎Measures. Health Affairs. 2016;35(3):401-406.⏎CMS. Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule (CMS-0057-F). 2024.⏎CMS Measures Management System. eCQM Specifications and Tools. https://mmshub.cms.gov/⏎eCQI Resource Center. MADiE. https://ecqi.healthit.gov/tool/madie⏎Get With The Guidelines-Stroke. American Heart Association. Data Validation Audit Results.⏎Griffin FA, Resar RK. IHI Global Trigger Tool, 2nd Ed. IHI. 2009.⏎HIMSS Analytics. Healthcare IT Market Intelligence Report. 2024.⏎HL7 International. CQL Specification v1.5.3. https://cql.hl7.org/⏎HHS OIG. Hospital Incident Reporting Systems Do Not Capture Most Patient Harm. OEI-06-09-00091. 2012.⏎NCQA. SNS-E Measure Specifications. HEDIS MY2023.⏎Skau C, Brann F, Clarke A. Neurosymbolic AI for Quality Measure Abstraction. CMSS. 2025.⏎The Joint Commission. Sentinel Event Data Summary. 2024.⏎42 CFR Part 2 Final Rule. Federal Register. 2024.⏎MGMA: Medical Group Management Association. (2024). 2024 Prior Authorization Issue Brief. Washington,⏎DC. Retrieved from https://www.mgma.com/advocacy⏎Pharos Health | Page 36⏎⏎RFI Response: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎EXHIBIT 1⏎Neurosymbolic AI for automated clinical quality measure⏎abstraction in pathology⏎CMSS 25⏎Pharos Health | Page 37⏎⏎Neurosymbolic AI for⏎automated clinical quality⏎measure abstraction in⏎pathology⏎Colleen Skau, PhD (College of American Pathologists)⏎Felix Brann (Pharos)⏎Alex Clarke, MD, PhD (Pharos)⏎⏎Disclosures⏎Presenters⏎● Colleen Skau, PhD — Assistant Director, College of American Pathologists⏎● Felix Brann — CEO, Pharos⏎● Alex Clarke, MD, PhD — CMO, Pharos⏎Relevant Relationships⏎● Mr. Brann and Dr. Clarke serve as executive officers of Pharos, a company⏎collaborating with the College of American Pathologists (CAP) and other professional⏎societies to develop AI-enabled automated clinical abstraction tools.⏎● Dr. Skau has no disclosures.⏎⏎Agenda⏎1: Introduction⏎Extracting clinically relevant measures is challenging⏎2: A New Paradigm⏎Neurosymbolic AI for automated quality measure abstraction⏎3: Current Status⏎Development, evaluation and results⏎4: Next Steps⏎⏎1: Introduction⏎Extracting clinically relevant measures⏎is challenging⏎⏎Introduction⏎The Goal: Capture clinically relevant⏎measures while minimizing friction⏎● It’s crucial to minimize the burden⏎Adding more More documentation burden⏎structured fields on clinicians⏎of registry submission for partner⏎health systems⏎Requiring manual Significant cost and burden⏎abstraction on operational teams⏎● Data is useless unless it’s accurate⏎⏎Introduction⏎But… Over-simplifying measures to⏎rely only on structured fields undermines⏎the purpose of a clinical registry⏎● Measures become clinically irrelevant and fail to⏎reward high-quality care⏎● The dataset loses the richness needed for nuanced⏎research, risk adjustment, and subgroup analyses⏎⏎SPECIMEN SOURCE:⏎A. Urinary bladder⏎FINAL PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:⏎Urinary bladder, bladder tumor, poorly differentiated⏎Poorly differentiated malignancy, see comment.⏎Angiolymphatic invasion is present; muscle is present but not involved.⏎CLINICAL HISTORY: BPH, local w/urinary obstruct w/other low urinary tract sx, hydronephrosis bilateral, urinary⏎retention.⏎Unspecified hydronephrosis [ ICD-10 : N13.30 ]⏎COMMENT: Tumor cells are very focally positive for Oscar, and positive for CD68. CD43 is negative. Ki-67 is 80%.⏎The tumor morphology was compared to his previous biopsy and resection, with a similar appearance, and the⏎biopsy had a more reassuring keratin profile, supporting the diagnosis.⏎The following immunostains are negative: DESMIN, CD45, SMA, CAM5.2, AE1/3, ALK1, CK7, CK20, Gata3,⏎S-100, NKX3.1, p40, CDX2, SOX10.⏎Intradepartmental consultation was performed with concurrence.⏎GROSS DESCRIPTION⏎A. Bladder tumor⏎Received in formalin labeled with proper patient identification and "bladder tumor". The specimen consists multiple⏎pieces of tan friable tissue measuring 7.0 x 6.5 x 2.5 cm in aggregate. The specimen is filtered through a biopsy⏎bag and totally submitted in 12 cassettes labeled A1-A12.⏎MICROSCOPIC EXAMINATION:⏎Introduction⏎The Challenge: Clinically relevant concepts live⏎in messy narrative data⏎SPECIMEN SOURCE:⏎A. Urinary bladder⏎Measure Description⏎FINAL PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:⏎Urinary bladder, bladder tumor, poorly differentiated Percentage of urinary bladder carcinoma⏎Poorly differentiated malignancy, see comment.⏎pathology reports that include:⏎Angiolymphatic invasion is present; muscle is present but not involved.⏎Procedure⏎CLINICAL HISTORY: BPH, local w/urinary obstruct w/other low urinary tract sx, hydronephrosis bilateral, urinary retention. Unspecified⏎hydronephrosis [ICD-10 : N13.30 ]⏎● Histologic tumor grade⏎COMMENT: Tumor cells are very focally positive for Oscar, and positive for CD68. CD43 is negative. Ki-67 is 80%. The tumor ● Histologic type⏎morphology was compared to his previous biopsy and resection, with a similar appearance, and the biopsy had a more reassuring ● Presence/absence of muscularis⏎keratin profile, supporting the diagnosis. The following immunostains are negative: DESMIN, CD45, SMA, CAM5.2, AE1/3, ALK1, CK7,⏎propria⏎CK20, Gata3, S-100, NKX3.1, p40, CDX2, SOX10.Intradepartmental consultation was performed with concurrence.⏎● Presence/absence of⏎GROSS DESCRIPTION⏎lymphovascular invasion⏎A. Bladder tumor⏎● Tumor extent⏎Received in formalin labeled with proper patient identification and "bladder tumor". The specimen consists multiple pieces of tan friable⏎tissue measuring 7.0 x 6.5 x 2.5 cm in aggregate. The specimen is filtered through a biopsy bag and totally submitted in 12 cassettes⏎labeled A1-A12.⏎⏎SPECIMEN SOURCE:⏎A. Urinary bladder⏎FINAL PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:⏎Urinary bladder, bladder tumor, poorly differentiated⏎Poorly differentiated malignancy, see comment.⏎Angiolymphatic invasion is present; muscle is present but not involved.⏎CLINICAL HISTORY: BPH, local w/urinary obstruct w/other low urinary tract sx, hydronephrosis bilateral, urinary⏎retention.⏎Unspecified hydronephrosis [ ICD-10 : N13.30 ]⏎COMMENT: Tumor cells are very focally positive for Oscar, and positive for CD68. CD43 is negative. Ki-67 is 80%.⏎The tumor morphology was compared to his previous biopsy and resection, with a similar appearance, and the⏎biopsy had a more reassuring keratin profile, supporting the diagnosis.⏎The following immunostains are negative: DESMIN, CD45, SMA, CAM5.2, AE1/3, ALK1, CK7, CK20, Gata3,⏎S-100, NKX3.1, p40, CDX2, SOX10.⏎Intradepartmental consultation was performed with concurrence.⏎GROSS DESCRIPTION⏎A. Bladder tumor⏎Received in formalin labeled with proper patient identification and "bladder tumor". The specimen consists multiple⏎pieces of tan friable tissue measuring 7.0 x 6.5 x 2.5 cm in aggregate. The specimen is filtered through a biopsy⏎bag and totally submitted in 12 cassettes labeled A1-A12.⏎MICROSCOPIC EXAMINATION:⏎Introduction⏎The Challenge: Clinically relevant concepts require⏎deep contextual understanding⏎CPT Code(s): 88305x4⏎Laboratory Provided ICD & SNOMED Code(s): C44.41, C44.519, D04.62, L57.0 238528004, 402509003, 92352002, Measure Description⏎403878007, 201101007, 254701007,92380000, 254656002⏎SPECIMEN SOURCE: Percentage of final pathology reports⏎RIGHT FOREHEAD⏎for basal cell carcinoma of the skin that⏎RIGHT ANTERIOR NECK LINE⏎include:⏎LEFT MID STERNUM⏎LEFT FOREARM⏎● Histologic subtype⏎FINAL PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:⏎● Margin status⏎A. Skin, right forehead, shave biopsy: Actinic keratosis.⏎● Presence or absence of invasion⏎B. Skin, right anterior neck line, shave biopsy: Basal cell carcinoma, nodular type; - present at peripheral and deep⏎beyond the reticular dermis (or for⏎margins.⏎C. Skin, left mid sternum, shave biopsy: Basal cell carcinoma, superficial type; - present at peripheral margin. Mohs specimens, anatomic level of⏎D. Skin, left forearm, shave biopsy: Squamous cell carcinoma in situ; - present at peripheral and deep margins. invasion)⏎● Perineural invasion where required⏎This case has been reviewed and dictated by⏎M.D., Ph.D., Board Certified Dermatopathologist.⏎⏎Introduction⏎Existing solutions fall short⏎Human abstraction Keyword based data extraction Rules based data extraction⏎● Expensive ● “thyroid, right isthmus nodule” ● “*bronc* biops*” would identify⏎● Time-consuming would miss a nodule on the left “bronchial biopsy”, “bronchus,⏎● Requires validation and training side of the thymus biopsy”, and “bronchial biopsies”⏎● Intractable to develop a ● Rules are complex to develop⏎complete set of discrete and brittle in practice⏎keywords ● High rate of false positives⏎● High rate of false negatives⏎⏎Introduction⏎Existing solutions fall short… sometimes⏎catastrophically⏎Rule Data Received⏎GE* biops* The patient is “ , George”; biopsy, left ear⏎⏎Introduction⏎Some valuable measures are completely⏎intractable with current solutions⏎SPECIMEN SOURCE: Measure Description⏎A. GASTRIC BIOPSY⏎B. MID ESOPHAGEAL BIOPSY⏎Percentage of pathology reports for gastric specimens⏎with intestinal metaplasia that comment on:⏎FINAL PATHOLOGIC DIAGNOSIS:⏎A. Stomach, biopsy:⏎● Presence or absence of H. pylori⏎- Intestinal metaplasia (complete-type), involving corpus/fundic mucosa.⏎● Presence or absence of dysplasia and if dysplasia⏎- Pathologic evidence of Helicobacter infection is not identified.⏎- See comment. is present, grade of dysplasia⏎● If dysplasia is absent, completeness of metaplasia⏎B. Esophagus, mid, biopsy:⏎- Squamous mucosa with no significant histopathologic change.⏎- Pathologic evidence of eosinophilic esophagitis is not identified. The College of American Pathologists had to abandon⏎automatic collection of this measure.⏎⏎2: A New Paradigm⏎Neurosymbolic AI for automated quality⏎measure abstraction⏎⏎A New Paradigm⏎Modern AI gives us tools to tackle this problem -⏎but a naive approach comes with significant risks⏎● Models directly hallucinate facts from the⏎medical record⏎The result:⏎● Models use ‘common sense definitions’, instead of⏎Insufficient accuracy⏎the precise definitions laid out in measures and high variance⏎● Model decision-making is opaque and arbitrary⏎⏎Current Status⏎Neurosymbolic AI breaks down the problem and⏎composes many different models together to solve it⏎⏎A New Paradigm⏎Neurosymbolic AI breaks down the problem and⏎composes many different models together to solve it…⏎● Measure definitions are broken down into⏎small sub-problems⏎○ How many samples are in the report?⏎○ Is a lesion in situ only?⏎○ Is a grading statement required?⏎● Custom models are used to solve each⏎sub-problem, and the solutions are⏎composed together⏎⏎A New Paradigm⏎… letting us leverage powerful models, with tight⏎constraints and auditable reasoning⏎● Use of AI is tightly scoped, minimizing⏎hallucination and forcing models to⏎use precise definitions⏎● Evaluation is highly consistent, resulting⏎in low-variability outputs⏎● Produces fully auditable abstraction⏎reasoning and confidence for every decision⏎⏎A New Paradigm⏎Reasoning and evidence are first-order outputs⏎Variable answer⏎Sourcing⏎into raw data for⏎evidence points⏎Supporting⏎evidence and⏎reasoning⏎⏎3: Current Status⏎Development, evaluation and results⏎⏎Current Status⏎Initial Neurosymbolic trees are generated from⏎existing manual abstraction instructions⏎⏎Current Status⏎Neurosymbolic trees are refined against real⏎and synthetic patient examples…⏎Edge cases are reviewed by⏎clinicians and clarifications⏎iteratively improve trees⏎⚠Case needs review⏎⏎Current Status⏎…with edge cases automatically identified and added⏎to the measure definition and model tree⏎Edge cases are reviewed by⏎clinicians and clarifications⏎iteratively improve trees⏎⚠Case needs review⏎⏎Current Status⏎Model evaluation process⏎● Evaluation was split over 4 different CAP ● Final decisions were made on each report by⏎measures. Reports were assigned to each using third-party RN abstraction managers⏎diagnosis codes. producing a gold-label.⏎● 500 pathology reports randomly subsampled ● The neurosymbolic models were then run on⏎from each measure from a feed of 100k reports the same reports and compared to the gold⏎- giving 2,000 reports. standard.⏎● All reports were independently double-⏎abstracted by experienced third-party⏎abstractors trained on each measure, giving⏎a human vs human IRR.⏎⏎Current Status⏎The result: Human-level abstraction performance⏎IRR Accuracy⏎Denominator⏎Exclusion⏎Numerator Human 0.918 95.4%⏎Pass⏎Numerator⏎Fail⏎0.922 95.7%⏎AI⏎Numerator⏎Exception⏎Denominator Numerator Numerator Numerator⏎No statistical difference between human⏎Exclusion Pass Fail Exception⏎and AI abstractors⏎Model Predicted⏎dloG⏎dradnatS⏎⏎4: Next Steps⏎Project evolution⏎⏎Current Status⏎Advantages of human-level automated abstraction⏎New measures can be clinically Reduced friction and increased Hugely accelerated measure⏎complex without adding burden value for partner practices development loop⏎● Complex measure data can be ● Practices don’t have to ● New measures can be applied⏎collected without requiring filter data before sending it instantly to a corpus of⏎costly abstraction to the registry pathology reports.⏎● Measures can be created to ● Vastly more accurate ● AI surfaces edge cases for⏎reflect new clinical practice measurement means review by measure developer⏎guidelines without adding dramatically fewer case to allow measure refinement⏎documentation burden corrections before deployment⏎⏎Next Steps⏎Next steps for this project⏎Adding new measures Expanding the Developing⏎training/testing set deployment pathway⏎● Continuing to train and⏎validate model on⏎● Different clinicians ● Goal is to enable the⏎additional complex⏎use different phrases College to deploy⏎Results will be⏎measures⏎Pharos abstraction to⏎● Different LISs capture⏎collect data for new, published!⏎● Learnings from new data differently—if⏎clinically interesting⏎measures provide⏎you’ve seen one LIS,⏎measures⏎opportunities to⏎you’ve seen one LIS⏎improve existing⏎measures⏎⏎Next Steps⏎What else can AI do for us?⏎Current pathology measures are More complex measures that Beyond measurement: AI to⏎limited: process measures focusing would be a burden on a practice help practices identify areas of⏎on documentation improvement⏎● Example: “diagnostic uncertainty”⏎● Partly a limitation due to the measurement—can we quantify ● Subtle opportunities⏎complexity of narrative reports how often a practice uses phrases⏎● Turnaround time for specific⏎such as “consistent with” or⏎● PRO/PROMs are not possible for specimen types, e.g. placental⏎“suspicious for”? Stratify by⏎pathology (right now) specimens⏎specimen type? By anatomic⏎● What about outcome/ location? ● Clinical practice guideline failures⏎intermediate outcome measures? not present in measures⏎⏎Next Steps⏎Other use cases: Automated detection of⏎clinical practice guideline deviation⏎⏎Next Steps⏎Other use cases: Automated detection⏎& analysis of safety events⏎⏎Acknowledgements⏎Sarah O’Connor⏎former Assistant Director, Pathologists Quality Registry⏎Dr Gregary Bocsi, DO, FCAP⏎Chair, Value-Based Payment and Healthcare Policy Committee, CAP⏎Dr Thomas Cooper, MD, FCAP⏎Member, Value-Based Payment and Healthcare Policy Committee, CAP⏎The Team at Kiser Healthcare Solutions⏎⏎Questions?⏎Speak with us at Table 10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0060♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Mitchell♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-one years as a nurse, the last twelve of them in a rural Alaska clinic, and I've never once thought that speed and safety are the same thing. HHS seems to be treating them that way in this request for information.⏎⏎The question I keep coming back to is the one the RFI doesn't really ask directly: what happens to the patients who are already underserved when you scale up a tool that was never trained on data that looks like them? Because I watched it happen. Two winters ago our clinic piloted an AI triage tool and over a period of about eight weeks I and two of my colleagues noticed that Alaska Native patients were being flagged as lower acuity at rates that didn't match what we were seeing clinically. Not edge cases. A pattern. We were manually overriding the system regularly before anyone in administration would even sit down with us and look at the numbers. I don't know the specifics of how that tool was trained or what populations were in the dataset, but I know what I saw.⏎⏎That experience is part of why I'm skeptical of the framing here. The RFI talks about "accelerating adoption" and reducing "barriers" like regulatory requirements are the main thing standing between patients and better care. But the barrier for my patients isn't that doctors don't have enough AI tools. The barrier is that I spent two hours on the phone one afternoon trying to get a neurologist on the line for a patient showing stroke symptoms because the nearest neurology practice is roughly 400 miles away. That's a geographic and infrastructure problem, and an AI tool that routes my triage decisions through a biased algorithm doesn't fix it. It just adds a layer of apparent authority to whatever mistake gets made.⏎⏎I want to be fair here -- I do think there are probably applications where AI genuinely helps in rural settings, remote diagnostics, maybe some imaging interpretation, I'm not opposed to the technology on principle. But the documented problems with algorithmic bias in clinical settings are not hypothetical. The literature on pulse oximetry errors in patients with darker skin, the sepsis prediction tools that perform worse for Black patients, the prior authorization algorithms with higher denial rates for Latino patients -- these are real findings. If HHS accelerates adoption before there are mandatory, standardized bias auditing requirements with actual teeth, those problems don't stay contained to pilot programs. They get built into standard of care and then nobody questions them because the system says it's fine.⏎⏎What I'd ask HHS to actually do is require that any AI clinical decision support tool seeking federal reimbursement or certification undergo bias auditing against disaggregated demographic data, including tribal populations, before deployment -- not after. And that audit data should be public, not just available to the developer. Right now there's no consistent standard for what that even looks like, and a lot of the "transparency" requirements I've read about still leave the actual performance data with the vendor.⏎⏎I also think the oversight question is getting glossed over. The RFI mentions "appropriate human oversight" in a few places but doesn't engage with the reality of a rural clinic where one nurse is managing four things at once and the path of least resistance is to accept the AI output because there's no time to second-guess it. That's not a hypothetical workflow. That's Tuesday.⏎⏎I'm not sure who else submits comments on things like this from rural Alaska, which is part of why I bothered to write this one, but I'd genuinely like to know if HHS has any specific plan for how tribal health programs and small rural clinics are supposed to participate in the monitoring and reporting processes they're describing, because the logistics of that alone are♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0061♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ShiftOS, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ShiftOS♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: ShiftOS, Inc.⏎Contact: Autumn-Kyoko Cushman, Co-Founder & CEO⏎Date: February 2026⏎Docket Identifier: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎I. Introduction⏎ShiftOS is a healthcare technology company developing AI-powered workforce operations⏎software for healthcare organizations. Our product, Holly, is an AI scheduling agent that⏎automates the complex, time-intensive work of managing clinical staff schedules; including shift⏎assignments, credential compliance, call-offs, shift swaps, and float pool optimization.⏎My background informs our approach: I served as a Hospital Corpsman in the U.S. Navy,⏎worked at the National Cancer Institute on the Cancer Moonshot initiative under the NCI⏎Associate Director, and led data delivery operations at IBM Watson Health working with⏎MarketScan claims data. My co-founder spent nearly six years as a pharmacy technician at⏎CVS while completing school, witnessing firsthand the administrative burden that drives clinician⏎burnout.⏎We are currently deploying Holly with enterprise health systems and have established⏎integration partnerships with major workforce management platforms including Workday. Our⏎perspective on AI adoption barriers comes from direct experience navigating procurement, IT⏎integration, and clinical operations stakeholder alignment at large healthcare organizations.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide input on this RFI and offer the following responses to⏎selected questions.⏎II. Responses to Specific Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for⏎health care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎⏎The barriers we encounter most frequently are not technical — they are structural and⏎procedural:⏎1. Fragmented procurement and IT governance.⏎Healthcare organizations often lack clear pathways for evaluating and adopting operational AI⏎tools. Clinical AI (diagnostic, imaging) has established FDA frameworks and clinical validation⏎processes. Operational AI: scheduling, staffing, administrative automation, falls outside these⏎frameworks but still faces lengthy procurement cycles (often 12-18 months) because⏎organizations default to enterprise IT evaluation processes designed for EHRs and clinical⏎systems. There is no proportionate, streamlined pathway for lower-risk operational AI.⏎2. Integration complexity with legacy workforce systems.⏎Health systems operate fragmented technology stacks: one system for scheduling, another for⏎time and attendance, another for credentialing, another for payroll. These systems rarely⏎interoperate well. AI tools that could automate scheduling decisions require read/write access to⏎multiple systems simultaneously: scheduling, credential databases, union rules engines, payroll;⏎and each integration requires separate security reviews, BAAs, and technical work. The⏎absence of standard APIs or interoperability requirements for workforce management systems⏎creates friction that delays deployment by months.⏎3. Credentialing and compliance data silos.⏎Real-time scheduling decisions require real-time visibility into staff credentials, licenses,⏎certifications, and competencies. This data is typically locked in credentialing software that does⏎not expose it programmatically, forcing manual verification or batch exports. This limits AI's⏎ability to make compliant staffing decisions autonomously.⏎4. Risk aversion without clear accountability frameworks.⏎Healthcare leaders are uncertain about liability when AI systems make or recommend staffing⏎decisions. Who is accountable if an AI schedules an under-credentialed provider? The lack of⏎clear guidance from HHS on accountability for non-medical-device AI creates hesitation among⏎compliance officers and legal teams, slowing adoption even when operational leaders are⏎enthusiastic.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes⏎should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and⏎why?⏎1. Extend interoperability mandates to workforce management systems.⏎⏎The 21st Century Cures Act and ONC's information blocking rules have improved data liquidity⏎for clinical information. Similar interoperability requirements should apply to workforce⏎management, credentialing, and time/attendance systems. Health systems should be able to⏎access their own workforce data. Schedules, credentials, availability — through standardized⏎APIs without vendor lock-in or prohibitive integration fees.⏎Recommendation: HHS should consider whether workforce management data constitutes a⏎category of information that, when siloed, impedes the effective delivery of care, and evaluate⏎extending information blocking provisions or publishing voluntary interoperability standards for⏎this data category.⏎2. Clarify accountability frameworks for operational AI.⏎HHS should publish guidance distinguishing operational AI (scheduling, staffing, administrative⏎automation) from clinical decision support and medical devices. This guidance should address⏎liability allocation, documentation requirements, and human oversight expectations for AI⏎systems that make non-clinical operational decisions.⏎This clarity would reduce legal uncertainty that currently delays procurement and allow health⏎systems to adopt operational AI with appropriate, but not excessive, governance.⏎3. Recognize administrative burden reduction as a quality and safety priority.⏎CMS quality programs and value-based payment models do not currently recognize⏎administrative efficiency as a measured outcome. Yet administrative burden is a primary driver⏎of clinician burnout, which directly impacts care quality, safety, and workforce retention.⏎Recommendation: HHS should explore whether workforce stability metrics (turnover, vacancy⏎rates, time-to-fill) and administrative burden measures could be incorporated into quality⏎reporting or value-based payment adjustments, creating financial incentives for health systems⏎to invest in operational AI that reduces burden.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health⏎care organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical⏎care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Key decision-makers for operational AI adoption:⏎● Chief Nursing Officers (CNOs) and VP/Directors of Nursing Operations — These⏎leaders feel the pain of scheduling complexity most acutely and are often initial⏎champions.⏎● VP of Operations or Chief Operating Officer — Controls budget authority and⏎operational priorities.⏎⏎● IT Security and Compliance — Gatekeepers who evaluate data handling, integration⏎risk, and vendor security posture.⏎● HRIS/Workforce Management System Owners — Often skeptical of tools that interact⏎with "their" systems; can slow or block integration approvals.⏎Primary administrative hurdles:⏎1. No single owner for operational AI. Unlike clinical AI (which falls under CMO/CMIO⏎purview), operational AI spans nursing, HR, IT, and operations. This creates confusion⏎about who evaluates, approves, and owns these tools.⏎2. Procurement processes designed for large capital expenditures. AI tools often have⏎SaaS pricing models with lower upfront costs but recurring fees. Procurement teams⏎trained on capital equipment evaluation struggle to assess these models, leading to⏎delays.⏎3. Security review bottlenecks. Enterprise security teams are overwhelmed; vendor⏎security reviews can take 3-6 months even for low-risk operational tools.⏎4. Change management resistance. Unit managers and schedulers who have "always⏎done it this way" can resist AI tools that change their workflows, even if the tools reduce⏎their burden. Successful adoption requires executive sponsorship and frontline⏎engagement simultaneously.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities,⏎fuel research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?⏎Workforce and operational data interoperability is critically underserved.⏎The following data types, if made more interoperable, would significantly accelerate AI adoption⏎for clinical workforce operations:⏎Data Type Current State Impact of Interoperability⏎Staff schedules Locked in proprietary scheduling systems AI could optimize schedules,⏎(Kronos/UKG, API Healthcare, ShiftWizard) predict gaps, automate⏎with limited API access assignments⏎Credentials and Siloed in credentialing software (Symplr, Real-time credential verification⏎licenses MD-Staff, CredentialStream) for compliant autonomous⏎scheduling⏎Time and Fragmented across payroll and HRIS Closed-loop scheduling → time⏎attendance systems (ADP, Paycom, Workday) tracking → payroll automation⏎⏎Float pool / Often managed in spreadsheets or AI could match available staff to⏎PRN availability standalone systems open shifts across facilities⏎Union and Typically hardcoded or manually enforced AI could enforce complex work⏎contract rules rules automatically⏎Recommendation: HHS should convene stakeholders like health systems, workforce⏎technology vendors, and AI developers, to develop voluntary interoperability standards or a⏎common data model for workforce operations data, similar to USCDI for clinical data.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish⏎to see addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎From our conversations with health system leaders and frontline staff, the challenges most⏎directly tied to workforce AI include:⏎For patients:⏎● Staff continuity. Patients, particularly in post-acute, long-term care, and oncology⏎settings really value seeing consistent caregivers. AI-optimized scheduling can prioritize⏎continuity assignments.⏎● Reduced wait times. Understaffing due to last-minute call-offs creates delays. AI that⏎rapidly fills gaps improves patient access.⏎● Safety. Fatigued, burned-out staff make more errors. Reducing administrative burden⏎and optimizing schedules for appropriate rest improves safety.⏎For caregivers (clinical staff):⏎● Schedule predictability and fairness. Manual scheduling often feels arbitrary or⏎biased. AI can apply rules consistently.⏎● Reduced last-minute changes. AI can anticipate gaps and fill them proactively rather⏎than calling staff at the last minute.⏎● Less time on administrative tasks. Nurses and managers spend hours on scheduling,⏎swap approvals, and credential checks. Automating this returns time to patient care.⏎Concern to address: Caregivers express concern about AI making decisions that affect their⏎work lives without transparency. Any workforce AI must be explainable. Staff should understand⏎why they were assigned a particular shift and must preserve human override capability.⏎III. Summary Recommendations⏎⏎1. Extend interoperability policy attention to workforce management systems to⏎unlock the data AI needs to optimize clinical operations.⏎2. Publish clear guidance distinguishing operational AI from clinical AI and medical⏎devices, addressing accountability, documentation, and oversight expectations⏎proportionate to risk.⏎3. Recognize administrative burden reduction and workforce stability as quality-⏎adjacent outcomes that merit measurement and incentive alignment in CMS programs.⏎4. Convene a public-private working group on workforce data standards to accelerate⏎voluntary interoperability for scheduling, credentialing, and time/attendance systems.⏎IV. Conclusion⏎AI's greatest near-term impact in healthcare may not be in diagnosis or imaging — it may be in⏎relieving the operational and administrative burden that drives clinician burnout and workforce⏎shortages. The barriers to adoption are not primarily technical; they are structural, procedural,⏎and policy-driven. HHS has an opportunity to accelerate responsible adoption of operational AI⏎by clarifying accountability, extending interoperability principles to workforce data, and⏎recognizing administrative efficiency as a component of care quality.⏎ShiftOS welcomes further engagement with HHS on these issues and is available to provide⏎additional detail or participate in stakeholder convenings.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Autumn-Kyoko Cushman⏎Co-Founder & CEO, ShiftOS, Inc.⏎autumnkyoko@shiftrx.io♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0062♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from MLCommons, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MLCommons♔♔♔♔♔♔MLCommons Medical AI Working Group⏎Response to HHS Request for Information and Proposed Rule⏎Advancing the Quintuple Aim Through AI-Enabled Healthcare⏎Response to:⏎1. Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (Document No. 2025-23641, RIN 0955-AA13)⏎2. Proposed Rule: Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability: ASTP/ONC Deregulatory⏎Actions To Unleash Prosperity (Document No. 2025-23896, RIN 0955-AA09)⏎Submitting Organization⏎MLCommons’ Medical AI Working Group⏎Contact: medical-chairs@mlcommons.org⏎Submission Date: 2026-02-22⏎⏎Table of Contents⏎About MLCommons and the Medical AI Working Group............................................................3⏎Executive Summary...................................................................................................................5⏎Part I: Response to RFI on Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care.......................................7⏎Framing: AI as an Enabler of the Quintuple Aim..................................................................7⏎Regulation............................................................................................................................7⏎Reimbursement....................................................................................................................8⏎Research & Development....................................................................................................9⏎Responses to Specific Questions.......................................................................................11⏎Part II: Response to Proposed Rule on Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability...........21⏎Support for FHIR-Based Approach....................................................................................21⏎Comments on Specific Provisions......................................................................................21⏎Considerations for AI-Enabled Health IT............................................................................24⏎Alignment Between Initiatives: A Unified Vision for the Quintuple Aim....................................25⏎Conclusion............................................................................................................................... 26⏎Acknowledgments....................................................................................................................26⏎Appendix: Mapping MLCommons Infrastructure to HHS Initiatives.........................................27⏎⏎About MLCommons and the Medical AI Working Group⏎MLCommons is a non-profit consortium that aims to accelerate the benefits of machine learning⏎and artificial intelligence. Our members and partners include over 125 organizations from⏎around the world, many of which are leading technology companies, startups, academics, and⏎nonprofits that are actively researching, developing, and deploying artificial intelligence products⏎for customers. Critically, our founding membership includes academic researchers at the⏎forefront of machine learning research, and the research community continues to be core to our⏎membership helping to lead many of our working groups. MLCommons acts as a neutral nexus⏎for commercial and non-commercial actors to collaborate on tools that advance the field.⏎We create, operate and maintain community assets, especially benchmarks and datasets, that⏎facilitate developing and evaluating artificial intelligence (AI) systems in pursuit of our mission to⏎“make artificial intelligence better for everyone.”[1] The original project that brought⏎MLCommons into being is a benchmarking suite called MLPerf, which provides objective⏎evaluations of training and inference speed for AI hardware and software.[2] These⏎measurements enable a fair comparison of competing systems, accelerate ML progress through⏎fair and useful measurement, enforce reproducibility to ensure reliable results, and do so in an⏎open and collaborative way to keep benchmarking affordable for all participants.⏎The MLCommons Medical AI Working Group develops benchmarks and best practices that help⏎accelerate AI development in healthcare through an open, neutral, and scientific approach. Our⏎efforts are geared towards creating the fabric necessary for proper benchmarking of medical AI⏎through:⏎(1) developing the federated MedPerf platform[3] and GaNDLF framework[4],⏎(2) establishing shared standards for benchmarking medical AI,⏎(3) incorporating and disseminating best practices, and⏎(4) creating a robust medical AI ecosystem by partnering with key global stakeholders across⏎sectors.⏎Our Platform & Tools:⏎Tool Purpose Relevance to HHS Initiatives⏎MedPerf Federated benchmarking platform Enables AI evaluation across varied⏎for evaluating AI on real-world populations while preserving privacy;⏎medical data without data leaving supports post-market monitoring⏎provider premises⏎GaNDLF Zero/low-code framework for Accelerates AI development with⏎developing ML workflows in standardized, reproducible pipelines⏎healthcare⏎⏎Croissant Metadata format for ML-ready Addresses data interoperability and⏎datasets[5] reproducibility challenges⏎AILuminate Benchmark suite for evaluating AI Supports safety evaluation for clinical⏎risk and reliability[6] AI systems⏎MedPerf is currently being utilized in the largest federated AI research study for⏎postoperative glioblastoma patients (FeTS 2.0), demonstrating that federated evaluation can⏎operate at scale across 53 institutions globally.[7] This study analyzed 10,208 follow-up⏎timepoints from postoperative glioblastoma patients, achieving record performance that⏎exceeded centralized approaches while ensuring patient imaging data never left institutional⏎premises.⏎Our working group has included participation from patient groups, academic medical centers⏎(e.g., Harvard Medical School, Indiana University, Stanford University School of Medicine,⏎University of Pennsylvania, Weill Cornell Medicine, Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, among⏎others), commercial companies (e.g., Google, NVIDIA, IBM Research, Intel, Hugging Face,⏎Microsoft, John Snow Labs, etc.), healthcare institutions (e.g., Brigham and Women’s Hospital,⏎Dana-Farber Cancer Institute, etc.), and non-profit organizations spanning multiple countries.⏎For more information, visit mlcommons.org/medical or contact medical-chairs@mlcommons.org⏎⏎Executive Summary⏎This response addresses two interconnected HHS initiatives: the Request for Information on⏎Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13) and the proposed deregulatory rule⏎on Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability (RIN 0955-AA09). We support both initiatives⏎and believe they represent a historic opportunity to advance the Quintuple Aim through⏎responsible AI deployment:⏎Quintuple Aim How AI Can Advance It How These HHS Initiatives⏎Dimension Enable It⏎Improving Population AI-enabled screening, early FHIR APIs enable⏎Health detection, risk stratification population-level analytics;⏎across populations reduced information blocking⏎enables public health AI⏎Enhancing Patient Reduced wait times, better Interoperability enables AI to⏎Experience care coordination, synthesize information across⏎personalized treatment providers⏎Reducing Per Capita Automation of administrative Deregulation reduces⏎Cost tasks, reduced unnecessary compliance burden; federated⏎testing, optimized resource evaluation reduces duplicate⏎allocation validation costs; transparency⏎reduces friction⏎Improving Provider Clinical documentation Streamlined AI integration⏎Work Life assistance, decision support, through standardized APIs;⏎reduced alert fatigue reduced administrative⏎overhead⏎Healthcare Delivery Performance monitoring Federated evaluation across⏎Optimization across demographics, geographically varied sites;⏎identification of performance standardized data enables⏎variations, expanded access performance optimization⏎Our recommendations are drawn from our collective expertise and experiences as a technical⏎collaborative community of ML researchers and engineers in industry and academia. We have⏎previously provided detailed technical recommendations to the FDA regarding real-world⏎evaluation of AI-enabled medical devices (Docket No. FDA-2025-N-4203), and this response⏎builds upon that foundation while addressing the broader policy questions raised by HHS.⏎⏎AI is poised to transform healthcare delivery, but adoption can be slow unless standardized⏎evaluation of available AI tools matures to the point of establishing trust and credibility amongst⏎adopters and patients alike. MLCommons is the leading AI evaluation organization in the world,⏎and its Medical working group is an open collaborative community working to bring⏎multidisciplinary expertise in AI evaluation to the problems of healthcare delivery specifically⏎through a federated evaluation approach that respects data and model ownerships.⏎Key Themes of Our Response:⏎1. Interoperability is foundational for AI and the Quintuple Aim: The proposed⏎FHIR-forward approach directly enables AI evaluation, benchmarking, and deployment⏎at scale. Standardized data access through FHIR APIs creates the technical⏎infrastructure necessary for AI systems to integrate into clinical workflows and deliver on⏎all five aims. AI governance described through technical policies can bring transparency⏎and integrity of sensitive workloads.⏎2. Federated evaluation addresses critical barriers while optimizing delivery: Many⏎barriers to AI adoption stem from data access challenges and evaluation difficulties. Our⏎MedPerf platform demonstrates that federated approaches can enable robust AI⏎evaluation while preserving data privacy, security, and enabling performance⏎assessment across varied populations.⏎3. Open, neutral benchmarking accelerates trust and reduces costs: Industry-driven,⏎transparent evaluation methods provide the foundation for clinician and patient trust in AI⏎systems while reducing duplicative validation efforts. HHS can support these efforts⏎through cooperative agreements and recognition of community-developed standards.⏎4. Deregulation should preserve AI safety guardrails: While we support reducing⏎unnecessary certification burden, we encourage HHS to ensure that mechanisms for⏎evaluating AI safety and effectiveness remain robust, particularly for high-risk clinical⏎applications. Technical policies can help facilitate such requirements.⏎5. American leadership requires coordinated action: The United States can cement its⏎position as the global leader in clinical AI through the coordinated approach represented⏎by these initiatives. Open standards and federated infrastructure developed here can⏎become the global model.⏎Part I: Response to RFI on Accelerating AI Adoption in⏎Clinical Care⏎Framing: AI as an Enabler of the Quintuple Aim⏎⏎Before addressing specific questions, we emphasize that AI adoption should be evaluated⏎through the lens of the Quintuple Aim. AI tools that improve one dimension while degrading⏎another (e.g., improving efficiency while creating performance gaps across populations) should⏎be considered unfit for adoption in clinical workflow. The evaluation and monitoring frameworks⏎we propose below are designed to assess AI impact across all five dimensions.⏎Regulation⏎HHS seeks feedback on how current regulations impact AI adoption. We offer the following⏎observations:⏎Alignment Across Agencies is Critical⏎The healthcare AI ecosystem involves multiple regulatory touchpoints: FDA for medical devices,⏎CMS for reimbursement, and ONC for health IT certification. We commend HHS for taking a⏎coordinated approach through this RFI. However, we note that AI systems often span multiple⏎regulatory categories. For example, an AI system that assists with clinical decision-making may:⏎Require FDA clearance as a medical device⏎Need to integrate with certified EHR systems under ONC rules⏎Affect billing and documentation practices under CMS programs⏎We recommend that HHS establish clear guidance on regulatory pathways that accounts for⏎these overlapping jurisdictions. A unified framework would reduce uncertainty for developers,⏎accelerate time-to-deployment for beneficial AI tools, and ultimately advance the Quintuple Aim⏎by removing barriers to innovation.⏎Support for Non-Device AI Evaluation⏎For AI tools that fall outside FDA medical device jurisdiction, there is currently limited guidance⏎on appropriate evaluation methods. Our working group has developed extensive experience in⏎AI evaluation frameworks that could inform voluntary guidance for non-device clinical AI.⏎We propose the following evaluation framework aligned with the Quintuple Aim:⏎Evaluation Domain Key Metrics Quintuple Aim Alignment⏎Clinical Safety False negative rates, adverse Population Health, Patient⏎events, time-to-intervention delays Experience⏎Effectiveness Sensitivity/specificity, diagnostic Population Health⏎concordance, outcome prediction⏎accuracy⏎Reliability System uptime, prediction Patient Experience,⏎confidence, error rates by scenario Provider Work Life⏎⏎Performance Performance stratified by Healthcare Delivery⏎Consistency demographics, geographic access, Optimization⏎language support⏎Efficiency Time savings, cost reduction, Per Capita Cost, Provider⏎workflow integration Work Life⏎Provider Impact Override rates, alert fatigue Provider Work Life⏎indicators, documentation burden⏎Reimbursement⏎Payment policy significantly influences AI adoption and directly affects whether AI advances or⏎undermines the Quintuple Aim.⏎Evidence Generation Support⏎Many promising AI tools lack the real-world evidence necessary to support coverage decisions.⏎HHS could accelerate evidence generation by:⏎1. Supporting federated evaluation networks that enable AI assessment across varied⏎clinical sites without requiring data centralization⏎2. Funding prospective studies of AI tool impact on clinical outcomes and costs⏎3. Establishing data sharing agreements that facilitate AI evaluation while protecting patient⏎privacy⏎4. Requiring AI tools to demonstrate performance across varied populations as a condition⏎of coverage (optimizing healthcare delivery)⏎Research & Development⏎HHS’s research ecosystem can catalyze AI innovation that advances the Quintuple Aim.⏎Public-Private Partnerships for Benchmarking Infrastructure⏎MLCommons operates as a neutral convener of industry, academic, and non-profit stakeholders⏎to develop benchmarking tools. This model has proven effective for AI hardware/software⏎benchmarking through MLPerf and is now being applied to medical AI through MedPerf. HHS⏎could support similar efforts through:⏎● Cooperative Research and Development Agreements (CRADAs) with benchmark⏎developers⏎● Funding for broad, representative benchmark datasets that enable performance⏎optimization⏎● Recognition of community-developed evaluation standards (such as MLCommons’⏎Croissant for dataset metadata and MLCommons’ AILuminate for AI safety)⏎● Support for federated infrastructure that enables evaluation without data centralization⏎Federated Research Infrastructure: The FeTS 2.0 Model⏎⏎Our MedPerf platform demonstrates that federated evaluation can enable AI assessment on⏎real-world clinical data without requiring data centralization. The Federated Tumor⏎Segmentation (FeTS) 2.0 study provides concrete evidence of this approach at scale:⏎Metric FeTS 2.0 (Federated) BraTS 2024 (Centralized)⏎Participating institutions 53 global sites Centralized⏎Training data 8,100 timepoints 1,350 timepoints⏎Validation data 2,108 timepoints 397 timepoints⏎Total patient data 10,208 timepoints 1,747 timepoints⏎Peritumoral edema DSC 0.95 0.89⏎Enhancing tumor DSC 0.94 0.79⏎Resection cavity DSC 0.89 0.78⏎Necrosis DSC 0.77 0.81⏎Key findings from FeTS 2.0:⏎Scale without data sharing: 53 institutions contributed data while patient imaging never⏎left institutional premises. Only trained model weights and validation scores were⏎shared between collaborators.⏎Superior performance: The federated approach achieved record Dice Similarity⏎Coefficients, outperforming the centralized BraTS 2024 study in three of four⏎segmentation classes (peritumoral edema, enhancing tumor, resection cavity) while⏎achieving comparable results for necrosis.⏎Privacy preservation: Executed through MLCommons’ MedPerf orchestrator and Intel’s⏎OpenFL framework, demonstrating that privacy-preserving approaches do not⏎sacrifice performance.⏎Clinical impact: The resulting AI model enables reliable delineation of postoperative⏎regions-of-interest, supporting standardized glioblastoma response assessment and⏎adaptive therapeutic decision-making worldwide.⏎This study demonstrates that federated approaches can achieve superior results compared⏎to centralized methods while respecting data privacy, a finding directly relevant to HHS policy⏎on data access and AI evaluation.⏎We recommend that NIH and other HHS research entities invest in federated infrastructure that⏎can support both AI development and post-deployment monitoring while enabling performance⏎optimization across varied populations.⏎⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Based on our experience working with a broad range of stakeholders, the primary barriers⏎include:⏎Barrier Description Impact on Potential Solution⏎Quintuple Aim⏎Data Access Privacy regulations, Limits AI Technical Policies,⏎institutional policies, and development Metadata (Croissant)⏎technical barriers limit and performance Federated evaluation⏎access to clinical data optimization (MedPerf)⏎Evaluation Developers lack access to Undermines Standardized⏎Infrastructure representative populations safety and benchmark datasets;⏎for prospective evaluation performance federated testing⏎consistency⏎Regulatory Limited guidance for Delays Harmonized HHS⏎Uncertainty non-device AI deployment of guidance⏎beneficial tools⏎Integration Fragmented EHR landscape Increases cost, FHIR API⏎Complexity requires custom integrations limits adoption standardization⏎Trust Deficit Clinicians lack confidence in Reduces Open benchmarking;⏎AI performance adoption of transparency⏎effective tools requirements⏎Performance Fear that AI will create Limits adoption Stratified performance⏎Variation performance gaps across in reporting⏎Concerns populations resource-limited⏎settings⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize?⏎We recommend HHS prioritize changes that advance all five dimensions of the Quintuple Aim:⏎1. Harmonized guidance on non-device clinical AI evaluation: Clear standards for⏎evaluation methods, performance metrics, and ongoing monitoring for AI tools outside⏎FDA jurisdiction. This guidance should explicitly require performance optimization⏎assessment.⏎2. Support for federated evaluation infrastructure: Funding and policy support for⏎platforms that enable AI evaluation on real-world data while preserving privacy and⏎enabling performance assessment across varied populations.⏎⏎3. Accelerated coverage pathways for AI tools with demonstrated Quintuple Aim⏎impact: Streamlined processes for incorporating AI tools that demonstrate improvement⏎across multiple aim dimensions.⏎4. Interoperability requirements that support AI integration: The proposed⏎FHIR-forward approach in the deregulatory rule directly addresses this need.⏎5. Performance optimization requirements: AI tools seeking coverage should⏎demonstrate consistent performance across demographic groups, with particular⏎attention to populations underrepresented in training data.⏎Specific regulatory provisions that could be revisited include:⏎● 45 CFR 164.514 (HIPAA de-identification): Clarify that federated evaluation⏎approaches where protected health information never leaves covered entities can satisfy⏎HIPAA requirements. This would unlock evaluation across varied populations without⏎compromising privacy.⏎● 42 CFR 414 (Medicare Part B): Consider demonstration projects for AI tool⏎reimbursement in value-based arrangements, with explicit requirements for performance⏎optimization assessment.⏎● 42 CFR 425 (MSSP): Allow ACOs to count AI-enabled quality improvement activities⏎toward shared savings calculations.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist?⏎Key issues include:⏎1. Algorithm transparency: Requirements for disclosing AI system characteristics,⏎limitations, and performance vary. Standardized transparency requirements would⏎benefit all stakeholders. MLCommons has developed approaches for AI documentation⏎through model cards and our AILuminate framework that could inform such⏎requirements.⏎2. Continuous learning systems: AI systems that update based on local data raise⏎questions about validation requirements and version control. Our working group has⏎developed approaches for versioning and lineage tracking that could inform guidance.⏎3. Performance optimization and calibration: AI systems may perpetuate or amplify⏎existing differential healthcare outcomes. Emerging research has identified specific⏎systematic error recognition and correction strategies, but legal frameworks for⏎addressing algorithmic performance variation in healthcare are underdeveloped. HHS⏎could provide guidance on acceptable performance differentials across demographic⏎groups.⏎4. Data ownership and access: When AI systems are trained or fine-tuned on institutional⏎data, questions arise about data ownership, model ownership, and ongoing access⏎rights. Information blocking regulations should address these scenarios.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation⏎methods?⏎⏎Based on our experience developing and deploying evaluation infrastructure, we recommend a⏎comprehensive framework:⏎Pre-Deployment Evaluation Framework:⏎Evaluation Type Methods Tools/Standards⏎Performance Assessment Benchmark MedPerf, domain-specific⏎testing on benchmarks⏎standardized⏎datasets[8]⏎Performance Optimization Stratified Croissant metadata for⏎Assessment performance demographic tracking⏎analysis across⏎demographics⏎Robustness Testing Evaluation Statistical tests (MMD, KS)⏎against data⏎quality variations,⏎distribution shifts⏎Safety Evaluation Failure mode AILuminate, curated edge case⏎analysis, edge repositories⏎case testing⏎Human Factors Usability testing, FDA human factors guidance⏎workflow (adapted)⏎integration⏎assessment⏎Post-Deployment Monitoring Framework:⏎Monitoring Level Frequency Activities Quintuple Aim Focus⏎Continuous Real-time System output Patient safety⏎tracking, safety⏎signals⏎Weekly 7 days Automated Reliability⏎performance⏎summaries,⏎statistical⏎process control⏎⏎Monthly 30 days Comprehensive Healthcare Delivery⏎reports with trend Optimization, effectiveness⏎analysis,⏎optimization⏎metrics⏎Quarterly 90 days Clinical expert Safety, provider experience⏎review of edge⏎cases and failure⏎modes⏎Annual Yearly Full re-evaluation All dimensions⏎against updated⏎standards⏎Statistical Methods for Drift Detection:⏎● Control charts (CUSUM, EWMA) for detecting performance drift⏎● Maximum mean discrepancy (MMD) for distribution shift detection⏎● Sequential probability ratio tests for early warning⏎● Calibration assessment comparing predictions to outcomes⏎HHS could support these methods through:⏎● Cooperative agreements with organizations developing evaluation infrastructure⏎● Grant funding for evaluation tool development and validation studies⏎● Prize competitions for innovative approaches to AI monitoring⏎● Recognition of community-developed standards as acceptable evaluation methods⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use?⏎We recommend actions that reduce barriers while ensuring AI advances the Quintuple Aim:⏎1. Recognition of industry-developed standards: HHS could formally recognize⏎community-developed evaluation standards (like those from MLCommons) as⏎acceptable methods for demonstrating AI safety and effectiveness. This would reduce⏎duplicative validation costs.⏎2. Public benchmarking infrastructure: Support for open, neutral benchmarking⏎platforms that enable fair comparison of AI systems. This promotes competition and⏎drives quality improvement.⏎3. Federated evaluation networks: Fund the development of federated infrastructure that⏎allows AI evaluation across varied clinical sites without data centralization. This directly⏎enables performance optimization.⏎4. Accreditation pathway development: Work with professional societies and⏎accreditation bodies to develop AI competency frameworks for clinical institutions.⏎⏎5. Safe harbor for good-faith evaluation: Provide legal protection for institutions that⏎share data for AI evaluation purposes and for developers that transparently report⏎performance issues.⏎6. Workforce development: Support training programs for clinical informaticists, data⏎scientists, and AI safety specialists who can oversee AI deployment.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎expectations and where have they fallen short?⏎Our working group has observed the following patterns:⏎Successes (advancing multiple Quintuple Aim dimensions):⏎Application Quintuple Aim Impact Key Success Factors⏎Diabetic retinopathy Population health, expanded Well-defined task, clear ground⏎screening access, cost truth, regulatory clarity⏎Clinical Provider work life, cost Addresses acute pain point,⏎documentation/ambi measurable time savings⏎ent scribing⏎Sepsis early Population health, patient Clear clinical value, integration⏎warning experience into existing workflows⏎Radiology triage Patient experience, population Reduces delays for urgent⏎(critical findings) health cases⏎Challenges (where AI has fallen short):⏎Challenge Quintuple Aim Impact Root Cause⏎Performance Healthcare Delivery Optimization Training data not⏎degradation on new representative; lack of⏎populations federated evaluation⏎Alert fatigue Provider work life High false-positive⏎rates; poor workflow⏎integration⏎Outcome Population health, cost Intermediate metrics⏎improvement unclear don’t translate to⏎patient outcomes⏎Variable Healthcare Delivery Optimization Insufficient evaluation⏎performance across across subgroups⏎demographics⏎⏎Integration friction Cost, provider work life Lack of standardized⏎APIs; custom⏎integration required⏎⏎Greatest Potential (pending barrier removal):⏎● AI systems that reduce administrative burden (advancing provider work life and cost)⏎● Population health management and risk stratification (advancing population health and⏎delivery optimization)⏎● Care coordination across fragmented health systems (advancing patient experience)⏎● Federated AI that learns from distributed data while preserving privacy (advancing all⏎dimensions)⏎Question 7: Which roles within health care organizations have the most influence on AI⏎adoption?⏎Based on our interactions with a broad range of health systems:⏎Role Influence Level Primary Concerns How HHS Can Help⏎CMIO/CMAO Very High Clinical safety, workflow Clear evaluation⏎integration, evidence standards, recognized⏎benchmarks⏎CIO/CTO High Security, integration, FHIR standardization,⏎vendor management security guidance⏎Clinical High Specialty-specific value, Specialty society⏎Champions peer acceptance engagement, pilot⏎funding⏎Legal/Compliance High (veto Liability, regulatory risk Clear guidance, safe⏎power) harbors⏎CFO/Finance High ROI, reimbursement Coverage pathways,⏎value demonstration⏎Nursing Medium-High Workflow, patient Workflow integration⏎Leadership safety, staff burden requirements⏎Quality/Safety Medium Outcomes, adverse Monitoring standards,⏎Officers events reporting requirements⏎Primary administrative hurdles include:⏎● Lengthy security and privacy review processes (6-18 months typical)⏎● Lack of standardized evaluation criteria leading to duplicative reviews⏎● Integration complexity with existing systems⏎● Unclear governance structures for AI oversight⏎● Procurement processes not designed for AI (SaaS, continuous updates)⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities and⏎accelerate AI development?⏎⏎Enhanced interoperability would have the greatest impact in areas that advance the Quintuple⏎Aim:⏎Priority Data Types for AI:⏎Data Type Current State AI Opportunity Quintuple Aim Impact⏎Clinical notes Limited NLP for Provider work life,⏎standardization documentation, population health⏎clinical insights⏎Medical imaging DICOM Diagnostic AI, Population health, cost⏎standard but treatment⏎metadata planning⏎inconsistent⏎Patient-generated Fragmented, Remote Population health, patient⏎data non-standard monitoring, experience⏎chronic disease⏎management⏎Social determinants Rarely Risk stratification, Healthcare Delivery⏎structured care coordination Optimization, population⏎health⏎Prior authorizations Manual, Automation, Cost, provider work life⏎fax-based real-time⏎decisions⏎Quality measures Retrospective, Real-time quality Population health, cost⏎burdensome monitoring⏎Data Standards Priorities:⏎● FHIR-based APIs for consistent data access (strongly support proposed rule)⏎● Standardized data quality indicators (enables AI to assess input reliability)⏎● Croissant-compatible metadata for ML datasets (enables reproducibility)⏎● Common representation of temporal relationships (critical for predictive AI)⏎● Standardized demographic data elements (enables performance optimization⏎assessment)⏎Benchmarking Tools Needed:⏎● Reference implementations for AI evaluation on FHIR data⏎● Synthetic data generation tools that preserve statistical properties⏎● Federated evaluation infrastructure compatible with certified EHR APIs⏎● Performance optimization frameworks with standardized demographic categories⏎The proposed FHIR-forward approach in the deregulatory rule directly addresses many of these⏎needs and should be finalized.⏎⏎Question 9: What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI?⏎Based on input from patient representatives in our working group:⏎Desired AI Applications (mapped to Quintuple Aim):⏎Patient/Caregiver Need AI Solution Quintuple Aim Dimension⏎Faster diagnosis Triage AI, Patient Experience⏎diagnostic support⏎Care coordination Information Patient Experience, Cost⏎synthesis across⏎providers⏎Understanding medical Plain language Patient Experience, Healthcare⏎information explanations, Delivery Optimization⏎health literacy⏎tools⏎Medication safety Interaction Population Health, Patient⏎checking, Experience⏎adherence⏎support⏎Caregiver support Care planning Patient Experience, Provider Work⏎tools, resource Life⏎navigation⏎Access in rural and Telehealth AI, Healthcare Delivery Optimization⏎resource-limited areas remote⏎diagnostics⏎Reduced out-of-pocket costs Administrative Cost⏎automation, prior⏎auth⏎Patient Concerns:⏎Concern Mitigation Strategy HHS Role⏎Privacy/security Federated approaches, strong encryption Clarify HIPAA⏎applicability⏎Transparency Disclosure when AI involved in care Require transparency⏎standards⏎Performance Stratified performance monitoring Require performance⏎variations optimization⏎assessment⏎⏎Human oversight Maintain physician role Guidance on⏎appropriate⏎autonomy levels⏎Broad access Ensure AI benefits reach all populations Coverage⏎requirements, rural⏎health focus⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize?⏎We recommend prioritizing research that advances the Quintuple Aim:⏎Research Area Quintuple Aim Impact Current Gap Recommended⏎Investment⏎Evaluation All dimensions Limited Federated evaluation⏎methodology real-world infrastructure⏎assessment⏎methods⏎Performance Healthcare Delivery Systematic error Broad benchmark⏎consistency Optimization detection and datasets, optimization⏎correction metrics⏎immature⏎Human-AI Provider work life, Limited Clinical workflow studies⏎interaction safety understanding⏎of optimal⏎collaboration⏎Continuous All dimensions Safety of Methods for safe model⏎learning adaptive updates⏎systems unclear⏎Multimodal AI Population health, Integration of Interoperability standards⏎effectiveness imaging, notes, for AI⏎labs, genomics⏎Federated Healthcare Delivery Privacy-preservi Infrastructure and⏎learning Optimization, privacy ng training at methods research⏎scale⏎Administrative Cost, provider work life Automation of Workflow automation⏎AI prior auth, studies⏎documentation⏎10a. Published findings on adopted AI tools:⏎⏎The literature on deployed clinical AI is growing but remains limited. Key sources include: -⏎FDA’s database of authorized AI/ML-enabled medical devices (1,000+ devices)[9] - Published⏎post-market studies (relatively rare; systematic selection toward positive results) - Health system⏎implementation reports (often not peer-reviewed) - Industry-sponsored studies (potential⏎conflicts of interest)⏎We recommend HHS support systematic evidence generation for deployed AI tools through: -⏎Real-world evidence initiatives with standardized outcome measures - Registry requirements for⏎AI-enabled devices - Federated post-market surveillance networks⏎10b. Literature on costs, benefits, and transfers:⏎The literature on AI economics in healthcare is nascent.[10] Most studies focus on potential⏎benefits (improved accuracy, time savings) rather than measured outcomes. Key gaps include:⏎● Limited prospective studies of AI impact on healthcare costs⏎● Insufficient data on implementation costs and time-to-value⏎● Lack of standardized economic evaluation frameworks for clinical AI⏎● Limited assessment of distributional effects (who benefits, who bears costs)⏎● Inadequate study of impact on healthcare workforce⏎We recommend HHS support health economics research specifically focused on clinical AI⏎tools, including: - Total cost of ownership studies (implementation, maintenance, training) -⏎Productivity impact assessments - Distributional analyses (effects across payer types, patient⏎populations) - Workforce impact studies⏎⏎Part II: Response to Proposed Rule on Health Data,⏎Technology, and Interoperability⏎We strongly support the proposed deregulatory actions and the FHIR-forward vision articulated⏎in this proposed rule. The transition to FHIR-based APIs creates essential infrastructure for AI to⏎advance the Quintuple Aim.⏎Support for FHIR-Based Approach⏎The proposed consolidation around FHIR-based APIs directly enables AI deployment and⏎evaluation:⏎Proposed Change AI Impact Quintuple Aim Benefit⏎FHIR API standardization Consistent data Cost, Provider Work Life⏎access reduces⏎integration cost⏎Removal of redundant Reduced Cost⏎criteria compliance burden⏎enables innovation⏎focus⏎USCDI v3.1 adoption Standardized data Population Health, Healthcare⏎elements improve Delivery Optimization⏎AI⏎training/evaluation⏎Information blocking AI systems can All dimensions⏎revisions access data like⏎human users⏎Modern AI Integration⏎The rule’s reference to emerging standards like Model Context Protocol (MCP) demonstrates⏎awareness of how AI systems interact with data sources. FHIR provides the data layer upon⏎which such protocols can operate. This forward-looking approach positions the United States as⏎a leader in AI-enabled healthcare interoperability.⏎Comments on Specific Provisions⏎Certification Criteria Removal (Section III.A)⏎We support the proposed removal of redundant certification criteria that are no longer necessary⏎given FHIR adoption. The proposal to remove 34 of 60 criteria represents significant burden⏎reduction.⏎However, we note the following consideration:⏎⏎Clinical Decision Support (Section III.A.1.b): The proposed revisions maintain core CDS⏎functionality while removing burdensome requirements. We support this approach. For⏎AI-enabled CDS specifically, we recommend guidance on:⏎o Performance monitoring requirements⏎o Update/versioning procedures⏎o User notification of AI involvement⏎Real World Testing Revisions (Section III.D.3)⏎We strongly support the proposed changes to Real World Testing (RWT) requirements. The⏎current RWT framework, while well-intentioned, has proven burdensome without proportionate⏎benefit.⏎The proposed changes align with our experience in federated evaluation:⏎Current Requirement Proposed Change Our Assessment⏎Annual RWT plans Descoped Support: plans often⏎formulaic, limited value⏎Detailed results reporting Descoped Support: focus on⏎meaningful metrics⏎instead⏎SVAP documentation Streamlined Support: accelerates⏎standards adoption⏎However, we recommend that HHS consider how RWT principles could be adapted for⏎AI-specific evaluation:⏎● Federated RWT: Allow developers to satisfy RWT through participation in federated⏎evaluation networks (like MedPerf) that assess performance across varied real-world⏎settings⏎● Continuous monitoring: Replace annual RWT cycles with continuous performance⏎monitoring for AI systems⏎● Performance optimization assessment: Require RWT to include performance⏎stratification across demographic groups⏎Standards Version Advancement Process (Section III.D.3)⏎We support streamlining SVAP requirements. Faster standards adoption is particularly important⏎for AI, where capabilities and best practices evolve rapidly.⏎We recommend: - Expedited SVAP pathways for AI-relevant standards (e.g., FHIR IGs for AI⏎use cases) - Recognition of MLCommons standards (Croissant, AILuminate) as SVAP-eligible -⏎Coordination with FDA on standards for AI-enabled medical devices⏎Insights Condition Revisions (Section III.D.5)⏎⏎We support the proposed focus on “use of FHIR in apps through certified health IT” as the⏎primary Insights measure. This metric directly tracks interoperability adoption.⏎For AI specifically, we recommend HHS consider future Insights measures such as: - Number of⏎AI-enabled applications accessing data through certified APIs - Volume of API calls for AI/ML⏎purposes - Performance metrics for AI systems integrated with certified health IT⏎USCDI v3.1 Adoption (Section III.B)⏎We support the adoption of USCDI v3.1 and the iterative process for expanding standardized⏎data elements. For AI applications, we note:⏎Data Element Category AI Relevance Recommendation⏎Clinical notes Essential for NLP-based AI Prioritize in future USCDI⏎versions⏎Imaging metadata Critical for radiology AI Standardize DICOM-FHIR⏎mapping⏎Social determinants Enables optimization-focused AI Expand SDOH elements⏎Patient-reported outcomes Supports outcome prediction Add PRO instruments⏎Data quality indicators Enables AI to assess input Add provenance/quality⏎reliability flags⏎We recommend that future USCDI development include explicit consideration of AI use cases⏎and engage the AI development community in prioritization.⏎Information Blocking Revisions (Section IV)⏎We strongly support the proposed revisions to information blocking definitions and exceptions.⏎These changes directly enable AI deployment while addressing documented misuse.⏎1. Clarification that “access” and “use” include automated means (Section IV.A): This⏎clarification is essential for AI systems that require programmatic access to EHI.⏎Autonomous AI agents and automated data pipelines should be able to access data⏎under the same conditions as human users.⏎Concrete scenario: A hospital deploys an AI system for sepsis early warning.⏎The system needs continuous access to vital signs, lab results, and clinical notes.⏎Under current ambiguity, EHR vendors have argued that automated AI access is not⏎covered by information blocking rules. The proposed clarification eliminates this⏎barrier.⏎2. Revision of Infeasibility Exception conditions (Section IV.B): Removing conditions⏎susceptible to misuse will improve data access for legitimate AI applications.⏎Concrete scenario: An AI developer requests API access to evaluate their⏎algorithm across multiple health systems. Under current rules, health systems have⏎cited the “third party seeking modification use” condition to deny access, claiming⏎⏎any external evaluation constitutes “modification.” The proposed removal of this⏎condition prevents such misuse.⏎3. Manner Exception clarifications (Section IV.C): Ensuring that data access cannot be⏎impeded by unreasonable contractual terms supports a competitive market for AI tools.⏎Concrete scenario: An EHR vendor offers API access but requires AI developers⏎to sign contracts with excessive indemnification, non-compete clauses, or⏎above-market pricing. The proposed clarification that contracts must be “market rate”⏎and cannot be “unconscionable” addresses this barrier.⏎4. TEFCA Manner Exception removal (Section IV.D): We support this proposal. The⏎information blocking regulations should apply uniformly regardless of the specific⏎exchange mechanism used. This ensures AI systems can access data through any⏎compliant pathway.⏎Considerations for AI-Enabled Health IT⏎As HHS resets the Certification Program foundation, we recommend consideration of future⏎requirements specific to AI-enabled health IT. These should be voluntary guidance rather than⏎burdensome certification requirements:⏎AI Consideration Recommended Approach MLCommons Resource⏎Performance monitoring Continuous monitoring with drift MedPerf monitoring⏎detection framework⏎Version control Documentation of model GaNDLF versioning⏎versions and changes⏎Transparency Model cards and performance AILuminate documentation⏎disclosure standards⏎Performance optimization Stratified performance reporting Croissant demographic⏎assessment metadata⏎Safety evaluation Testing against edge cases and AILuminate safety⏎failure modes benchmarks⏎We recommend HHS develop voluntary guidance that recognizes industry-developed standards⏎(such as those from MLCommons) as acceptable approaches, rather than creating new⏎prescriptive requirements.⏎⏎Alignment Between Initiatives: A Unified Vision for the⏎Quintuple Aim⏎These two HHS initiatives are deeply interconnected. Together, they create the foundation for AI⏎to advance all five dimensions of the Quintuple Aim:⏎AI Adoption Barrier Deregulatory Solution Quintuple Aim Impact⏎Integration complexity FHIR API standardization Cost, Provider Work Life⏎Data access limitations Information blocking revisions All dimensions⏎Evaluation infrastructure Federated access through Healthcare Delivery⏎standardized APIs Optimization, Population⏎Health⏎Innovation barriers Reduced certification burden Cost⏎Performance optimization Standardized demographics in Healthcare Delivery⏎gaps USCDI Optimization⏎Trust deficit Transparency through Patient Experience⏎recognized standards⏎American Leadership⏎The coordinated approach represented by these initiatives positions the United States as the⏎global leader in AI-enabled healthcare. By establishing: - Open, standardized APIs (FHIR) -⏎Clear rules against information blocking - Reduced regulatory burden on innovation - Support⏎for federated evaluation (preserving privacy while enabling assessment)⏎The United States can create the model that other nations follow, cementing American⏎competitiveness in the growing global market for healthcare AI.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎The MLCommons Medical AI Working Group supports HHS’s efforts to accelerate AI adoption in⏎clinical care while modernizing health IT infrastructure. The FHIR-forward approach creates an⏎essential technical foundation for AI deployment, and the focus on reducing unnecessary⏎regulatory burden while preserving safety mechanisms reflects appropriate balance.⏎AI has the potential to advance all five dimensions of the Quintuple Aim: - Population⏎Health: Early detection, risk stratification, preventive interventions - Patient Experience:⏎Reduced wait times, better coordination, personalized care - Per Capita Cost: Administrative⏎automation, reduced waste, optimized resource use - Provider Work Life: Documentation⏎assistance, decision support, reduced burden - Healthcare Delivery Optimization:⏎Performance monitoring across demographics, expanded access⏎Realizing this potential requires: - Continued coordination across HHS agencies (FDA, CMS,⏎ONC) - Support for open, neutral evaluation infrastructure - Engagement with a broad range of⏎stakeholders including developers, providers, and patients - Recognition of industry-developed⏎standards and best practices - Explicit attention to performance optimization in all AI evaluation⏎and deployment⏎We offer our expertise and infrastructure to support HHS in achieving these goals. Our MedPerf⏎platform, GaNDLF framework, Croissant metadata standard, and AILuminate safety⏎benchmarks demonstrate that open, neutral approaches can accelerate AI adoption while⏎maintaining rigorous evaluation standards and optimizing performance across populations.⏎The alternative to coordinated action is fragmented approaches that slow innovation, create⏎compliance burden, and ultimately delay patient access to beneficial AI tools. With thoughtful⏎implementation of the approaches outlined in these initiatives, HHS can establish American⏎leadership in clinical AI while ensuring safety, effectiveness, and optimized performance for all⏎patients.⏎Acknowledgments⏎This response reflects the collective expertise and real-world experience of MLCommons⏎Medical AI Working Group members, including:⏎● Academic medical centers and community hospitals⏎● Clinical practitioners across multiple specialties, and other healthcare professionals⏎● Data scientists and AI researchers⏎● Patient advocates⏎We thank all contributors for their insights and commitment to advancing safe, effective, and⏎optimized AI in healthcare. For questions or further discussion, please contact:⏎medical-chairs@mlcommons.org⏎⏎Appendix: Mapping MLCommons Infrastructure to HHS⏎Initiatives⏎The following table maps specific HHS requirements and goals to MLCommons technical⏎solutions:⏎HHS Requirement/Goal MLCommons Solution Operational Mechanism⏎Real-world AI evaluation MedPerf Federated evaluation across⏎decentralized sites; algorithms⏎travel to data, preserving⏎privacy⏎Standardized ML workflows GaNDLF Zero/low-code framework⏎ensures reproducible AI⏎development pipelines⏎Dataset interoperability Croissant Metadata format enables⏎ML-ready datasets with⏎standardized documentation⏎AI safety assessment AILuminate Benchmark suite evaluates AI⏎risk and reliability across⏎standardized scenarios⏎Performance optimization MedPerf + Croissant Federated evaluation with⏎assessment demographic metadata⏎enables stratified performance⏎analysis⏎Post-market monitoring MedPerf monitoring Continuous performance⏎tracking with drift detection⏎across deployment sites⏎Transparency / Model cards + AILuminate Standardized documentation⏎documentation of AI system characteristics⏎and limitations⏎FHIR integration MedPerf FHIR adapters Reference implementations⏎for AI evaluation on⏎FHIR-accessible data⏎⏎Example: How MedPerf Addresses Information Blocking Scenarios⏎Scenario Current Barrier How MedPerf Helps⏎Multi-site AI Data cannot leave institutions Algorithms travel to data; only⏎validation aggregate metrics returned⏎Performance Demographic data too sensitive Local stratified analysis; only⏎optimization to share summary statistics aggregated⏎assessment⏎Continuous No infrastructure for distributed Federated monitoring dashboard⏎monitoring surveillance with site-level and aggregate views⏎Competitive Institutions reluctant to share Neutral third-party orchestration;⏎evaluation performance data blinded comparisons⏎Alignment with Quintuple Aim:⏎MLCommons Population Patient Cost Provider Healthcare⏎Tool Health Experience Work Life Delivery⏎Optimization⏎MedPerf Enables Ensures AI Reduces Streamlines Enables⏎validation works for all duplicate evaluation stratified⏎across patients validation assessment⏎populations⏎GaNDLF Reproducible Consistent AI Reduces Standardized Reproducible⏎development behavior development workflows optimization⏎cost analysis⏎Croissant Dataset Transparency Reduces Clear data Demographic⏎documentatio data prep specs metadata⏎n cost⏎AILuminate Safety Trust in AI Reduces Confidence Systematic⏎assessment safety in tools error⏎incidents detection⏎⏎References⏎[1] Machine learning is one of the key techniques through which AI systems are built.⏎[2] Peter Mattson, et al, “MLPerf: An Industry Standard Benchmark Suite for Machine Learning⏎Performance,” IEEE Xplore, 2020. https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/9001257⏎[3] Karargyris, A., Umeton, R., Sheller, M.J. et al. Federated benchmarking of medical artificial⏎intelligence with MedPerf. Nat Mach Intell 5, 799-810 (2023).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s42256-023-00652-2⏎[4] Pati, S., Thakur, S.P., Hamamci, I.E. et al. GaNDLF: the generally nuanced deep learning⏎framework for scalable end-to-end clinical workflows. Commun Eng 2, 23 (2023).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s44172-023-00066-3⏎[5] Akhtar, M., et al. “Croissant: A metadata format for ML-ready datasets.” Advances in Neural⏎Information Processing Systems 37 (2024): 82133-82148.⏎[6] “The AILuminate Benchmark,” MLCommons, accessed January 2026,⏎https://mlcommons.org/ailuminate/⏎[7] LaBella, D., Kassem, H., Edwards, B., Sheller, M., Pati, S., Aristizabal, A., Huang, R., Baid,⏎U., Karargyris, A., Bakas, S., et al. “FeTS 2.0: Federated learning sets benchmark in post-op⏎GBM segmentation.” Neuro-Oncology, Volume 27, Supplement 5, November 2025, Page v289.⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/neuonc/noaf201.1148⏎[8] Maier-Hein, L., Reinke, A., Godau, P. et al. Metrics reloaded: recommendations for image⏎analysis validation. Nat Methods 21, 195-212 (2024).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41592-023-02151-z⏎[9] FDA, “Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (AI/ML)-Enabled Medical Devices,”⏎updated 2025,⏎https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/artificial-intelligence-and-m⏎achine-learning-aiml-enabled-medical-devices⏎[10] Sahni, N., Stein, G., Zemmel, R., & Cutler, D. M. (2023). The potential impact of artificial⏎intelligence on healthcare spending. The Economics of Artificial Intelligence: Health Care⏎Challenges. University of Chicago Press.⏎[11] Vasey, B., et al. (2022). Guidelines and quality criteria for artificial intelligence-based⏎prediction models in healthcare: a scoping review. npj Digital Medicine, 5, Article 2.⏎⏎List of Acronyms⏎● ACO: Accountable Care Organization⏎● AI: Artificial Intelligence⏎● API: Application Programming Interface⏎● ASTP: Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎● CDS: Clinical Decision Support⏎● CFO: Chief Financial Officer⏎● CFR: Code of Federal Regulations⏎● CIO: Chief Information Officer⏎● CMAO: Chief Medical Analytics Officer⏎● CMIO: Chief Medical Information Officer⏎● CMS: Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎● CRADA: Cooperative Research and Development Agreement⏎● CTO: Chief Technology Officer⏎● CUSUM: Cumulative Sum (control chart)⏎● DICOM: Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine⏎● DSC: Dice Similarity Coefficient⏎● EHI: Electronic Health Information⏎● EHR: Electronic Health Record⏎● EWMA: Exponentially Weighted Moving Average⏎● FDA: Food and Drug Administration⏎● FHIR: Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources⏎● GBM: Glioblastoma Multiforme⏎● HHS: Department of Health and Human Services⏎● HIPAA: Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act⏎● IEC: International Electrotechnical Commission⏎● IEEE: Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers⏎● IG: Implementation Guide⏎● IT: Information Technology⏎● KS: Kolmogorov-Smirnov (test)⏎● MCP: Model Context Protocol⏎● ML: Machine Learning⏎● MMD: Maximum Mean Discrepancy⏎● MSSP: Medicare Shared Savings Program⏎● NIH: National Institutes of Health⏎● NLP: Natural Language Processing⏎● ONC: Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎● PRO: Patient-Reported Outcomes⏎● RFI: Request for Information⏎● RIN: Regulation Identifier Number⏎● ROI: Return on Investment⏎● RWT: Real World Testing⏎● SaaS: Software as a Service⏎● SDOH: Social Determinants of Health⏎● SVAP: Standards Version Advancement Process⏎● TEFCA: Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement⏎● USCDI: United States Core Data for Interoperability♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0063♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ethical, humane and highly- regulated and evidence-based psychotherapeutic models and services must be at the forefront of any AI tools for therapeutic services. As the population's needs for these services increase, so should any regulations and ethical standards of the technologies. ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0064♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Ramirez♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔Stryker Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔Stryker Corporation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎My frustration with this RFI is not what people might expect from someone in my position. I have spent the better part of two decades at Stryker navigating FDA clearance pathways, payer coverage determinations, and the patchwork of state-level requirements that govern how our devices and software products reach patients. I am not someone who reflexively argues for slower regulation. What I am is someone who has watched, repeatedly, what happens when deployment outpaces governance — and what concerns me about this particular RFI is that HHS seems to be treating acceleration as the goal rather than as a potential output of getting the foundational architecture right first.⏎⏎The RFI asks HHS to pull regulatory, reimbursement, and research levers simultaneously. That framing assumes those levers can be safely deployed before anyone has resolved the governance questions that would tell you in which direction to pull them. Liability classification for non-device AI is the clearest example I can give. Our own internal analysis found that ambiguity in that space delayed commercial deployment for our non-SaMD AI product line by an average of 8.3 months per product. That is real cost, real delay. But the answer to that problem is not to embed those products in federal reimbursement structures while the liability questions are still unresolved — the answer is to resolve the liability questions. Once a product category is attached to a CMS billing code, the political economy around that code becomes extremely difficult to change. I have seen this with other device categories. The reimbursement structure calcifies, the vendors build business models around it, and course correction becomes operationally and politically very expensive even when the underlying clinical evidence turns out to be weaker than expected.⏎⏎Stryker Corporation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎The RFI does not ask directly about liability frameworks, which is itself telling. It asks about reimbursement pathways, regulatory modernization, and R&D investment — all of the deployment-facing questions — without first asking what accountability structure should govern the AI once it is embedded in clinical workflows. That sequencing concern is not abstract. In our 2024 Annual Health AI Policy Forum in September, stakeholders from across the industry — payers, developers, health systems — ranked regulatory harmonization and liability clarification as the top two policy priorities. Not faster reimbursement. Harmonization and clarification. The industry is telling HHS what it actually needs, and this RFI is asking a different question.⏎⏎I want to be careful here because I recognize the other side of this. There are genuinely beneficial AI applications sitting in development queues right now that could improve patient outcomes, and regulatory delay has real costs too. I am not arguing for a moratorium. What I am arguing is that HHS should be honest about the sequencing problem. Establishing unified audit standards, resolving the SaMD/non-SaMD classification boundary for AI, and clarifying malpractice exposure for AI-assisted clinical decisions — those are prerequisites, not parallel workstreams. The HealthScoreAI survey from Q2 2024 found that 54% of AI developers were spending an average of $1.2 million annually on duplicative compliance activities precisely because that unified standard does not exist. Accelerating adoption into that environment does not reduce that burden. It compounds it.⏎⏎Regarding the specific RFI question about reimbursement pathway modernization: CMS should not establish new AI-specific billing codes or coverage categories until the Office of the Inspector General and ONC have jointly published guidance on audit standards for AI-generated clinical recommendations. The sequencing matters. Creating a reimbursement pathway before the audit standard exists means the audit standard, when it eventually arrives, will have to accommodate an already-embedded commercial infrastructure rather than being designed on its own merits. That is exactly the ratchet effect I am describing. Once the billing codes exist and vendors have⏎⏎Stryker Corporation | HHS Health Sector AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 3⏎⏎⏎built revenue projections around them, the political pressure on any subsequent audit or accountability rulemaking becomes enormous. I have watched this dynamic play out in the IVD space and I expect it to be worse with AI because the technology changes faster than any coverage determination process is designed to handle.⏎⏎The question of how much AI should be present in clinical decision-making is not a question HHS can answer unilaterally or should try to answer under acceleration pressure. It involves tradeoffs that patients, clinicians, and the public have not been given a meaningful opportunity to deliberate. I say this as someone who genuinely wants to see well-governed AI reach patients — the absence of that deliberation is a long-term risk to the public legitimacy of health AI that will ultimately affect every company in this space, including ours.⏎⏎Rebecca Ramirez⏎Vice President, Regulatory Affairs⏎Stryker Corporation⏎Kalamazoo, MI 49002⏎⏎Contact for follow-up questions: regulatory.affairs@stryker.com [submission copy — verify direct contact routing before filing]♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0065♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Esposito, Dominick, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dominick♔Esposito♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Practically, buy-in from providers to use new AI tools, their confidence in their efficacy,⏎and their skepticism that the tools are just going to be “another expense.” As a patient⏎and parent, I have seen some AI tools been positively received by providers. For⏎example, my sons’ endocrinologists use AI scribes regularly to support notetaking and⏎follow up (both my boys have Type 1 diabetes). So, these types of tools have helped⏎our medical professional community become comfortable with AI, but future innovations⏎have to take small steps forward, I believe, for most providers to embrace them.⏎Providers believe in rigorous evidence; they are trained on the randomized clinical trial⏎being the gold standard for care. And, then in practice, they collect data through every⏎observation they make with their own patients. For AI to flourish in support of clinical⏎care, providers will need to feel confident that the “algorithms” behind the AI are based⏎on appropriate data. At first, they will want to vet that the “answers” or “support” they are⏎receiving is accurate and in line with their own experience before beginning to trust it.⏎Gaining providers’ trust will be one avenue to getting their buy-in that the cost of any⏎new tools are worth it and not just another expense.⏎Policymakers may be able to facilitate the use of AI tools by supporting independent⏎assessments of tools in a sort of clearinghouse. Or, maybe less costly, by identifying the⏎preferred characteristics and operational parameters required for high quality tools used⏎in clinical care. Some of this information can come from providers themselves who⏎would be on the front lines working with tools.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further⏎support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (⏎e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Assessments of AI for clinical care could be rooted in an implementation science⏎framework. The question asked specifically, and appropriately, about workflow which is⏎an underlying and assumed benefit of the injection of AI into clinical care. That is, the⏎use of AI by clinicians should, hopefully, ease their workflow and allow for more⏎meaningful interaction with patients. The promise of the availability of data from the use⏎of AI agents in clinical care offers a unique opportunity to assess the implementation of⏎this technology. With much less burden than in other research before it, we could⏎answer questions like: How do providers use the tools available? Is the information from⏎the tools accurate? Do providers continue to use the same tool for similar patients or⏎does their use drop off over time? Is the practice of care for specific conditions different⏎with the tools and in what ways?⏎1⏎⏎The deployment of new tools into clinical practice could allow for very robust testing in⏎short amounts of time. It would be important, however, that testing occur in a multitude⏎of different and varied locations to understand how different providers in different⏎settings might use the new technologies. The CMS Innovation Center, for example,⏎could consider a model that supports the use of new technologies in a tiered approach.⏎First, by soliciting organizations that sell such AI tools to participate and put their tools⏎“to the test” and second to solicit providers and provider groups to participate. It may⏎have to offer further incentives to providers in some settings (e.g., rural communities).⏎CMS could also request large health systems that already use in-house AI tools to also⏎participate with their own technologies. There is some precedence for this type of⏎approach of “putting your approach to the test” with the test of disease management⏎programs by CMS from 2003 to 2008. There are ways to evaluate these tools without⏎the need for randomized controlled trials or “usual care” comparison groups as well. Any⏎assessment of tolls might also consider patient-facing tools that support providers and⏎patients as well.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎The development of standards for industry-testing could go a long way to support⏎providers’ buy-in and confidence that AI tools are efficacious. It may be resource-⏎intensive, however, to go as far as accreditation and certification and only companies⏎that have available resources (or are able to charge users a premium) may be to afford⏎these steps.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider⏎specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎For this question and the next two, I will lean heavily on my experience as a father of⏎children with Type 1 Diabetes and a son with a father and mother-in-law with the same⏎disease. For many individuals living with T1D, there is a lot of data available that is not⏎always easy accessible to them or their providers and certainly not integrated with other⏎information. For example, many individuals with T1D (though nearly not enough) use⏎continuous glucose monitors (CGMs) to track their blood sugar continuously but not⏎many providers or patients themselves have access to the data (which is collected in 5-⏎minute intervals). Freeing these data up for use by patients who may, say, use AI⏎algorithms to support their care and optimize their insulin dosages could be a major step⏎forward for people living with T1D. The highs and lows that come with less than⏎accurate insulin dosing are a frequent cause of stress and concern for these patients⏎and their caregivers and, too often, result in concomitant mental health conditions as⏎2⏎⏎well. Those patients who additionally use an automated insulin delivery device (casually⏎referred to as an insulin pump) could also then bring together information on insulin⏎dosage with their blood sugar levels to support stronger control. There are already non-⏎FDA approved, community-built apps for this purpose (for example, Trio) that are⏎moving in this direction but the case of T1D could be a promising pilot opportunity for⏎the federal government given the prevalence of T1D in the United States and its annual⏎incidence rate. (My children or other family members do not use this app but we have⏎met families that do.)⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎As a caregiver, a significant concern that I have is reliability of the potential AI⏎technology. Taking the case of T1D, for example, it is a disease that can be very⏎challenging and frustrating to manage. Until we have cell therapy cures, there is no⏎“silver bullet” solution. Speaking as a father of two teenagers with T1D, each one of my⏎boys is completely different in his reaction to insulin and the same carbohydrate-laden⏎foods. This is typical of the community of individuals with T1D. So, as a parent, I face⏎this dichotomy. On the one hand, I desperately would love for someone to harness the⏎power of AI/agentic AI/etc. to develop a tool to support my children so that some stress⏎is relieved from them and my wife daily on making decisions. It is established that⏎people with T1D make 180 to 300 additional health-related decisions a day.1 We would⏎love a tool to relieve the stress of making these decisions. On the other hand, I would⏎be cautious about any tool that purported to be a solution without knowing that it was⏎tested and vetted in a standardized and rigorous fashion. So, my concern? Can we⏎have an efficient process to vet tools that is timely, is patient-centered, and cost-efficient⏎so that we can provide families confidence that the tools being rolled out into any clinical⏎care setting are safe and reliable?⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Please, consider prioritizing the Type 1 diabetes space for exploration of AI research.⏎While my comments above have focused on individuals with T1D, I would also implore⏎that just as important an area are those individuals who have evidence of the antibodies⏎for T1D but are not yet insulin dependent. We have the technology and ability today to⏎screen for all the antibodies that science has proven result in a degradation of one’s⏎beta cells. One of my sons was diagnosed as such and we monitored him for multiple⏎1 The stress of making diabetes decisions⏎3⏎⏎years before he became insulin dependent. Unfortunately, not everyone who could⏎develop T1D is tested. And, more unfortunately, “crashing into” T1D is a very traumatic⏎event for people with this disease and their loved ones.⏎In addition to supporting individuals who are currently insulin dependent, AI research⏎could also support our knowledge of whom might be most likely to have antibodies,⏎could support individuals who have these antibodies, and support providers and⏎caregivers. The amount of information for individuals who are about to become or are⏎insulin-dependent has proliferated and making sense of it for each individual case can⏎be overwhelming. For both patients and providers (many of whom do not know a lot⏎about T1D), education modules tied to AI could support better learning and the stress of⏎this disease.⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0066♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Quiet Signal Technologies LLX, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Quiet Signal Technologies LLX♔♔♔♔♔♔In response to Questions 3 and 8 regarding governance, accountability, and interoperability, we recommend consideration of the following principle to support responsible and trusted AI adoption in clinical care:⏎⏎AI systems that significantly influence clinical documentation or clinical decision-making should incorporate appropriate identity and authorization controls at the point of use, enable clear user attribution and purpose-of-use awareness, and support interoperable records consistent with existing federal standards.⏎⏎Applying existing identity, authorization, and interoperability principles to AI-enabled clinical workflows can reduce uncertainty, strengthen accountability, and promote patient trust while maintaining alignment with established federal health IT frameworks.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0067♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment on AI RFI: Ensuring Patients Directly Benefit from AI in Clinical Care⏎⏎As HHS promotes the adoption of AI in clinical care, it must ensure that these technologies expand competition and patient benefit — not further concentrate control within existing provider systems.⏎⏎Healthcare markets often lack meaningful competition due to control of all the information. Large provider entities control access to diagnostic data, medical records, imaging data, and interpretive tools. This can result in program vulnerabilities, such as provider manipulation of the data in a way that does not benefit patients or even for nefarious purposes. If AI systems are deployed primarily within these vertically integrated environments, innovation may improve internal efficiency without improving patient agency, choice, or outcomes.⏎⏎AI policy should explicitly promote:⏎•	Patient access to complete and portable health data⏎•	The ability to use independent, clinically validated AI tools⏎•	Transparent disclosure of AI systems influencing care decisions⏎•	Competitive markets for interpretation and decision support⏎⏎Without deliberate safeguards or appropriate payment models, AI risks reinforcing gatekeeping structures in which providers control when and how patients access analytic insight or data and overall AI benefits. That dynamic limits competition and can slow quality improvement.⏎⏎True acceleration of AI in clinical care should widen the market — allowing independent analytic services, technology companies, and alternative care pathways to compete on accuracy, precision, timeliness, and outcomes. Patients should not receive innovation only through provider-controlled channels and on institutional terms.⏎⏎HHS should ensure that AI policy increases competition for high-quality care, strengthens data portability, and expands patient choice. Innovation should not centralize control — it should redistribute it in ways that promote the best outcomes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0068♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Massive Bio, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Massive Bio♔♔♔♔♔♔Massive Bio – Request for Information (RFI): RFI on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎(Submitted via https://www.regulations.gov/commenton/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001 on January 8, 2026)⏎Submitted on behalf of: Massive Bio, Inc.⏎One Park Place. 621 NW 53rd St, Suite 125, Boca Raton, Florida 33487, USA⏎Point of Contact:⏎Name: Dr. Arturo Loaiza-Bonilla, MD, MSEd, FACP (Co-Founder & Chief Medical AI Officer)⏎Email: arturolb@massivebio.com⏎Public Comment on the 2026 Request for Information (RFI): RFI on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care (RIN⏎0955-AA13)⏎Subject: Response to Request for Information (RFI): RFI on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎To the Office of The Director, National Cancer Institute:⏎Introduction⏎On behalf of Massive Bio, Inc, a leader in leveraging artificial intelligence to connect cancer patients to optimal treatments and⏎clinical trials, thank you for the opportunity to provide input on the development of the 2026 RIN 0955-AA13: RFI on Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care. As Co-Founder and Chief Medical AI Officer, I witness daily the transformative⏎potential of AI to revolutionize healthcare, particularly in the complex field of oncology. We strongly support the Administration’s⏎commitment – as outlined in Executive Order 14179 – to sustain and enhance America’s AI leadership in order to promote human⏎flourishing, economic competitiveness, and national security (Removing Barriers to American Leadership in Artificial Intelligence –⏎The White House).⏎Our response is grounded in successful real-world implementation, demonstrating how AI can democratize access to advanced⏎care and clinical trials. We fully endorse the Administration’s vision for American AI leadership and propose a "Genesis⏎Project" for health—a coordinated national effort to operationalize AI transformation in clinical care.⏎The Core Challenge⏎The primary barriers to AI adoption are no longer technological, but systemic: fragmented data silos, misaligned reimbursement⏎incentives, regulatory uncertainty regarding liability, and the need for trust4. To unlock the potential of AI, HHS must align its⏎"regulatory, reimbursement, and research & development levers" simultaneously.⏎Our Strategic Position⏎Massive Bio stands ready as a key implementation partner. Through our Reticulum Nexus platform and partnerships with the⏎American Cancer Society (ACS), we have proven that AI can be deployed responsibly at a national scale to break down silos⏎between academic centers and community care. We urge HHS to move from strategy to execution, launching demonstration⏎projects that propel the U.S. healthcare system forward.⏎Key Recommendations for HHS⏎Accelerating AI in Clinical Care: A "Three-Lever" Approach⏎To transition AI from "bench to bedside," Massive Bio recommends HHS prioritize the following high-impact changes:⏎1. Modernize Regulation & Governance (The Safety Lever)⏎• Update FDA Frameworks: Finalize the "Total Product Lifecycle" approach for Software as a Medical Device (SaMD),⏎allowing AI algorithms to learn and improve over time without requiring new submissions for every update.⏎• Clarify Liability via Safe Harbors: Establish clear guidance that clinicians remain the final authority, but provide legal⏎"safe harbors" for providers who follow recommendations from HHS-certified or validated AI tools.⏎• Voluntary AI Certification: Launch an ONC-administered voluntary certification program for clinical AI tools to validate⏎safety, bias mitigation, and interoperability, giving providers the confidence to adopt them.⏎2. Align Reimbursement & Incentives (The Motivation Lever)⏎• Create CPT Codes for AI Services: Introduce billing codes for "AI-assisted decision support" and care coordination to⏎reimburse the time and complexity of using AI tools that improve outcomes.⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 1⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎• Reward Outcomes in Value-Based Care: Explicitly incorporate AI usage into Alternative Payment Models (APMs) and⏎MIPS. For example, award bonus points to ACOs that use AI to identify high-risk patients or match cancer patients to⏎clinical trials13.⏎• Modernize Prior Authorization: Encourage a "gold card" system where AI-driven requests that meet evidence-based⏎criteria receive automatic, real-time approval, reducing administrative burden14.⏎3. Strengthen Infrastructure & Research (The Enabler Lever)⏎• Enforce Interoperability Standards: Double down on FHIR and mCODE adoption to break down data silos. Mandate⏎"bulk data" access capabilities to fuel AI research and ensure diverse, representative training datasets.⏎• Prioritize Implementation Science: Shift research funding toward "implementation science"—studying how to integrate⏎AI into clinical workflows effectively—rather than just algorithm development16.⏎• Establish a National AI Testing Sandbox: Create a public-private "validation lab" where vendors can test algorithms on⏎federated, de-identified datasets to benchmark performance and fairness without compromising patient privacy⏎Conclusion⏎By pulling these levers, HHS can build an ecosystem of trust and utility. Massive Bio is prepared to collaborate with⏎HHS, offering our technology and operational expertise to turn this vision into a reality for American patients.⏎Introduction⏎Massive Bio appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for⏎Information, “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care” (RIN 0955-AA13). As an AI-driven⏎oncology company, Massive Bio is uniquely positioned to offer bold, visionary insights aligned with President Trump’s agenda for⏎American AI leadership and HHS’s transformative goals[1][2]. Our perspective is grounded in real-world implementation: Massive⏎Bio has pioneered patient-consented, patient-centric oncology data aggregation and AI analytics at national scale. We have⏎demonstrated how artificial intelligence can propel the U.S. health care system forward by improving patient outcomes, reducing⏎costs, and expanding access to cutting-edge care[3][2]. We stand ready as a key implementation partner to HHS, particularly in⏎cancer care, to operationalize the Administration’s bold vision of AI-powered healthcare delivery.⏎About Massive Bio: Founded in 2015, Massive Bio has become a leader in AI-enabled cancer care, serving over 160,000 patients to⏎date across all 50 states and globally[4][5]. We collaborate with the American Cancer Society (ACS) and other stakeholders to⏎break down silos between academic centers and community care, bringing AI-driven solutions directly to patients and⏎providers wherever they are. For example, Massive Bio’s technology powers ACS ACTS – a nationwide program helping⏎patients of any background find personalized clinical trial options – providing AI-matched trial lists and support resources to⏎patients in every state[6][7]. Our Reticulum Nexus platform, unveiled at the ESMO 2025 Congress, is an AI-driven, multi-⏎agent system that orchestrates the end-to-end oncology journey from initial patient outreach and consent to data analytics,⏎trial matching, and last-mile enrollment logistics[8][9]. Reticulum Nexus connects previously fragmented data sources⏎through native FHIR/mCODE interoperability, linking electronic health records (EHRs), labs, sponsors, community clinics and⏎registries on one fabric[10]. What once took hundreds of hours of manual effort now runs as an auditable, human-in-the-⏎loop AI workflow, resulting in “speed with empathy: more patients reached, faster answers, and auditable decisions”[9]. We⏎have proven that AI can be deployed responsibly at national scale outside of academic silos, as evidenced by our partnership⏎with ACS and recognition by the White House Cancer Moonshot for our AI-powered trial pre-screening hubs[11][12]. These⏎efforts have dramatically shortened trial enrollment timelines and boosted participation rates by proactively identifying⏎eligible patients through genomic and clinical data[12]. Importantly, Massive Bio’s approach keeps patients at the center:⏎data is used only with patient consent, de-identified for research insights, and integrated with “privacy-by-design”⏎governance to maintain trust[10][13]. Our mission is to democratize access to advanced treatments – using AI to open⏎clinical trials to diverse populations across geography, race, age, and socioeconomic status – while accelerating value-based⏎care through precision targeting of therapies to those who will benefit most[14][15]. We leverage real-world data (RWD)⏎from thousands of patients to generate evidence and optimize trial design, all in alignment with HHS’s “OneHHS” strategy to⏎extend AI’s benefits across the health system[16][17].⏎In this comment, we respond to each of the ten questions posed in the RFI. For each, we provide concrete answers grounded in⏎Massive Bio’s experience and supported by real examples. We also offer forward-looking recommendations on how HHS can⏎use its regulatory, reimbursement, and research & development levers[3][17] to unlock AI’s impact in oncology and beyond.⏎Central themes of our responses include: modernizing regulation to foster innovation while protecting patients; aligning⏎reimbursement and value-based payment models to reward AI-driven improvements; investing in interoperability and data⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 2⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎standards to fuel AI development; and ensuring that evidence generation and implementation science keep pace with⏎technology. We frame our suggestions in the spirit of HHS’s Genesis Project – analogous to a health-focused “moonshot” – to⏎bring operational AI transformation to clinical reality on a national scale. Massive Bio stands ready to partner with HHS to⏎deliver on this ambitious agenda, translating policy into on-the-ground results for American patients.⏎Question 1. Biggest Barriers to Private-Sector AI Innovation and Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Q1: “What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care?”⏎Key Barriers: Despite rapid advances in AI, private-sector innovators face significant hurdles bringing AI tools from⏎bench to bedside. Major barriers include:⏎• Fragmented Data and Interoperability Challenges: Quality healthcare AI depends on large volumes of diverse data, but⏎health data today is siloed across EHR systems, labs, imaging archives, and devices. Data fragmentation – and the lack of⏎seamless interoperability – is a top barrier to AI innovation. Many AI developers struggle to obtain diverse,⏎representative datasets due to information blocking or inconsistent standards. For example, oncology data lives in⏎disparate systems (EMRs, genomic testing labs, clinical trial databases) that historically do not communicate. This⏎fragmentation hinders training robust AI models and limits an AI tool’s ability to ingest real-time patient data during⏎clinical use. Massive Bio has confronted this challenge directly: our AI platform integrates data from community clinics,⏎academic centers, and patient-reported sources using common standards (FHIR, mCODE)[10]. However, not all⏎developers have the resources or permissions to aggregate such data. HHS’s focus on “data liquidity” and patient data⏎trust is well placed – improving interoperability is essential to overcome this foundational barrier[18].⏎• Regulatory Uncertainty and Risk Aversion: Innovators face ambiguity about how existing health regulations apply to AI,⏎especially software not clearly classified as medical devices. Uncertainty around FDA oversight, HIPAA, and liability for⏎AI-driven decisions creates a chilling effect. Startups fear investing in a tool that might later be deemed a regulated⏎device requiring lengthy approval, while providers fear using AI without clear legal guidelines. For instance, if an AI⏎clinical decision support system suggests a treatment and an error occurs, it’s unclear who is liable – the clinician, the⏎hospital, or the software maker?[19] This uncertainty leads to risk-averse institutional cultures, where hospital⏎compliance teams often err on the side of caution and delay adoption of new AI tools. Clear guidance is needed to⏎assure innovators and users that they can deploy AI safely within existing legal frameworks (more on this in Q3). Until⏎then, many promising AI solutions remain stuck in pilot phases. HHS’s RFI aptly notes that digital health regulatory⏎frameworks must evolve to account for AI-driven tools while maintaining safety[17] – the private sector needs this⏎evolution to proceed confidently with innovation.⏎• Lack of Reimbursement and Misaligned Incentives: The current payment landscape does not reward – and can even⏎penalize – the use of AI in clinical practice. Providers have little financial incentive to adopt AI tools that, for example,⏎prevent hospitalizations or automate tasks, because fee-for-service reimbursement is tied to volume of services. An AI⏎that improves efficiency or avoids an expensive therapy might reduce revenue under traditional models. Meanwhile,⏎there are few billing codes or payment models that directly reimburse AI-driven care improvements (e.g. no codes for⏎“AI-assisted diagnosis” or “AI care coordination”). The absence of reimbursement pathways is repeatedly cited by⏎providers as a barrier to investing in AI[20]. This is particularly relevant in oncology: many AI tools could help identify the⏎most cost-effective therapy or an appropriate clinical trial (saving costs of ineffective treatments), but providers face⏎upfront costs to integrate these tools without immediate reimbursement. Additionally, prior authorization processes⏎can unintentionally discourage innovation – if an AI recommends a novel therapy, insurers may not approve it without⏎extensive documentation, delaying care. Massive Bio’s approach of offering clinical trial options before prior⏎authorization barriers arise is one way to sidestep this, ensuring patients can access alternative cutting-edge treatments⏎without waiting for insurance approvals. However, broader payment reform is needed so that AI that reduces costs and⏎improves outcomes is financially rewarded, not discouraged. We discuss specific reimbursement levers in Q2.⏎• Clinical Integration and Workflow Challenges: Integrating AI into clinical workflows remains difficult. Busy healthcare⏎providers are often overwhelmed with technology (EHR alerts, documentation burdens), and a new AI tool can feel like⏎added complexity unless it is seamlessly embedded. If AI outputs are not presented at the right time and in an intuitive⏎way, clinicians may ignore them. Training requirements can also be a barrier – clinicians need to trust and understand⏎the AI, which takes education. In smaller practices or rural hospitals, there may be limited IT support to implement⏎advanced AI systems[21]. This creates an adoption gap: large health systems with resources charge ahead, while smaller⏎providers lag behind, exacerbating disparities[21]. Massive Bio has observed this gap in oncology; community oncologists⏎often lack the decision support tools available at major cancer centers. Our solution has been to provide AI-driven trial⏎matching as a turn-key service accessible via a simple web interface or phone call, which lowers the barrier for small⏎practices to participate. Nonetheless, widespread clinical adoption will require HHS to support easier integration⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 3⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎(through standards and technical assistance) and to cultivate an AI-ready workforce. Change management and training⏎are as important as the technology itself – clinicians need to see AI as a helpful colleague, not a hindrance.⏎• Trust, Transparency, and Bias Concerns: Finally, a pervasive barrier is the concern among both providers and patients⏎about the trustworthiness of AI. Healthcare is fundamentally human; if AI is seen as a “black box” making life-and-death⏎suggestions, clinicians may be reluctant to rely on it. Past high-profile failures have made some skeptical – for example,⏎early systems like IBM’s Watson for Oncology did not live up to the hype, undermining confidence in AI’s capabilities.⏎Additionally, well-publicized studies have shown that some healthcare algorithms had biases that could perpetuate⏎disparities, for instance under-representing Black patients in care management programs due to biased training⏎data[22][23]. These issues create caution in adoption: providers worry about safety and bias, and patients worry about⏎fairness and privacy. For the private sector, this means extra effort is needed to demonstrate algorithm validation,⏎fairness, and explainability. Massive Bio has invested heavily in transparency – our trial matching AI provides clinician-⏎readable rationales for why a patient is eligible for a trial, and our “Radiant Core” control plane ensures every AI⏎decision is auditable for quality and compliance[24][25]. We have also focused on diversity in our data (partnering with⏎organizations like the American Cancer Society and community clinics) so that our AI recommendations are equitable⏎and inclusive[15][26]. Building trust through such measures is crucial. HHS can assist by establishing or endorsing⏎standards for AI transparency and bias mitigation, giving providers and the public greater confidence to embrace AI⏎tools.⏎In summary, the biggest barriers to private-sector AI innovation and adoption in health care are not technological – they are⏎systemic. Data silos, unclear regulations, misaligned payments, workflow integration challenges, and trust issues all conspire to⏎impede progress. These barriers are interrelated and require a coordinated strategy across HHS agencies (ONC, CMS, FDA, OCR,⏎etc.) to address. We are encouraged that HHS recognizes many of these challenges in this RFI. By systematically lowering these⏎barriers – as outlined in the following responses – HHS can unleash a wave of private-sector innovation, allowing AI to fulfill its⏎promise of improving clinical care.⏎Question 2. Regulatory, Payment Policy, and Programmatic Changes to Incentivize AI in Clinical Care⏎Q2: “What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of⏎AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.”⏎Massive Bio believes HHS can accelerate AI adoption by acting decisively on three fronts – regulation, reimbursement, and⏎program design – the very levers highlighted in the RFI[3][17]. We recommend the following priority changes:⏎A. Evolve Regulatory Frameworks to Foster Innovation with Safety:⏎1. Modernize FDA’s Digital Health Oversight: HHS should work with the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to⏎update guidance on AI/ML-based software in clinical care. Current FDA regulations (21 CFR Part 820 and related⏎guidances) were not designed with continuously learning algorithms in mind. We urge HHS to finalize and implement⏎the FDA’s proposed framework for AI/ML SaMD (Software as a Medical Device) that allows for a “total product⏎lifecycle” approach. This would let developers make certain algorithm updates under pre-agreed protocols (e.g. a⏎“Predetermined Change Control Plan”) without requiring new submissions each time. Such an approach maintains⏎safety and effectiveness while allowing AI tools to improve over time. Additionally, clarification is needed on what⏎constitutes a regulated “medical device” versus clinical decision support exempt from regulation under 21st Century⏎Cures Act Section 3060. HHS should issue guidance (or support FDA in doing so) that clearly delineates the boundary⏎– for example, predictive analytics that drive therapy decisions might be regulated, whereas administrative AI or⏎transparent decision support might not. Clear rules will reduce uncertainty and spur innovation, as companies will⏎know whether to pursue FDA clearance or not. We also suggest HHS consider regulatory sandboxes or pilot⏎programs in partnership with FDA where novel AI solutions (especially those addressing high-need areas like cancer)⏎can be tested in a controlled setting with temporary regulatory flexibility. This can generate evidence to inform⏎permanent regulatory changes.⏎1. Address Liability and Safe Harbors: To encourage providers to adopt AI, HHS should explore creating safe harbors or⏎guidance around liability when using approved AI tools. Fear of malpractice exposure is a deterrent to clinical AI use. For⏎example, if a physician follows an AI recommendation that is consistent with clinical guidelines or an HHS-endorsed tool⏎and an adverse event occurs, that physician should not be held to a higher liability standard than if they had followed⏎standard practice. HHS, possibly via the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ) or in collaboration with⏎professional societies, could develop “best practice” guidelines for AI-assisted care. Providers adhering to those could be⏎granted certain protections (similar in concept to safe harbors for following evidence-based guidelines). Clarifying⏎liability can reduce the “novel legal issues” noted in the RFI[19]. Likewise, HHS could encourage states to update medical⏎malpractice laws or insurer policies to recognize the role of AI. Additionally, we recommend HHS consider working with⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 4⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎the Office of Inspector General (OIG) to ensure that Stark Law and Anti-Kickback regulations do not inadvertently⏎impede AI tool adoption. For instance, hospitals might want to provide an AI decision support tool to community⏎physicians to improve care coordination, but worry this could be seen as remuneration. Providing exceptions or guidance⏎for sharing AI tools that improve quality (akin to the EHR donation safe harbor) would help spread AI to smaller⏎practices.⏎2. Strengthen Data Privacy & Sharing Policies for AI: Privacy regulations should be updated to facilitate responsible AI⏎development while protecting patients. HHS’s Office for Civil Rights (OCR) could clarify how HIPAA permits the use of de-⏎identified data for algorithm training and explicitly allow patients to authorize use of their data for AI R&D. Many⏎patients are willing to contribute data to improve care as long as it’s secure and anonymous – policies should empower⏎that. We recommend HHS develop standard patient consent frameworks for AI (potentially building on the model of⏎research biobanks) so that private companies can create opt-in patient data repositories for AI training under HHS⏎oversight. Furthermore, HHS should promote the adoption of Privacy Enhancing Technologies (like federated learning⏎and differential privacy) by issuing guidance or even amending the HIPAA Privacy Rule to recognize these techniques as⏎safeguards. For example, allowing health systems to participate in federated model training (where data doesn’t leave⏎their servers) without it being considered a HIPAA disclosure could accelerate cross-organization data collaborations. In⏎summary, privacy rules should not be static barriers but rather enablers of “safe, secure, and allowed” data use for⏎AI[18]. By updating regulations to explicitly accommodate modern AI data practices, HHS can both protect individuals⏎and advance innovation.⏎B. Align Reimbursement and Payment Policy with AI-Driven Care:⏎1. Create Reimbursement Pathways for AI Services: CMS should establish clear reimbursement mechanisms for clinical uses of AI⏎that add value. We recommend developing new CPT codes or modifiers for AI-assisted procedures and decision support. For⏎example, if an oncologist uses an AI platform to identify a therapy or clinical trial for a patient, there could be a billable code for⏎the time and complexity involved in that AI-informed care coordination. Similarly, radiologists who utilize AI to read scans or⏎pathologists using AI for slide analysis should have that reflected in billing (e.g., an add-on code indicating “AI augmentation⏎used”). These codes could initially be introduced as Category III (tracking) codes to collect data, then progress to payment once⏎value is demonstrated. Additionally, CMS’s technology add-on payments (NTAP) for new innovations might be extended to AI: if a⏎hospital adopts an AI system that demonstrably reduces complications or readmissions, an add-on payment could offset the cost.⏎The underlying principle is to reward outcomes and efficiency – if AI reduces total cost of care or improves quality, providers⏎should share in the savings. In oncology, for instance, if an AI tool helps avoid ineffective chemotherapy by matching a patient to a⏎targeted therapy trial at no cost, the payer saves money and the patient potentially has better outcomes. CMS could capture this⏎by allowing some form of shared savings or payment for the care coordination that led to trial enrollment. Without such⏎reimbursement, providers may hesitate to take the extra steps to use AI for trial matching, even though it benefits the system⏎overall. We applaud HHS for explicitly asking how reimbursement structures can be “simplified and better aligned to⏎support…efficient, deflationary technologies”[17]. One specific policy to revisit is Medicare’s reimbursement for telehealth and⏎remote monitoring: as AI becomes embedded in these services (e.g., AI chatbots for patient follow-up, AI-enhanced remote⏎patient monitoring for symptoms), CMS should ensure coverage and payment parity. The recent expansion of telehealth codes⏎could be expanded further to cover “AI-assisted remote care” so long as there is oversight by a clinician. Likewise, CMS might⏎consider demo programs where it reimburses AI-driven preventive care interventions that reduce hospitalizations (for example,⏎paying a monthly fee for an AI predictive service that helps manage high-risk patients, similar to chronic care management fees).⏎1. Incorporate AI into Value-Based Care Models and Quality Programs: HHS should leverage alternative payment models⏎(APMs) to drive AI adoption. Accountable Care Organizations (ACOs), bundled payment programs, and specialty models⏎like the Oncology Care Model (OCM) are natural vehicles for AI since they reward outcomes and cost reduction. We⏎recommend that CMS explicitly encourage or even require the use of advanced analytics/AI in these models. For⏎example, in a future Oncology Value-Based model, CMS could include quality measures or bonus points for utilizing⏎evidence-based AI decision support that improves care (like matching stage IV cancer patients to trials or genomic-⏎guided therapies). CMS Innovation Center (CMMI) could pilot an “AI Accelerator” within ACOs – perhaps giving⏎participating ACOs technical assistance grants to implement AI tools for care coordination, and then measuring the⏎impact on cost and quality. If successful, that could be scaled. Furthermore, HHS can align Medicare Advantage and⏎Medicaid policies to support AI: for instance, allowing plans to count investments in AI tools as quality improvement⏎activities in their Medical Loss Ratio (MLR) calculations, thereby incentivizing insurers to invest in provider-facing AI. On⏎the quality measurement front, HHS could work with organizations like NCQA and The Joint Commission to incorporate⏎AI-enabled care processes into accreditation and scoring. A concrete near-term step: include use of clinical AI in Merit-⏎Based Incentive Payment System (MIPS) Improvement Activities. Clinicians could earn credit for implementing an AI tool⏎that, say, identifies care gaps or reduces medication errors. Aligning quality incentives will nudge providers toward AI as⏎a means to achieve targets.⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 5⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎2. Reduce Administrative Barriers and Prior Auth Burdens via AI: We suggest HHS revise policies that inadvertently⏎discourage AI by imposing unnecessary administrative hurdles. A prime example is prior authorization. HHS can⏎modernize prior auth regulations to recognize AI-based determinations. For instance, if an AI algorithm (certified for⏎accuracy) finds that a patient meets evidence-based criteria for a certain test or referral, payers could be required to⏎automatically approve it or use a “gold card” approach for providers using such tools. This would flip prior auth into a⏎more automated, AI-driven process that reduces delays. CMS could pilot this in Medicare Advantage: encourage MA⏎plans to integrate AI for initial screening of auth requests, granting immediate approval when criteria are met (perhaps⏎tying this to the recently proposed rules on electronic prior auth to ensure timely decisions). By doing so, patients get⏎needed care faster and the burden on providers is lessened[27]. From Massive Bio’s perspective, offering patients⏎clinical trial alternatives is one way to avoid the prior auth maze altogether – if HHS were to formally support policies⏎that “clinical trial enrollment can be considered a prior auth exception” (for instance, pausing prior auth on standard⏎treatment when a patient opts for an eligible clinical trial), it would be a strong incentive for providers to utilize AI⏎matching services proactively. Essentially, reward pathways that solve the prior auth challenge either by automation or⏎by providing alternative options (like trials) that render prior auth moot.⏎C. Programmatic and Policy Design Changes:⏎1. Expand Public-Private Partnerships and Challenge Programs: HHS should use its convening power to create partnerships that⏎accelerate AI implementation. One idea is an AI Clinical Care Challenge series – prize competitions (as authorized by the America⏎COMPETES Act) around specific needs (e.g., “AI for Cancer Detection Challenge” or “AI for Reducing Hospital Readmissions”).⏎These could be run by HHS’s IDEA Lab or ONC, with winners receiving funding or pilot opportunities at HHS hospitals (like the VA or⏎IHS)[28]. Such competitions signal HHS’s priorities and incentivize private innovators to focus on high-impact areas. Additionally,⏎HHS could establish Centers of Excellence for AI in Clinical Practice via grant funding (perhaps through AHRQ or HRSA for⏎community health centers). These centers (academic or community consortiums) would develop, test, and disseminate best⏎practices for AI adoption – effectively demonstration sites that others can learn from. For example, Massive Bio is a founding⏎member of the federally sponsored CancerX public-private partnership[29][30]; continued support and funding for such⏎consortiums will drive multi-stakeholder solutions to common barriers. We recommend HHS also collaborate with the Department⏎of Energy and NSF on the Genesis Mission initiative – while DOE’s Genesis Mission focuses on AI for scientific research[31][32], a⏎parallel HHS “Genesis Project” could focus on AI for health challenges, ensuring healthcare is a key domain in national AI strategy.⏎By aligning with broader national AI missions, HHS can tap into additional resources and talent for healthcare AI.⏎1. Revise ONC Certification and CMS Conditions of Participation: ONC’s EHR certification program has been effective in⏎standardizing certain functionalities. HHS could consider an ONC-administered AI Certification program – voluntary at⏎first – where AI clinical tools are assessed for basic safety, interoperability, and bias mitigation. If a tool meets the⏎criteria, it gets an HHS “stamp of approval,” giving providers confidence to adopt it. This kind of certification could later⏎tie to CMS conditions (e.g., hospitals using AI for clinical decisions must use certified tools to qualify for Medicare⏎reimbursement, ensuring quality). Similarly, CMS could update hospital Conditions of Participation to require an AI⏎governance plan for hospitals deploying AI (ensuring they address bias, monitor outcomes, etc.). While adding⏎requirements may seem like a burden, done thoughtfully it can actually encourage hospitals to embrace AI with proper⏎oversight rather than avoid it.⏎2. Revisit CFR provisions hindering data sharing: HHS might identify specific regulations in 42 CFR (Medicare/Medicaid) or⏎45 CFR (Public Welfare, including HIPAA rules) that unnecessarily restrict innovative uses of AI. For example, 42 CFR Part⏎2 (confidentiality of substance use records) is one area that, while important, complicates integrating behavioral health⏎data into AI models; aligning Part 2 with HIPAA as recently proposed will help comprehensive AI care. Another example:⏎45 CFR 164 (HIPAA Privacy Rule) – defining de-identification standards that allow for the sharing of key data like genomic⏎information (which currently can be tricky under the “identifier” removal rules). Clarifying that codified genomic variants⏎or certain derived data can be shared if individuals cannot be readily identified would help AI in precision medicine. We⏎encourage HHS to take a holistic look at its regulations to find and fix such friction points.⏎In summary, HHS should act boldly to update regulations, realign incentives, and design programs that collectively turbocharge⏎AI adoption in care. By prioritizing the changes above, HHS will send a strong signal that America is committed to operationalizing⏎AI for health. These steps will incentivize companies like Massive Bio to continue innovating in areas like oncology, knowing that a⏎supportive policy environment is in place. Importantly, none of these changes sacrifice patient safety or privacy – rather, they⏎modernize protections and payments to fit AI, ensuring that the effective use of AI is rewarded and scaled. Massive Bio is⏎especially enthusiastic about these changes because they would directly augment our ability to develop and deploy AI for clinical⏎trials and precision oncology. For example, clearer FDA guidance would streamline enhancement of our trial-matching algorithms;⏎new reimbursement for AI-driven care coordination would allow more clinics to adopt our platform; and partnership programs⏎would enable us to demonstrate our tech in more public settings. We believe these policy moves are not only justified – they are⏎urgently needed to keep pace with technology and to realize AI’s potential to “propel the American health care system⏎forward.”[3]⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 6⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎Question 3. Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical-Device AI – and HHS’s Role⏎Q3: “For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and implementation issues that⏎challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?”⏎AI tools that are not regulated as medical devices (e.g., clinical support software, administrative AI systems, etc.)⏎indeed present new challenges for our health care legal and governance frameworks. Massive Bio engages⏎with several such AI applications (for example, our AI-driven patient triage and scheduling tools are not FDA-⏎regulated devices but are used in care workflows), so we have first-hand insight into these issues. Key novel⏎issues and recommended HHS roles include:⏎• Liability and Accountability for AI-Influenced Decisions: When an AI system influences clinical decisions, it blurs⏎traditional lines of accountability. If an algorithm (say, an AI risk score or triage recommendation) contributes to a clinical⏎decision that leads to harm, there is ambiguity over who is legally responsible – the clinician who accepted the AI’s⏎advice? The hospital that deployed the tool? The software developer? This lack of clarity is a “novel” issue because our⏎malpractice system assumes human decision-makers. HHS should convene stakeholders (providers, developers, insurers,⏎patient advocates) to develop consensus guidelines on AI liability. While legislation may ultimately be needed, in the⏎near term HHS can issue guidance that clarifies expectations. For instance, HHS could endorse the approach that⏎clinicians remain the final authority and thus primarily liable, but that following validated AI recommendations could be⏎considered in determining the standard of care. Simultaneously, HHS could work with professional liability insurers to⏎encourage coverage models that don’t penalize providers for using AI that improves care. By framing when it is⏎appropriate to rely on AI (e.g., if tool meets certain validation criteria), HHS can give providers confidence to use AI⏎without fear of unjust legal exposure. Additionally, HHS might explore model legislation for states that addresses AI⏎liability – for example, laws that provide a rebuttable presumption that if a clinician followed an HHS-certified AI⏎recommendation, they met the standard of care. This would address liability concerns in a balanced way.⏎• Indemnification and Vendor Responsibility: Hospitals and health systems often seek indemnification from AI vendors⏎for any harm caused by their software. However, vendors are wary of open-ended liability, especially since the end-users⏎ultimately make clinical decisions. This dynamic can slow adoption – negotiations over indemnity can be lengthy or some⏎vendors refuse to enter the healthcare market. HHS could help by developing model contract language or best practices⏎for AI procurement in healthcare, perhaps through ONC or AHRQ. By standardizing how responsibility is shared (e.g.,⏎vendor liable for software faults, provider liable for misuse), HHS can ease the burden on individual organizations to sort⏎this out. Another approach is HHS fostering the creation of an insurance pool or fund for AI-related incidents –⏎analogous to the National Vaccine Injury Compensation Program, which removed direct manufacturer liability as a⏎barrier to vaccine availability. A similar concept could be considered for certain AI: an industry-wide fund that⏎compensates patients in rare cases of AI error causing harm, thereby limiting the need for each vendor to carry massive⏎liability insurance. HHS’s role would be in studying feasibility and potentially advocating for enabling legislation if⏎needed.⏎• Privacy and Data Use Ethics: Unlike traditional medical devices, many AI systems feed on vast datasets – including⏎patient information – raising concerns about privacy beyond HIPAA’s original scope. Two novel concerns are: (1) AI⏎models trained on patient data might inadvertently “remember” sensitive details (creating risk of re-identification or⏎unintended disclosure), and (2) companies may seek to use patient data for secondary AI development in ways patients⏎don’t expect. To address these, HHS should update privacy regulations and guidance as discussed in Q2. Specifically,⏎HHS/OCR should clarify how de-identification rules apply to complex data like genomic or free-text data used in AI. It⏎may be necessary to develop new guidelines or safe harbors for AI model training, ensuring that if certain best practices⏎are followed (like data aggregation, removal of direct identifiers, bias testing to protect minority groups), the use is⏎permissible. HHS might also support development of technical standards for AI model security – for instance,⏎encouraging the use of encryption, access controls, and audit trails for AI systems that handle patient data. The idea of AI⏎systems themselves needing to be HIPAA-compliant is novel. Perhaps HHS could assert that significant AI models should⏎undergo a Privacy Impact Assessment or be certified for how they handle data. In essence, HHS should extend its existing⏎privacy and security frameworks to explicitly cover the AI lifecycle (data collection → model training → deployment →⏎data outputs). This might include guidance on issues like using cloud-based AI services (which introduces third-party⏎concerns) or leveraging patient data to create synthetic data for AI (potentially a privacy-preserving practice HHS could⏎endorse).⏎• Bias, Fairness, and Health Equity Concerns: Not specifically legal in the traditional sense, but a governance issue: AI tools⏎can inadvertently propagate bias against protected classes (race, gender, etc.), leading to disparities in care. While anti-⏎discrimination laws (like Civil Rights Act Title VI for entities receiving federal funds) could be interpreted to cover biased⏎AI, this is uncharted territory. HHS should take an active role in setting expectations that AI in clinical settings must be⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 7⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎fair and unbiased. We suggest HHS, through the Office of Civil Rights and Office of Minority Health, issue guidance or⏎even regulations requiring that any AI used in clinical care undergo bias testing and mitigation. This could involve⏎routine audits to see if outcomes differ by race or other factors. If significant bias is found and not corrected, that could⏎be considered non-compliance with, say, healthcare civil rights obligations. HHS might incorporate this into Medicare⏎Conditions of Participation or certification criteria. The role of HHS here is to ensure accountability: just as hospitals must⏎have infection control programs, in the future they should have algorithmic bias control programs. This is a new⏎frontier, but one where HHS leadership is crucial to prevent AI from widening disparities. Massive Bio, for instance,⏎proactively designs its trial matching AI to expand options for underserved populations[15]. We welcome HHS’s help in⏎codifying such practices industry-wide.⏎• Transparency and Explainability Requirements: A novel implementation issue is how to govern “black box” algorithms.⏎Traditional medical practice relies on explainable logic (doctors can be asked why they chose a treatment). With AI, if⏎neither the user nor the patient can understand the basis for a recommendation, it challenges informed consent and⏎accountability. HHS should consider policies that push for algorithmic transparency appropriate to context. For non-⏎device AI, which isn’t going through FDA premarket review, this could mean requiring that patients be informed when AI⏎is involved in their care and, upon request, given a basic explanation of how it works (similar to how patients have a right⏎to explanations of their care plan). Moreover, HHS can promote standards for explainability – e.g., if an AI recommends a⏎cancer trial, it should provide the oncologist with the key reasons (patient has X mutation, Y clinical criteria matched) in⏎human-readable form. This is something Massive Bio has implemented (transparent rationales)[33], and it greatly aids⏎clinician acceptance. HHS might not need a new law for this; even a guidance or adding a requirement in Medicare⏎Advantage plan oversight (since MA plans are increasingly using AI for utilization management) that beneficiaries are⏎informed of AI use would set a precedent.⏎In all the above areas, HHS’s role is to provide guidance, standards, and a forum for resolution of these novel issues. The⏎Department can use its influence to shape soft law (guidelines, best practices) that preempt the need for constantly reacting in⏎courts after problems occur. We specifically recommend HHS establish a Healthcare AI Governance Council – bringing together⏎FDA, ONC, CMS, OCR, OIG, and external experts – to continuously monitor these emerging issues and update policies. This council⏎could issue annual reports or recommendations to keep regulations nimble. The council could tackle questions like: Should there⏎be an “AI Incident Reporting System” (analogous to FDA’s adverse event reporting, but for AI errors or near-misses)? How do we⏎harmonize state laws (e.g., some states may start passing their own rules on AI liability or data use – HHS could offer a model to⏎encourage consistency)?⏎Massive Bio’s perspective is that sensible guardrails will ultimately increase adoption of AI, by ensuring trust and accountability.⏎HHS should neither micromanage nor take a hands-off approach, but rather serve as an enabler and coordinator on these⏎governance challenges. By addressing liability, privacy, bias, and transparency in a proactive way, HHS will help remove these⏎as points of friction or fear. For example, once providers know HHS’s stance on AI-related liability and see a clear framework,⏎they will be more willing to integrate AI (as one physician told us, “I just want to know I won’t be hung out to dry if I use the⏎AI recommendation in good faith”). Similarly, by ensuring AI adheres to privacy and equity standards, HHS will build public⏎trust, which is foundational for widespread AI use. In summary, HHS should play an active role by updating governance⏎structures to accommodate AI, thereby protecting patients and providers while unleashing the technology’s benefits.⏎Question 4. Promising Evaluation Methods for Non-Device AI – and How HHS Can Support Them⏎Q4: “For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics,⏎robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support⏎these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?”⏎Evaluating AI tools that are not subject to FDA pre-market review is a critical challenge. Without a formal⏎regulatory approval process, we need other methods to ensure these AI systems are safe, effective, and fit⏎seamlessly into clinical workflows. Fortunately, the industry and research community have been developing⏎evaluation approaches. Massive Bio has practical experience in this area – we continuously evaluate our AI⏎matching algorithms both before deployment (in silico and pilot testing) and after deployment (real-world⏎performance monitoring) to ensure they function as intended for patients and clinicians. Below, we outline⏎promising evaluation methods and suggest how HHS can bolster them:⏎Promising Pre-Deployment Evaluation Methods:⏎• Retrospective Validation with Gold-Standard Datasets: Before an AI is used in live clinical care, one robust method is to⏎test it on retrospective data where outcomes or “ground truth” decisions are known. For example, an AI that⏎recommends cancer clinical trials can be validated on past patient cases: feed in historical patient data and see if the AI⏎would have identified the trials that those patients actually enrolled in (or that experts would have chosen). Similarly,⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 8⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎diagnostic AIs can be tested against existing datasets of images or cases with known diagnoses. These validations⏎measure accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, etc. A promising trend is the creation of open clinical datasets and challenges⏎– for instance, the NIH has released large imaging datasets for AI algorithm benchmarking. Community competitions⏎(such as MICCAI challenges in imaging) have driven algorithm improvements. HHS can encourage this by supporting the⏎curation and sharing of de-identified clinical datasets as evaluation benchmarks. Metrics like area-under-curve (AUC) for⏎accuracy, or positive predictive value, are common, but evaluation should go beyond just accuracy: robustness across⏎subgroups (e.g., how does performance vary by age, ethnicity, hospital site?) is key. Pre-deployment robustness testing⏎might involve intentionally stress-testing the AI with edge cases or noisy data to see if it still performs. For example, at⏎Massive Bio, we test our trial matching AI on data with missing fields or atypical patient profiles to ensure the system can⏎handle real-world messiness.⏎• Simulation and Clinical Vignettes: Another pre-deployment approach is simulation studies. In a controlled setting,⏎clinicians can be presented with cases (vignettes) and the AI’s recommendation, and researchers measure how the⏎clinicians respond, whether the AI’s input improved decision quality or speed. Think of it as a “clinical Turing test” – does⏎the clinician aided by AI do better than without? This human-centered evaluation assesses usability and usefulness, not⏎just technical accuracy. For instance, we have conducted user-acceptance testing with oncologists for our AI interface:⏎we simulate a patient consult with and without the AI suggesting trials, and gather feedback. Key metrics from such⏎studies include time saved, reduction in cognitive workload (maybe via NASA-TLX surveys), and user trust scores.⏎These metrics capture workflow integration and human factors, which are as important as pure accuracy. If an AI is⏎accurate but too cumbersome or confusing, clinicians won’t use it. Measuring Net Promoter Score or satisfaction of⏎clinicians in pilot usage can predict whether the AI will be adopted at scale.⏎• Prospective Pilot Studies (Pre-deployment in limited setting): Especially for high-impact AI, conducting a prospective⏎pilot in a limited real-world setting before wider rollout is valuable. This is akin to a phase 4 trial but done proactively.⏎For example, test an AI in one unit or one hospital for a period, with oversight, gather outcome data and iterate. While⏎technically this is “deployment,” it’s part of evaluation before scaling up. Promising methods include A/B testing or⏎stepped-wedge trial designs where some clinicians or sites use the AI and others serve as control, then outcomes (clinical⏎outcomes or process measures) are compared.⏎Promising Post-Deployment Evaluation and Monitoring:⏎• Continuous Performance Monitoring and Feedback Loops: Once an AI is live, it should not be a static “fire-and-forget”⏎tool. A strong method is implementing ongoing monitoring of the AI’s outputs versus actual outcomes. For instance, if⏎our AI predicts a patient is eligible for a trial, we track whether that patient actually enrolls and benefits; if not, why not?⏎This helps catch any drift in the AI’s effectiveness. In hospital settings, AI systems often have dashboards tracking their⏎performance (e.g., how many sepsis alerts were true positives vs false). Monitoring metrics could include real-world⏎accuracy, error rates, override rates (how often clinicians disagree with or ignore the AI), and outcome impact (did⏎using the AI correlate with better patient outcomes or lower costs?). For example, Cleveland Clinic monitored its AI⏎sepsis alerts and found a ten-fold reduction in false positives and a 46% increase in sepsis cases identified after⏎implementing a particular AI, which validated the tool’s performance in practice[34]. We also look at disparities post-⏎deployment: are there any biases emerging (e.g., is the AI systematically less effective for certain subpopulations)?⏎Continual auditing for fairness is a key part of post-deployment evaluation.⏎• Robustness Testing in Production: Real-world can throw curveballs that weren’t in the training data. A good practice is⏎periodic robustness testing of AI in production by injecting test cases. For example, an institution might occasionally run⏎a set of test patient profiles through their live clinical AI (without affecting actual care decisions) to see if outputs remain⏎consistent and robust. Another aspect is monitoring for data drift: if the patient population or medical practice changes⏎over time, the AI’s model might need recalibration. Automated drift detection algorithms can alert if the input data⏎distribution shifts significantly or if outcome metrics deteriorate. This triggers model retraining or revision. At Massive⏎Bio, we retrain our matching algorithms regularly as new trials and guidelines emerge; we also use Radiant Core, our AI⏎control plane, to supervise agents and flag anomalies in the workflow in real time[13]. This kind of built-in monitoring⏎(agents overseeing agents) is an emerging best practice in AI operations (AI Ops).⏎• User Feedback and Governance Committees: Human-centered post-deployment evaluation includes formalizing user⏎feedback channels. Many hospitals have set up AI or Clinical Decision Support oversight committees that review AI⏎performance and clinician feedback, somewhat like a Pharmacy & Therapeutics committee for algorithms. They track if⏎clinicians are reporting any issues, confusion, or near-misses involving the AI. Metrics like “number of incident reports⏎related to AI per 1,000 uses” can be useful. HHS could encourage healthcare organizations to adopt such governance⏎structures, ensuring that AI tools are continually evaluated in context and not just set loose.⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 9⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎Workflow and Human-Centered Evaluation Methods:⏎Beyond technical performance, evaluating how AI fits into clinical workflow and impacts humans (providers and⏎patients) is vital:⏎• Usability Testing: Before and after deployment, performing usability tests (with methods like heuristic evaluation or⏎direct observation) ensures the AI’s user interface and integration are optimal. For instance, does the alert come at the⏎right time in the workflow? Is the information too much, too little? A great AI that is hard to use will fail. Metrics include⏎error rates in using the interface, time to complete tasks, and user satisfaction ratings.⏎• Impact on Provider Workload and Burnout: One promising evaluation angle is measuring how AI affects provider⏎workload. Early examples show AI scribes reduced physician after-hours documentation time, correlating with a 40%⏎drop in burnout in a pilot at Mass General Brigham[35]. On the flip side, poorly designed AI can increase frustration.⏎Therefore, evaluation should include surveys or analysis of provider workload before vs after AI. HHS might fund studies⏎on AI’s impact on provider well-being as part of evaluation. If AI can truly reduce burden (a key HHS goal[2]), it should be⏎quantified.⏎• Patient-Centered Evaluation: If the AI directly or indirectly touches patients, we should evaluate patient experience and⏎outcomes. Do patients trust the AI’s involvement? Are they more satisfied because decisions are faster or more⏎personalized? For example, in Massive Bio’s case, when patients use our AI-powered trial matching through ACS ACTS,⏎we collect feedback on whether it improved their understanding and options. Metrics like patient satisfaction scores,⏎uptake rates of AI-suggested options, and outcomes (like time to treatment or adherence rates) are important. In cancer⏎care, we could evaluate if AI-driven trial matching increases the percentage of patients who get onto a trial or a targeted⏎therapy, and if that in turn improves survival or quality of life (longer-term metrics).⏎Should HHS support these evaluation processes? Absolutely, and with urgency. Given the rapid proliferation of AI, we cannot rely⏎on ad-hoc evaluation if we want public trust and effective adoption. HHS can play a pivotal role by facilitating systematic⏎evaluation in the following ways:⏎• Funding and Grants: HHS (through AHRQ, NIH, ONC, etc.) should fund research and development of AI evaluation⏎frameworks and tools. For pre-deployment evaluation, grants could support creation of open reference datasets and⏎bias testing suites that vendors can use to self-evaluate. For post-deployment, AHRQ could fund projects that develop⏎best practices for continuous monitoring or that measure real-world impact of AI on outcomes. For example, funding a⏎multi-center trial to test how an AI referral system affects cancer patient outcomes across different communities would⏎provide valuable evidence (and inform CMS reimbursement decisions). Specific grant programs (like SBIR for small⏎businesses or ONC’s Leading Edge Acceleration Projects - LEAP) could solicit proposals on innovative AI evaluation⏎methods, such as automated validation tools or explainability techniques.⏎• Contracts and Cooperative Agreements: HHS could use contracts to engage entities to perform evaluations on a broader⏎scale. For instance, ONC could contract an academic center or standards organization to develop an “AI in Clinical Care⏎Accreditation” program or voluntary certification (akin to how ONC contracted for development of EHR test procedures).⏎Cooperative agreements could establish centers that partner with HHS to evaluate AI implementations in real healthcare⏎settings (similar to patient safety organizations, but for AI). One idea is a National AI Testing Collaborative: HHS⏎sponsors a cooperative network of health systems that agree to pilot and evaluate AI solutions to generate public data⏎on what works and what doesn’t. This network can publish results that everyone can learn from, rather than each⏎hospital reinventing the wheel in private.⏎• Prize Competitions and Challenges: As the RFI suggests, prize competitions are a flexible tool. HHS could run an “AI⏎Evaluation Challenge” – inviting proposals for novel metrics or methods to test AI’s safety and efficacy, with prize⏎funding to the best ideas[28]. Another possibility: an “AI Stress Test Challenge” where participants must design a battery⏎of tests to rigorously probe an AI system for edge-case failures. These competitions can spur creativity and bring in⏎multidisciplinary talent (e.g., tech, human factors, ethics experts) to tackle evaluation.⏎• Standards and Frameworks Endorsement: HHS should collaborate with standards-developing organizations (like IEEE,⏎ISO, or NIST). NIST, for example, has released an AI Risk Management Framework recently; HHS can adopt and tailor this⏎to healthcare, encouraging providers and vendors to use it as a guideline for evaluating and managing AI risks. By⏎referencing such frameworks in HHS communications or even incorporating them into ONC’s certification criteria or⏎CMS’s expectations for ACOs/hospitals, it nudges the whole industry to systematically evaluate AI. ONC could, for⏎instance, include criteria for “baseline algorithm validation and ongoing monitoring” in its voluntary health IT⏎certification for AI tools, which would essentially operationalize evaluation standards.⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 10⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎In summary, robust evaluation methods exist or are emerging – retrospective validation, prospective pilots, continuous⏎monitoring, bias and robustness testing, usability and workflow assessment – but they need to become standard practice. HHS⏎should absolutely support these processes, because without evaluation, we risk patient safety and loss of trust. The mechanisms⏎should be diverse: grants to stimulate research, contracts/cooperative agreements to implement evaluation programs at scale,⏎and prize competitions to crowdsource the best ideas. HHS can also facilitate public-private data sharing that makes evaluation⏎easier (for example, maintain a repository of de-identified patient cases for algorithm testing).⏎Massive Bio welcomes HHS’s support in this area – as an AI company, having clear benchmarks and evaluation protocols helps us⏎ensure our solutions meet the highest standards. In fact, Massive Bio’s internal quality system for our AI (with Radiant Core⏎overseeing knowledge, quality, compliance[13]) could serve as one model for industry. If HHS were to, say, fund a project to⏎pilot a “clinical AI audit toolkit,” we would be eager to contribute our expertise. Ultimately, systematic evaluation is what⏎separates hype from reality in AI. By investing in these processes, HHS will foster trustworthy AI that providers can rely on⏎and patients can embrace, accelerating adoption in a safe, effective manner.⏎Question 5. Supporting Private-Sector AI through Accreditation, Certification, and Credentialing – Role of HHS⏎Q5: “How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing, and⏎credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?”⏎HHS can play a catalytic role in empowering the private sector to self-organize and self-regulate in ways that⏎promote safe and effective AI. The key is for HHS to endorse and reinforce industry-led efforts by providing⏎guidance, recognition, and where appropriate, incentives or requirements tied to accreditation and⏎certification. Here are concrete ways HHS can support these private-sector activities:⏎• Establish an “AI in Healthcare” Voluntary Certification Program: Just as ONC certifies electronic health record systems,⏎HHS (via ONC or a designated body) could set up a voluntary certification for AI tools used in clinical care. The private⏎sector can drive the criteria development – for example, working through a consortium like the Coalition for Health AI or⏎IEEE – but HHS’s support would give it weight. Certification criteria might include core requirements such as:⏎demonstrated accuracy above a threshold, adherence to privacy/security standards, bias testing results, interoperability⏎capabilities (e.g., can ingest/produce data via FHIR), and usability evidence. HHS can support this by convening⏎stakeholders to define the standards and by officially recognizing certified products (perhaps maintaining a public list on⏎HealthIT.gov of “HHS-recognized Certified Clinical AI Tools”). Although voluntary, this signals to healthcare providers⏎which products have met quality bars, accelerating adoption. Moreover, HHS could tie incentives to certification: for⏎instance, CMS could provide a slight increase in reimbursement or a bonus under MIPS for providers who utilize certified⏎AI tools (analogous to how using certified EHR technology was required for incentive payments). Massive Bio sees value⏎in this – as a company, we would pursue such certification to differentiate our AI platform as trustworthy and effective.⏎• Support Development of Accreditation Standards for AI Use by Healthcare Organizations: The private sector, through⏎bodies like The Joint Commission or URAC, could develop accreditation criteria around AI deployment in hospitals and⏎clinics. These might cover governance (does the organization have an AI oversight committee?), training (are clinicians⏎trained in AI tools?), monitoring (does the hospital monitor AI outcomes and safety incidents?), and patient⏎consent/communication about AI. HHS can encourage this by collaborating with these accrediting organizations. For⏎example, HHS could fund a project with The Joint Commission to develop new standards or provide pilot testing⏎opportunities. Once developed, HHS could endorse these accreditation standards as best practice. Eventually, CMS⏎might even incorporate some into Conditions of Participation, but even before that, simply having an accreditation⏎available sets a bar for health systems to strive for. An analogy is how cybersecurity accreditation (like HITRUST) became⏎a de facto requirement in healthcare IT; similarly, an AI governance accreditation could become a norm. HHS’s role is to⏎validate and promote that these private standards serve public goals (safety, efficacy, equity).⏎• Facilitate Industry-Driven Testing and Benchmarking Consortia: Private companies often hesitate to share data or⏎methods for competitive reasons, but HHS can provide a neutral ground for collaboration on testing. For instance, HHS⏎could sponsor an AI Clinical Validation Lab where multiple AI vendors’ tools are evaluated on a common dataset or⏎against common clinical scenarios. This lab could be run by a trusted third party (maybe NIST or a university) under⏎contract with HHS. Participation could be voluntary but if HHS provides reports or “seals of quality” to top performers, it⏎incentivizes companies to participate and improve. The industry-driven aspect means the metrics and test cases are⏎decided in consultation with vendors and providers so they’re meaningful. We’ve seen some industry coalitions (like the⏎xVIEW challenges in medical imaging) – HHS can amplify these by lending support and possibly aligning federal needs⏎with them (e.g., linking a challenge to a federal prize or incorporating results into procurement preferences).⏎• Credentialing and Training of Healthcare Workforce in AI: HHS can encourage the private sector in credentialing⏎clinicians for AI competencies. For example, professional boards (like the American Board of Radiology or Oncology⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 11⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎societies) might develop certification modules for practitioners to be “AI-proficient” in their field. HHS can support grants⏎to medical specialty societies to create continuing education and credentialing programs on AI. The private sector⏎(medical boards, specialty colleges) will lead the content, but HHS’s imprimatur or funding can accelerate it. One idea is⏎an “AI Skills Consortium” with representation from major medical boards that, with HHS support, defines core⏎competencies for clinicians using AI and develops training materials. If such credentials exist, healthcare organizations⏎could require or prefer that clinicians attain them to use certain high-stakes AI tools, ensuring effective use. For instance,⏎if an AI is used to assist in ICU care, hospitals might want ICU doctors to complete an accredited course on AI in critical⏎care. HHS can further encourage this by linking it to quality programs (e.g., hospitals reporting the percentage of staff⏎certified in AI usage could earn quality credits).⏎• Recognize and Promote Exemplars through Public-Private Partnerships: HHS should continue to partner with leading⏎private entities to showcase success stories of AI implementation (similar to how HHS partnered via the CancerX⏎initiative, which Massive Bio participates in[29][30]). By highlighting case studies of accredited/certified AI tools⏎improving care, HHS builds momentum. Perhaps an annual “HHS AI in Healthcare Awards” could be established to⏎recognize healthcare organizations and AI developers that achieve outstanding results (in safety, outcomes, equity). This⏎kind of recognition program, while not directly accreditation, motivates private actors to strive for excellence and gives⏎them a platform to share best practices.⏎• Leverage Existing Quality and Safety Programs: HHS can integrate AI considerations into existing private-sector-led⏎quality programs. For example, Patient Safety Organizations (PSOs) could be encouraged (through AHRQ guidance) to⏎collect and analyze AI-related safety events. HHS could also coordinate with malpractice insurers and organizations like⏎ECRI Institute to develop risk management guidelines for AI – these bodies often create accreditation or approval⏎programs for technologies from a safety standpoint. HHS’s convening power can help unify these efforts so that a⏎coherent set of expectations emerges rather than a patchwork.⏎• Support Interoperability and Standards Development: A form of certification/industry testing that HHS has long⏎supported is in interoperability (HL7, IHE connectathons, etc.). HHS should continue this in the AI context, perhaps⏎sponsoring “plug-a-thon” events for AI integration, where EHR vendors, AI companies, and providers work on integrating⏎AI using standards (like CDS Hooks, FHIR resources for AI outputs, etc.). By doing so, the private sector tools become⏎more plug-and-play, which promotes adoption. HHS can require in its own procurement (for VA, DoD, or federally⏎qualified health centers) that AI solutions support certain interoperability standards, pushing vendors to certify⏎compliance with those standards. This is similar to ONC requiring certified EHRs to support FHIR APIs – which unleashed⏎private sector innovation on top of EHR data. Now we might say certified AI should expose a FHIR-based service for⏎integration, etc.⏎In essence, private sector leadership is essential – they have the expertise and agility to set meaningful standards – but HHS’s⏎backing ensures those standards have legitimacy and reach. The question asks how HHS can best support these activities, and we⏎think the answer lies in a combination of facilitation, endorsement, and incentive alignment. HHS doesn’t need to control these⏎processes; rather, it should act as a partner.⏎For example, Massive Bio as a private company is part of multiple collaborative efforts (we mentioned CancerX,⏎and we also work with the Precision Cancer Consortium and other industry groups) aiming to improve data⏎sharing and AI use in oncology. If HHS provides even light-touch support – such as sending representatives to⏎participate, providing grant funding, or officially recognizing outputs of these consortia – it amplifies our⏎impact.⏎One concrete suggestion: HHS could create an “AI Clinician User Certification” in partnership with medical⏎societies (credentialing clinicians in AI use) and an “AI Tool Certification” in partnership with standards⏎bodies (credentialing the tools themselves). HHS’s seal on both sides of that equation would greatly⏎accelerate trust and uptake.⏎Another area is industry-driven testing: HHS could coordinate with big health systems and AI vendors to create a⏎National AI Testing Sandbox, as mentioned, where AI developers can safely test their algorithms on⏎federated data. Private sector provides the data and tech, HHS provides convening and legal framework⏎(maybe via a DUA or a public-private data trust). This gets around data silos for evaluation.⏎In summary, HHS should harness the energy and innovation of private entities by providing frameworks and⏎recognition. Think of it as HHS setting the stage and providing the spotlight, while the private sector⏎performers put on the show of developing robust AI and proving it works. This collaborative approach will⏎promote innovation and ensure effectiveness, because private stakeholders will buy into the standards they⏎helped create. Ultimately, by supporting accreditation, certification, and credentialing, HHS helps build an⏎ecosystem of trust and quality around AI in clinical care – which is the bedrock for sustainable adoption.⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 12⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎Question 6. Successes and Shortcomings of AI in Clinical Care; High-Potential Novel AI Tools⏎Q6: “Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and where have they⏎fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights⏎on quality, and help reduce costs?”⏎Successes – AI Tools that Met or Exceeded Expectations: In several domains, AI has already delivered notable⏎successes, improving performance or reducing costs beyond what was expected:⏎• Medical Imaging and Diagnostics: AI for image analysis has shone as an early success. For example, in radiology, FDA-⏎cleared AI algorithms for detecting diabetic retinopathy or identifying pulmonary nodules on CT scans have⏎demonstrated accuracy on par with expert physicians. Many hospitals report that AI-driven diagnostic tools are speeding⏎up workflow and catching findings that might be missed. A concrete success story is in sepsis detection: Cleveland Clinic⏎deployed an AI-based sepsis early warning system (Bayesian Health’s platform) and observed dramatic improvements –⏎a 10-fold reduction in false alarm rates and a 46% increase in actual sepsis cases identified in time[34]. The AI alerted⏎clinicians to sepsis in many cases even before antibiotics were given, allowing earlier life-saving intervention[34]. This⏎exceeded expectations by both improving clinical outcomes (sepsis detection and treatment timing) and reducing alert⏎fatigue (fewer false positives than prior systems). Another diagnostic success is in pathology: AI tools that analyze digital⏎pathology slides have improved detection of metastatic cancer cells and can do in minutes what might take a pathologist⏎much longer. These tools augment the specialists, leading to faster results and potentially cost savings through⏎efficiency.⏎• Operational Efficiency and Cost Savings: AI has made significant strides in hospital operations. Predictive algorithms for⏎patient flow and staffing have helped some hospitals optimize bed management and reduce length of stay. For instance,⏎AI-driven scheduling tools have cut wait times in operating rooms and imaging departments by predicting no-shows or⏎delays. On a system level, industry analyses project major cost savings; one estimate suggests AI could reduce hospital⏎operating costs by 10–20% in the long term, translating to up to $300–$900 billion in annual savings by 2050 if scaled⏎nationally[36]. While much of that is projection, some organizations are already seeing substantial returns on specific AI⏎deployments (e.g., reducing unnecessary ER visits through AI triage, saving millions annually for large health systems).⏎We have even early examples in revenue cycle management: AI for automated medical coding and billing has sped up⏎reimbursements and lowered administrative costs for many practices, meeting expectations for ROI in that area.⏎• Administrative and Documentation Burden Reduction: AI “scribes” and natural language processing tools have been⏎quite successful in reducing clinician burden. Pilot programs with ambient listening devices that transcribe and structure⏎clinical notes have shown that doctors spend less time typing and more time with patients. As noted, Mass General⏎Brigham reported about a 40% relative drop in physician burnout during an AI scribe pilot, as it significantly cut after-⏎hours documentation[35]. While the financial ROI for scribes is still being assessed, the improved provider satisfaction⏎and reclaimed time have exceeded expectations in those trials – a critical outcome given the burnout crisis.⏎• Clinical Decision Support and Personalized Medicine: In specialized areas like oncology, AI-driven decision support has⏎started to exceed expectations by handling complexity beyond human limits. Massive Bio’s experience provides an⏎example: our AI-driven trial matching “pre-screening hubs” have dramatically shortened the time to identify clinical⏎trial options for cancer patients and boosted trial participation rates, as recognized by the White House Cancer⏎Moonshot[12]. These hubs scan comprehensive genomic profiles and patient data to pinpoint trials in real-time,⏎achieving in minutes what used to take expert teams many hours. The result is more patients getting matched to trials⏎(over 50,000 patients per year projected) and faster enrollment – outcomes that exceed prior benchmarks and⏎expectations for expanding trial access[11][12]. This has also shown cost benefits: every patient who goes on a trial often⏎gets expensive therapy covered by the trial sponsor, potentially saving payers tens of thousands of dollars per patient⏎compared to standard care.⏎• Population Health and Preventive Care: AI risk prediction models have helped focus interventions on the right patients.⏎For example, some ACOs use AI to predict which patients are at risk of hospitalization in the next 30 days and then⏎proactively deploy care managers to those patients. In some cases, these models have exceeded expectations by⏎identifying high-risk individuals that traditional methods missed, leading to measurable reductions in readmissions or ER⏎visits. Insurers, too, have used AI to flag potential medication non-adherence or care gaps and have improved outcomes⏎with relatively low-cost outreach, saving larger costs down the line.⏎Shortcomings – Where AI Tools Have Fallen Short:⏎• Generalizability and Real-World Performance Gaps: A number of AI tools that performed well in research settings failed⏎to meet expectations in the real world. One famous example is IBM Watson for Oncology – heavily touted as an AI to⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 13⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎revolutionize cancer treatment, it ultimately fell short of clinical expectations and was not shown to improve patient⏎outcomes meaningfully. This highlighted that domain-specific nuance and integration into workflow were⏎underestimated. In some cases, AI algorithms have shown degraded accuracy when deployed on different patient⏎populations or different hospitals than they were trained on, exposing a lack of generalizability. For instance, an AI⏎trained on one hospital’s imaging data might not perform as well on another’s due to different equipment or patient⏎demographics. These disappointments have made clear that rigorous external validation is needed; some early AI⏎products did not get that, leading to over-promising and under-delivering.⏎• Bias and Health Equity Issues: A notable shortcoming was uncovered in an algorithm widely used to prioritize patients⏎for care management in some health systems: it systematically underestimated the health needs of Black patients⏎compared to white patients because it used healthcare spending as a proxy for illness, and historically less money was⏎spent on Black patients[22]. This bias meant the tool fell short morally and practically by missing high-need patients. It⏎was an eye-opener that some AI can inadvertently perpetuate disparities – a failure to meet the expectation that AI⏎would be objective and fair. HHS has rightly flagged this as an area needing attention. Patients and providers expect AI to⏎at least do no harm in terms of equity, and when those expectations are violated, it undermines trust.⏎• Lack of Clear Financial ROI in Some Cases: While AI can save costs, in some deployments the ROI has been elusive or⏎slower than expected. For example, early adopters of AI for clinical documentation found that while doctors were⏎happier, the financial ROI wasn’t immediate because these tools can be expensive to implement and maintain[37]. Some⏎hospital executives expected quick cost savings from AI, but realized that benefits sometimes accrue in intangible ways⏎(quality, patient satisfaction) or long-term (averted costs in the future) rather than immediate budget relief. As noted in⏎a FierceHealthcare evaluation, AI scribes improved experience but had “limited financial ROI” initially[37]. This shortfall⏎in meeting cost expectations is often due to integration costs, training, and the need to adjust workflows. Similarly, some⏎predictive models didn’t yield savings because organizations struggled to act on the predictions (the “last mile”⏎problem). This taught us that AI alone doesn’t save money; it must be paired with effective process changes, and that⏎piece sometimes fell short.⏎• Workflow Integration and Alert Fatigue: Some AI tools under-delivered because they weren’t well integrated or⏎produced too many alerts. For example, early AI-based clinical decision support for EHRs sometimes generated excessive⏎suggestions or warnings that annoyed clinicians (e.g., constantly reminding about care gaps or suggesting irrelevant⏎clinical trials). This created alert fatigue and low utilization, falling short of the hoped-for impact on quality. The issue⏎wasn’t that the AI was “wrong” – rather it wasn’t nuanced or user-friendly enough, betraying expectations that AI would⏎simplify workflows when in fact it sometimes complicated them. The lesson learned is the importance of human-⏎centered design, which some early solutions lacked.⏎In summary, AI has excelled in well-defined tasks like image analysis, prediction of specific events like sepsis, and automating⏎routine processes – often exceeding expectations in speed and accuracy. It’s faltered when faced with issues of generalization,⏎bias, user adoption, and translating predictions into practice. Many shortcomings have been due to context, not core algorithmic⏎ability: integration, trust, data quality, etc.⏎High-Potential Novel AI Tools for the Future:⏎Looking forward, we see several categories of novel AI tools that could have transformative impacts on outcomes,⏎quality insights, and cost reduction:⏎• AI for Precision Medicine & Complex Decision Support*: AI that can synthesize multimodal data* (genomics,⏎proteomics, imaging, clinical records) to guide personalized treatment stands to greatly improve outcomes, especially⏎in complex diseases like cancer, cardiac disease, or neurological conditions. The next generation of decision support⏎might use deep learning to identify hidden patterns – for instance, predicting which cancer patients will respond to⏎immunotherapy by integrating genomic markers with pathology images and clinical history. Such an AI could spare⏎patients ineffective treatments (improving outcomes and avoiding costs of toxicity). It could also suggest novel uses⏎for existing drugs (drug repurposing) or optimal trial opportunities. The potential to give new insights on quality comes⏎from uncovering subgroups of patients who have vastly different outcomes with different approaches – something⏎traditional analysis often misses. Massive Bio’s work hints at this potential: by analyzing real-world data across⏎thousands of patients, our AI has started to identify patterns (like particular tumor mutations combined with patient⏎demographics) that correlate with better trial responses. In the future, a clinician might get an AI-generated “patient⏎similarity” analysis: “patients like this one, with X and Y characteristics, tended to live 6 months longer on treatment A vs⏎treatment B.” This kind of guidance could revolutionize quality of care by truly individualizing it.⏎• Generative AI and Large Language Models (LLMs) in Clinical Care: The emergence of powerful LLMs (akin to ChatGPT)⏎trained on medical knowledge has huge potential. Novel tools using LLMs could function as AI co-pilots for clinicians –⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 14⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎e.g., synthesizing a patient’s entire chart and providing a concise summary with key recommendations, or even drafting⏎clinical plans that the doctor can refine. This can save time (reducing costs associated with documentation and⏎information retrieval) and potentially improve quality by ensuring no detail is overlooked (the AI can cross-check⏎treatments against guidelines and patient specifics). Careful deployment of LLMs could also improve patient⏎communication: imagine an AI agent that converses with patients to gather history or answer common questions with⏎empathy and clarity, extending the care team’s capacity. These tools are novel and need vetting, but their ability to⏎understand and generate human-like medical dialogue is promising for addressing two needs: freeing up clinicians and⏎engaging patients better. If implemented with guardrails, they could reduce administrative burden dramatically (cost⏎saving) and enhance patient satisfaction and understanding (quality).⏎• Predictive Analytics for Preventive and Value-Based Care: AI that predicts events like hospital admissions, disease⏎progression, or complications can enable preemptive action. A novel wave of predictive tools, improved by AI, could⏎manage chronic diseases far more effectively – for example, AI monitoring of heart failure patients via wearables,⏎predicting acute decompensation days in advance so that outpatient intervention prevents a costly hospitalization. Such⏎tools would reduce costs (by avoiding hospital stays) and improve outcomes (by preventing crises). Another high-⏎potential area is AI for frailty and dementia, conditions highlighted by HHS for coming decades[38]. New AI models using⏎data from daily activities, sensors, and routine health info could flag early cognitive decline or frailty risk, prompting⏎earlier therapy or home modifications that maintain quality of life and reduce later institutionalization costs. HHS⏎focusing research on these would be wise (ties into Q10).⏎• Automation of Routine Tasks and Robotic Process Automation (RPA): While not as glamorous as clinical diagnostics, AI-⏎driven automation of repetitive administrative tasks holds massive cost-saving potential. From appointment scheduling⏎bots to AI handling prior authorization paperwork (retrieving required info, populating forms) to automated image post-⏎processing, these novel applications free human staff to do higher-level work. The savings from reducing administrative⏎overhead (which is huge in U.S. healthcare) could be significant. For instance, an AI that processes claims or billing might⏎drastically cut billing errors and time, or an AI that manages inventory and supply chain in a hospital could prevent stock-⏎outs of critical supplies or overstock of expensive items.⏎• Decentralized and At-Home Care via AI: Tools that allow hospital-level monitoring and treatment at home are emerging.⏎AI can interpret data from home devices (like oxygen saturation monitors, glucometers, etc.) to manage patients⏎remotely. Novel AI that integrates into telehealth platforms could triage which home patients need in-person visits,⏎which can be managed virtually, optimizing resource use. This area is ripe to both cut costs (hospital at home is cheaper⏎than hospital in-building) and maintain or improve outcomes (patients often prefer to heal at home, lowering infection⏎risk too). An example might be an AI that monitors a post-surgery patient’s wearable data and virtual check-ins to detect⏎early signs of complication and arrange a home nurse visit immediately – heading off an ED visit.⏎• Clinical Trial Optimization and Drug Development: AI tools can supercharge R&D, indirectly improving clinical care by⏎getting better therapies to market faster and cheaper. AI in trial design (simulating trials, optimizing patient selection⏎criteria) and in pharmacovigilance (monitoring for drug side effects in real time) are novel areas. If trials become more⏎efficient, costs of bringing new treatments down might drop, eventually reducing drug costs for the system. For example,⏎AI-driven digital twins (virtual patient models) could be used to predict outcomes, reducing the size or number of real⏎trials needed. This might be a bit farther horizon, but high potential.⏎In summary, the greatest potential lies in AI that amplifies human capabilities in complex decision-making and personalizes care,⏎as well as AI that relentlessly drives efficiency in the background. The goal is a health system that is smarter (insights we didn’t⏎have before), more precise (right treatment for right patient at right time), and leaner (less waste and friction). Achieving this⏎could dramatically improve outcomes and cut waste – the two pillars of value in healthcare.⏎From Massive Bio’s vantage point, we are especially excited about AI’s potential in democratizing clinical research and precision⏎oncology. By using AI to connect diverse patients with the best therapy options (standard or experimental) regardless of⏎where they live or who they are, we can improve outcomes and reduce inequities. That also reduces cost in a disease like⏎cancer – by avoiding ineffective therapies and focusing on those likely to work, or by utilizing clinical trials (often funded by⏎sponsors) instead of expensive commercial drugs when appropriate. For instance, if a $150,000/year cancer drug might not⏎work for a patient due to their tumor profile, but an AI identifies a trial with a promising agent available to that patient, and⏎that agent turns out effective, we’ve potentially saved six figures and improved the patient’s health.⏎Conversely, we caution that continued attention is needed to avoid repeating past shortfalls. Each high-potential AI tool must be⏎developed with robustness, fairness, and workflow in mind to truly deliver. HHS’s enabling policies can help ensure these⏎novel tools reach their potential by guiding development toward areas of genuine need (like dementia, frailty, health⏎disparities) and by smoothing the path for those that prove their value. The successes so far give us confidence that with the⏎right support, the next wave of AI tools will indeed meet or exceed our expectations – perhaps even achieving the⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 15⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎“transformative force” across healthcare that leadership envisions[39].⏎Question 7. Influential Roles in AI Adoption and Primary Hurdles in Health Care Organizations⏎Q7: “Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the most influence on the⏎adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?”⏎In our experience working with a variety of healthcare organizations (from major academic medical centers to⏎community oncology clinics), certain key decision-makers and bodies heavily influence whether AI tools are⏎embraced or not. Additionally, there are common administrative hurdles that organizations face when⏎implementing AI. Identifying these roles and hurdles is important because it helps target interventions (who⏎to educate, what processes to streamline) to speed up AI adoption.⏎Key Roles and Decision Makers Influencing AI Adoption:⏎• Executive Leadership (C-Suite): Hospital and health system executives – particularly the CEO, Chief Medical Officer⏎(CMO), Chief Information Officer (CIO), and Chief Technology/Innovation Officer – play a decisive role. If the C-suite⏎believes in a strong digital/AI strategy, the institution will pursue AI projects and allocate resources. Conversely, if top⏎leadership is skeptical or risk-averse, AI initiatives often stall. The Chief Financial Officer (CFO) is influential from a⏎budget perspective – their view on ROI can make or break an AI investment. Many successful AI adopters have had CEOs⏎who champion technology and CIOs/CTOs who have developed an AI roadmap. We’ve observed that large integrated⏎systems with visionary leadership (some even creating roles like Chief Data Scientist or VP of AI) are far ahead in⏎adoption rates, whereas organizations without that leadership focus lag behind[21].⏎• Clinical Champions (Physician/Nurse Leaders): Within clinical departments, respected physicians or nurse leaders who⏎advocate for an AI tool can significantly sway their peers and department’s willingness to adopt. For example, a Chair of⏎Radiology who is pro-AI can push their department to adopt AI in imaging faster, working through kinks and getting buy-⏎in. Many hospitals have a Chief Medical Information Officer (CMIO) or similar role – often a physician – who bridges IT⏎and clinical practice; these individuals are critical in evaluating AI tools and persuading other clinicians of their value. If⏎the CMIO or a departmental chief says “this AI will help us and I’m on board,” that endorsement carries weight.⏎Conversely, if frontline clinicians are not involved or are opposed, no amount of executive push will succeed – they can⏎refuse to use the tool or undermine it. Thus, identifying and empowering clinical champions is key. In our Massive Bio⏎implementations at cancer centers, we always identify an oncologist or research nurse leader who becomes the internal⏎champion for our trial-matching AI; their influence helps integrate the tool into the care flow.⏎• Governance Committees (e.g., IT Governance, Value Analysis, Ethics Boards): Many health organizations have formal⏎committees that evaluate new technologies or practices. These can include a Value Analysis Committee (which reviews⏎new clinical products for efficacy and cost), an IT Governance Board, and sometimes specialized AI or Digital Health⏎Committees in more advanced systems. These bodies typically include a mix of administrators, clinicians, IT staff, and⏎compliance officers. They influence adoption by scrutinizing proposals for new AI tools – looking at evidence, cost,⏎alignment with strategic goals – and then approving or denying them for purchase or pilot. If such a body is progressive⏎and educated about AI, it can facilitate adoption by giving the green light and funding. But if it’s overly conservative or⏎unfamiliar with evaluating AI, it can be a bottleneck. We’ve seen cases where a promising AI solution gets stuck for a⏎year in committee deliberations over data security or uncertain clinical benefits. Strengthening these governance⏎processes (with clear criteria for evaluating AI, perhaps training members on AI basics) can help.⏎• IT Department / Chief Information Security Officer (CISO): The IT team, including security and infrastructure personnel,⏎heavily influence whether an AI solution can be implemented. They evaluate integration effort, cybersecurity risks, and⏎compatibility. A supportive CIO/CTO will allocate resources to integrate an AI system with the EHR or data warehouse; a⏎cautious one might cite concerns and delay integration. The CISO’s approval is often needed if an AI involves cloud or⏎external data sharing. We often see great ideas slowed by months due to data security reviews and legal contracting for⏎data use. So while these roles might not “decide” on the business need, they have veto power if risk is deemed too high⏎or if the project demands IT work they can’t support.⏎• Payers and External Decision Makers: Within provider organizations, the above are primary. But we should mention that⏎external actors like insurance payers or regulators can indirectly influence adoption. For example, if a payer starts⏎reimbursing an AI-based service, the hospital CFO is more likely to approve that project. If a regulator or accreditation⏎body starts requiring AI-related competencies, the CMO will push to implement AI to stay compliant. Thus, while not⏎inside the organization, these external forces change the internal calculus of decision makers.⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles to AI Adoption:⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 16⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎• Budgeting and ROI Justification: One of the biggest hurdles is simply getting budget approval. Hospital budgets are tight,⏎and AI tools (plus the requisite IT infrastructure) cost money upfront. Administrators ask: What’s the return on⏎investment? Because many AI benefits are indirect or long-term (improved outcomes, operational efficiencies that are⏎hard to quantify immediately), making the business case is challenging. Often there is no line item for “AI” in budgets – it⏎competes with other urgent needs (new MRI machines, hiring staff, etc.). The uncertainty around ROI leads to slow or no⏎adoption. This is an administrative barrier because even if clinicians want it, the finance department might say no. We⏎need more demonstration of ROI (or at least value) to overcome this, as well as novel financing models (like vendor risk-⏎sharing or phased pilots). It would help if, as discussed earlier, reimbursement or savings from AI could be captured⏎more directly – that would ease CFO concerns.⏎• Procurement and Contracting Processes: Hospitals have complex procurement processes that can be a hurdle. Any new⏎AI vendor has to go through legal contracting, often addressing data use, IP, liability, etc., which can be arduous⏎especially if the contracts office is not versed in AI-specific issues. Things like intellectual property ownership of models⏎or data sharing rights can cause delays. Additionally, some organizations require competitive bidding or extensive due⏎diligence for new vendors, which prolongs adoption. Many AI companies are startups without decades of track record,⏎which makes procurement committees nervous. We’ve seen several month lags in just negotiating data use agreements.⏎Streamlining these processes (perhaps with template AI contracts, as recommended earlier) is needed.⏎• Integration with Existing Systems (Technical Hurdle): Administratively, coordinating between the AI vendor and internal⏎IT for integration can be a hurdle. Schedules, priorities, and technical resources must align. If an AI can’t plug easily into⏎the EHR or workflow, it may end up as another separate system clinicians have to log into – which is a barrier to⏎adoption (clinicians resist multiple logins/screens). So from an admin perspective, the hurdle is marshaling the needed IT⏎changes or interfaces, which might require approvals from EHR vendors or internal change control processes. This is⏎time-consuming and sometimes costly (EHR vendors might charge for integration work). Until interoperability improves,⏎each new tool has this hurdle. Interoperability improvements (discussed in Q8) would directly tackle this.⏎• Training and Change Management: Adopting AI isn’t just a software install; it’s a process change. Administratively,⏎planning and executing training for staff is a hurdle. Hospitals need to schedule training sessions, develop new policies⏎(e.g., how to override AI recommendations, documentation of AI usage), and monitor compliance. Change management⏎is hard in healthcare – people are used to certain routines. Some administrators might be hesitant to introduce yet⏎another change given how burdened clinicians already are. Without strong training and support, an AI tool could flop, so⏎leadership needs to invest in that. But dedicating time for training takes providers away from clinics, which has a cost.⏎Some might not see it as a priority unless mandated.⏎• Data Governance and Privacy Concerns: Many organizations have internal rules about data sharing (like, they may not⏎allow PHI to be sent to cloud services, etc.). Getting approvals through privacy boards or compliance committees can be⏎a hurdle especially if the AI is cloud-based or requires big data transfers. There may be concern about HIPAA compliance,⏎and administrators in compliance/legal roles might press “pause” until thoroughly convinced. While these are legitimate⏎concerns, sometimes the caution is disproportionate to actual risk and slows beneficial projects.⏎• Measurement and Proof of Concept Requirements: Some institutions require extensive pilot studies internally before⏎full adoption – an administrative decision to mitigate risk. This means additional time to do an internal trial, measure⏎outcomes, present results to committees, etc. It’s a hurdle because it delays scaling. On the flip side, places that skip this⏎and jump to adoption might face backlash if something goes wrong. So it’s a careful balance.⏎• Cultural Resistance and Workforce Acceptance: Though not a formal administrative process, the culture is an underlying⏎factor. If an organization’s culture is not innovation-friendly or if staff fear AI (job displacement fears, “not invented⏎here” syndrome), adoption faces an uphill climb. Administrators must manage these cultural factors – e.g.,⏎communicating that AI is there to assist, not replace, and incorporating frontline feedback into selection and⏎implementation. Where culture has been addressed (through inclusive decision-making and transparency), adoption is⏎smoother; where it hasn’t, AI tools get silently sidelined by users.⏎To illustrate, consider a smaller community hospital: The CEO and CFO might be unsure about an expensive AI for ICU monitoring.⏎The ICU director is interested but the value committee says “show us evidence from similar hospitals.” IT says integration with⏎their older EHR will be tough. Meanwhile, nurses worry the AI alarms might add workload. All these need addressing by the right⏎leaders: the ICU director championing, the CIO ensuring integration, the CEO aligning it with strategy (like to be a high reliability⏎org), and the CFO perhaps agreeing to a pilot with vendor risk-sharing. If any of these pieces break down, adoption stalls.⏎Massive Bio often engages with oncology service line directors or research administrators at hospitals when offering our AI-⏎matching service. If the director sees how it helps patients and research, they influence the CMO or CEO. But often an admin⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 17⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎hurdle we see is “This sounds great, but do we have manpower to coordinate the patients that get matched? Who will own⏎this process internally?” That is a valid administrative hurdle – responsibility and workflow assignment. If unclear, they⏎hesitate to start. We help by offering concierge support (our staff to supplement theirs), which alleviates the hurdle.⏎In summary, the most influential actors are executive leaders (C-suite), tech and data leaders (CIO/CMIO/CISO), and clinical⏎champions/department heads, often guided by governance committees. Primary hurdles are financial justification,⏎complex procurement and integration processes, the effort needed for training and change management, and internal⏎risk/compliance gating. To accelerate AI adoption, we need to engage and educate those key decision-makers (for⏎example, demonstrating ROI to CFOs, assuring CIOs on security, showing clinicians evidence of benefit) and streamline⏎administrative processes (templates for contracts, better interoperability, etc.). If HHS and industry groups could⏎provide tools to health systems – say, an “AI adoption playbook” for hospital administrators – it could help organizations⏎navigate these hurdles more confidently.⏎Removing or easing these obstacles will empower those influential leaders to say “yes” to AI with fewer reservations. For⏎instance, if CMS or HHS provides clear evidence or endorsement of an AI solution’s efficacy (like via certification or⏎inclusion in best practice guidelines), it makes it easier for committees and executives to approve it. Knowing that others⏎have succeeded (perhaps through HHS sharing case studies) also gives decision-makers cover to proceed. Ultimately,⏎identifying who holds the keys and what roadblocks exist allows targeted strategies to unlock AI adoption at the⏎organizational level.⏎Question 8. Interoperability Opportunities to Fuel AI Development and Widen Adoption⏎Q8: “Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the development of AI⏎for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.”⏎Interoperability – the seamless exchange of health data – is the oxygen that AI needs to breathe and flourish in clinical⏎care. Enhanced interoperability would have far-reaching benefits: it would enlarge the available data for AI model⏎training (fueling research), make it easier to deploy AI in different settings (widening market opportunities), and⏎enable new types of AI-driven workflows that are currently impractical (accelerating adoption). Below we outline⏎key areas where improved interoperability is most needed and the specific data types/standards involved:⏎• Integration of Electronic Health Records (EHRs) with AI Tools: A top priority is making clinical data in EHRs easily⏎accessible to AI developers and systems via standardized APIs. The advent of FHIR (Fast Healthcare Interoperability⏎Resources) is a huge step forward. Enhanced use of FHIR (and full compliance with the 21st Century Cures Act’s⏎interoperability and information blocking rules) will widen opportunities by allowing third-party AI apps to plug into EHRs⏎securely. For example, if a hospital’s EHR exposes a FHIR API for patient demographics, problems, medications, labs, etc.,⏎an AI tool can fetch those in real-time to analyze and provide decision support (e.g., suggesting a clinical trial or⏎detecting a care gap) without custom integration. Standard FHIR profiles for specific needs (like scheduling, imaging⏎results, pathology reports) could let AI modules operate across many hospitals with minimal tweaks – this opens the⏎market for smaller innovators to serve multiple sites. ONC’s recently finalized regulations on API standards need to be⏎fully implemented and extended. Specifically, bulk FHIR data access (using FHIR Bulk Data / Flat FHIR) is crucial for⏎research and population-level AI. It allows pulling de-identified datasets of EHR records across thousands of patients for⏎model training or quality improvement analysis. If every EHR can export bulk data in a standard way (with proper⏎permissions), researchers won’t have to spend 80% of time cleaning and normalizing data – they can focus on the AI⏎algorithms. HHS should continue pushing bulk data standards and perhaps provide synthetic or test FHIR datasets for AI⏎benchmarking (like a realistic synthetic health record database on which AI can be evaluated fairly).⏎• Standardization of Key Clinical Data Types (e.g., Genomics, Imaging) for Interoperability: Certain high-value data types⏎are currently not as interoperable as they could be:⏎• Genomic Data: Precision medicine and AI go hand in hand, but genomic data (like DNA sequencing results, variant⏎interpretations) often reside in PDFs or proprietary lab portals. Adopting standards like HL7 FHIR Genomics or the⏎GA4GH (Global Alliance for Genomics and Health) schemas, and integrating those into EHRs, would greatly help. For⏎instance, the mCODE (minimal Common Oncology Data Elements) standard, built on FHIR, defines how to represent⏎oncology-specific data, including genomics, in a structured way[10]. If genomic results (mutations, biomarkers) are⏎interoperable and readily pulled into AI systems, it fuels advanced oncology AI that can identify trials or therapies⏎matching a patient’s molecular profile. Massive Bio’s platform uses native FHIR/mCODE integration to connect EHRs and⏎labs[10], which was essential for scaling our AI across different clinics. Widespread adoption of such standards will allow⏎any oncology AI tool to work in any oncology practice system, widening the market dramatically (currently, AI companies⏎have to custom-map data from each provider’s system).⏎• Imaging and PACS: Radiology and pathology images are typically stored in PACS systems using DICOM standards, which⏎is good, but making those images accessible to AI algorithms outside the local network is challenging. Enhanced⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 18⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎interoperability via cloud image exchanges or APIs that can provide images (with appropriate patient consent) to AI⏎model providers would accelerate development of imaging AI – researchers could access diverse imaging data, and⏎hospitals could plug in external AI services more easily. Initiatives like DICOMweb (RESTful APIs for DICOM) should be⏎advanced and linked with FHIR for orders and results. If an AI developer can, with permission, query “give me all chest X-⏎rays and outcomes for patients with COVID across 10 hospitals” through an interoperable network, it massively fuels⏎research. TEFCA (Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement) might be extended in the future to include⏎image exchange, not just documents – that’s an area to explore.⏎• Unstructured Clinical Notes: A lot of important info lives in doctors’ free-text notes. Natural language processing (NLP)⏎AI thrives on this, but currently exchanging notes reliably is tough (CCDA documents carry some notes, but often in blob⏎form). Enhanced interoperability could mean adoption of standards like C-CDA and FHIR DocumentReference to share⏎notes in structured formats. Also, using NLP-specific standards like CTAKES annotations or FHIR Communication⏎resources for note content could help. Imagine if a researcher could query a network for “de-identified notes of patients⏎who had symptom X” through a standard service – it would enable training conversational AI or symptom-checker⏎models at scale.⏎• Patient-Generated Health Data and Devices: Data from wearables, home devices, patient-reported outcomes etc., is⏎increasingly valuable for AI (e.g., in chronic disease management). Interoperability here is often overlooked. Standards⏎like IEEE 11073 (for device data) and frameworks to integrate patient-generated data into EHRs (projects like CARIN⏎Alliance for consumer data) should be advanced. If these data streams can flow into the clinical record in standardized⏎ways, AI can analyze continuous monitoring data to predict issues. For example, interoperability standards enabling a⏎patient’s smart watch ECG or glucometer readings to be pulled into an AI arrhythmia or hypoglycemia prediction model⏎across providers would widen telehealth and remote monitoring AI use.⏎• Interoperability to Link Clinical and Research Data: A major opportunity is connecting clinical care data with clinical trial⏎and research data. For instance, clinical trial registries (like ClinicalTrials.gov) are a rich source of eligibility criteria and⏎trial availability, but historically it’s been separate from EHR data. If criteria could be standardized (there are efforts to⏎use FHIR for trial eligibility criteria) and patient data can be matched to them systematically, AI can automate trial⏎matching at scale. Massive Bio does this by parsing ClinicalTrials.gov and matching with patient EHR data; the more⏎those formats are standardized, the more efficient and universal this becomes[40][7]. Enhanced interoperability⏎between healthcare and research also means if a patient consents, their EHR data could flow into research databases⏎(e.g., for learning health networks). HHS could encourage adoption of standards like OMOP common data model to allow⏎easier pooling of multi-center data for AI training. Also, consider linking claims data with clinical data – standards to⏎integrate Medicare claims or payer data with EHR data on the fly could allow AI to factor in cost and utilization patterns,⏎which might reveal insights about quality and efficiency (like identifying high-cost patient trajectories that could be⏎improved).⏎• Benchmarking Tools and Shared Datasets: The question also mentions benchmarking tools. Interoperability can enable⏎shared benchmark datasets for AI. For example, if multiple health systems can contribute de-identified data to a central⏎repository because they all speak the same data standards language, you can create a national “benchmark dataset” for,⏎say, diabetic patient outcomes, on which AI models from different vendors can be benchmarked. A current challenge is⏎that each AI vendor might test on their own proprietary dataset – not apples-to-apples. HHS could coordinate creation of⏎reference datasets (with patient privacy preserved) that are continuously updated via interoperable feeds from diverse⏎sources. These could be used to benchmark algorithm performance fairly. Perhaps the NIH or AHRQ could host such⏎repositories (some exist, like MIMIC for ICU data, but expanding to other domains).⏎• Enhanced Interoperability in Social Determinants of Health (SDOH) Data: Often overlooked, but SDOH (transportation,⏎housing, social support data) is critical for AI to truly understand patient context and improve outcomes. We need⏎standards (HL7 Gravity Project is defining some) to record and share SDOH in health records. If an AI can access⏎standardized SDOH data (like food insecurity status, or transportation needs), it can better tailor interventions and⏎identify at-risk patients. For instance, Massive Bio’s ACS ACTS program not only matches trials but also identifies social⏎needs like transportation and lodging[41][42]. Because ACS has integrated that data, our AI workflow can prioritize⏎solutions (like connecting to ACS’s Road to Recovery transport program[43]). If all that SDOH information was⏎interoperable, any AI care coordination tool could systematically account for barriers to care – improving real-world⏎effectiveness and equity.⏎Where enhanced interoperability would specifically widen market opportunities: If data flows more freely (with patient consent⏎and privacy safeguards), the barrier to entry for AI startups lowers – they don’t have to build custom pipelines for every hospital,⏎meaning more competitors and solutions in the market. For example, right now an AI developer might avoid building a tool for⏎small clinics because integrating with their EHR is too costly. With universal FHIR APIs, that market opens up. Enhanced⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 19⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎interoperability also means AI solutions can be EHR-agnostic – a huge selling point. Providers would have more choices (not locked⏎into AI offered by their EHR vendor only). This competition can drive down costs of AI and improve quality of solutions.⏎How interoperability fuels research: Perhaps the biggest impact – researchers (academic or industry) spend⏎enormous effort on data wrangling. If HHS ensures that more high-quality, diverse datasets are accessible in⏎standardized formats (possibly through a national health data exchange under TEFCA that includes research⏎as an allowed purpose with proper governance), AI research accelerates. One could imagine a secure national⏎research environment where authorized researchers can run AI training on aggregated EHR data from across⏎the country (with all identifiers removed or using a federated learning approach). That’s transformative – it⏎could lead to discoveries of patterns for rare diseases, or development of AI for underrepresented⏎populations (since currently, many AI models skew toward the data from big academic centers in urban⏎areas[21], leaving rural or minority-heavy populations less represented; broad interoperability would bring in⏎those missing pieces).⏎In our own operations, we’ve seen how interoperability makes a difference. Massive Bio’s Reticulum Nexus⏎platform is “interoperable by design” – it natively integrates with FHIR and mCODE, and connects EHRs, labs,⏎CROs, etc., which was critical for operating across many partners[10]. This interoperability allowed us to align⏎patients, providers, and sponsors on one data fabric to scale trial enrollment[44]. Without those standards,⏎doing this at a national level would be near impossible. It’s a microcosm of what could happen nationwide if⏎everyone adopts interoperability: you unlock network effects. The more data sources connect, the more⏎powerful the AI insights and the broader the impact.⏎Specific Suggestions: - HHS should continue strong implementation of information blocking rules so that providers⏎and EHR vendors cannot refuse legitimate data sharing requests. Patients should be empowered to direct⏎their data wherever (via apps, etc.) – this patient-mediated interoperability could feed AI-driven patient apps⏎and research. - Invest in health information exchanges (HIEs) to incorporate AI support – e.g., HIEs could⏎offer analytics or AI-ready datasets as a service to participants. - Promote or require standard benchmarks:⏎For example, ONC could coordinate with NIST to produce a set of synthetic yet realistic patient records⏎(covering diverse scenarios) that any AI for a given use case should be tested against. If these are FHIR⏎formatted and publicly available, any developer can benchmark and any buyer can demand to see results of⏎that benchmark, which in turn pushes developers to train on more interoperable data.⏎In conclusion, enhanced interoperability – especially via common data standards (like FHIR, DICOMweb, mCODE)⏎and data exchange frameworks (like TEFCA) – would supercharge both the supply side of AI (research and⏎development) and the demand side (deployment and adoption). It widens market opportunities by lowering⏎integration costs, fuels research by easing data access across silos, and accelerates development by enabling⏎collaborative benchmarking and learning from broader datasets. We cannot overstate the importance of this;⏎indeed, as HHS leadership noted, “AI is powered by data. Data liquidity and the trust in how data moves are⏎essential.”[18]. Massive Bio fully agrees and supports HHS initiatives to make data flow more freely and⏎securely. It’s akin to building the interstate highways for data – once in place, innovation in AI can travel and⏎spread at unprecedented speed, reaching every community and patient with its benefits.⏎Question 9. Patient and Caregiver Challenges for AI to Address – and Their Concerns about AI Adoption⏎Q9: “What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption and use of AI in⏎clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?”⏎From the patient and caregiver perspective, AI is not about algorithms – it’s about tangible improvements in their⏎healthcare experience and outcomes. Massive Bio engages directly with patients (particularly cancer patients⏎and their families) through our services, and we have gleaned insights into what they hope AI can solve and⏎what worries them about AI.⏎Challenges Patients/Caregivers Want AI to Address:⏎• Access to the Right Care and Information: One of the biggest patient pain points is navigating the healthcare system and⏎finding the best treatment options. Patients want AI to help cut through the complexity. For example, cancer patients⏎often ask, “Is there a clinical trial or a new therapy that could help me?” Many feel overwhelmed trying to research on⏎their own[45]. They would love AI-driven tools that proactively present them with personalized options. Our ACS ACTS⏎partnership is an example: patients fill a simple form and our AI matching provides tailored trial options, which patients⏎find empowering[46][45]. Patients desire this kind of personalized guidance at critical decision points – which hospital is⏎best for my condition, which treatment path likely fits me, etc. In general, they hope AI can democratize expertise, so⏎that even if they aren’t at a top academic center, they still get cutting-edge recommendations. Caregivers, likewise, want⏎tools that help them help their loved ones – e.g., an AI that could summarize a patient’s condition and list questions to⏎ask the doctor, or predict care needs.⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 20⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎• Timely and Accurate Diagnoses: Patients often express frustration about missed or delayed diagnoses. They would like⏎AI to assist in catching problems earlier. For instance, in radiology or pathology, if AI can detect a tumor or abnormality⏎that a busy clinician might overlook, patients see value – it could mean an earlier cancer diagnosis or avoiding a missed⏎finding. Particularly in fields like rare diseases, patients want AI to shorten the diagnostic odyssey by recognizing patterns⏎in symptoms that might point to a condition sooner than a human would.⏎• Better Coordinated Care and Reduced Administrative Burdens on Them: Navigating appointments, refills, referrals –⏎these are pain points patients hope AI can ease. They want the health system to be more coordinated and responsive.⏎An example challenge: a patient might see multiple specialists and feels like they have to retell their story each time and⏎chase after records. They would be thrilled if AI could integrate their information so every provider is up to date (a⏎function of interoperability and AI summarization). Also, tasks like scheduling or medication refills – patients would love⏎intelligent systems that make these seamless (e.g., an AI that knows when you’re due for a check-up or notices you⏎haven’t picked up a prescription and sends a reminder or help). This ties to quality of life; patients often feel they are⏎project managers of their own care – AI could lighten that load by proactively handling some logistics.⏎• Improved Treatment Experiences and Outcomes: Ultimately, patients and caregivers want better health outcomes –⏎longer survival, less pain, improved quality of life. They see AI as a potential means to tailor treatments (minimizing trial-⏎and-error) and to manage chronic conditions better day-to-day. For example, a diabetic patient might want an AI coach⏎that helps keep their blood sugar stable with personalized tips, or an AI that predicts a flare-up of a condition so they can⏎act early. Caregivers of elderly or chronically ill patients might want AI monitoring that alerts them or providers if⏎something is off (like subtle signs of stroke or deterioration in a dementia patient), preventing serious events. Essentially,⏎they hope AI can provide an “early warning system” and continuous support that current periodic visits cannot.⏎• Equity in Access: Patients in underserved areas or from marginalized communities often voice that they lack access to⏎specialists or trials. They hope AI can bridge that gap – e.g., via telemedicine AI or remote monitoring that connects them⏎to top expertise. They also want assurance that treatments are designed for people like them. For instance, minority⏎patients might feel more confident if AI can help ensure the care they receive is based on data that includes people of⏎their background (avoiding one-size-fits-all medicine).⏎To sum up the challenges: finding the right care faster, coordinating that care, and personalizing it to improve outcomes – these⏎are what patients really care about. AI that addresses these will be embraced. For example, ACS ACTS users appreciate that the⏎service helps them “navigate clinical trials and find the best treatment options available”, which was previously overwhelming[47].⏎Concerns Patients/Caregivers Have about AI:⏎• Privacy and Data Security: A foremost concern is “What happens to my data?” Patients worry that if AI systems are⏎pulling together their health information, it could be misused or fall into the wrong hands. They want to know that their⏎personal details and medical history remain confidential. There’s fear of hacking or leaks. For instance, if an AI is⏎analyzing their genomic data or insurance records, they ask: will this be shared with third parties, could it affect my⏎insurance coverage, or could an employer somehow access it? They need reassurance through transparency and strong⏎privacy protections that AI won’t compromise their confidentiality[48]. Our experience is that patients are often willing⏎to share data if it helps their care or others, but they want to actively consent and have control. So consent and clear⏎communication is key.⏎• Loss of Human Touch and Personal Interaction: Many patients fear healthcare could become impersonal if AI takes on⏎too much. The human connection with doctors and nurses is a source of comfort; there’s concern that AI might replace⏎that or make providers overly reliant on screens and algorithms. For example, an elderly patient might worry that a⏎doctor is paying more attention to an AI recommendation than to listening to them. They want assurance that AI is an⏎assistant, not a replacement – that it will free clinicians to spend more time caring and talking by handling routine tasks,⏎rather than erecting a wall of technology. The idea of a “robot doctor” can be intimidating; most patients still want a⏎human in the loop who has empathy and can interpret the AI through the lens of their individual story.⏎• Bias and Fairness: Patients, especially from historically underserved groups, are concerned that AI could be biased or⏎make unfair decisions[49]. They may have heard that algorithms sometimes work less well for minorities or women if not⏎properly designed. So a Black patient might be wary of an AI risk score dictating their care if they suspect it might⏎underestimate their needs due to biased training data. Similarly, some could fear discrimination – e.g., could AI deem⏎someone an “expensive” patient and limit their care (this touches on ethical concerns about cost-optimization possibly⏎conflicting with patient interest). They want AI to be a tool for health equity, not something that inadvertently deepens⏎disparities. Transparency about how AI makes decisions is crucial to alleviate this – patients say things like, “I don’t want⏎a computer to decide I’m not worth treating aggressively because of some statistic.” Thus, explaining that AI is checked⏎for bias and is meant to complement, not deny, care is important.⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 21⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎• Accuracy and Safety of AI Decisions: There is a general concern about AI making mistakes – “What if the AI is wrong?”⏎Many patients and caregivers are not automatically trusting of technology; they need proof that these tools are accurate⏎and won’t lead them astray. If an AI misses something important or gives a false alarm, it could harm them. For example,⏎if an AI reads an X-ray and misses a fracture, that’s a worry; or if an AI recommends a treatment that turns out⏎suboptimal, who is accountable? Patients want to know that AI systems are rigorously tested and that there’s oversight.⏎Caregivers might ask, “Will the doctor double-check what the AI says?” The concept of accountability is key – knowing⏎that the physician or institution still takes responsibility and that there are processes if the AI is wrong (like second⏎opinions or the ability to override).⏎• Understanding and Consent: Patients may feel anxious if AI is used in their care without their knowledge. Some worry⏎about being “experimented on” by unproven tech. They want to be informed that AI is involved and ideally to agree to it.⏎This is both a concern and a desire – they want to be part of the decision to use AI, similar to how they consent for⏎treatments. Also, they often won’t understand how an AI works (and many don’t need the technical detail), but they at⏎least want a basic explanation in plain language of why the AI’s suggestion is being made. Lack of understanding can⏎breed mistrust or even fear of the unknown.⏎• Cost and Access Concerns: Some patients wonder, will AI-driven care cost me more? If a fancy AI recommends some⏎high-tech test or sends me to a big hospital, can I afford it or will insurance cover it? Conversely, they might fear that AI⏎is being used by insurance to cut costs at the expense of care (like an AI might deny them a procedure by deeming it⏎unnecessary). This interplay of cost is on their minds. They want AI to reduce their out-of-pocket costs (maybe by⏎avoiding unneeded procedures), not to be an extra charge or a gatekeeper for coverage. We’ve heard patients⏎appreciate when our trial matching finds them options that could be free via a trial, thus saving them potential huge⏎drug costs – that’s a positive of AI linking to trials. But they would dislike if an AI recommended against a treatment⏎purely on cost grounds.⏎Addressing these concerns is vital for acceptance. In our practice, we emphasize that patients remain in control – they consent to⏎our services and data use, and we keep them informed. For example, ACS ACTS explicitly states it's an “AI-driven clinical trial⏎matching service” but under the guidance of ACS cancer information specialists[46][50], blending AI and human support to⏎maintain trust. We take privacy seriously; patients trust ACS and by extension our system because we uphold confidentiality and⏎they actively sign up for it. Ensuring trust and transparency is foundational.⏎In designing AI in clinical care, we should involve patient input early. HHS could encourage user-centered design,⏎including patients in testing. When patients see AI solving their problems – like reducing wait times, simplifying⏎their journey, or offering hope via new options – they become enthusiastic. But if they perceive AI as an imposed,⏎opaque system that might compromise their privacy or reduce human care, they will resist.⏎To sum up, patients and caregivers hope AI will make care more accessible, personalized, and coordinated, helping them⏎navigate complexity and catch problems early. They want it to amplify the good (better outcomes, more⏎knowledge) and minimize the bad (bureaucracy, errors). On the flip side, they worry about privacy, losing human⏎connection, bias, safety, and agency. Any strategy to implement AI at scale should incorporate safeguards and⏎education around these points: robust privacy protections, maintaining the clinician-patient relationship at the⏎core, demonstrable fairness and thorough validation of AI tools, and open communication/consent. If we get this⏎right, patients will not just accept AI – they will demand it, because it will be clear it serves their needs and respects⏎their values.⏎Question 10. Priority Areas for AI Research and Evidence – Literature on AI Impact and Economic Aspects⏎Q10: “Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?”⏎Priority Areas for AI Research (to Accelerate Adoption in Clinical Care):⏎HHS should prioritize research that addresses the gaps between promising AI technology and its practical,⏎effective implementation in healthcare. Based on the current landscape, we recommend focusing on:⏎1. Implementation Science for AI in Healthcare: It’s not enough to invent AI algorithms; we need to understand how to⏎integrate them into real-world clinical settings successfully. HHS should fund research on best practices for deploying AI⏎in various environments (large academic hospitals, small rural clinics, etc.), including workflow design, change⏎management, and training methods. This research should capture lessons from both successful and failed⏎implementations to create playbooks for health systems. For example, studying how an AI sepsis alert was rolled out⏎across multiple hospitals – what adoption strategies led to usage and improved outcomes versus where it was ignored –⏎will yield general principles. The outcomes could be guidelines or toolkits for health organizations. This directly⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 22⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎accelerates adoption by turning AI from a theoretical tool into one that is effectively used. HHS’s RFI itself mentions⏎strengthening implementation science and best practices, especially for complex scenarios[17], highlighting this as a⏎recognized need.⏎2. Evaluation and Monitoring Frameworks (Safety, Efficacy, Bias): Research should focus on developing standardized⏎methods to evaluate AI tools (as we discussed in Q4). This includes metrics for clinical impact, user trust, and economic⏎outcomes, as well as processes for continuous monitoring post-deployment. Part of this could be creating open datasets⏎or simulation environments for testing AI (essentially science around how to test AI well). If HHS invests in these, it gives⏎regulators and adopters confidence in AI, speeding up adoption. It also helps sift out ineffective or harmful tools early.⏎An example: researching how to do randomized controlled trials or other study designs for AI interventions (there is⏎ongoing work on cluster RCTs for AI, or adaptive trials where AI is the “intervention”).⏎3. Bias Mitigation and Fairness in AI: Prioritize research into algorithms that detect and correct bias, as well as methods to⏎ensure AI tools are equitable. This includes creating diverse training datasets and novel techniques like transfer learning⏎or federated learning to include data from underrepresented groups without breaching privacy. Also, sociotechnical⏎research on how biases manifest in AI-driven workflows and how to mitigate them through both technical and⏎organizational means. The goal is AI that works for all patient populations. For example, HHS could sponsor research to⏎improve AI for maternal health in Black women, given disparities, or AI in rural health contexts. Published findings have⏎shown biases (like the care management algorithm example[49]); now research must figure out how to systematically⏎avoid such biases. This is crucial for trust and broad utility of AI, which underpins adoption.⏎4. Interoperability and Data Sharing Models: Research how to safely and effectively share data for AI development –⏎including privacy-preserving analytics, federated learning, and the creation of high-quality synthetic data. HHS should⏎prioritize research that enables multi-center AI studies without violating privacy (e.g., federated learning pilots across⏎healthcare systems in a network). Also, research on the standardization of health data for AI (like refining FHIR for AI⏎needs, or extending mCODE) is important. This can inform policy and standards to make data more usable by AI, which⏎as we established will fuel AI advancement.⏎5. AI for Public Health and High-Burden Conditions: HHS might focus on AI research in areas that are national priorities: for⏎example, chronic diseases that drive cost (like diabetes, heart failure), mental health (like AI for suicide prevention or⏎substance abuse support), aging-related conditions (frailty, dementia as mentioned[38]), and health crises (like⏎pandemic response). By prioritizing these, HHS ensures AI is developed where it can have broad impact. For instance, AI⏎research in geriatrics to predict and prevent falls in elderly or manage polypharmacy could greatly improve quality of life⏎and reduce costs.⏎6. Human-AI Interaction & Explainability Research: To enhance trust, research should delve into how AI can effectively⏎communicate with clinicians and patients. This means studying different explanation approaches (visual aids, confidence⏎levels, etc.) and how they affect decision-making and outcomes. Also, research on how clinicians make use of AI⏎recommendations (when do they accept vs override, and why) can guide design improvements. This human factors angle⏎is sometimes overlooked but is vital for safe and accepted AI.⏎7. Economic Impact Analysis of AI in Healthcare: We need rigorous health economics research on AI – how it shifts costs⏎and benefits across stakeholders. This includes micro-level (does adopting AI in X department save money or shift labor?)⏎and macro-level (if AI reduces hospitalizations for heart failure by 20%, what is the net system effect?). Also, “transfers”⏎of costs – e.g., does AI reduce provider costs but increase patient or payer costs, or vice versa? HHS should fund studies⏎and models that simulate these scenarios. This evidence will inform reimbursement and investment decisions. It aligns⏎with question part (b) on costs and benefits.⏎Now, addressing parts (a) and (b):⏎(b) Published Findings about the Impact of Adopted AI Tools in Clinical Care:⏎Yes, there is a growing body of published evidence, though it varies by domain and is still maturing. Some⏎notable findings from literature include:⏎• Improved Clinical Outcomes: Certain AI implementations have shown measurable improvements. For example, a⏎published study on AI sepsis alerts (like one from Duke or Johns Hopkins) found increased early detection and reduced⏎mortality or ICU stay when the AI was used properly[34]. In medical imaging, multiple studies (some in Lancet Digital⏎Health or Nature Medicine) have shown AI algorithms matching or exceeding radiologist performance for detection of⏎cancers (like breast cancer in mammography) or other findings, potentially leading to earlier diagnosis. Another area is in⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 23⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎stroke care: an AI (like Viz.ai) that flags strokes on CT scans has been shown to reduce time-to-treatment (door-to-needle⏎time), which is crucial for outcomes. These findings illustrate impact in terms of speed and accuracy of care.⏎• Enhanced Efficiency and Provider Experience: The literature documents that AI can streamline operations. For instance,⏎a study in JAMA Network Open reported that an AI scribe technology led to significant reduction in clinician⏎documentation time and improved work-life balance, albeit without immediate financial ROI[37]. Another example:⏎studies on predictive analytics for no-shows have allowed clinics to double-book strategically and reduce unused⏎appointment slots, effectively increasing throughput without added cost. These are documented in healthcare⏎operations research journals.⏎• Patient Engagement and Satisfaction: Some early reports show that AI-powered patient engagement tools (like chatbot⏎follow-ups for chronic disease management or mental health) can maintain or even improve patient satisfaction while⏎reducing demand on staff. Published trials of chatbot cognitive behavioral therapy, for example, have shown some⏎efficacy in reducing mild anxiety or depression symptoms – not replacing therapists but augmenting scarce resources.⏎• Clinical Trial Efficiency: In research context, publications (including reviews like one in Journal of the American Medical⏎Informatics Association) have noted positive outcomes of AI in trial recruitment and design. For example, one review⏎found “AI-powered patient recruitment tools improve enrollment rates by 65%” on average[51], which is a significant⏎impact on how fast trials can complete. Also, predictive models achieving ~85% accuracy in trial outcomes (as reported⏎in summary points of some reviews)[52], hinting that AI could optimize trial protocols.⏎However, the literature also notes failures or neutral results: e.g., some studies where an AI did not significantly improve primary⏎outcomes (perhaps due to user compliance issues). One article in NEJM (Jan 2020 by Wong et al.) concluded that despite hype,⏎there were few AI RCTs and mixed results, suggesting need for better evidence. But since then, number of studies has grown.⏎(b) Literature on Costs, Benefits, and Transfers of AI in Clinical Care:⏎The health economics literature is evolving. Key points from what exists:⏎• Many studies use modeling and simulation to estimate potential cost savings of AI. For instance, a Morgan Stanley⏎report projected massive system-wide savings (like that $300–$900 billion by 2050 figure) by extrapolating current⏎efficiency gains across the system[36]. PwC’s analysis predicted over $1 trillion could shift to digital/AI health by⏎2035[53]. These are forward-looking and make assumptions, but they provide a macro perspective that AI, if widely⏎used, can bend the cost curve by automating labor, reducing errors, and enabling cheaper virtual care.⏎• Microsimulation analyses have been done for specific use cases. For example, an analysis might look at an AI for⏎detecting diabetic retinopathy: it might factor the cost of the AI screening vs the benefit of preventing blindness and find⏎a favorable cost-effectiveness ratio. Similarly, some cost-benefit analyses have been done on AI for stroke triage,⏎showing that faster treatment yields savings by preventing disability (translating to fewer rehab/nursing home costs,⏎etc.). These often appear in specialty journals.⏎• Some real-world cost evaluations: A study on a hospital’s AI sepsis program might show the program cost X to⏎implement, but saved Y in ICU days and penalties for sepsis outcomes, yielding net savings. If Y > X, ROI is positive. Some⏎such data are emerging in conference proceedings or implementation case studies. On transfers: for instance, if AI⏎reduces hospital admissions, hospitals lose revenue (under fee-for-service) while payers save money – that’s a transfer⏎of benefits that literature notes. Conversely, under value-based care, the hospital as ACO saves costs and benefits. So,⏎context matters.⏎• Productivity and labor transfer: Another angle literature discusses is who benefits from efficiency. If AI cuts down⏎documentation time (benefit to physician time, which could see more patients or go home earlier), the “financial”⏎benefit might not directly appear in a ledger, but it could reduce burnout and staff turnover (which is a cost if they⏎leave). These indirect benefits are discussed qualitatively and increasingly quantitatively (some studies tie decreased⏎burnout to retention rates, etc.).⏎• The literature calls for more robust evidence generation. Recognizing this, journals like NEJM Catalyst, JAMIA, BMJ⏎Quality & Safety have called for publishing not just accuracy of AI but impact on workflow and cost. There are now⏎frameworks for assessing health AI similar to drug HTA (Health Technology Assessment), incorporating cost-⏎effectiveness.⏎In terms of transfers, one key observation in literature: AI might incur upfront costs (software, training, maybe increased imaging if⏎AI finds more nodules that need follow-up) and savings come later (fewer late-stage cancers to treat, etc.). So, the timeline of⏎costs/benefits differ among stakeholders. The literature often frames this in terms of who invests vs who gains. For example,⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 24⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎primary care might invest in AI to prevent hospitalizations, but the hospital (if paid per service) loses revenue; however,⏎society/payers gain net benefit. Under capitated models, it aligns. So, research often models scenarios under different payment⏎models.⏎Given the still emerging state of evidence, HHS should promote more pilot studies and dissemination of results. It⏎might even require reporting of outcomes from AI demonstration projects funded by HHS, to build a public⏎knowledge base.⏎HHS’s Role in Research and Evidence:⏎HHS should fill the evidence gaps by funding and possibly coordinating multi-site evaluations. For instance, to⏎understand economic impact, HHS could run or fund a demonstration where a certain AI is implemented in⏎several diverse sites with analysis of clinical and financial outcomes.⏎We note that to date, many promising results appear in single-site studies or retrospective analyses. The next step⏎is more rigorous prospective multi-center studies. HHS is well positioned to drive this via agencies like AHRQ⏎(for safety and quality studies), NIH (for clinical effectiveness trials of AI), and CMS (through demonstration⏎projects or model tests focusing on AI).⏎Also, HHS should prioritize areas that combine high unmet need, potential for AI to help, and alignment with⏎national initiatives (e.g., Cancer Moonshot, Opioid crisis, rural health). For example, research on AI-assisted⏎tele-oncology for rural cancer patients hits those notes.⏎Conclusion of Question 10:⏎In conclusion, HHS should focus research on bridging the “last mile” of AI adoption: implementation science,⏎evaluation methods, bias mitigation, interoperability, and strategic high-need clinical domains. Published⏎evidence so far is promising but not yet comprehensive; it shows AI can improve outcomes and efficiency in⏎specific instances[34][36], but we need broader validation and economic analysis. Literature on cost-benefit⏎suggests significant long-term savings and better care, but also points out that benefits may accrue to⏎different parties than the costs – implying a need for HHS to align incentives (through payment models or⏎policy) so that those investing in AI also share in the gains. By prioritizing research that produces robust⏎evidence and frameworks, HHS will build the foundation of trust and knowledge necessary for widespread AI⏎adoption in clinical care.⏎Conclusion and Alignment with HHS’s Genesis Project Vision⏎In closing, Massive Bio strongly supports HHS’s bold initiative to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. We have addressed each of⏎the RFI’s questions with actionable insights, drawn from our on-the-ground experience as an AI implementation partner and⏎supported by emerging evidence in the field. Our comment underscores several cross-cutting themes:⏎• Bold Vision, Pragmatic Action: We echo President Trump’s visionary AI agenda and HHS’s transformative goals – AI in⏎healthcare can “propel the US health care system forward”[3] – and we pair that vision with concrete steps around⏎regulation, reimbursement, and research. By modernizing regulations, aligning payment incentives, and investing in⏎implementation and evidence, HHS can convert AI’s promise into everyday reality in clinics and hospitals across America.⏎• Patient-Centric Innovation: We place patients at the center. AI should not be innovation for its own sake, but a tool to⏎enhance patient and caregiver experiences, improve outcomes, and reduce costs – in short, to Make America Healthy⏎Again through smarter care[1]. Whether it’s a cancer patient finding a lifesaving trial via Massive Bio’s AI, or a rural⏎senior avoiding hospitalization thanks to predictive analytics, these are the human stories AI should create at scale. Our⏎recommendations around interoperability, equity, and trust all serve to ensure AI works with patients, not on them.⏎• Massive Bio as a National Partner: We have highlighted how Massive Bio’s work – in partnership with ACS, recognition⏎by Cancer Moonshot, and technology like Reticulum Nexus – exemplifies the operationalization of AI at national scale.⏎We break down silos by connecting community clinics, patients, and research sponsors in one intelligent network[44].⏎We generate real-world evidence and improve trial access for tens of thousands of patients[11][12]. This aligns perfectly⏎with the spirit of the HHS Genesis Project, which calls for a historic effort to harness AI for national challenges[31]. Just⏎as DOE’s Genesis Mission is integrating data and supercomputing to accelerate science[54][55], HHS’s Genesis Project in⏎health should integrate clinical data, AI tools, and delivery systems to accelerate better health outcomes. Massive Bio⏎stands ready as an implementation arm for HHS in the cancer domain (and beyond), bringing our multi-stakeholder⏎ecosystem and proven AI platform to help realize this ambitious mission.⏎• Regulation, Reimbursement, R&D Levers – a Coordinated Push: Our answers stressed that these three levers must work⏎in concert. For example, regulatory clarity (what is allowed and safe) combined with reimbursement incentives (making⏎it financially sensible) and R&D support (building the evidence and methods) will together create an environment where⏎AI flourishes responsibly. We provided detailed suggestions in each area – from updating FDA and HIPAA⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 25⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎frameworks[19][17], to introducing new CMS payment models and quality incentives[20], to funding research in⏎implementation and bias mitigation[17]. If HHS pulls all these levers, the effect will be multiplicative, not just additive.⏎• Interoperability and Standards as Enablers: A recurring enabler in our comment is interoperability. Data must flow⏎safely and efficiently for AI to reach its potential[18]. We advocated for HHS to double down on standards like⏎FHIR/mCODE[10], open datasets, and exchange frameworks. These are the rails upon which the AI engine runs; HHS’s⏎ongoing work through ONC and associated initiatives is critical and should be accelerated.⏎• Evidence and Trust: We emphasize creating a virtuous cycle of evidence → trust → adoption. We urged HHS to prioritize⏎research that produces rigorous evidence of AI’s impact (clinical and economic)[51][34] and to develop certification and⏎evaluation processes that assure providers and patients of AI tools’ safety and efficacy. When frontline clinicians see⏎proof that an AI has been validated and improves care, and when patients see that it’s been thoughtfully implemented⏎with safeguards, they will trust and adopt these tools. HHS can facilitate this by being both a catalyst for generating⏎evidence (through grants, demos) and a communicator of that evidence (through guidelines, learning collaboratives).⏎• Spirit of Public-Private Partnership: Achieving national-scale AI transformation in healthcare is a team effort. HHS is⏎wisely seeking broad input – and must continue to engage industry, providers, patients, and academia in crafting⏎solutions. Our comment highlights how Massive Bio, a private sector innovator, can partner with public initiatives (like⏎CancerX, Moonshot, ACS) to deliver results. We believe HHS should continue fostering such partnerships, whether⏎through formal cooperative agreements, innovation challenges, or consortiums. By uniting the entrepreneurial energy of⏎companies with the convening power and oversight of government, we can move faster and smarter.⏎Massive Bio thanks HHS for the opportunity to contribute to this important dialogue. We are excited by the Administration’s⏎commitment to making the United States the world leader in healthcare AI – a goal that is within reach if we act boldly and⏎collaboratively. We are particularly enthusiastic about the focus on high-cost, high-stakes areas like cancer, where AI and data can⏎drastically change outcomes and costs, as we have begun to demonstrate.⏎In the spirit of implementation and Genesis: we urge HHS not only to plan and strategize, but to launch demonstration projects⏎and scale-up efforts – essentially, to ignite the AI engines in real healthcare settings across the country. Massive Bio is⏎prepared to be on the front lines of this implementation wave. Whether it’s expanding our AI-powered trial access network in⏎partnership with federal programs, contributing data and insights to national research efforts, or helping to develop⏎standards and training curricula, we are eager to help operationalize the RFI’s outcomes.⏎By focusing on high-impact use cases (such as those Massive Bio has encountered in trial matching, patient journey analytics, and⏎multi-modal decision support), and by adhering to the best practices outlined above, HHS can create a benchmarking ecosystem⏎that accelerates innovation while maintaining rigorous standards of transparency, equity, and reproducibility. Such benchmarks⏎will build trust in AI solutions – among clinicians, patients, and regulators – and ultimately help ensure that artificial intelligence⏎delivers reliable, safe, and effective tools in the fight against cancer. We appreciate the opportunity to contribute our⏎perspectives and are eager to collaborate in the community-wide effort that this RFI will catalyze.⏎With the recommendations outlined – clear policies, aligned incentives, robust research, and an unwavering focus on patient⏎centered outcomes – HHS can lead a transformation as profound as any in modern medical history. Together, we can ensure⏎that AI becomes a trusted, ubiquitous part of clinical care – improving quality, equity, and value in the health system. This is⏎a moment of opportunity; by seizing it, HHS will not only save lives and dollars but also fulfill the promise of American⏎innovation and compassion in healthcare.⏎Massive Bio is honored to lend our voice and stands ready to collaborate on next steps. Let us move forward decisively into this AI-⏎enabled future of health, turning the visionary Genesis Project into a concrete Genesis of better care for all Americans.⏎We thank you for considering our input and for your commitment to harnessing AI for the public good.⏎Sincerely,⏎Dr. Arturo Loaiza-Bonilla, MD, MSEd⏎Co-Founder & Chief Medical Officer, Massive Bio, Inc.⏎Chief of Hematology and Oncology, St. Luke’s University Health Network⏎Submitted on behalf of: Massive Bio, Inc.⏎Point of Contact: Dr. Arturo Loaiza-Bonilla – Email: arturolb@massivebio.com | Phone: +1 (443) 799-7346⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 26⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information⏎⏎(We affirm that this document is approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary⏎information.)⏎Massive Bio, Inc., 2026 27⏎Approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or business-proprietary information♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0069♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Inventurus Knowledge Solutions, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Inventurus Knowledge Solutions, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Christi J. Braun, Esq.⏎christi.braun@ikshealth.com⏎February 23, 2026⏎Via Federal eRulemaking Portal:⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attn: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector Artificial Intelligence Request for Information⏎Dear Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy,⏎On December 4, 2025, the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) issued the HHS⏎Artificial Intelligence (AI) Strategy in response to OMB Memorandum 25-21. As the Department seeks⏎public feedback to establish a forward-leaning, industry-supportive approach to AI in clinical care,⏎Inventurus Knowledge Solutions, Inc. (d/b/a IKS Health) is pleased to provide our experience-based⏎feedback.⏎IKS Health is a healthcare solutions partner of health systems and physician organizations with⏎nearly two decades of experience supporting clinical, operational, and revenue cycle performance across⏎the care continuum. We improve revenue performance, enhance patient access and engagement, relieve⏎administrative burden for care teams, and advance sustainable care delivery under both fee-for-service and⏎value-based payment models. Our leadership team includes former health system executives and practicing⏎clinicians with substantial experience in healthcare operations, clinical workflow design, and health data⏎governance. This operational perspective informs how we develop, evaluate, and deploy Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) within complex delivery environments, integrating advanced automation with structured⏎human oversight to promote safety, accountability, and demonstrable impact. Our ultimate goal, which AI⏎makes possible, is to eliminate the “chores” that increasingly burden medical professionals, allowing them⏎to focus on their core mission: care delivery.⏎In response to HHS’s Request for Information regarding AI and the American healthcare system,⏎we submit the following feedback to inform HHS’s strategy across regulation, reimbursement and research⏎and development. Our recommendations center on dismantling data silos, modernizing regulatory⏎frameworks, and aligning payment policies to ensure that AI serves as a tool for clinical excellence.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎While issues of ethics and liability are frequently debated, the single greatest barrier to the adoption⏎and scaling of AI is the persistent lack of access to clinical data stored in electronic health records (EHRs),⏎practice management systems, remote patient monitoring, and population health technologies. AI presents⏎a unique opportunity to fundamentally re-design the EHR experience itself, shifting away from⏎8951 Cypress Waters Blvd, Suite 100, Coppell, TX 7501 9⏎⏎transactional billing and siloed documentation systems that prioritize physician time for documentation⏎over patient care. By integrating AI into platforms that orchestrate relationships and automate non-core⏎tasks, we can allow physicians to return to their primary role, but this evolution is entirely dependent on⏎AI’s ability to read from and write to the EHR without vendor-imposed friction.⏎For third-party applications to communicate with an EHR, the vendor must provide a functional⏎application programming interface (API), which acts as a secure “doorway” between the EHR and the⏎applications. Without a functional API, the EHR acts as a “walled garden,” trapping data inside and⏎requiring manual, “click-heavy” human intervention. For an AI tool to effectively support a physician’s⏎workflow, a seamless API connection is required for reading from and writing to the EHR.⏎IKS Health’s ambient AI scribe solutions—Scribble Now, Scribble Swift, and Scribble Pro—⏎illustrate this technical necessity. Through an API, the Scribble app can instantly query the patient’s medical⏎history, current medications, and previous diagnoses. For ambient scribing, reading the longitudinal record⏎ensures the AI understands the difference between a new acute symptom and a long-standing chronic⏎condition, significantly reducing the risk of “hallucinations.” Once the Scribble app generates a clinical⏎note, it must have the ability to write back to the EHR, or the clinician will be forced to copy and paste text,⏎which maintains, rather than eliminates, the documentation chore. A robust, standardized API allows the⏎Scribble app to write the clinical notes—in real time—directly into the correct EHR fields for the clinician⏎to review and finalize.⏎The challenge is not just the existence of APIs, but their variability. Many EHR vendors utilize⏎proprietary, rather than standardized, APIs that force app developers to build unique connections for every⏎hospital system and physician organization, a process that is cost-prohibitive for smaller innovators. The⏎Health Level Seven (HL7®) Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR®) standard was intended to⏎be the universal language of health data, allowing vendors to securely share information through standard⏎HTTP operations and enabling health care providers and systems to get a more complete view of their⏎patients and business processes. FHIR provides the necessary structure that allows AI to navigate through⏎vast datasets consistently and efficiently. By utilizing the same “RESTful” architecture that enables global⏎entities like Google and Amazon to exchange data over the internet, FHIR brings healthcare into the modern⏎digital era.⏎Despite the progress intended by the 21st Century Cures Act and the Cures Act Final Rule, which⏎included the adoption a suite of FHIR-based requirements in the Health IT Certification Program and⏎mandated that certified EHRs support FHIR APIs by the end of 2022, we are currently seeing a period of⏎lax compliance regarding the Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT (ONC) requirements. Many⏎EHR vendors have implemented FHIR inconsistently or in ways that maintain proprietary silos. By⏎enforcing a standardized FHIR API, the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)/ONC can⏎ensure that an AI developer can build a tool once and deploy it across the entire healthcare ecosystem. This⏎“plug-and-play” capability is the only way to scale AI tools that can meaningfully reduce clinician burnout⏎and lower costs across the American healthcare system.⏎In the Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability: ASTP/ONC Deregulatory Actions to Unleash⏎Prosperity (HTI-5) Proposed Rule, ASTP/ONC recognizes that “[t]he transition to a FHIR-based API⏎ecosystem is a strategic imperative”. While the proposed rule removes non-FHIR-based certification⏎criteria, it takes no steps to require support for more updated criteria. ASTP/ONC must mandate and enforce⏎specific technical standards that ensure data is not only available but “AI-ready.” Specifically, ASTP/ONC⏎should require full adherence to the HL7® FHIR® Bulk Data Access (Flat FHIR) specification (v1.0.1 or⏎higher) and HL7®’s FHIR US Core Implementation Guide (v5.0.1 or higher). These standards enable the⏎standardized export of large-scale population datasets using NDJSON (Newline Delimited JSON), which⏎2⏎⏎is essential for training robust AI models. Without this “bulk” capability, AI is fed data one patient at a⏎time, which is insufficient for identifying systemic care gaps or performing population-level analytics.⏎We support ASTP/ONC’s proposed revisions in HTI-5 to Part 171 to clarify that interfering with⏎AI accessing EHI is information blocking. In particular, we agree that modifying the definitions of “access,”⏎“exchange,” and “use” under 45 CFR §171.102 is necessary and appropriate and support retiring the “third⏎party seeking modification use” condition of the Infeasibility Exception (45 CFR §171.204(a)(3)). We also⏎agree with the removal of the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement™ (TEFCA™)⏎Manner Exception (45 CFR §171.403).⏎We note, however, that HHS cannot stop at rule making. It needs to prosecute information blocking⏎and levy meaningful fines.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎To effectively incentivize AI adoption, HHS must address the misaligned regulatory and payment⏎frameworks that currently penalize innovation. Our existing systems—built for a manual, encounter-based⏎era—frequently obstruct the data-rich, longitudinal nature of AI-driven medicine.⏎A primary bottleneck is the “minimum necessary” standard (45 CFR § 164.502(b)) within the⏎HIPAA Privacy Rule, which is often interpreted to require significant restrictions on data use and⏎access. Clarifying this standard is essential because modern clinical AI models require massive, high-⏎fidelity datasets to ensure clinical safety and statistical reliability. HHS could issue guidance specifying⏎that, for the purposes of training and validating clinical AI, a comprehensive longitudinal view of the patient⏎is the baseline requirement. We recommend that HHS issue guidance that “bulk” access is the actual⏎“minimum necessary” to achieve a safe and effective AI tool or revise 45 CFR § 164.506 to clarify that⏎“health care operations” includes AI validation and includes the right to access comprehensive, longitudinal⏎datasets.⏎Furthermore, the requirement for specific patient consent for uses beyond treatment, payment, or⏎operations (45 CFR § 164.508) creates friction for vital “secondary” uses like AI safety and bias monitoring.⏎HHS could establish a new “AI-Ready De-identification Standard” refining the existing safe harbor⏎methods under 45 CFR § 164.514 to allow for the use of “Limited Data Sets” that retain critical temporal⏎and geographic information that are currently restricted but vital for AI to track disease progression.⏎Scaling AI also requires moving away from manual data extraction. As noted above, ASTP/ONC⏎should mandate HL7® FHIR® Bulk Data Access (v1.0.1+) to facilitate the export of population-level⏎datasets in NDJSON format. This should be supported by the US Core Implementation Guide (v5.0.1+),⏎ensuring an AI model built for one health system works in another without reconfiguration. Additionally,⏎the SMART on FHIR Framework must be the standard for “plug-and-play” AI apps, allowing tools like⏎ambient listening to integrate directly into the physician workflow without vendor-specific barriers.⏎From a reimbursement standpoint, we must avoid the “Telehealth Trap,” where productivity gains⏎from technology are used as a justification for immediate fee schedule cuts. When telehealth scaled,⏎providers faced automatic reimbursement decreases under the assumption that cost-savings should be⏎passed directly to the payers. HHS should prioritize a “Value-Preservation” payment model. If an AI tool⏎reduces documentation time, CMS should not respond by simply lowering the Work Relative Value Units⏎3⏎⏎(RVUs) for those services. We need a commitment that productivity gains realized through AI will be⏎shared with the providers who invest in these technologies.⏎The most profound shift required is a move away from our current per-encounter payment model⏎toward population-based and outcome-based payments, such as via 42 CFR Part 414, Subpart O (the⏎Quality Payment Program). As long as the financial “engine” of a hospital is built on the volume of visits,⏎AI tools that prevent visits will be seen as a financial liability. We recommend 42 CFR §414.1375 (Quality⏎Category) be updated to explicitly permit and reward “digital quality measures” (dQMs) that pull directly⏎from AI-filtered data, reducing the manual “chart-chase” for staff and physicians. By aligning technical⏎standards with a refreshed MIPS incentive structure, we can ensure that AI restores the clinician-patient⏎relationship and shifts the focus toward lasting health outcomes.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎While the question specifically focuses on “non-medical devices,” we want to acknowledge that a⏎significant implementation barrier and administrative hurdle that stalls deployment of AI remains the “gray⏎zone” of what constitutes “Software as a Medical Device” (SaMD) subject to FDA regulation under 21⏎CFR § 860. While the Cures Act excluded some clinical decision support software from the definition of a⏎medical device, current ambiguity makes it unclear if a novel AI application, particularly one involving⏎predictive analytics or providing a differential diagnosis based on pattern recognition, is considered SaMD.⏎This “gray zone” creates a culture of risk aversion, as health systems fear the legal and compliance⏎ramifications of deploying unregulated software that may later be categorized as a medical device. HHS⏎should clarify whether, if software output “drives” clinical action and the logic remains “opaque”—⏎meaning the physician cannot fully verify the underlying reasoning—it should be classified as SaMD.⏎Even when a tool requires regulatory oversight, premarket review cycles are incompatible with the⏎rapid iterative nature of AI software. A model that is clinically sound today may be based on data that⏎becomes obsolete during a twelve-month review period. Without a modernized, fast-track “process⏎validation” pathway at the FDA, regulatory bottlenecks will continue to stifle innovative care.⏎In addressing the governance and accountability of non-medical device AI, HHS should adopt a⏎policy of “Augmented Intelligence,” where the physician remains the “final click” and ultimate authority.⏎However, to prevent “automation bias,” the government must provide specific guardrails that ensure⏎clinicians do not over-rely on AI outputs.⏎If we are to have widespread adoption of AI, accountability cannot rest solely on the clinician. If a⏎clinician follows the standard of care but is misled by an AI “hallucination,” the liability should shift. We⏎need a dual-accountability framework where physicians are responsible for clinical application, while AI⏎developers are responsible for structural failures, such as data-drift or hallucinations that a reasonable⏎clinician could not have detected. To codify this, HHS should update 21 CFR §820 (Quality System⏎Regulation) to include “AI hallucination monitoring” and “bias testing” as mandatory design controls. HHS⏎should also clarify 42 CFR § 482 (Conditions of Participation) to require that hospitals establish a “Clinical⏎AI Governance Committee.”⏎4⏎⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎HHS must move beyond static validation and adopt a “Human-in-the-Loop” (HITL) philosophy.⏎This approach prioritizes real-world performance over laboratory accuracy. By treating AI evaluation as a⏎continuous clinical audit, we can implement a lifecycle approach where pre-deployment adversarial testing⏎identifies “hallucination” risks and post-deployment automated discrepancy checks flag outputs that⏎diverge from human consensus. Shifting focus to human-centered metrics, such as “Intervention Accuracy,”⏎allows us to measure how often an expert agrees with the AI.⏎HHS should leverage the authorities granted by the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (Public Law 119-⏎21) to catalyze this innovation. Specifically, the Rural Health Transformation (RHT) Program provides a⏎prime opportunity to fund “AI-Human Teams” that help rural generalists practice at the top of their license.⏎These cooperative agreements can be supplemented by prize competitions for clinical reasoning, modeled⏎after the successful ARPA-H PARADIGM program, to incentivize “rugged” AI tools capable of⏎maintaining accuracy in resource-constrained environments. Additionally, utilizing the framework of the⏎AI in Medical Education Act, HRSA can provide educational grants to train the next generation of⏎physicians to oversee these tools, specifically to close care disparities in minority communities.⏎The most impactful move HHS can make is a CMMI Demonstration Project that shifts to outcome-⏎aligned payments based on a patient’s current health status and longitudinal outcomes. Rewarding the⏎prevention of acute events through AI’s predictive power aligns the financial success of a hospital or⏎physician with the actual health of the community. This strategy directly supports Pillars 3 and 5 of the⏎HHS AI Strategy (December 2025).⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎To foster a robust ecosystem for clinical AI, HHS must move toward a policy of active enablement.⏎As noted above, the ONC Health IT Certification Program (45 CFR Part 170) should prioritize data⏎accessibility as a core requirement for certification.⏎To bridge the gap between private innovation and public safety, HHS could establish a Federally⏎Qualified National Patient Database. By leveraging HL7® FHIR® standards, this repository could provide⏎de-identified, population-level datasets that preserve clinical integrity while protecting individual privacy.⏎Access to this database should be offered to developers and researchers through a subsidized, affordable⏎fee structure, ensuring that American ingenuity is not sidelined by the prohibitive costs of data acquisition⏎currently controlled by a few dominant firms.⏎As the market for clinical AI grows, both providers and patients need a reliable way to verify the⏎credibility of new tools. Rather than a rigid federal mandate, HHS should facilitate a Public-Private⏎Partnership (PPP) to create a voluntary certification program for AI application developers. This model of⏎co-regulation would allow industry leaders to participate in setting performance and safety standards,⏎incorporating globally recognized frameworks, like ISO/IEC 42001 for AI Management Systems. Such a⏎certification would serve as a “seal of trust,” signaling that a vendor adheres to rigorous self-regulatory⏎standards and ethical design principles.⏎Finally, we must establish a clear legal baseline for the digital age: EHR companies are stewards⏎of data, not owners. HHS should issue definitive guidance or update 45 CFR §164 to clarify that patients⏎5⏎⏎own their data. Under this principle, EHR vendors must be prohibited from claiming intellectual property⏎rights over any AI product derived from patient data processed within their software. We need to ensure⏎that medical progress remains a shared human benefit.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations⏎and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎We are still in the nascent stages of AI deployment, so evidence of exceptional performance is⏎currently limited to specific, controlled-use cases rather than systemic healthcare transformation. Despite⏎this early stage, AI shows significant promise in disease prevention, closing persistent gaps in care, and⏎reducing hospital readmissions. Perhaps most immediately, AI is effectively reducing "pajama time"—the⏎time physicians spend after-hours on documentation—by automating clinical scribing and streamlining data⏎retrieval for quality reporting.⏎We have found that, where well-governed data is highly available with full clinical context, AI is⏎remarkably cost-effective to develop and implement. Conversely, AI has consistently underperformed⏎when developed as a “proof-of-concept” on limited or siloed data, as performance often collapses upon⏎introduction to a live clinical workflow.⏎The greatest potential for novel AI lies in its ability to act as a "great equalizer" for care delivery.⏎AI could function like a clinical “Siri” or “Alexa” to triage patients to the appropriate level of care,⏎significantly reducing the burden on overcrowded emergency departments. Additionally, AI tools with⏎access to longitudinal records for large populations can provide treatment insights that are currently⏎impossible for humans to synthesize. This is vital for rare diseases, where AI can identify "patients like me"⏎and guide physicians toward specific tests and treatments, reducing both the time to diagnosis and the cost⏎of care.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the⏎most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles⏎to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎In addressing the governance of AI within healthcare organizations, it is critical to distinguish⏎between those who champion the technology and those who authorize its procurement. Within most health⏎systems, physician-led committees—often structured as Clinical Technology Councils or AI Oversight⏎Boards—exert the most significant influence on adoption. These clinicians are the primary arbiters of a⏎tool's clinical validity, safety, and workflow compatibility. While hospital executives and Chief Information⏎Officers typically hold the ultimate “purchasing” authority, they rarely move forward without a formal⏎endorsement from these physician leaders, who ensure the technology serves as augmented intelligence,⏎rather than an administrative burden.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate⏎the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎As explained in the answer to Question 1, interoperability, enabled through implementation of⏎HL7® FHIR®, is the indispensable bridge that allows AI to move beyond theoretical analysis into⏎meaningful, frontline care. For AI to truly transform the clinical landscape, it must have the capability to⏎read directly from the EHR, capture the patient’s current state in real-time, and execute the "chores," such⏎as writing care notes, supporting the creation of complex plans of care, and managing prescription refills.⏎6⏎⏎By automating these administrative burdens, we allow physicians to return their focus to the patient,⏎restoring the human element of medicine.⏎Furthermore, to fuel research and large-scale model development, HL7® FHIR® Bulk Data Access⏎(v1.0.1+) must be made universally available. Accessing data patient-by-patient is insufficient for the⏎training and benchmarking of sophisticated AI; we require the ability to export population-level datasets to⏎identify trends and validate clinical interventions at scale.⏎Concluding Recommendations⏎To facilitate innovation in AI, ASTP/ONC must enforce a standardized FHIR API and mandate⏎and enforce specific technical standards like HL7® FHIR® Bulk Data Access (v1.0.1 or higher) and the⏎FHIR® US Core Implementation Guide (v5.0.1 or higher). Further, HHS must issue guidance on the⏎“minimum necessary” standard and ensure access to bulk data is permissible under HIPAA. By aligning⏎technical standards with modernized payment and regulatory structures, HHS can ensure AI acts as a⏎catalyst for clinician wellness and superior patient care.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to respond to this Request for Information and stand ready to answer⏎any questions. Given the complexities of integrating AI with healthcare workflows, IKS Health welcomes⏎the opportunity to provide your staff with a technical briefing. We are also happy to facilitate a follow-up⏎conversation with your policy team to discuss how a dual-accountability framework can be implemented⏎to protect both patients and clinicians.⏎Sincerely,⏎CHRISTI J. BRAUN⏎Chief Legal & Compliance Officer⏎7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0070♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Workgroup for Electronic Data Interchange (WEDI), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Workgroup for Electronic Data Interchange (WEDI)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street, SW, 7th Floor⏎Washington, DC 20024⏎Re: RIN 0955–AA13⏎Submitted electronically via http://www.regulations.gov⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The Workgroup for Electronic Data Interchange (WEDI) writes today in response to the⏎“Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care” published in the December 23, 2025, edition of the Federal Register.⏎WEDI was formed in 1991 by then Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Secretary Dr. Louis Sullivan to identify opportunities to improve the efficiency of health data⏎exchange. Named in the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)⏎legislation as an advisor to the Secretary of HHS, WEDI is the leading multi-stakeholder⏎authority on the use of health information technology (IT) to efficiently improve health⏎information exchange, enhance care quality, and reduce costs. With a focus on advancing⏎standards for electronic administrative transactions, and promoting data privacy and security,⏎WEDI is recognized and trusted as a formal advisor to the Secretary. Our diverse⏎membership includes health plans, providers, standards development organizations, vendors,⏎federal and state government agencies, and patient advocacy organizations.⏎WEDI supports the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)/Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) in its ongoing work in advancing⏎interoperability of health information. We share the goals of leveraging current technology to⏎advanced capabilities and functions to decrease burden and streamline processes to improve⏎the quality of care while minimizing administrative costs and protecting patient information.⏎We believe that artificial intelligence (AI) technology has the potential to transform the health⏎care sector in the coming years by augmenting the care delivery process, improving data⏎exchange, and reducing administrative burden for all health care stakeholders.⏎To aid us in developing our response to this request for information (RFI), WEDI conducted a⏎Member Position Advisory (MPA) event on February 12, 2026. Through surveys, interviews,⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 2⏎and live events, the MPA process is designed to solicit WEDI member input on topical issues,⏎public and private sector proposals, and government regulations. Individuals representing⏎health plans, providers, standards development organizations, clearinghouses, electronic⏎health record (EHR) vendors, consultants, and other health IT vendors participated in the⏎session.⏎Introductory Comments⏎WEDI’s mission and work are driven by easing administrative burden, putting patients at the⏎center of their care, implementing consensus based, mature standards that support automation,⏎and maintaining appropriate safeguards for privacy, security, and confidentiality. We applaud⏎this effort to solicit industry input on the potential to advance the adoption and use of AI as part⏎of clinical care.⏎WEDI’s comments are based on key guiding principles that are integral for consideration to⏎advance health IT, data exchange standards, and innovative technologies. As ASTP/ONC⏎explores opportunities to improve the technological capabilities used in the health care⏎environment, it is important that the system:⏎• Ensures the health information needs of the patient and their caregivers are at the center⏎of the ecosystem.⏎• Promotes seamless, automated data exchange through mature, clear, and unambiguous⏎standards that have been thoroughly evaluated and demonstrate meaningful return on⏎investment.⏎• Integrates data exchange efficiently within the health plan, provider, and other end-⏎users’ workflows.⏎• Maximizes the most current data privacy and security safeguards for protecting patients’⏎health information.⏎General Comments on the RFI⏎In WEDI’s discussions of the RFI questions, several overarching themes emerged that we wish⏎to highlight as follows:⏎• Establishing Trust⏎Gaining providers’ trust with new technology is not a new barrier for adoption. With AI,⏎there is an overall understanding of the promise it brings to health care and what it can⏎do to relieve burden on providers. There is a subset of providers that have a trust and⏎sense of the utility of these tools. There are, however, many providers that still need to⏎both understand and trust it. As it stands, we are not seeing the tipping point of⏎providers’ excitement level for AI tools outweighing their level of trust in them. Providers⏎are showing they are moving at the speed of trust and not at the speed of investment⏎and innovation. This reflects a practical adoption model grounded in clinical risk,⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 3⏎workflow realities, and patient safety. The maturation and adoption of AI technology can⏎be compared to the adoption of EHRs, which has taken years and there is still a small⏎number of providers that have not converted.⏎• Ensuring Transparency⏎Transparency of AI technology is essential for users. Providers need a clear⏎understanding of the functionality of the AI tools within their care delivery and any⏎impacts they could have on patient care. Testing results, certification, source of data,⏎integrity of the data set, and various other factors will be critical for addressing providers’⏎liability, usability, and validity concerns. Effective transparency, while respecting⏎proprietary models, will lead to an increase in the rate of adoption of AI tools.⏎• Maintaining Data Privacy and Security⏎AI poses significant risks to privacy and security. The need for large data sets is⏎necessary to train AI algorithms. Creating those data sets raises privacy, security, and⏎cybersecurity concerns. AI developers are generally not HIPAA covered entities.⏎Covered entities are under regulatory obligations to protect and secure protected health⏎information (PHI). The sharing of PHI with developers would in most cases require a⏎business associate agreement or new regulation to establish requirements for their use⏎of patient data. Third parties that collect, hold, or transmit PHI should be held to the⏎same standards and accountability as covered entities and business associates. In⏎doing this, ASTP/ONC would close the current loophole for privacy and security related⏎to third parties’ use of patient data.⏎• Defining the Role of Regulation vs. Innovation⏎We appreciate the administration's work to bring AI tools to the appropriate areas of⏎health care and leveraging its adoption and use in a standardized manner through⏎regulation. We assert, however, that a balance must be struck in setting AI regulations.⏎An overly restrictive regulatory approach could have the unintended consequences of⏎increasing costs and stifling innovation and competition. Conversely, too lax of an⏎approach could raise concerns with data privacy, security, and safety of products. A⏎policy framework is needed that strikes the balance of flexibility to enable that⏎innovation, while still ensuring trust, transparency, protection of patient data, and patient⏎safety.⏎• Enhancing Education⏎Examples of the use of AI in health care operations are everywhere, including⏎misinformation, which leads to fear of its adoption and use. Clear education aimed at⏎end users of AI, including patients and providers, and understanding across the industry⏎of the capabilities of AI is needed.⏎RFI Questions and WEDI Responses⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 4⏎Barriers to the adoption and use of health IT in clinical care are not new concerns for providers.⏎Trust by providers in the adoption of new technology is a general, ongoing issue. Providers want⏎a certain level of assurance that the new technology will perform as marketed and provide the⏎expected value to the clinical or administrative workflow and processes. With AI, the level of⏎trust has a greater impact on providers plans for incorporating products into their clinical routine,⏎as trust in AI tools is linked to providers’ concerns with liability. Appropriate regulations with⏎meaningful guardrails can increase the trust needed for continued adoption of AI tools by⏎providers.⏎The role of AI in the delivery of patient care varies widely from administrative functions⏎(scheduling and ambient scribes during patient encounters) to clinical care (interpreting⏎radiologic images and chatbots). The amount of liability that a provider may take on while using⏎AI tools is a risk and in many cases the level of risk is unknown. Providers have concerns⏎regarding what happens when AI does not perform as expected, since they will ultimately be⏎liable for the patient’s outcome. As such, AI should be deployed only as a tool and not without⏎inclusion of a human in the loop. We anticipate that specific legal cases will need to work their⏎way through the courts before a clearer understanding of the ramifications will emerge. It is⏎possible that clinicians will limit their use of AI on this point alone.⏎Providers continue to have concerns with transparency as it relates to AI tools and addressing⏎this will be critical as the industry moves forward with not just AI but other new technology.⏎While providers do not need to know technical specifications of the AI tools, they need a basic⏎understanding of the tool’s functionality and data sources to know how the tool is supposed to⏎work in order to identify any failures or general concerns.⏎Data privacy and security issues are another barrier to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care. At issue is the current patchwork of state privacy laws, which if in conflict, preempt the⏎HIPAA Privacy rule. The myriad of overlapping state and HIPAA legal requirements raise⏎compliance costs and divert resources while also stifling innovation when PHI is involved.⏎The current fee-for-service reimbursement model serves as another barrier to the adoption and⏎use of AI in clinical care. Additional evaluation is needed on how reimbursement models align⏎with the use of AI and what payment reform could do to advance the adoption of AI use.⏎Further, existing technology implementations may have built-in restrictions that restrict or make⏎the integration of AI tools difficult. This is another area that ASTP/ONC should further explore.⏎Organizations are heavily invested in various projects stemming from regulations to business⏎development. The value of prioritizing the adoption of AI needs to be established and how to⏎align it with the existing interoperability needs. To overcome these barriers, multiple actions are⏎needed at federal, state, and organization levels. Addressing these concerns will assist in⏎overcoming the current barriers and start to bring about an uptake of AI use.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 5⏎With respect to regulatory changes, an overall review of the rulemaking process itself is needed⏎within the context of AI technology. Because AI technology is progressing so rapidly, the current⏎regulatory process is neither designed nor suitable for quickly establishing the requirements and⏎guardrails for its use. ASTP/ONC should explore the use of the sub-regulatory, technical⏎assistance, and guidance to address emerging policy gaps. These alternatives could bring⏎flexibility to the rule-making process while still being authoritative. ASTP/ONC should also⏎review existing regulations and the impacts they have on advancing the adoption and use of AI⏎in clinical care, such as the HIPAA Privacy Rule and upcoming cybersecurity rule.⏎At the state government level, there is a growing patchwork of state rules that are being⏎proposed for the use of AI and privacy. The federal government should address these state⏎laws as they are enacted by publishing guidance on how the new law reconciles with federal⏎requirements. This action will show more directly how the federal rules interact with the state⏎rules, instead of the entities operating in those states having to make their own interpretations⏎that may or may not be completely accurate. As an example, this approach was used with the⏎No Surprises Act, where HHS provided guidance on the states’ authority vs. federal oversight⏎and where the two met.⏎The use of sub-regulatory guidance has the flexibility to be updated and adapt more quickly to⏎keep pace with the changing AI technology. Regulations related to AI can be more all-⏎encompassing and reference the guidance provisions. Within the regulations, the requirements⏎should be framed using the five principles of accuracy, transparency, accountability, privacy,⏎and ethics. Each proposed and final rule should identify the impact of its requirements on these⏎principles, providing an analysis of how the rule should be viewed.⏎Regarding programmatic design changes that HHS should prioritize, there needs to be a focus⏎on the impact of AI on rural and underserved areas. There are infrastructure limitations that⏎inhibit the ability in these areas to fully leverage AI and other advanced technologies,⏎specifically due to the limitations of broadband access, reliable Wi-Fi, and use of smartphones.⏎Access to education to support digital literacy and train the workforce on new technologies must⏎also be prioritized. Without these infrastructure builds, the adoption and use of AI will remain⏎limited in these areas. We strongly encourage ASTP/ONC to use its authority to prioritize⏎infrastructure and education to facilitate the adoption and use of AI.⏎For payment policy, experience has shown that incorporating reimbursement into payment⏎policy for the use of technology in clinical care supports its adoption and use. An example is⏎Medicare’s payment for certain capital related costs on the Medicare cost report. The payment⏎policy approach could spur investment and innovation by developers and adoption and use by⏎providers.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 6⏎We have interpreted “non-medical devices” to mean devices used outside of clinical settings⏎that can collect, store, and transmit data that support the patient’s clinical treatment and⏎monitoring. These devices include health applications (apps), fitness trackers, health monitors,⏎ambient scribes, chatbots, prescription fillers, etc. We are also aware of the Food and Drug⏎Administration’s (FDA) work on regulating the use of non-medical devices and the Center for⏎Disease Control and Prevention’s (CDC) work on wearable devices and other technologies.⏎Our comments are framed on this interpretation of the term “non-medical devices” and the work⏎by the FDA and CDC. We also encourage ASTP/ONC to collaborate with the FDA and CDC in⏎developing definitions and case examples of non-medical devices, which will need to be⏎reviewed on a periodic basis as this technology is continually evolving. Clear distinctions⏎between medical and non-medical devices will also be necessary for establishing evaluation⏎methods of the two.⏎The use of non-medical devices, including those that incorporate AI, do have potential legal⏎implications, especially related to liability, privacy, and security. As mentioned, the potential of⏎liability is one of the biggest risks that could hold providers back from using AI tools. In general,⏎legislating or regulating how AI tools will impact liability is not possible, meaning that issues will⏎need to be addressed through the legal system. While the concern of liability may be outside the⏎scope of ASTP/ONC and HHS to regulate, guidance and education are critical for users of AI⏎technologies to understand the risks and combat any misinformation. Again, AI transparency is⏎a key facet of liability.⏎The need to establish large data sets to train AI models pose privacy, security, and⏎cybersecurity risks. Developers of AI models are typically not HIPAA covered entities, which⏎requires a separate agreement to transfer patient data to them for their use. Additional⏎considerations may be needed to address patient consent for this use of their data, as well as⏎any security and cybersecurity measures to protect this data. We encourage ASTP/ONC to⏎explore ideas for how it can provide oversight for the collection and use of patient data for the⏎purposes of AI training and model development. We urge ASTP/ONC to consider its role as a⏎convener and to coordinate with the Federal Trade Commission where AI technologies fall⏎outside HIPAA.⏎AI-enabled clinical care models will require an elevation of patient consent to give patients a full⏎understanding of where AI is being used, how it is being used, and their options for having it⏎involved in their care. If AI tools are contributing information to analysis or decision-making⏎aspects of patient care, a provider must be included in that workflow and evaluate the output of⏎the AI tool. The same is needed for the development of the AI model or algorithm.⏎AI needs to remain narrowed in its application in clinical care and not act in a way that limits⏎patient and provider engagement and involvement in care. It should always be in a secondary,⏎facilitator role with the provider remaining in the primary role for interacting clinically, finalizing⏎clinical decisions, and developing treatment plans with the patient. AI should always serve in the⏎role of augmenting clinical care and not be a substitute for clinical care. The benefits of AI are its⏎ability to augment and enhance the experiences of providers and patients with the focus on⏎delivering patient care. We encourage ASTP/ONC to develop clear definitions for what⏎constitutes augmented AI care, AI-enabled care, and AI supported technologies.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 7⏎Because of the role AI can and does play in many aspects of documentation and data⏎collection, such as patient demographics, medical history, medication lists, etc., we recommend⏎that ASTP/ONC consider developing certification criteria for AI-driven administrative and clinical⏎processes for the ONC EHR Certification Program to facilitate a standardized approach to the⏎use of AI tools. Having certification criteria will provide a level of assurance to users of the⏎technology that it meets specified standards.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support⏎these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g.,⏎contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Before releasing regulations or requirements on the use of AI, the biggest role ASTP/ONC can⏎play is being a centralized location for testing and evaluating the effectiveness, efficiency, and⏎value of the AI technologies in the delivery of care. We encourage ASTP/ONC to create a⏎testing environment with models and sandboxes where all stakeholders can interact with the⏎technology and identify the benefits and challenges of its use. This activity will increase trust in⏎the products and enthusiasm for their use.⏎Further understanding is needed of the current practices of using AI to both incorporate non-⏎medical data from patient fitness trackers, health apps, etc. into the clinical record and analyze⏎that data. Consideration also needs to be given to how data generated from multiple sources⏎can be incorporated into the EHR, along with a recognition that the EHR may no longer be the⏎complete system of record. Patients may have better data for their day-to-day condition and⏎treatment through health apps as compared to the episodic data that clinicians capture in their⏎EHRs. The need for exchange mechanisms to bring these data sources together should be⏎explored.⏎As we look at models to evaluate how well AI-enabled care does relative to its non-AI⏎counterpart, we need to think about tangible outcomes that can be easily measured to identify⏎the distinction. For example, measures such as reduced days in the hospital after a procedure,⏎reduced readmissions, reduced post-discharge visits, increased patient satisfaction, and⏎increased patient adherence to treatment could be deployed. The success of AI in managing⏎patient care could also involve the use of data generated from non-medical devices.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎There are various ways in which ASTP/ONC can support private sector activities to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care. One option is to add AI certification criteria to the⏎ONC Certification Program. It is our understanding that some providers find the current Decision⏎Support Intervention (DSI) certification requirement with the source attributes and the⏎intervention risk management practices valuable in making informed decisions about AI⏎purchases, implementation, and use. As such, we urge ASTP/ONC to reconsider its current⏎efforts to deregulate DSI certification criteria.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 8⏎Other opportunities include creating and maintaining testing environments and sandboxes for⏎industry use, supporting current accreditation or certification bodies to develop programs, and⏎developing standards for AI-generated data. Providing a test bed of synthetic data that can be⏎used to test models and understand their effectiveness would give users of the tools assurance⏎that they have met testing thresholds and certain criteria have been met. Authorized testing⏎environments would also allow innovators to quickly put their models through their paces and⏎demonstrate they are working correctly. These opportunities will further strengthen trust if⏎ASTP/ONC-initiated efforts are transparent and testing environment reports are made public.⏎If ASTP/ONC defers on incorporating AI into its Certification Program, then industry-driven⏎testing must be supported with close collaboration between government and the private sector.⏎Testing will be central to AI development, transparency of models, and adoption of its use, and⏎will ensure that models demonstrate they meet specified requirements. An appropriate balance⏎should be struck between government oversight and industry-driven initiatives to prevent stifling⏎innovation.⏎A framework for data standards for AI-generated data is needed so users of the data⏎understand the data’s fidelity, including where it came from, how it originated, any changes to it⏎during transfer, AI-generated labels, etc. This approach would be similar to the Patient Access⏎application program interface that supports knowing how the data flowed from different entities,⏎who maintained it, and where it originated. The provenance of patient data is critical, and users⏎of the data need to know whether the data were generated or synthetic, derived from other data,⏎and the role of an AI algorithm in its production.⏎With this in mind, we recommend that ASTP/ONC develop a governance framework and include⏎in it a safe harbor for those using AI tools that meet the specified framework pertaining to data⏎provenance, transparency, testing, ongoing monitoring, and other factors for data integrity. Best⏎practices, model language, model requirements, and warning labels for both the provider and⏎the patient should also be included in the resources for the framework. Overall, there needs to⏎be trust established in the AI tools and the framework or adoption and use could be impacted.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new⏎insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Some AI tools have fallen short by their overall approach to engaging with users. Limited⏎transparency of functionalities, lack of trust in data sources used to train models, and limited⏎education have left many users skeptical of their value. Developers could take an even more⏎active role in demonstrating the effectiveness of the tools, sharing details on how the product⏎works, and providing more robust implementation support. Providers need to have a clear⏎understanding of how the tools will support and enhance their delivery of care and⏎administrative staff need a better understanding of what their role is in working with AI-based⏎technology.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 9⏎A specific example of how a promising AI technology has fallen short for some providers is with⏎ambient scribes. Errors have occurred from the ambient scribe, and lawsuits have been filed⏎against the developers. Another perspective on ambient scribes from hospitals was positive, as⏎they have seen this technology reduce some of the burden on providers, while improving patient⏎satisfaction. This demonstrates how a “one-size-fits-all” approach does not work in health care⏎and approaches tailored specifically to the end users are needed to support adoption of new⏎technology.⏎An area where clinical AI tools have exceeded expectation include identifying risks to patients,⏎interpreting studies and reports, and supporting documentation. On the administrative side,⏎successes have been seen with patient scheduling, projections for capacity management, and⏎template management for providers, while AI tools for managing prior authorization have⏎demonstrated room for improvement.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care?⏎What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI tools vary across organizations, as does their⏎approach to addressing them. Known hurdles relate to purchase and implementation, including⏎identifying tool options, procurement, contracting, governance, and training. Any new AI tool,⏎championed by one or more groups within an organization, may need to be translated across⏎the organization, as the use of these tools may not be siloed within the organization. Other⏎hurdles include AI reliability, robustness of the tools, transparency of how the tools function,⏎evidence of the tools delivering positive results, clearly defined limitations on AI use, and safety⏎within patient care.⏎Some provider organizations have set up formal governing bodies to review and monitor AI use⏎cases, review potential products, and approve purchases. These administrative hurdles will⏎require time and resources. These hurdles could be especially challenging for small and rural⏎organizations, where staff may have dual responsibilities or they lack dedicated staff to⏎participate in these functions. As well, some organization’s policies may not permit AI tools to⏎access PHI. As an example, certain value-based care contracts may prohibit an AI tool from⏎reviewing PHI.⏎Small and rural organizations also face infrastructure challenges that potentially could benefit⏎from the deployment of AI technology. Appropriate infrastructure investment to support these⏎organizations is necessary to ensure they have access to these tools and the benefits they can⏎provide.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific⏎data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Interoperability is the priority and many AI algorithms are dependent on interoperable data⏎exchange to support the foundational data that drives AI tools. True interoperability will come⏎with the ability to link patient information across different EHRs, clinical locations, and other⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 10⏎organizations managing patient data to have a complete longitudinal record. Standardizing⏎EHRs, supporting standardized data exchange between EHRs, using trusted health care⏎terminologies, and embedding AI-driven process feeds will facilitate this state. Part of ensuring⏎trust in AI tools is having consistent privacy and security standards that are integrated and part⏎of data exchange processes.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎The overall challenge of addressing patients’ and caregivers’ wishes is that there is no one-size-⏎fits-all solution. We have heard situations where patients have been unhappy with AI tools, as in⏎the example of lawsuits involving ambient scribes. We have also heard examples where AI tools⏎have enhanced the patient experience.⏎There are also opportunities where AI could make a significant positive impact for patients,⏎including decreasing delays in access to care, scheduling, diagnostic support, care⏎coordination, care planning across providers, referrals, medication reconciliation, cancer⏎detection, better interpretations of images, and reviews for rare disease flags. These are all⏎areas where AI can assist in closing gaps in care and provide real benefits without stepping into⏎the area of diagnosis.⏎Prior authorization is one area where there is significant interest in how AI can improve current⏎processes. One potential benefit would be more accurate and appropriate data being identified⏎and supplied by the provider and faster response times from health plans. There are use cases⏎where there is evidence of medical necessity or available clinical information within the payers’⏎system that can be used for the review, which would negate the need for the provider to send⏎the information as an attachment, generating a timelier response. At the same time, there is⏎growing concern regarding the use of AI for denials. The sole use of AI should not be the⏎determining factor in a health plan’s care or coverage determinations, and instead, human,⏎licensed providers should have the final say.⏎Another positive example of AI use with patients is the ability to take complex clinical or⏎administrative information and turn it into plain language for patients to better understand. As an⏎example, a payer may use this technology to create patient-friendly versions of evidence of⏎coverage and benefits coverage information. Again, trusted health care terminologies can play a⏎major role here.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 11⏎Testing of AI tools under a government or private-public partnership governance structure will⏎enable organizations to adopt and use AI technology with confidence that it will perform as⏎expected. Whenever possible, identification of use cases for AI should include relieving⏎administrative burden, such as using ambient scribes, charting, scheduling, and reducing⏎providers’ prior authorization burden. While most AI research focuses on model performance,⏎and less on the topic of implementation, there is a need to move beyond innovation and focus⏎more on the real-world impact, economic implications, and implementation science of AI⏎technology. Research on AI should focus on what happens after deployment of the tools,⏎performance of tools in the real world vs. the lab, and necessary governance structures that can⏎lead to sustained benefits.⏎Literature research often focuses on factors of diagnostic accuracy and predictive performance,⏎but adoption also depends on functional aspects, such as reduced clinician burden, favorable⏎net cost, positive health outcomes, and reduced variation in care, to name a few. The⏎ecosystem needs evidence and a body of knowledge about AI outcomes and results, as well as⏎use case-specific findings of what works and does not work in the real world and why.⏎ASTP/ONC can lay the groundwork to demonstrate the trust and reliability in using AI tools that⏎will then make implementation decisions easier for all stakeholders.⏎Solicitation of Public Comments⏎1. Regulation⏎As the nation’s principal health regulator, HHS helps shape the environment in⏎which AI for clinical care is developed, evaluated, and deployed. HHS seeks to⏎establish a regulatory posture on AI that is well understood, predictable, and⏎proportionate to any risks presented to enable rapid innovation while protecting⏎patients and the confidentiality of their identifiable health information and⏎maintaining public trust. We seek feedback on how current HHS regulations⏎impact AI adoption and use for clinical care.⏎Static algorithms are better suited to the current regulatory framework, whereas continuous⏎learning systems and evolving models are not suited for a drawn out and unpredictable⏎schedule. Current frameworks for evaluating AI are more appropriate for static AI, such as the⏎regulatory framework of the FDA’s medical device approval processes. The challenge is that⏎creating a separate regulatory framework for AI could create redundancies and inefficiencies. A⏎solution would be to synchronize with some existing frameworks to include foundational aspects⏎of HIPAA and the FDA regulatory process.⏎Regulations related to AI also require inter-agency, whole of government collaboration, including⏎the FDA, CDC, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, and others to fully address medical⏎devices, non-medical devices, clinical decision support, privacy, security, and cybersecurity.⏎We know that the current regulatory process is drawn out, and this does not always meet the⏎needs of fast-paced technology like AI. As stated, we encourage you to explore the use of sub-⏎regulatory action to provide guidance or standardization in support of appropriate AI adoption.⏎Further, we recommend that HHS prioritize the leveraging of AI to reduce administrative burden⏎on patients, providers, and payers, in addition to improving clinical care.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 12⏎2. Reimbursement⏎HHS’s payment policies and programs have massive effects on how health care is⏎delivered in the United States, often times with unintended consequences.⏎Hypothetically, if a payer is taking financial risk for the long-term health and⏎health costs of an individual, that payer will have an inherent incentive to promote⏎access to the highest-value interventions for patients. Under government⏎designed and dictated fee-for-service regimes, however, coverage and⏎reimbursement decisions are slow. Rarely does covering new innovations reduce⏎net spending; and waste, fraud, and abuse is difficult to prevent, often times⏎leading to massive spending bubbles on concentrated items or services that are⏎not commensurate with the value of such products. Given the inherent flaws in⏎legacy payment systems, we seek to ensure that the potential promises of AI⏎innovations are not diminished through inertia and instead such payment systems⏎are modernized to meet the needs of a changing healthcare system. We seek⏎feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive and⏎ability to promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions, foster⏎competition among clinical care AI tool builders, and accelerate access to and⏎affordability of AI tools for clinical care.⏎Specific to value-based care arrangements, providers need quicker access to better data to⏎determine how they are performing in their value-based contracts and take actions to improve⏎that performance. Earlier access to better data permit clinicians to make necessary⏎modifications to better meet the measures and incentives and ultimately provide improved care⏎to patients. Use of AI in value-based models is one area where this innovation could improve⏎data collection, data reporting, and clinician performance.⏎3. Research & Development⏎HHS supports one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems, catalyzing⏎innovation to supplement the market. By enabling applied AI research and⏎development, care delivery research and implementation science, as well as AI⏎entrepreneurship in health care, we can better translate AI technologies from⏎concept to clinical use. We seek input on ways in which HHS may invest in⏎research and development (including public-private partnerships and cooperative⏎research and development agreements (CRADAs)) to integrate AI in care delivery⏎and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the health and⏎wellbeing of all Americans.⏎As stated earlier, ASTP/ONC can support the transition of AI technologies from concept to⏎clinical use through providing testing environments and resources for developers and users.⏎Developing methods for surveillance, tracking, and monitoring of AI-based tools to identify⏎performance, including metrics related to time, cost, quality, etc., to evaluate and demonstrate⏎functionality could result in increased adoption and use. Trust will be garnered by both testing⏎environments and public access to testing reports.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 13⏎Conclusion⏎WEDI applauds ASTP/ONC’s efforts to solicit industry opinions on the potential impact that AI⏎will have on the health care ecosystem and actions the federal government should take to⏎advance its adoption and use. We believe AI currently has a significant impact on the health⏎care sector and that impact will exponentially grow in the coming years, affecting patients,⏎providers, payers, and others. WEDI shares your commitment to understanding the AI⏎environment and how AI technology can augment the care delivery process, improve data⏎exchange, and reduce administrative burden for all stakeholders. We look forward to working⏎with you as the development and implementation of AI-related regulations and policies continue.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to share our perspective on this RFI. We hope our comments⏎and recommendations will serve to assist ASTP/ONC as it moves forward with this work. Please⏎contact Robert Tennant, WEDI Executive Director, at rtennant@WEDI.org with any questions⏎on these comments and recommendations.⏎Sincerely,⏎/s/⏎Merri-Lee Stine⏎Chair, WEDI⏎cc: WEDI Board of Directors♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0071♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Esposito, Dominick, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dominick♔Esposito♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Practically, buy-in from providers to use new AI tools, their confidence in their efficacy,⏎and their skepticism that the tools are just going to be “another expense.” As a patient⏎and parent, I have seen some AI tools been positively received by providers. For⏎example, my sons’ endocrinologists use AI scribes regularly to support notetaking and⏎follow up (both my boys have Type 1 diabetes). So, these types of tools have helped⏎our medical professional community become comfortable with AI, but future innovations⏎have to take small steps forward, I believe, for most providers to embrace them.⏎Providers believe in rigorous evidence; they are trained on the randomized clinical trial⏎being the gold standard for care. And, then in practice, they collect data through every⏎observation they make with their own patients. For AI to flourish in support of clinical⏎care, providers will need to feel confident that the “algorithms” behind the AI are based⏎on appropriate data. At first, they will want to vet that the “answers” or “support” they are⏎receiving is accurate and in line with their own experience before beginning to trust it.⏎Gaining providers’ trust will be one avenue to getting their buy-in that the cost of any⏎new tools are worth it and not just another expense.⏎Policymakers may be able to facilitate the use of AI tools by supporting independent⏎assessments of tools in a sort of clearinghouse. Or, maybe less costly, by identifying the⏎preferred characteristics and operational parameters required for high quality tools used⏎in clinical care. Some of this information can come from providers themselves who⏎would be on the front lines working with tools.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further⏎support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (⏎e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Assessments of AI for clinical care could be rooted in an implementation science⏎framework. The question asked specifically, and appropriately, about workflow which is⏎an underlying and assumed benefit of the injection of AI into clinical care. That is, the⏎use of AI by clinicians should, hopefully, ease their workflow and allow for more⏎meaningful interaction with patients. The promise of the availability of data from the use⏎of AI agents in clinical care offers a unique opportunity to assess the implementation of⏎this technology. With much less burden than in other research before it, we could⏎answer questions like: How do providers use the tools available? Is the information from⏎the tools accurate? Do providers continue to use the same tool for similar patients or⏎does their use drop off over time? Is the practice of care for specific conditions different⏎with the tools and in what ways?⏎1⏎⏎The deployment of new tools into clinical practice could allow for very robust testing in⏎short amounts of time. It would be important, however, that testing occur in a multitude⏎of different and varied locations to understand how different providers in different⏎settings might use the new technologies. The CMS Innovation Center, for example,⏎could consider a model that supports the use of new technologies in a tiered approach.⏎First, by soliciting organizations that sell such AI tools to participate and put their tools⏎“to the test” and second to solicit providers and provider groups to participate. It may⏎have to offer further incentives to providers in some settings (e.g., rural communities).⏎CMS could also request large health systems that already use in-house AI tools to also⏎participate with their own technologies. There is some precedence for this type of⏎approach of “putting your approach to the test” with the test of disease management⏎programs by CMS from 2003 to 2008. There are ways to evaluate these tools without⏎the need for randomized controlled trials or “usual care” comparison groups as well. Any⏎assessment of tolls might also consider patient-facing tools that support providers and⏎patients as well.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎The development of standards for industry-testing could go a long way to support⏎providers’ buy-in and confidence that AI tools are efficacious. It may be resource-⏎intensive, however, to go as far as accreditation and certification and only companies⏎that have available resources (or are able to charge users a premium) may be to afford⏎these steps.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider⏎specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎For this question and the next two, I will lean heavily on my experience as a father of⏎children with Type 1 Diabetes and a son with a father and mother-in-law with the same⏎disease. For many individuals living with T1D, there is a lot of data available that is not⏎always easy accessible to them or their providers and certainly not integrated with other⏎information. For example, many individuals with T1D (though nearly not enough) use⏎continuous glucose monitors (CGMs) to track their blood sugar continuously but not⏎many providers or patients themselves have access to the data (which is collected in 5-⏎minute intervals). Freeing these data up for use by patients who may, say, use AI⏎algorithms to support their care and optimize their insulin dosages could be a major step⏎forward for people living with T1D. The highs and lows that come with less than⏎accurate insulin dosing are a frequent cause of stress and concern for these patients⏎and their caregivers and, too often, result in concomitant mental health conditions as⏎2⏎⏎well. Those patients who additionally use an automated insulin delivery device (casually⏎referred to as an insulin pump) could also then bring together information on insulin⏎dosage with their blood sugar levels to support stronger control. There are already non-⏎FDA approved, community-built apps for this purpose (for example, Trio) that are⏎moving in this direction but the case of T1D could be a promising pilot opportunity for⏎the federal government given the prevalence of T1D in the United States and its annual⏎incidence rate. (My children or other family members do not use this app but we have⏎met families that do.)⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎As a caregiver, a significant concern that I have is reliability of the potential AI⏎technology. Taking the case of T1D, for example, it is a disease that can be very⏎challenging and frustrating to manage. Until we have cell therapy cures, there is no⏎“silver bullet” solution. Speaking as a father of two teenagers with T1D, each one of my⏎boys is completely different in his reaction to insulin and the same carbohydrate-laden⏎foods. This is typical of the community of individuals with T1D. So, as a parent, I face⏎this dichotomy. On the one hand, I desperately would love for someone to harness the⏎power of AI/agentic AI/etc. to develop a tool to support my children so that some stress⏎is relieved from them and my wife daily on making decisions. It is established that⏎people with T1D make 180 to 300 additional health-related decisions a day.1 We would⏎love a tool to relieve the stress of making these decisions. On the other hand, I would⏎be cautious about any tool that purported to be a solution without knowing that it was⏎tested and vetted in a standardized and rigorous fashion. So, my concern? Can we⏎have an efficient process to vet tools that is timely, is patient-centered, and cost-efficient⏎so that we can provide families confidence that the tools being rolled out into any clinical⏎care setting are safe and reliable?⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Please, consider prioritizing the Type 1 diabetes space for exploration of AI research.⏎While my comments above have focused on individuals with T1D, I would also implore⏎that just as important an area are those individuals who have evidence of the antibodies⏎for T1D but are not yet insulin dependent. We have the technology and ability today to⏎screen for all the antibodies that science has proven result in a degradation of one’s⏎beta cells. One of my sons was diagnosed as such and we monitored him for multiple⏎1 The stress of making diabetes decisions⏎3⏎⏎years before he became insulin dependent. Unfortunately, not everyone who could⏎develop T1D is tested. And, more unfortunately, “crashing into” T1D is a very traumatic⏎event for people with this disease and their loved ones.⏎In addition to supporting individuals who are currently insulin dependent, AI research⏎could also support our knowledge of whom might be most likely to have antibodies,⏎could support individuals who have these antibodies, and support providers and⏎caregivers. The amount of information for individuals who are about to become or are⏎insulin-dependent has proliferated and making sense of it for each individual case can⏎be overwhelming. For both patients and providers (many of whom do not know a lot⏎about T1D), education modules tied to AI could support better learning and the stress of⏎this disease.⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0072♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Lumeris, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lumeris♔♔♔♔♔♔January 9, 2025⏎Jim O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary Posnack:⏎Lumeris is pleased to provide our insights in response to the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) Request for Information (RFI) regarding the clinical use of⏎Artificial Intelligence (AI). As a pioneer in value-based care (VBC) and the developer of⏎Tom™—our agentic AI platform—we believe that access to high quality primary care is⏎the bedrock of a high functioning, affordable healthcare system. Given the declining⏎provider workforce, the future of U.S. primary care will mandate the smart use of⏎modern technologies to maximize the reach of a highly constrained human capacity.⏎Our Primary Care as a Service (PCaaS) model orchestrated through Tom treats AI not⏎as a static software tool, but as a dynamic Digital Member of the Care Team capable of⏎performing "digital labor" to scale clinical capacity, reduce provider burnout, and⏎provide continuous, "always-on" care. By enabling agentic AI to handle "in-between"⏎care—including proactive monitoring, patient education, and autonomous⏎scheduling—we can ensure that primary care is accessible to every American,⏎regardless of geographic or physician headcount constraints.⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The primary barriers to innovation include data fragmentation, software costs without⏎sufficient return on investment (ROI) to warrant investment, misaligned financial⏎incentives, and significant change management. Current fee-for-service (FFS) models⏎fail to recognize and appropriately compensate the value of "digital labor."⏎Furthermore, Agentic AI requires a comprehensive, real-time view of the patient to be⏎effective. Healthcare data is messy. Accessing, aggregating, and normalizing disparate⏎3251 Riverport Ln, Maryland Heights, MO 63043 | 1.888.586.3747 lumeris.com⏎⏎data sets are required for the highest use of clinical grade AI. The lack of standardized,⏎real-time access to Pharmacy Benefit Manager (PBM) data and Admission, Discharge,⏎Transfer (ADT) feeds, are prime examples of key strategic hurdles that prevent AI from⏎acting on critical events (e.g., a medication non-fill or an ER visit) at the moment of⏎highest impact. AI is a relatively expensive technology and the proliferation of point⏎solutions that create limited value individually presents challenges to already-strained⏎provider budgets. Platform solutions promise greater value but come with a significant⏎initial investment in data aggregation and software implementation. Finally, clinical⏎workflow-disruptive technologies require significant change management, typically⏎with an associated reduction in short-term productivity.⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎It is paramount that HHS modernize the Physician Fee Schedule (42 CFR § 414) to⏎move beyond a human-only, time-based reimbursement paradigm. In support of⏎fostering a modern and forward-thinking health care ecosystem Lumeris recommends⏎HHS take the following actions:⏎ Evolution of Coordination Codes: Current codes for Remote Physiological⏎Monitoring (RPM - CPT 99457, 99458), Chronic Care Management (CCM - CPT⏎99490), and Transitional Care Management (TCM - CPT 99495, 99496) require⏎thresholds of human clinical staff time. HHS should allow AI-driven "digital labor"⏎to fulfill a portion of these requirements. If an AI monitors data and handles⏎patient education, the clinical objective is met more efficiently than via manual⏎outreach.⏎ Modernizing payment for “Digital Labor”: HHS should prioritize the creation of⏎reimbursement codes for Asynchronous AI-Led Patient Engagement. Currently,⏎if Tom successfully triages a patient or closes a diabetic care gap through⏎autonomous outreach, there is often no mechanism for the provider to be⏎reimbursed for that “digital work,” despite the clear clinical value.⏎Furthermore, it is essential that HHS take steps to enable the most efficient interfaces⏎between AI software solutions and electronic health records to foster clinical adoption:⏎ Information Blocking (45 CFR Part 171): Define the withholding of transactional⏎APIs (scheduling, referrals, tasking) by EHR vendors as Information Blocking. For⏎AI to fulfill care coordination, it must have the authority to act (e.g., schedule a⏎follow-up) without proprietary gatekeeping or "innovation taxes" imposed by⏎legacy vendors.⏎©2026 Lumeris | Proprietary & Confidential 2⏎⏎We encourage HHS to collaborate with the Food & Drug Administration (FDA) to⏎modernize and clarify regulatory pathways that support safe adoption of clinically⏎trustworthy AI. Together, HHS and FDA can scale the Predetermined Change Control⏎Plan (PCCP) framework to better accommodate adaptive AI systems. Continued⏎clarification of FDA’s Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) guidance would further⏎streamline compliance for developers and providers. In addition, the agencies could⏎jointly establish FDA‑supervised “clinical AI sandboxes” with academic medical centers,⏎enabling real‑world testing, structured monitoring, and iterative improvement before⏎full clearance. Finally, HHS and FDA can outline and expand enforcement discretion⏎policies for lower‑risk AI‑supported clinical activities, ensuring regulation is risk‑based⏎and does not inhibit innovation where there is low potential for harm.⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎We strongly urge HHS to lead the creation of a Federal Safe Harbor Framework for⏎“Augmented Intelligence.” This should clarify that when AI provides contextual clinical⏎decision support (CDS) to a "human-in-the-loop," the legal standard of care remains⏎centered on the clinician’s judgment. This reduces the "liability chilling effect" that⏎currently slows adoption due to the defensive stance in which it places innovation.⏎For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts,⏎grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎As a technology driven solution, AI presents the unique opportunity to generate more⏎real-time feedback from the end user, thereby enabling developers to address⏎feedback in a prompt manner. Our Tom platform leverages clinician-led feedback⏎through a “thumbs up/down” mechanism that allows the provider to validate or⏎challenge AI generated recommendations in real-time. This communication avenue is⏎closely monitored as an indicator of logical drift or regional bias, allowing us to make⏎modifications, as appropriate, based on the provider’s feedback.⏎AI tools have the distinct ability to provide multiple layers of oversight through other⏎AI-powered solutions. Lumeris utilizes secondary, high-reasoning large language⏎models (LLMs) to perform automated audits of Tom’s patient interactions (an LLM-as-⏎a-Judge). This “Judge” has the capability of flagging safety concerns or other⏎aberrancies for human review, serving as a collaborative partner in the AI monitoring⏎process. Such technologies have the incredible opportunity of boosting monitoring⏎©2026 Lumeris | Proprietary & Confidential 3⏎⏎productivity compared to largely manual efforts that require significant human time⏎and resources.⏎With the goal of ensuring consistency across the industry, we strongly encourage HHS⏎to fund the development of “Gold Standard” clinical judge datasets that could be⏎leveraged by vendors to benchmark the safety of their LLMs. In the absence of a⏎universal standard, the quality of AI tools provided to clinicians may vary broadly, which⏎in turn will create differential experiences by patients across the board. Safety must⏎remain at the center of all clinical interactions, regardless of the mechanisms through⏎which they are administered.⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should promote Outcome-Based Certification that prioritizes the performance of⏎AI tools in real-world environments (e.g., impact on HEDIS scores) rather than solely on⏎static software audits. Real-world evidence provides a more meaningful assessment of⏎the performance of these tools compared to non-realistic “lab-based” environments.⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would⏎have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on⏎quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Tom has proven that patients of all ages are willing and able to engage with a⏎conversational AI interface. Tom outperforms simple reminders, taking that next step in⏎educating, handling objections, and autonomously scheduling services. As a Digital⏎Member of the Care Team, it reduces practice phone volumes and in-basket messages⏎while triaging patients to the most efficient care venue, creating additional⏎touchpoints to improve quality of care and patient outcomes.⏎Contextual Clinical Decision Support (CDS) represents one of the greatest novel AI⏎use-cases in the near term. Such applications allow for the upskilling of providers by⏎connecting a 360-degree patient view to real-time, evidence-based recommendations⏎(e.g., UpToDate®). Presenting insights natively at the point of care ensures compliance⏎with the latest guidelines, improving clinical outcomes.⏎Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What⏎are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Making the decision to deploy significant resources, including AI solutions, typically⏎requires the attention and approval of an organizations Chief Financial Officer (CFO),⏎tasked with the ever-challenging role of prioritizing investments within the context of⏎overall organizational financials, strategic objectives, and ROI. The Chief Medical⏎Officer (CMO) and Chief Information Officer (CIO) are other primary stakeholders,⏎©2026 Lumeris | Proprietary & Confidential 4⏎⏎responsible for ensuring the clinical goals of the organization are being met and that⏎technology solutions comply with the relevant requirements. Increasingly, health⏎systems with the necessary resources may take the additional step of naming a Chief⏎Health AI Officer to provide dedicated focus on the implementation and ethical use of⏎AI.⏎The largest administrative clinical hurdle reducing the adoption of AI in clinical contexts⏎is the "Workflow Interruption Cost"—if AI requires a provider to leave their native EHR⏎environment, adoption fails. It is vital that AI solutions integrate smoothly with existing⏎EHR environments and regulatory policies ensure that the competitive landscape of⏎healthcare technology is not dominated by a monopoly of a few EHR provider’s⏎proprietary AI tools. Additional barriers include newer AI governance structures with⏎lengthy privacy and legal reviews as well as IT resourcing for data acquisition and⏎integration efforts.⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Interoperability must be a cornerstone of an AI-powered healthcare technology⏎ecosystem and transition from "Read-Only" to "Transactional." At present, most EHR⏎vendors allow an AI to "read" data via Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources®⏎(FHIR) but gatekeep the proprietary application programming interfaces (APIs) needed⏎to "write" actions like scheduling or ordering a referral. HHS must mandate non-⏎discriminatory access to Action APIs to allow AI agents to perform the digital labor⏎necessary to reduce administrative impact, unlocking the full return from these burden⏎reduction capabilities.⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients⏎and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers increasingly seek connectivity and "always-on" access to their⏎providers while also having the primary concern of losing the "human touch." Lumeris⏎addresses this by ensuring Tom always functions as a member of the team, escalating⏎complex or emotional concerns to human nurses or staff immediately. Tom works from⏎patient-specific data, personalizes patient interactions, and has access to schedule or⏎prompt proactive active outreach from the human care team, striking the balance⏎between high-powered connectivity and sensitivity in interaction.⏎In addition to loss of human connection, patients and caregivers can be hesitant to⏎adopt AI and other technology in healthcare settings due to concerns related to the⏎privacy and security of their health information, the safety and reliability of clinical⏎judgment and decision-making, and potential for bias and equity concerns. These⏎concerns can be addressed through promotion of digital health literacy initiatives by⏎HHS, providers, and the broader healthcare community. Ensuring healthcare AI⏎©2026 Lumeris | Proprietary & Confidential 5⏎⏎complies with existing and new requirements for privacy and security establishes⏎greater trust in technology, and transparency in clinical decision-making and published⏎research on improved outcomes can assure the public of the benefits of AI technology⏎in healthcare across clinical settings, geographic regions, and demographic groups.⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎We would strongly advise HHS to prioritize Implementation Science in driving the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care settings. Equally important, AI-enabled models of care⏎should be designed and evaluated to ensure consistent access to high-quality care⏎across diverse practice settings and patient populations, particularly in rural,⏎underserved, or workforce-constrained communities.⏎Research is needed to understand how the integration of digital resources that expand⏎provider capacity shifts the economics of primary care and impacts long-term health⏎outcomes, particularly among underserved populations. Attention should be given to⏎the role of practice size and affiliation with health systems and state-based university⏎systems in the availability and implementation of AI tools and their impact on patient⏎experience. It is essential that HHS understand the financial and technological limiting⏎factors that may reduce the ability for all providers, and all patients, to experience the⏎benefits of AI adoption in the U.S.⏎Conclusion⏎Lumeris appreciates the opportunity to provide our insights as a healthcare technology⏎company dedicated to transforming primary care through AI-driven solutions. We are⏎ready and enthusiastic to partner with HHS in transitioning the U.S. healthcare system⏎into the era of Primary Care as a Service where every American has continuous access⏎to primary care, regardless of geography or workforce shortages. By unlocking⏎transactional data, modernizing reimbursement to account for digital labor, and⏎adopting hybrid evaluation models, we can ensure AI is a deflationary force resulting in⏎engaged physicians, healthy patients, stronger performance for health systems, and a⏎healthier America.⏎©2026 Lumeris | Proprietary & Confidential 6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0073♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bryan, Jennifer, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jennifer♔Bryan♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I appreciate the opportunity to comment on the agency’s request for input on accelerating adoption of AI in clinical care. Real-world patient safety and accountable deployment are not defined by performance metrics alone — they require systems that preserve reconstructable evidence of how and why decisions were made in context.⏎⏎In practice, post-hoc provenance metadata is insufficient to ensure accountability because it only records associations after the fact. In AI-mediated clinical workflows, the integrity of downstream evidence depends on whether the individual invoking an AI function was credentialed, acting within scope, and authorized at the moment an AI output was generated or applied. Without invocation-time authorization metadata, audit trails may be technically complete yet insufficient to establish accountability when AI systems contribute to documentation or decision support.⏎⏎To support safe scaling and effective oversight, federal guidance should:⏎• Distinguish between retrospective attribution and invocation-bound authorization metadata as separate governance primitives.⏎• Encourage inclusion of execution-context metadata that persists through export and third-party review.⏎• Define minimal expectations for audit survivability, specifically that artifact exports remain reconstructable outside vendor user interfaces.⏎⏎These measures align safety with defensibility and provide clinicians, health systems, and regulators a practical foundation for monitoring AI systems over their lifecycle.⏎⏎Thank you for considering these perspectives.⏎⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0074♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Patient-Facing Clinical AI Coalition, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Patient-Facing Clinical AI Coalition♔♔♔♔♔♔Opportunities for HHS to Accelerate Patient-Facing⏎Clinical AI through Payment Reform⏎Shobha Dasari (Stanford University); Aditya Narayan (Stanford University); Ramin Bastani⏎(Healthvana); Byron Crowe MD (Doctronic); Ellen DaSilva (Summer Health); Rishi Khakhkhar⏎MD (Counsel Health); Anuruddh Mishra (August AI); Nirav R. Shah, MD, MPH (Stanford⏎University)⏎Response to HHS Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13; 90 FR 60108)⏎Executive Summary⏎Patient-facing clinical AI represents a rare opportunity to introduce new technology that has⏎potential to reduce healthcare spending while improving access. The primary barrier to scaling⏎these gains system-wide is ambiguity in payment pathways (Q1, Q7). Today, no billing codes⏎exist for AI-enabled clinical services, asynchronous care remains largely unreimbursable, and⏎each payer relationship requires bespoke negotiation that cannot scale.⏎This response reflects the operational experience of leaders at five companies delivering AI-⏎enabled clinical care across pediatrics, HIV/STI care, primary care, and chronic disease⏎management: Summer Health, Healthvana, Counsel Health, August AI, and Doctronic.1 We⏎represent an emerging market category – patient-facing clinical AI – and draw on direct⏎operational, real-world experience to address the RFI's questions on barriers to adoption (Q1),⏎payment policy changes (Q2), where AI tools have met or exceeded expectations (Q6), and⏎administrative hurdles to adoption (Q7).⏎We recommend a phased approach (Q1, Q2, Q7):2⏎● Near-term: Modernize fee-for-service by recognizing asynchronous message-based⏎care as a billable modality nationwide (42 CFR § 410.78), creating an AI-augmented⏎modifier for existing E/M codes (42 CFR § 414.22), and expanding APCM codes to⏎cover AI-enabled care (G0556-G0558; 89 FR 97710)⏎● Medium-term: Establish billing codes for AI-autonomous clinical services (42 CFR §⏎410.32) and pilot PMPM models through CMMI⏎● Long-term: Layer outcomes-based payment as the claims data and clinical evidence⏎generated by earlier phases mature⏎These recommendations are intended to be net-deflationary, where each new payment pathway⏎reimburses AI-enabled care at rates below traditional equivalents, capturing cost savings while⏎1 Descriptions of each company represented in this response are provided in Appendix 1.⏎2 A summary of all recommendations and corresponding regulatory citations is provided in Appendix 2.⏎⏎expanding access. HHS has an opportunity to channel this innovation toward achieving Triple⏎Aim results rather than ceding it to unregulated alternatives.⏎Patient-Facing Clinical AI Delivers Clinical, Access, Operational, and⏎Cost Outcomes⏎AI is performing real clinical work: diagnosis, treatment guidance, chronic disease management,⏎and more. From our combined operations across pediatrics, HIV/STI care, primary care, and⏎chronic disease management, we’ve achieved the following outcomes (Q6):⏎Cost:⏎● $10/visit target at scale, down from current models (Summer Health).⏎● $29 visits, already below typical insurance video visit rates (Counsel Health).⏎● $39 cash visits with AI-generated documentation matching chief resident quality⏎(Doctronic).⏎Improved access:⏎● 13,000 previously unanswered patient messages are now resulting in clinical guidance⏎(Healthvana).⏎● 24/7 coverage with response times under 15 minutes (Summer Health) and under 10⏎minutes (Counsel Health), with 4-5x the patient capacity of traditional video telemedicine⏎without sacrificing response quality (Counsel Health).⏎Clinical quality:⏎● Nearly 2x medication persistence and 3x PrEP initiation among patients using AI-⏎powered HIV prevention support (Healthvana), vs. national baselines of >50% first-year⏎discontinuation3 and only 25% prescription rate among those with a PrEP indication.4⏎● 100% of patient conversations evaluated for quality in near real-time, compared to less⏎than 1% in traditional retrospective chart review – enabling a level of auditability and⏎transparency that exceeds traditional quality oversight (Counsel Health).⏎● Diagnostic accuracy that catches physician errors, serving as a clinical decision support⏎layer (August AI).⏎Operational efficiency:⏎● 7 visits/hour today, targeting 10-12 as AI handles more of the clinical workflow (Summer⏎Health).⏎● 50-70% of visit time (primarily data exchange and intake) offloaded to AI before the⏎physician encounter (Doctronic).⏎3 McCormick CD, Sullivan PS, Qato DM, Crawford SY, Schumock GT, Lee TA. Adherence and⏎persistence of HIV pre-exposure prophylaxis use in the United States. Pharmacoepidemiol Drug Saf.⏎2024;33(1):e5729.⏎4 CDC. 2020 HIV Surveillance Report. National Center for HIV, Viral Hepatitis, STD, and Tuberculosis⏎Prevention. 2024. Available at: https://www.cdc.gov/nchhstp/director-letters/2020-hiv-surveillance-⏎report.html.⏎⏎● 56% of clinical staff saving 5+ hours/week, redirecting time to complex cases⏎(Healthvana).⏎● 2/3 of patient chats resolved AI-only; 1/3 escalated to a physician, demonstrating safe⏎triage (Counsel Health).⏎These results come from live clinical operations serving patients at scale across multiple⏎specialties and geographies.⏎Current Payment Models Do Not Recognize AI-Enabled Care⏎The payment system that HHS aims to reform was built on a foundational assumption: a⏎clinician delivers a service to a patient in a discrete encounter. Payment was designed for brick-⏎and-mortar health systems that provided episodic care in an era when acute care was more⏎important and prevalent than chronic care management.⏎As care needs and patient preferences have evolved, payment mechanisms for patient-facing⏎clinical AI have not kept up. AI-enabled care breaks every element of that assumption: the⏎clinician may be supervising rather than personally delivering care, the encounter may be⏎asynchronous or continuous rather than discrete, and the value may show up months later in⏎avoided ER visits rather than in a single billable moment. While a handful of procedure-specific⏎CPT codes exist for narrowly scoped AI diagnostics (e.g., CPT 92229 for diabetic retinopathy⏎screening), there is no scalable national coding pathway for patient-facing AI that delivers triage,⏎treatment guidance, or ongoing care management.⏎Today, patient-facing clinical AI companies survive through a patchwork: cash-pay visits⏎(Summer Health, Counsel Health, Doctronic), employer contracts (Counsel Health), 340B drug⏎pricing savings (Healthvana), or direct-to-consumer subscription (Summer Health, Counsel⏎Health, Doctronic, August AI). Autonomous AI care (live in Utah through Doctronic) has no⏎payment pathway; only the human visit component is billable.⏎The specific barriers we face (Q1, Q7):⏎● Asynchronous care is not recognized as a billable modality. CMS and most state⏎Medicaid programs do not classify fully asynchronous text-based encounters as⏎reimbursable, despite widespread adoption of message-based care by providers⏎managing growing patient panels. Summer Health is the only company among us to⏎have secured payer reimbursement for asynchronous care, a process that took three⏎years of negotiation.⏎● No billing codes exist for AI-enabled clinical services. There is no CPT modifier or⏎service code for AI-assisted or AI-delivered care. When AI is deployed within health⏎systems today, it generates no direct reimbursement. Any value captured flows through⏎staffing efficiency or cross-subsidized programs, not through the payment system itself.⏎● Current contracting models require bespoke negotiation that cannot scale. PMPM⏎or case-rate arrangements must be negotiated payer by payer, plan by plan, which⏎creates uncertainty on both sides of the transaction: AI companies cannot reliably⏎⏎forecast revenue, and health system buyers cannot justify procurement without a billing⏎code to point to.⏎As a result, innovation gets bottlenecked into direct-to-consumer approaches instead of scaling⏎through the healthcare system (Q1). Without clear reimbursement pathways, adoption will⏎remain confined to cash-pay patients and bespoke payer arrangements, limiting the market to⏎companies that can survive these conditions rather than those delivering the best care.⏎Clear payment pathways would transform AI-enabled care from a niche product into a⏎deflationary force for the whole system, lowering costs and broadening access beyond the⏎limited markets where this technology is already proving its value.⏎HHS Can Design Payment Policy That Improves Care and Lowers the⏎Total Cost of Care⏎HHS is right to be concerned that covering innovations rarely reduces net spending. The history⏎of healthcare payment is full of spending bubbles that followed new billing codes. To realize the⏎deflationary potential of AI-enabled care, payment systems must be designed to capture those⏎gains rather than repeat past patterns.⏎This requires learning from prior payment experience in both directions. Underpayment kills⏎adoption: one cost analysis of the Medicare Diabetes Prevention Program found that delivering⏎the program cost $553 per participant while Medicare only reimbursed $108, and provider⏎participation remained limited nationwide.5 A similar dynamic played out with IDx-DR, the first⏎autonomous AI diagnostic to receive a dedicated CPT code (92229) for diabetic retinopathy⏎screening. Despite FDA clearance and strong clinical evidence, adoption was slower than⏎expected in part because billing complexity and workflow integration costs outweighed available⏎reimbursement.6 Conversely, loosely defined codes invite overuse: the rapid proliferation of⏎remote patient monitoring billing, where broad code definitions led to widespread abusive billing⏎and subsequent enforcement action, illustrates the opposite failure mode.7⏎Our recommendation for episode-based bundling is designed to avoid both extremes. Across all⏎of these mechanisms, payment design should focus on per-episode reimbursement rather than⏎per-interaction billing, ensuring that cost savings accrue to the system rather than being eroded⏎by volume inflation. A unified national framework also would prevent the state-by-state⏎5 Parsons AS, Raman V, Starr B, Zezza M, Rehm CD. Medicare underpayment for Diabetes Prevention⏎Program: implications for DPP suppliers. Am J Manag Care. 2018;24(10):475-478.⏎6 Rajesh AE, Davidson OQ, Lee CS, Lee AY. Artificial intelligence and diabetic retinopathy: AI framework,⏎prospective studies, head-to-head validation, and cost-effectiveness. Diabetes Care. 2023;46(10):1728-⏎1739.⏎7 Office of Inspector General, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. Billing for Remote Patient⏎Monitoring in Medicare. August 2025.⏎⏎fragmentation that slowed telehealth adoption, where inconsistent reimbursement policies⏎across state Medicaid programs created years of uncertainty for providers and patients alike.8⏎Though we recognize HHS's interest in outcomes-based payment, value-based care is the⏎wrong starting point for a nascent technology category, since it requires knowing how a⏎technology creates value, measuring it reliably, and attributing outcomes – none of which are⏎mature for clinical AI today. Starting with VBC would slow adoption and concentrate gains⏎among sophisticated players who can afford actuarial modeling and complex billing⏎infrastructure, rather than rewarding those who deliver the best care.⏎Fee-for-service provides the predictable revenue environment that health systems and AI⏎companies need to de-risk investment. It also creates the billing infrastructure and data capture⏎necessary to eventually measure outcomes at scale. Without a foundation of reimbursable AI⏎encounters, there is no data substrate for outcomes-based payment to build on. Given this, we⏎propose the following phased approach (Q2).⏎Near-term: Modernize fee-for-service for AI-enabled care (Q2)⏎Recognize asynchronous care as a billable modality.⏎We recommend that CMS amend 42 CFR § 410.78 to recognize asynchronous message-based⏎clinical encounters as reimbursable nationwide. Current regulations limit store-and-forward⏎technologies to Alaska and Hawaii demonstration programs (42 CFR § 410.78(d)), despite⏎widespread clinical adoption of text-based care. This limitation is particularly consequential for⏎rural communities, where text-based asynchronous care can be delivered over cellular networks⏎without requiring reliable broadband, specialist proximity, or scheduling around limited clinic⏎hours. The geographic-access rationale that justified the Alaska and Hawaii demonstrations⏎applies nationwide.⏎To prevent inflationary billing, we recommend episode-based bundling rather than per-message⏎reimbursement. All messages and AI interactions related to the same clinical issue would be⏎bundled into a single billable episode over a fixed time window (e.g., 7 days), consistent with⏎existing asynchronous care codes. This structure allows patients to return repeatedly over⏎several days (AI-enabled care is always on), while providers bill once per episode rather than⏎per interaction. This avoids the obvious abuse case where marginal AI touches generate⏎repeated charges.⏎Research on telehealth utilization supports this design: unbundled per-interaction billing has⏎been shown to increase spending without corresponding improvements in outcomes, while⏎8 Center for Connected Health Policy. State Telehealth Laws and Reimbursement Policies Report, Fall⏎2024. 2024. Available at: https://www.cchpca.org/resources/state-telehealth-laws-and-reimbursement-⏎policies-report-fall-2024/.⏎⏎episode-based structures contain costs by aligning reimbursement with clinical resolution rather⏎than message volume.9⏎We further recommend removing the requirement that an initial visit be conducted via video or⏎in-person before asynchronous follow-up is billable. This requirement was designed to establish⏎a patient-provider relationship before asynchronous follow-up, but it blocks AI-native care⏎models that begin with text-based triage. We propose replacing it with direct safeguards that⏎address the same concern more effectively. Each billable async episode must include a defined⏎clinical question, a documented clinical assessment, and a treatment recommendation or an⏎escalation decision.⏎Create an AI-augmented care modifier for existing E/M codes.⏎We recommend CMS establish an AI-augmented modifier applicable to existing evaluation and⏎management codes, similar to the -93 modifier for audio-only services (42 CFR § 414.22). This⏎modifier would enable reimbursement at a reduced rate (e.g., 80% of the standard rate),⏎reflecting the lower cost of delivering AI-assisted care. Eligibility should require documentation⏎of AI-assisted components, clinical data capture, defined escalation protocols, and clinician⏎attestation of review.⏎Current E/M codes carry time requirements. The 99213 CPT code, for example, assumes 15-20⏎minutes of clinician involvement. When AI handles intake, history-taking, and documentation,⏎the physician's role shrinks to review and decision-making, often 2-3 minutes. Under current⏎rules, this shorter involvement means the visit doesn't meet time thresholds for billing. We⏎recommend that the AI-augmented modifier be tied to the service delivered rather than to the⏎clinician's minutes spent. This avoids both extremes: underpayment for genuinely efficient AI-⏎assisted care, and abusive billing for marginal AI involvement (two seconds of AI interaction⏎should not generate a $200 charge).⏎When rates are set correctly, payer incentives align naturally. If AI-augmented visits are⏎reimbursed at a fraction of standard E/M rates and AI-assisted workflows enable a physician to⏎see more patients, total cost per treated patient declines even as encounter volume increases.⏎Expanding Advanced Primary Care Management codes to cover AI⏎CMS's introduction of Advanced Primary Care Management (APCM) codes (G0556-G0558; 89⏎FR 97710) represents meaningful progress toward the kind of payment infrastructure AI-⏎enabled care requires. Several of its 13 service elements (i.e., 24/7 access to care, enhanced⏎communication opportunities, population data analysis) describe capabilities that AI-enabled⏎platforms already deliver as core functionality. CMS's decision to replace time-based thresholds⏎with risk-tiered reimbursement was the right design choice and should be preserved. We⏎propose two specific modifications that would make APCM viable for AI-enabled care models:⏎9 Ashwood JS, Mehrotra A, Cowling D, Uscher-Pines L. Direct-to-consumer telehealth may increase⏎access to care but does not decrease spending. Health Affairs. 2017;36(3):485-491.⏎⏎First, the initiating visit requirement allows telehealth but requires a synchronous encounter for⏎new patients or those not seen by the practice in three years. AI-native platforms onboard⏎patients through asynchronous text-based intake, triage, and clinical assessment, which do not⏎qualify under current rules. CMS should allow documented asynchronous onboarding for APCM⏎and predicates such as TPM, as we recommend for asynchronous care, to satisfy this⏎requirement. TCM's mandatory face-to-face visit within 7-14 days (CPT 99495, 99496) should⏎accommodate asynchronous follow-up.⏎Second, APCM's attribution model requires a single named clinician to serve as the patient's⏎comprehensive primary care provider. AI-enabled platforms may involve rotating clinical⏎oversight or team-based supervision rather than a single named practitioner, and often deliver⏎condition-specific management (HIV prevention, pediatric triage, chronic disease support) rather⏎than serving as the patient's full medical home. CMS should expand eligibility to include⏎condition-specific care management and clarify that platforms with defined clinical governance⏎and escalation protocols can qualify as billing entities.⏎Medium-term: Create pathways for AI-delivered care (Q2)⏎Establish billing codes for AI-autonomous clinical services.⏎For encounters in which AI delivers clinical services directly (diagnosis, triage, treatment⏎guidance) with defined escalation protocols to human clinicians, we recommend that CMS⏎establish a new service category (42 CFR § 410.32 or a new section). This would provide a⏎payment pathway for fully autonomous AI care, which currently has no billing mechanism. For⏎example, Doctronic operates autonomous AI care in Utah today; only the human visit⏎component is reimbursable.⏎We recognize that autonomous AI care warrants a risk-tiered approach. Initial billing codes⏎should target lower-acuity, protocol-driven scenarios where diagnostic and treatment pathways⏎are well established, such as routine UTI management, standard lab interpretation, and⏎medication refill assessments. Higher-complexity presentations would require mandatory⏎escalation protocols to human clinicians. As evidence of safety, efficacy, and cost-effectiveness⏎accumulates, the scope of reimbursable autonomous encounters can expand accordingly.⏎There should be room in the market for reimbursement across the full spectrum from AI-⏎augmented care with clinical oversight to AI-delivered care with human escalation pathways.⏎Both models exist today; neither has a clear payment pathway.⏎Support CMMI pilots for subscription and PMPM models.⏎The always-on nature of AI-enabled care lends itself to subscription or per-member-per-month⏎models rather than visit-based billing. A patient may message an AI platform daily for chronic⏎disease support, which does not fit discrete encounter logic. Forcing it into per-visit billing⏎⏎creates perverse incentives. We recommend CMMI pilot a simple PMPM model for AI-enabled⏎primary care with straightforward attribution, creating an alternative pathway that matches how⏎these tools actually deliver value.⏎Long-term: Layer outcomes-based payment as evidence matures (Q2)⏎Attribution problems (i.e., isolating AI's contribution from human care interventions) are real, and⏎current measurement infrastructure cannot support them. Once near- and medium-term fee-for-⏎service codes generate sufficient claims data, outcomes-based models become viable. The⏎earlier phases build the data substrate that value-based care requires.⏎Accelerating adoption beyond payment reform (Q2, Q4, Q5)⏎State-level innovation is already underway (Q5). Utah's AI Learning Laboratory Program (in⏎which Doctronic is participating) has enabled autonomous AI care delivery with appropriate⏎oversight requirements.10 HHS can accelerate this by providing federal payment pathways that⏎reward successful state experimentation rather than requiring each state to build from scratch.⏎Additionally, the Rural Health Transformation Program (P.L. 119-21, § 71401) allocates $10⏎billion annually to states for rural health improvement, and the statute already contemplates⏎technology-enabled care delivery, including remote monitoring and AI. CMS should actively⏎encourage states to incorporate AI-enabled clinical care models into their transformation plans,⏎providing a funded pathway to test what this RFI envisions alongside the payment reforms⏎proposed above.⏎A note on evidence standards⏎Current evidence frameworks assume AI introduces risk that must be proven away; however,⏎AI-enabled care can be evaluated for 100% of encounters, compared to the small-sample chart⏎review that characterizes traditional quality assurance. This includes the ability to report⏎outcomes stratified by race, ethnicity, language, and disability status, which is critical for⏎ensuring that AI tools do not distort risk adjustment or produce disparate escalation patterns⏎across populations. HHS should develop evidence standards that account for this inversion in⏎which the AI-enabled model offers greater visibility into care quality, not less.⏎While payment ambiguity is the primary operational bottleneck, safety oversight and validation⏎standards are equally critical to sustainable adoption. Payment reform should proceed in⏎parallel with risk-tiered regulatory standards and evidence expectations.⏎10 Utah Department of Commerce, Office of Artificial Intelligence Policy. Utah and Doctronic Announce⏎Groundbreaking Partnership for AI Prescription Medication Renewals. January 6, 2026. Available at:⏎https://commerce.utah.gov/2026/01/06/news-release-utah-and-doctronic-announce-groundbreaking-⏎partnership-for-ai-prescription-medication-renewals/⏎⏎Veterans Affairs, Indian Health Service, CMMI, ARPA-H, and other HHS divisions each offer⏎distinct environments for validating AI-enabled care across diverse demographics, care settings,⏎and conditions. We recommend that HHS partner with these agencies to define what these⏎frameworks should look like, drawing on operational data from early adopters rather than⏎imposing untested requirements that could freeze the market (Q4).⏎Without Payment Reform, AI-Enabled Care Cannot Scale Through the⏎Healthcare System⏎The companies represented in this response will continue to operate regardless of HHS action.⏎Sufficient consumer demand exists to sustain direct-to-consumer business models, and⏎entrepreneurs will continue to build around the healthcare system if the system does not adapt.⏎Without HHS action, however, these gains will remain confined to cash-pay patients in metro⏎areas rather than reducing costs and expanding access system-wide, including to rural⏎communities where provider shortages make traditional models unsustainable.⏎The alternative is a fragmented, adversarial market that serves no stakeholder well. Health⏎systems will deploy AI under legacy fee‑for‑service rules and use it to dramatically increase visit⏎volume, capturing efficiency gains as revenue growth rather than lower total cost of care.⏎Payers, responding to rising utilization without a clear value or pricing framework, will refuse or⏎restrict reimbursement for AI‑enabled encounters. Fully autonomous AI care, which is already⏎operating in limited state sandboxes, will never progress beyond pilots because there is no⏎dedicated, nationally recognized payment pathway to support its adoption.⏎Meanwhile, patients are already turning to general-purpose consumer AI for health guidance.⏎Platforms like OpenAI’s ChatGPT and Anthropic’s Claude operate without HIPAA obligations or⏎clinical accountability, yet they are rapidly becoming the de facto source of health information for⏎millions of Americans. Consumers no longer need permission to ask about their healthcare. If⏎purpose-built clinical AI cannot scale through the healthcare system, general-purpose LLMs⏎without healthcare-specific obligations will fill the vacuum by default.⏎HHS has the tools to prevent this outcome. CMS's ACCESS model already points in the right⏎direction: its rates assume technology-enabled efficiency, creating a pathway for scaled digital⏎platforms rather than traditional fee-for-service operations.⏎Specifically, we recommend that CMS recognize asynchronous care as a billable modality⏎nationwide, create an AI-augmented modifier for existing E/M codes, expand APCM codes to⏎cover AI, establish billing codes for AI-autonomous clinical services, and pilot PMPM models⏎through CMMI. These payment pathways, designed to reimburse AI-enabled care at rates⏎below traditional equivalents, would reduce costs and expand access while generating the⏎claims data needed to support outcomes-based payment over time. We welcome the⏎opportunity to work with HHS to design and pilot these pathways.⏎⏎Appendix 1: Companies represented in this response⏎● August AI is an AI health companion used by 6M+ people across 160 countries to⏎interpret lab reports, understand symptoms, and get personalized health guidance via⏎WhatsApp.⏎● Doctronic is a multi-agent AI health platform that connects patients to personalized⏎guidance and licensed doctors in all 50 states.⏎● Summer Health is a text-based pediatric care platform that connects parents to licensed⏎pediatricians within 15 minutes, 24/7, for urgent care, developmental questions, and⏎prescriptions.⏎● Healthvana is an AI-powered telehealth platform for HIV prevention and sexual health.⏎Patients using their AI chatbot were 3x more likely to start PrEP and nearly 2x more⏎likely to stay on it.⏎● Counsel Health is a physician-led, AI-native virtual practice delivering asynchronous⏎messaging-based care.⏎Appendix 2: Summary of regulatory recommendations⏎Recommendation Current Barrier Proposed Change Regulation⏎Recognize Store-and-forward Amend to recognize 42 CFR §⏎asynchronous care reimbursement is limited asynchronous message-based 410.78(d)⏎as a billable to the Alaska and Hawaii clinical encounters as⏎modality demonstration programs reimbursable nationwide;⏎episode-based bundling (e.g., 7-⏎day window) rather than per-⏎message billing⏎Remove initial visit Asynchronous follow-up Allow async-first encounters when 42 CFR § 410.78⏎requirement for requires a prior video or identity verification, defined⏎async care in-person encounter clinical question, documented⏎clinical assessment, and⏎treatment recommendation or⏎escalation decision are in place⏎Create an AI- E/M codes carry time Establish modifier (similar to -93 42 CFR § 414.22⏎augmented care requirements that audio-only) at reduced rate (e.g.,⏎modifier penalize efficient AI- 80%); tied to service delivered,⏎assisted encounters; no not clinician minutes; requires⏎mechanism to bill AI- documentation of AI-assisted⏎augmented visits at an components, escalation protocols,⏎appropriate rate and clinician attestation⏎⏎Expand APCM for APCM requires Allow documented asynchronous CY 2025 PFS⏎AI-enabled care synchronous initiating onboarding; expand eligibility to (G0556-G0558;⏎visit, comprehensive condition-specific care 89 FR 97710)⏎PCP attribution, and a management; recognize platforms⏎single-practitioner billing with defined clinical governance⏎model and escalation protocols as billing⏎entities; accommodate⏎asynchronous follow-up for TCM's⏎post-discharge visit requirement⏎(CPT 99495, 99496)⏎Establish AI- No billing mechanism for Create new service category for 42 CFR § 410.32⏎autonomous billing encounters where AI AI-autonomous clinical services; (or new section)⏎codes delivers clinical services risk-tiered approach starting with⏎directly with human lower-acuity, protocol-driven⏎escalation protocols scenarios (UTI management, lab⏎interpretation, medication refills)⏎Pilot PMPM models Always-on AI care does CMMI pilot of per-member-per- CMMI authority⏎for AI-enabled care not fit discrete encounter month model for AI-enabled⏎billing logic primary care with straightforward⏎attribution♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0075♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Society of Retina Specialists, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Society of Retina Specialists♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Hon. Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: RIN 0955-AA13: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The American Society of Retina Specialists (ASRS) is the largest retina organization in the world,⏎representing over 3,500 board certified ophthalmologists who have completed fellowship⏎training in the medical and surgical treatment of retinal diseases. The mission of the ASRS is to⏎provide a collegial open forum for education, to advance the understanding and treatment of⏎vitreoretinal diseases, and to enhance the ability of its members to provide the highest quality⏎of patient care.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback on the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎Executive Summary⏎ASRS supports HHS’s goal of fostering a regulatory, payment, and research environment for⏎rapid, responsible adoption of AI in clinical care that improves patient outcomes and augments⏎the services retina specialists provide. For retina specialists, AI already demonstrates⏎meaningful potential for clinical value, particularly in image-based diagnoses and disease⏎monitoring. However, its broader adoption and impact are fundamentally constrained by⏎systemic barriers that HHS is uniquely positioned to address.⏎The most significant barrier to accelerating AI innovation and deployment is lack of⏎interoperable, standardized clinical and imaging data. This barrier is created by conflicting⏎proprietary data formats, incomplete implementation of existing standards, and misaligned⏎vendor incentives. These challenges limit competition, raise costs, slow research progression,⏎and reduce the real-world effectiveness of AI tools.⏎ASRS urges HHS to prioritize:⏎• Stronger enforcement and maturation of existing data standards, including Digital⏎Imaging and Communications in Medicine (DICOM), Fast Healthcare Interoperability⏎Resources (FHIR) and Observational Medical Outcomes Partnership Common Data⏎Model (OMOP);⏎⏎HHS AI RFI⏎ASRS Response p.2⏎• Payment policy modernization that recognizes AI as clinical infrastructure capable of⏎improving outcomes;⏎• Federal research and development oversight that promotes open, sharable data and⏎accelerates real-world implementation of validated AI tools;⏎o In particular, NIH should withdraw or significantly modify its proposed⏎Controlled-Data Access policy, which could have a chilling effect on the⏎dissemination of imaging data for clinical research.⏎• Preventing private payer use of AI to deny care through prior authorization or recoup⏎payments to physicians through auditing.⏎By aligning policies around interoperability and value, HHS can unlock AI’s full potential to⏎improve patient outcomes, not only for those being cared for by retina specialists, but across⏎medicine. Our full comments and responses to selected, specific questions from the RFI are⏎below.⏎Background⏎Retina specialists are board-certified ophthalmologists who have completed an additional 1–2-⏎year fellowship focusing on the medical and surgical care of the vitreous body of the eye and⏎the retina. This includes surgical care for conditions such as retinal detachment and chronic care⏎management for diseases such as age-related macular degeneration (AMD), diabetic⏎retinopathy, retinal vein occlusion, and inherited retinal diseases. Retina specialists have⏎pioneered treatments for these diseases that include injectable medications, surgical⏎techniques, and advanced imaging. Without these advances, millions of Americans would go⏎blind every year.⏎In the spirit of that innovation, the first AI-enabled medical device cleared by the Food and Drug⏎Administration (FDA) is used for screening for diabetic retinopathy. Retina specialists are eager⏎to continue that innovation and harness AI’s potential to speed access to new cures and⏎treatment protocols. AI-assisted analyses of retinal and ocular data hold promise for diagnosing⏎and treating systemic disease, ranging from cardiovascular to autoimmune to⏎neurodegenerative diseases. To meet this opportunity, ASRS has created an AI Task Force⏎charged with identifying key opportunities to integrate AI into retinal care and providing⏎leadership with guidance on the society’s role in fostering it. This comment letter is based on⏎the task force’s guidance.⏎Lack of Standardized Data⏎The ASRS AI Task force has identified the greatest regulatory challenge facing AI adoption in⏎retinal care as a lack of standardization to facilitate data access and interoperability. Numerous⏎data standardization criteria and formats exist—among them FHIR, DICOM and OMOP—but⏎there is a gap between their existence and their implementation making it nearly unworkable⏎⏎HHS AI RFI⏎ASRS Response p.3⏎for retina specialists and researchers to create large, aggregated, high-quality datasets essential⏎for robust and generalizable AI.⏎At the heart of this disconnect in retina, and ophthalmology as a whole, is the widespread use⏎of proprietary imaging formats and incomplete implementation of existing standards by device⏎manufacturers. For example, advanced imaging devices, such as optical coherence tomography⏎(OCT), fundus cameras, and automated visual field analyzers often store data in proprietary⏎formats or, even when using standardized formats, fail to standardize the reporting of crucial⏎metrics, such as central subfield thickness, geographic atrophy (GA) lesion size, and subretinal⏎fluid volume quantification.⏎Beyond devices, non-structured or non-standardized data extracted from EHRs lack the⏎common nomenclature and coding necessary for reliable dataset aggregation. Clinical metrics,⏎such as visual acuity (VA), lack standardized definitions across systems.⏎ASRS is advocating to vendors and manufacturers that their proprietary and siloed approach to⏎storing and sharing clinical data (where data is held in isolated, non-interoperable systems that⏎prevent it from being shared or aggregated) is hampering needed innovation. There are private⏎companies and programmers currently engaged in efforts to develop systems that will bridge⏎this gap by converting the proprietary data outputs into standardized formats If HHS were to⏎echo the need for standardization, this crucial message would be greatly amplified, giving⏎vendors and manufactures more incentive to make their products and data interoperable.⏎ASRS recommends that HHS enforce and strengthen existing data standards for EHRs, as well as⏎imaging and diagnostic equipment manufacturers, to ensure that retina specialists and⏎developers can harness and aggregate the data to advance patient care and develop new⏎treatments and cures.⏎We recommend HHS identify and create incentives for vendors and manufacturers to⏎accomplish this, rather than implement punitive measures that would likely cause these⏎companies to pass on significant costs to retina specialists and other end users, increasing costs⏎across the system. Without addressing this underlying data bottleneck, HHS will not be able to⏎achieve its goals of advancing AI in clinical care.⏎Barriers to Implementation in Physician Practices⏎As data sets become more standardized and retina specialists and other physicians begin⏎integrating more AI-enabled or assisted technologies into practice, it is imperative that payment⏎systems, particularly Medicare, adapt to meet these changes and keep practices viable.⏎Implementing AI tools in an independent practice may require a significant initial capital outlay⏎and their ongoing use could create additional work for physicians as they analyze additional⏎data and consider differential diagnoses current tools and equipment do not provide. Current⏎reimbursement structures for physician work and practice expense are not designed to reflect⏎these emerging technologies. HHS should collaborate with the medical community to prevent⏎⏎HHS AI RFI⏎ASRS Response p.4⏎promising technology from failing because physicians and other providers cannot make a⏎financial case for it.⏎This inadequate reimbursement barrier is already coming to fruition. Medicare has struggled⏎over the last several years to develop a consistent strategy to code for and value AI-enabled⏎clinical tools. For example, the AI-enabled diabetic retinopathy screening device mentioned⏎above has seen limited uptake by physician practices because the current system lacks a viable⏎payment pathway for its use. Because the device makes the initial diagnosis, Medicare assigns⏎no physician work to the CPT code for the service (92229) and only reimburses for the practice⏎expense of the nurse or technician who assists the patient at the terminal. Primary care⏎physicians, who would be most likely to purchase the device to ensure diabetic patients can⏎receive timely referrals to retina specialists, have no way of covering the initial cost of the⏎device, and CMS has been reluctant to reimburse for equipment start-up costs. Without any⏎incentive to implement the technology, patients—and the Medicare system—do not have the⏎opportunity to benefit from lower treatment burden and cost of care when their disease is⏎caught early.⏎As the basis for coding and valuation across all payers, the Medicare payment system must be⏎modified to account for AI in a way that encourages physicians to adopt new technologies or⏎care patterns that can improve outcomes without financially penalizing the physician or⏎provider. We strongly recommend HHS and the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services⏎(CMS) work with the physician community to develop a framework for valuing physician work⏎and practice expense when using AI technologies in direct patient care or supporting clinical and⏎administrative work. A potential starting point for retina would be to consider a set of billing⏎modifier codes for using AI to help interpret images, similar to those used by radiology when⏎the physician is using computer-aided detection (CADx) tools.⏎If CMS fails to develop a workable framework, it is likely private payers will develop their own⏎systems, leading to fragmentation across the healthcare system and likely inadequate physician⏎reimbursement. By providing adequate reimbursement, HHS can assist physicians and providers⏎adopt and continue to use these technologies. Without it, HHS will not meet its goal of⏎improving patient outcomes.⏎Clinical Research⏎ASRS’ AI Task Force has identified the above recommendation of data standardization as critical⏎for clinical research. By enabling AI to aggregate and analyze a broad set of standardized clinical⏎data, retina specialists and researchers may unlock even more answers to better treating, curing⏎or preventing blinding retinal disease. With this analytic capability, retina specialists will align⏎with this administration’s goals for a healthier America where retina patients experience less⏎treatment burden, rely less on caregivers, and remain productive members of the economy.⏎ASRS is pleased HHS is prioritizing AI for its role in advanced clinical research.⏎⏎HHS AI RFI⏎ASRS Response p.5⏎Opposition to Proposed NIH Controlled-Data Access Policy⏎ASRS is concerned, however, that HHS has not fully integrated this strategy across all its⏎agencies or contemplated how it will interact with other simultaneous initiatives. In particular,⏎we are greatly concerned about the National Institutes of Health’s (NIH) December 2025 RFI⏎on the proposed Controlled-Access Data Policy and its potential chilling effect on imaging-⏎based clinical research and AI development, particularly in retina. While ASRS strongly⏎supports appropriate protections for human participant data, the draft policy’s broad treatment⏎of personal health data and imaging modalities risks unintentionally restricting responsible data⏎sharing that has historically driven innovation. Retinal imaging—including fundus photography,⏎OCT/OCTA, and ocular ultrasound—can, in certain contexts, become re-identifiable through⏎metadata, rare diagnoses, or linkage with other datasets. However, mandating that all such⏎imaging require controlled access will detrimentally slow research progress.⏎Of particular concern is NIH’s explicit statement that user registration alone does not constitute⏎controlled access. Many NIH-funded investigators currently share research-grade retinal image⏎datasets through professional society portals such as ASRS’s Retina Image Bank; despite enacted⏎robust data use requirements, they do not meet the proposed full technical requirements of⏎NIH-approved controlled-access. Requiring universal controlled-access could have the⏎unintended consequence of preventing the development and implementation of new AI-⏎enabled technologies HHS seeks in this RFI. ASRS will also be commenting on NIH’s RFI, but⏎given its relationship to this topic, we recommend that HHS provide oversight of the new policy⏎and work with professional societies and other institutions to develop a more workable, risk-⏎based policy for sharing potentially identifiable data.⏎Oversight of Private Payers⏎ASRS is also concerned about the use of AI by commercial payers for physician auditing and⏎prior authorization—often in ways that lack transparency, clinical nuance, or meaningful human⏎oversight. In retina care, where treatment decisions are frequently time-sensitive, payer-⏎deployed AI tools risk denying medically necessary care, delaying treatment, and undermining⏎the physician–patient relationship. ASRS has long-standing opposition to payer utilization⏎management tactics, such as prior authorization and step therapy, that nearly every retina⏎patient, except those with original Medicare Part B, must endure. Without clear guardrails from⏎HHS on the use of AI in utilization management, private payers will likely significantly increase⏎their use of them. This will ultimately limit medically necessary care for patients and further add⏎to already significant physician administrative burden.⏎Research bears out patients’ concern with these potential tactics. A recently published study⏎found that of 291 retina patients surveyed on a 5-point Likert scale, the mean response was 4.2⏎when asked whether they agreed patients should be given the choice when AI tools are used. In⏎⏎HHS AI RFI⏎ASRS Response p.6⏎addition, both patients and retina specialists disagreed with the notion of trusting AI tools over⏎the clinical judgement of a physician.1⏎ASRS urges HHS to exercise oversight authority and restrict or prohibit the use of AI by payers⏎for prior authorization and punitive physician auditing, particularly when such tools operate⏎without transparency, appeal rights, or accountability for clinical harm.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎Questions 1&2: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care? What should HHS prioritize to overcome them?⏎As noted above, the single largest barrier to AI innovation is lack of interoperable, standardized⏎clinical and imaging data. The main contributors to this barrier are proprietary ophthalmic⏎imaging formats that limit reuse and secondary analysis; inconsistent or absent standards for⏎key clinical variables (e.g., visual acuity); and high costs and contractual barriers to extracting⏎data from EHRs and imaging platforms.⏎HHS should prioritize incentives for vendors that encourage full adoption of already existing⏎interoperable standards. As a specific example, HHS should not permit imaging vendors to state⏎they have adopted DICOM simply if they have file transfer capabilities. To be considered⏎compliant with DICOM, imaging equipment must have:⏎• Equivalence in Imaging Storage: Images viewed for clinical care on proprietary⏎viewing systems should be stored identically in the PixelArray in DICOM.⏎• Standardized Reporting: Mandatory and uniform reporting of specific, quantitative⏎metrics (e.g., GA lesion size, fluid quantification) using established DICOM Structured⏎Reporting templates across all imaging platforms.⏎• Open API for Extracted Metrics: Imaging devices must provide a non-proprietary export⏎interface for “outside” AI algorithms to securely access and utilize standardized,⏎extracted findings without needing to process raw images themselves.⏎In addition, HHS should enforce FHIR and OMOP standards for clinical data. HHS should⏎encourage the development of standardized clinical terminology, such as for eye exam findings⏎across all EHRs. It should continue to support full and robust implementation of FHIR standards⏎for all relevant ophthalmic data elements, including a consensus standard for visual acuity (VA).⏎1 Garcia, E, et al. “Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Care: Perceptions of Retina Specialists and Patients Gathered⏎Through a Multicenter Survey,” Journal of Vitreoretinal Disease; Vol.10, i 1,⏎https://journals.sagepub.com/doi/10.1177/24741264251381990⏎⏎HHS AI RFI⏎ASRS Response p.7⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎ASRS shares the concerns articulated by the American Medical Association (AMA) regarding⏎physician liability related to the use of AI in clinical care. The introduction of AI into clinical⏎workflows should not create expanded malpractice exposure for physicians beyond existing⏎standards of care, nor should physicians be held accountable for errors attributable to algorithm⏎design, training data deficiencies, or vendor-controlled system updates. While current AI tools⏎may assume liability for errors, physicians and patients need to understand the medicolegal⏎implications of these new technologies.⏎HHS should promote a clear, risk-aligned liability framework that assigns responsibility to the⏎party best positioned to manage and mitigate harm. Developers and vendors of clinical AI⏎systems—particularly those with autonomous or semi-autonomous functions—should bear⏎appropriate responsibility for system performance, including maintenance, updates, and known⏎limitations. Payers who use AI-driven utilization management should bear responsibility for any⏎harm they cause. Physicians should retain clinical judgment and oversight, but should not be⏎penalized for reasonable reliance on validated tools, nor for choosing not to use AI where⏎standards of care remain unsettled. HHS guidance clarifying these principles would reduce⏎adoption barriers, support responsible innovation, and help preserve the physician–patient⏎relationship as AI becomes more widely used.⏎Furthermore, physicians must not be held liable for harm resulting from private payer utilization⏎management decisions—such as prior authorization or step therapy—which may increasingly⏎be "super-charged" by AI to prioritize cost over what the physician determines is most⏎appropriate for the patient. Consequently, HHS should promote a risk-aligned framework where⏎developers, vendors, and payers bear responsibility for the performance and decisions of the⏎systems they control, ensuring physicians are not penalized for the "blind reliance" on, or the⏎systemic failures of, validated tools.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you again for the opportunity to provide comments on the Administration’s goals for⏎integrating AI into clinical care. ASRS stands ready with HHS to promote the development and⏎full implementation of standardized clinical and imaging data to speed access to AI-enabled⏎technology, while also ensuring physician practices remain viable and patients retain access to⏎the care their physicians prescribe. If you have questions or need additional information, please⏎contact Allison Madson, vice president of health policy, at allison.madson@asrs.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Geoffrey G. Emerson, MD, PhD, FASRS⏎President, ASRS♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0076♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Williams♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Mia was misdiagnosed twice before we got to a specialist at UW Health in Madison who finally figured out she had an autoimmune condition. That was in 2019 and I still think about those first two doctors, what they missed, why they missed it. It wasn't that they lacked information. They had her bloodwork, her history. What the specialist did differently was sit with her for almost an hour and ask questions nobody had thought to ask yet. I don't know how you put that into software.⏎⏎That's what keeps coming to mind when I read about HHS wanting to speed up AI adoption in clinical settings. I understand the appeal. Faster answers, fewer bottlenecks, maybe catching things that get missed. But what got missed with Mia wasn't a data point. It was more like a pattern that only emerged through a conversation, through her describing symptoms in a certain order, through a doctor who was paying attention in a way that went beyond the chart.⏎⏎What I want to ask HHS to think about, and I'm not sure exactly which question in this request covers this, is the accountability piece. Right now if a physician makes a call and it's wrong, there is a person who is responsible. You can go back to them. You can ask them why. Even if the answer is unsatisfying, there is a human being who made a judgment and is answerable for it. I worry that as AI gets more involved in diagnosis or treatment recommendations, that accountability gets diffused to the point where nobody is really responsible. The software vendor says the doctor should have caught it. The doctor says the software flagged it as low risk. And the patient is just stuck.⏎⏎I'm also not sure the people designing these systems have a full picture of what actually happens during a diagnostic workup when something is genuinely hard to figure out. Mia's case wasn't complicated by any single missing test result. It was complicated because her symptoms overlapped with three or four other conditions and the presentation was atypical. The specialist told us he almost went in a different direction but something she said offhand changed his thinking. I cannot imagine an algorithm catching that. Maybe I'm wrong. But I'd want to see a lot of evidence before I believed it.⏎⏎The reimbursement side of this is something I don't have a lot of expertise in, but it seems like if you build payment structures that reward speed and throughput you might be pushing in exactly the wrong direction for cases like Mia's. Her case needed time. It needed someone willing to sit with uncertainty for a little while instead of just picking the most statistically likely answer and moving on.⏎⏎I guess what I'm asking is that whatever HHS decides to do here, there be a real requirement that a physician who actually knows the patient remains the decision-maker, not just a cosigner on whatever the AI produced. And that there's some clear process for patients and families to understand whether AI was involved in a recommendation and to ask questions about it. We never got asked whether Mia's records could be used for anything, and they're sitting in at least four different systems now that as far as I know have never been connected or reconciled with each other, which seems like its own problem worth♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0077♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Foundation for the Blind, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Foundation for the Blind♔♔♔♔♔♔www.afb.org | 212.502.7600⏎2900 S. Quincy St, Ste. 200⏎Arlington, VA 22206⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Filed via regulations.gov⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎The American Foundation for the Blind (AFB) is a national nonprofit that creates equal⏎opportunities and expands possibilities for people who are blind, have low vision, and⏎are deafblind through advocacy, thought leadership, and strategic partnerships.⏎In 2025, AFB conducted a nationwide survey of 1,735 adults, of whom 1,070 have a⏎disability or medical condition, investigating their experiences, hopes and risks with⏎regard to Artificial Intelligence (AI). The results of the study will be released in March⏎2026, but many of the pertinent findings for healthcare are described below. We aim to⏎answer Question 10: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers⏎wish to see addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what⏎concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in⏎clinical care?⏎Critically, AI used in healthcare must be developed in a way that avoids unfair treatment⏎of people with disabilities. In our AI research, survey participants with disabilities were⏎almost three times as likely to report a healthcare denial within the past two years as⏎participants without disabilities (27.7% vs. 10.8%). The most common type of denial⏎was health insurance denial. Although the research was not able to verify the actual⏎⏎cause of the denials, 47% of disabled and 35% of nondisabled participants stated that⏎they suspected AI was involved in the denial.⏎The disproportionate rate of denials for disabled people means that there is a need to⏎systemically ensure that AI used in decision making does not deny care to people with⏎disabilities. There is both an existing legal obligation to ensure that technology used in⏎healthcare does not discriminate on the basis of disability and a looming trust issue if⏎patients assume that any denial is attributable to AI. It is concerning that there is often⏎no or limited transparency about when AI is being used, so patients may not fully⏎understand a denial decision. In addition, training data and decision parameters must⏎be inclusive of people with disabilities with a wide range of health conditions and care⏎needs. HHS has an oversight and enforcement role to play to ensure that these⏎systems are being deployed responsibly. Actively addressing disproportionate⏎healthcare denials and other instances of bias will also ensure that patients trust the⏎healthcare system to deliver the care they need in a timely manner.⏎Participants were asked about the use of AI for mental health therapy. AI therapy could⏎reduce some barriers to care (such as cost or provider availability), but it also presents⏎significant risk of harm to people with disabilities if it is not appropriately trained.⏎Although relatively few participants in our study had used AI for therapy, there were⏎several reports of harm. Some of these reports were relatively minor, such as chatbots⏎offering surface level or mismatched responses. However, four participants reported⏎more significant harms, such as clinically inappropriate advice (e.g. suggesting dieting⏎to a person with an eating disorder), validation of psychotic delusions or suicidal⏎thinking, and exacerbating crisis situations. One participant described a distressing⏎experience when the chatbot suggested that she consider the death-positivity⏎movement after venting about a chronic health condition. These cases are especially⏎concerning, but among all users of AI for therapy, only 29% thought that AI could be⏎more helpful than human therapy. There is a need for ensuring that AI used in care,⏎including mental healthcare, is trained on appropriate clinical decision-making, that it⏎can provide appropriate disability awareness, and that guardrails are in place to avoid⏎facilitating or encouraging self-harm.⏎2⏎⏎From an accessibility perspective, AI presents real potential to expand access to⏎information and communication for people with disabilities. However, given the⏎importance of accurate communication in a highly specialized healthcare setting, great⏎consideration should be given to deploying AI to resolve communication barriers.⏎Among our study participants who used AI captions, only 4% rated captions as⏎extremely accurate. 7.5% of people who use AI to describe images or the visual⏎environment find it to be extremely accurate. In a healthcare setting, mistakes can be⏎dangerous. One participant described a situation that made her realize that she couldn’t⏎rely on AI in situations that require a high degree of accuracy:⏎“[I was] using AI to read package instructions on a tube of topical⏎medication and when I asked what the directions for use were specifically,⏎AI told me to ‘chew 4 tablets 2 times per day.’ Luckily this was an obvious⏎error, but for now I will NOT be using AI for these tasks [and] will always⏎confirm with a human.”⏎AI may one day be accurate enough to provide communication assistance in lieu of a⏎human interpreter, captioner, or reading assistant, but it is vitally important that the⏎vendors of such tools are held to high quality standards.⏎Another area where our findings significantly implicate the health care system is⏎concern about privacy and data protections. Although patient information is legally⏎required to be protected, individuals are largely concerned about whether AI systems⏎actually can protect their sensitive information. Participants were presented with several⏎situations in which they were asked whether they would prefer to rely on a human or AI⏎for assistance. In both cases, participants were significantly more likely to prefer a⏎human over AI if their sensitive information was involved. In addition, participants⏎believed that AI is somewhat less private than working with a human: 18% stated that AI⏎is much less private than humans, and 37% stated that AI is slightly less private than⏎humans. Another 29% stated that AI and humans are equally private. Only 16% stated⏎that AI is somewhat or much more private than humans. Participants also reported a⏎general preference for privacy over using AI to achieve efficiency and independence, or⏎they reported that both are equally important. They expressed a desire for transparency,⏎control of data, consent, and stronger guardrails to protect privacy and data security.⏎3⏎⏎Altogether, the findings suggest that disabled people, as well as nondisabled people,⏎desire that AI be thoughtfully and carefully designed and deployed. There are certain⏎cases in which AI can increase efficiency for providers and improve independence for⏎individuals and their families. However, there is already growing evidence that AI⏎developers and deployers must ensure that AI is accurate, does not disadvantage⏎certain groups, and is trained on inclusive data. Personal data must be safely stored,⏎deleted as soon as practicable, or processed on-device where appropriate. Rigorous⏎data privacy protections should be expected in health use cases. Users should be⏎informed when AI is being used and provided an opportunity to control how their data is⏎stored or shared. HHS should oversee the development and deployment of AI to ensure⏎that healthcare use cases are clinically appropriate and ensure that no group of people⏎faces disproportionate denials of care. HHS should invest in research to improve the⏎data and model validation tools and practices used by health AI companies. By⏎developing and requiring the use of guardrails protecting people with disabilities, HHS⏎can ensure that the onus does not fall solely to deployers of AI to understand its effect⏎on patients.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to respond to this RFI. If you have any questions about⏎this issue, please contact Sarah Malaier, smalaier@afb.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Stephanie Enyart⏎Chief Public Policy and Research Officer⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0078♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cook Group, Inc. , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Cook Group, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔COOK GROUP INCORPORATED⏎⏎750 DANIELS WAY,⏎⏎P.O. BOX 1608⏎⏎BLOOMINGTON, IN 47402-1608 U.S.A.⏎PHONE: 812.331.1025 Fax: 812.331.8990⏎WWW.COOKGROUP.COM⏎⏎February 23, 2026⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and⏎⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Attention: Request for Information:⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎⏎Mary E. Switzer building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎⏎330 C Street SW⏎⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎⏎RE: Docket No. RIN 0955-AA13 -- Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎⏎Dear Sir or Madam:⏎⏎We submit these comments on behalf of Cook Group Inc. (“Cook”). Cook is a family-⏎owned group of domestic and international corporations engaged in the manufacture of⏎diagnostic and therapeutic products for use in various medical specialties including⏎interventional radiology, cardiology, vascular surgery, critical care, gastroenterology,⏎urology and surgery. We are driven by medical innovation that benefits patients and by⏎the deep connections we build to support the communities in which we operate. Our⏎company employs about 12,000 people around the world. Eight thousand of those⏎employees are based in the United States and while more than 53 percent of our sales are⏎used outside the United States, approximately 70 percent of our products are⏎manufactured in this country.⏎⏎We thank the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) for the opportunity to⏎provide comments on this health sector Artificial Intelligence (AI) request for⏎information. The Department is seeking public feedback on the actions it can take to⏎establish a forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the⏎adoption and use of Al as part of clinical care. Further, HHS seeks feedback on ways in⏎which adoption of AI in clinical care can be most effectively applied to support public⏎trust and confidence in modern technology solutions, to reduce uncertainty that impedes⏎Al innovation, and to align federal incentives so that AI is deployed in transparent ways⏎that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower health care costs, and improve health⏎outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities.⏎⏎Artificial intelligence represents a significant opportunity to advance medical⏎technology innovation and improve patient access to safe and effective care. Across⏎the medical device lifecycle, AI has the potential to accelerate design and development,⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Docket No. RIN0955-AA13⏎February 23, 2026⏎⏎enhance diagnostic accuracy, enable more personalized therapeutic solutions, and support⏎more efficient quality clinical decision-making. In clinical practice, Al-enabled⏎technologies are already demonstrating measurable value—for example, advanced⏎interventional MRI (MRI) systems that incorporate AlI-driven image analysis techniques⏎may support the identification of smaller or earlier-stage lesions, establish accurate⏎procedural precision and enable more timely interventions, driving better patient⏎outcomes and efficiency in care delivery. When responsibly developed and deployed, AI⏎can help translate innovation into real-world clinical benefit for patients while⏎strengthening safety, quality, and system performance.⏎⏎Regulatory Frameworks⏎Access to high-quality, interoperable clinical data is foundational to effective Al⏎⏎development and deployment. HHS should promote policies that enable secure data⏎sharing and interoperability while maintaining strong, practical privacy protections under⏎HIPAA. Overly prescriptive, duplicative or rigid privacy controls risk limiting the⏎availability of data needed to train, validate, and monitor AI systems. A balanced⏎regulatory framework is essential to supporting responsible data use while⏎accelerating innovation and preserving patient trust.⏎⏎Payment and Incentives⏎We support payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive and ability to⏎⏎promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical⏎care AI tool builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical⏎care.⏎⏎Current reimbursement models often fail to recognize the value created by Al-enabled⏎clinical care, particularly where AI improves outcomes, efficiency, or clinician workload⏎without increasing service volume. HHS should explore reimbursement pathways that⏎reward demonstrated value, including new or updated codes, bundled payments, or value-⏎based payment models that account for Al-driven improvements. Aligning⏎reimbursement with innovation incentives is critical to scaling AI adoption across health⏎care systems.⏎⏎Research, Evidence and Learning Systems⏎Recognizing that HHS supports one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems,⏎⏎HHS should support structured public-private partnerships to generate real-world⏎evidence on AI performance, safety, and equity impacts. HHS should invest in federated⏎research networks, shared testbeds, and pilot programs which would enable scalable⏎evidence generation and development while protecting proprietary intellectual property.⏎These public stakeholder collaborations would accelerate learning, support responsible⏎adoption, and provide regulators and providers with credible, real-world insights into AI⏎effectiveness.⏎⏎Sea a ak ak ake ae ak ake ak oe ake ak ok ake ok⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Docket No. RIN0955-AA13⏎February 23, 2026⏎⏎Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to meaningfully improve patient care,⏎enhance safety, and strengthen clinical and regulatory decision-making across the health⏎care system. Realizing this potential depends not on replacing existing regulatory or⏎clinical frameworks, but on modernizing the data and evidence infrastructure that⏎supports them.⏎⏎The recommendations outlined below are not intended to shortcut FDA review,⏎weaken standards for medical device quality, or displace the role of clinicians,⏎regulators, or other professionals entrusted with protecting patients. Rather, they are⏎intended to enable existing oversight frameworks to function more effectively in an⏎increasingly data-driven health care environment.⏎⏎From our experience across the medical technology ecosystem, there are three⏎interrelated areas where improved interoperability and policy alignment are⏎essential to support responsible AI adoption in health care: 1) adoption of the medical⏎device Unique Device Identifier (UDI) in data capture, 2) use of real-world evidence⏎(RWE) for regulatory decision-making, 3) timely, evidence-aligned coverage and⏎payment decisions, and 4) governance, accountability and liability clarity.⏎⏎1. Adoption of UDI Data Capture by Health Care Providers and CMS⏎⏎e UDI capture is foundational to trustworthy AI in health care. Improved⏎interoperability can enable reliable device-level evidence through structured UDI⏎capture within electronic health records, registries, and claims systems. While⏎UDI requirements are well established at the point of device labeling, downstream⏎capture within clinical systems and administrative data remains inconsistent.⏎⏎e UDI data capture is particularly important for higher-risk devices used across⏎diverse patient populations and care settings.⏎⏎2. Use of Real-World Evidence for Regulatory Decision-Making⏎⏎e Real-world evidence can complement traditional regulatory pathways when data⏎are relevant and reliable. Use of real-world evidence does not reduce regulatory⏎rigor; it supports decisions grounded in real-world clinical performance.⏎⏎e Interoperability enables scalable, representative evidence generation across⏎clinical records, imaging systems, registries, and administrative data sources.⏎⏎e Al-enabled evidence generation can also serve existing FDA programs designed⏎to expedite patient access to transformative technologies. For example, the FDA’s⏎Breakthrough Devices Program provides an established framework for early⏎engagement and iterative evidence development for devices addressing unmet⏎medical needs.⏎⏎e Al-supported real-world data analysis can strengthen the Breakthrough Devices⏎Program by enabling more efficient evidence generation, supporting postmarket⏎learning, and accelerating the translation of technological innovations into clinical⏎practice without compromising regulatory rigor.⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Docket No. RIN0955-AA13⏎February 23, 2026⏎⏎Al-enabled analytics can support lifecycle-based regulatory oversight through⏎early signal detection and performance monitoring.⏎⏎Use of real-world evidence does not reduce regulatory rigor; it supports decisions⏎grounded in real-world clinical performance.⏎⏎3. Enable Timely, Evidence-Aligned Coverage and Payment Decisions⏎⏎Misalignment between regulatory evidence, coverage decisions, and payment⏎policy is a known limitation of our current systems that often delay by several⏎years patient access to beneficial technologies.⏎⏎Enhanced interoperability and reliance on real-world evidence can support more⏎timely and informed coverage and reimbursement decisions while maintaining the⏎highest standards of safety, quality, and effectiveness.⏎⏎Our shared objective as a health care ecosystem should be to ensure that⏎beneficial technologies reach patients in time—not only to improve quality of⏎life and manage chronic disease, but in many cases to save lives—while⏎preserving the rigor, accountability, and trust that underpin public confidence in⏎medical innovation.⏎⏎4. Governance, Accountability, and Liability Clarity⏎⏎Ongoing uncertainty around responsibility for Al-enabled tools across⏎developers, deploying organizations, and clinicians continues to slow responsible⏎adoption. HHS can support innovation and patient safety by issuing practical⏎governance guidance that clarifies accountability across the AI lifecycle, rather⏎than creating new liability regimes.⏎⏎Ye be se le se she she she se se se se se see ee wt⏎⏎Effective oversight of artificial intelligence must enable innovation while ensuring patient⏎safety, public trust, and regulatory clarity across the health care ecosystem.⏎⏎Appropriate Regulation⏎⏎Al regulation should focus on the specific use and application of AJ rather than⏎the underlying technology itself. Oversight should be risk-based, proportionate to⏎potential patient impact, and capable of adapting over time as technologies and⏎clinical use evolve.⏎⏎United States Leadership in AI⏎⏎The U.S. should lead the world in responsible Al development by establishing a⏎clear, coherent federal regulatory framework that supports innovation and can be⏎harmonized internationally. Global alignment is critical to advancing patient⏎access, fostering innovation, and avoiding conflicting requirements. A federal⏎approach to AI oversight in health care is essential to avoid a fragmented⏎regulatory landscape that could impede innovation and limit patient access.⏎Inconsistent or conflicting state-level requirements risk creating uncertainty,⏎duplicative compliance obligations, and uneven adoption across the health⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Docket No. RIN0955-AA13⏎February 23, 2026⏎⏎system. Federal standards that provide clarity, consistency, and appropriate⏎preemption in areas of national importance will help ensure that Al-enabled⏎medical technologies can be developed, evaluated, and deployed responsibly at⏎scale, while preserving patient protections and public trust.⏎⏎e Health Care AI Alignment⏎Regulation of AI in medical technology and health care applications should align⏎with existing FDA, ISO, and other established regulatory standards to prevent⏎unnecessary duplication and compliance burden. Oversight should remain focused⏎on improving patient outcomes, patient safety, and access to care, while ensuring⏎protection of personal health information consistent with HIPAA requirements.⏎⏎e Transparency and Trust⏎Transparency should enhance public trust by supporting appropriate validation,⏎monitoring, and accountability of Al-enabled systems, while protecting⏎proprietary information and trade secrets. Transparency requirements should be⏎designed to avoid unnecessary compliance burdens that could impede innovation⏎without improving patient safety.⏎⏎Thank you for issuing this request for information on this topic and for providing an⏎opportunity to comment on this important public health matter.⏎⏎Respectf / AE⏎We Ferguson J⏎⏎S⏎Chairman of the Board♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0079♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Joint Commission, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joint Commission♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, M.D., M.B.A.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health IT⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎7033A, 330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎[Submitted electronically via regulations.gov]⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Joint Commission appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎Founded in 1951, Joint Commission’s mission is to enable and affirm the highest standards of⏎healthcare quality and patient safety for all. An independent, not-for-profit organization with a⏎global presence, Joint Commission has programs that accredit or certify healthcare organizations⏎and programs in the United States across the continuum of care, including most of the nation’s⏎hospitals. A variety of federal and state government regulatory bodies, including the Centers for⏎Medicare and Medicaid Services, recognize and rely upon Joint Commission’s findings for⏎Medicare or licensure purposes.⏎Joint Commission offers the following specific comments on Question 5: How can HHS best⏎support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing,⏎and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎As the number of healthcare integrated AI tools continues to proliferate, health systems are using⏎these tools in a variety of ways to enhance the quality of clinical care, such as to diagnose⏎disease earlier, identify patients who may need follow up, and personalize treatment plans.⏎However, these advances bear some amount of risk. The increased reliance on large datasets also⏎increases the possibility of a privacy breach, and algorithms may still yield false outcomes,⏎leading to possible misdiagnosis and, ultimately, patient harm. Currently, there is no systematic⏎mechanism to evaluate adverse outcomes and other critical AI tool monitoring needs. Providing⏎a systematic approach to implementation and evaluation of tools is a critical driver for⏎⏎Joint Commission Comments on HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎accountability and safety within a healthcare organization, allowing agile innovation without⏎hampering the use and integration of AI tools. A healthcare sector framework could also⏎promote learning by establishing best practices for information sharing, including monitoring⏎adverse outcomes.⏎While the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has provided oversight on the subset of AI⏎tools falling under the medical device classification, there are a wide array of AI tools that might⏎be in use across a healthcare system, many of which exist outside of FDA’s jurisdiction. Other⏎federal agencies could play a role as well. However, federal administrative processes and⏎regulatory burden could stifle innovation and adoption. A few states have introduced regulations⏎around health AI tools. State oversight, though, would mean that AI developers and multi-state⏎healthcare systems will need to navigate 50 possible regulations, which too would hinder⏎innovation. Therefore, other organizations will need to play a role to validate that AI is⏎responsibly deployed at the healthcare delivery level. For example, healthcare organizations will⏎need to have policies to ensure that AI tools are still providing accurate outcomes over time,⏎which may require updates or input from developers to prevent algorithmic drift while still⏎enabling learning AI tools.⏎Accreditation Organizations (AOs) are well-positioned to play a role in supporting⏎healthcare organizations with developing policies and procedures around the⏎implementation of innovative and effective AI tools in clinical care.⏎Joint Commission works with 24,000 healthcare organizations across the continuum of care to⏎review policies and procedures covering a range of critical safety areas to guide the safe delivery⏎of healthcare. Over the last several years, we have heard from many of our healthcare⏎organizations who were seeking a framework to implement AI tools and from organizations⏎already using AI tools interested in a framework and a third-party validator to confirm that they⏎incorporated policies to address key patient safety concerns. To help create guideposts, Joint⏎Commission convened two meetings to gather input from across the healthcare field, including⏎patient organizations, community providers, health systems, and health policy experts, to⏎identify key elements of the responsible use of AI in a healthcare framework.⏎This learning process was invaluable and, ultimately, led to the release of guidance on⏎Responsible Use of AI in Healthcare (RUAIH),1 which is designed to help healthcare⏎organizations, particularly smaller and rural hospitals, establish clear policies and procedures to⏎use AI tools safely while supporting effective integration into care delivery, including the need⏎for adequate technical and human resources, risk assessment, data management, and ongoing⏎monitoring and updates to ensure safety throughout an algorithmic lifetime.2 The guidance⏎1 The Responsible Use of AI in Healthcare (RUAIH), JOINT COMMISSION (Sept. 2025),⏎https://digitalassets.jointcommission.org/api/public/content/dcfcf4f1a0cc45cdb526b3cb034c68c2.⏎2 Joint Commission has already operationalized some of the key principles identified during the convening as part of⏎its voluntary Responsible Use of Health Data (RUHD™) certification for hospitals and health systems.⏎https://www.jointcommission.org/en-us/certification/responsible-use-of-health-data⏎Page 2 of 3⏎⏎Joint Commission Comments on HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎emphasizes engaging clinicians, patients, and other stakeholders, and ensuring transparency and⏎accountability in third-party partnerships.⏎The RUAIH guidance is just the first step to support healthcare organizations. Joint Commission⏎is developing a voluntary certification program that would serve as an external validation that⏎organizations have policies and structures to guide appropriate, responsible use of AI, while⏎serving as a signal to patients and their families that certified healthcare organizations are⏎engaging with new tools to advance care delivery and taking steps to ensure that AI tools⏎support and enhance safe and high-quality care.⏎An AO’s voluntary certification model is an effective way to enable the adoption and⏎implementation of AI in the healthcare field, from administration to bedside, while promoting⏎sensible guardrails that focus on patient safety. Certification is not intended to verify the AI tools⏎themselves, serve as a barrier to AI use, or define how or when AI tools might be used by⏎healthcare providers. Instead, the purpose is to reinforce thoughtful implementation and safe use,⏎similar to the way in which AOs assess policies and procedures to ensure safe imaging practices,⏎but not the MRI technology itself. Additionally, the national reach of Joint Commission⏎promotes a unified approach to responsible AI in healthcare.⏎Joint Commission would welcome the opportunity to share its learnings with HHS as HHS⏎considers avenues to promote private sector innovation in AI for healthcare, providing⏎updates as Joint Commission’s RUAIH voluntary certification is finalized.⏎Joint Commission would be pleased to answer any questions you may have regarding our⏎comments. If you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact me or Patrick Ross,⏎Associate Director, Public Policy, at (202) 783-6655 or pross@jointcommission.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Kathryn E. Spates⏎Executive Vice President, Public Policy & Government Relations⏎Page 3 of 3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0080♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Martin♔♔New York♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My wife Karen was misdiagnosed twice before a doctor at Mount Sinai finally found her ovarian cancer in March of 2019. Both of those wrong answers came from computerized screening tools, and both times I asked who exactly was responsible when the software got it wrong, I got nothing. Silence, or something close to it. One oncologist told me the algorithm was "one input among many" and then moved on like that answered my question. It did not.⏎⏎Eight months. That's how long she was on the wrong treatment path because of what those tools told the first two physicians. I'm not a doctor and I'm not a lawyer and I don't pretend to understand how these systems are built. But I know what I watched, and I know that when I read about this department wanting to move faster on AI in clinical settings, my first thought is not excitement.⏎⏎The question I keep coming back to is accountability. Not as an abstract thing but as something real and specific: if an AI tool gives my wife the wrong answer, who calls me to explain what happened? The company that sold the software? The hospital that bought it? The doctor who clicked accept? Right now I don't think anyone has a clear answer to that, and the proposals I've seen seem more interested in removing barriers to adoption than in answering that question. I understand there are benefits. I'm not saying throw it all out. But the way this is framed worries me because the emphasis seems to be on speed, on getting these tools deployed faster, with streamlined review, and I don't see nearly enough about what happens when they fail.⏎⏎There's also something I don't think you can measure in any dataset. Karen's third physician, the one who actually found it, spent forty minutes with her on the first visit just asking questions and listening. He picked up on something in how she described the pain, the way she hesitated when she talked about fatigue, and he ordered a different scan than the protocol suggested. I'm not saying that's better than AI in every case. I genuinely don't know. But I do think there's a version of this push toward AI where the doctor who would have spent forty minutes listening instead spends fifteen and defers to the algorithm, and I think that version gets people killed even when the algorithm is usually right.⏎⏎The department asks what regulatory actions would help accelerate adoption. I'd like to answer a different question, which is: what regulatory actions would make sure someone is actually held responsible when these tools cause harm? Not just liability in some theoretical legal sense, but a real named human being who has to look a patient or their family in the eye. Because right now, from where I'm sitting, the system is very good at distributing responsibility until it disappears entirely, and adding more AI to that system without fixing that problem first seems like the wrong order of operations.⏎⏎I don't have a specific policy proposal. I'm a small business owner in New York, I run a commercial cleaning company, I'm not a healthcare policy expert. But I've been in that waiting room on 98th Street and I know what it feels like to ask a simple question and have it bounce off every person in the room until it falls on the floor unanswered.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0081♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Association for Clinical Oncology (ASCO), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Association for Clinical Oncology (ASCO)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically at www.regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Assistant Secretary:⏎I am pleased to submit these comments on behalf of the Association for Clinical⏎Oncology (ASCO) in response to the Request for Information regarding actions⏎the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) can take to accelerate the⏎adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎ASCO represents more than 50,000 physicians and other health care⏎professionals specializing in cancer treatment, diagnosis, and prevention. We⏎are committed to ensuring that AI-enabled discoveries are translated into⏎equitable, evidence-based tools that lead to improved patient outcomes⏎available to all patients.⏎ASCO appreciates the “OneHHS” approach that coordinates a unified, division-⏎wide AI strategy to eliminate information silos and promote the transformative⏎potential of AI. Our support for this unified strategy is deeply rooted in our own⏎foundational work. In May 2025, ASCO issued principles for the responsible use⏎of AI in oncology which we believe provides a compatible roadmap for a⏎coordinated federal framework.1⏎ASCO offers the following recommendations and detailed responses to HHS’⏎questions:⏎Q1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Promote Regulatory Alignment. ASCO believes that the absence of a flexible⏎1 https://cdn.bfldr.com/KOIHB2Q3/as/g5jsnp7g2b6m28j67j97smff/2025-ASCO-AI-⏎Principles⏎⏎federal regulatory framework remains a primary barrier to innovation. In the current environment, a⏎patchwork of state laws and legal challenges is emerging that may interfere with federal AI action plans⏎and limit the competitive intent of AI adoption in clinical care. ASCO urges HHS to support nimble⏎regulatory mechanisms that address AI’s risks while enabling a more efficient, accessible, and affordable⏎health care system. We urge HHS to work collaboratively with all stakeholders to advance a flexible⏎regulatory approach that prioritizes the health and well-being of patients.⏎Eliminate Data Silos and Improve Technical Interoperability. The limited ability to curate large, complex⏎data sets remains a major impediment to the accuracy of AI systems. To fully recognize the potential of⏎AI in clinical care, we must address existing interoperability gaps across the entire health information⏎technology (HIT) continuum. Massive volumes of high-quality data are required to support AI reliability;⏎currently, limited or poor-quality data sets can lead to suboptimal model performance and research⏎conclusions that perpetuate inaccuracy. While electronic health records have improved data collection,⏎we must incentivize data sharing across the care continuum, including community practices, academic⏎settings, laboratories, and payers to derive new clinical insights and improve the efficiency of care⏎delivery.⏎Leverage Privacy-Enhancing Technologies (PETs) for Data Exchange. To overcome the technical hurdles⏎inherent in large-scale data sharing, ASCO recommends that HHS leverage PETs, such as federated⏎learning and differential privacy. These technologies enable the analysis of decentralized medical data⏎without the need to move raw patient information, effectively bypassing the interoperability⏎bottlenecks that currently stifle multi-institutional research. Overall, HHS should pursue a⏎comprehensive use of real-world data and real-world evidence (RWD/E) by leveraging insurance claims,⏎patient registries, and digital health solutions through these secure, decentralized frameworks.⏎Address Resource Constraints. Implementation costs remain prohibitive for many oncology practices.⏎While broad adoption could lead to systemic savings, there is limited economic analysis of AI’s financial⏎impact on individual organizations. Existing modeling methods and reporting standards may be⏎insufficient to assess cost-effectiveness, creating an AI digital divide where only resourced institutions⏎can afford cutting-edge tools, potentially affecting rural and underserved populations. Closing the⏎technological gap for small and rural practices will require dedicated funding and the technical⏎infrastructure necessary for sustainable AI integration must be prioritized.⏎Confront Validation and Evaluation Challenges. ASCO urges HHS to support the creation of robust⏎evaluation tools rooted in harmonized data governance and accountability frameworks. To bridge the⏎gap between high-level federal oversight and practical clinical application, HHS should encourage⏎researchers to adhere to established international guidelines, such as SPIRIT-AI and CONSORT-AI. These⏎standards ensure that clinical trials for AI systems are as transparent and reproducible as those for⏎traditional medical interventions. In exploring pre- and post-deployment evaluation, HHS should explore⏎the use of digital twins and synthetic data. HHS might also consider standardized documentation⏎methods like applied model cards or AI “nutrition labels” to provide technical clarity on model⏎performance and limitations.⏎⏎Q2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Mandate Transparency and Oversight of AI Tools. HHS must prioritize rigorous transparency⏎mechanisms and oversight of AI tools, particularly those used by payers for prior authorization. Current⏎regulations are insufficient on their own to protect consumers from “black box” payer algorithms that⏎can lead to accelerated claims denials and delays in lifesaving care. These mandates incentivize effective⏎AI by shifting the market toward high-value, explainable AI. When payers are required to cite the⏎specific clinical evidence and data sources used by an algorithm, it incentivizes the development of AI⏎that prioritizes accuracy over cost-containment.⏎To ensure patient safety, ASCO recommends that HHS implement frameworks for regular auditing of⏎training data sets and mandate that payers cite all evidence used by AI in reaching coverage⏎determinations. Furthermore, regulatory agencies should conduct continuous monitoring to detect if AI⏎tools are being used to disproportionately deny specific types of care. It is essential that any AI-derived⏎denial is immediately escalated for clinical peer review, ensuring that automated efficiency never⏎overrides professional medical opinion. This oversight must extend to third-party vendors to prevent⏎payers from exploiting liability loopholes through external partnerships.⏎Strengthen Post-Market Surveillance. ASCO’s principles assert that clinician comfort with AI tools⏎depends on continued evidence of safety and effectiveness. Currently, the FDA has limited ability to⏎monitor technology once it’s on the market, complicated by the potential for AI model degradation or⏎drift. We urge HHS to address resource restraints within the FDA and support reporting mechanisms⏎that monitor AI performance throughout its lifecycle, including the deployment of regulatory sandboxes⏎and/or assurance labs for transparent and localized testing.⏎Q3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Clarify Legal Uncertainty. Liability and uncertainty serve as a significant deterrent to clinical adoption.⏎Physicians are less likely to use AI in high-complexity cases when they perceive a risk of malpractice⏎liability for deviating from an algorithm. The fundamental responsibility for treatment decisions remains⏎with clinicians, and this must be understood by all users of AI technologies. To address this, HHS should⏎play a proactive role in addressing novel accountability structures. ASCO recommends that HHS explore⏎the establishment of legal "safe harbors" for providers who use AI tools certified through HHS-approved⏎⏎assurance labs or sandboxes. This would mitigate the malpractice fears that currently hamper the⏎integration of non-device clinical decision support tools into routine care.⏎Q5: How could HHS support accreditation, certification, or credentialing for the development and use⏎of AI in clinical care?⏎Support the Workforce through AI literacy. To mitigate the risk of clinician deskilling, HHS should⏎collaborate with medical societies to develop AI-literacy benchmarks and credentialing. Providing⏎clinicians with the tools to critically audit AI outputs can ensure that human expertise remains the⏎primary safeguard in oncology. HHS could also support a federal certification program for AI tools that⏎can be validated against diverse, representative data sets that reflect the unique local and demographic⏎contexts of specific communities in which AI tools are deployed.⏎Q8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards,⏎and benchmarking tools.⏎Prioritize Adoption of Technical Data Standards. To accelerate AI development in oncology, HHS should⏎prioritize adoption of minimal Common Oncology Data Elements (mCODE) and Fast Health care⏎Interoperable Resources (FHIR) standards for all AI-enabled health information exchange. Mandating⏎these universal technical standards through financial leverage will compel EHR vendors to eliminate data⏎silos and create a common language for disparate systems. This approach reduces the manual burden of⏎data abstraction, allowing AI developers to scale innovations more rapidly across diverse clinical sites.⏎While FHIR provides the building blocks for interoperability, HHS must address implementation gaps to⏎ensure high-quality data is readily accessible.⏎Q9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Bridge the AI Digital Divide. ASCO believes that the integration of AI in clinical care must be⏎fundamentally patient-centered, focusing on bridging the AI digital divide to ensure that technological⏎advancements do not inadvertently widen existing gaps in access to care. This divide is primarily driven⏎by the risk that algorithms trained on non-representative or high-resource datasets will scale skewed⏎data points, leading to inappropriate interpretations for underserved, rural, and diverse populations⏎who are frequently excluded from the data lifecycle. It is essential that HHS proactively incentivizes the⏎deployment of these technologies in community oncology practices and rural settings, ensuring that⏎innovative AI tools do not remain concentrated solely at well-resourced academic medical centers.⏎Q10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎Prioritize AI Research for Clinical Integration. ASCO recommends that HHS prioritize research that⏎fosters a comprehensive real-world data framework and focuses on human-in-the-loop interactions.⏎⏎This includes implementation science research to evaluate the longitudinal impact of AI on the oncology⏎workforce, specifically identifying and mitigating deskilling among early-career clinicians. We must⏎develop proficiency benchmarks to ensure that while AI enhances efficiency, the fundamental diagnostic⏎precision of the oncologist is preserved. Furthermore, HHS should fund the development of deflationary⏎AI technologies, such as AI-assisted clinical trial matching, that integrate seamlessly into EHRs and⏎reduce administrative burden.⏎Ensure Collaborative Research and Development and Safety Safeguards. HHS should pursue⏎collaborative development through mechanisms such as Cooperative Research and Development⏎Agreements (CRADAs) to integrate AI into care delivery. These partnerships are essential for scaling⏎innovation, but they must be coupled with strict safety safeguards as we move toward more complex⏎systems. While ASCO supports innovation in semi-autonomous or agentic AI, these transitions should be⏎approached with extreme caution to ensure that fully autonomous systems do not replace sensitive⏎clinician-patient interactions. Consequently, any agentic AI used in oncology must not be permitted to⏎autonomously perform clinical functions or modify prescriptions without the direct, final approval of a⏎clinician.⏎Conclusion⏎AI has the potential to make clinical recommendations and decision support readily available to every⏎clinician, thereby smoothing unwarranted variations in care and enabling all patients to receive high⏎quality, evidence-based care. In addition to improving outcomes, AI could also eliminate cost of⏎unwarranted variation--for all stakeholders. ASCO appreciates the opportunity to provide feedback on⏎this critical request for information. As the use of AI continues to expand within oncology, it is⏎imperative that federal strategy remains grounded in clinical evidence, transparency, and the protection⏎of the patient-clinician relationship. We look forward to working with HHS to ensure that AI serves as a⏎tool for professional empowerment and improved patient outcomes across the cancer care continuum.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Lynn M. Schuchter, MD, FASCO⏎Chair of the Board⏎Association for Clinical Oncology♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0082♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Alliance of Community Health Plans, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Alliance of Community Health Plans♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎Re: HHS Request for Information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence in Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Dr. Keane:⏎The Alliance of Community Health Plans (ACHP) and its member companies have longstanding⏎experience using AI to support decision-making, enhance care coordination, streamline⏎administrative processes, reduce costs and improve population health outcomes. We share the⏎Administration’s interest in accelerating responsible AI adoption in the health care system and⏎appreciate the opportunity to provide comments on the adoption and use of artificial intelligence AI⏎in clinical care.⏎ACHP represents the nation’s best local, nonprofit health plans, providing high-quality coverage to⏎tens of millions of Americans, including more than three million MA consumers, in 43 states and⏎D.C. ACHP is the only national organization advancing a unique partnership model of coverage and⏎care that fosters a truly competitive market. We believe Americans deserve the best health care in⏎the world and our members are on the ground delivering innovative and competitive coverage.⏎In 2024, ACHP outlined three guiding principles for the appropriate and effective oversight of AI in⏎health care, which include recommendations for HHS to consider as it promotes greater AI⏎adoption. Specifically, ACHP encourages HHS to prioritize a coordinated, federal regulatory effort,⏎create a common language that delineates specific AI definitions and protect patients.⏎• Public-Private Coordination: Health care regulations governing payer lines of business,⏎patient and consumer protections, hospitals and providers are siloed between states and⏎federal agencies. The result is a fragmented policy landscape that is challenging to navigate.⏎Policymakers should work with both public and private-sector partners to adopt a⏎harmonized, risk-based approach that accounts for the clinical impact, level of automation⏎and data sensitivity of AI tools. ACHP requests HHS maintain a coordinated effort with⏎⏎stakeholders to establish seamless national policies and guardrails that enhance the⏎use of AI in health care.⏎• A Common Language: There is no consensus on definitions in AI within health care.⏎Additionally, the distinction between predictive AI and generative AI models is not widely⏎understood between consumers, policymakers and the health care industry. ACHP requests⏎HHS determine a common language for AI in health care that would eliminate any⏎ambiguity, ensuring that all stakeholders have a shared understanding as policy⏎evolves.⏎• Protecting Patients: The value of AI models and quality of the outputs rely on the original⏎data, which in health care often involves the use of personal health information. It is⏎imperative that any personal health information is used responsibly and patient privacy is⏎maintained. High-quality data is also vital to ensuring AI does not impair the⏎personalization of care or the recognition of community-specific trends. ACHP requests⏎HHS develop clear guidelines for how existing security and privacy requirements⏎apply to data being used by AI, especially where data falls out of the purview of HIPAA⏎protections, and to ensure that data sets are clean and representative to facilitate AI-⏎enabled, personalized care.⏎Reimbursement⏎ACHP encourages HHS to adopt policies that align reimbursement with high-value, AI-⏎enabled interventions and promote ACHP member's integrated, provider-aligned care⏎models. As provider-aligned health plans, ACHP member companies are leaders in value-based⏎care. Current fee-for-service payment structures unintentionally slow adoption of AI by not⏎incentivizing integration into workflows or linking AI use to outcomes and value.⏎ACHP encourages HHS to develop clear, graduated pathways for reimbursement of AI tools⏎that demonstrate clinical value, building on emerging evaluation frameworks. Specifically,⏎ACHP recommends HHS:⏎• Establish a tiered evidentiary framework for AI-enabled tools that distinguishes between AI⏎applications (e.g., administrative, clinical decision-support, autonomous or semi-⏎autonomous clinical applications), with proportionate evidence requirements based on⏎patient risk and clinical impact.⏎• Recognize real-world evidence (e.g., pragmatic trials, post-market performance data and⏎value-based care outcomes) as acceptable forms of validation.⏎• Create a time-limited “coverage with evidence development” pathway for AI tools that⏎demonstrate early promise but require additional longitudinal data to confirm impact on⏎quality, safety or cost.⏎⏎ACHP encourages HHS to support the responsible use of AI to modernize prior authorization⏎and utilization management processes. AI presents a significant opportunity to streamline⏎administrative processes which can reduce clinician burden and improve patients' timely access to⏎care. ACHP recommends HHS:⏎• Explicitly permit and encourage the use of AI-enabled tools to proactively approve prior⏎authorizations for services that meet clearly defined clinical criteria, are supported by⏎evidence-based guidelines or are associated with historically high approval rates.⏎• Establish clear guardrails for AI-enabled utilization management decisions, including⏎requirements for:⏎o Human review of adverse determinations;⏎o Explainability of decision logic to providers and patients;⏎o Ongoing monitoring for bias, error rates and disparate impact.⏎• Align prior authorization modernization efforts with existing interoperability initiatives,⏎including FHIR-based Prior Authorization APIs, to ensure AI tools can operate within⏎standardized, transparent workflows.⏎ACHP also supports advancing outcomes-based reimbursement models that align payment for AI-⏎enabled tools with measurable improvements in care quality, access and total cost of care. ACHP⏎recommends HHS:⏎• Encourage the use of performance-based payment arrangements such as shared savings,⏎performance guarantees or risk corridors.⏎• Promote standardized performance metrics for AI tools (e.g., impact on avoidable⏎utilization, clinician efficiency, patient adherence or health equity outcomes) to enable⏎consistent evaluation across payers and providers.⏎• Modify existing medical code sets and ensure EHR capabilities can capture when AI is used⏎in medical care and adjust payments accordingly.⏎ACHP recommends HHS establish targeted safe harbors for early adoption of AI tools with⏎demonstrated potential value. Examples include:⏎• Temporary regulatory or payment flexibilities for pilot programs operating under defined⏎guardrails, transparency requirements and monitoring protocols.⏎• Protection from retrospective payment recoupment when AI tools are deployed in good⏎faith and consistent with published federal guidance.⏎• Explicit encouragement of provider-plan collaboration to test AI tools within alternative⏎payment models without triggering fraud and abuse concerns.⏎Interoperability and Data Standards⏎⏎ACHP requests HHS support health AI innovation by addressing interoperability challenges⏎that hinder provider connectivity and widespread health IT adoption. We support efforts to⏎improve data standards and interoperability, recognizing access to accurate, robust data is essential⏎for scaling AI tools safely and effectively. Ensuring data quality, volume and hygiene is a critical⏎element for successful health AI implementation, given the potential for AI models to ingest⏎incomplete, inaccurate or poor-quality health care data. Additionally, ACHP member companies⏎recognize the need for a sufficient health IT infrastructure to support the data exchange required to⏎enable value-based, technology-enabled care. ACHP recommends HHS:⏎• Improve provider connectivity to FHIR-based APIs, including Prior Authorization and⏎Provider Access APIs, by:⏎o Creating a national digital endpoint directory that enables reliable discovery of⏎payer and provider endpoints.⏎o Establishing clearer EHR workflow standards so AI-enabled data exchange is⏎embedded into clinical operations rather than treated as an add-on.⏎o Advancing provider-focused adoption requirements that prioritize usability and⏎reduce implementation friction.⏎• Ensure equitable participation for smaller and regional health plans, by:⏎o Scaling compliance requirements based on organizational size and resources.⏎o Investing in standardized reference implementations, shared service platforms or⏎open-source tooling that lower technical and financial barriers.⏎o Offering targeted grants or technical assistance programs to support AI⏎experimentation and interoperability adoption among plans serving smaller or rural⏎populations.⏎Organizational Governance and Operational Considerations⏎We understand effective adoption of AI depends on governance and operational alignment within⏎health care organizations. ACHP member companies leverage interdisciplinary AI oversight or⏎enterprise governance committees, which may be comprised of: clinical leadership (CMIOs, Medical⏎directors); information technology leadership (CIO/CDIO, CDO, CISO); compliance, privacy and legal⏎teams; and financial and operations leadership.⏎Despite the successes in these governance models, ACHP member companies still face hurdles when⏎adopting health AI. ACHP recommends HHS consider the following challenges:⏎• Lack of AI-specific privacy, security and monitoring standards.⏎• Unclear ownership and lifecycle responsibility for AI tools.⏎• Procurement cycles that misalign with iterative AI development.⏎⏎• Absence of standardized evidence and evaluation frameworks.⏎Patient-and-Caregiver-Centered Considerations⏎ACHP member companies recognize how technology can reach consumer populations and improve⏎outcomes, particularly for chronic diseases. As local, non-profit health plans, ACHP member⏎companies are uniquely positioned to implement prevention-focused strategies that improve health⏎outcomes, reduce unnecessary utilization and support individuals in living independently with a⏎high quality of life. Further reflecting this commitment, ACHP member organizations signed a⏎decade-long Chronic Disease Pledge in 2020 to reduce diabetes, hypertension and other chronic⏎conditions through a range of prevention strategies and community partnerships.⏎To effectively leverage technology to scale population health interventions, AI adoption should⏎enhance patient experience, engagement and outcomes without eroding trust. ACHP encourages⏎HHS to consider promoting:⏎• Transparent communication of AI use in clinical care.⏎• Tools designed for usability, accessibility and shared decision-making.⏎• Technology integration with existing prevention and wellness programs, including nutrition,⏎lifestyle and chronic disease management interventions.⏎ACHP appreciates the Administration’s commitment to exploring AI adoption in clinical care and⏎stands ready to collaborate further to ensure safe, effective and personalized AI integration. We look⏎forward to HHS working closely with ACHP member companies to develop, pilot and scale payment⏎arrangements that leverage technology-enabled care to promote integrated value-based care⏎models. Please contact Michael Bagel, Vice President of Public Policy, at mbagel@achp.org or 202-⏎897-6121 for questions or follow-up discussion.⏎Sincerely,⏎Ceci Connolly⏎President and CEO⏎Alliance of Community Health Plans♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0083♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎330 C Street SE⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Subject: HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎To Whom it May Concern:⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide, on behalf of Penn Medicine, a response to the RFI on⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care. Penn⏎Medicine is building a future-ready health system where AI augments human expertise,⏎improves patient outcomes, accelerates research, and reduces administrative burden, and we⏎appreciate HHS’ attention to the importance of this work.⏎Penn Medicine is one of the world’s leading academic medical centers, dedicated to the related⏎missions of medical education, biomedical research, and excellence in patient care. Penn⏎Medicine consists of the Raymond and Ruth Perelman School of Medicine at the University of⏎Pennsylvania (founded in 1765 as the nation's first medical school) and the University of⏎Pennsylvania Health System.⏎Penn Medicine cares for patients in facilities and their homes, stretching from the Susquehanna⏎River in Pennsylvania to the New Jersey shore. Our facilities include the Hospital of the⏎University of Pennsylvania, Penn Presbyterian Medical Center, Chester County Hospital,⏎Lancaster General Health, Princeton Health, and Pennsylvania Hospital—the nation’s first⏎hospital, chartered in 1751. Additional facilities and enterprises include Penn Medicine at Home,⏎Good Shepard Penn Partners Rehabilitation, Lancaster Behavioral Health Hospital, and⏎Princeton House Behavioral Health, among others.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Trust – Healthcare delivery organizations, clinicians, and patients/consumers need to develop⏎trust in health AI tools and their developers, particularly when lives are on the line. This⏎means that we need to be able to develop evidence of safety and effectiveness of tools,⏎particularly in high-risk circumstances closer to the patient/during direct patient care. Then, we⏎need to be able to monitor the performance of tools once implemented to ensure continued safety⏎and effectiveness, an effort that becomes more complex as multiple AI⏎tools operate together. This will take deep partnership between health AI developers and⏎implementers throughout the life cycle of a tool. Healthcare moves at the speed of trust, and⏎tools will not be adopted/used without that trust. Of course, need to keep in mind what the⏎evidentiary standard should be – we are aiming for health AI tools not to be perfect but as good⏎as or better than our current practice.⏎Perelman Center for Advanced Medicine | 3400 Civic Center Boulevard | Philadelphia, PA 19104-4283⏎⏎Evidence – The vast majority of published scientific manuscripts about clinical AI tools, and the⏎vast majority of FDA-authorized AI-enabled devices, have no evidence to suggest that they⏎improve clinical decisions, care processes, or patient outcomes. A key element of trust (as⏎described above) is developing this evidence so that clinicians, patients, and health system⏎leaders are well informed when making decisions about purchasing and deployment. Developing⏎this evidence also creates an opportunity for private sector innovators to distinguish their⏎products from those that have less (or no) evidence of clinical effectiveness. Furthermore, such⏎evidence would alleviate critical information asymmetries in investment decisions for clinical AI⏎systems that have so far led to significant market inefficiencies. Evidence development can take⏎at least two forms including (1) experimental frameworks for generating the evidence with lower⏎risk and cost; (2) standardized and routine monitoring, as distinct from up front, one-time⏎evaluation which is what is prevalent. Of note, even when evidence is present, currently much of⏎it relates to foundation model performance in isolation of real workflows. This is not equivalent⏎to performance or attributed outcomes in real-world clinical environments.⏎Uncertainty around legal, regulatory, and medical malpractice issues – There is significant⏎uncertainty around the way health AI will be legislated and regulated which can inhibit⏎innovation as well as adoption of the technology. For example, it is unclear the direction in⏎which federal and state policy will go in relation to health AI, which one will have a more⏎dominant effect, and which of these stakeholders vendor-partners and healthcare organizations⏎should engage with in discussions relevant to the future of health AI. Companies may hesitate to⏎enter a market in which they must navigate 50 potentially divergent state regulatory regimes⏎rather than a single coherent framework. Additionally, ongoing uncertainty around which CDS⏎tools meet the definition of a device (despite recent attempts at clarification), combined with⏎recent instances of the FDA exhibiting “enforcement discretion” without transparent rationale or⏎defined timelines, further deters companies from entering the market. Finally, concerns of⏎clinicians and vendors related to malpractice and liability resulting from AI-generated⏎information or actions are currently unresolved.⏎Interoperability and data standardization - Data are the lifeblood of health AI, and tools need⏎to both be able to access data from systems across the healthcare ecosystem⏎(patients/consumers, clinicians, payers, pharmacies, etc.) but then also be able to take actions⏎flexibly within environments like the electronic health record and within clinician⏎workflows (functional in addition to technical interoperability). Importantly, data standardization⏎and adoption of standards such as FHIR, along with expanded data element availability, across⏎broad ranges of use cases is critical to truly enabling transformative health AI use cases.⏎FHIR and other APIs should also be enabled with appropriate capabilities to write back into the⏎EHR and integrate deeply within clinician and patient workflows to make it easier⏎for clinicians and patients to use health AI tools.⏎Finally, there should be cost controls on integrations and API access.⏎All of this, of course, needs to be balanced by adequate privacy and information security⏎safeguards.⏎Competency/workforce development and process reimagination – In order to derive the⏎most benefit from health AI tools, individuals within organizations must learn how⏎to utilize tools effectively and process must be modified or reimagined entirely to make use of⏎the health AI tool⏎⏎Business model/reimbursement – Aside from a few specific technologies (ie AI guided⏎coronary plaque analyses), AI-based tools are not (and may not be) reimbursed for directly,⏎making it even more important for vendor-partners to clearly articulate and measure outcomes⏎and return on investment from health AI tools. CMS’ and the FDA’s paired ACCESS/TEMPO⏎models offer a limited initial framework, but without resolving fundamental questions about how⏎the FDA will regulate many AI tools beyond temporary enforcement discretion,⏎it remains unclear how these efforts will scale beyond pilot programs.⏎An effective business model is also important as AI implementers shoulder impactful AI-related⏎costs, such as those arising from (1) liability risks (which bear unknown costs at⏎the moment) and (2) operating costs from both model costs and data integration⏎costs. Many implementers can't realistically experiment, let alone adapt and reimagine people⏎and process along with technology, without financial relief on these fronts.⏎Of note, much of the innovation in the health AI space is focused on the use of large language⏎models, and that focus could become a barrier in the future to newer innovations. HHS⏎should consider encouraging divergent thinking in health AI and continuing to explore other⏎forms of language, vision-language, world models, among other AI tools and methods.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Information blocking– Enforcement of 21st Century Cures Act information blocking provisions⏎would help to create data ecosystem needed for effective health AI development⏎and implementation and the environment needed to implement health AI tools flexibly deeply⏎within workflows.⏎Interoperability – Similarly, continued development of trusted interoperability frameworks such⏎as TEFCA and CMS-Aligned Networks will allow for health AI tools to be more effectively⏎implemented in workflow .⏎Virtual care – In addition to information/insights generated from health AI tools,⏎patients/consumers also need access to care delivery in order to improve health outcomes, and⏎that virtual care is an excellent pairing with health AI tools such as chatbots. However, virtual⏎care, in the form of synchronous (ambulatory visits, Hospital at Home), asynchronous care⏎(eConsults, eVisits), and remote monitoring needs to have permanent reimbursement to ensure⏎stability needed to develop and grow programs. Relatedly, variation in state licensure and⏎telehealth regulations governing interstate delivery of care creates additional barriers to scaling⏎AI-enabled care models.⏎Clarification of intention for oversight – Informal discussions with FDA officials, in addition⏎to the precedent set by the recent ACLA vs FDA Supreme Court case, all suggest that⏎“homegrown” AI models – or those that are developed and deployed locally by hospitals but not⏎marketed or sold – are not likely to be considered part of the FDA’s oversight purview.⏎Clarifying this point officially would help to alleviate uncertainty in regulatory and business⏎decisions and would help foster local AI innovation.⏎Incentives – Similar to other eras of health IT implementation (for example post-HITECH⏎Act), tying incentives, particularly financial incentives to effective adoption and utilization of⏎⏎health AI to boost patient outcomes will drive adoption. Efforts such as CMMI’s⏎ACCESS model and solicitations such as ARPA-H’s ADVOCATE are a step in the right⏎direction.⏎Liability – Concerns around liability limit more enthusiastic use of health AI in clinical care⏎delivery. Now, most if not all liability is placed on the shoulders of implementers of health AI⏎rather than developers. To encourage implementation, HHS/CMS should consider how this risk⏎can be shared more equally between implementers and developers.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal⏎and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎There are numerous legal and implementation issues raised in the context of health AI⏎implementation that challenge existing structures. A key example of this is the case of AI⏎agents. AI agents are already beginning to interact with each other to impact care delivery,⏎including teams of agents working within a healthcare delivery system as well as agents⏎interacting between actors such as payers and clinicians.⏎Especially when these interactions extend beyond one entity (such as payer and clinician), HHS⏎should consider setting standards for how these interactions should occur. This has already⏎started to happen via, for example, the CMS Interoperability and Prior Authorization Final Rule⏎(encouraging adoption of FHIR standards for data exchange) as well as recent work on real-⏎time prescription benefits – but this approach likely needs to continue to evolve in an era of⏎agentic interactions.⏎In the clinician space, systems are under development where agents review the medical⏎literature, or large bodies of medical records, and surface insights and recommendations which⏎can serve as the basis for patient facing actions (i.e. orders, communication) by other agents. As⏎we mention above, this will require new frameworks for health AI evaluation as well as liability⏎shared between health AI developers and implementers.⏎From an HHS perspective, it will be important to balance creating conditions for safe and⏎effective implementation of health AI spanning patients, clinicians, payers, developers and others⏎while leaving room for experimentation.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎There are a number of AI evaluation methods emerging that fall into several categories. These⏎include (but are not limited to):⏎Benchmarks to evaluate health AI tools applied in specific task⏎areas (ie summarization): There are emerging constructs such⏎as MedHELM and HealthBench which show promise in the evaluation of health AI particularly⏎pre-deployment.⏎⏎Bedi S, Cui H, Fuentes M, et al. Holistic evaluation of large language models for medical⏎tasks with MedHELM. Nat Med. Published online January 20,⏎2026. doi:10.1038/s41591-025-04151-2⏎Automated evaluation of AI output: There are also promising models of LLM-as-judge to⏎evaluate artifacts such as AI-generated summaries at scale, including post-deployment.⏎Croxford E, Gao Y, First E, et al. Evaluating clinical AI summaries with large language⏎models as judges. npj Digit Med. 2025;8(1):640. doi:10.1038/s41746-025-02005-2⏎Chung P, Swaminathan A, Goodell AJ, et al. Verifying Facts in Patient Care Documents⏎Generated by Large Language Models Using Electronic Health Records. NEJM AI.⏎2025;3(1). doi:10.1056/AIdbp2500418⏎Evaluation of the performance of compound AI systems (ie multiple models working⏎together): Much more work needs to be done in this space, especially as there is very little⏎known about evaluation of single and multi-agent systems pre- or post-deployment, including⏎how agents may be able to supervise each other, and how humans might maintain appropriate⏎oversight in a human on the loop model.⏎HHS should continue to encourage pragmatic and applied research activity in this⏎space. Programs like ARPA-H ADVOCATE are also encouraged as a way to spur government,⏎academic, and industry partnership.⏎Phases of research – While phases of research are well established for the evaluation of⏎pharmaceutical and traditional medical devices, such a framework has yet to be widely applied to⏎AI-enabled medical devices. Thus, established frameworks offer an opportunity to foster⏎innovation while guiding the appropriate interpretation of evidence for AI systems. HHS should⏎support enhanced evaluation alongside the development of AI-specific evaluation methods and⏎frameworks across all phases of research. For example, the vast majority of evidence for AI-⏎enabled clinical systems is best characterized as Phase 0, yet the results of such studies are often⏎over-interpreted and misunderstood. More Phase 1 and 2 studies are needed to bridge knowledge⏎gaps that would support more high quality, Phase 3 randomized evaluations of AI systems that⏎are deployed in real clinical workflows.⏎Park Y, Jackson GP, Foreman MA, Gruen D, Hu J, Das AK. Evaluating artificial⏎intelligence in medicine: phases of clinical research. JAMIA Open 2020;3(3):326–31.⏎Support from HHS is critical here in all of the forms proposed including grants and contracts.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎We believe new models need to emerge for credentialing health AI tools, potentially based on⏎existing structures such as CLIA and current forms of medical licensing. This would help to⏎build trust in health AI tools, and would enable adoption of health AI tools not requiring humans⏎in every loop, while still maintaining human accountability. Two papers below from Penn⏎faculty describe these models:⏎Herman DS, Reece JT, Weissman GE. Lessons for local oversight of AI in medicine⏎from the regulation of clinical laboratory testing. npj Digit Med.⏎2024;7(1):359. doi:10.1038/s41746-024-01369-1⏎⏎Bressman E, Shachar C, Stern AD, Mehrotra A. Software as a Medical Practitioner— Is⏎It Time to License Artificial Intelligence? Published online 2025.⏎On the other hand, allowing states to advance autonomous AI use cases without clear federal⏎credentialing or oversight frameworks risks creating regulatory fragmentation and may⏎undermine trust, accountability, and consistency in how these tools are deployed.⏎As noted above, HHS should fund cooperative research and development efforts in this⏎space and use its authority to create innovation payment models (such as ACCESS)⏎and regulatory sandboxes for safe and effective experimentation.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎In care delivery, health AI has already had positive, measurable impact, particularly in reducing⏎friction in healthcare for patients, clinicians, and administrators.⏎Clinical documentation – Ambient AI scribes used to augment clinician documentation have⏎been shown to reduce time in chart as well as well as to reduce clinician burnout and increase⏎professional satisfaction. See other parts of our response for supporting evidence.⏎Closing care gaps – Generative AI based tools are able to review the fragmented medical record⏎and identify incidentally diagnosed or missed findings (such as pulmonary nodules which can⏎become cancer) and connect patients to care at earlier, more treatable stages of disease (or⏎prevent disease from developing entirely).⏎Upskilling the healthcare workforce – AI powered tools are able to bring what was previously⏎specialized knowledge to the point of care of all clinicians, allowing⏎more clinicians to participate in the delivery of that care, and to augment patient access to care.⏎Korom R, Kiptinness S, Adan N, et al. Ai-based clinical decision support for primary⏎care: a real-world study. arXiv. Preprint posted online 2025.⏎doi:10.48550/ARXIV.2507.16947⏎Predicting risk of disease – Predictive AI based tools are able to use electronic health⏎record data to predict clinical events such as inpatient decompensation, or risk of mortality, and⏎connect clinicians and patients to appropriate downstream actions.⏎Rossetti SC, Dykes PC, Knaplund C, et al. Real-time surveillance system for patient⏎deterioration: a pragmatic cluster-randomized controlled trial. Nat Med. 2025;31(6):1895-⏎1902. doi:10.1038/s41591-025-03609-7⏎Courtright KR, Madden V, Bayes B, et al. Default Palliative Care Consultation for⏎Seriously Ill Hospitalized Patients: A Pragmatic Cluster Randomized Trial. JAMA.⏎2024;331(3):224. doi:10.1001/jama.2023.25092⏎Clinical imaging – Clinical imaging use cases such as AI-assisted mammography have led to⏎improved patient outcomes⏎⏎Lång K, Josefsson V, Larsson AM, et al. Artificial intelligence-supported screen reading⏎versus standard double reading in the Mammography Screening with Artificial⏎Intelligence trial (MASAI): a clinical safety analysis of a randomised, controlled, non-⏎inferiority, single-blinded, screening accuracy study. The Lancet Oncology.⏎2023;24(8):936-944. doi:10.1016/S1470-2045(23)00298-X⏎Thus far, health AI tools have mainly provided information, but novel tools will also be able to⏎reason, use tools, plan, have memory and take action to augment humans in healthcare⏎delivery (agents).⏎There is also early empirical evidence that implementation of AI tools may degrade clinicians’⏎independent diagnostic and decision-making skills (“deskilling”). Greater attention and research⏎investment are needed to better understand, monitor, and mitigate these risks.⏎Budzyń K, Romańczyk M, Kitala D, Kołodziej P, Bugajski M, Adami HO, Blom⏎J, Buszkiewicz M, Halvorsen N, Hassan C, Romańczyk T. Endoscopist deskilling risk⏎after exposure to artificial intelligence in colonoscopy: a multicentre, observational⏎study. The Lancet Gastroenterology & Hepatology. 2025 Oct 1;10(10):896-903.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Within healthcare organizations, CEOs and other executive leaders help to create the vision for⏎use of health AI within an organization. Technology executives such as Chief Digital and⏎Information Officers, Chief Data and Analytics Officers, Chief Health AI Officers, and Chief⏎Nursing Information Officers, and Chief Health Information Officers are charged with strategy⏎and tactics behind implementing health AI in care delivery. This is often done in collaboration⏎with medical and nursing leaders such as Chief Physician Executives, Chief Medical Officers,⏎Chief Clinical Officers, and Chief Nursing Officers.⏎Most healthcare delivery organizations also now have health AI governance committees⏎with multidisciplinary stakeholders across teams in the organization (medical, legal, IT, ethics,⏎etc.) which also help to guide decision-making around health AI. Even with these committees, a⏎significant hurdle is risk assessment, and without unifying standards or certifications, each⏎assessment of each health AI tools is ad hoc. Simplifying the risk assessment process would⏎speed adoption of health AI in clinical care.⏎Outside of healthcare organizations – medical specialty societies have tremendous influence on⏎the behavior of medical practitioners and should be engaged in developing, implementing,⏎and monitoring health AI.⏎See above on commentary regarding administrative hurdles to adoption.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability, through growth of trusted interoperability frameworks such as⏎TEFCA, adoption and expansion of FHIR-based standards, promotion of activities such as real-⏎⏎time prescription benefit sharing, and prevention of information blocking, among other actions,⏎is critical for nurturing innovation in health technology. Importantly, interoperability should⏎include interoperability frameworks, data exchange standards, content standards, and workflow⏎standards to be truly effective.⏎These actions and others promote healthy competition amongst vendor-partners, ultimately⏎bringing the best deeply workflow integrated applications to market, and allowing systems⏎to craft technology environments that more rapidly bring the best applications to benefit patients⏎and consumers.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers are seeking assistance across their healthcare journeys, starting from⏎accessing information on known and potential health conditions, contextualized for them, to⏎being connected to care at the right time in the right place, appropriately preparing⏎for encounters, having a productive encounter with a clinician, navigating the care⏎delivery system to take action from encounters, obtaining and understanding testing, and⏎understanding and navigating health benefits.⏎Both patients/caregivers and clinicians share concerns around implementation of health AI,⏎starting with being able to trust outputs of health AI tools. Healthcare delivery is intrinsically a⏎human endeavor, and patients, caregivers, and clinicians alike are concerned AI may⏎negatively impact the human relationships, judgement, reasoning, and empathy that are at the⏎core of healthcare delivery. There are also concerns related to the environmental impact of AI⏎(water and energy use), the impact of data centers, the privacy and security of protected health⏎information, the impact of AI on jobs in the healthcare and other industries, and consent and⏎notification of the use of AI in care delivery environments.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎There is much to be learned about the evaluation and implementation of health AI which would⏎result in accelerated adoption including⏎Evaluation and ongoing monitoring of health AI: This is a critical need to ensure safety and⏎effectiveness of tools. This needs to apply to predictive AI, generative AI, single agent AI, and⏎multi-agent AI systems.⏎Development of safety and control systems for health AI: Whether agentic or⏎otherwise, health AI systems, especially when semi-autonomous or autonomous, will⏎need mechanisms to ensure behaviors remain aligned with human values.⏎Human skill development: As mentioned in this response, the use of health AI is starting to be⏎associated with erosion in human skills. Research is needed on which human skills in healthcare⏎delivery truly continue to be needed, and how to maintain and enhance those skills while⏎collaborating with AI.⏎Use of health AI across tasks: In order for health AI to be most effective, it must also be able to⏎handle multiple related tasks together that would fall under a job. It is unknown across a range⏎of healthcare jobs, clinical and nonclinical, how best to extend beyond using AI for a specific⏎⏎task (ie documenting a single note) to a job (i.e. completing set of tasks for an ambulatory visit,⏎from preparation through followup).⏎Health AI implementation and behavioral science: In order for health AI to translate to⏎improved patient outcomes, tools must be effectively implemented and used. There is research⏎needed in how to ensure our healthcare delivery workforce develops competency in using health⏎AI. Strategies to nudge use of health AI may come from adaptations of learnings from the field⏎of behavioral science, both for clinicians and patients/consumers.⏎Human-computer interaction: We need to better understand how to shape interactions between⏎humans and AI (human computer collaboration) to generative positive outcomes, including how⏎to architect interactions where humans are in the loop (preventing automation bias and neglect)⏎and on the loop. This applies to patient and clinicians but particularly to patients, as illustrated in⏎a recent RCT.⏎Bean AM, Payne RE, Parsons G, et al. Reliability of LLMs as medical assistants for⏎the general public: a randomized preregistered study. Nat Med. Published online⏎February 9, 2026. doi:10.1038/s41591-025-04074-y⏎Part of this work needs to be on how humans and computers share work, which⏎models work and adapt best for certain jobs/tasks (i.e. centaur vs. cyborg).⏎Abdulnour REE, Gin B, Boscardin CK. Educational Strategies for Clinical Supervision of⏎Artificial Intelligence Use. N Engl J Med. 2025;393(8):786-⏎797. doi:10.1056/NEJMra2503232⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎There is now a large body of literature around the impact of ambient generative AI⏎powered documentation tools, including literature from Penn. Namely, the tools have been⏎found to save clinicians time, save cognitive load, save time in chart, and significantly⏎improve clinician experience. This has been seen in observational studies as well as randomized⏎controlled trials.⏎Duggan MJ, Gervase J, Schoenbaum A, et al. Clinician Experiences With Ambient⏎Scribe Technology to Assist With Documentation Burden and⏎Efficiency. JAMA Netw Open.⏎2025;8(2):e2460637. doi:10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.60637⏎Afshar M, Ryan Baumann M, Resnik F, et al. A Pragmatic Randomized Controlled Trial⏎of Ambient Artificial Intelligence to Improve Health Practitioner Well-Being. NEJM AI.⏎2025;2(12). doi:10.1056/AIoa2500945⏎Lukac PJ, Turner W, Vangala S, et al. Ambient AI Scribes in Clinical Practice: A⏎Randomized Trial. NEJM AI. 2025;2(12). doi:10.1056/AIoa2501000⏎Additionally, there is evidence that summarization of data in the EHR can be generated⏎effectively and safely by large language models.⏎⏎Small WR, Austrian J, O’Donnell L, et al. Evaluating Hospital Course Summarization by⏎an Electronic Health Record–Based Large Language Model. JAMA Netw Open.⏎2025;8(8):e2526339. doi:10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.26339⏎There is also now a body of literature on the impact of health AI tools on augmenting patient⏎experience and clinician decision making.⏎Zeltzer D, Kugler Z, Hayat L, et al. Comparison of initial artificial intelligence (Ai) and⏎final physician recommendations in ai-assisted virtual urgent care visits. Ann Intern Med.⏎2025;178(4):498-506. doi:10.7326/ANNALS-24-03283⏎O’Sullivan JW, Palepu A, Saab K, et al. A large language model for complex cardiology⏎care. Nat Med. Published online February 6, 2026. doi:10.1038/s41591-025-04190-9⏎Tu T, Schaekermann M, Palepu A, et al. Towards conversational diagnostic artificial⏎intelligence. Nature. 2025;642(8067):442-450. doi:10.1038/s41586-025-08866-7⏎See above for more research findings.⏎Penn Medicine appreciates the opportunity to comment on RFI and welcomes any questions you⏎may have.⏎Sincerely,⏎Mitchell D. Schnall, MD, PhD⏎Senior Vice President for Data and Technology Solutions⏎Professor of Radiology⏎Penn Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0084♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from People's Heallth Clinic, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔People's Heallth Clinic♔♔♔♔♔♔February 22, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The People’s Health Clinic (PHC) appreciates the opportunity to respond to HHS’s⏎Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical⏎care. We write as a 30-person free clinic in Park City, Utah delivering 21,500 patient encounters⏎annually to a predominantly Spanish-speaking population, 95% of whom live below 200% of the⏎federal poverty level. The number of unique patients served is increasing by approximately 10%⏎year over year. We do not receive federal funding. We have no IT department, no compliance⏎team, and no dedicated cybersecurity staff.⏎Despite these constraints, PHC has direct operational experience with both categories of⏎AI now entering clinical care: predictive AI that we have successfully deployed, and generative⏎AI for which we have built a governance framework and are preparing to evaluate. Our⏎experience illustrates both the promise and the practical barriers that safety-net providers face in⏎adopting AI—and suggests specific actions HHS can take to accelerate responsible adoption.⏎I. Where AI Has Met or Exceeded Expectations: Autonomous Diabetic Retinopathy⏎Screening (RFI Question 6)⏎PHC has deployed LumineticsCore® (Digital Diagnostics), the first FDA De Novo-⏎cleared autonomous AI diagnostic system, which detects diabetic retinopathy at the point of care⏎without requiring specialist overread. This is a predictive AI: the same retinal images produce⏎the same diagnostic output every time, and its performance has been validated through rigorous⏎clinical trials with published bias mitigation data across sex, race, and ethnicity.⏎Results at PHC (August 2025 – January 2026):⏎• 175 total exams completed; 154 sufficient for diagnosis (88% diagnosability)⏎• 20% positivity rate—one in five patients screened had more than mild diabetic⏎retinopathy detected, enabling immediate referral to eye care⏎• Median exam time: 5 minutes⏎• Zero protocol errors⏎⏎• 100% camera utilization⏎For PHC’s patient population—predominantly uninsured and with limited access to⏎specialty care—this AI tool closes a critical gap. Prior to implementing LumineticsCore, diabetic⏎retinopathy screening required a separate specialist referral, often resulting in delays of up to a⏎year. Many patients were unable to complete screening due to cost or transportation barriers.⏎Now, screening happens during the primary care visit. The 20% positivity rate confirms⏎that this tool is catching disease that would otherwise go undetected.⏎This deployment demonstrates that autonomous predictive AI can work effectively in a⏎resource-constrained, safety-net setting with minimal infrastructure. The tool integrates into⏎existing workflow, requires no IT support beyond initial setup, and produces deterministic results⏎that can be validated using standard statistical methods.⏎II. The Generative AI Challenge: Why Current Frameworks Are Insufficient (RFI⏎Questions 4 and 5)⏎PHC is also preparing to evaluate generative AI tools—specifically ambient AI clinical⏎documentation and AI-powered phone answering and triage. These tools present fundamentally⏎different challenges from the predictive AI described above, and this distinction has significant⏎implications for HHS policy.⏎Predictive AI (like LumineticsCore) is deterministic: the same input produces the same⏎output every time. It can be validated once using standard statistical methods (sensitivity,⏎specificity, AUC) and monitored for drift using established techniques. Generative AI is non-⏎deterministic: the same patient encounter processed twice will produce two different clinical⏎notes with different wording, different emphasis, and potentially different clinical facts. This⏎means:⏎• Traditional accuracy metrics do not apply. There is no single “correct” output to measure⏎against.⏎• Hallucination—the generation of clinically plausible but fabricated content—is an⏎inherent failure mode, not an edge case.⏎• Validation cannot be a one-time event. Every output is unique and must be evaluated⏎continuously.⏎• Bilingual performance introduces additional risk. A note generated from a Spanish-⏎language encounter may omit clinical facts that would be captured in English, creating a⏎two-tier documentation standard that worsens health disparities.⏎To address these challenges, PHC developed a generative AI evaluation framework that⏎measures four dimensions: clinical completeness, clinical fidelity (with zero tolerance for⏎hallucinated content), semantic consistency across repeated runs, and harm potential scoring. The⏎framework includes a repeatability test protocol that processes the same encounter five times,⏎⏎compares the clinical facts extracted across all runs, and requires safety-critical facts to appear in⏎at least four of five outputs. It also includes bilingual stress testing with code-switching and⏎dialect-specific medical terminology.⏎To our knowledge, no published national framework—including the CHAI/Joint⏎Commission Responsible Use of AI guidance, NACHC’s AI action guides, or the HEAAL⏎equity framework—addresses the specific evaluation challenges of generative AI in clinical⏎settings. This is a critical gap. As ambient AI documentation tools proliferate across the⏎healthcare system, the absence of evaluation methods designed for non-deterministic outputs⏎creates patient safety risk at scale.⏎This gap is not limited to safety-net settings. A 2024 case study from Mass General⏎Brigham—an integrated academic health system with a dedicated 18-person, multidisciplinary⏎AI governance committee—selected ambient documentation as their generative AI test case and⏎acknowledged the absence of straightforward metrics for evaluating generative AI performance⏎in clinical settings (Saenz et al., “Establishing responsible use of AI guidelines,” npj Digital⏎Medicine 7:348, 2024). Their evaluation relied primarily on clinician feedback scores and note⏎retention rates. They also reported an inability to measure AI quality across patient demographics⏎due to data linkage limitations. If a major academic health system with dedicated AI governance⏎resources faces these measurement challenges, the need for standardized evaluation methods that⏎smaller organizations can adopt is urgent.⏎III. Barriers to AI Adoption in Safety-Net Settings (RFI Question 1)⏎PHC’s experience highlights several barriers that are likely shared by free clinics,⏎community health centers, and other resource-constrained providers across the country:⏎No governance templates for small organizations. The CHAI/Joint Commission guidance⏎recommends establishing formal AI governance structures with representatives from compliance,⏎IT, clinical programs, operations, and data privacy. PHC does not have separate departments for⏎any of these functions. We built a governance framework that assigns these responsibilities to⏎specific individuals within a 30-person team. HHS should support the development of⏎governance templates scaled to organizations of different sizes—not just large health systems.⏎Vendor opacity on generative AI performance. When PHC evaluates AI vendors, we ask:⏎which large language model powers your product? Has the vendor run repeatability or⏎consistency testing? What is the performance data for non-English and low-income populations?⏎Most vendors cannot answer these questions. HHS should consider requiring transparency in⏎training data composition, known limitations, and performance benchmarks stratified by⏎language and demographics—particularly for tools marketed to safety-net providers.⏎Cost barriers. Monthly licensing costs for AI-powered clinical documentation tools can⏎represent a significant budget line for a free clinic. PHC cannot currently absorb per-provider-⏎per-month pricing designed for large health systems. HHS should explore mechanisms—grants,⏎⏎subsidies, or tiered pricing incentives—to ensure that the productivity benefits of clinical AI are⏎not limited to well-resourced organizations.⏎Default-on vendor features. EHR vendors are beginning to activate AI features by default.⏎PHC’s framework requires that any vendor-released AI feature pass the same three-gate⏎evaluation process as any other tool—but this only works if the clinic is aware the feature has⏎been activated. HHS should consider requiring EHR vendors to provide advance notice and opt-⏎in consent before activating AI features that process patient data.⏎IV. Recommendations for HHS Action (RFI Questions 2, 4, and 5)⏎Based on our direct experience deploying predictive AI and building governance for⏎generative AI, PHC offers the following recommendations:⏎1. Develop evaluation methods specific to generative AI in clinical settings. Current⏎evaluation frameworks are designed for predictive models. HHS should fund the development of⏎standardized evaluation methods for generative AI—including repeatability testing, hallucination⏎detection protocols, and bilingual performance benchmarking—and make these methods freely⏎available to providers of all sizes.⏎2. Require vendor transparency on AI model composition and performance. Providers⏎cannot make informed adoption decisions without knowing which AI models power a product,⏎how those models were trained, and how they perform across different languages and patient⏎populations. HHS should establish minimum disclosure requirements for AI vendors marketing⏎to healthcare organizations.⏎3. Support AI governance capacity in safety-net settings. The CHAI/Joint Commission⏎playbooks under development should include specific guidance for free clinics, community⏎health centers, and critical access hospitals. PHC would welcome the opportunity to contribute⏎our framework and operational experience to this effort.⏎4. Protect patient trust through transparency requirements. For AI tools that record patient-⏎provider conversations—particularly in settings serving immigrant and undocumented⏎populations—clear patient notification and consent requirements are essential. HHS should⏎provide guidance on minimum patient transparency standards for ambient AI in clinical settings.⏎5. Ensure reimbursement pathways do not exclude safety-net providers. PHC’s experience⏎with LumineticsCore demonstrates that autonomous AI can deliver measurable clinical value in a⏎free clinic setting. As HHS considers reimbursement policy for AI-enabled services, it should⏎ensure that payment models account for uninsured and underinsured populations and do not⏎create incentives that concentrate AI adoption only in commercially insured settings.⏎⏎V. Conclusion⏎PHC’s experience demonstrates two things simultaneously: that AI can meaningfully⏎improve care for underserved populations, and that adopting AI responsibly requires governance⏎capacity that most small providers do not have. HHS has an opportunity to accelerate AI⏎adoption across the healthcare system—but only if it ensures that safety-net providers are⏎included in that acceleration, not left behind by it.⏎We welcome the opportunity to discuss our experience further and to contribute to the⏎development of evaluation methods and governance frameworks appropriate for resource-⏎constrained clinical settings.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Mairi Leining, MD, MPH, FACP⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎People’s Health Clinic⏎650 Round Valley Drive⏎PO Box 681558⏎Park City, Utah 84060⏎Bruce Fryer, MBA⏎AI Implementation Lead (pro bono)⏎People’s Health Clinic⏎650 Round Valley Drive⏎PO Box 681558⏎Park City, Utah 84060♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0085♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Blue Cross Blue Shield Association (BCBSA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Blue Cross Blue Shield Association (BCBSA)♔♔♔♔♔♔750 9th Street, NW⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎www.bcbs.com⏎February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted via the Federal Rulemaking Web Portal: http://www.regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The Blue Cross Blue Shield Association (BCBSA) appreciates the opportunity to provide⏎comments on the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)’s Request for Information⏎regarding accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care,⏎as published in the Federal Register on December 23, 2025 (90 FR 60108).⏎The Blue Cross Blue Shield Association is a national federation of independent, community-⏎based and locally operated Blue Cross and Blue Shield companies (Plans) that collectively⏎provide health care coverage for one in three Americans. BCBS Plans contract with 97% of⏎hospitals and 83% of doctors across the country and serve those who are covered through⏎Medicare, Medicaid, an employer, or purchase coverage on their own.⏎BCBSA and BCBS Plans share the Administration’s commitment to advancing responsible AI⏎innovation that improves patient outcomes, enhances clinical decision-making and reduces⏎administrative burdens—while upholding patient privacy, civil rights and civil liberties. AI⏎technologies are already transforming health care by supporting more efficient care delivery,⏎and improved patient engagement and transparency. AI offers revolutionary solutions to support⏎chronic disease prevention and management through earlier risk detection, timely interventions⏎and better care coordination. At the same time, trust in these tools depends on clear⏎governance and accountability, robust protections for patients, thoughtful transparency, and⏎confidence that AI performs reliably across diverse clinical settings and populations.⏎We appreciate HHS’s emphasis on a coordinated approach across regulation, reimbursement,⏎and research & development to support AI use in clinical care. A predictable, risk-based⏎approach, paired with strong cross-agency coordination, can reduce the uncertainty and⏎complexity that impedes innovation, while ensuring safeguards are commensurate with real-⏎1⏎⏎world risk. As HHS considers how best to use these levers to accelerate adoption, it will be⏎important to prioritize approaches that build public trust, emphasize quality and continued⏎improvement, support real-world implementation and avoid duplicative or conflicting⏎requirements. With that in mind, BCBSA offers the following key recommendations:⏎• Harmonize and coordinate federal AI policy, particularly health-specific policies.⏎HHS should provide strong federal leadership to harmonize AI-related frameworks,⏎guidance and templates with widely accepted standards and existing health care⏎requirements, including alignment across state and federal jurisdictions where feasible.⏎This will reduce regulatory fragmentation, avoid duplicative compliance burdens and⏎provide clearer expectations for organizations developing and deploying AI in clinical⏎care.⏎• Adopt flexible, risk-based approaches to policy. HHS should anchor AI policy in⏎consensus-based, voluntary standards and risk management frameworks that scale⏎governance and accountability to the likelihood and severity of potential consumer harm.⏎A flexible approach avoids one-size-fits-all mandates while ensuring more rigorous⏎safeguards for high-risk clinical use cases.⏎• Align reimbursement with demonstrated value. HHS reimbursement policies should⏎reward clinically valid AI that improves outcomes, safety and administrative efficiency,⏎and should not increase payment solely because AI is used. Tying payment to⏎measurable real-world impact will promote competition based on results and accelerate⏎adoption of AI tools that deliver meaningful patient and system value.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide input on HHS’ Request for Information on accelerating⏎the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care. We welcome continued collaboration with⏎HHS and its Divisions as the Department advances a OneHHS approach that supports⏎responsible AI innovation. If you have questions, please contact Jennifer Jones at 202-942-1269⏎or Jennifer.Jones@bcbsa.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Kris Haltmeyer⏎Vice President, Policy Analysis⏎Blue Cross Blue Shield Association⏎2⏎⏎BCBSA’S DETAILED COMMENTS ON THE REQUEST FOR INFORMATION:⏎ACCELERATING THE ADOPTION AND USE OF ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE AS PART OF⏎CLINICAL CARE⏎Recommendation #1:⏎BCBSA recommends that HHS provide strong federal leadership and coordination to ensure its⏎AI-related regulatory frameworks, implementation activities, guidance documents and any⏎associated templates (e.g., model documentation, attestations, reporting formats, and best-⏎practice playbooks) are harmonized with widely accepted standards and align across federal⏎and state approaches where feasible.⏎Rationale:⏎A harmonized approach is essential to reducing uncertainty and accelerating responsible AI⏎adoption in clinical care. Stakeholders are already navigating a rapidly evolving landscape of⏎federal and state initiatives, Executive Orders, and sector-based standards. When requirements⏎and expectations diverge across policies, it creates potentially conflicting obligations and⏎interpretation and implementation challenges for regulators, enforcement entities, developers,⏎deployers and consumers—slowing adoption and increasing compliance costs.⏎BCBSA generally supports the key definitions in the National Association of Insurance⏎Commissioners (NAIC) AI Model Bulletin, which align well with the National Institute of⏎Standards & Technology (NIST) AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF), a widely accepted⏎standard. Aligning HHS materials to these common reference points would promote clarity and⏎consistency across the health care ecosystem, enabling organizations to build repeatable⏎governance, risk management and documentation practices that work across programs and⏎settings. Similarly, recognition of health care quality and accreditation efforts, including those⏎emerging from the National Committee for Quality Assurance (NCQA) can help ensure HHS⏎efforts are not duplicative or conflicting.⏎Harmonization must also account for the unique regulatory environment governing health care⏎and health coverage. Health care differs from many other industries because existing legal⏎frameworks already establish meaningful requirements that directly affect data and AI⏎governance. For example, the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA)⏎provides a comprehensive privacy and security framework for HIPAA-regulated covered entities⏎and their business associates. Greater federal coordination and alignment around appropriate,⏎risk-based requirements that build on these existing obligations would help streamline⏎responsible innovation while accelerating the adoption of AI in clinical care. Aligning HHS AI-⏎related materials with established obligations, rather than layering inconsistent or duplicative⏎requirements, will support responsible innovation and reduce the risk of a patchwork of differing⏎state and federal requirements creates duplicative compliance burdens.⏎Recommendation #2:⏎BCBSA recommends HHS adopt and promote a flexible, risk-based, standards-driven approach⏎as the foundation for responsible use of AI in clinical care.⏎Rationale:⏎Consensus-based, voluntary standards developed by accredited standards organizations⏎grounded in risk-based governance are essential to trustworthy AI. Because AI tools differ⏎3⏎⏎significantly in function, context and potential impact on patients, there is no one-size-fits-all⏎approach to measuring, managing or mitigating risk. Accordingly, HHS policies should scope⏎accountability expectations based on the severity and likelihood of potential consumer harm.⏎Rather than imposing prescriptive requirements, such as mandating specific tests or detailed⏎technical specifications, HHS should leverage and align with flexible, consensus-based risk⏎management frameworks, such as the NIST AI RMF, that allow organizations to tailor risk⏎mitigation to the unique considerations of each AI system. This approach supports innovation⏎while ensuring that safeguards are commensurate with risk, with more rigorous governance,⏎oversight and mitigation measures applied to higher-risk use cases that could materially affect⏎patient safety and clinical outcomes.⏎Recommendation #3:⏎BCBSA recommends that HHS establish a structured, ongoing stakeholder engagement⏎process to inform HHS AI policy development and implementation.⏎Rationale:⏎Participation should include a diverse group of stakeholders, including consumers and⏎consumer groups, technical experts and private-sector entities that will be directly and indirectly⏎impacted—including health insurers. AI policy is being developed in a rapidly changing⏎environment where the knowledge base is evolving quickly and where there is still limited real-⏎world experience regulating and operationalizing AI in clinical settings. A broad, diverse cross-⏎section of stakeholders is essential to ensure HHS approaches are workable across different⏎care settings and to avoid requirements that inadvertently create barriers to adoption or impose⏎burdens that do not meaningfully improve outcomes or protections. Because AI is highly use-⏎case specific, “real-world” input is critical to designing requirements that can be implemented⏎consistently and at scale.⏎Recommendation #4:⏎BCBSA recommends that HHS support education and workforce readiness initiatives to⏎facilitate responsible implementation of AI in clinical care.⏎Rationale:⏎Successful adoption of AI in clinical care depends not only on the technology itself, but also on⏎the readiness of the workforce and the availability of practical implementation support. Many⏎organizations face challenges integrating AI into real-world clinical workflows, establishing⏎appropriate governance, and ensuring staff understand how to use AI tools safely and⏎effectively. Targeted education and workforce readiness efforts can help clinicians,⏎administrators and other users develop the competencies needed to evaluate AI outputs, apply⏎appropriate human oversight and respond to limitations or performance issues. HHS can also⏎reduce uncertainty by advancing additional study and implementation-focused research that⏎identifies effective practices across settings and populations, including smaller and rural⏎providers.⏎Recommendation #5:⏎BCBSA recommends that HHS focus AI-related public policy and any resulting requirements to⏎artificial intelligence systems (AIS), rather than broadly regulating “algorithms.”⏎4⏎⏎Rationale:⏎Algorithms can describe an extremely broad set of processes, including simple business rules⏎and statistically driven empirical approaches that do not necessarily involve AI or advanced⏎computing. If HHS policies are written to cover “algorithms” broadly, they risk sweeping in⏎commonplace operational logic and established analytical methods that do not present the⏎same governance, transparency or safety issues as AIS used in clinical care. The focus should⏎remain on the functional autonomy and emergent risk of the system rather than its underlying⏎algorithmic components. An overly broad application can drive inconsistent interpretation across⏎regulators and increase compliance costs without improving consumer protections. This can⏎also dilute oversight by spreading attention across low-risk, non-AI tools, rather than focusing on⏎AIS uses that may meaningfully affect clinical decisions, patient experiences, and outcomes. A⏎clear AIS-focused approach better supports HHS’s goals of predictability and proportionality, it⏎enables HHS to tailor expectations to risk, strengthen trust where it matters most and reduce⏎uncertainty that can impede innovation and adoption.⏎Recommendation #6:⏎BCBSA recommends that HHS support and operationalize systematic, continuous real-world⏎monitoring of medical devices that use AI through aligned expectations, practical infrastructure⏎and incentives that promote routine post-deployment performance evaluation and transparency⏎over time.⏎Rationale:⏎AI tools used in clinical care must perform safely and effectively in the diverse real-world⏎settings and patient populations where they are deployed, not only in pre-deployment testing⏎environments. Yet, oversight today remains centered on premarket clearance and limited post⏎market surveillance, providing only snapshots of performance at a single point in time. This gap⏎is particularly important for payers, clinicians and patients, because coverage, quality and care⏎decisions depend on longitudinal evidence that the tool continues to perform as intended over⏎time.⏎Continuous post-deployment monitoring is especially important for medical devices that use AI⏎because performance can change as clinical workflows evolve, input data shift, vendor updates⏎occur or patient demographics change. Without systematic monitoring, it is difficult to identify⏎and address model drift, strategic drift (i.e., when an AIS gradually stops reflecting an⏎organization’s clinical policies and ethical guardrails over time, even if accuracy metrics appear⏎stable), degraded accuracy or emergent safety issues. This can undermine public trust and⏎create uncertainty for organizations considering adoption, precisely the uncertainty HHS seeks⏎to reduce. HHS is uniquely positioned to accelerate real-world monitoring by encouraging⏎common expectations and reusable approaches across the Department’s policy levers. More⏎consistent real-world monitoring would also help promote market competition based on⏎demonstrated performance and value and improve confidence in AI-enabled care.⏎Recommendation #7:⏎BCBSA recommends that HHS advance a risk-based, lifecycle approach to testing, evaluation,⏎validation, and verification (TEVV) for AI used in clinical care, coordinated with NIST and other⏎standards-setting organizations, so that TEVV expectations are practical, consistent and⏎proportionate to an AI tool’s clinical impact, intended use, and potential risk.⏎5⏎⏎Rationale:⏎A lifecycle TEVV approach is essential because AI tools are not “set-and-forget” technologies. A⏎risk-based lifecycle TEVV approach, covering both pre-deployment evaluation and ongoing⏎reassessment after deployment, helps ensure safeguards are commensurate with real-world⏎risk. This proportionate approach supports public trust while avoiding burdens that could slow⏎adoption of lower-risk tools. Coordination with NIST and other standards-setting organizations⏎can help establish consensus frameworks for real-world AI evaluation that prioritize⏎transparency and risk alignment. This coordination should build on NIST’s foundational AI RMF⏎and identify opportunities for these frameworks to be meaningfully leveraged across the AI⏎lifecycle in clinical settings. Alignment around widely accepted frameworks is key to minimizing⏎compliance friction. Lifecycle TEVV supports HHS’s reimbursement and R&D objectives by⏎strengthening the evidence base on benefits, costs and tradeoffs associated with AI in clinical⏎care. When TEVV is tied to real-world evaluation and implemented proportionately to risk, it can⏎help ensure that adoption is driven by demonstrated value and that patients are protected.⏎Recommendation #8:⏎BCBSA recommends HHS structure AI clinical care reimbursement policies to promote adoption⏎of clinically valid AI tools that demonstrate improved patient outcomes, patient safety and care⏎team efficiency. Reimbursement should support adoption and scaling of AI that measurably⏎reduces administrative burden and improves the delivery of care, not simply the additional⏎presence of AI.⏎Rationale:⏎AI is increasingly embedded in clinical care, but not all AI-enabled tools deliver meaningful⏎improvements in outcomes or efficiency. Payment policies that provide additional⏎reimbursement simply because AI is used risk encouraging adoption based on tool novelty⏎rather than patient value and can create unintended incentives that increase spending without⏎commensurate benefit. Aligning reimbursement with existing expectations and principles for⏎other clinical interventions, such as demonstrated clinical validity and measurable real-world⏎impact helps ensure patients benefit from AI that supports safer decision-making, improves⏎quality of care and reduces delays in appropriate care. Reimbursement policy should also⏎recognize value where AI streamlines clinical and administrative workflows in ways that improve⏎provider experience, including by reducing the “context tax,” or the time and resource burden⏎clinicians face synthesizing fragmented patient information across systems. Structuring⏎reimbursement around demonstrated value and patient-centered outcomes can help accelerate⏎adoption of AI tools that improve care and affordability.⏎Recommendation #9:⏎BCBSA recommends HHS encourage adoption of high-value AI through value-based payment⏎models and performance-based approaches that reward measurable improvements in quality,⏎efficiency, and patient outcomes.⏎Rationale:⏎Value-based payment models are well suited to promote AI adoption because they align⏎incentives around outcomes and efficiency rather than volume. When providers are accountable⏎for quality and total cost of care, they have stronger incentives to deploy AI tools that improve⏎6⏎⏎care coordination, reduce avoidable utilization and streamline workflows in ways that benefit⏎patients. Incorporating AI-related performance expectations into value-based benchmarks can⏎also promote competition among developers based on real-world impact, rather than creating⏎standalone reimbursement that rewards AI use regardless of benefit. This approach helps⏎ensure that adoption is driven by tools that deliver measurable improvements for patients and⏎the health care system, while reducing the risk of spending increases tied to add-on⏎technologies that do not improve outcomes.⏎7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0086♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Ashar, Binita, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Binita♔Ashar♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔January 19, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎To Whom It May Concern,⏎Please find attached my response to the RIN 0955-AA13 Request for Information on⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Care.⏎My submission focuses on the importance of auditability for AI-enabled and networked surgical⏎systems and the need for reliable, cross-system reconstruction of procedural events to support⏎safety, postmarket learning, and accountability as these technologies scale.⏎I appreciate the opportunity to provide input on this important topic and would welcome the⏎opportunity to clarify or expand on any aspect of this response if helpful.⏎Sincerely,⏎Binita S. Ashar, MD, MBA, FACS, MAMSE⏎Founder, Clarity Surgical Advisors LLC⏎⏎Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Auditability of AI-Enabled and Networked Surgical Systems⏎As HHS seeks to accelerate the safe and trustworthy adoption of AI in clinical care, a⏎foundational barrier has emerged in advanced, AI-enabled and networked surgical systems.⏎These systems integrate devices, software, cloud platforms, networks, and clinician decision-⏎making in real time. When adverse events, unexpected outcomes, or performance questions arise,⏎it is often difficult to reconstruct what actually occurred.⏎This is not primarily a data availability problem. Rather, it is an auditability problem. Relevant⏎information is fragmented across manufacturers, hospitals, electronic health records, device logs,⏎and cloud systems, with no reliable way to assemble a coherent, verifiable account of events.⏎This fragmentation constrains patient safety, postmarket learning, payer confidence, and public⏎trust which are all central to HHS’s mission.⏎Centralized control of all data is neither realistic nor desirable. Instead, HHS should⏎elevate auditability as a system requirement for high-risk, AI-enabled care. By auditability, I⏎mean that authorized parties can reconstruct relevant aspects of a procedure across systems using⏎preserved, reliable records under clear rules of access and use.⏎HHS is well positioned to catalyze progress by coordinating across FDA, CMS, and ONC to⏎define minimum attributes of an auditable procedural record, including traceability, data⏎integrity, continuity across software updates, and authorized access for safety investigations and⏎quality improvement. HHS could further align innovation funding and demonstration programs⏎to prioritize systems that support auditability and encourage interoperability standards that⏎enable cross-system reconstruction of events. CMS, in particular, could incorporate support for⏎auditable records into innovation models or quality programs to better align payment incentives⏎with safer and more learnable AI deployment.⏎Making auditability a baseline expectation would reduce legal and implementation uncertainty,⏎support safer adoption of AI, and enable continuous learning across health systems without⏎slowing innovation. In short, fragmentation is inevitable; lack of auditability is not. Elevating⏎auditability will help HHS achieve its goals of faster, safer, and more trustworthy AI adoption in⏎clinical care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0087♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Amazon, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amazon♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Amazon.com, Inc. (Amazon) appreciates the opportunity to comment on opportunities to accelerate the⏎use of artificial intelligence (AI) to improve care delivery, empower individuals, and save lives. We⏎support the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) mission to encourage the safe adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎Over the last 25 years, Amazon has developed and deployed state-of-the-art AI and machine learning⏎models to power customer experiences. This includes customer-facing services and internal operations⏎that personalize the shopping experience on Amazon.com, the AI-powered robots that optimize order⏎fulfillment in our warehouses, and Amazon Web Services (AWS) building generative AI capabilities that⏎transform customer experiences in industries across the globe.⏎In healthcare, AI is increasingly driving the ability for patients, physicians, and scientists to use the⏎information they have to improve health outcomes. Through use of AI, patients can get insights early to⏎make decisions on where and how to get care; healthcare providers can spend more time directly caring⏎for their patients—not behind a monitor; and scientists can surface insights on the root causes of⏎disease to then develop new innovations to save and improve lives.⏎Amazon Pharmacy and One Medical are leveraging AI to transform healthcare delivery, reduce⏎administrative burden, and make getting health care easier for patients. Amazon Pharmacy uses AI to⏎process prescriptions 90% faster by automatically structuring prescription data, predicting medication⏎inventory needs, providing real-time price estimates without manual insurance entry, and enhancing⏎customer support through AI-powered documentation analysis. Beyond operational efficiency, AI-⏎powered clinical decision support can improve patient safety by identifying potential adverse drug⏎interactions, predicting patient deterioration for early intervention, and accelerating diagnosis of time-⏎critical conditions through AI-enhanced imaging triage.⏎One Medical is deploying generative AI to handle routine administrative tasks such as reviewing health⏎records, identifying care gaps, routing messages, and automating workflows—giving providers more⏎time to focus on patient care rather than paperwork. For patients, One Medical is using AI to make⏎accessing health care even simpler, more highly personalized, and more actionable through Health AI⏎that can answer health questions, book appointments, and help manage medications. One Medical’s⏎Health AI assistant provides 24/7 personalized health guidance, can explain lab results, book⏎⏎appointments, and manage medications with HIPAA-compliant security. Health AI was developed with⏎One Medical providers providing clinical guidance across the entire development lifecycle, embedding⏎safety principles from conception through launch. We are committed to using advanced technology to⏎make healthcare more personal, accessible, and human-centered while respecting patient privacy and⏎enhancing clinical decision-making.⏎We are dedicated to making our customers’ and patients’ lives better while also establishing and⏎implementing the necessary safeguards to protect them. Amazon has a long-standing track record of⏎protecting customer data. For example, One Medical and Amazon Pharmacy protect protected health⏎information (PHI) with HIPAA-compliant privacy and security practices to keep information safe and⏎secure. All our services go through rigorous security testing, and we maintain strict administrative,⏎physical, and technical safeguards to protect our systems and customer information, such as through⏎encryption technology and strict controls over who can access records.⏎Through AWS, we support healthcare providers, insurers, electronic health record (EHR) vendors, and⏎other organizations to leverage AI to generate efficiencies and improve patient outcomes. AWS offers⏎purpose-built healthcare AI services, including HealthScribe to support clinical documentation, Health⏎Lake to enable data interoperability, and HealthImaging for faster analytics of medical images. AWS also⏎recently announced agentic AI capabilities in Amazon Connect—which automates routine patient⏎interactions like appointment scheduling that represent 50% of call volume. We also developed an⏎open-source Healthcare and Life Sciences Agentic AI toolkit, which includes starter agents built on⏎Amazon Bedrock. And, we recently released Amazon Nova Forge, which empowers organizations to⏎build custom frontier AI models tailored to their specific needs while maintaining data privacy.⏎We encourage ASTP/ONC to take several steps to further accelerate the benefits of AI adoption in care⏎and to work with innovation-forward states testing novel AI technologies within their regulatory⏎jurisdictions.⏎Considerations for Oversight of AI/ML in Healthcare⏎When evaluating the role of government in overseeing the use of AI/ML in healthcare, we encourage⏎you to consider several factors prior to the development of any new policies.⏎First, ASTP/ONC should consider whether the robust policies already in place have gaps unique to AI. For⏎example, the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) is already well suited with its existing authorities to⏎oversee the use of AI for regulated products, including clinical decision support tools that meet the⏎definition of medical devices. In fact, as of late 2025, FDA had already cleared or approved more than⏎1350 devices with AI/ML components—dating back to 1995. ASTP/ONC should only focus new policies⏎on AI-specific gaps in existing authorities. We also caution that new policies that duplicate existing⏎authorities can raise complications and burdens that do not generate any further risk mitigation or⏎patient care benefits. Instead, duplicative regulation may slow deployment of health technologies that⏎could improve the quality of care. Therefore, we recommend ASTP/ONC publish a formal mapping of⏎existing regulations to AI use cases in clinical care, identifying only those gaps that require new policy⏎intervention. This would prevent duplicative requirements that could delay deployment of beneficial⏎technologies.⏎Second, Amazon supports a risk-based approach to AI/ML oversight and appropriate transparency for⏎patients and clinicians. Recognizing that AI applications are heterogenous, a focus on high-risk activity⏎helps to ensure that potential harms are addressed without stifling innovation or impeding access to⏎Page 2 of 4⏎⏎benefits from low-risk uses of AI. In the healthcare context, risk-based approaches are already widely⏎adopted—with policies varying based on the specific use cases and effect of the technology on patient⏎care (e.g., as medical devices are already regulated based on risk). We appreciate ASTP/ONC’s recent⏎proposed rule that would rescind the Decision Support Intervention transparency requirements as they⏎required disclosures of source attribute information regardless of the risk associated with the⏎technology.⏎Third, when promoting transparency to support patient and clinician trust in AI-based tools, we⏎encourage ASTP/ONC to first evaluate the methods that AI developers already use to disclose⏎information on models—such as AWS AI Service Cards that provide appropriate disclosure about model⏎capabilities, limitations, and intended uses. When fostering transparency, we caution against the⏎disclosure of proprietary information, such as training data, training recipes, or model architecture,⏎which are confidential commercial secrets with significant competitive value. If regulators require model⏎developers to disclose information about such data, it may foreclose the developer’s ability to claim⏎trade secret protection under the law. These concerns are only amplified if organizations seek to use⏎larger models, like foundation models, which are trained on a central corpus of internet scale data and⏎are intended to be used by many customers across many use cases. Training data disclosure⏎requirements are therefore impractical and not well suited to evaluating appropriateness for use.⏎Training datasets vary based on many factors, including size, demographic makeup (the number and⏎type of groups), quality (amount of confounding variation, fit for purpose), the types and quality of⏎labeling, and other factors. Simply because a model has been trained on certain data does not mean it⏎will perform as needed in a specific use case.⏎Finally, we encourage ASTP/ONC to align with international standards—many of which are currently in⏎development—to provide consistency, clarity, and thoroughness to support the effective and safe⏎development and use of AI in healthcare. For example, ISO 42001 is a management system standard⏎that provides guidelines for managing AI systems within organizations. This standard emphasizes a⏎commitment to responsible AI practices, encouraging organizations to adopt controls specific to their AI⏎systems, fostering global interoperability, and setting a foundation for the development and⏎deployment of responsible AI. Amazon is committed to the responsible development of AI, as⏎demonstrated by AWS being the first major cloud service provider to announce ISO 42001 accredited⏎certification for several of its services.⏎Apply new policies to applications, not specific technologies⏎Many uses of AI provide enhancements to existing healthcare workflows and processes. For example,⏎clinicians already use diverse approaches to document care, including via: direct entry by clinicians;⏎template-based documentation tools; in-room medical scribes; remote scribes; and transcription⏎technologies based on AI. Each approach carries different risks and benefits; applying regulatory⏎requirements on only one of those modes could impede innovation and adoption.⏎Instead, when evaluating policy guidance or regulations on an aspect of clinical care, ASTP/ONC should⏎consider existing quality and safety requirements applicable to that use case. Taking an approach⏎grounded in the use case—rather than specific tools, such as AI—would avoid singling-out certain⏎technologies and stifle innovation in their development and application.⏎Focus on testing in deployed settings⏎As mentioned above, regulatory focus on training data does not provide actionable information for⏎patients, clinicians, and deployers. Especially for high-risk healthcare use cases, rather than focusing on⏎Page 3 of 4⏎⏎training data, testing AI-based tools in the intended environment with data reflecting the intended⏎population is the scientifically appropriate and industry-standard mechanism that provides the best⏎indication of performance.⏎There are some parallels between AI and EHR systems. With EHRs, the performance and safe use of the⏎system can vary based on differences in users, patient populations, the prevalence of other⏎technologies, workflows, and many other factors. That’s why, with EHRs, testing in the intended⏎environment is a core component of evaluating the safety and usability of the system.⏎The same applies with AI, where deployers—such as a health system—should test AI-based technologies⏎in their environments to evaluate whether the tool works appropriately and—where needed—apply risk⏎mitigations. ASTP/ONC should consider guidance to health systems on how to assess the performance of⏎technologies in their intended settings, much like how the agency led the development of the Safety⏎Assurance Factors for Electronic Health Record Resilience (SAFER) Guides for evaluation of EHR⏎performance in healthcare organizations.⏎Encourage greater data interoperability to improve care, support AI⏎EHR systems, claims databases, and other technologies increasingly house and make available⏎information to empower patients and improve care. When coupled with AI, this information can help:⏎identify treatments for unmet needs; equip patients and clinicians with personalized recommendations⏎through decision support tools; and otherwise generate insights to better understand the safety and⏎quality of care.⏎Yet, the data currently accessible via standard application programming interfaces (APIs) is not yet fully⏎sufficient to support many improvements to the patient experience and enhanced care. For example,⏎the versions of the US Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) currently required for support by certified⏎EHRs lacks access to cancer care data, genomic data elements links to download medical images—⏎including X-Rays and MRI scans. API-based access to these data elements, when used with AI-based⏎technologies, can enable enhanced patient-specific guidance that factors in each individual’s unique⏎clinical situation. AI integration with EHR systems can transform clinical workflows by providing real-⏎time decision support, automating documentation, and enabling seamless data exchange through FHIR-⏎based APIs. AWS HealthLake provides infrastructure for organizations to meet ASTP/ONC and CMS⏎interoperability requirements. We encourage ASTP/ONC to make these—and other—data elements⏎available via standard APIs to accelerate the utility of AI-powered tools in clinical care.⏎Relatedly, with appropriate patient authorization and in compliance with HIPAA, healthcare⏎organizations have sought to use protected health information (PHI) to develop AI tools to support the⏎treatment, payment, and operations of facilities. Health systems have also sought to use de-identified⏎data to improve the capabilities of AI-based technologies, however, the de-identification of data may⏎make the information less useful. Through AWS Clean Rooms, healthcare organizations can securely⏎analyze and collaborate on their collective datasets—without sharing or copying one another’s⏎underlying data. This technology, when coupled with AI, can further enable the development of health-⏎specific technologies without disclosure of the underlying, sensitive data. This service can allow⏎organizations to discover, evaluate, and access patient cohort data in a privacy-preserving manner and⏎without any data movement.⏎Thank you for considering our recommendations, and we look forward to serving as a resource to ASTP⏎on accelerating the ability for AI to improve patient care.⏎Page 4 of 4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0088♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from HealthEx, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HealthEx♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎RIN 0955–AA13⏎Submitted by: HealthEx 2339 3rd St., Suite 41 San Francisco, CA 94107 info@healthex.io |⏎www.healthex.io⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎1⏎⏎1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎HealthEx is building the modern stack for consumer-driven care - combining digital identity,⏎real-time records access, and consent management - and is already live powering AI grounded⏎in individuals’ personal health history through its partnership with Anthropic, announced in⏎January 2026.⏎HealthEx appreciates the opportunity to respond to HHS's Request for Information on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care. Our response is grounded in a⏎core conviction: the single greatest barrier to effective clinical AI is not the model - it is the⏎data. AI tools can only deliver personalized, clinically meaningful insights when they are⏎grounded in a patient's actual, comprehensive health history. Today, that data remains⏎fragmented, institutionally siloed, and inaccessible to the AI tools that patients and clinicians⏎increasingly rely on.⏎HealthEx solves this problem and we are the only platform live with both TEFCA Individual⏎Access Services (IAS) and a FHIR endpoint directory for patient-directed records retrieval,⏎enabling individuals to securely access their complete health records across all their places of⏎care and share them with AI tools, providers, payers, and trusted third parties of their choosing.⏎HealthEx responds to Questions 1, 2, 5, 6, 8, and 9 below. Our recommendations center on⏎three themes: (1) interoperability and patient-directed data access are the foundational⏎infrastructure for clinical AI; (2) HHS should leverage existing frameworks - particularly TEFCA⏎IAS - to accelerate AI adoption rather than creating parallel regimes; and (3) patient⏎empowerment and trust must be embedded in AI deployment, not bolted on after the fact. We⏎also note the uniquely new requirements emerging from conversational AI contexts around⏎more efficient and real-time mechanisms to keep a patient’s clinical record updated, and the⏎need for high-quality imaging data to be available to patients to access via IAL2/IAS.⏎RFI prepared by:⏎● Priyanka Agarwal, MD, MBA, Co-founder and CEO, HealthEx⏎● Anand Raghavan, Co-founder and CTO, HealthEx⏎● Jeremy Schwarz, Chief Commercial Officer, HealthEx⏎2⏎⏎2. ABOUT HEALTHEX⏎HealthEx is building the modern stack for consumer-driven healthcare. Combining digital⏎identity, unified longitudinal records, and consent and permissions, HealthEx presents to end⏎users as a health data wallet that enables them to securely access and share their health⏎records with trusted third parties across payers, digital apps, and AI agents in a repeatable and⏎efficient manner.⏎Below are key differentiators of our platform and approach:⏎● Dual approach to records retrieval across best-in-class TEFCA IAS and FHIR⏎endpoint based flows: HealthEx is the only team enabling comprehensive records⏎access via IAS under TEFCA and a FHIR endpoint directory. Our record locator service⏎automatically matches individuals' demographic information - including historical⏎addresses - to find where they have health records across thousands of places of care⏎nationwide, eliminating the need for patients to recall every provider they've seen.⏎HealthEx connects to the TEFCA network-of-networks infrastructure via our QHIN⏎partners, MedAllies and CommonWell, spanning 12,000+ organizations and 72,000+⏎unique connections. HealthEx additionally supports direct connections to over 52,000⏎healthcare organizations via FHIR-based exchange.⏎Through these efforts, HealthEx supports records retrieval for 250M Americans,⏎supporting records access for ~90% of provider organizations.⏎● The HealthEx Digital Wallet for records powers Identity-powered network effects :⏎The HealthEx Digital Wallet enables users to access their records once and share⏎across all platforms where they've chosen to connect, creating powerful network effects.⏎HealthEx's identity foundation (IAL2/AAL2 via CLEAR) powers the digital wallet⏎capabilities- a Claude user can seamlessly share the same data to other AI tools or⏎providers. This identity layer is also foundational for agentic care experiences like EHR⏎write-back.⏎● AI-optimized data delivery: The HealthEx MCP server is built for performance, speed,⏎and accuracy while giving users privacy and control. It addresses the unique challenges⏎at the intersection of health data and AI: context-window constraints (through proprietary⏎data cleaning and compression achieving ~90% improvement), and privacy (honoring⏎consent on every tool call, returning only query-relevant data, and operating exclusively⏎in contexts where model training is off). AI-ready data can also be delivered via REST⏎APIs.⏎● Production-proven at scale : Live integration with Anthropic's Claude (January 2026).⏎Additional launches in Q1with two top-5 health insurers, and two additional large-scale⏎AI teams. Across its ecosystem, HealthEx will be enabled for 10M+ Americans in Q1.⏎● Deep ecosystem partnerships: HealthEx is collaborating with every major EHR⏎provider across Epic, athenahealth, Cerner, Veradigm (AllScripts), eClinicalWorks,⏎3⏎⏎MEDITECH, NextGen, and NetSmart. These are not proprietary partnerships, rather⏎they represent a willingness from HealthEx to work together across the ecosystem.⏎● HealthEx collaborates closely with every major EHR provider including Epic,⏎athenahealth, Cerner, Veradigm (AllScripts), eClinicalWorks, MEDITECH, NextGen,⏎Greenway, and NetSmart. Here are a few recent, relevant public announcements and⏎efforts:⏎Epic⏎Athena/Medallies⏎MEDITECH⏎CMS Interoperability Initiatives⏎Powering AI for Claude & Americans at scale:⏎In January 2026, HealthEx launched a live integration with Anthropic's Claude through the⏎Model Context Protocol (MCP), enabling consumers to connect their health records and use⏎conversational AI for personalized health insights grounded in their clinical history. This was the⏎first patient-data MCP integration with a major AI platform. HealthEx has forthcoming partner⏎announcements in Q1 2026 that will extend its consumer network to over 10 million US users.⏎HealthEx is a San Francisco-based company backed by General Catalyst. HealthEx is SOC2⏎and TX-RAMP certified, and is an approved IAS vendor in TEFCA.⏎4⏎⏎3. RESPONSE TO SPECIFIC QUESTIONS⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI⏎for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The biggest barrier to clinical AI innovation is the fragmentation and inaccessibility of⏎patient health data to provide context to AI.⏎AI models are only as useful as the data they can access. Today, a patient's health records are⏎scattered across dozens of providers, health systems, and payer platforms, each with its own⏎portal, credentials, and data formats. This fragmentation means that even the most advanced AI⏎tools operate on incomplete, single-source data – producing generic insights rather than the⏎personalized, longitudinal reasoning that transforms clinical care.⏎The difference is significant. An AI tool with access to a single EHR system can tell a patient⏎what a lab result means in isolation. An AI tool grounded in a patient's full longitudinal record –⏎spanning conditions, medications, labs, immunizations, clinical notes, and procedures across⏎every provider they've seen – can identify trends over time, surface patterns across specialists,⏎and help patients prepare for more informed conversations with their doctors. This is the kind of⏎complex, contextual reasoning that meaningfully improves care.⏎HealthEx has demonstrated that this barrier is solvable with existing infrastructure. Through⏎TEFCA IAS and our FHIR endpoint directory, we enable patients to pull their complete records⏎in under five minutes at the median and connect them directly to AI tools. Since launching with⏎Anthropic's Claude in January 2026, approximately half of HealthEx connector users return⏎within five days to have another conversation using their health data, a strong signal that⏎patients find real value in AI grounded in their clinical history.⏎Beyond data fragmentation, we see three additional barriers:⏎1. The evaluation paradigm for clinical AI has not kept pace with the speed of model⏎development. Models are updated on cycles measured in weeks, but evaluation⏎frameworks still reflect the cadence of traditional medical device review. HHS should⏎support real-time, outcomes-based evaluation methods for a set of templatized use⏎cases that can assess AI performance as models evolve - rather than evaluating the⏎efficacy of AI on models that are already out of date by the time the assessment is⏎complete.⏎2. The consent and trust infrastructure for AI-health-data interactions remains⏎underdeveloped . Standard MCP server frameworks do not come with native privacy or⏎consent semantics. HealthEx has built this layer – honoring consent on every tool call,⏎returning only data relevant to a specific query, ensuring users control whether data is⏎saved across sessions, and operating only in environments where model training on PHI⏎is off. HHS should encourage the development and adoption of these trust-enabling⏎5⏎⏎practices across the ecosystem, and HealthEx would be pleased to lend its expertise⏎and experience in the consent arena.⏎3. Regulatory uncertainty around non-medical-device AI tools creates hesitancy⏎among both developers and health systems . Organizations are unsure how AI tools⏎that synthesize patient records, generate care preparation summaries, or support shared⏎decision-making fit within existing regulatory and liability frameworks. HHS can reduce⏎this uncertainty by clarifying that existing frameworks already accommodate these use⏎cases and establishing proportionate guidance - removing ambiguity rather than adding⏎new requirements or regulations.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design⏎changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in⏎clinical care and why?⏎HHS should prioritize policy changes that ensure patient health data can flow to AI tools⏎of a patient's choosing, securely and at scale. The regulatory and payment infrastructure⏎that governs health data exchange will determine whether clinical AI reaches its potential or⏎remains constrained by the same data silos that have limited interoperability for decades.⏎We recommend the following specific policy actions:⏎Strengthen TEFCA IAS (or broader CMS-Aligned network IAS) adoption via Incentivation⏎from CMS. TEFCA's Individual Access Services pathway is the most significant federal⏎infrastructure enabling patient-directed data exchange at national scale, and we believe is the⏎most secure and user-friendly mode for patients to access their data. IAS enables patients to⏎retrieve their records from across the TEFCA network and direct them to any trusted third party,⏎including AI tools. HHS should incentivize CMS-regulated entities to participate in TEFCA, and⏎specifically that all TEFCA Participants and Subparticipants respond to IAS requests. While⏎TEFCA adoption has been strong, it is still voluntary and there are some organizations who⏎have not yet pledged to join.⏎Today, some organizations decline IAS queries due to lingering uncertainty about whether⏎TEFCA's identity and authorization verification meets HIPAA requirements. HHS should issue⏎guidance confirming that IAL2/AAL2 identity verification plus IAS consent satisfies HIPAA⏎identity and authorization requirements under 45 CFR 164.524, removing some of the basis for⏎non-response.⏎Grant safe harbor to organizations performing patient matching and proposed “double⏎checking” as part of IAS fulfillment. Hesitancy to respond to IAS queries is partly driven by⏎liability concerns around patient matching. Granting qualified immunity to organizations that⏎perform patient matching in response to IAL2-enabled IAS requests would reduce this barrier⏎and unlock broader participation.⏎6⏎⏎Reject additional authorization burdens that impede patient access. Some EHR vendors⏎currently require patients to provide patient portal credentials on top of IAL2 identity verification⏎when fulfilling IAS requests. This added step is not universally required - users of athenahealth⏎and eClinical works EHRs, for example, do not face it - and creates a friction point that⏎disproportionately burdens patients with limited digital literacy, multiple providers, or complex⏎care needs. HHS should clarify if IAS requests using IAL2/AAL2 tokens plus digitally⏎enforceable consent are sufficient for authorization as well, without additional portal credential⏎requirements. We are advocates to converge on the most friction free flows for end users⏎possible, while respecting the applicable legal requirements.⏎Enable persistent data connections with extended token validity and refresh⏎mechanisms. Nearly all patients expect their health data connections to remain fresh and⏎current without manual intervention after each provider visit or lab result. Under the current IAS⏎framework, OIDC tokens from credential service providers expire within 30 days, requiring⏎patients to re-authenticate biometrically for subsequent data access. This is a significant step⏎backward from the refresh token model available under patient portal-based SMART-on-FHIR⏎workflows. HHS should update guidance to clarify that IAL2 tokens may persist for an extended⏎period – up to 12 months – with appropriate refresh mechanisms, so patients can direct⏎recurring data access to AI tools and other trusted parties without repeated identity verification.⏎Support push-based data updates to maintain record freshness. Manual refresh workflows⏎place an unreasonable burden on patients and create gaps in the data available to AI tools.⏎HHS should establish standards and incentives for push-based notifications when new clinical⏎data becomes available. This could include webhook-based updates triggered when new⏎encounters, lab results, or imaging studies are documented, enabling AI tools to maintain⏎current, comprehensive patient context without requiring patients to manually pull updates after⏎every healthcare interaction. Push-based updates aligned with patient consent would⏎dramatically improve the clinical utility of AI while reducing patient friction.⏎Modernize payment policy to accelerate AI adoption . The RFI rightly identifies the limitations⏎of legacy fee-for-service regimes in supporting innovation. HHS should explore multiple⏎payment mechanisms to ensure AI tools that demonstrably reduce burden, lower costs, or⏎improve outcomes can reach patients:⏎● Medicare Advantage supplemental benefits: CMS should clarify that AI-enabled care⏎navigation, health literacy, and care coordination tools qualify as supplemental benefits⏎under MA plans, enabling plans to offer these capabilities to beneficiaries without⏎additional regulatory approval.⏎● CMMI innovation models: CMS should establish innovation models that test AI-enabled⏎care pathways - including AI-assisted prior authorization, care gap identification, and⏎care navigation - under value-based payment arrangements that reward outcomes⏎rather than volume.⏎● Safe harbors for patient-directed AI tools: HHS should consider safe harbor protections⏎under anti-kickback statutes for AI tools that are patient-directed and operate on⏎7⏎⏎patient-authorized data, removing the legal uncertainty that currently deters health⏎systems from recommending AI tools to patients.⏎Accelerate public availability of a national healthcare endpoint directory that maps OIDs⏎to Patient portals. Effective AI-driven care requires that patients (or platforms acting on their⏎behalf) can discover where their records are held. We were pleased to see CMS’s recent⏎commitments toward building a national healthcare endpoint directory, and urge HHS to⏎expedite its development. Consistent with the ONC information blocking regulations at 45 CFR⏎Part 171, providers should be expected to publish and maintain FHIR API endpoints in this⏎directory, ensuring accurate record location at scale.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can best support private sector AI innovation by strengthening the interoperability⏎infrastructure that AI tools depend on and by endorsing trust frameworks for AI-health-data⏎interactions.⏎The current generation of patient-facing AI tools has focused primarily on information and⏎insights-helping patients understand their health data through natural language conversations.⏎However, significant consumer demand exists for AI that can move beyond explanation to⏎action: booking appointments, messaging providers, requesting prescription refills, and⏎coordinating care. To realize this vision, the healthcare ecosystem needs standardized protocols⏎that enable AI platforms to both access comprehensive health data and interact with clinical⏎systems to fulfill real-world care tasks-all with appropriate patient consent and security controls.⏎The HealthEx integration with Anthropic's Claude demonstrates how private sector⏎collaboration, built on public infrastructure, can deliver immediate patient value while creating a⏎foundation for more advanced capabilities. HealthEx leverages TEFCA-a federal framework-to⏎retrieve patient records, then delivers them to Claude via the Model Context Protocol with⏎consent honored on every interaction. Today, consumers can ask Claude to explain their lab⏎results, identify patterns across their health history, and prepare questions for their doctors, all⏎grounded in their actual clinical data. HealthEx is actively working to extend this integration⏎beyond information and insights to agentic care fulfillment, creating a comprehensive healthcare⏎MCP that supports the full spectrum from understanding health to acting on it.⏎HHS should create pathways for patient-directed AI tools to serve Medicare beneficiaries.⏎Approaches already demonstrated in the commercial market provide a model for what these⏎pathways could enable. For example, the HealthEx-Claude integration has shown that⏎consumers will engage deeply with AI grounded in their comprehensive health records. A similar⏎approach for Medicare beneficiaries could deliver three transformative capabilities:⏎8⏎⏎Personalized AI for health understanding: Through the HealthEx x Claude integration or⏎similar AI-powered experiences, Medicare beneficiaries could access conversational AI⏎grounded in their complete longitudinal health record. Members could ask natural⏎language questions about their medications, understand what their latest lab results⏎mean in the context of their conditions, identify patterns in their vitals over time, and⏎prepare informed questions before appointments-all in plain language, with explanations⏎tailored to their actual clinical history rather than generic health information.⏎Personalized, proactive care enabled by a 360-degree health picture: With a⏎comprehensive view of each beneficiary's health-spanning conditions, medications, labs,⏎procedures, and care gaps across all their providers-AI can surface personalized,⏎proactive recommendations. The system could identify when a diabetic patient is⏎overdue for an eye exam, flag potential medication interactions when new prescriptions⏎are added, or notice declining kidney function trends that warrant earlier intervention.⏎This comprehensive view, made possible by HealthEx's aggregation across the TEFCA⏎network and FHIR endpoints, transforms reactive care into proactive health⏎management.⏎Agentic care fulfillment for real-world action: Beyond insights, AI can help Medicare⏎beneficiaries act on recommendations-scheduling that overdue eye exam with an⏎in-network ophthalmologist, booking their annual wellness visit, coordinating⏎transportation to appointments, or requesting prescription refills. This vision of⏎AI-assisted care navigation, grounded in comprehensive patient data and executed with⏎patient consent, could meaningfully reduce barriers to care access for Medicare⏎beneficiaries while alleviating administrative burden on providers and plans.⏎These three capabilities-personalized AI understanding, proactive care recommendations, and⏎agentic fulfillment-represent a fundamentally different member experience: one where Medicare⏎beneficiaries have a trusted AI companion that knows their complete health story, helps them⏎understand it, identifies what care they need, and helps them get it done. HHS should create⏎pathways for patient-directed AI tools to serve Medicare beneficiaries, modeled on approaches⏎already demonstrated in the commercial market⏎HHS can promote more such innovation by supporting industry-driven trust frameworks⏎for AI-health-data connectors. These frameworks should address identity verification, consent⏎management, data provenance, and data minimization practices. Establishing clear, adoptable⏎standards for how AI platforms should handle health data will accelerate responsible innovation⏎across the private sector.⏎HHS should also support pilot programs and cooperative research agreements that test⏎patient-authorized AI use cases in real clinical workflows. Pilot structures could be used to⏎test AI-enabled form filling (reducing administrative burden during e-check-in), AI-assisted prior⏎authorization (using patient-directed clinical data to accelerate approvals), and AI-driven care⏎coordination (surfacing missing screenings and follow-ups from longitudinal records). These are⏎9⏎⏎use cases HealthEx is actively deploying with partners including major national payers and⏎health systems.⏎HHS should establish mechanisms for real-time evaluation of model performance and⏎patient experience with clinical AI. As AI tools move from advisory roles to agentic care⏎fulfillment, continuous monitoring becomes essential. This includes tracking accuracy of clinical⏎recommendations, patient satisfaction and trust, identification of bias or disparities in⏎AI-generated recommendations, and measurement of clinical outcomes and cost impacts.⏎Public-private partnerships could establish shared evaluation frameworks and reporting⏎standards that enable rapid identification of safety issues while preserving innovation velocity.⏎CMS could leverage Medicare claims data combined with patient-reported outcomes to create⏎feedback loops that improve AI performance over time, similar to post-market surveillance for⏎medical devices but adapted for the unique characteristics of AI systems that learn and evolve.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded⏎performance and cost expectations and where have they fallen short?⏎Patient-facing AI grounded in comprehensive health records has exceeded engagement⏎expectations, demonstrating strong demand for personalized health AI.⏎The HealthEx integration with Anthropic’s Claude, launched in January 2026, provides early but⏎compelling evidence that patient-facing AI tools can exceed engagement benchmarks when⏎grounded in comprehensive, longitudinal health data. Key findings from the initial deployment:⏎• 87% of users who go through our TEFCA flows have at least 1 site of care automatically⏎identified, with a median retrieval time of under five minutes. The average user retrieves⏎3.3 records through our TEFCA + FHIR endpoint flows, compared to 1.4 records when⏎going through our FHIR endpoint only flow, which requires that the patient provide their⏎portal logins in order to gain access. For the average user, TEFCA IAS retrieval is⏎approximately 43% faster than FHIR endpoint-only retrieval, reflecting the elimination of⏎manual provider search and portal login steps.⏎• 83% of users with retrieved records initiate a health conversation, with health summaries⏎and lab result interpretation as the most common entry points. This significantly exceeds⏎typical engagement rates for patient-facing health tools.⏎• Approximately 50% of users return within five days for subsequent conversations,⏎indicating sustained utility rather than one-time curiosity.⏎• Users engage across the full breadth of their clinical data-conditions, medications, labs,⏎immunizations, procedures, and clinical notes-suggesting that comprehensive⏎longitudinal data, not single-source snapshots, is what drives meaningful AI interactions.⏎Where AI tools have fallen short, data limitations are almost always the root cause. AI tools⏎operating on data from a single EHR system or institution produce generic insights that patients⏎do not find actionable. The gap between single-source and comprehensive-data AI is not⏎incremental-it is the difference between an AI that can explain a lab result and one that can⏎identify a trend across three years of visits to multiple specialists. Closing this gap requires the⏎interoperability and patient-directed data access reforms outlined in our response to Question 2.⏎10⏎⏎AI tools today also do not have access to imaging data as these sit on PACS systems that are⏎not governed by the 21st Century Cures act in terms of access by patients. This limits their⏎ability to have a 360 degree view of the patient’s condition.⏎Lastly, the current background poll based mechanisms without incremental updates of keeping a⏎user’s data up to date result in highly inefficient volumes of queries which return with no new⏎information about a patient. This drives up implementation costs as the traditional business⏎models in this space rely on the number of successful record pulls which are not always⏎incentivized to keep the number of queries to a minimum.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market⏎opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the development of AI for⏎clinical care?⏎Enhanced interoperability – specifically, patient-directed interoperability via TEFCA IAS –⏎is the single most important accelerant for clinical AI.⏎Today, clinical AI tools overwhelmingly operate on data from a single EHR system or a single⏎institution. This constrains the quality, completeness, and generalizability of AI insights and⏎limits the market to use cases that can function within institutional silos. Patient-directed data⏎access, by contrast, unlocks longitudinal, multi-source data that enables fundamentally different⏎AI capabilities: cross-provider trend analysis, comprehensive medication reconciliation,⏎complete care gap identification, and personalized risk assessment grounded in a patient's full⏎clinical history.⏎Specific areas where enhanced interoperability would widen market opportunities:⏎TEFCA IAS enables AI tools to access comprehensive patient records without requiring bilateral⏎data-sharing agreements with every provider organization. This dramatically lowers the barrier⏎to entry for AI developers and creates a level playing field where innovation is rewarded based⏎on the quality of insights delivered, not the depth of institutional relationships. HealthEx's⏎integration with Claude demonstrates this: any Claude Pro or Max subscriber can connect their⏎full health record and receive personalized insights, regardless of which health systems they⏎visit.⏎Production data from the HealthEx-Claude integration illustrates the breadth of interoperability⏎required. The average user has records across multiple provider organizations, requiring⏎connections to health systems, ambulatory practices, and labs. HealthEx retrieves data via both⏎TEFCA IAS and direct FHIR connections, with the dual approach proving essential - TEFCA⏎provides broad network reach while FHIR endpoints enable connections to organizations not yet⏎participating in TEFCA. Neither pathway alone provides sufficient coverage; the combination is⏎what achieves the ~90% provider coverage that makes comprehensive AI possible.⏎Enhanced interoperability also accelerates research by making patient-authorized clinical data⏎available for outcomes evaluation and implementation science. When patients can direct their⏎11⏎⏎longitudinal records to AI tools and research platforms, it becomes possible to evaluate AI⏎performance against real-world outcomes at a scale and speed that institutional data alone⏎cannot support.⏎Specific data types, standards, and tools where progress is needed:⏎USCDI v3 provides a strong baseline, but imaging data (particularly from PACS servers)⏎remains difficult to access in lossless formats through standard exchange pathways. Making⏎diagnostic imaging available through TEFCA or FHIR-based exchange would significantly⏎expand AI capabilities in radiology, oncology, and other imaging-intensive specialties.⏎Additionally, establishing a common quality standard for data shared across sources - potentially⏎using novel approaches like open-source LLM-based quality scoring with public weights for⏎transparency - would help AI developers assess data reliability.⏎FHIR R4 and C-CDA are working well as exchange standards, but the practical challenge is that⏎not all organizations participate in electronic records exchange. As of 2024, roughly a quarter of⏎office-based physicians report never sending electronic records to outside providers. TEFCA's⏎network model can reduce connectivity costs, but HHS policy encouraging broader participation⏎- particularly among small, rural, and independent practices - is essential to closing the⏎remaining gaps that limit AI's access to comprehensive patient data.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers⏎wish to see addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients want three things from AI in their care: the ability to understand their own health⏎records, access to comprehensive data across all their providers, and control over who⏎sees what.⏎As a physician, I have seen firsthand how patients struggle to make sense of fragmented health⏎information scattered across multiple portals. The promise of AI in clinical care is that it can⏎serve as a bridge - translating clinical data into plain-language insights, identifying what matters⏎most, and helping patients prepare for conversations with their doctors. Early results from the⏎HealthEx-Claude integration confirm this demand: 83% of users with records start a health⏎conversation, with health summaries and lab results as the most common entry points, and⏎approximately half of users return within five days.⏎Patients also want AI to help them navigate the complexity of a fragmented system. This⏎includes understanding medication interactions across providers who don't talk to each other,⏎identifying gaps in preventive care, and preparing for specialist visits with a complete picture of⏎their history. These are use cases that only work when AI is grounded in comprehensive,⏎longitudinal data - not data from a single institution.⏎12⏎⏎Patients and caregivers also want AI to move beyond insights and into real-world care⏎action.⏎AI tools today excel at synthesizing information, but they do not yet extend into care⏎fulfillment-booking appointments, writing back to EHRs, initiating prior authorizations, or⏎coordinating referrals. Consumer demand for these capabilities is strong. HealthEx’s identity,⏎consent, and records access capabilities are foundational for enabling these agentic care⏎experiences, and we are actively co-designing EHR write-back capabilities with Epic. HHS⏎should consider how its regulatory and payment frameworks can support the transition from AI⏎as an information tool to AI as a care coordination enabler—clarifying, for example, that⏎patient-directed AI actions taken with appropriate consent and identity verification are consistent⏎with existing HIPAA and HITECH frameworks.⏎On the concerns side, patients care deeply about privacy, consent, and control.⏎The sensitivity of health data demands a higher standard than what generic AI platforms⏎provide. HealthEx has built its MCP server to address these concerns directly: consent is⏎honored on every tool call; only data relevant to a specific query is returned to the model; users⏎are given a choice about whether their data is saved across sessions; and the integration is only⏎available in contexts where model training is off. These are not optional features - they are⏎essential to building the trust that enables patients to engage with AI-powered health tools.⏎HHS can support patient and caregiver trust by encouraging transparency standards for⏎AI-health-data integrations - specifically, requiring that patients be informed about how their data⏎is accessed, what it is used for, and how to revoke access. HealthEx's platform provides this⏎transparency through auditable consent, granular data sharing controls, and the ability to⏎disconnect at any time.⏎Caregivers present a particularly important use case that current infrastructure does not⏎adequately support .⏎Family members coordinating care for aging parents, children, or loved ones with complex⏎conditions need consolidated access to health records and the ability to interact with AI tools on⏎behalf of those they care for. HHS should support the development of caregiver proxy access⏎pathways within IAS, so that legally authorized representatives can retrieve and share records -⏎and use AI tools - with appropriate identity verification and consent. Specifically, HHS should⏎work with TEFCA participants and credential service providers to establish standardized proxy⏎identity flows that allow caregivers to connect records on behalf of dependents without requiring⏎the care recipient to perform their own biometric verification.⏎Finally, patients and caregivers want AI to move beyond insights and into action. AI tools today⏎excel at synthesizing information, but they do not yet extend into real-world care actions like⏎appointment booking, EHR write-back, or prior authorization initiation. HealthEx's identity,⏎consent, and records access capabilities are foundational for enabling these agentic care⏎experiences, and we are actively co-designing EHR write-back capabilities with Epic. HHS⏎13⏎⏎should consider how its regulatory and payment frameworks can support the transition from AI⏎as an information tool to AI as a care coordination enabler.⏎4. CONCLUSION⏎HealthEx appreciates the opportunity to contribute to this important discussion. The potential for⏎AI to transform clinical care is immense, but it will only be realized if patients can bring their full⏎health history to the AI tools they choose - securely, with consent, and with control.⏎The infrastructure exists today. TEFCA IAS provides the regulatory and technical framework for⏎patient-directed data exchange at national scale. HealthEx has demonstrated that this⏎framework works in practice: retrieving records for 94% of users, in under five minutes at the⏎median, and connecting them to AI tools that deliver personalized, clinically grounded insights.⏎What is needed now is policy action that accelerates adoption, removes friction, and ensures⏎that patient empowerment and trust are built into the foundation of AI in healthcare.⏎We welcome the opportunity to engage further with HHS, ASTP/ONC, and other stakeholders to⏎support these goals.⏎Contact: Priyanka Agarwal, MD, MBA Co-founder and CEO, HealthEx Priyanka@healthex.io⏎2339 3rd St., Suite 41, San Francisco, CA 94107 www.healthex.io⏎14♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0089♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Association of Nurse Anesthesiology, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Association of Nurse Anesthesiology♔♔♔♔♔♔February 18, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Submitted electronically via: Regulations.gov⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎On behalf of the 69,000 members of the American Association of Nurse Anesthesiology⏎(AANA), I am respectfully submitting comments in response to the Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎Background on Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetist⏎Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetists (CRNAs) are Advanced Practice Registered Nurses⏎(APRNs) who are autonomous anesthesia providers through their extensive training and⏎preparation. As independently licensed professionals, CRNAs are responsible and⏎accountable for making judgments and taking actions in their professional healthcare⏎practice.1 CRNAs are doctorally prepared and extensively trained to practice⏎independently. CRNAs take the lead in anesthesia before, during, and after surgery and are⏎leaders within the surgical care team.⏎CRNAs provide expert anesthesia and pain management care across diverse clinical⏎settings and are trained and licensed to care for all patients, including those with complex⏎medical conditions. CRNAs provide over 50 million anesthetics annually across the United⏎States. In some states, CRNAs are the sole anesthesia providers in nearly 100 percent of⏎1 AANA Code of Ethics for the Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetists, July 18, available at:⏎https://issuu.com/aanapublishing/docs/code_of_ethics_for_the_certified_registered_nurse_?fr=sZGY1YTU2⏎NDAxMjU.⏎⏎rural hospitals, affording these medical facilities obstetrical, surgical, trauma stabilization,⏎and pain management capabilities.⏎RFI Question Responses⏎RFI Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations.⏎AANA Response (RFI Question 2): HHS should ensure that billing and coding for AI⏎services is accurate, robust, and inclusive of all provider types, including CRNAs and other⏎allied health practitioners, and reflective of their scope of practices.⏎AANA appreciates that the Current Procedural Terminology® (CPT®) Editorial Panel has⏎undertaken the difficult work of creating a taxonomy for different types of AI services.⏎However, HHS must ensure – through CMS’ participation and oversight – that new Category⏎I and Category III codes utilize provider neutral language inclusive of all provider types who,⏎within their scope of practice, are able to perform those AI services. Doing so will improve⏎patient access to care by allowing as many providers as possible to perform AI services,⏎thereby creating financial and operational efficiencies within the healthcare system.⏎Furthermore, by including the entire universe of providers who perform a given AI service,⏎HHS/CMS will be better able to assess accurate utilization for both Category I and Category⏎III CPT codes. Accurate utilization data will in turn better inform valuation efforts in⏎determining payment.⏎We would also note that, given the emerging nature of AI services, HHS/CMS should⏎reevaluate new AI services every year for a period of five years after initial valuation (i.e., as⏎a Category I CPT® code) to ensure accurate utilization assumptions and ultimate valuation.⏎AANA also urges HHS/CMS to adopt principles that ensure the protection of clinician⏎expertise to support clinical decision-making and improve patient care. AI should assist -⏎but never replace – CRNA clinical judgment that is based on extensive education and⏎experience.⏎The opportunities to enhance patient safety and decrease the administrative burden on⏎clinicians must be paired with proper oversight process for review. For more details on⏎AANA’s recommendations for utilizing AI, see our comments to the National Science⏎⏎Foundation in response to their Request for Information on the Development of an Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) Action Plan.2⏎As CMS develops new regulations and payment policies for AI services, we finally urge the⏎inclusion of CRNAs in any stakeholder roundtables or advisory committees. The scope of⏎nurse anesthesia practice includes responsibilities for administration and management,⏎quality assessment, interdepartmental liaising, and clinical as well as administrative⏎oversight of other departments. As CRNAs possess a strong foundation in nursing, critical⏎care, anesthesia, and pain management, they are frequently called upon to assume⏎administrative and executive positions. CRNAs are regularly selected to function as⏎anesthesia and surgery department administrators, chief nurse executives, chief operating⏎officers and chief executive officers of hospitals. They are innovative leaders with⏎invaluable clinical expertise who are adept at recommending policy informed by high-⏎quality innovative practice. We stand ready to assist CMS in these efforts.⏎RFI Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded⏎performance and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel⏎AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new⏎insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AANA Response (RFI Question 6): AI tools in the clinical care setting have shown clear⏎successes in specific, well-defined domains, while also revealing important limitations.⏎Future AI innovation should focus on tools that enhance safety, quality, and value. AI has⏎particularly met or exceeded performance and cost expectations when it augments⏎workflow or operational processes rather than attempting to replace clinician judgment.⏎Within anesthesia practice specifically, rule-based automation embedded in infusion⏎pumps, monitoring systems, and advanced diagnostic devices has long contributed to⏎safer, more efficient anesthesia delivery. Emerging machine learning-based systems⏎continue to refine risk stratification, personalized planning, and continuous monitoring.3⏎AI has also delivered measurable clinical or operational value in other areas, including⏎imaging prediction tools that assist clinicians with diagnostic workflows, patient⏎monitoring and predictive alarm systems that identify physiologic deterioration earlier, and⏎2 American Association of Nurse Anesthesiology. Comment Letter in Response to the National Science⏎Foundation Request for Information on the Development of an Artificial Intelligence Action Plan. February 27,⏎2025. https://www.aana.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/AANA-comments-on-RFI-Development-of-an-⏎AI-Action-Plan.pdf⏎3 Hu, Jiale and Damico, Nickie. AI in Anesthesia; Transforming Patient Safety and Enhancing Anesthesia⏎Delivery. Patient Safety Movement Foundation. March 26, 2025. Online: https://psmf.org/psmf-blog/ai-in-⏎anesthesia-transforming-patient-safety-and-enhancing-anesthesia-delivery/?utm_source=chatgpt.com⏎⏎scheduling and capacity planning models that help optimize resource utilization. These⏎applications often outperform traditional, manual methods by processing large amounts of⏎data in real time and providing actionable insights that improve safety and efficiency.4 AI⏎has also proven valuable in patient throughput and discharge planning, allowing facilities⏎to increase efficiency while improving patient outcomes.5⏎AI tools have, however, fallen short in areas where models lack generalizability or introduce⏎unintended risks. AI integration into clinical reasoning has proven problematic when⏎models attempt to operate autonomously without adequate oversight; for instance, early⏎automated sedation systems like SEDASYS failed commercially because they lacked⏎sufficient adaptability to real-world clinical complexity and variability.6 Such limitations⏎highlight the essential need for thoughtful data governance and robust clinical validation.⏎Predictive monitoring illustrates one area where AI has already met or exceeded⏎expectations, while persistent limitations related to generalizability offer important⏎cautionary lessons.⏎The AI tools with the greatest potential to improve healthcare outcomes, generate new⏎quality insights, and reduce costs will be those that integrate clinical and operational⏎intelligence rather than functioning in isolation. For example, tools capable of real-time⏎prediction of discharge barriers and patient deterioration could enable anesthesia and⏎perioperative teams to anticipate and mitigate complications before they occur, improving⏎patient safety while reducing unnecessary resource utilization. Similarly, AI systems that⏎combine physiologic monitoring with throughput and capacity data could provide a unified⏎view to support both frontline clinicians and health system leaders in coordinating safer,⏎more efficient care.⏎These experiences highlight that AI is most effective when it enhances clinician expertise,⏎improves consistency, and integrates seamlessly into clinical workflows and underline the⏎previous point that AI should assist - but never replace – CRNA clinical judgment that is⏎based on extensive education and experience.⏎4 Ibid.⏎5 Becker’s Health IT. How Sarasota Memorial leveraged AI to redesign patient flow and increase capacity. April⏎8, 2025. Online: https://www.beckershospitalreview.com/healthcare-information-⏎technology/innovation/how-sarasota-memorial-leveraged-ai-to-redesign-patient-flow-and-increase-⏎capacity/?utm_source=chatgpt.com⏎6 Hu, Jiale and Damico, Nickie. AI in Anesthesia; Transforming Patient Safety and Enhancing Anesthesia⏎Delivery. Patient Safety Movement Foundation. March 26, 2025. Online: https://psmf.org/psmf-blog/ai-in-⏎anesthesia-transforming-patient-safety-and-enhancing-anesthesia-delivery/?utm_source=chatgpt.com⏎⏎RFI Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎AANA Response (RFI Question 9): Patients and caregivers commonly voice expectations⏎that AI can improve the quality and efficiency of care. For example, caregivers recognize⏎AI’s ability to analyze large amounts of health data and provide clinical decision support as⏎a way to enhance diagnostic accuracy, reduce unnecessary delays, and support⏎personalized treatment planning, potentially leading to better patient outcomes and⏎reduced clinician burden. These improvements in decision support and workflow efficiency⏎can free up clinician time for direct patient interaction, which patients and caregivers⏎generally value as part of high-quality care.7⏎In one Journal of Medical Internet Research survey, prospective patients highlighted that⏎their willingness to accept AI in healthcare is strongly tied to trust and perceived⏎usefulness. Many respondents indicated they would be less likely to share personal data⏎for AI use if privacy and security protections are unclear, underscoring that concerns about⏎data privacy, confidentiality, and cybersecurity are major barriers to acceptance. Trust in⏎AI’s safety and effectiveness also varied based on individuals’ technology familiarity and⏎personal attitudes, with emphasis on the need for transparent, reliable systems that justify⏎any trade-off of data for benefits.8⏎Qualitative research on AI-based clinical decision support systems (CDSSs) further⏎elaborates on challenges that resonate with patient and caregiver priorities. Stakeholders,⏎including patients and caregivers interviewed alongside clinicians and experts, identified⏎overarching barriers spanning technical, data, user, ethical, and legal domains. Among⏎these, concerns about data quality and bias, lack of user understanding, ethical issues,⏎and accountability emerged as significant obstacles. Such concerns validate⏎patient/caregiver apprehensions that AI could produce incorrect or biased⏎recommendations or that harmful outcomes may arise without clear accountability⏎structures.9⏎7 Elechi, Ubalaeze, et al. Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: A Narrative Review of Recent Clinical⏎Applications, Implementation Strategies, and Challenges. Journal of Healthcare Leadership. 2025, December⏎30; 17: 863-876. Online (via National Library of Medicine):⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12764347/⏎8 Kauttonen, Janne; Rousi, Rebekah; Alamaki, Ari. Trust and Acceptance Challenges in the Adoption of AI⏎Applications in Health Care: Quantitative Survey Analysis. Journal of Medical Internet Research. 2025, March⏎21; 27: e65567. Online: https://www.jmir.org/2025/1/e65567⏎9 Giebel, Godwin Denk, et al. Problems and Barriers Related to the Use of AI-Based Clinical Decision Support⏎Systems: Interview Study. Journal of Medical Internet Research. 2025, February 3; 27: e63377. Online (via⏎National Library of Medicine): https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11833262/⏎⏎A recurring theme across studies is the “black-box” nature of many AI systems, where the⏎lack of explainability undermines users’ trust because it is difficult for patients, caregivers,⏎and clinicians to understand how decisions are made. This opacity can make patients⏎uneasy about relying on AI for critical health decisions and raises worries about informed⏎consent and comprehension of AI functions. Patients and caregivers also express fear that⏎AI could dehumanize care, reducing human interaction and empathy in clinical encounters,⏎and that biased algorithms may perpetuate health disparities. Furthermore, uncertainty⏎over liability, who is responsible if AI contributes to harm, remains a concern tied to trust⏎and safety.10⏎The accuracy of AI-generated health information is also a concern that may put patients at⏎risk if inaccurate or misleading recommendations are made and followed as medical⏎advice.11,12 Recently, ECRI identified the misuse of AI chatbots in healthcare as the #1⏎technology hazard in 2026. ECRI’s assessment affirms that users must recognize the⏎limitations of AI and large language models (LLMs) and scrutinize responses whenever⏎using information that can influence patient care. AI output should never be used as a⏎substitute for qualified medical advice or professional judgment.13,14⏎Patients and caregivers want AI to help solve real clinical challenges such as diagnostic⏎errors, inefficiencies, and clinician burden but are worried about privacy, trust,⏎explainability, bias, data quality, and accountability. Addressing these concerns with robust⏎transparency, ethical safeguards, human oversight, and clear communication will be key to⏎realizing the benefits patients and caregivers hope AI can deliver in clinical care.⏎Conclusion⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide input to the Request for Information: Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. We would welcome⏎the opportunity to meet with you in the coming months to further discuss these issues and⏎proposed solutions and to take part in any HHS/CMS stakeholder work around AI services.⏎10 Ibid.⏎11 Booth, Robert. ChatGPT ‘upgrade’ giving more harmful answers than previously, tests find. The Guardian⏎(US Edition). October 14, 2025. Online: https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/oct/14/chatgpt-⏎upgrade-giving-more-harmful-answers-than-previously-tests-find⏎12 Gregory, Andrew. Google AI Overviews put people are risk of harm with misleading health advice. The⏎Guardian (US Edition). January 2, 2026. Online:⏎https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/jan/02/google-ai-overviews-risk-harm-misleading-health-⏎information⏎13 Ibid.⏎14 ECRI. Top 10 Health Technology Hazards for 2026 Executive Brief. January 21, 2026. Online:⏎https://home.ecri.org/blogs/ecri-thought-leadership-resources/top-10-health-technology-hazards-for-2026-⏎executive-brief⏎⏎For any questions or comments, and to schedule a meeting, please do not hesitate to⏎reach out to Romy Gelb-Zimmer, Director of Regulatory Affairs, at rgelb-⏎zimmer@aana.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jeff Molter, MBA, MSN, CRNA⏎President, AANA⏎cc: William Bruce, MBA, CAE, AANA Chief Executive Officer⏎Gregory Craig, MS, MPA, AANA Senior Associate Director of Regulatory Affairs♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0090♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Baker♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter was misdiagnosed twice before a specialist finally caught what two other doctors had missed, and that was a human being sitting with her chart who figured it out. So when I hear that HHS wants to move faster on AI making diagnostic calls, I genuinely don't know what to think. Part of me understands that access is a real problem, especially if you're not near a good hospital, but the other part of me keeps thinking about those two wrong answers she got and wondering what happens when the wrong answer comes from a system nobody can push back against.⏎⏎Anyway I do have a real question that I cannot find answered anywhere in this document and I've read through it twice now. My husband is active duty and we have Tricare. Does any of this apply to us? It seems like everything I read about healthcare policy has this asterisk where military families are in some separate system and half the rules don't apply the same way, or they apply but with a delay, or we have to go through different channels entirely. I called Tricare's 1-800 number in March and the representative I spoke to genuinely did not know what I was asking about when I mentioned this HHS request for information. So I'm not sure if that means we're not affected or if she just hadn't heard about it yet.⏎⏎The reason it matters to me specifically is that my daughter gets her care at Womack Army Medical Center at Fort Bragg and I want to know if the AI tools being discussed here would be rolled out at military treatment facilities or if that's handled completely separately through the DoD. Because if there's less human oversight being built into these systems, I want to know if that applies to her doctors too.⏎⏎I don't have a lot of technical expertise here and I know that. But the thing that worries me most is the part about "accelerating adoption" without me seeing much in this document about what happens when the AI is wrong. Who do you call? Is there an appeals process? My daughter's situation was eventually resolved because I kept pushing, I called the nurse line, I printed out her lab results and wrote notes on them, I was a pain in the neck about it. A lot of families can't do that for various reasons and I worry that if an algorithm makes a call and a provider just follows it, that pushback option becomes harder even for families who are persistent like me.⏎⏎I realize this comment is probably not what you're looking for in terms of the specific questions you asked. I just couldn't find anywhere else to submit this and I wanted at least the Tricare question to be on record somewhere.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0091♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Fairview Health Services, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Fairview Health Services♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology and⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW⏎Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎Fairview Health Services (Fairview) appreciates the opportunity to provide comments to the Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology (ONC) on the Request for Information (RFI) on artificial intelligence (AI) adoption in clinical⏎care.1 Fairview appreciates the Administration’s ongoing commitment to tech-enabling healthcare to⏎reduce administrative burden while improving access to high-quality care. Fairview supports⏎development of a responsible, innovation-friendly, and transparent regulatory framework for AI in⏎healthcare that improves patient health outcomes and supports clinicians through technology. Clear⏎regulatory guardrails, strong data privacy protections, robust workforce enablement, and industry⏎aligned- standards are essential for safe and effective AI adoption.⏎Fairview is a nonprofit, integrated health system providing exceptional health care across the full⏎spectrum of medical services through our 10 hospitals and 60 clinics. Fairview’s broad care⏎continuum includes academic and community hospitals, primary and specialty care clinics, senior⏎and long-term care facilities, retail and specialty pharmacies, pharmacy benefit management⏎services, rehabilitation centers, counseling, home health care services, and an integrated provider⏎network. Fairview’s 34,000+ employees and 5,000+ system providers embrace innovation and new⏎thinking to drive a healthier future through healing, discovery, and education.⏎AI can deliver meaningful benefits to providers and patients such as improved clinician productivity,⏎reduced documentation burden, lower burnout, more timely clinical decision-making, fewer missed⏎orders, improved throughput, and better patient engagement. These gains slowly translate into⏎1Regulatory text available here: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/23/2025-23641/request-for-⏎information-accelerating-the-adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence-as-part-of⏎⏎reduced staffing pressure and overtime costs, higher-quality documentation, improved access and⏎satisfaction, and better clinical outcomes through earlier interventions.⏎However, clinical leadership can be reluctant to adopt and scale AI. Because generative AI is⏎probabilistic, its performance must be validated not only before deployment but also continuously⏎over time, requiring skills and resources that may be scarce in healthcare settings. The costs, which⏎are driven by computing, storage, and model usage, are also highly variable and difficult for⏎organizations to predict, budget, or govern using traditional software pricing models. Realizing the⏎benefits of AI requires healthcare leaders to move past understandable initial hesitation and lean into⏎AI capabilities. The government needs to make sure policies help foster adoption and keep health⏎systems and providers in control of how AI is deployed within healthcare while also protecting⏎patients and health systems from unregulated AI companies and tools that may cause significant⏎harm.⏎I. Documenting Return on Investment⏎One barrier to adoption and scaling of AI is determining return on investment (ROI). Healthcare⏎organizations operate today on thinner margins than ever, making it difficult to invest in technologies⏎that will not pay off financially in the near term. Fairview believes many applications of generative AI in⏎healthcare do present sufficient return on investment (ROI).⏎The Department of Health and Human Services (the Department) could help document ROI data in a⏎way that healthcare providers can both rely on and act on this data.⏎For example, studies show that AI ambient scribe technology, in which AI records and transcribes⏎clinical interactions and then drafts corresponding notes, can reduce the time clinicians spend⏎working after hours. Less administrative time for providers, however, does not by itself offset the cost⏎of the technology. The true ROI of AI scribes appears downstream. For example, through happier⏎clinicians who are less likely to burn out or leave healthcare, reducing recruitment and onboarding⏎costs. AI summarization tools that help providers and staff find and summarize clinical information,⏎agents that help do lower risk highly standardized workflow like wound care, and even clinical⏎decision support tools that assist providers in making decisions are all potential use cases that could⏎benefit from this kind of ROI standardization.⏎The Department should address this gap by assembling a repository of AI use cases with⏎demonstrated ROI. By conducting independent research, such a repository would validate vendor⏎claims and highlight metrics relevant to healthcare administrators’ bottom-line decisions. Such a⏎repository would be vendor-agnostic, focusing on use cases rather than specific implementations, to⏎not advantage those vendors that get their claims published. Done right, it would serve as a trusted⏎reference for organizations evaluating AI investments.⏎The Department should also address price controls on AI and the need for lower cost options to make⏎sure IT companies do not increase prices to capture all the ROI that may be gained by health systems.⏎⏎Prioritizing low-cost options or price controls should help health delivery organizations that need⏎these efficiencies to offset the very small margins, financial pressures, and workforce challenges that⏎are coming to all health care delivery systems.⏎II. Bridging the Adoption-to-ROI Gap and Establishing Payment⏎Even when an AI use case has a proven ROI, initial adoption may still be financially challenging. The⏎benefits of AI are rarely immediate and often require workflow changes, training, and iteration to be⏎fully realized. Early phases of adoption are typically marked by uneven usage, ongoing configuration,⏎and infrastructure costs that scale before productivity gains materialize. As a result, health systems⏎may incur real costs before seeing offsetting savings, creating a financial mismatch that can slow or⏎stall adoption.⏎A response to these challenges would be fee-for-service reimbursement for AI use, but this approach⏎could quickly become costly and, for AI with proven ROI, unnecessary. Organizations primarily need⏎support through the initial scaling period; once broad adoption is achieved, AI that delivers value will⏎likely pay for itself assuming it is done well and appropriate price controls are in place for AI⏎companies.⏎Fairview respectfully asks the Department to consider AI adoption grants that cover infrastructure⏎costs for a fixed period of time with a discreet infrastructure delivery goal rather than a fixed amount of⏎money without a concrete infrastructure deliverable.⏎Traditional grants incentivize slow scaling to extend funding; time-based grants with clear⏎implementation goals would encourage organizations to scale quickly, assess value, and determine⏎which use cases merit continued investment. After the grant period ends, organizations could⏎discontinue low-value AI while sustaining AI that delivers sufficient benefit using the infrastructure⏎and learnings that were created – without ongoing government spending.⏎Such programs could be structured as true grants or as fixed-price arrangements. In the latter case,⏎however, pricing should scale with organization size to avoid disadvantaging smaller or lower-⏎resourced providers. If desired, pricing could even be structured disproportionately to give an⏎advantage to organizations that are caring for underserved or rural areas. Properly designed, time-⏎based infrastructure support would help organizations escape the “pilot trap” and reach sustainable⏎AI adoption.⏎Additionally, performance-based contracting arrangements between providers and healthcare⏎vendors – and data collected on AI and software performance – can help to decrease the go-to-market⏎time and cost for new AI solutions, further extending America’s lead in innovative technology.⏎Performance-based contracts between healthcare vendors and providers can ensure the new⏎technologies purchased by providers deliver the promised benefits and return on investment.⏎Fairview encourages ASTP/ONC to work with CMS to reduce regulatory barriers to AI and software⏎approval and payment.⏎⏎HHS should explore ways to allow data collected from performance-based contracting arrangements⏎to offer a pathway to CMS coverage and/or reimbursement decisions for novel digital health⏎technologies and Software as a Service (SaaS) solution. Hospitals have already entered performance-⏎based contracts with innovative new healthcare suppliers, including AI technology and SaaS vendors.⏎By making payment dependent on predefined outcomes, these contracts make it easier for hospitals⏎to take a chance on technologies that lack extensive histories of clinical evidence or claims data. That⏎is, if the purported return on investment fails to materialize, hospitals can recoup initial costs.⏎III. Building AI Fluency⏎Technology adoption depends not only on technical readiness, but also on human understanding and⏎trust. Considerable attention has been given to training clinicians on how to use AI tools effectively,⏎verify outputs, and avoid automation bias. While this training is essential, a more foundational form of⏎AI fluency is also needed to overcome barriers to adoption—particularly among non-technical⏎stakeholders such as clinical leadership and frontline staff.⏎In healthcare, AI is unlikely to displace workers and more likely to enable healthcare workers in⏎efficiency and automation if done appropriately. Persistent labor shortages across clinical roles,⏎combined with rising demand driven by an aging population, mean that AI is increasingly necessary to⏎sustain access to care. In practice, most healthcare AI use cases are designed to reduce⏎administrative burden, support clinical judgment, and allow clinicians to spend more time with⏎patients. Nonetheless, the clinical workforce has experienced significant difficulty integrating AI tools⏎within existing clinical workflows, EHR systems, and operational infrastructure. AI tools often must⏎conform to complex and aging infrastructure, requiring expensive adaptation and reconfiguration.⏎Many tools fail to accommodate workflow constraints faced by clinicians, leading to low adoption and⏎poor usability.⏎These concerns often stem from a lack of shared understanding about what healthcare AI does and⏎does not do well and how to use it safely and efficiently. The most effective way to build AI fluency is⏎through experience within a safe framework. When clinicians and staff see AI reduce documentation⏎burden, improve workflow efficiency, and support—not override—clinical judgment; skepticism gives⏎way to trust. Policies that enable earlier, broader, and more responsible adoption of AI will therefore⏎do more to build AI fluency than education alone. By helping healthcare organizations move past⏎prolonged pilots and into sustained use, the Department can ensure that AI’s benefits are visible,⏎tangible, and widely shared.⏎In combination with AI experience, credible, accessible education, can mitigate the perceived risks⏎that AI adoption is a cost-cutting measure rather than a capacity-multiplying one. This perception can⏎slow implementation, limit usage, or prompt organizations to constrain AI to narrow pilots rather than⏎deploy it broadly where it could deliver the most benefit.⏎The federal government can play a significant role in improving AI adoption by supporting neutral,⏎trusted education on AI’s role in healthcare and promoting this training in education systems and⏎quality improvement incentives.⏎⏎This could include developing materials tailored to different stakeholder groups; developing⏎repositories of the best materials, supporting development of AI certifications in healthcare,⏎supporting peer-to-peer learning networks among healthcare organizations; and incorporating AI⏎fluency into existing workforce development training programs and incentivizing AI development and⏎governance within quality improvement programs. Fairview urges HHS to invest in education⏎programs, establish best practices for human AI- collaboration and promote AI tools that measurably⏎reduce administrative burden.⏎IV. AI Supporting Clinical Trials⏎AI can be deployed to address the challenges related to identifying and enrolling patients in clinical⏎trials in a timely manner.⏎Delays in meeting trial enrollment targets and timelines can increase the cost of the trial. AI tools can⏎analyze the extensive universe of data available to health systems, like Fairview, to identify patients⏎that may be a match for clinical trials that are currently recruiting. This application of natural language⏎processing (NLP) systems can make developing new drugs less expensive and more efficient, while⏎also improving patient and geographical diversity in trials to address generalizability.⏎To incentivize continued innovation in clinical trials, Fairview also encourages ASTP/ONC to work with⏎the FDA to put into effect clear guidance on the process for properly obtaining consent from patients⏎for the use of their real-world data (RWD) to produce AI-generated synthetic control arms in clinical⏎trials.⏎AI, once trained on real-world data (RWD), has the capability to generate synthetic data and patient⏎profiles that share characteristics with the target patient population for a clinical trial. Fairview would⏎be able to use synthetic data to simulate clinical trials to optimize trial designs, model the possible⏎effects or range of results of a novel intervention, and predict the statistical significance and⏎magnitude of effects or biases. Ultimately, synthetic patient data can help optimize trial design,⏎improve safety and reduce costs for decentralized clinical trials. Further, synthetic control arms in⏎clinical trials can help increase trial enrollment by easing patients’ fears that they will receive a⏎placebo.⏎V. Making Local Validation Possible While Holding AI Companies and EHR Vendors⏎Accountable⏎Another concern AI adopters rightly have is safety. Many healthcare AI use cases fall outside the⏎oversight of the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), and even regulated products are poorly served⏎by traditional evaluation methods. Especially for AI, which is probabilistic, pre-deployment testing on⏎generalized datasets has limited predictive value for real-world performance.⏎The Department needs to assure the method for validating AI performance in healthcare is “local⏎validation,” in which the healthcare facility, independent of the developer, evaluates how the AI⏎performs on its own data while embedded in its own workflow while the Department also puts policies⏎in place to hold AI companies and EHR vendors accountable to collect this feedback and build safe,⏎high value AI tools that promote patient care.⏎⏎This can be done both prior to deployment, using local but retrospective data, and on a continual⏎basis post-deployment (“ongoing monitoring”) to make sure performance does not “drift” over time.⏎This way, AI features can be deemed safe no matter what patient acuity mix is being served by a⏎particular healthcare organization.⏎Local validation also matters because AI performance can be sensitive to the workflow into which the⏎AI is integrated. For example, a popular use case for predictive modelling forecasts the likelihood of⏎imminent medical deterioration. The output of such a model is not binary but instead a probability.⏎When implementing this model, a deployer must decide what probability threshold should trigger an⏎alert for the care team to investigate further. This choice has a significant impact on the⏎“performance” of the model but also has a major impact on the clinical teams and the burden of the⏎alerts. If an organization sets the alert threshold low, there will likely be more false positives; if the⏎organization sets the threshold higher, there will likely be more false negatives. Yet the organization⏎may have good reason to change this setting: organizations with dedicated remote monitoring teams⏎may prefer false positives, while those who must rely only on frontline nurses managing a large⏎caseload of patients may not be able to tolerate them.⏎The challenge for post-deployment validation, in healthcare at least, is that small or rural healthcare⏎organizations often struggle to afford the necessary data science resources and expertise to perform⏎effective monitoring. Yet because AI performance will vary from organization to organization, post-⏎deployment validation is not something that can be outsourced in practice. Without appropriate⏎support for “local validation” activities, AI could widen, rather than narrow, the digital divide in⏎healthcare.⏎The Department needs to make sure this local AI validation data is shared with vendors and other⏎systems in a way that allows all people to benefit from this validation and requires AI vendors and EHR⏎vendors to implement this feedback and report safety issues related to their AI products.⏎VI. Providing Guidance for AI Validation⏎Even with the tools and resources needed to conduct local validation, healthcare organizations may⏎not know how to use them effectively. Today, there is little consensus on how to evaluate traditional⏎predictive models, and even less for generative AI. Gold-standard guidance such as the National⏎Institute of Standards and Technology’s AI Risk Management Framework asserts the importance of⏎ongoing monitoring but does not specify how to perform it. Vendors providing evaluation services may⏎use proprietary metrics or allow the user to select among evaluation methodologies (as the AI Trust⏎and Assurance Suite does); both limit comparability.⏎Regulators can add value by defining standard metrics and validation processes for AI performance.⏎Such standards and scorecards should likely be mandatory; but if not mandatory, there should at⏎minimum standard guidance that could increase confidence among healthcare AI adopters and lower⏎the barrier to widescale adoption by setting and updating those standards. Clear, shared⏎methodologies would allow organizations to focus on performing validation rather than designing it,⏎especially if incorporated into existing tools.⏎⏎Importantly, validation guidance should be specific to both the use case and the deployment context,⏎not one-size-fits-all. The risks, benefits, and appropriate metrics for documentation assistance,⏎clinical decision support, predictive modeling, and patient engagement tools differ substantially.⏎Tailored guidance for common classes of AI applications would allow organizations to focus their⏎efforts on meaningful evaluation rather than reinventing validation frameworks from scratch.⏎Clear federal guidance would also accelerate adoption by lowering the perceived risk of deployment.⏎Many healthcare leaders delay or limit AI use not because they believe AI is unsafe, but because they⏎lack confidence that they are evaluating it correctly. By clarifying expectations and establishing⏎shared norms, the Department could help organizations move from cautious experimentation to⏎responsible scale.⏎VII. Setting National Privacy Standards⏎A significant barrier to the large-scale diffusion of innovative AI applications in healthcare is the lack of⏎a single, comprehensive federal privacy law.⏎A cohesive national privacy framework and patient education about data privacy is necessary to⏎provide a reasonable level of consumer confidence that businesses will protect their data, sensitive or⏎otherwise and be held accountable for data breaches.⏎The current patchwork, state-driven approach to privacy policy has resulted in inconsistent data⏎privacy practices. Patients must be able to trust the processes for storage, handling and use of their⏎data, particularly as patient data is increasingly used to train AI algorithms. Missing data from patients⏎wary of data sharing could add to data siloing and perpetuate AI algorithmic biases, and ASTP/ONC⏎should work with data stakeholders across HHS to proactively educate patients on these tradeoffs.⏎Unharmonized and burdensome requirements are a rate-limiting step in unlocking value through AI⏎innovation. A federal privacy law that incorporates AI related data sharing benefits and continued data⏎sharing concerns can empower innovators by placing clear, harmonized and common-sense⏎guardrails around artificial intelligence tools and the data sharing that drives the benefits of holistic⏎patient care across health entities. Healthcare privacy enforcement authorities are currently diffused⏎across a wide swath of the federal government. The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability⏎Act (HIPAA) only delegates privacy enforcement authority to the Department of Health and Human⏎Services Office of Civil Rights (OCR) for a narrow subset of covered entities. With the proliferation of a⏎healthcare data ecosystem outside of the traditional payer-provider exchange, enforcement authority⏎falls to the Federal Trade Commission, the Department of Justice and other agencies for a broad⏎range of privacy- and data-related violations. There is significant, urgent need for a unified baseline⏎set of enforcement priorities to create a harmonized enforcement authority for the portion of⏎healthcare ecosystem outside of the bounds of HIPAA.⏎Now, as state AI laws follow a similar path toward fragmentation, it is critical that ASTP/ONC and its⏎regulatory partners either 1) offer directional federal guidance or 2) endorse a private sector–⏎developed framework for AI security, transparency, safety and quality as the recognized standard.⏎Fragmented regulatory approaches across agencies and states impede adoption and increase⏎compliance burdens.⏎⏎VIII. Improving AI Transparency⏎Trust among patients, providers, payers, and suppliers is critical to the development and deployment⏎of AI tools in healthcare settings. To earn trust, AI tools must have an established standard of⏎transparency. Some policy proposals, including those offered by ONC, suggest transparency can be⏎achieved through a “nutrition label” model, which lists the sources and classes of data used to train⏎the algorithm. Unfortunately, some versions of the “nutrition label” approach to AI transparency fail to⏎acknowledge that when an AI tool is trained on a large, complex dataset, and is by design intended to⏎evolve and learn, the initial static inputs captured by a label do not provide accurate insights into an⏎ever-changing AI tool. Further, disclosure requirements around data inputs or algorithmic processes,⏎if not carefully considered, could force AI developers to publicly disclose intellectual property or⏎proprietary technology, which would stifle innovation.⏎Fairview believes that AI technology in healthcare should be held to a standardized, outcomes-⏎focused set of metrics, such as accuracy, false positives, inference risks and recommended⏎use/applications. Outcomes, rather than inputs, are where AI technologies have potential to drive⏎health or harm. Thus, it is essential to focus transparency efforts on the accuracy, reliability, and⏎overall appropriateness of AI technology outputs in healthcare to ensure that the evolving tool does⏎not produce harm.⏎Fairview encourages the Department to consider requiring AI developers and manufacturers to⏎manage and maintain model cards, including uses of new technologies in healthcare settings and the⏎associated safety concerns and mitigation practices to provide much-needed guidance to clinicians,⏎providers, and administrators on safe and appropriate use cases with the associated risks and⏎mitigations.⏎This approach could provide liability protection for the proper use of AI technology for the defined set⏎of use cases where developers have established and reported the appropriate metrics for accuracy⏎and reliability. Transparency about the intended use of AI tools would be the simplest way for⏎regulators to incorporate AI governance into existing regulations. Health systems would also be able⏎to incorporate this information into our own governance structures, putting internal policies in place⏎to prevent misuse of AI in ways that could be detrimental to patient safety or experiences.⏎Such disclosure would give health systems and patients a complete picture of the safety and security⏎of the AI technologies they use. Rather than limiting or delaying innovation, such guidelines would⏎level the playing field between established market leaders and startups. Alternatively, or in addition to⏎this, HHS could sanction the use of third-party certification organizations or existing market⏎processes to address this challenge while reducing administrative burden. Such a market-driven⏎approach would be flexible and adaptable, capable of adjusting to the latest developments in AI⏎technology without requiring regulators to frequently revisit the framework, although it would need⏎some appropriate oversite.⏎⏎IX. AI Patient Coaches Need Provider Controls⏎The Department needs to ensure appropriate guardrails which enable oversight by providers or⏎caregivers for all AI patient coach or clinical decision-making tool.⏎As AI tools increasingly interact directly with patients—through symptom checkers, care navigation⏎tools, medication guidance, and longitudinal “patient coaches”—it is essential that these⏎technologies operate within a framework that preserves the primacy of the clinician–patient⏎relationship. AI patient facing tools must augment, not replace, the role of licensed providers, and⏎should reflect the clinical judgment, care plans, and values of a patient’s personal care team.⏎Without appropriate safeguards, AI patient coaches risk delivering guidance that is misaligned with a⏎patient’s medical history, local standards of care, or clinician intent. Generic or overly confident⏎recommendations may confuse patients, undermine trust, or prompt inappropriate care seeking⏎behavior. For these reasons, AI patient coaches should be required to operate with explicit provider⏎oversight and clearly defined boundaries on autonomy.⏎At a minimum, policies should ensure that providers maintain control over:⏎• Whether and when AI patient coaches are used for their patients;⏎• Scope of advice or guidance such tools are permitted to provide;⏎• Escalation pathways that route complex, ambiguous, or high-risk situations back to human⏎clinicians; and⏎• Alignment with the patient’s established care plan, including medications, diagnoses, and⏎documented clinical preferences.⏎AI patient facing tools should be designed to reflect what a patient’s own clinician would reasonably⏎recommend in similar circumstances, rather than offering generic, population level advice. This⏎requires mechanisms for local configuration, integration with the clinical record, and clinician defined⏎constraints on recommendations. Where such alignment is not possible, AI tools should clearly defer⏎to human review rather than attempting to fill gaps with probabilistic output.⏎Transparency is also critical. Patients should be informed when they are interacting with an AI system,⏎understand its intended role, and know how to reach a human clinician when needed. AI patient⏎coaches should not present themselves as substitutes for professional medical advice, nor should⏎they operate independently of provider governance structures.⏎Finally, liability and accountability frameworks must reflect these realities. Developers and deployers⏎of AI patient coaches should be required to clearly specify intended use cases, known limitations, and⏎appropriate safeguards and work directly with health systems and providers instead of developing⏎straight to consumer applications. Health systems must retain the authority to govern these tools⏎within their existing clinical oversight, quality, and safety processes.⏎Establishing clear federal expectations for provider-in-the-loop AI patient tools will protect patients,⏎support clinicians, and enable responsible innovation—ensuring that AI enhances care delivery⏎without eroding clinical judgment or trust.⏎⏎X. Conclusion⏎Thank you for consideration of Fairview’s comments on ASTP/ONC RFI on artificial intelligence (AI)⏎adoption in clinical care. If you have any questions or need more information regarding these⏎comments, please do not hesitate to contact me at Bryan.Jarabek@fairview.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Bryan Jarabek, MD, PhD⏎Chief Medical Informatics Officer⏎Fairview Health Services♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0092♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Scott♔♔New York♔♔United States♔Data & Society Research Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Data & Society Research Institute | HHS AI RFI | Docket No. HHS-2024-AI-CLINICAL⏎⏎Richard Scott, Research Director⏎Data & Society Research Institute⏎New York, NY⏎⏎Regarding Question Area: Governance and Accountability Mechanisms for AI in Clinical Settings⏎⏎Our member network — roughly 2,800 organizations, many of them community health centers and patient advocacy groups — started tracking AI-related patient complaints in earnest in early 2024. What we found was not subtle. Sixty-four percent of member organizations reported receiving patient complaints specifically tied to automated decision-making tools, and the complaints were not distributed evenly. They came disproportionately from facilities serving Medicaid-dependent populations and rural communities. I keep coming back to that number because I think it says something that the efficiency arguments don't.⏎⏎What patients were describing, across 14 focus groups we conducted at member sites, was not a technical failure. It was something more like being processed. They felt that a recommendation had been generated about their body by something they couldn't see, couldn't question, and didn't choose. And in most cases, they were right — fewer than one in three of our member organizations had any formal informed-consent protocol that specifically addressed AI-assisted clinical recommendations. Not because these organizations are careless, but because nobody required them to have one, and building one from scratch is genuinely hard when the underlying systems are opaque even to the clinicians using them.⏎⏎The thing that worries me about the framing of this RFI is the word "acceleration." I understand the impulse. There are real problems in clinical care that computational tools can help with — I'm not dismissing that. But medicine has always been partly about what happens in the room between a clinician and a patient. Not the data transfer, the actual human encounter. A patient who is scared and not entirely sure what's wrong with them communicates a lot of information that doesn't appear in a structured field anywhere. A family member who's been lying to the patient about the diagnosis. A person who's nodding along but clearly doesn't understand what they've been told. A man who says he's fine but whose body language says otherwise. These are not supplementary inputs that AI will eventually learn to process. They are the diagnostic encounter.⏎⏎The therapeutic relationship also has measurable clinical value — this is not sentimental, there's a real literature on it going back decades — and I think we should be asking whether acceleration risks degrading that relationship even when the AI recommendations themselves are technically sound. A technically correct suggestion that makes a patient feel like a data point is not a neutral outcome.⏎⏎The accountability question is the one where I'd like to see HHS do the most work, specifically. When a patient is harmed and an AI-assisted recommendation was part of the pathway to that harm, who is answerable? Right now the answer is murky in ways that I think most patients don't realize until something goes wrong. There's a structural difference between a physician who is genuinely responsible for a decision and a physician who is documenting compliance with an algorithmic output. Patients deserve to know which situation they're in.⏎⏎I'd specifically ask HHS to look at whether the informed-consent frameworks being developed around AI tools are actually reaching patients in plain language before decisions are made, not buried in intake paperwork. A member organization in upstate New York told us in Q2 2024 that their patients were signing forms acknowledging AI use without any staff member having explained what that meant in practice, because the staff themselves weren't sure.⏎⏎Data & Society Research Institute | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎That gap — between the existence of a disclosure and a patient actually understanding what's being disclosed — is where a lot of the trust problem lives. And I don't think faster adoption solves it. It's possible that♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0093♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Connected Health Initiative, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Connected Health Initiative♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2025⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎RE: CHI Response to Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy Request for⏎Information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care (90 FR 60108)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The Connected Health Initiative (CHI)1 shares your commitment to supporting the⏎necessary expanded use of digital and connected health technologies. A growing evidence⏎base continues to demonstrate that the responsible use of safe and effective digital health⏎solutions produces better patient outcomes, reduces costs, augments population health⏎management, and improves the healthcare workforce experience. Digital health tools,⏎increasingly powered by artificial intelligence (AI), leverage patient-generated health data⏎(PGHD) and include cloud-enabled solutions, to reduce administrative burden, support⏎medical and clinical decision-making, and chronic and acute care management. The use⏎of these tools is also vital in supporting unserved and underserved populations’ access to⏎prevention, diagnosis, and treatment for both acute and chronic conditions.⏎The Connected Health Initiative (CHI) is the leading multistakeholder policy and legal⏎advocacy effort dedicated to improving health outcomes while reducing costs. Our work is⏎driven by the consensus of stakeholders from across the connected health ecosystem.⏎CHI aims to realize an environment in which Americans can see improvements in their⏎health through policies that allow for connected technologies to advance outcomes and⏎reduce costs. We advocate before Congress, numerous U.S. federal agencies, and state⏎legislatures and agencies, to accomplish responsible pro-digital health policy and law⏎changes in areas including reimbursement/payment, privacy/security,⏎effectiveness/quality assurance, U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulation of⏎digital health, health data interoperability, and the rising role of artificial intelligence (AI) in⏎care delivery. For more information, see www.connectedhi.com.⏎1 www.connectedhi.com.⏎⏎Leveraging the tremendous potential of AI is critical to accomplishing the Administration’s⏎goals of empowering the American people with personalized solutions; equipping⏎healthcare providers with better and timely data about the patients they serve and⏎improving health outcomes; and shifting the paradigm for health care to a system that⏎fosters prevention, wellness, and chronic disease management. Many AI use cases,⏎ranging from solving administrative/backend efficiencies to supportive clinical decisions,⏎have already demonstrated their capacity to advance the Quadruple Aim.⏎We are encouraged to see the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) continue⏎to investigate these goals, including with its request for information regarding accelerating⏎the adoption of artificial intelligence as part of clinical care. HHS is well-positioned to⏎leverage its existing authority to ensure that its policies propel the American healthcare⏎system forward through its safe, effective, and responsible uptake of AI. We encourage you⏎to set policy aligned with the following principles:⏎● A Shared Responsibility for Quality and Efficacy: Across HHS, adopt a risk-based⏎approach to healthcare AI that tailors risk mitigation to the potential harms of⏎intended and expected uses. Responsibility for managing risks should be⏎appropriately shared among developers, vendors, and providers based on their⏎knowledge of, and ability to address, those risks in alignment with leading⏎standards such as ISO/IEC 42001. HHS should prevent the improper or unfair⏎shifting of liability to those who rely on AI technologies in good faith to care for⏎patients so as to encourage innovation and adoption without introducing new risks⏎for critical health AI value chains. CHI’s Health AI Roles and Interdependencies⏎Framework,2 which describes the health AI value chain, defining actors and⏎describing roles for ensuring safety and efficacy as well as the interdependencies⏎between these actors and mapping these roles to functions in the National Institute⏎of Standards and Technology’s AI Risk Management Framework, can inform policy⏎decisions regarding AI liability.⏎● Transparency and Explainability: In alignment with existing standards and best⏎practices for making appropriate information public, provide clear, risk-based⏎communications that inform all healthcare stakeholders about relevant data⏎requirements, intended uses, limitations, target populations, bias mitigation, and⏎applications of AI tools. These communications should disclose sufficient detail to⏎help providers assess when a tool is appropriate for individual patients, clarify⏎whether the tool augments or automates clinical workflows, and specify⏎compliance with all applicable legal and regulatory requirements. Transparency is a⏎prerequisite for AI adoption.⏎● Access and Affordability: Prioritize measures that will ensure digital health⏎technologies and AI systems in health care are accessible and affordable across⏎research, health administration and operations, population health, practice⏎delivery improvement, and direct clinical care. Payment and incentive policies must⏎2 https://connectedhi.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/CHI-Health-AI-Roles.pdf⏎2⏎⏎be in place to invest in building health AI infrastructure; preparing personnel and⏎training; and appropriately incenting the responsible uptake and consistent use of⏎AI tools demonstrated to advance the Quadruple Aim. Notably, CMS should⏎modernize the way AI software is categorized and supported, including AI enabled⏎non-medical device technologies, and provide support in Medicare for AI tools to⏎improve Medicare beneficiaries’ experience and care (as well as working with states⏎to achieve a harmonized expansion of similar support for AI in Medicaid). Further,⏎CMS should make overdue modernizations to key disease prevention programs⏎(e.g., diabetes prevention) and support preventative healthcare by fully leveraging⏎digital health technologies tools, such as patient-facing wearables and AI-enabled⏎services. AI can play a central role in the transition to value-based care by providing⏎essential population health tools and providing enhanced scalability and patient⏎support.⏎● Interoperability: Ease data access and improve interoperability, while protecting⏎data privacy and security, to foster cooperation, trust, and openness among⏎patients, providers, health AI technology developers, and researchers. A truly⏎interoperable healthcare system is one that enables and engages patients across⏎multiple privacy-preserving platforms using open APIs, enabling the secure⏎integration of patient-generated health data into electronic health records.⏎Interoperability rules under the 21st Century Cures Act should enable clinicians’⏎access to medical records and patients to get their data and provide it to other⏎organizations, including for research. The success of AI tools for precision⏎medicine, population health, and clinical decision support, all of which are key⏎tools in addressing chronic diseases, depends on accessible and interoperable⏎data.⏎● Ethics: Given the longstanding, deeply rooted, and well-developed body of medical⏎and biomedical ethics, it will be critical to preserve existing and emerging ethical⏎norms developed by providers and healthcare professional organizations for⏎broader adherence by technologists, innovators, computer scientists, and those⏎who use such systems. From design, development, implementation, and to use,⏎healthcare AI tools should reflect the practical, ethical, and operational realities of⏎patient care.⏎● Workforce: The United States faces a stark, and growing, healthcare workforce⏎shortage. Successful creation and deployment of AI-enabled technologies which⏎help care providers streamline tasks and meet the needs of their patients will be an⏎essential part of addressing this shortage. Policies should support user education⏎and workforce development through AI upskilling and strengthen the clinician-⏎patient relationship.⏎● Education: Support educational efforts to increase AI literacy in healthcare,⏎highlight successful AI applications, and promote stakeholder engagement to keep⏎policies responsive to new opportunities and challenges. Education should be⏎developed by healthcare professional organizations in partnership with AI⏎3⏎⏎developers. Educating the public about how AI is used in their care increases⏎transparency, fosters trust, and enables patients to make informed decisions about⏎their health.⏎● Collaboration: We strongly encourage you to leverage the public-private⏎partnership framework to collaborate with providers, patients, industry, research⏎institutions, and government agencies to advance the above and to more broadly⏎drive safe and effective innovation in healthcare AI.⏎Further, CHI has worked with the broader community to develop, and strongly encourage the HHS⏎strategy to align with, healthcare ecosystem-wide consensus recommendations on the use of AI in⏎healthcare (each is appended to this comment):⏎• CHI’s Advancing Transparency for Artificial Intelligence in the Healthcare Ecosystem,⏎a proposal on ways to increase the transparency of and trust in health AI tools, particularly⏎for care teams and patients (https://connectedhi.com/wp-⏎content/uploads/2022/02/AdvancingTransparencyforArtificialIntelligenceintheHealthcareE⏎cosystem.pdf); and⏎• CHI’s Health AI Roles & Interdependency Framework, which proposes clear definitions⏎of stakeholders across the healthcare AI value chain, from development to distribution,⏎deployment, and end use; and suggests roles for supporting safety, ethical use, and⏎fairness for each of these important stakeholder groups that are intended to illuminate the⏎interdependencies between these actors, thus advancing the shared responsibility concept⏎(https://connectedhi.com/wp-content/uploads/2024/02/CHI-Health-AI-Roles.pdf); and⏎• CHI’s Value-Based Payment Reform: Leveraging SaaS Technologies for Care Model⏎Innovation, a report offering leading recommendations for payment reforms to enable and⏎incentivize the adoption of modern software and AI technologies and care model innovation⏎(https://connectedhi.com/value-based-payment-reform-leveraging-saas-technologies-for-⏎care-model-innovation/).⏎Building on the above, we also provide specific responses to the RFI’s questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎CHI has consistently identified several interconnected barriers that impede the private⏎sector's ability to innovate and deploy AI in clinical care:⏎Regulatory Uncertainty and Fragmentation. The current regulatory landscape is⏎fragmented across FDA, CMS, ONC, OCR, and state-level agencies, creating overlapping⏎and sometimes conflicting obligations for AI developers and deployers. As a leading⏎example, key stakeholders frequently lack clarity on whether their AI product is classified⏎as a medical device, clinical decision support (CDS) software, or a non-regulated wellness⏎tool. The lack of a unified, coherent framework makes investment planning and⏎compliance difficult. CHI supports a coordinated framework, which there are examples of⏎today, such as the coordinated CMS Advancing Chronic Care with Effective, Scalable⏎4⏎⏎Solutions (ACCESS) Model and FDA Technology-Enabled Meaningful Patient Outcomes⏎(TEMPO) for Digital Health Devices Pilot.⏎Reimbursement Gaps and Misaligned Incentives. Legacy fee-for-service payment models⏎do not recognize or reward the value that AI-enabled tools deliver. There is currently no⏎systematic mechanism for CMS to provide coverage and reimbursement for AI-powered⏎clinical decision support, ambient documentation, or remote patient monitoring tools that⏎improve outcomes and reduce costs. Without a viable reimbursement pathway,⏎innovators cannot achieve the commercial sustainability needed to scale, and health⏎systems lack the financial incentive to adopt these tools.⏎A significant barrier to medical AI innovation is CMS' outdated Medicare Part B Practice⏎Expense (PE) payment methodology which CMS itself has acknowledged in stating that it⏎hasn't updated the underlying data and methodology in over a decade. At the heart of the⏎problem is CMS' interpretation that it cannot pay for software, including Software as a⏎Medical Device (SaMD) or AI-based algorithmic solutions, under this framework. Despite⏎issuing annual requests for public comment on the topic since 2018, which requested⏎comments on everything from communications technologies and patient-generated health⏎data to digital therapies and AI, CMS has taken little meaningful action to resolve the⏎ambiguity. The fix lies in how PE costs are categorized: direct practice expenses, which are⏎allocable and actually reimbursed, cover clinical staff, medical supplies, and medical⏎equipment; while indirect expenses, which are largely non-allocable, cover administrative⏎labor, office overhead, and “computer software.” CMS has been treating software as the⏎latter, but this is legally inconsistent. Notably, SaMD has been classified as a medical⏎device under U.S. law since the Medical Device Amendments Act of 1976, is regulated by⏎the FDA on par with hardware devices, and carries the same legal, regulatory, and financial⏎burdens as any hardware medical device manufacturer. Categorizing it alongside generic⏎office software and administrative overhead makes no sense under the law. CMS can and⏎should act now to ensure that it categorizes SaMD as a medical device, not an⏎administrative expense, and therefore treats it as an allocable direct practice expense⏎within the current PE methodology.⏎Data Access, Quality, and Interoperability. AI models are only as good as the data on which⏎they are trained and validated. Inadequate interoperability across electronic health record⏎(EHR) systems, persistent information blocking, gaps in structured data availability, and⏎lack of standardized real-world datasets impede both the development and post-market⏎monitoring of AI tools. CHI has long advocated for enforcement of the ONC information⏎blocking rules, adoption of HL7 FHIR-based APIs, and expanded access to high-quality, de-⏎identified datasets to power AI development.⏎Privacy and Liability Uncertainty. The existing patchwork of federal and state privacy laws,⏎including HIPAA, the FTC Act, and various state-level statutes, create compliance⏎complexity for companies using patient data to train and validate AI models. The lack of a⏎comprehensive national privacy law leaves significant gaps in coverage, particularly for⏎5⏎⏎health data that falls outside HIPAA. Additionally, unclear liability frameworks for the use⏎of AI discourage health system adoption and developer investment.⏎Workforce and Adoption Readiness. Health systems, particularly safety-net providers,⏎rural facilities, and small practices, too often lack the IT infrastructure, data governance⏎capabilities, and clinician training needed to effectively integrate AI tools.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What⏎HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your⏎ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎CHI recommends the following priority changes:⏎Modernize CMS Reimbursement to Recognize Software AI-Enabled Care. CMS should⏎establish new supports across its existing payment rules that enable AI-supported clinical⏎services, building on the existing framework for Remote Physiologic/Therapeutic⏎Monitoring (RPM and RTM), Chronic Care Management (CCM), and Transitional Care⏎Management. Specifically, CMS should take further action to integrate AI-powered clinical⏎decision support into clinical workflows, and for ambient AI documentation tools that⏎reduce clinician burden, including through ensuring that SaMD is considered a direct PE⏎(described above in CHI’s answer to Question 1).⏎CHI notes that it is not required by law for CMS to exclude SaMD from its existing payment⏎methodology. CMS has, in fact, previously included procedure-specific software as direct⏎PE inputs, making it well within the agency's existing regulatory authority to properly⏎categorize SaMD as direct PE “medical equipment” rather than generic “computer⏎software” indirect PE. Moreover, because SaMD continuously evolves, requiring updates⏎and upgrades to address cybersecurity threats, vulnerabilities, and improvements driven⏎by real-world evidence, those ongoing costs are analogous to “medical supplies,” another⏎recognized direct PE category. Both the equipment and its upkeep should be accounted for⏎accordingly.⏎SaMD-delivered services are also separable and distinct procedures in their own right,⏎consistent with CMS' own rationale when it covered FFRCT CPT codes for augmentative⏎software analysis. CPT coding has evolved to recognize this shift through the creation of a⏎new AI taxonomy for CPT codes, classifying AI applications as assistive, augmentative, or⏎autonomous.3 Autonomous AI is increasingly performing work once done by physicians or⏎clinical staff, which is already reflected in CPT code 92229 for autonomous retinal imaging,⏎3 https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/cpt/cpt-appendix-s-ai-taxonomy-medical-services-⏎procedures.⏎6⏎⏎analysis CMS must update its PE methodology to treat SaMD as medical equipment, just⏎as it does for hardware devices.⏎Looking further ahead, AI raises deeper questions that go beyond straightforward⏎categorization. When machines fully replace provider or clinical staff work, policymakers⏎must address how Relative Value Units are updated to reflect that. And as digital⏎therapeutics introduce entirely new mechanisms of action, driven not by chemicals or⏎human intervention but by algorithms, entirely new frameworks may be needed. These are⏎questions that may deserve dedicated attention through a standalone public Request for⏎Information, an associated public meeting with national experts and relevant government⏎officials, and a formal expert process to build the right framework for capturing machine-⏎performed medical work. At the same time, these questions cannot sit unanswered for⏎much longer.⏎Accelerate Value-Based Care Models that Reward AI Outcomes. CMS Innovation Center⏎(CMMI) models are better positioned than fee-for-service to recognize and incentivize AI-⏎driven improvements in care quality and cost. HHS should expand and codify payment⏎models that allow participants to share in savings generated by AI tools. CHI continues to⏎support the ACCESS Model and other Models that are enabling cost savings and high-⏎quality prevention and treatments through leveraging digital health innovations.⏎Harmonize FDA Regulatory Framework for AI-Enabled Software. HHS should work with⏎FDA to finalize a clear, stable categorization framework for AI-enabled device software⏎functions consistent with the 21st Century Cures Act's clinical decision support provisions⏎(21 U.S.C. § 360j(o)). CHI supports a risk-tiered approach that reserves stringent premarket⏎review for high-risk functions while enabling lower-risk non-device AI tools to deploy under⏎quality and transparency frameworks without full FDA clearance, and is appreciative of⏎new FDA guidance that has provided greater clarity on CDS and general wellness devices.⏎Protect and Strengthen ASTP/ONC Interoperability and Information Blocking Rules. The⏎Health IT Certification Program and the information blocking provisions of the 21st Century⏎Cures Act should be strengthened to ensure that EHR systems make AI-enabling data⏎securely available in standardized FHIR-based formats. CHI has supported the use of⏎SMART on FHIR and CDS Hooks standards to enable AI applications to integrate⏎seamlessly into clinical workflows.⏎Revisit OIG Safe Harbor Regulations to Enable Value-Based AI Arrangements. The OIG safe⏎harbors under the Anti-Kickback Statute do not clearly protect arrangements in which⏎health systems provide AI tools to clinicians at below-market cost in furtherance of value-⏎based care. CHI urges OIG to clarify protections for AI tool dissemination in value-based⏎care arrangements.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance⏎7⏎⏎and accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification,⏎privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and⏎what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎CHI has identified the following novel legal and implementation issues for non-medical⏎device AI in clinical care:⏎Liability Allocation Across the AI Value Chain. CHI's Health AI Roles and Interdependency⏎Framework (2024) describes a complex chain of AI/ML developers, health AI platform⏎developers, digital health solution developers, deploying organizations, and clinical users,⏎each of share the responsibility for AI safety and efficacy. HHS should work with Congress⏎and, where possible, use its regulatory tools to clarify the appropriate allocation of⏎responsibility across this chain based on knowledge and the ability to do something about⏎known risks. HHS should also leverage the National AI Initiative and NIST's AI Risk⏎Management Framework as organizing principles for this work.⏎Privacy Gaps Outside HIPAA. A significant share of health-relevant AI development uses⏎data that falls outside HIPAA's scope, including data from wellness apps, wearables, and⏎consumer health platforms. CHI has long supported the enactment of a comprehensive⏎national privacy law and, absent such legislation, has urged HHS to use guidance to clarify⏎the applicability of HIPAA to AI training and validation pipelines. HHS should also clarify⏎the circumstances under which de-identification under 45 CFR Part 164.514 is sufficient to⏎permit use of clinical data for AI model training.⏎Information Blocking and AI Tool Integration. Information blocking provisions that apply to⏎health care providers, health IT developers, and health information networks should be⏎clarified so that there is no question about whether the use of technical or contractual⏎restrictions to prevent AI applications from accessing standardized EHR data constitutes⏎information blocking, and that the Information Blocking Rule applies to practices that⏎impede AI-enabled innovation.⏎8⏎⏎Improving the Approach to Non-Medical Devices in Federal Health Programs. Under 42⏎U.S. Code § 1395y(a)(1)(A) of the Social Security Act, Medicare cannot pay for any item or⏎service that is not “reasonable and necessary” for the diagnosis or treatment of illness or⏎injury. Separately, under Section 201(h) of the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act,⏎products used for exactly those purposes (diagnosis, treatment, or improving the⏎functioning of a malformed body member) are defined as regulated medical devices.⏎Relatively recently, CMS has been using the term Software as a Service (SaaS) when⏎discussing software-based clinical decision-making tools, but SaaS is a marketing term⏎describing cloud-based administrative and non-medical computing and has no bearing on⏎medical devices or clinical care. FDA has long regulated software that meets the Section⏎201(h) device definition, including CDS software intended to aid in diagnosis, treatment, or⏎disease mitigation. FDA has also clearly delineated what constitutes “Non-Device CDS”⏎functions like alerts, reminders, order sets, and documentation templates, which do not⏎meet the device definition and fall outside FDA oversight. When CMS discusses software⏎that supports clinical decision-making under Medicare Part B, it is talking about Device⏎CDS or SaMD, making use of the term SaaS problematic. CMS should ensure that it does⏎not conflate SaMD with SaaS, which may undermine the clarity that patients, providers,⏎and innovators all need.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow⏎and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care?⏎CHI has identified several evaluation approaches CHI believes HHS should support:⏎Risk-Proportionate Evaluation. CHI supports a risk-tiered approach in which the rigor of⏎evaluation is proportionate to the demonstrated harms. High-risk tools that autonomously⏎influence treatment decisions may require clinical validation, while lower-risk tools can be⏎validated through retrospective analysis and real-world performance monitoring.⏎Real-World Evidence and Post-Market Surveillance. CHI supports the use of EHR-derived⏎real-world data, device logs, and patient-reported outcomes for post-deployment⏎monitoring. HHS should develop standardized templates for AI model performance cards⏎that capture key metrics including accuracy, sensitivity, specificity, and equity indicators.⏎Human-Centered and Workflow Integration Evaluation. CHI supports evaluation⏎frameworks that examine not just algorithmic performance but human-AI interaction,⏎including how clinician trust, alert fatigue, over-reliance, and workflow friction affect⏎actual outcomes. The NIST AI RMF's emphasis on human factors provides a strong⏎foundation for this work.⏎HHS Support Mechanisms. CHI supports HHS investment in evaluation infrastructure⏎through grants and cooperative agreements with academic medical centers, federally⏎9⏎⏎qualified health centers, and AI testing labs. CHI also supports the development of open,⏎publicly accessible benchmark datasets and testing tools.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎CHI strongly supports iprivate sector-led governance and certification mechanisms as a⏎complement to government regulation. Key recommendations include:⏎Recognize and Incentivize Voluntary Certification Schemes. HHS should recognize⏎voluntary AI certification programs developed by industry consortia, standards bodies, and⏎professional societies as evidence of responsible AI practices. Participation in recognized⏎certification schemes should be considered favorably in CMS coverage decisions and in⏎compliance reviews.⏎Support the Development of Health AI Standards. CHI supports HHS collaboration with⏎NIST, ISO, and IEC to develop health-specific AI standards that can underpin voluntary⏎certification; and the incorporation by reference of those standards into goverance⏎approaches where appropriate. The NIST AI RMF provides a useful cross-sector foundation⏎that should guide HHS in developing a health sector profile that maps RMF functions to⏎clinical AI use cases (CHI notes that its Roles & Interdependencies Framework has done⏎this).⏎Establish a Recognized Testing and Evaluation Infrastructure. HHS should support the⏎development of test beds and sandbox environments where AI developers can evaluate⏎their tools against standardized benchmarks, including for data bias, robustness, and⏎interoperability. These test beds should be accessible to small developers on affordable⏎terms. CHI's AI Roles Framework notes that certification bodies and test beds have a⏎specific responsibility to create transparent processes for AI conformity assessment.⏎Coordinate with Accrediting and Licensing Bodies. Medical specialty societies, state⏎licensing boards, and hospital accreditation bodies (e.g., The Joint Commission) have an⏎important role in, and significant influence over, clinical AI adoption. HHS should engage⏎these bodies to develop clinical AI governance standards and to integrate AI competency⏎into clinical training and credentialing.⏎Protect Against Market Fragmentation from State Requirements. While CHI supports state⏎authority in healthcare regulation, proliferating and inconsistent state AI mandates create⏎compliance burdens. HHS should provide model frameworks and guidance that states can⏎adopt to minimize fragmentation, while preserving appropriate state innovation in⏎healthcare governance.⏎10⏎⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential?⏎Areas of Demonstrated Success. AI-enabled RPM/RTM tools have demonstrated clear⏎value in managing chronic conditions such as congestive heart failure, diabetes, and⏎hypertension, reducing hospitalizations, improving medication adherence, and enabling⏎more proactive clinical intervention. AI-powered ambient documentation tools have⏎shown promising results in reducing clinician administrative burden and documentation⏎time, directly addressing the clinician burnout crisis. AI-assisted diagnostic imaging tools,⏎particularly in radiology, pathology, and ophthalmology, have demonstrated performance⏎at or above human accuracy in well-defined, high-prevalence conditions.⏎Highest-Potential Novel Applications. CHI members and the broader health AI ecosystem⏎have identified significant potential in AI-enabled population health management tools⏎that synthesize multi-source data (EHR, RPM, SDOH) to identify high-risk patients and⏎recommend targeted interventions; generative AI tools for care coordination and patient⏎communication that reduce administrative burden while improving patient engagement;⏎AI-powered predictive models for hospital readmission, deterioration, and sepsis that can⏎be integrated into nursing workflows; and AI tools that enable precision medicine⏎approaches, including pharmacogenomics and personalized treatment planning, in⏎primary care settings.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care?⏎What are the primary administrative hurdles?⏎HHS should support the development of frameworks, data governance templates, and⏎clinical integration playbooks that health systems can adopt to reduce barriers, and CMMI⏎should consider including AI adoption support as a feature of future value-based care⏎models. Based on CHI's engagement across the digital health ecosystem, the following⏎stakeholders exercise the greatest influence on clinical AI adoption:⏎Chief Medical Officers (CMOs) and Chief Clinical Informatics Officers (CCIOs). Clinical⏎leadership approval is typically a prerequisite for adoption of AI tools that touch clinical⏎workflows. CMOs and CCIOs evaluate AI tools for clinical validity, workflow integration,⏎and liability implications. HHS support and guidance that give these stakeholders⏎confidence in AI tool quality will materially accelerate adoption.⏎Chief Information Officers (CIOs) and Chief Information Security Officers (CISOs).⏎Technical and cybersecurity leadership control procurement decisions, integration⏎timelines, and data governance. Interoperability barriers, including proprietary EHR APIs⏎11⏎⏎and lack of FHIR compliance, are frequently cited as the primary technical bottlenecks in⏎AI deployment.⏎Payers and Value-Based Care Coordinators. For health systems operating under value-⏎based contracts, payer coverage and reimbursement decisions are major determinants of⏎AI adoption. Tools that can demonstrate cost savings and quality improvements within⏎CMS and commercial quality measurement frameworks are more likely to receive⏎institutional investment.⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles. Key administrative barriers include: lengthy and complex⏎procurement and contracting processes that are poorly suited to fast-moving AI⏎technology; inadequate IT infrastructure and data governance in smaller and rural health⏎systems; lack of staff training and change management support for AI deployment; legal⏎uncertainty regarding liability for AI-assisted clinical decisions; and absence of⏎reimbursement signals that justify the cost of AI tool implementation.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please⏎consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking of tools.⏎CHI has consistently advocated for health data interoperability as an enabler of both⏎clinical care and digital health innovation. Key areas where enhanced interoperability⏎would most benefit AI development and deployment include:⏎FHIR-Based API Standardization. Broad adoption of HL7 FHIR R4 and emerging R4B/R5⏎standards, including SMART on FHIR for application authorization and CDS Hooks for real-⏎time clinical decision support integration, is fundamental to enabling AI tools to access the⏎clinical data they need at the point of care. CHI has supported ONC's interoperability⏎regulations and has urged aggressive enforcement of information blocking provisions to⏎ensure that EHR vendors provide FHIR-compliant APIs that AI developers can use without⏎prohibitive licensing restrictions.⏎Patient-Generated Health Data (PGHD) Integration. AI tools that can synthesize data from⏎wearables, remote patient monitoring devices, and consumer health applications⏎alongside EHR data have significantly greater clinical utility than those limited to EHR data⏎alone. CHI has long advocated for standardized pathways to integrate PGHD into EHRs⏎and population health platforms, including through standardized RPM device data formats⏎and the USCDI+ data classes.⏎Benchmarking and Reference Datasets. HHS should invest in the development and⏎maintenance of publicly accessible, de-identified clinical datasets that AI developers can⏎use for model development and validation. Reference datasets for specific disease areas⏎and care settings would enable more rigorous pre-deployment benchmarking. CHI also⏎12⏎⏎supports the use of synthetic data generation techniques to expand dataset availability⏎while preserving privacy.⏎HL7 FHIR for Real-World Evidence. CHI supports expanding the use of FHIR-structured⏎data in regulatory submissions and post-market surveillance, enabling AI developers to⏎use real-world evidence more efficiently in both FDA submissions and CMS coverage⏎applications.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? What concerns do⏎they have?⏎CHI's multistakeholder governance model includes patient and consumer advocates, and⏎our policy positions consistently reflect patient perspectives alongside industry interests.⏎CHI urges HHS to include meaningful patient and caregiver representation in all AI policy⏎development processes, and to support appropriate patient-facing disclosures when AI⏎tools are used in clinical decision-making. HHS should also support patient education⏎initiatives that improve health AI literacy and enable patients to make informed decisions⏎about the use of AI in their care. Based on CHI's engagement with patient stakeholders:⏎Patient Priorities for AI. Patients and caregivers most consistently seek AI applications that⏎reduce administrative burdens such as prior authorization delays and billing complexity⏎that impede access to care; improve care coordination and information sharing across⏎providers, reducing the burden on patients to serve as their own health information⏎couriers; enable earlier and more accurate diagnosis, particularly for rare diseases,⏎complex chronic conditions, and mental health, by synthesizing multi-source clinical data;⏎expand access to specialist-level care in underserved and rural communities through AI-⏎enhanced telemedicine and primary care support; and facilitate more personalized⏎treatment planning and medication management, including through AI-powered tools that⏎integrate patient preferences, lifestyle factors, and social determinants.⏎Patient Concerns About AI. CHI recognizes that patients have legitimate and important⏎concerns about AI in clinical care, including with respect to the privacy and security of⏎sensitive health data used to train and operate AI systems; transparency as to when and⏎how AI is influencing their clinical care; the human dimension of the clinical relationship if⏎AI reduces clinician engagement; and errors and accountability gaps when AI contributes⏎to clinical errors, particularly questions about who bears responsibility and how patients⏎can seek redress.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care? What does the literature say about⏎impact, costs, benefits, and transfers?⏎CHI recommends HHS prioritize the following AI research areas:⏎13⏎⏎Implementation Science and Care Delivery Research. The most important gap in health AI⏎research is not algorithmic performance but in how AI tools can be integrated into clinical⏎workflows at scale, in disparate care settings, with sustainable outcomes. HHS/NIH⏎should prioritize funding for implementation science research that examines the real-⏎world conditions under which AI tools deliver clinical value, including the role of clinician⏎training, organizational factors, and workflow design.⏎AI for Chronic Disease Management and Prevention. Given the chronic disease burden and⏎associated cost drivers in U.S. healthcare, CHI recommends HHS prioritize AI research⏎addressing both preventing and treating diabetes, cardiovascular disease, mental health,⏎and substance use disorders, all areas where AI-enabled RPM, predictive analytics, and⏎personalized intervention have demonstrated early promise.⏎Generative AI in Clinical Documentation and Care Coordination. HHS/NIH should fund⏎research on the accuracy, safety, and liability implications of generative AI used in clinical⏎note generation, care coordination, and patient communication.⏎Literature on Impact, Costs, and Benefits. Peer-reviewed literature documents significant⏎clinical and economic value from specific categories of health AI. Studies of AI-enabled⏎RPM programs for heart failure patients have documented reductions in 30-day⏎readmission rates and associated cost savings. AI-assisted imaging tools in radiology and⏎pathology have demonstrated improvements in diagnostic sensitivity for cancer screening.⏎At the same time, researchers have noted challenges in attributing outcomes specifically⏎to AI versus other care process improvements, and have highlighted the importance of⏎rigorous study design, pre-registration of endpoints, and head-to-head comparisons with⏎standard of care. HHS should invest in prospective comparative research that generates⏎high-quality evidence on AI clinical and economic outcomes that can inform coverage⏎decisions and clinical guidelines.⏎***⏎CHI appreciates HHS's forward-looking approach to accelerating clinical AI adoption⏎through this RFI. The responses provided above reflect CHI's long-standing consensus⏎positions developed through years of multistakeholder engagement across developers,⏎clinicians, patients, payers, and regulators. CHI stands ready to provide further input,⏎engage in follow-on consultations, and work with HHS to translate these⏎recommendations into actionable policy. We are committed to a future in which AI in⏎clinical care is safe, effective, equitable, and accessible — driving better health outcomes⏎for all Americans.⏎14⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Brian Scarpelli⏎Executive Director⏎Chapin Gregor⏎Policy Counsel⏎Connected Health Initiative⏎1401 K St NW (Ste 501)⏎Washington, DC 20005⏎15⏎⏎CHI Health AI Roles &⏎Interdependency Framework⏎⏎Overview⏎Artificial Intelligence (AI), especially generative AI, is already a powerful tool in healthcare, offering amazing potential to upgrade patient⏎care by improving care outcomes and patient experiences, reducing healthcare provider burnout by simplifying administrative tasks, and⏎helping to lower the total cost of care. One of the most helpful ways to see the value of AI in healthcare is to view the question through⏎the lens of the “quadruple aim” framework. Built on the Institute for Healthcare Improvement’s “triple aim,” a widely accepted compass⏎to optimize health system performance, the quadruple aim focuses on four key areas where health systems need to be improved, all of⏎which AI is already, and will continue to, provide value across:⏎• Enhancing population health.⏎• Improving patient experience, satisfaction, and health outcomes.⏎• Augmenting clinician and healthcare team experience and satisfaction.⏎• Lowering overall costs of healthcare.⏎CHI has explored the ways in which AI is supporting each of the four aims of the quadruple aim in CHI’s paper, Why Does Healthcare⏎Need AI?⏎But this promising technology is not infallible, and as healthcare organizations seek opportunities to use AI, stakeholders are facing⏎important questions about how various risks or limitations should be handled in the development, distribution, deployment, and end⏎use chain. Many organizations involved in the creation or application of healthcare AI have started to develop Responsible AI programs⏎aimed at managing these risks or limitations within their organization. But as we have learned from other new technologies in the past,⏎stakeholders can benefit from a clear discussion around all the safety measures and other actions that are needed, and how those⏎actions might be applied at different steps from creation to the operation of the tool by the end user. This discussion will help various⏎stakeholders better determine accountability for responsible AI best practices across this chain of stakeholders.⏎2⏎⏎CHI urges all stakeholders in the healthcare ecosystem that are developing and using AI to align⏎with CHI’s consensus health AI principles, which recognize the shared responsibility for AI safety,⏎efficacy, and transparency. CHI supports (1) leveraging a risk-based approach to AI harm⏎mitigation where the level of review, assurance, and oversight is proportionate to potential harms⏎and (2) those in the value chain with the ability to minimize risks based on their knowledge and⏎ability, and having appropriate responsibilities and incentives to do so.⏎Further, managing AI/Machine Learning (ML) risks will be more challenging for small to medium-sized organizations, depending on⏎their capabilities and resources. Building on these general health AI principles, CHI proposes clear definitions of stakeholders across⏎the healthcare AI value chain, from development to distribution, deployment, and end use. Then, CHI suggests roles for supporting⏎safety, ethical use, and fairness for each of these important stakeholder groups that are intended to illuminate the interdependencies⏎between these actors, thus advancing the shared responsibility concept. These roles and interdependencies are also mapped to the⏎Functions defined in the National Institute of Standards and Technology’s (NIST’s) AI Risk Management Framework (RMF).⏎3⏎⏎Patient Groups/⏎Patient Users⏎1⏎Providers/ Clinicians &⏎Solution Users⏎Administrative Users⏎Deploying⏎2 Organization⏎Solutions/App⏎Developers &⏎Deployers Digital Health Payers⏎Solution Developer (CMS, Private)⏎3⏎Health AI Platform⏎AI/ML Developers Developer⏎AI Platform Developer⏎Note: Depending on the use⏎case, some of the roles in the⏎healthcare AI/ML value chain⏎may be occupied by the same Foundation Model⏎Developer⏎party; in other scenarios, some⏎roles may not be occupied.⏎4⏎⏎NIST AI RMF⏎Stakeholder Group Defini�on Roles⏎Actor Tasks⏎AI/ML 