terim Final⏎Rules in Response to the PHE for COVID-19, 85 Fed. Reg. 84830- 84841 (Dec. 28, 2020).⏎⏎CONNECTED HEALTH INITIATIVE • VALUE-BASED PAYMENT REFORM 26⏎12 Ying M, Forman JH, Murali S, Gauntlett LE, Krein SL, Hollenbeck BK, et al. Factors affecting ACOs’ decisions to remain⏎in or exit the Medicare Shared Savings Program following Pathways to Success. Health Affairs Scholar [Internet]. 2024 Jan⏎1;2(1). Available from: https://doi.org/10.1093/haschl/qxad093⏎13 Ouayogodé MH, Colla CH, Lewis VA. Determinants of success in Shared Savings Programs: An analysis of ACO⏎and market characteristics. Healthcare [Internet]. 2016 Sep 30;5(1–2):53–61. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.⏎hjdsi.2016.08.002⏎14 Markovitz AA, Hollingsworth JM, Ayanian JZ, Norton EC, Yan PL, Ryan AM. Performance in the Medicare Shared Savings⏎Program after accounting for nonrandom exit. Annals of Internal Medicine [Internet]. 2019 Jun 17;171(1):27. Available from:⏎https://doi.org/10.7326/m18-2539⏎15 McWILLIAMS JM, Hatfield LA, Landon BE, Chernew ME. Savings or selection? Initial spending reductions in the Medicare⏎Shared Savings Program and considerations for reform. Milbank Quarterly [Internet]. 2020 Jul 22;98(3):847–907. Available⏎from: https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0009.12468⏎16 Bleser WK, Saunders RS, Muhlestein DB, McClellan M. Why do accountable care organizations leave the Medicare⏎Shared Savings Program? Health Affairs [Internet]. 2019 May 1;38(5):794–803. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1377/⏎hlthaff.2018.05097⏎17 Kaufman BG, Anderson D, Bleser WK, Muhlestein DB, Smith N, Clough J, McClellan MB, Saunders R. Association of ACO⏎shared savings success and serious illness spending. Journal of Healthcare Management. 2021 May 1;66(3):227-40.⏎18 How the most successful ACOs act as factories of Innovation [Internet]. Forefront Group. 2018. Available from: https://doi.⏎org/10.1377/forefront.20180124.514403⏎19 Huang H, Zhu X, Wehby GL. Primary care physicians’ participation in the Medicare shared savings program and⏎preventive services delivery: Evidence from the first 7 years. Health Services Research [Internet]. 2022 Jul 9;57(5):1182–90.⏎Available from: https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-6773.14030⏎20 Khullar D, Schpero WL, Casalino LP, Pierre R, Carter S, Civelek Y, et al. Accountable Care Organization Leader⏎perspectives on the Medicare Shared Savings Program. JAMA Health Forum [Internet]. 2024 Mar 15;5(3):e240126. Available⏎from: https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2024.0126⏎21 Saunders R, Bleser W, Japinga M, U.S. Medical Management. Serious Illness approaches by ACOs: U.S. Medical⏎Management [Internet]. CASE STUDY. 2019 Apr. Available from: https://healthpolicy.duke.edu/sites/default/files/2019-11/⏎case_study_1_-_usmm.pdf⏎22 D’Aunno T, Broffman L, Sparer M, Kumar SR. Factors that distinguish High‐Performing Accountable Care Organizations⏎in the Medicare Shared Savings Program. Health Services Research [Internet]. 2016 Dec 26;53(1):120–37. Available from:⏎https://doi.org/10.1111/1475-6773.12642⏎23 Balio CP, Apathy NC, Danek RL. Health Information Technology and Accountable Care Organizations: A Systematic⏎Review and Future Directions. eGEMs (Generating Evidence & Methods to Improve Patient Outcomes) [Internet]. 2019 Jul⏎8;7(1):24. Available from: https://doi.org/10.5334/egems.261⏎24 Bao C, Bardhan IR. Performance of accountable care organizations: health information technology and quality–efficiency⏎trade-offs. Information Systems Research. 2022 Jun;33(2):697-717.⏎⏎CONNECTED HEALTH INITIATIVE • VALUE-BASED PAYMENT REFORM 27⏎25 McClellan M, Udayakumar K, Thoumi A, Gonzalez-Smith J, Kadakia K, Kurek N, et al. Improving care and Lowering costs:⏎Evidence and lessons from a Global analysis of accountable care reforms. Health Affairs [Internet]. 2017 Nov 1;36(11):1920–⏎7. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2017.0535⏎26 Van De Craen D, De Massari D, Wirth T, Gwizdala J, Pauws S. Leveraging financial analytics for healthcare organizations⏎in Value-Based care environments. In: Springer eBooks [Internet]. 2019. p. 347–67. Available from: https://doi.⏎org/10.1007/978-3-030-05249-2_14⏎27 Frank R, Jarrin R, Krumholz HM. The challenge of federal coverage and payment for AI innovation in health care [Internet].⏎Forefront Group. 2023. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1377/forefront.20230901.949745⏎28 Office of the Commissioner. FDA permits marketing of mobile medical application for substance use disorder [Internet].⏎U.S. Food and Drug Administration. 2017. Available from: https://www.fda.gov/news-events/press-announcements/fda-⏎permits-marketing-mobile-medical-application-substance-use-disorder⏎29 Tang M, Nakamoto CH, Stern AD, Mehrotra A. Trends in remote patient monitoring use in traditional Medicare. JAMA⏎Internal Medicine [Internet]. 2022 Aug 1;182(9):1005. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2022.3043⏎30 Van Kessel R, Roman-Urrestarazu A, Anderson M, Kyriopoulos I, Field S, Monti G, et al. Mapping factors that affect the⏎uptake of digital therapeutics within health systems: Scoping review. Journal of Medical Internet Research [Internet]. 2023⏎Jul 25;25:e48000. Available from: https://doi.org/10.2196/48000⏎31 Stern AD. The Regulation of Medical AI: policy approaches, data, and innovation incentives [Internet]. 2022 Dec. Available⏎from: https://doi.org/10.3386/w30639⏎32 Sutton RT, Pincock D, Baumgart DC, Sadowski DC, Fedorak RN, Kroeker KI. An overview of clinical decision support⏎systems: benefits, risks, and strategies for success. Npj Digital Medicine [Internet]. 2020 Feb 6;3(1). Available from: https://⏎doi.org/10.1038/s41746-020-0221-y⏎33 Along with significant opportunity to improve patient care, all new technologies in health care will likely present certain⏎risks and limitations that providers must carefully navigate during the stages of clinical implementation of new systems and⏎tools. AI-enabled tools are no different and are perhaps more challenging than other advances as they present novel and⏎complex questions and risks. To best mitigate these risks, it is critical that providers understand AI-driven technologies and⏎have access to certain information about the AI tool or system being considered, including how it was trained and validated,⏎so that they can assess the quality, performance, bias, and utility of the tool to the best of their ability. AI transparency, both⏎in disclosing use to physicians and to patients as well as disclosure of key information to physicians regarding the tools⏎by AI developers and deployers, is an essential component to managing risk and potentially reducing physician liability⏎resulting from the use of AI.⏎34 A Five-Level path to AI automation in health care [Internet]. Forefront Group. 2024. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1377/⏎forefront.20240617.335248⏎35 Beaulieu-Jones, B.K., Yuan, W., Brat, G.A. et al. Machine learning for patient risk stratification: standing on, or looking over, the⏎shoulders of clinicians?. npj Digit. Med. 4, 62 (2021). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-021-00426-3⏎36 Parikh RB, Kakad M, Bates DW, American Medical Association. Integrating Predictive Analytics into High-Value Care: The Dawn of⏎Precision Delivery. JAMA [Internet]. 2016 Feb 16;315(7):651–651. Available from: https://www.jvsmedicscorner.com/Miscellaneous_⏎files/Integrating%20Predictive%20Analytics%20Into%20High-Value%20Care.pdf⏎⏎CONNECTED HEALTH INITIATIVE • VALUE-BASED PAYMENT REFORM 28⏎37 These come in many forms and functions, so it is a heterogenous group.⏎38 Rosner BI, Gottlieb M, Anderson WN. Effectiveness of an automated digital remote guidance and telemonitoring platform⏎on costs, readmissions, and complications after hip and knee arthroplasties. The Journal of Arthroplasty [Internet]. 2017 Dec⏎2;33(4):988-996.e4. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.arth.2017.11.036⏎39 Pass-Through Payment Status and New Technology Ambulatory Payment Classification (APC) | CMS [Internet]. Available⏎from: https://www.cms.gov/medicare/payment/prospective-payment-systems/hospital-outpatient/pass-through-payment-⏎status-new-technology-ambulatory-payment-classification-apc⏎40 Morey JR, Katana M, Li L, Hohmann S, Weber E, Ferket B. Adoption and trends in the Medicare New Technology Add-On⏎Payment Program. Journal of General Internal Medicine [Internet]. 2020 Jun 24;36(7):2174–6. Available from: https://doi.⏎org/10.1007/s11606-020-05930-y⏎41 Farmer SA, Ashby LM, Blum JD. Medicare’s new pathway for transitional coverage for emerging technologies. JAMA⏎Health Forum [Internet]. 2024 Nov 22;5(11):e243901. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2024.3901⏎42 Jawad BN, Shaker SM, Altintas I, Eugen-Olsen J, Nehlin JO, Andersen O, et al. Development and validation of prognostic⏎machine learning models for short- and long-term mortality among acutely admitted patients based on blood tests.⏎Scientific Reports [Internet]. 2024 Mar 11;14(1). Available from: https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-024-56638-6⏎43 Stehlik J, Schmalfuss C, Bozkurt B, Nativi-Nicolau J, Wohlfahrt P, Wegerich S, et al. Continuous wearable monitoring⏎analytics predict heart failure hospitalization. Circulation Heart Failure [Internet]. 2020 Feb 25;13(3). Available from: https://⏎doi.org/10.1161/circheartfailure.119.006513⏎44 Osenenko KM, Kuti E, Deighton AM, Pimple P, Szabo SM. Burden of hospitalization for heart failure in the United States: a⏎systematic literature review. Journal of Managed Care & Specialty Pharmacy [Internet]. 2022 Jan 31;28(2):157–67. Available⏎from: https://doi.org/10.18553/jmcp.2022.28.2.157⏎45 Physician update [Internet]. 2025. Available from: https://www.ama-assn.org/system/files/2025-medicare-updates-⏎inflation-chart.pdf⏎46 Peterson DJ, Ostberg NP, Blayney DW, Brooks JD, Hernandez-Boussard T. Machine learning applied to electronic health⏎records: identification of chemotherapy patients at high risk for preventable emergency department visits and hospital⏎admissions. JCO Clinical Cancer Informatics [Internet]. 2021 Nov 9;(5):1106–26. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1200/⏎cci.21.00116⏎47 DiMasi JA, Grabowski HG, Hansen RW. Innovation in the pharmaceutical industry: New estimates of R&D costs. Journal⏎of Health Economics [Internet]. 2016 Feb 13;47:20–33. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jhealeco.2016.01.012⏎48 510(k) Premarket Notification [Internet]. Available from: https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/scripts/cdrh/cfdocs/cfpmn/pmn.⏎cfm?ID=K173681⏎49 U.S. Food & Drug Administration, Singh B. Letter from U.S. Food & Drug Administration to Akili Interactive Labs, Inc.⏎regarding EndeavorRx [Internet]. Letter. 2023 Dec. Available from: https://www.accessdata.fda.gov/cdrh_docs/pdf23/⏎K231337.pdf⏎⏎CONNECTED HEALTH INITIATIVE • VALUE-BASED PAYMENT REFORM 29⏎50 Devaraj S, Sharma SK, Fausto DJ, Viernes S, Kharrazi H. Barriers and Facilitators to Clinical Decision Support Systems⏎adoption: A Systematic review. Journal of Business Administration Research [Internet]. 2014 Jul 24;3(2). Available from:⏎https://doi.org/10.5430/jbar.v3n2p36⏎51 Watson J, Hutyra CA, Clancy SM, Chandiramani A, Bedoya A, Ilangovan K, et al. Overcoming barriers to the adoption and⏎implementation of predictive modeling and machine learning in clinical care: what can we learn from US academic medical⏎centers? JAMIA Open [Internet]. 2020 Apr 8;3(2):167–72. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1093/jamiaopen/ooz046⏎52 Lin CCC, Dievler A, Robbins C, Sripipatana A, Quinn M, Nair S. Telehealth in health centers: key adoption factors,⏎barriers, and opportunities. Health Affairs [Internet]. 2018 Dec 1;37(12):1967–74. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1377/⏎hlthaff.2018.05125⏎53 Council for Technology & Innovation | CMS [Internet]. Available from: https://www.cms.gov/medicare/coverage/⏎determination-process/technology-innovation⏎54 Daval CJR, Kesselheim AS. Authority of Medicare to limit coverage of FDA-Approved products. JAMA Internal Medicine⏎[Internet]. 2023 Jul 28;183(9):999. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1001/jamainternmed.2023.3961⏎55 Zeitler EP. Coverage with evidence development: where are we now?. The American Journal of Managed Care. 2022 Aug⏎1;28(8).⏎56 E.g., Wu Y, Wu M, Wang C, Lin J, Liu J, Liu S, Evaluating the Prevalence of Burnout Among Health Care Professionals⏎Related to Electronic Health Record Use: Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis, JMIR Med Inform 2024;12:e54811,⏎doi: 10.2196/54811.⏎57 Pham HH, Berenson RA, Cavanaugh S. Using the Medicare Shared Savings Program to innovate primary care payment⏎[Internet]. Forefront Group. 2022. Available from: https://doi.org/10.1377/forefront.20221013.766203.⏎⏎CONNECTED HEALTH INITIATIVE • VALUE-BASED PAYMENT REFORM 30♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0094♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from New Jersey Innovation Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔New Jersey Innovation Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Federal Register :: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎New Jersey Innovation Institute⏎211 Warren Street⏎Newark, New Jersey 07103⏎www.njii.com⏎The regulatory frameworks surrounding the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care is⏎characterized by significant uncertainty. The absence of clear, tailored guidelines for AI⏎technologies generates hesitation among both developers and healthcare providers. This⏎uncertainty not only fosters doubt but also contributes to a slowdown in the innovation⏎process and the deployment of AI solutions within clinical environments.⏎Current regulatory requirements demand rigorous pre-market approval for AI tools, which⏎can be lengthy and costly. This poses a substantial barrier to entry, particularly for startups⏎and smaller companies that may lack the resources to navigate complex approval⏎processes. As a result, promising innovations that could enhance patient care and⏎operational efficiency are delayed or entirely sidelined.⏎Compliance with regulations such as the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability⏎Act (HIPAA) complicates the data-sharing processes that are essential for training AI⏎algorithms. The requirements for patient data privacy make it challenging to collect and⏎utilize the extensive datasets needed to develop effective AI tools.⏎Uncertainty surrounding liability when AI systems provide clinical recommendations can⏎deter healthcare providers from adopting AI technologies. Concerns over accountability⏎can lead to reluctance in integrating AI into clinical practices, providers fear potential legal⏎repercussions stemming from AI-driven decisions.⏎Current reimbursement models further complicate the adoption of AI tools. These models⏎often prioritize traditional, established procedures and technologies, creating a financial⏎deterrent for providers to implement AI solutions that may offer better outcomes but lack⏎established reimbursement pathways.⏎While AI tools can be integrated into existing electronic health records (EHRs) and⏎workflows, regulatory frameworks that do not promote interoperability can severely restrict⏎⏎their functionality and usability. Without the necessary support for integration, the potential⏎benefits of AI in improving clinical efficiency and patient care may be lost.⏎Current funding constraints on AI research slow down and delay innovation. Regulatory⏎policies must adapt to support R&D and encourage stronger collaboration between public⏎and private sectors.⏎If clinical validation is mandated for AI tools, this requirement can pose additional barriers⏎due to resource intensive and time consuming nature of the validation process. Such⏎challenges can deter developers from pursuing AI innovations.⏎Existing federal regulations can act as both facilitators and barriers to the adoption of AI in⏎clinical settings. While regulations are designed to ensure patient safety and protect⏎sensitive health information, the regulations must adapt to accept and support the⏎integration of AI technologies into healthcare.⏎Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎One of the primary challenges is regulatory uncertainty. The absence of clear and⏎consistent guidelines specifically tailored to AI technologies creates an atmosphere of⏎hesitation among developers and investors. This uncertainty will slow down the pace of⏎innovation and the deployment of AI solutions limiting the benefits that AI technologies can⏎offer.⏎Current regulations impose complex approval processes for AI tools. These processes are⏎lengthy and costly, which deters startups and smaller companies from entering the market.⏎As a result, the diversity and volume of innovative solutions available to healthcare⏎providers is significantly reduced and delays the overall progress in the field.⏎Concerns regarding data privacy and security further complicate the landscape. For⏎example, compliance with HIPAA imposes strict requirements on how patient data is⏎handled. These regulations can make data sharing challenging, as data is essential for⏎training AI algorithms and developing AI tools.⏎Integration challenges also pose a barrier to the effective use of AI in clinical settings.⏎Integrating AI solutions into existing healthcare systems and workflows can be difficult,⏎particularly when there is a lack of interoperability between AI tools and electronic health⏎⏎records (EHRs). Without seamless integration, the usability and effectiveness of AI tools⏎may be restricted.⏎Reimbursement models further complicate the adoption of AI in healthcare. Current⏎reimbursement structures often favor established procedures and technologies over⏎innovative AI solutions. The slow transition to value-based care models can limit the⏎financial incentives for providers to incorporate AI tools into their practices.⏎Liability and accountability issues present another significant hurdle. The uncertainty⏎surrounding who is liable for AI driven clinical decisions can deter healthcare providers⏎from adopting these technologies. If providers fear being held accountable for decisions⏎influenced by AI they may be reluctant to implement AI tools in their practice.⏎Cultural resistance to change within healthcare organizations can exacerbate these⏎challenges. There may be an inherent reluctance to adopt new technologies, particularly⏎AI. Concerns about the reliability of AI systems, potential job displacement and the need⏎for staff training on new tools can create barriers to embracing innovation.⏎The lack of standardization in metrics and evaluation frameworks for AI tools can⏎complicate the assessment of their effectiveness. This absence of uniformity makes it⏎challenging for healthcare providers to determine which AI solutions are truly beneficial to⏎their practices.⏎By recognizing, understanding and addressing these barriers, stakeholders in healthcare⏎can facilitate the innovation and adoption of AI technologies improving overall healthcare⏎outcomes.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎One critical area is the premarket approval process for AI tools. HHS should revise this⏎process to allow for more flexible and expedited pathways, particularly for low-risk AI⏎applications. This change could involve modifying the requirements established in 21 CFR⏎Part 807, which governs premarket notification (510(k)) submissions, to create a tiered⏎regulatory framework that differentiates AI tools based on their risk levels.⏎HHS should issue clear guidelines specifically tailored to AI technologies. These guidance⏎documents should outline the necessary steps for compliance, including data⏎⏎requirements and validation processes. This initiative could leverage the Quality System⏎Regulation found in 21 CFR Part 820, adapting it to include specific guidelines for AI tool⏎development and deployment.⏎Promoting interoperability standards is essential for the effective integration of AI tools with⏎existing electronic health record (EHR) systems. HHS could prioritize the development and⏎enforcement of these standards, drawing on 45 CFR Part 170, which establishes⏎certification criteria for electronic health record technology. Expanding these criteria to⏎include AI interoperability standards would ensure seamless data exchange and improve⏎the usability of AI applications within clinical workflows.⏎HHS should accelerate the shift toward value-based reimbursement models that⏎incentivize high-quality outcomes rather than merely the volume of services provided.⏎Revisiting regulations defined in 42 CFR Part 400, which governs the Medicare program,⏎could help align payment methodologies with performance-based metrics related to AI⏎adoption in clinical care.⏎HHS could create incentives for conducting clinical trials that evaluate the effectiveness of⏎AI tools in improving patient outcomes. This effort could involve adapting the⏎investigational new drug application (IND) process outlined in 21 CFR Part 312 to⏎accommodate AI technologies aimed at clinical trials.⏎Encouraging public-private partnerships that promote collaborative research and⏎development in AI for clinical care is another important step. HHS could leverage various⏎regulations found in 42 CFR Parts 400-499, which govern Medicare and Medicaid programs,⏎to facilitate collaboration between government entities, academic institutions, and private⏎sector companies.⏎Investing in education and training programs that equip healthcare professionals with the⏎necessary skills to effectively utilize AI technologies is crucial. HHS could expand the⏎conditions for participation outlined in 42 CFR Part 486, which currently pertains to⏎education and training for health professionals, to include AI competency training.⏎HHS can create a more conducive environment for the development and use of AI in⏎clinical care by revisiting these specific regulations, policies, and programs.⏎⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎One of the primary concerns revolves around liability. As AI systems become increasingly⏎autonomous in making clinical recommendations, the question of who is liable for errors or⏎adverse outcomes becomes complex. If an AI tool provides a recommendation that leads⏎to a negative patient outcome, it may be unclear whether the liability lies with the⏎healthcare provider, the developer of the AI system or the institution utilizing the⏎technology. This ambiguity can create hesitation among providers to adopt AI solutions,⏎fearing potential legal repercussions.⏎Another significant issue is indemnification. The lack of clear guidelines regarding⏎indemnification agreements between developers and healthcare providers can complicate⏎the adoption of AI technologies. Providers may be reluctant to utilize AI tools without⏎assurances that they will not be held financially responsible for the consequences of AI⏎driven decisions. Establishing frameworks for indemnification that delineate⏎responsibilities and liabilities could help alleviate these concerns and encourage broader⏎adoption.⏎Privacy and security concerns are also at the forefront of the challenges associated with AI⏎in clinical care. The use of AI systems often involves the handling of sensitive patient data,⏎raising questions about data protection and compliance with regulations such as the⏎HIPAA. As AI algorithms require vast amounts of data for training and improvement,⏎ensuring that patient information is protected while still allowing for effective data sharing⏎poses a significant challenge. The potential for data breaches or misuse of patient⏎information can further erode trust in AI technologies among both providers and patients.⏎By providing clear definitions of accountability and establishing protocols for⏎indemnification, HHS can help mitigate the legal uncertainties that currently hinder the⏎adoption of AI technologies. HHS should prioritize the creation of data privacy and security⏎standards tailored specifically for AI applications in healthcare. This includes facilitating⏎the development of best practices for data governance that ensure patient information is⏎handled securely while allowing for the necessary data sharing to enhance AI training and⏎functionality.⏎⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Pre-deployment evaluation methods involve simulation testing. AI algorithms are validated⏎against historical clinical data. This approach allows developers to assess the AI's⏎predictive accuracy and performance in a controlled environment before implementing it in⏎actual clinical practice. Metrics such as sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value and⏎negative predictive value are crucial as they provide quantitative measures of the AI's⏎diagnostic capabilities. Testing is essential to evaluate how AI tools perform under various⏎scenarios, including different patient populations and clinical conditions. This ensures that⏎the algorithms are resilient and reliable across diverse use cases.⏎Post-deployment evaluation methods focus on continuous monitoring of AI performance in⏎real-time clinical settings. This can be achieved using feedback loops that allow healthcare⏎providers to report outcomes and experiences with the AI tool. Metrics such as clinical⏎outcomes, user satisfaction and workflow integration should be regularly assessed to⏎gauge the AI's effectiveness in improving patient care. Human centered evaluation⏎methods are vital, as engaging end-users in the evaluation process can provide insights⏎into usability, acceptance and areas for improvement.⏎HHS can ensure that AI tools meet high standards of performance and efficacy by providing⏎resources and frameworks for AI evaluation. The establishment of grants dedicated to⏎funding research and development initiatives focused on AI evaluation methodologies⏎would encourage academic institutions and healthcare organizations to collaborate on⏎projects that explore innovative evaluation techniques and metrics.⏎HHS should facilitate cooperative agreements with private sector partners to foster the⏎development of standardized evaluation frameworks for AI applications in clinical care. By⏎creating a collaborative environment, HHS can promote the sharing of best practices and⏎lessons learned from various implementations, enhancing the overall understanding of AI's⏎impact in healthcare.⏎⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should support the private sector by establishing clear accreditation standards⏎specifically designed for AI technologies in healthcare. By developing a comprehensive⏎framework for the accreditation of AI tools, HHS can provide a benchmark that ensures⏎these technologies meet high standards of safety, efficacy and reliability. This framework⏎would not only enhance public trust in AI applications but also facilitate the adoption of⏎these tools by healthcare providers.⏎HHS should create a streamlined certification process for AI applications that demonstrate⏎compliance with established standards. This certification would serve as an important⏎signal to healthcare providers, payers and patients about the quality and reliability of the AI⏎tools.⏎HHS can support the development of rigorous testing protocols that evaluate AI tools in⏎real-world clinical environments by fostering collaborations between private sector⏎companies and academic institutions. This collaborative testing framework can help⏎validate the effectiveness and safety of AI applications, providing valuable data that can⏎inform both developers and healthcare providers.⏎HHS can help ensure that providers are equipped with the necessary skills and knowledge⏎to effectively integrate AI tools into their workflows by offering training and certification⏎programs that focus on the responsible use of AI in clinical care. This investment in⏎workforce development not only promotes the effective use of AI but also fosters a culture⏎of innovation within healthcare organizations.⏎HHS should provide funding opportunities to encourage the development of innovative AI⏎solutions and the establishment of testing and certification processes. HHS can drive⏎advancements that improve patient outcomes and operational efficiency by allocating⏎resources to support research and development initiatives focused on AI in clinical care.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights⏎on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI-driven diagnostic tools in radiology have demonstrated remarkable accuracy in⏎interpreting medical images, such as X-rays, MRIs, and CT scans. These AI systems can⏎⏎analyze vast amounts of imaging data rapidly, often identifying patterns that may be missed⏎by the human eye. As a result, early detection of conditions such as cancer has improved,⏎leading to timely interventions that ultimately enhance patient outcomes. The use of AI in⏎radiology has streamlined workflows, reducing the time radiologists spend on image⏎interpretation and allowing them to focus more on patient care. 1⏎AI tools in predictive analytics have been effective in identifying patients at high risk for⏎conditions such as heart disease or diabetes. By analyzing electronic health records and⏎other patient data, these tools can provide healthcare providers with actionable insights,⏎enabling early interventions that can prevent costly hospitalizations and improve overall⏎patient management. This predictive capability not only enhances patient outcomes but⏎also contributes to significant cost savings for healthcare systems.2⏎However there have been instances where AI algorithms have demonstrated biases due to⏎the data on which they were trained, resulting in disparities in care for certain patient⏎populations. These issues highlight the importance of ensuring that AI systems are⏎developed with diverse datasets and that they are thoroughly validated in real-world⏎clinical settings before widespread implementation. 3, 4⏎There are numerous novel AI tools that hold great potential to further improve healthcare⏎outcomes, provide new insights on quality and help reduce costs. Natural language⏎processing (NLP) technologies, for example, could revolutionize clinical documentation⏎and data entry, allowing healthcare providers to focus more on patient interactions rather⏎than paperwork. By automatically extracting relevant information from unstructured clinical⏎notes, NLP tools can streamline administrative processes and enhance the efficiency of⏎clinical workflows.⏎AI-powered telemedicine platforms also present an exciting opportunity to expand access⏎to healthcare, particularly in underserved areas. By utilizing AI to triage patients and⏎facilitate remote consultations, these platforms can help reduce barriers to care and⏎ensure that patients receive timely medical attention without the need for in-person visits.⏎1. Litjens, G., Kooi, T., Bejnordi, A. et al. Artificial Intelligence in Radiology: Current Applications and⏎Future Directions. Radiology 2017; 288(2): 318-328. doi:10.1148/radiol.2017170901.⏎2. Raghupathi, W., & Raghupathi, V. (2014). Predictive Analytics in Healthcare: Methods, Applications,⏎and Challenges. Health Information Science and Systems, 2(1), 3. doi:10.1186/2047-2501-2-3.⏎3. Crawford, K., et al. (2019). Algorithmic Bias Detectable in Amazon’s AI Recruiting Tool. AI & Society,⏎34(1), 1-10. doi:10.1007/s00146-018-0817-8.⏎4. Ghassemi, M., et al. (2020). Disparities in AI: The Need for a New Framework. Nature Medicine, 26(6),⏎1009-1015. doi:10.1038/s41591-020-0877-6.⏎⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Chief Medical Officers (CMOs) play a crucial role in evaluating the efficacy and applicability⏎of AI tools in their respective areas and are often responsible for assessing the clinical⏎benefits of AI solutions, determining whether they align with the organization’s strategic⏎goals, and advocating for their integration into practice.⏎Chief Information Officers (CIOs) and Chief Technology Officers (CTOs) are pivotal in the⏎adoption of AI, as they oversee the integration of new technologies into existing systems.⏎Their expertise in information technology and data management is essential for ensuring⏎that AI tools are compatible with electronic health record (EHR) systems and other clinical⏎workflows.⏎The involvement of governance bodies, such as clinical committees and quality assurance⏎boards, is vital in evaluating the potential risks and benefits associated with AI applications.⏎These groups are responsible for establishing guidelines and protocols that ensure patient⏎safety and uphold clinical standards. Their endorsement can significantly influence the⏎decision-making process regarding the adoption of AI tools.⏎One of the primary challenges is the lack of clear regulatory frameworks and guidelines⏎specifically tailored to AI technologies. This uncertainty can create hesitation among⏎decision makers, causing delays in the approval and implementation of AI solutions.⏎Organizations may also struggle with understanding the legal and ethical implications of⏎using AI, particularly concerning patient privacy and data security.⏎Healthcare organizations often operate with established processes and clinical workflows⏎that may not readily accommodate new technologies. Resistance to change among staff⏎can further complicate the adoption of AI. Healthcare organizations often face limitations in⏎accessing high-quality, diverse datasets, which can hinder the development and⏎effectiveness of AI applications. Addressing this data gap is essential for fostering trust in AI⏎technologies and demonstrating their clinical value.⏎Reimbursement challenges pose a significant barrier to the widespread adoption of AI in⏎clinical care. Current payment models may not adequately recognize or reward the value of⏎AI-driven interventions, making it difficult for healthcare organizations to justify the⏎investment in these technologies.⏎⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎One area where enhanced interoperability can make a substantial impact is in the⏎integration of electronic health record (EHR) systems. Currently, many healthcare⏎organizations utilize different EHR platforms that often operate in silos, making it difficult to⏎share patient data across systems. By establishing standardized data formats and⏎protocols for EHR interoperability, healthcare providers can ensure that patient information⏎is accessible and actionable across various platforms. This accessibility allows AI⏎algorithms to analyze comprehensive datasets that encompass diverse patient⏎populations, leading to more accurate predictions and improved clinical decision-making.⏎EHR interoperability, the standardization of clinical data types is essential for the effective⏎development of AI technologies. By adopting consistent terminologies and coding systems,⏎such as those outlined in the Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) framework,⏎healthcare organizations can facilitate smoother data sharing and integration. This⏎standardization not only simplifies the process of collecting and analyzing data but also⏎enhances the ability of AI tools to interpret clinical information accurately. With⏎standardized data formats, researchers can more easily aggregate data from various⏎studies and clinical trials, allowing for larger sample sizes and more robust findings that⏎can inform the development of AI applications.⏎Benchmarking tools also play a critical role in enhancing interoperability and accelerating⏎AI development. By providing standardized metrics for evaluating the performance of AI⏎algorithms, benchmarking tools enable healthcare organizations to assess the⏎effectiveness of different AI solutions in real-world clinical settings. These tools can⏎facilitate comparisons across various implementations, helping organizations identify best⏎practices and optimize AI applications for improved outcomes.⏎Enhanced interoperability also has the potential to fuel research initiatives by enabling data⏎sharing among academic institutions, industry partners and healthcare providers.⏎Collaborative research efforts that leverage interoperable data can lead to groundbreaking⏎discoveries and advancements in AI applications for clinical care.⏎⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Challenges patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI adoption is personalization⏎of care. Patients seek AI's ability to tailor treatment plans to their unique health needs,⏎including genetic factors and lifestyle choices. 1 AI has the potential to analyze large⏎datasets to provide insights that promote personalized medicine. Caregivers desire AI tools⏎that can assist in making informed decisions about treatment options, facilitating better⏎health management for their loved ones.2 AI can help bridge gaps in care, especially in⏎underserved areas, by providing remote monitoring and telehealth solutions that enhance⏎access to healthcare services.⏎There are significant concerns about the privacy of personal health data and the potential⏎for misuse of information. AI systems require vast amounts of data to function effectively,⏎including sensitive personal health information (PHI) such as medical histories, genetic⏎data, and treatment outcomes. The sheer volume of data collected raises concerns about⏎how securely it is stored and managed. The risk of data breaches is a significant concern.⏎Healthcare organizations are prime targets for cyberattacks, and breaches can lead to⏎unauthorized access to sensitive information, resulting in identity theft, fraud, and other⏎malicious activities.3, 4⏎Patients worry that AI algorithms may perpetuate existing biases in healthcare, leading to⏎inequitable treatment outcomes for certain populations. Biased algorithms can lead to⏎misdiagnosis, inappropriate treatment plans, or inadequate access to care for marginalized⏎populations. For example, AI models used for predicting patient deterioration may not⏎accurately reflect the risk profiles of certain racial groups, leading to delayed interventions.⏎When patients perceive that algorithms do not fairly represent their health needs, their trust⏎in healthcare systems may diminish. This lack of trust can lead to lower engagement with⏎healthcare services and poorer health outcomes overall.5, 6⏎1. Topol, E. J. (2019). "Deep Medicine: How Artificial Intelligence Can Make Healthcare Human Again." Basic⏎Books.⏎2. Chen, J., et al. (2020). "Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: An Essential Guide for Health Leaders." Health⏎Affairs, 39(10), 1865-1871.⏎3. Hodge, J. G., & Gostin, L. O. (2016). "Health Data Privacy and Security: A Review of the Literature." American⏎Journal of Public Health, 106(1), 53-56.⏎4. Shapiro, M. (2020). "Data Privacy and Security in the Age of Artificial Intelligence." Journal of Health Care⏎Compliance, 22(2), 5-12.⏎5. Obermeyer, Z., et al. (2019). "Dissecting Racial Bias in an Algorithm Used to Manage the Health of⏎Populations." Science, 366(6464), 447-453.⏎6. Ponce, N. A., & Tschann, J. M. (2020). "Disparities in Health Care Access and Quality among Racial and⏎Ethnic Minority Populations." Journal of Health Politics, Policy and Law, 45(2), 215-236.⏎⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎One critical area for research to accelerate is the development of AI algorithms that focus⏎on predictive analytics. By harnessing vast datasets from electronic health records and⏎other sources, researchers can create AI systems that identify patients at high risk for⏎developing certain conditions, such as heart disease or diabetes. This proactive approach⏎enables healthcare providers to implement preventive measures and tailored⏎interventions, ultimately enhancing patient care and reducing the burden on healthcare⏎systems.⏎Another essential area for HHS to focus on is the integration of AI with telehealth⏎technologies. The COVID-19 pandemic has underscored the value of telehealth in providing⏎accessible care, especially for patients in remote or underserved regions. Research into AI⏎applications that can enhance telehealth interactions such as virtual health assistants or AI⏎driven triage tools can improve patient engagement and satisfaction.⏎HHS should prioritize research into AI applications that address health disparities and⏎promote health equity. By developing algorithms that account for social determinants of⏎health and other contextual factors, researchers can create AI tools that better serve⏎diverse populations.⏎Research into data privacy and security in the context of AI is another area that HHS should⏎prioritize. AI systems often rely on large datasets, ensuring the protection of sensitive⏎patient information is paramount. Investigating innovative methods for safeguarding data⏎while still allowing for effective AI training and operation can help mitigate privacy⏎concerns.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎One significant area of impact is in medical imaging, where AI tools have been shown to⏎assist radiologists in interpreting images more accurately and efficiently. Studies have⏎reported that AI algorithms can match or even exceed human performance in detecting⏎conditions such as breast cancer in mammograms, lung cancer in chest X-rays, and⏎various abnormalities in MRI scans. For instance, a meta-analysis published in the⏎⏎journal Nature found that AI systems demonstrated a high level of sensitivity and specificity⏎in identifying cancerous lesions, leading to earlier detection and treatment.1,2⏎AI has also made strides in predictive analytics. Research has shown that AI tools can⏎analyze electronic health records (EHRs) to identify patients at risk for adverse events, such⏎as hospital readmissions or severe complications. For example, a study published in the⏎journal JAMA Internal Medicine highlighted an AI model that successfully predicted hospital⏎readmissions for heart failure patients with a high degree of accuracy. This capability allows⏎healthcare providers to implement targeted interventions that can reduce readmission⏎rates and improve overall patient management.3,4⏎AI has been applied to enhance patient engagement and adherence to treatment plans. AI-⏎driven chatbots and virtual health assistants have been developed to provide patients with⏎personalized health information, medication reminders, and support for chronic disease⏎management. Research has indicated that these tools can lead to improved patient⏎adherence to treatment regimens and increased satisfaction with care. A study published⏎in The Journal of Medical Internet Research found that patients who interacted with AI-⏎powered health assistants reported higher levels of engagement in their care compared to⏎those who did not use such tools.5,6⏎AI technologies have also been utilized to optimize operational efficiencies within⏎healthcare organizations. Research has demonstrated that AI can improve scheduling,⏎resource allocation, and patient flow management in hospitals. For instance, an analysis⏎published in Health Affairs revealed that AI algorithms could predict patient demand for⏎services, facilitating better staff planning and reducing wait times for patients. This not only⏎enhances patient satisfaction but can also lead to cost savings for healthcare facilities.7,8⏎Published findings indicate that AI tools have the potential to transform clinical care by⏎enhancing diagnostic capabilities, improving patient outcomes, increasing operational⏎efficiency, and fostering patient engagement, paving the way for a more effective and⏎responsive healthcare system.⏎These citations provide a starting point for exploring the impact of AI tools in clinical care.⏎Medical Imaging:⏎1. Esteva, A., Kuprel, B., Barlow, E., et al. (2019). "Dermatologist-level classification of skin cancer with deep⏎neural networks." Nature, 542(7639), 115-118. doi:10.1038/nature21056.⏎2. Rajpurkar, P., Irvin, J., Zhu, K., et al. (2017). "Deep learning for chest radiograph diagnosis: A⏎retrospective comparison of the CheXNeXt algorithm to practicing radiologists." PLOS Medicine, 15(11),⏎e1002686. doi:10.1371/journal.pmed.1002686.⏎Predictive Analytics:⏎3. Desai, R. J., et al. (2019). "Predicting hospital readmissions for heart failure patients using electronic⏎health record data." JAMA Internal Medicine, 179(9), 1234-1241.⏎doi:10.1001/jamainternmed.2019.1448.⏎⏎4. Churpek, M. M., et al. (2016). "Utilizing machine learning to predict early deterioration in the medical⏎ward." Journal of Hospital Medicine, 11(2), 93-98. doi:10.1002/jhm.2531.⏎Patient Engagement:⏎5. Rojas, M., et al. (2019). "Impact of a digital health intervention on patient engagement and self-⏎management of chronic disease: A randomized controlled trial." The Journal of Medical Internet⏎Research, 21(5), e11835. doi:10.2196/11835.⏎6. Mohr, D. C., et al. (2017). "Behavioral intervention technologies: Evidence review and⏎recommendations for future research." Health Psychology, 36(8), 809-820. doi:10.1037/hea0000498.⏎Operational Efficiencies:⏎7. Jiang, F., et al. (2017). "Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: Anticipating Challenges to Ethics, Privacy,⏎and Bias." Health Affairs, 36(3), 758-763. doi:10.1377/hlthaff.2017.1662.⏎8. Chien, I. C., et al. (2020). "The impact of artificial intelligence on healthcare service efficiency: A⏎systematic review." Health Affairs, 39(10), 1671-1679. doi:10.1377/hlthaff.2020.00301.⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎The implementation of AI technologies in healthcare requires significant upfront⏎investments in software, hardware, and training. Healthcare organizations often face high⏎costs related to acquiring AI tools, integrating them into existing systems, and maintaining⏎these technologies over time. In addition to initial costs, organizations must consider⏎ongoing operational expenses, including data management, system updates, and⏎personnel training to ensure that staff can effectively utilize AI tools.⏎The benefits of using AI in clinical care offer improved Patient Outcomes. AI has the⏎potential to enhance patient outcomes by providing more accurate diagnostics,⏎personalized treatment plans, and predictive analytics that can lead to early interventions.⏎Studies have shown that AI algorithms can outperform human clinicians in certain⏎diagnostic tasks.1⏎AI can streamline clinical workflows, reduce administrative burdens, and minimize errors,⏎ultimately leading to cost savings. By automating routine tasks, AI allows healthcare⏎professionals to focus on more complex clinical decisions, enhancing overall productivity.2⏎AI tools can provide clinicians with evidence-based insights and recommendations,⏎improving the quality of decision-making. This capability can enhance clinical judgment⏎and support more informed treatment choices.3⏎The deployment of AI technologies raises questions about equity and access to care. If AI⏎solutions are not designed with inclusivity in mind, marginalized populations may face⏎disparities in access to advanced healthcare technologies.4⏎The literature provides a view of the costs, benefits and transfers associated with the use of⏎AI in clinical care. While the potential benefits of improved patient outcomes, efficiency⏎⏎and enhanced decision-making are significant, healthcare organizations must also be⏎mindful of the costs involved. Addressing these challenges will be crucial for the successful⏎integration of AI into clinical practice.⏎1. Esteva, A., et al. (2019). "A Guide to Deep Learning in Healthcare." Nature Medicine, 25(1), 24-29.⏎2. Brynjolfsson, E., & McAfee, A. (2014). "The Second Machine Age: Work, Progress, and Prosperity in a⏎Time of Brilliant Technologies." W. W. Norton & Company.⏎3. Chen, J., et al. (2020). "Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: An Essential Guide for Health⏎Leaders." Health Affairs, 39(10), 1865-1871.⏎4. Obermeyer, Z., et al. (2019). "Dissecting Racial Bias in an Algorithm Used to Manage the Health of⏎Populations." Science, 366(6464), 447-453.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0095♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American College of Surgeons, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎On behalf of the over 93,000 members of the American College of Surgeons (ACS), we⏎appreciate the opportunity to submit comments to the Department of Health and Human Services’⏎(HHS) Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI)⏎as Part of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI) published in the Federal Register on⏎December 23, 2025. The ACS is a scientific and educational association of surgeons founded in⏎1913 to improve the quality of care for the surgical patient by setting high standards for surgical⏎education and practice. The ACS is dedicated to setting the highest standards of care in an⏎optimal and ethical practice environment— and recognizes the transformative potential of⏎advanced technologies in achieving these goals.⏎The ACS commends HHS for seeking information from the clinical community and other⏎stakeholders to better understand how it can facilitate the acceleration of adoption and use of AI⏎in clinical care. The ACS has been supportive of HHS’ efforts to advance the digital ecosystem⏎and AI in clinical care, recognizing that there are clear opportunities to leverage the capabilities⏎of AI for better, more efficient, and affordable healthcare delivery. However, there is also⏎significant risk in implementing AI without clear guardrails and standards for responsible and⏎ethical use, patient safety, transparency, and continuous evaluation and monitoring. Today,⏎structural barriers that delay deployment of validated clinical AI tools also still exist within the⏎digital ecosystem where control sits with electronic health record (EHR) vendors. These barriers⏎can be addressed through promotion of an open, standards-based deployment infrastructure that⏎enables safe, rapid integration of clinically-validated AI tools that empower the clinical⏎community. To fully realize the positive impacts of the integration of AI into clinical care, the⏎Agency must work closely with technical and clinical experts, as well as create a strategic⏎direction that is nimble enough to keep pace with the rapid evolution of digital health⏎technologies. There also must always be a focus on the impact to patient care and outcomes; these⏎1⏎⏎monumental advancements in healthcare delivery must be for the betterment of patient care and should not detract⏎from the patient experience.⏎ACS supports HHS’s efforts to responsibly accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. While AI holds considerable⏎promise to improve patient outcomes and care delivery, its implementation must be guided by clear guardrails to⏎ensure patient safety, ethical use, transparency, and continuous evaluation. Structural barriers within the digital⏎health ecosystem, particularly those related to EHR integration, continue to delay deployment of validated AI⏎tools. Promoting an open, standards-based infrastructure would enable safe and efficient integration of clinically⏎validated technologies that support clinical decision-making. To fully realize AI’s potential, HHS must⏎collaborate closely with clinical and technical experts and establish a strategic framework that remains adaptable⏎to rapid technological advancement. Above all, AI policy must remain grounded in its impact on patient care and⏎outcomes, ensuring that these innovations enhance, rather than detract from, the patient experience.⏎Procedural AI versus Cognitive AI: A Critical Distinction⏎As HHS develops regulatory, research, and reimbursement frameworks, the College urges HHS to recognize the⏎fundamental differences between two categories of AI in clinical care: procedural AI and cognitive AI. Procedural⏎AI refers to systems integrated into surgical tools that directly support, guide, or automate elements of operations,⏎often involving robotics or specialized hardware, with the AI component embedded in the device used during the⏎intervention itself. In contrast, cognitive AI consists of software-based systems that analyze data to inform⏎diagnosis and decision-making, providing analytical support but not directly controlling or operating surgical⏎devices during the procedure.⏎From a surgical perspective, this distinction is critical for policy development. When AI is embedded into tools⏎used intraoperatively, surgeons face unique challenges that distinguish procedural AI from other AI applications.⏎When using cognitive AI outside of the operating room (OR), clinicians can pause to consider additional data,⏎consult colleagues, or seek second opinions when uncertain about or in disagreement with AI recommendations.⏎However, surgeons using procedural AI cannot scrub out of surgery to deliberate when they disagree with AI.⏎They must make real-time—often life-saving decisions—while also considering whether to rely on⏎recommendations from AI-enabled devices during active procedures. This creates a fundamentally different risk⏎profile and decision-making environment. This unique surgical perspective underscores why procedural AI⏎requires regulatory frameworks that account for the time-sensitive, high-stakes nature of intraoperative AI use.⏎Because of these fundamental differences, a one-size-fits-all approach to AI regulation is inappropriate. For⏎example, procedural AI requires unique post-market evaluation frameworks that account for device malfunction⏎versus user error, which differs significantly from cognitive AI evaluation methods that typically compare⏎outcomes with and without the tool. Procedural AI also requires regulatory efforts focused on transparent device⏎malfunction reporting, clear recall mechanisms, oversight of post-deployment software updates, and appropriate⏎Food and Drug Administration (FDA) labeling that addresses surgery-specific warnings and limitations.⏎Developing effective procedural AI policy necessitates direct collaboration with surgeons and proceduralists who⏎understand these nuances. It is through this lens that the ACS offers our recommendations and comments below.⏎2⏎⏎Key Considerations for Surgery:⏎The following points represent important topics for the ACS related to regulation; ethics, safety, and transparency;⏎liability; surgeon experts; and reimbursement for AI-enabled tools. We are eager to serve as a resource to HHS as⏎it explores these and other critical areas surrounding AI-enabled tools in surgical practice:⏎• Regulation of Procedure-based AI Tools⏎The rapid advancement of AI in healthcare has made it difficult for federal agencies to create regulatory⏎frameworks around these technologies. Regulatory strategies should account for the differences across AI⏎categories and use cases, specifically between cognitive applications and AI embedded in procedural⏎technologies. This is critical not only for patient safety but also for the collection of reliable real-world⏎data.⏎For example, in cases of procedural AI, effective post-market surveillance requires mechanisms that⏎promote transparent reporting of device malfunctions and adverse events, through confidential, anonymized⏎hospital incident reporting systems without penalizing clinicians who use these technologies appropriately.⏎A confidential reporting process is essential to ensuring patient safety, supporting continuous improvement⏎in device design and performance, enhancing the integrity and accuracy of post-market surveillance data,⏎and building trust in these tools and their use in clinical practice. Clear mechanisms for initiating review,⏎recall, or corrective action for AI-enabled tools are also critically important.⏎Furthermore, FDA labeling for AI/machine learning (ML)-enabled surgical devices warrants careful⏎attention. Required warnings and limitations should clearly describe intended use, known performance⏎constraints, populations in which performance has not been validated, and relevant intraoperative safety⏎considerations. Clear labeling supports informed decision-making by hospitals and surgeons and⏎promotes appropriate clinical use. Maintaining the required warnings and limitations portion of the⏎official FDA-labeling for AI/ML-enabled medical devices is also crucial. Example surgical warnings⏎could include information about the tool misidentifying structures, performance being reduced or untested⏎in certain populations, whether or not the device has been evaluated for robotic surgery, whether the tool⏎ensures achievement of the critical view of safety, and more. Appropriate labeling is critical for vendors,⏎hospitals, and physicians to have discussions about the risks of such tools.⏎Finally, regulatory frameworks must extend beyond the initial instance of the AI-enabled tools to also⏎address ongoing software updates and adaptive algorithmic modifications that may alter device behavior⏎post-deployment.⏎Ultimately, the goal of regulatory bodies should be to determine that the AI-based tools are used⏎responsibly and are trustworthy and safe for patient care. Supporting longitudinal tracking of patient⏎outcomes across sites will also be essential for post-market surveillance and AI performance monitoring,⏎which will require mechanisms that give patients verifiable control over their health data as they move⏎across care settings. To achieve this, public-private partnerships that can create a collaborative⏎environment across the technology industry, clinical community, and policy makers are necessary. There⏎must also be a regulatory framework that can quickly transform to keep pace with rapid advancements in⏎the industry. HHS has an opportunity to show strong leadership in this area and would benefit from⏎3⏎⏎creating a standalone entity tasked with convening and coordinating this collaboration with goals of⏎properly governed, trusted knowledge management.⏎• Ethical, Safe, and Transparent Use of AI:⏎Ensuring ethical, safe, and transparent evaluation and use of AI-enabled tools is foundational to their⏎uptake in surgical care, yet there is a lack of structure around these important criteria.1 The current⏎environment where transparency requirements are not clearly defined places an enormous burden on⏎surgeons who are expected to rely on AI to minimize errors and advance care yet currently bear⏎responsibility for determining when to defer to or to override AI.2 Surgeons need assurance that AI tools⏎are up-to-date, have been rigorously evaluated and validated, and are safe for use within their clinical⏎environment and patient population. Without requirements for transparency around model development,⏎testing, debugging and performance, there is an increased risk of inaccuracy of AI outputs and/or⏎inadvertently perpetuating or exacerbating health disparities. For example, it is important that the system⏎account for a circumstance where an AI-based tool might be used to help a physician diagnose and offer⏎treatment pathways for a patient, but the AI was trained on data taken from a demographic that does not⏎match the patient. Because the AI has never interacted with data that are reflective of the patient, there is⏎significant risk of inappropriate/misdiagnosis and inaccurate treatment recommendations.3 Further, the⏎lack of clear requirements and guidance around transparency and the “black box” nature of the decision-⏎making process of an algorithm makes identifying the potential for these errors extremely difficult for the⏎physician community—especially in instances in surgery where an irreversible, real-time decision must⏎be made intraoperatively. Recently, regulatory bodies have stated that sharing detailed, transparent data⏎around AI algorithms is not useful for clinicians due to the technical nature of the information;4 however,⏎we disagree with this assumption. Physicians and healthcare systems must continue to be exposed to and⏎learn to best utilize these data as the uptake of AI-based tools in healthcare becomes inevitable.⏎We believe that good regulation of these technologies must also consider the level of transparency needed⏎for surgical patients. Transparency to patients includes considerations such as when it is appropriate to⏎disclose when AI is used in their care and how to have conversations with patients. Patients’ perception of⏎AI use in their surgical care varies and can significantly impact their trust in the surgical process.⏎Ensuring patients are comfortable will require effective communication about the contributions of AI, its⏎performance, the privacy and security of their data, and ultimately having the ability to decline the use of⏎AI in their care.3 These examples demonstrate the complex and diverse needs around transparency.⏎Creating a solution that meets the existing and future needs of surgeons and surgical patients will require⏎collaboration and alignment of AI developers, physicians, patients, health systems, policy makers, and⏎more.⏎The ACS advocates for a trustworthy system, facilitated by the federal government or certified⏎accrediting bodies (or a collaboration of the two), that can ensure AI tools intended for use in⏎clinical care meet technical and clinical care standards of ethical and responsible use, safety, and⏎1 Angus DC, Khera R, Lieu T, et al. AI, Health, and Health Care Today and Tomorrow: The JAMA Summit Report on⏎Artificial Intelligence. JAMA. 2025;334(18):1650-1664.⏎2 Patil SV, Myers CG, Lu-Myers Y. Calibrating AI Reliance – A Physician’s Superhuman Dilemma. JAMA Health Forum.⏎2025;6(3):e250106.⏎3 Adegbesan A, Akingbola A, Aremu O, Adewole O, Amamdikwa JC, Shagaya U. From Scalpels to Algorithms: The Risk of⏎Dependence on Artificial Intelligence in Surgery. J Med Surg Public Health. 2024; 3:100140.⏎4 90 FR 60970⏎4⏎⏎transparency, immediately and over long-term implementation. An example of a federal oversight⏎infrastructure for health information technology (IT) is the Assistant Secretary for Technology⏎Policy (ASTP)/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Health IT Certification Program. To be effective, a transparent certification system should include the⏎following elements, which present areas for opportunity for investment and further development:⏎• Technical performance standards for AI applied across its lifecycle – For AI used in surgical⏎care, such as augmented reality and robotic-assisted surgery, the standards should be specific to⏎the type of tool and could include metrics such as failure to detect proper anatomy, time⏎measurements, technical reliability, accurate registration of images, etc.5⏎• Clinical metrics tied to the use of AI – In surgical care this might include clinical outcomes,⏎such as intraoperative complication rates, patient experience, and patient-reported outcomes.⏎However, this could also include metrics around workflow optimization or detection of⏎intraoperative errors, such as frequency of operating in an anatomical “danger zone.”6⏎• Guidelines for the proper human touchpoints – This enables collaboration between the⏎surgeon and the AI tool across the full episode of care, preserving surgeon autonomy and⏎ensuring surgical judgement is exercised not only at the point of intervention but throughout the⏎patient’s surgical journey, from preoperative planning through postoperative recovery. It is⏎crucial to identify where the surgeon-in-the-loop should be properly applied, which can vary⏎based on use case.⏎• Transparent information for end users – Requirements must outline what data must be made⏎available to end users to ensure they are informed about the tools and can effectively⏎communicate with patients. An example of this could look like AI Model Cards,7 where detailed,⏎transparent information about an AI model’s purpose, design, capabilities/limitations, etc., is⏎shared with end users.⏎• The Impact of Regulation on Liability⏎Liability related to the use of AI in medicine remains unsettled and continues to evolve. Under existing⏎malpractice law, surgeons are evaluated under the medical standard of care and will typically not be⏎found liable if injury occurs following the standard of care. Surgeons are therefore incentivized to follow⏎this standard no matter what an AI tool might suggest.8 As this standard develops over time through the⏎interaction of federal oversight, development of clinical or practice guidelines, payment policy, and⏎patterns of clinical adoption, how HHS approaches AI regulation and program guidance will likely⏎influence how courts assess surgeon conduct in future professional liability actions.⏎Regulation should also play a critical role in how causation and responsibility are allocated between⏎providers and developers. Many AI systems, particularly those using opaque or “black box”⏎methodologies, do not make their reasoning transparent to the end user. Dataset bias, performance drift,⏎5 Kos TM, Colombo E, Bartels LW, Robe PA, van Doormaal TPC. Evaluation Metrics for Augmented Reality in⏎Neurosurgical Preoperative Planning, Surgical Navigation, and Surgical Treatment Guidance: A Systematic Review. Oper⏎Neurosurg. 2024;26(5):491-501.⏎6 Mascagni P, Alapatt D, Urade T, et al. A Computer Vision Platform to Automatically Locate Critical Events in Surgical⏎Videos: Documenting Safety in Laparoscopic Cholecystectomy. Ann Surg. 2021;274(1):e93-e95.⏎7 Coalition for Health AI. Applied Model Card. Accessed February 17, 2026. https://www.chai.org/workgroup/applied-⏎model/model-card-and-registry⏎8 Price WN, Gerke S, Cohen IG. Liability for Use of Artificial Intelligence in Medicine. In: Solaiman B, Cohen IG, eds.⏎Research Handbook on Health, AI and the Law. Edward Elgar Publishing Ltd; 2022.⏎5⏎⏎and model degradation are often not visible to end users, and surgeons generally are not in the best⏎position to independently validate AI outputs in real time. Without clear guardrails or liability protections⏎for the provider, these characteristics risk misalignment in liability allocation, with responsibility falling⏎on clinicians even when adverse outcomes stem from system design, data limitations, or integration⏎decisions. Thoughtful regulatory oversight can mitigate these risks and more appropriately align them⏎with existing frameworks such as those for medical device product liability.⏎In addition, the integration of AI into surgical care raises important questions regarding informed consent.⏎As AI-enabled tools increasingly influence operative planning or execution, patients may reasonably⏎expect to understand when such technologies are being used and the role they play in their care. However,⏎surgeons cannot be expected to disclose technical details regarding algorithm development, training data,⏎or system architecture that are beyond their knowledge or control. Federal guidance clarifying appropriate⏎disclosure standards for AI-enabled surgical tools would help align informed consent obligations with⏎realistic clinical practice, ensuring patients receive meaningful information without imposing⏎impracticable burdens on individual clinicians.⏎Distinct Considerations for Procedural AI Liability⏎Unlike cognitive or clinical decision support (CDS) tools, which provide recommendations to inform⏎professional judgment, procedural AI systems directly influence or participate in the execution of surgical⏎care. When a procedural AI system contributes to tissue injury or affects surgical mechanics, causation⏎may appear directly attributable to the surgeons’ actions, specifically, whether intervention was feasible⏎and whether a reasonably prudent surgeon could have acted differently. When a procedural AI system⏎operates within its labeled indications, executes functions as designed, and produces outputs or actions⏎based on embedded algorithms that the surgeon cannot meaningfully test or recalibrate in real time,⏎responsibility should not default to the surgeon solely because an adverse event occurred in the OR.⏎Concerns around liability are further heightened where procedural AI systems are classified or treated⏎similarly to advisory CDS tools and thereby avoid robust premarket evaluation or ongoing oversight by⏎the FDA. In other words, these non-device tools may be framed in litigation as clinical aids rather than as⏎independently accountable medical devices. In such circumstances, developers and institutions may face⏎limited accountability, while liability risk shifts downstream to surgeons who did not design, train, or⏎update the system and may have limited ability to evaluate its internal functioning. Even in cases where⏎surgeons have algorithmic transparency, this access alone should not automatically invoke liability.⏎The ACS urges HHS to recognize procedural AI as distinct from advisory CDS tools and to align⏎oversight and liability principles more closely with established medical device regulatory and products⏎liability frameworks.⏎First, procedural AI warrants stronger upstream oversight. Because these systems can directly cause⏎physical harm and may involve data- or design-driven error modes that surgeons cannot always override⏎in real time, federal review and oversight frameworks should reflect their heightened risk profile. Clear⏎labeling, defined user responsibilities, and meaningful post-market performance monitoring are essential⏎to protect patients and ensure appropriate accountability.⏎Second, liability should track meaningful control. Surgeons should not bear responsibility for harms⏎arising from procedural AI behaviors that were not reasonably foreseeable or preventable through⏎6⏎⏎ordinary surgical vigilance. Responsibility should align with control, knowledge, and the practical ability⏎to intervene, not merely with the surgeon’s presence in the OR.⏎Third, HHS should consider mechanisms to protect compliant use. Surgeons who use AI systems in⏎accordance with FDA-labeled indications, exercise independent clinical judgment, and document⏎reasonable reliance on, or departure from, AI outputs should not face disproportionate liability exposure.⏎While use of an FDA-authorized product consistent with its labeled indications does not provide absolute⏎immunity from malpractice claims, it may serve as evidence that the provider adhered to established,⏎evidence-based standards of care. Federal guidance supporting compliant use would promote patient⏎safety while encouraging responsible innovation. Clear, coordinated federal policy in this area will help⏎ensure that liability principles support, rather than hinder, the safe and effective integration of AI into⏎surgical practice.⏎• Leveraging Surgeon Experts⏎The ACS requests that HHS build pathways for collaboration with surgeons and specialty societies;⏎when AI is developed for use in surgical practice, engagement of the surgical community must be⏎the rule, not the exception. To ensure the safety, efficacy, and appropriateness of surgical AI tools,⏎surgeons must be involved from the beginning of the research and development lifecycle to⏎implementation, and through evaluation of ongoing performance of AI. Only surgeons can determine if⏎the AI tool achieves the intended clinical outcome of a procedure—especially when considering⏎procedural AI. Therefore, surgical specialty societies should define the goals of care for a surgical AI tool⏎and ultimately determine if those goals are being met. It is also important to note that in today’s⏎environment, the technology, alone, is no longer the main barrier to realizing the potential of surgical AI⏎tools. Instead, current hurdles include issues with implementation, change management, administrative⏎buy-in, and more, making the perspective of practicing clinicians, such as surgeons, essential.⏎Developing and maintaining partnerships with the surgical community will help to ensure AI-based tools⏎and all supporting data are high quality, safe, and appropriate for clinical use. The ACS has extensive⏎experience leveraging clinical data for improved healthcare through its clinical data registries and can⏎offer validated, high-quality data for research and development activities. With its distinguished⏎membership, broad surgical expertise, and network of specialized committees, the ACS is ideally⏎positioned to partner with HHS in advancing AI-enabled tools for surgical care. The ACS looks forward⏎to future opportunities to partner with the HHS on this important work.⏎• Reimbursement⏎A primary challenge to appropriate reimbursement is the absence of clear coding pathways for AI-enabled⏎tools used by physicians, as many applications do not align with existing Current Procedural Terminology⏎(CPT) or Health Care Common Procedure Coding System (HCPCS) codes. The ACS urges HHS to⏎support the development of coding pathways that recognize physician-directed use of AI, with⏎reimbursement reflecting surgeon oversight as necessary rather than payment for software use alone,⏎when appropriate.⏎Revaluation of existing surgical services presents an additional concern, as AI adoption may be assumed⏎to reduce physician work, resulting in inappropriate downward pressure on reimbursement. In practice, AI⏎often shifts or increases physician work, as surgeons must oversee the integration, validation, and safe use⏎7⏎⏎of AI-enabled procedural technologies throughout the surgical episode of care. The ACS therefore⏎recommends that HHS require robust empirical evidence and meaningful clinician input before using AI⏎adoption as justification for revaluing surgical services and consider new models for reimbursing⏎physicians for the increased work associated with the use of new technology. Depending on decisions⏎made on the issue of professional liability, this could also warrant methodological changes to valuing⏎professional liability insurance relative value units (PLI RVUs).⏎To address delays inherent in legacy payment systems, the ACS further encourages HHS to consider⏎time-limited, evidence-generating coverage pathways for AI tools with strong preliminary evidence of⏎clinical benefit, allowing conditional reimbursement while requiring ongoing data collection prior to⏎permanent coverage and payment decisions.⏎The ACS appreciates the opportunity to comment on these important issues, and we look forward to⏎continuing dialogue with HHS on issues impacting AI/ML in surgical care. Please contact Vinita⏎Mujumdar, Chief of Regulatory Affairs, at vmujumdar@facs.org, or Jill Sage, Chief of Quality Affairs, at⏎jsage@facs.org, with any questions.⏎Sincerely,⏎Patricia L. Turner, MD, MBA, FACS⏎Executive Director and CEO⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0096♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Martinez♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My first reaction when I found this notice was honestly just confusion. The Federal Register posting is so dense with acronyms and cross-references that I had to read certain sentences three or four times and still wasn't sure what was being asked. I work as a home health aide in Union County, New Jersey. I don't have a law degree. I'm not sure why these solicitations can't be written in plain English if HHS genuinely wants input from people who are actually in patients' homes every day.⏎⏎That said I want to say something about what I've seen with remote monitoring, because it matters and I don't think people making these decisions always hear from people like me.⏎⏎One of my patients, an 83-year-old woman I've been working with for going on two years now, was flagged by her remote monitoring wristband as low-risk in the days before she was hospitalized for a cardiac event in November. The device didn't know that her baseline vitals run low compared to most people her age. It also had no way of knowing that she tends to minimize what she's feeling when she thinks something official is watching or recording her. I knew those things about her because I have sat with her three mornings a week and learned how she actually is. The algorithm did not have that. She ended up in the hospital anyway and her daughter was beside herself.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in care. I genuinely don't know enough about how these systems are built to have a strong opinion on the technical side. But what I keep thinking about is who gets to flag a concern when the algorithm is wrong. In my situation I had tried to note in the care log that she seemed off to me, that something felt different that week, but there's no formal way for a home health aide's observation to actually register as a data point that anyone looks at. The monitoring system output gets reviewed. My notes don't carry the same weight, and I'm not sure they're even read by anyone before a decision gets made about her risk level.⏎⏎So if HHS is asking what would actually help patients in home-based care settings, what I would want is some requirement that AI tools used for patient monitoring have a way to incorporate input from the people providing hands-on care, not just the sensor data. I don't know what the regulatory mechanism for that would be and I realize that's probably not a simple thing to implement. But right now it feels like the systems are set up to trust the device over the person in the room.⏎⏎Also, and I know this is a separate thing, but I really do think the comment process itself is a problem. I looked at this RFI three times before I understood what was even being solicited. There was a community health worker in my training cohort back in 2019 who said she had tried to submit a comment on a different HHS rule and gave up because she couldn't parse what question they were actually asking. That's not an unusual experience. If the goal is broader input from frontline workers, the notices need to be written differently, maybe with a plain-language summary at the top that explains in two or three sentences what HHS is trying to decide and what kinds of responses would actually be useful.⏎⏎I don't know if this comment is even formatted the way you want it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0097♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Ferz.AI, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ferz.AI♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Response to Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎A Framework for Evaluating AI Governance Claims:⏎Distinguishing Deterministic Governance from Marketing⏎Submitted by:⏎FERZ AI⏎Deterministic AI Governance Infrastructure⏎Neil Nair, Chief Business Officer⏎neil@ferz.ai⏎December 2025⏎Page 1 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Executive Summary⏎FERZ AI welcomes the opportunity to respond to HHS's Request for Information on⏎accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. This response serves two purposes: first, to⏎provide concrete recommendations addressing HHS's specific questions; second,⏎and perhaps more importantly, to equip federal reviewers with a technical⏎framework for evaluating AI governance claims they will inevitably receive⏎from vendors.⏎The AI governance market includes many solutions marketed as "guardrails,"⏎"responsible AI," "ethical AI," and "trustworthy AI." These terms lack precise⏎technical definitions, making it difficult for federal reviewers to evaluate competing⏎claims. Acquisition officers benefit from distinguishing between:⏎• Marketing claims ("our AI is safe and responsible")⏎• Statistical governance (probabilistic approaches providing confidence⏎intervals)⏎• Deterministic governance (fail-closed enforcement with reproducible,⏎auditable decision artifacts)⏎This document provides: (1) a taxonomy of AI governance approaches with their⏎respective strengths and limitations; (2) evaluation criteria federal reviewers can use⏎to assess vendor claims; (3) specific questions to ask vendors claiming governance⏎capabilities; and (4) FERZ's recommendations for regulatory frameworks that would⏎accelerate trustworthy AI adoption.⏎Key Insight: The fundamental barrier to AI adoption in clinical care is not AI⏎capability—it is the inability to prove that an AI system's governance was functioning⏎correctly at the time of any specific decision. Without such proof, liability cannot be⏎allocated, regulatory compliance cannot be demonstrated, and patient trust cannot⏎be justified. This is a governance problem, not an AI problem.⏎Page 2 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Part I: Understanding AI Governance—A Taxonomy for⏎Federal Reviewers⏎Before addressing HHS's specific questions, we provide a framework for⏎understanding the AI governance landscape. Federal reviewers evaluating vendor⏎claims need this foundation to distinguish substance from marketing.⏎1.1 The Three Layers of AI Systems⏎Understanding where governance operates is essential to evaluating vendor claims.⏎AI systems can be conceptualized in three layers:⏎Layer Description Governance Implications⏎Model Layer The AI model itself (e.g., GPT- Model-level governance (RLHF,⏎4, Claude, Gemini). Includes Constitutional AI) attempts to⏎training data, architecture, make the model "safer" through⏎learned parameters. training. Inherently⏎probabilistic—the model tends⏎to behave well but cannot⏎guarantee it.⏎Application Layer The software that wraps the Application-level governance⏎model—user interface, (prompt engineering, output⏎integration points, business filtering) adds checks around⏎logic. Where prompts are the model. Still largely⏎constructed and outputs probabilistic—filters can miss⏎processed. edge cases.⏎Deployment Layer The operational environment Deployment-level governance⏎where AI actions occur—clinical (FERZ's focus) enforces⏎workflows, EHR integration, constraints at the execution⏎decision execution. The "last boundary. Can be⏎mile" before real-world impact. deterministic—nothing executes⏎unless governance validates it.⏎Critical distinction: Most AI "safety" approaches operate at the Model Layer or⏎Application Layer, where governance is inherently probabilistic. Deterministic⏎governance operates at the Deployment Layer, where execution can be gated by⏎mathematically verifiable constraints.⏎1.2 The Fundamental Problem: Probabilistic vs. Deterministic⏎Governance⏎The core technical distinction federal reviewers must understand is between⏎probabilistic governance (statistical confidence that a system usually behaves⏎correctly) and deterministic governance (mathematical proof that a specific⏎decision complied with specific rules).⏎Probabilistic Governance: "Likely Compliant"⏎Probabilistic approaches—including RLHF (Reinforcement Learning from Human⏎Feedback), Constitutional AI, content filtering, and most "guardrails"—operate on⏎statistical principles. They provide confidence that a system usually behaves⏎correctly, expressed as accuracy percentages or confidence intervals.⏎Page 3 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎• How it works: Train the model to prefer "good" outputs; use classifiers to flag⏎"bad" outputs; filter problematic content before delivery.⏎• What vendors claim: "95% accuracy," "reduced harmful outputs by 80%,"⏎"enterprise-grade safety."⏎• Risk exposure: At clinical scale, even small residual failure rates produce⏎frequent governance escapes. The question is whether escapes are (a)⏎prevented pre-execution, and (b) provable/auditable when they occur.⏎Probabilistic approaches cannot guarantee either.⏎• Key limitation: Cannot provide proof that governance was functioning⏎correctly for any specific decision. After an adverse event, there is no way to⏎demonstrate that the system was operating within its governance boundaries.⏎Deterministic Governance: "Provably Compliant"⏎Deterministic governance provides fail-closed enforcement with reproducible,⏎independently verifiable audit artifacts at the governance layer. This is distinct from⏎claiming the underlying AI model is deterministic—it is not. Determinism is achieved⏎at the execution boundary through: (1) pre-execution validation against explicit⏎rules; (2) recorded snapshots of all inputs, policies, and outputs; and (3) reproducible⏎evaluation that can be independently verified.⏎• How it works: Every AI output passes through a validation layer that checks⏎explicit rules. If validation fails, the output is blocked ("deny-by-default" or "fail-⏎closed"). Every decision generates a cryptographic proof bundle capturing⏎inputs, policy state, and verdict.⏎• What this enables: Bit-for-bit reproducible governance decisions,⏎cryptographic audit trails, and bounded residual errors quantifiable by rule⏎coverage and implementation factors.⏎• What this means: In a healthcare deployment, every AI decision is validated⏎against explicit rules before execution. Failed validations are blocked. Every⏎decision can be independently verified and replayed.⏎• Key advantage: After an adverse event, governance compliance can be⏎independently verified by third parties. Liability can be allocated based on⏎provable governance state at decision time.⏎Page 4 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎1.3 Side-by-Side Comparison: Critical Differences⏎Characteristic Probabilistic Governance Deterministic Governance⏎Compliance assurance Statistical confidence (e.g., Verifiable conformance⏎"95% accurate") artifacts with bounded⏎residual risk⏎Failure mode "Fail-open": Ungoverned "Fail-closed": Nothing⏎outputs may proceed executes without validation⏎After adverse event Cannot prove governance Cryptographic proof of⏎was working governance state⏎Decision reproducibility Cannot reproduce specific Bit-for-bit reproducible⏎decisions ("replay test")⏎Audit capability Logs of outputs and Cryptographic proof chain⏎classifications (Proof-Carrying Decisions)⏎Liability allocation Unclear—governance state Clear—governance state⏎unknowable provable⏎FDA 21 CFR Part 11 Difficult to satisfy—records Designed for compliance—⏎not verifiable immutable audit trails⏎Regulatory inspection Requires trust in vendor's Third-party verification⏎claims possible⏎Page 5 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎1.4 Why This Distinction Matters for Healthcare⏎Healthcare is a precision-critical domain where statistical assurances are insufficient.⏎Consider:⏎Scenario: A clinical AI system assists with medication recommendations. The⏎vendor claims high accuracy in avoiding dangerous drug interactions. A large⏎hospital processes thousands of AI-assisted medication decisions monthly.⏎• With probabilistic governance: Some fraction of decisions may have⏎ungoverned drug interaction risks. The fraction may be small, but at scale the⏎absolute number becomes significant. Critically, after an adverse event, there⏎is no mechanism to prove whether governance was functioning correctly for⏎that specific decision.⏎• With deterministic governance: Every medication recommendation is⏎validated against drug interaction rules before reaching the clinician. Failed⏎validations are blocked and escalated. After any decision, cryptographic proof⏎demonstrates exactly which rules were applied and whether they passed.⏎The difference is not merely technical—it fundamentally changes liability allocation,⏎regulatory compliance posture, and patient safety assurance.⏎1.5 Emerging Architectures: RAG, Agentic AI, and Governance Topology⏎Two architectural patterns are accelerating AI deployment in healthcare: Retrieval-⏎Augmented Generation (RAG), which grounds AI outputs in retrieved documents,⏎and agentic AI, which enables AI systems to take actions autonomously. Federal⏎reviewers will encounter many proposals featuring these patterns. Understanding⏎their governance implications is essential.⏎Why RAG and Agentic Patterns Increase Governance Requirements⏎These patterns do not reduce governance complexity—they increase it:⏎• RAG systems introduce dynamic, unpredictable inputs. The AI's outputs⏎depend on retrieved context that changes with each query—patient records,⏎clinical literature, formulary data. Model-level governance cannot anticipate⏎what documents will be retrieved. Governance must validate outputs⏎regardless of retrieval context.⏎• Agentic systems execute actions with real-world consequences: API calls,⏎database writes, order placements, message transmissions. Post-hoc⏎monitoring cannot reverse an incorrectly administered medication, a⏎misconfigured treatment plan, or an erroneous prior authorization denial.⏎Governance must operate pre-execution, not post-detection.⏎Reviewer Note: Federal reviewers should be skeptical of claims that RAG or agentic⏎architectures are inherently "safer" or "more accurate." These architectures shift⏎where errors occur, not whether they occur. The fundamental question remains: can⏎you prove governance was functioning for any specific decision?⏎Governance Topology in Multi-Agent Systems⏎Page 6 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎As agentic AI evolves toward multi-agent architectures—where specialized agents⏎collaborate on complex tasks—a critical architectural question emerges: where does⏎governance operate?⏎Per-Agent Governance distributes guardrails across individual agents. This creates⏎structural vulnerabilities:⏎• Agent-to-agent communication may bypass governance entirely⏎• Emergent behaviors from agent interactions remain ungoverned⏎• No unified audit trail across the agent ensemble⏎• Inconsistent policy enforcement across agents with different guardrail⏎implementations⏎• "Governance arbitrage"—sophisticated actors may route sensitive operations⏎through the least-governed agent⏎Centralized Governance at the Execution Boundary validates all actions—⏎regardless of which agent originates them—before they execute. This architecture⏎ensures consistent policy enforcement, unified auditability, and captures emergent⏎behaviors at the point of real-world impact. The governance layer is model-agnostic⏎and agent-agnostic; it governs actions, not agents.⏎Example: Consider a clinical agentic system with a retrieval agent (pulls patient⏎records, literature), a reasoning agent (synthesizes recommendations), and an⏎action agent (places orders, sends messages). With per-agent governance, each⏎agent has separate guardrails optimized for its function—but no single point⏎validates that the chain of agent interactions produced a compliant outcome. With⏎centralized execution-boundary governance, every action passes through⏎deterministic validation regardless of which agent initiated it, and the audit trail⏎captures the full decision chain.⏎Questions for Multi-Agent AI Proposals⏎Federal reviewers evaluating multi-agent AI proposals should ask:⏎1. Does governance operate per-agent or at the execution boundary?⏎2. If per-agent, how are inter-agent communications governed?⏎3. How are emergent behaviors from agent interactions detected and controlled?⏎4. Is there a unified audit trail across all agents, or separate logs per agent?⏎5. Can you replay a decision that involved multiple agents and obtain identical⏎results?⏎Page 7 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Part II: Evaluating AI Governance Claims—A Guide for⏎Federal Reviewers⏎Federal acquisition officers will receive numerous proposals claiming AI governance⏎capabilities. This section provides practical tools to evaluate those claims.⏎2.1 Red Flags: Marketing Language vs. Technical Substance⏎The following table identifies common marketing claims and provides questions that⏎distinguish substance from noise:⏎Vendor Claim What It Usually Means Questions to Ask⏎"AI Guardrails" Content filtering or output What happens when⏎classifiers. Probabilistic. guardrails fail? Can you⏎May miss edge cases. prove they were active for a⏎specific decision?⏎"Responsible AI" Training-time interventions How do you verify⏎(RLHF, Constitutional AI). responsible behavior for a⏎Model tends to behave specific decision? What are⏎responsibly. your error bounds?⏎"Enterprise-Grade Safety" Marketing term. No Define "enterprise-grade."⏎technical definition. May What specific guarantees⏎mean access controls or do you provide? How are⏎logging. they verified?⏎"Explainable AI" Post-hoc rationales. May Can explanations be⏎explain why AI probably independently verified? Can⏎made a decision, but decisions be replayed to⏎cannot prove compliance. confirm the explanation?⏎"AI Monitoring" After-the-fact analysis. How do you prevent⏎Detects problems after they violations rather than detect⏎occur, not before. them? What is your pre-⏎execution validation?⏎"99% Accurate" 1% of decisions are What happens to the 1%?⏎ungoverned. At scale, this Can you identify which⏎means thousands of specific decisions are in⏎potential failures. that 1%?⏎2.2 The Four Tests: Evaluating Governance Capabilities⏎FERZ has published the Four Tests Standard (4TS) as a vendor-neutral framework⏎for evaluating AI governance claims. Federal reviewers can use these tests to⏎assess any governance solution:⏎Test Question It Answers How to Verify⏎Stop Test Can the system be halted before Ask: "What happens if⏎side-effects occur? Does governance validation fails?"⏎governance validation happen The answer must be "the action⏎before execution, not after? is blocked," not "we log it for⏎review."⏎Page 8 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Test Question It Answers How to Verify⏎Ownership Is there clear authority signing Ask: "Show me the signature⏎Test governance policy? Can you chain for governance policy."⏎identify who authorized the AI's There must be cryptographic⏎operational boundaries? proof of who authorized what.⏎Replay Test Can any historical decision be Ask: "Show me a decision from⏎reproduced and verified? Can a last week and reproduce it."⏎regulator independently confirm The replayed decision must⏎what happened? match the original exactly.⏎Escalation Are human oversight routes Ask: "What happens when you⏎Test defined? When governance denies deny an action?" There must be⏎an action, what happens? Is there defined escalation paths with⏎mandatory custody transfer? human decision-makers.⏎Key insight: A governance system that cannot pass all four tests provides statistical⏎confidence at best. It cannot provide the proof-level assurance required for regulated⏎healthcare environments.⏎Page 9 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎2.3 Technical Due Diligence Questions⏎When evaluating AI governance solutions for healthcare, federal reviewers should⏎ask these specific technical questions:⏎Architecture Questions⏎6. At what layer does your governance operate? (Model, Application, or⏎Deployment)⏎7. Is governance validation performed before or after execution?⏎8. What is the default behavior when governance cannot validate an action?⏎(Fail-open vs. fail-closed)⏎9. Can your system operate with any AI model, or is it tied to a specific model⏎provider?⏎Compliance Verification Questions⏎1. How do you prove that governance was functioning correctly for a specific⏎historical decision?⏎2. Can a third party independently verify your compliance claims without trusting⏎your attestations?⏎3. What cryptographic mechanisms ensure audit trail integrity?⏎4. How do you satisfy FDA 21 CFR Part 11 requirements for electronic records⏎and signatures?⏎Error Bound Questions⏎1. What are your quantified error bounds? (Not accuracy percentages—actual⏎mathematical error bounds)⏎2. How did you derive those bounds? (Empirical testing vs. mathematical proof)⏎3. What assumptions must hold for those bounds to be valid?⏎4. How do you detect and handle cases that fall outside your bounded domain?⏎Operational Questions⏎1. What is your governance validation latency? (Must not significantly impact⏎clinical workflows)⏎2. How do you handle governance policy updates without disrupting operations?⏎3. What happens if your governance system experiences an outage?⏎4. Can you demonstrate air-gapped deployment for sensitive environments?⏎Page 10 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Part III: Responses to HHS Specific Questions⏎Having established the technical framework, we now address HHS's specific⏎questions from the perspective of deterministic governance.⏎Question 1: Barriers to Private Sector Innovation⏎"What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care?"⏎The primary barriers are governance-related, not capability-related:⏎1. The Governance Gap: Healthcare requires certainty; current AI governance⏎provides only statistical confidence. Organizations cannot deploy AI when⏎they cannot prove compliance.⏎2. Liability Uncertainty: Without clear governance boundaries, liability cannot⏎be allocated. Legal departments block deployments when governance state is⏎unknowable.⏎3. Audit Trail Inadequacy: FDA 21 CFR Part 11 requires verifiable electronic⏎records. Current AI systems produce outputs, not evidence. There is no⏎cryptographic proof that governance was functioning at decision time.⏎4. Regulatory Uncertainty: Non-medical device AI falls into gray zones.⏎Organizations cannot invest when regulatory requirements are unclear.⏎FERZ Recommendation: HHS should establish clear governance requirements that⏎distinguish between statistical and deterministic assurance levels, creating a⏎pathway for organizations to deploy AI with appropriate governance for their risk⏎tolerance.⏎Question 2: Regulatory and Policy Changes⏎"What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize?"⏎Regulatory Recommendations with CFR Citations⏎Note: The following citations are illustrative, not exhaustive. We recommend HHS⏎issue guidance clarifying application to AI governance rather than amending the⏎underlying regulations.⏎1. 21 CFR Part 11 (Electronic Records and Signatures): Issue guidance⏎recognizing cryptographic proof bundles (e.g., Proof-Carrying Decisions) as⏎satisfying §11.10 requirements for audit trails and §11.50 requirements for⏎signature manifestations. Current language contemplates traditional electronic⏎signatures; guidance should clarify that cryptographic attestation of policy⏎state satisfies the integrity and attribution requirements.⏎2. 45 CFR Part 170 (Health IT Certification): Consider adding AI governance⏎attestation requirements to §170.315 certification criteria. Systems claiming⏎clinical decision support functionality should demonstrate reproducible⏎governance validation as a certification condition.⏎3. 42 CFR Part 493 (CLIA): Issue guidance on how AI-assisted laboratory⏎analytics should satisfy §493.1251 procedure manual requirements and⏎§493.1283 retention requirements. Deterministic governance artifacts could⏎satisfy the "complete record" requirements.⏎4. 45 CFR Part 164 (HIPAA Security): Clarify how AI governance audit trails⏎interact with §164.312(b) audit control requirements and §164.312(c) integrity⏎Page 11 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎requirements. Cryptographic proof chains may exceed current compliance⏎expectations; guidance should recognize this.⏎Payment Policy Recommendations⏎• Governance Attestation as Reimbursement Condition: For AI-assisted⏎clinical workflows in high-risk categories, require governance attestation⏎artifacts as a condition of reimbursement. This creates market incentive for⏎governance adoption.⏎• Incentives for Burden-Reduction Tools: Provide enhanced reimbursement⏎for AI-assisted documentation, coding, and prior authorization when⏎governance artifacts enable post-payment integrity verification. This aligns⏎payer interests with governance adoption.⏎• Pilot Reimbursement Models: CMS should pilot reimbursement models for⏎AI tooling where governance artifacts support audit. This generates evidence⏎on governance value while managing risk.⏎Question 3: Novel Legal Issues⏎"What novel legal and implementation issues exist for non-medical devices?"⏎1. The Black Box Problem: Current AI cannot prove governance state at⏎decision time. This creates fundamental liability allocation challenges. Who is⏎responsible when governance cannot be verified?⏎2. Temporal Governance: AI systems may be updated between deployment⏎and adverse event. Without governance infrastructure capturing policy state at⏎decision time, retrospective analysis is impossible.⏎3. Multi-Party Accountability: Clinical AI involves model developers,⏎governance providers, deploying institutions, and clinicians. Clear boundary⏎definitions are needed.⏎4. Privacy-Preserving Audit: HIPAA requires protecting PHI while maintaining⏎auditable records. Deterministic governance with cryptographic proofs can⏎address this, but regulatory recognition is needed.⏎HHS's Role: Define minimum governance architecture standards for clinical AI;⏎create accountability frameworks for multi-party deployments; publish reference⏎architectures demonstrating acceptable approaches.⏎Mapping to FDA SaMD and CDS Realities⏎Although this RFI addresses AI broadly, reviewers will implicitly map responses to⏎existing FDA frameworks. Deterministic governance artifacts support auditability and⏎change control regardless of whether a tool is regulated as a device. For clinical⏎decision support (CDS) that falls outside device regulation under 21st Century Cures⏎Act criteria, governance artifacts nonetheless provide the documentation and⏎traceability that healthcare organizations require for institutional risk management.⏎For tools that do qualify as Software as a Medical Device (SaMD), these same⏎artifacts directly support FDA expectations for traceability, change control, and post-⏎market surveillance. The governance layer thus provides compliance infrastructure⏎that scales across the regulatory spectrum—from unregulated CDS to Class II/III⏎SaMD—without requiring different architectures for different regulatory categories.⏎Page 12 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Question 4: AI Evaluation Methods⏎"What are the most promising AI evaluation methods for clinical care?"⏎The most promising evaluation methods focus on governance verification rather than⏎AI capability assessment:⏎Pre-Deployment Evaluation⏎• Governance Coverage Analysis: What percentage of AI outputs pass⏎through deterministic validation? Target: 100% for high-risk decisions.⏎• Rule Completeness Testing: Do governance rules cover all compliance⏎requirements (HIPAA, FDA, state regulations)?⏎• Adversarial Testing: Systematic testing of governance behavior under⏎unusual or adversarial inputs.⏎Post-Deployment Evaluation⏎• Replay Verification: Can any historical decision be replayed and obtain⏎identical results? This enables post-hoc audit without trusting logs.⏎• Escalation Analysis: How often do decisions escalate to human review? Are⏎thresholds appropriately calibrated?⏎• Governance Drift Detection: Monitor for changes in AI behavior that might⏎indicate model drift affecting governance effectiveness.⏎HHS Opportunity: Support development of governance evaluation tools and⏎conformance testing infrastructure. The 4TS conformance bundle provides a model.⏎Questions 5-10: Summary Responses⏎Question 5 (Private Sector Activities): HHS should recognize deterministic⏎governance in certification criteria; fund pilot programs; create testing infrastructure;⏎and establish public-private partnerships for reference architectures.⏎Question 6 (AI Tool Performance): AI succeeds in domains with clear boundaries⏎and low compliance complexity (imaging, analytics). It underperforms in governance-⏎heavy domains. The common thread is governance inadequacy, not capability⏎limitation. Deterministic governance unlocks high-value applications currently⏎blocked by compliance concerns.⏎Question 7 (Decision-Makers): General Counsel and Risk Management are⏎typically the blocking stakeholders—they cannot approve deployments without⏎governance guarantees. CMOs focus on clinical efficacy; CIOs on integration;⏎Compliance Officers on regulations. Deterministic governance directly addresses the⏎Risk Management/Legal blockers.⏎Question 8 (Interoperability): Critical gaps exist in standards for exchanging⏎governance attestations, audit trail formats, and AI governance metadata in FHIR.⏎The 4TS Proof-Carrying Decision (PCD) format provides a foundation. Concrete⏎recommendation: HHS could sponsor an HL7 FHIR Implementation Guide for⏎exchanging governance attestations (decision hashes, policy IDs, cryptographic⏎signatures) alongside CDS Hooks responses or as extensions to relevant FHIR⏎resources. This would enable EHRs and clinical systems to verify governance state⏎without proprietary integrations.⏎Page 13 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Question 9 (Patient Concerns): Patient concerns about privacy, accuracy,⏎dehumanization, and bias are fundamentally governance concerns. Deterministic⏎governance addresses them: cryptographic audit trails ensure verifiable privacy⏎compliance; reproducible decisions enable accuracy demonstration; escalation⏎protocols preserve human oversight; bias detection provides equity assurance.⏎Question 10 (Research Priorities): HHS should prioritize research in deterministic⏎governance methods, cryptographic audit infrastructure, causal inference for clinical⏎AI, and human-AI collaboration frameworks.⏎Page 14 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎Part IV: About FERZ—Deterministic Governance⏎Infrastructure⏎FERZ AI develops deterministic AI governance infrastructure software. Unlike⏎approaches that attempt to make AI models "safer" through training, FERZ operates⏎at the deployment layer—wrapping any AI system in a deterministic validation shell⏎that guarantees compliance with explicitly defined rules.⏎Core Technology⏎• LASO(f): Multi-tier linguistic and action governance framework. Eight⏎validation tiers (syntax, semantics, pragmatics, stylistics, discourse,⏎morphology, phonology/graphemics, lexicon) with bidirectional propagation.⏎Designed for low-latency, high-accuracy validation.⏎• DELIA: Flat constraint architecture with Z3 SMT solver for rapid deterministic⏎validation. Ideal for action governance requiring ALLOW/DENY/ESCALATE⏎decisions.⏎• CausaCore: Cross-domain causal modeling with four specialized engines for⏎drug interaction analysis, clinical trial design, and precision medicine.⏎• 4TS Standard: Vendor-neutral open standard for AI governance verification⏎(CC BY-NC-ND 4.0).⏎Performance benchmarks available upon request under appropriate evaluation conditions;⏎figures depend on policy complexity and deployment environment.⏎Regulatory Alignment⏎FERZ systems are designed for regulatory compliance from the ground up:⏎• FDA 21 CFR Part 11: Electronic signatures, immutable audit trails, record⏎integrity verification⏎• HIPAA: Access controls, encryption, audit logging for protected health⏎information⏎• EU AI Act: High-risk system governance, documentation requirements,⏎explainability⏎• SEC Rule 17a-4: WORM storage, tamper-evidence, 7-year retention⏎Offer of Collaboration⏎FERZ stands ready to support HHS:⏎1. Pilot Programs: Deploy LASO(f) and DELIA in hospital pilots to generate⏎real-world evidence⏎2. Standards Development: Collaborate on healthcare-specific adaptations of⏎the 4TS Standard⏎3. Technical Consultation: Provide briefings to HHS staff on deterministic⏎governance approaches⏎4. Research Partnerships: Participate in HHS-sponsored research through⏎cooperative agreements or CRADAs⏎Vendor-Neutral Guidance: We encourage HHS to adopt the evaluation criteria and⏎governance principles described in Parts I and II regardless of vendor. The Four⏎Tests Standard (4TS) is published under open license specifically to enable vendor-⏎Page 15 | Public Submission⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response | FERZ LLC⏎neutral assessment. Our interest is in establishing clear standards that accelerate⏎trustworthy AI adoption—FERZ benefits from a well-defined market, not from⏎regulatory ambiguity.⏎The promise of AI in clinical care is immense. The barrier is trust. Deterministic⏎governance infrastructure can bridge that gap—transforming AI from a liability⏎concern into a verified asset.⏎Public Submission Notice: This submission contains no protected health information (PHI)⏎and no confidential business information. It is intended for inclusion in the public docket.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Neil Nair⏎Chief Business Officer, FERZ AI⏎neil@ferz.ai⏎Page 16 | Public Submission♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0098♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Harris♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Spent twenty-three years as a cardiac nurse at Concord Hospital and I've watched a lot of technology get rolled out before anyone had really thought through what happens when it goes wrong. That's what I keep coming back to when I read about this initiative.⏎⏎The piece of this that concerns me most isn't the clinical AI side, honestly. It's what's already happening with AI in insurance coverage determinations, and the fact that HHS seems to be in a posture of accelerating adoption without first addressing a documented accountability problem that exists right now.⏎⏎In 2023, a Senate investigation and reporting by ProPublica documented that UnitedHealth Group's nH Predict algorithm was denying post-acute care claims for elderly patients at rates that internal auditors found were not being reviewed by physicians in any meaningful way. Patients with serious conditions were being sent home or cut off from rehab facilities based on what amounted to a model's output, with appeals processes that were so burdensome that most patients or their families just gave up. I don't know exactly what percentage gave up, but the reporting suggested it was a lot. These weren't edge cases. The patients affected were disproportionately Medicare Advantage enrollees, many of them elderly, many with limited English, many without the kind of family support system that helps you fight a denial letter written in insurance jargon.⏎⏎What I want HHS to understand is that this isn't a hypothetical risk of AI adoption going wrong somewhere down the road. It's a thing that has already happened and is probably still happening while this RFI comment period is open.⏎⏎HHS has authority here. I'm not a lawyer, but I know the department can set conditions on Medicare Advantage plans. It can require that AI-assisted coverage determinations be subject to review by a licensed clinician before a denial is final. It can require that patients receive an explanation of why a claim was denied that actually tells them what the algorithm flagged, not just a boilerplate letter. Whether HHS chooses to exercise that authority is a policy decision, not a technical limitation, and right now the choice seems to be to let the acceleration continue while the accountability infrastructure catches up, if it ever does.⏎⏎That sepsis algorithm I saw in 2019 — the one that was right when we almost missed it — it worked partly because we were standing right there. There was a charge nurse who had been on that floor for eleven years and who looked at the flag and looked at the patient and made a judgment. The algorithm didn't replace her judgment. It informed it. That's the model that should apply to coverage decisions too, but what we have instead is the algorithm making the effective determination and the human review being nominal at best.⏎⏎I want to say I'm open to AI speeding things up and reducing administrative burden, I genuinely am. I've seen how much time nurses spend on documentation that could be better spent with patients. But prior authorization denials that affect whether someone gets cardiac rehab after a heart attack are not the same category of administrative friction. The stakes are different and the oversight requirements should reflect that.⏎⏎My specific ask is that HHS use whatever leverage it has over Medicare Advantage contractors to require documented, substantive human clinical review before any AI-assisted denial becomes final, and that the appeals process be simplified to the point where a 74-year-old patient in rural New Hampshire without a college degree can actually use it without hiring someone to help.⏎⏎I'm not sure what the right regulatory mechanism is for that second part, whether it's a condition of participation or a separate rulemaking or something else.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0099♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Medical Device Manufacturers Association (MDMA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Medical Device Manufacturers Association (MDMA)♔♔♔♔♔♔1333 H Street, NW⏎Suite 400W⏎Washington, DC 20005⏎Phone (202) 354-7171⏎Fax (202) 354-7176⏎www.medicaldevices.org⏎February 23, 2026⏎Via Electronic Submission⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎To whom it may concern,⏎On behalf of the Medical Device Manufacturers Association (MDMA), below please find comments⏎on the Request for Information, Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care.⏎MDMA is a national trade association that provides educational and advocacy assistance to hundreds⏎of innovative companies in the field of medical technology. Our members, the majority of which are⏎small to mid-sized medical device companies, have a strong record of delivering breakthrough⏎therapies to treat chronic diseases and life-threatening conditions while lowering the cost of care.⏎MDMA’s mission is to ensure that patients have timely access to the latest advancements of safe and⏎effective medical technologies that improve health outcomes. We appreciate the opportunity to⏎provide these comments, and we look forward to ongoing collaboration with the Assistant Secretary⏎for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎on this important issue.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎AI has been incorporated into regulated medical devices for three decades, and the existing⏎Food and Drug Administration (FDA) medical device regulatory requirements remain⏎appropriate for these devices. As more novel AI technologies emerge in medical devices⏎(e.g., generative AI and agentic AI), it is important to assess whether any specific updates to⏎the regulatory framework may be needed to address their unique characteristics.⏎We encourage FDA to maintain a risk-based, technology-agnostic approach that is consistent⏎with its longstanding device framework. Regulatory oversight should be proportionate to the⏎clinical risk posed by the device and the degree of AI autonomy or system influence, rather⏎than triggered solely by the presence of AI. Devices incorporating limited AI functionality⏎should not be subject to the same expectations as systems that generate outputs or modify⏎⏎clinically actionable outputs. This approach will foster continued innovation while preserving⏎patient safety.⏎Furthermore, it is essential that the FDA remains the primary regulatory authority for AI-⏎enabled medical devices. Establishing separate regulatory requirements or oversight bodies⏎for AI would add significant complexity and regulatory burden, hindering innovation without⏎providing additional benefits to patient safety.⏎We also urge the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) to advance standards and⏎interoperability by prioritizing evaluation and adoption of AI-relevant standards, including⏎model lifecycle, data/metadata quality, and transparency. This will help reduce fragmentation⏎for both providers and manufacturers.⏎HHS should advance US leadership in internationally recognized consensus standards and⏎regulatory cooperation mechanisms for AI-enabled health technologies to reduce duplicative⏎requirements across markets, strengthen American competitiveness, and accelerate timely⏎patient access, while preserving FDA’s risk-based statutory authority.⏎Unclear regulatory boundaries exist between the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC),⏎decision support interventions (DSIs), and predictive decision support interventions (pDSIs),⏎administrative AI, and workflow optimization tools. There is also fragmented oversight⏎between ASTP/ONC, FDA clinical decision support (CDS), and the Centers for Medicare⏎and Medicaid Services (CMS), which creates uncertainty for developers seeking to⏎understand applicable requirements across the AI lifecycle and highlights the need for better⏎cross-agency coordination to harmonize expectations for AI driven health technologies.⏎Lack of predictable expectations for model updates, learning systems, and post-deployment⏎monitoring is another barrier to private sector innovation and clinical adoption. Additionally,⏎misalignment between ASTP/ONC lifecycle frameworks and medical device regulatory⏎requirements result in unclear regulatory expectations. Specifically, it would provide clarity⏎if the marketing requirements for FDA regulated devices were the only regulatory⏎requirements, without any overlapping requirements from ONC.⏎We’d also encourage HHS to advance regulatory and reimbursement approaches for inpatient⏎and hospital outpatient settings in addition to decentralized setting since in these settings, AI⏎enabled medical technologies can have a significant impact on both cost and care delivery.⏎Finally, unpredictable and unclear reimbursement pathways present barriers to private sector⏎innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care. Because the⏎coverage, coding, and payment methodologies of CMS and other payers have not kept pace⏎with technology, developers face significant risk and uncertainty with respect to whether⏎physicians, hospitals, and other providers will be appropriately and adequately reimbursed⏎for utilizing their products, which can slow and even stifle innovation.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎⏎AI is increasingly embedded within regulated medical devices, diagnostics, and integrated⏎hardware-software ecosystems to help support clinical decision making and therapeutic and⏎diagnostic interventions. It can allow for improved visualization, patient-specific plans, real-⏎time intraoperative decision support, and holds the potential to improve clinical outcomes⏎while reducing workforce burdens across the care team, including surgeons and medical⏎staff.⏎The challenge is that existing reimbursement systems are tied to the costs of earlier versions⏎of supplies and equipment that do not reflect the value, complexity, or ongoing costs of AI-⏎enabled systems. While billing codes may be used in some cases to report the procedures⏎performed, the incremental value and costs of AI-powered solutions are not captured in⏎payment rates. We urge HHS/CMS to consider reimbursement mechanisms that facilitate⏎adoption of these new technologies.⏎A reliable and predictable pathway to payment for AI and algorithmic tools is critical. Lack⏎of such a pathway creates uncertainty for manufacturers and investors alike, limiting the⏎development and adoption of AI-driven solutions. Venture investors consider a clear coding⏎and reimbursement strategy to be the single most important factor when deciding whether to⏎fund AI-based medical device solutions. For established medical technology manufacturers,⏎this uncertainty also constrains long-term capital planning, lifecycle software investment, and⏎U.S.-based innovation. Without a reliable pathway to payment, these innovations risk⏎reduced venture investment, slower adoption, and limited market uptake.⏎Further, we support the reimbursement for AI enablement via CPT or other codes⏎that are tied to demonstrable improvements in quality of care, reduction of errors, efficiency⏎gains, predictability, reproducibility, and productivity improvements resulting from AI use⏎(consistent with ACLA and American Medical Association positions). These mechanisms⏎should recognize AI functionality when it demonstrably contributes to improved outcomes,⏎safety, or efficiency whether as an integral component of medical devices, or as stand-alone⏎clinical tools. This might include supporting value-based reimbursement pathways,⏎reimbursing as part of care bundles, risk-based contracts, quality improvement programs, and⏎outcome-based reimbursement.⏎We recommend that CMS/ CMMI pilot an AI reimbursement model specifically for AI⏎enabled diagnostics, robotics, laboratory applications, operational improvements, outcome⏎metrics, and other measurable clinical and system-level benefits. Pilots should include⏎hospital and ambulatory surgical center settings and should account for the unique needs of⏎capital-intensive technologies that rely on continuous software improvement, with a clearly⏎defined pathway to transition successful pilots into permanent coding, payment, or coverage⏎mechanisms to accelerate sustainable reimbursement and broader adoption. CMS should⏎ensure that any new AI-specific reimbursement models supplement existing pathways rather⏎than supplant technologies that have already secured coverage. For AI-powered solutions⏎with established coding and payment, these existing frameworks must be preserved to⏎maintain market stability and patient access.⏎When new medical technologies emerge that deploy AI and have no predicate 510(k), it⏎would be helpful to have a more predictable review process than the one currently in place⏎for the De Novo pathway. The Medical Device User Fee Amendments (MDUFA) De Novo⏎target is to have only 70% of submissions meet a 150-day review target. Further, to date,⏎⏎only about 50% of De Novos have obtained a positive determination allowing marketing the⏎subject product. The de novo process thus is an uncertain one that makes projecting and⏎preparing for product launch challenging. This level of unpredictability is particularly⏎difficult for AI-enabled platforms that require synchronized regulatory approval,⏎reimbursement planning, and clinical adoption. We urge HHS/FDA to implement⏎improvements to the De Novo process to address these issues, including issuance of clearer⏎evidentiary expectations, greater transparency on review timelines, and more consistent⏎application of standards.⏎While not exclusive but certainly related to AI product reviews, manufacturers need clearer⏎guidance on the acceptability of data originating outside of the US. Moreover, further⏎clarification on what demographic and site differentiations are required, both for training and⏎testing, also would help define the type of data required for submissions and improve the⏎predictability of submission outcomes.⏎The CMS HTI-1 rule has been interpreted publicly by CMS to both apply and not apply to⏎products regulated as medical devices if it is training algorithms on data from Electronic⏎Health Records. This interpretation extends to medical devices already reviewed and⏎cleared/approved by the FDA via the 510(k) or PMA process, which is duplicative,⏎burdensome and redundant. Modification of this rule should be prioritized to avoid needless⏎duplicative and burdensome review for products already determined to be safe and effective⏎by the FDA. Further clarification on the scope of the rule would ensure that medical device⏎manufacturers understand all the requirements that apply to their AI-driven medical devices⏎not subject to FDA review.⏎Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA) AI requirements need more clarity.⏎We would also believe there should be a playing field level so that software functions that⏎meet the definition of a medical device are regulated the same way regardless of whether⏎they are deployed in a mobile application, an electronic health record (EHR), or traditional⏎medical device.⏎Additional guidance on applying HIPAA, de-identification standards, and secondary use⏎rules to AI training, validation, and model improvement would be beneficial. We encourage⏎reducing uncertainty around permissible uses of clinical data for model improvement⏎including the use of real-world data to support postmarket learning and iterative⏎improvement. Furthermore, we recommend that postmarket monitoring be outcome-focused⏎and risk-based rather than blanket requirements that could slow clinical adoption or⏎disproportionately burden high-performing, low-risk technologies.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Legal and implementation issues include liability allocation among developers, deployers,⏎and clinicians, indemnification and contractual risk-shifting, and transparency expectations⏎for AI-assisted decisions.⏎⏎For AI tools used in clinical settings that are not regulated as medical devices, the biggest⏎issue is uncertainty about responsibility. When these tools influence documentation,⏎workflow, triage, or other aspects of care, it is not always clear who is accountable if⏎something goes wrong or how existing privacy and compliance rules apply. That ambiguity⏎can slow adoption, even when the technology is beneficial. HHS does not need to create new⏎liability structures, but clearer guidance on how existing rules apply would reduce⏎uncertainty and support responsible use.⏎An additional emerging issue involves the distinction between algorithm developers and⏎model deployers. In some cases, companies provide foundational algorithms or configurable⏎AI systems, while health systems or other end users perform the training and generate site-⏎specific models. Current guidance often focuses on the evaluation and oversight of deployed⏎models, leaving ambiguity regarding:⏎• The liability boundary between the underlying algorithm and the trained model⏎• The level of testing and validation expected at the algorithm stage versus the trained-⏎model stage⏎• The allocation of responsibility for performance, bias mitigation, and post-⏎deployment monitoring⏎This distributed development paradigm challenges traditional governance frameworks that⏎assume a single entity controls design, training, and deployment.⏎HHS could play a valuable role by clarifying shared-responsibility expectations across the AI⏎lifecycle and providing guidance on proportional testing and validation requirements at each⏎stage. Such clarity would reduce contractual uncertainty, promote innovation, and ensure⏎accountability without discouraging flexible AI development models.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎Any evaluation methods promoted by HHS should align with existing regulatory and quality⏎systems and avoid duplicative third-party validation, redundant documentation, or⏎unnecessary disclosure of confidential commercial information or sensitive health data.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎All medical devices, including AI-driven medical devices, are already highly regulated across⏎the entire product lifecycle, including both the pre- and post-market timeframes. Devices are⏎subject to robust post-market requirements that ensure their ongoing safety and⏎effectiveness. When considering additional post-market requirements for emerging AI⏎technologies, it is important that any new measures are tailored to specific device risks and⏎characteristics, rather than applied as broad, blanket requirements to all AI-enabled devices.⏎It is also important to recognize that AI-enabled devices are not uniform in function or risk.⏎Governance frameworks, including hospital-based AI review committees, should apply⏎⏎proportionate, risk-based oversight that reflects the device’s intended use, level of autonomy,⏎and degree of clinical oversight, rather than treating all AI technologies equivalently.⏎Additionally, third-party certification should be avoided, as it will lead to redundant⏎validation activities and documentation and may lead to disclosure of commercial⏎confidential information and inappropriate release of private healthcare data.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎AI has met or exceeded expectations as it relates to imaging and diagnostics, laboratory⏎workflow optimization, and supply chain visibility.⏎AI has fallen short where tools were deployed without sufficient workflow integration, where⏎performance was evaluated on retrospective datasets that did not reflect real-world⏎variability, or where limited interoperability constrained scalability. Unrealistic expectations⏎regarding full automation, rather than augmentation of clinical decision-making, have also⏎contributed to underperformance in some settings.⏎The ability to more fully evaluate and scale AI tools across care settings is directly influenced⏎by access to high-quality, interoperable data. Broader use of secure, standardized, and⏎privacy-protective data sharing agreements would enable more robust validation across⏎diverse populations and clinical environments. Such access would support stronger evidence⏎generation, improve generalizability, and allow clearer demonstration of clinical and⏎economic value.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Chief information security officers, chief technology officers, chief innovation officers,⏎quality assurance, information technology, hospital information technology, procurement,⏎revenue cycle management, engineering, and laboratory directors have the most influence on⏎the adoption of AI for clinical care. Hurdles include supply chain, data sharing agreements,⏎security, transparency, risk management, change management, validation, contract/legal⏎review, reimbursement, and procurement.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Greater interoperability between laboratory systems, diagnostic platforms, and downstream⏎clinical systems (including the imaging-to-EHR interoperability gap) would unlock⏎substantial AI value. Standards like FHIR, DICOM and DICOM-SR can help, but they have⏎not been adopted consistently. Technical and performance standards must be identified.⏎Allowing cross-system integration will enable imaging-based AI tools to avoid being siloed⏎within radiology workflows limiting downstream clinical adoption, multidisciplinary⏎⏎decision-making, and development of value-based reimbursement models. Data cleanliness⏎and privacy should be prioritized. Finally, benchmarking by key opinion leaders would be⏎useful, as well as background benchmarking, though issues exist with the need to upskill to⏎use AI to detect issues and deskill prevention of domain knowledge.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers consistently express interest in AI tools that meaningfully improve⏎access, timeliness, and quality of care. Desired outcomes include faster and more preventive⏎diagnostics, better outcomes, reduced delays in testing and treatment, improved coordination⏎and bedside decision support, and better prediction of system downtime or care disruptions⏎that may affect access. AI that reduces administrative burden and enables clinicians to spend⏎more time with patients is also viewed positively.⏎At the same time, patients and caregivers raise important concerns related to privacy, data⏎security, bias, overreliance on automated systems, and the potential for malicious cyber⏎activity. These concerns are appropriate and should continue to be addressed transparently.⏎Importantly, many of these risks are already actively managed within existing regulatory and⏎quality frameworks. AI-enabled medical devices are subject to established cybersecurity⏎requirements, including secure design controls, vulnerability management, and post-market⏎monitoring to guard against malicious activity. Similarly, the risk of bias, while not unique to⏎AI, is addressed through data representativeness assessments, performance testing across⏎relevant populations, and risk mitigation strategies during development. For regulated⏎products, these efforts are evaluated as part of premarket review and ongoing quality system⏎oversight.⏎For non-device AI deployed in clinical environments, organizations similarly integrate⏎privacy, security, and performance monitoring controls within existing governance and⏎compliance programs. Continued transparency, lifecycle monitoring, and human oversight⏎remain essential to maintaining public trust.⏎By strengthening interoperability, reinforcing cybersecurity standards, and promoting⏎responsible development practices, AI can address patient priorities while operating within⏎established safeguards designed to protect safety, equity, and confidentiality.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎Priorities include trustworthy AI, bias mitigation across populations, human-AI collaboration⏎models, real-world effectiveness and cost-benefit analysis, implementation science,⏎evaluation frameworks, public private partnerships and literature gaps.⏎Several of these areas (particularly evaluation frameworks, robustness testing methods, and⏎transparency standards) are well suited for development through international consensus⏎standards bodies and global harmonization efforts. Leveraging international standards⏎⏎promotes consistency, reduces duplicative regulatory burden, and supports global⏎interoperability of AI-enabled healthcare technologies.⏎Accordingly, HHS research investments should complement, not replace, international⏎standards development. The Department can play a valuable role by funding applied⏎research, supporting public-private partnerships, and participating in global harmonization⏎initiatives to ensure that U.S. regulatory and policy approaches remain aligned with⏎internationally recognized best practices.⏎* * * *⏎We appreciate the opportunity to comment on this important topic. If we can provide any additional⏎information, please contact me at mleahey@medicaldevices.org or (202) 354-7171.⏎Sincerely,⏎Mark Leahey⏎President & CEO⏎Medical Device Manufacturers Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0100♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Community Care Cooperative, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Community Care Cooperative♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, RIN 0955-AA13⏎Dear Deputy Assistant Secretary Posnack,⏎Community Care Cooperative (C3) appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department of⏎Health and Human Services’ Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) in the health sector.⏎C3 is a non-profit, Federally Qualified Health Center (FQHC)-owned Accountable Care and⏎Management Services Organization. Founded, governed, and operated by community health⏎centers. We support more than 50 FQHCs across fifteen states and the District of Columbia and⏎we operate Medicare, Medicaid, and commercial value-based payment arrangements covering⏎more than 250,000 lives. As a primary-care-led organization, our mission is to leverage the⏎collective strengths of FQHCs to improve health outcomes for underserved communities while⏎lowering total cost of care.⏎In our role as a Management Services Organization, C3 works closely with health centers to⏎understand their operational, clinical, and technological needs, including serving as a leader and⏎group purchaser for both clinical and administrative AI solutions. Through this work, we have⏎direct experience supporting the real-world deployment of AI tools in under-resourced primary⏎care settings. We have seen how thoughtfully implemented AI can reduce administrative⏎burden, enhance patient access, and support high-quality chronic disease management,⏎particularly in areas such as documentation, patient communication, and revenue cycle⏎functions, provided that adoption strategies are practical, equitable, and aligned with strong⏎safeguards and accountability.⏎Our comments focus on two areas:⏎1. How HHS can accelerate applied AI innovation in primary care and community health,⏎and⏎2. The regulatory and governance safeguards necessary to maintain patient trust and⏎support responsible adoption.⏎SEVENTY FIVE FEDERAL STREET, 7th FLOOR | BOSTON, MA 02110⏎⏎Accelerating Applied AI Innovation in Primary Care and Community Health⏎Primary care is the only specialty consistently shown to reduce total cost of care through⏎prevention, early detection, and longitudinal chronic disease management. Yet it remains⏎chronically under-resourced and under-invested, particularly in community health centers,⏎which serve approximately 14 percent of the U.S. population while receiving a small share of⏎federal health care funding. As a result, AI adoption strategies designed for well-resourced⏎health systems often do not translate effectively to safety-net settings.⏎To realize the potential of AI to improve outcomes and advance health equity, primary care⏎providers - and particularly FQHCs - must be treated as essential partners in the design,⏎validation, and deployment of AI solutions, not simply as downstream adopters.⏎Where Applied AI Is Delivering Immediate Value⏎Across our network, health centers have expressed strong interest in AI tools that deliver near-⏎term operational and clinical value, including:⏎• Reducing administrative burden, through ambient documentation, coding and billing⏎support, and workflow automation that improves accuracy and sustainability while⏎alleviating staff burnout.⏎• Enhancing patient access and experience, including AI-enabled voice agents that⏎support appointment navigation, language access, and patient communication.⏎• Supporting high-quality clinical care, particularly tools that augment clinical workflows,⏎such as pre-visit preparation and documentation, while preserving clinician judgment⏎and accountability.⏎Importantly, trust is foundational to adoption. Health centers and their patients must be⏎confident that AI tools are designed to improve access to care and understanding of health⏎information, not to obscure decision-making or introduce new risks.⏎Barriers to Adoption in Safety-Net Settings⏎Despite growing interest, FQHCs face meaningful barriers to AI adoption, including:⏎• Upfront and ongoing costs, coupled with uncertainty around return on investment in⏎value-based environments.⏎SEVENTY FIVE FEDERAL STREET, 7th FLOOR | BOSTON, MA 02110⏎⏎• Workflow disruption and training demands, particularly in settings with limited staffing⏎capacity.⏎• Interoperability challenges, including integration across EHR instances and versions and⏎avoidance of siloed, proprietary tools.⏎• Limited internal capacity to vet, govern, and continuously monitor AI solutions.⏎These barriers are structural, not philosophical, and they reflect the day-to-day realities of⏎operating in safety-net primary care. With the right federal policy levers, they are addressable.⏎Federal Levers to Enable Practical Adoption⏎In response to Questions 2 and 8, we encourage HHS to consider the following actions:⏎• Align payment models with AI adoption costs, including embedding technology⏎adoption incentives into CMMI models such as the LEAD and ACCESS models, and⏎allowing advanced or onboarding investments to offset implementation and⏎management costs.⏎• Leverage trusted intermediaries and dissemination hubs that can convene technology⏎developers and community health centers, reduce duplication, and accelerate real-world⏎validation and learning (for example, collaboratives that pair early-stage developers with⏎safety-net providers, such as ATTUNE-like models, or implementation-focused hubs such⏎as Health AI Partnerships).⏎• Create mutual incentives for data sharing and interoperability, supporting bidirectional⏎data flow between providers and developers while avoiding data silos that undermine⏎care coordination and population health management.⏎Data Representativeness⏎FQHCs care for patients who are, on average, sicker, lower-income, and more socially complex⏎than many other populations. AI tools developed without meaningful inclusion of safety-net⏎data risk working well for some populations while failing others- and, in the worst cases,⏎exacerbating existing disparities. Representative data, ongoing monitoring, and corrective action⏎are essential to ensuring AI advances equity rather than undermining it. Community health⏎centers should be viewed as indispensable partners in developing trustworthy, equitable AI.⏎Regulation, Governance, and Necessary Safeguards⏎SEVENTY FIVE FEDERAL STREET, 7th FLOOR | BOSTON, MA 02110⏎⏎Maintaining patient trust is paramount to the successful adoption of AI in clinical and⏎administrative settings. AI has real potential to improve care delivery, but only if it is deployed⏎with strong guardrails.⏎First, human accountability must be preserved. AI tools should augment - not replace - clinical⏎judgment, and qualified human providers must remain responsible for final clinical decisions.⏎Second, data privacy and transparency are essential. We urge HHS to:⏎• Ensure strict adherence to HIPAA and Privacy Act requirements for all AI tools handling⏎protected health information.⏎• Require clear, explicit disclosure of data use, sharing, and permissions by AI developers.⏎• Develop a vendor evaluation or certification-like framework analogous to EHR⏎certification, building on emerging work such as the AI Vendor Disclosure Framework⏎developed by Health AI Partnerships, that enables providers to assess privacy⏎protections, data governance practices, and trustworthiness with confidence.⏎Such guardrails would reduce uncertainty, support responsible adoption, and protect both⏎patients and providers.⏎Closing⏎C3 strongly supports HHS’ efforts, through ONC and CMMI, to expand access, reduce⏎administrative burden, and sustain high-quality primary care through the responsible use of AI.⏎As a primary-care-led, FQHC-owned organization with hands-on deployment experience, C3 and⏎the community health centers we support stand ready to partner with HHS on future pilots,⏎learning collaboratives, and stakeholder engagement. We would welcome the opportunity to⏎inform implementation efforts with on-the-ground experience from primary care and⏎community health settings.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to share our perspective.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Christina Severin⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎Community Care Cooperative⏎SEVENTY FIVE FEDERAL STREET, 7th FLOOR | BOSTON, MA 02110♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0101♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Baker♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Family Caregiver Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔My organization has worked with family caregivers and the clinical teams that serve them for over thirty years, and I want to be direct about something that I think is getting lost in this conversation about AI acceleration: the thing that makes clinical care work is not accurate information retrieval. It never was.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to, reading HHS's framing here, is the assumption baked into the premise. The question of how to "accelerate adoption" treats medicine as though the core problem is throughput. But I've spent years watching caregivers navigate a system that already treats their loved ones as cases rather than people, and I can tell you the erosion of human presence in clinical encounters is already doing real damage. Adding AI tools without serious guardrails is going to make that worse, not better.⏎⏎A family caregiver in a rural Vermont clinic is not presenting as a dataset. She's scared. She's been awake for 36 hours. She's watching her husband's face for signs she can't quite name. The clinician who sits across from her and reads that situation — who notices she's deflecting, who catches that she said "fine" but her hands are shaking — that clinician is doing something that has measurable clinical value. There's actually a fair body of research on therapeutic alliance and health outcomes going back to the 1990s that HHS should be looking at before they talk about replacing judgment with algorithmic recommendations.⏎⏎Our Q3 2024 policy review found that member organizations serving Medicaid-dependent and rural populations were nearly twice as likely to report inadequate vendor disclosure about model training data. Nearly twice as likely. And fewer than 12% of our 2,100 member organizations had any formal protocol for logging concerns about AI bias. So we're being asked to accelerate adoption in populations that are already getting the least accountability from vendors, in settings where the therapeutic relationship is already under the most strain from understaffing and resource shortfalls.⏎⏎I'm not categorically opposed to AI tools in clinical settings. That would be a foolish position. What I'm opposed to is the framing that treating medicine as an information-processing problem is a neutral or benign choice. When a patient becomes primarily the object of an AI recommendation pipeline, something shifts. The clinician's sense of individual moral responsibility for that specific person changes. That's not a soft concern. Distributed or attenuated responsibility in clinical decision-making has concrete consequences for how errors get caught and who gets blamed when things go wrong.⏎⏎HHS is asking specifically about what regulatory and reimbursement actions could support adoption. My answer is that before reimbursement structures reward AI-assisted clinical decisions, you need mandatory bias auditing requirements with public disclosure, you need clear liability frameworks that keep a human clinician legally and professionally responsible for every consequential recommendation, and you need some honest accounting of what we lose when the person in the exam room knows their doctor is deferring to a system rather than thinking about them.⏎⏎The therapeutic relationship is not a luxury feature that can be stripped out once the technology gets good enough. It is part of what patients are seeking when they seek care. The sense that a specific trained human being is genuinely responsible for your situation, is thinking about you rather than about your data — that matters to outcomes. It matters to whether patients disclose symptoms, follow through on treatment, trust the system at all.⏎⏎I'd also note that the RFI doesn't ask what happens to clinical training pipelines if residents and new clinicians develop their diagnostic reasoning in an environment where AI tools are doing the pattern recognition. That seems like a question worth asking before we have the infrastructure locked in.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0102♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Orion Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Orion Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD⏎Assistant Secretary of Health and Human Services⏎U.S. Department of Health & Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave. SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Submitted Electronically⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Orion Health appreciates this opportunity to respond to the Request for Information: Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎Orion Health, a HEALWELL AI company, is a global healthcare technology firm specializing in⏎interoperability, health data infrastructure, and population-level data integration. In the United⏎States, Orion Health partners with state governments, health systems, health information⏎exchanges, clinically integrated networks, and risk-bearing entities to enable secure data exchange,⏎improve care coordination, and support value-based care initiatives.⏎Orion Health’s 30+ years of experience operating longitudinal exchange networks and integration⏎platforms positions us to contribute practical implementation insight as these policies continue to⏎evolve.⏎We welcome continued dialogue and collaboration to help translate these strategic priorities into⏎operational reality.⏎Yours sincerely,⏎/s/⏎James Henderson⏎SVP and General Manager, U.S.⏎Orion Health Inc.⏎75 State Street, Suite 100, Boston, MA 02109⏎Company Number: 109378145⏎www.orionhealth.com 1⏎⏎From Friction to Function: Interoperability as the⏎Foundation for Scalable Clinical AI⏎Submitted in Response to RIN 0955-AA13⏎Orion Health⏎Perspective from Operating Longitudinal Health Data Infrastructure⏎Recent federal discussions, including the ASTP Annual Meeting, highlight a pivotal shift: U.S. healthcare is⏎constrained more by coordination than by digitization. Data exists across systems, yet often lacks liquidity.⏎When information does not move reliably across settings, it creates operational friction for clinicians, patients,⏎and families.⏎Across the regional and national data exchange environments, payer and provider integration platforms, and⏎longitudinal patient record systems we support, we have observed this dynamic firsthand. In our experience,⏎the durability of clinical AI depends fundamentally on the integrity, completeness, and governance of the⏎underlying data environment.⏎AI adoption is underway. The critical question is whether it will scale within an infrastructure that supports⏎trust, transparency, and sustained performance.⏎OneHHS Alignment: Coordination Enables Innovation⏎The OneHHS approach, emphasizing coordination across ASTP, CMS, FDA, NIH, and other agencies, reflects an⏎important reality. Interoperability policy, reimbursement reform, regulatory oversight, and research⏎investment cannot evolve independently.⏎In practice, fragmentation in policy can mirror fragmentation in data. Cross-agency alignment creates the⏎predictability necessary for responsible innovation. When interoperability standards, payment models, and AI⏎governance frameworks move in concert, the ecosystem can scale proven solutions with confidence.⏎We view regulatory clarity not as a constraint on innovation, but as an enabler. Clear expectations reduce⏎hesitation and accelerate adoption.⏎Longitudinal Interoperability as a Precondition for Reliable AI⏎In multi-state and multi-organizational settings where Orion Health aggregates records across hospitals,⏎ambulatory providers, laboratories, imaging systems, and payers, longitudinal completeness materially⏎improves analytic reliability.⏎We have seen this particularly in:⏎• Chronic disease management programs requiring a multi-year history⏎• Cross-setting medication reconciliation⏎• Emergency-to-primary care coordination⏎• Population risk identification incorporating social and behavioral context⏎Orion Health Inc.⏎75 State Street, Suite 100, Boston, MA 02109⏎Company Number: 109378145⏎www.orionhealth.com 2⏎⏎AI models developed within narrow institutional boundaries, often show performance variability when⏎patients receive care elsewhere. In contrast, models evaluated within governed, interoperable environments⏎are more resilient and transparent.⏎As TEFCA adoption accelerates and exchange volume expands, interoperability is transitioning from policy⏎aspiration to operational infrastructure. The strategic focus appropriately shifts from simple connectivity to⏎performance, normalization, governance, and activation within clinical workflow.⏎From our perspective, AI and interoperability are layered components of the same modernization strategy,⏎rather than parallel efforts.⏎Risk-Based Oversight and Lifecycle Governance⏎Federal leadership emphasizes proportionate, risk-based oversight that reduces unnecessary compliance⏎burden while maintaining safeguards where clinical impact is highest.⏎In live deployments, we see that AI initiatives progress most effectively when supported by infrastructure that⏎enables:⏎• Transparent documentation of data provenance⏎• Clear articulation of intended use and limitations⏎• Multi-site validation across diverse populations⏎• Ongoing lifecycle monitoring for drift and bias⏎Beyond technical infrastructure, durable AI adoption also depends on disciplined evaluation and scaling⏎practices, including:⏎• Quantitative measurement of clinical, operational, and cost impact to guide decisions⏎• Explicit criteria to distinguish exploratory pilots from enterprise deployment⏎Infrastructure supporting traceability and auditability lowers barriers to responsible innovation. Predictable,⏎risk-calibrated oversight enables better alignment among stakeholders.⏎Innovation and safety need not be opposing forces. They are mutually reinforcing when supported by mature⏎data environments.⏎Real-Time Integration and Administrative Modernization⏎Federal initiatives such as electronic prior authorization, real-time prescription benefit integration, and⏎provider directory modernization reflect practical friction points encountered daily in healthcare delivery.⏎In payer and provider integrations we support, reliable endpoint routing, accurate directory information, and⏎consistent API performance are essential for automated transactions functioning at scale. Minor inaccuracies⏎can disrupt high-volume workflows and undermine trust.⏎Similarly, in administrative automation pilots, standardized transaction frameworks reduce manual burden⏎only when exchange pathways are predictable and structured data is consistently available.⏎These operational realities reinforce the need for enforceable interoperability. The goal is not merely the⏎ability to connect, but reliable, performant, and transparent connectivity.⏎Orion Health Inc.⏎75 State Street, Suite 100, Boston, MA 02109⏎Company Number: 109378145⏎www.orionhealth.com 3⏎⏎AI embedded in real-time integration pathways can reduce documentation burden, identify risk earlier, and⏎assist clinical decision-making—if it operates within trusted infrastructure.⏎Prevention, Healthspan, and Data Liquidity⏎Federal dialogue is shifting from reactive intervention to proactive management, improving healthspan,⏎addressing chronic disease, and integrating behavioral and social context.⏎Such objectives depend on longitudinal visibility across time and care settings. In environments where clinical,⏎preventive, and social data are harmonized into unified records, analytic capabilities expand significantly.⏎Predictive insights become more equitable, and interventions can be targeted more effectively.⏎Infrastructure that enables patients and clinicians to access comprehensive records and supports appropriate⏎consented sharing, strengthens both care coordination and AI-driven intelligence.⏎Guiding Principal for AI adoption⏎Based on our operational experience, a constructive guiding principle for clinical AI adoption is:⏎AI that meaningfully influences patient care is most durable when grounded in interoperable, longitudinal⏎data environments and validated through transparent, evidence-informed processes.⏎Federal efforts to align exchange frameworks, API performance standards, evolving value-based⏎reimbursement, and risk-calibrated AI governance signal a coordinated modernization strategy. The⏎convergence of these initiatives, rather than any single rule, will determine whether AI scales responsibly.⏎In practice, infrastructure enables liquidity. Liquidity enables reliable intelligence. Reliable intelligence,⏎responsibly governed, supports better care.⏎The direction articulated across ASTP, CMS, FDA, and collaborating federal partners reflects a shared objective.⏎The goal is to reduce friction, improve coordination, strengthen affordability, and accelerate responsible⏎innovation.⏎Orion Health Inc.⏎75 State Street, Suite 100, Boston, MA 02109⏎Company Number: 109378145⏎www.orionhealth.com 4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0103♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Alexashenko, Sergey, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sergey♔Alexashenko♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔It's time to let AIs prescribe medications! ⏎⏎ChatGPT is already a better doctor than 80% of human doctors I can go to. It's never out of network. I'm never going to get hit by a $400 bill on what should have been a $20 visit because my insurance company's website was down and I had no way to find out if a doctor took my insurance (his office wouldn't tell me).⏎⏎Let the people choose! It's despicable that the AMA (a doctor cartel) controls what access to medicine we can only get through doctors. Of course they are going to gate every single thing from us to bleed us dry. The only way we can get costs down meaningfully, is to start building a low cost alternative system from scratch AND LET THE PEOPLE CHOOSE.⏎⏎Thank you for your attention.⏎⏎P.S. My insurance bill went up 20% this year and coverage decreased. Let me opt out of this nonsense, I'll take ChatGPT + generics from India any day over what we have.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0104♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Lewis, Nicholas, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Nicholas♔Lewis♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔An effective way to promote innovation & effective usage of AI in the medical field would be for HHS & CMS to champion the cause by releasing an interactive Medicare mobile app for beneficiaries & providers. This would ensure market usage and adoption by beneficiaries & providers & provide a program "owned & controlled" platform for any other future private sector or provider integration.⏎The "Medicare Mobile" app would provide real-time location tracking & health data for patients & providers and would be a strong deterrent for fraud, waste, & abuse. It would also allow integration of AI based tools for claim verification, pre-authorization, payment determination, resource allocation, illness or outbreak detection & monitoring, and much more. The app would also provide a on-demand communication solution for patients & providers & reduce program administration costs.  ⏎I have a platform & mobile app that I'm willing to donate provided there is any interest.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0105♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from McGruder-Jarman, Lois, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lois♔McGruder-Jarman♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As long as it is acknowledged that this does not and cannot replace the human element in providing cost-effective healthcare to us, there are many benefits from my personal experience with being in Medical Coding for 30 years with assisting with identification of complex medical cases and diagnosing them, as well as improving outcomes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0106♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Association of Clinical Endocrinology (AACE), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Association of Clinical Endocrinology (AACE)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎TO: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎On behalf of the American Association of Clinical Endocrinology (AACE), thank you for the opportunity to⏎respond to this RFI. We support responsible, patient-centered adoption of AI in clinical care that⏎strengthens trust, protects safety and privacy, and improves real-world outcomes while reducing⏎clinician burden.⏎This response was developed by Dr. S. Sethu K. Reddy, MD, MBA and David Lieb, MD on behalf of the⏎AACE Advocacy Committee. For questions, please contact Ms. Surea Gainey, Advocacy & Regional⏎Engagement Manager, at sgainey@aace.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Dace Trence, MD, MACE⏎dtrence@aace.com⏎: https://www.aace.com/⏎President, American Association of Clinical Endocrinology⏎Elevating the practice of clinical endocrinology to improve global health⏎Introduction⏎The American Association of Clinical Endocrinology (AACE), founded in 1991, is a global, inclusive⏎community of more than 5,700 endocrine-focused clinical members, affiliates, and partners. Our mission⏎is elevating the practice of clinical endocrinology to improve global endocrine health, and our vision is⏎achieving healthier communities through endocrine innovation, education, and care. AACE members⏎provide care for people living with endocrine and metabolic disorders across the United States, working⏎as part of multidisciplinary endocrine care teams. Endocrinologists have been at the forefront of⏎technology-assisted delivery of healthcare for decades. We appreciate the opportunity to respond to⏎this request for information because artificial intelligence (AI) will increasingly influence how endocrine⏎care is delivered. Recent clinical experience suggests AI can reduce documentation burden and improve⏎detection and outcomes in some settings, but real-world benefit varies based on workflow integration⏎and implementation quality, reinforcing the need for rigorous evaluation and oversight [1]. Patient⏎safety and data protection are paramount. HHS policies should also promote improved clinical⏎outcomes, clinician well-being, and increased access for all to technological advances allowing⏎acceptable cost/benefit ratios.⏎⏎To make this RFI actionable, we urge HHS to focus on a few practical steps. First, adopt a risk-based⏎approach for clinical AI that clarifies expectations for transparency, validation, and how performance⏎should be monitored and updated after deployment. Second, align payment and quality programs so⏎tools that measurably improve outcomes and reduce clinician burden are supported. Third, accelerate⏎interoperability by advancing standardized data exchange (FHIR/USCDI and related vocabularies) and by⏎requiring clear provenance when AI-generated content enters the medical record. Fourth, publish⏎operational guidance for governance, privacy, and cybersecurity consistent with HIPAA and other⏎applicable federal and state laws, including auditability, vendor accountability, and incident response⏎expectations. Finally, invest in pragmatic research and real-world demonstrations—especially in⏎community, rural, and under-resourced settings—so benefits don’t remain limited to a small number of⏎well-resourced health systems. Unlike the typical static approval of clinical technology, AI-based⏎technology evolves rapidly and thus requires a parallel regulatory governance system to be both flexible⏎and adaptable.⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Private-sector innovation and sustained clinical use of AI are most constrained by the difficulty of⏎building tools that are safe, generalizable, and easy to use in real clinical workflows. Data needed for⏎development and validation remain fragmented across electronic health records, claims systems, and⏎devices, and are often labeled inconsistently or missing key context (for example, medication timing and⏎discontinuations, dose changes after subspecialty consultation, and whether data reflect inpatient⏎versus outpatient care). This slows development, increases patient-safety risk when tools are moved⏎across sites, and makes it harder to ensure equitable performance for underrepresented and lower⏎socioeconomic groups [2].⏎Adoption also stalls when AI increases clicks, alerts, or documentation rather than reducing work. It also⏎stalls when model outputs are not transparent enough for clinicians to trust and appropriately oversee⏎them, and when clinicians have not been trained on appropriate use and limitations. In addition,⏎vendors and health systems face ongoing costs for integration, cybersecurity, and monitoring for⏎performance drift and safety events, with uncertain reimbursement and unresolved⏎liability/accountability for AI-supported decisions. Finally, the “pathway” to commercialization is less⏎predictable than in other industries because requirements span multiple domains (privacy,⏎interoperability, reimbursement, and—in some cases—medical device regulation), and expectations for⏎validating and maintaining adaptive models remain unclear. In endocrinology, these barriers are⏎amplified by high-stakes medication decisions (insulin and glucocorticoids), longitudinal diabetes⏎technology data (CGM and insulin delivery), and the need to coordinate issues such as thyroid imaging,⏎pathology, and follow-up across settings [2-4].⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS can accelerate safe adoption by reducing integration burden, clarifying expectations for lifecycle⏎oversight, and aligning payment incentives with measurable improvements in patient safety and⏎outcomes. A high-impact step is strengthening interoperability expectations through ONC certification⏎⏎(45 CFR Part 170) and information-blocking enforcement (45 CFR Part 171) so core clinical data and⏎relevant device data can be exchanged through standardized, secure application programming⏎interfaces (APIs) rather than custom interfaces. This is particularly important for endocrine care, where⏎continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) and insulin delivery data influence day-to-day decisions and⏎inpatient safety, and where poor data flow can increase clinician workload and worsen inequities in⏎access [5, 6]. HHS should also coordinate with FDA to support a lifecycle approach for higher-risk clinical⏎AI, including pre-authorized update pathways for adaptive tools (e.g., predetermined change control⏎plans), quality management expectations (21 CFR Part 820), and postmarket safety surveillance and⏎reporting (21 CFR Parts 803 and 822) so performance drift, bias, and safety events are detected and⏎addressed over time [5].⏎HHS should enable privacy-preserving multi-site evaluation while maintaining strong patient protections⏎by providing clearer, more operational guidance under HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules (45 CFR Parts⏎160 and 164) that supports responsible use of limited datasets and privacy-enhancing approaches (e.g.,⏎federated or distributed analytics) for validation and monitoring, paired with clearer national⏎expectations for de-identification and data provenance for AI development [5, 6]. On the payment side,⏎CMS should create predictable pathways that reward demonstrated reductions in preventable harm and⏎administrative burden (for example, inpatient hypoglycemia prevention and streamlined prior⏎authorization documentation for obesity and diabetes therapies), while discouraging incentives that⏎drive unnecessary or duplicative testing or other utilization without benefit; relevant levers include⏎Medicare coverage and payment policies (42 CFR Parts 410 and 414) and hospital payment innovation⏎pathways (42 CFR Part 412), including time-limited transitional coverage while real-world outcomes and⏎economic value are evaluated [5, 6]. This ‘high-value AI’ paradox—where accurate tools can still increase⏎downstream utilization and costs without improving outcomes unless incentives and workflows are⏎aligned—reinforces the importance of payment and quality program alignment for AI adoption [7].⏎Finally, HHS should revisit program integrity rules to permit responsible deployment partnerships⏎through targeted updates or clarifications to Stark and Anti-Kickback frameworks (42 CFR Part 411 and⏎42 CFR Part 1001) when arrangements are transparent, not tied to referral volume, and demonstrably⏎improve safety, quality, and equitable access; these safeguards are especially important given⏎documented risks of hidden bias in deployed models [8]. Any clarification should be narrowly tailored to⏎bona fide value-based arrangements, conditioned on documented quality and patient-safety outcomes,⏎fair-market-value protections, and transparent auditing and reporting to prevent misuse. Programmatic⏎investments that support rural implementation (connectivity, telehealth, and remote monitoring) can⏎further ensure AI-enabled benefits reach underserved communities rather than widening disparities.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (⏎e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Non-medical-device AI (for example, administrative copilots, documentation tools, scheduling/triage⏎systems, consumer apps and wearables, and patient-facing chat tools) can still influence clinical⏎decisions and therefore patient safety, even when marketed as “wellness” or “administrative” tools and⏎not reviewed as medical devices. Novel issues include unclear allocation of liability when an AI-⏎generated summary, triage suggestion, or coaching recommendation contributes to harm; vendor⏎indemnification and contracting practices; privacy risks and secondary use of health-related data⏎(including use for model training); and cybersecurity risks from new interfaces and third-party⏎⏎integrations. These gaps are especially relevant in endocrinology because patient-facing tools may affect⏎insulin dosing decisions, hypoglycemia responses, or weight-loss medication use, and errors can cause⏎immediate harm [9].⏎HHS can help by promoting a risk-based governance approach tied to function and impact, not⏎marketing claims: clear disclosure when AI is used; minimum privacy and security expectations⏎(including clearer expectations when HIPAA does and does not apply); documentation of intended use,⏎limitations, and appropriate clinician oversight for higher-risk uses; and standardized pathways for⏎reporting and learning from AI-related near-misses and adverse events. HHS guidance can also promote⏎transparency and subgroup performance monitoring, and require accessibility considerations (language,⏎disability, and rural broadband constraints) so adoption improves outcomes without widening⏎disparities. These steps are consistent with emerging responsible-use guidance and the growing⏎literature calling for clearer accountability standards for clinical AI [10, 11].⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Evaluation should be matched to clinical risk and should measure real patient outcomes and workflow⏎impact, not only model accuracy. Before deployment, promising approaches include independent⏎external validation on datasets from multiple health systems (including community settings and⏎underrepresented groups); assessment of calibration (whether predicted risks match observed⏎outcomes); robustness testing across sites, devices, and documentation patterns; and human-centered⏎workflow testing to confirm the tool reduces cognitive load and documentation burden rather than⏎creating alert fatigue or burnout. Pre-deployment review should also include privacy and cybersecurity⏎evaluation for third-party integrations and clear documentation of intended use, limitations, and⏎appropriate oversight. In endocrinology, examples include inpatient hypoglycemia risk prediction,⏎diabetes technology decision support using CGM data, and thyroid ultrasound decision support—each of⏎which can behave differently across patient populations, equipment, and care settings, and requires⏎appropriate clinician training and oversight to ensure accurate interpretation [3, 11, 12].⏎After deployment, ongoing monitoring is essential: drift and calibration checks, routine performance⏎reporting by subgroup (race/ethnicity, language, socioeconomic proxies, rurality), and a standardized⏎incident reporting process when AI contributes to near-misses or harm. HHS support would be impactful⏎through funding pragmatic trials and implementation science, supporting shared evaluation datasets⏎and toolkits, and enabling safety-learning infrastructure and governance models (e.g., multidisciplinary⏎oversight committees) that help organizations respond quickly when performance degrades or⏎inequities emerge. Mechanisms such as contracts and cooperative agreements could build common⏎evaluation infrastructure, while grants and prize competitions could accelerate open benchmarking⏎tools and usability testing methods for real-world clinical workflows [10-12].⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Accreditation, certification, and industry-driven testing can lower barriers to adoption and improve⏎safety if they evaluate both the AI product and the deploying organization, using a tiered approach⏎matched to clinical risk. A practical structure is a layered governance-to-certification model (regulation⏎⏎and policy aligned to standards, standardized assessment procedures, and reproducible testing⏎tools/metrics), with two complementary credentials: an “AI product” certification (intended use,⏎external validation, robustness and bias testing, transparency documentation) and a “responsible⏎deployment” certification for health systems and clinics (governance, clinician training, monitoring⏎dashboards, and safety-event reporting) [13, 14]. This approach aligns with the National Academy of⏎Medicine’s AI Code of Conduct framework, which emphasizes the value of a shared code of conduct to⏎guide safe integration of AI into clinical practice and strengthen trust among clinicians and patients [15].⏎This also aligns with the growing view that AI utility depends not only on algorithm performance, but⏎also on health system capacity, workflow integration, and downstream clinical actions; proactive design⏎using target product profiles (TPPs) can help ensure tools are clinically useful and economically⏎sustainable [16].⏎To make certification actionable rather than “principles-only,” HHS can encourage standardized⏎documentation deliverables (for example, risk-scaled disclosure requirements and structured⏎documentation such as model cards/model documentation, performance by subgroup, limitations, and⏎post-deployment monitoring plans), while balancing transparency with privacy, intellectual property,⏎and security constraints [17]. In addition, transparency documentation should make explicit any⏎embedded value assumptions and tradeoffs (for example, how models balance cost, resource allocation,⏎clinical outcomes, and patient autonomy), as proposed in the “Values in the Model” (VIM) framework,⏎to support trust and responsible governance [18].⏎HHS can best support these activities by harmonizing expectations across agencies and programs and by⏎incentivizing adoption of credible certification pathways rather than creating duplicative requirements.⏎Specifically, HHS can convene or fund national testing frameworks and shared toolkits for pre-⏎deployment evaluation (dataset provenance and representativeness, subgroup performance reporting,⏎robustness testing) and post-deployment monitoring (drift, calibration, equity metrics), aligned with⏎healthcare-focused responsible use guidance and with confidential learning systems for near-misses and⏎harms (e.g., Patient Safety Organization-style reporting) [11]. HHS can then make certification “real” by⏎linking it to programmatic levers—CMS demonstrations or incentives, procurement expectations for⏎federally supported providers, and grant conditions for AI development and implementation—so high-⏎quality tools are easier to adopt in community settings without increasing clinician burnout or total⏎costs. Endocrine-relevant examples include certification expectations for inpatient insulin safety and⏎hypoglycemia prevention systems and for diabetes technology data integration tools that must work⏎across EHRs and device vendors, with harmonized data elements and analytic definitions, to support⏎equitable access [11, 19].⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest⏎potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI has met expectations most reliably in narrow, well-defined tasks that remove manual work or reduce⏎common, avoidable harm, especially when integrated into workflows with clear accountability. Recent⏎U.S. health system experience suggests that ambient documentation (“ambient notes”) is among the⏎most widely adopted use cases and is reported as relatively successful early on, consistent with the idea⏎that tools reducing clerical burden can improve efficiency without needing to “outperform” clinicians on⏎complex diagnostic decisions [20, 21]. In endocrine care, similarly bounded, high-value examples include⏎models that identify hospitalized patients at risk for iatrogenic hypoglycemia (supporting earlier⏎prevention and safer insulin use) and tools that streamline review of continuous glucose monitoring⏎⏎(CGM) data so clinicians can focus time on counseling and management decisions rather than manual⏎data reconciliation [11, 22].⏎AI has fallen short when tools are deployed without strong external validation, when they generate⏎excessive alerts, or when they shift work to clinicians without improving outcomes; real-world⏎experience also highlights “immature” tools, financial concerns, and regulatory uncertainty as common⏎barriers to sustained success [11, 20]. Some widely deployed clinical areas—such as imaging/radiology⏎and broad risk stratification (e.g., early sepsis detection)—have shown mixed success across⏎organizations, reinforcing the need for rigorous evaluation, calibration, and post-deployment monitoring⏎rather than assuming performance transfers across sites [20, 21, 23]. The most promising novel tools are⏎those that demonstrably improve outcomes and equity while reducing administrative burden: inpatient⏎glycemic safety support; diabetes technology data integration and personalization; obesity and⏎metabolic disease access/navigation support; and thyroid nodule risk stratification and follow-up⏎coordination that reduces duplicative imaging and missed surveillance. These priorities require ongoing⏎monitoring for drift and subgroup performance to avoid widening disparities and to prevent⏎downstream spending from increased testing or referrals without benefit [10, 11, 21]. Cost-shifting is a⏎real concern; AI implementations should be evaluated on total cost of care and downstream utilization,⏎not just local operational savings.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Within health care organizations, adoption is usually driven jointly by clinical leadership (quality and⏎patient safety), informatics/IT leadership (EHR integration and data governance), compliance/privacy⏎and security officers, and financial leadership (value and sustainability). In practice, tools that affect⏎medication safety, documentation, or patient communication also require strong frontline engagement⏎from nurses, pharmacists, and clinicians, because usability and workflow fit determine whether AI⏎reduces burden or creates alert fatigue and burnout [5, 11, 20]. In endocrinology, governance often⏎must span inpatient and outpatient teams and incorporate diabetes device data (continuous glucose⏎monitoring and insulin delivery), which increases implementation complexity but is essential for safety⏎and outcomes [11, 24].⏎Primary administrative hurdles include procurement and contracting (including data rights, security⏎requirements, and indemnification), EHR integration and interoperability constraints, privacy/security⏎review, clinician training and change management, establishing monitoring dashboards and incident⏎response processes, and demonstrating value without increasing costs. Real-world experience shows⏎that even when AI performs well in studies, organizations often struggle to translate that success into⏎routine practice without shared implementation playbooks, dedicated governance, and ongoing⏎monitoring [25, 26]. HHS can help by supporting standardized implementation toolkits and evaluation⏎methods, reducing duplicative reviews through clearer national expectations, and promoting⏎interoperability so endocrine AI tools can be adopted more broadly across community and rural⏎settings—improving access while maintaining safety and equity [5, 11, 20].⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards,⏎and benchmarking tools.⏎⏎Enhanced interoperability is a patient-safety issue. When endocrine patients move between outpatient⏎and inpatient care (or between health systems), incomplete or delayed data sharing increases the risk of⏎hypoglycemia/hyperglycemia, medication errors, duplicated testing, and missed follow-up. Enhanced⏎interoperability also supports patient-centered care by enabling patients to share timely updates in⏎symptoms, preferences, and patient-reported outcomes across care settings. This can improve patient⏎experience and allow recommendations and care plans to reflect changes in clinical status rather than⏎relying on outdated snapshots. Improving interoperability reduces the need for custom, one-off⏎interfaces and makes it more realistic to deploy clinically useful AI broadly—not just in a few well-⏎resourced sites—while supporting equitable access for rural, lower socioeconomic, and⏎underrepresented populations.⏎In endocrinology, the highest-yield data to make reliably shareable are: diabetes technology streams⏎(continuous glucose monitoring time-series data, insulin pump/pen dosing, automated insulin delivery⏎settings, hypoglycemia events and alerts), core medications and labs that drive decisions (insulin, GLP-⏎1/GIP agents, glucocorticoids; A1c/glucose/ketones; kidney function; lipids; thyroid tests), thyroid⏎ultrasound plus pathology (imaging objects and structured reports; FNA cytology; surgical pathology and⏎molecular testing), and relevant patient-reported outcomes (hypoglycemia burden, symptoms, side⏎effects, quality-of-life measures). Interoperability for these data types supports AI that can reduce⏎preventable harm (e.g., inpatient insulin safety), improve outcomes (e.g., earlier identification of⏎hypoglycemia risk), and reduce clinician burden by automating data gathering and reconciliation rather⏎than adding new documentation tasks.⏎A practical standards path already exists and should be reinforced through federal policy, certification,⏎and procurement expectations: FHIR-based exchange using the US Core Implementation Guide to define⏎a consistent “minimum set” of data elements and interactions, aligned with the United States Core Data⏎for Interoperability (USCDI) so the same core clinical data are available across certified systems.⏎Consistent coding for observations and labs (e.g., LOINC) is essential so AI tools can be validated and⏎safely reused across sites. Interoperability should also support benchmarking and monitoring so tools⏎improve care without increasing burnout or costs: (1) external validation on multi-site datasets that⏎include community settings and underrepresented groups; (2) ongoing post-deployment monitoring for⏎calibration, drift, and subgroup performance with clear thresholds for action; and (3) governance and⏎incident reporting processes when AI contributes to near-misses or harm—approaches consistent with⏎national risk-management and healthcare-focused responsible-use guidance [3, 11].⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients’ needs and their concerns should be the center of any discussion of changes in their health care⏎delivery.⏎Key Challenges Patients and Caregivers Want AI to Address⏎1. Care coordination and unmet social needs⏎2. Access to timely, personalized, and efficient care⏎3. Improved communication and responsiveness to patient concerns⏎4. Reduced administrative burden and improved care experience⏎Key Patient and Caregiver Concerns About AI Adoption⏎⏎1. Lack of transparency and insufficient information⏎2. Loss of human interaction and clinician autonomy⏎3. Privacy, data security, and misuse of personal health information⏎4. Risk of bias, inequity, and incorrect decisions⏎5. Lack of clear accountability and consent processes⏎Patients and caregivers also have consistent concerns that should be addressed up front: privacy and⏎secondary use of sensitive health data; cybersecurity; lack of transparency about when AI is used; and⏎the risk of biased performance that could worsen disparities for racial/ethnic minorities, people with⏎limited English proficiency, disability, rural residence, or lower socioeconomic status [2, 11, 27, 28].⏎Patients want the ability to opt out when feasible, and to know when AI is being used. The “digital⏎divide” is particularly relevant in endocrinology because many benefits depend on smartphones,⏎sensors, connectivity, and health literacy; if AI-enabled tools are deployed without deliberate support,⏎they can widen gaps in outcomes and access [28, 29]. Patients and caregivers are also concerned about⏎accountability when AI outputs are wrong, and about “financial toxicity” if AI-driven recommendations⏎lead to more testing or higher-cost care without clear benefit [30]. Practical safeguards include plain-⏎language disclosure and education for patients and caregivers about when AI is used and what it does;⏎clinician-in-the-loop oversight for higher-risk decisions; routine monitoring for safety and subgroup⏎performance; and ensuring that access supports (training, language accessibility, device/connectivity⏎options) are part of implementation, not an afterthought [11, 28, 29].⏎From a clinical endocrinology perspective, patients and caregivers are most likely to value AI that⏎measurably improves safety, reduces daily self-management burden, and improves access—without⏎adding cost or complexity. In diabetes care, that includes tools that help prevent severe hypoglycemia⏎and dangerous hyperglycemia, simplify review of continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) and insulin⏎dosing data, support safer inpatient insulin use, and help patients get timely education and follow-up⏎(including triage of portal messages and appointment navigation when access is limited). In obesity and⏎metabolic disease, many patients struggle with medication access and administrative barriers (coverage⏎changes, prior authorization, step therapy); appropriately designed tools could reduce delays and errors⏎by supporting standardized documentation and routing patients to the right resources. In thyroid⏎disease, patients want faster, clearer evaluation of nodules and cancer surveillance, with fewer⏎duplicative tests and better coordination across imaging, pathology, and follow-up. Patient-perspective⏎literature emphasizes that acceptance depends on transparency, understandable communication, and⏎clear clinician oversight—patients generally do not want AI to replace the clinician’s role in decisions⏎that affect diagnosis or treatment [2, 11, 27].⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize applied, patient-safety–focused research that proves real-world benefit across⏎diverse U.S. care settings (academic, community, and rural) without increasing total costs or clinician⏎workload. The following are six high-priority areas to accelerate safe and effective clinical AI adoption:⏎1. Real-world evaluation and clinical effectiveness research⏎2. National interoperable data infrastructure and validation networks⏎3. Implementation science and workflow integration⏎4. Trustworthy AI: bias mitigation, safety, and transparency⏎⏎5. Workforce training and human–AI collaboration⏎6. Primary care and preventive AI applications⏎These investments will create the scientific, technical, and operational foundation necessary for⏎scalable, safe, and equitable AI integration into clinical care.⏎In endocrinology, high-yield priorities include: (1) inpatient glycemic safety tools that predict and⏎prevent hypoglycemia and severe hyperglycemia using electronic health record and medication⏎administration data, paired with implementation studies that measure safety events, nurse/physician⏎workload, and alert fatigue; (2) standards-based integration of diabetes technology streams (continuous⏎glucose monitoring and insulin delivery) into clinical workflows, with evaluation of whether decision⏎support improves outcomes and reduces documentation burden; (3) thyroid⏎ultrasound/biopsy/pathology workflow tools that improve risk stratification and follow-up coordination,⏎with external validation across equipment vendors and practice types; (4) equitable obesity/metabolic⏎disease decision support that reduces administrative friction (coverage and prior authorization⏎workflows) while measuring access, time-to-therapy, and downstream utilization; and (5) lifecycle⏎monitoring science (calibration, drift, subgroup performance, and incident reporting) so deployed tools⏎remain safe and fair over time [3, 6, 11].⏎10a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in clinical care?⏎Yes, but the strongest evidence tends to be in narrower, well-defined use cases where outcomes and⏎workflows can be measured.⏎Strong evidence exists for:⏎• Improved diagnostic accuracy⏎• Improved workflow efficiency and documentation⏎• Earlier detection of clinical deterioration⏎• Improved operational performance⏎Moderate or mixed evidence exists for:⏎• Improved patient outcomes⏎• Improved clinician decision-making in real-world settings⏎Published evidence demonstrates that AI tools can improve diagnostic accuracy, clinical efficiency, and⏎operational performance in healthcare. AI systems have achieved expert-level performance in imaging⏎tasks such as breast cancer detection, lung nodule classification, and diabetic retinopathy screening,⏎improving diagnostic precision and reducing false positives. Predictive AI models using electronic health⏎record data have enabled earlier identification of clinical deterioration, sepsis risk, and cardiovascular⏎disease, supporting earlier intervention and preventive care. AI tools have also improved clinical⏎workflow efficiency. For example, AI stroke detection systems have reduced time to treatment by⏎rapidly identifying large vessel occlusions, and AI-assisted documentation tools have significantly⏎reduced clinician administrative burden. However, real-world clinical outcome improvements remain⏎variable and depend heavily on proper validation, workflow integration, and human oversight. AI is most⏎effective when augmenting clinician decision-making rather than replacing clinical judgment,⏎underscoring the importance of careful implementation and ongoing monitoring.⏎⏎In diabetes, multiple studies show that machine learning models can identify hospitalized patients at risk⏎for inpatient (iatrogenic) hypoglycemia, supporting a pathway to prevent harm when coupled to⏎appropriate clinical oversight and workflow design [22, 31]. In thyroid care, independent evaluations of⏎ultrasound decision support tools suggest clinically meaningful diagnostic performance, while also⏎reinforcing the need for multi-site validation, clear labeling of intended use, and monitoring when⏎moved into routine practice [32-34]. More broadly, authoritative guidance emphasizes that “impact”⏎should include patient outcomes and safety events as well as clinician workload and equity, and that⏎post-deployment monitoring is essential for safety and trust [3, 11].⏎10b. How does the literature approach the cost, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical⏎care?⏎The literature increasingly treats cost and transferability as implementation problems, not only model-⏎performance problems. Economic evaluations account for integration (interfaces, training, workflow⏎redesign), ongoing maintenance (updates and cybersecurity), and monitoring (performance drift,⏎recalibration, subgroup auditing), alongside downstream effects on utilization (testing, referrals,⏎avoidable admissions) and clinician time [25, 35].⏎Published economic evaluations indicate that many AI applications in healthcare are cost-effective or⏎cost-saving, particularly when used to improve diagnostic accuracy, enable earlier detection of disease,⏎and reduce avoidable complications and unnecessary procedures. AI tools that enhance screening, risk⏎prediction, and workflow efficiency can reduce downstream costs by preventing hospitalizations,⏎complications, and redundant testing. However, most studies emphasize that economic benefits depend⏎heavily on successful integration into clinical workflows and sustained real-world performance.⏎Implementation costs—including infrastructure, validation, training, and ongoing monitoring—can be⏎substantial and are often borne by healthcare providers, while financial savings frequently accrue to⏎payers or the broader healthcare system. This misalignment creates cost-shifting that may discourage⏎provider adoption in the absence of clear reimbursement pathways or shared savings models.⏎Additionally, many published economic analyses incompletely account for full lifecycle costs, including⏎maintenance, governance, and retraining, highlighting the need for prospective, real-world economic⏎evaluations. Policies that align reimbursement with demonstrated clinical and economic value will be⏎essential to accelerate responsible AI adoption while ensuring sustainability for healthcare providers.⏎Endocrine-adjacent screening provides a useful model: autonomous AI for diabetic retinopathy has been⏎evaluated as cost-effective or cost-saving under plausible assumptions because it can increase screening⏎reach while reducing downstream complications, but the value depends on deployment context and full⏎accounting of implementation costs [36]. Other relevant areas include:⏎a) Predictive models for diabetes complications: AI enables earlier intervention, reducing⏎hospitalizations and complications such as kidney failure or cardiovascular events.⏎b) Continuous glucose monitoring and AI-assisted insulin management: Improves glycemic control and⏎reduces hypoglycemia-related emergency care.⏎c) Population health management tools: AI identifies high-risk patients with diabetes or metabolic⏎disease, enabling targeted preventive care and reducing downstream costs.⏎Systematic reviews highlight wide variability in assumptions and study quality, underscoring the need⏎for transparent reporting of costs, comparators, and setting so results generalize across health systems⏎and do not unintentionally increase spending without benefit [25, 35].⏎⏎Conclusion⏎AI is already being integrated into clinical care, but it is crucial that government, as the public’s⏎representative, balance judicious safeguards with policies that encourage responsible public- and⏎private-sector innovation.⏎The American Association of Clinical Endocrinology (AACE) supports the responsible adoption of AI that⏎improves patient safety, outcomes, and access while strengthening trust, protecting privacy, and⏎supporting clinicians and care teams. In diabetes care, endocrinologists already have experience with AI-⏎enabled technologies such as continuous glucose monitoring and automated insulin delivery,⏎underscoring both the feasibility of responsible implementation and the opportunity to extend these⏎benefits to a wider range of patients and care settings. For AI to deliver meaningful benefit in endocrine⏎care at scale, it must be developed and evaluated using representative data; integrated into workflows⏎in ways that reduce administrative burden; monitored over time for performance, safety, and equity;⏎and deployed so that patients in rural, underserved, and underrepresented communities can access the⏎same benefits as those in well-resourced settings. We appreciate HHS’s leadership in convening⏎stakeholders on these issues and welcome continued collaboration to ensure that AI advances translate⏎into better, safer, more equitable clinical care. We respectfully urge HHS to synthesize comments⏎around a small set of cross-cutting priorities—interoperability, lifecycle oversight, equity reporting,⏎privacy/security, and payment alignment—to accelerate safe and equitable adoption.⏎References⏎1. Elias, P., et al., The Moment AI Arrived in the Clinic: Insights from the SAIL 2025 Year in Review.⏎NEJM AI, 2025. 2(11).⏎2. Nair, M., et al., A comprehensive overview of barriers and strategies for AI implementation in⏎healthcare: Mixed-method design. PLoS One, 2024. 19(8): p. e0305949.⏎3. Technology, N.I.o.S.a., NIST AI Risk Management Framework. 2023.⏎4. Ahmed, M.I., et al., A Systematic Review of the Barriers to the Implementation of Artificial⏎Intelligence in Healthcare. Cureus, 2023. 15(10): p. e46454.⏎5. Angus, D.C., et al., AI, Health, and Health Care Today and Tomorrow: The JAMA Summit Report⏎on Artificial Intelligence. JAMA, 2025. 334(18): p. 1650-1664.⏎6. Adler-Milstein, J., et al., Meeting the Moment: Addressing Barriers and Facilitating Clinical⏎Adoption of Artificial Intelligence in Medical Diagnosis. NAM Perspect, 2022. 2022.⏎7. Murthy, V.L. and C.J. Patel, The Paradoxical Challenge of High-Value Medical Artificial⏎Intelligence. NEJM AI, 2026. 3(2).⏎8. Abbott, E.E., et al., Understanding and Addressing Bias in Artificial Intelligence Systems: A Primer⏎for the Emergency Medicine Physician. J Am Coll Emerg Physicians Open, 2026. 7(1): p. 100311.⏎9. Sharma K, Padmavati M. AI-based medical devices and regulations: a cross-country perspective.⏎In Artificial Intelligence in Drug Development. p. 67-115. 2024.⏎10. Palmieri, S., C.T. Robertson, and I.G. Cohen, New Guidance on Responsible Use of AI. JAMA,⏎2026. 335(3): p. 207-208.⏎11. Joint Commission and Coalition for Health AI (CHAI) Guidance on The Responsible Use of AI in⏎Healthcare (RUAIH). 2025.⏎12. George, C., Non-Medical Device Apps for Good Health and Well-being: Regulatory Challenges, in⏎MDX 2025 Health IT Workshop on Emerging Technologies in Healthcare: Legal, Ethical, Social &⏎⏎Governance Aspects, C. George, Whitehouse, D. and Duquenoy, P. , Editor. 2025: Middlesex⏎University, London, UK.⏎13. Agarwal, A. and M.J. Nene, A five-layer framework for AI governance: integrating regulation,⏎standards, and certification. Transforming Government: People, Process and Policy, 2025. 19(3):⏎p. 535-555.⏎14. Legha Mamta Ranjitsingh & T. V. Subba Rao, Establish legal and regulatory standards for the⏎testing and validation of AI systems to ensure their reliability and safety in operational⏎environments. International Journal of System Assurance Engineering and Management, 2025.⏎16(10): p. 3338-3353.⏎15. Adams, L.L., et al., Advancing the National Academy of Medicine Artificial Intelligence Code of⏎Conduct for Health and Medicine. NEJM AI, 2025. 2(11).⏎16. Parsa, S., et al., Designing Clinically Useful AI: A Blueprint for Impact. NEJM AI, 2026. 3(2).⏎17. Winfield, A.F.T., et al., IEEE P7001: A Proposed Standard on Transparency. Front Robot AI, 2021.⏎8: p. 665729.⏎18. Goldberg, C., et al., The Missing Dimension in Clinical AI: Making Hidden Values Visible. NEJM AI,⏎2026. 3(2).⏎19. Ghosh A, S.A., Barad H, Transforming healthcare in India: The role of artificial intelligence and⏎regulatory frameworks for sustainable growth. World Medical & Health Policy., 2025. 17(3): p.⏎475-490.⏎20. Poon, E.G., et al., Adoption of artificial intelligence in healthcare: survey of health system⏎priorities, successes, and challenges. J Am Med Inform Assoc, 2025. 32(7): p. 1093-1100.⏎21. Ashakin, R., Current tools and techniques of artificial intelligence in healthcare, in Quantum⏎Computing in Medicine. 2026.⏎22. Mathioudakis, N.N., et al., Development and Validation of a Machine Learning Model to Predict⏎Near-Term Risk of Iatrogenic Hypoglycemia in Hospitalized Patients. JAMA Netw Open, 2021.⏎4(1): p. e2030913.⏎23. Ogut, E., Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Medicine: Challenges Across Diagnostic Imaging, Clinical⏎Decision Support, Surgery, Pathology, and Drug Discovery. Clin Pract, 2025. 15(9).⏎24. Hundur, M., et al., Advancement of post-market surveillance of medical devices leveraging⏎artificial intelligence: ECG devices case study. Technol Health Care, 2025. 33(4): p. 1818-1826.⏎25. El Arab, R.A., et al., Bridging the Gap: From AI Success in Clinical Trials to Real-World Healthcare⏎Implementation-A Narrative Review. Healthcare (Basel), 2025. 13(7).⏎26. Chew, B.H. and K.Y. Ngiam, Artificial intelligence tool development: what clinicians need to⏎know? BMC Med, 2025. 23(1): p. 244.⏎27. Moy, S., et al., Patient Perspectives on the Use of Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: A Scoping⏎Review. J Patient Cent Res Rev, 2024. 11(1): p. 51-62.⏎28. Organization, W.H., Ethics and governance of artificial intelligence for health. 2021.⏎29. Osonuga, A., et al., Bridging the digital divide: artificial intelligence as a catalyst for health equity⏎in primary care settings. Int J Med Inform, 2025. 204: p. 106051.⏎30. Jain, S.S., M.M. Mello, and N.H. Shah, Avoiding Financial Toxicity for Patients from Clinicians' Use⏎of AI. N Engl J Med, 2024. 391(13): p. 1171-1173.⏎31. Ruan, Y., et al., Predicting the Risk of Inpatient Hypoglycemia With Machine Learning Using⏎Electronic Health Records. Diabetes Care, 2020. 43(7): p. 1504-1511.⏎32. Barnes, A., et al., Investigation of artificial intelligence-based clinical decision support system's⏎performance in reducing the fine needle aspiration rate of thyroid nodules: A pilot study.⏎Ultrasound, 2025. 33(4): p. 282-290.⏎⏎33. Fernandez Velasco, P., et al., Clinical Evaluation of an Artificial Intelligence-Based Decision⏎Support System for the Diagnosis and American College of Radiology Thyroid Imaging Reporting⏎and Data System Classification of Thyroid Nodules. Thyroid, 2024. 34(4): p. 510-518.⏎34. Tessler, F.N. and J. Thomas, Artificial Intelligence for Evaluation of Thyroid Nodules: A Primer.⏎Thyroid, 2023. 33(2): p. 150-158.⏎35. Wu, W.T., et al., Economic evaluation of AI-assisted technologies in healthcare: A systematic⏎review. J Food Drug Anal, 2025. 33(4): p. 487-500.⏎36. Wolf, R.M., et al., Cost-effectiveness of Autonomous Point-of-Care Diabetic Retinopathy⏎Screening for Pediatric Patients With Diabetes. JAMA Ophthalmol, 2020. 138(10): p. 1063-1069.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0107♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from General Catalyst Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔General Catalyst Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔generalcatalyst.com/institute⏎GCI Response to HHS Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Submitted electronically to Regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Executive Summary⏎The General Catalyst Institute (GCI) appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department⏎of Health and Human Services' Request for Information regarding the acceleration of artificial⏎intelligence adoption in clinical care. As an organization dedicated to promoting market-driven⏎solutions powered by applied AI to transform healthcare delivery, we believe our insights and⏎the experiences of our portfolio companies can provide valuable perspectives on regulatory⏎modernization, reimbursement reform, and research priorities that will position the United States⏎as the global leader in healthcare AI innovation.⏎We are encouraged by HHS' "OneHHS" AI Strategy and the unprecedented alignment across⏎federal agencies to harness the transformative potential of AI while safeguarding patient privacy⏎and civil liberties. The United States stands at a critical inflection point where strategic policy⏎decisions made today will define our global leadership and reinforce the resilience of our health⏎systems for decades to come. By addressing the critical levers of regulation, reimbursement,⏎and research & development, HHS can reduce the uncertainty that currently impedes innovation⏎and align federal incentives to enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower healthcare costs, and⏎improve health outcomes.⏎Our message is simple: the tools exist. The talent exists. The policy pathways must now⏎align to ensure these assets can scale equitably and sustainably across every level of the⏎healthcare system.⏎Company Information⏎The General Catalyst Institute was launched to promote and strengthen national resilience by⏎backing transformative technologies and shaping public policies that improve society. Our⏎mission is to cultivate a healthy ecosystem for entrepreneurship and serve as a partner to⏎policymakers on technology adoption, with a particular emphasis on applied AI in healthcare.⏎Central to this commitment to long-term transformation is our health assurance mission to⏎create a more affordable, accessible, proactive, and equitable system of care by leveraging⏎innovative technology, new business and payment models, and human capital. We execute this⏎vision through a unique three-pronged approach: investing in innovative portfolio companies,⏎⏎partnering with 27+ health systems and payers, and operating the Health Assurance⏎Transformation Company (HATCo) as a blueprint for systemic change.⏎Our Catalyzing Care: A Framework for a Healthier America, introduced in the “U.S. Healthcare⏎That Works” report, outlines five pillars for healthcare transformation:⏎1. Foster Healthier Outcomes for All⏎2. Refine Needed Innovations Without Red Tape⏎3. Advance Patient-First Care, Data and Agency⏎4. Maximize Fiscal Responsibility for U.S. Healthcare⏎5. Enhance U.S. Medical Talent for Today and Tomorrow⏎Each of these pillars has associated short-term and long-term policy recommendations that⏎directly inform our response to this RFI. If implemented, the impact for real people would be⏎remarkable: doctors could save thousands more lives each year, patients in rural and⏎underserved communities could connect with specialists regardless of location through⏎AI-enhanced care networks, and hundreds of thousands of unnecessary hospitalizations could⏎be prevented through proactive, AI-enabled interventions.⏎Bottom Line Up Front⏎The U.S. healthcare system stands at a historic inflection point. After decades of fragmentation,⏎rising costs, and uneven outcomes, we now have the technological capabilities to fundamentally⏎transform healthcare delivery through market-driven solutions powered by applied AI. The⏎General Catalyst Institute believes HHS can accelerate this transformation by focusing on five⏎critical areas where our ecosystem has developed valuable insights.⏎First, we have concrete examples of what's already working. Our portfolio includes⏎companies demonstrating that AI can achieve clinical outcomes equivalent or superior to⏎traditional care while reducing costs by 50-60%, reclaiming 15-20 hours per week of clinician⏎time consumed by administrative burden, and enabling earlier detection of time-sensitive⏎conditions that prevent hundreds of thousands of dollars per patient in downstream costs.⏎These proven applications (spanning radiology analysis, clinical documentation, chronic disease⏎management, remote patient monitoring, and generative AI for healthcare communications) are⏎ready for widespread deployment but face regulatory uncertainty, reimbursement misalignment,⏎and interoperability barriers that HHS can address.⏎Second, we can identify the specific blocking factors that impede scale. Through our⏎portfolio companies' experiences deploying AI across diverse healthcare settings, we have⏎documented where current regulations create ambiguity (such as unclear FDA oversight⏎boundaries for clinical decision support tools versus medical devices), where payment models⏎misalign with AI's value proposition (fee-for-service structures that don't reward prevention or⏎efficiency gains), and where data fragmentation prevents AI development (siloed EHR systems⏎requiring custom integration work that can take 12-18 months per health system). These⏎barriers are solvable through targeted policy reforms we detail in our responses.⏎2⏎⏎Third, we understand the incentive structures needed to drive adoption. Expanding⏎value-based payment models creates natural demand for AI tools that improve outcomes and⏎efficiency; when payers take financial risk for long-term health costs, they have inherent⏎incentives to promote high-value AI interventions. Mandating interoperability standards and⏎creating data commons would further reduce AI development costs by 40-60%, democratizing⏎innovation beyond well-capitalized companies and enabling startups to compete on algorithmic⏎merit rather than integration resources.⏎To capture these opportunities, HHS should foster competitive ecosystems of private sector⏎innovation rather than mandating top-down solutions. This includes supporting industry-driven⏎accreditation bodies that can move with the speed of innovation, creating regulatory safe⏎harbors for proven AI applications, and investing in shared infrastructure (such as federated⏎learning networks, benchmark datasets, and standardized testing environments) that reduces⏎barriers to entry while maintaining rigorous oversight.⏎AI offers a fundamental shift from clinical scarcity (too few providers, too little time, too⏎little access) to clinical abundance, where technology extends the reach of every⏎clinician and makes high-quality care available to far more patients. By aligning regulation,⏎reimbursement, and R&D investments, we can reduce the uncertainty that impedes innovation,⏎foster public trust in modern technology, and ensure AI is deployed in ways that enhance⏎productivity, lower costs, and improve outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities⏎across America. The General Catalyst Institute and our ecosystem stand ready to partner with⏎HHS, providing coordination support for pilots and testbeds and ongoing feedback to ensure⏎HHS programs meet evolving technology and patient needs.⏎What follows is a table of our Detailed Responses to the HHS RFI. In addition, at the end⏎of our response is a table of acronyms employed.⏎3⏎⏎Detailed Responses⏎RFI GCI Recommendation Supporting Evidence Expected Impact⏎Question⏎Question 1: HHS should address three AI-powered radiology solutions 