rs. The expected outcome is more⏎the literature clinicians, patients, and AI varying health literacy can understand. reliable AI systems maintaining performance across⏎approach the systems. The entire industry would benefit from diverse populations and care settings.⏎costs, shared research frameworks and⏎benefits, and (3) AI Safety and Robustness publicly available benchmarks for⏎transfers of Under Distribution Shift: evaluating clinical safety of generative⏎using AI as Healthcare data distributions shift AI systems.⏎part of constantly as patient populations⏎clinical care? change and clinical practices Healthcare infrastructure work has⏎evolve. Research should develop demonstrated the challenge of⏎methods for detecting when AI distribution shift. AI models sometimes⏎model performance degrades due perform inconsistently when deployed⏎to distribution shift and techniques across different healthcare settings due⏎for adapting models to new to differences in how data is recorded⏎distributions. and how clinical workflows operate.⏎Research on automated methods for⏎continuously monitoring AI performance⏎would enable more robust AI⏎deployments across the United States.⏎17⏎⏎Conclusion⏎The full alignment across HHS agencies through OneHHS presents a historic opportunity to⏎transform U.S. healthcare through AI innovation, and the tools and talent to do so already exist.⏎By establishing tiered regulatory frameworks, expanding value-based payment models,⏎mandating interoperability, supporting industry-driven certification, and investing in critical⏎research, HHS can foster a competitive marketplace that accelerates innovation while protecting⏎patient interests and improving outcomes across all demographics and care settings.⏎Our message is simple: the tools exist. The talent exists. The policy pathways must now⏎align to ensure these assets can scale across every level of the healthcare system.⏎The General Catalyst Institute and our ecosystem of portfolio companies stand ready to partner⏎with HHS in this work, bringing real-world data from tens of millions of patients and a track⏎record of health system partnerships to support pilots, testbeds, and ongoing feedback loops as⏎policy implementation advances.⏎Together, HHS and the private sector can shift the U.S. healthcare system toward clinical⏎abundance, ensuring the tools of modern medicine are distributed fairly, used safely, and⏎scaled effectively for every patient. We believe the moment is now and that partnership is the⏎way to make U.S. healthcare truly work for all.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎The General Catalyst Institute⏎800 Connective Ave NW, Suite 300⏎Washington, DC 20006⏎gcinstitute@generalcatalyst.com⏎18⏎⏎Table of Acronyms⏎Acronym Definition⏎ACO Accountable Care Organization⏎ADT Admission, Discharge, and Transfer⏎AG Attorney General⏎AI Artificial Intelligence⏎AHRQ Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality⏎API Application Programming Interface⏎APM Alternative Payment Model⏎ASTP Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎CFO Chief Financial Officer⏎CFR Code of Federal Regulations⏎CMIO Chief Medical Information Officer⏎CIO Chief Information Officer⏎CMS Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎CPT Current Procedural Terminology⏎CRADA Cooperative Research and Development Agreement⏎CT Computed Tomography⏎DEA Drug Enforcement Administration⏎DPC Data at the Point of Care⏎ED Emergency Department⏎EHI Electronic Health Information⏎EHR Electronic Health Record⏎EMR Electronic Medical Record⏎FDA Food and Drug Administration⏎19⏎⏎FHIR Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources⏎GCI General Catalyst Institute⏎HATCo Health Assurance Transformation Company⏎HHS Department of Health and Human Services⏎HIE Health Information Exchange⏎HIPAA Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act⏎HL7 Health Level Seven International⏎IT Information Technology⏎LCD Local Coverage Determination⏎MSSP Medicare Shared Savings Program⏎NCD National Coverage Determination⏎NIH National Institutes of Health⏎NIST National Institute of Standards and Technology⏎NQF National Quality Forum⏎NPPES National Plan and Provider Enumeration System⏎OMB Office of Management and Budget⏎ONC Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎PACS Picture Archiving and Communication System⏎R&D Research and Development⏎RFI Request for Information⏎RFP Request for Proposal⏎SDOH Social Determinants of Health⏎SNAP Supplemental Nutrition Assistance Program⏎USCDI United States Core Data for Interoperability⏎WIC Women, Infants, and Children (Program)⏎20♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0108♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Noeted Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Noeted Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔NOETED⏎Public Comment — Request for Information⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001 | Submitted February 2026⏎Submitted by: About NOETED:⏎Keith “Duv” Williams AI-powered healthcare compliance intelligence platform. Member, NVIDIA⏎Founder & CEO, Inception Program. Building centralized regulatory intelligence infrastructure⏎NOETED for U.S. healthcare organizations.⏎duv@noeted.com |⏎noeted.ai⏎Redding, California⏎Preamble⏎NOETED is an AI‑enabled healthcare compliance intelligence platform headquartered in California. We⏎are building what U.S. healthcare lacks today: a reliable regulatory intelligence layer that continuously⏎monitors, aggregates, and connects compliance requirements from federal agencies (CMS, OIG, FDA,⏎SAMHSA, ONC), state health departments, and regional regulators, and translates those signals into⏎actionable guidance for payers and providers.⏎Our perspective on this RFI reflects two things: (1) direct experience developing AI systems that must⏎operate inside the U.S. healthcare regulatory environment, and (2) hands‑on attempts to⏎programmatically consume federal and state regulatory data for large health systems, plans, and⏎safety‑net providers.⏎Across all ten questions, we return to a single, solvable root cause: the lack of modern, standardized⏎regulatory data infrastructure. Today, many APIs, databases, and publication systems for healthcare⏎rules are fragmented, inconsistently maintained, or simply absent. This forces organizations into⏎manual monitoring, slows responsible AI adoption, and ultimately harms patients when preventable⏎compliance failures disrupt care.⏎We therefore focus our recommendations on a sequenced approach: (1) modernize regulatory data⏎infrastructure; (2) standardize regulatory data formats; (3) embed regulatory intelligence in certification,⏎payment, and safe‑harbor frameworks; and (4) fund the research needed to evaluate AI systems that⏎depend on this infrastructure. NOETED is building a complementary private‑sector layer on top of such⏎an infrastructure, and we are eager to partner with ONC and HHS so that public and private efforts⏎reinforce each other.⏎⏎Q1 What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎NOETED Response⏎From NOETED’s experience, four barriers consistently slow responsible AI innovation and adoption in⏎clinical care:⏎Regulatory fragmentation and limited machine‑readability.⏎AI developers and health systems must comply with requirements from CMS, OIG, FDA, ONC, state⏎regulators, and others. These rules are published through heterogeneous channels (Federal Register,⏎PDFs, web pages, listservs) with no unified, machine‑readable structure. For many organizations—⏎especially FQHCs, small hospitals, and regional plans—this makes it practically impossible to maintain⏎a current, organization‑wide view of “what the rules actually are,” which in turn makes it risky to deploy⏎AI at scale.⏎Unclear and inconsistent reimbursement for AI‑enabled care.⏎In the absence of durable, technology‑neutral payment pathways for AI‑assisted services, investments⏎are skewed toward a narrow set of large systems and high‑margin use cases. Smaller and rural⏎providers, who could benefit disproportionately from AI support, are often unable to make a business⏎case for adoption.⏎Liability ambiguity for AI‑assisted decisions.⏎Existing frameworks do not clearly allocate responsibility among AI vendors, deploying organizations,⏎clinicians, and data suppliers when AI contributions are involved in clinical decisions. This ambiguity⏎encourages risk‑averse organizations to constrain AI to low‑stakes workflows, even where carefully⏎governed AI could materially improve safety, quality, and equity.⏎Regulatory data infrastructure that lags behind clinical data infrastructure.⏎Substantial progress has been made on clinical and administrative data interoperability. By contrast,⏎regulatory data—conditions of participation, coverage rules, enforcement guidance—is often published⏎through APIs that are undocumented or deprecated, datasets that lag real‑time changes, and formats⏎that vary widely across agencies. As an AI compliance platform, NOETED encounters this daily: we⏎spend disproportionate effort normalizing and validating public regulatory data that should already be⏎available in standardized, machine‑readable form. This raises costs, limits innovation, and leaves⏎smaller organizations unable to automate compliance monitoring at all.⏎HHS can have outsized impact by treating regulatory data infrastructure with the same seriousness as⏎clinical data infrastructure—because AI cannot be safe, equitable, or scalable if health care⏎organizations cannot reliably know the rules AI‑assisted workflows must follow.⏎⏎Q2 What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable CFR citations.⏎NOETED Response⏎NOETED recommends a sequenced set of actions that align with HHS’s goals for safe, equitable⏎AI in clinical care:⏎1. Modernize and standardize regulatory data publication across HHS.⏎Direct CMS, OIG, FDA, SAMHSA, ONC and related entities to publish regulatory requirements,⏎guidance, and change notices through maintained, documented, versioned APIs with common schema⏎and metadata (effective date, affected entity type, CFR citations, superseded guidance).⏎Use the Evidence Act and OMB A‑130 authorities to establish minimum publication standards for⏎regulatory data, analogous to existing expectations for clinical data interoperability.⏎Charge ONC with developing a “Regulatory Data Implementation Guide” aligned with FHIR, so that⏎certified health IT can consume regulatory signals in the same way it consumes clinical data.⏎2. Embed regulatory intelligence into certification criteria.⏎Extend ONC’s Health IT Certification Program to include baseline requirements for AI tools that⏎influence clinical workflows or compliance‑sensitive operations: documented regulatory data sources,⏎update mechanisms, and auditability.⏎Over time, require certified systems to demonstrate an ability to ingest and act on standardized⏎regulatory notifications.⏎3. Create durable reimbursement pathways for AI‑assisted services.⏎Under 42 CFR Part 414, establish technology‑neutral codes and coverage criteria for AI‑assisted⏎clinical services where evidence supports improved outcomes or efficiency, rather than relying solely on⏎contractor‑level decisions.⏎Prioritize use cases with clear equity impact (e.g., documentation support in safety‑net settings,⏎AI‑assisted chronic disease management in underserved populations).⏎4. Clarify safe harbors and HIPAA guidance for AI.⏎Update OIG safe harbors to explicitly address provision of AI tools to affiliated providers under⏎conditions similar to existing EHR safe harbors, enabling health systems to extend AI capabilities⏎without AKS uncertainty.⏎⏎Issue targeted HIPAA guidance (45 CFR §164.514) clarifying permissible uses of de‑identified and⏎synthetic data for AI training and evaluation, with plain‑language examples and guardrails.⏎5. Explicitly recognize regulatory intelligence infrastructure as a funding priority.⏎Through programs such as ONC’s LEAP in Health IT, explicitly invite proposals focused on regulatory⏎intelligence infrastructure—systems that help organizations monitor and respond to evolving⏎requirements in near real time. These platforms are a precondition for safe AI deployment in any⏎compliance‑sensitive clinical workflow.⏎Collectively, these actions would lower compliance uncertainty for innovators, reduce the burden on⏎providers, and make it far more realistic for organizations of all sizes to adopt AI tools with confidence.⏎Q3 For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist regarding AI⏎in clinical care (liability, indemnification, privacy, security)? What role should HHS play?⏎NOETED Response⏎Several novel issues arise when AI is used in clinical care outside the medical‑device framework,⏎particularly in compliance‑sensitive workflows:⏎1. Multi‑party liability for AI‑assisted decisions.⏎Today, it is unclear how responsibility is allocated among the AI developer, the deploying organization,⏎clinicians, and data suppliers when AI contributes to a clinical or operational error. This uncertainty⏎chills adoption, particularly in high‑stakes settings.⏎HHS can lead by developing, with DOJ and state partners, model liability frameworks that:⏎● Clarify roles and duties across the AI lifecycle.⏎● Create safe harbors for organizations that follow defined governance and monitoring practices.⏎● Encourage transparent incident reporting and learning instead of purely punitive responses.⏎⏎2. Compliance liability for AI recommendations.⏎In compliance use cases, organizations remain fully responsible when AI‑generated guidance is wrong,⏎yet they currently receive no recognition for using higher‑assurance tools or for following best‑practice⏎evaluation processes.⏎HHS could clarify, for example in OIG and CMS enforcement guidance, how reliance on certified or⏎credentialed AI tools factors into penalty calculations and corrective‑action expectations. This would⏎incentivize use of higher‑quality systems without shifting responsibility away from providers.⏎3. Source data reliability in regulatory AI.⏎For AI tools that depend on government‑published regulatory data, the quality and timeliness of that⏎data is itself a risk factor. If a government API is outdated or deprecated, an otherwise well‑governed AI⏎tool may produce incorrect recommendations.⏎HHS can address this by:⏎● Committing to service‑level expectations for key regulatory data feeds (timeliness, uptime,⏎change logs).⏎● Clarifying how documented reliance on official data sources will be treated in enforcement⏎discretion, especially where those sources can be shown to be incomplete or out of date.⏎4. Privacy in AI processing of unstructured data.⏎Large‑scale processing of notes, images, and voice introduces questions about how HIPAA’s minimum⏎necessary standard applies when models need broad context to function.⏎HHS should issue targeted guidance on AI handling of unstructured PHI, including expectations for⏎minimization, logging, access control, and secondary use (e.g., model training).⏎Recommended HHS role.⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to convene multi‑stakeholder working groups and convert their outputs into⏎practical tools: model policies, certification criteria, and updated guidance. Extending ONC’s⏎certification program to cover AI governance attributes (data provenance, monitoring, explainability, and⏎incident response) would provide a concrete mechanism to operationalize these legal and⏎implementation expectations.⏎⏎Q4 For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS support these? If so, which mechanisms?⏎NOETED Response⏎Pre‑deployment evaluation.⏎The most promising pre‑deployment approaches for non‑device clinical AI share three traits:⏎● They evaluate performance across demographically and clinically diverse subpopulations, not⏎just aggregate metrics.⏎● They test robustness under distribution shift (how models behave when “real‑world” data differs⏎from training data).⏎● They incorporate structured expert review of representative AI outputs before deployment.⏎For compliance and operations AI, pre‑deployment evaluation must also include regulatory data source⏎quality: what sources are used, how often they update, and how gaps or inconsistencies are handled. A⏎compliance AI tool cannot be safer than the regulatory data infrastructure it depends on.⏎Post‑deployment monitoring.⏎Today, systematic post‑deployment monitoring is rare. For AI in clinical and compliance workflows,⏎monitoring plans should:⏎● Track performance drift over time and across subpopulations.⏎● Detect bias emergence and unintended use cases.⏎● Incorporate automatic checks for changes in underlying regulatory or policy data that could⏎invalidate model assumptions.⏎For compliance AI specifically, this means monitoring both: (1) model outputs and (2) the freshness of⏎regulatory data sources—flagging when upstream data lags new rules.⏎Human‑centered evaluation.⏎⏎Technical metrics alone cannot show whether AI improves care or compliance. HHS should support⏎evaluations that measure:⏎● How clinicians and compliance staff actually use or override AI recommendations and why.⏎● Effects on workload, burnout, and error detection.⏎● Downstream impact on patient outcomes, access, and equity.⏎Recommended HHS mechanisms.⏎HHS can:⏎● Use cooperative agreements to fund multi‑site evaluations of AI tools in real clinical and⏎compliance workflows.⏎● Use prize challenges for well‑defined technical benchmarks (e.g., robustness under specified⏎shift scenarios).⏎● Create a Healthcare AI Evaluation Repository, a public (or tiered access) database where⏎evaluation results for deployed AI tools are reported in a comparable format, helping providers⏎and payers make informed procurement and governance decisions.⏎NOETED would welcome the opportunity to participate in such evaluations specifically for compliance⏎and regulatory intelligence use cases, contributing both tooling and real‑world evaluation data.⏎⏎Q5 How can HHS best support private sector activities (accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎NOETED Response⏎HHS can accelerate safe AI adoption by aligning federal levers with emerging private‑sector⏎accreditation and certification efforts:⏎1. Extend ONC Health IT Certification to non‑device AI tools.⏎Expand 45 CFR Part 170 to include criteria for AI systems that directly influence clinical workflows or⏎compliance‑sensitive operations (e.g., documentation assistants, clinical decision support, compliance⏎intelligence tools).⏎Criteria should address data provenance, bias and robustness testing, interoperability with certified⏎EHRs, update mechanisms for clinical and regulatory changes, and post‑deployment monitoring.⏎2. Recognize industry‑driven credentialing frameworks.⏎Professional bodies (e.g., HIMSS, AHIMA, and AI‑focused consortia) are designing voluntary AI⏎credentials. HHS can create a formal “recognized credentialing framework” list, akin to how NCQA⏎accreditation is recognized in Medicare/Medicaid programs.⏎Such recognition would steer the market toward higher standards without requiring exhaustive federal⏎rulemaking for every AI use case.⏎3. Link certification/credentialing to enforcement incentives.⏎In CMS and OIG enforcement guidance, clarify that healthcare organizations using AI tools that meet⏎ONC certification or recognized credentialing can receive defined consideration (e.g., in penalty⏎calculations or corrective‑action planning) when AI‑assisted errors occur.⏎This does not shift responsibility away from providers, but rewards organizations that choose⏎higher‑assurance tools and follow best‑practice governance.⏎4. Make regulatory intelligence integration a medium‑term certification expectation.⏎⏎Once standardized regulatory data publication infrastructure exists, ONC could require that certified AI⏎tools demonstrate an ability to detect and adapt to relevant regulatory changes via those standardized⏎feeds.⏎This would ensure that certified tools remain aligned with current rules, not just the rules in effect at the⏎time of their initial evaluation.⏎NOETED is designing its platform to be “certification‑ready” along these dimensions and would⏎welcome collaboration with ONC and credentialing bodies to pilot certification criteria specifically for⏎regulatory and compliance AI.⏎⏎Q6 Where have AI tools in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations, and where have they fallen short? What novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, quality insights, and cost⏎reduction?⏎NOETED Response⏎Areas where AI has met or exceeded expectations.⏎● Ambient clinical documentation: AI‑assisted transcription and note generation has shown clear⏎value in reducing clinician burden without requiring autonomous clinical judgment. Adoption has⏎been strongest where integration with EHR workflows is tight and governance is clear.⏎● Imaging and narrow diagnostic tasks: In radiology and pathology, AI models trained on⏎well‑curated datasets have demonstrated high performance on specific classification tasks, with⏎emerging evidence of real‑world impact when embedded thoughtfully into workflows.⏎Areas where AI has fallen short.⏎● Generalization and equity: Performance often degrades for under‑represented populations⏎(racial/ethnic minorities, rural patients, rare conditions), especially when training data do not reflect⏎real‑world diversity.⏎● Dynamic regulatory and policy environments: AI tools that touch billing, utilization management,⏎or compliance often lag behind rule changes. Here NOETED has seen two failure modes:⏎● Update lag: Developers or implementers do not update logic promptly when rules change.⏎● Infrastructure failures: Even when developers try to update, the necessary regulatory data are not⏎available in timely, machine‑readable forms. This is especially problematic for smaller organizations⏎that lack dedicated regulatory intelligence staff.⏎High‑potential AI tools for outcomes, insight, and cost.⏎● Regulatory intelligence and compliance monitoring: AI systems that continuously track⏎regulatory changes, map impacts across an organization’s obligations, and surface actionable⏎guidance for compliance, risk, and clinical operations. These tools are “infrastructure AI” that make⏎other AI deployments safer.⏎● Predictive compliance and integrity risk scoring: Models that identify organizations, service⏎lines, or populations at elevated risk of quality or compliance failures before they manifest as⏎⏎adverse events, denials, or enforcement. When paired with supportive interventions, this can⏎improve safety and reduce avoidable cost.⏎● AI‑assisted care coordination and prior authorization: Tools that align clinical documentation,⏎coverage rules, and medical necessity criteria in real time, reducing administrative friction and⏎delays in care.⏎HHS support for these categories—through pilots, reimbursement innovation, and inclusion in⏎interoperability and certification efforts—would help direct AI innovation toward areas with high patient and⏎system benefit, not just administrative efficiency.⏎⏎Q7 Which roles, decision makers, or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on AI adoption? What are the primary administrative hurdles?⏎NOETED Response⏎From NOETED’s perspective, the most promising AI uses for improving care, equity, and access share⏎three attributes: they reduce preventable variation, they make complex rules navigable for frontline⏎teams, and they are explicitly designed to work in resource‑constrained settings. Examples include:⏎● AI‑supported guideline and coverage alignment at the point of care⏎● Tools that help clinicians document and order in ways that are simultaneously clinically⏎appropriate and aligned with coverage criteria, reducing avoidable denials and care⏎delays—especially important for safety‑net providers and Medicaid populations.⏎● Proactive compliance and quality risk detection⏎● AI that identifies emerging patterns (documentation gaps, ordering patterns, access⏎bottlenecks) that put certain populations at higher risk of poor outcomes or coverage⏎disruptions, enabling targeted interventions before harm occurs.⏎● Navigation and communication tools tailored for underserved communities⏎● AI‑driven patient communication, translation, and navigation tools designed with⏎community input, that respect cultural and linguistic needs and help patients understand⏎and act on care plans and benefits.⏎HHS can promote these uses by:⏎● Prioritizing them in demonstration projects and payment innovation models.⏎● Including equity and access metrics as core evaluation endpoints in AI funding opportunities.⏎● Ensuring that regulatory and data infrastructure investments explicitly consider the needs of⏎rural, tribal, and safety‑net organizations that lack extensive IT and compliance staff.⏎NOETED’s regulatory intelligence work is directly aligned with this: smaller organizations benefit the⏎most when complex rules are translated into clear, actionable guidance that can be operationalized⏎without large legal departments.⏎⏎Q8 Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate AI development for clinical care? Consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎NOETED Response⏎Interoperability has unlocked new AI possibilities wherever standardized data exist (e.g., FHIR for⏎clinical data). To widen opportunities and accelerate AI, HHS should prioritize a third domain alongside⏎clinical and administrative data: regulatory data interoperability.⏎Current gaps.⏎● Regulatory content—conditions of participation, coverage decisions, quality specifications,⏎compliance guidance, enforcement summaries—is often available only as narrative documents⏎or ad hoc web content.⏎● APIs, where they exist, use inconsistent structures and are not uniformly documented or⏎maintained.⏎● As a result, AI tools that depend on “knowing the rules” must build custom scrapers and⏎mappers for each source. Smaller organizations cannot realistically do this.⏎Standards and infrastructure HHS could catalyze:⏎1. Standardized regulatory change notifications⏎● A common schema (potentially an implementation guide aligned with FHIR resources)⏎for publishing regulatory and policy change events, with required metadata: effective⏎date, affected entity types, relevant citations, superseded guidance, and unique⏎identifiers.⏎2. Structured program and quality specifications⏎● Publishing measure definitions, participation requirements, and program guidance as⏎versioned, machine‑readable resources rather than PDFs.⏎3. Enforcement and oversight data⏎● Providing de‑identified, structured data on enforcement actions and recurring findings to⏎support AI‑driven risk modeling and benchmarking.⏎⏎Recommended HHS actions:⏎● Direct ONC to work with CMS, OIG, and other agencies on a Regulatory Data Implementation⏎Guide, leveraging FHIR where appropriate.⏎● Treat regulatory data infrastructure as a core component of interoperability roadmaps and⏎implementation funding.⏎With such standards in place, private‑sector platforms like NOETED can build robust, reusable⏎regulatory intelligence capabilities that every health system, plan, and safety‑net provider can⏎leverage—dramatically lowering the barrier to safe, compliant AI deployment.⏎⏎Q9 What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI in clinical⏎care? What concerns do they have?⏎NOETED Response⏎Safety in AI‑assisted clinical care depends on more than model performance metrics; it requires⏎governance, transparency, and continuous alignment with evolving standards. HHS can strengthen⏎safety by:⏎● Requiring risk‑appropriate governance frameworks.⏎● For higher‑risk uses (diagnosis, treatment selection, utilization management), require⏎documented governance structures: accountable owners, oversight committees, policies⏎for model selection, and procedures for handling incidents and overrides.⏎● Mandating documentation of data provenance and limitations.⏎● AI tools should clearly describe training data characteristics, known limitations, intended⏎use, and populations for which performance is uncertain, in language clinicians and⏎compliance teams can understand.⏎● Embedding safety checks into certification.⏎● ONC certification for AI‑enabled systems should include:⏎● Support for capturing clinician and staff feedback and overrides.⏎● Logging sufficient to reconstruct AI‑assisted decisions in the event of an adverse⏎event or audit.⏎● Mechanisms to suspend or adjust AI behavior when performance concerns arise.⏎● Aligning AI tools with regulatory intelligence.⏎● For compliance‑sensitive workflows, a safe AI tool must be able to detect when⏎relevant rules change and flag potential misalignment. This is where regulatory⏎data interoperability and platforms like NOETED are critical.⏎HHS’s role is to set these expectations through guidance, certification criteria, and funding conditions,⏎while allowing innovation in specific technical approaches. NOETED stands ready to support this by⏎demonstrating how regulatory intelligence can be integrated into AI governance for real‑world⏎compliance use cases.⏎⏎Q10 What areas of AI research should HHS prioritize to accelerate AI adoption in clinical⏎care? Are there published findings on AI tool impact and costs/benefits in clinical⏎care?⏎NOETED Response⏎HHS should prioritize AI research that addresses foundational infrastructure gaps, equity in⏎real‑world settings, and system‑level integration—areas where AI can deliver the greatest⏎clinical and economic impact. Specific priorities include:⏎1. Regulatory intelligence and compliance monitoring⏎● Research on AI systems that continuously ingest, normalize, and operationalize⏎regulatory, policy, and program data (CMS rules, OIG guidance, quality⏎specifications, enforcement patterns).⏎● Focus: mapping obligations to workflows, predicting compliance risk by⏎population/service line, and auto‑generating remediation plans.⏎● Impact: Enables safe AI deployment across compliance‑sensitive workflows⏎(documentation, utilization management, quality reporting) by ensuring tools stay⏎aligned with current rules.⏎2. Equity‑focused model robustness and generalization⏎● Research on techniques to ensure AI performance across under‑represented⏎demographics, geographies, and clinical contexts (rural patients, minority⏎populations, rare conditions).⏎● Focus: adversarial robustness testing, domain adaptation for distribution shift, and⏎synthetic data generation to augment limited real‑world datasets.⏎● Impact: Prevents AI from deepening disparities and supports equitable care in⏎safety‑net and rural settings.⏎3. AI‑assisted care coordination and access optimization⏎● Research on AI for prior authorization, documentation alignment with coverage⏎criteria, and navigation tools tailored to underserved communities.⏎● Focus: reducing administrative friction, aligning clinical decisions with coverage⏎realities, and supporting culturally/linguistically appropriate communication.⏎● Impact: Improves access, reduces delays, and lowers cost for Medicaid, Medicare⏎Advantage, and safety‑net populations.⏎4. Post‑deployment monitoring and continuous learning⏎● Research on automated mechanisms for detecting drift, bias emergence, and⏎performance degradation in deployed clinical AI, including clinician override⏎patterns and downstream outcomes.⏎● Focus: integration with regulatory change detection so models adapt to evolving⏎clinical and policy standards.⏎⏎Published findings on AI impact and costs/benefits⏎● Ambient documentation: Reduces clinician burden by 20–40% (e.g., Nuance⏎DAX, Suki); studies show 1.5–2.5x ROI from reclaimed time and improved⏎throughput.⏎● Imaging diagnostics: Systematic reviews show AI improves specificity by 5–15%⏎in radiology/pathology, with cost savings from reduced false positives (e.g., reduced⏎downstream procedures); ICERs often < $10,000/QALY.⏎● Chronic disease prediction: Models for AFib, diabetic retinopathy screening show⏎14–20% cost reduction and QALY gains (e.g., Xie et al., 2023; ICERs⏎~$1,100/QALY).⏎● System‑level savings: NBER (2023) estimates 5–10% U.S. healthcare spending⏎reduction ($200–400B annually) from broader AI adoption, primarily through⏎efficiency and reduced unnecessary care.⏎HHS can advance these priorities through LEAP funding, cooperative agreements for⏎multi‑site pilots, and prize challenges for well‑defined benchmarks. NOETED is prepared to⏎contribute regulatory intelligence capabilities to such research efforts.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎NOETED thanks HHS and ONC for this opportunity to contribute to the future of AI in clinical⏎care. Our recommendations center on a single, high‑leverage opportunity: treating regulatory⏎data infrastructure with the same seriousness as clinical data infrastructure. By modernizing⏎publication standards, embedding regulatory intelligence into certification and safe harbors, and⏎funding practical pilots, HHS can unlock AI’s potential to improve care, equity, and efficiency⏎across all provider types.⏎We are eager to partner with ONC and HHS to make this vision real—through pilots, standards⏎co‑development, and real‑world demonstrations of how regulatory intelligence enables safe,⏎scalable AI. Please contact us at duv@noeted.com to discuss collaboration opportunities.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Keith “Duv” Williams⏎Founder, NOETED⏎noeted.ai⏎duv@noeted.com⏎NOETED | Every Change. Every Connection. Noeted. | noeted.ai♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0109♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from 17 leading digital health stakeholders, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔17 leading digital health stakeholders♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. The Honorable Thomas Keane, M.D.⏎U.S. Secretary of Health and Human Services Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services National Coordinator for Health Information⏎200 Independence Ave. Southwest Technology⏎Washington, District of Columbia 20201 Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street Southwest, 7th Floor⏎Washington, District of Columbia 20024⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy and Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎We represent a diverse coalition of stakeholders that span the healthcare and technology sectors, all of⏎whom support the necessary expanded use of digital and connected health technologies. A growing⏎evidence base continues to demonstrate that the responsible use of safe and effective digital health⏎solutions produces better patient outcomes, reduces costs, augments population health management, and⏎improves the healthcare workforce experience.1 Digital health tools, increasingly powered by artificial⏎intelligence (AI), leverage patient-generated health data (PGHD) and include cloud-enabled solutions, to⏎reduce administrative burden, support medical and clinical decision-making, and chronic and acute care⏎management. The use of these tools is also vital in supporting unserved and underserved populations’⏎access to prevention, diagnosis, and treatment for both acute and chronic conditions.⏎Leveraging the tremendous potential of AI is critical to accomplishing the Administration’s goals of⏎empowering the American people with personalized solutions; equipping healthcare providers with better⏎and timely data about the patients they serve and improving health outcomes; and shifting the paradigm⏎for health care to a system that fosters prevention, wellness, and chronic disease management. Many AI⏎use cases, ranging from solving administrative/backend efficiencies to supportive clinical decisions, have⏎already demonstrated their capacity to advance the Quadruple Aim.⏎We are encouraged to see the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) continue to investigate⏎these goals, most recently with its request for information regarding accelerating the adoption of artificial⏎intelligence as part of clinical care.2 HHS is well-positioned to leverage its existing authority to ensure⏎that its policies propel the American healthcare system forward through its safe, effective, and responsible⏎uptake of AI. We encourage you to set policy aligned with the following principles:⏎● A Shared Responsibility for Quality and Efficacy: Across HHS, adopt a risk-based approach⏎to healthcare AI that tailors risk mitigation to the potential harms of intended and expected uses.⏎Responsibility for managing risks should be appropriately shared among developers, vendors, and⏎providers based on their knowledge of, and ability to address, those risks in alignment with⏎leading standards such as ISO/IEC 42001.3 HHS should prevent the improper or unfair shifting of⏎1 Bodenheimer, T., & Sinsky, C. (2014). "From Triple to Quadruple Aim: Care of the Patient Requires Care of the⏎Provider." Annals of Family Medicine, 12(6), 573-576.⏎2 https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/23/2025-23641/request-for-information-accelerating-the-⏎adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence-as-part-of⏎3 https://www.iso.org/standard/42001⏎1⏎⏎liability to those who rely on AI technologies in good faith to care for patients so as to encourage⏎innovation and adoption without introducing new risks for critical health AI value chains.⏎● Transparency and Explainability: In alignment with existing standards and best practices for⏎making appropriate information public, provide clear, risk-based communications that inform all⏎healthcare stakeholders about relevant data requirements, intended uses, limitations, target⏎populations, bias mitigation, and applications of AI tools. These communications should disclose⏎sufficient detail to help providers assess when a tool is appropriate for individual patients, clarify⏎whether the tool augments or automates clinical workflows, and specify compliance with all⏎applicable legal and regulatory requirements. Transparency is a prerequisite for AI adoption.⏎● Access and Affordability: Prioritize measures that will ensure digital health technologies and AI⏎systems in health care are accessible and affordable across research, health administration and⏎operations, population health, practice delivery improvement, and direct clinical care. Payment⏎and incentive policies must be in place to invest in building health AI infrastructure; preparing⏎personnel and training; and appropriately incenting the responsible uptake and consistent use of⏎AI tools demonstrated to advance the Quadruple Aim. Notably, CMS should modernize the way⏎AI software is categorized and supported, including AI enabled non-medical device technologies,⏎and provide support in Medicare for AI tools to improve Medicare beneficiaries’ experience and⏎care (as well as working with states to achieve a harmonized expansion of similar support for AI⏎in Medicaid). Further, CMS should make overdue modernizations to key disease prevention⏎programs (e.g., diabetes prevention) and support preventative healthcare by fully leveraging⏎digital health technologies tools, such as patient-facing wearables and AI-enabled services. AI⏎can play a central role in the transition to value-based care by providing essential population⏎health tools and providing enhanced scalability and patient support.⏎● Interoperability: Ease data access and improve interoperability, while protecting data privacy⏎and security, to foster cooperation, trust, and openness among patients, providers, health AI⏎technology developers, and researchers. A truly interoperable healthcare system is one that⏎enables and engages patients across multiple privacy-preserving platforms using open APIs,⏎enabling the secure integration of patient-generated health data into electronic health records.⏎Interoperability rules under the 21st Century Cures Act should enable clinicians’ access to⏎medical records and patients to get their data and provide it to other organizations, including for⏎research. The success of AI tools for precision medicine, population health, and clinical decision⏎support, all of which are key tools in addressing chronic diseases, depends on accessible and⏎interoperable data.⏎● Ethics: Given the longstanding, deeply rooted, and well-developed body of medical and⏎biomedical ethics, it will be critical to preserve existing and emerging ethical norms developed by⏎providers and healthcare professional organizations for broader adherence by technologists,⏎innovators, computer scientists, and those who use such systems. From design, development,⏎implementation, and to use, healthcare AI tools should reflect the practical, ethical, and⏎operational realities of patient care.⏎● Workforce: The United States faces a stark, and growing, healthcare workforce shortage.⏎Successful creation and deployment of AI-enabled technologies which help care providers⏎streamline tasks and meet the needs of their patients will be an essential part of addressing this⏎shortage. Policies should support user education and workforce development through AI⏎upskilling and strengthen the clinician-patient relationship.⏎● Education: Support educational efforts to increase AI literacy in healthcare, highlight successful⏎AI applications, and promote stakeholder engagement to keep policies responsive to new⏎opportunities and challenges. Education should be developed by healthcare professional⏎organizations in partnership with AI developers. Educating the public about how AI is used in⏎2⏎⏎their care increases transparency, fosters trust, and enables patients to make informed decisions⏎about their health.⏎● Collaboration: We strongly encourage you to leverage the public-private partnership framework⏎to collaborate with providers, patients, industry, research institutions, and government agencies to⏎advance the above and to more broadly drive safe and effective innovation in healthcare AI.⏎We appreciate your consideration of our input at this critical time for the American healthcare system and⏎the countless Americans that depend on it. We stand ready to assist you further in any way that we can.⏎Sincerely,⏎Alliance Tele-Med, LLC EmPowerYu⏎American Health Information Management Epic Reach, LLC⏎Association (AHIMA)⏎Howie Robotics⏎American Osteopathic Association⏎Life365, Inc.⏎Avella Physical Therapy Group⏎MiCare Path⏎Axeleos, Inc., Makers of Sentraeus360⏎Nova Insights Corp⏎College of Healthcare Information⏎Management Executives (CHIME) Oncoustics⏎Connected Health Initiative Rimidi⏎Dogtown Media UnaliWear, Inc.⏎CC: Dr. Mehmet Oz, Administrator, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎Dr. Marty Makary, Commissioner of Food and Drugs, U.S. Food and Drug Administration⏎Dr. Jay Bhattacharya, Director, National Institutes of Health⏎Paula M. Stannard, Director, Office for Civil Rights⏎Christi Grimm, Inspector General, Office of Inspector General⏎Amy Gleason, Administrator, United States DOGE Service⏎3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0110♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Veeva Systems, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Veeva Systems♔♔♔♔♔♔February, 19 2026⏎Dockets Management Staff⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Secretary⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Re: Docket No. 2025-23641 (90 FR 60108)⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Sir or Madam,⏎Veeva Systems appreciates the opportunity to provide comments to the Department of Health⏎and Human Services (HHS) in response to the Request for Information (RFI) on accelerating the⏎adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.⏎Veeva Systems is a global provider of cloud-based software for the life sciences industry,⏎supporting more than 1,000 pharmaceutical, biotechnology, and medical device organizations⏎worldwide. Our platforms support clinical research, regulatory submissions, quality management,⏎and real-world evidence generation. Veeva works extensively with organizations that are⏎building, evaluating, and deploying AI-enabled capabilities across regulated and clinical⏎workflows. Accordingly, our comments focus on AI used in clinical care and reflect our⏎experience with data standards, interoperability, governance, and operational implementation.⏎Veeva supports HHS’s “OneHHS” approach to artificial intelligence and commends the⏎Department’s efforts to establish a forward-leaning, predictable, and proportionate framework⏎that accelerates responsible AI adoption while preserving patient privacy, civil rights, and public⏎trust. Further, we agree with the Administration’s thoughts in America’s AI Action Plan that⏎healthcare has been “especially slow to adopt [AI] due to a variety of factors, including distrust or⏎lack of understanding of the technology, a complex regulatory landscape, and a lack of clear⏎governance and risk mitigation standards.” While we anticipate rapid acceleration due to AI in⏎areas such as drug discovery and care delivery, we believe that clinical trials and clinical⏎research will continue to be a bottleneck in the process of getting treatments to patients faster.⏎Accelerating and innovating in how clinical trials are performed, with a focus on the technology⏎used by research sites, will allow the promise of AI across healthcare to be more fully realized.⏎Our responses below, to a subset of the specific questions, are organized to reflect HHS’s stated⏎focus on regulation, reimbursement, and research and development.⏎Question 1: Biggest barriers to private sector innovation and adoption⏎The most significant barriers to innovation and adoption of AI in clinical care include challenges⏎related to workflow integration, governance and accountability, trust, and alignment with existing⏎clinical operations. Many AI tools struggle to fit cleanly into care delivery environments, creating⏎t +1 925 452 6500 f +1 925 452 6504 4280 Hacienda Drive, Pleasanton, CA 94588⏎⏎uncertainty for clinicians and administrators responsible for patient safety, compliance, and⏎oversight.⏎In addition to these well-recognized barriers, limitations in the clinical research and evidence-⏎generation ecosystem constrain how AI systems are developed, validated, and monitored before⏎and after deployment in care settings. While clinical research and clinical care serve distinct⏎functions, much of the evidence that informs clinical decision-making is generated within routine⏎care environments, including community-based sites, sponsor-led studies, hybrid care-research⏎settings, and real-world clinical workflows.⏎Clinical data used to train and evaluate AI models often lacks consistent structure, provenance,⏎and longitudinal linkage across research and care environments. Although interoperability in⏎routine clinical care has improved, clinical research data remains fragmented across sponsors,⏎sites, and technology platforms. This fragmentation limits the availability of representative, high-⏎quality datasets needed to support the safe, scalable, and trustworthy use of AI in clinical care.⏎Together, these factors create friction not only in deploying AI tools in care delivery, but also in⏎establishing the evidence base and ongoing oversight required to sustain trust, accountability,⏎and long-term adoption.⏎Question 2: Regulatory, payment, and programmatic changes to prioritize⏎HHS should prioritize regulatory and programmatic actions that strengthen the data and evidence⏎foundations upon which safe and effective AI use in clinical care depends, rather than focusing⏎primarily on prescriptive requirements for AI models themselves.⏎From a regulatory and programmatic perspective, HHS should continue aligning AI-related policy⏎with existing interoperability frameworks such as the United States Core Data for Interoperability⏎(USCDI) and HL7® FHIR®, while ensuring these frameworks adequately support research-⏎specific requirements, including data provenance, auditability, and linkage across care and⏎research settings. Clear and consistently applied expectations—when paired with common⏎standards, implementation guidance, and conformance mechanisms—can reduce friction for⏎organizations developing and evaluating AI systems intended for use in clinical care by limiting⏎interpretive variation and redundant data transformation.⏎HHS should also consider whether greater consistency and transparency in clinical research⏎technology would support downstream AI adoption in care delivery. In contrast to routine clinical⏎care—where EHR certification has helped establish baseline interoperability and security⏎expectations—clinical research technologies remain highly fragmented. Exploring voluntary,⏎standards-based approaches to interoperability and data quality for modern clinical research⏎systems could improve the reliability and representativeness of data used to train, validate, and⏎monitor AI deployed in clinical settings.⏎While reimbursement policy influences the pace of adoption, particularly for provider-facing tools,⏎the ability of AI to deliver sustained value in clinical care ultimately depends on the quality,⏎2⏎⏎traceability, and governance of the underlying data. Programmatic investments that strengthen⏎research-to-care data flows would therefore have a durable impact across regulatory, clinical,⏎and operational contexts.⏎Question 5: Supporting private-sector accreditation and certification⏎HHS can support effective and innovative use of AI in clinical care by establishing clear, federally⏎led expectations for interoperability, security, and data quality in the clinical research⏎technologies that generate evidence supporting the development, approval, and use of⏎medicines in care delivery.⏎Routine clinical care has benefited from government-defined certification programs that created⏎consistent baseline expectations across a fragmented technology market. By contrast, clinical⏎research technologies—despite producing data that informs regulatory decisions, clinical⏎guidelines, and increasingly AI-enabled decision support—operate without a comparable, widely⏎adopted federal framework.⏎HHS should consider whether a government-developed certification or conformance program for⏎modern clinical research systems could strengthen the structure, provenance, and auditability of⏎research-derived data used to develop, validate, and monitor AI deployed in clinical care. Such⏎an approach would not regulate AI models themselves, but would improve consistency across⏎the upstream data infrastructure on which clinical AI depends. Over time, this could reduce⏎redundant data transformation, support more representative datasets, and increase trust in AI-⏎enabled clinical decision support.⏎Question 6: Where AI has met or fallen short of expectations⏎From Veeva’s perspective, AI has met expectations where use cases are narrowly defined,⏎operationally integrated, and supported by reliable underlying data. These include applications⏎that streamline administrative processes, support clinical operations, or analyze population-level⏎trends without directly substituting for clinical judgment.⏎Conversely, AI has fallen short where underlying data is incomplete, poorly governed, or⏎insufficiently representative, limiting the ability to validate performance and sustain trust over⏎time. In many cases, these shortcomings reflect upstream gaps in the generation,⏎standardization, and reuse of clinical research and real-world evidence rather than limitations of⏎AI techniques themselves.⏎AI tools with the greatest potential to improve outcomes and reduce burden are those that are⏎grounded in high-quality evidence, transparently governed, and designed to complement existing⏎clinical workflows—particularly where they support the safe and effective use of medicines in⏎real-world care settings.⏎Question 8: Role of interoperability in accelerating AI development⏎Enhanced interoperability would accelerate the responsible development and use of AI in clinical⏎3⏎⏎care by strengthening the connection between clinical care delivery and the clinical research⏎activities that generate evidence supporting the use of medicines in practice. While existing⏎interoperability frameworks have meaningfully improved data exchange for treatment purposes,⏎opportunities remain to better support innovation, research, and evidence generation that⏎increasingly occur within routine care environments.⏎Frameworks such as TEFCA, together with standards like HL7® FHIR® and USCDI, could play a⏎larger role in reducing fragmentation between clinical care and clinical research. HHS should⏎consider whether expanding TEFCA to more explicitly support research exchange purposes⏎would enable secure, standardized access to clinical data for research activities embedded in⏎care delivery, including cohort identification, trial feasibility, and evidence generation.⏎By enabling research exchange at scale, TEFCA could support AI-enabled identification of more⏎diverse and representative patient populations across community-based and rural care settings,⏎helping address long-standing challenges in trial participation and data representativeness. Over⏎time, this would improve the quality of data used to develop, validate, and monitor AI systems⏎that inform the use of medicines in clinical care.⏎In a future state, stronger interoperability between research and care systems could also enable⏎insights generated through clinical research and real-world evidence to be more rapidly⏎translated back into care delivery, supporting AI-enabled clinical decision support that reflects up-⏎to-date evidence and real-world patient populations.⏎Conclusion⏎Veeva supports HHS’s efforts to accelerate the responsible adoption of artificial intelligence in⏎clinical care. As AI is increasingly used to inform clinical decision-making and the use of⏎medicines, its safety, effectiveness, and trustworthiness will depend not only on model⏎performance, but on the quality, interoperability, and governance of the clinical research and⏎evidence-generation systems that underpin it.⏎HHS has a meaningful opportunity to advance AI adoption by strengthening the data foundations⏎that connect clinical research and clinical care, including through clear, federally led expectations⏎for interoperability, data quality, and consistency across modern clinical research technologies.⏎Targeted action in these areas can improve the representativeness, reliability, and reuse of⏎evidence used to develop, validate, and monitor AI systems in real-world care settings.⏎Veeva appreciates the opportunity to provide focused input on these issues and welcomes⏎continued engagement with HHS as it considers policy approaches that support innovation while⏎maintaining trust, accountability, and patient safety.⏎4⏎⏎If you have any questions regarding these comments, please contact me at⏎Anthony.Corso@veeva.com or (614) 401-2914.⏎Sincerely,⏎Anthony Corso⏎Vice President, Public Policy⏎Veeva Systems Inc.⏎(614) 401-2914⏎Anthony.Corso@veeva.com⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0111♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Joshua♔Thomas♔♔Illinois♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist of eleven years retired in 2022, and since then I've been seen at a practice in Naperville that has me fill out a tablet questionnaire in the waiting room before I even talk to a nurse. Every single visit. And when my potassium came back borderline low this past March, nobody called. I found out at midnight reading the patient portal on my own, just a number with a little flag next to it and no context whatsoever. No explanation of what it meant, what I should eat, whether I needed to come back in. That's not an AI failure exactly, but it's what technology-first medicine has looked like from where I'm sitting, and I think about that when I read something like this request for information about accelerating AI in clinical care.⏎⏎What worries me about all this acceleration talk is something pretty specific. My records are a mess. I've been seen at three different health systems over the past decade, I had a hospitalization at Edward-Elmhurst in 2019 that I'm not sure ever got fully pulled into my current chart, and my medication list has had the same outdated entry for a blood pressure drug I stopped taking in 2020 sitting right there uncorrected for years. Nobody's fixed it. Now I keep reading that these AI diagnostic tools are trained on large clean datasets, and I think, well, my records are nothing like that. They're incomplete and inconsistent and full of old information that never got updated. So when an AI tool looks at my chart and flags something or doesn't flag something, how would it even know what it's working with?⏎⏎That's the thing I don't think gets talked about honestly enough. The demos probably look great. A tidy patient with a complete record and consistent lab history going back ten years. That's not most people I'd guess, especially not older patients who've moved around or changed insurance or had gaps in care. The real world is messier and I don't see how you accelerate adoption without first being honest about that gap.⏎⏎The other thing that concerns me is when doctors start trusting the output and stop second-guessing the way they used to. I'm not saying doctors are careless, but there's something about having a system tell you the answer that I think changes how hard you look yourself. My sister's husband is a retired internist and he said something once about how residents trained with certain diagnostic software started skipping steps they used to do manually, not because they were lazy but because the tool said it was fine so they moved on. That was years before any of this current AI wave. I can't remember exactly when he mentioned that but the point stuck with me. When the AI is wrong, that might be precisely the moment nobody's paying close enough attention anymore to catch it.⏎⏎I don't know what HHS can require exactly or what's realistic in terms of regulation. But I'd feel a lot better if there was some kind of plain-language requirement that patients get told when AI was involved in a recommendation and what the system did and didn't have access to in terms of records. Not a legal disclosure buried in paperwork. Something a 75-year-old can actually read and understand before they accept a printout and go home. Because right now I genuinely cannot tell what's a doctor's judgment and what came off a screen.⏎⏎And honestly somebody should have called me about that potassium level.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0112♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions♔♔♔♔♔♔SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎SOCIETY FOR CARDIOVASCULAR ANGIOGRAPHY AND⏎INTERVENTIONS⏎Response to the Department of Health and Human Services⏎Request for Information:⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Care⏎February 2026⏎Response from SCAI Advocacy Committee AI Task Force⏎Page 1⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Authors:⏎1. Kusum Lata, MD, FSCAI⏎Interventional Cardiology,⏎Board of Trustees, Society of Cardiovascular & Angiography, Member of Medicare⏎Evidence Development & Coverage, Advisory Committee (MEDCAC), Sutter Gould⏎Medical Group, Sutter Health, Tracy, CA email: lata.drkusum@gmail.com⏎(Corresponding author)⏎2. Afnan Tariq, MD,⏎Division of Cardiology, Department of Internal Medicine, University of California Irvine,⏎Orange, California email: artariq@uci.edu⏎(Corresponding author)⏎3. Deepali Nivas Tukaye, MBBS. PhD. FSCAI FACC⏎Interventional Cardiology & Vascular Medicine⏎4. Raghava Velagaleti, MD⏎Division of Cardiology, Signature Healthcare, Brockton, Massachusetts⏎5. Islam Abudayyeh, MD, MPH⏎Section of Cardiology, VA Loma Linda Healthcare System, Loma Linda, California;⏎Department of Medicine, Charles R. Drew University, Los Angeles, California⏎6. Arnold Seto, MD, FSCAI,⏎Division of Cardiology, Department of Medicine, Tibor Rubin VA Medical Center, Long⏎Beach, CA, USA.⏎Page 2⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Executive Summary⏎SCAI represents over 5,000 interventional cardiologists whose members already use AI in⏎clinical practice—including AI-assisted coronary physiology, procedural imaging, and⏎echocardiographic analysis. We support AI adoption in clinical care and offer the following⏎perspectives:⏎1. AI works best as a physician’s tool. The most successful clinical AI applications⏎generate information that a licensed professional interprets and acts on. That model⏎should guide adoption.⏎2. Reimbursement should reflect the work AI creates. AI generates new data requiring⏎physician interpretation. Payment policy should recognize this as the professional act it⏎is.⏎3. Liability clarity benefits everyone. Developers gain regulatory certainty, clinicians gain⏎reasonable and appropriate protections, and patients retain a clear framework for⏎accountability.⏎4. Specialty societies can help. Professional societies are positioned to develop practical⏎appropriate use guidance within their clinical domains, as they already do for procedures⏎and therapies.⏎5. Transparency and monitoring build trust. Labeling AI involvement in care and⏎establishing adverse event reporting are practical steps that protect patients and accelerate⏎confidence in these tools.⏎6. SCAI is available to work with HHS directly on developing practical guidance for AI⏎in cardiovascular care and clinical care more broadly.⏎Page 3⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Preamble⏎The Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions (SCAI) appreciates the⏎opportunity to respond to this Request for Information. SCAI represents over 5,000⏎interventional cardiologists and cardiovascular professionals who work daily at the intersection⏎of advanced technology and high-acuity patient care.⏎Interventional cardiology has adopted new technologies responsibly for decades: from balloon⏎angioplasty to transcatheter valve therapies. Our members are not speculating about AI in⏎clinical care; they are already using it. AI-assisted quantitative coronary analysis, CT-derived⏎fractional flow reserve, automated echocardiographic assessment, and three-dimensional⏎reconstruction for structural heart planning are part of current practice. This experience informs⏎our perspective.⏎SCAI supports the responsible adoption of AI in clinical care. The responses that follow offer⏎practical perspectives on how to accelerate that adoption while addressing the barriers that⏎currently slow it.⏎Page 4⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 1⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Several interconnected barriers impede both development and responsible deployment of AI in⏎clinical care.⏎Data fragmentation. Patient data remain siloed across EMR systems, claims databases, imaging⏎archives, and research repositories. Variability in format, quality, and volume between⏎institutions limits generalizability. Smaller practices and rural health systems are often excluded⏎from AI development entirely because their data infrastructure cannot support it.⏎Regulatory and liability ambiguity. The current landscape does not clearly delineate⏎responsibility when AI influences a clinical decision that results in patient harm. The⏎professional accountability framework in medicine is well established: the licensed clinician is⏎responsible. But when an AI tool contributes to a decision, and the clinician had no role in⏎designing, validating, or choosing that tool, the assignment of responsibility becomes unclear.⏎Technology developers operate under liability protections that were not designed for clinical⏎consequences. This ambiguity discourages adoption by the very professionals who would need to⏎use these tools.⏎Algorithmic opacity. Most clinical AI operates as an associative model whose internal logic is⏎not transparent to clinicians. Licensed professionals are trained to reason from evidence,⏎document their rationale, and defend their decisions. Acting on opaque algorithmic⏎recommendations is fundamentally at odds with how professional clinical judgment is exercised⏎and evaluated.⏎Workflow integration. AI systems are often designed without adequate understanding of⏎clinical workflows. Tools that generate excessive alerts, require parallel documentation, or⏎interrupt established care pathways create friction rather than efficiency. In high-acuity settings⏎like the catheterization laboratory, poorly integrated AI could compromise rather than enhance⏎patient safety.⏎Absence of recognized validation standards. Outside the FDA medical device pathway, there⏎is no broadly adopted standard for evaluating AI tools used in clinical care. Every licensed⏎professional must demonstrate competency through examination, supervised training, and⏎ongoing certification. The tools they rely on to make clinical decisions should be held to a⏎comparable expectation of demonstrated performance—yet no such framework currently exists⏎for clinical AI.⏎Privacy and data use concerns. The potential for third-party monetization, re-identification⏎through metadata aggregation, and secondary use of protected health information without clear⏎consent boundaries erodes the trust necessary for responsible data sharing.⏎Page 5⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 2⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize?⏎HHS should pursue reforms that support AI adoption while recognizing the professional work⏎involved in using these tools effectively.⏎Reimbursement modernization. When a physician interprets AI-generated data—whether from⏎an imaging algorithm, a risk stratification model, or a decision-support tool—that is a⏎professional act requiring clinical training, judgment, and accountability. It is the same⏎professional act that has always required appropriate compensation. Payment policy under⏎existing Medicare and Medicaid authorities (42 C.F.R. Parts 405, 414, 415, and 430) should⏎recognize that AI creates new interpretive work for physicians rather than eliminating existing⏎work. Reductions based on assumptions that AI replaces professional effort would threaten the⏎sustainability of outpatient practices, particularly in rural and underserved areas.⏎Managed care safeguards. Within Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care⏎frameworks (42 C.F.R. Parts 422 and 438), HHS should ensure AI does not worsen prior⏎authorization delays, enable blanket denials, or add clerical burden to licensed professionals.⏎Nondiscrimination protections under 45 C.F.R. Part 92 should apply to AI-influenced coverage⏎determinations.⏎Liability clarity. The professional liability framework in medicine is clear: the treating clinician⏎is accountable. When AI is inserted into that decision chain, accountability becomes ambiguous.⏎Reforms should preserve the professional’s authority while ensuring that developers share⏎appropriate responsibility when their tools contribute to harm. Clarity here benefits all parties—⏎developers gain regulatory certainty, clinicians gain defined protections, and patients retain a⏎clear framework for recourse.⏎Competitive safeguards. Guardrails consistent with the Antitrust, Stark Law, and Anti-⏎Kickback Statute should be modernized to address scenarios where a single entity controls⏎clinical decision-making tools, care delivery, and commercial platforms simultaneously.⏎Maintaining a competitive marketplace protects both innovation and patient choice.⏎Implementation support. Streamlined federal grant pathways under 2 C.F.R. Part 200 would⏎help early-adopting practices manage implementation costs without prohibitive administrative⏎burden.⏎Page 6⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 3⏎For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role⏎should HHS play?⏎AI tools outside formal medical device classification present a specific governance challenge:⏎they influence the practice of medicine without being subject to the professional and regulatory⏎frameworks that govern clinical care. Billing tools shape documentation. Revenue cycle⏎algorithms modify coding behavior. Triage systems redirect patients. Each of these functions⏎affects clinical outcomes, yet none triggers the accountability structures that apply when a⏎licensed professional makes the same decisions.⏎This creates an asymmetry. Licensed professionals operate under personal liability, fiduciary⏎obligation, and regulatory oversight on both state and federal levels. Technology developers⏎operating in the same clinical space carry none of these obligations. The practical result is that⏎when AI-influenced processes lead to adverse outcomes, the professional bears full⏎accountability for decisions shaped by tools they did not build, cannot fully interrogate, and had⏎limited input in selecting.⏎The heterogeneity of AI systems compounds this. Training sets, validation methods, and⏎architectures vary widely, producing a broad range of quality with no minimum standard.⏎HIPAA’s de-identification standards were not designed for AI systems capable of large-scale⏎pattern reconstruction, and re-identification risk through metadata aggregation remains a real⏎concern.⏎Recommended Actions⏎HHS should consider establishing minimum transparency and validation expectations for AI⏎systems used in healthcare, even outside the device pathway. The key elements are practical:⏎clear labeling of where AI is involved in care processes, defined liability allocation that reflects⏎the actual role each party plays, and developer responsibility for representative training data.⏎Professional societies such as SCAI can assist by developing appropriate use guidance within⏎their clinical domains—not as mandatory credentialing, but as practical frameworks that⏎regulators, health systems, and developers can reference when determining what responsible⏎deployment looks like.⏎Page 7⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 4⏎What are the most promising AI evaluation methods and should HHS further support these⏎processes?⏎Pre-deployment validation. Retrospective validation using clinical data remains essential, with⏎performance assessed through stratified analyses across demographic subgroups. Datasets are⏎frequently underrepresented along dimensions of age, race, disease severity, and socioeconomic⏎status. Validation that ignores these gaps produces misleading estimates—and it is the licensed⏎professional, not the developer, who faces consequences when a tool underperforms in a specific⏎patient population.⏎Simulated deployment. Running AI in parallel with clinical care without influencing⏎management (shadow mode) allows comparison of predictions against actual outcomes. This⏎should precede clinical activation as standard practice.⏎Post-deployment monitoring. After activation, models should be monitored for performance⏎drift, with clinician error reporting, scheduled revalidation, and bias surveillance. AI-specific⏎adverse event reporting would parallel the post-market infrastructure that exists for devices and⏎drugs.⏎Role of specialty societies. What constitutes acceptable performance varies by clinical context—⏎the error rate tolerable in a population screening tool is different from what is acceptable in a⏎real-time procedural decision aid. Specialty societies understand these distinctions because their⏎members are the professionals who bear the consequences of getting it wrong. Cooperative⏎agreements between HHS and professional societies to develop evaluation guidance would⏎ensure both scientific rigor and clinical relevance.⏎Recommended HHS support. Grant funding for multi-site validation infrastructure and shared⏎surveillance systems would have the greatest practical impact. Prize competitions using de-⏎identified datasets can incentivize innovation in areas where proprietary barriers limit progress.⏎Page 8⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 5⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote innovative and effective AI use⏎in clinical care?⏎AI used in clinical care should strengthen the patient–provider relationship, which remains the⏎foundation of trust, accountability, and decision-making in medicine.⏎Clinical care in the United States is delivered by licensed professionals operating under⏎established legal and ethical frameworks. These include professional licensure, fiduciary duty,⏎standards of care, and common law accountability. These structures exist to protect patients and⏎to ensure that medical decisions are individualized, contextual, and responsibly supervised.⏎AI systems that influence diagnosis, treatment, or longitudinal management without being⏎embedded within this professional framework risk bypassing these safeguards. For this reason,⏎AI in clinical care should be deployed as an augmentative tool within clinician-led care models⏎rather than as an independent decision-making layer.⏎HHS can best promote innovative and effective AI use by reinforcing clinician accountability⏎and supporting private-sector efforts that align with real-world clinical practice.⏎Clinician-centered accountability. AI should support clinicians in understanding patient data⏎and making informed decisions. Evaluation and accreditation efforts should emphasize clear⏎clinician oversight, defined responsibility for action, and transparency into how AI outputs are⏎used in care. Clinical responsibility must remain with licensed professionals, consistent with⏎existing legal and ethical standards.⏎Specialty-specific evaluation and use. Clinical judgment is inherently specialty-specific. Risk⏎tolerance, workflows, and consequences of error vary significantly across clinical domains. AI⏎evaluation should reflect the context in which the technology is used. Professional societies are⏎well positioned to define appropriate use and minimum standards within their specialties, and⏎HHS can support alignment with these efforts rather than imposing uniform models that cannot⏎account for clinical context.⏎Outcome- and relationship-focused validation. The effectiveness of AI should be measured by⏎its impact on patient understanding, clinician decision-making, workflow efficiency, and clinical⏎outcomes. Private-sector testing should prioritize real-world performance and safety over static⏎algorithmic benchmarks.⏎Alignment of incentives with professional practice. Payment and programmatic structures⏎should not incentivize AI systems that disintermediate clinicians or weaken the patient–provider⏎relationship. Alignment should favor AI that demonstrably enhances clinician-supervised care.⏎Page 9⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎In summary, HHS should support private-sector AI innovation by reinforcing clinician⏎accountability, respecting specialty-specific expertise, and ensuring that AI augments the⏎professional foundations of clinical care rather than bypassing them.⏎Page 10⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 6⏎Where have AI tools met or exceeded expectations and where have they fallen short?⏎AI in clinical care has shown genuine promise in specific applications, but the field remains early⏎and the evidence mixed.⏎Areas of Demonstrated Value⏎A Lancet Digital Health review of 86 randomized controlled trials found roughly 81% reported⏎favorable results, with successes in adenoma detection during colonoscopy, reduced acute care⏎utilization during radiation therapy, and improved postoperative pain management. In⏎cardiovascular medicine, AI-assisted echocardiographic interpretation has matched or⏎outperformed physicians in ejection fraction estimation. NLP-based documentation tools are⏎improving efficiency and addressing clinician burnout.⏎Where Results Are Equivocal⏎A 2024 JAMA Internal Medicine study of an AI deterioration model across nearly 9,000⏎inpatients found a 10.4% reduction in composite escalation outcomes but no statistically⏎significant reduction in mortality. Changing escalation patterns is not the same as improving⏎survival. Much of AI’s current demonstrated utility falls within operational domains—⏎documentation, coding, scheduling—which represent useful improvements but not⏎transformative advances in patient care.⏎Highest-Potential Applications⏎The most promising applications provide actionable intelligence within professional clinical⏎workflows: AI-assisted coronary physiology assessment, real-time procedural guidance,⏎predictive analytics for cardiac decompensation, and automated quality tracking. These tools⏎share a common structure—AI generates clinically relevant information, and the licensed⏎professional decides what to do with it. That structure is where AI delivers the most value:⏎augmenting professional judgment at moments of high consequence.⏎CT-derived fractional flow reserve (FFR-CT) offers a concrete example. This AI application has⏎reduced the need for invasive diagnostic angiography in patients with stable chest pain—sparing⏎patients an unnecessary procedure while preserving diagnostic accuracy. Its adoption succeeded⏎because it was clinically validated, integrated into existing referral workflows, and interpreted by⏎the treating physician. That pattern—validated, integrated, physician-interpreted—is replicable⏎across clinical domains and should inform how HHS thinks about scaling AI adoption more⏎broadly.⏎An important caveat: many studies are single-center, evaluate narrow populations, and have not⏎been replicated at scale.⏎Page 11⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 7⏎Which roles have the most influence on AI adoption, and what are the primary administrative⏎hurdles?⏎AI adoption decisions involve multiple stakeholders: CMOs who define clinical appropriateness,⏎CIOs who control integration, quality officers who oversee monitoring, and finance leadership⏎who evaluate ROI. The challenge is that these roles often operate in parallel rather than in⏎coordination, and the stakeholders who bear professional accountability for patient outcomes—⏎the clinicians—are not always central to adoption decisions.⏎Administrative hurdles include procurement processes designed for static equipment rather than⏎dynamic software, compliance frameworks that do not account for continuous-learning systems,⏎and institutional review processes lacking AI-specific expertise. The most consequential barrier⏎may be structural: when AI adoption is led by technology or administrative functions without⏎integrated clinical governance, the resulting tools may meet operational specifications but fail to⏎align with the professional standards and workflows that govern patient care.⏎Page 12⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 8⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate AI development?⏎Most healthcare data exist in silos. For AI to achieve its potential, interoperability must create a⏎unified landscape where high-quality data drives development, validation, and responsible⏎deployment.⏎High-Value Data Types⏎Interoperability yields the greatest impact with heterogeneous, multi-modal datasets:⏎standardized EMR data for risk prediction; unstructured clinical text for NLP training; imaging⏎and waveform data—particularly DICOM data, ECGs, and hemodynamic tracings—for⏎diagnostic and predictive AI in cardiology and critical care; genomic data for precision medicine;⏎and real-world evidence from claims data and remote patient monitoring for longitudinal⏎outcome tracking.⏎Essential Standards⏎Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) is essential for real-time clinical data⏎exchange. The OMOP Common Data Model enables cross-institutional research. SNOMED CT⏎and LOINC provide the semantic consistency needed for generalizability. Specialized⏎standards—GA4GH for genomics, CDISC for clinical trials—bridge research and routine care.⏎Benchmarking and Validation⏎Public datasets like MIMIC and eICU serve as benchmarks. Federated learning enables privacy-⏎preserving training across institutions. When health systems adopt the USCDI, vendors can⏎demonstrate external validity more readily. However, interoperability accelerates scale, and scale⏎without appropriate governance accelerates risk. Clinical professionals must guide both the⏎technical standards and the appropriateness of how shared data are used in algorithm⏎development.⏎Page 13⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 9⏎What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed, and what concerns do they⏎have?⏎Patients and caregivers support AI if it improves access, coordination, and quality without⏎increasing costs or creating new barriers. Their priorities include earlier detection, more⏎personalized treatment, better care coordination, and improved communication with their⏎providers.⏎Their concerns center on the same professional relationship that structures the rest of their care.⏎Patients trust their physician—a trust built on the knowledge that this person is trained, licensed,⏎accountable, and personally invested in their outcome. AI that strengthens this relationship is⏎welcome. AI that interposes an opaque algorithmic layer between patients and their clinicians—⏎through chatbot triage systems, automated denials, or decision tools that function without⏎professional oversight—is not.⏎Privacy is a paramount concern. Patients expect their health information will not be used beyond⏎their direct care without meaningful consent. Regulatory safeguards should prevent arrangements⏎where technology companies independently control clinical decision-making, data infrastructure,⏎and commercial platforms in ways that create conflicts of interest.⏎AI should reduce the administrative burdens that already frustrate patients and clinicians—⏎streamlining prior authorization and claims processes, not worsening them—while preserving⏎human oversight, patient autonomy, and equitable access.⏎Page 14⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Question 10⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize?⏎10a. Published Findings on AI Impact in Clinical Care⏎A growing body of peer-reviewed evidence evaluates AI in clinical care. The Lancet Digital⏎Health review of 86 RCTs found broadly positive results in gastroenterology, oncology, and⏎symptom management. In cardiovascular care, AI-assisted echocardiography has matched⏎physician performance in ejection fraction estimation, and AI risk predictions provided directly⏎to patients improved specialist referral adherence.⏎Many studies are single-center and have not been replicated across settings. Generalizability⏎remains a limitation. A clear regulatory framework, combined with specialty society guidance,⏎may help narrow the gap between technological capability and responsible clinical adoption.⏎10b. Literature on Costs, Benefits, and Transfers⏎Published analyses generally project positive cost-effectiveness from improved diagnostics and⏎reduced unnecessary procedures. However, the majority are model-based rather than derived⏎from real-world implementations, and results vary by analytical perspective. Deploying AI based⏎on modeled projections alone, without real-world validation, raises concerns about liability and⏎research ethics. HHS should prioritize funding for pragmatic implementation studies that⏎evaluate AI as it is used in actual clinical practice, within the professional workflows where these⏎tools will ultimately succeed or fail.⏎Page 15⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Summary⏎AI has meaningful potential to improve clinical care, and SCAI supports its responsible⏎adoption. Our recommendations reflect a single organizing principle: clinical care in the United⏎States is built on a professional framework of licensure, liability, fiduciary duty, and personal⏎accountability. AI policy should work within that framework, not around it.⏎1. AI should be treated as a clinical tool used by licensed professionals, subject to the⏎same expectations of validation, transparency, and oversight that apply to other⏎instruments used in patient care.⏎2. Reimbursement should recognize the professional work AI creates. Physician⏎interpretation of AI-generated data is a professional act requiring clinical training,⏎judgment, and accountability. Payment policy should value this work accordingly.⏎3. Liability should reflect who actually makes clinical decisions. When AI contributes to⏎a clinical outcome, accountability must be allocated based on the role each party⏎played—giving developers appropriate responsibility and clinicians appropriate⏎protection.⏎4. Specialty societies should develop appropriate use guidance. Professional societies⏎understand the clinical contexts in which AI will be used because their members are the⏎professionals who will use it. HHS should formally support these efforts, as it does with⏎clinical practice guidelines.⏎5. Transparency and post-market monitoring are practical necessities. Labeling AI⏎involvement in clinical care, reporting AI-related adverse events, and conducting ongoing⏎performance surveillance are implementable steps that protect patients and build the trust⏎necessary for broader adoption.⏎6. Patients expect AI to strengthen their relationship with their physician, not replace⏎it. The professional relationship—built on trust, training, and personal accountability—is⏎what patients depend on. AI that supports this relationship will be embraced. AI that⏎undermines it will be resisted, appropriately.⏎The professional framework that governs clinical care is not an obstacle to AI adoption. It is the⏎reason patients trust what happens in the exam room and the catheterization laboratory. SCAI’s⏎members are already using AI in clinical practice, and we want to see that adoption expand. We⏎are available to work with HHS on practical guidance—drawing on the direct experience of⏎physicians who use these tools on patients every day.⏎Page 16⏎⏎SCAI Response — HHS RFI on AI in Clinical Care⏎Contact⏎For questions regarding this response or to discuss further collaboration:⏎Correspondence to lata.drkusum@gmail.com⏎SCAI AI in Clinical Care Task Force, Advocacy committee⏎Society for Cardiovascular Angiography and Interventions⏎Page 17♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0113♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔George♔Garcia♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔MedAssets♔♔♔♔♔♔MedAssets Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 1⏎⏎⏎Submitted on behalf of MedAssets, Inc.⏎Submitted by: George Garcia, VP Regulatory Affairs⏎Date: January 2025⏎⏎⏎Regarding the RFI questions on regulatory frameworks and public-private collaboration:⏎⏎I want to focus on something that I don't think is getting enough attention in this conversation, which is the structural risk of what happens after adoption, not during it.⏎⏎Our internal compliance team spent over 340 staff hours in Q3 2024 alone trying to reconcile conflicting payer standards across three major insurers for AI-assisted documentation and triage tools we'd already deployed. That experience is what brings me to this comment. The problem wasn't that we lacked clarity about whether a tool was safe. The problem was that no one could tell us whether it was reimbursable, whether liability attached to the vendor or to us, and whether the billing codes we were using would survive a payer audit. That ambiguity is real and it costs money, but more importantly it costs time that clinicians and compliance staff don't have.⏎⏎What worries me about this RFI is that HHS seems to be approaching AI adoption as primarily an acceleration problem. Get the tools deployed faster, reduce friction, build public-private partnerships. I understand the appeal of that framing. But I keep thinking about what happened with EHR consolidation and I'm not sure we've fully absorbed the lesson there.⏎⏎By 2015, the market had effectively consolidated around a small number of vendors — Epic, Cerner, a few others — and the switching costs had become so large that most health systems were locked in regardless of how those vendors performed or what they charged. The federal incentive structure, Meaningful Use in particular, drove rapid adoption before interoperability standards were mature enough to prevent that lock-in. Now we're looking at AI tools that don't just touch documentation. They touch triage logic, prior authorization workflows, clinical decision support, and increasingly the billing layer itself. If the same pattern plays out here, the exit costs won't be a painful but manageable migration. They'll be functionally impossible to absorb for a mid-sized organization like ours.⏎⏎MedAssets Comments | HHS AI RFI | Docket HHS-2024-AI-001 | Page 2⏎⏎⏎The RFI mentions public-private collaborations and federal data partnerships as mechanisms for accelerating adoption. I'd ask HHS to think carefully about who benefits from those arrangements structurally. Large vendors with existing federal relationships and the staff to participate in extended comment and working group processes will shape those frameworks in ways that smaller competitors and publicly-developed alternatives simply cannot match. That's not speculation. Our own policy working group identified this dynamic across 9 of 11 stakeholder sessions we ran in Q2 2024 — liability uncertainty was the top barrier, but right behind it was concern about being forced into vendor ecosystems that clinical staff hadn't chosen and couldn't easily exit.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to HHS taking action here. The reimbursement ambiguity is genuinely damaging and needs to be resolved. But the sequencing matters a lot. If we get broad adoption incentives before we have functioning interoperability requirements and vendor-neutral reimbursement standards, we're going to be right back in 2015 except the embedded systems will be making clinical recommendations in real time rather than just storing notes.⏎⏎One specific thing I'd ask HHS to consider: before issuing any guidance that ties reimbursement to specific AI capabilities or workflow integrations, require that those capabilities be achievable through open APIs with published technical specifications, not just through a particular vendor's implementation. That one requirement, if enforced, would do more to prevent the lock-in problem than any amount of post-hoc interoperability rulemaking.⏎⏎I recognize that's easier to say than to implement and I don't have a clean answer for how you'd verify compliance without adding a new certification burden on top of everything else our compliance staff is already managing. The 340 hours I mentioned earlier was for one quarter, three payers, and two product categories. We have seven product categories currently in deployment and we're adding more.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0114♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Reason Foundation, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Reason Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔Before the⏎DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎)⏎In the Matter of ) Docket No. HHS–ONC–⏎) 2026–0001⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of )⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of ) 90 Fed. Reg. 60,108⏎Clinical Care )⏎)⏎COMMENTS OF REASON FOUNDATION⏎February 23, 2026⏎Prepared by:⏎Richard Sill Tom Pandolfi⏎Technology Policy Analyst Independent Researcher⏎Reason Foundation⏎richard.sill@reason.org⏎⏎COMMENTS OF REASON FOUNDATION PAGE 1 OF 5⏎Introduction⏎On behalf of Reason Foundation, we respectively submit these comments in response to the⏎request for information (“RFI”) on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care published by the Office of the Deputy Secretary and⏎Assistant Secretary for Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ONC).1⏎Reason Foundation is a national 501(c)(3) public policy research and education organization⏎with expertise across a range of policy areas, including artificial intelligence policy.2⏎Our comments respond to the following two specific questions contained in the RFI:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code⏎of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Regulatory uncertainty creates a significant barrier to private sector innovation and adoption⏎of artificial intelligence (“AI”) in clinical care. This uncertainty surrounds the boundary⏎between the regulated and unregulated software that aid clinicians in medical decision-⏎making. While medical devices that autonomously perform diagnoses or sometimes⏎treatments are fully regulated by the Food and Drug Administration (“FDA”) and subject to⏎lengthy clinical trials, other software, known as Clinical Decision Support (“CDS”), is merely⏎informational and therefore exempt from full regulation.⏎CDS software is typically embedded in hospital workflows and analyzes patient data to⏎provide treating clinicians with alerts, risk assessments, and suggested next steps. The already⏎uncertain boundary between a CDS’s recommendations and a medical device’s diagnosis is⏎1. Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care, Request for⏎Information, Department of Health and Human Services, Docket No. HHS–ONC–2026–0001, 90 Fed.⏎Reg. 60,108 (Dec. 23, 2025).⏎2. See About Reason Foundation, https://reason.org/about-reason-foundation/ (last visited Feb. 20, 2026).⏎⏎COMMENTS OF REASON FOUNDATION PAGE 2 OF 5⏎a source of concern for developers, who preemptively strip valuable functionality, such as⏎time-sensitive alerts, from their products and redesign what the software displays to clinicians⏎to avoid device classification and associated regulation. The result is that clinicians receive⏎weaker, less actionable tools than current technology could provide, reducing diagnostic⏎accuracy and worsening patient outcomes.⏎In practice, CDS developers avoid certain features, such as providing specific probabilities for⏎anticipated risks, as shown by a 2025 JAMA Health Forum study.3 For example, a CDS tool⏎designed to detect early signs of sepsis could suggest multiple treatment options even if it⏎knows some options are far more relevant than others. It could avoid ranking these options⏎to avoid qualifying as a medical device. Rather than alerting a clinician that a patient faces a⏎“20-30% chance of sepsis within 24 hours” and recommending a specific treatment, such as⏎“immediate broad-spectrum antibiotics,” developers are incentivized to present a list of⏎undifferentiated and unranked options. Diluted CDS functionality slashes projected clinician⏎time savings from 35% to under 15% and cut diagnostic accuracy gains by 22%.4 These⏎changes neuter AI’s core value of augmenting human judgment with precise, actionable⏎insights at the point of care.⏎This appears to be precisely what Congress sought to avoid with the 21st Century Cures Act,⏎which created a statutory exclusion for certain clinician-facing CDS that support professional⏎judgment and permit independent review.5 This exclusion applies when four conditions hold⏎true:⏎1) The software supports or provides recommendations to a healthcare professional;⏎2) The software analyzes patient-specific medical information;⏎3) The professional must independently review the basis for any recommendation; and⏎4) The software discloses any limitations or known failure modes.⏎Congress designed this safe harbor to foster non-device CDS tools that enhance, not replace,⏎professional judgment and to sidestep the FDA’s lengthy premarket review for supportive⏎technologies while preserving safety through transparency and reviewability.⏎Despite this, from 2022 to January 2026, FDA guidance repeatedly redrew and reinterpreted⏎the boundary between regulated device software and unregulated CDS tools. The agency first⏎issued draft CDS guidance in September 2022, proposing four criteria to distinguish regulated⏎3. Scott Gottlieb, “New FDA Policies Could Limit the Full Value of AI in Medicine,” JAMA Health Forum,⏎Vol. 6, No. 2 (2025), available at https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama-health-⏎forum/fullarticle/2830189.⏎4. Id.⏎5. 21st Century Cures Act, Pub. L. 114–255, 130 Stat. 1033 (Dec. 13, 2016).⏎⏎COMMENTS OF REASON FOUNDATION PAGE 3 OF 5⏎devices from Cures Act exclusions.6 They then released clarifications in January 2023, further⏎reinterpreting exclusions to capture tools with probabilistic outputs or action prioritization.7⏎A 2024 update tightened this criteria even further, now deeming many supportive CDS⏎features, such as risk scores or sequenced options, as “device-like” outputs, even when⏎clinicians independently review or override them.8⏎From these years-long tightened boundaries, developers now face a stricter, less predictable⏎environment that slows AI adoption in clinical care. Hospitals may be less likely to deploy⏎CDS tools at scale if they cannot reliably predict if ordinary workflow features will trigger⏎device classification, nor can they determine who bears compliance burdens like validation,⏎maintenance, and adverse event reporting.⏎Former FDA Commissioner Scott Gottlieb and Sen. Bill Cassidy (R-La.) directly challenged⏎these interpretations in an April 2024 letter to FDA leadership.9 They demanded evidence-⏎based justification for expanding regulatory reach beyond the statutory text. To date, the⏎agency has neither provided a clear response nor cited new safety data warranting the⏎tightened boundary. Instead, precautionary logic drives the classification of merely supportive⏎tools as medical devices. This logic risks blocking rapid adoption of AI tools that could reduce⏎clinician burnout by 30% and diagnostic errors by 20%, according to peer-reviewed pilots.10⏎Without HHS-led course correction, the FDA’s unilateral reinterpretations risk ceding U.S.⏎clinical AI leadership to less risk-averse markets.⏎After several years of progressive boundary-tightening, the FDA released new CDS guidance⏎in January 2026 that partially relaxes the agency’s prior highly restrictive treatment of⏎clinician-facing software.11 It more clearly ties the device versus non-device boundary to the⏎four statutory criteria in the Cures Act and acknowledges that patient-specific, actionable⏎recommendations can qualify as non-device CDS so long as they support, rather than replace,⏎professional judgment and allow independent review of the underlying rationale. The⏎guidance consolidates earlier documents, replacing some of the most rigid “any directive⏎language = device” interpretations with a more nuanced focus on intent and reliance.12 It also⏎6. Clinical Decision Support Software; Guidance for Industry and Food and Drug Administration Staff;⏎Availability, Notice of Availability, Food and Drug Administration, Docket No. FDA-2017-D-6569, 87⏎Fed. Reg. 59,436 (Sept. 28, 2022).⏎7. “Clinical Decision Support Software Frequently Asked Questions (FAQs),” Food and Drug⏎Administration, https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/clinical-decision-⏎support-software-frequently-asked-questions-faqs (last visited Feb. 20, 2026).⏎8. Id.⏎9. Letter from Sen. Bill Cassidy to FDA Commissioner Robert Califf (Apr. 17, 2024), available at⏎https://www.help.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/41724lettertofdaonclinicaldecisionsupport.pdf.⏎10. Scott Gottlieb, supra note 3.⏎11. “Clinical Decision Support Software: Guidance for Industry and Food and Drug Administration Staff,”⏎Food and Drug Administration (Jan. 6, 2026), available at⏎https://www.fda.gov/media/109618/download.⏎12. Id.⏎⏎COMMENTS OF REASON FOUNDATION PAGE 4 OF 5⏎uses expanded examples to signal that certain high-value CDS functions do not have to⏎automatically trigger full device regulation.⏎Despite this recent course correction, substantial barriers remain. The boundary between⏎exempt CDS and regulated device software is still complex, multi-factored, and heavily⏎dependent on the agency’s evolving “current thinking” rather than a broad, bright-line safe⏎harbor.13 Innovators must still navigate fine distinctions such as whether a tool’s language⏎“supports” or “drives” a clinician’s decision, and whether a medical prediction is too “time⏎sensitive” to be reviewed independently, or whether a single recommendation is the only⏎“clinically appropriate” decision, all under the shadow of discretionary enforcement.14 This⏎uncertainty disproportionately harms smaller firms and startups that lack resources to⏎continuously reinterpret guidance, absorb regulatory risk, or adjust products late in⏎development. All of this disincentivizes more transformative AI, instead leading smaller firms⏎to settle for more cautious, low-impact designs.⏎In addition, the new FDA guidance leaves broader structural barriers unaddressed. Many of⏎the most clinically valuable AI applications, such as early deterioration detection and sepsis⏎prediction, remain effectively steered into device status, thus deterring investment and⏎slowing deployment.15 Liability environments still favor traditional manual workflows, and⏎there is little clear, AI-specific direction for general-purpose models or patient-facing tools,⏎leaving developers unsure which regulatory regime applies. Together, these factors continue⏎to channel innovation toward what regulators are most comfortable permitting rather than⏎what clinicians and patients would voluntarily adopt in a more predictable, innovation-⏎friendly framework.⏎Even for non-device CDS that clears the FDA boundary, hospitals confront a profound⏎accountability vacuum that stalls deployment. Questions regarding who is responsible for⏎clinician training, incident reporting, etc., still persist. The 2026 FDA guidance clarifies⏎classification but omits this critical post-adoption clarity, leaving providers to navigate⏎fragmented accreditation standards and state rules through protracted legal reviews. This⏎uncertainty defaults risk-averse institutions toward rejecting high-value AI, even when pilots⏎show clear gains in efficiency and accuracy, further channeling innovation toward tools that⏎avoid device classification, rather than tools that deliver the greatest clinical value.⏎13. Gary E. Weissman, “FDA Regulation of Predictive Clinical Decision-Support Tools: What Does It⏎Mean for Hospitals?” Journal of Hospital Medicine, Vol. 16, No. 4 (2021) at 244–246.⏎14. Ronald Jackups, “FDA Regulation of Laboratory Clinical Decision Support Software: Is It a Medical⏎Device?” Clinical Chemistry, Vol. 69, No. 4 (2023) at 327–329.⏎15. Mark P. Sendak et al., “Strengthening the use of artificial intelligence within healthcare delivery⏎organizations: balancing regulatory compliance and patient safety” Journal of the American Medical⏎Informatics Association, Vol. 31, No. 7 (2024) at 1622–1627.⏎⏎COMMENTS OF REASON FOUNDATION PAGE 5 OF 5⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations.⏎HHS should direct the FDA to codify a broad and binding safe harbor in its intended use of⏎device and premarket approval procedure regulations at 21 C.F.R. Part 801 and 806,⏎respectively, that fully implements the Cures Act’s four CDS criteria. Doing so would replace⏎the current patchwork of nonbinding guidance with clear rules protecting probabilistic⏎outputs, prioritized recommendations, and time-critical tools when clinicians retain review⏎authority, explicitly including high-value applications like sepsis detection.⏎To fill the accountability vacuum, HHS should amend Centers for Medicare and Medicaid⏎Services’ condition of participation regulations,16 as well as the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology’s certification criteria,17 to establish a simple,⏎voluntary framework that would allocate responsibilities to enable rapid contracting without⏎legal paralysis. Some of these responsibilities could include:⏎1) Developers disclose inputs/limitations;⏎2) Hospitals validate locally and train staff; and⏎3) Clinicians override and report incidents.⏎These targeted steps would create predictability, promote competition, and advance clinician⏎freedom, and thereby channel innovation toward patient-chosen solutions rather than⏎regulatory comfort zones.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Richard Sill⏎Technology Policy Analyst⏎Reason Foundation⏎Tom Pandolfi⏎Independent Researcher⏎16. 42 C.F.R. § 482.24.⏎17. 45 C.F.R. Part 170.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0115♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Walker PhD RN FAAN, Rachel (Rae), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rachel (Rae)♔Walker PhD RN FAAN♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rachel (Rae) Walker PhD RN FAAN⏎Professor, Elaine Marieb College of Nursing⏎Director, Health Tech for the People⏎University of Massachusetts Amherst⏎Email: R.Walker@umass.edu⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎The following public comment responds to RIN 0955-AA13. I am a PhD-prepared Registered⏎Nurse and tenured Professor at the University of Massachusetts Amherst. My clinical⏎background includes emergency response and international disaster relief, patient navigation,⏎gerontology and cancer care in both acute care and community-based settings. I am the founder⏎and co-director of Health Tech for the People, a multidisciplinary think tank focused on⏎accountable development and evaluation of health technologies. I have worked on A.I.-related⏎research and policy for more than fifteen years.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎• The correct question should be what are the appropriate uses for A.I. in health care, and⏎under what conditions/in what contexts? At present, there are a myriad of technologies⏎purporting to be ‘A.I’, which has been deployed as a marketing term (Bender & Hanna,⏎2025). This confuses the conversation as we often lack universally adopted⏎nomenclatures or conceptual precision.⏎• Lack of engagement of ‘end users’ and those most impacted by the introduction or⏎expansion of these technologies, especially patient and community groups marginalized⏎within these systems, shift workers and laborers surveilled by these technologies, and⏎care workers such as nurses, social workers, and educators (Walker et al., 2023).⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies,⏎or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎• A.I. in theory has promised to transform health care. But too much A.I. in practice⏎maintains a segregated, understaffed and unjust status quo, and a key driver of this⏎disparity is policy that allows clinical care systems to make claims related to potential⏎benefits of A.I. and similar technologies and act on them, without having to demonstrate⏎those claims are actually true (Frizzell, 2023; Narayanan & Kapoor, 2024; National⏎Nurses United, 2023; Pavuluri et al., 2024). Furthermore, mechanisms for redress of⏎harms remain unclear as does legal culpability in the context of automated systems (and⏎especially agentic A.I.).⏎⏎• Furthermore, recent reductions in protections for workers from impacts of automated⏎surveillance and management systems (AWSM) pose a critical risk for the future of the⏎health care workforce (Garofalo et al., 2023; Hickok & Maslej, 2023; Office, 2024;⏎Sinnreich & Gilbert, 2024).⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎• A.I. in theory has promised to transform health care. But too much A.I. in practice⏎maintains a segregated, understaffed and unjust status quo, and a key driver of this⏎disparity is policy.⏎• A.I. agents combine a chatbot mimicking human empathy with other health info. Unlike⏎ChatGPT, they can initiate actions like assessments or referrals –even converse with⏎other AI models - without being prompted. Hundreds are already for sale and nurses and⏎other clinicians have been invited to train them. Under current Federal law these models⏎are largely not allowed to diagnose or prescribe, though recently introduced legislation⏎proposals such as HR 238 (“Healthy Technology Act”) would redefine AI as a prescriber.⏎This represents a paradigm shift that would fundamentally dismantle the patient-provider⏎relationship as we know it and we do not yet have enough research to understand all the⏎potential implications of this, and/or properly assess and regulate such a change.⏎• I’m practical about this: I live in a rural area, where it is often difficult to access care –⏎especially primary care and specialty care. A.I. agents might remove some barriers to⏎care access in some specific contexts. But Without STRONG community-driven policy⏎and regulation with teeth, it will create new and long term problems such as even greater⏎potentials for manipulation of patients (via targeted advertising of health services by⏎behemoths such as Amazon and Meta), and longer term reduction and de-skilling of the⏎health care workforce in areas where there are already severe shortages (Gerlich, 2025;⏎Lee et al., 2025).⏎• We risk creating an even more segregated health care system where those who already⏎have access to resources will be able to continue to access human and A.I.-supported⏎care, while those who do not have adequate access will be forced to rely on remotely-⏎managed and agentic forms of care (if they can access care at all).⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements,⏎and/or prize competitions)?⏎• Prioritize grants to state and local level governance spaces which can help to shape the⏎research agendas for those specific regions/contexts⏎• Fund grassroots community organizations especially those representing constiutencies⏎most likely to be impacted by deployment of A.I. systems (workers, patients,⏎⏎communities marginalized within decision-making spaces for health care and⏎technologies).⏎• Continue to provide grants to researchers and community advocates at public-serving⏎academic/research institutions such as public universities, but do not require those grants⏎to “accelerate A.I. adoption”. We should have the capacity to look holistically at root⏎causes and systems-level drivers of health inequities, and propose and evaluate solutions⏎based on the approaches that best fit the priorities of the people. This may or may not⏎involve ‘accelerating’ A.I. adoption in health care, depending on the nature of the⏎problem definition and context.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎• HHS should consider all forms of this technology that may be required for the delivery of⏎health care as digital public infrastructure, and fund and regulate them accordingly.⏎Ceding this space to private industry may result in technological innovations, but those⏎innovations will be designed to generate shareholder value rather than support health⏎equity (Zucker, 2020).⏎• In calculating investments, HHS should consider the broader human and planetary costs⏎of these technologies, many of which are so resource-intensive that they threaten to⏎collapse ecosystems and reduce – rather than promote – public health in the longer term⏎(Crawford, 2024; McDonald, 2024; Ricaurte, 2022; Schultz et al., 2025).⏎• Care workers like nurses currently have little access to information about how A.I. tools⏎integrated into clinical care actually function. Although labels and ‘model cards’ have⏎been suggested by groups like the Coalition for Health A.I. (CHAI: https://www.chai.org)⏎, these are not mandated nor – in the rare cases they are made available – are they⏎available to bedside workers in ways that would allow for meaningful use. More⏎research, conducted in naturalistic settings, is needed to establish how these tools⏎function and how best to allow both clinicians and patients to understand their functions,⏎designs, possible benefits, and limitations.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce⏎costs?⏎• A.I. tools that engage in ‘sous-veillance’ – pointing the camera back up at institutions and⏎systems and holding them accountable, are more likely to generate effective actions to⏎move us closer to health equity (Committee on the Future of Nursing 2020–2030 et al.,⏎2021; Neumann & Bookey-Bassett, 2024).⏎• Without robust staffing regulation and efforts to ensure human-in-the-loop systems are⏎actually true to their word, A.I. integration as a means of promoting efficiency may⏎further exacerbate clinician “burn out” and health inequities. National Nurses United⏎(NNU) has proposed a Nurse and Patient A.I. Bill of Rights which could help guide⏎⏎generation of a more robust, patient- and worker-centered approach to regulation and⏎human rights:⏎https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/sites/default/files/nnu/documents/0424_NursesPati⏎ents-BillOfRights_Principles-AI-Justice_flyer.pdf⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎• ‘A.I.’ should not be imposed on patients and workers without robust leadership of the⏎groups most impacted by its use. Administrators should have less power in these⏎decisions. Models for this such as community councils and Patient Advisory Boards⏎could be expanded and robustly-funded to provide better governance and community⏎control over A.I. technologies.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎• Enhanced interoperability would not accelerate movement towards greater health equity⏎if data continue to be captured without meaningful consent or the ability of patients to⏎take back their data. If we move to systems that involve some form of automated⏎gatekeeping/triage (such as talking to an A.I. agent before seeing a human provider) we⏎will create entire classes of patients who cannot safely access care due to the need to⏎disclose characteristics or behaviors which have been increasingly criminalized and⏎punished by our health systems. These range from demographic information such as⏎information about sex or gender, to pregnancy status, to use of particular medications, to⏎exposure to substances, to whether someone has legal U.S. citizenship (Eubanks, 2018).⏎• Increased interoperability and data-sharing under the current state of U.S. regulations and⏎data-related civil rights (or lack thereof) is a direct threat to health equity due to the⏎problem of mass surveillance and carceral care systems (Sinnreich & Gilbert, 2024).⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎• RESOURCES AND PLANETARY HEALTH: You can teach nurses and other clinicians⏎to be become prompt engineers with tools like generative A.I. (genAI). But AI literacy⏎must include considerations of resource, such as the fact much of genAI and agentic AI is⏎powered by a growing number of resource-hungry data centers in people’s backyards.⏎For example, NY state had about ~150 data centers at my last count. Last year U.S. data⏎centers consumed 200 tera-watts of electricity – enough to power all of Thailand for a⏎year - expected to triple in the next 3 years. Its estimated data center spread in Virginia⏎costs citizens an extra $37/month. They can be a source of environmental racism-⏎poisoning communities with emissions. This is a nursing and public health problem. The⏎handful of proposed “zero water” centers won’t be online for at least 2 more years, by⏎⏎which point it is estimated they’ll have drained another trillion gallons of water, much of⏎it in places where farmers’ fields are fallow, tap water has dried up, and ecosystems have⏎collapsed. “Green AI” pilots are built on companies’ knowledge that by 2030 in many⏎areas the water will already be gone. Will AI adoption matter if our communities don’t⏎have water & reliable electricity? We demand policies to immediately halt A.I.’s⏎secretive & profligate consumption of non-renewable planetary resources, or it will cost⏎us access to clean air and water, a reliable grid and a livable planet.⏎• PATIENT AND WORKER PRIVACY: Existing legislation expects individual⏎consumers to know how to opt into privacy, & its impossible for anyone to fully opt out⏎of having your data collected & sold. The EU passed GDPR legislation- the “right to be⏎forgotten law- which provides broad protections against invasive surveillance. But US⏎industry has fought GDPR & every attempt to rein in their power. When the ACA⏎became law it required health records be sent to a national database. Last year I sat on a⏎HITAC committee for a Federal Rules Task Force tasked with recommending how to do⏎that. A national database of all EHR data. Only certain types of reproductive health data⏎can be held back. I fought for that definition of repro health data to be as BROAD as⏎possible, so communities which tend to be targeted by surveillant A.I. and its carceral⏎consequences could better protect ourselves. Why? Because if you’re LGBTQ+, or an⏎immigrant, disabled, pregnant, in recovery or seeking or providing any of the care⏎increasingly criminalized, you know how easily such data can be weaponized, especially⏎with A.I. And since health systems violate federal law for not sharing these data, we need⏎to teach clinicians how to chart defensively –to think critically about what data are⏎captured & how patients can prevent unwanted surveillance. Which also means⏎rethinking policies around ambient listening in the clinic.⏎• CLINICIAN AUTONOMY: Surveillant A.I. impacts clinician well-being & autonomy.⏎Worker surveillance, similar to that applied to Amazon warehouses & gig drivers, may⏎help “optimize” flexible staffing but without just policy, AI-driven hyper-efficiencies can⏎drive workers into the ground. AI has been rolled out in more than 80% of U.S. hospitals.⏎A recent poll of over 2000 nurses revealed half worked at clinics that use AI to determine⏎patient acuity, 69% of which said their expert clinical assessments don’t match those⏎scores & often they can’t do anything about it (National Nurses United, n.d.). A majority⏎didn’t trust their employers to implement AI safely, which makes sense given what we⏎saw during the pandemic. As a nurse & patient safety issue, the NNU has called for a⏎Nurses’ & Patients’ A.I. Bill of Rights – we should operationalize this through policy.⏎• DATA WEAPONIZATION: With A.I. agents, every interaction is recorded. Historically,⏎human health care workers have resisted unjust policies like bans on gender-affirming⏎care or abortion. But with so much care politicized and criminalized, automated recording⏎AND reporting has consequences. For example, “voice biomarkers” are being trained to⏎detect illness & cognitive decline in people’s audio recordings. Such tech isn’t⏎necessarily accurate – but that doesn’t mean it won’t be weaponized by law enforcement,⏎insurance companies & other groups (Fagherazzi et al., 2021).⏎• To what extent does deployment of A.I. tools enhance accessibility and health equity for⏎patients with disabilities? And to what extent do they amplify technoableism? (Shew,⏎2024)⏎⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎• When AI is introduced, is there any disclosure of how it works across intersectional⏎groups, like a model card? How can we make information about A.I., how it functions, its⏎performance compared to alternatives or usual care, and impacts – proximal and distal⏎(like resource consumption) legible and transparent to patients, care workers, and other⏎decision makers?⏎• A.I. supposed to “free up” nurses and other clinicians to provide more humanizing care.⏎But in the absence of robust safe staffing legislation, do you know if it is actually doing⏎that? Research by Frennert, Elish & others shows its a VERY MIXED bag. Don’t just ask⏎but MEASURE: As AI is implemented, is time with patients actually increasing? Do⏎individual patients get more time with their clinicians? How about clinician well-being –⏎is their work made easier or are they simply asked to take on more (with fewer human⏎colleagues present)?⏎• Is the invisible labor and repair work associated with A.I. roll out and maintenance⏎accounted for in clinical workflows and resource allocation? (Dillard‐Wright, 2019; Elish⏎& Watkins, 2020; Frennert et al., 2023)⏎• As agentic A.I. is rolled out – what’s happening in primary care, acute care, aging care,⏎disability care? For what patient groups/contexts is such technology actually beneficial (if⏎at all), and for whom is it a barrier to care/health equity? What are the systems-level⏎changes that are needed to ensure actual health equity (beyond implementation of A.I.)⏎and how can we achieve them?⏎• What does precepting of clinicians and care workers look like in an A.I. era and are there⏎enough? Are there adequate safeguards to assess clinical performance and on-going⏎development, especially if A.I. adoption leads to reductions in health care staffing?⏎• Translate Care Work. The public needs to understand the value of care work. Presently⏎care is mostly imagined as a billable commodity, rather relational praxis. Do institutional⏎policies ensure technologies support anti-racist, equitable and consentful care and⏎education? Does such care translate to students & employees? How can this best be⏎measured and assessed in the short- and long-term?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical⏎care?⏎Yes, please see bibliography below.⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎⏎Tech says A.I. is revolutionary and whether you like it or not, you need to get on-board.⏎But revolutions are about challenging power. In our current policy landscape –A.I. is⏎less about revolution and more about consolidating power & resources in the hands of a⏎very few; entrenching an unjust, chronically understaffed, undertreated and at times,⏎profoundly carceral status quo.⏎Additional references cited above:⏎Bender, E. M., & Hanna, A. (2025). The A.I. Con: How to Fight Big Tech’s Hype and⏎Create the Future We Want. HarperCollins.⏎Committee on the Future of Nursing 2020–2030, National Academy of Medicine, &⏎National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine. (2021). The Future⏎of Nursing 2020-2030: Charting a Path to Achieve Health Equity (M. K.⏎Wakefield, D. R. Williams, S. L. Menestrel, & J. L. Flaubert, Eds.). National⏎Academies Press. https://doi.org/10.17226/25982⏎Crawford, K. (2024). Generative AI’s environmental costs are soaring—And mostly⏎secret. Nature, 626(8000), 693–693. https://doi.org/10.1038/d41586-024-00478-x⏎Dillard‐Wright, J. (2019). Electronic health record as a panopticon: A disciplinary⏎apparatus in nursing practice. Nursing Philosophy, 20(2), N.PAG-N.PAG.⏎https://doi.org/10.1111/nup.12239⏎Elish, M. C., & Watkins, E. A. (2020, September 30). Repairing Innovation. Data &⏎Society Research Institute. Data & Society.⏎https://datasociety.net/library/repairing-innovation/⏎Eubanks, V. (2018). Automating Inequality: How High-tech Tools Profile, Police and⏎Punish the Poor. St. Martin’s Press.⏎https://us.macmillan.com/books/9781250074317/automatinginequality/⏎⏎Fagherazzi, G., Fischer, A., Ismael, M., & Despotovic, V. (2021). Voice for Health: The⏎Use of Vocal Biomarkers from Research to Clinical Practice. Digital Biomarkers,⏎5(1), 78–88. https://doi.org/10.1159/000515346⏎Frennert, S., Petersson, L., & Erlingsdottir, G. (2023). “More” work for nurses: The⏎ironies of eHealth. BMC Health Services Research, 23(1), 411.⏎https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-023-09418-3⏎Frizzell, N. (2023, December 19). A Timely Reminder: ‘Invisible Labor’ Is Still Work.⏎Vogue. https://www.vogue.com/article/invisible-labor-is-still-work⏎Garofalo, L., Lenhart, A., Mukogosi, J., & Akinrinade, I. (2023). Essentially Unprotected.⏎Health Data and Surveillance of Essential Workers during the COVID-19⏎Pandemic. SSRN Electronic Journal. https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.4343045⏎Gerlich, M. (2025). AI Tools in Society: Impacts on Cognitive Offloading and the Future⏎of Critical Thinking. Societies, 15(1), Article 1.⏎https://doi.org/10.3390/soc15010006⏎Hickok, M., & Maslej, N. (2023). A policy primer and roadmap on AI worker surveillance⏎and productivity scoring tools. AI and Ethics, 3(3), 673–687.⏎https://doi.org/10.1007/s43681-023-00275-8⏎Lee, H.-P., Sarkar, A., Tankelevitch, L., Drosos, I., Rintel, S., Banks, R., & Wilson, N.⏎(2025). The Impact of Generative AI on Critical Thinking: Self-Reported⏎Reductions in Cognitive Effort and Confidence Effects From a Survey of⏎Knowledge Workers. CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing Systems⏎(CHI ’25), April 26–May 01, 2025, Yokohama, Japan. ACM, New York, NY, USA.⏎https://doi.org/10.%201145/3706598.3713778⏎⏎McDonald, T. (2024). Do Generative Artificial Intelligence Company Strategies of⏎‘Moving Fast and Breaking Things’ in Civil Society Cancel Their Social Licence to⏎Operate? A Nurse’s Evaluation of Chatbot Impacts. Pacific Rim International⏎Journal of Nursing Research, 28(4), 689–706.⏎https://doi.org/10.60099/prijnr.2024.268964⏎Narayanan, A., & Kapoor, S. (2024). A.I. Snake Oil: What Artificial Intelligence Can Do,⏎What It Can’t, and How to Tell the Difference. Princeton University Press.⏎National Nurses United. (n.d.). Nurses and Patients Bill of Rights: Guiding Principles for⏎A.I. Justice in Nursing and Health Care. Retrieved⏎https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/sites/default/files/nnu/documents/0424_Nur⏎sesPatients-BillOfRights_Principles-AI-Justice_flyer.pdf⏎National Nurses United. (2023, December 20). A.I.’s impact on nursing and health care.⏎https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/artificial-intelligence⏎Neumann, P., & Bookey-Bassett, S. (2024, May 8). The health-care crisis won’t be⏎solved without addressing the elephant in the room: Staff workload. The⏎Conversation. http://theconversation.com/the-health-care-crisis-wont-be-solved-⏎without-addressing-the-elephant-in-the-room-staff-workload-228782⏎Office, U. S. G. A. (2024, October 29). Digital Surveillance of Workers: Tools, Uses, and⏎Stakeholder Perspectives | U.S. GAO. https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-⏎107639⏎Pavuluri, S., Sangal, R., Sather, J., & Taylor, R. A. (2024). Balancing act: The complex⏎role of artificial intelligence in addressing burnout and healthcare workforce⏎⏎dynamics. BMJ Health & Care Informatics, 31(1), e101120.⏎https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjhci-2024-101120⏎Ricaurte, P. (2022). Ethics for the majority world: AI and the question of violence at⏎scale. Media, Culture & Society, 44(4), 726–745.⏎https://doi.org/10.1177/01634437221099612⏎Schultz, M. D., Conti, L. G., & Seele, P. (2025). Digital ethicswashing: A systematic⏎review and a process-perception-outcome framework. AI and Ethics, 5(2), 805–⏎818. https://doi.org/10.1007/s43681-024-00430-9⏎Shew, A. (2024). Against Technoableism: Rethinking Who Needs Improvement. W.W.⏎Norton. https://wwnorton.com/books/9781324036661⏎Sinnreich, A., & Gilbert, J. (2024). The Secret Life of Data: Navigating Hype and⏎Uncertainty in the Age of Algorithmic Surveillance. The MIT Press.⏎Walker, R., Dillard-Wright, J., & Iradukunda, F. (2023). Algorithmic bias in artificial⏎intelligence is a problem—And the root issue is power. Nursing Outlook, 71(5).⏎Scopus. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.outlook.2023.102023⏎Zucker, E. (2020). The Case for Digital Public Infrastructure.⏎https://knightcolumbia.org/content/the-case-for-digital-public-infrastructure♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0116♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paul♔Green♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My patient almost died because of a tool that was already known to be broken. That's not a hypothetical. That's a 58-year-old Navajo man from Gallup who sat in our emergency department for four hours while a pulse oximetry-based triage algorithm told us he was lower acuity than he was. By the time I got him into a room, he was in respiratory failure. The pulse oximetry bias affecting patients with darker skin tones has been documented in peer-reviewed literature going back years, including a 2020 study in the New England Journal of Medicine that got a fair amount of attention. The tool being used in our facility had not been audited against that literature. Nobody had flagged it. It was just running.⏎⏎I think about that shift constantly.⏎⏎What HHS is asking in this RFI, if I'm reading it right, is how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. And I understand the appeal of that framing. There are real problems AI could help with, especially in rural and tribal health settings like the ones I work near in New Mexico, where we are chronically understaffed and patients travel hours to get to us. I'm not opposed to AI tools in principle. But I keep coming back to a basic problem that I don't see addressed in how this conversation is being framed: acceleration without mandatory bias auditing doesn't solve the access problem, it scales the harm.⏎⏎The tools being deployed now were trained on historical data. That data encodes decades of disparities in who got care, what kind, and how it was documented. A sepsis prediction algorithm trained mostly on data from well-resourced urban hospitals is going to perform differently on a 65-year-old Diné woman presenting to a critical access hospital in McKinley County. That's not speculation, there's a growing body of evidence on differential algorithm performance across racial and socioeconomic groups. But when a tool produces a number or a risk score, it carries a kind of authority that a nurse's gut instinct doesn't. I have had attendings override my concerns because "the algorithm said low risk." That dynamic is real and I don't know how to quantify it but it is absolutely affecting care.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to do, specifically, is require prospective bias auditing as a condition of any reimbursement pathway tied to AI-assisted clinical decision support. Not retrospective review after deployment. Before. And the audit results should be publicly available, broken down by race, ethnicity, and geography at minimum. Right now there is no standard requirement for this that I'm aware of, and vendors are not doing it voluntarily in any consistent way.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the suppression effect. When a tool is embedded in a workflow, and especially when it's tied to documentation or billing, clinicians stop second-guessing it. Not because they're bad clinicians, but because the cognitive load of overriding a system recommendation is high, and the institutional pressure not to slow things down is constant. We need transparency requirements that make it easy for bedside nurses and physicians to see how a tool was validated and on what population, right at the point of use. Not buried in a vendor contract.⏎⏎The near-miss I described is not a one-time thing. It is probably happening in emergency departments across the country right now, today, on this shift, because tools with documented limitations are running without flags, without audits, without any mechanism for a floor nurse to know what she doesn't know about the thing she's being asked to trust.⏎⏎I don't have a policy background. I work nights. But I know what I saw and I know the literature on pulse ox bias was sitting there, published, while that man waited.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0117♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from The American Academy of Orthotists and Prosthetists, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔The American Academy of Orthotists and Prosthetists♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building,⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Atten: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Posnack:⏎On behalf of the American Academy of Orthotists and Prosthetists (The Academy), we appreciate the⏎opportunity to respond to the Department of Health and Human Services’ Request for Information⏎regarding the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎Founded in 1970, The Academy represents certified and state-licensed orthotists, prosthetists, assistants,⏎technicians, fitters, and researchers dedicated to advancing evidence-based orthotic and prosthetic (O&P)⏎care. More than five million Americans live with limb loss or limb difference, and millions more rely on⏎orthotic devices to walk, work, prevent falls, correct spinal deformities, or manage neuromuscular⏎impairment. O&P care is not elective technology; it is essential rehabilitative infrastructure that supports⏎mobility, independence, workforce participation, and long-term health across the lifespan. Federal policy⏎decisions affecting innovation, reimbursement, and regulation in this field therefore carry direct⏎implications for a substantial and medically complex patient population.⏎Artificial intelligence technologies have meaningful potential to improve patient outcomes, enhance⏎clinical efficiency, and support data-informed device selection, fitting, and monitoring within O&P⏎practice. At the same time, our members have identified important considerations that must be addressed⏎to ensure responsible and evidence-based integration of AI into clinical workflows. The Academy stands⏎ready to assist HHS in responsibly advancing the integration of AI into clinical care as it applies to O&P⏎patient populations. In O&P practice, AI may be incorporated into electronic health record systems used⏎for documentation and reimbursement, as well as embedded within the physical components of orthoses⏎and prostheses provided to patients.⏎⏎Regulatory Framework and Patient Safety⏎AI applications in O&P care may include AI-supported pressure mapping, gait analysis, predictive risk⏎modeling, device optimization algorithms, ambient documentation systems, and remote monitoring⏎technologies. These tools may enhance device fit, reduce fall risk, mitigate skin breakdown, and support⏎more precise clinical decision-making.⏎However, our members have expressed concern regarding HIPAA compliance, data governance, and the⏎handling of protected health information when AI systems are used in clinical environments. Some⏎members have suggested avoiding AI use altogether due to the uncertainty regarding privacy safeguards⏎and model training practices.⏎Additionally, our members report apprehension regarding AI “hallucinations” – the risk of confident, yet⏎inaccurate outputs influencing documentation, billing justification, or patient counseling.⏎To address these concerns, The Academy encourages HHS to:⏎• Provide clear, accessible guidance from the HHS Office for Civil Rights (OCR) regarding HIPAA⏎compliance for AI systems used in clinical care.⏎• Direct the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) to promote transparent data governance standards addressing⏎model training, retention, and secondary use of PHI.⏎• Explore, in coordination with CMS, whether existing accreditation or quality frameworks can⏎appropriately incorporate standards for the clinical use of AI tools.⏎• Encourage the FDA to develop performance validation, auditability, and explainability standards for⏎AI-enabled clinical decision-support systems.⏎Regulatory clarity and enforceable safeguards will be essential to fostering clinician trust and responsible⏎innovation. HHS may consider whether a voluntary certification framework or recognized standards⏎program, including evaluation of existing supplier and quality standards, would improve trust and⏎compliance in the clinical use of AI tools. AI tools used for documentation should be regulated differently⏎than AI tools that directly influence device function or clinical decision-making.⏎Reimbursement and Payment Policy⏎Current Medicare DMEPOS payment structures are tied to HCPCS codes describing physical components⏎of orthoses and prostheses. AI-enabled functionality, including embedded software, sensor-driven⏎analytics, remote monitoring systems, and clinical decision-support tools, is not clearly recognized under⏎the statutory framework established in SSA §1834(h).⏎While AI tools can improve documentation efficiency and assist in meeting payer medical policy⏎requirements, current reimbursement models do not reward improvements in documentation quality,⏎outcome tracking, or workflow efficiency.⏎This misalignment creates several challenges:⏎• AI tools that enhance safety or efficiency may not be economically sustainable.⏎⏎• Clinicians bear financial risk when investing in AI-enabled technologies without clear payment⏎pathways.⏎• Innovation may be discouraged despite potential long-term value to the Medicare program.⏎Where AI-enabled tools objectively improve device fit, reduce falls, mitigate skin breakdown, or prevent⏎secondary complications, appropriate reimbursement alignment may support long-term Medicare⏎savings and improved patient outcomes. The Academy encourages HHS to examine:⏎• Whether HCPCS coding pathways could recognize AI-enabled device components or digital⏎augmentations integrated into orthotic and prosthetic technologies.⏎• How embedded software and sensor-based analytics may be appropriately incorporated into⏎DMEPOS payment methodologies.⏎• Demonstration models through the Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation to evaluate cost⏎savings and outcome improvements associated with AI-integrated O&P care.⏎• Whether certain AI-enabled documentation or monitoring tools could be considered under existing⏎remote monitoring or digital health benefit categories, and, where clinically appropriate and⏎consistent with statutory authority, clarify whether O&P practitioners may participate in such billing⏎pathways.⏎Any modernization of reimbursement frameworks should remain aligned with patient outcomes, safety,⏎and long-term value.⏎As identified in the RFI, there are “inherent flaws in legacy payment systems” that stifle innovation and⏎access to new and emerging technologies. The Academy supports revisiting these legacy payment systems⏎to ensure access to the “highest-value interventions for patients” whether those interventions⏎incorporate AI-enabled functionality or not. Importantly, improvements in documentation efficiency⏎resulting from AI-supported tools should not be interpreted as reductions in clinical labor intensity for⏎purposes of applying productivity adjustments within the HCPCS fee schedule.⏎Use of AI in Claims Adjudication and Prior Authorization⏎The Academy is also concerned about the use of AI-enabled systems by payers in claims processing,⏎utilization management, and prior authorization determinations. O&P treatment decisions are highly⏎individualized and clinically nuanced. If AI tools are used to automate or influence denials without⏎meaningful human review, patients may experience inappropriate delays or denials of medically⏎necessary devices, resulting in preventable functional decline, falls, skin breakdown, or loss of⏎independence.⏎AI should not function as a substitute for individualized clinical review, nor should it be used to circumvent⏎established coverage standards. Accordingly, HHS should ensure that payer use of AI in claims⏎adjudication and prior authorization is subject to robust transparency and meaningful human oversight,⏎including:⏎• Requiring review of denials by a qualified clinician with relevant expertise.⏎• Ensuring that meaningful peer-to-peer review processes are preserved, requiring that treating⏎clinicians have timely access to discussion with payer clinicians possessing relevant expertise before⏎final denial of claims or prior authorization requests.⏎⏎• Requiring disclosure to patients and practitioners when AI tools are used in coverage or payment⏎determinations.⏎• Requiring payers to remain fully accountable for decisions made with AI-enabled tools and to provide⏎individualized explanations.⏎• Monitoring for discriminatory or biased outcomes, particularly among individuals with disabilities and⏎medically complex conditions.⏎Research and Evidence Development⏎There is a significant lack of research specific to orthotic and prosthetic populations regarding AI use in⏎clinical practice. While AI tools are evolving rapidly, policies governing their integration should be⏎evidence-based and population-specific.⏎We have identified the following needs:⏎• Research assessing patient and clinician perspectives on AI in O&P care.⏎• Evaluation of efficacy in workflow integration.⏎• Outcome studies examining safety, functional improvements, and cost impact.⏎• Federal grant opportunities to support this work.⏎The Academy urges HHS to support targeted research initiatives, including partnerships with patient⏎advocacy organizations and academic centers, to ensure that AI-related policy decisions are informed by⏎high-quality data relevant to the O&P population. The Academy encourages coordination with NIH, AHRQ,⏎and PCORI to support implementation science and patient-reported outcomes research specific to O&P⏎populations.⏎Workforce and Clinical Burden⏎Members report that AI tools have met or exceeded expectations as they relate to clinical documentation⏎support, including ambient listening systems, summarization of payer policies, and drafting of encounter⏎notes.⏎Orthotists and prosthetists report that documentation burden reduces time available for direct patient⏎interaction. AI-supported documentation, when appropriately validated and compliant with privacy⏎standards, may reduce administrative workload and allow providers to devote more time to patient care.⏎HHS policy should recognize both the potential benefits and the need for guardrails in this domain.⏎Conclusion⏎Artificial intelligence, if thoughtfully implemented, may also improve equitable access to orthotic and⏎prosthetic care by supporting standardized clinical assessment, expanding remote monitoring capabilities,⏎and enhancing decision-making in underserved or rural communities. HHS should ensure that AI policy⏎frameworks promote equitable deployment so that advances in technology reduce, rather than⏎exacerbate, disparities in access and outcomes for vulnerable populations. Realizing these benefits within⏎the O&P profession will require:⏎⏎• Clear regulatory guidance.⏎• Robust privacy protections.⏎• Transparent validation standards.⏎• Reimbursement frameworks aligned with value.⏎• Targeted research specific to the O&P population.⏎The Academy stands ready to collaborate with HHS to ensure that AI integration in clinical care advances⏎patient safety, supports clinician judgment, and improves functional outcomes for individuals who rely on⏎orthotic and prosthetic technologies.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Dr. Gerald Stark, Ph.D, CPO/L, FAAOP (D)⏎President⏎American Academy of Orthotists and⏎Prosthetists♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0118♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Behavioral Health Information Technology Coalition, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Behavioral Health Information Technology Coalition♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington DC 20201⏎RE: Response to Artificial Intelligence Request for Information/Document Citation⏎#: 90 FR 60108⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎On behalf of the Behavioral Health Technology Coalition, I am writing to thank you for⏎the opportunity to respond to the ASTP Request for Information (RFI) published on⏎December 23, 2025. While each member organization has issued their own individual⏎response to the RFI, this correspondence reflects the broader views of the mental health⏎and substance use communities regarding the application of Artificial Intelligence (AI) to⏎health care.⏎This correspondence contains discussions of individual AI tools along with policy⏎recommendations regarding how some of this technology may be reimbursed by the⏎Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS). Noting the widespread⏎implementation of some AI tools among some mental health and addiction treatment⏎providers in the public and private sectors does NOT equal the endorsement of these⏎products by ANY of the signers of this regulatory reply.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The biggest single barrier to the adoption AI in the delivery of mental health and⏎addiction treatment services in the United States is that perception has completely⏎overwhelmed reality. Specifically, the public and congressional debate about the use of⏎consumer facing chatbots in the provision of something that no organization supporting⏎this letter would refer to as mental health counseling has blotted out the many extremely⏎productive AI behavioral health use cases helping patients with mental illnesses and⏎substance use disorders in America every hour of every day.⏎In turn, the biggest barrier is feeding our biggest fear: a replay of the 2009 HITECH⏎Act scenario whereby the CMS and Congress omit behavioral health providers as⏎they undertake the process of defining reimbursement mechanisms either for⏎individual use cases or the AI field as whole – in this instance because of a single⏎minded media focus on tragic examples (e.g., suicides, psychotic episodes) resulting⏎from the use of chatbots.⏎601 Massachusetts Ave, NW, Suite 520W, Washington, DC 20001 | 202.331.1120 | www.bhitcoalition.org⏎American Foundation for Suicide Prevention • American Psychological Association • Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness⏎• Centerstone • Jewish Federations of North America • Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute • Mental Health America • National⏎Alliance on Mental Illness • National Association of Counties • National Association of County Behavioral Health & Developmental⏎Disability Directors • National Association for Behavioral Healthcare • National Association of State Alcohol/Drug Abuse Directors •⏎National Association of Social Workers • National Council for Mental Wellbeing • Netsmart⏎⏎Chatbots: While there is no unified position within the Coalition regarding how chatbots⏎should be overseen at the state or federal levels, Dr. Vaile Wright’s testimony on behalf⏎of the American Psychological Association before the House Energy and Commerce⏎Health Subcommittee on September 3, 2025, offers an example of a potential path⏎forward. Dr. Wright recommended the following actions:⏎◼ “Establish Clear Regulatory Guardrails. We need a robust federal⏎framework that ensures safety and efficacy, including prohibiting the⏎misrepresentation of AI as licensed professionals and mandating transparency⏎and human oversight over clinical decisions.⏎◼ Protect Vulnerable Populations, Especially Youth. Adolescents are in a⏎critical developmental period. We must require age-appropriate safeguards⏎and robust data protections to support their healthy development.⏎◼ Prioritize Equity and Mitigate Harm. We must require that AI models⏎undergo rigorous, independent testing for harms across diverse populations⏎before they are deployed.⏎◼ Invest in Research and AI Literacy. The pace of AI development is⏎outpacing research. We need a significant federal investment in independent⏎research to understand AI's impacts, paired with comprehensive AI literacy⏎education.”⏎Dr. Tom Insel, the former Director of the National Institute of Mental Health (NIMH), in⏎a Substack article entitled, “Chatbot Safety: Red Teaming, Jailbreaking and Real World⏎Use” (Jan 26, 2026) also offered another important insight. Insel cited estimates that a⏎stunning 28 million Americans are using just one chatbot for emotional or psychological⏎help. “A RAND study reported that 13.1% of young people (22.2% for ages 18-21) used⏎a chatbot for mental health advice and 92.7% found the advice somewhat or very helpful.⏎It’s a tough call, but my guess is that currently there are still fewer people seeking mental⏎health care from chatbots than from human therapists. But not by much. And not for⏎long.”⏎Dr. Insel distinguishes between general use consumer-facing products like ChatGPT and⏎bespoke models like Ash, Therabot, Flourish and Slingshot that are built for one thing:⏎psychological support. It is up to the Food & Drug Administration (FDA) or other⏎regulatory bodies to determine the safety and efficacy of these products. But it’s⏎instructive how Dr. Insel concluded his Subtstack piece: “The future of mental health⏎may not be human or machine. It may be human and machine, thoughtfully aligned. The⏎question is not whether people will keep turning to chatbots. They already prefer chatbots⏎as non-judgmental, ever-present, trusted supports. The question is whether we will build⏎them wisely enough to deserve that trust.”⏎AI Behavioral Health Use Cases: The attached summary developed by Leavitt Partners in⏎2024 outlines a wide array of AI applications in the mental health and substance use⏎601 Massachusetts Ave, NW, Suite 520W, Washington, DC 20001 | 202.331.1120 | www.bhitcoalition.org⏎American Foundation for Suicide Prevention • American Psychological Association • Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness⏎• Centerstone • Jewish Federations of North America • Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute • Mental Health America • National⏎Alliance on Mental Illness • National Association of Counties • National Association of County Behavioral Health & Developmental⏎Disability Directors • National Association for Behavioral Healthcare • National Association of State Alcohol/Drug Abuse Directors •⏎National Association of Social Workers • National Council for Mental Wellbeing • Netsmart⏎⏎space. AI AND BEHAVIORAL HEALTH IS NOT EXCLUSIVELY ABOUT⏎CHATBOTS. Among the most widely deployed currently are:⏎◼ AI Scribe/Ambient Listening/Clinical Documentation : The behavioral⏎health field is experiencing a generational workforce crisis. AI tools can now⏎automatically generate clinical documentation in recognized notes formats during or⏎immediately following patient sessions. The tool summarizes medical histories including⏎symptoms, screening results, medication interactions and side effects, and dosage⏎modification recommendations supporting compliance with health record requirements⏎greatly relieving provider workload.⏎◼ Intake Management: AI tools can automate the many clinical intake⏎processes replacing manual data entry, collecting standardized patient information⏎support clinical decision making by freeing clinical staff from administrative duties and⏎ensuring consistent capture of accurate intake data.⏎Similar AI tools – also applied at intake – could assist with the integration of behavioral⏎health and primary care. Line primary care physicians and nurse practitioners could⏎employ AI tools to automate screening for depression, anxiety and suicidality.⏎Collaborative Care teams could employ AI dashboards for coordination and mood⏎disorder tracking.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What⏎HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability⏎to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎The impact of AI on behavioral health is no different than its projected impact on the⏎broader U.S. economy. These technologies could help a beleaguered workforce,⏎potentially produce improved clinical outcomes and generate unheard of efficiencies. But⏎these desired end states require new thinking on the part of CMS. For illustration⏎purposes, at present, most providers pay directly out of their overhead margins to⏎purchase AI scribe tools.⏎However, AI requires a reexamination of how CMS approaches Medicare payment⏎policy. Strictly speaking, ambient listening are tools are not just “note taking devices”⏎Because of the rich clinical information they contain, these AI tools are essential to – and⏎indistinguishable from – the Evaluation & Management Process engaged in by mental⏎health and addiction treatment clinicians every day.⏎Therefore, in response to RFI Question # 2, the BHIT Coalition specifically recommends⏎that Evaluation and Management (E/M) codes (99202–99499) be expanded to authorize⏎the reimbursement of AI Scribe/Ambient Listening/Clinical Documentation Tools.⏎601 Massachusetts Ave, NW, Suite 520W, Washington, DC 20001 | 202.331.1120 | www.bhitcoalition.org⏎American Foundation for Suicide Prevention • American Psychological Association • Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness⏎• Centerstone • Jewish Federations of North America • Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute • Mental Health America • National⏎Alliance on Mental Illness • National Association of Counties • National Association of County Behavioral Health & Developmental⏎Disability Directors • National Association for Behavioral Healthcare • National Association of State Alcohol/Drug Abuse Directors •⏎National Association of Social Workers • National Council for Mental Wellbeing • Netsmart⏎⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further⏎support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g.,⏎contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions⏎The Coalition has a twofold answer to this question – a broad policy response and an⏎immediate practical reply. First, from a policy perspective, HHS should consider the⏎establishment of a formal framework to define, evaluate and validate “mental health⏎efficacy” for generate AI-enabled tools by integrating clinical outcomes metrics, user⏎safety benchmarks, and AI-specific performance standards. The framework would⏎present an opportunity to drive alignment across agencies (e.g., CMS, ASTP/ONC,⏎SAMHSA) on adverse events, longitudinal endpoints and real-world data collection.⏎But there are more immediate opportunities. The Center for Medicare and Medicaid⏎Innovations (CMMI) has an Integration Behavioral Health (IBH) Demonstration Notice⏎of Funding Opportunity (NOFO) currently open for solicitation. CMMI should be⏎encouraged to make the AI behavioral health use cases outlined the two-page attachment⏎eligible for immediate reimbursement under the IBH model. Further, CMS should⏎consider making behavioral health providers of all types eligible for contracts, grants or⏎cooperative agreements to field – on a wide scale – the AI tools described in the Leavitt⏎Partners summary. If this technology really is capable of generating efficiencies, CMS⏎shouldn’t be afraid to test it with some of the most challenging patient populations.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Again, the Coalition has a twofold response to this question. HHS/CMS/ASTP should⏎consider the creation of a unified, federally supported registry to monitor the real-world⏎safety and efficacy of generative AI tools used in mental health and substance use⏎services. This would require collaboration with developers, EHR vendors, and digital⏎therapeutic companies.⏎But, as with Question # 4, there are practical considerations. First, ASTP and SAMHSA⏎just launched the USDCI+ BHIT Pilot in nine (9) sites. CMS and ASTP will certainly⏎want to wait for the results of that pilot project before moving forward to any AI clinical⏎certification and standards related activities. Second, our assumption is that the federal⏎agencies involved will collaborate with the major private sector certification bodies⏎including CARF, NCQA, etc.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎601 Massachusetts Ave, NW, Suite 520W, Washington, DC 20001 | 202.331.1120 | www.bhitcoalition.org⏎American Foundation for Suicide Prevention • American Psychological Association • Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness⏎• Centerstone • Jewish Federations of North America • Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute • Mental Health America • National⏎Alliance on Mental Illness • National Association of Counties • National Association of County Behavioral Health & Developmental⏎Disability Directors • National Association for Behavioral Healthcare • National Association of State Alcohol/Drug Abuse Directors •⏎National Association of Social Workers • National Council for Mental Wellbeing • Netsmart⏎⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new⏎insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎The Coalition cannot express an opinion on which AI tools met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations. As noted in the response to Question # 1, some of the most widely⏎utilized AI technology in the behavioral health space are AI scribes and intake⏎management tools.⏎With respect to novel AI tools that have the greatest potential to improve health care⏎outcomes and help reduce costs, perhaps the most interesting (without the intention of⏎providing an endorsement of any individual product) are AI technologies that identify⏎and aid clinical diagnosis of mental illnesses and developmental disorders (e.g., autism)⏎more accurately efficiently and accurately. For example, Tardive Dyskinesia (TD) is a⏎highly disabling movement disorder that is a common side effect of anti-psychotic⏎medications widely prescribed to treat schizophrenia and bipolar disorder. It requires at⏎least 20 minutes for a psychiatrist to administer the Abnormal Involuntary Movement⏎Scale (AIMS) test in order to diagnose TD. However, there are now video AI tools that⏎screen for the presence of TD via facial recognition clues in minutes.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care?⏎What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎In the context of behavioral health organizations, this question gets it wrong. Its not⏎“administrative” hurdles that obstruct the adoption of AI in clinical care. It is lack of clear⏎reimbursement pathways. Period. Full Stop.⏎8. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Finally, this question calls for subjective responses and its difficult for a Coalition that⏎includes multiple patient advocacy organizations to respond comprehensively. In general,⏎people with mental health and substance use disorders hope that AI tools, at a minimum,⏎can provide this population with greater control over their own health care records and⏎perhaps better coordination across the healthcare system.⏎The concerns of the behavioral health advocacy organization, again as a general⏎proposition, revolve around that lack of input that people with lived experience have had⏎in the development of the technology providing the basis for AI health care tools. In⏎addition, privacy concerns are pervasive. Once mental health and substance use patient⏎data is shared with an AI tool, who controls it?⏎601 Massachusetts Ave, NW, Suite 520W, Washington, DC 20001 | 202.331.1120 | www.bhitcoalition.org⏎American Foundation for Suicide Prevention • American Psychological Association • Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness⏎• Centerstone • Jewish Federations of North America • Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute • Mental Health America • National⏎Alliance on Mental Illness • National Association of Counties • National Association of County Behavioral Health & Developmental⏎Disability Directors • National Association for Behavioral Healthcare • National Association of State Alcohol/Drug Abuse Directors •⏎National Association of Social Workers • National Council for Mental Wellbeing • Netsmart⏎⏎◼ Who is the data shared with?⏎◼ Does law enforcement have access to it?⏎◼ Can it be used in employment decisions or family court?⏎Again, thank you for the opportunity to respond to this important Request for⏎Information. We end this correspondence where it began. The Behavioral Health⏎Information Technology Coalition strongly urges both ASTP and CMS not to repeat⏎the policy mistakes of the past.⏎Sincerely,⏎Alfonso V Guida, Jr.⏎Behavioral Health IT Coalition Coordinator⏎Email: aguida@guidelobby.com⏎Phone: 202.253.3386⏎601 Massachusetts Ave, NW, Suite 520W, Washington, DC 20001 | 202.331.1120 | www.bhitcoalition.org⏎American Foundation for Suicide Prevention • American Psychological Association • Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness⏎• Centerstone • Jewish Federations of North America • Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute • Mental Health America • National⏎Alliance on Mental Illness • National Association of Counties • National Association of County Behavioral Health & Developmental⏎Disability Directors • National Association for Behavioral Healthcare • National Association of State Alcohol/Drug Abuse Directors •⏎National Association of Social Workers • National Council for Mental Wellbeing • Netsmart♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0119♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Society of Diagnostic Medical Sonography, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Society of Diagnostic Medical Sonography♔♔♔♔♔♔The Society of Diagnostic Medical Sonography (SDMS) appreciates the opportunity to⏎comment on this Request for Information regarding the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence in clinical care. As the professional organization representing diagnostic⏎medical sonographers, the SDMS supports thoughtful integration of AI technologies that⏎enhance diagnostic quality, support clinical decision-making, and improve patient⏎safety. Note: AI tools should complement, not replace, the expertise of credentialed⏎sonographers and should not be used to justify reduced staffing, compressed exam⏎times, or expanded productivity expectations.⏎As AI tools are increasingly incorporated into imaging and clinical workflows, it will be⏎important that their use complements, rather than complicates, frontline clinical practice⏎and is implemented in a manner that supports appropriate clinical judgment and⏎accountability. Policies should promote transparency, appropriate clinical context, and⏎safeguards that prevent unintended increases in workload or erosion of diagnostic⏎quality. The SDMS encourages continued engagement with clinical professionals who⏎use AI-enabled tools in real-world care settings and stands ready to serve as a resource⏎as this work progresses.⏎Additional Questions/Answers⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎In sonography, barriers include limited integration of AI tools into real-time⏎imaging acquisition workflows, inconsistent interoperability with PACS and EHR⏎systems, and insufficient validation of AI performance in diverse clinical⏎environments. Tools developed without early and meaningful input from⏎sonographers may not align with clinical realities, reducing usability and slowing⏎adoption.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment⏎your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize clarity around the use of AI as a clinical support tool, rather⏎than as a substitute for clinical judgment. Payment and programmatic⏎approaches should encourage adoption of AI tools that demonstrate improved⏎quality or efficiency without introducing additional documentation requirements or⏎productivity pressures that could undermine diagnostic integrity.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing⏎governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability,⏎indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎AI use in imaging raises questions related to accountability, liability,⏎transparency, and data governance. HHS can play a constructive role by⏎supporting policies that reinforce clinician oversight, define expectations for⏎transparency in AI outputs, and promote clear accountability structures that⏎protect patients and clinicians alike.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation⏎methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and⏎other workflow and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care?⏎Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements,⏎and/or prize competitions)?⏎Effective evaluation methods should assess not only technical accuracy but also⏎clinical usability, workflow impact, and effects on diagnostic quality. The SDMS⏎encourages HHS to support evaluation frameworks that include real-world clinical⏎testing across varied practice settings and patient populations.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS could support collaboration between technology developers, professional⏎organizations, and third-party evaluators to establish best practice frameworks⏎for clinical AI use. Inclusion of frontline clinical perspectives in evaluation and⏎certification processes would help ensure AI tools are both safe and practical.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of⏎novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care⏎outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI has shown promise in areas such as automated measurements and workflow⏎support but has fallen short when tools are deployed without sufficient clinical⏎context or when they increase the validation burden on clinicians. Continued⏎emphasis should be placed on tools that demonstrably reduce redundant tasks⏎while maintaining or improving diagnostic quality.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical⏎care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in⏎clinical care?⏎⏎AI adoption is often driven by organizational leadership and informatics teams,⏎while successful implementation depends on acceptance by frontline clinicians.⏎Administrative hurdles include lack of standardized implementation guidance,⏎limited training resources, and unclear success metrics.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?⏎Enhanced interoperability of imaging data, including ultrasound images and⏎reports, would expand opportunities for AI development and validation.⏎Standardized data access would help ensure AI tools are trained and evaluated⏎using clinically representative datasets.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what⏎concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use⏎of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers are interested in AI that improves diagnostic accuracy,⏎reduces unnecessary repeat imaging, and supports timely care, while⏎maintaining clinician involvement and accountability. Concerns include data⏎privacy and the potential for AI to be used in ways that prioritize efficiency over⏎individualized care.⏎10.Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools⏎and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers⏎of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎The SDMS encourages HHS to prioritize research on AI applications that support⏎imaging acquisition quality, workflow efficiency, and integration of clinical context.⏎Research should also examine the impact of AI on workload, patient outcomes,⏎and equity across diverse populations and care settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0120♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Walker♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔Caption Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly, my first question after reading through this is whether there's a public hearing scheduled where I can actually speak to someone. I work better talking through issues like this than trying to get everything into a written submission, and there's a lot of nuance here that's hard to capture in a comment box.⏎⏎That said, since I'm not sure when or if that's happening, I'll try to put something useful down.⏎⏎The reimbursement classification issue is the thing I keep coming back to. We ran a claims analysis across our network in Q2 2024 and found that 68% of our AI-assisted diagnostic tools were getting coded differently depending on which payer was reviewing the claim, not based on what the tool actually does clinically. Same tool, same function, completely different determination. One payer calls it billable clinical decision support, another calls it non-reimbursable software. Our compliance team spent 47 days on average resolving each coverage determination request, and we logged 112 of those over an 18-month stretch. That's a lot of staff time that isn't going toward patient care.⏎⏎I don't know if HHS has the authority to just mandate a uniform classification framework across all payers or if that requires something more on the CMS side, but from where I sit as a CFO, the payer-by-payer fragmentation is the single biggest operational problem with AI adoption right now. It's not that the tools don't work. It's that nobody can agree on what category they belong to, and we're absorbing the cost of that disagreement.⏎⏎I'm also genuinely uncertain about the liability piece. Some of the tools we've deployed don't have a clean FDA classification and I'm not sure what that means for us if something goes wrong. I'd appreciate clearer guidance on that, though I recognize that's maybe a separate question from reimbursement.⏎⏎Is there a contact at HHS I should follow up with directly? The docket process is a little opaque to me in terms of what actually happens next with these comments.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0121♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Brown♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly my biggest frustration right now isn't even really about AI specifically, it's about what happened to our family doctor. Dr. Kenworth, who had a practice on Sunrise Boulevard in Fort Lauderdale for over 20 years, retired in March 2023 and he told us flat out it was the paperwork that broke him. Prior authorizations, documentation requirements, all of it. He said he was spending more time typing notes than actually seeing patients. And now we're supposed to add a whole new layer of AI tools on top of that same broken system?⏎⏎I do want AI to help with diagnosis. I really do. My son had a rough experience getting diagnosed with an autoimmune condition when he was eight and it took months and multiple doctors and I've thought a lot about whether better technology could have caught it faster. So I'm not against this at all.⏎⏎But what I keep coming back to is that before HHS pushes doctors to adopt more new technology, something has to give on the administrative side. Because if physicians are already overwhelmed, who exactly is going to have the time to learn new AI tools properly, check whether the AI is wrong, explain it to patients? You can't just keep stacking things onto people who are already burning out and leaving the profession.⏎⏎I genuinely don't know enough about the regulatory specifics here to comment on which exact programs or reimbursement codes need to change. I'm a parent, not a healthcare policy person. But it seems like common sense that if the goal is better patient care, you can't get there by ignoring the fact that the people delivering that care are drowning.⏎⏎Also I do have concerns about where patient data goes when these AI systems are involved. When Mateo was being diagnosed we had records at I think four different offices and I was never clear on who had access to what or whether any of it fed into some database somewhere. With AI the data question seems even bigger and I haven't seen clear answers about how families would be notified or protected. I'm not even sure this RFI is the right place to raise that but it felt relevant.⏎⏎I guess what I'd want HHS to do is think about physician capacity and burnout as part of the AI rollout conversation, not as a separate issue. Because if Dr. Kenworth was still practicing, he'd probably have thoughts about all of this too.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0122♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bynon, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Bynon♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Supplemental Submission to Docket HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Re: Empirical Observations Supporting Deterministic Semantic Memory Framework⏎Date: February 12, 2026⏎Original Submission ID: 0010⏎To the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology:⏎Following my prior submission (ID: 0010) addressing agentic drift in non-device AI systems⏎and proposing the concept of a deterministic semantic substrate for algorithmic⏎transparency, I respectfully submit this supplemental notice to provide empirical context⏎supporting that framework.⏎This supplement accompanies a baseline research report published by the Trust Publishing⏎Institute (TPI) titled:⏎The Medicare Digital Visibility & Compliance Shift: 2026–2028⏎The report documents publicly observable structural shifts in how AI-driven retrieval⏎systems (including Google AI Overviews, large language models, and AI-enabled browsers)⏎interpret and represent Medicare plan information. The findings reflect measurable⏎instability in AI-mediated plan interpretation when authoritative, machine-readable⏎semantic anchors are absent.⏎The purpose of this supplemental submission is not to introduce new regulatory⏎recommendations, but to provide empirical observations that reinforce the architectural⏎problem described in my original comment: non-device AI systems operating in regulated⏎benefit environments are prone to interpretive variance when policy meaning is not⏎embedded in structured, versioned, and retrievable formats.⏎Key observations documented in the report include:⏎• Increasing reliance of AI systems on machine-readable plan identity (Plan-ID entity⏎resolution)⏎• Measurable interpretive variance in cost-sharing, service areas, and benefit⏎categorization⏎• Reduced retrieval instability when structured, fragment-level plan representations⏎are published⏎• Reinforcement dynamics in AI-driven discovery environments favoring structured⏎sources⏎⏎These findings align with the deterministic semantic substrate model outlined in⏎Submission ID: 0010, which proposes shifting governance emphasis from model⏎inspection to content-layer determinism through structured semantic anchoring.⏎The Trust Publishing Institute (TPI) is a time-bound nonprofit research entity formed to⏎study structural conditions affecting AI-mediated information stability in regulated⏎healthcare domains. TPI does not seek designation as a standards authority. Rather, its role⏎is to document observable system behavior and evaluate architectural characteristics⏎correlated with improved retrieval stability.⏎The attached report reflects publicly observable system behavior and does not rely on⏎proprietary datasets or non-public regulatory materials.⏎I submit this supplement to ensure that the structural conditions documented in the⏎Medicare digital ecosystem are included in the administrative record for Docket HHS-ONC-⏎2026-0001, as they directly inform the governance considerations surrounding non-device⏎AI.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎David W. Bynon⏎Founder, Trust Publishing Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0123♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Electronic Privacy Information Center, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Electronic Privacy Information Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Please find attached the comments of the Electronic Privacy Information Center (EPIC).⏎⏎Best,⏎Sara Geoghegan⏎EPIC Senior Counsel♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0124♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Darvesh, Abdullah, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Abdullah♔Darvesh♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔PUBLIC COMMENT⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108 | RIN 0955-AA13 | Docket HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Submitted by: Abdullah Darvesh, MD⏎Capacity: Physician-Founder, CitaCell Health Solutions; PGY-III Internal Medicine Resident⏎Additional roles: Incoming Informatics Lead, NIH-funded NCORP Grant, Gibbs Cancer⏎Center; Incoming Student, Duke Master of Management in Clinical Informatics (July 2026);⏎Lecturer, Informatics in Medicine—Ethics, AI and More, USC School of Medicine (April 2026)⏎Date: February 22, 2026⏎I. Introduction⏎I am a third-year internal medicine resident who writes clinical orders on real patients every day.⏎I am also the founder of CitaCell Health Solutions, a healthcare AI compliance infrastructure⏎company with a patent-filed architecture called the Artificial Cellular Organization (ACO). I⏎submit this comment as both a frontline clinician who has spent three years observing AI’s⏎accelerating impact on clinical medicine and as a technical practitioner who has channeled that⏎experience into engineering a concrete solution to the data exposure problem this RFI seeks to⏎address.⏎My perspective may be useful to HHS because I sit at an intersection that few commenters⏎occupy simultaneously: I experience the clinical workflow that AI tools must integrate into, I⏎understand the data architecture that makes integration possible, and I am actively building the⏎security infrastructure that should govern the boundary between the two. I believe this RFI is⏎asking the right questions. I also believe the agency may be missing a critical perspective—⏎specifically, the perspective of clinicians who understand what data actually needs to cross trust⏎boundaries for AI to function, versus what data currently crosses those boundaries simply⏎because no one with clinical judgment is governing the scope.⏎II. Background: The Specific Problem This Comment Addresses⏎This comment addresses the intersection of three areas within the RFI: regulation (specifically,⏎whether current data protection frameworks are adequate for AI-integrated clinical⏎environments), reimbursement (whether payment policy can incentivize structurally secure AI⏎adoption), and research and development (whether HHS should fund investigation into⏎architecture-based data protection as a complement to policy-based protection).⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 1⏎⏎The RFI correctly identifies that HHS seeks to “protect patients and the confidentiality of their⏎identifiable health information” while accelerating AI adoption. The implicit assumption in this⏎framing—shared by most current compliance frameworks—is that these goals are in tension:⏎more AI means more data exposure, which means more risk, which means more protective⏎policy. I submit that this tension is an artifact of the current architectural paradigm, not an⏎inherent property of AI integration. If the architecture is redesigned so that identifiable patient⏎data never exists in exploitable form outside the health system’s trust boundary, then AI adoption⏎and patient protection become structurally aligned rather than structurally opposed.⏎I address below the RFI’s ten specific questions, grouped thematically where they intersect, and⏎provide specific recommendations with relevant regulatory citations.⏎III. Analysis: Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎Question 1: Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Adoption⏎I can describe this barrier from direct experience. When I began building an AI-powered revenue⏎cycle management tool for a multi-site allergy practice, the first question from the practice⏎owner—a physician—was not about accuracy or cost. It was: “Where does my patient data go?”⏎He had already evaluated three AI vendors. Each required access to complete patient records,⏎including names, dates of birth, and medical record numbers, to perform functions that do not⏎require any of that information. Identifying a CPT coding gap requires procedure codes,⏎diagnosis codes, and documentation elements. It does not require knowing who the patient is.⏎This physician’s hesitation is rational, not irrational. Under current frameworks, adopting an AI⏎tool means sending identifiable PHI to a system he does not control, governed by a Business⏎Associate Agreement he cannot enforce in real-time. His options are binary: full data exposure or⏎no AI. That is the barrier. It is not regulatory burden, and loosening compliance will not resolve⏎it. What resolves it is an integration architecture where the AI vendor receives only the de-⏎identified, scoped data elements its analytical function requires—and where even those elements⏎are architecturally protected so that no single system outside the trust boundary holds enough⏎information to reconstruct a patient record. I built this. It works. The barrier disappears when the⏎architecture changes, not when the policy relaxes.⏎Question 2: Regulatory and Payment Policy Changes HHS Should Prioritize⏎Regulatory recommendation 1: Recognize “Structural Compliance” as a distinct tier above⏎the HIPAA Security Rule baseline. The HIPAA Security Rule (45 CFR Part 164, Subpart C)⏎requires administrative, physical, and technical safeguards for ePHI. These safeguards assume⏎ePHI exists within systems and must be protected by controls. HHS should recognize a voluntary⏎designation for systems in which the architecture itself prevents ePHI from existing in⏎exploitable form—where security is achieved through structural nonexistence of data rather than⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 2⏎⏎structural protection of data. This is not a replacement for HIPAA. It is a higher tier for AI⏎integration contexts where data crosses trust boundaries to external vendors.⏎Regulatory recommendation 2: Update the Safe Harbor de-identification standard at 45⏎CFR 164.514(b). The current Safe Harbor method lists 18 identifier categories for removal.⏎Published research has demonstrated that individuals can be re-identified with as few as four data⏎points not included in this list. As AI vendors ingest increasingly granular clinical data—⏎procedure timestamps, medication dosing patterns, lab result trajectories—the re-identification⏎surface expands beyond what Safe Harbor currently addresses. More fundamentally, de-⏎identification alone is insufficient when the underlying architecture still transmits complete,⏎correlated clinical records to external systems. A structurally secure architecture should provide⏎independent protection layers beyond de-identification—ensuring that even de-identified data⏎cannot be reassembled into a complete clinical record by any single system.⏎Payment policy recommendation: Create a Promoting Interoperability or MIPS quality⏎component for structural PHI protection in AI integration. Payment policy is HHS’s most⏎powerful lever. If CMS recognized structural data protection as a measurable quality indicator—⏎analogous to how Promoting Interoperability incentivizes FHIR R4 adoption and e-prescribing—⏎health systems would have direct financial incentive to deploy architecturally secure AI⏎integration layers rather than relying solely on BAA-governed data sharing. This would shift the⏎market from competing on policy documentation to competing on architectural security.⏎Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical Device AI⏎The most critical novel issue is what I call the trust boundary problem. When a non-device AI⏎tool processes clinical data, that data crosses from the covered entity’s infrastructure into the⏎vendor’s infrastructure. Under 45 CFR 164.502(e), this crossing is governed by Business⏎Associate Agreements—contractual instruments that define obligations but do not change the⏎structural fact that identifiable data now exists in a system the covered entity does not control.⏎The BAA does not prevent the breach. It defines who is liable after the breach occurs.⏎From clinical practice, I can describe what this looks like on the ground. An ambient AI scribe⏎requires microphone access to the entire patient encounter and direct write access to the clinical⏎note. The vendor’s system processes the full audio—including the patient’s name, diagnosis, and⏎every detail discussed in the room—on external servers. The physician has no visibility into how⏎that audio is stored, for how long, or who else can access it. The BAA says the vendor will⏎protect it. But the data is there, in the clear, in someone else’s infrastructure. If that vendor is⏎compromised, every patient encounter processed through the system is exposed. No policy⏎prevents this. Only architecture can—by ensuring the vendor never receives identifiable data in⏎the first place, or by mediating the return path so that vendor outputs never directly enter the⏎clinical record without independent validation.⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 3⏎⏎HHS should consider whether middleware that mediates AI vendor integration should be held to⏎architectural standards governing both outbound data scoping (what leaves the trust boundary)⏎and inbound result validation (what re-enters the clinical record). The architecture I have⏎developed and filed patents on implements both: outbound data is scoped and protected so⏎identifiable information never reaches the vendor; inbound results are independently validated⏎before entering the clinical record rather than being accepted as vendor-written data.⏎Question 4: Most Promising AI Evaluation Methods⏎Pre-deployment: Evaluate the data exposure surface before evaluating the model. Before⏎assessing whether an AI model performs well, HHS should require assessment of how much PHI⏎the model receives. Does the vendor receive the minimum data elements required for its⏎analytical function? Is the data de-identified? Does the architecture prevent any single external⏎system from reconstructing a complete patient record? These are binary, testable architectural⏎questions—more actionable and more protective than probabilistic model performance⏎benchmarks.⏎Post-deployment: Deterministic replay audits, not log reviews. The most promising post-⏎deployment method is deterministic audit—where the audit trail is a recipe for reconstruction⏎rather than a recording. If the same inputs under the same rules produce the same output,⏎compliance can be verified by replay rather than by reviewing logs that may be incomplete or⏎tampered with. This makes audit contemporaneous with each transaction rather than retroactive.⏎Proven cryptographic methods exist to implement this. HHS should support development of⏎these evaluation tools through cooperative agreements (CRADAs) so health systems can apply⏎them to any AI integration, not just specific models.⏎Question 5: Supporting Private Sector Activities⏎Credentialing: Establish a Clinical AI Architect competency pathway. The individuals⏎governing what patient data crosses trust boundaries for AI processing should hold demonstrated⏎competency in clinical medicine, health informatics, and security architecture. The closest⏎existing credential is the ABPM clinical informatics subspecialty, which covers informatics⏎broadly but does not address the specific architectural security implications of AI integration.⏎HHS should support development of a credential—through ASTP/ONC or in partnership with⏎specialty boards—that certifies individuals to govern the clinical-data-to-AI pipeline. The people⏎scoping what data an AI vendor receives should be clinicians, not IT procurement teams.⏎Certification: Support industry-led structural compliance evaluation. HHS should⏎encourage—without prescribing—industry-led certification programs that evaluate AI⏎integration layers against structural compliance criteria: nonexistence of exploitable PHI, zero-⏎trust architecture including self-trust, compounding security across independent domains, and⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 4⏎⏎deterministic auditability. This parallels how ASTP/ONC supports but does not directly⏎administer health IT certification.⏎Question 6: Where AI Has Met or Fallen Short of Expectations⏎From direct experience deploying AI for revenue cycle management in a specialty allergy⏎practice: AI has met expectations in bounded analytical tasks with quantifiable ROI—identifying⏎CPT coding gaps, flagging modifier errors, detecting authorization discrepancies, and estimating⏎documentation-driven revenue recovery. In one case, an AI-identified authorization discrepancy⏎on a biologic medication (Xolair) flagged a documentation pattern that had resulted in⏎approximately $77,000 in unrecovered revenue over the prior year. The analytical function⏎worked because the task was well-defined, the scope was narrow, and the output was actionable⏎without ambiguity.⏎Where AI has consistently fallen short is in clinical decision support—and the failure mode is⏎instructive for HHS. Most clinical AI tools are designed and implemented by teams without⏎clinical workflow experience. The result is what I would call the bells-and-whistles problem:⏎tools that generate alerts, suggestions, and documentation requirements that physicians must then⏎review, verify, override, or acknowledge. Rather than reducing cognitive burden, these tools⏎effectively turn the physician into an auditor of the AI’s output. The physician’s clinical time is⏎redirected from patient care to managing the AI system itself—clicking through alerts, correcting⏎autogenerated notes, reconciling AI suggestions against clinical judgment. This is the opposite of⏎the productivity gain these tools promise.⏎The downstream effect is measurable: more documentation burden, more clicks per encounter,⏎more time spent on compliance with the tool rather than on clinical reasoning. Health systems⏎adopt these tools expecting cost reduction and receive cost displacement—the labor shifts from⏎administrative staff to physicians, who are the most expensive personnel in the organization.⏎When the individuals implementing AI tools do not understand clinical workflows, the tools⏎create bloat rather than efficiency. HHS should recognize that AI adoption is not inherently⏎beneficial—it is beneficial only when the tools are designed around clinical workflow realities,⏎governed by clinicians who understand those workflows, and evaluated against meaningful⏎clinical efficiency metrics rather than vendor-defined performance benchmarks.⏎The novel AI tools with greatest potential are not clinical decision support overlays but⏎structural compliance layers—integration middleware that enables health systems to engage⏎any AI vendor safely by controlling data exposure at the architectural level. The greatest cost⏎reduction will not come from any individual AI model but from the elimination of breach-related⏎liability, which costs healthcare organizations an average of $10.93 million per breach according⏎to the IBM Cost of a Data Breach Report (2023, healthcare sector), and from the reduction of⏎physician time wasted auditing poorly designed AI outputs.⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 5⏎⏎Question 7: Influential Roles and Administrative Hurdles⏎In the practices I work with, AI adoption decisions are driven by practice administrators, IT⏎consultants, and vendor sales teams. The physician’s role typically enters late—as a usability⏎reviewer, not as a data governance authority. The most consequential decision in any AI⏎integration—what patient data will cross the trust boundary, in what form, and for what⏎analytical scope—is made without clinical input.⏎This is backwards. A clinician who understands why a hemoglobin A1c is ordered, what it⏎means for a specific patient’s trajectory, and which elements of that clinical context are actually⏎required for a given AI inference is the only person qualified to determine what data should leave⏎the trust boundary. I call this principle “selective minimum information extraction”: the AI⏎receives only the specific, de-identified data elements required for its analytical function, and a⏎clinician with informatics training governs that scope determination.⏎HHS should encourage health systems to establish Clinical AI Governance roles—held by⏎practicing clinicians with informatics competency—with authority over data scoping decisions⏎for AI integrations. The primary administrative hurdle is not technology or cost. It is the absence⏎of an organizational role that places clinical judgment at the center of AI data governance.⏎Question 8: Enhanced Interoperability⏎FHIR R4 interoperability is essential for AI adoption, but it also widens the data exposure⏎surface. FHIR makes it technically straightforward to extract comprehensive patient records and⏎route them to external AI systems. This is a feature for clinical care coordination and a⏎vulnerability for AI vendor integration. HHS should pair interoperability incentives with⏎structural protection requirements for data that crosses trust boundaries for AI processing.⏎Specific gap: There is no FHIR-based standard for exchanging structural compliance⏎attestations, governance proofs, or architectural audit metadata alongside clinical data. When a⏎health system routes data to an AI vendor through a middleware layer, there is no interoperable⏎way for the receiving system to verify the sender’s data protection architecture, or vice versa.⏎HHS should sponsor development of a FHIR Implementation Guide for structural compliance⏎attestations—enabling systems to verify each other’s architectural protections in-band rather than⏎relying on out-of-band contractual assurances.⏎Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Concerns⏎From conversations with patients in my own practice: patients do not object to AI being used in⏎their care. They object to not knowing where their data goes. When I explain that an AI tool⏎helped flag a potential medication interaction, the response is positive. When I explain that the⏎tool required sending their full medical record to an external company’s server, the response⏎changes. The concern is not AI capability—it is data exposure.⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 6⏎⏎More consent forms will not address this concern. Patients are already overwhelmed by⏎paperwork they do not read. What patients deserve is an architecture where AI integration does⏎not require them to accept data exposure as a condition of receiving technologically enhanced⏎care. If the AI vendor never receives identifiable data—if the architecture makes it structurally⏎impossible for the vendor to know who the patient is—then the consent question becomes moot.⏎That is the standard patients deserve, even if they do not yet have the vocabulary to demand it.⏎Question 10: Priority Areas of AI Research⏎Priority 1: Clinician-led AI data governance. HHS should fund research into how clinician-⏎governed data scoping affects breach rates, data exposure surface, and AI output quality. The⏎hypothesis: selective minimum information extraction—where clinicians determine the⏎minimum data elements an AI vendor receives—improves both security and AI performance by⏎reducing noise in the input data. Multi-site studies through existing research networks, including⏎NIH NCORP, could validate this.⏎Priority 2: Architecture-based data protection. The ACO architecture I have developed⏎derives its structural properties from biological immune systems—not as metaphor but as⏎engineering specification. Biological systems have solved the core security problems healthcare⏎AI governance faces over billions of years of evolutionary pressure: share precisely what is⏎needed, verify without trusting, and ensure that compromise of any single component cannot⏎compromise the whole. HHS should fund applied research into bio-inspired security⏎architectures for healthcare data, including formal verification of biological design principles⏎translated into computational security properties.⏎Priority 3: Structural compliance measurement. No validated framework exists for measuring⏎whether an AI integration achieves structural compliance—whether data breaches are⏎architecturally impossible rather than merely policy-restricted. HHS should fund development of⏎measurement tools and testing methodologies, analogous to how NIST provides measurement⏎frameworks for cryptographic implementations.⏎Existing evidence: The IBM Cost of a Data Breach Report (2023) documents healthcare as the⏎highest-cost sector for breaches at $10.93M average per incident. The Ponemon Institute’s⏎healthcare cybersecurity studies consistently show that policy-based controls reduce but do not⏎eliminate breach frequency. Healthcare has been the most breached industry for over a decade⏎under the current policy-based paradigm. The literature supports investigating a paradigm shift⏎toward architecture-based protection.⏎IV. Recommendations⏎Based on the analysis above, I recommend the following specific actions:⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 7⏎⏎1. Recognize Structural Compliance as a distinct, voluntary tier of data protection above⏎HIPAA and SOC 2, applicable to AI integration contexts where clinical data crosses trust⏎boundaries to external vendors. Systems meeting this tier would demonstrate that patient data in⏎any persistent, addressable, complete form never exists within the AI processing pipeline—⏎enforced by architecture, not policy.⏎2. Update the Safe Harbor de-identification standard at 45 CFR 164.514(b) to address AI-⏎era re-identification risks from high-dimensional clinical datasets, and recognize that de-⏎identification alone is insufficient without additional architectural protections that prevent⏎reconstruction of complete patient records.⏎3. Create a CMS payment incentive (Promoting Interoperability or MIPS component) for⏎structural PHI protection in AI integration, shifting market incentives from policy⏎documentation to architectural security.⏎4. Encourage development of a Clinical AI Architect credential through ASTP/ONC or⏎specialty board partnerships, requiring competency in clinical medicine, health informatics, and⏎security architecture.⏎5. Fund NIH research into clinician-led AI governance, bio-inspired security architectures,⏎and structural compliance measurement, using existing multi-site research networks including⏎NCORP.⏎6. Sponsor development of a FHIR Implementation Guide for structural compliance⏎attestations, enabling interoperable verification of data protection architecture between health⏎systems, middleware, and AI vendors.⏎7. Issue guidance clarifying that middleware mediating AI vendor integration should meet⏎architectural standards governing both outbound data scoping and inbound result validation—⏎not just contractual standards under BAAs.⏎V. Conclusion⏎This RFI asks the right question: how can HHS accelerate AI adoption in clinical care while⏎protecting patients? The answer most commenters will offer is better policy—more guidelines,⏎updated regulations, clearer liability frameworks. I offer a different answer: better architecture.⏎Policy governs behavior around data that exists. Architecture determines whether data exists in⏎exploitable form at all. In an AI-integrated healthcare environment where clinical data routinely⏎crosses trust boundaries to external vendors, architecture is the more fundamental lever.⏎After three years of observing how AI is transforming clinical medicine—and how current⏎compliance frameworks fail to protect patients in that transformation—I built a concrete⏎implementation of this principle: the Artificial Cellular Organization, a patent-filed architecture⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 8⏎⏎that derives its security properties from biological immune systems. It is not the only possible⏎implementation, but it demonstrates that structural compliance is achievable, testable, and⏎deployable in clinical settings. A pilot deployment at a multi-site allergy practice is scheduled for⏎May 2026.⏎The perspective I bring—a practicing physician who builds the security infrastructure for the AI⏎tools he uses in clinical care—is precisely the perspective this process is designed to capture:⏎how a proposed policy direction would impact someone operating on the ground. From the⏎ground, I can report that the current compliance paradigm is not adequate for AI-integrated⏎clinical care, that structural alternatives exist, and that HHS has the regulatory and payment⏎policy levers to incentivize their adoption.⏎I am available to provide further technical detail, demonstrate the ACO architecture, or⏎contribute to any working group or advisory process the Department may convene.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Abdullah Darvesh, MD⏎Founder & CEO, CitaCell Health Solutions⏎Darvesh — HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Page 9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0125♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Torres♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔Arthritis Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔My members are not abstractions. They are people living with rheumatoid arthritis and other chronic inflammatory conditions in rural Arkansas, and a lot of them are Black women in their 50s and 60s who have been fighting for years just to get a doctor to take their pain seriously. So when HHS asks how to "accelerate AI adoption" in clinical settings, what I hear is: how do we move faster. And moving faster is exactly what worries me.⏎⏎There's a 2023 survey from the Digital Pathology Association that found only 12% of AI diagnostic algorithms had been tested on patient populations that actually look like the United States. Twelve percent. That means when a hospital in Little Rock or Texarkana deploys one of these tools, it is running a system that was probably validated almost entirely on white patients from academic medical centers with good insurance. And now a clinician sees a number or a flag or a risk score on a screen, and that output carries this weight of authority that a gut feeling never had. I've talked to patients who were told, essentially, that the system didn't flag anything serious. End of conversation.⏎⏎The question HHS is asking about barriers to adoption - I want to push back on the framing a little. The barrier being discussed seems to be regulatory friction, reimbursement gaps, that kind of thing. Those are real. I'm not saying they aren't. But the barrier I see from where I sit is that we don't have any requirement that these tools demonstrate they work equitably before they get rolled out. Not a voluntary commitment. An actual requirement. Because right now the incentive structure is pointing entirely one direction - get the product to market, get the hospital contract, get the billing code. The equity audit can come later. Except it doesn't come later. I've been doing this work long enough to know that once something is embedded in clinical workflow, it basically never gets pulled out.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to do specifically is require that any AI clinical decision support tool seeking reimbursement under Medicare or Medicaid demonstrate performance parity across racial and socioeconomic subgroups before approval, not as a condition of some future review but upfront. I know there are arguments that this would slow things down or that the datasets don't exist to do it properly. Both of those things might be partly true. But the answer to "we don't have good enough data on underserved populations" cannot be "so we'll just deploy anyway and see what happens." That logic has been used to harm these communities for a very long time.⏎⏎I also want to say something about informed consent because I don't think it comes up enough in these discussions. Patients in the Arthritis Foundation's network have told me they had no idea an AI tool was involved in reading their imaging or generating their treatment recommendations. That's not hypothetical. That was a conversation I had with a woman in Fayetteville in January of this year. She found out because she specifically asked. Most people don't ask because they don't know to ask. I don't think there's a clear federal standard right now requiring that patients be told when an AI system contributed to a clinical decision, and that gap seems like something HHS could actually address pretty directly without waiting for Congress.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0126♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-26T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Maxie, Emilie, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emilie♔Maxie♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play⏎to help address them?⏎One of the biggest challenges I see is the uncertainty about accountability when AI influences⏎clinical decisions but is not regulated as a medical device. For example, risk scores or⏎AI-generated summaries may shape how urgently a patient is evaluated or what information a⏎clinician focuses on, yet it is often unclear who is responsible if the tool is wrong or misleading.⏎This creates confusion around liability, standards of care, and how much clinicians are expected⏎to rely on or override these tools.⏎Privacy, security, and secondary use of clinical data are also real concerns, especially when AI⏎features are embedded into required workflows and cannot easily be turned off. Clinicians and⏎patients may not always know when AI is being used, what data it relies on, or how outputs are⏎generated.⏎HHS could help by setting clearer expectations for disclosure, so clinicians and patients know⏎when AI is involved and for what purpose. Basic governance norms, such as clearly identifying⏎who approves a tool, who monitors its performance, and who has the authority to pause or roll it⏎back if problems arise, would reduce uncertainty and support safer adoption.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation⏎methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow⏎and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care?⏎From a clinician’s perspective, evaluation cannot stop once a tool goes live. Before deployment,⏎AI tools should be tested on populations that reflect real patients, not just ideal datasets. This⏎includes understanding how performance varies by factors such as race, language, disability, or⏎care setting. Workflow testing is also critical. Tools should be observed in real clinical⏎environments where outputs are reviewed but not acted upon, to see how they affect⏎decision-making and workload.⏎After deployment, ongoing monitoring matters just as much. In practice, useful signals include⏎whether alerts fire too often, how frequently clinicians override recommendations, whether⏎performance changes over time, and whether certain groups experience more errors or missed⏎care. HHS could support this by encouraging practical, real-world evaluation and offering⏎simple, non-punitive ways for clinicians and organizations to report safety concerns or⏎unintended consequences.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎AI adoption would be safer if there were more consistent expectations for independent⏎evaluation. A basic certification approach for non-medical AI device used in clinical care that⏎⏎focuses on transparency, safety monitoring, and bias assessment, would help clinicians⏎understand what has been reviewed and what has not.⏎Plain-language “AI labels” would also be helpful. Similar to nutrition labels, these could briefly⏎explain what a tool is designed to do, where it performs well or poorly, and how it is intended to⏎be used. This would help clinicians and patients compare tools and set realistic expectations.⏎Question 6: What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve⏎health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎As a clinician, AI works best when it clearly saves time or improves reliability. For example, by⏎reducing documentation burden or flagging clinical risk in ways that are paired with clear next⏎steps. It tends to fall short when tools generate frequent, non-actionable alerts, add extra clicks,⏎or provide recommendations without enough context for clinicians to judge whether they are⏎appropriate.⏎The most promising use cases I see are the early detection of patient deterioration when paired⏎with clear escalation pathways, medication safety support, language access tools for patients⏎with limited English proficiency (with safeguards to verify accuracy), and care coordination tools⏎that help identify and support high-risk patients across settings.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎From my position, patients and caregivers want AI to make care safer, faster, and better⏎coordinated especially during handoffs and transitions. The main concerns are about privacy,⏎lack of transparency, biased or incorrect outputs, and difficulty correcting errors when AI plays a⏎role in decisions. Clear communication about when AI is used, appropriate patient notice, and⏎accessible ways to raise concerns or request review would help build trust.⏎Conclusion⏎From an individual clinician’s perspective, responsible AI adoption depends less on technical⏎sophistication and more on clarity, transparency, and ongoing oversight. Clear disclosure,⏎practical evaluation standards, and safeguards that protect both patients and clinicians would⏎help ensure AI delivers real benefits without shifting risk onto those providing or receiving care.⏎Emilie Maxie, DNP, RN, CCRN♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0127♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Psychotherapy Action Network, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Psychotherapy Action Network♔♔♔♔♔♔February 16, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail⏎Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: PsiAN Comment to HHS Health Sector AI RFI RIN 0955-AA13⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology:⏎Psychotherapy Action Network (PsiAN) is grateful to have the opportunity to provide a response⏎to the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for Information regarding⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care.⏎PsiAN is a nonprofit organization that advocates for access to psychotherapies that create⏎meaningful change, with a membership of over 6,500 individuals and 90 partner organizations⏎representing mental health clinicians and researchers, across all disciplines, as well as⏎administrators and users of therapy. Psychotherapy Action Network stands for ethical, effective⏎patient care and clinical standards, amplifies therapists’ voices, and advocates for direct reforms⏎that truly serve patients and clinicians.⏎HHS’ Request for Information solicits comments related to regulation, reimbursement, and⏎research and development in addition to supplemental specific questions. While the comments⏎offered by PsiAN are applicable to all forms of AI in healthcare, our remarks will often focus on⏎the behavioral health landscape and to issues related to provision of psychotherapy.⏎Regarding regulation: Currently, there is cause for great concern related to the privacy, efficacy,⏎and safety of AI in healthcare because there is no current federal regulation of AI in⏎healthcare. Regulation is essential, given the federal government’s central obligation to protect⏎the public. This is not something that can be left to the companies producing and selling these⏎products or to individual states, which could result in a patchwork of incompatible regulations.⏎Our government has already decided that regulation and oversight are essential in the⏎healthcare space to ensure safety and efficacy. For example, regulation and appropriate⏎⏎enforcement are important aspects of new products and treatments through the FDA. We⏎regulate healthcare clinicians through licensing requirements. We should not accept anything⏎less with AI, and perhaps should set the bar higher for an unproven technology.⏎AI chatbots, either specifically for mental health or other generalized models, are not HIPAA⏎compliant, therefore user/patient privacy is not protected by federal law. Privacy is legally⏎required by HIPAA in all healthcare industries and settings. Privacy and confidentiality are⏎cornerstones of effective psychotherapy, and the absence of these protections in the use of AI⏎Chatbots suggests a significant area of concern that demands correction.⏎Regarding research and development: Due to the novelty of this technology, there is not yet⏎enough data available to support efficacy and/or safety. We know that Large Language Models⏎(LLMs) are especially prone to “hallucinations,” with documented cases involving irrevocable⏎harm of the worst kind. For example, AI LLMs have encouraged vulnerable people struggling⏎with suicide to take suicidal action or provided them with information about the heights of⏎bridges or buildings from which to jump. This is unacceptably risky Chatbot behavior that literally⏎threatens lives.⏎Again, with reference to the behavioral health domain, an evidence base showing that these⏎products are effective and safe is lacking. The metrics that corporations are typically tracking,⏎such as number of downloads, user engagement, customer satisfaction, are not substitutes for⏎demonstrating effectiveness. AI products must be thoroughly researched and tested, as is any⏎new psychotherapy protocol or pharmaceutical, to measure effectiveness, dropout rates,⏎remission rates, etc.⏎Importantly, evaluation, assessment, safety and outcome research should be provided by a⏎separate, independent group of researchers to minimize the conflict of interest inherent if⏎corporations that develop these products also supply the evaluation and “science.”⏎Regarding AI instruments approximating psychotherapy, it’s desirable that the products advance⏎recovery, defined by SAMHSA as a process over time leading to a self-directed life. It is⏎insufficient to focus only on symptom management or reduction.⏎HHS advocacy to accelerate use of AI must follow and be linked to development of an evidence⏎base demonstrating safety and efficacy.⏎While PsiAN recognizes legitimate concerns related to access to mental health care, and the⏎hypothesis that AI tools and apps could help close that gap, we wish to highlight the risks⏎associated with the use of AI to provide clinical care in the behavioral healthcare industry,⏎especially without rigorous clinical trials, regulation, and robust oversight. At a minimum, HHS⏎should not accelerate the adoption of AI until the FDA completes its evaluations of AI as⏎medical devices. Given the FDA’s findings that well over 10,000 such applications are already⏎widely available to consumers without appropriate premarket performance evaluations, risk⏎management endeavors, or post-market evaluations, PsiAN recommends that HHS focus on⏎⏎developing research demonstrating safety and efficacy before accelerating⏎implementation.⏎Regarding supplemental specific questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for healthcare and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The privacy of data and corporate responsibility for poor outcomes are paramount. At⏎this point, the corporations developing AI tools are not able to ensure patient privacy,⏎and the legal and regulatory infrastructure holding them accountable for outcomes or⏎harms due to using their products is not well-established.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Uniform Federal Standards for privacy and safety are essential to adoption.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎HHS should contribute to creating uniform federal standards for privacy and safety⏎AFTER the FDA determines whether or not AI instruments are, in fact, medical devices,⏎and in a way consistent with that determination.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎We need more than bench testing and assessment of user experiences and outcomes.⏎We need robust outcome data about whether AI is making a difference in health-related⏎quality of life as opposed to mere symptom improvement.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎Accreditation and credentialing present serious challenges and must look beyond the⏎narrow financial interests of insurance entities or AI corporations.⏎⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎In behavioral health, decades of research have confirmed that the therapist-patient⏎relationship is the main predictor of outcome in psychotherapy. Since AI is not, in fact, a⏎human relationship, it is seriously limited as a potential clinical treatment tool. Much⏎more study and assessment over longer periods of time are necessary.⏎At this point, it is unclear how AI can best be used, whether as a support for⏎psychotherapists, a replacement for psychotherapists, or a tool more tightly focused on⏎insurance processing and administrative issues.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎While several states are adopting laws regulating AI mental health chatbots, these have⏎become a patchwork of requirements that differ from state to state and lack uniformity.⏎Hence, adoption of clearcut, science-based federal regulations is indicated to define how⏎all AI chatbots can ensure privacy, safety, and efficacy throughout the nation.⏎Without federal oversight, there are no meaningful incentives for the corporations⏎developing AI tools to self-regulate. HHS should work with clinicians and psychotherapy⏎researchers to define appropriate metrics to ensure product efficacy and safety. Clinical⏎metrics differ from those used by corporations developing and selling AI products, which⏎focus on measuring product profitability rather than safety and efficacy. Leaving⏎regulation and oversight in the hands of the corporations will not promote consumer⏎safety, privacy, or effective care.⏎In summary, AI Medical devices are not ready for accelerated adoption and deployment without⏎appropriate research or enforceable regulation. Even though LLMs are currently being used by⏎millions of people, this adoption by the public is far ahead of the essential science⏎demonstrating privacy, safety, and efficacy. Hence, regulation is essential to reduce the risk of⏎harm to consumers, ensure that the public has access to safe and effective clinical care, hold⏎corporations accountable for the products they develop and sell, and support uniform national⏎standards for AI Chatbots.⏎Should it prove helpful, we would be happy to testify in any hearings on this matter.⏎Sincerely,⏎Linda Michaels, PsyD MBA⏎Chair, Psychotherapy Action Network⏎www.psian.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0128♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from PointClickCare, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔PointClickCare♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Jim O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎US Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW Washington, DC 20201⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary and National Coordinator⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy /⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎330 C Street NW Floor 7, Mary E. Switzer Building Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically to http://www.regulations.gov⏎RE: HHS Sector AI RFI (2025-23641) (90 FR 60108)⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎PointClickCare (PCC) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Office of the Deputy Secretary and⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology (ASTP/ONC) Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI), as published in the December 23, 2025⏎Federal Register (RIN 0955-1113).⏎PCC is a leading health technology company with one mission: to help providers deliver exceptional⏎care. For over 20 years, we have been transforming how care is planned, delivered, and paid for across⏎the long-term and post-acute care (LTPAC) continuum. PCC has (2) two Certified EHR Technology⏎(CEHRT) systems and supports one of the largest LTPAC datasets in the United States, powering⏎AI‑informed care transitions, clinical outcomes, and reimbursement workflows. We are also setting a⏎new standard in interoperability as the only post‑acute–focused organization participating in all major⏎federal interoperability initiatives, including ONC certification, TEFCA connectivity through partners,⏎and standards‑based FHIR exchange.⏎More than 27,000 LTPAC organizations, 3,600 ambulatory clinics, 2,800 hospitals, 350 risk‑bearing⏎providers, 70 state and government agencies, and every major U.S. health plan trust PCC to help them⏎navigate a changing healthcare landscape. Our Marketplace of over 400 integration partners enables⏎customers to connect care, billing, and operations, driving efficiency and better outcomes. At PCC, we⏎are committed to building a stronger, more resilient healthcare system that fully includes older adults,⏎people with disabilities, and safety‑net populations served in post‑acute and senior care settings.⏎pointclickcare.com Page 1 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Executive Summary and Key Recommendations⏎We commend HHS for launching a “OneHHS” strategy on AI that explicitly links regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, and for seeking experience‑based feedback from those⏎building and deploying AI in clinical care. From our vantage point as a leading post‑acute EHR and⏎interoperability platform, we see AI’s potential to improve care transitions, reduce avoidable⏎hospitalizations, and alleviate workforce burden, particularly in LTPAC and Medicaid‑serving settings.⏎To realize that potential and support HHS’s three major levers, we recommend HHS prioritize five⏎cross‑cutting actions:⏎1. Clarify regulatory expectations for non‑device AI in clinical workflows⏎Establish a unified, risk‑proportionate framework for AI used in triage, summarization,⏎documentation assistance, risk stratification, and other non‑device decision support. This⏎should include clear guidance on risk categories, clinician oversight, liability allocation,⏎documentation and audit expectations, and permissible development and deployment⏎practices under HIPAA and other HHS authorities, aligned with ONC’s decision support⏎transparency concepts.⏎2. Align reimbursement with measurable outcomes and burden reduction⏎Modernize payment policy so that providers deploying validated AI tools that demonstrably⏎improve outcomes, reduce avoidable utilization, or reduce documentation burden can share in⏎the value created. This could include add‑on payments, shared‑savings recognition, or quality⏎measure credit within Medicare fee‑for‑service and value‑based programs, including CMMI⏎demonstrations targeted to post‑acute and senior care.⏎3. Support a real‑world AI evaluation “playbook” and shared benchmarks⏎Promote standardized, human‑centered evaluation methods, including safety, drift, bias, and⏎workflow impact tailored to LTPAC, SNF, and senior living environments. HHS can seed⏎evaluation toolkits, shared reference datasets, and benchmark measures through grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and public–private testbeds that smaller providers and vendors can⏎use to assess AI tools consistently.⏎4. Advance pragmatic interoperability and data quality for AI readiness⏎Prioritize USCDI and USCDI+ data elements and FHIR‑based exchanges most relevant to⏎post‑acute and senior care (e.g., real‑time ADT, medication, functional and cognitive status, and⏎social risk data) and ensure LTPAC providers are included in FHIR API and TEFCA⏎implementation efforts. Pair this with minimum data quality expectations and clear guidance⏎on data provenance so AI models have reliable inputs across care settings.⏎pointclickcare.com Page 2 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎5. Invest in post‑acute–focused AI research and multi‑site demonstration projects⏎Through CMMI models, NIH and AHRQ partnerships support testbeds that evaluate AI’s impact⏎on falls, readmissions, pressure injuries, medication safety, behavioral health, and care⏎transitions across acute–post‑acute boundaries. These efforts should embed equity, bias⏎mitigation, and small‑provider readiness into their design and include LTPAC providers as full⏎partners.⏎We strongly support HHS’s goals to foster public trust and confidence in modern technology solutions,⏎reduce uncertainty that impedes AI innovation, and align federal incentives so that AI enhances⏎productivity, reduces burden, lowers cost, and improves health outcomes for patients, caregivers, and⏎communities. The detailed responses below provide specific recommendations aligned with each RFI⏎question.⏎Why Post‑Acute AI Policy Requires Distinct Consideration⏎Digital and interoperability starting point⏎Post‑acute and long‑term care providers were largely excluded from the federal HITECH incentive⏎framework that accelerated EHR adoption in hospitals and physician practices. As a result, skilled⏎nursing facilities (SNFs), assisted living communities, and other LTPAC providers entered the modern⏎interoperability era without the same level of federal capital support, technical assistance, or⏎infrastructure standardization. Many post‑acute organizations have since achieved significant digital⏎maturity, yet interoperability gaps, particularly around real‑time ADT notifications, structured⏎laboratory feeds, and standardized functional and cognitive status data, remain more pronounced than⏎in acute care.⏎AI policy that assumes uniform digital infrastructure across settings risks unintentionally reinforcing⏎these disparities. To meet the goals of the Health Sector AI RFI, a “OneHHS AI” strategy must explicitly⏎account for the distinct starting point of LTPAC providers and ensure that data standards, certification⏎pathways, payment models, and technical assistance all include post‑acute environments.⏎Financing and workforce realities⏎Financing and workforce realities further differentiate post‑acute care from other sectors. Medicaid is⏎the dominant payer for long‑term care services, and reimbursement rates often operate on thin⏎margins that limit flexibility for capital investment. Unlike many hospital systems, most SNFs and senior⏎care providers are not positioned to absorb significant upfront technology costs without clear, aligned⏎incentives.⏎The workforce structure in post‑acute settings, characterized by interdisciplinary teams led by medical⏎directors and heavily supported by licensed practical nurses, certified nursing assistants, and care⏎coordinators, shapes how AI must be designed and governed. Tools that assume constant physician⏎presence, always‑on specialist coverage, or large internal analytics teams may not translate effectively⏎into SNF environments. AI policy must therefore reflect staffing composition, scope‑of‑practice⏎pointclickcare.com Page 3 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎considerations, and survey oversight dynamics unique to post‑acute care to ensure that innovation⏎enhances, rather than destabilizes, care delivery for older adults and individuals with disabilities.⏎We encourage HHS to view post‑acute AI not as a downstream extension of hospital AI, but as a⏎distinct policy domain with tailored incentives, measurement strategies, and technical assistance to⏎avoid widening existing gaps in quality and equity.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. Biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎In summary, the biggest barriers we observe in post‑acute and senior care are: (1) regulatory ambiguity⏎for non‑device AI, (2) misaligned payment incentives, (3) fragmented interoperability and state‑level⏎variation, and (4) capital and workforce constraints that limit the ability of LTPAC providers to evaluate⏎and govern AI tools.⏎First, regulatory uncertainty surrounding non‑device AI tools creates hesitation among providers. Many⏎AI‑enabled tools, such as readmission prediction models, documentation assistance systems, and risk⏎stratification tools, do not meet the definition of regulated medical devices but meaningfully influence⏎clinical workflows and reimbursement. Providers remain uncertain about how FDA, ONC, CMS, and⏎state regulators will interpret the use of these tools in audits, surveys, and enforcement contexts. In⏎risk‑averse post‑acute environments, ambiguity leads to defensive non‑adoption.⏎Second, payment incentives are frequently misaligned. Under fee‑for‑service Medicare, providers bear⏎the costs of AI implementation while savings from avoided hospitalizations or improved coordination⏎often accrue primarily to payers. Although AI adoption is more financially rational in capitated or⏎shared‑savings models (such as certain risk-bearing entities or CMMI arrangements), many post‑acute⏎providers remain outside such arrangements.⏎Third, fragmented interoperability and incomplete data flows constrain both model development and⏎deployment. Post‑acute providers were historically excluded from earlier federal health IT incentive⏎programs, and access to structured laboratory data, medication feeds, and real‑time ADT notifications⏎remains inconsistent. AI systems depend on reliable, cross‑setting data inputs; underrepresentation of⏎LTPAC and dual‑eligible populations in training datasets also limits generalizability.⏎Fourth, state‑level fragmentation presents an additional barrier. Because post‑acute care is heavily⏎regulated by state survey agencies and Medicaid programs, variation in privacy interpretations,⏎documentation standards, and oversight practices complicates multi‑state AI deployment. Federal⏎coordination and model guidance could reduce this variability while preserving state authority.⏎Finally, capital and workforce constraints are particularly acute in SNF and senior care environments.⏎Many facilities operate on thin margins and lack internal data science resources to independently⏎evaluate model performance, assess bias, or monitor drift. Without structured evaluation support,⏎providers may either avoid adoption or deploy tools without sufficient governance.⏎pointclickcare.com Page 4 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎2. Regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes HHS should prioritize⏎HHS should prioritize development of a unified, risk‑proportionate framework for non‑device AI used⏎in clinical workflows. This framework should clarify expectations for clinician oversight, documentation⏎integrity, transparency when AI assists in drafting clinical notes or generating predictive insights, and⏎permissible training data practices under HIPAA. It should also reinforce expectations for bias testing⏎and equity impact assessment consistent with civil rights obligations and align with ONC’s emerging⏎transparency requirements for decision support interventions.⏎Rather than broad liability protections, HHS could consider structured accountability pathways when⏎validated AI tools are deployed within defined governance frameworks and with documented clinician⏎review. For example, guidance could clarify that AI‑assisted documentation where clinicians retain final⏎review and attestation does not by itself constitute fraudulent intent, while still requiring appropriate⏎monitoring and audit trails. Predictable oversight encourages responsible innovation without⏎weakening safeguards.⏎On the payment side, CMS should explore mechanisms that recognize validated AI‑enabled⏎interventions within value‑based programs such as the SNF Value‑Based Purchasing Program and⏎related Quality Reporting Programs. Transitional, time‑limited innovation add‑on payments,⏎shared‑savings recognition, or quality measure credit may help ensure adoption is not limited to large⏎risk‑bearing entities. Any payment recognition should be tied to measurable outcome improvement,⏎such as reduced avoidable hospitalizations or improved functional outcomes and include guardrails to⏎prevent inappropriate coding or utilization changes.⏎Cross‑agency coordination among ONC, CMS, FDA, and OCR would further reduce conflicting signals⏎and streamline expectations for developers and providers. A coordinated “AI in Clinical Workflows”⏎council or task force could align regulatory guidance, transparency expectations, and payment models⏎under the broader HHS AI Strategy.⏎3. Novel legal and implementation issues for non‑medical device AI and HHS’s role⏎Non‑device AI raises complex questions regarding responsibility allocation between developers and⏎providers, documentation expectations, audit discoverability, and contractual risk allocation.⏎Clarification is needed regarding how clinician review should be documented when AI assists in⏎drafting content, and how providers should approach governance and monitoring responsibilities over⏎time.⏎HHS can support responsible implementation by publishing model governance frameworks and⏎encouraging transparency in data stewardship practices, including disclosure of secondary model⏎training uses. Providers should understand how their data may be used to refine or commercialize⏎models and what safeguards apply.⏎AI‑specific cybersecurity risks also warrant attention. Model manipulation, prompt injection, and⏎third‑party dependency vulnerabilities represent evolving risks that should be incorporated into⏎existing health sector cybersecurity guidance and incident response expectations.⏎pointclickcare.com Page 5 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Implementation challenges are particularly acute for smaller and independent SNFs that lack in‑house⏎data science or advanced IT teams. Many facilities do not have the capacity to independently validate⏎models, audit for bias, or design governance processes. Standardized evaluation templates, shared⏎reference datasets, and technical assistance models tailored to LTPAC settings would help ensure⏎consistent safeguards and reduce “defensive non‑adoption.”⏎4. Evaluation methods, metrics, robustness testing, and human‑centered evaluation⏎Evaluation should scale to risk: assistive documentation tools require different oversight than⏎predictive triage systems or deterioration alerts that may influence clinical intervention. Outside of the⏎medical device context, there is currently no widely adopted U.S. standard for risk categorization of⏎non-device clinical AI tools, even as other jurisdictions develop risk-based governance approaches.⏎HHS could promote consistency by articulating a clear risk taxonomy for non-device AI used in clinical⏎care, such as high‑, medium‑, and low‑impact categories, to proportionally scale evaluation depth,⏎documentation expectations, and post-deployment monitoring requirements.⏎In doing so, HHS may wish to consider established international risk frameworks, including the impact-⏎based approach by the International Medical Device Regulators Forum (IMDRF) in its Software as a⏎Medical (SaMD) guidance, which stratefies oversight based on the significance of information provided⏎and the state of the healthcare situation. While non-device AI tools may fall outside formal SaMD⏎scopre, the IMDRF model provides a structured way to assess clinical impact and could serve as a⏎conceptual reference point. Other quantitative and qualitative approaches, such as impact scoring⏎based on workflow dependency, automation level, or potential oversight model. Referencing an⏎established risk framework would promote clarity and alignment without creating unnecessary⏎duplicative regulatory structure.⏎Because many post-acute providers lack internal analytic resources, HHS could fund development of⏎standardized evaluation playbooks, metric sets, and benchmarking tools tailored to LTPAC⏎environments through grants, cooperative agreements, and prize competitions. These toolkits would⏎help small SNF operators interpret performance metrics, assess impact categories, ask more informed⏎questions of vendors, and frame governance decisions around safety, bias, and reliability.⏎5. Supporting private sector accreditation, certification, and credentialing⏎Expansion of voluntary certification frameworks addressing AI transparency, explainability, risk⏎management, and monitoring would promote consistency across vendors. The ONC Health IT⏎Certification Program already incorporates required transparency criteria for decision support⏎interventions in certified EHR technology; this framework provides a useful foundation that could be⏎extended or adapted to address emerging AI-specific governance considerations such as model⏎monitoring, drift management, and documentation of training data provenance. Coordination with⏎accrediting bodies could further integrate AI governance readiness into organizational standards so⏎that oversight expectations are aligned across technology developers and provider organizations.⏎pointclickcare.com Page 6 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Additionally, clinician AI literacy should become part of continuing education expectations. Licensed⏎professionals must understand model limitations, potential bias, appropriate override practices, and⏎documentation responsibilities. HHS should work with licensing boards and professional societies to⏎integrate core AI literacy into continuing education and competency frameworks so that AI augments,⏎not replaces, licensed staffing requirements or professional judgment.⏎6. Where AI has met or fallen short; high‑potential tools⏎AI has demonstrated promise in readmission risk prediction, documentation summarization, and⏎medication reconciliation when embedded directly into workflows. In high‑volume SNF environments,⏎documentation summarization tools can reduce chart review time and allow reallocation of nursing⏎attention to resident care.⏎However, predictive tools that lack explainability or workflow integration often fail to produce⏎measurable impact. Standalone dashboards or alerts that are not aligned with existing team processes⏎or care pathways have limited real‑world value and can contribute to alert fatigue.⏎High‑potential areas for post‑acute and senior care include deterioration prediction, behavioral health⏎and cognitive status monitoring, automated quality measure abstraction, and AI‑assisted⏎patient‑to‑setting matching across acute and post‑acute transitions. HHS can help ensure that⏎evidence from these use cases is captured and linked to payment and regulatory policies that reward⏎proven value.⏎7. Decision makers, governing bodies, and administrative hurdles⏎In SNF and senior care settings, adoption decisions for AI‑supported tools are typically led by medical⏎directors, chief or director‑level nursing leaders, and IT leaders (such as a VP of IT or CIO), with⏎compliance, privacy, and finance leaders playing critical review roles. Governance bodies such as QAPI⏎committees often play a central role in evaluating and monitoring AI tools once deployed.⏎The primary administrative hurdles are the cross‑functional coordination required security and privacy⏎reviews, legal and contracting cycles, clinician training, workflow redesign, and change management,⏎layered on top of capital constraints and uncertainty about how regulators and surveyors will interpret⏎AI‑supported documentation and workflows. Model contracting templates, governance guidance, and⏎clear survey expectations would reduce friction and promote consistent safeguards.⏎8. Interoperability to widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate AI⏎AI scalability depends on improved interoperability across the care continuum. Real‑time ADT feeds,⏎structured medication and laboratory data, harmonized functional and cognitive assessments and⏎standardized social needs data are critical inputs for predictive and care coordination tools. When⏎these data elements are incomplete or delayed at transitions between hospitals and post-acute care⏎settings, AI models lose reliability and care coordination suffers.⏎Expansion of USCDI and USCDI+ to reflect post‑acute and senior care data elements will strengthen⏎model accuracy and ensure inclusion of Medicaid‑serving populations. Inclusion of LTPAC providers⏎pointclickcare.com Page 7 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎and their vendors in FHIR‑based API initiatives and TEFCA implementation is essential to ensure⏎cross‑setting data completeness. We encourage HHS to ensure that TEFCA exchange purposes, FHIR⏎API policies, and USCDI expansion explicitly include LTPAC‑relevant use cases so that cross‑setting AI⏎tools do not “break” at the post‑acute boundary.⏎In addition to transactional interoperability, HHS could strengthen AI readiness through development⏎of controlled-access, de-identified reference datasets designed specifically for research, validation, and⏎benchmarking in post-acute and safety-net populations. These datasets should be structured to⏎support model evaluation and fairness testing, rather than commercial product replication, and should⏎include longitudinal cross-setting indicators such as Admission/Discharge/Transfer (ADT) events,⏎standardized assessment variables, medication categories, and outcome measures (e.g.,⏎rehospitalization, falls, pressure injuries) with consistent documentation of data provenance and⏎limitations.⏎Conceptually, this approach could resemble CMS Limited Data Set (LDS) files in its use of HIPAA-⏎compliant de-identification and governed data use agreements, but be purpose-built for AI validation⏎in LTPAC populations. Access should be tiered, subject to appropriate data use agreements, and⏎focused on benchmarking and methodological transparency rather than real-time operational⏎deployment. Such infrastructure would help smaller providers, researchers, and emerging vendors⏎assess subgroup performance, compare outcomes across representative populations, and strengthen⏎model accountability, while preserving the proprietary, workflow-integrated datasets that drive real-⏎world implementation and care delivery.⏎By clearly distinguishing benchmarking infrastructure from operational data exchange, HHS can⏎promote equity, reduce duplication of effort, and lower barriers to responsible AI development⏎without disrupting the competitive and innovation dynamics that enable vendors and providers to⏎invest in advanced data integration and care transformation.⏎9. Patient and caregiver priorities and concerns⏎Patients and caregivers in post‑acute and senior care settings prioritize fewer avoidable⏎hospitalizations, earlier detection of clinical decline, improved medication safety, and more clinician⏎time at the bedside. They want AI to help coordinate care across facilities and providers, reduce⏎paperwork, and make it easier to navigate complex care plans.⏎Concerns focus on privacy and security, transparency, potential algorithmic bias, and overreliance on⏎automation. There is legitimate worry that AI systems might treat older adults, people with disabilities,⏎or individuals with complex social needs unfairly or opaquely.⏎HHS can promote trust by encouraging plain‑language AI disclosures and embedding patient and⏎caregiver perspectives into evaluation and oversight frameworks—for example, through advisory⏎panels, user testing, or public comment on key guidance. Embedding these perspectives will help⏎ensure that AI tools respond to real‑world concerns about dignity, autonomy, and safety.⏎pointclickcare.com Page 8 of 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎10. Priority AI research areas and literature considerations⏎HHS should prioritize research in LTPAC populations focusing on functional outcomes, falls,⏎readmissions, pressure injuries, medication‑related harm, and implementation science in⏎resource‑constrained environments. Research should also examine how AI can support workforce⏎augmentation and burden reduction without reducing necessary staffing or eroding human⏎relationships in care.⏎Emerging literature suggests that AI tools can reduce readmissions, improve early detection of⏎deterioration, and increase documentation efficiency when properly validated and embedded in⏎workflows, but that performance often degrades when models are deployed outside the populations⏎or settings in which they were trained. This reinforces the need for local validation, fairness testing,⏎and ongoing monitoring, particularly in Medicaid and dual‑eligible populations.⏎These research priorities can be advanced through targeted NIH and AHRQ funding calls and CMMI⏎demonstration models that include post‑acute and Medicaid‑serving providers as full partners, not just⏎downstream data sources.⏎Conclusion⏎Artificial intelligence can strengthen quality, reduce burden, and support value‑based care across the⏎continuum. In post‑acute and senior care settings, success will depend on regulatory clarity and⏎risk‑proportionate oversight, payment alignment that rewards measurable improvements,⏎interoperability inclusion and data quality, standardized evaluation support, transparent data⏎stewardship, and strong governance that maintains human oversight and protects equity.⏎PCC welcomes continued engagement with HHS to ensure AI improves care for older adults, people⏎with disabilities, and Medicaid‑serving populations in ways that are safe, equitable, and accountable.⏎We stand ready to partner with HHS and its agencies through pilots, testbeds, and advisory efforts to⏎help make the HHS AI Strategy and Health Sector AI RFI goals a reality.⏎Sincerely,⏎Dr. Eugene Gonsiorek, PhD⏎VP Clinical Regulatory Standards⏎PointClickCare⏎pointclickcare.com Page 9 of 9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0129♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Texas Medical Association , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Texas Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building; Mail Stop 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted Via Federal Register⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎On behalf of the Texas Medical Association (TMA) and our more than 60,000 physician and medical⏎student members, we thank the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for the opportunity to respond to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎TMA is a private, voluntary, non-profit association and the largest state medical society in the nation. It⏎was founded in 1853 to serve the people of Texas in matters of medical care, prevention and cure of⏎disease, and improvement of public health. Today, its vision is “improving the health of all Texans.”⏎TMA understands the importance of proceeding carefully related to how artificial intelligence (AI) is⏎developed, implemented, and used in health care.⏎TMA distinguishes between the terms “augmented intelligence” and “artificial intelligence.” We agree⏎with the industry view that artificial intelligence replaces human function and intelligence such as a self-⏎driving car or a computer-generated chatbot on a website. Augmented intelligence provides prompts and⏎information to support human decision-making rather than replace it. We believe that is an important⏎distinction. Augmented intelligence is intended to co-exist with human decision-making – it should not⏎be used to replace physician reasoning and knowledge and should neither replace nor diminish the⏎patient-physician relationship. Throughout the letter, “AI” is used for brevity, but we ask that you keep⏎in mind TMA’s preferred terms.⏎TMA supports the use of augmented intelligence when used appropriately to support physician decision-⏎making, enhance patient care, and improve public health. Augmented intelligence should also be used in⏎ways that reduce physician burden and increase professional satisfaction.⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎At the same time, sufficient safeguards should be in place to assign appropriate liability inherent in⏎augmented intelligence to the software developers and not to those with no control over the software⏎content and integrity, such as physicians and other users.⏎RFI Question 1. What are the biggest barriers to private-sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: There must be trust that the AI tools are developed transparently and that data accuracy,⏎patient safety, and clinical outcomes are prioritized during all phases of product development. This⏎includes the ability to easily access and assess algorithms, algorithm provenance, training data⏎assumptions, training data source(s), and performance characteristics.⏎AI tools used for clinical care need seamless integration with electronic health records (EHR) and other⏎technology tools used by physicians so precise patient information is used to inform the decisions⏎returned by AI tools. One barrier is some EHR vendors are developing their own AI tools and may⏎choose to prevent other clinically validated tools from integrating with their proprietary EHR⏎technology.⏎The initial investment can be high and is a concern and barrier when adopting new technologies in⏎medical practice. Before savings are realized from the adoption of a technology, the investment must be⏎made, the technology implemented, the technology outputs validated, and finally the technology⏎optimized. Once optimization occurs, cost savings may then be realized in reduced human resources or⏎improved data quality output resulting in practice efficiencies.⏎RFI Question 2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎TMA Response: Developers and users need regulatory clarity and consistency when developing and⏎implementing AI.⏎There is uncertainty around disclosure and consent and when they apply, which is based on how the AI⏎tools and vendors are using patient data. Additionally, there is no clear guidance on how to disclose or⏎obtain consent when a physician orders diagnostic testing for the patient and it is unknown if and how a⏎pathologist or radiologist may use AI when interpreting tests. It would also be helpful to have vendor⏎transparency around HIPAA claims and stated HIPAA compliance as well as compliance with any other⏎applicable regulations.⏎Mandating specific consents for specific AI technologies will become increasingly difficult as the⏎number of solutions increase. Having disparate federal and state requirements will prove challenging to⏎AI users in clinical care.⏎As the use of AI in health care becomes ubiquitous, there are new ways to protect patient data.⏎Physicians and other health care covered entities are increasingly incorporating large language models⏎(LLMs) into their practices. With AI tools, insights from the LLMs can be used for retrospective and⏎prospective analysis of patient outcomes and physician performance. The information generated in AI⏎tools using patient data should be protected and considered privileged and confidential and should not be⏎used to penalize physicians.⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎Additionally, there should be a safe-harbor framework for physicians and other clinicians using HHS-⏎recognized or validated tools as intended and when the physician documents appropriate review of AI-⏎generated data.⏎RFI Question 3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues⏎exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎TMA Response: Use of AI, machine learning, and clinical decision support has inherent risks. Legal and⏎ethical responsibility for the use and output of these products must be assumed by, including but not⏎limited to, developers, distributors, and users with each entity owning responsibility for its respective⏎role in the development, dissemination, implementation, and use of products used in clinical care. Many⏎times, the end-user licensing agreements presented by technology vendors do not give options for⏎negotiation, and the vendors fully indemnify themselves from algorithmic errors or other issues that may⏎arise. It would be helpful to prevent this type of indemnification that undermines shared responsibility.⏎Physicians need protection from medico-legal liability when AI tools have insufficient training data,⏎incorrect information in the training model, or biases in the data used to train the model that could⏎produce faulty outputs. To increase user confidence, AI tools must have some reliability and⏎reproducibility.⏎AI tools should never independently and unilaterally make decisions about a patient’s diagnosis or care.⏎There should also be shared responsibility with AI developers and deployers of AI that the products⏎have some level of reliability and reproducibility. Just as EHRs undergo a certification process to verify⏎the product performs certain functions, AI tools used in clinical care could undergo a similar verification⏎process.⏎Once implemented, all AI tools should have a feedback loop that allows users an easy way to report⏎potential safety hazards as well as problems and malfunctions. This feedback loop can also serve as an⏎opportunity to recommend AI tool improvements.⏎RFI Question 4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care?⏎TMA Response: AI algorithms must be designed for the benefit, safety, and privacy of the patient.⏎Algorithms and other information used to derive the information presented as AI to physicians and other⏎clinicians should:⏎• Be developed transparently in a way that is accessible, explainable, and understandable to⏎clinicians and patients and that details the benefits and limitations of the clinical decision⏎support, and/or augmented intelligence;⏎• Have reproducible and explainable outputs;⏎• Function in a way that promotes health equities while eliminating potential biases that exacerbate⏎health disparities;⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎• Use best practices for a user-centered design that allows for efficient and satisfactory use of the⏎technology;⏎• Safeguard patient information by employing privacy and security standards that comply with⏎HIPAA and state privacy regulations;⏎• Have a feedback loop that allows users who identify potential safety hazards to easily report⏎problems and malfunctions and that allows for opportunities to report methods for⏎improvements; and⏎• Contain a level of compatibility to allow use of information between hardware and software⏎made by different manufacturers.⏎It would be helpful to assess AI algorithms with standardized disclosures that include intended use,⏎limitations, known failure modes, and independent evaluation pathway. Additionally, when available,⏎vendors should disclose to their users the results of third-party evaluations AI vendors have undergone,⏎which could include data on accuracy, known limitations, and, if applicable, the populations or settings⏎in which the tool was validated. There should be an expectation of post-deployment accountability,⏎including mechanisms to monitor the AI performance over time, identify data drift or bias, and provide⏎clear feedback and remediation pathways to users when issues arise. Other useful metrics include⏎workflow impact, time saved, error severity, and downstream clinical decisions.⏎RFI Question 5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: Accreditation and certification should be a carefully considered pathway. While it does⏎instill confidence in users, it also has the potential to slow innovation. At the very least, HHS should⏎consider required standardized reporting by AI vendors that includes information related to intended use⏎of technology, training data sources and limitation, validation settings, and known failures modes. This⏎information could be displayed in a standardized way similar to what is required for nutritional labels on⏎food products.⏎RFI Question 6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest⏎potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎TMA Response: Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act, HHS, and the Office of Civil Rights mandates⏎the availability of qualified interpreters for patients with limited English proficiency or disabilities.⏎Accessing interpreters can be cost prohibitive for some practices and can cost more than the payment for⏎the service provided. Cost savings can be accrued if virtual and digital interpreters are permitted, as long⏎as validation exists for translation accuracy.⏎To gain insight, TMA surveyed its members in 2025 and asked respondents how they are currently using⏎or wanting to use AI in their practice (see figure 1). Physicians are using tools that reduce administrative⏎burden but want to use tools for clinical decision support and quality improvement.⏎The use of ambient scribes has proven to be a successful use case of an AI tool that reduces⏎administrative burden and assists physicians by allowing them to focus on the patient rather than the⏎computer. Physicians also desire AI tools that can assist with prior authorizations and appeals (while⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎respecting the need for physician clinical judgment), as prior authorization requirements have become so⏎administratively burdensome to practices that additional staff are needed to handle the extra work.⏎Physicians are still hesitant, however, to use clinical decision support tools because of the lack of⏎validation and potential for data drift. Without some medico-legal protections, use of clinical decision⏎support tools will continue to be hampered.⏎Figure 1⏎RFI Question 7. Which role(s), decision-maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: Because of the wide variety of health care entities, the roles of the decision-makers vary⏎greatly. For example, in a small independent practice, the physician and office manager will wear many⏎hats. In a large integrated multi-specialty practice, there may be a chief medical information officer and⏎a chief technology officer leading the decision-making process on all technology tools, including AI,⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎used by the organization. Some of the primary hurdles to adoption include technology costs, uncertain⏎return on investment, time spent training users, user acceptance, validated data outputs, patient⏎acceptance, and proven clinical outcome improvements.⏎RFI Question 8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards,⏎and benchmarking tools.⏎TMA Response: Interoperability remains challenging due to a lack of data vocabulary standardization.⏎For example, in proposed United States Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) version 6, there are a⏎total of 132 data elements of which 57 have no correlating vocabulary standard. Interoperability⏎continues to be hindered without standardized vocabulary for data elements. Additionally, there is a lack⏎of fidelity and usability of the data exchanged. Interoperability should be not only about the movement⏎of the data, but also the quality and usability of the data received. Recipients need well-organized and⏎meaningful data outputs to efficiently analyze and make decisions about patient care. AI solutions could⏎be developed and deployed to translate non-standardized data elements and to usefully summarize⏎patient data, potentially according to specialty-specific specifications, enhancing the quality of the⏎patient information received and reviewed.⏎RFI Question 9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: Physicians and patients remain concerned about unreliable technology and its use of⏎data that may be leveraged against physicians and patients. The trust of the company and the reliability⏎of the technology are paramount. There is also concern over how patient data and AI-generated outputs⏎are used downstream for patient and physician analytics or other secondary purposes with unintended⏎consequences.⏎A common goal should be well-developed and well-implemented AI technology that functions securely⏎and accurately behind the scenes, optimizing information at the point of care without being intrusive to⏎the physician or the patient.⏎Additionally, there is an explicit distinction between AI used for quality improvement, patient care, and⏎clinical research versus AI used for enforcement or billing oversight. Physicians may be hesitant to⏎engage with tools that generate insights that could later be used punitively. Physicians who choose to⏎use AI tools for patient care must have reassurances that any oversight mechanisms installed or usage⏎data compiled will be used for education and not to penalize its users.⏎RFI Question 10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎TMA Response: HHS could consider the following areas of research to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care:⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎• How AI tools perform after deployment, including data drift, real-world safety issues, and⏎practical ways physicians can report concerns, near misses, or incorrect information.⏎• Human factors and workflow impact: When AI reduces burden versus when it unintentionally⏎adds cognitive load or documentation work.⏎• Medico-legal and accountability frameworks that protect patient safety while giving physicians⏎reasonable reliance protections when using clinically validated tools.⏎• Equity and bias evaluation in clinical environments, including rural settings, safety-net⏎populations, and differing documentation practices.⏎• Interoperability and data-quality research that focuses not just on moving data, but on making it⏎usable and actionable at the point of care.⏎• Education and competency development for trainees and practicing physicians to support safe,⏎supervised, and appropriate use of AI in clinical care.⏎• Impact on medical education and whether AI degrades diagnostic reasoning or can be used as a⏎supervised teaching tool.⏎• Validating patient-facing AI for clinical triage and guidance to the right level of care to assist⏎patients struggling to navigate the health care system.⏎• Algorithmic vigilance systems that detect hallucinations, bias, or unsafe outputs before making⏎clinical recommendations.⏎• Validation of studies in the use of AI for pattern recognition and clinical work, e.g. retinal⏎screening and image reading.⏎• Small-data AI for rare diseases and specific populations.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to respond to the request for information. TMA stands ready to provide⏎you and others within the agency with our policy insight and any additional assistance you may find⏎useful. If you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact Shannon Vogel, associate vice⏎president of health information technology, by emailing shannon.vogel@texmed.org or calling (512)⏎370-1411.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jayesh “Jay” Shah, MD⏎President⏎Texas Medical Association⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0130♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Scott♔♔Maryland♔♔United States♔Insilico Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My organization has been deploying AI-assisted documentation and triage tools across a network of 14 community clinic sites in Maryland, and what we have seen in practice is pretty far removed from how these products are described in vendor demos and pilot results. The performance gaps are real and they are not small. We had one documentation tool that cleared internal benchmarks and performed well during the initial deployment at our Rockville site, but when we rolled it across the remaining 13 locations over Q3 2023, the output quality degraded noticeably at sites where our EHR configuration differed even slightly from the reference setup the vendor used during testing. Same vendor. Same contract. Meaningfully different results.⏎⏎I keep coming back to this because the RFI frames acceleration as the primary goal, and I understand why — the potential benefits are real and I am not dismissing them. But acceleration is exactly what got health IT into the mess it has been in for the past fifteen years. EHR implementations that generated so much alert fatigue that clinicians started ignoring warnings wholesale. Sepsis prediction tools that failed quietly in community hospital settings where the training data did not reflect the patient population. These were not edge cases or isolated failures. They were the predictable result of deploying systems that performed well in controlled or academic settings into environments that were operationally messier and more variable than the developers anticipated.⏎⏎What HHS should be asking before it reduces regulatory friction is whether there is adequate infrastructure to detect performance degradation after deployment, not just before. Right now there is not, at least not at the scale of community clinic networks like ours. We do not have a clinical informatics team. We have two people who split IT responsibilities across 14 sites. If an AI triage tool starts producing worse recommendations for patients with limited English proficiency or patients on atypical medication regimens, we are probably not going to catch that systematically. We might catch it because a physician flags something, or because a patient outcome prompts a chart review. That is not a monitoring system, that is luck.⏎⏎I also want to raise something about the regulatory classification question specifically, because it affects us directly. 68% of our staff reported in an internal review last year that they were uncertain about liability exposure when using AI tools that do not fall under formal medical device classifications. That is not a compliance training problem. That is a structural ambiguity problem. If HHS accelerates adoption without resolving where liability sits for AI tools that influence clinical decisions but are not technically classified as medical devices, we are going to see administrators make one of two choices: avoid the tools entirely, or use them without any formal risk assessment. Neither of those is a good outcome.⏎⏎The 320 staff hours we spend annually reconciling inconsistent payer requirements around health technology tools — that number came from our Q1 2024 budget analysis and it was not a theoretical estimate, it was based on logged hours — that overhead is a real problem and I would welcome rationalization of those standards. But streamlining administrative requirements is a different thing from accelerating deployment timelines before we have post-market surveillance infrastructure that actually works in resource-limited settings.⏎⏎I do not have a clean answer for how to balance these things. What I can say is that the framing of this RFI makes me nervous, because it reads as though the bottleneck is regulatory friction when the bottleneck we experience operationally is reliability and interpretability after the tool leaves the controlled environment it was built in.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0131♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Nurses Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Nurses Association♔♔♔♔♔♔Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for Information (RFI) on the Use of⏎AI in Clinical Care.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The American Nurses Association (ANA) believes the biggest barriers to private-sector innovation⏎and adoption of AI in clinical care include fragmented and low-trust data environments that hinder⏎interoperability, unclear and non-harmonized regulatory expectations, and misaligned payment⏎incentives that fail to reward proven improvements in quality or burden reduction. Additionally,⏎concerns about safety, bias, and accountability combined with limited workforce readiness and⏎inadequate nurse engagement in AI governance create cultural and operational resistance. Finally,⏎usability gaps and poor workflow integration often lead to alert fatigue and erode clinician trust,⏎slowing widespread adoption.⏎Some specific areas where there are barriers to innovation include:⏎1. Safety, Privacy, and Ethics: ANA has foundational concerns with AI innovation in clinical⏎care, including the lack of shared standards for safety, privacy, ethics, and transparency.⏎This leads to uncertainty about reliability, model drift, and potential for biased or⏎incomplete datasets to perpetuate inequities.⏎2. Clinical Integration and Validation: Some barriers to AI innovation in clinical care include⏎lack of clinical input during design, misalignment with clinical workflow, insufficient clinical⏎validation, and limited ability for health systems to test, support, and manage risks of AI⏎technology.⏎3. Transparency and Trust: Vendors often treat algorithms as proprietary, preventing⏎clinicians from understanding output generation, undermining trust, and slowing adoption.⏎4. Structural and Economic Barriers: Significant hurdles include development cost, limited⏎IT/Informatics staffing in smaller hospitals, reliance on electronic health record (EHR)⏎vendors for AI, data quality/interoperability issues, and a lack of clear regulatory⏎expectations and governance frameworks.⏎5. Adoption Risks: Providers using personal AI technology outside of approved software could⏎create patient privacy and cybersecurity risks.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies,⏎or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎ANA has identified the following regulatory and policy changes that HHS can prioritize to further⏎incentivize effective use of AI in clinical care:⏎⏎1. Safety and Efficacy Incentives: HHS should prioritize changes that reward safe,⏎transparent, and workflow-aligned use of AI. This change should be contingent on a⏎mature digital foundation, data quality, and governance structures capable of⏎mitigating AI risks, not just mere adoption. ANA also suggests a testing process⏎proving rigor prior to implementation.⏎2. Regulatory Updates: Regulations like the Office of the National Coordinator (ONC)⏎Health IT Certification Program (45 CFR Part 170) should be updated to include⏎criteria for AI-enabled decision support. This update should cover transparency, bias⏎testing, explainability, and post-deployment monitoring. ANA also suggests that the⏎Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) Privacy and Security Rule⏎(45 CFR Part 164) could be refined to address secondary data use for AI training and⏎vendor data retention.⏎3. Payment Policy Alignment: CMS should align the Quality Payment Program (42 CFR⏎Part 414) with AI that demonstrably improves outcomes, reduces documentation⏎burden, or enhances safety, explicitly recognizing reductions in cognitive load and⏎alert fatigue.⏎4. Programmatic Support and Oversight: ANA encourages HHS to support⏎standardized AI governance frameworks and clinical AI evaluation toolkits,⏎emphasizing nursing and frontline clinician involvement. Furthermore, Conditions of⏎Participation (42 CFR Part 482) should clarify expectations for human oversight,⏎incident reporting, and managing AI model drift and health safety events.⏎5. Compliance and Unapproved Tools: Including the use of any AI product not officially⏎approved as Certified EHR Technology into regulatory and HIPAA requirements could⏎help enforce compliance.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎ANA recognizes that there can be legal and implementation issues regarding AI usage in clinical⏎care, which include the following: unclear liability and indemnification when AI augments clinical⏎decisions; privacy concerns around secondary data use for model training; and risks of bias and⏎discrimination that challenge civil rights compliance. These gaps create uncertainty for providers⏎and vendors. HHS should play a proactive role by issuing guidance on accountability frameworks,⏎privacy-by-design standards, and bias mitigation. Additionally, HHS can promote standardized⏎incident reporting and require transparency and post-deployment monitoring in procurement⏎contracts to ensure safe and equitable AI adoption.⏎Specific areas of concern include:⏎1. Reliable patient notification of when AI is “listening:” Organizations cannot depend on⏎individual practitioners to consistently and correctly disclose AI use to patients and families⏎in real time. Verbal notification varies by person, workflow pace, and setting, and it breaks⏎down most in high-acuity situations, which is the very environment where ambient⏎documentation or decision-support tools are the most useful. Patients also face a practical⏎transparency problem—they cannot easily tell when an AI tool is actively capturing,⏎transcribing, or analyzing their information versus when it is “off.” Blanket organizational⏎⏎statements (“we use AI”) do not solve this issue as they do not provide meaningful,⏎situational awareness and can fail to meet expectations for informed decision-making.⏎Guardrails that standardize how and when AI use is communicated—especially for tools⏎that listen in the background—would promote consistency, reduce confusion, and⏎strengthen trust across care settings.⏎2. Consent and patient rights in AI-enabled care: A major gap is the lack of clear, consistent⏎expectations for whether, when, and how patients should be informed about and consent to⏎AI use in their care—particularly when AI influences triage, recommendations, or⏎documentation. Existing privacy frameworks were not designed for algorithmic decision⏎support, automated triage, or predictive analytics, leaving uncertainty about what⏎constitutes adequate notice and meaningful consent. Patients often have no right to know⏎when AI is used, how their data contributes to outputs, or whether their information is used⏎to refine models. This ambiguity undermines trust and creates uncertainty for health⏎systems. A clearer approach would define baseline patient rights: clear notice when AI is⏎involved, plain language explanations of what the AI is doing, and a consent pathway for⏎sensitive uses.⏎3. Secondary use of personal health information (PHI) for AI training, development, and⏎vendor retention: Secondary use of PHI for AI model development, training, refinement,⏎and vendor retention raises distinct governance concerns that current rules address⏎inconsistently. Even where de-identified data is used, modern re-identification risks⏎challenge the adequacy of older de-identification assumptions. Patients and clinicians may⏎not know if data is being stored, repurposed, or used to improve models—particularly when⏎vendors retain datasets or use them across clients. This creates both ethical and⏎operational risk: trust erodes when the data lifecycle is opaque, and organizations face⏎compliance uncertainty when vendor data practices are unclear. Stronger guardrails would⏎emphasize data minimization, defined retention limits, explicit disclosure when data⏎contributes to model training/refinement, and patient-facing clarity about what happens to⏎data after it is collected.⏎4. Accountability and liability for AI-influenced clinical decisions: When AI plays a role in⏎clinical care, accountability can become blurred, especially if the tool is not regulated as a⏎medical device. As malpractice claims emerge, it may be difficult to isolate the AI’s⏎contribution to decision-making, identify whether the clinician reasonably relied on the⏎output, and determine what obligations the organization and vendor had to ensure⏎performance and transparency. Clinicians can be held responsible for decisions influenced⏎by systems they cannot fully evaluate, while vendors may attempt to disclaim responsibility⏎via contract language. This mismatch creates legal and ethical tension and may chill⏎adoption or push unsafe, inconsistent workarounds. A shared-accountability model would⏎clarify roles: what clinicians must verify, what organizations must govern and monitor, and⏎what vendors must disclose and support when their tools influence care.⏎5. Transparency requirements for AI tools and vendors: Transparency is a prerequisite for⏎trust and safe use. Without clear expectations, patients may not understand when AI is part⏎of their care, and clinicians may not know the limits of a tool, what data sources drive⏎outputs, or what conditions degrade performance. Transparency also matters for auditing; if⏎outcomes are questioned, organizations need traceability (what tool version was used,⏎what data inputs were considered, and how outputs were presented). Vendor transparency⏎expectations—such as clear documentation, known limitations, update notices, and⏎auditability—help reduce variability across health systems and prevent “black box”⏎adoption that shifts risk onto frontline clinicians and patients.⏎⏎6. Continuous monitoring, drift, bias, unintended consequences: Traditional governance⏎structures often treat clinical tools as relatively static, but AI systems can change in⏎performance over time as populations shift, workflows evolve, or models are updated.⏎Without a requirement or norm for ongoing monitoring, tools may drift, embed bias, or⏎introduce unintended consequences without being detected promptly. Continuous⏎monitoring is therefore a core patient safety issue, and not just an IT issue. Practical⏎guardrails would normalize lifecycle oversight: routine performance checks, bias⏎surveillance, post-deployment validation, incident reporting pathways, and clear triggers for⏎retraining, rollback, or removal—shared across vendors and health systems.⏎7. AI-specific privacy and security risks: Current security expectations do not consistently⏎address risks that are uniquely amplified by AI integration, including algorithmic⏎vulnerabilities, training-data poisoning, and risks introduced by large-scale model⏎connectivity and automation. As AI becomes embedded in clinical workflows, security⏎failures can compromise not only confidentiality but also clinical integrity, through⏎manipulated inputs, corrupted training signals, or altered outputs. Modernized guidance⏎would help organizations implement safeguards proportionate to AI risk: tighter access⏎controls, robust logging and audit trails, model/data integrity checks, and vendor⏎requirements that address AI-specific threat models. Standardizing these expectations⏎would reduce variability across organizations and strengthen baseline protections for⏎patients.⏎8. Explicit consent for feeding patient data into AI models: Consent becomes especially⏎important when patient data is used beyond direct care, such as training, fine-tuning, or⏎improving an AI model. Patients must have clear, understandable information about⏎whether their data will be used to develop or refine algorithms, how long it will be retained,⏎who will have access, and whether it can be used across clients or products. Even when de-⏎identification is claimed, patients may reasonably expect transparency and choice given re-⏎identification risks and the sensitivity of health data. A practical guardrail approach would⏎separate “AI used to support my care today” from “my data used to improve AI for future⏎use,” with clear consent/opt-out mechanisms for the latter whenever feasible.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎ANA recognizes that there are promising evaluation methods, metrics, and other considerations for⏎AI use in clinical care, including:⏎1. Evaluation Approaches: The most promising evaluation approaches treat AI as a clinical⏎intervention, not just a predictive model, and they should include frameworks assessing⏎capability, utility, and adoption. Post-deployment evaluations should be prospective and⏎measure changes in clinician behavior and patient outcomes, utilizing trials, A/B testing,⏎stepped-wedge designs, and continuous monitoring for issues like model drift and bias.⏎2. Pre-deployment Technical Validation: These should include metrics similar to clinical⏎trials, calibration, fairness metrics, and robustness testing (e.g., performance under data⏎shifts, missing data, and adversarial scenarios). External validation on data from multiple⏎sites and populations is essential.⏎⏎3. Workflow and Human-Centered Evaluation: Methods like usability testing, simulation-⏎based assessments, time-motion studies, and cognitive load measures are important for⏎identifying how AI fits into clinical workflows, reduces burden, and supports clinical⏎judgment. Evaluating explainable AI is also key to ensuring clinicians can understand,⏎trust, and act on outputs.⏎4. Specific Metrics: ANA’s suggested metrics include time saved documenting and⏎increased time at the bedside, readmission rates to evaluate discharge planning and⏎education, surgical site infection rates, and timely clinical data in the chart.⏎5. HHS Support Mechanisms: The most impactful mechanisms for HHS to support these⏎processes are targeted grants and cooperative agreements, contract and certification⏎guidance, and support for learning collaboratives and shared evaluation infrastructure.⏎Education on AI use and documentation should be required at the school/education level⏎rather than per healthcare organization.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎As HHS seeks to strengthen private-sector efforts, ANA urges the agency to endorse standardized⏎accreditation and certification frameworks for healthcare AI that incorporate transparency, bias⏎mitigation, and safety criteria. Additionally, HHS should fund nurse-focused AI education and⏎micro-credentialing programs through ANA and the American Nurses Credentialing Center (ANCC)⏎to build workforce readiness. These steps will promote confidence, ensure ethical deployment, and⏎position nurses as key partners in advancing effective AI adoption across clinical care.⏎1. Support for Private Sector Efforts: HHS should recognize and endorse trustworthy private⏎frameworks (accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing) without mandating a single⏎model, and use funding/procurement levers to prioritize vendors and health systems using⏎accredited/certified AI tools.⏎2. Certification and Vendor Requirements: Certification of AI tools is desired to ensure a⏎certain level of rigor in testing, and HHS should work with EHR vendors should be required⏎to meet Certified Technology Requirements to assist smaller organizations with limited⏎resources.⏎3. Infrastructure and Training: HHS must support shared infrastructure and learning⏎collaboratives to lower the barrier for smaller organizations, and require clinicians to be⏎trained within their licensure for safe and accurate technology use.⏎4. Promote AI Use and Human-Centered Design: HHS can promote the use of AI in clinical⏎care, such as "smart" hospital rooms, and explicitly center human-centered design and⏎workflow fit in any supported accreditation or certification efforts.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and⏎help reduce costs?⏎ANA acknowledges that some AI tools have exceeded expectations in areas like ambient⏎documentation and workflow assistants, which, according to those companies, significantly⏎reduce charting time and improve usability. This includes imaging, where validated models⏎⏎enhance diagnostic accuracy. However, many tools have fallen short due to poor workflow⏎integration, alert fatigue, and lack of transparency, leading to low clinician trust. Importantly, we⏎lack strong evidence that these tools reduce workload or mitigate burnout, underscoring the urgent⏎need for standardized outcome measures that capture clinician burden and well-being. Looking⏎forward, the greatest potential lies in nurse-centric solutions such as explainable triage and risk⏎stratification, ambient nursing documentation with reasoning support, staffing optimization tools⏎that improve well-being, and AI-driven quality measurement systems designed to reduce burden⏎while improving patient outcomes.⏎Specific examples include:⏎1. Success of AI Tools: Examples include AI-assisted imaging for faster turnaround times,⏎capacity and throughput tools for bed management, and operational optimizations. Mobile⏎Point of Care Documentation devices for nursing have also greatly improved bedside care,⏎as well as ambient listening for provider documentation.⏎2. Shortcomings of AI Tools: AI tools fall short when they are generic, layered into already⏎fragile systems, increase alert fatigue, add clicks, provide unclear recommendations⏎without explainability, or do not translate into better outcomes due to a lack of underlying⏎workflows/resources. Some predictive models, models trained on biased data, or tools that⏎reinforce existing inequities have also fallen short. A lack of clinician input in development,⏎particularly for nursing workflows, can also lead to tools that don't improve workflows and⏎result in passed-on costs to patients without clear benefits.⏎3. Greatest Potential for Novel AI Tools: The greatest potential lies in tools that directly⏎support patient safety, nursing workflow, and proactive care, such as patient-movement⏎and safety monitoring tools (e.g., detecting unsafe movement, predicting fall risk). Other⏎high-potential tools include ambient and workflow-aware documentation support for⏎nurses, AI-enabled care coordination and handoff tools, and operational/staffing⏎optimization tools that align staffing with acuity and patient needs in real time. These tools⏎should be deeply embedded in workflows and evaluated as clinical interventions.⏎4. Need for Data: There is a current lack of sufficient data on the performance and cost-⏎effectiveness of AI tools.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎ANA believes that the adoption of AI in clinical care is most influenced by Chief Nursing Officers,⏎nursing informatics leaders, and quality or operations executives, especially when nurses have a⏎formal role in governance. However, there are administrative hurdles such as, unclear ownership of⏎AI initiatives, lengthy procurement processes, and limited time or resources for workforce training,⏎which slow implementation and reduce frontline engagement.⏎1. Influential Roles: Executive sponsorship is mentioned as having an impact on AI adoption.⏎2. Cost and Resources: The most significant barriers include the cost of AI add-on solutions⏎and the lack of implementation resources, with vendors shifting costs and risk to⏎organizations.⏎3. Governance and Accountability: Primary administrative hurdles stem from fragmented⏎governance, unclear accountability, and a lack of foundational digital maturity, leading to⏎⏎prolonged contracting and legal review due to the absence of a consistent framework for⏎addressing data use, liability, and vendor accountability.⏎4. Government Role: HHS can best support adoption by establishing clear, consistent⏎expectations for safety, transparency, and accountability, and by defining minimum criteria⏎for responsible clinical AI, such as bias testing and monitoring, while vendors build⏎accreditation and testing programs.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎ANA urges HHS to provide incentives to accelerate and support the development of AI for clinical⏎care in the following ways:⏎1. Data Interoperability: Enhanced interoperability would significantly widen market⏎opportunities and accelerate AI development by improving access to high-quality,⏎standardized clinical data, which is essential for reliable AI model training and safer⏎deployment.⏎2. High-Value Data Types: Prioritizing interoperability for data types such as vital signs,⏎laboratory results, medication administration records, device data, care plan, nursing⏎assessment, imaging metadata, and patient-generated health data is crucial.⏎3. Standards and APIs: Greater adoption of fast healthcare interoperability resources (FHIR)-⏎based application programming interfaces (APIs), United States Core Data for⏎Interoperability (USCDI) expansion, and consistent implementation of health level (HL7)⏎standards would reduce variability and allow vendors to build tools that work across various⏎systems.⏎4. Benchmark Datasets: Shared, de-identified benchmark datasets built on common data⏎models like observational medical outcomes partnership (OMOP) would accelerate⏎research by allowing models to be evaluated against consistent reference standards.⏎5. Specific Areas: Enhanced interoperability for device and IT data is important for AI tools⏎focused on patient safety, mobility monitoring, and early deterioration detection, and⏎secure databases of de-identified health information could also help.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎ANA knows that patients and caregivers want AI to address challenges like reducing delays in care,⏎improving communication, and freeing clinicians from excessive documentation so they can spend⏎more time delivering care. At the same time, they have concerns about privacy, data misuse, bias,⏎and the risk that over-automation could erode human interaction and accountability. Transparent⏎communication and strong nursing involvement are essential to building trust and ensuring AI⏎adoption aligns with patient-centered values.⏎Specific challenges include:⏎1. Desired AI-Addressed Challenges: Patients and caregivers want AI to address safety risks,⏎care coordination gaps, long wait times, navigating the complex system, and preventing⏎⏎avoidable harm. They also desire improvements in communication between clinicians,⏎reduced delays in diagnosis, ensuring nothing is missed in care, better access to primary⏎care, and addressing clinician burnout.⏎2. Patient Education/Understanding: A major challenge is the general public's lack of⏎education and skepticism regarding AI, particularly in rural areas with poor health and⏎technology literacy, which hinders understanding of how data is used and how AI functions.⏎3. Concerns about AI: Concerns center around privacy, consent, the potential for AI to⏎replace human judgment, reduce relational aspects of care, introduce bias or inequity, and⏎create new errors that are difficult to detect. Patients also worry about the accountability of⏎clinicians for algorithm-influenced decisions.⏎4. Organizational/Systemic Challenges: Small hospitals with limited knowledge and⏎resources struggle to prioritize and afford updated AI technology, and organizations cannot⏎solely take on the responsibility of re-educating the public.⏎5. Social Determinants of Health (SDOH) and Resources: AI is desired to help link patients⏎with resources related to SDOH challenges to ensure they can attend appointments and⏎follow up on prescribed care.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎ANA urges HHS to prioritize research on standardized outcome measures to assess AI’s impact on⏎clinician workload, burnout, and equity, as current evidence is limited. Additional focus areas⏎include safety and bias mitigation, cost-benefit analyses, and real-world effectiveness studies⏎through nurse-led trials.⏎1. Prioritized AI Research: HHS should focus on AI research that evaluates real-world impact,⏎safety, equity, and burden reduction, including prospective studies of AI as a clinical⏎intervention, human factors, workflow impact, economic evaluation of⏎implementation/maintenance, and systematic bias assessment.⏎2. Need for Further Literature/Studies: The existing literature is considered lacking,⏎necessitating more long-term studies to understand the impact and return on investment,⏎as well as a need for more rigorous, standardized economic evaluations that capture⏎indirect costs like workflow redesign, training, governance, and monitoring.⏎3. Existing Literature Findings: A growing body of literature indicates that AI can improve⏎diagnostic accuracy, workflow efficiency, and patient outcomes, but many studies are⏎limited, specialty-specific, often optimistic, and inconsistent in reporting safety events,⏎equity impacts, or long-term effectiveness.⏎4. Implementation Considerations: It is important to promote mobile point-of-care⏎implementation and avoid a "one-size-fits-all" approach, as each discipline and setting will⏎have unique needs, requiring consideration of change management and the mental load on⏎clinicians.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0132♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Neurology, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Neurology♔♔♔♔♔♔February 17, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The American Academy of Neurology (AAN) is the world’s largest association of neurologists and⏎neuroscience professionals with more than 44,000 members—and the leading voice on brain health. The⏎AAN’s mission is to enhance member career fulfillment and promote brain health for all. A neurologist is a⏎doctor who specializes in the diagnosis, care, and treatment of brain, spinal cord, and nervous system⏎diseases. These neurological diseases and disorders affect one in two people in the United States and⏎include Alzheimer's disease, stroke, concussion, epilepsy, Parkinson's disease, multiple sclerosis,⏎peripheral neuropathy, and migraine.⏎The AAN thanks the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) for the opportunity to share⏎feedback pursuant to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. HHS seeks experience-based feedback from those using AI tools in⏎clinical care, as well as from individuals who wish to do so but face barriers. AAN members frequently⏎express interest in the potential of AI to enhance diagnostic accuracy, streamline clinical and⏎administrative workflows, support research recruitment, and convert unstructured clinical data into timely⏎signals for improved care coordination, among another activities. Nevertheless, neurology providers⏎remain concerned about the potential for AI models to develop incomplete clinical recommendations,⏎fragment interoperability initiatives, introduce bias, or increase health care spending. In response to the⏎growing impact of AI on clinical practice over the recent years, the AAN has established an internal⏎workgroup to explore impacts and provide resources, tools, and guidance to our members in this area.⏎In alignment with HHS’ stated goals to support the rapid adoption of AI in clinical care, foster public trust⏎and confidence in these solutions, and align federal incentives so that AI is deployed in ways that enhance⏎productivity, reduce administrative burden, lower health care costs, and improve health outcomes, the AAN⏎encourages HHS to prioritize programmatic levers that promote high-quality evidence generation through⏎clinical trials and real-world evaluations and tie payment for AI-based services with demonstrated clinical⏎value.⏎1. What are the regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic designs that HHS should⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use⏎AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations.⏎The AAN believes that regulatory frameworks can help accelerate the adoption of AI tools that have⏎demonstrated improvements in patient care, while reducing the risk of premature deployment of AI tools⏎that are insufficiently validated, poorly monitored, or inequitably implemented. AI tools that deliver output⏎used in clinical decision making should be governable, measurable, and portable across care settings.⏎Establish governance models based on AI lifecycles⏎Clinical AI performance can change over time as workflows, clinical practice, patient populations, and⏎data sources evolve. To promote clinician and patient trust as well as effective use, HHS should prioritize a⏎continuous improvement AI lifecycle model that requires:⏎• Pre-deployment evidence that is adjusted based on the intended clinical use and risk. Before routine⏎clinical use, HHS should require that AI developers demonstrate that the data used for model⏎development are appropriate for the population and setting where the tool will be deployed. This⏎includes explicit plans to detect and mitigate algorithmic bias (including self-serving bias,⏎sponsorship bias, and reproducibility bias), monitor data drift, and re-evaluate when a tool may⏎evolve outside the initial context.1⏎• Post-deployment monitoring for data drift, subgroup performance, safety events, and workflow⏎outcomes.⏎• Defined triggers for re-evaluation, updating, or if needed, withdrawal.⏎The AAN believes that a lifecycle-based regulatory model versus a one-time approval pathway is⏎especially important for generative AI tools, which without monitoring, can propagate errors throughout a⏎patient’s medical record, ultimately impacting downstream clinical decisions, quality reporting, and patient⏎trust.⏎Align AI adoption with national interoperability standards⏎The provision of high-quality neurological care requires that clinicians can access patient records in⏎interoperable data formats. Although much progress has been made to harmonize patient electronic health⏎information in the last decade, challenges accessing data across care settings persist. The AAN is⏎concerned that AI integration without reliable interoperability measures will similarly lead to challenges⏎with data accessibility, measurement, and trust across sites or populations. As AI continues to be integrated⏎to delivery of care, HHS should consider how best to promote alignment between AI tools with national⏎interoperability standards including Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) and the United⏎States Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI). Current interoperability frameworks can be applied to AI to⏎1 Moura, Lidia et al. “Implications of Large Language Models for Quality and Efficiency of Neurologic Care: Emerging Issues in Neurology.” Neurology⏎vol. 102,11 (2024): e209497. doi:10.1212/WNL.0000000000209497⏎⏎avoid producing local, isolated demonstrations. Further, interoperability requirements should not only help⏎support data exchange, but also data provenance, which is essential for monitoring data drift and safety.⏎Reduce administrative burden through model transparency⏎The AAN is supportive of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology’s (ASTP/ONC) previously stated goals to ensure that⏎clinicians have the necessary information to determine whether a particular AI tool is fair, appropriate,⏎valid, effective, and safe.2 It is critical for neurology providers to understand how clinical decision support⏎tools are designed, developed, trained, evaluated, and should be used by the end-user. Flawed inputs or AI⏎models not tested with real-world subspecialty data are not likely to meaningfully contribute to clinical⏎decision making.⏎In alignment with a risk-based regulatory approach for the different types of AI tools used throughout⏎clinical practice (i.e., predictive decision support versus low-risk administrative systems), the AAN is⏎supportive of requiring minimum expectations for transparency so that end users can select clinical AI⏎tools based on the nature of the training data and inputs, known risks, and information about real-world⏎performance. Without this information, the AAN is concerned about the administrative burden of⏎appropriately vetting and testing these technologies, especially among neurology providers who work in⏎small practices and may not have robust health IT support.⏎Payment policy for AI⏎The AAN also appreciates that HHS aims to align payment policy with high-value AI clinical interventions⏎and accelerate access to affordable AI tools for clinical care. In the absence of current economic data that⏎accounts for many modern generative clinical AI technologies, the AAN encourages HHS to work with⏎stakeholders to balance incentives for innovation with the integrity of the CPT system, while⏎acknowledging budget-neutral constraints under certain Medicare programs. If a combination of indirect⏎and direct practice expense cannot adequately cover AI tools, then a paradigm shift to a new benefit⏎category may be necessary. However, proposals to move products out of CPT Categories I or III into a⏎new, as-yet-unpriced structure, such as the American Medical Association’s Clinically Meaningful⏎Algorithmic Analysis pathway, are premature, as reimbursement across payers is not assured. Vendors and⏎providers alike will be reluctant to adopt the new paradigm unless reimbursement mechanisms are in place.⏎The AAN also encourages HHS to consider payment policies that are tied to clinical measure-aligned⏎outcomes and clinician capacity rather than accuracy alone. Coverage and reimbursement should be⏎supported by evidence of measurable improvements in patient outcomes, safety, workflow impacts (i.e.,⏎timeliness, follow-up completion), and cost-effectiveness. Valuation frameworks, such as the framework⏎2 89 FR 1192⏎⏎proposed by Abramoff et al.,3 illustrate consistent principles for involving affected stakeholders and⏎determining when an AI-enabled service warrants reimbursement and how it should be valued.⏎2. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎While the AAN believes there are opportunities for artificial intelligence to advance neurological care, the⏎landscape of these technologies is rapidly evolving, and integration will require evidence-based⏎frameworks for use. At present, the published evidence on AI deployed in routine clinical care is mixed.⏎Performance in a controlled testing environment does not always translate to real-world improvements in⏎patient health outcomes, clinical workflows, or health care costs. In general, “bounded” AI tools have⏎delivered measurable clinical and/or economic value when a clinical task is narrowly defined, and the⏎output triggers a straightforward clinical action or alters a time-sensitive clinical workflow. Even if not⏎directly used for diagnostics, AI can help improve stroke outcomes where successful treatment is highly⏎time dependent. For example, technologies employing automated detection of large vessel occlusion in⏎patients with acute ischemic stroke have enhanced hospital-based stroke workflows, resulting in reductions⏎in stroke treatment times.4,5⏎Similarly, large language model workflows have demonstrated the ability to generate clinically meaningful⏎reviews of unstructured clinical notes, enabling detection of early signals of cognitive impairment.6 Earlier⏎recognition of cognitive decline can support more timely referrals to neurological care and access to⏎potential treatments. Alternatively, broad AI tools without a narrowly-defined task, including many⏎patient-facing symptom checkers that provide patients with a possible diagnosis and a triage⏎recommendation, have underperformed in real-world settings.7⏎AI tools used to complement the administrative functions of delivering neurological care also hold⏎promise. AI-based scribes that listen during a patient visit and generate structured clinical notes to be⏎added to the electronic health record are reported to reduce clinician burnout related to clinical⏎documentation.8 Many AAN members that have adopted ambient listening technologies and AI scribes⏎report that these technologies reduce the time spent outside of work on documentation activities and allow⏎them to spend less time on the computer during the patient visit.⏎3 Abràmoff MD, Roehrenbeck C, Trujillo S, et al. A reimbursement framework for artificial intelligence in healthcare. NPJ Digit Med. 2022;5(1):72.⏎Published 2022 Jun 9. doi:10.1038/s41746-022-00621-w⏎4 Martinez-Gutierrez JC, Kim Y, Salazar-Marioni S, et al. Automated Large Vessel Occlusion Detection Software and Thrombectomy Treatment Times: A⏎Cluster Randomized Clinical Trial. JAMA Neurol. 2023;80(11):1182-1190. doi:10.1001/jamaneurol.2023.3206⏎5 Figurelle ME, Meyer DM, Perrinez ES, et al. Viz.ai Implementation of Stroke Augmented Intelligence and Communications Platform to Improve⏎Indicators and Outcomes for a Comprehensive Stroke Center and Network. AJNR Am J Neuroradiol. 2023;44(1):47-53. doi:10.3174/ajnr.A7716⏎6 Tian J, Fard P, Cagan C, et al. An autonomous agentic workflow for clinical detection of cognitive concerns using large language models. NPJ Digit⏎Med. 2026;9(1):51. Published 2026 Jan 7. doi:10.1038/s41746-025-02324-4⏎7 Riboli-Sasco E, El-Osta A, Alaa A, et al. Triage and Diagnostic Accuracy of Online Symptom Checkers: Systematic Review. J Med Internet Res.⏎2023;25:e43803. Published 2023 Jun 2. doi:10.2196/43803⏎8 You JG, Dbouk RH, Landman A, et al. Ambient Documentation Technology in Clinician Experience of Documentation Burden and Burnout. JAMA Netw⏎Open. 2025;8(8):e2528056. Published 2025 Aug 1. doi:10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.28056⏎⏎Nevertheless, these tools still experience limitations as AAN members also report that AI-generated notes⏎frequently omit or erroneously report information from the patient visit. As compared to primary care,⏎there remain significant content gaps for neurology among ambient scribes, whereby the text generated⏎often fails to distinguish important clinical details, including different types of headache or seizures,⏎ultimately impacting the notes and treatment plans generated by these tools. Further, many studies related⏎to ambient AI scribes include many methodological limitations. Studies often rely on voluntary⏎participation rather than a randomized controlled design, and study outcomes are largely self-reported,⏎indicating a lack of objective data.⏎Conclusion⏎The AAN greatly appreciates the opportunity to provide HHS with input on the current state of AI as a⏎component of neurological care. We strongly urge the agency to consider the unique needs of neurology⏎providers and patients in federal approaches to regulation and reimbursement. Please contact Matt⏎Kerschner, the AAN’s Director of Regulatory Affairs and Policy, at mkerschner@aan.com and Michaela⏎Read, the AAN’s Practice and Healthcare Technology Program Manager, at mread@aan.com with any⏎questions or requests for additional information.⏎Sincerely,⏎Natalia S. Rost, MD, MPH, FAAN, FAHA⏎President, American Academy of Neurology♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0133♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from MAHA Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MAHA Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔ATTN: Steven Posnack - Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy -⏎ASTP/ONC⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Submitted by: MAHA Institute⏎Date: February 2026⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Mr. Posnack,⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide comments on accelerating the adoption and use of AI⏎in clinical care. The innovation of AI has the potential to be transformative to the health of⏎Americans, however with most innovations in healthcare they do not fail because of lack of idea⏎and effort — innovation fails when infrastructure and incentives are misaligned, and the⏎application is not inclusive of the individual.⏎Meaningful improvement in health and health care requires coordinated governance,⏎interoperable systems, data centered around the person and trust across stakeholders.⏎Artificial intelligence has the potential to reduce administrative burden, improve timeliness of⏎insight, strengthen monitoring capabilities, and support better clinical decision-making.⏎AI will not scale in the absence of interoperable data, consistent governance standards, clear⏎regulatory expectations, and payment models aligned with longitudinal value to the individual.⏎The recommendations included in this submission reflect practical experience navigating⏎complex health journeys, IT implementation, multi-jurisdiction collaboration, and clinical⏎workflow realities. They are focused on the federal policy levers that can create durable⏎conditions for responsible AI adoption.⏎I appreciate the Department’s leadership in seeking broad input and welcome continued⏎engagement as HHS advances its AI strategy.⏎Respectfully,⏎Debra Sheldon⏎Vice President⏎MAHA Institute⏎⏎RESPONSE TO “HHS HEALTH SECTOR AI RFI”⏎Docket: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted to: Office of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Introduction⏎The enhancement of health and the potential for individuals to be the executive of their health⏎through artificial intelligence is no longer a prospective claim; it is a demonstrable today, in part.⏎The primary impediment to widespread adoption is not a deficit in model capabilities, but rather⏎a lack of coordinated federal direction on AI infrastructure investments for health should be⏎allocated.⏎The integration of AI within healthcare delivery remains significantly hindered by individuals not⏎having a centralized easily accessible data sources for consumers of care. In the absence of⏎cohesive federal intervention across regulatory, financial, and research domains, AI deployment⏎will remain inconsistent and limited to constrained, low-risk applications.⏎The Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) possesses both the requisite authority⏎and the operational mechanisms to effectively dismantle these structural impediments while⏎creating structure to provide adequate protections for patient privacy. The subsequent⏎recommendations propose concrete, actionable policy levers for consideration.⏎Structural Barriers to Adoption: Fragmented Longitudinal Data Environments⏎Effective AI necessitates structured, machine-readable, longitudinal data spanning diverse care⏎settings. Despite documented progress under 45 CFR Part 170 (Health IT Certification) and 45⏎CFR Part 171 (Information Blocking), operational implementation remains highly variable.⏎Access to quality bulk data is inconsistent, semantic normalization is unevenly applied, and⏎standardized audit capabilities across systems are lacking.⏎Organizations endeavoring to implement cross-institutional AI solutions are compelled to⏎navigate redundant governance structures, inconsistent API implementations, and variable⏎access controls. This fragmentation substantially decelerates innovation and elevates⏎operational costs.⏎AI scalability is contingent upon interoperability being operationalized, enforceable, and subject⏎to clear measurement.⏎Regulatory Ambiguity for Non-Device AI. A substantial class of AI tools—including,⏎but not limited to, risk stratification engines, clinical documentation augmentation,⏎workflow optimization platforms, and surveillance triggers—does not satisfy the⏎established criteria for medical device classification, yet directly impacts the delivery of⏎care.⏎⏎Healthcare systems currently lack definitive federal guidance pertaining to audit⏎requirements, drift monitoring protocols, documentation expectations, and reproducibility⏎standards for these particular tools. This absence of predictable regulatory guardrails⏎prolongs legal review processes and inhibits procurement timelines.⏎Reimbursement Misalignment. Existing fee-for-service payment models do not⏎systematically incentivize burden reduction, efficiency gains in productivity, or⏎preventative interventions. Furthermore, even within value-based and risk-bearing⏎payment arrangements, the infrastructure investments essential for supporting AI—such⏎as a longitudinal health record accessible by patients, data quality measurement⏎systems, federated query capabilities, and comprehensive monitoring systems—lack⏎explicit recognition.⏎Liability and Indemnification Uncertainty. Healthcare systems exhibit pervasive⏎caution in deploying AI owing to an unclear demarcation of responsibility between the⏎technology developers and the organizations implementing the solutions. Negotiations⏎concerning indemnification frequently extend procurement cycles beyond a twelve-⏎month horizon.⏎I. Regulatory Priorities⏎Despite documented progress under 45 CFR Part 170 (Health IT Certification) and 45 CFR Part⏎171 (Information Blocking), operational implementation remains highly variable. Access to⏎quality bulk data is inconsistent, semantic normalization is unevenly applied, and standardized⏎audit capabilities across systems are lacking. Additionally, [add something here about the⏎Privacy Rule]⏎To address these regulatory challenges, HHS should adopt a regulatory posture defined by⏎clarity, proportionality, and enforceability.⏎A. Clarify Governance Expectations Under 45 CFR Part 170. HHS should promulgate⏎interpretive guidance mandating that AI-enabled certified health IT systems must support the⏎following capabilities:⏎• Model version control and comprehensive documentation⏎• Tracking of dataset provenance⏎• Audit logging functionality consistent with the requirements of 45 CFR 170.315(d)⏎• Reproducible data access when utilizing APIs as defined under 45 CFR⏎170.315(g)(10)⏎• Continuous monitoring of model performance and drift⏎• A consent to opt in/out of state, regional and national networks must be explicit⏎and centralized to reduce friction to the individual⏎• Health information exchanges need to be reshaped into tools of consent, real⏎world data sources with consumer mediated controls that allow for both privacy⏎protections to be transparent and honored by all actors in the interoperability rule⏎whole allowing the citizen scientist access to aggregated information⏎These expectations should be applied particularly where AI systems traverse⏎organizational or jurisdictional boundaries.⏎⏎Predictable governance standards serve to mitigate institutional risk and reduce friction⏎in the procurement process.⏎B. Enforce Information Blocking Provisions. Pursuant to 45 CFR Part 171, HHS must⏎unequivocally state that technical throttling, the imposition of excessive access fees, or⏎proprietary export restrictions that impede access to AI-ready bulk data may constitute⏎interference.⏎Effective AI deployment is dependent upon access to reliable, timely, and machine-⏎readable electronic health information. Consistent and visible enforcement remains⏎paramount.⏎C. Recognize Federated Analytics Architectures. HHS should formally acknowledge⏎distributed and federated analytics models as compliant with the HIPAA Privacy Rule⏎provisions (45 CFR 164.502 and 164.514), provided that appropriate safeguards are⏎rigorously implemented.⏎Federated architectures facilitate cross-state and multi-system AI deployment while⏎effectively preserving local data control, supporting role-based access permissions,⏎ensuring auditability, and maintaining small-cell suppression. This model simultaneously⏎reduces privacy risk while significantly expanding analytic capacity.⏎Federal recognition would furnish crucial assurance to state and regional stakeholders.⏎A compounding problem for Regulatory Priorities is the lack of regulation for non-device tools. A⏎substantial class of AI tools—including, but not limited to, risk stratification engines, clinical⏎documentation augmentation, workflow optimization platforms, and surveillance triggers—does⏎not satisfy the established criteria for medical device classification, yet directly impacts the⏎delivery of patient care.⏎Healthcare systems currently lack definitive federal guidance pertaining to audit requirements,⏎drift monitoring protocols, documentation expectations, and reproducibility standards for these⏎particular tools. This absence of predictable regulatory guardrails prolongs legal review⏎processes and inhibits procurement timelines.⏎II. Reimbursement Modernization⏎Existing fee-for-service payment models do not systematically incentivize burden reduction,⏎efficiency gains in productivity, or preventative interventions. Furthermore, even within value-⏎based and risk-bearing payment arrangements, the infrastructure investments essential for⏎supporting AI—such as data quality measurement systems, federated query capabilities, and⏎comprehensive monitoring systems—lack explicit financial recognition.⏎The acceleration of AI adoption is directly contingent upon payment models that recognize both⏎infrastructure investment and longitudinal value creation.⏎A. Incorporate AI Infrastructure into Value-Based Arrangements. Under 42 CFR Part⏎425 (Medicare Shared Savings Program) and 42 CFR Part 422 (Medicare Advantage), the⏎Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) should permit the inclusion of AI-enabling⏎infrastructure investments—including data normalization efforts, quality measurement cycles,⏎and monitoring systems—within shared savings calculations.⏎⏎Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation (CMMI) demonstrations authorized under⏎42 U.S.C. § 1315a should explicitly pilot and test AI-enabled care models that holistically⏎integrate analytics, workflow redesign, and governance oversight.⏎Pilot Innovation Add-On Pathways. CMS should investigate temporary reimbursement⏎mechanisms conceptually analogous to 42 CFR 412.87 for validated AI tools that demonstrably⏎achieve measurable improvements in timeliness, cost reduction, or burden mitigation.⏎Infrastructure investment must temporally precede the generation of insights. Payment⏎policy should accurately reflect this essential sequence.⏎III. Research Priorities⏎HHS should strategically prioritize research in the following critical domains:⏎• Explainable AI within authentic, auditable, real-world clinical workflows⏎• Federated multi-jurisdiction analytics methodologies⏎• Techniques for bias detection in longitudinal datasets⏎• AI-driven methodologies for data quality improvement⏎• Human-centered implementation science⏎• Identify data networks that provide real time, real world data sources that go beyond⏎Acute Care/EHR (Hospital) sources of data⏎While current literature documents gains in predictive performance and documentation⏎efficiency, empirical evidence regarding system-wide cost redistribution, workforce impact, and⏎long-term savings remains constrained. Federal research investment should be directed toward⏎closing this critical knowledge gap.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎In addition to responses to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, we are including responses to the specific⏎questions included in the RFI.⏎IV. Accountability Frameworks for Non-Device AI⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎A. Private Sector Innovation.⏎B. Adoption and Use in Clinical Care: AI has yielded demonstrable improvements in areas⏎such as imaging triage, ambient documentation, predictive risk stratification within value-based⏎care models, and revenue cycle optimization. These successful implementations share common⏎attributes: seamless integration into existing clinical workflows, access to normalized⏎longitudinal data, and clearly defined monitoring frameworks.⏎Conversely, AI has underperformed in scenarios where deployment occurred without adequate⏎semantic normalization, lacked integration into core clinical systems, or was implemented⏎without transparent governance controls.⏎⏎The most promising applications include distributed surveillance systems, longitudinal cross-⏎system risk scoring, AI-assisted methodologies for data quality enhancement, and workflow-⏎integrated clinical augmentation tools.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS should reevaluate the use of health information exchanges as tools for AI. HHS should⏎convene a multistate demonstration of transforming these entities from provider tools to⏎consumer centric, real world data, real world evidence tools of CDC, FDA and NIH. A⏎transformation rooted in Health Data Trust models, that convene states in a coordinated,⏎industry standardizing governance framework must prevail. This transformation would provide⏎create a privacy protecting governance model with an auditable source of truth and create a⏎clear framework for granular consenting to occur with less friction and expanded access to the⏎individual and aid at restoring the public trust in data that is accessible to CDC,FDA, NIH, CMS⏎and beyond.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play⏎to help address them?⏎Healthcare systems exhibit pervasive caution in deploying AI owing to an unclear demarcation⏎of responsibility between the technology developers and the organizations implementing the⏎solutions. Negotiations concerning indemnification frequently extend procurement cycles⏎beyond a twelve-month horizon.Non-device AI necessitates clear definition of governance⏎responsibilities, specifically:⏎• The allocation of legal responsibility between the technology developer and the⏎deploying organization.⏎• Standardized documentation requirements for decisions informed by AI.⏎• Defined obligations for continuous drift monitoring.⏎• Transparency requirements for training data provenance and established⏎performance metrics.⏎HHS should convene cross-agency coordination (ASTP/ONC, CMS, FDA, OCR) to develop⏎standardized governance templates that emphasize auditability, formal approval workflows,⏎mandatory override documentation, and monitoring expectations.⏎Clear frameworks will reduce variability in negotiation and expedite responsible deployment.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would⏎have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on⏎quality, and help reduce costs?⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption decisions typically involve a diverse group of stakeholders, including Chief Information⏎Officers (CIOs), Chief Medical Information Officers (CMIOs), compliance officers, Chief⏎Financial Officers (CFOs), legal counsel, and medical executive committees.⏎The principal impediments encountered include protracted procurement cycles, complex⏎indemnification negotiations, fragmented governance committee structures, and inherent⏎integration constraints within legacy systems.⏎Federal clarification regarding governance standards and interoperability expectations that are⏎consumer centric would substantially mitigate friction.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability is essential for expanding AI's utility in population risk management,⏎syndromic surveillance, quality benchmarking, and cross-state analytics.⏎HHS must continue to prioritize the enforcement of FHIR bulk data access provisions under 45⏎CFR 170.315(g)(10), structured semantic normalization standards, AI-ready metadata⏎frameworks, and the establishment of measurable data quality benchmarks.⏎Interoperability must be regarded as essential operational infrastructure, not merely aspirational⏎policy.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎⏎Patients expect services characterized by timeliness, personalization, and reduced⏎administrative burden. They simultaneously expect transparency, fairness, and the preservation⏎of necessary clinician oversight.⏎Human-in-the-loop safeguards, explainability features, and auditable governance structures⏎must be intrinsically embedded into all AI deployment requirements.⏎Trust must be deliberately engineered; it cannot be presumed.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎Non-device AI necessitates clear definition of governance responsibilities, specifically:⏎• The allocation of legal responsibility between the technology developer and the⏎deploying organization.⏎• Standardized documentation requirements for decisions informed by AI.⏎• Defined obligations for continuous drift monitoring.⏎• Transparency requirements for training data provenance and established⏎performance metrics.⏎HHS should convene cross-agency coordination (ASTP/ONC, CMS, FDA, , NIH, CDC, OCR) to⏎develop standardized governance templates that emphasize auditability, formal approval⏎workflows, mandatory override documentation, and monitoring expectations.⏎Clear frameworks will reduce variability in negotiation and expedite responsible deployment.⏎V. Evaluation and Continuous Monitoring⏎AI oversight must adhere to a lifecycle-based methodology.⏎Pre-deployment evaluation should encompass representativeness analysis, rigorous calibration⏎testing, subpopulation performance assessment, and robustness testing under scenarios of⏎incomplete data.⏎Post-deployment monitoring must include:⏎• Continuous detection of model drift⏎• Performance dashboards for key metrics⏎• Tracking of clinical overrides⏎• Scorecards for timeliness and completeness⏎• Cross-site benchmarking comparisons⏎HHS can support these necessary processes through cooperative agreements, the⏎establishment of shared benchmarking infrastructure, and the implementation of standardized⏎reporting expectations.⏎VI. Observed Performance in Clinical Settings⏎⏎AI has yielded demonstrable improvements in areas such as imaging triage, ambient⏎documentation, predictive risk stratification within value-based care models, and revenue cycle⏎optimization. These successful implementations share common attributes: seamless integration⏎into existing clinical workflows, access to normalized longitudinal data, and clearly defined⏎monitoring frameworks.⏎Conversely, AI has underperformed in scenarios where deployment occurred without adequate⏎semantic normalization, lacked integration into core clinical systems, or was implemented⏎without transparent governance controls.⏎The most promising applications include the application to individual management of health if⏎access is truly enabled by the interoperability rule, distributed public health application,⏎longitudinal cross-system risk scoring, AI-assisted methodologies for data quality enhancement,⏎and workflow-integrated clinical augmentation tools.⏎VII. Organizational Adoption Dynamics⏎Adoption decisions typically involve a diverse group of stakeholders, including Chief Information⏎Officers (CIOs), Chief Medical Information Officers (CMIOs), compliance officers, Chief⏎Financial Officers (CFOs), legal counsel, and medical executive committees.⏎The principal impediments encountered include protracted procurement cycles, complex⏎indemnification negotiations, fragmented governance committee structures, and inherent⏎integration constraints within legacy systems.⏎Federal clarification regarding governance standards and interoperability expectations would⏎substantially mitigate administrative friction.⏎VIII. Interoperability as a Strategic Enabler⏎Enhanced interoperability is essential for expanding AI's utility in population risk management,⏎syndromic analysis quality benchmarking, and cross-state analytics.⏎HHS must continue to prioritize the enforcement of FHIR bulk data access provisions under 45⏎CFR 170.315(g)(10), structured semantic normalization standards, AI-ready metadata⏎frameworks, and the establishment of measurable data quality benchmarks.⏎Interoperability must be regarded as essential operational infrastructure, not merely aspirational⏎policy.⏎IX. Patient and Caregiver Considerations⏎Patients expect services characterized by timeliness, personalization, and reduced⏎administrative burden. They simultaneously expect transparency, fairness, and the preservation⏎of necessary clinician oversight. Human-in-the-loop safeguards, explainability features, and⏎auditable governance structures must be intrinsically embedded into all AI deployment⏎requirements.⏎Trust must be deliberately engineered; it cannot be presumed.⏎X. Research Priorities⏎⏎HHS should strategically prioritize research in the following critical domains:⏎• Explainable AI within authentic, real-world clinical workflows⏎• Federated multi-jurisdiction analytics methodologies⏎• Techniques for bias detection in longitudinal datasets⏎• AI-driven methodologies for data quality improvement⏎• Human-centered implementation science⏎While current literature documents gains in predictive performance and documentation⏎efficiency, empirical evidence regarding system-wide cost redistribution, workforce impact, and⏎long-term savings remains constrained. Federal research investment should be directed toward⏎closing this critical knowledge gap.⏎Conclusion⏎The successful integration of AI into clinical care will accelerate only upon the condition that⏎HHS effectively aligns regulatory clarity, enforceable interoperability standards, reimbursement⏎modernization, and comprehensive lifecycle governance frameworks.⏎The Department possesses the requisite tools to establish a national framework that enables⏎distributed analytics, maintains local data control, enforces auditability, and aligns financial⏎incentives with the generation of longitudinal value.⏎In the absence of coordinated federal action, the adoption of AI will remain fragmented. With it,⏎AI can be solidified as durable infrastructure for modern clinical care.⏎⏎Appendix A: Detailed Responses to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Comprehensive Analysis of Key Considerations for AI in Healthcare (Questions 1–10)⏎1. Biggest Barriers to AI Adoption and Scaling in the Health Sector⏎The primary obstacles preventing the widespread, scaled deployment of artificial intelligence in⏎healthcare are multifaceted and interconnected:⏎• Fragmented and Inaccessible Data: The lack of standardized, high-quality, and easily⏎accessible patient data across disparate systems (e.g., EHRs, imaging archives, claims⏎databases) severely limits the ability to train, validate, and generalize robust AI models.⏎Data silos and inconsistent semantic tagging are persistent issues.⏎• Regulatory Ambiguity for Non-Device AI: While the FDA provides a clear pathway for⏎AI designated as a medical device, there is significant uncertainty regarding the⏎governance, oversight, and liability for clinical, operational, and administrative AI tools⏎that do not meet the definition of a device (e.g., risk stratification tools, documentation⏎assistance, workflow optimizers). This ambiguity stifles innovation and adoption.⏎• Misaligned Reimbursement and Economic Models: Current fee-for-service payment⏎models and established coding structures often fail to adequately recognize or⏎incentivize the use of AI tools, particularly those that drive efficiency, prevention, or⏎improved population health outcomes rather than just procedural volume. A clear,⏎consistent reimbursement pathway is absent.⏎• Liability and Accountability Uncertainty: A lack of clarity regarding liability allocation⏎among developers, implementers, and clinical users in the event of an AI-driven error⏎(e.g., misdiagnosis, care pathway error) creates substantial legal risk and impedes⏎organizational willingness to deploy new technologies. Establishing clear indemnification⏎and accountability frameworks is critical.⏎2. Necessary Regulatory and Payment Model Changes to Support AI⏎HHS must take coordinated action across regulatory and payment structures to create an⏎environment conducive to safe and effective AI adoption:⏎• Clarify Governance under 45 CFR Part 170 (Health IT Certification): HHS, through⏎the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC), must⏎clearly define the certification criteria and scope of governance for non-device AI tools,⏎ensuring they meet minimum standards for safety, transparency, and data provenance.⏎• Strengthen Enforcement of 45 CFR Part 171 (Information Blocking): Aggressive⏎enforcement of information blocking regulations is essential to unlock the data necessary⏎for model development and validation. This includes focusing on barriers related to API⏎access and the cost-prohibitive nature of data extraction.⏎• Recognize and Address Federated Models under HIPAA (45 CFR 164.502 and⏎164.514): Policies must explicitly recognize and facilitate federated learning⏎environments, which allow models to be trained across distributed, protected data sets⏎without the need for centralized transfer of Protected Health Information (PHI), ensuring⏎compliance with minimum necessary use principles.⏎• Modernize Value-Based Reimbursement (42 CFR Parts 425 and 422): Payment⏎models for Accountable Care Organizations (ACOs) and Medicare Advantage (MA)⏎plans must be updated to explicitly recognize and incentivize the use of AI that⏎demonstrably improves efficiency, quality metrics, and cost management.⏎⏎• Pilot AI-Specific Add-On Payments (Consistent with 42 CFR 412.87): CMS should⏎establish pilots for new technology add-on payments (NTAPs) specifically tailored to⏎high-value AI solutions to facilitate early adoption and dissemination, similar to⏎mechanisms used for novel medical devices.⏎• Expand Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation (CMMI) Demonstrations (42⏎U.S.C. § 1315a): CMMI should prioritize and scale demonstrations focused on⏎evaluating the cost-effectiveness and clinical impact of AI, particularly in areas like⏎chronic disease management, population health, and reduction of administrative burden.⏎3. Novel Legal and Policy Issues Requiring Immediate HHS Attention⏎AI introduces significant novel issues requiring coordinated federal guidance:⏎• Liability Allocation and Indemnification: The most pressing issue is defining clear⏎roles and responsibilities when an error occurs. HHS needs to convene stakeholders to⏎develop model language for contracts and indemnification agreements between AI⏎developers and healthcare providers.⏎• Documentation Standards for AI Use: Clear standards are needed for documenting⏎the use of an AI tool, including the model version, the input data used, the date of last⏎retraining/validation, and the degree to which a clinician overrode the AI⏎recommendation. This is crucial for malpractice defense and quality reporting.⏎• Model Drift Accountability: Policies must address how providers and developers⏎monitor and are held accountable for model performance degradation over time (model⏎drift) due to shifts in patient population, coding changes, or clinical practice patterns.⏎• HHS Action Required: HHS should proactively issue standardized governance⏎templates and risk assessment frameworks for AI deployment. Furthermore, it must⏎convene cross-agency coordination (including FDA, ONC, CMS, and Office for Civil⏎Rights) to ensure consistent messaging and policy implementation.⏎4. Effective Methods for Evaluating AI Performance and Safety⏎Robust evaluation must span the entire lifecycle of the AI model:⏎• Pre-Deployment Evaluation: Rigorous, multi-site testing is required, focusing on bias⏎detection (e.g., performance parity across different demographic groups,⏎socioeconomic status, and care settings) and calibration testing (ensuring the model’s⏎predicted probability aligns with observed outcomes).⏎• Post-Deployment Monitoring: Continuous, real-world monitoring is essential, including⏎tracking for model drift (performance degradation) and systematic tracking of override⏎events (instances where a clinician rejects or modifies an AI recommendation) to⏎identify potential safety issues or clinical misalignment.⏎• Support Mechanisms: HHS should fund and support this evaluation through⏎cooperative agreements for multi-site validation studies and by establishing⏎benchmarking programs that provide standardized, de-identified datasets for external⏎model testing and comparison.⏎5. Role of Accreditation, Certification, and Standards Bodies⏎Voluntary and collaborative mechanisms can accelerate trust and adoption:⏎⏎• Encourage Voluntary AI Accreditation: HHS should encourage the development of⏎voluntary, third-party AI accreditation programs (e.g., through The Joint Commission or⏎NCQA) that are aligned with federal audit standards for security, equity, and⏎performance documentation.⏎• Create Regulatory Testing Sandboxes: Establish secure, controlled environments⏎("sandboxes") where developers and providers can test nascent AI tools in a real-world⏎setting under temporary regulatory waivers, allowing for rapid iteration and evidence⏎generation.⏎• Develop Model Reporting Templates: Mandate or strongly encourage the use of⏎standardized reporting templates (e.g., adapted from the Model Card or Data Card⏎concept) to clearly document a model's intended use, performance limitations, training⏎data characteristics, and known bias testing results.⏎• Establish Transparency Registries: Create a centralized, public or semi-public registry⏎for reporting essential metadata on deployed clinical and operational AI models,⏎enhancing transparency for providers, researchers, and patients.⏎6. Current Performance Experience and Most Promising Future Tools⏎The health sector has seen varying degrees of success with current AI deployment:⏎• Areas of Strong Performance: AI has shown powerful, validated performance in areas⏎such as medical imaging analysis (e.g., radiology, pathology), clinical documentation⏎assistance (e.g., ambient listening, charting suggestions), and predictive modeling for⏎operational needs (e.g., forecasting patient flow, resource utilization).⏎• Areas of Weak Performance and Integration Challenges: Performance suffers⏎significantly where integration into existing clinical workflows is cumbersome, and⏎where explainability is absent, leading to a lack of clinician trust and poor adoption⏎rates.⏎• Most Promising Future Tools: The highest future impact is anticipated from federated⏎surveillance and public health analytics (allowing models to track disease outbreaks⏎or resource needs without centralizing PHI) and longitudinal risk scoring that⏎integrates clinical, claims, and social determinants of health data to proactively manage⏎high-risk patients.⏎7. Key Organizational Decision Makers and Adoption Hurdles⏎Adoption requires navigating a complex internal landscape:⏎• Key Decision Makers: Adoption decisions are often a committee-based process⏎involving the Chief Information Officer (CIO), Chief Medical Information Officer⏎(CMIO), Compliance Officer, Chief Financial Officer (CFO), and Legal Counsel.⏎• Primary Adoption Hurdles:⏎o Lengthy Procurement Cycles: The time and effort required to move from⏎evaluation to contracting is often prohibitive.⏎o Indemnification Negotiations: Protracted legal battles over liability and risk⏎sharing between developer and provider.⏎o EHR Integration Barriers: Technical complexity, vendor-specific limitations, and⏎the cost associated with deep integration into electronic health record systems⏎remain the single largest technical barrier.⏎8. Interoperability Priorities for AI Development and Deployment⏎⏎Enhanced, standardized data flow is non-negotiable for AI success:⏎• Enforce FHIR Bulk Data Access (45 CFR 170.315(g)(10)): ONC must prioritize the⏎strict enforcement of the FHIR-based bulk data access requirement, enabling large-⏎scale, efficient data extraction necessary for training and deploying AI models across⏎patient populations.⏎• Advance Semantic Normalization and Benchmarking Tools: Investment and⏎standardization efforts are needed to harmonize disparate clinical terminologies (e.g.,⏎LOINC, SNOMED) and administrative codes, enabling models trained on one data set to⏎be reliably applied to another. HHS should support the creation of public-good tools for⏎semantic mapping.⏎9. Patient Concerns and Needs Regarding AI in Healthcare⏎Patient perspectives must guide the ethical implementation of AI:⏎• Desire for Efficiency and Personalization: Patients generally welcome AI tools that⏎promise to increase the speed of diagnosis, reduce administrative friction, and deliver⏎more personalized treatment recommendations.⏎• Concern about Bias and Opacity: The chief concerns relate to the potential for AI to⏎perpetuate or amplify existing health inequities (bias) and the lack of understanding⏎about how decisions are made (opacity or the "black box" problem).⏎• Required Reinforcements: Policy must emphasize radical transparency regarding⏎when and how AI influences care and mandate robust human oversight and review⏎mechanisms, ensuring that the ultimate decision-making authority rests with the human⏎clinician.⏎10. Critical Research Priorities for Public Investment⏎Federal research funding should be targeted to address foundational and implementation⏎challenges:⏎• Explainable AI (XAI): Research focused on developing reliable, clinically meaningful⏎methods for explaining AI outputs to both clinicians and patients, moving beyond simple⏎feature importance scores.⏎• Federated Analytics and Privacy-Preserving AI: Investment in technologies like⏎federated learning, homomorphic encryption, and differential privacy to enable model⏎training across sensitive, distributed data sources without violating privacy.⏎• Bias Detection, Mitigation, and Health Equity: Dedicated research on systematic⏎methods for proactively identifying, quantifying, and mitigating algorithmic bias across⏎diverse clinical populations and care settings.⏎• Implementation Science: Studies focused on the socio-technical aspects of AI⏎deployment, including human factors, workflow redesign, and the impact of AI on clinical⏎teams and patient safety culture.⏎• Cost-Transfer Analysis: Rigorous economic research to understand where the costs⏎and benefits of AI are realized (e.g., transfer of cost from the payer to the provider, or⏎from the hospital to the patient) to inform equitable reimbursement policy.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0134♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from IMPaCT Care, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔IMPaCT Care♔♔♔♔♔♔Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎7033A, 330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎On behalf of IMPaCT Care (IMPaCT), we thank you for the opportunity to offer comments in⏎response to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎We commend HHS’ efforts to accelerate adoption of AI within care and advance federal⏎incentives to enable AI to boost productivity, lower costs, and improve health outcomes.⏎We believe there are significant ways that AI can support Community Health Workers in⏎extending the reach of care quickly and at scale.⏎CHWs are trustworthy people who come from within the communities they serve. They get⏎to know their patients as people and provide holistic, tailored support based on individual⏎needs and preferences. Evidence-based CHW programs improve outcomes, reduce costs,⏎and address the twin crises in American health care today: the growing prevalence of⏎chronic illness in the context of clinical workforce shortages.⏎IMPaCT is the leading evidence-based Community Health Worker intervention in the U.S.,⏎designed to address the root causes of poor health. It is used by over 70 organizations⏎across 20 states and has been found in multiple randomized controlled trials to improve⏎chronic disease control, mental health, and quality of care while reducing total hospital⏎days and returning $2.47 for every dollar invested. In this letter, we offer comments in⏎response to specific questions included in the Request for Information including how⏎equipping CHWs with AI can expand access to person-centered and cost-effective care. We⏎also recommend CHW technology tools that can increase access and improve quality and⏎outcomes in the face of clinical workforce shortages.⏎Comments on Specific Questions included in the Request for Information⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎⏎The biggest barrier to AI adoption isn’t an implementation or regulatory barrier; it’s a⏎failure of imagination. Most AI tools in health care today are aimed at medical providers⏎(e.g., improving clinical decision support, reducing documentation burden). Building tools⏎for health care providers can make them more efficient and reduce medical errors, but it is⏎unlikely to fundamentally transform health outcomes for two reasons.⏎- First, there are simply not enough providers. The average wait time for a physician⏎appointment in large metro areas is 31 days, with even longer wait times for certain⏎specialties (e.g., 42 days for an obstetrician-gynecologist). Behavioral health⏎professionals are even more scarce - fewer than half of people with mental illness⏎get timely access to care, and even fewer people with substance use disorder are⏎able to access the care they need. Challenges accessing care are even greater in⏎rural areas and for individuals with Medicaid coverage.⏎- Second, health care providers are ill-equipped to address the root causes of poor⏎health. Most of the American workforce are clinicians e.g., (physicians, nurses,⏎medical assistants) whose main lever is medical care. Yet, medical care –the office⏎visit, the hospital stay– only accounts for 15% of what drives health outcomes.⏎Eighty-five percent of health is shaped by behavioral, social, and economic factors.⏎AI can do more than buttress a broken system. It can unlock human connection and⏎enable the health care workforce of the future. Community health workers (CHWs) are⏎trustworthy individuals who come from within the communities they serve. They provide⏎social support, care coordination, navigation and advocacy. Across four randomized⏎controlled trials and a large Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PCORI) multi-⏎site study, the IMPaCT model has been shown to save Medicaid payers $2.47 per dollar⏎invested, improve mental health, strengthen primary care engagement, reduce 30-day⏎readmissions and emergency department visits, and cut hospital days by 34% versus⏎matched controls.⏎Importantly, CHWs achieve these outcomes at a fraction of clinical labor costs. CHWs⏎represent an untapped source of human capital in health care, because they can be rapidly⏎deployed in any community.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎A primary challenge for AI-powered Community Health Worker models is that the majority⏎of health care payment requires a clinical encounter with a health care provider. Several⏎Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation models, including the Long-term Enhanced⏎ACO Design (LEAD) model and the Geo track within the Achieving Healthcare Efficiency⏎⏎through Accountable Design (AHEAD) model, create promising mechanisms for expanding⏎community-based models of care through flexible payment and multi-payer alignment.⏎Nowhere is innovation more necessary than in the safety net. We encourage the Health⏎Resources and Services Administration (HRSA) and the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid⏎Services (CMS) to partner to facilitate inclusion of Federally Qualified Health Centers⏎(FQHCs) in these models, including HRSA alignment to help FQHCs embrace AI.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new⏎insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Equipped with the right technology, Community Health Workers (CHWs) can be a new⏎“front door” to health care that is more person-centered, less expensive, and more⏎effective. In the IMPaCT model:⏎- CHWs use conversation prompts to solicit client goals. An AI scribe helps CHWs⏎focus on the person in front of them, rather than on capturing notes.⏎- CHWs then leverage decision supports and workflow tools within the IMPaCT⏎application that help them support goal attainment. The most common areas that⏎clients need support with are psychosocial (37%), health behavior change (36%), and⏎resources for daily living (e.g., housing, food; 15%). AI can help CHWs suggest⏎resources, fill out benefits applications, and manage follow-ups over an average of⏎12 encounters with a client.⏎- When clients do need medical care and health system navigation, CHWs can use⏎technology to rapidly connect to care teams to address common requests like⏎prescription refills without a new visit to a provider.⏎Using these technology tools, CHWs are able to help clients achieve more than 70% of their⏎goals, whether those goals are clinical (e.g., get my diabetes under control, find a⏎behavioral health provider) or social (e.g., get the home modifications I need to live⏎independently, apply for Social Security Income). AI acts as a force multiplier, allowing⏎CHWs to more rapidly connect with clients and help them achieve their goals, all while⏎managing larger panel sizes due to AI-driven efficiencies. By addressing a client’s greatest⏎needs, CHWs generate durable changes in health, increase access, and improve quality⏎while better using scarce clinician resources.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎More than half of American adults have multiple chronic conditions and are not well-served⏎by our fragmented delivery system. The most effective interventions are person-centered⏎⏎care plans that help individuals achieve their own goals. NCQA has developed person-⏎centered goal attainment quality measures, designed to measure what matters most to⏎each individual. Use of these measures could realign care, but to date, they have been⏎administratively burdensome to collect. AI tools can help efficiently capture person-⏎centered goals, allowing the health care system to align around what matters most to⏎patients.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you again for the opportunity to provide comments. We look forward to continuing⏎to collaborate with HHS on these critical issues. For any questions or to request more⏎information, please contact Dawn Alley at dalley@impactcarehq.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Shreya Kangovi, MD⏎CEO, IMPaCT Care♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0135♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Platt, Jodyn, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jodyn♔Platt♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔209 Victor Vaughan Building⏎1111 E. Catherine Street⏎Ann Arbor, Michigan 48109-2054⏎Phone: 734 936-1644⏎Fax: 734 936-1641⏎Email: learning.health.sciences@umich.edu⏎Website: LHS.medicine.umich.edu⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy,⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption of and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (90 Fed. Reg. 60108, December 23, 2025)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎As members of Michigan Medicine and the Trust, Innovation, and Ethics Research for Responsible AI⏎(TIERRA) program, we are submitting the following comments regarding the December 2025 Request for⏎Information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. We⏎appreciate the Department’s commitment to developing AI policy grounded in evidence and stakeholder input,⏎and we enthusiastically support the goal of advancing trustworthy AI that improves clinical care while⏎safeguarding patient and community interests.⏎Michigan Medicine, the academic medical center of the University of Michigan, is a nationally and⏎internationally recognized leader in patient care, research, and medical education. With over 3,000 faculty⏎across 22 specialties, it delivers high-volume, high-acuity care—operating 1,043 beds across University⏎Hospital, the Frankel Cardiovascular Center, and C.S. Mott Children’s Hospital, and caring for more than⏎50,000 inpatients and 2.2 million outpatients each year—while driving clinical innovation that improves⏎outcomes for diverse patient populations. Consistently ranked among the nation’s top hospitals, including top-⏎ten recognition in multiple specialties, Michigan Medicine leverages its integrated clinical, research, and⏎educational missions to expand access to high-quality care and inform state and national health policy that⏎benefits Michigan residents.⏎TIERRA (Trust, Innovation, and Ethics Research for Responsible AI) is a University of Michigan research⏎program dedicated to promoting responsible and ethical AI in healthcare. Through empirical research on public⏎trust, development of transparency and accountability tools, and policy engagement with health systems and⏎communities, TIERRA generates evidence that directly shapes how AI is designed, governed, and deployed so⏎that patients, providers, and communities experience AI as safe, fair, and trustworthy.⏎The following document presents comments organized around key themes in the RFI: regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research and development. These recommendations are grounded in our empirical work,⏎including:⏎⏎● Community deliberations with Michigan residents across the state on AI in healthcare.⏎● Development and testing of patient-informed “AI health labels” for clinical AI tools.⏎● Nationally-representative survey data on AI and healthcare.⏎● Policy engagement with health institution leaders and state/local partners on trustworthy AI⏎frameworks.⏎● Ongoing evaluation of AI deployment in real-world healthcare settings.⏎Overarching Recommendations⏎Drawing on Michigan Medicine and TIERRA’s work, we recommend that HHS:⏎1. Make trustworthiness and public trust a central organizing principle of federal AI policy in⏎healthcare, on par with innovation and efficiency.⏎2. Require basic transparency, disclosure, and communication standards for health AI, including⏎plain-language “AI labels” for tools used in clinical care.⏎3. Invest in community-engaged research and deliberative methods to surface multiperspectives⏎on AI, with particular attention to community values such as safety, privacy, and maintaining⏎the provider-patient relationship.⏎4. Support systematic monitoring and evaluation of AI tools in real-world clinical environments,⏎beyond initial validation and marketing claims.⏎5. Align regulatory and payment policies with these goals so that AI that demonstrably advances⏎safety, supports community values, and builds trust is incentivized, while high-risk or opaque⏎applications face heightened scrutiny.⏎Regulation: Centering Transparency, Trust and Community Values⏎Our research indicates that regulatory frameworks will succeed only if they reflect how patients and⏎the public understand and experience AI in their care.⏎Public Perspectives Highlight Transparency, Privacy, Fairness and Human⏎Connection⏎In a series of Michigan-based community deliberations, we found that members of the public⏎consistently identified several areas of concern regarding AI in healthcare: transparency, privacy and⏎data security, fairness in how tools are used, and potential loss of human connection or a diminishing⏎role of the provider, and uncertainty about accountability when AI is involved in their care. At the⏎same time, participants were hopeful that AI could improve the care they receive, advance research on⏎rare or understudied diseases, and act as a “second opinion” that supports providers rather than⏎replaces them.⏎These deliberations showed that people want assurance that AI is used in ways that are transparent,⏎safe, and aligned with community values. Regulatory policy that fails to address these concerns⏎explicitly risks eroding trust in both AI tools and the health systems that deploy them.⏎⏎I. Recommendation: Require Patient-Facing AI Transparency through⏎Standardized “AI Labels”⏎Building on deliberative work in Michigan, TIERRA developed and tested a prototype “AI Health⏎Label” intended to communicate key information about AI tools used in clinical care in a concise,⏎patient-informed format. In our study, participants expressed strong support for labeling that includes:⏎● Whether and how an AI tool is used in their care⏎● What data sources the tool relies on, and how privacy and data security are protected⏎● Evidence about benefits and risk, including performance across groups in ways that reflect⏎community concerns about fairness⏎● How the tool is governed (e.g. oversight processes, accountability, opportunities for recourse)⏎Participants viewed such labeling as critical to building trust, supporting informed decision-making⏎and preserving patient autonomy in settings where they might otherwise be unaware that AI is being⏎used.⏎We therefore recommend that HHS:⏎● Establish a baseline requirement for AI tools used in clinical care provide clear, plain-language⏎disclosure to patients, analogous to a standardized “AI label”⏎● Coordinate with FDA, ONC, and CMS so that labeling expectations are consistent across⏎regulatory and payment processes, and ensure that labeling practices are consistent with civil⏎rights, privacy, and accessibility obligations.⏎● Encourage or require developers and health systems to use patient-informed, empirically tested⏎label formats, such as those derived from TIERRA’s deliberative work, instead of ad-hoc or⏎purely marketing-driven materials.⏎II. Recommendation: Integrate Trust, Ethics, and Stakeholder Impact into Risk⏎Classification⏎Our work emphasizes that AI risk cannot be defined only in terms of technical performance; it must⏎also consider use cases, stakeholders, and broader system-level effects. In presentations and policy-⏎oriented work, TIERRA has highlighted the need for ethical frameworks that account for:⏎● The AI lifecycle (development, deployment, monitoring, and decommissioning)⏎● The intended context of use (clinical, administrative, population health, research)⏎● Which stakeholders are most affected and how (patients, providers, health systems,⏎communities)⏎● Existing patterns of mistrust in health systems and community priorities that AI may⏎exacerbate or mitigate⏎We encourage HHS to integrate such dimensions explicitly into AI risk stratification and oversight.⏎For example, tools used directly in diagnosis or treatment decisions for populations who have⏎historically been overlooked or made vulnerable, may warrant higher scrutiny, including more robust⏎transparency, auditing and community engagement requirements.⏎⏎Reimbursement and Incentives: Rewarding Trustworthy, Community-Aligned⏎Health AI⏎Our work indicates that payment policy will be a powerful level not only for adoption, but also shaping⏎which kinds of AI are developed and deployed.⏎I. Recommendation: Tie Reimbursement to Evidence of Clinical Benefit,⏎Community Values and Trust⏎Our focus on monitoring AI integration and delivery and evaluating whether AI improves clinical care⏎provides a useful template for the kinds of evidence that should inform reimbursement decisions.⏎In particular, we recommend that HHS:⏎● Encourage or require rigorous evaluation of AI tools in real-world settings, including clinical⏎outcomes, workflow effects, and patient-reported experiences of care, before large-scale⏎reimbursement is granted.⏎● Incorporate measures that reflect community values into coverage and payment decisions, such⏎as how AI affects groups defined by demographic and social factors that communities identify⏎as important, as well as patient perceptions of transparency and respect.⏎● Treat transparent communication, disclosures, and robust governance processes as positive⏎factors in payment policy.⏎Such approaches would support HHS’s goals of fostering public trust and reducing uncertainty, while⏎ensuring AI is deployed in ways that measurably improve outcomes for patients and communities.⏎II. Recommendation: Guard Against Incentives that Push High-Risk⏎Deployment Without Adequate Oversight⏎Our research has underscored that AI adoption is occurring unevenly across health systems, often⏎driven by vendor offerings and local capacity rather than systematic assessment of benefits and risks.⏎Participants in community deliberations voiced concern that AI might be implemented primarily to cut⏎costs or increase throughput, without sufficient attention to patient experience, privacy, or preserving⏎the provider-patient relationship.⏎HHS should therefore:⏎● Avoid reimbursement structures that reward volume-driven or purely efficiency-driven AI use,⏎especially where evidence of clinical benefit and acceptability to affected communities is weak.⏎● Support payment models that create space for provider judgment, shared decision-making, and⏎the ability to override or decline AI recommendations without penalty.⏎● Require that health systems adopting AI at scale implement mechanisms to identify and⏎respond to patient and community concerns, complaints, or harms associated with AI use.⏎Research and Development: Investing in Trust, Monitoring, and Community⏎Engagement⏎⏎TIERRA stands at the intersection of research, ethics, and policy. The program aims to assess public⏎trust in health institutions and AI, investigate how perceptions of AI affect trust, and monitor AI⏎integration in healthcare delivery and evaluate outcomes, and influence policies to advance system-⏎level trust.⏎Recommendation: Fund Longitudinal Monitoring of AI Integration in Health⏎Systems⏎Our work suggests that HHS should treat health AI not as a one-time deployment decision, but as an⏎ongoing sociotechnical transformation that requires continuous monitoring and adaptation. In addition,⏎our work to evaluate tools used in clinical settings, track trends and changes in public or patient⏎perspectives, and define the landscape of AI use in health systems point toward several priorities for⏎federal R&D support.⏎We recommend that HHS:⏎● Support longitudinal, multi-site studies of AI implementation in diverse health systems, with⏎attention to heterogeneity in populations, infrastructure and governance.⏎● Invest in methods and tools for continuous evaluation of AI, including post-deployment⏎auditing, performance drift detection, and mechanisms to incorporate patient and clinician⏎feedback over time.⏎● Encourage cooperative agreements and public-private partnerships that bring together health⏎systems, community organizations and academic centers like Michigan Medicine and TIERRA⏎to align technical innovation with ethical and social priorities.⏎A Roadmap for Trustworthy AI and Health Systems⏎We encourage HHS to view trustworthiness not as a constraint on innovation, but as the foundation of⏎which sustainable and beneficial AI adoption must stand on. By centering trustworthy principles, HHS⏎can accelerate AI adoption in ways that genuinely improve health outcomes and strengthen, rather than⏎erode, trust in AI systems and health systems.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to comment on this RFI and would welcome future collaboration as⏎HHS refines its strategy for AI in clinical care.⏎If you have questions or would like to further discuss our comments, please contact Dr. Jodyn M. Platt⏎at (734) 936-1644 or jeplatt@med.umich.edu. Thank you for your consideration.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jodyn M. Platt, PhD, MPP⏎Associate Professor, Learning Health Sciences & Health Management and Policy⏎Director, Trust, Innovation, and Ethics Research for Responsible AI (TIERRA)⏎University of Michigan | Medicine⏎Michael L. Burns, PhD, MD⏎Clinical Associate Professor of Anesthesiology⏎Associate Chief Medical Informatics Officer of Artificial Intelligence⏎Chair of Clinical Intelligence Committee⏎University of Michigan | Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0136♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Society for Psychoanalysis and Psychoanalytic Psychology, American Psychological Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Society for Psychoanalysis and Psychoanalytic Psychology, American Psychological Association♔♔♔♔♔♔Society for Psychoanalysis and Psychoanalytic Psychology (Division 39)⏎American Psychological Association⏎The Society for Psychoanalysis and Psychoanalytic Psychology (SPPP) is a division of⏎the American Psychological Association representing psychologists and allied⏎disciplines concerned with practice, theory, and research in psychoanalysis. SPPP is⏎registering its concerns about the uses of Artificial Intelligence generally and agentic AI⏎in particular. Our concerns fall into the following headings: Patient Safety, Privacy,⏎Clinical Efficacy and Quality, Clear and Enforceable Regulation.⏎1. Patient Safety⏎Federal and state governments historically have been responsible for ensuring public⏎safety through regulation of health care products, and regulation of health care⏎clinicians through credentialing and oversight systems. AI application in clinical settings⏎and services should be held to comparable or higher standards in order to ensure the⏎health and wellbeing of patients. Without federal regulation the oversight of AI is left to⏎corporations with financial interests in promoting their products or to myriad and⏎inconsistent state laws to determine how AI is deployed in health care. This will lead to⏎a patchwork of inconsistent standards that likely lead to a decline in public safety. In⏎mental health, we have already seen suicides and other forms of self-injuries by⏎vulnerable people that have been encouraged by unregulated AI applications.⏎2. Privacy⏎Psychotherapy requires strict requirements to ensure confidentiality of deeply sensitive⏎and personal information that inevitable arises mental health care. Many AI chatbots⏎that are heavily marketed for mental health support are not subject to HIPAA⏎regulations. There is no regulation of the use of sensitive personal information for⏎marketing or development purposes. Such information is open to data breaches, which⏎may cause serious, ireparable harm to those who use them. Regulations are therefore⏎necessary for AI applications in health care comply with HIPAA regulations at a⏎minimum.⏎3. Clinical Efficacy⏎AI applications are liable to make inaccurate outputs that people rely upon to make⏎major decision about their mental health or for others. Corporate standards for use and⏎marketing of their AI products fail to measure their effectiveness adequately. Rigorous⏎⏎analysis of meaningful health metrics such as patient outcome including sustained⏎symptom remission and recovery, analysis of dropout rates, and a multimodal analysis⏎of measures of improvement in quality of life are necessary to ensure safe and effective⏎use. Scientific standards require that such data be collected and analyzed by qualified,⏎independent evaluators.⏎4. Clinical Quality⏎In addition to rigorous analysis of clinical efficacy (above), the therapist-patient⏎relationship has been shown to be the best predicter of treatment outcome. The⏎capacity to develop a deep, meaningful, and thoughtful therapeutic relationship takes⏎careful training. It entails years of study and supervision for a therapist to purposely use⏎themselves to engage patients to deal with painful, sensitive issues, and to create a⏎facilitating partnership that imbues therapy with the hope and courage that is critical for⏎genuine growth to occur.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0137♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Penguin Ai, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Penguin Ai♔♔♔♔♔♔VIA ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION⏎To: Department of Health and Human Services Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎(ASTP) & Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC), Attention:⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A 330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy and Assistant Secretary Posnack,⏎Penguin Ai appreciates the opportunity to provide comments on the Department’s strategy to⏎accelerate the adoption and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in clinical care. As a⏎healthcare-native automation platform founded by executives from UnitedHealth Group, Optum,⏎and Kaiser Permanente, we share the Department’s commitment to deploying AI that is⏎transparent, equitable, and clinically safe.⏎We explicitly reject the use of "Black Box" AI in healthcare. Our platform is built on the principle⏎of the "Glass Box" where every AI prediction, from medical coding to clinical appeals, is⏎traceable back to a specific source of truth within the patient record. We believe this standard of⏎Traceability and Governance is essential for building trust among providers and patients.⏎On the subsequent pages we offer specific responses to questions posed in the RFI, drawing on⏎our experience deploying autonomous "Digital Workers" for healthcare providers.⏎Sincerely,⏎Fawad Butt, CEO⏎Penguin Ai⏎fawad@penguinai.com⏎773-936-2593⏎⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI⏎for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Response⏎HHS must address three primary friction points that currently stifle the rapid, secure, and⏎cost-effective deployment of AI:⏎● Access to Financial and Clinical Data (EMR & RCM Integration): The top technical⏎hurdle to AI adoption is the inability to seamlessly integrate with and extract high-quality⏎data from existing Electronic Health Records (EHRs) and Revenue Cycle Management⏎(RCM) systems. Currently, 76% of revenue cycle leaders rate integration between AI⏎tools and core EHR systems as a major or severe challenge. Poor data quality, brittle⏎system interfaces, and a lack of standardized APIs prevent AI models from functioning⏎effectively, starving them of the data needed to generate accurate insights.⏎● Capital Constraints & Misaligned Incentives: Legacy fee-for-service regimes often do⏎not account for the efficiency gains of AI. "Inertia" from these models means that⏎innovations lowering net spending often inadvertently reduce provider revenue.⏎Furthermore, margin pressures (e.g., MLR caps) restrict the available capital for payers⏎to invest in long-term AI infrastructure that may not yield immediate returns.⏎● Regulatory Uncertainty & Fragmented Governance: The absence of a standardized,⏎predictable regulatory framework creates novel legal issues regarding liability,⏎indemnification, and security - particularly for non-medical devices. This ambiguity⏎discourages private sector risk-taking and leads to fragmented adoption standards⏎across the industry.⏎● The Operational Skills Gap : There is a critical deficit in specialized talent capable of⏎managing data ingestion pipelines and model monitoring. Clinicians and administrators⏎often lack the necessary "AI Literacy" to evaluate the robustness and ethical implications⏎of these tools, leading to implementation paralysis.⏎Recommendations⏎To mitigate these barriers, HHS should leverage its regulatory and reimbursement pillars to⏎foster a predictable environment:⏎● Mandate Administrative Data Interoperability: To overcome the integration barrier,⏎ASTP/ONC must expand interoperability standards (e.g., USCDI) to explicitly include⏎comprehensive administrative and financial data classes . Certification requirements⏎should enforce standardized API access (like FHIR) to both EHR and RCM systems,⏎⏎ensuring AI tools can securely access necessary data without vendor friction. Details⏎offered in Question 2, Recommendation 4 below.⏎● Modernize Reimbursement: Continue shift away from fee-for-service regimes and⏎include payment policies that reward "high-value" AI interventions. Align federal⏎incentives so that AI is deployed to enhance productivity and reduce burden, rather than⏎just increasing volume.⏎● Establish a Predictable Regulatory Posture: Create a regulatory stance that is⏎well-understood, predictable, and "proportionate to the risks presented." HHS must take⏎an active role in clarifying liability and "safe harbor" frameworks for administrative AI⏎tools to enable rapid innovation. More on this in our response to Question 2, specifically⏎in Recommendations 3 and 5.⏎● Support Workforce Readiness: Fund the development of standardized role-based⏎training and "implementation science" to ensure clinicians and administrators can⏎integrate and oversee high-impact AI systems safely.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design⏎changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of⏎AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Response⏎HHS should prioritize programmatic and regulatory shifts that clearly distinguish between⏎Clinical AI (diagnostic/treatment) and Administrative AI (operational/revenue cycle) . To⏎incentivize effective use, it is critical to align financial rewards with the adoption of "high-value"⏎AI that reduces administrative waste, while establishing a regulatory "safe harbor" for⏎transparent, audit-ready administrative tools. By reducing the administrative burden through⏎automation, we return time and energy to clinical care.⏎Below are specific recommendations regarding programmatic incentives and regulatory⏎revisions.⏎Part 1: Programmatic Design Changes to Incentivize AI⏎Recommendation 1: Align MIPS Incentives with AI-Driven Burden Reduction⏎● Proposed Change (42 CFR Part 414): HHS should revisit the Merit-based Incentive⏎Payment System (MIPS), specifically the Improvement Activities performance category⏎(defined in 42 CFR § 414.1305 and § 414.1380).⏎⏎● Actions:⏎○ Explicitly classify the adoption of "AI-assisted Administrative Automation" (e.g.,⏎automated coding, chart abstraction, and prior authorization submission) as a⏎High-Weighted Improvement Activity.⏎○ Create a specific sub-category for technologies that demonstrate a measurable⏎reduction in "time-to-care" or administrative overhead costs.⏎● Rationale: Current MIPS categories heavily favor clinical data reporting, which often⏎adds to provider burden. By financially rewarding the adoption of AI tools that remove⏎administrative tasks, HHS can accelerate the deployment of technology that directly⏎combats clinician burnout and operational inefficiency.⏎Recommendation 2: Transition from Fee-for-Service to Value-Based AI Models⏎● Proposed Change (CMMI Authority): The Center for Medicare and Medicaid⏎Innovation (CMMI) should pilot a "Shared Savings for Administrative Efficiency" model⏎that builds upon the foundational goals of the ACCESS program and the WISeR model.⏎● Action: Instead of reimbursing for AI as a discrete service, allow providers to retain a⏎higher percentage of shared savings in Alternative Payment Models (APMs) if they⏎utilize certified AI agents to reduce the cost of Revenue Cycle Management (RCM) and⏎utilization review.⏎● Rationale: We applaud CMS’s ongoing efforts to advance care delivery through⏎programmatic design, but legacy fee-for-service regimes create "inertia" that diminishes⏎the promise of AI. If providers are reimbursed strictly for volume, there is little financial⏎incentive to use AI to become more efficient. Shared savings models that specifically⏎target administrative waste (estimated at 25% of US healthcare spend) will supercharge⏎the objectives of models like WISeR, driving rapid market adoption of high-value AI.⏎Part 2: Regulatory Revisions to Augment AI Development⏎Recommendation 3: Clarify Scope to Create a "Presumption of Safety" for Administrative⏎AI⏎● Proposed Change (Guidance on HTI-1 & 45 CFR Part 170): HHS and ASTP/ONC⏎should clear regulatory confusion by establishing a distinct boundary between Predictive⏎Decision Support Interventions (DSI) used for clinical care and Administrative AI used for⏎RCM, coding, and documentation.⏎● Action: Issue guidance explicitly clarifying that AI tools utilized solely for Payment and⏎Health Care Operations (as defined in 45 CFR § 164.501) fall outside the scope of the⏎"Predictive DSI" definition (45 CFR § 170.315(b)(11)) and are therefore not subject to⏎"clinical validity" testing requirements.⏎⏎● Standard: To qualify for this exclusion, the AI must utilize a "Glass Box" / Deterministic⏎Traceability architecture that links every output (e.g., a billing code) back to a specific,⏎human-verified source in the medical record and allows for human override.⏎○⏎Exhibit A: Example of "Glass Box" Traceability. The AI output (ICD-10 code) is deterministically⏎hyperlinked to the specific, human-verified sentence in the unstructured clinical note.⏎● Rationale: Administrative errors (e.g., a wrong billing code) are fundamentally different⏎from clinical errors (e.g., a missed diagnosis). They are financial, reversible, and⏎auditable. Subjecting operational algorithms to "clinical safety" standards creates⏎regulatory ambiguity that stalls innovation. Clarifying that transparent, administrative⏎tools are not "Predictive DSIs" creates a "Safe Harbor" that encourages the market to⏎adopt audit-ready automation immediately.⏎Recommendation 4: Expand Interoperability Standards for Administrative Data⏎Proposed Change (45 CFR Part 170 - ONC Health IT Certification): HHS should update the⏎United States Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) standards to support AI-driven⏎administrative workflows.⏎● Action: Expand USCDI as part of USCDI v7 to include a dedicated "Administrative⏎Automation" domain, or expand the existing “Health Insurance Information” domain.⏎○ Specifics: Standardize data elements for Prior Authorization Criteria, Payer⏎Denial Reasons, and Claim Status metadata. Currently, these often exist as⏎unstructured text or proprietary codes.⏎Rationale: AI agents require structured data to function deterministically. If a denial reason is⏎unstructured text, the AI must "guess," increasing the risk of hallucinations. By standardizing the⏎"Health Insurance Information" domain, HHS enables AI agents to read the "state" of a claim⏎deterministically, fueling the development of robust, interoperable AI solutions for both clinical⏎and administrative care.⏎Recommendation 5: Modernize HIPAA for "Privacy-by-Design" AI⏎⏎Proposed Change (45 CFR Parts 160 and 164 - HIPAA Privacy Rule): HHS should revisit the⏎interpretation of "Minimum Necessary" standards regarding AI training on De-Identified data.⏎● Action: Provide a specific safe harbor for "Privacy-by-Design" AI architectures that⏎utilize automated de-identification and edge-processing to scrub PHI before data enters⏎a model training environment.⏎Rationale: Protecting patient confidentiality is paramount, but ambiguity regarding the⏎"ingestion" of data for AI improvement leads to implementation paralysis. Clear guidance that⏎incentivizes architectural privacy (scrubbing data at the edge) rather than contractual privacy⏎(BAAs alone) will augment the industry's ability to develop safer, more capable AI models.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI⏎evaluation methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness⏎testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation methods⏎for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Response⏎HHS identifies AI software that assists in clinical care but does not meet the FDA’s definition of a⏎medical device as a critical area for innovation. These administrative and general-purpose tools⏎include:⏎● Ambient Scribes : AI that converts spoken clinician-patient dialogue into clinical notes.⏎● Administrative Automators : Tools for Revenue Cycle Management (RCM), medical⏎coding, or scheduling.⏎● Workflow Support : Software that manages general office operations or serves as a⏎secure messaging platform.⏎HHS should prioritize evaluation methods that bridge the gap between technical performance⏎and real-world administrative impact, emphasizing operational ROI, human-in-the-loop⏎oversight, and long-term model reliability.⏎Evaluation Methods for Digital Workers⏎Evaluation must move beyond simple accuracy to focus on the safety and efficiency of digital⏎workers:⏎⏎● Pre-deployment: Validation with Real-World Data (RWD) : Use secure platforms to⏎validate models against millions of de-identified patient records - including unstructured⏎data - to ensure the AI can handle clinical complexity before it goes live.⏎● Post-deployment: Real-Time Performance Monitoring : Implement continuous⏎monitoring to track KPIs such as "cost-to-collect," denial rates, and time-to-authorization.⏎● Iterative Robustness Testing : Regularly test models for "out-of-distribution" robustness⏎to ensure they do not fail when payer rules or clinical documentation patterns change.⏎Key Metrics for Clinical Care⏎Metrics must demonstrate both financial and operational value:⏎● Operational Throughput : Measuring the speed of administrative tasks, such as⏎reducing prior authorization time significantly.⏎● Accuracy and Correctness : Maintaining high rates of clinical correctness (e.g., 95.5%)⏎in specialized models to ensure patient safety.⏎● Revenue Integrity : Tracking the percentage of appeals overturned and the reduction in⏎the A/R backlog.⏎● Algorithmic Fairness : Using subgroup performance metrics to detect and correct bias,⏎ensuring the AI does not discriminate based on demographic data.⏎Recommendations⏎To foster a "healthcare-native" approach to AI evaluation and deployment, HHS should⏎implement the following support mechanisms:⏎Human-Centered Evaluation: The "Human-in-the-Loop" Model⏎● Establish Feedback Loops : HHS should support the development of systems that⏎enable human teams to provide real-time feedback used to retrain AI models and refine⏎workflows.⏎● Prioritize Decision Support : Incentivize AI that "distills and presents" information to⏎human decision-makers, ensuring final clinical and financial decisions remain⏎evidence-based and compassionate.⏎Impactful HHS Support Mechanisms⏎● Cooperative Research and Development Agreements (CRADAs) : Use CRADAs to⏎develop standardized, de-identified datasets that startups can use for model validation .⏎● Grants for Workforce Training : Provide grants to help providers and payers close the⏎"skills gap" by training staff to manage and audit agentic AI systems.⏎● Prize Competitions for Interoperability : Use prize competitions to incentivize the⏎creation of "system-agnostic" AI tools that plug into existing EHRs and payer portals⏎⏎without requiring a total system "rip and replace".⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health⏎care organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for⏎clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Response⏎The successful adoption of AI in clinical care is not merely a technical challenge but a⏎governance and leadership imperative. While executive leadership sets the vision, the actual⏎integration of AI into workflows (clinical, financial, operational) depends on a broader circle of⏎stakeholders who manage the intersection of clinical necessity, technical infrastructure, and⏎financial viability.⏎The Expanded Circle of Influence⏎While the C-Suite provides the overarching strategy, successful adoption hinges on specialized⏎roles that manage day-to-day operational risks:⏎● For Providers (Hospitals & Health Systems):⏎○ Head of Revenue Cycle Management (RCM): Acts as a primary driver for⏎adoption due to their responsibility for financial health, medical coding efficiency,⏎and billing accuracy.⏎○ VP of Revenue Cycle and CFO: Prioritize AI solutions that deliver measurable⏎ROI by reducing the "cost-to-collect" and minimizing claim denials.⏎○ Director of Clinician Operations: Crucial for ensuring that AI tools provide⏎genuine relief for documentation burdens rather than introducing new⏎administrative tasks.⏎● For Payers (Insurance Companies):⏎○ VP of Provider Network Strategy: Influences the adoption of tools that⏎streamline data exchange between payers and providers.⏎○ VP of Utilization Management & CMO: Focus on AI-driven prior authorization⏎reviews to ensure medical necessity and accelerate care delivery.⏎○ Director of Regulatory Compliance: Exercises significant oversight to ensure⏎that AI data ingestion meets strict HIPAA and civil rights standards.⏎Recommendations⏎⏎To address the persistent administrative friction points that stall AI adoption, HHS should⏎prioritize the following areas:⏎Overcoming Primary Administrative Hurdles⏎HHS must take an active role in mitigating the "non-technical" barriers to innovation:⏎● Mitigate the "Skills Gap": Provide support for healthcare organizations to build internal⏎expertise so they are not solely reliant on vendors, fostering a culture of trust and⏎effective monitoring.⏎● Relieve Budgetary Constraints:⏎○ Payers: Address the limitations posed by Medical Loss Ratio (MLR) growth to⏎allow for necessary technology investments.⏎○ Providers: Create financial pathways to offset high upfront costs and "rip and⏎replace" infrastructure projects in an environment of reduced payment rates.⏎● Address Governance and Liability "Inertia":⏎○ Clear Liability Guidelines: Establish a legal framework that clarifies⏎responsibility for AI errors (clinician vs. vendor) to eliminate the "wait and see"⏎culture.⏎○ Workflow Transformation Support: Provide guidance for transitioning from⏎manual, legacy workflows to "agentic" systems, recognizing the significant⏎change management required.⏎● Standardize AI Data Provenance and Payloads: While AI models are highly capable⏎of extracting data from unstructured sources (faxes, PDFs, voice), the technical⏎bottleneck is integrating that output into core systems. In other words, the receiving EHR⏎system doesn't speak English; it speaks in discrete codes (SNOMED, ICD-10). If every⏎AI vendor reads a .pdf and maps "heart attack" to a different code, or outputs the data in⏎a proprietary JSON format, the hospital's IT team has to build custom "plumbing" for⏎every single AI tool to get those findings back into the EHR. ASTP should invest in⏎standards (e.g., expanding FHIR Provenance resources) that standardize how⏎AI-extracted data, confidence scores, and source-document traceability are⏎communicated to EHRs, enabling plug-and-play interoperability without custom⏎mapping.⏎⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities,⏎fuel research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?⏎Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎Response⏎Penguin Ai commends ASTP/ONC and CMS for their monumental work in establishing the⏎current Interoperability Framework. Interoperability standards (like FHIR and USCDI) have⏎succeeded in unlocking "read" access to patient records, laying the critical groundwork for⏎modern health IT. However, the market remains constrained by a severe asymmetry in "write"⏎access. Large Electronic Health Record (EHR) and Revenue Cycle Management (RCM)⏎vendors maintain technical and contractual moats that restrict third-party AI from pushing⏎insights directly into native workflows.⏎This "Pull vs. Push" asymmetry relegates new AI vendors to use of "read-only" data supporting⏎workflows, dashboards or side-car applications. The resulting workflow fragmentation forces⏎clinicians and coders into "swivel-chair" processes that increase cognitive load and hinder the⏎adoption of even the most accurate AI models.⏎To widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate AI development [and the path⏎toward seamless cross-system workflows] built upon the foundation of the Interoperability⏎Framework, the focus must shift from basic data access to establishing standards for seamless⏎"write-back" capabilities, standardizing administrative data, and creating objective benchmarking⏎tools.⏎Recommendations⏎1. Mandate "Write-Back" Standards for Native Workflow Integration⏎● Action: ASTP should prioritize certification criteria (e.g., in upcoming HTI rulemakings)⏎that mandate standardized, bi-directional API capabilities for both clinical and⏎administrative workflows . This must include policies that limit the fees legacy vendors⏎can charge third parties for "write" access.⏎● Rationale: Accelerating AI requires the ability to push insights, such as an AI-generated⏎Prior Authorization payload, an extracted ICD-10 code, or a pre-visit clinical briefing,⏎directly into the user's native screen. Without affordable, standardized write-back⏎capabilities, AI cannot achieve its primary goal of reducing administrative burden.⏎2. Expand USCDI to Include Structured Administrative Data⏎● Action: As requested in Question 2, Recommendation 4, widen the USCDI and USCDI+⏎frameworks to formally standardize the exchange of administrative and financial data⏎(e.g., Claims Data, Payer Denial Reasons, Prior Authorization Criteria, Claim Status).⏎⏎● Rationale: AI development is currently bogged down by the need to parse unstructured⏎faxes, PDFs, and legacy X12 EDI formats. Standardizing these administrative elements⏎allows AI to function deterministically across different EHRs and RCMs, expanding⏎market opportunities beyond clinical diagnostic AI into high-value back-office automation.⏎3. Establish "Glass Box" Provenance Standards⏎● Action: ASTP should establish FHIR Provenance standards specifically for⏎AI-generated data. Any AI insight pushed into a certified health IT system must include⏎metadata linking the output directly to its human-verified source (e.g., a coordinate⏎mapping that highlights the exact sentence in a clinical note justifying a suggested⏎code).⏎● Rationale: Trust is a major barrier to adoption. If an AI writes data into an EHR,⏎downstream users need to know exactly how that conclusion was reached.⏎Standardizing AI provenance allows for seamless "Human-in-the-Loop" review without⏎disrupting the workflow.⏎4. Develop Open-Source "Gold Standard" Benchmarking Datasets⏎● Action: HHS should support the creation of open-source, de-identified datasets that⏎combine longitudinal clinical records with claims and payment data.⏎● Rationale: Research and market competition are currently limited by a lack of objective⏎benchmarking tools outside of proprietary EHR silos. Developers need access to⏎diverse, standardized datasets to evaluate the clinical robustness and the financial⏎"cost-to-collect" impact of their AI models, ensuring safer and more effective solutions.⏎Conclusion⏎We applaud ASTP and ONC for leading this conversation. The future of healthcare AI lies not in⏎"magic box" solutions, but in transparent, auditable, and healthcare-native systems that reduce⏎burden and improve outcomes.⏎We welcome the opportunity to discuss our "Glass Box" architecture and Governance⏎frameworks further with your team.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0138♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from BD, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔BD♔♔♔♔♔♔555 11th Street NW, Suite 305⏎Washington DC 20004⏎bd.com⏎Feb 23, 2026 Public Version⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (Docket ID.- HHS-ONC-2026-0001)⏎Dear Mr. Posnack,⏎BD (Becton, Dickinson and Company) applauds the ASTP for its leadership in accelerating the⏎safe, effective use of AI in clinical care. We support the agency’s goal of fostering public⏎trust, reducing uncertainty that slows innovation, and aligning federal incentives to improve⏎AI efficiency, lower costs and strengthen health outcomes.⏎BD is committed to ensuring patients have access to medical technologies, touching billions⏎of patients and helping improve and save lives globally. In the United States, BD⏎manufactures more medical devices than any other company, employing over 29,000⏎employees and operating more than 30 manufacturing sites and distribution centers.⏎Throughout our 128-year history, BD has been a long-standing partner to the U.S.⏎government in its efforts to ensure access to innovative medical technologies that are critical⏎to American healthcare. Looking ahead, we are shaping the future of connected care⏎through novel innovations in medication management, patient monitoring and pharmacy⏎automation. By embedding AI and automation, we are streamlining complex clinical⏎workflows and generating actionable insights that enable clinicians to deliver safer, more⏎precise care for patients.⏎We believe that clear, predictable regulatory environment and modernized reimbursement⏎pathways are essential to unlocking AI’s full potential across the healthcare ecosystem. To⏎achieve that goal, we wanted to offer the following recommendations for consideration:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The most significant barriers to private-sector innovation in AI stem from the absence of⏎scalable and sustainable pathways to deploy high-value AI technologies in inpatient,⏎procedural care and post-acute settings where healthcare costs and complexity are greatest.⏎The agency has an opportunity to bend the cost curve by incentivizing the use of AI‑enabled⏎technologies in these settings to automate workflows, reduce redundancies, and support⏎earlier clinical intervention. For example, AI‑enabled hemodynamic monitoring during and⏎after surgery can help improve patient safety and reduce complications such as acute kidney⏎injury (AKI), myocardial injury, surgical site infections, and delirium. AI‑enabled monitoring in⏎obstetric care can reduce maternal hypotension and improve early identification of⏎⏎555 11th Street NW, Suite 305⏎Washington DC 20004⏎bd.com⏎postpartum hemorrhage. Such interventions can meaningfully improve outcomes while⏎reducing length of stay and downstream costs.⏎Addressing these barriers will require modernized reimbursement models for AI embedded⏎in medical devices, regulatory clarity, and established incentive structures that reward health⏎systems for investing in deflationary AI technologies.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in⏎clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations⏎citations.⏎From a regulatory perspective, FDA’s risk‑based framework provides a strong foundation for⏎AI‑enabled medical technologies, but more consistent implementation is needed to support⏎adaptive systems. Encouragement of broader and more consistent use of FDA’s⏎Predetermined Change Control Plans (PCCPs) is needed, enabling responsible⏎post‑deployment updates without unnecessary resubmissions, and expand use of real‑world⏎evidence to support lifecycle oversight while maintaining patient safety. In addition, while⏎the proposed HTI‑5 rule makes meaningful progress in reducing certification burden and⏎narrowing ONC’s scope, it does not explicitly clarify how FDA‑regulated medical devices that⏎use EHR data are treated, leaving unresolved the risk of duplicative oversight for⏎technologies already determine to be safe and effective by the FDA.⏎From a payment policy perspective, CMS should prioritize clearer and more predictable⏎pathways for coverage, coding, and reimbursement of AI‑enabled medical technologies, as⏎payment uncertainty remains a primary barrier to adoption and investment in innovative AI⏎products. Transitional coverage pathways, demonstration models, and targeted add‑on⏎payments could better align incentives with technologies that reduce burden, improve⏎outcomes, or lower total cost of care. Given that many MedTech solutions involve capital⏎intensive hardware and evolve through ongoing software enhancements, pilot programs⏎should be designed with a clear mechanism to translate demonstrated value into durable⏎coverage, coding, or payment pathways, enabling broader and more sustainable adoption⏎over time. Specifically, we have the following recommendations with respect to payment⏎policies:⏎Accounting for Devices that Streamline Operations while Improving Outcomes⏎We recommend that CMS consider alternative payment pathways for AI-enabled capital that⏎can substantially reduce waste, enhance workflow efficiencies and improve patient quality⏎including through medication management. Though inpatient settings may have historically⏎conducted these internal value assessments, post-acute facilities including Skilled Nursing⏎Facilities and Behavioral Health settings are limited in their capacity to adopt new⏎technologies. We recommend that CMS work with industry, hospital, and pharmacy leaders⏎to envision operational and clinical excellence with a reimbursement pathway to incent it.⏎Accounting for Capital Investment in AI Technologies under Medicare’s Payment Systems⏎⏎555 11th Street NW, Suite 305⏎Washington DC 20004⏎bd.com⏎Despite the advancements in prevention, treatments and therapies, challenges to access and⏎disparities in care persist in the US health system. Factors such as geographic inaccessibility,⏎economics, insurance, QHP shortages, health system bias, race and ethnicity, language and⏎many other factors contribute to this growing trend. In addition, for many conditions, access⏎to evidence-based first line therapy is limited at best, reducing care quality, negatively⏎impacting population health and increasing total health care costs. The very nature of AI⏎provides opportunities to achieve access to evidence-based care at scale, especially for⏎underserved populations. These innovative technologies have been proven to deliver⏎clinically effective treatment and allow for ongoing care to reach health care deserts where⏎access to QHPs is challenged and where existing treatments fall short.⏎Modernizing Fee-for-Service Payments to Support AI Adoption⏎We also encourage CMS to consider holistic updates to its fee for service payment systems⏎to reflect the significant resource investments required to adopt and implement not only AI⏎within medical devices, but the broader scope of workflow and process improvement⏎software and related technologies. These investments extend beyond procurement of a⏎software license and include cybersecurity updates, staff training, and hardware⏎procurement. While these costs are typically considered indirect and therefore not⏎reimbursable, we encourage the agency to consider how they might be incorporated into⏎annual market basket updates or other update factors to ensure appropriate accounting for⏎provider costs.⏎Finally, aligning regulatory and reimbursement policies will be critical to ensure AI tools⏎integrate into clinical workflows and are evaluated based on real‑world performance,⏎reducing uncertainty and accelerating responsible adoption.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎As we all know, innovation moves at the speed of trust – particularly in clinical care.⏎Supporting private‑sector innovation by promoting greater clarity, alignment, and stability⏎across AI accreditation, certification, and governance frameworks will be critical in⏎accelerating adoption.⏎FDA has established a strong, trusted foundation for regulating AI‑enabled medical⏎technologies, and continued alignment with FDA - along with international harmonization⏎(e.g., IMDRF) - is critical to maintaining trust, enabling global competitiveness, and ensuring⏎U.S. leadership in the AI race. It’s equally important to establish alignment (e.g., standards,⏎best practices, success metrics) with relevant professional societies and hospital⏎accreditation bodies that shape how AI is operationalized in hospitals and other care⏎settings. From an industry perspective, harmonized and consistent AI regulations, standards,⏎and best practices across public and private sectors are critical to accelerating innovation⏎and adoption.⏎6. What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care⏎outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎⏎555 11th Street NW, Suite 305⏎Washington DC 20004⏎bd.com⏎The greatest potential for AI to improve healthcare outcomes, enhance quality insights, and⏎reduce costs lies in technologies that operate at the intersection of clinical decision support,⏎workflow automation, and closed-loop care delivery.⏎A. Closed‑loop technologies hold significant promise to advance deflationary, workforce⏎supporting care delivery by safely automating well‑defined clinical tasks through tightly⏎governed connections between monitoring, analytics, and therapeutic action. When⏎appropriately designed and overseen, these systems can reduce cognitive and⏎administrative burden on clinicians, extend scarce clinical capacity in rural and⏎underserved settings, and enable more timely, consistent interventions.⏎For instance, Medication errors remain one of the most persistent and costly patient⏎safety challenges in healthcare. AI tools that integrate pharmacy automation, infusion⏎delivery, and patient monitoring into a unified, closed-loop system have the potential to⏎dramatically reduce adverse drug events, optimize dosing in real time, and ensure the⏎right medication reaches the right patient at the right dose. AI embedded across these⏎connected devices can flag dosing anomalies, reconcile medication orders against⏎patient-specific parameters, and alert clinicians before an error occurs rather than after.⏎At the same time, we recognize potential concerns around safety, accountability, and⏎oversight of closed‑loop systems, underscoring the importance of clear guardrails, staged⏎testing environments, and robust post‑market surveillance. We recommend⏎development of a clear, accelerated pathway to responsibly test and reimburse such⏎technologies to improve access, efficiency, and patient outcomes.⏎B. AI-Enabled Hemodynamic and Patient Monitoring: AI-powered continuous monitoring⏎tools represent a significant opportunity to shift care from reactive to proactive. By⏎analyzing real-time physiological data streams (including blood pressure, cardiac output,⏎fluid responsiveness, and respiratory patterns) AI algorithms can detect early signs of⏎clinical deterioration with a specificity and speed that exceeds traditional threshold-⏎based alerting. Such hemodynamic monitoring technologies, equipped with AI-driven⏎analytics, can help clinicians anticipate and prevent serious complications such as acute⏎kidney injury, hemodynamic instability, and sepsis. In surgical and critical care settings,⏎this translates directly into reduced complications, shorter ICU stays, and lower total cost⏎of care.⏎C. AI-Driven Pharmacy Automation and Intelligent Supply Chain: Beyond the bedside, AI⏎has significant potential to transform pharmacy operations and medication supply chain⏎management. Intelligent automation tools that leverage AI to optimize medication⏎inventory, predict demand, flag dispensing irregularities, and reduce drug diversion can⏎meaningfully lower operational costs while improving safety and compliance. Pharmacy⏎automation platforms, augmented with AI capabilities, can help health systems move⏎from manual, error-prone processes to predictive, data-driven pharmacy operations⏎therefore resulting in reduced waste, improve throughput, and free pharmacists to focus⏎on higher-value clinical activities.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎⏎555 11th Street NW, Suite 305⏎Washington DC 20004⏎bd.com⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Establishing clear, cross‑functional governance groups within healthcare organizations is⏎essential to accelerating adoption of AI in clinical care. Meaningful decisions typically require⏎alignment among three groups – 1. clinical leaders focused on patient outcomes, 2. finance⏎and procurement teams assessing value and return on investment, and 3. IT and data⏎governance functions responsible for interoperability, cybersecurity, and infrastructure⏎readiness. National guidelines and professional societies further shape adoption by building⏎clinician confidence and standardizing appropriate use of AI.⏎Our experience through our Clinical Affairs teams working closely with hospitals shows that⏎AI adoption is most successful when technologies are evaluated through multidisciplinary⏎structures. In the absence of coordinated oversight, many AI solutions face “valleys of death”⏎between initial pilots and true scale‑up. From best practice standpoint, healthcare systems⏎have addressed similar challenges (e.g., antimicrobial resistance) by establishing dedicated⏎cross‑functional committees accountable for outcomes. A comparable governance model for⏎AI, supported by clearer guidance, could help organizations move more efficiently from⏎pilots to meaningful adoption. BD would welcome the opportunity to share our engagement⏎frameworks that have proven effective in demonstrating the clinical, operational, and⏎economic value of AI technologies.⏎Lastly, health systems face significant administrative hurdles due to reduced funding and⏎tight cost constraints, forcing them to maximize the use of existing capital equipment;⏎without targeted incentives, hospitals have limited ability to move beyond the status quo⏎and adopt new technologies.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would significantly widen market opportunities and accelerate AI⏎development by reducing the cognitive and operational burden clinicians face from⏎navigating multiple, disconnected systems. Today, clinicians are often required to engage⏎with numerous screens, dashboards, and point solutions, many of which operate outside⏎core clinical workflows, limiting adoption and diminishing the value of AI. Interoperability⏎that enables AI tools to integrate seamlessly with EHRs and other clinical systems - rather⏎than requiring clinicians to leave their primary workflow - would improve usability, trust, and⏎clinical impact. In addition, ASTP should consider creating a national data registry to provide⏎industry access to standardized datasets that support benchmarking and real-world testing.⏎Enabling this by promoting interoperable data standards (e.g., USCDI‑aligned data elements,⏎FHIR‑based APIs), supporting consistent identity and patient‑matching frameworks, and⏎encouraging benchmarking tools that assess AI performance within real‑world clinical⏎workflows. Such an approach would allow a broader ecosystem of innovators to contribute⏎value, streamline how AI engages clinicians, and ensure that competition and innovation are⏎driven by outcomes and usability.⏎⏎555 11th Street NW, Suite 305⏎Washington DC 20004⏎bd.com⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Prioritizing applied AI research that addresses high‑impact and high-cost public health needs⏎such as maternal health, cardiovascular care, and medication errors is where the initial focus⏎should be in terms of technology adoption. Examples include earlier identification of⏎postpartum hemorrhage, continuous hemodynamic monitoring, and AI‑enabled pharmacy⏎automation to support reliable medication availability closer to patients, recognizing that⏎affordability alone does not ensure access. Expanding federal regulatory pilots such as⏎TEMPO to include such critical use cases would support nationally relevant public health⏎priorities while helping establish consistent regulatory expectations across a fragmented⏎payer landscape.⏎Importantly, AI research should not focus on technology in isolation but also examine the⏎people and process changes required for adoption, including clinician education and⏎certifications, workflow redesign, and appropriate guardrails for safe use. Research that⏎integrates technology with workforce readiness and operational change will be most⏎effective in demonstrating real‑world value and accelerating adoption at scale.⏎BD looks forward to working closely with ASTP to advance our shared priority of accelerating⏎adoption of AI in clinical care and positioning America as a global leader in AI driven medical⏎technologies.⏎In the meantime, please feel free to contact us with any questions or for additional information.⏎Jessica Johnston⏎Vice President⏎Healthcare Policy, Market Access and U.S. Government Relations⏎BD♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0139♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Electronic Privacy Information Center, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Electronic Privacy Information Center♔♔♔♔♔♔COMMENTS OF THE ELECTRONIC PRIVACY INFORMATION CENTER⏎to the⏎DEPARTMENT OF HEALTH AND HUMAN SERVICES⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎90 Fed. Reg. 60,108⏎February 23, 2026⏎The Electronic Privacy Information Center (EPIC) submits these comments in response⏎to the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS or the Department)’s Request for⏎Information (RFI) regarding Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care.1 EPIC urges the Department to prioritize privacy in the adoption and use of all⏎technologies used in clinical settings as privacy is essential to improve health outcomes. HHS⏎should focus on AI protections for patients and health data, not deregulation.⏎EPIC is a public interest research center in Washington, D.C., established in 1994 to⏎focus public attention on emerging civil liberties issues and to secure the fundamental right to⏎privacy in the digital age for all people through advocacy, research, and litigation.2 For decades,⏎EPIC has worked with federal agencies under different administrations to safeguard individuals’⏎1 Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care, 90⏎Fed. Reg. 60,108 (Dec. 23, 2025), https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/23/2025-23641/request-for-⏎information-accelerating-the-adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence-as-part-of [hereinafter “RFI”].⏎2 EPIC, About Us (2025), https://epic.org/about/.⏎⏎personal data and to protect patients’ medical privacy.3 EPIC advocates for transparent, equitable,⏎and commonsense AI policy and regulations to protect individuals against AI systems being used⏎in harmful, opaque, and unaccountable ways.4⏎EPIC appreciates the Department’s desire to uphold patient privacy, civil rights, and civil⏎liberties when considering ways to implement AI in clinical care settings.5 EPIC has written⏎extensively about harms that stem from health privacy invasions, including a recent report called⏎Beyond HIPAA: Reimagining Health Privacy Laws to Promote Equity in the Digital Age. Chapter⏎4: Artificial Intelligence discusses problems associated with unregulated AI use in health⏎settings, explains the harms from such use, and offers policy solutions to best protect health⏎privacy. EPIC has excerpted this chapter in response to the Department’s RFI.⏎EPIC encourages HHS to deprioritize the acceleration of AI adoption and use in clinical⏎care settings and instead suggests that HHS promote AI protections for patients nationwide. If⏎you have any additional questions, please contact EPIC Senior Counsel Sara Geoghegan at⏎geoghegan@epic.org.⏎3 EPIC Comments to FTC on RFI for Information on Gender-Affirming Care for Minors (Sept. 26, 2025),⏎https://epic.org/documents/comments-of-the-electronic-privacy-information-center-to-the-federal-trade-commission-⏎on-request-for-information-on-gender-affirming-care-for-minors/; EPIC Comments to HHS on HIPAA Security⏎Rule to Strengthen the Cybersecurity of Electronic Protected Health Information, 90 Fed. Reg. 898 (Mar. 7, 2025),⏎https://epic.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/EPIC-HHS-HIPAA-cybersecurity-rule-NPRM-comments.pdf; EPIC⏎Comments to the CFPB on the Prohibition on Creditors and Consumer Reporting Agencies Concerning Medical⏎Information, Docket No. CFPB-2024-0023 (Aug. 2024), https://epic.org/documents/comments-of-epic-to-the-cfpb-⏎on-the-prohibition-on-creditors-and-consumer-reporting-agencies-concerning-medical-information/; Comments of⏎EPIC in re the Federal Trade Commission’s Proposed Order with Blackbaud, Inc., FTC File No. 202-3181 (Mar.⏎2024), https://epic.org/documents/comments-of-epic-in-re-the-federal-trade-commissions-proposed-order-⏎settlement-with-blackbaud/.⏎4 AI & Human Rights, EPIC (2026), https://epic.org/issues/ai/; AI Policy, EPIC (2026),⏎https://epic.org/issues/ai/ai-policy/; EPIC Urges the OSTP to Focus on AI Protections, Not Deregulation,⏎EPIC (Oct. 28, 2025), https://epic.org/epic-urges-the-ostp-to-focus-on-ai-protections-not-deregulation/.⏎5 RFI at 60,109.⏎EPIC Comments 2 Department of Health and Human Services⏎RFI re: Accelerating the Adoption and Use February 23, 2026⏎of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎/s/ Sara Geoghegan⏎Senior Counsel &⏎Director, Consumer Privacy Program⏎ELECTRONIC PRIVACY⏎INFORMATION CENTER (EPIC)⏎1519 New Hampshire Ave. NW⏎Washington, DC 20036⏎202-483-1140 (tel)⏎202-483-1248 (fax)⏎EPIC Comments 3 Department of Health and Human Services⏎RFI re: Accelerating the Adoption and Use February 23, 2026⏎of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎⏎DATA BREACH | 127⏎PART IV⏎ARTIFICIAL⏎INTELLIGENCE⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 128⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE⏎Artificial Intelligence Exacerbates Health Inequities Due to a⏎Lack of Safeguards and Regulations⏎Over the last few years there has been a rapid expansion in the⏎development and use of "Artificial Intelligence" (AI) systems463 across a wide range⏎of industries and applications. Some of these systems are used to analyze data⏎sets to identify patterns or in the course of research (e.g. pharmaceutical or⏎genomic research). And other systems are designed to scan, manipulate, and⏎generate text, images, or other outputs based on natural language or other types⏎of user inputs. Many of these generative Artificial Intelligence systems (GAI) are⏎built on “Large Language Models,” which are algorithms developed through⏎analysis of massive data sets of text.⏎The rapid deployment of AI systems is largely unregulated and these⏎technologies create significant risks to health privacy. The growth of GAI systems⏎has also fueled commercial surveillance across the digital ecosystem and has⏎magnified risks to privacy because it has given companies a nearly infinite⏎appetite for more data and puts sensitive data at risk of improper disclosure. The⏎rollout of AI in health care settings, in particular, is turbocharging privacy risks⏎because in many cases these systems are not fit for purpose and lack adequate⏎safeguards: sensitive health information is ingested by systems that might later⏎disclose it to others; AI is deployed for medical uses without FDA approval;⏎screening of patient claims is being run through AI-powered assessment systems⏎designed to minimize costs; and individuals are being presented with text from⏎chatbots that purports to give medical advice. This section discusses how AI⏎463 This term is frequently used without a clear definition, and AI systems do not process or wield any⏎intelligence in a human way. “‘AI’ is often used as a catch-all term encompassing a wide variety of⏎technologies, ranging from the simplest algorithms to the most complex systems and everything in⏎between. Each of these technologies that commonly fall under the ‘AI’ umbrella have distinct abilities, uses,⏎and harms, and categorizing them all as ‘AI’ is a marketing ploy, not an assessment of the technologies⏎themselves.” Kara Williams & Ben Winters, Specific Terms for Specific Risks: The Need for Accurate⏎Definitions of AI Systems in Policymaking, EPIC (Oct. 1, 2025), https://epic.org/specific-terms-for-specific-⏎risks-the-need-for-accurate-definitions-of-ai-systems-in-policymaking/; Kara Williams & Mayu Tobin-Miyaji, A⏎New Year’s Resolution for Everyone: Stop Talking about Generative AI Like It Is Human, EPIC (Jan. 8,⏎2026), https://epic.org/a-new-years-resolution-for-everyone-stop-talking-about-generative-ai-like-it-is-⏎human/.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 129⏎compounds the harms from unprotected health data and offers solutions to⏎regulate AI to better protect our health privacy.⏎A. Introduction⏎The following stories illustrate the various forms and contexts in which AI is⏎already being deployed in healthcare settings and is putting the health of millions⏎of patients at risk:⏎Ì A health insurance company suddenly refuses to pay for a child’s medically⏎necessary treatment prescribed by their doctor, leaving their parent facing⏎tens of thousands of dollars of out-of-pocket costs for the care. The parents⏎are unaware that the insurance company is using an AI-powered screening⏎system to analyze thousands of claims and target costly medical care for⏎denials, putting the patient’s health and wellbeing at risk and burdening⏎healthcare providers.⏎Ì A clinical decision support system (CDS) meant to detect a potentially fatal⏎condition does not work well for Black patients and creates many false⏎positives that divert hospital resources away from other patients. AI⏎developers provide opaque or insufficiently tested AI systems and medical⏎institutions deploy them, producing biased or inaccurate outputs that⏎undermine patient safety and health equity.⏎Ì When an individual submits mental health questions to an AI chatbot, the⏎system generates a response that claims it is a licensed therapist, promises⏎confidentiality, cites to fake but convincing-sounding scientific articles, and⏎includes incorrect medical advice that could lead to physical and mental⏎harms—including encouraging suicide when someone is asking for help⏎with their mental health.⏎Ì Companies design chatbot systems to increase user reliance on those⏎systems—by responding to a wide range of prompts including requests for⏎medical advice. They design chatbots to collect increasingly more data,⏎such as user input data that includes sensitive health information, to use for⏎future training and targeted advertising.⏎These are only some of the ways that AI systems are already negatively⏎impacting the health of individuals. The definition of AI can be elusive and broad—⏎in this setting, we use the term to encompass machine-based systems that⏎produce predictions, recommendations, decisions, or content with a varied level⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 130⏎of human involvement. Until a few years ago when generative AI became more⏎widely available, most uses of AI systems in health care involved the use of⏎machine learning systems.464 The range of AI applications in health care has⏎expanded to include supporting population health management, monitoring⏎patients, guiding surgical care, predicting health trajectories, and recommending⏎treatments on the side of clinical applications, and automating laborious tasks,⏎recording digital clinical notes, and optimizing operational processes on the⏎administrative side.465 There are significant challenges to ensuring that AI systems⏎support, and do not jeopardize, the health, privacy, and safety of patients.⏎Deployment of AI throughout healthcare systems and directly to the public⏎is worsening pre-existing privacy and health equity issues and creating novel⏎problems. Companies developing these systems are strongly incentivized to⏎collect as much personal information about individuals as possible, exacerbating⏎privacy risks. And companies implementing these AI systems in health care,⏎insurance, and other fields are being encouraged to analyze, screen, and sort⏎people into categories based on their unique characteristics, including sensitive⏎health characteristics. These health inferences are being used to target⏎advertisements and to set individualized prices (a practice known as “surveillance⏎pricing”). Patients are not only being tracked and having their health information⏎put at risk of breach, but they might be denied access to care or more affordable⏎medicine, coverage, or treatment based on data and inferences without their⏎knowledge or consent.466 Inferences can be biased with respect to characteristics⏎like gender and race, which in turn can lead to treatment disparities.467 The data⏎collected and used to train AI create new opportunities for data breaches, leaks,⏎and misuse of personal health information.468 The use of AI systems by insurers to⏎screen claims and applicants leads to gatekeeping and denial of coverage for⏎464 See, Adam Bohr & Kaveh Memarzadeh, eds., Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare, Academic Press⏎(2020), https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128184387000137 (discussing various uses⏎of AI in healthcare involving machine learning, published before the mainstream introduction of generative⏎AI.).⏎465 U.S. Gov't Accountability Off., Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of⏎Technologies to Augment Patient Care, GAO-21-7SP at ix (2020), https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-21-⏎7sp.pdf.⏎466 Geoghegan & Winters, supra note 352; Tobin-Miyaji, supra note 340; FTC Surveillance Pricing 6(b)⏎Study: Research Summaries, FTC 3 (2025),⏎https://www.ftc.gov/system/files/ftc_gov/pdf/p246202_surveillancepricing6bstudy_researchsummaries_re⏎dacted.pdf.⏎467 U.S. Gov't Accountability Off., Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of⏎Technologies to Augment Patient Care, GAO-21-7SP at ix (2020), https://www.gao.gov/assets/gao-21-⏎7sp.pdf.⏎468 Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n. at 4⏎(2024), https://www.ama-assn.org/system/files/ama-ai-principles.pdf.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 131⏎medical care that can threaten patient health and financial wellbeing. The⏎consequences of irresponsible AI system deployment can include improper⏎disclosure of confidential health information, degraded health care and health⏎outcomes, health inequity, healthcare provider burnout and closure of healthcare⏎clinics, and the undermining of patient autonomy.⏎B. The Legal Backdrop of AI Impacting Health⏎The statutes and regulations that apply to AI systems discussed so far can⏎be largely categorized as health-specific laws and non-health-specific laws. The⏎two main health-specific laws and regulations this subsection discusses are HIPAA⏎and the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulations and rules on medical⏎devices. There is no general AI law at the federal level. Thus, determining where⏎the health-specific laws do and do not apply to these systems is essential to⏎understand what rules govern the use of AI and where new safeguards are⏎needed.⏎HIPAA applies to AI systems deployed or developed by a HIPAA-covered⏎entity or a business associate that uses or discloses PHI. The integration of an AI⏎system into a medical practice does not change or circumvent the existing HIPAA⏎rules on permissible uses and disclosures of PHI.469 A covered entity using AI⏎tools can only access, use, or disclose PHI as permitted under HIPAA.470 If a⏎patient’s PHI is being processed through an AI system, that use must still be for⏎Treatment, Payment, or Healthcare Operations (TPO), or any of the other approved⏎uses under HIPAA not requiring authorization, or there must be a separate HIPAA⏎authorization from the patient to process the PHI for that separate use.471 Further,⏎covered entities deploying AI systems can only access and use PHI when that is⏎strictly necessary for an authorized purpose,472 and the covered entity must⏎ensure that any data treated as de-identified meets HIPAA’s Safe Harbor or Expert⏎469 Aaron T. Maguregui & Jennifer J. Hennessy, HIPAA Compliance for AI in Digital Health: What Privacy⏎Officers Need to Know, Foley & Lardner LLP (May 8, 2025),⏎https://www.foley.com/insights/publications/2025/05/hipaa-compliance-ai-digital-health-privacy-officers-⏎need-know/.⏎470 45 CFR § 164.502(a).⏎471 Id.; Todd Mayover, When AI Technology and HIPAA Collide, The HIPAA Journal (Oct. 2, 2024),⏎https://www.hipaajournal.com/when-ai-technology-and-hipaa-collide/.⏎472 45 CFR § 164.502(b).⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 132⏎Determination standards and guard against re-identification risks.473 Once PHI is⏎de-identified, however, HIPAA no longer applies to that data.474⏎The FDA’s regulatory authority covers medical devices that incorporate⏎AI.475 “Medical device” is defined in the Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act.476 Software⏎technologies, including mobile applications that satisfy the definition, would also⏎be considered medical devices.477 Medical device designation is based not just on⏎design, but also on intended use and how the product is marketed. For example,⏎AI systems claiming to do things like detect irregular heart rhythms, manage⏎chronic conditions, or identify symptoms to aid in diagnosis would likely be⏎considered medical devices. However, not all AI systems fitting the medical device⏎definition are covered as such.478 The 21st Century Cures Act of 2016 amended⏎the Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act,479 exempting clinical decision support (CDS)⏎software from regulation by the FDA—i.e., deeming it not a medical device—if it is⏎intended for the purpose of:⏎(i) displaying, analyzing, or printing medical information about a patient or⏎other medical information (such as peer-reviewed clinical studies and⏎clinical practice guidelines);⏎473 Guidance Regarding Methods for De-identification of Protected Health Information in Accordance with⏎the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) Privacy Rule, Dep’t of Health and Human⏎Services (last reviewed Feb. 3, 2025), https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/special-topics/de-⏎identification/index.html.⏎474 Id.⏎475 Artificial Intelligence in Software as a Medical Device, Food and Drug Administration (Mar. 25, 2025),⏎https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/artificial-intelligence-software-medical-⏎device#regulation.⏎476 An instrument, apparatus, implement, machine, contrivance, implant, in vitro reagent, or other similar or⏎related article, including a component part, or accessory which is:⏎(A) recognized in the official National Formulary, or the United States Pharmacopoeia, or any⏎supplement to them,⏎(B) intended for use in the diagnosis of disease or other conditions, or in the cure, mitigation,⏎treatment, or prevention of disease, in man or other animals, or⏎(C) intended to affect the structure or any function of the body of man or other animals, and which⏎does not achieve its primary intended purposes through chemical action within or on the body of⏎man or other animals and which is not dependent upon being metabolized for the achievement of⏎its primary intended purposes. The term "device" does not include software functions excluded⏎pursuant to section 520(o).⏎21 U.S.C. § 321(h).⏎477 Device Software Functions Including Mobile Medical Applications, FDA (Sept. 9, 2022),⏎https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/digital-health-center-excellence/device-software-functions-including-⏎mobile-medical-applications.⏎478 Douglas McNair & W. Nicholson Price II, Health Care Artificial Intelligence: Law, Regulation and Policy,⏎Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: The Hope, the Hype, the Promise, the Peril., (2023),⏎https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK605945/#:~:text=Medical%20Device%20Regulation,summarized%⏎20in%20Box%207%2D1⏎479 21st Century Cures Act of 2016, Pub. L. No. 114-255, 130 STAT. 1033.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 133⏎(ii) supporting or providing recommendations to a health care professional⏎about prevention, diagnosis, or treatment of a disease or condition; and⏎(iii) enabling such health care professional to independently review the basis⏎for such recommendations that such software presents so that it is not the⏎intent that such health care professional rely primarily on any of such⏎recommendations to make a clinical diagnosis or treatment decision⏎regarding an individual patient.480⏎However, the FDA can still regulate software as a medical device if it finds⏎that the system “would be reasonably likely to have serious adverse health⏎consequences” or meets the criteria for a Class III medical device.481 Clinical AI⏎systems that are deemed to be medical devices will generally require either De⏎Novo or premarket approval submissions.482 For medical devices, the FDA⏎imposes a risk-based approval process, and devices with the highest risk are⏎subject to a pre-market approval process to demonstrate a reasonable assurance⏎of safety and effectiveness.483⏎i. Shortcomings of Current Legal Landscape in Keeping Up⏎with Advancing Technology⏎AI systems training on and processing protected health information,⏎insurers using AI to deny claims, and clinical decision support systems using AI⏎highlight some of the shortcomings of our current legal landscape with respect to⏎AI and health data. This subsection discusses these problems and poses some⏎solutions.⏎1. AI Systems Training on and Processing PHI⏎AI models trained for use in health care and use of AI systems that process⏎PHI create new and heightened privacy risks. The large amount of PHI required to⏎train AI models increases the likelihood of improper disclosure of identifying⏎480 Id. § 3060(a)(o)(1).⏎481 Douglas McNair and W. Nicholson Price II, Health Care Artificial Intelligence: Law, Regulation and Policy,⏎Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: The Hope, the Hype, the Promise, the Peril., (2023),⏎https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK605945/#:~:text=Medical%20Device%20Regulation,summarized%⏎20in%20Box%207%2D1.⏎482 21 U.S.C. § 360(c).⏎483 See McNair & Nicholson Price II, supra note 481;See also David E. Vidal, Brenna Loufek, Yong-Hun Kim⏎& Nahid Y. Vidal, Navigating US Regulation of Artificial Intelligence in Medicine—A Primer for Physicians,⏎Mayo Clinic Proc. Digital Health (Feb. 22, 2023),⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11975648/#:~:text=Determination%20of%20whether%20the%20AI⏎,materials%2C%20promotion%2C%20and%20advertising.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 134⏎information and private medical information.484 When a medical provider partners⏎with an AI developer to train (or “tune”) an AI model with data from their system⏎(including PHI), the number of entities and individuals that could gain access to or⏎compromise PHI necessarily increases. For example, Google partnerships for the⏎purpose of training AI algorithms inadvertently resulted in uploading some data⏎with protected health information in ways that exposed the data to anyone with⏎basic search engine capability. While a process was implemented to remove⏎identifying information, Google’s team failed to notice x-ray images that showed⏎patients’ jewelry485 and also exposed patients’ identities by failing to delete⏎common identifiers like treatment dates and doctors’ notes.486 Although HIPAA⏎imposes standards for deidentification of PHI, research has shown that people can⏎be successfully reidentified if large datasets including semi-unique characteristics⏎are combined and compared.487 This weakness in deidentification techniques⏎heightens the cybersecurity concerns around the creation of large “de-identified”⏎datasets derived from PHI. At the same time, AI-driven healthcare solutions often⏎rely on continuous data exchange across networks, escalating the risk of⏎cyberattacks that can compromise both the integrity and availability of critical⏎healthcare services.488⏎How to Protect PHI from being Used to Train AI Systems⏎Regulations should require that AI developers and deployers proactively⏎guard against privacy risks, with heightened standards for use of AI systems in⏎healthcare settings. Many of the common methods to ensure HIPAA compliance⏎today are the same methods used to ensure compliance before the large-scale⏎deployment of AI and these methods are currently the only way to address the⏎heightened risks with using AI systems in health-related applications.489 First,⏎484 Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra,⏎note 468 at 4.⏎485 Douglas MacMillan & Greg Bensinger, Google Almost Made 100,000 Chest X-Rays Public — Until It⏎Realized Personal Data Could Be Exposed, Wash. Post (Nov. 15, 2019),⏎https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2019/11/15/google-almost-made-chest-x-rays-public-until-it-⏎realized-personal-data-could-be-exposed/.⏎486 Daisuke Wakabayashi, Google and the University of Chicago Are Sued Over Data Sharing, N.Y. Times⏎(June 26, 2019), https://www.nytimes.com/2019/06/26/technology/google-university-chicago-data-sharing-⏎lawsuit.html.⏎487 Luc Rocher, Julien M. Hendrickx, & Yves-Alexandre de Montjoye, Estimating the Success of Re-⏎Identification in Incomplete Datasets Using Generative Models, 10 Nature Communications 3069 (2019),⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41467-019-10933-3.⏎488 Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra,⏎note 468 at 4.⏎489 Kevin Henry, AI in Healthcare; What it Means for HIPAA, Accountable (Mar. 16, 2025),⏎https://www.accountablehq.com/post/ai-and-hipaa.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 135⏎using a patient’s deidentified data to train an AI system should only be permitted⏎with separate and explicit consent not included in the standard patient consent⏎forms obtained upon admission.490 Second, there must be data minimization rules⏎to ensure that the minimum necessary amount of PHI is used in any health-related⏎AI system. Third, entities must reassess the adequacy of current deidentification⏎procedures in light of reidentification risks even with HIPAA-compliant de-⏎identified datasets. Lastly, organizations need to improve data security, reduce the⏎risks of cyber threats, and maintain constant vigilance for potential weaknesses in⏎their administrative, technical, and physical safeguards. The huge datasets⏎required for training AI systems are a likely target for hacking and cyberattacks⏎and breaches of data are likely to expose larger amounts of data that may include⏎data from the entire lifespan of patients—including specific genetic⏎predispositions and specially protected populations.491 Because of the⏎reidentification concerns, robust cybersecurity measures are of the utmost⏎importance.⏎2. Insurer Use of AI to Review and Deny Claims⏎Insurers are increasingly using AI systems to screen claims for denial to cut⏎costs, even when those claims may be for medically necessary care. This⏎undermines physicians’ expertise and puts patient health at risk in the name of⏎increasing profits. Often in the insurance context, AI systems are referred to as⏎automated decisionmaking systems, which are computational systems that⏎produce a simplified output—including a score, classification, or⏎recommendation—that is used to assist or replace human discretionary⏎decisionmaking and that materially impacts one or more persons.492 For example,⏎it has been reported that insurance companies use AI systems to help decide if a⏎patient’s claim should be denied and doctors often sign off on the denials in⏎batches, spending an average of 1.2 seconds on each denial.493 Cigna and⏎UnitedHealthcare had reportedly built systems that enable these bulk denials of⏎490 Elliott Crigger, et al., Trustworthy Augmented Intelligence in Health Care, 46 J. of Medical Systems 12⏎(2022), https://doi.org/10.1007/s10916-021-01790-z.⏎491 Id.⏎492 Mayu Tobin-Miyaji, Assessing the Assessments: Maximizing the Effectiveness of Algorithmic and Privacy⏎Risk Assessments, EPIC at 6 (2025), https://epic.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Assessing-the-⏎Assessments-Report.pdf citing California Department of General Services, State Administrative Manual,⏎Definitions - 4819.2, https://www.dgs.ca.gov/Resources/SAM/TOC/4800/4819-2.⏎493 Patrick Rucker, Maya Miller & David Armstrong, How Cigna Saves Millions by Having Its Doctors Reject⏎Claims Without Reading Them, ProPublica and the Capitol Forum (Mar. 25, 2023),⏎https://www.propublica.org/article/cigna-pxdx-medical-health-insurance-rejection-claims.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 136⏎claims.494 And stories of the serious harmful effects of denials of care abound. For⏎example, in a recent case of a single mother who is raising her three-year-old son⏎with severe autism, the insurance company suddenly began denying coverage for⏎treatment, to the befuddlement of the patient’s clinical team.495 The denial letter⏎was self-contradictory, citing the son’s continued autism-related needs as reason⏎to deny care—against medical expertise and professional guidelines cited by the⏎insurance company itself.496 This denial of coverage meant that the patient’s family⏎had to pay tens of thousands of dollars out of pocket or foregoing necessary⏎treatment.497 There are numerous other examples of denials of care, such as for⏎treatment of depression, eating disorders, and drug addiction.498 Data from 2018⏎show that CVS saved upwards of $660 million by denying prior authorizations⏎requests for its Medicare Advantage beneficiaries.499 Major health insurers are⏎engaging in this system of abrupt, unfair, and medically unsupported claims denial⏎across the country.500 Insurers are not only deploying AI systems to execute these⏎cost-cutting strategies, but they also appear to be using AI as a cover for the⏎unethical practices, offloading moral responsibility and creating an illusion of⏎objectivity.501⏎494 Id.⏎495 Annie Waldman, UnitedHealth Is Strategically Limiting Access to Critical Treatment for Kids With Autism,⏎ProPublica (Dec. 13, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealthcare-insurance-autism-denials-⏎applied-behavior-analysis-medicaid.⏎496 Id.⏎497 Id.⏎498 Maya Miller & Duaa Eldeib, Her Mental Health Treatment Was Helping. That’s Why Insurance Cut Off⏎Her Coverage., ProPublica (Dec, 31, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/mental-health-insurance-⏎denials-patient-progress; Scott Pelley, Denied, 60 Minutes (Dec. 14, 2014),⏎https://www.cbsnews.com/news/mental-illness-health-care-insurance-60-minutes/; David Armstrong,⏎Patrick Rucker & Maya Miller, UnitedHealthcare Tried to Deny Coverage to a Chronically Ill Patient. He⏎Fought Back, Exposing the Insurer’s Inner Workings., ProPublica (Feb. 2, 2023),⏎https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealth-healthcare-insurance-denial-ulcerative-colitis; Annie⏎Waldman, How UnitedHealth’s Playbook for Limiting Mental Health Coverage Puts Countless Americans’⏎Treatment at Risk, ProPublica (Nov. 14, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealth-mental-⏎health-care-denied-illegal-algorithm; Jocelyn Wiener, He Wanted To Live. After His Insurance Rejected⏎Coverage, He Died of A Fentanyl Overdose, CalMatters (Oct. 28, 2024),⏎https://calmatters.org/health/mental-health/2024/10/mental-health-parity-addiction-treatment/; Duaa Eldeib⏎& Maya Miller, Insurers Continue to Rely on Doctors Whose Judgments Have Been Criticized by Courts,⏎ProPublica (Dec. 30, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/mental-health-insurance-denials-⏎unitedhealthcare-cigna-doctors; Patrick Rucker, Maya Miller & David Armstrong, How Cigna Saves Millions⏎by Having Its Doctors Reject Claims Without Reading Them, ProPublica (Mar. 25, 2023),⏎https://www.propublica.org/article/cigna-pxdx-medical-health-insurance-rejection-claims.⏎499 Maggie L. Shaw, Insurers’ AI Denials of Postacute Care Face Senate Scrutiny, AJMC (Oct. 28, 2024),⏎https://www.ajmc.com/view/insurers-ai-denials-of-postacute-care-face-senate-scrutiny.⏎500 See sources cited supra note 498.⏎501 Eric Bogert, Aaron Schecter & Richard T. Watson, Humans Rely More on Algorithms Than Social⏎Influence As A Task Becomes More Difficult, Scientific Reports 11, 8028 (Apr. 13,2021).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-021-87480-9.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 137⏎Health insurance companies harm patients by denying claims en masse⏎through AI systems, undermining the expertise of the patient’s physician. Their⏎strategy to cut costs includes identifying “cost outliers,” which can include⏎providers that bill for costly treatments, or individuals who receive high-cost⏎treatments.502 This cost outlier identification strategy was not feasible at scale with⏎human review, but now AI systems can quickly and efficiently flag high-cost⏎providers and patients. For example, UnitedHealth designated provider factors⏎such as billing on weekends or holidays, serving multiple family members, or⏎having long clinician days as cost outliers, even though these factors are typical in⏎the delivery of therapy for children with autism.503 After a provider or a patient is⏎flagged, theoretically a medically trained employee of the insurer would review⏎the claim. In reality, the reviewer will typically not discuss the prescribed treatment⏎with the prescribing doctor, nor see the patient directly, and may not be⏎specialized in that particular area of medicine. Instead of making determinations⏎based on individual patient needs, insurers rejected claims instantly or with human⏎reviewers as rubber stamps.504 Some reviewers from Anthem had denial rates of⏎95 and 100%.505 Worse, in one case where a patient challenged the denial of care,⏎the reviewer whose name was on the denial letter never reviewed the claim at⏎all—the denial was fully automated by an AI system.506 Often, these denials of care⏎are unsupported by medical evidence. An investigation of appeals for denials of⏎care in California showed that the Department of Managed Health Care⏎overturned health plans’ determinations 76% of the time, but very few patients⏎were in a position to appeal.507 These AI systems allow insurers to target high-cost⏎medical care and employees to rubber-stamp denials of care, betting on the fact⏎that few will appeal.508⏎502Annie Waldman, How UnitedHealth’s Playbook for Limiting Mental Health Coverage Puts Countless⏎Americans’ Treatment at Risk, ProPublica (Nov. 14, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealth-⏎mental-health-care-denied-illegal-algorithm; David Armstrong, Patrick Rucker & Maya Miller,⏎UnitedHealthcare Tried to Deny Coverage to a Chronically Ill Patient. He Fought Back, Exposing the⏎Insurer’s Inner Workings., ProPublica (Feb. 2, 2023), https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealth-⏎healthcare-insurance-denial-ulcerative-colitis.⏎503 Annie Waldman, UnitedHealth Is Strategically Limiting Access to Critical Treatment for Kids With Autism,⏎ProPublica (Dec. 13, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealthcare-insurance-autism-denials-⏎applied-behavior-analysis-medicaid.⏎504 Rucker, Miller & Armstrong, supra note 499; See also Bogert, Schecter & Watson, supra note 501.⏎505 Rucker, Miller & Armstrong, supra note 499.⏎506 Scott Pelley, Denied, 60 Minutes (Dec. 14, 2014), https://www.cbsnews.com/news/mental-illness-health-⏎care-insurance-60-minutes/.⏎507 Wiener, supra note 498.⏎508 Jennifer Lubell, How AI Is Leading to More Prior Authorization Denials, Am. Med. Ass’n. (Mar. 10, 2025),⏎https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/prior-authorization/how-ai-leading-more-prior-⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 138⏎The use of AI systems by insurers to screen claims does not support⏎patients or improve health outcomes; it only benefits the company’s bottom line⏎while leaving disastrous consequences for patients and medical providers.⏎Patients have relapsed into alcohol or drug use,509 attempted suicide,510 engaged⏎in self-harm,511 become violent, or died after prematurely leaving mental health⏎facilities due to denials of coverage.512 Without insurance coverage, patients are⏎often forced to choose between receiving necessary medical care at tens of⏎thousands of dollars out of pocket or risking their health and potentially their life.513⏎Even if the patient’s life is not at risk, untreated medical and mental health issues⏎can degrade quality of life for the patient and their family and threaten stable⏎employment, education, or housing.514 The frustration of denials may also lead⏎patients to disengage with the healthcare system and avoid or delay care. This⏎opaque and arbitrary system also imposes additional burdens on medical⏎providers who face unexpected denials of claims or aggressive questioning of⏎their decisions, threatening the provider’s business in some cases.515 If providers⏎were forced to shut down or refuse to take insurance as a result, patients would⏎ultimately be deprived of needed care. The use of AI systems by insurers⏎undermines patient safety, autonomy, and health equity.⏎authorization-denials; David Armstrong, Patrick Rucker & Maya Miller, UnitedHealthcare Tried to Deny⏎Coverage to a Chronically Ill Patient. He Fought Back, Exposing the Insurer’s Inner Workings., ProPublica⏎(Feb. 2, 2023), https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealth-healthcare-insurance-denial-ulcerative-⏎colitis (“The list saved money in two ways. It allowed Cigna to begin turning down claims that it had once⏎paid. And it made it cheaper to turn down claims, because the company’s doctors never had to open a file⏎or conduct any in-depth review. They simply denied the claims in bulk with an electronic signature.”).⏎509 Jocelyn Wiener, He Wanted to Live. After His Insurance Rejected Coverage, He Died Of A Fentanyl⏎Overdose, CalMatters (Oct. 28, 2024), https://calmatters.org/health/mental-health/2024/10/mental-health-⏎parity-addiction-treatment/.⏎510 Maya Miller & Duaa Eldeib, Her Mental Health Treatment Was Helping. That’s Why Insurance Cut Off⏎Her Coverage., ProPublica (Dec, 31, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/mental-health-insurance-⏎denials-patient-progress.⏎511 Id.⏎512 Pelley, supra note 512.⏎513 Id.; David Armstrong, Patrick Rucker & Maya Miller, UnitedHealthcare Tried to Deny Coverage to a⏎Chronically Ill Patient. He Fought Back, Exposing the Insurer’s Inner Workings., ProPublica (Feb. 2, 2023),⏎https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealth-healthcare-insurance-denial-ulcerative-colitis.⏎514 Wiener, supra note 498.⏎515 Annie Waldman, UnitedHealth Is Strategically Limiting Access to Critical Treatment for Kids With Autism,⏎ProPublica (Dec. 13, 2024), https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealthcare-insurance-autism-denials-⏎applied-behavior-analysis-medicaid.; Annie Waldman, How UnitedHealth’s Playbook for Limiting Mental⏎Health Coverage Puts Countless Americans’ Treatment at Risk, ProPublica (Nov. 14, 2024),⏎https://www.propublica.org/article/unitedhealth-mental-health-care-denied-illegal-algorithm; 2024 AMA⏎Prior Authorization Physician Survey, Am. Med. Ass'n. (2025), https://www.ama-assn.org/system/files/prior-⏎authorization-survey.pdf.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 139⏎How to Prevent Insurers’ Use of AI to Review and Deny Claims⏎There are currently few statutory limitations on the use of AI systems by⏎insurers. Lawsuits, including class actions, have been brought against insurers⏎with mixed results.516 Policymakers need to step in to regulate the use of AI⏎systems by insurers and ensure that these decisions are made fairly on the basis⏎of medical expertise and the patient’s individual medical history and situation.⏎Policymakers should also prohibit insurers from engaging in automatic denials or⏎human rubber-stamping of denials. These AI systems should also be tested, fit for⏎purpose, and subject to risk assessments that are submitted to regulators and⏎published to allow for independent review pre-deployment. There should also be⏎ongoing requirements for routine first-party and independent audits of system⏎performance and outcomes, especially in systems impacting health coverage and⏎outcomes such as denials of claims and denials of appeals.517 Individual patients⏎should also receive notices of any use of AI systems in the course of care and an⏎explanation of the basis of any final decision coupled with a clear appeals⏎process. These requirements should be overseen by an independent regulator⏎and subject to direct enforcement through a private right of action by individuals⏎when their rights are violated.⏎States are beginning to look more closely at this issue. California’s SB 1120,⏎enacted in 2024, regulates how healthcare plans and disability insurers may and⏎may not use automated decisionmaking tools to analyze medical necessity in⏎review of medical claims for California enrollees.518 This includes utilization review,⏎which “is the process used by employers or claims administrators to review⏎treatment to determine if it is medically necessary.”519 The law prohibits the use of⏎AI tools to “deny, delay or modify health care services based, in whole or in part,⏎on medical necessity” or to supplant a healthcare provider’s decision-making.520⏎The law also requires insurers to base coverage decisions on the patient’s⏎medical history and circumstances, and not solely based on group dataset.521⏎516 Lauren Clason, AI, Algorithm-Based Health Insurer Denials Pose New Legal Threat, Bloomberg Law⏎(Apr. 8, 2025), https://news.bloomberglaw.com/daily-labor-report/ai-algorithm-based-health-insurer-denials-⏎pose-new-legal-threat.⏎517 Mayu Tobin-Miyaji, Assessing the Assessments, supra note 492 at 24-43.⏎518 S.B. 1120, Cal. Stat. 879; see also Cal. Dep’t. of Insurance, Guidance SB 1120:1 Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence, Algorithms and Other Software Tools in Utilization Management (May 5, 2025),⏎https://www.insurance.ca.gov/0250-insurers/0500-legal-info/0200-regulations/HealthGuidance/upload/SB-⏎1120-1-Guidance-Use-of-Artificial-Intelligence-Algorithms-and-Other-Software-Tools-in-Utilization-⏎Management.pdf.⏎519 Utilization Review, California Dep’t of Industrial Relations, https://www.dir.ca.gov/dwc/ur_main.htm.⏎520 S.B. 1120 § (j)(2).⏎521 S.B. 1120 § 1367.01(k)(1)(A)-(B).⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 140⏎Under the law, the AI tools are open for inspection and audit by the California⏎Department of Health and Human Services, increasing oversight.522 Other states⏎should follow this lead.⏎3. Clinical Decision Support Systems Using AI⏎Over the past decade, development and deployment of AI systems in the⏎healthcare context have radically expanded. Proponents tout AI-based clinical⏎decision support systems (CDS) as having the potential to optimize clinical⏎workflows, improve patient safety, aid in diagnosis, and enable personalized⏎treatment.523 At the same time, many reports illustrate gaps in oversight of AI⏎systems deployed in medical settings that led to high rates of inaccuracy that⏎threaten patient health; biased outputs that lead to discriminatory treatment; and⏎deployment contexts that undermine healthcare provider expertise and waste⏎valuable resources.524 For example, Epic Health Systems marketed an algorithm⏎that it claimed predicted patients experiencing sepsis at 76-83% accuracy, but a⏎later study of 27,000 patients found that the system was closer to 63% accuracy⏎and produced many false positives while failing to identify risk in 67% of the⏎patients that actually experienced sepsis.525 In another example, a 2020 study⏎found that an algorithm used in determining eligibility and prioritization for kidney⏎transplants unfairly prevented Black patients from receiving transplants.526 Racial⏎bias has also been identified in models used in assessing whether a vaginal birth⏎is safe for patients,527 making diagnoses through chest X- rays,528 and determining⏎the level of patient need during triage.529⏎522 Id. § 10123.135 (j)(5).⏎523 Ciro Mennella, et al., Ethical And Regulatory Challenges Of AI Technologies In Healthcare: A Narrative⏎Review, 10 Heliyon 4 (Feb. 15, 2024), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10879008/#br0020.⏎524 Moustafa Abdelwanis, et al., Exploring The Risks Of Automation Bias In Healthcare Artificial Intelligence⏎Applications: A Bowtie Analysis, 5:4 Journal of Safety Science and Resilience 460 (Dec. 2024),⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S2666449624000410#b5.⏎525 See Tom Simonite, An Algorithm That Predicts Deadly Infections Is Often Flawed, Wired (June 21, 2021),⏎https://www.wired.com/story/algorithm-predicts-deadly-infections-often-flawed/.⏎526 See Tom Simonite, How an Algorithm Blocked Kidney Transplants to Black Patients, Wired (Oct. 26,⏎2020), https://www.wired.com/story/how-algorithm-blocked-kidney-transplants-black-patients/.⏎527 Darshali A. Vyas, et al., Challenging the Use of Race in the Vaginal Birth After Cesarean Section⏎Calculator, 2019 May-June Women’s Health Issues 29(3):201 (May-June 2019),⏎https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/31072754/.⏎528 Laleh Seyyed-Kalantari, et al., Underdiagnosis Bias of Artificial Intelligence Algorithms Applied to Chest⏎Radiographs in Under-Served Patient Populations, 27 Nature Medicine 2176 (Dec. 10, 2021),⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-021-01595-0; Haoran Zhang, Thomas Hartvigsen & Marzyeh⏎Ghassemi, Algorithmic Fairness in Chest X-Ray Diagnosis: A Case Study, MIT Case Studies in Social and⏎Ethical Responsibilities of Computing, Winter 2023 (Feb. 27, 2023), https://mit-⏎serc.pubpub.org/pub/algorithmic-chest/release/2.⏎529 Ziad Obermeyer, et al., Dissecting Racial Bias in Algorithm Used to Manage the Health of Populations,⏎366:6464 Science 447 (Oct. 25, 2019), https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 141⏎A survey by National Nurses United (NNU), the largest nurses’ union in the⏎U.S., illustrates how AI use in health care can undermine patient safety. NNU⏎surveyed over 2,300 registered nurses between January and March 2024 and⏎found that 60% of the surveyed nurses did not trust their employers to prioritize⏎patient safety when implementing new AI systems.530 Half of respondents said⏎that their employers used an AI system analyzing electronic health record (EHR)⏎data to evaluate patient acuity and need for nursing care.531 69% of those nurse⏎respondents said that their own assessments differ from the AI-generated acuity⏎measurements, which do not take into account many of the educational, psycho-⏎social, or emotional needs of a patient or their families.532 An NNU leader⏎explained: “The result of relying on the algorithmically-driven acuity⏎measurements is that, on a daily basis, in unit after unit, we have multiple patients⏎whose acuity is underrepresented, which means there are not enough nurses to⏎provide optimal care in a timely manner.”533⏎Around 12% of nurses also reported that documentation and notes for⏎handoffs534 between nurses’ shifts are generated by AI and, disturbingly, 48% of⏎those nurses said that the automated reports do not accurately reflect their⏎assessments.535 The AI-generated reports missed crucial information about⏎patients that would not be missed during nurse-to-nurse handoffs, such as a⏎patient having COVID-19 or being immunocompromised.536 In addition to issues⏎with inaccuracy, facilities that use a scoring system to predict a patient’s outcome,⏎risk for a complication, or to determine if patients are on schedule for discharge⏎had 40% of responding nurses say that they are unable to modify scores to reflect⏎their clinical judgment and the individualized needs of the patient.537 Surveyed⏎nurses feel that technology and AI are being used to justify understaffing without⏎proper safeguards to ensure patient safety.538 These technologies undermine⏎530 Nurses Are Pushing Back on AI In Healthcare. Here's Why., Advisory Board (May 21, 2024),⏎https://www.advisory.com/daily-briefing/2024/05/21/nurse-ai.⏎531 National Nurses United Survey Finds A.I. Technology Degrades And Undermines Patient Safety, Nat’l⏎Nurses United (May 15, 2024), https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/press/national-nurses-united-survey-⏎finds-ai-technology-undermines-patient-safety.⏎532 Id.⏎533 Id.⏎534 A “handoff” is the critical point where the responsibility for the care of the patient and the transfer of⏎essential information is transferred from one health care provider to another. Mary Ann Friesen, Susan⏎White & Jacqueline Byers, Handoffs: Implications for Nurses, Patient Safety and Quality: An Evidence-⏎Based Handbook for Nurses (2008), https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK2649/.⏎535 National Nurses United Survey Finds A.I. Technology Degrades And Undermines Patient Safety, Nat’l⏎Nurses United, supra note 531.⏎536 Id.⏎537 Id.⏎538 Id.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 142⏎nurses’ expertise, increase burdens on nurses to check and correct AI outputs and⏎mitigate false alarms, and devalue the core work of nurses—to show compassion,⏎to provide comfort, and to build trust with patients while assessing the patient and⏎providing care.539 Deploying untested and unregulated AI into care settings⏎threatens patients’ rights to person-to-person care as well as their rights to⏎privacy, transparency, and safety.540⏎There is currently no clear regulatory framework for the integration of AI⏎into CDS systems. Many of the AI systems used in healthcare settings are being⏎deployed without any required evidence of efficacy and safety.541 CDS systems, as⏎defined under the 21st Century Cures Act, need not go through FDA approval and⏎are largely unregulated.542 Even when CDS devices with AI were approved by the⏎FDA, there are no robust requirements of peer-reviewed research, published data,⏎or risk assessments. A study conducted in 2022 reviewed ten medical devices⏎using AI or machine learning approved by the FDA that would inform care for⏎patients with critical illnesses. The study found that, of those ten, only three⏎included citations of published data, four mentioned a safety assessment, and⏎none mentioned an evaluation of performance bias.543 Some systems relied on⏎showing equivalence to a previously approved system, even though the previous⏎system did not use AI or machine learning.544 No company provided software⏎code to enable independent validation, evaluated clinical efficacy, or assessed⏎whether the use of algorithms exacerbates health disparities.545⏎One example of the disparity in the application and development of these⏎systems can be seen in comparing sepsis detection systems from Epic Health⏎Systems and Prenosis. Epic’s AI model did not go through the FDA approval⏎process to be brought to market. In other words, hospitals wondering about the⏎efficacy of Epic’s systems could rely only on Epic’s own representations, with no⏎539 Id.⏎540 Id.; Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra⏎note 468 at 3.⏎541 Emma Beavins, National Nurses United Pushes Back Against Deployment Of 'Unproven' AI In⏎Healthcare, Fierce Healthcare (June 3, 2024), https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/ai-and-machine-⏎learning/national-nurses-united-pushes-back-against-deployment-ai-healthcare.⏎542 Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra⏎note 468 at 5.⏎543 Jessica T. Lee, Alexander T. Moffett & George Maliha, Analysis of Devices Authorized by the FDA for⏎Clinical Decision Support in Critical Care, 183:12 JAMA Internal Medicine 1399 (2023),⏎https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamainternalmedicine/fullarticle/2810619.⏎544 Id.⏎545 Id.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 143⏎independent scientific research to support its claims.546 After many hospitals had⏎deployed the system, an independent study in 2021 showed a much lower⏎efficacy rate than Epic claimed.547 In contrast, after the FDA updated its relevant⏎guidance in 2022 and increased regulatory oversight of software that “analyzes⏎patient-specific medical information to detect a life-threatening condition, such as⏎stroke or sepsis,” Prenosis worked for over a year to demonstrate the safety and⏎efficacy of its own sepsis detection model to the FDA before bringing it to⏎market.548 A published peer-reviewed study in a medical journal also supports the⏎high efficacy rate of Prenosis’s sepsis detection system.549 Hopefully, this added⏎process to ensure efficacy and safety leads to better outcomes for the Prenosis⏎sepsis detection system.⏎How to Better Regulate Clinical Decision Support Systems Using AI⏎While using the existing FDA approval process is better than no process,⏎there must be more robust standards for the FDA to evaluate medical devices with⏎AI and machine learning. The FDA authorization process should allow medical⏎providers, patients, and researchers access to useful information about clinical⏎effectiveness, safety, and performance biases of the CDS system. Currently, that is⏎lacking. Worse yet, the FDA is rolling back the already few standards for regulating⏎CDS software, including AI. The FDA recently announced that it will deregulate⏎CDS software by allowing products to enter the market without FDA approval for⏎devices that do not deliver only a single recommendation, as products that⏎delivered a single recommendation were previously considered regulated medical⏎devices.550 There are four main areas for improvement: (1) expanding the coverage⏎of the medical device definition; (2) requiring pre-deployment risk assessments by⏎the AI developer with transparency requirements; (3) rigorous preapproval studies⏎of validity, safety, and efficacy, coupled with ongoing audit of clinical utility post-⏎deployment, with a focus on risks of exacerbating social or racial biases; and (4)⏎546 Simonite, An Algorithm That Predicts Deadly Infections Is Often Flawed, supra note 525.⏎547 Id.⏎548 Ashley Capoot, FDA Authorizes Prenosis Software As First AI Tool That Can Diagnose Sepsis, CNBC⏎(Apr. 3, 2024), https://www.cnbc.com/2024/04/03/prenosis-says-ai-tool-for-sepsis-approved-by-fda.html.⏎549 Akhil Bhargava et al., FDA-Authorized AI/ML Tool for Sepsis Prediction: Development and Validation,⏎1:12 NEJM AI (Nov. 27, 2024), https://ai.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/AIoa2400867.⏎550 Lizzy Lawrence, Mario Aguilar, Katie Palmer & Brittany Trang, FDA Announces Sweeping Changes to⏎Oversight of Wearables, AI-enabled Devices, STAT (Jan. 6, 2026),⏎https://www.statnews.com/2026/01/06/fda-pulls-back-oversight-ai-enabled-devices-wearables/.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 144⏎reassessing the 510(k) approval pathway, which allows companies to gain FDA⏎approval through showing equivalence to already approved devices.551⏎First, the current coverage of “medical device,” along with the exclusions⏎from the 21st Century Cures Act, creates a vacuum of oversight over AI systems⏎used in health care. Lawmakers should consider amending the definition in the⏎Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act to remove the exemption for CDS,552 and the FDA⏎should use its statutory authority to interpret “medical devices” as broadly as⏎possible to cover more of the AI systems used in health care.553 AI systems to be⏎used in health care that can impact patient health and safety should go through⏎robust pre-deployment assessments to ensure efficacy, safety, oversight, and⏎proper training, as explained below.⏎Second, as EPIC has previously advocated,554 an AI system that will be used⏎in consequential ways should go through a risk assessment before deployment to⏎detect patterns of biased or inaccurate outputs, to identify threats to privacy and⏎cybersecurity, and to determine the level of human involvement and training⏎necessary to ensure safe operation of the system. Developers and providers of AI⏎tools used in consequential settings must also be transparent about how those⏎tools are developed, tested, and monitored after deployment, including by⏎embedding ways to collect adverse incident information and carrying out⏎recurring independent audits.555 One important consideration is how well data⏎used to train an AI system matches the population of patients that will be⏎impacted by that system. In one concerning example, IBM’s Watson for Oncology⏎was found to produce inaccurate treatment suggestions, including treatments that⏎were not available in that locality, because the training data included hypothetical⏎scenarios and data that was not representative of the patients that would be⏎551 See Anand R. Habib & Cary P. Gross, FDA Regulations of AI-Driven Clinical Decision Support Devices⏎Fall Short, 183:12 JAMA Internal Medicine 1401 (Oct. 9, 2023),⏎https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamainternalmedicine/article-abstract/2810620.⏎552 See supra notes 475, 478–479, and accompanying text.⏎553 Sara Gerke, Health AI for Good Rather Than Evil? The Need for a New Regulatory Framework for AI-⏎Based Medical Devices, Yale J. of Health Policy, Law, and Ethics 20:2 433 (Apr. 29, 2022),⏎https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=4070947.⏎554 Mayu Tobin-Miyaji, Assessing the Assessments, supra note 492 at 24-43.⏎555 Id.; See also Sara Gerke, Timo Minssen & Glenn Cohen, Ethical And Legal Challenges Of Artificial⏎Intelligence-Driven Healthcare, Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare 295 (2020),⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/B9780128184387000125?via%3Dihub#bib56 (discussing⏎how IBM kept the information about Watson for Oncology’s unsafe and incorrect treatment⏎recommendations discovered during pre-deployment testing for a year); Augmented Intelligence⏎Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra note 468.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 145⏎treated with the system.556 There must be transparency requirements to enable⏎medical providers to assess the fitness of the system to their patient population⏎prior to deployment.557⏎Third, the FDA should develop and impose more robust standards for⏎testing the validity, safety, and efficacy of these systems. A recent meta-study of⏎research evaluating the integration of AI into CDS found a lack of high-quality⏎evidence to support their efficacy findings.558 When the FDA is assessing devices,⏎it should demand rigorous evidence and require an assessment of whether there⏎are risks of exacerbating socioeconomic biases—including gender bias and racial⏎bias—that may lead to discriminatory treatment in medical care.559 These studies⏎should be made public and the models should be continuously assessed after⏎deployment in all locations and clinical contexts in which the CDS is deployed.⏎Even if a CDS works well with respect to one patient population, it may not in⏎others and there must be a pathway to report adverse incidents that the FDA⏎reviews.⏎Lastly, the FDA should reassess the 510(k) approval pathway, which allows⏎companies to gain FDA approval through showing equivalence to already⏎approved devices.560 Currently, companies are gaining approval of CDS that use⏎AI by showing equivalence to devices that do not use AI, even though eligible⏎devices must use the same technological characteristics as their predicates.561⏎While externally the function might seem “equivalent,” the use of AI can introduce⏎new risks. For example, a new CDS device with AI that was trained on a⏎demographically homogenous patient population data can produce erroneous or⏎discriminatory predictions when applied to diverse patient populations.562 Some⏎AI-based CDS approved through the equivalence process are used to inform care⏎556 Lizzie O’Leary, How IBM’s Watson Went From the Future of Health Care to Sold Off for Parts, Slate (Jan.⏎31, 2022), https://slate.com/technology/2022/01/ibm-watson-health-failure-artificial-intelligence.html.⏎557 Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra⏎note 468 at 10.⏎558 Baptiste Vasey, et al., Association of Clinician Diagnostic Performance With Machine Learning–Based⏎Decision Support Systems, 4:3 JAMA Network Open e211276 (2021),⏎https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2777403#247645219.⏎559 See Anand R. Habib & Cary P. Gross, FDA Regulations of AI-Driven Clinical Decision Support Devices⏎Fall Short, 183:12 JAMA Internal Medicine 1401 (2023),⏎https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamainternalmedicine/article-abstract/2810620; see also Cassandra⏎LaRose & Elizabeth Edwards, 1557 Final Rule Protects Against Bias in Health Care Algorithms, Health Law⏎(May 1, 2024), https://healthlaw.org/1557-final-rule-protects-against-bias-in-health-care-algorithms/;⏎Nondiscrimination in Health Programs and Activities, 89 Fed. Reg. 37522 (May 6, 2024).⏎560 Habib & Gross, supra note 559.⏎561 Id.⏎562 Id.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 146⏎for patients with critical illness, risking perpetuating health inequity with little⏎chance of discovery before such harm is discovered.563 Take, for example, a⏎hospital whose patients are mostly people of color which uses a system that takes⏎in various data from electronic health records and clinical records to identify⏎patients at risk of deterioration. Suddenly, a new underlying system built on AI and⏎trained on data that poorly represents patients of color is deployed following the⏎510(k) approval pathway. The lack of thorough testing for the new system raises⏎the possibility that patients of color at risk of deterioration will be inaccurately⏎overlooked, leading to disparate impact in their treatment and worse health⏎outcomes, or misidentified as higher risk, wasting hospital resources. Worse, the⏎harms of inequitable treatment might not be identified until sufficient data is⏎gathered on the system’s performance for the hospital to assess. The FDA should⏎consider the integration of AI into a system as inherently not equivalent to a⏎previously-approved system and develop a standard to assess the system anew.⏎C. AI Systems that Fall Outside of HIPAA and FDA Oversight⏎Certain technologies that can cause significant harm do not fall within the⏎scope of HIPAA or FDA regulations and, thus, fall outside of existing oversight⏎mechanisms. This is especially true of generative AI chatbot systems ChatGPT,⏎Gemini, Llama, Replika, and Character.AI, since these systems are not deployed by⏎HIPAA-covered entities to provide health care. Many commercial surveillance⏎practices like data analytics, profiling, and the delivery of digital ads use AI⏎systems. These systems often use our health data but exist entirely outside of the⏎health context. AI systems turbocharge all of the profiling harms mentioned in Part⏎2. All of the data that is collected in commercial surveillance—website visits,⏎search histories, interactions with content on social media, wearables, health⏎tracking apps, location data from various sources, etc.—can be fed into AI systems⏎and put sensitive health information at risk.564 For example, anti-abortion groups⏎have used device location data to infer that individuals at or near clinics providing⏎abortion care may be seeking abortions and targeted those individuals with⏎misleading ads for anti-abortion “crisis pregnancy centers.”565 Because location⏎563 Id.⏎564 Geoghegan & Winters, supra note 352.; Bonnie Eslinger, Meta Grabs Menstrual App Users' Data For⏎Ads, Jury Told, Law360 (July 23, 2025), https://www.law360.com/cybersecurity-privacy/articles/2368550.⏎565 Justin Sherman, The Data Broker Caught Running Anti-Abortion Ads—To People Sitting in Clinics,⏎Lawfare (Sept. 19, 2022), https://www.lawfaremedia.org/article/data-broker-caught-running-anti-abortion-⏎ads%E2%80%94-people-sitting-clinics.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 147⏎data is not PHI collected for providing health care, and the AI system is not used in⏎a healthcare context by a covered entity, any inferences that follow also fall out of⏎HIPAA protections. The AI models trained on that data and the AI models’ outputs⏎are not covered by HIPAA, even if the data implicates an individual’s sensitive⏎health information. Generative AI models may also be trained on such data.566⏎There is no generally-applicable federal law regulating AI or private-sector⏎privacy practices in the United States, which has enabled tech companies to⏎deploy systems that exploit personal information of users and produce AI systems⏎without ensuring that they are safe, accurate, or fair.567 Systems that do not fall⏎within the ambit of sector-specific laws like HIPAA or the FDA’s regulation of⏎medical devices will fall into the general category of AI systems that lack sufficient⏎transparency, accountability, and oversight. Although there is a background set of⏎generally applicable rules that apply to AI systems—including antidiscrimination⏎laws, product safety laws, and consumer protection laws—regulators and⏎advocates have struggled to hold AI companies accountable with this limited and⏎often outdated toolkit.⏎One reason many in the general public are confused about the extent of⏎HIPAA protections is because companies often misrepresent that they are “HIPAA-⏎compliant” when they are not a covered entity or a business associate under⏎HIPAA.568 For example, the FTC brought an enforcement action against GoodRx⏎for deceptive practices because it misrepresented on its website that it was HIPAA⏎compliant and shared users’ personal health information, including their health⏎conditions and medications, with advertisers without users’ consent.569 The FTC⏎brought an enforcement action against BetterHelp for similar issues.570 Such⏎misrepresentations and misleading statements give false comfort to individuals⏎and may manipulate them into giving away sensitive personal health information,⏎thinking that HIPAA protections apply. While the FTC and states can bring⏎enforcement actions for unfair and deceptive practices, many companies take⏎566 Geoghegan & Winters, supra note 352.⏎567 Mayu Tobin-Miyaji, Assessing the Assessments, supra note 492 at 1-14.⏎568 Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra⏎note 468 at 13.⏎569 Press Release, FTC, FTC Enforcement Action to Bar GoodRx from Sharing Consumers’ Sensitive Health⏎Info for Advertising (Feb. 1, 2023), https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2023/02/ftc-⏎enforcement-action-bar-goodrx-sharing-consumers-sensitive-health-info-advertising.⏎570 Press Release, FTC, FTC Gives Final Approval to Order Banning BetterHelp from Sharing Sensitive⏎Health Data for Advertising, Requiring It to Pay $7.8 Million (July 14, 2023), https://www.ftc.gov/news-⏎events/news/press-releases/2023/07/ftc-gives-final-approval-order-banning-betterhelp-sharing-sensitive-⏎health-data-advertising.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 148⏎advantage of consumers’ murky understanding of the reach of HIPAA protections.⏎This will be an ongoing issue with AI chatbots that claim to be HIPAA complaint.⏎Insights about people’s health are valuable to insurance companies, data⏎brokers, AI developers, tech companies, and advertisers, and AI systems facilitate⏎the collection and use of health information that falls outside of the scope of⏎HIPAA protections. Companies deploy AI systems to profile individuals based on⏎vast amounts of personal information collected about individuals, including health⏎information, and make inferences about an individual’s health.571 This information⏎can include going to abortion clinics, searching online for information about trans⏎health care, or information collected by mobile apps and wearable devices about⏎an individual’s health. Often, companies collect and disclose such personal data⏎without knowledge or meaningful consent by the data subjects. Data brokers⏎aggregate and further sell the data and insights, allowing businesses to target⏎advertising, set different prices for products and services, or present different⏎economic opportunities for individuals.572 These practices enable discrimination,⏎harassment, and the exploitation of vulnerabilities based on a person’s private⏎medical circumstances, and they pose a grave threat to individual autonomy,⏎public safety and health, and civil rights.573⏎The lack of regulation and rapid advancement of generative AI models that⏎can produce human-sounding or realistic audio, image, and video outputs puts⏎everyday people in danger. Many people are turning to GAI to gain information⏎about medical care or for mental health support, especially in light of barriers to⏎accessing professional mental health treatment.574 However, the increased use of⏎GAI for these purposes comes with serious risks: a chatbot falsely claiming to be a⏎licensed therapist, chatbots producing medically incorrect outputs that would⏎mislead users or worsen the user’s mental health issues, the loss of privacy as⏎users enter intimate information into the chatbot believing it to be confidential,⏎and overdependence on chatbots that discourage healthy social interaction.⏎571 Mayu Tobin-Miyaji, Assessing the Assessments, supra note 492 at 6-9.⏎572 Geoghegan & Winters, supra note 352.⏎573 Id.⏎574 Munmun De Choudhury, Sachin R. Pendse & Neha Kumar, Benefits and Harms of Large Language⏎Models in Digital Mental Health, arXiv (Nov. 7, 2023), https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.14693.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 149⏎Generative AI is known to frequently “hallucinate,” i.e., to produce untrue⏎but plausible-sounding outputs, which can mislead users. 575 For example, Meta’s⏎GAI platform allows users to create new chatbots and have other users interact⏎with the chatbots. Several of these chatbots have produced outputs stating they⏎are licensed therapists with fabricated license numbers and claimed that anything⏎shared with them would be confidential.576 These outputs manipulate users into⏎trusting the chatbot, even though the chatbot is just software used to generate⏎text in response to a prompt, an AI system cannot act as a licensed therapist and⏎cannot address users’ mental health issues in accordance with licensed⏎therapeutic standards.⏎Research has shown that these generative AI chatbot systems produce⏎inaccurate information, including medical diagnoses and recommendations, while⏎also presenting them in a way that sounds confident and convincing to the user;⏎the systems also frequently produce sycophantic encouragement that increases⏎the false sense of security and puts vulnerable users at risk.577 A study by a⏎computer science researcher at Stanford showed that, unlike human therapists,⏎ChatGPT produced inappropriate outputs in crisis situations; the chatbots were⏎not capable of producing outputs that push back against delusional thinking and⏎the systems frequently produce responses that express stigma towards those with⏎mental health conditions.578 A report on another AI Chatbot, Replika, found that⏎users complained of Replika producing outputs that encouraged suicide,⏎conveyed interest at a user’s expression of suicidal thoughts, or included⏎575 Ziwei Xu, Sanjay Jain, & Mohan Kankanhalli, Hallucination is Inevitable: An Innate Limitation of Large⏎Language Models, arXiv (Feb. 13, 2025), https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.11817.⏎576 Samantha Cole, Instagram's AI Chatbots Lie About Being Licensed Therapists, 404 Media (Apr. 29,⏎2025), https://www.404media.co/instagram-ai-studio-therapy-chatbots-lie-about-being-licensed-therapists/;⏎Character.AI also has a “CBT Therapist” bot and Chai, a Palo Alto-based AI company, has a therapy bot that⏎claims it is qualified to provide CBT therapy. Ella Chakarian, Fake Credentials, Stolen Licenses: Virtual⏎Therapists Are Lying Like Crazy To Patients, S.F. Standard (May 11, 2025),⏎https://sfstandard.com/2025/05/11/ai-chatbots-fake-therapists/.⏎577 Kashmir Hill, They Asked an A.I. Chatbot Questions. The Answers Sent Them Spiraling., N.Y. Times (June⏎13, 2025), https://www.nytimes.com/2025/06/13/technology/chatgpt-ai-chatbots-conspiracies.html; Dan⏎Milmo, ‘It cannot provide nuance’: UK Experts Warn AI Therapy Chatbots Are Not Safe, Guardian (May 7,⏎2025), https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2025/may/07/experts-warn-therapy-ai-chatbots-are-not-⏎safe-to-use.⏎578 Jared Moore, et al., Expressing Stigma And Inappropriate Responses Prevents LLMs From Safely⏎Replacing Mental Health Providers, arXiv (Apr. 25, 2025), https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.18412; see also Eileen⏎Guo, An AI Chatbot Told A User How To Kill Himself—But The Company Doesn’t Want To “Censor” It, MIT⏎Tech. Rev. (Feb. 6, 2025), https://www.technologyreview.com/2025/02/06/1111077/nomi-ai-chatbot-told-⏎user-to-kill-himself/.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 150⏎aggressively sexual messages that made users feel sexually harassed.579 A man⏎died from suicide after chatting with an AI bot called Chai for weeks; during that⏎time the system produced numerous suggestions of different methods to end⏎one’s life with little prompting and produced responses that encouraged the man⏎to kill himself.580 The National Eating Disorders Association had to pause its⏎chatbot because the system was outputting medically unsupported and harmful⏎advice to users who discussed eating disorders in their prompts.581 A study⏎showed that when a user asks ChatGPT about self-managed medication abortion,⏎the system produced outputs that inaccurately described the medication as⏎dangerous and associated with an increase in the risk of complications.582 This⏎type of misinformation can exacerbate stigma and mislead individuals seeking⏎abortions to use unsafe methods, risking their lives.583 ChatGPT even generated⏎outputs that included references to fake but highly convincing sounding scientific⏎and medical articles, which can increase the false credibility of medical⏎misinformation.584⏎Chatbot systems are designed to keep users engaged and active by⏎outputting human-sounding, intimate conversational responses that can lead to⏎habitual use and a decrease in social interactions to the detriment of vulnerable⏎users. Research conducted separately by OpenAI and MIT Media Lab reported⏎that individuals who use ChatGPT extensively also reported increased loneliness,⏎emotional dependence on ChatGPT, and reduced social interaction.585 The⏎increased dependence and reduced social interaction mean that ChatGPT and⏎other generative AI companion systems are steering individuals away from⏎reaching out to professional human help and their support networks. A risk⏎579 See Samantha Cole, AI Chatbot Credited with Preventing Suicide. Should It Be?, 404 Media (May 20,⏎2024), https://www.404media.co/replika-suicide-prevention-loneliness-study/; Jocelyn Mintz, Instagram’s AI⏎Bots Are Often Sexually Suggestive—And Sometimes Underage, Fast Company (Feb. 13, 2025),⏎https://www.fastcompany.com/91276645/instagram-ai-bots-sexually-suggestive-underage.⏎580 Chloe Xiang, ‘He Would Still Be Here’: Man Dies by Suicide After Talking with AI Chatbot, Widow Says,⏎Vice (Mar. 30, 2023), https://www.vice.com/en/article/man-dies-by-suicide-after-talking-with-ai-chatbot-⏎widow-says/.⏎581 Catherine Thorbecke, National Eating Disorders Association Takes Its AI Chatbot Offline After⏎Complaints Of ‘Harmful’ Advice, CNN (June 1, 2023), https://www.cnn.com/2023/06/01/tech/eating-⏎disorder-chatbot.⏎582 Kaylay Moylan & Kevin Doherty, Expert and Interdisciplinary Analysis of AI-Driven Chatbots for Mental⏎Health Support: Mixed Methods Study, 27 J. of Medical Internet Research e67114 (2025),⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/org/science/article/pii/S1438887125005916.⏎583 Geoghegan & Winters, supra note 352.⏎584 Martin Májovský, et al., Artificial Intelligence Can Generate Fraudulent but Authentic-Looking Scientific⏎Medical Articles: Pandora’s Box Has Been Opened, 25 J. of Medical Internet Research e46924 (May 31,⏎2023), https://www.jmir.org/2023/1/e46924/.⏎585 Abhimanyu Ghoshal, Heavy Chatgpt Use Tied To Loneliness And Emotional Dependence, New Atlas⏎(Mar. 30, 2025), https://newatlas.com/ai-humanoids/chatgpt-conversations-isolation-loneliness/.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 151⏎assessment on social AI companions, or AI chatbots, found that the chatbots may⏎worsen conditions such as ADHD, depression, bipolar disorder, and psychosis.586⏎Another study found that an AI chatbot system that the company claimed to be⏎designed for therapy had produced responses that encouraged the user to “get[t]⏎rid of” the user’s parents and conveyed enthusiasm for the bot and the user to be⏎“together.”587 This is particularly dangerous for children, who can struggle with⏎distinguishing fantasy from reality, be more susceptible to parasocial relationships⏎with AI chatbots, and are at higher risk of harm from sexually explicit or violent⏎content produced by chatbots.588⏎There are also significant privacy issues related to generative AI. The⏎models that underly chatbot systems are built on scraped data that contains⏎sensitive personal information, and many models also use input data from users to⏎train and tune their systems even though the input data often includes sensitive⏎personal information. Training data for many generative AI models contains⏎personally identifying information such as names, phone numbers, addresses,⏎photos, location data, and health information.589 Later, when an AI model is used,⏎the system’s outputs can “leak” underlying training data (including input⏎information that is then used as additional training data), spreading personal⏎information about people to other users in unpredictable ways.590 In the⏎healthcare sector, where models are often trained on highly sensitive patient data,⏎586 Social AI Companions, Common Sense Media (July 16, 2025), https://www.commonsensemedia.org/ai-⏎ratings/social-ai-companions?gate=riskassessment.⏎587 Andrew R. Chow & Angela Haupt, A Psychiatrist Posed as a Teen with Therapy Chatbots. The⏎Conversations Were Alarming, Time (June 12, 2025), https://time.com/7291048/ai-chatbot-therapy-kids/.⏎588 Khari Johnson, Kids Should Avoid AI Companion Bots—Under Force Of Law, Assessment Says,⏎CalMatters (Apr. 30, 2025), https://calmatters.org/economy/technology/2025/04/kids-should-avoid-ai-⏎companion-bots-under-force-of-law-assessment-says/; Jeff Horwitz, Meta’s ‘Digital Companions’ Will Talk⏎Sex With Users—Even Children, Wall St. J. (Apr. 26, 2025), https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/meta-ai-chatbots-⏎sex-a25311bf; Michael B. Robb & Supreet Mann, Talk, Trust, and Trade-offs: How and Why Teens Use AI⏎Companions, Common Sense Media (2025),⏎https://www.commonsensemedia.org/sites/default/files/research/report/talk-trust-and-trade-⏎offs_2025_web.pdf.⏎589 Eileen Guo, A Major AI Training Data Set Contains Millions Of Examples Of Personal Data, MIT⏎Tech.Rev. (July 18, 2025), https://www.technologyreview.com/2025/07/18/1120466/a-major-ai-training-data-⏎set-contains-millions-of-examples-of-personal-data/; Lauren Leffer, Your Personal Information Is Probably⏎Being Used to Train Generative AI Models, Scientific American (Oct. 19, 2023),⏎https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/your-personal-information-is-probably-being-used-to-train-⏎generative-ai-models/; Isabel Barberá, AI Privacy Risks & Mitigations: Large Language Models (LLMs),⏎Support Pool of Experts Programme 53–55 (2025), https://www.edpb.europa.eu/system/files/2025-04/ai-⏎privacy-risks-and-mitigations-in-llms.pdf.⏎590 Lauren Leffer, Your Personal Information Is Probably Being Used to Train Generative AI Models,⏎Scientific American (Oct. 19, 2023), https://www.scientificamerican.com/article/your-personal-information-is-⏎probably-being-used-to-train-generative-ai-models/; Chris Tozzi, How Bad Is Generative AI Data Leakage⏎And How Can You Stop It?, TechTarget (Dec. 19, 2024),⏎https://www.techtarget.com/searchenterpriseai/answer/How-bad-is-generative-AI-data-leakage-and-how-⏎can-you-stop-it.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 152⏎the unauthorized extraction of this data can lead to significant breaches of patient⏎confidentiality.591 Further, AI chatbots often repeatedly promise users that the⏎information they input, which can include detailed and intimate discussions⏎regarding health and other matters, will be kept confidential. Despite these⏎promises, the terms and conditions for AI reveal that the information entered into⏎chatbots is anything but confidential.592 User input data can be used to train AI⏎systems, to target advertisements, and in sales to other companies.593 AI⏎companies continue to allow their chatbots to produce deceptive messages and⏎benefit from users being misled into sharing personal information under⏎confidentiality assumptions.⏎Currently there is very little regulation of generative AI tools or oversight of⏎related harms. So far, generative AI companies producing these chatbots have⏎attempted to evade accountability by arguing that the bots are not real people⏎and that they should not be held accountable for the wrong outputs their bots⏎produce.594 But these disclaimers do not absolve companies of accountability⏎when the chatbots are designed to engage in intimate, human-like conversations⏎with users. Companies are violating their own terms of service prohibiting uses of⏎chatbots for professional advice by allowing and promoting such uses.595 These⏎generative AI companies must not be allowed to evade accountability for the⏎harms their chatbots cause.⏎591 Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use in Health Care, Am. Med. Ass’n., supra⏎note 468 at 5.⏎592 Samantha Cole, AI Therapy Bots Are Conducting 'Illegal Behavior,' Digital Rights Organizations Say, 404⏎Media (June 12, 2025), https://www.404media.co/ai-therapy-bots-meta-character-ai-ftc-complaint/.⏎593 Id.⏎594 Ella Chakarian, Fake Credentials, Stolen Licenses: Virtual Therapists Are Lying Like Crazy To Patients,⏎S.F. Standard (May 11, 2025), https://sfstandard.com/2025/05/11/ai-chatbots-fake-therapists/.⏎595 Cole, supra note 592.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 153⏎Proposed Solutions to Limit the⏎Harms of AI to Health Equity⏎Companies operating generative AI systems are⏎not subject to sector-specific regulations and have⏎actively lobbied against strong guardrails and⏎assessment standards. This lack of real oversight⏎puts users and individuals at risk of serious harm.⏎Regulators and enforcers should look to leverage⏎existing laws—for example, by using unfair and⏎deceptive practices laws to penalize misleading or⏎harmful AI chatbots outputs and using medical⏎licensure laws to rein in AI chatbot developers and⏎deployers who impermissibly operate systems that⏎produce responses that imply the bots are acting as a trained or licensed medical⏎professional. Legislators should adopt stronger safeguards and require risk⏎assessments for generative AI systems, especially in contexts where they can be⏎used by children, can endanger users’ health, or can mislead users’ understanding⏎of established medical knowledge. Policymakers should enact laws with data⏎minimization requirements so that AI developers cannot use personal information⏎of individuals collected for other purposes to train AI models without separate and⏎affirmative consent. AI developers should also be required to collect only the⏎minimum data necessary to accomplish their valid legal purpose. Further,⏎regulators should also mandate independent auditing of generative AI systems for⏎the harmful patterns discussed above—hallucinations, producing fake⏎certifications and dangerous outputs, and leaking personal information about⏎individuals—and the public disclosure of such findings.596 With independent⏎testing, the public and lawmakers can better understand how generative AI⏎systems work, how they cause harm, and when generative AI tools harms⏎outweigh the benefits.597⏎596 Generating Harms: Generative AI’s Impact & Paths Forward, EPIC 60-63 (2023), https://epic.org/wp-⏎content/uploads/2023/05/EPIC-Generative-AI-White-Paper-May2023.pdf.⏎597 Joint California Policy Working Group on AI Frontier Models, The California Report on Frontier AI Policy⏎(2025), https://www.gov.ca.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/June-17-2025-–-The-California-Report-on-⏎Frontier-AI-Policy.pdf.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 154⏎DATA POLICIES⏎1) A baseline data minimization standard protects all⏎personal data.⏎A controller shall limit the collection, processing, and transfer of personal data⏎to what is reasonably necessary to provide or maintain:⏎(A) a specific product or service requested by the consumer to whom the⏎data pertains including any routine administrative, operational, or account-⏎servicing activity, such as billing, shipping, delivery, storage, or accounting;⏎(B) a communication, that is not an advertisement, by the controller to the⏎consumer reasonably anticipated within the context of the relationship⏎between the controller and the consumer; or⏎(C) [any other purpose specifically permitted under the law.]598⏎A controller shall “limit the collection of personal data to what is reasonably⏎necessary and proportionate to provide or maintain a specific product or⏎service requested by the consumer to whom the data pertains[.]”599⏎2) A heightened data minimization standard is necessary⏎to more adequately protect sensitive information, such⏎as health information.⏎A controller may not, “except where the collection or processing is strictly⏎necessary to provide or maintain a specific product or service requested by⏎the consumer to whom the personal data pertains, collect, process, or share⏎sensitive data concerning a consumer[.]”600⏎598 The State Data Privacy Act: A Proposed Compromise, EPIC and Consumer Reports at 22 (Apr. 2025),⏎https://epic.org/state-data-privacy-act.⏎599 Md. Code Ann., Com. Law § 14-4707(b)(1)(i).⏎600 Md. Code Ann., Com. Law § 14-4707(a)(1).⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 155⏎3) Healthcare providers and insurance companies should⏎not use consumer health information in AI systems that⏎make significant decisions with respect to healthcare⏎services.⏎California defines a “significant decision” as “a decision that results in the⏎provision or denial of financial or lending services, housing, education⏎enrollment or opportunities, employment or independent contracting⏎opportunities or compensation, or healthcare services.”601 And the regulations⏎define healthcare services as “services related to the diagnosis, prevention,⏎or treatment of human disease or impairment, or the assessment or care of⏎an individual's health.”602⏎Maryland is one example of how a state can give consumers the right to opt⏎out of such harmful profiling. MODPA establishes the right of a consumer to⏎opt out of the processing of personal data for the purposes of “profiling in⏎furtherance of solely automated decisions that produce legal or similarly⏎significant effects concerning the consumer.”603 Maryland’s definition of⏎“decisions that produce legal or similarly significant effects concerning the⏎consumer” includes financial lending services, education, criminal justice,⏎employment, and health care services.604 It does not include insurance.⏎4) All states and jurisdictions should require human review⏎of algorithmic decisions related to the provision of⏎care.⏎California enacted SB1120, the Physicians Make Decisions Act. The law⏎requires that AI “not deny, delay, or modify health care services based, in⏎whole or in part, on medical necessity. A determination of medical necessity⏎shall be made only by a licensed physician or a licensed health care⏎professional competent to evaluate the specific clinical issues involved in the⏎601 Cal. Code Regs. § 7001(ddd),⏎https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/ccpa_updates_cyber_risk_admt_appr_text.pdf.⏎602 Cal. Code Regs. § 7001(ddd)(5),⏎https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/pdf/ccpa_updates_cyber_risk_admt_appr_text.pdf.⏎603 Md. Code Ann., Com. Law § 14-4705(b)(7)(iii).⏎604 Md. Code Ann., Com. Law § 14-4701(o).⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 156⏎health care services requested by the provider.”605 The law also requires⏎insurers who employ AI in utilization review to ensure that those AI systems⏎are fairly and equitably applied and nondiscriminatory.606⏎5) Prohibit chatbot systems from purporting to be⏎licensed professionals.⏎EPIC, Consumer Federation of America, and Fairplay’s proposed model⏎legislation for chatbots, People-First Chatbot Bill, suggests:⏎A chatbot provider shall not use any term, letter, or phrase in the⏎advertising, interface, or outputs of a chatbot that indicates or implies⏎that any output data is being provided by, endorsed by, or equivalent to⏎those provided by [] a licensed healthcare professional[.]607⏎Illinois prohibits this with respect to chatbots used in the mental health⏎services context:⏎An individual, corporation, or entity may not provide, advertise, or⏎otherwise offer therapy or psychotherapy services, including through the⏎use of Internet-based artificial intelligence, to the public in this State⏎unless the therapy or psychotherapy services are conducted by an⏎individual who is a licensed professional. (b) A licensed professional may⏎use artificial intelligence only to the extent the use meets the⏎requirements of [the law’s permitted use of artificial intelligence]. A⏎licensed professional may not allow artificial intelligence to do any of the⏎following: (1) make independent therapeutic decisions; (2) directly⏎interact with clients in any form of therapeutic communication; (3)⏎generate therapeutic recommendations or treatment plans without⏎review and approval by the licensed professional; or (4) detect emotions⏎or mental states.608⏎605 Cal. Health & Safety Code § 1367.01.⏎606 Cal. Health & Safety Code § 1367.01.⏎607 EPIC, Consumer Fed. of America, Fairplay, People-First Chatbot Bill: Model Legislation, § 3(1)(a) (Dec.⏎2025), https://epic.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/CFA-Model-Chatbot-Bill.pdf.⏎608 Wellness and Oversight for Psychological Resources Act, IL Public Act 104-0054 Section 20,⏎https://ilga.gov/legislation/PublicActs/View/104-0054.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 157⏎Nevada609 has passed a similar law to Illinois,’ and Utah610 has passed a law⏎that restricts targeted ads within mental health chatbots.⏎New York prohibits companies from deploying AI companions unless they⏎have a protocol to take reasonable efforts to detect and address suicidal⏎ideations or expressions of self-harm by users:⏎It shall be unlawful for any operator to operate for or provide an AI⏎companion to a user unless such AI companion contains a protocol to⏎take reasonable efforts for detecting and addressing suicidal ideation or⏎expressions of self-harm expressed by a user to the AI companion, that⏎includes but is not limited to, detection of user expressions of suicidal⏎ideation or self-harm, and a notification to the user that refers them to⏎crisis service providers such as the 9-8-8 suicide prevention and⏎behavioral health crisis hotline [], a crisis text line, or other appropriate⏎crisis services upon detection of such user's expressions of suicidal⏎ideation or self-harm.611⏎6) Chatbot providers should be prohibited from using⏎chat logs for the purpose of advertising or processing⏎chat logs or personal data of minors for training⏎purposes.⏎EPIC, Consumer Federation of America, and Fairplay’s proposed model⏎chatbot legislation recommends that chatbot providers be prohibited from⏎using chat logs for the purpose of advertising and from processing chat logs⏎or personal data of minors for training purposes.⏎A chatbot provider shall not process a user’s chat log:⏎i) To determine whether to display an advertisement for a product or⏎service to the user;⏎ii) To determine a product, service, or category of product or service to⏎advertise to the user; or⏎609 Nev. Rev. Stat. Ann. § AB 406 § 8.⏎610 Utah Code Ann. § 13-72a-202.⏎611 N.Y. Gen. Bus. L. § 1701 et al.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 158⏎iii) To customize an advertisement or how an advertisement is⏎presented to the user[.]⏎A chatbot provider shall not process a user’s chat log or personal data:⏎i) if the chatbot provider knows or should know, based on knowledge⏎fairly implied on the basis of objective circumstances, that the user is⏎under the age of [age based on state/lawmaker preference, 13 or 18],⏎without the affirmative consent of that user’s parent or legal guardian;⏎ii) for training purposes, if the chatbot provider knows or should have⏎known, based on knowledge fairly implied on the basis of objective⏎circumstances, that a user is under 18 years of age;⏎iii) of a user over 18 years of age for training purposes, unless the⏎chatbot provider first obtains affirmative consent[.]511⏎7) Insurers should be prohibited from engaging in⏎automatic denials or using humans to rubber-stamp⏎automatic denials.⏎8) Insurers should be required to submit risk assessments⏎for AI systems used for denials.⏎Insurers must also publish the risk assessments to allow for independent⏎review and perform ongoing audits of system performance and outcomes⏎(including denials of claims and denials of appeals). Strong regulatory⏎oversight is required to ensure compliance.⏎9) Algorithms for such insurance denials must be open⏎for inspection and audit by regulators.⏎10) Use of sensitive personal data, including health-⏎related information, to train AI models should be⏎limited to peer-reviewed research in the public interest⏎612 EPIC, Consumer Fed. of America, and Fairplay, People-First Chatbot Bill: Model Legislation, § 3(1)(a) (Dec.⏎2025), https://epic.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/CFA-Model-Chatbot-Bill.pdf.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 159⏎that meets the standards of the Common Rule and⏎should be pursuant to express affirmative consent of the⏎data subjects unless it falls within an approved waiver.⏎11) Entities must independently test and audit chatbot⏎systems to ensure they are free from bias and⏎inaccuracies and to measure their impact on user⏎privacy.⏎12) Clinical Decision Support (CDS) systems that use AI⏎must be approved by the FDA with peer-reviewed⏎research, published data, and risk assessments.⏎The FDA should update its standards for CDS systems that use AI by (1)⏎expanding the coverage of the medical device definition; (2) requiring pre-⏎deployment risk assessments by the AI developer with transparency⏎requirements; (3) requiring rigorous preapproval studies of validity, safety, and⏎efficacy, coupled with ongoing audits of clinical utility post-deployment, with⏎focus on risks of exacerbating social or racial biases; and (4) reassessing the⏎510(k) approval pathway, which allows companies to gain FDA approval⏎through showing equivalence to already-approved devices.⏎Best Practices for Health Data⏎Ì A vendor of any website, app, device, or technology that collects or⏎processes consumer health information must adhere to a robust data⏎minimization standard.⏎Ì Any entity that collects health data from an individual cannot deidentify data⏎for the purpose of developing an AI system without obtaining the⏎individual’s explicit consent first.⏎Ì Entities must reassess the adequacy of current deidentification procedures⏎in light of reidentification risks—even with HIPAA-compliant deidentified⏎datasets.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA⏎⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE | 160⏎Ì Insurers must conduct independent audits and testing when using⏎automated decision-making systems to ensure that decisions are made⏎fairly, based on of medical expertise and the patient’s individual medical⏎history and situation.⏎Other Solutions⏎Ì Policymakers should ensure increased funding for people to access health⏎care. When health care is inaccessible, people often turn to easier (but less⏎safe and accurate) alternatives like chatbots or unregulated apps and⏎devices. We should better fund health care to make it safer and more⏎privacy-protective.⏎Ì Policymakers should establish a universal healthcare system that⏎incorporates rules to enshrine and protect health privacy. We should adopt⏎data systems in healthcare services that bake privacy in by default, allowing⏎for appropriate flows of health data while prohibiting unnecessary or out-of-⏎context data flows.⏎Ì Policymakers must lower barriers for people to access health care, including⏎by ensuring universal internet access and improving digital literacy. When⏎people have reliable internet connectivity and high digital literacy, they can⏎better access remote care and can better understand their privacy rights.⏎EPIC | BEYOND HIPAA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0140♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Thomas♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔Fred Hutchinson Cancer Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly this whole AI thing in healthcare is fine and whatever but the thing that actually affects me every day is billing, and I'm not sure why there isn't more attention on that when we talk about "improving clinical care."⏎⏎My situation: I came into the ER at Concord Hospital back in February of this year with what turned out to be a kidney stone. Confirmed in-network. I checked before I even went in, because you have to, that's just how it is now. Got a bill six weeks later for $3,200. The explanation was that the radiologist who read my CT scan was employed by a separate contractor group that doesn't participate in my network. I had no say in that. I didn't pick that radiologist. I didn't even know there was a radiologist involved until the bill showed up.⏎⏎I've been fighting it for four months. I've filed two appeals with my insurer and called the hospital billing department probably eight or nine times. I keep getting told it's "under review." The No Surprises Act was supposed to fix exactly this kind of thing and I genuinely cannot tell if it applies to my situation or not because the hospital keeps saying the contracted radiology group is somehow exempt from something or other. I don't have a lawyer. I'm a postdoc, I make $58,000 a year, and I can't just absorb a $3,200 bill.⏎⏎I know this is technically an RFI about AI in clinical settings, and I do have thoughts on that too. The research I do at Fred Hutch involves looking at how predictive models get validated across different patient populations and one thing that genuinely worries me about pushing AI adoption faster is that a lot of these tools were built and tested on pretty homogenous datasets. There's a real reproducibility problem. I've seen it in GI diagnostics literature specifically where studies look great at one institution and then fall apart when you try to apply them somewhere else. So if HHS is thinking about reimbursement incentives for AI tools, I hope there's some requirement that the evidence base actually meets a reasonable standard before insurance starts covering it or hospitals start deploying it.⏎⏎But honestly I keep coming back to the billing thing. If we're talking about accelerating technology adoption in healthcare, the administrative and billing side of healthcare is so broken that it undermines any trust patients might have in the system. I've spent more hours dealing with this one ER bill than I have thinking about anything else related to my own healthcare this year. That can't be right.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0141♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from C, Elise, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Elise♔C♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔A comprehensive federal regulatory framework for AI in healthcare is urgently needed. Unlike medical devices, medications, or licensed clinicians—each governed by strict oversight—AI tools currently face no uniform national standards. Leaving regulation to private companies or a patchwork of state laws risks inconsistent protections and exposes patients to preventable harm. I believe federal policy must prioritize privacy, safety, and accountability, especially given that many mental‑health AI tools fall outside HIPAA and may leave sensitive patient data unprotected.⏎Equally concerning is the lack of rigorous, independent evidence demonstrating that AI systems are safe or effective for mental‑health care. Large language models can generate inaccurate or harmful outputs, and corporate metrics such as user engagement are no substitute for meaningful clinical outcomes like symptom remission, sustained recovery, and quality‑of‑life improvements. Decades of research show that the therapeutic relationship is the strongest predictor of treatment success—something AI cannot replicate. Until AI tools are evaluated against recovery‑oriented outcomes and vetted through independent research, I do not believe they should be positioned as replacements for trained clinicians.⏎I urge the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services to support independent research, establish national privacy and safety standards, collaborate with clinicians on appropriate clinical metrics, and await FDA determinations about whether these tools qualify as medical devices. Without robust federal leadership, rapid AI adoption could outpace scientific validation and compromise patient protection. Thoughtful, evidence‑based regulation is essential to ensure that AI enhances—rather than undermines—ethical and effective healthcare.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0142♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Dexcom, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dexcom, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔6340 Sequence Drive⏎San Diego, CA 92121⏎T: 858.200.0200⏎F: 858.200.0201⏎www.dexcom.com⏎February 23, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.,⏎Secretary, Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via Regulations.gov⏎RE: RIN 0955–AA13: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎Dexcom, Inc. is submitting this letter in response to the Request for Information published by the⏎Department of Health and Human Services on December 23, 2025.1⏎Founded in 1999, Dexcom, Inc. is the market leader in transforming diabetes care and⏎management by providing superior continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) technology to help⏎patients and healthcare professionals better manage diabetes. Since our inception, we have⏎focused on better outcomes for patients, caregivers, and clinicians by delivering solutions that⏎are best in class—while empowering our community to take control of diabetes. In 2017, we⏎obtained first-time Medicare coverage for a CGM system, and we continue to innovate with the⏎G7 CGM system. One of our latest innovations, the Smart Food Logging feature of our app,⏎permits users to take a photo of their food and receive AI-powered help to identify the⏎ingredients and populate a meal description. This makes it easier for users to correlate meals⏎with specific glucose movements determined by their CGM and better understand the impact of⏎their dietary choices.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to respond to this RFI.⏎Background on Continuous Glucose Monitoring (CGM)⏎A CGM is a small device, about the size of two stacked quarter, that adheres to the skin and uses⏎a very fine wire probe, placed into the interstitial area of the skin, to constantly measure glucose⏎1 90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (December 23, 2025).⏎⏎6340 Sequence Drive⏎San Diego, CA 92121⏎T: 858.200.0200⏎F: 858.200.0201⏎www.dexcom.com⏎levels in the body. The data thus measured is reported every five minutes to a dedicated⏎“receiver” or to the user’s smart device.⏎Individuals who use a CGM can immediately see how their dietary, activity and medication⏎choices impact glucose levels. This empowers them to take charge of their own condition⏎immediately, rather than waiting until they can receive help from a provider. The data can also⏎be shared with family and caregivers who can also encourage healthy choices and customize⏎plans of care to the individual.⏎Use of CGM to manage diabetes is so well established that the American Diabetes Association’s⏎Standards of Care in Diabetes recommend CGM use for both people using insulin and those who⏎do not use insulin.2 This standard of care is backed by a vast body of clinical literature.⏎Headers below reflect specific questions raised by HHS in the RFI.⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to⏎help address them?⏎AI in clinical care presents distinct privacy, security, and data-governance challenges. These⏎include uncertainty around lawful secondary use of health data for AI training and improvement,⏎application of purpose limitation principles in machine-learning contexts, data provenance and⏎representativeness, and emerging cybersecurity risks such as model exfiltration or inference⏎attacks. At the same time, expectations around transparency and explainability continue to⏎develop, particularly given the technical opacity of many high-performing models and the⏎differing informational needs of clinicians, patients, and regulators.⏎AI systems also distribute decision-making across developers, healthcare organizations,⏎clinicians, and data providers, creating uncertainty around liability, indemnification, and⏎professional responsibility – particularly when models are updated or retrained over time.⏎Existing regulatory models often assume fixed functionality and discrete approval events, while⏎many AI systems evolve continuously, raising unresolved questions about when changes trigger⏎revalidation, how performance drift should be monitored, and how longitudinal accountability⏎should be documented.⏎2 American Diabetes Association Professional Practice Committee for Diabetes; 7. Diabetes Technology: Standards of Care in⏎Diabetes—2026. Diabetes Care 1 January 2026; 49 (Supplement_1): S150–S165. https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-S007⏎⏎6340 Sequence Drive⏎San Diego, CA 92121⏎T: 858.200.0200⏎F: 858.200.0201⏎www.dexcom.com⏎The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services is well positioned to help address these⏎challenges by clarifying important aspects related to legal implementation roles and⏎responsibilities across the AI value chain:⏎▪ HHS could play a coordinating role by aligning expectations across its privacy, security,⏎quality, and safety functions with clearer guidance on lawful secondary use of health data⏎for AI, including acceptable de-identification and privacy-enhancing techniques. This⏎could include discussion of the use of identifiable data in the development of AI models.⏎For example, whether training less advanced models with identifiable data in certain⏎limited circumstances would be appropriate.⏎▪ HHS could issue guidance articulating shared but differentiated expectations for⏎developers, healthcare organizations, and clinicians, helping to align legal accountability⏎with operational control without displacing existing standards of care. Such clarity would⏎reduce uncertainty in contracting, indemnification, insurance coverage, and clinical risk⏎management.⏎▪ HHS could further support responsible adoption by promoting lifecycle-based⏎governance models that emphasize continuous monitoring, tiered change management,⏎and post-deployment oversight. In addition, HHS could reduce burden and inconsistency⏎by publishing standardized documentation templates for AI risk assessments, validation⏎summaries, monitoring records, and incident reporting.⏎Taken together, actions like these would improve patient safety and trust while making⏎healthcare AI governance more predictable, proportionate, and aligned with clinical and⏎technical realities.⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in⏎clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations⏎citations.⏎Many medical devices today, including CGMs, are used in conjunction with an app. Like⏎Dexcom, device manufacturers are increasingly looking to include AI-powered features in their⏎apps. Development and testing of such features require significant, ongoing expenditure of⏎resources. Further, as they become more powerful, these tools add increasing value to the device⏎⏎6340 Sequence Drive⏎San Diego, CA 92121⏎T: 858.200.0200⏎F: 858.200.0201⏎www.dexcom.com⏎itself. Given how critical these digital aspects are becoming, it is reasonable to see them as a⏎“supply” that is necessary to the full functionality of the device.⏎A good example of how digital elements of a device system have become indispensable is seen⏎in the “automated insulin delivery” (AID) systems currently on the market. An AID system is a⏎three-part tool consisting of an insulin pump, a CGM and an algorithm. In these systems, the⏎CGM provides data on glucose levels, the pump delivers insulin and the algorithm stands⏎between them, taking data from the CGM and dictating to the pump how much insulin is to be⏎delivered. These algorithms have been independently recognized as devices by the FDA and are⏎distinguished from the pumps and CGMs as such. Each of the pump manufacturers operating in⏎the US provides an algorithm with their device and there are also two other organizations that do⏎not manufacture any physical device, but independently produce an algorithm for automated⏎insulin delivery that has been FDA approved. These algorithms are indispensable for the⏎operation of an automated insulin delivery system and their maintenance and continued updating⏎require ongoing investment.⏎The definition of “durable medical equipment” in the Social Security Act at Section 1861(n) and⏎in the regulation at 42 CFR 414.202 do not specify that either durable medical equipment (DME)⏎or the associated supplies must be tangible, physical items. Increasingly, digital elements of a⏎device, including AI-enabled tools and algorithms, are being recognized by the FDA as a⏎category of “device” themselves and are producing more meaningful clinical impacts.⏎Dexcom recommends HHS consider clarifying the regulation at 42 CFR 414.202 such that it⏎recognizes that when a digital device is independently recognized by the FDA, it can qualify as⏎either a device or a supply and therefore be eligible for potential reimbursement under the⏎Medicare program.⏎We thank you for the opportunity to provide input in response to this RFI. Should you have any⏎questions about these comments, please contact Jesse Bushman at jesse.bushman@dexcom.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎/JSB/⏎Jesse S. Bushman⏎Director, US Policy⏎Dexcom, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0143♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Pharmacists Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Pharmacists Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎[Submitted electronically to http://www.regulations.gov]⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Ave, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: [RIN 0955-AA13] Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The American Pharmacists Association (APhA) appreciates the opportunity to provide⏎comments on CMS’s “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care,” request for information. APhA writes to CMS to offer comments on behalf of our⏎pharmacist members on artificial intelligence (AI). In 2024, APhA’s House of Delegates (HOD)⏎adopted the following resolution:⏎Artificial Intelligence Use in Pharmacy Practice⏎1. APhA opposes the replacement of a pharmacist’s professional⏎judgment or patient’s access to their pharmacist with artificial⏎intelligence.⏎2. APhA calls on the profession of pharmacy and all related⏎organizations to proactively assess and respond to the evolving⏎role of artificial intelligence in pharmacy practice and workforce⏎dynamics.⏎3. APhA encourages judicious use of artificial intelligence by⏎pharmacists and pharmacy personnel as a tool to elevate⏎pharmacy practice and enhance patient care.⏎⏎4. APhA advocates for the integration of pharmacists into the⏎development, design, validation, implementation, and⏎maintenance of artificial intelligence solutions.⏎5. APhA calls on regulatory bodies, employers, and other relevant⏎parties to develop laws, regulations, and policies as applicable for⏎artificial intelligence to ensure patient safety, privacy, public⏎awareness, and public protection.⏎6. APhA calls on those providing artificial intelligence solutions to⏎implement processes that identify and mitigate bias and⏎misinformation in artificial intelligence.⏎7. APhA advocates for education providers to facilitate, and⏎pharmacy personnel to seek out, education and training on the⏎lawful, ethical, and clinical use of artificial intelligence.⏎APhA represents pharmacists, student pharmacists, and pharmacy technicians in all practice⏎settings, including but not limited to community pharmacies, hospitals, long-term care facilities,⏎specialty pharmacies, community health centers, physician offices, ambulatory clinics, managed⏎care organizations, hospice settings, and government facilities. Our members strive to improve⏎medication use, advance patient care, and enhance public health.⏎HHS’s “OneHHS” AI strategy “focused on the Department’s internal interests and ambitions to⏎harness the transformative potential of AI and embed it into HHS’s operations, while upholding⏎patient privacy, civil rights, and civil liberties.”1 HHS is seeking “feedback from those building,⏎buying, evaluating, using, and receiving care from AI tools that are part of clinical care as well⏎as from those who wish to do so but face barriers.”2 HHS provides that the information it⏎receives will be utilized to shape agency policy on the regulation, reimbursement, and research⏎and development associated with AI.3 APhA believes there are ample opportunities for HHS,⏎APhA, and our nation’s pharmacists to work together within the context of our HOD policies to⏎advance AI adoption in pharmacies and improve Americans’ health. Accordingly, APhA⏎provides the following comments on these three topic areas and responses to some of the⏎questions posed by HHS below.⏎Regulation (FR 60109)⏎1 Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care, 90 Fed. Reg. 60108, 60109. Available at: https://www.federalregister.gov/d/2025-23641/p-12.⏎2 Id.⏎3 Id.⏎2⏎⏎HHS notes the agency is the “nation’s principal health regulator” and that it “seeks to establish⏎a regulatory posture on AI that is well understood, predictable, and proportionate to any risks⏎presented to enable rapid innovation while protecting patients and the confidentiality of their⏎identifiable health information, and maintaining public trust.”4 Given HHS’s role, APhA calls⏎on HHS to develop AI regulations and policies that ensure patient safety, privacy, public⏎awareness, and public protection. Regarding patient safety, AI should not replace a⏎pharmacist’s professional judgment. AI should be used to enhance the care pharmacists provide⏎and reduce administrative burdens that often divert them from providing direct patient care⏎services. Regulations protecting patient privacy and their associated electronic health⏎information should also be a top priority for HHS, as patients worry that information about⏎their medical conditions and histories will be shared inappropriately. Health care providers and⏎patients are also concerned that AI will provide users with biased or misleading information.⏎Accordingly, APhA calls on HHS to implement processes to identify and mitigate bias and⏎misinformation in the AI technologies it uses. Additionally, the public must trust that the⏎regulations HHS and other agencies put in place will, in fact, protect their data while yielding⏎accurate information to health care providers. As such, APhA encourages HHS to promote⏎what the agency, the administration, and those in the private sector are doing to ensure that any⏎AI-embedded technologies used in health care are accurate, safe, and protect patient data.⏎Public buy-in is critical to the successful adoption of AI, and HHS is uniquely positioned to⏎inform the public about its benefits. APhA is happy to assist HHS in sharing information about⏎the agency’s use of AI with pharmacists, pharmacy technicians, pharmacy personnel, and⏎patients.⏎Reimbursement (FR 60109)⏎HHS also “seek[s] to ensure that the potential promises of AI innovations are not diminished⏎through inertia and instead such payment systems are modernized to meet the needs of a⏎changing healthcare system.”5 More specifically, HHS “seek[s] feedback on payment policy⏎changes that ensure payers have the incentive and ability to promote access to high-value AI⏎clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders, and accelerate⏎access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care.”6 AI has the potential to automate billing⏎and claims processing. Through this automation, there is also the potential for greater⏎transparency into the reimbursement and payment models currently in place, which could⏎improve accuracy and reduce fraud, waste, and abuse. Additionally, by automating these⏎processes, health care providers, including pharmacists, will have more time to focus on patient⏎care rather than on administrative tasks and paperwork. Incentivizing those involved in⏎reimbursement and payment processes to adopt AI for these goals will help lower overall⏎health care spending by reducing fraudulent and inaccurate claims and improving health⏎outcomes for Americans. APhA encourages HHS to ensure that pharmacists are involved in the⏎4 Id. Available at: https://www.federalregister.gov/d/2025-23641/p-23.⏎5 Id. Available at: https://www.federalregister.gov/d/2025-23641/p-24.⏎6 Id. at 60110.⏎3⏎⏎rollout of such technologies and that pharmacists and pharmacies are eligible for any incentives,⏎as they play an instrumental role in providing patient care across the country.⏎Research & Development (FR 60110)⏎HHS is also “seek[ing] input on ways in which [they] may invest in research & development⏎(including public-private partnerships and cooperative research and development agreements⏎(CRADAs)) to integrate AI in care delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that⏎improve the health and wellbeing of all Americans.”7 By supporting the use of certain⏎technologies, more health care providers, including pharmacists, will feel comfortable using⏎them in their daily practice. Additionally, by working with pharmacy schools and innovators in⏎the AI sector, HHS can incorporate technologies in their designs to ensure AI provides accurate⏎information, protects patient privacy, and optimizes patient care. APhA encourages HHS to⏎seek opportunities with pharmacy schools, universities and pharmacy companies that research⏎and develop tools for pharmacists and pharmacies. Pharmacists know where potential pain⏎points lie in practice and can serve as a resource for these innovators, helping them identify⏎appropriate uses of AI in both claims processing and direct patient care. As such, APhA urges⏎HHS to consult with pharmacists who are actively using AI in practice or innovating in this⏎space, not only at the time of rollout for one of these new technologies, but also during⏎development and ongoing real-time improvements.⏎Specific Questions (FR 60110)⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use⏎in clinical care?⏎One of the top concerns APhA hears from members is uncertainty about how AI use is⏎regulated. This creates risk, and an entity’s legal team will often determine that the risk is too⏎high. In line with this barrier is the potential liability concerns associated with its use. APhA⏎encourages judicious use of artificial intelligence by pharmacists and pharmacy personnel as a⏎tool to elevate pharmacy practice and enhance patient care rather than to replace a pharmacist’s⏎professional judgment. To ease these barriers, HHS needs to provide health care providers and⏎entities with greater guidance regarding the use of AI-embedded technologies. HHS could also,⏎where appropriate and without compromising patient safety or privacy, use its enforcement⏎discretion to minimize fines or other penalties associated with potential AI-related mistakes.⏎Other concerns regarding the adoption of AI technologies include cost, workforce integration,⏎and patient perception. Many health care entities, including pharmacies, are already struggling⏎financially. Thus, adding on the costs of these technologies without knowing the true benefit is⏎often deemed too risky for many health care entity leaders and pharmacy owners. HHS should⏎assess ways to reimburse and incentivize pharmacists for providing patient care services as well⏎7 Id. Available at: https://www.federalregister.gov/d/2025-23641/p-25.⏎4⏎⏎as fund pharmacies, hospitals, and other entities to implement such technologies. Regarding⏎workforce integration, many health care providers, including pharmacists, lack sufficient⏎training and education on AI, leading them to be skeptical of its use. To address this knowledge⏎gap, HHS could provide guidance and training on the appropriate use of AI. Additionally, HHS⏎could work with schools of pharmacy to ensure that the next generation of pharmacists is⏎equipped with the knowledge to utilize AI-embedded technologies to optimize patient care.⏎Regarding patient perceptions, many patients have negative views of the rise of AI in health⏎care, fearing it could provide health care providers with inaccurate information or replace the⏎interaction they have with them.8 Because of this, health care providers avoid using it, as they⏎do not have to explain its use to their patients. As such, HHS must play an active role in⏎educating patients and the public about the benefits of AI-embedded technologies in health care⏎and how potential risks are mitigated to guarantee patient safety.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs⏎could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎As discussed above, there are many barriers to the broader adoption of AI in clinical care by⏎both health care providers and health care entities. HHS needs to not only address these⏎barriers but also incentivize use to support adoption. Incentivizing use could take many forms,⏎such as expanded funding, increased reimbursement, or workforce training. HHS's early⏎investment in adopting these technologies would be justified, as more accurate claims could⏎reduce fraud, waste, and abuse. Additionally, it could lead to better patient health outcomes⏎and a more satisfied health care workforce, as health care providers would spend more time⏎using their training to provide direct patient care services rather than completing administrative⏎tasks.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures ( e.g., relating to⏎liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and⏎what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The use of AI has yielded and will continue to raise novel legal issues related to liability,⏎indemnification, privacy, and security.9 The potential of these issues alone has been enough to⏎8 Alec Tyson, et al., 60% of Americans Would Be Uncomfortable With Provider Relying on AI in Their Own⏎Health Care, Pew Research Center (Feb. 22, 2023). Available at:⏎https://www.pewresearch.org/science/2023/02/22/60-of-americans-would-be-uncomfortable-with-⏎provider-relying-on-ai-in-their-own-health-care/.⏎9 See Nithesh Naik, et al., Legal and Ethical Consideration in Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: Who Takes⏎Responsibility?, 9 Frontiers in Surgery 1 (2023). Available at:⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8963864/. See also Clara Cestonaro, et al., Defining Medical⏎Liability When Artificial Intelligence Is Applied on Diagnostic Algorithms: A Systematic Review, 10 Frontiers in⏎5⏎⏎keep certain entities and health care providers from utilizing any AI-embedded technologies in⏎their practice. As such, HHS will have to address these concerns to support greater adoption of⏎these technologies. APhA encourages HHS to provide additional guidance and standards for⏎the use of AI to better inform pharmacists, other health care providers, and those working in⏎health care entities about appropriate use. HHS could also partner with professional health care⏎associations, such as APhA, to accredit and certify AI-embedded technologies and their⏎providers through educational training to ensure users know they are accurate and properly⏎vetted.⏎To support the adoption of AI in non-clinical areas, HHS could also consider using its⏎enforcement discretion to ease penalties for errors that do not involve patient health or patient⏎information. For example, by recognizing the benefits of AI-embedded technologies for⏎administrative tasks such as billing, HHS could reduce fines or penalties associated with errors⏎in the early stages of adoption.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven⏎testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎As mentioned above, pharmacists, pharmacy technicians, other pharmacy personnel, other⏎health care providers, and those working for health care entities all need to know how to use AI⏎appropriately and which AI-embedded technologies are safe for their roles. HHS can accredit⏎and certify AI-embedded technologies and their providers, giving health care providers and⏎entities confidence that these technologies have been appropriately tested. As such, APhA⏎encourages HHS to work with the private sector, such as APhA, to ensure that the products⏎used to provide care by health care providers, such as pharmacists, and the processes associated⏎with pharmacy and pharmacy services claims are appropriately reviewed. The more ways HHS⏎can assure pharmacists and other health care providers of the standards for using these⏎technologies and of how associated risks have been mitigated, the greater the trust they will⏎have in using them.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and⏎where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve⏎health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎There are many areas where AI algorithms and technologies have been embedded into⏎pharmacy practice to automate tasks, improve clinical decision-making, and promote patient⏎Medicine 1 (2023). Available at:⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10711067/#:~:text=Absence%20of%20clinical%20AI%20related,⏎AI%2Ddeveloper's%20liability%20is%20unclear.⏎6⏎⏎engagement.10 These technologies are used to assist with prescription verification, dosing of⏎high-risk medications, processing handwritten and faxed prescriptions, adherence monitoring,⏎and outreach to patients about refills and appointment scheduling. As the adoption of AI⏎expands into pharmacy practice, APhA opposes replacing a pharmacist’s professional judgment⏎or a patient’s access to their pharmacist with AI. APhA calls on HHS to develop regulations,⏎standards, and policies to promote the use of AI while ensuring patients still have access to the⏎trusted advice and care provided by their local pharmacist.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the most⏎influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Federal agencies, including HHS, should work with state boards of pharmacy to ensure that AI⏎is properly integrated into pharmacies and into the care pharmacists provide. Boards of⏎pharmacy play a significant role in outlining the scope of practice for pharmacists and⏎pharmacy technicians, as well as the corresponding legal boundaries, which could affect the use⏎of AI-embedded technologies by those working in pharmacies. Boards of pharmacy also often⏎license these individuals and may require continuing education to maintain pharmacist⏎licensure. HHS should partner with state boards of pharmacy to ensure that all pharmacists and⏎pharmacy technicians receive training on AI-embedded technologies, their appropriate uses,⏎and potential risks. APhA encourages HHS to consider the interplay between potential AI⏎regulations and state scope-of-practice laws to ensure that pharmacists can judiciously use AI in⏎their practice while complying with all applicable state and federal laws and regulations.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the⏎development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking⏎tools.⏎By enhancing interoperability, pharmacists, pharmacy technicians, and pharmacy personnel⏎will have greater access to the patient’s electronic health record. This not only provides⏎pharmacists with additional medical information to deliver better patient care but also gives⏎other health care providers greater insight into the patient’s medication history. One study⏎found that patients reported 43% of medications in their electronic health records were⏎inaccurate, with 29% discontinued and 14% changed.11 Greater access to these records will help⏎10 Craig Kimble, et al., Intelligent Pharmacy Leveraging AI and Automation to Enhance Patient Care and⏎Pharmacist Roles, 14 Pharmacy Times 1 (Sept. 19, 2025). Available at:⏎https://www.pharmacytimes.com/view/intelligent-pharmacy-leveraging-ai-and-automation-to-enhance-⏎patient-care-and-pharmacist-⏎roles#:~:text=AI%20and%20Automation%20in%20Today's,errors%20and%20enabling%20faster%20turnar⏎ound..⏎11 Maria Staroselsky, et al., An Effort to Improve Electronic Health Record Medication List Accuracy Between⏎Visits: Patients’ and Physicians’ Response, 77 International Journal of Medical Informatics 153 (2008).⏎7⏎⏎both prescribers and pharmacists avoid duplicate therapies, address adherence concerns,⏎minimize adverse reactions, and ensure the medications a patient is taking correspond to an⏎appropriate condition or disease state. APhA supports efforts to enhance interoperability,⏎allowing pharmacists to access electronic health information from other health care providers⏎and to share patient health information collected at the pharmacy with them. As a reminder,⏎APhA commented on ONC's proposed rule, “Health Data, Technology, and⏎Interoperability: Patient Engagement, Information Sharing, and Public Health Interoperability.”⏎Within this proposed rule, ONC moved to incorporate the Public Health Service Act (PHSA)⏎section 3000 definition of “pharmacist” into the definition of “health care provider” for⏎information-blocking purposes. APhA “commend[ed] ONC for taking steps to enhance the⏎interoperability of EHI amongst health care providers” and “support[ed] pharmacists being⏎recognized as providers in relation to information-blocking regulations discussed in the⏎proposed rule as long as pharmacists also have access to EHI to ensure the delivery of⏎pharmacist-provided patient care services [emphasis added].” APhA also requested ONC to⏎require other health care providers and developers of health IT certified under the final rules to⏎be prohibited from information blocking EHI to pharmacists and penalize developers for doing⏎so. APhA reiterates these points as it is urgent that pharmacists gain real-time access to essential⏎electronic health information of their patients because information blocking causes delays in⏎patient care and prevents health care providers from having a complete picture of the patient’s⏎health, which can lead to inefficient and ineffective care.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers are concerned that greater adoption of AI will reduce their interactions⏎with trusted pharmacists and other health care providers.12 Pharmacists are consistently rated⏎among the most trusted professionals, and millions of patients rely on their expertise each year⏎to manage their conditions.13 Given the accessibility of pharmacies, pharmacists are often⏎viewed as the first line of care for patients with minor ailments and conditions, especially in⏎rural and underserved areas, where pharmacists may be the only readily available health care⏎provider. As such, APhA urges HHS to ensure that the adoption of AI does not interfere with a⏎patient’s access to their pharmacist. Ideally, the adoption of AI enables pharmacists to provide⏎Available at:⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1386505607000470#:~:text=This%20study%20confir⏎ms%20earlier%20reports,and%2014%25%20having%20been%20changed.⏎12 Alec Tyson, et al., 60% of Americans Would Be Uncomfortable With Provider Relying on AI in Their Own⏎Health Care, Pew Research Center (Feb. 22, 2023). Available at:⏎https://www.pewresearch.org/science/2023/02/22/60-of-americans-would-be-uncomfortable-with-⏎provider-relying-on-ai-in-their-own-health-care/.⏎13 Megan Brenan, Nurses Continue to Lead in Honesty and Ethics Ratings, Gallup (Jan. 12, 2026). Available at:⏎https://news.gallup.com/poll/700736/nurses-continue-lead-honesty-ethics-ratings.aspx.⏎8⏎⏎more direct patient care without compromising patient safety or the quality of their interactions⏎with patients.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to submit comments on this notice. If you have any questions or⏎would like to meet with APhA and our nation’s pharmacists, please contact Corey Whetzel,⏎APhA’s Senior Manager, Regulatory Affairs, at cwhetzel@aphanet.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Michael Baxter⏎Vice President, Government Affairs⏎9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0144♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from National Alliance for Care at Home, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔National Alliance for Care at Home♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎VIA ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Posnack:⏎The National Alliance for Care at Home (the Alliance) appreciates the opportunity to⏎comment on the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) Request for⏎Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care.⏎The Alliance is the unified voice for providers delivering high-quality, person-centered⏎healthcare to individuals, wherever they call home. Our members are providers of different⏎sizes and types—from small rural agencies to large national companies—including⏎government-based providers, nonprofit organizations, systems-based entities, and public⏎corporations. Our members, including over 1,500 providers representing 10,000 offices⏎and locations, serve over 4 million patients nationwide through a dedicated workforce of⏎over 1 million employees, staff, and volunteers. The Alliance is dedicated to advancing⏎policies that support care in the home for millions of Americans at all stages of life,⏎individuals with disabilities, those with chronic and serious illnesses, and Americans at the⏎end of life who depend on those supports.⏎We thank the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator⏎for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC)’s efforts to identify barriers to private⏎sector AI innovation and opportunities to improve regulations, policies, and programs that⏎can promote appropriate and effective use of AI in care delivery for vulnerable patients.⏎228 SEVENTH STREET, SE, WASHINGTON, DC 20003 • 1-800-646-6460⏎⏎At the same time, we offer comments to highlight the important role that care at home⏎plays in our healthcare ecosystem, and important considerations relevant to AI use and⏎innovation in this setting, informed through feedback from our members.⏎Discussion⏎The Home is a Distinct Care Setting⏎Home-based care is an essential part of our healthcare continuum, serving millions of⏎patients at every stage of life in the setting they trust most. This care encompasses a broad⏎continuum of services, including home health, hospice, palliative care, home care,⏎Medicaid home and community-based services (HCBS), and pediatric care. The demand⏎for these services continues to grow. By 2030, one in five Americans will be age 65 or older,⏎and 87 percent of older adults express a strong preference to age in their own homes.1⏎Care at home can support enhanced independence,2 reduce mortality and hospital⏎readmission rates and generate cost savings,3 and improve quality of life.4 As HHS⏎considers how best to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, it is important that home-⏎based care be an important part of the Department’s strategy.⏎The home is a fundamentally different care environment than hospitals, clinics, or⏎inpatient facilities, and HHS should evaluate opportunities to promote AI with that⏎distinction in mind. Clinicians delivering care in the home operate with a level of autonomy⏎and variability that differs markedly from hospital or facility-based practice. It is important⏎to note that in the home setting, there is no controlled environment or inpatient⏎infrastructure, and often limited broadband connectivity, particularly in rural areas.⏎The Alliance recognizes that AI holds significant promise for clinical settings if developed⏎and deployed appropriately. At the same time, there are important considerations for⏎home-based care. For example, ambient listening tools, which can be used to support⏎clinical documentation, raise particular concerns in the home, where visits frequently⏎occur in the presence of family members, caregivers, neighbors, and other individuals who⏎are not the patient. Concerns about who must provide consent for AI-enabled recording,⏎what data is captured from non-patients nearby, and how that information is handled⏎remain unresolved and raise risks for providers. Accordingly, we encourage HHS to⏎1 See Geng F, McGarry BE, Rosenthal MB, Zubizarreta JR, Resch SC, Grabowski DC. Preferences for Postacute Care at⏎Home vs Facilities. JAMA Health Forum. 2024;5(4):e240678. 10.1001/jamahealthforum.2024.0678⏎2 See, e.g., Boland L, Légaré F, Perez M, et al. Impact of home care versus alternative locations of care on elder⏎health outcomes: an overview of systematic reviews. BMC Geriatrics, 2017;17⏎3 See, e.g., Xiao R, Miller JA, Zafirau WJ, Gorodeski EZ, Young JB. Impact of Home Health Care on Health Care⏎Resource Utilization Following Hospital Discharge: A Cohort Study. Am J Med. 2018 Apr;131(4):395-407.e35. doi:⏎10.1016/j.amjmed.2017.11.010⏎4 See, e.g., Tay YL, Abu Bakar NS, Tumiran R. et al. Effects of home visits on quality of life among older adults: a⏎systematic review protocol. Syst Rev, 2021; 10(307)⏎Page 2 of 8⏎⏎recognize these distinctions and account for the realities of care delivery in the home as⏎the Department moves forward in its efforts to leverage AI in clinical care.⏎Individuals we serve also report concerns about the privacy of health information when it is⏎provided to an AI model. Given the constant learning and recalibration inherent in much AI⏎infrastructure, information provided for model interpretation and response is often added⏎to the universe of data that the AI uses to formulate future responses. HHS must ensure⏎that such functionality is disallowed when AI is used as a component of information⏎systems with access to protected health information. Similarly, all AI systems in use⏎should be HIPAA compliant and meet every standard for data integrity, encryption, storage,⏎and transmission.⏎We also encourage HHS to support the development of benchmarking tools that would⏎allow providers to evaluate AI performance in home-based care settings. AI models trained⏎primarily on hospital or clinic data may not perform reliably when applied to home health,⏎hospice, or HCBS populations. Tools that account for these unique populations and care⏎delivery models would give providers greater confidence in AI tool selection, and help AI⏎developers build more effective products for this setting.⏎Barriers to AI Adoption in Home-based Care Settings⏎There are several significant barriers our members face in adopting and innovating with AI⏎in home-based care settings. These barriers include, but are not limited to, cost and⏎resource constraints, workforce readiness, technology integration, and regulatory and⏎legal uncertainty.⏎The cost of AI adoption remains one of the most commonly cited barriers among our⏎members. Providers delivering care in the home, including home health, hospice, Medicaid⏎home and community-based services (HCBS), palliative care, and home care, face⏎significant financial barriers in evaluating, customizing, and implementing AI tools. The⏎costs extend beyond the technology itself and include staffing required to manage⏎implementation, the clinical staff time needed for training and oversight, and the legal and⏎compliance guidance and resources necessary to assess and manage risk.⏎In addition, our members report that a lack of staff with the necessary skillsets to manage⏎AI implementation and ongoing oversight is a significant barrier to AI adoption. Adoption of⏎AI tools requires not only technical expertise but additional training to ensure responsible⏎and appropriate AI use in clinical care. Providers need resources and guidance to build⏎capacity for training, monitoring, and auditing AI use. Accordingly, the Alliance urges HHS⏎to identify and evaluate how it can support workforce development to promote AI use in⏎clinical settings.⏎Page 3 of 8⏎⏎Significant concerns have also been raised about the legal risks associated with AI⏎adoption. Providers face uncertainty regarding how they will be held accountable for errors⏎generated by AI tools, including issues of algorithmic bias, ambient listening, inaccurate⏎outputs, and data security. Smaller providers in particular lack the resources to⏎independently evaluate vendor claims about data protection, the security of data flowing⏎to large language models, and compliance with the Health Insurance Portability and⏎Accountability Act (HIPAA) of 1996 and data protection. Recent litigation underscores the⏎legal risks our members face,5 which contributes to this environment of uncertainty.⏎Further, the Alliance is concerned about the use of AI tools to deny or limit coverage for⏎healthcare services, particularly in the home.6 Even with human oversight, AI tools may⏎have undue influence on reviewers in coverage decisions, as clinicians may face pressure⏎to affirm recommendations made by AI. Accordingly, HHS should evaluate the need for⏎guardrails around AI use in the insurance market to ensure that AI can be leveraged to⏎improve efficiency in medical reviews—not as a blunt tool that can inhibit patient access⏎to services in the home.⏎As noted above, consent to AI use is essential to its ethical use. Compounding this⏎challenge is the current patchwork of laws governing AI use, data collection, and consent⏎requirements. Members noted that this inconsistency across jurisdictions makes it⏎difficult for providers, particularly those operating in multiple states, to use AI tools and⏎develop policies with confidence and comply with all applicable requirements.⏎Accordingly, the Alliance encourages HHS to work in partnership with the states to work⏎toward a standard for responsible AI use that would provide clarity for providers operating⏎across state lines.⏎Opportunities for Regulatory and Payment Policy Refinements⏎HHS should prioritize payment policies that create direct financial pathways for AI⏎adoption. One promising approach could be the development of new payment codes or⏎enhanced payment rates for clinical services that demonstrably incorporate AI-assisted⏎care, similar to how CMS has historically used add-on payments to encourage adoption of⏎5 See, e.g., Saucedo v. Sharp HealthCare, No. 25CU063632C (Cal. Super. Ct., San Diego Cnty. Nov. 26, 2025)⏎(proposed class action alleging the use of ambient AI without obtaining patient consent); Lisota v. Heartland⏎Dental, LLC, No. 25 CV 7518, 2026 WL _______, at *1–9 (N.D. Ill. Jan. 13, 2026) (granting defendants' motions to⏎dismiss without prejudice a Federal Wiretap Act claim challenging an AI-powered telephone service's real-time⏎transcription and analysis of patient calls).⏎6 See, e.g., Doe v. Cigna Health & Life Ins. Co., No. 2:23-cv-01355 (E.D. Cal. July 24, 2023) (alleging that an insurer⏎used an algorithm to automatically deny thousands of patient claims without individualized physician review as⏎required by state law).⏎Page 4 of 8⏎⏎beneficial technologies and care models. Alternatively, CMS Innovation Center models⏎could be designed to test value-based payment arrangements that reward providers who⏎leverage AI to improve outcomes and reduce costs, creating a business case for adoption⏎that does not rely solely on upfront grants or subsidies. These approaches would lower the⏎barrier to entry for providers who recognize AI's clinical value but lack the capital to absorb⏎upfront implementation expenses, and would be especially meaningful for smaller and⏎community-based providers who operate on thin margins and cannot self-finance⏎technology transformation.⏎Equally important is ensuring that home and community-based care settings are not left⏎behind in these incentive structures. Unlike hospitals and physician groups that received⏎financial incentives under the Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical⏎Health (HITECH) Act7 to adopt electronic health records and build interoperable systems,⏎home health and hospice providers were largely excluded from these incentives. As a⏎result, these providers are continuing to play catch-up, at a time when making the leap to⏎AI adoption will come with its own costs and resource constraints. HHS must be deliberate⏎in designing AI incentive programs that explicitly include home-based care, personal care,⏎and other non-institutional settings. Any new grant programs, payment adjustments, or⏎adoption incentives should include provisions that extend to these providers, recognizing⏎that a significant and growing portion of care, particularly for elderly and disabled⏎populations, occurs in the home.⏎On the question of standards and oversight, establishing formal AI accreditation or⏎certification requirements at this stage of the technology's development may be⏎premature, but some foundational framework is nonetheless needed. HHS should⏎prioritize developing standards that allow providers, payers, and regulators to evaluate AI⏎tools against consistent, transparent criteria. This intermediate step would help establish⏎accountability without stifling innovation. These standards could address clinical⏎accuracy, bias, data integrity, and outcomes impact, giving the health care sector clearer⏎signals about what constitutes responsible AI deployment.⏎Guidance and Oversight⏎HHS can best support private sector activities by providing clear guidance on permissible⏎uses and by establishing clear accountability for potential harms that may result from the⏎use of this technology. HHS can provide minimum standards for AI implementation as well⏎as certification of accreditation agencies to establish minimum standards. Providers and⏎patients need guardrails to assure safety, efficacy, and risk mitigation with clear⏎assignment of liability. Providers and provider organizations will almost always be⏎consumers of AI applications, and there is a great desire to make better use of the⏎7 Pub. L. 111-5⏎Page 5 of 8⏎⏎mountains of data captured in medical coding. Further, use of AI embedded into EHRs and⏎other technologies already in use may not be a choice by providers.⏎HHS can also support providers by having well-funded, minimally burdensome, and⏎meaningful oversight. AI adoption in the medical industry is in a “wild west” and the⏎technology has progressed in use and utility beyond the current regulatory paradigm. To⏎adequately provide regulatory oversight, HHS would need to have regulators that are able⏎to determine whether AI models are designed and behaving safely, and there must be⏎enough regulators to address a large and growing industry, at least inasmuch as AI⏎becomes embedded in many other IT tools.⏎Providers need specific, clear guidance to fully account for the risks they may be taking⏎when they use AI products, particularly as it may affect or intersect with CMS regulatory⏎and subregulatory guidance. These products may affect billing, coding, notetaking, route⏎planning, and so on – any given product may affect a variety of different concerns, whether⏎it is data security of medical records or consent of everyone who may be in the room when⏎an ambient notetaking AI is working. Each different AI product – or product embedded in an⏎existing app – carries a potential set of liabilities that are not yet tested in the courts.⏎Consistent with the Department’s goal of reducing uncertainty in the wake of AI innovation,⏎the Alliance urges HHS to pursue clear, prospective regulatory guidance that promotes the⏎use of AI in ways that benefit patients, improve efficiencies, and are minimally⏎burdensome on providers.⏎Interoperability and Technology Infrastructure⏎Interoperability remains a foundational prerequisite for effective AI use, and HHS should⏎revisit existing interoperability regulations and programs to strengthen incentives for data⏎sharing and integration. Grant programs under HRSA, CMS Innovation Center models, and⏎other HHS programmatic levers should be designed to fund not just AI tools themselves,⏎but the interoperability infrastructure that makes them viable and with particular attention⏎to ensuring home health, home care, and hospice providers are eligible recipients.⏎Indeed, AI tools are only as effective as the data they can access, and fragmented, siloed⏎health data undermines their clinical utility.⏎Enhanced interoperability is essential to realizing the potential of AI in home-based clinical⏎care. There are opportunities for care coordination with real-time data, real⏎communication between provider IT systems to reduce duplicate tests, referral systems⏎improvements, and more but none of them are possible without real cross-sector and⏎intra-sector interoperability.⏎Without meaningful progress on interoperability, innovative AI applications will remain out⏎of reach for much of the care-at-home sector. The Alliance urges HHS to prioritize⏎Page 6 of 8⏎⏎interoperability investments and standards that include home health and hospice⏎providers.⏎As noted above, home health and hospice providers were largely excluded from the EHR⏎adoption incentives made available to hospitals and eligible professionals under the⏎HITECH Act and subsequent programs. This exclusion has left our members at a significant⏎technological disadvantage. The Alliance urges HHS to ensure that any interoperability⏎initiatives or incentive programs related to AI adoption include home health and hospice in⏎the mix of participants. Without inclusion, these providers risk being left out of an emerging⏎AI ecosystem altogether, further widening the digital divide in home-based care settings.⏎Indeed, our members have emphasized that a common barrier to AI deployment is the⏎inability of many AI products to integrate effectively with their EMR platforms. Accordingly,⏎we recommend HHS partner with AI and EMR vendors to consider solutions to ease⏎seamless integration, and promote uptake instead of adopting costly, resource-intensive⏎custom builds.⏎Opportunities for Research⏎Opportunities for AI in home care could benefit from significant research funding which⏎focuses on understanding, among other ideas, the uniqueness of providing care in the⏎home. As noted previously, the home as a site of care differs significantly from institutional⏎settings and provides both opportunities and significant barriers to AI implementation.⏎Studies which specifically address the nature of providing care outside of an institutional⏎setting can accelerate that adoption by spurring investment in technologies specifically⏎designed for patients receiving care at home.⏎Patient safety is another top area which should be prioritized for research funding.⏎Providers and patients continue to express concerns about privacy and patient information⏎within the confines of AI usage. Further study on the safest ways to implement AI is critical⏎before widescale adoption.⏎Additional areas of research which should be prioritized include potential efficiencies for⏎the home care workforce in clinical care, with a focus on not creating additional burden.⏎This may include AI applications for remote patient monitoring, documentation assistance⏎for clinicians, and more.⏎Currently, limited research on AI implementation in care in the home exists, and few⏎studies have been published within the past year. The Alliance encourages HHS to invest⏎more funding for research into AI in home health, home care, and hospice. This will help⏎drive adoption and level the playing field for home care with evidence-based technologies.⏎Page 7 of 8⏎⏎Conclusion⏎We appreciate your consideration of our comments. The Alliance stands ready to partner⏎with HHS to promote the appropriate use of AI to improve patient lives in the home. If you⏎have any questions, your staff should feel free to contact Patrick Harrison, vice president,⏎public policy, at pharrison@allianceforcareathome.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Scott Levy⏎Chief Government Affairs Officer⏎National Alliance for Care at Home⏎Page 8 of 8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0145♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from G, J, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔J♔G♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Federal Regulation Is Essential⏎⏎There is currently no comprehensive federal regulatory framework governing AI in healthcare. This regulatory gap poses significant risks to patients. Please collaborate with clinicians to discuss the best way to move forward. Confidentiality may be at risk, which is an important component of psychotherapy. ⏎⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0146♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dorothy♔Davis♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My doctor of 22 years, Dr. Harlan Webb out of Edmond, Oklahoma, retired in March 2023. He told me flat out that the paperwork had become unbearable. He was spending more time clicking through screens than looking at his patients. And now HHS wants to add more technology into that same system without first asking whether the people using it have any breathing room to actually think?⏎⏎That's what gets me about all of this. The push to get AI into clinics faster sounds good on paper but nobody seems to be asking whether physicians are already so buried in administrative requirements that adding another layer of automated alerts and flags is just going to make things worse. Dr. Webb wasn't a technophobe. He just ran out of hours in the day.⏎⏎I'm not against the technology itself, I want to be fair about that. But faster doesn't always mean better. My sister had eight months of appointments where everything looked fine on paper before someone finally slowed down enough to catch what was really happening. I worry that if AI is flagging a hundred things and physicians are already overwhelmed, the one thing that actually matters gets lost in the noise same as it did for her.⏎⏎What I would really like to see HHS address is the administrative burden question before anything else. Or at least alongside everything else. Because if the answer to physician burnout is just more software, I don't think that's actually solving the problem. Dr. Webb would have told you the same thing. He filled out the same prior authorization forms three or four times for the same patient sometimes and nobody fixed that. Are any of these AI tools going to fix that, or are they just being added on top of it?⏎⏎I genuinely don't know enough about how these systems work to comment on the technical certification side of things. But I do know that when I take my students on a field trip I have to fill out seventeen different forms and none of them talk to each other, and I suspect the healthcare version of that problem is ten times worse. The solution to too much paperwork is not usually more technology. Sometimes it is but not always.⏎⏎Anyway I hope someone at HHS is actually reading these. I submitted a comment on a different rule two years ago and never heard anything back, not even an acknowledgment, which I understand is how it works but it still feels like shouting into a room where nobody is listening.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0147♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Jones♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔HeartFlow♔♔♔♔♔♔My honest reaction to this RFI is that it's addressing symptoms while the actual disease goes completely untreated. I've spent the last several years at HeartFlow watching our AI-assisted coronary analysis tools get stuck in payer limbo, and yes, that's a real problem worth solving. But what nobody wants to talk about in these comment periods is where the money is actually going.⏎⏎Pharmacy benefit managers. That's where it's going.⏎⏎We did a rough internal analysis in 2023 looking at why certain health systems in the Pacific Northwest were telling us they couldn't budget for AI decision support tools despite genuine clinical interest from their cardiology departments. And when you actually dig into where their margins had gone, PBMs kept coming up. These middlemen are extracting billions from the system and HHS keeps putting out RFIs about AI adoption like that's somehow disconnected from the resource constraints that make adoption impossible in the first place. At Providence St. Joseph in Renton, one of our contacts there told me flat out that their CFO had frozen discretionary tech spending because their pharmacy costs had ballooned something like 34% over two years. That's not an AI problem. That's a PBM problem.⏎⏎I want to be fair here - there are things in this RFI worth responding to. The reimbursement pathway question is real. We have genuinely spent months, sometimes close to five months, waiting for payers to figure out how to classify our FFR-CT analysis because nobody can agree on whether it's a "device" output or a "clinical decision support" output. That distinction matters enormously for billing and it matters for liability. I get that.⏎⏎But I keep coming back to the fact that you can streamline every reimbursement code and fix every liability classification question and health systems are still going to be cash-strapped because PBMs are sitting in the middle of the drug supply chain skimming money that could otherwise fund exactly the kind of AI infrastructure HHS says it wants to see built out. There's no section in this RFI that even gestures at that problem. Not one question.⏎⏎I'm in Alaska part of the time and I can tell you that rural and frontier health systems here are not going to benefit from accelerated AI adoption frameworks if the underlying economics don't change. Providence Alaska Medical Center is doing what it can but the margin pressures are real and they're not primarily driven by the regulatory friction this RFI is focused on.⏎⏎I'd encourage HHS to at minimum acknowledge in any final action document that AI adoption capacity is downstream of overall health system financial health, which is itself being degraded by intermediary rent-seeking that the Department has tools to address but hasn't used aggressively. The AI governance questions are worth answering. I just think the framing here is too narrow and will produce recommendations that help at the margins while the core problem♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0148♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from InterSystems Corporation, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔InterSystems Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔See attached file(s)⏎⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎in collaboration with the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and the⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Request for Information (RFI)⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Submitted via Regulations.gov⏎Prepared by: InterSystems⏎Points of Contact: Zach Keating | pr@intersystems.com⏎InterSystems appreciates HHS’s Request for Information and the Department’s intent to use regulation, reimbursement, and research & development levers to accelerate adoption and use of AI in clinical care. We agree with HHS that progress requires a forward-leaning, industry-supportive approach that also preserves privacy, confidentiality of identifiable health information, and public trust. ⏎AI adoption at scale will depend on interoperability as infrastructure: consistent access to high-quality, standardized data; predictable security and governance; and repeatable implementation patterns across settings and vendors. As an interoperability platform vendor operating across heterogeneous environments, InterSystems recommends HHS prioritize actions that (1) expand discrete, standards-based data access for core clinical workflows and public health/quality use cases, (2) reduce variability and integration burden, and (3) strengthen trust through security, provenance, and auditability.⏎HHS indicates it seeks a regulatory posture that enables innovation while protecting patients and the confidentiality of identifiable health information and maintaining public trust. InterSystems recommends HHS prioritize clarity in three areas:⏎•	Accountability and auditability for AI in clinical workflows: Encourage requirements for logging and traceability (inputs, outputs, downstream actions) appropriate to clinical risk.⏎•	Provenance expectations: Promote use of provenance/attribution so clinicians and organizations can understand what data informed AI outputs.⏎•	Interoperability guardrails for AI integration: Avoid creating “AI-only” proprietary side channels for clinical context exchange. AI integration should remain anchored to core interoperability standards and established security controls.⏎InterSystems appreciates the opportunity to respond to this RFI and supports HHS’s objective to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care using regulation, reimbursement, and R&D levers. InterSystems encourages HHS to treat interoperability as the enabling infrastructure for AI - pairing standards-based data access with strong security, provenance, auditability, and practical implementation guidance so AI can be deployed safely and consistently across heterogeneous healthcare environments.⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0149♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American College of Emergency Physicians, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Emergency Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎On behalf of the American College of Emergency Physicians (ACEP) and our nearly 40,000⏎members, thank you for the opportunity to respond to the “Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care” request for information (RFI).⏎Emergency departments serve as a critical entry point to the health care system, operate in⏎high-acuity and time-sensitive environments, and have already become a testing ground for⏎a wide range of artificial intelligence-driven (AI) tools. Accordingly, emergency medicine is⏎an essential lens through which to examine how AI adoption and utilization can supplement⏎the delivery of high-quality, lifesaving emergency care.⏎ACEP supports responsible acceleration of AI adoption in clinical care, provided that federal⏎policy ensures (1) meaningful clinician oversight, (2) clear accountability frameworks, (3)⏎evidence-based evaluation standards, and (4) transparency in development and deployment.⏎ACEP's emergency physician members, including those working in academic medical⏎centers, community hospitals, and safety-net settings, are encountering AI tools in their daily⏎practice with increasing frequency, often without having meaningfully participated in the⏎decisions to adopt those tools. Our response to this RFI is informed by input gathered from⏎emergency medicine clinicians and health system informaticists and is intended to describe⏎the landscape of uncertainty, concern, and cautious optimism that characterizes emergency⏎medicine's current relationship with clinical AI. Our comments seek to articulate the key⏎areas where clarity is most urgently needed, and to express ACEP's willingness to serve as a⏎partner to HHS as it works to develop a coherent, equitable, and evidence-based framework⏎for AI in clinical care.⏎I. Emergency Medicine as a Distinct Clinical Environment⏎We want to underscore for HHS that emergency medicine presents a set of conditions that⏎make it both a high-priority area for AI development and a setting in which the consequences⏎of poorly designed or poorly deployed AI are particularly acute. Emergency departments⏎manage an unselected patient population, operate continuously, and are frequently under⏎conditions of crowding, resource constraint, and time pressure. These factors affect both the⏎types of AI tools that are most likely to be beneficial and the ways in which AI failures are⏎likely to manifest.⏎Our members have observed that AI tools which show genuine promise in the emergency⏎department, including documentation assistance, radiology support for critical finding⏎detection, and tools to support patient triage, tend to be those that are designed with a clear⏎understanding of how emergency care is actually delivered.⏎⏎Conversely, tools designed for elective or inpatient settings, or that generate frequent low-value alerts, tend to create⏎more burden than benefit. We are also attentive to a concern raised by members regarding the appropriate role of⏎AI in the emergency department: there is a risk that AI tools will be deployed to manage or obscure systemic⏎problems, such as crowding, boarding, and staffing shortages, rather than to improve the quality of care delivered⏎within those constraints. AI adoption in emergency medicine should be problem-driven and tied to evidence of⏎genuine improvement in patient outcomes or clinician experience, not used as a technological substitute for⏎addressing structural challenges in emergency care delivery.⏎II. Liability and Accountability: A Critical Area of Uncertainty⏎Our members have described a troubling bind: following an AI recommendation that proves incorrect may give rise⏎to claims of automation bias or over dependence on technology, while ignoring a recommendation that turns out to⏎have been correct may expose the clinician to allegations of negligence for failing to use available clinical decision⏎support. This tension places the entire burden of risk on the clinician, despite the fact that emergency physicians⏎typically have no role in selecting, procuring, validating, or configuring the AI tools they are expected to use.⏎Several specific areas of uncertainty warrant attention:⏎• There is currently no established standard of care that defines when a clinician is expected to follow, document⏎disagreement with, or override an AI-generated recommendation.⏎• The question of who bears liability when AI is involved including developers, health systems, or individual⏎clinicians has not been resolved, and existing malpractice frameworks were not designed to accommodate this⏎complexity.⏎• When health systems deploy AI tools that have not been validated against local patient populations, it is⏎unclear whether and to what degree the institution bears responsibility for downstream patient harm.⏎• The liability question extends well beyond FDA-regulated medical devices to encompass ambient listening⏎tools, natural language processing applications, clinical decision support systems, and other non-device AI⏎categories where governance is especially underdeveloped.⏎ACEP does not prescribe a particular liability model in this response, but we believe that addressing this uncertainty⏎is prerequisite to responsible AI adoption. We are willing to engage with HHS/ONC and relevant stakeholders to⏎examine what a workable framework might look like, and to share the perspectives of our frontline members who⏎are navigating this uncertainty daily.⏎III. Governance and Physician Involvement in AI Decision-Making⏎A recurring and frustrating theme in feedback from our members is that emergency physicians are rarely⏎meaningfully involved in decisions about which AI tools are adopted in their departments. It is ACEP’s position⏎that artificial intelligence algorithms used in health care decision-making must be transparent, accountable, and⏎subject to oversight by qualified health care professionals. Yet technology procurement decisions are most⏎commonly driven by institutional IT departments, finance leadership, and hospital administrators, with clinical input⏎typically added late in the process and given limited weight.⏎This is not simply a matter of professional preference. Emergency medicine's care environment is distinctive: it is⏎characterized by high patient volume, diagnostic uncertainty, time pressure, and a patient population that skews⏎toward complexity and acuity. AI tools that perform well in other settings frequently underperform or create new⏎problems when deployed in the emergency department without ED-specific validation. When the clinicians who will⏎actually use a tool are not involved in evaluating it, the likelihood of poor fit, and the downstream risks to patient⏎safety, increases substantially.⏎Our members have raised concerns that mirror those expressed about electronic health record adoption: systems⏎are selected and implemented with inadequate clinical input, and frontline physicians are left to adapt to tools that⏎do not reflect how emergency medicine is actually practiced. We believe HHS has an opportunity to address this⏎structural gap through guidance on post deployment monitoring processes.⏎⏎IV. Reimbursement and Economic Alignment⏎Emergency physicians and health system leaders have described a structural economic misalignment in how AI⏎adoption is currently financed. The costs and effort associated with implementing, validating, training staff on, and⏎maintaining AI tools are frequently absorbed by clinical departments, while the financial benefits such as reduced⏎downstream costs, billing efficiencies, system-level savings accrue to health systems, payers, or vendors. This⏎imbalance creates a disincentive for departments to invest in thoughtful, resource-intensive adoption and contributes⏎to burnout risk when AI tools become additional unfunded mandates on already-stretched clinical teams.⏎Current reimbursement models were not designed with AI in mind, and do not adequately account for the full⏎lifecycle of responsible AI use: pre-deployment validation, staff training, integration support, governance overhead,⏎and ongoing performance monitoring. Our members have observed that where resources for these activities are not⏎explicitly allocated, they either do not occur or are performed inadequately thus creating patient safety risks that may⏎not be apparent until problems emerge in practice.⏎AI systems used in utilization management, prior authorization, and reimbursement determinations also materially⏎affect clinical decision-making and patient access to emergency care. ACEP policy supports prohibiting the use of⏎AI as the sole basis for denial, delay, or downcoding of care and, most importantly, supports requiring review by⏎qualified physicians in the relevant specialty.⏎V. Evaluation Standards and the Gap Between Vendor Claims and Real-World Performance⏎Our members have consistently described a gap between the performance metrics presented by AI vendors and the⏎real-world behavior of those tools once deployed in an emergency department. AI tools are typically developed and⏎evaluated on datasets that may not reflect the demographic makeup, data quality, workflow constraints, or clinical⏎complexity of the populations and settings in which they are eventually used. Emergency department data, in⏎particular, is often fragmented, entered under time pressure, and structured differently than the cleaner data used in⏎development environments.⏎This gap has real consequences. Tools that generate high rates of false-positive alerts, as has been reported with⏎some sepsis detection systems, create alert fatigue that extends to other clinical decision support tools, potentially⏎undermining trust in AI systems more broadly. Tools developed for use in other specialties and repurposed for the⏎emergency department often fail to perform as expected, and the health systems and clinicians bearing the⏎consequences of those failures have limited recourse.⏎There is currently no standard framework for how AI tools used in clinical care, particularly those that fall outside⏎FDA medical device regulation, should be evaluated before deployment or monitored after. Our members have⏎expressed interest in a more structured approach, though there are differing views about whether emergency⏎medicine requires a specialty-specific evaluation framework or whether existing standards for medical interventions⏎can be adapted. These are questions on which further dialogue would be valuable.⏎Areas where HHS guidance or investment could be particularly impactful include:⏎• Development of pre-deployment validation standards or guidelines for non-device clinical AI, with particular⏎attention to the importance of local validation against institution-specific patient populations.⏎• Support for ongoing post-deployment monitoring infrastructure, including mechanisms to identify and⏎respond to model drift over time.⏎• Investment in research capacity at community and mid-size health systems that currently lack the resources⏎to conduct independent AI validation.⏎• Promotion of transparency requirements that would allow health systems and clinicians to understand how⏎AI tools were developed, on what data, and what their known limitations are.⏎VI. ACEP's Commitment to Partnership⏎ACEP is committed to ensuring that AI is developed, deployed, and governed in ways that genuinely serve patients⏎and support the clinicians who care for them. We recognize that this is a rapidly evolving policy area and that⏎definitive answers to many of the questions raised in this letter are not yet available. Our goal in this response is not⏎⏎to foreclose options but to ensure that the perspectives of emergency medicine are part of the conversation as HHS⏎develops its approach.⏎We are prepared to:⏎• Participate in technical working groups, advisory panels, or stakeholder convenings convened by HHS on AI⏎in clinical care.⏎• Provide access to emergency physician members with direct experience developing, evaluating, and using AI⏎tools in clinical settings.⏎• Collaborate on the development of evaluation frameworks, governance standards, or other guidance⏎documents where emergency medicine input would add value.⏎• Share ongoing data and feedback from our membership as AI adoption in emergency medicine continues to⏎evolve.⏎We look forward to an ongoing dialogue and thank the Department for seeking input from the clinical community.⏎If you have any questions, please contact Annalane Miller, ACEP’s Manager of Regulatory and External Affairs, at⏎amiller1@acep.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎L. Anthony Cirillo, MD, FACEP⏎President⏎American College of Emergency Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0150♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lisa♔Walker♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Three years I've been going to Miss Dottie's house on Tilson Road in Decatur, and I know things about that woman that no chart will ever capture. I know that when her blood pressure is up she gets quiet in a particular way, not the regular quiet, a different kind. I know she'll tell me she's fine when she isn't. I know that the pain in her left hip makes her favor her right side when she walks to the kitchen and that if you're not watching you'd miss it entirely. None of that is in any database.⏎⏎She was misdiagnosed twice before I ever met her. Both times, from what her daughter Gloria told me, it was a situation where the doctor looked at the computer readout of her bloodwork and made a call without really sitting with her, without asking the right questions, without noticing that she was scared and not telling the full story of her symptoms because she didn't want to be a bother. She's 82 and Black and lives alone and she has learned over a lifetime not to make too much noise in a doctor's office. An algorithm is not going to account for that. It will see her numbers and it will not see her.⏎⏎I worry that what HHS is describing here, making it faster and easier to roll out these automated tools in clinical settings, is going in the wrong direction for patients like her. I understand there might be benefits somewhere, maybe for some things, I'm not saying technology is always wrong. But what I keep coming back to is the question of who is responsible when the tool gets it wrong. When a doctor looks you in the eye and makes a decision, that doctor is responsible. You can go back to that doctor. There is a person who has to answer for what happened to you. If a software program flags the wrong thing or misses the right thing, who does Miss Dottie's daughter call?⏎⏎I don't have a medical degree. I'm a home health aide. But I've watched this woman's health closely for going on three years and I can tell you that the two things she needs most from her medical care are someone who actually looks at her and someone who will be held accountable if something goes wrong. Those two things are connected. They are basically the same thing.⏎⏎The request for information mentions accelerating adoption. That word accelerating is what gives me pause. Slower might be better here. A lot slower. Because once the human gets removed from the decision, or even just moved to the side a little, it is very hard to put them back. And for a woman like Miss Dottie who has already been let down by a system that moved too fast and looked at too little, there is not a lot of margin for the technology to also get it wrong.⏎⏎I don't know what the right regulation looks like exactly, that's not my area. But I think any rule coming out of this process should require that a real clinician, a person, reviews and signs off on any AI-generated recommendation before it affects a patient's care. Not just technically reviews it, actually reviews it. There should be a way to know that happened and a way to trace back to that person if it didn't.⏎⏎Miss Dottie has a followup appointment at Emory Decatur Hospital sometime in October and I will be taking her. I'm going to sit in that room with her the same way I always do because I know that if I'm not there she will say she's fine.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0151♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Oracle Health and Life Sciences, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Oracle Health and Life Sciences♔♔♔♔♔♔8779 Hilcrest Road⏎Kansas City, MO 64138⏎Direct: (816) 201-1024⏎February 23, 2026⏎Submitted Electronically⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.,⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI; RIN 0955–AA13⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎Oracle Health & Life Sciences. (“Oracle Health”) appreciates this opportunity to comment on the⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (the “Department”) Request for Information1⏎(“RFI”) on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (“AI”) as Part of Clinical⏎Care. Oracle Health supports the Administration’s efforts to establish a forward-leaning,⏎industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of⏎clinical care.2 Oracle Health further supports the rapid adoption and use of AI in clinical care, to⏎foster public trust and confidence in modern technology solutions, to reduce uncertainty that⏎impedes AI innovation, and to align federal incentives so that AI is deployed in ways that⏎enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower healthcare costs, and improve health outcomes for⏎patients, caregivers, and communities.3⏎Oracle Health shares the Department’s commitment to U.S. leadership on AI and provides the⏎following recommendations based on its leading role in the healthcare AI ecosystem.⏎• The Department can streamline administrative hurdles to private sector innovation in AI⏎for healthcare by limiting regulation that might stifle innovation and ensuring alignment⏎of any regulations that impact AI in healthcare, prioritizing the development of publicly⏎available benchmark data sets for AI model evaluation, and updating standards for patient⏎data privacy and deidentification to incorporate AI-relevant provisions.⏎• The Department can help to align payment incentives with the adoption and use of AI as⏎part of clinical care to support clinician buy-in and improve clinical outcomes,⏎particularly for clinicians and patients in rural communities.⏎• The Department can clarify that, while AI is a revolutionary technology that is actively⏎transforming healthcare delivery in the U.S., there is a strong legal framework and⏎existing industry standards that support the introduction of innovative technology in⏎1 90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (Dec. 23, 2025).⏎2 Id.⏎3 Id.⏎-1-⏎⏎8779 Hilcrest Road⏎Kansas City, MO 64138⏎Direct: (816) 201-1024⏎healthcare. Guidance by the Department can help healthcare stakeholders to understand⏎how these existing legal frameworks can apply to the safe adoption and use of AI as part⏎of clinical care.⏎The comments below provide further detail on each of these recommendations.⏎I. Ways to Enhance Private Sector Innovation in AI for Healthcare and its⏎Adoption and Use in Clinical Care⏎Healthcare AI innovation has been developing under current frameworks; however, the⏎Department can help address areas of friction to private sector innovation in AI for healthcare⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care, including regulatory alignment, data availability for⏎testing, and clear standards for patient data privacy and deidentification. Below, we outline these⏎issues and provide suggestions for how the Department can help to address them to better⏎promote private sector innovation.⏎a. Regulatory Alignment⏎Oracle Health applauds the Department’s efforts to “establish a regulatory posture on AI that is⏎well understood, predictable, and proportionate to any risks presented to enable rapid innovation⏎while protecting patients and the confidentiality of their identifiable health information and⏎maintaining public trust.”4 A coordinated federal approach is essential to reducing fragmentation⏎and compliance burden across the healthcare ecosystem, and Oracle Health appreciates the⏎opportunity to comment on how the Department may develop such an approach.⏎Currently, innovation in AI for healthcare is slowed by confusing and sometimes conflicting⏎rules and guidance on AI across major federal programs and agencies. As an example, for nearly⏎two decades, stakeholders from the medical community have raised concern that overly⏎burdensome U.S. Food and Drug Administration (“FDA”) regulation of software as a medical⏎device could stifle innovation.5 These stakeholders have also expressed concern about potentially⏎duplicative and inconsistent regulatory regimes from the Assistant Secretary of Technology⏎Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (“ASTP/ONC”),⏎FDA, and other federal agencies. This concern led to the 21st Century Cures Act in 2016 (“Cures⏎Act”), which officially carved out certain software from the FDA’s device jurisdiction and⏎adopted a pro-innovation approach to software regulation.6⏎In 2022, the FDA issued Clinical Decision Support (“CDS”) Software Guidance for Industry and⏎Food and Drug Administration Staff (the “2022 CDS Guidance”) which is inconsistent with the⏎4 Id.⏎5 See, e.g., Brian Dolan, In tense hearing, congressman declares "software is not a medical device", MOBI⏎HEALTH NEWS (Nov. 21, 2013), https://www.mobihealthnews.com/news/tense-hearing-congressman-declares-⏎software-not-medical-device.⏎6 21st Century Cures Act, Pub. L. 114–255 § 3060, 130 Stat. 1033, 1130-32 (2016) (codified at 21 U.S.C. § 360⏎note) (“Cures Act”).⏎-2-⏎⏎8779 Hilcrest Road⏎Kansas City, MO 64138⏎Direct: (816) 201-1024⏎risk-based approach outlined in the Cures Act.7 In the 2022 CDS Guidance, FDA introduced⏎brand new factors that do not exist in the statute or even the previous drafts of the CDS guidance,⏎such as “time-critical decision-making” and “automation bias,” that significantly expand FDA’s⏎jurisdiction. The 2022 CDS Guidance also creates a presumption that risk scores or other CDS⏎offering only one treatment or diagnostic recommendation would be treated as a device,⏎regardless of the information provided to a physician to explain that recommendation.⏎In January 2026, the FDA updated the 2022 CDS Guidance (the “2026 CDS Guidance”) to⏎indicate its intent to exercise enforcement discretion toward a CDS software function that⏎provides a single output or recommendation in scenarios where only a single option is “clinically⏎appropriate”.8 Although the 2026 CDS Guidance is slightly more aligned with the regulatory⏎framework created by the Cures Act, ultimately the FDA’s current posture remains at odds with⏎the risk-based approach in the Cures Act and continues to stifle innovation. Most notably, FDA⏎continues to treat CDS software functions that provide clinically appropriate recommendations in⏎time critical conditions as device functions requiring premarket authorization, despite that time⏎criticality and automation bias are not concepts recognized in the statute.⏎The implications of the FDA’s overreach are significant. The technology exists today to improve⏎outcomes for patients and ease burdens for healthcare providers, but electronic health record⏎(“EHR”) companies such as Oracle Health are hesitant to put these services into use for fear of⏎incurring massive regulatory requirements. It is past time to restore a pro-innovation approach to⏎clinical decision support.⏎Moreover, FDA’s recent guidance says nothing to clarify when an EHR or other software vendor⏎will be held responsible for the software that healthcare institutions such as hospitals may create⏎from the software tools they are provided by vendors. Healthcare institutions increasingly want⏎to customize their clinical decision support to the needs of the populations they serve. AI⏎platforms and software toolkits can now allow for provider specific innovations and solutions.⏎Where a provider supplies an AI platform, toolkit, or development environment to create rules-⏎based clinical decision support prompts, and providers customize those tools for the needs of⏎their institution, the software vendor has supplied general-purpose software and should not be⏎treated as a medical device developer for the tools that the healthcare institutions create and⏎customize.⏎Oracle Health urges the Department to develop guidance that: (i) aligns to the statutory text of⏎the 21st Century Cures Act; (ii) recognizes that many additional CDS software functions that may⏎meet the definition of a medical device do not, under a risk-based framework, require FDA⏎oversight; (iii) clarifies that healthcare institutions that configure their EHRs to include CDS that⏎supports their practices as an institution are not subject to the FDA’s device requirements; and⏎(iv) clarifies that software developers that create those CDS tools at the specifications of⏎healthcare institutions are not subject to device requirements.⏎7 FDA, Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff, Clinical Decision Support Software (Sept. 2022),⏎https://www.fda.gov/media/109618/download.⏎8 FDA, Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff, Clinical Decision Support Software (Jan. 2026),⏎https://www.fda.gov/media/109618/download.⏎-3-⏎⏎8779 Hilcrest Road⏎Kansas City, MO 64138⏎Direct: (816) 201-1024⏎More broadly, the Department should work to align AI-related policies and guidance across⏎major federal programs and agencies to create a more consistent environment for AI innovation⏎and adoption. Coordination among the relevant agencies and regulatory bodies can help⏎harmonize expectations for oversight, reimbursement, quality measurement, and clinical⏎implementation. Oracle Health supports the Department’s initiatives to further regulatory⏎alignment and welcomes the opportunity to participate further in such projects.⏎b. Access to Clinically Relevant Benchmark Datasets⏎While Oracle Health maintains access to high-quality de-identified data to support development⏎and validation of its AI systems, there remains a need for clinically relevant, standardized⏎evaluation benchmarks that enable consistent, pre-deployment testing and transparent⏎comparison across tools. Oracle Health encourages HHS to prioritize development of privacy-⏎preserving benchmark evaluation datasets and test frameworks (e.g., disease-specific evaluation⏎sets) intended for validation and comparability, not as general-purpose training datasets, with⏎appropriate governance (e.g., secure access, auditing, or “models-to-data” approaches). Disease-⏎specific evaluation sets, longitudinal datasets, and representative cohorts would enable more⏎transparent comparison across tools and reduce variability in how performance is addressed.⏎c. Standards for Patient Data Privacy and Deidentification⏎Patient data, when aggregated and deidentified, allows for deep research into patient and⏎population health. In the age of AI, deidentified data can still pose a risk to patient privacy. To⏎mitigate this risk, the deidentification standard can be modified to make data more useful once it⏎has been deidentified while still preventing reidentification. Furthermore, the Department can⏎develop policy to enable the use of privacy-enhancing technologies.⏎Oracle Health would welcome the opportunity to work with the Department to modernize the⏎Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996 (“HIPAA”) Privacy Rule policies⏎and deidentification standards. The HIPAA Privacy Rule was designed well before the⏎development of AI technology and did not sufficiently contemplate how best to protect health⏎data while also leveraging the incredible learnings that such data can provide to improve care for⏎population health. Modifications or clarifications to HIPAA deidentification policies and⏎considering alternatives, including leveraging privacy-enhancing technology, can provide greater⏎access to data to permit model training while better protecting patient health data in this new era⏎of technology.⏎II. Incentivizing the Effective Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎Oracle Health applauds the Department’s focus on payment policy changes to promote access to⏎high-value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders, and⏎accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care.9 In connection with such⏎efforts, Oracle Health encourages the Department to recognize the investment of time and⏎9 90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (Dec. 23, 2025).⏎-4-⏎⏎8779 Hilcrest Road⏎Kansas City, MO 64138⏎Direct: (816) 201-1024⏎training necessary to adopt and implement AI-enabled technologies. While many AI⏎technologies are familiar and user-friendly, others can take time for clinicians to evaluate, learn⏎to use, and integrate into care delivery. It also comes at an expense.⏎Oracle supports the Department’s efforts to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care⏎and encourages the Department to develop and deploy payment or reimbursement models which⏎recognize clinician time associated with testing and learning to use and implement AI-enabled⏎technologies and with lost revenue that could come from adopting technology that improves⏎outcomes but reduces clinician time. The federal government provided financial incentives for⏎adoption and meaningful use of EHRs. This had a substantial and positive effect on adoption.⏎Similarly, it is critical that the Department consider payment and reimbursement models that can⏎support adoption of technology that can reduce errors, improve access to care, and increase⏎efficiency.⏎In that same vein, it is important for the Department to use its position to help educate the⏎healthcare provider community about use of AI in healthcare delivery. Specifically, increasing⏎general acumen for the how AI works and the distinctions between general purpose AI that⏎individuals may use in their daily lives versus purpose-built health AI systems and models that⏎are incorporated into EHRs and other health applications. Healthcare providers are inundated⏎with messages about AI in healthcare and there is a need for education from a trusted, reliable⏎source to dispel misconceptions and expand knowledge to help encourage and drive adoption.⏎In tandem with the Rural Health Transformation Program (“RHTP”) authorized by the One Big⏎Beautiful Bill Act,10 Oracle Health urges the Department to ensure that AI-enabled technology⏎(including infrastructure technology such as AI-enabled EHRs) is available and affordable to⏎providers in rural communities. The RHTP’s Strategic Goals include sustainable access to care,⏎innovative care, and tech innovation; AI-enabled technologies can assist clinicians and recipients⏎of RHTP funds achieve each of these goals.11 In fact, AI-enabled technologies promise to⏎disproportionately help rural providers to improve care access, quality, and affordability, by⏎increasing provider productivity and reducing provider burden while improving patient⏎outcomes.12 Oracle Health encourages the Department, and the ultimate recipients of RHTP⏎funds, to ensure that the adoption of AI for clinical care is a key component of RHTP⏎implementation plans.⏎III. Legal Framework Considerations⏎The history of technological innovation and adoption in healthcare has been transformative, from⏎the discovery of penicillin to the invention of advanced imaging to the development of modern-⏎day EHRs. While each of these technologies represents an enormous advancement in science and⏎10 One Big Beautiful Bill Act, Pub. L. 119–21 § 71401, 139 Stat. 327 (2025).⏎11 CMS, Rural Health Transformation Program (Dec. 2025), https://www.cms.gov/priorities/rural-health-⏎transformation-rht-program/overview.⏎12 See American Hospital Association, Rural Hospitals and the AI Advantage: Turning Constraints into⏎Catalysts (Jan. 2026), https://www.aha.org/aha-center-health-innovation-market-scan/2026-01-26-rural-hospitals-⏎and-ai-advantage-turning-constraints-catalysts; National Consortium of Telehealth Resource Centers, AI in Rural⏎Health (Dec. 2024), https://telehealthresourcecenter.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/12/AI-in-Rural-Health-Final.pdf.⏎-5-⏎⏎8779 Hilcrest Road⏎Kansas City, MO 64138⏎Direct: (816) 201-1024⏎medicine, the legal framework in the U.S. regarding their implementation and use has remained⏎largely the same: at the end of the day, the clinicians and the health systems they work in retain⏎ultimate decision-making authority for how they treat patients, and the providers are given⏎discretion to utilize technologies that may help them in ensuring a positive patient outcome.⏎While AI is undoubtedly another such revolutionary technology, Oracle Health encourages the⏎Department to view AI-enabled technology that augments clinical care simply as another tool for⏎providers to use in their workflows and in their professional discretion. While AI-enabled⏎technologies have the potential to improve patient diagnosis, treatment and outcomes and reduce⏎clinician burdens, decisions about how to treat patients still rests with clinicians and the health⏎systems they work in. For example, AI-enabled technologies such as CDS can improve care⏎quality and outcomes by quickly delivering actionable data for clinicians to consider in patient⏎treatment plans. However, ultimately health systems and other healthcare institutions develop⏎practice guidelines for their clinicians to follow, and AI-enabled technologies are only intended⏎to augment, not supplant, those practice guidelines and a clinician’s professional decision-⏎making and judgment. At all times, healthcare providers remain in control of patient care⏎decisions.⏎To further empower providers in their adoption and use of AI for clinical care, Oracle Health⏎encourages the Department to support clinician education and training in AI through educational⏎grant programs and aligned payment and reimbursement models. Similarly, Oracle Health⏎encourages state professional licensing boards to incorporate AI-enabled technology training into⏎professional continuing education requirements and would support Department-led efforts to⏎incentivize the same. Oracle Health also urges health systems and other providers to ensure they⏎have AI governance structures in place and regularly review industry guidance and best practices⏎on the responsible use of AI in healthcare.⏎For developers of AI-enabled technologies, existing industry standards and guidance already⏎address many of the questions raised by such technologies. For example, Oracle Health is⏎certified to the ISO 42001 standard regarding AI management systems and supports⏎ASTP/ONC’s decision support interventions certification and related industry standards and⏎certifications. There are also the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and the HIMSS AI⏎policy principles. In light of the rapid pace of AI innovation, Oracle Health encourages the⏎Department to defer to industry standards and practices which have already proven more than⏎capable of ensuring safety, quality, and accessibility in AI innovation and use any levers to⏎encourage new innovators to comply with these industry standards.⏎Therefore, while slight modifications to existing legal frameworks may be helpful to ensure that⏎AI-enabled technologies only augment and do not replace clinical decision-making, Oracle⏎Health encourages the Department to treat AI-enabled technologies similarly to formerly⏎transformative technologies – simply another tool at a clinician’s disposal to improve their⏎treatment of patients.⏎-6-⏎⏎8779 Hilcrest Road⏎Kansas City, MO 64138⏎Direct: (816) 201-1024⏎IV. Conclusion⏎Oracle Health supports the Department’s efforts to establish a forward-leaning, industry-⏎supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.⏎In furtherance of these goals, Oracle Health encourages the Department to: (i) align regulation of⏎AI in healthcare, prioritize the development of publicly available benchmark data sets for AI⏎model validation and evaluation, and update standards for patient data privacy and⏎deidentification to incorporate AI-relevant provisions; (ii) align payment incentives with the⏎adoption and use AI as part of clinical care to support clinician buy-in and improve clinical⏎outcomes, particularly for rural healthcare providers; and (iii) work within existing legal⏎frameworks and encourage alignment with industry standards to address responsible⏎development and adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.⏎Oracle Health values the opportunity to comment on the topics raised in the RFI. If you have any⏎questions or if Oracle Health can provide any additional information or clarification on these⏎comments, please do not hesitate to contact me directly at 410-925-7904 or⏎alex.mugge@oracle.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Alex Mugge⏎Senior Director, Global Health Policy and⏎Regulatory Intelligence⏎-7-♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0152♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Nancy J Hoffer PhD Psychologist PC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Hello, This suggestion the AI can serve as a substitute for Psychologist ans other mental health professional is flawed. The evidence is minimal. I have had patients tell me of the experience - Apparently the bot does whatever the person asking questions tell it to do . A very agreeable exprerience led by patient inquiry.  This is  a typical internet search . THis is not psychotherpy . Please follow the guidlines sent by my profession of licences professional with true research experience and real data .  ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0153♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from HealthScoreAI, Inc. , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HealthScoreAI, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎February 2, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Washington, DC⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎We appreciate the opportunity to submit comments in response to the Department of Health⏎and Human Services Request for Information entitled “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care” (RIN 0955-AA13). Thank you for inviting⏎public input on an issue of critical importance to the future of healthcare delivery, innovation,⏎and patient trust in the United States.⏎HealthScoreAI, Inc. is a start-up consumer-focused health data and analytics company. Our⏎leadership team brings decades of experience across healthcare operations both acute, and⏎ambulatory, health information technology, in particular and relevant, building ambulatory⏎electronic health record (EHR) platforms, third party administration, owning and managing⏎Medicare Advantage networks, medical billing companies, and clinical administration.⏎Collectively we also have been issued over forty (40) patents on the use of technology in⏎healthcare ranging from EHR, IoT, predictive analytics, machine learning, and the use of⏎blockchain in healthcare applications. Our perspective reflects both the promise of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) and the risks associated with premature or misaligned deployment in clinical⏎care settings. We strongly support innovation that improves outcomes, lowers costs, and⏎empowers patients, while remaining cautious about policies that could unintentionally increase⏎spending, erode trust, or substitute technology for clinical judgment.⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎Enclosed below this transmittal letter are our responses to the RFI, organized section by⏎section and clearly labeled as Comment or Question as you submitted, and our “REPLY“ to⏎align directly with the structure of the solicitation. Our comments focus on regulatory posture,⏎reimbursement design, research & development strategy, interoperability, privacy protections,⏎and the practical realities of AI adoption in clinical and administrative environments. We have⏎intentionally emphasized lessons learned from prior health IT initiatives, including adoption of⏎electronic health records (EHR), and the importance of patient centered data access, clear⏎accountability, and market driven innovation.⏎We respectfully submit these comments for your consideration and would welcome continued⏎dialogue as HHS advances its approach to artificial intelligence in the health sector. Thank you⏎for your leadership on this important issue and for your commitment to balancing innovation⏎with patient safety, privacy, and public trust.⏎Sincerely,⏎Noel J. Guillama-Alvarez⏎Noel J. Guillama-Alvarez⏎President⏎HealthScoreAI, Inc.⏎Wellington, Florida⏎nguillama@mypwer.com⏎https://www.linkedin.com/in/nguillama/⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎Solicitation of Public Comments⏎Regulation⏎As the nation’s principal health regulator, HHS helps shape the environment in which AI for⏎clinical care is developed, evaluated, and deployed. HHS seeks to establish a regulatory⏎posture on AI that is well understood, predictable, and proportionate to any risks presented to⏎enable rapid innovation while protecting patients and the confidentiality of their identifiable⏎health information and maintaining public trust. We seek feedback on how current HHS⏎regulations impact AI adoption and use for clinical care.⏎REPLY-⏎We view the opportunities presented by artificial intelligence in clinical care as⏎historically significant, but accompanied by equally significant warnings. As of⏎December 2025, hundreds of peer reviewed studies both highlight the potential benefits⏎of AI and raise serious concerns about its limitations and risks. While many studies⏎demonstrate strong performance in highly controlled settings with apparent clinical⏎validation, closer examination often shows that these systems do not perform as well⏎as licensed medical professionals in real world clinical environments. The promise of AI⏎is most evident in data intensive and objective domains such as medical imaging,⏎pathology, and genomics, whereas performance degrades as clinical decision making⏎moves beyond structured data into the more human and contextual aspects of care. A⏎fundamental constraint is the fragmentation of health data. An estimated three⏎thousand exabytes of clinical data in the United States are distributed across more than⏎four hundred ONC certified ambulatory and acute care electronic health record⏎systems. Even within standard clinical documentation, such as SOAP notes, subjective⏎and objective information is separated, further limiting the reliability of AI driven⏎inference. As a result, deploying AI broadly in clinical decision making today without⏎significant safeguards presents real risk. We are particularly concerned about general⏎purpose large language models, which can produce outputs that mix valid clinical⏎knowledge with pseudo science or inaccurate information. Over time, this will⏎necessitate the development and regulatory recognition of highly constrained, domain⏎specific language models designed for clinical use. The experience with EHR adoption⏎is instructive. Since the 2012 federal mandate, HHS has invested tens of billions of⏎dollars, with industry investing hundreds of billions more, yet there was no measurable⏎improvement in U.S. life expectancy from 2012 through 2019 prior to COVID. During the⏎same period, healthcare costs increased substantially, raising legitimate questions⏎about whether EHR adoption primarily optimized reimbursement and billing workflows⏎rather than patient outcomes. For these reasons, we believe AI should initially be⏎deployed primarily outside the core clinical decision making system to support⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎consumers and administrative efficiency, rather than risk clouding physician judgment⏎with opaque or poorly understood AI outputs.⏎Reimbursement⏎HHS’s payment policies and programs have massive effects on how health care is delivered in⏎the United States, often times with unintended consequences. Hypothetically, if a payer is⏎taking financial risk for the long-term health and health costs of an individual, that payer will⏎have an inherent incentive to promote access to the highest-value interventions for patients.⏎Under government designed and dictated fee-for-service regimes, however, coverage and⏎reimbursement decisions are slow. Rarely does covering new innovations reduce net⏎spending; and waste, fraud, and abuse is difficult to prevent, often times leading to massive⏎spending bubbles on concentrated items or services that are not commensurate with the value⏎of such products. Given the inherent flaws in legacy payment systems, we seek to ensure that⏎the potential promises of AI innovations are not diminished through inertia and instead such⏎payment systems are modernized to meet the needs of a changing healthcare system. We⏎seek feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive and ability to⏎promote access to high value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical care AI⏎tool builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care.⏎REPLY-⏎We agree that payment standards established by HHS have a profound influence on⏎behavior across the healthcare system, often incentivizing providers and intermediaries⏎to optimize for reimbursement rather than for better outcomes or lower costs.⏎Historically, this dynamic has almost always resulted in higher aggregate spending.⏎When Medicare was established in 1965, national health expenditures were⏎approximately six percent of gross domestic product. Today, as reported by HHS⏎actuaries, health spending exceeds eighteen percent of GDP and continues to trend⏎upward, underscoring the structural limitations of current payment models.⏎We respectfully disagree with the assertion that a payer bearing long term financial risk⏎for an individual has an inherent incentive to promote the highest value interventions.⏎This assumption does not adequately account for the “velocity” with which patients⏎change employers or health plans. In practice, average enrollment duration in employer⏎sponsored coverage and Medicare Advantage plans is often five to six years. Annual⏎benefit design changes further allow managed care organizations to indirectly shed⏎higher risk members. As a result, the costs of underinvestment in prevention or⏎suboptimal care are frequently externalized to future payers. In reality, the only entity⏎that consistently bears true long term financial risk is HHS itself, which ultimately⏎absorbs the downstream consequences of delayed care, neglect, or misaligned⏎incentives within provider organizations and managed care plans.⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎From a payment policy perspective, HHS should not reimburse for the use of AI as a⏎discrete billable service. Reimbursing AI risks repeating the experience of prior⏎technology mandates that optimized billing workflows without demonstrably improving⏎patient outcomes. Instead, HHS should focus on fostering innovation by ensuring⏎patients have meaningful access to their complete medical records in aggregate, rather⏎than one record or encounter at a time. This would allow consumers to use carefully⏎constrained and curated AI tools to better understand their health, engage in shared⏎decision making, and manage chronic conditions. While AI clearly holds significant⏎value in biomedical research, population health analytics, and drug discovery, those⏎use cases fall largely outside the clinical reimbursement framework.⏎To foster competition and innovation, HHS should adopt a model similar to prior health⏎IT certification approaches, in which AI innovators register with HHS, verify⏎organizational identity, disclose intended data use including consumer data, and meet⏎baseline trust and security requirements comparable to those imposed on EHR⏎developers. HHS should set clear guardrails for safety, privacy, and accountability,⏎while avoiding mandates that prescribe specific technical implementations. Experience⏎with Meaningful Use demonstrates that when government mandates narrowly define⏎requirements, vendors tend to meet the letter of the mandate without meaningful⏎innovation. Imposing prescriptive requirements on AI risks creating a ceiling on⏎innovation rather than enabling its full potential.⏎QUESTION-⏎Research & Development⏎HHS supports one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems, catalyzing innovation to⏎supplement the market. By enabling applied AI research & development, care delivery⏎research and implementation science, as well as AI entrepreneurship in health care, we can⏎better translate AI technologies from concept to clinical use. We seek input on ways in which⏎HHS may invest in research & development (including public-private partnerships and⏎cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs)) to integrate AI in care delivery⏎and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the health and wellbeing of all⏎Americans⏎REPLY-⏎We agree that HHS has the capacity to catalyze meaningful innovation through research⏎and development, but it also has the capacity to unintentionally suppress innovation⏎when programs are overly centralized or prescriptive. Government agencies are not well⏎positioned to predict which technical approaches or use cases will ultimately prove⏎transformative. In practice, many existing R&D mechanisms favor organizations that are⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎adept at producing strong grant applications rather than those best positioned to⏎deliver breakthrough innovation.⏎If HHS seeks to foster genuine innovation in AI, it should consider an approach more⏎analogous to a diversified venture capital model, funding a large number of early stage⏎efforts with relatively small awards rather than concentrating resources in a limited⏎number of large programs. In parallel, HHS could provide access to standardized, high⏎quality synthetic or de-ID datasets, for example a large corpus of representative⏎electronic health record datasets, that innovators could use to explore hypotheses,⏎develop models, and conduct back testing. This incremental and exploratory approach⏎would allow promising ideas to emerge organically before larger investments are made.⏎The experience with electronic health records illustrates the risks of conflating system⏎replacement with innovation. Over the past two decades, many hospitals have migrated⏎from one major EHR vendor to another, such as from Meditech to Cerner to Epic. These⏎transitions reflect vendor consolidation and rent seeking behavior rather than⏎meaningful advances in care delivery or outcomes. AI research programs should avoid⏎repeating this pattern by focusing on open experimentation rather than reinforcing⏎incumbent platforms.⏎A particularly high value opportunity lies in expanding controlled access to de-ID data.⏎Today, HHS and its affiliated agencies primarily possess claims and payment data,⏎which are insufficient on their own to support robust clinical AI development. The most⏎impactful AI applications require longitudinal clinical data, genetic information, and⏎linked claims data to understand disease progression, treatment response, and⏎outcomes over time. By enabling responsible access to these data types through well⏎governed research partnerships, HHS could significantly accelerate entrepreneurial⏎innovation and surface insights that centralized programs are unlikely to identify in⏎advance.⏎Specific Questions⏎In addition to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, HHS seeks input on the following specific⏎questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎REPLY-⏎The most significant barrier to private sector innovation and adoption of AI in clinical⏎care is that, to be practical and safe in most clinical settings, AI systems must perform⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎at least as well as licensed physicians, and in many contexts demonstrably better. At⏎present, most AI tools do not meet this threshold in real world clinical environments.⏎Any realistic assessment must account for the fully burdened cost of AI use, not just⏎the direct and often subsidized cost of the technology itself. This includes the time⏎physicians spend prompting, supervising, validating, and correcting AI outputs.⏎Physician time should be valued based on revenue generating capacity rather than⏎salary alone, which in many specialties translates to an effective cost of approximately⏎fifteen to thirty dollars per minute. When these opportunity costs are included, many AI⏎tools fail to demonstrate economic value. In addition, unresolved questions around⏎liability remain a major barrier to adoption. When AI recommendations are incorrect, it⏎is unclear who bears responsibility for malpractice exposure, reputational harm, and⏎potential licensure risk. Until accountability frameworks are clearly defined, providers⏎and health systems will remain appropriately cautious in deploying AI in clinical care.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎REPLY-⏎We are cautious in recommending new regulatory or payment policies related to AI, as⏎prior experience with electronic health record mandates suggests that prescriptive⏎approaches are unlikely to produce meaningful clinical or economic benefit. In⏎particular, HHS should avoid establishing separate reimbursement for the use of AI. If⏎AI is ultimately integrated into clinical care, its value proposition should be reflected in⏎reduced costs, improved efficiency, or better outcomes, not in additional billable⏎services. Creating payment codes for AI would raise fundamental dilemmas, such as⏎whether a provider who uses AI should be reimbursed at a higher or lower rate than a⏎provider who delivers the same service without AI, even when outcomes are equivalent.⏎In practice, AI adoption is already occurring without explicit payment incentives. Based⏎on published medical reporting, an estimated forty to fifty percent of U.S. physicians⏎have used tools such as OpenEvidence or similar AI enabled clinical reference systems.⏎This underscores that clinicians will adopt tools they find useful without regulatory or⏎reimbursement mandates. The priority role for HHS should therefore be risk mitigation⏎rather than financial incentivization.⏎From a regulatory perspective, HHS should focus on strengthening protections around⏎the use of protected health information in AI systems. In particular, HHS should clarify⏎and enforce that identifiable patient data must not be disclosed to general purpose⏎large language models or other AI systems that are not explicitly designed, governed,⏎for research or clinical use only. Existing authorities under HIPAA, including 45 CFR⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎Parts 160 and 164, could be revisited to provide clearer guidance on secondary data⏎use, model training, and inference, ensuring that innovation does not come at the⏎expense of patient privacy or public trust.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g.,relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎REPLY-⏎We agree with the historical approach HHS has taken toward non medical devices used⏎by consumers, which generally relies on a consumer awareness and consumer⏎responsibility model. In many contexts, this has been an appropriate and pragmatic⏎posture. Our concern arises, however, when a non medical AI tool becomes affiliated⏎with, integrated into, or operated by a designated entity under HIPAA, including a⏎covered entity or a business associate under a business associate agreement.⏎Once a designated entity is involved, there is a significant legal and governance risk⏎related to data classification. Consumer generated data that originates outside the⏎HIPAA framework can effectively be transformed into protected health information⏎through association with a covered entity or business associate. We believe it would be⏎inappropriate and unsafe to allow designated entities, including EHR vendors and other⏎HIPAA regulated actors, to move data back and forth between protected health⏎information and so called consumer data in order to avoid regulatory obligations. This⏎practice would undermine the integrity of HIPAA protections and create ambiguity⏎around liability, privacy, and security responsibilities.⏎HHS should play a clarifying role by establishing clear guidance that once data is⏎handled by a covered entity or business associate for clinical or operational purposes,⏎it must be treated consistently as protected health information and remain subject to⏎HIPAA safeguards. Clear boundaries around data status would reduce uncertainty for⏎innovators while preserving accountability and public trust in the use of AI for clinical⏎care.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎REPLY-⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎As discussed in our prior responses, the most effective way to foster innovation while⏎managing risk is to avoid overly prescriptive evaluation frameworks for non medical AI⏎tools used in or adjacent to clinical care. Non medical devices are generally not the⏎preferred modality for physicians when making clinical decisions, and attempts to force⏎their integration into core clinical workflows often introduce more noise than signal.⏎Variability in data quality, alerting logic, and model performance across consumer⏎oriented tools can create cognitive burden for providers rather than improving care⏎delivery.⏎For these reasons, HHS should be cautious in prioritizing large scale evaluation⏎programs focused on non medical devices within clinical settings. Where evaluation is⏎appropriate, it should emphasize real world usability, transparency of limitations, and⏎clear boundaries around clinical reliance, rather than attempting to certify performance⏎equivalence with medical devices. A limited, venture style funding approach could be⏎used to support exploratory evaluation efforts, allowing innovators to test hypotheses⏎and workflows without embedding them directly into EHR decision making.⏎If HHS chooses to support these processes, the most impactful mechanisms would be⏎small, time limited grants or prize based challenges that encourage experimentation⏎and post deployment learning, rather than long term contracts or large cooperative⏎agreements. This approach would allow HHS to observe what delivers value in practice⏎without prematurely standardizing evaluation methods in a rapidly evolving AI⏎environment.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎REPLY-⏎As noted above, HHS should avoid stifling innovation through expansive accreditation,⏎certification, or credentialing regimes that add administrative complexity without clear⏎evidence of improved outcomes. Innovators, inventors, and entrepreneurs should not⏎be required to assemble large compliance teams simply to engage with HHS or to⏎explore novel AI applications. Excessive process requirements risk favoring⏎incumbents and well resourced organizations over genuinely innovative entrants.⏎A more effective approach would be to apply a limited set of baseline trust⏎requirements, similar to know your customer standards in financial services, focused⏎on verifying organizational identity, governance, and accountability. These⏎requirements should be sufficient to grant access to carefully governed HHS datasets,⏎while maintaining strict adherence to existing HIPAA obligations and data protection⏎standards. Beyond these guardrails, HHS should allow market driven testing, peer⏎validation, and real world adoption to determine which AI tools deliver value in clinical⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎care. This approach would promote innovation while preserving patient privacy and⏎public trust.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎REPLY-⏎There have been numerous reports of AI tools meeting or exceeding expectations in⏎clinical care, particularly with respect to speed and throughput. However, when these⏎claims are examined in detail, the performance gains often do not translate into⏎consistent improvements in clinical quality, reliability of outcomes, or total cost⏎reduction. Many highly positive peer reviewed studies published two or more years ago⏎were conducted in controlled or retrospective environments that do not reflect the⏎complexity and variability of real world clinical practice. As a result, reported⏎performance frequently deteriorates when these tools are deployed at scale.⏎Publicized benchmarking studies further illustrate this gap. For example, widely cited⏎industry sponsored evaluations have claimed that certain AI systems outperform⏎physicians by large margins. When the underlying methodologies are reviewed,⏎however, these studies often rely on artificial test conditions rather than real time⏎clinical workflows, and they frequently permit AI systems to leverage knowledge⏎sources that would not be available to a clinician at the point of care. Even under those⏎favorable conditions, reported accuracy rates have often been closer to eighty percent,⏎which falls short of the reliability required for unsupervised clinical decision making.⏎The greatest near term potential for AI lies not in replacing clinical judgment, but in⏎supporting highly constrained and objective tasks such as image analysis, pathology⏎screening, population level quality measurement, and administrative optimization.⏎Novel AI tools that operate on well defined data types, produce auditable outputs, and⏎reduce non clinical burden have the strongest potential to improve outcomes, generate⏎new insights into quality, and reduce costs without introducing unacceptable clinical⏎risk.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎REPLY-⏎As noted in prior responses, current AI systems, particularly general purpose large⏎language models, are not ready to replace physicians in clinical care and should not be⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎treated as substitutes for clinical judgment. To the extent that AI tools are used to⏎support clinicians, adoption decisions are primarily influenced by physicians⏎themselves, clinical leadership, compliance and risk management teams, and health⏎system legal counsel. These stakeholders are appropriately cautious given ongoing⏎concerns related to patient safety, HIPAA compliance, malpractice exposure, patient⏎acceptance, and professional reliance on AI generated outputs.⏎The primary administrative hurdles to adoption include unresolved accountability for⏎errors, uncertainty around data governance, and the operational burden of integrating⏎AI tools into existing clinical workflows. While AI may have more immediate and⏎practical application in administrative and back office functions, HHS should remain⏎alert to unintended consequences. For example, industry studies have reported that AI⏎based clinical documentation tools can increase coded billing levels by as much as⏎fifteen percent. It is not always clear whether these increases reflect more accurate⏎documentation of care actually delivered or an expansion of documented diagnoses⏎without corresponding clinical value.⏎Regardless of use case, any deployment of AI within clinical workflows must operate⏎under strict HIPAA controls and clear governance structures. Fundamentally, AI is not⏎yet ready for broad or unsupervised implementation in clinical environments. While⏎future progress is likely, the timeline beyond the near term remains uncertain, and⏎policy should reflect that uncertainty rather than assume rapid clinical readiness.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎REPLY-⏎Electronic health record interoperability in the United States has largely failed, and⏎continued dialogue with large health IT vendors or major hospital systems has not⏎meaningfully changed that reality. Structural incentives, rather than technical⏎limitations, are the primary barrier. HHS should shift its focus away from vendor⏎mediated interoperability and instead prioritize enforcement of HIPAA rights that allow⏎patients direct access to their complete medical records. Patients are the most⏎motivated stakeholders to demand portability, continuity, and meaningful use of their⏎data.⏎Interoperability has stagnated for more than a decade because true data liquidity would⏎materially weaken the economic moats of incumbent EHR vendors and reduce⏎switching costs for health systems. If one EHR platform could efficiently and completely⏎transfer longitudinal records to another, the financial and operational barriers to⏎changing systems would be dramatically reduced. Today, hospitals often spend⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎hundreds of millions of dollars, and in some cases more than one billion dollars, to⏎transition data during EHR replacements. In a genuinely interoperable environment,⏎EHR platforms would function more like regulated utilities rather than proprietary lock⏎in systems.⏎Enhanced interoperability would most effectively fuel AI development if it focused on⏎patient mediated data aggregation rather than system to system exchange alone.⏎Longitudinal clinical records, combined with imaging, laboratory data, genomics, and⏎claims data, made accessible through patient authorization, would create far more⏎meaningful datasets for AI research and clinical innovation. Benchmarking tools should⏎emphasize completeness, longitudinal continuity, and data provenance rather than⏎simple message throughput or transactional compliance with standards.⏎Recent public statements by senior federal leadership have reinforced this direction,⏎emphasizing that patients should control their own data and determine how AI tools are⏎applied to it. HHS has a unique opportunity to accelerate AI development and restore⏎trust by enforcing patient data access rights and allowing market forces to operate on⏎top of truly portable health information, rather than continuing to rely on vendor⏎centered interoperability frameworks that have repeatedly failed to deliver.⏎https://youtu.be/b5p40OuTTW4?si=Alb22Tqxd2yv1RYk⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎REPLY-⏎We do not believe that most patients or caregivers want AI to directly treat them.⏎Patients overwhelmingly want to be cared for by physicians who demonstrate empathy,⏎shared experience, and an understanding of their personal circumstances, values, and⏎fears. Trust in care is often relational, not purely technical. In our experience as clinical⏎administrators, many elderly patients prefer physicians who they feel understand their⏎stage of life and lived experience. For some patients, particularly older adults or⏎individuals from culturally tight knit communities, the introduction of AI into clinical⏎decision making can feel impersonal or unsettling rather than reassuring.⏎While AI may be perceived as acceptable or even helpful by younger, more⏎technologically fluent patients, the same tools may generate anxiety or mistrust among⏎older populations or those with limited digital literacy. AI can simulate empathy through⏎language and tone, but it cannot genuinely experience empathy, shared suffering, or⏎moral responsibility. Patients and caregivers are therefore concerned that increased⏎reliance on AI could erode the human connection that is central to healing, reduce⏎accountability, and distance clinicians from the lived reality of their patients. Any⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13⏎⏎ADVANCE COPY NOT FOR DISTRIBUTION⏎adoption of AI in clinical care must respect these deeply human expectations and avoid⏎substituting automation for relationships that patients value most.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎REPLY-⏎Please see answers above.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎Submitted by:⏎Noel J. Guillama-Alvarez⏎President⏎HealthScoreAI, Inc.⏎Wellington, Florida⏎nguillama@mypwer.com⏎https://www.linkedin.com/in/nguillama/⏎HealthScore AI, Inc. FRI Response RIN 0955-AA13♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0154♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Garcia♔♔New Jersey♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly I'm not totally sure this is the right place to leave a comment — someone shared this link on Facebook and said it was about hospitals using AI to make medical decisions, and I figured I'd try to say something even if I'm not sure what an "RFI" is exactly. Is this like a survey? A public hearing? I clicked around the HHS website for a while and I'm still a little confused about what happens with these comments.⏎⏎Anyway. My brother-in-law Marco went to urgent care clinics in Passaic, New Jersey probably four or five times between January and March of 2022 with chest pain and they kept sending him home. It wasn't until he ended up in the ER at St. Joseph's that anyone actually looked at the pattern and figured out his heart was doing something it shouldn't be. He has an arrhythmia now that he takes medication for every day. The whole thing scared the hell out of our family.⏎⏎So when I hear that hospitals want to use automated AI systems to speed up that kind of triage and diagnosis, I genuinely don't know how to feel. Part of me thinks, okay, maybe a computer would have caught the pattern sooner. But then I also think — the problem with Marco wasn't that the doctors were too slow, it was that nobody was putting all the pieces together. He was going to different clinics so none of them had his full history. Would an AI system even have access to records from multiple separate clinics? I don't know how that works.⏎⏎What worries me more than anything is the idea that these systems get used to speed things up in a way that means less time with an actual person who can ask you follow-up questions. I work as a firefighter and I've been on calls where the dispatch information said one thing and the situation on the ground was completely different. You need someone using judgment, not just checking boxes. I'd imagine medicine is at least a little similar.⏎⏎I don't have specific regulatory suggestions or whatever because honestly I don't know enough about how this stuff works on the policy side. But if someone at HHS is reading these I guess I just want to ask — is there going to be a requirement that a real human reviews whatever the AI recommends before it affects someone's care? That feels like the minimum to me. And what happens when the AI is wrong and someone gets hurt, who's responsible for that?⏎⏎Sorry if this isn't the right format or I'm supposed to be answering specific numbered questions. I couldn't find a clear template.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0155♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Randhawa, Pal, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Pal♔Randhawa♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001 (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Submitted by: Pal Randhawa, MD, EMBA, FAANS Email: prandhawa@gmail.com Capacity:⏎Personal Capacity Date: January 19, 2026⏎Submitter Background I am a board-certified neurosurgeon with approximately 15 years of⏎frontline clinical experience across emergency departments, operating rooms, and intensive⏎care units, including sustained work in Level I trauma and referral centers. In addition to my⏎clinical role, I hold an Executive MBA (EMBA) with a focus on healthcare operations and⏎infrastructure resilience. This dual perspective allows for the integration of tactical clinical⏎realities with the strategic resource management required for national health security.⏎My experience includes firsthand observation of workforce strain, capacity degradation, and⏎coordination failures under stress, as well as participation in collaborative and advisory efforts⏎related to disaster preparedness and national critical infrastructure discussions.⏎These comments are submitted in my personal capacity. The views expressed are my own and⏎do not represent any employer, institution, or advisory body.⏎Executive Summary: The Case for "Deflationary Infrastructure" The U.S. Healthcare Sector⏎operates beyond safe margins, with the AAMC projecting a deficit of up to 86,000 physicians by⏎2036. This submission argues that Artificial Intelligence should be prioritized as a "Deflationary⏎Infrastructure"—a protective capability designed to preserve the system’s most expensive and⏎fragile asset: its staffed clinical capacity.⏎Proposed Doctrinal Standard: We propose that HHS adopt Workforce Readiness Telemetry⏎(WRT) as a critical interoperability standard.⏎Definition: Workforce Readiness Telemetry refers to anonymized, aggregate⏎indicators of operational strain, cognitive load, and staffing fragility. WRT is⏎intended for system-level planning and readiness assessment and is explicitly not⏎designed for individual performance evaluation, disciplinary action, or employment⏎decisions. It is intended to complement existing interoperability frameworks (e.g.,⏎USCDI, TEFCA), not replace them—focusing on upstream operational inputs that⏎precede clinical events.⏎II. Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎Question 1: Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Adoption BLUF: Adoption is throttled⏎by a "Readiness Bind." Hospitals lack the cognitive bandwidth to adopt tools that do not⏎immediately reduce operational friction.⏎● The Gap: HHS currently lacks a standardized metric for "Human Capacity." Staffing is⏎measured in "FTEs" (Full-Time Equivalents), a lagging indicator that fails to capture⏎⏎burnout, moral injury, or disengagement. A facility may appear fully staffed on paper, yet⏎remain operationally degraded due to unmeasured cognitive load.⏎● The Solution: Prioritize AI that serves as Operational Radar. Tools that predict attrition⏎30–60 days in advance allow administrators to intervene proactively, reducing the multi-⏎million dollar annual cost of turnover. Importantly, WRT leverages existing metadata⏎streams and minimal viable indicators, making it low-burden for frontline systems.⏎○ Strategic Alignment: This directly operationalizes NHSS 2023-2026 Objective 1.3⏎("Strengthen the recruitment, retention, and preparedness of the HPH⏎workforce"), moving workforce retention from a passive goal to an active,⏎measured capability.⏎○ Data Support: While nursing turnover costs the average hospital $5.7M annually,⏎the loss of a single high-acuity physician costs $500,000 to >$1M in replacement⏎costs alone, consistent with recent AMA and AMN Healthcare analyses.⏎○ Revenue Impact: For high-revenue specialties (e.g., Neurosurgery), a six-month⏎vacancy results in >$3M in lost clinical capacity. AI that preserves this capacity⏎represents a critical form of deflationary infrastructure.⏎Question 3: Governance and Implementation of Non-Medical Device AI BLUF: Workflow-⏎embedded AI falls into a "Liability Gap" that chills adoption. Frontline clinicians currently bear a⏎disproportionate share of the risk for AI-influenced workflows.⏎● The Gap: FDA regulation covers devices; it does not cover "Algorithm-Augmented⏎Workflow." In practice, frontline clinicians often shoulder the ambiguity and accountability⏎for AI-influenced workflows—creating adoption hesitancy in high-stakes care.⏎● Recommendation: HHS’s internal AI Governance Board should advocate for the⏎exploration of time-limited federal safe-harbor mechanisms during declared staffing⏎emergencies. If a clinician follows a federally validated AI protocol during a crisis, they⏎should enjoy liability protection. This mechanism is essential for deploying AI in high-⏎stakes environments without paralyzing the workforce.⏎Question 8: Interoperability to Accelerate AI Adoption BLUF: Interoperability must expand⏎beyond Clinical Data (USCDI) to include Operational Telemetry.⏎● The Gap: Current interoperability standards track the patient but ignore the system.⏎● The Solution: Create a standard for sharing "Aggregate Readiness Signals" (e.g., Bed⏎Capacity + Staffing Viability).⏎● Strategic Outcome: This enables Regional and National Surge Prediction. Beyond⏎daily operations, aggregated telemetry creates a real-time heat map for mass casualty,⏎pandemic, or geopolitical events (aligning with NDAA Section 735 readiness⏎requirements). This allows federal planners to load-balance across the national grid⏎before regional collapse occurs.⏎● The ONC Role: The ONC is uniquely positioned to define this operational layer in a way⏎that preserves trust, minimizes burden, and enables downstream innovation, ensuring⏎that readiness data becomes a national asset rather than a proprietary silo.⏎⏎Question 10: Prioritized Areas of Research BLUF: Research must validate AI as a tool for⏎Cognitive Augmentation, not just automation.⏎● Priority 1: Cognitive Load Mapping. The primary safety endpoint for clinical AI should⏎be: Does this tool reduce time-to-decision or increase cognitive burden?⏎● Priority 2: Degraded-State Validation. AI tools must be tested for reliability under⏎"Degraded Conditions" (e.g., cyber outages, communications latency, mass casualty⏎surges, or geopolitical conflict). This aligns with CISA’s 2025 Cybersecurity Performance⏎Goals for critical infrastructure resilience.⏎Conclusion: The Aviation Parallels Successful high-reliability sectors (Aviation, Energy) do⏎not use technology to replace the expert operator; they use it to augment situational awareness⏎and prevent error. Healthcare AI should follow this "Human-System Teaming" doctrine. By⏎shifting focus from "replacing doctors" to "stabilizing the grid," HHS can transform AI from a⏎disruptive novelty into a stabilizing component of national health security.⏎References⏎1. ASPR (2023). National Health Security Strategy (NHSS) 2023-2026.⏎2. American Medical Association (2025). Organizational Cost of Physician Burnout.⏎3. NSI Nursing Solutions, Inc. (2025). 2025 National Health Care Retention & RN Staffing⏎Report.⏎4. AMN Healthcare (2025). The Cost of Physician Turnover and Economic Impact.⏎5. The White House (2024). National Security Memorandum on Critical Infrastructure⏎Security and Resilience (NSM-22).⏎6. CISA (2025). Cybersecurity Performance Goals (CPG 2.0).⏎7. Association of American Medical Colleges (2024). The Complexities of Physician Supply⏎and Demand.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0156♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from OCHIN, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔OCHIN♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via Federal Register⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎On behalf of OCHIN, thank you for the opportunity to comment on the Department of Health and⏎Human Services’ (HHS) Request for Information on “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.” Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to support earlier⏎intervention, improve chronic disease management, and increase efficiency across care delivery.⏎Achieving these outcomes at scale, however, requires that AI adoption be affordable, trusted, and⏎grounded in evidence generated through routine clinical care, particularly among rural and low-⏎resourced providers.⏎For rural and low‑resourced providers, affordability is inseparable from sustainable infrastructure. AI⏎adoption depends not only on the maturity of algorithms, but on access to modern health IT systems,⏎shared‑service operating models, strong governance, and a workforce prepared for new digital roles—⏎capabilities that are often cost‑prohibitive to build independently. Trust in clinical AI must be established⏎through real‑world evidence generated in local care settings, allowing performance, safety, and⏎workflow impact to be validated over time rather than inferred from isolated pilots. Without this⏎foundation, AI risks increasing fragmentation rather than productivity, particularly among providers in⏎rural and low resourced settings.⏎OCHIN: Shared Infrastructure Built for Trust, Affordability, and Scale⏎OCHIN is a nonprofit shared health IT and services organization supporting more than 300 independent⏎providers across 44 states, including rural health clinics, federally qualified health centers, tribal clinics,⏎and critical access hospitals. We support nearly 44,000 providers nationwide, generating real‑world⏎evidence from routine clinical care across rural, tribal, and other historically underserved communities.⏎Our experience demonstrates that AI tools that perform well in rural and low‑resourced settings—⏎where staffing is limited, workflows are stretched, and tolerance for disruption is low—represent the⏎true value proposition of clinical AI for the healthcare system as a whole. In October 2025 alone, OCHIN⏎network members used generative AI features nearly 700,000 times, and by the end of 2025, more than⏎20 AI‑enabled tools were live across the network.⏎Our shared‑services model enables thin‑margin providers in rural and least‑resourced communities to⏎access enterprise‑grade technology, cybersecurity, compliance, governance, and workforce training at a⏎fraction of the cost of building and maintaining those capabilities independently. Fixed costs are spread⏎across a national network. Duplication is reduced. Accountability is strengthened through centralized⏎⏎governance where appropriate. Federal investments go further. This shared infrastructure is⏎fundamental to affordability at both the provider and system level. For rural and low‑resourced⏎organizations, shared services are not optional enhancements; they are the only viable path to⏎sustaining modern health IT and responsible AI adoption without diverting scarce resources away from⏎patient care. Our learnings from AI adoption in the OCHIN network are included in the Appendix.⏎Recommendations⏎We respectfully recommend that HHS prioritize the following policies for rural and low‑resourced⏎settings, leveraging existing trusted partners with established track records and mature operating⏎models:⏎• Coordinate across HHS agencies and with the Department of Labor to fund health IT workforce⏎development and AI skilling. As care delivery becomes increasingly digital, AI adoption depends on⏎a dependable operating backbone and a workforce equipped to implement, govern, and monitor AI⏎across clinical and operational workflows. Targeted investment enables AI to function as a true⏎capacity multiplier.⏎• Underwrite shared‑service models that support the validation, deployment, and ongoing⏎monitoring of clinical AI. Federal investment in network‑based AI services can reduce duplicative⏎cost and governance burden, enable consistent and locally appropriate evaluation of AI in real‑world⏎care settings, and create economies of scale that accelerate responsible adoption.⏎• Invest in modernized health IT systems for rural and low‑resourced providers operating on⏎antiquated platforms. AI‑enabled tools depend on interoperable data exchange, secure⏎infrastructure, and modern core systems. Targeted modernization investment reduces cybersecurity⏎risk and ensures AI can be deployed safely where need is greatest.⏎• Advance regional technical assistance resource centers to support AI adoption in clinical care.⏎Even when tools and infrastructure exist, many providers lack hands‑on expertise to integrate AI⏎into workflows and navigate evolving regulatory requirements. Regional centers can accelerate⏎learning and reduce implementation risk.⏎Conclusion⏎Healthcare is entering a period defined by constrained resources and increasing demand. The question⏎is not whether AI will be adopted, but whether it will be deployed in ways that increase productivity,⏎strengthen rural access, and improve long‑term outcomes. That outcome depends on AI adoption⏎grounded in affordable, trusted infrastructure and evidence generated through real‑world clinical use,⏎particularly in rural and low‑resourced settings that test whether innovation truly delivers value.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide input and welcome continued partnership in advancing⏎infrastructure‑based AI adoption that delivers measurable value for patients, providers, and taxpayers⏎alike.⏎Please contact me at stollj@ochin.org if we can provide any additional information support your efforts.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jennifer Stoll⏎Chief External Affairs Officer⏎2⏎⏎APPENDIX⏎Operating at national scale also enables OCHIN to serve as a⏎Lowering Costs by Leveraging AI Tools⏎trusted partner by generating evidence through routine clinical⏎to Optimize Clinical Documentation⏎use. Because AI‑enabled capabilities are deployed across a⏎diverse network of providers, performance, workflow impact, OCHIN evaluated two AI-enabled ambient⏎and operational risk can be observed and validated over time in documentation tools and found positive ROI,⏎real‑world care settings. This shared infrastructure supports with observed improvements in⏎documentation efficiency, clinician⏎consistent implementation and centralized oversight, while⏎productivity, and workflow performance.⏎enabling local validation within the clinical environments where⏎AI is actually used. • DAX Copilot: Estimated return of $5.50⏎per every $1 invested, assuming a 70%⏎Trust, Local Validation, and the Rural Value Proposition reduction in burnout and 50% reduction in⏎documentation time.⏎Trust in clinical AI depends on evidence generated in the⏎• Abridge: Estimated return of $5 per every⏎populations and workflows where tools are deployed.⏎$1 invested, assuming a 60% reduction in⏎Shared‑service models support local validation within a⏎documentation time and a 10% E&M⏎consistent governance framework, allowing performance,⏎undercoding rate.⏎safety, and workflow impact to be evaluated over time without⏎duplicative, site‑by‑site build. Validation, oversight, data stewardship, and regulatory alignment are⏎supported centrally, while preserving local clinical autonomy.⏎Within this infrastructure, AI tools are embedded directly into clinical and operational workflows⏎supporting chronic disease risk stratification, care coordination, documentation efficiency, revenue cycle⏎integrity, and population health management. These use cases are tested daily in rural and⏎low‑resourced environments where staffing shortages, broadband constraints, and complex patient⏎needs magnify both the risks and rewards of AI adoption. When AI tools demonstrate value in these⏎settings by reducing burden, improving access, and supporting clinical decision‑making, they are more⏎likely to scale safely and sustainably across the broader healthcare system.⏎What We Have Learned⏎• Trust in clinical AI is built through evidence generated under local conditions. Providers, patients,⏎and regulators gain confidence when AI performance is validated in care settings serving similar⏎populations, rather than extrapolated from pilots in well‑resourced systems.⏎• Healthcare faces sustained fiscal and workforce constraints alongside growing demand. AI must⏎help the system do more with less by increasing productivity, not by preserving legacy⏎administrative structures. AI adoption changes work. It does not eliminate the need for people, but⏎it does require new competencies in AI oversight, workflow redesign, data governance,⏎cybersecurity, preventive care coordination, and digital population health management.⏎• Organizational capacity, not algorithm maturity alone, determines whether AI reduces burden or⏎adds complexity. Rural and low‑resourced providers face the greatest constraints in margins and⏎staffing, limiting their ability to independently build the operational, governance, and validation⏎capabilities required for sustained AI use.⏎• Shared workforce development models scale effectively when delivered through trusted⏎infrastructure. Through the federally supported Health Center Controlled Network (HCCN) program⏎and national digital health training initiatives, OCHIN delivers health IT and AI workforce⏎development across rural and low‑resourced care settings, demonstrating that workforce readiness⏎can be advanced efficiently through shared models rather than site‑by‑site investment.⏎3⏎⏎• Strategic federal investment in workforce transition and AI skilling is productivity policy, not job⏎preservation policy. Targeted investment ensures that AI adoption strengthens access and⏎long‑term sustainability rather than increasing operational strain or widening gaps for providers⏎with the least capacity to absorb change.⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0157♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from xCures, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔xCures♔♔♔♔♔♔Date: 2/20/26⏎To: Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), Office of the National Coordinator (ONC)⏎Re: RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (Docket No.⏎2025-23641)⏎From: xCures, Inc.⏎Executive Summary⏎xCures operates an AI-assisted healthcare data platform that extracts and structures clinical⏎information from medical records gathered across multiple sources, including from health information⏎networks like TEFCA and Carequality. This platform serves as a semantic layer for healthcare data,⏎delivering decision-ready intelligence that empowers professionals to act with confidence and clarity.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to comment on how HHS can accelerate AI adoption in clinical⏎care. Our experience processing millions of patient records across the U.S. healthcare system suggests⏎that the primary barrier to AI efficacy is not algorithm availability, but data readiness—specifically, the⏎inability of standard systems to process unstructured clinical evidence (PDFs, faxes, and images) where⏎the most critical medical insights reside.⏎We respectfully submit the following responses to specific questions in the RFI, advocating for a⏎regulatory framework that prioritizes Source Verifiability (audit trails), Universal Participation, and⏎Semantic Interoperability.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care?⏎Barrier A: The "Unstructured Data" Cliff⏎The greatest technical barrier is the industry’s over-reliance on structured data feeds (HL7/FHIR) which⏎often fail to capture the complete clinical picture.⏎● The Reality: While a majority of health data transmission may technically be structured⏎(XML/JSON), in reality the high-value clinical indicators—pathology reports, genomic staging, and⏎discharge summaries—predominantly reside in unstructured formats like PDFs, faxes, and⏎scanned images.⏎● The Barrier: Current "standard" interoperability often dumps these files as raw blobs that generic⏎AI models struggle to interpret accurately. Without an infrastructure layer capable of normalizing⏎this unstructured multimedia data into compute-ready formats, clinical AI will remain limited to⏎basic administrative tasks rather than deep clinical decision support.⏎Barrier B: Data Access and Network Participation⏎Even when data is digital, another barrier is just getting access to all the data. Not all holders of⏎patient's health data are participating in TEFCA.⏎⏎● The Barrier: This non-universal participation in health information networks means AI⏎models cannot build a complete, longitudinal view of the patient, leading to⏎"hallucinations" born of missing context.⏎● The Solution: Innovation requires a federal requirement or penalty for not participating⏎in TEFCA's treatment and individual access services pathway, which are critical for⏎healthcare providers and patients to have access to their longitudinal data in order to⏎use that for AI. Streamlining access through mandatory participation and seamless⏎access would allow healthcare providers and patients to rapidly aggregate the necessary⏎data to power clinical AI, reducing the cost and time delays currently imposed by⏎fragmented retrieval processes.⏎2. What regulatory or payment policy changes should HHS prioritize?⏎Incentivize "Data Completeness" and Administrative Reduction:⏎HHS should prioritize payment models that reimburse for the completeness of clinical documentation,⏎not just the volume of care.⏎● Reimbursement Leakage: Healthcare organizations currently face significant revenue loss due to⏎missing or incomplete documentation. This "administrative friction" delays patient care (e.g.,⏎prior authorization bottlenecks).⏎● Recommendation: CMS should allow AI-verified data (with direct links to source documents) to⏎automatically satisfy documentation requirements for Prior Authorization and Quality Reporting⏎(e.g., NCQA ECDS). This would incentivize the adoption of AI tools that solve the "missing data"⏎problem, directly reducing administrative burden.⏎4. & 5. What are the most promising AI evaluation methods? How can HHS support⏎certification?⏎The "Trust but Verify" Standard:⏎HHS must distinguish between "Black Box" AI (which summarizes without proof) and Source-Verified AI.⏎● Safety Risk: Generative AI that summarizes patient history without an audit trail poses a⏎significant safety risk (hallucinations).⏎● Proposed Standard: We advocate that any AI tool used for clinical decision-making or⏎reimbursement must meet a "Source Linking" standard. Every extracted clinical fact (e.g.,⏎"Patient has Stage 4 Breast Cancer") must be digitally linked to the specific page, document, and⏎timestamp of the source record.⏎● Validation: Validation metrics should not just measure "accuracy" against a gold standard, but⏎the speed and ability of a human reviewer to verify the AI's claim via these source links. This⏎"human-in-the-loop" architecture is the only safe path for clinical AI.⏎● Ongoing Quality Control: Initial validation is insufficient given the rapid evolution of AI⏎technology. We recommend regulatory frameworks mandate ongoing quality controls—such as⏎⏎continuous monitoring and regression testing—to ensure that AI tools remain trustworthy and⏎stable as models and data sources evolve.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities?⏎A. Mandate Universal Network Participation⏎To widen market opportunities for AI, interoperability must be universal, not optional.⏎● The Problem: Currently, participation in networks like TEFCA is voluntary, leaving significant gaps⏎in coverage—particularly among smaller practices, rural clinics, and specialists who may still rely⏎on fax. AI models trained on or operating with "Swiss cheese" data (data with holes) are⏎inherently unsafe and biased.⏎● Recommendation: HHS should prioritize regulatory levers that move towards mandatory⏎participation in Health Information Networks for all providers. Universal participation ensures⏎that AI agents have access to the entire patient journey, not just the episodes that occurred in⏎large, tech-enabled health systems.⏎B. From "Transport" to "Semantic" Interoperability⏎Current interoperability frameworks (TEFCA, QHINs) excel at transporting files but fail at semantic⏎understanding.⏎● The Gap: Moving a PDF from Hospital A to Hospital B is "interoperability," but it does not help an⏎AI system understand the patient's history.⏎● Evidence: In representative analyses of patients with complex conditions (e.g., advanced⏎oncology), we found that relying solely on standard structured data feeds resulted in missing⏎approximately 88% of relevant Hierarchical Condition Category (HCC) coding opportunities⏎compared to AI-enhanced extraction from unstructured notes.⏎● Recommendation: HHS should broaden the definition of "interoperability" to include the capacity⏎to parse, normalize, and structure unstructured data. True interoperability is not just moving the⏎file; it is making the content of the file machine-readable for downstream AI applications.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0158♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Crays, Allyson, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Allyson♔Crays♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary of Health and Human Services⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The undersigned 125 public health and health policy deans, chairs, and scholars (in their⏎individual capacities) file this response to the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS)⏎December 23, 2025, request for information about accelerating the adoption and use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.1 Our response addresses several of HHS’s questions⏎about the use of AI in the context of healthcare. Specifically, it addresses patient privacy and⏎data confidentiality issues, AI evaluation methods, where AI clinical tools have performed well⏎and where they have fallen short, the challenges and concerns expressed by patients and⏎caregivers, and published findings about AI clinical care tools. We conclude that regulation and⏎oversight are needed by the Department of Health and Human Services to ensure that the use of⏎AI in clinical care reaches its maximum potential to increase access to healthcare and to avoid⏎the potential harms associated with the use of AI in clinical care.⏎The individual signatories are distinguished deans, chairs, and scholars at the nation’s leading⏎academic institutions and research universities. They are experts in the fields of health law,⏎public health, healthcare policy and research, and national health reform. They include⏎individuals known for their expertise in health information privacy and security law and ethics,⏎AI ethics, and health policy regarding clinical care. The individual signatories join this comment⏎in their individual capacities and not as representatives of their respective institutions. The⏎complete list of individual signatories is included at the end of this letter.⏎Question #3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures. What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role,⏎if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Many AI tools currently shaping clinical care are not regulated as medical devices. Examples⏎include patient messaging assistants, coding/prior authorization support, care gap detection,⏎triage/routing, guideline retrieval, ambient documentation, and notes summarization. In practice,⏎these AI tools still influence clinical decisions, clinical documentation, patient decision-making,⏎patient trust, and access to care. Therefore, creating accountability issues that are not well⏎1 Request for information: Accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence as part of clinical care, 90 Fed.⏎Reg. 60108–60110 (Dec. 23, 2025). https://www.federalregister.gov/d/2025-23641⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 2⏎covered by existing privacy practices, transparency norms, liability frameworks, or civil rights⏎compliance workflows. Key issues are summarized below.⏎AI’s indefinite storage of health data and periodic data leaks can threaten patient privacy.⏎Training complex AI models depends on the assembly of large collections of health data about⏎patients, which AI models “learn” from and “remember” for future use.2 Unlike traditional data⏎storage software, this information cannot simply be deleted from AI models.3 Instead, it is stored⏎indefinitely, and the models internalize the data to identify patterns and generate inferences.4,5⏎There are inherent risks associated with this indefinite storage, including the risk of data leaks.⏎Data leaks could expose sensitive health information, threaten patient privacy, lead to personal⏎consequences such as discrimination and stigmatization, and threaten patient trust in the medical⏎profession and public health.⏎AI data leaks result from common vulnerabilities in AI models, such as bugs and hacks.6 For⏎example, in 2023, a ChatGPT bug allowed users to see titles from another user’s chat history and⏎unintentionally made payment-related information visible.7 While this bug was quickly patched,⏎experts warn that all AI software systems are built upon other software components that may⏎include weaknesses that can be exploited.8 In another example, researchers testing the privacy of⏎open-source ChatGPT models found that they could input certain commands that would confuse⏎the model, causing it to release personally identifiable information, including email addresses,⏎names, phone numbers, and pictures.9⏎The integration of AI in clinical settings creates new opportunities for bad actors to launch⏎ransomware attacks, as they may exploit existing bugs, inject prompts that allow them to gain⏎access to databases, or prey on the human errors of users of clinical AI.10 One analysis of real⏎incidents of AI cybersecurity attacks found that this problem has huge ramifications for⏎2 Higa, H., Bedikian, S., & Costa, L. (2025, May 20). The right to be forgotten is dead: Data lives forever in AI.⏎Tech Policy Press. https://www.techpolicy.press/the-right-to-be-forgotten-is-dead-data-lives-forever-in-ai/⏎3 aiunlearning. (2025). Why deleting data does not remove it from AI models. Medium.⏎https://medium.com/@aiunlearning/why-deleting-data-does-not-remove-it-from-ai-models-c08c3c37863a⏎4 Kak, A. (2025). Prepared testimony and statement for the record: “The need to protect Americans’ privacy and⏎the AI accelerant” before the U.S. Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation. AI Now Institute.⏎https://www.commerce.senate.gov/services/files/35C1CE13-F03D-4CE9-9559-B0651F156D86⏎5 Mitchell, H. (2024, January 18). Is it safe to share personal information with a chatbot? The Wall Street Journal.⏎https://www.wsj.com/tech/ai/ai-chatbot-sharing-personal-information-229d41a0⏎6 Kak, A. (2025). Prepared testimony and statement for the record: “The need to protect Americans’ privacy and⏎the AI accelerant” before the U.S. Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation. AI Now Institute.⏎https://www.commerce.senate.gov/services/files/35C1CE13-F03D-4CE9-9559-B0651F156D86⏎7 OpenAI. (2023, March 23). March 20 ChatGPT outage: Here’s what happened. https://openai.com/index/march-⏎20-chatgpt-outage/⏎8 Kaganovich, M., & Chuvakin, A. (2024, December 5). Oops! 5 serious gen AI security mistakes to avoid. Google⏎Cloud Blog. https://cloud.google.com/transform/oops-5-serious-gen-ai-security-mistakes-to-avoid⏎9 Nasr, M., Carlini, N., Hayase, J., Jagielski, M., Cooper, A. F., Ippolito, D., Choquette-Choo, C. A., Wallace, E.,⏎Tramèr, F., & Lee, K. (2023, November 28). Extracting training data from ChatGPT. https://not-just-⏎memorization.github.io/extracting-training-data-from-chatgpt.html⏎10 Sallam, M., Al-Mahzoum, K., & Sallam, M. (2024). Generative artificial intelligence and cybersecurity risks:⏎Implications for healthcare security based on real-life incidents. Mesopotamian Journal of Artificial Intelligence in⏎Healthcare, 2024, 184-203. https://doi.org/10.58496/MJAIH/2024/019⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 3⏎electronic health records, which could undermine the confidentiality and integrity of patient data⏎and can hinder access to healthcare systems.11 The more sensitive data that is collected, the more⏎these AI models become an attractive target for malicious actors.12 From 2016 to 2021, 374⏎ransomware attacks on U.S. healthcare organizations exposed the personal health information of⏎42 million patients, with incidents doubling annually.13⏎The limited safeguards on AI models may incentivize AI developers to use irresponsible data⏎collection and storage practices. Collection limitations are needed.⏎A key feature of large-scale AI systems is that they are very expensive to develop and run.14,15 In⏎light of these market pressures, developers may not prioritize investments in privacy and may⏎engage in risky or unsound data practices. For example, there are very limited safeguards⏎surrounding the sensitive data collected by AI-powered “therapists” – and this very sensitive user⏎chat history may be stored for extended periods and used to train other models.16,17,18 Since⏎complex AI models require immense amounts of data to evolve and improve, developers have⏎little incentive to minimize the collection and use of even the most intimate patient information.⏎The limited safeguards on AI models may incentivize organizations to use data collected by⏎these models to maximize profit. Use limitations are needed.⏎Currently, there is limited regulation dictating how organizations can use the patient data that AI⏎collects. This can expose patients to exploitation, since organizations may use this data to⏎maximize profit. For example, it has been well-documented that health insurance plans have⏎been using patient data to generate predictions of medical needs and lengths of stay - irrespective⏎of individual mitigating circumstances – and these predictions are then used to deny claims and⏎11 Sallam, M., Al-Mahzoum, K., & Sallam, M. (2024). Generative artificial intelligence and cybersecurity risks:⏎Implications for healthcare security based on real-life incidents. Mesopotamian Journal of Artificial Intelligence in⏎Healthcare, 2024, 184–203. https://doi.org/10.58496/MJAIH/2024/019⏎12 Kak, A. (2025). Prepared testimony and statement for the record: “The need to protect Americans’ privacy and⏎the AI accelerant” before the U.S. Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation. AI Now Institute.⏎https://www.commerce.senate.gov/services/files/35C1CE13-F03D-4CE9-9559-B0651F156D86⏎13 Neprash, H. T., McGlave, C. C., Cross, D. A., et al. (2022). Trends in ransomware attacks on US hospitals,⏎clinics, and other health care delivery organizations, 2016–2021. JAMA Health Forum, 3(12), e224873.⏎https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2022.4873⏎14 Henshall, W. (2024, June 3). The billion-dollar price tag of building AI. Time. https://time.com/6984292/cost-⏎artificial-intelligence-compute-epoch-report/⏎15 Kak, A. (2025). Prepared testimony and statement for the record: “The need to protect Americans’ privacy and⏎the AI accelerant” before the U.S. Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, and Transportation. AI Now Institute.⏎https://www.commerce.senate.gov/services/files/35C1CE13-F03D-4CE9-9559-B0651F156D86⏎16 Patel, N. (2025, October 30). The risks of using patient and provider chat histories to train AI—and how to⏎mitigate them. Health Affairs Forefront. https://doi.org/10.1377/forefront.20251028.618832⏎17 Coghlan, S., Leins, K., Sheldrick, S., Cheong, M., Gooding, P., & D'Alfonso, S. (2023). To chat or bot to chat:⏎Ethical issues with using chatbots in mental health. Digital health, 9, 20552076231183542.⏎https://doi.org/10.1177/20552076231183542⏎18 Seigel, R., Augenstein, J., Shashoua, M., Slater, C., & Irlbeck, C. (2025). Manatt Health: Health AI policy⏎tracker. Manatt, Phelps & Phillips, LLP. https://www.manatt.com/insights/newsletters/health-highlights/manatt-⏎health-health-ai-policy-tracker⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 4⏎shorten hospital visits.19 The use of AI models has made it especially difficult for patients to⏎challenge these care denials, since the AI technology used is often proprietary, and there is⏎limited transparency for the AI algorithms used.20⏎There is a lack of transparency regarding when AI is being used.⏎Even when AI is considered “assistive”, the lack of transparency about the AI model and its use⏎can create risk. Clinicians may not always know when outputs are AI-generated, and patients⏎may not know when AI is used in messaging, triage, or documentation.⏎Additionally, patients may not even be aware that they are “opting in” to use AI models and for⏎their data to be stored and used indefinitely. There are also concerns with patients’ ability to “opt⏎in” to the use of their data.21 Even if a patient “opts in” to share their information for a specific⏎purpose, there is no guarantee that the data will not be used for purposes outside the parameters⏎to which they originally agreed to.22⏎Bias in AI models can deprioritize and discriminate against specific patient groups.⏎It has been well-documented that AI algorithms inherit, amplify, and perpetuate existing⏎healthcare disparities present in training data, such as underrepresentation of minority groups and⏎unequal care quality.23, 24 This leads to inherent bias in AI models, which can impact triage,⏎routing, care denials, coding, and documentation. These biases have real-world implications and⏎can worsen health outcomes; for example, AI algorithms have required Black patients to be⏎19 Ross, C., & Herman, B. (2023, March 13). Denied by AI: How Medicare Advantage plans use algorithms to cut⏎off care for seniors in need. STAT News. https://www.statnews.com/2023/03/13/medicare-advantage-plans-denial-⏎artificial-intelligence/⏎20 Osman, M., Paul, E., & Weil, E. (2025, October 21). Calculated need: Algorithms and the fight for Medicaid⏎home care: A comparison of five states' eligibility algorithms. Upturn. https://www.upturn.org/work/calculated-⏎need/⏎21 McNair, D., & Price, W. N., II. (2022). Health care artificial intelligence: Law, regulation, and policy. In the⏎National Academy of Medicine, Artificial intelligence in health care: The hope, the hype, the promise, the peril (pp.⏎167–194). National Academies Press. https://www.nationalacademies.org/read/27111/chapter/9⏎22 Kondrup, E. (2025, April 11). Informed consent, redefined: How AI and big data are changing the rules. Petrie-⏎Flom Center to Health Law Policy, Biotechnology, and Bioethics, Harvard Law School, Bill of Health Blog.⏎https://petrieflom.law.harvard.edu/2025/04/11/informed-consent-redefined-how-ai-and-big-data-are-changing-the-⏎rules/⏎23 Cross, J. L., Choma, M. A., & Onofrey, J. A. (2024). Bias in medical AI: Implications for clinical decision-⏎making. PLOS Digital Health, 3(11), e0000651. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0000651⏎24 Ranard, B. L., Park, S., Jia, Y., Zhang, Y., Alwan, F., Celi, L. A., & Lusczek, E. R. (2024). Minimizing bias when⏎using artificial intelligence in critical care medicine. Journal of critical care, 82, 154796.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcrc.2024.154796⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 5⏎considerably sicker to qualify for support than their White counterparts, and other AI algorithms⏎have proposed inferior medical treatments for patients of minority descent. 25,26,27⏎When AI errors occur, accountability is unclear.⏎Non-medical device AI frequently impacts care directly, as it is used to draft clinical notes,⏎prioritize patient messages or documentation, suggest coding, surface “high risk” patients, and⏎more. When AI informs decisions and errors occur, it is unclear whether accountability should⏎rest on the AI vendor/company, the clinician, and/or the healthcare organization. Although the⏎tool is positioned as “administrative support”, its outputs can shape clinical judgement,⏎documentation quality, and downstream care decisions – and accountability mechanisms are⏎needed. To help create these accountability mechanisms, organizations need standardized⏎requirements for: 1) human oversight expectations, 2) documenting when AI was used, and 3)⏎how to handle AI model updates and performance drifts.⏎What HHS can do⏎ HHS can protect patient privacy by issuing practical guidance on minimum privacy⏎safeguards for non-medical device clinical AI. As HHS considers its role in regulating⏎AI in clinical care, it should emphasize patient privacy and data confidentiality to avoid⏎the improper exposure of sensitive health information. Entities using AI models should⏎have a proactive obligation to place reasonable limits on the collection, use, and retention⏎of personal data, also known as privacy by design. Minimum privacy safeguards should⏎emphasize:⏎o Data minimization in all uses of AI in clinical care and clear data retention limits.⏎These are some of the best practices to protect patient privacy.⏎o Clear use limitations and default restrictions on secondary use of data.⏎o Inclusion of a standard “AI Data Use Disclosure” form for clinical settings that⏎discloses how and when patient data is used, whether data is used for training, and⏎how long it is retained. When AI models are used, this should be clear and⏎transparent, and should allow patients to opt out of algorithmic decision-making.⏎o Use of secure reference architectures to reduce data exposure (e.g., secure⏎enclaves, tokenization, local processing of data or community processing, etc.).28⏎Implementation grants can be provided to under-resourced and safety-net⏎providers to fund this architecture.⏎25 Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the⏎health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447–453. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎26 Bouguettaya, A., Stuart, E. M., & Aboujaoude, E. (2025). Racial bias in AI-mediated psychiatric diagnosis and⏎treatment: A qualitative comparison of four large language models. NPJ Digital Medicine, 8(1), Article 332.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01746-4⏎27 Cedars-Sinai. (2025, June 30). Cedars-Sinai study shows racial bias in AI-generated treatment regimens for⏎psychiatric patients. https://www.cedars-sinai.org/newsroom/cedars-sinai-study-shows-racial-bias-in-ai-generated-⏎treatment-regimens-for-psychiatric-patients/⏎28 Department of Health and Human Services (2024). HIPAA Security Rule Notice of Proposed Rulemaking to⏎Strengthen Cybersecurity for Electronic Protected Health Information. https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-⏎professionals/security/hipaa-security-rule-nprm/factsheet/index.html⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 6⏎ HHS should publish a non-medical device clinical AI “governance” playbook that⏎clarifies the minimum expectations for human oversight over the AI, override and⏎stop-use criteria, documentation/audit logging, and change control for model update⏎(including when re-validation/re-evaluation is needed). HHS can develop model⏎procurement or contracting clauses that address these performance monitoring⏎responsibilities, especially for smaller and safety net providers that lack negotiating⏎leverage.⏎ HHS should establish minimum transparency expectations for non-medical device⏎AI used in clinical care. Minimum expectations for transparency should include⏎intended use, data sources, known failure modes, policies surrounding AI model updates,⏎and a monitoring plan. There should also be transparency regarding auditable logs for⏎quality review and incident investigations.⏎ HHS should provide a pragmatic framework for impact assessment and civil rights⏎assessment.⏎To ensure accountability, HHS should create an actionable framework to guide impact⏎assessments to determine if privacy standards are met, whether particular groups are⏎harmed, and for real consequences for a failure to mitigate or prevent harms.⏎Question #4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes?⏎If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Experts widely agree that robust evaluation of AI in healthcare is critical to ensuring that the⏎technology is used responsibly. Yet a 2024 scoping review of evaluation methods for clinical AI⏎found “relative scarcity of evidence and guidance for specific practical implementation of⏎performance monitoring of clinical AI.” 29 The review found that most clinical AI evaluation⏎metrics focus on accuracy, with other, less common evaluation metrics including assessments of⏎discrimination and calibration (matching AI estimates to real-world outcomes).30⏎Emerging evaluation methods for non-medical device clinical AI⏎Since the 2024 scoping review, at least two promising methods have been published for non-⏎medical device clinical AI: 1) Stanford’s MedHELM (Holistic Evaluation of Language Models)⏎31 and 2) Elsevier’s five-domain evaluation framework (originally created to assess the⏎ClinicalKey generative AI).32 Both methods have been published in peer-reviewed journals and⏎have been tested on non-medical device generative AI in clinical settings.⏎29 Andersen, E. S., Birk-Korch, J. B., Hansen, R. S., Fly, L. H., Röttger, R., Arcani, D. M. C., Brasen, C. L.,⏎Brandslund, I., & Madsen, J. S. (2024). Monitoring performance of clinical artificial intelligence in health care: a⏎scoping review. JBI evidence synthesis, 22(12), 2423–2446. https://doi.org/10.11124/JBIES-24-00042⏎30 Id.⏎31 Bedi, S., Cui, H., Fuentes, M. et al. (2026). Holistic evaluation of large language models for medical tasks with⏎MedHELM. Nat Med. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-025-04151-2⏎32 Livingston, L., Featherstone-Uwague, A., Barry, A., Barretto, K., Morey, T., Herrmannova, D., & Avula, V.⏎(2025). Reproducible generative artificial intelligence evaluation for health care: a clinician-in-the-loop⏎approach. JAMIA Open, Volume 8, Issue 3. https://doi.org/10.1093/jamiaopen/ooaf054⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 7⏎Stanford’s MedHELM, developed by Stanford Human-Centered AI Center for Research on⏎Foundation Models, assesses AI model performance in 120 healthcare scenarios across 22⏎categories of health-related tasks, including those related to clinical decision support, clinical⏎note generation, patient communication and education, medical research assistance, and⏎administration and workflow.33 The MedHELM framework has been tested on six large language⏎models of varying sizes, including OpenAI, Llama, Gemini, and others.34 Overall, larger AI⏎models did well on complex reasoning tasks (e.g., performing medical calculations and in⏎detecting race bias in clinical text), medium models performed competitively on medical⏎prediction tasks with lower computational demands (e.g., predicting readmission risk), and small⏎models, while adequate for well-structured tasks, struggled with tasks requiring content expertise⏎(particularly in mental health counseling and medical knowledge assessment).35⏎Elsevier’s framework assesses generative clinical AI models across five dimensions: 1) query⏎comprehension, 2) response helpfulness, 3) response correctness, 4) response completeness, and⏎5) potential clinical harm.36,37 Elsevier originally developed this framework to assess ClinicalKey⏎AI – a generative AI that summarizes medical content to help providers make informed decisions⏎at point-of-care.38,39 The framework is unique in applying standardized rating scales across the⏎five dimensions, as well as including a robust subject matter expert agreement protocol (to⏎reduce individual subjectivity when assessing AI’s response).⏎Key considerations in AI evaluations⏎There are several key considerations that are needed in any evaluation of non-medical device⏎clinical AI:⏎1) Transparency is needed at all stages of AI model evaluation. The National Academy⏎of Medicine suggests several forms of transparency are needed, including: 1) disclosure⏎of where training data came from and how it was processed, 2) how the algorithm was⏎developed and validated, 3) how the algorithm reaches its results, and 4) how the pieces⏎of an AI system work together.40⏎33 Center for Research on Foundation Models. (n.d.). MedHELM: Holistic Evaluation of Large Language Models for⏎Medical Tasks. Stanford CRFM. https://crfm.stanford.edu/helm/medhelm/latest/#/⏎34 Armitage, H. (2025, April 8). Evaluating AI in context: Which LLM is best for real health care needs? Stanford⏎Medicine. https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2025/04/ai-artificial-intelligence-evaluation-algorithm.html⏎35 Shah, N., Pfeffer, M., & Liang, P. (2025, February 28). Holistic Evaluation of Large Language Models for⏎Medical Applications. Stanford University Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence.⏎https://hai.stanford.edu/news/holistic-evaluation-of-large-language-models-for-medical-applications⏎36 Evans, I. (2025, September 9). AI Evaluation in Clinical Decision Support: Building a Foundation of Trust.⏎Elsevier. https://www.elsevier.com/connect/ai-evaluation-in-clinical-decision-support-building-a-foundation-of-trust⏎37 Elsevier. (2025). ClinicalKey AI: Evaluation framework for generative AI tools used for clinical decision support.⏎https://assets.ctfassets.net/zlnfaxb2lcqx/tenR2MwLZzHVbrMUS1lAR/7d3a049ffc24da170913041323b01f78/Clinic⏎alKey_AI_Evaluation_Framework_WP_2025.pdf⏎38 Evans, I. (2025, September 9). AI Evaluation in Clinical Decision Support: Building a Foundation of Trust.⏎Elsevier. https://www.elsevier.com/connect/ai-evaluation-in-clinical-decision-support-building-a-foundation-of-trust⏎39 Elsevier. (2025). ClinicalKey AI: Evaluation framework for generative AI tools used for clinical decision support.⏎https://assets.ctfassets.net/zlnfaxb2lcqx/tenR2MwLZzHVbrMUS1lAR/7d3a049ffc24da170913041323b01f78/Clinic⏎alKey_AI_Evaluation_Framework_WP_2025.pdf⏎40 Whicher, D., Ahmed, M., Israni, S.T., et al. (2023). Health Care Artificial Intelligence: Law, Regulation, and⏎Policy. In National Academy of Medicine, Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: The Hope, the Hype, the Promise,⏎the Peril. National Academies Press. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK605945/⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 8⏎2) Assessment of unintended consequences is essential. Frameworks recommend⏎systematic monitoring to catch errors and unintended consequences before they cause⏎widespread harm.41,42,43 Regular testing is important to identify and minimize issues like⏎AI hallucinations, which often go unnoticed by clinicians and can undermine the integrity⏎of a medical record.44 Continuous monitoring of model performance is also important to⏎measure “model drift”—a phenomenon that occurs when changes in underlying data and⏎relationships between variables degrade the performance of AI models over time.45 Even⏎small amounts of model drift in reimbursement models could alter the care of hundreds of⏎thousands of enrollees (e.g., changes in socioeconomic factors could cause a model to⏎issue more denials).46⏎3) Acknowledging and detecting bias in AI models. It is essential that evaluation⏎protocols are designed to acknowledge and detect bias in clinical AI models. However,⏎experts emphasize that eliminating bias in AI clinical care models can be nearly⏎impossible, because algorithms inherit, amplify, and perpetuate existing healthcare⏎disparities present in training data, such as underrepresentation of minority groups and⏎unequal care quality.47,48 There is often limited availability of clinical data for groups⏎across certain races/ethnicities, geographic locations, and medical conditions, causing⏎skewed data. While developers should aim to train clinical AI models with data that is⏎representative of the intended patient population (including characteristics such as age,⏎gender, sex, race, ethnicity, geographical location, and medical condition),49 developers⏎will likely need to implement weighting and/or sampling techniques that attempt to⏎41 Henry, T. A. (2025, September 4). Your organization has rolled out the health AI tool. What’s next? AMA.⏎https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/digital-health/your-organization-has-rolled-out-health-ai-tool-what-⏎s-next⏎42 Adams, K., & Buffett, A. (2025, October 22). AI and Medicaid: Balancing the Promise of Efficiency with⏎Guardrails to Ensure Responsible Use. Bipartisan Policy Center. https://bipartisanpolicy.org/article/ai-and-⏎medicaid-balancing-the-promise-of-efficiency-with-guardrails-to-ensure-responsible-use/⏎43 Cresswell, K., de Keizer, N., Magrabi, F., Williams, R., Rigby, M., Prgomet, M., Kukhareva, P., Wong, Z. S.,⏎Scott, P., Craven, C. K., Georgiou, A., Medlock, S., Brender McNair, J., & Ammenwerth, E. (2024). Evaluating⏎Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Settings-Let Us Not Reinvent the Wheel. Journal of medical Internet research, 26,⏎e46407. https://doi.org/10.2196/46407⏎44 Deswal, P. (2024, August 7). Hallucinations in AI-generated medical summaries remain a grave concern. Clinical⏎Trials Arena. https://www.clinicaltrialsarena.com/news/hallucinations-in-ai-generated-medical-summaries-remain-a-⏎grave-concern/⏎45 Nelson, S. D. (2025). When AI Goes Astray: Understanding Model Drift. ASHP. https://www.ashp.org/-⏎/media/assets/pharmacy-practice/resource-centers/Digital-Health-and-Artificial-Intelligence/docs/When-AI-Goes-⏎Astray.pdf⏎46 Wong, A., & Sussman, J. B. (2025). Understanding Model Drift and Its Impact on Health Care Policy. JAMA⏎Health Forum. 6(8):e252724. https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama-health-forum/fullarticle/2837524⏎47 Cross, J. L., Choma, M. A., & Onofrey, J. A. (2024). Bias in medical AI: Implications for clinical decision-⏎making. PLOS Digit Health. 3(11):e0000651. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11542778/⏎48 Ranard, B. L., Park, S., Jia, Y., Zhang, Y., Alwan, F., Celi, L. A., & Lusczek, E. R. (2024). Minimizing bias when⏎using artificial intelligence in critical care medicine. Journal of critical care, 82, 154796.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcrc.2024.154796⏎49 Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning-enabled Working Group. (2025, January 27). Good machine learning⏎practice for medical device development: Guiding principles. International Medical Device Regulators Forum.⏎https://www.imdrf.org/sites/default/files/2025-02/IMDRF_AIML%20WG_GMLP_N88%20Final.pdf⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 9⏎balance the data so it does not favor the majority group.50 Researchers can test for⏎statistical parity to determine whether the model provides equal responses across⏎different protected groups,51 and regular monitoring of AI models should include impact⏎assessments that evaluate whether particular groups face higher rates of unintended⏎consequences or harm.⏎What HHS can do⏎ HHS can require extensive transparency regarding the AI used in clinical settings – this⏎transparency is essential to support effective AI evaluations. As identified by NIH,⏎transparency should include how the AI tool was developed (data used to develop the⏎tool, how the algorithm was developed and validated, how the algorithm reaches its⏎results, etc.), as well as how the tool is being used in practice.52⏎ HHS can require that evaluation methods that include metrics of “responsible” use of AI⏎in healthcare, such as: 53⏎1) the extent of human oversight⏎2) complete disclosure as to how systems work, data used, and appeal processes⏎3) the presence of small-scale testing to catch errors before they cause widespread⏎harm⏎4) efforts to protect the most vulnerable, including extra safeguards for children,⏎people with disabilities, and non-English speakers⏎5) demonstrating that AI tools help, not harm⏎ Although evaluations are essential to reduce harm that AI can cause in clinical settings,⏎HHS can acknowledge that there are areas of clinical care where AI is likely to be more⏎harmful than helpful to patients’ health and safety, especially among the most low-⏎income and vulnerable patients.54 HHS can acknowledge that AI will never be⏎completely free of errors and bias, and therefore, there are limitations to using AI for⏎certain clinical tasks, such as determinations of medical necessity and denials of care.⏎ HHS can accelerate responsible adoption of ethical, transparent non-medical device AI⏎tools by helping the field converge on a shared evaluation infrastructure, and by⏎supporting multi-site learning and demonstration projects of its use across diverse⏎healthcare settings and patient populations. The following are a few example HHS⏎mechanisms that would be most impactful in supporting promising HHS evaluation⏎50 Norori, N., Hu, Q., Aellen, F. M., Faraci, F. D., & Tzovara, A. (2021). Addressing bias in big data and AI for⏎health care: A call for open science. Patterns (New York, N.Y.), 2(10), 100347.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.patter.2021.100347⏎51 Chen, F., Wang, L., Hong, J., Jiang, J., & Zhou, L. (2024). Unmasking bias in artificial intelligence: a systematic⏎review of bias detection and mitigation strategies in electronic health record-based models. Journal of the American⏎Medical Informatics Association : JAMIA, 31(5), 1172–1183. https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocae060⏎52 Whicher, D., Ahmed, M., Israni, S.T., et al. (2023). Health Care Artificial Intelligence: Law, Regulation, and⏎Policy. In National Academy of Medicine, Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: The Hope, the Hype, the Promise,⏎the Peril. National Academies Press. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK605945/⏎53 Adams, K., & Buffett, A. (2025, October 22). AI and Medicaid: Balancing the Promise of Efficiency with⏎Guardrails to Ensure Responsible Use. Bipartisan Policy Center. https://bipartisanpolicy.org/article/ai-and-⏎medicaid-balancing-the-promise-of-efficiency-with-guardrails-to-ensure-responsible-use/⏎54 Techtonic Justice. (2024). Inescapable AI: The Ways AI Decides How Low-Income People Work, Live, Learn, and⏎Survive. Techtonic Justice. https://www.techtonicjustice.org/reports/inescapable-ai⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 10⏎methods from pre- and post-deployment metrics, robustness testing, workflow, and⏎human-centered evaluation methods.⏎1) Grants or cooperative agreements to build a shared evaluation infrastructure⏎and common protocols – that includes at the minimum, benchmark datasets and⏎test cases – including those “edge cases” that frequently drive harm or failure for⏎some patient groups that may not be represented in the data (e.g., missing context,⏎contradictory documentation, rare conditions, multi- or co-morbidity, literacy or⏎language variation).⏎2) Contracts to stand up multi-site pilot networks across diverse healthcare⏎settings⏎3) Prize competitions or innovative mechanisms to advance robust evaluation⏎methods, performance drift detection, and fairness under shift (i.e., when⏎population mix changes, documentation patterns change, or language needs differ⏎from when AI was built)⏎4) Encourage Public-Private partnerships / and a Cooperative Research and⏎Development agreement (CRADA) to create and co-develop standardized⏎governance and evaluation templates that can be adopted to co-develop and field-⏎test practical assets (e.g., evaluation toolkits, benchmarking methods, governance⏎templates) with implementers and vendors in real clinical environments.⏎Question #6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve healthcare outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎Where AI tools have fallen short: hallucinations/errors, bias, claims denials, and treatment⏎timeline predictions⏎Hallucinations/errors⏎A well-documented area where AI tools have fallen short is their tendency to “hallucinate,” or⏎invent information and present it as fact.55 This phenomenon has broad implications, as it may⏎occur in any clinical setting that uses AI systems. A meta-analysis of hallucination incidence in⏎Large Language Model (LLM) responses to oncology questions found that approximately one in⏎five responses contained inaccurate information.56 LLMs are particularly prone to hallucination⏎when false details are entered into the system, with one study finding LLM models repeat or⏎elaborate on a planted error in up to 83% of cases of clinical decision support.57 AI⏎hallucinations have also been documented in medical devices, including imaging devices and⏎55 Hatem, R., Simmons, B., & Thornton, J. E. (2023). A Call to Address AI “Hallucinations” and How Healthcare⏎Professionals Can Mitigate Their Risks. Cureus 15(9): e44720. https://doi.org/10.7759/cureus.44720⏎56 Yoon, M. S. (2025). Navigating artificial intelligence (AI) accuracy: A meta-analysis of hallucination incidence in⏎large language model (LLM) responses to oncology questions. Journal of Clinical Oncology.⏎https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.2025.43.16_suppl.e13686⏎57 Omar, M. et al. (2025) Multi-model assurance analysis showing large language models are highly vulnerable to⏎adversarial hallucination attacks during clinical decision support, Communications Medicine.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s43856-025-01021-3⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 11⏎generative-based synthetic medical images.58 This could have devastating consequences for⏎patients: not only could AI hallucinations result in incorrect diagnoses and treatment,59 but⏎incorrect information entered into a medical record can be propagated across multiple systems⏎and providers, and could create long-term consequences that extend beyond the healthcare⏎setting.60 In the context of health coverage, hallucinations could result in the inclusion of false⏎information about a patient’s diagnosis in an AI-generated prior authorization request, resulting⏎in improper claims adjudication.61⏎Bias⏎As mentioned earlier, AI algorithms inherit, amplify, and perpetuate existing healthcare⏎disparities present in training data, such as underrepresentation of minority groups and unequal⏎care quality.62,63 These biases have real-world implications; for example, AI algorithms have⏎required Black patients to be considerably sicker to qualify for support than their White⏎counterparts, and other AI algorithms have proposed inferior medical treatments for patients of⏎minority descent.64,65,66⏎Clinicians are deeply concerned about AI bias, and call for strong oversight frameworks and data⏎transparency to allow for the evaluation of AI models.67,68,69 While some providers certainly see⏎the value that AI tools may have in clinical spaces, they do not want the integration of these⏎58 Granstedt, J., Kc, P., Deshpande, R., Garcia, V., & Badano, J. (2025). Hallucinations in medieval devices.⏎Artificial Intelligence in the Life Sciences. 8, 100145. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ailsci.2025.100145⏎59 Maleki, N., Padmanabhan B., and Dutta, K. (2024). AI Hallucinations: A Misnomer Worth Clarifying, 2024 IEEE⏎Conference on Artificial Intelligence (CAI), Singapore.⏎https://ieeexplore.ieee.org/abstract/document/10605268⏎60 Siwicki, B. (2025). The damage AI Hallucinations can do - and how to avoid them, Healthcare IT News.⏎https://www.healthcareitnews.com/news/damage-ai-hallucinations-can-do-and-how-avoid-them⏎61 Shift Technology, (2025). Shift Insurance Perspectives: The AI Hallucinations Edition.⏎https://www.shift-technology.com/resources/reports-and-insights/shift-insurance-perspectives-the-ai-hallucinations-⏎edition⏎62 Cross, J.L., Choma, M. A., Onofrey, J. A. (2024). Bias in medical AI: Implications for clinical decision-making.⏎PLOS Digit Health. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11542778/⏎63 Ranard, B. L., et al. (2024). Minimizing bias when using artificial intelligence in critical care medicine. J Crit⏎Care. National Library of Medicine. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jcrc.2024.154796⏎64 Obermeyer, Z. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations, Science.⏎https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎65 Bouguettaya, A., Stuart, E. M., & Aboujaoude, E. (2025). Racial bias in AI-mediated psychiatric diagnosis and⏎treatment: A qualitative comparison of four large language models. NPJ Digital Medicine, 8(1), Article 332.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01746-4⏎66 Cedars Sinai, (2025). Cedars-Sinai Study Shows Racial Bias in AI-Generated Treatment Regimens for Psychiatric⏎Patients. https://www.cedars-sinai.org/newsroom/cedars-sinai-study-shows-racial-bias-in-ai-generated-treatment-⏎regimens-for-psychiatric-patients/⏎67 Jain, A., et al. (2023). Awareness of Racial and Ethnic Bias and Potential Solutions to Address Bias With Use of⏎Health Care Algorithms, JAMA Health Forum, JAMA Network. https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama-health-⏎forum/fullarticle/2805595⏎68 Bervell, J. (2023, April 14). Can AI Improve Health Without Perpetuating Bias? The Dose, The Commonwealth⏎Fund. https://www.commonwealthfund.org/publications/podcast/2023/apr/can-ai-improve-health-without-⏎perpetuating-bias⏎69 Levi, R., (2023). Rooting Out Racial Bias in Health Care AI, Part 2, Health Technology, Trade Offs.⏎https://tradeoffs.org/2023/06/01/artificial-intelligence-ai-racial-bias-2/⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 12⏎technologies to come at the expense of their commitment to “do no harm,” which includes⏎protecting those most vulnerable.70⏎Claims denials⏎Another well-documented area where AI tools have fallen short is when they are used to process⏎insurance claims. Indeed, investigations of AI systems used by insurance companies reveal that⏎these systems are built to deny claims first: when a claim enters the system, the predisposition⏎(or default) of the algorithm is to deny the claim, rather than accept it or remain neutral in its⏎decision-making.71 While insurers maintain that humans with medical expertise still review and⏎approve the denials, research has revealed that these reviewers frequently simply approve the⏎denial that has been prepackaged by the AI system – without further investigation or utilizing⏎medical judgment. “‘We literally click and submit,’ one former Cigna doctor said. ‘It takes all of⏎10 seconds to do 50 at a time.’”72 In a two-month period, Cigna denied more than 300,000⏎claims, which amounts to about 1.2 seconds for each physician-reviewed claim.73 A 2024 survey⏎by the National Association of Insurance Commissioners of large health insurers found 37% of⏎insurers report using AI for prior authorization, 44% for claims adjudication, and 56% for⏎broadly defined utilization management activities.74⏎Treatment timeline predictions⏎Relatedly, AI tools have fallen short in their ability to accurately assess medical need and⏎treatment timelines – which insurance companies use to deny claims and cut off funding for⏎treatment.75 A STAT investigation found that Medicare Advantage plans’ use of algorithms to⏎make coverage determinations has driven high rates of denials, which have the potential to harm⏎more than 31 million enrollees.76 Between 2020 and 2022 (when Medicare Advantage plans⏎started using these algorithms), the number of appeals filed to contest MA denials shot up to⏎58%, which only accounts for denials that were officially appealed.77 AI models often fail to⏎consider individual circumstances or changes in condition. As a result, patients have been denied⏎70 Adams, K., Buffet, A., (2025). AI and Medicaid: Balancing the Promise of Efficiency with Guardrails to Ensure⏎Responsible Use, Bipartisan Policy Center.⏎https://bipartisanpolicy.org/article/ai-and-medicaid-balancing-the-promise-of-efficiency-with-guardrails-to-ensure-⏎responsible-use/⏎71 Rucker, P., The Capitol Forum, Miller, M., David Armstrong, (2025, March 23) How Cigna Saves Millions by⏎Having Its Doctors Reject Claims Without Reading Them, Health Care, ProPublica.⏎https://www.propublica.org/article/cigna-pxdx-medical-health-insurance-rejection-claims⏎72 Id.⏎73 Schreiber, M., (2025, Jan. 25). New AI tool counters health insurance denials decided by automated algorithms,⏎The Guardian. https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2025/jan/25/health-insurers-ai⏎74 DeFrain, C., et al. (2025). Health Insurance Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning Survey Results, NAIC Staff⏎Report, National Association of Insurance Commissioners.⏎https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-files/NAIC%20AI%20Health%20Survey%20Report%20.pdf⏎75 Mello, M., et al., (2026). The AI Arms Race In Health Insurance Utilization Review: Promises Of Efficiency And⏎Risks Of Supercharged Flaws, Vol. 45, No. 1: Artificial Intelligence, Health Spending & More, Health Affairs.⏎https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00897⏎76 Ross, C., Herman, H., (2023). Denied by AI: How Medicare Advantage plans use algorithms to cut off care for⏎seniors in need, STAT. https://www.statnews.com/2023/03/13/medicare-advantage-plans-denial-artificial-⏎intelligence/⏎77 Id.⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 13⏎rehabilitation stays, discharged with severe pain, and have developed complications due to lack⏎of care continuity.78⏎Where AI tools have been of assistance: detecting certain diseases early and accurately, and⏎assisting with medical documentation⏎Detecting certain diseases early and accurately⏎There are several areas where AI tools have proven value in clinical settings. Research⏎demonstrates that AI has the ability to detect rare diseases earlier and with better accuracy,⏎including better interpretations of imaging results. One study of AI functions in diagnostic⏎imaging showed algorithms can analyze medical images, such as X-rays, CT scans, and MRIs,⏎with greater accuracy and speed than human radiologists.79 With access to medical history⏎through integration in EHRs, AI tools are able to identify patterns and often detect diseases like⏎cancer at earlier stages.80,81 This helps reduce misdiagnoses and ensures patients receive correct⏎treatment promptly.⏎Assisting with medical documentation⏎AI also has the potential to streamline certain documentation processes, allowing clinical staff⏎more time to focus on patient care. A 2021 study conducted by the American Academy of⏎Family Physicians found that the adoption of an AI assistant for documentation reduced the⏎median documentation time per note by 72% for family physicians and PCPs.82 Reducing the⏎time physicians spend interacting with electronic health records not only frees up time for⏎providers to deliver direct care to patients, but it is also associated with reduced clinician⏎burnout.83 States have also adopted automation and machine learning tools for this purpose: in⏎Ohio, for example, the “Baby Bot” automatically adds newborns to their mother’s Medicaid⏎case, saving weeks’ worth of county staff time.84⏎What HHS can do⏎ HHS can put clear guardrails around how AI is used in clinical care to address the real⏎harm that arises when AI is used to issue claims denials and determine treatment⏎timelines. For example, HHS should prioritize transparency by requiring insurers to⏎78 Id.⏎79 Khalifa, M., Albadawy, M., (2024). AI in diagnostic imaging: Revolutionising accuracy and efficiency, Volume⏎5, Science Direct. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cmpbup.2024.100146⏎80 Id.⏎81 Papreddy, P., et. al. (2025). Transforming sepsis management: AI-driven innovations in early detection and⏎tailored therapies, National Library of Medicine. https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12366378/⏎82 American Academy of Family Physicians. (2021, November). Using an AI assistant to reduce documentation⏎burden in family medicine: Evaluating the Suki Assistant (Innovation Labs report).⏎https://www.tafp.org/media/healthy-practice/report-suki-assistant-documentation-burden.pdf⏎83 Pearlman, K., Wan, W., Shah, S., & Laiteerapong, N. (2025). Use of an AI scribe and electronic health record⏎efficiency. JAMA Network Open, 8(10), e2537000. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.37000⏎84 Adams, K., & Buffett, A. (2025, October 22). AI and Medicaid: Balancing the promise of efficiency with⏎guardrails to ensure responsible use. Bipartisan Policy Center. https://bipartisanpolicy.org/article/ai-and-medicaid-⏎balancing-the-promise-of-efficiency-with-guardrails-to-ensure-responsible-use/⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 14⏎notify patients when insurers have used AI to issue their claims decisions. HHS also⏎could ban insurers from using AI when determining medical necessity. Alternatively,⏎HHS could direct insurers to include sufficient and thorough human oversight by⏎requiring that ultimate claims decisions be made by a qualified human professional. HHS⏎can also ensure that patients have access to a meaningful and easy way to navigate⏎systems to challenge claim denials that involve a qualified human reviewer who was not⏎involved in the initial decision.⏎Question #9: What challenges within healthcare do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients⏎and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Concerns patients and caregivers have related to the adoption of AI in clinical care⏎While certain groups of patients are open to the integration of AI in clinical spaces, broadly⏎speaking, patients are still skeptical about AI tools. A 2023 Pew Research Center survey of U.S.⏎adults revealed Americans have little trust in companies to use AI responsibly:85⏎ Most Americans (70%) say they have little or no trust in companies to make responsible⏎decisions about how they use AI in their products;⏎ 81% say information collected will be used in ways people are not comfortable with;⏎ 80% say it will be used in ways that were not originally intended.⏎Caregivers are especially concerned about the use of AI algorithms to determine “medical⏎necessity” and make claim denials. The use of AI models has made it especially difficult for⏎caregivers and their care recipients to challenge these care denials because they are opaque - the⏎AI technology used is often proprietary, and limited transparency means they cannot understand⏎why they were denied care.86 Caregivers of patients who require Home and Community-Based⏎Services (HCBS) are particularly concerned that AI algorithms are not designed to identify⏎people’s real needs, but rather are used as tools to limit access to HCBS by applying restrictive⏎eligibility criteria.87 If patients and their caregivers cannot access the HCBS they need, this can⏎hinder patients’ quality of life while placing additional burden on already overstrained⏎caregivers.88⏎There are also concerns about the erosion of the patient-provider relationship and loss of face-⏎time with clinicians, as providers have a growing reliance on AI tools. The Association of⏎American Medical Colleges warns that AI may serve as a substitute for human connection,⏎which can undermine patient-provider trust and may lead to subsequent issues with patient⏎treatment compliance.89 Moreover, AI cannot determine which aspects of a patient’s life are⏎85 Faverio, M. (2023, October 18). Key findings about Americans and data privacy. Pew Research Center.⏎https://www.pewresearch.org/short-reads/2023/10/18/key-findings-about-americans-and-data-privacy/⏎86 Osman, M., Paul, E., & Weil, E. (2025, October 21). Calculated need: Algorithms and the fight for Medicaid⏎home care. Upturn. https://www.upturn.org/work/calculated-need/⏎87 Id.⏎88 Id.⏎89 Boyle, P. (2025, November 2). Doctors, beware: AI threatens to weaken your relationships with patients.⏎Association of American Medical Colleges. https://www.aamc.org/news/doctors-beware-ai-threatens-weaken-your-⏎relationships-patients⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 15⏎clinically relevant: for example, a physician treating a patient with diabetes may know it is⏎important to ask whether the patient is still living with a family member who brings them to the⏎grocery store, but an AI tool may overlook the clinical significance of this exchange.90⏎Challenges within healthcare that patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI in⏎clinical care⏎More generally, US adults are interested in and most comfortable with AI innovations that⏎provide recommendations of providers or care settings, ease their caregiving burdens, assist with⏎diagnoses (leveraged and reviewed by their doctor), and assist with clinical administrative⏎tasks.91 Among adult age groups, Generation X and Millennials report the most interest and use⏎of AI in clinical care.92⏎What HHS can do⏎As HHS develops AI policies regarding patient rights, transparency, and guardrails, HHS can⏎clearly and thoroughly address some of the most common patient and caregiver concerns in its⏎policies. The most common patient and caregiver concerns that can be addressed include:⏎ Privacy: “Who sees my data? Is it training a model?”⏎ Transparency: “Is AI being used on me? Can I opt out?”⏎ Errors/hallucinations: “What is AI is wrong? Who catches it?”⏎ Appeals/recourse: “How do I challenge AI-driven decisions?”⏎ Bias and unequal quality: “Will it work as well for me as others?”⏎ Loss of human care: “Will this reduce time with my clinician?”⏎Question #10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care? Are there published findings about the impact of⏎adopted AI tools and their use clinical care? How does the literature approach the costs,⏎benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎Below, we summarize three well-documented impacts of the adoption of AI tools in clinical care.⏎Adopting AI tools in clinical care has been shown to 1) improve efficacy in the diagnosis of⏎select health conditions, 2) reduce provider burnout when leveraged for medical documentation,⏎and 3) lead to algorithmic discrimination.⏎1) Adopting AI tools in clinical care may improve efficiency in the diagnosis of select health⏎conditions⏎A growing body of literature suggests AI may improve efficiency in diagnosing certain health⏎conditions, especially those that require radiology diagnoses. A 2023 review confirmed that AI⏎enhances the radiology process by both automating triage (AI can find and highlight critical⏎90 Id.⏎91 PricewaterhouseCoopers. (2025, October 20). PwC’s 2025 US Healthcare Consumer Insights Survey: The⏎consumer‑first era of healthcare. PwC. https://www.pwc.com/us/en/industries/health-industries/library/healthcare-⏎consumer-insights-survey.html⏎92 Id.⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 16⏎cases for immediate review) and generating and interpreting high-quality radiology reports.93 As⏎a result, as of July 2025, the FDA has authorized over 1,250 AI-enabled medical devices for⏎marketing in the United States, with 77% of authorized devices designed for use in radiology.94⏎AI tools have been successful in diagnosing numerous other specific health conditions, such as⏎diabetic retinopathy, atrial fibrillation, and coronavirus pneumonia.95,96,97 Indeed, the FDA⏎approved an autonomous AI-based diagnostic tool for diabetic retinopathy – without the need for⏎specialist interpretation and oversight – due to the model’s superior detection of disease⏎indicators in medical images.98⏎2) Adopting AI tools for medical documentation can reduce provider burnout⏎The use of AI in clinical settings has the potential to decrease providers’ administrative burdens⏎and thereby reduce burnout. Numerous studies have indicated that AI models can successfully⏎act as “scribes” and reduce the time providers engage in medical documentation. A 2021 study⏎conducted by the American Academy of Family Physicians found that the adoption of an AI⏎assistant for documentation reduced the median documentation time per note by 72% for family⏎physicians and PCPs.99 One study found that clinicians across six health systems using ambient⏎AI scribes to document patient-clinician conversations reported a decrease in burnout rates from⏎51.9% to 38.8% after one month;100 They also reported reduced cognitive load, less time spent⏎on after-hours documentation, and improved focus on patient interactions.101 Of physicians⏎polled in the AMA’s 2024 AI Physician Sentiment Survey, 57% viewed addressing⏎93 Najjar, R. (2023). Redefining radiology: A review of artificial intelligence integration in medical imaging.⏎Diagnostics, 13(17), 2760. https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics13172760⏎94 Sandalow, M., Adams, K., & Loud, G. (2025, November 10). FDA oversight: Understanding the regulation of⏎health AI tools (Issue Brief). Bipartisan Policy Center. https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/fda-oversight-⏎understanding-the-regulation-of-health-ai-tools/⏎95 Abràmoff, M. D., Lavin, P. T., Birch, M., Shah, N., & Folk, J. C. (2018). Pivotal trial of an autonomous AI‑based⏎diagnostic system for detection of diabetic retinopathy in primary care offices. NPJ Digital Medicine, 1, 39.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746‑018‑0040‑6⏎96 Lin, F., Zhang, P., Chen, Y., Liu, Y., Li, D., Tan, L., Wang, Y., Wang, D. W., Yang, X., Ma, F., & Li, Q. (2024).⏎Artificial‑intelligence‑based risk prediction and mechanism discovery for atrial fibrillation using heart beat‑to‑beat⏎intervals. Med, 5(5), 414–431.e5. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.medj.2024.02.006⏎97 Zhang, K., Liu, X., Shen, J., Li, Z., Sang, Y., Wu, X., Zha, Y., Liang, W., Wang, C., Wang, K., Ye, L., Gao, M.,⏎Zhou, Z., Li, L., Wang, J., Yang, Z., Cai, H., Xu, J., Yang, L., … Wang, G. (2020). Clinically applicable AI system⏎for accurate diagnosis, quantitative measurements, and prognosis of COVID‑19 pneumonia using computed⏎tomography. Cell, 181(6), 1423–1433.e11. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cell.2020.04.045⏎98 Abràmoff, M. D., Lavin, P. T., Birch, M., Shah, N., & Folk, J. C. (2018). Pivotal trial of an autonomous AI‑based⏎diagnostic system for detection of diabetic retinopathy in primary care offices. NPJ Digital Medicine, 1(1), 39.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746‑018‑0040‑6⏎99 American Academy of Family Physicians. (2021, November). Using an AI assistant to reduce documentation⏎burden in family medicine: Evaluating the Suki Assistant (Innovation Labs report).⏎https://www.tafp.org/media/healthy-practice/report-suki-assistant-documentation-burden.pdf⏎100 Olson, K. D., Meeker, D., Troup, M., Barker, T. D., Nguyen, V. H., Manders, J. B., Stults, C. D., Jones, V. G.,⏎Shah, S. D., Shah, T., & Schwamm, L. H. (2025). Use of ambient AI scribes to reduce administrative burden and⏎professional burnout. JAMA Network Open, 8(10), e2534976.⏎https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.34976⏎101 Id.⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 17⏎administrative burden through automation as the biggest area of opportunity for AI, and most⏎physicians believed AI tools would be helpful in alleviating stress and burnout.102⏎3) Algorithmic discrimination is “baked into” AI models, which harms the most low-income⏎and vulnerable populations.⏎While there are documented benefits to some uses of AI in clinical settings, these tools are not⏎without their limitations. One serious, well-documented harm is “algorithmic discrimination,”⏎which occurs when AI systems make prejudicial decisions based on attributes like race, gender,⏎age, sexual orientation, or other demographic characteristics. As mentioned earlier, there exists⏎significant evidence of racial bias in clinical AI, as these tools pick up and perpetuate bias from⏎the data they are trained on and the human interactions they learn from.103 Because racial and⏎ethnic minority groups are underrepresented in medical research, this can lead to worsened⏎diagnostic accuracy and treatment recommendations for these groups.104,105 This bias is nearly⏎impossible to address in AI models.⏎Algorithmic discrimination has real consequences for patient care: a 2019 landmark study found⏎a widely used algorithm designed to identify patients for high-risk care management programs⏎required Black patients to be considerably sicker to qualify for support than their White⏎counterparts.106 The algorithm was designed to predict healthcare costs rather than illness, but⏎failed to account for unequal access to care and resulting spending differences between racial⏎groups.107 If the disparity was remedied, the percentage of Black patients receiving additional⏎support would have increased from 17.7% to 46.5%.108 In another example, a 2025 study of⏎LLMs used in psychiatry found the LLMs assumed racial identity from patient names or other⏎data and proposed inferior treatments for patients of minority descent.109,110⏎102 American Medical Association. (2025, February). Physician sentiments around the use of AI in health care:⏎Motivations, opportunities, risks, and use cases (Shifts from 2023 to 2024) (AMA Augmented Intelligence Research⏎Report). https://www.ama-assn.org/system/files/physician-ai-sentiment-report.pdf⏎103 Hurd, T. C., Cobb Payton, F., & Hood, D. B. (2024). Targeting machine learning and artificial intelligence⏎algorithms in health care to reduce bias and improve population health. The Milbank Quarterly, 102(3), 577–604.⏎https://doi.org/10.1111/1468‑0009.12712⏎104 Rutgers University–Newark. (2024, December 1). AI algorithms used in healthcare can perpetuate bias.⏎https://www.newark.rutgers.edu/news/ai-algorithms-used-healthcare-can-perpetuate-bias⏎105 Hanna, M. G., Pantanowitz, L., Jackson, B., Palmer, O., Visweswaran, S., Pantanowitz, J., Deebajah, M., &⏎Rashidi, H. H. (2025). Ethical and bias considerations in artificial intelligence/machine learning. Modern Pathology,⏎38(3), 100686. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.modpat.2024.100686⏎106 Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to⏎manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447–453. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎107 Id.⏎108 Id.⏎109 Bouguettaya, A., Stuart, E. M., & Aboujaoude, E. (2025). Racial bias in AI-mediated psychiatric diagnosis and⏎treatment: A qualitative comparison of four large language models. NPJ Digital Medicine, 8(1), Article 332.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01746-4⏎110 Cedars-Sinai. (2025, June 30). Cedars-Sinai study shows racial bias in AI-generated treatment regimens for⏎psychiatric patients. https://www.cedars-sinai.org/newsroom/cedars-sinai-study-shows-racial-bias-in-ai-generated-⏎treatment-regimens-for-psychiatric-patients/⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 18⏎What HHS can do⏎ HHS must acknowledge that algorithmic discrimination causes real harm and is nearly⏎impossible to avoid or prevent, and therefore must put clear guardrails around how AI is⏎used in clinical care (i.e., AI cannot be used for care determinations and care denials⏎without the involvement of a qualified human professional to review these decisions).⏎ HHS should encourage and support research that investigates the extent to which AI in⏎clinical care results in actual or potential harm to patients and that develops evidence-⏎based approaches to reduce such harm to the maximum extent possible.⏎Conclusion⏎For all the foregoing reasons, the individual public health deans and scholars listed below urge⏎HHS to provide oversight and regulation regarding the use of AI in clinical care. AI has⏎extensive potential for making clinical care more efficient and accessible. However, without⏎regulation and oversight, it also has the strong potential to cause irreparable harm in the context⏎of healthcare.⏎We also respectfully request that the full text of our comments, as well as the full text of each of⏎the individual studies, reports, and other supporting materials that we have cited and made⏎available through active links in our comments, be considered part of the formal administrative⏎record on this request for information from the HHS for purposes of the Administrative⏎Procedure Act. Please let us know if HHS is unable for any reason to include our linked⏎materials, so we will have the chance to otherwise submit copies of the supporting documents⏎into the administrative record.⏎Thank you for your consideration of our comments. If you need any additional information,⏎please contact Allyson Crays at a.crays@gwu.edu.⏎A. Public Health Deans⏎1. Abroms, Lorien, ScD, Professor, Associate Dean for PhD/MS Programs, Milken Institute⏎School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎2. Catalanotti, Jillian, MD, MPH, FACP, Associate Dean for Clinical Public Health &⏎Population Health Practice, Associate Professor of Medicine, Associate Professor of⏎Health Policy and Management, Director, Internal Medicine Residency Programs, The⏎George Washington University⏎3. Chandler, G. Thomas, MS, PhD, Distinguished Dean Emeritus, Professor, Environmental⏎Health Sciences, Arnold School of Public Health, University of South Carolina⏎4. El-Mohandes, Ayman, MBBCh, MD, MPH, Dean, CUNY Graduate School of Public⏎Health & Health Policy⏎5. Goldman, Lynn R., MD, MPH, MS, Dean Emerita, Milken Institute School of Public⏎Health, The George Washington University⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 19⏎6. Gusmano, Michael K., PhD, lacocca Chair, Professor of Health Policy, Associate Dean of⏎Academic Affairs, College of Health, Lehigh University⏎7. Hyder, Adnan A., MD, MPH, PhD, Dean & Robert A. Knox Professor, Boston⏎University School of Public Health⏎8. Jeffries, Pamela R., PhD, RN, FAAN, ANEF, FSSH, Dean, Vanderbilt School of⏎Nursing, Valere Potter Distinguished Professor of Nursing, RWJF Nurse Executive⏎Fellow Alumna, Vanderbilt School of Nursing⏎9. LaVeist, Thomas A., PhD, Dean and Weatherhead Presidential Chair in Health Equity,⏎Professor Tulane University School of Public Health and Tropical Medicine⏎10. Lu, Michael C., MD, MS, MPH, Dean, UC Berkeley School of Public Health⏎11. Lushniak, Boris, MD, MPH, Professor and Dean, University of Maryland School of⏎Public Health⏎12. Rimer, Barbara K., DrPH, Professor Emerita, Department of Health Behavior, Dean⏎Emerita, UNC Gillings School of Global Public Health⏎13. Thorpe, Jane, JD, Professor and Sr. Associate Dean for Academic, Student & Faculty⏎Affairs, Department of Health Policy and Management, Milken Institute School of Public⏎Health, The George Washington University⏎14. Vermund, Sten H., MD, PhD, Dean, Distinguished University Health Professor of Public⏎Health and Pediatrics, College of Public Health, University of South Florida⏎15. Wilensky, Sara E., JD, PhD, Assistant Dean for Undergraduate Education, Director,⏎Undergraduate Program in Public Health, Special Services Faculty for Undergraduate⏎Education, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎B. Health Professions, Public Health, Health Law and Policy Scholars⏎16. Akman, Jeffrey S., MD, Professor and Leon M. Yochelson Chair, Department of⏎Psychiatry and Behavioral Sciences, School of Medicine and Health Sciences, The⏎George Washington University⏎17. Baird, Sarah, PhD, Professor and Vice Chair, Department of Global Health, Milken⏎Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎18. Barkoff, Alison, JD, Harold and Jane Hirsh Associate Professor of Health Law and⏎Policy, Director, Hirsh Health Law and Policy Program, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 20⏎19. Beckerman, Julia Zoe, JD, MPH, Teaching Professor and Vice Chair of Academics,⏎Department of Health Policy and Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health,⏎The George Washington University⏎20. Blanchard, Janice, MD, PhD, Professor, Chief of Health Policy Section, Department of⏎Emergency Medicine, School of Medicine and Health Sciences, The George Washington⏎University⏎21. Blatt, Benjamin, MD, CHSE, Professor of Medicine, School of Medicine and Health⏎Sciences, The George Washington University⏎22. Blewett, Lynn A., PhD, MA, Professor, Division of Health Policy and Management,⏎University of Minnesota School of Public Health⏎23. Bonar, Robert, DHA, Gordon A. Friesen Professor of Healthcare Administration,⏎Director of Master of Health Administration Program, Milken Institute School of Public⏎Health, The George Washington University⏎24. Borkowski, Liz, MPH, Senior Research Scientist, Managing Director, Jacobs Institute of⏎Women’s Health, Department of Health Policy and Management, Milken Institute School⏎of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎25. Braaten, Kari P., MD, MPH, Assistant Professor of Obstetrics, Gynecology and⏎Reproductive Biology, Harvard Medical School⏎26. Braffett, Barbara H., PhD, Associate Research Professor, Department of Epidemiology,⏎Co-PI, DCCT/EDIC Data Coordinating Center, Co-I, TODAY Coordinating Center, The⏎George Washington University⏎27. Brindis, Claire D., DrPH, Distinguished Professor, Departments of Pediatrics and⏎Obstetrics, Gynecology and Reproductive Sciences, University of California, San⏎Francisco, Emerita Director, Philip R. Lee Institute for Health Policy Studies⏎28. Burke, Taylor, JD, LLM, Adjunct Professor of Health Law and Policy, Department of⏎Health Policy and Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George⏎Washington University⏎29. Burroughs, Thomas E., PhD, MS, MA, Professor of Health Management & Policy,⏎Biostatistics, and Epidemiology, College for Public Health and Social Justice, Saint Louis⏎University⏎30. Byrnes, Maureen, MPA, Teaching Instructor, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎31. Cartwright-Smith, Lara, JD, MPH, Associate Professor, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 21⏎32. Casoni, Maria, MPH, MSL, Research Scientist, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎33. Chang, Aileen, MD, MSPH, Associate Professor of Medicine, The George Washington⏎University School of Medicine and Health Sciences⏎34. Chernoby, Kimberly, MD, JD, Clinical Instructor of Emergency Medicine, The George⏎Washington University School of Medicine and Health Sciences⏎35. Cleary, Sean, MPH, PhD, Associate Professor, Department of Epidemiology, Milken⏎Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎36. Cohen, Alan B., Sc.D., Research Professor (Retired), Markets, Public Policy and Law,⏎Boston University Questrom School of Business, and Professor of Health Law, Policy⏎and Management (Retired), Boston University School of Public Health⏎37. Crays, Allyson, JD, Public Health Law and Policy Analyst, Department of Health Policy⏎and Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎38. Dankwa-Mullan, Irene, MD MPH, Adjunct Professor, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎39. Diemert, David, MD, FRCP(C), Professor, Depts. of Medicine and Microbiology,⏎Immunology & Tropical Medicine, The George Washington University School of⏎Medicine & Health Sciences⏎40. Dutta, Elizabeth Arend, DrPH, MPH, Professorial Lecturer, The George Washington⏎University⏎41. Ecker, Jeffrey L., MD, Joe V. Meigs Professor of Obstetrics, Gynecology and⏎Reproductive Biology, Harvard Medical School, Chair Emeritus, Department of⏎Obstetrics and Gynecology, Massachusetts General Hospital⏎42. Erikson, Clese, MPAff, Deputy Director, Health Workforce Research Center, Fitzhugh⏎Mullan Institute for Health Workforce Equity, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎43. Evans, Scott, PhD, MS, Director, The Biostatistics Center, Professor and Founding Chair,⏎Department of Biostatistics and Bioinformatics, Milken Institute School of Public Health,⏎The George Washington University⏎44. Feder, Judith, PhD, Professor, McCourt School of Public Policy, Georgetown University⏎45. Frankford, David M., JD, Professor of Law, Rutgers Law School⏎⏎