ne Chair &⏎Director, Sumner M. Redstone Global Center for Prevention and Wellness, Professor,⏎Department of Global Health & Department of Epidemiology, Milken Institute School of⏎Public Health, The George Washington University⏎57. Horton, Katherine, RN, MPH, JD, Research Professor, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎58. Huberfeld, Nicole, Edward R. Utley Professor of Health Law, Co-Director, BU Program⏎on Reproductive Justice, Chair, BU Health Law Program, Boston University School of⏎Law and School of Public Health⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 23⏎59. Imershein, Sara, MD, MPH, FACOG, Clinical Professor, OB-GYN, School of Medicine⏎and Health Sciences, The George Washington University⏎60. Jacobs, Feygele, DrPH, MS, MPH, Professor and Director, Geiger Gibson Program in⏎Community Health, Department of Health Policy and Management, Milken Institute⏎School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎61. Jordan, Jeanne A., PhD, HCLD(ABB), MT(ASCP), Professor Emerita, Department of⏎Epidemiology, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎62. Jost, Timothy Stoltzfus, JD, Professor Emeritus, Washington and Lee University School⏎of Law⏎63. Katz, Ingrid, MD, MHS., Director, Yale Institute for Global Health⏎64. Ku, Leighton, PhD, MPH, Professor, Department of Health Policy and Management,⏎Director, Center for Health Policy Research, Milken Institute School of Public Health,⏎The George Washington University⏎65. Lantz, Paula, PhD, Associate Director, International Policy Center, James B. Hudak⏎Professor of Health Policy, Director of BA Undergraduate Programs, Gerald R. Ford⏎School of Public Policy, Professor of Health Management and Policy, University⏎Professor of Diversity and Social Transformation, School of Public Health, University of⏎Michigan⏎66. LaPuma, Peter, PhD, CIH, PE, Professor, Department of Environmental and⏎Occupational Health, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎67. Law, Sylvia A., JD, Elizabeth K. Dollard Professor Emerita of Law, Medicine and⏎Psychiatry, NYU Law School⏎68. Levi, Jeffrey, PhD, Professor Emeritus, Department of Health Policy and Management,⏎Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎69. Lewis-O’Connor, Annie, PhD, NP, MPH, Instructor in Medicine, Harvard Medical⏎School, Founder and Director, C.A.R.E. Clinic, Brigham and Women’s Hospital⏎70. Lillie-Blanton, Marsha, DrPH, Adjunct Professor, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎71. Magnus, Manya, PhD, MPH, Professor and Chair, Department of Epidemiology, Milken⏎Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎72. Mariner, Wendy K., JD, LLM, MPH, Professor of Health Law, Ethics and Human Rights⏎Emerita, Boston University School of Public Health⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 24⏎73. Markus, Anne R., PhD, MHS, JD, Professor and Chair, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎74. Mason, Diana J., RN, PhD, FAAN, Senior Policy Service Professor, Center for Health⏎Policy and Media Engagement, School of Nursing, The George Washington University⏎75. McCarthy, Melissa L., ScD, MS, Professor of Health Policy and Emergency Medicine,⏎Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎76. McDonnell, Karen A., PhD, Associate Professor, Department of Prevention and⏎Community Health, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎77. Michaels, David, PhD, MPH, Professor, Department of Environmental and Occupational⏎Health, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎78. Michener, Jamila, PhD, Associate Professor of Government, Cornell University⏎79. Monroe, Anne K., MD, MSPH, Associate Professor, Department of Epidemiology,⏎Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎80. Murphy, Caitlin, MPP, Research Scientist, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎81. Musumeci, MaryBeth, JD, Associate Teaching Professor, Milken Institute School of⏎Public Health, The George Washington University⏎82. Newcomer, Kathryn, PhD, MA, Professor of Public Policy and Public Administration,⏎Trachtenberg School of Public Policy and Public Administration, The George⏎Washington University⏎83. Oberlander, Jonathan, PhD, Professor, Department of Social Medicine, Professor,⏎Department of Health Policy & Management, University of North Carolina at Chapel Hill⏎84. Parmet, Wendy E., JD, Matthews University Distinguished Professor of Law,⏎Northeastern University⏎85. Perreira, Krista M., PhD, Professor, Department of Social Medicine, UNC School of⏎Medicine⏎86. Perrin, James M., MD, Professor of Pediatrics Emeritus, Harvard Medical School, John⏎C. Robinson Distinguished Chair in Pediatrics, MassGeneral for Children⏎87. Peterson, Mark A., PhD, Professor of Public Policy, Political Science, Health Policy and⏎Management, and Law, UCLA Meyer and Renee Luskin School of Public Affairs⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 25⏎88. Pittman, Patricia, PhD, Professor of Health Policy and Management, Director of Health⏎Workforce Research Center, Department of Health Policy and Management, Milken⏎Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎89. Pollack, Harold, PhD, Helen Ross Distinguished Services Professor, Associated Faculty,⏎Public Health Sciences, Crown Family School of Social Work, Policy, and Practice,⏎Administration, University of Chicago⏎90. Poteat, Tonia, PhD, MPH, PA-C, Professor, Duke University School of Nursing,⏎Research Professor of Global Health, Duke Global Health Institute⏎91. Price, Olga Acosta, Associate Professor and Vice Chair, Department of Prevention and⏎Community Health, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎92. Reeves, Matthew, MD, MPH, FACOG, Chief Executive Officer, DuPont Clinic; Clinical⏎Associate Professor of Obstetrics and Gynecology, George Washington University⏎School of Medicine and Health Sciences; Adjunct Clinical Associate Professor of⏎Obstetrics and Gynecology, Stanford University School of Medicine; Adjunct Associate⏎Professor of Population, Family, and Reproductive Health, Johns Hopkins Bloomberg⏎School of Public Health⏎93. Regenstein, Marsha, PhD, Professor, Department of Health Policy and Management,⏎Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎94. Rempe, Shannon, JD, MPH, Program Director, Advancing Public Health and Health⏎Equity in the Courts, Northeastern University⏎95. Ricciardi, Richard, PhD, CRNP, FAAP, FAAN, Executive Director, Center for Health⏎Policy and Media Engagement, Professor, School of Nursing, The George Washington⏎University⏎96. Rich-Edwards, Janet, ScD, MPH, Associate Professor of Medicine, Harvard Medical⏎School, Associate Professor in Epidemiology, Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public⏎Health⏎97. Richards, Adam K., MD, PhD, MPH, DTM&H, Senior Associate, Department of⏎International Health, Johns Hopkins Bloomberg School of Public Health⏎98. Riegelman, Richard, MD, MPH, PhD, Professor of Epidemiology and Founding Dean,⏎Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎99. Rittenberg, Eve, MD, Assistant Professor of Medicine, Harvard Medical School⏎100. Rocco, Philip, PhD, Associate Professor of Political Science, Marquette University⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 26⏎101. Rosenbaum, Sara, JD, Professor Emerita, Health Law and Policy, Department of Health⏎Policy and Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George⏎Washington University⏎102. Sage, William, MD, JD, Professor of Law, Medicine, and (by courtesy) Government⏎and Public Service, Assistant Vice President of the Health Science Center, Texas A&M⏎University⏎103. Schneider, Andy, JD, Research Professor of the Practice, McCourt School of Public⏎Policy, Georgetown University⏎104. Seiler, Naomi, JD, Professor, Department of Health Policy and Management, Milken⏎Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎105. Silberman, Pam, JD, DrPH, Professor Emerita, Director, Executive Doctoral Program in⏎Health Leadership, Department of Health Policy and Management, UNC Gillings⏎School of Global Public Health⏎106. Silverman, Hannah, MPH, Senior Research Associate, Department of Health Policy and⏎Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George Washington⏎University⏎107. Siminoff, Laura A., PhD, Laura H. Carnell Professor of Public Health, Department of⏎Social and Behavioral Sciences, Temple University⏎108. Skinner, Daniel, PhD, Professor of Health Policy, Department of Social Medicine,⏎Heritage College of Osteopathic Medicine, Ohio University⏎109. Slifkin, Becky, PhD, Professor Emerita, Department of Health Policy and Management,⏎UNC Gillings School of Global Health⏎110. Sparer, Michael, JD, PhD, William Henry Welch Professor of Public Health,⏎Department of Health Policy and Management, Columbia University Mailman School⏎of Public Health⏎111. Strasser, Julia, DrPH, MPH, Assistant Research Professor, Department of Health Policy⏎and Management, Director, Reproductive Health Workforce & Policy Research Center,⏎Fitzhugh Mullan Institute for Health Workforce Equity, Milken Institute School of⏎Public Health, The George Washington University⏎112. Suter, Sonia M., JD, MS, Professor of Law, The Kahan Family Research Professor of⏎Law, Co-Director, Health Law & Policy Program, The George Washington University⏎Law School⏎113. Swartz, Katherine, PhD, MS, Professor of Health Policy and Management, Emerita,⏎Harvard T.H. Chan School of Public Health, Harvard University⏎⏎The Honorable Secretary Kennedy⏎February 23, 2026⏎RE: Public Health Deans and Scholars’ Response to Department of Health and Human Services’ Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Page 27⏎114. Tavrow, Paula, Ph.D., Adjunct Professor, Department of Community Health Sciences,⏎University of California Los Angeles Fielding School of Public Health⏎115. Vichare, Anushree, PhD, MBBS, MPH, Assistant Professor, Department of Health⏎Policy and Management, Milken Institute School of Public Health, The George⏎Washington University⏎116. Vyas, Amita N., PhD, MHS, Professor, Director, Maternal & Child Health Program,⏎Department of Prevention and Community Health, Milken Institute School of Public⏎Health, The George Washington University⏎117. Waldman, Ronald, MD, MPH, Professor Emeritus of Global Health, Milken Institute⏎School of Public Health, The George Washington University⏎118. Warren, Keegan, JD, LLM, Executive Director, Institute for Healthcare Access, Texas⏎A&M University Health Science Center⏎119. Wasserman, Alan G., MD, MACP, Eugene Meyer Emeritus Professor of Medicine,⏎Department of Medicine, The George Washington School of Medicine and Health⏎Sciences⏎120. Watson, Sidney D., JD, Jane and Bruce Robert Professor of Law and Scholar in⏎Residence, Center for Health Law Studies, Saint Louis University School of Law⏎121. Westmoreland, Timothy M., JD, Professor from Practice, Emeritus, Georgetown⏎University School of Law⏎122. Wise, Paul H., Richard E. Behrman Professor of Child Health and Society, Senior⏎Fellow, Freeman Spogli Institute for International Studies, Core Faculty, Center on⏎Democracy, Development and the Rule of Law, Affiliated faculty at the Center for⏎International Security and Cooperation, Stanford University⏎123. Wisner, Katherine L., MD, MS, DLFAPA, Professor of Psychiatry and Pediatrics,⏎Developing Brain Institute, Children’s National Hospital, Professor of Obstetrics and⏎Gynecology, George Washington University School of Medicine⏎124. Yang, Y. Tony, DSc, LLM, MPH, Endowed Professor of Health Policy, The George⏎Washington University⏎125. Young, Heather A., PhD, MPH, Vice Chair/Professor/MPH Epidemiology Co-⏎Director/PhD Epidemiology Director, Department of Epidemiology, Milken Institute⏎School of Public Health, The George Washington University♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0159♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Advocate Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Advocate Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 16, 2026⏎The Hon. Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary, U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201.⏎Submitted electronically⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care; HHS Health Sector AI RFI [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On behalf of Advocate Health, we appreciate the opportunity to comment on the recently⏎published Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care.1 Our full comments are outlined below.⏎About Advocate Health⏎Advocate Health is a leading nonprofit integrated health system in the United States⏎providing care under the names Advocate Health Care in Illinois; Atrium Health in the⏎Carolinas, Georgia, and Alabama; and Aurora Health Care in Wisconsin. Advocate Health is a⏎national leader in clinical innovation, health outcomes, consumer experience, and value-⏎based care, with Wake Forest University School of Medicine serving as the academic core of⏎the enterprise. Headquartered in Charlotte, North Carolina, Advocate Health serves⏎approximately six million patients and is engaged in hundreds of clinical trials and research⏎studies.⏎Advocate Health is nationally recognized for its expertise in cardiology, neuroscience,⏎oncology, pediatrics, and rehabilitation, as well as organ transplants, burn treatments, and⏎1 90 FR 60108⏎⏎specialized musculoskeletal programs. Advocate Health employs an estimated 167,000 team⏎members across 69 hospitals and more than 1,100 care locations and offers one of the⏎nation’s largest graduate medical education programs with more than 2,000 residents and⏎fellows across more than 200 programs. Additionally, Advocate has five skilled nursing⏎facilities with 572 licensed beds. Committed to ensuring access to care for all, Advocate⏎Health provides more than $6 billion in annual community benefits.⏎Background⏎a. AI Regulation: How Current HHS Regulations Impact AI Adoption for Clinical Care⏎The primary federal regulatory frameworks affecting the adoption of AI in clinical care are⏎Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) regulations and the Food and⏎Drug Administration’s (FDA) oversight of medical devices. To create an environment that⏎supports responsible and scalable AI integration in healthcare, federal policymakers should⏎modernize HIPAA to address contemporary data sharing practices, strengthen privacy and⏎security protections, and provide clearer guidance on the appropriate use of patient data for⏎AI development and deployment.⏎Federal legislation and regulation should set clear, consistent national standards for liability,⏎accountability, transparency, and responsible AI use in both clinical care and operations.⏎These standards should include a uniform definition of “artificial intelligence” and distinguish⏎regulatory expectations across different AI types, including machine learning tools, computer⏎vision systems, and large language models.⏎Because states regulate medical licensure, federal policymakers should also outline the⏎appropriate boundaries for state level AI laws. States should retain the ability to regulate⏎clinical practice, but within a federal framework that prevents conflicting or overly restrictive⏎rules. This alignment is essential for multistate health systems and nationwide AI⏎development.⏎b. Reimbursement: Policy Changes to Ensure Payers Incentivize Access to High Value AI⏎Clinical Interventions⏎The cost of implementing AI solutions, including the necessary infrastructure for monitoring,⏎governance, model evaluation, workforce training, and cybersecurity presents a significant⏎⏎barrier for small, rural and critical access hospitals as well as providers serving areas with⏎significant socio-economic needs. Without targeted reimbursement policies or federal⏎financial support, these organizations will be unable to adopt emerging AI technologies, even⏎as such tools become standard components of high-quality care. As a result, disparities in⏎access to advanced clinical tools may widen hampering one of this administration’s key goals⏎of improving population health outcomes.⏎Federal policy should ensure that payers have both the incentive and the operational ability⏎to support access to clinically validated, high value AI tools. This may include establishing⏎clear reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled services, offering supplemental payments or⏎grant-based support for under-resourced providers, and creating mechanisms to evaluate the⏎cost effectiveness and clinical benefit of AI tools across diverse care settings.⏎c. Research and Development: Opportunities for HHS Investment to Advance AI Integration⏎in Care Delivery⏎HHS has a critical role in supporting research and development efforts that advance the safe,⏎equitable, and evidence-based integration of AI into clinical care. Strategic investments⏎should focus on:⏎• Supporting research into AI models that address social determinants of health and⏎structural inequities.⏎• Funding pilot programs that test AI applications in under resourced settings, including⏎rural and safety net providers.⏎• Advancing long-term R&D initiatives aimed at improving population health, care⏎delivery efficiency, and equitable access to emerging technologies.⏎Specific Questions⏎In this section, we present our detailed responses to HHS’s specific questions from the RFI.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎⏎Barriers to Clinical Use of Large Language Models⏎Several significant barriers currently limit the safe and effective use of large language models⏎(LLMs) in clinical care. First, these systems may generate inaccurate or fabricated information⏎(“hallucinations”), creating risks if clinicians rely on outputs without sufficient validation.⏎LLMs may also omit critical clinical details or fail to surface relevant information, particularly⏎when inputs are incomplete or ambiguous.⏎Second, there is no clear or consistent framework governing liability when an AI system⏎provides misleading, incomplete, or unsafe recommendations. This uncertainty poses⏎challenges for health systems, clinicians, and vendors, and may slow adoption of otherwise⏎promising tools.⏎Third, the financial burden associated with implementing LLM-based solutions—including⏎vendor licensing fees, internal development costs, technical infrastructure, and ongoing⏎monitoring and governance—is substantial. These costs are especially prohibitive for small,⏎rural, and resource constrained healthcare organizations, raising concerns about widening⏎disparities in access to advanced clinical technologies.⏎At Advocate Health, we mitigate these issues by leveraging retrieval-augmented generation⏎(RAG) and small language models (SLMs) and integrating our core protocols to limit⏎hallucinations. Additionally, with these guardrails in place, the use of these LLMs results in a⏎sensitivity that is better than human performance alone. For smaller organizations, these⏎guardrails may not be easily implemented.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in⏎clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations⏎citations.⏎HIPAA, Privacy, and Security Considerations⏎HIPAA should be reviewed and modernized to address privacy and security implications⏎related to transmitting protected health information to technology companies such as⏎OpenAI, Anthropic, and Google. As these entities increasingly handle sensitive patient data,⏎including information from medical records, wearables, and patient generated sources, they⏎⏎should be required to adhere to the same privacy, security, and compliance standards applied⏎to traditional healthcare providers and certified health IT systems.⏎Additionally, the use of highly regulated data—such as Medicare Shared Savings Program⏎(MSSP) information—is governed by strict program requirements and contractual obligations.⏎These restrictions may limit the ability of providers and Accountable Care Organizations⏎(ACOs) to use such data in AI solutions or even to store it within AI-enabled platforms⏎intended for internal use. Updated guidance is needed to clarify permissible uses of regulated⏎data in AI development and operational workflows, particularly for organizations participating⏎in federal value-based programs.⏎FDA Oversight and Regulatory Alignment⏎On January 6, 2026, FDA published revised final guidance documents on Clinical Decision⏎Support Software (“Revised CDS Guidance”) and its General Wellness Policy for Low Risk⏎Devices (“Revised General Wellness Guidance”). FDA’s revised CDS and General Wellness⏎guidance documents directly address industry concerns about overregulation by broadening⏎the categories of digital health products the agency chooses not to regulate, either based on⏎its interpretation of statutory authority or as a matter of policy. We commend the FDA for⏎publishing these updated guidance documents.⏎However, FDA’s regulatory approach should be considered jointly with broader federal AI⏎policies to ensure coherence, minimize duplication, and create predictable expectations for⏎developers, clinicians, and health systems. Clear definitions, risk-based frameworks, and⏎coordinated interagency guidance would significantly improve the regulatory environment for⏎clinical AI.⏎AI Use by Payers and Impacts on Care Access⏎Legislation and regulation must also account for the increasing use of AI by payers to⏎determine prior authorizations, coverage approvals, and claims denials. These tools exert⏎direct and significant influence on the clinical care that patients receive. To protect patients⏎and promote accountability, payers using AI for coverage determinations should be required⏎to maintain transparency regarding:⏎• The role of AI in claims decisions,⏎• Rates of denials, appeals, and overturns,⏎⏎• The specific AI models or algorithms used, and⏎• Demonstrated cost efficiencies achieved through AI.⏎Further, any financial savings attributable to AI-enabled administrative efficiencies should be⏎expected to translate into lower premiums or reduced cost sharing for beneficiaries rather⏎than solely accruing to payer institutions.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address⏎them?⏎Liability Issues⏎Even when AI tools are not regulated as medical devices, they increasingly influence real⏎clinical and social service decisions. This creates practical and legal challenges, particularly for⏎safety‑net providers and community‑based organizations that lack extensive compliance or⏎legal support. In community health settings, significant uncertainty remains regarding liability.⏎Clinicians and community partners are often unsure whether they face increased exposure if⏎they rely on an AI‑generated recommendation that results in harm, or if they decline to⏎follow such a recommendation and harm nevertheless occurs. These ambiguities place⏎disproportionate burdens on federally qualified health centers, rural hospitals, and⏎faith‑based or nonprofit programs that already operate with limited legal resources and⏎heightened perceived risk.⏎HHS regulations could provide meaningful clarity by:⏎• Affirming that reasonable reliance on validated clinical decision support tools does⏎not, by itself, create new or expanded liability.⏎• Encouraging malpractice and institutional liability frameworks to distinguish clearly⏎among clinician errors, organizational governance failures, and defects in the AI tools⏎themselves.⏎Privacy Issues⏎Many AI developers, data brokers, and digital health platforms operate outside the scope of⏎HIPAA yet routinely process highly sensitive health and social information. When failures or⏎⏎misuse occur, lines of responsibility are often unclear, leaving patients uncertain about where⏎to seek recourse. To address these gaps, HHS could:⏎• Require clear documentation of roles and responsibilities among AI developers,⏎healthcare organizations, and vendors.⏎• Extend HIPAA‑aligned privacy and security standards to key non‑covered entities that⏎participate in healthcare‑related AI activities.⏎• Establish transparency requirements so patients and caregivers can readily identify⏎who is accountable for AI‑driven recommendations and decisions at each stage of⏎care.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Metrics and Evaluation Frameworks for AI⏎AI performance metrics should remain locally defined, as their relevance depends on how⏎each tool is integrated into clinical workflows. Federal regulation of specific metrics would be⏎overly prescriptive and could hinder innovation. However, federal guidance should encourage⏎the use of governance structures, such as Advocate Health’s Framework for the Appropriate⏎Implementation and Review of AI (FAIR AI)2 principles or internal AI oversight committees, to⏎ensure organizations systematically evaluate AI tools for safety, equity, and alignment with⏎patient-care priorities.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎Limiting Additional Accreditation or Credentialing Requirements⏎2 Wells BJ, Nguyen HM, McWilliams A, Pallini M, Bovi A, Kuzma A, Kramer J, Chou SH, Hetherington T, Corn P,⏎Taylor YJ, Cuison A, Gagen M, Isreal M; FAIR-AI Consortium. A practical framework for appropriate implementation⏎and review of artificial intelligence (FAIR-AI) in healthcare. NPJ Digit Med. 2025 Aug 11;8(1):514. doi:⏎10.1038/s41746-025-01900-y. PMID: 40790350; PMCID: PMC12340025.⏎⏎Efforts to promote innovative and effective use of AI in healthcare should not rely on creating⏎new accreditation, certification, or credentialing requirements. These processes often impose⏎substantial administrative and financial burdens without delivering meaningful improvements⏎in quality or safety. Registry abstraction and reporting already represent significant expenses⏎for healthcare organizations, and federal and state agencies, as well as accrediting bodies,⏎should be constrained from adding additional requirements that further increase⏎administrative load without demonstrable value.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎Areas of Highest Potential Impact for AI in Healthcare⏎AI has the greatest potential to drive meaningful improvements in healthcare by enhancing⏎administrative efficiency, strengthening workforce capacity, accelerating research, and⏎enabling more precise and timely personalized care. High-impact administrative applications⏎include billing, supply chain optimization, and clinical data abstraction. Workforce supporting⏎tools, such as those for scheduling, documentation, and radiology, can reduce burden and⏎improve productivity. In addition, AI can significantly advance research and development⏎efforts and support more individualized, data driven clinical decision making.⏎AI can assist with clinician and patient satisfaction. By supporting tools such as ambient⏎listening and chart summarization, reducing documentation burden, improving efficiency, AI⏎tools enable clinicians to work at the top of their license. These capabilities not only free⏎clinicians to spend more meaningful time with patients but also help health systems manage⏎ongoing workforce shortages more effectively⏎Shortcomings of current AI tools⏎• Analysis of social drivers: In our community health portfolio, we routinely⏎encounter patients who fully understand their care instructions, adhere to⏎prescribed medications, attend follow-up appointments, and make recommended⏎lifestyle changes, yet remain unable to carry out their care plans due to significant⏎social barriers. Many lack reliable transportation to medical visits. Others cannot⏎afford their medications even with insurance coverage. Some do not have stable⏎⏎housing or consistent access to nutritious food. Many work multiple jobs with⏎limited ability to take time off for medical needs.⏎o Current clinical AI tools do not appear to incorporate these critical social⏎factors—such as the availability of community resources, individual⏎financial constraints, or the practical feasibility of care recommendations—⏎into their assessments or outputs. These systems may identify a patient as⏎high risk for hospital readmission, but they cannot determine whether that⏎same patient has the means to attend a follow-up appointment or afford⏎necessary prescriptions once insurance benefits are exhausted.⏎• Linguistic/cultural gap: Most existing AI systems are trained predominantly on⏎English language data and interactions from majority populations. While⏎translation functionalities may technically be available, these models often lack⏎the cultural, linguistic, and contextual grounding necessary to generate⏎recommendations that are meaningful or actionable in patients’ day-to-day lives.⏎Novel AI Tools with the Greatest Potential⏎An integrated tool for social determinants of health (SDOH) screening and resource⏎navigation represents one of the most impactful opportunities for next-generation clinical AI.⏎Such a system could synthesize individual clinical data with community level indicators, such⏎as food access, housing stability, transportation availability, and other relevant social⏎determinants, to assess patient needs accurately and predict the likelihood of successful care⏎plan completion. Importantly, this assessment would extend beyond clinical risk factors to⏎incorporate material, social, and logistical barriers that frequently impede adherence.⏎A tool of this kind could automatically match patients with real time community resource⏎availability and generate referrals to appropriate social services, community-based⏎organizations, and financial assistance programs. Ideally, it would also track resource⏎utilization and identify unmet needs or systemic gaps, thereby informing both individualized⏎care planning and longer-term community investment strategies. In this model, AI would⏎augment, rather than replace, the critical work performed by community health workers and⏎care navigators by equipping them with data driven insights and automated administrative⏎support.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎⏎Roles, Decision Makers, and Governance Structures⏎The appropriate roles and governance structures for AI adoption vary based on the size and⏎complexity of a healthcare organization. Large systems like Advocate Health typically employ⏎an AI or Digital Health Officer and maintain data governance and AI governance committees,⏎national and specialty service lines, clinical informatics teams, and IT departments responsible⏎for implementing models and vendor solutions.⏎AI deployment should be guided by the organization’s strategic priorities, with consideration⏎of both immediate and long-term value. Returns on AI investment should be assessed not⏎only through short-term financial metrics but also through broader impacts on the patient⏎and provider experience, access to care, and overall system performance.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Ensuring Data Access and Transparency in AI Development⏎Given the near monopoly among a few major electronic health record (EHR) vendors, these⏎systems should permit greater interoperability and connectivity with AI developers, including⏎health systems conducting internal AI development. Currently, EHR vendors control tightly⏎how data can be exported and integrated into organizational data platforms. As a result, even⏎large systems such as Advocate Health face constraints on how their own data can be⏎accessed, processed, and used to develop advanced AI solutions, including those that rely on⏎machine learning and large language models. This limits innovation and slows progress⏎toward more effective, data driven care.⏎Federal policy could also establish a transparent framework governing the quality and risk⏎profile of data used to train large language models (LLM). Such a framework would clearly⏎indicate whether training data were scraped from the internet, derived from medical records,⏎or sourced from clinical research—like how scientific evidence is graded. Greater⏎transparency would allow healthcare organizations to better assess the reliability, safety, and⏎appropriateness of LLM based tools used in clinical or operational settings.⏎Enhanced interoperability represents a critical lever for expanding market opportunities.⏎Current federal interoperability requirements are primarily focused on read-only access to⏎individual patient-level data, which constrains both the scope and scale of AI applications.⏎⏎While these application programming interfaces (API) facilitate patient-mediated data⏎exchange and point-in-time clinical insights, they do not reliably support population-level⏎data aggregation needed for model training.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patient Privacy and Concerns About Agentic AI⏎Patients express significant concerns about the privacy of video, audio, and personal health⏎data, as well as the risk that agentic AI systems may provide incorrect or biased information.⏎Updating and modernizing HIPAA would help address these concerns by strengthening⏎protections for emerging data types and clarifying expectations for the safe, transparent use⏎of AI in clinical and patient facing settings.⏎Conclusion⏎In conclusion, we would like to highlight our key comments below.⏎• Given the rapid pace at which AI solutions are emerging in healthcare, alongside a⏎continuously expanding field of technology vendors and a growing patchwork of⏎state specific legislation, there is an urgent need for clear, coordinated federal⏎action.⏎• Federal leadership is essential to modernize HIPAA, increase transparency for AI⏎used by payers, and prevent interoperability barriers created by EHR vendors.⏎• Adoption must focus on clinical value and long-term strategic benefit, not additional⏎administrative burden.⏎• AI’s highest near-term impact lies in administrative simplification, workforce⏎support, personalized care, and research acceleration.⏎• Healthcare organizations need flexible governance structures, not prescriptive⏎federal mandates, to evaluate AI tools in alignment with organizational strategy and⏎patient outcomes. Adopting a risk‑based framework like Advocate Health’s FAIR‑AI⏎enables responsible, scalable AI use while avoiding rigid rules that can stifle⏎innovation and slow adoption.⏎⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback on HHS’s Health Sector AI RFI. If you have⏎any questions about our comments or need any additional information, please do not⏎hesitate to contact Sabra Rosener, VP, Federal Affairs and Government Policy⏎(Sabra.Rosener@aah.org). We stand ready to serve as a resource as you continue your⏎important efforts to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.⏎Sincerely,⏎James A. Crowder⏎Chief Technology and AI Officer♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0160♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Association of periOperative Registered Nurses (AORN), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Association of periOperative Registered Nurses (AORN)♔♔♔♔♔♔January 22, 2026⏎Health and Human Services⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎RIN: 0955-AA13⏎On behalf of the Association for perioperative Registered Nurses (AORN), we write in response to HHS’s⏎December 23rd request for information on what HHS can do to accelerate the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.⏎As the professional membership association for perioperative registered nurses, AORN defines, supports,⏎and advocates for patient and staff safety through exemplary practice in all phases of perioperative⏎nursing care using evidence-based guidelines, continuing education and clinical practice resources.⏎AORN’s evidence-based Guidelines for Perioperative Practice address 36 perioperative practice areas⏎and set the gold standard for interdisciplinary perioperative practice.⏎New and scheduled to be published May 21, 2026, is the AORN Guideline for Integration of Artificial⏎Intelligence. Based on PRISMA systematic review methodology, this Guideline will first be available for⏎public comment from Dec. 23, 2025 through January 31, 2026.⏎AORN agrees with the HHS on the importance of health care providers effectively using AI-enabled⏎medical devices and tools to enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower health care costs, and improve⏎health outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities. We have adopted an evidence-informed⏎position that is simultaneously optimistic and cautious. AORN believes that AI is suitable for⏎augmentation of clinical decision making, but AI cannot and must never be a replacement for a human’s⏎clinical judgement. The human expertise, empathy and accountability required for clinical decision-⏎making in practice cannot be replicated. Clinicians should be full partners in preparing for the future of AI⏎that intersects their respective practice settings. AI integration methods matter – when integrated ethically⏎and responsibly, AI has the potential to improve health care including patient outcomes, patient⏎experience, and health care worker experience. When ineffectively integrated, AI has the potential to do⏎harm. Risk mitigation should be prioritized in practice and risk mitigation strategies must be nimble and⏎responsive to the ever-evolving AI risk landscape - both the known and unanticipated. AI education is⏎essential and should be implemented in the academic setting and in the organizations where the AI-⏎enabled technology is used. Competency should be assessed before using any AI-enabled technology.⏎Based on our Guideline authors’ recent review of the literature and research during development of⏎AORN’s Guideline for Integration of Artificial Intelligence, AORN offers the following feedback:⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Barriers for private sector innovation in AI adoption and use in clinical care exist in multiple forms:⏎• Lack of involvement of end-users (i.e., health care professionals whose job roles and⏎responsibilities intersect with the AI tool used in clinical care) throughout the planning and⏎development processes.⏎⏎• Insufficient reporting and publication of AI tool development, testing, and validation processes.⏎• Limited ability for health care organizations to effectively evaluate, monitor, and maintain AI⏎tools after deployment, including issues of interoperability with existing technology infrastructure⏎and internal network capabilities.⏎• Regulatory uncertainty related to approvals and accountability.⏎• Resource constraints and issues of interoperability with existing technology infrastructure,⏎especially in under-resourced health care organizations (e.g., rural, urban, non-academic medical⏎centers).⏎• Clinician trust.⏎Each of these barriers contributes to the disconnect between the private sector enthusiasm, the influx of⏎funding for novel AI-enabled technology, and real-world implementation in clinical care settings.⏎Successful development, implementation, and maintenance of AI-enabled technology require early and⏎intentional interdisciplinary collaboration among disciplines including technology developers and end-⏎users (e.g., health care organizations, health care professionals) to identify and address internal and⏎external barriers to innovative solutions that can enhance organizational efficiency, patient care delivery,⏎and improve patient outcomes. Additionally, developers of AI-enabled technology must prioritize⏎transparent reporting and publication of development processes, data collection and processing activities,⏎and performance evaluation (i.e., internal and external validation of AI performance in real-world⏎applications) to support user trust and adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎Resource constraints present a unique obstacle when integrating AI into clinical care settings. The⏎literature indicates that many health care organizations are not currently equipped with technology⏎infrastructure that is capable of meeting the increased demands for computational power and data-⏎handling that AI-enabled technology requires.1,2⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical⏎care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS should prioritize changes to the Code of Federal Regulations related specifically to de-identification⏎and re-identification of individually identifiable health information and the use of AI-enabled technology.⏎Minimum necessary actions to limit and protect individually identifiable health information may vary⏎according to the AI model type and architecture, individual health or other information being collected⏎and used, and the amount of data required to re-identify an individual. To comply with § 45 CFR⏎164.514(b), additional considerations should be made to account for identifiers not already included in the⏎current list of identifiers in § 45 CFR 164.514(b)(2)(i). With as few as four (4) pieces of data, a person⏎may be reidentified, with that data not being exclusive to the items identified in paragraphs (b)(2)(i)(A)⏎through (b)(2)(i)(R). Addressing this issue can provide health care organizations with additional guidance⏎for data privacy and sharing practices that are required to protect individually identifiable health⏎information and ultimately increase trust in the adoption and use of AI in clinical care.⏎Additional regulatory and policy solutions might include:⏎⏎• requiring continuing education and professional development training in health care organizations⏎specific to the safe use of the AI-enabled technologies deployed in the organization and used by⏎health care workers;⏎• clarifying the requirements for “informed consent” when using AI assisted technologies;⏎• adopting a Total Product Lifecycle approach: iterative improvements while maintaining safety⏎standards3;⏎• adopting Good Machine-Learning Practice: standards for ensuring quality and organizational⏎excellence3;⏎• establishing requirements for safety reporting: a clearinghouse for adverse events similar to⏎MedWatch for pharmaceuticals4;⏎• establishing requirements for standardized reporting of relevant AI model characteristics that explain⏎the purpose, design, development, use, and limitations of the model similar to a nutrition facts label or⏎model card; and⏎• establishing requirements for a Unique AI Identifier: a transparency label to enhance trust and⏎accountability in AI systems.4⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The deployment of AI in health care presents novel governance and accountability challenges, arising⏎from unresolved questions regarding responsibility, liability, and oversight when automated systems fail⏎or when risks go unrecognized. Current legal and regulatory frameworks do not yet provide clear⏎guidance on how accountability should be assigned among developers, implementers, and end-users,⏎particularly in situations where insufficient safeguards or unvalidated model behavior contribute to patient⏎harm or degraded clinical performance (i.e., model drift). As a result, the absence of defined pathways for⏎recourse or corrective action can impede both safety and trust. Addressing these gaps is essential to⏎ensure that AI-enabled technology is integrated in a manner that supports consistent standards of care,⏎transparent accountability, and responsible operational use.⏎Processes for implementation of AI-enabled technology are unique to the local context in which the⏎technology will be deployed and require a robust internal governance framework to provide a structured,⏎standardized approach for each phase of integration into the clinical setting (e.g., identification,⏎evaluation, deployment, lifecycle management). HHS should dedicate resources to investigating the use⏎and influence of the development and use of a governance framework in health care organizations on⏎outcomes that are important to patients (e.g., quality of life, perceived health) and outcomes related to⏎health conditions (e.g., surgical site infections, mortality, morbidity). HHS also should create mechanisms⏎for mandatory reporting of serious adverse events linked to the use of AI-enabled technology in the⏎clinical setting.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎Several evaluation frameworks and reporting guidelines have been developed to facilitate health care⏎organizations’ evaluation of AI-enabled technology before widespread integration, including⏎• Model cards (i.e., standardized, plain-language document that explains the key characteristics of⏎AI-enabled technology to understand what the model is, how it works, and how it should [and⏎should not] be used),⏎• Expert/consensus guidelines for reporting and publishing AI-enabled technology development,⏎testing, and validation processes (e.g., FUTURE-AI,5 CHEERS-AI,6 CONSORT-AI,7 CLAIM,6⏎MINIMAR,8 STARD-AI,6 SPIRIT-AI,7 TRIPOD+AI6), and⏎• Expert/consensus frameworks for health care professionals’ evaluation of AI-enabled technology⏎(e.g., PROBAST+AI,9 DECIDE-AI,10 CAIR Checklist,11 20 Critical Questions12).⏎Although they were developed by experts in relevant fields (e.g., data science, statistical analysis,⏎computer science, use of the reporting guidelines, health care), the evaluation frameworks are yet to be⏎validated for use and efficacy in positively influencing the evaluation and adoption of AI-enabled⏎technology, as well as the influence of the use of these evaluation frameworks on patient outcomes and⏎organizational outcomes (e.g., end-user satisfaction, workflow burden, usability, operational efficiency,⏎financial impacts to the organization). HHS should dedicate resources to investigate the development and⏎influence of the use of standardized evaluation frameworks for AI-enabled technology in health care⏎organizations on these outcomes.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎HHS can best support private sector activities, effective and safe use of AI-enabled technology, and⏎trustworthy AI by incentivizing the private sector to adopt activities and organizational priorities that⏎promote trustworthy AI. The concept of trustworthy AI is widely published in the literature with much of⏎the published literature referring to⏎• Transparency (i.e., extent to which individuals who interact with or use AI have access to⏎information about the systems and their outputs),⏎• Reliability and validity (i.e., ability to consistently perform as required and intended, without⏎failure),⏎• Accountability (i.e., responsibility of the individual(s) or organization(s) involved in the design,⏎development, evaluation, deployment, and maintenance of AI-enabled technology to uphold safe,⏎ethical, and equitable health care delivery practices),⏎• Fairness (i.e., ethical and equitable development and use of AI-enabled technology to prioritize⏎that all patients receive accurate, unbiased, and high-quality care, regardless of background,⏎identity, or circumstances),⏎• Utility (i.e., extent to which AI-enabled technology can be used to achieve specific goals and⏎objectives to improve patient outcomes, clinical decision-making, and health care delivery), and⏎⏎• Fitness of purpose (i.e., measure of how well AI-enabled technology meets the goals and⏎objectives of the deploying organization based on the intended use, requirements and preferences,⏎and organizational priorities).⏎Facilitating or requiring accreditation, certification, or credentialing of organizations that develop and⏎distribute AI-enabled technology for use in the health care sector (herein referred to as ‘AI developers’)⏎would incentivize these entities to adopt evidence-informed and ethically grounded practices for⏎developing, testing, validating, and transparently reporting all relevant information about AI-enabled⏎products (e.g., data collection and preprocessing practices, use of representative and diverse datasets,⏎transparent performance and validation processes, benefits and limitations, intended and unintended uses,⏎requirements for technical resources). By incentivizing AI developers to adopt an industry standard for⏎accreditation or similar, health care organizations would be encouraged to seek out and prioritize AI⏎developers that have demonstrated rigorous, sanctioned development processes, thereby enhancing trust⏎and adoption of these innovative technologies.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce⏎costs?⏎Published literature detailing the real-world performance and cost of AI-enabled technology is limited,⏎with much of the literature reporting feasibility studies and small, nonexperimental studies with limited⏎generalizability. Additional research is required to investigate the large-scale adoption and effects of AI-⏎enabled technology in clinical settings. Some examples of AI-enabled technology performance that has⏎fallen short of expectations for efficacy, safety, and generalizability include the integration of a sepsis⏎early warning system developed and integrated with an electronic health record platform,13 and AI-⏎enabled technology to enhance detection and removal of colorectal polyps and adenomas during⏎gastroenterology procedures.14⏎Artificial intelligence tools that would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes are⏎tools that are able to provide outputs that are transparent and explainable by the end-user to highlight⏎which input features most influenced a model’s prediction. Explainability is crucial for supporting trust in⏎the AI model, regulatory oversight, bias detection, error identification and mitigation, and improved⏎adoption by clinicians who need to understand the basis of AI outputs, as autonomous AI-enabled⏎technology is not encouraged in patient care delivery.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Primary influencers on the adoption of AI for clinical care are the intended end-users. Published⏎organizational experience reports15 detail the influence and necessity for early inclusion of health care⏎professionals whose roles and responsibilities intersect with AI use. Involvement and prioritization of the⏎needs, preferences, and priorities of the end-user can improve the development process by including⏎visual displays, alert type and frequency, and even technology platforms that incentivize the end-user to⏎use AI-enabled technology as intended. This involvement also facilitates trust in technology by repeated⏎⏎exposure to and use of the technology, explanation of the potential benefits and consequences of use, and⏎the factors of use that matter most to the end-user (e.g., visual displays, ease of use, education, effects on⏎user workflow). Other roles that influence adoption of AI include individual(s) in the organization with⏎the responsibility and authority to oversee the evaluation, integration, and maintenance of AI-enabled⏎technology. Organizational experience reports detail the creation of health care executive roles dedicated⏎to these tasks (e.g. Chief Health AI Officer). This role can serve as the executive presence and authority⏎to develop, maintain, and update an AI governance framework across the organization and should remain⏎a separate and dedicated role.⏎Administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care are numerous. Fragmented organizational⏎processes for identifying, evaluating, and deploying AI-enabled technology present concerns for⏎inadequate governance oversight at the organizational level, in addition to duplicative or inefficient⏎evaluation or deployment processes. Lack of familiarity with relevant regulations, competing priorities⏎and incentives for adoption across an organization, and lack of validated governance framework present⏎additional challenges for health care organizations to overcome.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability to accelerate the development and adoption of AI-enabled technology in the⏎health care setting requires industry adoption for reporting of the different phases of the lifecycle of AI.⏎Regulation for the use of standardized AI model cards (see Response to Question 4 for additional⏎information) or other reporting requirements encourage transparent development processes and consumer⏎evaluation prior to purchase and deployment. Additionally, development of large, de-identified national⏎data sets for the use and development of AI-enabled technology may enhance interoperability and⏎accelerate AI research. Federated learning as a strategy for anonymizing data and training AI models⏎through decentralized data repositories can enhance accessibility to datasets and improve privacy.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎AI has the promise to revolutionize the health care sector by rapidly increasing efficiency, decreasing⏎administrative burden, and augmenting clinical decision-making to improve patient outcomes. Caregivers⏎and patients alike believe that AI has the potential to offset administrative tasks with more time for⏎genuine patient interaction and connection. This is coupled with concerns for privacy (e.g., ambient⏎listening technology being used to identify or scrutinize patients or caregivers), self-determination,⏎retaliatory or discriminatory care delivery based on protected characteristics, and a loss of the human⏎element of health care delivery.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎All research should prioritize improvement of patient outcomes and clinician user experience with other⏎outcomes recognized as secondary (eg, cost savings, efficiency). In other words, the research should be⏎⏎structured so that the aim is always to improve the human (ie, patient and clinician) experience above⏎other perceived improvements. Areas of AI research the HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care include standardized methods for reporting and evaluating AI-enabled⏎technology pre- and post-deployment, effects of implementing a structured governance framework on⏎organizational operations and patient outcomes, methods for detecting and mitigating bias in the⏎development and use of AI-enabled technology, implications for use of agentic AI tools in clinical care,⏎and the use of AI to reveal factors contributing to adverse events in clinical care.⏎Question 10(a): Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎A substantial and rapidly expanding body of published research and other reports examines the clinical⏎impact of adopted AI-enabled technology across diverse care settings. Systematic reviews and health-⏎economic evaluations report improvements in diagnostic accuracy, workflow efficiency, and patient⏎outcomes, although the strength and quality of evidence vary across specialties and methodologies.16,17⏎Recent reviews also note that while many studies demonstrate positive effects, the evidence base remains⏎heterogeneous and would benefit from more rigorous, real-world evaluations. Overall, the literature⏎provides clear indications that AI tools already in use have measurable clinical and operational impacts,⏎though continued research is needed to assess long-term performance and safety. The AORN Guideline⏎for Integration of Artificial Intelligence includes a section dedicated to examples of clinical application of⏎AI enabled technology that have been studied in the perioperative practice setting.18⏎Question 10(b): How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎Costs and benefits of AI must be approached on an individualized and ongoing basis; unique to each⏎organization, governance structure, and AI-enabled technology being integrated into the organization.⏎Costs and benefits are characterized by identifying improved diagnostic accuracy, reduced clinician⏎burden, effects on patient outcomes, efficiency or time savings, and frequency of adverse events. There is⏎a need for more comprehensive and methodologically consistent evaluations of the costs and benefits.⏎Additionally, the literature indicates that AI adoption not only generates costs and benefits but also shifts⏎resource allocation, labor distribution, and financial responsibilities across stakeholders, although these⏎transfers are less consistently measured than direct costs or benefits.⏎Should you have any questions, please feel free to contact our Senior Director of Evidence-Based⏎Perioperative Practice:⏎Lisa Spruce, DNP, RN, ACNP, CNOR, CNS-CP, ACNS, FAAN⏎lspruce@aorn.org⏎Sincerely,⏎David Wyatt, PhD RN CNOR NEA-BC FAORN FAAN⏎AORN CEO/Executive Director⏎⏎References⏎1. Rony MKK, Parvin MR, Ferdousi S. Advancing nursing practice with artificial intelligence:⏎Enhancing preparedness for the future. Nurs Open. 2024;11(1). Doi:10.1002/nop2.2070.⏎2. Ranjbar A, Mork EW, Ravn J, et al. Managing risk and quality of AI in healthcare: Are hospitals⏎ready for implementation? Risk Manag Healthc Policy. 2024;17:877-882.⏎Doi:10.2147/RMHP.S452337.⏎3. M. Mashar, S. Chawla, Fangyue Chen, Baker Lubwama, Kyle Patel, Mihir A. Kelshiker, P.⏎Bachtiger, and N. Peters. “Artificial Intelligence Algorithms in Health Care: Is the Current Food⏎and Drug Administration Regulation Sufficient?” JMIR AI, 2023.⏎4. Steven Labkoff, Bilikis Oladimeji, J. Kannry, Anthony Solomonides, Russell Leftwich, Eileen⏎Koski, Amanda L. Joseph, et al. “Toward a Responsible Future: Recommendations for AI-⏎Enabled Clinical Decision Support.” J. Am. Medical Informatics Assoc., 2024.⏎5. Lekadir K, Frangi AF, Porras AR, et al. FUTURE-AI: International consensus guideline for⏎trustworthy and deployable artificial intelligence in healthcare. BMJ. 2025;388:e081554.⏎Doi:10.1136/bmj-2024-081554.⏎6. Collins GS, Moons KGM, Dhiman P, et al. TRIPOD+AI statement: Updated guidance for⏎reporting clinician prediction models that use regression or machine learning methods. BMJ.⏎2024;385:e078378. Doi:10.1136/bmj-2023-078378.⏎7. Rivera SC, Liu X, Chan A, et al. Guidelines for clinical trial protocols for interventions involving⏎artificial intelligence: The SPIRIT-AI extension. BMJ. 2020;370:m3210.⏎Doi:10.1136/bmj.m3210.⏎8. Crossnohere NL, Elsaid M, Paskett J, et al. Guidelines for artificial intelligence in medicine:⏎Literature review and content analysis of frameworks. J Med Internet Res. 2022;24(8):e36823.⏎Doi:10.2196/36823.⏎9. Moons KGM, Dame JAA, Kaul T, et al. PROBAST+AI: An updated quality, risk of bias, and⏎applicability assessment tool for prediction models using regression or artificial intelligence⏎methods. BMJ. 2025;388:e082505. Doi:10.1136/bmj-2024-082505.⏎10. Vasey B, Nagendran M, Campbell B, et al. Reporting guideline for the early stage clinical⏎evaluation of decision support systems driven by artificial intelligence: DECIDE-AI. BMJ.⏎2022;377:e070904. Doi:10.1136/bmj-2022-070904.⏎11. Olczak J, Pavlopoulos J, Prijs J, et al. Presenting artificial intelligence, deep learning, and⏎machine learning studies to clinicians and healthcare stakeholders: An introductory reference⏎with a guideline and a Clinical AI Research (CAIR) checklist proposal. Acta Orthop.⏎2021;92(5):513-525. Doi:10.1080/17453674.2021.1918389.⏎12. Vollmer S, Mateen BA, Bohner G, et al. Machine learning and artificial intelligence research for⏎patient benefit: 20 critical questions on transparency, replicability, ethics, and effectiveness. BMJ.⏎2020;368:l6927. Doi:10.1136/bmj.l6927.⏎13. Boag W, Hasan A, Kim JY, et al. The algorithm journey map: a tangible approach to⏎implementing AI solutions in healthcare. Npj Digital Medicine. 2024;7(1):87.⏎Doi:10.1038/s41746-024-01061-4.⏎⏎14. Aziz M, Haghbin H, Sayeh W, et al. Comparison of artificial intelligence with other interventions⏎to improve adenoma detection rate for colonoscopy: A network meta-analysis. J Clin⏎Gastroenterol. 2024;58(2):143. Doi:10.1097/MCG.0000000000001813.⏎15. Abraham J, Bartek B, Meng A, et al. Integrating machine learning predictions for perioperative⏎risk management: Towards an empirical design of a flexible-standardized risk assessment tool. J⏎Biomed Inform. 2023’137:104270. Doi:10.1016/j.jbi.2022.104270.⏎16. Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, et al. External validation of a widely implemented proprietary⏎sepsis prediction model in hospitalized patients. JAMA Intern Med. 2021;181(8):1065-1070.⏎Doi:10.1001/jamainternmed.2021.2626.⏎17. Loftus T, Altieri M, MD MS, et al. Artificial intelligence-enabled decision support in surgery:⏎State-of-the-art and future directions. Ann Surg. 2023;278(1):51-58.⏎Doi:10.1097/SLA.0000000000005853.⏎18. Guideline for integration of artificial intelligence. In: Guidelines for Perioperative Practice.⏎Denver, CO: AORN, Inc; (in press).♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0161♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Milrod, Barbara, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Milrod♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔This technology has not been studied in a responsible manner, as one would for any "health" intervention, and already we are seeing vulnerable people falling prey to irresponsible and essentially pablum useless suggestions tailored by marketing firms to maintain engagement. Responsible research needs to be conducted about true risks of these apps-of note, no studies with such a focus have been undertaken yet. This cannot be permitted to play any role in mental health care. At the moment, this is like releasing an untested, malleable, biological weapon on our most vulnerable citizens.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0162♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Federation of American Hospitals, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Federation of American Hospitals♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Via electronic submission at http://www.regulations.gov⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy,⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care; 90 Fed. Reg. 60,108 (December 23, 2025)⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎As the national representative of more than 1,000 leading tax-paying hospitals and health systems throughout the⏎United States, the Federation of American Hospitals (FAH) appreciates the opportunity to submit comments to the⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ONC) regarding the above-referenced Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. We commend the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) focus on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) specifically within clinical care. FAH members are at the⏎forefront of responsibly integrating AI to enhance clinical care, support the health care workforce and improve system-⏎wide efficiency. When deployed thoughtfully, AI can equip health care providers with better information, reduce⏎administrative burdens, and help direct limited resources where they are needed most. Federal policy should⏎acknowledge and reinforce this leadership by establishing clear, practical guardrails that encourage innovation while⏎safeguarding patients and maintaining public trust. Our comments below offer recommendations to achieve these⏎objectives.⏎In considering the development and use of AI tools in health care, it is important to first understand how AI technology⏎is defined, and particularly whether a tool incorporates generative AI and machine learning (ML) approaches.⏎Generative AI and ML systems adapt by learning from the data they process, whereas rule-based tools operate⏎predictably and are commonly used in health care to support evidence-based clinical decision-making or clearly defined⏎operational tasks. We note that the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has authorized over 1,300 devices with AI⏎aspects for marketing in the United States, with AI serving either as a stand-alone product or support other medical⏎technologies (such as AI embedded in a drug pump or surgical robot).⏎Advances in generative AI and ML are creating opportunities and challenges. FAH and its members appreciate the⏎promise of AI and the need to carefully balance oversight to ensure safe and appropriate development and use⏎with the need to innovate and continue to advance this transformational technology. If developed and used⏎responsibly, AI has the potential to revolutionize health care delivery, including improving patient care, operational⏎efficiency, and health outcomes, along with countless other advancements in the field.⏎We emphasize the unique attributes of health care (e.g., size of the industry; impact on health and safety; health data⏎privacy) and the needs of the multiple health sector actors that will interact with AI technologies, such as AI developers,⏎health care technologists, health care organizations, health care providers and patients/consumers. We also note the⏎need to consider the topic of liability, which is a new and challenging aspect of AI. While health care providers bear⏎1⏎⏎responsibility for the care they provide, the developers of commercial AI products must also be accountable if safety,⏎bias, or other harms are caused by the AI tool itself or a flaw in the tool’s development.⏎We appreciate the RFI’s emphasis on identifying actions that HHS can take to “accelerate the adoption and use of AI⏎as part of clinical care,” and with that tenet in mind, we make the following key recommendations, among others, to⏎accomplish that goal, as further discussed below:⏎• Establish a Uniform Regulatory Framework: FAH strongly recommends a singular federal regulatory⏎framework, including a national federal privacy law, that expressly preempts state laws, for the use of AI in⏎health care. A patchwork of individual state AI regulations severely limits the ability to deploy AI at scale in⏎health care and limits the potential positive impact AI could have on a health care system’s performance.⏎• Create a Coordinated, Risk-Management Federal Approach for Clinical AI: FAH supports a uniform, risk-⏎based approach to AI implementation and oversight, allowing hospitals the flexibility to assess internal risk⏎and implement protections accord to scale, and with a focus on incorporating the existing risk management⏎and security framework already established in health care under HIPAA and HITECH.⏎• Modernize Payment Policy to Support AI-Enabled Clinical Care: The absence of stable, payment⏎pathways is a major barrier to adoption for many AI tools, particularly when embedded within clinical⏎infrastructure rather than offered as discrete, separately billable services, and payment for AI software is⏎insufficient for broader adoption. Thus, payment policy is needed to encourage competition and innovation,⏎especially for AI tools meeting evidence and performance standards.⏎• Provider Collaboration: FAH encourages HHS to institutionalize mechanisms for ongoing provider⏎engagement in clinical AI policymaking,⏎• Other Important Areas for Consideration: liability for AI tools; recognition of permissible clinical AI uses;⏎patient transparency and consent balanced with AI workflow; and research to support workforce AI readiness.⏎Establish a Uniform Regulatory Framework⏎Regulatory fragmentation remains the most significant barrier to responsible AI adoption in clinical care and the United⏎States has the opportunity at the federal level to establish a uniform and practical framework to promote AI in health⏎care, considering the context of existing laws and regulations that can be leveraged as a baseline to ensure safe and⏎effective use of AI in health care. Where new laws and regulations are necessary, we strongly recommend a⏎singular federal regulatory framework, including a national federal privacy law, that expressly preempts state⏎laws, for the use of AI in health care. We caution against a patchwork of individual state AI regulations, as this⏎severely limits the ability to deploy AI at scale in health care and limits the potential positive impact AI could⏎have on an overall health care system’s performance. As acknowledged by the December 11, 2025, Executive⏎Order directing development of a minimally burdensome national policy framework for AI, the current framework of⏎individual state AI regulations is extremely unwieldy and actively hampers the ability of health systems to leverage AI in⏎an efficient and/or large scale manner. A single federal regulatory framework would go a very long way in alleviating⏎these difficulties. A more uniform approach to AI regulation would catalyze rather than hamper AI development and⏎adoption in health care.⏎We also urge establishment of standard definitions and benchmarking tools to support effective⏎implementation of AI for health care. For example, the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) has a⏎long history of working across the public and private sectors to develop consensus-based, workable standards and⏎tools to advance the use of technologies that improve the lives of individuals. We recommend that NIST develop a⏎single set of definitions within the area of AI that can be leveraged by others, avoiding contradictory legal and⏎regulatory approaches. In addition, NIST has the appropriate technical knowledge and experience to develop⏎benchmarking metrics and other tools that both AI developers and deployers would be able to leverage as they⏎seek to ensure that AI solutions are safe, secure, trustworthy, and fit to purpose in health care. For example,⏎NIST could lead development of standards for model testing, validation, benchmarking and lifecycle monitoring of AI⏎2⏎⏎technologies used in health care. We recommend maintaining a database of validated data sets used for AI technologies⏎to facilitate greater explainability for these technologies.⏎Create a Coordinated, Risk-Management Federal Approach for Clinical AI⏎FAH supports a uniform, risk-based approach to AI implementation and oversight. Risk management is a key⏎aspect of ensuring that AI solutions, generative and rules-based, are appropriately developed, disseminated,⏎and monitored over time. For AI solutions in particular, a risk management approach can help both developers and⏎health care providers efficiently focus technical and organizational controls on higher risk deployment.⏎Risk management approaches are deeply integrated with health care systems, including both existing workflows and⏎regulatory schemes. Hospitals and health care systems have extensive experience in, and have long deployed, risk⏎management approaches to ensure the safety of health care services and the privacy and security of health information.⏎At the federal level, the existing risk management landscape includes a range of safety and privacy requirements, such⏎as the Medicare Conditions of Participation and Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA). In addition,⏎health care technologies have established risk management for electronic medical record (EMR) and health record⏎(EHR) workflows. Any AI regulatory requirements that conflict with existing risk management processes will slow down⏎progress in realizing the benefits of technology and could inadvertently result in less effective risk management of⏎complex health care systems and organizations. An AI regulatory framework should take into consideration the⏎extent to which the AI model directly impacts patient care and should focus on processes to ensure algorithms⏎are transparent, auditable, ethical, fair and safe by incorporating the existing risk management framework⏎already established in health care. To that end, as discussed above, it is critical that hospitals and health care systems⏎are involved in the creation of AI policies for health care, including establishing standards around risk categories for AI⏎use cases.⏎Further, while the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS), ASTP/ONC⏎and Office for Civil Rights (OCR) each play an essential role in protecting patient safety, supporting interoperability,⏎ensuring appropriate payment, and safeguarding privacy, the absence of coordinated risk-based guidance creates⏎uncertainty for health systems attempting to deploy AI at scale.⏎Health systems routinely face unresolved questions regarding when AI functionality embedded in clinical workflows⏎constitutes a regulated medical device, how ongoing model updates or retraining affect regulatory status, and where⏎accountability resides when AI tools are developed by vendors but configured, implemented, and monitored locally.⏎This uncertainty slows adoption and can result in overly cautious implementation decisions that limit patient benefit. For⏎this HHS-led effort, we encourage the development of coordinated, cross-agency guidance that clearly explains how⏎existing regulatory frameworks apply to common clinical AI use cases, such as documentation assistance, predictive⏎risk models, clinical prioritization, and diagnostic support. Importantly, such guidance should distinguish between tools⏎that are of high risk of impacting patient safety and those that are of low risk to patient safety and calibrate oversight⏎accordingly.⏎Leverage Existing HIPAA Frameworks for Clinical AI Data Governance⏎FAH strongly supports leveraging existing health care privacy and security frameworks to govern clinical AI.⏎The HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules and subsequent Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical⏎Health Act (HITECH) already provide a comprehensive foundation for managing patient data, and duplicative or parallel⏎AI-specific privacy regimes risk creating confusion and unnecessary burden without improving patient protection. If HHS⏎decides to pursue prescriptive mandates in future regulations regarding transparency, disclosures, and opt-out rights⏎for developers and deployers, we recommend excluding HIPAA Covered Entities from such requirements by adding a⏎robust and comprehensive safe harbor provision. Transparency expectations should be calibrated to clinical risk and⏎operational feasibility, avoiding requirements that would necessitate dual workflows or conflict with established clinical⏎processes.⏎Clarify Federal Guidance for Non-Medical Device AI in Clinical Care⏎Many AI applications used in clinical care fall outside traditional medical device regulation, creating novel legal and⏎implementation challenges for providers and vendors. HHS should not broadly regulate all non-medical device AI⏎3⏎⏎but should play a coordinating and enabling role by issuing clear, non-binding guidance that explains how⏎existing federal frameworks apply across common deployment models. Such guidance should clarify⏎accountability expectations without prescribing rigid responsibility splits, including considerations related to⏎documentation, ongoing monitoring, model updates and change management, incident reporting boundaries, and⏎security obligations. Consistent with a “One HHS” approach, HHS should align expectations across OCR, ONC, CMS,⏎FDA, and relevant research agencies, promote standardized best practices for procurement, monitoring, and equity⏎assessment, fund shared evaluation and governance infrastructure, and publish voluntary but influential model⏎contracting and accountability guidance to support safe, scalable, and equitable use of non-medical device AI in clinical⏎care.⏎Explicitly Recognize the Full Scope of Permissible Clinical AI Uses⏎As AI adoption expands in health care, federal policy should clearly reflect the full range of ways AI is used in clinical⏎and operational settings. Many current and emerging applications extend beyond traditional predictive analytics and⏎require explicit recognition to avoid unintended regulatory gaps. Generative AI models increasingly are used to support⏎clinical documentation by drafting clinical impressions, reports, and summaries that are reviewed, edited, and finalized⏎by clinicians. Regulatory frameworks should explicitly recognize the creation and drafting of clinical content as⏎a permissible and appropriate use of AI when coupled with clinician oversight, rather than limiting policy⏎constructs to pattern recognition or prediction alone.⏎Clinical AI applications also rely on multi-modal inputs, including audio and video. Audio inputs support ambient⏎documentation and workflow efficiency, while video inputs enable safety-focused use cases such as virtual sitters and⏎fall risk monitoring. References to “data sets” in regulatory language may be ambiguous as applied to live or near-real-⏎time feeds. HHS should establish clear guidance clarifying that appropriately governed processing of audio and video⏎inputs is permissible to support patient safety and modern care delivery. Finally, policy language should avoid⏎constraining AI use to narrow concepts such as “assisting treatment” alone. In practice, AI supports a broad operational⏎scope that directly enables clinical care, including triage, patient identification, background safety monitoring, and early⏎risk detection. Regulatory frameworks that recognize this broader context will better support scalable, responsible AI⏎adoption without forcing artificial workflow separations that reduce efficiency or compromise care quality.⏎Collaborate with Providers to Enable Safe and Scalable AI Adoption⏎FAH member experience consistently demonstrates that effective AI policy cannot be developed without deep⏎engagement from health care providers who are responsible for delivering care, managing data regulated under HIPAA⏎and other health data privacy and security laws, and integrating technology into complex clinical workflows. As AI moves⏎from pilot use into routine clinical operations, provider engagement must extend beyond consultation toward sustained⏎operational partnership. Health systems bring critical expertise regarding workflow design, clinician adoption, patient⏎communication, and unintended consequences that may not be evident in premarket evaluation alone. We encourage⏎HHS to institutionalize mechanisms for ongoing provider engagement in clinical AI policymaking, including⏎participation in pilots, demonstrations, and post-deployment monitoring initiatives, such as FDA post-market⏎surveillance. These partnerships will help ensure that federal guidance is feasible across diverse care settings and⏎aligned with the realities of clinical practice.⏎Balance Patient Transparency with Integrated Clinical AI Workflows⏎As HHS considers approaches to patient transparency, engagement, and trust in clinical AI, it is essential to account⏎for the integrated nature of many AI applications within health care delivery. Proposals that rely on individualized opt-⏎out mechanisms for AI systems, while well-intentioned, may unintentionally undermine both care quality and operational⏎feasibility. Many AI tools used in clinical care are deeply embedded within electronic health record systems and care⏎delivery platforms, enhancing safety and efficiency in real time. For example, AI-enabled clinical decision support may⏎flag potential drug–drug interactions or surface risk indicators as clinicians are actively ordering medications or⏎managing care. In these contexts, enabling individual opt-outs would require the creation of parallel non-AI workflows⏎to replicate the same functions through manual review. Such parallel systems are technically complex, costly to⏎maintain, and may increase the risk of error rather than reduce it.⏎Clinical experience also demonstrates that AI can improve diagnostic accuracy and timeliness, particularly in time-⏎sensitive and resource-constrained settings. AI-assisted radiology tools, for example, can rapidly identify imaging⏎patterns consistent with acute stroke, supporting faster diagnosis and treatment in emergency departments where⏎specialist availability may be limited. Requiring human-only review or bifurcated workflows based on individual⏎4⏎⏎preference could delay care and negatively affect outcomes, even for patients who might otherwise choose to opt in⏎but are unable to do so because of their clinical condition.⏎The technical and practical challenges associated with individualized opt-out requirements, including software redesign,⏎dual workflow maintenance, and additional staff training, risk deterring health care organizations from adopting AI⏎solutions altogether. In some cases, limiting AI use could also result in worse patient outcomes where no viable human⏎substitute exists. Maternal-fetal monitoring provides one illustrative example: AI-enabled systems could continuously⏎analyze high-frequency physiologic data to identify emerging risk patterns and support earlier clinical intervention,⏎capabilities that are difficult to replicate reliably through manual review alone.⏎For these reasons, we encourage HHS to avoid policies that rely on individualized opt-out mechanisms for⏎integrated clinical AI systems. A more balanced approach would emphasize transparency about how AI is used in⏎care delivery, incorporate general consent for AI use within care settings consistent with existing workflows for efficiency⏎purposes, and rely on robust oversight mechanisms to ensure safety, efficacy and equity through continuous monitoring⏎of model performance and outcomes.⏎Modernize Payment Policy to Support AI-Enabled Clinical Care⏎In the Hospital Outpatient Prospective Payment System for Calendar Year 2026 Final Rule, CMS acknowledged that⏎rapidly emerging software technologies, including software-as-a-service (SaaS) tools designed to support clinical⏎decision-making, do not yet fit cleanly within existing payment frameworks. The absence of stable, repeatable payment⏎pathways remains a major barrier to adoption for many AI tools—particularly those embedded within clinical⏎infrastructure rather than offered as discrete, separately billable services.⏎Under the Inpatient Prospective Payment System, the New Technology Add-on Payment (NTAP) has provided a limited⏎pathway for reimbursement of certain AI software, but its current scope is narrow and insufficient to support broader⏎deployment. Similarly, while the American Medical Association has established Current Procedural Terminology (CPT)⏎codes for some AI applications, these codes largely focus on diagnostic testing and do not address many⏎documentation, workflow, and decision-support use cases. Clear guidance on coverage expectations and medical⏎record documentation requirements for these technologies would support more consistent adoption. Additionally,⏎moving from fragmented Category III CPT codes toward more stable, valued reimbursement pathways for AI-⏎augmented analysis would further support responsible adoption.⏎To encourage competition and innovation, payment policies should be structured so that any AI tool meeting⏎the same evidence and performance standards may qualify under the same payment framework, regardless of⏎whether it is a vendor or development model. This approach would avoid preferential treatment and support both⏎commercial and internally developed tools that meet clinical and operational standards. In addition, HHS should⏎continue to play a central role in governing the use of AI in medical necessity determinations and utilization⏎management, ensuring that such tools protect patient access, reduce administrative friction, and align with established⏎coverage principles. Lastly, AI-related costs should be recognized as allowable expenses within value-based payment⏎models, coupled with incentives for outcomes-based contracting. Payment policies should avoid penalizing providers⏎for earlier or more accurate identification of high-risk conditions enabled by AI.⏎Clarify Developer and Deployer Expectations Without Redefining Liability⏎As AI adoption accelerates, it is essential that federal policy clearly distinguish between AI developers and AI⏎deployers. Health care providers frequently deploy third-party AI tools within clinical environments and may also⏎develop internal tools that are not commercialized. Federal guidance should recognize this reality and avoid⏎categorizing health care providers as developers subject to obligations intended for entities that design, commercialize,⏎and market AI products.⏎At the same time, deployment of AI in clinical care should reinforce the principle that AI is intended to support—not⏎replace—clinical judgment. Consistent with emerging federal guidance, including themes articulated in the Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services AI Playbook, AI tools should be implemented with appropriate human-in-the-loop⏎governance. Clinicians must remain the final decision-makers and validators of AI-informed outputs, whether those⏎outputs relate to care recommendations, documentation support, clinical test interpretation, diagnostic assistance, or⏎workflow prompts.⏎5⏎⏎Maintaining human oversight reflects longstanding clinical accountability models in which clinicians are responsible for⏎patient care decisions regardless of the tools used to support those decisions. AI tools should augment human work by⏎improving efficiency, accuracy, and situational awareness while preserving clinician accountability. Clear role definitions⏎and human-in-the-loop expectations also align with payment and compliance considerations. As federal payment⏎systems evolve to accommodate software-enabled technologies, billed services must continue to reflect properly⏎delivered and documented care, regardless of whether AI tools are used to support clinical workflows. Reinforcing these⏎principles supports patient safety, program integrity, and responsible innovation without imposing developer-level⏎obligations on health care providers.⏎Shared Responsibility and Developer Accountability⏎There is a shared responsibility between the developers and deployers/end-users of AI tools to build and deploy them⏎in a way that is safe, effective, and secure. While health care providers bear responsibility for the care they⏎provide, the developers of commercial AI products must be accountable for the safety and reliability of their⏎products and required to be truthful in marketing their products, especially since safety, bias, privacy and⏎security, or other harms may be caused by a flaw in the tool itself. Commercial AI and machine learning developers⏎must provide deployers/end-users of their tools with guidance on ethical use, such as when it is necessary to have “a⏎human in the loop,” and the limits of their models. Deployer/end-users will also need guidance on how to provide⏎oversight of AI tools that are in use to ensure that they are functioning appropriately over time, along with collaboration⏎from developers in properly monitoring AI technology performance over time.⏎Adopt an Evidence-Centric Lifecycle Approach to Clinical AI Evaluation⏎HHS should adopt an evidence-centric, lifecycle approach to evaluation of clinical AI that governs how tools⏎are validated, monitored, and maintained over time. This approach should include rigorous multisite pre-deployment⏎validation, standardized and transparent reporting artifacts, and instrumented continuous monitoring post-deployment⏎to assess performance, safety, and equity as models evolve. Public clinical AI test beds and pragmatic trials should be⏎used to support real-world validation across diverse care settings and patient populations, complemented by⏎independent test laboratories capable of generating reproducible and comparable evidence. Standardized methods for⏎robustness testing, drift monitoring, bias and fairness assessment, human factors and usability, and workflow impact⏎should be applied consistently across the lifecycle.⏎To operationalize this framework, HHS should leverage policy and programmatic levers—including grants, Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services Innovation Center pilots, Cooperative Research and Development Agreements⏎(CRADAs), and support independent evaluation infrastructure—to embed these requirements into practice. In doing so,⏎HHS should build on and recognize mature, provider-tested evaluation, accreditation, and governance approaches⏎already developed and in use across the private sector, rather than introducing entirely new frameworks that would⏎require broad retooling by providers and vendors. Aligning lifecycle evidence generation with payment policy will be⏎essential to rewarding clinical value, discouraging premature deployment, and enabling scalable, responsible adoption.⏎In addition, HHS should enable and recognize robust, voluntary private accreditation and certification frameworks for⏎clinical AI that are standards-based and updated as technologies evolve. Recognition of standardized test beds,⏎credentialing programs, and evaluation processes—supported by public reference datasets, aligned federal guidance,⏎and appropriate procurement incentives—would help create trusted market signals without imposing prescriptive⏎regulatory requirements.⏎Prioritize Implementation-Focused Research and Workforce Readiness⏎To accelerate responsible adoption of AI in clinical care, HHS should prioritize research investments that⏎strengthen both evaluation infrastructure and workforce-centered implementation science. HHS should fund⏎regulatory science and develop a shared evaluation infrastructure to support the responsible adoption of clinical AI,⏎including the creation of clinical AI test beds for real-world validation and investment in high-quality, ethically sourced,⏎representative AI-ready datasets. Use of CRADAs to co-develop reusable platform components and common⏎evaluation tools would help establish durable measurement standards that promote transparency, unlock competition⏎and reduce contracting and implementation costs.⏎Research involving nurses and the nursing workforce is a critical area that the HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care. Nurses represent one of the largest segments of the United States health care workforce⏎and are foundational to care delivery across virtually all clinical settings. Numbering more than five million professionals⏎across registered nurses, licensed practical and vocational nurses, and advanced practice registered nurses, the⏎6⏎⏎nursing workforce plays a central role in health care delivery. While AI research in other clinical domains offers⏎substantial promise, research focused on understanding adoption drivers, workforce impacts and implementation⏎challenges specific to nursing is essential for scalable and sustainable deployment.⏎Nurses also serve as primary adopters and sustainers of clinical technologies. Over time, they have integrated⏎increasingly complex devices and digital tools into care environments while maintaining the human-centered aspects⏎of care. As AI-enabled technologies continue to expand, research designed by and for nurses will be critical to ensuring⏎these tools enhance care quality and efficiency without introducing unintended burden. In alignment with HHS’s goal of⏎designing infrastructure responsive to user needs, priority research areas should include nurses’ perspectives on AI,⏎baseline understanding of AI-enabled tools, drivers and barriers to adoption across care settings, and strategies to⏎support safe, compliant integration of AI into evidence-based nursing workflows. Research examining both the benefits⏎and risks of AI in specific nursing contexts will be essential to supporting responsible adoption and strengthening public⏎trust.⏎*******************************⏎FAH appreciates the opportunity to address these critical issues, and looks forward to collaborating with ASTP/ONC in⏎advancing the use of AI for the benefit of patients, providers, communities and federal health care programs. As leaders⏎in thoughtfully deploying AI, our member tax-paying hospitals can play a pivotal role with HHS as it develops and⏎implements AI regulatory clarity, patient-centered transparency, payment policy and implementation-focused research⏎while leveraging existing privacy and compliance frameworks. This RFI represents a meaningful opportunity to move⏎from high-level AI principles to actionable, clinically grounded policy. If you have any questions or would like to discuss⏎further, please do not hesitate to contact Katie Tenoever, SVP & General Counsel, at ktenoever@fah.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎/s/⏎Charlene MacDonald⏎President and CEO⏎7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0163♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Mountjoy, Keith, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Keith♔Mountjoy♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS Request for Information⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence in Clinical Care⏎Docket No. 2025-23641⏎Submitted by:⏎Keith Mountjoy⏎Founder ~ Resonance Tracing Instrument (RTI)⏎Independent Researcher and Instrument Developer⏎Email: kmountjoy@quamitrylabs.com⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Response:⏎One of the primary barriers to AI adoption in clinical care is the lack of real-time, continuous⏎physiological measurement data. Most clinical AI systems rely on static or episodic inputs such⏎as imaging, laboratory results, or charted observations, which forces AI models to infer dynamic⏎internal state changes indirectly.⏎This creates challenges in trust, explainability, and generalization. When AI outputs cannot be⏎clearly tied to observable physical signals, clinicians are hesitant to rely on them for decision⏎support. Additionally, post-hoc validation workflows increase cost and slow adoption.⏎Another barrier is the mismatch between AI development and clinical workflows. AI tools are⏎often designed as decision-makers rather than as assistive measurement and observability layers,⏎which increases regulatory friction and liability concerns.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical⏎care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Response:⏎HHS should prioritize policies that distinguish between measurement-first AI systems and⏎decision-making AI systems. Measurement platforms that provide real-time physiological⏎signals and structured state indicators can reduce uncertainty without directly issuing clinical⏎recommendations, yet they are often evaluated under frameworks designed for diagnostic⏎devices.⏎Programmatic support for pilot-scale, non-authoritative AI systems would accelerate adoption⏎while maintaining safety. This includes R&D pathways that allow AI-enabled measurement tools⏎to be deployed for monitoring, validation, and workflow support prior to full diagnostic claims.⏎HHS may consider revisiting programmatic alignment under existing research and innovation⏎authorities (e.g., cooperative agreements and demonstration programs) to support technologies⏎that enhance observability and explainability for clinical AI, rather than focusing exclusively on⏎end-decision tools.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎Response:⏎For non-medical devices that support clinical care, the most promising evaluation methods⏎emphasize signal quality, stability, and state consistency rather than outcome prediction⏎accuracy alone. Metrics such as repeatability, drift detection, signal-to-noise ratio, and⏎confidence scoring are critical for ensuring reliability in real clinical environments.⏎⏎AI systems that manage instrumentation or measurement stacks should be evaluated on their⏎ability to:⏎• detect noise, motion artifacts, and coupling variability⏎• maintain stable operation across diverse patient populations⏎• produce interpretable state outputs rather than opaque predictions⏎HHS could further support these processes through targeted grants or cooperative agreements⏎focused on AI-assisted measurement validation, particularly in environments with high⏎variability and noise, such as bedside or outpatient settings.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎Response:⏎HHS should prioritize AI research that strengthens the measurement and observability layer of⏎clinical care. This includes AI systems that enhance real-time physiological sensing, manage⏎complex instrumentation, and provide structured, explainable internal state tracking that can be⏎used by clinicians and downstream AI models.⏎Published literature increasingly recognizes that AI performance in clinical settings is limited by⏎input data quality and temporal resolution. Research that integrates AI with real-time physical⏎measurement platforms can help close this gap by providing continuous, trustworthy signals⏎rather than relying solely on retrospective datasets.⏎Supporting AI-enabled measurement platforms through federal R&D investment would⏎accelerate adoption while improving safety, explainability, and clinician trust.⏎⏎Public–Private Research and Pilot Pathways⏎To accelerate adoption while maintaining safety and trust, HHS could support public–private⏎pilot programs that focus on AI-enabled measurement and observability technologies rather⏎than clinical decision automation. Pilot frameworks that allow early-stage systems to be⏎evaluated as non-authoritative monitoring tools would enable real-world validation, workflow⏎integration, and data generation without increasing clinical risk. Such programs could be⏎structured through cooperative agreements, demonstration projects, or targeted R&D funding⏎that emphasizes signal reliability, explainability, and interoperability with existing clinical⏎systems. These pilots would help bridge the gap between research prototypes and scalable⏎clinical deployment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0164♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ALIGNMT AI Inc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ALIGNMT AI Inc♔♔♔♔♔♔Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎I. Executive Summary⏎ALIGNMT AI is an AI governance platform provider that works with health systems, payers,⏎regulatory bodies, and AI developers to operationalize AI governance frameworks, risk⏎assessment, and accountability structures for healthcare AI. We offer these comments from the⏎perspective of practitioners who see daily the gap between the theoretical promise of healthcare⏎AI and the governance and oversight realities on the ground.⏎We are broadly supportive of HHS's ambition to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. We are⏎equally convinced that acceleration without robust governance is not a shortcut - it is a liability.⏎The history of health IT is replete with examples of rapidly deployed technology that generated⏎unintended harm, eroded clinician trust, and ultimately set back adoption rather than advancing⏎it.⏎Our comments focus primarily on two areas where we believe HHS can take the most⏎consequential action:⏎● Standards and oversight: Establishing a coherent, risk-proportionate, and enforceable⏎governance framework for clinical AI - particularly for the large and growing category of⏎non-medical device AI tools - that reduces uncertainty for developers while providing⏎meaningful accountability for patients.⏎● Patient safety and equity: Ensuring that the imperative to accelerate AI adoption does⏎not come at the cost of the populations most likely to be harmed by poorly governed AI,⏎including patients from underserved communities, those with disabilities, and those with⏎limited health literacy.⏎We urge HHS to resist the false choice between speed and safety. The best governance⏎frameworks are enabling, not obstructive - they create the predictability and trust that⏎sustainable AI adoption requires.⏎Our core recommendation: HHS should establish a tiered, risk-proportionate oversight⏎framework for clinical AI that is coherent across FDA, ONC, and CMS - backed by quality⏎and equity requirements, transparency standards, and meaningful post-market⏎surveillance - and that applies to all AI tools used in consequential clinical decisions, not⏎only those that meet the FDA's definition of a medical device.⏎II. Barriers to Innovation (Question 1)⏎⏎From our experience advising health systems and developers, the most consequential barriers⏎to responsible AI adoption in clinical care are governance-related, not technical:⏎Regulatory Fragmentation and Ambiguity⏎The fundamental governance problem facing clinical AI in the United States is jurisdictional⏎incoherence and incompleteness. FDA regulates AI that meets the definition of Software as a⏎Medical Device (SaMD) under 21 C.F.R. Part 880. ONC shapes health IT through certification⏎requirements under 45 C.F.R. Part 170. CMS influences behavior through payment policy. Each⏎operates on a different timeline, with different requirements, and for different subsets of the AI⏎ecosystem.⏎The result is that a large and clinically consequential category of AI tools - non-device clinical⏎decision support (CDS) that does not meet FDA's criteria under 21 U.S.C. §399d - operates in a⏎near-regulatory vacuum. These tools may analyze patient records, generate differential⏎diagnoses, recommend treatment pathways, flag patients for escalation, guide provider or⏎patient queries, suggest care plans, or provide clinical reference information. This category also⏎includes AI tools offered directly to patients and consumers — whether through patient portals,⏎mobile health applications, or direct-to-consumer platforms — that generate symptom⏎assessments, triage recommendations, care navigation guidance, or health coaching, and tools⏎offered by providers to patients as an extension of the clinical encounter. These tools occupy⏎the same regulatory vacuum as provider-facing non-device CDS, despite often operating with⏎less clinical oversight and reaching populations with greater health literacy variability. They are⏎used in life-affecting decisions. Yet they face no mandatory evaluation requirements, no⏎performance standards, no transparency obligations, and no post-deployment surveillance or⏎performance measurement requirements.⏎This is not primarily a barrier to innovation. It is a patient safety gap that also, secondarily,⏎creates business risk for health systems that deploy these tools and reputational risk for⏎developers whose products underperform in ways they did not anticipate.⏎Liability Ambiguity Creates Perverse Incentives⏎The current liability environment simultaneously discourages adoption by cautious health⏎systems or clinicians and fails to adequately incentivize responsible development by AI vendors.⏎When a clinical AI tool contributes to a harmful outcome, there is no settled legal framework⏎governing how responsibility is allocated across the developer, the deploying health system,⏎and the treating clinician. This ambiguity causes legal departments to counsel non-adoption,⏎even when the clinical evidence for a tool is strong - and it means that developers face limited⏎accountability when their tools cause harm.⏎Clear governance frameworks - including defined standards of care for AI deployment,⏎transparency requirements, and appropriate safe harbors for compliant adopters - would benefit⏎both sides of this equation.⏎Inequitable Access to Governance Infrastructure⏎⏎Smaller health systems, rural providers, federally qualified health centers (FQHCs), community⏎health centers (CHCs), physician and clinician practice groups, generally lack the internal⏎capacity to conduct meaningful AI governance: they cannot independently validate vendor⏎claims, audit models for quality or bias, or establish robust post-deployment surveillance. This⏎creates an equity paradox: the organizations that serve the most vulnerable patients are least⏎equipped to protect those patients from poorly performing AI - and least able to capture AI's⏎benefits from a position of informed decision-making.⏎III. Regulatory Priorities (Question 2)⏎We recommend HHS treat the following regulatory actions as the highest-priority interventions⏎for accelerating responsible AI adoption:⏎3.1 Establish a Unified, Tiered Federal Framework for Clinical AI Governance⏎The most impactful single action HHS could take is to develop - in collaboration across FDA,⏎ONC, and CMS - a unified risk-tiered framework that applies consistently to all AI tools used in⏎consequential clinical decisions, regardless of whether those tools qualify as FDA-regulated⏎medical devices.⏎We recommend a three-tier structure for classifying healthcare AI tools based on their purpose⏎of use for risk management:⏎● Tier 1 — Autonomous or high-stakes decision AI: Tools that make or substantially⏎determine clinical decisions with limited clinician override capacity. These should be⏎subject to the most rigorous evaluation requirements, including prospective clinical⏎validation, mandatory demographic performance disaggregation, and active post-market⏎surveillance.⏎● Tier 2 — Clinical decision support with clinician review: Tools that generate⏎recommendations reviewed and acted upon by a clinician. These should require pre-⏎deployment validation documentation, transparency disclosures, and periodic⏎performance reporting. Patient and consumer-facing AI tools — including those offered⏎through providers via patient portals or care management platforms and those offered⏎directly to consumers outside a clinical relationship — require explicit placement within⏎this framework. Where such tools generate clinical recommendations, triage decisions,⏎or care guidance that a patient acts upon without an intervening clinician review, they⏎should be classified at minimum as Tier 2 and evaluated accordingly, notwithstanding⏎the absence of a traditional provider-patient workflow. Direct-to-consumer tools that⏎operate fully outside any provider relationship warrant additional consideration given the⏎reduced safety net available when recommendations are misunderstood or misapplied.⏎⏎● Tier 3 — Administrative and workflow AI: Tools that do not directly affect clinical⏎decision-making (e.g., scheduling, coding, documentation assistance). These warrant⏎lighter oversight focused primarily on data privacy and security.⏎This framework would not require new statutory authority for many of its elements - it could be⏎implemented through ONC certification criteria (45 C.F.R. §170.315), CMS conditions of⏎participation, requirements for AI developers under the CMS Health Tech Ecosystem, and HHS-⏎wide guidance. It would also provide the clarity that developers and health systems need to plan⏎for compliance.⏎3.2 Mandatory Transparency and Disclosure Standards for Tier 1 and Tier 2 Tools⏎Health systems currently evaluate AI vendors with almost no standardized information required⏎from the developer. Vendors make performance claims using their own metrics, their own test⏎sets, and their own definitions. There is no equivalent of the FDA's Summary of Safety and⏎Effectiveness Data (SSED) for non-device clinical AI.⏎HHS should require, as a condition of use in any health systems, hospitals, physicians, or⏎clinicians funded by or in receipt of federal funds related to the provision of designated health⏎services as defined by 42 CFR 411.351, that developers of Tier 1 and Tier 2 clinical AI tools⏎provide standardized transparency documentation including:⏎● Training data characteristics: source, date range, demographic composition, and any⏎known gaps or biases.⏎● Validation methodology: whether validation was prospective or retrospective, internal or⏎external, and the characteristics of the validation population.⏎● Performance metrics: disaggregated by race, ethnicity, sex, age, primary language, and⏎insurance status at minimum.⏎● Known limitations and contraindications: specific patient populations or clinical contexts⏎where the tool has demonstrated degraded performance.⏎● Update and versioning history: what has changed in each model version and how⏎changes have been validated.⏎This standard should be developed in collaboration with NIST (building on the AI Risk⏎Management Framework), standards development organizations (HL7, IEEE), and industry, and⏎should be codified as an ONC certification criterion under 45 C.F.R. §170.315.⏎3.3 Post-Market Surveillance Requirements⏎The FDA's real-world performance monitoring framework for AI/ML-based SaMD represents a⏎sophisticated and practical model for post-market surveillance. HHS should develop an⏎analogous framework for non-device clinical AI that requires health systems and developers to⏎monitor and report on:⏎● Model drift: changes in AI tool performance over time as patient populations, clinical⏎practices, or data inputs change.⏎⏎● Disparate impact monitoring: ongoing tracking of whether performance gaps across⏎demographic subgroups are emerging, widening, or narrowing post-deployment.⏎● Adverse event reporting: a mechanism analogous to MedWatch by which clinicians and⏎patients can report suspected AI-related clinical errors.⏎This surveillance infrastructure should be administered through a multi-agency accountability⏎structure that reflects the actual reach of federal authority over the AI developer community.⏎ASTP/ONC is a logical administrative home for surveillance reporting — building on existing⏎infrastructure such as the Health IT Feedback and Inquiry Portal — and reporting obligations⏎should be linked to any standing an HIT developer holds under ONC's certification program.⏎However, ONC's reach is limited to certified HIT developers, and certification itself is voluntary⏎except where it conditions provider participation in Promoting Interoperability programs. Pure AI⏎developers and direct-to-consumer health IT vendors have little practical motivation to seek⏎ONC certification and therefore fall outside that accountability structure. To achieve the reach⏎necessary for meaningful post-market surveillance across the full AI developer community, CMS⏎should establish parallel reporting obligations tied to participation in CMS's Health IT Ecosystem⏎as a recognized vendor or developer — conditions that carry direct market consequences for a⏎broader class of AI developers than ONC certification alone. Additionally, HHS should engage⏎OIG as an enforcement backstop, recognizing that OIG holds investigative and enforcement⏎authority over certified HIT developers in matters involving Information Blocking and fraud, and⏎that this authority could extend meaningfully to material misrepresentations in AI performance⏎disclosures. No single agency has sufficient reach to cover the full landscape; a coordinated⏎ONC-CMS-OIG framework is necessary to ensure that surveillance obligations have genuine⏎teeth across the developer community.⏎3.4 HIPAA Modernization for Responsible AI Development⏎The current HIPAA Privacy Rule (45 C.F.R. Parts 160 and 164) was designed before large-⏎scale machine learning was foreseeable. Its application to AI training and validation creates⏎genuine compliance uncertainty. HHS should issue guidance clarifying:⏎● That training and validating AI models on de-identified data meeting the standards of 45⏎C.F.R. §164.514 does not constitute impermissible use or disclosure, and that a⏎business associate covered entity's AI governance program constitutes a legitimate⏎"healthcare operations" function under §164.501.⏎● That federated learning approaches — in which model training occurs at the site of the⏎data without raw data leaving institutional control — satisfy the Privacy and Security⏎Rule's requirements for minimum necessary and authorized use requirements when⏎implemented with appropriate technical security and administrative security controls.⏎● That patients must be meaningfully informed when AI plays a material role in their⏎diagnosis or treatment, consistent with the principle of informed consent, and that this⏎notification requirement applies regardless of whether the AI tool is FDA-regulated.⏎IV. Novel Legal and Implementation Issues (Question 3)⏎⏎From our governance advisory practice, we identify the following as the most consequential⏎unresolved legal and implementation challenges for non-device clinical AI:⏎4.1 The Accountability Gap⏎The central legal problem with non-medical-device clinical AI is the accountability gap: there is⏎no party who is clearly responsible for ensuring the tool performs safely across the full range of⏎patients and clinical contexts in which it is deployed. Developers disclaim liability through⏎contractual terms of use. Health systems and clinicians rely on vendor performance⏎representations they lack the expertise to verify. Clinicians are expected to exercise⏎independent judgment but are rarely given the information they need to calibrate their trust in an⏎AI tool. This problem is made materially worse by ASTP/ONC's proposed HTI-5 rule, which⏎removes requirements for exactly this kind of performance and transparency information for⏎developers certifying to 170.315(b)(11) — apparently on the mistaken assumption that such⏎information is not useful to end users in their clinical workflow. Transparency documentation⏎about model training, validation methodology, known limitations, and demographic performance⏎is not primarily a point-of-care reference tool for end users; it is essential infrastructure for health⏎systems and clinicians as buyers and implementers of clinical AI — necessary for responsible⏎evaluation, procurement, governance, and ongoing oversight. Removing these requirements⏎from the certification framework at the moment when clinical AI deployment is accelerating most⏎rapidly compounds the accountability gap rather than addressing it, and HHS should treat the⏎HTI-5 proposal on this point as a cautionary example of how regulatory action can inadvertently⏎erode the governance foundation that responsible AI adoption requires.⏎HHS should take the following steps to close this gap:⏎● Establish, through CMS Conditions of Participation (42 C.F.R. Parts 482 and 485), that⏎accredited hospitals and health systems bear primary accountability for ensuring that AI⏎tools deployed in clinical care have been appropriately evaluated, monitored, and⏎documented. This places responsibility with the deploying institution — which has the⏎most context about its patient population — rather than exclusively with developers.⏎● Clarify, through OIG guidance modeled on existing safe harbor provisions at 42 C.F.R.⏎§1001.952, that health systems that deploy AI tools in compliance with HHS-established⏎governance standards have an affirmative defense in regulatory proceedings arising⏎from AI-related adverse events. This creates a compliance incentive without eliminating⏎accountability.⏎● Require AI developers as part of condition of use in any health systems, hospitals,⏎physicians, or clinicians funded by or in receipt of federal funds related to the provision⏎of designated health services as defined by 42 CFR 411.351 to provide contractual⏎representations regarding model performance, update notification, and post-deployment⏎support, with HHS enforcement authority for material misrepresentations.⏎● Direct ASTP/ONC to retain and strengthen — rather than reduce — the transparency⏎and risk management documentation requirements currently embedded in the⏎certification program under 170.315(b)(11) for predictive decision support interventions.⏎As discussed above, the proposed HTI-5 rule would eliminate requirements that are⏎⏎foundational to responsible AI procurement, evaluation, and governance by health⏎systems and clinicians. These requirements should be preserved as a floor, not⏎removed, and should be treated as a governance asset of the certification program⏎rather than an administrative burden on developers.⏎○ HHS should further require that any HIT or AI developer seeking recognition as a⏎participating vendor or developer within CMS's Health IT Ecosystem meet⏎equivalent transparency and risk management documentation standards as a⏎condition of that participation — ensuring that developers who have not pursued⏎or do not hold ONC certification are nonetheless subject to comparable⏎disclosure obligations when they seek access to the federal market through⏎CMS-recognized channels.⏎4.2 Informed Consent and Patient Notification⏎There is currently no federal requirement that patients be informed when AI plays a material role⏎in their clinical care. This is a meaningful gap: patients have a right to understand how decisions⏎about their care are made, and that right is not contingent on whether a tool is FDA-regulated.⏎HHS should work with OCR to develop a practical patient notification standard — not a consent⏎form, but a clear notification obligation — that applies to Tier 1 and Tier 2 AI tools.⏎A distinct but related issue arises with patient and consumer-facing AI tools, where the patient⏎or consumer is the direct user rather than the subject of an AI-assisted provider decision. In⏎these contexts, disclosure obligations run directly to the individual using the tool, and must⏎address not only that AI is involved but the nature of its limitations, the absence of a clinical⏎relationship, and the appropriate role of the tool's output relative to professional medical advice.⏎HHS should clarify that notification standards apply with equal force to consumer-facing tools⏎offered through CMS's Health IT ecosystem, including those offered by providers to patients⏎and those offered directly to consumers through app marketplaces or digital health platforms.⏎4.3 Civil Rights and Algorithmic Discrimination⏎Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act (42 U.S.C. §18116) prohibits discrimination in HHS-⏎funded health programs on the basis of race, color, national origin, sex, age, and disability. AI⏎tools that systematically underperforms for protected groups — due to non-representative⏎training data, proxy variables that correlate with protected characteristics, or differential⏎deployment across patient populations — may constitute discriminatory practices under this⏎statute.⏎The HHS Office for Civil Rights (OCR) should issue enforcement guidance clarifying that health⏎systems have a proactive obligation to evaluate AI tools for disparate impact on protected⏎groups before deployment and on an ongoing basis. This is not a new legal standard — it is an⏎application of existing law to a new technology context. Clear guidance would provide health⏎systems with the framework to comply and would create meaningful accountability for⏎discriminatory AI deployment.⏎⏎Governance Principle: Accountability for AI in clinical care must be distributed across⏎the development, deployment, and monitoring lifecycle — not concentrated at any single⏎point. HHS governance frameworks should reflect this by placing complementary⏎obligations on developers, deploying health systems, and ongoing monitoring processes.⏎V. AI Evaluation Methods (Question 4)⏎Evaluation of non-device clinical AI should occur across three phases: pre-procurement, pre-⏎deployment, and post-deployment. HHS can play a meaningful role in supporting all three.⏎5.1 Pre-Procurement Evaluation⏎Health systems and providers, particularly smaller organizations and physician/clinician⏎practices, lack standardized tools for evaluating AI vendor claims. HHS should support the⏎development of:⏎● A publicly available AI procurement checklist and evaluation rubric, developed by⏎ASTP/ONC in collaboration with CMS under their Health Tech Ecosystem and health⏎system associations, that provides health systems with a minimum standard of due⏎diligence to apply before contracting with AI vendors.⏎● A national AI tool registry (modeled on ClinicalTrials.gov and administered by NIH or⏎ASTP/ONC) where developers register clinical AI tools, submit transparency⏎documentation, and post validation evidence. This allows health systems, researchers,⏎and payers to compare tools on a common informational basis.⏎5.2 Pre-Deployment Validation⏎Before deploying a clinical AI tool in a live clinical environment, providers should conduct local⏎validation on a representative sample of their own patient population. HHS should:⏎● Develop and publish standardized validation protocols for common clinical AI use cases⏎(sepsis prediction, imaging analysis, medication management, etc.) that providers can⏎apply without needing to design evaluations from scratch.⏎● Require, as a condition of use in CMS-funded programs, that Tier 1 AI tools undergo⏎prospective shadow mode validation — in which the AI generates recommendations that⏎are tracked but not acted upon — for a defined period before live deployment, with pre-⏎specified performance thresholds as go/no-go criteria.⏎● Fund, through AHRQ grants, independent evaluation support for FQHCs, rural health⏎clinics and independent clinician groups, and safety-net providers that lack internal⏎validation capacity.⏎5.3 Post-Deployment Monitoring⏎Post-deployment monitoring is the most underinvested phase of AI governance. HHS should⏎encourage that providers deploying Tier 1 and Tier 2 AI tools maintain and periodically review:⏎⏎● Model performance dashboards tracking accuracy, alert acceptance rates, clinician⏎override rates, and demographic performance disaggregation on an ongoing basis.⏎● Structured incident reporting processes for suspected AI-related clinical errors, with a⏎defined escalation pathway to the AI developer and, for serious adverse events, to HHS.⏎This reporting regime should be modeled after the Patient Safety Organization (PSO)⏎program established under the Patient Safety and Quality Improvement Act of 2005 and⏎administered by AHRQ, which provides a proven federal framework for the voluntary⏎collection and analysis of patient safety event data reported by healthcare providers,⏎with legal protections for reported information that encourage candid disclosure. Under⏎that model, AHRQ certifies and lists PSOs that receive provider-reported safety data,⏎aggregate it through standardized Common Formats, and contribute non-identifiable⏎data to the Network of Patient Safety Databases (NPSD) for systemic learning. HHS⏎should direct AHRQ to extend the PSO program's Common Formats and reporting⏎infrastructure to expressly capture AI-related adverse events and near-misses as a⏎discrete event category, enabling health systems to report suspected AI-related clinical⏎errors through existing PSO relationships with the same legal protections that apply to⏎other patient safety work product — while ensuring that serious adverse events trigger a⏎defined escalation pathway to the AI developer and to HHS for regulatory follow-up. This⏎approach leverages existing infrastructure rather than creating a parallel reporting⏎bureaucracy, and it connects AI safety surveillance to the broader patient safety learning⏎system in a way that can generate actionable evidence at scale.⏎● Annual re-validation against updated local data to detect model drift.⏎VI. Patient Safety and Equity (Questions 3, 9)⏎We want to be direct: AI governance that does not explicitly center on patient safety and health⏎equity is incomplete governance. The populations most likely to be harmed by poorly governed⏎AI - patients from racial and ethnic minority communities, patients with disabilities, patients with⏎limited English proficiency, patients in underserved rural and urban settings — are also the⏎populations least likely to have advocates within health systems' AI procurement and⏎governance processes.⏎6.1 Mandatory Equity Evaluation as a Governance Standard⏎Patient and consumer-facing AI tools present a compounded equity risk. These tools are⏎increasingly marketed to and used by patients managing chronic conditions, navigating care⏎access, or seeking health information — populations that disproportionately include individuals⏎with limited health literacy, limited English proficiency, and limited access to professional clinical⏎guidance to contextualize AI-generated recommendations. HHS equity standards should⏎explicitly apply to direct-to-consumer clinical AI and to tools offered by providers through patient-⏎facing channels, with particular attention to whether these tools have been validated for the⏎literacy levels, languages, and access contexts of the populations likely to use them.⏎⏎HHS should establish, as a federal standard applicable to all AI tools used in Tier 1 and Tier 2⏎clinical decisions in healthcare providers in receipt of federal funds for provision of designated⏎health services" that are overseen by the FDA as SaMD, by ASTP/ONC as certified products to⏎170.315(b)(11), or as are provided by AI developers participating in CMS's Health Tech⏎Ecosystem, a mandatory equity evaluation requirement that includes:⏎● Pre-deployment demographic performance disaggregation: vendors must provide⏎performance data broken down by race, ethnicity, sex, age, primary language, and⏎insurance status. Tools that cannot provide this data should not be deployed in federally⏎funded settings until they can.⏎● Local equity audit: health systems must evaluate whether AI tool performance in their⏎own patient population reflects the performance claimed by the vendor, with particular⏎attention to any disparities for demographic subgroups overrepresented in their patient⏎panels.⏎● Ongoing disparate impact monitoring: post-deployment monitoring must include⏎demographic disaggregation, with a defined threshold for performance disparity that⏎triggers mandatory review and corrective action.⏎This is not a burdensome standard — it is a baseline that any responsible developer should⏎already be meeting. Making it mandatory ensures that the market cannot compete on the basis⏎of ignoring equity.⏎6.2 Structural Inclusion of Patient and Community Voices⏎HHS AI governance processes — both at the federal policy level and in the local governance⏎frameworks that health systems establish — currently lack meaningful patient and community⏎representation. This is a structural deficit that leads to governance frameworks designed⏎primarily by and for the interests of developers and deploying institutions.⏎We recommend:⏎● HHS establish a permanent Patient and Caregiver AI Advisory Council with a formal⏎advisory role to ASTP/ONC, FDA's Digital Health Center of Excellence, and CMS's⏎Innovation Center. Council membership should reflect the diversity of the patient⏎population, with particular representation for communities historically underserved by⏎health care.⏎● ASTP/ONC incorporate community representation requirements into the AI governance⏎standards it develops for certified health systems — analogous to community benefit⏎requirements for nonprofit hospitals — recognizing that communities affected by AI⏎deployment have a legitimate interest in how those tools are governed. These⏎community representation requirements should extend beyond the health systems that⏎deploy AI tools to the developers who create them. HHS should require that any AI⏎developer seeking or maintaining recognition as a participating vendor or developer⏎within CMS's Health IT Ecosystem demonstrate meaningful engagement with the⏎⏎communities their tools are designed to serve — including communities defined by race,⏎ethnicity, primary language, disability status, and geography — as a condition of that⏎participation. This is particularly important for developers whose tools are deployed in⏎safety-net, rural, or underserved settings, where the gap between the populations⏎represented in model development and the populations affected by model deployment is⏎most likely to be consequential. Community engagement in this context does not require⏎a prescribed structure, but should at minimum include documented processes for⏎incorporating community input into tool design, validation, and ongoing governance —⏎with HHS establishing minimum standards for what constitutes meaningful engagement⏎as distinct from nominal compliance.⏎6.3 Equitable Access to AI Governance Capacity⏎The equity problem in clinical AI is not only about whether AI tools perform equitably for diverse⏎patients. It is also about whether the organizations that serve those patients have the capacity⏎to govern AI responsibly. Safety-net providers, FQHCs, rural health clinics, and Critical Access⏎Hospitals disproportionately serve vulnerable populations and are disproportionately under-⏎resourced for AI governance.⏎HHS should address this through:⏎● Dedicated AHRQ and HRSA funding for AI governance capacity-building at FQHCs,⏎rural health clinics, and safety-net providers — including training, shared evaluation⏎resources, and access to standardized procurement tools.⏎● A shared services model, potentially administered through regional health information⏎exchanges (HIEs) or ASTP/ONC's Trusted Exchange Framework and Common⏎Agreement (TEFCA) infrastructure, through which smaller organizations can access AI⏎governance and validation support without bearing the full cost independently.⏎● CMS reimbursement consideration for AI governance activities — including model⏎validation, equity auditing, and post-deployment monitoring — as legitimate quality⏎improvement expenditures under existing value-based care frameworks.⏎VII. Interoperability for Governance (Question 8)⏎From a governance perspective, interoperability is not only a technical enabler for AI — it is also⏎the foundation for meaningful AI accountability. Tools that operate on fragmented, inaccessible,⏎or non-standardized data are impossible to audit, validate, or monitor effectively.⏎HHS should prioritize the following interoperability investments to support AI governance:⏎● Standardized FHIR-based AI model cards: HL7 should develop and ONC should adopt⏎as federally recognized interoperability standards FHIR implementation guides for AI⏎model documentation — standardized, machine-readable disclosures of model training⏎data, validation evidence, performance metrics, and intended use — that can be⏎embedded in health system AI registries and queried by governance tools.⏎⏎● Demographic data standardization: The ability to conduct meaningful equity audits⏎depends on the availability of standardized, structured race, ethnicity, language, and⏎disability data in EHR systems. HHS should strengthen requirements for collection and⏎exchange of this data under ONC certification criteria and ensure USCDI includes SDOH⏎and demographic variables sufficient for equity auditing.⏎● Federated audit infrastructure: ASTP/ONC, AHRQ and CMS should invest in the⏎development of federated audit tools that allow regulators, researchers, and⏎accreditation bodies to evaluate AI tool performance across health systems without⏎requiring raw patient data to leave institutional control. This is technically feasible and⏎would dramatically improve post-market surveillance capacity.⏎VIII. Supporting Private Sector Governance Activities⏎(Question 5)⏎Private sector governance activities - including accreditation, certification, and industry-driven⏎standards - have an important role to play, but HHS should be clear-eyed about their limitations.⏎Voluntary industry standards, unmoored from regulatory requirements, tend toward the least-⏎common-denominator. For high-risk clinical AI, self-regulation is insufficient.⏎We recommend a complementary model:⏎● HHS-anchored minimum standards: ASTP/ONC should establish minimum governance⏎standards for Tier 1 and Tier 2 clinical AI as certification criteria under 45 C.F.R.⏎§170.315. These represent the regulatory floor.⏎● Accreditation as a market signal above the floor: HHS should recognize and support⏎independent accreditation programs — from bodies such as the Joint Commission,⏎NCQA, or a new AI-specific accreditation body — that evaluate health systems' AI⏎governance practices against standards above the regulatory minimum. Accreditation⏎status should be visible in CMS quality reporting and usable by payers in network⏎contracting decisions. Beyond its role as a market signal, HHS should give serious⏎consideration to making demonstrated AI governance accreditation a Condition of⏎Participation (CoP) under 42 C.F.R. Parts 482 and 485 for hospitals and health systems⏎that deploy Tier 1 or Tier 2 clinical AI tools in their care delivery operations. Establishing⏎accreditation or equivalent governance standards as a CoP would transform AI⏎governance from a voluntary best practice into a baseline accountability requirement for⏎participation in Medicare and Medicaid — giving it the same structural weight that⏎existing CoPs carry for infection control, patient rights, and quality assurance. HHS⏎should initiate rulemaking to explore this pathway, with particular attention to how⏎accreditation standards could be designed to be achievable by smaller and safety-net⏎health systems without creating compliance burdens that effectively disadvantage the⏎organizations that serve the most vulnerable patient populations. In the interim, prior to⏎any CoP rulemaking, accreditation status should function as a meaningful market signal⏎⏎through its visibility in CMS quality reporting and its usability by payers in network⏎contracting and value-based care arrangement decisions.⏎● Developer certification programs: ASTP/ONC or a designated third party should operate⏎a voluntary AI developer certification program that evaluates clinical AI tools against⏎published transparency and equity standards. Certification should not be required as a⏎standalone matter but should confer meaningful and concrete market obligations and⏎advantages. Specifically, certification — or demonstrated compliance with equivalent⏎transparency and equity standards — should be established as a condition of⏎participation in CMS's Health IT Ecosystem as a recognized AI vendor or developer,⏎ensuring that the federal market is not accessible to developers who have not met⏎minimum governance standards. Beyond ecosystem participation, certification should be⏎tied to the use of health information technology in CMS reimbursement pathways that⏎already carry HIT requirements — including CMS Innovation Center models that require⏎the use of Certified Electronic Health Record Technology (CEHRT) as a condition of⏎participation, and CMS Advanced Alternative Payment Models (Advanced APMs) under⏎the Quality Payment Program — such that AI tools used by providers operating under⏎these models must meet the same certification or equivalent standards as a condition of⏎their use in those payment arrangements. This approach gives certification genuine⏎regulatory consequence rather than treating it as a purely voluntary market signal. This⏎certification program should explicitly extend to developers of patient and consumer-⏎facing clinical AI tools, including those distributed through app marketplaces or offered⏎directly to consumers, recognizing that these developers are a material part of CMS's⏎Health IT Ecosystem and that the absence of a clinical intermediary makes governance⏎standards more — not less — important for protecting consumers. Preferred status in⏎federal procurement should apply to certified developers across all of these categories.⏎IX. Research and Development Priorities (Question 10)⏎We recommend HHS prioritize the following research investments through NIH, AHRQ, and⏎ASTP/ONC:⏎● Governance science: Rigorous research on what AI governance frameworks and⏎practices actually work — what evaluation methods predict real-world safety, which⏎organizational governance structures improve outcomes, and how to design⏎accountability mechanisms that do not inadvertently impede beneficial adoption. This⏎field is nascent, and federal investment would have high leverage.⏎● Equity research: Studies specifically examining AI performance disparities across⏎demographic subgroups, the mechanisms by which bias enters clinical AI systems, and⏎the effectiveness of bias mitigation techniques in real-world clinical settings. NIH should⏎create a dedicated funding mechanism for clinical AI equity research.⏎● Implementation science: Research on the organizational, workforce, and cultural factors⏎that determine whether clinically effective and responsibly governed AI is actually⏎adopted and used appropriately in practice — with particular focus on community health⏎settings.⏎⏎● Longitudinal outcomes research: Long-term studies evaluating the population health⏎effects of widespread clinical AI adoption, including effects on health equity, total cost of⏎care, and the patient-clinician relationship. Without this evidence base, the field cannot⏎make credible claims about AI's societal value.⏎X. Conclusion⏎AI has genuine and significant potential to improve clinical care. Realizing that potential requires⏎governance frameworks that are rigorous enough to protect patients — particularly the most⏎vulnerable — while predictable enough to support responsible investment and innovation.⏎The United States does not currently have such a framework. The choice facing HHS is not⏎between acceleration and safety: it is between building the governance infrastructure now, in a⏎deliberate and coherent way, or encountering it reactively, after patient harm has eroded public⏎trust in ways that would be far more damaging to AI adoption in the long run.⏎We urge HHS to invest in the governance foundation that durable AI adoption requires: a⏎unified, tiered oversight framework; mandatory transparency and equity standards; meaningful⏎patient voice; and post-market surveillance with teeth. These are not obstacles to innovation.⏎They are the conditions under which innovation can earn and sustain public trust.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to submit these comments and welcome further dialogue with⏎HHS as it develops its approach.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0165♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from PeriGen, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔PeriGen♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment on HHS Request for Information: Accelerating⏎AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment on this request for information regarding⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. Our⏎organization has extensive experience deploying AI-driven decision-support tools in⏎obstetrics, which informs our perspective on the opportunities and challenges of⏎integrating AI into clinical workflows.⏎Background⏎We are responding specifically to HHS’s request for input on regulatory frameworks,⏎reimbursement structures, and research priorities that can enable safe, effective, and⏎equitable AI adoption in clinical care.⏎PeriGen is a perinatal technology company that uses supervised machine learning to⏎provide clinicians with early‑warning notifications of concerning trends in both maternal⏎and fetal clinical status. PeriGen products are FDA cleared and utilized in 22% of American⏎hospital and health systems that provide childbirth services. In 2025, our AI obstetric⏎software provided technology support for 800,000 births. Our comments focus on maternal⏎and neonatal health, a high-acuity area where AI can significantly improve outcomes.⏎Analysis⏎Artificial intelligence offers significant potential to enhance patient safety, reduce clinician⏎burden, and lower healthcare costs. In obstetrics, for example, predictive analytics can⏎identify early warning signs of maternal and fetal complications, enabling earlier⏎intervention and reducing the incidence of preventable adverse events. These events⏎impose substantial financial, emotional, and reputational costs on patients, families,⏎communities, and healthcare providers alike. However, several barriers impede adoption:⏎• Regulatory Uncertainty: Current frameworks for non-device software lack clarity,⏎which can create delays and discourage innovation.⏎• Payment Misalignment: Fee-for-service models rarely incentivize preventive⏎technologies. AI tools that reduce complications and improve efficiency should be⏎recognized within value-based care programs.⏎• Data Interoperability: Fragmented systems and inconsistent standards limit AI’s⏎effectiveness. Enhanced interoperability and pathways for intentional workflow⏎integration would widen market opportunities and accelerate innovation.⏎These barriers risk slowing progress toward HHS’s stated goals of improving outcomes and⏎reducing costs. Additionally, without clear guidance, smaller providers and rural hospitals⏎may struggle to implement AI solutions, exacerbating health disparities.⏎www.PeriGen.com | 100 Regency Forest Drive Suite 200 Cary, NC 27518 | Info@PeriGen.com | 984.208.4250⏎⏎Recommendations⏎To address these challenges, we recommend that HHS:⏎1. Modernize Regulatory Pathways: Establish clear, proportionate guidelines for AI-⏎enabled clinical tools, including non-device software, while maintaining patient⏎safety and trust.⏎2. Align Reimbursement with Outcomes: Create payment policies that incentivize⏎technologies proven to reduce complications and improve efficiency.⏎3. Invest in Interoperability and Research: Support initiatives that enhance data⏎liquidity and fund applied research to validate AI’s impact on clinical outcomes,⏎particularly in complex care scenarios such as obstetrics.⏎4. Prioritize Equity: Ensure implementation strategies include support for resource-⏎limited settings to prevent widening disparities.⏎Conclusion⏎AI can be a powerful tool to improve maternal and neonatal outcomes, reduce costs, and⏎enhance provider efficiency. By modernizing regulations, aligning reimbursement, and⏎investing in interoperability and research, HHS can accelerate safe and equitable adoption⏎of AI in clinical care.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide input,⏎Matthew Sappern⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎PeriGen⏎www.PeriGen.com | 100 Regency Forest Drive Suite 200 Cary, NC 27518 | Info@PeriGen.com | 984.208.4250♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0166♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Society of Radiologic Technologists, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Society of Radiologic Technologists♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎ATTN: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎February 23, 2026⏎Submitted electronically: http://www.regulations.gov⏎Subject docket ID: (HHS-ONC-2026-0001; 90 FR 60108) HHS Health Sector RFI—Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy⏎The American Society of Radiologic Technologists represents more than 155,000 medical imaging technologists⏎and radiation therapists across the nation. Our mission as the premier professional association for the medical⏎imaging and radiation therapy community is to advance and elevate the medical imaging and radiation therapy⏎profession and enhance quality, safe patient care. With that mission in mind, ASRT appreciates the opportunity⏎to comment on the ASTP's request for information “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care.⏎Artificial Intelligence has the potential to elevate patient outcomes, expand access to care, and improve⏎efficiency. However, such implementation, especially within medical imaging and radiation therapy, must⏎prioritize patient safety through clear, strong national governance.⏎AI can be a tool that augments clinical expertise but cannot be a substitute for judgement and experience of⏎healthcare providers. Federal policy should ensure that AI deployment strengthens person-centered care while⏎creating pathways for responsible adoption.⏎HHS must issue clear national guidance on AI governance and accountability within health systems, particularly⏎for post-deployment use. Today, both clinicians and institutions face uncertainty about expectations for⏎oversight, documentation, monitoring, and responsibility allocation, especially in multi-vendor environments⏎where AI tools interact with EHRs, imaging systems, treatment planning systems, and delivery devices. This⏎uncertainty slows adoption and increases risk. HHS can play a critical role by articulating baseline governance⏎expectations applicable to both FDA-regulated AI devices and non-device clinical AI. Such guidance must affirm⏎that all final decision-making remains with the appropriate licensed physician, and that AI technologies cannot⏎override the medical imaging and radiation therapy professionals’ expertise in accordance with the physician’s⏎orders and clinical indications. Expectations for transparent logging of AI recommendations, continuous⏎performance monitoring, version and update management, and incident reporting must also be clearly⏎established.⏎In conclusion, HHS must prioritize the critical safeguards that protect patients from inappropriate and dangerous⏎care. Through strong, clear national governance on the reasonable adoption of AI, HHS will be able to balance⏎the evolving nature of new technologies with a mandate to implement federal policy that prioritizes patient care⏎⏎and access. Any adoption of artificial intelligence in clinical settings must be utilized as a tool within workflow⏎and cannot be implemented as a replacement for clinical education and expertise.⏎ASRT would be honored to serve as a resource for questions related to AI in medical imaging and radiation⏎therapy and look forward to working with HHS as implementation frameworks are developed.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide comments. Please do not hesitate to reach out me at⏎jbradley@asrt.org if you have any questions.⏎Jason J. Bradley⏎Executive Vice President, Governance & Public Policy⏎American Society of Radiologic Technologists♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0167♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Health IT End-Users Alliance, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Health IT End-Users Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Jim O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎US Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary and National Coordinator⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎330 C Street NW⏎Floor 7, Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically to www.regulations.gov⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Dr. Keane:⏎The Health IT End-Users Alliance (the Alliance) appreciates the opportunity to provide input on the Office of⏎the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care, or HHS Health Sector AI RFI, as published in the December⏎23, 2025, Federal Register (RIN 0955-AA13).⏎The Alliance brings together health information professionals, physicians, hospitals, and other front-line⏎healthcare providers and organizations that use health information technology (IT) to ensure that policy⏎and standards development activities reflect the complex web of clinical and operational challenges facing⏎those who use such technologies today. By working collaboratively, the Alliance is focused on priorities for⏎how technology can best support clinical care and operations.⏎The Alliance appreciates HHS’ interest in creating a policy and regulatory environment that is optimal to⏎realizing the benefits of AI. AI is rapidly transforming healthcare, with developments and innovations⏎producing promising non-clinical and clinical benefits in various settings and specialties. Concerns remain⏎related to how AI models are developed, trained, used, and monitored, and the significant impact AI has on⏎healthcare operations, patient care delivery, and health outcomes. Due to those concerns, widespread⏎⏎adoption of AI in healthcare requires thoughtful oversight and governance frameworks to minimize risks⏎and ensure the appropriate, safe, and ethical use of AI. Health IT end-users are at the forefront of AI use in⏎healthcare and are well-equipped to collaborate on common principles to ensure the proper balance⏎between innovation and use of AI with appropriate guardrails.⏎The Alliance published a consensus statement in April 2025 reflecting the current state of AI in healthcare⏎including principles intended to guide policymakers as they work to ensure appropriate oversight of AI tools⏎without hampering innovation. As end-users are often brought into the development cycle for technology⏎during implementation, the Alliance’s consensus statement highlights the need for end-users to be⏎engaged throughout the entire AI development lifecycle. The Alliance recommends HHS prioritize⏎policymaking activities that bring developers, policymakers, and end-users to the table from a⏎project’s outset to ensure AI tools are built in ways that fit organizations’ unique needs, integrate with⏎existing workflows, promote trust and confidence in the use of such tools, and inform organization-⏎specific governance frameworks for end-users.⏎As HHS pursues policies and regulations to determine the appropriate methods and areas for governance⏎and oversight of AI in clinical care, the Alliance urges HHS to consider implementing policies that assist the⏎industry in fostering innovation in AI while maintaining the protection of health data, lowering⏎administrative burden, and improving health outcomes. Recommendations on specific areas of policy and⏎regulatory oversight include:⏎Regulation and oversight of AI. AI tools should augment, not replace, end-users’ expertise, and policy⏎must ensure these tools supplement cognitive and administrative tasks while preserving human judgment.⏎AI tools in healthcare require a risk-based approach to oversight where the level of scrutiny and validation⏎should be proportionally accounted for in policymaking to minimize the disparate harm and consequences⏎the tool might introduce. The Alliance urges HHS to leverage ASTP/ONC’s statutory coordinating role and its⏎component agencies, including the Office for Civil Rights (OCR), Centers for Medicare and Medicaid⏎Services (CMS), and other relevant agencies to ensure policies are aligned across the federal government⏎to avoid competing and confusing standards that could lead to non-compliance and increased burden.⏎This includes ensuring HIPAA regulatory frameworks incorporate data handling practices by AI tools to⏎protect patient privacy and prohibit data/information exchange beyond minimum necessary, secondary⏎use of data without consent, or data handling practices that may enable unintended or unauthorized data⏎reidentification.⏎Safety and transparency. Transparency in the development of AI tools and what data are used in decision-⏎making, governance, and ongoing testing and maintenance plans is critical. AI developers should provide⏎clear, understandable information describing how the AI solution makes predictions, tailored in a way to⏎best suit the needs of end-users. Such transparency requirements should provide a conceptual model on⏎the importance of data used for AI tool inferences and how data is used. That transparency includes⏎ensuring patients and healthcare organizations can confidently trust companies will maintain⏎⏎confidentiality of data. For trust to be maintained, AI developers should disclose how data from patients⏎and end-users is collected, stored, used, and shared.⏎Liability. The Alliance encourages HHS to ensure AI companies dealing with health data are held to the⏎same standards HIPAA-covered entities are held to today. HHS should determine the appropriate balance⏎of accountability that considers the role of AI developers in the creation, maintenance, and use of clinical⏎and non-clinical tools and the role of end-users in the use of these tools. HHS should also partner with the⏎Federal Trade Commission to ensure AI developer oversight includes unfair and deceptive trade practices.⏎Providers should not be held liable for an AI tool’s performance if they have completed good faith⏎evaluations and taken steps to mitigate quality or safety concerns. Policies must reflect that developers are⏎often best positioned to prevent harm due to their knowledge of the development, function, and intended⏎use of these tools.⏎Cybersecurity. As AI systems become deeply integrated into clinical and operational environments, their⏎exposure to sophisticated cyber threats presents an escalating risk to patient safety and organizational⏎integrity. End-users depend on AI tools that are secure by design, yet they often lack visibility into the⏎system’s vulnerabilities or control over its defenses. AI developers, not end-users, are best positioned to⏎mitigate and respond to cyberattacks given their direct access to system architecture, code, and⏎infrastructure. The Alliance recommends HHS strengthen regulatory guardrails to ensure that developers⏎bear primary responsibility for securing AI systems against intrusions, data exfiltration, and model⏎manipulation. Holding end-users liable for breaches or system failures they cannot control would⏎undermine trust and deter adoption. Robust, enforceable cybersecurity requirements for developers,⏎aligned with federal health data protections, are essential to protect patients, sustain trust in AI, and⏎accelerate responsible innovation in healthcare.⏎Real-world testing. Consistent with the Alliance’s Real-world Testing Consensus Statement, it is critical to⏎conduct real-world testing of AI tools across a variety of clinical settings to confirm these technologies are⏎operating as expected without adverse patient consequences. Real-world testing and documentation of⏎identified issues, including implicit biases, are of critical importance to end-users to advance AI adoption⏎in healthcare. End-users are often included at the end of the policymaking and technology development⏎process, not the beginning, which leads to technologies and policies that do not meet their intended goals.⏎It is crucial for HHS to prioritize policies that include end-users throughout the policymaking and⏎technology development processes to ensure regulatory and technology innovation goals, as well as the⏎needs of end-users, are met.⏎The Alliance is actively working with the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) on its AI⏎Standards Zero Drafts Pilot Project, an initiative seeking to broaden stakeholder participation in the⏎creation of AI standards. We applaud NIST’s commitment to working with stakeholders on this effort to⏎implement directives within the Trump Administration’s AI Action Plan. Thus far, the Alliance has provided⏎feedback on the four initial topic areas identified by NIST for draft standards documents and the first⏎⏎outline on testing, evaluation, verification, and validation. We have also held a listening session with NIST⏎to provide more detailed feedback. We encourage HHS to coordinate with NIST to advance nationwide AI⏎standards and ensure any AI policy frameworks are harmonized with such standards.⏎The Health IT End-Users Alliance thanks HHS for the opportunity to provide input on this request for⏎information. We are committed to being a partner in identifying unnecessary barriers to AI innovation,⏎development, and implementation while ensuring patient safety and improving the healthcare experience⏎for all stakeholders involved. To discuss our feedback further or if you have any questions, please contact⏎Tara O’Donnell, Manager, Regulatory Affairs at Tara.ODonnell@ahima.org.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0168♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from National Multiple Sclerosis Society, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔National Multiple Sclerosis Society♔♔♔♔♔♔February 4, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Ave., SW⏎Washington, DC 20001⏎Comments submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The National Multiple Sclerosis Society (Society) thanks the U.S. Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) for seeking feedback on how current HHS regulations affect the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. As medical technology advances, the Society applauds HHS for⏎working to ensure that the use of AI in clinical care, with proper guardrails, will allow all Americans to⏎live their best lives.⏎Multiple Sclerosis (MS) is an unpredictable disease of the central nervous system. Currently, there is no⏎cure. Symptoms vary from person to person and may include disabling fatigue, mobility challenges,⏎cognitive changes, and vision issues. An estimated 1 million people live with MS in the United States.⏎Early diagnosis and treatment are critical to minimize disability. Significant progress is being made to⏎achieve a world free of MS.⏎The Society, founded in 1946, is the global leader of a growing movement dedicated to creating a world⏎free of MS. The Society provides global leadership and funds research for a cure, drives change through⏎advocacy, and provides programs and services to help people affected by MS live their best lives. To⏎fulfill this mission, we fund cutting-edge research, drive change through advocacy, facilitate professional⏎education, collaborate with MS organizations around the world, and provide services designed to help⏎people affected by MS move their lives forward. Additionally, the Society sees itself as a partner to the⏎government in many critical areas. While we advocate for the government’s involvement in accelerating⏎the discovery, development, and delivery of new treatments, we do it as an organization whose⏎research investment has exceeded $1.2 billion to date.⏎The Society acknowledges that HHS is one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems. We⏎applaud HHS for seeking input on ways it may invest in research and development to integrate AI into⏎care delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the health and well-being of⏎all Americans. We agree that AI will continue to transform and improve the healthcare landscape, if it is⏎used in the appropriate setting, there is transparency about why it is being utilized, the goals of using AI⏎are clearly outlined for all stakeholders, and the needs of patients and patient representatives are⏎considered in the process. It is important to assess whether the use of AI introduces specific risks and⏎harms, such as algorithms that could amplify errors and preexisting biases in the source data.⏎⏎The Society focuses our response to this request for information (RFI) on specific question #9 - What⏎challenges within healthcare do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption and use⏎of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and⏎use of AI in clinical care? Our response, outlined below, focuses on the following themes: patient-⏎involved research and development of AI technologies, transparency and communication in AI⏎development, and ensuring health equity in AI use.⏎AI has the potential to significantly improve MS clinical care by enhancing how clinical data are⏎integrated, analyzed, and translated into actionable insights. AI can also support safety, effectiveness,⏎and quality evaluations by integrating diverse data sources—such as genetic databases, clinical studies,⏎and real-world evidence—to improve understanding of disease heterogeneity, progression, and⏎treatment response. Applying AI to MS clinical care could lead to more personalized and effective⏎treatment regimens, optimizing outcomes while minimizing unnecessary side effects.⏎However, the reliability and equity of AI tools hinge on the quality and representativeness of the data⏎used to train these models. This underscores the critical importance of engaging individuals with lived⏎experience of MS, especially from underrepresented communities, throughout the model development⏎process. Their input ensures that AI tools are not only scientifically rigorous but also socially and⏎ethically aligned with the diverse needs of people living with MS, thereby strengthening both the⏎credibility and applicability of resulting innovations.⏎Patient-Involved Research and Development of AI Technologies⏎Individuals living with chronic conditions are the experts on their disease. These individuals, along with⏎their carepartners and loved ones, hold a wealth of information that can inform the design of novel⏎therapeutics and emerging technologies to support care delivery. The Society urges HHS and AI⏎developers to meet with patients and their carepartners early and often throughout the development of⏎AI technologies. Capturing the lived experiences of a diverse group of individuals living with chronic⏎conditions, including MS, can ensure that the technologies developed are meaningful to patients and⏎reflect their needs and concerns. This patient-involved research is essential for designing technologies⏎that will benefit all patients and make them feel comfortable using them. The Society urges HHS to⏎engage with the patient community by facilitating focus groups and patient-focused product⏎development meetings. These meetings will not only allow for AI developers to gather input from those⏎with lived experience but also build trust in the products as they were developed with the patient⏎community.⏎Transparency and Communication in AI Development⏎The Society believes that for the adoption of AI to be successful, HHS and AI developers must be⏎transparent and communicate to the patient community the goal of utilizing AI in clinical care, the scope⏎of what these technologies can and cannot do, how the technology was developed, and how an⏎individual’s data is shared and secured. This information should be available on HHS’s website.⏎We encourage HHS to work with community and patient groups so that educational efforts come from⏎trusted sources. Additionally, the development of educational materials should consider how target⏎audiences best receive information, include meaningful messages, and identify who is best suited to⏎deliver the information. Focused offerings from peers in both formal and informal settings may help gain⏎Page 2 of 4⏎⏎trust in these technologies. Below, the Society offers suggestions on how best to engage with the⏎patient community:⏎• Develop a range of educational offerings to reflect different ways people learn and receive⏎information.⏎• Ensure all materials reflect health literacy principles and are written in plain language.⏎• Create educational materials that are multilingual, reflect cultural differences, include social⏎determinants of health resources, and promote shared decision-making. Educational materials⏎should include the current considerations and best practices of MS treatment so people can⏎make informed decisions.⏎• Increase connections to diverse communities and use trusted sources, ambassadors,⏎carepartners, and peers.⏎We advise HHS to consider a series of stakeholder feedback opportunities to allow the Department to⏎share its plan for the strategic use of AI in clinical care and to gather stakeholder feedback to inform the⏎process. Additionally, we recommend that HHS work cross-functionally with experts in academia and⏎the patient community to routinely examine the algorithms and data that AI uses to ensure they do not⏎cause harm or miss signals important to both HHS and patients.⏎In addition to providing transparent communications to all relevant stakeholders in accessible and⏎stakeholder-specific language, the Society urges HHS to regularly engage with other government⏎agencies as well as throughout the federal government, to ensure there is consistency with how AI is⏎being used across the federal health and research agencies (e.g., FDA, NIH, CMS, CDC, etc.). Sharing⏎learnings and best practices in AI use will help avoid duplicative efforts and create standards for uniform⏎adoption of these technologies.⏎Ensuring Population Health in the Use of AI⏎Despite the promise of AI in accelerating biomedical innovation, its benefits must be equitably⏎distributed to avoid perpetuating disparities in healthcare. The Society urges HHS to recognize concerns⏎about algorithmic bias, incomplete datasets, and unintended consequences that could⏎disproportionately affect certain patient populations. To address these challenges, the Society⏎advocates for:⏎• The use of representative and high-quality data to build AI models that accurately reflect diverse⏎patient populations.⏎• The establishment of a review process by an advisory panel of individuals with lived experience⏎(e.g., a panel of individuals living with MS).⏎• The establishment of routine audits, validation processes, and accountability measures to detect⏎and mitigate bias.⏎• The integration of population health principles into AI development, ensuring that technological⏎advances do not exacerbate existing care gaps but instead drive meaningful improvements in⏎health outcomes for all patients.⏎By prioritizing transparency, patient empowerment, and innovation in AI deployment, HHS can help⏎foster accountability and build trust while ensuring that emerging technologies contribute to a more⏎inclusive and effective healthcare system. The Society looks forward to continued collaboration with⏎Page 3 of 4⏎⏎HHS and other key stakeholders to shape policies that uphold these principles and advance the well-⏎being of individuals living with MS.⏎Thank you again for your commitment to fostering innovation in medical technology development. As AI⏎continues to become a major influence in healthcare, the Society looks forward to working with you to⏎ensure that the voices of individuals with lived experience are heard in the development and refinement⏎of these technologies. If you have any questions, please contact Nicole Boschi, Director of Regulatory⏎Affairs, at nicole.boschi@nmss.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Steffany Stern⏎Vice President, Advocacy⏎National Multiple Sclerosis Society⏎Page 4 of 4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0169♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Community Oncology Alliance, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Community Oncology Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator⏎for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎On behalf of the Board of Directors of the Community Oncology Alliance (“COA”), we are⏎submitting this comment letter regarding the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (“RFI”).⏎COA is an organization dedicated to advocating for the complex care and access needs of patients⏎with cancer and the community oncology practices that serve them. COA is the only nonprofit⏎organization in the United States dedicated solely to independent community oncology practices,⏎which serve the majority of Americans receiving treatment for cancer. Since its grassroots⏎founding close to 25 years ago, COA’s mission has been to ensure that patients with cancer⏎receive quality, affordable, and accessible care in their own communities where they live and⏎work.⏎COA is committed to providing solutions to the challenges that community oncology patients⏎and physicians face throughout the care journey. We seek to ensure that cancer care is delivered⏎with the utmost quality and in an accessible and affordable manner.⏎Recognizing the role artificial intelligence (“AI”) plays in clinical care and the ongoing⏎momentum around its use, COA has established an AI and Digital Transformation Task Force.⏎Our feedback and recommendations below are informed by the community oncologists and⏎practice leaders who are actively adopting AI within their practices.⏎Background and Summary of Feedback and Recommendations⏎Independent community oncology practices are the backbone of cancer care delivery in the⏎United States, caring for most patients close to home across urban, suburban, rural, and⏎underserved communities. Because this is where the majority of treatment decisions and⏎longitudinal follow-up occur, supporting digital transformation in community settings is essential⏎to achieving national goals for quality, health outcomes, access, and affordability in cancer care.1⏎1 Hughes-Halbert, C. National Cancer Institute. “Bringing Research to the Community to Reduce Cancer Disparities.” 21 September 2017.⏎Available Here.⏎⏎The digital transformation required to integrate AI into clinical care is substantial, necessitating not only financial⏎investment but also significant time and human resources. Implementing AI solutions involves a steep learning curve⏎and the adaptation of existing workflows, which can overwhelm small practices with limited personnel and financial⏎bandwidth. Without proper incentives, the risks may outweigh the potential benefits, making it especially challenging⏎for community practices to engage in this essential evolution.⏎Historically, the introduction of electronic health records (“EHRs”) provides a fitting precedent. Incentives in that⏎case enabled practices to invest in the necessary technology and training while offsetting the initial transition costs.⏎To facilitate the adoption of AI tools, we urge the Department of Health and Human Services (“HHS”) to consider a⏎similar incentive structure. This could include financial reimbursement models for implementing AI applications that⏎drive improved patient outcomes, efficiency, and cost-effectiveness. By promoting access to high-value AI clinical⏎interventions, we can ensure that community practices remain viable and do not face pressures to consolidate into⏎higher-cost hospital service settings.⏎Failing to effectively transition into a digital-first approach could jeopardize the sustainability of smaller practices.⏎As pressures mount from larger health care systems that can leverage advanced technologies to reduce costs and⏎enhance patient care, the risk of community oncology practices falling behind is real. This would ultimately lead to⏎reduced patient choice and increased health care costs for the communities we serve.⏎Our responses focus on questions 1, 2, 6, and 9 in the RFI. Next are our summary responses, followed by more⏎detailed responses.⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎The biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care include⏎financial barriers and resource constraints; uncertainty regarding regulatory expectations, accountability, and legal⏎liability for non-device AI tools; and limited access to interoperable data across care settings.⏎1. Financial Barriers and Resource Constraints⏎Up-front and non-recoverable implementation costs may limit practices’ adoption of AI tools and, once⏎implemented, a lack of reimbursement for AI-enabled clinical or administrative functions places pressure on⏎practices’ long-term and sustained AI use. For practices to fully leverage AI in clinical care, the financial⏎incentives must account for the full range of costs associated with AI use, including additional investment in⏎technical infrastructure and expertise. Even when AI tools are clinically promising, practices may be unable to⏎deploy them safely or effectively without significant external support. This creates a widening gap between large,⏎well-resourced health systems and community-based practices, potentially exacerbating disparities in access to⏎innovation.⏎2. Uncertainty Regarding Regulatory Expectations, Accountability, and Legal Liability⏎For providers, most professional liability policies were not designed with AI-assisted clinical workflows in mind.⏎Providers must also be able to understand the limitations, training data sources, and intended use of AI systems⏎– providers cannot reasonably assess risk or ensure appropriate clinical oversight without transparency.⏎Malpractice risk is another concern, as providers lack clarity on whether the use of AI tools may affect⏎malpractice coverage or exposure, particularly when tools influence documentation, clinical reasoning, or⏎treatment decisions. On the vendor side, many AI vendors offer limited service-level agreements (“SLAs”)⏎2⏎2⏎⏎related to model performance, uptime, data provenance, or error rates. This creates a mismatch between the⏎clinical risk assumed by providers and the contractual protections offered by vendors.⏎3. Limited Access to High-Quality, Interoperable, and Clinically Usable Data⏎Even where interoperability standards exist, such as those promulgated through the Health Data, Technology,⏎and Interoperability rules, access to usable, high-quality data remains a major barrier to effective AI deployment.⏎Clinical data across care settings is often incomplete, inconsistently structured, poorly normalized, and⏎fragmented across multiple systems, vendors, and care sites. Practices face challenges related to data quality⏎and heterogeneity, interoperability gaps, context loss across care settings, and data governance and access⏎restrictions.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could⏎be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎HHS should consider several strategies to incentivize the adoption of AI and support digital transformation in clinical⏎care. We recognize that congressional action may be required to enable HHS to set up these initiatives. However,⏎our hope is that HHS will adopt these initiatives whenever possible. COA recommends a multi-faceted approach that⏎includes:⏎1. Financial Incentives and Infrastructure Development⏎i. Grants and Funding Opportunities: Establishment of grant programs specifically for community⏎practices to fund AI implementation projects and require transparent reporting of safety, performance,⏎and health outcomes impact.⏎ii. Tax Incentives: Working with the Internal Revenue Service (“IRS”), HHS should provide tax incentives⏎for qualifying AI technologies and investments in digital infrastructure.⏎iii. Pilot Programs: Launch a pilot program to incentivize early adopters of AI technologies, including⏎through the Center for Medicare & Medicaid Services (“CMS”) Innovation Center Authority at Social⏎Security Act (“SSA”) § 1115A.⏎2. Training and Partnerships⏎i. Data Sharing Initiatives: Enhanced interoperability standards and government-led initiatives to⏎encourage data sharing among practices and AI systems would make it easier to demonstrate the efficacy⏎of AI tools through shared insights and benchmarks. CMS could also leverage the voluntary CMS⏎Health Technology Ecosystem pledges to further accelerate AI adoption.⏎ii. Recognition Programs: Establish awards or recognition for practices that successfully implement AI-⏎driven solutions, boosting visibility in the community and providing positive reinforcement for⏎innovative efforts. HHS’s Million Hearts Hospitals & Health Systems Recognition Program could be⏎used as a model, and the Health Technology Ecosystem pledges could include an AI-specific pledge.⏎iii. Public-Private Partnerships: Collaborations between government entities and private sector⏎technology developers could create tailored AI solutions for small practices. This could also include⏎shared investment in research and development. Partnerships should be paired with shared⏎implementation playbooks, model contracts, and standardized governance requirements to reduce⏎3⏎3⏎⏎procurement friction and duplication across practices. Elements of CMS’s Advancing Chronic Care⏎with Effective, Scalable Solutions (“ACCESS”) Model and Make America Healthy Again: Enhancing⏎Lifestyle and Evaluating Value-Based Approaches Through Evidence (“MAHA ELEVATE”) Model⏎could be used to better expand access to AI-supported oncology care, as could partnerships with, and⏎voluntary commitments from, private organizations.⏎iv. Training and Education Programs: Develop or subsidize training programs for health care providers⏎to improve competency in using AI tools, with CMS serving as a resource repository for sharing best⏎practices. We also recommend CMS develop a centralized AI initiatives webpage, with all internal and⏎external CMS initiatives related to AI, including HHS-provided training and education programs.⏎3. Regulatory Streamlining⏎HHS should simplify the regulatory process for new AI tools, ensuring faster approval and easier integration⏎into practices. At the same time, health care providers must be able to adopt tools without ambiguity, and there⏎must be consistency, enforcement, and accountability. HHS guidance for both scenarios will accelerate AI⏎adoption.⏎4. Reimbursement⏎i. Performance-Based Reimbursement Models: Adequate, sustained reimbursement is needed to⏎support the longevity of AI adoption in clinical care. Per-member-per-month payments, shared-savings⏎attribution within value-based programs, bonus points towards the Promoting Interoperability⏎performance category, or final score in the Merit-Based Incentive Payment System (“MIPS”) program,⏎would provide a further incentive for investment in AI. Current CMS models, such as the ACCESS⏎Model, could also be leveraged to reward practices for successfully using AI to improve outcomes⏎while allowing for flexibility in the tools used.⏎ii. Ongoing Reimbursement for and Valuation of AI Tools: AI tools must be adequately reimbursed,⏎and reimbursement must keep pace in a rapidly changing environment, with CMS’s reimbursement⏎methodology for AI tools under Medicare, via the Physician Fee Schedule (“PFS”), being particularly⏎impactful.2 As new AI tools are being developed and existing tools continue to be updated, the⏎underlying payment methodology for AI tools may need to be reevaluated more frequently, and⏎methodologies should consider the differences in AI tools compared to many of the services CMS⏎currently reimburses.3 Providers also need updated codes to submit claims for AI tools.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and⏎where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health⏎care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Community oncologists are using AI tools in many ways, including clinical decision-making support and to reduce⏎administrative burden. AI applications also include cancer detection, screening, diagnosis, tumor analysis, biomarker⏎assessment, and drug discovery.4 However, AI pilot programs conducted by community oncology practices have⏎found that: 1) data quality and preparation remain significant barriers; 2) operational lift is frequently underestimated;⏎2 Longyear R and Berenson R. “Artificial Intelligence Payment Policies: Challenges For CMS And The Medicare Physician Fee Schedule.” Health Affairs.⏎January 2026. Available Here.⏎3 Ibid.⏎4 Luchini C, et al. “Artificial intelligence in oncology: current applications and future perspectives.” British Journal of Cancer. 26 November 2021. Available⏎Here.⏎4⏎4⏎⏎3) quality-of-life improvements do not always translate into financial return on investment (“ROI”); 4) voice and⏎patient-facing AI tools remain immature; and 5) revenue cycle applications show promise but face trust barriers.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and⏎use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers generally support the use of technology that improves access, reduces delays, and enhances⏎coordination of care. At the same time, they value transparency, strong privacy protections, and continued human⏎clinical oversight. Policies that reinforce these expectations will be essential to sustaining trust in the use of AI in⏎clinical care. COA also has serious concerns regarding the use of AI in utilization management and the impact on⏎patients. We have long expressed concerns about how PA harms patients, and introducing AI into the process poses⏎even greater risks for patients.5,6 Providers and patients are often not aware that AI is involved in utilization review,⏎with one survey finding that less than a quarter of insurers tell providers when AI is used.7⏎Following are further detailed responses to questions 1,2, 6, and 9.⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎There are several practical barriers that limit the adoption of AI in clinical care, including financial barriers and⏎resource constraints, uncertainty regarding regulatory expectations and accountability for non-device AI tools, and⏎limited access to interoperable data across care settings. Without targeted federal guidance, incentives, and technical⏎assistance, AI adoption risks becoming concentrated in only the largest health systems – undermining broader policy⏎goals of scalable innovation in health care delivery.⏎1. Financial Barriers and Resource Constraints⏎Financial barriers are a significant hurdle to the adoption of AI in clinical care. Up-front and non-recoverable⏎implementation costs may limit practices’ adoption of AI tools, and once implemented, a lack of reimbursement⏎for AI-enabled clinical or administrative functions places pressure on practices’ long-term and sustained AI use.⏎For practices to fully leverage AI in clinical care, the financial incentives must account for the full range of costs⏎associated with AI use.⏎Limited technical infrastructure and expertise at the practice level are critical and often overlooked barriers,⏎particularly within small and midsized community oncology groups. Many practices lack the internal technical⏎capacity needed to implement, validate, and govern AI tools responsibly. Many practices lack dedicated data⏎engineering or informatics staff; AI governance or model evaluation frameworks; infrastructure to monitor⏎performance, bias, or drift over time; resources to conduct vendor due diligence or technical validation; and⏎expertise to integrate AI tools safely into clinical workflows. As a result, even when AI tools are clinically⏎promising, practices may be unable to deploy them safely or effectively without significant external support. This⏎creates a widening gap between large, well-resourced health systems and community-based practices, potentially⏎exacerbating disparities in access to innovation.⏎2. Uncertainty Regarding Regulatory Expectations, Accountability, and Legal Liability⏎5 Community Oncology Alliance. “COA Comments on WISeR Model.” 14 August 2025. Available Here.⏎6 Community Oncology Alliance. “COA Comments on 2027 Medicare Advantage and Part D Proposed Rule.” 23 January 2026. Available Here.⏎7 DeFrain K et al. “Health Insurance Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning Survey Results.” National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC).⏎2025. Available Here.⏎5⏎5⏎⏎Uncertainty regarding regulatory expectations, accountability, and legal liability for non-device AI tools used in⏎clinical workflows is a major barrier to adoption. While the FDA has established regulatory pathways for⏎Software as a Medical Device (“SaMD”), many AI tools currently being deployed in health care – such as clinical⏎decision support, ambient documentation, summarization tools, and workflow automation – fall outside⏎traditional medical device classifications. While the FDA recently released updated Clinical Decision Support⏎Software – Guidance for Industry and FDA Staff, uncertainty remains for providers in several critical areas8:⏎i. Accountability and liability: It remains unclear how legal responsibility is apportioned among clinicians,⏎health systems, and vendors when AI tools contribute to clinical decision-making errors. This uncertainty⏎is particularly concerning given the potential for AI hallucinations, data bias, or incomplete context in large⏎language models.⏎ii. Malpractice risk: Most professional liability policies were not designed with AI-assisted clinical⏎workflows in mind. Providers lack clarity on whether use of AI tools may affect malpractice coverage or⏎exposure, particularly when tools influence documentation, clinical reasoning, or treatment decisions.⏎iii. Vendor accountability and transparency: Many AI vendors offer limited SLAs related to model⏎performance, uptime, data provenance, or error rates. This creates a mismatch between the clinical risk⏎assumed by providers and the contractual protections offered by vendors.⏎iv. Trust and defensibility: Clinicians must understand the limitations, training data sources, and intended⏎use of AI systems. Without transparency into model behavior and performance characteristics, providers⏎cannot reasonably assess risk or ensure appropriate clinical oversight. The potential for bias, hallucinations,⏎and errors underscores the need for transparency about the underlying assumptions of AI tools. Ultimately,⏎if a data set being used in an AI model does not match a patient, it introduces biases and limits the⏎applicability of the tool and findings to the patient.9⏎3. Limited Access to High-Quality, Interoperable, and Clinically Usable Data⏎Limited access to high-quality, interoperable, and clinically usable data across care settings is another barrier to⏎AI adoption. Even where interoperability standards exist, such as those promulgated through the Health Data,⏎Technology, and Interoperability rules, access to usable, high-quality data remains a major barrier to effective AI⏎deployment. Clinical data across care settings is often incomplete, inconsistently structured, poorly normalized,⏎and fragmented across multiple systems, vendors, and care sites. Key challenges include:⏎i. Data quality and heterogeneity: Real-world clinical data frequently contain missing values, inconsistent⏎coding, unstructured text, and variable documentation practices. AI models trained on such data may⏎produce unreliable or biased outputs.⏎ii. Interoperability gaps: While standards such as Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (“FHIR”) have⏎improved data exchange, real-world implementation remains inconsistent. Many data elements critical for⏎oncology care – such as staging, biomarker data, treatment intent, or outcomes – are not reliably structured⏎or exchanged.⏎8 Food & Drug Administration. “Clinical Decision Support Software. Guidance for Industry and Food and Drug Administration Staff.” 29 January 2026.⏎Available Here.⏎9 Patt D. “How AI Is Reshaping Oncology Practice: Insights From ASCO’s AI Task Force.” 12 June 2025. Available Here.⏎6⏎6⏎⏎iii. Context loss across care settings: Cancer care frequently spans health systems, laboratories, imaging⏎centers, and specialty providers. AI tools often lack access to a longitudinal, unified patient record, limiting⏎their clinical utility.⏎iv. Data governance and access restrictions: Privacy regulations, contractual limitations, and institutional risk⏎concerns frequently restrict data sharing, even when legally permissible, further limiting the data available⏎for AI development and validation.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could⏎be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎HHS should consider several strategies to incentivize the adoption of AI and support digital transformation in clinical⏎care. We recommend a multifaceted approach that focuses on financial incentives and infrastructure development,⏎training and partnerships, regulatory streamlining, and reimbursement. We recognize that congressional action may⏎be required to enable HHS to stand up some of these initiatives. However, our hope is that HHS will adopt these⏎initiatives whenever possible.⏎1. Financial Incentives and Infrastructure Development⏎i. Grants and Funding Opportunities: We recommend that HHS establish grant programs specifically for⏎community practices to fund AI implementation projects, including funding non-recoverable start-up costs⏎(integration, data governance, model validation/bias testing, cybersecurity), and require transparent⏎reporting of safety, performance, and health outcomes.⏎ii. Tax Incentives: Working with the IRS, HHS should provide refundable tax credits, deductions, or⏎accelerated depreciation for qualifying AI technologies and investments in digital infrastructure (including⏎cybersecurity and interoperability upgrades), helping lower the financial burden of implementation,⏎particularly for smaller independent practices.⏎iii. Pilot Programs: We support the launch of pilot programs that incentivize early adopters of AI technologies,⏎allowing practices to test AI applications in a controlled environment while providing funding and support.⏎These could be conducted by CMS using its CMS Innovation Center Authority at SSA § 1115A – we provide⏎specific examples in other sections of this letter below.⏎2. Training and Partnerships⏎i. Data Sharing Initiatives: Enhanced interoperability standards and government-led initiatives to encourage⏎data sharing among practices and AI systems would make it easier to demonstrate the efficacy of AI tools⏎through shared insights and benchmarks. This includes alignment with existing interoperability⏎requirements under 45 CFR Part 171.⏎The CMS Health Technology Ecosystem and its call for the health care industry to voluntarily align around⏎a shared framework for data and access is one step towards this initiative. Many companies have pledged⏎to deliver results in the first quarter of this year, and we look forward to seeing how those results positively⏎impact patients. To further accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, CMS could develop a pledge category⏎that recognizes organizations leveraging AI tools and promotes the sharing of best practices across these⏎7⏎7⏎⏎organizations.10 As with the rest of the pledges, CMS could implement this effort without needing to⏎introduce new regulations.⏎ii. Recognition Programs: We recommend HHS establish awards or recognition for practices that⏎successfully implement AI-driven solutions, boosting visibility in the community and providing positive⏎reinforcement for innovative efforts. HHS’s Million Hearts Hospitals & Health Systems Recognition⏎Program may serve as a framework for an AI recognition program that facilitates excellence in AI-⏎supported care and innovation and could include other specialties in addition to oncology.11 The Health⏎Technology Ecosystem pledges we referenced above would also increase visibility into organizations⏎driving the adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎iii. Public-Private Partnerships: Collaborations between government entities and private sector technology⏎developers could create tailored AI solutions for small practices. This could also include shared investment⏎in research and development. Partnerships should be paired with shared implementation playbooks, model⏎contracts, and standardized governance requirements (privacy, accountability, auditability) to reduce⏎procurement friction and duplication across practices.⏎CMS’s ACCESS Model is a step towards connecting beneficiaries with technology-enabled care. As CMS⏎may consider additional tracks or conditions in the future, we hope to see greater opportunities for⏎oncologists and patients receiving cancer treatment. Similarly, we recommend that the MAHA ELEVATE⏎Model’s structure of funding cooperative agreements, with the goal of complementing or augmenting care,⏎be leveraged in the future to better expand access to AI-supported oncology care.⏎The Health Technology Ecosystem voluntary commitments from private companies are another example⏎of CMS leveraging public-private partnerships, and we believe partnerships and voluntary commitments⏎could be expanded to include voluntary financial commitments to drive AI in clinical care. For example,⏎most recently, CMS announced that 10 health technology companies voluntarily pledged to support states⏎in preparing to implement new Medicaid community engagement requirements. Per CMS, the companies⏎will offer states more than $600 million in no-cost and significantly discounted technology products and⏎services.12 In 2019, CMS also led the Artificial Intelligence Health Outcomes Challenge, using its CMS⏎Innovation Center Authority at SSA § 1115A, and partnered with the American Academy of Family⏎Physicians (“AAFP”) and Laura and John Arnold Foundation (now Arnold Ventures) to award up to $1.65⏎million to challenge participants.13⏎iv. Training and Education Programs: HHS should develop or subsidize training programs for health care⏎providers to improve competency in using AI tools. This could include online courses, workshops, and on-⏎site training to ensure practitioners feel confident in adopting new technologies, with HHS serving as a⏎repository for sharing best practices. Role-based training for the different individuals involved in AI⏎implementation (physicians, nurses, pharmacists, navigators, and billing), as well as change-management⏎support, would also be beneficial.⏎As HHS continues to work to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, we recommend that CMS develop an⏎AI initiatives webpage that provides a centralized location for all internal and external CMS initiatives⏎related to AI. While the agency has some webpages that outline the agency’s AI focuses, a central page⏎would streamline the process for patients, providers, and companies to understand where there are⏎10 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Early Adopters and All Pledgees.” Available Here.⏎11 Department of Health and Human Services. “Million Hearts® Hospitals & Health Systems Recognition Program”. Available Here.⏎12 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Fact Sheet: Pledges from Medicaid Technology Companies to Support Community Engagement⏎Implementation and Related Medicaid System Improvements.” 29 January 2026. Available Here.⏎13 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “CMS Artificial Intelligence Health Outcomes Challenge.” 10 June 2019. Available Here.⏎8⏎8⏎⏎opportunities for them to get involved in new initiatives.14,15 This webpage could also contain HHS-⏎provided training and education programs, similar to how the Quality Payment Program (“QPP”) Resource⏎Library functions.16⏎3. Regulatory Streamlining⏎i. Regulatory Streamlining: Simplify the regulatory process for new AI tools, ensuring faster approval and⏎easier integration into practices. Establishing clear guidelines for safe AI use will encourage more providers⏎to adopt these technologies. This also includes clearer expectations for non-device AI governance and⏎HIPAA-aligned privacy protections under 45 CFR Part 164.⏎Health care providers must be able to adopt tools without ambiguity, and there must be consistent and clear⏎enforcement and accountability. HHS intends to establish “guidelines that maximize the potential benefits⏎of use of AI in research and discovery and sustain trustworthiness of tools by minimizing security and⏎privacy risks (e.g., for personal health data).”17 We recommend that HHS guidelines also expand beyond⏎research and discovery, to when these tools are implemented and developers provide ongoing updates.⏎4. Reimbursement⏎i. Performance-Based Reimbursement Models: Adequate, sustained reimbursement is needed to support⏎the longevity of AI adoption in clinical care. CMS has multiple levers to provide reimbursement for AI-⏎supported care. We suggest CMS:⏎• Create reimbursement models that reward practices for successfully integrating AI to improve⏎patient outcomes, aligned with guideline-concordant care, patient satisfaction scores, and⏎operational efficiencies, such as reduced administrative burden and delays in access to care. As⏎we noted above, expanding the ACCESS Model to include oncology-related clinical tracks would⏎provide community oncologists with greater flexibility and choice in implementing technology-⏎supported care, though financial incentives need to align with practices’ costs in expanding their⏎use of AI-supported care. Currently, ACCESS Model tracks are limited to the following chronic⏎conditions: Early-Cardio-Kidney-Metabolic (“eCKM”), Cardio-Kidney-Metabolic (“CKM”),⏎Musculoskeletal (“MSK”), and Behavioral Health (“BH”).18 Initiatives could also be aligned⏎with existing quality and interoperability frameworks.⏎• Reimbursement could include per-member-per-month payments or shared-savings attribution⏎within value-based programs, such as those authorized under SSA §1899 and CMS Innovation⏎Center Authority at SSA § 1115A.⏎• Small practices could be offered bonus points towards the Promoting Interoperability performance⏎category or final score in the MIPS program to provide a further incentive for investment in AI.⏎5. Ongoing Reimbursement for and Valuation of AT Tools: CMS’s reimbursement methodology for AI tools⏎under Medicare, via the PFS, is particularly impactful because other payers, such as commercial payers and⏎Medicaid, often look to Medicare to inform their own reimbursement methodologies.19 Undervaluing AI tools in⏎14 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Technology-Enabled Care and Artificial Intelligence.” Available Here.⏎15 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Artificial Intelligence at CMS.” Available Here.⏎16Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Resource Library.” Available Here.⏎17 Department of Health and Human Services. “Artificial Intelligence (AI) Strategy.” Available Here.⏎18 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “ACCESS Technical Frequently Asked Questions.” Available Here.⏎19 Longyear R and Berenson R. “Artificial Intelligence Payment Policies: Challenges For CMS And The Medicare Physician Fee Schedule”. Health Affairs.⏎January 2026. Available Here.⏎9⏎9⏎⏎the PFS may have a ripple effect on practices’ ability to use AI tools in clinical care, especially when coupled⏎with COA’s longstanding concerns that the PFS does not keep pace with inflation.20⏎AI tools differ from many of the services CMS currently reimburses because they are scalable, do not have⏎physical limitations on use, and automate work.21 AI tools also have different pricing models that may be more⏎difficult to capture when estimating costs – for example, AI tools may use an implementation fee, a subscription-⏎based cost, a license fee at the user level, a per administration fee, or a combination of these fees.22 As new AI⏎tools are being developed and existing tools continue to be updated, the underlying payment methodology for AI⏎tools may need to be reevaluated more frequently.23⏎Alongside reimbursement, providers need the operational tools to submit claims for AI tools. The American⏎Medical Association (“AMA”) has developed new Current Procedural Terminology (“CPT”) codes that include⏎AI (AMA refers to AI as “augmented intelligence”). Ongoing updates to this code set, along with coverage for⏎and recognition of these codes, are necessary to continue accelerating the adoption of AI in clinical care.24⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and⏎where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health⏎care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Tools that provide interactive clinical decision-making support have the potential to improve health care outcomes,⏎provide new insights into quality, and reduce costs. For example, treatment guidelines, such as the National⏎Comprehensive Cancer Network (“NCCN”) Guidelines, are a key resource for evidence-based and consensus-driven⏎cancer care. These guidelines are updated at least annually by independent expert panels and play a critical role in⏎the oncology space because they are often used by payers to determine coverage, and by providers to inform treatment⏎pathways. However, these guidelines are typically published as static PDFs.25⏎More dynamic implementations – such as interactive decision trees or AI-enabled query tools – have the potential to⏎improve access to guideline-based recommendations at the point of care. When thoughtfully designed, such tools⏎could support a more consistent application of evidence-based medicine while reducing cognitive burden on⏎clinicians.⏎AI tools aimed at reducing administrative burden – such as ambient documentation, chart summarization, coding⏎assistance, and patient identification for clinical trials – have shown promise in improving clinician experience and⏎operational efficiency. Several community oncology practices have piloted these tools with encouraging qualitative⏎results.⏎For example, one COA member practice has onboarded about 500 doctors using an AI scribe, which provides chart⏎summarization and ICD-10 coding suggestions. The practice also uses AI to identify patients for clinical trials, who⏎might otherwise be overlooked, through a pilot that assesses patient eligibility and screens them for suitability,⏎ultimately providing more patients with access to clinical trials. Talking to a patient about a clinical trial can take⏎four times as long as talking to a patient about standard of care therapy, so having these AI tools provides an⏎opportunity to increase clinical trial access. Importantly, the AI scribe provides clinical decision support and does⏎not supplant providers’ autonomous decisions.26⏎20 https://mycoa.communityoncology.org/publications/comment-letters/coa-comments-on-cy-2026-pfs⏎21 Longyear R and Berenson R. “Artificial Intelligence Payment Policies: Challenges For CMS And The Medicare Physician Fee Schedule”. Health Affairs.⏎January 2026. Available Here.⏎22 Ibid.⏎23 Ibid.⏎24 O’Reilly K. “288 new CPT codes cover digital health, AI and more.” American Medical Association. 12 September 2025. Available Here.⏎25 Mullangi et al. “AI to Support Modern Cancer Care—The Augmented Oncologist.” JAMA Oncology. 4 September 2025. Available Here.⏎26 Patt D. “How AI Is Reshaping Oncology Practice: Insights From ASCO’s AI Task Force.” 12 June 2025. Available Here.⏎10⏎10⏎⏎These are only a few examples of the many ways in which AI has the potential to support cancer care – applications⏎also include cancer detection, screening, diagnosis, tumor analysis, biomarker assessment, and drug discovery.27⏎Cancer researchers continue to investigate new use cases for AI, expanding the potential for its adoption in clinical⏎care.28⏎That said, most implementations to date remain early in maturity, and widespread evidence demonstrating measurable⏎cost savings or improved outcomes remains limited. Emerging evidence suggests that the financial ROI for these⏎tools remains modest and highly variable, particularly in community-based practice settings.⏎Recent peer-reviewed literature supports this observation. A 2024 study from the University of California, San⏎Francisco, published in JAMA Network Open, evaluated the impact of ambient AI scribes on clinician productivity⏎and billing, finding improvements in documentation and work relative value units (“RVUs”). The study did not find⏎evidence of an increase in billing denials with AI scribe adoption.29 However, the resulting financial gains may be⏎insufficient to generate substantial net ROI to support practice implementation after accounting for licensing,⏎implementation, and operational costs.⏎Similarly, an analysis from Kaiser Permanente published in The New England Journal of Medicine highlighted both⏎the promise and limitations of ambient AI tools, noting that while clinician experience may improve, financial and⏎operational returns remain uncertain and highly context dependent.30⏎These findings align with the experience of many community oncology practices, including COA members, where⏎AI pilots have demonstrated technical feasibility and quality-of-life improvements but have not yet produced⏎consistent or scalable financial returns.⏎Collectively, these experiences suggest that while AI holds significant potential to improve care delivery, most current⏎applications are still in an early stage of development. Real-world implementation challenges observed in community⏎oncology must be addressed to facilitate sustained adoption. Across multiple AI pilot programs conducted by⏎community oncology practices, several consistent themes have emerged:⏎• Data quality and preparation remain significant barriers: AI tools that rely on clinical documents,⏎scanned records, or faxes require extensive human oversight and data normalization. Even well-performing⏎tools often require prolonged quality assurance efforts to achieve acceptable accuracy. In practice, accuracy⏎rates approaching, but not reaching, 100 percent remain common, limiting the extent to which automation⏎can replace manual review.⏎• Operational lift is frequently underestimated: The effort required to vet vendors, negotiate contracts,⏎implement integrations, train staff, monitor performance, and manage ongoing quality control is substantial.⏎These indirect costs are often not reflected in vendor ROI projections.⏎• Quality-of-life improvements do not always translate into financial ROI: Many AI tools deliver value⏎through reduced cognitive burden, improved clinician satisfaction, or streamlined workflows. While⏎meaningful, these benefits do not always translate into measurable cost savings or revenue generation –⏎particularly in fee-for-service or hybrid reimbursement environments.⏎27 Luchini C, et al. “Artificial intelligence in oncology: current applications and future perspectives.” British Journal of Cancer. 26 November 2021. Available⏎Here.⏎28 Pancholi N. “Eye on AI: Applying Artificial Intelligence to Drive Cancer Research, Part 2.” American Association for Cancer Research. 18 August 2025.⏎Available Here.⏎29 Holmgren AJ, et al. “Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes and Physician Financial Productivity.” JAMA Network. 9 January 2026. Available Here.⏎30 Tierney A et al. “Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes to Alleviate the Burden of Clinical Documentation.” 21 February 2024. NEJM Catal Innov Care⏎Deliv. Available Here.⏎11⏎11⏎⏎• Voice and patient-facing AI tools remain immature: Early pilots of conversational or voice-based agents⏎have struggled to meet both patient expectations and clinical workflow needs. Aligning patient intent, clinical⏎appropriateness, and operational efficiency remains challenging, and few implementations have demonstrated⏎sustained value.⏎• Revenue cycle applications show promise but face trust barriers: AI tools aimed at coding, prior⏎authorization (“PA”), or denial management may eventually generate measurable savings; however, most⏎organizations are not yet comfortable reducing staffing levels without prolonged validation periods. As a⏎result, anticipated financial efficiencies have not yet been realized.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and⏎use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers generally support the use of technology that improves access, reduces delays, and enhances⏎coordination of care. At the same time, they value transparency, strong privacy protections, and continued human⏎clinical oversight. Policies that reinforce these expectations will be essential to sustaining trust in the use of AI in⏎clinical care.⏎1. Concerns Regarding AI Use in Utilization Management, Including Prior Authorization⏎AI use in utilization review is of great concern to COA. We have long expressed concerns about how PA places⏎a significant burden on providers and patients.31,32 A survey of physicians found that 94 percent of physicians⏎surveyed stated that PA had a “somewhat or significant negative impact” on patient clinical outcomes, and more⏎than a quarter of physicians reported that PA led to a serious adverse event for a patient in their care. Sixty-one⏎percent of physicians surveyed indicated that they are concerned that AI increases or will increase PA denial⏎rates.33 In turn, denials delay patient access to treatment, which is particularly pressing for patients with cancer,⏎as delays in treatment initiation have been associated with increased mortality across several cancer types.34⏎These findings underscore the risks of AI use in an immensely burdensome process that adversely impacts patient⏎care, particularly as insurers’ use of AI grows. A survey from the National Association of Insurance⏎Commissioners (“NAIC”) found that 84 percent of responding health insurers use AI/machine learning.35,36⏎Utilization management is a primary use, with 71 percent reporting they currently or are exploring using AI for⏎utilization management practices.37 While we acknowledge that AI may speed up utilization review, providers⏎and patients need confidence that wrongful denials will not occur because of a lack of human oversight.⏎Unfortunately, at this time, many patients and providers are not even aware in cases where AI is involved in⏎utilization review; NAIC’s survey found that less than a quarter of insurers tell providers when AI is used.38⏎While CMS has taken some steps to regulate Medicare Advantage organizations’ (“MAOs”) use of AI in making⏎coverage determinations and has stated that it is the responsibility of the MAO to ensure that AI complies with⏎31 Community Oncology Alliance. “COA Comments on WISeR Model,” 14 August 2025. Available Here.⏎32 Community Oncology Alliance. “COA Comments on 2027 Medicare Advantage and Part D Proposed Rule.” 23 January 2026. Available Here.⏎33 American Medical Association. “2024 AMA prior authorization physician survey.” February 2025. Available Here.⏎34 American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network (ACS CAN). “The Costs of Cancer: Addressing Patient Costs.” 2020. Available Here.⏎35 Mello M et al. “The AI Arms Race In Health Insurance Utilization Review: Promises Of Efficiency And Risks Of Supercharged Flaws.” January 2026.⏎Available Here.⏎36 DeFrain K et al. “Health Insurance Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning Survey Results.” National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC).⏎2025. Available Here.⏎37 DeFrain K et al. “Health Insurance Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning Survey Results.” National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC).⏎2025. Available Here.⏎38 DeFrain K et. al. “Health Insurance Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning Survey Results.” National Association of Insurance Commissioners (NAIC).⏎2025. Available Here.⏎12⏎12⏎⏎CMS’s requirements for how coverage determinations are made, we are concerned that these regulations may⏎provide insufficient beneficiary protections.39,40 HHS Office of Inspector General (“OIG”) recently issued an⏎updated Medicare Advantage Industry Segment-Specific Compliance Guide that echoes concerns regarding the⏎use of AI in PA and coverage decisions.41 However, patient protections must expand across the health care sector.⏎For example, in a recent Medicaid and CHIP Payment and Access (“MACPAC”) session, staff found that federal⏎oversight of automation in AI is especially limited in Medicaid.42⏎To add further guardrails regarding plans’ use of AI and to promote transparency, we recommend CMS expand⏎PA reporting requirements to require health plans to also report when AI was used in PA decisions, stratified at⏎the item or service level for both drugs and services, with a breakdown of the percentages of whether requests⏎were approved or denied. CMS is already implementing public reporting requirements for PA of services, but⏎these are likely to mask trends in PA decisions since they are in the aggregate and are for services only.43 We⏎believe that data collection and public reporting from health plans that use AI in coverage decisions are vital for⏎providers, patients, and CMS, and support strategies that facilitate the collection of accurate, transparent data.⏎However, increased transparency and data collection are not a substitute for policies that make the role of human⏎oversight clear.⏎Recent research from Stanford University highlights several concerns and risks related to the use of AI: doubts⏎regarding the thoroughness of human review for AI-made decisions; users’ lack of familiarity with AI and how⏎it works; lack of information regarding what drives an AI prediction; underperformance, where models miss the⏎mark; unintended consequences, such as the creation of perverse incentives related to denials; and variance in⏎governance practices across health care organizations.44⏎While all of these issues are of significant concern, COA is particularly worried about how AI’s role in utilization⏎management might impact the use of precision medicine, which plays a vital role in oncology. Precision medicine⏎and targeted therapies enable physicians to tailor treatments to each patient’s cancer based on its genetic and⏎molecular characteristics. Restrictive insurance policies already often fail to keep pace with these innovations,⏎and the use of AI in utilization management for these treatments may further delay patient access to needed cancer⏎care.45⏎2. Concerns Regarding Patient Privacy and Security⏎Patient privacy and security are also critical in facilitating AI adoption. HHS itself has reported a 256 percent⏎increase in large breaches involving hacking and a 264 percent increase in ransomware over five years, making⏎data and security concerns particularly pressing.46 Patients must be able to trust that their information will be used⏎in a HIPAA-compliant manner, that they will be informed of when their data will be used within an AI tool, and⏎that safeguards are in place in the event of a data breach.⏎39 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Frequently Asked Questions related to Coverage Criteria and Utilization Management Requirements in CMS⏎Final Rule (CMS-4201-F).” Available Here.⏎40 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Medicare Program; Contract Year 2024 Policy and Technical Changes to the Medicare Advantage Program,⏎Medicare Prescription Drug Benefit Program, Medicare Cost Plan Program, and Programs of All-Inclusive Care for the Elderly.” 12 April 2023. Available⏎Here.⏎41 Department of Health and Human Services Office of Inspector General. “Medicare Advantage Industry Segment-Specific Compliance Program Guidance.”⏎February 2026. Available Here.⏎42 Medicaid and CHIP Payment and Access Commission. “January 2026 Public Meeting.” 29-30 January 2026. Available Here.⏎43 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. “Medicare and Medicaid Programs; Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act; Advancing Interoperability and⏎Improving Prior Authorization Processes for Medicare Advantage Organizations, Medicaid Managed Care Plans, State Medicaid Agencies, Children's Health⏎Insurance Program (CHIP) Agencies and CHIP Managed Care Entities, Issuers of Qualified Health Plans on the Federally-Facilitated Exchanges, Merit-Based⏎Incentive Payment System (MIPS) Eligible Clinicians, and Eligible Hospitals and Critical Access Hospitals in the Medicare Promoting Interoperability⏎Program.” 8 February 2024. Available Here.⏎44 Mello M et al. “The AI Arms Race In Health Insurance Utilization Review: Promises Of Efficiency And Risks Of Supercharged Flaws.” Health Affairs.⏎January 2026. Available Here.⏎45 Community Oncology Alliance. “COA Position Statement on Physician Autonomy and Patient Access to Cancer Treatment.” 24 March 2025. Available⏎Here.⏎46 Department of Health and Human Services Office for Civil Rights. “HHS Office for Civil Rights Issues Letter and Opens Investigation of Change Healthcare⏎Cyberattack.” 13 March 2024. Available Here.⏎13⏎13⏎⏎Conclusion⏎Structured incentives tailored to support community practices through the adoption of AI are vital for fostering⏎innovation, ensuring access to advanced treatments, and maintaining the competitive edge necessary to thrive in an⏎evolving health care landscape. We propose that these incentives be modeled after successful strategies used during⏎the EHR rollout, with the aim of encouraging engagement and investment in AI integration across the board. A multi-⏎faceted approach, focusing on financial incentives and infrastructure development, training and partnerships,⏎regulatory streamlining, and reimbursement, is critical to accelerate AI adoption. At the same time, policies that⏎expand AI use must incorporate transparency, strong privacy protections, and continued human clinical oversight.⏎Thank you for your request for information and consideration of these comments. COA is available to discuss any⏎of the concerns or recommendations in this letter and look forward to working with you on promoting the health and⏎well-being of Americans with cancer.⏎Sincerely,⏎Debra Patt, MD, PhD, MBA, FASCO Ted Okon, MBA⏎President Executive Director⏎14⏎14♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0170♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Podiatric Medical Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Podiatric Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., JD, LLM⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care; RIN 0955-AA13⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On behalf of the American Podiatric Medical Association (APMA), the premier professional⏎organization representing the vast majority of the nation’s doctors of podiatric medicine, also known as⏎podiatrists or podiatric physicians and surgeons, I thank you for the opportunity to respond to the⏎Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) “Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.”⏎APMA applauds HHS’ proactive approach and embrace of artificial intelligence (AI) in advancing its⏎ultimate goals of improved patient care and clinical outcomes while reducing clinician burden.⏎However, we respectfully urge HHS and its affiliated agencies to prioritize policies that integrate AI⏎technology to supplement, not supplant, the work of physicians. HHS must proceed thoughtfully and⏎deliberately, ensuring that the perspectives of all stakeholders are carefully evaluated and meaningfully⏎incorporated before moving forward too quickly.⏎Specifically, APMA urges HHS to ensure the following in any application or regulation of AI in health⏎care decisions, claims review, and other related processes:⏎1. Transparency and human oversight in coverage decisions;⏎2. Establishment of interoperability standards for AI in health care settings so that assistive AI⏎reduces physician administration burden, versus adding to it; and⏎3. Development of advisory bodies that provide opportunity for physician engagement and input so⏎that all perspectives, including those of podiatric physicians, are represented in the development⏎process for all AI regulatory and sub-regulatory guidance.⏎Transparency and Human Oversight in Coverage Decisions⏎While the use of AI-driven algorithms is becoming increasingly common in prior authorization (PA)⏎models, federal guardrails ensuring clinically accurate claim determinations have failed to keep pace.⏎Plans and payers largely omit public data around their processes that are often vague or opaque, leaving⏎⏎major concerns from physicians. Additionally, AI algorithms regularly rely on generalized data that⏎oversimplifies patient encounters and leads to wrongful denials of care. Training data may include⏎biased samples, which can limit their applicability to relevant populations and inappropriately restrict⏎access to care. Furthermore, AI-driven algorithms can automatically downcode evaluation and⏎management services, leading to reduced reimbursement for podiatric physicians.⏎Given these concerns, APMA urges HHS to promote policies that strengthen transparency and ensure⏎sufficient guardrails around the use of AI in coverage decisions. Specifically, APMA recommends that⏎HHS mandate or ensure the following:⏎- Prioritization of patient-centered data and physician judgment in AI-driven PA models⏎APMA underscores the need for direct physician oversight and collaboration on AI⏎o⏎models and technology that impact patient care and the PA process. It is equally⏎important to ensure human oversight in AI-supported coverage decisions. AI-based⏎denials should be reviewed by qualified physicians who have the clinical training needed⏎to inform decisions regarding medical necessity.⏎- Federal reporting requirements to ensure transparency in data and processes for coverage⏎decisions with PA using AI, including public access to algorithms and explanations of the data⏎used to train the AI. The reporting requirements should also detail the percentage of claims⏎determined by AI, including approval and denial rates.⏎- Strict enforcement measures to ensure AI-driven models use individualized clinical records⏎rather than broad data sets to determine claim denial or approval.⏎- Disclosure to both providers and patients when AI is part of the PA decision.⏎Physician Representation in Emerging AI Regulation⏎Physicians are one of the primary stakeholders impacted by the unregulated use of AI, including in⏎payer claims decisions. Including their perspective is essential in ensuring effective and ethical design,⏎deployment, and regulation of AI use in healthcare settings. With respect to regulations and federal⏎oversight, perspectives from physicians will help create regulations that are ethically grounded and⏎clinically sound and help to maximize the likelihood that AI serves as a tool to enhance, rather than⏎hinder access to care. To help achieve this goal, HHS should create health care-related AI advisory⏎bodies which include podiatric physicians, to better inform any policy proposals or recommendations.⏎Assistive AI’s Role in Reducing Administrative Burden⏎Lastly, APMA agrees that there is opportunity for increased efficiencies and administrative burden⏎reduction in clinical practice when physicians incorporate AI. When used as a supportive tool1, assistive⏎AI reduces burden with patient documentation and visit summaries, and creates opportunity for⏎physicians to provide more direct, patient-centered care. Assistive AI also presents various opportunities⏎to strengthen health record interoperability by helping to standardize, interpret, and connect disparate⏎data sources, which in turn can bolster care coordination to improve patient outcome.⏎1 APMA is in alignment with the idea of “augmented intelligence”, a conceptualization of artificial intelligence put forth⏎by the American Medical Association that focuses on AI’s assistive role, emphasizing that its design enhances human⏎intelligence rather than replaces it.⏎⏎APMA supports development of AI interoperability standards in healthcare. Standardization promotes⏎secure data exchange, reduces fragmentation, and enables seamless communication between electronic⏎health records (EHRs), diagnostic platforms, and clinical tools. Equally important are clear safeguards⏎that preserve physician autonomy in clinical decision-making. AI systems should function as supportive⏎tools, with providers remaining the primary and final decision-makers in their patients’ care.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you again for the opportunity to respond to this request for information. If you require additional⏎information, please contact Gail Reese, APMA Director Health Policy and Practice, at⏎greese@apma.org or 301-581-9230. Thank you for your time and consideration.⏎Sincerely,⏎Brooke A. Bisbee, DPM⏎President♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0171♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Morrow, Caroline J , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Caroline J♔Morrow♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 18, 2026⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Dear Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC Leadership,⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide input on this Request for Information. I am an⏎independent healthcare technology consultant with a background in quality improvement and⏎healthcare operations. My comments are informed by direct experience advising healthcare⏎organizations on technology adoption, clinical workflow optimization, and regulatory⏎compliance. I submit these comments as an individual.⏎AI adoption in clinical care is accelerating rapidly, and this momentum should be sustained.⏎However, the speed of adoption must be matched with proportionate diligence to ensure that AI⏎tools serve the best interests of patients and practitioners alike. My comments address four areas⏎of the RFI where I believe HHS action can have the greatest impact.⏎Question 1: Barriers to Innovation and Adoption⏎Data fragmentation remains the most significant structural barrier to effective AI in clinical care.⏎Health data continues to be siloed across delivery systems, payers, and public health entities,⏎limiting the representativeness and quality of datasets that underpin AI models. When AI⏎algorithms are trained on incomplete or non-representative data, the resulting tools risk⏎perpetuating existing health disparities and producing biased clinical recommendations. Research⏎has documented that AI bias in biomedicine can originate from insufficient data, sampling bias,⏎and the use of health-irrelevant features (Yang et al., Journal of Biomedical Informatics, 2024,⏎DOI: 10.1016/j.jbi.2024.104646).⏎HHS should prioritize policies that enable responsible data sharing across care settings while⏎maintaining rigorous patient privacy protections. Breaking down data silos through expanded⏎interoperability requirements, standardized data formats, and clear safe harbor provisions for⏎data sharing in AI development will empower AI developers to build tools grounded in high-⏎quality, representative datasets. High data quality is the prerequisite for high algorithmic⏎accuracy, and high accuracy is the prerequisite for patient safety.⏎Additionally, small and medium-sized practices face disproportionate barriers to AI adoption due⏎to limited capital, technical infrastructure, and administrative bandwidth. HHS should consider⏎incentive structures (whether through grants, technical assistance programs, or favorable⏎reimbursement policies) that lower the adoption threshold for these organizations, ensuring the⏎benefits of clinical AI are not concentrated among large health systems.⏎An emerging dimension of this challenge is the growing ability of clinicians themselves to⏎develop AI tools tailored to their practice. The increasing accessibility of open-source AI models⏎⏎and low-code development platforms means that a physical therapist can now build a pose⏎estimation tool for movement assessment, or a primary care clinic can develop a predictive⏎scheduling model using their own operational data. These clinician-developed tools have the⏎advantage of being built by domain experts who understand the clinical context intimately.⏎However, most clinicians building these tools lack the regulatory and compliance expertise to⏎evaluate whether their creation crosses the threshold into software as a medical device (SaMD)⏎territory, or to conduct appropriate clinical validation. HHS should support this practitioner-led⏎innovation through accessible compliance guidance, technical assistance resources, and grant⏎programs that help clinicians build safe, validated tools while making clear that any AI tool used⏎in clinical workflows must be developed and applied within the clinician’s scope of practice and⏎domain expertise.⏎Questions 3–4: Evaluation Methods, Legal Issues, and Accountability⏎For non-medical device AI tools used in clinical care, evaluation frameworks must reflect the⏎clinical context in which these tools operate. Specifically, the calibration of false positive and⏎false negative rates should be aligned to the clinical risk profile of the use case. An AI tool that⏎assists in screening for a life-threatening condition requires a fundamentally different error⏎tolerance than one optimizing appointment scheduling. HHS should encourage or support the⏎development of risk-stratified evaluation standards that require AI developers to demonstrate⏎how their models manage the trade-off between sensitivity and specificity relative to patient⏎harm.⏎Liability and Accountability⏎A central governance challenge for clinical AI is aligning accountability with decision-making⏎authority. I offer the following framework for the Department’s consideration, built on the⏎distinction between human-in-the-loop and autonomous AI architectures:⏎• Augmented workflows (human-in-the-loop): When an AI tool provides clinical⏎decision support and a licensed clinician retains decision-making authority,⏎accountability for patient outcomes should rest with the clinician, consistent with existing⏎standards of professional practice. The AI tool is an instrument; the clinician is the⏎decision-maker. This is no different from a clinician who misinterprets a diagnostic⏎image or disregards a contraindication, the professional who exercises judgment bears⏎responsibility for that judgment.⏎• Autonomous workflows (no human-in-the-loop): When an AI tool operates without⏎clinician intervention at the point of clinical decision, accountability should be shared⏎between the deploying organization and the AI product owner. The deploying health⏎system bears responsibility analogous to its existing credentialing and privileging⏎obligations: it must validate, monitor, and govern the AI tool’s performance within its⏎care environment, just as it would a newly credentialed clinician. The AI product owner⏎⏎carries product liability for the tool performing as represented. This dual accountability⏎mirrors existing institutional and professional liability structures and provides a familiar⏎governance model for health systems.⏎Scope of Practice Congruency⏎Regardless of whether an AI tool operates in an augmented or autonomous capacity, its use and⏎validation should be bounded by scope of practice and domain expertise. It would not be⏎appropriate for a physical therapist to rely on a clinical decision support tool designed for⏎neurologists, nor for a clinic to deploy a near-autonomous surgical AI system outside the⏎oversight of appropriately credentialed surgical professionals. Validation of autonomous AI tools⏎should follow peer-level review logic, which is analogous to the peer review processes used in⏎quality management systems, where the evaluators possess domain expertise congruent with the⏎tool’s clinical function. HHS guidance on clinical AI governance should reinforce this principle⏎to prevent scope mismatches that could compromise patient safety.⏎Transparency and Automation Bias Mitigation⏎Even in human-in-the-loop architectures, automation bias presents a material patient safety risk.⏎Clinicians may defer to AI output without exercising independent judgment, particularly under⏎time pressure or when the tool’s reasoning is opaque. Addressing this risk requires action on two⏎fronts:⏎1. AI developers should be expected to design tools that are transparent in their reasoning,⏎surface confidence levels, and clearly communicate limitations, enabling meaningful⏎clinician oversight rather than perfunctory confirmation.⏎2. Clinical training programs and continuing education should evolve to build competency⏎in evaluating AI-generated recommendations critically, treating AI literacy as a core⏎clinical skill rather than an elective technical interest.⏎Scoping reviews of EHR-integrated digital technologies have identified challenges including⏎alert fatigue, workflow integration difficulties, and algorithmic bias, with a noted research gap on⏎whether these tools address or worsen health inequities (Murthi et al., Journal of Medical⏎Systems, 2024, DOI: 10.1007/s10916-024-02097-5). These findings underscore the need for⏎post-deployment surveillance that accounts for differential performance across patient⏎populations and for transparency requirements that support genuine clinical oversight.⏎Question 6: Where AI Has Met or Fallen Short of Expectations⏎Ambient AI documentation tools represent one of the most promising and well-evidenced⏎applications of AI in clinical care today. A prospective study at Stanford Health Care found that⏎ambient AI scribes produced statistically significant reductions in physician task load and⏎burnout, with improvements in usability and perceived documentation quality (Shah et al.,⏎JAMIA, 2025, DOI: 10.1093/jamia/ocae295). Similarly, a quality improvement study at the⏎⏎University of Kansas found that an ambient AI platform significantly improved clinician⏎perceptions of documentation ease and reduced after-hours EHR work, with 48% of respondents⏎reporting capacity to see an additional patient (Albrecht et al., JAMIA Open, 2025, DOI:⏎10.1093/jamiaopen/ooaf013).⏎A 2025 systematic review confirmed these patterns across multiple implementations, finding that⏎9 of 10 studies reported efficiency improvements and 7 of 10 reported positive effects on⏎clinician wellness (Hassan et al., Applied Clinical Informatics, 2025, DOI: 10.1055/a-2597-⏎2017). However, the same review noted that AI scribe accuracy varies and frequently requires⏎manual edits, reinforcing the need for continued clinician oversight even in well-performing⏎applications.⏎Reducing clinician burnout is not merely a workforce satisfaction issue, it is a patient safety⏎imperative. Burned-out clinicians are more prone to medical errors and less able to engage⏎meaningfully with patients. AI tools that restore clinician time and attention to direct patient care⏎represent a high-value investment. HHS should consider how reimbursement structures and⏎programmatic incentives can accelerate adoption of these administrative AI tools, particularly in⏎resource-constrained settings where documentation burden is most acute.⏎Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Concerns⏎Patients and caregivers increasingly encounter AI-generated health information, both within⏎clinical settings and through direct-to-consumer channels. A critical concern is that patients often⏎cannot distinguish between reliable, clinically validated AI output and AI-generated content that⏎may contain inaccuracies or hallucinations. When patients act on erroneous AI-generated⏎medical information, whether by deferring necessary treatment or pursuing inappropriate⏎interventions, the consequences can be serious.⏎HHS should consider how any AI product that provides medical information directly to patients⏎can be held to standards commensurate with the clinical impact of that information. This does⏎not necessarily require a single regulatory framework, but it does require clarity on⏎accountability. Patients deserve to know whether the AI tools informing their care decisions have⏎been validated against clinical evidence and whether a licensed professional stands behind the⏎output.⏎At the same time, AI has significant potential to improve the patient experience by enabling⏎more personalized treatment recommendations, reducing wait times through more efficient⏎clinical operations, and supporting shared decision-making between patients and their care⏎teams. Ensuring that clinical AI tools are built on high-quality, representative data is the⏎foundation for delivering on this promise equitably across patient populations.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎The pace of AI adoption in clinical care should be sustained, but it must be accompanied by⏎deliberate action to ensure data quality, algorithmic accountability, clinician trust, and patient⏎safety. As the evidence base for clinical AI matures, HHS should anticipate a future in which the⏎adoption of validated AI tools becomes an expected component of the standard of care and⏎should ensure that the regulatory and reimbursement environment supports that transition⏎responsibly rather than reactively. Accountability structures should align with decision-making⏎authority, with clinicians responsible for the professional judgment they exercise using AI tools,⏎and AI product owners and deploying organizations sharing responsibility for autonomous⏎systems. These frameworks should be grounded in existing clinical governance principles,⏎including scope of practice, peer-level validation, and continuous quality monitoring, which are⏎adapted for a technology-augmented care environment.⏎I would respectfully urge the Department to recognize that rigorous data quality standards and⏎transparent validation processes are not impediments to rapid adoption: they are prerequisites for⏎the patient trust that sustains it. Insufficient attention to governance and compliance when AI⏎innovations enter clinical workflows acts as a silent killer of adoption: trust does not collapse in a⏎single event but erodes incrementally, degrading patient confidence in AI-assisted care until⏎adoption stalls and the root cause is difficult to trace. Reducing oversight prematurely risks high-⏎profile failures that would set back public confidence in clinical AI and ultimately slow the very⏎adoption trajectory HHS seeks to accelerate. If patients do not trust AI-assisted care, they will⏎avoid providers who use it, withhold information from AI-enabled systems, or seek redundant⏎second opinions, all of which undermine the cost savings and outcome improvements that⏎clinical AI promises. Building trust through demonstrated rigor is the fastest path to durable,⏎broad-based adoption.⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to use its regulatory, reimbursement, and research authorities to⏎create an environment where evidence-based AI tools can flourish—improving outcomes,⏎reducing burden, and making care more equitable and accessible. I appreciate the Department’s⏎commitment to seeking broad public input on these critical questions.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Caroline J. Morrow, PT, DPT⏎Board Certified Orthopedic Clinical Specialist, Board Certified Healthcare Executive, Certified⏎Professional in Healthcare Quality⏎Managing Director, CJM Strategic Consulting, LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0172♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Physician Associates, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Physician Associates♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The American Academy of Physician Associates (AAPA), on behalf of the over 190,000 PAs (physician⏎associates/physician assistants) throughout the United States, would like to provide comments on the Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) Request for Information (RFI). In the RFI, HHS seeks feedback from a broad range of stakeholders⏎on how best to harness the transformative potential of AI while ensuring consumer protections. Specifically, HHS⏎identifies three approaches (regulation, reimbursement, and research/development) that it hopes to apply to⏎support rapid adoption and use of AI.⏎The PA profession is at the forefront of care provision, working in concert with other health professionals to⏎provide high-quality care to patients. PAs have been shown to improve access to care while providing high levels⏎of quality and patient satisfaction comparable to physicians.1 The positive impact of PAs on patient care stands to⏎grow. The PA profession is one of the fastest-growing occupations, per the Bureau of Labor Statistics, with a⏎projected 20% increase in PAs from 2024 to 2034.2 As such, PAs are currently, and will increasingly be, immersed⏎in several of the direct clinical and indirect processes in which AI is expected to be integrated.⏎AAPA appreciates HHS’s commitment to improving beneficiary experience through increased use of AI. We share⏎HHS’s optimism regarding this burgeoning technology. AAPA has long supported HHS’s efforts to incentivize⏎1 Medicare Payment Advisory Commission. 2019. June 2019 Report to the Congress: Medicare and the Health Care⏎Delivery System. https://www.medpac.gov/document-type/report/⏎2 US Bureau of Labor Statistics, US Department of Labor. Occupational Outlook Handbook. Physician Assistants. August⏎28, 2025. https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/physician-assistants.htm⏎⏎expanded use of modern technology to increase productivity, improve patient outcomes, and reduce healthcare⏎costs. As society is still beginning to understand the various ways AI can be applied to meet these goals, we⏎continue to support HHS’s proactive efforts to ensure sufficient regulation that promotes safety and public trust,⏎while incentivizing the development and broad adoption of AI for the betterment of patient care. It is within this⏎context that we draw your attention to our comments.⏎AAPA Recognizes the Various Potential Benefits of AI⏎AAPA concurs with HHS that AI can transform care processes and delivery. One way we anticipate AI will positively⏎affect healthcare is by enabling the aggregation of large amounts of research data and drawing initial conclusions.⏎As such, AI may provide early disease detection, assess whether treatments and care regimens are effective, and⏎illuminate trends in patient adherence.⏎A second way AAPA foresees AI's positive impact is its potential to expedite administrative processes, thereby⏎improving efficiency and reducing provider burnout. In prior rules and RFIs, CMS has postulated on the potential⏎for AI to reduce the burden of the prior authorization process, automating the identification of when prior⏎authorizations are necessary, compiling portions of the required information a payer needs that can be found in a⏎health information system to process a prior authorization, and drafting a prompt payer response to providers.⏎AAPA supported these concepts in previous comments.3⏎A third way AI can positively impact healthcare is by reducing provider burnout by taking and organizing notes⏎during a patient encounter and using these notes to develop suggested diagnoses and personalized treatment⏎plans for qualified health professionals to review. These AI-proposed diagnoses and treatment plans would⏎effectively act as supplemental data for health professionals to consider. However, while AAPA recognizes the⏎transformative power of AI, we similarly recognize the need for guidelines to ensure proper development and use⏎of AI-integrated technologies.⏎Considerations for the Implementation of AI in Healthcare⏎Given AI's significant potential in healthcare, there is a corresponding risk of negative outcomes if AI policy is not⏎thoroughly developed and oversight is insufficient. AAPA has noted several considerations and corresponding⏎recommendations below.⏎3 American Academy of Physician Associates. Comments to the Request for Information; Health Technology Ecosystem.⏎Submitted June 12, 2025. https://www.aapa.org/download/146978/?tmstv=1770047702⏎2⏎⏎Avoid Unnecessary Restrictions on AI Usage⏎During the initial development of AI policies, HHS must avoid restrictive language that could create unintended⏎barriers to care. Ensuring appropriate and provider-inclusive language at the outset will avoid the need for⏎rectification later. Specifically, language in any developed federal code, as well as accompanying explanatory text,⏎should not use physician-centric language that confines the usage of AI to this subset of health professionals.⏎Instead, language should be broad enough to include any appropriate health professional. Language that⏎unnecessarily confines health professionals, who provide a significant share of patient care can blunt the use of AI⏎and minimize its potential advantages. Meanwhile, scope of practice determinations regarding who is qualified to⏎provide services involving AI should be made at the state level.⏎• AAPA Recommendation: Ensure developed language regarding AI is broad enough as not to restrict⏎health professionals like PAs from providing these services and guarantee that the patients PAs serve⏎can benefit from AI.⏎Avoid Unnecessary Restrictions on AI Reimbursement⏎As HHS develops inclusive language on which health professionals are authorized to use AI technology, as⏎recommended by AAPA in the previous section, the agency should also ensure that any language regarding⏎reimbursement for AI services is not physician-centric. Language regarding who may bill and receive⏎reimbursement for AI services must include any health professional authorized to directly bill for medical services⏎under Medicare. This is important because, of the estimated 1.5 million practitioners in the U.S. in 2033, about⏎50% will be physicians and 50% will be PAs and other non-physician practitioners.4⏎• AAPA Recommendation: Ensure developed language regarding AI reimbursement is broad enough as⏎not to restrict health professionals otherwise authorized to bill for services under Medicare, like PAs,⏎from billing for these services.⏎4 The 50/50 statistic comes from comparison of total number of providers from footnote 2 along with those of footnote⏎1 combined with: Bureau of Labor Statistics, U.S. Department of Labor, Occupational Outlook Handbook, Nurse⏎Anesthetists, Nurse Midwives, and Nurse Practitioners. https://www.bls.gov/ooh/healthcare/nurseanesthetists-nurse-⏎midwives-and-nurse-practitioners.htm⏎3⏎⏎Align Reimbursement with the Value Proposition of AI⏎AI technologies used in clinical care are intended to generate value by improving efficiency, reducing⏎administrative burden, enhancing clinical decision-making, and preventing unnecessary utilization. These benefits⏎are often realized at the system level rather than through discrete, billable services. Under fee-for-service⏎payment models, efficiency gains may be unrewarded or penalized, creating a disincentive for adoption even⏎when AI may improve quality and patient outcomes. Reimbursement policy should avoid framing AI as an add-on⏎service and instead support payment structures that enable providers and payers to capture the value of⏎improved efficiency and care delivery.⏎Premature creation of AI-specific billing codes, modifiers, or payment add-ons risks incentivizing volume and⏎utilization rather than value. Historically, such approaches have contributed to spending growth without⏎commensurate improvements in patient outcomes. Maintaining flexibility in payment policy is essential to allow AI⏎use cases to mature without creating artificial incentives.⏎• AAPA Recommendation: HHS should avoid the unnecessary creation of standalone billing codes or⏎service-level payment add-ons for AI and instead prioritize reimbursement approaches that reward⏎outcomes, efficiency, and total cost of care through value-based, population-based, and shared-savings⏎payment models.⏎Safeguard Sensitive Information⏎The development of AI technologies must incorporate patient privacy protections regarding the inadvertent⏎dissemination of personal and sensitive information. Sufficient protection must be included to ensure that⏎patients using AI technologies integrated into a system cannot access individualized information about other⏎patients. Similarly, providers should not be able to access identifiable, individualized patient information outside⏎their care. Vendors of AI technology must be treated as HIPAA-covered entities, and all relevant patient⏎protections must be incorporated into the AI technologies they develop.⏎• AAPA Recommendation: HHS must ensure or develop regulatory requirements that users and vendors⏎of AI technology are considered and regulated as HIPAA-covered entities.⏎4⏎⏎Ensure Appropriate Oversight of AI Diagnoses and Authorizations⏎AI has significant potential to aggregate data to identify accurate diagnoses and treatments and to expedite⏎processes. However, as with any technology, results are not guaranteed to be flawless from inception. This is⏎especially true of AI, which requires regular informational feedback to learn and improve. In healthcare, an⏎incorrect diagnosis, treatment, or coverage determination can harm a patient’s health. Incorrect inputs, biases, or⏎incomplete context considered by AI could prove deadly. As such, healthcare-related AI technology cannot be⏎granted an assumption of veracity without oversight from a licensed clinician.⏎• AAPA Recommendation: HHS should include in code and accompanying text a clear requirement for⏎safeguards, which could include human review of information generated by AI, surrounding diagnoses,⏎treatment recommendations, or coverage determinations.⏎Support Adoption of AI Among Providers⏎AAPA believes the use of AI by health professionals will present a significant opportunity to streamline processes⏎and improve patient care. However, new technologies take time and money to implement. Frequently,⏎infrastructure needs to be adopted, processes developed, and training provided. However, HHS could expedite AI⏎adoption by providing financial incentives for implementation, producing educational materials, and offering⏎training and best practices.⏎While the technology is new, HHS should refrain from making the adoption of AI technologies compulsory. Health⏎professionals, like society at large, are still trying to understand the capabilities and limitations of this new⏎technology and how to adopt and implement it into their workflows. However, HHS may wish to set potential⏎timelines for the inclusion of AI adoption and utilization in existing and future incentive programs, with the⏎financial benefits and corresponding penalties for AI adoption and use increasing over a predetermined time.⏎However, multiple timelines for AI adoption may be required, as AI integration will take many distinct forms with⏎varying purposes (e.g., diagnostic technology, EHR integration, prior authorization). HHS should further consider⏎additional financial support for small provider groups to offset the costs of software and necessary infrastructure.⏎• AAPA Recommendation: HHS should consider developing a timeline under which it will incorporate⏎financial bonuses and penalties for AI adoption and usage into its current and future incentive⏎programs. HHS should also consider providing training and educational materials, as well as⏎supplementary financial support for small provider groups to subsidize the initial adoption of required⏎infrastructure.⏎5⏎⏎AI and the Importance of Accurate Data in Incentivizing and Publicly Reporting the Use of AI⏎AAPA views the integration of AI into clinical services as an opportunity to improve the provision of care. We⏎believe that health professionals who appropriately use AI technology stand to benefit financially from improved⏎quality of care and reduced costs. As a result, it is likely that health professionals using AI in a useful manner will⏎see improvement in performance metrics and corresponding financial benefits under various incentive programs.⏎This may act as an additional incentive for AI adoption and use. However, these financial incentives can only be⏎recognized if services are accurately attributed to the health professional who provided them.⏎CMS has acknowledged in previous technology-related rules the importance of appropriately attributing services⏎to the health professional who provided them. AAPA concurs with CMS’s assessment. Unfortunately, certain⏎current fee-for-service Medicare policies obscure the actual provider of care. “Incident to” is a Medicare billing⏎provision that allows medical services personally performed by one health professional in the office or clinic⏎setting to be submitted to the Medicare program under the name of another health professional. Of particular⏎interest to us is when “incident to” is performed by PAs and attributed to physicians. PA services submitted under⏎Medicare’s “incident to” billing provision and attributed to physicians skew assessments of provider care quality,⏎resource utilization, and other measures relevant to value-based care, creating inaccurate data regarding both PAs⏎and physicians. Consequently, AAPA requests that HHS work with Congress to address the complications of⏎inaccurate data collection caused by “incident to” by ensuring services are accurately attributed to the health⏎professional who rendered the service. CMS should further evaluate how policies such as split (or shared) visit⏎billing and global surgical packages obfuscate the contributions of individual health professionals. Addressing⏎policies that incorrectly identify the provider of care will ensure that any financial or publicly facing ratings⏎regarding the proper use of AI are attributed to the health professionals who deserve them.⏎• AAPA Recommendation: AAPA recommends that HHS work with Congress to address the complications⏎of inaccurate data collection caused by “incident to” and further explore similar transparency concerns⏎brought about by split (or shared) visit billing and global surgical packages so the benefits of AI are⏎attributable to the appropriate health professional.⏎6⏎⏎Professional Title⏎AAPA requests that all references to PAs in regulations and policies be listed as “physician assistants/physician⏎associates,” as recognized in 20 CFR § 220.46 (a)(9).5 This accurately reflects PAs who currently graduate with⏎degrees as either “physician assistant” or “physician associate” and are state-licensed as a “physician assistant” or⏎“physician associate,” but who all graduate from programs accredited by the same accrediting organization⏎(Accreditation Review Commission on Education for the Physician Assistant), are certified by the same certifying⏎organization (National Commission on Certification of Physician Assistants), and have the same scopes of practice.⏎Although the profession has been known as “physician assistant,” the official title of the profession is now⏎recognized as “physician associate” to more accurately reflect the breadth of education, training, experience, and⏎services of PAs. This is reflected in the title of the AAPA, other professional organizations,6 professional training⏎programs,7 and state and territory laws and licensure.8 Despite the recognized title of “physician associate,” it is⏎anticipated to take some time for the title change from “physician assistant” to occur in all states and jurisdictions⏎in which PAs practice. Therefore, a dual reference to “physician assistant” and “physician associate” is⏎recommended to avoid confusion. AAPA urges CMS to reference the profession by the dual title “physician⏎assistant/physician associate.”⏎AAPA further requests that HHS implement federal protections regarding the use of health professional titles by⏎AI. Specifically, we recommend that HHS review a law9 implemented by California as of January 1, 2026, which⏎enforces existing license misrepresentation laws against any person or entity that develops or deploys AI or GenAI⏎that, in the AI’s or GenAI’s advertising or functionalities, uses one or more “terms, letters, or phrases to indicate or⏎imply possession of a license or certificate to practice a health care profession…” This law may serve as a roadmap⏎5 Code of Federal Regulations: Medical evidence. 20 CFR § 220.46. 2025. https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-⏎20/chapter-II/subchapter-B/part-220/subpart-F/section-220.46⏎6 Several Constituent Organizations, which are independent organizations affiliated with AAPA, have reflected the title⏎Physician Associate in their professional organization’s legal name. Examples: Connecticut Academy of Physician⏎Associates https://connapa.org/aboutconnapa, Kansas Academy of Physician Associates⏎https://kansaspa.mypanetwork.com, Academy of Physician Associates in Cardiology https://www.cardiologypa.org,⏎and Association of Physician Associates in Obstetrics and Gynecology https://apaog.wildapricot.org.⏎7 Several universities, which are accredited by the Accreditation Review Commission on Education for the Physician⏎Assistant (ARCPA), have Physician Associate Programs and graduate students in Physician Associate Studies. Examples:⏎Yale School of Medicine, Physician Associate Program, https://medicine.yale.edu/pa, Wichita State University, Physician⏎Associate Program https://www.wichita.edu/academics/health_professions/pa/, Alvernia University, Physician⏎Associate Program https://www.alvernia.edu/academics/ug/bio-pa.⏎8 Oregon Governor Tina Kotek Signs Law Changing PA Title (April 5, 2024)⏎https://www.aapa.org/newscentral/2024/04/oregongovernor-tina-kotek-signs-law-changing-pa-title/. See also, Or.⏎Rev. Stat. § 677. See also, Wis. Stat. § 448.974(1)(a)(2)-(6). See also, 185 N. MAR. I. ADMIN. CODE § 185-10-4101(p)⏎9 AB-489 Health care professions: deceptive terms or letters: artificial intelligence. California Legislative Information.⏎Accessed February 19 2026. https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billTextClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB489⏎7⏎⏎to a similar federal protection of heath professional titles. Similarly, we suggest that the transition to increased⏎use of AI will require a broad review of existing protectionary laws, beyond those related to title, to determine⏎applicability to those responsible for technology that has yet to be included in regulatory language.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide comments regarding the Artificial Intelligence request for information.⏎AAPA welcomes further discussion with HHS regarding these important issues. For any questions you may have⏎please do not hesitate to contact Sondra DePalma, AAPA Vice President of Reimbursement & Professional⏎Practice, at sdepalma@aapa.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Todd Pickard, DMSc, PA-C, DFAAPA, FASCO⏎President & Chair of the Board⏎American Academy of Physician Associates⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0173♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Alliance for Connected Care, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Alliance for Connected Care♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Assistant Secretary of Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health IT⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Response to the AI in Clinical Care Request for Information⏎Assistant Secretary of Technology Policy Posnack,⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback on how HHS can encourage flexible, efficient, and⏎clinically appropriate artificial intelligence (AI) adoption in care delivery. The agency’s tech-enabled care⏎agenda and consumer empowerment initiatives lend themselves readily to the already-emerging AI-⏎enabled care delivery methods. As virtual care practitioners and advocates, the Alliance for Connected⏎Care is pleased to provide our input on how HHS can drive the public and private sector toward a health⏎care system that rewards outcomes, patient-centered care, and high-value services.⏎The Alliance for Connected Care (“The Alliance”) is dedicated to improving access to care through the⏎reduction of policy, legal, and regulatory barriers to the adoption of connected care capabilities, including⏎telemedicine, remote patient monitoring (RPM), and AI-enabled care. Our members are leading health⏎care and technology organizations from across the spectrum, representing health systems, health payers,⏎technology innovators, and patient and provider groups, including many types of clinician specialty and⏎patient advocacy groups who wish to better utilize opportunities enabled by virtual care.⏎As has long been true in health care, patients consistently seek easier navigation, timely and continuous⏎support between visits, and simpler payment structures. Virtual care has made many strides in increasing⏎access to services and empowering clinicians to create the best available care; however, the next⏎generation of care is now upon us. Tens of millions of Americans have already turned to non-medical AI⏎chatbots to help them diagnose conditions, determine treatment plans, evaluate medication conflicts and⏎other functions. The fact that patients are seeking this support outside of the medical community⏎underscores a policy imperative. Our mission should be to further empower health care professionals and⏎the capabilities they employ to meet the needs of their patients.⏎Once we sort out the myriad of regulatory and payment issues, the ideal world is one in which a⏎coordinated care team, consisting of both human and agentic AI capabilities, is able to provide a patient⏎with both the expertise they require and the convenience of these consumer tools. Patients should have⏎access to asynchronous care, aided by agentic AI and connected to their primary medical record, without⏎worrying whether their data is secure or if they can trust the platform.⏎1⏎⏎Through the below points, the Alliance argues that AI-enabled care is an evolution of existing tech-enabled⏎care delivery and should be integrated into existing medical services. Federal policy should fully unlock⏎digital health while remaining technology-neutral, modality-agnostic, and focused on clinical⏎accountability and outcomes. Payment should reward value delivered to patients and drive increased⏎efficacy and data-driven coordination across the patient’s clinical care team.⏎The Alliance recommends that HHS use its authority and work with Congress and states to advance the⏎following objectives:⏎• Strengthen virtual care foundations for AI-enabled care.⏎Recognize AI-enabled care as a natural evolution of connected care, not a new kind of care⏎o⏎in need of unique policy or regulatory solutions.⏎Clear the decks on longstanding barriers to telehealth and remote patient monitoring that⏎o⏎will be equally burdensome to the expansion of AI-enabled care.⏎Avoid repeating the policymaking mistakes made in other digital health areas, where⏎o⏎narrow allowances later hampered patient access.⏎• Allow the integration of AI-enabled care into existing medical services, when clinically⏎appropriate⏎Pursue regulatory and reimbursement changes that allow for modality-agnostic care⏎o⏎delivery to facilitate the expansion of AI-enabled care.⏎Circumvent barriers like time-based reimbursement to the expansion of AI-enabled care.⏎o⏎Further grow AI-enabled care delivery in models based on outcomes, where these⏎o⏎changes can be made most easily.⏎Enable innovative “regulatory sandboxes” that create AI regulatory relief pathways that⏎o⏎allow for temporary exemptions from regulation, subject to rigorous due diligence, safety⏎constraints, and narrowly scoped use cases at the state and federal level.⏎STRENGTHEN VIRTUAL CARE FOUNDATIONS FOR AI-ENABLED CARE⏎AI-enabled care represents the natural next step in the evolution of connected and virtual care.⏎Just as telehealth expands access by expanding availability and bringing clinicians closer to patients⏎regardless of geography, agentic AI continues that progress by enhancing clinical capacity, enabling⏎continuous engagement between visits, and further customizing treatment plans and workflows around⏎patient needs. Agentic AI can strengthen patient-centered services, improve outcomes, and increase⏎system efficiency without disrupting the foundational principles of medical practice.⏎RPM improved care by making ongoing monitoring and clinical support available to patients when and⏎where they were needed. AI-enabled care builds on this foundation, leveraging timely and actionable⏎health information, strengthening clinical decision-making, supporting even more continuous engagement⏎between visits, and enabling more meaningful asynchronous care capabilities. These capabilities represent⏎2⏎⏎tangible improvements that patients value and that will measurably enhance outcomes without changing⏎the underlying intent of the service.⏎It is our overarching position that federal policy should approach agentic AI capabilities not as a standalone⏎treatment tool, but as an extension and modernization of modern digital health services. The majority of⏎these patient-facing capabilities fall within the definitions of treatment already covered as medical services⏎and should be viewed as the evolution of that care. To the extent that separate policy is required, AI-⏎enabled care should be evaluated, regulated, and reimbursed based on the quality of care delivered and⏎the health outcomes achieved. By aligning oversight and payment frameworks with this evolution, HHS⏎can foster innovation while preserving trust, accountability, and access to high-quality care.⏎We also recommend that existing regulatory approaches at both the state and federal level be leveraged⏎wherever possible. Policymakers should only act where genuine gaps exist, rather than taking steps that⏎could either 1) create duplicative requirements on the same care or 2) create unnecessary parallel⏎regulatory structures that dramatically complicate coverage and treatment using these AI-enabled⏎capabilities.⏎Clear the decks on longstanding restrictions on telehealth and RPM that will be equally burdensome to⏎the expansion of AI-enabled care.⏎The Alliance has led on issues related to connected care regulation and reimbursement for many years.⏎Throughout that period, we have seen short-sighted regulatory and reimbursement approaches allow for⏎the care delivery model of the moment, without considering the continuing evolution of that care. Nearly⏎all of the policy challenges faced in the expansion of telehealth and RPM apply to the further⏎transformation of care with the addition of agentic AI capabilities.⏎Despite rapid technological advancement, existing regulatory structures continue to pose significant⏎barriers to responsible AI integration. HHS should eliminate outdated telehealth-era restrictions such as⏎rigid interaction rules and geographic limitations, ensuring regulatory frameworks reflect that clinical value⏎is determined by outcomes, not modality. Outdated interaction and location-based care delivery⏎requirements, designed for a pre-digital health environment, limit clinicians’ ability to leverage AI-enabled⏎tools in remote and distributed care settings. These barriers also impede models that rely on continuous⏎monitoring, asynchronous communication, and/or digitally supported decision-making.⏎With this in mind, we call on policymakers to seek broad solutions to care delivery that remove traditional⏎payment and access barriers for all connected care capabilities, including telehealth, RPM, and agentic AI⏎capabilities. We call on HHS to lead efforts (along with Congress/states) to:⏎• End short-term extension-to-extension policymaking that hampers the ability of innovators to⏎invest in tech-enabled care for seniors. Examples include recent lapses of Medicare telehealth and⏎hospital at home, and near lapses of telehealth prescribing allowances. The ongoing and very real⏎risk to these foundational elements of a modern health care system undermines the promise of⏎3⏎⏎healthcare transformation and stifles innovation and expansion (including investments in AI-⏎enabled care).⏎• Eliminate Medicare geographic and originating site limitations, allowing patients to communicate⏎remotely with care, regardless of location. Not only do these restrictions limit telehealth, but this⏎outdated regulatory structure is entirely unsuited for a future in which on-demand agentic support⏎is a crucial part of the care delivery ecosystem.⏎• Remove archaic distant site practitioner-type limitations on connected care. Any provider who can⏎deliver clinically appropriate care should be able to do so. This language can be modernized to⏎focus on clinical appropriateness, with the understanding that care may not always include a⏎human practitioner.⏎• Permanently remove the concept of an in-person visit as a “guardrail” on connected care access.⏎These restrictions serve to limit access to care, particularly for those with transportation, mobility,⏎frailty, or stigma-related barriers. This concept is fundamentally incompatible with the promise of⏎AI-enabled care, which will help to resolve in-person access constraints.⏎• Address duplicative state licensing regulations that limit health care competition and stifle care⏎delivery innovation. While there is robust debate currently about state-based AI regulation,⏎barriers to the ability to practice across state lines remain a significant drag on the opportunity to⏎scale national care-delivery networks that leverage both human and agentic capabilities.⏎• Modernize health plan network adequacy requirements to reflect real-world access measures,⏎such as beneficiary access and satisfaction, rather than outdated time and distance standards. The⏎realization of outcome-based measurements for network adequacy is equally important for a⏎world in which a portion of patient care is provided by agentic AI capabilities.⏎Avoid repeating the policymaking mistakes made in other digital health areas, where specific allowances⏎later hampered patient access.⏎Our experience with telehealth, through which policymakers created statutory and regulatory⏎permissibility designed for one moment, without anticipation of the evolution of that care should be a⏎lesson for policymaking in the age of AI. Policymaking barriers, such as Congressional Budget Office⏎scoring, have caused major telehealth wins to age poorly and ultimately become major impediments to⏎patient access. Federal policymakers should avoid repeating this cycle by regulating AI to the point of⏎stagnation or focusing primarily on guardrails when incentive-aligned policy frameworks more effectively⏎promote safe, scalable innovation. These guardrails (such as location requirements for telehealth)⏎ultimately become barriers to care long after the concern they were designed to address has passed.⏎We believe HHS should avoid tool-specific regulatory regimes that delay deployment of clinically effective⏎AI-enabled services. Lessons from telehealth demonstrate that technology-specific rules create⏎fragmentation, limit scalability, and distort care delivery. HHS should focus on payment for services and⏎outcomes, with flexibility for agentic AI capabilities to be deployed as clinically proven and validated over⏎time. A risk-based, technology-neutral approach focused on clinical function, safety, and outcomes will⏎better support responsible innovation.⏎4⏎⏎Similarly, workforce regulations should be modernized to enable clinicians to safely extend their reach⏎using agentic AI tools. HHS can support team-based, technology-supported care while preserving oversight⏎and accountability. As a result, providers would feel empowered to use the best tools available to provide⏎the highest quality care possible, without being concerned about jumping through hoops required for the⏎billing of that service.⏎ALLOW THE INTEGRATION OF AI-ENABLED CARE INTO EXISTING MEDICAL SERVICES, WHEN CLINICALLY⏎APPROPRIATE⏎Pursue regulatory and reimbursement changes that allow for modality-agnostic care delivery to⏎facilitate the expansion of AI-enabled care.⏎Agentic AI has the potential to significantly expand clinical capacity and improve care delivery. This promise⏎will only be realized at scale if HHS adopts regulatory and reimbursement changes that allow for modality-⏎agnostic care delivery, evaluated on outcomes. AI-enabled clinical services should not be evaluated and⏎reimbursed based on the specific tools, algorithms, or modalities used to deliver those services. In the⏎near-term, payers should take steps toward the reimbursement of clinicians for clinically meaningful⏎outputs delivered through validated, guideline-based workflows—whether those workflows are executed⏎by human team members or AI-enabled agents. Federal policies that are organized around care delivery⏎and outcomes, not the specific technologies used to achieve them will create the necessary flexibility for⏎future evolution as these capabilities mature.⏎AI-enabled care holds promise for expanding patient access to timely, continuous, and clinically⏎meaningful services, especially through RPM, chronic disease management, and patient engagement.⏎These capabilities enable earlier intervention, reduce avoidable hospitalizations, and lower total cost of⏎care while ensuring patients—particularly those in rural, underserved, mobility-limited, or clinician-⏎shortage settings—can receive appropriate care without relying solely on in-person encounters. Virtual⏎behavioral health and primary care further illustrate agentic AI’s clinician-extending potential, supporting⏎on-demand asynchronous care and regular patient engagements.⏎AI-enabled virtual and hybrid care should be integrated into existing delivery and payment systems, rather⏎than siloed into technology-specific reimbursement structures. This integration will promote continuity,⏎efficiency, and equitable access to virtual care. Importantly, by retaining the same reimbursement⏎structures for services, with flexibility for modality, we empower clinicians and patients to fully control the⏎choice for how care is delivered rather than creating payment incentives for one modality over another.⏎Circumvent barriers like time-based reimbursement to the expansion of AI-enabled care.⏎One of the most significant reimbursement barriers to AI-enabled clinical care is the continued reliance on⏎time-based payment. Rewarding clinician minutes rather than outcomes, efficiency, or productivity gains⏎discourages adoption of models that extend clinician capacity and improve care delivery.⏎CMS’s recent Advanced Primary Care Management (APCM) codes signal movement away from rigid time⏎tracking; these demonstrate a pathway that could continue to be expanded to better accommodate AI-⏎5⏎⏎enabled workflows. Unfortunately, current asynchronous billing codes—including Chronic Care⏎Management (CCM), Principal Care Management (PCM), Remote Therapeutic Monitoring (RTM), RPM,⏎and Online Digital Evaluation and Management (Digital E/M)—remain heavily tied to time-based inputs.⏎This structure paradoxically penalizes clinicians who deploy AI tools that improve efficiency.⏎Payment policy should not penalize AI-driven efficiency, such as faster diagnoses, fewer unnecessary visits,⏎or reduced clinician time per episode, when outcomes improve. HHS must work with stakeholders to⏎continue progress in efforts to move beyond time-based reimbursement to reward efficient, outcome-⏎driven services, allowing AI-enabled care to evolve naturally within the market. A practical near-term⏎policy option, included in a recent NEJM Catalyst article, proposed that HHS consider a dual billing⏎pathway similar to synchronous Evaluation and Management services—permitting reimbursement based⏎on medical decision-making (MDM) complexity rather than time alone. This modification would allow⏎clinicians to retain accountability for care delivered under their supervision while capturing the clinical⏎value, liability, and oversight associated with AI-enabled workflows.⏎Agentic AI meaningfully expands clinical capacity (far beyond the requisite minutes that a clinician might⏎otherwise bill), enabling proactive outreach, behavioral coaching, medication adherence support, and⏎early intervention at a scale not possible under traditional time-bound models. Payment frameworks⏎should recognize this expanded capacity as an opportunity to reduce downstream utilization and total cost⏎of care.⏎Further grow AI-enabled care delivery in models based on outcomes, where these changes can be made⏎most easily.⏎Continued transition toward outcomes-based, value-aligned payment is essential for the expansion of care⏎with flexibility for AI-enabled capabilities. Evaluation and payment for these services must be tied to⏎clinical outcomes, rather than process-driven payments and measures in order to unlock the capabilities⏎of AI-enabled care. Greater opportunities to align payment incentives around outcomes, and with⏎appropriate flexibility, will allow the market to determine the most effective methods of delivery including⏎AI-enabled capabilities as a natural evolution of care.⏎As a first step, HHS should establish reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled care within existing covered⏎service lines. These services include remote, asynchronous, and longitudinal models where there are clear,⏎low-hanging opportunities for AI to augment existing services, such as RPM which could more clearly⏎depend on continuous, technology-supported engagement rather than discrete care management⏎encounters.⏎HHS can lay the foundation for linking payment more directly to long-term health outcomes while⏎preserving near-term compatibility with existing billing infrastructure by treating high-value clinical⏎outputs as measurable and reimbursable units of care. Paying for validated outputs of care also creates a⏎pathway toward broader outcome-based reimbursement. AI-enabled workflows can document patient⏎follow-through on care plans, such as completion of ordered diagnostics, medication initiation, adherence,⏎and symptom improvement. These measurable clinical actions serve as proximal indicators of improved⏎6⏎⏎health outcomes and can be standardized, audited, and ultimately tied to value-based payment⏎arrangements when a fully outcome-based model is not possible.⏎As CMS has recently engaged in through the ACCESS model, federal demonstrations through CMMI offer⏎another important opportunity to scale access-expanding AI-enabled care models, align payment with⏎measurable outcomes, and support regulatory sandboxes that enable innovation with appropriate risk-⏎based safeguards. Through these combined actions, HHS can take steps to expand options for AI-enabled⏎care as an integrated extension of modern care delivery thus expanding access and strengthening the⏎workforce.⏎Enable innovative “regulatory sandboxes” that allow for temporary exemptions from regulation, subject⏎to rigorous due diligence, safety constraints, and narrowly scoped use cases.⏎HHS should more broadly seek to encourage experimentation with AI-enabled care, while continuing to⏎ensure risk-based oversight as needed. This should include partnerships with states to test novel AI⏎capabilities within their regulatory jurisdictions.⏎As mentioned above, there is significant opportunity to continue to build on CMMI payment model⏎exploration to create regulatory flexibility in the testing of AI services at scale in real-time. Flexibility on⏎both the existing virtual care restrictions mentioned previously as well as additional AI-specific flexibilities⏎should accompany outcome-based payment models testing the ability of AI-enabled care.⏎There is robust interest in AI-focused activity at the state level. It may be possible to divert some of that⏎energy into productive testing and evaluation of AI-enabled health care services through state or regional⏎sandboxes in which AI-enabled health care is tested within a regionally constrained, and narrowly scoped⏎situation with risk-based oversight.⏎Regulatory flexibility should be paired with structural safeguards that preserve program integrity. AI-⏎enabled clinical workflows can be configured to follow evidence-based pathways, blocking inappropriate⏎actions and ensuring all interventions are clinically justified. Additionally, AI-enabled systems will offer⏎enhanced auditability compared to traditional models as each action can be logged, traceable, and⏎evaluated against predefined criteria. HHS should incorporate auditability and pathway-based safeguards⏎into sandbox and demonstration models, enabling innovation while strengthening fraud prevention and⏎oversight.⏎INITIAL OPPORTUNITIES FOR AI-ENABLED CARE⏎The Alliance believes that there are specific areas in digital health that are most ripe for AI-enabled care⏎innovation. This evolution builds directly on telehealth’s success in bringing clinicians closer to patients⏎and reflects what individuals increasingly expect from a modern, responsive health system. These⏎opportunities include –⏎• Expand chronic disease management support between visits, including medication adherence,⏎symptom tracking, and behavioral coaching. Asynchronous care strengthened by agentic AI⏎7⏎⏎represents a critical access pathway, enabling patients to receive guidance, monitoring, and timely⏎clinical intervention between traditional encounters. This should include options to move beyond⏎time-based reimbursement – including MDM or outcome-based reimbursement.⏎RPM, as previously noted, is a particularly strong opportunity as the combination of⏎o⏎agentic AI and RPM strengthens patient engagement and self-management through⏎personalized feedback, education, and adherence support without increasing workforce⏎strain. HHS should work to remove outdated payment and program design barriers that⏎discourage the appropriate use of automation and AI to deliver higher-value care.⏎• Remove barriers to agentic AI-enabled triage, intake, screening, and follow-up, including⏎autonomous or semi-autonomous patient interactions that are part of a reimbursable interaction.⏎While barrier removal is the first step, clinical efficacy of specific interventions will still need to be⏎evaluated and risk related to this care will need to be evaluated.⏎While we have largely discouraged “AI codes” one specific opportunity is the reimagining⏎o⏎of virtual check-ins as a payment pathway for agentic AI. These Medicare codes are a⏎classic example of telehealth payment restrictions making an otherwise promising service⏎unworkable, all for a relatively low-value code. However, the objective for this care—in⏎which a patient is contacted between other services to manage their condition—is an⏎ideal structure for agentic AI.⏎• Convene experts and stakeholders to reimagine what core virtual interactions, including remote⏎supervision and interprofessional consultations should look like in a world where clinicians have⏎AI capabilities assisting them.⏎• Recognize the trajectory of care and begin to create flexibility within many other services for⏎agentic AI tools to be deployed as they become clinically proven.⏎HHS has a critical opportunity to ensure that AI strengthens—not fragments—the clinician-patient⏎relationship. By aligning payment with clinically meaningful outputs and modernizing outdated modality⏎restrictions, the Department can ensure patient AI adoption happens in alignment with the patient care⏎team, rather than outside of it.⏎Thank you for your consideration of these recommendations. We look forward to continuing to work with⏎you on this important next step in America’s tech-enabled care journey. Please do not hesitate to contact⏎Rikki Cheung at rikki.cheung@connectwithcare.org with any questions or to schedule a meeting.⏎Sincerely,⏎Chris Adamec⏎Executive Director⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0174♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Abridge AI, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Abridge AI, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Re: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎To: Department of Health and Human Services, Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎and the Oice of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology,⏎On behalf of Abridge, we appreciate the opportunity to comment on the HHS Oice of the⏎Deputy Secretary in collaboration with ASTP/ONC Request for Information (RFI) on what HHS⏎can do to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care. Abridge is grateful for⏎the chance to contribute to this vital conversation regarding what HHS can do in this area.⏎Founded in 2018, Abridge is the leading enterprise-grade AI platform for clinical⏎conversations. Powered by a purpose-built system of intelligence for healthcare, the⏎company is building real-time connections between patients, providers, and payers, starting⏎from clinically useful and compliant documentation, and extending through prior⏎authorization, risk adjustment, and clinical decision support. This year, Abridge is projected⏎to support more than 80 million patient-clinician conversations across 250 of the largest and⏎most complex health systems in the U.S., including the Department of Veteran Aairs. With⏎deep EHR integration, support for 28+ languages, and 50+ specialties, Abridge is used across⏎the entire care journey, including outpatient, emergency department, inpatient, and nursing⏎workflows.⏎Abridge is seing the industry standard for the trusted deployment of AI across health⏎systems. Features like Linked Evidence map AI-generated clinical documentation to source⏎data, helping clinicians quickly trust and verify the output.⏎Abridge was awarded Best in KLAS for Ambient AI in 2025 and 2026, in addition to other⏎accolades, including TIME Best Inventions of 2024 and 2025, CNBC Disruptor 2025, and Fast⏎Company’s Most Innovative Companies 2025.⏎1⏎⏎With deep integration into the operational and clinical workflows of some of the largest and⏎most complex healthcare systems in the US, Abridge has a unique perspective. Our partner⏎experience team works closely with these institutions, giving us direct visibility into the daily⏎friction points for both patients and caregivers. Patients and caregivers are looking to AI to⏎address fundamental healthcare challenges, primarily through improved diagnostic⏎accuracy, personalized treatment plans, and reduced administrative delays. They specifically⏎hope AI will facilitate early disease detection such as identifying anomalies in medical⏎imaging or predicting cardiac events and help manage complex, rare conditions by surfacing⏎evidence-based insights that a human clinician might miss. There is also a strong desire for⏎AI to lower the administrative burden on the patient-provider relationship; by automating⏎clinical documentation, AI can return valuable time to clinicians, allowing for more focused,⏎face-to-face patient engagement rather than screen-centered care.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the eective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations.⏎To incentivize and eectively use AI in clinical care, HHS should prioritize transitioning from⏎fee-for-service models to outcomes-oriented payment frameworks and clarifying regulatory⏎definitions that enable healthcare to fully capture the gains of recent breakthroughs in⏎agentic AI . By aligning financial incentives with clinical results and modernizing standards for⏎autonomous systems, HHS can reduce administrative burdens and foster a more innovative⏎health technology ecosystem.⏎Priority Regulatory and Programmatic Changes⏎HHS has already taken important steps to advance interoperability, promote value-based⏎care, and modernize data exchange standards. We support these eorts and believe the⏎next phase of policy development should focus on ensuring that existing frameworks fully⏎enable responsible, enterprise-grade AI deployment in clinical care.⏎Based on our experience, we believe that the following structural and policy modernizations⏎would do the most to accelerate adoption and unlock new opportunities:⏎● Codifying "Autonomous Agents" in Information Blocking Rules (45 CFR § 171.102):⏎HHS should explicitly include automated means and autonomous AI systems acting as⏎2⏎⏎clinician "agents" within the definitions of "access" and "use". This clarity would eliminate⏎the technical or contractual ambiguities that can work to block AI-enabled workflows.⏎Building upon the existing Information Blocking framework, clarifying this interpretation⏎would reduce technical or contractual ambiguities that can unintentionally impede⏎AI-enabled workflows, while remaining consistent with the statute’s intent to prevent⏎inappropriate interference with information exchange.⏎● Narrowing the "Infeasibility Exception"(45 CFR § 171.204): Current rules allow for⏎claims of "infeasibility" to deny rightful data exchange even when technical solutions⏎exist. Removing the "third party seeking modifications" condition would prevent⏎platforms from using technical complexity as a pretext. Additional clarification regarding⏎application of this exception would help ensure it is not interpreted in ways that⏎unnecessarily delay secure, technically feasible data exchange, consistent with the rule’s⏎interoperability objectives.⏎● Establishing "Agent-Ready" Bidirectional APIs (45 CFR § 170.315(g)(10)): Future⏎FHIR-based API requirements should shift from "read-only" access to supporting⏎bidirectional workflows. This enables authorized AI agents to securely "write back"⏎structured data, such as clinical notes or adverse events, into the system of record.⏎● Modernizing HIPAA De-identification for Multimodal Data (45 CFR § 164.514(b)): HHS⏎should provide clearer guidance and establish pilot programs for de-identifying⏎unstructured data types like audio, text, and ambient clinical inputs. Current frameworks⏎have fallen behind in providing the guidance ideally required for modern AI technologies⏎to refine models while maintaining rigorous privacy standards.⏎Payment Policy Prioritization⏎CMS should prioritize payment models that reward clinical insight over documentation⏎volume. CMS has made sustained progress in transitioning from volume-based⏎reimbursement toward value-based and outcomes-driven models. AI-enabled clinical⏎infrastructure can help accelerate and operationalize these existing policy objectives.⏎● Outcome-Aligned Payments (OAPs): Shift toward recurring payments for managing⏎qualifying conditions, with full payment tied to measurable health outcomes rather than⏎specific billable activities.⏎● Transcript-Grounded Documentation for Risk Adjustment: Incentivizing the use of⏎high-fidelity, auditable AI documentation can beer capture patient complexity, which is⏎often missed in traditional claims data. AI can identify and structure critical non-clinical⏎3⏎⏎factors such as housing, food, or transportation insecurity that directly impact health⏎outcomes but are rarely coded in standard documentation.⏎● CMMI Pilots: Explore Innovation Center (CMMI) demonstration authority under Section⏎1115A of the Social Security Act to test AI-enabled administrative simplification models.⏎Carefully structured pilots could measure impacts on clinician time, documentation⏎integrity, risk adjustment accuracy, and patient outcomes, building on existing Innovation⏎Center eorts.⏎● AI-Driven Benchmarking and Metrics in APMs: Incorporate AI solutions to objectively⏎determine if Alternative Payment Models (APMs) are eectively contributing to⏎value-based care goals by analyzing large datasets of de-identified clinical encounter⏎documentation. Additionally, consider incorporating ambient AI documentation metrics⏎into Quality Payment Program (QPP) improvement activities.⏎● Payment Policy details: Clarify that AI-assisted documentation tools that improve⏎coding accuracy and reduce administrative burden are permissible and compliant within⏎Medicare Advantage risk adjustment and Medicare FFS documentation standards.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎Data must be clinically useful to train robust AI, but current regulatory standards for⏎anonymizing that data are struggling to keep pace with modern technology. Abridge⏎recommends that HHS prioritize a modernized framework for de-identifying unstructured⏎multimodal data, such as audio, text, and ambient clinical inputs to resolve legal ambiguity⏎and accelerate safe innovation.⏎HHS should move beyond categorical removal lists toward a risk-based, standards-driven⏎approach that emphasizes auditability and security controls.⏎● Create "Privacy-Preserving" Evaluation Sandboxes: HHS should establish regulatory⏎sandboxes where developers can test models on de-identified real-world data under⏎strict oversight. These environments would allow for subject maer expert feedback⏎loops to ensure models perform accurately before full production deployment.⏎● Modernize Standard for Multimodal Data (45 CFR § 164.514(b)): HHS can clarify⏎acceptable practices for obfuscating multimodal inputs and develop federal standards⏎4⏎⏎for "trustworthy AI documentation" that align technical safeguards like encryption with⏎ethical requirements like transparency and auditability.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability is the primary catalyst for shifting AI from a series of "silos" to an⏎integrated clinical partner. By breaking down barriers between disparate systems, the⏎healthcare industry can unlock significant market opportunities, fuel critical research, and⏎accelerate AI development. For AI adoption to transition from isolated departmental pilots to⏎comprehensive, enterprise-wide strategies, enhanced interoperability is essential.⏎Standardized data exchange would allow third-party AI "agents" to act on behalf of clinicians,⏎which opens markets for automated documentation, prior authorization, and real-time⏎clinical decision support. A centralized, platform-based model minimizes the need for⏎multiple direct connections, allowing hospitals to add new AI solutions without overwhelming⏎IT sta.⏎Abridge thanks the HHS Oice of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC for the opportunity to⏎provide commentary on this critical RFI. We remain commied to partnering with HHS and the⏎broader healthcare ecosystem to advance the responsible, and eective deployment of AI in⏎clinical care, ensuring these transformative technologies deliver tangible improvements for⏎patients and providers alike.⏎We are eager to collaborate further and contribute to the ongoing dialogue. For any inquiries⏎or additional information, please reach out to Damika Barr at govaairs@abridge.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Tim Hwang⏎General Counsel, Abridge⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0175♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Marquardt, LPC, Christopher, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Marquardt, LPC♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I don't think this is advisable. I think keeping as much of the therapeutic process between a therapist and their client is best practice. Trust, confidentiality and attachments are the hallmarks of any therapeutic technique; the more technology becomes a part of this process the less likely their confidentiality or privacy can be ensured. ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0176♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Dental Standards Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dental Standards Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Supplemental Detail (Expanded Comment from DSI)⏎To: Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Submitted via: Regulations.gov⏎Re: “HHS Health Sector AI RFI” (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Introduction⏎On behalf of the Dental Standards Institute (DSI), we appreciate the opportunity to respond to⏎this Request for Information (RFI) regarding the acceleration of artificial intelligence (AI)⏎adoption and use as part of clinical care (2025-23641).⏎DSI is a standards development organization focused on emerging technology, interoperability,⏎and patient-centered care in dentistry. Historically, dentistry lacked a unified, technically focused⏎standards authority aligned with modern health IT and interoperability priorities. DSI was formed⏎to fill this gap and has already made substantial progress in advancing the foundational⏎standards required for dental interoperability, patient-centered data portability, and responsible⏎technology adoption.⏎Information about DSI is available at dentalstandardsinstitute.com.⏎HHS has a unique opportunity to ensure dentistry is not excluded from national AI policy and⏎infrastructure development. Dentistry is one of the largest, most frequently accessed clinical⏎domains in the United States,yet it remains structurally disconnected from the broader⏎healthcare data ecosystem. AI adoption will not succeed at scale without trust infrastructure,⏎including interoperability, standardized data capture, independent validation, and clear⏎accountability structures.⏎Overarching Position⏎AI can improve health outcomes, reduce administrative burden, and expand access to care.⏎However, accelerated AI adoption without standards and transparency risks producing a⏎⏎fragmented ecosystem of opaque tools, inconsistent performance claims, and avoidable patient⏎harm.⏎Dentistry is particularly sensitive to these risks because:⏎● diagnostic coding is inconsistently used⏎● imaging workflows vary widely by practice and device⏎● clinical documentation remains largely unstructured⏎● dental and medical records remain disconnected⏎● vendor validation is often self-reported and difficult to compare across tools⏎For dentistry to contribute meaningfully to national AI objectives, HHS must prioritize policies⏎that reinforce human oversight, independent validation, and interoperability, while aligning⏎reimbursement structures to support diagnosis-based care.⏎Key Recommendations to HHS⏎1. Clinical AI Must Be Decision-Support, Not Decision-Substitution⏎DSI strongly urges HHS to reinforce that AI used in clinical care must remain decision-support,⏎not decision-substitution.⏎Dentistry is not ready for autonomous clinical AI. Even where AI tools demonstrate strong⏎performance in controlled settings, real-world deployment introduces risks including imaging⏎variation, bias, drift, and workflow overreliance. The most dangerous failure mode is silent⏎normalization, where AI output becomes treated as clinical truth without sufficient human⏎review.⏎HHS should establish clear expectations that:⏎● human review remains mandatory for AI outputs affecting diagnosis, treatment planning,⏎or documentation⏎● AI-generated recommendations must be traceable, auditable, and version-controlled⏎● accountability remains clear: clinicians and organizations remain responsible for clinical⏎decisions⏎● post-deployment monitoring must be expected, not optional⏎Accelerating adoption without reinforcing human oversight will erode public trust and increase⏎preventable patient harm.⏎2. Dentistry’s Treatment-Based Structure Limits AI: Diagnosis Must Become Standard⏎and Reimbursable⏎⏎Dentistry has historically been treatment-driven rather than diagnosis-driven. Unlike medicine,⏎dentistry has not consistently implemented diagnostic coding in routine practice, and the⏎reimbursement ecosystem has not strongly incentivized diagnosis capture.⏎This structural issue is a major barrier to safe, scalable AI adoption.⏎AI systems depend on structured diagnostic labeling to train, validate, and improve. When a⏎clinical domain operates primarily through procedure documentation, models risk being⏎optimized for predicting treatment patterns rather than reflecting clinical truth and patient⏎outcomes.⏎Additionally, the lack of standardized diagnosis capture limits dentistry’s ability to participate in:⏎● integrated oral-systemic care⏎● longitudinal chronic disease management⏎● value-based care models⏎● research and real-world evidence generation⏎● cross-disciplinary AI decision support⏎DSI recommends that HHS, including CMS, prioritize policies that accelerate diagnosis-based⏎dentistry by supporting:⏎● routine diagnostic coding in dental workflows⏎● adoption of standardized diagnostic terminologies⏎● reimbursement models that recognize diagnosis as a reimbursable clinical act⏎If dentistry is expected to contribute to national AI-enabled clinical care goals, it must be⏎structurally enabled to document diagnosis in a consistent, interoperable manner.⏎3. Independent External Validation Must Become a Norm for Dental AI Tools⏎A major barrier to trust and adoption of dental AI tools is the absence of consistent independent⏎validation.⏎Most dental AI products today rely on:⏎● proprietary datasets⏎● internal testing protocols⏎● limited transparency into training populations⏎● non-standard reporting of sensitivity/specificity⏎● inconsistent disclosure of subgroup performance⏎● unclear post-deployment drift monitoring⏎Self-validation is not validation.⏎⏎HHS should prioritize efforts that support the creation and scaling of independent benchmarking⏎and validation frameworks for AI tools used in clinical care, including dentistry. Such efforts⏎could include:⏎● third-party audit mechanisms⏎● standardized evaluation metrics and reporting requirements⏎● independent testing datasets representing diverse populations and real-world imaging⏎variability⏎● mandatory subgroup performance reporting⏎● post-deployment monitoring expectations and drift detection requirements⏎This aligns directly with HHS’s emphasis on evaluation methods, robustness testing, and⏎governance structures that promote trust (2025-23641).⏎4. Interoperability is the Primary Bottleneck to High-Value AI in Dentistry⏎HHS asks directly where enhanced interoperability would widen market opportunities and⏎accelerate AI development (2025-23641). Dentistry is a clear answer.⏎The most valuable AI use cases in dentistry, those that improve systemic outcomes and reduce⏎total cost of care, depend on longitudinal data exchange between dental and medical systems.⏎However, dental data remains highly fragmented across practice management systems,⏎imaging systems, payer workflows, and unstructured clinical notes.⏎Without interoperability, AI will remain limited to isolated point solutions rather than contributing⏎to true healthcare transformation.⏎DSI recommends HHS prioritize interoperability policies that explicitly include dentistry,⏎including support for:⏎● structured oral health data exchange standards⏎● dental imaging interoperability and metadata normalization⏎● consistent capture and exchange of diagnostic findings⏎● alignment between dental standards and broader medical exchange frameworks⏎(including FHIR-based infrastructure)⏎● patient-centered portability of dental records⏎DSI has already advanced work in dental interoperability and patient-centered data portability,⏎and we encourage HHS to support and accelerate national adoption of these standards-based⏎approaches.⏎5. HHS Should Support a National Dental AI Standards and Validation Partnership⏎⏎Dentistry historically lacked a unified infrastructure for AI governance, interoperability alignment,⏎and standardized evaluation. DSI was formed to address this gap and has already begun⏎advancing the foundational work required to support responsible AI adoption in dentistry.⏎We recommend HHS/ONC support and accelerate the development of a national dental AI trust⏎framework, including:⏎● standardized validation datasets across multiple imaging devices and clinical settings⏎● third-party testing and certification mechanisms⏎● minimum transparency requirements for dental AI vendors⏎● standardized performance reporting formats⏎● audit log requirements for AI recommendations in clinical workflows⏎● guidance for post-deployment monitoring and model drift surveillance⏎This framework should support private sector innovation while ensuring consistent trust,⏎accountability, and patient safety.⏎DSI stands ready to convene clinicians, DSOs, vendors, payers, and patient advocates to⏎support HHS in defining and operationalizing this infrastructure.⏎6. Standardized Transparency Disclosures (“AI Nutrition Labels”) Should Be Encouraged⏎A practical and high-impact policy step would be to encourage standardized disclosures for AI⏎tools used in clinical care.⏎DSI recommends that HHS promote a consistent “AI nutrition label” disclosure format including:⏎● intended use and clinical scope⏎● dataset provenance and size⏎● demographic distribution of training/testing sets⏎● performance metrics by subgroup⏎● known failure conditions and limitations⏎● model update frequency and version control approach⏎● drift monitoring approach⏎● documentation requirements for human review⏎Such disclosures would reduce uncertainty for purchasers, support responsible adoption, and⏎increase public trust.⏎7. Dentistry is a High-Leverage Domain for Prevention and Oral-Systemic Health⏎Integration⏎Dentistry represents a major opportunity to strengthen national prevention strategy.⏎⏎Oral inflammation and dental disease correlate strongly with broader chronic disease risk.⏎Dental visits are often more frequent than physician visits for many Americans, meaning⏎dentistry is a critical clinical setting for early risk detection and prevention workflows.⏎If AI is responsibly implemented, with interoperability, diagnostic modernization, and⏎independent validation, dentistry can serve as a high-leverage entry point for improving⏎systemic health outcomes while reducing downstream healthcare costs.⏎Responses Aligned to HHS Specific Questions⏎The recommendations above align directly with multiple questions posed in the RFI (2025-⏎23641).⏎Question 1: Biggest barriers to innovation and adoption⏎● limited interoperability of dental data⏎● lack of consistent diagnostic coding adoption⏎● inconsistent imaging workflows and device variability⏎● opaque vendor validation and lack of independent benchmarking⏎● unclear accountability and auditability expectations⏎Question 2: Regulatory, payment policy, and programmatic changes⏎● accelerate diagnostic coding adoption in dentistry⏎● modernize reimbursement incentives to support diagnosis capture⏎● expand interoperability priorities to include dentistry explicitly⏎● establish clear expectations for auditability and human oversight⏎Question 3: Governance and accountability issues⏎● clinicians remain accountable for clinical decisions⏎● AI recommendations must be logged and auditable⏎● liability and indemnification expectations must remain clear⏎Question 4: Evaluation methods and robustness testing⏎● independent benchmarking datasets⏎● standardized subgroup reporting⏎● real-world multi-site testing⏎● post-deployment performance monitoring and drift detection⏎Question 5: Accreditation, certification, and testing⏎● support third-party testing ecosystems⏎● encourage industry-wide certification models⏎⏎● tie incentives to independently validated performance, not marketing claims⏎Question 8: Interoperability opportunities⏎● structured oral health data exchange⏎● dental imaging interoperability and metadata standards⏎● diagnostic representation and portability⏎● alignment with broader medical interoperability frameworks⏎Conclusion⏎HHS has a timely opportunity to accelerate AI adoption in a way that improves outcomes,⏎reduces burden, and strengthens public trust. Dentistry must be explicitly included in this effort.⏎AI adoption will not scale safely without trust infrastructure: interoperability standards, diagnostic⏎modernization, independent validation, and clear accountability. Dentistry historically lacked⏎unified leadership in these areas. DSI was formed to fill this gap and has already advanced⏎foundational work in interoperability, patient-centered record portability, and calibration efforts⏎that support consistent clinical and payer-facing workflows.⏎DSI urges HHS to prioritize:⏎1. mandatory human oversight and accountability⏎2. modernization of diagnosis capture and reimbursement alignment in dentistry⏎3. independent external validation frameworks for dental AI tools⏎4. dental interoperability as a prerequisite for scalable clinical AI⏎5. standardized transparency disclosures for AI tools⏎6. real-world monitoring expectations for deployed AI systems⏎DSI welcomes the opportunity to collaborate with HHS/ONC, CMS, NIH, and FDA to accelerate⏎responsible AI adoption in dentistry and to ensure dentistry becomes an integrated contributor⏎to national health outcomes.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Dental Standards Institute (DSI)⏎joindsi.com | dentalstandardsinstitute.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0177♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Citrix Systems, Inc., a Cloud Software Group company, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Citrix Systems, Inc., a Cloud Software Group company♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎To: Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎From: Citrix Systems, Inc., a Cloud Software Group company⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎Subject: Comment on RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎I. Executive Summary: The Infrastructure for AI Readiness⏎While much of the healthcare industry's focus remains on the development of AI-⏎powered applications, Citrix identifies the primary bottleneck to adoption as the⏎deployment and delivery layer. In a healthcare environment characterized by shared⏎workstations, mobile clinicians, and strict HIPAA requirements, an AI tool is only as⏎effective as its availability at the point of care.⏎Citrix provides an industry leading platform that serves as a digital foundation for many⏎healthcare organizations in support of delivering secure, connected healthcare services.⏎With a footprint that includes a majority of the largest healthcare organizations in the⏎United States and over 4,900 healthcare customers worldwide, our role is to provide the⏎governance layer and platform that allows healthcare organizations to transition from AI⏎experimentation to large-scale clinical use without compromising security or⏎productivity. Our platform also helps remove friction at the bedside to ensure that⏎technology works invisibly in the background, allowing care teams to stay focused on⏎patients.⏎II. Response to Specific RFI Questions (1,3,5,6)⏎Q1: What are the primary barriers to the adoption of AI in clinical settings?⏎Citrix identifies the primary barrier to AI adoption as a deployment gap — the technical⏎and operational friction that prevents an AI insight from reaching a clinician at the point⏎of care.⏎To overcome this, HHS should address the following structural barriers:⏎● The "Last Mile" delivery problem: High-performing AI models have low adoption⏎in clinical settings because they are not integrated into the roaming workflows of a⏎hospital. In environments dominated by shared workstations, a clinician cannot⏎afford the "cognitive tax" of logging into a separate, disconnected AI application.⏎Citrix Systems Inc.,⏎851 W Cypress Creek Rd, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33309⏎⏎● Compute and hardware disparity: Modern clinical AI agents require significant⏎computational power. Most healthcare systems are burdened by "technical debt,"⏎utilizing legacy endpoints that cannot natively support resource-heavy AI⏎processing.⏎● Provider abrasion and "Login Fatigue": Clinicians are facing record levels of⏎burnout. If an AI tool adds even 30 seconds to a workflow or requires additional⏎authentication events, it will be bypassed.⏎● Governance-Induced latency: The traditional process for vetting and provisioning⏎new software in a hospital takes months, preventing clinical teams from iterating on⏎AI tools at the speed of innovation.⏎Q1 Recommendation:⏎HHS should prioritize frameworks that support unified workspace delivery. By⏎decoupling AI applications from physical hardware constraints; this allows healthcare⏎organizations to scale innovation instantly across legacy infrastructure while eliminating⏎clinician "login fatigue" through seamless, governed workflow integration at the point of⏎care.⏎Q3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance⏎and accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy,⏎and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist, and what role, if⏎any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The AI regulatory and legal framework is rapidly evolving, fragmented across US state⏎regulations, as well as global regulations that have extraterritorial applicability such as⏎the EU AI Act. Technology vendors provide the AI capabilities and are regulated as⏎such, but the extent to how those AI capabilities are deployed and used, together with⏎the associated legal and regulatory risks, at its core rests with the legal, security and⏎compliance leadership teams within the individual healthcare organizations.⏎Fundamentally, it is the type of tools and technologies that should be implemented that⏎HHS can best help influence organizations to meet their regulatory, legal and customer⏎obligations.⏎Given the pace of change, the guidance should be technology agnostic to⏎accommodate the unknown. Insofar as the implementation aspect of AI is concerned,⏎the primary challenge for non-medical device AI is the "Shadow AI" security gap and⏎subsequent data drift, where clinicians may input Protected Health Information (PHI)⏎into unauthorized public AI models. This not only leads to sensitive data residing on⏎unmanaged endpoints but also creates the risk that these public models could learn⏎Citrix Systems Inc.,⏎851 W Cypress Creek Rd, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33309⏎⏎from and retain PHI, potentially compromising patient privacy. To address this, robust⏎controls must be implemented to prevent the use of public AI models within the⏎healthcare workplace, ensuring that only approved, governed AI tools are utilized for⏎clinical purposes.⏎To address these risks, Citrix recommends that HHS prioritize a Zero Trust Access⏎framework that allows Risk & Resilience Leaders (CISOs) to quantify risk reduction in⏎an objective manner that will support their compliance and financial objectives, including⏎objective and verifiable data points that are required by the insurance market to⏎underwrite the associated risks.⏎• Granular, Role-Based Governance for AI Identities: HHS should encourage⏎adaptive, role based, and context aware access policies where AI availability and⏎scope of permitted use is tied strictly to a user’s clinical role and context aware⏎data loss prevention (DLP) rules. Since 71% of CISOs report that AI has access⏎to core systems, but only 16% govern that access effectively, Citrix recommends⏎a governed environment that enforces least-privilege for AI "identities" to prevent⏎unauthorized data exfiltration together with DLP rules that limit activities such as⏎printing, pasting or downloading of sensitive data.⏎• Improving MTTR and MTTC Metrics: Underwriters now demand proof of strict⏎controls to offer competitive coverage. Citrix suggests the use of virtualized,⏎governed sessions that enable IT to:⏎o Reduce Mean Time to Contain (MTTC): Quarantine unstable or⏎compromised devices within 30 minutes of threat detection.⏎o Improve Mean Time to Respond (MTTR): Slashing response times from⏎hours to minutes using automated session isolation and lockout for risky⏎behavior.⏎• Standardized Patching Cycles: To lower premiums and justify deductibles,⏎organizations must prove cybersecurity maturity through patching cycle time. By⏎centralizing AI application delivery, IT leaders can automate policy updates,⏎ensuring that the clinical ecosystem is hardened against attack precursors⏎without manual deskside toil.⏎• Quantifiable Risk Posture: Citrix recommends that HHS incentivize the use of⏎Agentic AI Posture Scores and Cyber Risk Quantification (CRQ). By⏎containing AI processing within hosted sessions, hospitals can demonstrate a⏎measurable reduction in "probable loss" (e.g., from $5M to $1M) by preventing⏎PHI from ever residing on local, unmanaged endpoints.⏎Citrix Systems Inc.,⏎851 W Cypress Creek Rd, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33309⏎⏎• Unified Forensic Auditability: Evaluating liability is a fact specific analysis and⏎requires a "provable" audit trail. Citrix recommends that HHS prioritize policies⏎supporting unified telemetry that tracks AI interactions across all devices. This⏎allows compliance teams to cut audit preparation time from 10 days to 2 days,⏎providing board-ready reporting that supports indemnification and insurance⏎negotiations.⏎Q3 Recommendation:⏎HHS should prioritize the adoption of a Zero Trust Access framework that enforces⏎context and role-based governance and unified telemetry for clinical AI. By delivering AI⏎tools through a governed, virtualized environment rather than on local endpoints,⏎healthcare organizations can effectively minimize the "Shadow AI" security gap, prevent⏎PHI data drift, and improve quantifiable risk metrics—such as Mean Time to Contain⏎(MTTC)—which are essential for maintaining cyber-insurance eligibility and⏎organizational resilience.⏎Q5. What type of workforce, skills, and resources (e.g., educational and training,⏎financial, etc.) are needed to enable and support the adoption and use of AI in⏎clinical care? How can HHS support these needs?⏎The successful adoption of clinical AI requires a workforce shift from traditional "break-⏎fix" IT to Clinical AI-Ops. Currently, healthcare IT teams are overburdened with manual⏎system maintenance, particularly on securing traditional PC endpoints, leaving little⏎capacity for the specialized oversight and optimization that AI requires. Citrix⏎recommends the following workforce and resource priorities:⏎• Transitioning IT to AI orchestrators: The workforce needs a new class of⏎professionals who understand both clinical workflow and the delivery of high-⏎compute AI models.⏎• Reclaiming workforce capacity through automation: IT staff cannot develop⏎new AI skills while buried under high ticket volumes and manual patching.⏎• Continuous enablement via telemetry: Training should not be a one-time⏎event, but a continuous process driven by real-world usage data.⏎Q5 Recommendation:⏎HHS should leverage existing high-standard technical training infrastructures to⏎establish a National Clinical AI-Ops Certification, while simultaneously incentivizing the⏎use of operational excellence automation to reclaim the IT workforce capacity⏎necessary to support and orchestrate AI innovation at scale.⏎Citrix Systems Inc.,⏎851 W Cypress Creek Rd, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33309⏎⏎Q6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations, and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give⏎new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎While many AI tools exhibit high accuracy in controlled environments, they often fall⏎short in clinical practice due to high abrasion and the inability to maintain performance⏎across a hospital’s disparate hardware.⏎To ensure AI meets both performance and cost expectations, Citrix recommends that⏎HHS prioritize metrics centered on workflow integration rather than just algorithmic⏎accuracy.⏎● Addressing performance shortfalls through "Time-to-Chart": AI performance in⏎clinical care should be measured by its impact on the clinician’s entire session, not⏎just the tool's isolated response time.⏎● Novel AI potential: The greatest potential for cost reduction and outcome⏎improvement lies in AI that acts as an invisible background assistant rather than a⏎standalone application.⏎● Cost-effectiveness via infrastructure optimization: Many AI tools fail cost⏎expectations because they may require expensive local hardware upgrades.⏎Q6 Recommendation: HHS should define AI performance and cost-effectiveness⏎through the lens of Clinical Throughput; by prioritizing novel AI that is decoupled from⏎hardware and integrated into roaming workspaces, HHS can ensure that innovation⏎reduces provider burden and lowers healthcare costs without requiring costly⏎infrastructure overhauls.⏎III. Proposed strategic framework for HHS⏎To successfully transition clinical AI from isolated pilots to a national standard, Citrix⏎recommends that HHS prioritize a regulatory and reimbursement framework built on⏎three interdependent pillars: User Experience, Tailored Security, and Operational⏎Excellence. This framework is essential because even the most advanced AI model will⏎fail if it is not accessible within the high-pressure, mobile environment of a hospital.⏎User Experience must be the primary driver of adoption; AI insights must be⏎integrated into a roaming workspace that ensures sub-10-second access at the⏎bedside, preventing the "login fatigue" that contributes to clinician burnout.⏎Tailored Security must provide the necessary guardrails through role-based⏎access and governed sessions, effectively closing the "Shadow AI" gap and⏎preventing data drift without introducing workflow friction.⏎Citrix Systems Inc.,⏎851 W Cypress Creek Rd, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33309⏎⏎Finally, Operational Excellence serves as the engine for workforce⏎transformation. By automating the "manual toil" of infrastructure management⏎such as patching and image delivery, healthcare systems can reclaim the IT⏎capacity required to orchestrate and scale AI innovation. Together, these pillars⏎form the Digital Foundation necessary for a resilient, AI-native healthcare⏎ecosystem.⏎IV. Conclusion: Advancing secure, connected care⏎Citrix is committed to providing the digital foundation required to support a modern, AI-⏎native healthcare system while protecting and enabling the data it needs to serve our⏎communities. Our mission of secure, connected care focuses on reducing the fatigue⏎and frustration of clinical teams by ensuring that technology operates invisibly and⏎reliably in the background.⏎We believe that the success of accelerating AI in healthcare depends not only on the⏎accuracy of clinical models but on a resilient and scalable delivery architecture that⏎protects patient privacy while giving time back to the clinician. We recommend that HHS⏎partner with industry leaders to define the national standards for Clinical AI-Ops,⏎prioritizing speed, security, and stability as essential for the next generation of American⏎clinical care.⏎V. Next Steps and Expert Consultation⏎Citrix welcomes the opportunity to further discuss these recommendations and share⏎additional technical evidence regarding the orchestration of clinical AI at scale.⏎We invite HHS leadership to engage in a specialized briefing with our Healthcare⏎Strategic Advisors and subject matter experts to explore how the Citrix platform can⏎support the department’s national objectives.⏎To schedule a follow-up discussion or a technical deep dive into these frameworks,⏎please contact our Healthcare Strategy team at sarah.fink@citrix.com⏎Citrix Systems, Inc., a Cloud Software Group company provides a secure app and⏎desktop delivery platform that companies of all sizes can use to enable secure, flexible⏎work. With Citrix, employees can work where and how they prefer, and IT can be⏎confident their information and devices remain safe. Click here to learn more about⏎Citrix solutions and the value they can provide.⏎For more information on the Citrix platform, please visit www.citrix.com.⏎Citrix Systems Inc.,⏎851 W Cypress Creek Rd, Fort Lauderdale, FL 33309♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0178♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from InStride Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔InStride Health♔♔♔♔♔♔InStride Health Response to HHS RFI: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎About InStride Health⏎InStride Health is a multi-state pediatric mental health provider delivering specialty care for⏎children, adolescents, and young adults with complex anxiety and obsessive-compulsive⏎disorder. As a provider organization directly accountable for clinical outcomes, licensure, and⏎patient safety, we engage with AI tools as clinical decision support and operational support,⏎embedded in real-world care delivery.⏎Our comments reflect direct experience implementing and governing AI-supported clinical⏎and operational workflows across fragmented state regulatory environments, particularly in⏎high-risk mental health and pediatric settings, including work examined in a recent case study⏎conducted in collaboration with The Wharton School’s Health Care Management Department.⏎The case study evaluated InStride’s implementation of an AI scribe and documented⏎measurable reductions in clinician documentation burden and improvements in clinician⏎experience through a human-in-the-loop, clinically governed AI deployment model.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎InStride Response:⏎For multi-state healthcare organizations seeking to innovate responsibly, the most immediate⏎barrier to AI innovation is state-by-state regulatory fragmentation combined with the⏎absence of clear federal baseline guidance. Requirements governing consent, data use, and⏎clinical accountability, particularly in mental health and pediatric care, vary significantly across⏎states. Regulations in states such as Illinois impose stricter standards that often necessitate⏎designing to the most restrictive interpretation or managing complex, state-specific⏎implementations. Without a consistent federal framework for AI use in clinical care, this⏎fragmentation significantly increases development cost, slows testing, and impedes responsible⏎scaling.⏎A second major barrier is uncertainty around accountability and liability when AI supports⏎clinical workflows. Existing clinical, regulatory, and legal frameworks were designed for human⏎decision-making and have not yet been clearly adapted to AI-supported care. Many AI tools⏎⏎influence clinical judgment without being regulated as medical devices, and vendor contracts⏎typically place responsibility entirely on providers. In the absence of clear federal guidance on⏎how accountability is assigned in these scenarios, organizations that prioritize patient safety⏎must limit experimentation and narrowly scope AI use cases, regardless of technical readiness.⏎An additional and increasingly salient barrier to adoption that we’ve observed is concern⏎among clinicians about the energy intensity and environmental impact of AI⏎technologies. Clinicians are becoming more aware of the computational and energy demands⏎associated with certain AI applications, and these concerns can meaningfully limit willingness to⏎adopt or expand AI use in care delivery settings. In the absence of shared standards,⏎transparency, or best practices regarding energy-efficient AI deployment, organizations are left⏎to navigate these tradeoffs independently. HHS could play an important coordinating role in⏎advancing guidance, shared learning, and innovation around lower-energy AI approaches or⏎offsetting efficiency gains, helping ensure that responsible AI adoption aligns with broader⏎sustainability goals in healthcare.⏎Collectively, these barriers reflect the need for clearer, more consistent federal frameworks that⏎enable innovation while maintaining accountability, patient safety, environmental responsibility,⏎and public trust.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes⏎should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care⏎and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited⏎to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide⏎specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎InStride Response:⏎HHS should prioritize regulatory and programmatic approaches that promote responsible AI⏎use in clinical care, where AI supports but does not replace human clinical judgment.⏎Clear federal guidance that distinguishes decision-support tools from autonomous clinical⏎decision-making would reduce uncertainty and enable providers to innovate with confidence⏎while maintaining patient safety.⏎Regulatory expectations should explicitly establish that AI-generated outputs are advisory,⏎require human review, and do not shift accountability away from licensed professionals. This⏎clarity is particularly important for multi-state providers, as consistent federal guidance would⏎help mitigate fragmented state-level interpretations that currently complicate responsible AI⏎design and deployment.⏎From a payment policy perspective, HHS should align incentives with demonstrated⏎improvements in care quality, safety, and access, rather than narrowly rewarding efficiency⏎or throughput. In this context, care quality should be defined by clinically meaningful outcomes⏎⏎and care processes, such as symptom improvement measured through validated clinical scales,⏎appropriate level-of-care matching, timely access to services, treatment engagement and⏎retention, continuity of care, and reductions in safety events or avoidable escalation. Many AI⏎applications that meaningfully reduce clinician burden or improve continuity of care lack clear⏎reimbursement pathways, limiting investment in higher-value use cases. Payment models that⏎reward outcomes rather than prescriptive processes would give providers flexibility to adopt AI⏎tools that support high-quality care while maintaining appropriate human oversight.⏎Outcome-aligned payment models would encourage innovation that benefits patients while⏎discouraging premature deployment of unproven or overly expansive tools.⏎HHS could more effectively accelerate responsible adoption by expanding value-based and⏎outcomes-oriented reimbursement models that allow providers to determine how best to deliver⏎care. Such approaches would create durable incentives for providers to evaluate, adopt, and⏎govern AI tools based on real-world clinical impact, while preserving accountability and public⏎trust.⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues⏎exist and what role should HHS play to help address them?⏎InStride Response:⏎AI used in clinical workflows raises novel legal and implementation challenges because it⏎increasingly influences care without fitting neatly into existing regulatory categories. Many AI⏎tools are neither regulated medical devices nor purely administrative systems, yet they shape⏎how clinicians interpret information, prioritize tasks, and frame clinical decisions.⏎This creates a structural accountability gap. In practice, vendor contracts typically disclaim⏎clinical liability, while clinicians and provider organizations remain fully responsible for care⏎decisions. At the same time, existing legal and regulatory frameworks provide limited guidance⏎on the appropriate scope, safeguards, and expectations for AI-supported workflows. The result⏎is a system in which responsibility is concentrated on providers, but the rules governing⏎acceptable AI use remain underdeveloped.⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to address this gap by clarifying accountability structures without⏎shifting clinical responsibility away from licensed professionals. This includes reinforcing that⏎clinicians remain responsible for care decisions, while also establishing clear federal⏎expectations around permissible AI use, required human oversight, and baseline standards that⏎AI tools must meet when used in clinical contexts. Such guidance would enable both providers⏎and innovators to design within well-defined boundaries, reduce uncertainty, and support⏎responsible innovation while maintaining patient safety and public trust.⏎⏎4. What are the most promising AI evaluation methods for clinical care, and⏎should HHS support them?⏎InStride Response:⏎Promising AI evaluation methods must extend beyond technical accuracy to include⏎human-centered and workflow-based measures. In clinical care, safety and effectiveness⏎depend not only on model performance, but on how AI affects clinician judgment, introduces or⏎mitigates bias, and interacts with real-world workflows.⏎Effective evaluation should include:⏎● Pre-deployment testing in representative clinical settings⏎● Post-deployment monitoring for drift and unintended consequences⏎● Assessment of impact on care quality, safety, and patient experience⏎HHS support for applied evaluation frameworks, through grants, cooperative agreements, or⏎public-private partnerships, would help standardize responsible AI deployment and accelerate⏎learning across the healthcare system.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎InStride Response:⏎HHS can best support innovative and effective AI use by encouraging use-case–specific,⏎industry-driven validation frameworks rather than one-size-fits-all certification requirements.⏎AI used in clinical care varies widely in risk and impact, from administrative support to decision⏎preparation, and validation approaches should reflect that variation.⏎Rather than focusing exclusively on model-level certification, HHS should support frameworks⏎that assess:⏎● intended use and scope,⏎● required level of human oversight,⏎● integration into clinical workflows,⏎● and ongoing monitoring after deployment.⏎Supporting industry-led validation efforts, informed by providers and grounded in real-world care⏎delivery, would promote safety and trust without slowing innovation. This approach would also⏎allow standards to evolve alongside technology, rather than hard-coding requirements that⏎quickly become outdated.⏎⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded⏎performance and cost expectations, and where have they fallen short?⏎What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve⏎health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎InStride Response:⏎AI has met or exceeded expectations in areas that support clinicians and enhance⏎decision-making, including administrative efficiency, documentation support, information⏎synthesis, workflow coordination, and clinical decision support. In certain well-defined use⏎cases, such as matching patients to appropriate levels of care or identifying clinical fit, AI tools⏎can perform at or above human benchmarks. When designed appropriately, these applications⏎reduce cognitive and administrative burden and allow clinicians to operate at the top of their⏎licenses, spending more time on clinical judgment, patient engagement, and care delivery⏎rather than clerical tasks.⏎Challenges arise not from AI’s analytical capability, but from how decision-support outputs⏎are framed, scoped, and integrated into clinical workflows. AI tools can introduce risk when⏎outputs are presented without appropriate context, limitations, or uncertainty signals, or when⏎they are applied beyond the conditions under which they were validated. In complex and⏎nuanced care scenarios such as mental health, where therapeutic relationships, patient history,⏎and developmental context are central, overgeneralization or overconfidence in AI outputs can⏎unintentionally influence clinical judgment.⏎The most promising AI tools are those that:⏎● operate within clearly defined, bounded workflows aligned to validated use cases,⏎● make uncertainty, limitations, and confidence levels explicit, and⏎● are designed to be reviewed, contextualized, and integrated by clinicians as part of⏎decision-making, rather than used in isolation.⏎AI tools built with these principles have the greatest potential to improve outcomes, enhance⏎care quality, and reduce costs while preserving clinical autonomy, patient safety, and trust.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical⏎care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in⏎clinical care?⏎InStride Response:⏎⏎AI adoption decisions in healthcare are influenced by a combination of clinical leadership,⏎compliance and risk management, IT and security teams, and executive leadership. Successful⏎adoption requires alignment across these groups, as each brings a distinct perspective on⏎patient safety, operational feasibility, regulatory risk, and organizational priorities.⏎Primary administrative hurdles include:⏎● uncertainty around liability and accountability when AI supports clinical workflows,⏎● challenges integrating AI into existing clinical workflows in ways that support clinical⏎judgment and care delivery without increasing cognitive burden or disrupting patient⏎care, and⏎● lack of clear regulatory guidance that leadership teams can rely on when making⏎adoption decisions across multiple jurisdictions.⏎Clear federal expectations around permissible scope, required oversight, and accountability for⏎AI-supported care would materially reduce internal friction. Such guidance would allow⏎organizations to evaluate AI tools based on clinical and operational value, rather than perceived⏎regulatory or legal risk, and would support more confident, responsible adoption.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please⏎consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎InStride Response:⏎Enhanced interoperability would most effectively accelerate AI development when it enables⏎reliable, well-governed access to clinically meaningful data, rather than simply increasing⏎data volume. For care delivery organizations, the greatest value lies in interoperable access to⏎structured clinical data, longitudinal patient histories, outcomes data, and operational signals⏎that reflect real-world care processes.⏎Standardization that preserves data provenance, context, and auditability is particularly⏎important for AI used in clinical workflows. Without clarity on how data were generated,⏎transformed, and applied, AI outputs can be difficult to evaluate, validate, or trust.⏎Interoperability efforts that include benchmarking tools and shared evaluation standards would⏎further support responsible AI development by enabling comparison across populations,⏎settings, and time.⏎HHS can play a critical role by advancing interoperability frameworks that support safe,⏎transparent, and accountable AI use, ensuring that data exchange accelerates innovation while⏎maintaining patient trust and clinical integrity.⏎⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to⏎see addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what⏎concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use⏎of AI in clinical care?⏎InStride Response:⏎Patients and caregivers want AI to address practical, day-to-day challenges in healthcare,⏎including difficulty accessing care, long wait times, administrative complexity, and fragmented⏎care experiences. AI that helps clinicians spend more time with patients and less time on⏎administrative tasks aligns closely with these expectations.⏎At the same time, patients and caregivers express concerns about privacy, accuracy,⏎transparency, and the risk that AI could replace human judgment or weaken therapeutic⏎relationships. These concerns are especially pronounced in mental health care for children and⏎adolescents, where trust, developmental context, and continuity of care are central to effective⏎treatment.⏎Responsible AI adoption must therefore prioritize clear communication about how AI is used,⏎strong human oversight, and safeguards that reinforce, rather than diminish the human⏎elements of care that patients and families value most.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part⏎of clinical care?⏎InStride Response:⏎HHS should prioritize applied, real-world research that examines how AI performs when⏎embedded in actual care delivery environments. In particular, research is needed on the impact⏎of AI-supported workflows on clinical decision-making, patient outcomes, clinician workload, and⏎equity over time.⏎Mental health and pediatric populations warrant focused attention, as risks, benefits, and ethical⏎considerations differ meaningfully from adult medical care. Longitudinal studies that assess⏎safety, bias, and durability of AI performance are essential to building trust and informing⏎responsible scaling.⏎⏎While the existing literature typically focuses on technical performance or theoretical cost⏎savings, greater emphasis is needed on implementation science in terms of how AI tools⏎change care processes, redistribute work, and affect quality and access in practice.⏎Public-private partnerships and pragmatic trials embedded in care settings would help translate⏎innovation into measurable clinical benefit.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0179♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Health AI Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Health AI Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Cover Note⏎Health AI Institute respectfully submits the attached comment in response to the HHS Request⏎for Information, Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care (FR Doc. 2025-23641).⏎Our comments are informed by applied experience evaluating and implementing AI in clinical⏎settings, convening stakeholders across the health care ecosystem, and leadership experience⏎spanning federal health services research, clinical informatics, health system operations, and AI⏎technology development. We focus on system-level actions HHS can take to reduce adoption⏎barriers, align incentives, and support evidence-to-practice translation while maintaining patient⏎safety and public trust.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to contribute and welcome continued engagement with HHS,⏎ASTP/ONC, CMS, and FDA on these issues.⏎Submitted on behalf of:⏎Health AI Institute⏎_____________________________________________________________⏎Response to HHS Request for Information⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎(FR Doc. 2025-23641)⏎Submitted by: Health AI Institute⏎I. Perspective and Context⏎Health AI Institute appreciates the opportunity to provide input on how HHS can accelerate the adoption⏎and effective use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. We support HHS’s goal of enabling⏎innovation while protecting patients, civil rights, and public trust.⏎Our perspective is grounded in applied, real-world experience. Health AI Institute engages across the⏎health AI lifecycle, including evaluation of AI tools in clinical environments; convening health systems,⏎payers, developers, patients, and regulators; and advising organizations responsible for governance and⏎deployment. Our leadership and affiliates include former federal health services research leadership⏎(including AHRQ), CMIOs, AI technologists, and health system executives responsible for⏎implementation at scale. This vantage point allows us to observe not only whether AI works, but why⏎adoption succeeds or fails in practice.⏎Across these engagements, we consistently find that AI adoption is constrained less by AI’s technical⏎capability than by misalignment among regulation, reimbursement, evidence generation, data⏎infrastructure, and clinical workflows.⏎Health AI Institute⏎Website: https://healthai.institute/⏎LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/health-ai-institute⏎⏎II. Cross-Cutting Barriers to AI Adoption⏎A. Regulatory uncertainty outside FDA-regulated medical devices: While FDA has clarified⏎pathways for AI-enabled medical devices, a growing share of AI used in clinical care falls outside⏎traditional device definitions (e.g., documentation support, care coordination, operational decision⏎support, and generative AI embedded in workflows). Across health systems we engage, legal and⏎compliance teams frequently delay or block deployment not due to known safety risks, but due to⏎uncertainty around accountability, liability, and post-deployment expectations. This uncertainty⏎disproportionately affects smaller and resource-constrained organizations.⏎B. Misaligned payment incentives: In real-world implementations, AI often delivers value through⏎cognitive efficiency, prevention, administrative burden reduction, and care coordination—⏎outcomes that are weakly rewarded under fee-for-service payment.⏎We repeatedly observe that the organizations investing in AI are not those capturing its financial⏎benefits, undermining sustainability even when clinical value is evident.⏎C. Evidence gaps focused on deployment, not accuracy: The primary evidence gap is no longer⏎whether AI models can perform discrete tasks, but whether they perform reliably, equitably, and⏎safely over time and across settings. Health care organizations struggle with post-deployment⏎monitoring, bias and drift detection, workflow integration, and economic evaluation.⏎D. Interoperability that is not AI-ready: Despite progress in data exchange, clinical data often⏎lacks the provenance, context, labeling, and longitudinal linkage required for scalable AI⏎evaluation and deployment. Interoperability that enables data movement but not data meaning⏎limits AI impact⏎E. Patient trust and transparency: Patients and caregivers express concerns related to⏎transparency, escalation when AI is wrong, and potential inequities. Addressing these concerns is⏎essential to durable adoption.⏎III. Strategic Recommendations for HHS⏎A. Regulation: prioritize clarity and learning (ASTP/ONC, FDA): HHS should emphasize⏎regulatory clarity and predictability, particularly for non-device AI used in clinical care. This⏎includes publishing cross-agency governance principles, clarifying post-deployment expectations,⏎and supporting time-limited learning environments or safe harbors for well-scoped AI use cases.⏎Reducing uncertainty will accelerate adoption while preserving patient protections.⏎B. Reimbursement: align payment with value (CMS): HHS should modernize payment policies⏎to recognize AI as enabling clinical infrastructure when it demonstrably improves quality, access,⏎or efficiency. High-value applications, including AI-enabled telehealth, remote monitoring, and⏎wearables for chronic disease management, often generate value through predictive insights⏎rather than encounters. Allowing AI costs within value-based models, supporting outcome-linked⏎payment pilots, and enabling temporary reimbursement pathways would materially reduce⏎adoption barriers.⏎C. Research & development: refocus on evidence-to-practice translation: HHS has an⏎opportunity to rebalance AI research toward real-world implementation and learning. Priorities⏎include post-deployment performance monitoring, workflow integration, organizational⏎governance, and economic impact analysis. This learning-oriented approach reflects the legacy of⏎federal health services research and learning health systems and is essential for AI in clinical care.⏎Health AI Institute⏎Website: https://healthai.institute/⏎LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/health-ai-institute⏎⏎D. Interoperable, AI-Ready Data Infrastructure (ASTP/ONC): Accelerating AI adoption⏎requires interoperable data infrastructure beyond exchange alone. AI depends on longitudinal,⏎context-rich data with consistent standards, provenance, and governance across care settings,⏎including patient-generated data. HHS can catalyze progress by aligning interoperability policy⏎with real-world AI use cases and supporting shared benchmarking and evaluation infrastructure.⏎E. Evaluation and trust infrastructure: Rather than certifying individual tools, HHS can catalyze⏎adoption by supporting shared evaluation infrastructure, independent testing ecosystems, and⏎accreditation aligned with federal principles. This approach promotes accountability without⏎constraining innovation.⏎IV. Illustrative Learning-Oriented Federal Actions⏎The following actions illustrate non-prescriptive, learning-oriented approaches HHS could use to reduce⏎risk and accelerate AI adoption in clinical care by aligning regulatory, payment, and evidence-generation⏎levers.⏎1. National Clinical AI Sandbox Program: A time-limited program enabling real-world⏎deployment of AI tools in clinical settings under defined regulatory and reimbursement⏎flexibility, paired with structured evaluation to generate evidence on safety, effectiveness, equity,⏎and workflow integration. (Primary beneficiaries: clinicians, health systems, regulators)⏎2. AI Evaluation Commons: A shared national resource supporting standardized benchmarks, AI-⏎relevant data standards, post-deployment performance metrics, and failure-mode reporting,⏎reducing duplication across health systems and enabling more transparent, comparable AI⏎evaluation. (Primary beneficiaries: health systems, developers, researchers)⏎3. Temporary AI Payment Pathways: Time-limited reimbursement pathways for high-evidence⏎AI tools across care settings within value-based care models, allowing real-world performance,⏎equity, and cost impacts to be evaluated prior to permanent coverage or payment decisions.⏎(Primary beneficiaries: patients, clinicians, value-based care organizations)⏎4. Organizational AI Readiness Grants: Targeted support for health care organizations to build⏎governance structures, clinician training, change management capacity, and human–AI workflow⏎integration necessary for safe and effective AI adoption. (Primary beneficiaries: clinicians,⏎health system leadership)⏎5. Patient Trust and Transparency Initiative: Development of patient-centered norms and tools⏎for disclosure, consent, escalation, and communication when AI is used in clinical care,⏎addressing patient concerns while strengthening public trust and accountability. (Primary⏎beneficiaries: patients and caregivers)⏎V. Conclusion⏎AI can improve outcomes, reduce burden, and enhance productivity across the health care system.⏎Achieving this potential will require coordinated federal action that aligns regulation, reimbursement,⏎research, and trust infrastructure with the realities of clinical care.⏎Health AI Institute appreciates HHS’s leadership and welcomes continued engagement to advance these⏎shared goals.⏎Health AI Institute⏎Website: https://healthai.institute/⏎LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/health-ai-institute⏎⏎About the Health AI Institute (HAI)⏎• The Health AI Institute (HAI) ) provides a nonpartisan, nonpolitical platform where business⏎leaders, technology experts, and industry partners can collaborate with policymakers to develop⏎AI strategies that drive innovation while ensuring responsible governance. Our mission is to⏎foster interdisciplinary collaboration, drive AI-powered innovation, and shape the future of⏎medicine by addressing the most pressing challenges in healthcare. We envision a future where⏎AI-enabled technologies enhance patient care, improve efficiency, and promote health across the⏎U.S. and beyond.⏎Health AI Institute⏎Website: https://healthai.institute/⏎LinkedIn: https://www.linkedin.com/company/health-ai-institute♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0180♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Sleep Medicine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Sleep Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎HHS Health⏎Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information – Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎On behalf of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine (AASM), we appreciate⏎the opportunity to comment on the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical⏎care. As clinicians who diagnose and manage sleep disorders every day, we see⏎both the promise and the practical challenges of AI firsthand. In sleep medicine,⏎AI has the potential to improve diagnostic accuracy, expand access to care, and⏎support earlier identification and treatment of common and costly conditions.⏎However, from a clinician’s perspective, meaningful adoption will only occur if⏎AI tools are reliable, transparent, independently validated, and clearly aligned with⏎patient safety and clinician accountability.⏎These comments reflect the experience of more than 9,000 AASM physician⏎members and the teams working in over 2,400 AASM-accredited sleep programs.⏎Our members care for patients across diverse practice settings, including hospital-⏎based labs, independent sleep centers, and office-based practices, many of which⏎serve Medicare beneficiaries. Across these settings, clinicians consistently⏎emphasize that AI must function as a clinical support tool—not a black box that⏎introduces new risks or uncompensated workload.⏎What are barriers to innovation, and how can HHS support the private sector?⏎Currently, several significant barriers hinder the responsible and sustainable⏎implementation of AI in clinical care.⏎1. Persistent uncertainty regarding medical-legal liability. Clinicians⏎remain unclear whether responsibility for errors, adverse outcomes, or⏎inappropriate recommendations generated by vendor-developed AI tools⏎rests with the treating physician or the technology developer. This⏎ambiguity creates unacceptable professional and malpractice risk,⏎particularly when clinicians have limited insight into how an algorithm was⏎trained, validated, or updated. Without clear allocation of accountability,⏎⏎many physicians are understandably reluctant to rely on AI outputs in clinical decision-⏎making, even when tools may offer potential benefits.⏎2. Lack of transparency and independent validation. Many AI systems function as “black⏎boxes,” providing little or no disclosure regarding training datasets, population⏎representativeness, validation methods, known limitations, or real-world performance⏎across diverse clinical settings. This lack of explainability prevents clinicians from⏎assessing whether an algorithm’s outputs are reliable for individual patients or whether⏎performance may vary across demographic or clinical subgroups. In sleep medicine,⏎where diagnostic determinations frequently drive long-term or lifelong therapy decisions,⏎including durable medical equipment (DME) prescriptions and chronic disease⏎management, uncertainty about the reliability of algorithmic outputs raises significant⏎patient safety concerns. Clinicians must be able to understand and defend the rationale for⏎clinical decisions, and opaque systems undermine this fundamental professional⏎responsibility.⏎3. Operational and workflow challenges further complicate implementation. AI tools are⏎often introduced without sufficient integration into existing electronic health record⏎systems or clinical processes, resulting in fragmented workflows, duplicative⏎documentation, and increased administrative burden. Rather than reducing workload,⏎poorly designed tools may add steps that require clinicians or staff to manually reconcile⏎data, verify outputs, and correct errors. These inefficiencies diminish potential benefits⏎and contribute to frustration and burnout among healthcare professionals.⏎4. Reimbursement presents a substantial barrier. Many clinicians are concerned that payers⏎may assume that AI automatically delivers efficiency gains and therefore reduce payment⏎rates or increase productivity expectations, despite the reality that safe AI use often⏎requires additional oversight. In practice, when AI outputs are unreliable, incomplete, or⏎inconsistently formatted, clinicians must spend additional unbilled time reviewing results,⏎reconciling discrepancies, validating data accuracy, and documenting clinical reasoning⏎to meet regulatory and liability standards. This time-intensive oversight represents⏎uncompensated labor and shifts costs from technology developers to frontline providers.⏎For small, independent, and community-based practices operating on narrow margins,⏎these added burdens may make adoption financially infeasible.⏎5. The nature of many AI systems introduces concerns about consistency and durability.⏎Algorithms that update over time without clear version control, locked validation, or re-⏎certification processes may produce different outputs for the same clinical scenario,⏎complicating longitudinal care and making it difficult to compare results over time. Such⏎variability undermines clinician confidence, complicates quality measurement, and⏎creates challenges for regulatory compliance and clinical documentation. Healthcare⏎providers require stable, validated tools whose performance characteristics are⏎predictable and reproducible.⏎⏎What can HHS do via regulations to incentivize the use of AI in clinical care?⏎To address the aforementioned barriers to implementation, in support of safe and effective adoption, we⏎urge HHS to establish clear regulatory and financial frameworks that reflect how care is actually⏎delivered by implementing the following:⏎1. We recommend that AI vendors bear legal responsibility for performance errors⏎attributable to their algorithms. Clinicians should not be held liable for defects in tools⏎they did not design, train, or control.⏎2. Transparency requirements should move AI toward a “glass box” model, with clear⏎disclosure of training datasets, validation processes, intended use cases, and⏎independently verified performance metrics. From a clinical standpoint, this information⏎is essential for informed use at the point of care.⏎3. Independent validation and standardized benchmarking should occur prior to deployment⏎and at regular intervals thereafter, as clinicians need confidence that tools perform⏎consistently across patient populations and practice settings.⏎4. Interoperability and validity across vendors are critical for comparing tools, preventing⏎vendor lock-in, and supporting continuity of care when systems change.⏎Meaningful adoption of AI will also require direct financial support and reimbursement structures that⏎recognize the real-world costs of implementation. Integrating validated AI tools requires significant⏎upfront and ongoing investments, including software, licensing fees, IT infrastructure, cybersecurity⏎protections, workflow redesign, staff training, and maintenance. For many physician practices,⏎particularly small, independent, and solo practices, these costs are substantial and may be prohibitive⏎without dedicated reimbursement pathways, grants, or implementation assistance. HHS should therefore⏎establish clear mechanisms to reimburse the clinical time and resources required to appropriately use,⏎monitor, and validate AI outputs in patient care.⏎Additionally, HHS should ensure that reimbursement is not reduced or restructured downward based on⏎assumptions that AI automatically creates efficiencies. Payment models should instead recognize AI as a⏎clinical decision-support tool that augments, rather than replaces, physician expertise and should ensure⏎that clinicians are compensated for the time and professional judgment required to safely integrate these⏎technologies into care delivery.⏎What are “novel” legal barriers that AI creates in the clinical space?⏎AI introduces novel regulatory and legal considerations with direct implications for clinical practice.⏎1. FDA clearance processes that rely predominantly on vendor-supplied validation may⏎create uncertainty when independent verification of safety and effectiveness is not⏎feasible.⏎2. Clinicians require clear regulatory guidance on whether updated or adaptive AI systems⏎require a renewed review and how material changes in algorithm performance are⏎disclosed.⏎3. Limited explainability further complicates informed consent, clinical documentation, and⏎shared decision-making, thereby increasing the risk of liability.⏎4. If AI use becomes the standard of care, practices that lack the financial resources to adopt⏎such tools may face disproportionate legal exposure.⏎⏎Clear and consistent federal guidance delineating vendor accountability and clinician responsibilities is⏎essential to mitigate these risks and support equitable implementation.⏎Where does the government have the most influence?⏎The federal government plays a central and indispensable role in shaping a trustworthy, accountable, and⏎sustainable AI ecosystem for clinical care. Because healthcare delivery operates within highly regulated⏎frameworks for payment, privacy, and safety, federal policy decisions directly influence whether AI tools⏎are adopted responsibly or prematurely without adequate safeguards. The U.S. Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) is uniquely positioned to accelerate safe adoption by establishing clear,⏎enforceable standards that prioritize patient safety, reliability, and accountability while avoiding⏎unnecessary administrative complexity.⏎1. HHS should develop and implement regulatory frameworks that require independent⏎validation of clinical AI tools, standardized performance metrics, and ongoing post-⏎market monitoring to ensure consistent safety and effectiveness across care settings and⏎patient populations. Establishing interoperability standards and common technical⏎requirements will promote competition, reduce vendor lock-in, and enable providers to⏎integrate AI solutions without costly system overhauls. Clear disclosure expectations⏎regarding intended use, limitations, characteristics of training data, and version updates⏎are also essential to enable clinicians to make informed decisions about when and how to⏎rely on AI-supported outputs in patient care.⏎2. Robust privacy and data governance protections are equally critical. Federal policy⏎should ensure that patient data are used solely for authorized clinical or quality⏎improvement purposes, are not sold or repurposed for commercial gain without explicit⏎consent and are protected by strong cybersecurity safeguards. Transparent data⏎stewardship requirements, consent standards, and accountability mechanisms are⏎necessary to maintain public trust and prevent misuse. Without these protections, both⏎patients and providers may hesitate to participate in AI-enabled systems, undermining⏎their potential benefits.⏎Through clear standards for safety, transparency, interoperability, liability, and data protection, HHS can⏎create a stable regulatory environment that encourages responsible innovation while ensuring that new⏎technologies enhance clinical care and patient trust.⏎How and where can advancing interoperability improve the AI market for clinical care?⏎Advancing interoperability is fundamental to realizing the full clinical utility, safety, and scalability of⏎AI-enabled technologies. Without common technical standards and the ability for systems to⏎communicate seamlessly across platforms, AI tools risk becoming siloed solutions that fragment care,⏎increase administrative burden, and limit their practical value to clinicians and patients. Interoperability⏎enables independent verification of performance across environments, promotes transparency, and⏎allows providers to evaluate and compare tools based on consistent, objective criteria. This⏎comparability is essential for building clinician trust and supporting evidence-based procurement⏎decisions.⏎From a market perspective, interoperability also promotes competition and innovation by reducing vendor⏎⏎lock-in and lowering switching costs for healthcare organizations. When providers are not tied to⏎proprietary systems, vendors must compete on quality, safety, usability, and price rather than exclusivity.⏎This dynamic can reduce costs for practices and health systems while accelerating improvements in tool⏎performance and reliability. For smaller practices, interoperable solutions are particularly important⏎because they minimize the need for costly custom integrations and enable adoption without wholesale⏎replacement of existing infrastructure.⏎Clinically, effective interoperability strengthens care coordination and continuity. Seamless data⏎exchange between AI tools, electronic health records, and other health information systems allows⏎clinicians to access complete and accurate patient information at the point of care, share insights with⏎patients and referring providers, and reduce duplicative testing or unnecessary procedures. This⏎capability is especially important for longitudinal conditions, such as sleep disorders and other chronic⏎diseases, where consistent tracking of diagnostic and treatment data over time is essential to safe and⏎effective management.⏎Standardized definitions, benchmarking methods, and validation criteria will ensure that outputs are⏎meaningfully comparable across tools and vendors, enabling regulators, purchasers, and clinicians to⏎assess effectiveness and safety on equal terms. Federal leadership in establishing and enforcing these⏎standards will be critical to creating a cohesive, competitive, and trustworthy AI ecosystem that supports⏎high-quality clinical care.⏎What challenges and concerns do patients and caregivers have with the adoption of AI technology?⏎Patients and caregivers have expressed significant concerns regarding the integration of AI into clinical⏎care, particularly related to transparency, bias, privacy, cost, liability, workforce impact, and the⏎preservation of the patient-clinician relationship. Many patients are unaware of when AI tools are used in⏎their care or how algorithmic outputs influence clinical decision-making. Without clear disclosure and⏎meaningful explanation, trust in both the technology and the healthcare system may be undermined.⏎Concerns about equitable performance across diverse populations and the protection of sensitive health⏎information are especially prominent. Patients expect assurance that AI systems are independently⏎validated, free from bias, and subject to strong privacy safeguards that prevent unauthorized use or⏎commercial exploitation of their data. Questions about potential cost implications and accountability for⏎errors further influence public confidence.⏎To address these concerns, federal policy should establish clear disclosure standards, require⏎independent validation and equitable performance evaluation, and ensure robust privacy protections.⏎Patient-facing education and transparent accountability frameworks will be essential to building and⏎sustaining trust in AI-enabled clinical care.⏎Are there specific research areas that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎HHS should prioritize research that rigorously evaluates whether AI demonstrably improves clinical care⏎and workflow from the frontline clinician perspective. Studies should assess measurable impacts on⏎clinician burden, time efficiency, patient access, and burnout, while ensuring that AI does not shift⏎additional uncompensated work or legal risk onto providers. Research should also examine real-world⏎implementation, including workflow integration, oversight requirements, and patient safety outcomes.⏎⏎Targeted pilot programs in high-prevalence, high-cost conditions, such as obstructive sleep apnea, may⏎offer meaningful opportunities to evaluate clinical effectiveness, cost savings, and scalability. In parallel,⏎HHS should explore payment models that support responsible, evidence-based adoption of AI,⏎recognizing that sustainable reimbursement structures are essential for widespread implementation.⏎AI holds significant promise to enhance care delivery; however, adoption will depend on demonstrated⏎safety, transparency, independent validation, clear accountability, and appropriate financial support. By⏎prioritizing these research areas, HHS can promote responsible innovation while ensuring that clinicians⏎can confidently integrate AI into patient care.⏎Thank you for your consideration of these comments. The AASM appreciates the Agency’s efforts⏎to receive input into accelerating the adoption and use of AI as a part of clinical care . We⏎encourage the Agency to adopt the recommended changes summarized in this letter. Please feel⏎free to contact Diedra Gray, AASM Director of Quality & Health Policy, at dgray@aasm.org or⏎630-737-9700, for additional information or clarifications.⏎Sincerely,⏎Anita V. Shelgikar, MD, MHPE⏎President, American Academy of Sleep Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0181♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from O'Brien, Mark , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mark♔O'Brien♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am writing to express serious concerns regarding the proposed acceleration of artificial intelligence (AI) integration into clinical care settings, particularly within behavioral health and psychotherapy.⏎While innovation in healthcare technology has the potential to improve access and efficiency, the rapid deployment of AI tools in direct patient care raises urgent concerns about patient privacy, safety, scientific validation, and the preservation of the therapeutic relationship. At present, the science and safeguards have not kept pace with the speed of implementation.⏎Behavioral health settings involve uniquely sensitive disclosures, including trauma histories, suicidal ideation, substance use, and deeply personal relational information. The introduction of AI systems into psychotherapy sessions—whether for documentation, clinical decision support, or conversational interaction—creates heightened risks related to data security, secondary data use, algorithmic bias, and patient consent. Even de-identified data in mental health contexts may be vulnerable to re-identification or misuse.⏎Furthermore, many AI-enabled clinical tools currently lack independent, peer-reviewed evidence demonstrating safety, efficacy, and clinical superiority over existing standards of care. In medicine, new interventions typically undergo rigorous testing prior to widespread adoption. AI systems that influence diagnosis, treatment recommendations, or therapeutic processes should be held to comparable evidentiary standards. Without this level of scrutiny, patients may be exposed to unintended harm, including inaccurate risk assessments, inappropriate treatment suggestions, or overreliance on automated outputs.⏎In psychotherapy specifically, the therapeutic alliance is one of the strongest predictors of clinical outcomes. Trust, confidentiality, and relational attunement are foundational. If patients are uncertain about how their data is being processed, stored, or potentially repurposed, this may inhibit disclosure and compromise care. Innovation must not erode the integrity of this relationship.⏎I urge the Department to ensure that AI integration into clinical care is guided by:⏎Rigorous, independent research demonstrating safety and effectiveness prior to widespread deployment.⏎Clear federal standards governing data storage, secondary use, transparency, and algorithmic accountability.⏎Explicit informed consent requirements for AI involvement in behavioral health settings.⏎Strong enforcement mechanisms to protect patient privacy and prevent commercial exploitation of sensitive health data.⏎Special regulatory consideration for behavioral health and psychotherapy, where the ethical stakes are uniquely high.⏎Technological progress in healthcare should be deliberate, evidence-based, and grounded in enforceable protections. Innovation must serve patients—not outpace the ethical and regulatory frameworks designed to safeguard them.⏎Thank you for your consideration of these concerns.⏎Respectfully,⏎Mark O'Brien, LCPC, CADC♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0182♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Consumer Technology Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Consumer Technology Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health & Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: RIN 0955-AA13⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎The Consumer Technology Association (CTA®) appreciates the opportunity to provide input on what the⏎Department of Health & Human Services (HHS) can do to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of⏎clinical care.⏎As North America’s largest technology trade association, CTA is the tech sector. CTA is the trade⏎association representing more than 1200 companies in the U.S. technology industry. Eighty percent of CTA⏎companies are small businesses and startups; others are among the world’s best-known brands. CTA’s⏎Health Division advances consumer-based, technology-enabled health solutions to improve health⏎outcomes and reduce overall health care costs. Our members use technology to improve nutrition, fitness,⏎mental health, lifestyle management, care access, care coordination, and more – and they are poised to⏎lead the next wave of American innovation with cutting-edge health technology.⏎CTA commends HHS on their leadership in advancing the use of technology, including artificial intelligence⏎(AI) in healthcare. Through executive action and agency direction focused on promoting innovation,⏎reducing unnecessary regulatory barriers and improving efficiency, the Administration has created an⏎environment that supports the responsible use of AI to improve treatments, accelerate the development of⏎new cures and lower unnecessary health care spending. CTA looks forward to continuing to work with you⏎to realize this shared vision.⏎General Comments⏎CTA member companies are pioneering advances across the spectrum – from AI-powered continuous⏎glucose monitors to remote patient monitoring, to the first FDA-cleared obstructive sleep apnea risk⏎detection feature for consumer wearables. CTA is an American National Standards Institute (ANSI)⏎accredited standards developer. To date, we have published more than 35 digital health standards including⏎five specifically dedicated to health AI. These standards reflect a risk-based approach and are designed to⏎be adaptable to a wide range of technologies while supporting innovation. Through the leadership of the⏎Health AI Planning Council, we are focused on developing standards that meet the industry’s needs.⏎⏎Additionally, in 2025, CTA released research focused on gauging healthcare providers’ (HCPs) attitudes⏎toward and current adoption of AI. CTA’s AI’s Impact and Opportunity Among Healthcare Practitioners⏎found:⏎• AI adoption in healthcare is in its early stages and lacks formal structure. HCPs report that AI⏎adoption in their organizations is still early-stage, with little training, guidance or formal strategy in⏎place. Many HCPs are independently exploring AI tools, relying on self-teaching or peer support⏎rather than organizational direction.⏎• HCPs' trust in AI is cautious and task-specific. While providers generally trust AI, this trust is⏎conditional and depends on the task. HCPs are more confident using AI for documentation,⏎diagnostics (with human oversight) and predictive analysis than they are for emotionally nuanced⏎care, ethical decisions or high-stakes judgments. They trust AI more for their own use than for⏎patients, citing concerns about misinformation, anxiety and weakened doctor-patient relationships.⏎Ultimately, they see AI as a tool to support — not replace — their expertise.⏎• HCPs recognize AI’s benefits but also significant barriers. HCPs who currently use AI report benefits⏎like improved efficiency and decision-making support, but they remain concerned about privacy and⏎security (e.g., compliance with the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act [HIPAA],⏎misuse of sensitive data), accuracy and reliability (e.g., incorrect information, bias), financial⏎constraints (e.g., cost, return on investment [ROI]) and resistance to change. Without clear solutions⏎to these issues, many are hesitant to adopt AI more broadly.⏎• Alleviating providers’ administrative burden is a key opportunity for AI in healthcare. Despite efforts⏎to streamline care, tech like electronic medical records (EMRs) and portals have added⏎administrative burden, pulling HCPs away from patient care and contributing to burnout amid staffing⏎shortages. Many see AI as a key solution to automate routine tasks, reduce admin time and enable⏎more meaningful patient interactions.⏎• There is a shared fear of an “AI crutch” rather than job replacement. HCPs aren’t worried about⏎being replaced by AI but are concerned about overreliance. They fear it could erode critical thinking,⏎foster blind trust in AI and weaken clinical skills without proper training and oversight.⏎• HCPs are overwhelmingly optimistic about the future of AI in healthcare. Despite current challenges,⏎HCPs remain optimistic about AI’s potential to boost efficiency and patient care. With proper support,⏎they see opportunities in automation, diagnostics, post-surgical monitoring and at-home care tools.⏎A well-calibrated and risk-based regulatory scheme will allow developers and users of health AI to explore⏎these modern tools with confidence, rather than increase operational burdens or create distrust of this⏎promising new technology.⏎Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎Regulatory Fragmentation and Variability⏎Between state laws, federal regulations and international frameworks, innovators are facing increasing⏎demands to comply with varied requirements. Without a federal framework, this will only become⏎increasingly complex for developers and deployers of AI to navigate. President Trump recognized this with⏎⏎his executive order, “Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence,” rightfully recognizing⏎that a patchwork threatens AI innovation and national competitiveness. Some specific examples include:⏎• Patchwork of state laws: In 2025, 1208 AI-related bills were introduced across all 50 states – 145 of⏎those were enacted into law. Already in 2026, 1422 bills across 45 states have been introduced.⏎• Ambiguity related to device classifications (e.g., software as a medical device (SaMD) v. wellness⏎tools), and⏎• Uncertainty about the parameters of agency jurisdiction (e.g., FDA's oversight of clinical decision⏎support (CDS) v. ASTP/ONC's oversight of predictive decision support interventions (PDSI).⏎Lack of Adequate Reimbursement⏎As White House Office of Science and Technology Policy Director Michael Kratsios said “AI technology is⏎already enhancing the digital revolution in sectors such as health care and agriculture, and further enabling⏎new industries such as robotics, drones, and self-driving vehicles.” Unfortunately, AI adoption in health care⏎has been hampered by lack of predictable reimbursement policies. Since 2018, the Centers for Medicare &⏎Medicaid Services (CMS) has requested feedback on updating Medicare payment methodology for⏎Software as a Service (SaaS)/Software as a Medical Device (SaMD). In 2025, CTA commended CMS for⏎recognizing that the current PE methodology does not accurately account for innovative health software⏎technologies like artificial intelligence (AI). After eight years of feedback and consideration, we urge HHS to⏎swiftly move to propose new updated payment methodology to better reflect that AI should not be⏎considered “indirect costs” (administrative labor, office expenses, computer software) and if it is an FDA-⏎approved medical device, it should be reimbursed as such. To date, FDA has approved more than 1300 AI-⏎enabled medical devices.1 It is past time to establish new policies that will ensure Medicare beneficiaries⏎have access to innovative technology tools that are medically reasonable, necessary and demonstrably⏎improve care.⏎Ultimately, CMS must move away from time-based reimbursement codes that incentivize inefficient clinician⏎workflows. Payment incentives should prioritize AI tools that enhance care delivery, improve efficiency, or⏎reduce total cost of care. CTA supports recent steps HHS has taken to support value-based payment⏎systems as we believe this will be crucial to AI and other software-based tools reaching their full potential⏎and utilization in the Medicare program.⏎Interoperability Challenges⏎CTA appreciates HHS’ recent focus on improving interoperability and enforcing information blocking⏎regulations. Interoperability is essential to health AI as it allows algorithms to securely access and integrate⏎data across disparate systems, ensuring models are accurate, unbiased, scalable and able to deliver⏎clinically meaningful insights at the point of care. While advances in AI can overcome some past challenges⏎in interoperability (e.g., lack of standardized data sets, inconsistent data quality), HHS must continue to⏎support data interoperability efforts, especially enforcement of regulations against business practices that⏎inhibit interoperability.⏎Clinician Perceptions⏎One significant barrier to the adoption of AI in health care is a persistent provider misconception that human⏎clinicians consistently achieve the best outcomes on their own. While clinical expertise is essential, evidence⏎1 https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/artificial-intelligence-enabled-medical-devices⏎⏎increasingly shows that AI systems can outperform humans in specific tasks, such as early detection of⏎conditions and pattern recognition in high-pressure environments like emergency departments, where⏎fatigue and time constraints affect decision-making. When AI is viewed as a threat to clinical judgment rather⏎than a complementary tool, opportunities to improve diagnostic accuracy, patient safety, and efficiency are⏎often missed. In the United States, an estimated 795,000 patients become permanently disabled or die⏎annually across care settings because dangerous diseases are misdiagnosed.2 By prioritizing AI tools that⏎improve front-end diagnostic quality particularly in high-acuity environments like emergency departments,⏎HHS can help mitigate preventable harm. Evidence from industry leaders demonstrates that clinical AI can⏎improve disease detection by as much as 81.5% and accelerate time-to-treatment by up to 90%,⏎transforming AI from a mere efficiency tool into a vital infrastructure layer for patient safety.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Federal Harmonization and Regulatory Certainty⏎HHS should establish clear jurisdictional lines for health AI within the agency as well as with other federal⏎agencies to reduce fragmentation and confusion. For example, to resolve jurisdictional ambiguity between⏎FDA and ASTP/ONC, the agency should make clear its oversight responsibilities for CDS and the⏎ASTP/ONC's oversight of predictive decision support interventions (PDSIs) integrated into electronic health⏎records. This will help prevent duplicative and potentially contradictory requirements for health technologies.⏎Further, recognizing that not all health AI applications will fall fully under HHS’ jurisdiction, HHS should also⏎coordinate with other federal agencies such as NIST and the FTC.⏎Modernizing and Clarifying Reimbursement⏎As CMS itself has recognized, it is inappropriate to categorize AI as “computer software” and therefore⏎indirect PE. However, moving AI to direct PE will also not be adequate for AI reimbursement. Currently,⏎RVUs consider the professional work of a provider or clinical staff to calculate direct PE. We are quickly⏎reaching a place where clinical work will be replaced by AI. HHS should convene stakeholders to modernize⏎and update the RVU considerations. In addition to clinical work, AI is also transforming provider⏎administrative tasks. HHS should clarify how AI outputs can be documented and counted toward existing⏎service requirements (e.g., time, data review, clinical decision support).⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎There are current gaps with existing governance and accountability structures, including:⏎• Unclear Roles & Responsibilities: Roles and responsibilities for errors are often unclear across the⏎developer and deployer. This is complicated more by issues discussed above, including conflicting⏎state laws and requirements.⏎2 Newman-Toker DE, Nassery N, Schaffer AC, Yu-Moe CW, Clemens GD, Wang Z, Zhu Y, Saber Tehrani AS, Fanai M, Hassoon A,⏎Siegal D. Burden of serious harms from diagnostic error in the USA. BMJ Qual Saf. 2024 Jan 19;33(2):109-120. doi: 10.1136/bmjqs-⏎2021-014130. PMID: 37460118; PMCID: PMC10792094.⏎⏎• Post-Market Monitoring: Similarly, there are not widely accepted protocols for how AI should be⏎monitored after it is deployed.⏎• Gaps in Privacy Protections: The current privacy framework – rooted in the Health Insurance⏎Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) – is outdated and incomplete. Many consumer-facing⏎digital health manufacturers and deployers are not considered covered entities under HIPAA and⏎often are not subject to business associate agreements with a HIPAA covered entity.⏎To address these gaps, HHS should focus on developing a risk-based framework that leverages existing⏎regulatory authority, promotes transparency, industry best practices and standards, and is focused on AI⏎applications.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎As an American National Standards Institute (ANSI) accredited standards development organization, CTA is⏎helping advance the use of AI in healthcare by driving industry consensus. To date, CTA’s Artificial⏎Intelligence Committee has already developed several health AI standards, including:⏎• Performance Verification and Validation for Predictive Health AI Solutions (ANSI/CTA-2135)⏎• Definitions/Characteristics of Artificial Intelligence in Health Care (ANSI/CTA-2089.1)⏎• The Use of Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Trustworthiness (ANSI/CTA-2090)⏎• The Use of Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Managing, Characterizing, and Safeguarding⏎Data (ANSI/CTA-2107-A)⏎We would welcome HHS’ involvement in CTA’s standards development process, as well as how CTA⏎standards can help support HHS’ health AI frameworks.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven⏎testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎As mentioned, HHS should establish a baseline regulatory framework focused on AI applications that⏎promotes responsible development, transparency, post-market monitoring, and risk management. HHS has⏎a clear role to play to set baseline “rules of the road.” In response, HHS should work with industry to build off⏎of these baseline standards with industry consensus-based standards that can be more reactive to quickly⏎evolving technology like AI.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the⏎development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability focused on cancer data, genomics and imaging data would help widen⏎opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the development of AI. This would enable researchers and⏎developers to link datasets that are currently siloed across institutions and platforms, ultimately improving⏎translational research by enabling faster validation of biomarkers, treatment response prediction and real-⏎⏎world evidence generation, ultimately shortening the path from discovery to clinical deployment and⏎improving cancer prevention, diagnosis and care.⏎Conclusion⏎CTA appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Request for Information on Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. We look forward to continuing to work with you to⏎advance the adoption of AI in healthcare to increase efficiency, lower costs and improve patient outcomes⏎Sincerely,⏎Michael Petricone⏎Senior Vice President, Government Affairs⏎Consumer Technology Association⏎René Quashie⏎Vice President, Digital Health⏎Consumer Technology Association⏎Catherine Pugh⏎Director, Digital Health⏎Consumer Technology Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0183♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AMGA, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AMGA♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane, M.D., M.B.A.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy &⏎National Coordinator for Health IT⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Secretary Keane,⏎On behalf of AMGA, we appreciate the opportunity to respond to the Department of Health and⏎Human Services’ Request for Information on Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Clinical Care. AMGA⏎represents multispecialty medical groups and integrated systems of care that collectively employ⏎or contract with more than 177,000 physicians and deliver care to more than one in three⏎Americans. Our members are leaders in value-based care delivery and have extensive experience⏎deploying advanced analytics, digital health tools, and early AI-enabled technologies across⏎diverse clinical settings.⏎AMGA strongly supports HHS’s objective of establishing a regulatory, reimbursement, and⏎research posture for AI that is predictable, risk-proportionate, and supportive of innovation⏎while protecting patients, privacy, and public trust. As emphasized in AMGA’s prior Health⏎Technology Ecosystem comments, technology policy is most effective when it reflects the⏎operational realities of clinical care and reduces administrative burden. AI adoption will succeed⏎only if it is integrated into aligned payment models, interoperable infrastructure, and clinically⏎grounded governance frameworks.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎AMGA members report that the most significant barriers to private-sector innovation and⏎adoption of AI in clinical care stem from misalignment between AI’s value proposition and⏎existing health care policy structures. Fee-for-service payment systems, in particular, do not⏎adequately reward the preventive, longitudinal, and population-level benefits that AI tools are⏎designed to deliver. As a result, providers often struggle to justify the substantial upfront⏎investments required to deploy AI responsibly, integrate it into clinical workflows, and maintain⏎it over time.⏎Regulatory fragmentation further compounds this challenge. Overlapping oversight across CMS,⏎⏎ONC, and OCR creates uncertainty regarding applicable standards, compliance obligations, and⏎enforcement risk—particularly when AI tools are embedded within EHRs or used as clinical⏎decision support. In addition, AI development and performance are constrained by incomplete⏎and inconsistent access to data, including limited availability of commercial and Medicare⏎Advantage claims data and variability in interoperability implementation across certified health⏎IT systems.⏎Administrative burden also remains a persistent barrier. Duplicative documentation⏎requirements, quality reporting obligations, and program-specific compliance processes divert⏎clinician and organizational resources away from innovation and care improvement.⏎State-based licensure requirements restrict the scalability of AI-enabled telehealth and remote⏎clinical support models, while rising cybersecurity risks impose additional operational and⏎financial costs that are not adequately recognized or offset under current payment and⏎regulatory frameworks.⏎Finally, recent CMS Innovation Center models, such as the WISeR model, further illustrate⏎provider concerns regarding the use of AI-enabled tools in utilization management and prior⏎authorization decision-making. WISeR characterizes certain services as “low value” despite those⏎services having already undergone rigorous review through National Coverage Determinations⏎(NCDs) and Local Coverage Determinations (LCDs). While CMS states that the model does not⏎directly modify NCDs or LCDs, it effectively re-scrutinizes established coverage policies and raises⏎concerns about duplicative oversight and the potential for informal policy shifts outside the⏎traditional rulemaking process. Such approaches risk undermining trust in evidence-based⏎coverage frameworks that providers rely upon for clinical certainty.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care?⏎To incentivize effective and responsible AI use in clinical care, AMGA urges HHS to prioritize⏎modernization of payment and regulatory frameworks so they better reflect how care is⏎delivered and how AI creates value. Payment models should reward prevention, care⏎coordination, and population health management—areas where AI tools have demonstrated the⏎greatest potential to improve outcomes and reduce costs.⏎Extending and stabilizing incentives for participation in Advanced Alternative Payment Models,⏎including the five percent APM bonus, would provide providers with the financial certainty⏎necessary to make long-term investments in AI-enabled infrastructure. HHS should also allow AI-⏎enabled tools and digital infrastructure to count as allowable expenditures in ACO benchmarking⏎and financial reconciliation methodologies.⏎Additionally, certification and reporting requirements that force costly EHR upgrades without⏎clear clinical or patient benefit should be reevaluated to ensure they do not inadvertently stifle⏎innovation.⏎As AI tools are increasingly incorporated into coverage, utilization management, and prior⏎authorization processes, HHS should establish clear guardrails to ensure transparency,⏎accountability, and alignment with established coverage policies. The wide concerns around the⏎WISeR model relate back to this fundamental issue. When AI is used to influence payment⏎2⏎⏎decisions, the standards governing its use should be clear, predictable, and subject to⏎appropriate rulemaking safeguards.⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist, and what role⏎should HHS play?⏎The use of AI in clinical care, particularly when deployed through non-medical devices or third-⏎party applications, raises novel legal and implementation challenges. These include uncertainty⏎regarding liability and accountability when AI tools are embedded in EHRs or used as clinical⏎decision support, as well as contractual ambiguity surrounding responsibility for AI-generated⏎recommendations. AI also amplifies privacy and cybersecurity risks due to the scale, sensitivity,⏎and aggregation of data involved, creating new compliance challenges at the intersection of⏎HIPAA, information blocking, and patient safety requirements.⏎AMGA believes HHS has an important role to play in clarifying governance expectations for AI-⏎enabled clinical decision support tools and supporting safe harbors for providers who use AI⏎responsibly within established clinical workflows. OCR privacy enforcement should be aligned⏎with the realities of AI-driven care delivery, and cybersecurity readiness should be recognized as⏎a foundational requirement for safe AI deployment rather than an optional or ancillary⏎consideration.⏎Models such as WISeR also raise important legal and implementation questions regarding⏎accountability when AI tools are used to support or inform prior authorization determinations.⏎Even if AI is positioned as an advisory tool, its operational integration into payment review⏎processes can materially affect patient access and provider reimbursement. HHS should clarify⏎how responsibility is allocated among technology vendors, CMS contractors, and providers when⏎AI-generated recommendations contribute to denials, delays, or downstream clinical⏎consequences.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what AI evaluation methods and metrics are most promising, and⏎should HHS further support them?⏎AMGA supports AI evaluation approaches that prioritize real-world clinical impact rather than⏎technical performance alone. Effective evaluation should focus on patient outcomes, workflow⏎integration, clinician usability, equity, and patient experience. Enterprise-level, outcome-based⏎measures—such as those aligned with CMS’s Universal Foundation and the AMGA Value⏎Measure Set—are particularly well suited to assessing AI’s contribution to coordinated, team-⏎based care.⏎HHS should further support these evaluation efforts through pilot programs, demonstration⏎projects, and implementation guides that test AI tools in diverse care settings. Grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and learning collaboratives focused on implementation science would⏎help ensure that AI tools are deployed safely and effectively. Importantly, guardrails against AI⏎bias and misuse should be embedded within evaluation frameworks, including ongoing⏎monitoring of performance across patient populations and transparency regarding limitations⏎and appropriate use.⏎5. How can HHS best support private-sector accreditation, certification, testing, and⏎credentialing?⏎AMGA recommends that HHS support private-sector accreditation and certification by⏎3⏎⏎developing modular, function-specific certification pathways for AI-enabled tools. A parallel⏎certification track focused on value-based and population health capabilities would better reflect⏎the operational needs of providers engaged in advanced care models. Certification approaches⏎should avoid forcing full EHR replacement or unnecessary system upgrades and should recognize⏎third-party validation and attestation pathways, particularly for small and rural providers with⏎limited resources.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded expectations, and where⏎have they fallen short?⏎AI tools have shown meaningful promise in areas such as risk stratification, identification of⏎rising-risk patients, care gap identification, proactive outreach, remote patient monitoring, and⏎chronic disease management. When supported by timely and complete data, AI-enabled⏎analytics can also enhance population health management by integrating claims and clinical⏎information into actionable insights.⏎However, AI tools often fall short when claims data is incomplete or delayed, interoperability⏎standards are inconsistently implemented, or payment models fail to reward avoided utilization⏎and downstream savings. Inadequate data quality or lack of representativeness can also limit AI⏎effectiveness and raise concerns about equity and reliability.⏎AI tools are most effective when they enhance care coordination and proactive population⏎management. They are more likely to generate provider concern when deployed primarily as⏎cost-containment or utilization control mechanisms without transparent guardrails. The WISeR⏎model illustrates this tension, particularly where prior authorization processes intersect with⏎services already deemed reasonable and necessary under existing Medicare coverage policies.⏎7. Which roles or governing bodies most influence AI adoption, and what administrative⏎hurdles exist?⏎Within health care organizations, AI adoption decisions are typically influenced by clinical⏎leadership, compliance and legal teams, IT and cybersecurity leadership, and finance and⏎population health leaders. Administrative hurdles include regulatory uncertainty, unclear return⏎on investment under fee-for-service payment, integration challenges with existing EHRs and⏎workflows, and limited internal resources. These challenges are particularly acute for small and⏎rural practices.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability accelerate AI development and adoption?⏎Enhanced interoperability is foundational to AI development and adoption in clinical care.⏎Standardized FHIR APIs across all certified EHRs, timely access to Medicare Advantage and⏎commercial claims data via APIs, and enforceable standards for data completeness, timeliness,⏎and usability under TEFCA would significantly improve AI performance and scalability. The⏎development of a national FHIR-based provider directory would further support attribution,⏎contracting, and care coordination while reducing administrative burden.⏎9. What challenges do patients and caregivers want AI to address, and what concerns do they⏎have?⏎Patients and caregivers want AI to improve care coordination, enable earlier intervention,⏎support personalized care, and reduce delays in accessing quality care. At the same time, they⏎have legitimate concerns about losing clinician judgment or human oversight, and the privacy⏎4⏎⏎and security of their health data. Maintaining trust will require that AI tools augment—not⏎replace—the clinician-patient relationship.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research HHS should prioritize?⏎AMGA recommends that HHS prioritize AI research focused on early risk identification,⏎preventive interventions, integration of patient-reported outcomes and longitudinal data, and⏎reduction of administrative burden and clinician burnout. Implementation science should be⏎emphasized to support safe, scalable deployment across care settings. Research should assess⏎real-world outcomes, equity impacts, and the distribution of costs and benefits across patients,⏎providers, and payers. AI bias mitigation and misuse prevention should be explicit research⏎priorities to ensure that AI tools improve care equitably across populations.⏎Conclusion⏎AMGA appreciates HHS’s leadership in advancing a clear, predictable, and risk-proportionate⏎framework for AI in clinical care.⏎AI has significant potential to improve patient outcomes, reduce administrative burden, and⏎support the transition to value-based care, but this promise can only be realized if regulatory,⏎payment, and interoperability policies are aligned with real-world clinical practice. AMGA and its⏎members stand ready to continue partnering with HHS to advance responsible, patient-centered⏎AI adoption.⏎Should you have questions, please do not hesitate to contact Darryl M. Drevna, Senior Director⏎of Regulatory Affairs, at 703.838.0033 ext. 339 or at ddrevna@amga.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jerry Penso, MD, MBA⏎President and Chief Executive Officer, AMGA⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0184♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Massachusetts Health & Hospital Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Massachusetts Health & Hospital Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and the Office of the National Coordinator⏎for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎ATTN: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via Regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13; 90 FR 60108)⏎Dear Deputy Secretary,⏎On behalf of the Massachusetts Health & Hospital Association (MHA) and our 64 acute⏎care hospital and health system members, thank you for the opportunity to comment on⏎HHS’s Request for Information (RFI) on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. MHA supports HHS’ whole of department⏎approach across regulation, reimbursement, and research and development, and offer⏎‑ ‑⏎below our recommendations organized to align with the RFI’s ten specific questions.⏎Our members’ input reflects broad, real world experience implementing AI tools across⏎clinical and operational workflows. Our members believe AI tools should adhere to⏎‑⏎“FAVES” principles (fair, appropriate, valid, effective, and safe). In particular, safety must⏎be the guiding principle for AI adoption. Efficacy without safety is insufficient. We believe⏎HHS should emphasize proportionate to risk safety, validation, transparency, and⏎governance as co equal aims with AI adoption and speed.⏎‑ ‑⏎‑⏎Executive Summary of Recommendations⏎MHA’s members offer these high-level recommendations for HHS:⏎• HHS should lead with safety and direction through proportionate-to-risk frameworks⏎and clinician-inclusive AI governance.⏎⏎• HHS should fund and enable AI access with real-world evidence through grants, pilots,⏎and demonstrations, especially for smaller, rural, and safety net providers.⏎• HHS should enable an evaluation and market infrastructure with standards-based⏎certification, benchmarking, and interoperability.⏎• HHS should clarify liability, privacy, and disclosure expectations without creating⏎burdensome mandates for healthcare providers.⏎MHA and our members offer these responses to the questions included in the RFI:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The greatest barriers to AI adoption and use in clinical care are safety, validation, and⏎funding gaps for provider adoption as well as high early-stage costs with unclear return on⏎investment; interoperability and integration frictions; the pace of change outstripping⏎governance and workflow redesign; strategic misalignment between vendor offerings and⏎health system priorities; and the risk of widening digital divides for smaller, rural, and⏎safety net providers.⏎Additionally, Massachusetts hospitals mentioned specific concerns in each area,⏎including:⏎• Safety & Validation: In particular, MHA members are concerned that there is⏎insufficient real-world evidence that AI tools improve outcomes, particularly across⏎diverse populations and care settings. Currently, there is overreliance on vendor test⏎environments rather than local validation across real-world populations. More grant⏎funding is needed to support co-development and conflict-free real-world validation⏎studies to identify clinically appropriate use cases that improve patient outcomes.⏎• Cost & ROI: Many AI vendors charge significant per-use and subscription fees, with⏎return on investment difficult to quantify at this time. For example, benefits such as⏎reduced physician burnout and improved patient care due to innovations, improved⏎care management, etc. are currently soft and difficult to capture in a meaningful way.⏎• Integration & Interoperability: There are currently limited or evolving APIs and⏎competing products create friction and challenges for healthcare providers, with lack of⏎integration into EHRs. Additionally, constrained IT and operational bandwidth slow⏎appropriate AI governance with safe implementation and monitoring. It is essential that⏎future AI innovations allow for integration and interoperability to be effective in⏎improving patient outcomes and reducing administrative efficiencies and cost.⏎2⏎⏎• Operational Capacity & Speed: MHA members are uneasy that rapid AI deployment⏎risks over-promising/under-delivering –which erodes clinician trust if workflows,⏎training, and guardrails lag.⏎• Clinical & Operational Relevance: Perhaps most notably, hospitals stated that AI tools⏎may target vendor-addressable markets rather than hospitals’ highest-value clinical⏎needs. In areas with high clinical and operational relevance, like ambient⏎documentation, there has been rapid and strong adoption of AI.⏎• Funding: There is significant concern that resource constraints will mean that smaller⏎community, safety net, and rural hospitals risk being left behind since AI adoption may⏎flow to larger, urban, better-resourced healthcare providers.⏎2. What are the regulatory, payment, and programmatic priorities to incentivize the⏎safe & effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎MHA and our members believe AI tools should adhere to FAVES principles (fair,⏎appropriate, valid, effective and safe). From the regulatory perspective, for the use non-⏎medical device clinical AI, we encourage HHS to establish voluntary, standards-based⏎certification for AI-enabled health IT modules that emphasize transparency (intended use,⏎training data provenance), lifecycle change logs, evaluation disclosures, and risk⏎management. Consider a “light-touch” governance expectation within hospital Conditions⏎of Participation for organizations that use AI – for example, an AI governance function⏎including clinician leadership – without prescribing specific tools or metrics. MHA⏎members also suggest that HHS provide HIPAA guidance clarifying BAAs, model-training⏎boundaries, de-identification, and prohibited secondary uses. Continue information⏎blocking guidance to support automation-friendly access to electronic health information⏎while preserving privacy and security.⏎In terms of payment, MHA encourages the administration to launch Center for Medicare⏎and Medicaid Innovation demonstrations that reward outcomes-validated AI use-cases⏎(e.g., faster stroke/PE care, documentation efficiency linked to access) with equity⏎adjustments for rural/safety-net providers. Explore bundled or add-on payments for⏎validated AI assistance integral to interpretations / procedures, tied to transparent⏎evidence standards and ongoing monitoring.⏎Among the programmatic recommendations, MHA encourages HHS to fund SBIR-style⏎pilots, cooperative agreements, and Cooperative Research and Development Agreements⏎(CRADAs) to generate generalizable, real-world evidence across diverse populations and⏎sites, including the prioritization of community, rural, and safety-net settings. In addition,⏎MHA members urge HHS to require EHR vendors to provide equal technical access to third⏎party applications, as in some cases, large vendors are using their control of workflows⏎and APIs to disadvantage third party apps and innovation.⏎3⏎⏎3. What novel legal and implementation issues exist for non-medical devices, and⏎what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎At present, there is little to no case law addressing liability for medical errors involving AI,⏎and existing legal frameworks do not adequately account for AI’s role in clinical⏎decision-making. Current doctrines, including traditional product liability and medical⏎malpractice standards, were not designed for situations in which clinical decisions are⏎informed by complex algorithms that may operate beyond the full understanding of either⏎physicians or patients. Absent clear standards, healthcare providers may be exposed to⏎disproportionate liability risk for harms associated with poorly performing AI tools. This risk⏎could lead to patient harm, discourage the use of beneficial technologies, limit innovation⏎in clinical care, and increase malpractice insurance costs through higher premiums,⏎deductibles, or restrictive policy exclusions related to AI use.⏎As AI systems become more advanced and personalized, they are increasingly positioned⏎as meaningful contributors to medical decision-making. These technologies may analyze⏎large volumes of individualized data – including medical records, diagnostic tests, imaging,⏎and data from wearable devices – to inform diagnosis and treatment. While such⏎capabilities hold significant promise for improving patient outcomes, they also introduce⏎new risks. When an AI system produces an incorrect or misleading output, the⏎consequences for patients can be serious, particularly if the error contributes to⏎misdiagnosis or inappropriate treatment. A further challenge arises when physicians⏎choose not to use AI tools, or when they override or disagree with AI-generated⏎recommendations based on their clinical judgment and patient harm subsequently⏎occurs. This scenario presents a legal dilemma with no clear precedent. Holding⏎healthcare providers liable both for relying on AI and for exercising independent judgment⏎against AI recommendations places them in an untenable position. In these⏎circumstances, policymakers must consider where responsibility should properly lie.⏎Additionally, it is important that regulators ensure that developer accountability is built-in⏎for AI product performance – especially for so-called “black box” AI systems where the⏎internal logic driving outputs is not fully explainable to clinicians or patients. If a⏎reasonable clinician could not have known – or had no reason to know – that an AI output⏎was erroneous, and it was reasonable to rely on that output based on the developer’s⏎representations, the accountability for resulting harm to patients should rest with the AI⏎developer.⏎MHA recommends that HHS consider and enable liability allocation among clinicians,⏎institutions, and vendors, and the evolving standard of care (including when omission of⏎proven AI could be considered substandard). MHA recommends that HHS work closely⏎with and take its guidance from nationally-recognized clinical professional societies in⏎formulating any guidance around specialty-based standards of care. In terms of clinician⏎use of outside tools, MHA urges HHS to clarify institutional duties, clinician responsibility,⏎and recommended governance controls. Regulatory oversight can help establish⏎4⏎⏎performance expectations, safety requirements, and accountability mechanisms for AI⏎developers, while giving healthcare providers greater confidence that approved tools meet⏎appropriate standards. Without such standards, providers may be left shouldering the⏎entirety of liability risk for AI-related harms, particularly when developers face limited⏎accountability for product performance. This lack of oversight not only increases risks to⏎patient safety but also creates additional liability exposure for healthcare providers who⏎seek to responsibly integrate innovative technologies into care.⏎For continuous learning and model updates, it is important that regulators set⏎expectations for material-update notifications, change logs, and re-validation⏎requirements. And for privacy and secondary use, MHA encourages HHS to clarify HIPAA-⏎consistent BAAs, de-identification, and restrictions on vendor model training using⏎PHI/limited datasets. HHS can reduce uncertainty through guidance and safe harbors tied⏎to risk-appropriate validation, monitoring, and transparency.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods,⏎metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care?⏎MHA members encourage the application of use-case–specific endpoints and metrics to⏎evaluate AI against outcomes that match the clinical intent (e.g., door-to-needle,⏎diagnostic accuracy, readmissions, patient-reported outcomes, patient experience; for⏎ambient tools: documentation time, message turnaround, burnout), rather than general⏎metrics. Additionally, there should be pre-deployment local validation across subgroups⏎with human-factors testing, workflow simulation, and bias analysis. Post-deployment⏎monitoring metrics should include safety incidents, drift, equity, override rates, and user⏎experience; and require vendor model cards and site-level telemetry. HHS should fund⏎grants/contracts for multi-site, real-world testbeds, benchmark registries, shared⏎protocols, and prize competitions. They should reinstate/expand SBIR-like funding for⏎paired developer-provider pilot projects to encourage technological innovation and⏎commercialization of products.⏎5. How can HHS best support private-sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, testing, credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎MHA recommends that HHS prioritize safety and endorse a neutral, standards-based⏎certification and credentialing framework that verifies transparency, intended-use⏎disclosures, update/change controls, and evaluation evidence. Regulators should⏎encourage industry-driven, real-world validation over test environment only results, and⏎support vendor and implementer credentialing for safety and lifecycle practices (e.g.,⏎change management, incident reporting, governance).⏎5⏎⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights⏎on quality and help reduce costs?⏎AI in ambient documentation has experienced strong early adoption and impact on⏎healthcare provider burden and “pajama time”, with promising retention/burnout⏎improvements in the longer-term and favorable costs relative to human scribes. It is⏎difficult to quantify hard savings beyond scribe substitution at the moment. In radiology, AI⏎has clear promise for targeted tasks (e.g., pulmonary embolism and large vessel occlusion⏎detection/triage), with mixed results in other areas.⏎Currently, AI integration and workflow are often the rate-limiting step in terms of⏎widespread adoption. Generative AI indirect clinical decision-making is not broadly⏎deployed at scale outside of research and pilot programs, and engaging prematurely⏎without further peer-reviewed validation studies could pose serious safety risks. The near-⏎term value of this is strongest in adjacent uses (summarization, drafting, inbox support)⏎with clinician oversight.⏎As for high-potential areas where AI could see broader adoption and impact,⏎language/medical interpretation tools, agentic population-health follow-up (e.g.,⏎readmissions outreach) and remote monitoring, and predictive analytics (e.g. for triage or⏎identifying high-risk patients), evaluated for safety, equity, and patient-centered outcomes⏎show promising outcomes in limited deployment. There is also significant opportunity to⏎use AI tools for back-end billing and coding, automated claims denial responses, and⏎informatics and interoperability functions such as mapping unstructured data to normative⏎value sets, routing schema cleanup, and grouper and order set maintenance.⏎7. Which roles, decision-makers, or governing bodies within healthcare organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎At the hospital and health system level, those who are most influential with regards to the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care include: AI governance committees; Chief Medical⏎Information Officers (CMIO) / Chief Nursing Informatics Officers (CNIO) / Chief Information⏎Officers (CIO); clinical department leaders; compliance/privacy staff; and quality/safety⏎leaders.⏎When considering administrative hurdles, hospitals have indicated that there is limited⏎operational bandwidth for the safe rollout of AI in clinical care, including training, change⏎management, and appropriate post-implementation monitoring of tools. Hospitals also⏎emphasized that leaders need to identify an AI strategy that is tied to their clinical⏎priorities. They are concerned that there is a risk that immature deployments erode⏎clinician trust. Moreover, the lack of hard ROI for most AI tools in clinical care today is a⏎6⏎⏎barrier to adoption. This is a place where funding to subsidize the use of tools with a soft,⏎yet unproven ROI and could help speed widespread adoption.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎MHA member hospitals and health systems recommend accelerating robust, scalable⏎FHIR APIs and Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine (DICOM) interoperability.⏎There needs to be support for standardized access to notes, orders, images, and⏎outcomes for evaluation. Additionally, there need to be appropriate safeguards around⏎sharing data with vendors. In terms of benchmarking and data access, HHS should enable⏎privacy-preserving data enclaves and de-identified datasets for external validation;⏎support common telemetry schemas for monitoring and benchmarking real-world⏎performance; and reduce friction in data-use agreements for lawful validation.⏎9. What challenges within healthcare do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Based on current use cases, patients recognize that AI deployment can lead to faster,⏎safer diagnosis and treatment, in addition to improved overall access, enhanced language⏎access, and more clinician engagement during patient encounters. Patients remain⏎concerned with issues of privacy, particularly for sensitive health information, in addition⏎to bias/fairness of algorithms and models used by AI. Additionally, patients want to have⏎clarity about when AI is being used.⏎With regards to transparency for patients on provider use of AI, MHA and our member⏎hospitals encourage the utilization and adoption of system-level notices (e.g., joint notice⏎of information practices) stating AI may be used; require contextual disclosures/consent⏎only where appropriate (e.g., two-party recording consent for ambient scribing; clear⏎labeling for patient-facing chatbots). Healthcare providers would like to avoid per-use⏎consent mandates that could let patients inadvertently opt in to lower-standard care as AI⏎use becomes embedded as a standard practice.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎MHA and our member hospitals and health systems encourage HHS to prioritize AI safety⏎and effectiveness “in the wild” using multi-site pragmatic trials with subgroup/equity⏎analyses, human-factors outcomes, and longitudinal drift monitoring.⏎Additionally, HHS would be well-served with real-world economic evidence in support of⏎the adoption of AI. There should be rigorous cost-effectiveness and ROI studies that link AI⏎7⏎⏎to throughput, access, and downstream utilization—evidence that can support payment⏎pathways for healthcare providers.⏎Finally, MHA and our members recommend prioritizing AI tools for research including:⏎clinical language/translation; radiology and time-critical triage; population-health follow-⏎up agents; and burden-reduction tools tied to measurable access and quality gains. We⏎also recommend prioritizing AI tools for back-end informatics and interoperability⏎functions such as mapping unstructured data to normative value sets, routing schema⏎cleanup, and grouper and order set maintenance.⏎Thank you for your consideration of these comments. Collectively, MHA and our members⏎look forward to engaging with HHS to protect patient safety and enable access to⏎healthcare through the use of artificial intelligence. Should you have any questions or⏎concerns, please reach out directly to Valerie Fleishman, Executive Vice President and⏎Chief Innovation Officer, Massachusetts Health & Hospital Association at⏎vfleishman@mhalink.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Valerie Fleishman, MBA⏎Executive Vice President and Chief Innovation Officer⏎Massachusetts Health & Hospital Association⏎Eric Alper, MD, SFHM, FACP, CPE⏎Senior Vice President, Chief Quality Officer, Chief Clinical Informatics Officer⏎UMass Memorial Health⏎Co-Chair, Massachusetts Health & Hospital Association Chief Medical Informatics Officer⏎Forum⏎Hannah K. Galvin, MD, FAAP, FAMIA, CHCIO, CDH-E⏎Chief Health Information Officer⏎Cambridge Health Alliance⏎Co-Chair, Massachusetts Health & Hospital Association Chief Medical Informatics Officer⏎Forum⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0185♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Doctronic, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Doctronic♔♔♔♔♔♔1460 Broadway⏎New York, NY 10036⏎www.doctronic.ai⏎February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎30 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Mr. Posnack-⏎Below please find the response of Doctronic to the Department’s Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence.⏎Doctronic is a provider of AI tools for the healthcare industry. Accuracy and quality of care are⏎our top priorities. Doctronic’s AI tools are designed to synthesize, analyze, and summarize⏎information, providing real-time insights and plans of action. Doctronic users may take the⏎communications that its AI tools provide to their healthcare providers, or they can schedule a⏎telehealth visit with physicians within Doctronic’s network. Over the past year, users have⏎accessed Doctronic’s AI tools nearly 23 million times.⏎Doctronic is secure, private, and HIPAA-compliant. Users can use it completely anonymously, or⏎sign up for a free or paid account to keep track of your chats and health summaries. Doctronic⏎does not train its AI tools on user data. Our AI-powered health assistant is trained exclusively on⏎peer-reviewed medical sources, and its treatment plans match those of board-certified clinicians⏎99.2% of the time.⏎Doctronic believes that artificial intelligence has the unique ability to both: (1) raise the floor of⏎quality healthcare, and (2) allow the provision of high-quality care at lower cost to payers and,⏎ultimately, to patients.⏎One overarching change to payment policy that we recommend based on our experience is a⏎restructuring of payment model for clinical engagements. Supported by our AI health assistant,⏎we have found that primary care telehealth visits with our human physician network take, on⏎average, less than five minutes for urgent care and 20 minutes for new patient primary care⏎visits. There is no reason for visits to be billed in fifteen minute increments, as these increments⏎create wasted cost for payers and lost opportunity for providers. Reducing payment increments⏎1⏎⏎would provide an incentive to payers to seek more efficient services. This policy change would⏎ideally be paired with the creation of a new cost code- at a much lower rate- for patient⏎engagement with an AI-based clinical engagements that facilitates shorter visits.⏎In addition to this broader point, Doctronic provides responses to the questions posed by the⏎RFI below:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The most significant barrier to private sector innovation in AI for health care is the lack of a clear⏎model for reimbursement for the clinical benefits offered by by AI tools. AI tools provide⏎numerous services that improve access to care, streamline the care process, and improve⏎quality of care. However, there are no independent codes on which to seek reimbursement for⏎many of these services. This creates two problems. First, because the costs of these services⏎are, in effect, built into traditional billing codes, there is a lack of transparency relating to the⏎benefits offered by AI. In addition, without a direct pathway to reimbursement, the incentives for⏎innovators to offer services directly are much lower. We recommend the creation of a new code⏎for AI tools engagement at a rate to be determined by the value saved by the cost of reduced⏎physician visits.⏎In addition to this barrier, contractual uncertainties also delay AI adoption. Three primary⏎contractual problems are:⏎● Lack of clarity regarding the appropriate apportionment of liability;⏎● Lack of clear standards for data ownership and data security; and⏎● Uncertain pathways with regard to continuity of care and portability of user health⏎information.⏎While questions of health data interoperability will not ultimately delay innovation in AI for health⏎care, the continued failure to enforce existing interoperability standards and timelines will reduce⏎the benefits that AI offers to the health care system.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎Doctronic recommends prioritizing the following payment changes:⏎● Create new billing codes for AI-performed clinical services, enabling direct⏎reimbursement for agentic care.⏎● Create new billing codes for clinician review of AI-performed clinical services.⏎● Change existing billing codes for clinical services to reduce minimum reimbursed visit to⏎5-minute increments.⏎Doctronic recommends prioritizing the following programmatic changes:⏎2⏎⏎● Clarify the appropriate use of AI technology to support State Medicaid goals, particularly⏎through Section 1115 Demonstration Waivers.⏎● Provide enhanced reimbursement to State Medicaid programs for the implementation of⏎AI technology that demonstrates long-term savings for the Medicaid program.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (⏎e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎HHS has already taken important steps to address novel legal and implementation issues⏎associated with non-medical devices through its most recent guidance on non-device clinical⏎decision supports. Please see our comments above relating to liability/indemnification, privacy,⏎and security issues.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎Doctronic believes that HHS should support and publicize the development of standards for the⏎evaluation of AI tools post-deployment. These standards should inform the private testing that⏎producers of AI tools should undertake. On the basis of the results of testing in accordance with⏎HHS standards, healthcare professionals should have sufficient information to exercise their⏎standard of care as to the deployment of AI tools on behalf of patients.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎Similar to FHIR guidance, HHS can best support effective AI use by providing and enforcing⏎appropriate standards for cybersecurity, data governance, and interoperability.⏎We also believe that validation of industry-driven testing and research findings will provide⏎greater trust in AI evaluation and efficacy. HHS can support these efforts by assessing private⏎sector evaluations and testing.⏎However, we believe that given the rapid progress of technological change, neither HHS nor an⏎external accreditation agency will be able to provide accreditation or certification that enhances⏎public trust in AI without unnecessarily stifling innovation. Instead of creating unnecessary⏎accreditation or certification pathways for AI models and products, we recommend HHS support⏎the validation of industry-driven testing and research.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short?⏎3⏎⏎Doctronic is unable to speak to tools outside of the context in we have conducted our own⏎evaluation. However, given our greater than 22 million user interactions, and our peer-reviewed⏎research findings that have identified that our AI-agent reaches the same treatment result as our⏎physician network 99.2% of the time, AI tools are capable of providing clinicians with clear and⏎reliable communication based on user generated inputs that allow for improved patient⏎experience, shorter visits, and lower systemic costs. We believe that our products have⏎exceeded performance and cost expectations.⏎What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes,⏎give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Based on the performance of our AI health assistant, we believe that AI tools have the ability to⏎significantly reduce administrative burden on health professionals in a number of areas. In⏎particular, with appropriate oversight, AI tools can improve:⏎● Care coordination⏎● Remote/continuous monitoring⏎● Claims processing⏎● Eligibility determination⏎● Prior authorization⏎● Non-urgent diagnostic tasks⏎● Medication management⏎● Supports on social determinants⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Currently, Doctronic provides communications that users can take to their healthcare providers⏎to improve access of care, quality of care, and patient confidence in their healthcare⏎experiences. Doctronic provides its users the option of using its AI tools anonymously or⏎forming an account with Doctronic to create a medical record on the Doctronic platform. For⏎those users who wish to use our AI tools anonymously, greater interoperability would be of⏎limited utility. However, those users who create Doctronic accounts and create medical records⏎on Doctronic would benefit greatly from the ability to integrate the medical records associated⏎with their Doctronic accounts with records held by other healthcare providers.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎The primary challenges that patients and caregivers have expressed to Doctronic as a⏎motivation to access our products focus on issues of cost and access.⏎Patients and caregivers confront a shortage of trained professionals and an increasing inability⏎to obtain high-quality care in a manner and time that is accessible. Doctronic users have⏎4⏎⏎expressed significant value in the ability to receive health information in a time and at a modality⏎of their choosing, whether this means simply accessing the AI health assistant, accessing a⏎teleheath visit immediately after using the health assistant, or using the communication⏎produced by the health assistant to streamline visits with their own primary care physician.⏎Below is a representative example of a user who experienced value from the AI tools:⏎Cost also remains a prohibitive factor for many to access the quality of care they deserve.⏎Patients and caregivers have expressed appreciation for products that provide high quality for⏎relatively low cost. The ability of AI to expand access to quality care at affordable prices should⏎remain the primary goal of adoption.⏎One concern that patients and caregivers have relating to AI is the fear of inaccurate⏎information. While Doctronic has high accuracy in the communications our products provide,⏎research suggests that general purpose LLMs not trained for specific clinical tasks don’t meet⏎the same level of accuracy.⏎5⏎⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Doctronic’s AI-powered health assistant is trained exclusively on peer-reviewed medical sources⏎and is not trained on user data. We have found our training methodology to be highly effective,⏎but generally AI tools improve with access to better, more accurate data. HHS should prioritize⏎the creation of safe, unbiased training data that will allow AI tools to improve their performance⏎without compromising patient data privacy. Synthetic data that helps train AI tools on rare⏎medical conditions or diseases would add significant value to many AI tools.⏎—⏎Thank you for your efforts in supporting innovation within health care. Doctronic has already⏎shown that AI tools offer significant benefits to users who lack affordable healthcare options.⏎We are happy to discuss our recommendations further at your convenience.⏎Sincerely,⏎CC: Dr. Thomas Keane, Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology⏎Dr. Mehmet Oz, Administrator, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎Dr. Marty Makary, Commissioner of Food and Drugs, U.S. Food and Drug Administration⏎Dr. Jay Bhattacharya, Director, National Institutes of Health⏎Paula M. Stannard, Director, Office for Civil Rights⏎Christi Grimm, Inspector General, Office of Inspector General⏎Amy Gleason, Administrator, United States DOGE Service⏎6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0186♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-12T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Allen♔♔Oregon♔♔United States♔Anxiety and Depression Association of America♔♔♔♔♔♔The patients we hear from at the Anxiety and Depression Association of America are not asking for faster diagnostics. They are asking for someone to sit with them.⏎⏎That sounds simple, maybe even naive given the scope of what HHS is considering here. But I keep coming back to it because the framing of this RFI — accelerating adoption, streamlining pathways, reducing friction — treats the problem of AI in clinical care as essentially a logistics problem. Get the tools into clinics faster. Figure out the reimbursement codes. And I think that framing is missing something that our members, both patients and clinicians, talk about constantly.⏎⏎Mental health care in particular is a relationship before it is anything else. I have been at this organization for over a decade and I have sat through more focus groups and listening sessions than I can count, and the thing people describe when they talk about a provider who actually helped them is almost never about accuracy of diagnosis. It is about feeling like another human being was truly present with them. Could read when they were deflecting. Knew when to push and when to back off. That is not a data problem. No training dataset captures what a good therapist picks up in the first five minutes of a session.⏎⏎What concerns me specifically about accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings — and this is where I want to focus because I think HHS is actually asking about implementation barriers, not just cheerleading for the technology — is that there is a kind of false objectivity that comes with algorithmic output. A physician who gets an AI-generated recommendation handed to them is in a weird position. The recommendation looks authoritative. It came from a system. And the research on automation bias is pretty clear that clinicians, under time pressure, tend to defer to that output even when their own clinical judgment is telling them something different.⏎⏎The Texas Medical Association ran a survey of over 4,200 physicians in 2024 and found that 63% were concerned that AI diagnostic tools had produced or could produce inequitable recommendations for patients in rural or low-income communities. And 41% of respondents said their patients had never been formally told that AI played a role in their assessment. That is not a minor implementation detail. That is a consent problem. And in mental health contexts specifically, where the relationship between provider and patient is doing a lot of the clinical work, having that relationship quietly mediated by an algorithm the patient doesn't know about feels like a real breach.⏎⏎I am not opposed to AI tools in healthcare categorically. There are probably administrative applications that make sense and free up clinician time. But the RFI keeps talking about clinical care, about diagnostic support, about treatment recommendations. That is a different thing entirely. When we are talking about a patient who is sitting in an office scared and not sure they want to be there, the presence of that clinician — the fact that a specific human being with their own judgment and accountability is looking at that person — is not incidental to the care. It is the care.⏎⏎HHS should at minimum require that any AI-assisted clinical assessment come with patient notification before the encounter, not buried in intake paperwork. And there should be a straightforward mechanism for patients to ask how a recommendation was generated. I am not sure what the right regulatory vehicle for that is, and I understand there are competing considerations around workflow and cost, but the current baseline of essentially no requirement is not acceptable.⏎⏎The other thing I would flag is that the research base on AI in behavioral health specifically is thin. Really thin. Most of what is cited in conversations like this comes from radiology or pathology, where the task is more bounded. Depression screening, risk assessment for self-harm, medication management for anxiety disorders — these are messier clinical problems and the evidence that AI improves outcomes in these areas is not there yet. I would like to see HHS require that any accelerated adoption pathway for AI in behavioral health settings be tied to prospective outcome data collection, not just post-market surveillance that may or may not be acted on.⏎⏎We can submit more detailed comments if HHS publishes a more targeted RFI on behavioral health applications specifically. Our contact for follow-up is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0187♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Psychological Association Services Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Psychological Association Services Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) (collectively,⏎ASTP/ONC), Department of Health and Human Services.⏎Subject: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted Electronically⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎The American Psychological Association Services, Inc. (APA) respectfully submits the⏎following response to your Request for Information. APA Services is the companion⏎organization of the American Psychological Association, which is the nation’s largest scientific⏎and professional nonprofit organization representing the discipline and profession of psychology,⏎as well as over 190,000 members and affiliates who are clinicians, researchers, educators,⏎consultants, and students in psychological science. APASI emphasizes that while AI offers⏎transformative potential for diagnostic precision and administrative efficiency, its deployment⏎must be governed by the foundational principles of psychological science. To ensure these tools⏎are effective, ethical, and equitable, the federal government must prioritize a human-centric⏎regulatory framework that safeguards patient safety and restores public trust.⏎APASI recognizes AI’s immense potential to revolutionize critical sectors, especially health⏎care. AI can enhance diagnostic precision, expand access to behavioral health treatment, and⏎alleviate administrative burdens that cause provider burnout. However, a sizeable barrier to the⏎development, deployment, and adoption of beneficial AI is the profound and justified lack of⏎public trust. As the RFI notes, "foster[ing] public trust" is "critical" for adoption. This trust is⏎fragile because the current market is flooded with unregulated, direct-to-consumer AI products⏎that make deceptive claims and pose significant risks.⏎The RFI correctly identifies "lack of regulatory clarity" and "regulatory mismatches" as primary⏎barriers. This ambiguity has created a digital Wild West that hinders the adoption of safe and⏎effective AI tools. To promote innovation, we must address these gaps by establishing clear,⏎evidence-based guardrails. This requires embedding psychological science—the study of human⏎cognition, behavior, and emotion—into the entire AI lifecycle -- the end-to-end process of⏎developing and deploying an AI system.⏎Below, we identify specific barriers and recommendations corresponding to the RFI's questions.⏎⏎I. Regulation⏎HHS must establish a regulatory framework that prioritizes patient safety and public trust.⏎• Prohibit Misrepresentation of AI as Licensed Professionals: A primary barrier to trust⏎is the "regulatory mismatch" allowing AI chatbots to misrepresent themselves as⏎"psychologists" or "therapists". For instance, a chatbots on a popular platform presenting⏎itself as a "psychologist," engaged in millions of chats and, in some cases, appeared to⏎validate users’ violent thoughts. Congress and HHS should make it illegal for AI to⏎generate fraudulent credentials or misrepresent itself as a licensed professional.⏎• Mandate Transparency and "Human-in-the-Loop": AI should be regulated as a tool⏎to augment, not replace, the clinical judgment and therapeutic relationships that are the⏎bedrock of quality care. Regulations must mandate persistent disclosure that a user is⏎interacting with an AI system and ensure a qualified human remains in the loop for high-⏎stakes clinical decisions.⏎• Strict Transparency and Informed Consent: It is imperative that more is known about⏎the data and methods used to train AI systems in the healthcare setting. Developers must⏎also be required to clearly, conspicuously, and persistently disclose to users that they are⏎interacting with an AI system, not a human. In clinical settings, authorities must obtain⏎informed consent, so patients understand how AI tools like predictive algorithms are⏎being used and specifically, what personally identifiable information is being retained⏎and accountability for information being released to third parties.⏎• Professional Competence and Liability: Policy should support continuing education for⏎the workforce, as professionals utilizing AI must maintain competence in the⏎technologies they employ. Over-reliance on technology without proper training,⏎oversight, and disclosure can lead to harm.⏎II. Reimbursement⏎Payment policies must incentivize evidence-based, high-value interventions rather than mere⏎engagement metrics.⏎• Incentivize Safety and Efficacy over Volume: Reimbursement should be tied to robust⏎clinical outcome data—such as remission rates and improvements in health-related⏎quality of life—rather than corporate metrics like user engagement or number of⏎downloads.⏎• Support Interdisciplinary Care: Payment models should incentivize the use of AI to⏎expand the reach of clinicians within a human-led care team, ensuring technology does⏎not displace the essential therapeutic relationships that are the bedrock of quality care.⏎This is essential in an environment where states are moving to limit the ability of⏎qualified providers to use evidence-based technologies to aid in the delivery of care.⏎III. Research & Development⏎The pace of AI development is far outpacing independent scientific research.⏎⏎• Embed Psychological Science in the AI Lifecycle: Policy should mandate that AI⏎developers collaborate with psychological scientists during the design phase to mitigate⏎risks related to cognitive bias and emotional manipulation. This same integration should⏎take place among federal regulatory, investigatory, and research teams tasked with⏎developing AI policies.⏎• Mitigate Bias and Disparities: AI models often reflect biases from non-representative⏎training data, such as an algorithm that unfairly assigned lower risk scores to Black⏎patients because it used healthcare costs as a proxy for illness. HHS must require⏎rigorous, independent pre-deployment testing and ongoing monitoring for harms across⏎diverse populations to ensure AI reduces, rather than entrenches, health disparities.⏎• Investment in Independent Research: HHS should prioritize significant federal⏎investment in independent, longitudinal research to understand the long-term impacts of⏎human-AI interaction on mental health and social development. This research must be⏎free from corporate conflicts of interest and facilitate data collection and transparency⏎requirements for companies seeking to provide health-care related products reliant on AI⏎technologies.⏎IV. Data Privacy and Vulnerable Populations⏎Current privacy laws are "structurally incompatible" with AI’s capability to infer intimate details⏎from vast datasets.⏎• Ensure HIPAA Compliance and Data Privacy: Current privacy frameworks are⏎structurally incompatible with AI’s ability to infer intimate mental health details from⏎collected data. HHS must establish a right to “mental privacy,” safeguarding data that can⏎indicate a person's emotional state without explicit patient consent. Furthermore, many⏎current AI chatbots are not HIPAA compliant, leaving patient privacy and security of⏎their health data unprotected by federal law.⏎• "Safe-by-Default" Standards for Youth: Protections for children and adolescents must⏎be the default setting, not an opt-in. This includes limiting manipulative design features⏎that exploit the "neurodevelopmental mismatch" of adolescence, characterized by high⏎social reward seeking and low impulse control.⏎Conclusion⏎APASI believes AI holds the potential to create a more accessible, effective, and equitable⏎healthcare system. The path to realizing this potential is not through deregulation, but through⏎the creation of a clear, evidence-based framework that builds public trust. The current lack of⏎regulatory clarity is the primary key concern to AI adoption.⏎We must ensure AI is a tool to augment, not replace, the clinical judgment and therapeutic⏎relationships that are the bedrock of quality care. The APASI and its member scientists stand⏎ready to collaborate with OSTP to develop and enact policies that advance these principles.⏎⏎Thank you again for the opportunity to weigh in on these questions. To the extent that our⏎psychologists can serve as a resource to your office, please feel free to reach out to Corbin Evans⏎(Deputy Chief of Advocacy for Science & Technology) at CEvans@apa.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Katherine B. McGuire⏎Chief Advocacy Officer⏎American Psychological Association⏎Services, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0188♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Leap Orbit, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Leap Orbit♔♔♔♔♔♔7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎Response to RFI (2025-⏎23641)⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care⏎Table of Contents⏎Cover Letter ..................................................................................................................................................... 2⏎Executive Summary ...................................................................................................................................... 4⏎Introduction: About Leap Orbit................................................................................................................ 5⏎Part I: Feedback on Primary Approaches for Federal Action ......................................................... 6⏎Part II: Responses to Select Questions ................................................................................................ 12⏎Conclusion ..................................................................................................................................................... 15⏎Page | 1⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎Cover Letter⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎(ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ONC) (collectively, ASTP/ONC)⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW⏎Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎As technologists, architects, and implementers of systems that contend daily with the⏎complexities of health data, Leap Orbit, LLC, is pleased to respond to ASTP/ONC’s⏎Request for Information (RFI) regarding Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care. Our response is predicated on a core belief: that the⏎vision for a digitally integrated healthcare future hinges on getting the foundational⏎data layers right.⏎In our response, we offer insights into key areas we believe are critical to this mission -⏎from voluntary frameworks to advance market-driven trust, to the need for technical⏎and administrative metadata and its implications for information blocking, to the⏎importance of incentivizing “AI-Ready” data infrastructure. Our experience developing⏎and operating platforms like Convergent, a provider data management (PDM) system⏎that has improved data integrity for our clients at a foundational level, informs these⏎insights, as does our work involving extensive real-world implementation of HL7 Fast⏎Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) standards, particularly the Da Vinci PDEX⏎Plan Net Implementation Guide (IG). These insights are further informed by our⏎collaborations with federal entities, including the Assistant Secretary for Technology⏎Policy (ASTP), the Health Resources and Services Administration (HRSA) (notably on the⏎UDS+ Modernization initiative), the Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services⏎Administration (SAMHSA), and the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC).⏎Page | 2⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎We use our work not as a product showcase, but as a case study in how specific⏎technological approaches can address systemic problems and inform sound policies to⏎accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to contribute to this vital conversation and to share the⏎lessons learned from our efforts to make shared health data accurate, accessible, and⏎useful for all Americans.⏎Sincerely,⏎David Finney⏎Partner & Co-Founder @ Leap Orbit, LLC⏎(443) 714-2797 | dfinney@leaporbit.com | www.leaporbit.com⏎Page | 3⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎Executive Summary⏎The Paradox of Connectivity: Why Better Health Data Infrastructure is⏎Non-Negotiable.⏎The primary bottleneck to clinical AI is no longer the complexity of the algorithms, but⏎the persistent “trust friction” created by fragmented, unverified data pipelines. The core⏎challenge, as Leap Orbit has found in its work, is a misalignment in how we approach⏎foundational data infrastructure. HHS, through this RFI, has wisely focused attention on⏎these underpinnings.⏎Through hard experience, we have learned that the acceleration of Artificial Intelligence⏎(AI) in clinical care is primarily hindered by a lack of "AI-Ready" foundational data. For AI⏎to be safe, equitable, and effective, it requires a "bedrock" of accurate, accessible, and⏎interoperable data with traceable provenance. Recognizing this need, we recommend⏎that HHS focus its initial efforts on three "Foundational Prerequisites":⏎ Market-Driven Trust Frameworks: Move from prescriptive "black box"⏎oversight toward fostering voluntary, industry-led standards for the integrity and⏎provenance of the data pipelines feeding clinical AI. This approach ensures⏎transparency without the weight of redundant regulatory mandates.⏎ Incentivizing Data Integrity: Structure payment policy to reward "Consent-⏎Aware" and "AI-Ready" infrastructure. By establishing reimbursement for digital⏎consent orchestration and data quality, HHS can incentivize the maintenance of⏎the high-fidelity provider and patient data necessary for autonomous AI to⏎function.⏎ Implementation Sandboxes for R&D: Foster and leverage public-private⏎technical communities to bridge the gap between high-level policy and real-⏎world implementation. These "sandboxes" allow for the rapid iteration of⏎emerging standards, like the Model Context Protocol (MCP) and FHIR, ensuring⏎they are refined by the market before being broadly adopted.⏎Page | 4⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎Introduction: About Leap Orbit⏎Founded in 2015, Leap Orbit, LLC, is an AI-first U.S.-based healthcare IT firm dedicated⏎to solving intricate health data challenges. We build and implement innovative⏎solutions, including our Convergent Provider Data Management platform and the RxGov⏎prescription drug monitoring (PDMP) suite. Our focus is on leveraging modern, cloud-⏎native architectures—primarily Microsoft Azure—and an Application Programming⏎Interface (API)-priority design philosophy (see developer docs). Increasingly, we see AI⏎not just as a tool, but as a foundational capability that we embed into the core of every⏎product to deliver smarter, more adaptive solutions that improve care and enhance⏎compliance.⏎Central to our work is a deep commitment to interoperability, exemplified by our⏎leadership in the real-world application of HL7 FHIR standards. This is demonstrated in⏎our contributions to national initiatives like HRSA's UDS+ Modernization and our⏎current and past work with ASTP, SAMHSA, and the CDC. Our Convergent platform, for⏎example, natively supports FHIR R4 and conforms to the Da Vinci PDEX Plan Net IG,⏎serving as a testament to our approach. For clients, this has resulted in successfully⏎standardizing over 98% of provider and facility addresses, a foundational step for⏎accurate directory services.⏎As a company committed to pushing boundaries and building what’s next, we believe in⏎"glass box" data automation—providing transparency and traceable data provenance—⏎because trust is paramount in health data. Our culture of experimentation and⏎innovation drives our perspective, which is shaped by the technical realities of building⏎and deploying solutions designed to make health data accurate, accessible, and useful.⏎Our commitment to these principles is further underscored by third-party validations⏎such as the Electronic Healthcare Network Accreditation Commission (EHNAC) Privacy &⏎Security Accreditation and DirectTrust Privacy & Security Re-Accreditation for our⏎services.⏎Page | 5⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎Part I: Feedback on Primary Approaches for Federal Action⏎Regulation: Impact of Current HHS Frameworks on AI Adoption⏎Current regulations often focus on the clinical end product(s) of an AI tool but neglect⏎the upstream pipeline – i.e., the process by which clinical data is ingested, mapped, and⏎cleaned or transformed before it reaches an AI model. By having limited or no visibility⏎into how the clinical outputs are generated or informed by AI, a vacuum of trust is⏎created among users. The following approaches can markedly improve the upstream⏎data pipeline and therefore mitigate the trust gap that currently impairs AI adoption in⏎clinical care settings:⏎ Voluntary Integrity Frameworks to Advance Market-Driven Trust: In⏎alignment with the deregulatory goals of HTI-5, HHS should pivot away from⏎prescriptive transparency mandates in the Health IT Certification Program and⏎instead focus on encouraging voluntary transparency for the “upstream” pipeline⏎(e.g., data cleansing, identity matching, and provider roster ingestion). Rather⏎than requiring "model cards" or specific source attributes by law, HHS should⏎foster an environment where traceable data provenance and confidence scores⏎are recognized as market-differentiators. In a deregulated environment, the⏎market requires provenance transparency to manage risk. This is achieved⏎through foundational data engineering - specifically, repeatable FHIR ingestion⏎pipelines that “bake” metadata and consent enforcement into the data stream at⏎the point of origin, rather than attempting to fix data quality downstream. Our⏎experience with the Convergent platform and CareLoaDr AI indicates that trust is⏎a prerequisite for automation; users are only willing to adopt AI in day-to-day use⏎when they can see “behind the curtain” of the system to understand specifically⏎how a record was matched or a provider’s credentials were verified.⏎ Enabling AI-Ready Interoperability through Automated Access: AI systems⏎require high-fidelity metadata to autonomously "use" clinical data without⏎"special effort". We strongly support the proposed revisions to 45 CFR § 171.102⏎in HTI-5 to explicitly include automated means, including autonomous AI and⏎Page | 6⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎agentic systems, within the definitions of "access," "exchange," and "use". This⏎modernization recognizes that AI is now a first-class "actor" in the health data⏎ecosystem.⏎To fully realize this vision, HHS should clarify, through sub-regulatory guidance⏎under the 21st Century Cures Act information blocking provisions and 45 CFR⏎Part 171, that failure to exchange clinically necessary provenance, integrity,⏎semantic, and consent metadata, when such metadata is reasonably available and⏎necessary for safe automated or clinical use, may constitute a limitation on “use”⏎under the definition of access, exchange, and use (AEU). We recommend that⏎HHS require that the metadata meet the following three criteria to avoid⏎overburdening implementers while enabling innovation:⏎ Necessary for clinical safety;⏎ Necessary for interpretability or automation;⏎ Already representable in existing standards (e.g., FHIR Provenance, Consent,⏎AuditEvent, USCDI elements).⏎A minimal set of metadata across clinical care AI use cases should include the⏎following categories:⏎ Provenance metadata (e.g., source, attribution),⏎ Integrity metadata (e.g., currency, completeness, validation status,⏎encounter context),⏎ Semantic metadata (e.g., code system, terminology version,⏎transformations, unit normalization, original text for coded elements),⏎ Sensitivity metadata (e.g., consent status, use restrictions, sensitivity⏎category, redisclosure constraints, revocation timestamp), and⏎ AI Generation metadata (e.g., AI processing indicator, model identifier,⏎confidence score, human validation status).⏎Current information blocking concerns often stem from a "black box" approach⏎where data is exchanged without context. Leap Orbit’s work with the Convergent⏎Page | 7⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎platform and the FHIR Da Vinci PDEX Plan Net Implementation Guide (IG)⏎demonstrates that when rich metadata is provided, AI systems can autonomously⏎trace errors back to source files and assign confidence scores. This level of⏎transparency prevents the "misuse and abuse" of information blocking exceptions⏎by ensuring that third-party AI software has the technical context required to be⏎safe and effective.⏎Finally, we recommend that HHS promote voluntary, standardized metadata⏎headers that includes the minimal set of metadata we describe above for AI-to-AI⏎communication – i.e., a “cover sheet” for every piece of data exchanged between⏎AI agents. An example might be a clinical AI agent designed to predict certain⏎conditions that receives lab results as raw JSON data from disparate sources;⏎having standardized metadata headers that contains provenance, quality scores,⏎and privacy/consent information enables the AI agent to read and digest that⏎data instantly without manual mapping. As a practical application, Leap Orbit⏎recommends that HHS pilot a “Structured Insight Extractor” initiative. This⏎program would utilize Large Language Models (LLMs) to ingest legacy, semi-⏎structured data - such as CCDA clinical summaries or Electronic Lab Reporting⏎(ELR) data - and extract them into high-fidelity FHIR resources. By automating⏎this upstream pipeline, HHS can ensure that clinical AI models are fed⏎standardized data with clear provenance, rather than being forced to infer clinical⏎context from unstructured noise.⏎ Standardizing Specialty Data as an Innovation Accelerator: HHS should⏎prioritize the use of the USCDI+ model (as pioneered in the SAMSHA/ASTP⏎Behavioral Health Information Technology Pilot) to establish voluntary,⏎predictable "data floors" for specialty care. By standardizing these specialty⏎bundles via USCDI+, HHS provides a predictable data floor. This eliminates the⏎need for every AI developer to build custom, site-specific ingestion code,⏎effectively lowering the barrier to entry for innovative clinical tools. The HTI-5⏎Proposed Rule seeks to reset the Certification Program's scope to prioritize FHIR-⏎Page | 8⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎based APIs that expand the data available for clinical efficiency. USCDI+ strongly⏎aligns with this goal by providing the structured context that Large Language⏎Models (LLMs) and agentic AI systems struggle to infer reliably. Rather than a⏎regulatory checkbox, USCDI+ should be positioned as a market-driven resource⏎that improves AI accuracy and reduces the heavy burden of constant human-in-⏎the-loop validation.⏎Reimbursement: Levers to Promote AI Adoption⏎The greatest administrative burden in clinical AI adoption is the “data ingestion” tax – or⏎the cost of cleaning “dirty” data. Payment policy and incentives should be used to lower⏎the burden of this tax and reward the quality of the underlying data ecosystem.⏎ Incentivizing "AI-Ready" Data Infrastructure: CMS should consider including⏎"Data Integrity Scores" as part of Value-Based Care (VBC) benchmarks or Merit-⏎Based Incentive Payment System (MIPS) reporting. For example, organizations⏎that can demonstrate a high level of accuracy in their provider directories and⏎consent frameworks should be eligible for incentive payments. Furthermore, HHS⏎should use payment policy to reward targeted applied machine learning that⏎improves the “ingredients” of the clinical record. Instead of leading with bespoke,⏎high-risk clinical modeling, the private sector should be incentivized to use Data⏎Science capacity to automate normalization, confidence scoring, and identity⏎resolution. Once the underlying data model is stable and identity is resolved with⏎high confidence, the risk profile for downstream clinical AI interventions is⏎significantly lowered. This trusted data foundation has a direct impact on payers,⏎providers, and patients. For payers, it enables compliance with federal mandates⏎and improves operational efficiency; for one national payer, automating CMS 4I⏎reporting through our solutions saved an estimated $240,000 per year and⏎reduced a four-week manual process to under 48 hours. For providers, it reduces⏎the administrative burden of attesting to their information across countless⏎systems. For patients, it means directory tools are reliable, which is crucial for⏎building trust and ensuring access to care. Organizations using autonomous AI⏎Page | 9⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎systems to automate care coordination and reporting should be eligible for⏎higher shared savings or performance bonuses if those systems leverage a⏎standardized, consent-aware infrastructure to improve patient outcomes.⏎ Incentivizing AI-Driven "Consent Orchestration": AI models, particularly those⏎used for behavioral health integration or sensitive environmental factors affecting⏎health, require data that is often protected by strict privacy laws like 42 CFR Part⏎2 (substance use) or state-specific mental health statutes. Currently, because⏎there is no standardized, automated way to orchestrate this consent across⏎different systems, this high-value data is often excluded from AI training sets and⏎real-time clinical feeds to avoid compliance risk.⏎HHS should consider incentivizing the adoption of FHIR-native consent⏎management (like our Consentric model) by establishing reimbursement codes⏎that reimburse providers for the use of AI tools that demonstrate "Consent-⏎Awareness" - the ability to programmatically respect granular patient restrictions⏎in real-time. By incentivizing the use of automated, standards-based consent⏎solutions, HHS can accelerate AI adoption by providing a scalable, real-time⏎mechanism to ensure that care delivery across networks like TEFCA is always⏎“consent-aware”, thereby reducing legal risk for providers while also allowing⏎patients to maintain control over their sensitive clinical narratives. HHS could⏎operationalize this through a “TEFCA Agentic Orchestrator” initiative. This⏎program would deploy autonomous AI agents across TEFCA to perform real-time,⏎consent-aware data surfacing. By filtering the massive volumes of network-⏎exchanged data to present only the most relevant, authorized information to a⏎clinician, this initiative would demonstrate how AI can move from being a source⏎of noise to a tool used for clinical coordination.⏎Research & Development: Public Private Partnerships and Pilot Programs⏎Technical standards and reimbursement incentives alone do not drive adoption;⏎Communities of Practice do. Leap Orbit recommends that HHS invest in⏎“Implementation Science" through national or regional technical communities whose⏎Page | 10⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎mission is to pilot and fine tune FHIR standards, ensuring they are truly “AI ready”⏎before wider implementation.⏎ The "Technical Community" Model for R&D: HHS should consider funding⏎permanent National Technical Communities (modeled after the HRSA UDS+ UTC)⏎to act as iterative implementation sandboxes. These test cooperatives allow⏎federal agencies to test – and revise – emerging FHIR Implementation Guides⏎(IGs) and newer standards like the Model Context Protocol (MCP) with real-world⏎vendors and providers.⏎HRSA’s UDS+ Unified Test Cooperative is an example of how a group of⏎providers, administrators, and health IT vendors collectively iterated on a new,⏎automated reporting standard leveraging FHIR with high adoption among⏎community health centers (CHCs) in its first year of implementation. Rapidly⏎iterating and refining FHIR IGs through these technical communities will feed the⏎“flywheel” of AI adoption by providing a bedrock of consistent "AI-ready" data⏎necessary to support the autonomous agentic AI systems now recognized under⏎modernized information blocking rules.⏎ Public-Private Partnerships for "Specialty AI Bundles": HHS should consider⏎using pilot programs (similar to the BHIT Pilot Program) to research how⏎specialized datasets, such as behavioral health or sensitive environmental factors⏎affecting health can be harmonized for AI consumption. This "Pilot-to-Standard"⏎approach ensures that federal R&D investments result in deployable, high-impact⏎clinical tools that move beyond "document transport" to the delivery of context-⏎aware data resources that fuel clinical efficiency and reduce provider burnout.⏎Page | 11⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎Part II: Responses to Select Questions⏎Q2: “What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes⏎should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care⏎and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to⏎augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide⏎specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Payment Policy: As mentioned above, HHS should establish specific reimbursement⏎pathways that recognize Digital Consent Management as a critical clinical support⏎service – i.e., reimbursing providers that utilize FHIR-native consent orchestration tools.⏎By establishing codes for the real-time orchestration of patient privacy preferences, CMS⏎can provide the financial bridge for providers to move beyond all-or-nothing data⏎exchange and adopt high-value AI interventions that are fully compliant with 42 CFR⏎Part 2. Through this reimbursement, providers are more incentivized to adopt⏎automated, standards-based tools that allow clinical AI to safely scale across networks⏎like TEFCA.⏎Programmatic Design: Additionally, HHS/CMS should incorporate "Data Readiness and⏎Provenance Scores" into Value-Based Care (VBC) benchmarks and reporting. Currently,⏎organizations lack a financial incentive to invest in technical metadata (e.g., provenance,⏎confidence scores, and semantic tags) that makes clinical records consumable by⏎autonomous systems without "special effort". Prioritizing the use of AI-assisted⏎ingestion (e.g., CareLoaDr AI) would automate the administrative burden of data⏎cleansing while providing "Glass Box" transparency. In an era of deregulated certification⏎(as emphasized in HTI-5) where "model cards" are being removed, the market requires⏎market-driven integrity scores to manage risk and helps to ensure that the appropriate⏎clinical care is being delivered.⏎Page | 12⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎Q5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry driven testing, and credentialing) to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should position itself as the "Facilitator of Industry-Led Testing & Benchmarking."⏎The most impactful support HHS can provide is to create the "Sandbox Environments"⏎where the private sector can voluntarily test the trustworthiness and interoperability of⏎their AI tools against real-world, standardized datasets. Specifically, HHS can best⏎support the private sector by funding National Testing Collectives (modeled after our⏎work with the HRSA UDS+ UTC). These collectives act as a bridge between federal policy⏎and private-sector innovation. Additionally, HHS should support an "AI-Ready"⏎designation - not a mandatory certification, but a voluntary testing badge achieved⏎through these technical communities.⏎Our work as frontline implementers and deep experience in convening diverse⏎stakeholders proves that industry-driven testing is most successful when it uses a⏎phased, incremental approach to iterate data standards before they are broadly⏎adopted.⏎Further, as HTI-5 expands the definition of "access" to include autonomous AI and⏎agentic systems, the primary barrier becomes identity and trust. HHS can support the⏎private sector by endorsing open standards that "credential" these agents.⏎We recommend that HHS promote development and voluntary adoption of a⏎standardized, FHIR-aligned AI Interaction Profile that enables secure, consent-aware⏎retrieval of clinical context and structured return of AI-generated outputs with⏎appropriate provenance and attribution metadata.⏎Using existing authority under the 21st Century Cures Act certification program and⏎related sub-regulatory guidance, HHS should encourage health IT developers to support⏎Page | 13⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎standardized AI interaction capabilities that prevent proprietary lock-in and ensure⏎auditable, interoperable, and safe AI integration.⏎HHS should avoid mandating any specific private protocol or requiring disclosure of⏎proprietary algorithms. Instead, it should promote open standards alignment,⏎conformance testing, and reference implementations through cooperative agreements⏎and federal funding mechanisms while allowing emerging standards such as the Model⏎Context Protocol (MCP), which acts as a "universal interface" for AI agents, to mature.⏎MCP allows AI to securely interact with clinical data while maintaining a⏎cryptographically signed audit trail and serves as a standardized “handshake” between⏎AI agents and data sources, such as FHIR - ensuring that context is preserved as⏎information moves between disparate systems. By prioritizing outcomes over methods⏎through standards alignment and testing, HHS can enable the private sector to⏎"credential" their AI tools, ensuring every action is permissioned, auditable, and⏎traceable, without foreclosing industry innovation and refinement.⏎Q8: “Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please⏎consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.”⏎The scalability trap must be solved for AI to move from a "bespoke pilot" to a scalable⏎clinical utility. Currently, every new AI deployment requires custom, site-specific data⏎mapping that creates a significant training debt. Enhanced interoperability fuels AI by⏎transforming fragmented information into “clean” data, i.e., data that is normalized,⏎attributed, and consent-cleared at the point of ingestion.⏎Enhanced interoperability widens market opportunities by eliminating the “bespoke⏎training debt” typical of current AI deployments. By prioritizing repeatable FHIR⏎ingestion pipelines, developers can ensure that provenance and consent are handled⏎programmatically. This allows AI teams to focus on targeted applied machine learning⏎for identity resolution and data stability, rather than spending much of their R&D⏎Page | 14⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎budget on site-specific data cleansing. This approach, moving from custom code to⏎repeatable pipelines, is the key to making clinical AI deflationary and scalable.⏎When tools like CareLoaDr AI can use "Glass Box" machine learning to automate the⏎mapping of complex provider rosters into standardized FHIR formats, it reduces the cost⏎of entry for all AI developers. To catalyze this shift, we propose a “National Golden⏎Provider Record” initiative. This multi-payer pilot would use AI to harmonize disparate⏎administrative rosters and credentialing feeds into a single, trusted source of truth. By⏎resolving provider identity at the point of ingestion, HHS would solve the scalability trap⏎for the market, providing AI developers with a predictable foundation of accurate⏎provider data and eliminating the need for redundant, site-specific identity resolution.⏎Enhanced interoperability also fuels research that supports whole-person care, as “plus”⏎categories of interoperability, such as behavioral health or sensitive environmental⏎factors affecting health are often hampered by data gaps. We recommend accelerating⏎the domain-specific extensions of USCDI+. Our experience managing the SAMHSA/ASTP⏎BHIT Pilot shows that these "specialty bundles" are the essential ingredients for⏎research-grade AI.⏎Additionally, to fuel large-scale research, HHS should prioritize Bulk FHIR for population-⏎level datasets, ensuring AI models can be trained on representative, cross-system data⏎without manual intervention.⏎Conclusion⏎The task of accelerating AI adoption as part of clinical care is a task of getting the⏎fundamentals right. While emerging technologies capture the imagination, Leap Orbit's⏎experience building foundational data systems like Convergent reinforces the lesson⏎that sustainable innovation rests upon a bedrock of accurate, accessible, and⏎interoperable core information. The challenges in achieving this for data as complex and⏎dynamic as all providers are significant, but as our own experience shows, they are not⏎insurmountable.⏎Page | 15⏎⏎7067 Columbia Gateway Drive⏎Suite 190⏎Columbia, MD 21046⏎inspired@leaporbit.com⏎As Leap Orbit’s experience with ASTP, HRSA, and the CDC demonstrates, the path to a⏎high-performing, AI-enabled health system begins with a relentless focus on the⏎foundational data architecture. By addressing the "prerequisites" of identity, consent,⏎and data integrity, HHS can ensure that AI becomes a trusted partner in clinical care⏎rather than a source of administrative complexity. The questions posed in this RFI are⏎central to that endeavor. As a company committed to tackling these complex data⏎challenges, we can collectively and incrementally shepherd approaches that support AI⏎adoption in clinical care (and/or adjacent) settings. We welcome continued engagement⏎on how to best achieve these shared, critical objectives.⏎Page | 16♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0189♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Corewell Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Corewell Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (0955-AA13)⏎Sent electronically via: http://www.regulations.gov⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎Corewell Health appreciates the opportunity to provide comments on the “Request for⏎Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care.” Corewell Health is a Michigan-based nonprofit integrated health system⏎with a team of 65,000+ dedicated people including more than 12,000 physicians and⏎advanced practice providers and more than 16,000 nurses providing care and services in⏎21 hospitals and 300+ outpatient locations. In addition, as an integrated health system,⏎Corewell Health includes Priority Health, a health plan that insures more than 1.3 million⏎lives. Corewell Health is not only Michigan’s largest health system but also Michigan’s⏎largest private employer. Through experience and collaboration, we are reimagining a⏎better, more equitable model of health and wellness. We submit these comments⏎through the lens of an integrated health system.⏎We appreciate that HHS is considering a multifaceted approach to encouraging the⏎responsible adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) through regulation, reimbursement,⏎and research and development. In our experience, the most significant barriers to AI⏎adoption are not regulatory restrictions primarily but uncertainty. Health systems⏎require clear evidence of safety and effectiveness, defined accountability structures⏎between vendors and purchasers, sustainable payment pathways, and modernized data⏎standards to support deployment. Absent these elements, adoption slows not due to⏎resistance, but due to prudent risk management. While AI has the potential to be a⏎positive tool in transforming health care, we appreciate that HHS is being deliberative in⏎developing a framework that establishes trust among all stakeholders—patients,⏎providers, payers, policymakers, and developers.⏎Supporting activities to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care⏎One of the most critical barriers preventing widespread adoption of AI in health care⏎may not be regulatory burdens but the need for assurance of the utility, efficiency, and⏎reliability of existing AI products for both clinical and non-clinical uses. To build off our⏎submission to the Request for Public Comment on the use of AI-enabled medical⏎devices, AI for clinical purposes should demonstrate exceptional performance in safety,⏎⏎effectiveness, and reliability to ensure it enhances patient care without introducing new⏎risks.⏎We would encourage a consensus process that meets the standards set by both⏎providers and payers before patients can access them for safe and effective care. From a⏎provider perspective, testing should validate clinical performance, support evidence-⏎based decision-making, and safeguard patient outcomes. Metrics of interest include⏎diagnostic concordance with human experts, time to result, alert precision and recall,⏎and rates of false positives or negatives. From a health plan perspective, real-world⏎evidence is essential to confirm medical necessity, justify reimbursement, and prevent⏎unnecessary costs. Any proposed metric should be weighted by clinical consequences,⏎with higher weighting for patient safety and outcome improvement compared to⏎workflow efficiency. The safety, effectiveness, and reliability of AI-enabled medical⏎devices must be measured through clinical performance validation, user accuracy,⏎workflow impact, and safety reporting.⏎In addition, evaluating real-world clinical use performance should be based on review of⏎high-quality studies published in peer-reviewed journals, not anecdotal observations or⏎marketing assertions. The data must be longitudinal, incorporating short-term⏎performance post-deployment, and ongoing monitoring as practice patterns evolve. The⏎data should demonstrate effect intervals extending well beyond the typical disease⏎progression timeframe to demonstrate durability of effects. For example, if a patient’s⏎cancer typically doubles in six months, we expect evidence of durable benefit, such as⏎remission lasting two to four years.⏎Such evaluations would not only be beneficial to large integrated health systems like⏎ours but also crucial to smaller providers to be confident in adopting AI solutions.⏎Without clear guidelines, the federal government risks exacerbating a new type of digital⏎divide within the health care sector with safety-net providers and their patients being⏎unable to harness AI’s transformative power.⏎Finally, HHS should review with stakeholder input whether existing regulatory⏎frameworks require modernization to reflect AI-enabled care delivery. Areas for review⏎could include HIPAA Privacy and Security Rule interpretations related to AI training and⏎secondary data use, alignment of requirements for AI-enabled clinical decision support⏎tools, and clarification of Stark and anti-kickback implications for subscription-based AI⏎models integrated into clinical workflows. Greater clarity in these areas would support⏎innovation while maintaining patient protection.⏎Creating a framework for responsibility between AI vendors and purchasers⏎Relatedly, HHS should develop a clear, comprehensive framework that identifies an AI⏎vendor’s responsibilities to ensure legal and regulatory compliance to give providers of⏎all sizes some assurances for its AI products.⏎100 Michigan Street NE | MC60 | Grand Rapids, MI 49503⏎26901 Beaumont Boulevard | Southfield, MI 48033⏎⏎As a non-profit entity, we have dedicated resources to our Artificial Intelligence Center of⏎Excellence (AI COE) at a considerable cost. This level of investment may not be feasible⏎for smaller organizations, especially safety-net providers in underserved or rural areas,⏎or sustainable in the long term even for large health systems. For externally developed⏎AI solutions, a framework should require how vendors (i.e., any vendor integrating AI⏎into their products) will demonstrate regulatory compliance to their customers. It would⏎be helpful for key health and non-health agencies to provide clear guidance on how to⏎effectively demonstrate and document sufficient compliance as AI technology continues⏎to evolve.⏎In addition to ensuring compliance, the performance of these tools is exceedingly⏎important to validate and monitor – especially when the patient population and data⏎some of these tools were trained on does not accurately reflect the patient population⏎and data the tools will be used to treat. The responsibility for this type of validation and⏎monitoring needs to be a shared responsibility between an AI vendor and the⏎purchasing organization to ensure safe and effective use across all patient populations.⏎Similar to the prior considerations for supporting AI evaluation, we also believe a⏎framework for responsibility here would be beneficial across the health sector to be⏎confident in adopting AI solutions. Without such clarity, the federal government risks⏎exacerbating a new type of digital divide within the health care sector with safety-net⏎providers and their patients being unable to harness AI’s transformative power. Worse⏎yet, there becomes an increasing risk of predatory AI vendors taking advantage of⏎smaller, less resourced organizations in ways that both harm their financial standing and⏎their patients and leads to skepticism among patients and providers on AI utilization.⏎For non-medical device AI tools, novel governance issues arise related to liability⏎allocation, indemnification, model transparency, bias mitigation, and post-deployment⏎monitoring. HHS should clarify expectations regarding shared accountability between AI⏎vendors and health care organizations, particularly where models influence clinical⏎decision-making but are not regulated as medical devices. Clear guidance regarding⏎documentation standards, auditability, and safe harbor protections for organizations⏎deploying AI under structured governance frameworks would meaningfully reduce⏎uncertainty.⏎Considering payment policy for AI utilization⏎Similar to our comments on the Calendar Year Medicare Physician Fee schedule, we⏎would encourage HHS to consider appropriate Medicare payment policies for⏎responsible AI utilization. For example, some digital technologies use a subscription⏎model: we use subscription-model algorithms to help with detection in imaging such as⏎CT and X-rays for mammography or bone fractures or in scheduling follow-up services⏎from imaging and clinical decision support for clinicians on various workflows that can⏎connect such workflows with orders, order sets, and clinical best practice⏎advisories. Such services enable better care management, especially for chronic⏎conditions, by providing clinicians with greater access to up-to-date, evidence-based⏎100 Michigan Street NE | MC60 | Grand Rapids, MI 49503⏎26901 Beaumont Boulevard | Southfield, MI 48033⏎⏎clinical standards and helping integrate resulting orders into electronic health records. As⏎a subscription, the vendor can update the service to keep it continuously informed⏎with new clinical and scientific research on best practices and foster continuous quality⏎improvement.⏎HHS should be aware that providers still need to allocate internal staff resources to⏎use the software. Payment policy should recognize that high-value AI deployment may⏎shift work rather than eliminate it, requiring investments in training, governance,⏎cybersecurity, and monitoring. Reimbursement pathways should align incentives toward⏎measurable outcome improvement and total cost of care reduction rather than volume-⏎based utilization of AI-enabled services. This may mean incorporating not only clinical⏎costs but also time need for aforementioned items such as training, cybersecurity, and⏎maintenance.⏎Supporting providers to develop a governance structure⏎HHS should include public-private partnerships to help providers develop the AI⏎governance infrastructure to manage risk and provide guardrails for deployment. Such a⏎structure can help provide transparency to patients and consumers on how AI is used⏎and can be beneficial in healthcare. For example, the Corewell Health AI COE oversees⏎the process for onboarding and monitoring AI technologies going forward. Our goal is to⏎develop a responsible and sustainable approach for using AI technology and similar tools⏎in ways that improve our work while also protecting our patient and plan member⏎information when there is high value and low risk.⏎Other hospitals and providers may need resources and guidance to adopt a similar⏎approach to our AI COE, and we too have appreciated tools developed by the federal⏎government to ensure we are adopting best practices. Such tools should be flexible⏎enough to be adopted by different types of stakeholders. For example, as required under⏎the National Artificial Intelligence Initiative Act of 2020 (P.L. 116-283), the AI Risk⏎Management Framework developed by the National Institute of Standards and⏎Technology (NIST) has been a valuable resource. The NIST framework helps ensure that⏎we are managing the potential risks of AI and promoting trustworthiness among staff,⏎patients, and our community. We appreciate that it is a voluntary tool that is not specific⏎either to sector or use-case.⏎Having such a transparent governance structure to show how AI is used in care delivery⏎is essential to building public trust and sustaining it. Patients should have clarity⏎regarding when AI informs clinical decisions, assurances regarding bias monitoring and⏎privacy protections, and appropriate avenues for recourse when concerns arise.⏎Responsible adoption requires not only technological safeguards but clear⏎communication.⏎100 Michigan Street NE | MC60 | Grand Rapids, MI 49503⏎26901 Beaumont Boulevard | Southfield, MI 48033⏎⏎Developing Interoperability and Data Standards⏎Enhanced interoperability is foundational to scaling AI in clinical care. Continued⏎advancement of FHIR-based APIs; expansion of USCDI to include structured imaging⏎metadata, genomic information, and standardized social risk data; and development of⏎benchmarking datasets for model validation would significantly widen market⏎opportunities and accelerate research. AI tools are only as effective as the quality and⏎standardization of the data on which they operate.⏎Developing a talent pool⏎We encourage HHS and other federal agencies to consider how to develop a pipeline for⏎more AI and cybersecurity professionals, including incentives to work in the health care⏎sector. In adopting potential AI solutions, health plans and health systems are⏎developing more in-depth reviews and implementation processes, which require⏎substantial resources and administrative capacity. Health care providers operate on⏎narrow margins yet must compete globally for talent with entities that have much larger⏎access to capital.⏎Applying a regulatory framework to direct-to-consumer health AI tools⏎Finally, patients and consumers are seeking out AI tools for their health needs on their⏎own initiative, and developers are launching new products to help them organize and⏎understand their health data. Such tools have the potential to empower patients to⏎make healthier choices and manage their own health conditions but also lead to⏎potential harm such as confusion, misinformation, and inappropriate use of patient⏎information. For example, HHS and the Federal Trade Commission may need to modify⏎existing resources to help developers and consumers understand their responsibilities⏎under existing privacy and security laws. Many consumers do not understand that⏎depending on who they share their personal health information with, HIPAA protections⏎may not necessarily apply.⏎We appreciate that HHS is considering some of these issues through Medicare⏎demonstrations such as the Advancing Chronic Care with Effective, Scalable Solutions⏎(ACCESS) Model. But many issues, including reimbursement, privacy, and security, may⏎be outside of HHS’s current regulatory authority and require congressional action. As⏎HHS considers whether and how to incorporate direct-to-consumer AI tools into the⏎broader health care system, it should reactions from stakeholders—providers, plans, and⏎most importantly patients—on a proposed regulatory framework.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you for the opportunity to share our perspective. Should you have any questions⏎regarding these comments or if you would like any additional information, please⏎contact Oliver Kim, Senior Director of Public Policy, Corewell Health Government⏎Relations & Public Policy, oliver.kim@corewellhealth.org.⏎100 Michigan Street NE | MC60 | Grand Rapids, MI 49503⏎26901 Beaumont Boulevard | Southfield, MI 48033⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Jason R. Joseph⏎Chief Digital and Transformation Officer⏎Corewell Health⏎100 Michigan Street NE | MC60 | Grand Rapids, MI 49503⏎26901 Beaumont Boulevard | Southfield, MI 48033♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0190♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-09T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Baker♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist of eleven years, Dr. Petrosian, retired in the spring of 2023, and the new group that took over his patients started doing things differently almost right away. I found out by accident that my cardiac imaging was being run through some kind of computer screening program before any physician actually reviewed it. I had to ask three times before anyone at the front desk could even explain it to me. That is not how medicine is supposed to work.⏎⏎I keep thinking about what it was like when my wife Darlene went through her breast cancer diagnosis. We sat across a desk from Dr. Holloway at Ruby Memorial Hospital in Morgantown, and she walked us through every single scan herself. She pointed at things on the screen. She paused when Darlene started to cry. She changed the order she was going to say things because she could see we were overwhelmed. No computer program does that. No algorithm can read the room the way that woman read the room that afternoon in October 2019, and I honestly believe the care Darlene got because of that kind of attention is part of why she is still here today.⏎⏎What I want HHS to understand is that patients who are scared are not just information-delivery problems waiting to be solved faster. When you are 78 years old and you are sitting in a cardiology waiting room, you are not thinking about efficiency. You are thinking about whether you are going to be okay. The question HHS seems to be asking is how to speed up AI adoption, and I understand there may be good reasons for that, I am not saying computers have no place in medicine. But the question that is not being asked loudly enough is what gets lost when you move fast.⏎⏎The thing that made me trust Dr. Petrosian for eleven years was knowing that he, specifically, was responsible for what happened to me. He ordered the test, he read the result, he called me. There was a person at the end of that chain. When my imaging goes through a computer program first and I never even find out unless I push for it, that chain breaks. Who is responsible if the program misses something? I genuinely do not know the answer to that and I am not sure anyone at the clinic does either.⏎⏎I would ask that HHS require that patients be told plainly, in writing, when artificial intelligence is being used in their diagnosis or treatment, and that a physician still has to sign off in a real and meaningful way, not just click approve on a computer-generated summary. I do not think that is too much to ask. Maybe there are situations where the AI catches things faster and that saves lives, I have read that claim, and I am not dismissing it entirely.⏎⏎But I do want someone at HHS to think about what it costs a 78-year-old patient in rural West Virginia when the medicine starts to feel like it is happening to him rather than with him. My follow-up appointment last March was twelve minutes long.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0191♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Citizens' Council For Health Freedom, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Citizens' Council For Health Freedom♔♔♔♔♔♔VIA ONLINE PORTAL⏎February 13, 2026⏎Michael Lipinski⏎Office of Policy⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎RE: Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability: ASTP/ONC Deregulatory Actions⏎to Unleash Prosperity⏎45 CFR Parts 170 and 171⏎RIN: 0955-AA09⏎Dear Mr. Lipinski:⏎Citizens’ Council for Health Freedom (CCHF) submits this comment to the above-⏎referenced proposed rule. CCHF is a 501(c)(3) organization and, for over two decades,⏎has fought to restore the mission of medicine by protecting doctor and patient freedom.⏎CCHF provides valuable resources, policy research, and unique initiatives.⏎CCHF supports the rule’s decisive shift away from heavy federal micromanagement⏎toward competition-driven innovation in health IT and supports the rule to the extent that⏎the rule accomplishes the following:⏎1. REDUCES REGULATORY COMPLIANCE: Cuts regulatory compliance costs⏎and allows market forces—not federal checklists—to shape technology⏎adoption and acknowledges that health care markets do not require⏎prescriptive regulation to enforce baseline functionality.⏎2. ENHANCES CONSUMER CHOICE: Emphasizes consumer choice and⏎innovation, enabling providers to select tools that best meet their needs rather⏎than those optimized for certification.⏎3. FOSTERS COMPETITION: Recognizes that innovation, AI integration, and⏎efficiency are better driven by consumer-oriented competition than by federal⏎micromanagement.⏎4. PROPERLY ALLOCATES RESOURCES: Shifts resources from regulatory⏎compliance toward patient care, product development, and cost-saving⏎innovation.⏎CCHF | 161 Rondo Avenue, Suite 923 | St. Paul, MN 55103 | 651-646-8935 | www.cchfreedom.org⏎Protecting patient and doctor freedom Since 1998⏎⏎5. REVERSES CONSOLODATION: Generates substantial long-term savings⏎and expands consumer-driven health care markets, reversing the concerning⏎trend of health market conglomerate consolidation.⏎Conversely, CCHF is apprehensive regarding the relaxation of health IT regulation in⏎health care, especially novel AI technology, for the following reasons:⏎1. PRIVACY INTRUSIONS: Patient privacy and care must remain a top priority⏎for the health care markets; it remains to be seen how less IT regulation and⏎the use of AI will advance these priorities.⏎2. HIPAA: The HIPAA rule, properly understood, is a data security rule rather than⏎a privacy rule. It permits confidential patient data to be shared broadly without⏎patient consent by “covered entities.” Other laws and rules require⏎interoperability, thus mandating the use of digital networks, frameworks,⏎Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreements (TEFCA), electronic⏎health records, clinical data platforms, qualified health information networks⏎(QHINs), and other technologies without patient consent—while requiring that⏎the data be shielded from unauthorized access. AI will have access to this⏎data, also without patient consent, for purposes the patient may not⏎support, and potentially to directly impact the patient’s care in ways that⏎could be harmful, insufficient, or generated through AI hallucinations.⏎3. ALGORITHMS: AI can be exploited in the health care market; several class-⏎action lawsuits are pending against large insurers, alleging the inappropriate⏎use of algorithms to deny Medicare Advantage and other claims.⏎4. HARM TO PATIENTS: The misuse of AI in health care markets will likely deny⏎necessary care, cause patient harm, and increase costs.⏎5. LIMITING ACCESS TO CARE: AI and patient data are used to manipulate and⏎control physician treatment decisions, limit treatments to a pre-set menu of⏎corporate-determined treatment options populated on the EHR system, and⏎standardize treatment in a way that does not meet the needs of unique patients⏎at the biological, physiological, emotional, financial, genetic, and cultural levels.⏎Critically, this proposed rule omits a proposal to restore medical privacy rights to⏎patients that were eliminated under the federal HIPAA privacy rule (CFR sections 160⏎and 164). Restoring medical privacy has the added benefits of rebuilding patient trust,⏎fostering frank conversations at the bedside, improving history and physicals, promoting⏎patient-centered care, and reducing physician burnout caused by the current bureaucratic⏎reporting system and the ethical conflict of sharing private information with countless⏎outsiders without patient consent.⏎Warm Regards,⏎/s/ Allison R. Lucas⏎2⏎CCHF | 161 Rondo Avenue, Suite 923 | St. Paul, MN 55103 | 651-646-8935 | www.cchfreedom.org⏎Protecting patient and doctor freedom since 1998⏎⏎Allison R. Lucas, J.D.⏎Director of Public Policy⏎Citizens’ Council for Health Freedom⏎allison@cchfreedom.org⏎3⏎CCHF | 161 Rondo Avenue, Suite 923 | St. Paul, MN 55103 | 651-646-8935 | www.cchfreedom.org⏎Protecting patient and doctor freedom since 1998♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0192♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Writerslogic, Inc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Writerslogic, Inc♔♔♔♔♔♔COMMENT ON HHS HEALTH SECTOR AI RFI⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: David Condrey⏎Affiliation: Founder & CEO, WritersLogic, Inc.⏎Date: February 19, 2026⏎Contact: david@writerslogic.com⏎Introduction⏎HHS has correctly identified that public trust is a precondition for AI adoption in clinical care.⏎This comment addresses a specific and underexamined barrier to that trust: the inability to⏎verify who produced a clinical document after the fact.⏎When AI-assisted documentation becomes indistinguishable from documentation produced by⏎a licensed clinician, accountability collapses. Malpractice attribution becomes probabilistic.⏎Informed consent records become unverifiable. The physician signature at the bottom of a note⏎attests presence but not authorship. Under current conditions, no technical mechanism exists to⏎answer the question a court, a regulator, or a patient’s family will eventually ask: did a human⏎clinician actually compose this, or did they ratify output they did not write?⏎This is not a future problem. It is a present one that will intensify as AI-assisted documentation⏎tools become standard across clinical settings.⏎1. The Provenance Gap in Clinical AI⏎HHS’s RFI frames accountability and transparency as central values for AI adoption in clinical⏎care. Current AI governance frameworks address accountability at the model level, through⏎audits, validation studies, and performance benchmarks. These are necessary. They are not⏎Sufficient.⏎What they do not address is accountability at the document level: the chain of evidence linking⏎a specific clinical record to the specific human who produced it, in real time, during the act of⏎Composition.⏎⏎Existing attribution methods work backward from a finished document. Statistical classifiers⏎attempt to distinguish AI-generated text from human-generated text based on patterns in the⏎output. These methods produce approximations. They are inadmissible as evidence of⏎authorship. They cannot establish, with cryptographic certainty, that a licensed clinician⏎composed a document rather than reviewed or edited AI-generated text.⏎The practical consequences for HHS’s stated goals are direct. Without verifiable authorship at⏎the document level, the following cannot be reliably established:⏎● Whether a clinical note was authored by a licensed professional or generated by an AI⏎system and co-signed without material human contribution⏎● Whether documentation submitted for Medicare or Medicaid reimbursement reflects⏎genuine clinical judgment or automated output⏎● Whether a patient’s informed consent record was produced through authentic human⏎engagement or AI-assisted templating⏎Each of these is a liability exposure, a fraud vector, and a trust erosion point that AI adoption at⏎scale will amplify.⏎2. Existing Technical Infrastructure⏎I am the developer of witnessd, an open-source cryptographic protocol for proving human⏎authorship through keystroke dynamics and process evidence. The protocol operates at the⏎moment of composition, not after the fact. It captures cryptographically signed evidence of⏎human keystrokes, timing patterns, and compositional sequence in real time, producing a⏎verifiable record that a human was present and active during the creation of a document.⏎The distinction that matters for HHS’s framework is between forensic inference and⏎prospective proof. Every current attribution method is forensic: it examines a finished⏎document and infers probable authorship. witnessd is prospective: it captures evidence during⏎composition that can be verified independently of the document’s content.⏎Applied in clinical documentation contexts, this means a clinician using a witnessd-enabled⏎documentation environment produces not just a signed note but a cryptographic record of the⏎human compositional process behind it. That record is auditable, tamper-evident, and⏎verifiable by regulators, payers, or legal parties without requiring access to AI detection tools⏎whose reliability remains contested.⏎The protocol is open-source and has Internet-Drafts currently under review by the IETF⏎SECDISPATCH working group:⏎⏎● draft-condrey-rats-pop-protocol-06 — Proof of Process (PoP): Architecture and Evidence⏎Format⏎● draft-condrey-rats-pop-appraisal-04 — Proof of Process (PoP): Forensic Appraisal and⏎Security Model⏎Both drafts are profiled against the IETF Remote Attestation Procedures (RATS) architecture⏎(RFC 9334). The companion reference implementation, witnessd, is written in Rust with SLSA⏎Level 3 reproducible builds (https://github.com/writerslogic/witnessd). My published research,⏎On the Insecurity of Keystroke-Based AI Authorship Detection: Timing-Forgery Attacks⏎Against Motor-Signal Verification (arXiv:2601.17280), examines the adversarial threat⏎landscape for authorship verification systems. I also submitted technical comments on⏎NIST-2025-0035 this month, positioning unverified AI output as an agent security threat⏎within the federal AI security framework.⏎3. Recommendations to HHS⏎HHS has three levers available: regulation, reimbursement, and research and development.⏎This comment addresses each.⏎Regulation.⏎HHS should consider requiring that AI-assisted clinical documentation tools deployed in⏎regulated care settings provide verifiable evidence of human authorship at the document level,⏎not merely a clinician signature or attestation. The distinction between a signature and a⏎cryptographic authorship record is the difference between assertion and evidence. Regulatory⏎guidance that draws this distinction would incentivize the development and adoption of⏎prospective authorship verification infrastructure.⏎Reimbursement.⏎CMS reimbursement policy already conditions payment on documentation requirements. HHS⏎should evaluate whether existing documentation standards are adequate to distinguish⏎AI-generated output from clinician-authored records, and whether reimbursement criteria⏎should require verifiable evidence of human authorship for high-stakes clinical documentation⏎categories. The absence of such standards creates a fraud vector that will grow as AI⏎documentation tools proliferate.⏎Research and Development.⏎HHS and NIH should fund development of open standards for clinical authorship verification,⏎with particular attention to interoperability with existing EHR systems. The technical⏎infrastructure described here is nascent but deployable. Federal investment in this area would⏎produce durable public infrastructure rather than proprietary solutions that fragment the market⏎and create interoperability failures.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎AI adoption in clinical care will accelerate regardless of regulatory posture. The question HHS⏎can answer now is whether that acceleration happens with or without verifiable human⏎accountability at the document level. The technical infrastructure to answer this question⏎prospectively, at the moment of composition, rather than forensically after the fact, exists and⏎is available for federal engagement.⏎I welcome the opportunity to provide additional technical detail, demonstrate the reference⏎implementation, or discuss these considerations further.⏎David Condrey⏎Founder & CEO, WritersLogic, Inc.⏎david@writerslogic.com⏎https://writerslogic.com⏎References⏎[PoP-Protocol] D. Condrey, “Proof of Process (PoP): Architecture and Evidence Format,”⏎Internet-Draft draft-condrey-rats-pop-protocol-06, IETF, 2026.⏎https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-condrey-rats-pop-protocol/⏎[PoP-Appraisal] D. Condrey, “Proof of Process (PoP): Forensic Appraisal and Security Model,”⏎Internet-Draft draft-condrey-rats-pop-appraisal-04, IETF, 2026.⏎https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft-condrey-rats-pop-appraisal/⏎[RFC 9334] H. Birkholz, D. Thaler, M. Richardson, N. Smith, W. Pan, “Remote ATtestation⏎procedureS (RATS) Architecture,” RFC 9334, IETF, January 2023.⏎[witnessd] Writerslogic, Inc., “witnessd: Cryptographic Authorship Witnessing System,”⏎Open-source implementation(Rust, SLSA Lvl 3). https://github.com/writerslogic/witnessd⏎[Condrey-2025] D. Condrey, “On the Insecurity of Keystroke-Based AI Authorship Detection:⏎Timing-Forgery Attacks Against Motor-Signal Verification,” arXiv:2601.17280, 2025.⏎[NIST AI 100-2e2025] NIST, “Adversarial Machine Learning: A Taxonomy and Terminology⏎of⏎Attacks and Mitigations.”⏎[C2PA] Coalition for Content Provenance and Authenticity, C2PA Technical Specification,⏎v2.2.⏎https://c2pa.org/specifications/♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0193♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from MEDITECH, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MEDITECH♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Re: RIN 0955-AA13–Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Deputy Secretary of Health and Human Services and Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy:⏎Medical Information Technology, Inc. (MEDITECH) is pleased to offer a response to⏎the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) Request for Information on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.⏎MEDITECH empowers healthcare organizations to expand their vision of what’s⏎possible with Expanse, the intelligent EHR platform. Around the globe, Expanse⏎transforms care and ushers organizations into the future with AI-infused solutions,⏎personalized workflows, next-level interoperability, and predictive analytics — all⏎working together to drive better outcomes.⏎MEDITECH strongly supports the HHS initiative to accelerate AI innovation and⏎adoption in clinical care. We recognize its potential to enhance efficiency, reduce⏎clinician burden, and improve outcomes while maintaining patient safety and data⏎privacy. As a leading EHR vendor, we believe a balanced federal approach is⏎crucial—one that provides clear, risk-based guidance while minimizing undue⏎regulatory burdens that stifle innovation. We advocate for leveraging existing EHR⏎infrastructure as a primary vehicle for safe and effective AI deployment.⏎MEDITECH is aligned with and supports responses being submitted by the HIMSS⏎Electronic Health Record (EHR) Association. We have incorporated some of the EHR⏎Association’s statements directly within our response with an attributable reference.⏎Below are our responses to the specific questions outlined in the RFI.⏎Barriers to Private Sector Innovation⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Response:⏎1⏎⏎As a leading EHR vendor, we believe that the private sector's ability to innovate with⏎AI is primarily hindered by several intersecting issues:⏎1) Regulatory Uncertainty and Inconsistency: The lack of a consistent, shared⏎framework for risk management and transparency has led to a patchwork of⏎state legislation. This forces vendors to focus on complying with multiple,⏎diverse requirements and drains resources from actual innovation.⏎2) Lack of Risk-Tiered Approach: Effective risk management is vital for safe AI⏎development and establishing a consistent industry understanding of residual⏎risk as AI technology advances. However, risk levels are not universal across all⏎use cases. A tendency toward a uniform assessment of risk imposes a⏎disproportionate compliance burden on low-risk applications and diverts⏎critical resources and focus away from risk mitigation and testing needed by⏎more novel, complex use cases.⏎3) Federal Regulation Redundancy: Vendors are potentially subject to⏎overlapping oversight from both ASTP/ONC and the FDA. This regulatory⏎redundancy, often termed "double regulation," means software must adhere⏎to both the ASTP/ONC Health IT Certification standards and FDA Medical⏎Device standards. This compliance burden slows speed-to-market.⏎4) Access to Data: Innovation requires access to large, diverse datasets to⏎develop, train, and test models. Currently, the complexity of aggregating⏎real-world data across multiple systems creates challenges with developing⏎fully generalizable AI models.⏎Regulatory, Policy, and Programmatic Changes⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Response:⏎MEDITECH encourages HHS to prioritize the following changes:⏎1) Establish a Federal Framework: Develop a unified, federally led framework⏎for healthcare AI that aligns with international standards.⏎● Address State Patchwork: Conflicting and duplicative state frameworks⏎have emerged through a lack of federal guidance. If a vendor meets⏎stringent federal transparency requirements (e.g., ONC HTI-1), this should⏎satisfy state-level requirements.⏎2⏎⏎● Ensure Global Competitiveness: Many developers operate in both the U.S.⏎and international markets. Adopting federal standards that harmonize with⏎global frameworks (e.g., ISO/IEC 42001:2023 and ISO/IEC 23894:2023) will⏎position US developers for success and growth in the global market.⏎2) Harmonize ONC and FDA Oversight: Regulatory redundancy stifles⏎innovation. We encourage HHS to harmonize ONC Decision Support⏎Intervention Transparency with the FDA Pre-market Review through the⏎following actions:⏎● Transparency Reciprocity: Compliance with the streamlined ONC⏎Decision Support Intervention (DSI) criteria should satisfy specific FDA⏎pre-market transparency requirements for low-to-moderate risk software.⏎● “Developer-based” Reviews: Consider FDA’s transition from⏎"product-based" reviews to "developer-based" excellence appraisals for⏎low-risk EHR modules. If an EHR vendor has a documented track record of⏎safe development, their integrated AI tools should qualify for expedited⏎clearance.⏎● Expanded Enforcement Discretion: Consider broadening enforcement⏎discretion for AI tools that optimize workflows (e.g., auto-scribing,⏎administrative triage, scheduling) without providing definitive diagnoses.⏎This aligns with the deregulatory goals of HTI-5 by exempting modules that⏎already satisfy ASTP/ONC’s Real World Testing requirements.⏎3) Adopt a Risk-tiered Approach: A risk-tiered framework aligns regulatory⏎requirements with use-case risk, enabling developers to allocate resources⏎more effectively. This approach allows innovation to begin with lower-risk⏎applications, while providing a clear pathway for scaling to higher-risk use⏎cases. For healthcare organizations, this approach also offers a practical⏎roadmap for adoption, starting with low-risk, high-value use cases, while⏎gradually building governance and monitoring capabilities.⏎● Categorize Use Cases: We encourage HHS to categorize AI use cases,⏎differentiating them as clinical, operational, or other, to serve as a⏎foundational step for establishing appropriate risk tiers.⏎● Apply Proportional Oversight: We encourage HHS to consider an⏎approach that ensures regulatory requirements and oversight are⏎proportional to potential clinical impact and safety. This allows developers⏎to pursue a diverse portfolio of AI use cases without facing uniform⏎compliance burdens.⏎3⏎⏎4) Address Transparency: Current regulation focuses on the benefit of⏎information "to help guide decisions at the time and place of care" (see Health⏎Data, Technology, and Interoperability: Certification Program Updates, 89 FR⏎1192, p. 619). However, even with plain language descriptions, the volume of⏎“Source Attribute” information may not be useful to the end-user.⏎● Define Minimum Essential Information: We recommend HHS support⏎stakeholders in reaching a consensus on the minimum essential⏎information required to ensure safe and accountable use. Focusing on⏎essential, actionable AI documentation reduces developer burden and⏎costly updates while ensuring critical information for AI use is available.⏎5) Create Reimbursement Incentives: We encourage HHS to create specific⏎CPT codes or value-based payment models that reward providers for using⏎evidence-based AI tools that demonstrably improve efficiency, reduce costs,⏎and improve outcomes.⏎Novel Legal and Implementation Issues⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎Response:⏎The use of AI in clinical care introduces liability and maintenance challenges that⏎current frameworks do not address.⏎1) Establish a Liability Framework: Liability is a complex legal issue when⏎AI-generated insights inform clinical decision-making.⏎● Duty of Care: MEDITECH is in alignment with the EHR Association’s⏎statements regarding the need for a clearer definition of accountability for⏎AI tools that are not regulated by FDA as medical devices: “A central issue⏎is the allocation of liability among developers, implementers, and clinicians⏎when AI-generated recommendations inform care decisions. Because⏎these tools usually operate in advisory or assistive roles and do not⏎constitute regulated medical devices, the boundaries of responsibility can⏎be ambiguous. To support safe deployment and reduce legal uncertainty,⏎the industry would benefit from model ‘duty of care’ frameworks and⏎safe-harbor practices for documenting AI-informed clinical decisions,⏎recognizing that the ultimate responsibility for patient care ultimately rests⏎with the authorizing clinician. Such structures would help ensure that⏎accountability aligns with real-world roles and capabilities while preserving⏎clinicians’ independent judgment.”⏎4⏎⏎● Clarify Third-Party Liability: Clearer rules are needed to address liability⏎when an AI intervention relies on a third-party model that degrades due to⏎unforeseen circumstances beyond the vendor's control.⏎2) Leverage Transparency for Legal Clarity: We encourage HHS to utilize the⏎modifications proposed by HTI-5 to reorient focus toward useful transparency⏎rather than exhaustive documentation.⏎● Presumption of Diligence: HHS should consider establishing a⏎presumption that clinicians employing AI tools that satisfy the Decision⏎Support Intervention (DSI) criteria–specifically, the transparency⏎requirements outlined in 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11)–have adhered to the⏎"standard of care."⏎● Empower Clinician Review: Prioritizing the usability of transparency⏎information over volume supports a clinician’s ability to “independently⏎review and understand the basis for recommendations,” which constitutes⏎the statutory prerequisite for a tool to maintain its designation as a⏎non-device under Section 520(o) of the Cures Act.⏎● Privacy and Security Requirements: There is uncertainty around⏎interpretations of HIPAA privacy and security requirements when AI⏎models are trained or updated using clinical data. HHS can play a⏎constructive role by clarifying expectations for AI transparency and⏎auditability, and aligning privacy guidance with real-world AI development⏎and deployment practices.⏎3) Ongoing Maintenance: AI models require ongoing monitoring and⏎maintenance to detect degraded performance caused by underlying changes⏎in input data (known as “data drift”). This introduces a new, resource-intensive⏎burden that creates disparity between organizations with different levels of⏎resources. To address this, MEDITECH recommends the following:⏎● Standards Version Advancement Process: HHS should consider⏎adopting the proposed HTI-5 deregulatory approach to Real World Testing⏎for non-device AI. Rather than mandating prescriptive, annual testing⏎plans, HHS could authorize a "Standards Version Advancement Process"⏎(SVAP) methodology. HHS could permit developers of EHR-integrated AI to⏎voluntarily adopt more contemporary interoperability standards (e.g.,⏎FHIR-based APIs) to monitor model robustness without awaiting formal⏎federal regulatory updates.⏎● Support Equitable Capacity: We encourage HHS to consider using grant⏎programs and offering technical assistance to support under-resourced⏎healthcare organizations. Funding should focus on enabling them to⏎adopt, maintain, and evaluate AI tools. This support could also facilitate the⏎publication of more diverse, formal evaluations of AI's impact on varying⏎specific workflows and patient populations.⏎5⏎⏎4) National Framework for Workforce Proficiency: AI implementation is⏎ineffective without a proficient workforce to deploy and maintain it. Without⏎appropriate training, AI tools may be underutilized, misapplied, or deployed in⏎ways that fail to achieve maximal impact. This capability includes being able to⏎clearly explain AI applications, potential risks, and necessary precautions to⏎patients. Investments in clinical workforce AI training and literacy at a national⏎level are essential for fostering more equitable integration of AI across the⏎healthcare landscape. MEDITECH encourages HHS to help set standards for⏎baseline AI competencies and enable national workgroups to support the⏎creation of a common set of best practices.⏎5) Clarify the Legal Status of Agentic: The legal status of "agentic" or⏎autonomous AI systems within EHRs remains ambiguous. HHS should finalize⏎the HTI-5 proposal to revise the definitions of "access" and "use" in 45 CFR⏎171.102 to explicitly incorporate automated and AI-driven processes. This⏎modification ensures that autonomous AI systems can retrieve and exchange⏎Electronic Health Information (EHI), thereby facilitating the adoption of⏎non-device AI agents for administrative and clinical support functions.⏎Evaluation Methods⏎For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support⏎these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts,⏎grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Response:⏎For successful and trusted AI adoption, solutions must seamlessly integrate into⏎current clinical workflows and address real-world problems. Current evaluation⏎approaches are fragmented, making it difficult for users and developers to⏎consistently assess safety, effectiveness, and usability. Greater alignment around⏎evaluation methods would support responsible adoption.⏎1) Support the Development of Standardized Metrics: HHS could play an⏎important convening role in encouraging clear, standardized measurement⏎indicators tailored to distinct categories of clinical care use cases. These⏎metrics should assess clinical effectiveness, safety, equity, and workflow⏎impact to ensure AI tools deliver meaningful value in real-world settings.⏎Greater consistency in evaluation methods would allow results to be more⏎easily interpreted across settings.⏎2) Prioritize User-Centered Evaluation: User-centered evaluation methods are⏎vital for assessing the impact of AI on factors like cognitive burden, user⏎6⏎⏎satisfaction, and perceived time savings. Evaluating human-AI interaction can⏎help identify areas where AI functions best as a complement to human⏎expertise, and where it may inadvertently degrade human decision-making.⏎MEDITECH jointly published a study, User-Centered Delivery of AI-Powered⏎Health Care Technologies in Clinical Settings: Mixed Methods Case Study, that⏎describes methods for applying user-centered research frameworks across the⏎design and development, and post-deployment stages.1⏎3) Encourage Structured Feedback: Users are best positioned to identify bias or⏎inaccuracies in AI output and surface any issues or concerns. Methods that⏎enable users to assess and respond to AI outputs are essential for ongoing⏎performance monitoring. Structured feedback channels can surface these⏎insights early and support continuous improvement while reinforcing human⏎oversight.⏎Private Sector Activities⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Response:⏎To promote the innovative and effective use of AI in healthcare, MEDITECH proposes⏎three key strategic recommendations:⏎1) Establish Workforce Training Programs: To enhance AI literacy and⏎competency across the healthcare industry, we recommend a coordinated⏎partnership between regulatory bodies, professional organizations, and the⏎private sector.⏎● Standardized, Role-Based: Programs should focus on developing⏎baseline, role-based workforce training programs and standardized⏎credentialing mechanisms. Training modules should be customized for⏎varying clinical and administrative roles, ensuring every member of a care⏎team understands concepts like data quality, bias, fairness, and validity.⏎● Public Funding: We encourage HHS to prioritize funding for these⏎programs to ensure all healthcare professionals have access to⏎foundational AI education. Publicly supported AI training would promote⏎equity, improve safety and trust, and enable organizations to better achieve⏎measurable outcomes.⏎2) Equitable Access to Accreditation/Certification: Access to voluntary⏎accreditation and certification processes must be kept affordable and⏎1 Schreier, M., Brandt, R., Brown, H., Saensuksopa, T., Silva, C., & Vardoulakis, L. M. (2025).⏎User-Centered Delivery of AI-Powered Health Care Technologies in Clinical Settings:⏎Mixed Methods Case Study. JMIR Human Factors, 12, e76241. https://doi.org/10.2196/76241⏎7⏎⏎available to all organizations and vendors regardless of size or financial status.⏎Programs should align with national regulatory frameworks. Public funding⏎through grants, subsidies, or vouchers can reduce cost barriers. To maintain a⏎competitive market, industry-led certifications and external governance⏎bodies should be viewed as supplemental. They must not create market⏎conditions that impose undue financial burdens on vendors who already meet⏎federal requirements.⏎3) Support Regulatory Sandboxes: We encourage HHS to consider⏎opportunities for safe experimentation through funding and support for⏎neutral AI testing environments (sandboxes) that utilize shared evaluation⏎frameworks and promote public knowledge dissemination. This approach⏎would enable the generation of evidence and insights that can be used to⏎inform policy and regulatory frameworks, while preserving industry capacity to⏎innovate.⏎Enhanced Interoperability⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific⏎data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Response:⏎To accelerate AI adoption and foster research, we encourage HHS to prioritize the⏎following interoperability advancements:⏎1) Advance National Interoperability Frameworks: HHS should continue to⏎advance interoperability through the TEFCA framework, CMS-aligned data⏎exchange networks, and standards-based APIs.⏎● Scalable AI Platforms: Enhanced interoperability enables EHRs to⏎function as scalable platforms for various AI-enabled workflows. This⏎broadens market opportunities, particularly for AI tools driven by⏎longitudinal, multi-source data, such as risk predictions, population health,⏎and care management.⏎● Standardize Context: Adoption of common standards further expands⏎these markets. US Core and the International Patient Summary enable⏎consistent clinical context, while HL7 FHIR APIs support model⏎development and real-time AI workflows.⏎2) Improve Data Quality for AI Readiness: High-quality, comprehensive data is⏎essential for developing robust, generalizable, and bias-free AI tools.⏎● Support Industry Collaboratives: We encourage HHS to continue to⏎support industry initiatives, such as the Sequoia Project’s “Taking Root⏎Movement,” which enhance AI readiness by improving data quality,⏎metadata consistency, and patient matching, thereby reducing⏎downstream deduplication and model bias.⏎8⏎⏎3) Leverage Patient-Generated Data: Strengthening interoperability around⏎patient access, contribution, and bidirectional exchange of patient-generated⏎data is critical for equity.⏎● Create Representative Datasets: Bidirectional exchange would enable⏎more representative and continuous datasets, improving AI training,⏎evaluation, and deployment.⏎● Promote Patient-Centered AI: This shift would support the development⏎and adoption of AI that is context-aware and effective across diverse⏎populations and care environments.⏎4) Expand Data Standards for AI Outputs and Monitoring: We encourage HHS⏎to continue to reinforce and expand data standards that support the use of AI⏎within clinical workflows and develop standards that define how AI predictions⏎and output are formatted in FHIR. We encourage HHS to consider ways to⏎help build mechanisms to test, collaborate, and evolve AI use cases. As use⏎cases move beyond pilot phases, standardized methods for users to share⏎real-world performance data will become more crucial for ongoing⏎monitoring, maintenance, and re-training processes.⏎Prioritized Research⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Response:⏎We recommend HHS prioritize AI research that addresses the growing lack of privacy⏎assurance, transparency, and trust associated with AI integration into clinical care. As⏎automated, AI-driven data processing expands, there is a high likelihood that⏎patients and clinicians will have limited visibility and control over how protected⏎health information is collected, analyzed, and shared, leading to misalignment with⏎HIPAA privacy expectations. This trust deficit represents a central barrier to⏎widespread adoption of clinical care that needs to be prioritized to enable the safe⏎and scalable use of AI in clinical care.⏎Thank you for your time and consideration. We appreciate the opportunity to share⏎our perspective and support HHS’ efforts to accelerate responsible AI adoption. We⏎look forward to continuing to work with HHS to promote equitable adoption of⏎innovative technologies across the healthcare landscape.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎MEDITECH⏎9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0194♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Scientific Knowledge Accelerator Foundation, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Scientific Knowledge Accelerator Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS RFI on AI for Clinical Care from SKAF⏎https://public-inspection.federalregister.gov/2025-23641.pdf⏎Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI)⏎Regarding feedback on how current HHS regulations impact AI adoption and use for⏎clinical care:⏎There is an FDA guidance document for Clinical Decision Support Software (January⏎2026, FDA-2017-D-6569) that explains certain CDS software functions are “excluded⏎from the definition of device” and thus excluded from regulation enforcement if they⏎meet all 4 of specific criteria, including criterion “(4) intended for the purpose of enabling⏎such health care professional to independently review the basis for such⏎recommendations that such software presents so that it is not the intent that such health⏎care professional rely primarily on any of such recommendations to make a clinical⏎decision or treatment decision regarding an individual patient”⏎In simple English, a developer of CDS software is motivated to avoid regulation of their⏎CDS software by providing clear rationale/evidence for the recommendation offered by⏎the CDS such that the health care professional can review the rationale/evidence and⏎independently decide on the recommended action. Regulation of CDS software is thus⏎limited to situations where the CDS software is replacing clinical judgment without an⏎opportunity to apply clinical judgment.⏎When the recommendation is determined by traditional human efforts, there are⏎traditional forms for explaining the rationale/evidence behind the recommendation, and⏎these forms ideally represent a balanced view of the benefits and harms (i.e. a⏎systematic review of the evidence) rather than a selective view that only reports the⏎evidence that is most supportive of the recommendation. Such forms often include the⏎certainty of or confidence in the evidence and other judgments related to the balance of⏎benefits and harms. Some representations of these forms have been called evidence-⏎based medicine (EBM), ideally used to represent an ‘answer’ based on systematic and⏎comprehensive evaluation of the evidence rather than selective reporting of ‘evidence’⏎to justify a desired ‘answer’.⏎When the recommendation is generated by AI, there is a need to represent the actual⏎rationale/evidence used by the AI and full consideration of the relevant data inputs⏎RATHER THAN selective reporting to justify the recommendation by the AI. There is a⏎high risk of bias if the rationale/evidence reported for ‘independent review’ by the health⏎care professional is selected AFTER the answer is determined by the AI rather than⏎⏎through a process of recognizing the data that contributes directly to the AI’s decision⏎making. In other words, generative AI can generate rationales to support its “answer”⏎that are contextually incorrect. By providing selective evidence for the healthcare⏎professional to review, the review is no longer independent and the AI can bias clinical⏎care.⏎A method of reporting the evidence supporting an AI-generated recommendation needs⏎to be human-interpretable (to meet the FDA guidance) AND needs to be machine-⏎interpretable (to enable accurate generation and representation by AI). A method that is⏎interoperable and re-usable would go a long way to help everyone across the⏎ecosystem achieve this optimal state of communication.⏎We have developed an interoperable and re-usable method. This method builds on the⏎technical standard for data exchange for health data, known as the HL7 FHIR standard,⏎or Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR). We have expanded FHIR to⏎support data exchange for science, and this is described in an Evidence Based⏎Medicine on FHIR (EBMonFHIR) Implementation Guide at⏎https://build.fhir.org/ig/HL7/ebm.⏎The FHIR Resources for Evidence Based Medicine Knowledge Assets ('EBMonFHIR')⏎Implementation Guide defines profiles and value sets for the representation of scientific⏎knowledge. This implementation guide is intended for developers of systems using⏎FHIR for data exchange of scientific knowledge and for authors of more specialized⏎implementation guides in this domain.⏎This implementation guide covers the broad scope of representation of scientific⏎knowledge, including (1) citations to represent identification, location, classification, and⏎attribution for knowledge artifacts; (2) components of research study design including⏎study eligibility criteria (cohort definitions) and endpoint analysis plans; (3) research⏎results including the statistic findings, definition of variables for which those findings⏎apply, and the certainty of these findings; (4) assessments of research results; (5)⏎aggregation and synthesis of research results; (6) judgments regarding evidence⏎syntheses and contextual factors related to recommendations; (7) recommendations;⏎and (8) compositions of combinations of these types of knowledge. The types of⏎interoperability covered include syntactic (Resource StructureDefinitions) and semantic⏎(value sets).⏎The EBMonFHIR Implementation Guide includes about 100 Profiles, Extensions, and⏎ValueSets to guide many aspects of data exchange for scientific knowledge.⏎⏎Regarding feedback on ways in which HHS may invest in research & development to⏎integrate AI in care delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that⏎improve the health and wellbeing of all Americans:⏎There are many research & development opportunities that can be considered across⏎the entire scope of the EBMonFHIR Implementation Guide.⏎One specific opportunity to highlight here (for substantial opportunity to improve CDS⏎adoption for specific clinical decisions to improve health and wellbeing) is the⏎SummaryOfNetEffect Profile. The SummaryOfNetEffect Profile is a structure definition⏎for combining all of the following data elements related to a decision:⏎1. Specification of the Population represented for that decision, e.g. people who⏎have chronic kidney disease, have anemia (low red blood cell count), and are⏎being treated with dialysis⏎2. Specification of the Intervention represented for that decision, e.g. erythropoiesis-⏎stimulating agents (ESA), which are medicines to stimulate the body to produce⏎more red blood cells⏎3. Specification of the Comparator represented for that decision, e.g. no ESAs⏎4. Specification of the outcomes to be considered for that decision, e.g. Needing a⏎blood transfusion, Mortality, and Vascular access thrombosis.⏎5. For each outcome, specifying:⏎a. The risk for the outcome without treatment (or with the Comparator), which⏎could be derived from evidence from clinical research or risk prediction⏎defined for an individual patient⏎b. The estimated change in the outcome resulting from treatment (use of the⏎Intervention), which should be derived from evidence from clinical⏎research⏎c. The desirability of the outcome (desirable or undesirable), which⏎determines whether the estimated change in the outcome resulting from⏎treatment is a benefit or harm⏎d. The relative importance of the outcome, which could be derived from⏎evidence from population surveys or individualized for personal decision⏎making⏎e. A net effect contribution (calculated by multiplying the effect estimate and⏎the relative importance)⏎6. Specification of a net effect estimate (statistically combining the net effect⏎contributions)⏎An example for a SummaryOfNetEffect for ESAs for CKD patients with anemia⏎receiving dialysis can be seen at https://fevir.net/resources/Composition/422055 -- this⏎⏎is evidence that can easily be shown in human-interpretable and machine-interpretable⏎forms.⏎A further representation of this evidence, designed to support individualized shared⏎decision making, can be seen at https://netbenefitcalculator.com/422055⏎There are nearly infinite combinations of how this evidence could be shared to support⏎CDS. But if HHS could invest in research & development to establish and support a⏎common basis for sharing this data, that would create new, long-term market⏎opportunities.⏎Navigation software (MapQuest, Google Maps) is far advanced today and used in⏎nearly infinite ways because there are common expectations in data sharing with both⏎human-interpretable and machine-interpretable interfaces. But there is currently no⏎standardized navigation system (“GPS”) for healthcare.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0195♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from LMI Consulting LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔LMI Consulting LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI Response⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology⏎Policy (ASTP), Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ONC) (collectively, ASTP/ONC), Department⏎of Health and Human Services.⏎Submitted by:⏎LMI Consulting, LLC⏎7940 Jones Branch Drive⏎Tysons, VA 22102⏎Laura Dial, Contracts Administration⏎ldial@lmisolutions.com⏎Restriction on use and disclosure of response information: This RFI response includes data that shall not be disclosed outside⏎the government and shall not be duplicated, used, or disclosed—in whole or in part—for any purpose other than to evaluate this⏎proposal. However, if a contract is awarded to LMI as a result of—or in connection with—the submission of these data, the government⏎shall have the right to duplicate, use, or disclose the data to the extent provided in the resulting contract. This restriction does not limit⏎the government’s right to use information contained in these data if they are obtained from another source without restriction. The⏎data subject to this restriction are on pages marked “use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on⏎the title page of this proposal.”⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎1.0 Executive Summary⏎This document provides a comprehensive examination of the challenges and opportunities⏎associated with the integration of artificial intelligence (AI) into clinical care and outlines proactive⏎measures that HHS can take to accelerate responsible and ethical adoption. AI has the potential to⏎be transformative in modernizing healthcare systems, improving operational efficiencies, reducing⏎clinician burnout, enhancing patient outcomes, improving diagnostic accuracy, and expanding⏎access to underserved populations. However, any strategy to accelerate AI in clinical settings will⏎need to address barriers to adoption, including fragmented data governance, low trust in AI⏎systems, liability questions, demographic bias, misaligned incentives, and workflow friction. As a⏎trusted partner in government transformation and digital enablement, LMI advocates for forward-⏎thinking strategies that promote the benefits of AI adoption and scalability while maintaining a⏎robust framework for safety, equity, and long-term sustainability.⏎Key Points:⏎Challenges to Adoption⏎• Trust & Liability: Clinicians and patients require confidence in AI accuracy and fairness,⏎while liability ambiguities complicate adoption.⏎• Outdated Infrastructure and Data Fragmentation: Fragmented and biased healthcare⏎data undermines the scalability and reliability of AI systems across diverse populations.⏎Additionally, aging infrastructure in large healthcare systems often proves incompatible⏎with modern AI platforms, limiting real-time data exchange and promoting the use of⏎standalone AI tools instead of seamless integration into existing systems.⏎• Operational Friction: Lack of human-centered design and clinician/patient perspectives⏎during development results in AI tools that fail to integrate seamlessly into workflows,⏎leading to "friction-filled" systems rather than optimized productivity.⏎• Economic Misalignment: Outdated fee-for-service models do not incentivize value-based⏎outcomes achievable through AI capabilities.⏎• Administrative Barriers to Adoption: Fragmented governance and oversight, lack of⏎resources or understanding around change management processes, complex regulatory⏎requirements, lack of standardized evaluation frameworks, and uncertainty around funding⏎and reimbursement all contribute to delayed AI deployment.⏎AI in Healthcare: Success so Far⏎• Reduced Administrative Burnout: Research shows that AI scribes have resulted in⏎decreased clinician burnout and increased patient capacity. A survey by the American⏎Medical Association in 2024 found that the percentage of medical practices using AI⏎almost doubled from 2023 and that clinicians are most comfortable with using these tools⏎to assist with administrative tasks, rather than direct patient care.⏎• Imaging and Diagnostics: Several emerging AI tools for radiology and pathology⏎demonstrate accuracy that rivals or exceeds human accuracy in detecting disease (Jones et⏎al., 2026; Phan et al., 2025).⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 1⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎• Operational Efficiency: AI-driven scheduling has increased productivity and improved⏎patient satisfaction by reducing wait times (Waligora et al, 2025).⏎• Patient Triage and Virtual Assistance: Chatbots and symptom-checkers in patient portals⏎have been successful in reducing unnecessary emergency room visits and provide safe⏎triage advice (Elechi et al., 2025).⏎LMI Recommendations for Strategic AI Integration⏎Recommendations for accelerating AI adoption span multiple modalities and are outlined below.⏎• Regulatory Modernization: To address barriers related to trust in AI, HHS can focus on⏎the establishment of risk-based oversight frameworks, standardized data privacy⏎requirements, and algorithm transparency and performance documentation.⏎• Governance Frameworks: Introduce guidelines for or creating standardized contracting⏎templates, transparency protocols, and model-lifecycle controls to address accountability⏎gaps in non-medical AI tools.⏎• Private Sector Collaboration: Foster public-private partnerships for scalable testing⏎infrastructure and enable third-party certification frameworks to encourage independent⏎validation of AI solutions.⏎• Research and Development Priorities: Focus on explainable AI outputs, robust⏎standards for data quality, performance benchmarks, and post-deployment monitoring and⏎drift detection to ensure AI systems evolve alongside dynamic healthcare practices without⏎undermining reliability.⏎• Surfacing Patient Perspectives: Patient perspectives have not been adequately⏎researched, but among those studies that have been done, the need for patient stakeholders⏎in AI development, human oversight, transparency, and importance of data privacy have⏎all been identified as key components for addressing patient trust in AI technologies.⏎• Workflow Integration and Organizational Change Management: Prioritize human-in-⏎the-loop models to incorporate clinician oversight, usability optimization, and cognitive⏎burden reduction for seamless adoption. Combine usability testing, training strategies, and⏎integrated change management to drive trusted adoption and scalable tools.⏎• Innovative Payment Models: Transition from fee-for-service to value-based care models,⏎like the model used by the CMS Advancing Chronic Care with Effective, Scalable⏎Solutions (ACCESS) initiative, to create incentives for adopting AI systems that⏎measurably improve patient outcomes and hospital efficiencies. Develop reimbursement⏎pathways that align with AI's unique capacity to achieve long-term operational and clinical⏎efficiencies.⏎AI holds immense promise for revolutionizing clinical care by addressing clinician burnout,⏎improving diagnosis, and increasing access for underserved populations. However, realizing this⏎potential requires coordinated regulatory reforms, better data governance, clear reimbursement⏎frameworks, human-centered implementation strategies, and alignment among clinicians, patients,⏎vendors, and healthcare systems. By addressing these barriers collectively, the U.S. healthcare⏎system can leverage AI to deliver safe, equitable, and high-quality care at scale.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 2⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎2.0 Defining AI⏎Before discussing strategies for promoting the development and adoption of AI tools it is important⏎to understand what “AI” means. The term “Artificial Intelligence” or “AI” is frequently used⏎inconsistently and interchangeably to describe different capabilities and use cases, particularly in⏎vendor communications and popular discourse. This ambiguity complicates evaluation of actual⏎system behavior, associated risks, and appropriate oversight.⏎For the purposes of this document, AI is defined as falling into one of the following two categories:⏎Machine Learning (ML)⏎ML techniques are already widely deployed in healthcare and include both traditional statistical⏎learning and modern deep learning methods. These systems typically operate on structured or⏎semi-structured data and produce bounded, task-specific outputs.⏎Examples include:⏎• Risk prediction models (e.g., sepsis risk, readmission risk)⏎• Medical imaging and signal analysis (e.g., radiology, pathology, ECG interpretation)⏎• Population health stratification and resource optimization⏎• Clinical decision support rules enhanced with learned parameters⏎These systems are often deterministic at inference time, evaluated against predefined performance⏎metrics, and more amenable to validation within existing regulatory and quality frameworks.⏎Foundation models and Large Language Models (LLMs)⏎Foundation models, such as LLMs and multimodal models, are pre-trained on large, heterogeneous⏎datasets and adapted for downstream tasks and generation of ‘new’ content.⏎Examples include:⏎1. Clinical documentation assistance and summarization⏎2. Natural language interfaces to EHR data⏎3. Patient-facing chatbots and triage assistants⏎4. Multimodal models combining text, images, and signals⏎Unlike many traditional ML systems, these models:⏎• Generate open-ended outputs⏎• Exhibit probabilistic and context-sensitive behavior⏎• Produce fluent but sometimes incorrect or misleading responses⏎• Introduce new risks such as hallucination, automation bias, cognitive load, and alert fatigue⏎As a result, LLMs and other foundation models require distinct evaluation methods, workflow⏎testing, and human-centered safeguards. In the document below, LMI will speak to both categories⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 3⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎of AI, their application in clinical spaces, and the ways HHS might support, promote, and regulate⏎their use.⏎3.0 Regulation and Adoption⏎Existing Barriers to AI Adoption in Clinical Settings:⏎With a solid foundation in the scope and capabilities of AI, the focus now shifts to the critical⏎challenges and opportunities that influence its integration into clinical care. Private sector⏎innovation in AI for health care and its adoption in clinical care is shaped by a range of barriers⏎that differ depending on the stage of development, implementation, and the stakeholders involved.⏎These include:⏎Trust in AI: Clinicians and patients alike must have confidence in the safety, accuracy, and overall⏎performance of AI tools. Clinicians remain liable for patient care, meaning AI must meet high⏎standards of reliability, while patient trust depends on accurate, unbiassed tools for decision-⏎making about their care.⏎Liability concerns: Both clinicians and AI vendors face risks related to clinical errors caused by⏎AI systems. While clinicians are legally accountable for patient outcomes, AI vendors are⏎increasingly being called upon to share liability for faulty algorithms and design defects.⏎AI System Biases: AI systems may unintentionally prioritize certain variables or identify invalid⏎correlations, leading to biased outcomes. The lack of transparency in decision-making ("black box"⏎issues) further complicates efforts to address these biases. AI tools are often more accessible in⏎higher-resource settings, potentially widening healthcare gaps for underserved populations. Over-⏎reliance on AI without human oversight may overlook critical individual and contextual factors as⏎well. Mitigating such biases requires diverse datasets, regular auditing, human oversight, and⏎inclusive collaboration to ensure these technologies improve care for all populations equitably⏎(James, 2024). There are several examples of biased AI models resulting in discrepancies in patient⏎care and outcomes. An independent study found that black patients were not identified with⏎respiratory illness due to pulse oximeters being less accurate on darker skin. (UK Department of⏎Health and Social Care, 2024) In a review of an algorithmic model to identify liver disease, AI⏎missed it in 44% of female patients vs 23% of males. (Straw & Wu, 2022).⏎Data Access: Access to high-quality, diverse, non-fragmented data is critical for developing robust⏎AI models. Health data is often siloed across multiple systems and formats, making it difficult to⏎aggregate and standardize. Also, privacy and security constraints such as HIPAA and PHI make⏎data sharing and reuse complex. Even when data can be accessed, concerns over breaches,⏎confidentiality and patient consent slow down innovation.⏎Legacy Systems and Interoperability: Large healthcare systems often rely on aging⏎infrastructure that cannot easily communicate with modern AI platforms. Incompatible data⏎systems and lack of standardized data formats (e.g., FHIR) hinder real-time data exchange. In⏎addition, many AI tools are implemented as separate, “bolted-on” applications rather than being⏎seamlessly integrated into systems such as the electronic health record. This forces clinicians and⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 4⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎care teams to toggle between systems causing frustration and workarounds which impact⏎workflows, safety and efficiency (Pavuluri et Al., 2024).⏎Disruption of Established Workflows: Poorly integrated AI can increase the cognitive load on⏎clinicians and care teams rather than reducing it. If an AI tool adds extra steps to already busy,⏎high-stress workflows for clinicians and care teams, it is likely to be abandoned (Elechi et al.,⏎2025).⏎Analysis of the Barriers to AI Adoption in Healthcare⏎Early machine learning–based AI applications have exhibited model drift, algorithmic errors,⏎unintended biases, and overfitting, leading to decreased accuracy, classification or prediction⏎errors, and reduced ability to generalize. Generative AI applications can experience all these⏎problems as well as “hallucinations,” and “sycophantic behavior” leading to blatantly incorrect or⏎difficult-to-detect false information that can harm patients (Christophe et al., 2026; Peng et al.,⏎2026; Sittig & Singh, 2025).⏎Together, these barriers form a multi-layered ecosystem problem — not just a technical one.⏎Overcoming them requires coordinated action across:⏎• Policy and regulations to clarify standards and liability⏎• Data governance that balances privacy with ethical access⏎• Clinical partnerships to build trusted evidence and workflow fit⏎• Funding and incentives to support innovation and long-term ROI⏎• Education and change management to build clinician confidence⏎Only by addressing these factors in tandem can private-sector AI innovation truly translate into⏎reliable, safe, and widely used tools in clinical care.⏎While tools incorporating AI are rapidly making their way into clinical practice, mechanisms to⏎ensure their safety and effectiveness remain underdeveloped. For instance, there is little guidance⏎for health care organizations (HCOs) and clinicians as they navigate the complexities of⏎implementing AI into routine care. To ensure that integrating AI-enabled systems into health care⏎transforms clinical decision-making and improves patient outcomes, it is essential to⏎proactively address known risks and establish safeguards to manage potential unknown risks as⏎they emerge.⏎Clinicians are still liable for patient care quality and outcomes and therefore need to feel that the⏎AI input is reliable and accurate. For example, documentation tools such as Deep Scribe or Freed⏎reduce administrative workload and empower providers to focus on patient care by automating⏎transcription. However, the Scribe doesn’t always get the transcription 100% right and human⏎involvement is needed to ensure accuracy and maintain patient safety. To integrate AI safely, there⏎is an ongoing need for a “human-in-the-loop”. This applies not only to administrative clinical⏎applications, but also to diagnostic applications of AI. Algorithms have shown proficiency in⏎detecting conditions such as tumors, malaria, and prenatal issues, performing at or above expert⏎levels in certain cases (Fan et al., 2025; Faratisha et al., 2026; Isewon, Oba, & Oyelade, 2025;⏎Kumar et al., 2025; Nishida et al., 2022; Pai et al., 2025; Yeganegi et al., 2025). However,⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 5⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎diagnostic AI has also exhibited biases, for example, relying on factors like medical spending as⏎proxies for care needs, which can lead to mislabeling or overlooking vulnerable patients⏎(Obermeyer et al., 2019).⏎Despite these challenges, AI offers hope for improving health care efficiency and accessibility,⏎especially amid shortages of clinicians and nurses. Healthcare systems often operate at razor-thin⏎margins, and AI has the potential to reduce personnel burdens, increase accuracy, and expand⏎access, particularly for underserved rural populations. AI tools that address clinician burnout—⏎such as enhancing documentation or decision support—are already easing operational strain and⏎enabling quality time with patients (Albrecht et al., 2024; Shah et al., 2025).⏎Concerns around liability where both the clinician (for overreliance on AI) and the AI vendor (for⏎defective design) can both be liable also represent a barrier to adoption. There are also initiatives⏎to hold AI vendors responsible for clinical harm. Previously, once the sale of AI capabilities was⏎complete, all risk transferred to the provider. More recently, there have been initiatives to also hold⏎AI vendors responsible for clinical harm. AI vendors are now being asked to not only provide⏎capabilities but to also solve problems and to share risk. Clarifying the boundaries of liability is⏎key for encouraging adoption and integration of AI in clinical settings, while also ensuring product⏎vendors are providing safe and reliable tools.⏎AI has been available since 2007 and as its capabilities grow, clinician trust continues to grow as⏎well. With 27 million uninsured Americans, over 100 million lacking a primary care provider, and⏎200,000 annual deaths each year from preventable medical errors, AI offers a way to ease⏎documentation burdens, reduce provider burnout, and improve focus on patient care (Briones &⏎Cohen, 2026; NACHC, 2023; Rodziewicz, et al., 2025). AI holds promise for enhancing⏎efficiency, cutting costs, and expanding access, particularly in underserved rural areas. In an⏎overburdened healthcare system with significant shortage of nurses and physicians AI presents⏎opportunities for trusted scalable care.⏎LMI Policy Recommendations for Addressing Barriers to AI Adoption:⏎As HHS aims to shift the U.S. healthcare system toward "deflationary technologies"—tools that⏎improve health outcomes while simultaneously lowering long-term costs, the current regulatory,⏎payment policy and program design create an environment where AI adoption may remain limited⏎to "experimentation" rather than becoming a standard part of safe, high-quality clinical care.⏎To incentivize AI in clinical care, HHS should focus on modernizing outdated systems to ensure⏎they do not hinder innovation. Key priorities involve moving from rigid fee-for-service models⏎to outcomes-based incentives and establishing clear, risk-based regulatory guardrails.⏎Revising current Payment Policy to reflect a prioritization of efforts and programs aimed at⏎transitioning from episodic or fee-for-service payment models to alternative models that⏎incentivize and reward the long-term efficiencies possible through reasonable adoption of AI⏎technologies is one part of a strategy that will incentivize the adoption of AI in healthcare.⏎Prioritizing emerging models like the CMS ACCESS Model (CMS, 2025) which aims to provide⏎stable, recurring payments for technology supported chronic care rather than traditional fee-for-⏎service payment models is a start on this journey. Coupling the CMS ACCESS Model program⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 6⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎with efforts that incentive providers who adopt AI integrations that demonstrably reduce⏎hospitalizations and/or improve diagnostic accuracy through “bonus points” or increased shared⏎savings opportunity are potential payment policy changes that would encourage the reasonable AI⏎adoption sought after here.⏎When examining the regulatory landscape, opportunities exist to remove the “bottlenecks” that⏎currently exist for developers and healthcare systems alike. Proposed regulatory changes to remove⏎these barriers and incentivize the effective use of AI in healthcare include a transition to a Risk-⏎Based Oversight model that employs rigidity on a sliding scale that is proportionate to the risk⏎itself. For example, low risk tools (e.g. administrative automation) would be subject to less⏎rigorous regulatory hurdles while high risk/impact tools (e.g. appeals and grievances and⏎utilization management) require increased scrutiny and a higher regulatory bar to clear.⏎Other regulatory design changes to consider would include:⏎1. Addressing Health IT Certification and Transparency by updating the ONC Health IT⏎Certification Program to include criteria for algorithm transparency and decision support,⏎ensuring clinicians understand and trust AI-generated recommendations. Specifically,⏎implement and refine the Health Data, Technology, and Interoperability (HTI-1)⏎requirements under 45 CFR Part 170 to strengthen decision support transparency that requires⏎certified health IT to provide users with a "consistent, baseline set of information" about⏎algorithms, including their fairness, validity, and safety as well as simplifying the certification⏎process for AI models to reduce development costs and administrative burdens.⏎2. Data Privacy and Data De-identification: Modernize 45 CFR § 164.514(b) to address the⏎risks of re-identification in the age of big data and AI. Specifically, move beyond the static⏎“safe harbor” list of 18 identifiers to as few as four pieces of non-exclusive data can now re-⏎identify an individual. Updates should provide more flexible, risk-based de-identification⏎methods that allow for richer datasets while maintaining high privacy standards.⏎3. Medicare Advantage and Prior Authorization: Update 42 CFR § 422.101(b)(6) and 42⏎CFR § 422.138 regarding utilization management and prior authorization to explicitly state⏎that Medicare Advantage (MA) Plans cannot use AI alone to deny coverage; decisions must⏎be based on an individual’s specific circumstance and a must retain a Human-in-the-Loop. MA⏎plans must make publicly available any internal coverage criteria used by the AI models.⏎4. FDA Lifecycle Management for AI Devices: Shift from “static” device approvals to dynamic⏎lifecycle oversight by formalizing the Predetermined Change Control Plans (PCCP) within the⏎existing frameworks of 21 CFR Part 820 (Quality System Regulation) and marketing⏎pathways (510(k), De Novo, PMA) to allow a PCCP to pre-authorize specific future algorithm⏎updates (e.g., iterative machine learning improvements) without needing a new marketing⏎submission for every change, provided they maintain rigorous quality standards.⏎Other programmatic design changes to support AI adoption include:⏎1. Create AI Implementation Grants through the CMS Innovation Center (CMMI) to pilot "AI⏎Accelerators" that provide upfront technical assistance grants for training and workflow⏎integration.⏎2. Establish Interoperability Standards that ensure “data liquidity” is maintained for AI tools⏎to pull/push information across platforms with minimal technical barriers.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 7⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎3. Clarify of Liability Guidelines to protect clinicians when AI models produce incorrect or⏎harmful recommendations. Currently, the risk of legal exposure in instances where AI makes⏎an error would be a major barrier to adoption of AI technologies in a clinical setting.⏎Administrative Influence on AI Adoption⏎Updated policy and regulation cannot drive AI integration in a vacuum. AI adoption in clinical⏎care is shaped by coordinated governance across clinical, technical, operational, and compliance⏎stakeholders. Administrative barriers, including fragmented oversight, procurement complexity,⏎regulatory requirements, and organizational change management, frequently present greater⏎challenges than technical limitations. Addressing these barriers through standardized governance⏎models, clear evaluation processes, and early multidisciplinary engagement will be critical to⏎advancing responsible and scalable AI adoption across healthcare organizations.⏎Adoption of new technology and processes is governed by a multidisciplinary decision structure⏎that balances patient safety, regulatory compliance, clinical effectiveness, operational feasibility,⏎and responsible data stewardship. Healthcare organizations typically require coordinated approval⏎across executive leadership, clinical governance bodies, technical authorities, compliance⏎functions, and operational stakeholders. Understanding these roles and the administrative barriers⏎they face is critical to enabling responsible, scalable AI adoption aligned with HHS priorities.⏎Key Decision Makers and Governing Bodies⏎• Clinical and Executive Leadership—including Chief Medical Officers (CMOs), Chief⏎Clinical or Nursing Officers (CCOs/CNOs), and executive leadership teams—determine⏎whether AI solutions align with clinical quality goals, workforce needs, patient safety⏎standards, and organizational risk tolerance. Clinical leadership endorsement is often the⏎most significant driver of institutional trust and adoption readiness.⏎• Information Technology, Data, and Security Leadership—including Chief Information⏎Officers (CIOs), Chief Digital or Analytics Officers, and Chief Information Security⏎Officers (CISOs)—serve as technical gatekeepers responsible for evaluating⏎interoperability with electronic health records, cybersecurity risks, infrastructure readiness,⏎and data governance compliance. Their approval is essential to implementation feasibility.⏎• Clinical Governance and Patient Safety Bodies—such as Clinical Informatics⏎Committees, Quality and Patient Safety Boards inclusive of patient advisory councils,⏎Clinical Decision Support governance groups, and Institutional Review Boards (IRBs)—⏎assess clinical evidence, bias mitigation strategies, monitoring requirements, and overall⏎patient risk. These bodies determine whether AI solutions meet standards for safe and⏎equitable clinical use.⏎• Finance, Compliance, and Acquisition Functions—including Chief Financial Officers⏎(CFOs), acquisition offices, legal counsel, and compliance teams—evaluate procurement⏎requirements, cost sustainability, reimbursement considerations, vendor risk, and⏎adherence to federal privacy and security regulations.⏎• Operational and Frontline Clinical Leadership—including physician champions,⏎nursing leaders, department chiefs, and clinical informaticists—validate workflow⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 8⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎integration, support clinician adoption, and ensure solutions align with real-world care⏎delivery practices.⏎Primary Administrative Barriers to Adoption⏎Healthcare organizations face several administrative challenges that can delay AI deployment:⏎• Fragmented Governance and Oversight: Multiple committees and unclear approval⏎pathways can create lengthy evaluation cycles, particularly in organizations without formal⏎AI governance frameworks.⏎• Complex Regulatory and Compliance Requirements: Privacy protections, safety⏎oversight, liability considerations, and evolving regulatory expectations increase review⏎timelines prior to clinical use.⏎• Acquisition and Contracting Complexity: AI solutions introduce nontraditional⏎licensing models, data use agreements, and cybersecurity assessments that extend⏎procurement processes.⏎• Lack of Standardized Evaluation Frameworks: Organizations often lack consistent⏎methodologies for assessing clinical effectiveness, operational impact, and algorithm⏎performance across diverse patient populations.⏎• Operational and Change Management Demands: Implementation requires updates to⏎clinical workflows, policies, training programs, and documentation practices, which can⏎slow adoption without coordinated planning.⏎• Funding and Reimbursement Uncertainty: Misalignment between budgets, uncertain⏎reimbursement pathways, and competing organizational priorities can delay investment⏎decisions.⏎4.0 Reimbursement: Feedback on how HHS can approach reimbursement regulation to⏎drive AI adoption⏎One of the primary barriers to AI adoption and development is the uncertainty around funding and⏎reimbursement. To align payment policies with the goal of promoting high-value clinical AI,⏎fostering competition, and increasing affordability, the following key policy changes should be⏎considered:⏎• Establishing Predictable Reimbursement Pathways through proposals like Health Tech⏎Investment Act (S. 1399) which would require CMS to place qualifying AI services into a⏎"New Technology" Ambulatory Payment Classification (APC) for at least five years⏎and/or Transitional Add-on Payments, similar to the Transitional Drug Add-on Payment⏎(TDAP), which provide temporary extra funding for new AI tools, allowing CMS to gather⏎data on their value before incorporating them into permanent bundled payments⏎(“Medicare reimbursement pathway for AI-enabled medical devices considered in Senate's⏎Health Tech Investment Act”, 2025; Levins, 2022).⏎• Prevention of the “overuse" of risks associated with fee-for-service models by shifting⏎toward Value-Based and Outcome-Aligned Models such as the ACCESS Model and⏎continuing to encourage contracts where manufacturers share the financial risk. For⏎example, providers might receive rebates if an AI tool fails to deliver the promised clinical⏎or economic outcome.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 9⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎• Incentivizing Equity, Interoperability, and Competition via policy changes designed to⏎ensure AI tools work for diverse populations and aren’t restricted to wealthy, urban health⏎systems. Proposed changes include financial rewards for bias mitigation where payers⏎may offer higher reimbursement rates for AI tools that demonstrate interoperability and⏎proven effectiveness across multiple demographic groups; and premarket Advance Market⏎Commitments (AMCs) that offer monetary prizes for solving specific clinical challenges,⏎with the requirement that the code eventually becomes more accessible to spur follow-on⏎competition.⏎• Modernizing Medicare Advantage (MA) Oversight with guidance to ensure AI tools are⏎used to support, not supplant clinical judgment. Guidance should include a prohibition on⏎automation bias meaning insurers cannot use AI to override established medical necessity⏎standards or discriminate against specific patient histories, ensuring that high-value care⏎remains the priority over automated cost-cutting.⏎Analysis of Reimbursement Challenges Facing AI Integration in Clinical Spaces⏎Many AI innovations improve or enhance workflows through workflow infrastructure (e.g.⏎automate components of a workflow) or as decision support (e.g. make prescriptive⏎recommendations). However, these capabilities don’t fit neatly into legacy health care billing⏎structures, which are traditionally designed around billable procedures and specific outcomes. For⏎example, an AI tool which assists in diagnosis or notes summarization may improve decision⏎support or save time, but that’s not a specific procedure or billable outcome. This misalignment⏎raises critical questions about reimbursement for AI-assisted or AI-enabled clinical functions.⏎Many AI tools provide indirect benefits, such as improving provider workflows or enhancing⏎decision-making, yet these contributions often involve broader infrastructure rather than discrete,⏎billable outcomes.⏎To address these complexities, health care leaders need to define clearer reimbursement⏎frameworks. Key considerations include establishing what aspects of AI are reimbursable—⏎such as AI as a service, AI-powered infrastructure, or AI-enabled outputs—while ensuring clarity⏎around safeguards like human oversight, documentation, and quality control. Additionally, it's⏎essential to outline standard criteria to justify reimbursement, which may involve demonstrating⏎measurable clinical value, operational efficiency, or patient safety improvements. Developing⏎a comprehensive checklist of dimensions for assessing AI reimbursement capabilities will be⏎crucial as the industry seeks to navigate this emerging area.⏎Payment models must evolve to reward measurable value rather than relying on traditional per-⏎use reimbursement structures. Value in health care should be defined by outcomes such as⏎improved patient care, enhanced clinical safety, or reduced administrative burden. For example, if⏎an AI-enabled tool significantly decreases diagnostic errors or reduces administrative workload,⏎these outcomes represent quantifiable improvements that could form the basis for reimbursement.⏎By incentivizing outcomes over isolated usage, payment models can more effectively align with⏎the capabilities and benefits of AI innovation. As AI continues to expand its role in health care,⏎these efforts will ensure its benefits are realized in a financially sustainable and equitable manner.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 10⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎5.1 Research and Development: Recommendations for prioritization of AI research and⏎development subject areas⏎In addition to targeting policy changes for regulation, reimbursement, and oversight of AI adoption⏎and integration into clinical workflows, it is equally important to invest in strategic innovation to⏎address technical, operational, and ethical barriers. By aligning research priorities with the needs⏎of health care providers and patients, organizations can develop AI solutions that not only meet⏎policy requirements but also enhance practical usability and outcomes, paving the way for broader⏎and sustainable integration into health care systems.⏎There are several key areas of AI research that should be prioritized to support and accelerate the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care. These focus areas have been identified not only for their potential⏎to drive adoption, but also for their ability to strengthen trust, reduce uncertainty, and implement⏎guardrails for the responsible use of AI tools. These priorities are discussed below.⏎Criteria for AI prioritization. In addition to identifying application areas, it is crucial to define⏎criteria that guide AI prioritization. These criteria ensure that research and development efforts⏎align with core objectives for successful adoption. They include:⏎• Adoption: Facilitating widespread, rapid integration into clinical workflows with minimal⏎disruptions.⏎• Trust: Building confidence among clinicians, patients, and the public in the safety and⏎reliability and resiliency of AI solutions.⏎• Reducing uncertainty: Mitigating ambiguity around AI risks, outcomes, and⏎implementation challenges, including administrative and reimbursement questions.⏎• Guardrails: Establishing clear guidelines and safeguards to ensure ethical and responsible⏎use of AI technologies.⏎Research Target #1: Explainability for Accountability⏎HHS should prioritize research on AI systems that support clinical reasoning by making their⏎outputs interpretable and aligned with how clinicians make decisions in real-world settings. Care⏎providers are ultimately accountable for patient outcomes and must be able to understand,⏎validate and challenge AI generated care recommendations. AI systems are at risk of⏎conveying conclusions or diagnosis while not stating the confidence or communicating⏎uncertainty. AI should be able to clearly identify which patient features or clinical events drive a⏎recommendation in a way that is rooted in science and that the care providers can validate.⏎A multidisciplinary approach is essential in building healthcare AI to ensure safety, efficacy, and⏎ethical compliance, as AI models require the combined expertise of data scientists, clinicians,⏎patients, ethicists, and IT professionals to address complex patient data, regulatory requirements,⏎and technical challenges. HHS should ensure such collaboration is supported to prevent⏎algorithmic bias, build trust in clinical decisions, integrate AI into existing workflows, and⏎monitor, audit, and update AI models as medical knowledge advances (Quinn et al., 2020).⏎Further, HHS should support research into how accountability is shared and documented across⏎stakeholders and multidisciplinary teams (such as stakeholders, facilities, technical and medical⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 11⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎teams, etc.). If HHS can create an environment where care providers and patients can trust the AI⏎recommendations and diagnosis, this will directly translate to increased adoption of AI as part of⏎clinical care.⏎Research Target #2: Knowledge Management Capabilities⏎All data-driven approaches—from machine learning to generative AI to rules-based approaches⏎and retrieval—depend on what content is accessible to the model, how that content is captured⏎(e.g. database schema, unstructured document, etc.), and how it is stored. The success of any AI⏎model is directly tied to the quality and structure of the data it relies upon. Fragmented data⏎pipelines, such as siloed data sources, or unstructured, messy data, severely impair the⏎functionality and reliability of any AI model. Addressing these challenges is crucial to unlocking⏎the full potential of AI in clinical care. To this end, HHS should prioritize several key research⏎areas in knowledge management:⏎1. Digitalization, integration and structuring of content: One of the foundational priorities for⏎the effective adoption of AI in clinical care is the digitization and integration of health care⏎data into a structured format. Healthcare data exists in a myriad of formats, each with varying⏎levels of security and sensitivity. A focus on developing and implementing systems that⏎securely integrate health care content while adhering to rigorous security standards is vital for⏎building robust and reliable AI technology. This requires advancements in encryption,⏎regulatory safeguards, and collaboration between technology developers and regulatory⏎bodies. Work has been done in areas such as data security protocols, but further efforts are⏎needed to ensure sensitive health care data can be effectively stored and processed while⏎meeting privacy and compliance requirements.⏎2. Safe use of imperfect health care data: In real-world settings, health care data is often⏎incomplete, noisy, or reflective of biases, as seen in many EHR systems. While perfect data⏎may not always be attainable, HHS can prioritize research focused on the development of⏎methods to use imperfect data safely and effectively. Techniques such as data quality⏎assessments, validation protocols, and error detection frameworks can help mitigate risks⏎associated with unreliable or inconsistent datasets. Furthermore, AI systems can be designed⏎to operate with partial or fragmented information, with safeguards to prevent misinformation⏎or harmful conclusions.⏎3. Development of performance benchmarks and standards: Trust is one of the most⏎significant barriers to AI adoption in clinical care, and a lack of transparency regarding how⏎models perform only exacerbates uncertainty among users. Establishing clear performance⏎benchmarks and standards can address this issue by providing proof of performance that⏎users—whether clinicians, patients, or regulators—can rely upon. By supporting research to⏎develop standardized methods for measuring and reporting AI performance, HHS can ensure⏎consistent evaluation practices across AI solutions.⏎Research Target #3: Improve AI Integration across Provider Network⏎Research focused on human-centered coordination workflows—particularly how AI can⏎enhance shared situational awareness among clinicians, nurses, and care managers across a⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 12⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎provider network without increasing administrative burden—can help advance AI technology for⏎seamless operations across organizations, data systems, and care teams. This makes it an especially⏎relevant area for HHS leadership and investment. By centering research on understanding how⏎AI can fit into real clinical workflows and reduce fragmentation instead of adding extra noise⏎into an already chaotic system, HHS can meet clinical staff, administrators, and patients⏎where they are and address real world needs.⏎As part of this focus, research should explore how AI outputs can be optimized for the unique roles⏎of care team members. For example, clinicians, nurses, and office staff each require different levels⏎of detail, patient information, and timing to perform their roles effectively. A well-designed AI⏎system would account for these variations and contribute to improving patient care while⏎minimizing alert fatigue, a critical factor for driving adoption of AI in clinical workflows. By⏎tailoring AI tools to the specific needs of each care team member, this research can ensure that AI⏎enhances care delivery across the network without adding unnecessary complexity.⏎A natural second phase of provider integration is the post-deployment modeling of AI systems⏎integrated into health care systems. Key focus areas include model or data drift and updates to⏎clinical practice patterns. Many AI tools demonstrate strong performance in controlled⏎development or pilot settings. Far less is known about the system performance post deployment.⏎As health care is always evolving, the AI used to support will need to keep pace as well. HHS⏎should invest in research aimed at monitoring key metrics such as diagnostic accuracy,⏎clinician workload, and AI response time and have the performance reports delivered in a way⏎that does not impose additional documentation burden on clinicians. If care providers can have⏎confidence that the AI they are working with is up to date, it will increase not only the adoption of⏎and trust in the AI tool but also reduce the churn of AI tools in the future.⏎5.2 Review of Existing Literature on AI in Healthcare Settings⏎To best understand the most promising areas of Healthcare AI research and development, it is⏎important to review the academic landscape as it currently exists. A growing body of published⏎literature, including systematic reviews, meta-analyses, and observational studies, has examined⏎how adopted AI tools influence clinical care, showcasing both potential benefits and emerging⏎risks.⏎One significant area of research into the benefits of AI in clinical spaces has surfaced evidence⏎showing that AI tools can achieve diagnostic performance comparable to human clinicians across⏎multiple medical domains. Takita et al. (2025) conducted a systematic review and meta-analysis⏎comparing generative AI with physicians, finding that AI models performed similarly to clinicians⏎overall, though performance was slightly lower than expert-level physicians in some tasks.⏎Complementing this, McGenity et al. (2024) reviewed AI applications in digital pathology and⏎reported high diagnostic accuracy, with pooled sensitivity and specificity exceeding 90% across⏎diverse histopathological tasks. A growing collection of studies indicate that AI can serve as a⏎reliable adjunct to clinical judgment, supporting diagnostic decision-making while emphasizing⏎the need for continued validation and careful integration. Beyond diagnostic accuracy, studies have⏎also examined whether AI integration translates into tangible improvements in patient outcomes.⏎Burdick et al. (2020) evaluated a sepsis prediction algorithm across multiple U.S. hospitals and⏎found that its use was associated with reductions in in-hospital mortality, length of stay, and 30-⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 13⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎day readmissions. Similarly, Yuan et al. (2025) conducted a meta-analysis of AI-powered early⏎warning systems and reported significant improvements in patient outcomes, including reduced⏎mortality and timely recognition of clinical deterioration. Collectively, these findings suggest that⏎AI can enhance patient safety and care quality, particularly when used to support early⏎intervention and proactive clinical decision-making.⏎Evidence from systematic reviews suggests that adopted AI tools can positively influence clinical⏎decision-making and care delivery across multiple healthcare roles as well. AI-enabled clinical⏎decision support systems have been shown to improve diagnostic accuracy, treatment selection,⏎medication safety, and workflow efficiency by providing patient-specific recommendations to⏎clinicians (Aggarwal et al., 2024). Similarly, nursing-focused research indicates that AI⏎technologies support earlier detection of patient deterioration, reduce hospital readmissions,⏎improve documentation accuracy, and enhance nurses’ confidence in clinical judgment (Mikkonen⏎et al., 2025). However, both reviews highlight variability in implementation and emphasize the⏎need for standardized evaluation, ethical oversight, transparency, and adequate training to⏎ensure AI tools safely augment clinical practice. A systematic review and meta-analysis examining⏎AI use in clinical documentation found that AI tools—particularly those using natural language⏎processing and generative models—were associated with significant reductions in documentation⏎time and workload (Zhao et al., 2026). AI-generated documentation was generally comparable in⏎quality to clinician-authored notes, suggesting potential benefits for reducing clinician burnout.⏎Nevertheless, the authors stressed that human oversight remains essential to maintain accuracy,⏎safety, and accountability in clinical records.⏎Emerging evidence also highlights potential risks associated with routine AI assistance. For⏎example, an observational study of colonoscopy practice reported that clinicians who routinely⏎relied on AI support demonstrated reduced detection performance when the AI was removed,⏎suggesting possible skill degradation over time (Budzyń et al., 2025). While causality cannot be⏎definitively established, these findings raise concerns about over-reliance on AI and the⏎importance of maintaining clinician expertise alongside technological augmentation. It is⏎important to note that the lag between conducting research and publications often spans 1-2 years⏎or more. In contrast the advancement in AI technologies is occurring quickly; as Dr. Dashun Wang⏎puts it, “The rapid rise of generative AI—which is reshaping research workflows faster than many⏎scientific institutions can keep up—only makes the stakes higher and the future shape of science⏎less certain” (Zhau, 2026). The strategy HHS adopts to support adoption of AI will need to include⏎consideration for the vast differences between the speed of research and the speed of innovation.⏎Economic Evaluation⏎The literature on artificial intelligence in clinical care frames the costs and benefits of AI adoption⏎through a spectrum of economic evaluation methods, ranging from traditional cost-effectiveness⏎analyses (CEA) and cost-utility analyses (CUA) to budget impact analyses (BIA) (El Arab & Al⏎Moosa., 2025). Currently, there are very few high-quality economic evaluations of AI in⏎healthcare (Wolff et al., 2020). Many studies report partial or isolated cost savings, and many⏎omit key costs such as implementation and maintenance.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 14⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎El Arab & Al Moosa, 2025 outline that many evaluations rely on static decision models that may⏎overestimate benefits, as they often exclude long-term, dynamic learning effects and critical cost⏎components such as infrastructure and training. In general, they found that across oncology,⏎cardiology, ophthalmology, and infectious disease applications, AI enhances diagnostic⏎accuracy, increases quality-adjusted life years (QALYs), and reduces overall costs by⏎minimizing unnecessary procedures and optimizing resource allocation.⏎Complementing these findings, other systematic reviews highlight methodological gaps that⏎temper overly optimistic conclusions. A separate review of health economic evaluations of medical⏎AI tools found that while most studies report cost savings or cost effectiveness, reporting quality⏎is often suboptimal and key cost categories are frequently omitted (Junior et al., 2026). For⏎instance, only a minority of evaluations explicitly included implementation or operational costs,⏎and many assessments focused on early-stage AI technologies with limited real-world validation.⏎This raises concerns that published economic benefits may be inflated or insufficiently robust for⏎policy or reimbursement decisions. The review calls for future studies to report complete cost⏎components, adhere to rigorous economic reporting standards, and assess AI systems at higher⏎technological maturity levels to ensure that cost benefits reflect real clinical deployment rather⏎than theoretical or pilot settings.⏎Another blind spot in AI adoption cost reporting pertains to the economic evaluation of Software⏎as a Medical Device (SaMD). While Wu et al. (2025) found that cost-utility and cost-effectiveness⏎analyses across 52 studies generally reported AI interventions as cost-effective, cost-beneficial, or⏎cost-saving, comparisons were difficult when studies excluded key expenses such as software⏎licensing, maintenance fees, and app access costs from reporting. This inconsistency limits the⏎reliability of conclusions about AI’s true economic value and underscores the need for⏎standardized frameworks that capture all relevant costs associated with SaMD.⏎Recent literature also emphasizes equity, bias, and distributional concerns. It was found that⏎evaluations often fail to stratify results by demographic or socioeconomic subgroups, potentially⏎masking disparities in who benefits from AI and who may incur costs or harms (El Arab & Al⏎Moosa, 2025). Equity considerations such as differential AI performance across populations,⏎varied access to digital infrastructure, or unequal capacity to absorb training and integration costs⏎are underreported, highlighting the need for inclusive and context-sensitive economic frameworks⏎that consider both monetary and justice-related dimensions of AI adoption.⏎In summary, current literature approaches the costs and benefits of AI in clinical care through a⏎plurality of economic evaluation methods, with most evidence pointing to potential cost savings⏎and improved clinical value, especially when AI enhances diagnostic accuracy or streamlines care⏎delivery (Wolff et al., 2020; Wu et al., 2025). However, persistent methodological limitations (e.g.,⏎incomplete cost inclusion, static modeling), the early maturity of many technologies, and⏎underrepresentation of equity impacts, caution against simple cost-benefit conclusions. Robust,⏎transparent, and context-specific studies, especially those incorporating full cost components over⏎time and subgroup analyses, are essential for translating promising economic signals into reliable⏎guidance for policymakers, health systems, and payers (El Arab & Al Moosa, 2025).⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 15⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎6.1 AI Approach: Recommendations on how to support AI integration and responsible use⏎in clinical settings⏎AI Evaluation and Oversight⏎The practical implementation of artificial intelligence (AI) tools in clinical care requires robust⏎evaluation methods to ensure effectiveness, reliability, and human-centered integration. Pre-⏎deployment assessments as well as post-deployment monitoring are both vital to ensuring AI⏎systems are trustworthy from the moment they enter the clinical workflow and continue to be over⏎time. Not only should evaluation focus on model function and accuracy, but it should also include⏎human centered metrics for usability, integration into workflows, and training. This multifaceted⏎approach paves the way for equitable and sustainable AI implementation across diverse healthcare⏎settings.⏎In pursuit of AI adoption in clinical care, leveraging standardized AI evaluation benchmark tests—⏎such as BIG-bench, Massive Multitask Language Understanding, GLUE and the Holistic⏎Evaluation of Language Models—serves as a powerful pre-deployment assessment strategy. These⏎tests provide valuable insights into the general reasoning, linguistic competence, and failure cases⏎of AI models and provide visibility into how the models follow instructions, conduct multi-step⏎reasoning, and ensure robustness across task types. AI models can be compared against each other⏎and human performance to identify which ones are worth investing in for specific use cases where⏎defined criteria are highly prioritized. A complete evaluation using these benchmark tests will⏎allow evaluators to assess where the model performs well and where performance is inadequate.⏎Pre-deployment robustness testing should be a high priority for HHS support as part of regulatory⏎policy. This includes perturbation testing and sensitivity analysis to minor data changes or missing⏎data. Models should be able to surface uncertainty and work with humans to review responses⏎when confidence is low. Before deployment, shadow runs and simulated deployment testing can⏎be very valuable in generating buy in with care providers without influencing care. Shadow runs⏎allow assessment of model decisions, output timing and error patterns, enabling gaps to be⏎identified and corrected before deployment.⏎Once deployed, AI systems must be continuously monitored to ensure that performance remains⏎stable as patient populations, medical knowledge, care practices and data evolve. Support for⏎evaluation approaches that enable early detection of model degradation and ensure timely updates⏎before patient care is compromised is important for ensuring long term success of AI tools. One⏎effective method to track post-deployment performance is input and output drift monitoring. Input⏎drift tracks shifts in patient demographics, diagnosis prevalence care provider patterns, and⏎documentation styles. This can be done using distributional distance metrics such as KL⏎divergence and maximum mean discrepancy. Distributional distance metrics measure how⏎different today’s data look compared to the data when the model was first deployed, providing⏎early signals when changes in patients, documentation, or workflows may threaten model⏎reliability—even before errors are visible in clinical outcomes. Output drift monitoring tracks⏎sudden changes such as an increase in high-risk flags or shift in recommendation thresholds.⏎Furthermore, humans should be constantly “in the loop” to ensure that the output drift reflects real⏎changes in care and not hallucinogenic spikes.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 16⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎The literature on AI evaluation methods for non-medical devices demonstrates a clear trend:⏎technical performance metrics are often prioritized, while workflow integration and human-⏎centered evaluation approaches receive comparatively less attention. To assess the overall efficacy⏎of an AI tool in clinical settings, researchers recommend a more holistic evaluation framework⏎(Jacob et al., 2025; El Arab et al., 2025).⏎Adoption evaluation efforts should examine how AI tools align with existing clinical processes⏎and identify potential benefits or disruptions. Examples of such methods include clinician usability⏎testing, time-on-task analysis, and end-to-end workflow assessments. Human-centered evaluations⏎should focus on the interaction between users and AI tools, addressing factors such as cognitive⏎load, training and adoption dynamics, and trust between clinicians and AI systems.⏎Evaluation methodologies should account for the diverse societal, cultural, and economic⏎variations across U.S. healthcare systems to ensure adaptability and inclusivity. By taking this⏎multifaceted approach, stakeholders can better understand the practical value and equitable⏎implementation of AI tools across clinical environments. This perspective aligns with the AI for⏎IMPACTS evaluation model (Jacob et al., 2025), which emphasizes holistic, sociotechnical⏎assessment across dimensions such as integration, monitoring, performance, acceptability, cost,⏎safety, and impact.⏎Incentive Structures to promote AI Development and Optimization⏎To best support the development and optimization of these processes and to encourage the⏎accuracy and reliability of AI tools, HHS should continue its tradition of publicly supported⏎challenges with academia and industry. Regardless of the method used, HHS should continue to⏎encourage innovation and exploration of AI tools related to accuracy, security, and end user⏎satisfaction.⏎Data Strategy for Improved Interoperability⏎Another critical focus for supporting AI adoption and development in healthcare spaces is⏎enhanced interoperability in healthcare data exchange. This serves as a critical enabler for⏎expanding market opportunities, fueling research innovation, and accelerating the development of⏎AI for clinical care. By harmonizing diverse data types including structured Electronic Health⏎Record (EHR) data, imaging data (DICOM), genomics and omics data, and patient-generated data,⏎healthcare systems can unlock richer AI insights and applications. For instance, structured EHR⏎data builds the foundation for predictive tools to improve chronic care management, while imaging⏎and genomics provide the basis for precision diagnostics and personalized medicine. Patient-⏎generated health signals offer longitudinal, real-time monitoring for risk prediction services, and⏎unstructured text enriched through NLP creates opportunities for enhanced clinical reasoning.⏎These advances empower vendors to design broader AI solutions that reach larger markets,⏎including underserved patient populations and specialized medical services.⏎Interoperability is further strengthened by standardized frameworks such as HL7’s Fast Healthcare⏎Interoperability Resources (FHIR) and Common Data Models (CDMs) like the Observational⏎Medical Outcomes Partnership (OMOP). FHIR, established as the standard for healthcare data⏎exchange through the 21st Century Cures Act, enables seamless communication across electronic⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 17⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎health records (EHRs) and digital tools using modular APIs (Office of the National Coordinator⏎for Health IT [ONC], 2026). FHIR simplifies app integration—allowing developers to plug into⏎EHR systems, fueling innovation in scalable healthcare platforms. Similarly, CDMs, like OMOP⏎and Sentinel, harmonize data from diverse institutions to facilitate multi-site research and⏎reproducible analytics for the FDA and NIH. By aligning clinical data standards for research,⏎interoperability accelerates market entry for AI tools while encouraging collaboration across the⏎healthcare ecosystem.⏎In addition to improving data access, benchmarking tools rooted in interoperability play a crucial⏎role in accelerating the development and adoption of clinical AI systems. Tools like FHIR-⏎AgentBench assess AI agents on realistic clinical tasks, test their ability to extract data, reason⏎within HL7 FHIR structures, and choose appropriate next steps without "hallucinating" responses⏎(Lee et al., 2025). By evaluating AI models on their ability to employ interoperable frameworks⏎effectively, benchmarks ensure that vendors prioritize functionality and data integration, not just⏎predictive accuracy.⏎Interoperability creates opportunities to fuel research by eliminating barriers to multi-institutional⏎data sharing and collaboration. Unified frameworks like FHIR enable academic researchers,⏎regulators, and industry stakeholders to analyze diverse datasets. Longitudinal and multimodal⏎data across geographic and demographic settings fosters equity-focused outcomes research, while⏎interoperable APIs make it easier for developers to refine tools for clinical trials and population⏎health analytics. Research collaborations enabled by interoperable systems attract investment⏎while ensuring that clinical AI pipelines remain evidence-based and scalable.⏎It would be valuable for HHS to create a health care centered benchmark dataset against which⏎AI tools can be consistently evaluated. This dataset could consist of task specific use cases and be⏎used to evaluate documentation assistance, triage, care coordination, and risk prevention scenarios⏎that AI systems are likely to support in real world clinical environments. Benchmark tasks should⏎reflect realistic clinical workflows, data availability at the point of care, and the longitudinal nature⏎of patient management rather than isolated predictions.⏎The constructed dataset should be representative of the patient population and have set robustness⏎and performance metrics. These metrics could include sensitivity, recall, precision, and Area⏎Under the Curve (AUC). It could also evaluate robustness and sensitivity to noisy or missing data.⏎The creation of the benchmark dataset would support reproducibility and reduce duplicative⏎evaluation efforts across the whole health care system. By treating benchmarking infrastructure⏎as a public good, HHS would lower barriers to entry and increase trust in the role of AI⏎systems in clinical care.⏎From streamlining AI tool integration to enabling reproducible research on diverse data sets,⏎interoperability not only improves the quality of healthcare data but also positions the industry for⏎innovative, scalable solutions that address emerging challenges in clinical care. By harmonizing⏎data and setting industry-wide benchmarks, healthcare systems can unlock the full potential of AI⏎to improve patient outcomes and advance precision medicine.⏎AI Security and Governance⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 18⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎While it is important that AI tools are accurate, it is also important to ensure security, governance⏎and patient privacy and safety when implementing AI in healthcare settings. AI tools used in⏎clinical care which are considered non-medical devices (such as documentation assistants,⏎summarization tools, etc.) may still influence clinical judgement, prioritization, and work⏎processes in ways that create risks around patient safety, privacy, and accountability. These tools⏎often operate as infrastructure within workflows, which can place them outside traditional⏎regulatory categories. This creates risk in areas not well addressed by traditional compliance⏎frameworks.⏎• New legal and implementation issues introduced. AI tools can introduce legal and⏎implementation issues including influence on clinical decision-making, evolution in accuracy⏎over time (through model updates or retraining, which can impact repeatability or unexplained⏎differences in behavior), and probabilistic rather than deterministic operation. Existing⏎governance and accountability structures typically assume functionality which does not change⏎from one day to the next and assume deterministic outputs.⏎• Liability and accountability ambiguity. Non-device AI tools may shape decisions without⏎clear assignment of responsibility among clinician, health system, and vendor. This ambiguity⏎is amplified by automation bias and by AI outputs presented in persuasive or sycophantic⏎natural language.⏎• Indemnification and contracting gaps. Many organizations lack leverage or technical⏎capacity to negotiate appropriate vendor obligations related to model updates, monitoring,⏎incident response, and performance drift.⏎• Auditability and documentation limitations. AI tools may not log model versions, inputs,⏎outputs, or clinician interactions in a structured way, complicating quality assurance, root-⏎cause analysis, and legal defensibility. AI tools are often ‘black-box’, challenging auditability⏎requirements.⏎• Privacy and secondary data use concerns. Foundation model deployments raise uncertainty⏎around PHI handling, data retention, and whether patient data may be used for training or fine-⏎tuning, even when HIPAA compliance is asserted.⏎• Security risks from expanded integration. AI tools often require broad access to EHRs and⏎clinical systems, increasing the attack surface and introducing new vulnerabilities through⏎third-party services and model manipulation risks.⏎• Human factors risk (cognitive load, alert fatigue, automation bias). Even highly accurate⏎tools can increase clinician burden or encourage over-reliance when outputs are verbose,⏎confident, and timesaving. These risks are not captured by traditional performance metrics⏎alone. For example, if AI is implemented poorly, it can create more clicks, more alerts leading⏎to user fatigue and impeding adoption.⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to support consistent governance of non-device AI through⏎coordinated action across federal agencies. HHS should prioritize:⏎1. Baseline governance guidance for AI tools used in clinical workflows including⏎minimum expectations for monitoring, human oversight and escalation, and regulatory⏎compliance. One approach could be to adapt existing models for risk management in⏎healthcare to develop guidelines and standards for AI model approval processes.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 19⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎2. Setting standardized transparency and auditability expectations, such as logging of⏎model version, input sources, clinician interaction outcomes (accepted/edited/overridden),⏎labeling if a record is AI generated, and confidence indicators (if applicable), to name just⏎a few possible metrics.⏎3. Evaluation and monitoring infrastructure. Coordinating development of evaluation⏎frameworks and benchmarking methods for non-device AI, including outcomes and human⏎factors measures (such as cognitive load, review fatigue, automation bias).⏎4. Privacy and security clarification for appropriate use of protected health information in⏎AI systems, such as acceptable architectures and protocols for cloud-hosted models, third-⏎party data handling, and de-identification expectations for training or fine-tuning.⏎5. Model procurement templates / recommended contracting clauses addressing update⏎notification, vendor responsibilities for drift, incident response, auditability, and data⏎retention. One potential avenue would be to publish recommended contract clauses for⏎common themes like transparency requirements, audit rights, disclosure of training data,⏎and update notifications.⏎HHS can effectively support advancements in these areas through mechanisms such as grants,⏎cooperative agreements, public-private partnerships, and prize competitions. Additionally, HHS⏎could play a crucial role in reducing uncertainty and fostering the safe adoption of non-device AI⏎by establishing governance expectations that are clear, transparent, and explicit. This would⏎mitigate risks associated with fragmented accountability, which often arises from opaque contract⏎language and complex workflow integrations.⏎Certification and Industry-Driven Testing of AI Tools⏎It follows naturally that if the AI tools themselves must be governed, so do the industries and⏎practitioners developing, installing, and maintaining them. The Senate’s overwhelming rejection⏎(99-1) of proposed legislation for a 10-year moratorium on state and local AI regulations favoring⏎centralized federal oversight highlights the challenges of decentralized regulatory influence on AI⏎(U.S. Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, & Transportation, 2025). The EU has shown⏎promising regulatory initiatives due to its ability to bring 27 countries together with one voice and⏎its mandatory conformity assessments for high-risk medical AI. It also requires extensive⏎documentation of data provenance, fairness safeguards, and human oversight. Through the EU's⏎Artificial Intelligence Act (AIA) algorithmic integrity is embedded within regulatory compliance⏎frameworks. The United States does not have such an overarching regulatory system. HHS has an⏎opportunity to center itself as a leader in this space to promote a regulatory framework that supports⏎the responsible, safe, and equitable use of AI.⏎HHS can further advance responsible AI adoption by enabling industry-driven testing through⏎voluntary federal certification frameworks for third-party AI certifiers, rather than certifying AI⏎tools directly. By publishing technical guidance, reference datasets, and evaluation frameworks,⏎HHS can create a common foundation for independent testing and encourage transparency around⏎performance, safety, and bias. The Office of the National Coordinator (ONC) Health IT⏎Certification Program provides a strong precedent: ONC defines certification criteria and⏎recognizes authorized certification bodies, while private entities conduct testing and certification⏎of health IT products. This approach creates market pull, as hospitals, payers, and providers⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 20⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎preferentially adopt technologies with recognized credentials, and could similarly incentivize AI⏎developers to participate in rigorous, independent evaluation.⏎Accreditation of organizations, programs, and systems. HHS can support private-sector⏎accreditation by recognizing or endorsing independent accrediting bodies that assess⏎organizational AI governance, model lifecycle management, and safety practices. This approach⏎allows HHS to promote consistent standards for clinical AI while leveraging private expertise and⏎avoiding duplicative oversight. A well-established precedent is CMS’s “deeming authority,” under⏎which approved national accrediting organizations, such as The Joint Commission and DNV, are⏎authorized to survey healthcare facilities for compliance with Medicare Conditions of⏎Participation. Importantly, CMS retains oversight through validation surveys, demonstrating how⏎federal agencies can rely on private accreditation while maintaining accountability and public trust.⏎This model could be extended to accrediting AI governance programs and deployment⏎environments in healthcare organizations.⏎Public-private partnerships to enable scalable testing ecosystems. Public-private partnerships⏎(PPPs) allow HHS to create the conditions necessary for credible, scalable, industry-led testing⏎without positioning the federal government as the tester of record. Through PPPs, HHS can co-⏎fund pilots, sandboxes, and shared infrastructure, responsibly provide access to federal data assets,⏎and convene stakeholders to align on evaluation priorities. The NIH Science and Technology⏎Research Infrastructure for Discovery, Experimentation, and Sustainability (STRIDES) initiative⏎illustrates this model by connecting NIH researchers and NIH-funded institutions to commercial⏎cloud services at discounted rates (NIH, 2025). STRIDES demonstrates how HHS can reduce⏎technical and financial barriers, enabling the private sector to build and test advanced biomedical⏎and AI capabilities at scale.⏎Credentialing and workforce development for individuals. HHS can support private-sector⏎credentialing and workforce development by defining high-level AI competency expectations,⏎funding external credentialing initiatives, and encouraging healthcare organizations to recognize⏎independently issued AI credentials. In this model, credentials are developed, issued, and⏎maintained by professional societies, certifying bodies, or training organizations—not by HHS⏎itself. HHS’s role is to align incentives and signal the importance of qualified AI use in clinical⏎care. Medicare and Medicaid provider credentialing offers a clear precedent: CMS does not⏎credential clinicians directly, but relies on private licensure, board certification, and employer-led⏎credentialing processes to determine eligibility for participation in federal programs. A similar⏎approach could be applied to AI-related clinical and oversight roles, ensuring safe and effective⏎use while supporting a private credentialing ecosystem.⏎6.2 AI Adoption: Successes and Failures⏎There are many examples of AI being used or having been attempted in clinical settings. Reception⏎of these tools are mixed due to variations in quality, functionality, rollout, user optimization,⏎training and maintenance. For example, Legacy Health IT, including many EHR systems were⏎designed around billing, not care. AI is now being layered upon that same architecture, so it⏎naturally optimizes the wrong thing. Another example includes the CMS ACCESS models which⏎lack software built directly into the EHR. Third party apps (Oracle, Epic, Meditech) connect via⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 21⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎CMS-required APIs instead, creating inefficiencies. It follows that AI will need its own platform⏎for clinical care.⏎To date, AI tools in clinical care have demonstrated a stark contrast between their performance in⏎administrative and diagnostic "closed loop" tasks versus complex bedside decision-making.⏎Where AI Has Met or Exceeded Expectations⏎• Automated Clinical Documentation: AI "scribes" (e.g., Nuance, DAX, Copilot) have⏎significantly reduced administrative burnout. Organizations like Rush University Medical⏎Center reported a 74% reduction in clinician burnout, while Northwestern Medicine saw⏎doctors able to treat ~11 more patients monthly due to time savings (Dahdah, 2024;Siwicki,⏎2025). Several recent studies support these anecdotal reports, indicating significant⏎reductions in task load and burnout along with reports that physicians could see additional⏎patients because of time saved (Albrecht et al., 2024; Shah et al., 2025).⏎• Imaging and Diagnostics: AI has reached maturity in radiology and pathology. Tools⏎often rival or exceed human accuracy in detecting colonic polyps, breast cancer in⏎mammograms, and early-stage skin cancers. Jones et al. (2026) speaks to a significant⏎finding in the use of AI and genomics data to assist in diagnosis of Pneumonia, a finding⏎which could result in mor than an 80% decrease in inappropriate antibiotics (Phan et al.,⏎2025).⏎• Operational Efficiency: AI-driven scheduling at organizations like HCA Healthcare has⏎yielded a 40% increase in productivity and a 60% enhancement in patient satisfaction by⏎reducing wait times (Waligora et al, 2025).⏎• Patient Triage and Virtual Assistance: Chatbots and symptom-checkers integrated into⏎patient portals have successfully cut unscheduled emergency visits while providing safe⏎triage advice (Elechi et al., 2025).⏎Where AI Has Fallen Short⏎• Real-World Predictive Accuracy: While models often show "excellent" accuracy in⏎controlled trials, they frequently fail at the bedside. A March 2025 study found that⏎mortality-prediction models missed 66% of high-risk cases in real-world hospital settings⏎(Pias et al., 2025).⏎• Early Disease Detection (Sepsis): Despite massive investment, many sepsis-detection⏎models have underperformed. Research at the University of Michigan revealed a widely used⏎model was wrong more often than if it was right, missing two-thirds of actual cases while⏎triggering frequent false alarms (Lu et al., 2021).⏎• Complex Clinical Reasoning: Leading AI systems struggle when standard medical⏎questions are modified to include "none of the above" or require managing incomplete⏎information, with accuracy dropping by more than a third in some tests.⏎• Scalability and Workflow Friction: An estimated 80% to 95% of AI projects fail to scale⏎beyond the pilot phase. Failure often occurs not due to technical flaws but because tools⏎are not integrated into existing workflows, forcing clinicians to log into separate systems⏎and adding "friction" rather than reducing it.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 22⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎• Bias and Cultural Nuance: Many models exhibit calibration drift or perform poorly for⏎non-white or low-income patient demographics because they were trained on limited, non-⏎diverse datasets.⏎• Failure to include patients in AI development: Healthcare has struggled to include⏎patients in AI development, often prioritizing technical, administrative, or efficiency goals⏎over user-centered design, leading to tools that disrupt clinical workflows and fail to⏎address actual patient needs. Key failures include opaque, non-inclusive development⏎processes, lack of patient input on, and inattention to, potential biases.⏎The use of AI in medical practices nearly doubled, from 38% in 2023 to 66% in 2024, highlighting⏎increased adoption of AI as an adjunct to patient care (American Medical Association, 2025).⏎However, providers are most comfortable with AI tools related to administration, such as billing,⏎transcription, discharge instructions, translation, research, and assistive diagnosis. A 2024 survey⏎of the American Medical Association (AMA) revealed that doctors want AI tools that save time,⏎improve accuracy, and strengthen patient care (AMA, 2025). In addition, they also want guarantees⏎such as transparency, oversight, and ethical guardrails. Integration is a key consideration. In this⏎survey, physicians emphasized the need for seamless EHR integration (84%), data privacy⏎assurances (87%), and feedback channels (88%) to drive adoption (AMA, 2025). There is a⏎recognized need to partner with AI not only for clinical input but for vendor liability when the AI⏎tool gets it wrong.⏎Furthermore, with respect specifically towards the use of LLMs for treatment, rather than⏎administrative purposes, research testing the use of LLMs for mental health treatment also raised⏎concerns for both privacy, safety, and for therapeutic outcomes (Moore et al., 2025). These⏎concerns included inappropriate chatbot responses to common and critical conditions as well as⏎expression of stigma towards mental health conditions (Moore et al., 2025). For this reason, in⏎most clinical settings, human interaction is still considered best practice for safe and adequate care.⏎While much of the discussion has been focused on the clinicians, administrators, vendors, and⏎regulators of AI, patient input into AI expectations is extremely underrepresented in research⏎(Moy et al, 2024; Nong & Ji, 2025). Studies, consisting mostly of smaller focus groups, have⏎investigated patient perspectives on AI use in healthcare and generally surfaced the following⏎priorities for AI:⏎1. That human oversight and decision making remains at the forefront of healthcare delivery.⏎2. That patients be involved in the AI development as stakeholders.⏎3. That the solutions be user friendly, easy to understand, and transparent in nature.⏎4. That data privacy remains of utmost importance.⏎(Foresman et al., 2025, Gundlack et al., 2025; Mikkelsen et al., 2023; Osnat, 2025)⏎Despite the concerns raised among patients regarding AI use in healthcare, it is also clear that⏎many see the advantages of using these tools and hope they result in faster information delivery,⏎reduced wait times, lower costs, and more personalized healthcare (Gundlack et al., 2025;⏎Mikkelsen et al., 2023; Osnat, 2025). A 2023 study by Adus, Macklin, & Pinto explored patient⏎perspectives on patient engagement with AI in healthcare and found that patients felt strongly⏎about the importance of patient involvement in AI development as well as comprehensive AI⏎education. Inclusion of diverse demographics in AI development was emphasized in this focus⏎group study as vital to useful AI integration (Adus, Macklin, &Pinto, 2023). In summary, while⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 23⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎patients recognize the potential benefits of AI in healthcare, they emphasize the need for human⏎oversight, transparent processes, and equitable representation in AI development. Prioritizing⏎patient perspectives will be essential to ensure that AI solutions align with the needs and values of⏎those they aim to serve.⏎7.0 Conclusions⏎Artificial Intelligence (AI) has the potential to transform healthcare by enhancing efficiency,⏎reducing clinician burnout, improving diagnostic accuracy, and expanding access to underserved⏎populations. However, for this transformation to manifest, critical barriers must be addressed.⏎Trust by clinicians, patients, and administrators in the tools being used must be earned through⏎transparent algorithms, pre- and post-deployment monitoring, bias mitigation, clear standards and⏎regulatory requirement, and adoption of data governance frameworks for adoption, evaluation, and⏎maintenance. A comprehensive, human-centered approach that emphasizes safety, accountability,⏎equity, and long-term sustainability is essential to the successful integration of AI into clinical⏎workflows.⏎This paper identifies actionable strategies across the healthcare landscape to enable responsible⏎and scalable adoption of AI in healthcare. By transitioning to value-based payment models,⏎improving data interoperability, fostering public-private collaborations, and implementing robust⏎evaluation frameworks, AI can be integrated in ways that align with clinician trust, patient safety,⏎and organizational priorities. Additionally, emphasizing human-in-the-loop design principles,⏎transparency protocols, and dynamic post-deployment monitoring provides critical safeguards to⏎mitigate risks such as automation bias, performance drift, and inequities in patient care as well as⏎common issues with LLMs and hallucination.⏎The path forward must prioritize alignment across stakeholders, including clinicians, patients,⏎vendors, and policymakers, to ensure that AI tools meet the diverse and evolving needs of the⏎healthcare system. As AI continues to advance, the U.S. healthcare system has an opportunity to⏎leverage its transformative capabilities while mitigating risks to achieve improved health outcomes⏎at lower costs. Through strategic action, public-private collaboration, and innovative governance⏎mechanisms, AI can drive solutions to longstanding challenges such as workforce shortages,⏎systemic inefficiencies, and disparities in care.⏎With its extensive expertise in operational transformation and mission-first⏎innovation, LMI stands ready to partner with HHS and other stakeholders to shape the future of⏎healthcare.⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 24⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎7.0 Citations⏎1. Adus, S., Macklin, J. & Pinto, A. Exploring patient perspectives on how they can and should be engaged⏎in the development of artificial intelligence (AI) applications in health care. BMC Health Serv Res 23,⏎1163 (2023). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-023-10098-2⏎2. Aggarwal, N., Ahmed, M., Basu, S., Curtin, J.J., Evans, B.J., Matheny, M.E., Nundy, S., Sendak, M.⏎P., Shachar, C., & Shah, R.U. (2024). Effectiveness of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Clinical Decision⏎Support Systems and Care Delivery. PubMed. https://doi.org/10.1007/s10916-024-02098-4⏎3. Albrecht, M., Shanks, D., Shah, T., Hudson, T., Thompson, J., Filardi, T., Wright, K., Ator, G.A., &⏎Smith, T. R. (2024). Enhancing clinical documentation with ambient artificial intelligence: a quality⏎improvement survey assessing clinician perspectives on work burden, burnout, and job⏎satisfaction. JAMIA open, 8(1). https://doi.org/10.1093/jamiaopen/ooaf013⏎4. American Medical Association (2025) AMA Augmented Intelligence Research: Physician sentiments⏎around the use of AI in healthcare: motivations, opportunities, risks, and use cases. https://www.ama-⏎assn.org/system/files/physician-ai-sentiment-report.pdf⏎5. Briones, E. M., & Cohen, R. A. (2026). Health Insurance Coverage: Early Release of Estimates From⏎the National Health Interview Survey, January–June 2025.⏎https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/data/nhis/earlyrelease/Health-Insurance-Coverage-Early-Release-of-⏎Estimates-January-June-2025.pdf⏎6. Budzyń, K., Romańczyk, M., Kitala, D., Kołodziej, P., Bugajski, M., Adami, H. O., Blom, J.,⏎Buszkiewicz, M., Halvorsen, N., Hassan, C., Romańczyk, T., Holme, Ø., Jarus, 