14⏎24. Kumar, S. M., Nath, S. N., Vani, H. C., Shrinivasa, B. M., Ahmed, N., Tomer, V., Bankoti, K., Gunjan,⏎M., Ali, S.S.B., Jaiswal, M.K., & Warkade, V. (2025). The role of artificial intelligence (AI) in the⏎elimination of malaria: a systematic literature review. Malaria Journal, 24(1), 427.⏎https://doi.org/10.1186/s12936-025-05661-7⏎25. Lee, G., Bach, E., Yang, E., Pollard, T., Johnson, A., Choi, E., Jia, Y., & Lee, J. H. (2025).⏎FHIR‑AgentBench: Benchmarking LLM agents for realistic interoperable EHR question answering⏎(arXiv:2509.19319). arXiv. https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2509.19319⏎26. Levins, H. (2022, July 13). Choosing a clinical AI reimbursement system. Penn LDI.⏎https://ldi.upenn.edu/our-work/research-updates/the-quandary-of-choosing-a-clinical-ai-⏎reimbursement-system/⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 26⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎27. Lu, J. H., Callahan, A., Patel, B. S., Morse, K. E., Dash, D., & Shah, N. H. (2021). Low adherence to⏎existing model reporting guidelines by commonly used clinical prediction models. MedRXiv, 2021-07.⏎https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.07.21.21260282⏎28. McGenity, C., Clarke, E. L., Jennings, C., Matthews, G., Cartlidge, C., Freduah‑Agyemang, H.,⏎Stocken, D., & Treanor, D. (2024). Artificial intelligence in digital pathology: A systematic review and⏎meta‑analysis of diagnostic test accuracy. npj Digital Medicine. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-024-⏎01106-8⏎29. Medicare reimbursement pathway for AI-enabled medical devices considered in Senate’s Health Tech⏎Investment Act: Insights: Sidley Austin LLP. Insights | Sidley Austin LLP. (2025, November 24).⏎https://www.sidley.com/en/insights/newsupdates/2025/05/medicare-reimbursement-pathway-for-ai-⏎enabled-medical-devices-considered⏎30. Mikkelsen, J. G., Sørensen, N. L., Merrild, C. H., Jensen, M. B., & Thomsen, J. L. (2023). Patient⏎perspectives on data sharing regarding implementing and using artificial intelligence in general⏎practice–a qualitative study. BMC health services research, 23(1), 335.⏎https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-023-09324-8⏎31. Mikkonen, K., Tuunainen, S., Oikarinen, A., Jansson, M., Woo, B., Zhou, W., Tam, W., Tuomikoski,⏎A., Kaakinen, P., Juntunen, J., & Jarva, E. (2025). Artificial Intelligence Technologies Supporting⏎Nurses' Clinical Decision‐Making: A Systematic Review. Journal of Clinical Nursing.⏎https://doi.org/10.1111/jocn.70156⏎32. Moore, J., Grabb, D., Agnew, W., Klyman, K., Chancellor, S., Ong, D. C., & Haber, N. (2025, June).⏎Expressing stigma and inappropriate responses prevents LLMs from safely replacing mental health⏎providers. In Proceedings of the 2025 ACM Conference on Fairness, Accountability, and⏎Transparency (pp. 599-627). https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2504.18412⏎33. Moy, S., Irannejad, M., Manning, S. J., Farahani, M., Ahmed, Y., Gao, E., Prabhune, R., Lorenz, S.,⏎Mirza, R., & Klinger, C. (2024). Patient perspectives on the use of artificial intelligence in health care:⏎a scoping review. Journal of Patient-Centered Research and Reviews, 11(1), 51-62.⏎https://doi.org/10.17294/2330-0698.2029⏎34. National Association of Community Health Centers (NACHC). (2023). Closing the Primary Care Gap:⏎How Community Health Centers Can Address the Nation’s Primary Care Crisis.⏎https://www.nachc.org/wp-content/uploads/2023/06/Closing-the-Primary-Care-Gap_Full-⏎Report_2023_digital-final.pdf⏎35. National Institutes of Health. (2025, March 10). STRIDES Initiative. Data Science at NIH.⏎https://datascience.nih.gov/strides⏎36. Nishida, N., Yamakawa, M., Shiina, T., Mekada, Y., Nishida, M., Sakamoto, N., Nishimura, T., Iijima,⏎H., Hirai, T., Takahashi, K., Sato, M., Tateishi, R., Ogawa, M., Mori, H., Kitano, M., Toyoda, H.,⏎Ogawa, C., Kudo, M., & JSUM AI investigators. (2022). Artificial intelligence (AI) models for the⏎ultrasonographic diagnosis of liver tumors and comparison of diagnostic accuracies between AI and⏎human experts. Journal of gastroenterology, 57(4), 309-321. https://doi.org/10.1007/s00535-022-⏎01849-9⏎37. Nong, P., & Ji, M. (2025). Expectations of healthcare AI and the role of trust: understanding patient⏎views on how AI will impact cost, access, and patient-provider relationships. Journal of the American⏎Medical Informatics Association: JAMIA, 32(5), 795–799. https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf031⏎38. Obermeyer, Z., Powers, B., Vogeli, C., & Mullainathan, S. (2019). Dissecting racial bias in an⏎algorithm used to manage the health of populations. Science, 366(6464), 447-453.⏎https://doi.org/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎39. Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT. (2026). FHIR ecosystem. https://isp.healthit.gov/fhir-⏎ecosystem⏎40. Osnat, B. (2025). Patient perspectives on artificial intelligence in healthcare: A global scoping review⏎of benefits, ethical concerns, and implementation strategies. International Journal of Medical⏎Informatics, 203, 106007. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2025.106007⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 27⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎41. Pai, A., Brunekreef, J., Marcus, E., Sanders, J., Da Silva Guimaraes, M., Menezes, R., Sanchez, C.I.,⏎Horlings, H.M., & Teuwen, J. (2025). A quantitative comparison between human experts and AI at⏎estimating tumor-stroma ratio. medRxiv, 2025-08. https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.08.26.25334451⏎42. Pavuluri, S., Sangal, R., Sather, J., & Taylor, R.A. (2024). Balancing Act: The Complex Rols of⏎Artificial Intelligence in Addressing Burnout and Healthcare Workforce Dynamics. BMJ Health and⏎Care Informatics, 31(1), 101120. https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjhci-2024-101120⏎43. Peng, D., Wang, Y., Preiksaitis, C., & Rose, C. (2026). SycoEval-EM: Sycophancy Evaluation of Large⏎Language Models in Simulated Clinical Encounters for Emergency Care. arXiv preprint⏎arXiv:2601.16529. https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.16529⏎44. Phan, H. V., Spottiswoode, N., Lydon, E. C., Chu, V. T., Cuesta, A., Kazberouk, A. D., Richmnond,⏎N., Deosthale, P., Calfee, C., & Langelier, C. R. (2025). Integrating a host biomarker with a large⏎language model for diagnosis of lower respiratory tract infection. Nature Communications, 16(1),⏎10882. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-66218-5⏎45. Pias, T. S., Afrose, S., Tuli, M. D., Trisha, I. H., Deng, X., Nemeroff, C. B., & Yao, D. D. (2025). Low⏎responsiveness of machine learning models to critical or deteriorating health⏎conditions. Communications Medicine, 5(1), 62. https://doi.org/10.1038/s43856-025-00775-0⏎46. Quinn, T.P., Senadeera, M., Jacobs, S., Coghlan, S., & Le, V. (2020). Trust and Medical AI: The⏎Challenges We Face and the Expertise Needed to Overcome Them. J Am Med Inform Assoc, 28(4):890-⏎894. https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaa268⏎47. Rodziewicz, T. L., Houseman, B., Vaqar, S., & Hipskind, J. E. (2024). Medical Error Reduction and⏎Prevention. In StatPearls. StatPearls Publishing. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK499956/⏎48. Shah, S. J., Devon-Sand, A., Ma, S. P., Jeong, Y., Crowell, T., Smith, M., Liang, A. S., Delahaie, C.,⏎Hsia, C., Shanafelt, T., Pfeffer, M. A., Sharp, C., Lin, S., & Garcia, P. (2025). Ambient artificial⏎intelligence scribes: physician burnout and perspectives on usability and documentation⏎burden. Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association: JAMIA, 32(2), 375–380.⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocae295⏎49. Sittig, D. F., & Singh, H. (2025). Recommendations to ensure safety of AI in real-world clinical⏎care. JAMA, 333(6), 457-458. https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2024.24598⏎50. Siwicki, B. (2025). Chicago’s rush rolls out ambient ai after successful pilot. Healthcare IT News.⏎Retrieved from https://www.healthcareitnews.com/news/chicagos-rush-rolls-out-ambient-ai-after-⏎successful-pilot.⏎51. Straw, I., & Wu, H. (2022). Investigating for bias in healthcare algorithms: a sex-stratified analysis of⏎supervised machine learning models in liver disease prediction. BMJ health & care informatics, 29(1),⏎e100457. https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjhci-2021-100457⏎52. Takita, H., Kabata, D., Walston, S. L., Tatekawa, H., Saito, K., Tsujimoto, Y., Miki, Y., & Ueda, D.⏎(2025). A systematic review and meta-analysis of diagnostic performance comparison between⏎generative AI and physicians. npj Digital Medicine, 8(1), 175. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-⏎01543-z⏎53. UK. Department of Health and Social Care. (2024) Equity in medical devices: independent review—⏎final report, The National Archives. https://www.gov.uk/government/publications/equity-in-medical-⏎devices-independent-review-final-report⏎54. U.S. Senate Committee on Commerce, Science, & Transportation (2025, July 1), Senate Strikes AI⏎Moratorium from Budget Reconciliation Bill in Overwhelming 99-1 Vote. [Press⏎Release].https://www.commerce.senate.gov/2025/7/senate-strikes-ai-moratorium-from-budget-⏎reconciliation-bill-in-overwhelming-99-1-vote/8415a728-fd1d-4269-98ac-101d1d0c71e0⏎55. Waligora, G., Sherwin, R., & Soucy, Z. (2025). Rapid Systematic Review: “CAN APPLICATION OF⏎ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE IMPROVE ED TRIAGE PERFORMANCE?”. The Journal of⏎Emergency Medicine. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jemermed.2025.04.001⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 28⏎⏎HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎56. Wolff, J., Pauling, J., Keck, A., & Baumbach, J. (2020). The economic impact of artificial intelligence⏎in health care: Systematic review. Journal of Medical Internet Research, 22(2), e16866.⏎https://www.jmir.org/2020/2/e16866/⏎57. Wu, W.-T., Chao, Y.-W., Lin, T.-K., Huang, C.-K., & Hsieh, P.-H. (2025). Economic evaluation of AI-⏎assisted technologies in healthcare: A systematic review. Journal of Food and Drug Analysis, 33(4).⏎https://doi.org/10.38212/2224-6614.3570⏎58. Yeganegi, M., Danaei, M., Azizi, S., Jayervand, F., Bahrami, R., Dastgheib, S. A., Rashnavadi, H.,⏎Masoudi, A., Shiri, A., Aghili, K., Noorishadkam, M., & Neamatzadeh, H. (2025). Research⏎advancements in the Use of artificial intelligence for prenatal diagnosis of neural tube defects. Frontiers⏎in Pediatrics, 13, 1514447. http://dx.doi.org/10.3389/fped.2025.1514447⏎59. Yuan, S., Yang, Z., Li, J., Wu, C., & Liu, S. (2025). AI-Powered early warning systems for clinical⏎deterioration significantly improve patient outcomes: a meta-analysis. BMC medical informatics and⏎decision making, 25(1), 203. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12911-025-03048-x⏎60. Zhao, J., Liu, H., Chen, Y., et al. (2026). Application of artificial intelligence tools and clinical⏎documentation burden: a systematic review and meta-analysis. BMC Medical Informatics and⏎Decision Making, 26, Article 29. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12911-025-03324-w⏎61. Zhau, C. (2026). AI has supercharged scientists—but may have shrunk science. AAAS Articles DO⏎Group. https://doi.org/10.1126/science.zc518iv⏎February 23, 2026 | HHS Health Section AI RFI Response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this RFI response. 29♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0196♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from McCollister, Anna , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anna♔McCollister♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Director, Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology,⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎Below are my comments, which I provide in response the the HHS Health Sector AI.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide input into the ASTP’s thinking and decision⏎making about shaping our approach to incorporating AI into clinical care.⏎Sincerely,⏎Anna McCollister⏎Patient Advocate⏎Technology Company Founder and Consultant⏎Member, HITAC⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider⏎specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Make IAS Possible in the Real World⏎AI has remarkable potential and some nascent demonstrated ability to support patients⏎and providers in providing care guidance and assistance, filling in major gaps in⏎physician-patient communications opportunities, information about issues that are⏎outside of the provider’s specialties and personalizing insights based on an individual’s⏎data. One of the biggest hurdles in the development and deployment of apps that can⏎⏎Anna McCollister⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 2 of 7⏎bridge the gap between and for patients and providers is the inability to access patient⏎data through IAS on existing data exchange systems, such as Carequality and TEFCA.⏎As we have known for years, attempts to get the data are more often than not thwarted⏎by providers who claim they cannot confirm that the patient requesting the data is in⏎fact the patient represented in the requested data. Out of fear of a potential accidental⏎disclosure of personal health data, providers stop the data from being exchanged. As a⏎result, the apps cannot access the data they need to help fuel the AI-generated⏎support. Patients are left waiting. Many have chosen to manually download their data⏎from their various portals and then upload the data into consumer LLMs, which have⏎no protections for privacy. Meanwhile, data brokers use well developed “digital finger⏎printing” algorithms to knit together data from individuals with remarkable accuracy⏎and completeness with zero regulatory oversight or limitations.⏎HHS’ Office of Civil Rights has resisted requests to provide guidance that could help⏎alleviate this issue. While OCR’s perspective is important - we absolutely must protect⏎against data breaches - there are ways this could be addressed. OCR could and⏎should develop a HITRUST-esque approach, whereby they define a gold standard for⏎proof of identification. These algorithms have been developed and used in other⏎contexts. They could work here, as well. Once the standards are in place, OCR should⏎grant any company who demonstrates they met those standards with a legal “safe⏎harbor”, protecting them against any enforcement actions and fines.⏎This kind of policy leadership is essential to make IAS real. Getting patients access to⏎their data for use in AI tools can be an essential part of not just self care management,⏎but also for clinical care. We cannot rely on physicians and provider systems to be the⏎gatekeepers for healthcare-focused AI tools. Patients must be an equal party to your⏎considerations. Enabling real patient data access and incorporation of their personal⏎data into AI tools will enhance clinical care, promote primary and secondary prevention⏎and improve disease outcomes.⏎Research Exchange Purpose⏎As a patient with complex chronic disease, it is essential that we enable and facilitate⏎data liquidity for research purposes, allowing researchers to better understand disease⏎patterns in the real-world setting. ASTP and TEFCA can play an important role in⏎enhancing this by creating a Research Exchange Purpose through TEFCA.⏎⏎Anna McCollister⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 3 of 7⏎One of the tensions that exists within TEFCA is the lack of an authorized exchange⏎purpose for research. From the outset, we have known that companies and⏎researchers would seek to access data through the exchange to enable secondary⏎research of integrated, aggregated health data. As ASTP worked to create and build⏎TEFCA, they rightfully prioritized the most essential exchange purposes. Research was⏎not among them. To get the system off the ground, that was the right call. However, at⏎this point in TEFCA’s development, we need to create a legitimate mechanism through⏎which authorized exchange can happen for research purposes. In so doing, we will⏎proactively alleviate some of the pressure on QHINs to police the use of the treatment⏎exchange purpose. At the same time, we need to be very clear about what research is⏎in scope and what is not in scope, creating stiff penalties for companies that misuse⏎the research purpose to harvest the data for monetization and other unintended uses.⏎In establishing the research exchange purpose, we need to set requirements for those⏎who use that exchange purpose, such as mandating patient consent and requiring⏎transparency about the specific ways the data was used, publicly releasing a list of the⏎ways the data was used, such as reports, summaries, publications or abstracts.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Improve Data Granularity⏎The clinical record for any patient is inherently incomplete. The EHR captures data⏎taken during clinical encounters and laboratory testing. The data contained within the⏎patient’’s record represent discrete elements related to health that are easily measured⏎in a clinical setting.⏎By their nature, these clinical visits are episodic. They are snapshots in time, without⏎context for what has happened and why since the last clinical encounter. Any clinician⏎and patient knows the data are not a representation of the patient’s full health status⏎and the things that contribute to their ongoing health management or disease⏎progression. For some diseases or status checks, that might be OK. But for chronic⏎disease management, especially for data-intensive chronic disease, such as diabetes,⏎hypertension, congestive heart failure and cardiovascular disease, among many others,⏎⏎Anna McCollister⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 4 of 7⏎basing the patient’s treatment decisions solely on the data contained in their EHR⏎would be unwise. The doctor needs more context, more data.⏎The good news is, much of this data exists in machine-readable, standardized formats⏎that could be incorporated into the EHR. Despite efforts over many years to encourage⏎incorporation of this data into certified EHRs, thus far, ONC/ASTP has not made it a⏎requirement.⏎In traditional clinical care, the gaps in data can be augmented by physician and patient⏎discussions or record exchange. It’s time consuming, clunky and not ideal, but it helps⏎guide the physician in truly understanding the patient’s status, concerns and needs.⏎With this context, the physician can then review the data and make choices about⏎treatment. But the context is absolutely essential for getting the best chance to pick⏎the treatment and care course that will work for that patient.⏎As we move into the era of AI at full speed, I am greatly concerned about introducing⏎AI tools into clinical decision making without requiring them to have context beyond⏎the EHR. As a patient with type 1 diabetes, I have more than once overheard⏎physicians making insulin dosing decisions in the ER that would have caused me⏎significant harm if implemented. Fortunately, to date, I have been sufficiently conscious⏎to be able to advise them that I am very insulin sensitive and correct their dosing⏎strategy, which very likely saved my life. The physicians were basing their dosing⏎decisions on well vetted algorithms developed by expert clinicians who relied on data⏎from well designed studies and years of clinical care. And for me, those guidelines⏎could have been deadly. The data about my insulin sensitivity factor is coded into my⏎insulin pump through an app that doses me very precisely in response to changes in⏎glucose.⏎Imagine I’m in the ER in two years. Decisions about medication and dosing that are⏎well established in clinical guidelines have been incorporated into an AI-driven tool that⏎automatically doses patients in diabetic ketoacidosis without supervision from a⏎physician or consultation with a patient who may be unconscious. If the data from the⏎patient’s pump and CGM are missing, that algorithm is essentially blind dosing a lethal⏎product. It has no idea what it doesn’t know. Insulin can easily kill patients if too much⏎is given.⏎⏎Anna McCollister⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 5 of 7⏎Now imagine, my pump and my artificial pancreas app data are incorporated into the⏎EHR or is readily accessible and can be incorporated into the ER’s AI tool. In this case,⏎there’s a very good chance the AI would do a better job than the ER doctor who may⏎know about diabetes but is not a specialized endocrinologist. In this instance, AI could⏎save the patient’s life.⏎The most important element in this hypothetical isn’t the AI. That part is doable now.⏎What’s missing is the granular data that actually gives sufficient context, history and⏎more sensitive measurements that will enable more accurate, timely and safe care. This⏎is one narrow example of how AI could go drastically wrong or be heroically helpful I⏎can cite many others from my own personal care and treatment regimens, and I am⏎one person.⏎Patients live in a world of risk. We measure it and weigh it daily, sometimes hourly.⏎When it is transparent and we are well enough, we can make decisions. When⏎decisions are automated, the risks change. In some ways they may be mitigated, but if⏎not done wisely, the risks to patients will be multiplied. This can be fixed in part by⏎more granular data. If we do not address this issue, AI is likely to do more harm than⏎good. We must enable patient-generated health data into the EHR in a structured,⏎machine-readable manner.⏎AI in Payment Decisions⏎As mentioned above, the data within the clinical record fails to represent many critical⏎factors that need to be considered when assessing a patient’s health, understanding⏎their disease and choosing a treatment that is appropriate. As patients and providers⏎have experienced for many years, payment decisions made based on these data⏎elements alone miss the context required to choose the treatment that works best for⏎patients. Insurers tend to focus on very specific clinical measures represented in the⏎patient’s data. These measures are often considered without truly understanding the⏎patient’s full health profile, lifestyle, personal needs or biological idiosyncrasies. Often⏎the measures chosen by insurance companies to determine if patients can access a⏎treatment or care option are based on randomized controlled trials of patient⏎populations who have very different characteristics than the patient in question. As a⏎result, insurers choose to take the most restrictive, conservative approach referenced⏎in a study and apply those conservative approaches in their treatment access⏎decisions. The result is denial of access. The patient and provider are forced to jump⏎⏎Anna McCollister⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 6 of 7⏎through multiple hoops to appeal, a process that for patients is often required to⏎happen through a letter sent via the postal service.⏎There are many things about this process that could benefit from AI, but not if it’s used⏎to automate the current decisonmaking algorithms. If the current algorithms are⏎automated, the process becomes further calcified, extracted from human⏎decisoinmaking. Judging from horror stories of deployments to date, nearly impossible⏎to appeal.⏎If HHS deploys or allows insurance companies to deploy AI-based access decisions,⏎they must require that the AI have a more complete data set and context. In addition,⏎there needs to be clear mandates around appeals processes, timeframes for decisions⏎and transparency about decision makers, rather than only offering a PO Box for an⏎appeals department. These denials create remarkable burden for patients and⏎caregivers, costing them countless hours of time, anguish, pain and frustration. Many⏎give up, which results in worsening health and ultimately - especially for Medicare -⏎higher costs of care.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Individualized Benefits Determination⏎On more than one occasion, I have been told by prior heads of CMMI that it is⏎impossible to create a mechanism for doing nuanced, individualized decisions about⏎patient treatment access at scale. In response, I replied that we can’t do that yet, but⏎we should figure out a way to make it happen. We have incredible data. AI has reached⏎new levels of sophistication. AI that is informed with granular data, combined with⏎patient and provider health goals could be much better at making decisions about⏎enabling access to care that works for that specific patient in promoting their very⏎specific health and care needs.⏎This will take work, but it is doable. And, frankly, I think it’s the only way that we can⏎truly enable personalized medicine, tertiary prevention and healthspan, especially⏎⏎Anna McCollister⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 7 of 7⏎within the context of Medicare. The existing “once size fits all” treatment algorithms⏎produce predictable results. The treatments work for some, work OK for others, don’t⏎work for many and often, for a subgroup of patients, cause harm. Making “one size fits⏎all” decisions about access may have made sense 20 years ago, but it doesn’t in the⏎age of AI.⏎I encourage HHS/CMS to invest in research into the development of AI tools that can⏎create personalized, context and goal-driven reimbursement decisions at scale. It⏎would be a bold, future-oriented approach to updating our reimbursement and access⏎decisions in ways that improve health, encourage more patient engagement in⏎measuring and maintaining aspects of their health and ultimately reducing the⏎substantial taxpayer burden of poorly managed chronic disease.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0197♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Rodriguez♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔Center for Responsible AI in Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The question I keep coming back to, reading through this RFI, is who exactly gets left holding the bag when an AI system gets something wrong and a patient is hurt because of it. HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption, but I haven't seen equivalent urgency around the question of legal and institutional accountability, and that gap is the thing that worries me most about the direction this process seems to be heading.⏎⏎I'll explain why this matters specifically for the practices we work with. Our center conducted a policy review of 22 commercially available radiology AI products — this was completed in early 2024 — and fewer than a third of those products published disaggregated performance metrics broken down by race, ethnicity, or socioeconomic status. That's a concrete, documentable gap. But when I try to think through what happens if one of those products performs significantly worse for, say, Black patients at a federally qualified health center in rural Minnesota, and a missed diagnosis causes harm, the accountability picture becomes genuinely incoherent. The clinician points to the tool. The hospital points to the vendor contract. The vendor contract — and I've reviewed several of these — routinely includes language disclaiming any clinical responsibility, often in pretty explicit terms, sometimes buried under FDA clearance language that implies a level of context-specific validation that the clearance process doesn't actually require.⏎⏎FDA 510(k) clearance for an AI diagnostic tool tells you something about substantial equivalence to a predicate device. It does not tell you how the system performs on the specific patient population at a specific deployment site, trained on whatever data was used, integrated into whatever EHR workflow that institution happens to run. I'm not saying FDA clearance is meaningless. But I am saying that clinicians and administrators — and patients — reasonably read "FDA cleared" as meaning "safe and effective for use," and that's not what the clearance actually certifies. The gap between those two things is where liability disappears.⏎⏎What I want HHS to specifically address, and what I think this RFI is currently underweighting, is the question of workflow lock-in. Once an AI system is embedded in clinical workflows and tied to reimbursement coding structures, it becomes practically very difficult to remove even when problems are identified. We've seen this with other health IT systems. The costs of removal, retraining, and workflow redesign create enormous institutional inertia, and what happens is that administrators end up tolerating known performance issues because the alternative is too expensive and disruptive. If HHS accelerates adoption through reimbursement incentives before accountability structures are in place, we are going to bake that dynamic in from the start.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I don't have a fully formed proposal here. I think there are genuine arguments for moving faster on some of this, and there are real patients who could benefit from AI-assisted diagnostics right now. But the accountability piece isn't a detail to work out later. Tort law as it currently stands does not map cleanly onto AI-assisted clinical decisions where the causal chain runs through a vendor's training data, a hospital's implementation choices, and a clinician's interpretation of a system output. State medical malpractice frameworks weren't designed for this. Federal preemption questions around AI device regulation are genuinely unsettled.⏎⏎The specific ask I'd make of HHS is that any reimbursement pathway for AI-assisted clinical services include, as a condition, documented vendor disclosure of training data composition and deployment-context performance metrics, and that the Department work with CMS to define what institutional responsibility looks like when an AI-assisted decision contributes to patient harm. I recognize the second part of that is genuinely complicated and probably requires coordination with DOJ and with Congress. But at minimum HHS should not be creating reimbursement incentives for technology deployment in an accountability vacuum, and I think the current draft of this RFI doesn't adequately♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0198♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Martinez♔♔Washington♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honest question first: does submitting a comment here actually change anything? I've been a nurse for 28 years, mostly in emergency and cardiac care in the Seattle area, and I've watched a lot of policy processes happen above my head. I'm submitting this anyway because I had a patient last year who almost didn't make it because of a pattern that a better system should have caught, and I can't just sit on that.⏎⏎She was 67, Latina, came in with what looked like atypical chest pain, and sat in triage for close to two hours before her troponin trend got flagged properly. She recovered. But I've thought about that shift probably a hundred times since. And when I read about HHS wanting to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, my first reaction was yes, because something like that might have caught what I was slow to catch on a busy night. My second reaction, almost immediately after, was dread.⏎⏎Because here's what nobody in these RFI documents seems to say plainly: if an algorithm was trained mostly on data from white male cardiac patients, it might have deprioritized her presentation just as fast as human bias does. Maybe faster, and with more confidence. That's not a hypothetical to me. That's the specific fear I carry into every conversation about these tools.⏎⏎So the two things I actually want to say are this:⏎⏎1. Before any AI diagnostic tool gets used on real patients in emergency or cardiac triage settings, there needs to be some mandatory requirement that the training data demographics are disclosed. Not buried in a technical appendix somewhere. Disclosed in a way that a nurse or a patient advocate can actually read and understand. I don't know exactly what regulation would do that or what office would enforce it, but somebody has to be responsible for it. Right now I genuinely don't know who that is.⏎⏎2. The question of patient data is something I'm less sure about, but it worries me. Maria's records, her imaging, her troponin levels — if those get fed into a training dataset for some vendor's algorithm, does she know that? Did anyone ask her? I'm not saying it shouldn't happen. I'm saying I don't know if the consent process for that is actually real or if it's buried in a terms of service that nobody reads.⏎⏎I realize I'm not giving you specific regulatory language or citing particular sections of anything. I'm a nurse, not a lawyer. But I work with these patients and I watch these systems get implemented from the ground up, and usually by the time the policy gets to the floor level it's already been decided. The comment period feels like it might be the only place where someone like me can say: please don't let the speed of adoption outrun the question of whose data the model was built on.⏎⏎I don't know how to close this out except to say that the auditing piece is the part that keeps me up at night. Who actually reviews these algorithms after they're deployed? Is it the vendor? A hospital committee? I asked our informatics team about this a few months ago and got three different answers from three different people, which is not reassuring.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0199♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Kern, Ryan, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ryan♔Kern♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎14 February 2026⏎Subject: Public Comment on Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎To the Department of Health and Human Services,⏎As a junior pre-medical student at Stanford University, my perspective on the integration of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) into clinical care is shaped by distinct but complementary experiences: extensive shadowing of⏎physicians across various clinical settings and my own encounters navigating the healthcare system as a patient.⏎From the provider side, I have consistently witnessed physicians dedicating a substantial portion of their valuable⏎time to administrative tasks, particularly documentation and navigating complex, often unintuitive, electronic health⏎record (EHR) systems. From the patient side, I have experienced firsthand the long wait times, fragmented⏎communication, and inefficiencies that define much of the modern healthcare experience. It is from this dual⏎vantage point that I offer this comment, emphasizing the strategic deployment of AI to alleviate these burdens,⏎enhance provider productivity, and ultimately strengthen the patient-doctor relationship.⏎One of the most immediate and impactful applications of AI in clinical care lies in the automation of⏎documentation. My shadowing experiences have frequently shown doctors spending hours on "charting," often⏎extending beyond their scheduled clinical shifts. This administrative overhead is a critical contributor to physician⏎burnout and directly reduces the time available for meaningful patient engagement. The widespread adoption of AI-⏎powered ambient listening tools, capable of recording and summarizing patient encounters into detailed clinical⏎notes, is essential. By integrating such technologies into the clinical workflow, HHS can help redirect the physician's⏎attention back to the patient, rather than the computer screen. Furthermore, by reducing the time required for each⏎patient encounter through automated documentation, AI can effectively augment the capacity of our existing⏎physician workforce, thereby alleviating the growing national doctor shortage without compromising the quality of⏎care.⏎The U.S. healthcare system also grapples with exceptionally high administrative costs when compared to⏎international counterparts, such as Taiwan. A considerable portion of this financial burden originates from intricate⏎⏎2⏎billing and coding requirements. AI presents a significant opportunity to streamline these processes. Specifically, AI⏎should be leveraged to automate administrative billing and insurance claims processing. This would not only lead to⏎a substantial reduction in administrative expenditures but also minimize errors that frequently result in claim denials⏎and delays in patient care. To prevent the fragmentation that characterized the early adoption of EHRs, HHS must⏎proactively establish clear standards for the use of AI in billing and administrative tasks. Without a unified⏎framework for these AI tools, there is a considerable risk of creating new layers of incompatibility, which would⏎ultimately increase rather than decrease costs.⏎A critical barrier to the broader adoption of AI in healthcare is the substantial "switching cost" associated⏎with transitioning away from dominant, legacy EHR systems like Epic. While these systems are often criticized for⏎being slow and unintuitive, they form the operational backbone of most hospital systems. It is my belief that a more⏎realistic and efficient approach involves the government partnering with these established EHR providers, rather⏎than expecting nascent startups to completely disrupt the market. HHS should implement incentives designed to⏎encourage legacy providers to modernize their systems, transforming them into streamlined, AI-powered platforms.⏎My observations confirm that current EHR interfaces can be taxing and inefficient for providers. Therefore,⏎government incentives should specifically target the integration of AI-driven, intuitive user interfaces within⏎existing EHRs to facilitate rapid and widespread adoption. This approach would allow for mass implementation at⏎an accelerated pace, leading to improved patient-doctor interaction time and helping to address the current shortage⏎and inefficiencies within our physician population.⏎Beyond administrative efficiency, AI holds immense potential to improve health equity and access,⏎particularly for underserved and rural populations where healthcare resources are strained. AI can serve as a crucial⏎first-line triage tool, helping patients assess the urgency of their symptoms. For instance, an AI-powered initial⏎screening could help a patient determine whether their condition warrants an immediate, and often hours-long, drive⏎to an emergency room or if the issue can be safely addressed through a scheduled telehealth visit. By providing this⏎initial guidance, AI can reduce the burden on over-capacity emergency departments and ensure that limited⏎healthcare resources are allocated to the most pressing cases.⏎Furthermore, AI can revolutionize diagnostic processes by enabling the multi-purpose use of medical scans.⏎Currently, imaging studies like MRI or CT scans are typically ordered to investigate a single condition, representing⏎a one-dimensional layer of analysis. Research has shown that AI models can be trained to analyze a single scan for a⏎⏎3⏎multitude of different conditions simultaneously. HHS should support a future where one scan can yield a broad⏎range of preliminary diagnoses for a physician to confirm, rather than the current paradigm of one scan for one⏎issue. This would not only be more efficient but would also increase the likelihood of catching incidental findings⏎and other potential issues that might otherwise be missed.⏎Finally, to ensure the successful and safe integration of these tools, AI must become a core component of⏎clinical training. As a future medical student, I believe it is imperative that the next generation of physicians is⏎taught not only the fundamentals of medicine but also how to work effectively with AI. This training must go⏎beyond basic use and include a deep understanding of AI's limitations, including its potential to "hallucinate" or⏎produce convincingly incorrect answers. Physicians should be trained to prompt AI systems skillfully to reduce error⏎rates and to critically parse AI-generated information. The goal should not be to wait for a completely error-free AI,⏎which may never be realized, but to train clinicians to use AI as a powerful, imperfect tool. This approach, where the⏎human remains in the loop and is accountable for the final judgment, will foster trust and ensure that the⏎combination is greater than the sum of its parts.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide this perspective.⏎Sincerely,⏎Ryan Kern⏎Stanford University, Class of 2027♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0200♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Gergely MD, Robert, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Gergely MD♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Following my patent regarding personal medical records, AI can be employed individually on a single comprehensive medical record. Summarizing the record using AI will optimize medical care.⏎⏎My patent can be found on my pinned post in my X profile. @rgergelymd⏎⏎https://x.com/rgergelymd/status/1922114512632873104?s=20♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0201♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from The Digital Health Counsel , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔The Digital Health Counsel♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Re: Request for Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care (Federal Register Doc. 2025-23641)⏎From: Tayab Waseem MD PhD, Jefferson Lin JD, Dave Schoolcraft JD, Stephanie Simmons⏎JD, on behalf of the Digital Health Counsel1⏎Subject: The "Parallel Cognitive Record" and the Necessity of Privilege for Clinical AI⏎Queries (Response to Questions 3 & 9)⏎To the Coordinator:⏎The Digital Health Counsel (DHC) is a multidisciplinary collaboration among clinicians,⏎technologists, legal experts, and policy professionals focused on the responsible development,⏎deployment, and governance of digital health technologies. Our work centers on identifying real-⏎world barriers to adoption, aligning innovation with patient safety, and ensuring that regulatory⏎frameworks reflect how technology is actually used in clinical practice.⏎We write to urge the Department to address a critical, emerging liability vector that creates a⏎massive, structural barrier to the safe adoption of AI in medicine: the creation of a "Parallel⏎Cognitive Record."⏎While the RFI correctly identifies "liability" as a potential barrier for non-device AI (Question⏎3), current policy discussions often miss the granular reality of how this liability manifests in the⏎daily workflow of a clinician. The risk is not merely that an AI tool might give a wrong answer;⏎the risk is that the physician’s interaction with the tool creates a discoverable, permanent record⏎of their internal thought process. This includes their doubts, their differentials, and their double-⏎checks, which can be weaponized in litigation.⏎Defining the Problem: The Parallel Cognitive Record (Response to Question 3)⏎Historically, the physician’s progress note served as the definitive legal account of a clinical⏎encounter. It is a "Performative Record", a sanitized, professional conclusion designed for⏎communication and billing.⏎However, the rapid adoption of Large Language Models (LLMs) and decision-support tools⏎(e.g., OpenEvidence, ChatGPT, UpToDate AI, DoxGPT) has created a "Cognitive Record"⏎which includes the raw, timestamped logs of the queries a doctor makes. Under current discovery⏎rules, these logs are treated as standard Electronically Stored Information (ESI).⏎1 The Digital Health Counsel is a multidisciplinary collaboration among individuals with shared interest in⏎digital health policy. Each signatory participates in their personal capacity only and not on behalf of any⏎employer, client, or other institution⏎⏎This creates a dangerous divergence between the official medical record and the digital exhaust⏎of the physician's workflow. These logs present a foreseeable litigation risk that plaintiff⏎attorneys may seek to use.⏎1. Impeach Clinical Expertise: Using a physician's prudent verification of basic facts⏎("What is the dosage for X?") as evidence of incompetence or lack of training.⏎2. Prove "Scienter" (Knowledge of Risk): Using a physician's exploration of alternative⏎treatments or "what-if" scenarios to prove they were subjectively aware of a risk that was⏎not documented in the final note, elevating negligence claims to recklessness.⏎3. Undermine Clinical Judgment: Highlighting the timestamped gap between a query and⏎a decision to suggest that the physician "shopped" for an answer that fit a desired⏎outcome rather than following the patient's presentation.⏎The Chilling Effect on Adoption (Response to Question 9)⏎The RFI asks what concerns exist regarding the adoption of AI. The examples above illustrate⏎the primary barrier: Radical Transparency creates Defensive Medicine.⏎If physicians understand that every question, they ask an AI tool (every admission of⏎uncertainty) is being logged and can be subpoenaed to impeach their expertise, they will stop⏎asking questions.⏎This creates a perverse incentive structure:⏎● Safe Behavior (for the Patient): The doctor uses AI to verify their knowledge, catching⏎potential errors before they happen.⏎● Safe Behavior (for the Doctor): The doctor relies solely on memory to avoid creating a⏎digital paper trail of "doubt."⏎We cannot expect clinicians to embrace these powerful diagnostic aids while the responsible use⏎of such tools– verifying clinical judgments through AI queries– simultaneously exposes them to⏎heightened litigation risk.⏎A Potential Solution: A Federal Standard of "Cognitive Privilege"⏎To accelerate the adoption of AI in clinical care, HHS must clarify that clinical queries to AI⏎decision-support tools constitute "Patient Safety Work Product" (PSWP).⏎Just as hospital Morbidity & Mortality conferences and Peer Review deliberations are privileged⏎to encourage open discussion of errors, AI query logs must be privileged to encourage the⏎open verification of knowledge.⏎We respectfully recommend that HHS, in coordination with the Agency for Healthcare Research⏎and Quality (AHRQ), issue guidance clarifying that:⏎⏎1. Protective Scope: Prompt histories generated during the course of patient care are⏎protected under the framework of the Patient Safety and Quality Improvement Act of⏎2005 (PSQIA).⏎2. Discovery Limits: These logs should be discoverable only in cases of criminal⏎misconduct, not standard civil liability, mirroring the protections afforded to peer review⏎files.⏎Without this "Cognitive Privilege," the adoption of AI will be stifled by fear of legal liability,⏎and the potential for these tools to reduce diagnostic error will be impeded.⏎Respectfully,⏎Tayab Waseem MD PhD, Jefferson Lin JD, Dave Schoolcraft JD, Stephanie Simmons JD on⏎behalf of the Digital Health Counsel.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0202♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Contner , Alex, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Alex♔Contner♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My recommendation would be to use AI to screen for red flags for patients needing orthopedic care. If they don’t have any red flags or if the likely diagnosis is things like rotator cuff tears, meniscus tears, carpal tunnel, spinal stenosis etc… these patients should be sent directly to PTs. PTs are much more cost effective and would reduce a ton of surgeries down the line for conditions that get the same outcomes with PT versus surgery and PT combined. Right now Medicare still requires a referral from a physician to see PT (the only insurance to require it). This basically assures that people go the orthopedic route and thus inflates high cost claimants for musculoskeletal surgery. Email for more details if needed ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0203♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Thomas♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-one years on cardiac floors in Tucson and I'll tell you what actually keeps patients from getting good care, and it isn't a shortage of algorithms. It's insurance companies and their pharmacy benefit managers eating up a huge chunk of every healthcare dollar before the money ever gets anywhere near a patient or a nurse or a doctor.⏎⏎I'm not sure this RFI is the right place to say this, but I keep thinking nobody at HHS is going to say it if regular people don't. We had a patient back around 2019, diabetic woman from the Tohono O'odham Nation, and we almost missed her blockage because three different screening tools flagged her low risk. Her floor nurse pushed back, a doctor actually listened, and she went home to her grandchildren. That story is about bias in datasets and I understand that's what this RFI is partly about. But you know what else almost killed her? The four-day prior authorization delay on her follow-up medication because her PBM kept kicking back the request.⏎⏎So here's my frustration. HHS wants to talk about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, and I get it, there's real potential there, I'm not dismissing that. But we're talking about layering more technology onto a system where pharmacy benefit managers are taking somewhere between $200 and $400 billion annually depending on which estimate you look at, and almost none of that money is generating patient care. It's generating profit for middlemen. And this document doesn't touch that. Not a word.⏎⏎I worked with nurses who spent forty-five minutes on a single prior auth call. Forty-five minutes. That's not a problem an AI diagnostic tool solves. That's a structural problem that HHS has the authority to address and keeps not addressing.⏎⏎I understand AI tools can help with some of the administrative burden and I do think there's something to that. If a good algorithm can speed up a prior auth review that's been sitting for three days, okay, I'm listening. But the question of who actually benefits from that efficiency is one I don't see answered anywhere in this request. If the PBM gets faster at denying claims because their AI is better trained, that's not an improvement for my former patients on the south side of Tucson.⏎⏎I guess what I want to say, and I know this is a tangent from the specific questions being asked, is that any serious effort to improve clinical care outcomes through technology has to reckon with the financial architecture underneath the care system. Otherwise you're building a faster car on a road that's been deliberately broken up.⏎⏎The bias question in AI diagnostics is real and I hope you take it seriously. Native patients, Black patients, elderly patients with atypical presentations, these populations have been underrepresented in the training data for most of these tools and that's documented. But I've said my piece about the bigger issue and I don't think I'll get another chance to say it in a forum that anyone in that building might actually read.⏎⏎My name is Christopher Thomas, I'm a retired RN, Tucson Arizona. I spent most of my career at a hospital that served a lot of uninsured and underinsured patients and I watched the money flow away from them my entire career.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0204♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Healthcare Trust Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Healthcare Trust Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via http://www.regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop:7033A⏎330 C Street, S.W., Washington, D.C. 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The Healthcare Trust Institute (HTI) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RFI) published by the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National⏎Coordinator of Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) in the Federal Register on⏎December 23, 2025.1⏎HTI is an alliance of healthcare organizations committed to promoting and implementing⏎effective protections for health information that engender trust in the healthcare system⏎and allow for the necessary exchange of health data to advance treatments, cures and⏎improved healthcare quality for individuals and populations. HTI members, which⏎include companies and organizations from across the U.S. healthcare economy, agree⏎that uniform national standards for the protection of health information are critical to⏎protect patient data and spur medical innovation.⏎We appreciate ASTP/ONC’s leadership in seeking input on how to accelerate the⏎1 90 Fed. Reg. at 60108 (December 23, 2025).⏎⏎adoption and responsible use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. AI tools⏎already support a wide range of clinical and operational functions across the healthcare⏎sector and have brought significant benefits in terms of improved quality, efficiency,⏎access, and outcomes. Their potential to further improve clinician care and the clinical⏎experience is enormous, but realizing this potential will require a policy approach that⏎encourages innovation, supports adoption at scale, and protects patient safety without⏎imposing unnecessary or duplicative regulatory burden.⏎A National Framework⏎ASTP/ONC asks about the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care. Currently, the most significant hurdle for⏎healthcare organizations is having to navigate and comply with a myriad of overlapping,⏎and potentially inconsistent, state laws regulating the use of AI and data, which is a key⏎building block for training AI models. Some of these laws are sector-specific, but many⏎are not. In 2025 alone, approximately 1,250 AI bills were introduced at the state level⏎seeking to regulate almost every aspect of the use of AI2, and the pace of new state⏎bills on AI is only increasing, with more and more being enacted each year. The number⏎and variations in state privacy laws has also increased sharply over recent years, with⏎many imposing significant new responsibilities and administrative burdens. Compliance⏎with this proliferation of state laws will be extremely challenging and onerous for the⏎vast majority of organizations that do not operate exclusively within one state.⏎Establishing a national standard or framework for the use of AI and health data in⏎healthcare is critical to ensure regulatory harmonization and avoid undue burden,⏎inefficiencies, and potential safety issues. For these reasons we support federal⏎preemption of state laws regulating AI and data privacy, especially for key provisions⏎where conflicting requirements would become impracticable or overly burdensome. This⏎would not apply to areas that fall under the authority of states to regulate, but only to AI⏎or privacy-specific laws. To the extent that states regulate or provide guidance in areas⏎that fall under the jurisdiction of states (like commercial insurance, data center⏎infrastructure, child safety protections, etc.), they should be encouraged to take a⏎coordinated approach using common models or frameworks to avoid inconsistencies⏎and variability.⏎Harmonize with Existing Frameworks to Protect Patients⏎Patient safety and trust are paramount to the acceptance of utilizing AI in clinical care,⏎and appropriate oversight is critical to ensure that the power of AI solutions benefit⏎patients and does not put their safety or wellbeing at risk. However, we believe that this⏎oversight and regulatory protection can largely be achieved through existing regulatory⏎frameworks, which already provide substantial protections for patients.⏎This is particularly important in the health sector, where various HHS agencies, such as⏎the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎(CMS) and the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator (ASTP/ONC) have already begun to issue regulations and guidance on the⏎2 National Conference of State Legislatures AI database⏎Page 2 of 5⏎⏎use of AI within their areas of jurisdiction. A one-size-fits-all approach to AI regulation,⏎as is being proposed by some states, is destined to fail as it becomes clear that⏎overlaying a totally new framework over existing, often very different regulatory regimes⏎is impractical and, in many cases, infeasible. For example, the FDA has issued multiple⏎guidance documents on AI and machine learning in software as a medical device.⏎Similarly, HHS has issued regulations under Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act⏎addressing the use of patient care decision support tools in clinical care, and⏎ASTP/ONC has issued transparency and risk management requirements for Predictive⏎Decision Support Interventions that are part of certified health information technology⏎(HIT), such as electronic health records. Any AI regulation should harmonize with these⏎existing requirements as well as with other regulatory requirements applicable to these⏎entities. It is also critical that the agencies within HHS and across other federal⏎departments that may have jurisdiction over activities of the health care sector⏎coordinate their approach to AI regulation so that there is consistency and⏎harmonization of requirements among agencies and across the sector.⏎For example, health care providers operate within established guardrails, including FDA⏎oversight where applicable, HIPAA and state privacy laws, professional standards of⏎care, accreditation requirements, and medical malpractice frameworks. These⏎mechanisms provide accountability and patient protections regardless of whether AI⏎tools are used. Similarly, health plans are using AI to support care management, quality⏎measurement, utilization management, and administrative automation such as prior⏎authorization and claims processing. These activities are already subject to program⏎integrity requirements, audit processes, consumer protection laws, and oversight⏎mechanisms that can be applied to AI-enabled functions without creating new,⏎duplicative rules.⏎Wherever possible, HHS should rely on and reinforce these existing guardrails rather⏎than layering on additional AI-specific requirements. Leveraging established oversight⏎promotes consistency, reduces compliance complexity, and allows health care⏎providers and plans to adopt AI responsibly while maintaining patient trust.⏎Avoiding Overregulation That Could Inhibit Innovation⏎Overly prescriptive or technology-specific regulation risks inhibiting innovation,⏎discouraging investment, and delaying the translation of promising tools into real-world⏎clinical use. For example, clinicians are increasingly deploying AI-enabled clinical⏎decision support tools to assist with imaging interpretation, pathology review, and early⏎detection of conditions such as sepsis or stroke. These tools augment clinician⏎judgment rather than replace it and are often refined continuously based on clinical⏎feedback and new evidence. Overly rigid requirements could slow iteration and limit the⏎ability of health care providers to tailor AI tools to their patient populations and care⏎settings.⏎Similarly, AI-powered documentation and workflow tools—such as ambient clinical⏎documentation, chart summarization, and coding assistance—are helping reduce⏎administrative burden and clinician burnout, allowing clinicians to spend more time on⏎Page 3 of 5⏎⏎direct patient care. These tools typically operate in the background of clinical workflows,⏎and prescriptive regulation could inadvertently discourage adoption even where patient⏎risk is low.⏎ASTP/ONC should prioritize flexible, principles-based approaches focused on⏎outcomes, transparency, and accountability, allowing innovation to evolve while⏎maintaining appropriate safeguards.⏎ASTP/ONC asks if any HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to⏎augment the ability to develop or use AI in clinical care. There are several potentially⏎deregulatory actions that HHS could take to encourage the development of AI tools in⏎clinical care. These include addressing regulations that make it more difficult and costly⏎to manage data need to train AI models, such as significantly revising proposed updates⏎to the 2024 HIPAA Security Rule and better aligning the 42 C.F.R. pt. 2 (2025)⏎regulations with HIPAA consistent with amendments enacted in the Consolidated⏎Appropriations Act of 2021, Pub. L. No. 116-260. Working together with Congress, HHS⏎should also seek to strengthen HIPAA’s preemption framework and explore ways to⏎extend comparable privacy and security standards for personal health information held⏎by entities not currently subject to HIPAA. Greater nationwide consistency in privacy⏎and security protections would both enhance patient trust and provide clearer rules of⏎the road for responsible AI development and utilization.⏎Aligning Reimbursement to Incentivize Adoption⏎Reimbursement policy will be a critical driver of AI adoption in clinical care. Even when⏎AI tools demonstrate clear clinical or operational value, health care providers often lack⏎a sustainable financial pathway to adopt and integrate these technologies.⏎For example, health systems are using AI to support population health management⏎and risk stratification, identifying patients at high risk for hospitalization or care gaps and⏎enabling earlier, more targeted interventions. AI-enabled tools that improve diagnostic⏎accuracy, care coordination, or patient engagement—such as automated outreach,⏎triage, or follow-up—can improve access and outcomes, particularly in underserved⏎communities. Reimbursement policies that recognize the value of AI solutions will⏎facilitate this type of preventive and/or proactive care by making it financially viable for⏎health care providers. This not only benefits patients by improving care, but ultimately⏎reduces costs for the entire healthcare system.⏎We encourage HHS to explore reimbursement approaches that recognize and reward⏎appropriate use of AI tools, support integration into clinical workflows, and allow⏎flexibility across care settings and specialties.⏎ASTP/ONC notes that, for non-medical devices, use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security),⏎and asks what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them. While ASTP/ONC⏎does not define "clinical care" in the RFI, it is important that regulation of AI in the health⏎Page 4 of 5⏎⏎sector clearly distinguish between treatment or care provided by human beings,⏎including with the help of AI tools, versus consumer interactions with AI chatbots, such⏎as to ask about symptoms and treatments. The latter should without question be⏎regulated, but not as the provision of clinical care by health care professionals. Instead,⏎we recommend that the Federal Trade Commission oversee these types of generative⏎AI tools to ensure that consumers understand their risks and limitations.⏎The “supply chain” of AI models makes it challenging to determine responsibility/liability⏎in the event of an inaccurate recommendation. This underscores the importance of⏎transparency standards for vendors, and post-deployment standards for developers to⏎ensure ongoing integrity of tools.⏎Research and Development⏎ASTP/ONC asks if there are specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care. We appreciate the approach of⏎regulatory sandboxes. Stakeholders from across the continuum including patients,⏎providers, developers, and payers should be included in these efforts.⏎Conclusion⏎Accelerating the adoption of AI in clinical care will require a balanced approach that⏎promotes innovation, aligns reimbursement with value, and relies on existing⏎safeguards to protect patients. By avoiding unnecessary regulation, incentivizing⏎responsible use through payment policy, and building on current oversight frameworks,⏎HHS can help ensure that AI advances clinical care in ways that are safe, effective, and⏎scalable across the health care system.⏎Thank you for your consideration of our comments. Please do not hesitate to contact⏎me at tina@hctrustinst.com or 202-750-1989 if you have any questions.⏎Sincerely,⏎Tina O. Grande⏎President, Healthcare Trust Institute⏎Page 5 of 5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0205♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Society for Immunotherapy of Cancer (SITC) , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Society for Immunotherapy of Cancer (SITC)♔♔♔♔♔♔The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The Society for Immunotherapy of Cancer (SITC) appreciates the opportunity to provide comment and⏎feedback on the Request for Information regarding Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎SITC is the world’s leading member-driven organization specifically dedicated to improving cancer patient⏎outcomes by advancing the science and application of cancer immunotherapy. SITC is a 501(c)(3) not-for-⏎profit medical professional society of influential research scientists, physician scientists, clinicians,⏎patients, patient advocates, government representatives and industry leaders dedicated to improving⏎cancer patient outcomes by advancing the science and application of cancer immunotherapy. Through⏎emphasis on high-caliber scientific meetings; dedication to education and outreach activities; focus on⏎initiatives of major importance in the field; and commitment to collaborations with like-minded domestic⏎and international organizations, government and regulatory agencies, associations and patient advocacy⏎groups, SITC brings together all aspects of the cancer immunology and immunotherapy community. SITC⏎aims to one day make the word “cure” a reality for cancer patients everywhere.⏎The utilization of Artificial Intelligence (AI) is an opportunity to accelerate progress across the cancer care⏎continuum. SITC is excited by the potential for continued implementation of AI while maintaining the⏎highest standards of patient safety, privacy, and scientific best practices. Based on SITC’s collective⏎perspective and expertise, please find comments below regarding the RFI:⏎Regulatory Considerations of AI Implementation⏎• Transparency regarding the use of large language model (LLM) AI within the U.S. Food and⏎Drug Administration (FDA) is important to help accelerate safe adoption across healthcare. As⏎AI use expands into predictive and risk stratification models, imaging and pathology tools, and⏎generative or clinical decision-support systems, visibility into regulatory evaluation and⏎internal use can help set field-wide expectations. Transparency around model purpose,⏎performance, data validation, limitations, updating processes, real-world monitoring, and⏎accountability structures may strengthen trust and support responsible clinical integration.⏎• Continued clarity on how AI model updates over time may affect clinical trial⏎implementation. Clear regulatory expectations for revalidation, documentation, and post-⏎deployment monitoring could help maintain trial integrity and consistent performance.⏎Because single institutions may lack sufficient data to detect rare failures or performance⏎⏎issues in underrepresented populations, collaborative, multi-institutional evaluation⏎frameworks could support earlier safety detection and more equitable performance⏎assessment.⏎• Clear pathways are critical for sharing AI tools that meet regulatory standards across⏎institutions and could help prevent siloed implementation. Shared validation approaches,⏎standardized performance reporting, and coordinated monitoring may improve access to safe⏎and effective AI-enabled care. Consortium-based models could also support more equitable⏎deployment across healthcare settings while enabling shared learning and continuous⏎performance oversight.⏎Research and Development Opportunities Through AI Implementation⏎• Increased investment in AI-driven biomarker discovery can accelerate identification of⏎clinically meaningful signals across multi-omic and clinical datasets and support emerging⏎clinical AI applications such as predictive risk models, imaging and pathology tools, and clinical⏎decision-support systems. Safe and effective adoption will require strong transparency⏎standards across model purpose, performance, validation, limitations, updating, and⏎accountability. Supporting collaborative, multi-institutional evaluation can further improve⏎detection of rare failures, ensure performance across unique populations, and promote⏎equitable access to AI-enabled care.⏎• Investment in infrastructure that allows for Bio-banking and sharing of ECQM records for⏎analysis in HIPAA compliant ways can accelerate and enable high-quality AI development,⏎validation, and real-world monitoring. Transparency across data provenance, governance, and⏎intended clinical use can strengthen trust and regulatory confidence. Multi-institutional data-⏎sharing and oversight models can improve detection of safety or performance issues and help⏎prevent fragmented AI implementation across healthcare systems.⏎SITC is excited to further assist or collaborate on this effort by discussing the above or additional topics⏎presented within the RFI. SITC leaders have vast expertise in immunotherapeutic drug development and⏎have diverse perspectives on the future of cancer care and research.⏎Thank you for providing an opportunity to provide comment on this important topic. Should you have any⏎questions, please do not hesitate to contact me at mdean@sitcancer.org. We look forward to⏎collaborating in the future on all efforts focused on providing cancer patients with safe and effective⏎treatment options.⏎Sincerely,⏎Mary Dean, JD, CAE⏎Executive Director⏎Society for Immunotherapy of Cancer♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0206♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from University of Pennsylvania, Neurovascular and Endovascular Research Team (NeuroVERT), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔University of Pennsylvania, Neurovascular and Endovascular Research Team (NeuroVERT)♔♔♔♔♔♔Department of Neurosurgery⏎University of Pennsylvania⏎3400 Civic Center Boulevard⏎15th Floor, South Pavilion⏎Philadelphia, PA 1910⏎February 20, 2026⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical⏎Care⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The biggest barrier to private sector innovation in AI for healthcare remains the persistent gap between⏎product developers and the diversity of real-world clinical users. When developers seek out clinical advisors,⏎they may convene panels and obtain advice from select individuals, but there remains significant variability as⏎a user compared to other industries. There are small but meaningful nuances in how a proceduralist or⏎surgeon views a tool compared to someone practicing in a credit-based inpatient model versus an outpatient⏎setting. Even when advisors are consulted, blind spots may persist regarding the diversity of patients between⏎specialties and the variability of real-world clinical workflows.⏎Internal regulation within health systems also remains a significant barrier. Many hospitals, including Penn⏎Medicine, maintain AI governance boards which verify any AI tool considered for implementation in practice.⏎Due to concerns over data ownership, privacy, intellectual property, and competitive advantage, the internal⏎threshold can be higher than what is required for other research initiatives or clinical tools, and as a result,⏎such issues inhibit institutional willingness to share data or participate in pilots. Today, data is increasingly⏎Related Docket: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎Submitters:⏎Aanand J. Mehta; Joseph Richards; Rahil Patel; Visish M. Srinivasan, MD.⏎On behalf of the University of Pennsylvania Department of Neurosurgery, Cerebrovascular Division research⏎group (NeuroVERT).⏎Date: February 20, 2026⏎Correspondence Information:⏎aanand.mehta@pennmedicine.upenn.edu⏎visish.srinivasan@pennmedicine.upenn.edu⏎⏎viewed as a strategic commodity, and health systems may hesitate to engage with private companies if they⏎perceive asymmetric value extraction or if concerns about data sharing persist.⏎A third barrier is workflow integration. Many AI tools appear useful when tested independently, but real⏎impact depends entirely on how they are implemented in day-to-day clinical practice. Without plug-ins or⏎seamless integration into PACS systems or EMRs, adoption becomes difficult. One ambient system, Dragon⏎Copilot (Microsoft), has been integrated into EpicCharts EMR and placed into live testing at Penn Medicine,⏎but rollout has been limited to a small number of beta users. Meanwhile, another tool through Doximity is⏎free and widely accessible, but lacks EMR integration. Clinicians must record a conversation, permit it to⏎transcribe to the cloud, then manually paste the output into the EMR. Manually transferring text diminishes⏎efficiency such that traditional dictation or typing becomes equally efficient. In real-world clinical⏎environments, if an AI tool does not function as a plug-and-play extension of existing workflows that creates⏎more opportunity for the clinician to directly engage with the patient, adoption is unlikely, regardless of its⏎theoretical benefits.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies,⏎or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Regulation that forces a more open-platform environment would significantly accelerate innovation.⏎Currently, the presence of a dominant EMR vendor (Epic) makes it difficult to innovate. When building, a⏎company is compelled to integrate directly into Epic’s ecosystem or it is otherwise effectively excluded. If an⏎innovative company wants to expand its platform beyond Epic, it must take on additional cost burdens to⏎build for smaller competitors (like Cerner), which is unrealistic for most startups, and therefore creates⏎fragmented scalability. For this, strengthening 45 CFR Part 170 Subpart C, and particularly §170.315, to allow⏎third-party AI applications to integrate without proprietary vendor barring would significantly advance the⏎rate of innovation.⏎Another area that could be revisited is the rigidity of HIPAA-related restrictions as they apply to AI-enabled⏎tools. Even when tools are HIPAA-compliant, there is institutional hesitation around data flow, transcription⏎to the cloud, and AI processing. Currently, 45 CFR Parts 164 Subparts C and E permit the use of PHI for⏎treatment, payment, and healthcare operations, but there exists no mention of cloud-based⏎processing/storage, ambient transcription, and perpetual AI model refinement, rendering such regulation an⏎institutional decision, and thus creating discontinuities between health systems regarding the rigidity of⏎⏎regulations to implement. Issuing updated guidance regarding acceptable AI tool architectures and even⏎clarifying the use of deidentified information in model training under §164.514 could reduce institutional⏎discrepancies while preserving patient protections.⏎Pushing EMRs to allow an open-ended innovation platform would allow AI plug-ins to be developed in a⏎manner that encourages portability across systems. From a regulatory perspective, such practice would reduce⏎the structural advantage of a single dominant vendor. Without open architecture, even well-designed tools⏎cannot scale. Interoperability mandates exist in principle, but in practice, integration still remains highly⏎vendor-controlled. Devising certification requirements to allow plug-in flexibility and standardized API⏎functionality while ensuring that the plug-in prioritizes patient confidentiality is therefore essential.⏎In addition, there are structural issues around incentive alignment and accountability. Detection-based tools⏎may maximize sensitivity to avoid missed diagnoses, but excessive false positives create alarm fatigue and⏎undermine clinical utility. Reimbursement structures, including CPT code development and zero-sum RVU⏎allocation, create further disincentives for implementation of novel AI applications. Liability frameworks⏎remain insufficiently defined when errors arise from misleading output or failed detections. To address such⏎concerns, CMS reimbursement regulations under 42 CFR Parts 410, 414, and 415 could be revised to⏎incentivize the use of AI-enabled services. Developing add-on payment models similar to the 42 CFR §412.87⏎(New Technology Add-on Payment framework) would amend current legislation mandating the redistribution⏎of RVUs from existing services if a clinician chooses to utilize an AI tool.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎With respect to novel legal and implementation issues raised by the use of non-medical device AI tools in⏎clinical care, liability is the most immediate concern. Physicians are hesitant to use tools when they are⏎effectively assuming all of the liability unless the tool is clearly helpful or demonstrably more likely to improve⏎outcomes. Further, there is ambiguity around how responsibility is allocated when clinicians use AI outputs as⏎a component of their judgement. For instance, tools “not intended for clinical diagnosis” like RapidAI—used⏎in stroke workflows—remain a significant input into clinical decision-making. The distinction often rests on⏎technicalities, such as viewing images on a mobile device rather than a full-resolution radiology workstation.⏎As AI applications broaden beyond narrow use cases, exposure risk increases. Therefore, some form of⏎⏎semi-indemnification framework or clarified shared-responsibility model would reduce hesitancy and⏎encourage appropriate use without placing the full legal burden on individual physicians.⏎In addition, invisible data use by non-device AI systems also poses another implementation issue. Whether⏎the responsibility of AI-informed decisions resides with IT (who prioritize security and stability), clinical⏎leadership (patient outcomes and workflow efficiency), or both. When governance structures are dominated⏎by IT or executive leadership removed from active practice, implementation may slow. Therefore, active⏎clinician champions who maintain substantial clinical effort are essential to balanced governance. Otherwise,⏎decision-making risks becoming disconnected from frontline workflow realities.⏎Another implementation challenge is the lack of a culture of iterative deployment; there is minimal⏎infrastructure for opt-in early adoption models. When Dragon Copilot was released to a limited group of⏎clinicians for beta use at Penn Medicine, the approach represented a constructive shift toward iterative⏎refinement. However, access remained restricted, and broader opt-in participation would allow more rapid⏎real-world learning. Allowing clinicians to opt in voluntarily could facilitate innovation without system-wide⏎disruption. HHS could support this by clarifying pathways for phased, opt-in deployment models that⏎preserve oversight while enabling faster feedback cycles.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Because non-medical AI tools are designed to accelerate workflow rather than determine clinical⏎management, the legal and implementation considerations differ from those of traditional diagnostic AI⏎systems. For example, a recently introduced Penn-native tool functions as a high-level clinical snapshot. For⏎context, when patients present with numerous comorbidities after a complex, prolonged hospital stay, it can⏎take substantial time to understand why the patient is being seen, particularly because most charts are⏎organized chronologically. The summary tool synthesizes that information into a concise overview. For⏎instance, for a patient with an incidental aneurysm, it is not necessary at the moment to know uber-specific⏎metrics (like kidney function or ejection fraction); it is sufficient to know that the patient has heart disease,⏎kidney disease, hypertension, and that the issue prompting the visit was incidental. Such synthesis materially⏎improves situational awareness without replacing clinical judgment.⏎⏎For tools of this nature, the relevant regulatory and programmatic focus should shift toward workflow⏎efficiency, integration, and measurable burden reduction. Appropriate metrics could include minimizing time⏎spent in the chart, reducing chart navigation burden, and lowering the raw number of clicks required to⏎prepare for a visit. The ratio of pre-chart review time to actual face-to-face visit time is also meaningful. A⏎physician’s primary goal remains to increase time speaking directly with the patient while background⏎administrative tasks become autonomous.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎HHS can best support private sector activities by identifying AI applications that have demonstrated a high⏎level of clinical improvement evidence and nudging accreditation bodies and professional standards⏎organizations to incorporate those tools into accreditation requirements as appropriate. When an AI system⏎shows robust, objective improvements compared to the standard of care—be it through accuracy, timeliness,⏎or workflow efficiency—it should drive quality expectations rather than remain optional or⏎market-dependent. For example, AI-driven stroke triage tools such as RapidAI or Viz.ai LVO have improved⏎stroke workflow metrics, including door-to-puncture times and revascularization efficiency. For⏎comprehensive stroke centers, it would not be unreasonable for accreditation standards to require⏎implementation of similar tools that demonstrably improve these metrics.⏎This approach could extend beyond stroke to other domains such as trauma triage and time-sensitive care⏎pathways. Though, the rebuttal to this proposition is that competition on quality does not reliably drive⏎adoption on its own. As a result, institutions may not independently invest in tools that improve performance⏎unless external incentives or standards reinforce their value.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎A highly valuable application of AI in clinical care would be AI-based querying of individual physician⏎performance directly within the EMR. Current tools such as Epic SlicerDicer are largely manual and limited.⏎Extracting meaningful insights from traditional databases typically requires formal research efforts, chart⏎abstraction, and months-long studies. It should be possible to use AI-based natural language processing⏎⏎queries within the EMR to analyze one’s own outcomes in real time. For example, a clinician should be able to⏎prompt and inquire about their 30-day readmission rate or mortality rate over the last year. That level of⏎accessibility should not require hundreds of chart reviews to generate a simple performance estimate, and⏎would significantly contribute to iterative improvement of systems.⏎Similarly, procedural detail extraction should be feasible using AI/NLP capabilities. For example, when⏎reviewing a recent aneurysm flow diverter case, a clinician may want to know precisely which instruments⏎were used. Since, all clicks are recorded, names are embedded in the chart, and instrument documentation⏎exists within structured and unstructured fields, AI tools should be able to extract and synthesize that⏎information. If such a process were simple and automated, more clinicians would maintain logs to iterate on⏎technique and refine practice patterns.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎A major driver in adoption decisions is cost. Institutions are often asked to make significant upfront⏎investments in tools without clear, immediately quantifiable returns on investment. Demonstrating⏎downstream savings, improved throughput, or reduced complication rates often requires implementation⏎before robust data can be generated, which creates a cyclical barrier.⏎Regulatory burdens on companies can also indirectly influence institutional adoption. When compliance⏎requirements are extensive, development and validation costs increase, and those costs are passed along in the⏎form of higher pricing. Although the HHS cannot directly control internal hospital purchasing processes,⏎federal regulatory structure can project price through its effect on vendor operating costs. If regulatory⏎pathways are streamlined—while maintaining safety and efficacy standards—lower development costs may⏎translate into improved value propositions for institutions performing cost-benefit reviews.⏎Primary administrative hurdles include internal cost justification and prolonged review cycles through⏎governance bodies. Lack of transparency in the decision-making structure often siloes clinical end users from⏎financial decision-makers. Clinicians may recognize workflow benefits, but final purchasing authority rests⏎with financial and IT leadership. Addressing cost uncertainty, clarifying reimbursement alignment, and⏎reducing regulatory-driven price escalation would meaningfully lower the primary administrative barriers to⏎AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎While this may not represent full interoperability in the bidirectional sense, sharing datasets amongst health⏎systems while allowing multiple AI models to run in parallel would be of significant utility. A⏎nationally-acclaimed neurosurgery program has implemented a practice where three different stroke AI⏎systems—such as RapidAI and Viz.ai LVO—run simultaneously. From the CT scanner, imaging data is sent⏎to three separate cloud environments, generating three independent reports or outputs. If discrepancies arise,⏎the physician makes the final determination. This serves as a straightforward one-way data transmission that is⏎simple enough to implement and significantly increases robustness in decision-making. If the goal is to keep⏎the human in the loop, triplicating models provides protection against isolated AI error.⏎This same principle could apply beyond imaging. Transplant risk calculators or surgical risk models could be⏎deployed in parallel, generating independent assessments for the same patient. The same approach could be⏎applied to dynamic surgical risk modeling before stroke intervention. A feedback cycle could be created⏎where hospitals monitor which system aligns most accurately with their cases and what their success rates are.⏎Such benchmarking would fuel competition because vendors could be tested head-to-head under real-world⏎conditions rather than relying solely on pre-clinical validation claims.⏎From a data standards perspective, this requires standardized imaging formats, structured clinical data⏎interoperability (e.g., FHIR-based APIs), and consistent access to timestamped workflow metrics. Beyond⏎simply data exchange, this mandates the ability to apply multiple models to the same structured and⏎unstructured data inputs without vendor lock-in. Benchmarking tools could then measure concordance rates,⏎time-to-treatment metrics, and outcome correlations across models.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Incorporating AI into clinical care would provide the potential to raise the floor of care by helping ensure that⏎patients receive guideline-consistent management regardless of geography or clinician experience. AI-driven⏎decision support embedded within the EMR can provide guardrails that improve safety, particularly for less⏎experienced clinicians, by reinforcing evidence-based pathways and reducing variability. At the same time, AI⏎has the potential to reduce bias by overlooking certain clinical heuristics that may influence human⏎⏎decision-making, though it is also vulnerable to inheriting bias if underlying datasets are biased. For this⏎reason feedback loops and continuous monitoring is essential.⏎Patients have often expressed gratitude and enthusiasm that such technologies exist. Tools such as RapidAI⏎for large vessel occlusions and small aneurysm detection have identified findings that might not have been⏎captured on standard radiology reads. In other contexts, AI could improve access and communication in⏎situations where, for instance, neurosurgical patients who face long referral timelines gain access to tools to⏎streamline triage and access. Patients increasingly engage with AI independently as well. Some bring printed⏎outputs from LLMs, which are treated similarly to information from internet searches: understanding what⏎the patient has interpreted and then contextualizing it clinically. These encounters can be constructive and⏎facilitate shared understanding, particularly among technically literate patients.⏎However, some patients experience anxiety, particularly around incidental findings, where more information⏎does not necessarily translate into clarity. It is often the situation that information generated through AI tools⏎may be orthogonal to clinical relevance and contribute to unnecessary concern. As AI becomes more⏎integrated into patient-facing and clinician-facing workflows, HHS should consider frameworks that support⏎transparency, patient education, and contextualization of AI outputs.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate adoption in clinical care should work to⏎refine the structure of existing medical data. One of the most important areas for prioritization is radiomics,⏎which is highly clinical compared to more general AI developments. Radiomics represents a domain where AI⏎can extract features and predictive signals from imaging that are not readily apparent through traditional⏎analysis.⏎A closely related priority is the use of natural language processing to transform unstructured narrative text⏎into structured data. Radiology reports and operative/clinical notes contain substantial information in⏎free-text format. NLP tools that convert these narratives into structured labels can enable automated⏎extraction of clinical findings, facilitate dataset curation, and support the training and validation of imaging⏎models. For example, platforms such as RapidAI or IlluminateAI use NLP to identify patients lost to⏎follow-up by scanning the EMR for documented findings without subsequent care.⏎⏎Finally, the implementation of AI medical devices has not come without significant discrepancies in care. In⏎practice nationally, AI devices have shown higher recall rates compared to their non-AI (predecessor)⏎counterparts. This can be attributed to the practice where many AI-enabled devices reach market through⏎pathways that do not require new clinical trials and may be cleared by citing prior non-AI devices as⏎predicates. Therefore, HHS should prioritize research into real-world performance monitoring, drift⏎detection, and lifecycle management. Post-market surveillance infrastructure is essential to ensuring the safety⏎and likelihood of clinician and patient success.⏎Published findings across multiple domains support prioritizing these areas. AI-enabled triage tools have⏎demonstrated improved thrombectomy speed in stroke workflows, increased clinical trial enrollment through⏎automated identification of eligible patients, and improved aneurysm detection. However, adoption remains⏎limited not by lack of technical performance but by implementation barriers. One of the most consistent⏎challenges is compensation and workflow alignment—specifically, how clinicians and institutions are⏎reimbursed for the additional effort required to deploy and interact with AI tools. Even when AI improves⏎throughput or outcomes, unclear reimbursement pathways and implementation burden slow adoption.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0207♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Orthotic and Prosthetic Alliance , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Orthotic and Prosthetic Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔1250 Connecticut Ave, NW, 8th Floor⏎Washington, DC 20036⏎Phone: 202-466-6550⏎Fax: 202-785-1756⏎_____________________________________________________________________________________⏎SUBMITTED ELECTRONICALLY VIA www.regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building,⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Atten: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Posnack:⏎The Orthotic and Prosthetic (“O&P”) Alliance appreciates the opportunity to provide comments⏎in response to the Department of Health and Human Services’ (“HHS”) Request for Information⏎on the regulation, reimbursement, and research of artificial intelligence (“AI”) in clinical care.⏎The O&P Alliance is a coalition of four major national O&P organizations representing over⏎11,000 certified O&P professionals and over 3,000 accredited O&P facilities that serve millions⏎of individuals with disabling conditions in need of orthotic braces and prosthetic limbs. The⏎O&P Alliance is committed to ensuring that individuals with limb loss and limb impairment⏎receive timely and medically necessary O&P care from qualified practitioners and suppliers.⏎AI has the potential to improve patient outcomes, expand access to care, and reduce⏎administrative inefficiencies. However, to realize these benefits, HHS will need to modernize⏎reimbursement and other regulatory frameworks to enable the adoption of clinically appropriate⏎AI-tools in O&P care. Also, HHS must also ensure that robust safeguards are in place to prevent⏎bias in AI systems and prevent the misuse of AI by payers that could restrict access to care or⏎erode patient protections, with little or no input from real clinicians who treat patients on a daily⏎basis. This dual approach would help ensure that AI-tools are used to enhance, rather than⏎replace, clinical judgement and patient-centered care.⏎_____________________________________________________________________________________________⏎American Academy of Orthotists and Prosthetists (AAOP)⏎American Board for Certification in Orthotics, Prosthetics, and Pedorthics, Inc. (ABC)⏎American Orthotic & Prosthetic Association (AOPA)⏎National Association for the Advancement of Orthotics and Prosthetics (NAAOP)⏎⏎HHS Must Adopt a Clinically Appropriate Framework to Support AI Use in O&P Care⏎AI holds significant potential for advancing O&P care across the full continuum of clinical⏎services, including pressure mapping, gait analysis, and remote monitoring for long-term⏎management of patients using prosthetic limbs and orthotic braces. AI-enabled tools can also aid⏎orthotists and prosthetists in selecting the most appropriate orthosis or prosthesis and reducing⏎the risk of adverse events (e.g., falls and skin breakdown). When appropriately implemented,⏎AI-enabled tools can materially improve patient outcomes. In addition, AI-powered tools can⏎help capture clinician-patient conversations, assist with documentation, and help reduce⏎administrative burden, which allows orthotists and prosthetists to devote more time to direct⏎patient care.⏎Given these benefits, it is critical that HHS adopt a regulatory framework that appropriately⏎recognizes AI as a potential component of O&P care. Current Medicare and Medicaid⏎regulations, however, do not adequately account for AI-enabled software and tools. For⏎instance, there are no Level II Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System (“HCPCS”) codes⏎that specifically describe or value AI-embedded software or tools in O&P technologies. As a⏎result, manufacturers who invest in these technologies often cannot achieve the necessary return⏎on investment due to the lack of reimbursement from Medicare or Medicaid, despite these tools⏎demonstrably improving patient care. This misalignment between technological advancements⏎and payment policy creates a barrier to innovation and adoption within the O&P profession, and⏎limits patient access to clinically beneficial technologies.⏎Therefore, we urge HHS to address the absence of clear coverage/reimbursement pathways for⏎AI-enabled tools integrated into O&P devices and technologies. This pathway should address,⏎for instance:⏎• Embedded software within orthoses or prostheses;⏎• AI-driven analytics used for clinical assessment, fitting, and tracking for patients in need⏎of orthoses or protheses;⏎• Ambient listening and documentation tools powered by AI to improve the⏎clinician/patient encounter and clinical workflow; and⏎• Remote monitoring platforms that support ongoing management of O&P care.⏎Modernizing regulatory frameworks may require the creation of new HCPCS codes and⏎reimbursement levels, revisions to established coverage and coding guidance, and alternative⏎payment mechanisms that recognize the value of AI-enabled tools in O&P care. Where statutory⏎constraints limit such reforms, HHS should work with Congress to obtain the necessary⏎authority.⏎HHS Must Implement Guardrails on the Use of AI for Claims Processing and Prior⏎Authorization Requests⏎Although AI may serve as a valuable clinical tool when used to assist clinicians in medical⏎decision-making, we are concerned that some payers are deploying AI-enabled systems to⏎_____________________________________________________________________________________________⏎American Academy of Orthotists and Prosthetists (AAOP)⏎American Board for Certification in Orthotics, Prosthetics, and Pedorthics, Inc. (ABC)⏎American Orthotic & Prosthetic Association (AOPA)⏎National Association for the Advancement of Orthotics and Prosthetics (NAAOP)⏎⏎automate claims/prior authorization denials or otherwise restrict access to care without⏎meaningful human review or individualized, clinically-based determinations of medical⏎necessity. In the context of O&P care, where treatment decisions are highly patient-specific,⏎such practices by payers may result in inappropriate denials or delays in care, leading to⏎preventable health deterioration, loss of mobility, and loss of independence. It may also force⏎orthotists and prosthetists to pursue lengthy and costly appeals to reverse the payer’s improper⏎claim or prior authorization denials. AI should not be permitted to function as a substitute for⏎clinical review nor should it be used by payers to circumvent existing coverage or reimbursement⏎standards at the expense of practitioners and patients.⏎Therefore, HHS should ensure that payer use of AI in claims adjudication and utilization⏎management remains subject to robust transparency and meaningful human oversight, including:⏎• Requiring that a clinician with relevant qualifications review all denials of claims and⏎prior authorization requests;⏎• Requiring payers to clearly inform patients and practitioners when and how AI tools are⏎used in claims processing or prior authorization decisions;⏎• Allowing practitioners and patients to opt-out of participation in any program that relies⏎on AI-enabled tools to render coverage or payment determinations;⏎• Requiring payers to remain fully responsible for determinations made with AI-enabled⏎tools and to explain the individualized basis for those decisions;⏎• Ensuing that patients and practitioners are able to confer with qualified clinicians and⏎other payer employees with respect to questions regarding prior authorization requests⏎and claim processing;⏎• Require payers to conduct internal audits to ensure that any AI-enabled tools comply with⏎all applicable coverage standards; and⏎• Require HHS to monitor for discriminatory or biased outcomes that could⏎disproportionately impact individuals with disabilities or other complex medical⏎conditions.⏎HHS Must Develop Polices that Address the Bias⏎The use of AI raises concerns regarding algorithmic bias and its potential to adversely affect⏎individuals with disabilities. Many AI tools rely on pattern recognition derived from large⏎datasets that often inadequately represent individuals with disabilities, including individuals with⏎limb loss and limb difference. Because O&P patients often present with complex, highly⏎individualized medical conditions, AI systems trained on generalized datasets may not accurately⏎capture their unique clinical and functional needs or outcomes. This skewed data can result in⏎significant bias that adversely impacts individuals with disabilities. Bias can also occur when⏎AI-tools rely on flawed proxies, such as healthcare costs, to aid in the clinical decision-making⏎process. For O&P patients, these proxies can underestimate clinical care needs and the long-⏎term value of certain treatments, which could result in inappropriate restrictions on care.⏎Accordingly, overreliance of AI, without appropriate safeguards, may ultimately lead to⏎inappropriate care, delayed care, or denied care.⏎_____________________________________________________________________________________________⏎American Academy of Orthotists and Prosthetists (AAOP)⏎American Board for Certification in Orthotics, Prosthetics, and Pedorthics, Inc. (ABC)⏎American Orthotic & Prosthetic Association (AOPA)⏎National Association for the Advancement of Orthotics and Prosthetics (NAAOP)⏎⏎To help address bias in AI systems, HHS should ensure that AI-enabled tools used by⏎practitioners and payers are subject to robust safeguards, including:⏎• Rigorous testing for accuracy and bias specific to individuals with disabilities;⏎• Ongoing monitoring of AI to identify disparate impacts on individuals with disabilities;⏎and⏎• Research to study prevalence of bias in AI-enabled tools used by practitioners and payers.⏎* * * * * *⏎To contact the O&P Alliance directly, please contact Peter Thomas, O&P Alliance Counsel, at⏎202-872-6730 or at Peter.Thomas@powerslaw.com, or Leela Baggett at⏎Leela.Baggett@powerslaw.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎D r. Gerald Stark, Ph.D, CPO/L, FAAOP (D) Nathan J. Kapa, CP⏎P resident President⏎A m e r i c a n A c a d e m y o f O r t h o t i s t s a n d N a t i o n al Association for the Advancement⏎Prosthetists o f O r t h o t i c s a n d P r o s t h e t i c s⏎.⏎Kevin Hill, CPO⏎Kimberly Hanson, CPRH⏎President⏎Senior Director of Reimbursement⏎American Board for Certification in⏎................ American Orthotic and Prosthetic Association⏎Orthotics⏎Orthotics, Prosthetics and Pedorthics, Inc.⏎_____________________________________________________________________________________________⏎American Academy of Orthotists and Prosthetists (AAOP)⏎American Board for Certification in Orthotics, Prosthetics, and Pedorthics, Inc. (ABC)⏎American Orthotic & Prosthetic Association (AOPA)⏎National Association for the Advancement of Orthotics and Prosthetics (NAAOP)♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0208♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Talbert, Eli, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Eli♔Talbert♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment on HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Docket: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Submitted by:⏎Eli Talbert, PhD⏎Senior Data Scientist, United States Special Operations Command⏎The submitter works on operational AI governance in defense decision-support contexts, with a⏎focus on accountability, documentation, and escalation design; this comment applies those⏎governance principles to clinical AI adoption and oversight.⏎RFI Questions Addressed: Q1, Q2, Q3, Q4, Q7, Q8, Q10⏎Executive Summary: Four Immediate Policy⏎Actions⏎Regulatory uncertainty and liability ambiguity are among the most significant barriers to clinical⏎AI adoption. The actions below would accelerate deployment by establishing a common⏎accountability floor—reducing the duplicative institutional governance costs and defensive non-⏎adoption that currently slows innovation. To that end, HHS should:⏎1. Issue ONC interpretive guidance requiring minimum decision logging for high-⏎impact AI-enabled clinical decision support integrated into EHR workflows.⏎(Responds to RFI Questions 2, 3, 8)⏎2. Clarify accountability allocation expectations for non-device AI through joint ONC–⏎FDA guidance. (Responds to RFI Questions 2, 3)⏎3. Direct CMS to incorporate accountable AI documentation standards into⏎Conditions of Participation for hospitals deploying high-impact AI tools. (Responds⏎to RFI Questions 2, 7)⏎4. Fund NIH/AHRQ implementation science pilots testing post-deployment human–AI⏎interaction metrics, including override monitoring. (Responds to RFI Questions 4, 10)⏎These actions are likely to reduce liability ambiguity, strengthen interoperability, and create⏎measurable governance standards that accelerate responsible adoption, though empirical⏎evaluation of adoption and compliance effects should accompany implementation. This comment⏎does not propose new regulatory agencies, pre-market approval requirements for non-device AI,⏎or blanket restrictions on deployment. It proposes minimum documentation and logging⏎standards that reduce the regulatory uncertainty currently driving defensive non-adoption and⏎duplicative institutional compliance costs.⏎1⏎⏎For purposes of this comment, “high-impact AI” refers to AI systems that materially influence⏎diagnostic, triage, medication, procedural, or discharge decisions, particularly in settings where⏎clinician workload, time pressure, or financial incentives may shape reliance patterns.⏎I. Close the Regulatory Gap for Non-Device⏎Clinical AI⏎[Responds to RFI Questions 1, 2, 3, 7]⏎Governance challenges in clinical AI mirror those in other high-stakes, time-pressured human-⏎algorithm decision environments: authority boundaries, escalation design, and documentation⏎standards determine whether hybrid systems remain accountable over time. In defense decision-⏎support systems, these are not optional governance add-ons but prerequisites for deployment⏎authorization. Clinical environments differ from military command structures in training⏎standardization, liability regimes, and autonomy structures, but both domains confront the same⏎core challenge: maintaining auditable authority boundaries in high-stakes human–algorithm⏎collaboration.⏎Existing HHS frameworks already address important components of this landscape, including:⏎ FDA regulation of Software as a Medical Device (SaMD)⏎ Predetermined Change Control Plans (PCCP) governing planned device modifications⏎ ONC HTI-1 Decision Support Intervention (DSI) transparency requirements (45 CFR §⏎170.315)⏎ CMS documentation standards under the Conditions of Participation (42 CFR § 482.24)⏎For clarity, this comment distinguishes between (a) FDA-regulated device software pathways,⏎including SaMD submissions and PCCP-governed modifications, and (b) ONC-certified health⏎IT functionality and CMS documentation expectations applicable to EHR-integrated decision⏎support that may not meet device definitions. The governance gap identified here primarily⏎concerns workflow-integrated, non-device AI functionality embedded within certified health IT⏎environments.⏎Within this non-device space, a structural gap remains. Current frameworks emphasize model⏎transparency, performance reporting, and change management. They do not specify:⏎ How clinician override behavior must be logged⏎ How authority boundaries between model and clinician are operationally defined⏎ How escalation pathways are monitored over time⏎2⏎⏎This creates ambiguity in accountability allocation and increases institutional hesitation to⏎deploy AI at scale.⏎HHS can close this gap through interpretive guidance clarifying documentation and⏎accountability expectations, using existing regulatory authority under 45 CFR § 170.315 (ONC⏎certification criteria) and 42 CFR § 482.24 (CMS Conditions of Participation for medical record⏎services).⏎II. Establish Minimum Decision Logging⏎Standards⏎[Responds to RFI Questions 2, 4, 8]⏎HHS should establish — through ONC guidance or certification updates under 45 CFR §⏎170.315 — a minimum logging requirement for high-impact AI-enabled CDS.⏎At minimum, systems should record:⏎1. Timestamp of model output⏎2. Model recommendation or risk score⏎3. Clinician action (accept / modify / override)⏎4. Override flag with standardized rationale category⏎5. Escalation pathway triggered (if any)⏎6. Linkage to downstream order or documented outcome⏎7. Patient notification flag (whether the patient was informed AI contributed to the decision)⏎Illustrative workflow: An EHR-integrated sepsis risk model generates a high-probability alert for⏎a patient in a medical-surgical unit. The attending clinician reviews the alert but determines that⏎the elevated score reflects a known chronic condition, and overrides the recommendation. Under⏎the proposed schema, the system logs the model output (Elements 1–2), the override action with⏎a standardized rationale code (Elements 3–4), and the absence of an escalation trigger (Element⏎5). Downstream, the patient’s outcome is linked to the decision (Element 6). This structured⏎record enables quality review to distinguish informed clinical judgment from unexamined⏎dismissal—without requiring real-time intervention or additional clinician workflow steps.⏎3⏎⏎Critically, this schema imposes minimal marginal burden. Elements 1–3 build directly on⏎existing certified health IT audit logging capabilities required under 45 CFR § 170.315. Elements⏎4–7 require structured configuration within EHR workflows but not novel infrastructure or⏎hardware investment. The absence of a common standard imposes higher costs: each⏎deploying institution must independently design governance documentation, negotiate⏎liability allocation with vendors, and defend ad hoc logging practices—duplicative effort⏎that a federal floor would eliminate. Standardization reduces institutional compliance burden⏎while creating the interoperable evidence base that regulators, payers, and health systems need to⏎support scaled deployment.⏎4⏎⏎Implementation Feasibility⏎Certified health IT must support audit logging of actions related to electronic health information.⏎However, AI-specific decision telemetry, including structured capture of model output, override⏎rationale, and escalation triggers, typically requires additional configuration or integration within⏎EHR workflows. Elements 1–3 build on existing audit logging capabilities, while Elements 4–7⏎generally require structured configuration but not novel infrastructure. The patient notification⏎flag would indicate whether AI involvement was disclosed in accordance with institutional⏎policy; this proposal does not prescribe the notification workflow itself. The schema should be⏎machine-readable and expressible via FHIR AuditEvent or equivalent interoperable standards,⏎consistent with USCDI expansion goals.⏎This aligns with:⏎ HTI-1 transparency requirements under 45 CFR § 170.315(b)(11)⏎ FDA Predetermined Change Control Plan (PCCP) provisions under FD&C Act § 515C⏎(21 U.S.C. § 360e-4), which address planned modifications and when additional⏎premarket submissions are required; post-deployment monitoring expectations may arise⏎under device quality system and postmarket reporting frameworks (e.g., 21 CFR Parts⏎803 and 820), though PCCP provisions are primarily change-control focused.⏎ Regulatory concern regarding superficial review or rubber-stamping of AI⏎recommendations, consistent with HHS and OIG compliance principles on meaningful⏎clinician oversight⏎Such a schema would not inhibit innovation. It would reduce uncertainty and demonstrate⏎accountable deployment. Additionally, structured logging enables detection of disparate override⏎or recommendation patterns across protected characteristics, supporting existing⏎nondiscrimination obligations under federal civil rights law and strengthening patient trust in AI-⏎assisted care.⏎III. Ground Oversight in Empirical Evidence⏎[Responds to RFI Questions 4, 10]⏎Automation bias in clinical decision support has been documented in peer-reviewed literature⏎spanning over a decade, with recent findings confirming that the problem persists in production⏎AI deployments:⏎ Goddard et al., Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association (2012):⏎Systematic review establishing that automation bias is a consistent and measurable⏎phenomenon in clinical decision support, identifying frequency, effect mediators, and⏎mitigators.⏎ Lyell & Coiera, Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association (2017): A⏎PRISMA-compliant systematic review of 40 studies that found that automation bias⏎increases as verification complexity rises. This dynamic is particularly relevant to AI-⏎5⏎⏎enabled clinical decision support, where clinicians often operate under high information⏎load and time pressure.⏎ Gaube et al., NPJ Digital Medicine (2021): In a randomized study of chest radiograph⏎interpretation, clinicians were influenced by both accurate and inaccurate advice.⏎Diagnostic accuracy declined substantially when participants received incorrect⏎recommendations, regardless of whether the advice was labeled as coming from an “AI⏎system” or a “human expert.” The source label affected perceived credibility ratings but⏎did not significantly change diagnostic performance.⏎ Sivaraman et al., Proceedings of the CHI Conference on Human Factors in Computing⏎Systems (2023): Showed that clinician trust calibration in AI-based treatment⏎recommendations is difficult to achieve, and that reliance patterns go beyond binary⏎accept/reject decisions—reinforcing the need for structured override logging.⏎ Khera, Simon & Ross, JAMA (2023): Editorial accompanying Jabbour et al. empirical⏎research; warned that even in controlled settings without time pressure, clinicians favored⏎AI-based decision-making systems despite contradictory or clinically nonsensical⏎information, identifying automation bias as a direct risk of harm from AI-driven CDS.⏎ Dratsch et al., Radiology (2023): In a study of AI-assisted mammography (BI-RADS)⏎categorization, incorrect AI suggestions significantly impaired diagnostic performance⏎across all experience levels. While greater expertise reduced the magnitude of the effect,⏎even very experienced radiologists remained measurably susceptible to incorrect⏎recommendations.⏎These findings collectively establish that performance metrics alone do not capture behavioral⏎adaptation in hybrid human–AI systems. Over-reliance patterns emerge even among experienced⏎clinicians and are amplified under conditions of high information load and complexity. Existing⏎evidence suggests that transparency or labeling interventions alone do not reliably eliminate⏎these effects.⏎Structured override monitoring enables evidence-based oversight without imposing blanket⏎restrictions on deployment. Logging enables institutions to detect, quantify, and respond to⏎behavioral adaptation patterns.⏎IV. Clarify Accountability Allocation for Non-⏎Device AI⏎[Responds to RFI Questions 2, 3]⏎HHS should clarify expectations regarding:⏎ Designation of accountable organizational owner for AI deployment⏎ Documentation requirements for AI-assisted high-risk decisions⏎ Distinction between device-regulated AI and workflow-integrated non-device AI⏎This clarification could be implemented through:⏎6⏎⏎ ONC interpretive guidance under HTI authority (45 CFR § 170.315)⏎ Joint FDA–ONC guidance on hybrid accountability allocation⏎ CMS Conditions of Participation updates referencing documentation standards (42 CFR §⏎482.24)⏎Clear guidance reduces defensive non-adoption driven by liability uncertainty. Such⏎clarification reduces uncertainty without altering tort standards; it establishes documentation⏎expectations that support, rather than supplant, existing malpractice and product liability⏎frameworks.⏎V. Fund Research on Human–AI Interaction in⏎Deployed Systems⏎[Responds to RFI Questions 4, 10]⏎HHS should fund applied research to:⏎1. Measure longitudinal override patterns in deployed systems.⏎2. Identify escalation pathway shifts following AI integration.⏎3. Develop standardized human–AI coordination metrics.⏎4. Pilot interoperable logging schemas across health systems.⏎Implementation science funding through NIH, AHRQ, or cooperative agreements (including⏎CRADAs) would generate the evidence base necessary for proportionate regulation. Contracts,⏎cooperative agreements, and prize competitions (as referenced in the RFI) are all viable⏎mechanisms; cooperative agreements may be particularly effective for multi-site logging schema⏎pilots that require cross-institutional coordination.⏎VI. Align Reimbursement Incentives with⏎Documentation Standards⏎[Responds to RFI Question 2]⏎This comment focuses primarily on regulation and research levers. However, the documentation⏎standards proposed above have natural connections to CMS payment policy that would reinforce⏎adoption:⏎ CMS could condition New Technology Add-on Payments (NTAPs) or Advanced APM⏎incentive payments on demonstrated AI governance documentation, including the⏎logging schema described in Section II.⏎ Quality measure reporting under the Merit-based Incentive Payment System (MIPS)⏎could incorporate AI-assisted decision documentation as an Improvement Activity,⏎7⏎⏎creating a positive incentive for adoption of accountable logging without mandating⏎specific technology choices.⏎ Aligning documentation standards across regulatory and reimbursement frameworks⏎would reduce the compliance burden for health systems deploying AI, rather than⏎creating parallel reporting obligations.⏎These reimbursement connections are noted here to signal their feasibility; detailed payment⏎policy design is beyond the scope of this comment but warrants dedicated analysis in future⏎rulemaking.⏎Conclusion⏎By clarifying documentation standards and encouraging minimal telemetry requirements, HHS⏎would create conditions likely to:⏎ Reduce liability ambiguity⏎ Support responsible innovation⏎ Strengthen patient trust⏎ Provide regulators with objective oversight tools⏎These measures are proportionate, technically feasible, and aligned with existing regulatory⏎architecture. Much of this could be pursued under existing authorities through interpretive⏎guidance, certification updates, and program conditions; however, binding requirements would⏎likely require notice-and-comment rulemaking or enforceable Conditions of Participation⏎consistent with Administrative Procedure Act constraints.⏎The views expressed in this comment are those of the author and do not necessarily reflect the⏎official policy or position of the Department of War, the U.S. Army, or the U.S. Government.⏎8⏎⏎Appendix: RFI Question Crosswalk⏎The following table maps this comment’s sections to the specific questions posed in the RFI.⏎RFI Q# RFI Question (abbreviated) Comment Section(s)⏎1 Biggest barriers to private sector AI innovation and adoption Section I⏎2 Regulatory, payment, or programmatic changes to prioritize Sections I, II, IV, VI⏎(with CFR citations)⏎3 Novel legal and implementation issues for non-device AI Sections I, IV⏎4 Most promising evaluation methods for non-device AI Sections II, III, V⏎7 Roles and governing bodies influencing AI adoption; Sections I, IV⏎administrative hurdles⏎8 Enhanced interoperability to widen markets and accelerate AI Section II (FHIR AuditEvent,⏎USCDI)⏎10 AI research priorities; published findings on adopted AI tools Sections III, V⏎Appendix: Key Regulatory Citations⏎CFR Citation Authority Relevance to This Comment⏎45 CFR § 170.315 ONC Health IT Certification Criteria Regulatory hook for requiring decision⏎logging in certified health IT under⏎HTI-1 DSI criteria⏎45 CFR § 170.315(b)(11) ONC DSI Transparency Requirements Specific HTI-1 provision for decision⏎support intervention source attributes⏎and transparency⏎42 CFR § 482.24 CMS Conditions of Participation: Hook for CMS to require AI-assisted⏎Medical Record Services decision documentation in hospital⏎medical records⏎FD&C Act § 515C (21 FDA Predetermined Change Control Change-control framework for planned⏎U.S.C. § 360e-4) Plans device modifications; post-deployment⏎monitoring expectations arise under⏎related device quality system and⏎postmarket reporting frameworks (21⏎CFR Parts 803, 820)⏎9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0209♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from basys.ai, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔basys.ai♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment on HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted by: Basys.ai (Company)⏎Basys.ai appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department of Health and Human Services' Request for Information⏎regarding accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical care.⏎Basys.ai is an AI-powered healthcare technology company focused on automating prior authorization, utilization management,⏎and medical review workflows for health plans and government contractors. Through our work with health plans, CMS-aligned⏎programs, and healthcare administrators, we have observed firsthand the operational barriers, regulatory friction, and workflow⏎inefficiencies that slow responsible AI adoption in clinical environments.⏎Our responses below are grounded in real-world implementation experience, including reducing administrative burden in⏎CMS-related review programs and participation in federal innovation initiatives. We have also included some of our publications⏎and announcements in the references at the end of this document, including a reference for the Healthcare Moonshot⏎publication that principally guides our response to the RFI.⏎1. Biggest Barriers to AI Innovation and Adoption in Clinical Care⏎a. Workflow Fragmentation and Legacy Infrastructure⏎Clinical decision-making remains embedded in siloed EHR systems, PDF documents, manual review queues, and⏎disconnected policy repositories. AI cannot scale without structured, interoperable inputs.⏎b. Regulatory Ambiguity for Non-Device AI⏎For administrative-clinical AI (e.g., medical necessity review, coverage validation), governance expectations are unclear.⏎Organizations are uncertain about liability, documentation, and audit standards.⏎c. Misaligned Incentives in Fee-for-Service Models⏎AI that reduces unnecessary utilization or administrative waste may not align with legacy reimbursement incentives.⏎d. Trust and Transparency Gaps⏎Health plans and providers require explainability, audit trails, and clinician override pathways. AI tools lacking structured⏎transparency slow adoption.⏎Upshot: In our experience, we have seen the biggest barriers to the adoption of AI are overconfidence and a lack of⏎transparency in AI implementation. We have addressed this challenge here and also discussed a resolution framework.⏎2. Regulatory and Payment Policy Changes HHS Should Prioritize From Our Wider Payer⏎Learning/Perspective⏎Regulatory⏎a. Establish Governance Framework for Non-Device Clinical AI⏎Clarify expectations for AI used in clinical-adjacent decision support (e.g., prior authorization, utilization review) distinct from⏎FDA-regulated devices.⏎b. Standardized Documentation and Audit Requirements⏎Provide guidance under HIPAA (45 CFR Parts 160 & 164) and related CMS program integrity frameworks to define acceptable⏎AI audit logs, traceability standards, and human-in-the-loop requirements.⏎c. AI Transparency Safe Harbor⏎Create regulatory safe harbors for AI systems that meet defined transparency, documentation, and clinician oversight⏎thresholds.⏎Payment Policy⏎a. Incentivize Administrative Efficiency in Value-Based Models⏎Align CMS payment programs so that AI-driven reductions in administrative burden and inappropriate utilization are financially⏎rewarded.⏎⏎b. Recognize AI-Enabled Care Pathway Optimization⏎Allow AI-supported clinical triage and alternative care pathway recommendations to be integrated into reimbursement⏎modernization efforts.⏎3. Novel Legal and Implementation Issues (Non-Medical Devices)⏎a. Liability Allocation⏎Clear delineation is needed between AI tool vendors, health plans, and clinical users when AI informs but does not⏎autonomously decide.⏎b. Auditability and Appeals Governance⏎Administrative AI must produce defensible rationales for coverage decisions, especially in Medicare and Medicaid programs.⏎c. Data Provenance and Model Drift⏎Standards are needed for documenting training data categories, monitoring model drift, and ensuring policy alignment.⏎Recommended HHS Role:⏎HHS should publish model governance guidance for non-device clinical AI, including recommended oversight committees,⏎documentation standards, and periodic validation expectations.⏎Upshot:⏎1. We have also covered some of the details and our plan in our Moonshot and HBR articles.⏎2. Impact in Prior Authorization/Utilization Management and Payment Integrity Optimization As a Reference Point:⏎● 73% reduction in administrative expenses related to prior authorization⏎● 85% fewer manual prior authorization decisions⏎● 95% reduction in decision time for automated determinations⏎● Over 98% accuracy in automated cases⏎4. Promising AI Evaluation Methods (Non-Medical Devices)⏎Based on our experience in AI-powered medical review:⏎Pre-Deployment⏎● Retrospective validation against adjudicated cases⏎● Policy-to-chart traceability testing⏎● Bias analysis across demographic cohorts⏎Post-Deployment⏎● Continuous accuracy benchmarking⏎● Human override tracking⏎● Appeals outcome monitoring⏎● Model drift detection⏎HHS should support evaluation pilots through:⏎● Cooperative agreements⏎● CMS innovation demonstrations⏎● Prize competitions focused on burden reduction and explainability⏎Basys.ai has participated in federal innovation ecosystems, including CMS-related demonstration initiatives (e.g., program⏎integrity innovation challenges), which provide strong testing environments for scalable AI governance models.⏎Upshot: Details outlined here.⏎5. Supporting Private Sector Accreditation and Certification⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by:⏎⏎● Encouraging industry-driven AI accreditation frameworks aligned with CMS program integrity standards⏎● Supporting independent third-party validation programs⏎● Developing standardized reporting templates for AI audit logs and documentation⏎Clear accreditation pathways would reduce procurement uncertainty for health systems and payers.⏎6. Where AI Has Met Expectations and Where It Has Fallen Short⏎Met or Exceeded Expectations⏎● Administrative burden reduction in utilization management⏎● Faster turnaround times in structured medical review⏎● Improved consistency in policy application⏎In CMS-aligned review environments, AI-supported workflows have demonstrated measurable reductions in manual review time⏎while maintaining clinical oversight.⏎Fallen Short⏎● Overreliance on unstructured LLM outputs without guardrails⏎● Insufficient integration into legacy workflows⏎● Lack of interoperability with policy databases and EHR systems⏎High-Potential AI Areas⏎● Policy-to-chart matching⏎● Longitudinal care gap detection⏎● Early risk stratification in chronic disease management⏎● Automated fraud, waste, and abuse pattern identification⏎Upshot and Learnings: While there are gaps with commercial insurers, there are models of AI adoption that we can⏎learn from - basys.ai can help fill that gap.⏎7. Key Decision Makers and Administrative Hurdles⏎Primary Influencers⏎● Chief Medical Officers⏎● Chief Information Officers⏎● Utilization Management Leadership⏎● Compliance and Risk Officers⏎Administrative Hurdles⏎● Lengthy procurement cycles⏎● Data integration complexity⏎● Unclear compliance standards⏎● Limited interoperability across payer-provider ecosystems⏎From our work with health plans, operational bottlenecks often arise not from model performance but from integration⏎challenges and documentation requirements.⏎8. Interoperability Priorities⏎Enhanced interoperability would accelerate AI adoption in:⏎● FHIR-based clinical data exchange⏎● Structured policy ingestion standards⏎● Standardized medical necessity criteria encoding⏎● X12 integration for administrative workflows⏎⏎Benchmarking tools should include:⏎● Standardized clinical review datasets⏎● CMS-aligned performance benchmarks⏎● Shared de-identified validation corpora⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎Desired Improvements⏎● Faster coverage determinations⏎● Reduced paperwork⏎● Transparent explanations for decisions⏎● Shorter appeals timelines⏎Common Concerns⏎● Fear of automated denials⏎● Bias in AI systems⏎● Lack of human oversight⏎AI deployment must emphasize transparency, clinician involvement, and accessible explanations to build trust.⏎At a high level, we deeply care about solving the administrative gaps, and it is personal to the founding of the⏎company as shared by Amber, our CEO, in his TED talk, we are also prepared with the learnings and the⏎implementation guidelines shared across our published learnings in Harvard Business Review, STAT News, and⏎Forbes articles.⏎10. AI Research Priorities for HHS⏎Priority Areas⏎● AI for administrative burden reduction⏎● Bias detection and mitigation frameworks⏎● Explainability methods tailored to clinical-administrative decisions⏎● Longitudinal population health modeling⏎a. Published Findings⏎Emerging literature suggests AI improves documentation efficiency and triage accuracy when embedded within structured⏎governance frameworks. Administrative AI is less studied and represents an important research opportunity.⏎b. Costs, Benefits, and Transfers⏎Literature often focuses on clinical accuracy but underestimates:⏎● Administrative cost savings⏎● Reduced clinician burnout⏎● Improved patient throughput⏎● System-wide cost avoidance⏎HHS can catalyze research measuring system-level efficiency gains, not just predictive performance.⏎Upshot: We have delineated our learnings and a framework here.⏎Final Note⏎Basys.ai maintains distribution partnerships connected to both provider (~90% adoption at full activation) and payer ecosystems⏎(97% adoption at full adoption), which we can promptly galvanize. These relationships can support pilot programs,⏎interoperability testing, and scalable validation efforts if HHS pursues demonstration or cooperative models to accelerate AI⏎adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎We appreciate HHS’s forward-leaning and industry-supportive approach and welcome continued engagement to responsibly⏎accelerate AI deployment in ways that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower costs, and improve patient outcomes.⏎References:⏎1. Amber Nigam’s (our CEO’s) Ted Talk on Aligning Incentives Among Key Stakeholders of Healthcare:⏎https://www.ted.com/talks/amber_nigam_aligning_incentives_among_key_stakeholders_of_healthcare⏎2. Amber shares his perspective in Harvard Business Review on using the latest AI technology for reducing administrative⏎burden in healthcare: https://hbr.org/2025/07/should-your-business-use-a-generalist-or-specialized-ai-model⏎3. Arpan Saxena, our CIO,⏎https://www.forbes.com/councils/forbestechcouncil/2026/02/04/cms-wrote-the-rules-now-can-ai-actually-follow-them/⏎4. Basys and Smart Data Solution Partnership Announcement:⏎https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/sponsored/helping-payers-transform-prior-auth-agentic-ai and⏎https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=4H0Cujqurk0⏎5. Harvard Spotlights basys and Amber Nigam, CEO and a Harvard alum:⏎https://hsph.harvard.edu/studio/news/alumni-insights-careers-in-entrepreneurship-and-innovation/⏎6. Basys and MedeAnalytic Partnership Announcement:⏎https://finance.yahoo.com/news/medeanalytics-basys-ai-announce-strategic-231500450.html⏎7. Basys and RELI Group Partnership Announcement:⏎https://washingtonexec.com/2025/11/reli-group-partners-with-basys-ai-to-advance-ai-driven-healthcare-integrity/⏎8. Dr. Oz’s Announcement of basys.ai being chosen as a Finalist for CMS’ Chili Cook-Off Challenge:⏎https://www.linkedin.com/feed/update/urn:li:activity:7399121695711858688/ and⏎https://www.cms.gov/priorities/crushing-fraud-waste-abuse/overview/crushing-fraud-chili-cook-competition⏎9. Amber made the list of top Healthcare CEO’s of 2025:⏎https://thehealthcaretechnologyreport.com/the-top-healthcare-technology-ceos-of-2025/⏎10. Healthcare Moonshot Concept: https://www.beckerspayer.com/payer/scan-group-its-time-for-a-healthtech-moonshot/⏎11. STAT News Article on Basys’ Founding Story:⏎https://www.statnews.com/2024/11/13/generative-ai-medicine-health-care-ai-racism/♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0210♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Waymark, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Waymark, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔2021 Fillmore Street Ste#1059⏎San Francisco, CA 94115-2708⏎P: (415) 212-8993⏎F: (866) 498-1987 (HIPAA-secure)⏎Department of Health and Human Services, Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of⏎the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology:⏎Waymark, Inc. (“Waymark” or “we” or “our”) is a healthcare technology company deploying AI-driven⏎population health management for Medicaid populations through managed care organization⏎partnerships. We bring practical experience implementing reinforcement learning-based care coordination,⏎predictive risk stratification, and automated intervention systems serving over 175,000 high-risk members⏎across multiple states. Our AI-driven interventions have achieved a 20.4% reduction in avoidable⏎emergency department visits, a 48.3% reduction in 30-day all-cause hospitalizations, and a $3.73:1 return⏎on investment in operational Medicaid managed care deployments.⏎Waymark appreciates the opportunity to respond to HHS's Request for Information on accelerating AI⏎adoption in clinical care.⏎The recommendations in this comment align with the Administration's stated priorities for AI policy and⏎healthcare reform:⏎Reducing Regulatory Burden: Our recommendations for safe harbors, clear guidance, and harmonized⏎federal standards would reduce compliance uncertainty and the duplicative state-by-state requirements⏎that currently impede multi-state AI deployment. Regulatory clarity enables faster deployment of⏎beneficial AI while maintaining appropriate safeguards.⏎Promoting American AI Leadership: The United States leads the world in healthcare AI innovation,⏎but regulatory fragmentation, payment misalignment, and an emerging patchwork of state AI laws risk⏎ceding this advantage to international competitors. Policy reforms enabling responsible AI deployment⏎would strengthen American leadership in this strategically important sector.⏎Lowering Healthcare Costs: AI-driven population health management directly addresses healthcare⏎cost growth by preventing avoidable acute care utilization, improving chronic disease management, and⏎enabling more efficient resource allocation. Payment reforms that reward these outcomes would⏎accelerate cost reduction.⏎Improving Healthcare Quality: AI enables personalized, proactive care that improves health outcomes⏎for patients. Quality measure reforms and research investments would generate evidence demonstrating⏎AI's contribution to better care.⏎Our response focuses on the highest-impact barriers to AI adoption and the most actionable policy⏎solutions. We prioritize three areas where HHS action would most directly accelerate responsible AI⏎deployment:⏎1. Transitioning Medicaid payment models toward value-based arrangements that reward⏎AI-driven outcome improvements. Current payment structures represent the single greatest⏎barrier to AI adoption. CMS guidance under 42 CFR Part 438 on performance-based payments⏎for AI-driven population health management, together with model contract language for states to⏎1⏎⏎adopt, would create the financial alignment necessary for managed care organizations to invest in⏎AI systems that reduce total cost of care.⏎2. Establishing regulatory safe harbors for population health AI systems, including fraud⏎and abuse safe harbors for AI-driven member engagement. Organizations deploying AI for⏎care coordination face unresolved questions under the Anti-Kickback Statute and the Beneficiary⏎Inducement Civil Monetary Penalty law. OIG guidance confirming the application of the care⏎coordination safe harbor to AI-driven engagement optimization, and clarifying permissible⏎AI-driven personalization of member communications, would remove the compliance uncertainty⏎that slows adoption.⏎3. Harmonizing state AI requirements for healthcare AI operating under federal programs.⏎An emerging patchwork of state AI laws, including Colorado's AI Act effective June 30, 2026,⏎and pending legislation in California, Texas, Illinois, and other states, creates duplicative⏎compliance obligations that disproportionately burden smaller innovators and delay beneficial AI⏎deployment across state lines. Federal leadership establishing baseline standards that satisfy⏎state-level requirements for AI operating within Medicaid managed care would provide the⏎regulatory certainty needed for multi-state scaling.⏎Our comments and recommendations on specific areas of the RFI are set forth below.⏎I.Introduction: Waymark Background⏎Waymark is a healthcare technology company focused on value-based care delivery for Medicaid⏎populations. We partner with managed care organizations to deploy AI-driven population health⏎management systems that combine predictive analytics with community-based care delivery. Our platform⏎serves rising risk, high-cost Medicaid members across Washington, Ohio, and Virginia, targeting⏎reductions in avoidable acute care utilization through proactive interventions.⏎Waymark’s AI systems are designed to augment–not replace–human clinical judgment and care delivery.⏎Our platform supports community health workers, care coordinators, therapists and pharmacists by:⏎Prioritizing Caseloads: AI identifies which members are at highest risk and most likely to⏎●⏎benefit from intervention, enabling care teams to focus limited time on highest-value activities⏎Surfacing Relevant Information: AI synthesizes claims, clinical and social determinants data to⏎●⏎provide care team members with actionable insights without requiring manual chart review.⏎Optimizing Outreach Timing: AI predicts optimal times and channels for member contact,⏎●⏎improving engagement rates and reducing wasted outreach attempts⏎Automating Routine Tasks: AI handles routine member communications, appointment⏎●⏎reminders, and care gap notifications, freeing care team members for complex relationship based⏎care.⏎Enabling Timely Escalations: AI monitors member data for indicators of clinical⏎●⏎deterioration, behavioral health crisis, or rising acute care risk, enabling care teams to escalate⏎interventions before avoidable emergency department visits or hospitalizations occur.⏎Waymark's AI platform is designed to be consistent with the framework established in FDA's January⏎2026 final guidance on Clinical Decision Support software, which implements the Cures Act exclusion for⏎Non-Device CDS under Section 520(o)(1)(E) of the FD&C Act. Several of Waymark's functions are⏎operational, not clinical, and do not meet the device definition. Functions that do not surface clinical⏎recommendations to healthcare professionals are designed to meet all four Non-Device CDS criteria.⏎This "augment, not replace" design is central to Waymark's model.⏎This augmentation model addresses healthcare workforce shortages by enabling existing staff to serve⏎more members effectively. Community health workers supported by AI can manage larger patient panels⏎while maintaining personalized, relationship-based care. Rather than displacing healthcare workers, AI⏎enables workforce multiplication that expands access to care coordination services.⏎2⏎⏎II.Responses to Request for Public Comment⏎A.Regulation⏎We recommend that CMS issue guidance establishing voluntary best practices for validation and⏎monitoring of AI systems used in Medicaid Managed Care that provide pre-deployment validation⏎standards, ongoing monitoring requirements, transparency, and document human oversight⏎requirements. We recommend that HHS establish clear standards for algorithmic fairness in clinical AI⏎systems, including requirements for disparate impact testing, ongoing performance monitoring across⏎demographic subgroups, and transparent reporting of model limitations.⏎While 42 CFR Part 438 permits MCOs to use AI for quality improvement and care coordination, there⏎are no specific standards for validating AI system accuracy, reliability, or equity when used in Medicaid⏎populations. AI systems used for risk stratification, care coordination, or utilization management in Medicaid⏎should be validated using Medicaid specific data and assess performance metrics, including sensitivity and⏎specificity. Systems should demonstrate that there are no statistically significant differences in⏎sensitivity/specificity across demographic groups or have a documented plan to address identified disparities⏎through algorithm refinement or targeted interventions.⏎MCOs using AI systems should implement continuous monitoring including performance tracking, algorithm⏎drift detection, adverse event monitoring, and monitoring AI system performance across demographic⏎subgroups.⏎In addition, MCOs should maintain and make available to the State documentation about their algorithm⏎development and validation, vendor information, and reports about the performance of the algorithm.⏎AI systems used in Medicaid managed care should also include a clinical review process, quality oversight on⏎AI system performance, the right for enrollees to decline AI interventions without penalty, and request⏎information about how they were identified as rising risk.⏎These standards should be proportionate to the risk level of the AI application and avoid overly⏎prescriptive technical requirements that would stifle innovation. Establishing industry-wide standards⏎would create appropriate competitive safeguards and ensure baseline quality across all AI systems⏎affecting patient care.⏎We recommend that HHS issue joint HHS guidance (OCR, CMS, SAMHSA, ONC) clarifying data⏎sharing requirements for AI systems in Medicaid.⏎Medicaid MCOs, providers, and state agencies are uncertain about when and how they can share Medicaid⏎enrollee data with AI vendors for:⏎Algorithm training and development⏎●⏎Risk stratification and prediction⏎●⏎Care coordination and intervention deployment⏎●⏎Quality improvement and program evaluation⏎●⏎HHS should clarify that State Medicaid agencies may share Medicaid claims, encounter, and enrollment⏎data with AI vendors for the following purposes:⏎(1)Program Integrity and Quality Improvement⏎Developing AI systems to detect fraud, waste, and abuse⏎●⏎Creating predictive models for quality improvement initiatives⏎●⏎Evaluating effectiveness of Medicaid programs and interventions⏎●⏎(2)Managed Care Oversight⏎Validating MCO-reported data and performance metrics⏎●⏎3⏎⏎Assessing MCO compliance with contract requirements⏎●⏎Supporting state-led delivery system reform initiatives⏎●⏎The guidance can include requirements for data sharing by states, which may include security⏎requirements, restrictions on further disclosure without state approval, and compliance monitoring⏎provisions.⏎We recommend that HHS update its 2012 de-identification guidance to address AI-driven⏎re-identification risks and clarify permissible uses of de-identified and limited data sets for population⏎health AI.⏎The HHS 2012 "Guidance Regarding Methods for De-identification of Protected Health Information" was⏎issued before widespread deployment of artificial intelligence and machine learning technologies. Since then,⏎AI techniques have demonstrated the ability to:⏎Re-identify individuals from datasets previously considered de-identified⏎●⏎Infer sensitive attributes from non-sensitive data patterns⏎●⏎Link multiple de-identified datasets to enable identification⏎●⏎Extract identifying information from unstructured text and images⏎●⏎This creates uncertainty for covered entities and business associates about:⏎Whether data de-identified using older methods remains de-identified under current technology⏎●⏎How to conduct Expert Determination analyses that account for AI re-identification risks⏎●⏎When AI training and validation constitute permitted uses of de-identified or limited data sets⏎●⏎What safeguards are necessary when sharing de-identified data with AI vendors⏎●⏎The guidance on the expert determination method (45 CFR 164.514(b)(1)) can be updated to address⏎these areas of uncertainty.⏎Additionally, HHS should clarify permissible uses of limited data sets under 45 CFR 164.514(e) when⏎used for AI purposes. For example, HHS can confirm that certain AI related activities, such as training⏎AI systems for care coordination and case management, developing predictive models for quality⏎improvement initiatives, and validating AI algorithm performance for population health management,⏎constitute "healthcare operations” under 45 CFR 164.501 and may use limited data sets pursuant to data⏎use agreements.⏎Any guidance document should take a risk based approach, acknowledging that safeguards should be⏎proportionate to the risk of re-identification.⏎HHS took an important step in February 2024 when SAMHSA and OCR issued a final rule aligning 42⏎CFR Part 2 with HIPAA. The final rule allows Part 2 programs to obtain a single patient consent for all⏎future uses and disclosures for treatment, payment, and health care operations; permits HIPAA covered⏎entities and business associates that receive Part 2 records under this consent to redisclose those records⏎in accordance with HIPAA; and applies the HIPAA de-identification standard under 45 CFR 164.514(b)⏎to Part 2 records. With the compliance date of February 16, 2026 now upon us, this is an appropriate⏎moment to address the remaining gaps that the final rule did not resolve, particularly with respect to⏎AI-driven care coordination for substance use disorder populations. The recommendations below build⏎on the foundation the 2024 final rule established.⏎We recommend that HHS issue guidance under 42 CFR Part 2 allowing AI-assisted substance use⏎disorder treatment coordination.⏎The behavioral health crisis demands AI solutions that can identify individuals at risk, predict crisis⏎events, and optimize intervention timing. Waymark's care teams include therapists delivering behavioral⏎health interventions, and our AI systems support behavioral health care coordination. However,⏎behavioral health AI faces unique barriers:⏎4⏎⏎Data Fragmentation: Behavioral health data is often maintained in separate systems from general⏎medical records, with limited integration into EHRs and claims databases. Mental health diagnoses may be⏎under-coded due to stigma concerns, and substance use disorder data faces additional restrictions under⏎42 CFR Part 2.⏎Current 42 CFR Part 2 restrictions create barriers to integrating SUD patients into population health⏎management programs, even when AI systems would improve care coordination. Given the breadth of⏎Medicaid populations served by managed care organizations, clarifying that population-level risk⏎stratification and care coordination prioritization can include SUD patients without requiring full Part 2⏎consent procedures would significantly improve care quality.⏎Crisis Prediction Challenges: AI systems predicting behavioral health crises (suicide risk, psychiatric⏎hospitalization, substance use relapse) require access to sensitive data including prior mental health⏎history, social determinants, and real-time indicators. Current data infrastructure and privacy frameworks⏎limit development of effective crisis prediction models.⏎Integration with Community Resources: Effective behavioral health AI must connect predictions to⏎actionable interventions, including crisis services, peer support, and community mental health resources.⏎Interoperability between healthcare AI systems and community mental health infrastructure is limited.⏎To address the issues above, we recommend that HHS:⏎Expand the 42 CFR Part 2 recommendations to address AI-assisted behavioral health care⏎●⏎coordination more broadly, including crisis prediction and intervention optimization⏎Fund development of behavioral health-specific AI benchmarking datasets that include⏎●⏎longitudinal mental health outcomes, crisis events, and treatment response data with appropriate⏎de-identification⏎Support integration standards connecting healthcare AI systems with 988 Suicide and Crisis⏎●⏎Lifeline, community mental health centers, and peer support networks⏎Establish guidance on permissible AI applications for behavioral health risk prediction, including⏎●⏎appropriate use of social media, wearable, and other non-traditional data sources⏎We recommend that HHS issue guidance clarifying: 1) the application of the care coordination safe⏎harbor to AI-driven member engagement and outreach optimization within value-based arrangements,⏎2)permissible AI-driven personalization of member communications and incentives under the⏎beneficiary inducement CMP, particularly for preventative care and care coordination activities, and 3)⏎documentation and transparency requirements for AI systems operating within fraud and abuse safe⏎harbors.⏎AI systems that personalize member outreach and engagement raise novel questions under existing fraud⏎and abuse frameworks. Automated engagement systems, including SMS and voice calling for member⏎outreach, may operate within care coordination arrangements. However, regulatory uncertainty exists⏎regarding:⏎Anti-Kickback Statute (42 U.S.C. § 1320a-7b(b)): When AI systems optimize member⏎●⏎engagement strategies—including timing, channel, and messaging—questions arise about⏎whether personalized outreach constitutes remuneration that could influence referrals or service⏎utilization. While care coordination arrangements may qualify for the care coordination safe⏎harbor (42 CFR § 1001.952(ee)), the application of this safe harbor to AI-driven engagement⏎optimization has not been addressed in OIG guidance.⏎Beneficiary Inducement Civil Monetary Penalty (42 U.S.C. § 1320a-7a(a)(5)): AI systems that⏎●⏎personalize incentives, nudges, or engagement strategies for Medicaid beneficiaries must navigate⏎the prohibition on offering remuneration to influence beneficiary selection of providers or⏎services. The preventive care exception (42 CFR § 1003.110) may apply to certain AI-driven⏎health promotion activities, but its scope for population health AI applications remains unclear.⏎Issuing guidance would reduce compliance uncertainty for organizations deploying AI to improve⏎member engagement and care coordination outcomes.⏎5⏎⏎We recommend that HHS: 1) coordinate with state regulators through the National Association of⏎Insurance Commissioners (NAIC) and National Association of Medicaid Directors (NAMD) to⏎develop harmonized AI governance standards for healthcare applications, 2) issue guidance establishing⏎that compliance with federal AI transparency, validation, and monitoring standards satisfies state⏎requirements for healthcare AI operating under federal programs, 3) consider whether ERISA-style⏎preemption of inconsistent state AI requirements is appropriate for AI systems operating within⏎federally-regulated healthcare programs, including Medicare Advantage and Medicaid managed care,⏎and 4) establish a federal floor for healthcare AI governance that provides regulatory certainty while⏎preserving state authority to address state-specific concerns.⏎Beyond variations in telemedicine and digital health regulations, an emerging patchwork of state AI laws⏎creates significant compliance complexity for multi-state healthcare AI deployments. Colorado's AI Act⏎(SB 24-205), effective June 30, 2026, imposes disclosure, impact assessment, and governance⏎requirements on "high-risk" AI systems, which may include healthcare risk stratification and care⏎coordination applications. Similar legislation is pending or under consideration in California, Texas,⏎Illinois, and other states.⏎For organizations like Waymark operating across multiple state Medicaid programs, inconsistent state AI⏎requirements create:⏎Duplicative compliance obligations requiring state-specific impact assessments, disclosures, and⏎●⏎governance documentation⏎Conflicting technical requirements that may be impossible to satisfy simultaneously across⏎●⏎jurisdictions⏎Uncertainty about which state's law applies when AI systems process data from members in⏎●⏎multiple states⏎Increased costs that disproportionately burden smaller innovators and may delay beneficial AI⏎●⏎deployment⏎Federal leadership on healthcare AI governance would reduce compliance fragmentation while ensuring⏎consistent patient protections across state lines.⏎B.Reimbursement⏎For Medicaid specifically, we recommend that CMS encourage states to adopt managed care contract⏎structures under 42 CFR Part 438 that include explicit performance-based payments for utilization⏎management and health outcome improvements achieved through AI deployment.⏎Current payment models represent the single greatest barrier to AI adoption in clinical care.⏎Fee-for-service reimbursement creates perverse incentives that discourage investment in AI systems that⏎reduce unnecessary utilization, even when such reductions improve patient outcomes and lower total⏎costs. Value-based payment models theoretically align incentives but face implementation challenges that⏎limit AI adoption.⏎Payment Model Barriers:⏎• Fee-for-service billing codes do not accommodate AI-driven care coordination, preventive⏎interventions, or population health management activities⏎• Medicaid managed care contracts often lack explicit incentive structures for AI-driven quality⏎improvement and utilization management⏎• Limited budget authority for managed care organizations to invest in AI infrastructure when⏎savings accrue to other entities in fragmented payment systems⏎CMS should develop and disseminate model contract language for Medicaid managed care agreements⏎that states can adopt. Sample contract provisions should include performance based payments for⏎demonstrable reductions in avoidable acute care utilization achieved through deployment of⏎evidence-based AI systems for population health management. The health plan should be required to⏎6⏎⏎provide annual documentation of AI system validation, fairness testing, and performance monitoring to⏎demonstrate evidence-based deployment.⏎Enhanced federal matching for states that adopt such value-based contract structures could accelerate this⏎transition. CMS should consider increasing FMAP percentages for states implementing⏎performance-based Medicaid managed care contracts that explicitly incentivize AI adoption for care⏎quality improvement.⏎We recommend that CMS enhance risk adjustment methodologies in capitated payment arrangements⏎to account for social determinants of health and rising risk trajectories, not just current diagnostic codes.⏎Existing high cost claimant-based risk adjustment creates gaming incentives around diagnosis capture⏎rather than true health improvement. Risk adjustment that incorporates SDOH factors, utilization⏎trajectories, and functional status would better align payment with actual population health needs and⏎create stronger incentives for AI investments targeting rising-risk members before they develop costly⏎complications.⏎We recommend that CMMI establish innovation waivers that allow alternative payment models for⏎AI-driven care delivery outside traditional billing structures as well as establish a demonstration model⏎testing AI-driven population health management.⏎The Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation should prioritize models that test population-based⏎payments for AI-driven care coordination, with shared savings mechanisms that reward total cost of care⏎reductions while maintaining or improving quality metrics. CMMI demonstration models would generate⏎evidence for broader payment reform while creating pathways for AI-driven care delivery.⏎This model structure would create aligned incentives for AI investment while generating rigorous⏎evidence on AI effectiveness in real-world care delivery. The demonstration model testing AI driven⏎population health management should include the following parameters:⏎Validation on diverse patient populations⏎●⏎Ongoing performance monitoring with drift detection⏎●⏎Disparate impact testing across demographic subgroups⏎●⏎Transparency documentation meeting specified standards⏎●⏎C.Research & Development⏎While private sector innovation has produced impressive AI capabilities, specific research infrastructure⏎investments would accelerate translation to real-world clinical settings.⏎We recommend that AHRQ invest in federated learning infrastructure for healthcare AI research,⏎potentially through cooperative agreements with health systems and payers.⏎This infrastructure would enable multi-site model training and validation without requiring data sharing,⏎addressing privacy concerns while improving model generalizability. Technical investments should include⏎privacy-preserving computation methods, differential privacy implementations, and secure multi-party⏎computation protocols. This approach would minimize breach risk while improving model performance⏎across heterogeneous Medicaid populations. Current limitations in accessing multi-site data for model⏎training restrict the ability to develop models that generalize across different states, health plan structures,⏎and demographic compositions.⏎To achieve this infrastructure, specific technical capabilities needed include: 1) secure aggregation⏎protocols that enable gradient sharing without exposing individual patient data; 2) differential privacy⏎guarantees that formalize privacy protections while maintaining model accuracy; and 3) federated model⏎architectures that accommodate heterogeneous data schemas across different EHR systems and claims⏎databases. AHRQ should consider funding demonstration projects testing federated learning for⏎population health AI applications including risk prediction, care gap identification, and intervention⏎response modeling.⏎7⏎⏎We recommend that ONC lead development of standardized SDOH data elements for population⏎health AI.⏎Current pilot implementations of Gravity Project standards and Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise⏎(IHE) SDOH profiles need widespread adoption to enable consistent capture and exchange of housing⏎stability, food insecurity, transportation access, and social isolation data. SDOH standardization would⏎improve predictive accuracy and enable better intervention targeting.⏎We recommend that FDA and CMS should establish real world evidence pathways for AI validation.⏎Traditional randomized controlled trials (RCTs) are often impractical for evaluating population health AI⏎due to:⏎Cluster-level interventions: AI systems typically operate at the organization or population level,⏎●⏎requiring cluster randomization with associated sample size and cost implications⏎Rapid iteration: AI systems improve continuously through retraining, making point-in-time RCT⏎●⏎results quickly obsolete⏎Ethical constraints: Withholding potentially beneficial AI interventions from control populations⏎●⏎raises ethical concerns, particularly for high-risk members⏎External validity: RCT conditions may not reflect real-world implementation contexts, limiting⏎●⏎generalizability⏎FDA and CMS should establish real-world evidence (RWE) pathways for AI validation that:⏎Accept quasi-experimental designs (difference-in-differences, regression discontinuity,⏎●⏎instrumental variables) as valid evidence of AI effectiveness when RCTs are impractical⏎Establish standards for observational study quality, including pre-registration, sensitivity analyses,⏎●⏎and transparency requirements⏎Create expedited review pathways for AI systems with strong RWE demonstrating safety and⏎●⏎effectiveness⏎Recognize continuous learning AI systems that demonstrate ongoing performance monitoring⏎●⏎and improvement as meeting validation requirements without repeated point-in-time studies⏎Align FDA device evaluation, CMS coverage determination, and quality measure development⏎●⏎around common RWE standards⏎RWE pathways would accelerate evidence generation for AI systems while maintaining scientific rigor⏎appropriate to the risk level of each application.⏎III.Responses to Specific Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The biggest barriers to private sector AI innovation in healthcare and its adoption in clinical care are 1)⏎payment system misalignment, 2) regulatory uncertainty for non medical device AI applications, 3) data⏎access, and 4) validation and evidence requirements for model development.⏎Payment System Misalignment⏎Fee-for-service payment creates negative ROI for AI systems that reduce unnecessary utilization.⏎Organizations have no financial incentive to deploy AI that prevents hospitalizations or reduces testing⏎when revenue depends on volume. Even in value-based arrangements, insufficient performance incentives⏎and short time horizons limit investment in AI. Medicaid managed care contracts particularly lack explicit⏎provisions rewarding AI-driven care management, creating misalignment between AI investment costs⏎and potential savings.⏎8⏎⏎Regulatory Uncertainty⏎Lack of clear regulatory pathways for non-medical device AI applications creates risk aversion.⏎Organizations are uncertain whether their AI systems require FDA approval, what liability exposure they⏎face, and what privacy safeguards apply when using de-identified data. This uncertainty delays deployment⏎and increases compliance costs.⏎Specific areas of concern include what documentation standards apply for model validation and⏎monitoring and how to manage liability when AI recommendations are mediated through human clinical⏎judgment.⏎FDA's January 2026 final guidance on Clinical Decision Support software is a positive example of the⏎regulatory clarity that accelerates innovation. By establishing clear criteria for Non-Device CDS under⏎Section 520(o)(1)(E) of the FD&C Act and adopting a risk-proportionate enforcement discretion⏎framework for lower-risk device CDS functions, the guidance gives developers a workable framework for⏎determining whether their HCP-facing software requires premarket review. However, the CDS guidance⏎does not address AI systems used for population-level risk stratification, care coordination prioritization,⏎or member engagement optimization, functions that operate upstream of individual clinical decisions and⏎do not fit neatly within the CDS framework. This is the specific regulatory gap where additional HHS⏎guidance is most needed. We recommend that CMS and OIG adopt the same risk-proportionate⏎approach FDA has taken in the CDS guidance when establishing safe harbors and best practices for⏎population health AI operating within Medicaid managed care.⏎Data Access and Interoperability⏎Fragmented EHR systems, limited information exchange, and restricted access to social determinants data⏎prevent development of comprehensive AI models. Claims data provides utilization history but lacks⏎clinical detail. EHR data has clinical detail but limited standardization across systems. Social services data⏎is siloed entirely. AI requires integrated longitudinal data that current infrastructure does not support.⏎Multi-site model training faces particular challenges as data cannot be centralized due to privacy concerns.⏎Validation and Evidence Requirements⏎Limited access to diverse patient populations for model development and validation restricts⏎generalizability. Randomized controlled trials are expensive and slow, while observational evaluation⏎methodologies lack standardization. Payers and health systems demand evidence of effectiveness but are⏎unwilling to support evaluation costs. Specific challenges include: establishing appropriate control groups⏎for AI intervention evaluation; controlling for selection bias when AI deployment is not randomized; and⏎attributing outcome changes to AI versus other concurrent quality improvement initiatives.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical⏎care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Building on the single-consent framework for treatment, payment, and health care operations established⏎in the 2024 Part 2 final rule, HHS should clarify the use of AI-generated outputs in substance use⏎disorder treatment under 42 CFR Part 2. Specifically:⏎• HHS should confirm that substance use disorder patient records de-identified in accordance with⏎45 CFR 164.514(b) may be used for artificial intelligence model training, population health⏎analytics, and care coordination without patient consent pursuant to 42 CFR 2.54.⏎• HHS should clarify that AI-generated outputs derived solely from de-identified data do not⏎constitute Part 2 records unless they identify or provide a reasonable basis to identify specific⏎patients.⏎• HHS should clarify that coordinating SUD treatment with AI systems can occur under a single⏎patient consent. Specifically, HHS should clarify that artificial intelligence systems used for⏎substance use disorder treatment coordination may access patient identifying information when a⏎Part 2 program has obtained a single consent for treatment, payment, and healthcare operations⏎9⏎⏎pursuant to 42 CFR 2.31, and that such AI systems are subject to the same use and disclosure⏎restrictions as other recipients of Part 2 records.⏎• Finally, HHS should provide guidance on Business Associate Agreement requirements when AI⏎vendors receive substance use disorder patient records from Part 2 programs that are also⏎HIPAA covered entities, clarifying the interaction between Part 2 consent requirements and⏎HIPAA business associate obligations.⏎We recommend that CMS issue guidance under 42 CFR Part 438 clarifying how states can structure⏎Medicaid managed care capitation rates and performance-based payments to create financial alignment⏎for MCO investments in AI-driven population health management that reduce total cost of care.⏎Current 42 CFR Part 438 regulations permit states to include technology costs in capitation rates and⏎structure performance-based payments for cost reduction, but lack of specific guidance on AI, which⏎creates uncertainty for states and MCOs about:⏎How to project costs and savings from AI investments in actuarial rate certifications⏎●⏎Optimal payment structures to incentivize AI adoption while maintaining actuarial soundness⏎●⏎Performance metrics that capture AI-driven improvements in care coordination and prevention⏎●⏎Multi-year investment frameworks when AI implementation costs precede savings realization⏎●⏎HHS could clarify that actuarially sound capitation rates under 42 CFR 438.4 may include 1) technology⏎costs, 2) AI-driven intervention costs (e.g., CHW deployment based on AI risk stratification), 3) projected⏎savings (e.g., from avoidable ER utilization or hospitalizations).⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation challenges exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them.⏎When AI systems provide care coordination recommendations that clinical staff follow, liability allocation⏎between AI vendors, healthcare organizations, and individual clinicians is unclear. Standard professional⏎liability insurance and vendor indemnification clauses were not designed for AI-mediated clinical⏎judgment. Specific issues include: who bears liability when AI recommendations are technically accurate⏎but lead to adverse outcomes; how to allocate responsibility when clinicians override AI⏎recommendations that would have prevented adverse events; and what documentation standards apply to⏎demonstrate appropriate use of AI in clinical decision-making. HHS should convene stakeholders to⏎develop model liability frameworks that appropriately allocate risk while maintaining accountability.⏎Patients and clinicians reasonably expect to understand how AI systems make recommendations, yet⏎vendors have legitimate intellectual property concerns about disclosing proprietary algorithms. Current⏎transparency requirements under 21st Century Cures Act focus on interoperability rather than algorithmic⏎explainability. Specific tensions include: what level of algorithm disclosure is necessary for informed⏎consent; whether clinicians require access to model architecture and training data to appropriately utilize⏎AI recommendations; and how to balance transparency needs with protection of competitive advantages.⏎HHS should establish tiered transparency standards based on AI system risk level and use case, with⏎higher-risk applications requiring more detailed disclosure.⏎AI systems that learn continuously from new data raise questions about when they constitute a new⏎intervention requiring revalidation. Static models avoid this issue but sacrifice performance as patient⏎populations and care delivery patterns evolve. Specific challenges include: what magnitude of model⏎parameter changes triggers revalidation requirements; how to detect and manage performance⏎degradation from distribution shift; and what governance processes should oversee continuous learning⏎systems. HHS should establish frameworks for continuous learning AI that define acceptable drift⏎thresholds, monitoring requirements, and revalidation triggers based on clinical risk.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎10⏎⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce⏎costs?⏎Where AI Has Met or Exceeded Expectations⏎Risk stratification models accurately predicting near-term acute care utilization have proven highly⏎effective. For example, Waymark’s operational deployments have generated measurable outcomes beyond⏎model accuracy metrics. Across our Medicaid managed care partnerships in Washington and Virginia,⏎AI-driven interventions have achieved:⏎Emergency department utilization: 20.4% reduction in avoidable ED visits among members⏎●⏎receiving AI-driven outreach compared to matched controls⏎Hospitalization rates: 48.3% reduction in 30-day all-cause hospitalizations for high-risk⏎●⏎members engaged through AI-driven care coordination⏎Total cost of care: $3.73:1 ROI per-member-per-month savings in populations receiving⏎●⏎AI-driven interventions, representing $253 in reduction in total medical expenditure per⏎intervened patient per month⏎Member engagement: 41.2% contact rate for AI-driven outreach compared to 10-30% for⏎●⏎traditional care management approaches⏎Quality measure performance: 11.8 percentage point improvement in HEDIS gap closure⏎●⏎rates using AI-driven intervention prioritization1⏎These outcomes demonstrate that population health AI can deliver measurable value when deployed⏎within value-based payment arrangements that align incentives for utilization reduction and outcome⏎improvement. We are prepared to share detailed methodology and results with HHS upon request.⏎Automated engagement systems using SMS and voice calling have exceeded expectations for member⏎reach and engagement rates. AI-driven messaging personalization improves response rates by 40-60%⏎compared to generic outreach,2 enabling scalable contact with high-risk members who historically had low⏎engagement with traditional care management. This automation allows care coordinators to focus time on⏎high-value in-person interventions rather than repetitive outreach attempts.⏎HEDIS gap closure prioritization using machine learning to identify members most likely to respond to⏎specific interventions has improved quality measure performance cost-effectively. Rather than universal⏎outreach, AI-driven interventions achieve similar completion rates with 30-40% fewer attempts,⏎generating substantial savings in care management labor costs while improving quality measure⏎performance.3⏎Where AI Has Fallen Short⏎Population health has not yet delivered consistently in two areas.⏎Complex reinforcement learning systems for sequential care coordination decisions have shown⏎●⏎promise in research settings but face implementation challenges. Model interpretability, clinical⏎trust, and liability concerns limit adoption of black-box sequential decision support. Clinicians⏎want transparent rationales for AI recommendations, which current reinforcement learning⏎approaches struggle to provide. Specific challenges include explaining why particular outreach⏎timing or intervention sequences are recommended, and communicating uncertainty bounds⏎around predicted outcomes.⏎Health related social needs prediction suffer from limited data availability and quality. While⏎●⏎claims and EHR data support clinical outcome prediction, predicting social service needs requires⏎data that is fragmented, unstandardized, and often unavailable. AI alone cannot compensate for⏎fundamental data infrastructure gaps. Current SDOH screening tools capture point-in-time⏎1 Baum A, Batniji R, Ratcliffe H, DeGosztonyi M, Basu S. Supporting Rising-Risk Medicaid Patients Through Early Intervention. NEJM Catalyst⏎Innovations in Care Delivery. 2024;5(11). DOI: 10.1056/CAT.24.0060.⏎2 Chacko J, Mazza K, Stathakos K, Kim D, Carlo L. Evaluating Technology-Driven Strategies for Enhancing Patient Outreach for Preventive⏎Care: A Systematic Review. Cureus. 2025 Feb 22;17(2):e79467. doi: 10.7759/cureus.79467. PMID: 40130097; PMCID: PMC11932718.⏎3 Results from internal Waymark outreach campaigns.⏎11⏎⏎assessments but lack longitudinal tracking of housing, food security, and transportation stability⏎over time.⏎Novel AI Tools with Greatest Potential⏎• Causal AI systems that predict individual treatment effects rather than just population-level⏎predictions, enabling truly personalized intervention selection based on expected benefit for each⏎patient⏎• Federated learning systems enabling multi-site model training without data sharing, improving⏎generalizability while maintaining privacy⏎• Multi-modal AI integrating claims, EHR, wearable, and social service data for comprehensive risk⏎assessment that captures clinical, behavioral, and social determinants simultaneously⏎• Natural language processing extracting structured data from unstructured clinical notes,⏎particularly for mental health symptoms, functional status, and patient-reported social needs⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the⏎primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Most Influential Roles for AI Adoption:⏎• Chief Medical Officers and Chief Quality Officers control clinical program priorities and have⏎authority to mandate AI tool adoption for quality improvement initiatives. Their clinical⏎credibility is essential for building physician trust in AI recommendations.⏎• Payer Medical Directors for managed care organizations influence coverage and reimbursement⏎decisions affecting AI deployment at provider organizations. They control quality measure⏎selection, care management program requirements, and performance-based payment structures⏎that drive AI adoption incentives.⏎• Chief Financial Officers determine ROI requirements and budget availability, requiring clear⏎evidence that AI investments will generate financial returns within acceptable time horizons,⏎typically 2-3 years for managed care organizations.⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles:⏎• Procurement processes requiring lengthy vendor evaluation, security reviews, and contract⏎negotiations that delay deployment by 12-18 months, particularly for health systems and state⏎Medicaid agencies with extensive approval requirements⏎• Information security requirements including comprehensive risk assessments, penetration testing,⏎and HIPAA compliance documentation that extend vendor onboarding, with particular⏎challenges around cloud-based AI systems and data residency requirements⏎• Clinical governance processes requiring physician champion identification, committee approvals,⏎and policy development before deployment, with resistance from clinicians concerned about AI⏎replacing human judgment or increasing documentation burden⏎• Training and change management needs for clinical and administrative staff who must learn new⏎workflows and technologies, with particular challenges when AI recommendations conflict with⏎established care protocols or clinical intuition⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would significantly accelerate AI development in care coordination activities⏎and medication adherence.⏎Non-clinical interventions by community health workers, care managers, and social service providers lack⏎standardized representation in health IT systems. FHIR CarePlan and ServiceRequest resources need⏎expansion to capture community-based interventions, member engagement activities, and social service⏎referrals. Interoperable care coordination data would enable AI to learn from diverse intervention⏎approaches across organizations. Current lack of standardization prevents training models on what⏎12⏎⏎interventions work for which patients in which contexts, forcing each organization to build models from⏎scratch using only their own limited experience.⏎Real-time medication adherence data requires integration of pharmacy claims, prescription refill data, and⏎potentially pill bottle sensors. NCPDP SCRIPT standard supports e-prescribing but lacks adherence⏎monitoring capabilities. Integration of Surescripts medication history with EHR data would improve⏎AI-driven adherence intervention targeting by identifying non-adherence patterns before clinical⏎deterioration occurs.⏎Standardized SDOH data elements are important for population health AI long-term. Current pilot⏎implementations of Gravity Project standards and IHE SDOH profiles need widespread adoption.⏎Priority data elements include housing stability, food insecurity, transportation access, and social isolation.⏎However, this represents a multi-year standardization effort with gradual benefits as adoption increases⏎across care settings. Standards should support both structured data capture and natural language⏎processing of unstructured SDOH assessments from community health workers.⏎In addition, improved data integration for dual-eligible populations would enable AI systems to address⏎the full complexity of care needs for this vulnerable population.⏎Approximately 12 million Americans are dually eligible for Medicare and Medicaid, representing a⏎disproportionate share of high-risk, high-cost members who would benefit most from AI-driven⏎population health management. However, AI development for this population faces significant data⏎integration barriers:⏎Fragmented data sources: Medicare claims data (through CMS) and Medicaid claims data⏎●⏎(through state agencies) are maintained in separate systems with different access mechanisms,⏎data formats, and use restrictions⏎Inconsistent identifiers: Lack of universal patient identifiers complicates linking Medicare and⏎●⏎Medicaid records for the same beneficiary⏎Regulatory complexity: Different privacy frameworks and data use agreements govern Medicare⏎●⏎versus Medicaid data, creating uncertainty about permissible AI applications using integrated data⏎D-SNP limitations: Dual Eligible Special Needs Plans have access to both Medicare and Medicaid⏎●⏎data but face restrictions on data sharing with AI vendors and care coordination partners⏎To address this fragmentation, CMS should:⏎Establish streamlined data use agreements enabling AI developers to access linked⏎●⏎Medicare-Medicaid data for population health applications serving dual-eligible beneficiaries⏎Develop common data models and standardized file formats for integrated Medicare-Medicaid⏎●⏎datasets suitable for AI model development⏎Issue guidance clarifying HIPAA and Medicaid confidentiality requirements for AI systems⏎●⏎operating on integrated dual-eligible data⏎Require Medicare-Medicaid Plans (MMPs) and D-SNPs to share integrated data with contracted⏎●⏎care coordination vendors, including AI-driven population health management partners, subject⏎to appropriate privacy protections⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Challenges Patients Want AI to Address:⏎• Care coordination complexity and fragmentation across multiple providers, specialists, and⏎service organizations, particularly for patients with multiple chronic conditions⏎• Difficulty accessing appropriate care at the right time, particularly for behavioral health and⏎specialist services where wait times can exceed months⏎• Medication management challenges including polypharmacy, side effects, and adherence barriers⏎related to cost, understanding, or complexity of regimens⏎13⏎⏎• Navigation of social service resources including food assistance, housing support, and⏎transportation, where eligibility requirements and application processes create substantial barriers⏎Patient and Caregiver Concerns About AI:⏎• Privacy and data security concerns about who has access to health information and how it is⏎used, particularly with cloud-based AI systems and third-party vendors⏎• Bias and fairness worries that AI systems might perpetuate existing disparities or discriminate⏎based on race, socioeconomic status, disability, or other protected characteristics⏎• Loss of human connection and empathy in healthcare delivery, particularly for patients with⏎complex needs requiring relationship-based care and trust-building over time⏎• Lack of transparency about when AI is being used and how it influences clinical decisions⏎affecting their care, with concerns about whether clinicians are being pressured to follow AI⏎recommendations even when clinical judgment differs⏎Balancing these opportunities and concerns requires patient engagement in AI governance, transparent⏎communication about AI use, and maintaining human oversight of AI-generated recommendations.⏎Patients generally support AI that augments rather than replaces human clinical judgment and care⏎delivery, and value AI that helps them navigate complex healthcare and social service systems more⏎effectively.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care? a) Are there published findings about the impact of⏎adopted AI tools and their use [in] clinical care? b) How does the literature approach the costs,⏎benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research in the following areas that directly support operational AI deployment and⏎minimize implementation barriers: 1) federated learning and privacy-preserving computation, 2) causal⏎inference and heterogeneous treatment effects, and 3) safety-shared reinforcement learning.⏎Federated Learning and Privacy-Preserving Computation:⏎Multi-site model training without centralized data aggregation is essential for improving AI⏎generalizability while maintaining privacy. Research priorities include federated learning algorithms robust⏎to heterogeneous data distributions across health plans and care settings; differential privacy⏎implementations with acceptable privacy-utility tradeoffs for population health applications; and secure⏎multi-party computation protocols scalable to healthcare data volumes. This research directly supports⏎better model development while minimizing breach risk.⏎Causal Inference and Heterogeneous Treatment Effects:⏎Most current healthcare AI focuses on prediction rather than causal effect estimation. Research is needed⏎on methods for estimating individual-level treatment effects from observational data, including⏎instrumental variable approaches adapted for healthcare settings, double machine learning⏎implementations, and causal forest methods. Priority applications include personalizing intervention⏎selection based on predicted benefit for each patient rather than population-average effects.⏎Safety-Shaped Reinforcement Learning:⏎Sequential clinical decision support requires reinforcement learning approaches that explicitly incorporate⏎safety constraints. Research is needed on conservative policy learning that avoids recommending⏎interventions with insufficient evidence; offline reinforcement learning with distributional robustness for⏎learning from historical data without risky online exploration; and hybrid approaches combining⏎model-based and model-free learning. Applications include medication titration, chronic disease⏎management, and care coordination sequencing.⏎Based on our review of the current literature, published literature on deployed AI in clinical care remains⏎limited compared to research prototypes. A critical gap exists between algorithm performance in research⏎settings versus operational effectiveness in real care delivery. Key findings include: sepsis prediction⏎models show AUC values of 0.75-0.85 but implementation studies reveal high false positive rates leading⏎14⏎⏎to alert fatigue; hospital readmission prediction models achieve modest discrimination but interventions⏎targeting high-risk patients show variable effectiveness; and natural language processing for clinical⏎documentation demonstrates time savings but accuracy and liability concerns persist.4 More⏎implementation science research is needed documenting how AI performs when integrated into actual⏎clinical workflows with all their complexity and constraints.⏎IV.Conclusion⏎Accelerating AI adoption in clinical care requires focused action on the highest-impact barriers. Based on⏎Waymark's operational experience deploying AI-driven population health management for high-risk Medicaid⏎populations, we have prioritized concrete policy recommendations that would most directly enable responsible⏎innovation while protecting patient safety and privacy.⏎Three policy changes represent the highest priorities. First, CMS should issue guidance under 42 CFR Part 438⏎establishing performance-based payment structures in Medicaid managed care contracts that explicitly reward⏎AI-driven reductions in avoidable acute care utilization, accompanied by model contract language for states to⏎adopt and enhanced federal matching for states that implement these value-based structures. Payment⏎misalignment remains the single greatest barrier to AI adoption, and addressing it would unlock investment in⏎AI systems that have already demonstrated measurable improvements in outcomes and cost.⏎Second, HHS should establish comprehensive regulatory safe harbors for population health AI, including OIG⏎guidance confirming the application of existing fraud and abuse safe harbors to AI-driven member⏎engagement and care coordination optimization. Organizations deploying AI to improve care for Medicaid⏎beneficiaries should not face unresolved legal exposure under the Anti-Kickback Statute and Beneficiary⏎Inducement CMP for activities that demonstrably improve health outcomes.⏎Third, HHS should lead federal harmonization of state AI requirements for healthcare applications operating⏎under federally regulated programs. Coordinating with NAIC and NAMD to develop common standards, and⏎establishing that compliance with federal AI transparency, validation, and monitoring requirements satisfies⏎state-level obligations for Medicaid AI systems, would eliminate the duplicative compliance burden that⏎currently impedes multi-state deployment by organizations like Waymark.⏎On the research front, investment in federated learning infrastructure represents the highest priority, as it⏎directly supports model development across diverse populations while minimizing breach risk. This would⏎enable AI systems to generalize across heterogeneous care settings without requiring centralized data⏎aggregation that creates privacy concerns.⏎We appreciate HHS's proactive approach to AI policy development and are prepared to contribute our⏎operational experience and research expertise to ongoing policy development efforts. The opportunity to⏎deploy AI for improved health outcomes, reduced costs, and more equitable care delivery is substantial, and⏎focused policy action on these high-priority areas can accelerate responsible innovation while maintaining⏎appropriate safeguards.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Rajaie Batniji⏎CEO, Waymark⏎4 Wong A, Otles E, Donnelly JP, Krumm A, McCullough J, DeTroyer-Cooley O, Pestrue J, Phillips M, Konye J, Penoza C, Ghous M, Singh K. External Validation of a⏎Widely Implemented Proprietary Sepsis Prediction Model in Hospitalized Patients. JAMA Intern Med. 2021 Aug 1;181(8):1065-1070. doi:⏎10.1001/jamainternmed.2021.2626. Erratum in: JAMA Intern Med. 2021 Aug 1;181(8):1144. doi: 10.1001/jamainternmed.2021.3907. PMID: 34152373; PMCID:⏎PMC8218233; Kansagara D, Englander H, Salanitro A, Kagen D, Theobald C, Freeman M, Kripalani S. Risk prediction models for hospital readmission: a systematic⏎review. JAMA. 2011 Oct 19;306(15):1688-98. doi: 10.1001/jama.2011.1515. PMID: 22009101; PMCID: PMC3603349; Aramaki E, Wakamiya S, Yada S, Nakamura Y.⏎Natural Language Processing: from Bedside to Everywhere. Yearb Med Inform. 2022 Aug;31(1):243-253. doi: 10.1055/s-0042-1742510. Epub 2022 Jun 2. PMID:⏎35654422; PMCID: PMC9719781.⏎15♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0211♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Litten, Elizabeth, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Elizabeth♔Litten♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment on HHS’s Request for Information on accelerating the⏎adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. These comments focus on⏎concrete, legally grounded steps HHS can take—through regulation, reimbursement, and⏎research and development—to reduce barriers, promote trustworthy adoption, and align⏎incentives, consistent with HIPAA and the ONC information blocking framework.⏎I. Executive summary⏎HHS should advance a forward-leaning, industry-supportive approach to clinical AI by: (1)⏎removing practical and economic barriers to accessing high-value clinical data for privacy-⏎preserving model development and evaluation, with targeted incentives for health record holders⏎and electronic health record (“EHR”) developers; (2) facilitating creation of “data twins”⏎programs and systems to generate robust, de-identified real-world data assets while protecting⏎patient privacy and ensuring patient autonomy via opt-out rights and permissioned compensation⏎pathways; (3) promulgating best-practice guidance for meaningful human review in clinical AI,⏎emphasizing privacy and security safeguards post-deployment; (4) addressing workflow⏎integration and EHR friction as fundamental barriers to sustainable clinical AI adoption; and (5)⏎clarifying HIPAA terms—including business associate “management and administration”—to⏎define permissible AI training and operations under business associate agreements (BAAs),⏎while preserving patients’ trust and rights. These recommendations align with the RFI’s goals to⏎use regulation, reimbursement, and R&D to accelerate adoption, lower burden and cost, and⏎improve outcomes.⏎II. Barriers and Recommendations in Alignment with HHS priorities⏎HHS’s RFI seeks input to establish a clear, risk-based regulatory posture and to use⏎reimbursement and R&D to enable safe, effective clinical AI at scale. This complements the⏎Department’s “OneHHS” AI Strategy and calls for concrete, experience-based feedback to⏎ensure rapid adoption while protecting privacy and public trust. The RFI specifically invites⏎recommendations on barriers to innovation, regulatory changes and definitions, evaluation⏎methods for non-device AI, and ways to support accreditation/certification and interoperability—⏎all central to the proposals below.⏎Barrier 1: Limitations on ability to access accurate and complete clinical data across⏎systems restrict the development of robust, real-world datasets within the health care⏎ecosystem⏎From a clinical perspective, data access barriers extend beyond legal and technical⏎interoperability. Even when fast healthcare interoperability (“FHIR”) application programming⏎interfaces (“APIs”) are used, data quality is often inconsistent (for example, often missing⏎problem lists, medications, and clinical notes) and APIs do not reliably expose the data types⏎needed for meaningful AI applications, including clinical notes, imaging narratives, prior⏎authorization data, and longitudinal context. Cost, contracting complexities, and "security⏎181858808.2⏎182526710.1⏎⏎reviews" can function as de facto blocking mechanisms, and tools that require extra clicks,⏎toggling, copy/paste operations, or dual documentation will not survive in production⏎environments. "Pilot success" frequently does not translate to sustainable deployment without⏎deep EHR integration and reliable uptime.⏎Recommendation 1: Leverage ONC’s interoperability and information blocking⏎frameworks to remove barriers to access clinical data across EHR systems while continuing⏎to protect patient privacy⏎HHS should leverage ONC’s interoperability and information blocking frameworks to reduce⏎friction and enable responsible access, exchange, and use of electronic health information (EHI)⏎for clinical AI development and evaluation (i) among covered entities and business associates⏎subject to HIPAA (including minimum necessary) requirements; and (ii) outside the realm of⏎HIPAA protection when consistent with HIPAA de-identification standards. Specifically:⏎• Establish targeted incentives and reimbursement supports. HHS should explore payment⏎policy options and grant mechanisms that reward providers, health information networks,⏎and EHR developers for enabling standardized, automated access to de-identified and⏎limited datasets for model development and post-deployment monitoring, consistent with⏎information blocking rules and the Manner Exception. Aligning incentives with TEFCA⏎participation and USCDI expansion would widen market opportunities and accelerate AI⏎development, as the RFI explicitly seeks.⏎• Clarify that practices impeding automated, standards-based data access may constitute⏎information blocking absent an applicable exception. ONC has already highlighted that⏎practices interfering with automation technology’s ability to access, exchange, or use EHI⏎are of concern; HHS should reinforce this through FAQs and guidance to support AI⏎model lifecycle needs while ensuring privacy and security.⏎• Encourage standardized de-identified data export APIs. HHS should consider ONC⏎certification criteria enhancements to ensure certified health IT can generate and transmit⏎de-identified datasets using consistent standards (e.g., FHIR bulk export profiles) to⏎support research and model evaluation, subject to governance controls and HIPAA⏎de-identification.⏎• Support real-world evaluation through data access. FDA’s ongoing inquiry into⏎measuring and evaluating AI-enabled tools in the real world underscores the need for⏎reliable post-deployment data access to assess model drift, bias, and safety—principles⏎that also apply to non-device clinical AI.⏎• Strengthen interoperability expectations for specific high-value data types and benchmark⏎data completeness, not just API presence.⏎• Create ONC/CMS incentives that favor measurable workflow impact such as time saved⏎and reduction in inbox burden, rather than generic "AI available" metrics.⏎2⏎181858808.2⏎182526710.1⏎⏎These steps would operationalize the RFI’s emphasis on a predictable, proportionate regulatory⏎posture and on aligning federal levers while expediting the creation of robust, real-world data⏎sets to enhance the reliability of AI tools.⏎Barrier 2: Use of personal identifiers without patient or consumer knowledge or consent⏎erodes trust and undermines individual autonomy⏎Clinicians increasingly report that patients ask questions such as "Is an AI tool involved?" and⏎"Did you use my data to train it?" Concerns are particularly acute for sensitive topics including⏎behavioral health, reproductive care, and HIV/SUD data. Recommendation 2: Adopt patient⏎consent standards and facilitate privacy-protective “data twins” with patient opt-out and⏎permissioned compensation⏎HHS should provide clearer public-facing guidance and clinician-facing scripts for consent and⏎notice in specific clinical contexts.⏎To scale high-quality, real-world datasets while protecting privacy, HHS should encourage the⏎creation of “data twins”—de-identified datasets that mirror health attributes of real persons—⏎under robust governance:⏎• Establish a clear policy framework for data twins. HHS should issue guidance clarifying⏎how HIPAA de-identification and health care operations concepts apply to creating⏎de-identified data twins for quality improvement, safety monitoring, and AI development,⏎with guardrails for re-identification risk management and vendor accountability.⏎• Require transparent notices and a simple opt-out. HIPAA does not protect de-identified⏎data, but HHS should encourage transparent patient notices and a standardized opt-out for⏎the creation or use of a data twin, balancing trust and innovation consistent with the RFI’s⏎focus on public confidence.⏎• Permit compensation pathways with safeguards. HHS should clarify that programs⏎offering remuneration to individuals for permitting use of their data for twin creation⏎(where applicable under state law and program design) can proceed with appropriate⏎disclosures, anti-exploitation protections, and without conditioning treatment or⏎benefits—promoting inclusion and representativeness in datasets.⏎• Promote interoperability for data twins. ONC should align USCDI/USCDI+ and TEFCA⏎policies to support standardized, de-identified data assembly for twins, enabling⏎benchmarking and external validation while upholding information blocking’s⏎pro-interoperability aims.⏎Together, these steps would advance trustworthy data generation for AI while respecting patient⏎autonomy and privacy expectations articulated across HHS’s AI posture.⏎3⏎181858808.2⏎182526710.1⏎⏎Barrier 3: Lack of industry-wide standards for human review, oversight, evaluation, and⏎description of clinical AI tools⏎The industry lacks standards for clinician involvement in review, oversight, evaluation, and⏎description of clinical AI tools throughout the tools’ lifecycles. Clinicians face significant trust⏎barriers when AI model outputs are not explainable to patients in terms of clinical rationale and⏎evidence anchors. Additionally, providers will not adopt tools that change silently through new⏎versions with new behaviors. Recommendation 3: Issue best-practice guidance for human⏎review, post-deployment oversight, evaluation, and decription of clinical AI tools⏎HHS should promulgate cross-industry guidelines for non-device clinical AI on meaningful⏎human involvement, privacy/security controls, and continuous model oversight:⏎• “Three-touch” human-in-the-loop for clinical AI decision-support tools. Require expert⏎review prior to deployment (to include review of data provenance and labeling, model⏎outputs, etc.); at early deployment (pilot validation); and post-deployment (ongoing⏎monitoring and governance).⏎• Human-in-the-loop for consequential decisions. Building on stakeholder input and⏎payer-side constraints that already require clinician involvement, HHS should require⏎AI-influenced determinations affecting access to services or clinical care receive timely,⏎qualified human review with appropriate documentation, reducing inappropriate denials⏎and supporting due process.⏎• Pre- and post-deployment monitoring. HHS should encourage routine monitoring for⏎model drift, bias, and performance across populations and settings, leveraging both⏎automated and human-expert review approaches tailored to the workflow, consistent with⏎FDA’s focus on real-world performance. HHS should publish a minimum evaluation and⏎monitoring checklist for clinical AI, covering both pre-deployment and post-deployment⏎requirements.⏎• Interoperability and measurement readiness. HHS should tie R&D support to the use of⏎standardized data elements (USCDI) and exchange frameworks (TEFCA) to enable⏎replicable evaluations and equitable performance assessments.⏎• "Change transparency." HHS should issue requirements for clinical AI tools to monitor⏎and provide notice of changes to the tools, including release notes, notification protocols,⏎and re-validation requirements when models are updated.⏎This guidance would complement the RFI’s aims to foster public trust and reduce uncertainty⏎that impedes innovation, particularly for non-device AI workflows in clinical care.⏎Barrier 4: Lack of regulatory guidance regarding use of AI in compliance with HIPAA⏎4⏎181858808.2⏎182526710.1⏎⏎Clinicians need regulatory clarity related to various legal issues, including for example the⏎standard of care when AI is used; what constitutes reasonable reliance versus independent⏎verification; malpractice risk when AI recommendations are wrong, missing, or biased; and⏎documentation expectations (e.g., inclusion in HIPAA notice of privacy practices; patient⏎consent). In addition, HIPAA lacks clarity as to the ability of a business associate to use patient⏎data for purposes of training its own (or a subcontractor’s) clinical AI tool.⏎Recommendation 4: Develop model guidance and safe-harbor concepts for documented,⏎risk-tiered AI use; clarify HIPAA terms to define permissible AI uses by covered entities⏎and business associates; issue HIPAA security guidelines to simplify procurement.⏎To remove ambiguity that slows responsible adoption, HHS should issue clarifications and⏎model BAA language addressing:⏎• AI training and “management and administration.” HHS should clarify when a business⏎associate’s training, tuning, or maintenance of AI models using PHI qualifies as “the⏎proper management and administration” of the business associate, and when separate⏎authorization or de-identification is required.⏎• Purpose limitation and data minimization. HHS should reinforce that business⏎associate-conducted AI activities must adhere to purpose limitations, minimum necessary,⏎and prohibitions on impermissible secondary uses such as marketing.⏎• Transparency and patient notices. HHS should include language regarding use of PHI in⏎connection with AI in the model Notice of Privacy Practices and describe permissible⏎uses by covered entities, including de-identified uses, consistent with building trust while⏎enabling efficient operations under existing HIPAA pathways.⏎• Security friction. HHS should issue security guidelines and/or create standardized⏎procurement and security templates specific to clinical AI to alleviate the burden on⏎smaller organizations that lack resources to navigate complex vendor onboarding⏎processes.⏎• Harmonization with information blocking. HHS should confirm that HIPAA-compliant⏎privacy/security controls and BAA requirements can co-exist with the obligation not to⏎engage in information blocking, and that reasonable, standardized safeguards do not⏎constitute improper interference with EHI access, exchange, or use.⏎These clarifications would help standardize contracting and compliance expectations, reducing⏎uncertainty and transaction costs across the health care ecosystem.⏎III. General Comments re: Reimbursement and Research and Development (R&D)⏎• Reimbursement pilots for validated AI. CMS should test payment models that reimburse⏎for the acquisition and use of AI that measurably reduces burden or improves outcomes,⏎5⏎181858808.2⏎182526710.1⏎⏎with requirements for post-deployment monitoring and equitable performance, as⏎contemplated by the RFI’s reimbursement focus. CMS should align payment so that high-⏎value AI-enabled workflows are financially viable—whether through time-based codes,⏎value-based care incentives, or other payment mechanism that encourages adoption of⏎high-value AI tools.⏎• R&D to build shared evaluation infrastructure. HHS should fund reference datasets,⏎benchmarking tools, and secure enclaves to support external validation and fairness⏎testing for non-device AI, aligning with FDA’s real-world performance focus and ONC⏎interoperability priorities.⏎• Support for accreditation and certification. HHS should catalyze private-sector⏎accreditation and certification programs for clinical AI governance and post-market⏎oversight, promoting consistency without duplicative regulation.⏎These investments directly address the RFI’s call to align regulation, reimbursement, and R&D⏎to accelerate adoption and lower costs while preserving safety and privacy.⏎IV. Conclusion⏎HHS can accelerate trustworthy clinical AI adoption by removing data access barriers through⏎interoperability and targeted incentives; addressing workflow integration as a fundamental⏎requirement for sustainable deployment; establishing a patient-respectful framework for⏎de-identified data twins; issuing best-practice guidance for human review and privacy-preserving⏎post-deployment oversight; establishing minimum evaluation standards for non-device AI;⏎addressing reimbursement misalignment that burdens clinicians; streamlining procurement and⏎security processes; and clarifying HIPAA terms to define permissible AI uses by covered entities⏎and business associates. These steps are squarely within the RFI’s vision to deploy AI in ways⏎that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower costs, and improve outcomes while maintaining⏎public trust and confidentiality.⏎Submitted in response to: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care; comments due February 23, 2026, via⏎Regulations.gov or mail/courier as specified.⏎Anand Patel, MD, Head of AI & Innovation, Medical Director of Radiology⏎Steve Fenlon, Chief Legal Officer⏎Rendr⏎Elizabeth G. Litten⏎Partner and Chief Privacy & HIPAA Compliance Officer⏎Fox Rothschild LLP⏎6⏎181858808.2⏎182526710.1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0212♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 2026 response to CMS RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize?⏎1. Reduce Information Asymmetry to Increase Competition⏎Information asymmetry stifles competition in health care. Large entities increasingly acquire, interpret,⏎control, and restrict access to diagnostic data — especially in radiology imaging. Diagnostic test⏎acquisition, interpretation, and data control are vertically integrated.⏎HHS should prioritize policies that reduce information asymmetry and expand meaningful data access⏎for both patients and independent analytic services. Interoperability must move beyond limited,⏎summary-level report exchange to full, usable datasets. AI cannot improve outcomes in a system where⏎transparency and data portability are limited.⏎2. Increase Patient Choice in Diagnostic Interpretation⏎The current system offers limited patient choice in diagnostic interpretation. While developments such⏎as direct patient lab ordering have improved access, diagnostic imaging services remains largely closed.⏎Given the absence of or decrease in independent diagnostic imaging services, HHS should encourage⏎models that decouple radiology data collection from interpretation. Patients should be able to choose⏎independent clinically validated AI tools to interpret their images — including tools developed by⏎technology companies operating outside vertically integrated health systems.⏎Competition at the interpretive layer will provide more useful information to patients, spur innovation,⏎and improve quality.⏎3. Recognize Radiology as a Precision Medicine Data Asset⏎Radiology imaging acquisition is fundamentally a complex data collection tool. Interpretation should⏎move beyond narrative summaries that answer a narrow clinical question and instead produce⏎structured anatomical and pathological inventories.⏎Imaging providers should compete not on volume, but on the quality and completeness of their⏎diagnostic outputs. Structured, standardized inventories of anatomy and pathology rather than a brief⏎or incomplete answer to a narrow clinical question noted on a report can serve as reusable groundwork⏎for precision medicine across time, providers, and analytic tools.⏎Standards should promote:⏎• Structured data capture⏎• Longitudinal comparability⏎• Reusability for AI training⏎• Precision treatment optimization⏎Radiology data should be recognized as a reusable asset owned by the patient. Currently, systems⏎acquire, interpret, manage, and control or restrict access to imaging data— and then deliver or not limit⏎⏎treatment based on that controlled or limited dataset. This can function as gatekeeping and limit patient⏎choice.⏎A competitive payment structure should allow independent AI companies to use acquired imaging data⏎to interpret radiology imaging or other tests for better outcomes. This will spur innovation in diagnostic⏎timeliness and quality, structured reporting standards, and precision-based therapies.⏎4. Tie AI Incentives to Measurable Outcomes⏎AI payment incentives should reward:⏎• Reduced time-to-diagnosis⏎• Fewer missed diagnoses⏎• Improved morbidity and mortality outcomes⏎AI policy should stimulate competition that measurably improves diagnostic accuracy while improving⏎efficiency to reduce costs. For example, cardiovascular disease remains one of the highest-cost areas in⏎Medicare and is largely diagnosed and managed through imaging. More complete, detailed, and⏎accurate image interpretation can lead to earlier intervention, lower downstream costs, and improved⏎outcomes.⏎What are the most promising AI evaluation methods? Should HHS support these processes?⏎1. The most promising approach is comparative, outcome-based evaluation in real-world settings —⏎especially for radiology imaging services. Evaluation should include:⏎• Institutional vs. independent AI interpretation comparisons⏎• Divergence analysis in abnormality detection⏎• Time-to-diagnosis tracking⏎• Missed pathology rates⏎• Downstream outcome measurement⏎2. Evaluation must address data integrity—an underrated and underdeveloped area.⏎AI underperformance is often driven by poor input data rather than algorithmic weakness. An AI⏎radiology system that reviews imaging, labs, pathology, and notes must also incorporate patient-⏎generated data. Provider documentation and diagnostic test results frequently:⏎• Omits or misstates relevant history⏎• Contains incomplete or incorrect clinical orders⏎• Maintains outdated or inaccurate diagnoses⏎• Fails to correlate imaging findings with patient-reported symptoms⏎If AI systems are trained or evaluated on distorted documentation, performance metrics will be⏎misleading. HHS should prioritize research on:⏎• Patient-facing radiology AI tools⏎⏎• Interfaces embedded in medical records with structured symptom tracking⏎• Integration of device-generated data (e.g., blood pressure)⏎• Comparative accuracy studies with and without patient-reported context⏎Including structured patient context can meaningfully improve diagnostic accuracy and clinical decision-⏎making.⏎Where would enhanced interoperability accelerate development?⏎1. Interoperability is most critical in imaging diagnostics. It should ensure:⏎• Full DICOM portability without compression⏎• Structured linkage between imaging, labs, pathology, and longitudinal records⏎• Integration of patient-reported symptoms⏎• Provenance and audit tracking across systems/users⏎• Historical imaging comparison capabilities, rather than just the reports⏎2. Radiology archives contain significant underutilized diagnostic capacity. Radiology imaging studies⏎hold far more signal than narrative reports capture. Market opportunity expands when:⏎• Independent analytic services can access standardized datasets⏎• Hospitals compete on the structure and completeness of diagnostic inventories⏎• Patients can submit imaging for competitive interpretation⏎• Researchers can benchmark models on complete, traceable data⏎3. Patients should be able to choose which AI tools to embed in their records and present AI-generated⏎summaries at visits. This supports collaborative, informed decision-making rather than unilateral⏎interpretive control.⏎What challenges do patients want addressed? What concerns exist?⏎1. Patients primarily seek:⏎• Timely and accurate diagnosis⏎• Earlier detection of serious conditions⏎• Accurate and complete medical records⏎• Detailed radiology reports that correlate findings with symptoms and longitudinal history⏎• Transparency about AI tools used and access to their outputs⏎Clinical AI must align with patient needs — not solely clinician workflows. Excluding patients as a⏎primary stakeholder risks embedding inefficiencies and unsafe assumptions into new systems.⏎2. A central but longstanding challenge is documentation distortion. For example: A patient with an⏎indeterminate liver lesion who recently experienced a serious cardiovascular event that was not⏎properly documented. Without the patient context, an AI tool may mischaracterize the lesion.⏎Similarly, if the imaging order reflects an inaccurate problem framing, interpretation may be⏎misdirected. Unless AI incorporates structured patient-reported history and symptom tracking,⏎⏎outputs will be incomplete or inaccurate. The strength of AI accuracy and efficiency is directly⏎related to the quality and degree of information it consumes.⏎3. Patients are concerned about:⏎• AI reinforcing inaccurate notes and outdated diagnostic history⏎• AI trained on incomplete or uncorrelated imaging reports⏎• Continued exclusion from interpretive processes⏎• Systems optimized for administrative efficiency rather than clinical outcomes⏎• Lack of transparency about which AI tools are used⏎The quality of the medical record directly determines the reliability of AI tools that affect patient⏎outcomes.⏎What AI research should HHS prioritize?⏎1. HHS should prioritize direct-care, patient-facing radiology imaging interpretation tools that:⏎• Analyze full imaging datasets rather than narrative summaries⏎• Produce structured anatomical and pathology inventories⏎• Correlate findings with patient history⏎• Fully and clearly disclose abnormalities⏎• Support cardiovascular imaging interpretation⏎Radiologist shortages and increasing imaging complexity make innovation urgent.⏎2. HHS should also prioritize research on patient-integrated contextual AI systems that:⏎• Review imaging, labs, pathology, notes, and patient-reported data simultaneously⏎• Identify inconsistencies between documentation and history⏎• Flag divergence⏎• Generate structured differential summaries⏎3. Studies should measure diagnostic accuracy and outcomes with and without integrated patient-⏎reported context.⏎Radiology data contains substantial unused diagnostic capacity. AI policy should unlock that capacity⏎through transparency, structured data standards, competition for quality, and alignment with patient⏎needs — not merely digitize existing workflows and limited outputs.⏎Thank you for this opportunity to comment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0213♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS, CMS, ASTP/ONC and the CDC should accelerate the movement to FHIR-ize with AI the National Electronic Disease Surveillance System (NEDSS)  and the National Syndromic Surveillance Program (NSSP) to exchange public health disease surveillance data and public health syndromic surveillance data.⏎⏎In addition, the states need to do state to state FHIR with AI exchange for NEDSS and NSSP.⏎⏎⏎this will be useful for contact tracing, case investigations, outbreak detection etc. ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0214♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Duke Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Duke Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Response to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide comments on the Request for Information: Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. As one of the nation’s⏎leading academic health systems, we appreciate the opportunity to provide comments as the⏎administration considers opportunities to promote AI innovation and adoption in clinical care.⏎Duke Health integrates the Duke University Health System (all of our hospitals, clinics, and the⏎Duke Health Integrated Practice), Duke University School of Medicine, Duke-NUS Medical School,⏎Duke University School of Nursing, and incorporates the health and health research programs⏎within the Duke Global Health Institute as well as those in schools and centers across Duke⏎University, including the Duke Robert J. Margolis Institute for Health Policy.⏎Our organization is committed to excellence through the adoption of AI innovations to improve⏎patient outcomes, reduce administrative burden, and streamline care delivery. To achieve this, we⏎established the Algorithm-Based Clinical Decision Support (ABCDS) Oversight Committee in⏎2020. This committee governs and reviews AI solutions for patient care. At that time, AI quality and⏎ethical considerations were not widely understood or adopted by stakeholders, and FDA guidance⏎was still under development. Over the past five years, we have worked to balance innovation with⏎high-quality, ethical care, ensuring responsible deployment of AI solutions.⏎Based on our experience, Duke Health offers the following considerations for promoting AI⏎innovation and adoption in clinical care:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎Currently, significant variation in legislation across states creates barriers to widespread⏎adoption. A shared federal legal and regulatory framework, developed in partnership with⏎states, would help reduce inconsistencies and minimize the compliance burden on companies⏎that would otherwise need to meet different requirements in each state.⏎⏎HHS can play a critical role by establishing greater transparency requirements for AI solution⏎testing, validation, and implementation practices. Building on the transparency elements⏎recommended in HTI-1, additional guidance related to AI workflow integration and⏎decision‑making based on model outputs could serve as a practical playbook for healthcare⏎organizations. Such transparency standards would help increase trust, provide operational⏎clarity, and ultimately drive broader adoption of AI tools in healthcare settings.⏎Incentives are needed for both healthcare organizations and end users of AI solutions to adopt,⏎purchase, and maintain these technologies. Strengthening infrastructure readiness will also be⏎essential to support successful implementation. (See below)⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎AI has significant potential to improve health outcomes for all Americans. However,⏎determining which AI solutions are truly effective remains a core challenge. Because AI models⏎are fundamentally data‑driven, their performance can vary substantially across health systems⏎due to differences in patient populations, care delivery patterns, and data quality. Ongoing⏎monitoring is therefore essential: only through real‑world evaluation can organizations⏎determine whether a given AI solution improves outcomes in their specific clinical environment.⏎Yet many healthcare organizations lack the necessary expertise to implement, validate, and⏎monitor AI tools effectively. They may also lack the technical infrastructure required to deploy⏎and maintain these algorithms at scale. Incentives that build organizational readiness for⏎responsible AI adoption are critical. These could include grants from HRSA, ASTP/ONC, and⏎the CMS Innovation Center to support AI readiness assessments, workforce development, and⏎infrastructure modernization. Additionally, a national AI technical assistance program—⏎modeled after ONC’s Health IT Regional Extension Centers—could provide hands‑on support to⏎organizations that currently lack in‑house expertise.⏎Today, each health system evaluates AI solutions largely in isolation, determining for itself⏎whether a tool is effective and whether its benefits outweigh its risks. A national approach would⏎reduce this duplication of effort. National registries could facilitate information sharing across⏎healthcare organizations by aggregating real‑world evidence on AI model performance, clinical⏎effectiveness, and safety risks. Such a system would accelerate learning, support continuous⏎model improvement, and promote the adoption of AI tools with demonstrated benefit.⏎⏎HHS could help lead this effort by establishing a federated national AI registry, supported by⏎CMS and ASTP, to collect standardized, privacy‑preserving performance metrics.1 Participation⏎could be required for AI tools used in federally funded care settings to ensure broad coverage⏎and transparency. Duke Health, for example, is co‑developing an information‑sharing platform⏎with Avanade called SAIGE that will enable organizations to share data on which AI solutions⏎are deployed and which provide meaningful clinical value. This type of infrastructure could⏎serve as a foundation for a national registry model.⏎The health care delivery system currently lacks appropriate incentive structures to support the⏎adoption and sustained use of AI‑based tools. Today, the return on investment for many AI⏎solutions is driven primarily by staff time savings; return on health is not easily translated into⏎return on investment, which, in turn, can make it difficult for organizations to justify investment.⏎Payment models that recognize and reward the use of AI tools demonstrated to improve clinical⏎quality, patient outcomes, or system efficiency would create a more durable incentive for⏎adoption. Aligning reimbursement with outcomes would enable hospitals to invest in AI as a⏎mechanism for improving care delivery rather than as a cost‑cutting measure.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Looking ahead, the emergence of next‑generation AI systems—particularly agentic AI—raises⏎new legal and operational challenges. When healthcare professionals are partially or fully out⏎of the decision‑making loop, accountability for errors becomes ambiguous. Robust evaluation⏎methods rooted in both automation safety and human‑factors engineering will be essential to⏎assess the outputs, behaviors, and failure modes of agentic AI. These approaches can help⏎ensure that automated clinical actions remain predictable, effective, safe, and aligned with⏎clinical standards, even as systems become more autonomous.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎postdeployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎Because AI solutions can perform differently across healthcare organizations, both⏎pre‑deployment and post‑deployment evaluation as well as ongoing monitoring should be⏎supported and encouraged. To further promote the use of effective AI, HHS could consider⏎1 A Federated Registration System for Artificial Intelligence in Health - PubMed⏎⏎allowing validated AI interventions to contribute toward existing quality performance metrics.⏎In addition, HHS could offer shared‑savings bonuses for organizations that implement AI tools⏎shown to reduce avoidable hospitalizations, decrease disparities, or measurably improve⏎clinical outcomes.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Alignment around technical standards and risk frameworks is essential to promote consistent,⏎safe, and scalable adoption of AI in clinical care. Clear federal support for widely recognized⏎technical and governance standards—such as structured AI lifecycle management, transparency⏎requirements, and post‑deployment monitoring practices—would help reduce duplication of⏎effort across health systems and provide greater regulatory clarity for both developers and⏎providers.⏎In parallel, targeted investment in workforce education and re‑skilling is critical. Many⏎healthcare organizations currently lack the clinical, technical, and operational expertise needed⏎to implement, validate, and continuously monitor AI tools in real‑world settings. Federal⏎support for training programs, technical assistance, and AI‑focused workforce development⏎would increase trust among clinicians and administrators, strengthen organizational readiness,⏎and ensure that AI tools are used appropriately, safely, and in ways that deliver measurable⏎clinical value.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce⏎costs?⏎Ambient listening solutions have reduced the amount of time clinicians spend on documentation,⏎and patients report that these tools improve the quality of patient‑provider interactions. But for⏎many AI tools, it is too early to assign an “exceeded” or “fallen short” label on their⏎implementation as health systems are in early stages of evaluating new tools. Examples include⏎computer vision with AI models to aid in prevention of falls from a hospital bed and prevention⏎of pressure ulcers. The same computer vision technology is likely to support workflow⏎modification to improve efficiencies of acute care delivery, perioperative operations, Emergency⏎Department operations, and the patient and staff experience in all these services.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the⏎most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎⏎A key driver of successful AI adoption is ensuring that each AI solution addresses a clearly⏎defined unmet clinical need. To achieve this, stakeholders across service lines and departments⏎should be engaged early in the selection process, including the build–buy–partner decision.⏎Governance bodies can play an essential coordinating role by guiding these decisions,⏎establishing clear accountability for AI deployment, and promoting transparency around which⏎AI solutions provide measurable benefit.⏎End‑user champions can further support adoption by coordinating pilot projects, training peers,⏎and educating potential users on the benefits of each AI tool as well as strategies for managing⏎associated risks. To cultivate a culture of innovation and support AI scaling, organizations can⏎designate AI ambassadors embedded across clinics who help facilitate adoption, provide⏎hands‑on support, and organize communication campaigns or events to raise awareness of the⏎value and appropriate use of AI solutions.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Currently, hospitals using electronic health record (EHR) systems have fairly good integration⏎of clinical data. However, there is a significant gap in integration of clinical data between⏎organizations not using the same EHR system. This has significant safety implications when⏎patients transfer between organizations. It also has a significant impact on the ability to screen⏎patients for clinical trial options if their health records are stored in a different EHR system. AI⏎is increasingly performative in finding the right patient for the right trial. However, without true⏎clinical data integration, that is very difficult to automate.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎There is significant opportunity for AI to address patient education and monitoring in the home⏎setting. Patients with life threatening or chronic diseases can find it hard to absorb information⏎in the clinic setting. Providing direct to patient education tools that can be used and interactive⏎in the home setting gives patients the ability to digest information in a way that is meaningful to⏎them. Increased education and understanding of their disease can lead to better symptom⏎monitoring and treatment plan adherence.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎Peer-reviewed publications are not consistent sources of organizations reporting outcomes and⏎risks of AI solutions, especially failures of AI solution implementation. See question 2 on AI⏎national registries.⏎Specific areas of research that can be considered for future funding are:⏎• Automated evaluation methods for AI solutions (agentic AI, LLM, predictive AI)⏎• Scalable frameworks for AI management - infrastructure recommendations through clinical⏎practice learnings⏎• New Robust AI methodologies⏎Closing⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide these comments. Duke Health’s faculty, researchers, and⏎physician scientists bring deep expertise across clinical care, health policy, and biomedical⏎innovation, and have regularly served as trusted advisors to both the Executive Branch and⏎Congress. We welcome continued engagement and stand ready to support HHS as it considers⏎policies to accelerate the safe, effective, and responsible adoption of AI in healthcare.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jeffrey Ferranti, MD, MS Eric Poon, MD MPH FACMI⏎Senior Vice President and Chief Digital Officer Chief Health Information Officer⏎Duke Health Duke Health♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0215♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Academy of Managed Care Pharmacy (AMCP), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Academy of Managed Care Pharmacy (AMCP)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The Academy of Managed Care Pharmacy (AMCP) thanks the Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ASTP/ONC) and the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) for the opportunity to⏎provide comments in response to the “Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13].”⏎AMCP is the nation’s leading professional association dedicated to increasing patient access to⏎affordable medicines, improving health outcomes, and ensuring the wise use of healthcare⏎dollars. Through evidence and value-based strategies and practices, AMCP’s nearly 8,000⏎pharmacists, physicians, nurses, and other practitioners manage medication therapies for the⏎270 million Americans served by health plans, pharmacy benefit management firms, emerging⏎care models, and government health programs.⏎Artificial intelligence has the potential to meaningfully improve clinical decision-making, care⏎coordination, and administrative efficiency across the health care system. However, realizing⏎these benefits at scale requires a policy environment that balances innovation with patient⏎safety, clinician accountability, and equity. Despite significant private-sector investment and⏎rapid technological advancement, adoption of AI in clinical care remains uneven and⏎constrained by regulatory uncertainty, fragmented data infrastructure, unresolved governance⏎questions, and misaligned payment policies. Addressing these barriers will be essential to⏎ensuring that AI is deployed responsibly, supports clinical judgment, and delivers measurable⏎value to patients, clinicians, and payers.⏎Barriers to Innovation and Adoption⏎Adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care has accelerated rapidly, often outpacing⏎clinicians’ capacity to integrate these tools safely and effectively. Global AI spending is⏎⏎projected to reach $2.5 trillion in 2026,1 underscoring both the scale of investment and the⏎urgency of addressing adoption barriers. The most significant obstacle is concern about patient⏎harm and professional liability when AI-generated recommendations diverge from clinical⏎judgment. Regulatory ambiguity around the boundary between clinical decision support and⏎regulated medical devices further discourages developers and health systems. Clinicians also⏎face practical challenges related to clinical workflow integration, interoperability, safeguarding⏎data, and reimbursement. Many AI applications with demonstrated technical promise fail to⏎scale or generalize across care settings because no established reimbursement framework⏎exists to support their routine use in clinical care.⏎Regulatory, Payment, and Programmatic Priorities⏎AMCP urges HHS to clarify regulatory boundaries across FDA, ONC, and CMS to reduce⏎uncertainty and promote responsible innovation. Clear, coordinated guidance distinguishing⏎non-device clinical decision support from Software as a Medical Device would reduce adoption⏎risk under existing frameworks (e.g., 21 CFR Parts 800–898; 45 CFR §170.315). Modernization⏎of payment policy is also essential to support appropriate clinical use of AI. Medicare and⏎Medicaid should better recognize and reimburse AI-enabled services that improve diagnostic⏎accuracy, efficiency, and quality, including through updates to 42 CFR Parts 410 and 414 and⏎alignment with value-based programs such as the Medicare Shared Savings Program (42 CFR⏎Part 425).⏎Support for Private Sector Innovation⏎HHS can best support private sector innovation by enabling, rather than duplicating,⏎industry-driven accreditation, certification, and testing frameworks. Endorsing consensus-based⏎standards for model validation, bias assessment, and post-deployment monitoring, developed in⏎collaboration with standards-setting organizations, would promote safe and effective AI use⏎while avoiding unnecessary regulatory duplication and preserving flexibility for innovation.⏎Governance and Administrative Hurdles⏎Within health care organizations, adoption decisions are most influenced by clinical informatics⏎leaders, quality and safety executives, and medical governance bodies. Effective AI governance⏎models should be distinct from typical data governance and have rely on clearly defined,⏎rigorously tested frameworks to ensure appropriate data stewardship, oversight, and⏎accountability.⏎Administrative hurdles, including procurement processes, privacy and security compliance⏎under HIPAA (45 CFR Parts 160 and 164), challenges integrating AI tools into electronic health⏎record (HER) systems, workforce concerns relating to job displacement, implementing⏎education and training programs, and the absence of mature governance models to support AI⏎oversight and accountability, further impede adoption.⏎1 Gartner, Press Release: Gartner Says Worldwide AI Spending Will Total $2.5 Trillion in 2026 (Jan. 2026). Available⏎at https://www.gartner.com/en/newsroom/press-releases/2026-1-15-gartner-says-worldwide-ai-spending-will-⏎total-2-point-5-trillion-dollars-in-2026⏎⏎Interoperability and Data Access⏎Enhanced interoperability is foundational to advancing the safe and effective use of AI in clinical⏎care. Expanded access to longitudinal, multi-setting data, including unstructured clinical notes,⏎imaging metadata, and outcomes data, would broaden market opportunities and accelerate the⏎development and evaluation of AI tools. Limited availability and standardization of unstructured⏎data is a significant constraint on AI applications in health care.⏎Gaps in clinical data, often driven by beneficiary movement across plans or care settings, can⏎impede clinician access to critical information at the point of care. In these circumstances,⏎clinician judgment remains essential to contextualize incomplete data and should not be⏎supplanted by AI-generated recommendations. Continued advancement of HL7 FHIR,⏎expansion of the United States Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) (45 CFR §170.213), and⏎the development of standardized benchmarking datasets are therefore essential to support⏎interoperability, improve data continuity, and enable responsible AI deployment.⏎Patient and Caregiver Considerations⏎Patients and caregivers are most receptive to AI solutions that improve access, accuracy,⏎personalization, and continuity of care while reducing administrative burden. Importantly,⏎patients expect AI to support, but not redefine, their role in clinical decision-making. Applications⏎such as ambient or live AI clinical documentation tools can strengthen the clinician-patient⏎relationship by allowing clinicians to focus on patient engagement rather than documentation,⏎thereby enhancing the care experience. Patient experience may also be meaningfully improved⏎when AI streamlines administrative processes such as prescription refills, prior authorization,⏎and scheduling.⏎At the same time, patients and caregivers have expressed persistent concerns regarding data⏎privacy, bias, transparency, and the potential erosion of the clinician–patient relationship.⏎Successful AI adoption must therefore augment, rather than replace, human clinical judgment⏎and preserve the relational aspects of care.⏎Research and Evidence Base⏎HHS should prioritize research addressing generalizability across populations and care settings,⏎bias identification and mitigation, human–AI interaction, post-market surveillance, and the⏎impact of AI on clinician workload and burnout. While existing literature demonstrates⏎performance gains in narrow or task-specific applications, evidence remains limited regarding⏎long-term clinical outcomes, health equity implications, and system-level cost savings.⏎Economic analyses should more explicitly examine how costs, benefits, and risk are distributed⏎across clinicians, health systems, and payers to inform sustainable adoption and appropriate⏎payment policy.⏎Conclusion⏎Taken together, these considerations underscore the need for a coordinated federal approach⏎that clarifies regulatory expectations, strengthens interoperability and data continuity, supports⏎appropriate governance models, and modernizes payment policy to reflect the realities of AI-⏎enabled care. HHS is uniquely positioned to enable this progress by aligning the roles of FDA,⏎ONC, and CMS; endorsing consensus-based standards; advancing evidence generation; and⏎ensuring that reimbursement and value-based programs appropriately recognize clinically⏎⏎meaningful AI applications. With thoughtful policy design, AI can be integrated into clinical care⏎in a manner that enhances quality, protects patients, supports clinicians, and promotes⏎sustainable innovation across the health care system.⏎AMCP appreciates your consideration of AMCP’s concerns and looks forward to continuing⏎work on these issues with CMS. If you have any questions regarding AMCP's comments or⏎would like further information, please contact Vicky Jucelin, AMCP’s Manager of Regulatory⏎Affairs, at vjucelin@amcp.org or (571) 858-5320.⏎Sincerely,⏎Susan A. Cantrell, MHL, RPh, CAE⏎Chief Executive Officer♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0216♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Optometric Association , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Optometric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The American Optometric Association (AOA) represents more than 50,000 doctors of optometry,⏎optometric professionals, and optometry students. Doctors of optometry take a leading role in patient⏎care with respect to eye health and vision care, as well as general health and wellbeing. As primary⏎health care providers, doctors of optometry have extensive, ongoing training to examine, diagnose, treat,⏎and manage ocular disorders, diseases, and injuries. The AOA serves the needs of the public and health⏎professionals through the provision of evidence-based clinical practice guidelines that promote⏎prevention, identification, treatment, and management strategies for eye and vision conditions/diseases to⏎improve the nation’s health. We appreciate the opportunity to submit comments in response to the⏎Department of Health and Human Services Request for Information entitled “Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care” (RIN 0955-AA13).⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎Barriers to adoption of AI exist due to several interconnected challenges. Many AI tools struggle to⏎integrate seamlessly into electronic health record (EHR) systems and established clinical workflows. In⏎addition, there is at times a lack of transparency in how diagnostic or treatment recommendations are⏎generated which raises physician concerns about safety, accountability, and liability. There are also⏎practical barriers that further compound the issue, as some health care practices may lack the necessary⏎digital infrastructure, IT support, and trained personnel to implement and sustain AI solutions effectively.⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Reimbursement structures should value clinical judgment, oversight, and responsibility physicians retain⏎when using AI-enabled diagnostic or risk-stratification tools. If AI use is not appropriately recognized in⏎payment models, clinicians and health systems lack incentives to invest in implementation, training, and⏎workflow integration.⏎HHS should encourage care models that ensure AI-identified findings lead to timely follow-up, referral⏎completion, including tracking appropriate doctor to which patients are referred, as well as ongoing⏎treatment. Systems should track whether patients flagged by AI tools actually receive confirmatory⏎evaluation, appropriate intervention, and continued care. A diagnosis or risk flag alone is not the goal. The⏎⏎true value of AI lies in improving patient outcomes through sustained treatment, care coordination, and⏎disease management. Without mechanisms to close the referral loop, AI-driven identification of disease⏎risks becoming an isolated event rather than a catalyst for better health.⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures ( e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Additional resources and information are needed to empower physicians to more quickly vet and verify⏎the utility of AI enabled non-medical devices that exist. However, any guidance, recommendations or⏎evaluations must also be nimble to keep apace with new innovations that are emerging rapidly.⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations⏎and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎One of the clearest success stories in clinical AI is in diagnostic imaging. In eyecare there is an FDA⏎approved tool that can autonomously identify diabetic retinopathy risk in primary care settings. In⏎eyecare, AI enabled screenings with high sensitivity and specificity may help identify risk, which is a risk⏎that only a comprehensive eye examination can begin to evaluate, mitigate, and/or address. As AI in⏎health care expands, AOA believes that important criteria must be met. AI should be evaluated based on⏎whether it can improve patient outcomes and has high sensitivity and specificity. Systems using AI to⏎identify patient risk of disease must be held accountable to ensure that referral loops are closed. The value⏎of AI cannot be based solely on the accurate identification of disease risk. Value of AI must be assessed⏎based on whether or not patients identified with potential disease risk receive necessary care and follow⏎up.⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Ongoing education is critical for patients and doctors to understand both the limitations and potential uses⏎of AI in health care. Patients typically want reassurance that:⏎• Clinicians remain responsible for decisions⏎• AI recommendations are reviewed, explained, and contextualized⏎• Care remains individualized, not automated⏎Patients consistently express that trust resides in the clinician relationship, not in the technology itself.⏎We appreciate the efforts of HHS to better understand AI adoption challenges and we look forward to⏎continuing to work with HHS to expedite adoption of AI enabled devices that are trusted, valid and can⏎improve patient care. If additional information is needed, please contact Kara Webb at kcwebb@aoa.org⏎Sincerely,⏎Jacquie Bowen, O.D.⏎President, American Optometric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0217♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from DiCello, Donna, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔DiCello♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a licensed clinical psychologist, I am writing to you to insure that AI undergoes complete scrutiny and vetting for mental health issues (I would like to see AI eliminated for this use but that’s not the real world). When we are dealing with the delicate component of a person’s mental health, it is critical that people receive credentialed, competent care, which AI cannot provide; this is especially concerning when dealing with children and adolescents, our most vulnerable.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0218♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Vega Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Vega Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, M.D.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎220 C St SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dr. Keane,⏎Vega Health appreciates the opportunity to provide comments to the Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy’s/Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT’s⏎(ASTP/ONC’s) request for information on accelerating the adoption and use of clinical⏎AI. Vega Health supports the Administration’s efforts to make safe, secure, and effective⏎clinical AI more readily available to the healthcare industry. In our detailed comments⏎below, Vega Health will focus on the last mile between regulatory intent and real-world⏎integration of new technology into the healthcare ecosystem. Vega Health's business⏎model is dedicated to ensuring widespread health system access to the most effective,⏎validated AI solutions. We are deeply aligned with ASTP/ONC’s mission in this space,⏎and our leadership understands the barriers that exist to scale effective healthcare AI.⏎As the agency seeks to accelerate adoption and use of this technology, we specifically⏎urge ASTP/ONC to consider the following:⏎• The role of federal deregulation and harmonization efforts in accelerating⏎adoption of innovative clinical AI solutions;⏎• Pathways to accelerating clinical AI adoption through partnerships with state⏎sandbox initiatives and market-driven incentives;⏎• Approaches to overcoming the obstacles limiting clinical AI implementation at⏎every layer of the healthcare AI stack;⏎• Lessons learned from decades of clinical AI implementation and evaluation at⏎leading health systems around the U.S.;⏎⏎• Strategies to promote interoperability and eliminate information blocking⏎practices that inhibit innovation; and⏎• Approaches to clinical AI research that can meaningfully accelerate adoption for⏎health systems.⏎Vega Health appreciates ASTP/ONC’s commitment to accelerating the adoption of⏎clinical AI to drive better patient outcomes and improve the sustainability of American⏎healthcare.⏎I. Background on Vega Health⏎Vega Health is dedicated to helping healthcare organizations realize measurable value⏎from healthcare AI. We operate a proprietary platform that connects health systems with⏎a curated marketplace of validated AI solutions. We also provide comprehensive⏎integration support and objective performance monitoring of AI solutions. Our mission is⏎to be the objective, trusted partner equipping health systems everywhere as they scale⏎AI solutions that improve care and operational outcomes.⏎For health systems, Vega Health provides:⏎• A proprietary platform that equips healthcare organizations to efficiently run⏎multiple AI solutions within their digital environment;⏎• A curated marketplace of AI solutions validated in real-world healthcare settings;⏎• Expert development and implementation support;⏎• Rigorous measurement and monitoring of clinical outcomes and operational⏎impact; and⏎• Partnership in co-developing AI solutions optimized for local patient populations.⏎For healthcare innovators, Vega Health offers:⏎• Commercialization pathways to scale AI solutions beyond sites of origin;⏎• Distribution channels to reach health systems seeking validated solutions;⏎• Implementation expertise to ensure successful deployments in new⏎environments; and⏎• Transparent monitoring evaluating effectiveness in real-world settings.⏎Vega Health’s approach to integrating and monitoring AI solutions is built on more than⏎a decade of real-world experience at the Duke Institute for Health Innovation (DIHI) and⏎through the Health AI Partnership (HAIP). At DIHI, our CEO and co-founder Dr. Mark⏎Sendak worked to develop, integrate, and monitor more than 60 AI solutions in live⏎clinical settings at Duke Health. This work included the development of Sepsis Watch,⏎still considered one of the most successful real-world healthcare AI integrations as⏎documented in the peer-reviewed literature.⏎⏎The work of DIHI and HAIP established foundational frameworks that now guide⏎responsible AI adoption and monitoring across the healthcare industry, including widely⏎cited guidelines for clinical AI integration (the HEAAL framework). HAIP’s work to share⏎best practice frameworks and build an effective practice network beyond academic⏎medical centers – including community hospitals, Federally Qualified Health Centers⏎(FQHCs), and rural health systems – highlighted many of the practical challenges of AI⏎implementation across diverse environments. Vega Health exists to bridge these critical⏎gaps. Many healthcare organizations lack the infrastructure, expertise, and resources to⏎source, integrate, and evaluate the effectiveness of AI solutions. This creates a⏎systemic risk as AI adoption accelerates, threatening to widen a ‘digital divide’ where⏎one class of health systems leverages AI while a second group falls farther behind. We⏎applaud the ASTP/ONC’s work to reduce regulatory burden for innovators seeking to⏎assist these healthcare organizations and look forward to this and future opportunities to⏎engage with the agency and its leadership.⏎Vega Health’s business model operationalizes many of the frameworks promulgated by⏎DIHI and HAIP. Through our platform architecture and monitoring approach, we guide⏎health systems through objective evaluation of technical AI model performance, user⏎adoption and behavior change, and outcome measures. We are committed to acting as⏎an objective third party that provides unbiased performance data to both health systems⏎utilizing AI solutions, as well as the developers of those solutions, all with the goal of⏎advancing innovation.⏎Vega Health measures performance across four dimensions: technical accuracy/fidelity,⏎user adoption patterns, achievement of clinical/operational outcomes, and return on⏎investment. We implement comprehensive monitoring strategies which use real-world⏎evidence to determine whether an AI solution is delivering value in practice. This⏎approach ensures that performance is evaluated not only in aggregate, but across⏎patient subpopulations. Our team has extensive experience working in clinical settings⏎to evaluate and monitor real-world AI performance, rather than operating at the level of⏎theoretical frameworks.⏎II. Barriers to Innovation (Question 1)⏎Vega Health strongly supports efforts across the Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) to simplify the duplicative and burdensome regulatory thicket that is⏎currently holding back innovative healthcare solutions from scaling and delivering value.⏎Currently, the state AI law landscape is disjointed and daunting: As of this writing , 46⏎states have enacted 284 bills, and 2026 has already seen 771 bills introduced across⏎31 states. Despite federal efforts to limit the scope of state AI legislation, the current⏎landscape forces innovators to spend time navigating complex regulations.⏎⏎There are two well-established pathways for AI regulation: development-based⏎regulation and use-based regulation. As Andreessen Horowitz, the Consumer⏎Technology Association (CTA), and others have argued, layering regulations on the⏎manner in which AI models can be developed will only serve to increase compliance⏎costs, make it more expensive to develop and scale AI solutions in multiple states, and⏎create a digital ecosystem where only entrenched market incumbents have the scale,⏎budget, and legal team necessary to successfully scale AI solutions. By eliminating⏎regulatory burden and focusing enforcement on already-unlawful outcomes, particularly⏎in heavily regulated sectors like healthcare, the Administration will further incentivize⏎innovative solutions to broken processes that can reduce costs and improve health⏎outcomes for patients and providers.⏎As Andreessen notes in the organization’s Roadmap for Federal AI Legislation, state⏎and federal regulation must focus on harmful uses of AI, not preemptively impose⏎barriers that limit market access. Developer-facing regulations drastically extend the⏎technology development phase and extend the time to market entry, making it⏎significantly more difficult for fast-moving innovators to bring solutions to market and⏎challenge incumbents. A competitive health AI market drives overall improvements to⏎privacy, cybersecurity, and safety by forcing companies to compete to deliver the best⏎product – and does not preclude current or future regulations from heavily penalizing⏎realized harms, failures, or misuse.⏎This regulatory simplification is particularly important for new market entrants and⏎innovators. Vega Health’s business is built on the ability to scale the most effective AI⏎models – technologies developed by leading Academic Medical Centers and already⏎validated in clinical settings around the country – to solve complex problems for health⏎systems of all sizes. The Vega Health marketplace hosts models developed by clinical⏎experts that improve clinical and operational workflows for patients and providers. The⏎Vega Health marketplace already includes models to predict patient deterioration, post-⏎surgery complications, emergency department triage, and in-hospital mortality.⏎The recent HTI-5 Notice of Proposed Rulemaking (NPRM) takes important steps⏎towards simplifying and aligning the security, interoperability, and privacy requirements⏎broadly underpinning health IT. ASTP/ONC is helping to lay the groundwork for⏎widespread AI innovation, development, and scaled adoption in healthcare settings of⏎all sizes – including the agencies’ collective focus on ending anti-competitive practices⏎by industry incumbents.⏎⏎Vega Health applauds ASTP/ONC’s deliberate efforts to reduce the regulatory,⏎administrative, and invented hurdles that have been leveraged by large incumbents to⏎disadvantage new market entrants, particularly those contracted by health systems to⏎improve outcomes by leveraging data contained within the electronic health record.⏎Complex and conflicting regulations governing AI, privacy, and interoperability have⏎been leveraged by some certified health IT vendors to obstruct this data access or⏎impose undue financial burden to utilize this data.⏎III. Incentives (Question 2)⏎The healthcare sector has one of the highest adoption rates of commercial AI, led by a⏎71% adoption rate among health systems. At the physician level, the American Medical⏎Association reports broad belief that AI will improve efficiency and burnout, diagnostics,⏎clinical outcomes, and even patient safety.⏎However, the uptake of clinical AI – and its potential benefits – is not distributed equally⏎among health systems. While 53% of health systems report high success with AI scribes,⏎adoption remains sharply divided: 86% among system-affiliated hospitals versus just⏎37% among independent facilities. Rural hospitals lag even further, with only 56%⏎reporting any AI integration compared to 81% of urban hospitals.⏎Across the United States, regulatory sandboxes have emerged as a key strategy to⏎accelerate AI innovation under necessary guardrails and oversight mechanisms. To date,⏎Utah, Texas, and Delaware have developed AI sandboxes that include healthcare or⏎healthcare-adjacent sectors; notably, Utah also recently announced a major pilot program⏎with a health AI vendor to use AI to refill prescription medications.⏎Despite this transformative potential, major health systems have yet to widely⏎participate in AI sandbox initiatives, slowing adoption of clinical AI solutions. This is⏎largely due to the nature of healthcare AI regulation – providers subject to state and⏎federal laws need certainty on both to comfortably pilot innovative clinical solutions.⏎Apart from disclosure requirements and medical licensing protections, state AI and data⏎privacy laws primarily govern direct-to-consumer healthcare occurring outside of health⏎systems – leaving a large grey area for clinical AI solutions that may be subject to⏎federal regulations and guidance.⏎There is a degree of urgency to bringing providers into health AI pilots and sandbox⏎initiatives: Without direct participation from provider organizations, AI vendors may⏎divert patients from physicians and undermine the local practice of medicine.⏎⏎To increase healthcare provider participation in AI sandbox initiatives, there must be⏎incentives that reduce the regulatory risk inhibiting internal health system innovation.⏎The regulatory requirement imposing the most risk and burden on these health systems⏎is the Food and Drug Administration’s (FDA’s) Guidance Document on Clinical Decision⏎Support (CDS) Software, which establishes criteria under which CDS software is⏎regulated as a medical device. While recent updates expanded federal enforcement⏎discretion, the risk of device classification still dampens investment in health system AI⏎innovations. The FDA can partner with states in the design of certain healthcare-⏎focused sandbox agreements to provide health systems with more robust regulatory⏎relief; in exchange, the states can provide the FDA with real-world AI evaluation and⏎monitoring evidence for novel CDS applications as part of the contract framework for AI⏎sandbox participation.⏎The proposed framework would incentivize health system participation in AI sandbox⏎initiatives while advancing evidence-based AI policy nationally. By requiring safety and⏎efficacy evidence to be generated as part of sandbox participation, the states could offer⏎the FDA a way to close a crucial gap in post-implementation monitoring of clinical AI.⏎Finally, the guardrails around sandbox participation could significantly advance safety⏎measures, cybersecurity, and reporting requirements currently hindering adoption of⏎clinical AI. This partnership offers a pathway to accelerated AI innovation and adoption⏎in clinical settings, transforming care for a high volume of patients in traditional⏎healthcare settings.⏎Furthermore, Vega Health urges ASTP/ONC to consider how market-based incentives⏎shape AI innovation and evaluation – a stark contrast to top-down “model card” and pre-⏎deployment requirements.⏎Industry efforts to compile AI “model cards” containing common data elements have not⏎worked as intended. Vega Health’s Co-Founder and CEO published the first Model⏎Facts label for machine learning models used by healthcare systems. Since that time,⏎DIHI and HAIP have facilitated the adoption of Model Facts labels by health systems to⏎improve communication with clinical front-line users. In contrast to this bottom-up⏎approach, other efforts to centralize “model cards” have not effectively served end⏎users. Rather, many such efforts became a gatekeeping exercise, with well-resourced⏎industry groups founded by large model developers competing to set the terms of⏎market entry and protect their own incumbent technologies.⏎Federal standards for the use of AI can protect consumers, but it is a futile exercise to⏎predict how AI model performance on an ambiguous and evolving set of metrics will⏎translate to real-world performance across vastly different clinical settings. Meaningful⏎⏎regulatory action can best protect patients and consumers by requiring disclosure of⏎safety incidents and punishing actual harm. Vega Health strongly supports ASTP/ONC’s⏎effort to eliminate this restriction on competition by removing model cards from the⏎health IT certification criteria.⏎In the healthcare sector, health systems procure AI technologies to fulfill specific⏎business objectives across administrative, clinical, and quality workflows. In order to⏎remain in compliance with federal regulations, assess organizational success, and⏎ensure the delivery of quality patient care, health systems have the right to require⏎some degree of model transparency and performance evaluation. However, these⏎metrics, the manner of assessment, and the confidentiality of reporting should be⏎contractually defined on a case-by-case basis, not set by conflicted coalitions or used to⏎limit access to app markets.⏎This does emphasize the need for transparent, objective post-deployment model⏎evaluation and monitoring in healthcare becomes even more important. Rather than⏎adding a federal stamp of approval to the AI model evaluation metrics proposed by⏎special interest groups, ASTP/ONC should encourage health systems and end users to⏎determine the most impactful metrics. Well-established contracting principles will then⏎shape the market and hold developers accountable for delivering on promised⏎performance, just as it has for Software-as-a-Service (SaaS), medical equipment, and⏎countless other markets.⏎Additionally, model evaluation should advance model procurement, implementation,⏎deployment, and maintenance or sunset decisions by the end user, not present another⏎barrier to market entry. As ASTP/ONC has recognized, federal standards for evaluating⏎and reporting on AI performance are inflexible and unnecessarily burdensome. Not only⏎does the performance of healthcare AI models vary across patient populations and⏎payer mixes, but benchmark and threshold performance will vary by health system. It⏎makes far more sense for health systems to determine what the minimum acceptable⏎model performance should be for their system and evaluate on an ongoing basis than to⏎allow large incumbents to set model evolution criteria to limit market competition.⏎As the healthcare technology market moves away from AI gatekeepers and towards⏎real-world model evaluation and monitoring, Vega Health is confident that a market-⏎based framework driven by innovation will be able to replace the restrictive model card⏎requirements that ASTP/ONC proposes to remove.⏎IV. Implementation Challenges & Generating ROI (Questions 3 & 6)⏎⏎Vega Health has a unique perspective and expertise developed during more than a⏎decade of working directly with health systems to build, integrate, and scale effective AI⏎solutions. First at Duke, and now at multiple regional and multistate health systems,⏎Vega Health has seen a series of recurring implementation challenges at each level of⏎the emerging health AI stack.⏎Fundamentally, Vega Health categorizes the health AI stack into a series of layers: the⏎database layer, the connection layer, the data mapping layer, the solution layer, and the⏎observability & governance layer. At each stage of AI model or solution development⏎and deployment, several obstacles have prevented pilots from scaling and generating⏎true ROI for health systems.⏎At the database layer, interoperability challenges persist across real-time FHIR-enabled⏎data, data elements that still require HL7-based exchange (like Admissions, Discharge⏎and Transfer notifications), financial and revenue cycle data, and delayed data that is⏎stored in data lakes or tables. For example, Medication Administration Records (MAR)⏎are critical to acute clinical AI solutions, but this data element is not yet available via⏎FHIR. Vega Health supports ASPT/ONC’s proposal to add MAR data to USCDI v7. The⏎range of data sources and formats – as well as local differences in the database⏎architecture, EHR selection, and data-level vendors of each health system – make it⏎immensely challenging to translate AI models from one health system to another.⏎Models often rely on a set of input variables, which must be mapped to a unique set of⏎data elements and fields for each health system. Vega Health is encouraged by the⏎recent HTI-5 NPRM targeting some of the artificial barriers erected by EHR vendors to⏎access this data in service of health system innovation.⏎This data grouping process is time-intensive and prone to quality assurance failures.⏎For example, each time a health system records a lab or measurement in a different⏎unit or under a different name in the EHR, there must be a process in place to map that⏎new data element to the corresponding standardized input variable for an AI model – a⏎process that repeats for each model running in a health system, each with a potentially⏎unique set of variable names. It is easy for this process to slip, risking model drift and⏎flawed outputs. If a health system does not have a robust real-time evaluation and⏎monitoring mechanism in place, these failures can take weeks or even months to catch⏎– risking incorrect alerts and best and patient safety incidents at worst.⏎Evaluating and monitoring AI solutions pose additional challenges for even the most⏎technologically mature health systems. Each AI pilot or vendor reports on safety and⏎performance metrics in a different format. Accessing and reviewing myriad individual⏎dashboards and downloadable performance reports complicates real-time model⏎⏎performance and observability. Even with processes in place to review each model’s⏎reported metrics at a regular cadence, it can be nearly impossible for clinical or⏎technical leaders at health systems to catch and correct drift or deteriorating⏎performance in real time. Further complicating the challenge is a disconnect between⏎model performance reporting provided by vendors and the clinical outcomes and⏎workflows impacted by the model outputs. Without a standardized command and control⏎center, each performance report must be translated to determine whether model⏎performance issues affect time, accuracy, frequency, confidence, precision, or several⏎of these elements in the clinical setting. Healthcare AI needs a unified observability⏎layer that can scale with new solutions, whether provided by a third party or developed⏎internally by the health system.⏎Finally, as health systems across the United States pilot and deploy an ever-increasing⏎number of AI solutions from a range of vendors, cybersecurity challenges continue to⏎proliferate. Health systems need a simple way to pause models for evaluation, take⏎them offline temporarily to resolve security incidents, or even sunset models that⏎become vulnerable legacy systems or fail to perform at an acceptable level. Leaving AI⏎solutions connected to the most crucial data and clinical applications in a health system⏎is an unmitigated vulnerability; as early AI pilots wind down and health systems make⏎long-term vendor selections, securely sunsetting AI solutions will become a key security⏎process in the coming years.⏎HHS must help health systems ensure that rapid innovation in clinical AI does not⏎exacerbate cybersecurity challenges facing health systems. Valuable patient data,⏎complex digital supply chains and internet-connected devices, costly and legacy⏎technology, and status as critical infrastructure make hospitals and health systems a top⏎target for cyber criminals and foreign threat actors. One 2025 report found that 93% of⏎health systems experienced a cyber incident over a 12-month period, and healthcare⏎data breaches are among the costliest incidents each year. The scale of this challenge⏎is immense, and the rapid development and implementation of internet-connected AI⏎technology in critical clinical settings does carry some risk. In addition to the technical⏎solution described above, HHS can provide technical assistance to help health systems⏎mitigate cyber risk from clinical AI, incorporate cybersecurity into emergency⏎preparedness funding programs like the Hospital Preparedness Program, and update⏎cybersecurity best practices – like the 405(d) Program – to include guidelines for AI⏎cybersecurity.⏎V. Evaluating Clinical AI (Questions 4 & 5)⏎Evaluating clinical AI is a unique challenge at the heart of more widespread adoption.⏎The earliest wave of health AI adoption was across administrative functions, with⏎⏎evaluation metrics similar to standard SaaS platforms that have been implemented and⏎evaluated in healthcare back offices for the better part of a decade. In the clinical⏎setting, healthcare’s digital maturity has long lagged other sectors like finance,⏎manufacturing, and technology. Now, as the adoption of clinical AI rapidly accelerates,⏎healthcare is leapfrogging other sectors that have traditionally led the maturity curve in⏎both AI spending and adoption rates.⏎However, healthcare’s longtime laggard status can provide immense benefit in the⏎evaluation of clinical AI. The tenets of technical AL/ML model performance and⏎optimization have already been well-established in both literature and practice. These⏎technical principles are largely unchanged in the clinical setting. One key lesson learned⏎from the use of AI/ML in advanced manufacturing is that feedback loops must link⏎telemetry to outcomes of interest. In a clinical setting, this means that adjustments to⏎model performance telemetry – accuracy, precision, sensitivity – must translate to⏎measurable changes in target clinical metrics, such as patient outcomes, quality⏎measures, response rates, or time to action.⏎In order to ensure that model telemetry is closely and accurately linked to clinical⏎outcomes, health AI should follow an implementation, testing, and deployment process⏎grounded in safety and rigorous evaluation. Vega Health’s model is built on licensing⏎clinically validated health AI models from leading health systems and deploying them in⏎health systems around the country. However, models that have been evaluated on one⏎data set and one health system’s unique patient mix are not guaranteed to operate⏎identically in another health system’s environment, or on a demographically different⏎patient population. Therefore, Vega Health has built an implementation process around⏎retrospective evaluation to validate performance, a silent pilot on real-time data from the⏎client patient population to tune telemetry, and ongoing real-time evaluation and⏎monitoring after go-live.⏎Additionally, clinical model performance must be measurable down to the individual⏎physician or end user level. Technical performance metrics in a vacuum – or even in⏎aggregate system-wide – do not fully convey whether a model is being appropriately⏎integrated into workflows and utilized by end users. Clinicians must understand how to⏎interpret and interact with model outputs, and the degree of success that a model⏎achieves in that regard is a crucial component of any evaluation.⏎Vega Health applauds HHS and ASTP/ONC for their time and attention towards⏎incentivizing and promoting AI capable of safely and securely improving patient⏎outcomes, reducing the cost of care, and enhancing clinical workflows. To best support⏎⏎the refinement of clinical AI evaluation science and adoption, HHS should draw lessons⏎from some of the most successful private sector models currently in operation.⏎We must ensure that widespread digital maturity and AI adoption across the healthcare⏎sector does not exacerbate a digital divide between the best-resourced academic⏎medical centers, and the community health centers that serve a high percentage of the⏎U.S. population. The leading academic medical centers and health systems in the⏎United States are centers of innovation and early adopters capable of developing and⏎piloting transformative technologies. However, these systems are able to innovate and⏎experiment due to a degree of financial security that most health systems lack. Financial⏎headwinds, poor payer mix, and shrinking margins have left many healthcare providers⏎– particularly rural and Critical Access Hospitals – without the luxury of launching⏎innovation hubs, incubators, and competitive pilot programs.⏎Vega Health co-founder and CEO Mark Sendak has been at the forefront of addressing⏎this challenge through the Health AI Partnership, a multi-stakeholder collaborative that⏎draws on educational materials, guidance, policies, and evaluations from leading U.S.⏎health systems to eliminate the digital divide and build AI capacity in the long tail of⏎healthcare.⏎Vega Health operates under these same principles to both accelerate innovation at⏎cutting-edge health systems, and make those same AI technologies available⏎affordably, safely, and securely for health systems and hospitals across the United⏎States. HHS should invest in hub-and-spoke programs that scale localized innovation⏎and evaluations to elevate the quality and reduce the cost of care nationally. Similar to⏎the design of the Rural Health Transformation Program, HHS should design grant⏎funding or competitive programs that incentivize AI evaluation and innovation to⏎incorporate rural, critical access, and community health providers. Taxpayer-funded⏎innovation should broadly benefit the American people, not simply reimburse innovation⏎that is already funded. For example, HHS could require that models evaluated on⏎specific patient populations or designed in leading health systems are easily scalable or⏎have distribution strategies that will make them broadly accessible and accelerate⏎adoption nationally.⏎Finally, the ROI used to evaluate clinical AI worth scaling must be tied to clinical⏎outcomes. The Vega Health Marketplace contains models that have been evaluated in⏎clinical settings across several use cases, including:⏎• Behavioral emergency and workplace violence⏎• Cardiac deterioration⏎⏎• ED triage to inpatient beds⏎• ED triage to ICU beds⏎• ICU patient hand-off⏎• Low-risk pulmonary embolism for hospital discharge⏎• Mortality risk during encounter⏎• Post-surgical complication risk prediction⏎• Sepsis⏎• Unanticipated deterioration to ICU⏎• 48-hour mortality⏎At Duke Health and other health systems, these solutions have driven real-world⏎improvements in clinician efficiency, quality of patient care, timeliness of patient care,⏎and improved management of health system bed capacity.⏎Effective real-world monitoring requires moving beyond narrow technical metrics to⏎assess AI performance across multiple, interconnected dimensions. Based on our⏎experience, Vega Health is committed to a four-dimensional monitoring framework that⏎captures whether AI solutions deliver sustained value in practice, and we urge⏎ASTP/ONC to continue removing regulatory barriers across the following components of⏎evaluation and monitoring.⏎Technical Accuracy and Model Fidelity. Technical performance remains the foundation⏎of AI monitoring, but real-world technical assessment differs substantially from pre-⏎market validation. In dynamic clinical environments, Vega Health monitors for model⏎drift, including changes in model inputs, outputs, or the relationship between them that⏎signal potential degradation. Monitoring data quality of model inputs is particularly⏎important. Vega Health utilizes the Machine Learning-Data Quality Assurance (ML-⏎DQA) framework developed at DIHI to assess completeness, conformance, and⏎plausibility of all model inputs. Finally, Vega Health conducts retrospective and⏎prospective evaluation of AI solutions in a secure development environment before⏎going live in a clinical setting, ensuring model performance is satisfactory and⏎preventing safety incidents.⏎User Adoption and Clinical Integration⏎Technical accuracy means very little if clinicians or end-users do not use AI tools⏎appropriately, or if the tools disrupt established clinical workflows. Vega Health⏎measures override rates, time-to-action on AI-generated alerts, and patterns of⏎engagement that reveal whether tools provide value or generate alert fatigue to reveal⏎critical insights that technical monitoring may miss. For example, high override rates⏎⏎may indicate low positive predictive value prompting alarm fatigue, or they may reveal⏎workflow misalignment and/or insufficient user training. The infrastructure to support⏎adoption monitoring includes integration with clinical workflow systems to capture⏎interaction data, mechanisms for collecting structured user feedback, and analytics⏎capabilities that can identify patterns across different clinical contexts and user groups.⏎Clinical and Operational Outcomes⏎The third monitoring dimension assesses whether AI systems achieve their intended⏎clinical and operational objectives. Technical accuracy and user adoption are⏎necessary, but insufficient. The central question is whether an AI solution improves⏎patient outcomes or organizational performance. This requires connecting AI integration⏎to downstream outcomes. Measuring outcome impact presents methodological⏎challenges, and the metrics tracked will vary significantly based on the nature of the AI⏎solution or AI-enabled device. By integrating AI performance data with electronic health⏎record (EHR) data and operational systems, Vega Health tracks patient outcomes,⏎operational efficiency metrics, and financial performance in relation to AI implementation⏎and usage patterns.⏎Return on Investment and Value Realization⏎The business case for AI monitoring itself becomes clear: without systematic⏎measurement, healthcare delivery organizations cannot distinguish successful⏎investments from expensive failures. On the benefit side, we seek to track both tangible⏎returns, and harder-to-quantify improvements such as clinician satisfaction, faster⏎decision-making, and reduced cognitive burden. ROI monitoring must account for the⏎full lifecycle of AI implementation. Initial investments in integration and training may⏎exceed short-term benefits, making it critical to track value realization over appropriate⏎time horizons. We have observed that AI solutions often deliver value differently than⏎anticipated – a tool designed to improve diagnostic accuracy may deliver value through⏎workflow efficiency gains, or vice versa. Comprehensive monitoring captures these⏎unexpected benefits while identifying solutions that fail to deliver value.⏎Comprehensive Monitoring in Practice⏎The four dimensions outlined above operate interdependently. Technical drift may⏎manifest first as changing adoption patterns or declining outcomes. Poor workflow⏎integration may appear as low adoption rates or negative user feedback before⏎impacting clinical outcomes. Effective monitoring requires tracking all dimensions, with⏎specific metrics tuned to the specific use case, and governance structures that can⏎⏎synthesize insights across them to inform decisions about continuing, adapting, or⏎sunsetting an AI solution.⏎The monitoring framework described above is not aspirational. It is derived from⏎practical implementation experience and refined through implementation across diverse⏎healthcare settings. The question is not whether comprehensive monitoring is⏎necessary, but how health systems will build or access the infrastructure and expertise⏎to do it effectively. As ASTP/ONC forges ahead with a new approach to regulating⏎health IT, including novel AI applications in clinical and administrative settings, it is⏎essential that the Assistant Secretary understand current capabilities and best⏎practices. Deregulatory efforts do not make patients less safe or health systems less⏎prepared; rather, they allow innovative AI solutions to take cost out of care, improve⏎broken processes, and put patient outcomes first.⏎VI. Promoting Interoperability (Question 8)⏎In the recent HTI-5 NPRM, ASTP/ONC rightly acknowledges continued reports of⏎leading EHR vendors leveraging scale and market dominance in the database and data⏎management layer to gain a competitive advantage in the application layer. These⏎actions amount to a weaponization of health IT certification standards and processes to⏎stifle innovation and competition, to the detriment of the broader healthcare ecosystem⏎and patients. EHR vendors have made it technically difficult and costly for health⏎systems themselves to access and use health data generated by their providers and⏎patients. These obstacles are amplified for health systems that want to utilize additional⏎vendors bringing innovative technologies like AI. Nearly two decades ago, lawmakers⏎required and funded the adoption of EHRs across healthcare under the expectation that⏎an interoperable database layer would accelerate the pace of innovation, improve the⏎quality of care for patients, and make healthcare data usable. HTI-5 has the potential to⏎bring that promise closer to reality by ending these practices.⏎Today, EHRs operate not only as health record databases but as healthcare application⏎developers. In reality, this has created a fundamentally misaligned incentive where the⏎technology vendors controlling health system data are granted broad leeway to impose⏎technical barriers, access fees, and administrative requirements on their competitors.⏎These practices stifle technological innovation in healthcare, drive up the cost of new⏎technologies for patients and providers, and contribute to the inflationary effect of⏎technology in healthcare.⏎Vega Health is encouraged by HHS’s announced crackdown on information blocking.⏎The loosely defined nature of information blocking exceptions has allowed well-financed⏎actors to simply reject interoperability requests and force lengthy, costly litigation.⏎⏎Information blocking was intended to make interoperability the default state, with actors⏎required to prove a request fell under an exception. Instead, dominant incumbents have⏎abused exceptions to the rule to require innovative patient- and provider-facing⏎technologies to prove that a request should be fulfilled.⏎In the absence of HHS enforcement actions to establish precedent and better define⏎exceptions, certified health IT vendors have shifted the burden and cost of proving⏎information blocking to innovators. Vega Health strongly supports efforts to eliminate or⏎tighten frequently abused exception-based information blocking loopholes.⏎Over the past decade, it has also become apparent that FHIR APIs are a crucial step⏎towards healthcare interoperability and standardized data exchange. FHIR-based data⏎exchange provides a roadmap for the kind of real-time data exchange necessary to⏎build meaningful AI and machine learning applications that leverage structured and⏎unstructured data in healthcare. As ASTP/ONC has frequently acknowledged, the future⏎of healthcare interoperability and data usability will be built on FHIR standards.⏎As ASTP/ONC continues to develop both the FHIR standard for data exchange and the⏎US Core for Data Interoperability (USCDI) definitions of crucial data elements, Vega⏎Health encourages policymakers to prioritize standardizing elements essential to the⏎implementation of AI/ML solutions in health systems. From experience implementing AI⏎solutions at multiple health systems, Vega Health has recognized that Medication⏎Administration Records are crucial inputs for commonly used clinical AI solutions, such⏎as inpatient deterioration or emergency department likelihood to admit predictive⏎models. Today, MAR data must be accessed from external databases, through custom⏎API infrastructure, or via HL7 data feeds – each approach complicating model⏎implementation and usability. Vega Health is encouraged by the inclusion of MAR data⏎in the draft USCDI v7 and encourages ASTP/ONC to continue expanding the set of⏎standard data elements as a key measure to accelerating the implementation and use⏎of clinical AI solutions.⏎Additionally, for FHIR APIs to sustain and scale health AI innovation, a few key⏎roadblocks must be resolved. In the HTI-5 NPRM, ASTP/ONC proposed a key⏎regulatory shift in the rulemaking authority derived from the 21st Century Cures Act and⏎sets the foundation for future API-based certification criteria. Vega Health applauds this⏎shift and encourages ASTP/ONC to use these criteria to set baseline technical⏎standards for APIs that can address some of the information blocking practices⏎described above. Vega Health recommends that ASTP/ONC take the following⏎suggestions under consideration when developing API-based certification criteria:⏎⏎Vega Health urges ASTP/ONC to require reasonable, market-based pricing for⏎data queries. Often, certified health IT vendors restrict the use of FHIR APIs by placing⏎prohibitive user fees or per-query fees on developers. These prices far exceed any API⏎development or maintenance costs that the certified health IT vendor may incur and⏎instead are instituted to suppress and restrict innovative technologies that a health⏎system may seek to deploy. The changes to the manner exception in the HTI-5 NPRM⏎acknowledge this challenge broadly, but ASTP/ONC should preemptively eliminate the⏎same practices as they apply to API query fees specifically.⏎Vega Health urges ASTP/ONC to place limits on the ability of certified health IT⏎vendors to cap the number of queries or flow of data through FHIR APIs. It should⏎not be up to data-layer technologies that health systems are required to utilize by⏎federal law to set limits on the ways in which health systems and their chosen vendors⏎can utilize or access system data. Complicating the picture, many certified health IT⏎vendors develop their own applications that are in direct competition with outside⏎vendors; the ability to place restrictions on competitors that do not extend to their own⏎enterprise solutions gives certified health IT an anticompetitive advantage and creates⏎misaligned incentives, limiting innovation and competition. While some limits may be⏎technically necessary, these limits should be set via documented API specifications,⏎health system or end user throughput limitations and cost containment, or contractual⏎agreement, not imposed inconsistently by certified health IT to gain a competitive⏎advantage.⏎Vega Health urges ASTP/ONC to establish and reinforce “right to connect” and⏎“right to build” permissions for health systems using certified health IT. As part of⏎API-based Health IT Certification Program criteria, ASTP/ONC should ensure that⏎health systems have the ability to access and utilize FHIR APIs for AI implementations⏎and development projects. Health systems have frequently encountered obstacles to⏎internal AI development or real-time data analysis when specific data elements are⏎unavailable through required certified health IT APIs.⏎Beyond common sets of data elements required by law or embedded into standards like⏎the US Core for Data Interoperability, certified health IT vendors place restrictions or⏎add multiple burdensome hurdles to prevent health systems and their chosen vendors⏎from building custom APIs that fully utilize health system data. These barriers include⏎contractual language that limits the ability of AI solution vendors to employ or sub-⏎contract domain experts with the relevant expertise to effectively integrate AI solutions⏎into legacy health IT systems. Legacy health IT incumbents are limiting innovation and⏎competition by preventing new entrants from being able to bring in the relevant⏎expertise to expand AI use in healthcare. This is antithetical to innovation,⏎⏎anticompetitive, and an artificial inhibitor of AI innovation and ROI. A universal “right to⏎build” permission would allow health systems and contracted vendors to take full⏎advantage of FHIR APIs to unlock lower costs and improved patient outcomes through⏎digital development.⏎Vega Health urges ASTP/ONC to set standard FHIR API configurations that are⏎publicly available to innovators and entrepreneurs. By shifting the Health IT⏎Certification Program to API-based criteria, ASTP/ONC has the opportunity to eliminate⏎the lack of standardization and technical hurdles that slow and complicate⏎implementation projects, adding unnecessary cost to healthcare. Uniform, universal API⏎documentation will accelerate innovation, make data and analytics insights more widely⏎accessible for lower-resourced health systems, and create more resilient healthcare⏎data systems for a more connected future state of healthcare.⏎VII. Clinical AI Research (Question 10)⏎In order to accelerate the adoption of clinical AI, HHS should prioritize simplifying the⏎process for and reducing the cost of testing new AI models quickly across diverse⏎patient populations and clinical settings. The FDA’s process for reviewing new drugs⏎and medical devices is costly and time-intensive, largely due to challenges with⏎designing clinical trials, identifying trial participants, and conducting trials either on-⏎premise or from a decentralized hub. However, testing AI solutions – systems built on⏎algorithm-based models that can be quickly tested on large datasets and tuned to⏎achieve desired results – can and should look fundamentally different.⏎Safety is a top concern for clinical AI applications. Whenever a new technology⏎influences clinician decisions and patient outcomes, it is imperative that performance is⏎predictable, controllable, and reliable. In order to ensure clinical AI safety and⏎performance, HHS should invest in research that improves both the timeliness and⏎representativeness of AI evaluation and testing.⏎Another significant consideration for the testing of new AI solutions is patient privacy.⏎Federal and state law protects the privacy of medical information, and patients have a⏎reasonable expectation that their data will not be exposed to researchers or used to⏎train or evaluate AI models without their consent.⏎Vega Health’s unique approach to AI development and implementation places models⏎directly into health system networks, limiting data exfiltration and making patient records⏎invisible to model developers and researchers. By standardizing data element and⏎variable definitions, Vega Health accelerates AI model pilots in clinical settings. Any⏎health system that has implemented the Vega Health Platform can quickly connect new⏎⏎AI models, regardless of where the model was developed or what variable definition the⏎model uses. Once a model is implemented onto the Vega Health Platform, it could be⏎evaluated through shadow pilots on real-world patient cohorts in a wide range of clinical⏎settings – all without ever exposing patient data to researchers, developers, or anyone⏎outside of the health system where the patient already receives care. This process⏎would also give health systems more autonomy and insight into how AI models are⏎tested – by giving health systems the ability to determine which models to evaluate,⏎consent to evaluation on their data, and provide visibility into any testing, HHS can⏎rapidly accelerate the pace of clinical AI research.⏎The feasibility and effectiveness of these silent trials has been established by⏎researchers at leading institutions, including Vega Health’s co-founder and CEO. HHS⏎should leverage these learnings and invest in increasing access to shadow pilots that⏎protect patient privacy, ensure model safety and performance before go-live, and⏎accelerate the pace of adoption of AI models in clinical settings across the country.⏎VIII. Conclusion⏎Vega Health appreciates ASTP/ONC’s commitment to accelerating the use of safe,⏎effective clinical AI solutions in healthcare. A whole-of-HHS approach will be necessary⏎to effectively reduce barriers to adoption and incentivize innovation that improves⏎patient outcomes and reduces the cost of delivering quality healthcare. If Vega Health⏎can be a resource, please do not hesitate to reach out to Vega Health co-founder and⏎CEO Mark Sendak (Mark.Sendak@vegahealth.com).⏎Sincerely,⏎Mark Sendak, MD, MPP⏎Co-founder and CEO, Vega Health♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0219♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Scientix.ai Inc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Scientix.ai Inc♔♔♔♔♔♔106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎PUBLIC COMMENT⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎RFI Title Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Docket ID HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Respondent Scientix.ai Inc.⏎Contact Bo Lora (bolora@scientix.ai)⏎Date February 20, 2026⏎Introduction⏎Scientix.ai Inc. is a Delaware C-Corp specializing in data infrastructure for regulated industries⏎like healthcare & life sciences. Our leadership includes veterans of Amazon Web Services⏎(AWS) and the pharmaceutical sector who specialize in resolving the structural friction that⏎prevents clinical organizations from extracting value from their data. We appreciate the⏎opportunity to provide input on this RFI and respond below to the questions where our⏎operational experience is most directly relevant.⏎Our perspective is grounded in a consistent observation across engagements: the primary⏎barriers to clinical AI adoption are infrastructural, not algorithmic. The AI models⏎themselves have reached impressive capability thresholds. What remains broken is the data⏎substrate they depend on, the fragmented systems, informal workarounds, and the absence of⏎deterministic validation layers that clinical AI requires to earn clinician trust.⏎Question #1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The biggest barriers to private-sector innovation are not algorithmic—they are infrastructural.⏎Three systemic forces constrain AI adoption in clinical care:⏎1. The Compounding Cost of System Integration⏎Current “application-first” paradigms treat data as a secondary byproduct of siloed software.⏎This creates a compounding integration burden where the complexity and cost of connecting⏎disparate systems consume the capital and time intended for innovation. Research consistently⏎finds that data workers spend over 40% of their time searching for, cleaning, and reconciling⏎data rather than analyzing it.[1] Vendors often protect proprietary data structures, leading to lock-⏎in and a fragmented ecosystem that is nearly impossible to maintain.⏎2. The Gap Between Documented Workflows and Operational Reality⏎Page 1⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Most AI implementations are built on how IT departments believe data flows (the documented⏎workflow), which rarely reflects operational reality. Clinical and administrative staff frequently⏎bypass official systems using unregulated spreadsheets and manual workarounds to bridge⏎technical gaps. When AI models are trained on the official data pathway alone, they fail to⏎generalize in real clinical environments—producing outputs that are technically plausible but⏎operationally wrong.⏎3. The Trust Gap: Probabilistic Risk in Deterministic Care⏎Clinical care demands deterministic accountability—the ability to trace exactly which data⏎informed a decision and verify that it was correct. Purely probabilistic AI (such as large language⏎models) cannot provide this traceability on its own. In high-stakes environments, non-⏎deterministic behavior creates a trust gap: hallucinations and inconsistent outputs are not⏎merely inconvenient, they are potentially dangerous. Most current architectures lack a⏎deterministic validation layer—a verifiable record of truth that enforces data integrity (exact⏎timestamps, immutable records) before probabilistic insights are applied on top.⏎We address these barriers by helping organizations transition from siloed, application-centric⏎architectures to context-first data infrastructure built on open standards (such as NGSI-LD),⏎where institutional knowledge becomes a persistent, governed asset rather than a byproduct of⏎any single vendor’s software.⏎Question #2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎HHS should prioritize regulatory and programmatic shifts that address the integration burden⏎and trust gap described above. This requires updating legacy frameworks that were designed⏎for static software, not adaptive AI systems.⏎Regulatory Priorities⏎Modernize HIPAA for AI-era data governance (45 C.F.R. Parts 160 and 164): Current de-⏎identification and re-identification rules under § 164.514 should be updated to facilitate real-⏎time, automated data governance that prevents silos without compromising patient privacy. As⏎AI systems require richer longitudinal datasets, the rules should clarify how organizations can⏎responsibly prepare data for model training while maintaining HIPAA compliance.⏎Expand algorithmic transparency under HTI-1 (45 C.F.R. § 170.315(b)(11)): The HTI-1 Final⏎Rule’s transparency requirements for Predictive Decision Support Interventions are a strong⏎foundation. HHS should expand these to include mandatory reporting of serious adverse events⏎attributed to AI-driven clinical recommendations, building on the existing 31 source attribute⏎disclosure requirements.⏎Establish deterministic validation safe harbors: Create “qualified AI” safe harbor principles⏎for tools that meet rigorous, HHS-endorsed standards for transparency and deterministic⏎Page 2⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎validation. This would mitigate liability risks for clinicians using AI as intended and incentivize⏎developers to build verifiable systems rather than opaque ones.⏎Payment Policy Reforms⏎Temporary Medicare payment codes for AI-enabled interventions: The traditional fee-for-⏎service model disincentivizes AI adoption because efficiency-driven tools (those reducing⏎hospitalizations or shortening visits) can lower short-term billable services. Temporary payment⏎codes—similar to those proposed for rural hospital access—would allow AI-enabled clinical⏎interventions to be reimbursed while evidence of long-term value accumulates.⏎Strengthen human-in-the-loop requirements for medical necessity (42 C.F.R. §⏎422.101(c)): CMS’s 2024 FAQs clarified that Medicare Advantage medical necessity⏎determinations cannot rely solely on algorithmic outputs without considering individual patient⏎circumstances. HHS should codify this principle more explicitly, ensuring that automated prior⏎authorization systems include meaningful clinical review rather than defaulting to algorithmic⏎denials.⏎Programmatic Design⏎Expand shared infrastructure through OneHHS: HHS should build shared, cloud-agnostic⏎infrastructure that allows agencies like the FDA, CDC, and CMS to reuse validated AI⏎components and standardized data models. This reduces duplicative investment and lowers the⏎barrier to entry for smaller innovators.⏎Fund operational readiness assessments before implementation: R&D programs should⏎incentivize organizations to map their actual operational workflows—including informal⏎workarounds and data gaps—before committing federal capital to AI software procurement.⏎Without this diagnostic step, AI implementations are built on faulty assumptions about how data⏎actually flows.⏎Page 3⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Summary of Priority CFR Revisions⏎Regulation Citation Recommended Change⏎HIPAA Security Rule 45 C.F.R. § 164.308 Update to include AI-specific risk analysis and data-⏎lineage requirements.⏎Health IT Certification 45 C.F.R. § Expand transparency requirements to include⏎(HTI-1) 170.315(b)(11) mandatory adverse event reporting for AI-driven⏎clinical recommendations.⏎Medicare Advantage 42 C.F.R. § Codify the requirement that algorithmic prior⏎422.101(c) authorizations include human review of individual⏎clinical circumstances.⏎HIPAA Privacy Rule 45 C.F.R. § 164.514 Clarify de-identification standards for AI training⏎datasets, enabling responsible real-time data⏎governance.⏎Question #6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded⏎performance and cost expectations and where have they fallen short?⏎Where AI Has Exceeded Expectations⏎Medical imaging and triage: AI has significantly outperformed expectations in diagnostic⏎imaging. A peer-reviewed Cedars-Sinai study found that AI-augmented radiological triage⏎reduced hospital length of stay for pulmonary embolism by 26.3%.[3] AI-driven triage of patient⏎portal messages reduced median clinician review time for high-acuity content from 22 hours to⏎approximately 5 hours.[4]⏎Administrative automation: Automated billing and scheduling represent the fastest-growing⏎use case. In 2026, 98% of surveyed health executives expect at least 10% cost savings from AI,⏎with 37% targeting savings above 20%.[2] Early adopters of agentic AI—systems that coordinate⏎tasks across billing, care delivery, and payments—report disproportionate productivity gains.[8]⏎Ambient documentation: Generative AI scribes have reduced the documentation burden on⏎clinicians, directly addressing one of the primary drivers of provider burnout.[9]⏎Where AI Has Fallen Short⏎Shallow clinical reasoning: Many tools optimize for drafting notes rather than improving⏎decisions. They frequently lack the longitudinal context (historical diagnoses, labs, patterns⏎across encounters) needed for effective value-based care.⏎Correction burden on clinicians: Clinicians report spending up to three hours per week⏎correcting or bypassing AI outputs due to missing context or misinterpreted patient data.[5] This⏎erodes the efficiency gains that AI was intended to deliver.⏎Page 4⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Bias and evaluation gaps: While 65% of hospitals use predictive models, less than half⏎evaluate these tools for potential bias.[6] Off-the-shelf products may not reflect local patient⏎populations, creating a quiet disparity in care quality.⏎Hallucination and misinformation: Healthcare-specific AI models still maintain hallucination⏎rates estimated at 10–30%. Research published in The Lancet Digital Health in February 2026⏎revealed that leading language models can repeat false medical claims as truth when the⏎misinformation is wrapped in realistic clinical language.[7]⏎Novel Tools with Greatest Potential⏎The greatest potential lies in shifting from passive automation to context-aware, agentic⏎systems: AI that autonomously gathers documentation from multiple sources, validates it⏎against clinical requirements, and presents verified recommendations—rather than AI that⏎simply generates text for human review. Equally promising are predictive remote monitoring⏎tools (AI-powered wearables detecting early warning signs before escalation) and infrastructure⏎layers that enforce data integrity at the point of creation, ensuring AI always reasons from⏎verified facts rather than fragmented snapshots.⏎Question #7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles?⏎While clinical AI champions, C-suite leaders (CIO/CDO/CEO), and multidisciplinary AI⏎committees each play roles in adoption decisions, our experience across regulated⏎environments reveals a more fundamental constraint that shapes all of these: the executive⏎digital fluency gap.⏎The Leadership Fluency Gap⏎The most profound and overlooked administrative hurdle in 2026 is not technical—it is the gap⏎between what organizational leaders think AI is and what it actually requires to succeed. While⏎the vast majority of healthcare organizations recognize AI’s importance, only about half of IT⏎leaders feel confident in their implementation abilities. This stems from an executive mental⏎model that treats AI as a passive software tool (purchase it, install it, benefit from it) rather than⏎an adaptive system that requires ongoing governance, data infrastructure, and organizational⏎redesign.⏎This fluency gap creates specific, observable pathologies:⏎Perpetual pilot programs: Leaders lacking digital fluency invest in disconnected, application-⏎first pilots. Each pilot adds another point-to-point integration, making the eventual cost of⏎connection higher than the innovation itself. The result is an ever-growing portfolio of⏎“successful” pilots that never scale.⏎Page 5⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Ignoring operational reality: Administrative focus remains on documenting official system⏎workflows while ignoring the informal workarounds (unregulated spreadsheets, manual data⏎transfers) that staff actually use. AI implementations built on the official workflow fail in practice⏎because they are trained on a fiction.⏎Undersized technical support: While 40% of IT leaders report staff are unequipped for AI⏎adoption, leadership frequently underestimates the need for specialized roles (information⏎architects, data governance leads) whose absence ensures that AI tools operate on unreliable⏎data.⏎The Role of HHS⏎HHS should prioritize programs that address this fluency gap directly. Profession-specific AI⏎competency frameworks would ensure that decision-makers understand not just the potential of⏎AI, but the infrastructure, governance, and organizational change it requires. Programmatic⏎design should reward organizations that demonstrate operational readiness—the ability to⏎produce a complete audit trail and documented alignment between clinical and technical⏎teams—before allocating federal capital to AI implementation.⏎Question #8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?⏎Interoperability is the single highest-leverage intervention for accelerating clinical AI. The current⏎landscape of fragmented “digital islands” forces every AI implementation to solve the same data⏎reconciliation problem independently—consuming resources that should go toward innovation.⏎Widening Market Opportunities⏎When data flows through standardized interfaces, specialized AI tools can be deployed across⏎different health systems without the prohibitive cost of custom integrations. This shifts the⏎market from monolithic vendor platforms to a modular ecosystem where best-in-class tools⏎compete on merit. Standardized APIs (including FHIR for clinical data exchange and context-⏎aware standards like NGSI-LD for metadata-rich environments) lower the barrier to entry for⏎smaller innovators, enabling startups to plug into enterprise health systems without rebuilding⏎data pipelines from scratch.⏎Fueling Research⏎Interoperability transforms research from retrospective data reconstruction to real-time, high-⏎fidelity analysis. Linking disparate signals (genomic markers, environmental exposures,⏎symptoms) into unified data models enables rare disease diagnostics that are impossible in⏎siloed databases. Standardized data collection (using CDISC standards for clinical trials, FHIR⏎for clinical data) ensures real-world data is born ready for analysis. Federated learning⏎models—where AI is trained across multiple institutions without moving raw patient data—⏎depend entirely on interoperable infrastructure to function.⏎Page 6⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Critical Priorities⏎Category Key Priority⏎Data Types Multimodal integration: combining EHRs, claims, genomic data, and real-time⏎biometric data from wearables into unified patient models.⏎Data Standards FHIR for clinical exchange; context-rich standards (NGSI-LD, JSON-LD) for⏎metadata-intensive environments where provenance and accuracy are first-⏎class attributes.⏎Benchmarking Agent-oriented benchmarks (e.g., MedAgentBench) that measure AI’s ability⏎to perform complex, multi-step clinical tasks—not just medical knowledge⏎tests.⏎Data Quality Data-quality-at-source protocols: ensuring every data point is findable,⏎accessible, interoperable, and reusable (FAIR) at the moment of creation.⏎Question #10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research that bridges the gap between symbolic reasoning (ontologies,⏎structured knowledge) and probabilistic modeling (large language models). Published findings⏎consistently show that clinical AI adoption fails when it lacks the deterministic validation layer⏎required to prevent hallucinations and performance degradation in high-stakes environments.⏎1. Ontological Harmonization⏎The primary bottleneck in clinical AI is not model capability but data fragmentation. Research⏎into hybrid mapping systems—coupling classical lexical matchers with LLMs—has shown⏎substantial improvements in aligning complex medical ontologies. HHS should fund research⏎into scalable methods for resolving semantic inconsistencies across EHR systems, claims⏎databases, and research repositories, ensuring that AI models reason from consistent⏎definitions rather than conflicting data.⏎2. Agentic AI with Deterministic Grounding⏎Research should advance AI systems that autonomously plan and execute clinical tasks⏎(agentic AI) while remaining anchored in verifiable data. The ARPA-H ADVOCATE program⏎offers a promising model for “supervisory agents” that ensure patient-facing AI remains safe and⏎aligned with clinical guidelines. Additional priorities include transforming clinical practice⏎guidelines into machine-readable constraints and developing uncertainty quantification methods⏎for real-time physiological data.⏎3. Reducing the Data Preparation Burden⏎Research consistently finds that the majority of data scientist and researcher time is consumed⏎by data preparation rather than analysis.[1] HHS should fund research into “data-quality-at-⏎source” protocols that ensure clinical data is standardized, labeled, and ready for AI⏎Page 7⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎consumption at the moment of creation—eliminating the retrospective data reconstruction that⏎currently drains research productivity. Equally important is research into preventing “model⏎collapse,” where AI models degrade when trained on recursively generated synthetic data rather⏎than high-fidelity, curated records.⏎Published Evidence on Adopted AI Tools (Questions 10a–10b)⏎The published literature on clinical AI economics has matured significantly. A systematic review⏎of economic evaluations found that 98% of studies concluded AI-based strategies were cost-⏎effective or cost-saving,[10] with projections suggesting annual net savings of $200–$360 billion⏎in U.S. healthcare (a 5–10% reduction in total spending).[11] However, fewer than half of studies⏎explicitly report their costing methods, and only 28% include implementation costs—indicating⏎that the full financial burden of AI adoption is systematically underestimated in the literature.⏎The literature also reveals that trust in medical AI flows hierarchically: from digital literacy to the⏎AI system, and only then to the physician and institution. This reinforces the importance of the⏎physician’s intermediary role in building institutional trust—and the danger of deploying AI in⏎ways that bypass clinical judgment.⏎Closing⏎We appreciate HHS’s commitment to gathering concrete, experience-based input on clinical AI⏎adoption. The consistent theme across our responses is that the barriers to AI in healthcare are⏎primarily infrastructural and organizational—not algorithmic. Investing in data infrastructure,⏎operational readiness, and executive digital fluency will yield greater returns than investing in AI⏎models alone. We welcome the opportunity to engage further with HHS on these priorities.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Bo Lora⏎Scientix.ai Inc.⏎bolora@scientix.ai⏎Page 8⏎⏎106 6th St, Suite 900-152⏎Austin TX 78701⏎Public Comment on RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎References⏎[1] IDC/Alteryx, “State of Data Preparation,” 2023. Also cited in JMP (SAS) research finding scientists⏎waste up to 40% of time on data preparation.⏎[2] Deloitte, “2026 US Health Care Outlook Survey: Many health care leaders are leaning into agentic AI,”⏎February 2026.⏎[3] Aidoc/Cedars-Sinai study published in Radiology Research and Practice (2022), PMC9411003. Mean⏎LOS for PE dropped from 7.91 to 5.83 days (p=0.034).⏎[4] JAMA Network Open, 2026. AI-driven triage reduced median clinician review time from 22.03 hours to⏎5.02 hours.⏎[5] Tateeda Healthcare IT research, 2026. Survey of clinicians on AI correction time.⏎[6] University of Minnesota School of Public Health, 2025. Analysis of hospital use of AI-assisted⏎predictive tools.⏎[7] The Lancet Digital Health, February 2026. Study on LLMs repeating false medical claims in clinical⏎contexts.⏎[8] Deloitte 2026 US Health Care Outlook Survey; PR Newswire release, February 2026.⏎[9] SullivanCotter, “AI and the Future of Health Care,” 2026.⏎[10] PMC systematic review (PMC12795340): 98% of economic evaluations found AI cost-effective or⏎cost-saving.⏎[11] Medrxiv preprint 2025. Projected annual net savings of $200B–$360B (5–10% reduction in total U.S.⏎healthcare spending).⏎Page 9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0220♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from National Association of Chain Drug Stores, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔National Association of Chain Drug Stores♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Submitted via: www.regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The National Association of Chain Drug Stores (NACDS) appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Request⏎for Information (RFI) on accelerating the adoption and responsible use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical⏎care. We applaud your commitment to advancing the appropriate use of AI to improve healthcare for the⏎American people and appreciate your consideration of our comments. NACDS represents chain pharmacies,⏎including traditional drug stores, supermarkets, and mass merchants with pharmacies. Nearly 90% of⏎Americans live within 5 miles of a community pharmacy1 and 85% of adults in the U.S. say pharmacists are⏎easy to access, ranking them the most accessible healthcare destination.2 Pharmacies are a trusted,⏎nationwide access point for medications, immunizations, preventive care, patient education and counseling,⏎and a growing range of health and wellness services that improve health outcomes.⏎To accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, NACDS recommends a pro-innovation, risk proportionate national⏎framework that maintains patient safety, privacy, transparency, equity, and public trust. In implementing AI⏎policy, HHS should rely on and reinforce existing guardrails, avoid duplicative regulation, align reimbursement⏎with value, and ensure harmonization across federal agencies and states. Furthermore, payment reform is⏎foundational. AI should support clinician-rendered care, and clinicians must be reimbursed for that care.⏎NACDS Recommendations⏎1. Establish a Harmonized, Risk-Proportionate Federal AI Framework⏎HHS should rely on and reinforce existing regulatory guardrails, across FDA, CMS, OCR, and ASTP/ONC,⏎rather than layering duplicative AI-specific policies. Interagency coordination and federal⏎1 Berenbrok L, Tang S, et al. Access to community pharmacies: A nationwide geographic information systems cross-sectional analysis. JAPhA. July⏎2022. https://www.japha.org/article/S1 544-3191(22)00233-3/fulltext⏎2 Polling data Conducted by Morning Consult (Commissioned by NACDS). October 2023. https://www.nacds.org/pdfs/Opinion-Research/NACDS-⏎OpinionResearch-National.pdf⏎1776 Wilson Blvd., Suite 200, Arlington, VA 22209 • P: 703.549.3001 • F: 703.836.4869 • NACDS.org⏎⏎Page 2 of 8⏎harmonization are essential to avoid fragmented oversight and inconsistent state-level requirements⏎that create uncertainty and slow responsible innovation. A unified national framework will provide⏎clarity, reduce compliance friction, and support scalable AI deployment in clinical care settings,⏎including community pharmacies.⏎2. Align Payment Policy with AI-Supported Clinical Value & Recognize Pharmacists as Providers in⏎Medicare Part B⏎Sustainable adoption of AI in clinical care depends on reimbursement reform. Pharmacists must be⏎recognized as providers under Medicare Part B so that pharmacist-rendered clinical services, including⏎those supported and scaled by AI, are eligible for reimbursement. Without provider recognition by⏎CMS, payment pathways remain limited and investment in AI-enabled clinical services is constrained⏎for pharmacies.⏎Reimbursement methodologies must also account for the cost of acquiring, implementing,⏎maintaining, and monitoring AI tools that support clinical care delivery. Just as payment rates reflect⏎the resources required to furnish services, reimbursement for AI-supported services should⏎incorporate the reasonable and necessary costs of technology infrastructure, system integration,⏎validation, cybersecurity safeguards, staff training, and ongoing performance monitoring. Without⏎recognition of these costs in rate-setting methodologies, providers may be financially disincentivized⏎from adopting innovative, AI-driven tools that improve quality, safety, and efficiency.⏎3. Strengthen Interoperability to Enable Authorized, Workflow-Integrated Data Access⏎Effective clinical AI tools require reliable, structured access to electronic health information within⏎workflow. HHS should strengthen interoperability to ensure that community pharmacies can obtain⏎and transmit clinically relevant data including screening and lab results , discharge summaries,⏎diagnoses, medication changes, care plans, and other key information in real time. Meaningful⏎interoperability will widen market opportunities, promote competition among developers, and enable⏎safe, effective AI integration.⏎4. Provide Clear Governance Guardrails for Non-Device AI⏎HHS should issue risk-tiered guidance clarifying documentation standards, liability allocation, oversight⏎expectations, privacy and data governance responsibilities, and audit treatment for AI tools that⏎support, but do not replace, clinical judgment. Safe harbors for good-faith adoption of AI tools would⏎reduce uncertainty and encourage responsible innovation while preserving accountability and public⏎trust.⏎5. Invest in Applied Research and Real-World Demonstrations⏎HHS should prioritize public-private partnerships and demonstration projects that validate AI-⏎supported clinical tools in real-world pharmacy settings.⏎Introductory Perspectives: A National Framework, Payment Alignment, and R&D Investment⏎1. The Impact of Current HHS Regulations on AI Adoption⏎HHS currently regulates many aspects of clinical AI use through existing frameworks, including FDA⏎oversight for software as a medical device where applicable; CMS program integrity and⏎⏎Page 3 of 8⏎documentation rules; HIPAA privacy and security regulations; Section 1557 nondiscrimination⏎requirements; and ASTP/ONC transparency and risk management requirements for predictive decision⏎support in certified health IT.⏎These guardrails provide meaningful patient protections and serve as a strong foundation for⏎responsible AI deployment. However, fragmentation and uncertainty across regulatory regimes can⏎slow AI adoption, particularly for non-device AI tools that support clinical documentation, medication⏎adherence outreach, and workflow prioritization. When expectations for oversight, documentation,⏎and audit treatment are unclear or inconsistently interpreted, providers are hesitant to deploy AI tools⏎even when those tools enhance quality and efficiency.⏎NACDS recommends that HHS rely on existing oversight structures rather than layering duplicative AI-⏎specific policies. Interagency coordination across FDA, CMS, OCR, and ASTP/ONC is essential to avoid⏎conflicting expectations and duplicative compliance burdens. Additionally, HHS should establish a⏎harmonized national framework that minimizes inconsistent state-level AI mandates. In particular, the⏎proliferation of overlapping state AI laws creates uncertainty and inefficiency for healthcare providers⏎operating across multiple jurisdictions. A coordinated federal framework that supersedes state laws⏎would reduce friction and enable responsible scaling of AI tools.⏎2. Payment Policy Changes to Incentivize High-Value AI and Promote Competition⏎Payment policy is a primary determinant of AI adoption. Even when AI tools improve quality, efficiency,⏎and access, providers frequently lack sustainable reimbursement pathways. In pharmacy settings, this⏎challenge is amplified by the lack of pharmacist recognition as healthcare providers in Medicare Part B,⏎limiting billing pathways for pharmacist-led clinical services, and uncertainty regarding documentation⏎standards when AI supports clinical workflows.⏎First, HHS should recognize and reimburse pharmacist-rendered services that AI helps scale. AI should⏎support pharmacist-led screening programs, adherence interventions, chronic care management,⏎preventive services, and other care. However, these services must be reimbursable to achieve⏎widespread adoption. Importantly, reimbursement must reflect not only the professional service⏎furnished but also the technology investments required to deliver AI-supported care. AI tools involve⏎upfront capital expenditures, integration costs, cybersecurity protections, ongoing licensing fees, and⏎continuous validation and monitoring. Payment policy should therefore incorporate technology⏎enablement costs into rate-setting methodologies and value-based payment calculations. Without⏎payment reform, AI will remain limited to administrative efficiencies rather than enabling scalable⏎clinical transformation.⏎Second, CMS should clarify billing and audit expectations for AI-assisted documentation. When a⏎licensed clinician reviews, edits as needed, and attests to documentation supported by AI, that⏎documentation should be treated consistently with traditional records. Clear guidance would reduce⏎audit uncertainty and encourage adoption of AI tools that reduce clinician burden.⏎Third, value-based payment models should reward prevention, adherence, and early intervention.⏎Pharmacists play a critical role in delivering these services through medication therapy management,⏎chronic disease monitoring, immunizations, test-and-treat protocols, targeted medication adherence⏎⏎Page 4 of 8⏎interventions, and others, often serving as the most accessible healthcare professional in many⏎communities. AI tools that enhance pharmacists’ ability to identify high-risk patients, close therapy⏎gaps, support transitions of care, proactively manage chronic conditions, and other care services, can⏎reduce avoidable hospitalizations and improve outcomes. These pharmacist-led, AI-supported services⏎should be financially supported, including through outcome-based payment structures that recognize⏎and reimburse measurable improvements in quality and total cost of care.⏎Finally, scalable reimbursement pathways, including demonstration programs and shared-savings⏎arrangements, can lower adoption barriers, particularly in rural and underserved communities where⏎pharmacies are often the most accessible healthcare providers.⏎3. HHS Investment in Research, Development, and Long-Term Market Creation⏎HHS can catalyze durable, high-value AI markets by investing in public-private partnerships that⏎validate AI-enabled clinical tools in real-world pharmacy settings. Demonstration projects in⏎community pharmacies can generate real-world evidence regarding safety, effectiveness, workflow⏎integration, and total cost of care.⏎Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎RFI Question 1. Biggest Barriers to Private Sector Innovation in AI For Health Care and its⏎Adoption/Use in Clinical Care⏎The biggest barriers to private sector innovation and adoption are misaligned incentives, regulatory⏎uncertainty, and data access friction, particularly for non-device AI that supports workflow⏎optimization and documentation.⏎Payment misalignment remains the most significant barrier. Many high-value AI-enabled interventions,⏎including screening and referral workflows, adherence targeting, and chronic care support do not have⏎dependable reimbursement pathways. In pharmacy settings, the lack of consistent payment for⏎pharmacist-rendered clinical services, including in Medicare Part B, suppresses investment in AI tools⏎designed to scale those services, and further constrains innovation. Pharmacists’ lack of recognition as⏎providers under Medicare Part B restricts reimbursement eligibility and weakens the market signals⏎necessary to drive AI deployment in pharmacy-based care models.⏎Fragmented interoperability also limits AI effectiveness. AI systems depend on timely, structured⏎access to relevant clinical data, such as laboratory results, discharge summaries, updated diagnoses,⏎and medication changes. Without reliable real-time data access, AI tools cannot safely or effectively⏎support clinical decision-making.⏎Finally, fragmented governance frameworks and overlapping federal and state requirements create⏎compliance complexity that slows innovation, particularly for organizations operating across multiple⏎jurisdictions.⏎⏎Page 5 of 8⏎RFI Question 2. Regulatory, Payment Policy, or Programmatic Design Changes HHS Should Prioritize⏎HHS should prioritize payment and program design reforms that make high-value AI-supported clinical⏎services financially viable, paired with clearer guardrails for documentation and audit treatment and⏎strengthened interoperability.⏎A. Payment and Program Design Priorities (CMS)⏎HHS should create and expand pathways that reimburse pharmacist-rendered clinical services⏎supported by AI. Without payment reform, AI will remain confined to “back office” functions such as⏎Interactive Voice Response (IVR) optimization, intake triage, and billing support. Existing CMS⏎authorities, including demonstration programs and value-based models, should be used to support⏎pharmacist-led, AI-supported clinical services, such as screening and preventive care, medication⏎adherence interventions, transitions-of-care services, and chronic disease management.⏎CMS should also clarify billing and documentation expectations when AI supports clinical⏎documentation. If a licensed clinician, including a pharmacist, reviews and attests to the⏎documentation record, AI assistance should not create noncompliance or heightened audit risk.⏎B. Interoperability and Information Access Priorities (ASTP/ONC)⏎HHS and ASTP/ONC should strengthen meaningful access to electronic health information (EHI) for all⏎care settings, including community pharmacies. Pharmacies must be able to access clinically relevant⏎EHI in structured formats integrated into workflow, not merely through document-based exchange.⏎Reliable interoperability will expand market opportunity, improve competition among AI developers,⏎and support safe, scalable adoption. NACDS looks forward to working with HHS and ASTP/ONC to⏎strengthen pharmacy interoperability and ensure that community pharmacies are fully integrated⏎participants in national health information exchange infrastructure. As recognized in ONC’s 2023⏎Pharmacy Interoperability and Emerging Therapeutics Task Force Report, pharmacies must be fully⏎integrated into nationwide health information exchange infrastructure to support clinical services⏎through secure, structured data exchange.3⏎RFI Question 3. Novel Legal/Implementation Issues for Non-Medical Device AI and HHS’ Role⏎Non-device AI tools raise legal and governance issues not fully addressed by traditional medical device⏎frameworks.⏎Liability allocation is a central concern impeding adoption of clinical AI tools. When AI suggests⏎prioritization or drafts documentation, responsibility for potential errors must be clearly delineated⏎among clinicians, provider organizations, and AI vendors. Clear expectations are needed regarding⏎standards of clinician review, documentation, and appropriate logging practices.⏎Privacy, security, and data governance considerations are also significant. Handling electronic health⏎information at scale for AI requires clear expectations for access controls, retention policies, model⏎3 Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC), Pharmacy Interoperability and Emerging Therapeutics Task Force⏎2023 Recommendations Report, Health Information Technology Advisory Committee (HITAC), November 9, 2023.⏎⏎Page 6 of 8⏎training boundaries, and incident response procedures, while ensuring requirements are not overly⏎burdensome.⏎HHS should publish risk-tiered guardrails for non-device AI, clarify documentation and oversight⏎expectations, and promote safe harbors for good-faith adoption of AI tools. Specifically, oversight⏎expectations should be proportionate to the level of clinical risk posed by the AI tool. Lower-risk tools⏎that support administrative functions should not face the same regulatory burden as higher-risk tools⏎that influence treatment decisions. A risk-based framework would provide clarity, protect patients, and⏎allow responsible innovation to advance without unnecessary barriers. Encouraging standardized⏎contractual best practices addressing vendor accountability and shared responsibility would further⏎reduce uncertainty.⏎RFI Question 7. Key Decision Makers/Governing Bodies and Primary Administrative Hurdles⏎Key Decision Makers: Adoption of AI in clinical care settings, including community pharmacy, is rarely⏎driven by a single individual within an organization. Instead, it reflects coordinated decision-making⏎across multiple leadership domains, each of which evaluates risk, value, and operational feasibility⏎through a distinct lens.⏎Clinical leadership, including pharmacy leadership and quality committees, plays a central role in⏎determining whether an AI tool aligns with patient safety standards, clinical workflows, and quality⏎improvement goals. These leaders assess whether the technology meaningfully enhances care, reduces⏎errors, improves health outcomes, or supports access. Clinical leaders are often highly influential⏎voices in determining whether AI is perceived as a tool that augments professional judgment or⏎introduces unacceptable risk.⏎Compliance, legal, and risk management teams also exercise substantial influence. These stakeholders⏎evaluate regulatory exposure, documentation standards, audit risk, liability allocation, and consistency⏎with federal and state requirements. If expectations regarding oversight, documentation, or audit⏎treatment are unclear or overly complex, compliance leadership may appropriately take a cautious⏎posture that slows adoption.⏎Information technology, security, and data governance leadership are equally critical decisionmakers.⏎AI tools require secure integration with existing pharmacy management systems, access to electronic⏎health information, and adherence to cybersecurity standards. IT leaders must assess data⏎architecture, Application Programming Interface (API) capabilities, access controls, model monitoring⏎requirements, and long-term system maintenance obligations. Concerns regarding data security,⏎privacy compliance, and operational resilience can significantly affect implementation timelines.⏎Finance and reimbursement leadership evaluate the return on investment, sustainability, and payment⏎viability of AI-supported services. Even when clinical and operational leaders support adoption,⏎uncertainty regarding reimbursement pathways, billing compliance, and audit posture may constrain⏎investment decisions. In pharmacy practice, the absence of consistent payment mechanisms for⏎pharmacist-rendered clinical services further limits financial justification for deploying AI tools⏎designed to scale those services.⏎⏎Page 7 of 8⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles: Vendor due diligence is among the most significant barriers.⏎Healthcare organizations must evaluate cybersecurity safeguards, HIPAA compliance, data⏎minimization practices, bias testing methodologies, model validation processes, and ongoing⏎monitoring plans. This process is resource-intensive and often requires multidisciplinary review.⏎Contracting and indemnification complexity further complicates adoption. AI vendor agreements⏎frequently involve negotiations over liability allocation, performance guarantees, data ownership,⏎audit rights, and incident response responsibilities. Providers are often reluctant to accept⏎disproportionate liability exposure for tools they do not control, particularly where regulatory⏎standards are still evolving.⏎Integration burden with existing systems presents another substantial challenge. AI tools must function⏎within high-volume, workflow-driven environments such as community pharmacies. Poorly integrated⏎systems can increase cognitive load, create alert fatigue, or require duplicative documentation.⏎Seamless interoperability with pharmacy management systems and external electronic health record⏎platforms is essential but not always readily achievable.⏎Finally, unclear reimbursement and audit posture remains a critical barrier. Organizations need⏎confidence that AI-assisted documentation and clinical decision support will be treated consistently⏎under program integrity reviews. Ambiguity in billing standards or audit expectations creates risk⏎aversion and delays adoption, even when the technology demonstrates clinical value.⏎Reducing these administrative hurdles through clearer guidance, aligned payment policy, standardized⏎evaluation frameworks, and predictable audit treatment would significantly accelerate responsible AI⏎adoption in clinical care settings.⏎RFI Question 8. Where Enhanced Interoperability Would Widen Market Opportunities and⏎Accelerate AI Development⏎Interoperability improvements that matter most for pharmacy-enabled AI include structured access to⏎relevant clinical information, such as medication history, laboratory data, diagnoses, discharge⏎summaries, care plans, and referral documentation within workflow.⏎If pharmacies can reliably access electronic health information in usable formats integrated into daily⏎workflow, the AI market expands dramatically. More developers can compete, more clinical use cases⏎become viable, and patient outcomes improve through safer, more efficient, and better coordinated⏎care.⏎Conclusion⏎Pharmacies are uniquely positioned to expand access to a wide variety of healthcare services through⏎responsibly deployed clinical AI tools. However, meaningful and scalable adoption requires payment reform,⏎documentation clarity, strengthened interoperability, and risk-proportionate governance guardrails. NACDS⏎stands ready to collaborate with HHS and across agencies to ensure that AI enhances clinical care in ways that⏎are safe, responsible, competitive, and scalable nationwide. NACDS is eager to collaborate to improve⏎pharmacy data interoperability and ensure community pharmacies are completely included in the nation's⏎⏎Page 8 of 8⏎health information exchange system. For any questions or further discussion regarding these comments,⏎p lease contact Kayla McFeely, Vice President, Health & Wellness Strategy and Policy at kmcfeely@nacds.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Steven C. Anderson, FASAE, CAE, IOM⏎President and Chief Executive Officer⏎National Association of Chain Drug Stores⏎###⏎NACDS represents traditional drug stores, supermarkets and mass merchants with pharmacies. Chains operate nearly⏎40,000 pharmacies, and NACDS’ chain member companies include regional chains, with a minimum of four stores, and⏎national companies. Chains employ nearly 3 million individuals, including 155,000 pharmacists. They fill over 3 billion⏎prescriptions yearly, and help patients use medicines correctly and safely, while offering innovative services that⏎improve patient health and healthcare affordability. NACDS members also include more than 900 supplier partners and⏎over 70 international members representing 21 countries. Please visit NACDS.org.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0221♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Reveleer, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Reveleer, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔In response to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (Published⏎Document: 2025-23641 (90 FR 60108)) by the US Department of Health and⏎Human Services, Office of the Secretary (RIN 0955-AA13), Reveleer is⏎submitting the following information for consideration to the specific⏎questions outlined in the published document.⏎Please direct any questions to the responses below to⏎alan.tam@reveleer.com.⏎1. Biggest barriers to AI innovation and adoption in clinical care⏎From Reveleer’s work with health plans and risk‑bearing providers in⏎value‑based care, the primary barriers are:⏎• Fragmented data and limited interoperability. Despite⏎interoperability policies, clinical and administrative data remain⏎scattered across an array of systems EHRs, claims platforms, HIEs,⏎point solutions, and document repositories, with critical signal⏎buried in unstructured notes. There is some adoption of⏎transmission standards through standard FHIR based APIs but this⏎only covers part of the data needs required for AI to⏎comprehensively and reliably gather the context for an accurate⏎response. Given the variety of formats driving the source data,⏎when AI is deployed on this landscape without first standardizing⏎and normalizing the inputs, it tends to amplify gaps, increase⏎hallucination risk, and produce inconsistent outputs that⏎clinicians do not trust.⏎⏎• Lack of an AI‑ready evidence layer. Many organizations still depend⏎on partial claims feeds, limited EHR connections, and manual chart⏎retrieval, preventing the creation of longitudinal,⏎provenance‑managed member records that can support risk adjustment,⏎quality, member eligibility, and care management consistently⏎across the care continuum.⏎• Insufficient governance, explainability, and auditability. Leaders⏎are wary of “black box” AI, hallucinations, bias, and opaque⏎decision paths—especially where AI touches coding, quality⏎reporting, or point‑of‑care decision support. Many offerings lack⏎strong MLOps, traceability to source documents, or clear⏎human‑in‑the‑loop controls. Explainable AI requires new approaches⏎like our “clinical corpus” model that delivers reliable medical⏎evidences in a language familiar to clinicians and auditors to⏎reduce the cognitive load driving administrative burden. By using⏎this approach, evidence is clear and defensible.⏎• Misaligned incentives and unclear measurement. Value‑based⏎contracts increasingly emphasize digital quality, equity, and⏎downside risk, but organizations lack standardized outcome and ROI⏎frameworks to compare AI‑assisted versus human‑only workflows. This⏎uncertainty slows investment and scale. By using incentive⏎management solutions like our Care Gap Manager, value alignment⏎streamlines the coordination between payer and provider to focus⏎on patient outcomes leading to lower costs for all.⏎• Workflow disruption and provider abrasion. AI tools that require⏎new portals, double documentation, or low‑precision suspect lists⏎create extra work and “audit anxiety,” leading clinicians and⏎coders to disengage even when underlying models are accurate. AI⏎prediction and recommendation engines like our Hybrid AI, are⏎built to support the placement of high value clinical next best⏎⏎actions in the right interface at the right time. EMRs must allow⏎these tools into their ecosystems so that the richest and most⏎reliable insight can drive the best patient outcome.⏎• Regulatory uncertainty and overlapping oversight. Rapidly evolving⏎rules around interoperability, PHI use, and expectations for⏎transparency and safety create perceived legal and compliance⏎risk, particularly for non‑device clinical AI that does not fit⏎neatly into existing FDA, CMS, HSS, and TEFCA paradigms.⏎As noted above, the Reveleer EVE framework —a predictive, explainable⏎AI layer operating over a unified evidence foundation—is designed to⏎mitigate these barriers, but they remain systemic obstacles to broader⏎private‑sector innovation and adoption.⏎2. Regulatory, payment, and programmatic changes HHS should prioritize⏎Reveleer recommends that HHS prioritize the following changes to⏎accelerate effective, safe AI use in clinical care, especially for⏎non‑device AI used in risk adjustment and quality workflows.⏎2.1. Codify expectations for explainable, evidence‑linked AI⏎• Establish industry-wide guidance that clinical AI used in coding,⏎quality measurement, and decision support must provide traceable⏎links from each recommendation back to specific clinical evidence⏎(e.g., note, lab, report) and maintain reproducible audit trails.⏎• Encourage architectures that explicitly separate evidence⏎extraction (which may use generative or agentic techniques) from⏎deterministic clinical reasoning to establish a clear boundary⏎separating AI from diagnoses generation or authoritative narrative⏎⏎content but instead organizes existing evidence and suggestions⏎for human review. This is core to Reveleer’s design of EVE Hybrid⏎AI, which combines two sequenced services—agentic evidence⏎extraction and clinician‑authored rules in a human managed⏎clinical corpus—to produce audit‑ready suspects.⏎Possible regulatory anchors (illustrative):⏎• Under 42 CFR part 422 (Medicare Advantage) and 42 CFR part 438⏎(Medicaid managed care), clarify that AI‑assisted risk adjustment⏎and quality workflows are acceptable when diagnoses and quality⏎elements meet existing documentation standards, maintain⏎traceability to source records, and are validated by human⏎experts.⏎• Through ONC’s health IT rulemaking, align deregulatory steps on⏎certification with explicit expectations around standards‑based⏎APIs, provenance metadata, and logging sufficient to support⏎explainable AI.⏎2.2. Align reimbursement and program design with AI‑enabled workflows⏎• Recognize AI‑assisted digital abstraction, prospective risk⏎suspecting, and real‑time quality gap closure as acceptable methods⏎of documentation and reporting under CMS quality programs (e.g.,⏎eCQMs, Star Ratings, HEDIS‑aligned measures), so long as evidence⏎and audit standards are met.⏎• Create value‑based payment model options (e.g., new Medicare⏎Advantage and ACO model tests through CMMI) that explicitly reward⏎investment in AI‑enabled evidence layers and prospective risk⏎adjustment—rather than only retrospective chart review—by linking⏎shared savings or bonuses to improvements in risk accuracy,⏎quality performance, and avoidable utilization attributable to⏎⏎AI‑enabled workflows. Then manage those incentives through⏎certified VBC incentive management applications like Reveleer Care⏎Gap Manager which is designed specifically to guide providers and⏎plans through annual VBC programs to ensure both parties⏎understand and agree on the program performance and outcome each⏎year.⏎Specific references (non‑exhaustive and illustrative):⏎• 42 CFR part 422 Subpart G (Payments to Medicare Advantage⏎Organizations): clarify that AI‑assisted coding and suspecting are⏎permissible when diagnoses conform to existing medical record⏎documentation rules and remain subject to RADV and other audit⏎requirements.⏎• 42 CFR parts 412 and 413: acknowledge AI‑enabled documentation⏎support as a form of clinical decision support and workflow⏎optimization, without treating these tools as autonomous decision⏎makers.⏎2.3. Strengthen, but simplify, interoperability⏎• Maintain and refine requirements that payers and providers expose⏎standardized APIs (e.g., FHIR‑based patient access and provider⏎directory APIs under CMS Interoperability and Patient Access⏎rules) while avoiding overly prescriptive certification that⏎impedes innovation. Emphasize outcome‑oriented metrics such as⏎latency, uptime, and data completeness that directly enable safe⏎AI.⏎• Encourage payer and provider adoption of interoperable evidence⏎layers that support reuse of curated clinical data across risk,⏎quality, and care management programs, not just passive⏎“warehousing.”⏎⏎• Require EHRs to make it easy for third party AI solutions to⏎provide enriched evidence, suggestions, and potential diagnosis to⏎relevant places within the provider workflow to enable choice of⏎vendor for the best clinical insights and guidance.⏎2.4. Create unified expectations for non‑device AI oversight inside HHS⏎• Issue cross‑agency guidance clarifying the roles of ONC, CMS, OCR,⏎OIG, and FDA for non‑device AI systems that support coding,⏎quality, and decision support. This would allow organizations to⏎build AI governance programs anchored in a single, coherent set of⏎expectations instead of anticipating overlapping or conflicting⏎requirements.⏎These changes would directly support platforms like EVE, which already⏎implement evidence‑linked, human‑in‑the‑loop AI within HIPAA‑ and⏎HITRUST‑aligned governance frameworks.⏎3. Novel legal and implementation issues, and HHS’s role⏎For non‑device AI used in clinical workflows, Reveleer sees several⏎emerging legal and implementation issues:⏎• Attribution of liability in human‑AI collaboration. When AI⏎surfaces a suspected diagnosis or quality gap that a clinician⏎accepts or rejects, questions arise about responsibility if⏎subsequent audits or outcomes suggest that the AI contributed to⏎over‑ or under‑coding, or to missed care. Provide clear guidance⏎for how accepted recommendations should be logged in systems and⏎rules as to the significance a single instance can have on the⏎continuous tuning of a model.⏎⏎• Use of PHI for training and cross‑client learning. Organizations⏎must navigate HIPAA, state privacy laws, and contract terms when⏎using PHI to train, fine‑tune, or adapt AI models. The line between⏎de‑identified and identifiable data, and between client‑specific⏎and shared models, is often unclear in practice. Expand on minimum⏎necessary guidance to include the use of AI and explicit consent⏎by the patient for this information to be used.⏎• Black‑box models and due process. When AI models are opaque, plans⏎and providers may struggle to demonstrate to regulators and courts⏎how coding or care decisions were reached, raising due‑process and⏎fairness concerns in payment disputes or audits. Establish rules⏎or guidance blocking the use of black box models in clinical use⏎cases.⏎• Indemnification and vendor risk allocation. Contracts increasingly⏎need to allocate risk where AI‑assisted workflows contribute to⏎errors in submissions or clinical decisions, especially when both⏎vendor AI design and client configuration choices interact.⏎• Dynamic model behavior and “drift.” Continuous learning and⏎retraining can change AI behavior in ways that are hard to⏎document and explain to auditors absent robust MLOps, versioning,⏎and change‑management practices.⏎HHS can help by:⏎• Publishing model contractual clauses or high‑level guidance that⏎clarifies shared responsibility among vendors, payers, and⏎providers in AI‑assisted workflows, emphasizing human final⏎decision‑making and traceability to source evidence.⏎• Clarifying under HIPAA that PHI may be used for training and⏎validation of non‑device clinical AI subject to strict purpose⏎⏎limitation, de‑identification where feasible, and robust access⏎controls, and by encouraging standardized language in business⏎associate agreements to cover AI use.⏎• Encouraging or requiring versioned records of datasets, model⏎artifacts, and configurations used in AI‑assisted clinical⏎workflows, so that AI behavior is reproducible and auditable over⏎time.⏎• Recognizing human‑in‑the‑loop, evidence‑linked AI as a distinct⏎category from fully autonomous systems, with commensurate⏎expectations for liability and oversight. This aligns with EVE’s⏎design, which treats AI as clinical decision support rather than⏎as a replacement for clinician judgment.⏎4. Evaluation methods, metrics, robustness testing, and human‑centered⏎evaluation⏎For non‑device AI integrated into clinical workflows, Reveleer uses and⏎recommends a layered evaluation approach.⏎4.1. Technical and robustness evaluation⏎Pre‑ and post‑deployment, models should be evaluated on:⏎• Precision, recall, and accuracy for task‑specific outputs (e.g.,⏎suspected diagnoses, chart prioritization, quality data element⏎extraction), segmented by population, contract type, and data⏎source. This should include standard definitions for selected⏎benchmark metrics to prevent misinterpretation of model quality in⏎the market.⏎⏎• Robustness and drift, via continuous monitoring for performance⏎degradation, shifts in input distributions, and outlier confidence⏎scores, with automated triggers for investigation and retraining.⏎• Fairness, via segmented performance analysis across demographic⏎and clinical subgroups to detect systematic differences in⏎sensitivity, specificity, or error rates.⏎4.2. Workflow and operational evaluation⏎Because AI’s value is realized in workflow, Reveleer tracks:⏎• Charts reviewed per coder per day, charts eliminated from need for⏎first-pass review, and reductions in time per chart in AI‑assisted⏎versus non‑assisted workflows.⏎• Noise rate (the amount of inaccurate or in appropriate suggestions⏎presented to the provider) and provider acceptance (the amount of⏎accepted recommendations by the provider) are the two key metrics⏎driving effective AI-driven suspecting. EVE Hybrid AI, for⏎example, has shown up to three‑fold noise reduction compared with⏎legacy NLP in prospective risk adjustment, which directly improves⏎provider engagement and reduces abrasion.⏎• Cycle times for risk and quality programs, including speed from⏎data ingestion to action at the point of care.⏎4.3. Clinical, financial, and user‑experience evaluation⏎Reveleer’s measurement framework covers four domains:⏎• Clinical outcomes, including condition detection and documentation⏎rates, adherence to evidence‑based care pathways, and avoidable⏎utilization.⏎⏎• Financial performance, including RAF accuracy, Star Ratings,⏎medical loss ratio, and ROI for AI‑enabled programs (e.g.,⏎increased value per chart, reduced administrative costs).⏎• Operational efficiency, including abstraction throughput, error⏎rates, and end‑to‑end cycle time for key value‑based workflows.⏎• User experience and AI trust, including coder and clinician⏎satisfaction, agreement rates with AI recommendations, and⏎adoption analytics.⏎HHS should support these processes by:⏎• Funding multi‑site evaluation consortia and pragmatic trials that⏎use standardized measurement frameworks to compare AI‑assisted and⏎human‑only processes in risk adjustment and quality.⏎• Supporting prize competitions and cooperative agreements for tools⏎that provide open benchmarking harnesses, synthetic test sets, and⏎measurement standards for non‑device AI in clinical care.⏎• Using federal contracts and grant conditions to require⏎evidence‑linked, explainable AI and continuous monitoring in⏎HHS‑funded pilots, normalizing these evaluation practices.⏎5. Supporting private‑sector accreditation, certification, and testing⏎To promote responsible innovation while avoiding duplicative⏎regulation, HHS could:⏎• Endorse or co‑develop voluntary AI assurance frameworks for⏎non‑device clinical AI that emphasize transparency, evidence⏎linkage, bias monitoring, and human oversight—analogous to HITRUST⏎and SOC 2 for security and privacy.⏎⏎• Partner with industry on “trustmarks” for AI‑ready clinical data⏎and interoperable evidence layers, signaling that organizations⏎meet standards for data quality, provenance tracking, and⏎standardized APIs necessary for safe AI.⏎• Support independent testing labs or collaboratives that benchmark⏎AI tools against standardized test cases (including synthetic⏎data) for risk adjustment, quality abstraction, and care gap⏎detection, with results published in a transparent registry.⏎• Leverage CMS contracts and quality programs to reward⏎participation in such accreditation schemes—for example, by⏎recognizing accredited AI workflows as meeting certain⏎documentation sufficiency criteria for quality programs, while⏎retaining audit rights.⏎These actions would reinforce the direction Reveleer has already taken⏎with EVE—emphasizing explainable, audit‑ready outputs and rigorous⏎governance—while giving the broader market clear reference points for⏎trustworthy AI.⏎6. Where AI has met or fallen short; high‑impact future tools⏎6.1. Areas where AI has met or exceeded expectations⏎Reveleer and its clients have observed strong performance where AI is:⏎• Powering prospective risk adjustment and clinical intelligence,⏎enabling more accurate risk stratification, earlier detection of⏎undiagnosed conditions, and improved provider documentation at the⏎point of care. Organizations using such tools have seen better⏎provider engagement and measurable RAF improvements.⏎⏎• Supporting digital quality abstraction and care gap closure,⏎increasing throughput and precision in quality measures and⏎improving Star Ratings through better gap targeting and closure.⏎• Improving operational efficiency and staffing resilience, allowing⏎organizations facing workforce shortages to process more charts⏎per coder with higher yield, effectively increasing coding and⏎abstraction capacity without proportional staffing increases.⏎• Delivering favorable cost‑effectiveness, as broader literature⏎shows AI can reduce screening costs, unnecessary procedures, and⏎readmissions when seamlessly integrated into clinical workflows.⏎6.2. Areas where AI has fallen short⏎Common shortfalls include:⏎• Noisy, non‑explainable suspect lists. Legacy NLP systems often⏎produce high volumes of low‑specificity suspects without clear⏎evidence links, overwhelming providers and eroding trust. This has⏎been a key driver of provider abrasion.⏎• Workflow misalignment and low adoption. Tools that sit outside⏎core EHR workflows, require extra log‑ins, or lack role‑specific UX⏎tend to underperform regardless of model accuracy.⏎• Insufficient measurement and drift management. Many⏎implementations lack rigorous pre/post evaluation and ongoing⏎monitoring, obscuring true impact and allowing subtle performance⏎degradation over time.⏎6.3. High‑potential future tools⏎Reveleer believes the most impactful future non‑device AI tools will:⏎⏎• Operate over unified, multimodal evidence layers, combining⏎structured data, unstructured content, and in the future images⏎and signals, to deliver richer, context‑aware clinical intelligence⏎to help clinicians deliver the right care at the right moment from⏎the right suggestion.⏎• Use hybrid, agentic evidence extraction with deterministic⏎clinical reasoning for a growing number of clinical workflows, as⏎EVE Hybrid AI does today for prospective risk adjustment, enabling⏎audit‑ready, configurable suspects with drastically reduced noise⏎and rapid adaptation to new risk models and documentation⏎standards.⏎• Embed digital quality measurement directly into care pathways⏎rather than treating quality as a standalone reporting workflow,⏎surfacing in‑encounter opportunities to close gaps while⏎maintaining measure‑aligned data and provenance for eCQMs and⏎Stars.⏎• Combine ambient AI and prospective AI, where ambient tools reduce⏎point‑of‑care documentation burden and EVE‑like engines orchestrate⏎pre‑ and post‑encounter planning and panel management across the⏎year. Leverage ambient data as a data set in prospective AI to add⏎another data dimension to recommendations leading to a virtuous⏎cycle of ever improving care.⏎7. Organizational roles and administrative hurdles⏎In Reveleer’s experience, AI adoption decisions in clinical care are⏎typically driven by:⏎⏎• Chief Medical Officers and clinical leadership, who assess⏎clinical validity, workflow fit, and provider trust.⏎• Chief Information Officers and IT/data leaders, who own data⏎integration, interoperability, and infrastructure readiness.⏎• Value‑based care and risk/quality program leaders, who are⏎accountable for RAF, Star Ratings, and total cost of care and must⏎see clear ROI and compliance alignment.⏎The primary administrative hurdles include:⏎• Fragmented governance across clinical, IT, compliance, and⏎finance, which slows decisions and leads to inconsistent AI⏎policies.⏎• Lack of structured AI change‑management programs (stakeholder⏎mapping, communication, training, escalation paths), which can⏎cause confusion about roles, responsibilities, and safety⏎oversight.⏎• Procurement and contracting processes that are not adapted to AI⏎(e.g., difficulty evaluating explainability, data usage terms, and⏎model lifecycle commitments).⏎• Unclear internal measurement standards, making it hard to⏎prioritize AI investments or justify scaling pilots.⏎Reveleer has found that formal AI steering committees—combining⏎clinical, operational, IT, and compliance leadership—and clear,⏎outcomes‑based measurement frameworks are critical to overcoming these⏎hurdles and enabling durable adoption of an enterprise AI engine like⏎EVE.⏎8. Interoperability opportunities⏎⏎Enhanced interoperability would materially widen AI market⏎opportunities and accelerate development in several areas:⏎• FHIR‑based, near‑real‑time exchange of clinical data across payers⏎and providers, beyond minimum USCDI, so AI engines can access⏎timely, rich evidence for risk adjustment, quality, and care⏎management rather than relying on delayed batch files and manual⏎chart retrieval.⏎• Standardized representation of data provenance and evidence links,⏎including where each data element came from, how it was⏎transformed, and which documents support a given diagnosis or⏎quality data element. This directly supports explainable AI and⏎auditability.⏎• Shared, de‑identified benchmarking datasets for risk adjustment,⏎quality measures, and care gap detection, enabling vendors and⏎health systems to benchmark AI performance and robustness fairly.⏎• Interoperable “evidence layers” across payers and providers, with⏎standard interfaces and vocabularies for longitudinal records that⏎can be reused across retrospective and prospective risk, quality,⏎and utilization management.⏎• Open EHRs to support AI-based insights tools to power the best⏎intelligence within the provider workflow. Closed or pay-to-play⏎EHR systems limit choice for the providers to the AI initiatives⏎within the EHR infrastructure and in parallel contributes to the⏎provider abrasion when new innovative tools are activated but must⏎operate outside of the primary clinical workflow tools.⏎The Reveleer platform already uses interoperable clinical repositories⏎and standardized APIs to support EVE across risk adjustment and⏎quality solutions; strengthening national interoperability in these⏎⏎areas would significantly expand the impact and safety of similar AI⏎engines.⏎9. Patient and caregiver challenges and concerns⏎9.1. Challenges patients and caregivers want AI to address⏎From the vantage point of value‑based care operations, patients and⏎caregivers most want AI to help with:⏎• Fragmented care and incomplete information. Patients want⏎clinicians to see the full picture of their health across settings⏎to ensure the care and advice they are getting considers their⏎health conditions and needs uniquely. AI operating over unified⏎evidence layers can help ensure important conditions and care gaps⏎are not overlooked.⏎• Delays and access barriers. AI‑enabled risk stratification and care⏎gap identification can prioritize high‑risk patients for outreach,⏎ensuring timelier care and reducing avoidable hospitalizations.⏎But knowing that healthcare is local, these systems need to⏎account for regional factors (medical personnel, geographic⏎isolation, socioeconomic status, and population demographics) and⏎physician-specific approaches to care.⏎• Administrative burden and confusion. Patients benefit when AI⏎reduces clinician documentation burden and back‑office⏎inefficiencies, freeing more time for communication and shared,⏎personalized decision‑making.⏎• Health equity and tailored care. AI can help identify disparities⏎in access and outcomes and support more targeted interventions,⏎⏎provided fairness is monitored and addressed. As above, need to⏎account for regional factors (medical personnel, geographic⏎isolation, socioeconomic status, and population demographics) and⏎physician-specific approaches to care.⏎9.2. Concerns about AI in clinical care⏎Common concerns include:⏎• Loss of human judgment and relationship. Patients and caregivers⏎worry that AI may replace, rather than augment, clinician⏎judgment. Reveleer deliberately positions EVE as clinical decision⏎support, with non‑binding recommendations and required human⏎validation.⏎• Privacy, data security, and secondary use. There is concern that⏎AI requires extensive sharing and repurposing of sensitive health⏎data. Reveleer addresses this with HIPAA‑ and HITRUST‑aligned⏎controls, de‑identification or redaction where appropriate, and⏎strict purpose limitations on data use.⏎• Bias and fairness. Patients may fear that AI will perpetuate or⏎worsen inequities. Continuous fairness monitoring and transparent⏎performance metrics by subgroup are essential to mitigate this⏎risk.⏎• Opacity and lack of recourse. Without explainability and clear⏎appeal mechanisms, patients may feel that algorithmic decisions⏎are unchallengeable. Evidence‑linked AI and human‑in‑the‑loop⏎workflows provide a foundation for explainable decisions and⏎remediation.⏎HHS can support trust by emphasizing evidence‑linked, human‑supervised⏎AI and promoting patient‑facing transparency about when and how AI is⏎used in care decisions.⏎⏎10. Priority AI research areas; literature on costs, benefits, and⏎transfers⏎10.1. Priority research areas⏎Reveleer recommends that HHS prioritize research in:⏎• Hybrid AI architectures for non‑device clinical decision support.⏎Comparative studies should evaluate hybrid⏎agentic‑plus‑deterministic approaches (like EVE Hybrid AI) against⏎pure generative or black‑box models in terms of accuracy,⏎auditability, provider acceptance, and regulatory compliance in⏎risk adjustment and quality workflows.⏎• AI‑enabled evidence layers and continuous learning systems. Studies⏎should examine how unified, provenance‑rich evidence layers coupled⏎with continuous‑learning AI engines affect outcomes, costs, and⏎resilience of value‑based care programs over multi‑year horizons.⏎• Digital quality measurement embedded in care. Research should⏎develop and validate methods for AI to maintain measure‑aligned⏎quality data and surface in‑encounter opportunities to close gaps,⏎instead of treating quality reporting as an after‑the‑fact⏎exercise.⏎• Human‑AI collaboration and workflow design. Empirical work should⏎focus on UX patterns, alerting strategies, and feedback loops that⏎maximize adoption, trust, and safety in AI‑augmented coding and⏎clinical workflows.⏎• Equity and fairness in AI‑enabled value‑based care. Research should⏎investigate how AI affects care across different populations and⏎⏎payment models, including potential unintended consequences in⏎risk selection and resource allocation.⏎10.2. Published findings on impact, costs, benefits, and transfers⏎The emerging literature on AI in clinical care shows:⏎• Across multiple clinical domains (e.g., screening, chronic disease⏎management, imaging), AI interventions have demonstrated strong⏎cost‑effectiveness or cost‑saving potential, often via reduced⏎unnecessary procedures, improved early detection, and fewer⏎readmissions.⏎• Evaluations of AI‑powered prospective risk and care management have⏎found improvements in documentation completeness, reduced⏎readmissions, and better alignment of reimbursement with true⏎disease burden, while lowering administrative costs through⏎automation of chart review and suspecting.⏎• Economic evaluations typically combine clinical outcomes⏎(sensitivity, specificity, readmission rates, adverse events) with⏎utilization changes (length of stay, number of procedures,⏎outpatient versus inpatient mix) to derive budget impact and ROI.⏎Implementation costs such as integration, training, and model⏎maintenance are explicitly accounted for, along with indirect⏎effects on workflow and provider satisfaction.⏎HHS‑funded research can extend this evidence base by focusing⏎specifically on non‑device AI in value‑based care, using standardized⏎frameworks to ensure that comparisons of AI‑assisted and traditional⏎workflows are fair, transparent, and actionable for regulators and⏎program designers.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0222♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Veradigm LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Veradigm LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎c/o Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, ASTP/ONC⏎7033A, 330 C Street S.W.,⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎ATTN: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, RIN 0955-AA13 – Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy and Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy Posnack,⏎On behalf of Veradigm, we appreciate the opportunity to comment on HHS’s Request for Information⏎regarding actions the federal government can take to accelerate the adoption of artificial intelligence⏎(AI) in clinical care. Veradigm has a proven history of delivering clinical and revenue cycle solutions that⏎help independent practices improve outcomes and financial performance. Our AI-forward data and⏎technology also help over 20,000 provider practices connect with health plans and biopharma to⏎eliminate inefficiencies, close care gaps, and create a more affordable health system. These⏎organizations play an essential role in American healthcare and often face distinct constraints,⏎including limited staff, limited capital for digital transformation, and a disproportionately high⏎administrative burden, which must be directly considered in any federal strategy to support scaled,⏎responsible clinical AI adoption and use.⏎We believe AI has the potential to meaningfully improve patient care, clinician workload, and financial⏎sustainability, but only when it is deployed with the right guardrails, supported by coherent regulatory⏎expectations, and accessible to providers regardless of size or location. For this reason, Veradigm⏎strongly supports a clear, harmonized federal framework for clinical AI that recognizes the unique⏎operational realities of small, rural, and independent practices. Our comments reflect real-world⏎implementation experience and align with our internal governance approach to AI at Veradigm. In⏎general, our comments accentuate and expand on five (5) core themes:⏎1. We support adoption of a consistent, risk-based regulatory approach that differentiates⏎high-risk clinical workflows. AI use cases in healthcare range from administrative automation⏎to high-risk diagnostic support. Regulation should be proportionate to potential patient harm,⏎⏎focusing the highest scrutiny on tools that directly influence diagnosis or treatment, while⏎enabling rapid innovation in low-risk domains. We recommend the use of established risk⏎analysis frameworks and clearly defined tiers and applicability thresholds.⏎2. Preserve clinical judgment and human-in-the-loop safeguards. AI should augment, not⏎replace, clinical judgment. Core safeguards should include transparency standards related to⏎model purpose, intended use, performance bounds, limitations, and training data⏎characteristics. We also support design expectations that retain human decision-making in⏎high-risk workflows and preserve clinician autonomy to override or dismiss AI⏎recommendations when workflows are more automated or lower risk.⏎3. Support practical and scalable governance expectations for providers and developers.⏎Governance expectations must be scalable to diverse care settings, from large health systems⏎to small independent practices. We recommend flexible compliance pathways, such as⏎simplified oversight structures for small practices; alignment of developer requirements with⏎standard software development lifecycle practices; and harmonized federal guidance to reduce⏎fragmentation across states and agencies.⏎4. Promote transparency without creating new liability exposure or barriers to innovation.⏎Developers should be responsible for transparency and sound development practices; clinicians⏎remain responsible for clinical use. HHS should clarify that transparency expectations do not⏎reassign clinical liability and consider safe-harbor provisions for organizations that implement⏎recognized best practices such as external validation, bias monitoring, limitation disclosures,⏎and post-deployment monitoring. De-identified, aggregate analysis of AI performance and⏎overrides should be encouraged and/or required for oversight and/or quality improvement⏎initiatives.⏎5. Strengthen federal investments in AI research, benchmarking, and post-deployment⏎monitoring. To accelerate safe adoption, HHS should prioritize applied research on model drift⏎detection and calibration, shared benchmarking datasets and evaluation methods, and public–⏎private partnerships to study real-world performance, fairness, and usability in clinical⏎workflows.⏎RFI Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎From Veradigm’s perspective, the most significant barriers fall into several related categories:⏎regulatory ambiguity, governance expectations that do not reflect the realities of resource constrained⏎providers, misaligned payment incentives, and workflow readiness challenges.⏎2⏎⏎Independent and rural providers are heavily affected by regulatory fragmentation, as they seldom have⏎compliance teams capable of interpreting varying state and federal frameworks. Uncertainty about⏎how existing oversight applies to any use of AI in a practice often results in delayed decisions, even⏎when AI has clear clinical value. A harmonized federal posture that differentiates between device and⏎non-device AI, and that scales requirements to risk and organizational capacity, would substantially⏎reduce hesitation among small providers. The deregulation efforts in the proposed HTI-5 rule that⏎remove source attribute and model card requirements from Certified Health IT create uncertainty for⏎both vendors and providers around what kind of information to expect and how to compare the⏎various AI tools and functionality that might be available. This uncertainty increases risk for developers⏎and clinicians evaluating clinical decision support tools.⏎In addition, Veradigm has observed that governance frameworks proposed in various policy discussions⏎often assume the presence of cross functional oversight committees or formalized validation processes⏎that small practices simply cannot support. While robust governance is essential, prescriptive⏎structures risk creating barriers for precisely the organizations that could benefit most from burden⏎reducing tools. Rightsizing expectations for deployers based on organizational scale and the risk profile⏎of the AI tool would advance adoption while protecting patient safety. Payment model misalignment⏎presents another barrier. Independent and rural practices operate on thin margins, making it difficult⏎to invest in even high value AI tools without a clear path to recoup the investment. Payment model⏎reform that could meaningfully impact the use of AI is especially critical to incentivize the adoption and⏎use of AI for independent practices.⏎RFI Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Veradigm encourages HHS to establish a formal federal framework tailored specifically to high-risk⏎clinical AI, one that supports innovation while ensuring patient safety and preserving clinical judgment.⏎Such a framework should provide clear expectations for transparency, documentation, risk⏎assessment, and lifecycle monitoring while explicitly scaling requirements to reflect organizational size.⏎This is particularly important for small and independent practices that cannot absorb the cost of large⏎governance programs.⏎HHS can also accelerate adoption by harmonizing oversight across agencies including ASTP/ONC, FDA,⏎and CMS, reducing administrative complexity and creating predictable expectations for developers and⏎providers alike. This harmonization should include consistent definitions, align documentation⏎requirements, and coordinate expectations for intended use statements, monitoring practices, and⏎safety claims.⏎Equally critical is payment modernization. CMS should consider add-on payments, AI specific credits⏎within quality programs, and pathways in alternative payment models that reward demonstrated⏎clinical value, burden reduction, or cost savings. Clear reimbursement signals are necessary for small⏎and rural providers who otherwise cannot justify the upfront investment.⏎3⏎⏎RFI Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to address them?⏎The ongoing development of clinical AI that is considered Non-Device CDS by FDA introduces new⏎questions related to liability, contracting, data use under HIPAA, and end user readiness. For⏎independent and rural providers that lack legal and compliance resources, this uncertainty can pose a⏎significant adoption barrier.⏎A key challenge is the allocation of responsibility among developers, implementers, and clinicians when⏎AI recommendations inform care decisions. Because these tools do not fall under FDA’s traditional⏎regulatory structure, EHR vendors like Veradigm look to requirements for Certified Health IT that clarify⏎obligations around clinical decision support tools that are embedded in the EHR. The recent FDA⏎Guidance for Industry and Food and Drug Administration Staff, published on January 29, 2026, related⏎to clinical decision support in non-medical devices provides clear and meaningful examples of⏎functionality that falls in and outside of the FDA purview, and is helpful in terms of understanding what⏎may be outside the scope of FDA oversight, but there remains a gap in terms of the specifics of⏎documentation and information that must be provided to clinicians in order for them to be considered⏎fully informed about a tool and/or how it was developed. We welcome more certainty on the⏎obligations of EHR vendors who are developing or deploying AI, as well as additional guidance that can⏎provide clarity on novel AI workflows and functionality to ensure uniform understanding of the⏎obligations developers have to end users.⏎HHS could further support responsible adoption by convening stakeholders to develop model liability⏎frameworks and safe-harbor approaches for documenting AI-informed decisions, ensuring⏎accountability aligns with real-world roles. We encourage any stakeholder group to include⏎independent physician practices and ambulatory EHR developers to ensure adequate representation of⏎the specific nuances of AI use in these care settings.⏎Additionally, there remains ambiguity around permissible uses of deidentified and limited data sets for⏎AI development and monitoring under HIPAA. HHS clarification of allowable pathways, coupled with⏎encouragement of privacy preserving methods such as federated learning, would help providers⏎support AI safety without compromising patient privacy.⏎Finally, the move toward AI augmented workflows raises questions related to training, credentialing,⏎and scope of practice. Alignment across CMS participation requirements, HRSA workforce programs,⏎and ONC safety expectations would provide much needed clarity for organizations with limited training⏎resources.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions).⏎4⏎⏎Evaluation of non-device AI tools must include a combination of technical validation and real-world⏎workflow assessment. Before deployment, developers and providers should define intended use,⏎validate models across representative populations and conduct human factors testing in realistic EHR⏎workflows. After deployment, monitoring for drift, calibration, disparities, and user trust should⏎continue, but in a way that small practices can feasibly manage. We advocate for flexible regulations⏎that allow institutions, both developers and deployers, to develop safeguards that align with⏎organizational priorities, capabilities and the commensurate risk of the AI functionality that is being⏎developed and/or used. Overly prescriptive pre- and post-deployment testing runs the risk of stifling⏎innovation and adding unnecessary burden for low-risk AI tools that have the potential to improve⏎administrative burden for provider practices.⏎HHS can support consistency and feasibility by funding shared evaluation frameworks, national⏎benchmarks, and transparent reporting templates that organizations of all sizes can adopt.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎Some independent practices, including many Veradigm customers, are already developing internal⏎governance and safety frameworks. However, these efforts are uneven and can be difficult for small⏎providers to navigate and maintain. HHS recognition of voluntary accreditation and certification⏎models, aligned with federal principles, would strengthen these frameworks and create more uniform⏎expectations across the market. Requiring uniform and standardized documentation requirements⏎would also promote consistency, transparency and trust.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Across the healthcare system, decisions about adopting AI tools typically rest with clinical leadership, IT⏎departments, compliance teams, and quality and safety committees or some combination thereof.⏎However, in independent physician practices, small provider groups, and many rural health systems,⏎these responsibilities are often consolidated into one or two individuals who are already managing⏎significant clinical and administrative workload. This creates a structural barrier to evaluating,⏎procuring, and safely implementing new AI tools.⏎HHS can play a pivotal role in reducing these hurdles by establishing clear, scalable federal⏎documentation and governance expectations that small practices can implement without requiring⏎dedicated committees or specialized staff. In addition, HHS could encourage or standardize pilot/test⏎environments that allow smaller practices to evaluate AI safely without committing immediately to full⏎deployment, something many decisionmakers view as essential but often lack the infrastructure to⏎support. HHS could also align CMS program requirements and procurement expectations, reducing⏎uncertainty around what evidence or documentation a practice must maintain when adopting AI.⏎5⏎⏎Finally, federal support for simple, EHR embedded guidance, implementation toolkits, and workflow⏎ready templates would materially aid independent practices that rely on vendors for practical, turnkey⏎solutions that fit within existing budget cycles and operational constraints.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎AI’s effectiveness depends heavily on consistent, high-quality data, yet many practices lack access to⏎the robust interoperability infrastructure that large systems use. HHS can address this by continuing to⏎advance consistent, enforceable API requirements and continuing to advance data elements supported⏎by USCDI ensuring that AI developers and clinicians can rely on predictable and complete data sources.⏎To further support responsible AI adoption, HHS should accelerate expansion of data elements⏎represented in USCDI and specialty or domain specific data elements such as those in USCDI+ that⏎reflect real clinical needs for AI use cases. Additionally, HHS can drive equity and transparency by⏎funding and maintaining national benchmarking criteria and evaluation datasets and enabling⏎providers of all sizes to compare model performance with consistent criteria. These steps would⏎significantly strengthen model reliability, external validation, and fairness while reducing the burden⏎on small practices that cannot independently generate or curate large-scale evaluation datasets or⏎domain-specific data elements that reflect real clinical needs.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎For clinicians, particularly those in independent practices, meeting patient expectations for⏎transparency and privacy expectations when AI is used at the point of care requires guidance,⏎educational resources, and clarity about communication obligations.⏎HHS can support these needs by developing plain language, patient-facing educational materials⏎explaining when and how AI may be used, what protections should be in place, how privacy is⏎safeguarded, and what recourse or options patients have if they have questions or concerns. Federal⏎leadership could also establish baseline expectations for transparency and clinician communication,⏎giving providers, especially those with limited staff, clear, accessible resources that build trust without⏎adding undue administrative burden. Additionally, HHS can reinforce patient confidence by clarifying⏎how existing privacy protections apply to AI workflows and by supporting common sense safety, and⏎performance monitoring requirements that reassure patients their care remains under appropriate⏎oversight.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎AI research has historically focused on algorithmic performance in controlled settings, but widespread⏎and equitable adoption requires deeper understanding of how AI performs in the real world, especially⏎in diverse practice settings such as independent clinics and rural communities. HHS can address this⏎6⏎⏎need by funding longitudinal real-world performance and safety studies that assess calibration, drift,⏎disparities, and clinical outcomes across different care environments. This is critical for providers who⏎lack the data science capacity to independently evaluate these issues.⏎HHS should also invest in economic impact analyses that quantify how AI affects clinician workload,⏎administrative burden, operational efficiency, and cost of care; evidence that is especially important⏎for small practices with limited financial margin. Finally, HHS can advance understanding of human–AI⏎teaming and workflow integration, providing evidence-based guidance on expectations for AI systems⏎that meaningfully support clinicians rather than adding new burdens. These federal research⏎investments would create a stronger foundation for payment policy, regulatory expectations, and⏎industry best practices.⏎Conclusion⏎Veradigm appreciates HHS’s leadership in developing a forward-thinking and equitable approach to⏎clinical AI. With harmonized expectations, scalable governance, strengthened transparency, support⏎for privacy preserving data use, and modernized payment pathways, HHS can create an environment⏎where AI improves outcomes, reduces burden, and enhances sustainability across all care settings.⏎We stand ready to collaborate with HHS to support the safe, effective, and equitable deployment of AI⏎nationwide.⏎Respectfully,⏎Tina Joros, JD⏎Vice President – Policy & Innovation⏎Veradigm LLC⏎7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0223♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-12T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from EHY Consulting LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EHY Consulting LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment on Request for Information (RFI)⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Federal Register Citation: 2025-23641 (90 FR 60108)⏎Submitting Entity: EHY Consulting LLC⏎Submitted by: Edward You⏎Date: January 2026⏎Opening statement⏎This submission responds directly to each of the ten questions in the HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎and is structured to support cross-program review. Each response stands alone while also⏎referencing related questions where system-level dependencies exist. The intent is not to⏎recommend a single policy outcome, but to surface implementation realities, governance gaps,⏎and second-order effects that emerge when AI systems are deployed within real clinical⏎workflows.⏎A workflow-to-entity crosswalk is included to clarify how responsibilities intersect across HHS⏎StaffDivs and OpDivs and to help avoid gaps between policy intent and operational execution.⏎EHY Consulting LLC respectfully submits these comments in alignment with the HHS Artificial⏎Intelligence Strategy to support informed, non-prescriptive decision-making across the health⏎sector. A consistent finding across AI deployments in clinical environments is that adoption and⏎safety are determined less by model accuracy in controlled settings than by how AI systems⏎interact with real-world clinical workflows, governance structures, data stewardship practices,⏎procurement decisions, and learning feedback loops.⏎Clinical AI functions as a socio-technical system: it shapes clinician behavior, redistributes⏎responsibility, and generates compounding learning from data produced through routine⏎operations. Accordingly, this submission answers each RFI question directly while cross-⏎referencing related responses to keep system-level implications visible and to inform⏎approaches to scaling clinical AI responsibly.⏎HHS equities map (StaffDivs and OpDivs)⏎Clinical AI adoption is inherently cross-cutting. The same AI-enabled workflow can engage⏎multiple authorities and equities across HHS. A practical way to avoid gaps is to map⏎responsibilities to the workflow itself.⏎⏎Workflow-to-entity crosswalk (illustrative)⏎Workflow / decision point Primary HHS equities What success looks like⏎(examples) (implementation)⏎Clinical deployment and CMS (payment policy, quality Incentives reward⏎payment incentives measures, demonstrations) measurable workflow value⏎and outcomes, not superficial⏎“AI presence”⏎Health IT, interoperability, ASTP/ONC (health IT Standards enable safe⏎data standards standards, interoperability integration and auditability;⏎policy) interoperability does not⏎imply unconstrained⏎secondary use⏎Privacy, patient rights, and OCR (HIPAA privacy/security Clear, enforceable⏎enforcement enforcement and related boundaries on access, use,⏎guidance) and disclosure; meaningful⏎transparency and recourse⏎Cybersecurity and national- CIO (enterprise security Security-by-design⏎risk posture practices), ONS (national requirements are⏎security risk and resilience enforceable; AI is treated as⏎processes) part of critical clinical⏎infrastructure⏎International alignment, OGA (international Policies reconcile domestic⏎standards, and data-sharing engagement, standards, safeguards with cross-border⏎regimes agreements) standards and data-sharing⏎realities⏎Contract enforcement and Office of Acquisition / RFPs and contracts require⏎vendor/subcontractor contracting functions (across auditability, learning⏎controls HHS) controls, and secure⏎operations; flow-down to⏎subcontractors⏎Safety of AI-enabled clinical FDA (device/SaMD, CDS Clear expectations for⏎products and decision policy) validation, change control,⏎support (where applicable) and post-market monitoring⏎Evidence generation and AHRQ (evidence and Evaluation frameworks⏎implementation science outcomes research) reflect real-world workflow⏎effects and heterogeneous⏎⏎settings⏎Public health integration CDC Defined boundaries between⏎(where AI crosses into clinical and public health⏎surveillance) uses; governance for⏎secondary use⏎Research and biomedical NIH Strong stewardship of⏎data ecosystems (where clinical-research interfaces⏎clinical AI relies on research and reuse pathways⏎pipelines)⏎Safety-net and underserved HRSA, IHS, SAMHSA Adoption pathways account⏎delivery settings for resourcing constraints,⏎equity, and operational⏎realities⏎Cross-cutting note: acquisition is the control plane that converts policy intent into enforceable⏎obligations. Interoperability, cloud use, vendor access, and model-update practices are⏎ultimately determined by contract terms, monitoring rights, and operational controls.⏎Cross-cutting analytic lens: data, workflows, and learning⏎Practical governance test for clinical AI deployments⏎Use case and end-user clarity: identify the decision-maker, operator, and accountable party;⏎specify the action taken because of the model output.⏎Data lifecycle boundaries: define what data enters, what leaves, retention periods, and what is⏎used for care delivery versus model improvement.⏎Learning accumulation and sovereignty controls: specify whether operational data may be used⏎for model improvement and under what constraints; treat derived artifacts (e.g., embeddings,⏎fine-tunes, prompts, logs, telemetry) as governed outputs.⏎Security posture and access paths: enforce identity, least privilege, logging, incident response,⏎and subcontractor controls; define cloud and remote access defaults with audit rights.⏎Bias introduction points and drift: identify where bias can enter (training data, workflow⏎selection, labeling, clinician reliance, feedback loops) and require continuous monitoring and⏎rollback.⏎Across multiple clinical domains (including high-impact areas such as precision oncology and⏎advanced therapies), the most durable advantage—and the most consequential vulnerability—⏎⏎often lies not in the AI model or the molecule, but in control over the learning system: the⏎workflow-generated data, outcomes, failure modes, and process metadata that enable iterative⏎optimization over time.⏎Therefore, it is useful to distinguish:⏎• Privacy: who can see my data.⏎• Sovereignty: who determines what my data means and how much it is worth.⏎• Security: who has access to my data and what they can do with it.⏎Many governance approaches address privacy and baseline security, but do not explicitly govern⏎learning accumulation (how data becomes compounding capability). In clinical AI, the latter⏎increasingly determines long-run trust, competitiveness, and resilience.⏎Responses to RFI questions (aligned to Federal Register ‘Specific⏎Questions’)⏎Each response below restates the Federal Register question verbatim (or with minimal⏎formatting) and provides a direct answer. Cross-references are included where useful for⏎synthesis across HHS teams.⏎1. Biggest barriers to private sector innovation and adoption/use in clinical care⏎Clinical AI frequently crosses mismatched risk frameworks (privacy, cybersecurity, clinical safety,⏎enterprise risk, and vendor product risk). These frameworks do not align by default, and gaps⏎emerge at their seams unless governance is explicitly integrated.⏎Workflow misfit is the dominant barrier. AI systems that perform well in development can fail in⏎practice when they add documentation burden, disrupt clinician cognitive flow, or create⏎ambiguous responsibility at the point of care.⏎Key barriers include:⏎• Unclear accountability for AI-influenced decisions, especially when AI is adaptive or when⏎vendor updates materially change behavior.⏎• Trust gaps driven by opacity, inconsistent performance across subpopulations, and unclear⏎post-deployment monitoring.⏎• Data provenance and integration challenges (EHR heterogeneity, fragmented identifiers,⏎incomplete documentation, variable labeling).⏎• Operational and security constraints (network segmentation, device integration, patch⏎management, identity and access management).⏎• Procurement realities: contracts that do not specify auditability, model-update controls,⏎data-use boundaries, or subcontractor constraints.⏎See also: Q2, Q3, Q4, Q7, Q8.⏎⏎2. Regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes; CFR citations⏎where applicable⏎Condition participation or incentives on auditability (minimum logs, update provenance, and⏎model-change notification).⏎Make data-use boundaries enforceable, clarifying whether operational data may be used for⏎model improvement and under what authorizations.⏎Across regulation, payment policy, and program design, the most leverage comes from making⏎governance and monitoring enforceable at the workflow level while avoiding incentives for⏎superficial AI adoption.⏎Priority areas for HHS to consider include:⏎• CMS-aligned incentives that reward measurable workflow value and outcomes rather than⏎mere AI integration (see Q6).⏎• Programmatic designs that require lifecycle governance (monitoring, drift management,⏎change control) as a condition of participation in demonstrations, quality programs, or⏎certifications (see Q4 and Q5).⏎• Clear, enforceable expectations for data stewardship, auditability, and⏎vendor/subcontractor controls, implemented through acquisition and contracting⏎requirements where feasible (see Q3 and Q5).⏎• Interoperability policies that distinguish data exchange for care delivery from secondary use⏎for model training and learning accumulation (see Q8).⏎See also: If HHS seeks specific CFR citations, a targeted follow-on request scoped to particular⏎programs (e.g., Medicare conditions of participation, quality reporting, certification programs)⏎would enable more precise citations by commenters.⏎3. Non-medical devices: novel legal and implementation issues; HHS role⏎Governance and contracts should explicitly cover workflow metadata and derived artifacts⏎(prompts, embeddings, fine-tunes, telemetry), which often represent the primary signals of⏎value and intent rather than mere exhaust.⏎For non-medical device AI used in clinical care (e.g., workflow, triage, documentation,⏎scheduling, or administrative decision support), key issues include liability allocation,⏎indemnification, privacy/security obligations, and governance of adaptive behavior post-⏎deployment.⏎Novel legal and implementation issues commonly arise from:⏎• Ambiguity in responsibility when AI influences clinician behavior or care pathways without⏎being a regulated medical device.⏎⏎• Contracting and indemnification gaps that fail to define change control, performance⏎representations, or audit rights.⏎• Privacy/security frameworks that govern access and disclosure, but do not explicitly govern⏎learning accumulation or model improvement using operational clinical data.⏎• Third-party and subcontractor risk (cloud hosting, analytics, support), especially when access⏎paths are opaque.⏎See also: HHS can help by publishing model contract clauses and minimum auditability/lifecycle⏎governance expectations usable by providers and payers, aligned across OCR, ASTP/ONC,⏎CIO/ONS, and CMS.⏎4. Non-medical devices: evaluation methods pre- and post-deployment;⏎mechanisms⏎Evaluate impact on clinician behavior, including automation bias, alert fatigue, de-skilling, and⏎reliance shifts over time.⏎The most promising evaluation approaches combine pre-deployment validation with post-⏎deployment monitoring that is sensitive to workflow effects, site-to-site variation, and⏎distribution shift.⏎Recommended evaluation components include:⏎• Human-centered workflow evaluation (cognitive load, alert fatigue, override rates, time-to-⏎task, downstream workflow changes).⏎• Robustness and stress testing across settings, subpopulations, and data quality conditions.⏎• Post-deployment drift detection and performance monitoring linked to outcomes where⏎feasible.⏎• Change-control governance for model updates, including criteria for rollback.⏎See also: HHS can support these via contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and prize⏎competitions (often with AHRQ/NIH/CMS partners), prioritizing monitoring, auditability, and⏎workflow-aware evaluation methods.⏎5. Support for accreditation, certification, testing, and credentialing⏎Establish a minimum governance checklist for certification programs, including update control,⏎monitoring, disclosure of data use for training, and incident response.⏎HHS can amplify private-sector accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing, and⏎credentialing by helping establish baseline expectations for trustworthy clinical AI that are⏎measurable and auditable.⏎Support could include:⏎⏎• Reference expectations for auditability (logging, traceability of inputs/outputs, update⏎history) and minimum cybersecurity posture for clinical AI deployments.⏎• Credentialing frameworks for AI governance roles within health care organizations (see Q7).⏎• Model contract language and procurement checklists that incorporate security-by-design⏎and lifecycle monitoring requirements (see Q3 and Q4).⏎• Public-private benchmarking and validation resources, including shared evaluation protocols⏎and testbeds (see Q8 and Q10).⏎6. Where AI met/exceeded vs fell short; novel tools with greatest potential⏎Distinguish value created for patients and providers from value transferred to vendors through⏎learning accumulation from operational data.⏎AI tends to meet or exceed expectations when it is tightly coupled to well-defined workflows⏎with clear success metrics (e.g., reducing administrative burden, improving triage consistency,⏎accelerating access, or reducing preventable harm). It tends to fall short when deployed as a⏎generic overlay without governance, monitoring, or integration into decision rights.⏎High-potential tool categories include:⏎• Workflow-aware clinical documentation support that measurably reduces administrative⏎burden without introducing silent errors.⏎• Risk stratification and triage tools with clear accountability and monitoring, especially in⏎resource-constrained settings.⏎• Tools that surface quality insights by analyzing workflow metadata and outcomes (not only⏎clinical text).⏎• Patient-facing navigation and care coordination supports that improve adherence and⏎reduce friction across care settings (see Q9).⏎See also: To accelerate learning on ‘what works,’ HHS can support standardized reporting and⏎privacy-preserving benchmarking (see Q10).⏎7. Organizational roles and administrative hurdles for adoption⏎Adoption decisions are typically shaped by clinical leadership, operational leadership,⏎compliance/privacy, IT/security leadership, and (increasingly) AI governance committees or⏎enterprise risk management functions.⏎Primary administrative hurdles include:⏎• Unclear ownership for AI lifecycle management and monitoring across clinical, IT, and⏎compliance functions.⏎• Procurement bottlenecks and contract limitations that impede auditability or change⏎control.⏎• Data access and interoperability friction across EHRs and ancillary systems (see Q8).⏎• Training, credentialing, and change management requirements for clinicians and staff.⏎⏎• Security approvals and network integration constraints, especially where tools require cloud⏎connectivity.⏎See also: This is why HHS-wide coordination across CMS, ASTP/ONC, OCR, CIO/ONS, OGA, and⏎acquisition functions is essential.⏎8. Interoperability opportunities; data types, standards, and benchmarking⏎tools⏎Interoperability expands the attack surface and secondary-use risk unless paired with access⏎control, provenance, and enforceable training boundaries.⏎Enhanced interoperability can widen market opportunities and accelerate AI where it improves⏎data provenance, reduces integration friction, and enables benchmarking—while still respecting⏎governance boundaries on secondary use and learning accumulation.⏎Priority opportunities include:⏎• Standardized representations of clinical workflows and outcomes (including⏎provenance/lineage metadata).⏎• Consistent patient identity and record linkage across care settings.⏎• Data standards and benchmarking tools that support monitoring and post-deployment⏎evaluation (see Q4).⏎• Mechanisms for privacy-preserving benchmarking and federated evaluation where direct⏎pooling is impractical.⏎See also: Interoperability policy should explicitly distinguish exchange for care delivery from⏎secondary use for model training and learning accumulation.⏎9. Patient/caregiver desired challenges and concerns⏎Patients increasingly want clarity not only on who sees their data, but who benefits from⏎derived learning and what recourse exists.⏎Patients and caregivers often prioritize improvements that reduce friction, shorten wait times,⏎improve communication, and increase transparency about decisions affecting care access and⏎quality.⏎Common concerns include:⏎• Privacy and security of sensitive health information, including secondary uses beyond direct⏎care.⏎• Unclear accountability when AI influences clinical decisions or access to services.⏎• Bias and inequitable treatment, especially when monitoring and recourse mechanisms are⏎weak.⏎⏎• Reduced human agency or difficulty obtaining explanations, corrections, and escalation⏎pathways.⏎See also: These concerns reinforce the need for lifecycle governance, auditability, and clear⏎decision rights (see Q3–Q5).⏎10. AI research priorities; literature on impact and costs/benefits/transfers⏎Prioritize methods to measure learning accumulation and value transfer across stakeholders,⏎and governance models that integrate privacy, sovereignty, and security operationally.⏎HHS research priorities that would accelerate responsible adoption include implementation⏎science for clinical AI, workflow-aware evaluation methods, and scalable approaches to⏎monitoring and governance.⏎Priority research areas include:⏎• Methods for post-deployment monitoring, drift detection, and change control in⏎heterogeneous clinical settings.⏎• Human-centered evaluation methods that quantify workflow impact and clinician reliance⏎over time.⏎• Privacy-preserving benchmarking, federated evaluation, and secure data-sharing⏎architectures.⏎• Governance models for adaptive systems that integrate privacy, sovereignty, and security⏎considerations.⏎See also: HHS can help curate and translate literature into practical evaluation playbooks and⏎clarify how costs/benefits/value transfer are measured across providers, payers, vendors, and⏎patients.⏎Closing⏎EHY Consulting LLC appreciates the opportunity to contribute to this RFI and supports HHS’s⏎efforts to advance responsible, effective, and trustworthy adoption of AI in clinical care. These⏎comments are intended to be practical and implementable, highlighting workflow-level realities⏎and second-order effects so that accelerated adoption does not outpace governance of safety,⏎trust, and resilience. We welcome continued dialogue to clarify implementation challenges and⏎governance considerations as policy and practice evolve.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0224♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Emily♔Taylor♔♔Kansas♔♔United States♔Health Catalyst♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly my bigger question for HHS right now is why my supplemental coverage through BlueCross went up 22% this past renewal cycle. January 2025, I open the invoice and just stared at it. That's what feels like it needs investigating to me more than anything else in this document.⏎⏎I get that this RFI is about AI in clinical settings and I do have thoughts on that from my work, but the insurance cost issue keeps pulling my attention back because it affects everything downstream. If premiums are climbing that fast for hospital administrators who know how to navigate the system, what's happening to patients who don't?⏎⏎On the actual AI question - the reimbursement side is a mess and I don't think HHS fully appreciates how many separate frameworks a mid-size hospital is trying to satisfy at once. We're not talking about two or three payer requirements that differ slightly. It's closer to four or five distinct compliance structures depending on which payer we're talking about and whether the tool touches billing versus clinical documentation versus something else entirely. The non-medical device category is especially confusing because nobody seems to agree on where liability sits if a tool assists a decision but isn't technically making one. I've had legal counsel give me two completely different answers on that in the same quarter.⏎⏎So yes I support HHS looking at reimbursement modernization but I'd really push back on the idea that clearer payment pathways alone will move the needle. The liability question has to get answered at the same time or CFOs like me are just going to keep delaying deployment because the downside risk isn't worth it yet. That calculus doesn't change just because CMS updates a billing code.⏎⏎Back to my premium situation though - 22% is not a rounding error. I've asked my broker twice to break down what's actually driving that increase and the explanation I got back was basically inflation and utilization trends which tells me nothing. If this department wants to build trust with health sector professionals around AI adoption, maybe start by making sure the people working in hospitals can actually understand their own insurance costs.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0225♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Providence / Providence St. Joseph Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Providence / Providence St. Joseph Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Providence⏎1801 Lind Avenue SW⏎Renton, WA 98057⏎providence.org⏎February 20, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: RIN 0955-AA13; Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎On behalf of Providence, thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback in response to the Request⏎for Information (RFI) on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical⏎Care that was published in the Federal Register on December 23, 2025.⏎Providence is a non-profit health system in the United States with a seven-state footprint that spans⏎Alaska, California, Montana, New Mexico, Oregon, and Texas. Our diverse family of organizations⏎employs 117,000 people who serve in 51 hospitals, more than 1,000 clinics, a health plan with more⏎than 600,000 beneficiaries, senior services, home health, hospice, PACE, housing, and many other health⏎and educational services. As a mission-driven organization, we are committed to understanding and⏎responding to the needs of the many communities we serve and to providing high-quality health care⏎for all. We have a special focus on serving hard working Americans who, despite their personal drive and⏎commitment to provide for their families, must depend on Medicaid coverage for access to care. Each⏎year we work to provide care and services where they are needed most, including investments in⏎community benefit that totaled $1.9 billion in 2024. Together, we are transforming care with a holistic⏎and deeply compassionate approach to medicine.⏎In this letter, we offer feedback on six areas of the RFI:⏎- Gaps in Regulation⏎- Accreditation to Promote Innovation⏎- Incentives for EHR Vendors⏎- Research and Development⏎- Health Care Organization Adoption⏎- Patient Expectations⏎1⏎⏎Gaps in Regulation⏎Providence appreciates HHS’s goal to establish a regulatory scheme for AI use that is well understood,⏎predictable, and proportionate to any risks presented. Clear compliance expectations will help spur⏎innovation while protecting patients and their information. From our experience developing and⏎deploying AI-powered tools, we have identified gaps in regulation that have prevented clinical AI⏎adoption.⏎Most importantly, patients need federal regulations to set expectations about consent for the use of AI⏎in clinical care. The patchwork of state-level consent rules has paved an unreasonable pathway for both⏎patients and providers to be confident in when consent is needed and how to appropriately secure it.⏎For instance, can patients place restrictions on an AI tool accessing their personal information in the way⏎they can request restrictions on the use and disclosure of their protected health information (PHI) under⏎existing federal rules? How does the HIPAA privacy rule interact with the use of AI on PHI when the⏎specific AI tool is owned by the provider, or a business associate, or another third party? Current⏎regulations on patient privacy and consent have not been able to keep pace with the rapid development⏎of AI tools in health care or the growing expectations of patients to know exactly when and how their⏎PHI will interact with an AI-powered tool.⏎Similarly, providers and AI developers need federal regulations to outline accountability standards.⏎When all entities take the required steps to vet new tools, protect patient rights, and appropriately⏎apply AI in the clinical setting, who should be responsible when the tool fails and a never-event occurs?⏎Federal accreditation or certification of AI tools would help all parties better understand their role in the⏎vetting process, but even the best technology can fail. Providers need direction and support from⏎federal regulators about where accountability should lie as we endeavor to negotiate with national⏎electronic health record (EHR) vendors and AI companies who have greater negotiating power than even⏎the most sophisticated health systems. HHS regulations in this area will help all parties keep the patient⏎at the center of those negotiations.⏎Providence greatly appreciates the president’s Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial⏎Intelligence executive order aimed at preventing 50 discordant state rules on the use of AI. As a multi-⏎state health system, we believe the federal government is in the best position to dictate AI use⏎standards that benefit patients and providers while supporting rapid innovation. At the same time, HHS⏎should describe which areas of regulation can fall within a state’s purview to make it clear what role if⏎any the states should play in health care AI. Over the past year, many states have taken legislation⏎action to regulate the use of AI in health care sensing that the federal government intended this issue to⏎be a state one. It is important for HHS to clearly outline the states’ roles and responsibilities in⏎regulation and enforcement so that provider and developer expectations are clear and hospital⏎resources are not futilely spent on compliance with state legislative schemes that will eventually be⏎preempted by federal rules.⏎Likewise, regulations should outline the areas in which providers will maintain discretion, for instance to⏎reject AI tools, apply more stringent vetting standards, or provide patient education and options about⏎their care.⏎2⏎⏎AI in Non-Medical Devices⏎One specific gap in regulation concerns the use of AI technology in non-medical devices. The FDA⏎has published guidance for digital health innovators that seek to use AI such as large language⏎models and machine learning in medical devices classified under the software as a medical device⏎definition. However, there is still a gap in how the federal regulatory landscape, including FDA and⏎HHS reimbursement like Medicare and Medicaid, will treat AI tools used in non-medical devices. We⏎believe this is preventing developers from bringing more of these tools to market.⏎Recently on January 6, the FDA announced that it will ease regulation of non-medical digital health⏎devices that do not make medical claims about diagnosis or treatment. Providence certainly shares⏎the FDA Commissioner’s goal to move regulations “at Silicon Valley speed,” but this lack of⏎regulatory standards is a barrier to clinical adoption. As described below, health care providers are⏎wary of adopting AI-enabled devices into clinical practice without fully vetting the technology and its⏎risks, but with federal regulatory parameters including approval from the FDA providers will be able⏎to smooth their internal vetting process to get the technology to patients sooner.⏎Accreditation to Promote Innovation⏎HHS asked for input on how it can support private sector accreditation or credentialing to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care. Providence fully supports a nationally recognized⏎accreditation path for AI tools. Absent accreditation by a third party, health care providers must invest⏎their own time and funds to determine whether each AI tool is safe to adopt. This not only drains⏎resources that hospitals could direct to patient care and workforce development, but it certainly slows⏎the adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎Providence is proud of the standardized risk stratification process that we have developed to examine⏎and improve AI tools before we integrate them into clinical practice. Using this process, Providence staff⏎plots the tool’s placement on a spectrum from research to direct patient care, along which patient fear⏎and outcome risk grows as the tool moves closer to the bedside. Our system also adjusts for patient⏎complexity. This carefully crafted stratification has informed our deployment of AI tools so that⏎executives and clinicians are informed about the relative risk of new tools and Providence can keep pace⏎with private innovations. But a national accreditation program would ensure all health care providers⏎have the option to move at the speed of innovation and all patients can be informed about the rigors⏎these accredited tools have passed.⏎To promote private sector accreditation or similar vetting, HHS should establish a forum for public-⏎private partnerships to take on this work. The financial and administrative burden of a new accreditation⏎program should not fall to the federal government, but a public-private relationship would help⏎strengthen the profile of the accreditation and lend trust for providers and patients. By requiring⏎accreditation for any applicable AI tools deployed for clinical care, HHS would accelerate the process,⏎alleviate the financial burden on providers who are vetting on their own today, and remove some⏎uncertainty about risk by knowing which parameters the accredited tools have already met.⏎Given the speed at which AI technology changes and improves, it will be important for any accreditation⏎process to include frequent auditing to maintain compliance. To reduce the burden on the accrediting⏎bodies and developers, this ongoing monitoring could take the form of a public registry of outcomes,⏎similar to the procedure that applies to clinical researchers undergoing clinical trials, to build public⏎3⏎⏎confidence in the use of AI. Vendors and providers need to make sure patients continue to be confident⏎and be in safe hands with clinical AI tools.⏎Incentives for Electronic Health Record (EHR) Vendors⏎There is little doubt that the EHR industry currently stands as a near monopoly with limited competition⏎among a handful of large companies offering core EHR software for health care providers across the⏎country. Within that market dynamic, it will be important for HHS to take steps to promote competition⏎from third party AI software developers so that innovation is not stalled and patients have access to the⏎latest and greatest improvements. That involves two key measures: 1) promoting interoperability of⏎third-party software into the core EHR products currently occupying the market; and 2) opening the⏎market to all interested developers to keep prices down.⏎Providence encourages EHR vendors to provide certain AI functionality to their customers with the⏎company’s baseline product. Vendors should not be permitted to keep AI away from providers who are⏎unable to afford add-on after add-on.⏎We recommend the ASTP clearly outline when and how its certified EHR vendors are permitted to⏎market or describe provider and patient tools as “AI-enabled” or “powered by AI.” In the realm of⏎medicine, AI has been a part of daily practice for many years – i.e., diagnostic aides, speech-to-text⏎notetaking - but patients today understand the presence of the “AI-enabled” stamp very differently than⏎decades ago. As the federal government proceeds with standard patient consent and/or accreditation⏎requirements for AI technology in clinical care, it’s important to make sure these requirements only⏎apply when it is truly necessary for patient protection. For example, if Providence were to encounter a⏎new software add-on option that the developer described as “powered by AI,” it would trigger our⏎internal risk stratification review before we could agree to adopt the tool and integrate it into our⏎practice. It is important in that circumstance for the EHR vendor to honestly describe the use of AI in⏎development of that tool before resources are spent to analyze the tool and its deployment is stalled⏎when it truly doesn’t use the kind of AI that required such a robust review.⏎Research and Development⏎HHS is interested in suggestions on accelerating care delivery research and implementation science, as⏎well as AI entrepreneurship in health care. Providence recommends HHS focus its power by prioritizing⏎research on the use of genetic data in AI tools and how to appropriately de-identify PHI as AI tools are⏎developed to protect patient privacy.⏎Health Care Organization Adoption⏎In our experience, the adoption of clinical AI tools by health care organizations and specifically hospitals⏎is influenced by three factors: 1) the broad network of third party providers that are involved in the⏎hospital’s ecosystem; 2) workforce concerns about job losses and new risks; and 3) lack of funding⏎support for new technology infrastructure needed to support AI-powered tools.⏎In the best sense possible, health care is a patchwork of people and organizations working together to⏎follow a patient and integrate their care needs. This makes it difficult for all providers in that patient’s⏎network to understand the technological sophistication and compliance of the others. This uncertainty⏎has prevented the use of AI in some instances, because Providence clinicians cannot be sure whether⏎the patient’s medical record contains any AI-generated content and if so, whether that has been⏎4⏎⏎reviewed by a live clinician before it was sent to our hospital. We cannot be sure whether a third-party⏎clinician used an AI tool to match a patient’s symptoms to a diagnosis and what level of consent the⏎patient has given about the use of that tool. Once the patient and their record travels more than two or⏎three degrees beyond our health system, we simply cannot know whether AI was used or the level of⏎risk it carries. Until all providers are operating under the same standard regulations from HHS or the⏎tools are nationally accredited, health care organizations will be wary to fully adopt AI across the care⏎continuum.⏎Health care organizations are not immune from the public’s gut reaction that AI tools will take away⏎human jobs, even in an industry that is as human centered as health. Organizational leaders are faced⏎with opposition from union voices about the future integration of AI tools in clinical practice. Even⏎ambient listening tools that use AI to make clinical notes following a patient visit and cut down on the⏎clinical charting needed can spark resistance from practitioner unions who are worried about the⏎resulting staff downsizing or increased visit frequency expectations. It is difficult to imagine how HHS⏎can help alleviate those fears in the workforce since they are shared among all industries where AI can⏎encroach on human jobs, but it’s important for HHS to understand that this is a barrier for health care⏎organizations moving forward with AI.⏎The third barrier is a lack of funding support for AI tools, and this goes beyond the funding needed to⏎purchase the actual tools EHR vendors and third parties are offering. AI-powered technology requires⏎more powerful wireless internet and Bluetooth capabilities. At a basic level, that means making space in⏎the IT closet or adding new rooms to house additional wiring and fans. At a broader level, it means⏎putting funding aside for staff training and compliance monitoring. HHS and its Centers for Medicare⏎and Medicaid Services (CMS) can work together to ensure funding support for AI adoption by offering⏎one-time grants for qualifying hospitals (i.e., rural and safety net hospitals) to make the IT upgrades⏎needed for AI integration in their facilities and by ensuring Medicare reimbursement policies can keep⏎pace with clinical acceleration that is expected as AI adoption grows. That is, clinicians would have the⏎freedom to focus on the patient during their check-up rather than making notes on their computer and⏎presumably those visits get shorter over time. Medicare coding and reimbursement policies need to be⏎mindful of the ongoing costs of supporting AI technologies that are making those visits more efficient⏎and causing the clinician to bill for a shorter time interval code with a typically lower payment rate. CMS⏎will need to examine how to make those payment rates sustainable for AI-powered clinical practice.⏎Patient Expectations⏎As President Trump described it in his executive order in December, AI is both a technological revolution⏎and a race. It is a topic of everyday conversation with patients around the country and their focus in our⏎experience has been singular: tell me when you are using AI with my information. The barrier we have in⏎meeting this expectation is the widely varied understanding on what “AI” means to patients. AI⏎technology is so prevalent in our lives, so what do patients really want to or need to know about when it⏎comes to their clinical care? Every email that uses autocorrect is using AI, but surely patients are not⏎interested in giving consent to their physician using spell check tools. However, if a clinician were relying⏎on a machine learning diagnostic tool to flag a potential cancerous lesion on an MRI image, the patient⏎would want to understand how the clinician used that AI tool and what steps he might have taken to⏎confirm the tool’s result. Providers and patients need direction from HHS about when to disclose AI and⏎how much disclosure is necessary and reasonable.⏎5⏎⏎This is where standardized notice and consent templates from HHS would help. Providence would⏎appreciate an opportunity to work with HHS to develop simple patient-facing language to educate the⏎public on how AI might be used for their clinical care. For example, a hospital could deliver notice to⏎patients in the emergency room to state “Your care team might use technology powered by AI that will⏎help them reach a diagnosis.” If national accreditation can be established, patient consent could involve⏎a statement such as “clinicians in our facility use AI technology certified by a federal agency, do you⏎consent to the use of that technology?” This standardization, which was similarly used for patient opt-in⏎to health information exchange, when provided by HHS with small customization options can go a long⏎way to assuage patient fears about AI technology and meet their expectation to be fully informed about⏎the presence of AI in their lives.⏎Conclusion⏎Providence is energized by the federal government’s interest in accelerating AI adoption in clinical care⏎and we look forward to sharing our experience and best practices with HHS and other providers as this⏎effort continues.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback in response to the RFI. We hope you find our input⏎informative.⏎Sincerely,⏎Ali Santore⏎Chief Communications & External Affairs Officer⏎Providence⏎6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0226♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Pope, Renee L, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Renee L♔Pope♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎Federal Register: 90 FR 60108 (Document No. 2025-23641) • RIN: 0955-AA13 • Published:⏎December 23, 2025 • Comment Deadline: February 23, 2026⏎Submitted by⏎Renee L. Pope, BSC, MPA, DHA Candidate⏎I. Introduction and Commenter Background⏎I submit this comment to support HHS in identifying concrete actions to accelerate safe,⏎effective, and equitable adoption of AI as part of clinical care—particularly in post-acute and⏎long-term care (PALTC) settings where readiness barriers are structural and persistent.⏎My perspective reflects extensive operational experience in PALTC settings, including exposure⏎to interdisciplinary workflows, documentation systems, incident reporting practices, and⏎regulatory compliance pressures across multiple facilities and contexts. My systems-level⏎governance perspective is also informed by my scholarly work, Human-Led Embodiment &⏎Co-Regulatory Augmentation (H-LECA), which emphasizes continuity, risk containment, and⏎human-led control in longitudinal human–AI interaction.⏎The RFI appropriately centers three federal levers—regulation, reimbursement, and research⏎& development—to reduce uncertainty, foster trust, align incentives, and accelerate adoption of⏎AI in clinical care.⏎II. General Response to the RFI (Cross-Cutting Themes)⏎A. Readiness is uneven across sectors; PALTC faces distinct foundational⏎barriers⏎Health care AI adoption is not limited by model capability alone. In PALTC, binding constraints⏎are frequently infrastructure, workforce bandwidth, data integrity, and organizational⏎instability—conditions repeatedly identified in long-term care implementation research as⏎barriers to introducing AI-enabled solutions and other digital technologies at scale (Groeneveld⏎et al., 2025; Hassan et al., 2024; Neves et al., 2024; Wong et al., 2024).⏎B. “Clinical AI” adoption will fail if the first wave is not operationally safe⏎⏎For PALTC, high-risk “clinical” AI should not be the entry point. A staged acceleration strategy⏎is more likely to be safe and scalable: prioritize non-clinical workflow AI (e.g., scheduling,⏎supply chain, documentation quality controls, administrative burden reduction), then progress to⏎higher-risk use only after readiness benchmarks are met. This aligns with evidence that success⏎depends on sociotechnical conditions, workflow integration, and sustained operational capacity⏎rather than tool availability alone (Hassan et al., 2024; Salwei et al., 2021).⏎C. Continuity, change-management, and post-deployment monitoring should be⏎treated as safety requirements⏎Predictability, public trust, and risk-proportionate oversight require attention to what happens⏎after deployment: workflow drift, updates, outages, and data shifts can degrade performance and⏎worsen inequities if monitoring is weak (Davis et al., 2025; Subasri et al., 2025). A continuity-⏎aware approach—incorporating detection of destabilizing changes and structured repair⏎pathways—aligns with lifecycle governance expectations for clinical AI (National Institute of⏎Standards and Technology [NIST], 2023).⏎III. Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. Biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for⏎health care and its adoption/use in clinical care⏎(a) Infrastructure barriers (PALTC)⏎• Unreliable connectivity and unmapped Wi-Fi coverage in resident care areas.⏎• Legacy or limited call-light systems with failures and limited auditability.⏎• Environmental and operational conditions that prevent “always available” digital⏎workflows.⏎Long-term care implementation studies report that infrastructure readiness and organizational⏎capacity strongly shape whether AI-enabled tools can be implemented safely and sustained over⏎time (Groeneveld et al., 2025; Neves et al., 2024; Wong et al., 2024).⏎(b) Data integrity barriers⏎• Documentation structures can produce ambiguous or inconsistent entries that⏎undermine data reliability for AI training and inference.⏎• Under high workload, documentation completeness drops, degrading model inputs and⏎increasing downstream risk.⏎⏎These concerns align with broader evidence that adoption depends on trustworthy data pipelines⏎and workflow-aligned data capture, and that failures frequently arise from sociotechnical⏎breakdowns rather than model architecture alone (Hassan et al., 2024; Salwei et al., 2021).⏎(c) Workforce and training barriers⏎• Chronic staffing shortages and turnover reduce capacity for onboarding, calibration,⏎monitoring, and governance.⏎• Training constraints reduce digital literacy and safe adoption readiness.⏎Long-term care adoption literature consistently identifies workforce bandwidth, training⏎supports, and staff acceptance as central determinants of success (Hassan et al., 2024; Wong et⏎al., 2024).⏎(d) Financial and corporate barriers⏎• Underinvestment in infrastructure and frequent ownership/management turnover disrupt⏎long-horizon modernization planning.⏎These constraints are consistent with the broader operating environment in skilled nursing⏎facilities and the documented prevalence of ownership changes (Assistant Secretary for Planning⏎and Evaluation, 2024).⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design⏎changes should HHS prioritize, and why? Include CFR⏎citations where applicable.⏎A. Regulation — baseline digital safety readiness for PALTC⏎HHS should clarify expectations that facilities meet minimum digital reliability and⏎governance readiness before implementing AI-dependent workflows in resident care contexts.⏎One pathway is to leverage existing operational and quality improvement obligations under 42⏎CFR Part 483, including:⏎• 42 CFR § 483.70 (Administration)⏎• 42 CFR § 483.75 (Quality Assurance and Performance Improvement; QAPI)⏎These sections establish administration and QAPI responsibilities; HHS can provide interpretive⏎guidance and practical readiness benchmarks that support safe, staged adoption and reduce⏎avoidable failures.⏎B. Regulation — incident reporting and governance alignment⏎⏎Fragmented definitions and pathways spanning state and federal expectations can increase⏎avoidable harm and legal confusion. HHS can reduce risk by issuing harmonized guidance and⏎model workflows supporting consistent classification and escalation consistent with lifecycle risk⏎management principles (NIST, 2023).⏎C. Reimbursement — tie incentives to demonstrated readiness and burden⏎reduction⏎CMS payment and program levers can accelerate adoption by funding readiness first and⏎rewarding tools that reduce administrative burden and improve safety. In PALTC, incentives⏎should prioritize:⏎• infrastructure modernization and digital reliability⏎• documentation quality and workflow integrity⏎• staged implementation linked to measurable readiness thresholds⏎This approach is consistent with evidence that workflow integration and organizational capacity⏎shape whether technologies reduce burden or add risk (Hassan et al., 2024; Salwei et al., 2021).⏎D. Programmatic changes — workforce AI literacy and implementation supports⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by supporting:⏎• AI literacy programs tailored to PALTC roles (CNA, nursing, administration)⏎• implementation toolkits (workflow mapping, minimum training, governance templates)⏎• demonstration projects with explicit safety metrics and transparency requirements⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role should HHS play?⏎Key issues include:⏎• Liability and accountability ambiguity among developers, facilities, clinicians, and⏎corporate owners for workflow-support AI outputs.⏎• Data provenance risk: flawed documentation inputs can generate flawed outputs,⏎creating legal exposure and patient harm.⏎• Privacy/security operationalization failures: breakdowns often occur at the workflow⏎layer (access controls, device handling, vendor management), not only the algorithm⏎layer.⏎HHS’s role can include model procurement language, governance templates, and minimum⏎evaluation/monitoring expectations consistent with recognized risk management frameworks for⏎AI systems (NIST, 2023; NIST, 2024).⏎⏎4. For non-medical devices, promising AI evaluation⏎methods (pre/post), metrics, robustness testing, and how⏎HHS should support⏎Recommended evaluation stack (pre-deployment through post-deployment)⏎• human-centered workflow evaluation (usability + burden impact)⏎• data quality validation (“fitness for use”)⏎• robustness testing (including outage/fallback behavior)⏎• bias and error monitoring appropriate to the use case⏎• post-deployment drift monitoring and incident reporting pathways⏎These elements align with established reporting and evaluation guidance for clinical AI⏎interventions and trials (Liu et al., 2020; Rivera et al., 2020; Vasey et al., 2022).⏎Continuity and change-management as safety properties (H-LECA-aligned)⏎Where AI tools become embedded longitudinally, evaluation should include:⏎• continuity expectations (predictable behavior within defined bounds)⏎• rupture handling (updates/outages/policy shifts communicated clearly; repair pathways⏎exist)⏎• auditability and user override as core criteria for safety and trust⏎These aims are consistent with emerging evidence on harmful data shifts and fairness drift and⏎the need for proactive post-deployment monitoring (Davis et al., 2025; Subasri et al., 2025).⏎HHS support mechanisms⏎HHS can accelerate best practice through:⏎• cooperative agreements and demonstration pilots⏎• grants for infrastructure readiness and evaluation capacity⏎• prize competitions for PALTC-specific safety monitoring tools⏎• public-private partnerships and applied implementation science⏎5. How can HHS support private sector accreditation,⏎certification, testing, and credentialing?⏎⏎HHS can catalyze:⏎• sector-specific accreditation standards for PALTC readiness (infrastructure +⏎governance)⏎• certification expectations for workflow AI safety (audit logs, override, downtime⏎behavior, error reporting)⏎• role-based credentialing for facility staff responsible for AI supervision and monitoring⏎This approach aligns with recognized needs for consistent governance and lifecycle oversight for⏎AI systems integrated into care delivery (NIST, 2023; Vasey et al., 2022).⏎6. Where have AI tools met/exceeded expectations vs fallen⏎short? What novel tools have greatest potential?⏎From an operational adoption perspective:⏎• AI has performed better in environments with robust infrastructure and consistent data⏎governance.⏎• In infrastructure-limited and data-inconsistent settings, tools tied to unreliable inputs or⏎connectivity often fall short or create unacceptable risk.⏎Implementation literature reinforces that outcomes depend on sociotechnical conditions and⏎workflow fit (Groeneveld et al., 2025; Hassan et al., 2024; Neves et al., 2024; Wong et al.,⏎2024).⏎Highest near-term potential for PALTC:⏎• administrative/workflow automation (scheduling, supplies, documentation quality)⏎• call-light response analytics and alert auditing⏎• staffing prediction and workload balancing⏎• carefully validated monitoring tools with explicit privacy constraints⏎7. Which roles/decision makers influence adoption, and what⏎are the administrative hurdles?⏎Influential roles commonly include:⏎• corporate leadership/ownership⏎• administrators and Directors of Nursing⏎• compliance/QAPI leadership and MDS coordination⏎⏎• EHR/IT vendors and integrators⏎Primary hurdles include turnover, competing compliance demands, and unclear accountability⏎for technology failures—barriers consistent with organizational-level findings in long-term care⏎technology adoption research (Hassan et al., 2024; Wong et al., 2024).⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market⏎opportunities and accelerate AI?⏎Priority needs include:⏎• stronger PALTC ↔ hospital ↔ lab ↔ pharmacy ↔ payer data exchange⏎• more standardized PALTC documentation structures⏎• benchmarking datasets and common definitions for outcomes relevant to PALTC safety⏎and quality⏎Interoperability standards such as HL7 FHIR and associated implementation guidance are⏎consistently identified as key enablers for scalable exchange and AI-ready data integration (Ayaz⏎et al., 2021; Gazzarata et al., 2024; Tabari et al., 2024).⏎9. What challenges do patients and caregivers want⏎addressed, and what concerns do they have?⏎Patients and caregivers commonly seek improved responsiveness, communication, safety⏎monitoring, and consistency in care delivery, while expressing concerns about privacy, reduced⏎human contact, and mistrust when systems are opaque or inconsistent (Neves et al., 2024).⏎10. AI research priorities for HHS; and published findings⏎about impacts and cost/benefit approaches⏎A. Research priorities⏎• infrastructure readiness and digital reliability as prerequisites to safe AI⏎• data quality improvement and documentation integrity interventions⏎• implementation science for workflow AI (burden reduction first)⏎• post-deployment monitoring, drift detection, and incident reporting methods⏎⏎• evaluation approaches for monitoring tools used with older adult populations with⏎explicit consent and privacy safeguards⏎These priorities align with evidence that sustained clinical AI benefit requires rigorous⏎evaluation and ongoing lifecycle oversight, including proactive detection of data shifts and⏎fairness drift (Davis et al., 2025; Liu et al., 2020; Subasri et al., 2025; Vasey et al., 2022).⏎B. H-LECA as a governance-informed research agenda (appropriate, limited⏎inclusion)⏎H-LECA does not claim clinical outcomes; its relevance is governance and safety design:⏎• continuity and behavior stability as a safety target distinct from “memory”⏎• rupture risk and repair mechanisms (updates/outages/policy shifts)⏎• auditability, interpretability, override, and bounded delegation in higher-risk workflows⏎HHS could support research and demonstrations that treat continuity/rupture safeguards as⏎measurable safety properties in longitudinal deployments, consistent with lifecycle governance⏎and monitoring expectations (NIST, 2023; Subasri et al., 2025).⏎IV. Conclusion⏎HHS can accelerate adoption of AI in clinical care by pairing innovation with prerequisite⏎readiness and risk-proportionate governance. For PALTC, a staged approach is most likely to⏎be safe and scalable:⏎1. modernize infrastructure and data reliability,⏎2. reduce burden via operational AI first,⏎3. require rigorous evaluation and post-deployment monitoring, and⏎4. expand to higher-risk clinical use cases only after readiness benchmarks are met.⏎A continuity-aware governance lens, including predictable behavior, rupture handling,⏎auditability, and human-led control, can reduce preventable failures that undermine trust and⏎safety in real deployments (Davis et al., 2025; NIST, 2023; Subasri et al., 2025).⏎References⏎Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation. (2024). Changes in ownership of skilled nursing⏎facilities from 2016 to 2021: Variations by size, occupancy rate, penalty amount, and type of⏎ownership (Research brief). U.S. Department of Health and Human Services.⏎⏎https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/856467326d4196842e5b3ce74df8931d/ChanO⏎wnVarRBJune2024.pdf⏎Ayaz, M., Pasha, M. F., Alzahrani, M. Y., Budiarto, R., & Stiawan, D. (2021). The Fast Health⏎Interoperability Resources (FHIR) standard: Systematic literature review of implementations,⏎applications, challenges and opportunities. JMIR Medical Informatics, 9(7), e21929.⏎https://doi.org/10.2196/21929⏎Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, National Center for Health Statistics. (n.d.).⏎FastStats: Nursing home care. https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/nursing-home-care.htm⏎Cruz Rivera, S., Liu, X., Chan, A.-W., Denniston, A. K., Calvert, M. J., Altman, D. G., Moher,⏎D., & SPIRIT-AI and CONSORT-AI Steering Group. (2020). Guidelines for clinical trial⏎protocols for interventions involving artificial intelligence: The SPIRIT-AI extension. Nature⏎Medicine, 26(9), 1351–1363. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-020-1037-7⏎Davis, S. E., Dorn, C., Park, D. J., & Matheny, M. E. (2025). Emerging algorithmic bias:⏎Fairness drift as the next dimension of model maintenance and sustainability. Journal of the⏎American Medical Informatics Association, 32(5), 845–854.⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf039⏎Gazzarata, R., Almeida, J., Lindsköld, L., Cangioli, G., Gaeta, E., Fico, G., & Chronaki, C.⏎(2024). HL7 Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (HL7 FHIR) in digital healthcare⏎ecosystems for chronic disease management: Scoping review. International Journal of Medical⏎Informatics, 189, 105507. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ijmedinf.2024.105507⏎Groeneveld, S., Groeneveld, S. W. M., van der Laan, N., & others. (2025). Factors that influence⏎the implementation of AI-driven lifestyle monitoring in long-term care for older adults. The⏎Gerontologist, 65(12), gnaf230. https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnaf230⏎Hassan, M., Kushniruk, A., & Borycki, E. (2024). Barriers to and facilitators of artificial⏎intelligence adoption in health care: Scoping review. JMIR Human Factors, 11, e48633.⏎https://doi.org/10.2196/48633⏎Hung, L., Chow, B., Shadarevian, J., O’Neill, R., Berndt, A., Wallsworth, C., Gregorio, M.,⏎Mann, J., & Son, C. (2023). Facilitators and barriers to using AI-enabled robots with older adults⏎in long-term care: A scoping review. BMJ Open, 13(10), e075278.⏎https://doi.org/10.1136/bmjopen-2023-075278⏎Liu, X., Cruz Rivera, S., Moher, D., Calvert, M. J., Denniston, A. K., Chan, A.-W., Darzi, A.,⏎Holmes, C., Yau, C., Ashrafian, H., Deeks, J. J., Ferrante di Ruffano, L., Faes, L., Keane, P. A.,⏎Vollmer, S. J., Lee, A.-Y., Jonas, A., Esteva, A., Beam, A. L., … Collins, G. S. (2020).⏎Reporting guidelines for clinical trial reports for interventions involving artificial intelligence:⏎The CONSORT-AI extension. Nature Medicine, 26(9), 1364–1374.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-020-1034-x⏎⏎National Institute of Standards and Technology. (2023). Artificial intelligence risk management⏎framework (AI RMF 1.0) (NIST AI 100-1). https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.100-1⏎National Institute of Standards and Technology. (2024). Artificial intelligence risk management⏎framework: Generative artificial intelligence profile (NIST AI 600-1).⏎https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.AI.600-1⏎Neves, B. B., Omori, M., Petersen, A., Vered, M., & Carter, A. (2024). Navigating artificial⏎intelligence in care homes: Competing stakeholder views of trust and logics of care. Social⏎Science & Medicine, 358, 117187. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.socscimed.2024.117187⏎Salwei, M. E., Carayon, P., Hoonakker, P. L. T., & Schoofs Hundt, A. (2021). Workflow⏎integration analysis of a human factors-based clinical decision support in the emergency⏎department. Applied Ergonomics, 97, 103498. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.apergo.2021.103498⏎Subasri, V., Duval, A., Barda, N., & others. (2025). Detecting and remediating harmful data⏎shifts for the responsible deployment of clinical AI models. JAMA Network Open, 8(6),⏎e2513685. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.13685⏎Tabari, P., Costagliola, G., De Rosa, M., & Boeker, M. (2024). State-of-the-art Fast Healthcare⏎Interoperability Resources (FHIR)-based data model and structure implementations: Systematic⏎scoping review. JMIR Medical Informatics, 12, e58445. https://doi.org/10.2196/58445⏎Vasey, B., Nagendran, M., Campbell, B., Clifton, D. A., Collins, G. S., Denaxas, S., Denniston,⏎A. K., Faes, L., Geerts, B., Ibrahim, M., & others. (2022). Reporting guideline for the early stage⏎clinical evaluation of decision support systems driven by artificial intelligence: DECIDE-AI.⏎BMJ, 377, e070904. https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj-2022-070904⏎Wong, K. L. Y., Hung, L., & Wong, J. (2024). Adoption of artificial intelligence–enabled robots⏎in long-term care homes by health care providers: A scoping review. JMIR Aging, 7, e55257.⏎https://doi.org/10.2196/55257♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0227♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Jones♔♔California♔♔United States♔American Thyroid Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My main question honestly is just about timing. When does something like this actually go into effect? I've been trying to plan ahead for retirement and healthcare costs are my single biggest concern at this point, I turn 65 in 2026 so the window matters a lot to me.⏎⏎I read through the summary a few times and I still can't quite tell if this is something that would change what happens when I walk into a clinic next year or if this is more of a 5-10 year horizon kind of thing. The way these rulemaking processes work I know there are comment periods and then final rules and then implementation deadlines and it just gets very hard to track as someone who isn't a healthcare lawyer.⏎⏎The part that caught my attention was about AI being used in clinical decision-making. I saw something on the news earlier this year about a hospital in Sacramento where a triage algorithm was apparently scoring patients differently based on their zip code, which seems like exactly the kind of thing that should be addressed before you start rolling this out more broadly. I don't know if that story was fully accurate but it stuck with me.⏎⏎Anyway I guess my actual comment is just to ask that whatever timeline HHS settles on, they make it publicly visible in plain language somewhere, not just in the federal register. I had to read this request for information three times before I understood it was asking for comments rather than announcing something that was already decided. That confusion probably keeps a lot of ordinary people from weighing in at all.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0228♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Lewis♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client — I'll call him Darnell, because he gave me permission to share his story in advocacy contexts — was 34 years old and in acute psychiatric crisis when an insurer's risk-stratification algorithm quietly scored him as low priority. This was in late 2022, through a county mental health program in central Indiana. Every clinical indicator I had access to said otherwise. It took me three weeks, somewhere around fourteen phone calls, and a formal written appeal before a human clinician actually looked at his file. By then he had already been discharged from case management.⏎⏎I don't know the name of the algorithm. The insurer wouldn't tell me. That's part of what I want HHS to understand when it talks about "accelerating adoption."⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to AI adoption and how to speed up deployment in clinical settings. I want to respond to a different question, which is: who bears the cost when these systems get it wrong, and do those people have any realistic way to push back?⏎⏎The answer, in my professional experience, is no. Not if you are poor, not if you are Black, not if you are navigating a mental health crisis, not if you don't have someone like me making calls on your behalf. Darnell had me. Most people don't. And I still barely got anywhere.⏎⏎What concerns me about the framing of this RFI is the word "accelerating." I understand why that word is there. I'm not completely opposed to AI in healthcare. There are probably real uses where it helps. But the populations I work with — people on Medicaid, elderly patients in rural counties, people with serious mental illness, people with limited English — they are not going to receive AI as a supplement to good human care. They're going to receive it as a replacement for good human care, because it's cheaper. That is the more likely outcome, and I think HHS knows this, and I think the "democratizing access" language in documents like this one is a way of not saying it out loud.⏎⏎There is a civil rights dimension to this that I feel like gets buried in the technical language. If an AI system produces racially disparate outcomes in how it prioritizes patients for services, that is a civil rights problem, not just a quality improvement problem. HHS has obligations under existing anti-discrimination law. I don't know the specific regulation numbers off the top of my head, but I know those obligations exist, and I know they apply here.⏎⏎What I would ask for, at minimum, is this: before any AI system is used to make coverage, prioritization, or resource allocation decisions for Medicaid patients or other vulnerable populations, there should be a required equity assessment. Not a voluntary one. Not a checkbox. An actual independent review of who the system disadvantages and how, before deployment, not after something goes wrong.⏎⏎I would also ask that there be a clear and accessible appeals process when an AI-generated recommendation affects someone's care. "Accessible" means accessible to someone in crisis. It means it can be completed in plain language. It means there is a real human being at the end of it within a timeframe that actually matters clinically.⏎⏎I keep thinking about the three weeks I spent on hold and writing letters while Darnell's situation deteriorated. I am a trained professional with institutional access and I still couldn't get a fast answer. What is the pathway for someone who doesn't have that?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0229♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Susan G. Komen, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan G. Komen♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎I write today on behalf of Susan G. Komen (Komen) in response to the Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI)⏎as part of clinical care (RFI).⏎Komen is the world’s leading nonprofit breast cancer organization representing the millions of people⏎who have been diagnosed with breast cancer. Komen has an unmatched, comprehensive 360-degree⏎approach to fighting this disease across all fronts – we advocate for patients, drive research⏎breakthroughs, improve access to high quality care, offer direct patient support and empower people⏎with trustworthy information. Komen is committed to supporting those affected by breast cancer today,⏎while tirelessly searching for tomorrow’s cures. We advocate on behalf of the estimated 324,600 people⏎in the United States who will be diagnosed with breast cancer and the 42,670 who will die from the⏎disease in 2026 alone.⏎Impact of Artificial Intelligence on the Breast Cancer Community⏎Komen appreciates HHS’ attention to this important issue, including the RFI’s queries on how current⏎regulations impact AI adoption and use in clinical care, and how to establish a predictable regulatory⏎posture which protects patients and public trust.⏎In breast cancer care, AI is an emerging and rapidly changing issue with the potential to revolutionize⏎clinical practices, but Komen believes robust guardrails and accountability mechanisms need to be⏎established and maintained to protect patients. AI can play a role in breast cancer screening, diagnosis,⏎staging, biomarker evaluation, prognostication and therapeutic response prediction.1 In mammography,⏎AI has the potential to reduce turnaround time on reading screenings, ease burden on the workforce,2⏎and improve efficacy.3 Currently, the Food & Drug Administration has approved roughly two dozen AI⏎1 Ahn JS, Shin S, Yang SA, Park EK, Kim KH, Cho SI, Ock CY, Kim S. Artificial Intelligence in Breast Cancer Diagnosis and Personalized Medicine. J⏎Breast Cancer. 2023 Oct;26(5):405-435. doi: 10.4048/jbc.2023.26.e45. PMID: 37926067; PMCID: PMC10625863.⏎2 Ahsen, M.E., Ayvaci, M.U.S., Mookerjee, R. et al. Economics of AI and human task sharing for decision making in screening mammography. Nat⏎Commun 16, 2289 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-025-57409-1⏎3 Lång K, Josefsson V, Larsson AM, Larsson S, Högberg C, Sartor H, Hofvind S, Andersson I, Rosso A. Artificial intelligence-supported screen⏎reading versus standard double reading in the Mammography Screening with Artificial Intelligence trial (MASAI): a clinical safety analysis of a⏎randomised, controlled, non-inferiority, single-blinded, screening accuracy study. Lancet Oncol. 2023 Aug;24(8):936-944. doi: 10.1016/S1470-⏎2045(23)00298-X. PMID: 37541274.⏎⏎products to help detect and diagnose cancer from mammograms.4 However, despite the promise of AI,⏎many challenges and questions remain.⏎Request for Information on Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎Patient Challenges and Concerns⏎Komen is concerned that use of AI in clinical care may expose patients to unexpected, increased costs.⏎Patients have reported being charged anywhere from $40-$100 extra for the use of AI to read⏎mammograms and without sufficient counseling or informed consent.5 Unexpected costs could deter⏎patients from seeking and completing life-saving breast cancer screening at regular intervals and may⏎also deter or delay adoption of appropriate technologies by patients or their providers.⏎Additionally, the data inputted into AI tools – both before and after deployment in a clinical setting – can⏎change its effectiveness. Research has shown that accessibility to bigger datasets from different sources⏎improves the performance of modeling.6 Right now, significant variation exists in the adoption of AI-⏎assisted mammography reading across the country which could change the quality of outcomes of the⏎technology. Clinics may work with different companies for AI-assisted mammography reading, and each⏎company’s algorithm may be trained on different datasets and achieve different end results. As such,⏎variability in algorithms is likely to increase over time as they learn and digest more data.⏎Model variation also could impact patients as the values encoded by AI algorithms affect their results.⏎For example, in breast cancer, a deep learning algorithm may prioritize minimizing false negatives over⏎minimizing false positives, perform differently depending on the characteristics of the breast tissue being⏎imaged, or not address all known risk factors.7 A major challenge in predicting breast cancer is the⏎creation of models that address these concerns and account for the conscious or unconscious bias⏎introduced by human coders. More research and evaluation are needed to ensure that only effective AI⏎models are being deployed and maintained in clinical settings.⏎Payment Policies⏎We encourage HHS to work with the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) to introduce new⏎billing codes which incentivize responsible use of AI. Despite FDA approvals of AI products to interpret⏎mammograms, no billing codes exist today for radiologists to use to charge health plans for their use.⏎Since providers cannot defray the costs of AI by billing insurance for its use, this lack of codes could be⏎contributing to the additional costs experienced by patients. Additional opportunities and information⏎on billing for the use of AI to interpret mammograms could create more predictability for patients and⏎providers alike.⏎CMS also could use reimbursement as an incentive for AI to be trained on standardized datasets which⏎are reflective of the general U.S. population and include requirements for periodically updating these⏎datasets. Providers and patients should be informed about the data for which AI technology is based,⏎4 U.S. Food & Drug Administration, Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Medical Devices, last updated Dec. 5, 2025, available at:⏎https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/artificial-intelligence-enabled-medical-devices?trk=article-ssr-frontend-⏎pulse_little-text-block; and Sheikh, Knvul (Apr. 9, 2025), A.I. Could Spot Breast Cancer Earlier. Should You Pay for It?, New York Times, available⏎at: https://www.nytimes.com/2024/04/08/well/live/ai-mammograms-breast-cancer.html.⏎5 Andrews, Michelle (Jan. 9, 2024), Mammography AI can cost patients extra. Is it worth it?, CBS News, available at:⏎https://www.cbsnews.com/news/mammogram-ai-cost-patients-is-it-worth-it/.⏎6 Rabiei R, Ayyoubzadeh SM, Sohrabei S, Esmaeili M, Atashi A. Prediction of Breast Cancer using Machine Learning Approaches. J Biomed Phys⏎Eng. 2022 Jun 1;12(3):297-308. doi: 10.31661/jbpe.v0i0.2109-1403. PMID: 35698545; PMCID: PMC9175124.⏎7 Stacy M. Carter, Wendy Rogers, Khin Than Win, Helen Frazer, Bernadette Richards, Nehmat Houssami. The ethical, legal and social⏎implications of using artificial intelligence systems in breast cancer care. The Breast, Vol. 49, 2020, Pages 25-32, ISSN 0960-9776,⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.breast.2019.10.001.⏎2⏎⏎specifically which populations are represented. Komen highlighted this issue in its comments to CMS on⏎its Proposed Rule on the 2026 Medicare Physician Fee Schedule.8 When it comes to mammography,⏎inadequate datasets could lead to inaccurate readings – as a country, we need to ensure that we are⏎effectively harnessing the technology of today to provide better outcomes for those who need it most.⏎Research⏎We also encourage HHS to invest in research into outcomes and performance of AI in breast cancer care.⏎Important research on use of AI in mammography is underway and needs to continue.⏎Last year, UCLA and UC Davis announced the first large-scale randomized trial of AI in breast cancer⏎screening in the United States.9 With a $16 million award from the Patient-Centered Outcomes Research⏎Institute (PCORI), the study, known as the PRISM Trial (Pragmatic Randomized Trial of Artificial⏎Intelligence for Screening Mammography) will evaluate whether AI can help support radiologists in⏎interpreting mammograms more accurately, with the goal of improving breast cancer screening and⏎reducing unnecessary callbacks and anxiety for patients. The multi-institutional clinical trial will study⏎hundreds of thousands of mammograms interpreted at academic medical centers and breast imaging⏎facilities in California, Florida, Massachusetts, Washington and Wisconsin. We encourage HHS to support⏎this and other research like it to evaluate how to responsibly optimize the use of AI while centering⏎patient experiences.⏎Conclusion⏎Komen appreciates the Administration’s interest in accelerating the responsible use of AI in clinical care⏎and for the opportunity to provide these comments. If you have any questions, or we may be of further⏎assistance, please do not hesitate to reach out to me at mguthrie@komen.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Molly Guthrie⏎Vice President, Policy & Advocacy⏎Susan G. Komen⏎8 Susan G. Komen, Comments on Proposed Rule on calendar year (CY) 2026 Medicare Physician Fee Schedule, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid⏎Services U.S. Department of Health & Human Services, Sept. 12, 2025, available at: https://www.regulations.gov/comment/CMS-2025-0304-⏎13145.⏎9 UCLA Health (Sept. 23, 2025), UCLA to lead $16 million national study on artificial intelligence in breast cancer screening [Press release],⏎available at https://www.uclahealth.org/news/release/ucla-lead-16-million-national-study-artificial-intelligence.⏎3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0230♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cleveland Clinic, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Cleveland Clinic♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa Myers, JD, MPA⏎Vice President, Government Relations⏎February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Submitted electronically via: Regulations.gov⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Cleveland Clinic is a not-for-profit, integrated healthcare system dedicated to patient-centered care,⏎teaching, and research. With a footprint in Northeast Ohio, Florida, and Nevada, Cleveland Clinic⏎Health System operates 23 hospitals, including a main campus near downtown Cleveland, with more⏎than 6,700 beds and 280 outpatient locations. Cleveland Clinic employs over 5,700 physicians and⏎researchers, and 16,800 nurses. Last year, our system had 15.7 million patient encounters, including⏎14.1 million outpatient visits and 333,000 hospital admissions and observations.⏎Cleveland Clinic appreciates the opportunity to provide feedback to the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) and the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National⏎Coordinator (ASTP/ONC) regarding the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in the delivery⏎of clinical care. This RFI represents a practical next step in translating federal AI strategies into policy⏎that directly shapes real-world clinical adoption.⏎Cleveland Clinic recognizes the current and critical inflection point for clinical AI, particularly in⏎primary care and other high-volume, lower-acuity settings. Advances in AI models and workflow⏎automation are expanding the range of clinical and operational tasks that can be supported through⏎software, while the U.S. health care system continues to face persistent access constraints, rising costs,⏎and clinician burnout. From Cleveland Clinic’s perspective, the greatest near-term opportunity for⏎clinical AI lies in end-to-end enablement of care delivery rather than isolated point solutions. When⏎deployed with appropriate oversight, AI can support intake, triage, routine care, documentation,⏎navigation, and follow-up in ways that expand access, improve continuity, and reduce provider⏎burden. Within this context, Cleveland Clinic’s perspectives are detailed below.⏎Section I: Framing the Opportunity for Clinical AI in Care Delivery⏎Regulation⏎To establish trust and encourage adoption, clear standards must be established to define how AI is⏎used in the delivery of patient care and expected outcomes. A regulatory framework that effectively⏎Page 1 of 9⏎⏎advances the adoption of AI in clinical care while setting protections for patient safety and investment⏎should encompass:⏎ Stronger interoperability frameworks and enforcement across the health care ecosystem,⏎particularly to support transitions of care across settings and health systems.⏎ A clear, risk-tiered clinical AI framework to evaluate AI tools, including those that operate⏎without a human in the loop, distinct from traditional medical device pathways. Core⏎regulatory elements that enable safe scaling should include:⏎o Clearly defined pilot guidelines with escalation and oversight protocols and explicit⏎triggers for clinician review, override, and intervention.⏎o Scaled commercialization only after demonstrating performance against defined⏎quality benchmarks.⏎o Minimum quality standards requiring performance comparable to representative⏎physician benchmarks in real-world clinical practice, evaluated through pilot data and⏎independent physician review using like-for-like clinical scenarios.⏎o Continuous performance monitoring to detect degradation and prevent model drift,⏎with adverse events investigated using processes comparable to existing medical⏎device oversight.⏎ Expedited review pathways for new clinical AI innovations that meet defined risk and⏎quality thresholds.⏎ Data ownership and stewardship protections that support provider investment in data⏎quality, governance, and responsible reuse.⏎Reimbursement⏎Payment approaches must recognize and appropriately compensate physicians and providers for the⏎investment, risk, and ongoing innovation required to deliver AI-enabled care. This includes not only⏎clinician time, but also the costs of implementation, workflow redesign, data governance, and clinical⏎oversight. While multiple reimbursement and compensation models may be viable, payment policy⏎should remain flexible rather than constraining innovation to a single structure. Aligning incentives⏎so that providers are rewarded for responsible deployment of AI in clinical practice is essential to⏎driving adoption, improving access, and sustaining high-quality care. This can be achieved in the⏎following ways:⏎ Telehealth parity with in-person services, recognizing that in-person escalation should be⏎the final step of care rather than the default starting point.⏎ Payment for services provided or augmented by AI without a human in the loop,⏎reflecting the clinical value delivered and the oversight responsibilities borne by physicians⏎and providers.⏎ Engagement with commercial payers to promote coverage for clinically validated AI⏎tools, reducing variability and uncertainty that can otherwise limit adoption.⏎Page 2 of 9⏎⏎Research and Development⏎National investment in applied AI research and development is crucial to building the evidence base⏎to support the safe and effective use of AI in clinical care. Key strategies to foster innovation, ensure⏎effective integration, and maintain leadership in the development and deployment of clinical AI⏎within the United States should include:⏎ Non-dilutive grants for innovators building clinical AI applications, alongside provider⏎pilot grants to support real-world testing and implementation.⏎ Federal investment in applied research, validation, and workforce readiness, accelerating⏎translation of promising AI tools from concept to routine clinical use.⏎ Protections for U.S.-developed clinical AI intellectual property and data, promoting fair⏎competition and long-term leadership in responsible clinical AI.⏎Finally, interoperability and data stewardship are foundational across regulation, reimbursement, and⏎research. From a regulatory perspective, interoperability standards enable longitudinal data access⏎and integration across systems. From a reimbursement perspective, providers incur significant costs⏎and labor to extract, clean, and organize clinical data, and adoption depends on whether payment and⏎incentive structures support that investment. From a research and development perspective,⏎interoperable, well-governed data is essential for training, validation, and scaling of clinical AI.⏎Policies that standardize access while supporting provider investment and stewardship are therefore⏎critical to aligning incentives, improving data quality, and sustaining innovation in clinical AI.⏎Section II: Responses to RFI Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Several interrelated barriers limit private sector innovation and adoption of clinical AI. To begin with,⏎regulatory frameworks remain fragmented and insufficiently predictable, with no clear AI-specific⏎pathway to support rapid clinical adoption and safe scaling. This uncertainty complicates investment⏎decisions and slows deployment of clinically promising tools.⏎Compounding regulatory uncertainty, reimbursement incentives are misaligned. Under current⏎payment policies, providers are often not reimbursed for care delivered with or augmented by AI,⏎making it difficult to justify sustained investment. Given already-thin hospital margins, AI initiatives⏎that improve quality or access but lack a clear revenue or cost-offset pathway are challenging for⏎health systems to sustain at scale.⏎Beyond payment, data-related challenges remain substantial. Clinical data are frequently fragmented,⏎inconsistent, or of variable quality, and interoperability across systems, settings, and vendors remains⏎limited. Providers appropriately prioritize patient privacy, security, and responsible data governance.⏎However, the significant cost and labor required to meet these standards are not consistently supported⏎by aligned incentives, which can limit providers’ ability to invest in high-quality, privacy-preserving⏎data preparation. This, in turn, constrains data quality, slows equitable adoption of clinical AI, and⏎increases the risk that models are trained or deployed without sufficient real-world validation.⏎Page 3 of 9⏎⏎At the same time, implementation barriers are high. Scaling clinical AI requires meaningful up-front⏎investment in infrastructure, engineering, governance, and change management. Integrating AI into⏎existing clinical workflows, including in federated analytics environments, can be complex and⏎resource-intensive without adequate institutional support.⏎Finally, adoption depends on trust and workforce readiness. Limited transparency, explainability, and⏎confidence in AI outputs can deter clinician use, while gaps in AI literacy, training, and organizational⏎change management further constrain uptake. Taken together, these barriers mean that even⏎technically strong AI tools often struggle to move from pilots to sustained, system-wide adoption.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical⏎care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎To address the interlocking barriers described above, HHS should prioritize policy changes that⏎reduce regulatory ambiguity, align incentives, and enable responsible scaling of clinically valuable⏎AI. From a regulatory perspective, HHS should establish a clinical AI-specific, risk-tiered regulatory⏎framework distinct from traditional medical device pathways. This framework should clarify⏎evaluation and monitoring expectations across HIPAA, HITECH, CMS, and ASTP/ONC; harmonize⏎agency requirements; and provide predictable pathways for approval, updating, and scaling of AI⏎tools, including those that operate without a human in the loop.⏎In parallel, reimbursement policy should be modernized to support adoption of high-value clinical AI.⏎Under current fee-for-service structures, payment often lags innovation, discouraging investment in⏎tools that improve care but do not immediately generate revenue or reduce utilization. HHS and CMS⏎should therefore advance payment approaches that recognize clinically valuable AI-enabled care⏎while allowing flexibility across reimbursement models.⏎Programmatic support can further reduce adoption friction. HHS should invest in federal pilot⏎programs and provider grants that defray the cost and complexity of adoption, including workflow⏎integration, system integration, and local validation. HHS should also address contractual barriers to⏎responsible AI use, such as overly restrictive “no-AI” clauses embedded in clinical systems.⏎Finally, HHS should advance national data infrastructure and interoperability, paired with incentives⏎that support provider participation in data reuse. Standardized data access, benchmarking⏎infrastructure, and bias-mitigation resources are essential to scaling AI safely, while policy should⏎recognize the significant cost and labor required to prepare clinical data for AI development and⏎deployment.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal⏎and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Non-medical-device clinical AI raises governance, legal, and implementation issues not fully⏎addressed by existing frameworks. In practice, AI capabilities are increasingly embedded within⏎software platforms and clinical systems without consistent disclosure, while responsibility for model⏎Page 4 of 9⏎⏎behavior, updates, and performance is often poorly defined across vendors and providers. Contractual⏎restrictions can further limit responsible quality and safety use of AI in care delivery.⏎To address these gaps, HHS should establish vendor disclosure requirements, promote standardized⏎contract language, and clarify shared accountability across developers and deploying organizations.⏎In particular, HHS should define governance and liability expectations for AI vendors supplying⏎models, agents, or AI-enabled features to health systems, including responsibilities for ongoing⏎monitoring and rapid remediation of performance degradation.⏎Liability and indemnification remain central challenges. Because AI outputs frequently influence but⏎do not dictate clinical decisions, ambiguity persists around responsibility when harm occurs. HHS⏎should therefore define how liability applies to assistive versus more autonomous AI and establish⏎safe harbors for providers and vendors that meet baseline governance, monitoring, and documentation⏎standards.⏎AI also introduces novel privacy and security risks, including inference-based re-identification,⏎derivative data generation, and multimodal processing not fully contemplated by existing HIPAA⏎guidance. Accordingly, HHS should update guidance to clarify standards for use of protected health⏎information (PHI) in ongoing model training and updating, including when health-system and patient⏎consent is required.⏎Finally, large language models (LLM) introduce distinct risks. At present, there are no widely adopted,⏎scalable methods to evaluate, monitor, and govern LLM performance in clinical settings. HHS should⏎prioritize development of national standards and tools for LLM evaluation, monitoring, and safety⏎that can be implemented consistently across health systems.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎Effective evaluation of non-medical-device AI requires a full lifecycle approach integrating pre-⏎deployment validation, post-deployment monitoring, and human-centered workflow assessment.⏎Prior to deployment, evaluation should include standardized documentation such as model cards⏎describing intended use, data sources, assumptions, and limitations; champion-challenger testing⏎under identical conditions; robustness and stress testing, including subgroup and bias analyses; and⏎transportability testing across sites, populations, and workflows. Workflow-integrated usability⏎testing with clinicians is essential to ensure interpretability, reduce cognitive load, and support safe⏎decision-making.⏎Once deployed, continuous quality monitoring becomes critical. This includes drift detection,⏎recalibration triggers, performance thresholds tied to clinical outcomes, and criteria for model pause,⏎retraining, or retirement. Auditability and traceability, including immutable logs of data access, model⏎versions, and inference outputs, are necessary to support accountability and incident investigation.⏎Shadow-mode trials and structured qualitative feedback loops help identify unintended consequences⏎before full activation.⏎Page 5 of 9⏎⏎HHS should support these processes through nationally aligned evaluation frameworks. The most⏎impactful mechanisms include contracts and cooperative agreements to build shared evaluation⏎infrastructure; competitive grants for provider-led pilots; prize competitions to incentivize novel⏎evaluation and explainability methods; and national standards for documentation, monitoring, and⏎risk-tier classification of non-device AI.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎HHS can best support private-sector innovation by strengthening the ecosystem for accreditation,⏎certification, and independent testing. As a starting point, HHS should set clear national baseline⏎expectations for safety, transparency, implementation, monitoring, and bias mitigation so that private⏎accrediting bodies can align around a consistent floor.⏎Building on that foundation, HHS should enable independent, trustworthy testing by supporting⏎neutral testbeds, including universities, consortia, and public-private partnerships, that evaluate⏎robustness, fairness, drift, and workflow fit using realistic clinical scenarios. HHS should also⏎recognize and incentivize credentialing and training programs for clinicians, analysts, and governance⏎teams, including voluntary HHS-recognized certifications for organizations demonstrating mature AI⏎governance.⏎Targeted funding mechanisms, including grants, cooperative agreements, and prize challenges, can⏎help scale effective validation tools, testing frameworks, and credentialing programs. Finally,⏎convening standards bodies, health systems, developers, and certification organizations can reduce⏎fragmentation and promote interoperable, industry-wide approaches to accreditation and assurance.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎AI has met or exceeded expectations in domains where data are relatively structured, workflows are⏎standardized, and model outputs map clearly to clinical or operational decisions. For example, sepsis⏎and early-warning models have improved early recognition and outcomes when tightly integrated into⏎workflows with clear escalation pathways. In diagnostics, AI applications regulated as medical⏎devices have increased throughput and improved consistency in radiology. Provider enablement tools⏎such as ambient documentation have reduced administrative burden and improved clinician⏎productivity when deployed with appropriate safeguards.⏎Operational applications have also performed well. AI-enabled forecasting of admissions, discharges,⏎surgical flow, bed needs, and staffing has generated measurable efficiency and cost gains. Revenue⏎cycle management tools supporting prior authorization and billing workflows have reduced⏎administrative friction in persistent pain points for providers.⏎At the same time, AI has fallen short where foundational conditions are weak. Fragmented data⏎environments, or environments where the meaning of clinical data changes over time or across⏎Page 6 of 9⏎⏎settings, degrade model performance and erode clinician trust. Tools deployed without clearly defined⏎success metrics, action pathways, or monitoring plans often fail to deliver sustained value. Clinical⏎event forecasting models illustrate this challenge, as performance metrics such as area under the⏎receiver operating characteristic (AUROC) can overestimate real-world utility while masking⏎downstream burden from false alerts when guardrails are insufficient.⏎The most promising next generation of AI tools are those that span care episodes, integrate⏎multimodal data, and deliver actionable insights embedded directly into clinical workflows. This⏎includes both autonomous and AI-augmented care models. In many cases, significant value is created⏎not necessarily by fully replacing clinician encounters, but by AI systems that perform intake,⏎synthesize patient history and signals, stratify risk, surface relevant evidence, and prepare care plans⏎in advance of a clinician interaction. These tools can materially improve visit efficiency, diagnostic⏎accuracy, and clinician experience, while preserving appropriate human oversight. Trajectory-⏎focused models that predict deterioration, recovery, or functional decline across settings further⏎extend this value by supporting proactive, rather than reactive, care delivery.⏎In parallel, there are substantial opportunities in AI-enabled care management and operational⏎intelligence. Tools that support transitions of care, longitudinal follow-up, adherence, and patient⏎education address persistent gaps that drive avoidable utilization and poor outcomes under episodic⏎care models. Administrative and operational applications are similarly critical to scalability and return⏎on investment, including AI-enabled prior authorization, revenue cycle workflows, and forecasting⏎of admissions, discharges, bed needs, and staffing. Across these cases, Cleveland Clinic’s experience⏎suggests that AI delivers the greatest impact when success metrics, caregiver action pathways, and⏎monitoring plans are clearly defined, and when tools are deployed with guardrails that translate⏎predictions into reliable, workflow-appropriate action.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the⏎primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption of clinical AI is shaped by a broad set of stakeholders. Senior clinical leaders, enterprise AI⏎governance bodies, and boards of directors influence decisions related to patient safety and⏎institutional risk. Legal, compliance, privacy, and cybersecurity leaders shape acceptable deployment⏎models and contractual terms. Data governance teams determine data readiness and monitoring⏎capabilities, while operational leaders assess workflow impact and scalability. Frontline physicians⏎exert substantial influence through their trust and day-to-day use of AI tools.⏎Administrative hurdles include fragmented governance with unclear decision rights, inconsistent data⏎quality, privacy and contractual constraints, and limited monitoring infrastructure. Integration into⏎electronic health records (EHRs) and clinical workflows remains technically complex, particularly in⏎federated environments. Gaps in AI literacy, training, and change management further slow adoption.⏎Overly centralized, executive-driven deployment can create risk when implementation outpaces⏎governance and clinical engagement.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Page 7 of 9⏎⏎Enhanced interoperability would have outsized impact by unlocking high-value data types currently⏎siloed or non-computable, including unstructured notes, imaging, pathology, genomics, and real-time⏎device data. Stronger adoption and enforcement of shared data standards such as FHIR read/write,⏎HL7, SNOMED, LOINC, and OMOP would improve portability of features, labels, and clinical logic⏎across systems and vendors.⏎Enterprise-grade semantic and operational interoperability across EHRs, operational data platforms,⏎quality measurement systems, and external data sources is essential to moving AI beyond local pilots.⏎Standardized system-to-system integration points, including common APIs and data models, would⏎reduce integration burden and widen market access.⏎Interoperable benchmarking and monitoring infrastructure, including shared datasets, standardized⏎outcome definitions, performance metrics, model cards, and drift-detection tools, would support⏎transparent evaluation, cross-institution research, and continuous improvement. Together, these⏎advances would reduce friction, fuel applied research, and accelerate development of AI that is⏎portable, verifiable, and workflow ready.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎AI provides a means to improve coordination, reduce delays, personalize care, and relieve⏎administrative and caregiving burden, such as repetitive data entry, scheduling difficulties, and gaps⏎in follow-up across care settings. AI can provide welcome relief by handling routine or burdensome⏎tasks that detract from time spent on direct human interaction and care.⏎At the same time, reservations persist regarding privacy, fairness, transparency, reliability, and⏎preservation of human-centered care. Concerns include whether AI-enabled care meets appropriate⏎quality standards, whether errors or bias will be detected and corrected, and whether patients are⏎adequately informed about AI use. Clear guardrails, transparency expectations, and accountability⏎standards are essential to sustaining trust.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care? Are there published findings about the impact of⏎adopted AI tools and their use in clinical care? How does the literature approach the costs,⏎benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize AI research that strengthens the evidence base for safe and effective clinical⏎adoption. Primary care is a priority, where AI can expand access, improve continuity, and reduce⏎provider burden through triage, routine care support, and proactive follow-up. Transition of care is⏎another high-impact area, where AI can improve coordination and follow-up across fragmented⏎settings.⏎The literature demonstrates meaningful benefits of AI when deployed with appropriate oversight,⏎including improved screening and triage, faster response times, reduced administrative burden, and⏎more consistent care delivery. Evidence also supports AI-enabled asynchronous functions such as⏎messaging, intake, and scheduling, as well as ambient tools that improve documentation quality and⏎clinician efficiency.⏎Page 8 of 9⏎⏎At the same time, research often under-characterizes real-world performance, equity impacts, and full⏎lifecycle costs. HHS should support research on bias mitigation, generalizability across populations⏎and settings, and economic evaluation of how costs, benefits, and value are transferred across⏎clinicians, health systems, and technology vendors as AI redistributes work across the care delivery⏎ecosystem.⏎Thank you for conducting a thoughtful process that allows us to provide input on such important⏎issues and for your consideration of this feedback. Should you need any further information, please⏎contact me at myersm21@ccf.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Melissa Myers, JD, MPA⏎Vice President, Government Relations⏎Page 9 of 9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0231♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Blackstone, MD, Robin Leigh Pavlich, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robin Leigh Pavlich♔Blackstone, MD♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Attachment A (Cover Letter / Title Page)⏎Date: February 22, 2026⏎To:⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology (ASTP/ONC)⏎Re: Request for Information (RFI): “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care”⏎Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎FR Doc.: 2025-23641⏎RIN: 0955–AA13⏎Subject Identifier: “HHS Health Sector AI RFI”⏎Submitted by:⏎Robin Leigh Pavlich Blackstone, MD⏎Physician and Health System Architect⏎Founder, Health 4.0 Alliance (Delaware Public Benefit Corporation)⏎Founder, H4 Alliance Trust⏎New York, NY⏎blackstonemd@mac.com⏎This submission reflects the professional analysis and views of the author in her individual capacity.⏎Public Posting Notice: No confidential or proprietary information is included in this submission.⏎Respectful Submission: Thank you for the opportunity to provide experience-based policy recommendations⏎aligned with HHS’s stated levers of regulation, reimbursement, and research & development.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0232♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American College of Gastroenterology , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Gastroenterology♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Mr. Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The American College of Gastroenterology (ACG) is pleased to respond to this Request for⏎Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care. ACG appreciates the Trump Administration’s request for public input to help⏎define policy priorities and actions for the deployment and advancement of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) in the healthcare sector. AI has already been integrated into many aspects⏎of medical practice, and its role will continue to expand. A typical gastroenterology⏎practice uses AI in numerous ways, including administrative tasks, diagnosis and⏎treatment, patient communication, and interactions with payors. Given the rapid pace of⏎innovation and a lack of consistent policy parameters, a federal framework to guide future⏎AI-related actions is essential. Below we provide recommendations and strategies to⏎advance the use of AI for clinical care.⏎ACG is a physician organization representing gastroenterologists and other⏎gastrointestinal (GI) specialists. Founded in 1932 and representing over 20,000 GI⏎clinicians, ACG’s mission is to enhance our members’ ability to provide world-class care to⏎patients with digestive disorders and to advance the profession through excellence and⏎innovation, grounded in the pillars of patient care, education, scientific investigation,⏎advocacy, and practice management. ACG leaders have been actively engaged in⏎developing AI for GI, working with subject-matter experts in our specialty to harness its⏎benefits for patient care and address the challenges medical providers and practices face⏎in achieving this goal.⏎We recommend addressing the following key barriers in the adoption of AI in the clinical⏎environment:⏎1⏎⏎Reimbursement⏎• ACG urges the agency to explore and model the implications of implementing⏎revisions to the definition of “software” for the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule⏎(PFS).⏎Regulations⏎• ACG supports the Administration’s efforts to reduce the regulatory burden on the⏎development and deployment of AI in the healthcare sector. We urge the⏎Administration to establish policies that truly reduce the burden and do not merely⏎shift responsibility for safety and efficacy from the developer to the practicing⏎clinician or other users of AI-enabled medical technology.⏎• The current regulatory framework for medical liability related to AI-enabled medical⏎technology is inadequate and unclear. Policymakers must develop clearer liability⏎rules and establish liability protections for the use of AI in healthcare.⏎• ACG recommends that the federal government incorporate proper medical⏎oversight of AI-enabled technology into any regulatory framework advanced by the⏎Administration.⏎Establishing a Reimbursement Pathway⏎The adoption of AI across the healthcare continuum raises several issues, including the⏎development of a reimbursement pathway to ensure continued innovation. In⏎gastroenterology, AI-enabled technology is used for endoscopy, image recognition, clinical⏎data analysis, and practice management.⏎The healthcare community’s current challenge is the lack of a methodology to capture the⏎costs of AI-enabled technologies that may enhance medical treatment. There is a lag⏎between the development of AI-enabled technology and the establishment of⏎reimbursement pathways to capture its costs. The absence of a clear reimbursement⏎pathway discourages innovation and reduces developers’ interest, leaving patients unable⏎to benefit from these advances. While there will not be a one-size-fits-all reimbursement⏎solution for AI, reimbursement is essential to sustaining innovation in AI-enabled⏎technologies and improving adoption of these technologies.⏎Stakeholders have proposed and CMS has, in limited cases, adopted a solution to⏎establish a predictable reimbursement pathway for the use of AI-enabled technology in the⏎Medicare outpatient setting: assigning a new AI-enabled medical device to a new⏎technology ambulatory payment classification (APC). While the new technology APC is⏎available, there are limitations in the utility, coupled with uncertainty and unpredictability⏎that chills adoption. The types of AI-enabled technologies that receive new technology⏎APCs are often expensive, and absent consistent reimbursement, providers face difficult⏎2⏎⏎decisions regarding whether to invest in such technologies. ACG urges CMS to further⏎explore this option and work with stakeholders to assess its feasibility and potential⏎unintended consequences.⏎In contrast, establishing a predictable payment pathway for AI-enabled medical⏎technology under the Medicare PFS presents unique challenges. CMS has acknowledged⏎the limitations of the current Medicare PFS in setting rates for AI-enabled CPT codes. The⏎agency has stated, “… AI applications are not well accounted for [in] our PE methodology⏎… [we] recognize that practitioners do incur resource costs for purchase and ongoing use⏎of software.” Similar comments were also made in its 2024 Medicare PFS Final⏎Regulations.⏎The traditional Resource-Based Relative Value Scale (RBRVS) was not designed for⏎software-based, non-human-labor technologies, creating challenges in assigning⏎appropriate values to AI-enabled medical devices. ACG calls on CMS to work with⏎stakeholders to identify alternative, formal, and predictable reimbursement pathways for⏎the Medicare PFS.⏎CMS has stated that a significant challenge in accounting for the costs of AI-enabled⏎medical devices stems from its current practice expense (PE) formula for calculating PE⏎RVUs. Under this formula, AI software (often referred to as “software as service” or SaaS)⏎is treated as overhead rather than a direct PE expense. As overhead, it is calculated as part⏎of the indirect PE portion of the PE RVU formula. In fact, the formula treats all software as⏎overhead. This was appropriate when the formula was developed and based on how⏎software was used at that time. The role of software in providing medical services has⏎evolved, and the PE formula should reflect this shift.⏎CMS has created a workaround by comparing, or “crosswalking,” PE RVUs for AI-enabled⏎medical devices to codes with similar values to the estimated costs of the AI software.⏎These estimates are generally based on invoices submitted by stakeholders. In 2026, there⏎are only three Category I codes with AI-enabled technology, and this solution becomes⏎less feasible as the number of AI-enabled Category I codes grows.⏎One potential solution is to revise the current methodology for calculating PE RVUs. In the⏎definition of software, an exception can be made to treat AI software as a supply rather⏎than as overhead. As a supply, it would be treated as a direct expense and accounted for⏎under the direct PE portion of the PE RVU formula. We believe this approach would more⏎accurately capture the actual costs of AI software and better reflect how it is used in⏎delivering medical services.⏎ACG urges the agency to explore and model the implications of revising the definition of⏎software for the Medicare PFS to more appropriately capture AI-enabled technologies used⏎in the delivery of healthcare services.⏎3⏎⏎Balancing Appropriate Regulatory Oversight, While Supporting Innovation⏎The adoption of AI across the healthcare continuum raises several regulatory issues, which⏎emphasizes the need for designing a regulatory framework that truly reduces burden and⏎does not merely shift responsibility; ensuring transparency about responsibility and⏎liability in the use of AI when errors occur; and establishing appropriate oversight by⏎medical professionals in the deployment of AI.⏎Supporting True Burden Reduction⏎ACG applauds the Administration for prioritizing administrative burdens on healthcare⏎providers. We support a regulatory framework for AI-enabled medical technology that⏎balances the flexibility needed to support innovation with appropriate oversight of evolving⏎medical technology. For example, the AI regulatory sandboxes announced by President⏎Trump to accelerate development and minimize federal oversight have great potential to⏎spur innovation. It is also important that this concept not merely shift the burden of⏎oversight from the AI technology developer to the AI technology user, or compromise⏎patient safety.⏎Appropriate oversight of AI-enabled medical technology, as with other medical⏎technologies, is needed to ensure it is fair, valid, effective, and safe. Throughout the⏎technology’s life cycle—from conception to development to implementation to ongoing⏎maintenance—there are critical roles for the entities involved in each stage (e.g.,⏎technology developers, distributors, users). As the regulatory framework is designed, the⏎technology’s entire life cycle and the entities engaged with it should be considered, and⏎appropriate roles and responsibilities assigned for regulatory oversight.⏎ACG supports the Administration’s efforts to reduce the regulatory burden on the⏎development and deployment of AI in the healthcare sector. We urge the Administration to⏎establish policies that truly reduce the burden and do not merely shift responsibility for⏎safety and efficacy oversight from the developer to the practicing clinician or other users of⏎AI-enabled medical technology.⏎Medical Liability⏎Like any medical technology, AI-enabled technology carries risks. Given the rapid pace of⏎AI adoption in the medical field, there is a critical need for clarity and consistency in⏎determining the responsible use of AI and medical liability when errors occur. The current⏎regulatory framework for medical liability is inadequate. The development of AI-enabled⏎technology introduces variables that may not apply in the current environment and raises⏎new medical-legal issues. Neither patients nor clinicians will feel comfortable using this⏎technology unless the framework for responsible-use and liability determination is⏎transparent. Standards for safe use and medical liability must be part of any national⏎strategy for AI.⏎4⏎⏎The current regulatory framework for medical liability related to AI-enabled medical⏎technology is inadequate and ill-suited to the challenges posed by AI. Policymakers must⏎develop clearer liability rules for the use of AI in healthcare.⏎Engagement with Healthcare Professionals⏎AI-enabled technology by private payers is a growing concern for prior authorization and⏎other claims-processing activities. AI-enabled prior authorization has the potential to⏎significantly reduce time-consuming manual tasks for clinicians and payers. Algorithms⏎can also be used to determine whether requested treatment and submitted justification⏎documents comply with the plan's medical criteria. Algorithms can be used to triage prior⏎authorization decision-making to an appropriate reviewer.⏎While these are appropriate uses for AI, ACG is concerned about using AI to make prior⏎authorization decisions without human review. Trained medical professionals need to⏎make these critical medical decisions, which can profoundly affect the trajectory of a⏎patient’s treatment and health outcomes. There have been real-world examples of payors⏎making prior authorization decisions using AI-enabled technology without medical⏎professionals’ review and input. Some states have begun passing legislation to bar this⏎practice. Federal leadership is needed to set standards for appropriate medical oversight⏎of AI-enabled technology.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to submit comments on how the Department of Health and⏎Human Services can support the rapid adoption and use of AI for clinical care. We hope to⏎continue to engage with the Administration on this critical issue. Please contact Brad⏎Conway, Vice President of Public Policy, Coverage & Reimbursement, at 301-263-9000 or⏎bconway@gi.org with any questions.⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0233♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bynon, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Bynon♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Supplemental Closing Statement⏎To: RFI Review Committee⏎From: David Bynon⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎Subject: Supplemental Closing Comments – HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Executive Summary⏎In reviewing the volume and tenor of recent comments, it is evident that many stakeholders⏎perceive a tension between responsible AI oversight and administrative burden. I⏎respectfully submit that this tension arises not from oversight itself, but from ambiguity in⏎how policy meaning and validation are structured.⏎The path to efficiency is not reduction of governance—but architectural refinement of how⏎governance is implemented.⏎On Oversight and Operational Load⏎Oversight becomes burdensome when compliance is interpretive, manual, and reactive. It⏎becomes efficient when policy meaning is explicit, machine-readable, and auditable by⏎design.⏎As outlined in my initial submission, a deterministic semantic framework allows AI systems⏎to retrieve from authoritative, versioned policy memory rather than infer from narrative text.⏎This approach shifts compliance from after-the-fact review toward structured, audit-ready⏎outputs.⏎Clarity reduces friction. Structure reduces interpretive drift. Deterministic retrieval reduces⏎downstream reconciliation.⏎On Cost-Efficiency and Innovation⏎Innovation is not constrained by documentation; it is constrained by uncertainty.⏎By aligning AI systems with structured, machine-verifiable policy representations,⏎organizations can reduce reconciliation costs currently borne by CMS, OMHA, and⏎⏎beneficiary support operations. When oversight is embedded at the data layer, validation⏎becomes continuous rather than episodic.⏎Efficiency in AI governance is achieved through standardization of semantic⏎responsibility—not through deregulation. In the context of ongoing government efficiency⏎initiatives such as the Department of Government Efficiency (DOGE), clarity — not⏎reduction — in policy semantics enables scalable oversight that aligns with both⏎organizational efficiency goals and AI governance principles.⏎On Data Transparency and Accountability⏎Concerns regarding data sovereignty are understandable in an evolving AI environment.⏎However, structured provenance, versioning, and machine-readable definitions protect⏎both regulated entities and the public interest.⏎A transparent, pre-defined semantic layer creates mutual accountability while preserving⏎proprietary implementation. It clarifies “what is policy” without exposing “how systems are⏎built.”⏎Closing Observation⏎As this RFI closes, I encourage the committee to consider that the future of high-impact AI⏎governance in healthcare will depend less on the volume of reporting and more on the⏎precision of structured policy memory.⏎The federal challenge is not whether to govern AI—but how to do so in a way that reduces⏎interpretive variance, minimizes audit friction, and preserves public trust in regulated⏎domains such as Medicare.⏎Respectfully,⏎David W. Bynon⏎Ref:⏎- HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0010 (https://www.regulations.gov/comment/HHS-ONC-⏎2026-0001-0010)⏎- HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0059 (https://www.regulations.gov/comment/HHS-ONC-⏎2026-0001-0059)♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0234♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Berger, Mitchell, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mitchell♔Berger♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Steven Posnack, Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, ASTP/ONC⏎From: Mitchell Berger (mazruia@hotmail.com)(writing in private capacity) Jan. 5, 2025⏎Re: Request for Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎(AI) as part of Clinical Care, https://www.federalregister.gov/public-inspection/2025-⏎23641/request-for-information-accelerating-the-adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence-as-⏎part-of, “HHS Health Sector AI RFI”⏎Dear Mr. Posnack: In response to HHS’s request for information (RFI) concerning suggestions⏎for Accelerating the Adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care,1 I write to suggest that HHS⏎further emphasize the ethical aspects of AI in its work addressing AI in clinical care, including⏎involving HHS and other federal bioethics staff and advisory committees, collaborating with⏎non-federal ethics and bioethics centers, departments and institutes, and potentially supporting⏎restoration of a Presidential-Level commission on bioethics to discuss AI and other topics.⏎As one author defines it, “AI ethics is a set of values, principles, and techniques that employ⏎widely accepted standards of right and wrong to guide moral conduct in the development and use⏎of AI technologies.”2 Ethical issues with respect to AI in clinical care may arise with respect to⏎data ownership, privacy, data sharing and potential (re)identification of individual patients. Some⏎commenters have expressed concerns about ethical implications associated with: potential use of⏎biased tools and algorithms; autonomous AI systems; governance and oversight of AI systems;⏎access to the benefits of AI (both for US-based and international/lower-middle income nation⏎populations, researchers and students); data and AI tool/system ownership; how use of AI will be⏎acknowledged (e.g., as in authorship and obtaining patient informed consent); billing and⏎reimbursement for use of AI tools/services; impacts of AI on jobs and provider-patient⏎relationships; long-term evaluation of AI impacts and potential dual uses/misuses of AI systems.⏎3 Concerns about the quality of AI systems also may raise ethical issues. As one recent article⏎notes, “AI is only as good as the data you feed into it.”4 Beyond considerations that may apply to⏎AI generally, use of AI for certain specific clinical applications (e.g., behavioral health,⏎pediatrics) may require additional scrutiny.5 Yet, while not without challenges, AI also may do⏎1 https://www.hhs.gov/press-room/hhs-ai-rfi.html⏎2 Leslie D., Understanding artificial intelligence ethics and safety: A guide for the responsible design and implementation of AI systems in the⏎public sector. The Alan Turing Institute. 2019. https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=3403301⏎3 Bouhouita-Guermech S, et al., Specific challenges posed by artificial intelligence in research ethics. Front Artif Intell. 2023 Jul 6;6:1149082;⏎https://www.coe.int/en/web/human-rights-and-biomedicine/common-ethical-challenges-in-ai; https://researchfeatures.com/ai-dual-use-⏎technology-cautionary-tale/;Khan, MS et al., Artificial intelligence for low income countries. Humanit Soc Sci Commun 11, 1422 (2024)⏎https://doi.org/10.1057/s41599-024-03947-w; Leslie D., Understanding artificial intelligence ethics and safety: A guide for the responsible⏎design and implementation of AI systems in the public sector. The Alan Turing Institute. 2019; J. Wick. Ethical Considerations of Artificial⏎Intelligence Use Abound, Pharmacy Times, Nov. 12, 2025, https://www.pharmacytimes.com/view/ethical-considerations-of-artificial-⏎intelligence-use-abound; H Klei Ethical Considerations for AI in clinical decision-making, AJMC, Sept. 7, 2024,⏎https://www.ajmc.com/view/ethical-considerations-for-ai-in-clinical-decision-making; Ning Y. et al., Generative artificial intelligence and ethical⏎considerations in health care: a scoping review and ethics checklist. Lancet Digit Health. 2024 Nov;6(11):e848-e856. doi: 10.1016/S2589-⏎7500(24)00143-2; EB Weiner et al., Ethical challenges and evolving strategies in the integration of artificial intelligence into clinical practice,⏎PLOS Digital Health, 2025; April 8;4(4)e0000810⏎4 https://www.biopharminternational.com/view/qualifying-ai-algorithms-in-pharmaceutical-manufacturing⏎5 Saeidnia, HR et al., Ethical Considerations in Artificial Intelligence Interventions for Mental Health and Well-Being: Ensuring Responsible⏎Implementation and Impact. Soc. Sci. 2024;13:381. https://doi.org/10.3390/socsci13070381; https://www.davidruttenberg.com/post/the-autism-⏎advantage-in-ai-ethics-why-neurodivergent-minds-are-essential-for-responsible-technology⏎⏎much to promote overall access to care, enhance quality of care and improve patient outcomes,⏎among other just and positive outcomes.6⏎The National Artificial Intelligence Research Resource (NAIRR), now under the auspices of the⏎National Science Foundation, emphasized ethical issues in its 2022 interim report and 2023 final⏎report which called for the creation of an ethics advisory board to guide AI research.7 NAIRR⏎commented in its interim report that “[t]o earn and maintain public trust, research in areas that⏎may impact privacy, civil rights, or civil liberties will need to be reviewed, approved, and⏎performed in a way that meets the expectations of civil society and protects subjects' rights.” In⏎its final report, NAIRR recommended developing an ethics advisory board to provide advice to⏎NAIRR and NSF on “issues of ethics, fairness, bias, accessibility, and AI risks and blindspots.”⏎To support an ethical component to its AI programs, HHS could include NIH’s bioethics staff⏎and others who have expertise and use its advisory committees to discuss and obtain input about⏎the ethical and moral implications of AI topics.8 HHS also can collaborate with academic and⏎nonprofit ethics and bioethics centers, institutes and departments.9 In an ideal world, private⏎companies, academic institutions, faith-based entities and other nonprofits will join HHS in⏎implementing their own ethical programs and advisory boards focused on AI and clinical care⏎issues. Toward this end, HHS could consider supporting restoration of the Presidential Bioethics⏎Commission that historically have helped research and set policy on bioethics topics.10⏎Historically, these commissions, which existed prior to 2017 under several Administrations, have⏎researched and made recommendations on such topics as access to care, cloning, whole genome⏎sequencing, neuroscience, synthetic biology, newborn screening, clinical research,⏎pandemics/emergency preparedness topics and aging/caregiving. With adequate staff and⏎funding, a new Presidential bioethics commission would well-positioned to examine and make⏎recommendations relating to the ethical aspects of AI and its use(s) in clinical care as discussed⏎in this RFI.⏎Sincerely,⏎Mitchell Berger⏎Note/disclaimer: The views expressed above are solely those of the author and should not be⏎imputed to other individuals nor to any public/private entity.⏎6 Id. at 3. See also, https://dihi.org/health-ai-partnership-an-innovation-and-learning-network-to-facilitate-the-safe-effective-and-responsible-⏎diffusion-of-health-ai-software-applied-to-health-care-delivery-settings/; https://healthitanalytics.com/news/more-legal-clarification-needed-for-⏎clinical-algorithm-development-use; Drugs aren't typically tested on women -- artificial intelligence could correct that bias,⏎https://www.sciencedaily.com/releases/2020/09/200930144424.htm⏎7 https://www.ai.gov/nairrtf/#INTERIM_REPORT_AND_REQUEST_FOR_PUBLIC_INFORMATION; NSF Strengthening and Democratizing⏎the U.S. Artificial Intelligence Innovation Ecosystem, 2023, https://nsf-gov-resources.nsf.gov/2023-10/NAIRR-TF-Final-Report-2023.pdf;⏎NAIRR Pilot Inaugural meeting final report, 2025, https://nairrpilot.org/resources/2025-inaugural-annual-meeting-final-report⏎8 NIH Dept. of Bioethics, https://www.cc.nih.gov/bioethics⏎9 https://bioethicstoday.org/ethics-centers-departments-programs/; https://members.appe-ethics.org/organizational-members/Search/institutional-⏎organizational-memberships-486004⏎10 J. Meaney, Bring Back the President’s Council on Bioethics, National Catholic Bioethics Center, Dec. 17, 2024,⏎https://www.ncbcenter.org/messages-from-presidents/councilonbioethics; M. Galvin, NAS and NAM Presidents, Other Experts Urge Biden⏎Administration to Reinstate Presidential Bioethics Commission, March 2022, National Academies,⏎https://www.nationalacademies.org/news/nas-and-nam-presidents-other-experts-urge-biden-administration-to-reinstate-presidential-bioethics-⏎commission; Hastings Center, Goals and Practice of Public Bioethics: Reflections on National Bioethics Commissions, May-June 2017,⏎https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/toc/1552146x/2017/47/S1; Adashi EY, Cohen IG. An Overdue Executive Order: Reinstating the National⏎Bioethics Commission. Am J Med. 2021 Oct;134(10):1199-1200. doi: 10.1016/j.amjmed.2021.04.018♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0235♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from M, C, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔C♔M♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔A.I. "psychotherapists" ?  These are algorithms.  Do you want your child parented by an algorithm?   Do you  tell YOUR friend or relative⏎to get therapy from an I.T. unlicensed.  Physicians and Psychologists must be trained and licensed to practice.  That is how it should stay.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
