UID-0237♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Gabriel, Stephen, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Stephen♔Gabriel♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Office of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC, Department of Health and Human Services⏎From: Stephen Gabriel⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001)⏎In response to HHS's request for information on accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, I write⏎to highlight structural barriers often invisible from hospital-centered policy perspectives. Dental⏎care serves as a useful case study because it combines significant population health need,⏎extreme software fragmentation, and systematic exclusion from federal health IT modernization⏎efforts. The patterns identified here likely extend to other ambulatory settings including⏎behavioral health, home health, and long-term care.⏎The current AI adoption problem in ambulatory settings traces directly to federal health IT policy⏎decisions enacted nearly two decades ago. The HITECH Act of 2009 authorized approximately⏎$27 billion to incentivize electronic health record adoption, operationalized through the⏎Meaningful Use program. This program successfully drove hospital EHR adoption to⏎near-universal levels and forced medical software vendors to adopt standardized data ontologies⏎and exchange protocols.1 However, the statutory framework created a wide gap between standard⏎medicine and specialized ambulatory care. Dentists were explicitly excluded from the Medicare⏎EHR Incentive Program. While theoretically eligible for the parallel Medicaid program,⏎participation required demonstrating 30% Medicaid patient volume, a threshold most private⏎dental practices could not meet because adult dental benefits are frequently carved out of state⏎Medicaid programs.2 The dental sector consequently received virtually no federal capital to⏎subsidize the transition to digital records and no federal mandate to adopt certified, interoperable⏎software.⏎Without this mandate, the dental software market evolved through localized free-market⏎dynamics, resulting in severe fragmentation. The current landscape features major vendors like⏎Dentrix, Eaglesoft, and Open Dental alongside a long tail of over 150 disparate clinical, imaging,⏎and practice management systems operating without standardized data exchange protocols.3 This⏎fragmentation creates a significant barrier for AI developers. In the broader medical sector, an AI⏎developer can use standard FHIR APIs to interface with dominant electronic health records,⏎immediately scaling products to thousands of hospitals. A dental AI developer must build, test,⏎and continuously maintain custom integration bridges for dozens of proprietary systems, many of⏎which actively restrict third-party access or use proprietary data storage formats. This technical⏎debt restricts scalability, inflates development costs, and leaves developers without the diverse,⏎multi-site training data required to build robust algorithms.⏎The fragmentation of source data leads to a secondary problem in AI evaluation and post-market⏎safety. The FDA's AI/ML-Enabled Medical Devices database shows over 1,300 authorized⏎devices by end of 2025, with 295 new clearances in 2025 alone.4 Approximately 52 of these are⏎dental AI devices, predominantly for oral radiology applications including caries detection and⏎cephalometric analysis.5 However, most clearances occur via the 510(k) pathway, which does not⏎require the extensive prospective clinical trials of Premarket Approval. Many 510(k) summaries⏎lack detail on demographic representation of training data and scope of cross-site validation. A⏎⏎systematic review of federated learning in healthcare found that only 5.2% of studies reached⏎real-world clinical implementation. This 94.8% failure rate was driven not by mathematical⏎flaws but by organizational, infrastructural, and economic barriers.6 Federated learning allows⏎algorithms to train on distributed data without centralizing protected health information, which⏎theoretically solves the privacy barrier. But it still requires computational overhead at edge nodes⏎and baseline data harmonization that fragmented ambulatory systems cannot provide.⏎The FDA has recognized these lifecycle management challenges. Title 21 CFR Part 820,⏎governing the Quality System Regulation for medical devices, underwent a major shift effective⏎February 2, 2026, transitioning to the Quality Management System Regulation (QMSR)⏎harmonized with ISO 13485:2016.7 This framework imposes stricter documentation⏎requirements for software validation and continuous post-market monitoring. For AI developers,⏎compliance requires real-world performance tracking to detect algorithmic drift. Yet without⏎standardized interoperability infrastructure in ambulatory settings, conducting effective⏎post-market surveillance on dental AI tools is not currently feasible. The regulatory expectation⏎for continuous monitoring does not match the reality of fragmented IT infrastructure.⏎Even if technical barriers could be overcome, the economic mechanism for AI adoption remains⏎broken. The CDT code system maintained by the American Dental Association contains no⏎codes for AI-assisted diagnostics.8 The CDT 2026 update introduced codes for point-of-care⏎saliva testing and cracked tooth diagnostics, but nothing for algorithmic image interpretation or⏎predictive software analysis. Without billing codes, practices cannot submit claims to third-party⏎payers for AI-enhanced services. The cost must be absorbed as overhead. This contrasts with the⏎CPT system, where Category III codes now allow tracking of AI use in radiology and pathology,⏎providing the foundation for eventual Medicare coverage determinations under 42 CFR Part⏎414.9 The absence of dental AI codes creates a reimbursement problem that pushes private⏎investment toward medical AI, where scalable payment pathways exist.⏎These structural barriers have direct public health consequences. HRSA data for Q1 FY2026⏎identifies 7,443 designated dental Health Professional Shortage Areas encompassing 63.7 million⏎Americans, with only 32.93% of clinical need currently met.10 AI-assisted diagnostic tools have⏎exactly the characteristics required to address this crisis. A dental hygienist could capture⏎radiographs and run them through an FDA-cleared triage system, flagging acute pathologies for⏎prioritized intervention by the covering dentist. But the safety-net practices operating in these⏎shortage areas are the very organizations most affected by the barriers identified here: least able⏎to afford AI software without reimbursement, least likely to operate interoperable systems, and⏎least represented in validation datasets that skew toward affluent urban academic centers.⏎Several targeted interventions would address these barriers. First, ONC should expand⏎certification criteria under 45 CFR 170.315 to address dental workflows and imaging AI,⏎potentially requiring certified software for clinics participating in Medicaid dental networks.11⏎Second, the ADA CDT Committee should develop emerging technology codes for AI-assisted⏎diagnostics, drawing precedent from CPT Category III codes, while CMS issues guidance to⏎state Medicaid directors on coverage pathways. Third, FDA guidance on AI device lifecycle⏎management under the new QMSR framework should include dental-specific examples given the⏎unique variability of dental imaging acquisition. Fourth, ASTP/ONC should fund dental FHIR⏎implementation guide development through HL7 cooperative agreements, integrating periodontal⏎⏎charting and dental imaging metadata into the US Core Data for Interoperability standard.⏎Finally, FDA should sponsor federated benchmarking registries that evaluate algorithms against⏎diverse datasets, including data from HPSA safety-net clinics, satisfying QMSR validation⏎requirements while ensuring algorithms perform safely across underserved populations.⏎The explicit policy exclusion of specialized ambulatory settings from historical health IT⏎initiatives has resulted in fragmented software ecosystems, poor interoperability, and nonexistent⏎reimbursement pathways. These failures ensure that the diagnostic potential of AI remains⏎concentrated in well-resourced academic medical centers, bypassing the populations who need it⏎most. Unlocking clinical AI across the full healthcare continuum requires more than software⏎innovation. It demands realignment of ONC certification standards, FDA lifecycle management⏎protocols, and CMS payment mechanics.⏎Sincerely,⏎Stephen Gabriel⏎The views expressed above are solely those of the author and should not be attributed to any organization.⏎References⏎1. CMS, Medicare and Medicaid EHR Incentive Programs. The Meaningful Use program transitioned to Promoting⏎Interoperability under MIPS.⏎2. HITECH Act of 2009, Medicaid EHR Incentive Program eligibility requirements. 30% Medicaid patient volume⏎threshold over 90-day period.⏎3. Dental software market fragmentation documented in industry analyses. Major vendors lack standardized data⏎exchange comparable to hospital EHR interoperability.⏎4. FDA Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning (AI/ML)-Enabled Medical Devices database.⏎https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/artificial-intelligence-and-machine-learning-ai⏎ml-enabled-medical-devices⏎5. FDA 510(k) Premarket Notification Database, dental AI device clearances through early 2026.⏎6. Teo ZL, et al. Federated machine learning in healthcare: A systematic review on clinical applications and⏎technical architecture. Cell Reports Medicine. 2024.⏎7. 21 CFR Part 820, Quality Management System Regulation (QMSR), effective February 2, 2026, harmonizing⏎with ISO 13485:2016.⏎8. American Dental Association, CDT 2026 code set. 60 updates including 31 new codes; no codes for AI-assisted⏎diagnostics.⏎9. 42 CFR Part 414 (physician fee schedule); 42 CFR Part 410 (supplementary medical insurance benefits).⏎10. HRSA Data Warehouse, Designated Health Professional Shortage Areas Quarterly Summary, Q1 FY2026.⏎https://data.hrsa.gov/⏎11. 45 CFR 170.315, ONC Health IT Certification Criteria.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0238♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AHIP, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AHIP♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎RE: Request for Information; Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955–AA13]⏎Submitted via email to www.regulations.gov⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Dr. Keane:⏎AHIP is the national association that represents health insurance plans that provide coverage,⏎services, and solutions for over 205 million Americans through Medicare and Medicaid,⏎employer-sponsored insurance, and the individual insurance market. AHIP appreciates the⏎opportunity to provide comments on the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS)⏎Request for Information (RFI) on “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎(AI) as Part of Clinical Care.”⏎As Americans increasingly interact with AI in many facets of life, including across the⏎health care system, it is important to create balanced policies that help realize the potential⏎of AI and promote innovation, while also prioritizing safety and building trust among⏎patients, providers, and other stakeholders. AHIP supports the Department’s efforts to build⏎on its December 2025 AI strategy by seeking public feedback on steps it can take to establish a⏎“forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach” to accelerate the adoption and use⏎of AI as part of clinical care. HHS can play an important role in advancing the use of AI,⏎including improving clinical outcomes, by prioritizing coordination and consistency within the⏎Department and across Departments, investing in AI standards development and education, and⏎continuing to collaborate with stakeholders by participating in, and utilizing recommendations⏎from, public-private partnerships, among other actions.⏎Below, we provide a snapshot of the current state of health plan use of AI to support clinical care⏎and provide input on specific questions and topics addressed in the RFI.⏎Health Plan Use of AI⏎AI is used to help consumers, employees, providers, and other partners in improving health⏎outcomes and the health care experience, as well as in gaining internal operational⏎efficiencies and reducing costs. Examples of where AI is being used to bring value to the health⏎care continuum include:⏎⏎Page 2⏎Clinical:⏎• Supporting provider partners by identifying gaps in care and potential safety issues.⏎• Improving predictive analytics of patient risks to enable proactive disease and care⏎management in partnership with providers.⏎• Researching disease pathways, treatment options, and likely outcomes.⏎• Supporting customized patient care plans, early interventions, and customized treatment.⏎• Cleaning, integrating, normalizing, and analyzing vast amounts of data faster and more⏎efficiently than traditional statistical methods.⏎Consumers:⏎• Offering chatbots for simple and routine member inquiries, such as customer support and⏎administrative questions.⏎• Facilitating accurate and timely answers to consumers' asking more detailed or complex⏎questions about their health benefits by equipping customer service representatives with⏎informational support tools.⏎• Providing health plan apps that include pricing estimates, among other features.⏎• Supporting consumer appeals processing by extracting information from faxed appeals and⏎instantly generating a fully formed digital case file, for human review.⏎Administrative:⏎• Near real-time approvals of prior authorizations based on appropriate documentation and⏎member clinical history. Potential prior authorization denials based on clinical factors are⏎always subject to review by a licensed and qualified clinician.⏎• Contributing to efficient claims processing and reductions in administrative costs, including⏎flagging claims processing issues to reduce errors and support claims operators.⏎• Promoting payment integrity by helping to identify patterns of payment abnormalities,⏎enabling health plans to engage in appropriate review, investigation, and corrective action.⏎Barriers to Private Sector Innovation⏎A Federal Approach⏎The Executive Order, Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence,⏎appropriately seeks to ensure “there is a minimally burdensome national standard — not 50⏎discordant State ones.” A unified federal approach for AI is essential to ensure consistency,⏎reduce duplicative compliance efforts, and promote innovation across health care. Fragmented,⏎state-level approaches risk creating conflicting or redundant obligations that increase⏎administrative burden and delay the deployment of responsible AI solutions. Divergence would⏎also occur among states and federal programs such as Medicare Advantage and Part D, which are⏎not subject to state laws governing AI use and oversight under relevant preemption provisions.⏎Office of Science and Technology Policy (OSTP) and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid⏎Services (CMS) should work together to ensure that federal and state requirements are⏎⏎Page 3⏎harmonized across these programs, resolving ambiguity that could otherwise create uneven⏎obligations or discourage innovation.⏎Effective coordination also depends on clear alignment within HHS agencies such as CMS, the⏎Food & Drug Administration (FDA), and the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP),⏎as well as across agencies such as HHS, the Department of Homeland Security, and the Federal⏎Trade Commission (FTC). Overlapping requirements could create uncertainty and slow⏎innovation. HHS can ensure a cohesive national framework in which agencies share best⏎practices, avoid redundancy, and promote common expectations for AI governance. Continued⏎reliance on established frameworks, such as the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, can⏎further support consistency and predictability in implementation.⏎Risked-based Oversight of AI in Health Care⏎Federal AI oversight should be proportional to risk, allowing flexibility for low-risk uses while⏎focusing on patient safety for high-impact applications. This approach will allow agencies to⏎focus regulatory attention where it is needed the most, like use cases that directly impact clinical⏎decision making, while allowing flexibility for administrative tools that pose minimal risk. A⏎risk-based approach includes identifying, analyzing, and prioritizing risks, then developing and⏎implementing mitigation strategies while monitoring and communicating throughout the process.⏎As HHS considers policies to advance clinical AI adoption, it may be helpful to recognize that⏎these technologies operate across distinct functional contexts with differing implications for⏎patient care and decision-making. These uses present varying levels of influence on clinical⏎decisions and patient actions, ranging from information support to shaping potential care⏎regimen. Recognizing these distinctions can help ensure governance and transparency⏎requirements are calibrated appropriately to the intended use and impact of the technology.⏎Privacy & Security Concerns⏎HIPAA-compliant AI systems, including large language models (LLMs), are critical for building⏎trust and driving consumer adoption. Consumers are more comfortable using AI tools when they⏎know their personal health information will not be used to train models or become publicly⏎accessible. In contrast, third-party, non-HIPAA compliant AI systems can undermine consumer⏎confidence because they do not offer the same robust protections for Protected Health⏎Information. More AI solutions could be leveraged to improve health care if more AI solutions⏎offered HIPAA-compliant options. Many AI tools available for deployment today are not⏎HIPAA-compliant, limiting the ability of HIPAA-covered entities to maximize the potential of⏎those tools for health care and health care financing use cases.⏎Safety Standards & Training⏎The notion of “garbage in, garbage out” is critical in the AI space. Crafting appropriate⏎prompts and assuring that patient data is communicated is critical to using AI in any clinical use⏎case. Therefore, clinicians who will be utilizing AI as part of their practice should have⏎appropriate training. Medical schools generally have not prepared clinicians for this new⏎⏎Page 4⏎technology and, while it is easy to develop any prompt for AI, it takes more advanced training⏎and skill to develop effective prompts that will enable safe AI clinical support.⏎AHIP encourages HHS to engage with public-private partnerships to advance efforts that focus⏎on the safety of AI-supported clinical decisions to prevent harm or death. HHS should further⏎ensure that AI vendors transparently disclose critical information that guided the development of⏎their health care AI products, particularly those used in clinical care that are not subject to FDA⏎approval. Experts should identify critical elements, such as what populations were used to train⏎the AI. Vendors should be required to publish ongoing statistics identified as critical by AI and⏎health care experts, such as what percentage of AI diagnoses are over-ruled by human clinicians⏎and how often AI fails to prompt clinicians for missing data critical to patent evaluation. Any AI⏎system that influences a medical decision must be subject to the same high standards of⏎transparency and safety, regardless of what that system is called (e.g., non-medical device,⏎Software as a Medical Device, digital therapeutic, etc.).⏎Transparency & Traceability⏎Transparency and explainability are critical to fostering trust and accountability in the use of AI.⏎Stakeholders must understand how AI systems are designed, validated, and governed to ensure⏎they operate safely, and in support of high-quality care. Vendors should share what guided the⏎development of their health care AI products, both those used in clinical care that are subject to⏎FDA approval and those that are not. Experts should identify critical elements, such as what⏎populations were used to train the AI. Transparency should focus on documenting model⏎purpose, data inputs, and governance, rather than requiring public disclosure of proprietary⏎algorithms that could compromise intellectual property or cybersecurity.⏎Voluntary, collaborative initiatives such as pilot programs, peer review, and regulatory⏎sandboxes can strengthen oversight while promoting responsible innovation. These efforts⏎enable payers, providers, and regulators to evaluate AI tools, address potential bias, and refine⏎approaches before broad implementation. HHS should work with relevant federal agencies,⏎including NIST, to advance voluntary transparency and explainability frameworks, such as pilot⏎programs and sandboxes, and share model evaluation mechanisms that encourage trust and⏎accountability across stakeholders.⏎AI tools used in high-risk clinical use cases warrant thoughtful governance and appropriate⏎oversight. However, given the continuing evolution of these technologies and nuances of use⏎cases in health care, governance approaches should be risk-based, grounded in existing⏎applicable frameworks, and use-case appropriate. HHS should prioritize ongoing collaboration⏎with stakeholders to advance consensus-driven standards development to promote safety,⏎accountability, and transparency in high-risk clinical AI use cases, while remaining operationally⏎feasible and supportive of innovation.⏎Adverse Events Reporting⏎While general transparency and explainability may be voluntary, more robust and stringent⏎safety reporting should be required for high-risk clinical AI uses. AHIP encourages HHS to⏎⏎Page 5⏎engage with public-private partnerships to define high-risk use cases and advance efforts that⏎monitor the safety of high-risk AI-supported clinical use cases to mitigate the risk of harm or⏎death. This should go beyond just adverse events. For example, HHS should ensure that AI⏎vendors transparently disclose critical information such as if AI fails to prompt clinicians for⏎missing data critical to patient evaluation and the tool’s output. Any AI system that provides⏎healthcare information used by patients or clinicians to make high-stakes decisions must be⏎subject to the same high standards of transparency and safety, regardless of what that system is⏎called (e.g. non-medical device, Software as a Medical Device, digital therapeutic,⏎etc.). Similarly, providers should also have to report adverse events or near misses in alignment⏎with existing practices for other sentinel events in health care. HHS should then evaluate the data⏎and publish results to inform patients and clinicians.⏎Regulatory, Payment Policy, or Programmatic Changes⏎Clinical Algorithms⏎The Nondiscrimination in Health Programs and Activities final rule issued in May 2024 finalized⏎provisions related to clinical algorithms, later revised to “patient care decision support tools.”⏎This section duplicates other consumer protection provisions that prohibit discrimination in⏎health programs and activities, creating confusion and unnecessary regulatory compliance⏎burden. HHS should rely on existing law and partner with industry stakeholders through ongoing⏎efforts to develop policy solutions that promote innovation, consistent industry standards,⏎accurate data collection, and common definitions. HHS should rescind 45 CFR § 92.210,⏎nondiscrimination in the use of patient care decision support tools.⏎Machine Translation⏎The Nondiscrimination final rule also sets limitations on the use of machine translation of⏎languages by covered entities. These restrictions risk slowing the adoption of AI-powered⏎translation tools, even as technology rapidly improves. In contrast, Executive Order No. 14224⏎and the Department of Justice’s updated limited English proficiency (LEP) guidance encourages⏎agencies to consider the responsible use of artificial intelligence and machine translation to⏎bridge language barriers and improve efficiency. The DOJ guidance states: “Technological⏎advances in translation services will permit agencies to produce cost-effective methods for⏎bridging language barriers and reducing inefficiencies with the translation process. The⏎Department encourages other agencies to follow its approach of considering responsible use of⏎artificial intelligence and machine translation to communicate with individuals who are limited⏎English proficient.” Adopting a less restrictive, risk-based approach to machine translation⏎would provide the flexibility needed to allow covered entities to utilize evolving AI technologies⏎to better serve diverse populations.⏎In addition, we encourage the federal government to support HHS in its continued work with⏎stakeholders to develop and evaluate alternative approaches to current notice requirements. Such⏎approaches can use technology to more effectively let people know language assistance is⏎available and how to access it, such as a universal language assistance symbol, and promote⏎electronic access without burdensome or costly regulatory requirements. Such a symbol could⏎⏎Page 6⏎appear not only in physical locations, but also on websites and in electronic documents. AI can⏎be used as back-end support once assistance is selected to surface the appropriate language in⏎writing or generate speech. The regulations should not hamper advancement as technology⏎evolves.⏎HHS should rescind 45 CFR § 92.201(c)(3) and align guidance related to machine⏎translation for LEP with Executive Order 1422 and the Department of Justice guidance.⏎Additionally, HHS should maintain regulatory flexibility to develop meaningful access for LEP⏎consumers that focuses on quick and streamlined access to interpretation and written translations⏎that are supported by technology.⏎Software as a Service (SaaS) Payment⏎CMS has appropriately devoted attention to advances in technology and its growing presence in⏎health care. At the outset, AHIP emphasizes the importance of rigorous evaluation by⏎CMS around efficacy, safety, and quality of clinical SaaS products in its coverage⏎determinations, a necessary and threshold factor for CMS payment. In establishing a payment⏎framework for SaaS, we recommend that CMS adopt a balanced, evidence-driven approach⏎based on a clear and transparent framework that seeks to anticipate future innovations. We⏎suggest CMS first classify SaaS tools by type of solution (e.g., diagnostic, therapeutic,⏎augmentative, patient-facing, operational) and then create a series of algorithms to associate the⏎technology with the appropriate payment mechanism.⏎In general, we recommend CMS establish a unified, site-neutral framework for paying for SaaS⏎across the PFS and OPPS. CMS payment policy should prioritize packaging when tools are⏎integral to a service, allow time-limited add-ons or coverage with evidence while evidence⏎is emerging, reserve separate payment for primary services, and avoid “buy-and-bill”⏎where feasible. Valuation by CMS should consider clinician work, practice expense, malpractice,⏎and the technology’s acquisition and capital-like costs, with periodic rebasing as efficiencies⏎materialize. We also suggest that CMS address SaaS through value-based payment models and⏎risk-based contracts that reward measurable outcomes and efficient care delivery, and pair⏎payment with quality measures and interoperability/cybersecurity expectations to protect patients⏎and ensure sustained value. AHIP’s full response to the CMS CY2026 Outpatient⏎Prospective Payment System (OPPS) Proposed Rule SaaS Request for Information is⏎attached.⏎HHS Support for Private Sector Activities⏎Standards and Frameworks⏎HHS can support the private sector by deferring to industry standards where they exist⏎and funding efforts to address ongoing gaps as technology evolves. Stakeholders in the⏎private sector have been collaborating for several years to develop governance, ethical, and⏎practice standards for organizations developing and deploying AI to protect consumers while⏎fostering AI. AHIP has joined business and technology leaders as well as consumer advocates to⏎advance principles, best practices, and standards. For example, AHIP has worked with the⏎⏎Page 7⏎Consumer Technology Association (CTA) on developing standards of trustworthiness and⏎recommendations for bias management and continues to work with CTA to identify areas in⏎which new standards are needed.12⏎AHIP strongly encourages use of these and other existing national frameworks and standards in⏎developing any new federal policy. This approach would help avoid duplication and promote a⏎streamlined oversight structure to support continued innovation. For example, the NIST AI Risk⏎Management Framework, developed with robust input from stakeholders, including AHIP,⏎provides a foundation for understanding and applying methods for “tiering” of risks associated⏎with AI.3 Further, as the Administration seeks to develop and promote the innovation potential⏎that AI offers, it is important that federal law preempt state laws. Many states are releasing new⏎provisions and requirements that could stifle the development of AI and hamper efforts to⏎standardize technologies. Alignment on common principles and uniform guardrails across⏎Congress and the Administration is critical for AI to be used to its full potential.⏎Parallel Processing of Approval and Coverage⏎Through the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), private health plans have been working more⏎closely with manufacturers to ensure sufficient data are available to support coverage decisions.⏎• The Center for Devices and Radiological Health (CDRH’s) Payor Communication⏎Task Force was created to facilitate structured, ongoing dialogue between device⏎manufacturers and payers— inclusive of both Medicare and private health plans. This⏎Task Force supported manufacturers in obtaining timely feedback that may shorten the⏎interval between FDA marketing authorization and coverage decisions.⏎• The Early Payor Feedback Program—part of the Task Force activities—enabled⏎device manufacturers to consult with public and private payers well before final FDA⏎clearance or approval. The intent of the program was to align evidence generation with⏎both regulatory and coverage needs so that manufacturers could better anticipate insurer⏎expectations regarding clinical utility, patient-centered outcomes, and real-world⏎applicability.⏎This sort of parallel processing could substantially speed up the pathway for Software as a⏎Medical Device (SaMD) to get regulatory approval, coverage determinations, and then CMS⏎payment rates. Given recent reorganization and reductions in force, AHIP is unclear whether⏎these programs continue to operate. Moreover, more robust collaboration that would permit data⏎sharing during the approval process—not necessarily through the FDA, but in parallel—could⏎even further benefit patients by bringing products to market more quickly. We urge CMS to⏎continue working with the FDA and even expand to the private sector parallel review⏎1https://shop.cta.tech/collections/standards/products/the-use-of-artificial-intelligence-in-healthcare-trustworthiness-⏎cta-2090⏎2 https://shop.cta.tech/a/downloads/-/b5481e81fe7f99aa/9d1895627bdd6e27⏎3 https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework⏎⏎Page 8⏎programs and manufacturer guidance opportunities to ensure all payers, public and⏎private, have the information necessary to make informed coverage decisions.⏎Promoting Adoption & Use⏎Smaller hospitals, providers, health plans and the consumers and patients they serve could⏎benefit greatly from AI tools. However, cost-prohibitive pricing from AI companies and lack of⏎resources could limit or even prohibit adoption by the very organizations that could benefit the⏎most. Additionally, to support smaller health care entities in their AI journeys caution should be⏎taken that any guardrails intended to ensure adequate data sets (e.g., size, diversity, etc.) do not⏎penalize organizations that may have appropriate purposes for training AI systems on relatively⏎small inhouse data sets. HHS can support these organizations by establishing AI resource centers⏎and/or promoting credible technology assessment organizations, such as the Petersen Health⏎Technology Institute, to demonstrate effectiveness of AI tools accessible to small and medium⏎size health care organizations and offering access to datasets and support for AI research.⏎Advancing Public-Private Efforts⏎AHIP believes public-private partnerships and collaboration can play an important role in⏎facilitating data-driven efforts to advance AI. HHS should promote ongoing opportunities for⏎public-private evidence generation and dissemination on best practices to support⏎successful AI development and use. This approach could include partnering with stakeholders⏎to better understand potential applications, use cases, and risks associated with AI. These⏎findings should be used to guide the development of best practices, guidelines, and standards and⏎prioritize resource allocation to ensure investments are high-value for the health care sector.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you for your consideration of our input. AHIP appreciates your efforts to engage⏎stakeholders as you identify priorities for future Department action. We look forward to working⏎with you to advance AI, including opportunities to support innovation in clinical care. If you⏎have questions, please contact me at dlloyd@ahip.org or 202-778-3246.⏎Sincerely,⏎Danielle A. Lloyd⏎Senior Vice President, Private Market Innovations & Quality Initiatives⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed⏎Rule AHIP Comments⏎Page 18 of 49⏎REQUEST FOR INFORMATION ON SOFTWARE AS A SERVICE (SAAS)⏎AHIP recommends that CMS adopt a balanced, evidence-driven, and incremental approach to⏎establishing payment for Software as a Service (SaaS). We recognize the growth of evidence-⏎based digital tools that can improve patient outcomes, reduce administrative burden, and advance⏎efficiency in care delivery. We also agree that cost should not be a barrier to providers and⏎patients accessing proven, high-value technologies. However, CMS should proceed thoughtfully⏎to create a framework that will protect patients, ensure consistent access, guard against fraud,⏎waste and abuse, and stand the test of time given the range of innovations being introduced. The⏎framework should also seek to improve health care affordability for beneficiaries and taxpayers⏎and avoid approaches that dramatically increase manufacturers’ profits at the expense of seniors⏎and taxpayers. We believe this will take more than marginal change including rethinking existing⏎CMS coverage and payment constructs.⏎In considering how to pay for SaaS, CMS should establish policies that promote access to new⏎technologies that are medically reasonable and necessary, meaningfully improve diagnosis or⏎treatment, and decrease or do not significantly increase costs for beneficiaries, taxpayers, or the⏎program. CMS should also avoid policies that create disincentives for advancing new⏎technologies and improving existing products. Moreover, CMS should consider the broader⏎value of technology, including quality, outcomes, efficiency, and patient experience. Digital⏎health technology, including SaaS and artificial intelligence (AI), is not simply a supply like a⏎syringe or the paper a medical record is printed on; these tools are reshaping the world we live⏎in. Medicare policies must contemplate the role of digital health technology not just as inputs⏎into its valuation scheme, but rather as infrastructure that influences clinical decision-making,⏎clinical care delivery, patient experience and engagement, and performance across systems.⏎The novelty of these products, rapid proliferation of new developments, and resulting changes to⏎health care present both new opportunities and new challenges. Unlike innovation in other spaces⏎such as pharmaceuticals, software consistently evolves through iterative updates, including those⏎pushed directly by manufacturers into systems, and advances in machine learning. These tools⏎present vast opportunities to improve patient outcomes, reduce preventable medical errors,⏎reduce administrative frustrations for providers and patients, increase efficiency, and reduce⏎costs. At the same time, they present the risk of reducing the quality of care and increasing costs⏎to drive manufacturer profits for unproven interventions. A strong quality and monitoring⏎framework is important to ensure tools continue to meet the standards for coverage and payment.⏎In addition to monitoring quality and safety, changes to CMS payment policies may be⏎warranted even after a product receives coverage and valuation. For example, to the extent a⏎product drives efficiency gains over time, CMS payment policy may need to be revisited to⏎adjust values downward.⏎Below, we have chosen not to adhere specifically to the order of the questions presented by CMS⏎but do generally answer the preponderance of them. We thought it was important to first discuss⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 19 of 49⏎overarching definitional issues and several precursor considerations including coverage, privacy⏎and security, and quality before moving to a set of frameworks for identifying the type of tool,⏎and potential CMS payment mechanisms, valuation methods, and quality mechanisms.⏎Definitions⏎At the outset, AHIP notes that, while the term “software as a service” is widely used, there is no⏎clear definition within the context of Medicare payment policy. Clear and consistent definitions⏎go beyond semantics and instead have critical implications for purposes of coverage, coding, and⏎payment policies. SaaS at its core is simply the delivery model by which the tool is accessed by⏎the end-user, most commonly via the internet or an app, but can be incorporated directly into⏎hardware. In health care, this could encompass anything ranging from a patient engagement tool,⏎such as a portal, to a prescription digital therapeutic. The range of tools captured under the SaaS⏎model in health care is extensive and therefore requires consideration to account for differences⏎in deployment and use. For example, specific considerations may apply when SaaS incorporates⏎machine learning or other forms of artificial intelligence. In our comments, we generally define⏎SaaS broadly; however, at times we specifically note how Software as a Medical Device⏎(SaMD), as a subset of SaaS, many need special consideration. We urge CMS to establish clear⏎definitions as it moves forward with coverage and payment policies in this area.⏎Coverage⏎Before determining payment, CMS must establish coverage for certain items and services used to⏎diagnose, treat, or prevent a medical condition. There are unique aspects of SaaS that should be⏎considered as part of this process that then impact later payment decisions.⏎Evidence⏎Products of a clinical nature (e.g., apps for diabetes management or tools that detect cardiac⏎conditions) must be rigorously evaluated for coverage before reimbursement is established by⏎CMS. CMS must consider whether there is evidence of a product’s safety, effectiveness, and⏎appropriateness to establish coverage of a new technology is reasonable and necessary. Medicare⏎does not cover items that are experimental, investigational, or not supported by adequate⏎evidence. Private health plans also conduct these types of reviews in determining whether to⏎establish coverage but often struggle with obtaining sufficient evidence from manufacturers.⏎While randomized control trials (RCTs) remain the gold standard, we recognize that RCTs are⏎expensive, time-consuming, and difficult for digital tools that integrate artificial intelligence and⏎evolve rapidly. In cases where RCT data are not available, CMS should require alternate⏎evidence such as retrospective data set validation, prospective observational studies, real-world⏎validation studies, and human factors testing. Sufficient evidence must demonstrate to CMS⏎safety, clinically meaningful and sustained benefits, cost effectiveness (recognizing CMS has⏎statutory limitations), and applicability and consistent access across populations. Well-designed,⏎prospective cohort studies with appropriate size and follow-up from real world evidence data can⏎mitigate limitations in the evidence base.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 20 of 49⏎Through the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), private health plans have been working more⏎closely with manufacturers to ensure sufficient data are available to support coverage decisions.⏎• The Center for Devices and Radiological Health (CDRH’s) Payor Communication⏎Task Force was created to facilitate structured, ongoing dialogue between device⏎manufacturers and payers— inclusive of both Medicare and private health plans. This⏎Task Force supported manufacturers in obtaining timely feedback that may shorten the⏎interval between FDA marketing authorization and coverage decisions.⏎• The Early Payor Feedback Program—part of the Task Force activities—enabled⏎device manufacturers to consult with public and private payers well before final FDA⏎clearance or approval. The intent of the program was to align evidence generation with⏎both regulatory and coverage needs so that manufacturers could better anticipate insurer⏎expectations regarding clinical utility, patient-centered outcomes, and real-world⏎applicability.⏎This sort of parallel processing could substantially speed up the pathway for Software as a⏎Medical Device (SaMD) to get regulatory approval, coverage determinations, and then payment⏎rates. Given recent reorganization and reductions in force, AHIP is unclear whether these⏎programs continue to operate. We urge CMS to continue working with the FDA on parallel⏎review programs and manufacturer guidance opportunities to ensure all payers, public and⏎private, have the information necessary to make informed coverage decisions.⏎We also encourage CMS to develop a framework for the ongoing monitoring of SaMD from a⏎coverage standpoint. Unlike traditional SaaS platforms, AI-enabled tools can continuously learn,⏎update outputs, and perform predictive or diagnostic functions that can directly influence clinical⏎decisions. These capabilities may require not only additional evidence to ensure appropriate⏎integration into CMS coverage and payment policy, but also more frequent monitoring. The⏎FDA has created a Predetermined Change Control Plans that recognize that an item or service⏎that integrates machine learning may change over time. CMS will need to consider policies that⏎permit a certain level of change without re-review (e.g., an annual update of data sources and⏎normalization) and a process to routinely re-review items and services that evolve beyond this⏎level of permitted change. CMS should seek guidance from stakeholders on a framework⏎and process for the review of items and services that incorporate machine learning.⏎Clinical Guidelines⏎Physician specialty societies also play a role in vetting technology and disseminating information⏎on its use by integrating it into clinical guidelines, or other related guidance, establishing its use⏎as part of the standard of care. Specialty society guidance can be particularly salient where the⏎software is not classified as SaMD and thus is not subject to vetting under the FDA approval and⏎regulatory pipeline. However, there is minimal evidence that SaaS-based digital health tools—⏎such as digital therapeutics, remote monitoring platforms, or AI-driven decision aids—are⏎explicitly referenced or incorporated into the majority of such clinical guidelines. AHIP is aware⏎of some examples in the app space such as the American Physical Therapy Association’s⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 21 of 49⏎(APTA)8 Digital Health App Formulary in partnership with ORCHA and the American⏎Psychiatric Association’s App Advisor.9 While the apps themselves may be free or paid directly⏎by the payer, it is still important to understand whether physicians are expected to use such⏎technologies as part of a service and to consider the time and intensity of, for example, reviewing⏎the data output as part of physician compensation by CMS.⏎Notwithstanding SaaS classification as a SaMD, it is important for CMS, and private payers, to⏎understand whether a technology is deployed by only a small set of early adopters or is more⏎widely deployed in establishing coverage and payment policies. The incorporation of technology⏎into medical specialty guidelines demonstrates that adoption of a technology is considered part⏎of the standard of care, which is a significant factor in coverage. It also provides key information⏎in determining how to pay for the technology. When not all physicians or outpatient departments⏎deploy the solution embedded into clinical guidelines, despite it being the standard of care, there⏎are policy mechanisms that can be used by CMS to encourage universal adoption. For example,⏎in moving from computed radiography (CR) to digital radiography (DR), CMS began to reduce⏎reimbursement for CR to incentivize the switch to DR in 2017. A similar approach could be used⏎by CMS for other technologies that are commonplace but not universally adopted.⏎In contrast, if a digital solution is novel and not yet part of the standard of care, a different⏎approach may be warranted whereby a CMS could utilize a simple add-on code that it only⏎covers under certain circumstances and monitor over time. This could be as part of a coverage⏎with evidence program or a temporary new technology add-on payment to ensure access prior to⏎a technology being packaged. Payment policies are not just about the rate— what codes must be⏎billed as part of the service, whether it is paired with a quality measure, whether prior⏎authorization is required— are all tools available to drive care delivery transformation. CMS⏎should work with specialty societies to ensure SaaS-based digital health tools are integrated⏎into their clinical guidelines or in other related guidance.⏎In order for specialty societies to accomplish this, however, they need more information on the⏎digital health tools. There are several organizations seeking to evaluate digital health tools that⏎could be helpful to CMS, private payers, and providers. The Digital Medicine Society (DiMe)⏎has begun work to create methodologies and frameworks for evaluating patient-facing digital⏎health software products, which may include SaaS, including toolkits that can be used as a pre-⏎assessment guide for developers to assess their product’s clinical quality. The Peterson Health⏎Technology Institute (PHTI) focuses on digital/SaaS health technologies evaluating them on⏎three dimensions: clinical effectiveness, cost-effectiveness and budget impact, and overall value⏎for patients, providers, and payers. ECRI and Hayes, now part of Symplr, are both organizations⏎that provide evidence-based assessments of healthcare technologies and services. CMS should⏎consider ways in which it can support outside efforts to assess technologies across a range⏎of factors to help determine if they are reasonable and necessary as well as provide insight⏎into valuation and appropriate payment structures.⏎8 https://www.apta.org/your-practice/practice-models-and-settings/digital-health-in-practice?⏎9 https://www.psychiatry.org/psychiatrists/practice/mental-health-apps⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 22 of 49⏎Benefit Category⏎We also note that CMS has benefit category constraints. CMS, for example, had to cover and pay⏎for RelieVRx (HCPCS E1905), a virtual reality headset with a sequential set of immersive⏎experiences used in Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT), under durable medical equipment. In⏎its coverage decision, CMS provided that “the medical software and the device on which it is⏎housed are so integral to each other that we consider them to be one whole device, not software⏎and a separate device.” This will not always be the case. Especially as we move toward more⏎autonomous AI where the software is doing the bulk of the work and the human in the loop is⏎simply part of quality control. Without a clear benefit category, CMS will continue to have to⏎wedge innovations into existing benefit categories. With a benefit category, CMS could also then⏎create a provider type, conditions for coverage, and cost reporting requirements, which we⏎discuss later. We urge CMS to seek Congressional authority for a new benefit category.⏎Privacy and Security⏎Technology evaluation, whether for coverage or payment, should include a thorough review of⏎privacy and data security considerations. Many new devices, for example, are wirelessly⏎connected to share data and assess functionality, which poses both security and privacy concerns.⏎CMS should also work with other agencies such as ASTP/ONC and the Cybersecurity and⏎Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) to assess other critical aspects of SaaS performance.⏎While SaaS has great potential to improve care, it also introduces new risks to patient safety and⏎privacy. For example, SaaS should be tested and potentially certified by ASTP to promote⏎interoperability in a manner that is consistent with patient safety and privacy. Confirming⏎information can be shared between outside software programs and a person’s electronic medical⏎record in a secure manner is essential to promoting care coordination, avoiding errors, and⏎allowing a patient to have control over their complete medical record. ASTP could also update⏎the SAFER Guides to include SaaS safety.⏎Quality⏎Whether CMS’s payments are made within FFS or through APMs, CMS should consider how to⏎evaluate the quality of care associated with the use of SaaS. As noted below, SaaS tools could⏎significantly disrupt how healthcare is delivered. Quality measures can ensure that the software⏎performs as intended in the real-world and that it supports accurate, safe, and evidence-based⏎care rather than introducing errors or creating gaps in care. CMS should consider developing⏎measures that assess domains such as patient safety, clinical outcomes, and patient experience.⏎Performance measures could assess if SaaS contributes to improved outcomes on current quality⏎measures. Through the establishment of nationwide interoperability, stratified measures could be⏎utilized to provide an opportunity to look at services that rely on SaaS and those that do not.⏎Additionally, measures could directly assess SaaS performance, including assessing patient⏎experience with patient-facing tools. Information on how use of a SaaS tool improves outcomes⏎will provide needed information promote adoption of these services when they improve care, as⏎well as information to help monitor safety risks.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 23 of 49⏎Type of Technology and Use⏎There are many types of SaaS-based digital health solutions. Below we propose a framework for⏎CMS to use classifying the types of solutions, which we believe will help identify appropriate⏎CMS payment mechanism depending on the type of solution:⏎Exhibit 1:⏎Clinical Tools: Tools akin to medical services that diagnose, treat, or prevent disease or injury.⏎• Diagnostics – Disease detection/monitoring (e.g., arrhythmia AI, radiology image⏎analysis).⏎• Treatment / Therapeutics – Active treatment delivery or digital therapeutics (e.g.,⏎insulin dosing algorithms, prescription digital therapeutics for psychosis).⏎Augmentative Tools: Tools that do not perform medical services but support clinical care⏎delivery by aiding clinicians providing a medical service.⏎• Clinical Decision Support (CDS)– Provider-facing workflow integration (e.g., AI CDS⏎in EHRs).⏎• Supplies – Minor items that aide in the provision of care (e.g., smart thermometers).⏎• Adjunctive Care – Supportive, supervised interventions by physicians outside of the⏎clinical setting (e.g., remote monitoring, mental health cognitive behavioral therapy. This⏎captures the administration, not the solution, which is captured below.⏎Patient-Facing Tools: Tools primarily designed for use by a patient outside of a clinical setting,⏎often to generate data, promote engagement, or support management.⏎• Prevention & Engagement – Connected devices, chatbots, and apps that collect and⏎transmit patient-generated data (e.g., wearables, smart inhalers, smart scales, wired⏎clothing, pain management apps) for wellness, lifestyle modification, adherence, and⏎patient engagement tools. This captures the solution, not the administration, which is⏎captured above.⏎Operations Tools: Tools that promote improvements in administrative functions or population-⏎level care management.⏎• Population Health & Analytics – Risk stratification, predictive modeling, outcomes⏎measurement.⏎Administrative & Operations – Non-clinical or back-office tools and operational AI⏎(e.g., staffing optimization, scheduling, claims efficiencies).⏎These descriptions are not perfectly exclusive. For example, some decision-support tools straddle⏎both Clinical and Administrative Support (e.g., AI systems that optimize imaging workflow and⏎simultaneously flag critical findings). As another example, telehealth platforms are at their⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 24 of 49⏎core are communication platforms that would fall under operational tools. However, such⏎platforms may also come with clinical and other administrative enhancements such as⏎triage chatbots, CDS, remote monitoring, and billing and population health tools. Thus,⏎how such platforms are addressed will depend on the totality of the services offered.⏎Payment⏎Principles⏎There are several high-level principles CMS should consider as it develops its SaaS payment⏎policies including promoting sustainable and transparent frameworks, prioritizing value and⏎affordability, taking a site neutral approach to the extent possible, packaging items and services⏎where practicable, and seeking to avoid “buy and bill” structures.⏎Transparent, Sustainable Framework⏎CMS should establish a transparent and sustainable framework for payment of SaaS-based tools.⏎The New Technology Add-On Payment (NTAP) under the Inpatient Prospective Payment⏎System (IPPS) offers a useful precedent in that it sets clear eligibility criteria and offers⏎opportunity for stakeholder input. Similarly, CMS should continue to anchor any SaaS payment⏎framework within existing annual rulemaking cycles. Embedding SaaS policy within annual⏎rulemaking provides transparency and ensures stakeholders have a regular opportunity to review⏎criteria, suggest updates, and recommend refinements as technologies evolve. A durable⏎framework with annual rulemaking as its foundation would allow CMS to: set transparent⏎criteria for initial coverage and enhanced payment; revisit those criteria each year with public⏎input, ensure policies adapt to clinical evidence and market experience; where applicable,⏎provide a clear glide path for SaaS tools to transition from temporary mechanisms into⏎permanent payment structures across settings; and, support alignment with other payers, who⏎often consider Medicare’s rulemaking in their analyses. By embedding SaaS payment in the⏎annual rulemaking process, CMS can foster innovation while ensuring consistency,⏎sustainability, and transparency.⏎Prioritize Value and Affordability⏎CMS should embrace technologies that have strong evidence of patient benefit and can be⏎delivered safely at a lower cost than legacy treatments for the same condition. To guard against⏎inadvertently inflating costs by paying for new treatments in a manner that redirects savings to⏎manufacturers, providers or other actors, CMS should use its authority to ensure savings gained⏎are shared with beneficiaries and taxpayers.⏎Site Neutral Approach⏎AHIP calls attention to CMS’s bifurcated approach to payment for facility and professional⏎services that vary rates based on site of service even when the same items and services are⏎furnished. As CMS embarks upon establishing payment policy for these novel digital solutions,⏎we urge CMS to contemplate a framework that can be applied consistently across both the OPPS⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 25 of 49⏎and PFS. While we understand why CMS has separate RFIs, given the reality that incremental⏎change will be necessary before more sweeping changes, we believe the agency should look⏎toward site neutral payment policies that neither incentivize beneficiaries to seek care in one⏎setting or the other or influence where providers offer items or services.⏎In particular, CMS should look to eliminate its current approach that bifurcates direct and⏎indirect non-physician costs by using practice expense (PE) relative value units (RVUs) under⏎the PFS and ambulatory packaging classifications (APCs) under the OPPS. Instead, we⏎encourage adoption by CMS of a unified approach to valuing SaaS products under both the PFS⏎and OPPS, recognizing that the cost of acquiring, licensing, and maintaining the same⏎technology should not change based on whether it is used in a hospital outpatient department or a⏎physician office. If the underlying technology is identical in functionality and clinical use, the⏎cost profile will be the same regardless of care setting and thus the use of separate payment⏎policies by CMS are unwarranted. While broader harmonization of the full CMS payment⏎systems may take more time given existing complexities embedded in payment policy, CMS⏎should not miss this opportunity to harmonize its methodology for capturing direct and⏎indirect costs of SaaS where practicable. For example, a virtual reality treatment using a⏎headset and software should have the same payment from CMS whether furnished by a physician⏎in an office or by a physician in an outpatient department. CMS’s attention to developing a⏎unified framework for SaaS payment presents a critical opportunity to set the framework for its⏎payment policy on the right path from the outset, rather than perpetuating the broken and⏎inconsistent structures embedded in its payment systems today.⏎Packaging⏎In general, we believe that it makes sense for CMS to package codes together where integral to a⏎service. By doing so, CMS allows providers greater leverage with manufacturers to hold down⏎costs compared to separate or pass through payments. However, we recognize that the use of⏎certain technologies is not universal yet, and thus the use by CMS of packaging policies may⏎overpay in scenarios where the technology is not used. As suggested above, we believe it will be⏎important for CMS to set a framework going forward. Per-use payments by CMS can be⏎problematic in certain circumstances. For example, per-use payments do not fully recognize the⏎scalability of AI and its various uses in health care, such as its ability to generate outputs⏎automatically without clinical decision making, and moreover, can create risk of overuse. As AI⏎continues to improve and strives to automate more tasks traditionally performed by clinical staff⏎and physicians, it can lead to more efficient practice of medicine and truncate the time clinicians⏎spend on diagnostics, screening, prognosis, or other functions.⏎CMS should also consider alternatives to avoid “buy and bill” for solutions. This requires⏎administrative work and risk for physicians and providers. It also creates an incentive for them to⏎purchase higher cost items in order to gain greater margins when payment is structured around a⏎percent of the total. CMS should consider direct payment for certain technologies, such as apps⏎and in-home equipment, where it is not critical for the physician or provider to purchase the item⏎and furnish it to the beneficiary. We recognize that the current benefit categories and provider⏎definitions that CMS must utilize may impede this today and an enrollment system would need⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 26 of 49⏎to be established by CMS for solutions providers, but CMS should be looking to the future and⏎not wedded to today’s payment options.⏎Buy and Bill Approach⏎CMS should also consider alternatives to avoid “buy and bill” for solutions. This requires⏎administrative work and risk for physicians and providers. It also creates an incentive for them to⏎purchase higher cost items in order to gain greater margins when payment is structured around a⏎percent of the total. CMS should consider direct payment for certain technologies, such as apps⏎and in-home equipment, where it is not critical for the physician or provider to purchase the item⏎and furnish it to the beneficiary. We recognize that the current benefit categories and provider⏎definitions may impede this today and an enrollment system would need to be established for⏎solutions providers, but we should be looking to the future and not wedded to today’s payment⏎options.⏎Payment Mechanisms⏎Below, we outline some of the potential payment mechanisms for CMS to use and suggest the⏎payment approach appropriate for CMS to use with respect to various digital solution types.⏎Exhibit 2: Potential CMS Payment Mechanisms⏎1. Practice Expense / Bundled Input⏎• Definition: Digital solutions that are considered part of the normal cost of delivering⏎a covered service, not separately identifiable.⏎• Examples: EHR-integrated clinical decision support used during an office visit; AI⏎workflow tools used by radiologists to sort images; supplies like a smart thermometer⏎or connected scale readings incorporated into routine visits.⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Folded into RVUs under the Physician Fee Schedule or⏎bundled into DRG/APC rates for hospital services.⏎2. Coverage with Evidence Development (CED) / Restricted Coverage⏎• Definition: Temporary coverage for digital solutions requiring further validation of⏎safety, effectiveness, or outcomes.⏎• Examples: Early-stage AI diagnostics; prescription digital therapeutics still⏎generating outcomes data.⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Add-on payment or restricted HCPCS/CPT code tied to⏎registry or mandated evidence collection.⏎3. Transitional Add-On Payments⏎• Definition: Short-term supplemental payments for emerging technologies not yet⏎priced into standard payment systems.⏎• Examples: AI-enabled imaging triage tool billed under NTAP (inpatient) or⏎Transitional Pass-Through (outpatient).⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Extra payment on top of DRG/APC until sufficient⏎evidence/pricing data are available.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 27 of 49⏎4. Permanent Add-On / New Codes⏎• Definition: Long-term recognition of a digital solution via new CPT or HCPCS⏎codes. Add-on codes are appropriate for services that, when provided, are in⏎conjunction with a separately payable service particularly when the service may or⏎may not be provided with the primary service.⏎• Examples: Category I CPT codes for AI-based diagnostic tests; permanent HCPCS⏎codes for FDA-approved digital therapeutics.⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Establish permanent fee schedule rates.⏎5. Packaging into Existing Services⏎• Definition: Digital solutions that are distinct from the base service but functionally⏎supportive and therefore logically bundled into the payment for a group of services.⏎These are solutions that are ancillary, supportive, dependent, or adjunctive to a⏎separately payable service.⏎• Examples: AI-driven image enhancement or triage packaged into radiology⏎interpretation payment; digital pre-op surgical planning software bundled into⏎orthopedic surgery payment; AI-assisted anesthesia monitoring included in a surgical⏎global fee.⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Provider reimbursed a single rate based on the presence⏎of codes for both the primary and packaged items and services.⏎6. Separate Payment⏎• Definition: Applied when the digital solution itself is the primary service delivered to⏎the patient or the focal point of the visit.⏎• Examples: FDA-cleared prescription digital therapeutics; AI-enabled diagnostic tests⏎ordered by a physician and billed separately.⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Standalone CPT/HCPCS code with direct⏎reimbursement. However, other supportive items and services may be packaged with⏎this solution as the primary service.⏎7. Alternative Payment Models (APMs)⏎• Definition: Digital solutions are incorporated into broader risk-bearing or value-⏎based contracts rather than fee-for-service codes.⏎• Examples: Predictive analytics guiding ACO care coordination or remote monitoring⏎platforms through prevention and wellness technologies to manage chronic⏎conditions.⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Payment through global budgets, bundled payments, or⏎shared savings rather than creating new FFS codes and payments; digital solutions⏎funded through the provider’s investment to meet quality/cost benchmarks.⏎8. Direct Manufacturer Payment⏎• Definition: For items and services that are not used in the office/facility but rather⏎used by the patient with the physician’s role as supervisory such as monitoring data⏎and outcomes.⏎• Examples: Nutrition apps, wired clothing, or a smart toilet⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 28 of 49⏎• CMS Payment Approach: Medicare could request authority for a new benefit⏎category from Congress and establish a new provider type.⏎In general, diagnostics and treatments lend themselves to separate payment by CMS as they can⏎be stand-alone or primary-driver services. However, CMS could package diagnostics with other⏎services depending on the care pathway and could package other services with treatments that⏎are primary drivers. Clinical support and supplies lend themselves to either packaging by CMS,⏎as integral to a primary service, or CMS using add-on codes if the primary service does not⏎adequately capture the costs. An administrative tool lends itself to the use by CMS of a relative⏎value adjustment; meaning it is not packaged per se but rather reflected in the existing item or⏎service code and payment. Adjunctive lends itself to direct payment of the technology by CMS⏎and administration codes for the physician or provider. Alternative payment mechanisms could⏎be helpful to CMS in efforts to create incentives to use certain solutions without creating specific⏎payment codes. Below we capture a framework for the first pass at mapping a digital solution to⏎potential CMS payment mechanisms.⏎Exhibit 3: Crosswalk: Digital Health Solution Types vs. CMS Payment Options⏎Solution PE CED NTAP Add- Packaging Separate APMs Direct to⏎Type On Payment Manufacturer⏎lacinilC⏎slooT⏎Diagnostics⏎✗ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✗⏎Treatment /⏎✗ ✔ ✔ ✔ ✗ ✔ ✔ ✗⏎Therapeutics⏎evitatnemguA⏎slooT⏎Clinical⏎✔ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✔ ✗ ✔ ✗⏎Support⏎Supplies⏎✔ ✗ ✗ ✔ ✔ ✗ ✔ ✔⏎Adjunctive⏎✗ ✔ ✗ ✔ ✗ ✔ ✔ ✗ Care⏎-tneitaP⏎gnicaF⏎slooT⏎Prevention⏎✔ ✔ ✗ ✔ ✗ ✔ ✔ ✔⏎&⏎Engagement⏎lifestyle,⏎adherence)⏎slooT⏎lanoitarepO⏎Analytics &⏎✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✔ ✗⏎Population⏎Health⏎Administr⏎✔ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✗ ✔ ✗⏎ation &⏎Operation⏎s⏎There are many facets that should be considered by CMS when narrowing down these options to⏎a specific payment approach. The mapping by CMS of particular digital health tools to certain⏎payment models should consider the maturity of the solution, clinical role, relationship to⏎Medicare benefit categories, level of autonomy and other factors. To summarize some of the⏎logic underlying our recommendation to CMS:⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 29 of 49⏎1. Is it the primary service? → Separate payment.⏎2. Is it supportive? → Minor = Practice Expense; Major = Packaging/Add-on.⏎3. Is it mature/ubiquitous? → Practice expense / Packaging⏎4. Is it emerging/uncertain? → Coverage with Evidence or Temporary Add-On.⏎5. Is it validated? → Permanent Add-On Code.⏎6. Does it only function at the population/system level? → APM.⏎Below we begin to build out a decision tree that considers some of these factors to arrive at a⏎final determination of a payment mechanism for CMS to use.⏎Exhibit 4: Algorithm for Determining CMS Payment Mechanism for Digital Health⏎Payment⏎Step 1. Is the digital solution the primary service being delivered?⏎If YES → Separate Payment by CMS (initially).⏎Example: FDA-cleared prescription digital therapeutic.⏎Cost impact:⏎• If it adds costs: Allow separate payment at first (often under Coverage with Evidence or⏎Temporary Add-On) but tie to outcomes data collection.⏎• If it replaces an existing service: Start with dual payments (old + new), then gradually⏎phase down the old as evidence shows equivalence or superiority. Example: AI-enabled⏎pathology tool replacing manual reads — could be paid separately at first by CMS, but⏎CMS could lower pathology professional fees over time to drive substitution.⏎• If it lowers costs directly: CMS should consider rebasing global rates downwards over⏎time while preserving provider margin during the adoption phase.⏎Step 2. Is the digital solution supportive of another covered service?⏎If NO → Population/System-Level Tool → CMS use of APM.⏎Example: Predictive analytics guiding ACO care management.⏎Cost impact:⏎• If adds cost but saves downstream: CMS should build into shared savings/risk-based⏎payments.⏎• If replaces manual care management: CMS should reduce practice expense⏎assumptions in APM benchmarks accordingly.⏎If YES → Go to Step 3.⏎Step 3. Is the supportive solution minor and widely used (like a supply or workflow tool)?⏎If YES → Practice Expense / CMS should utilize Bundled Input.⏎Cost impact:⏎• If low-cost and supportive: Absorb into CMS’s practice expense valuation.⏎• If replaces manual labor: CMS should adjust RVUs downward for the replaced inputs⏎(e.g., radiology AI replacing junior radiologist prep work).⏎• If drives net savings: CMS should allow it to remain bundled; savings flow through⏎rebasing by CMS of practice expense inputs over time.⏎If NO (distinct clinical contribution) → Go to Step 4.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 30 of 49⏎Step 4. Does the solution need further evidence?⏎If YES → CMS should utilize Coverage with Evidence Development (CED) or Temporary⏎Add-On (NTAP/TPT).⏎Cost impact:⏎• If adds new cost without proven benefit: CMS should cover only with registry⏎requirements and sunset unless value is shown.⏎• If replaces existing service: CMS should keep both covered temporarily, then phase⏎down the old service fee if the digital proves non-inferior or superior.⏎If NO (validated use) → Go to Step 5.⏎Step 5. Should it be packaged into the base service or paid separately?⏎If supportive but distinct → CMS should utilize packaging.⏎Cost impact:⏎• If adds cost: CMS should temporarily increase bundled rate to reflect adoption, then⏎rebase as costs normalize.⏎• If replaces existing steps: CMS should lower the base rate inputs for the replaced⏎elements while holding provider margins steady in transition.⏎If primary or standalone → Permanent Add-On / CMS should Utilize Separate⏎Code.⏎Cost impact:⏎• If adds cost and value: CMS should pay separately but monitor for overutilization.⏎• If replaces existing service: CMS should shift payment by reducing the old code⏎valuation (e.g., imaging read fee) and transferring value to the digital service code.⏎• If lowers cost significantly: CMS should rebase system-wide DRG/APC or PFS rates⏎downward once adoption stabilizes.⏎Step 6. If the solution only functions at population/system level (no direct patient billing⏎pathway).⏎Payment → CMS should utilize Alternative Payment Models.⏎Cost impact:⏎• If adds cost but reduces downstream utilization: CMS should fund through shared⏎savings or capitation.⏎• If replaces manual workflows (e.g., nurse outreach): CMS should adjust benchmarks⏎downward as adoption scales.⏎We note that given the innovative nature of many of these technologies and the rapid rate at⏎which they evolve, CMS may need to more heavily rely on the coverage with evidence or⏎temporary add-on pathway at first to develop the claims and cost data needed to support a⏎pathway to permanent coverage and payment.⏎Capital⏎The framework above assumes the additional costs to the system are on margin. However, CMS⏎should consider the possibility of large-scale, capital-intensive technologies. Under the OPPS⏎there is not an explicit capital payment like under the inpatient PPS; instead, acquisition costs for⏎large-cale inputs are bundled with operating costs. However, outpatient departments have cost⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 31 of 49⏎reports where they are able to apportion costs across services through revenue codes to defray⏎capital costs. Nor does the current PE valuation methodology per se reflect such capital⏎investments. For example, if future innovation enables deployment of robotic systems that rely⏎on SaaS and perform functions traditionally furnished by nursing staff, the costs of acquiring,⏎licensing, and amortizing such capital investment would not be fully incorporated under the⏎current PE framework.⏎In such cases, CMS’s existing methodology would undervalue the investment. Temporary⏎policies such as, or akin to, the New Technology Add-on Payment (NTAP) provide a mechanism⏎for CMS to recognize new items; however, these approaches are narrow in scope, temporary, and⏎may not be sustainable for integrating capital-based technologies. As technology continues to⏎evolve in ways that blur the line between labor and capital, e.g., substituting staff time with⏎automation, the current CMS methodology risks lagging behind innovation and failing to⏎adequately capture resource costs.⏎Moreover, both the OPPS and PFS are budget neutral systems. The only way to increase the⏎budget is through the update systems— the market basket under OPPS and the statutorily fixed⏎conversion factor increases under PFS. Under the OPPS the market basket is a Laspeyres index⏎that measures the change in the cost of a fixed “basket” of inputs over time. It is rare that the⏎Bureau of Labor Statistics makes an adjustment to its indices for major net-new items (a rare⏎example of when it did was when seat belts were introduced into cars). It did not account for, for⏎example, the introduction of electronic health records. In part, this created the need for⏎Congressional intervention through the Meaningful Use program. CMS should keep in mind⏎the constraints of the payment update systems and consider budget neutrality adjustments⏎(both up and down) as new innovations are introduced in healthcare.⏎Given the budget neutral system and the lack of a mechanism to increase payments via the⏎update factors, we are concerned that, given the role of relative values in CMS’s payment⏎methodology, relative value shifts in favor of technology-heavy services will result in an⏎imbalance across specialties. We are particularly concerned there may be a detrimental impact on⏎primary care versus proceduralists. It is possible that more of the solutions primary care will use⏎will be expected by CMS’s methodology to be absorbed into the notion of practice expense or⏎packaged, while proceduralist may more often rely on solutions that are likely to have add-on⏎codes or are paid separately by CMS. CMS should conduct ongoing analyses as we move⏎forward to ensure it does not unintentionally erode overall payments to primary care and⏎does not discourage the use of digital health solutions by primary care physicians.⏎Valuation⏎Once the appropriate payment mechanism is selected by CMS, the next step is to determine its⏎value. We believe the general construct of physician payment valuation is applicable to CMS use⏎—Practice Expense (PE), Work, and Malpractice values. However, we believe an additional⏎component is needed for CMS use that considers the cost of the technology itself from a capital⏎perspective. While some digital solutions may have de minimis costs, some may be significant as⏎innovations evolve.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 32 of 49⏎It is important to note that there is an interplay between these four factors. The factors may move⏎independently. One advantage of this framework for valuation by CMS is that it does not just⏎rely on manufacturers’ price but balances the potential impact of the solution on the factors—⏎capturing both cost increase and efficiencies gained.⏎Ideally, increased efficiency and improved outcomes should reduce costs and produce savings in⏎the long run. CMS must monitor the impact of establishing reimbursement for new technologies⏎to ensure that AI is driving down costs and improving outcomes, rather than increasing overall⏎costs or leading to overutilization. Moreover, while reimbursement by CMS may be warranted to⏎offset the costs of acquisition of AI to avoid disincentivizing use of evidence-based technology,⏎as costs decline due to new competition or changes in the market, CMS’s reimbursement must be⏎re-evaluated and adjusted commensurate with developments.⏎Digital Solution Acquisition⏎Currently, given the novel innovations, there is not much data on which to assess value. If CMS⏎were able to create a new benefit category, provider type, and conditions for coverage, it could⏎also create a cost reporting system to help establish payment rates. Otherwise, CMS could rely⏎on provider costs captured in the hospital cost reports. This option has some drawbacks,⏎however: 1) it does not capture physician office costs, 2) the lack of hospital cost accounting⏎systems necessitate the use of rough cost to charge ratios, and 3) it would not support CMS⏎obtaining information on digital solutions that it may choose to pay for directly. However, either⏎of these approaches would be preferable to the use by CMS of a method akin to average⏎wholesale, acquisition, or sales price that does little to control prices. Regardless of the method,⏎CMS needs to seek ways in which to obtain data to understand the price of the solution itself.⏎Practice Expenses⏎Practice expenses (PE) are a critical component of Medicare payment policy as they capture the⏎direct and indirect costs associated with furnishing care, including software. For SaaS and AI-⏎based tools in particular, PE inputs are a particularly relevant element of valuation in Medicare.⏎A solution may reduce or eliminate distinct physician work while at the same time increasing⏎acquisition costs and/or PE. Direct PE includes medical supplies and services, medical⏎equipment including initial costs and peripheral costs such as software and maintenance,⏎nonphysician clinical labor wages/benefits. Indirect PE includes non-clinical labor⏎wages/benefits, lease/rent, office services like cleaning, nonmedical office supplies and⏎equipment, IT supplies and services, EHR systems. CMS has historically treated SaaS as indirect⏎PE where associated costs are not allocable to an individual patient or individual service.⏎As CMS has acknowledged, costs associated with software—including licensing, analysis fees,⏎integration into existing technology, and ongoing monitoring and updates—are not well⏎accounted for in these existing PE methodologies. Unlike a piece of equipment, an analysis fee⏎for software does not require physical space in an office and may not require administrative staff⏎hours to maintain it. SaaS tools also include fixed and variable components that can be more⏎difficult to price in a way that reflects actual resource cost.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 33 of 49⏎In certain cases, SaaS costs are appropriately captured in CMS’s indirect PE methodology rather⏎than incorporated as a separate direct PE input. However, survey data that informs indirect PE⏎costs relies on data associated with software, licensing, and analysis fees with minimal costs.⏎New SaaS tools could impact many facets of PE including staffing levels or time, power⏎required, space needed, related supplies etc. As technology evolves, costs may no longer be⏎minimal and existing data sets used to inform indirect PE RVUs may no longer be appropriate⏎for use by CMS in capturing the associated costs.⏎A similar challenge exists in the hospital outpatient setting. Hospitals apportion indirect costs⏎through the cost report, often distributing overhead based on square footage of a unit or other⏎proxies that do not align with how SaaS solutions generate costs. SaaS tools generally do not⏎drive incremental physical space, utilities, or custodial expenses in the way traditional inputs do.⏎Instead, they may augment existing or substitute for equipment, staffing, or other direct practice⏎resources. As a result, the current hospital cost allocation methods used by CMS risk⏎undervaluing the resource costs of SaaS tools and misaligning incentives for adoption.⏎In considering valuation and how to reflect SaaS costs, CMS should ensure SaaS-related inputs⏎are accounted for in a consistent, evidence-based manner that supports appropriate access and⏎sustainable adoption, while laying the groundwork for longer-term reforms to unify CMS⏎payment methodologies. To this end, we echo our comments suggesting that CMS align its⏎approach to paying for SaaS across the PFS and OPPS.⏎Work⏎The American Medical Association (AMA) Current Procedural Terminology® (CPT) Appendix⏎S10 sets forth a taxonomy for classifying AI tools in the context of clinical work. This framework⏎could be used as a starting point by CMS for contemplating how clinician work valuation should⏎be addressed in the context of Medicare SaaS payment. While the AMA’s distinctions are⏎specific to CPT coding, they can inform CMS payment policy by aligning reimbursement and⏎oversight requirements with the level of clinical independence and associated risk. Appendix S⏎categorizes AI-enabled services as:⏎• Assistive: Detects data without analysis or conclusions, like computer-aided detection⏎imaging. Requires physician or other qualified health professional (QHP) interpretation⏎and report.⏎• Augmentative: Analyzes or quantifies data in a clinically meaningful way, like⏎continuous glucose monitoring. Requires physician or other qualified health professional⏎(QHP) interpretation and report.⏎• Autonomous: Interprets data and independently generates conclusions, like diagnostics⏎assessment for diabetic retinopathy.⏎Level 1: The autonomous AI draws conclusions and offers diagnosis and/or⏎o⏎management options, which are contestable and require physician or other QHP⏎action to implement.⏎10 https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/cpt/cpt-appendix-s-ai-taxonomy-medical-services-procedures.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 34 of 49⏎Level 2: The autonomous AI draws conclusions and initiates diagnosis and/or⏎o⏎management options with alert/opportunity for override, which may require⏎physician or other QHP action to implement.⏎Level 3: The autonomous AI draws conclusions and initiates management, which⏎o⏎requires physician or other QHP initiative to contest.⏎In assessing the value of work, particularly compared to existing services, this type of framework⏎could help CMS assess efficiency gains. Something that is autonomous supplants more of the⏎physician’s work than something that is augmentative, and augmentative more than something⏎assistive. Even with more autonomous systems, however, physician oversight, such as reviewing⏎technology outputs, communicating results to patients, and documenting findings, should be⏎considered in valuation. Of course, acquisition costs and changes to practice or malpractice⏎expense could offset these gains, thus it is essential for CMS to consider all four factors in⏎tandem.⏎Malpractice⏎CMS should also consider whether and how malpractice costs should be factored into the⏎valuation of SaaS. Under the current physician relative value system, malpractice RVUs reflect⏎the cost of professional liability insurance associated with furnishing a service. However, it is⏎unclear how liability will be assigned in the context of SaaS and care delivery, whether from a⏎regulatory or judicial perspective, which will impact the costs associated with malpractice⏎insurance and potential judgments. For example, a SaaS diagnostic product could be flawed,⏎which could accrue a level of risk for the physician relying on the output, the facility where the⏎technology is deployed, and the manufacturer of the product. In addition, as SaaS tools evolve,⏎the absence of an established framework for evaluation could make it increasingly challenging⏎for providers and physicians to assess a technology before use or know what led to an inaccurate⏎result. This risk is especially significant for technologies that use techniques like deep neural⏎networks where the model’s inner workings are not transparent. Moreover, as SaaS becomes⏎more autonomous and the role of professionals decreases, there could be a shift in liability to⏎manufacturers. Insurance, excess insurance, and reinsurance carriers will have to determine⏎premiums that account for this lack of clarity, which may lead to higher malpractice premiums⏎until more data and experience are available. CMS should monitor for changes in malpractice⏎expenses and consider whether adjustments need to be made for specialties that rely more⏎heavily on technology.⏎This challenge is particularly acute for SaaS products used outside of traditional clinical settings.⏎Examples include mobile health applications that manage medication adherence, support⏎cognitive behavioral therapy, or prompt patient reporting of symptoms; at-home devices such as⏎connected blood glucose monitors or bathmats that sense the presence of ulcers; and wearable⏎technologies like smartwatches with atrial fibrillation detection or continuous activity tracking.⏎Reduced clinical oversight heightens the importance of accountability frameworks, oversight⏎mechanisms, and safeguards to ensure patient safety and appropriate use. In some cases,⏎providers may not be best suited to conduct continuing support of patient product use outside the⏎clinical office for products with complex functionality, frequent software updates, or integration⏎requirements. For products used outside of traditional health care locations, CMS should⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 35 of 49⏎give special consideration to direct payment to manufacturers so that there are conditions⏎for coverage and enforcement mechanisms. CMS should have the manufacturer assume⏎responsibility for providing technical support and patient assistance. For example, CMS should⏎have the manufacturer offer patient-facing technical support to troubleshoot device issues,⏎resolve connectivity problems, and provide education on correct use. CMS should also have the⏎manufacturer provide training resources to both patients and clinicians so that the product’s⏎capabilities and limitations are clearly understood.⏎Data Sources⏎Across these four factors— Digital Solution Acquisition, PE, Work, and Malpractice— CMS⏎should look to a variety of data sources, including publicly available information, manufacturer-⏎submitted cost data, and other market-based inputs, to more accurately assess the costs⏎associated with SaaS tools and determine valuation. These sources can complement Medicare⏎claims data and provide a more holistic picture of true technology costs. Current claims data,⏎whether public or private, do not provide sufficiently granular information. Private payers⏎struggle with obtaining the data needed for coverage and payment analyses, as well as⏎assessments of the quality and privacy and security of technologies. Manufacturer data⏎agreements via pilot programs, value-based contract arrangements, and grant sponsorships for⏎strategic emerging technologies are possibilities for private payers to obtain some of this⏎information, but more global work by CMS to drive transparency would have a much greater⏎impact. Private payers recommend that CMS devise a system to create more transparency⏎around manufacturers’ costs and provider acquisition costs.⏎FFS Alternatives⏎While we have mostly provided recommendations on how CMS should pay for SaaS within the⏎context of FFS, the CMS Innovation Center may be a more appropriate fit for testing Medicare⏎SaaS reimbursement given it serves as a laboratory for innovation, has the ability to waive⏎certain generally applicable regulations to permit testing of new concepts, stipulates rigorous⏎evaluation, and pilots time-limited models that can be halted if cost or quality concerns arise. In⏎addition to using the Innovation Center to test the actual reimbursement of SaaS, we also⏎encourage CMS to consider better integration of technology into APMs. Overutilization is less of⏎a concern in models that incorporate total cost of care or shared accountability frameworks,⏎making value-based models an attractive laboratory for experimenting with different⏎approaches.⏎CMS’s Innovation Center should develop risk-based contracts, for use in voluntary partnership⏎with health plans, as a pathway for incorporating SaaS into care delivery and reimbursing SaaS⏎solutions. As CMS has recognized in its characterization of SaaS products as indirect PE inputs,⏎SaaS products can deliver ongoing value by improving efficiency, care quality, or patient⏎outcomes across populations rather than being tied to a discrete clinical encounter. A risk-based⏎contracting approach from the CMS Innovation Center would allow payers and providers to⏎align payment with measurable performance metrics more effectively than in fee-for-service,⏎with the goal of driving reductions in avoidable utilization, improvements in adherence, or⏎demonstrated operational savings, while safeguarding against potential inappropriate utilization⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 36 of 49⏎or excessive cost growth like in per-use arrangements. A risk-based model from the Innovation⏎Center would better reflect the longitudinal and outcomes-driven nature of many SaaS tools,⏎while ensuring accountability and value for both Medicare beneficiaries and the program.⏎SaaS tools are being integrated into chronic care management and risk-based payment models to⏎support earlier detection of disease progression, improve patient engagement, and enhance care⏎coordination. These tools can:⏎• Aggregate and analyze patient-reported and device generated data between visits;⏎• Trigger alerts for care teams when certain thresholds are met, enabling earlier⏎intervention;⏎• Support medication management and adherence monitoring;⏎• Provide patients with tailored education and engagement resources. Identify high risk⏎patients, stratify populations, and support proactive interventions; and⏎• Expand access and empower patient self-management for lower-complexity needs,⏎allowing clinical staff to focus more time on higher-acuity care.⏎One early example is Project Sonar, developed in partnership with a gastroenterology practice⏎and a health plan.11 This specialty model home used cloud-based technology to collect patient-⏎reported outcomes from individuals with Crohn’s disease between office visits. Nurses and⏎physicians could then identify increasing or worsening symptoms early, intervene, and prevent⏎hospitalization. The health plan provided prospective, per-patient-per-month enhanced payments⏎to support chronic care management. Project Sonar demonstrates that SaaS-enabled remote⏎symptom monitoring can improve outcomes for patients with chronic disease and reduce overall⏎spending, demonstrating how software-based tools can advance outcomes under alternative⏎payment arrangements.⏎CMS should leverage its Innovation Center to pilot new models of payment and care that⏎leverage or test use of SaaS, including payment mechanisms for appropriate products. The⏎Innovation Center could test incentives for incorporation of SaaS to support care delivery that are⏎linked to quality measures evaluating performance.⏎Quality⏎Whether payments are made within FFS or through APMs, CMS should consider how to⏎evaluate the quality of care associated with the use of SaaS. As noted above, SaaS tools could⏎significantly disrupt how healthcare is delivered. Quality measures ensure that the software⏎performs as intended in the real-world and that it supports accurate, safe, and evidence-based⏎care rather than introducing errors or creating gaps in care. CMS should consider developing⏎measures that assess domains such as patient safety, clinical outcomes, and patient experience.⏎Performance measures could assess if SaaS contributes to improved outcomes on current quality⏎measures. For example, as we establish nationwide interoperability stratify measures to look at⏎services that rely on SaaS and those that do not. Additionally, measures could directly assess⏎SaaS performance, including assessing patient experience with patient-facing tools. Information⏎11 https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/private/pdf/253406/ProjectSonarSonarMD.pdf.⏎⏎CY 2026 PFS Proposed Rule⏎AHIP Comments⏎Page 37 of 49⏎on how use of a SaaS tool improves outcomes will promote adoption of these services as well as⏎help monitor for safety risks.⏎CMS should also work with other agencies such as ASTP/ONC and the Cybersecurity and⏎Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) to assess other critical aspects of SaaS performance.⏎While SaaS has great potential to improve care, it also introduces new risks to patient safety and⏎privacy. For example, SaaS should be tested and potentially certified by ASTP to promote⏎interoperability. Confirming information can be shared between outside software programs and a⏎person’s electronic medical record is essential to promoting care coordination, avoiding errors,⏎and allowing a patient to have control over their complete medical record. ASTP could also⏎update the SAFER Guides to include SaaS safety. Coordination with the FDA and other agencies⏎will also be critical to address safety, liability, and vendor accountability as tools are continually⏎updated.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0239♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from MHK, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MHK♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services,⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and ONC for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Subject: HHS RFI Response: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary and ONC Leadership,⏎MHK appreciates the opportunity to provide comments on the Request for Information (RFI) published on⏎December 23, 2025, regarding strategies to accelerate the adoption and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in⏎clinical care. We commend HHS, ASTP, and ONC for inviting stakeholder input across regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research and development to responsibly scale AI tools that improve outcomes, reduce⏎burden, and lower costs for patients and government programs.⏎MHK delivers solutions that support more than 90 million lives served by health plans and pharmacy benefit⏎managers nationwide, in care management, utilization management (including prior authorization), pharmacy⏎management, appeals and grievances, and population health. Our mission is to enable health plans to deliver⏎efficient, compliant, and patient-centered care through advanced technology solutions. AI can transform care⏎delivery, reduce administrative burden, and improve outcomes when implemented responsibly and⏎transparently.⏎MHK supports HHS’s vision to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care and has developed responsible AI⏎enablement within our platform. The AI Agentic event framework MHK has designed and implemented⏎internally has been recognized by our channel partner, Amazon Web Services (AWS), as a best-in-class⏎approach compared to other solutions in the market. Within this framework, we are leveraging AI agents to⏎streamline processes and reduce administrative burdens. Our approach prioritizes compliance, transparency,⏎and interoperability to ensure trust and safety. We offer the following detailed responses to the RFI questions⏎based on our experience designing, validating, and integrating AI into payer workflows. We also welcome the⏎opportunity to meet, share our learnings, and partner with you.⏎Sincerely,⏎Ashley Cabrera Geoff Felder Maurice Herbelin, MD⏎Chief Compliance Officer Chief Technology Officer Chief Medical Officer⏎ACabrera@mhk.com Geoff.Felder@mhk.com Maurice.Herbelin@mhk.com⏎⏎Detailed Responses to RFI Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎• Despite progress through FHIR and related ONC initiatives, access to complete, high-quality,⏎standards-based data across EHRs, payer systems, and ancillary sources remains inconsistent.⏎AI systems rely on longitudinal, well-structured datasets, and fragmentation increases⏎development cost, limits generalizability, and slows deployment. Model Context Protocol (MCP)⏎integration can accelerate adoption by enabling secure access to clinical data across client⏎environments without requiring data migration or format standardization.⏎• Fee-for-service reimbursement models do not always incentivize technologies to⏎reduce utilization, shorten administrative cycle times, or lower the total cost of care. Therefore,⏎AI tools that improve efficiency or prevent unnecessary services may lack a direct⏎reimbursement pathway.⏎• Public concern regarding algorithmic bias, lack of transparency, and the risk of automation-⏎driven denials, particularly in prior authorization, creates skepticism and slows adoption even⏎where safeguards exist. One approach is to avoid training proprietary AI models⏎independently and instead use industry-standard foundation models (Anthropic Claude,⏎Google Gemini) and provide clinical context through Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) via⏎MCP-connected data sources and unstructured document processing. This can reduce the⏎introduction of training data bias. The bias concerns can be limited to prompt injection⏎(malicious input manipulation), cross-tenant data access, decision confidence fairness, and RAG⏎context completeness (ensuring the right clinical evidence is retrieved for all patient types).⏎• Ambiguity in compliance frameworks, particularly around HIPAA and state-specific privacy⏎regulations, can hinder adoption. Implementing comprehensive HIPAA Technical Safeguards (45⏎CFR § 164.312) could streamline adoption. This should include: Access Control with 3-tier⏎capability token system (24-hour job tokens, 1-hour agent tokens with automatic expiration),⏎Audit Controls with 365-day CloudWatch retention and 7-year archive capability, Integrity⏎Controls with client-specific KMS encryption, Person/Entity Authentication via AWS Cognito⏎OAuth2, and Transmission Security with TLS 1.3. Our audit framework generates 9 report types⏎(daily/monthly/quarterly/annual/on demand), providing concrete evidence of compliance.⏎⏎• Uncertainty regarding legal frameworks and governance, particularly relating to liability⏎allocation, indemnification, and the documentation of human oversight, delays contracting and⏎implementation processes, especially for AI applications within utilization management or⏎clinical decision support integrated into electronic health records (EHRs). It is critical to have⏎human-in-the-loop governance at multiple levels, including decision separation, confidence-⏎based routing, progressive automation with safeguards, and administrative controls.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care, and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical⏎care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎• HHS should prioritize payment models that reward AI-enabled reductions in administrative⏎burden, improvements in the timeliness of care, and measurable improvements in outcomes.⏎Introduce value-based incentives for AI-enabled solutions and documentation tools that⏎demonstrate reduced administrative burden, costs, and improved outcomes. Additionally, HHS⏎should simplify approval pathways for AI-driven utilization management tools, provided they⏎maintain clinical oversight by licensed professionals.⏎• HHS policy should clearly distinguish between AI that assists clinicians or reviewers and AI⏎that autonomously determines coverage or clinical actions. Existing CMS guidance stating that⏎AI may support but not replace individualized medical necessity determinations should be⏎codified.⏎⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that the use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (including liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist, and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎• We recommend establishing predictable regulatory guidance that balances innovation with⏎patient safety. When AI informs but does not independently make clinical⏎or utilization management decisions, existing legal frameworks do not clearly address:⏎o Allocation of liability when AI recommendations are followed or overridden⏎o Responsibility for performance drift, bias, or misclassification⏎o Documentation standards demonstrating meaningful human review⏎• HHS could play a constructive role by:⏎o Issuing guidance on minimum governance expectations for clinical AI, including⏎human-in-the-loop workflows, audit trails, and documentation requirements.⏎o Providing optional model contract language addressing indemnification and liability⏎for AI-assisted clinical use cases.⏎o Clarifying permissible uses and disclosures under HIPAA for AI training, monitoring,⏎and safety auditing, including recommended de-identification practices.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎• Promising evaluation methods and metrics include: bias and fairness testing using parity⏎metrics; robustness testing with documentation standards; post-deployment continuous⏎performance surveillance of FHIR loads and connections; and payer-linked outcomes⏎(appeal rates, overturn rates, adverse events) for AI in prior authorization and care⏎coordination.⏎• HHS should support funding public-private development, such as shared datasets⏎(clinical and claims), evaluation protocols, and independent measurement teams to⏎prioritize tools, ambient documentation, prior authorization, and health care navigation.⏎We recommend establishing grants for model registries and audit tools that use⏎standardized metric schemas and reporting. HHS can also support competitions that⏎incentivize reproducible processes that reduce bias and improve clinician trust, with⏎performance-based awards.⏎⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎• HHS should support a voluntary, risk-tiered assurance framework aligned with ONC⏎certification and TEFCA. HHS should also recognize the distinction between AI platforms⏎that train proprietary models and those that orchestrate industry foundation models.⏎• HHS should encourage third-party accreditation for prior authorization using AI and⏎clinical documentation using AI (scribes), with audit trails that demonstrate⏎human-in-the-loop governance, explainability, and outcomes (timeliness, overturn⏎rates, adverse events).⏎• Codify payer expectations and guardrails for AI use in coverage decisions and ensure⏎the architecture embeds key guardrails.⏎• Funding AI literacy and governance training for clinicians, medical directors,⏎and utilization management professionals. Training curricula could incorporate⏎anonymized examples to teach clinicians how to interpret AI confidence scores,⏎recognize performance drift, and exercise effective oversight.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations, and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights into quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎• Ambient clinical documentation tools have met expectations by reducing clinician⏎burnout, preserving workforce capacity, and improving documentation quality.⏎• AI deployment often lags expectations due to integration complexity, governance⏎requirements, and validation burden.⏎• AI tools with High-Potential include prior authorization orchestration with real-time⏎data retrieval and human override, population-level risk navigation integrating social⏎drivers and benefits data, and explainable quality signal extraction beyond traditional⏎Healthcare Effectiveness Data and Information Set (HEDIS) measures.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the⏎primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎• Roles that are key influencers and decision makers among health plans include:⏎o Chief Medical Officer/Medical Directors (clinical sponsorship, guardrails).⏎o Chief Information Officers and Clinical Informatics (workflow integration, EHR⏎alignment).⏎⏎o Utilization Management/Case Management Leaders for payer-side adoption in⏎prior authorization, concurrent review, and care coordination.⏎o Compliance/Privacy/Legal Officers.⏎• Primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI include⏎o Interoperability friction, such as fragmented EHRs, a lack of standardized APIs,⏎and mixed protocols.⏎o Payment uncertainty, including the fact that fee-for-service rarely rewards⏎deflationary technology, and coverage decisions could be slow.⏎o Site-specific performance drift and bias audits, which could lead to validation⏎and monitoring burdens.⏎o Workforce readiness and change management are impacted by clinician trust,⏎documentation standards, and governance.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎• FHIR to enable plug and play across exchanges for clinical, claims, and prior⏎authorization datasets.⏎• Claims and clinical linkage for outcomes and total costs supported by AI tools (LOS,⏎readmissions, adverse events, equity impacts).⏎• Benchmarking tools such as national model registries with aligned metrics for pre- and⏎post-deployment data.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎• Some of the challenges that patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI include:⏎o Faster access to care by reducing prior authorization delays and having real-time⏎decisions, as well as transparent reasons for determinations.⏎o Fewer diagnostic delays because of AI triage and timely routing.⏎o Increasing clinician presence: AI would reduce time spent on charting and⏎increase time spent listening to ambient documentation.⏎• Some of the concerns that patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI include:⏎o Patients express concern about algorithmic bias and unequal denials, particularly⏎in utilization management.⏎o There is significant concern about batch denials caused by automation.⏎o Sharing data over networks raises privacy and trust concerns.⏎⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part⏎of clinical care?⏎• The specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize include:⏎o Implementation science for AI-enabled prior authorization⏎o Real-world performance drift and monitoring methodologies⏎o Total cost of ownership and payment models for deflationary technologies⏎o Explainable quality measures linked to clinical outcomes⏎Conclusion:⏎MHK is committed to advancing responsible AI adoption in clinical care, and we applaud the⏎Administration’s efforts to accelerate its use across the health care system. We believe⏎collaboration between HHS, health plans, and technology partners is essential to achieving⏎better outcomes, reducing costs, and improving patient experience. The AI agentic event⏎framework we have built internally has been recognized by our channel partner, Amazon Web⏎Services, as a best-in-class approach compared with other solutions in the market. Based on our⏎experience designing, validating, and integrating AI into payer workflows, we would welcome⏎the opportunity to meet, share our learnings, and partner with you.⏎For reference, some of MHK’s current AI initiatives include:⏎• Automated Prior Authorization: Leveraging AI to reduce turnaround times while⏎maintaining compliance.⏎• AI Agentic Enablement: Extracting insights from clinical notes to support decision-making.⏎• Predictive Analytics for Care Management: Identifying high-risk members for early⏎intervention.⏎• Member Engagement Tools: AI-driven outreach tools to improve adherence and⏎satisfaction.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0240♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Submitted via the Federal eRulemaking Portal⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎As the leading organizations for nutrition professionals, the Academy of Nutrition and Dietetics⏎(the Academy) and the American Society for Nutrition (ASN) appreciate the opportunity to⏎provide comment on the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care published in the Federal Register on December 23,⏎2025. Representing more than 112,000 registered dietitian nutritionists (RDNs), nutrition and⏎dietetic technicians, registered (NDTRs), and advanced-degree nutritionists, the Academy is the⏎largest association of nutrition and dietetics practitioners working across clinical care, public⏎health, research, education, food systems, and industry. ASN is a global scientific society⏎representing over 8,000 researchers, clinicians, and policy experts who advance nutrition science⏎to improve public health and clinical outcomes. Collectively, these organizations represent a⏎substantial segment of the health workforce and research community, directly engaged in the⏎development, evaluation, and implementation of technologies and innovative tools, such as AI,⏎in clinical care, population health, and precision nutrition.⏎We offer the following comments and recommendations on the RFI on accelerating the adoption⏎and use of AI as part of clinical care.1,2 This RFI appropriately recognizes that regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research and development must be aligned to foster responsible, effective,⏎and scalable adoption of AI across the health care system. We appreciate HHS’s efforts to solicit⏎concrete, experience-based feedback across these domains to support responsible, effective, and⏎scalable AI integration into health care delivery.⏎1 Office of Management and Budget. Accelerating Federal Use of Artificial Intelligence through Innovation, Governance, and⏎Public Trust (M-25-21). 2025.⏎2 U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. HHS Artificial Intelligence Strategy. 2025.⏎⏎Executive Summary:⏎• Federal funding for AI adoption across regulation, reimbursement, and research; without⏎sustained investment, policy reforms alone will not achieve scalable or equitable⏎implementation.⏎• Absent sustained federal investment in data infrastructure, implementation science, and⏎workforce capacity, accelerated AI adoption risks producing fragmented, opaque, and⏎inequitable deployment, increasing safety risks and undermining public trust rather than⏎improving care.⏎• Ambiguities in HIPAA, information blocking, and clinical decision support governance⏎disproportionately hinder the development of continuous-learning AI systems and smaller⏎or safety-net providers.⏎• Current reimbursement models fail to recognize AI as an enabler of clinical⏎infrastructure, particularly disadvantaging prevention-, nutrition-, and population health–⏎focused AI tools.⏎• National data assets such as NHANES and the NIH All of Us Research Program function⏎as essential AI infrastructure and require sustained, expanded funding to support model⏎validity, equity, and public trust.3,4⏎• Federal investment in applied implementation science and workforce capacity is⏎necessary to translate AI innovation into real-world clinical impact.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎1.1 Funding as the Foundational Constraint⏎Across regulation, reimbursement, and research, federal policy increasingly encourages rapid AI⏎adoption. In contrast, federal investment in the underlying public goods required for AI success,⏎longitudinal datasets, representative population surveillance, implementation science, and⏎workforce capacity, has not kept pace with AI's growth. This creates a structural paradox: AI⏎tools are expected to improve productivity, reduce burden, and enhance outcomes, yet the⏎foundational infrastructure required to validate, monitor, and sustain those tools remains fragile⏎or under-resourced.⏎As a result, regulatory uncertainty, reimbursement inertia, and uneven adoption are not solely⏎policy design problems; they are predictable consequences of underinvestment in health care.⏎Addressing this funding gap should be viewed as a prerequisite to achieving HHS’s stated goals⏎of public trust, safety, innovation, and health system transformation.⏎3 National Institutes of Health. All of Us Research Program Strategic Vision.⏎4 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES): Program⏎Overview.⏎2⏎⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations.⏎2.1 Regulatory Barriers Affecting AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎2.1.1 HIPAA Privacy Rule and Secondary Use of Data (45 CFR Parts 160 and 164),⏎The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) Privacy Rule presents⏎unresolved challenges for AI systems that require ongoing model training, validation, and⏎updating. Ambiguity remains regarding whether secondary data use for model retraining⏎constitutes health care operations, research, or a hybrid activity requiring additional authorization⏎or institutional review board oversight. This uncertainty disproportionately affects smaller health⏎systems and safety-net providers, which lack the necessary legal and compliance resources to⏎interpret evolving expectations.⏎These challenges reflect a deeper mismatch between existing governance frameworks and the⏎nature of AI-based systems. Unlike traditional statistical analyses, which are hypothesis-driven⏎and executed once at study completion, AI systems require iterative development, repeated⏎evaluation, and ongoing performance monitoring. Treating AI as a static analytic output rather⏎than a continuously evolving system creates regulatory ambiguity and discourages responsible⏎model updating, even when such updating is necessary to maintain accuracy and mitigate bias.⏎Without clear guidance, health care organizations may limit or avoid continuous-learning AI⏎models, even when such models are essential to maintaining accuracy, mitigating bias, and⏎ensuring clinical safety. HHS should consider issuing guidance or establishing safe harbors⏎clarifying permissible AI-related secondary data use under 45 CFR §164.506, particularly for de-⏎identified, federated, or privacy-preserving learning approaches. 5⏎2.1.2 Information Blocking and AI-Generated Outputs (45 CFR Part 171)⏎The information blocking regulations raise unresolved questions about whether AI-generated⏎outputs, such as predictive risk scores, decision-support insights, or model-derived features,⏎constitute electronic health information subject to access, exchange, and use requirements.7⏎Uncertainty regarding enforcement expectations discourages deeper integration of AI into⏎electronic health record (EHR) workflows and creates hesitation among developers and health⏎systems.⏎HHS, through ONC, should clarify how AI-generated artifacts are treated under existing⏎information-blocking rules and consider how future United States Core Data for Interoperability⏎(USCDI) expansions may incorporate AI-relevant data elements without stifling innovation or⏎exposing clinicians and organizations to unintended compliance risks.⏎5 45 CFR 164.506 -- Uses and disclosures to carry out treatment, payment, or health care operations.⏎https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-C/part-164/subpart-E/section-164.506⏎3⏎⏎2.1.3 Governance, Liability, and Accountability for Clinical Decision Support⏎For non-medical device AI tools used in clinical decision support, there is no harmonized federal⏎framework addressing liability, documentation standards, clinician reliance thresholds, or audit⏎expectations. This governance gap contributes to inconsistent adoption and undermines clinician⏎trust.⏎HHS could play a constructive role by supporting model governance frameworks, safe-harbor⏎pilots, and consensus standards that clarify accountability while preserving clinician judgment⏎and autonomy. Importantly, these efforts require dedicated funding to support real-world testing,⏎evaluation, and refinement.⏎2.1.4 Interoperability and Data Management Constraints⏎Interoperability limitations and fragmented data governance represent material barriers to the⏎safe and effective deployment of AI in clinical care. AI systems depend on access to⏎longitudinal, high-quality, and standardized data across care settings, yet health data remain⏎siloed across EHR vendors, public health systems, payers, and federal programs. Inconsistent⏎data models, limited adoption of standardized terminologies, and variable data quality constrain⏎model performance and generalizability, particularly for nutrition, prevention, and population⏎health applications.⏎While ONC has advanced interoperability through the United States Core Data for⏎Interoperability (USCDI), many data elements critical to AI, such as nutrition intake, social⏎determinants of health, functional status, and longitudinal outcomes, remain inconsistently⏎captured or poorly standardized.6 HHS should prioritize sustained investment in data⏎harmonization, standards development, and infrastructure that enable AI tools to operate across⏎systems without the need for bespoke integration efforts. Absent such investment,⏎interoperability requirements risk becoming unfunded mandates that further disadvantage⏎smaller and resource-constrained providers.⏎Without dedicated investment in applied implementation science, AI systems are likely to be⏎deployed unevenly across care settings, validated inadequately on local populations, and⏎monitored inconsistently after deployment. These implementation failures represent safety risks,⏎not merely inefficiencies, as models that perform well in development environments may⏎degrade or behave unpredictably in real-world clinical contexts.⏎2.1.5 Transparency, Explainability, and the “Black Box” Challenge⏎Lack of transparency in AI model development and operation, often referred to as the “black⏎box” problem, poses challenges for clinician trust, patient acceptance, and effective regulatory⏎oversight. Clinicians are expected to integrate AI-generated insights into clinical decision-⏎making; however, they frequently lack access to information regarding model training data,⏎6⏎United States Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI). Interoperability Standards Platform (ISP). Published September 30,⏎2025. https://isp.healthit.gov/united-states-core-data-interoperability-uscdi⏎4⏎⏎performance characteristics across populations, known limitations, or mechanisms for error⏎detection and mitigation.⏎HHS should consider policy approaches that promote proportional transparency for AI used in⏎clinical care, including expectations for disclosing the model's purpose, data provenance,⏎validation methods, and performance monitoring, without requiring disclosure of proprietary⏎source code. Clear federal expectations regarding explainability and documentation would⏎reduce uncertainty for developers and users alike, support informed clinical use, and mitigate⏎risk without stifling innovation.⏎These transparency requirements must be paired with federal investment to support evaluation⏎infrastructure, post-deployment monitoring, and clinician education. Without funding to⏎operationalize transparency, disclosure requirements alone are unlikely to significantly improve⏎safety or trust.⏎2.2 Reimbursement Barriers to AI Adoption⏎2.2.1 AI as Enabling Infrastructure Rather Than a Billable Service⏎Current reimbursement systems are primarily designed to compensate for discrete services and⏎human labor, rather than supporting infrastructure that enhances care delivery across episodes,⏎settings, or populations. Many AI tools function as clinical infrastructure, supporting prevention,⏎care coordination, risk stratification, and quality improvement, yet they often lack reimbursement⏎pathways under fee-for-service and even many value-based models.⏎Absent explicit recognition of AI as a covered enabling technology, health systems face⏎difficulty justifying investment, particularly when financial returns accrue over long time⏎horizons or outside traditional billing constructs. CMS should explore demonstration models and⏎payment reforms that treat AI-enabled clinical infrastructure as a component of high-value care,⏎both allowable and incentivized.⏎2.2.2 Structural Disincentives for Prevention, Nutrition, and Population Health AI⏎AI reimbursement pilots and payment reforms have disproportionately focused on imaging,⏎diagnostics, and acute care applications. In contrast, AI tools supporting prevention, nutrition,⏎social determinants of health, and population health management remain structurally⏎disadvantaged, despite their potential to reduce long-term costs and improve outcomes.⏎This imbalance is particularly problematic for emerging fields, such as precision nutrition and⏎personalized preventive care, where AI-enabled insights rely on longitudinal data and early⏎intervention. CMS should explicitly include AI-driven nutrition risk identification, intervention⏎targeting, and monitoring within innovation models, chronic care management pathways, and⏎value-based arrangements.7⏎7 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Innovation Center Models and Value-Based Care Initiatives.⏎5⏎⏎As a result, AI tools that support early nutrition risk identification, longitudinal dietary⏎monitoring, or population-level prevention are often not deployed, even when evidence suggests⏎long-term cost savings and improved outcomes, because financial returns are misaligned with⏎short-term reimbursement structures.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care? a. Are there published findings about the impact of⏎adopted AI tools and their use in clinical care? b. How does the literature approach the⏎costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎10.1 Research and Development Investment Priorities and Concerns of Programs at Risk of⏎Defunding with Direct Impact on AI and Nutrition⏎Several federal programs critical to AI readiness, nutrition science, and population health are⏎vulnerable to funding instability. Reductions in these programs would directly impair the⏎development, validation, and equitable deployment of AI tools in clinical and community⏎settings. These include: the CDC’s Nutrition, Physical Activity, and Obesity (NPAO) Program,⏎which supports population-level nutrition surveillance and intervention evaluation; the Agency⏎for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ), which plays a central role in implementation⏎science and real-world evaluation of health technologies; USDA Food and Nutrition Service⏎(FNS) data systems, including SNAP and WIC, which generate essential data for AI applications⏎addressing food insecurity and diet quality; and the CMS Innovation Center (CMMI), which⏎provides a primary mechanism for testing AI-enabled payment and care delivery models.8,9,10,11⏎10.1.1 NHANES as National AI Infrastructure⏎The National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey (NHANES) is not merely a public health⏎surveillance tool; it is a foundational national infrastructure that can be utilized for training,⏎validating, and auditing AI models related to nutrition, metabolic health, chronic disease, and⏎health disparities.12 Funding instability or reductions in NHANES directly undermine this data⏎collection infrastructure for the purposes of publicly available information for AI⏎generalizability, bias detection, and equity.⏎HHS should ensure sustained and expanded funding for NHANES, including modernization⏎efforts that support higher-frequency data collection, digital phenotyping, and linkage with EHR⏎and claims data to enhance AI readiness.⏎8 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. Nutrition, Physical Activity, and Obesity Program Overview.⏎9 Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality. Health Services Research and Implementation Science Portfolio.⏎10 U.S. Department of Agriculture, Food and Nutrition Service. SNAP and WIC Data and Research Initiatives.⏎11 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation: Strategy and Model Portfolio.⏎12 National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey. National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey. Published December⏎18, 2024. https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/nhanes/index.html⏎6⏎⏎10.1.2 Expansion and Domain Deepening of the All of Us Research Program⏎The NIH All of Us Research Program represents a critical national investment in precision⏎medicine. The All of Us Research Program represents a new paradigm of big data sharing in⏎healthcare that supersedes individual small investigator run RCTs. However, its potential to⏎support AI-driven clinical care will remain limited without expanded funding for cohort⏎retention, longitudinal follow-up, and domain-specific data collection, particularly in nutrition,⏎lifestyle exposures, and food systems.⏎Targeted expansion of All of Us to support precision nutrition, metabolomics, and AI-enabled⏎lifestyle modeling would significantly strengthen the evidence base for equitable AI deployment⏎in clinical care. In addition, funding improvements to the cloud environment in the All of Us⏎Researcher Workbench infrastructure will reduce technical barriers to data queries and AI-⏎prediction of non-communicable disease states within this incredible data source.⏎10.1.3 AI Readiness Requires Labor⏎Importantly, AI-readiness is not a default property of datasets. Transforming raw population data⏎into AI-ready inputs requires substantial investment in data engineering, preprocessing,⏎harmonization, and quality control. These activities require skilled personnel and sustained⏎funding yet are often invisible in traditional research budgets and reimbursement models.⏎Underinvestment in this labor directly compromises model validity, generalizability, and equity.⏎10.1.4 Applied Implementation Science for AI⏎Federal AI research funding has heavily emphasized algorithm development and proof-of-⏎concept studies, while underinvesting in implementation science, workflow integration, clinician⏎training, and patient trust.13,14 Without funding to understand how AI performs in real-world care⏎settings, many promising tools remain confined to academic or pilot environments.⏎HHS should prioritize grants, cooperative agreements, and public-private partnerships focused⏎on the applied implementation science of translating AI from concept to sustained clinical use.⏎E. Federal Workforce Capacity⏎Finally, federal agencies themselves require sustained funding to recruit, train, and retain AI-⏎literate staff capable of evaluating technologies, overseeing post-market performance, and⏎engaging meaningfully with the private sector.⏎Federal oversight of AI in clinical care depends on internal workforce capacity with sufficient AI⏎literacy and domain expertise. Agencies require staff capable of evaluating model development⏎13 Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality. Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Implementation and Evaluation⏎Considerations.⏎14 U.S. Food and Drug Administration. Measuring and Evaluating AI-Enabled Medical Device Performance in the Real World.⏎Digital Health Center of Excellence.⏎7⏎⏎practices, assessing post-deployment performance, interpreting probabilistic outputs, and⏎engaging with developers on issues of bias, drift, and validation. Without sustained investment in⏎federal workforce development, regulatory frameworks risk becoming procedural rather than⏎substantive, limiting their ability to ensure safety, equity, and accountability.⏎F. Public–Private and Public–Academic Partnerships to Build AI Infrastructure Capacity⏎Despite growing interest in accelerating the adoption of artificial intelligence in clinical care, the⏎availability of personnel with deep technical expertise in data engineering, data science, and AI⏎systems remains a binding constraint. Individuals with the skills required to design, maintain,⏎and monitor AI-ready data infrastructure are in limited supply within academic and public-sector⏎settings, and private industry is able to offer substantially higher compensation and career⏎pathways that federal agencies and universities cannot realistically match. As a result, critical⏎functions such as data preprocessing, pipeline development, system integration, and post-⏎deployment monitoring are often under-resourced or unevenly supported in publicly funded⏎research and clinical environments. This workforce imbalance constrains the federal⏎government’s capacity to steward AI responsibly, not because of a lack of interest or policy⏎intent, but because the underlying technical labor required to operationalize AI at scale is⏎structurally misaligned with existing funding and staffing models.⏎Sustained federal investment in AI adoption for clinical care will require workforce and⏎infrastructure strategies that are both scalable and fiscally responsible. In particular, the limited⏎supply and high cost of experienced data engineers, data infrastructure specialists, and AI⏎systems engineers pose a practical constraint on the federal government’s ability to build and⏎maintain AI-ready data ecosystems through direct hiring alone.⏎To complement and strengthen permanent federal staffing models, HHS should also prioritize⏎creating structured public–private and public–academic partnership approaches that enable⏎shared investment in AI-relevant public goods while preserving public oversight and⏎accountability. These partnership models can expand federal capacity without requiring the⏎government to compete directly with private-sector salaries.⏎Specifically, HHS should consider the following partnership mechanisms:⏎• Cost-shared personnel models, in which federal funds support partial effort for data⏎engineers and infrastructure specialists embedded in academic or industry settings to⏎develop and maintain AI-ready public infrastructure.⏎• Term-limited fellowship and rotation programs that allow experienced technical⏎professionals to contribute to federally supported AI infrastructure and governance efforts⏎for defined periods, while maintaining career continuity outside permanent federal⏎service.⏎• Jointly governed infrastructure projects focused on shared, reusable assets such as data⏎preprocessing pipelines, harmonization frameworks, quality control systems, and post-⏎deployment monitoring tools that support safe and equitable AI deployment across care⏎settings.⏎8⏎⏎• These partnership-based models are particularly well suited to the functions that are most⏎essential for AI readiness but often underfunded, including data engineering, data⏎curation, system integration, and ongoing performance monitoring.⏎In addition to direct funding, non-monetary incentives can further strengthen participation in⏎these partnerships, including:⏎• Access to unique federal data assets, such as nationally representative population datasets⏎and longitudinal health data, made available under clear governance, transparency, and⏎conflict-of-interest safeguards.⏎• Structured pilot and evaluation pathways that allow AI tools developed using public⏎infrastructure to be tested, audited, and refined in real-world clinical environments prior⏎to broader adoption.⏎• Recognition and leadership opportunities for participating partners through named⏎programs, public attribution, and involvement in standards-setting and governance⏎activities.⏎• By investing in partnership-based capacity-building models, HHS can extend the impact⏎of federal funding, accelerate AI readiness across the health system, and strengthen⏎national stewardship of AI-enabled clinical care without expanding permanent federal⏎headcount. These approaches complement, rather than replace, direct federal investment⏎in workforce development and are essential to building durable, trustworthy AI⏎infrastructure at scale.⏎V. Summary of Recommendations⏎HHS should prioritize the following actions to accelerate responsible AI adoption in clinical⏎care:⏎1. Establish sustained federal funding streams that support AI-relevant public goods,⏎including longitudinal population datasets, implementation science, data engineering, and⏎workforce development, recognizing these as prerequisites for safe and equitable AI⏎adoption.⏎2. Issue regulatory guidance clarifying the permissible use of secondary data for AI model⏎training and updating under HIPAA (45 CFR Parts 160 and 164).⏎3. Clarify the treatment of AI-generated outputs under information blocking regulations (45⏎CFR Part 171) and future USCDI expansions.⏎4. Modernize CMS payment models to recognize AI as an enabler of clinical infrastructure,⏎with explicit inclusion of prevention-, nutrition-, and population health-focused AI tools.⏎5. Protect and expand funding for NHANES and the NIH All of Us Research Program as⏎core national infrastructure for AI.⏎6. Increase federal investment in the implementation science of applied AI, real-world⏎evaluation, and clinician and patient engagement.⏎9⏎⏎7. Strengthen federal agency workforce capacity to oversee, evaluate, and steward AI⏎deployment across the health sector.⏎8. Optimize public and private partnership pipelines and knowledge sharing opportunities⏎for maximum data sharing, precision and broadening technological innovation.⏎V. Conclusion⏎HHS has an opportunity to shape a health sector AI ecosystem that is innovative, trustworthy,⏎and equitable. Achieving this vision will require aligning regulatory clarity and payment reform⏎with sustained federal investment in data infrastructure, research, implementation capacity, and⏎workforce development. Without such investment, AI policy risks remaining aspirational rather⏎than operational.⏎Thank you for considering these comments. We appreciate HHS’s leadership in advancing the⏎responsible adoption of AI and look forward to continued engagement on these critical issues.⏎Please do not hesitate to contact Kelly Horton, the Academy’s Senior Vice President of Public⏎Policy and Government Relations at khorton@eatright.org with any questions or requests for⏎additional information.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Diana Thomas, PhD Keiy Murofushi, MS, RDN, LD⏎Co-Chair AI Task Force Co-Chair AI Task Force⏎10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0241♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Echo IQ, Inc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Echo IQ, Inc♔♔♔♔♔♔February 18, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI – Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of the HHS Health Sector⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎Echo IQ appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department of Health and Human Services’⏎Request for Information titled “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎the HHS Health Sector.” We commend HHS for advancing a coordinated “OneHHS” strategy to⏎harness the transformative potential of artificial intelligence and responsibly embed it into⏎healthcare delivery, operations, and policy.⏎Echo IQ develops EchoSolv™, an FDA-cleared artificial intelligence-enabled clinical decision-⏎support platform designed to augment echocardiography and assist clinicians in identifying⏎patients at risk for significant cardiovascular disease, including heart failure and structural heart⏎disease. EchoSolv does not replace physician interpretation; rather, it strengthens and standardizes⏎it. By providing augmentative clinical decision support at the point of care, EchoSolv empowers⏎physicians to make more informed decisions, reduces diagnostic variability, and supports earlier,⏎more precise identification of disease. This approach aligns directly with HHS’s priority of⏎harnessing AI to empower — not replace — the healthcare workforce.⏎Clinical validation data demonstrate that AI-assisted echocardiographic analysis can improve⏎identification of patients with clinically significant disease compared to standard interpretation⏎alone. Earlier detection enables more timely intervention, improves longitudinal care management,⏎and may reduce avoidable hospitalizations and downstream costs. Because echocardiography is a⏎foundational cardiovascular diagnostic modality, AI augmentation at the modality level has the⏎potential to improve detection and management across multiple cardiovascular conditions over⏎time.⏎3828 Kennett Pike | Suite 212 | Greenville, DE 19807⏎www.echoiq.ai⏎⏎As HHS evaluates pathways to accelerate safe and effective AI adoption, Echo IQ respectfully⏎emphasizes that scalable deployment will depend on modernized reimbursement frameworks that⏎recognize AI-enabled diagnostic augmentation at the modality level — not solely at the individual⏎disease level. A reimbursement structure limited to a single indication may require repeated coding⏎and valuation efforts as new validated outputs are added, potentially slowing innovation and⏎adoption. A modality-based framework would better align with CMS goals of encouraging high-⏎value technology adoption while reducing administrative friction.⏎We also note that the American Medical Association’s CPT process is currently grappling with how⏎to categorize and value emerging AI technologies, including distinctions among assistive,⏎augmentative, and autonomous tools. To date, consensus has been difficult to achieve, and coding⏎pathways for certain AI-enabled services remain uncertain.⏎While these issues are complex, prolonged lack of clarity within the AMA CPT framework may delay⏎adoption of clinically validated AI technologies. Guidance and coordination from HHS and CMS⏎could help ensure that coding and payment structures evolve in step with federal goals to⏎responsibly accelerate AI adoption in healthcare.⏎Echo IQ believes this is an opportunity for HHS to demonstrate leadership in aligning regulatory⏎authorization, workforce empowerment, and payment modernization in a coordinated manner.⏎Clear policy signals supporting appropriate valuation of augmentative AI diagnostic technologies⏎would encourage responsible adoption, improve patient access, and strengthen the United States’⏎leadership in healthcare innovation.⏎Echo IQ stands ready to serve as a constructive partner to HHS as the Department advances its AI⏎strategy. We welcome the opportunity to meet with HHS leadership and staff to further discuss how⏎reimbursement modernization, modality-based valuation approaches, and coordinated federal⏎engagement can help accelerate responsible adoption of AI technologies that measurably improve⏎patient outcomes.⏎Thank you for your leadership and for considering our perspective.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Dustin Haines⏎CEO, Echo IQ⏎3828 Kennett Pike | Suite 212 | Greenville, DE 19807⏎www.echoiq.ai♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0242♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-27T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔White♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly I'm not even sure this is the right place to put this but I followed a link someone shared on Facebook and it said the government was asking for input on computers being used to make medical decisions, so here I am.⏎⏎My name is Deborah White and I'm a home health aide outside of Waco Texas. I've been doing this work for going on 19 years.⏎⏎The reason I wanted to say something is because of what happened to my patient Evelyn, who is 82. Back in February her clinic apparently ran her information through some kind of computer program after she had a fall, and the system marked her as low priority for a follow-up. I don't know the details of how that works, I'm not a doctor or a tech person. But what I do know is that her daughter had to make phone calls for almost two weeks before anyone actually examined Evelyn properly, and when they finally did they found she had a hairline fracture that the computer had not flagged. She was walking on it. For two weeks.⏎⏎I am in that house five days a week. I know when Evelyn is not moving right, I know when she's holding herself different than usual. I said something seemed off. Nobody asked me.⏎⏎So I guess what I want to say is, whatever this rule is trying to do, I hope someone is thinking about what happens when these programs get it wrong and there's nobody whose job it is to catch the mistake. Because from where I sit the computer doesn't know Evelyn. It knows whatever numbers are in her file. And those are not the same thing.⏎⏎I don't know what an RFI is, I'll be honest. I'm hoping this message gets to somebody who does. If there's a more official way I'm supposed to submit this please let me know, I couldn't find a phone number on the page I was directed to.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0243♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Lilly, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lilly♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Submitted electronically via:regulations.gov⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Docket: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Eli Lilly and Company (Lilly) appreciates the opportunity to inform HHS’s approach to accelerating AI⏎adoption in clinical care. As a leading biopharmaceutical innovator with extensive AI deployment across⏎drug discovery, clinical trials, manufacturing quality, and patient support, Lilly offers practical insights on⏎regulation, reimbursement, and research & development policy.⏎Lilly’s AI Leadership⏎Lilly is leveraging AI throughout the drug lifecycle through strategic partnerships with NVIDIA (AI-⏎accelerated drug discovery infrastructure),1 Isomorphic Labs (drug discovery), OpenAI (antimicrobial⏎discovery), BigHat Biosciences (antibody design), and Thrive Global (patient behavior change). Internally,⏎we have deployed AI for manufacturing quality, clinical trial optimization, and post-market surveillance. This⏎operational experience informs our recommendations for accelerating AI adoption while safeguarding public⏎health, privacy, and trust.⏎Three Pillars for AI Acceleration⏎Pillar 1: Regulatory Clarity Enables Innovation⏎Lilly supports risk-proportionate, adaptive regulatory frameworks that provide certainty while maintaining⏎rigorous safety and efficacy standards. The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) should also leverage AI⏎internally to streamline review processes while maintaining transparent governance and protecting⏎confidential information.⏎Pillar 2: Reimbursement Modernization Is Critical⏎Legacy fee-for-service (FFS) payment systems represent the single largest barrier to AI adoption in clinical⏎care. The fundamental problem: FFS systems are structurally misaligned with AI’s value proposition. AI tools⏎deliver value through preventive interventions that avoid future costs, efficiency gains that reduce⏎unnecessary procedures, and quality improvements through earlier detection, none of which generate⏎billable services under FFS economics, which reimburses based on volume rather than outcomes. 2 Without⏎payment reform, AI innovations may remain throttled by reimbursement inertia regardless of clinical value⏎or regulatory approval.⏎Pillar 3: Strategic Research & Development (R&D) Investment Accelerates Translation⏎Public-private partnerships and targeted research funding can bridge the gap between AI development and⏎real-world clinical implementation.⏎1 See Lilly and NVIDIA Announce Partnership to Accelerate AI-Driven Drug Discovery (Nov. 2023), available at https://investor.lilly.com/news-⏎releases/news-release-details/lilly-and-nvidia-announce-partnership-accelerate-ai-driven-drug⏎2 HHS Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care, 90 Fed. Reg. 60108, 60109 (Dec.⏎23, 2025) (acknowledging that AI risks being "throttled by payment inertia, not science or regulation").⏎⏎HHS AI RFI Lilly Comments⏎Page 2⏎Priority Recommendations⏎I. REGULATORY CLARITY⏎1. Expand Adaptive Clinical Trial Frameworks⏎Current Barrier: Traditional clinical trial regulations (e.g., 21 CFR Parts 312, 314) assume static protocols,⏎fixed endpoints, and predetermined sample sizes, creating friction for AI-enabled adaptive designs that⏎optimize recruitment in real time.⏎FDA should clarify acceptable approaches to AI-driven adaptive randomization, dynamic endpoint⏎modification, and continuous learning algorithms, including AI-driven dosing recommendations such as⏎precise timing of next dose administration. This clarity would accelerate precision prevention therapies⏎requiring large, long-duration studies.⏎2. Modernize HIPAA De-identification Standards for AI Era⏎Current Barrier: The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) de-identification⏎framework under 45 CFR §164.514 is structurally incompatible with modern data operations. The safe⏎harbor’s 18-identifier checklist neither accounts for AI-enabled re-identification risks nor provides flexibility⏎for privacy-preserving techniques that may offer stronger protections. More critically, the expert⏎determination pathway requires individualized expert certification for every dataset configuration, which is⏎an operational impossibility for organizations running continuous, enterprise-scale tokenization and⏎pseudonymization pipelines.⏎HHS should modernize §164.514 in three targeted ways: (1) establish a formal pseudonymization tier that⏎provides regulatory recognition for tokenized data held under controlled re-linkage architectures, rather⏎than treating it identically to unprotected personal health information (PHI); (2) replace per-instance expert⏎determinations with a certification framework developed by the National Institute of Standards and⏎Technology (NIST) for approved privacy-preserving methodologies (including differential privacy, federated⏎learning, and synthetic data generation) that satisfy the “very small risk” standard by design; and (3) issue⏎affirmative guidance on permissible use of health data for AI model training, closing the significant⏎interpretive gap that currently drives inconsistent compliance practices and impedes beneficial AI⏎development. These changes would reduce compliance friction while providing more consistent and⏎technically grounded privacy protections than the existing framework achieves.⏎3. Establish Platform Technology Designations for AI-Enabled Therapeutics⏎Current Barrier: AI platforms that generate multiple therapeutic candidates must undergo separate,⏎redundant regulatory review for each candidate, despite using the same validated underlying technology.⏎FDA should expand its Platform Technology Designation program to AI-enabled drug discovery platforms,⏎allowing one-time validation of core AI methodology with streamlined review for subsequent therapeutics⏎generated using the validated platform. This accelerates development of AI-discovered therapeutics,⏎particularly for rare diseases and areas of high unmet need.⏎4. Clarify Governance for Foundation Models and Third-Party AI⏎Current Barrier: Many pharmaceutical AI applications leverage foundation models (e.g., AlphaFold or large⏎language models) developed by third parties, but current guidance doesn’t address validation requirements⏎when sponsors lack full access to training data or architecture. 3⏎3 PhRMA, Use of AI in the Drug Lifecycle and Regulatory Activities at 3 (2025) ("Many AI tools, including foundation models, used in drug⏎development are created by third parties, which can present challenges and opportunities.").⏎2⏎⏎HHS AI RFI Lilly Comments⏎Page 3⏎FDA should issue guidance addressing third-party AI tools and foundation models (like those Lilly uses in⏎partnership with OpenAI and Isomorphic Labs), establishing tiered validation approaches and model⏎registries to enable efficient oversight without requiring full architectural transparency from technology⏎partners. This will make it easier for companies developing innovative medicines to partner with companies⏎developing innovative AI models, with each leveraging their core strengths to benefit patients. For example,⏎Lilly’s collaboration with OpenAI uses foundation models to discover novel antimicrobials; these models⏎were trained on datasets outside our control, but performance-based validation would enable oversight⏎without requiring Lilly to develop foundation models itself. 4⏎5. Provide Additional Examples of Credibility Assessment Framework Application⏎Current Barrier: FDA’s credibility assessment framework provides a strong foundation, but sponsors need⏎additional examples showing application to diverse use cases to ensure consistent implementation.⏎FDA’s draft guidance on AI to support regulatory decision-making provides a strong foundation. Additional⏎examples showing application to diverse use cases (real-world evidence, exploratory endpoints, combination⏎products, biologics manufacturing) would accelerate industry adoption and regulatory consistency. This⏎reduces regulatory uncertainty and accelerates market entry for validated tools.⏎II. REIMBURSEMENT MODERNIZATION⏎6. Create Medicare Coverage Pathways for AI-Enabled Preventive Diagnostics⏎Current Barrier: Medicare’s statutory framework (42 USC §1395y(a)(1)(A)) has been interpreted to⏎exclude coverage for “screening tests,” creating a categorical barrier to AI-enabled diagnostics that identify⏎disease risk before symptoms manifest. 5⏎The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) should clarify its coverage authority to distinguish AI-⏎enabled diagnostic procedures (targeted testing based on validated biomarkers, genetic risk, or AI-predicted⏎risk scores) from traditional screening tests (population-wide testing without risk stratification). Example:⏎AI-powered digital cognitive assessments can detect early cognitive decline and enable care planning yet⏎lack clear Medicare coverage pathways because they may be classified as screening rather than diagnostic⏎tools. 6 This reform would enable earlier intervention rather than reactive late-stage treatment.⏎7. Expand Voluntary Value-Based Payment Models for AI Clinical Decision Support⏎Current Barrier: Healthcare systems have no direct financial incentive to adopt AI clinical decision support⏎tools under FFS payment; efficiency gains from AI may actually reduce revenue (fewer hospital days, fewer⏎procedures). While CMS’s WISeR model tests AI-enabled prior authorization to reduce waste, no parallel⏎incentives exist for AI tools that proactively improve clinical outcomes. 7⏎4 See Lilly, Lilly collaborates with OpenAI to discover novel medicines to treat drug-resistant bacteria (Apr. 2024), available at⏎https://investor.lilly.com/news-releases/news-release-details/lilly-collaborates-openai-discover-novel-medicines-treat-drug.⏎5 42 U.S.C. § 1395y(a)(1)(A). CMS has historically interpreted this provision to exclude coverage for screening tests in asymptomatic individuals⏎unless Congress has specifically authorized such screening, as it has for mammography (42 U.S.C. § 1395x(jj)), colorectal cancer screening (42 U.S.C. §⏎1395x(pp)), and certain other preventive services.⏎6 Digital cognitive assessments (DCAs) leverage AI to standardize cognitive testing, streamline administration and interpretation, and enable better⏎disease tracking and care planning. Medicare requires cognitive assessments during Annual Wellness Visits,yet validated digital tools face⏎reimbursement uncertainty despite their potential to improve detection of cognitive decline, particularly as primary care providers increasingly⏎perform assessments beyond specialist settings. See Coalition to Evaluate Older Adults Innovations (CEOi), Digital Cognitive Assessments, available at⏎https://alzdiagnostichub.org/digital-cognitive-assessments/; see also Linus Health (example of AI-powered DCA platform for early cognitive⏎impairment detection).⏎7 See CMS, Wasteful and Inappropriate Service Reduction (WISeR) Model (Dec. 2025), available at⏎https://www.cms.gov/priorities/innovation/innovation-models/wiser (testing AI-enabled prior authorization to reduce inappropriate utilization);⏎see also Lilly, Aspen Ideas: Health—Imagine a World Without Disease, Fireside Chat Talking Points at 7-8 (June 2025) (advocating for CMS to use⏎Medicare Advantage risk-adjustment factors and MIPS to incentivize preventive interventions).⏎3⏎⏎HHS AI RFI Lilly Comments⏎Page 4⏎CMS should create complementary alternative payment models (via 42 CFR Part 425, §414.1380) that⏎reward healthcare systems for adopting validated AI tools that demonstrably improve outcomes or reduce⏎total cost of care. Medicare Advantage risk-adjustment bonuses, Merit-based Incentive Payment System⏎(MIPS) quality points, and shared savings programs should explicitly incentivize evidence-based AI adoption⏎that enhances care quality, not just reduces utilization. Balanced incentives would be created for AI adoption⏎focused on both waste reduction and outcome improvement.⏎8. Extend Medicare Eligibility for Conditions Requiring Early Intervention⏎Current Barrier: Medicare eligibility begins at age 65 (with exceptions for end-stage renal disease and⏎amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS)), creating barriers to early intervention for chronic diseases with long⏎preclinical phases. The recently introduced BRIDGE Act addresses Alzheimer’s disease by eliminating⏎waiting periods for symptomatic young-onset patients, but broader payment system reforms are needed as⏎AI technologies increasingly enable identification of high-risk asymptomatic individuals requiring preventive⏎intervention decades before age 65. 8⏎Congress should pass the BRIDGE Act and use it as a model for condition-specific Medicare eligibility⏎expansions as scientific evidence supports early intervention. As AI-powered precision medicine advances,⏎HHS should establish frameworks ensuring payment systems can accommodate validated preventive⏎therapies for high-risk populations identified through genetic testing, biomarker analysis, and AI-enabled⏎risk prediction, enabling intervention during optimal treatment windows regardless of age-based eligibility⏎thresholds—an approach that improves patient outcomes and reduces long-term program costs.⏎9. Prevent Misuse of AI in Coverage Determination⏎Current Barrier: Growing concern that payers may deploy AI tools to automate coverage denials or⏎accelerate prior authorization rejections without adequate clinical oversight or transparency. 9⏎HHS should establish guardrails for payer use of AI in coverage decisions, including transparency⏎requirements (disclosure of AI system purpose, data sources, decision criteria), clinical oversight mandates⏎(meaningful physician review, with AI authorized to approve coverage but all denials requiring human⏎clinician determination), timely appeals processes, and bias auditing. AI should improve care delivery, not⏎automate barriers to access through coverage denial. 10 Public trust is built by preventing algorithmic⏎barriers to care while allowing AI to reduce administrative burden for routine approvals.⏎III. RESEARCH & DEVELOPMENT INVESTMENT⏎10. Accelerate Surrogate Endpoint Validation for Preventive Therapies⏎Current Barrier: Preventive therapies often require large, long-duration trials (5-10 years with thousands⏎of participants), making development timelines incompatible with rapid AI innovation cycles. 11⏎The National Institutes of Health (NIH) and FDA should fund research developing and validating AI-⏎discovered biomarkers and digital phenotypes that can serve as surrogate endpoints for preventive⏎therapies. Apply the oncology paradigm (where 70% of cancer drug approvals use surrogate endpoints like⏎8 See Bridging Relief in Delayed Government Enrollment for Young-Onset Alzheimer's Disease Act of 2025 (BRIDGE Act), 119th Cong. (2025)⏎(proposing to eliminate disability insurance and Medicare waiting periods for individuals with young-onset Alzheimer's, following the model of⏎existing exceptions for amyotrophic lateral sclerosis). Lilly supports this important legislation. More broadly, as AI technologies enable increasingly⏎sophisticated risk stratification and earlier disease detection across multiple therapeutic areas, payment systems must evolve to enable coverage for⏎validated preventive interventions when scientific evidence supports early treatment of high-risk asymptomatic populations.⏎9 PhRMA, Artificial Intelligence Policy Priorities at 1 (Aug. 2025) ("AI-enabled solutions should be designed to help patients, providers, and⏎purchasers make informed decisions about the tools themselves and their health care. Such AI tools should be used to improve patient outcomes—⏎not to automate or accelerate barriers to care through coverage denial nor reinforce flawed utilization management practices.").⏎10 IBID⏎11 See Lilly, Aspen Ideas: Health—Imagine a World Without Disease, Fireside Chat (June 2025) (explaining that clinical trials for primary and⏎secondary prevention "often require more people and more time").⏎4⏎⏎HHS AI RFI Lilly Comments⏎Page 5⏎response rate or progression-free survival, reducing approval timelines by 1-1.5 years) to other disease⏎areas including neurodegenerative diseases, cardiovascular disease, and metabolic disorders. 12 This would⏎dramatically reduce time and cost to validate preventive therapies, making development economically⏎viable.⏎11. Invest in Bias Detection and Mitigation Research⏎Current Barrier: AI algorithms trained on historical data may perpetuate or amplify existing health⏎disparities; detecting and mitigating biases require specialized research expertise.⏎NIH should establish a dedicated research program focused on fairness metrics for clinical AI, algorithmic⏎auditing methods, bias mitigation techniques, and prospective studies measuring real-world impact of AI⏎tools on health disparities. AI algorithms used in drug development and clinical care must be free from bias⏎and avoid exacerbating health inequities. For example, in Alzheimer’s disease, which disproportionately⏎impacts women and certain minority populations, AI algorithms must account for sex-based and racial⏎differences in disease presentation, progression, and treatment response to ensure equitable outcomes. 13⏎Ensuring AI tools reduce rather than exacerbate health disparities is essential to building public trust.⏎12. Develop Real-World Performance Monitoring Infrastructure⏎Current Barrier: AI tools that perform well in controlled research settings may degrade when deployed in⏎diverse real-world environments; robust post-deployment monitoring remains methodologically⏎challenging.⏎HHS should fund research on automated performance monitoring systems, benchmarking frameworks, and⏎rapid validation protocols for algorithm updates. Cooperative agreements with health systems, academic⏎medical centers, and AI developers can create shared infrastructure for ongoing performance evaluation.⏎This enables continuous improvement while maintaining safety and building payer confidence in AI⏎reliability.⏎13. Foster International Harmonization⏎Current Barrier: Divergent international regulatory approaches to AI create duplicative requirements and⏎slow global development timelines.⏎HHS should prioritize engagement through the International Council for Harmonisation (ICH), International⏎Coalition of Medicines Regulatory Authorities (ICMRA), International Medical Device Regulators Forum⏎(IMDRF), and Council for International Organizations of Medical Sciences (CIOMS) to align on common AI⏎terminology, harmonized validation standards, and mutual recognition agreements. Recent FDA⏎collaborations with Health Canada and the United Kingdom’s Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory⏎Agency (UK MHRA) on machine learning-enabled medical devices demonstrate the value of this approach. 14⏎Regulatory burden is reduced for developers and accelerates global access to AI innovations.⏎12 Id. at 5 ("About 70% of all FDA approvals for cancer medicines come via trials that measure surrogate endpoints (RR and PFS) rather than overall⏎survival. As a result, using those surrogates meant those approvals came 1-1.5 years sooner than if they had used overall survival—critical time⏎savings for a fatal disease."); citing Hwang TJ, et al., Association Between FDA and EMA Expedited Approval Programs and Therapeutic Value of New⏎Medicines: Retrospective Cohort Study, 366 BMJ l4340 (2019), available at https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6503556/.⏎13 Alzheimer's disease affects nearly two-thirds of Americans living with the disease are women, and Black Americans are about twice as likely to⏎develop Alzheimer's compared to white Americans. See Alzheimer's Association, 2024 Alzheimer's Disease Facts and Figures, 20 Alzheimer's &⏎Dementia 3708 (2024). AI algorithms used in Alzheimer's drug development and clinical decision support must account for these disparities to⏎ensure equitable performance across demographic groups.⏎14 ee FDA, Health Canada, and UK MHRA, Good Machine Learning Practice for Medical Device Development: Guiding Principles (Oct. 2021), available⏎at https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/good-machine-learning-practice-medical-device-development-guiding-⏎principles; FDA, Proposed Regulatory Framework for Modifications to Artificial Intelligence/Machine Learning (AI/ML)-Based Software as a Medical⏎Device (SaMD), Discussion Paper and Request for Feedback (Apr. 2019), available at https://www.fda.gov/media/122535/download (discussing⏎predetermined change control plans).⏎5⏎⏎HHS AI RFI Lilly Comments⏎Page 6⏎14. Support Responsible FDA Adoption of AI for Review Efficiency⏎Current Barrier: As FDA scales AI adoption, lack of transparent governance creates uncertainty about⏎protecting confidential information, ensuring data quality, and maintaining human oversight.⏎FDA should continue deploying AI tools to streamline workflows and improve review consistency, while⏎establishing transparent governance frameworks addressing six critical considerations:⏎(1) Protect confidential commercial information, ensure AI tools are never trained on sponsor data without⏎permission, and ensure third-party vendors cannot access proprietary information; 15⏎(2) Maintain accountability, publish annual transparency reports on which AI tools are deployed, how they⏎contribute to decisions, and validation metrics; 16⏎(3) Ensure data quality, establish standards for training data representativeness and conduct bias audits; 17⏎(4) Require human oversight, mandate human review for novel therapeutics, expedited pathways, and safety⏎signals; 18⏎(5) Achieve cross-center consistency, create agency-wide AI governance body to harmonize deployment; 19⏎and⏎(6) Secure systems, protect against cyberattacks and adversarial manipulation. 20⏎Regular stakeholder engagement on FDA’s AI use is essential to building trust and sharing best practices. 21 It⏎enables FDA to realize AI efficiency gains while maintaining gold standard review and protecting sponsor⏎confidential information.⏎Responses to Key HHS Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to AI adoption in clinical care?⏎Based on Lilly’s experience: (1) Reimbursement inertia (FFS payment systems fail to reward AI-enabled⏎prevention, efficiency gains, or quality improvements), (2) Regulatory uncertainty (unclear validation⏎standards), (3) Data access constraints (privacy regulations and fragmentation), (4) Liability ambiguity⏎(unclear responsibility allocation), and (5) Workforce readiness (insufficient AI literacy). Reimbursement is⏎the dominant barrier; many healthcare systems identify valuable AI tools but cannot justify adoption under⏎current payment models.⏎Question 3: What novel legal issues exist for non-medical device AI?⏎Two critical issues: (1) intellectual property (IP) protection during regulatory review, current trade secret⏎protections may be inadequate for AI algorithmic submissions to FDA; (2) Federated learning governance,⏎unclear rules on model ownership, institutional contribution recognition, and consent requirements when⏎data never leaves originating institutions. HHS should convene working groups to develop model liability⏎15 PhRMA, Use of AI in the Drug Lifecycle and Regulatory Activities at 4 (2025) ("FDA must ensure AI tools deployed within the Agency are not⏎trained on data submitted by industry or used for any other purpose without express permission of the sponsor. Furthermore, FDA must ensure⏎compliance with all applicable laws to protect sponsors' data and prevent unauthorized access or use, including third-party access.").⏎16 . at 4-5 ("It is important for FDA to share experiences regarding how AI models used by the Agency are being trained and developed, what criteria⏎the Agency uses to assess AI model credibility and trustworthiness, what specific AI models it uses (including from third-party developers), how the⏎AI model contributes to regulatory activities, and how FDA uses human-in-the-loop to ensure appropriate oversight and accuracy of AI models.").⏎17 Id. at 5 ("Data quality is paramount for training AI models, and FDA must ensure the data is fit-for-purpose.").⏎18 Id. at 5 ("FDA should continue to employ a reasonable human-in-the-loop process with proper training and accountability for clear and effective⏎oversight when using AI tools to assist with regulatory activities.").⏎19 Id. at 5-6 ("FDA should ensure that AI tools are consistently applied across the Agency, as appropriate, so that the benefits these tools offer can be⏎fully realized. Ensuring consistency in implementation, particularly when products require review by more than one center (e.g., combination⏎products), will be essential for achieving Agency-wide benefits. Siloed use of AI could lead to divergent results and delayed implementation.").⏎20 Id. at 6 ("AI systems utilized by FDA must be secure. FDA should ensure that the AI systems are not penetrable or susceptible to outside influence,⏎which could impact outputs or affect the performance of the trained models and processes.").⏎21 Id. at 5 ("There should continue to be an opportunity for regular stakeholder engagement around FDA's adoption and use of AI. Furthermore, FDA⏎should continue to seek stakeholder input and provide opportunities for direct communication on FDA's use of AI, including how the Agency will⏎address concerns and provide clarity on the use and limitations of AI in regulatory activities. Transparent communication will be key to maintaining⏎scientific integrity and public trust.").⏎6⏎⏎HHS AI RFI Lilly Comments⏎Page 7⏎frameworks, strengthen confidentiality protections for AI submissions, and issue guidance clarifying HIPAA⏎applicability to federated learning.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector AI innovation?⏎HHS can support innovation by demonstrating responsible AI adoption within its own operations. When FDA⏎successfully deploys AI tools, it sets standards, builds confidence, creates shared learning, and identifies⏎common barriers. However, this requires transparent communication through stakeholder engagement. 22⏎FDA should publish case studies, host workshops, maintain open dialogue, and work with NIST and⏎international regulators on best practices. See Recommendation #14.⏎Question 7: What influences AI adoption decisions within healthcare organizations?⏎Key decision-makers include C-suite leadership (strategic AI investment decisions based on return on⏎investment (ROI)), clinical department chairs (specialty-specific adoption), information technology (IT)⏎governance committees (technical integration), value analysis committees (cost-effectiveness), and front-⏎line clinicians (ultimate determinants through usage). Critical insight: Even when leadership approves AI⏎adoption, implementation fails without front-line clinician buy-in. Primary administrative hurdles include IT⏎integration complexity (legacy electronic health record (EHR) systems lack standardized application⏎programming interfaces (APIs)), credentialing processes (no clear approach for “credentialing” AI tools),⏎change management resources (limited capacity for training and workflow redesign), and contracting delays⏎(procurement processes poorly suited to AI tools requiring ongoing updates).⏎The Stakes Are High: If HHS acts decisively, the United States will maintain global leadership in healthcare⏎AI with enormous benefits for patients. If payment inertia and regulatory uncertainty persist, innovation will⏎migrate offshore and American patients will lose access to transformative technologies developed elsewhere.⏎Lilly stands ready to partner with HHS in building an AI-enabled healthcare system that is innovative,⏎equitable, and worthy of public trust.⏎Sincerely,⏎Diogo Rau⏎Executive Vice President and Chief Information and Digital Officer⏎Eli Lilly and Company⏎22 Id. at 5.⏎7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0244♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sandra♔Wright♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔National Kidney Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔National Kidney Foundation⏎Submitted to HHS Docket re: Health Sector AI RFI - Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Sandra Wright, Executive Director⏎National Kidney Foundation⏎⏎The question HHS keeps not asking is the one that matters most to me: when an AI-assisted decision harms a patient, who exactly is responsible? I have spent the better part of three months trying to get a straight answer to that question from legal counsel, from vendor representatives, from hospital compliance officers. Nobody will give one. That is not a mystery or an oversight. That is the system working as designed.⏎⏎I want to speak specifically to the reimbursement and deployment acceleration questions in this RFI, because that is where I think the real danger sits. Our patient population is overwhelmingly Medicare and Medicaid-enrolled, many of them managing end-stage renal disease with comorbidities that already make clinical decision-making complicated. When a nephrology practice in, say, Little Rock embeds an AI-assisted care pathway tool into its workflow and that tool misclassifies a patient's GFR trend and delays a referral, the question of who bears liability becomes genuinely unanswerable under current law. The clinician says they followed the tool's recommendation. The hospital says the clinician exercised independent judgment. The vendor contract - and I have read several of these now - contains disclaimers that the output is "informational only" and not a "clinical determination." So we have a patient who was harmed and a circle of parties each pointing at someone else.⏎⏎FDA 510(k) clearance does not fix this. I want to be direct about that because I think the clearance pathway gets cited as though it resolves the accountability question and it simply does not. Clearance tells you that a device is substantially equivalent to a predicate device. It does not tell you whether the tool performs safely on a 68-year-old Black woman with diabetic nephropathy and limited English proficiency in a rural Arkansas clinic. The clinical deployment context is almost never what was tested. And yet once these systems get embedded in EHR workflows and once CMS starts attaching reimbursement incentives to their use, practices will not remove them even when problems surface. The switching costs are too high. The contracts have termination penalties. The staff have reorganized workflows around the tool. I have heard this directly from our affiliated practice networks - not speculation, actual feedback from practice managers who said in so many words that they knew a tool had issues but removal was not realistic.⏎⏎NKF Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎That lock-in dynamic is what concerns me most about HHS moving to accelerate adoption right now. The accountability architecture is broken before we have even started scaling. Once reimbursement incentives are attached and workflows are reorganized, the leverage to demand fixes disappears. Vendors know this. A system where nobody is clearly on the hook for clinical harm is not an accident of regulatory gaps that will eventually get tidied up - it is a predictable outcome of an environment where deployment speed is incentivized and liability exposure is contractually minimized.⏎⏎What I am asking HHS to do before issuing any acceleration framework is to answer, in writing, with enforceability: who bears legal responsibility when an AI tool embedded in a clinical workflow contributes to patient harm? Not "the clinician retains responsibility" as a general principle, because that answer has already proven inadequate in contexts where clinicians reasonably relied on cleared, institutionally mandated tools. Something more specific than that.⏎⏎We submitted a formal set of related questions to the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy on March 14, 2024 and have not received a substantive response. I am not sure whether this RFI is the right venue for that follow-up or whether we should be directing it elsewhere, but it seems relevant to note that♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0245♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔King♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Electronic Frontier Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔The thing that keeps coming back to me when I read this RFI is the gap between what HHS is asking about — how to accelerate AI adoption — and what we're actually seeing happen to patients right now. The Department is soliciting ideas about the future while a very concrete, very present harm is already running at scale. AI-driven prior authorization systems are denying medically necessary care every day, and the patients who are being hurt most are exactly the ones this Department has legal obligations to protect.⏎⏎I work in government affairs and I've spent years watching how policy gets made in health care, and what I'm seeing with AI in the insurance context is unlike anything I've encountered before. The speed is the issue. These systems are processing denial decisions faster than any human reviewer could possibly catch up with, and the appeals burden gets pushed onto patients — many of whom are elderly, don't speak English as a first language, are on Medicaid, or are dealing with a serious illness that makes fighting bureaucratic decisions genuinely impossible. We know from reporting on UnitedHealth's claims review tool — which drew significant congressional scrutiny after a ProPublica investigation documented denial rates climbing sharply after the algorithm was deployed — that this isn't hypothetical. These are real people being told no, frequently, by a system that may have been trained on data that doesn't reflect their demographics or their clinical circumstances.⏎⏎There's a related point from within the urologic care space specifically that I want to flag here. Fewer than one in five AI tool vendors in urology practices can actually provide documentation showing their algorithms were validated against racially and socioeconomically diverse patient cohorts. That's a finding from member survey data presented at the AUA 2024 Annual Scientific Meeting. That is a vendor accountability gap that exists right now, before any further acceleration of adoption. And the patients in the practices most likely to be affected — practices where half or more of patients are rural, low-income, or non-English-speaking — are the ones with the least institutional support to push back when something goes wrong.⏎⏎HHS has authority here that it is not currently using. The question this RFI keeps dancing around is how to get AI adopted faster, but the predicate question — whether AI-generated coverage determinations should require meaningful human review before they result in a denial — is something HHS can act on now. Not after more stakeholder engagement. Not pending further research. The legal hooks exist. Choosing not to use them while simultaneously signaling enthusiasm for accelerating AI integration is a policy choice with foreseeable consequences, and I think the Department should be honest with itself about what it's choosing.⏎⏎I want to be careful not to sound like I'm opposed to AI in health care broadly. I'm not. There are genuinely promising applications and I understand why the Department wants to move in this direction. But the framing of this RFI treats adoption speed as the primary variable to optimize, and I don't think that's the right frame when we're talking about populations that have historically been on the losing end of health system decisions.⏎⏎What I'd specifically ask HHS to address: any action plan coming out of this RFI process should include a mandatory human review requirement for AI-generated prior authorization denials, with documented oversight trails — not just a recommendation, an actual requirement with enforcement teeth. And there should be equity auditing standards for any AI tool used in coverage determinations, with public-facing disclosure of validation data. Not a self-attestation checkbox. Actual documentation, reviewable by researchers and patient advocates.⏎⏎The comment period closes and then there will be a response document, and I've seen enough of those to know how these things go. What I hope doesn't happen is that the response focuses on the exciting applications and treats the accountability questions as something to work out later through sub-regulatory guidance that never quite arrives.⏎⏎Ashley King⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎Electronic Frontier Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0246♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from GeneDx, LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔GeneDx, LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔To: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎From: GeneDx⏎February 21, 2026⏎II. Solicitation of Public Comments⏎GeneDx appreciates the opportunity to provide input on how the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) can accelerate the effective adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) to⏎propel the American health care system forward. As a leader in delivering improved health⏎outcomes through genomic insights, we are applying AI to accelerate and enhance genetic⏎diagnostics. AI is shaping the future of medical genetics, and we are and we are leveraging our⏎rich data through AI-informed tools to bring answers to more patients, reduce manual workflows,⏎and scale precision medicine for all patients.⏎AI supports our business in three main ways. First, AI helps unlock GeneDx’s full potential to⏎offer more diagnoses to more patients. For example, we enhance the power of our interpretation⏎platform with Multiscore, an advanced AI-powered decision support tool designed to⏎revolutionize genetic analysis by improving diagnostic efficiency, streamlining workflows, and⏎enhancing clinical insights. The tool helps clinical geneticists focus on the most relevant genes⏎during their analysis. Second, AI helps us drive increased speed and accuracy across our⏎operations. Last year, we announced our acquisition of Fabric Genomics, a leader in AI⏎interpretation – combining Fabric Genomics’ AI-driven interpretation platform with GeneDx’s⏎unparalleled rare disease data set to make patient genome interpretation faster, more scalable,⏎and more impactful. Third, AI enables our entire enterprise to operate more efficiently and⏎effectively. We also leverage Natural Language Processing, Machine Learning, and Large⏎Language Models to drive growth and efficiency wherever possible, including as we transition⏎ourselves to the Lab of the Future.⏎As GeneDx works to build the Lab of the Future, we are designing a laboratory optimized for⏎continuous improvement—harnessing automation, robotics, and AI-driven algorithms to increase⏎throughput, quality, and consistency. Propelled by one of the world’s most comprehensive and⏎clinically curated genomic and phenotypic datasets, and supported by our expert team of⏎geneticists, this model enables a 24/7, highly scalable operation capable of growing from⏎approximately 100,000 patients per year to more than one million, while delivering faster and⏎more accurate answers. This approach allows us to scale efficiently, unlock economies of scale,⏎and help ensure genomic diagnostics are accessible to more patients.⏎GeneDx applauds HHS for seeking engagement on how AI can improve patient and caregiver⏎experiences and outcomes, reduce provider burden and improve quality of care, and reduce⏎health care costs for consumers and government. We particularly appreciate HHS’ focus on⏎future-looking responses, as GeneDx works to build the Lab of the Future.⏎GeneDx, LLC⏎205 & 207 Perry Parkway, Gaithersburg, MD 20877⏎888-729-1206 | support@genedx.com⏎GeneDx.com⏎⏎Our comments below focus on strategies to support the safe and effective clinical adoption of AI.⏎Thank you for soliciting our input.⏎Specific Questions⏎In addition to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, HHS seeks input on the following specific⏎questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎GeneDx appreciates the importance of understanding barriers to private sector innovation in⏎AI-enabled health care. Given the breadth and complexity of this topic, GeneDx would⏎welcome the opportunity to engage further with HHS through future listening sessions,⏎workshops, or other multi-stakeholder forums.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎To incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care, HHS could consider the following:⏎• Clarifying risk-based pathways that differentiate software as a medical device (SaMD)⏎from non-device clinical support to reduce unnecessary regulatory burdens, while⏎prioritizing safety.⏎• Developing reimbursement frameworks for clinically validated AI tools to boost⏎innovation.⏎• Providing regulatory safe harbors and pilot programs for innovative AI integration.⏎• Endorsing continued delays in scheduled payment reductions to the Clinical Laboratory⏎Fee Schedule under the Protecting Access to Medicare Act to ensure adequate⏎reimbursement rates and maintain patient access to high-quality diagnostic services,⏎particularly for individuals and families affected by suspected rare disease.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎GeneDx recognizes that liability, indemnification, privacy, and accountability⏎frameworks for AI-enabled tools in clinical care are evolving. We believe further multi-⏎stakeholder engagement is necessary before prescribing specific policy solutions and⏎would welcome continued dialogue with HHS on these issues.⏎GeneDx, LLC⏎205 & 207 Perry Parkway, Gaithersburg, MD 20877⏎888-729-1206 | support@genedx.com⏎GeneDx.com⏎⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎GeneDx supports continued exploration of AI evaluation methods that emphasize clinical⏎validity, transparency, robustness, and appropriate human oversight. We encourage HHS to⏎continue convening experts to advance best practices in this area.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI⏎use in clinical care?⏎Certification to bolster credibility and grants to fund innovation would help promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care, as well as a mechanism to share best practices across⏎companies in the health care sector. This should include standards for the diversity of datasets⏎that are leveraged to train AI-informed models; and standards for incorporating evidence⏎generated from AI-informed models into variant classification frameworks.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would⏎have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on⏎quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI tools deployed in genomic testing have exceeded performance and cost expectations. Much of⏎genetic interpretation relies on analyzing large datasets, presenting an opportunity for the use of⏎AI tools, designed to find patterns in the setting of highly complex data, and integrate multiple⏎data types. AI can be leveraged to derive insights from cumulative testing datasets and provide⏎predictions of patients’ genetic conditions. For example, GeneDx’s Multiscore decision support⏎tool prioritizes genes in exome and genome sequences by ranking them based on alignment with⏎a patient’s clinical presentation. Incorporating phenotypic similarities into gene ranking criteria,⏎allows GeneDx to better serve patients whose symptoms may not fit textbook descriptions, and⏎enabling the identification of a definitive or possible molecular diagnosis even in the setting of⏎atypical or expanded clinical presentations. This approach enables faster, more accurate⏎identification of genetic conditions, providing a scalable solution for clinical interpretation⏎embedded directly within GeneDx’s platform, and reducing the time required for manual review⏎by analysts, ultimately shortening the overall turnaround time for delivering results.⏎As the number of patients who could benefit from genomic sequencing continues to grow, AI-⏎enabled tools can help extend the reach of the limited genetics workforce and promote more⏎equitable access to timely, accurate diagnoses.⏎Although one in ten people live with a rare disease, many go undiagnosed, untreated, and⏎unsupported. On average, finding the correct diagnosis can take five years, more than 16 medical⏎GeneDx, LLC⏎205 & 207 Perry Parkway, Gaithersburg, MD 20877⏎888-729-1206 | support@genedx.com⏎GeneDx.com⏎⏎tests, and $10,000 in health care costs. By enabling earlier diagnosis and treatment, novel AI⏎tools in genomics offer significant potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights⏎on quality, and help reduce costs.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What⏎are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Chief Medical Officers and Chief Information Officers have the most influence on the adoption⏎of AI for clinical care. GeneDx also has a Director of AI to accelerate adoption and innovation.⏎Additionally, Dr. Thomas Fuchs, Chief AI Officer at Eli Lilly and Company, serves on our board⏎of directors. Fuchs has extensive experience scaling AI and machine learning infrastructure and⏎algorithms for practical, high-impact applications in diagnostic medicine.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Interoperability would widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the development⏎of AI for clinical care, as AI is only as powerful as the data it is trained on. Leveraging⏎large, diverse, and unbiased genomic data in a responsible and expert-informed manner would⏎help extend the boundaries of knowledge and find answers for more patients. Population⏎frequency variant data or “synthetic patients,” as leveraged for DNA sequence models, protein-⏎sequence models, and knowledge-based models of disease are also essential.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients⏎and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎With the adoption of AI in clinical care, patients and caregivers hope to see shorter diagnosis⏎times and improved access to high-quality, affordable genomic diagnostics and downstream⏎precision care. At the same time, many have concerns about the privacy and confidentiality of⏎health and genomic data, as well as transparency in AI-driven decision-making. For this reason,⏎we prioritize strict data confidentiality and a human-in-the-loop approach – deploying AI in a⏎human-centric way to ensure quality of care for patients. The other concern is the quality and⏎characteristics of the data being leveraged. Attention must be paid to train AI tools with data that⏎are diverse to avoid perpetuating biases in the medical and genomic care spaces.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their⏎use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize the potential of AI in genomic sequencing.⏎GeneDx, LLC⏎205 & 207 Perry Parkway, Gaithersburg, MD 20877⏎888-729-1206 | support@genedx.com⏎GeneDx.com⏎⏎An increasing number of clinical guidelines, payor policies, and, more recently, legislation,⏎support GS and ES as the standard of care, indicating that the number of patients who could⏎benefit from this testing will outpace the capacity of the geneticist workforce.⏎While there are many ways that AI can enhance the business of genetic testing, such as⏎automating billing and for customer support, AI's potential in variant classification and⏎interpretation is particularly compelling. AI uses within medical genetics include automating⏎aspects of the clinical workflow, providing predictions of patients' genetic conditions, and⏎deriving insights from the cumulative testing dataset, including uncovering new gene–disease⏎relationships.⏎The workflows of a genetics laboratory include analysis and extraction of features such as HPO⏎terms from clinical documents, review of the genetic literature, assignment of American College⏎of Medical Genetics (ACMG) criteria to variants (Richards et al. 2015), and generation of a test⏎report to communicate results to patients and clinicians. In all these use cases, AI can provide⏎solutions to perform tasks more reproducibly, more thoroughly, and faster. For example,⏎conventional or generative AI can be used to extract structured clinical information from clinical⏎documents (Fu et al. 2024; Srivastava et al. 2024), mine the genetics literature for evidence⏎attributable to the variants (Lyu et al. 2024; Wei et al. 2018), and pull together relevant test⏎information and information from the literature to draft a genetic test report. AI has been used for⏎similar tasks within other knowledge-intensive fields such as legal (e.g., https://www.clio.com)⏎and software (e.g., https://www.cursor.com) realms. The summarization and synthesis of⏎genetic findings from many publications and internal and external cases can be enhanced by⏎agentic workflows that generate knowledge-grounded first drafts of passages to consider for⏎inclusion in the final patient report.⏎Identifying relevant variants and determining their pathogenicity is another extremely important⏎part of the workflow that can be powered by AI (Aradhya S. et al. 2023) This is a process of⏎whittling thousands of potential variants down to one or several that are responsible for the⏎patient's clinical presentation. There has been great advancement in DNA sequence models like⏎ESM-var (Meier et al. 2021) and protein-sequence models such as AlphaMissense (Cheng⏎et al. 2023) that can predict pathogenicity of genetic variants. Additionally, knowledge-based⏎models of disease such as SHEPHERD (Alsentzer et al. 2024) provide powerful tools for⏎recommending the causative variant or gene in a particular patient's case. Many of these methods⏎rely on clever strategies to find data upon which to train, for example, population frequency⏎variant data in the case of AlphaMissense or “synthetic patients” for SHEPHERD. Large⏎commercial laboratories have robust patient datasets of ES and GS data paired with detailed⏎clinical information for patients suspected of rare disease, which can also be used to train highly⏎effective models of variant pathogenicity.⏎In addition to helping to solve a single genetic case, AI can leverage large public and commercial⏎genetic datasets that couple genotype and phenotype information to extend the boundaries of⏎knowledge and find answers for more patients. AI models can integrate the mountains of⏎GeneDx, LLC⏎205 & 207 Perry Parkway, Gaithersburg, MD 20877⏎888-729-1206 | support@genedx.com⏎GeneDx.com⏎⏎genomic data to find new gene-disease candidates, help understand pathogenic non-coding⏎variants, and generate models of polygenic risk (Greene et al. 2017; Wells et al. 2019).⏎GeneDx, LLC⏎205 & 207 Perry Parkway, Gaithersburg, MD 20877⏎888-729-1206 | support@genedx.com⏎GeneDx.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0247♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Lee♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Dale spent nearly four months being bounced around by a regional clinic in Bismarck after an automated screening tool categorized him as low-risk. The staff just went with what the system said. Nobody sat down and looked at him the way a doctor actually looks at a patient. By the time someone did, his situation had gotten considerably worse than it had to be. That's where I'm coming from with this comment.⏎⏎What bothers me about this whole effort to speed up AI adoption in clinical care is that it seems to treat the problem of medicine as basically an information problem. Like if you just get the right data processed fast enough, you get good care. But that's not what I watched happen with my brother. The information about him was right there in the system. The problem was that nobody questioned the output. The staff deferred to the tool, and Dale got treated like a checkbox instead of a sick person who was scared and needed someone to actually engage with him.⏎⏎I work as a librarian. I spend a lot of time thinking about how people find and use information, and one thing I've noticed is that tools are only as good as the judgment of the people using them. We've had this debate in library science for decades, honestly. Databases don't replace the reference interview. They never have. There's something that happens when a person sits across from another person and pays attention that no search algorithm captures. I imagine medicine is the same way, probably more so.⏎⏎What I want HHS to think hard about before pushing faster adoption is the question of accountability. If an AI tool flags someone incorrectly, or misses something, who is answerable? The vendor? The clinic administrator who decided to deploy it? The nurse who was covering three patients at once and trusted the screen because she didn't have time not to? I don't think those questions are anywhere close to settled, and I worry that moving fast on adoption means moving fast past those questions rather than through them.⏎⏎Dale's situation resolved, eventually. He's doing okay now. But four months is a long time when something is wrong with you and nobody's treating it. And I keep thinking about how the efficiency argument was probably part of why that clinic adopted that tool in the first place. Smaller staff, faster throughput, lower costs. I get it. Rural healthcare is stretched thin. That's real. But I don't think the answer to being stretched thin is a system that gives staff permission to stop looking at the patient.⏎⏎I also want to say something about the rural context specifically, because I don't think it gets enough attention in these discussions. The clinics serving communities like mine in North Dakota are often understaffed to begin with, and when a new tool comes in, there's enormous pressure to use it as a substitute for the capacity you don't have rather than as a supplement to the capacity you do. That's a structural problem that no AI deployment policy fixes on its own, and I'd want HHS to be honest about that rather than suggesting that AI access is the same thing as healthcare access.⏎⏎I don't know enough about the specific regulatory mechanisms HHS is considering to comment on most of the technical questions. But on the basic question of whether we should be moving quickly or carefully on this, my answer is carefully. Much more carefully than the framing of this RFI suggests.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0248♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from 1upHealth, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1upHealth♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary Thomas Keane, MD⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A,⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201.⏎CC: Steven Posnack, Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, ASTP/ONC⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎This comment is by 1upHealth, a scalable serverless cloud data provider leveraging the power of⏎the FHIR data standard to combine claims and clinical data for analytics and APIs to change⏎behavior. We applaud the work of ASTP/ONC to enable modern APIs and power a digital health⏎economy giving patients apps of their choice. We appreciate the ongoing work of ASTP/ONC in⏎exploring how to make AI more practical for patient care.⏎General Points⏎AI relies on data and in particular good AI relies on good data. Without adequate data downstream⏎HHS AI policies will be ineffective. HHS, in particular CMS payment policies, should focus on data⏎fluidity and transparency and do so with robust privacy protections. As a practical matter this⏎means incenting use of FHIR APIs and requiring public key cryptographic protections for the only⏎parties with an actual right to patient data – namely the patient, the patient’s providers and the⏎patient’s payer.⏎The fundamental challenge to get data for legitimate uses including AI is that CMS provider⏎payment policies heavily incent making data NOT AVAILABLE whether for AI or any pro-⏎280 Summer St, Floor 7, Boston MA 02210 | 508-245-8558 | 1up.health⏎⏎competitive purpose. Core to this is disproportionate payment for procedures so the dominant US⏎business model has become that of an oligopoly provider using a single EHR where vast amounts⏎of data are used internally and shared with other oligopoly customers but where external release of⏎that same information to potential competition is heavily gated by cumbersome procedures,⏎disproportionate costs, ancient protocols and at times outright throttling of data flows. This⏎“information blocking” has been the pattern in healthcare for decades after widespread⏎interoperability became the norm in every other sector of the economy.⏎As a start CMS payments should be made dependent on provider documentation being made⏎available with modern RESTful APIs. The CMS standards group could modify X12 so that every⏎provider claim includes the URL where documentation for the billed service becomes available to⏎patient’s, their apps (both AI and otherwise) as well as true HIPAA Covered Entities.⏎HHS could be the center of a modern digital healthcare economy serving patients. While HHS has⏎historically supported FHIR APIs in the original 2020 Cures Act rule and with payer provided APIs⏎under the CMS 0057 rule. Now HHS appears to support institutional information blocking in the⏎form of TEFCA. The 2016 Cures Act defines information blocking as interfering with the “access,⏎exchange and use” of patient data – TEFCA does all three. TEFCA’s brokers interfere with data⏎access, TEFCA’s ancient AOL / Yahoo era protocols interfere with data exchange and TEFCA’s⏎document-only architecture interferes with data use. TEFCA proponents (not surprisingly⏎incumbent data holders and those trying to sell patient data to third parties circumventing HIPAA)⏎state that TEFCA will be moving to FHIR (the JSON based data standard). They have claimed this⏎for the last 5 years yet TEFCA does not appear to have performed a single bona fide API⏎transaction using FHIR.⏎Modern AI works best with massive amounts of data. TEFCA’s antiquated architecture and⏎baroque protocols are not up the task of moving the large amounts of data needed for robust AI.⏎The cellphone Internet economy does billions of JSON transactions DAILY.⏎The Internet uses robust public key cryptography and “zero-trust” security for data needing⏎protection. This is in stark contrast to TEFCA’s “just trust me” faux-HIPAA “permitted uses”. This⏎end run around HIPAA is so brittle that TEFCA participants – the same QHINs which are supposed⏎to vet other participants – are currently engaged in multiple lawsuits around their wholesale⏎misappropriation of patient data. The data being resold here has almost invariably been⏎subsidized by taxpayers.⏎Actual privacy and security should be the ground assumption for HHS AI work. RESTful and Bulk⏎FHIR APIs anchored by public key cryptography can provide this level of privacy and security just⏎as they do in banking and defense. HL7 has extensive implementation guides for healthcare⏎versions of these APIs. HHS AI use should build on these APIs. Using such APIs CMS could⏎analyze Medicare claims for fraud (matching actual diagnoses, plausible providers and appropriate⏎services). MA, Medicaid and commercial plans could use similar tools and because working APIs⏎are real-time this can be done before fraudulent claims are paid. Using the Bulk FHIR APIs which⏎are batch mode population level APIs, CMS and other payers such as the VA and DOD could use⏎AI to correlate patterns of care and best practices with outcomes and do so on a broad basis.⏎280 Summer St, Floor 7, Boston MA 02210 | 508-245-8558 | 1up.health⏎⏎Today CMS measures quality with cumbersome data collection - QRDA for providers and Primary⏎Source Verification for payers.⏎It is key for HHS to realize that enablement of ethical AI and patient-controlled digitally based⏎competition are largely one and the same. Both rely on the same data and the same privacy⏎security models. While one can see alternate data acquisition tools such as screen-scraping⏎(robotic process automation) or any number of proprietary EHR vendor-gated strategies⏎widespread use of AI in healthcare will require public APIs. Patients want apps which are smart,⏎real-time, low cost and maximally effective. They do not want to be bound to only those services⏎provided by incumbents and their “portals” or TEFCA’s convoluted IAS barriers.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The biggest barrier to the use of AI in healthcare is the reality that much if not most clinical data is⏎held by a few EHR vendors and only available to whatever AI programs those vendors decide to⏎allow. The vast bulk of data hidden here has been paid for by US taxpayers.⏎One major HHS policy would be to require that the EHR vendors patient-portals are required to run⏎entirely through public APIs rather than hidden connections. Any patient data including scheduling⏎which is not available at the same level of performance to outside competitive patient record⏎products should be considered information-blocking. Cures Act enforcement should be initiated⏎against both the EHR and the provider using that EHR. Having patient-controlled apps on a⏎somewhat level playing field with provider / vendor-controlled apps would allow patients to get⏎apps giving the patient their AI optimized medical advice and guidance the form patients as⏎consumers decide on rather than being bound to their provider portal.⏎Another massive policy opportunity to open up data for AI is in how CMS pays Medicare⏎Advantage plans. The Star ratings program and HEDIS measures use outdated data feeds. This⏎data is not exposed to public APIs which AI vendors could use to improve the efficiency and cost-⏎effectiveness care. One outgrowth of current Star rating policy are the so-called vendor Payer⏎Platforms where EHR vendors use private APIs to help providers and payers game higher Star⏎ratings. CMS should zero out payments for any relevant Star rating measures unless that data⏎was obtained through a public API with each data element tagged with the metadata of that public⏎API call. By requiring all Star ratings exclusively use public API data the entire quality⏎measurement landscape could then be modernized by AI. This would have secondary public⏎benefits of helping reform quality measurement and helping payers better negotiate on behalf of⏎taxpayers and beneficiaries.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎The Cures Act requires that ALL data be made available. The 2020 ONC Cures Act rule predated⏎widespread use of LLMs and had as its only requirement that each data field had a vocabulary tag⏎280 Summer St, Floor 7, Boston MA 02210 | 508-245-8558 | 1up.health⏎⏎of the vendor’s choice. ASTP should consider fleshing out the opportunities to more richly enable⏎AI with this Cures Act requirement.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Requiring patient permission using modern cryptographic principles would be a solid foundation for⏎AI data use.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Requiring the large volumes of healthcare data needed for quality measurement and Star ratings to⏎flow through public APIs rather than NCQA Primary Source Verification screen reviews or private⏎payer provider platforms (which inevitably become anti-competitive) would be fundamental⏎interoperability reforms. Incenting modern high-performance RESTful FHIR APIs rather than⏎TEFCA’s brittle, expensive, slow and insecure document flows should be central to Federal policy.⏎Thank you for reviewing these comments.⏎Sincerely,⏎Donald W. Rucker, MD⏎Chief Strategy Officer, 1upHealth⏎280 Summer St, Floor 7, Boston MA 02210 | 508-245-8558 | 1up.health♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0249♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Association of Physicists in Medicine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Association of Physicists in Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔American Association of Physicists in Medicine⏎Response:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/23/2025-23641/request-for-information-accelerating⏎-the-adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence-as-part-of⏎Executive Summary⏎The American Association of Physicists in Medicine (AAPM) appreciates the opportunity to provide⏎comments to the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) on accelerating the adoption and⏎use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. AAPM is a professional society representing⏎more than 9,000 medical physicists and scientists dedicated to advancing the safe and effective use of⏎physics and engineering in medicine. The following comments reflect the collective expertise of AAPM⏎medical physicist members who operate at the intersection of advanced technology, clinical practice,⏎regulatory oversight, and patient safety, and offer a perspective grounded in both innovation and⏎real-world implementation within highly regulated healthcare environments.⏎AAPM’s recommendations emphasize that successful AI adoption in safety-critical clinical domains⏎depends not only on technological advancement, but also on validation, lifecycle monitoring,⏎governance clarity, interoperability, workforce preparedness, and aligned incentives. In healthcare⏎environments where AI tools may influence diagnosis, treatment planning, and patient management,⏎innovation must be accompanied by structured oversight, evidence generation, and transparent⏎accountability. Federal policies should recognize these realities and support coordinated ecosystems⏎that integrate technology developers, healthcare institutions, regulators, payers, and professional⏎societies.⏎Across the RFI topics, AAPM highlights several cross-cutting priorities:⏎● Strengthening validation and benchmarking infrastructure by investing in shared reference⏎datasets, independent testing frameworks, post-deployment monitoring systems, and clinically⏎meaningful performance metrics that enable objective evaluation and sustained oversight of AI⏎tools used in patient care.⏎● Establishing risk-based governance and oversight models that align regulatory⏎expectations with clinical impact, clarify lifecycle accountability, and provide consistent guidance⏎for non-medical-device AI embedded in healthcare workflows.⏎● Aligning reimbursement and programmatic incentives with measurable clinical value by⏎modernizing CMS payment and quality programs to reward demonstrable improvements in⏎safety, efficiency, consistency, and patient outcomes enabled by validated AI applications.⏎1⏎⏎● Advancing interoperability and data harmonization through standardized data models,⏎integration of imaging and structured clinical data, and inclusion of audit logs, version control,⏎and performance metadata necessary for scalable validation and benchmarking.⏎● Developing workforce capacity and defined oversight roles by supporting role-based⏎competency frameworks and training pathways for professionals responsible for AI⏎commissioning, quality assurance, monitoring, and governance.⏎● Supporting private-sector standards and profession-led assurance activities that⏎complement federal oversight, reduce duplicative requirements, and ground AI governance⏎expectations in established clinical safety practices.⏎● Expanding implementation science and economic evaluation research to generate⏎evidence on cost-effectiveness, workflow impact, and long-term clinical outcomes associated⏎with AI-enabled care.⏎● Promoting transparency, patient trust, and equitable access by encouraging clear⏎communication regarding AI use, supporting bias mitigation and fairness research, and ensuring⏎validated technologies can be responsibly adopted across diverse healthcare settings.⏎AAPM looks forward to continued engagement with HHS and its agencies to develop policies that⏎accelerate responsible AI innovation while strengthening the validation and governance frameworks⏎necessary to protect patients and sustain public trust.⏎(i) What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎AI innovation in healthcare is increasingly constrained not by algorithm development capacity, but by⏎gaps in validation standards, governance clarity, implementation infrastructure, and workforce⏎readiness. In safety-critical domains such as medical imaging and radiation therapy, AI tools are being⏎introduced into complex, regulated clinical environments that require rigorous commissioning, quality⏎assurance, and lifecycle oversight. However, many AI systems, particularly those not regulated as⏎medical devices, lack consistent frameworks for evaluation, monitoring, and accountability. These⏎structural uncertainties slow adoption, increase institutional risk, and limit the scalability of otherwise⏎promising technologies.⏎AAPM identifies the following primary barriers and corresponding federal opportunities:⏎● Lack of standardized validation and benchmarking frameworks.⏎AI tools are frequently evaluated using heterogeneous datasets and metrics, making it difficult⏎for healthcare institutions to assess generalizability across patient populations, equipment⏎platforms, and care settings. Federal support for nationally recognized validation standards,⏎shared benchmarking datasets, and transparent performance reporting would reduce⏎uncertainty and strengthen institutional confidence.⏎● Regulatory and lifecycle ambiguity.⏎AI systems may be continuously updated or adapted after deployment, yet guidance regarding⏎post-market monitoring, retraining, performance drift, and institutional governance⏎responsibilities remains inconsistent. Clear federal expectations regarding lifecycle oversight,⏎documentation, and accountability would reduce risk and improve implementation consistency.⏎● Fragmented data infrastructure and limited interoperability.⏎High-quality AI development and validation depend on diverse, well-curated datasets, but⏎healthcare data remain siloed and inconsistently structured. Investment in interoperable data⏎standards, federated data-sharing models, and privacy-preserving collaboration mechanisms⏎would improve dataset representativeness and accelerate safe innovation.⏎2⏎⏎● Insufficient workforce training and institutional governance capacity.⏎Effective AI integration requires professionals capable of critically evaluating model⏎performance, identifying bias, overseeing commissioning, and managing lifecycle monitoring.⏎Federal workforce development initiatives that incorporate AI literacy, validation principles, and⏎governance frameworks into clinical education would support responsible adoption.⏎By addressing validation gaps, clarifying governance expectations, strengthening data infrastructure,⏎and investing in workforce capacity, HHS can reduce implementation risk and enable safe, scalable,⏎and evidence-based AI adoption across clinical settings.⏎(ii) What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations⏎AI adoption in clinical care will accelerate only if regulatory expectations are clear, governance⏎responsibilities are defined, and reimbursement structures align with validated improvements in safety⏎and quality. In safety-critical domains such as medical imaging and radiation therapy, institutions must⏎integrate AI into existing quality assurance programs, documentation systems, and clinical workflows.⏎Policy uncertainty or misaligned incentives can delay adoption even when tools demonstrate⏎measurable clinical value.⏎AAPM recommends the following priority policy actions:⏎● Establish risk-based oversight guidance for AI used in clinical care that aligns⏎expectations with clinical impact rather than device classification alone.⏎HHS should publish cross-agency guidance clarifying governance expectations for AI tools that⏎influence clinical decision-making, including commissioning, documentation, performance⏎monitoring, and update management. Oversight intensity should scale with clinical risk and⏎potential patient impact, reducing unnecessary burden for low-risk tools while preserving⏎safeguards for high-impact applications.⏎● Clarify how existing HHS authorities apply to AI model development, deployment, and⏎monitoring, particularly under HIPAA privacy and security requirements.⏎Healthcare institutions require clear guidance on permissible data uses for AI development,⏎validation, and operational monitoring. HHS should issue interpretive guidance clarifying how⏎healthcare operations, quality assessment, and performance improvement activities involving AI⏎are treated under the HIPAA Privacy Rule and Security Rule (see 45 C.F.R. Part 164, Subparts⏎C and E ). Clear expectations regarding de-identification, audit controls, and ongoing model⏎monitoring would reduce compliance uncertainty.⏎● Modernize CMS payment structures to ensure validated AI-enabled improvements in⏎safety and quality are financially sustainable.⏎AI tools that reduce errors, improve consistency of care, enhance workflow efficiency, or support⏎earlier detection should not face unintended reimbursement barriers. CMS should review⏎value-based payment models and hospital payment structures to ensure they are compatible⏎with investments in validated AI tools and do not create disincentives for adoption.⏎● Incorporate validated AI-enabled safety and quality improvements into CMS quality⏎reporting frameworks where appropriate.⏎CMS quality programs strongly influence institutional priorities and investment decisions. CMS⏎3⏎⏎should allow AI-enabled improvements in safety, accuracy, or efficiency to be recognized within⏎relevant quality reporting and performance measurement programs under the Quality Payment⏎Program framework (see 42 C.F.R. Part 414, Subpart O ) and applicable hospital quality⏎reporting regulations, when supported by objective evidence.⏎(iii) For AI tools that are not regulated as medical devices, what novel legal or⏎implementation issues exist, and what role should HHS play in addressing them?⏎AI systems that influence clinical decision-making but are not regulated as medical devices introduce⏎governance and accountability challenges that are not fully addressed by existing frameworks. These⏎tools may support triage, workflow prioritization, image analysis, documentation, or decision support⏎functions without falling squarely within traditional device classifications. As a result, institutions face⏎uncertainty regarding responsibility for validation, oversight, monitoring, liability exposure, and lifecycle⏎management.⏎Unlike traditional software tools, many AI systems are dynamic. Models may be updated by vendors,⏎retrained on new datasets, or fine-tuned locally. Without clear guidance, healthcare organizations must⏎independently determine how to manage version control, performance drift, documentation, and⏎change management within existing quality systems. This variability creates inconsistent oversight⏎practices and may increase institutional risk.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎● Clarify institutional and vendor accountability expectations for non-device AI used in⏎clinical care.⏎HHS should provide guidance outlining shared responsibilities among developers, vendors, and⏎healthcare institutions when AI tools influence clinical workflows or patient care decisions. Clear⏎expectations regarding documentation, validation prior to deployment, and ongoing monitoring⏎would reduce liability ambiguity.⏎● Provide guidance on lifecycle management and post-deployment oversight.⏎HHS should clarify expectations related to model updates, retraining, performance drift⏎monitoring, and documentation practices. Institutions require clear standards for when⏎revalidation is necessary and how changes should be recorded within quality assurance⏎systems.⏎● Encourage transparent contractual practices that preserve institutional oversight.⏎Healthcare organizations should retain sufficient access to performance data, audit rights, and⏎documentation to independently evaluate AI tools used in their facilities. HHS can promote⏎model transparency principles and best practices that support institutional governance without⏎mandating disclosure of proprietary code.⏎● Develop model governance frameworks and toolkits for health systems.⏎HHS could support the development of template governance structures, oversight committee⏎models, commissioning checklists, and escalation protocols to assist institutions in safely⏎integrating non-device AI into clinical environments.⏎(iv) What evaluation methods, metrics, and monitoring approaches are most promising⏎for AI tools used in clinical care, and how should HHS support their development and⏎implementation?⏎Effective AI adoption in clinical care depends on rigorous pre-deployment validation and sustained⏎post-deployment monitoring. In safety-critical domains such as medical imaging and radiation therapy,⏎4⏎⏎technologies are traditionally commissioned using structured acceptance testing, independent⏎verification, and routine quality assurance protocols. AI systems, particularly those that are adaptive or⏎frequently updated, require comparable lifecycle oversight to ensure continued safety and⏎effectiveness.⏎Pre-deployment evaluation should rely on clinically meaningful performance metrics that align with the⏎tool’s intended use and reflect real-world clinical conditions. Technical metrics such as accuracy or area⏎under the curve may be insufficient without contextual measures of workflow impact, error reduction,⏎generalizability across institutions, and performance across diverse patient populations.⏎Post-deployment monitoring is equally important. AI models may experience performance drift over⏎time due to changes in patient demographics, imaging equipment, clinical protocols, or data acquisition⏎practices. Without systematic monitoring, degradation in performance may go undetected. Structured⏎monitoring should include mechanisms to identify data drift, assess adverse outcomes, evaluate bias,⏎and determine when revalidation is required.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎● Support development of nationally recognized validation standards and benchmarking⏎datasets.⏎HHS should invest in shared reference datasets and independent benchmarking initiatives that⏎enable objective comparison of AI tools across institutions and clinical environments.⏎● Promote clinically meaningful performance metrics.⏎Evaluation frameworks should emphasize patient safety, error reduction, workflow impact, and⏎consistency of care rather than relying solely on retrospective technical performance metrics.⏎● Fund research on post-deployment monitoring and performance drift detection.⏎HHS should prioritize longitudinal studies and methodological research focused on identifying,⏎measuring, and mitigating performance degradation over time.⏎● Encourage independent and profession-led validation models.⏎Third-party or profession-led testing frameworks can complement vendor-reported performance⏎claims and strengthen trust in AI tools used in patient care.⏎● Support development of standardized documentation and reporting practices.⏎Clear expectations for performance reporting, update tracking, and adverse event⏎documentation will improve transparency and accountability.⏎By strengthening validation standards, promoting clinically meaningful metrics, and investing in lifecycle⏎monitoring infrastructure, HHS can ensure that AI adoption in clinical care remains evidence-based,⏎transparent, and aligned with patient safety.⏎(v) How can HHS support private-sector accreditation, certification, and industry-driven⏎testing efforts to accelerate the responsible adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Private-sector organizations, including professional societies, standards bodies, and accreditation⏎entities, play an essential role in translating emerging technologies into safe clinical practice. In⏎domains such as medical imaging and radiation therapy, profession-led guidance, commissioning⏎standards, and quality assurance frameworks have historically complemented federal oversight and⏎enabled consistent implementation across diverse healthcare settings. As AI tools become more⏎integrated into clinical workflows, similar profession-led and industry-supported assurance mechanisms⏎will be critical to maintaining safety, consistency, and public trust. Federal policy can accelerate⏎5⏎⏎responsible adoption not by duplicating these efforts, but by recognizing, aligning with, and⏎strengthening them.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎● Recognize and align with profession-led standards and guidance for AI commissioning,⏎validation, and lifecycle oversight.⏎Federal expectations should complement rather than conflict with existing clinical quality⏎frameworks developed by professional societies and accreditation bodies.⏎● Support development of accreditation and certification pathways for professionals⏎responsible for AI oversight.⏎Individuals charged with commissioning, monitoring, and governance of AI systems should have⏎clearly defined competencies and training standards.⏎● Encourage independent testing and benchmarking initiatives through public–private⏎partnerships.⏎Transparent evaluation frameworks can strengthen trust in AI tools without requiring disclosure⏎of proprietary source code.⏎● Promote coordination between HHS agencies and accreditation organizations.⏎Reducing duplicative compliance requirements and harmonizing governance expectations will⏎ease administrative burden while preserving accountability.⏎● Provide grant or cooperative agreement funding to support shared testing infrastructure⏎and governance toolkits.⏎Resources that can be adopted by institutions of varying size and capacity will help ensure⏎equitable and consistent implementation.⏎By supporting private-sector assurance activities and aligning federal policy with established clinical⏎quality frameworks, HHS can strengthen accountability while enabling scalable and responsible AI⏎adoption across the healthcare system.⏎(vi) Where have AI tools in clinical care met expectations, where have they fallen short,⏎and what lessons should inform future policy and research priorities?⏎AI tools have demonstrated measurable benefits in several clinical domains, particularly when deployed⏎in well-defined use cases and paired with appropriate human oversight. In medical imaging and⏎radiation oncology, AI applications have shown value in image enhancement, automated segmentation,⏎workflow prioritization, quality assurance support, and reduction of repetitive manual tasks. These⏎applications can improve efficiency, increase consistency, and reduce certain types of human error⏎when integrated thoughtfully into established clinical processes.⏎However, AI tools have fallen short in situations where validation has been limited, generalizability has⏎not been adequately assessed, or workflow integration has been insufficiently planned. Performance⏎may degrade when deployed across different patient populations, equipment platforms, or institutional⏎settings. In some cases, overly optimistic performance claims have not translated into measurable⏎improvements in patient outcomes or safety. These gaps underscore the importance of robust⏎validation, ongoing monitoring, and realistic expectations regarding the capabilities and limitations of AI⏎systems.⏎Economic evaluation also remains underdeveloped. While AI tools may improve workflow efficiency or⏎reduce variability, the long-term return on investment, cost-effectiveness, and impact on patient⏎6⏎⏎outcomes are not always systematically measured. Policymakers and healthcare institutions require⏎clearer evidence regarding value to support sustainable adoption.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎● Prioritize funding for real-world performance and outcomes research.⏎HHS should support longitudinal studies evaluating AI tools across diverse clinical environments⏎to assess generalizability, safety, and measurable impact on patient outcomes.⏎● Expand research on economic evaluation and cost-effectiveness.⏎Federal programs should encourage rigorous assessment of return on investment, workflow⏎impact, and resource utilization associated with AI-enabled care.⏎● Support comparative effectiveness research.⏎Studies comparing AI-enabled workflows with optimized non-AI workflows will help clarify where⏎AI provides meaningful clinical advantage.⏎● Encourage dissemination of lessons learned from both successful and unsuccessful⏎deployments.⏎Structured mechanisms for sharing implementation experiences, including challenges and⏎limitations, will strengthen collective learning and reduce duplication of effort.⏎● Invest in implementation science focused on workflow integration and human–AI⏎interaction.⏎Understanding how AI tools influence clinician behavior, decision-making, and system-level⏎processes is essential to realizing safe and effective adoption.⏎By grounding AI policy in real-world evidence, economic evaluation, and implementation science, HHS⏎can promote innovation that delivers measurable clinical value while maintaining patient safety and⏎public trust.⏎(vii) Which stakeholders and decision-makers influence the adoption of AI in clinical⏎care, and what organizational or administrative barriers limit effective integration?⏎The adoption of AI in clinical care is shaped not only by regulatory and reimbursement policy, but also⏎by internal healthcare governance structures. Decisions to procure, deploy, and monitor AI tools⏎typically involve clinical leadership, information technology departments, compliance and privacy⏎officers, quality and safety committees, and technical subject matter experts such as medical physicists.⏎In safety-critical areas such as imaging and radiation oncology, AI tools must be integrated into existing⏎quality assurance programs and clinical workflows to ensure safe and effective use.⏎Organizational fragmentation is a common barrier. AI deployment responsibilities are often distributed⏎across departments without clear ownership of lifecycle oversight. Institutions may lack defined⏎governance structures to oversee commissioning, performance monitoring, version control, and⏎incident response. Smaller or resource-limited healthcare settings may also lack dedicated personnel⏎with expertise in AI evaluation and quality management. Without structured governance, adoption may⏎be inconsistent, reactive, or insufficiently documented.⏎Workflow integration presents additional challenges. AI tools that are technically sound may fail to⏎deliver value if they disrupt clinical processes, create parallel workflows, or introduce ambiguity⏎regarding clinician accountability. Effective implementation requires multidisciplinary collaboration and⏎clearly defined escalation pathways.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎7⏎⏎● Develop model governance frameworks for healthcare institutions adopting AI.⏎HHS could support creation of template oversight structures, including AI governance⏎committees, commissioning protocols, documentation standards, and escalation pathways to⏎guide consistent institutional practice.⏎● Support workforce training and interdisciplinary collaboration.⏎Federal initiatives should promote training programs that bring together clinicians, physicists,⏎data scientists, and compliance professionals to strengthen institutional oversight capacity.⏎● Fund implementation science research focused on workflow integration.⏎Research evaluating how AI tools interact with clinical processes, staffing models, and⏎decision-making pathways will help identify best practices for safe deployment.⏎● Provide technical assistance resources for small and rural healthcare institutions.⏎Equitable adoption requires that resource-limited settings have access to guidance and⏎infrastructure support for safe AI integration.⏎● Encourage incorporation of AI governance into existing quality and patient safety⏎programs.⏎Integrating AI oversight into established quality assurance systems will reduce duplication and⏎reinforce accountability.⏎By supporting structured governance models and interdisciplinary collaboration, HHS can help⏎healthcare organizations integrate AI in a manner that strengthens safety, accountability, and clinical⏎effectiveness.⏎(viii) What data infrastructure, interoperability standards, or information-sharing⏎approaches are needed to accelerate AI development and adoption in clinical care while⏎protecting privacy and security?⏎AI development and responsible deployment in clinical care depend on access to high-quality, diverse,⏎and well-annotated data. However, healthcare data remain fragmented across institutions, vendors, and⏎systems, often stored in incompatible formats or subject to inconsistent metadata standards. In imaging⏎and radiation oncology, while standards such as DICOM provide a foundation for data exchange,⏎variability in implementation, annotation practices, and linkage to structured clinical data can limit⏎scalability and cross-institutional validation.⏎Interoperability challenges affect not only model development but also lifecycle oversight. Effective AI⏎governance requires access to performance logs, version histories, audit trails, and contextual⏎metadata that allow institutions to evaluate how models behave across different environments. Without⏎harmonized standards for these data elements, benchmarking and post-deployment monitoring⏎become difficult.⏎Privacy and security considerations further complicate information sharing. Institutions must balance⏎the need for diverse training and validation datasets with compliance obligations and protection of⏎patient information. Smaller institutions may lack the infrastructure needed to participate in collaborative⏎validation efforts, exacerbating disparities in AI development and adoption.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎● Advance harmonized interoperability standards for imaging, structured clinical data, and⏎performance metadata.⏎HHS should support refinement and consistent implementation of standards that enable linkage⏎8⏎⏎of imaging data, clinical context, audit logs, and versioning information necessary for AI⏎validation and monitoring.⏎● Support federated and privacy-preserving data-sharing models.⏎Federal investment in federated learning frameworks and secure data enclaves can enable⏎multi-institutional collaboration without requiring centralized data transfer.⏎● Invest in shared benchmarking and validation infrastructure.⏎National or regional AI evaluation hubs and shared testbeds can reduce disparities between⏎large academic centers and smaller institutions while promoting objective comparison of AI⏎tools.⏎● Strengthen cybersecurity and secure deployment guidance for AI systems.⏎Clear expectations regarding secure integration, access controls, and model integrity will protect⏎patient data and reinforce trust in AI-enabled care.⏎● Encourage integration across data commons and research repositories.⏎Linking imaging data commons with electronic health record–based repositories will improve⏎generalizability and support real-world performance evaluation across diverse populations.⏎By strengthening interoperability, promoting secure data-sharing models, and investing in shared⏎infrastructure, HHS can accelerate AI innovation while protecting patient privacy and supporting⏎equitable adoption across healthcare settings.⏎(ix) What challenges do patients and caregivers wish AI to address in clinical care, and⏎what concerns should inform federal policy as AI adoption accelerates?⏎Patients and caregivers generally seek healthcare technologies that improve safety, access, timeliness,⏎and consistency of care. AI has the potential to reduce diagnostic delays, decrease variability in⏎treatment planning, enhance quality assurance, and support more efficient clinical workflows. In⏎resource-constrained environments, AI may also help extend expertise to underserved or rural⏎communities, potentially reducing disparities in access to high-quality care.⏎At the same time, patients have legitimate concerns regarding transparency, bias, accountability, and⏎data use. Many individuals may not understand when or how AI tools are contributing to their care,⏎raising questions about informed consent and explainability. Concerns about algorithmic bias are⏎particularly salient, especially if training datasets do not adequately represent diverse patient⏎populations. Patients may also worry about data privacy, security, and the potential for misuse of health⏎information in AI development or deployment.⏎Trust depends not only on technological performance, but also on clear communication and sustained⏎human oversight. Patients generally expect that clinicians remain accountable for care decisions, even⏎when supported by AI systems. Ensuring that AI tools augment rather than replace professional⏎judgment is critical to maintaining public confidence.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎● Promote transparency regarding AI use in clinical care.⏎HHS should encourage clear communication standards that inform patients when AI tools⏎contribute to diagnosis, treatment planning, or workflow decisions in meaningful ways.⏎● Support research on bias mitigation and equitable performance.⏎Federal funding should prioritize evaluation of AI performance across diverse demographic⏎groups and care settings to reduce disparities and strengthen fairness.⏎9⏎⏎● Encourage human-in-the-loop governance models.⏎Policy frameworks should reinforce the expectation that clinicians retain ultimate responsibility⏎for patient care decisions, with AI functioning as a decision-support tool.⏎● Strengthen guidance on data privacy and secondary data use in AI development.⏎Clarifying permissible uses and safeguards will enhance patient confidence while supporting⏎innovation.⏎● Develop patient-centered educational resources.⏎HHS can support the creation of accessible materials that explain AI capabilities, limitations,and⏎safeguards to improve public understanding.⏎By proactively addressing transparency, equity, and accountability, federal policy can strengthen patient⏎trust while enabling responsible AI adoption that advances safety and quality of care.⏎(x) What research gaps must be addressed to accelerate the safe, effective, and⏎equitable adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎While AI development in healthcare has progressed rapidly, important evidence gaps remain in⏎real-world performance evaluation, lifecycle governance, human–AI interaction, and economic impact.⏎Many AI tools are validated using retrospective datasets under controlled conditions, yet limited⏎evidence exists regarding long-term performance across diverse institutions, equipment platforms, and⏎patient populations. Without rigorous real-world evidence, policymakers and healthcare organizations⏎may struggle to distinguish between tools that offer measurable clinical benefit and those that primarily⏎improve workflow efficiency without affecting outcomes.⏎Research on lifecycle management is similarly underdeveloped. As AI systems evolve through software⏎updates or retraining, institutions require evidence-based guidance on when revalidation is necessary⏎and how performance drift should be detected and mitigated. Standardized methodologies for⏎monitoring AI behavior over time remain in early stages of development.⏎Human–AI interaction represents another critical gap. AI tools may alter clinician decision-making⏎patterns, workload distribution, and escalation practices in ways that are not fully understood.⏎Evaluating how clinicians interpret AI outputs, manage uncertainty, and respond to algorithmic⏎recommendations is essential to ensuring safety and accountability.⏎Economic evaluation is also needed. Healthcare systems require clearer evidence regarding⏎cost-effectiveness, return on investment, and impact on resource utilization. Without systematic⏎economic analysis, adoption decisions may be driven by short-term efficiency gains rather than⏎long-term patient benefit.⏎AAPM recommends the following federal actions:⏎● Prioritize funding for real-world performance and generalizability studies.⏎HHS should support longitudinal, multi-institutional research evaluating AI tools across diverse⏎patient populations and care environments.⏎● Invest in lifecycle monitoring and performance drift methodologies.⏎Research focused on update management, retraining triggers, and standardized revalidation⏎protocols will strengthen long-term oversight.⏎● Expand support for human–AI interaction and implementation science research.⏎Understanding how AI tools influence clinician behavior and workflow integration is essential to⏎maximizing safety and effectiveness.⏎10⏎⏎● Support rigorous economic evaluation studies.⏎Federal programs should fund cost-effectiveness analyses and return-on-investment studies to⏎inform sustainable adoption decisions.⏎● Encourage development of shared benchmarks and reporting standards.⏎Common evaluation frameworks will promote transparency and comparability across AI tools⏎and healthcare settings.⏎By addressing these research gaps through coordinated federal investment, HHS can build the⏎evidence base necessary to support safe, accountable, and value-driven integration of AI into clinical⏎care.⏎For additional information or questions, please contact Lauren DePutter, AAPM’s Director of⏎Government Affairs and External Relations, at lauren@aapm.org .⏎11♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0250♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Overjet , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Overjet♔♔♔♔♔♔OVERJET, INC.⏎367 Boylston Street, Boston, MA 02116⏎www.overjet.com⏎February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎ASTP/ONC⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Dear Mr. Posnack:⏎Overjet, Inc. appreciates the opportunity to respond to this Request for Information on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. Overjet is a dental⏎artificial intelligence company headquartered in Boston, Massachusetts, that develops FDA-⏎cleared AI solutions for the analysis of dental radiographic images. Our products are deployed⏎across dental service organizations (DSOs), insurance carriers, individual dental practices, and⏎government programs, where they assist clinicians in identifying dental pathology and⏎supporting evidence-based treatment decisions. To date, Overjet’s AI has been used to analyze⏎tens of millions of dental radiographic images across thousands of practice locations⏎nationwide.⏎This response draws on Overjet’s direct experience as a developer, deployer, and continuous⏎evaluator of clinical AI tools in one of the most widely practiced yet frequently overlooked⏎domains of health care: dentistry. The perspectives offered here are informed by the expertise⏎of our clinical and regulatory leadership, including prior federal service at the Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services, the Food and Drug Administration, and the National Institutes of⏎Health.⏎We commend HHS for framing this RFI around three actionable policy levers—regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development—and for explicitly seeking experience-based⏎input from AI developers and clinical users. Our comments address each of these domains and⏎respond to specific questions posed in the RFI, with an emphasis on dental AI as a critical and⏎underrepresented use case in national AI policy. Overjet is also submitting a separate response⏎to the Diagnostic Imaging Interoperability RFI (RIN 0955–AA11), and we respectfully request⏎that the two submissions be read as complementary.⏎Summary of Key Recommendations⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 1⏎⏎Overjet respectfully urges HHS to consider the following priority actions as it develops policy to⏎accelerate AI in clinical care:⏎• Create reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted dental diagnostics through new or add-on⏎CPT/CDT codes and inclusion of AI-enabled quality metrics in value-based payment models,⏎including Medicare Advantage Star Ratings.⏎• Support independent, university-based dental AI evaluation infrastructure through competitive⏎research grants, benchmarking datasets, and cooperative agreements—moving beyond vendor-⏎driven validation to build public trust and clinical evidence.⏎• Mandate inclusion of dental data classes in the United States Core Data for Interoperability⏎(USCDI) and support interoperability standards for dental imaging to break down medical-dental⏎data silos that limit AI’s potential to improve systemic health outcomes.⏎• Prioritize federal research investment in medical-dental AI integration, AI-enabled quality⏎measurement, health equity in dental AI, and longitudinal outcomes research.⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 2⏎⏎I. Regulation⏎Question 1: Biggest Barriers to Private Sector Innovation in AI for Health Care⏎Overjet identifies three primary barriers to dental AI innovation and adoption:⏎Absence of dental-specific considerations in AI policy. Federal AI policy discussions—⏎including FDA’s AI/ML-enabled device framework, ONC’s certification criteria, and CMS’s⏎coverage determinations—have been developed primarily around hospital-based and physician-⏎oriented use cases. Dentistry, which accounts for approximately 200,000 practicing clinicians in⏎the United States, operates in a distinct clinical environment with different imaging modalities⏎(intraoral periapical radiographs, bitewing radiographs, panoramic images, cone-beam⏎computed tomography), different practice structures (predominantly small private practices and⏎mid-size DSOs rather than large health systems), and different electronic health record⏎ecosystems. AI policy that does not account for these differences risks creating frameworks that⏎are poorly suited to dental care—or that inadvertently exclude dental AI from the benefits of⏎federal support.⏎Lack of trusted benchmarks and evaluation infrastructure. There is currently no nationally⏎recognized benchmarking framework for dental AI performance. Unlike radiology, where⏎organizations such as the American College of Radiology have established AI validation⏎processes, dental AI lacks equivalent infrastructure for independent evaluation, post-market⏎surveillance, and comparative assessment. This gap creates uncertainty for purchasers and⏎regulators alike and depresses adoption. HHS could catalyze progress by supporting the⏎development of dental AI benchmarking standards, potentially through NIST collaboration or⏎through competitive grants to academic dental institutions.⏎Question 2: Regulatory, Payment Policy, or Programmatic Design Changes HHS⏎Should Prioritize⏎Overjet recommends four priority actions:⏎First, CMS should develop reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted dental diagnostics.⏎Currently, there is no Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) code, Current Dental Terminology⏎(CDT) code, or Healthcare Common Procedure Coding System (HCPCS) code that captures⏎the distinct clinical value of AI-assisted radiographic analysis in dentistry. Overjet’s products⏎assist clinicians in identifying caries, measuring bone levels, and supporting treatment⏎planning—functions that improve diagnostic accuracy and reduce inter-examiner variability.⏎These contributions have measurable value that is not captured by existing coding structures.⏎We recommend that CMS work with the American Dental Association and AI developers to⏎establish appropriate coding and coverage mechanisms. Options include a new CDT code for⏎AI-assisted radiographic interpretation, an add-on code to existing diagnostic imaging codes⏎(D0220, D0230, D0330), or inclusion of AI-assisted diagnostics in value-based dental payment⏎models.⏎Second, HHS should ensure that Medicare Advantage organizations’ dental benefits are⏎evaluated for quality and adequacy in a manner that accounts for AI-enabled quality⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 3⏎⏎measurement. Recent analyses indicate that approximately 4.7 million Medicare Advantage⏎beneficiaries have been affected by the erosion of supplemental dental benefits as plans have⏎reduced coverage while continuing to market dental access as an enrollment incentive. This⏎pattern highlights a structural gap: there is no quality measurement infrastructure in dental care⏎that would allow CMS, beneficiaries, or plans themselves to objectively assess whether the⏎dental care being delivered meets clinical standards. AI-assisted radiographic analysis can⏎provide precisely this capability—objective, consistent measurement of diagnostic and treatment⏎quality across large patient populations. We recommend that CMS explore the integration of AI-⏎derived dental quality metrics into the Medicare Advantage Star Ratings framework or as a⏎condition of supplemental benefit adequacy determinations. Without quality measurement, there⏎is no mechanism to distinguish plans that deliver meaningful dental care from those that offer⏎nominal benefits with limited clinical value.⏎Third, HHS should address the regulatory gap in dental AI by ensuring that FDA’s evolving⏎Predetermined Change Control Plan framework, the total product lifecycle approach for AI/ML-⏎enabled devices, and any forthcoming AI regulatory guidance explicitly include dental imaging⏎AI within their scope. Dental AI products face the same continuous learning and adaptation⏎challenges as other clinical AI tools, but the dental use case is rarely addressed in regulatory⏎guidance documents. Explicit inclusion would provide dental AI developers with regulatory⏎predictability and reduce the uncertainty that currently slows innovation. See 21 CFR Part 892⏎(Radiology) for relevant device classification frameworks.⏎Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical Device AI⏎The dental AI market presents a particularly acute illustration of the governance challenges⏎identified in this question. Dental practices are predominantly small businesses with limited⏎legal, compliance, and IT infrastructure. When a dental practice acquires an AI tool—whether⏎FDA-cleared or not—questions of clinical liability, data governance, and informed consent are⏎often unaddressed by existing practice policies.⏎HHS can play a constructive role by issuing model guidance for dental practices on the clinical⏎governance of AI tools, including recommended practices for informed consent, clinician⏎override documentation, and adverse event reporting. We note that liability frameworks should⏎distinguish between AI tools that have undergone FDA clearance (and thus have established⏎intended uses, contraindications, and performance characteristics) and those that have not.⏎This distinction is critical to establishing proportionate accountability structures.⏎Additionally, HIPAA’s current framework was not designed with AI-mediated image analysis in⏎mind. When a dental image is transmitted to a cloud-based AI system for analysis, questions⏎arise about the classification of AI-generated annotations (are they part of the patient’s⏎designated record set?), the scope of business associate agreements, and the application of the⏎minimum necessary standard to image data. We recommend three specific actions: (1) that the⏎HHS Office for Civil Rights issue a FAQ specifically addressing AI-mediated clinical image⏎analysis under HIPAA, including guidance on whether AI-generated annotations constitute⏎protected health information within the designated record set; (2) that OCR develop model⏎business associate agreement language for cloud-based clinical AI services; and (3) that HHS⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 4⏎⏎clarify the applicability of the minimum necessary standard to image data transmitted for AI⏎analysis, recognizing that clinical images inherently require full transmission for accurate⏎interpretation.⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 5⏎⏎II. Reimbursement⏎Question 5: Supporting Private Sector Accreditation, Certification, and Credentialing⏎HHS can most effectively support private sector AI governance activities by establishing a clear⏎federal baseline against which voluntary programs can be measured. In the dental AI context,⏎this means:⏎• Recognizing FDA clearance as the foundational tier of AI validation for products that⏎analyze dental images, and communicating this standard clearly to payers, state⏎regulators, and dental practices.⏎• Supporting the development of dental AI certification programs within existing⏎accreditation bodies—such as the Commission on Dental Accreditation (CODA) or the⏎Joint Commission—that go beyond FDA clearance to assess ongoing performance,⏎clinical integration, and patient safety.⏎• Fostering models of independent, university-based clinical AI evaluation funded through⏎competitive research grants. The emerging model of industry-funded, university-⏎conducted competitive research—in which academic dental institutions apply for grants⏎to independently evaluate and validate AI tools—offers a promising pathway for building⏎clinical evidence that is credible, reproducible, and free from vendor bias. HHS could⏎support this model through AHRQ, NIDCR, or HRSA mechanisms, or through⏎cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs) that pair AI developers⏎with academic research centers.⏎• Encouraging dental schools to incorporate AI literacy into predoctoral and residency⏎curricula, so that the next generation of dentists is equipped to evaluate, implement, and⏎oversee AI tools in practice. HHS could support this through HRSA training grants or⏎through updates to CODA accreditation standards.⏎Question 6: Where AI Tools Have Met or Exceeded Expectations⏎Overjet’s deployments across dental service organizations and insurance carriers provide direct⏎evidence of AI’s capacity to improve clinical outcomes while reducing costs. Our AI has been⏎used to analyze tens of millions of dental radiographic images, providing a substantial real-world⏎evidence base:⏎Diagnostic consistency. In multi-provider dental organizations, AI-assisted radiographic⏎analysis has measurably reduced inter-examiner variability in caries detection and bone loss⏎measurement. Published literature demonstrates that inter-examiner agreement in dental⏎radiographic interpretation is historically low, with kappa values often below 0.5 for caries⏎detection. Overjet’s AI provides a consistent, objective reference point that has been shown to⏎narrow this variability, leading to more consistent treatment recommendations across clinicians⏎and practice locations. In deployed settings, practices using Overjet’s AI have reported⏎meaningful improvements in diagnostic agreement rates across providers.⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 6⏎⏎Utilization management. Dental insurance carriers using Overjet’s AI for claims review have⏎reported significant improvements in the accuracy and efficiency of radiographic review,⏎including reduced reliance on manual clinical review for routine claims and the identification of⏎billing patterns inconsistent with radiographic findings. These efficiencies directly reduce⏎administrative costs while improving the clinical integrity of claims adjudication.⏎Quality measurement at scale. AI-assisted analysis enables the objective measurement of⏎clinical quality across large patient populations—something previously impossible in dental care,⏎where quality measurement has been limited to process measures and patient surveys rather⏎than clinical outcome assessment. This capability represents a transformative advance: for the⏎first time, payers and health systems can assess whether the diagnostic and treatment⏎decisions made across their networks are consistent with radiographic evidence, enabling data-⏎driven quality improvement at a population level.⏎Where AI has fallen short of expectations, the primary challenges have been related to⏎integration complexity rather than analytical performance. Dental practices use a fragmented⏎ecosystem of practice management systems, imaging software, and clinical workflows. The⏎technical burden of integrating AI tools into these workflows remains significant, particularly for⏎small practices. HHS could address this by supporting interoperability standards for dental⏎imaging (as addressed in the companion Diagnostic Imaging Interoperability RFI, RIN 0955–⏎AA11) and by incentivizing practice management software vendors to adopt open integration⏎standards.⏎Question 7: Decision Makers and Administrative Hurdles to AI Adoption⏎In dental care, AI adoption decisions are influenced by a distinct set of stakeholders compared⏎to hospital-based health care:⏎• In dental service organizations, clinical leadership (typically chief dental officers) and⏎operations leadership jointly evaluate AI tools based on clinical value, workflow impact,⏎and return on investment.⏎• In independent dental practices, the practicing dentist is typically the sole decision⏎maker, and adoption decisions are heavily influenced by peer recommendations, dental⏎society endorsements, and perceived ease of integration.⏎• In dental insurance, chief dental officers and medical directors evaluate AI tools for⏎claims review and utilization management based on accuracy, regulatory compliance,⏎and operational efficiency.⏎The primary administrative hurdles in dental AI adoption include the lack of reimbursement⏎(clinicians cannot bill for AI-assisted analysis), the cost of integration with existing practice⏎management systems, uncertainty about regulatory requirements, and the absence of⏎professional society guidance on AI implementation. Each of these is amenable to federal policy⏎intervention through the mechanisms described throughout this response.⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 7⏎⏎III. Research & Development⏎Question 4: Promising AI Evaluation Methods⏎Overjet’s experience across tens of millions of dental image analyses has demonstrated the⏎importance of several evaluation approaches:⏎Pre-deployment clinical validation against expert consensus panels. Our FDA clearance⏎process involved validation studies comparing AI performance to panels of licensed dental⏎specialists. This approach—using independent, multi-examiner expert consensus as the⏎reference standard rather than single-examiner ground truth—is critical in dental imaging where⏎inter-examiner variability is well-documented.⏎Post-deployment performance monitoring with demographic stratification. We⏎continuously monitor our AI’s performance across patient populations, practice types, and⏎imaging equipment to identify and address performance variations. This is particularly important⏎in dental care, where imaging quality varies significantly between practices and between digital⏎sensor manufacturers.⏎Workflow integration assessment. Technical accuracy alone is insufficient. We evaluate how⏎AI outputs are used by clinicians in practice—including rates of clinician agreement, override⏎patterns, and impacts on treatment planning. These human-centered metrics are essential for⏎understanding real-world clinical value.⏎HHS should support the development of these evaluation methods through competitive grants⏎to academic dental institutions, cooperative agreements with industry, and the creation of dental⏎AI-specific evaluation datasets. Prize competitions, as mentioned in the RFI, could be⏎particularly effective for encouraging innovation in dental AI validation methodologies.⏎Question 8: Where Enhanced Interoperability Would Accelerate AI Development⏎Enhanced interoperability would profoundly accelerate dental AI development and deployment.⏎Three specific areas merit priority attention:⏎Dental imaging standards. Dental radiographic images are frequently stored in proprietary⏎formats within practice management systems, with no standardized exchange pathway. While⏎medical imaging benefits from mature DICOM infrastructure, dental imaging often lacks⏎consistent DICOM implementation. The adoption of standardized image formats and exchange⏎protocols for dental imaging would expand the data available for AI development and enable⏎interoperable deployment across practice management systems. We address this in greater⏎detail in our response to the Diagnostic Imaging Interoperability RFI (RIN 0955–AA11).⏎Clinical data integration across medical and dental records. Dental findings have significant⏎implications for systemic health—including the detection of carotid artery calcifications on⏎panoramic radiographs, oral manifestations of diabetes and autoimmune conditions, and⏎medication-related oral health risks such as xerostomia. AI tools that bridge medical and dental⏎data require interoperable data standards that currently do not exist. The inclusion of dental⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 8⏎⏎data classes in the United States Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) would be a critical step.⏎Preliminary analyses suggest that systematic AI-enabled screening of existing dental panoramic⏎radiographs for incidental findings such as carotid artery calcifications could identify individuals⏎at elevated cardiovascular risk at a fraction of the cost of dedicated screening programs—⏎representing a significant opportunity for early intervention and cost savings that is currently lost⏎due to the medical-dental data divide.⏎Benchmarking datasets. The development of curated, de-identified dental imaging datasets⏎with expert-validated annotations would enable independent benchmarking of dental AI⏎products, comparative effectiveness research, and the training of next-generation AI models.⏎HHS could support this through NIH or NIDCR funding mechanisms.⏎Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Perspectives on AI⏎Patients and caregivers stand to benefit substantially from dental AI in several ways. Chief⏎among these is the problem of diagnostic variability: it is well-documented that patients can⏎receive significantly different diagnoses and treatment recommendations depending on which⏎dentist they visit, a phenomenon that has been the subject of investigative journalism and⏎academic research alike. AI-assisted analysis provides an objective, evidence-based reference⏎point that can reduce unwarranted treatment variation and give patients greater confidence that⏎recommended treatments are consistent with radiographic findings.⏎Additional patient benefits include visual explanations of dental conditions that improve health⏎literacy and informed consent, quality assurance that treatment recommendations are evidence-⏎based, and the potential for AI-enabled second opinions that are accessible and affordable.⏎These benefits are particularly important for vulnerable populations—including elderly patients,⏎individuals with limited English proficiency, and those in underserved communities—who may⏎have fewer resources to evaluate whether recommended treatments are appropriate.⏎Patient concerns about dental AI center on data privacy (particularly for images of their face and⏎teeth), the fear that AI will replace clinical judgment rather than augment it, and the lack of⏎transparency about when and how AI is being used in their care. HHS can address these⏎concerns by encouraging the development of patient-facing AI disclosure standards, supporting⏎patient education initiatives, and ensuring that AI tools are deployed as clinical decision support⏎rather than autonomous diagnostic systems.⏎Question 10: Priority Areas for AI Research⏎Overjet recommends that HHS prioritize AI research in the following areas:⏎• Medical-dental AI integration: Research on AI systems that bridge dental imaging⏎findings with systemic health conditions, including the use of dental panoramic⏎radiographs for screening of carotid artery calcifications, osteoporosis, and airway⏎abnormalities. The return on investment for these applications is potentially substantial:⏎dental panoramic radiographs are already acquired as part of routine dental care for⏎millions of Americans annually, and AI-enabled analysis of these existing images for⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 9⏎⏎incidental systemic findings could enable population-level screening at minimal marginal⏎cost.⏎• AI-enabled quality measurement in dental care: Development and validation of AI-⏎derived quality measures for dental care that can be used in value-based payment⏎programs, accreditation, and public reporting. This research is foundational to⏎modernizing dental payment systems and ensuring accountability for clinical quality.⏎• Health equity in dental AI: Research on the performance and impact of dental AI⏎across diverse patient populations, including evaluation of potential algorithmic bias⏎related to demographic factors, imaging equipment variations, and practice settings.⏎• Longitudinal outcomes research: Studies examining how AI-assisted dental⏎diagnostics affect patient outcomes over time, including the impact on disease⏎progression, treatment appropriateness, and health care costs.⏎• Medication-related oral health impacts: Research on AI-driven risk assessment tools⏎that analyze patient medication profiles to identify individuals at elevated risk for oral⏎health complications such as xerostomia, which affects millions of Americans taking⏎common medications and significantly increases the risk of caries, periodontal disease,⏎and oral infections.⏎Published findings on dental AI indicate that these tools can achieve performance comparable⏎to or exceeding specialist-level diagnosis for certain conditions, including caries detection and⏎periodontal bone loss measurement. However, the literature on real-world clinical impact—as⏎distinct from analytical performance—remains limited. HHS should prioritize implementation⏎science research that evaluates dental AI in practice settings rather than laboratory conditions⏎alone.⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 10⏎⏎Conclusion⏎Overjet commends HHS for undertaking this comprehensive inquiry into accelerating AI in⏎clinical care. We urge the Department to ensure that dental AI is explicitly included in the⏎regulatory frameworks, reimbursement pathways, and research agendas that emerge from this⏎process. Dentistry serves the vast majority of Americans, involves complex diagnostic imaging,⏎and stands to benefit enormously from responsible AI deployment—yet it has been largely⏎absent from federal AI policy discussions to date.⏎The dental AI use case is not peripheral to the goals of this RFI—it is central. Dental care is one⏎of the few clinical domains where AI has already been deployed at scale, subjected to FDA⏎review, integrated into routine clinical workflows, and demonstrated measurable impact on⏎diagnostic quality and cost efficiency. The lessons learned from dental AI deployment can⏎inform broader federal AI policy, and the barriers facing dental AI are illustrative of the⏎challenges that will confront clinical AI adoption across all specialties.⏎Overjet is prepared to serve as a resource to HHS as it develops policy in this domain. We bring⏎the perspective of a company that has navigated FDA clearance, deployed AI across multiple⏎market channels, and continuously evaluated real-world performance—and we are led by⏎individuals with deep experience in federal health policy, dental clinical practice, and AI⏎development.⏎We welcome the opportunity to discuss these comments further and to provide additional⏎evidence or technical detail as requested.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Natalia Chalmers, DDS, MHSc, PhD⏎Chief Dental Officer and Head of Clinical Innovation⏎Overjet, Inc.⏎367 Boylston Street, Boston, MA 02116⏎Overjet, Inc. | Page 11♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0251♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Alsabti, Sam, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sam♔Alsabti♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment on “HHS Health Sector AI RFI” | Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎February 22nd, 2026⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13; Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001)⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and ASTP/ONC Leadership,⏎I appreciate the opportunity to respond to the Department’s Request for Information on⏎accelerating AI adoption in clinical care. I write as a practicing physician, clinical researcher, and⏎the founder of an AI-powered clinical reasoning platform, a combination that gives me a⏎perspective I believe is underrepresented in policy conversations about health AI.⏎I am a cardiology fellow in Southeast Michigan who has spent the past decade inside the clinical⏎training pipeline, from medical school through internal medicine residency to subspecialty⏎fellowship. I have passed board examinations with the American Board of Internal Medicine and⏎Certification Board of Nuclear Cardiology, and continue to train full-time while simultaneously⏎building and shipping an AI clinical reasoning platform. My comments below draw on both my⏎experience as a clinician navigating the healthcare system’s inefficiencies and my experience as a⏎technologist building AI tools to address them.⏎I. The Clinical Reasoning Gap as a Root Cause of Healthcare Waste⏎The Department’s RFI asks where AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve⏎healthcare outcomes and reduce costs (Question 5). I want to draw attention to a root cause that⏎is rarely discussed in AI policy: the systematic failure in teaching clinical reasoning frameworks⏎in medical education, and its direct downstream impact on clinical waste.⏎During my internal medicine residency, I led quality improvement research examining⏎echocardiogram utilization among medical residents for syncope evaluation at our institution.⏎The findings were striking: approximately 13% of echocardiogram orders for syncope we⏎reviewed met ACC/AHA guideline criteria. The remaining 87% represented unnecessary testing,⏎studies that added cost, consumed sonographer time, delayed care for patients with genuine⏎indications, and occasionally produced incidental findings that triggered further unnecessary⏎workups.⏎Page 1⏎⏎Comment on “HHS Health Sector AI RFI” | Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎This was not an isolated finding. Our review of the literature indicated the pattern was systemic⏎across U.S. hospitals. The root cause was not physician laziness or incompetence. It was the⏎absence of structured clinical reasoning frameworks at the point of care. Physicians and advance⏎practice practitioners order echocardiograms for syncope because they were never systematically⏎trained to reason through when an echocardiogram is and is not indicated. They default to⏎pattern-matching and predefined EMR order sets (“syncope → cardiac workup”) rather than⏎applying guideline-based reasoning.⏎This example generalizes across specialties. Diagnostic errors remain the third leading cause of⏎death in the United States. Unnecessary testing drives an estimated $200 billion in annual waste.⏎The common thread is a gap in clinical reasoning: the cognitive process by which physicians⏎synthesize patient data, generate differential diagnoses, and make evidence-based decisions. This⏎gap begins in medical education and persists throughout a physician’s career.⏎II. AI-Powered Clinical Reasoning: From Education to Decision Support⏎I’ve been building Prosivo (https://prosivo.ai) to address this gap. The platform uses large⏎language models to guide medical students and practicing clinicians through realistic patient⏎cases via Socratic dialogue, the same method an expert attending physician would use at the⏎bedside. Rather than presenting static multiple-choice questions, it adapts to the learner’s level,⏎tracks their evolving differential diagnosis, and teaches structured clinical reasoning frameworks⏎in real time.⏎The architecture is intentionally designed as a two-stage pipeline:⏎1. Clinical Reasoning Education (live): An AI-powered Socratic tutor that works across⏎training levels (medical student through attending) and across health professions (MD,⏎nursing, PA). This product requires no FDA clearance, no EHR integration, and has short⏎institutional sales cycles.⏎2. Clinical Decision Support (in development): The same reasoning engine, deployed at⏎the point of care, guiding clinicians through guideline-based decision frameworks in real⏎time. This product will require regulatory clarity, EHR interoperability, and⏎reimbursement pathways — precisely the levers this RFI addresses.⏎The education product is not a stepping stone. It is the enabling infrastructure. It builds the⏎clinical reasoning dataset, validates the AI’s ability to guide clinical thinking, and establishes⏎institutional relationships that make clinical deployment possible. This architecture has direct⏎implications for all three of the Department’s focus areas.⏎III. Responses to Specific Questions⏎A. Barriers to Private Sector Innovation (Question 1)⏎Page 2⏎⏎Comment on “HHS Health Sector AI RFI” | Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎As a physician who has built and shipped an AI clinical tool while practicing full-time, I have⏎experienced the barriers firsthand:⏎• Regulatory ambiguity for non-device AI: The current framework draws a binary⏎distinction between “medical device” and “not a medical device.” Clinical reasoning⏎tools, like AI that helps clinicians think through differential diagnoses and guideline⏎application, occupy an uncertain middle ground. They are not diagnostic devices (they do⏎not produce a diagnosis), but they are more clinically consequential than administrative⏎tools. The lack of a clear regulatory category for “clinical reasoning support” creates⏎uncertainty that chills investment and slows development.⏎• The physician-founder gap: The people best positioned to identify clinical AI⏎opportunities, such as practicing physicians, face the highest barriers to building solutions.⏎Clinical training demands 60–80+ hour weeks with no flexibility for entrepreneurship.⏎There is no NIH mechanism, SBIR pathway, or HHS program specifically designed to⏎support physician-technologists who want to build clinical AI tools while maintaining⏎their practice. The result is that most health AI companies are founded by technologists⏎who have never practiced medicine, leading to tools that optimize for metrics that don’t⏎map to clinical workflow.⏎• Institutional procurement inertia: Medical schools and health systems lack⏎procurement frameworks for AI-powered education and clinical reasoning tools.⏎Traditional purchasing categories (textbooks, simulation equipment, EHR modules) don’t⏎accommodate AI software that sits between education and clinical decision support. HHS⏎could accelerate adoption by establishing model procurement guidelines for clinical AI⏎tools.⏎B. Evaluation Methods for Non-Device AI (Question 3)⏎For AI clinical reasoning tools that do not qualify as medical devices, I recommend an evaluation⏎framework with three components:⏎3. Clinical accuracy benchmarking: Does the AI’s reasoning align with published clinical⏎guidelines (ACC/AHA, ACS, ACEP, etc.)? This can be measured through structured case⏎libraries with known correct reasoning pathways, similar to how board examinations are⏎validated.⏎4. Learner outcome measurement: For education-stage AI tools, does exposure to the tool⏎improve clinical reasoning performance on standardized assessments? This is measurable⏎through pre/post testing, clinical skills examination scores, and longitudinal tracking of⏎diagnostic accuracy in practice.⏎5. Workflow integration assessment: For clinical-stage tools, does the AI reduce⏎unnecessary testing, improve guideline adherence, and integrate without disrupting⏎clinical workflow? These metrics are measurable through claims data, order pattern⏎analysis, and time-motion studies.⏎Page 3⏎⏎Comment on “HHS Health Sector AI RFI” | Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎HHS should consider supporting the development of standardized clinical reasoning⏎benchmarks, analogous to how USMLE validates medical knowledge, that AI tools can be⏎evaluated against. This would give health systems, payers, and regulators a common framework⏎for assessing clinical reasoning AI without requiring device-level regulatory oversight for non-⏎device tools.⏎C. Payment Policy and Reimbursement (Question 2)⏎The current fee-for-service model creates a paradox for clinical reasoning AI: the tools that⏎reduce unnecessary testing directly reduce the revenue that funds the institutions using them. A⏎physician who uses an AI-guided reasoning framework to determine that a syncope patient does⏎not need an echocardiogram generates less revenue than one who orders the test reflexively.⏎I recommend HHS consider the following reimbursement approaches:⏎• Value-based incentives for guideline adherence: CMS quality programs (MIPS, APMs)⏎should include specific measures for AI-assisted guideline adherence, creating positive⏎financial incentives for health systems that deploy clinical reasoning AI tools.⏎• Reimbursement codes for AI-augmented clinical reasoning: Just as CPT codes exist⏎for clinical decision support consultations, HHS should explore coding pathways for AI-⏎assisted clinical reasoning at the point of care — recognizing the cognitive work of⏎applying guideline-based frameworks rather than simply ordering additional tests.⏎• Training reimbursement pathways: GME funding mechanisms should recognize AI-⏎powered clinical reasoning training as a legitimate educational expense. If an AI tutor⏎demonstrably improves residents’ diagnostic reasoning, the institutions deploying it⏎should be able to account for this within their GME cost reporting.⏎D. High-Impact Areas for AI Research Investment (Question 5)⏎Based on my clinical research and product development experience, I believe the highest-impact⏎areas for HHS AI research investment are:⏎6. Clinical reasoning education at scale: AI that teaches the next generation of physicians⏎to reason through clinical problems, and not to just memorize facts, has multiplicative⏎downstream effects. Every physician who learns structured clinical reasoning through an⏎AI tutor carries that improved decision-making framework into decades of practice. The⏎return on investment is measured not in individual encounters but in career-long⏎practice patterns.⏎7. Guideline adherence at point of care: My echo/syncope research demonstrates that⏎guideline non-adherence is pervasive and costly. AI tools that integrate clinical practice⏎guidelines into the reasoning process at the point of ordering, not as alerts that clinicians⏎dismiss, but as reasoning frameworks that clinicians engage with, represent a high-value⏎R&D target.⏎Page 4⏎⏎Comment on “HHS Health Sector AI RFI” | Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎8. Cross-profession clinical reasoning infrastructure: Clinical reasoning is not unique to⏎physicians. Nurses, advanced practice providers, and other health professionals all make⏎clinical decisions that benefit from structured reasoning support. AI architectures that are⏎content-agnostic, where the reasoning engine generalizes across professions, offer⏎dramatically better economics than profession-specific tools. HHS should invest in⏎platforms, not point solutions.⏎E. Interoperability and Market Opportunities (Question 7)⏎Enhanced interoperability would significantly accelerate clinical reasoning AI. Specifically:⏎• Standardized learner performance data: There is no interoperability standard for⏎clinical reasoning performance data. If a medical student uses an AI reasoning tutor in⏎school and later becomes a resident using an AI decision support tool, there is no way to⏎connect those datasets. A FHIR-based standard for clinical reasoning competency data⏎would enable longitudinal tracking from education through practice.⏎• EHR-embedded clinical reasoning APIs: Current EHR systems (Epic, Cerner/Oracle)⏎offer clinical decision support hooks, but they are optimized for simple alert-based⏎interventions (“drug interaction detected”), not for the kind of multi-step clinical⏎reasoning dialogue that mirrors how physicians actually think. HHS should encourage⏎EHR vendors to develop APIs that support richer AI-clinician interaction at the point of⏎care.⏎IV. A Note on the Physician-Technologist Perspective⏎I want to close with a broader observation. The most impactful clinical AI tools will be built by⏎people who have practiced medicine. Not because technologists lack talent, but because clinical⏎reasoning is a domain where the problem definition requires lived experience. You cannot design⏎a tool to improve how physicians think through differential diagnoses unless you have personally⏎struggled with that process, unless you have been the medical student who scored in the 85th⏎percentile on board examinations but froze at the bedside, the resident who witnessed colleagues⏎ordering unnecessary tests because they lacked access to reasoning frameworks, and the fellow⏎who decided to build the tool rather than wait for someone else to.⏎HHS has an opportunity to not only accelerate AI adoption but to cultivate the physician-⏎innovators who will build the most impactful tools. Programs that reduce the barriers for⏎practicing clinicians to develop and deploy clinical AI, whether through dedicated funding⏎mechanisms, regulatory sandboxes, or partnership frameworks with academic medical centers,⏎would yield outsized returns relative to their cost.⏎Page 5⏎⏎Comment on “HHS Health Sector AI RFI” | Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎V. Conclusion⏎The Department’s RFI arrives at a critical inflection point. Large language models have reached⏎the capability threshold to conduct meaningful interactive clinical dialogue. The technology to⏎teach clinical reasoning at scale and to support it at the point of care now exists. But the⏎regulatory, reimbursement, and institutional frameworks have not caught up.⏎I urge the Department to:⏎1. Establish a clear regulatory category for non-device clinical reasoning AI that provides⏎proportionate oversight without imposing device-level requirements on tools that augment⏎clinical thinking.⏎2. Create reimbursement pathways that reward guideline adherence and clinical reasoning⏎quality, rather than volume of tests and procedures.⏎3. Invest in clinical reasoning education infrastructure as a high-leverage R&D priority,⏎recognizing that improved reasoning at the training stage prevents waste at the practice stage.⏎4. Develop programs that support physician-technologists in building clinical AI from⏎inside the healthcare system, not just from outside it.⏎I would welcome the opportunity to discuss any of these recommendations in further detail. The⏎intersection of clinical reasoning education and AI-powered decision support represents one of⏎the highest-impact opportunities to reduce healthcare costs while improving patient outcomes,⏎and it is an area where the United States can and should lead.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Sam Alsabti, D.O.⏎Cardiology Fellow⏎Board Certified, American Board of Internal Medicine⏎Founder, Prosivo (prosivo.ai)⏎Southeast Michigan⏎Submitted via regulations.gov in response to Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Page 6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0252♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Digital Pathology Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Digital Pathology Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 19, 2026⏎VIA Electronic Submission to www.regulations.gov⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use⏎of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎On behalf of the Digital Pathology Association (“DPA”), please accept the following comments in⏎response to the request for information (“RFI”) from the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎(“ASTP”) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (“ONC”),⏎entitled “Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care”.1⏎The Digital Pathology Association is a global community of over 4,500 pathologists, scientists,⏎technologists, and industry leaders dedicated to the evolution of modern medicine. Founded in 2009,⏎our mission is to facilitate awareness, education, and adoption of digital pathology and AI applications⏎across healthcare and life sciences. By sharing best practices and demonstrating the efficiencies of new⏎technologies, we empower our members to enhance workflows and, ultimately, improve patient care.⏎Our impact is driven by a diverse international membership and guided by six core pillars:⏎Collaboration, Education, Membership, Regulatory, Reimbursement, and Standards.2⏎Given our focus on revolutionizing patient care with digital pathology, we appreciate the opportunity⏎to respond to a subset of the questions, providing our perspective on steps the Agency can take to drive⏎innovation and the speed of adoption of digital pathology in patient care.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎Innovators, providers, and patients face numerous different obstacles to the use of AI in clinical care⏎including, but not limited to, the following:⏎a. Investment and other costs of digital pathology are not systematically captured in⏎reimbursement mechanisms developed and managed by CMS.⏎1⏎See 90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (link)⏎2⏎https://digitalpathologyassociation.org/⏎T 317.816.1630 Toll-Free 877.824.4085⏎info@digitalpathologyassociation.org⏎⏎In the development of digital pathology solutions, companies and laboratories incur meaningful⏎research & development costs as well as other operational costs (related to image acquisition,⏎computing and data storage, slide digitization, image analysis software, etc.). It is critical that these⏎costs are captured and contemplated as CMS establishes inputs into and ultimate payment rates for⏎these types of solutions under Medicare Part A and Part B. While CMS has nationally priced a number⏎of different AI solutions – particularly under the Outpatient Prospective Payment System (OPPS) and⏎the Physician Fee Schedule (PFS), the Agency has yet to take a formal position on its approach for⏎rate-setting for these services. The absence of an established policy on its pricing methodology (i.e.,⏎how rates are established) translates to a lack of Agency transparency and predictability, a challenge⏎that can serve as a deterrent or roadblock to innovation.⏎b. Challenges in the timeline to and process for obtaining coding creates uncertainty.⏎The three pillars of reimbursement are coding, coverage, and payment. The ability to obtain procedure⏎codes that reflect the service furnished to patients is critical in order to obtain reimbursement.⏎Historically, algorithmic analyses of previously performed diagnostic tests had pursued coding through⏎the Proprietary Laboratory Analyses3 pathway. This pathway was beneficial to laboratories and⏎manufacturers for the following reasons:⏎▪ Limited time between code request and code effective date (significantly shorter than other⏎pathways);⏎▪ Specificity of the code descriptor to appropriately describe the innovation, and;⏎▪ Proprietary nature of the codes.⏎Unfortunately, recent clarification by the AMA CPT Editorial Panel eliminated this coding pathway⏎for many digital pathology solutions. As a result, there are fewer pathways available to obtain a⏎procedure code and these pathways (Category III and Category I) have longer timelines and higher⏎bars for success. By removing the option to pursue a PLA code, there is also greater uncertainty with⏎respect to how these solutions will be viewed and thus priced by CMS.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎As the Agency is contemplating policy or programmatic changes that would facilitate the use and⏎adoption of AI in clinical care, the DPA puts forth the following recommendations for consideration:⏎▪ Under the Outpatient Prospective Payment System, the New Technology APC payment pathway is⏎a critically important option, particularly for AI-based solutions that do not qualify for transitional⏎pass-through payment. To give stability and predictability to payment in the hospital outpatient⏎3⏎Proprietary Laboratory Analyses (PLA) codes were developed by the American Medical Association to identify⏎specific and unique laboratory tests.⏎T 317.816.1630 Toll-Free 877.824.4085⏎info@digitalpathologyassociation.org⏎⏎setting, CMS should set a minimum length of time of five years for all qualified services to remain⏎in a New Tech APC.⏎▪ With OPPS, CMS has numerous different clinical APCs intended to represent services with similar⏎clinical and resource requirements. Given key differences between AI-based and non-AI-based⏎services, CMS should create a new clinical APC family for algorithmic services to ensure that⏎solutions in the same APC are in fact similar or clinically comparable and with respect to resource⏎requirements.⏎▪ For certain types of services, particularly ones that do not involve physician work, the cost for⏎furnishing the care is comparable across the different sites of service (e.g., hospital outpatient,⏎physician office). CMS should establish a process for quickly and consistently setting a payment⏎rate under the Physician Fee Schedule that (a) reflects the costs of the innovation and (b) is⏎consistent with the pricing approach taken by the Agency under OPPS.⏎▪ CMS/CMMI should establish a new demonstration model for AI solutions to help fund, facilitate,⏎and highlight the clinical and potential cost benefits of digital pathology workflows. This pilot⏎should apply across the different settings of care and should take into consideration the costs of⏎these capital-intensive technologies that necessitate on-going investment and continuous⏎refinement of the underlying models supporting the solution. The end goal of this model would be⏎the establishment of coding, coverage, and payment pathways to ensure appropriate reimbursement⏎and promote continued adoption of evidence-based AI technologies.⏎▪ For solutions recognized in guidance (e.g., National Comprehensive Cancer Network) or physician⏎society clinical practice guidelines, the Medicare Administrative Contractors should expedite the⏎establishment of positive, evidence-based local coverage determinations. The MACs have the⏎flexibility to move quickly under the current regulations and should be encouraged to do so in⏎these circumstances. These positive coverage policies would be beneficial not only to traditional⏎Medicare beneficiaries but also the ever-growing Medicare Advantage patient population. Positive⏎Medicare coverage policies will create a more favorable reimbursement environment, promoting⏎appropriate, scientifically rigorous, and clinically meaningful AI innovation.⏎* * * * *⏎The Digital Pathology Association appreciates the Agency’s recognition of the need to accelerate the⏎adoption and use of artificial intelligence as part of clinical care. We would welcome the opportunity⏎to discuss these RFI comments with you in greater detail. Please feel free to contact Abbey Norris at⏎877.824.4085 or ANorris@digitalpathologyassociation.org.⏎Sincerely yours,⏎/s/⏎Michael Rivers⏎Chair, Reimbursement Taskforce⏎Digital Pathology Association⏎T 317.816.1630 Toll-Free 877.824.4085⏎info@digitalpathologyassociation.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0253♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Federal Regulation Is Essential⏎⏎There is currently no comprehensive federal regulatory framework governing AI in healthcare. This regulatory gap poses significant risks to patients.⏎⏎Healthcare products and treatments are traditionally subject to oversight to ensure safety and effectiveness. For example, the U.S. Food and Drug Administration evaluates medical devices and pharmaceuticals before market entry. Licensed clinicians are regulated through established credentialing and state oversight systems. AI systems deployed in clinical settings should be held to comparable, or higher, standards.⏎⏎Leaving oversight to corporations or to a patchwork of state laws would create inconsistent protections and expose patients to avoidable harm. PsiAN calls for uniform federal standards to govern privacy, safety, and accountability.⏎⏎Privacy Concerns and HIPAA Gaps⏎⏎One of the most urgent concerns involves patient privacy. Many AI chatbots marketed for mental health support are not compliant with HIPAA requirements, meaning patient data may not be protected under federal healthcare privacy law.⏎⏎Confidentiality is foundational to effective psychotherapy. Without legally enforceable safeguards, patients may unknowingly expose sensitive information without adequate protection. No acceleration of AI adoption should occur without clear, enforceable federal privacy standards.⏎⏎Lack of Evidence for Safety and Effectiveness⏎⏎Large language models (LLMs), while technologically advanced, are prone to generating inaccurate or fabricated outputs. There have been documented cases of AI systems providing harmful guidance to vulnerable individuals, including those experiencing suicidal ideation.⏎⏎Corporate metrics such as user engagement, downloads, and customer satisfaction are not substitutes for rigorous clinical outcome data. New AI tools must be evaluated using meaningful health metrics, including symptom remission, dropout rates, sustained recovery, and improvements in quality of life.⏎⏎Importantly, evaluation and safety research should be conducted by independent researchers, not by the corporations developing and selling the products, so as to minimize conflicts of interest.⏎⏎AI and the Limits of the Therapeutic Relationship⏎⏎Decades of psychotherapy research confirm that the therapist-patient relationship is the strongest predictor of treatment outcome. AI systems, regardless of sophistication, do not constitute human relationships.⏎⏎A critical policy question: Should AI function as a support tool for licensed clinicians? As an administrative aid? Or as a replacement for psychotherapy? At present, there is insufficient evidence to justify AI as a substitute for trained mental health professionals.⏎⏎Recovery in behavioral health involves more than symptom reduction. The Substance Abuse and Mental Health Services Administration defines recovery as a long-term, self-directed process leading to improved health and meaningful life participation. AI systems must be evaluated against these broader, recovery-oriented outcomes, not simply short-term symptom change.⏎⏎The Role of HHS Moving Forward⏎⏎Policymakers are seeking solutions to persistent gaps in mental healthcare access. While AI is often proposed as a potential solution, as a clinical psychologist, I caution that widespread deployment without adequate safeguards could introduce new risks.⏎⏎I recommend that the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services:⏎⏎Support rigorous, independent research on AI safety and efficacy⏎⏎Establish uniform national standards for privacy and accountability⏎⏎Collaborate with clinicians and psychotherapy researchers to define appropriate clinical metrics⏎⏎Await determinations from the FDA regarding whether AI tools qualify as medical devices before accelerating adoption⏎⏎Public use of AI has advanced rapidly, but scientific validation has not kept pace. Without enforceable federal oversight, corporations lack strong incentives to prioritize patient protection over profitability.⏎⏎Protecting Patients in the Age of AI⏎⏎AI medical tools are not ready for accelerated adoption in clinical care without meaningful regulation and a robust evidence base.⏎⏎Federal leadership is necessary to reduce risk of harm, protect patient privacy, ensure clinically effective treatment, and establish consistent national standards. By grounding policy in science and clinical expertise, HHS can help ensure that innovation in AI supports, rather than undermines, ethical and effective healthcare.⏎⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0254♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cedar Cares, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Cedar Cares, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy /⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Washington, D.C.⏎Re: Request for Information- Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Cedar Cares, Inc. (“Cedar”) appreciates the opportunity to respond to HHS’s⏎Request for Information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. Cedar strongly supports the Administration’s⏎goal of positioning the United States as a global leader in artificial intelligence⏎innovation while removing unnecessary regulatory barriers that impede responsible⏎deployment of AI across healthcare.⏎Cedar is a healthcare technology company dedicated to improving the healthcare⏎financial experience. Founded in 2016, Cedar’s platform unifies patient billing,⏎payments, insurance coverage, and support into a single solution —driving improved⏎results for healthcare providers and better financial journeys for patients. Cedar⏎helps patients access the coverage, aid, and payment options they need with⏎confidence. To date, Cedar has supported more than 50 million patients nationwide.⏎Cedar’s platform uses AI and machine learning to help solve the growing complexity⏎of patient financial engagement and deliver personalization at scale. Our experience⏎deploying AI-enabled tools in regulated healthcare environments informs the⏎comments below.⏎I. Regulatory and Programmatic Changes to Incentivize AI Adoption⏎Cedar encourages HHS to prioritize regulatory clarity, interoperability reforms, and⏎targeted modernization efforts that enable AI tools to access high-quality data in a⏎predictable and scalable manner. In many cases, barriers to AI adoption stem from⏎32 Avenue of the Americas, 18th Fl | NYC 10013 1⏎⏎fragmented data access, artificial interoperability barriers created by incumbents,⏎duplicative administrative requirements, and uncertainty regarding permissible⏎automated access under existing rules.⏎HHS can accelerate responsible AI deployment by:⏎● Reinforcing that automated and AI-enabled access to health information via⏎standardized APIs is permissible when consistent with HIPAA and information⏎blocking safeguards, and ensuring access via those APIs is available and does⏎not include discriminatory pricing (i.e., without disguised information⏎blocking);⏎● Enforcing the adoption of HL7 FHIR standards across the healthcare⏎ecosystem to cover a broader range of healthcare data, ensuring consistent⏎and secure data exchange. While USCDI v3 was recently incorporated into⏎the standards (which we applaud), actual implementation of the FHIR⏎standards still remains fragmented and incomplete, especially for some types⏎of data, like healthcare billing;⏎● Continuing to modernize and streamline certification and compliance⏎requirements that no longer reflect current technical realities; and⏎● Aligning AI policy with broader interoperability initiatives, including CMS’s⏎patient-directed access and data exchange efforts, and HHS’s initiatives to⏎stop healthcare data information blocking.⏎Providing clear, operational guidance will reduce ambiguity for providers and⏎technology developers while maintaining appropriate privacy and security⏎protections.⏎II. Governance and Accountability Considerations for Non-Medical Device AI⏎For AI tools that are not regulated as medical devices, governance challenges⏎typically relate to transparency, accountability, and contractual risk allocation rather⏎than direct clinical safety oversight. In Cedar’s experience, these considerations are⏎32 Avenue of the Americas, 18th Fl | NYC 10013 2⏎⏎often effectively addressed through contractual safeguards, audit, and defined⏎use-case limitations.⏎HHS can support innovation by clarifying baseline expectations around⏎transparency, documentation, auditability, and data stewardship, while avoiding rigid⏎or default liability frameworks that could discourage responsible AI development.⏎Clear, risk-based guidance would allow AI-enabled tools to evolve while preserving⏎accountability.⏎III. Supporting Private-Sector Certification and Testing⏎Cedar appreciates HHS’s interest in supporting private-sector accreditation,⏎certification, and testing frameworks. Industry-led benchmarking and validation⏎initiatives are often better positioned to evolve alongside rapidly advancing AI⏎technologies and adapt to emerging clinical and operational use cases.⏎HHS can play a constructive role by encouraging voluntary, consensus-driven⏎standards and facilitating collaboration across stakeholders, rather than imposing⏎static or prescriptive certification regimes that may quickly become outdated.⏎IV. Interoperability as a Catalyst for AI Innovation⏎Enhanced interoperability remains one of the most effective levers available to⏎accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. Standardized, API-based access to⏎longitudinal clinical and coverage data enables AI tools to support care⏎coordination, operational efficiency, and patient engagement.⏎Cedar encourages HHS to ensure that digital health companies and application⏎developers that directly support patients in all aspects of their medical care⏎(including tools that enable them to afford and pay for their care) are able to⏎participate meaningfully in health information exchange networks under clear,⏎objective, and consistently applied criteria. Interoperability frameworks should⏎promote patient-directed access to health information through applications of a⏎patient’s choice and prevent contractual, pricing, or technical practices that⏎functionally limit or block participation.⏎32 Avenue of the Americas, 18th Fl | NYC 10013 3⏎⏎Policies that reduce gatekeeping and promote fair, reasonable, and⏎non-discriminatory access to interoperable data will expand market opportunities,⏎fuel research and development, and advance patient-centered innovation.⏎Conclusion⏎Cedar supports HHS’s efforts to accelerate the responsible adoption of AI through⏎interoperability, regulatory clarity, and modernized governance frameworks.⏎Thoughtful policy refinement can enable innovation while maintaining strong⏎privacy, security, and accountability safeguards.⏎Cedar appreciates the opportunity to provide these comments and welcomes⏎continued engagement with HHS as these policy efforts advance.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Vanessa Gage⏎General Counsel & Chief Compliance Officer⏎Cedar Cares, Inc.⏎32 Avenue of the Americas, 18th Fl | NYC 10013 4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0255♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Vantage Precision Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Vantage Precision Health♔♔♔♔♔♔1⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building; Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎Vantage Precision Health (VPH) is grateful for the opportunity to submit comments in⏎response to the Department of Health and Human Services' (HHS) Request for⏎Information (RFI) regarding the acceleration of artificial intelligence (AI) adoption as part⏎of clinical care, published in the Federal Register at 90 FR 60108 on December 23,⏎2025. Vantage Precision Health is a consulting and advisory firm specializing in AI⏎adoption strategy and program evaluation for behavioral health and aging services⏎providers. We work directly with community mental health centers, substance use⏎treatment organizations, psychiatric rehabilitation programs, and providers of long-⏎term care for older adults the very organizations that would benefit profoundly from⏎thoughtful AI integration, and that face the greatest risks if that integration is poorly⏎designed, inadequately evaluated, or prematurely accelerated.⏎Our perspective is shaped by daily engagement with providers who operate at the⏎intersection of chronic underfunding, and the collision of growing demand for services⏎combined with workforce shortages. These providers serve populations people living⏎with serious mental illness, substance use disorders, co-occurring conditions, and age-⏎related cognitive and functional decline whose needs are complex, whose⏎vulnerabilities are significant, and whose voices are too often absent from national⏎technology policy conversations. We offer these comments in the hope that HHS will⏎center the experiences of these populations and their providers as it develops its AI⏎policy framework.⏎Our Overarching Position⏎Vantage Precision Health strongly supports the responsible adoption of AI in clinical⏎care and believes that well-designed, rigorously evaluated AI tools can meaningfully⏎1⏎⏎2⏎expand access, reduce administrative burden, improve care coordination, and advance⏎health equity in behavioral health and aging services. We are, however, deeply⏎concerned that the current policy environment characterized by a lack of uniform⏎federal standards, inadequate reimbursement pathways, and a dearth of independent⏎outcome research creates conditions in which the promise of AI may be undermined⏎by premature deployment, vendor-driven evaluation, and the exclusion of safety-net⏎behavioral health providers from the innovation ecosystem.⏎We therefore urge HHS to pursue a balanced approach that simultaneously accelerates⏎access to high-value AI tools and establishes the regulatory, reimbursement, and⏎research infrastructure necessary to ensure that those tools are safe, effective, and⏎equitable. Acceleration without this infrastructure will not serve patients, providers, or⏎the public interest.⏎Behavioral Health and Aging Services: A Distinct Policy Context⏎Behavioral health and aging services providers occupy a unique and underserved⏎position in the health information technology landscape. Because these providers were⏎largely excluded from the Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical⏎Health (HITECH) Act of 2009, many lack the electronic health record (EHR)⏎infrastructure, interoperability capacity, and data governance frameworks that form the⏎foundation for AI integration. The costs of AI solutions remain prohibitive for many⏎community-based behavioral health organizations, in part because AI vendors must⏎build compatibility across dozens of disparate EHR systems a burden that is passed⏎directly to providers. Sustainable public financing for AI adoption in this sector is not yet⏎in place, and reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted services remain unclear.⏎At the same time, the populations served by behavioral health and aging services⏎providers present distinctive clinical and ethical considerations for AI deployment. The⏎therapeutic relationship is a primary driver of outcomes in mental health treatment and⏎the talk-therapy components of addiction treatment, and AI tools that supplant rather⏎than support that relationship risk undermining care quality. Additionally, older adults,⏎particularly those with cognitive impairment, may face heightened risks from AI⏎systems that are not designed with their needs and capacities in mind (similar to what⏎occurred during the COVID-19 pandemic with many long-term care residents unable to⏎communicate effectively with family and friends via smartphones or tablets). And the⏎intersection of AI with sensitive behavioral health data including information⏎protected under 42 CFR Part 2 raises privacy and confidentiality concerns that⏎require specific regulatory attention.⏎Key Recommendations⏎Uniform standards for AI safety, privacy, and efficacy. HHS should establish uniform⏎federal standards for AI safety, privacy, and efficacy that are specifically calibrated to⏎the behavioral health and aging services context. These standards should address the⏎2⏎⏎3⏎unique confidentiality requirements of substance use disorder treatment records, the⏎clinical and ethical dimensions of AI use with populations experiencing serious mental⏎illness or cognitive decline, and the interoperability gaps that continue to disadvantage⏎safety-net providers.⏎Reimbursement pathways for AI tools in BH and AS. HHS should develop and fund⏎reimbursement pathways that enable behavioral health and aging services providers to⏎adopt high-value AI tools particularly those that reduce administrative burden,⏎support care coordination, and enhance clinical decision-making without bearing⏎unsustainable implementation costs. This should include reimbursement for provider⏎training and patient education, recognition of the workforce investment required for⏎responsible AI adoption, and incentives for AI tools that demonstrate measurable⏎improvements in health-related quality of life rather than engagement metrics alone.⏎Oversight and funding of outcomes research with healthcare AI tools. HHS should⏎invest in independent, rigorous outcomes research on AI in behavioral health and aging⏎services, conducted by researchers without financial ties to AI vendors. The evidence⏎base for AI in these settings remains nascent especially in behavioral health and the⏎metrics used by commercial developers e.g., downloads, user engagement, and⏎customer satisfaction are not adequate substitutes for clinical outcome data. HHS⏎should prioritize research that examines the impact of AI tools such as therapy⏎chatbots on recovery, functional status, and quality of life, and that attends to⏎differential effects across populations defined by race, ethnicity, age, disability status,⏎and socioeconomic circumstances.⏎Uniform data and software standards. HHS should oversee and fund the⏎development of uniform data and software standards for healthcare AI. Nascent tools,⏎e.g. ambient-listening AI scribes, should be tested using the same representative,⏎minimally biased data. The AI tools should also be required to be interoperable, as the⏎lack of this requirement for EHRs and associated databases has produced decades of⏎difficulty with adoption and use. This has especially affected behavioral health⏎providers, who were not included in the HITECH Act s funding pool and have lagged far⏎behind the rest of medicine in the development of EHRs and other specialized⏎technologies for mental health and substance use disorder treatment.⏎Engagement of providers and patients in shaping AI data and tool governance. HHS⏎should ensure that behavioral health and aging services providers, and the patients and⏎communities they serve, have a meaningful voice in AI governance. This includes⏎representation on any technical expert panels convened by ASTP/ONC, inclusion in⏎pilot programs and cooperative agreements, and active outreach to organizations⏎serving populations that are historically underrepresented in health technology policy.⏎Non-medical device status. Currently, AI scribes and many other AI tools impacting⏎clinical care are not recognized as medical devices by the FDA. This classification is⏎critical for adequate oversight and needs to be changed. In the case of AI scribes,⏎3⏎⏎4⏎behavioral health has unique requirements for them because therapy is almost⏎exclusively language-based, and some of the most critical patient communication lies⏎in ambiguity, not explicitness. The architecture and training of AI scribes to write⏎concise, fluent notes impedes their correct interpretation of many ambiguous or⏎nuanced, yet critical, behavioral health utterances (e.g., I don t know how much longer⏎I can go on reflecting possible suicidal ideation and I just couldn t stand it anymore⏎signaling a potential relapse).⏎Conclusion⏎Vantage Precision Health is committed to advancing the responsible and equitable⏎adoption of AI in behavioral health and aging services. We believe that HHS has a⏎historic opportunity to shape an AI policy framework that expands access, improves⏎outcomes, and builds public trust and we are eager to contribute our expertise to⏎that effort. We would welcome the opportunity to participate in any technical panels,⏎listening sessions, or collaborative research initiatives that ASTP/ONC convenes in⏎connection with this RFI, and we are available to provide additional information or⏎clarification at any time.⏎Thank you for your careful consideration of our comments.⏎Respectfully,⏎Linda Hermer, Ph.D.⏎Founder and CEO⏎Vantage Precision Health⏎6200 Westchester Park Drive, Suite 803⏎College Park, MD 20740⏎linda@vantageprecisionhealth.com⏎352-328-4857⏎Vantage Precision Health is a consulting and advisory firm specializing in AI adoption⏎strategy and program evaluation for behavioral health and aging services providers. The⏎firm works with community mental health centers, substance use treatment⏎organizations, psychiatric rehabilitation programs, and home- and community-based⏎services providers for older adults to design, implement, and evaluate technology-⏎enabled programs that improve care quality, expand access, and advance health⏎equity.⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0256♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Clark♔♔Alaska♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honest question, and I mean this genuinely: if I submit a comment here, does it actually change anything? I've never done this before and I'm not sure how these processes work. But something happened with one of my clients that I keep thinking about, so I figured I should at least try.⏎⏎My client Dora is 74 years old, lives in Bethel, Alaska, and she waited almost six weeks for a specialist to look at her imaging results. Six weeks. The nearest qualified physician was hundreds of miles away and there just wasn't anyone closer who could read what they were seeing. When an AI tool finally flagged something her remote provider had missed, I was genuinely relieved. It probably made a real difference for her.⏎⏎But then I sat with her while she tried to figure out what had happened, and she didn't understand that a computer system had been involved in reviewing her records. Nobody had told her. She's Yup'ik, and there's already a long history of her community not being fully informed about medical decisions being made on their behalf, and this just felt like another version of that, even if the outcome was good this time.⏎⏎So I'm not opposed to AI in healthcare. I saw it help someone I care about. What I'm asking, or I guess what I want HHS to know, is that the speed part and the consent and transparency part have to happen together. I don't know how you write that into a regulation exactly, that's not my expertise. But Dora should have known what system had access to her records and what it was doing with them. That seems like it shouldn't be complicated to require.⏎⏎I also wonder, and this is maybe a separate issue, whether these AI tools are being tested on patient populations that look anything like the people I work with in rural Alaska. I genuinely don't know the answer to that. I just know that a lot of the technology I've seen in healthcare seems built for patients in cities with consistent internet access and English as their first language, and then it gets rolled out everywhere else as an afterthought. If HHS is going to push faster adoption, I'd want someone to be asking whether the algorithms were ever validated on patients from places like Bethel.⏎⏎I don't have a policy solution to offer. I'm a home health aide, not a researcher. But I do think the people actually sitting with patients in remote areas should probably be part of whatever conversation is happening about how this gets implemented, and right now I'm not sure that's happening either.⏎⏎I guess I'll just submit this and see.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0257♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Helfie AI, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Helfie AI♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎BY ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Attention: Request for Information: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Keane:⏎Thank you for the opportunity to submit comments in response to ONC’s Request for Information⏎regarding the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. We commend ONC for its⏎continued leadership in advancing innovation that improves healthcare quality, access, and efficiency.⏎The current trajectory of AI adoption in healthcare closely parallels that of telehealth prior to the COVID⏎public health emergency. While the underlying technologies were mature and demonstrated measurable⏎value, widespread implementation lagged due to regulatory, reimbursement, and cultural barriers. The⏎pandemic catalyzed regulatory flexibility, payment alignment, and broader clinical acceptance,⏎accelerating telehealth integration into routine care delivery.⏎AI-enabled tools appear to be at a similar inflection point. Health systems are increasingly recognizing⏎AI’s capacity to enhance access, improve operational efficiency, and support clinical decision-making at⏎scale.⏎Significant gaps in access, workforce capacity, and chronic disease management persist across the United⏎States. Addressing these challenges does not require waiting for a crisis-driven catalyst; rather, it calls for⏎proactive policy alignment that fosters responsible innovation.⏎For example, we feel that Helfie AI’s platform can enhance current remote patient monitoring programs,⏎be utilized for pre-admission triage and post-discharge support, and can augment the management of⏎chronic disease in a safe and effective way. Using a standard smartphone and advanced AI algorithms,⏎Helfie uses remote photoplethysmography to estimate and trend a patient’s vital signs: blood pressure,⏎heart rate, oxygen saturation, respiratory rate, HRV, and physiologic stress. It also performs cough⏎analysis to identify chronic respiratory issues like COPD, asthma, and others. Helfie’s suite of AI-⏎1⏎⏎powered checks are highly accurate, take seconds to perform, and do not require the purchase of⏎additional devices. Combined with powerful medical insights, Helfie makes early disease detection and⏎prevention simple, accessible, and delightful. All from a patient’s own hands, at population scale -no⏎matter where they are, or who they are. Our comprehensive platform delivers a personalized health AI for⏎patients through the aggregation of unique data streams and robust interoperability.⏎To facilitate broader patient access to technologies of this nature, continued efforts to streamline⏎regulatory pathways within the FDA Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) framework are important.⏎HHS initiatives such as the TEMPO program demonstrate meaningful progress towards improved⏎approval processes. Establishing clearer pathways for technologies engaged with FDA review to⏎transition efficiently into parallel adoption mechanisms, including ACCESS-related initiatives, would⏎further promote timely and responsible deployment.⏎At the same time, regulatory modernization must be accompanied by strong safeguards for patients. AI-⏎enabled clinical tools should adhere to rigorous standards of privacy, security, transparency, and data⏎governance. Patients must remain empowered participants in their care, supported by technologies that⏎meet the highest thresholds for trust, accountability, and clinical integrity.⏎Our objective is to reduce preventable morbidity and mortality by enabling earlier identification and⏎proactive management of health conditions before they progress to complex and costly acute episodes.⏎With thoughtful regulatory alignment and sustained collaboration, AI-enabled tools can advance patient-⏎centered care at population scale.⏎We appreciate ONC’s engagement on this important issue and stand ready to contribute further as⏎policies evolve to support safe, effective, and equitable integration of AI into clinical practice.⏎Sincerely,⏎Ranya Habash, MD⏎Chief Medical Officer⏎Habash@helfie.ai⏎+1 310.343.8842⏎2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0258♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from College of Healthcare Information Management Executives (CHIME), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔College of Healthcare Information Management Executives (CHIME)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Submitted via the Federal eRulemaking Portal: http://www.regulations.gov⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as part of Clinical Care/HHS Health Sector AI RFI [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy and Dr. Keane:⏎The College of Healthcare Information Management Executives (CHIME) appreciates the⏎opportunity to comment on the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of the⏎Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) request for information (RFI) on⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care, as published⏎in the Federal Register on December 23, 2025 (Vol. 90, No. 244).⏎Background⏎CHIME is an executive organization dedicated to serving chief information officers (CIOs), chief⏎medical information officers (CMIOs), chief nursing information officers (CNIOs), chief⏎innovation officers (CIOs), chief digital officers (CDOs) and other senior healthcare IT leaders.⏎With thousands of individual members, and hundreds of CHIME Foundation healthcare IT⏎business and professional service firm members, CHIME and its communities provide a highly⏎interactive, trusted environment enabling senior professional and industry leaders to collaborate,⏎exchange best practices, address professional development needs, and advocate for the⏎effective use of information management to improve the health and care in the communities⏎they serve.⏎Key Recommendations and Takeaways⏎Our members – hospital and healthcare system CIOs, CISOs, and digital health leaders – are⏎early adopters and stewards of AI technologies and are acutely aware of both their promise and⏎their risks. We welcome HHS’s recognition that regulatory frameworks, reimbursement policies,⏎and research investments must evolve in tandem to ensure AI tools are safe, effective, and⏎deployable at scale. CHIME strongly believes that we must responsibly advance AI adoption,⏎1⏎⏎improve patient outcomes, reduce clinician burden, and lower healthcare costs, while⏎maintaining the trust and confidence of patients and providers.⏎AI is no longer a distant concept. It is reshaping the very foundation of healthcare delivery. From⏎enhancing diagnostic accuracy to streamlining administrative workflows, AI offers⏎unprecedented opportunities to improve patient outcomes, reduce clinician burden, and foster⏎innovation across the care continuum. Yet, with these opportunities come profound⏎responsibilities.⏎Healthcare providers stand at the intersection of technology and trust. As stewards of patient⏎safety and privacy, they must navigate a rapidly evolving landscape where algorithms influence⏎clinical decisions and data flows beyond traditional boundaries. The CHIME Principles for⏎Responsible AI provide a comprehensive approach describing both challenges and potential⏎solutions to help nurture responsible AI adoption. They serve as a guide to understanding and⏎implementing AI responsibly anchored in principles of transparency, fairness, and patient-⏎centered care.⏎Core Pillars of CHIME’s AI Principles⏎• Protecting Patients and Their Data: Outlines expectations for robust cybersecurity⏎practices, streamlined privacy laws, clear patient consent policies, and standardized⏎security requirements for third-party technologies.⏎• Fostering Patient-Centered Care: Emphasizes the importance of human oversight,⏎adoption of voluntary frameworks and standards, risk-based monitoring, and ongoing⏎transparency regarding AI’s role in clinical decision-making.⏎• Empowering Providers: Highlights the need to equip providers, especially small and⏎under-resourced organizations, with education, resources, and support while promoting⏎transparency, innovation, and equitable access to AI-enabled tools.⏎As HHS recognizes, AI is inherently data-driven and dependent on robust interoperability,⏎governance, and safeguards to ensure appropriate use, privacy, and security. The Department’s⏎RFI is timely and necessary as providers navigate a rapidly evolving AI ecosystem in which⏎clinical utility, regulatory clarity, reimbursement pathways, and liability frameworks remain⏎fragmented and uncertain. We offer the following comments to highlight practical considerations⏎from frontline providers and to recommend policy approaches that balance innovation with⏎patient safety, provider accountability, and sustainable implementation.⏎Detailed Input & Recommendations⏎In this RFI, HHS is seeking concrete, experience-based feedback from those building, buying,⏎evaluating, using, and receiving care from AI tools that are part of clinical care as well as from⏎those who wish to do so but face barriers. CHIME’s feedback addresses the questions outlined⏎in this RFI, which seeks public feedback on the actions the Department can take to establish a⏎forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and use⏎of AI as part of clinical care.⏎AI holds significant promise to enhance clinical decision-making, streamline workflows, and⏎improve patient access and outcomes. At the same time, successful and sustainable AI⏎adoption requires clear, risk-based regulatory pathways, interoperable data infrastructure, and⏎policies that foster innovation while safeguarding patient privacy and security. HHS’s recognition⏎2⏎⏎that federal regulatory, reimbursement, and research policies must be better aligned to support⏎responsible AI integration is both timely and necessary.⏎CHIME’s input seeks to provide practical, provider-informed recommendations to help the⏎Department advance a flexible, innovation-enabling policy framework that supports real-world⏎clinical implementation without imposing duplicative or prescriptive requirements that could⏎impede adoption or increase provider burden. AI is not a replacement for human judgment; it is⏎a tool to augment clinical expertise. By embracing innovation while safeguarding trust, HHS can⏎ensure that AI fulfills its promise: improving health for all.⏎AI implementation in clinical care raises complex questions regarding data governance,⏎interoperability, workforce readiness, liability, and a growing “digital divide.” HHS’s focus on⏎patient trust, data security, and interoperability reflects foundational prerequisites for scalable AI⏎adoption and aligns with long-standing provider priorities. CHIME members wish to emphasize⏎the need for clear, risk-based policies, alignment with existing health IT frameworks, and⏎pragmatic solutions that reflect the realities of healthcare system operations.⏎CHIME members are actively deploying and evaluating AI-enabled tools in clinical,⏎administrative, and operational settings. However – broadly, private-sector AI innovation and⏎clinical adoption for healthcare providers are slowed by financial, technical and data constraints,⏎regulatory and market-structure misalignment, and organizational challenges in implementation⏎and scale.⏎Peer-reviewed evidence indicates that AI-based clinical decision support tools embedded within⏎electronic health records (EHRs) are widely deployed, in part because certain tools have⏎historically fallen outside FDA oversight and because activation costs are perceived as modest;⏎however, clinicians and health systems continue to raise concerns regarding accuracy, clinical⏎value, and utility.1 The study further found that, “Outside [of] medical imaging and EHR-⏎embedded applications, dissemination of clinical AI tools has been slower. The primary barrier is⏎likely that healthcare systems responsible for buying these tools do not believe they offer⏎enough value. The costs of not just licensing a tool but also of ensuring adequate training,⏎digital infrastructure, maintenance, and monitoring can be considerable. There is no⏎reimbursement for most AI tool use, and even in select cases where insurers provide⏎reimbursement, it does not offset the full costs.”1⏎For those under-resourced providers attempting to adopt AI, some simply lack the purchasing⏎power to afford it, including those solutions available through their EHR. AI uptake is also⏎occurring at a higher rate among larger providers – while smaller, lesser-resourced providers⏎struggle to keep pace with long-standing issues like solvency and reimbursement.⏎A study published in JAMIA in May 2025 examining mid- to large-provider adoption of AI⏎concluded that systems with greater financial and technical resources are more likely to adopt⏎and scale AI technologies. The rationale for AI adoption cited most frequently among⏎respondents was related to clinician burden. All survey respondents indicated successful⏎adoption of ambient AI but outside of this use case, AI adoption was uneven among providers.⏎The authors found that “Other select AI use cases such as imaging/radiology and clinical risk⏎stratification are adopted by many, though successes are modest.”⏎1 Angus DC, Khera R, Lieu T, et al. AI, Health, and Health Care Today and Tomorrow: The JAMA Summit Report on Artificial⏎Intelligence. JAMA. 2025;334(18):1650–1664. doi:10.1001/jama.2025.18490⏎3⏎⏎National Frameworks are Needed⏎CHIME continues to reiterate that the U.S. needs a comprehensive national data privacy law to⏎better protect consumers’ sensitive health information and inform consumers of how their data is⏎being used without duplicating what is already required under HIPAA. Furthermore, CHIME⏎members believe that a federal privacy law and a federal AI law that preempts state laws are⏎both needed to reduce burden and improve efficiency.⏎The amount of health data being created using AI is staggering in size. Advanced models⏎analyzing x-rays, magnetic resonance imaging (MRIs), clinical notes, and genomic data⏎contribute massive amounts of unstructured data. This data is in addition to the data being⏎created by smart devices that are continuously collecting health metrics. A single major hospital⏎can generate upwards of 50 petabytes of data annually which translates to approximately 170⏎years of HD video footage according to the World Economic Forum. The exponential growth of⏎health data requires heightened responsibility from healthcare providers to safeguard its⏎security, integrity, and responsible application.⏎The existing state patchwork of privacy laws has created an enormously complicated legal⏎landscape for healthcare providers and other stakeholders. Varying sets of state laws present⏎complexity, burden, and added costs to the healthcare system. Thus, the need for a national⏎data privacy law has become increasingly important. Five years ago, there were five states with⏎privacy laws enacted; today 20 states have privacy laws. As providers move to adopt AI and the⏎types of tools they are adopting are more complex, the privacy challenges are only mounting. A⏎federal law that preempts the myriad of state laws could bring greater efficiency to providers and⏎reduce administrative burden and complexity.⏎In the absence of a federal law on AI, and interest in AI at an all-time high, states continue⏎passing their own laws which have addressed mental health AI tools (i.e., chatbots), AI in⏎insurance and claims, clinical decision support systems, patient data privacy and algorithmic⏎transparency. Similar to the need for a federal privacy law that preempts state laws, a single⏎federal AI law that preempts state laws is needed.⏎AI Regulation, Reimbursement, and Research & Development⏎Regulatory Clarity and Flexibility will Spur Innovation⏎CHIME respectfully submits that a modernized, streamlined regulatory framework is essential to⏎unlocking the full innovative potential of AI in clinical care. While appropriate safeguards remain⏎critical to protect patients and maintain public trust, overly prescriptive or duplicative⏎requirements can inadvertently delay deployment, increase costs, and divert scarce resources⏎from patient care, and innovative cybersecurity investments. To foster meaningful innovation,⏎federal policy should emphasize regulatory clarity, and flexibility – creating an environment in⏎which healthcare providers can responsibly test, refine, and scale AI solutions without⏎unnecessary administrative friction. Thoughtful regulatory reform will not diminish accountability;⏎rather, it will enable providers to innovate more rapidly and effectively in service of better⏎outcomes, lower costs, and a more resilient healthcare system.⏎Navigating Patient Consent⏎Providers are using AI in a variety of ways. As AI becomes increasingly embedded in clinical⏎workflows, healthcare providers face mounting pressure to modernize patient consent policies.⏎4⏎⏎A central challenge lies in distinguishing between explainable AI, which attempts to explain how⏎an algorithm arrived at its answer or output, and interpretable AI, which allows users to more⏎intuitively understand how an AI-driven decision was reached. While both frameworks aim to⏎enhance trust and transparency, they present different implications for consent. Explainable AI⏎often requires complex, technical justification that may overwhelm or confuse patients, whereas⏎interpretable AI lends itself to clearer communication but may be limited in scope.⏎As more providers adopt AI, they must determine how to handle informing the patients they treat⏎about its use in their care. Determining how and when to inform patients, and to what degree it⏎is being used is still an evolving area for providers. The integration of AI into clinical workflows –⏎ranging from ambient listening tools (a voice recognition technology that captures a patient⏎interaction with a clinician) to traditional clinical decision support systems to chatbots – raises⏎difficult questions about what, when, and how to best disclose its use.⏎As AI tools such as clinical decision support systems, ambient listening technologies, and⏎scheduling assistants become more prevalent in healthcare, it’s increasingly important to clarify⏎how patient consent applies under HIPAA’s Treatment, Payment, and Healthcare Operations⏎(TPO) framework – particularly since many of these tools may operate in the background or⏎across multiple functions. For example, ambient listening used for clinical documentation may⏎fall under ‘treatment’ or ‘operations’ depending on its use, while revenue cycle AI tools clearly⏎support ‘payment.’ Clarifying these distinctions helps ensure that patients understand when their⏎data can be used without explicit consent under TPO, and when additional authorization may be⏎required.⏎In some organizations, consent is obtained at the beginning of the patient’s care. The challenge⏎lies in determining how much detail to disclose about AI and machine learning (ML) tools that⏎can impact patient care. While some argue that disclosing every automation would overwhelm⏎patients, others believe that complete transparency is crucial. To address this, some institutions⏎have built an inventory of AI tools and share summaries with internal teams, with plans to make⏎this information available to patients in the future. They use platforms, including their websites,⏎to inform patients about AI usage rather than providing notifications for every instance. Surveys⏎indicate that patients do not want to be notified every time AI is used. This underscores the⏎need to consider whether a single consent form should be used to inform patients about⏎AI usage.⏎Providers continue to evaluate how extensively AI should be integrated into their organizations,⏎as they are cautious about increasing the discoverability of sensitive information and are⏎carefully considering the best approach to ensure patient consent and data privacy. As AI⏎continues to permeate the healthcare delivery landscape, one member reflected, “getting⏎consent for each and every use of AI will be wildly impractical.”⏎Balancing Innovation with Appropriate Oversight and Support for Providers⏎Current regulatory frameworks provide an important baseline for oversight of health information⏎technology; however, the 21st Century Cures Act2 includes exemptions that have led to⏎inconsistencies in the regulatory treatment of third-party AI tools and applications. One CHIME⏎member – a practicing emergency physician – emphasized that these inconsistencies can allow⏎certain AI-enabled functionalities to evade FDA oversight, creating gaps in safety and⏎effectiveness assurance as generative and agentic AI increasingly blur the line between⏎2 Pub. L. No. 114-255, § 3060 (2016)⏎5⏎⏎administrative and clinical use and strain traditional intended-use frameworks. HHS could⏎further clarify and modernize these exemptions to ensure appropriate oversight of high-risk AI⏎tools while preserving flexibility for low-risk innovation. For example, the January 2026 update to⏎the U.S. Food and Drug Administration’s (FDA) General Wellness: Policy for Low Risk Devices⏎guidance demonstrates a thoughtful approach to delineate when certain digital health and⏎software functions fall outside traditional medical device oversight based on intended use and⏎risk. The guidance reiterates that software functions intended solely to maintain or encourage a⏎healthy lifestyle and are unrelated to the diagnosis, cure, mitigation, prevention, or treatment of⏎disease may fall outside the statutory device definition, while other low-risk products may be⏎subject to enforcement discretion.⏎Given that FDA operates within HHS, CHIME encourages the Department to further promote⏎greater cross-agency alignment and clarity regarding how risk-based oversight frameworks⏎apply to AI-enabled technologies across the continuum of wellness and clinical care. Doing so⏎would help ensure that regulatory boundaries are sufficiently clear for providers, support⏎appropriate accountability for higher-risk third-party AI tools, and reduce ambiguity that can slow⏎responsible and innovative adoption.⏎A recent peer-reviewed analysis published in JAMA highlights significant ambiguity in⏎accountability for evaluating the health effects of AI tools in healthcare. The article observes⏎that, while developers of AI tools subject to FDA review bear initial responsibility for evaluation,⏎such assessments may not extend to real-world clinical outcomes. Developers of tools exempt⏎from FDA clearance often design evaluations around product claims – such as subscriber⏎engagement for direct-to-consumer applications or financial performance for revenue cycle⏎management solutions – without systematically assessing potential health impacts. Healthcare⏎delivery organizations (HDOs) may conduct independent evaluations, yet many lack sufficient⏎funding or specialized expertise to do so comprehensively. Federal support through grants⏎exists but is limited, leaving patients and communities – critical stakeholders whose insights are⏎essential to understanding real-world effects – routinely excluded from these assessments.1⏎Mitigating Risk and Liability for Providers⏎AI use in healthcare is mostly voluntary at present, but as evidence of its effectiveness grows,⏎not using AI could be seen as falling below the standard of care, potentially exposing providers⏎to malpractice claims. Conversely, when AI is used and an adverse outcome occurs, liability⏎could involve the clinician, the health system, or the AI developer. Although relevant case law is⏎currently limited, all parties must have strategies to address potential liability risk.1 Healthcare⏎providers and systems face well-established risks – including malpractice liability, reputational⏎harm, regulatory or board sanctions, loss of medical licensure, credentialing restrictions, and⏎financial exposure – whereas AI developers are generally far better positioned financially to⏎withstand potential legal challenges.⏎Therefore, CHIME members believe that one of the most urgent policy needs is a clear⏎and workable framework for patient consent and liability related to the use of generative⏎AI in clinical care delivery. As generative AI tools become increasingly embedded across⏎clinical, administrative, and operational workflows, providers anticipate that such tools will soon⏎be ubiquitous and integral to routine care delivery.⏎It is neither feasible nor clinically appropriate for patients to opt out of all AI-enabled functions⏎within modern health systems, and that highly granular disclosures for each individual AI use⏎case would be operationally impractical and could undermine care delivery. At the same time,⏎6⏎⏎the absence of clear federal guidance creates uncertainty regarding consent obligations and⏎litigation risk.⏎Accordingly, CHIME recommends the establishment of a standardized, technology-⏎neutral patient consent framework and a legal safe harbor for the use of generative AI in⏎healthcare, analogous and aligned to existing informed consent and privacy frameworks⏎(i.e., HIPAA), to reduce legal ambiguity, support responsible innovation, and protect both⏎patients and providers. Such a framework could include model language, baseline disclosure⏎principles, and liability protections for good-faith use of AI consistent with federal standards,⏎thereby mitigating the risk of fragmented state-level requirements and opportunistic litigation⏎while preserving patient trust and transparency.⏎Removing Cost Barriers⏎CHIME members report that, although AI-enabled tools are improving care delivery and patient⏎engagement, current reimbursement structures do not adequately account for the associated⏎costs. One member described deploying an AI-driven agent to proactively contact patients with⏎hypertension and other chronic conditions to improve monitoring and adherence. While the⏎organization anticipates downstream clinical and financial benefits from improved outcomes, it is⏎currently absorbing the upfront and operational costs because no direct reimbursement pathway⏎exists for these services.⏎Similarly, another member described comparable challenges during telehealth implementation,⏎where the costs of equipment, infrastructure, and staffing facilitators exceeded available⏎reimbursement, despite measurable improvements in access and quality. These experiences⏎underscore a persistent misalignment between innovation that advances patient care and⏎payment models that fail to recognize or support the associated investments. In the absence of⏎sustainable reimbursement mechanisms, providers are effectively required to subsidize⏎technologies that enhance quality, safety, and patient engagement – an approach that is not⏎scalable over time.⏎One CHIME member – a regional safety-net/anchor system – shared that emerging⏎technologies, including AI-enabled clinical tools, are evaluated through structured governance⏎processes based on defined clinical use cases, business need, return on investment, and the⏎level of autonomy and associated patient safety risk. A core principle of these governance⏎frameworks is education and accountability – recognizing that, in the absence of comprehensive⏎regulatory oversight, providers bear primary responsibility for determining whether a technology⏎is safe, effective, and appropriate for clinical deployment.⏎To operationalize risk-based decision-making, this organization classifies AI and other emerging⏎technologies into categories such as novel, platform, or commodity, with each category⏎informing tailored acceptance criteria and risk-benefit thresholds. This tiered approach reflects⏎the need for differentiated regulatory and oversight expectations based on maturity, autonomy,⏎and clinical impact, and underscores the importance of aligning federal policy with provider-⏎driven risk governance models.⏎CHIME members cite AI-enabled radiology tools as illustrative of the current reimbursement⏎gap. These technologies are increasingly integrated into clinical workflows to perform⏎preliminary computational image analysis, support clinical decision-making, and algorithmically⏎prioritize radiologist worklists based on predicted acuity. Despite their measurable impact on⏎7⏎⏎workflow optimization, timeliness of interpretation, and patient safety, existing payment⏎methodologies do not clearly recognize or account for these technology-enabled services.⏎In practice, these systems rapidly analyze routine imaging studies upon acquisition, flag⏎potential abnormalities, and elevate high-risk cases for expedited clinician review. One member⏎indicated that this AI-driven triage can reduce turnaround times for routine films from⏎approximately 20 hours to roughly one hour, materially improving clinical workflow efficiency and⏎patient care timeliness. These tools are used as an adjunct to, rather than a replacement for,⏎clinician review, with radiologists continuing to perform final reads and clinical validation.⏎Importantly, this workflow introduces a dual-layer interpretive process: an initial algorithmic⏎assessment for triage and prioritization, followed by the radiologist’s definitive clinical⏎interpretation and diagnostic reporting. Both steps represent separate yet distinct contributions⏎to the diagnostic process and require governance, validation, and integration into clinical⏎operations.⏎Such workflows effectively create a dual-review paradigm – an initial AI-assisted prioritization or⏎preliminary assessment followed by clinician interpretation – yet current payment models do not⏎recognize the clinical value, operational efficiency, or quality improvement associated with AI-⏎enabled workflow optimization. At this time, the absence of a reimbursement mechanism or⏎established payment pathway for providers that have adopted this AI-driven triage model results⏎in providers bearing all of the associated costs.⏎HHS has an opportunity to advance its stated goals of reducing administrative burden,⏎accelerating responsible innovation, and improving patient access to timely, high-quality care by⏎providing clear, flexible policy guardrails for AI-enabled clinical workflows. Thoughtful⏎clarification regarding the regulatory and reimbursement treatment of tools including AI-assisted⏎triage and clinician interpretation would promote adoption of technologies that demonstrably⏎increase efficiency, support clinician decision-making, and improve patient outcomes, while⏎avoiding prescriptive requirements that could slow innovation or impose unnecessary⏎compliance costs on providers. We encourage the Department to pursue a risk-based,⏎outcomes-focused approach with reimbursement that enables providers to integrate AI⏎tools into clinical practice in a manner that is scalable, interoperable, and aligned with⏎patient safety and quality objectives.⏎Supporting Provider-led AI Innovation Efforts⏎CHIME member Baptist Health Jacksonville provides a compelling example of how health⏎systems can innovate effectively and efficiently to save the lives of some of the most vulnerable⏎patient populations they care for. Child sex trafficking is a hidden epidemic, with an estimated 1⏎in 7 endangered runaways in the U.S. being likely victims.3 In 2024 alone, the National Center⏎for Missing & Exploited Children received more than 27,800 reports of possible child sex⏎trafficking.3 Research indicates that nearly 88% of trafficking victims interact with healthcare⏎providers while being exploited, making healthcare providers uniquely situated to identify⏎potential trafficking cases and intervene.4 The organization developed and embedded a⏎pediatric emergency care screening protocol in its EHR to systematically detect potential child⏎sex trafficking. This protocol integrates automated risk alerts, trauma-informed clinician training,⏎3 National Center for Missing & Exploited Children. Child sex trafficking. MissingKids.org. Published 2025. Accessed September 2,⏎2025. https://www.missingkids.org/theissues/trafficking⏎4 Lederer LJ, Wetzel CA. The health consequences of sex trafficking and their implications for identifying victims in healthcare⏎facilities. Annals Health L. 2014;23:61.⏎8⏎⏎and streamlined reporting workflows. Over one year of implementation, the program achieved⏎high screening compliance and identified numerous at-risk children, and the tool has since been⏎incorporated into Epic’s EHR to enable broader dissemination to other health systems using the⏎platform.⏎There is a need for system-level adoption of such tools and policy measures, including funding⏎and training initiatives, to maximize healthcare organizations’ (HCOs) impact in the fight against⏎the significant public health crisis5 of child sex trafficking. Yet emergency departments (EDs),⏎already stretched thin, often miss opportunities to detect and aid trafficked children. The⏎experience at Baptist Health demonstrates that integrating specialized trafficking screenings into⏎EHR workflows can help close this gap. However, to use the screening tool successfully,⏎organizations must also train clinicians on how to sensitively screen patients and what to do⏎when a screening is positive.⏎Further research is needed to determine the screening tool’s accuracy and refine it to reduce⏎false positives and to improve its identification rate for younger children and non-English⏎speakers. Survivors of trafficking and frontline healthcare staff should be engaged in the design⏎and continuous improvement of screening tools to ensure that they are practical, ethical, and⏎effective.⏎To extend these successes, HHS should consider dedicated funding to help EDs implement⏎trafficking screening programs, hire specialized personnel, and provide ongoing staff education.⏎Similarly, the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) could reimburse screening and⏎intervention efforts as part of routine care. Health systems of all sizes have a role to play, not⏎only in treating the medical consequences of trafficking, but in helping to prevent it, starting from⏎the moment a vulnerable child comes through their doors.⏎HHS and CMS are well-positioned to support these and other innovative and effective efforts⏎through targeted funding, guidance, and reimbursement frameworks, thereby enabling health⏎systems to maximize their impact in addressing this and other significant public health crises.⏎This is just one example of the critical work our members are doing in their communities that⏎illustrates how – with modest resources – health systems can create impactful, evidence-⏎informed interventions that protect the most vulnerable patients and serve as a model for⏎replication nationwide.⏎Members have observed that as trust and reliance on AI tools increase, associated costs are⏎rising substantially. For example, one member noted that an AI imaging tool in use since 2019⏎has experienced a 110 percent cost increase, highlighting the potential financial burden on⏎HDOs and underscoring the need for consideration of cost impacts in AI adoption and⏎reimbursement policy.⏎CHIME encourages HHS to expand the range of incentives available to promote the use⏎of AI in clinical care, as such incentives are essential to accelerating adoption.⏎Additionally, CMS should consider implementing enhanced reimbursement mechanisms⏎or bonus payments for healthcare providers who deploy AI in clinical care, recognizing⏎the operational costs, efficiency gains, and measurable improvements in patient care⏎that such technologies can deliver.⏎5 Land A, Kino L. Florida ranks third in the number of reported human trafficking cases nationwide. WUFT News. Published⏎February 6, 2024. Accessed September 2, 2025. https://www.wuft.org/public-safety/2024-02-06/florida-ranks-third-in-thenumber-of-⏎reported-human-trafficking-cases-nationwide⏎9⏎⏎CHIME has and continues to be a staunch champion when it comes to the need for the use of⏎innovation and digital health solutions – including AI – aimed at facilitating better patient care.⏎However, it is imperative that providers across the full continuum of care – including acute,⏎ambulatory, post-acute, behavioral health, individual providers, and safety-net settings – are⏎deliberately and equally included in any AI framework’s design, implementation, and operational⏎support strategies. Further, Medicare spending for beneficiaries receiving post-acute care (PAC)⏎services is substantial and represents a significant opportunity for innovation. Given the⏎considerable resources CMS devotes to beneficiaries in long-term and post-acute care (LTPAC)⏎settings, targeted policy levers, aligned financial incentives, and dedicated technical assistance⏎will be essential to ensure these providers are fully included as the health system transitions⏎toward a more connected, interoperable, and patient-centered data environment.⏎Another way that regulatory burdens could be lessened is by updating the CMS Stark⏎and Office of the Inspector General (OIG) Anti-Kickback Statute (AKS) policies to clarify⏎the provisions involving cybersecurity donation policies. Larger providers continue to⏎express reluctance to donate cyber technology to other lesser-resourced organizations⏎because they fear they could incur liability if the smaller provider experiences a breach⏎or cyber incident. These liability fears have deterred better resourced organizations from⏎making these much-needed donations. Further, expanding the definition of what types of⏎technology can be donated (e.g., AI tools) would also spur greater uptake.⏎Providers are increasingly turning to chatbots for care management to enhance patient⏎engagement and streamline administrative tasks which may offer cost-effective care modalities.⏎These AI-powered chatbots can assist patients with appointment scheduling, medication⏎reminders, and answer frequently asked questions, thereby reducing the workload on⏎healthcare staff. Additionally, chatbots can provide personalized health advice and monitor⏎patient symptoms, ensuring timely interventions and improving overall care management. This⏎innovative approach not only improves efficiency but also ensures that patients receive⏎consistent and accessible support.⏎One member piloting an AI-enabled conversational system reported meaningful reductions in⏎depression, loneliness, and feelings of social isolation among residents in a senior living⏎community, describing the technology as a “game changer” for resident engagement. This⏎experience was highlighted in The Wall Street Journal in June 2025, which reported early⏎evidence that AI-enabled conversational companions may alleviate loneliness and improve⏎mental health among older adults in senior living settings.⏎Recent peer-reviewed research further supports these observations. A feasibility study6⏎published in the Journal of the American Medical Directors Association evaluated an AI–based,⏎voice-driven telephone intervention designed to facilitate conversational engagement among⏎older adults in post-acute and long-term care (PALTC) settings. Participants engaged with the⏎system through scheduled and on-demand telephone interactions over a four-week period.⏎“Preliminary analyses showed reductions in depressive and anxiety symptoms, especially⏎among participants with higher baseline severity.”6⏎Loneliness, depression, and anxiety are prevalent among older adults in PALTC settings and⏎are associated with adverse health outcomes, increased healthcare utilization, and diminished⏎quality of life. Traditional psychosocial interventions often face workforce constraints and⏎6 Palace, Zachary J., et al. “Leveraging an AI-driven voice companion to enhance mental health in older adults in post-acute and⏎long-term care settings: A feasibility study.” Journal of the American Medical Directors Association, vol. 27, no. 3, Mar. 2026, p.⏎106047, https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jamda.2025.106047.⏎10⏎⏎challenges related to scalability and sustained engagement. AI-enabled conversational tools⏎may serve as a scalable adjunct to existing care models, supporting social engagement and⏎behavioral health monitoring while reducing strain on clinical and caregiving staff. Such tools⏎may also support family caregivers by providing consistent social engagement and monitoring⏎signals between visits, potentially reducing caregiver burden and improving communication with⏎care teams. Additional research is needed, with the study noting that, “Future work should⏎include larger, randomized controlled trials with extended follow-up to validate results and⏎explore population-specific benefits.” However, the early findings suggest that AI-driven⏎conversational technologies have significant potential to enhance patient-centered care and⏎support healthy aging.⏎Tools such as AI-enabled conversational companions in PALTC settings illustrate the potential of⏎non-diagnostic AI to address social isolation, support behavioral health, and augment limited⏎workforce capacity. HHS could further support pre- and post-deployment evaluation⏎processes for non-medical AI tools that show promise through targeted federal funding⏎and coordination mechanisms. Grants and cooperative agreements through the⏎Department could support pragmatic clinical trials, implementation science studies, and⏎standardized evaluation frameworks for these tools, including those used for⏎conversational AI in aging and behavioral health contexts. Prize competitions could⏎incentivize scalable and interoperable tools. These mechanisms would accelerate⏎evidence generation while reducing duplicative testing burdens on providers and⏎vendors.⏎A coordinated federal approach to evaluation of non-medical AI tools – grounded in risk-based,⏎outcomes-focused principles – could support responsible innovation, promote interoperability,⏎and reduce burden while ensuring patient-centered adoption of AI-enabled technologies across⏎the healthcare system.⏎Frameworks & Standards⏎As providers chart their AI course, they are looking for ways to deploy and use AI in an ethical⏎manner. Many hospitals and healthcare systems have AI councils, acceptable use, and/or⏎governance policies established within their organizations, though rural, safety-net, and smaller⏎providers are either just getting started or have not yet taken the leap. Providers may also be⏎served by having structures and processes to report potential safety events related to the AI⏎tools. In the beginning, this could be an internal process. There are emerging AI frameworks⏎and standards that serve as a good starting point for HDOs embarking on or refining their AI⏎adoption journey. They are intended to enable responsible, ethical, and effective advancements⏎in health AI on behalf of the patients and communities they serve. Resources exist to help⏎providers navigate the AI journey; CHIME’s AI Principles offer a detailed overview of existing⏎standards and frameworks, and below we offer a high-level overview of just a few.⏎The NIST AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF), which was developed in conjunction with⏎the private sector, and “intended for voluntary use and to improve the ability to incorporate⏎trustworthiness considerations into the design, development, use, and evaluation of AI products,⏎services, and systems.” America’s AI Action Plan calls for revising the existing Framework.⏎Providers are already deeply familiar with the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF) and have⏎voluntarily adopted it. Released in 2023, the NIST AI RMF offers a complementary framework to⏎better manage risks to individuals, organizations, and society associated with AI. NIST is also⏎developing a way to reach consensus faster around AI standards development – typically a⏎lengthy process – through their “Zero Drafts” pilot project.⏎11⏎⏎The National Academy of Medicine’s Artificial Intelligence Code of Conduct is “aimed at⏎providing a guiding framework to ensure that AI algorithms and their application in health, health⏎care, and biomedical science perform accurately, safely, reliably, and ethically in the service of⏎better health for all.”⏎The Health AI Partnership (HAIP) is a multi-stakeholder, provider-driven effort spearheaded by⏎Duke University, and offers tools to help providers navigate the purchase and deployment of AI⏎including designing and testing clinician workflow and disseminating information to end users.⏎Their Health Equity Across the AI Lifecyle (HEAAL) Framework is community-informed⏎framework developed through extensive collaboration with an interdisciplinary team of⏎developers and inputs from seventy seven practitioners involved in various functions of HDOs.⏎HEAAL is a comprehensive measure intended to be used whenever an HDO considers⏎implementing an AI solution – and is empowering providers to safely and fairly use AI through⏎the use of standards to focus on procurement, development, clinical integration, and lifecycle⏎management.⏎Privacy, Security, & Third-Party Solutions⏎AI’s integration into healthcare heightens the need for robust cybersecurity and privacy⏎protections. Providers face increasing regulatory burdens, fragmented policies, and rising⏎threats from AI-driven cyberattacks; therefore, the critical and inextricable domain of⏎cybersecurity as it intersects with the clinical deployment of AI cannot and should not be⏎ignored. CHIME’s AI Principles recognize that trustworthy, scalable, and clinically responsible AI⏎cannot exist apart from a resilient cybersecurity posture, given the uniquely targeted threat⏎environment facing healthcare providers and the sensitivity, volume, and interoperability of⏎clinical data assets.⏎We therefore urge the Department to evaluate clinical AI policy within the broader⏎operational reality confronting hospitals and health systems – namely, a persistently⏎hostile cyber landscape marked by ransomware, third-party supply chain vulnerabilities,⏎and adversarial nation-state activity – while aligning federal expectations for AI⏎governance with existing statutory and regulatory obligations under the Health Insurance⏎Portability & Accountability Act (HIPAA), the Health Information Technology for Economic⏎and Clinical Health Act (HITECH), and related frameworks. As further detailed in our⏎principles, cybersecurity must be treated not as an ancillary safeguard, but as a foundational⏎prerequisite to safe, ethical, and sustainable clinical AI adoption.⏎Vendors Routinely Shift More Risk to Providers⏎HIPAA privacy protections extend only to HIPAA covered entities (CEs) which include providers,⏎payers and healthcare clearinghouses, as well as any business associates (BAs) of these⏎entities. Companies that have access to PHI and do business with a HIPAA CE are required by⏎law to safeguard the data. However, some vendors try to avoid risk by refusing to agree to⏎certain provider terms and provisions in BAAs, shifting more risk to providers. As providers ink⏎agreements with AI vendors, they have reported vendors attempting to shift liability by refusing⏎to accept key terms in the BAA or overriding restrictions in the BAA – exposing them to⏎additional risk.⏎Our members also report vendors using contracts that append the BAA as an attachment and⏎use language in the main contract that overrides all other language as it pertains to data use.⏎12⏎⏎Some AI vendors have refused to sign BAAs with providers, while others have elected to train⏎their models using de-identified data instead. Although HIPAA requires a standard BAA, the⏎regulatory language sets only a minimum threshold—stating that a BA may use or disclose PHI⏎solely as permitted by the agreement with the CE. The challenge arises when vendors embed⏎broad data use rights into the main service agreements, effectively circumventing the intent of⏎the BAA. Many vendors assert that de-identified data falls outside HIPAA’s scope, using this⏎claim to justify expansive data use, often in ways that make providers uncomfortable. However,⏎de-identification is highly subjective and context-dependent, and vendor language—such as⏎assurances that “de-identified data will not be used in any way that could identify a natural⏎human”—raises serious concerns about enforceability and the potential for re-identification. As⏎HHS’ OCR has emphasized, the Privacy Rule recognizes two permissible de-identification⏎methodologies: (1) a formal determination by a qualified expert, or (2) the removal of specified⏎identifiers coupled with the absence of actual knowledge that the remaining information could be⏎used to identify an individual. Importantly, even when these methodologies are properly applied,⏎the resulting data retains a residual risk of identification; while the probability is very small, it is⏎not zero, and de-identified data may, in certain circumstances, be linked back to the individual to⏎whom it pertains.⏎In a perfect world, the original intent of HIPAA would be reinforced: data should be used solely⏎for the purposes outlined in the agreement and only in ways that directly support the services for⏎which the provider has contracted. Unfortunately, many providers cannot afford to walk⏎away from certain vendors, placing them in an impossible bind—underscoring the urgent⏎need for increased oversight by the Office for Civil Rights (OCR) to ensure that the spirit⏎of HIPAA is upheld.⏎Healthcare providers are increasingly constrained when purchasing and procuring applications,⏎medical devices, AI solutions, and other digital technologies. Vendors often deliver products with⏎insufficient baseline security assurances, forcing providers to shoulder the burden of conducting⏎individual risk assessments that are costly, duplicative, and resource-intensive. This fragmented⏎approach undermines efficiency, slows adoption of innovative tools, and can leave critical⏎vulnerabilities unaddressed across the ecosystem.⏎Third-Party Risk and Burden for Providers⏎A marketplace should be established for certified apps – including those leveraging AI –⏎which have demonstrated their ability to meet a baseline set of security requirements.⏎Establishing consistent, transparent, and enforceable security standards for third-party⏎technologies is essential to reducing provider burden, strengthening supply chain⏎resilience, and protecting patients from escalating cyber threats.⏎Providers continue to grapple with substantial vulnerabilities inherent in third-party solutions,⏎underscoring the need for remedial action to safeguard the confidentiality, integrity, and⏎availability of Americans’ health data. Providers need confidence that the technologies they⏎purchase are secure; however, in the absence of a uniform set of standards that must be met,⏎our members are forced to conduct their own risk assessments when procuring essential⏎medical devices, apps, and AI solutions.⏎A rigorous review of third-party devices and applications is needed to ensure compliance and⏎security best practices. Providers are saddled with these time-consuming and costly processes⏎as they conduct their own assessments on the same technology being purchased by thousands⏎of other providers, which is inefficient, redundant, and a waste of precious healthcare resources.⏎13⏎⏎When delivering updates to software that deliver new AI capabilities or updates or changes AI⏎components, vendors should be required to disclose those AI components, the type of AI and⏎the data used, as well as disclose their related security assessment including addressing⏎relevant threats and vulnerabilities. CHIME firmly believes that shifting risk and burden onto⏎providers is slowing AI adoption in clinical care.⏎Conclusion⏎In closing, CHIME appreciates the opportunity to provide input in response to this RFI and to⏎contribute to HHS-wide policy development concerning the clinical use of AI. On behalf of our⏎members, we offer practical, provider-informed recommendations grounded in real-world⏎implementation experience.⏎Our comments are intended to assist the Department in determining how its regulatory,⏎reimbursement, and research and development authorities can be most effectively calibrated to⏎support the responsible and timely adoption of AI in clinical care; foster public trust and⏎confidence in modern health technologies; reduce regulatory and liability uncertainty that may⏎impede innovation; and better align federal incentives so that AI deployment enhances⏎productivity, alleviates administrative burden, lowers healthcare costs, and improves health⏎outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities.⏎To realize AI’s full potential, providers must be equipped with the knowledge, resources,⏎technical infrastructure, and policy certainty necessary to overcome adoption barriers. CHIME⏎and its members remain steadfast in their commitment to leveraging technology to deliver high-⏎quality, patient-centered care and to advancing interoperability and secure, appropriate access⏎to health information across the care continuum. At the same time, we respectfully urge HHS to⏎ensure that future rulemaking or guidance does not inadvertently impose duplicative or⏎prescriptive requirements that could divert limited resources away from patient care,⏎investments in digital technology such as AI tools, or cybersecurity investments.⏎CHIME and our members look forward to continuing to be a trusted stakeholder to the⏎Department, and stand ready to serve as an ongoing resource as HHS advances its AI policy⏎objectives. Collaborative engagement between the public and private sectors will be essential to⏎achieving our shared goals and ensuring that AI strengthens – rather than complicates – the⏎delivery of healthcare in the United States.⏎Should you have any questions or if we can be of assistance, please contact Chelsea Arnone,⏎Senior Director, Federal Affairs at carnone@chimecentral.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Russell P. Branzell, CHCIO, LCHIME⏎President and CEO⏎CHIME⏎14♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0259♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bethany R. Russell, Ph.D., P.A. , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Bethany R. Russell, Ph.D., P.A.♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS HEALTH SECTOR AI RFI RESPONSE⏎Submitted by:⏎Bethany R. Russell, Ph.D., LMHC, RPT, NCC⏎Assistant Professor, Florida Gulf Coast University⏎Principal, Bethany R. Russell, Ph.D., P.A.⏎Date: February 11, 2026⏎Docket: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎As a licensed mental health counselor, researcher, and AI clinical technology developer, I submit⏎this response to advocate for accelerated adoption of artificial intelligence in clinical mental⏎health care. My recommendations center on three critical reforms: (1) establishing⏎performance-based reimbursement models that reward measurable clinical outcomes rather than⏎service volume; (2) creating a uniform federal gold standard for psychotherapy diagnosis to⏎ensure accurate, consistent, and specific clinical assessments; and (3) prioritizing⏎biomarker-validated preventative care interventions that leverage AI to identify at-risk⏎populations before crisis occurs.⏎The current mental health care system operates with inadequate diagnostic precision, reactive⏎rather than preventative care models, and payment structures that disincentivize innovation. AI⏎technologies—when properly validated and implemented—offer transformative potential to⏎address these systemic failures while reducing costs and improving patient outcomes.⏎I. REGULATION: ESTABLISHING CLEAR PATHWAYS FOR NON-MEDICAL⏎DEVICE AI TOOLS⏎Current Regulatory Barriers⏎The most significant barrier to AI adoption in mental health care is regulatory uncertainty for⏎non-medical device AI tools. Unlike FDA-regulated medical devices, AI-enabled digital mental⏎health interventions exist in a regulatory gray zone where:⏎• Liability frameworks remain undefined when AI recommendations inform clinical⏎decision-making⏎• Privacy and security requirements vary across state and federal jurisdictions⏎• Clinical validation standards are inconsistent or absent⏎⏎• Reimbursement eligibility is unclear⏎These ambiguities create prohibitive risk for healthcare organizations considering AI adoption,⏎particularly in mental health where subjective clinical judgment has historically dominated⏎practice.⏎Recommended Regulatory Framework⏎HHS should establish a clear, tiered regulatory pathway for non-medical device AI clinical tools⏎based on risk level and clinical impact:⏎Tier 1 - Administrative AI Tools: Minimal regulation for tools performing scheduling,⏎documentation, billing support, or other non-clinical functions.⏎Tier 2 - Clinical Decision Support: Moderate oversight for AI tools that provide clinical⏎recommendations while maintaining clinician authority over final decisions. This tier should⏎include:⏎• Mandatory pre-deployment validation studies demonstrating clinical utility⏎• Post-deployment monitoring requirements with annual performance reporting⏎• Clear liability frameworks establishing that clinicians retain ultimate responsibility for patient⏎care⏎• Privacy and security requirements aligned with HIPAA but simplified for implementation⏎Tier 3 - Autonomous Clinical Interventions: Stringent regulation for AI tools that deliver⏎therapeutic interventions directly to patients without real-time clinician oversight.⏎A Uniform Federal Gold Standard for Psychotherapy Diagnosis⏎Mental health diagnosis currently suffers from unacceptable variability. The DSM-5 provides⏎categorical frameworks with overlap of symptoms and lacking real-time updates, but real-world⏎application varies dramatically based on clinician training, theoretical orientation, assessment⏎time, and reimbursement pressures. This diagnostic imprecision undermines treatment⏎effectiveness, research validity, and quality measurement.⏎HHS should mandate a uniform federal gold standard for psychotherapy diagnosis that includes:⏎1. Structured Clinical Assessment Protocols: Require use of validated, standardized assessment⏎instruments (e.g., PHQ-9, GAD-7, PCL-5) administered at intake and regular intervals.⏎⏎2. Diagnostic Criteria Validation: Establish minimum evidentiary thresholds for diagnosis,⏎including symptom duration, severity scores, and functional impairment measures.⏎3. AI-Enabled Diagnostic Support: Promote AI tools that integrate multiple data sources⏎(structured assessments, clinical interviews, patient-reported outcomes) to generate differential⏎diagnoses with confidence levels and supporting evidence.⏎4. Continuous Diagnostic Refinement: Require diagnostic review at predetermined intervals with⏎AI-assisted tracking of symptom trajectories and treatment response patterns.⏎5. Interoperability Requirements: Mandate that all diagnostic data use standardized terminologies⏎(ICD-10, SNOMED CT) and exchange formats (FHIR) to enable longitudinal tracking and⏎research.⏎This gold standard would dramatically improve diagnostic accuracy, enable meaningful⏎outcomes measurement, and create the data infrastructure necessary for AI-driven quality⏎improvement.⏎Specific CFR Citations for Revision⏎45 CFR Part 164 (HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules): Revise to explicitly address AI-enabled⏎clinical tools, clarifying that:⏎• AI processing of protected health information for clinical decision support constitutes permitted⏎"treatment" operations⏎• De-identification standards account for re-identification risks in AI model training datasets⏎• Patients have explicit rights to understand how AI influences their care⏎42 CFR Part 2 (Substance Use Disorder Records): Modernize to permit AI-enabled care⏎coordination for substance use disorders while maintaining patient privacy protections.⏎II. REIMBURSEMENT: PERFORMANCE-BASED METRICS THAT INCENTIVIZE⏎OUTCOMES⏎Current Payment Model Failures⏎Fee-for-service reimbursement in mental health care creates perverse incentives:⏎• Clinicians are paid for time spent, not outcomes achieved⏎• High-volume, low-complexity services are financially rewarded over intensive,⏎outcome-focused interventions (the acute intervention becomes a chronic intervention)⏎⏎• Preventative care is undercompensated relative to crisis management⏎• AI tools that improve efficiency reduce billable time, disincentivizing adoption⏎These structural flaws explain why mental health care consistently fails to achieve optimal⏎outcomes despite massive expenditures.⏎Performance-Based Payment Reform⏎HHS should transition mental health reimbursement from volume-based to performance-based⏎models using AI-enabled measurement:⏎Outcome-Linked Payment Tiers:⏎• Base rate: Standard reimbursement for services meeting minimum quality thresholds⏎• Performance bonus: 15-25% additional reimbursement for achieving predefined outcome⏎targets⏎Measurable Performance Metrics:⏎• Symptom reduction: Percentage of patients achieving clinically significant improvement on⏎validated instruments⏎• Functional improvement: Changes in work attendance, social functioning, quality of life⏎measures⏎• Crisis prevention: Reduction in emergency department visits, hospitalizations, suicide attempts⏎• Treatment engagement: Retention rates, session attendance, homework completion⏎• Recovery maintenance: Sustained symptom remission at 6 and 12-month follow-up⏎AI-Enabled Measurement Infrastructure:⏎• Real-time outcomes dashboards aggregating patient-reported outcomes⏎• Predictive analytics identifying patients at risk of treatment dropout or clinical deterioration⏎• Automated risk stratification for care intensity allocation⏎• Benchmarking tools comparing provider performance against regional and national norms⏎Preventative Care Premium Payments⏎HHS should create enhanced reimbursement for AI-enabled preventative interventions that⏎demonstrate reduced downstream costs:⏎• Population Health Screening: Reimburse AI-powered screening tools that identify at-risk⏎individuals in primary care, occupational health, or community settings before mental health⏎crises develop⏎⏎• Biomarker-Validated Prevention: Provide premium payments for preventative interventions⏎targeting individuals with validated biomarkers of mental health risk (e.g., inflammation⏎markers, HPA axis dysregulation, genetic risk profiles)⏎• Early Intervention Bonuses: Offer enhanced reimbursement for treating individuals in⏎prodromal or early-stage conditions before full disorder onset⏎This approach aligns financial incentives with public health objectives while creating market⏎demand for AI tools that enable precision prevention.⏎Case Example: MPowerMe.app⏎MPowerMe.app represents the type of AI-enabled tool that performance-based payment would⏎incentivize. This digital therapeutic platform delivers evidence-based interventions for anxiety⏎and depression with:⏎• Validated clinical outcomes: Demonstrated symptom reduction in controlled trials⏎• Real-time progress monitoring: Continuous measurement enabling adaptive treatment⏎• Scalable delivery: Cost-effective intervention reaching patients unable to access traditional care⏎• Preventative potential: Early intervention capability before crisis-level severity⏎Under current fee-for-service models, tools like MPowerMe.app face reimbursement barriers⏎because they don't fit traditional "therapy session" billing codes. Performance-based payment⏎would instead reward them for achieving measurable clinical improvements at lower cost than⏎traditional care delivery.⏎III. RESEARCH & DEVELOPMENT: BIOMARKER-VALIDATED PREVENTATIVE CARE⏎Priority Research Areas⏎HHS should prioritize R&D funding for AI applications that enable biomarker-validated⏎preventative mental health care:⏎1. Biomarker Discovery and Validation:⏎• Identification of inflammatory, metabolic, genetic, and neurophysiological markers predicting⏎mental health risk⏎• Development of accessible, cost-effective biomarker assessment tools⏎• Validation studies linking biomarker profiles to specific intervention responses⏎⏎2. Precision Prevention Algorithms:⏎• AI models integrating biomarkers, clinical assessments, and social determinants to predict⏎individual risk trajectories⏎• Decision support tools recommending preventative interventions matched to risk profiles⏎• Population health surveillance systems identifying communities at elevated risk⏎3. Adaptive Intervention Platforms:⏎• AI-enabled digital therapeutics that personalize content based on real-time response patterns⏎• Predictive analytics identifying optimal treatment timing and intensity⏎• Automated escalation protocols when preventative interventions prove insufficient⏎4. Implementation Science:⏎• Workflow integration studies addressing how AI tools fit into existing clinical practice⏎• Health equity research ensuring AI benefits reach underserved populations⏎• Economic analyses quantifying cost savings from preventative AI interventions⏎Recommended Funding Mechanisms⏎• Public-Private Partnerships: CRADAs between NIH/NIMH and AI developers to accelerate⏎tool validation while maintaining scientific rigor⏎• Prize Competitions: Incentivize development of AI tools achieving specific benchmarks (e.g.,⏎30% reduction in depression symptoms within 8 weeks at <$200 per patient)⏎• Cooperative Agreements: Support healthcare systems implementing and evaluating AI tools in⏎real-world settings with shared data contributing to public knowledge⏎• Small Business Innovation Research (SBIR) Grants: Fund early-stage AI mental health⏎companies conducting rigorous validation studies, and not only fund the large developers, take⏎VC approach and offer seed money to thousands of companies in the form of tax credits, some⏎companies might just “hit it out of the park”.⏎IV. RESPONSES TO SPECIFIC RFI QUESTIONS⏎Question 1: Biggest Barriers to Private Sector AI Innovation⏎Regulatory Uncertainty: Developers cannot assess liability exposure or regulatory compliance⏎requirements for non-medical device tools. Especially with a state-by-state patchwork of⏎⏎regulation and pending legislation.⏎Reimbursement Ambiguity: Without clear payment pathways, AI tools lack sustainable business⏎models.⏎Data Access Limitations: Restrictive data sharing policies prevent AI model development and⏎validation.⏎Clinical Validation Requirements: Lack of standardized evaluation frameworks creates⏎prohibitive validation costs.⏎Question 2: Priority Regulatory and Payment Policy Changes⏎See detailed recommendations in Sections I and II above. Key priorities:⏎• Establish clear regulatory tiers for AI clinical tools (45 CFR 164 revisions)⏎• Implement performance-based payment models (42 CFR 414)⏎• Mandate uniform diagnostic standards in mental health⏎• Create preventative care premium payments⏎Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical Devices⏎Liability Allocation: When AI recommendations inform clinical decisions, liability frameworks⏎must clearly delineate responsibility between AI developers, healthcare organizations, and⏎individual clinicians.⏎HHS should issue guidance establishing that:⏎• Clinicians using AI decision support tools as intended retain professional liability for patient⏎care⏎• AI developers are liable for algorithmic errors not reasonably detectable by clinicians⏎• Healthcare organizations must establish governance processes for AI tool selection and⏎monitoring⏎Indemnification Structures: HHS should facilitate development of standardized indemnification⏎agreements between AI vendors and healthcare organizations.⏎Privacy Beyond HIPAA: AI tools that learn from patient data may create privacy risks not⏎addressed by current regulations. HHS should clarify requirements for:⏎• Patient consent for AI-assisted care⏎• Transparency about how AI influences clinical decisions⏎⏎• Data retention and deletion requirements for AI model training datasets⏎Question 4: Most Promising AI Evaluation Methods for Non-Medical Devices⏎Pre-Deployment Evaluation:⏎• Randomized controlled trials comparing AI-enabled care to standard care⏎• Prospective validation studies in diverse clinical settings⏎• Algorithmic bias testing across demographic subgroups⏎• Usability testing with clinicians and patients⏎Post-Deployment Evaluation:⏎• Continuous performance monitoring with automated alerting for degradation⏎• A/B testing of algorithm refinements⏎• Adverse event surveillance systems⏎• Patient and clinician satisfaction metrics⏎HHS Support Mechanisms:⏎• Grants for independent validation studies conducted by academic medical centers⏎• Cooperative agreements establishing AI evaluation networks across healthcare systems⏎• Contracts developing standardized evaluation frameworks and benchmarking tools⏎• Prize competitions for novel evaluation methodologies⏎Question 5: Supporting Private Sector Accreditation and Certification⏎HHS should facilitate industry-driven quality assurance through:⏎• Accreditation Standards Recognition: Endorse or co-develop accreditation programs (e.g.,⏎through Joint Commission, NCQA) specific to AI clinical tools⏎• Professional Credentialing: Support training and certification programs for clinicians using AI⏎tools (e.g., "AI-Assisted Psychotherapy" credentials)⏎• Industry Testing Consortia: Fund pre-competitive collaborations establishing shared testing⏎environments and datasets for AI tool evaluation⏎• Quality Registries: Support creation of national registries tracking AI tool performance in⏎real-world settings⏎Question 6: AI Tools Meeting/Exceeding Expectations vs. Falling Short⏎⏎Success Stories:⏎• Suicide risk prediction: AI tools analyzing EHR data to identify high-risk patients enabling⏎preventative interventions⏎• Treatment matching: Algorithms predicting optimal antidepressant responses based on clinical⏎and genetic profiles⏎• Teletherapy efficiency: AI-assisted documentation reducing clinician administrative burden⏎Shortcomings:⏎• Diagnostic AI tools trained on narrow populations demonstrating bias in diverse settings⏎• Chatbot interventions with high dropout rates due to poor engagement⏎• Clinical decision support tools creating alert fatigue and workflow disruption⏎Greatest Potential Impact:⏎• Precision prevention tools using biomarkers to identify at-risk individuals for early intervention⏎• Adaptive digital therapeutics personalizing interventions based on real-time response patterns⏎• Population health surveillance systems predicting mental health crises in communities⏎• Automated quality measurement reducing documentation burden while improving⏎accountability⏎Question 7: Key Decision Makers and Administrative Hurdles⏎Most Influential Roles:⏎• Chief Medical Officers and Chief Information Officers jointly determine AI adoption in health⏎systems⏎• Payer Medical Directors control reimbursement policy enabling or constraining AI use⏎• Practice Administrators in smaller settings make purchasing decisions based on ROI⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles:⏎• Procurement processes requiring extensive vendor due diligence⏎• IT integration challenges with legacy EHR systems⏎• Staff training requirements and workflow disruption during implementation⏎• Lack of clear ROI metrics demonstrating value⏎Question 8: Interoperability Priorities for AI Development⏎Data Types:⏎• Patient-reported outcomes (PROs) using standardized instruments⏎• Clinical assessments and diagnoses using structured terminologies⏎⏎• Treatment plans and interventions with machine-readable coding⏎• Biomarker data (laboratory results, genetic profiles, wearable device outputs)⏎Data Standards:⏎• FHIR for health data exchange⏎• LOINC for laboratory and clinical observations⏎• SNOMED CT for clinical concepts⏎• ICD-10 for diagnoses⏎Benchmarking Tools:⏎• National quality measure repositories with machine-readable specifications⏎• Standardized AI model performance metrics enabling cross-tool comparison⏎• Open datasets for algorithm validation and bias testing⏎Enhanced interoperability would enable:⏎• AI models trained on multi-institutional datasets improving generalizability⏎• Real-time data sharing enabling population health surveillance⏎• Longitudinal patient data access improving predictive accuracy⏎• Reduced vendor lock-in fostering competitive AI tool markets⏎Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎Challenges Patients Want Addressed:⏎• Access barriers: Long wait times, geographic limitations, cost constraints, language⏎• Treatment effectiveness: Persistent symptoms despite multiple interventions⏎• Fragmented care: Poor coordination between mental health and medical providers⏎• Stigma: Discrimination and privacy concerns limiting treatment seeking⏎Patient Concerns About AI:⏎• Privacy: Fear of data breaches or discriminatory use of mental health information⏎• Depersonalization: Preference for human connection over algorithmic interactions⏎• Accuracy: Concern about AI errors in high-stakes clinical decisions⏎• Autonomy: Desire to maintain control over treatment decisions⏎Addressing Concerns:⏎• Transparency: Clear communication about how AI supports rather than replaces clinicians⏎• Privacy protections: Robust data security and explicit consent for AI use⏎• Human oversight: Maintaining clinician involvement in all significant clinical decisions⏎• Patient choice: Allowing patients to opt out of AI-assisted care without penalty⏎⏎Question 10: Priority AI Research Areas⏎High-Priority Research:⏎a) Biomarker-Validated Prevention: Studies linking measurable biological, psychological, and⏎social markers to mental health risk with AI-enabled predictive models⏎b) Treatment Personalization: Research identifying which interventions work for which⏎individuals under what circumstances⏎c) Implementation Science: Studies addressing how AI tools integrate into clinical workflows,⏎organizational structures, and payment systems⏎d) Health Equity: Research ensuring AI tools reduce rather than exacerbate mental health⏎disparities⏎e) Long-term Outcomes: Longitudinal studies tracking sustained impact of AI-enabled⏎interventions beyond immediate symptom reduction⏎Published Impact Findings:⏎The literature on adopted AI tools in mental health care is emerging but limited. Systematic⏎reviews identify:⏎• Moderate evidence for AI-enhanced suicide risk prediction⏎• Promising but inconsistent evidence for digital therapeutics effectiveness⏎• Limited evidence for AI-driven diagnostic accuracy improvements⏎• Minimal evidence on cost-effectiveness and long-term sustainability⏎This evidence gap underscores the need for HHS-funded research evaluating real-world AI tool⏎impact.⏎Literature on Costs, Benefits, and Transfers:⏎Current literature approaches AI costs and benefits primarily through:⏎• Clinical trial efficacy studies (benefits to patients)⏎• Health economic analyses (cost per quality-adjusted life year)⏎• Implementation research (organizational costs and workflow impacts)⏎Critical gaps exist regarding:⏎• Transfer effects: How AI efficiency gains affect clinician employment, income, and⏎⏎professional satisfaction⏎• Equity impacts: Whether AI tools reduce or exacerbate disparities based on race, geography, or⏎socioeconomic status⏎• System-level effects: How AI adoption in one care setting impacts utilization and costs in other⏎settings⏎V. CONCLUSION⏎Artificial intelligence offers transformative potential to address fundamental failures in mental⏎health care delivery: diagnostic imprecision, reactive rather than preventative approaches,⏎misaligned financial incentives, and inadequate access. Realizing this potential requires bold⏎HHS action to establish clear regulatory pathways, implement performance-based payment⏎models, and prioritize biomarker-validated preventative care research.⏎The recommendations in this response center on three core principles:⏎1. Regulatory Clarity: Establish tiered oversight frameworks and uniform diagnostic standards⏎that enable innovation while protecting patients⏎2. Payment Alignment: Transition from volume-based to performance-based reimbursement that⏎rewards measurable clinical outcomes⏎3. Prevention Priority: Invest in AI tools that identify and intervene with at-risk populations⏎before crisis occurs⏎These reforms would create market conditions favoring AI tools like MPowerMe.app that⏎demonstrate clinical effectiveness, cost-efficiency, and scalable access. They would accelerate⏎adoption of technologies enabling precision mental health care while maintaining the therapeutic⏎relationships that remain central to effective treatment.⏎I respectfully urge HHS to prioritize these recommendations in shaping policy that unleashes⏎AI's potential to transform mental health care for all Americans.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Bethany R. Russell, Ph.D., LMHC, RPT, NCC⏎Principal, Bethany R. Russell, Ph.D., P.A.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0260♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Wolters Kluwer, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Wolters Kluwer♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Response from Wolters Kluwer to HHS’ Request for Information on⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Care⏎Below is Wolters Kluwer’s response to the Department of Health and Human Services’⏎Request for Information (RFI) entitled Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13). We appreciate the opportunity to share⏎our views.⏎As way of background, Wolters Kluwer is a leading global provider of clinical⏎technology and evidence-based solutions that assist effective decision-making and outcomes⏎across the healthcare continuum. Key solutions include UpToDate®, UpToDate® Lexidrug™,⏎UpToDate® Patient Engagement, Medi-Span®, Sentri7®, Lippincott® Solutions, Ovid®, and⏎Health Language®. Wolters Kluwer had annual revenue in 2024 of $6.4 billion.⏎Summary of Our Policy Recommendations⏎To help accelerate the adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care, HHS should⏎define a regulatory policy framework that focuses on the following principles:⏎1) FDA should focus its active regulatory enforcement on AI-enabled medical devices⏎that are fully “agentic,” and impact patient care without human intervention;⏎2) FDA should exercise enforcement discretion for any AI-enabled medical devices⏎featuring ‘human-in-the-loop’ design;⏎3) HHS should define transparency and disclosure standards that are well-understood,⏎predictable and proportionate to substantial risks to patients;⏎4) Existing copyright laws and protections for medical content used to develop AI⏎solutions supporting clinical care should continue to be enforced;⏎5) Existing state medical malpractice laws that seek to optimize the safe practice of⏎medicine should be maintained.⏎Discussion⏎In the RFI, HHS notes that it seeks to “establish a regulatory posture on AI that is well⏎understood, predictable, and proportionate to any risks presented to enable rapid innovation⏎while protecting patients and the confidentiality of their identifiable health information and⏎maintaining public trust.” We strongly agree with these overarching goals, particularly in⏎creating a regulatory framework that is predictable and easy to understand for developers, and⏎fosters trust among clinicians and medical professionals.⏎To strike the appropriate balance between rewarding innovation for AI solutions while⏎also ensuring such solutions are effective and safe for patients, we recommend FDA focus its⏎active regulatory oversight on AI-enabled device software functions that pose the highest risk to⏎patient safety. In our view, these are functions that are fully “agentic,” meaning they can act⏎autonomously, making care decisions and/or acting without human intervention.⏎⏎Though the deployment of agentic functions can add significant value and efficiency to⏎the clinical workflow, freeing up trained human professionals to address more complex patients,⏎they also pose significant threats to patient safety if the software does not perform as designed.⏎Remaining AI-enabled device software functions that are non-agentic, and thus incapable of⏎acting independently of human intervention, would still technically remain under FDA’s⏎regulatory authority, but be subject to enforcement discretion. FDA could provide guidance that⏎defines the terms of such discretion. The software’s design would require a trained human⏎intermediary to make a clinical decision based on their experience and judgment, with the⏎software itself having no agentic capability.⏎We were gratified to hear FDA Commissioner Marty Makary, in addressing the⏎Consumer Electronics Show in early January, appear to endorse such an approach. In his⏎remarks, Dr. Makary indicated that the Agency’s focus going forward will be to “apply⏎regulatory standards” to AI-enabled solutions that are “being used to trigger, automatically,⏎medical interventions.” Focusing FDA on AI-enabled functions that are fully agentic and⏎autonomous also aligns with the Agency’s historical regulation of medical devices that operate⏎independent of human intervention.⏎Dr. Makary also emphasized the need for FDA to avoid paternalism and “trust clinicians⏎to do what’s right for their patients.” Underscoring the importance of human-in-the-loop design,⏎the Commissioner added that FDA should trust doctors to use what information they find useful⏎in making their medical decision, for which they bear ultimate responsibility.⏎Simplifying the regulatory framework is only part of the challenge. Clinicians and other⏎medical professionals will be the principal actors in driving adoption of AI tools at the point of⏎care, but it has been our experience that they will not use solutions they do not trust. Such trust⏎can only be earned with appropriate transparency and disclosure concerning the AI-enabled⏎solution.⏎The need for trust was starkly illustrated by a survey conducted in late 2024 by Wolters⏎Kluwer, which found that 40% of the U.S. physicians polled were ready to use generative AI⏎(GenAI) when interacting with patients at the point-of-care. The findings reflected a rapid⏎acceptance of the new technology more broadly, with 68% saying they had recently changed⏎their views and were more likely to think that GenAI would be beneficial to healthcare.⏎However, the survey also revealed that physicians will not blindly follow GenAI⏎recommendations and that they would be selective when evaluating and implementing GenAI⏎tools. For example, before relying on GenAI tools for assistance in clinical decision-making,⏎91% of respondents indicated they would need to know that GenAI-sourced materials were⏎created by doctors and medical experts. Similarly, 89% reported they would require vendors to⏎be transparent about where information came from, who created it, and how it was sourced. If⏎these survey results are indicative of the wider provider community, and we believe they are,⏎⏎clinicians will not use GenAI-enabled tools at the point of care unless they understand how such⏎solutions were developed and trust their output.⏎At the time of this writing, HHS is sending conflicting signals about the types of⏎disclosures it will require AI developers to make, with different requirements being issued or⏎proposed by FDA and the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy. For example, the draft⏎HTI-5 rule issued in December proposes to scale back and align disclosures for evidence-based⏎and predictive decision support interventions, with many of the latter considered regulated⏎devices. Other FDA guidance spells out labeling recommendations for AI-enabled device⏎software functions, but the Agency has yet to specify similar recommendations for devices using⏎GenAI. The recently revised Final Guidance on Clinical Decision Support Software provides⏎labeling recommendations for certain CDS solutions that will be given enforcement discretion,⏎some of which may be enabled by AI.⏎Given this, we recommend HHS provide clarity as to the types of information that⏎developers of AI solutions used at the point of care should disclose to medical professionals and⏎end users. At a minimum, clinicians should have access to information that explains the⏎provenance of any care recommendations, how such recommendations were developed, and by⏎whom, and any substantial risks to patients associated with relying on the recommendation.⏎Our responses to specific RFI questions follow:⏎1) What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your⏎ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎We previously commented to HHS and FDA on the Agency’s September 2022 Final⏎Guidance entitled Clinical Decision Support Software (FDA-2017-D-6569), which provided⏎FDA’s interpretation of statutory language included in the 21st Century Cures Act of 2016 that⏎exempted low-risk CDS software from FDA’s regulatory scope.⏎We commend FDA for recently issuing a revised Final Guidance granting enforcement⏎discretion to decision support software that issues a single clinically appropriate care⏎recommendation. We also strongly support the Agency’s view that the level of software⏎automation is a critical factor that should be considered in determining whether the health care⏎professional has had sufficient opportunity to independently review the basis for the care⏎recommendation.⏎However, we continue to be concerned that FDA will consider the time-critical nature of⏎medical decision as a factor to determine whether a software solution should be regulated. As we⏎have pointed out in previous comments, the 21st Century Cures Act does not authorize FDA to⏎consider time-sensitivity in medical decision-making as a criterion in defining which CDS⏎software would remain under the Agency’s regulatory purview. Rather, Congress clearly⏎indicated that the developer’s design or “intended use” of the software should control.⏎⏎Moreover, in practice, identifying time-critical decisions is not as clear as it might seem⏎due to numerous factors, including the patient, their presentation, and the setting in which⏎treatment is being delivered. For example, the management of a patient with gastrointestinal⏎bleeding may be emergent if the patient is severely bleeding and is in shock, whereas it may be⏎less urgent if the patient had a single episode of bleeding an hour earlier and is hemodynamically⏎stable. Thus, given the context-specific nature of time-critical decisions, we believe its use as a⏎factor to determine which CDS software should be regulated is impracticable, unfeasible, and⏎beyond the scope of FDA’s statutory authority.⏎While FDA’s recent clarification on CDS that provides a single recommendation is⏎welcome, we fear FDA’s continued use of time-criticality will continue to stifle new products⏎and deprive providers and patients of enhanced CDS solutions that can improve health outcomes⏎and lower costs.⏎We therefore recommend FDA adopt a simpler regulatory approach to CDS software that⏎focuses solely on the software’s level of automation, with CDS that is designed to trigger care⏎interventions without a human intermediary being subject to FDA oversight. We note this focus⏎on automation aligns with our earlier recommendation that FDA’s regulatory oversight of all AI-⏎enabled devices should focus on software that is “agentic,” capable of acting without human⏎intervention.⏎2) For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎While we appreciate HHS’ implied distinction in the question between medical devices⏎and non-medical devices, our response addresses two important legal issues that apply to both.⏎Though not within HHS’ regulatory purview, we feel compelled to raise the issue of⏎copyright protection and the continued enforcement of federal copyright laws. Copyright and⏎AI are commonly discussed in the context of the creative arts, but it also applies to clinical⏎content such as care guidelines, published medical research or patient information leaflets. Such⏎material can be used by AI developers to train and/or test AI-enabled software solutions used in⏎clinical care to gauge alignment with evidence-based care. Per existing law, before developers⏎can use copyrighted content for creating their AI solutions, permission must first be obtained⏎from the content creator.⏎Enforcement of existing copyright protections for clinical content should be maintained.⏎Such protections enable clinical content creators to maintain a single, authoritative version,⏎preventing unauthorized edits, distortions or selective quoting. Even small alterations to medical⏎content can introduce clinical risk, misinterpretation or error, putting patients at risk.⏎Empowering the authors of clinical guidelines to control updated versions ensures the continued⏎scientific and medical integrity of the content.⏎⏎Producing scientifically grounded medical research, practice guidelines or patient⏎information leaflets also requires significant upfront investment in time, resources and costs.⏎Copyright protection not only enables creators to recoup their upfront costs, it encourages future⏎investment in new research, and in follow-on content that applies such research and findings to⏎day-to-day clinical care.⏎The second legal issue is the call by some that clinicians be given a shield from legal⏎liability if a patient was harmed when AI was used in their care. We strongly disagree.⏎Medicare professionals must continue to be responsible for the care they provide. Shielding them⏎from such responsibility could put patient safety at risk, sow distrust among patients and⏎undermine the professional’s duty of care. HHS should therefore continue to support existing⏎state tort laws that promote the safe practice of medicine, and clinicians, hospital and health⏎systems using AI-enabled software to assist in clinical decision-making should continue to be⏎subject to state medical malpractice laws.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to comment. If HHS staff have questions or would like to⏎discuss our responses in more detail, please contact Bob Hussey at bob@bobhussey.com who⏎can arrange a discussion with the appropriate Wolters Kluwer staff.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0261♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Edward♔Williams♔♔West Virginia♔♔United States♔Milken Institute School of Public Health at GWU♔♔♔♔♔♔My main question reading through this request is what any of it has to do with the fact that rural hospitals keep closing at a rate nobody in Washington seems particularly alarmed about. I've been at GWU going on three years now doing post-doc work focused on rural health access and I keep expecting someone to connect these dots in a meaningful way.⏎⏎Garrett Regional Medical Center in West Virginia nearly closed in 2020. These are not abstract statistics to me. I grew up 40 minutes from a hospital that everybody understood was the only option for a hundred miles in certain directions. When we talk about accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings, I genuinely want to know which clinical settings we're picturing. Because the facilities where AI would presumably make the biggest difference in terms of diagnostic capacity and specialist shortages are often the same facilities that can't make payroll or that lost their only OB unit in 2019.⏎⏎The CHIME survey data I've seen circulating puts real numbers on something I've watched play out anecdotally for years - about 41% of health system IT leaders say the published AI studies don't match the patient populations they actually serve. That's a reproducibility problem and it's also a rural problem because the populations most underrepresented in training data and validation studies tend to be the same ones served by struggling small hospitals in places like Mingo County or McDowell County.⏎⏎I don't have a tidy policy fix to offer here. I'm not sure HHS has a tidy policy fix either. What I do think is that any investment priorities coming out of this RFI should require that vendors demonstrate performance on datasets that actually include rural, low-resource, and Medicaid-heavy populations before anything gets recommended for broader adoption. Otherwise we're building better tools for systems that already have resources and calling it progress.⏎⏎The reimbursement question is also one I'd want HHS to take seriously but I genuinely don't know enough about the billing side to comment responsibly on the specifics. That's probably a separate comment from someone who lives in that world.⏎⏎What I keep coming back to is that a hospital running on a skeleton staff with outdated infrastructure is not going to be positioned to implement AI tools no matter how many federal RFIs get published about it. The structural problem doesn't go away because the technology got better. And I worry this process becomes another round of recommendations that assume a level of institutional capacity that just isn't present in the communities that need the most help.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0262♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Taylor♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔National Hispanic Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My biggest question reading through all of this is whether patients like the ones I work with are going to end up paying more out of pocket when their doctors start using these AI tools. I keep coming back to that and I'm not sure I'm getting a clear answer from the documents HHS put out.⏎⏎I work with the National Hispanic Medical Association, specifically with training programs and affiliated clinics that serve a lot of Medicaid patients and folks who are uninsured. When I talk to clinicians at these sites, which I did most recently at a meeting in Albuquerque in March, they're already getting pressure to use algorithmic screening tools for things like depression and anxiety. That part is happening whether this rulemaking moves forward or not. What I can't figure out is who pays when a clinic upgrades its software to run these tools. Does that cost get passed along somehow? And do patients have any say?⏎⏎The consent piece worries me a lot. In our 2023 member survey we found 68% of respondents said they didn't think their patients were getting adequate information about when AI was involved in screening or treatment decisions. That's not a small number. And I genuinely don't know if there's anything in the existing rules that requires a clinician to tell a patient "by the way, a computer flagged you for this." I'd like to know if HHS is planning to address that directly or if it's being left to individual providers.⏎⏎The other thing I want to raise, and I'm not sure exactly where in the RFI this fits, is that a lot of the training data for mental health AI tools just doesn't include enough people who look like the patients at our affiliated clinics. There's published research on this. When the model hasn't seen enough examples from Latino communities, or from people with limited english proficiency, there's real reason to worry that it's going to produce different quality recommendations for those patients. I don't think accelerating adoption fixes that problem, I think it might make it worse if nobody is required to audit these tools before deploying them in safety net settings.⏎⏎I realize I'm probably not using the exact regulatory language HHS is looking for here and I apologize for that. I'm more on the research side than the policy side. But the practical question I keep coming back to for the patients I care about is pretty simple: if my doctor is using a computer to help decide what's wrong with me, do I get to know that, and does anyone check whether that computer works as well for me as it does for someone else. I don't see a clear answer to either of those questions in what I've read so far.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0263♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Small, Vikki, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Vikki♔Small♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔PUBLIC COMMENT⏎RE: RIN 0955–AA13, Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (FR Doc. 2025–23641)⏎Submitted via: Regulations.gov⏎I am a senior leader responsible for digital health and inclusion-focused innovation at a⏎safety-net academic medical center in Brooklyn. I am developing an institutional⏎framework for responsible AI governance and adoption, informed by direct⏎implementation experience deploying patient-facing AI tools in a safety-net setting. I⏎submit these comments in my individual professional capacity. I am responding to RFI⏎Question 2 (policy priorities), Question 9 (patient and caregiver needs), and Question⏎10 (research priorities).⏎This comment focuses on patient- and caregiver-facing clinical AI: portal chatbots, voice⏎agents, automated intake workflows, and AI-enabled outreach used directly by patients⏎and caregivers. These tools can improve access and efficiency, but those benefits will⏎not reach everyone unless tools are built for real-world conditions—limited English⏎proficiency, low health literacy, disability access needs, and uneven digital access. In⏎safety-net settings, the most helpful federal actions are practical requirements that⏎protect patients without imposing unrealistic burden.⏎Existing civil rights and accessibility obligations apply to AI-enabled care⏎HHS should make clear that AI-enabled patient communication does not reduce or⏎replace existing legal responsibilities. Title VI and Section 1557, the ADA and Section⏎508, and the Plain Writing Act apply to AI-enabled communication pathways just as they⏎apply to in-person, written, and telephonic communication. HHS can accelerate⏎adoption by translating these obligations into baseline requirements that are clear for⏎vendors and feasible for under-resourced providers.⏎Four baseline requirements for patient-facing AI (RFI Q2 and Q9)⏎1. Transparency⏎Patients should be told at the start of an interaction that AI is involved, what it can and⏎cannot do, and how to reach a person at any time. Recommended baseline: plain-⏎language disclosure at the start of the interaction, plus a persistent “get help from a⏎person” option.⏎2. Language access⏎In safety-net settings, language access is a basic safety requirement. If disclosures and⏎instructions are only available in English, patients with limited English proficiency are⏎shut out of safe use. Recommended baseline: support English and the top 2–3⏎languages by patient volume at the site, with an easy language switch and a clear⏎pathway to human help in all supported languages.⏎3. Plain language⏎⏎Transparency only works if patients understand what they are being told. Patient-facing⏎AI should be written at or below a 6th-grade reading level, consistent with the Plain⏎Writing Act, and should prioritize short, single-task instructions. Recommended⏎baseline: plain-language scripts, one action per step, and brief comprehension supports⏎for critical actions (for example, “Would you like me to repeat that?”).⏎4. Human escalation⏎AI tools should not become a gatekeeper to care. If a patient requests help or an issue⏎remains unresolved after one clarification attempt, the tool should immediately route the⏎patient to appropriate staff—without additional automated menus. Recommended⏎baseline: immediate escalation on request, with appropriate routing (nurse, pharmacist,⏎scheduling), and clear emergency guidance when clinically appropriate.⏎A brief example: a portal chatbot provides accurate information but only in English, with⏎high reading complexity and no confirmation of understanding. A caregiver⏎misunderstands next steps, leading to delayed care. This is preventable through⏎multilingual support, plain language, comprehension prompts, and reliable escalation.⏎Pre-deployment readiness and ongoing monitoring (RFI Q2)⏎Before go-live, organizations should define readiness criteria in advance, stress-test⏎predictable failure modes through structured scenario-based simulation (covering⏎language, literacy, and device barriers), and verify performance against explicit Go/No-⏎Go thresholds. Informal usability checks are not sufficient.⏎Once in use, providers and vendors should monitor completion, fallback, and escalation⏎rates—disaggregated by language group—as early warning indicators of uneven⏎performance. HHS should also encourage organizations to integrate AI-related risks into⏎existing patient safety monitoring systems, classifying AI workflow incidents as high-⏎impact when they could affect clinical decisions, documentation accuracy, or care⏎access.⏎Recommended baseline: if fallback or escalation rates exceed 20% for any language⏎group for two consecutive months, require corrective action plans and pause high-risk⏎pathways until issues are resolved.⏎Research and development priorities (RFI Q10)⏎Federal grants and contracts should prioritize tools developed with and validated in⏎diverse populations—Medicaid beneficiaries, people with disabilities, limited English⏎proficiency populations, and safety-net settings. Too often, tools are built and validated⏎in well-resourced systems and then deployed elsewhere with weaker performance and⏎new access barriers.⏎HHS should also support scenario-based simulated caregiver persona testing as an⏎accepted preliminary validation step in proposals for patient-facing AI tools. This pre-⏎deployment method identifies language, literacy, accessibility, and device-specific⏎failures before patients are exposed—reducing compliance risk, lowering remediation⏎costs, and preventing avoidable care delays. Alongside this, HHS should fund⏎⏎implementation research on adoption in resource-constrained settings and support⏎shared infrastructure that under-resourced providers can use without prohibitive⏎licensing costs.⏎Closing⏎I urge HHS to establish four baseline requirements— transparency, language access,⏎plain language, and reliable escalation—via guidance regulation, or contract language.⏎Pairing these baselines with practical pre-deployment readiness criteria, simple ongoing⏎monitoring, and targeted research investment will protect patients, reduce avoidable⏎burden, and improve access and outcomes across diverse communities.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Vikki S. Small, MPH, LSSGB⏎Submitted in individual professional capacity♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
