iveperception.NeuralNetw.2019;115:30–49⏎2017;139(2):e20163911 81.SolomonidesAE,KoskiE,AtabakiSM,etal.DefiningAMIA’sartificial⏎74.LiD,LiaoC.Incidentaldiscoveryofnonpaternityduringprenatal intelligenceprinciples.JAmMedInformAssoc.2022;29(4):585–591⏎testingofgeneticdisease.FetalDiagnTher.2008;24(1):39–41⏎82.Kemper AR, Uren RL, Clark SJ. Adoption of electronic health re-⏎75.Federal Trade Commission. Children’s online privacy protection cords in primary care pediatric practices. Pediatrics. 2006;118(1):⏎rule. Available at: https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2013/ e20–e24⏎12 FROMTHEAMERICANACADEMYOFPEDIATRICS⏎Downloaded from http://publications.aap.org/pediatrics/article-pdf/154/4/e2024068509/1717386/peds.2024-068509.pdf⏎by guest⏎on 23 February 2026♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0265♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Hardin, Bianka , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Bianka♔Hardin♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔A comprehensive federal regulatory framework for AI in healthcare is urgently needed. Unlike medical devices, medications, or licensed clinicians—each governed by strict oversight—AI tools currently face no uniform national standards. Leaving regulation to private companies or a patchwork of state laws risks inconsistent protections and exposes patients to preventable harm. I believe federal policy must prioritize privacy, safety, and accountability, especially given that many mental‑health AI tools fall outside HIPAA and may leave sensitive patient data unprotected.⏎Equally concerning is the lack of rigorous, independent evidence demonstrating that AI systems are safe or effective for mental‑health care. Large language models can generate inaccurate or harmful outputs, and corporate metrics such as user engagement are no substitute for meaningful clinical outcomes like symptom remission, sustained recovery, and quality‑of‑life improvements. Decades of research show that the therapeutic relationship is the strongest predictor of treatment success—something AI cannot replicate. Until AI tools are evaluated against recovery‑oriented outcomes and vetted through independent research, I do not believe they should be positioned as replacements for trained clinicians.⏎I urge the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services to support independent research, establish national privacy and safety standards, collaborate with clinicians on appropriate clinical metrics, and await FDA determinations about whether these tools qualify as medical devices. Without robust federal leadership, rapid AI adoption could outpace scientific validation and compromise patient protection. Thoughtful, evidence‑based regulation is essential to ensure that AI enhances—rather than undermines—ethical and effective healthcare.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0266♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Deborah♔Taylor♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Forty-one years of nursing and I keep coming back to the same question when I read something like this: what do we think medicine is actually for?⏎⏎I spent almost twenty years caring for a woman at our clinic in Kokomo. Same woman, same room, same charts going back to 2004 or so. I knew her. I knew that when she described her pain as "pressure" she really meant something closer to burning, because that's how she talked about it, and I knew that because I had asked her the same questions enough times to understand the gap between her words and what she meant. No intake form captures that. No model trained on population data captures that. And this woman — older, Black, someone whose symptoms had been waved off or misread for years before I ever met her — needed that kind of knowing. She was misdiagnosed twice in her sixties. It was a younger nurse who finally caught what had been hiding in plain sight, and she caught it because she had taken the time to actually know her patient's history, not just review it.⏎⏎I don't know how to say this so it sounds like regulatory language, but I'll try. When HHS asks what actions it can take to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, I think the Department has to also ask what it is willing to risk losing. Because there is something that happens between a patient and a nurse or a doctor that has real clinical value, not just emotional value, real clinical value, and when you start routing judgment through a machine recommendation, even a good one, you start to change what that relationship is. The patient becomes a set of inputs. The clinician becomes someone who approves or overrides an output. That is a different thing than what I have spent forty-one years doing.⏎⏎I'm not saying none of this technology has any use. I can see where it might help with something like flagging a missed medication interaction or sorting through imaging. I'm not opposed to tools. But I am worried about what happens when the tool becomes the default and the human judgment becomes the thing that needs to be justified. Especially for patients who are scared or in pain or who can't quite articulate what's wrong. Those patients need someone who can notice the way they're holding their breath or hear the catch in their voice. That is not a romantic idea about nursing. That is a clinical skill and it matters.⏎⏎What concerns me most about this request for information is that it frames the question as how to accelerate adoption, and I think before HHS moves on that, someone needs to be asking who these systems were trained on and whether the people who built them ever cared for a patient like Rosemary. Because the history of medicine getting it wrong for older Black women is long and it predates any algorithm, and I have not seen anything in what I've read about these tools that convinces me we have solved that problem just because we've made it faster.⏎⏎I also wonder practically, when something goes wrong and the AI flagged one thing and the nurse saw another thing and the doctor went with the AI, who is responsible for that. I don't think that question has been answered clearly anywhere I've looked.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0267♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am a Clinical Psychologist and Associate Professor at a research university. AI medical tools are NOT READY for accelerated adoption in clinical care without meaningful regulation and a robust evidence base. Federal leadership is necessary to reduce risk of harm, protect patient privacy, ensure clinically effective treatment, and establish consistent national standards. By grounding policy in science and clinical expertise, HHS can help ensure that innovation in AI supports, rather than undermines, ethical and effective healthcare.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0268♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Medtronic, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Medtronic♔♔♔♔♔♔Medtronic⏎950 F Street NW⏎Washington, DC 20004⏎USA⏎Tel: 202.393.0444⏎www.medtronic.com⏎February 23, 2026⏎ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎To Whom It May Concern,⏎Medtronic is the world's leading medical technology company, specializing in implantable⏎and interventional therapies that alleviate pain, restore health, and extend life. We are⏎committed to the continual research and development necessary to produce high-quality⏎products and innovative therapies that improve health outcomes for all patients. Medtronic’s⏎devices and therapies address over 70 chronic conditions and diseases, across four⏎portfolios, including Cardiovascular, Medical Surgical, Neuroscience, and Diabetes.⏎As a medical technology developer, we applaud ASTP/ONC for issuing an RFI on actions the⏎Department can take to establish a forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure⏎approach to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care. HHS has a pivotal⏎role to play in creating a cohesive regulatory, payment, and evaluation environment that⏎encourages AI adoption while protecting patient safety and data privacy.⏎1⏎⏎In particular, we appreciate this opportunity to provide input regarding how reimbursement⏎approaches can be most effectively applied to support the rapid adoption and use of AI in⏎clinical care, to reduce uncertainty that impedes AI innovation, and to align federal incentives⏎so that AI is deployed in ways that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower health care⏎costs, and improve health outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities. We also⏎appreciate that the RFI raises additional questions, such as those related to regulatory,⏎interoperability, and cybersecurity considerations. We have worked extensively with our⏎trade associations, AdvaMed and MDMA, to provide additional feedback on these extremely⏎important topics.⏎Specifically, we offer comments on the following questions raised in the RFI:⏎• Barriers to Private Sector Innovation in AI and Its Adoption and Use (Question 1)⏎• Payment Policy Changes to Incentivize the Effective Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎(Question 2)⏎• Performance and Cost Expectations and Experiences in the Deployment of AI Tools⏎(Question 6)⏎Our responses to each of these questions as they relate to reimbursement are outlined in⏎detail below.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The most significant barrier to AI innovation, adoption, and use in clinical care that we seek to⏎highlight in this response is the lack of a clear, predictable reimbursement pathway under⏎Medicare to account for the costs of these technologies. AI tools used in clinical care have⏎the potential to reduce overall health care costs by improving clinical efficiency, supporting⏎more timely and accurate clinical decision-making and enabling high quality care in the face⏎of healthcare workforce shortages. Additionally, many AI tools used in clinical care improve⏎patient outcomes through more accurate and earlier disease detection, and better treatment⏎recommendations. At the same time, adoption of these technologies imposes real and often⏎significant upfront and ongoing costs on hospitals and physician practices that are not⏎adequately recognized under current reimbursement frameworks.⏎2⏎⏎As HHS and CMS consider barriers to adoption of AI tools for clinical care, it cannot be⏎understated the importance that adequate reimbursement will play to support future⏎adoption. CMS has long recognized that improvements in efficiency mean less work for⏎physicians and have accordingly adjusted Physician Fee Schedule payment to account for⏎that work reduction. These increases in efficiency are due in part to improvements in⏎technology including use of AI tools for clinical care; however, there is no corresponding⏎payment to support adoption of these technologies by hospitals and physician practices.⏎Under prevailing fee-for-service payment models, many of these savings accrue outside the⏎provider setting that bears the cost of adopting and maintaining the AI technology.⏎For providers, implementation of AI tools used in clinical care requires meaningful⏎investment, including but not limited to:⏎• Acquisition or subscription costs for AI software and/or enabled hardware;⏎• Integration with existing clinical IT systems, including EHRs, PACS, and device⏎infrastructure;⏎• Cybersecurity, data governance, and compliance resources;⏎• Training of clinicians and staff and ongoing workflow optimization; and⏎• Maintenance, updates, validation, and performance monitoring over time.⏎These costs are incremental to existing operating expenses and are often incurred without a⏎corresponding increase in reimbursable services. In some cases, AI may even reduce billable⏎volume by preventing unnecessary interventions, creating a financial disincentive for⏎adoption despite clear clinical and operational value. As a result, the current reimbursement⏎environment may unintentionally slow adoption of high-value AI technologies, particularly for⏎smaller hospitals, rural providers, and independent physician practices with limited capital⏎and IT resources. Without targeted reimbursement pathways or value-based incentives,⏎providers may be unable to justify investment in AI even when such tools demonstrably⏎improve efficiency, safety, and patient outcomes.⏎Aligning reimbursement policy with the economic realities of AI implementation is essential⏎to ensure that providers are not penalized for adopting technologies that improve care⏎delivery and system-wide efficiency. Thoughtful reimbursement strategies can accelerate⏎responsible AI adoption while promoting sustainability for hospitals and physician practices.⏎3⏎⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What⏎HHS regulations, policies, or programs should be revisited to augment your⏎ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎As CMS further considers the establishment of a reimbursement framework for software as a⏎service (SaaS), we believe that future policies should strike an appropriate balance, by easing⏎financial barriers to hospital and physician adoption without providing incentives for over-⏎utilization. In the context of Medicare’s Outpatient Prospective Payment System (OPPS) for⏎hospitals, CMS has relied upon temporary New Technology Ambulatory Payment⏎Classifications (APCs) and transitional pass-through (TPT) payments for devices to provide⏎this balance. However, there is currently no pathway for physicians to receive reimbursement⏎for utilizing SaaS in their practices. We urge HHS to direct CMS to adapt these mechanisms to⏎enable adoption of AI in clinical care in hospital outpatient, ambulatory surgical center (ASC)⏎settings, and physician practices under fee-for-service Medicare, while in parallel pursuing⏎value-based payment mechanisms for long-term sustainability, as outlined in detail below.⏎New Technology APCs⏎The use of procedural C-codes with payment established through New Technology APCs,⏎enables patient access during the initial period of clinical adoption, while simultaneously⏎facilitating the collection of hospital cost data to inform permanent APC assignments in the⏎future. CMS could effectively utilize this approach for AI to increase hospitals’ ability to invest⏎in innovative technologies to advance the speed, accuracy, and quality of diagnostic and⏎therapeutic decision making in health care, ultimately enabling improved standards of care.⏎More specifically, we support the payment pathway outlined in The Health Tech Investment⏎Act (S. 1399) for algorithm-based healthcare services (ABHS) using New Technology APCs,⏎which we believe CMS could adopt using existing authorities. The payment pathway outlined⏎in this legislation provides a dedicated framework for Medicare to reimburse for ABHS,⏎defined as services provided with an FDA approved or cleared medical device that utilizes AI,⏎machine learning (ML), or other similar technology to produce clinical outputs or generate⏎clinical conclusions with physician or other qualified health care professional involvement in⏎the screening, detection, planning, diagnosis, or treatment of an individual’s condition or⏎disease. Under this pathway, CMS could adapt the New Technology APC application process⏎4⏎⏎and criteria to ensure that all ABHS, including those that are performed in conjunction with an⏎underlying service, are eligible for payment under the OPPS. In addition, ABHS could be⏎assigned to a New Technology APC for no less than five years until there is sufficient data on⏎costs to assign the service to a clinical APC or create a new APC.⏎The New Technology APC pathway is also a suitable approach with respect to coding for⏎ABHS, because it allows CMS to create procedural C-codes as needed and outside the⏎American Medical Association (AMA) CPT Editorial Panel process. CPT codes are established⏎by the AMA primarily to describe physician services, and therefore may not be sufficient for⏎hospital and ASC billing purposes in all cases. For procedures involving sophisticated clinical⏎software, including AI, acquired on a subscription or per-use basis, CPT codes may not reflect⏎facilities’ costs to perform the procedure, and therefore fail to comprehensively describe the⏎service from the standpoint of the facility. In such cases, the creation of procedural C-codes⏎by CMS may be necessary to facilitate appropriate billing and payment for ABHS by hospitals⏎and ASCs.⏎In the absence of finalized legislation, we believe CMS should propose a unified and⏎predictable reimbursement framework leveraging the New Technology APC pathway for⏎software technologies based on CMS-defined categories and existing statutory authority, to⏎help address the current uncertainty around valuation and to encourage consistent adoption⏎of these technologies in hospital outpatient and ASC settings of care.⏎TPT Payment for Devices⏎Under the OPPS, temporary TPT payments are used to enable hospital adoption of⏎implantable medical devices that are new, costly, and that have either evidence⏎demonstrating substantial clinical improvement compared to available treatments, or⏎Breakthrough Device status as designated by the FDA. The current regulation at 42 CFR⏎419.66(b)(3) requires devices to come into contact with human tissue, and therefore excludes⏎AI technologies. We urge CMS to consider adapting the TPT requirements, or consider⏎developing a TPT-like framework, to accommodate high-value AI innovations that would⏎otherwise not be accounted for under the cost-based prospective payment system⏎methodology, in order to reduce hospital barriers to adoption.⏎5⏎⏎Physician Fee Schedule⏎At present, there are no established payment pathways for physicians to be reimbursed for⏎the acquisition or use of SaaS solutions in the office setting. As medical technology advances,⏎particularly in the area of implantable devices, physicians lack a predictable mechanism (and⏎in some cases, any mechanism) to recover investments associated with these innovations. For⏎example, emerging implantable devices are being designed with the capability for future⏎FDA approved software “unlocks,” enabling enhanced therapeutic functionality over time.⏎These new, enhanced software-based capabilities will allow patients and clinicians to benefit⏎from technological advancements without requiring full device replacement, thereby⏎eliminating clinical risks of reoperation, and improving outcomes while preserving the value⏎of the original implant.⏎Value-Based Payment⏎While improvements to Medicare fee-for-service reimbursement methodologies are vital in⏎the short-term to ensure AI innovation in clinical care does not stagnate, we encourage HHS⏎to maintain an eye toward a future in which payers’ and providers’ incentives are aligned to⏎provide the best possible care to patients at the lowest possible cost. In doing so, CMS⏎should explore how stronger incentives for high-value AI clinical interventions can be created⏎through existing quality measurement programs and through CMMI payment models. For⏎example, outcomes associated with the use of AI in clinical care could be studied and⏎rewarded through a framework similar to that of the upcoming ACCESS model.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give⏎new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎As indicated above, AI tools used in clinical care can improve patient outcomes through⏎more accurate and earlier disease detection, and better treatment recommendations. At the⏎same time, adoption of these technologies imposes real and often significant upfront and⏎ongoing costs on hospitals and physician practices that are not adequately recognized under⏎current reimbursement frameworks. We offer two examples of AI technologies – AI-assisted⏎6⏎⏎colonoscopy and fractional flow reserve angiography (FFRangio) – that exceed performance⏎expectations yet face challenges in terms of their deployment in clinical care to illustrate the⏎barriers that inadequate and unclear reimbursement pathways can present.⏎AI-Assisted Colonoscopy⏎Medtronic’s GI Genius™ CADe Intelligent Endoscopy Unit enhances patient care through the⏎use of AI to identify suspicious lesions in real time for evaluation during a colonoscopy, and is⏎clinically proven to increase the adenoma detection rate (ADR). In a randomized controlled⏎trial, the GI Genius™ system resulted in an average absolute ADR improvement of 14.4%⏎compared with standard colonoscopy,1 increasing the odds that patients with lesions will be⏎detected and treated earlier. Importantly, each 1% improvement in ADR has been shown to⏎lead to a 3% reduction in interval colorectal cancer over a 10-year follow-up period.2 GI⏎Genius™ has been further shown to reduce the Adenoma Miss Rate.3 Despite the⏎tremendous benefit the use of GI Genius™ can provide – both to individual patients and at a⏎population health level – there are currently no coding and payment mechanisms to account⏎for the costs of facilities and physicians adopting this technology.⏎FFRangio⏎Evidence shows that when fractional flow reserve (FFR) is used to help classify coronary⏎lesions there is a significant reduction in stenting, lower costs, and improved outcomes.4,5⏎Traditional FFR is a wire-based procedure, but FFRangio provides a less invasive method of⏎evaluating hemodynamic relevance of coronary artery stenosis by using AI analysis of routine⏎angiography images to create three-dimensional functional models of the coronary tree.⏎FFRangio is shown to have a comparably high degree of diagnostic accuracy to wire-based⏎1 Repici, Alessandro et al. “Efficacy of Real-Time Computer-Aided Detection of Colorectal Neoplasia in a Randomized⏎Trial.” Gastroenterology vol. 159,2 (2020): 512-520.e7. doi:10.1053/j.gastro.2020.04.062.⏎2 Corley, Douglas A et al. “Adenoma detection rate and risk of colorectal cancer and death.” The New England journal of⏎medicine vol. 370,14 (2014): 1298-306. doi:10.1056/NEJMoa1309086.⏎3 Wallace, Michael B et al. “Impact of Artificial Intelligence on Miss Rate of Colorectal Neoplasia.” Gastroenterology, S0016-⏎5085(22)00238-4. 11 Mar. 2022, doi:10.1053/j.gastro.2022.03.007.⏎4 Tonino PAL, De Bruyne B, Pijls NHJ, et al. Fractional Flow Reserve versus Angiography for Guiding Percutaneous Coronary⏎Intervention. N Engl J Med. 2009;360(3):213-224.⏎5 Fearon WF, Yeung AC, Lee DP, et al. Economic Evaluation of Fractional Flow Reserve–Guided Percutaneous Coronary⏎Intervention in Patients With Multivessel Disease. Circulation. 2010;122(24):2545-2550.⏎7⏎⏎FFR and is cost-saving,6,7 but adoption of the technology has been slow because of the⏎complexity and uncertainty of existing reimbursement pathways and lack of adequate⏎hospital payment. Perhaps most troubling, because wire-based FFR is more expensive,⏎hospitals often receive higher payments under the OPPS relative to FFRangio, which may⏎unintentionally incentivize hospitals to maintain the status quo of utilizing a more invasive⏎analog procedure over an AI-based technology which would achieve comparable results at a⏎lower cost.⏎***⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide input on accelerating the adoption of AI in clinical⏎care. Thoughtful and comprehensive approaches to ensure hospitals, ASCs, and physicians⏎are adequately reimbursed for use of these technologies will encourage adoption, reduce⏎uncertainty and speed innovation. Thank you for your consideration of our input on these⏎important questions and we look forward to further engagement with HHS. If you have⏎questions or need further information, please contact me at (202) 441-1938 or⏎carrie.w.bullock@medtronic.com.⏎Regards,⏎Carrie Bullock⏎Vice President, Health Policy, Reimbursement & CMS Strategy⏎Medtronic⏎6 Witberg G, et al. Diagnostic performance of angiogram derived fractional flow reserve. J Am Coll Cardiol Intv. 2020 Feb, 13 (4)⏎7 https://www.prnewswire.com/apac/news-releases/provision-study-first-ever-prospective-rct-of-cathworks-ffrangio-outcomes-⏎compared-to-invasive-wire-based-physiology-in-japan-meets-its-primary-endpoint-302288035.html.⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0269♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Children's National Hospital, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Children's National Hospital♔♔♔♔♔♔Children’s National Hospital⏎Comment on HHS Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Cover Letter⏎Children’s National Hospital⏎111 Michigan Avenue NW⏎Washington, DC 20010⏎Submitted via Regulations.gov⏎Docket: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Sir or Madam,⏎Children’s National Hospital appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department of⏎Health and Human Services’ Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use⏎of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.⏎As one of the nation’s leading pediatric academic medical centers and a pioneer in the⏎development of machine learning technology for children’s health, Children’s National⏎offers a distinct perspective on AI adoption. Caring for minors introduces heightened⏎considerations related to privacy, consent and assent, data representativeness, safety⏎thresholds, and family trust.⏎Our comments focus on regulatory clarity, pediatric AI infrastructure, liability and⏎accountability, and implementation science. Our team has expertise in guiding safe and⏎trusted AI development through its lifecycle, from ideation and design to clinical⏎implementation and adoption. We view this RFI as the beginning of an ongoing dialogue⏎and welcome continued engagement.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Children’s National Hospital⏎⏎Regulations.gov Comment⏎This submission reflects Children’s National Hospital’s response to the HHS Request for⏎Information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care. The response emphasizes a pediatric-focused lens, risk-proportionate governance,⏎national pediatric AI infrastructure, and shared accountability models to enable safe and⏎responsible AI adoption.⏎⏎Children’s National Hospital Comment on⏎HHS Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Executive Summary⏎Children’s National Hospital strongly supports HHS’s efforts to accelerate the responsible⏎adoption of artificial intelligence in clinical care. We submit these comments to emphasize⏎the importance of a pediatric-first policy lens. AI used in the care of minors raises distinct⏎considerations related to privacy, consent, data sufficiency, clinical risk tolerance, and family⏎trust that are not fully addressed by adult-centric frameworks.⏎We recommend that HHS prioritize:⏎1. Risk proportionate regulation of AI technology with mandatory transparency around⏎pediatric performance and training data.⏎2. National pediatric AI research collaboration and governance infrastructure, including⏎federated data and model evaluation resources.⏎3. Multi-stakeholder approaches to liability and accountability that enable trust and⏎adoption without negatively impacting innovation.⏎4. Implementation science, clinician and family education, and pilots aligned to⏎pediatric outcomes and burden reduction.⏎5. Establishment of a national pediatric AI council, structured as a public–private⏎partnership to provide coordinated guidance on standards, evaluation, deployment,⏎and oversight of AI systems affecting children.⏎I. Regulation⏎Pediatric-Specific Transparency and Algorithmic Accountability⏎For both medical and non-medical device AI used in clinical care, Children’s National⏎recommends that HHS establish clear expectations for algorithmic accountability, including⏎standardized disclosures by AI developers and vendors. These disclosures should include:⏎• The populations on which models were trained and validated, with explicit⏎identification of pediatric representation and appropriate stratification.⏎• Transparent performance limitations and failure modes, particularly where pediatric⏎data are sparse or absent.⏎• Ongoing monitoring and update strategies for models that learn or evolve over time.⏎⏎In pediatrics, the absence or under-representation of children in training data can materially⏎affect safety and effectiveness. Transparency regarding training populations would allow⏎health systems to make informed adoption decisions and protect patients.⏎Privacy, Consent, and Behavioral Health⏎Pediatric care introduces additional complexity related to guardianship, adolescent⏎confidentiality, and sensitive behavioral health data. HHS guidance clarifying how existing⏎privacy and consent frameworks apply when AI systems process or generate clinical content⏎would significantly reduce uncertainty and variability across organizations.⏎Scalable Governance Models⏎Only a small number of pediatric institutions currently have the resources to implement⏎comprehensive AI governance. HHS can accelerate safe adoption by supporting the⏎development of shared pediatric governance frameworks, policies, and monitoring tools⏎that can be guided by major clinical centers equipped for AI deployment and broadly⏎adopted across children’s hospitals.⏎II. Reimbursement⏎Children’s National recognizes that reimbursement policy for AI remains an evolving area. We⏎encourage HHS to explore pilot programs that align payment with measurable pediatric⏎value, including:⏎• Reductions in avoidable emergency department utilization and hospitalizations.⏎• Improvements in preventive care adherence.⏎• Clinician time savings from AI-enabled documentation and workflow tools, allowing⏎greater focus on patient and family engagement.⏎• Cost savings from AI-enabled early diagnosis and treatment.⏎Such pilots should be paired with rigorous evaluation to inform longer term policy decisions.⏎III. Research and Development⏎A National Pediatric AI Consortium⏎Children’s National strongly supports the creation of a national, HHS led Pediatric AI⏎Consortium. Pediatric cohorts are often small at individual institutions, limiting model⏎⏎development and evaluation. A federated approach—enabling shared learning while⏎preserving privacy—would:⏎• Expand access to high quality pediatric AI resources.⏎• Support benchmarking, robustness testing, and bias assessment.⏎• Enable participation by smaller and under-resourced pediatric centers.⏎Under HHS’s leadership, the consortium could create and manage national public resources⏎for pediatric AI including open-access datasets, AI benchmarking initiatives, and publicly⏎maintained code repositories--to support transparency, reproducibility, and continued⏎development.⏎Methodological Focus and Rare Diseases⏎One in three children born with a rare disease—a group of conditions that collectively affect⏎25–30 million Americans—will not survive to their fifth birthday. We recommend prioritizing⏎AI methodologies suitable for data-limited conditions, including rare diseases that impact⏎the survival and quality of life of patients of all ages. Investments in methods such as⏎federated learning, data-efficient modeling, and computationally efficient deployment will⏎have broad downstream impact.⏎Predictive and Preventive Care⏎Early detection of health risks, coupled with timely intervention, can save lives and improve⏎quality of life across the lifespan. We recommend prioritizing predictive AI methodologies⏎that integrate multimodal information—including electronic health records, clinical notes, lab⏎results, imaging studies, and patient history—to identify risks prior to symptom onset. Such⏎approaches can promote and support preventive care strategies that enable children at risk⏎to grow into healthy adults.⏎Implementation Science and Public Education⏎Patient and caregiver acceptance remains a significant barrier to AI adoption in pediatrics.⏎HHS support for implementation science, clinician training, and public education initiatives⏎will be essential to building trust and understanding of AI’s appropriate role in care.⏎⏎IV. Responses to Specific HHS Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private‑sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎From a pediatric health system perspective, the most significant barriers to AI adoption and⏎innovation include:⏎Limited pediatric data availability and fragmentation. Pediatric cohorts at individual⏎institutions are relatively small, particularly for rare diseases, neonatal populations, and⏎subspecialty conditions. While data sharing consortia exist, few are oriented toward the full⏎lifecycle needs of AI development, validation, and deployment.⏎Resource and capability constraints. Only a small number of pediatric institutions possess⏎the technical, operational, and governance capacity to responsibly develop or adopt AI. This⏎creates barriers in access and slows system‑wide learning.⏎Lack of pediatric‑specific performance transparency. Many AI tools entering the market⏎lack clear disclosure regarding the populations on which they were trained or validated. In⏎pediatrics, the absence or under‑representation of children in training data can materially⏎affect safety and effectiveness.⏎Legal and liability uncertainty. The absence of clear accountability frameworks, particularly⏎for non‑medical‑device AI, creates risk aversion among clinicians and health systems, directly⏎impeding adoption.⏎Trust and acceptance gaps. While clinicians are becoming more receptive to AI as they gain⏎experience, patient and caregiver trust remains uneven, underscoring the need for broad⏎education and transparency across all AI users and stakeholders.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by reducing pediatric data fragmentation, investing in shared⏎AI infrastructure, and lowering resource and governance barriers so that innovation is not⏎limited to a small number of well‑resourced pediatric institutions.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes⏎should HHS prioritize to incentivize effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Children’s National recommends that HHS prioritize the following:⏎⏎Risk‑proportionate governance for clinical AI. HHS should establish clear expectations for⏎AI systems used in clinical care that scale oversight to clinical risk, autonomy, and impact—⏎without treating all AI as regulated medical devices.⏎Mandatory transparency and disclosure expectations. AI vendors should be required to⏎disclose training populations, intended use, known limitations, and pediatric performance⏎metrics where applicable. This would improve informed decision‑making and patient safety.⏎Programmatic support for pediatric AI infrastructure. Federal investment in shared⏎pediatric AI resources—benchmarks, federated validation environments, and governance⏎templates—would accelerate adoption across diverse pediatric care settings.⏎Exploratory payment pilots. While pediatric reimbursement remains complex, HHS should⏎pilot outcome‑aligned reimbursement models that recognize AI’s contributions to care⏎quality, clinician efficiency, and caregiver burden reduction.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by establishing risk‑proportionate expectations for medical-⏎device and non‑medical‑device AI, requiring pediatric performance transparency, and⏎piloting payment models aligned to measurable pediatric value.⏎3. For non‑medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues⏎exist, and what role should HHS play?⏎Liability and accountability for non‑medical‑device AI represent one of the most significant⏎unresolved barriers to adoption.⏎Currently, accountability largely rests with the individual clinician using AI, even when the⏎underlying model is opaque, continuously learning, or externally developed. This dynamic⏎undermines efficiency gains and discourages responsible adoption.⏎Children’s National recommends that HHS:⏎• Convene a multi‑stakeholder working group including clinicians, health systems, AI⏎vendors, regulators, payers, and insurers.⏎• Examine shared‑responsibility models that align accountability with control and⏎transparency.⏎• Explore the role of insurance and contractual safe harbors for organizations that meet⏎defined governance and monitoring standards.⏎⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to facilitate consensus in this area and reduce fragmentation⏎across the health sector. HHS can accelerate adoption by convening multi‑stakeholder⏎efforts to develop shared accountability and liability frameworks that align responsibility with⏎transparency, control, and governance maturity.⏎4. For non‑medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation⏎methods, and should HHS further support these processes?⏎In pediatrics, effective evaluation must account for age‑related variation, developmental⏎differences, and smaller cohorts.⏎Promising approaches include:⏎• Pre‑deployment validation using pediatric‑specific datasets and age‑stratified⏎metrics.⏎• Prospective monitoring in “shadow mode” prior to clinical activation.⏎• Ongoing post‑deployment surveillance for model drift, bias, and alert burden.⏎• Human‑centered evaluation assessing workflow integration and cognitive load.⏎HHS support for standardized pediatric evaluation frameworks, open benchmarking tools,⏎and applied research funding would substantially improve consistency and safety. HHS can⏎accelerate adoption by supporting standardized pediatric evaluation frameworks,⏎age‑stratified performance benchmarks, and post‑deployment monitoring resources⏎applicable across diverse care settings.⏎5. How can HHS support private‑sector activities such as accreditation,⏎certification, and testing?⏎HHS can support voluntary, industry‑driven approaches by:⏎• Endorsing standardized AI “model cards” tailored to clinical and pediatric contexts.⏎• Supporting public registries that track AI tools, validated use cases, and reported⏎safety events.⏎• Encouraging harmonization of testing and certification efforts to reduce duplicative⏎burden.⏎⏎Such efforts should prioritize transparency and learning over prescriptive regulation. HHS⏎can accelerate adoption by endorsing voluntary, harmonized transparency and certification⏎mechanisms that prioritize learning, safety, and consistency without imposing unnecessary⏎regulatory burden.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded⏎expectations, and where have they fallen short?⏎Areas exceeding expectations include operational and administrative domains such as⏎clinical documentation, summarization, and workflow optimization. These applications⏎reduce burden without directly introducing patient safety risk. In clinical space, the earliest⏎and most successful adoption is in radiology and imaging sciences where decisions are⏎complex and clinical burnout is high.⏎Areas falling short include highly specialized, high‑stakes clinical decision‑making with⏎imminent impact on a patient’s health, where decision accountability is undefined.⏎Particularly in pediatrics, trust, data sufficiency, and validation remain challenges. The lack of⏎explainability in the process of decision of certain AI technologies negatively impacts⏎adoption of the trust of clinical users.⏎High‑potential future applications include care coordination copilots, medication⏎reconciliation for complex pediatric dosing, rare‑disease pattern detection, and AI‑assisted⏎imaging workflows that support, but do not replace, clinical judgment.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by focusing incentives and research on high‑value pediatric⏎use cases while supporting the data, validation, and trust foundations needed for safe⏎expansion into higher‑stakes clinical domains as well as by establishing AI-and-human-in-⏎the-loop clinical workflows with defined and structured accountability of the decision⏎processes.⏎⏎7. Which roles and governing bodies most influence AI adoption within⏎health care organizations?⏎In pediatric institutions, AI adoption is shaped by a combination of clinical leadership,⏎nursing leadership, informatics, technical leadership, research leadership, privacy and risk⏎management, and legal and compliance teams. Administrative hurdles include procurement⏎complexity, EHR integration, data accessibility, and workforce education.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by providing shared policy templates, governance standards,⏎and implementation guides tailored to pediatric settings.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities and⏎accelerate AI development?⏎Key pediatric data domains include growth trajectories, immunization records, neonatal⏎monitoring data, weight‑based medication dosing, and behavioral health information.⏎Enhanced interoperability through pediatric‑specific standards and federated access models⏎would improve both market competition and research productivity.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by advancing pediatric‑specific data standards and federated⏎interoperability models that enable scalable AI development while preserving privacy and⏎institutional autonomy.⏎9. What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed, and⏎what concerns do they have?⏎Patients and caregivers seek improved care navigation, reduced administrative burden, and⏎earlier detection of health issues. At the same time, concerns remain regarding privacy,⏎misuse of sensitive data, and over‑reliance on automated decision‑making.⏎Transparent communication, clear consent options, and education are essential to addressing⏎these concerns.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by investing in transparency, education, and consent‑aware AI⏎practices that build trust among patients, caregivers, and families, particularly in sensitive⏎pediatric contexts.⏎⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research HHS should prioritize?⏎Children’s National recommends prioritizing:⏎• Longitudinal studies of AI’s impact on pediatric outcomes and equity.⏎• Research on clinician‑AI teaming and workflow optimization.⏎• Economic evaluations that incorporate caregiver burden and social impact.⏎• Methods research addressing data scarcity, rare diseases, and bias mitigation.⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by prioritizing pediatric AI research that addresses data⏎scarcity, longitudinal outcomes, caregiver burden, and real‑world implementation⏎effectiveness.⏎V. Closing⏎For AI technologies to be adopted broadly and efficiently across national clinical settings,⏎healthcare institutions must address a set of clinical, cultural, computational, and regulatory⏎considerations that have not previously been encountered at scale. At Children’s National⏎Hospital, our team has contributed to the development of practical recommendations for AI⏎deployment, emphasizing the need to align clinical staffing, data workflows, and⏎computational infrastructure to enable safe implementation, monitoring, and continuous⏎evaluation of AI systems. In parallel, Children’s National Hospital plays leadership roles in the⏎professional societies most influential in both the development and adoption of clinical AI,⏎including the Medical Image Computing and Computer Assisted Intervention Society⏎(MICCAI), the Radiological Society of North America (RSNA), and the American Academy of⏎Pediatrics (AAP).⏎We stand ready to support HHS’ initiatives to advance responsible AI adoption and⏎recommend the establishment of a National Pediatric AI Council, structured as a public–⏎private partnership of scientists, clinicians, ethicists, patient and family representatives,⏎regulators, and industry stakeholders. Such a council would provide coordinated guidance on⏎standards, evaluation, deployment, and oversight of AI systems affecting children.⏎The pediatric healthcare community has reached a pivotal moment for improving their⏎patient well-being. AI has the potential to deliver significant gains in efficiency, access, and⏎quality across the national healthcare system if it is deployed safely and adopted responsibly.⏎Children’s National Hospital appreciates HHS’s leadership in this area and welcomes⏎⏎continued engagement on policies and programs that will enable AI to improve pediatric⏎health outcomes nationwide.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0270♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AdvaMed, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AdvaMed♔♔♔♔♔♔1301 Pennsylvania Avenue, NW⏎Suite 400⏎Washington, D.C. 20004–2654⏎P :: 202.783.8700⏎F :: 202.783.8750⏎W:: AdvaMed.org⏎February 23, 2025⏎Via Electronic Mail⏎Mr. Stephen Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordination for Health⏎Information Technology (ASTP/ONC)⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Posnack,⏎AdvaMed, the Advanced Medical Technology Association, appreciates the opportunity to submit⏎comments in response to your December 23, 2025, request for information1 (RFI) on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.⏎AdvaMed is the world's largest association representing manufacturers of medical devices,⏎diagnostic products, and medical technology. AdvaMed's member companies, which range from⏎the largest to the smallest medical product innovators and manufacturers, produce innovations⏎that transform health care through earlier disease detection, less invasive procedures, and more⏎effective treatments. AdvaMed advocates for a legal, regulatory and economic environment that⏎advances global health care by assuring worldwide patient access to the benefits of medical⏎technology. The Association promotes policies that foster the highest ethical standards, timely⏎product approvals, appropriate reimbursement, and access to international markets.⏎We recognize AI as a transformative tool with the potential to improve health outcomes, enhance⏎the efficiency of patient care, reduce costs, and advance healthcare. Right-sized and clarified⏎regulations can promote the development, deployment, and adoption of innovative and⏎trustworthy AI-enabled solutions. AdvaMed is uniquely well-positioned to provide feedback on⏎1 https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/23/2025-23641/request-for-information-accelerating-the-⏎adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence-as-part-of⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 1 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 2 of 17⏎frameworks and policy considerations for AI, as our members have been developing and⏎deploying AI-enabled medical devices supporting patient care for over 25 years.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to submit our high-level recommendations on regulatory reforms⏎to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as clinical care.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎A. Regulation of AI-Enabled Medical Devices⏎The medical device industry has more than three decades of experience developing AI-enabled⏎technologies that advance health care. As of December 2025, FDA has authorized over 1,300 AI-⏎enabled medical devices2, reflecting the sector’s longstanding expertise in innovating AI⏎applications in health care. This experience underscores a core principle: innovation thrives⏎when regulatory oversight is transparent, predictable, and capable of keeping pace with⏎technological advancements. These goals can be achieved by advancing regulatory science⏎through the issuance of FDA guidance and timely recognition of international consensus⏎standards, upholding the FDA’s existing risk-based regulatory paradigms while adapting when⏎needed to accommodate novel technologies, and ensuring FDA is properly resourced to⏎accomplish these objectives.⏎B. Coverage and Payment for AI-Enabled Medical Devices⏎Leaders of this Administration have been outspoken on their concern for the poor health⏎outcomes in the United States despite the abundant resources at our disposal. AdvaMed and the⏎broader medtech community share this deep concern. We also know medtech is a proven part of⏎the solution. Medtech works. But it only works if patients have access to it and clinicians are⏎empowered to use it. Access is complicated by many factors: inadequate or no health insurance⏎coverage; lack of provider coverage of medtech-enabled procedures; inadequate provider⏎reimbursement for medtech-enabled procedures; limited access to specialists and care due to⏎factors including geography and health care provider shortages; limited understanding of⏎available treatment options; and other out-of-pocket expenses associated with health care, such⏎as high deductibles and copayments.⏎As you know, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) plays a critical role in⏎patient access to health care and medtech. Medicare, as the nation’s largest health insurer, shapes⏎the health of its 67 million enrollees. It sets precedent in its coverage policies for Medicaid and⏎private insurers. Unfortunately, outdated Medicare laws and regulations often create unnecessary⏎barriers to patient access to many technological innovations, including artificial intelligence⏎software as a medical device and other items and services, along with digital and at-home care.⏎For the past eight years, CMS has issued requests for comments or requests for information⏎related to the adoption of technology and digital medicine, but has taken little action to provide⏎2 https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/artificial-intelligence-enabled-medical-⏎devices⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 2 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 3 of 17⏎clarity on coverage and/or payment for these transformative technologies and the services⏎performed with them.⏎Adoption of and subsequent beneficiary access to novel digital health technologies, including AI,⏎is conditioned on whether there are appropriate Medicare payment pathways that provide⏎stability and certainty for providers adopting these technologies. While CMS has explored⏎payment policies in specific cases to recognize the role of software as a service (SaaS), software⏎as a medical device (SaMD), and AI technologies in health care delivery, a lack of clear and⏎consistent coverage and payment policies for these categories of technologies significantly limits⏎access under the Medicare program. Our specific recommendations for revisions to CMS’⏎regulation of AI and digital health technologies are discussed below.⏎C. Data Privacy⏎Access to high-quality data is pivotal for AI model innovation, as it is crucial for development⏎and validation. Updating data regulatory frameworks, such as HIPAA, to facilitate ethical and⏎secure data-sharing practices will further enhance the development of sophisticated AI tools.⏎AI researchers require improved access to healthcare data that is both rich enough to support⏎meaningful scientific development and sufficiently de-identified to protect patient privacy from⏎wrongful disclosure or abuse. Policymakers can support advances in healthcare AI innovation by⏎allowing broader use of healthcare data for research, provided identifiers are removed and other⏎HIPAA rules are met (e.g., HIPAA’s security requirements and its limitations on “selling” or⏎marketing without prior written permission).⏎In parallel, a broad, prospective informed consent framework would enable individuals to⏎contribute their data to a centralized repository for future research, enabling reuse across studies⏎and institutions.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations.⏎A. Regulation of AI-Enabled Medical Devices⏎Effective clinical use of AI-enabled medical devices depends on the development and⏎availability of safe, effective technologies—a responsibility shared by industry and FDA.⏎Medical device manufacturers have a strong record of advancing health care with innovative,⏎reliable AI-enabled devices, and FDA has a long history of reviewing and authorizing such⏎devices. Once in clinical use, both parties continue to monitor device safety through their⏎respective regulatory requirements and responsibilities. FDA’s existing premarket and post-⏎market regulations ensure patients and clinicians have access to trustworthy devices. However,⏎by prioritizing certain programmatic improvements, the availability of AI-enabled devices in⏎clinical use can be accelerated without compromising safety or performance.⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 3 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 4 of 17⏎The De Novo3 premarket regulatory pathway outlined in 21 CRF Part 860, Subpart D, was⏎created with the goal of fostering the development of innovative medical devices of low to⏎moderate risk. Since its inception in 1997, FDA has authorized approximately 450 devices⏎through the De Novo paradigm. However, FDA’s performance reports4 indicate that less than⏎50% of De Novo submissions are granted. This low rate of success is in stark contrast to the⏎other premarket pathways (>80% success rate for 510(k) submissions annually). The De Novo⏎program would benefit from greater transparency and consistency in FDA’s evidentiary⏎expectations for De Novo submissions. FDA has the authority to implement adaptive, risk-based⏎paradigms, such as increasing the utilization of real-world evidence and conditional authorization⏎with post-market obligations, that if implemented consistently, could meaningfully increase the⏎success rate of the De Novo program and increase patient access and clinical use of AI-enabled⏎devices without compromising patient safety.⏎In 2022, Congress passed predetermined change control plan (PCCP) legislation5. The PCCP⏎authority enables manufacturers to innovate more efficiently and rapidly while still ensuring the⏎safety and effectiveness of the device. For AI-enabled devices, in particular, it enables the⏎regulatory framework to keep better pace with the rapid innovation inherent to AI, thereby⏎ensuring clinicians and patients have timely access to improved devices. This authority is a⏎promising example of how new and right-sized policy development can occur in response to⏎changes in technology or regulatory needs. FDA’s AI PCCP guidance document6 is a valuable⏎tool to promote consistent understanding and implementation of this new process. However, it is⏎important that FDA’s implementation of the PCCP framework continues to evolve as AI⏎technology and its use in medical devices continue to evolve. A restrictive or static approach to⏎implementation of the PCCP authority diminishes its intended efficiency to implement device⏎improvements. We encourage FDA to authorize PCCPs that promote efficient post-market⏎changes for AI-enabled devices. If implemented with the intended flexibility, authorized PCCPs⏎can enable the regulatory frameworks to keep pace with the rapid innovation inherent to AI.⏎Clear and well-scoped guidance is a valuable tool that helps innovators understand regulatory⏎expectations and supports the development of safe and effective devices. In January 2025, FDA⏎issued draft guidance on lifecycle management and marketing submission recommendations for⏎3 Congress authorized the De Novo classification process in the Food and Drug Administration Modernization Act⏎(FDAMA) of 1997. The De Novo classification process is authorized under section 513(f)(2) of the Federal Food,⏎Drug, and Cosmetic Act (the FD&C Act). FDA De Novo regulations are outlined in 21 CFR Part 860, Subpart D⏎4 https://www.fda.gov/industry/medical-device-user-fee-amendments-mdufa-fees/mdufa-reports⏎5 On December 29, 2022, section 3308 of the Food and Drug Omnibus Reform Act of 2022, Title III of Division FF⏎of the Consolidated Appropriations Act, 2023, Pub. L. No. 117-328 (“FDORA”) added section 515C⏎“Predetermined Change Control Plans for Devices” to the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic (FD&C) Act. Section⏎515C of the FD&C Act (21 U.S.C. 360e-4) has provisions regarding predetermined change control plans (PCCPs)⏎for devices.⏎6 FDA’s guidance titled, “Marketing Submission Recommendations for a Predetermined Change Control Plan for⏎Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Device Software Functions” was issued as a final guidance document in August⏎2025. https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/marketing-submission-⏎recommendations-predetermined-change-control-plan-artificial-intelligence⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 4 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 5 of 17⏎AI-enabled devices7. It important that the recommendations in the final guidance are⏎appropriately scoped to align with FDA’s established risk-based and least burdensome regulatory⏎principles. Advanced AI systems, such as deep learning and deep neural network models, present⏎unique validation and lifecycle management considerations that may warrant targeted regulatory⏎attention. By contrast, more traditional or less complex machine learning approaches are⏎generally well addressed under existing FDA frameworks. However, the draft guidance appears⏎to apply a one-size-fits-all approach to all AI-enabled devices. It is important that the final⏎guidance distinguish between these categories to allow FDA to focus oversight where risk and⏎uncertainty are greatest while avoiding unnecessary regulatory burden for established⏎technologies. Additionally, certain elements of the current draft, particularly the blanket⏎recommendations for enhanced post-market oversight and the extensive transparency and⏎documentation requirements would create substantial burden without proportionate clinical⏎benefit. We encourage FDA to continue refining these elements in the final guidance to ensure⏎that recommendations therein remain clinically meaningful and consistent with the least⏎burdensome provisions of the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act.⏎Early and frequent dialogue between innovators and FDA reviewers helps enhance mutual⏎understanding and can facilitate the development of innovative devices. Direct one-on-one⏎engagement between FDA and innovators throughout the device development phase, such as⏎through FDA’s Breakthrough Devices Program8, can reduce regulatory surprises and accelerate⏎the path to market for medical devices. A stable and appropriately-resourced FDA that attracts⏎and retains top talent ensures the FDA workforce has the right knowledge base to keep pace with⏎the rate of innovation and promotes consistency during the regulatory review phase. Continued⏎support and reauthorization of user fee agreements like MDUFA9 are vital because they ensure⏎accountability and resource availability for both the FDA and industry.⏎B. Securing Coverage and Payment for AI Under CMS’ Payment Systems⏎As noted above, a lack of clear and consistent coverage and payment policies for AI and digital⏎health technologies significantly limits beneficiary access to novel and innovative technologies⏎under the Medicare program. Below are our recommendations for specific changes to CMS’⏎regulations to expand access to AI and related technologies.⏎1. Hospital Outpatient Prospective Payment System (OPPS)⏎7 FDA draft guidance; “Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Device Software Functions: Lifecycle Management and⏎Marketing Submission Recommendations” https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-⏎documents/artificial-intelligence-enabled-device-software-functions-lifecycle-management-and-marketing⏎8 The Breakthrough Devices Program is implemented under section 515B of the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic⏎Act (FD&C Act) (21 U.S.C. 360e-3), as created by section 3051 of the 21st Century Cures Act (Cures Act),⏎amended by section 901 of the FDA Reauthorization Act of 2017, and amended by Section 3001 of the SUPPORT⏎for Patients and Communities Act (the SUPPORT Act) (the “Breakthrough Devices Program”). The Breakthrough⏎Devices Program is a voluntary program for certain medical devices and device-led combination products that⏎provide for more effective treatment or diagnosis of life-threatening or irreversibly debilitating diseases or⏎conditions.⏎9 Medical Device User Fee Amendments (MDUFA); https://www.fda.gov/industry/fda-user-fee-programs/fda-user-⏎fees-explained⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 5 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 6 of 17⏎a. New Technology Ambulatory Payment Classification (APC) Improvements⏎In the CY 2023 OPPS/ASC final rule, CMS established a policy for the separate payment of⏎SaaS add-on codes, excluding SaaS from the packaged payment policy at 42 CFR 419.2(b)(18).⏎We are greatly encouraged by CMS acknowledging the uniqueness of these technologies, and⏎recognizing the need for separate payment and consideration of these services. As CMS⏎recognized in the CY 2023 OPPS/ASC final rule, the number of such services going through the⏎FDA review process has and will continue to rapidly increase. We encourage CMS to provide⏎much needed stability and certainty regarding SaaS by formalizing the exception to the packaged⏎payment policy in regulatory text. We further recommend CMS establish a dedicated section of⏎the OPPS rule to SaMD and related technologies moving forward, as opposed to limiting⏎discussion and consideration of these services within the New Technology APC section of the⏎preamble text.⏎Beyond the current SaaS pathway, we believe additional changes are needed to ensure⏎appropriate payment systems are in place for novel SaMD, including algorithm-based healthcare⏎services (ABHS)10. We therefore offer the following three recommendations.⏎First, we ask the agency to revise the New Technology Ambulatory Payment Classification⏎(APC) application process for these FDA-regulated, software-based interventions. The⏎application process and criteria should be tailored to the unique characteristics of these⏎technologies, while staying true to the policy priorities CMS established as part of the current⏎New Technology APC application requirements (including the creation of procedural C-codes as⏎needed). As an example, the application should reflect the impact of a SaMD on care pathways⏎and how these services assist practitioners in the delivery of care.⏎Second, we encourage CMS to modify the current New Technology APC policies as they relate⏎to SaMD both currently assigned to a New Technology APC and for future technologies⏎(including via a potential New Technology APC for SaMD application pathway). Specifically,⏎we recommend CMS: (1) provide stability for SaMD developers by assigning these technologies⏎to a New Technology APC for at least five years; and (2) waive the Universal Low Volume APC⏎policy for these technologies when assigned to a New Technology APC. Both recommendations⏎are intended to ensure stability during the New Technology APC period. As it relates to the five-⏎year price stability period, we note this would align with the lifespan of a Category III code and⏎is necessary to ensure appropriate data collection and analysis can occur while hospitals adopt⏎novel SaMD. Further, this five-years of assigned payment stability is intended to ensure there are⏎not variations in New Technology APC assignment based on misreported or omitted cost⏎information. In recent years, including the CY 2024 OPPS/ASC final rule, we have seen how the⏎existing policies create payment aberrations that pose serious threats to the adoption of these⏎novel SaMD, such as confusion among adopters and a chilling effect on innovation.⏎10 ABHS are clinical analytical services delivered by FDA-cleared devices to a healthcare practitioner that use⏎artificial intelligence, machine learning, or other similarly designed software to produce clinical outputs for the⏎diagnosis or treatment of a patient’s condition. ABHS provide quantitative and qualitative analyses, including new,⏎additional clinical outputs that detect, analyze, or interpret data to improve screening, detection, diagnosis, and⏎treatment of disease.⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 6 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 7 of 17⏎Third, we urge CMS to be proactive in considering clinical APC assignments for SaMD. While⏎some technologies can be appropriately assigned to an existing clinical APC, CMS should begin⏎to consider policy options for future SaMD that may not meet the criteria for assignment to⏎current clinical APCs. In recognition of the continual evolution of SaMD, we urge CMS to⏎continue to work with developers to ensure these technologies can be appropriately transitioned⏎out of New Technology APC payments after five years and be assigned to an appropriate clinical⏎APC, including potentially creating new APCs when necessary.⏎b. Transitional Pass-Through Payment (TPT) Improvements⏎Under TPT and the new technology APC policies for eligible new devices, CMS provides higher⏎payments to aid with the uptake by hospitals of eligible new medical devices. The current TPT⏎criteria specifically require a device must —⏎• Be an integral part of the service furnished;⏎• Be used for one patient only;⏎• Come in contact with human tissue; and⏎• Be surgically implanted or inserted (either permanently or temporarily) or applied in or⏎on a wound or other skin lesion.⏎Furthermore, the device cannot be any of the following:⏎• Equipment, an instrument, apparatus, implement, or item of this type for which⏎depreciation and financing expenses are recovered as depreciable assets; or⏎• A material or supply furnished incident to a service (for example, a suture, customized⏎surgical kit, scalpel, or clip, other than radiological site marker).⏎As AI and SaMD are increasingly introduced for more and more procedures and frequently⏎include components that do not come into contact with patients or represent a capital⏎expenditure, these criteria are inappropriately exclusionary for these technologies. In particular,⏎because capital costs are included in establishing the APC payment rate, the same rationale⏎applies equally when calculating the operating costs for pass-through payments. We therefore⏎urge CMS to consider improvements to the TPT program allowing SaMD and other technologies⏎that do not come into contact with patients, but enable clinicians to provide optimal care to be⏎eligible for additional payments under this program.⏎2. Physician Fee Schedule⏎We encourage CMS to consider holistic updates to its fee-for-service payment systems to reflect⏎the significant resource investments required to adopt and implement not only SaMD, but the⏎broader scope of workflow and process improvement software and related technologies. These⏎investments extend beyond procurement of a software license and include cybersecurity updates,⏎staff training, and hardware procurement. While these costs are typically considered indirect and⏎therefore not reimbursable, we encourage the agency to consider how they might be incorporated⏎into annual market basket updates or other update factors to ensure appropriate accounting for⏎provider costs. Given the new and innovative nature of SaMD, we recommend CMS consider⏎payment policies that are harmonized across payment systems and reflect that many of these⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 7 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 8 of 17⏎technologies may lack historical claims data. As one option, CMS may consider utilizing G⏎codes for supply of SaMD and the associated monitoring. This approach, which the agency has⏎already similarly implemented in its DMHT payment policy, would apply to SaMD that have an⏎FDA indication, have undergone rigorous clinical and safety reviews, and include quality⏎controls. The physician could procure the license, prescribe to the patient, and receive⏎reimbursement.⏎In addition to bringing consistency to payment, this approach would further allow CMS to⏎recognize the critical and distinct value of the different types of SaMD. Technologies designed⏎for chronic care management, preventative care, and early diagnosis are all vastly different with⏎varying costs and substantial potential value for patients, clinicians, and the health care system.⏎We encourage the agency to implement payment policies that reflect the diversity of individual⏎SaMD and the collective benefits these technologies offer.⏎3. Durable Medical Equipment, Prosthetics, Orthotics, and Supplies (DMEPOS) Fee Schedule⏎The DME benefit category can be used for certain SaMD that meet the regulatory definition of⏎DME. However, the current definition creates significant barriers to access under this program.⏎We strongly recommend CMS revise its DME regulations to provide more specific direction for⏎how digital therapeutic software technologies can be covered and paid under the program by:⏎• Clarifying DME coverage policies to reflect how digital therapeutics can be covered as⏎DME;⏎• Revising accreditation standards for the special characteristics of SaMD;⏎• Aligning payment policy with the duration the SaMD technology is intended to be used;⏎• Ensuring distribution flexibility that supports patient access, understanding that transfer⏎of title is not always appropriate or desired for these products; and⏎• Clarifying how the requirements within the statutory definition of DME apply to SaMD⏎(e.g., recognizing that some digital therapeutic devices, which are regulated by the FDA⏎as SaMD, have a three-year extended life but may not be appropriate for use by multiple⏎individuals and thus may not be appropriate for rental).⏎4. Additional Considerations⏎a. Accounting for AI-Enabled Devices that Streamline Operations while Improving Outcomes⏎We further recommend that CMS consider alternative payment pathways for AI-enabled capital⏎that can substantially reduce waste, enhance workflow efficiencies or improve patient quality⏎and better outcomes including through medication management. Though inpatient settings may⏎have historically conducted these internal value assessments, post-acute facilities including⏎Skilled Nursing Facilities and Behavioral Health settings are limited in their capacity to adopt⏎new technologies. We recommend that CMS work with industry, hospital, and pharmacy leaders⏎to envision operational and clinical excellence with a reimbursement pathway to incent it.⏎b. Accounting for Capital Investment in Standalone AI Technologies under Medicare’s Payment⏎Systems⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 8 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 9 of 17⏎Despite the advancements in prevention, treatments and therapies, challenges to access and⏎disparities in care persist in the US health system. Factors such as geographic inaccessibility,⏎economics, insurance, QHP shortages, health system bias, race and ethnicity, language and many⏎other factors contribute to this growing trend. In addition, for many conditions, access to⏎evidence-based first line therapy is limited at best, reducing care quality, negatively impacting⏎population health and increasing total health care costs. The very nature of SaMD provides⏎opportunities to achieve access to evidence-based care at scale, especially for underserved⏎populations. These innovative technologies are proven to deliver clinically effective treatment⏎and allow for ongoing care to reach health care deserts where access to QHPs is challenged and⏎where existing treatments fall short.⏎In addition to the SaMD recommendations discussed above, we encourage CMS to consider⏎holistic updates to its fee for service payment systems to reflect the significant resource⏎investments required to adopt and implement not only SaMD, but the broader scope of workflow⏎and process improvement software and related technologies. These investments extend beyond⏎procurement of a software license and include cybersecurity updates, staff training, and hardware⏎procurement. While these costs are typically considered indirect and therefore not reimbursable,⏎we encourage the agency to consider how they might be incorporated into annual market basket⏎updates or other update factors to ensure appropriate accounting for provider costs.⏎C. Data Privacy Regulatory Updates and Clarifications⏎1. “Health Care Operations” under HIPAA⏎We recommend amending the definition of “health care operations” at 45 CFR § 164.501 to⏎explicitly include AI model development and validation for the delivery, management, or⏎improvement of health care services, with appropriate guardrails. The current definition of health⏎care operations permits a HIPAA covered entity to perform specified activities without⏎individual authorization, including “conducting quality assessment and improvement activities . .⏎. provided that the obtaining of generalizable knowledge is not the primary purpose of any⏎studies resulting from such activities.” Where the primary purpose of such activities is obtaining⏎generalizable knowledge, the activity would instead be categorized as research, as defined at 45⏎CFR § 164.501. However, as HHS itself has recognized, the distinction between quality⏎assessment and improvement activities and research can be unclear. This is often the case in the⏎context of product innovation, where the line between health care operations-type improvements⏎and research and development can be difficult to discern. When evaluating activities that fall⏎within this gray area, covered entities must choose between following HIPAA’s research rules,⏎which require the entity to obtain certain permissions and take additional compliance steps,⏎thereby delaying initiation of the activity, or risking enforcement exposure if a regulator⏎disagrees with the covered entity’s conclusion that the activity qualifies as health care operations.⏎Proposed language: Amend paragraph (1) of the definition of “health care operations” in 45 CFR⏎§ 164.501:⏎Health care operations means any of the following activities of the covered entity to⏎the extent that the activities are related to covered functions:⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 9 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 10 of 17⏎(1) Conducting quality assessment and improvement activities, including⏎outcomes evaluation and development of clinical guidelines, provided that the⏎obtaining of generalizable knowledge is not the primary purpose of any⏎studies resulting from such activities; AI model development and validation⏎for the delivery, management, or improvement of health care services; patient⏎safety activities (as defined in 42 CFR 3.20); population-based activities⏎relating to improving health or reducing health care costs, protocol⏎development, case management and care coordination, contacting of health⏎care providers and patients with information about treatment alternatives; and⏎related functions that do not include treatment.⏎The proposed modifications would help provide certainty to covered entities that encounter this⏎ambiguity when developing and improving AI models used for specified health care purposes. It⏎would also enable them to fast-track AI innovations by not subjecting lower-risk activities to the⏎burdensome HIPAA research rules. In addition, with its purpose limitation, including by⏎extending health care operations model development only to internal activities, the proposal⏎maintains protections for individuals, requiring compliance with existing HIPAA rules where⏎protected health information (PHI) is sought to be used for other purposes.⏎2. HIPAA De-Identification Safe Harbor Flexibility⏎We recommend enhancing the flexibility of the de-identification standard at 45 CFR § 164.514⏎by allowing Safe Harbor de-identified datasets to retain otherwise restricted indirect identifiers⏎that are important for AI development. HIPAA provides two means through which PHI may be⏎de-identified. The first is the Safe Harbor method, which requires the removal of specified data⏎elements and the absence of actual knowledge that the remaining information can be used to⏎identify an individual. The second requires a formal determination from a qualified expert that,⏎based on an analysis meeting certain criteria, the risk of re-identifying an individual in the⏎dataset is very small. Once data has been de-identified under either method, it is no longer⏎subject to HIPAA’s restrictions on the use and disclosure of PHI. This makes HIPAA de-⏎identified data valuable for various purposes, including AI product development and training.⏎The Safe Harbor method is generally easier to apply, as it does not require specialized⏎knowledge that most organizations do not have in-house. This allows internal teams to de-⏎identify data as needed in the regular course of their operations. The expert determination⏎method, however, usually requires organizations to engage a third-party expert, which demands⏎valuable time, money, and other valuable resources. Notwithstanding, many organizations are⏎forced to rely on the expert-determination method, given its flexibility, which provides a⏎pathway to creating and using more valuable de-identified datasets. Under that method, an expert⏎can deem a dataset as de-identified, even if it has data elements that could not be included under⏎the Safe Harbor, provided the expert’s analysis meets HIPAA requirements. Dates related to⏎individuals (e.g., date of service) and zip codes are examples of data elements that HIPAA-⏎regulated entities often identify as valuable for product development that must be excluded from⏎a Safe Harbor de-identified dataset, and that often are approved through expert determinations.⏎Proposed language: Amend 45 C.F.R. § 164.514(b)(2) by adding paragraphs 45 C.F.R. §⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 10 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 11 of 17⏎164.514(b)(2)(iii) and (iv):⏎(b) Implementation specifications: Requirements for de-identification of protected⏎health information. A covered entity may determine that health information is⏎not individually identifiable health information only if:⏎. . .⏎(2) (i) The following identifiers of the individual or of relatives, employers, or⏎household members of the individual, are removed:⏎(A) Names;⏎(B) All geographic subdivisions smaller than a State, including street⏎address, city, county, precinct, zip code, and their equivalent geocodes,⏎except for the initial three digits of a zip code if, according to the⏎current publicly available data from the Bureau of the Census:⏎(1) The geographic unit formed by combining all zip codes with the⏎same three initial digits contains more than 20,000 people; and⏎(2) The initial three digits of a zip code for all such geographic units⏎containing 20,000 or fewer people is changed to 000.⏎(C) All elements of dates (except year) for dates directly related to an⏎individual, including birth date, admission date, discharge date, date of⏎death; and all ages over 89 and all elements of dates (including year)⏎indicative of such age, except that such ages and elements may be⏎aggregated into a single category of age 90 or older;⏎(D) Telephone numbers;⏎(E) Fax numbers;⏎(F) Electronic mail addresses;⏎(G) Social security numbers;⏎(H) Medical record numbers;⏎(I) Health plan beneficiary numbers;⏎(J) Account numbers;⏎(K) Certificate/license numbers;⏎(L) Vehicle identifiers and serial numbers, including license plate⏎numbers;⏎(M) Device identifiers and serial numbers;⏎(N) Web Universal Resource Locators (URLs);⏎(O) Internet Protocol (IP) address numbers;⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 11 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 12 of 17⏎(P) Biometric identifiers, including finger and voice prints;⏎(Q) Full face photographic images and any comparable images; and⏎(R) Any other unique identifying number, characteristic, or code, except⏎as permitted by paragraph (c) of this section; and⏎(ii) The covered entity does not have actual knowledge that the information⏎could be used alone or in combination with other information to identify⏎an individual who is a subject of the information.⏎(iii) Notwithstanding paragraphs (b)(2)(i) and (b)(2)(ii) of this section, a⏎covered entity may determine that health information is not individually⏎identifiable if it contains identifiers described in paragraph (b)(2)(iv),⏎provided that the dataset contains only the minimum necessary of such⏎data elements required for a specific AI development or training use case,⏎is used only for such purposes, and the covered entity complies with⏎paragraph (b)(2)(ii).⏎(iv) Identifiers listed in paragraphs (b)(2)(i)(B) and (b)(2)(i)(C) may be⏎included in a de-identified dataset, in accordance with paragraph⏎(b)(2)(iii).⏎The proposal seeks to relieve HIPAA-regulated entities of the burden of relying on expert⏎determinations to create valuable de-identified datasets for model development and training.⏎This would allow those entities to dedicate more resources toward efficiently creating and⏎improving AI-enabled products that can enhance the experience and outcomes of patients and⏎consumers, while mitigating potential risks associated with the re-identification of individuals in⏎de-identified datasets. Under the proposal, only the minimum necessary indirect identifiers⏎would be permitted in a Safe Harbor de-identified dataset, as required by the circumstances of a⏎particular use case.⏎3. Use of AI to Process PHI for Treatment, Payment, or Health Care Operations⏎We recommend clarifying that it is permissible to process the minimum necessary PHI through a⏎third-party generative AI tool for treatment, payment, or health care operations purposes,⏎provided that certain conditions are met. In accordance with the HIPAA Privacy Rule at 45⏎CFR, Subparts A and E, HIPAA requires an individual to authorize uses and disclosures of PHI,⏎unless an exception applies. Per 45 CFR § 164.502, one such exception allows PHI to be used⏎and disclosed for treatment, payment, and health care operations. A HIPAA covered entity may⏎disclose PHI to service providers, known as business associates, for these purposes, provided that⏎certain contractual requirements are in place and the business associate does not use or disclose⏎PHI for its own purposes. However, given the ways in which generative AI tools process data⏎and iteratively improve and learn, it is not always clear to covered entities whether PHI may be⏎input into generative AI tools, even where the developer of the tool intends to serve as a business⏎associate and has established safeguards to protect the confidentiality of PHI.⏎Uncertainty about the permissibility of HIPAA-regulated entities using these tools, which are⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 12 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 13 of 17⏎routinely leveraged in other sectors, could chill adoption and delay the health care industry’s⏎realization of their benefits. Thus, it would be helpful to clarify that where PHI is processed⏎through a generative AI tool for treatment, payment or health care operations, only the minimum⏎amount of PHI is processed for such purposes, and there is limited risk of the PHI being used or⏎further disclosed for other purposes, given zero-day retention or similar controls, this qualifies as⏎a permissible use and disclosure of PHI under HIPAA. Notably, this offers individuals⏎additional protections in the treatment context, given that the current HIPAA rules do not require⏎a health care provider’s disclosure for treatment purposes to meet the minimum necessary⏎standard.⏎Proposed guidance: HHS should issue an FAQ or other guidance clarifying the permissibility of⏎these activities to avoid a situation where the lack of regulatory clarity may delay AI adoption,⏎increase compliance costs, and slow innovation.⏎4. Patient Rights⏎We recommend clarifying that an individual’s rights with respect to their PHI are subject to a⏎reasonableness standard. HIPAA provides individuals with multiple rights regarding their PHI,⏎as specified at 45 CFR §§ 164.522–164.528, including rights to access, amend, or receive an⏎accounting of disclosures of their PHI. When an individual exercises these rights, their requests⏎may cover information dating back as many as six years. Upon receipt of an individual rights⏎request, a HIPAA-regulated entity must timely review certain of its systems for responsive⏎records, and action the request as appropriate.⏎The rules granting these rights were written decades ago, and did not contemplate the⏎sophistication and complexity of modern AI technologies. It can be difficult and time-⏎consuming for an organization to validate where data resides within such systems, and to⏎produce the requested information or make requested changes. This challenge is compounded as⏎organizations deploy AI systems that interconnect, such as multi-agent systems, and that process⏎and share data in ways that can be difficult to track and document. Amending the rules to⏎require reasonable efforts to respond to individual rights requests would recognize the⏎practicalities organizations face when responding to these requests, while still honoring the⏎individual rights set out under HIPAA.⏎Proposed guidance: HHS should issue guidance clarifying that a HIPAA-regulated entity’s⏎reasonable efforts to action an individual’s request to exercise rights with respect to PHI⏎processed by an AI system satisfy the entity’s obligations under HIPAA. This can help avoid⏎applications of HIPAA rules that hinder beneficial AI uses.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎We believe HHS can most effectively support private sector activities by prioritizing the⏎development and use of consensus-based standards rather than imposing new regulatory⏎expectations such as third-party evaluation infrastructures for AI tools. Consensus standards⏎developed through accredited bodies provide a flexible yet rigorous foundation for testing,⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 13 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 14 of 17⏎accreditation, and certification activities without unnecessarily exposing proprietary methods or⏎increasing compliance costs. Such standards can guide voluntary industry-driven testing, support⏎credentialing efforts, and foster interoperability in a way that aligns with private sector⏎innovation imperatives and real-world needs.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎A. Regulation of AI-Enabled Medical Devices⏎Maintaining the U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA) as the sole federal regulator of⏎medical devices is essential to protecting patients while supporting continued innovation. FDA⏎has decades of experience overseeing medical devices through a risk-based, lifecycle approach⏎that already accounts for software, machine learning, and AI-enabled technologies. Creating⏎additional or parallel regulatory requirements at other federal agencies would be duplicative,⏎confusing, and inefficient; increasing compliance burden for industry and resource strain for⏎regulators without improving safety. Fragmented oversight risks inconsistent standards, delayed⏎patient access to beneficial technologies, and uncertainty for manufacturers and healthcare⏎providers. Policymakers should reinforce FDA’s primary authority over medical devices and⏎ensure that cross-agency AI initiatives defer to FDA where medical devices are concerned, rather⏎than introducing new regulatory layers that undermine an established, effective framework.⏎B. American Medical Association (AMA) Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) Hurdles⏎The CPT code set, maintained under the authority of the AMA, is the de facto language for⏎reporting medical services and procedures across the U.S. healthcare system. Payers—including⏎Medicare, Medicaid, and commercial insurers—and providers rely on CPT codes to describe the⏎procedure performed, measure utilization, and set payment rates. Because CPT serves as the⏎backbone for billing, clinical documentation, reimbursement, health information exchange, and⏎utilization analysis, having an accurate and appropriate CPT code is often a prerequisite for⏎widespread adoption of any medical service or technology, especially for new technologies that⏎don’t resemble traditional clinician-performed procedures.⏎AI and digital health technologies face unique hurdles in obtaining CPT codes that appropriately⏎reflect the technology itself and/or the resources required for the accompanying service.⏎Category III codes, where those early AI tools that have been able to obtain a CPT code have⏎landed, are often considered “experimental and investigational” by payers and therefore either⏎not reimbursed or reimbursed at rates far below the actual cost of the procedure and technology.⏎Transitioning from a Category III code to a Category I code often requires demonstrating⏎“widespread clinical use” and presenting strong clinical evidence, which can take years to⏎develop even after FDA market authorization. These limitations result in codes that often lag⏎behind real-world innovation, which slows commercial adoption of these technologies, limits⏎clinician use, and adversely affects investment in next-generation technologies based on a lack of⏎clear reimbursement pathways.⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 14 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 15 of 17⏎Recently, the AMA’s Digital Medicine Coding Committee (DMCC) proposed a potential new⏎coding framework, called “Clinically Meaningful Algorithmic Analyses” (CMAA) defined as “is⏎a mechanism to codify algorithmic analysis of clinically relevant patient data (e.g., biophysical⏎signals, imaging data, lab results) to produce clinically meaningful output or conclusions that⏎impact patient care.”11 While we appreciate AMA’s continued engagement with industry on this⏎proposal, we remain concerned about the impact of creating a differentiated code set for⏎algorithmic analyses without a defined pathway to coverage and payment. Shoehorning AI⏎services into this proposed CMAA code set without a clear payment pathway could stifle⏎innovation and investment in this space. Algorithm-only services that would potentially fall⏎under the CMAA code set as drafted exist in other sections of CPT and have received coverage⏎and payment under the Physician Fee Schedule (PFS), Hospital Outpatient Prospective Payment⏎System (OPPS), or Clinical Laboratory Fee Schedule (CLFS). Creation of new code set without⏎a defined pathway to coverage and payment not only jeopardizes new technologies but risks⏎disrupting coverage and payment for existing services. As AMA continues conversations around⏎a potential CMAA code set, we strongly urge CMS to take a more active role in ongoing⏎conversations around coding for AI and digital health technology-enabled procedures, and to⏎consider use of its own code creation authorities where appropriate to ensure timely⏎reimbursement for these technologies.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?⏎We see interoperability as critical for unlocking the full potential of AI in clinical settings.⏎Enhanced interoperability across health systems and platforms would reduce data fragmentation⏎and enable richer, more representative datasets for training and validating AI models.⏎Specifically, standardization of clinical data formats and exchange protocols that preserving⏎patient privacy protections would accelerate research and model development by making it easier⏎to aggregate and harmonize data across diverse sources.⏎Preserving key clinical elements in de-identified datasets (such as age, gender, diagnoses, and⏎outcomes) while maintaining privacy safeguards would expand the utility of data for AI research⏎without compromising confidentiality. In addition, interoperability efforts grounded in consensus⏎standards can facilitate benchmarking of model performance, support comparative evaluations⏎across systems, and ultimately broaden market opportunities by enabling tools to perform⏎reliably in varied care environments.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎We believe HHS should prioritize research that supports the development of international⏎consensus standards and regulatory science tools that promote regulatory efficiency and the⏎development of trustworthy and effective AI-enabled devices.⏎11 American Medical Association. CPT® workgroups and committees. American Medical Association. Updated⏎February 2, 2026. https://www.ama-assn.org/about/cpt-editorial-panel/cpt-workgroups-and-committees⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 15 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 16 of 17⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical⏎care?⏎FDA has authorized over 1,300 AI-enabled medical devices that leverage the transformative⏎power of AI to improve the patient experience and enable clinicians to better understand⏎diseases, achieve faster, more accurate diagnostic results, and determine appropriate treatment⏎pathways. Illustrative examples of how patients and clinicians benefit from incorporating AI-⏎enabled technologies into clinical care are highlighted in AdvaMed’s AI Policy Roadmap12 and⏎Advancing Patient Care: AI in Medical Devices document13. We additionally offer the following⏎specific examples of AI-enabled devices with accompanying resources that quantify the impact⏎and benefits of these devices when used in clinical care:⏎• Medtronic’s GI Genius™ intelligent endoscopy module is a computer-aided detection⏎system that uses AI to identify pre-cancerous and cancerous polyps in real-time during⏎colonoscopies, assisting physicians in reducing the rates of missed polyps. There are over⏎30 publications, including 8 randomized controlled trials (RCTs), that demonstrate the⏎clinical impact of the GI Genius™ module.14⏎• Stryker’s Triton device incorporates an AI tool designed for estimating surgical blood⏎loss, most commonly applied in obstetric procedures. This technology provides a real-⏎time, quantitative method to estimate blood loss during surgery, offering advancement⏎over traditional gravimetric and visual assessment methods. Rapid and accurate⏎estimation of blood loss can enable the clinical care team to make more informed and⏎timely decisions regarding postpartum hemorrhage (PPH) management and treatment⏎protocols. Published studies demonstrate that implementation of the quantitative blood⏎loss estimation can improve accuracy of blood loss estimation compared to gravimetric⏎and visual techniques.15,16 Reported clinical outcomes include an increase in the detection⏎12 AdvaMed’s AI Policy Roadmap (2025): https://www.advamed.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/AI-Policy-⏎Roadmap.pdf⏎13 AdvaMed’s Advancing Patient Care: AI in Medical Devices document (2025): https://www.advamed.org/wp-⏎content/uploads/2026/02/Advancing-Patient-Care-AI-in-Medical-Devices.pdf⏎14 https://www.medtronic.com/content/dam/medtronic-wide/public/united-states/products/digestive-⏎gastrointestinal/gastrointestinal-artificial-intelligence/gi-genius-intelligent-endoscopy-module-infographic-info-⏎sheet.pdf⏎15 Doctorvaladan SV, Jelks AT, Hsieh EW, Thurer RL, Zakowski MI, Lagrew DC. Accuracy of Blood Loss⏎Measurement during Cesarean Delivery. AJP Rep. 2017 Apr;7(2):e93-e100. doi: 10.1055/s-0037-1601382.⏎16 Rubenstein AF, Block M, Zamudio S, Douglas C, Sledge S, Tully G, Thurer RL. Accurate Assessment of Blood⏎Loss during Cesarean Delivery Improves Estimation of Postoperative Hemoglobin. Am J Perinatol. 2019⏎Mar;36(4):434-439. doi: 10.1055/s-0038-1669397.⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 16 ::⏎⏎ASTP/ONC RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 17 of 17⏎rate of PPH after cesarean sections compared to use of visual blood loss estimation,17,18⏎as well as possible reduction in transfusion dose and shorter length of stay.13⏎As the U.S. Government seeks to ensure AI-enabled products across healthcare are used safely,⏎we appreciate the opportunity to provide feedback on regulatory priorities and recommendations⏎impacting medical technologies. AdvaMed member companies take seriously the trust patients⏎place in them and have consistently acted to identify best practices balancing innovation with⏎patient protections. Thank you for the opportunity to submit these comments. Please consider⏎AdvaMed as a resource while you continue to consider regulations related to AI and medical⏎devices.⏎Thank you for your consideration of AdvaMed’s comments. Please do not hesitate to contact us⏎with any questions using the contact information below.⏎Sincerely,⏎Terry Chang, M.D., Esq.⏎Vice President, Assistant General Council⏎Director, Medical & Legal Affairs⏎AdvaMed⏎tchang@advamed.org⏎Geeta Pamidimukkala, MS⏎Vice President, Technology & Regulatory Affairs⏎AdvaMed⏎gpamidimukkala@advamed.org⏎Zach Hornberger⏎Senior Director, Digital Health & Imaging Technology⏎AdvaMed⏎zhornberger@advamed.org⏎Kirsten Tullia, JD, MPH⏎Senior Vice President, Payment & Health Care Delivery Policy⏎Head of Research⏎AdvaMed⏎ktullia@advamed.org⏎17 Rubenstein AF, Zamudio S, Al-Khan A, Douglas C, Sledge S, Tully G, Thurer RL. Clinical Experience with the⏎Implementation of Accurate Measurement of Blood Loss during Cesarean Delivery: Influences on Hemorrhage⏎Recognition and Allogeneic Transfusion. Am J Perinatol. 2018 Jun;35(7):655-659. doi: 10.1055/s-0037-1613675.⏎18 Katz D, Wang R, O'Neil L, Gerber C, Lankford A, Rogers T, Gal J, Sandler R, Beilin Y. The association between⏎the introduction of quantitative assessment of postpartum blood loss and institutional changes in clinical practice: an⏎observational study. Int J Obstet Anesth. 2020 May;42:4-10. doi: 10.1016/j.ijoa.2019.05.006.⏎advamed.org :: @AdvaMedUpdate :: AdvaMed 17 ::♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0271♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise (IHE) International, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise (IHE) International♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted via Regulations.gov⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎February 23, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Washington, DC⏎Dear Sir or Madam,⏎Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise (IHE) appreciates the opportunity to respond to the⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care.⏎At the outset, IHE believes it is important to state clearly that the Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ASTP/ONC) has been a principal sponsor, strategic collaborator, and long-standing policy⏎partner of IHE for more than two decades. ASTP/ONC’s sustained engagement has helped⏎translate federal health IT priorities into practical, testable, real-world interoperability⏎across the U.S. healthcare system. This collaboration has supported national objectives⏎related to patient safety, data exchange, security, clinical workflow integration, and public⏎trust; foundational elements that are equally critical to the successful adoption of Artificial⏎Intelligence (“AI”) in clinical care.⏎IHE is a global, non-profit, multi-stakeholder initiative that convenes clinicians, vendors,⏎health systems, standards development organizations, and regulators to develop⏎implementation guidance (“IHE Profiles”) and to validate those specifications through⏎rigorous, public, real-world testing events (Connectathons). IHE does not develop AI⏎algorithms, sell products, certify vendors, or provide clinical services. Our role is to ensure⏎that complex health IT ecosystems, including AI-enabled systems, can reliably exchange⏎data, integrate into clinical workflows, and be governed safely at scale.⏎Interoperability as a Prerequisite for Clinical AI Adoption⏎Across healthcare, and particularly in data-intensive domains such as medical imaging,⏎IHE’s experience consistently demonstrates that the most significant barriers to AI⏎IHE Response - HHS Health Sector AI RFI 1⏎⏎adoption are not algorithmic performance, but fragmentation of data, workflows, and⏎accountability across systems.⏎Effective clinical AI depends on:⏎● Timely access to high-quality clinical data from multiple systems⏎● Reliable clinical context (orders, protocols, patient characteristics)⏎● Predictable integration into operational workflows⏎● Traceability of AI outputs for audit, oversight, and continuous improvement⏎Absent robust interoperability, AI tools become difficult to deploy safely, expensive to⏎maintain, and challenging to evaluate once in use. These challenges increase operational⏎risk for providers, slow innovation, and ultimately undermine public trust.⏎IHE’s ongoing work reflects this reality. For example, IHE Radiology has developed the⏎AI Results (AIR) and AI Result Assessment for Imaging (AIRA) Profiles, which⏎define how AI-generated findings and their subsequent assessment can be encoded,⏎stored, queried, and retrieved using established healthcare standards such as DICOM⏎and HL7, while aligning with modern web-based interoperability approaches including⏎DICOMweb and HL7 FHIR. Rather than introducing parallel AI-specific data models,⏎this work demonstrates how existing standards infrastructure can be profiled and used⏎in combination to support interoperable, traceable AI integration within clinical systems.⏎Links to IHE Radiology AI specifications:⏎AI Results (AIR)⏎https://www.ihe.net/uploadedFiles/Documents/Radiology/IHE_RAD_Suppl_AIR.pdf⏎AI Result Assessment for Imaging (AIRA)⏎https://www.ihe.net/uploadedFiles/Documents/Radiology/IHE_RAD_Suppl_AIRA.pdf⏎In addition, IHE Radiology is advancing Manifest-Based Access to DICOM Objects⏎(MADO), which supports the structured exchange of a manifest identifying specific⏎DICOM objects—such as relevant prior studies, series, or images—associated with a⏎current clinical encounter. By enabling systems to reference and retrieve imaging⏎objects that are highly likely to be clinically relevant, rather than requiring manual search⏎across entire prior studies, MADO supports more efficient workflows and more reliable⏎data access. Such structured data access is particularly important in AI-enabled⏎environments where relevance, context, and traceability are essential.⏎Link to MADO Public Comment Draft:⏎https://www.ihe.net/uploadedFiles/Documents/Radiology/IHE_RAD-Suppl_MADO_Rev1-⏎0_PC_2025-12-08.pdf⏎Barriers to Innovation and Adoption⏎From an interoperability and implementation perspective, key barriers include inconsistent⏎use of standards for AI-related data exchange, limited mechanisms for tracing AI outputs⏎to source data and model versions, and the absence of scalable approaches for post-⏎IHE Response - HHS Health Sector AI RFI 2⏎⏎deployment performance monitoring. These gaps frequently force providers and⏎innovators into bespoke, vendor-specific integrations that do not scale and inhibit⏎competition.⏎IHE’s experience in imaging environments illustrates these challenges clearly. AI⏎outputs must be represented in standardized formats, stored alongside imaging studies,⏎retrieved in predictable ways, and subject to structured review and assessment. The⏎AIR and AIRA Profiles were developed specifically to address these integration and⏎traceability concerns within diagnostic imaging workflows, enabling structured⏎representation of AI findings and documentation of human or system assessment of⏎those results.⏎Evaluation and Post-Deployment Monitoring⏎IHE strongly supports HHS’s emphasis on real-world evaluation of AI systems. Pre-⏎deployment validation alone cannot ensure sustained safety, equity, and effectiveness⏎across diverse care environments.⏎Interoperability-enabled evaluation approaches—including standardized representations⏎of AI inputs, outputs, confidence measures, and audit trails—are essential to support⏎ongoing monitoring, bias detection, and performance benchmarking.⏎The AIRA Profile, for example, defines mechanisms to document structured assessment of⏎AI results, enabling systems to record whether AI outputs were accepted, modified, or⏎rejected, and to support quality monitoring and post-market surveillance activities. This⏎type of standards-based assessment infrastructure can contribute meaningfully to the⏎governance and oversight models contemplated in this RFI.⏎Federal support for evaluation frameworks that rely on interoperable data exchange, rather⏎than proprietary reporting mechanisms, would materially strengthen the AI ecosystem.⏎Supporting Accreditation and Industry-Driven Testing⏎IHE encourages HHS to continue supporting and aligning with private-sector conformance⏎testing, accreditation, and certification activities that validate not only algorithmic claims,⏎but real-world interoperability and workflow integration.⏎IHE’s Connectathon testing program provides a structured, multi-vendor environment⏎where interoperability capabilities—including AI-related integration—can be validated.⏎Vendors that successfully demonstrate interoperability through multi-vendor testing may⏎be recognized through mechanisms such as the IHE Connectathon SEAL, which⏎provides a transparent, non-regulatory signal that specific interoperability capabilities⏎have been objectively tested.⏎ASTP/ONC’s long-standing collaboration with IHE demonstrates how public-private⏎partnerships can reduce provider burden while increasing predictability, safety, and⏎trust—outcomes that are directly applicable to AI-enabled clinical systems.⏎IHE Response - HHS Health Sector AI RFI 3⏎⏎Offering a Practical, Testable Execution Pathway⏎While ASTP/ONC and many healthcare stakeholders are familiar with IHE’s role, other⏎federal partners involved through HHS’s interagency coordination may be less aware of⏎existing mechanisms that already support real-world validation and testing of complex⏎health IT capabilities.⏎IHE respectfully offers its experience and infrastructure as a practical execution pathway⏎that HHS and its federal partners could leverage to explore scalable, non-regulatory⏎approaches to AI validation and oversight.⏎Specifically, IHE provides:⏎● A neutral, multi-stakeholder forum where vendors, providers, and regulators⏎collaboratively define implementable requirements aligned with policy goals⏎● Operational implementation guidance that translates standards and policy intent⏎into deployable system behavior⏎● Public, real-world testing environments (Connectathons) where interoperability,⏎workflow integration, and traceability can be validated across multiple vendors and⏎systems⏎● Feedback loops that surface real-world implementation challenges early, before⏎they become systemic risks⏎Rather than creating new federal testing programs or bespoke validation frameworks, HHS⏎could consider how existing, industry-supported mechanisms—such as IHE Profiles and⏎Connectathons—might be used or extended to evaluate interoperability, auditability, and⏎workflow integration of AI-enabled clinical systems in a practical, repeatable manner.⏎IHE has recently launched a new program, IHE PRISM, designed to prioritize⏎interoperability in support of key market imperatives. This framework provides an ideal⏎structure to accelerate the adoption and responsible use of artificial intelligence in clinical⏎care. The IHE team looks forward to exploring with ASTP/ONC and other stakeholders how⏎this new approach can help advance shared goals in this area.⏎IHE does not regulate vendors or assess clinical efficacy. However, decades of experience⏎demonstrate that transparent, collaborative testing of real-world system behavior⏎materially improves safety, reduces implementation burden, and accelerates responsible⏎adoption. These same principles are directly applicable to AI-enabled clinical care.⏎Looking Forward⏎IHE views the adoption of AI in clinical care as a continuation of longstanding federal⏎health IT priorities rather than a departure from them. The same principles that enabled⏎nationwide electronic health record adoption, imaging exchange, and health information⏎IHE Response - HHS Health Sector AI RFI 4⏎⏎networks—clear expectations, implementable guidance, and real-world testing—are⏎equally necessary to ensure AI delivers on its promise.⏎IHE appreciates ASTP/ONC’s leadership and sustained partnership and stands ready to⏎continue supporting HHS and its federal partners in advancing practical, testable⏎approaches to AI adoption that enhance patient safety, clinical effectiveness, and public⏎trust.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise (IHE)⏎Aaron Goldmuntz⏎Managing Director⏎IHE International⏎www.ihe.net⏎IHE Response - HHS Health Sector AI RFI 5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0272♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donald♔Brown♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔Center for Democracy and Technology♔♔♔♔♔♔The framing of this RFI bothers me, and I want to be direct about why. HHS is asking how to "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical care, which presupposes that speed is the right goal. We don't think it is, and I want to focus my comments specifically on the questions around implementation incentives and what the Department frames as "barriers to adoption."⏎⏎The Center for Democracy and Technology works extensively on algorithmic accountability, and over the past two years we have seen a consistent pattern in how health systems are actually deploying these tools. The gap between how AI is marketed to regulators and payers and how it functions at the point of care is significant. Hospitals and insurers have every financial reason to use AI as a cost-reduction mechanism rather than a care-improvement one. Prior authorization automation is the clearest example. The tools being deployed by major commercial insurers are not primarily designed to improve clinical outcomes. They are designed to process denial decisions faster and at lower administrative cost. UnitedHealth Group's use of an algorithm called nH Predict to deny post-acute care claims, which drew substantial scrutiny in late 2023 and into 2024, is exactly the kind of deployment that this RFI's framing of AI as a care accelerant tends to obscure.⏎⏎I keep thinking about the labor dimensions of this, which the RFI doesn't really address at all. AI-powered productivity monitoring, automated clinical documentation tools, and algorithmic scheduling systems are changing what it means to work as a nurse, a medical coder, a home health aide. Many of those jobs are held disproportionately by women and workers of color. When a documentation tool adds fifteen minutes of review burden per patient encounter because the generated note keeps misattributing medications or getting the laterality wrong, that cost doesn't show up in any ROI calculation the vendor presents to the C-suite. It shows up in the body of the nurse at 10pm. The promise that these tools reduce clinician burnout is genuinely unverified at scale. I am not saying it's impossible that they help in some settings, but the evidence base is thin and the anecdotal reports coming out of implementation sites are mixed at best.⏎⏎There is also a power problem that HHS needs to take seriously. When an AI system generates a care recommendation and a frontline worker disagrees with it, what institutional mechanism exists for that worker to push back? In most settings, none that are meaningful. The vendor's system is a black box. The hospital's contract with the vendor almost certainly includes clauses that limit the hospital's ability to audit the model or share its outputs externally. The worker has no standing, often no time, and may face implicit pressure to defer to the system because overrides create documentation obligations. This is a transfer of decision-making authority to opaque third-party vendors who have no accountability relationship with patients or with communities.⏎⏎HHS should not be asking how to remove barriers to adoption. It should be asking what conditions would make adoption trustworthy. Those are different questions and they lead to different policy answers.⏎⏎Specifically regarding the RFI's questions on reimbursement incentives: we strongly oppose any approach that ties payment incentives to AI adoption rates or that treats AI integration as a quality indicator absent evidence of patient benefit. Attaching financial rewards to adoption creates exactly the wrong pressure on health systems. It will push deployment timelines faster than safety or equity review can keep pace with, and it will reward the systems with the most capital to spend on vendor contracts rather than the ones delivering the best care.⏎⏎One more thing I want to flag. The demographic disparities in how algorithmic systems affect patients are already documented well enough to act on. CDT has reviewed data from multiple sources, including survey findings presented at the National Multiple Sclerosis Society's 2024 Annual Research and Policy Summit, showing that Black and Hispanic patients with MS were significantly more likely than white patients to report having treatment plans altered by automated systems without explanation. That is not a hypothetical future harm. HHS accelerating adoption without mandatory bias audits, without patient notification requirements, and without accessible appeals processes will make those disparities worse.⏎⏎The question of who captures the financial benefit of healthcare AI and who absorbs its costs is not a technology question. It's a governance question, and right now the answer is that vendors and health system executives capture the benefit and workers and patients♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0273♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Newton's Tree, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Newton's Tree♔♔♔♔♔♔This response is based on experience of testing and deploying healthcare AI for care in healthcare organisations. ⏎⏎As highlighted in the introduction to this requisition for information (RFI), uncertainty of the healthcare staff is a key issue that must be addressed in order to improve AI adoption. This is a common finding in multiple Western jurisdictions. Fundamental to addressing this issue is evidence that an AI product works locally. The need for this evidence is unsurprising as medicine is empirical. However, multiple research studies have highlighted that AI products are sensitive to their environment (i.e. an AI product  that works well in one healthcare organisation, may not work well in another, geographically close organisation). Therefore, rapid local evaluations of AI products help improve confidence that they can be trusted in live care. This should be done with a local sandbox, which is something we provide to healthcare organisations. ⏎⏎Regarding question 4 of this RFI, it is important to note that the methodology of evaluation is relative to the organisation and product involved. For example, some organisations may need to test usability as well as effectiveness. Question 4 also considers post deployment methodologies to ensure safety, which is significant because of AI’s sensitivity to its environment, and an environment can change. Emerging best practice to address this is a continuous monitoring dashboard. We have developed such a dashboard, and worked with the Medicines and Healthcare products Regulatory Agency in the UK on a project regarding it, which was favourable to its use.⏎⏎The regulation section of this RFI also highlights the importance of protecting the confidentiality of patient’s health information. A key way of ensuring this is by keeping data within the estate of the healthcare organisation. That is achieved by using an AI deployment platform. Further, an added benefit is that established technical infrastructure can be reused for other AI deployments, reducing the time to use in care.⏎⏎Finally, for question 5, it is important that local evaluations, mentioned above, are done independently of the vendor to cultivate confidence in their findings. ⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0274♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Hughes, Tracy, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Tracy♔Hughes♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a Licensed Clinical Social Worker with over 30 years of experience, I am writing to express grave concerns re: the risks of accelerating the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.  While there is great need for expanded medical and mental health services, it is critical that the services individuals and families are safe, prioritize the wellbeing of clients, and protect privacy.  We have seen AI chatbots encouraging youth to consider suicide, and private startups that provide remote therapy have compromised protected health information, as well as abruptly and unsafely terminating clients' therapy with their providers.  Furthermore, we know from years of research that the greatest predictor of successful therapy and clinical change is the therapeutic relationship.  The adoption of AI in this arena needs to be carefully considered and studied for the safety and efficacy of treatment.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0275♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from OpenNotes Lab Nursing, Patients & AI Collaborative, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔OpenNotes Lab Nursing, Patients & AI Collaborative♔♔♔♔♔♔Dear Department of Health and Human Services Office of the Secretary,⏎We have prepared the following comments in response to your recent RFI:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care.⏎Question 1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Response. Private‑sector innovation in clinical AI is shaped less by algorithmic⏎performance than by entrenched sociotechnical, infrastructural, and market‑structure⏎barriers. Across health systems, poor workflow fit, concentrated control of integration⏎pathways, variable technical capacity, and ambiguity in regulatory expectations⏎collectively slow development, prolong procurement, and reduce the viability of new⏎entrants in the clinical AI ecosystem.⏎Sociotechnical and Workflow Barriers. Sociotechnical constraints consistently⏎emerge as the most significant impediments to AI innovation and adoption.⏎Inadequate interprofessional co‑design, particularly insufficient integration of⏎nurses, who make up the largest segment of the healthcare workforce, combined⏎with weak alignment to real‑world workflows increases cognitive burden and⏎undermines trust, a pattern documented repeatedly across qualitative and⏎scoping reviews.1,2 Health‑system survey findings similarly show that⏎administrative AI tools, such as ambient clinical documentation, achieve faster⏎and more successful uptake than diagnostic decision‑support tools because they⏎align more naturally with existing documentation workflows and entail lower⏎perceived clinical risk.3⏎Market Concentration and Vendor Dependence. Market structure further⏎constrains innovation. Two electronic health record (EHR) vendors dominate the⏎acute‑care EHR market, jointly holding approximately two‑thirds of national⏎1 Hassan, M., Kushniruk, A., & Borycki, E. (2024). Barriers to and Facilitators of Artificial Intelligence⏎Adoption in Health Care: Scoping Review. JMIR Human Factors, 11, e48633.⏎https://doi.org/10.2196/48633⏎2 Jacob, C., Brasier, N., Laurenzi, E., Heuss, S., Mougiakakou, S. G., Cöltekin, A., & Peter, M. K. (2025).⏎AI for IMPACTS Framework for Evaluating the Long-Term Real-World Impacts of AI-Powered Clinician⏎Tools: Systematic Review and Narrative Synthesis. Journal of Medical Internet Research, 27, e67485.⏎https://doi.org/10.2196/67485⏎3 Poon, E. G., Lemak, C. H., Rojas, J. C., Guptill, J., & Classen, D. (2025). Adoption of artificial⏎intelligence in healthcare: survey of health system priorities, successes, and challenges. Journal of the⏎American Medical Informatics Association : JAMIA, 32(7), 1093–1100.⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf065⏎⏎market share, and one vendor alone accounted for nearly two‑thirds of all⏎hospital EHR purchasing decisions in 2024.4,5 As a result, most AI products must⏎conform to the integration pathways, APIs, and strategic priorities of these⏎dominant platforms, creating structural disadvantages for smaller AI developers⏎and limiting the diversity of viable innovation pathways.6⏎Infrastructure Gaps, Technical Fragility, and Regulatory Ambiguity. Infrastructure⏎limitations amplify these challenges in under‑resourced rural and non‑academic⏎settings, where outdated IT systems, limited broadband capacity, and budget⏎constraints hinder the computational and data‑handling requirements needed for⏎AI deployment.7,8 Even within well‑resourced health systems, technical fragility⏎remains a barrier: scanner‑ or device‑related variation, batch effects, and other⏎forms of data drift can quietly erode model performance when AI tools encounter⏎hardware, laboratory instruments, or sequencing platforms that differ from those⏎used during development and validation.7,8 Finally, regulatory ambiguity⏎surrounding AI tools that fall outside traditional medical‑device pathways⏎contributes to prolonged procurement cycles, as legal, compliance, and⏎risk‑management teams must interpret uncertain oversight and liability⏎frameworks. In many organizations, these reviews extend contracting timelines⏎from several months to nearly a year, particularly for first‑in‑class or⏎enterprise‑scale AI deployments.1,3,9⏎Question 2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations.⏎4 Landi, H. (2025). Epic gains more ground in hospital EHR market share, widens its lead over Oracle⏎Health: KLAS. Fierce Healthcare.⏎5 Pennic, F. (2025). KLAS: Epic Dominates 2024 EHR Market Share Amid Focus on Vendor Partnership;⏎Oracle Health Sees Losses Despite Tech Advances. HIT Consultant.⏎6 Marwaha, J. S., Landman, A. B., Brat, G. A., Dunn, T., & Gordon, W. J. (2022). Deploying digital health⏎tools within large, complex health systems: key considerations for adoption and implementation. NPJ⏎digital medicine, 5(1), 13. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-022-00557-1⏎7 Brown, K. E., & Davis, S. E. (2026). Gaps in artificial intelligence research for rural health in the United⏎States: a scoping review. Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association: JAMIA, 33(2),⏎509–520. https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf206⏎8 Davis, K. Telehealth and Health Information Technology in Rural Healthcare (Last Updated: 2/9/2026).⏎Rural Health Information Hub.⏎9 Mennella, C., Maniscalco, U., De Pietro, G., & Esposito, M. (2024). Ethical and regulatory challenges of⏎AI technologies in healthcare: A narrative review. Heliyon, 10(4), e26297.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e26297⏎⏎Response. HHS should prioritize regulatory, payment, and programmatic reforms that⏎support a total product lifecycle (TPLC) approach to clinical AI, strengthen transparency⏎and governance, and align reimbursement with demonstrated clinical and operational⏎value. These efforts should address gaps in post‑deployment monitoring, value‑based⏎payment structures, organizational oversight, and supply‑chain transparency, all of⏎which are necessary to ensure that AI tools function safely, reliably, and equitably under⏎real‑world clinical conditions.⏎Regulatory Priorities. A TPLC posture is essential for both device and non‑device⏎AI tools. Consistent with emerging FDA direction for AI‑enabled technologies,⏎one‑time pre‑deployment accuracy assessments are insufficient; instead,⏎regulators should require ongoing monitoring to understand how models perform⏎and evolve in dynamic clinical environments.10,11 Minimum expectations should⏎include routine performance surveillance, documentation of model versioning and⏎provenance, including hardware and firmware dependencies, transparent change⏎logs, and formalized drift‑detection processes with documented mitigation⏎pathways.⏎Payment Policy Priorities and Rewarding Verified Value. HHS should modernize⏎payment policy to incentivize measurable improvements rather than mere⏎technology acquisition. In alignment with the RFI’s critique of fee‑for‑service⏎spending bubbles, CMS should pilot value‑based AI add‑ons within existing⏎payment frameworks to reimburse tools that demonstrably reduce clinician⏎burden, expand access, or improve outcomes.12 Such models would help shift⏎adoption incentives away from procurement volume and toward verifiable⏎improvements in efficiency, care quality, and patient experience.⏎Alignment with Federal Standards. To strengthen governance consistency across⏎the health‑care ecosystem, HHS should encourage practices analogous to the⏎Office of Management and Budget’s M‑24‑10 guidance, including AI inventories,⏎named governance leadership, and minimum risk‑management standards⏎tailored for clinical use cases involving protected health information.13 These⏎expectations would help health systems adopt more structured, transparent, and⏎10 Sandalow, M., Adams, K., & Loud, G. (2025). FDA Oversight: Understanding the Regulation of Health⏎AI Tools. Bipartisan Policy Center.⏎11 U.S. Food and Drug Administration Total Product Life Cycle for Medical Devices. (2023). U.S. Food and⏎Drug Administration.⏎12 Tierney, A. A., Reed, M. E., Grant, R. W., Doo, F. X., Payan, D. D., & Liu, V. X. (2025). Health Equity in⏎the Era of Large Language? Models. American Journal of Managed Care, 31(3).⏎https://doi.org/10.37765/ajmc.2025.89695⏎13 OMB M-24-10 Advancing Governance, Innovation, and Risk Management for Agency Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence. The White House.⏎⏎accountable approaches to AI oversight, especially for non‑device tools that fall⏎outside traditional regulatory frameworks.⏎Transparency and Supply‑Chain Assurance. Finally, HHS should support the⏎creation of a standardized AI Bill of Materials (AIBOM) to improve visibility into⏎model lineage, data sources, dependencies, and training or evaluation artifacts.14⏎A standardized AIBOM would build on existing federal and industry initiatives and⏎address the growing need for transparency in AI supply chains, supporting more⏎robust contracting, auditing, and post‑market surveillance.⏎Question 3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS⏎play to help address them?⏎Response. Non-device AI challenges legacy accountability structures because opaque,⏎adaptive models make it difficult to allocate liability among developers, hospitals, and⏎clinicians, and because ‘black-box’ architectures undermine explainability and⏎reproducibility of decision-making; in addition, when such tools influence care decisions⏎outside clearly defined regulatory indications, they raise practical concerns about⏎off-label use and exposure to unresolved medico-legal risk.15 Privacy questions also⏎emerge when models infer sensitive attributes or generate secondary data that are not⏎cleanly addressed by existing frameworks. HHS should build on emerging⏎responsible‑AI guidance by issuing model contract provisions for clinical AI, covering⏎performance and drift‑monitoring obligations, bias‑mitigation duties, and notification and⏎update service‑level commitments, clarifying expectations for human oversight of⏎non‑device AI in clinical workflows, and exploring safe‑harbor‑style protections for⏎organizations that implement specified governance and monitoring standards. These⏎steps would align with broader federal AI principles emphasizing transparency, human⏎oversight, and risk‑based governance, while directly addressing the contractual and⏎liability uncertainties that currently fuel institutional risk aversion. Clear expectations for⏎human‑in‑the‑loop oversight are needed to ensure that adaptive or generative AI⏎systems remain under meaningful clinical supervision, a safeguard emphasized across⏎recent federal AI strategy documents and FDA’s supervised agentic AI implementations.⏎14 Vandendriessche, W., Thijsman, J., D'hooge, L., Volckaert, B., & Sebrechts, M. (2026). AIBoMGen:⏎Generating an AI Bill of Materials for Secure, Transparent, and Compliant Model Training. arXiv preprint⏎arXiv:2601.05703.⏎15 Cestonaro, C., Delicati, A., Marcante, B., Caenazzo, L., & Tozzo, P. (2023). Defining medical liability⏎when artificial intelligence is applied on diagnostic algorithms: a systematic review. Frontiers in Medicine,⏎10, 1305756. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2023.1305756⏎⏎Question 4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation⏎methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow⏎and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support⏎these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts,⏎grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Response. Robust evaluation must extend beyond accuracy to encompass workflow fit,⏎human‑AI teaming, and safety. Pre‑deployment, health systems should use⏎shadow‑mode comparisons, usability and workload testing, and bias assessment across⏎subgroups to detect risks before tools influence care. Evaluation frameworks should⏎also assess the integrity of human‑in‑the‑loop workflows, including how clinicians verify,⏎override, and contextualize AI‑generated recommendations, and how these oversight⏎points affect safety, workflow reliability, and cognitive load under real‑world conditions.⏎These human‑factors assessments should explicitly evaluate cognitive load impacts and⏎identify temporal clustering effects (e.g., whether shift‑change patterns or simultaneous⏎user activity degrade model responsiveness or reliability).2 Post‑deployment,⏎organizations need formal drift‑monitoring frameworks that continuously track input⏎data‑distribution shifts, model performance, and key human‑AI interaction signals, such⏎as clinician overrides, building on approaches already piloted for radiology and other⏎imaging AI tools.9,16 Federal advisory discussions similarly emphasize evaluating⏎generative AI for sociotechnical impact (workflow disruption, accountability, safety), not⏎accuracy alone.17 HHS can accelerate rigor by funding multi-site evaluation testbeds,⏎issuing human-factors guidance, and deploying cooperative agreements or prize⏎competitions to mature standardized benchmarking.⏎Question 5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Response. Successful adoption correlates with governance maturity, leadership⏎alignment, and cross-stakeholder collaboration, which are dimensions that can be⏎operationalized through standardized organizational readiness and maturity models for⏎16 Cook, C. J., Klug, J. R., Kandler, B. W., Baez-Suarez, A., Dachowicz, A. P., Blezek, D. J., . . . Kline, T.⏎L. (2026). State of the AI: Post-Deployment Monitoring of Radiology-Focused Internally Developed AI.⏎Mayo Clinic Proceedings: Digital Health, 100342.⏎https://doi.org/https://doi.org/10.1016/j.mcpdig.2026.100342⏎17 Cestonaro, C., Delicati, A., Marcante, B., Caenazzo, L., & Tozzo, P. (2023). Defining medical liability⏎when artificial intelligence is applied on diagnostic algorithms: a systematic review. Frontiers in Medicine,⏎10, 1305756. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2023.1305756⏎⏎clinical AI.18 HHS should recognize, or help convene, third‑party certification and⏎independent testing programs that evaluate safety, bias, robustness, including⏎hardware/firmware variability, and workflow outcomes using common reporting artifacts⏎(e.g., model cards plus AIBOM‑style disclosures). Accreditation and certification efforts⏎should be complemented by workforce‑level competency development to ensure that⏎organizational governance expectations are matched by staff skills and readiness.⏎Targeted workforce micro‑credentialing for clinicians, informaticians, and operational⏎leaders would further professionalize AI governance and reduce adoption risk.⏎Question 7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Response. Artificial intelligence adoption in clinical care is shaped not only by⏎technological performance but also by governance structures, workforce readiness, and⏎implementation strategy. Importantly, responsible AI use does not end at initial⏎implementation and must be integrated into the daily, ongoing operational work of⏎healthcare organizations.⏎Key Point 1-AI Adoption Requires Structured Governance and Oversight⏎Adoption of AI tools within healthcare organizations depends on clearly defined⏎governance pathways that involve executive leadership, clinical leadership,⏎informatics teams, compliance officers, and frontline stakeholders. The National⏎Academy of Medicine emphasizes that safe and effective integration of AI⏎requires institutional oversight mechanisms, defined accountability structures,⏎and transparency in system design and deployment.19 Similarly, the World Health⏎Organization underscores the importance of regulatory clarity, lifecycle⏎monitoring, and institutional governance in its guidance on artificial intelligence⏎for health.20 Without structured oversight, organizations may hesitate to adopt AI⏎due to uncertainty regarding validation standards, risk management, and⏎responsibility. As a best practice, federal guidance could encourage⏎patient-inclusive governance structures, clear accountability for AI oversight⏎18 Nair, M., Svedberg, P., Larsson, I., & Nygren, J. M. (2024). A comprehensive overview of barriers and⏎strategies for AI implementation in healthcare: Mixed-method design. PloS One, 19(8), e0305949.⏎https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0305949⏎19 National Academy of Medicine. (2023). Artificial intelligence in health care: The hope, the hype, the⏎promise, the peril. National Academies Press (US). https://doi.org/10.17226/27111⏎20 World Health Organization. (2023). Regulatory considerations on artificial intelligence for health.⏎https://www.who.int/publications/i/item/9789240078879⏎⏎within leadership roles, and organizational strategies that explicitly address⏎transparency, safety, and ethical use of AI across clinical and administrative⏎functions.⏎Key Point 2- Workforce Readiness and Competency Development are Critical⏎A significant administrative hurdle to AI adoption is the preparedness of⏎healthcare professionals to evaluate, interpret, and appropriately integrate⏎AI-supported outputs into clinical reasoning and documentation. The National⏎Academy of Medicine highlights workforce training and competency development⏎as essential components of responsible AI integration.20 Workforce readiness⏎directly shapes documentation transparency, communication with patients, and⏎decision-making confidence. Without structured education and clear expectations⏎for clinician oversight, organizations may experience hesitancy, inconsistent use,⏎or inappropriate reliance on AI-supported tools. Investment in competency⏎frameworks and practical training is therefore central to accelerating safe and⏎sustainable adoption. This need for workforce preparation is ongoing and⏎“evergreen,” particularly given routine staff turnover, the growth of contingent⏎workforce models, and the critical role of nonclinical staff who frequently serve as⏎primary interfaces with patients in both hospital and outpatient settings.⏎Key Point 3 - Implementation Challenges Extend Beyond Technical Performance⏎AI adoption barriers frequently arise not from algorithm performance alone but⏎from workflow disruption, ambiguity regarding liability, change-management⏎demands, and integration into existing documentation and quality oversight⏎processes. Implementation efforts must address how AI tools fit within clinical⏎workflows, how responsibility is allocated when AI informs decisions, and how AI⏎involvement is reflected in documentation without compromising clarity or⏎compliance. Regulatory developments such as the ONC’s Health Data,⏎Technology, and Interoperability (HTI) rulemaking, including HTI-1 provisions on⏎algorithm-transparency requirements for certified health IT, highlight the growing⏎importance of documentation disclosure and explainability in operational⏎settings.21 Addressing these nontechnical and operational factors is essential to⏎translating AI innovation into sustainable clinical practice.⏎Clear federal guidance that supports multidisciplinary governance, workforce⏎competency development, and transparent documentation standards may help reduce⏎institutional uncertainty and accelerate the responsible adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎21 Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology. (2023). Health data, technology,⏎and interoperability: Certification program updates, algorithm transparency (HTI-1 Final Rule).⏎⏎Question 9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Response. Patients and caregivers generally appreciate the potential of artificial⏎intelligence in clinical care to improve understanding, access, and coordination of⏎information, but meaningful adoption hinges on addressing concerns regarding trust,⏎transparency, explainability, and accountability.⏎Key Point 1- Improve Understanding and Navigation⏎Patients and caregivers frequently encounter challenges in interpreting clinical⏎documentation, coordinating information across care teams, and obtaining clear⏎explanations of their conditions and treatments. AI-enabled tools that summarize⏎clinical content, enhance clarity, and support navigation through health records⏎hold promise for addressing these challenges. Research indicates that a majority⏎of surveyed hospital patients, across multiple countries, expressed generally⏎positive overall attitudes toward AI use in health care, suggesting receptivity to⏎technologies that improve comprehension and accessibility when they are⏎integrated thoughtfully into care processes.22⏎Key Point 2- Concerns Related to Trust, Accountability, and Equity⏎Despite general openness to AI’s potential, patients express caution about⏎specific aspects of AI use. The study by Busch and colleagues (2025) found that⏎patients preferred AI systems that are explainable and that clinical⏎decision-making remains under physician oversight rather than autonomous AI⏎control. Attitudes varied by demographic, health status, and technological⏎literacy, with notable skepticism about AI’s accuracy in some diagnostic⏎information. These findings highlight that trust in AI is variable and is strongly⏎influenced by transparency, clinician involvement, and appropriate tailoring of AI⏎implementation.⏎Key Point 3- Transparency and Interpretability Must Guide Deployment⏎As patients increasingly interact with electronic health records and clinical⏎information, transparency and interpretability become essential for engagement⏎and trust. Evidence shows that patients are not uniformly confident in all aspects⏎of AI use, underscoring the need for clear communication about how AI is⏎applied, what it contributes, and how human clinicians remain responsible for⏎22 Busch, F., Hoffmann, L., Xu, L., et al. (2025). Multinational attitudes toward AI in health care and⏎diagnostics among hospital patients. JAMA Network Open, 8(6), e2514452.⏎https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.14452⏎⏎oversight. Prioritizing systems that clearly indicate AI involvement, explain⏎reasoning, and reinforce clinician validation may enhance patient comfort and⏎support equitable participation in care. Aligning implementation strategies with⏎patient preferences for explainability and oversight can help ensure that AI⏎adoption strengthens rather than undermines patient experience. Patients and⏎caregivers may expect AI-supported care to align with principles of shared⏎decision-making, particularly when AI-informed documentation and clinical⏎recommendations are visible through patient-facing records and portals.⏎Policies and implementation strategies that prioritize transparency, clinician oversight,⏎and clear communication about AI involvement may help ensure that adoption⏎strengthens patient trust and engagement rather than introducing uncertainty or⏎confusion.⏎Question 10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care? a. Are there published findings⏎about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in clinical care? b. How does the⏎literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎Response. HHS should prioritize research that strengthens the real‑world performance,⏎safety, and interoperability of clinical AI systems across diverse care environments.⏎Evidence from U.S. health‑system surveys shows that AI tools succeed when they⏎reduce documentation burden and integrate smoothly into workflows, whereas⏎higher‑risk diagnostic tools face more limited uptake, suggesting that research on⏎implementation science, human-AI teaming, and workflow fit remains foundational for⏎improving adoption. A second priority concerns rigorous evaluation frameworks that⏎capture long‑term clinical, organizational, and economic impact. The AI for IMPACTS⏎systematic review highlights the need for multidimensional evaluation, encompassing⏎integration, governance, safety, cost, and scalability, which expands beyond accuracy⏎metrics and aligns closely with a total‑product‑lifecycle approach increasingly⏎emphasized in federal policy.2,23 Third, as large language models and emerging⏎multi‑modal AI systems become embedded in documentation, summarization, and⏎decision‑support tasks, research should assess their reliability, error modes,⏎human‑oversight requirements, and performance across subpopulations and care⏎settings. Research is also needed to define optimal human‑in‑the‑loop models for large⏎language models, agentic systems, and multimodal AI, determining when human review⏎is required, how oversight affects safety and equity, and how to balance automation⏎23 Kiyasseh, D., Cohen, A., Jiang, C., & Altieri, N. (2024). A framework for evaluating clinical artificial⏎intelligence systems without ground-truth annotations. Nature Communications, 15(1), 1808.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41467-024-46000-9⏎⏎benefits with clinician accountability. Fourth, HHS should support studies addressing⏎robustness and context transfer, as peer‑reviewed findings show that variations in⏎scanner vendors, acquisition protocols, and biomedical data platforms can meaningfully⏎degrade model performance, highlighting the need for harmonization strategies and⏎drift‑monitoring frameworks to ensure equitable deployment across resource‑variable⏎sites. Finally, HHS should invest in economic and operational evaluations that quantify⏎the value of AI in clinical practice. Despite widespread deployment, few studies⏎systematically measure time returned to patient care, downstream utilization effects, or⏎total cost of ownership. Early evidence suggests that administrative AI offers clearer⏎near‑term benefits than diagnostic tools, but rigorous multi‑site assessment is required⏎to inform reimbursement models and ensure that financial incentives align with verifiable⏎improvements in efficiency, quality, and access.⏎10(a). Published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools⏎Published data from national health‑system surveys show that ambient⏎documentation and other workflow‑support tools generate the most consistent⏎early improvements, reducing documentation time, lowering burden, and⏎improving satisfaction, while diagnostic AI tools demonstrate more variable⏎performance and lower perceived success.3,24 Review evidence further indicates⏎that long‑term impact depends on sustained monitoring of governance, usability,⏎safety, and economic outcomes rather than one‑time accuracy evaluations.25⏎10(b). Costs, benefits, and transfers in the literature⏎The literature approaches the economics of AI cautiously: a recent systematic⏎review of clinical AI economic evaluations found only a small number of formal⏎cost-effectiveness and budget‑impact studies compared with the volume of⏎clinical AI trials, with substantial heterogeneity in methods and frequent omission⏎of key cost components. Most published analyses emphasize near‑term⏎operational efficiencies, such as reductions in unnecessary procedures or⏎screening costs, rather than comprehensive assessments that fully account for⏎acquisition, integration, maintenance, downstream outcomes, and distributional⏎effects. Existing reviews and actuarial analyses therefore call for standardized⏎approaches to quantify operational savings, avoided adverse events, and⏎24 Lukac, P. J., Turner, W., Vangala, S., Chin, A. T., Khalili, J., Shih, Y. T., Sarkisian, C., Cheng, E. M., &⏎Mafi, J. N. (2025). Ambient AI Scribes in Clinical Practice: A Randomized Trial. NEJM AI, 2(12),⏎10.1056/aioa2501000. https://doi.org/10.1056/aioa2501000⏎25 Nguyen, T. D., Whaley, C. M., Simon, K., Mehta, N., Yu, H., McBain, R. K., Mehrotra, A., & Cantor, J. H.⏎(2025). Adoption of Artificial Intelligence in the Health Care Sector. JAMA Health Forum, 6(11), e255029.⏎https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2025.5029⏎⏎workforce redistribution, and to clarify how AI‑generated value is allocated among⏎clinicians, health systems, payers, and patients.26⏎26 El Arab, R. A., & Al Moosa, O. A. (2025). Systematic review of cost effectiveness and budget impact of⏎artificial intelligence in healthcare. NPJ Digital Medicine, 8(1), 548.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01722-y⏎Sincerely,⏎The OpenNotes Lab Nursing, Patients & AI Collaborative⏎Laura M Moffat, DNP, APRN, AGCNS-BC⏎Alaa Albashayreh, PhD, RN⏎Patricia Folcarelli RN, MA, PhD⏎Shelley Calder, DNP,RN,CEN⏎Suzanne Sullivan Fink, MSN, RN⏎Cait DesRoches, MS, DrPh⏎Brian Laneau, MSN, RN⏎Rosie Bartel, MA⏎Amanda Norris, MDiv, MTS♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0276♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Albert, Amy, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amy♔Albert♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔PsiAN’s response urges federal policymakers to prioritize patient safety, privacy, clinical efficacy, and enforceable regulation before expanding AI use in healthcare, particularly in behavioral health and psychotherapy. AI has no place in mental health services! ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0277♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cordovano, Grace , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Grace♔Cordovano♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎James O’Neill⏎Deputy Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave. SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Thomas Keane, MD⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (“ASTP”)⏎U.S. Department of Health & Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave. SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎By electronic submission⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care; RIN 0955–AA13⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane and Deputy Secretary O’Neill:⏎I’m grateful for the opportunity to comment on the Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS), in collaboration with the Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ASTP/ONC), Request for Information on Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as part of Clinical Care from the patient,⏎carepartner, and caregiver perspective as we work towards building safe, effective,⏎collaborative, and accessible AI in clinical care for all patients and families.⏎From the perspective of a board-certified patient advocate, the primary carepartner to⏎two disabled adults, and a patient myself, I have experienced firsthand, personally and⏎professionally, both the promise and the failures of our healthcare system. In 2008, I⏎was misdiagnosed with advanced lymphoma. Because I knew to request my medical⏎records and seek a second opinion, months of testing, invasive procedures, endless⏎imaging, labs, video-assisted thoracic surgery, and waking up in recovery after surgery⏎in isolation for suspected tuberculosis ultimately revealed that I did not have cancer, but⏎rather a fungal infection acquired on my honeymoon that required no treatment, only⏎monitoring. Looking back, I can’t help but wonder if an AI powered software to read my⏎radiology could have provided a differential diagnosis that could have prevented months⏎invasive and traumatic testing and procedures as well as unnecessary, costly care. That⏎experience shaped my understanding of what is at stake when information,⏎interpretation, and decision-making tools fail, or succeed, in clinical care.⏎Executive summary⏎AI is already shaping clinical decisions across the health system, yet the patients whose⏎lives those decisions affect are largely excluded from visibility, participation, and⏎⏎oversight. The greatest barriers to responsible private-sector innovation are not solely⏎technical or regulatory but rather they are structural: unclear accountability, misaligned⏎incentives, reimbursement gaps, workforce readiness challenges, privacy concerns, and⏎insufficient mechanisms for real-world validation and patient partnership. Patients face a⏎paradox: AI is influencing our care while we often cannot access, understand, or⏎question the outputs used to make decisions about us.⏎Large language models and AI powered tools are transforming how patients,⏎carepartners, and caregivers engage with our health information, interpret diagnoses,⏎and navigate daily clinical realities while waiting weeks or months for appointments. Our⏎use of AI represents patient engagement, empowerment, and personalized⏎operationalization of care decisions in real time. Like any tool, these technologies carry⏎both promise and risk, which is precisely why patient, carepartner, and caregiver voices⏎must be embedded not only in listening exercises but in co-creation, co-development,⏎governance, and funded leadership roles that shape how AI is designed and deployed.⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to close this trust and governance gap by aligning policy,⏎payment, evaluation, and transparency expectations so innovation scales alongside⏎safeguards. Designed, implemented, and governed intentionally with patients as⏎partners, AI can improve outcomes, reduce costs, strengthen trust, and enable⏎clinicians to focus on what matters most: caring for people. Healthcare does not occur⏎only within the four walls of medicine, and neither should the governance, oversight, or⏎design of the technologies that increasingly shape it.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎After having the privilege of serving on numerous multi-stakeholder efforts and⏎taskforces on AI in health care, including the National Academy of Medicine’s AI Code of⏎Conduct project and the Digital Medicine Society’s AI Playbook work, the most⏎commonly discussed barriers to private sector innovation that I am hearing from various⏎stakeholders in AI include concerns about regulatory uncertainty, implementation⏎challenges, lack of reimbursement frameworks, ownership of liability for consequent⏎harm, privacy concerns, and lack of appropriate workforce and workforce training to⏎support assessment.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎⏎As we carefully define what effective use of AI in clinical care looks like, there are⏎concerns and problems defined by the patient, carepartner, and caregiver stakeholder⏎voice that need to be considered as we examine regulatory, payment policy, and⏎programmatic changes the HHS should prioritize to incentivize collaborative, trusted use⏎of AI in the healthcare ecosystem.⏎AI outputs and clinical decision support have been successfully used in patient care for⏎well over the last decade. Outputs in the form of, for example, risk prediction scores,⏎diagnostic support outputs, treatment optimization recommendations, early warning⏎alerts, medication safety intelligence, and the likes of prognostic and trajectory⏎modelling are routinely used to make informed decisions about patient care.⏎Building trust and transparency in AI is one of the cornerstone conversation topics in US⏎and global healthcare sectors. Ironically, patients and families have essentially little⏎knowledge of and no access to any of the AI intelligence that runs behind the scenes of⏎our care. AI derived outputs that are used to make decisions about an individual’s care⏎or coordination of care are not documented in our medical records. Not having access⏎to AI derived outputs has a broad spectrum of consequences, including but not limited⏎to:⏎• robbing us of opportunities to participate in shared decision-making in our or our⏎loved one’s care using intelligence from AI derived outputs.⏎• depriving us of clarity about the impact of use of AI in our care on the costs of our⏎care, and anecdotally, leading to growing concerns about our out-of-pocket costs⏎increasing as a result of the deployment AI powered technologies, such as⏎ambient listening.⏎• preventing us from learning about the transformative power of AI in our care and⏎outcomes and the celebrating positive impacts AI powered tools can bring;⏎downstream effects include denying us the opportunities to become trusted⏎sources of information in evangelizing and educating our families, friends, places⏎of worship, and communities about the positive impacts of AI.⏎• shielding us from the inconsistencies, hallucinations, errors, and deviations of AI⏎outputs that are experienced by our physicians and care teams, preventing us⏎from fully appreciating the art of medicine; downstream effects include denying⏎us the opportunities to become trusted sources of information in evangelizing and⏎educating our families, friends, places of worship, and communities about the⏎potential harms and negative impacts of AI.⏎• denying us the opportunities to inspect and curate the outputs and intelligence⏎generated for potential errors; patients, carepartners, and caregivers can help⏎assist in reducing “garbage in and garbage out” concerns.⏎⏎• being routinely excluded, overlooked, and denied opportunities to participate as⏎partners in the co-design, implementation, and integration of AI tools and⏎technologies that are going to impact our lives.⏎As we examine what HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to⏎augment use of AI our clinical care, I strongly recommend the following considerations.⏎AI-derived outputs used to make decisions about an individual’s care or coordination of⏎should be recognized as EHI (45 CFR § 171.102) and hence the designated record set⏎(45 CFR § 164.501) and consequently, the Right of Access (45 CFR § 164.524) should⏎apply. Individuals should have a right to access AI outputs as part of our medical record.⏎Measures should be taken to include access to AI outputs as in scope for⏎considerations of information blocking (45 CFR Part 171).⏎Programmatically speaking, patients, carepartners, and caregivers can help advance⏎the effective use of AI in our care. Listening to patient voices is not enough. Including⏎patients on stages to talk about our experiences with AI is not enough. Budgeting for⏎patient stakeholder voice partnership and leadership is necessary as we explore⏎specific changes that can be implemented programmatically at scale. Currently there⏎are zero federally patient designated AI governance, engagement and strategy focused⏎leadership roles. Innovation means doing things a new way. The time is now to fund the⏎roles that are needed to do the work.⏎At minimum, patients need to know where AI is used in our care, when it mattered, how⏎we can interpret it, how it impacts our costs of care, and how to operationalize on the⏎knowledge obtained. It should be documented as to how our doctors adjudicate⏎disputes, i.e., model output stands but input corrected, output rejected because of⏎patient history, output recalculated to include missing patient records.⏎It is well documented that errors in our medical record are common. The downstream⏎effects of these rampant errors in our records contribute to outputs that could cause⏎harm. Patients, carepartners, and caregivers should have a right to not only access AI⏎outputs used in our care and amend errors in our records, but also address errors in⏎potential AI outputs used in our care. A feedback loop is missing that can help support⏎post-deployment monitoring. As a patient, where can I go to report an error on an AI tool⏎that was implemented in my care? Who do I report that to at my healthcare system or⏎cancer center? I’ve yet to find a contact or point person or feedback portal.⏎Standard of care fails or in some cases isn’t even available for patients living with life-⏎altering, life-limiting conditions, rare disease, or multiple comorbidities. There are⏎⏎structured, standardized pathways, i.e., Right to Try or Expanded Access⏎(compassionate use), where patients who have exhausted all approved treatment⏎options can make an informed decision to try innovative treatment approaches that⏎have yet to be FDA approved when all standard of care has been exhausted or no⏎standard of care is available. We currently do not have comparable pathways for AI or⏎digital health for patients with serious or immediately life-limiting conditions. Innovation⏎can not only move from top-down. Patients and families are playing proactive roles in⏎our care and many are pleading for access to various innovations to manage their⏎condition only to be told by their health system, physician, cancer center, “we don’t do it⏎that way here.” I strongly encourage colleagues at FDA to explore Right to Try and/⏎Expanded Access type pathways for patients to access promising AI powered⏎innovations who are unable to participate in a clinical trial, or where a clinical trial may⏎not (yet) be available, to access certain investigational digital health treatment or⏎precision medicine options.⏎We are currently witnessing a metamorphosis of the doctor-patient relationship.⏎Programmatically, doctors and care providers need training for engaging with patients,⏎carepartners, and caregivers who are utilizing AI to drive our informed decision making.⏎There needs to be programming to also best support and educate patients, our⏎carepartners and caregivers, on how to best use AI in our care. These necessary⏎updates in education and training must be co-designed with the patient and family⏎stakeholder voice. Individuals who share their lived experience and experience to co-⏎create and reimage physician and nursing training as well as co-creating credible⏎educational resources to level the playing field for all patients should be fairly⏎compensated using models such as the National Health Council’s Fair Market Value⏎calculator or frameworks from PCORI.⏎We need to compensate and reimburse for the human work and the art of medicine that⏎ensures AI is safe and effective, including oversight, patient education, and shared⏎decision-making. We want to be able to discuss AI in our care with the doctors and care⏎teams we trust with our care. We need to reimagine how we virtually, digitally, and⏎asynchronously engage in our care. Patients need safe, trusted, secure spaces to⏎collaborate with our physicians, care teams, community health workers, social workers,⏎etc., in LLMs. I can invite a patient, carepartner, or caregiver to collaborate in a shared⏎google doc, but we could be more efficient if I could collaborate within an LLM shared⏎working space, where everyone can see my prompting, the outputs, the interrogations,⏎the feedback, the hallucinations and errors instead of me sending screenshots in group⏎texts. ChatGPT has a feature to start a group chat that in its current state, is useless⏎and misleadingly named. I am unable to invite my fellow patient advocates, patients,⏎⏎carepartners, caregivers, or colleagues in medicine into my deep dives on any given⏎day of patient advocacy work. The time is now to dream bigger.⏎America’s AI Action plan mentions sandboxes for research and testing. Using patient⏎and care team-led sandboxes to test payment policies that reimburse clinician time to⏎validate and interpret results, exploring the impact of shared decision-making⏎conversations where AI outputs guide choices. Multi-disciplinary reviews, such as⏎tumor-board style governance for predictive tools, could be implemented as another⏎sandbox use case. Payment should be paired with minimal documentation standards,⏎i.e., what output, how it impacted care, shared decision-making, and impact on⏎outcomes.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance⏎and accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy,⏎and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if⏎any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Patients, carepartners, and caregivers are encountering a new category of risk in AI-⏎enabled clinical care: decisions affecting our care may be influenced by systems we⏎cannot see, question, understand, or collaborate with. The core issue is not whether AI⏎can be useful, but whether governance has kept pace with deployment. Current legal⏎and operational frameworks do not clearly define responsibility when AI contributes to⏎clinical decision-making, how errors should be investigated, or how patients can⏎challenge outputs derived from incorrect or incomplete data. Privacy concerns are⏎equally urgent. AI development and deployment rely on extensive health data flows that⏎patients often cannot easily track or meaningfully understand, creating legitimate⏎concerns about misuse, secondary use, and security vulnerabilities. HHS is uniquely⏎positioned to close these governance gaps by setting consistent national expectations⏎for transparency, documentation, and accountability when AI informs clinical care.⏎Patients should be able to know when AI influenced a decision, understand the basis for⏎its output, and access mechanisms to question or correct it when necessary. Federal⏎leadership is essential to ensure that innovation scales alongside safeguards so that⏎advances in clinical AI strengthen patient trust rather than undermine it.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human- centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further⏎support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful⏎(e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎⏎From the patient and care partner perspective, HHS should ensure that non-device AI⏎used in clinical care is evaluated across its lifecycle for real-world safety, equity,⏎transparency, and usability, and technical accuracy through standardized pre-⏎deployment validation, post-deployment monitoring, and robustness testing. Patient-⏎partnered sandbox environments described above can be impactful and should be⏎strongly considered. There should be clear disclosure in clinical workflows, especially in⏎patient-facing disclosure when AI informs care decisions. CMS should align payment⏎and quality programs to support validated, monitored AI and the clinician time required⏎for safe use, oversight, patient education, and shared decision-making.⏎HHS could support real-world AI evaluation networks with patients as funded partners⏎by prioritizing this work within existing appropriations and authorities. HHS could⏎prioritize cooperative agreements and grants to build real-world evaluation networks⏎with patients as funded partners and co-creators, supplemented by targeted contracts⏎and prize competitions to accelerate techquity, explainability, and safety testing. CMS⏎demonstration and innovation models could fund real-world testing through CMMI pilots,⏎alternative payment models, i.e. ACCESS, and quality-reporting incentives to move⏎evaluations real-world practice. If patients are to be meaningfully placed in charge of⏎our care, federal policy must support us with the tools to evaluate the technologies⏎shaping clinical decisions about us and our lives. A dedicated funding stream should be⏎established for patient-driven AI evaluation networks to ensure empowerment is paired⏎with evidence, accountability, and safety.⏎HHS should align AI policy with established federal patient-engagement frameworks⏎(e.g., PCORI, FDA PFDD, AHRQ models) that treat patients, carepartners, and⏎caregivers as end-users and partners in design, evaluation, and oversight, while fairly⏎compensating individuals for their time, expertise, and lived experiences.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎From the patient and care partner perspective, HHS can best support private-sector⏎accreditation, certification, testing, and credentialing efforts for clinical AI by establishing⏎clear national expectations for transparency, safety monitoring, and real-world⏎performance while requiring meaningful patient, carepartner, and caregiver involvement⏎in design, evaluation, implementation, and governance. HHS should align payment,⏎certification, and procurement policies to incentivize adoption of independent validation⏎programs that assess not only technical accuracy but also techquity, usability, patient⏎⏎comprehension, and metrics that include patient perspectives on value. Federal⏎agencies can further accelerate responsible innovation by supporting public-private-⏎patient testing collaboratives and publishing model evaluation criteria that embed⏎patient stakeholder participation as a core standard. Trustworthy clinical AI is built not⏎only on strong algorithms, but on structured inclusion of the patient, carepartner, and⏎caregiver voices it is designed to serve.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give⏎new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI tools have demonstrated the greatest real-world impact when they address access⏎gaps, reduce administrative burden, or enable earlier clinical action within patient care⏎workflows. Some examples of AI tools that were deployed and met or exceeded in⏎clinical care, performance, and cost expectations include:⏎• Autonomous diabetic retinopathy screening in primary care⏎FDA-authorized autonomous AI systems for diabetic retinopathy have⏎demonstrated accurate detection of referable disease in primary care settings,⏎enabling point-of-care identification and referral without requiring immediate⏎specialist interpretation, which can help close screening gaps where specialty⏎access is limited.⏎• Ambient clinical documentation tools⏎Ambient listening has been documented to demonstrate meaningful reductions in⏎clinician documentation time and administrative burden as well as improving⏎clinician well-being.⏎• Hospital deterioration prediction models⏎AI-driven early-warning models analyzing EHR data in real time have reduced⏎adverse events by identifying clinical decline earlier and enabling earlier clinical⏎intervention.⏎• 4. AI clinical decision support for unnecessary testing (SmartAlert system)⏎Significant volume reductions of repeat laboratory testing demonstrating cost⏎savings through eliminating low-value care.⏎With respect to novel tools that could have the greatest potential for improving⏎healthcare outcomes comes to mind a Waze for Healthcare that harnesses the power⏎multi-stakeholder data intelligence and crowd-sourced peer health support in real-time⏎to help patients make informed decisions about the next steps of their care journeys⏎where they are.⏎⏎Patients and families are currently experiencing harm from automated or AI-assisted⏎payer systems used to process claims at scale, including batch denials and algorithm-⏎supported downcoding of reimbursements. When coverage or payment determinations⏎are automated but correction pathways remain slow, opaque, or difficult to access,⏎patients can face delayed care, unexpected costs, and administrative barriers that⏎directly affect health outcomes. Oversight, transparency, and clear accountability⏎standards must keep pace with automated decision tools so that efficiency gains do not⏎come at the expense of patient access, provider sustainability, or medically necessary⏎care.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider⏎specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎The greatest opportunities for AI advancement lie where patient data still remains⏎siloed. Interoperability that connects clinical records, claims, imaging, labs, genomics,⏎wearable data, and patient-reported outcomes would dramatically expand research⏎capability, improve model accuracy, and allow real-world validation across diverse⏎populations as well as enable robust patient-navigation technologies. Standardized⏎formats and terminologies such as FHIR, USCDI, LOINC, and SNOMED CT make it⏎possible for systems to exchange information safely and meaningfully, while⏎benchmarking infrastructure and shared evaluation datasets enable transparent⏎performance comparisons. At the same time, new or expanded standards are needed to⏎support consistent exchange, interpretation, and documentation of AI-derived outputs,⏎such as risk scores, predictions, recommendations, confidence levels, and model⏎provenance, so that these outputs can move across systems, be understood by⏎clinicians and patients, and be evaluated for safety and accuracy over time. Patients⏎experience the consequences of fragmented data every day; when our information⏎cannot move, innovation cannot scale. Interoperability is therefore not only a technical⏎priority but a patient safety and equity imperative.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Patients and caregivers want AI in clinical care to address persistent system failures⏎and gaps in care that routinely negatively impact our outcomes, including delayed or⏎missed diagnoses, fragmented care coordination, administrative burden that limits our⏎clinician’s time, inequitable access to specialty expertise, preventable medical errors,⏎⏎and financial toxicity. Tools that accelerate time to diagnosis, close care gaps, identify⏎risk earlier, reduce patient administrative burden, streamline treatment planning and⏎clinical trial navigation are examples of solutions viewed as high-value because they⏎improve both safety and the lived experience of our or our loved one’s care. At the⏎same time, patients and caregivers have significant concerns about the adoption of AI,⏎particularly when its role in decision-making is not transparent, when outputs cannot be⏎accessed, reviewed, or discussed, when systems may encode bias or worsen⏎disparities and gaps in care, or when accountability is unclear if harm occurs. Duty of⏎care and do no harm are key. There is also concern that poorly implemented AI could⏎increase cognitive burden on clinicians, generate alert fatigue, or shift decision⏎responsibility onto patients without adequate support. Many patients do not have a⏎primary carepartner or caregiver, may not be digitally savvy or may have language and⏎accessibility need. Patients consistently emphasize that trust depends on clear⏎disclosure of when AI is used, understandable explanations of how it informs care, the⏎ability to correct or challenge outputs, and assurance that human clinical judgment⏎remains responsible for final decisions. Patients do not oppose AI in care; we oppose⏎invisible, unaccountable AI that affects our health, our care, and our lives without our⏎knowledge or voice.⏎As we critically examine the concerns of the patient stakeholder voice, I strongly⏎encourage all of HHS agencies to read the AI Rights for Patients. This document was⏎created by a cohort convened by The Light Collective, a national community of patient⏎activists, clinicians, and health data experts committed to building tech and digital rights⏎for patients. There are no assumptions of what patients want in this rights framework as⏎this was fully co-created with 100% patient voices.⏎Closing⏎I applaud HHS's leadership in advancing responsible AI adoption in patient care. The⏎patient, carepartner, caregiver, and consumer stakeholder perspectives are critically⏎important in the advancement of responsible adoption of AI in patient care. Patient care⏎happens not only within the four walls of medicine, but also in the privacy of our homes,⏎places of work, worship, and community, and play.⏎I applaud HHS’s leadership in advancing responsible AI adoption in clinical care and for⏎recognizing that the voices of patients, carepartners, caregivers, and consumers are⏎essential to getting this right. Thank you for the opportunity to contribute to this⏎important national conversation. Patients are not asking to slow innovation, we are⏎asking to strengthen it. We want to help build an AI-enabled health system that is safer,⏎more transparent, more equitable, and more humane than the one we have today. I⏎urge the Department to build on this moment by ensuring that patient partnership is not⏎symbolic but structural, embedded in policy design, evaluation, governance, and⏎funding priorities. With intentional policy, meaningful partnership, and shared⏎accountability, AI can fulfill its promise not just as a technological advance but as a⏎⏎public good. Patients, carepartner, and caregivers stand by ready to partner, to⏎collaborate, and lead with our lived experiences and expertise. The question is not⏎whether we are prepared to lead alongside you, but whether the system is ready to⏎support and prioritize authentic partnership.⏎Respectfully,⏎Grace Cordovano⏎Grace Cordovano, PhD, BCPA⏎Board Certified Patient Advocate & Founder⏎Enlightening Results♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0278♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from SanLuca AI, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔SanLuca AI♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS AI RFI Response 2026⏎Date: February 13, 2026⏎Submitted by: Loretta Heinen, VP Business Development⏎Organization: SanLuca AI, Inc.⏎Contact Information: loretta@solarahealth.ai⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎RESPONSE:⏎● Regulatory ambiguity: AI utilization in care delivery is a new category - where does it fit in and⏎what rules apply? How do we address regulatory requirements without stunting innovation?⏎● Safety concerns & subsequent poor adoption: Establishing safeguards to govern AI interactions⏎and content in patient health contexts, with specific controls to prevent inappropriate diagnosis,⏎treatment recommendations, or other clinical misuse.⏎● Reimbursement barriers: Reimbursement has traditionally been tied to human interactions⏎throughout the care delivery process. Shifting certain patient interaction components to⏎technology or AI is not formally recognized, which can limit the ability to bill & be reimbursed⏎for services delivered through digital or hybrid models.⏎● Perceived risk of role displacement: The integration of technology into clinical workflows can⏎generate apprehension among clinicians when it overlaps with their scope of care, despite⏎persistent shortages in high-demand specialties like primary care and behavioral health.⏎● ROI considerations: While the technology may be cost-competitive, providers are evaluating it in⏎the context of broader concerns and its demonstrated ability to drive improved clinical outcomes⏎and lower total cost of care over time.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code⏎of Federal Regulations citations.⏎⏎RESPONSE:⏎Reduce data-sharing friction⏎● Clarify/standardize “safe” AI enablement under HIPAA via clearer implementation guidance on⏎permitted uses/disclosures and de-identification pathways:⏎○ HIPAA permitted uses/disclosures baseline: 45 CFR § 164.502⏎○ De-identification standard (safe harbor / expert determination): 45 CFR § 164.514⏎○ Security/technical safeguards expectations (critical for AI vendors as BAs): 45 CFR §⏎164.312⏎● Modernize operational guidance for Part 2 data (SUD) to enable safe, consented exchange for⏎whole-person AI support (where applicable): 42 CFR Part 2⏎Make interoperability real (so AI can work across settings)⏎● Strengthen enforcement + clarity around information blocking to improve access to EHI for care⏎and evaluation (with reasonable exceptions): 45 CFR Part 171 (definition and exceptions)⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist (e.g.,⏎liability, indemnification, privacy, security) and what role, if any, should HHS play to⏎help address them?⏎RESPONSE:⏎Liability ambiguity: “Who’s responsible” when AI advice contributes to harm - developer vs. clinician vs.⏎health system (especially with continuously learning systems). The need to clearly define where liability⏎of one participant in a care journey ends and the next begins will inform regulatory needs & requirements⏎for each participant.⏎Indemnification + contracting: Health systems increasingly demand strong indemnities, audit rights,⏎model-change notices, and incident response SLAs.⏎Privacy/security: Training, logging, and telemetry can expand PHI exposure; needs BAAs, least-privilege⏎access, and clear retention policies.⏎IP + data rights: Who owns derived features, fine-tuned weights, and performance telemetry?⏎HHS role: Provide model governance playbooks, standard contract clauses, and best-practice guidance for⏎PHI-minimizing architectures (without forcing everything into “device” pathways).⏎⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and workflow/human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If⏎so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, prize competitions)?⏎RESPONSE:⏎For artificial intelligence systems used in clinical care, particularly those intended to support or inform⏎clinical decision making, rigorous pre and post deployment evaluation is essential regardless of regulatory⏎classification. Evaluation should begin with clear articulation of intended use, target population, clinical⏎setting, and the specific decisions the system is designed to influence. It is also important to clearly define⏎the level of autonomy the system is intended to exercise, ranging from informational support to⏎conditional recommendation to more independent action within defined boundaries.⏎Pre deployment assessment should include retrospective validation across diverse and representative⏎datasets, external testing in independent health systems, and careful analysis of performance across⏎clinically relevant subgroups. In addition to measures of discrimination, evaluation should assess⏎calibration, clinically meaningful operating thresholds, and decision analytic impact. Prospective⏎evaluation methods such as silent deployments, pragmatic trials, or cluster based study designs can⏎provide insight into real world performance before widespread rollout.⏎Safety must be addressed through both technical safeguards and operational controls. From a technical⏎perspective, systems should undergo structured hazard analysis to identify foreseeable failure modes,⏎including incorrect recommendations, omissions, automation bias, hallucinated outputs, and performance⏎degradation under distribution shift or missing data. Where systems are designed to act with greater⏎independence, guardrails should include clear scope limitations, rule based constraints aligned with⏎clinical guidelines, confidence thresholds that trigger human review, and automatic fallbacks when inputs⏎fall outside validated conditions. From an operational perspective, organizations should establish real time⏎monitoring and escalation pathways⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎RESPONSE:⏎Establish voluntary, lightweight certification focused on: safety case, monitoring plan, transparency, and⏎interoperability readiness (not “one-size-fits-all”).⏎Fund/endorse independent test labs and publish minimum reporting standards (like “model cards” +⏎clinical workflow cards).⏎⏎Encourage industry-led credentialing for “AI safety officer / clinical AI steward” roles inside provider⏎orgs.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights⏎on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎RESPONSE:⏎Met/exceeded⏎● Imaging/screening triage + radiology support (strong ROI when it reduces second reads or speeds⏎throughput). Recent evidence includes AI-supported mammography screening trials showing⏎noninferiority/safety in population screening.⏎● Documentation burden reduction (“ambient scribe” category) showing measurable time savings⏎and clinician experience improvements in real-world/QI and trials.⏎Fallen short⏎● Predictive Analysis⏎○ Sepsis prediction/early warning in some settings due to poor external validity, alert⏎fatigue, and workflow mismatch (Epic model has published external validations showing⏎limitations).⏎Highest-potential novel tools⏎● Digital Behavioral Health Tools: Use AI to deliver safe, protocol-based care (e.g., Cognitive⏎Behavioral Therapy) at scale, expanding access and integrating behavioral health into chronic⏎disease management to address the mental health gaps that worsen outcomes.⏎● Automated Referral Coordination: Streamline referrals by automating intake, data routing,⏎scheduling, and returning consult notes to the ordering provider — including connections to⏎community resources. The biggest barrier is fragmented EMR integration across systems; solving⏎this would enable seamless, end-to-end care.⏎● Safety Analyzers: Use AI to review clinic conversations and notes to detect hidden medical risks⏎before they lead to ED visits.⏎7. Which roles, decision makers, or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to adoption?⏎⏎RESPONSE:⏎Influencers⏎● CMIO/CNIO/CTO, clinical department chairs, quality/value-based care leadership,⏎Primary hurdles⏎● Security review, EHR integration (SMART/FHIR + workflow), clinical governance approval,⏎safety protocols, and proving ROI under real constraints (staffing, bandwidth).⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎RESPONSE:⏎Data types: longitudinal meds, labs, problem lists, imaging metadata, referrals, scheduling,⏎utilization/claims, SDOH, and patient-reported outcomes.⏎Standards: Expand consistent use of FHIR resources + USCIS-aligned data quality, plus event-driven⏎APIs for near-real-time updates. Provide transition tools and methodologies for systems with more legacy⏎EHR implementations to FHIR standards. Explore usage of Model Context Protocol (MCP) as an optional⏎access method. Continue to incorporate human-in-the-loop safety monitoring and escalation frameworks.⏎Benchmarking: shared, de-identified evaluation corpora for (a) longitudinal prediction, (b)⏎summarization safety, (c) care-gap detection, with subgroup reporting and drift checks.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? What concerns do patients⏎and caregivers have related to adoption and use?⏎RESPONSE:⏎Wants addressed⏎⏎● Faster access, fewer delays, clearer care plans, reduced paperwork, and proactive outreach⏎(especially for chronic care).⏎Concerns⏎● Patient safety, Data privacy, bias/inequity, over-automation (loss of human judgment), job⏎displacement, and unclear recourse when AI is wrong.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their⏎use in clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎RESPONSE:⏎Priority research areas⏎● Pragmatic, multi-site clinical trials of AI-in-workflow (not just retrospective AUC).⏎● Bias + equity measurement standards tied to real outcomes.⏎● Efficacy of AI- vs human-delivered mental health care (cognitive behavioral therapy) and the⏎downstream impacts of integrated care via AI on clinical goals of chronic condition management⏎a. Published findings on impact⏎● Dr. Jessica Schleider has completed studies comparing virtually-delivered to human-delivered⏎single session interventions, showing that outcomes are similar in both groups. Research study is⏎currently being designed to extend this hypothesis exploring different treatment delivery⏎modalities to compare AI-delivered care to human delivered care specifically for single session.⏎● [other AI in care delivery studies]⏎b. Costs/benefits/transfers in the literature⏎● Benefits often show up first as time saved, throughput, and clinician experience, while financial⏎ROI can lag or shift across stakeholders (provider vs payer).♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0279♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AI in clinical care will support Observation Grouping to group clinical findings⏎⏎https://build.fhir.org/observation.html#obsgrouping⏎⏎grouping panels for clinical findings for laboratory, imaging, procedures, pathology, genomics is becoming a bigger need for clinicians to group and filter ⏎⏎which will help to reduce clinician burden and administrative burden⏎⏎the NLM, terminology standards development organizations (like SNOMED International and Regenstrief Institute) could be interested in this future effort⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0280♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Aidoc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Aidoc♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Mr. Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Section AI RFI⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dr. Mr. Keane,⏎Aidoc is grateful for the opportunity to respond to the Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎The need for clinical AI is immense. It was recently published that an estimated 900,000⏎diagnostic errors occur in the US annually, leading to nearly 400,000 deaths and serious⏎disablements.1 The need for AI further increases with the increasing shortage of clinicians,⏎significantly reducing access to care, especially in rural communities.⏎We believe clinical AI provides the largest step function in care delivery in recent history. Many of⏎those diagnostic errors, delays and access gaps are preventible, paving the way for the largest⏎public health benefit in decades.⏎At the same, we have the largest technological shift with clinical AI finally reaching diagnostic-⏎level accuracy. Prior clinical AI technologies were valuable yet narrow in scope, focusing on one⏎disease at a time. Only in the last few months foundation model-based AI advancements have⏎proven themselves in clinical settings, unlocking the ultimate potential impact of identifying every⏎disease throughout a patient’s scan, and doing so with very high accuracy, paving the way to the⏎transformative opportunity mentioned above. However, this is not without addressing and⏎mitigating its new risks, and establishing the required changes to regulatory framework.⏎As a pioneer in clinical AI, Aidoc has moved beyond the era of fragmented "point solutions" to⏎establish the industry’s most robust health system infrastructure for scalable, responsible AI⏎deployment. Our proprietary AI Operating System (aiOS™) serves as the critical clinical layer⏎bridging the gap between medical imaging and the Electronic Medical Record (EMR). By⏎orchestrating the flow of data and AI insights, we transform passive practices into proactive clinical⏎action across six major service lines (including neurology, cardiovascular, and oncology),⏎demonstrating that AI can augment the standard of care without disrupting existing workflows.⏎1 Newman-Toker DE; Just how many diagnostic errors and harms are out there, really? It depends on⏎how you count;BMJ Quality & Safety 2025;34:355-360.⏎⏎Aidoc’s commitment to clinical rigor and technical innovation is reflected in the clinical value we⏎deliver and the scale at which our solutions are deployed across both regulated and non-regulated⏎domains.⏎- Global Reach & Scale:⏎Aidoc is currently deployed across more than 250 health systems consisting of 1,600⏎hospitals in 44 U.S. states and 19 countries worldwide. This extensive footprint provides⏎us with direct insight into the diverse operational and clinical challenges faced by⏎healthcare providers across different systems and geographies.⏎- Vast Clinical Intelligence:⏎To date, aiOS™ has analyzed more than 100 million patient cases, leveraging the⏎largest portfolio of FDA-cleared AI devices in its category. This scale provides a unique⏎longitudinal perspective on how clinical AI can impact patient outcomes, workflow⏎efficiency, and population health management.⏎aiOS™ enables data normalization, continuous performance monitoring, and built-in⏎governance controls. It allows health systems to deploy and manage multi-condition⏎clinical AI solutions without requiring infrastructure re-architecture. With more than 100⏎million patient cases analyzed, aiOS™ is the most widely deployed clinical AI platform in⏎healthcare.⏎- Frontier Foundation Models:⏎Developed in collaboration with global AI industry leaders AWS and NVIDIA, CARE™⏎(Clinical AI Reasoning Engine) is Aidoc’s proprietary diagnostic foundation model. CARE⏎enables the rapid development of multi-output comprehensive medical devices with high⏎levels of accuracy, allowing for significant enhancement of both clinical utility and⏎operational efficiency.⏎In January 2026, Aidoc received FDA clearance for the first multi-triage application⏎powered by CARE, encompassing double-digit indications within a single abdominal CT⏎scan and covering numerous acute abdominopelvic imaging-detectable conditions. The⏎milestone followed a Breakthrough Device designation from the FDA, reflecting the clinical⏎relevance and the need for the device in U.S healthcare. This application represents the⏎first of many CARE-powered solutions currently under development at Aidoc.⏎The mission driving this technology is the mitigation of diagnostic harm. As mentioned above,⏎each year, an estimated 900,000 Americans are impacted by diagnostic errors resulting in death⏎or permanent disability and the vast majority occurring in hospital settings. Clinical AI has the⏎documented ability to help mitigate this harm. For example, studies show that AI-enabled⏎⏎technology can yield a 40% reduction in time to treatment2 and 2-day reduction in Intensive Care⏎Unit length of stay.3⏎We believe AI represents an evolutionary step-change in the practice of medicine. We welcome⏎the opportunity to partner with HHS to establish a progressive, scalable policy approach that⏎accelerates adoption while upholding the highest bar for patient safety and consumer trust. By⏎aligning regulation, reimbursement, and R&D investments, we can reduce the uncertainty that⏎impedes innovation and ensure AI is deployed to enhance productivity, reduce clinician burden,⏎and improve health outcomes for communities across America.⏎Aidoc’s response to HHS RFI⏎Aidoc’s experience demonstrates that AI has moved beyond fragmented “point solutions” to⏎become a critical infrastructure layer for modern medicine. To fully realize the clinical potential of⏎AI, we encourage HHS to embrace a forward-looking policy approach that supports patient safety,⏎diagnostic quality, and sustained innovation. Establishing clear standards for clinical AI⏎governance will help ensure reliable performance while enabling continued technological⏎advancement.⏎Accordingly, this response focuses on key insights derived from Aidoc’s large-scale deployment⏎experience and outlines a strategic roadmap centered on:⏎Regulatory and Standards Evolution⏎The FDA has demonstrated increasing openness to regulatory innovation. We recommend⏎continued adaptation of premarket pathways and the development of post-market standards⏎that reflect the unique characteristics and development pace of AI technologies. Such evolution⏎should support a safe, effective, and efficient quality framework for AI-enabled clinical solutions,⏎regardless of whether they are classified as medical or non-medical devices.⏎Payment Innovation⏎Establishing reimbursement pathways for AI-driven improvements in diagnostic quality could⏎help unlock broader clinical and population-level benefits. Innovative payment models may⏎support adoption of high-impact technologies that improve outcomes, enhance access, and⏎reduce downstream healthcare costs.⏎By aligning policy development with clinical implementation realities, HHS can help ensure that⏎technological innovation is deployed safely, effectively, and at scale for the benefit of both⏎clinicians and patients.⏎2 Chalian, et al. "Enhancing Pulmonary Embolism Management: The Impact of AI-Based Triage and⏎Prioritization on Diagnostic Timeliness and Treatment Initiation." RSNA Annual Meeting, 2024.⏎3Burch et al. Improving Patient Outcomes with an AI-Enhanced Pulmonary Embolism Response Team in⏎a Large Healthcare Network - 2024 PERT Consortium. Available at:https://pertconsortium.org/wp-⏎content/uploads/2024/09/Improving-Patient-Outcomes-with-an-AI-Enhanced-Pulmonary-Embolism-⏎Response-Team-in-a-Large-Healthcare-Network.pdf⏎⏎Please note that Aidoc chose to address questions where it believes it can provide meaningful⏎insight to HHS and did not respond to every question detailed in the RFI.⏎Responses to Individual Questions:⏎HHS question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation for AI⏎for healthcare and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Aidoc believes that the most significant barriers to private sector innovation in healthcare AI - and⏎to its responsible adoption in clinical care - are structural rather than technological. While AI⏎capabilities continue to advance rapidly, the surrounding healthcare ecosystem has not evolved⏎at the same pace. Reimbursement models, validation standards, implementation processes, and⏎workforce preparedness remain fragmented.⏎These structural gaps create uncertainty for developers, misaligned incentives for health systems,⏎friction during deployment, and inconsistent governance across the AI lifecycle. Collectively, they⏎slow innovation, limit investment, and delay patient access to clinically meaningful technologies.⏎The primary barriers fall into these interconnected categories:⏎● Potential regulatory opportunities and lifecycle governance gaps⏎● Misaligned reimbursement and financial incentives⏎● Fragmented evaluation and monitoring infrastructure⏎● Workforce readiness, trust, and liability concerns⏎Addressing these systemic challenges in a coordinated manner would accelerate responsible⏎innovation while strengthening patient safety, trust, equity, and long-term sustainability of AI-⏎enabled clinical care.⏎i. Potential Regulatory Opportunities and Lifecycle Governance Gaps⏎i.a Full AI Lifecycle Quality Governance⏎Innovation is constrained by the lack of harmonized federal governance across the⏎full AI Total Product Lifecycle (TPLC).⏎The current regulatory landscape is imbalanced: some AI tools are deployed with⏎minimal lifecycle oversight, while others face unclear or very robust requirements.⏎This creates market uncertainty—allowing some products to enter clinical use⏎without defined standards while potentially slowing innovation for others.⏎Aidoc believes foundational AI Quality Management System (QMS) principles⏎should apply across the AI lifecycle, including clear definition of intended use and⏎users, instructions for use, required user training, defined validation, post-⏎⏎deployment surveillance, change management, and performance transparency &⏎explainability.⏎Establishing baseline AI lifecycle governance expectations would reduce⏎uncertainty, clarify liability and shared accountability, and most important - support⏎patient safety, similar to foundational controls applied to low-risk medical devices⏎(such as class I devices, which require a QMS, but a premarket review by FDA is⏎not needed). We believe that unified basic standardization would support medical⏎and non medical devices, and will also help a unified federal standard motion,⏎allowing to reduce state legislation and fragmentation in this domain.⏎i.b Risk–Benefit Frameworks That Better Capture System-Level Clinical⏎Value⏎Current regulatory benefit assessments often focus primarily on diagnostic⏎performance metrics, particularly in novel regulatory pathways (De Novo⏎submissions). However, AI-enabled clinical tools frequently generate value⏎through workflow optimization and system-level impacts that can influence patient⏎outcomes.⏎These efficiency-related impacts are often treated as operational rather than⏎quality outcomes, limiting their influence on regulatory risk–benefit determinations.⏎A more comprehensive framework incorporating system-level clinical impact would⏎better reflect real-world AI value.⏎i.c Alignment of Validation Pathways With Clinical Risk and Evidence⏎Maturity⏎Validation requirements remain inconsistent across device categories. In⏎particular, they can impose disproportionate burdens on multi-indication and⏎multimodal AI systems that are essential for scalable clinical decision support.⏎We appreciate that FDA has increasingly adopted innovative regulatory⏎approaches, and we strongly support the continued use and expansion of such⏎pathways. Further alignment between validation rigor, clinical risk, technological⏎maturity, and real-world evidence generation would reduce unnecessary⏎development friction while preserving high standards of safety and effectiveness.⏎ii. Misaligned Reimbursement and Financial Incentives⏎Current payment policies do not adequately incentivize improvements in front-end⏎diagnostic quality and preventive care.⏎The absence of clear reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled detection, triage, and care⏎coordination technologies limits health system investment in solutions that can prevent⏎⏎high-harm diagnostic errors, reduce downstream treatment costs, and support health⏎equity.⏎Without aligned financial incentives, adoption decisions are often driven by short-term⏎operational return on investment rather than long-term system value.⏎This misalignment slows deployment of technologies that may generate substantial⏎population-level clinical and economic benefits but do not fit neatly within existing⏎reimbursement structures.⏎iii. Fragmented Evaluation and Continuous Monitoring Infrastructure⏎There remains fragmentation between initial regulatory clearance (when applicable, for⏎medical devices) and sustained real-world performance evaluation.⏎Developers and healthcare organizations lack standardized and widely adopted⏎frameworks for holistic AI evaluation that extend beyond traditional performance metrics.⏎Meaningful assessment should include:⏎● Patient outcome impact⏎● Workflow integration and operational effectiveness⏎● Longitudinal performance stability⏎● Detection and mitigation of model drift⏎● Equity and population health considerations⏎Fragmented evaluation structures lead to duplicated effort, slow knowledge sharing, and⏎reduced collective confidence in AI-enabled clinical systems.⏎A coordinated post-market evidence generation and monitoring infrastructure, which can⏎be established as part of an AI Total Product Lifecycle (TPLC) framework as mentioned⏎in item i.a above, would promote transparency, accelerate learning across healthcare⏎systems, and support continuous safety and effectiveness assurance.⏎v. Workforce Preparedness, Trust, and Liability Considerations⏎Workforce readiness is a critical component of safe AI adoption.⏎Most practicing clinicians receive limited formal training in AI literacy, which may reduce⏎confidence in interpreting AI outputs and exercising appropriate clinical oversight, in⏎addition to increasing the likelihood of usage-related risks, first and foremost, the risk of⏎automation bias.⏎Expanding AI literacy within clinical education and professional development programs⏎would support safer, more effective integration of AI technologies into clinical workflows.⏎⏎Liability and accountability uncertainty further constrain adoption. Healthcare systems⏎often perceive regulatory and legal risk during early deployment phases, particularly when⏎governance boundaries between developer, institution, and clinician are not clearly⏎defined.⏎Clarified accountability frameworks and potential safe harbor mechanisms could reduce⏎adoption hesitation, especially for smaller or resource-constrained institutions such as⏎rural healthcare providers.⏎HHS question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design⏎changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care⏎and why?⏎We believe that HHS has a tremendous opportunity to adapt regulatory and payment policies to⏎meaningfully accelerate the impact of AI on clinical care.⏎i. Regulatory Policy:⏎ii.a Establish Harmonized AI Lifecycle Governance Across Clinical Settings⏎A central barrier to effective AI adoption is the lack of federally harmonized governance across⏎the full AI TPLC. Importantly, this gap does not operate in isolation - it directly contributes to⏎fragmentation in evaluation infrastructure and to uncertainty experienced by the clinical workforce.⏎HHS should prioritize establishing baseline lifecycle governance expectations for clinical AI tools,⏎regardless of whether they qualify as medical devices or undergo premarket review. Foundational⏎Quality Management System (QMS) expectations should include:⏎● Clear definition of intended use and intended users⏎● Transparent and actionable instructions for use⏎● Adequate clinical validation and performance characterization⏎● Post-deployment surveillance and safety monitoring⏎● Structured change management for model updates⏎● Appropriate explainability and transparency to end users⏎Without consistent baseline governance, safety and quality uncertainty increases, and interpretive⏎burden is shifted to healthcare institutions. In practice, health systems are forced to build ad hoc⏎oversight, monitoring, and validation structures—often duplicating efforts and applying⏎inconsistent standards. Standardized lifecycle expectations would clarify shared accountability⏎between developers and healthcare systems, reduce redundancy, and create the structural⏎foundation upon which coordinated evaluation systems and workforce confidence can be built.⏎⏎We would like to further zoom in on two interrelated aspects that are inherent components of a⏎comprehensive TPLC framework: (1) fragmented evaluation and continuous monitoring⏎infrastructure, and (2) workforce preparedness, trust, and liability considerations. These are not⏎separate challenges, but downstream manifestations of incomplete lifecycle governance.⏎Addressing them explicitly within the TPLC structure would significantly strengthen safe and⏎scalable AI adoption.⏎Fragmented Evaluation and Continuous Monitoring Infrastructure⏎The absence of harmonized lifecycle governance contributes directly to fragmentation between⏎initial regulatory clearance (when applicable for medical devices) and sustained real-world⏎performance evaluation.⏎Where baseline expectations for post-deployment monitoring, performance reassessment, and⏎change management are not clearly defined at the federal level, developers and healthcare⏎organizations lack standardized and widely adopted frameworks for holistic AI evaluation. As a⏎result, evaluation efforts often remain siloed—focused narrowly on premarket performance⏎metrics rather than continuous, real-world effectiveness.⏎Meaningful assessment should include:⏎● Patient outcome impact⏎● Workflow integration and operational effectiveness⏎● Longitudinal performance stability⏎● Detection and mitigation of model drift⏎● Equity and population health considerations⏎Fragmented evaluation structures lead to duplicated efforts, slower knowledge sharing, and⏎reduced collective confidence in AI-enabled clinical systems. Critically, they also weaken the⏎feedback loop that should connect post-market evidence generation back into lifecycle⏎governance and model improvement.⏎A coordinated post-market evidence generation and monitoring infrastructure—established as⏎part of a comprehensive AI TPLC framework—would operationalize the governance principles⏎described above. By embedding structured surveillance, shared data standards, and continuous⏎learning mechanisms into the lifecycle itself, such an infrastructure would promote transparency,⏎accelerate cross-system learning, and support ongoing safety and effectiveness assurance.⏎Workforce Preparedness, Trust, and Liability Considerations⏎Lifecycle governance and monitoring fragmentation also have direct implications for clinician trust⏎and liability perception.⏎When governance expectations are unclear and post-market monitoring is inconsistent, clinicians⏎are left uncertain about the reliability, scope, and limitations of AI tools deployed in their workflows.⏎⏎This uncertainty compounds existing gaps in AI literacy and increases the risk of inappropriate⏎reliance or automation bias.⏎Workforce readiness is therefore inseparable from lifecycle governance. Clear intended use⏎definitions, transparent validation standards, explainability requirements, and structured change⏎management processes all support clinicians in understanding when and how AI outputs should⏎inform decision-making.⏎At present, most clinicians receive limited formal training in AI literacy, which may reduce⏎confidence in interpreting AI outputs and exercising appropriate oversight. Expanding AI literacy⏎within undergraduate medical education, residency programs, and continuing professional⏎development initiatives would strengthen safe integration—but these efforts must be supported⏎by consistent governance signals and reliable monitoring infrastructure.⏎Liability and accountability uncertainty further constrain adoption. Healthcare systems often⏎perceive heightened regulatory and legal risk during early deployment phases, particularly when⏎governance boundaries between developer, institution, and clinician are not clearly defined. In⏎the absence of harmonized lifecycle standards, responsibility is diffused and often shifted⏎downstream.⏎Clarified accountability frameworks tied to adherence to defined lifecycle governance standards—⏎would reduce hesitation and align incentives. Such clarity is especially important for smaller or⏎resource-constrained institutions, including rural healthcare providers, which may lack the⏎capacity to independently construct robust AI governance ecosystems⏎ii.b Modernize Risk–Benefit Assessment Frameworks⏎Current regulatory benefit frameworks often focus narrowly on diagnostic accuracy metrics,⏎particularly in novel pathways such as De Novo classifications. However, AI-enabled clinical tools⏎frequently generate value through workflow optimization and system-level improvements that⏎influence patient outcomes.⏎For example, AI that accelerates identification and discharge of low-risk patients in crowded⏎emergency departments can reduce bed shortages, improve triage efficiency, and enable faster⏎care for critically ill patients. These impacts materially influence morbidity and mortality but are⏎often categorized as “efficiency” rather than “quality,” limiting their regulatory weight in risk–benefit⏎determinations.⏎HHS should encourage regulatory approaches that:⏎● Recognize workflow acceleration and throughput improvements as clinical quality⏎drivers⏎● Incorporate system-level outcomes into benefit assessment⏎● Evaluate downstream impact on time-to-treatment, diagnostic delay, and⏎resource allocation⏎⏎A broader risk–benefit framework would better reflect real-world clinical value and reduce⏎disincentives for meaningful innovation.⏎ii.c Align Validation Pathways With Clinical Risk and Real-World Evidence⏎It is evident the FDA has been working tirelessly to innovate safe and effective methods of product⏎evaluation. We’d like to encourage that continued motion. Certain radiology software products⏎continue to rely heavily on pre market studies. While we believe in the importance of robust pre⏎market assessment, there are ways to balance the load better between pre and post market,⏎relying more heavily on real world evidence.⏎These requirements can disproportionately burden multi-indication and multimodal AI systems⏎that are essential for scalable clinical decision support.⏎HHS should prioritize:⏎● Expanded acceptance of real-world evidence in validation⏎● Alignment of evidentiary rigor with clinical risk and technological maturity⏎● Flexible validation pathways for multi-indication and adaptive AI systems⏎Reducing unnecessary development friction—while preserving high safety standards—would⏎accelerate responsible deployment of clinically meaningful AI technologies.⏎ii. Payment Policy:⏎CMS has taken important steps to create progressive reimbursement programs that can spur⏎technological innovation. The current ACCESS and TEMPO are key starting point initiatives that⏎may meaningfully catalyze collaboration between health systems and industry technology⏎partners to address care gaps and accelerate innovation. However, the current programs are⏎focused primarily on chronic conditions and ambulatory care.⏎CMS has an opportunity to extend this impact further to address the crisis of diagnostic error and⏎substantially mitigate serious harm. Today, an estimated 900,000 serious diagnostic errors occur⏎in the US annually, leading to nearly 400,000 deaths and serious disablements.4 These errors are⏎largely preventable, with nearly 90% of them occurring in a hospital setting (inpatient or ED). By⏎creating reimbursement pathways based on improving measurable front-end diagnostic quality⏎for high-harm conditions, CMS can elevate Diagnostic Harm to become a policy priority and⏎meaningfully reduce preventable harm related to diagnostic errors.⏎Current reimbursement pathways are slow to evolve, narrowly scoped compared to the⏎foundational infrastructure AI represents, and often poorly suited to AI-enabled care, particularly⏎4 Newman-Toker DE; Just how many diagnostic errors and harms are out there, really? It depends on⏎how you count;BMJ Quality & Safety 2025;34:355-360.⏎⏎when payment models require a direct physician work component rather than recognizing⏎automated diagnostic and coordination value.⏎One could argue that certain AI-enabled activities can be financed under existing fee-for-service⏎mechanisms. However, relying on fee-for-service alone creates structural barriers to adoption and⏎limits the public health impact of AI:⏎1. It weakens incentives to invest in earlier detection interventions where the return accrues⏎over years, such as cardiovascular disease or cancer, because the entity funding the⏎intervention is often not the entity capturing downstream savings.⏎2. This dynamic disproportionately disadvantages community hospitals that care for higher⏎proportions of Medicare and Medicaid beneficiaries and operate on thinner margins, even⏎though these institutions could realize outsized benefit from improved diagnostic quality⏎and proactive care pathways.⏎3. Fee-for-service does not consistently reward solutions that reduce total cost of care, which⏎reduces incentives for both developers and health systems to prioritize AI that lowers⏎avoidable utilization, prevents complications, and improves longitudinal outcomes.⏎As an example, heart disease is the leading cause of death in the United States, yet proven tools⏎to support population screening - such as coronary artery calcium (CAC) scoring - remain⏎underused due largely to reimbursement gaps. Although nearly half of U.S. adults have⏎measurable coronary calcium and elevated CAC scores increase coronary event risk sevenfold,⏎most insurers do not routinely cover CAC analysis. As a result, access is limited and follow-up is⏎inconsistent, with approximately 30% of moderate-to-severe cases receiving no preventive⏎therapy. This creates a significant missed opportunity for early, equitable intervention before⏎costly and life-threatening cardiac events occur.⏎AI-enabled CAC analysis fundamentally changes the reimbursement equation by leveraging⏎imaging that is already being performed. Rather than requiring new tests, AI can retrospectively⏎and prospectively analyze existing chest CT scans - performed on millions of Americans each⏎year - to identify incidental coronary calcium at scale. When paired with AI-driven care⏎management workflows that ensure appropriate follow-up, this approach transforms CAC⏎screening from an underutilized diagnostic tool into a scalable preventive care pathway. Evidence⏎shows opportunistic screening significantly increases statin initiation, demonstrating clear⏎downstream clinical action and measurable value.⏎For CMS and other payers, reimbursing AI-based CAC analysis and management represents a⏎high-impact, infrastructure-light investment aligned with value-based care and health equity goals.⏎The cost of inaction is substantial - missed incidental CAC in the Medicare chest CT population⏎alone represents billions of dollars in first-year medical costs and lost life-years. By establishing⏎reimbursement pathways for AI-driven CAC analysis and follow-up management, for example,⏎CMS can incentivize health systems to close a major preventive cardiology gap, reduce avoidable⏎hospitalizations, and shift cardiovascular spending from emergency response to proactive risk⏎reduction.⏎⏎The CAC example provided, is just an example, intended to illustrate that traditional code-based⏎reimbursement may not fully address these needs, but thoughtful innovation in payment design⏎could unlock adoption at national scale and deliver population-level impact unmatched by any⏎prior diagnostic reform.⏎Coupled with this opportunity, we recognize the complexity of modernizing payment policy. We⏎propose two potential paths forward:⏎First, CMS could modernize and expand existing payment pathways for emerging technologies,⏎particularly in hospital-based and emergency care settings where AI can meaningfully reduce⏎diagnostic delay and prevent harm. CMS could consider establishing pathways that allow certain⏎AI technologies to be recognized as distinct, separately payable services, rather than being⏎bundled into existing procedure or imaging codes or tied to physician work. Many AI systems⏎perform clinically meaningful, resource-intensive functions, such as independent data analysis,⏎risk stratification, and clinical decision support, that are not adequately reflected in current bundled⏎payments. Creating mechanisms to unbundle and appropriately value these technological⏎services would help ensure payment accurately reflects the resources involved, support sustained⏎innovation, and promote access to technologies that improve care quality, efficiency, and⏎outcomes.⏎Second, as an alternative to purely code-based reimbursement, CMS should consider⏎establishing a dedicated Diagnostic Quality Improvement payment pathway, potentially through⏎CMMI demonstration authority, that rewards measurable reductions in diagnostic delay,⏎preventable harm, and avoidable downstream utilization for high-impact conditions. Such a model⏎could be structured around performance-based payments tied to validated outcome metrics,⏎enabling health systems to adopt AI-enabled infrastructure that demonstrably improves front-end⏎diagnostic accuracy and care coordination. Thoughtful innovation in payment design would unlock⏎adoption at national scale and deliver population-level impact unmatched by prior diagnostic⏎reforms.⏎HHS question 3: For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist, and what role should HHS play?⏎The use of AI in clinical care outside traditional medical device regulatory pathways introduces⏎novel legal, governance, and implementation challenges. These challenges primarily stem from⏎structural gaps in accountability architecture rather than from the intrinsic technological risk of AI⏎itself.⏎As clinical AI systems increasingly influence clinical workflows and decision-making processes,⏎policy focus should shift toward establishing baseline trust, safety, and responsibility frameworks⏎while preserving flexibility for innovation. In particular, clarifying governance expectations across⏎the AI lifecycle would help address many of the uncertainties associated with AI tools that fall⏎outside conventional medical device regulation.⏎⏎We believe that if the recommendation outlined in Section II.A—establishing harmonized AI⏎lifecycle governance across clinical settings—is implemented, many of the legal and⏎implementation challenges associated with non-medical-device clinical AI applications could be⏎substantially mitigated. A consistent lifecycle governance framework would help define⏎accountability boundaries, support transparent validation and monitoring practices, and reduce⏎ambiguity regarding the roles of developers, healthcare institutions, and clinicians.⏎Accordingly, HHS can play a critical role in advancing baseline governance standards, promoting⏎workforce readiness, and facilitating shared evidence and monitoring infrastructures that support⏎safe and responsible deployment of clinical AI across regulatory categories.⏎HHS question 5: How can HHS best support private-sector activities (e.g.,⏎accreditation, certification, credentialing) to promote AI use?⏎We believe that thoughtful evolution of payment and reimbursement design could play a critical⏎role in enabling the national-scale adoption of clinical AI and supporting population-level clinical⏎and operational impact that cannot be achieved through technology advancement alone. Aligning⏎economic incentives with demonstrated clinical value and real-world performance may help⏎facilitate the responsible integration of AI into clinical care pathways.⏎In parallel, we believe that greater harmonization and standardization across the TPLC for clinical⏎AI developers could strengthen confidence in AI-enabled systems, thereby supporting broader⏎private-sector adoption and clinical reliance on validated technologies. Both of these dimensions⏎are further discussed in our response to Question 2 above.⏎HHS question 6: Where has AI in clinical care met or exceeded expectations, and⏎where has it fallen short? What novel tools hold the most promise?⏎AI tools in clinical care have most consistently met or exceeded performance and expectations in⏎well-defined, workflow-integrated use cases, particularly imaging triage, early detection of high-⏎risk conditions, and care coordination, where they have improved timeliness of diagnosis and⏎⏎treatment5,6,7, reduced ED8, inpatient9,10, and ICU length of stay11,12 and improved follow-up and⏎intervention rates.13,14⏎Beyond the published literature, adoption trends reinforce this progress. On the Aidoc platform⏎alone, the average number of use cases deployed per health system has grown from roughly⏎three just a few years ago to more than a dozen today. Clinicians and administrators are⏎expanding deployment because they are seeing tangible clinical and operational value.⏎By contrast, AI has fallen short when there is misalignment between an algorithm’s intended use⏎and real world clinical workflows, or when financial incentives do not support the required changes⏎in practice. Even strong technical performance does not translate into clinical impact if ground⏎truth definitions are inconsistent, integration is weak, or adoption is superficial. A clear example⏎is incidental findings. As discussed in the payment reform section, many health systems choose⏎not to implement the workflow changes required to act on these findings because the financial⏎incentives are unclear or misaligned.⏎Adoption is also uneven. Community based systems, particularly those operating primarily in fee⏎for service environments, often struggle to identify sustainable payment pathways. As a result,⏎the benefits of AI are not being distributed uniformly across the healthcare system.⏎As a specific example: as AI technology continues to make rapid advancement, more⏎comprehensive detection becomes possible at scale. Broad adoption of, for example,⏎comprehensive imaging AI, would enable significant improvements to diagnostic speed and⏎accuracy, particularly in acute care settings.⏎5 Radiology AI - Wiklund, Peder, and Koshiar Medson. "Use of a Deep Learning Algorithm for Detection⏎and Triage of Cancer-associated Incidental Pulmonary Embolism." Proceedings of the Radiological⏎Society of North America (RSNA) 2023 Annual Meeting, 18 Oct. 2023,⏎https://doi.org/10.1148/ryai.220286.⏎6 Chalian, et al. "Enhancing Pulmonary Embolism Management: The Impact of AI-Based Triage and⏎Prioritization on Diagnostic Timeliness and Treatment Initiation." RSNA Annual Meeting, 2024.⏎7 Chokhawala, H., H. Cuellar, R. Shah, and A. Savardekar. "Enhancing Stroke Care Efficiency through AI⏎Vendor Transition: A Comparative Study of Workflow Metrics Before and After Implementation." SNIS⏎Conference, 2024, LSUHS, Shreveport, LA. Abstract presentation.⏎8 Davis MA, Rao B, Cedeno PA, Saha A, Zohrabian VM. Machine Learning and Improved Quality Metrics⏎in Acute Intracranial Hemorrhage by Noncontrast Computed Tomography. Curr Probl Diagn Radiol.⏎2022;51(4):556-561. doi:10.1067/j.cpradiol.2020.10.007⏎9 Petry, Michael et al. “Decreased Hospital Length of Stay for ICH and PE after Adoption of an Artificial⏎Intelligence-Augmented Radiological Worklist Triage System.” Radiology research and practice vol. 2022⏎2141839. 18 Aug. 2022, doi:10.1155/2022/2141839⏎10 Davis, Melissa A., et al. "Machine Learning and Improved Quality Metrics in Acute Intracranial⏎Hemorrhage by Noncontrast Computed Tomography." Current Problems in Diagnostic Radiology, vol. 51,⏎no. 4, July–August 2022, pp. 556-561, Elsevier, https://doi.org/10.1067/j.cpradiol.2020.10.007.⏎11 Improving Patient Outcomes with an AI-Enhanced Pulmonary Embolism Response Team in a Large⏎Healthcare Network - Burch et al. 2024 PERT Consortium⏎12 Gupta, Kavish, MD, et al. "Mechanical Thrombectomy, Artificial Intelligence and the Activation of a⏎Pulmonary Embolism Response Team." PERT Consortium 2023, abstract presentation.⏎13 Kostiuk, Valentyna, et al. "Artificial Intelligence Improves Initial Evaluation and Follow-Up of Patients⏎with AAA." Abstract, Vascular Annual Meeting, 2024. Yale School of Medicine, New Haven, CT.⏎14 Randazzo, Michael, et al. "Hospital-Wide Integration of a Natural Language Processing Algorithm to⏎Detect Inferior Vena Cava Filters in Imaging Reports and Improve Device Removal Rates." ACC⏎Abstracts, 2024.⏎⏎Looking ahead, AI tools with the greatest potential are those that support proactive and⏎longitudinal care management, including timely, accurate identification of high-risk patients;⏎ensuring appropriate follow-up; improving care coordination; and enabling earlier intervention to⏎improve outcomes and reduce avoidable costs.⏎HHS question 7: Which decision makers drive AI adoption within healthcare⏎organizations, and what are the administrative hurdles?⏎Aidoc’s experience shows that when a broad enough portfolio provides real clinical value, AI⏎adoption can shift from departmental "point solutions" to enterprise-wide strategy.⏎Such effective adoption requires a synchronized triple sponsorship model and a governance⏎structure that treats AI as a high powered always-on-assistant rather than just software.⏎i. Influential Roles and Decision-Makers⏎The adoption of clinical AI is driven by a multi-disciplinary trifecta of leadership that ensures the⏎technology is sharpening clinical judgment rather than replacing it:⏎● Triple Sponsorship: Successful integration requires three distinct sponsors:⏎○ Executive Sponsor: Owns the enterprise impact and provides the authority to⏎allocate resources and remove obstacles.⏎○ Clinical Sponsor: A physician champion (e.g., CMO, CMIO or Service Line Leader)⏎who provides direction on workflows and ensures the tool is designed for the "joy⏎of practice" and improvement of patient care.⏎○ IT/Technical Partner: Anticipates integration needs and owns the long-term⏎reliability and security of the system.⏎● Clinical Champions : These front-line clinicians are essential for generating excitement⏎and surfacing early challenges. They act as microphones to build conviction across the⏎medical staff.⏎● Enterprise AI Governance Committees: Many health systems are formalizing AI Councils⏎that include legal, ethics, and clinical stakeholders. Their role is to be the Chief Ethical⏎Officers of the organization's data, treating algorithmic bias with the same urgency as a⏎hospital-acquired infection.⏎ii. Primary Administrative Hurdles⏎It has been our experience and observation that even with strong clinical support, several⏎administrative bottlenecks frequently stall implementation:⏎● The Integration Gap & Change Fatigue: The greatest hurdle is dropping a technically⏎sound solution into a poorly understood clinical reality. If AI creates more noise or cognitive⏎load, adoption fails. Success requires mapping the end-to-end patient journey to ensure⏎the AI becomes an invisible collaborator.⏎● Vendor Credentialing Fatigue: Redundant security and compliance reviews currently take⏎⏎6–12 months per facility. Aidoc addresses this through models like our Implementation⏎Palooza, an on-site working session that brings all stakeholders (clinical, IT, and vendors)⏎into one room to troubleshoot technical roadblocks in real-time.⏎● Governance Wait Times: Traditional governance often slows down routine changes.⏎Effective governance must be agile and enable speed with control and utilizing technology⏎for continuous monitoring so oversight is an ongoing practice rather than a one-time⏎approval.⏎● Lack of ROI framework: In today’s financial environment, AI adoption requires a clear⏎return on investment. The challenge is that clinical AI often delivers value downstream⏎and across silos. Earlier detection can reduce ICU length of stay, prevent complications,⏎and decompress the ED. These are enterprise level gains, but they rarely sit neatly within⏎one department’s budget. Lastly, oftentimes incentives are insufficient for improving longer⏎term care.⏎● Health systems that evaluate AI only on short term billing lift risk undervaluing it. The right⏎lens is enterprise ROI, including reduced length of stay, avoided adverse events, improved⏎throughput, performance under value based contracts, and workforce stability.⏎● Measurement of the Human Dividend: Organizations often struggle to move beyond⏎traditional ROI (like billing efficiency) to measure the human dividend such as the⏎reduction in burnout, concerns about what urgent findings may be waiting in a queue of⏎unread images, or the return of joy to clinical practice.⏎By supporting standardized certification and interoperable monitoring infrastructure, HHS can⏎help reduce these administrative frictions. This allows healthcare leaders to focus on the⏎clinical AI renaissance and reclaim the art of medicine by letting AI handle the science of data⏎while humans focus on the connection with the patient.⏎HHS question 9: What challenges do patients and caregivers want AI to address,⏎and what concerns do they hold?⏎Leveraging AI to improve diagnostic quality is a key focus of patients and caregivers. Diagnostic⏎error harm represents a significant but often under-recognized challenge in American healthcare.⏎Imaging AI can play a critical role in diagnostic workflows within emergency and inpatient settings⏎and is central to the diagnosis of several high-impact conditions that contribute substantially to⏎national error rates, including stroke, pneumonia, pulmonary embolism/deep vein thrombosis, and⏎lung cancer. Research suggests that many inpatient diagnostic errors are preventable, with nearly⏎85% of identified adverse outcomes potentially avoidable. Strengthening hospital-based⏎diagnostic workflows therefore represents a high-leverage opportunity to address the broader⏎diagnostic safety challenge.⏎Beyond safety, AI also offers an unprecedented opportunity to improve access to high-quality⏎care across diverse and underserved populations. Clinical AI systems can be deployed across⏎care environments- from large integrated delivery networks to remote rural hospitals. At a time⏎when healthcare systems are under pressure to expand access, AI can help level disparities by⏎⏎enabling more consistent delivery of high-quality clinical decision support regardless of⏎geographic or institutional setting.⏎Federal initiatives focused on rural health transformation present an important opportunity to align⏎policy with emerging evidence. Care delays are not inevitable; coordinated policy, technology⏎deployment, and infrastructure development can reduce preventable harm and improve⏎outcomes. Ultimately, while a patient’s ZIP code may determine which hospital they enter, it⏎should not determine the sophistication of the technology used to support life-saving clinical⏎decisions.⏎Nevertheless, concerns regarding AI adoption remain, primarily around quality, effectiveness,and⏎safety. We believe these concerns can be meaningfully addressed through the establishment of⏎harmonized AI TPLC governance frameworks, as discussed in response to Question 2.⏎Another important consideration is cost. For solutions that address core pain points in the U.S.⏎healthcare system, innovative and flexible payment and reimbursement models may play a key⏎role in supporting sustainable adoption and scaling of beneficial AI technologies. While many⏎clinical AI tools have the potential to reduce total cost of care in aggregate—by preventing⏎downstream complications, shortening length of stay, and reducing avoidable utilization—their⏎value is often realized across different stakeholders than those responsible for initial procurement⏎and implementation. Federal leadership can help bridge this misalignment by advancing payment⏎models that recognize the longitudinal value of diagnostic accuracy, including transitional⏎reimbursement pathways, value-based incentives tied to measurable patient outcomes, and⏎mechanisms that support adoption in resource-constrained hospitals. Aligning reimbursement⏎with demonstrated clinical and economic impact would accelerate responsible AI deployment,⏎ensure equitable access across care settings, and enable sustained innovation while maintaining⏎fiscal stewardship.⏎HHS question 10: Are there AI research areas HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎adoption?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in⏎clinical care?⏎There are over 230+ peer-reviewed publications and scientific conference presentations⏎from health systems evaluating the real-world impact of adopted Aidoc solutions in clinical⏎care.⏎These studies demonstrate how AI can be integrated into radiology triage, care⏎coordination, and patient management workflows in order to reduce time to diagnosis and⏎treatment, decrease emergency department, inpatient, and ICU length of stay, improve⏎awareness of clinically significant findings, increase appropriate and guideline-directed⏎interventions, and reduce patient leakage and gaps in follow-up care.⏎While these findings are encouraging, important gaps remain in measuring AI’s impact on⏎diagnostic quality and patient outcomes. HHS could prioritize research that evaluates how⏎⏎AI affects diagnostic accuracy, timeliness, and clinical outcomes in real-world care.⏎Although AI has the potential to usher in a new era of diagnostic quality, standardized⏎metrics and validated frameworks to quantify these improvements are still lacking. Federal⏎support for developing these measures will be critical to demonstrating clinical value,⏎building trust, and accelerating responsible adoption. A representative selection of⏎published studies is included as an Appendix.⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎The literature generally evaluates the costs and benefits associated with clinical AI using⏎budget impact models using retrospective economic analyses or interventional (pre/post)⏎studies.⏎These approaches typically assess financial impact at the patient encounter level and⏎across downstream services, rather than over lifetime horizons. This reflects the relatively⏎early stage of adoption and the limited availability of long-term outcomes and cost data.⏎Most studies therefore focus on near-term operational and financial effects, such as⏎changes in length of stay, procedural utilization, and follow-up adherence, which can be⏎directly linked to hospital costs and revenues.⏎Sincerely,⏎Elad Walach, MS⏎CEO, Aidoc⏎Aswin Chandrakantan, MD⏎President & Chief Commercial Officer, Aidoc⏎Amalia Schreier, LL.B., LLM⏎SVP Regulatory Affairs and Legal, Aidoc⏎Jesse M Ehrenfeld, MD MPH⏎Global Chief Medical Officer, Aidoc⏎⏎Appendix: Supporting Literature in response to 10a⏎Service Citation⏎Line Module Metric Value Unit url Citation⏎IVC (Inferior Increased ACC2024 - 