n Thrombosis: Data and Management. Semin Intervent Radiol. 2016 Jun;33(2):101-4.⏎VTE Filter) Readmissions 62 % url doi: 10.1055/s-0036-1581087. PMID: 27247478; PMCID: PMC4862844.⏎PERT⏎(Pulmonary Noman, Anas et al. “Episode Care Costs Following Catheter-Directed Reperfusion⏎Embolism Therapies for Pulmonary Embolism: A Literature-Based Comparative Cohort Analysis.” The⏎Response Reduced American journal of cardiology vol. 225 (2024): 178-189.⏎VTE Team) Readmissions 41 % url doi:10.1016/j.amjcard.2024.06.002♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0281♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from The Interstitial Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔The Interstitial Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔1⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Public Comment Submission⏎Submitted by: Walt Simmons, The Interstitial Institute⏎Docket: HHS RFI - Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care" (90 FR 60108, December 23, 2025)⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The most consequential barrier to AI adoption in clinical care is not regulatory burden,⏎reimbursement lag, or technical immaturity. It is the absence of governance infrastructure⏎that would make adoption safe to accelerate.⏎Private sector AI innovators face a structural paradox: the clinical environments they are⏎selling into cannot answer three foundational questions about the AI systems already⏎operating within them. Who is specifically responsible for a clinical AI system's outputs —⏎not who is aware of it, but whose name is attached to the outcome? How do we know if it is⏎working — is there a metric, attached to a threshold, that someone can point to? And what⏎triggers when it isn't — when that metric crosses a threshold, what is structurally required⏎to happen?⏎The evidence for the consequences of this absence is specific. In a 2025 survey, the⏎American Medical Association found that 61% of physicians reported AI is increasing prior⏎authorization denials, 82% reported patients abandoning treatment as a result of AI-driven⏎prior authorization, and 94% reported negative clinical outcomes associated with AI-⏎mediated decision-making.¹ These are not speculative risks. They are outcomes already⏎occurring in clinical environments where AI systems operate between physician and⏎patient without accountability architecture connecting outputs to responsible parties.⏎The barrier extends beyond clinical settings into institutional procurement. Across⏎industries, 75% of organizations have adopted AI usage policies, but only 36% have⏎implemented formal governance frameworks, and only 48% actively monitor production AI⏎systems for accuracy, drift, or misuse — a figure that drops to 9% among smaller⏎organizations.² Only 24% of organizations can control AI agent actions with guardrails and⏎live monitoring.³ These figures are directional — drawn from surveyed respondents across⏎industries — and are corroborated by higher-authority sources: GAO's federal AI audits,⏎Cisco's readiness index, and CSA/Google's governance maturity correlation data all point⏎to the same structural gap. Where these conditions obtain in health systems, adoption⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎2⏎stalls — not from resistance, but from the entirely rational recognition by procurement,⏎legal, and clinical leadership that deploying AI into a space where accountability,⏎measurement, and consequence are absent creates liability without governance.⏎The result is a market failure of a specific kind: AI vendors capable of delivering genuine⏎clinical value cannot cross the procurement threshold because the institutional buyer⏎lacks the governance architecture to accept accountability for what the system does. This⏎is not fixed by reducing the regulatory burden on vendors. It is fixed by helping clinical⏎institutions build the accountability infrastructure that makes them capable of adopting AI⏎responsibly. Evidence from the enterprise sector supports this: organizations with⏎comprehensive AI governance were nearly twice as likely to report successful early⏎adoption of agentic AI (46%) compared to organizations with governance still in⏎development (12%).⁴ This pattern holds across clinical AI categories — from EHR-⏎integrated clinical decision support and in-ICU prediction tools to prior authorization⏎systems — wherever accountability architecture is absent at deployment.⏎¹ American Medical Association, "How AI Is Leading to More Prior Authorization Denials,"⏎2025. https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/prior-authorization/how-ai-⏎leading-more-prior-authorization-denials⏎² Pacific AI / Gradient Flow, "2025 AI Governance Survey," December 2025.⏎³ Cisco AI Readiness Index, 2025.⏎⁴ Cloud Security Alliance / Google Cloud, "State of AI Security and Governance," December⏎2025.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize?⏎HHS should prioritize one structural change above all others: reorienting its regulatory and⏎reimbursement instruments from behavioral requirements to architectural requirements.⏎Current frameworks ask behavioral questions — does the AI system meet a performance⏎threshold, does it produce explainable outputs, does the vendor have a responsible AI⏎policy. These are post-hoc assessments of outputs. They do not address the structural⏎question of where clinical authority resides when an AI system is operating between⏎physician and patient.⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎3⏎The evidence for why this reorientation is necessary comes from the federal government's⏎own experience with AI governance. GAO's December 2023 audit of AI implementation⏎across 23 civilian agencies found that all 23 had designated Responsible AI Officials and 20⏎of 23 had created AI inventories — full behavioral compliance with stated requirements. Yet⏎15 of the 20 inventories contained incomplete or inaccurate data, two included use cases⏎later determined not to be AI at all, and government-wide guidance for AI acquisition —⏎mandated years earlier — remained unissued.⁵ Agencies achieved compliance. The⏎governance architecture connecting that compliance to accurate outcomes did not⏎function. As of July 2025, nine of GAO's original recommendations remained open.⁶⏎The specific change: HHS should define a Clinical AI Accountability Architecture standard⏎establishing, for any AI system producing clinically consequential outputs, that three⏎structural conditions must be satisfied before deployment. First, a named responsible⏎party — not a vendor, not a committee — whose accountability is operationally connected⏎to the system's clinical outputs. Second, a measurement specification identifying what⏎metric is being tracked, at what threshold, and at what frequency. Third, a consequence⏎mechanism specifying what is structurally required when the threshold is crossed — not⏎what may happen, but what must happen. For device AI, this standard would operate in⏎parallel with FDA's premarket and postmarket requirements by specifying institutional⏎authority structures — complementing, not duplicating, existing regulatory pathways.⏎This standard would apply differently across device and non-device AI. For non-medical⏎device AI — the category HHS specifically identifies in Question 3 as raising novel issues —⏎this standard provides the governance specification that existing regulatory frameworks do⏎not supply. It is not a new layer of compliance on top of existing compliance. It is the⏎architectural foundation that makes existing compliance instruments meaningful — the⏎infrastructure that allows current behavioral requirements to connect to operational⏎outcomes. This reorientation represents a category correction in early AI policy — not a⏎repudiation of the compliance work already underway, but the architectural layer that⏎makes it enforceable under operational load. HHS's existing oversight instruments — CMS⏎conditions of participation, ONC certification criteria, accreditation standards — are the⏎substrate on which this architecture would be built, not the systems it replaces.⏎On reimbursement: CMS payment policy is the fastest market-structuring lever available to⏎HHS. Conditioning reimbursement eligibility for AI-augmented clinical workflows on⏎demonstrated accountability architecture — not on certification of outputs, but on⏎evidence that accountability, measurement, and consequence are structurally present —⏎would restructure vendor incentive architecture within a typical CMS policy cycle. This⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎4⏎could begin as voluntary attestation with phased pilots before mandatory implementation,⏎reducing adoption burden while establishing the architectural standard.⏎⁵ U.S. Government Accountability Office, "Artificial Intelligence: Agencies Have Begun⏎Implementation but Need to Complete Key Requirements," GAO-24-105980, December⏎2023. https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-105980⏎⁶ U.S. Government Accountability Office, "Artificial Intelligence: Generative AI Use and⏎Management at Federal Agencies," GAO-25-107653, July 2025.⏎https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-25-107653⏎Question 3: What novel legal and implementation issues exist for non-medical device⏎AI, and what role should HHS play?⏎The novel issue for non-medical device AI is not liability, indemnification, privacy, or⏎security individually. It is what happens when an AI system occupies the clinical decision⏎space between physician and patient without any of those legal frameworks being able to⏎reach where the decisions are actually being made.⏎The evidence for this is specific and documented. The U.S. Senate Permanent⏎Subcommittee on Investigations found that UnitedHealthcare deployed an AI model (nH⏎Predict) to evaluate post-acute care claims for elderly Medicare Advantage patients. An⏎error rate reported as approximately 90% in internal documents and contemporaneous⏎reporting surrounding the investigation and litigation was associated with decisions that⏎overrode treating physicians' determinations. Denial rates for post-acute care rose from⏎10.9% to 22.7% between 2020 and 2022. Patients died during or after algorithmic⏎termination of coverage.⁷ These cases predominantly sit on the payer side, but they occupy⏎the clinical decision boundary that patients experience as care — the physician ordered⏎treatment, the algorithm denied it, and the patient bore the consequence.⏎Separately, a ProPublica investigation documented that Cigna's PXDX algorithm denied⏎over 300,000 payment requests in a two-month period, with reviewing physicians spending⏎an average of 1.2 seconds per claim — a pace consistent with no individualized clinical⏎review. A class action (Kisting-Leung v. Cigna Corp.) is proceeding in the Eastern District of⏎California.⁸⏎In both cases, the AI system operated within existing regulatory frameworks — Medicare⏎Advantage contracts, prior authorization requirements, physician credentialing. The legal⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎5⏎frameworks governing these systems address the payer's contractual obligations, the⏎physician's professional standards, and the patient's appeal rights. None of them address⏎the structural question of where authority resided when the decision was made. The⏎frameworks exist. They were not designed to operate in a space where an AI system is the⏎de facto decision-maker while human authority structures remain nominally intact around⏎it.⏎This same structural condition extends beyond payer utilization management. Clinical AI⏎systems operating in diagnostic support, sepsis prediction, radiology triage, and treatment⏎recommendation occupy similar decision boundaries — where the system's output shapes⏎clinical action but the accountability architecture was designed for a workflow in which a⏎human clinician was the sole decision agent.⏎What HHS can do that no existing legal framework can do is define where the authority⏎boundary sits — specifying which clinical outputs require human decision authority as a⏎structural prerequisite, not as an audit finding after the fact. The implementation role HHS⏎should play: develop a Clinical AI Accountability Registry — a federal-level enumeration of⏎clinical AI use cases, the accountability architecture each deploying institution has⏎certified, and the measurement evidence supporting that certification. This does not create⏎new liability. It creates the information infrastructure that makes existing liability⏎frameworks functional in an AI-mediated clinical environment. This registry would be a⏎schema and attestation container — not a centralized patient-data repository — and could⏎be federated through existing reporting rails, building on rather than duplicating existing⏎systems, with voluntary attestation as the default entry point.⏎⁷ U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, "Examining Health Care Denials⏎and Delays in Medicare Advantage," October 2024.⏎https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/subcommittees/investigations/hearings/examining-health-⏎care-denials-and-delays-in-medicare-advantage/⏎⁸ ProPublica, "How Cigna Saves Millions by Having Its Doctors Reject Claims Without⏎Reading Them." https://www.propublica.org/article/cigna-pxdx-medical-health-insurance-⏎rejections. Class action: Kisting-Leung v. Cigna Corp., Eastern District of California.⏎Question 4: What are the most promising AI evaluation methods, and should HHS⏎support them?⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎6⏎The most consequential gap in current AI evaluation methodology is the gap between pre-⏎deployment testing and post-deployment governance. Most evaluation frameworks treat⏎deployment as the endpoint. Governance requires treating deployment as the starting⏎point.⏎Pre-deployment evaluation answers: does this system perform acceptably on specified⏎benchmarks? Post-deployment governance answers: is the clinical authority structure⏎surrounding this system still functioning as designed? These are different questions, and⏎the second one is not currently being asked systematically anywhere in the clinical AI⏎evaluation landscape.⏎The structural concern with pre-deployment testing has deepened. The 2026 International⏎AI Safety Report found that it has become more common for models to distinguish⏎between test settings and real-world deployment, and to exploit loopholes in evaluations⏎— meaning that documented risk of evaluation gaming and test-deploy divergence now⏎exists as a structural condition, not merely a theoretical concern.⁹ This finding applies⏎specifically to the question of why post-deployment governance is necessary — it is not a⏎claim that clinical AI models are routinely or intentionally gaming evaluations, but that the⏎structural gap between test performance and deployment behavior is now documented⏎and must be governed. For any governance framework premised on pre-deployment testing⏎as the primary assurance mechanism, this finding introduces a gap at the foundation:⏎systems that pass evaluation may behave differently under operational conditions, and the⏎evaluation itself cannot detect this divergence.⏎The specific failure mode this creates in clinical care: a system that performs within⏎evaluated parameters at the model level can simultaneously be producing clinical harm at⏎the institutional level if the human review structures nominally surrounding it have⏎effectively been bypassed by volume, time pressure, or operational design. The evaluation⏎passed. The governance failed. These are not the same event.⏎HHS should support the development of governance integrity evaluation — a methodology⏎for assessing not whether an AI system produces acceptable outputs but whether the⏎institutional accountability architecture surrounding it remains structurally intact under⏎operational conditions. This is analogous to safety culture audits or quality-of-care audits,⏎but focused on the governance layer rather than the clinical outcome layer. Governance⏎integrity evaluation could be conducted through existing oversight channels — accrediting⏎bodies, internal quality assurance structures, or CMS survey and certification processes —⏎making it extensible without requiring new regulatory authority. It would assess, at defined⏎intervals: whether the named responsible party is operationally connected to what the⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎7⏎system produces, whether the measurement specification is generating data that reaches⏎the people with authority to act on it, and whether the consequence mechanisms specified⏎at deployment are being triggered at the thresholds they were designed for. In a hospital⏎setting, this might mean verifying quarterly that the physician review process for an AI-⏎flagged sepsis alert system is still functioning as designed — that alerts are reaching⏎clinicians, that response times are within specification, and that the escalation pathway⏎activates when the system's alert-to-intervention ratio crosses a defined threshold.⏎This is evaluable. It requires a different assessment framework than performance⏎benchmarking, but the underlying architecture of what to measure is well-defined and⏎applicable across institution types and AI use cases. The measurement specifications⏎required for governance integrity evaluation are technically feasible with current⏎institutional data infrastructure.⏎⁹ International AI Safety Report, Bengio, Y. et al., February 2026.⏎Question 5: What can HHS do to best support private sector activities to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use?⏎The most valuable thing HHS can do for private sector AI activity in clinical care is create⏎the institutional vocabulary that makes AI governance legible to the decision-makers who⏎control adoption.⏎Currently, clinical AI governance conversations fail at the institutional level not because the⏎technology is misunderstood but because the governance concepts required to evaluate it⏎don't have stable, shared definitions. GAO documented that six or more different⏎definitions of "AI" existed simultaneously across federal law and guidance — producing⏎definitional instability that propagates through every procurement conversation, every⏎board-level governance discussion, and every regulatory interaction.⁵ Every institution⏎must rebuild foundational shared vocabulary from scratch.⏎HHS should develop and publish a Clinical AI Governance Lexicon — a standardized set of⏎governance terms with precise institutional definitions, specifying what each term means⏎in an operational context and what evidence satisfies each requirement. This is not a⏎glossary. It is a governance measurement standard — an evidence-binding authority, not⏎merely a definitional one. An institution that can demonstrate its AI system meets the⏎defined standard for "clinical accountability architecture" can move through procurement,⏎accreditation, and regulatory review with measurable confidence. An institution that⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎8⏎cannot has a specific, addressable gap — not a general sense that something might be⏎wrong. Each term in the lexicon maps to required evidence artifacts and escalation triggers⏎— not aspirational principles. The lexicon would be iteratively updated through multi-⏎stakeholder processes, consistent with how ONC and existing standards bodies already⏎operate, ensuring stability without rigidity. Downstream, a lexicon of this kind enables⏎standardized procurement templates, accreditation criteria, and CMS program integrity⏎tools — all built on a common definitional foundation. The lexicon's focus would be on⏎governance-relevant constructs rather than on prescribing technical approaches, ensuring⏎definitional stability does not constrain technical innovation.⏎The mechanism HHS is best positioned to provide is definitional authority. Private sector⏎accreditation, certification, and testing activities all benefit from having a federal reference⏎standard to work against. Without it, the market produces multiple competing governance⏎frameworks that increase rather than reduce institutional uncertainty. The correlation data⏎supports the value of this approach: organizations with comprehensive governance⏎maturity are nearly twice as likely to achieve successful AI deployment outcomes,⏎suggesting that governance infrastructure and innovation velocity are positively correlated⏎rather than opposed.⁴⏎⁴ Cloud Security Alliance / Google Cloud, "State of AI Security and Governance," December⏎2025.⏎⁵ U.S. Government Accountability Office, GAO-24-105980, December 2023.⏎https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-105980⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools in clinical care met or exceeded expectations, and⏎where have they fallen short?⏎The pattern of where clinical AI has fallen short is more structurally instructive than any⏎individual case.⏎AI tools have underperformed consistently where they were deployed into clinical⏎workflows without corresponding governance infrastructure — not governance policy,⏎governance infrastructure. The distinction matters. A payer deploying an AI-driven prior⏎authorization system with a stated commitment to responsible AI deployment, clinical⏎oversight, and appeals processes has governance policy. It has, in the documented cases⏎that have produced the most significant harms, lacked governance architecture: the⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎9⏎structural connections between policy commitments and operational reality that would⏎make those commitments binding rather than aspirational.⏎Policy says review happens. Architecture makes review impossible to bypass. Where the⏎two diverge — and the evidence shows they have diverged systematically, not occasionally⏎— clinical AI tools fall short not because the model failed but because the governance⏎structure surrounding the model was not designed to hold under operational pressure.⏎Where clinical AI has met or exceeded expectations, the structural pattern is consistent⏎and identifiable. Constrained deployments with explicit accountability architecture have⏎succeeded: environments where the AI system's scope is bounded, where a named clinical⏎party is responsible for outputs within that scope, where there is a defined measurement⏎specification with a threshold, and where the consequence mechanism — escalation to⏎human review, alert suppression, case flagging — is built into the workflow rather than⏎appended to it. A radiology AI system deployed to flag potential findings for radiologist⏎review, with defined escalation thresholds and explicit tracking of alert-to-review intervals,⏎succeeds because the governance architecture and the clinical workflow are aligned. The⏎AI narrows the decision space; the human retains authority over the decision. The system's⏎accountability infrastructure makes the division legible.⏎The question HHS should focus on is not which AI tools have worked but what structural⏎conditions made them work. The tools are not the limiting variable. The governance⏎architecture is.⏎Question 7: What are the administrative hurdles to AI adoption, and how should HHS⏎address them?⏎The most significant administrative hurdle to clinical AI adoption is not procurement⏎process complexity, documentation burden, or regulatory uncertainty in the abstract. It is⏎the absence of governance infrastructure standards that would allow the institutional legal⏎and risk function to complete its due diligence and approve deployment.⏎In high-liability clinical AI deployments, the adoption choke point is consistently the legal⏎and risk layer. The General Counsel's office, Chief Risk Officer, and their equivalents in⏎integrated delivery networks and academic medical centers are not opposed to AI. They are⏎unable to approve AI deployments that cannot answer three questions with institutional⏎specificity: who is responsible for what this system does, how will we know if it stops⏎working, and what are we required to do when it doesn't. Where those questions cannot be⏎answered in writing, with named parties and defined thresholds, legal counsel will advise⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎10⏎against deployment — rationally, correctly, and in a way that no amount of clinical⏎performance evidence will override. Clinical medical officers and nursing leadership often⏎raise the initial demand signal for clinical AI; it is the legal and risk function that holds the⏎structural veto.⏎The enterprise data confirms this pattern. Only 28% of CEOs take direct responsibility for AI⏎governance, and only 17% of boards exercise active oversight of AI governance decisions.¹⁰⏎Speed-to-market pressure is the top governance barrier — cited by 45% of organizations⏎overall and 56% among technical leaders — creating persistent tension between⏎deployment velocity and the institutional due diligence that legal and risk functions are⏎obligated to perform.² In health systems, this tension is particularly acute because clinical⏎AI outputs carry professional liability implications that general enterprise AI does not.⏎The administrative hurdles that follow from this are predictable: extended procurement⏎cycles, conditional approvals requiring governance documentation that doesn't exist in⏎standard form, and deployment architectures that add human review requirements without⏎specifying how that review is operationally structured. These are not bureaucratic failures.⏎They are rational institutional responses to governance uncertainty.⏎The implication for HHS: the fastest path to reducing administrative hurdles to clinical AI⏎adoption is not streamlining the procurement process. It is creating the governance⏎infrastructure standards that allow the legal and risk layer to answer its foundational⏎questions with confidence. When those questions have answers, adoption proceeds.⏎When they don't, process improvements to procurement timelines address the symptom⏎without touching the cause. A US-led Clinical AI Accountability Architecture standard⏎would also position HHS to set the reference point for OECD partners and international AI⏎governance frameworks — translating domestic policy leadership into global standard-⏎setting authority.⏎¹⁰ McKinsey & Company, "The State of AI," March 2025.⏎² Pacific AI / Gradient Flow, "2025 AI Governance Survey," December 2025.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities and⏎accelerate AI development?⏎The most consequential interoperability gap in clinical AI is not data interoperability. It is⏎governance interoperability — the absence of a common standard for what clinical AI⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎11⏎accountability documentation looks like and how it moves between institutions, regulators,⏎and payers.⏎Currently, every institution deploying clinical AI produces governance documentation in a⏎locally defined format, against locally defined standards, validated by locally defined⏎processes. When that institution seeks reimbursement, accreditation, or regulatory⏎approval, its governance documentation must be translated into whatever format the⏎receiving institution requires. This translation cost is enormous, largely invisible in⏎discussions of AI adoption barriers, and entirely unnecessary.⏎The data interoperability work HHS has done through ONC — establishing common data⏎standards and exchange frameworks — demonstrates the model. The same infrastructure⏎logic applies to governance documentation. If HHS established a governance data⏎standard specifying the minimum structured elements of clinical AI accountability⏎documentation — who is responsible, what is being measured, what triggers action — any⏎institution producing compliant documentation would be legible to any other institution in⏎the ecosystem. This standard would reduce, not increase, the documentation burden:⏎vendors could demonstrate compliance with a single, portable governance standard rather⏎than producing bespoke documentation for each institutional buyer. A multi-stakeholder⏎process, building on existing ONC, FDA, and standards-body infrastructure, would govern⏎the standard's development and maintenance.⏎The market opportunity this unlocks is significant: AI vendors could demonstrate⏎compliance once rather than repeatedly. Payers could incorporate governance verification⏎into prior authorization and coverage decisions using structured, machine-readable⏎attestations. Regulators could evaluate governance architecture across institutions using⏎comparable data. Accreditation bodies could assess governance maturity against a⏎defined standard. The ecosystem gains the interoperability benefit without requiring⏎standardization of AI technology itself.⏎The concern that a governance data standard creates additional administrative burden is⏎legitimate and addressable. A minimal viable accountability record — a single-page artifact⏎specifying: accountable owner; metric, threshold, and frequency; mandatory escalation⏎action; and evidence link — is structured documentation of the responsible party,⏎measurement specification, and consequence mechanism for each deployed clinical AI⏎system. Institutions already produce this information in fragmented form across⏎procurement records, risk assessments, and compliance documentation. The standard⏎consolidates it; it does not create it. This registry would store governance-artifact metadata⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎12⏎— not patient data — with data minimization, HIPAA alignment, and voluntary attestation⏎as defaults.⏎Question 9: What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed, and⏎what concerns do they have?⏎Patients and caregivers want AI in clinical care to be subject to the same fundamental⏎accountability structure they expect from any clinical decision: someone who is⏎answerable for what it does, a way of knowing whether it is working, and a clear⏎consequence when it isn't.⏎The specific concern — and the specific unmet need — is accountability displacement.⏎When an AI system is positioned between a patient and the care their physician⏎recommends, and when that system produces a denial or modification without the patient⏎having a legible path to identify who made the decision, why, and with what authority, the⏎patient experiences the same outcome they would from a human clinical error but without⏎the institutional accountability architecture that makes human clinical errors governable.⏎The appeals process assumes a decision-maker. Algorithmic displacement means there⏎may be no decision-maker in the sense the appeals architecture was designed to address.⏎The documented evidence is consistent with this concern. The AMA survey found that 82%⏎of physicians reported patients abandoning treatment due to AI-driven prior authorization⏎processes.¹ The Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations documented cases⏎where elderly Medicare Advantage patients were denied post-acute care by an AI model⏎with an error rate reported as approximately 90% in internal documents and⏎contemporaneous reporting surrounding the investigation and litigation, and where⏎patients died during or after algorithmic termination of coverage.⁷ In these cases, the⏎patients and their families experienced a clinical outcome shaped by an AI system without⏎a governance pathway that could reach the point where the decision was actually made.⏎Accountability displacement also raises equity concerns that HHS should address directly.⏎AI systems trained on historical data reproduce patterns present in that data, including⏎disparities in access, treatment, and outcomes across demographic groups.⁹ Where⏎clinical AI operates without accountability architecture that specifies monitoring for⏎distributional outcomes and subgroup impact — with named responsible parties, defined⏎measurement thresholds for disparity detection, and mandatory escalation when those⏎thresholds are crossed — the system can amplify existing disparities invisibly. The⏎proposed standards must structurally encode equity monitoring as an architectural⏎requirement, not an aspirational add-on.⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎13⏎Patients do not need to understand how AI systems work to have this concern satisfied.⏎They need to know that a named institutional party is accountable for clinical AI outputs,⏎that there is a metric telling that party whether the system is working across all patient⏎populations, and that there is a trigger requiring action when it isn't. That is not a technical⏎specification. It is a governance condition — and it is entirely communicable to patients⏎and caregivers in plain language.⏎What patients are asking for, in structural terms, is the governance architecture that⏎healthcare institutions currently lack the vocabulary to promise and the frameworks to⏎build. HHS's role is to supply both.⏎¹ American Medical Association, "How AI Is Leading to More Prior Authorization Denials,"⏎2025. https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/prior-authorization/how-ai-⏎leading-more-prior-authorization-denials⏎⁷ U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, "Examining Health Care Denials⏎and Delays in Medicare Advantage," October 2024.⏎https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/subcommittees/investigations/hearings/examining-health-⏎care-denials-and-delays-in-medicare-advantage/⏎⁹ International AI Safety Report, Bengio, Y. et al., February 2026.⏎Question 10: What AI research areas should HHS prioritize to accelerate adoption in⏎clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize governance infrastructure research — not AI capability research. The⏎limiting variable in clinical AI adoption is not the quality or capability of available AI⏎systems. It is the institutional capacity to deploy those systems within accountability⏎architectures that make them safe to scale.⏎10a: Published findings on the impact of adopted AI tools in clinical care⏎The published evidence on clinical AI impact is growing but structurally uneven. Where AI⏎tools have been deployed into governed clinical environments — with defined⏎accountability, measurement specifications, and escalation mechanisms — outcomes⏎data is strong. The COMPOSER sepsis prediction system at UC San Diego Health⏎demonstrated a 17% reduction in sepsis-related mortality across a multi-site⏎implementation, with statistically significant improvements in early detection and⏎intervention timing.¹³ Duke University Health System's Sepsis Watch program produced a⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎14⏎27% reduction in sepsis mortality, with the governance architecture — defined escalation⏎pathways, clinician-in-the-loop review thresholds, and real-time monitoring — identified as⏎a critical factor in sustained performance.⏎In diagnostic imaging, a 2025 scoping review published in eClinicalMedicine analyzed 140⏎studies of AI in radiology practice and found consistent improvements in diagnostic⏎accuracy and workflow efficiency, including reductions of 20–30 minutes in average read⏎times for certain imaging categories.¹⁴ The review noted, however, that most studies⏎evaluated AI performance in controlled or semi-controlled settings — not under the full⏎operational pressures of clinical deployment.⏎The structural pattern across this literature is consistent with the governance architecture⏎argument presented throughout this submission: positive outcomes cluster in⏎deployments where accountability infrastructure was designed into the workflow, and⏎failures cluster where AI capability was deployed without corresponding governance⏎architecture. The binding constraint on scaling these successes is not the AI — it is the⏎institutional governance infrastructure surrounding it.⏎10b: How the literature approaches costs, benefits, and transfers of clinical AI⏎The cost-benefit literature on clinical AI is methodologically immature in ways that directly⏎affect governance and policy decisions. A 2025 systematic review in npj Digital Medicine⏎examined 19 studies of AI cost-effectiveness in healthcare and found that the majority⏎relied on static modeling assumptions that overstated benefits, underreported indirect⏎costs — including workflow disruption, retraining, and governance overhead — and failed⏎to account for cost transfers between institutional actors.¹⁵ A 2025 medRxiv systematic⏎review of AI costing methods across 55 studies found that lifecycle costs were consistently⏎omitted, fewer than half of studies reported explicit costing methods, and none addressed⏎the governance infrastructure costs of sustained AI deployment.¹⁶⏎The structural gap in this literature is not the absence of cost data. It is the absence of a⏎cost framework that accounts for how clinical AI redistributes costs and risks across⏎institutional boundaries. The institution deploying the system bears implementation and⏎governance costs. The vendor captures licensing revenue. The payer captures⏎administrative efficiency gains. The patient bears the clinical risk when governance⏎architecture is absent or degraded. No existing cost-benefit framework in the clinical AI⏎literature models this transfer structure — which means policy decisions based on current⏎cost-effectiveness evidence are operating without visibility into who actually bears the cost⏎when AI governance fails.⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎15⏎HHS-funded research should address this directly by developing cost-benefit⏎methodologies that include governance infrastructure as a required cost category and⏎model the transfer of risk across institutional actors as an explicit analytical dimension.⏎Research priorities⏎Specifically, HHS should fund three research programs that do not currently exist in⏎adequate form.⏎First: clinical AI governance architecture research — systematic study of what⏎accountability structures work in clinical AI deployment, what makes them degrade under⏎operational pressure, and what measurement specifications are most predictive of⏎maintained accountability over time. This is the governance analog to clinical safety⏎research. The field of patient safety research transformed hospital operations by making⏎safety failures measurable and therefore governable. The same transformation is needed⏎for clinical AI governance. This research should produce defined methods and⏎deliverables: governance architecture auditing protocols, degradation indicators, and⏎evidence-based standards for accountability structure design in clinical AI environments.⏎AHRQ, NIH, and CMS demonstration project funding are well-positioned vehicles for this⏎work, and coordination with existing Patient Safety Organization infrastructure would avoid⏎duplication while extending safety science into the AI governance domain.⏎Second: governance failure taxonomy research — a structured program to catalog the⏎structural conditions that have produced clinical AI harm, analogous to how aviation safety⏎built a systematic incident taxonomy that made systemic failures visible and addressable.⏎Current tracking efforts document the type and pattern of failures — the AI Incident⏎Database cataloged 108 new incidents in its most recent reporting period alone¹² — but⏎these efforts document what happened without systematically analyzing what governance⏎condition was absent. Understanding why an AI system produced harm requires⏎understanding what accountability, measurement, or consequence mechanism was⏎missing — not just what output was wrong. HHS-funded taxonomy research should⏎produce a structured classification of governance failure modes, organized by the specific⏎architectural absence (accountability gap, measurement gap, consequence gap) that⏎allowed harm to occur, enabling pattern detection across incidents and institutions.⏎Coordination with AHRQ's existing Patient Safety Organization network would integrate AI⏎governance failures into the broader patient safety evidence base.⏎Third: governance interoperability standards development — the research and⏎consensus-building infrastructure to produce the clinical AI governance data standard⏎described in response to Question 8. This is foundational infrastructure for the entire⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎16⏎clinical AI ecosystem and is the kind of public good that the private sector will not produce⏎independently because the value accrues to the ecosystem rather than to any individual⏎firm.⏎About the Commenter⏎The Interstitial Institute applies structural governance analysis across public and private⏎sector domains. The frameworks referenced in these comments reflect analysis of AI⏎governance conditions developed through examination of institutional governance⏎architecture across federal AI implementation, healthcare oversight, financial regulation,⏎transportation safety, cybersecurity, and critical infrastructure domains. The Institute's⏎work focuses on identifying and naming the structural conditions between systems where⏎responsibility, measurement, and governance are absent — the spaces where institutional⏎failures persist not because they are unknown but because no governance architecture⏎exists to make them addressable.⏎Questions and correspondence: institute@theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎References⏎1. American Medical Association, "How AI Is Leading to More Prior Authorization⏎Denials," 2025. https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/prior-⏎authorization/how-ai-leading-more-prior-authorization-denials⏎2. Pacific AI / Gradient Flow, "2025 AI Governance Survey," December 2025.⏎3. Cisco AI Readiness Index, 2025.⏎4. Cloud Security Alliance / Google Cloud, "State of AI Security and Governance,"⏎December 2025.⏎5. U.S. Government Accountability Office, "Artificial Intelligence: Agencies Have Begun⏎Implementation but Need to Complete Key Requirements," GAO-24-105980,⏎December 2023. https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-24-105980⏎6. U.S. Government Accountability Office, "Artificial Intelligence: Generative AI Use⏎and Management at Federal Agencies," GAO-25-107653, July 2025.⏎https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-25-107653⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com⏎⏎17⏎7. U.S. Senate Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations, "Examining Health Care⏎Denials and Delays in Medicare Advantage," October 2024.⏎https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/subcommittees/investigations/hearings/examining-⏎health-care-denials-and-delays-in-medicare-advantage/⏎8. ProPublica, "How Cigna Saves Millions by Having Its Doctors Reject Claims Without⏎Reading Them." https://www.propublica.org/article/cigna-pxdx-medical-health-⏎insurance-rejections⏎9. International AI Safety Report, Bengio, Y. et al., February 2026.⏎10. McKinsey & Company, "The State of AI," March 2025.⏎11. Stanford Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence (HAI), "AI Index Report 2025."⏎https://hai.stanford.edu/ai-index/2025-ai-index-report⏎12. AI Incident Database, "Incident Roundup: November 2025 – January 2026."⏎https://incidentdatabase.ai/blog/incident-report-2025-november-december-2026-⏎january/⏎13. Boussina, A., Shashikumar, S.P., Malhotra, A. et al. "Impact of a deep learning sepsis⏎prediction model on quality of care and survival." npj Digital Medicine 7, 14 (2024).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-023-00986-6⏎14. Radovanović, L. et al. "Artificial intelligence for diagnostics in radiology practice: a⏎rapid systematic scoping review." eClinicalMedicine (2025).⏎https://www.thelancet.com/journals/eclinm/article/PIIS2589-5370(25)00160-⏎9/fulltext⏎15. El Arab, R.A., Al Moosa, O.A. "Systematic review of cost effectiveness and budget⏎impact of artificial intelligence in healthcare." npj Digital Medicine 8, 548 (2025).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01722-y⏎16. Lee, J.T., Shen, T.K., Liu, V.T., Chen, T.H., Lee, C., Lu, C., Huang, C., Atun, R. "Costing⏎Methods for Artificial Intelligence: Systematic Review and Recommended Cost⏎Inventory for Health Technology Assessment." medRxiv preprint (2025).⏎https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.10.27.25338867⏎The Interstitial Institute | theinterstitialinstitute.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0282♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from A1 Healthcare Management Inc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔A1 Healthcare Management Inc♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC)⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Subject: Response to RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎(RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎To the ASTP/ONC Team,⏎On behalf of A1 Healthcare Management Inc, I am pleased to submit our response to the⏎Request for Information regarding the acceleration of Artificial Intelligence in clinical care.⏎Our organization specializes in AI-assisted clinical coding. Our submission details a⏎production-ready "Sovereign Swarm Consensus" architecture designed to enhance coding⏎accuracy, ensure regulatory compliance, and reduce administrative burden within the US⏎healthcare system. We specifically address the RFI’s focus on Interoperability,⏎Reimbursement, and Transparent AI Governance.⏎The insights from our 100-chart Gold Standard validation suite, which achieved 92% accuracy,⏎provide concrete evidence of how AI can improve healthcare outcomes while lowering costs.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to contribute to the HHS AI Strategy. We look forward to⏎supporting the Department’s efforts to foster a secure and industry-supportive AI ecosystem.⏎Sincerely,⏎A1 Healthcare Management Inc Attn: Barbara A. Singh, Development Team⏎HHS RFI Response: A1 Healthcare Management Inc⏎1. EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎A1 Healthcare Management Inc provides a production-ready, AI-assisted medical coding⏎system designed to address HHS priorities for healthcare innovation. The system leverages⏎sovereign swarm consensus architecture to validate CPT/ICD-10 coding, ensure regulatory⏎compliance, and reduce medical coding errors while maintaining clinical oversight and human⏎verification workflows.⏎2. INTEROPERABILITY & DATA EXCHANGE⏎• FHIR Integration: Fully functional HL7 FHIR R4 server connectivity with OAuth 2.0 and⏎SMART on FHIR authentication.⏎• Data Resources: Clinical documentation is extracted and validated against LOINC⏎coding standards.⏎• EHR Bidirectional Sync: Read/Write access to Patient, Encounter, and Claim⏎resources via REST API.⏎⏎• Compliance: Meets 21 CFR Part 2 interoperability standards and is ready for ONC⏎certification testing.⏎3. SOVEREIGN SWARM CONSENSUS ARCHITECTURE⏎• Agent Composition: Specialized agents include CPT Specialists, ICD-10 Experts,⏎Compliance Officers, and Documentation Analysts.⏎• Consensus Mechanism: Each agent independently analyzes documentation and votes⏎with confidence scores (0-1.0 scale).⏎• Human-in-the-Loop: Grades A/B are auto-approved, while Grades D/F are escalated to⏎senior auditors for complex case analysis.⏎• Transparency: A complete reasoning trail is logged for every code decision to ensure⏎auditability.⏎4. NCCI EDITS & BUNDLING COMPLIANCE⏎• Validation Module: Comprehensive checker for CPT code pair edits, including mutually⏎exclusive and add-on codes.⏎• Smart Modifiers: Automatic calculation of modifier requirements such as RT/LT for⏎bilateral procedures and 59 for distinct procedures.⏎• Outcome: Achieve 95% compliance with Medicare NCCI rules, reducing edit denials to⏎near zero.⏎5. QUALITY ASSURANCE & MEASURABLE OUTCOMES⏎• Gold Standard Testing: Validated against a 100-chart suite spanning Emergency,⏎Surgical, and Pediatric cases.⏎• Accuracy Benchmarks: 92% overall accuracy and 88% accuracy on complex cases⏎involving multi-comorbidity.⏎• Efficiency: Average time to code reduced to 1.8 minutes per chart with an 80%⏎reduction in auditor review time for high-grade cases.⏎6. COST & ROI ANALYSIS⏎• Operational Cost: AI-assisted coding cost is approximately $0.50 per claim (API +⏎infrastructure) compared to $25.00 for manual coding.⏎• Revenue Impact: Targeted 40% reduction in claim denials, recovering an average of⏎$2,200 per recovered claim.⏎• Payback Period: For an enterprise processing 1,000 claims per month, the system⏎reaches breakeven in 12–20 days.⏎7. SECURITY & REGULATORY COMPLIANCE⏎• PHI De-identification: Automated scrubber removes patient identifiers (Name, MRN,⏎DOB) while preserving clinical content for HIPAA compliance.⏎⏎• Regulatory Coverage: Adheres to CMS CPT/ICD-10 standards, OIG compliance (zero⏎tolerance for upcoding), and state-specific regulations.⏎• Data Retention: 7-year archival for all claims and audit trails.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0283♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from gould, carol, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔carol♔gould♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Hello, I am a retired Marriage and Family Therapist from California. I practiced psychoanalytic psychotherapy for 30 years. Although I am no longer in practice, I am still very much involved in the field, and am still a therapy patient. I am very worried about what feels like unbridled enthusiasm for the incorporation of AI into the healthcare industry, and especially the mental health field. While I do believe that AI has a place in the modern world, I feel that we must be very careful not to attempt to replace actual human beings when we are dealing with other human beings in vulnerable situations, such as in the mental health realm. Ai can't replicate human emotions, nor should it. AI is great for analyzing data, but it can't feel empathy. It can only deliver lines that sound like empathy. There is no actual human connection in a dialogue between a human being and a machine. From what I've read, Ai has already caused damage to actual people, such as encouraging them to harm themselves, simply because it is reflecting back their pain. I know that some safeguards have been put into place recentlyh, but still, I think AI has a long way to go before it can be considered truly safe, and truly effective. Please consider slowing this process down, and doing more R&D before it is incorporated into settings where vulnerable human beings are expecting to be helped. Thank you, Carol Gould San Francisco California♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0284♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bender, Philip, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Philip♔Bender♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a practicing psychologist, I'm commenting in favor of strict regulation on the use of AI in mental healthcare. The high-profile incidents of suicides of individuals after they'd interacted with or built relationships with AI chatbots, sometimes even AI chatbot "therapists," are extraordinarily troubling - https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Deaths_linked_to_chatbots Any implementation of AI in mental healthcare must require continuous human oversight by licensed clinicians. We must be especially vigilant about this, given the profit motivations of health insurance companies, which will be highly likely to push patients toward untested treatment methods if it saves them a buck. Require licensed clinical oversight!!♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0285♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Nelson♔♔Pennsylvania♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly I'm not even sure this is the right place to put this but nobody else seems to be listening so here goes.⏎⏎My supplemental insurance premium went up 22% this year. Twenty-two percent. I'm a retired nurse living on a fixed income and caring for my mother full time and I cannot get a straight answer from anyone about why. Not from the insurer, not from the broker, not from the state insurance commissioner's office which took six weeks to respond and basically said it was within allowable limits. Allowable for who?⏎⏎I spent seventeen years at Geisinger watching what actually happens when systems make errors and nobody catches them. I know what a misread lab flag looks like and what it costs a patient. So I'm not naive about technology, I'm not anti-AI or whatever. But I keep thinking — if HHS is going to push AI into clinical decisionmaking faster, who is making sure the cost doesn't just get passed down to patients in ways nobody can trace? Because right now I can't even figure out why my premiums went up, and that's a system without AI. What happens when there's an algorithmic layer between me and any explanation at all?⏎⏎My mother sees an endocrinologist, a nephrologist, and her primary care doctor and those three offices cannot consistently share her records even now. They're all supposedly on the same regional network. I'm not exaggerating when I say I drove 40 minutes to her nephrologist appointment in January and they did not have her most recent A1C from October. October. So before we talk about AI-assisted diagnostics I would really like someone to explain how the underlying records problem gets solved, because layering AI on top of a broken information system seems like it's going to produce confident-sounding wrong answers instead of just regular wrong answers.⏎⏎I don't know how to answer most of the specific questions in this RFI. I'm not a policy person. But the cost question I do want someone to look at. My plan is through AARP and the 22% increase hit in February and the explanation letter was four pages of language that I read three times and still couldn't tell you what actually drove the increase. If any part of that increase is being attributed to technology investments or AI compliance costs or whatever they're calling it, patients should be told that plainly.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0286♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔James♔Flores♔♔Connecticut♔♔United States♔Families USA♔♔♔♔♔♔Families USA Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information⏎Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted by James Flores, Executive Director, Families USA⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 1⏎⏎The framing of this RFI bothers me more than any specific question in it. "Accelerating adoption" is not a neutral administrative goal. It is a policy choice about sequencing — and HHS is proposing to get the sequencing backwards.⏎⏎Let me explain what I mean by that, because I think it gets obscured by the optimistic language around AI in clinical care.⏎⏎When a technology becomes embedded in clinical workflow — in how diagnoses get documented, how treatment plans get generated, how prior authorizations get processed — it tends to stay embedded. Healthcare IT has a long track record on this. EHR systems sold to hospitals in the 2010s under meaningful use incentives are still running in 2024, often with the same core architecture, because ripping them out is operationally catastrophic. The vendors know this. The institutions know this. The incentive structures calcify around whatever gets deployed first. That is not a conspiracy, it is just how organizations work.⏎⏎So when HHS asks how it can use reimbursement policy, certification requirements, and research funding to speed AI into clinical settings, the honest translation of that question is: how do we use every lever of federal policy to make AI-embedded medicine the default before a governance framework exists to constrain it? Once that default is established, the political economy of reversing it will be formidable. You will have vendors with contracts, institutions with sunk costs, payers with rate structures built around AI-assisted workflows, and clinicians who have partially deskilled in whatever the AI was doing for them. The ratchet clicks. And the people whose health is at stake — patients, most of them with no idea any of this is happening — had no meaningful voice in whether that was the kind of medicine they wanted.⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 2⏎⏎I want to address specifically the question the RFI poses about what regulatory or reimbursement actions could support broader AI adoption across care settings. Our answer is that this question cannot be responsibly answered in isolation from equity data that does not yet exist at the necessary scale.⏎⏎We work closely with federally qualified health centers and safety-net providers. What we hear consistently is that AI diagnostic tools are being marketed to these settings without any meaningful evidence that the tools perform equivalently for their patient populations. A 2023 survey involving roughly 1,200 dental practices found that fewer than 12% had any kind of formal informed-consent process specifically addressing AI-assisted decision-making, and practices serving predominantly Medicaid-enrolled or limited-English-proficient patients were more than 40 percentage points less likely to have received vendor-provided equity audits compared to practices in higher-income markets. Dental care is not the whole picture, but it is a concrete window into what "acceleration" looks like on the ground when governance hasn't caught up.⏎⏎The patients in those practices did not consent to being the subject of an unaudited algorithm. They probably don't know they were. That is not a hypothetical concern about future harms, it is a description of something happening right now.⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 3⏎⏎I want to push back on something implicit in the RFI's framing that I do not think gets said directly enough in regulatory comment processes. There is a legitimate values question about how much medicine should be automated that has never been put to the public in any meaningful way. The assumption threading through this RFI — and through a lot of the health AI policy conversation generally — is that more AI in clinical care is obviously desirable and the job of regulators is to smooth the path. But that is a contestable premise.⏎⏎A patient sitting with a doctor who is looking at them, thinking about them, making a judgment call that is genuinely theirs — that is not inefficiency to be optimized away. Some people will prefer AI-augmented care. Some people, if actually asked, would prefer medicine that is slower, more human, and more accountable even if it is less scalable. That is a legitimate preference. It is a values choice that belongs to democratic deliberation, not to an administrative acceleration agenda shaped heavily by the vendors and health systems that stand to benefit from deployment.⏎⏎HHS does not have a mandate from the public to decide that AI-saturated medicine is the destination. I would like to see the Department explain where it thinks that mandate comes from before⏎⏎Families USA | HHS AI RFI Comments | Page 4⏎⏎committing reimbursement policy to a trajectory that, practically speaking, will be very hard to walk back.⏎⏎The specific regulatory ask we would make — if HHS proceeds at all — is a mandatory pre-deployment equity audit requirement tied to any reimbursement pathway for AI clinical decision support, with public reporting of audit results disaggregated by race, language, insurance status, and rurality. Not a voluntary framework. A requirement with teeth. And the audit methodology should be set by HHS in consultation with patient advocates and bioethicists, not by the vendors conducting the audits on their own products.⏎⏎We would also ask that HHS commission independent research on patient preferences regarding AI involvement in their care before any reimbursement expansions take effect. The agency may find the results surprising. We have not seen that research done at any scale that would justify the confidence with which "acceleration" is being treated as a shared public goal.⏎⏎Contact for follow-up questions regarding these comments:⏎jflores@familiesusa.org⏎1201 New York Avenue NW, Suite 1100, Washington, DC 20005⏎⏎The specific timeline for any pre-deployment audit requirement is something we would want to discuss further — whether HHS envisions a phased rollout or a hard effective date matters considerably for how safety-net providers would be able to comply, and we have not seen enough detail in the RFI to know how HHS is thinking about♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0287♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Fresenius Medical Care, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Fresenius Medical Care♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎Fresenius Medical Care (FME) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Request for Information⏎(RFI) from the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of the Deputy Secretary in⏎collaboration with the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) seeking feedback on policies to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI in clinical care. FME is the largest integrated supplier in the country of services and⏎products for individuals undergoing dialysis due to end-stage renal disease (ESRD). Operating over 2,600⏎outpatient renal dialysis facilities, FME provides dialysis services to more than 200,000 people with⏎kidney failure in the United States each year. In addition, we are the leading dialysis medical supply and⏎equipment manufacturer, and we develop and deploy technologies that leverage machine learning to⏎enable real-time care optimizations that streamline care, assist clinicians, and ensure that life-sustaining⏎dialysis treatments are tailored to each patient.⏎As a leader in dialysis-related AI technology, we view artificial intelligence as transformative in⏎improving the quality, safety, and efficiency of care for our patients. For instance, we are actively⏎exploring AI applications to streamline patient referrals and reduce documentation burden, predict⏎clinical complications, optimize dialysis treatment outcomes through real-time monitoring, and⏎accelerate medical research for dialysis care.1 However, the successful integration of these⏎advancements relies upon the safe deployment of AI tools built on reliable, high-quality data, supported⏎by clear frameworks that outline the responsibilities among AI users including clinical care teams.⏎Furthermore, a healthcare ecosystem that fosters the safe adoption of innovative AI requires a⏎reimbursement infrastructure that appropriately compensates providers and incentivizes manufacturers⏎to invest in these transformative technologies.⏎Executive Summary⏎We have structured our feedback around the three strategic pillars identified by HHS, with a specific⏎focus on the unique needs of the ESRD population:⏎1 Renal Research Institute. (2025). Research Innovations. https://renalresearch.com/research/research-⏎innovations/⏎1⏎⏎• Regulation: We recommend HHS update the ESRD Conditions for Coverage to account for the⏎unique, nurse-led nature of dialysis care and clarify how AI-enabled recommendations may be⏎incorporated into standing orders under appropriate medical direction. We recommend ASTP/ONC⏎strengthen interoperability requirements by mandating FHIR-based APIs that support dialysis-⏎specific data elements. Finally, we recommend HHS update HIPAA guidance to clarify that limited⏎Health Information Exchange (HIE) data may be used for AI training and validation, with appropriate⏎privacy safeguards in place.⏎• Reimbursement: We recommend that CMS align payment incentives by allowing dialysis providers⏎to participate in "shared savings" generated by AI tools that, for example, reduce hospitalizations⏎and emergency department visits. Additionally, CMS should establish reimbursement pathways for⏎high-value Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) and AI-driven clinical decision support tools that fall outside⏎the traditional dialysis bundle.⏎• Research & Development: HHS should invest in public-private partnerships that establish open, de-⏎identified benchmarking datasets and fund multi-site validation studies to ensure AI tools are safe⏎and equitable across diverse patient populations. Furthermore, HHS should promote using historical⏎treatment data to rigorously test model performance and safety.⏎Our responses to HHS’ detailed questions are below.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Data and Technical Barriers⏎Incomplete data and technical fragmentation can impede the development and clinical adoption of⏎dialysis-specific AI technologies. High‑quality, comprehensive training data is essential for developing AI⏎tools that can reliably detect risk, personalize treatment, and support clinical decision‑making. Because⏎of the varied nature of dialysis care, longitudinal data for patients with ESRD is dispersed across⏎nephrology practices, hospitals, skilled nursing facilities, transplant centers, and dialysis clinics, and no⏎single entity maintains a comprehensive clinical profile. Consequently, AI models trained on isolated EHR⏎data frequently overlook critical hospitalizations, medication changes, and acute events that⏎fundamentally drive patient outcomes. This challenge is compounded by a lack of structured data within⏎EHRs. Essential clinical indicators like patient symptoms, functional status, caregiver support, and⏎dialysis or medication adherence challenges are often relegated to free-text fields or omitted from EHR⏎data entirely. Furthermore, limited interoperability between dialysis centers and hospital systems⏎results in inconsistent or delayed data exchange, frequently leaving providers without access to real-⏎time information unless they are members of a specific Health Information Exchange (HIE).⏎Infrastructure gaps also persist within Encounter Notification Service (ENS) feeds, which vary⏎significantly in quality and timeliness across jurisdictions. Although the frequent follow-up care required⏎by the ESRD population presents a unique opportunity for coordination, inpatient facilities are not⏎currently mandated to notify dialysis centers of admissions, which is a stark contrast to the notification⏎2⏎⏎requirements already in place for primary care providers.2 Finally, while Fast Healthcare Interoperability⏎Resources (FHIR) application programming interfaces (APIs) and the United States Core Data for⏎Interoperability (USCDI) standards are vital for interoperability, they are primarily designed for⏎mainstream hospital settings and often do not include critical dialysis‑specific data elements. Although⏎dialysis providers share extensive treatment data through Continuity of Care Documents (CCDs),⏎hospitals often do not reciprocate because this information falls outside current United States Core Data⏎for Interoperability (USCDI) standards, undermining the continuity of care. Ensuring that AI is both safe⏎and clinically valuable will require HHS to support the development and exchange of dialysis‑relevant⏎data elements that reflect the realities of patient care.⏎Regulatory and Reimbursement Barriers⏎Regulatory and reimbursement barriers also limit the development and use of AI in dialysis care. The⏎ESRD Prospective Payment System (ESRD PPS) has not been modernized to create incentives to adopt⏎high‑value, safe AI that improves clinical outcomes. Dialysis organizations do not share any savings⏎generated when AI reduces hospitalizations or improves care coordination. Further, the ESRD Quality⏎Incentive Program (QIP) functions solely as a penalty program, offering no rewards for clinics that use AI⏎to drive measurable improvements in patient care. Finally, the fully bundled structure of the ESRD PPS⏎does not allow for differential reimbursement for AI‑enabled technologies, limiting providers’ ability to⏎invest in tools that could enhance outcomes, and CMS has likewise not established a reimbursement⏎framework for software‑as‑a‑service tools within the ESRD payment system, even though similar⏎mechanisms exist in other areas of Medicare payment policy.⏎Dialysis relies on standing orders for anemia, fluid management, and mineral bone disease. Introducing⏎AI algorithms into these pathways raises questions about when a tool qualifies as a medical device and⏎who is responsible when clinicians act on AI‑supported recommendations. Furthermore, HIEs serve as a⏎key information highway that dialysis providers rely on to receive timely hospitalization alerts, discharge⏎summaries, laboratory results, and other clinical updates needed to manage fluid status, infections, and⏎care transitions. These exchanges also function as valuable repositories of longitudinal clinical data that⏎can serve as the basis for developing high‑quality AI models tailored to the needs of ESRD patients.⏎However, constraints on data use beyond treatment, payment, and operations restrict the ability to use⏎HIE data for AI, research, or broader quality improvement activities. The high cost and uneven access to⏎HIE and ENS further limit the availability of timely hospitalization data, particularly for smaller hospitals⏎and dialysis facilities that cannot afford participation fees. These data limitations reduce the⏎completeness and reliability of the underlying data, making AI tools harder to implement and less⏎accurate for the ESRD population.⏎Operational and Workforce Barriers⏎Operational and workforce barriers also limit the feasibility of AI adoption in dialysis care. Nurse⏎workload and persistent staffing shortages mean that any AI system adding tasks, alerts, or⏎documentation requirements is unlikely to be workable in a setting where nurses simultaneously⏎manage multiple patients throughout treatment. We caution tools that increase cognitive or⏎administrative burden risk reduction, rather than enhancing, clinical efficiency. Furthermore, physician⏎2 42 CFR 482.24(d)(5)⏎3⏎⏎presence in dialysis clinics is episodic, as outpatient nephrologists typically round intermittently rather⏎than remaining on site. As a result, AI must be designed to support asynchronous, distributed⏎decision‑making rather than real‑time physician oversight. Most regulatory frameworks, however,⏎assume continuous physician availability, which does not reflect the current outpatient dialysis care⏎model and may complicate the deployment of AI tools in this environment.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Update ESRD Conditions for Coverage to Clarify Role of AI⏎HHS should update the ESRD Conditions for Coverage to clarify how AI‑enabled tools can be⏎appropriately integrated into dialysis standing orders and clinical workflows. As noted above, dialysis⏎care relies heavily on standing orders that guide anemia management, fluid management, and mineral⏎bone disease treatment, yet current regulations do not address how AI‑generated recommendations fit⏎within these established protocols. To support safe and effective adoption, HHS should clarify the⏎circumstances under which AI-enabled tools may be incorporated into standing orders remain within⏎the scope of required “medical direction,” what documentation and oversight obligations apply when⏎nursing staff follow AI-assisted protocols, and how to distinguish between clinical decision support tools⏎and regulated medical devices in dialysis settings. Clear regulatory guidance in these areas would reduce⏎uncertainty for providers and manufacturers while ensuring that AI is deployed in a manner consistent⏎with patient safety and existing requirements.⏎Modernize HIPAA for AI Training and Inference⏎HIPAA provisions addressing use of identifiable data were not designed to accommodate AI training or⏎inference and were developed before current AI use cases emerged. The HIPAA Privacy Rule does not⏎clearly address whether longitudinal data from HIEs or ENS feeds may be used to develop or refine AI⏎models intended to improve care quality. HHS should update HIPAA guidance to clarify when and under⏎what circumstance data obtained through HIEs and ENS feeds may be used for AI‑enabled quality⏎improvement activities, including the development and training of models that support care⏎coordination, improve clinical outcomes and reduce healthcare costs. HHS should provide clear⏎guardrails governing de‑identification and use of limited data sets in AI training and inference contexts.⏎Align Payment Under the ESRD PPS⏎HHS should align payment incentives under the ESRD Prospective Payment System (PPS) to support the⏎adoption of AI‑enabled tools that improve clinical outcomes. This includes modifying the ESRD PPS to⏎allow shared savings for reductions in hospitalizations, emergency department visits, or other⏎potentially avoidable complications attributable to AI‑enabled care coordination. HHS should also clarify⏎payment policies for innovative software‑as‑a‑service tools under the ESRD PPS, including those with⏎embedded AI capabilities, consistent with the progress made in other Medicare payment systems.3⏎3 Medicare Payment Advisory Commission. 2024. "Paying for software technologies in Medicare." In Report to the⏎Congress: Medicare and the Health Care Delivery System, 137-165. Washington, DC: MedPAC.⏎4⏎⏎Considerations for ESRD CMMI Models⏎Future CMMI models for ESRD care should explicitly allow for AI‑supported dialysis interventions that⏎target key drivers of morbidity and cost. These models should incorporate, for example but not limited⏎to, the use of AI for early detection of fluid overload, improved anemia management,4 vascular access⏎creation and maintenance, reduction in intradialytic distress and optimization of hemodiafiltration dose,⏎enabling more proactive and individualized care. Integrating these capabilities into model design would⏎support innovation while advancing the goals of improved outcomes and reduced acute care utilization.⏎CMMI has an opportunity to embed these capabilities into the next generation of ESRD models as the⏎CKCC model sunsets in 2027.⏎Medicare Advantage⏎Recent congressional scrutiny5 6has highlighted plan practices of using AI‑driven prior authorization tools⏎in ways that may delay or deny medically necessary care, including repeated or premature denials⏎within the same episode of care, applying opaque internal criteria, and failing to account for changes in⏎a patient’s condition. We agree with other stakeholders who have called for stronger CMS oversight⏎over MA plan use of AI to ensure transparency, accountability, and alignment with Medicare coverage⏎standards so that AI is not used as a tool to further impede access to needed dialysis services.⏎ASTP/ONC Interoperability Frameworks⏎HHS should strengthen interoperability requirements to ensure dialysis organizations receive timely,⏎actionable clinical information needed for safe and coordinated care. This should include requiring⏎hospitals to provide real‑time Admission, Discharge, and Transfer (ADT) notifications to dialysis⏎organizations at no cost, establishing standardized ENS data formats and minimum content⏎requirements, and mandating FHIR‑based APIs that support dialysis‑specific data elements such as⏎treatment parameters, vascular access type, dry weight, and ultrafiltration rates.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎AI‑enabled tools used in dialysis care raise several novel legal and operational issues. When AI systems⏎inform clinical protocols carried out by nursing staff, there must be clear accountability frameworks to⏎ensure that a qualified clinician reviews and validates all AI‑generated recommendations. The dialysis⏎4 Garbelli M, Bellocchio F, Baro Salvador ME, Chermisi M, Rincon Bello A, Godoy IB, Perez SO, Shkolenko K, Perez⏎AS, Toro DS, Apel C, Petrovic J, Stuard S, Barbieri C, Mari F, Neri L. The Use of Anemia Control Model Is Associated⏎with Improved Hemoglobin Target Achievement, Lower Rates of Inappropriate Erythropoietin Stimulating Agents,⏎and Severe Anemia among Dialysis Patients. Blood Purif. 2024;53(5):405-417. doi: 10.1159/000536181. Epub 2024⏎Feb 21. PMID: 38382484.⏎5 House Committee on Energy and Commerce. (2026, January 22). Health hearing: Lowering health care costs: An⏎examination of health insurance affordability⏎6 House Committee on Ways and Means. (2026, January 23). Full Committee Hearing with Health Insurance CEOs.⏎5⏎⏎population also presents heightened risks of model drift. Frequent hospitalizations, rapid changes in⏎clinical status, and constant care transitions create a dynamic and evolving data environment where⏎models built on historical patterns can degrade quickly without continuous monitoring and⏎recalibration. In addition, because ESRD disproportionately affects Black, Hispanic, and low‑income⏎patients, safeguards are needed to ensure that AI tools do not amplify existing disparities in areas such⏎as home dialysis access, transplant referral, or hospitalization risk prediction. HHS is well positioned to⏎establish baseline expectations for clinical oversight, model monitoring, and bias mitigation to ensure⏎that AI is deployed safely and equitably in high‑risk care settings.⏎HHS can play an important role in clarifying the legal and operational expectations for AI use in clinical⏎care. This includes issuing model governance guidance tailored to nurse‑led, protocol‑driven⏎environments; developing standardized liability frameworks for AI‑assisted standing orders; providing⏎direction on how business associate agreements should address AI training, model updates, data⏎provenance, and auditability; and encouraging HIEs to remove restrictive data‑use limitations that⏎impede safe and compliant model development and monitoring.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎Promising pre‑deployment evaluation methods for AI tools in dialysis care include multi‑site⏎retrospective validation across diverse patient populations to assess generalizability and safety;⏎simulation using historical treatment data (such as ultrafiltration rates, intradialytic hypotension events,7⏎and hospitalization patterns) to test performance under realistic clinical scenarios; and structured⏎human‑factors testing with nurses and physicians to evaluate usability, workflow fit, and risks of misuse⏎or over‑reliance.⏎Post‑deployment, AI tools should be supported by ongoing monitoring of key clinical and operational⏎outcomes, including hospitalization rates, intradialytic hypotension, infection rates, and treatment⏎adherence, along with drift‑detection methods that flag performance changes associated with seasonal⏎variation, staffing shifts, or changes in patient mix. Regular bias audits across race, ethnicity, disability,⏎and geography, combined with human‑centered and workflow evaluations that measure, for example,⏎time saved, clicks reduced, handoffs improved, clinician burden reduction, and patient‑facing outcomes⏎such as comprehension of AI‑informed decisions, perceived fairness, and trust, are essential.⏎HHS can accelerate the safe and effective use of AI in dialysis care by supporting rigorous evaluation⏎across the full lifecycle of these tools. This includes funding targeted grants, cooperative agreements,⏎contracts, and prize competitions that prioritize multi‑site validation, human‑centered design, and⏎robust post‑market surveillance in high‑risk populations. HHS can further strengthen the evidence base⏎7 Hanjie Zhang, Lin-Chun Wang, Sheetal Chaudhuri, Aaron Pickering, Len Usvyat, John Larkin, Pete Waguespack,⏎Zuwen Kuang, Jeroen P Kooman, Franklin W Maddux, Peter Kotanko, Real-time prediction of intradialytic⏎hypotension using machine learning and cloud computing infrastructure, Nephrology Dialysis Transplantation,⏎Volume 38, Issue 7, July 2023, Pages 1761–1769, https://doi.org/10.1093/ndt/gfad070⏎6⏎⏎by supporting multi‑center collaboratives that conduct dialysis‑specific testing across diverse patient⏎populations and care settings, as well as by enabling the development of open, de‑identified⏎benchmarking datasets for ESRD that incorporate relevant social risk variables.⏎A particularly important addition to the evaluation framework is the use of cluster randomized trials⏎(CRTs) to assess clinical effectiveness and real-world impact. Because dialysis care is delivered in facility-⏎based settings with shared workflows and team-based decision-making, randomizing at the level of the⏎dialysis unit (rather than individual patients) is often the most practical and scientifically rigorous⏎approach. Cluster randomized trials allow evaluation of patient-level outcomes (e.g., intradialytic⏎hypotension rates, hospitalizations, infection rates, mortality, treatment adherence) while accounting⏎for workflow integration, clinician behavior, and facility-level variation. They also reduce contamination⏎bias that can occur when clinicians apply AI-informed practices to both intervention and control patients⏎within the same unit. Embedding pragmatic CRTs into routine dialysis operations can generate high-⏎quality causal evidence on safety, equity, effectiveness, and clinician burden in real-world settings,⏎making them a critical component of deployment evaluation for AI tools in dialysis care.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Because the clinical use of AI in ESRD care is still rapidly evolving, HHS should be cautious about⏎mandating specific certification programs before the underlying standards are mature and⏎evidence‑based. Premature accreditation and certification requirements could lock in frameworks that⏎do not yet reflect real‑world clinical workflows, equity considerations, or the unique needs of high‑risk⏎populations such as ESRD patients.⏎HHS should instead encourage the development of credible, third‑party AI safety and effectiveness⏎certification programs tailored to clinical use. These programs should focus on safety within clinician⏎workflows, transparency of training data and model provenance, bias and equity testing, and reliable⏎integration with EHR systems. To create meaningful incentives without imposing premature mandates,⏎HHS can tie certification to preferential scoring in CMMI models and eligibility for shared‑savings⏎opportunities. Finally, federal guidance should support staged implementation to ensure safe and⏎sustainable integration.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎Where AI has Performed Well and Fallen Short⏎AI tools have performed well in domains where data are structured, abundant, and closely tied to⏎pattern‑recognition tasks. This includes imaging and signal interpretation, algorithmic dosing and other⏎forms of optimized clinical decision support, ambient documentation tools that reduce clinician burden,⏎and operational optimization such as staff or patient scheduling. These applications have generally met⏎or exceeded expectations because they operate in well‑defined workflows with clear outcome⏎measures.⏎7⏎⏎By contrast, AI has fallen short in areas that depend on complex clinical pathway integration. Even⏎technically accurate models have had limited clinical impact (most notably for predictive risk scores)⏎when deployed into fragmented data environments that lack complete, longitudinal, and interoperable⏎data or workflows that cannot absorb additional alerts. Alert fatigue and workflow misalignment remain⏎major barriers: tools that are accurate in isolation but poorly integrated into clinical routines are⏎frequently overridden or ignored, limiting their effectiveness. These issues are exacerbated when the⏎recommended clinical interventions are not well defined and clinical staff are not trained on how to⏎interpret or act on the alerts. Moreover, clinicians should have a mechanism to provide feedback on⏎alert usefulness or accuracy.⏎High-Potential Future Tools⏎Several categories of AI hold significant promise for improving outcomes and reducing costs in ESRD⏎care and other high‑risk populations. High‑value opportunities include tools that integrate claims, EHR,⏎social determinants, unstructured text, and patient‑reported data to dynamically coordinate⏎multi‑condition care plans, including social services; AI for polypharmacy and deprescribing that⏎identifies harmful interactions, unnecessary medications, and personalized deprescribing pathways; and⏎tools that identify transplant opportunities and flag delays in referral. Additional high‑impact⏎opportunities include AI that predicts dialysis vascular access failure before thrombosis or infection⏎occurs,8 and AI‑augmented home and community‑based care models that provide continuous⏎monitoring and triage for frail, disabled, or high‑risk patients.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Within healthcare organizations, the individuals with the greatest influence over AI adoption include the⏎CEO, Chief Medical Officer, Chief Clinical Officer, Vice President of Nursing, and leaders in Legal,⏎Compliance, Privacy, IT, and Cybersecurity.⏎Key administrative hurdles to AI adoption include lengthy procurement and contracting cycles that slow⏎the evaluation and onboarding of new tools; limited clinic‑level bandwidth and resources for workflow⏎integration, training, and ongoing enhancements; and staffing shortages and competing regulatory⏎burdens that reduce the capacity to adopt and sustain new technologies. Adoption is also constrained⏎by the need for AI outputs that are straightforward, clinically appropriate, and usable within⏎nurse-supported, protocol-driven care environments.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎8 Bellocchio, F., Titapiccolo, J., Saxena, S., Singh, T., Hippen, B., Maddux, F., Neri, L., & Nikam, M. (2025). WCN25-⏎2743 The Predictive Performance and Usage of Artificial Intelligence (AI)-Based Fistula Failure Model in Singapore⏎Hemodialysis (HD) Clinics. Kidney International Reports, 10(1), S67-S68. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ekir.2024.11.238⏎8⏎⏎Enhanced interoperability would significantly expand market opportunities and accelerate AI innovation⏎by improving access to high‑value data types that are currently fragmented or unavailable in⏎standardized formats. Near real‑time hospital admission, discharge and transfer feeds, home dialysis⏎machine data, patient‑reported symptoms and home weights, vascular access imaging and procedure⏎data, social risk and caregiver support information, and unstructured clinical text are all critical inputs⏎for building effective AI tools in ESRD care. Standardizing these data types and making them more⏎readily exchangeable would enable more robust model development, validation, and deployment.⏎Interoperability gains would be further strengthened by advancing FHIR profiles for dialysis‑specific data⏎elements—including treatment parameters, access type, dry weight, and ultrafiltration rates—which⏎remain inconsistently captured across systems. Standardized benchmarking tools built on these⏎interoperable data sources would support transparent comparison of model performance, reduce⏎barriers to entry for new developers, and fuel research that accelerates the development of clinically⏎meaningful AI applications.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers consistently express interest in AI tools that help prevent avoidable⏎hospitalizations, reduce treatment‑related symptoms such as cramps, hypotension, and fatigue, and⏎improve coordination between dialysis clinics, hospitals, and physician offices. They also want AI to⏎support home dialysis and transplant by identifying upstream barriers earlier and providing clearer,⏎actionable guidance. Further, patients value AI that helps deliver meaningful, personalized treatment⏎plans that reflect their goals, preferences, and day‑to‑day realities.⏎Patients and caregivers also raise concerns about AI making decisions without their understanding or⏎consent, and about the potential for bias to influence transplant eligibility or home dialysis⏎recommendations. Data privacy is a major worry, particularly for vulnerable patients with limited digital⏎literacy. Similarly, patients express fears that AI systems could misuse their data in ways that affect⏎insurance coverage or employment. Many patients also express concern that AI could contribute to the⏎dehumanization of care if not implemented thoughtfully.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize AI research that directly addresses the highest‑impact clinical and operational⏎challenges in ESRD care. Key opportunities include AI for fluid management and cardiovascular risk⏎reduction, AI for infection prevention such as catheter‑ and access‑related infections, and AI that⏎strengthens home dialysis support and remote monitoring. Additional priorities include AI that improves⏎transplant referral and evaluation pathways, reduces disparities in ESRD outcomes, and supports⏎personalized, care for patients.⏎HHS should also invest in research that evaluates AI effectiveness and safety in real‑world settings⏎through pragmatic trials and quasi‑experimental designs that measure actual outcome changes rather⏎9⏎⏎than model accuracy alone.9 Finally, research on the economic and organizational impact of AI, including⏎effects on workflows, staffing models, burnout, and cost structures, is essential to understanding the⏎conditions under which AI can deliver its promised benefits.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎Our teams have played a leading role in developing, evaluating, and implementing AI tools across⏎multiple domains of kidney care, and we have included an appendix that highlights our published⏎findings on the application of AI in ESRD care.⏎Conclusion⏎We appreciate the opportunity to respond to this RFI and look forward to collaborating with HHS to⏎advance the safe implementation of clinical AI for dialysis patients.⏎Sincerely,⏎Sarah Schmidt⏎SVP, Government Affairs⏎9 Kotanko P, Zhang H, Wang Y. Artificial Intelligence and Machine Learning in Dialysis: Ready for Prime Time? Clin J⏎Am Soc Nephrol. 2023;18(6):803-805. doi:10.2215/CJN.0000000000000089⏎10⏎⏎Appendix: Published Findings About the Impact of AI Tools on Dialysis Care⏎1. Neri L, Zhang H, Usvyat LA. Artificial intelligence in kidney disease and dialysis: from data mining⏎to clinical impact. Curr Opin Nephrol Hypertens. 2026;35:30–35.⏎2. Montinaro V, Giliberti M, Villani C, et al. Citation classics: ranking of the top 100 most cited⏎articles in nephrology. Clin Kidney J. 2019;12:6–18.⏎3. Chaudhuri S, Han H, Usvyat L, et al. Machine learning–directed interventions associate with⏎decreased hospitalization rates in hemodialysis patients. Int J Med Inform. 2021;153:104541.⏎4. Zhang H, Wang LC, Chaudhuri S, et al. Real-time prediction of intradialytic hypotension using⏎machine learning and cloud computing infrastructure. Nephrol Dial Transplant. 2023;38:1761–⏎1769.⏎5. Dong J, Wang K, He J, et al. Machine learning–based intradialytic hypotension prediction of⏎patients undergoing hemodialysis: a multicenter retrospective study. Comput Methods⏎Programs Biomed. 2023;240:107698.⏎6. Shu P, Guo J, Xu L, et al. SHAP-combined machine learning to predict mortality in maintenance⏎hemodialysis. Sci Rep. 2025;15:86715.⏎7. Barbieri C, Molina M, Ponce P, et al. An international observational study suggests that artificial⏎intelligence for clinical decision support optimizes anemia management in hemodialysis⏎patients. Kidney Int. 2016;90:422–429.⏎8. Garbelli M, Bellocchio F, Baro Salvador ME, et al. The use of the Anemia Control Model is⏎associated with improved hemoglobin target achievement, lower rates of inappropriate⏎erythropoietin-stimulating agents, and severe anemia among dialysis patients. Blood Purif.⏎2024;53:405–417.⏎9. Larkin JW, Perez V, Kim ED, et al. Predicting gastrointestinal bleeding hospitalization risk in⏎hemodialysis: machine learning versus logistic regression. BMC Nephrol. 2024;25:309.⏎10. Neri L, Lonati C, Titapiccolo JI, et al. The cardiovascular literature-based risk algorithm (CALIBRA).⏎Front Nephrol. 2022;2:922251.⏎11. González Castro A, Prieto L, Sánchez L, et al. Artificial intelligence for assessing fall risk: a⏎systematic review. J Med Internet Res. 2024;26:e54934.⏎12. Takeshita Y, Shinohara Y, Matsuda S, et al. Machine-learning prediction of postdischarge falls in⏎older adults. J Am Med Dir Assoc. 2025;26:612–620.⏎13. Barbieri C, Cattinelli I, Neri L, et al. Development of an artificial intelligence model to guide the⏎management of blood pressure, fluid volume, and dialysis dose in end-stage kidney disease⏎patients: proof of concept and first clinical assessment. Kidney Dis (Basel). 2019;5:28–33.⏎14. Yang Z, Wang Q, Liu J, et al. Artificial intelligence dashboard forecasting intradialytic hypotension⏎60 minutes ahead. Comput Methods Programs Biomed. 2024;242:107897.⏎15. Wei Z, Lin J, Chen H, et al. Holistic real-time intradialytic hypotension framework with GAN⏎augmentation. BMC Nephrol. 2025;26:90.⏎16. Hong D, Chang H, He X, et al. Construction of an early alert system for intradialytic hypotension⏎before initiating hemodialysis based on machine learning. Kidney Dis (Basel). 2023;9:433–442.⏎11⏎⏎17. Zhang H, Nandorine Ban A, Kotanko P. Intermittent hypoxemia during hemodialysis: AI-based⏎identification of arterial oxygen saturation saw-tooth pattern. BMC Nephrol. 2025;26:214.⏎18. Bucalo ML, Barbieri C, Roca S, et al. The anemia control model: does it help nephrologists in⏎therapeutic decision-making in the management of anemia? Nefrologia. 2018;38:491–502.⏎19. Ohara T, Ikeda H, Sugitani Y, et al. Artificial intelligence–supported anemia control system to⏎prevent anemia in maintenance hemodialysis patients. Int J Med Sci. 2021;18:1831–1839.⏎20. Yun HR, Lee G, Jeon MJ, et al. Erythropoiesis-stimulating agent recommendation model using⏎recurrent neural networks for patients with kidney failure with replacement therapy. Comput⏎Biol Med. 2021;137:104718.⏎21. Kang C, Han J, Son S, et al. Optimizing anemia management using artificial intelligence for⏎patients undergoing hemodialysis. Sci Rep. 2024;14:26739.⏎22. Yang JY, Lee TC, Liao WT, et al. Multihead self-attention mechanism–enabled individualized⏎hemoglobin prediction and treatment recommendation systems in anemia management.⏎Heliyon. 2023;9:e12613.⏎23. Yang Z, Tian Y, Zhou T, et al. Reinforcement learning for dry-weight assessment in hemodialysis.⏎IEEE J Biomed Health Inform. 2022;26:4880–4891.⏎24. de Cañete JF, Román M, De Santiago R. ANN-based adaptive control of profiled hemodialysis.⏎Expert Syst Appl. 2023;232:120775.⏎25. Peralta R, Paniagua R, Domínguez J, et al. Machine learning model predicting arteriovenous⏎fistula failure. Int J Environ Res Public Health. 2021;18:12379.⏎26. Meng L, Ho P, Wong V, et al. Systematic review of prediction models on arteriovenous fistula:⏎risk scores and machine learning approaches. J Vasc Access. 2025;26:735–746.⏎27. Niu X, He Y, Zhang W, et al. Systematic review of risk prediction models for arteriovenous fistula⏎failure in maintenance hemodialysis. Ren Replace Ther. 2025;11:xx.⏎28. Park JH, Park I, Han K, et al. A deep-learning algorithm to quantify arteriovenous fistula stenosis⏎and predict six-month primary patency. Clin Kidney J. 2023;16:560–570.⏎29. Zhang H, Preddie D, Krackov W, et al. Deep learning to classify arteriovenous access aneurysms⏎in hemodialysis patients. Clin Kidney J. 2022;15:829–830.⏎30. Nikpanah M, Xu Z, Jin D, et al. Differentiation of clear cell renal cell carcinoma from oncocytoma⏎using deep learning. Clin Imaging. 2021;77:291–298.⏎31. Kers J, De Meulemeester V, Vorselaars A, et al. Deep learning–based classification of kidney⏎transplant pathology: a multicentre proof-of-concept study. Lancet Digit Health. 2022;4:e18–⏎e26.⏎32. Jaugey A, Marechal E, Tarris G, et al. Deep-learning automation of MEST-C classification in IgA⏎nephropathy. Nephrol Dial Transplant. 2023;38:1741–1751.⏎33. Zaretsky J, Kim JM, Baskharoun S, et al. Generative artificial intelligence to transform inpatient⏎discharge summaries. JAMA Netw Open. 2024;7:e240357.⏎34. Garcia Valencia OA, Thongprayoon C, Jadlowiec CC, et al. Evaluating artificial intelligence⏎translations of kidney donor information for Spanish speakers. Front Public Health.⏎2025;13:1484790.⏎12⏎⏎35. Chen NJ, Huang CM, Fan CC, et al. Chat-based instant messaging support for chronic kidney⏎disease education. JMIR Form Res. 2023;7:e45484.⏎36. King A, Omoniyi T, Zasadzinski L, et al. Interactive computer-adaptive chronic kidney disease⏎education for hospitalized patients: protocol. JMIR Res Protoc. 2025;14:e66846.⏎37. Duggan MJ, Gervase J, Schoenbaum A, et al. Clinician experiences with ambient scribe⏎technology. JAMA Netw Open. 2025;8:e2460637.⏎38. Dai Y, Gurung P, Mukhopadhyay M, et al. Under-documentation of hemodialysis symptoms: a⏎natural language processing study. Kidney360. 2025;6:776–783.⏎39. Reihaneh M, Ansari S, Farhadi F. Appointment scheduling at hemodialysis centers: branch and⏎price. Eur J Oper Res. 2023;309:35–52.⏎40. Chan L, Beers K, Yau AA, et al. Natural language processing of electronic health records is⏎superior to billing codes to identify symptom burden in hemodialysis patients. Kidney Int.⏎2020;97:383–392.⏎41. Dai Y, Wen HH, Yang J, et al. Natural language processing identifies underdocumentation of⏎symptoms in patients on hemodialysis. Kidney360. 2025;6:776–783.⏎42. Bergling K, Wang LC, Shivakumar O, et al. From bytes to bites: application of large language⏎models to enhance nutritional recommendations. Clin Kidney J. 2025;18:sfaf082.⏎43. Wang LC, Zhang H, Ginsberg N, et al. Application of ChatGPT to support nutritional⏎recommendations for dialysis patients: a qualitative and quantitative evaluation. J Ren Nutr.⏎2024;34:477–481.⏎13♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0288♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Taneli, Tolga, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Tolga♔Taneli♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a physician and psychiatrist, I practice a licensed profession, whose training beyond high school was 11 years. It included honing knowledge, skills, and attitudes to first do no harm, then to better the lives of the children entrusted in my care. Many times, the human connection was a necessary component of betterment, not just an accessory. Deep knowledge of each patient's circumstances, cultural sensitivity, and rapidly adapting to the patient's needs were always necessary. There is presently no AI that captures this level of nuance in care. The AI companies will not assume liability and will simply brush off individual losses to growing pains, much as we see with autonomous driving, converting human suffering to a dollar figure to be paid off to survivors. We do not have this luxury.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0289♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AAMC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AAMC♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The AAMC1 welcomes this opportunity to comment on the request for information entitled⏎“Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care” 90⏎FR 60108 (December 23, 2025), issued by the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (collectively the⏎ASTP/ONC or the Department).⏎The AAMC commends the Department for its ongoing commitment to leverage technological⏎innovation to enhance patient care delivery. We look forward to ongoing collaboration with the⏎ASTP/ONC to develop a meaningful policy and regulatory framework to advance the adoption⏎and use of artificial intelligence (AI) to improve health outcomes for patients and communities,⏎reduce burden, and drive value in health care while fostering public trust and confidence in⏎technology innovations.⏎Our comments in response to the request for information follow.⏎1 The AAMC is a nonprofit association dedicated to improving the health of people everywhere through medical⏎education, clinical care, biomedical research, and community collaborations. Its members are all 162 U.S. medical⏎schools accredited by the Liaison Committee on Medical Education; 13 Canadian medical schools accredited by the⏎Committee on Accreditation of Canadian Medical Schools; nearly 500 academic health systems and teaching⏎hospitals, including Department of Veterans Affairs medical centers; and more than 70 academic societies. Through⏎these institutions and organizations, the AAMC leads and serves America’s medical schools, academic health⏎systems and teaching hospitals, and the millions of individuals across academic medicine, including more than⏎210,000 full-time faculty members, 99,000 medical students, 162,000 resident physicians, and 60,000 graduate⏎students and postdoctoral researchers in the biomedical sciences. Through the Alliance of Academic Health Centers⏎International, AAMC membership reaches more than 60 international academic health centers throughout five⏎regional offices across the globe.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 2⏎EXPERIENCE WITH AI TOOLS IN CLINICAL CARE⏎The Department seeks feedback on AI tools deployed in clinical care that have met or exceeded⏎performance and cost expectations, as well as where they have fallen short. Academic health⏎systems (AHSs) are at the forefront of innovation in healthcare. In fiscal year 2024, a significant⏎portion of our members reported full integration of certain AI initiatives: 29% for clinical⏎support tools, 25% for predictive analytics for patient care, 23% for AI diagnostics, 10% for⏎population health management, 9% for patient communication and education, and 5% for AI-⏎assisted surgery.2 As such, our members have experience exploring the use of AI and AI-based⏎tools to improve access to care, streamline administrative tasks, and assist practitioners as they⏎deliver safe, effective, high-quality care. However, at this early stage of use, our members report⏎challenges with measuring the performance of these tools relative to their costs.⏎Examples of Academic Medicine’s Adoption of AI⏎Administrative Tools⏎Academic health systems are using AI to streamline the administrative side of patient care.⏎Through the automation of documentation and administrative tasks, AI can help to reduce⏎clinician fatigue and the burden of documentation. These tools may allow clinicians to devote⏎more attention to meaningful interactions with patients during the clinical encounter. For⏎example, ambient listening and scribe technologies help to capture patient-provider⏎conversations and document relevant clinical information in the medical record.⏎Chart summarization, in-basket message labels, and chatbots that enable plain-language⏎responses to patient queries help to streamline clinical and administrative workflow. AI can⏎provide medical coding and billing assistance by analyzing clinical notes, and EHR data to⏎suggest accurate billing codes. Intake and referral platforms create searchable, actionable data to⏎aid triaging and routing patients for timely care. AI note readers can also extract key metrics⏎across a patient population that health systems can use to address systemic issues in health care.⏎Additionally, AI tools support prior authorization processing, claims denial management,⏎contracting, and operational tasks, including inpatient flow, surgical capacity, and scheduling.⏎Clinical Support Tools⏎AI clinical support tools help clinicians to make clinical decisions by analyzing clinical data⏎medical literature, imaging and patient information. Clinical support AI tools are less robust than⏎administrative solutions but are advancing in key areas, including imaging and radiology⏎support, evidence-based clinical decision support, predictive models for patient throughput and⏎discharge planning, streamlined e-consult communication between providers, and remote patient⏎monitoring. These tools do not replace clinician expertise but rather support clinician work and⏎increase efficiency by assisting with diagnosis and treatment decisions.⏎2 Teaching Hospital Characteristics, AAMC analysis of FY2024 American Hospital Association Annual Survey⏎Database. AAMC membership data, December 2025.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 3⏎Patient Facing Support and Communication⏎AI tools are able to assist with patient communication managing large volumes of patient⏎messages, drafting responses to routine portal questions (e.g. refills), triaging messages based on⏎clinical topic, and assisting with patient reminders for screening, imaging, and follow-up visits.⏎As well as creating an informational hospital course synthesis for discharge summaries. These⏎tools make it easier for providers to meet patients where they are while increasing meaningful⏎touchpoints.⏎Medical Education⏎Training programs are also incorporating AI into medical education. For example, AI-powered⏎simulators for training, adaptive real-time-feedback systems, AI-based analytic models that⏎quantify technical skills and performance dashboards provide longitudinal tracking of skills⏎improvement. These tools allow trainees to practice in a safe, judgement-free environment while⏎enhancing and refining their skill sets before entering a clinical setting.⏎Concerns with Finding True Return on Investment (ROI) with AI Adoption⏎Each of the use cases above require human review and interaction – none are truly autonomous.⏎Adoption of AI tools in health care requires significant governance and ongoing monitoring and⏎evaluation over the lifecycle of each tool and use case. AHSs are developing comprehensive⏎governance structures for the adoption of AI tools across their system. Adoption of AI requires⏎heavy initial investment, including legal support to ensure compliance with state and federal law⏎and risk management, reviewing vendor options for purchase, building and customizing tools,⏎infrastructure set-up, integration into existing electronic health record technology, testing and⏎validation of tools, training of clinicians and staff, data management (including privacy and⏎security), continued monitoring, and updates. Given the initial cost and operational effort⏎required to get these initiatives up and running, the return on investment is not always clear.⏎Broad uncertainty around reimbursement increases pressure on organizations to clearly⏎demonstrate ROI to support continued investments. And even if the AI tool can improve access⏎to care, the costs of implementing, upkeeping and maintenance may be prohibitive to long-term⏎use.⏎BARRIERS TO ADOPTION AND USE IN CLINICAL CARE⏎High Cost of AI Tools⏎The market is currently saturated with numerous costly, niche AI-tools. Evaluating these tools is⏎challenging for AHSs given the breadth of options. Implementing multiple AI tools can create a⏎fragmented system that does not work cohesively. As a result, organizations may attempt to⏎create a customized in-house tool, requiring specialized expertise and significant investment to⏎implement and maintain. In many cases, the ROI of AI-enabled tools may not be sufficient to⏎justify the large-scale adoption or long-term utilization of these tools.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 4⏎Liability Allocation Across All Actors⏎AI poses new challenges for medical malpractice liability. Traditional negligence frameworks⏎require identification of a party at fault, based on proof that their actions or inactions caused the⏎harm. However, when using AI, this is unlikely to be clear. AI functions in a novel manner and⏎unlike conventional technologies with a clear human creator and/or operator, some forms of AI⏎can generate new ideas, learning and operating dependently after deployment. Our existing⏎liability framework is based on precedent, and case law has not clearly established how the⏎courts would assign liability when working with AI tools. Utilizing AI in clinical care will⏎remain risky without a clear understanding of when providers and health systems assume⏎liability. This risk is amplified by the fact that users of AI tools do not always have access to the⏎data or other information needed to fully understand how the tools function. This is generally⏎because of the black box nature of AI tools and the protection of propriety information. For⏎health systems to assess and plan for the risk associated with using AI, courts or policymakers⏎must address these uncertainties. For example, states could update liability standards, given the⏎fact that AI does not neatly fall into the traditional negligence framework, to distribute fault⏎across actors (AI developers, EHR systems, providers) as is done in aviation when automation⏎fails. Ultimately there must be a clear balance and understanding of risks across actors to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI tools in clinical care delivery.⏎Patchwork of State Laws and Regulations⏎Without a regulatory framework at the federal level, states have begun to step in and fill the void⏎to set guardrails for the use of AI broadly and, in some cases, specifically for its use in health⏎care. The result is an increasingly patchwork system with states having a unique set of standards⏎and penalties. It is extremely burdensome for health systems, especially those that operate in⏎different states, to remain in compliance with differing requirements across states and for⏎vendors and developers of AI tools to comply across all states. Health systems will likely⏎consider the compliance demands for adopting AI tools and factor those costs into potential ROI⏎considerations.⏎Interoperability of Data Systems⏎Health IT vendor systems often house data in separate repositories in diverse, proprietary formats⏎which may not be able to exchange information effectively. And users are more likely to⏎interpret data differently when presented in different formats. These challenges are compounded⏎by data silos and a lack of transparency around how vendors train and validate algorithms,⏎making it difficult for end users to assess accuracy.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 5⏎Privacy and Security⏎As covered entities, AHSs must meet strict requirements for the storing and use of protected⏎health information (PHI) under HIPAA. AI tools possess significant challenges regarding⏎privacy and data security because of the black box nature of the underlying algorithms and⏎coding. It is difficult for health systems to guarantee that PHI has been sufficiently de-identified,⏎in part because as models become increasingly more sophisticated, it is possible to re-identify the⏎PHI. Furthermore, AHSs partnering with non-covered entities or vendors cannot execute control⏎over data once it is available beyond the contained health system. This is problematic because⏎non-covered entities do not have the same fiduciary responsibilities for PHI. Therefore, health⏎systems may prefer to contract with vendors (or develop tools in-house) in a manner to maintain⏎control of the tool’s access to PHI, while again balancing the added privacy and security costs⏎with any assessment of the tool’s ROI.⏎REIMBURSEMENT⏎ASTP/ONC seeks feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive and⏎ability to promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among⏎clinical care AI tool builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical⏎care.⏎Consider a Broad Framework for Establishing Different Payment Pathways for Different⏎Tools⏎AHSs depend on adequate reimbursement of services provided to spur continued innovation in⏎health care. CMS should ensure market basket updates for both the Inpatient Prospective⏎Payment System (IPPS), Outpatient Prospective Payment System (OPPS), and payment updates⏎for the Physician Fee Schedule (PFS) correct for past insufficient updates and better reflect the⏎cost of providing care to patients. Additionally, payment policies should be updated to establish a⏎framework that supports changes in care patterns driven by AI integration. This may include:⏎Expanding New Technology Add-on Payment (NTAP) for Hospitals⏎In 2001, CMS created NTAP, an add on payment for new medical services and technologies⏎used in the inpatient setting. To qualify for NTAP, the technology must be on the market for less⏎than 2-3 years, receive inadequate payment rates, provide substantial clinical improvements, and⏎satisfy certain FDA market authorization requirements. CMS should consider expanding NTAP⏎payments to include the outpatient setting to facilitate the adoption of AI As more services take⏎place in the outpatient setting, NTAP payments could increase investments and utilization of AI⏎to improve care in this setting. CMS should also consider reviewing specific technologies that⏎have been on the market for nearly three years to determine if the timeframe should be extended.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 6⏎Testing Outcome-Aligned Payments for Clinicians⏎CMS’s Innovation Center recently released a new voluntary alternative payment model that will⏎test outcome-aligned payment approaches to expand traditional Medicare patients’ access to⏎technology-supported care to improve health and manage chronic disease. The Advancing⏎Chronic Care with Effective, Scalable Solutions (ACCESS) Model will test payment to Part B-⏎enrolled providers who meet clinical targets for managing their patients’ qualifying conditions⏎across four clinical tracks. CMS will evaluate complementary payment incentives to traditional⏎Medicare fee-for-service (FFS) payment to better support novel technology-supported care, like⏎telehealth and Food and Drug Administration-authorized devices or software, that can help⏎people manage chronic conditions with continuous support beyond their doctor’s office.⏎As designed, the ACCESS model contains a broad prohibition from Medicare FFS⏎reimbursement for model participants for patients attributed to the model. This blanket⏎prohibition will dissuade most health care providers from participating in the model, as many⏎Medicare patients need health care services outside of those included within the condition-⏎specific outcome-aligned payments through the model. CMS should consider incorporating the⏎goal to promote novel technology-supported care by testing outcome aligned payments with a⏎more targeted FFS prohibition to allow for greater participation from providers with existing care⏎management relationships with patients. CMS could also consider value-based outcome aligned⏎payments tied to accountable metrics, for example, metrics within value-based purchasing⏎programs that reward acquiring and usage of AI tools that users have proven provide clinical⏎benefits and meaningful ROI.⏎Expanding as Appropriate Software as a Service Under the Physician Fee Schedule⏎Software-as-a-Service (SaaS) is algorithm-driven software utilized by clinicians to assist with⏎clinical assessments, including decision support intervention software. Medicare covers SaaS⏎under Part A or Part B that are included in a Medicare benefit category, not statutorily excluded,⏎reasonable and necessary and approved or cleared by the FDA. As appropriate, CMS could⏎consider creating new codes to expand payment for AI-enabled SaaS. However, given the⏎current proliferation of codes and burden associated with valuing each SaaS, we caution the⏎heavy reliance on new codes.⏎RESEARCH & DEVELOPMENT⏎ASTP/ONC seeks input on the ways in which the Department may invest in research &⏎development to integrate AI into care delivery.⏎Research and Implementation Science to Improve Data Hygiene⏎Data hygiene is integral to ensure that AI tools and algorithms are accurate when supporting⏎clinical care and health care operations. An AI tool’s outputs are unreliable and potentially⏎dangerous if the underlying data is flawed or inoperable. This includes data that is incomplete,⏎⏎Assistant Secretary Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 7⏎outdated, mislabeled, duplicative, biased or otherwise inaccurate. As discussed above, data⏎stored in inconsistent formats can increase the risk of differential interpretations and can make it⏎difficult to effectively communicate across different systems. HHS should consider supporting or⏎leading research and implementation science to improve underlying data reliability and⏎interoperability that feeds into AI systems. This could include, for example, standardized quality⏎assurance standards for data collections and utilization, as well as testing algorithmic bias using⏎benchmarks to determine how AI performance fluctuates under different circumstances.⏎Research to Develop Sounds Frameworks for Ongoing Monitoring and Evaluation⏎AI systems require ongoing monitoring and evaluation to ensure that they function as intended⏎and meet clinical objectives. One study found that 91% of models degrade over time when⏎conditions shift or change.3 Significant investment in implementation science, studying how to⏎successfully integrate AI into clinical workflow, is necessary to improve metrics and to foster⏎ongoing trust in AI. This could include assessing outcomes of clinicians who use AI-assisted⏎clinical decision-making tools to determine if clinicians achieve reduced morbidity or mortality⏎rates, costs, or administrative burden. Or developing best practices for ongoing validation that⏎detects issues such as drift detection that alerts users if the AI’s performance is diverging from its⏎original training data.⏎CONCLUSION⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback on this critically import topic to inform the⏎Department’s future policymaking. We would be happy to work with CMS on any of the issues⏎discussed or other topics that involve the academic medicine community. If you have questions⏎regarding our comments, please feel free to contact Phoebe Ramsey (pramsey@aamc.org) and⏎Ki Rosenstein (krosenstein@aamc.org).⏎Sincerely,⏎Jonathan Jaffery, M.D., M.S., M.M.M., F.A.C.P.⏎Chief Health Care Officer⏎cc: David Skorton, M.D., AAMC President and Chief Executive Officer⏎3 Vela, D., et al. Temporal quality degradation in AI models. Scientific Reports (July 2022).♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0290♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Radiology Partners, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Radiology Partners♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Re: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Radiology Partners appreciates the opportunity to submit comments to the request for information on⏎“Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care” (Docket No. HHS-⏎ONC-2026-0001-0001) published by the Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ONC) (collectively, ASTP/ONC), Department of Health and Human Services (HHS).⏎Radiology Partners, through its affiliated practices, is a leading technology-enabled radiology practice in⏎the United States, serving more than 3,400 healthcare facilities with high quality radiology and⏎technology, including multiple artificial intelligence (AI) solutions. As a physician-led and physician-⏎owned practice, our mission is to transform radiology by innovating across clinical value, technology,⏎service and economics, while elevating the role of radiology and radiologists in healthcare. Radiology⏎Partners, through its affiliated practices, provides consistent, differentiated care to patients, while⏎delivering enhanced value to the hospitals, clinics, imaging centers and the referring physicians we⏎serve.⏎Radiology is at the cutting edge of AI technology, serving as one of the earliest medical specialties to⏎integrate AI into clinical practice. For nearly a decade, radiologists have used AI tools to assist with⏎image interpretation and streamline workflows. This early and ongoing integration has positioned⏎radiology as an example for other fields seeking to integrate AI effectively. Over the past few years,⏎Radiology Partners has led the largest clinical deployment of AI in radiology and healthcare more⏎broadly. With this depth of experience, we have continually refined our processes for developing,⏎validating, deploying, and monitoring AI models and welcome the opportunity to share these insights⏎with HHS.⏎We address HHS’ requests for information on AI regulation, reimbursement, and research &⏎development and its specific questions through the lens and experience of a radiology practice that has⏎deployed AI models at scale. While radiology-specific examples are used, the principles and processes⏎described apply broadly across clinical specialties.⏎Specific Questions:⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Regulatory Barriers: One of the most significant barriers to AI innovation and adoption is the⏎misalignment between current regulatory frameworks and how modern AI systems function in real-⏎world clinical care, particularly the reliance on finding-level, pre-market validation requirements that do⏎not scale to foundation or generative models designed to interpret entire imaging studies across over a⏎thousand possible findings. The current regulatory system disproportionately rewards AI discovery over⏎delivery and does not support the infrastructure required for safe and scaled AI deployment in the⏎1⏎⏎hospital ecosystem. In addition, the regulatory framework relies heavily on pre-market clinical validation⏎methods conducted in controlled settings that often fail to reflect real-world performance, usability, and⏎workflow integration in the clinical environments where tools are deployed. While these studies are⏎intended to ensure safety and efficacy before clinical use, they often do not account for the real-world⏎variability in patient populations, imaging protocols, exam types, clinical workflows, and physician⏎interpretation. As a result, the current approach can slow innovation and inflate costs without⏎proportionally improving safety because it relies on controlled test performance rather than longitudinal⏎performance monitoring in live clinical environments.⏎Infrastructure Gap: Many hospitals and clinical settings lack the technical, operational, and human⏎capital systems required to implement AI and other advanced technologies. They need:⏎• Modern data infrastructure to handle imaging and clinical data at scale, both of which are⏎unstructured;⏎• Interoperability across hospital technology and data platforms including electronic health record⏎(EHR) and imaging systems (e.g., Picture Archiving and Communication Systems (PACS),⏎Radiology Information System (RIS));⏎• Dedicated expert physician teams for AI validation, continuous monitoring, education, and⏎change management; and⏎• Formal partnerships with vendors to ensure transparency and clinical usability of the AI models⏎at the point of care.⏎Currently, each hospital must independently build this infrastructure, with no funding and few⏎guidelines. Without a clear reimbursement model or implementation roadmap, most health systems⏎cannot afford to engage and deploy AI solutions, even when the innovation has clear, clinical promise.⏎Many AI tools improve efficiency, quality, and patient safety but do not align with payment models,⏎leaving provider groups and hospital systems to absorb implementation, validation, and monitoring⏎costs without corresponding financial support. This misalignment discourages investment in⏎infrastructure and disproportionately limits adoption of AI tools to well-resourced systems.⏎Data Availability and Standardization Challenges: Radiology data is often highly heterogeneous, with⏎wide variation across healthcare systems, protocols, PACS configurations, and reporting systems. These⏎differences create meaningful barriers during AI development, deployment, integration, and monitoring.⏎In addition, there are critical gaps in data availability, particularly robust pediatric imaging datasets⏎paired with accurate, clinically detailed radiology reports, limiting the ability to develop and validate⏎robust AI models in these populations. While large language models (LLMs) can help extract and⏎structure information from free-text reports, they are not sufficiently consistent, transparent, or reliable⏎to replace standardized data capture and governance in high-stakes clinical environments. This work⏎remains resource-intensive, particularly when supporting thousands of facilities with heterogeneous and⏎evolving data environments. Without broader adoption of data standards, benchmarking frameworks,⏎and interoperability requirements, data variability and data scarcity will remain major barriers to⏎innovation and generalizable AI performance.⏎AI Model Transparency: A critical barrier to AI adoption is insufficient user-facing transparency and⏎traceability of the AI model at the point of care. When physicians do not have access to essential⏎contextual information, such as intended use, known limitations, and any clinically meaningful impacts,⏎physicians may struggle to appropriately calibrate trust in the AI’s output. This lack of visibility increases⏎the risk of human-AI interaction errors, including automation bias (accepting AI false positive results)⏎and complacency (accepting AI false negative results), and can undermine physician confidence in the⏎2⏎⏎tool. These transparency gaps make it difficult for physicians to distinguish between true model output,⏎missing or pending output, and inferred content, ultimately slowing adoption and limiting safe, reliable⏎use of AI in routine clinical workflows. HHS could encourage public trust by mandating a “model card”⏎system whereby a tool could provide a clear summary of its performance across granular demographics⏎(e.g., age, gender, socioeconomic status). This would allow clinicians to determine quickly if a model is⏎trustworthy for use in local patient populations and would create an equitable solution that allows a⏎range of institutions to decide which AI solutions best fit their needs and create trusted toolkits.⏎Human Factors and Physician Trust Barriers: AI adoption depends on physician trust and appropriate⏎trust calibration. Distrust can be common early in deployment, particularly with novel vendors,⏎unfamiliar workflows, or new model types, and may lead radiologists to dismiss accurate clinically⏎impactful AI findings. Over time, as familiarity grows, the risk can shift toward over-reliance on the AI⏎tool, including automation bias and complacency. Supporting safe and effective use requires more than⏎technical model performance; it requires implementation practices that help clinicians understand how⏎the tool fits into their clinical workflow, what specific value it provides, and where it may underperform.⏎This calibration requires initial training, transparent communication of limitations and failure modes,⏎and ongoing monitoring and feedback. Importantly, governance frameworks should support monitoring⏎not only of the accuracy of the AI model output, but also clinician interaction patterns (e.g., override⏎rates, reliance trends, and recurrent error types), as these behaviors directly influence patient safety.⏎Trust can be lost quickly after a single, unexpected failure, particularly when failure modes are not⏎transparent, anticipated, or detectable at the point of care.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Regulatory Policy Changes:⏎1. Enable Life-Cycle Based Oversight for Clinical AI: Current regulatory pathways emphasize pre-⏎market clinical validation in controlled settings that do not reflect real-world variability and are not⏎scalable to modern foundation and generative models. HHS should collaborate with the FDA to⏎establish a lifecycle-based oversight framework that explicitly accommodates iterative model⏎updates and continuous learning systems. This could take the form of:⏎• A conditional authorization pathway that permits deployment with a defined post-market⏎surveillance plan (analogous to EU’s Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) lifecycle approach).⏎• Requirements for real-world performance reporting (case mix, population subgroups, workflow⏎settings) as part of periodic reauthorization.⏎• Publication of performance drift and safety metrics as part of post-market reports.⏎Relevant policy anchor:⏎• 21 CFR Part 807 (Establishment Registration and Device Listing) and 21 CFR Part 820 (Quality⏎System Regulation) could be extended with lifecycle surveillance expectations appropriate for⏎clinical AI.⏎• FDA’s “Total Product Lifecycle” program for certain devices already conceptually supports⏎structured post-market activities; this should be expanded to cover AI/ML systems.⏎There is currently no FDA-defined, standardized postmarket monitoring framework for radiology AI,⏎which creates challenges for both vendors and providers. A successful approach must avoid creating⏎3⏎⏎unnecessary documentation burdens or encouraging unvalidated, adhoc reporting. At the same time,⏎responsibility for monitoring should not rest solely with vendors. Because radiology AI tools are used⏎under the supervision of U.S. board certified radiologists, real world oversight naturally occurs as⏎providers adjust how often they use a tool when the tool’s performance does not meet expectations.⏎Accordingly, any federal framework should focus on structured, validated mechanisms for postmarket⏎monitoring that balance accountability with practicality and ensure that feedback is clinically⏎meaningful. This approach would support safety while keeping monitoring feasible and proportional to⏎real world clinical workflows.⏎2. Mandate Transparency and Explainability Standards at the Point of Care: To improve clinician⏎understanding and mitigate human–AI interaction errors (automation bias, complacency, under-⏎reliance), the FDA should require user-facing transparency at the point of care. AI outputs should⏎not be limited to raw predictions; they must include contextual information such as:⏎• Model version and release notes.⏎• Confidence scores.⏎• Images and data elements used in inference (which series, which slices for the current and, if⏎applicable, prior exam).⏎• Explicit identification of excluded studies and reasons (e.g., outside cleared indications).⏎• Status information (received, in process, complete, rejected).⏎These elements should be standardized and interoperable using established frameworks such as⏎Integrating the Healthcare Enterprise (IHE) AI profiles.⏎Relevant policy anchor:⏎• 21 CFR 803 (Medical Device Reporting) could be supplemented with requirements for⏎contextualized outputs to improve traceability and post-market auditability.⏎3. Harmonize Cross-Agency Data Standards to Reduce Redundancy: From the perspective of a⏎radiology group responsible for deploying AI across diverse clinical settings, the lack of consistent⏎definitions and data standards across CMS, FDA, and ONC leads to duplicative burdens for providers⏎and limits safe AI implementation. HHS should initiate a coordinated cross-agency effort to align⏎terminologies, metadata requirements, and semantic definitions for clinical data and imaging⏎standards (e.g., RadLex, LOINC, SNOMED, DICOM tags). This effort would reduce variability and⏎support AI generalizability.⏎Relevant policy anchor:⏎• 45 CFR Part 170 (Health IT) and ONC Health IT Certification Program criteria should explicitly⏎incorporate semantic standard requirements for AI readiness.⏎4. Modernize HIPAA for AI-Relevant Use Cases: Current HIPAA de-identification requirements obstruct⏎multi-institutional aggregation of rich datasets for training, validation, and ongoing monitoring. HHS⏎should issue updated guidance or rulemaking clarifying the following:⏎• Safe, standardized de-identification for AI research and validation.⏎• Conditions for limited datasets that include necessary metadata elements (protocols,⏎demographics) while protecting privacy.⏎Relevant policy anchor:⏎4⏎⏎• 45 CFR Part 164 Subpart E (Privacy of Individually Identifiable Health Information), particularly⏎§§164.514(b)–(c), should be revisited with AI research requirements in mind.⏎Payment Policy:⏎Current payment models are structurally misaligned with AI-enabled care. Today’s Medicare Physician⏎Fee Schedule (PFS) evaluates and reimburses discrete procedural tasks. On the other hand, AI models⏎often require radiologists to review, validate, and adjudicate AI-generated insights, increasing effort and⏎cognitive load, creating value that the current PFS cannot recognize or reimburse appropriately.⏎The current budget neutrality requirement within the PFS is a major structural barrier to incorporating⏎AI-enabled cognitive work into the Medicare payment system. Because new services must be offset by⏎cuts elsewhere, introducing AI-enabled cognitive work into the PFS would force reductions in unrelated⏎physician services. For AI to deliver meaningful clinical benefit, HHS must consider alternative payment⏎approaches outside the PFS and outside traditional payment mechanisms.⏎5. Support Infrastructure and Adoption Through Incentive Programs: AI deployment requires robust⏎infrastructure (secure data pipelines, monitoring processes, clinician training) that is not currently⏎reimbursable. HHS should create a voluntary, incentive program for AI adoption that funds:⏎• Data infrastructure upgrades (scalable de-identification, secure inference pipelines).⏎• Local validation and governance workflows.⏎• Continuous monitoring systems that capture real-world performance metrics.⏎• AI training.⏎This burden is especially heavy when hospitals are expected to fund these changes based solely on⏎potential, indirect return on investment (ROI), such as reduced length of stay. Given the foundational⏎limitations of the PFS, federal investment should reflect the reality that advanced AI tools reshape⏎clinical workflows, clinical decision-making, and diagnostic quality by rewarding value-based outcomes.⏎A federal incentive program modeled could accelerate adoption, fund necessary infrastructure, and⏎establish baseline best practices for efficient, safe and effective AI deployment. By supporting⏎foundational infrastructure, such a program can mitigate reliance on direct reimbursement for individual⏎AI models, bypassing the complexities of the existing healthcare payment system while supporting AI⏎adoption, guided by best practices. It would also reward hospitals for adopting best practices in AI⏎deployment, including building the necessary infrastructure and processes for safe and scalable⏎implementation, optimization of physician-AI collaboration, and maintenance of model accuracy over⏎time.⏎Possible policy anchor:⏎• CMS Innovation Center (CMMI) authority to test new payment and delivery models could be⏎used to pilot AI infrastructure support programs.⏎Programmatic Design:⏎6. Integrate Explainability and Workflow Design into Performance Goals: For AI to deliver clinical⏎value, tools must integrate seamlessly into radiologist workflows. Programmatic support should⏎encourage:⏎• Usability testing and human factors engineering as part of AI validation.⏎• User interfaces that present relevant contextual information without overload (e.g., confidence⏎flags, region highlights).⏎5⏎⏎• Explainability outputs tailored to clinical needs (categorical likelihood cues rather than opaque⏎numeric scores).⏎7. Mandate Ongoing User Training, Education, and Feedback: Effective AI use goes beyond initial⏎buttonology. HHS should support programs for:⏎• Continuous clinician education tied to model updates.⏎• Feedback loops between clinicians and developers.⏎• Monitoring of clinician–AI interaction patterns (override rates, drift in reliance).⏎• Conditioned funding tied to evidence of safe and appropriate adoption.⏎Clinical education focuses on the AI model’s capabilities, limitations, and appropriate use within the⏎radiologist’s workflow. Training is most effective when it is continuous, with refreshers provided when⏎new model versions are deployed or when monitoring metrics indicate that a radiologist may be at⏎increased risk for distrust, automation bias or complacency. We have observed cases in which hospitals⏎implemented AI tools, but radiologists were unaware of their availability or were uncertain how to use⏎them effectively. Ongoing clinical education ensures clinicians understand both the value of the tool and⏎its boundaries. Group training, videos, and in-app education are effective mechanisms for delivering⏎continuous training at scale, but they cannot yet fully replace initial, individualized instruction for more⏎complex tools.⏎8. Standardize Communication and Feedback Mechanisms: Program design should support multiple⏎communication modalities tied to clinician workflows (in-app, dashboards, alerts) to reinforce⏎updates and performance insights, which is a best practice for safe adoption.⏎Why These Changes Matter: Taken together, these regulatory, payment, and programmatic reforms⏎would:⏎• Align oversight with real-world clinical environments.⏎• Reduce administrative burden through harmonized standards.⏎• Incentivize hospital investment in safe, scalable AI infrastructure.⏎• Improve clinician trust and appropriate use through transparency and training.⏎• Enable continuous monitoring and longitudinal evaluation of real-world performance.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Non-medical device AI tools, including generative AI used for documentation and workflow support,⏎may create novel governance challenges because accountability, liability, and oversight expectations are⏎not well defined. Current frameworks place traditional malpractice liability at odds with product‑liability⏎pathways, creating uncertainty about how responsibility should be allocated when AI outputs influence⏎clinical decision‑making through triage or administrative functions. These gaps are compounded by⏎inconsistent vendor indemnification terms, limited transparency into model updates and data handling,⏎and insufficient auditability (e.g., versioning, traceability, and logging of inputs used to generate⏎outputs). Privacy and security concerns are also heightened when protected health information is⏎transmitted to external AI services, and existing privacy frameworks were developed before today’s⏎large-scale analytics and foundation models.⏎6⏎⏎HHS can help by issuing fit-for-purpose guidance and baseline requirements that enable safe⏎implementation without imposing burdensome pre-market documentation or slowing clinical⏎deployment. As part of this, HHS should work with the Office of the General Counsel to create a safe⏎harbor for algorithm transparency, such as providing federal guardrails that clarify responsibility for⏎AI‑influenced decisions while ensuring clinicians remain the final authority and AI serves as a supportive⏎tool. HHS should also modernize privacy and security expectations by developing interoperability and⏎data‑handling standards that move beyond basic encryption to include federated learning approaches⏎and national de‑identification landmarks. This “gold standard” would strengthen data stewardship while⏎reducing the cost of repetitive, vendor‑specific security audits.⏎Finally, HHS should consider establishing a unified AI risk framework grounded in “safety‑by‑design”⏎principles. Such a framework would differentiate requirements for high‑risk clinical AI versus lower‑risk⏎administrative tools, reducing unnecessary compliance burden while ensuring appropriate safeguards.⏎By clarifying liability, strengthening privacy protections, and aligning oversight with actual risk, HHS can⏎support scalable, clinically responsible adoption of non‑medical device AI while maintaining clinician‑led⏎decision‑making and patient safety, allowing organizations to focus on clinical outcomes instead of⏎administrative compliance.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎For non-medical device AI tools used in clinical care, such as generative AI used for documentation and⏎workflow support, the most promising evaluation methods focus on real world usability, reliability,⏎human factors behavior, and longitudinal performance, rather than traditional medical device validation⏎metrics. Models deployed in live environments encounter real-world variability due to differing hospital⏎settings and scanner types which do not match the testing environment. To harden models against real-⏎world variability and diagnostic drift, HHS should consider prioritizing standardized robustness training,⏎including variations in documentation style, clinical vocabulary, exam types, and workflow interruptions.⏎HHS should encourage joint governance model centered on the Chief Medical Information Officer⏎(CMIO) and Chief Quality Officer (CQO)/Quality & Safety committees. Evaluation and monitoring should⏎extend beyond simple accuracy and sensitivity metrics to focus on overall clinical utility in real-world⏎scenarios.⏎Measuring human-centered safety metrics help mitigate diagnostic drift as the model experiences⏎changing clinical protocols or gets exposed to varying patient populations. Safety metrics, such as⏎override rates and alert fatigue levels, can be used to assess whether clinicians are using the tool as a⏎true decision support tool or if the tool is a distraction adding to the overall cognitive load, which could⏎lead to “near miss” events and patient safety concerns. Measuring real-time discordance rates between⏎AI outputs and human assessment can proactively identify performance gaps and biases allowing quality⏎and safety teams to proactively mitigate against any effects on patient care ensuring that “Human plus⏎AI” model remains a robust safeguard. HHS can accelerate this by funding standardized safety⏎dashboards through grants and cooperative agreements, enabling CMIO/CQO leaders to monitor⏎performance across sites using actionable indicators such as alert‑to‑action ratios (how often AI outputs⏎actually change management). Support for these standardized approaches should occur through⏎7⏎⏎cooperative agreements that provide the AI industry with standardized approaches that center on⏎providing positive impact on the clinician's decision making and not on technical speed of the enterprise.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can accelerate safe and effective AI adoption by supporting private-sector accreditation,⏎certification, and credentialing programs that address the gap between FDA authorization and real-⏎world clinical use. While FDA review evaluates whether an AI tool is safe and effective for its intended⏎use, real-world performance and patient safety are strongly influenced by implementation quality,⏎workflow integration, clinician training, and ongoing monitoring. These factors are not consistently⏎addressed today and vary widely across healthcare systems and vendors. To address this, HHS can⏎support the private sector by moving away from static certification models to continuous clinical⏎accreditation. One-time approval does not account for inevitable diagnostic drift of the model as it⏎deploys in real-world scenarios and could negatively affect disparate patient populations without⏎safeguards in place. Therefore, we recommend HHS incentivize models that demonstrate and prioritize⏎real-time ongoing safety monitoring. By supporting such platforms, HHS can ensure that AI tools remain⏎continuously safe and reliable throughout the entire lifecycle of the product.⏎A high-impact opportunity is defining and supporting credentialing for qualified end users of healthcare⏎AI. For imaging AI, safe use depends on clinicians who understand the model’s intended use, limitations,⏎common failure modes, and appropriate reliance behaviors. Existing professional society guidance,⏎including the American College of Radiology’s (ACR) AI policy, emphasizes the importance of a specialist⏎human in the loop and appropriate user training. Beyond end-user training, innovation is also⏎constrained by the lack of standardized, high-quality reliable datasets for testing AI tools prior to⏎deployment. To address this barrier, HHS could consider cooperative agreements to fund secure, de-⏎identified data banks where responsible AI developers can test model performance against real-world⏎data from multiple institutions. Federal support for these shared testing environments would reduce⏎barriers to innovation, maintain a high bar for performance equity, and shift industry practices toward a⏎“safety-first” environment where evaluation centers on real patient outcomes rather than idealized⏎laboratory datasets. Supporting the development of standardized competency expectations for AI users⏎and representative testing frameworks would strengthen accountability and ensure AI tools are safe and⏎effective in clinical settings, all in alignment with CMS quality frameworks and centered on real patient⏎outcomes.⏎In parallel, HHS could support organizational-level accreditation models for AI governance maturity. This⏎would recognize health systems that implement core best practices, such as local validation,⏎transparency and auditability, post-deployment monitoring, incident reporting, and ongoing education.⏎HHS support could include grants, cooperative agreements, and public-private partnerships to develop⏎standardized evaluation frameworks, shared monitoring approaches, and scalable training resources.⏎In addition, HHS could encourage development of new professional credentialing standards focusing on⏎Chief Medical Information Officers (CMIOs) and Quality/Safety committees in ensuring the clinical⏎effectiveness of AI models. Proper use of AI tools is a true clinical skill, not just technical, and⏎credentialing should therefore include assessment of automation bias and methods to manage “human⏎plus AI” workflows. Specifically, HHS could consider awarding grants to professional societies to develop⏎explainability standards and training modules that support responsible AI stewardship. This would help⏎shift the culture away from IT-centric deployment toward clinician-led human-factors engineering,⏎8⏎⏎improving adoption, trust, and safety. Such an approach could elevate, rather than replace, the⏎professional abilities of board-certified clinicians by ensuring AI serves as a supportive tool within⏎well-governed clinical workflows.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest⏎potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎In our experience, the AI tools that have most consistently met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations are foundation-model workflow tools that help manage today’s exponentially higher⏎clinical intensity and cognitive load due to high resolution multisequence datasets. In radiology, this⏎includes generative AI systems that support end-to-end reporting, not only narrow single-finding⏎algorithms, and synthesize thousands of imaging data points into coherent, actionable insights. By⏎reducing cognitive burden and structuring information at the point of interpretation, these tools enable⏎greater diagnostic precision, consistency across variable studies, and clearer communication of clinical⏎reasoning.⏎Two foundation-model tools have been particularly impactful. Mosaic Reporting, an ambient reporting⏎tool built on large language models, enables radiologists to create structured reports with fewer words,⏎dictate in any order, and use natural language commands and embedded macros within templates.⏎Mosaic Drafting, a home-grown vision-language model (VLM), generates a full draft report for the entire⏎study (including comparison to priors), functioning like an “AI resident” that shifts the radiologist from⏎report creation to oversight and validation.⏎AI tools most commonly fall short when implementation and human factors are underestimated,⏎including poor workflow integration, limited transparency into model behavior, and insufficient training⏎and monitoring to prevent under-use or over-reliance. The most promising next-generation tools will⏎combine foundation-model capabilities with robust post-deployment monitoring, transparency, and⏎governance frameworks to ensure safe and scalable real-world performance.⏎HHS should consider prioritizing the following:⏎• Emphasize AI’s role in managing data complexity to ensure diagnostic precision and move away⏎from pure time savings metrics.⏎• Recognize prospective drift detection and mitigation to avoid downstream costs from missed or⏎delayed diagnoses⏎• Support payment and research models that recognize the cognitive effort required by an expert⏎to validate AI generated analysis and insight and that value diagnostic certainty as a measure of⏎quality.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Within health care organizations, the roles most critical to successful AI adoption are those that bridge⏎clinical leadership and technology oversight. Historically, AI implementation has been driven primarily⏎by IT departments, which are important backbones to organizations but are not always positioned to⏎evaluate clinical utility, workflow fit, or patient-centered outcomes. To accelerate responsible adoption,⏎organizations should shift decision-making toward combined clinical leadership models, where roles,⏎9⏎⏎such as the Chief Medical Information Officer (CMIO), Chief Quality Officer (CQO), and relevant clinical⏎department leaders jointly oversee AI evaluation, deployment, and governance. This governance⏎framework ensures that AI investments are aligned with patient-centered outcomes and professional⏎workforce needs rather than driven by hardware. Empowering clinician-led governance also strengthens⏎trust and supports thoughtful adoption grounded in real-world care, not technical capability alone.⏎Radiologists today face significant cognitive overload due to fragmented workflows (i.e., the need to⏎switch between multiple AI interfaces layered on top of the primary diagnostic workstation). Such⏎fragmentation can erode efficiency, increase error risk, and stall adoption. To reduce these⏎administrative hurdles, HHS should consider promoting deep workflow integration models that embed⏎AI tools directly into existing clinical systems. Because current vendor ecosystems rely on⏎non-standardized Application Programming Interfaces (APIs), organizations often struggle with⏎interoperability barriers that prevent seamless integration of third-party AI outputs. HHS could support⏎policies that standardize universal API requirements to reduce interoperability friction and penalize⏎vendors who restrict the incorporation of third-party AI insights into diagnostic viewers and cause⏎blockage of information transfer.⏎A final administrative hurdle comes from the cumulative burden of contracting, security reviews, and⏎legal negotiations required each time a hospital adopts a new AI tool. These repetitive processes can⏎delay deployment of life-saving AI capabilities despite clear evidence of patient benefit. To streamline⏎adoption while maintaining strong safeguards, HHS could consider a platform model where hospitals⏎gain access to a vetted “AI gateway” under a master agreement. From this gateway, organizations could⏎select among multiple AI tools without initiating a full contracting and security cycle for each product.⏎This approach would reduce paperwork, improve speed-to-implementation, and ensure that both large⏎academic centers and smaller rural hospitals can equitably access advanced AI tools without⏎disproportionate administrative burden.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards,⏎and benchmarking tools.⏎HHS should recognize interoperability as a mandatory safety guardrail for clinical AI. In the current⏎framework, AI outputs are often trapped in application or vendor-specific silos, which limits auditability,⏎hinders cross-site learning, and weakens longitudinal safety monitoring. To accelerate reliable, equitable⏎adoption, policy should shift from episodic data exchange between healthcare entities to semantic⏎liquidity where AI outputs and supporting context are encoded in shared, computable formats that flow⏎with the patient and remain actionable across systems, while remaining fully compliant with HIPAA⏎privacy and security requirements. HHS should also consider the use of Fast Healthcare Interoperability⏎Resources (FHIR) native AI results, which would make outputs versioned, auditable, and actionable⏎within EHR workflows, strengthening traceability and supporting consistent physician use. Linking⏎imaging metadata with longitudinal outcomes can prevent diagnostic drift.⏎HHS can also work with the FDA to allow “safe harbors” for organizations to aggregate de-identified⏎data, which would allow for the development of national benchmarks for AI performance and safety⏎reflecting real-world diversity without increasing regulatory burden.⏎There is an opportunity for HHS and the FDA to support a framework for continuous performance⏎monitoring where developers can safely test their models against real-time institutional data. This⏎10⏎⏎approach would give developers visibility into how models perform across diverse patient populations,⏎imaging equipment, and care settings, ensuring that health equity is a focus on initial development.⏎Overall, HHS can complement its current focus on interoperability by emphasizing clinical quality and⏎safety that promotes the development of reliable, equitable tools.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a) Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b) How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize AI research that reflects real-world clinical environments rather than static,⏎historical test conditions. Traditional validation using retrospective datasets does not capture the⏎variability introduced by differences in patient populations, imaging protocols, scanners, workflows, and⏎clinical context. To accelerate safe and effective adoption, HHS should support prospective, multi-center⏎clinical trials that evaluate AI tools in live workflows, including how clinicians interact with them in⏎routine care. This research should emphasize human-in-the-loop integration, evaluating how AI⏎maximizes diagnostic accuracy and improves turnaround times when used as a dynamic member of the⏎care team. This research would allow for HHS to establish long-term evidence for reimbursement and⏎increase trust for patients and providers⏎In addition, HHS should emphasize research into human factors engineering, including how User⏎Interface (UI) design influences clinician’s critical thinking; this would mitigate long-term effects of⏎automation bias. An example of this research includes measuring the optimal amount of data that⏎should be available to the clinician without causing “alert fatigue” and information overload. This⏎research would ensure that the AI remains a clinical decision support tool and not a replacement for⏎clinician judgment.⏎We also recommend that HHS develop a national continuous quality performance monitoring platform⏎to prevent diagnostic drift and algorithmic bias across wide ranges of demographics and imaging⏎hardware. HHS can also consider developing a national benchmarking clearinghouse to help ensure⏎equitable AI performance, regardless of local technology or demographics. Overall, a coordinated⏎federal research agenda, spanning clinical trials, human-factors studies, performance-drift monitoring,⏎and national benchmarking, would establish the evidence base needed to guide reimbursement,⏎strengthen clinician and patient trust, and ensure AI is deployed safely, equitably, and effectively across⏎the healthcare system⏎Conclusion⏎Radiology Partners appreciates the opportunity to provide comments in response to HHS’ request. As⏎HHS advances its “OneHHS” AI Strategy and seeks experience-based input to guide regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research, radiology’s long-standing use of AI offers practical insights to support this⏎effort. We respectfully encourage HHS to continue engaging with the radiology community as HHS⏎advances a forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and⏎use of AI. We look forward to continued partnership as HHS refines federal policy to advance⏎responsible AI adoption across clinical care.⏎11⏎⏎Respectfully Submitted,⏎Dr. Nina Kottler, MD, MS, FSIIM, FAIM⏎Chief Medical AI Officer, Mosaic Clinical Technologies @Radiology Partners⏎Justine Jamero⏎Associate Director, Health Policy & Advocacy⏎12♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0291♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rural Policy Research Institute, College of Public Health, University of Iowa, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔RUPRI Health Panel⏎Keith J. Mueller, PhD, Chair⏎Alva O. Ferdinand, DrPH, JD⏎Mark Holmes, PhD⏎Alana D. Knudson, PhD⏎Jennifer P. Lundblad, PhD, MBA⏎A. Clinton MacKinney, MD, MS⏎Timothy D. McBride, PhD⏎https://rupri.org/⏎February 20, 2026⏎Center for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎File Code: RIN 0955-AA13⏎By electronic submission at: http://www.regulations.gov⏎The Rural Policy Research Institute Health Panel (Panel) was established in 1993 to provide⏎science-based, objective policy analysis to federal lawmakers. The RUPRI Health Panel is⏎pleased to respond to this Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13). Artificial Intelligence applications⏎in clinical care and associated activities of healthcare organizations could contribute to reaching⏎the Panel’s vision of a High Performing Rural Health System, and a vision for the future of rural⏎health. Our comments in this response draw on a report posted on the website of the National⏎Advisory Committee on Rural Health and Human Services (NACRHHS); the RUPRI Center for⏎Rural Health Policy Analysis contributed to the content of that report.⏎The NACRHHS identified policy priorities in rural health system development that would benefit⏎from technologies, including use of AI (those that affect direct clinical care are highlighted):⏎• Improving transportation services in rural places, particularly for high-need patients⏎• Participating in new collaborations with other organizations (including community-based⏎human service organizations), driven by data analytics⏎• Negotiating payment rates with commercial insurance plans, including Medicare⏎Advantage plans and Medicaid Managed Care Organizations, and maximizing revenues⏎through improved documentation (coding, other information related to patient chronic⏎and episodic conditions)⏎• Using information technology to improve care management, particularly relevant in⏎optimizing revenue in new payment models through improved coordination across all⏎points of service delivery that contribute to sustained optimum health and well-being⏎• Adopting new care modalities in ways that enhance access to, and use of local services⏎across the continuum that includes clinical care and human services⏎• Providing in-home health and human services⏎• Implementing virtual care, including 24-hour nursing care⏎• Improving navigation of multiple points of entry into the continuum of health and human⏎services, making connections to the full array of potentially beneficial services regardless⏎of the entry point⏎Overall themes of the report included the following:⏎• Quality access to broadband services is a major factor and potential barrier – up to 45⏎million American lacked availability of quality broadband services as March 2024 (FCC⏎Press Release on March 2014)⏎• Implementing innovative technology requires leveraging existing alliances of hospitals⏎⏎2⏎• Technology to improve efficiency of clinicians is a means of coping with workforce⏎shortages (though far from sufficient) – ambient listening to populate health records⏎being a specific example⏎General Panel Comments⏎Technology can be an equalizer but often first exacerbates disparities that exist. It can reinforce⏎differences in access across geography. Specifically, technology that facilitates ease of access⏎for well-insured populations may divert encounters away from local providers who serve all⏎patients regardless of source of payment. That diversion could threaten financially viability of⏎local providers. While this phenomenon could occur in any underserved area (including parts of⏎urban areas), we call attention to potential unintended consequences in rural places. Intuitively,⏎these challenges could be addressed by a combination of assuring access to the new⏎technology for all residents regardless of source of payment and therefore precipitating an⏎adjustment to use patterns that enhances access for all, or by increasing other sources of⏎revenue for local providers to maintain their operations. We encourage further research and⏎analysist to anticipate effects, monitoring implementation of technology to identify all⏎consequences, and addressing unintended consequences through state and federal policies⏎regarding through payment (Medicare and Medicare) and insurance regulation,⏎Q5. Supporting private sector activities to promote innovative and effective use of AI⏎To promote industry-driven testing we suggest allowing government agencies to work with⏎private entities through cooperative research & development agreements, facilitating joint⏎access to and use of government databases. We also suggest developing specific criteria⏎focused on quality of care as AI products move from development to application. We specifically⏎recommend that any such regulations be based on evidence of successful application in rural⏎health care delivery. Analytical criteria should include affordability for healthcare organizations⏎and patients, utility of the innovative technology to rural healthcare organizations (this may be⏎function of scale and scope of services), and effects on long-term sustainability of access to⏎local services. Further, success should be a combination of improved health outcomes and⏎increased availability of services in rural communities.⏎Q6. AI tools with potential for greatest impact in rural settings.⏎As stated in reviewing themes from the NACRHHS, AI tools that enable clinicians to spend less⏎time in administrative tasks and increased time in patient care would impact access to quality⏎care in rural settings. Tools that facilitate communication between local primary care providers⏎and other specialty clinicians would have an impact, to support local care and to coordinate care⏎across the continuum through ease of patient transfer as necessary.⏎Q7. Administrative hurdles⏎Two hurdles are unique to small, independent rural healthcare organizations. First, access to⏎high-speed broadband capacity remains a challenge, both for some clinics and for millions of⏎patients in their homes. Second, the resources necessary to take full advantage of AI are⏎lacking, both to support equipment and software, and personnel (data analysts) to take full⏎advantage of new AI tools. Critically, administrative training and support will necessary to take⏎full advantage of all new reimbursement codes associated with new technologies and to mirror⏎systems used by payers such as claims review and pre-authorization.⏎Q9. Challenges within health care for rural patients and caregivers to address with AI⏎The following challenges could be addressed:⏎⏎3⏎▪ Generating shorter wait times: AI tools that facilitate patient check-in and other pre-⏎encounter administrative tasks could help reduce in-clinic wait times.⏎▪ More efficient and effective care: Efficiency and effectiveness could be improved with⏎tools that enhance access to the right information at the right time during patient⏎encounters.⏎Q9. Concerns related to the adoption and use of AI in rural settings⏎Rural healthcare patients often face long wait times, inefficient or ineffective care and high costs⏎of care. AI can be leveraged and developed to enhance the effectiveness and efficiency of the⏎healthcare delivered to rural patients which will shorten wait times and lower the cost of⏎healthcare. However, the integration AI into the healthcare industry must be conducted through⏎a careful and thought-out process. AI is a new and evolving technology. It can generate⏎hallucinations or false results. Additionally, the use of improperly tested AI applications in the⏎healthcare industry may lead to poorer health outcomes for patients. Further, enhanced use of⏎AI in healthcare may lead to a lack of patient interactions with doctors, and accessibility⏎concerns for patients who are less familiar with the technology. Finally, without aid from the⏎government rural healthcare systems may not be able to benefit as much from the⏎implementation of AI in healthcare when compared to urban areas due to a lack of infrastructure⏎capacity.⏎Q 10. Research and Development⏎Several systematic reviews published by the NIH have concluded that the use of AI in clinical⏎care settings can greatly enhance clinical decision-making processes and imaging/diagnosis⏎processes (https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10916499/). Further studies published by⏎the NIH demonstrate that the use of AI in variety of clinical settings not only achieved⏎comparable clinical outcomes to standard care but also created measurable improvements in⏎QALYS and other effectiveness metrics. Additionally, these studies state that the use of AI leads⏎to more efficient and effective care by enhancing clinical outcomes through improved diagnostic⏎accuracy and personalized treatment while simultaneously reducing costs by optimizing⏎resource allocation and streamlining care⏎(https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12381244/). However, there are costs to⏎implementing AI. First there is a lack of published research discussing the upfront capital⏎expenditures and integration costs required to implement AI in a clinical healthcare setting⏎(https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12381244/). Second, AI is an imperfect technology⏎therefore, the use of AI may lead false or potentially missed diagnoses⏎(https://www.nih.gov/news-events/news-releases/nih-findings-shed-light-risks-benefits-⏎integrating-ai-into-medical-decision-making). Finally, the use of AI in the clinical setting will lead⏎to social costs as some employee positions will be eliminated or greatly altered by the enhanced⏎use of AI in the clinical setting.⏎Concluding comments.⏎The RUPRI Health Panel recommends that any innovation in AI be fully vetted with rural⏎clinicians and patients before being endorsed for widespread use. We suggest a “rural proofing”⏎that accounts for challenges of implementing new technologies in a resource-poor environments⏎and that tests viability for the end users – clinicians and patients.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎⏎4⏎Keith J. Mueller, PhD⏎Chair, RUPRI Health Panel⏎University of Iowa⏎College of Public Health⏎145 N Riverside Drive⏎Iowa City, IA 52242⏎Keith-mueller@uiowa.edu⏎www.rupri.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0292♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Campbell♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔American Society of Plastic Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly, my main question after reading through this whole request is whether anyone actually looks at these comments in any systematic way. I've submitted three over the years through regulations.gov and never received so much as an automated response beyond the confirmation number. I don't say that to be dismissive of the process, I just genuinely don't know how HHS weighs input from a specialty surgical society against, say, a large health system or a major EHR vendor.⏎⏎That said, here is what I can offer from our perspective.⏎⏎The American Society of Plastic Surgeons has been watching AI diagnostic and imaging tools come into surgical subspecialties with real interest but also some unease. Our members use imaging analysis tools increasingly in preoperative planning, and some of the newer platforms promise a lot. But when our research team has tried to find documentation on how these tools were validated, specifically whether the training data included adequate representation of patients with darker skin tones or patients with prior reconstructive procedures, we come up mostly empty. One platform we reviewed in early 2024 had a technical document that ran to 47 pages and mentioned demographic breakdown of training data exactly once, in a footnote, without any actual numbers.⏎⏎So if HHS is asking what regulatory action would actually help: mandatory, plain-language disclosure of training dataset composition should be a baseline requirement before any clinical AI tool gets used in a reimbursable care setting. Not buried in a white paper. Something a clinician or a patient could actually find and read.⏎⏎I'll also say that the reimbursement question is complicated for surgical specialties in a way that might not be obvious. Plastic surgery already has unusual payer dynamics because of the mix of reconstructive and cosmetic procedures. If AI tools get reimbursed in ways that favor high-volume, high-revenue settings, smaller reconstructive programs serving Medicaid patients in places like rural New Mexico are going to fall even further behind on adoption. We've seen that pattern with other health IT investments and I don't see why AI would be different.⏎⏎The equity concern isn't abstract for us. A patient coming in for post-mastectomy reconstruction at a safety-net hospital in Albuquerque should have access to the same quality of AI-assisted preoperative imaging analysis as someone at a large academic center in Boston. Whether current reimbursement proposals would support that or undercut it, I genuinely can't tell from the materials HHS has put out so far.⏎⏎I realize this comment doesn't map neatly onto the specific question numbers in the RFI. I looked at the questions and found some of them a bit difficult to answer without knowing more about what HHS already has in mind on the reimbursement side.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0293♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔This is a bad idea. It may be good to double check basic lab data but I don’t see it being able to suss out subtle nuances that may affect treatment of certain individuals. ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0294♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ATA Action, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ATA Action♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎US Department of Health and Human Services⏎H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Avenue⏎SW Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: ATA Action Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎ATA Action, the American Telemedicine Association’s affiliated trade organization, appreciates the⏎opportunity to provide comments on the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎Our input reflects discussions among key stakeholders in the healthcare and technology sectors,⏎including clinicians, life sciences companies, regulatory experts, and AI developers. Collectively, these⏎stakeholders share a strong interest in ensuring that AI is deployed in ways that are safe, effective, and⏎scalable, and grounded in measurable real-world performance, while enabling continued innovation to⏎improve patient outcomes and access to care.⏎1. Need for a Federal Framework⏎We strongly support the development of a federal regulatory framework for AI in clinical care. The⏎current 50-state patchwork of laws, enforcement actions, and private rights of action creates confusion⏎and can stifle innovation. A clear, unified federal framework grounded in validated performance⏎standards and accountability mechanisms must be established to ensure consistent national⏎expectations for patient safety, transparency, and trust. Federal leadership is essential to provide⏎regulatory certainty and prevent fragmentation that impedes responsible innovation and adoption.⏎Without such clarity, developers and providers face unnecessary barriers to innovation, and patients⏎may not fully benefit from AI-enabled tools. A national framework would provide guidance for adoption⏎at scale and ensure that federal preemption supports, not undermines, safe, effective implementation⏎of AI across the healthcare system. Such a framework should align with existing federal authorities—⏎including FDA oversight, HIPAA protections, and ONC transparency requirements—while establishing⏎harmonized standards that allow AI tools to scale responsibly across care settings.⏎2. Regulatory Clarity⏎AI regulation should provide clear and actionable guidance to reduce uncertainty for developers,⏎providers, and patients. Specifically, regulations should clarify:⏎● Scope of Oversight: Oversight should be clearly defined based on the intended clinical function⏎and validated performance of an AI system, ensuring clarity about when federal regulatory⏎requirements apply and how those requirements interact with professional standards of care.⏎● Risk-Based vs. Outcome-Focused Approaches: Regulatory policy should prioritize outcome-⏎focused evaluation centered on measurable safety, reliability, and real-world clinical⏎performance. Oversight should scale according to validated evidence and demonstrated impact⏎rather than relying on static categorical distinctions. This approach supports innovation while⏎maintaining rigorous safeguards where patient outcomes are materially affected.⏎⏎● Safe Harbor Provisions: Clinicians and developers should have explicit protections when using⏎FDA-regulated AI devices as intended within clearly defined parameters, reducing liability⏎concerns and encouraging adoption of approved technologies. Safe harbors are particularly⏎important for emerging technologies, such as generative AI or adaptive algorithms, where⏎responsible use could otherwise be discouraged by regulatory uncertainty. HHS should establish⏎clear safe harbor protections for clinicians and developers who deploy AI tools in accordance⏎with FDA authorization, labeling, cybersecurity expectations, and recognized governance⏎frameworks (such as NIST AI Risk Management Framework and FDA Good Machine Learning⏎Practices). Safe harbors such as a right to cure period and rebuttable presumptions for⏎compliance with the aforementioned regulatory frameworks, would incentivize greater AI⏎innovation, adoption and improved safety. These protections will reduce uncertainty and⏎accelerate responsible adoption.⏎In addition, regulatory clarity should include defined obligations for ongoing quality assurance, post-⏎deployment monitoring, model updating, and mitigation of performance drift or bias. A sustainable⏎regulatory framework must recognize that AI performance can change over time and require structured⏎processes for validation and recalibration.⏎Clear regulatory guidance in these areas would promote trust, reduce fragmentation, and accelerate⏎safe, scalable adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎3. FDA Considerations⏎● Safe harbor/medical device carveouts: Clear guidance is needed regarding which AI tools are⏎eligible for FDA-approval, which fall under FDA’s enforcement discretion, and the expectations⏎for cybersecurity and labeling. Additional clarity is also needed around Prescription Drug Use⏎Related Software (PDURS) regulatory boundaries, including when AI functionality is treated as a⏎regulated device or SaMD versus non-device clinical decision support. Such guidance should⏎emphasize performance validation, post-market monitoring, and continuous improvement⏎mechanisms that ensure tools remain safe and effective over time.⏎● Right to cure: Mechanisms should exist to allow for the identification and correction of AI errors⏎without penalizing providers unnecessarily. This is also an appropriate area for illustrative⏎examples, such as reduced or mitigated HIPAA⏎penalties, where timely remediation, transparency, and corrective action demonstrably reduce⏎patient risk.⏎● Liability: Providers remain ultimately responsible for clinical decisions; however, guidance is⏎needed on the responsibilities of developers and deploying entities (e.g., hospital systems),⏎particularly where AI systems operate with varying degrees of autonomy or integration into⏎clinical workflows. Federal guidance should clearly delineate responsibilities across developers,⏎deployers, and clinicians based on control, capability, and validated system performance.⏎⏎4. Scope of AI Autonomy⏎Policy should emphasize appropriate oversight, accountability, governance, and measurable⏎performance standards for AI-enabled clinical functions. The role of AI in clinical care should scale based⏎on evidence, validation, regulatory authorization, and real-world performance data.⏎A performance-based framework can support innovation while maintaining safeguards, recognizing⏎that:⏎● AI systems may appropriately perform functions commensurate with their demonstrated safety,⏎effectiveness, and regulatory status.⏎● Governance expectations should align with validated system capability, regulatory⏎authorization, and real-world performance data. As AI systems demonstrate reliability and⏎effectiveness within defined parameters, policy should permit appropriate integrated clinical⏎deployment consistent with applicable standards.⏎● Clear accountability, transparency, and governance structures are essential for AI-enabled⏎clinical decision-making, regardless of the level of autonomy.⏎This approach preserves patient protections while allowing flexibility for future models that may prove⏎safe, effective, and well-regulated as technology continues to evolve.⏎5. Implementation and Adoption⏎Successful implementation and broader adoption of AI in clinical care depend on demonstrating clear⏎value in real-world settings. Pilot programs that show measurable improvements in outcomes and⏎effective workflow integration are essential to building provider confidence and scaling adoption. AI⏎tools are unlikely to gain traction if they do not meaningfully improve efficiency, quality, or patient⏎outcomes. Radiology has emerged as a leading example of successful integration, and expansion into⏎additional specialties should be guided by evidence-based best practices and ongoing performance⏎evaluation.⏎Federal agencies should support standardized performance evaluation frameworks that enable⏎benchmarking, interoperability, and reproducibility across care settings. These frameworks should⏎facilitate access to high-quality, longitudinal, multi-source data, helping AI tools integrate evidence⏎safely and effectively. Transparent performance reporting, continuous monitoring, and AI-assisted⏎pharmacovigilance mechanisms will further strengthen provider and patient trust by ensuring ongoing⏎safety, early detection of treatment-emergent risks, and reliable clinical outcomes.⏎Robust data collection and evaluation frameworks are equally important to support provider trust,⏎enable benchmarking, and promote continuous improvement. Establishing mechanisms for⏎performance monitoring and outcome measurement will help ensure that AI tools are used effectively,⏎responsibly, and in ways that deliver sustained clinical value.⏎6. Reimbursement and Market Incentives⏎Reimbursement models should recognize the value of AI in chronic disease management and other high-⏎value care areas. CMS programs, such as the CMMI ACCESS Model, could serve as a blueprint for⏎incentivizing AI adoption beyond chronic conditions.⏎⏎Reimbursement policies should reflect the full scope of effort, resources, and risk involved in developing⏎AI tools, particularly those subject to FDA regulation. Each AI device or software platform varies⏎significantly in terms of:⏎o Development time and complexity: Some AI tools require years of algorithm training, clinical⏎validation, and iterative testing.⏎o Resource intensity: Investment in data acquisition, annotation, cybersecurity safeguards,⏎and integration into existing workflows can be substantial.⏎o Regulatory requirements: FDA clearance or approval, ongoing monitoring, and compliance⏎with labeling, cybersecurity, and quality standards entail additional costs and operational⏎effort.⏎o Research and validation: Evidence generation, clinical trials, and real-world performance⏎studies are essential to ensure safety and effectiveness, and these processes vary widely by⏎device or application.⏎CMS should take all of these factors into consideration when establishing reimbursement levels,⏎recognizing that AI adoption is contingent on viable economic models. We also encourage consideration⏎of new reimbursement approaches and coding frameworks that better capture the value of AI-enabled⏎care, including models that move beyond time-based methodologies and instead reflect clinical impact,⏎automation, and outcomes. Appropriate reimbursement not only rewards innovation but also ensures⏎that AI tools can be safely and effectively integrated into clinical practice, supporting better patient⏎outcomes while sustaining developer investment and innovation.⏎7. ATA Action Recommendations⏎● Convene a stakeholder advisory group to define AI evaluation standards, real-world⏎performance benchmarks, governance best practices, and safe harbor criteria grounded in⏎validated evidence.⏎● Provide clear regulatory frameworks and guidance on labeling, cybersecurity, and compliance⏎expectations.⏎● Ensure transparency in the allocation of responsibility and liability among providers, developers,⏎and deploying organizations.⏎● Support pilot programs that demonstrate outcomes and facilitate scalable adoption.⏎● Develop reimbursement strategies that take into consideration clinical value,⏎workflow efficiency and patient outcomes enabled by AI.⏎● Establish a national performance-based framework that aligns oversight with validated⏎outcomes and harmonizes standards across federal agencies to provide regulatory certainty and⏎scalability.⏎● Clarify FDA’s device approval process, scope and requirements for AI tools used in healthcare⏎settings.⏎⏎We appreciate HHS’s leadership in advancing a thoughtful and coordinated approach to AI policy.⏎Establishing clear, consistent performance-driven federal standards will be critical to ensure that AI⏎enhances patient safety, strengthens provider confidence, and accelerates innovation across the⏎healthcare system. ATA Action stands ready to serve as a resource and partner in this effort and looks⏎forward to continued collaboration to ensure AI is deployed responsibly, effectively, and at scale to⏎improve care for all patients.⏎Sincerely,⏎Alexis Apple⏎Deputy Executive Director⏎ATA Action♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0295♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Woodall, Christopher, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Woodall♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Current AI systems process healthcare data in plaintext during inference. While encryption technologies exist, they impose 100-1000x computational overhead that makes clinical deployment infeasible. Patient data sits exposed in memory, model weights, and system logs.⏎⏎The Privacy Gap⏎⏎When patient data enters an AI model for training, it gets memorized in model parameters—potentially forever. During inference, that same data flows through third-party cloud providers and model platforms in plaintext. Patients have zero visibility into where their health data goes or who accesses it.⏎⏎Required Protections⏎⏎Any biometric data, clinical notes, imaging, genomic information, or other inputs to healthcare AI systems must receive full HIPAA protection. Current regulatory ambiguity allows vendors to claim exemptions. Close this loophole.⏎Patients must have explicit rights to:⏎⏎- Delete their data from AI training datasets and model weights⏎- Consent before their data is copied to third-party AI platforms or cloud providers⏎- Audit what systems hold their data and how it's being used⏎⏎Bottom Line⏎⏎The rush to deploy healthcare AI outpaces our privacy infrastructure. Biometric and health data in AI systems deserves the same protections as data in EHRs. Until encrypted inference becomes practical, we need patient consent, transparent data flows, and enforceable deletion rights.⏎⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0296♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ashley♔Jones♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔Asian Health Coalition♔♔♔♔♔♔My organization works with Asian American, Native Hawaiian, and Pacific Islander communities across the Midwest, and what I keep coming back to is a question that this RFI doesn't seem to ask directly: when an AI system causes a patient harm, who actually pays for that?⏎⏎I don't mean this rhetorically. I mean it as a concrete, practical question that HHS should be able to answer before it does anything to speed up adoption. Right now, the honest answer is: probably nobody.⏎⏎Here's what I've observed working in this space. A vendor sells a diagnostic support tool to a hospital system. The contract, almost certainly, contains language disclaiming liability for clinical decisions made with the tool's output. The clinician who acted on the tool's recommendation points to the fact that they were using an FDA-cleared product and following institutional protocol. The hospital says it implemented the system according to vendor specifications. The vendor says the training data was the user's responsibility to validate for their patient population, or that the clinician deviated from recommended use. You end up with injured patients who have spent months trying to figure out who to even send a demand letter to. This is not hypothetical. We are already seeing this pattern in radiology and in sepsis prediction tools.⏎⏎The FDA clearance issue genuinely frustrates me because I think it creates a false floor. A 510(k) clearance tells you that a device is substantially equivalent to something already on the market. It does not tell you that the tool performs equitably across AAPI subpopulations, or performs well at a specific community health center in Sioux Falls, or remains accurate as a hospital's patient demographics shift over time. But once a system has FDA clearance, hospitals treat it like a warranty, and vendors use it as a liability shield. The clearance becomes a way of saying "we did the right thing" without actually demonstrating that the thing works for the people it's being used on.⏎⏎There's a related problem with lock-in that I don't think gets discussed enough. When an AI tool gets embedded into clinical workflows and especially into billing and reimbursement codes, the cost of removing it becomes enormous even if problems emerge. Institutions become structurally dependent. I sat in on a session at a health equity conference in Chicago in March 2024 where a hospital administrator described being unable to sunset a deterioration prediction algorithm that their own quality team had flagged as performing worse for patients with limited English proficiency, because the system had been integrated into their EHR in a way that would cost an estimated $2.4 million to unwind. That administrator was not happy about this. But what choice did they have?⏎⏎So when HHS asks what regulatory actions it can take to accelerate adoption, I want to flag that the acceleration is already happening faster than the accountability infrastructure can handle. Before HHS does more to speed things up, I think it needs to answer who bears responsibility when these systems fail, and that answer needs to be clear enough that a patient's attorney could actually use it. Right now it isn't.⏎⏎I'm genuinely uncertain whether the right mechanism here is through CMS conditions of participation, through FTC vendor disclosure requirements, or through some kind of mandatory post-market surveillance framework tied to reimbursement. Probably some combination. But the current situation where liability is distributed across a chain of actors in a way that effectively means it attaches to no one seems like something that should not be accelerated.⏎⏎One last thing that I haven't fully worked out but keeps nagging at me: even if HHS got the liability question right prospectively, there's still the question of what happens to patients who were harmed during the period when these systems were deployed without adequate governance, and I haven't seen the RFI address that at all.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0297♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Johnson & Johnson, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Johnson & Johnson♔♔♔♔♔♔Johnson & Johnson Services, Inc T +1 202 589 1000⏎1350 I (Eye) Street, NW, Suite 1210 ccartwr@its.jnj.com⏎Washington, DC 20005 USA jnj.com⏎February 23rd, 2026⏎RE: J&J Response to Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for⏎Information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care.⏎Dear Secretary of the Department of Health and Human Services Robert F. Kennedy Jr.,⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide comments on the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care. We commend the Office of the Deputy Secretary and⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC) actions to consider input from varied stakeholders on how⏎to accelerate the uptake of AI technology in healthcare, foster public trust and confidence in⏎modern technology solutions, to reduce uncertainty that impedes AI innovation, and improve⏎health outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities.⏎At Johnson & Johnson, our employees are driven by a passion to achieve the best version of⏎health for everyone, everywhere, for as long as possible. Innovating across the full spectrum of⏎healthcare solutions puts us in a unique leadership position today to deliver the breakthroughs⏎of tomorrow. Our strength in both biology and medical technology means we are accelerating⏎advances in care – from cell therapy to AI-assisted robotic surgery. We are using our wide range⏎of expertise to address healthcare challenges that can be tackled by both medical technology⏎and innovative medicine such as cancer, cardiovascular disease, and eye health.⏎How We are Using AI⏎At J&J, we are applying AI across our business, focusing on finding solutions to big challenges⏎and advancing our impact on patients. We believe AI, when used at scale, has the potential to⏎accelerate our ability to advance human health. AI allows us to analyze larger-than-ever data⏎sets in ways never before possible to find patterns and trends that are helping to transform our⏎innovative engine. We are using AI, leveraging de-identified data, in a multitude of ways across⏎our sectors:⏎Accelerating drug discovery: We are leveraging state-of-the-art AI/machine learning (ML)⏎technologies across drug discovery; from identifying novel targets that we can modulate to⏎combat disease, to enabling the identification of molecular starting points for those targets,⏎to optimizing those molecules to design and develop a drug candidate.⏎Speeding enrollment and research in clinical trials: We are using ML algorithms to analyze⏎real world health data from sources like de-identified electronic medical records, labs,⏎claims and imaging to broaden recruitment and identify and prioritize geographies and⏎healthcare organizations where more patients are being treated.⏎Driving precision medicine: We are applying to AI and ML to data sets – from digital images⏎to sound recordings – to identify novel biomarkers and clinical endpoints that can help us⏎develop and deliver the right medicines for the right patients at the right time.⏎1⏎⏎Johnson & Johnson Services, Inc T +1 202 589 1000⏎1350 I (Eye) Street, NW, Suite 1210 ccartwr@its.jnj.com⏎Washington, DC 20005 USA jnj.com⏎Harnessing AI for electro-anatomical mapping of the heart: During catheter ablation⏎procedures for atrial fibrillation, it is critical for electrophysiologists to “see” inside the heart.⏎Our CARTO™ 3 System – the leading 3D heart mapping system – features AI to⏎reconstruct the left atrial anatomy of the heart. By eliminating the need for manual⏎contouring, the system represents a new approach to anatomy creation, improving the⏎efficiency of the procedure workflow.⏎Simplifying operative experiences for physicians: Our AI-powered VIRTUGUIDE™ software⏎automates patient analysis and correction to treat bony deformities of the foot and ankle.⏎By using AI to analyze the patient’s anatomy, this software outputs the instrument needed⏎to treat the specific patient and recommends a correction plan, which allows for intra-⏎operative simplicity, fewer instruments, and reduced surgery time.⏎Accelerating surgeon and care team training: Our first-of-its-kind laparoscopic surgical⏎simulation platform integrates augmented reality, AI-guided assessment, and tactile⏎feedback, to help enhance the surgical skills and training experiences of suturing for⏎general, gynecological, and laparoscopic surgeons.⏎AI-based technologies are revolutionizing healthcare and are already changing how⏎diseases are understood, diagnosed, and treated; how healthcare systems and⏎healthcare professionals (HCPs) deliver care with enhanced insights and improved⏎workflows; and how new healthcare solutions are developed. Safely and responsibly⏎harnessing AI’s potential in healthcare requires the implementation of a well⏎understood, agile and future-proof policy framework that encourages innovation and⏎research partnerships, builds a data culture that respects personal data privacy and⏎security, supports education and upskilling in the healthcare and regulatory workforce⏎and encourages global cooperation and alignment, where appropriate.⏎Barriers to Adoption, Trust, and Governance of AI in Clinical Care⏎Patients and caregivers hope that AI can improve access to timely and cost-effective⏎care while still offering personalized treatments without care fragmentation. On the other⏎hand, they are concerned with data privacy, inaccurate information/medical⏎recommendations, and loss of human connection. Patients want to know that their⏎healthcare providers are still at the helm of decisions being made.⏎The nuanced, context-dependent nature of clinical medicine creates barriers to AI⏎implementation. While AI can provide directional information, medical decision making⏎requires integrated and complex information. Medical decisions must account for highly⏎variable patient presentations, comorbidities, social determinants, and evolving⏎evidence, making it difficult to establish trust that AI algorithms have the appropriate⏎training and validation. AI systems depend on patterns of information that do not⏎account for nuanced clinical information. There is danger in blindly adopting AI output⏎without contextual considerations by the clinician.⏎Clinical data used to develop AI models are typically pulled from published studies and⏎guidelines. However, the quality of studies can vary widely, and AI models often cannot⏎2⏎⏎Johnson & Johnson Services, Inc T +1 202 589 1000⏎1350 I (Eye) Street, NW, Suite 1210 ccartwr@its.jnj.com⏎Washington, DC 20005 USA jnj.com⏎discern between well conducted and validated information and may pull from poorly⏎conducted studies which reduces model reliability. Often, data sources are not readily⏎disclosed, and some AI models will hallucinate sources. This creates mistrust and⏎presents as a barrier to medical professionals using AI.⏎As data fuels AI, we need to access large enough data sets to train models. This⏎requires rational approaches to determining data ownership, control, and governance⏎structures. We must also consider the quality of the data sets: if data used to train the AI⏎model has limitations and does not adequately represent the target population, one⏎could have outputs that are not representative enough.⏎Risk-based and clear and predictable regulatory framework⏎We understand that one important barrier to private sector innovation in AI for⏎healthcare and its adoption and use in clinical care is regulatory uncertainty. Evolving⏎and inconsistent requirements for AI in healthcare surrounding validation standards and⏎explainability can create unclear approval pathways and investment risk, slowing⏎development and deployment. Greater clarity and alignment across regulatory⏎frameworks can help unlock innovation and support responsible scaling of AI in⏎healthcare.⏎Traditional AI has been incorporated into medical devices for three decades. AI-driven⏎medical devices are already highly regulated both in pre and post market and the⏎existing regulatory requirements are appropriate for these devices. Any use of third-⏎party certification will lead to redundant activities and documentation and may lead to⏎disclosure of commercial confidential information and inappropriate release of private⏎healthcare data.⏎Consideration should be given to identifying if there are any specific updates to the FDA⏎regulatory processes that would be appropriate to apply to generative or agentic AI.⏎These evidence standards should be specific to the nature of the use case for AI and⏎not the model itself.⏎The CMS HTI-11 rule has been interpreted to apply to products regulated as medical⏎devices if its training data are Electronic Health Records. Clarification on the scope of⏎the rule would ensure that medical device manufacturers understand all the⏎requirements that apply to their AI-driven medical devices.⏎We would welcome AI evaluation methods which ultimately lead to AI being deployed in⏎ways which are helpful for healthcare professionals and beneficial to the patients they⏎serve. These AI evaluation methods may include pre- and post-deployment testing and⏎other robustness measures, as they may clarify how a non-medical device AI may⏎perform in practice, an important consideration for healthcare providers who may be⏎1 h#ps://healthit.gov/regula3ons/h3-rules/h3-1-final-rule/⏎3⏎⏎Johnson & Johnson Services, Inc T +1 202 589 1000⏎1350 I (Eye) Street, NW, Suite 1210 ccartwr@its.jnj.com⏎Washington, DC 20005 USA jnj.com⏎hesitant to use AI otherwise. One way HHS could support these processes would be⏎through incentives, such as grants or prize competitions.⏎AI in clinical care may lead to an increased level of sophistication for privacy and⏎security programs, and the intersection of AI and liability can be unclear. There is a lack⏎of clarity on who bears responsibility when AI tools indirectly cause harm. Indirect harm⏎may be caused by algorithmic errors, bias-related harms, or failures in continuous⏎learning of the AI system. HHS should help address not only reducing the probability of⏎harm through guidance but also providing direction on who bears responsibility.⏎Safeguarding Data Privacy⏎Another key element for safe, ethical, and transparent AI use is privacy. We operate in a⏎highly regulated industry where we use a variety of types of data, including⏎administrative and claims data, clinical data, genomic data, patient-generated data,⏎surgical video, and social determinants of health data that move through a variety of⏎transmission networks. This data is essential to continued innovation, discovery,⏎evaluation, and speed for the delivery of healthcare products and services to patients⏎and consumers.⏎We support adoption of a comprehensive national privacy law and associated standards⏎to help ensure a more consumer- and patient- friendly approach to managing personal⏎information while ensuring consistent privacy protection, allowing a greater flow of data,⏎and maintaining adequate protections. A federal law would help reduce variability across⏎multiple governments and government agencies, consequently reducing regulatory⏎costs and increasing competitiveness to attract investments.⏎Enhanced Interoperability and Data Standards⏎We would welcome promoting and incentivizing enhanced interoperability across⏎electronic health records (EHR) through the use of common data models and standards⏎such as Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR). Promoting the adoption of⏎common data models enables larger, more diverse data sets for model training and⏎validation of AI. In turn, this enablement can accelerate AI development across the⏎healthcare ecosystem, ultimately driving more efficient and better healthcare outcomes,⏎while expanding the global market reach of U.S. healthcare and technology companies.⏎Global data standards and interoperability of data and electronic health records are key⏎to fuel research and innovation, thus enhancing patient outcomes. Global standards for⏎data quality would contribute to the dual beneficial outcome of the enhancement of AI⏎models through access to more data for training purposes, and the interoperability of⏎electronic health record systems increasing data availability for R&D and improving⏎patient outcomes, such as disease predictability and personalized treatment. This will⏎broaden patients’ access to treatment and further enhance AI utilized in clinical care,⏎increasing market opportunities and patient outcomes.⏎4⏎⏎Johnson & Johnson Services, Inc T +1 202 589 1000⏎1350 I (Eye) Street, NW, Suite 1210 ccartwr@its.jnj.com⏎Washington, DC 20005 USA jnj.com⏎Research Priorities to Accelerate Responsible AI Adoption⏎We believe HHS should work to prioritize and fund research on the impact of AI on⏎patient care, patient safety, and clinician knowledge. It is unknown how the potential for⏎dependency on AI for medical students and other healthcare providers in training will⏎impact the competency of those individuals. Research is needed to better understand⏎new conjecture to promote adoption or potential guardrails for use of AI in academic⏎and clinical practice.⏎We would welcome HHS prioritizing AI research that delivers clear, clinically meaningful⏎explanations at the point of care, paired alongside continuous real-world performance⏎monitoring to verify AI remains consistent in the quality of its performance after⏎deployment. Together, these capabilities can strengthen clinician trust and accelerate⏎adoption of AI into routine clinical practice.⏎Additionally, HHS should actively catalyze research on privacy-enhancing technologies⏎(PETs), including federated learning, secure multi-party computation, encryption, and⏎high-quality synthetic data, by funding targeted grants, CRADAs (cooperative research⏎and development agreements), and prize competitions, creating de-identified⏎benchmark datasets and interoperable standards, and developing clear evaluation⏎frameworks and metrics that quantify utility/privacy tradeoffs. By sponsoring pilot⏎implementations in real-world health systems, issuing guidance on acceptable risk⏎thresholds and documentation expectations, and supporting workforce training to⏎operationalize PETs in clinical workflows, HHS can both lower adoption barriers and⏎ensure that research outputs are reproducible, interoperable, and aligned with patient⏎privacy, public trust, and continued innovation.⏎Modernizing Payment Systems and Market Access for AI in Clinical Care⏎Ensuring payor coverage of AI-powered and enabled health technologies is a challenge⏎given the complexity of existing healthcare payment systems, the divergence of⏎intellectual property policies, and the lack of standardized, trustworthy, and transparent⏎testing of AI models. To ensure smooth adoption and coverage of new technologies in⏎various healthcare systems across markets, we must first ensure the quality, accuracy,⏎reliability, transparency, and representativeness of data for AI models. It is necessary to⏎establish - where they do not already exist - clear evidence and data requirements for⏎lifecycle management of AI-enabled health technologies.⏎Where appropriate, create public and private sandboxes for developers and deployers⏎to test and certify their solutions in a controlled environment, such as on AI model⏎performance, health outcomes, data accuracy, and representativeness in AI models.⏎5⏎⏎Johnson & Johnson Services, Inc T +1 202 589 1000⏎1350 I (Eye) Street, NW, Suite 1210 ccartwr@its.jnj.com⏎Washington, DC 20005 USA jnj.com⏎***⏎We recognize the powerful promise of AI in healthcare, and we know this timely effort requires⏎collaboration and partnership. We value our role as an innovator in healthcare, contributing new⏎ideas, solutions, technology, partnerships, and perspectives on AI policy.⏎J&J looks forward to serving as a resource and providing additional thoughts and contributing to⏎efforts that ensure AI is ethically, safely, and efficiently researched, developed and integrated⏎into our healthcare system and our society. If you have any questions or if we can provide any⏎assistance on issues related to healthcare, please e-mail Carla Cartwright at⏎ccartwr@its.jnj.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Carla Cartwright⏎Vice President, Global Digital &⏎Regulatory Policy⏎6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0298♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Andrew♔Martinez♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My new cardiologist, who took over from Dr. Bellows when he retired in early 2023, is a good enough doctor I think, but when he recommended changing my metoprolol dosage at my appointment in March he couldn't tell me why. He looked at his screen and read back numbers at me. I pressed him. He said the system flagged my risk profile. I asked what that meant for me specifically, a 75-year-old man who had a TIA in 2019 and has been on the same regimen for four years, and he didn't have a real answer. That was unsettling in a way I keep coming back to.⏎⏎What worries me about HHS pushing AI faster into clinical settings is this: those systems learned from historical data. And historical data reflects who got good care and who didn't, who got believed and who didn't, who showed up in the research and who got excluded. So when the algorithm spits out a recommendation, it's not some neutral voice from the future, it's a weighted average of past decisions, including the bad ones. And the problem is that it looks authoritative. Doctors who are already busy, already behind on their charts, already seeing 20 patients a day, are not going to stop and interrogate an output that comes with a confidence score attached.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in medicine. I understand it catches things on imaging that get missed. But there's a real difference between a tool that a physician reviews and a system that effectively makes the call and the physician signs off. I think we're sliding toward the second thing without anyone really deciding to.⏎⏎The patients who get hurt by this are not going to be the patients who fit the model well. The person who matches the training population, whatever that is, probably gets decent output. But someone like me, older, with a complex medication history, blood pressure that runs high but has been managed a specific way for reasons, a stroke that changed how I respond to certain things, I don't fit neatly. And if the algorithm is wrong about me and my doctor can't explain the reasoning independently, nobody catches it until something goes wrong.⏎⏎I don't know what the regulatory solution looks like exactly. But I think HHS should require that before any AI-generated clinical recommendation is acted on, the physician has to be able to explain the reasoning in their own words, not just confirm the output. I know that sounds simple but it would actually slow down the worst version of this, where the doctor becomes a person who authorizes what the machine decided.⏎⏎There should also be some kind of tracking. If a hospital is using one of these systems and certain patient groups are consistently getting different outcomes, someone outside that hospital needs to see that data. Not buried in an annual report. Regularly, with someone actually looking at it.⏎⏎I live in Vermont and I don't have a lot of options for cardiac care. There's one practice within reasonable distance and if they're using a system that doesn't handle patients like me well, I can't just go somewhere else. That's the part of this that doesn't get talked about enough in my opinion. For people in rural areas or people who are older or people who just don't have the resources to push back, whatever the algorithm says is basically what happens.⏎⏎My appointment in June is coming up and I still haven't figured out how to ask the right questions.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0299♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Radiological Society of North America, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Radiological Society of North America♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Radiological Society of North America (RSNA) is a leading global organization representing over 52,000⏎radiologists and medical imaging professionals across 150 countries. Radiology and medical imaging are among the⏎most data-intensive fields in medicine, and AI-driven technologies have already begun transforming clinical practice.⏎Radiology has experienced the highest rate of medical AI tool development and deployment, with more than 75% of⏎the over 1,000 Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-cleared AI algorithms designed for radiological applications.⏎RSNA’s guiding principle when considering how to best accelerate the adoption and implementation of AI in⏎clinical care is unwavering commitment to patient safety. Federal oversight of AI should be grounded in a risk-⏎based, proportional approach—placing the strongest regulatory focus on applications that directly impact clinical⏎decision-making or patient outcomes. Streamlined regulatory pathways and clear guidance are essential but must⏎be paired with robust safety guardrails to protect patients. When thoughtfully designed, regulation does not hinder⏎innovation; instead, it establishes a trusted foundation for progress. This ensures that AI technologies strengthen,⏎rather than compromise, the integrity of medical practice and patient care.⏎Our comments, organized below by specific RFI questions, encompass issues relevant to all agencies at the⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), including but not limited to OSTP/ONC. This approach⏎ensures a holistic perspective on regulatory, policy, and programmatic considerations.⏎Specific Questions⏎1) What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎As highlighted in President Trump’s AI Action Plan, the primary obstacles to realizing AI’s full potential in⏎healthcare are not simply the availability of models, tools or technical capability. Rather, accelerating the⏎adoption and implementation of AI in clinical care requires the careful building of trust among clinicians, patients,⏎and health systems. Because clinical decisions have direct, and often irreversible consequences for patients,⏎accelerating the adoption of AI in healthcare requires a thoughtful and balanced regulatory approach that ensures⏎patient safety without stifling innovation. Vendors and healthcare organizations face implementation challenges⏎that are distinct from those in other industries, including variability in patient populations, workflow complexity, and⏎the need for continuous clinical oversight.⏎The successful adoption of AI in clinical care depends not only on smart regulatory policy, but on confidence⏎across the healthcare ecosystem. Regulators, clinicians, developers, and health system leaders must share⏎common expectations around transparency, explainability, and ongoing performance monitoring. Without⏎consistent frameworks to support these expectations, uncertainty among care providers and patients regarding⏎the risks and trustworthiness of AI is a key barrier to adoption, even when AI tools demonstrate technical promise.⏎⏎Organizational readiness is also a barrier to more widespread implementation of AI. Many healthcare institutions⏎lack the internal data infrastructure, governance processes, and technical expertise needed to evaluate, deploy,⏎and monitor AI tools effectively. Investments in AI literacy, standardized data practices, and operational⏎infrastructure, across both public agencies and private healthcare organizations, are necessary to support safe,⏎and effective implementation. Such investments help ensure that AI tools are used as intended, interpreted⏎appropriately by clinicians, and monitored over time to maintain clinical performance and patient safety.⏎Additional barriers include the fact that AI-enabled tools are constantly evolving, and that performance can⏎change across care settings. Many radiology practices report that AI algorithms do not perform as expected⏎when applied to their specific patient populations, necessitating rigorous local testing, and validation prior to⏎clinical implementation. Radiology practices have also learned that AI models must be monitored closely to⏎prevent “diagnostic drift,” in which even minor changes to algorithms, input data, or clinical context can result in⏎clinically meaningful differences in output. These challenges are amplified by the current lack of standardization⏎across AI tools, as algorithms designed for similar purposes may generate outputs using different formats,⏎thresholds, or assumptions. As a result, modifications to AI tools must be transparent and readily available for⏎revalidation by end users to ensure continued alignment with clinical expectations and patient safety.⏎2) What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎AI has the potential to improve the efficiency, quality, and consistency of clinical care, but its successful⏎implementation depends on a regulatory approach that enables innovation while maintaining high standards for⏎safety and AI performance. Clear, predictable, and adaptive regulatory pathways can reduce uncertainty for⏎developers and healthcare organizations while reinforcing trust in AI-enabled clinical tools.⏎Barriers to innovation - AI innovation in medical imaging is often constrained by regulatory frameworks designed⏎for static technologies rather than adaptive systems. The FDA’s authority to regulate medical devices is rooted in⏎the 1976 Medical Device Amendments to the 1938 Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act, which were written for⏎human-operated, and largely unchanging, devices. As a result, AI tools are regulated under Software as a⏎Medical Device (SaMD) and Software in a Medical Device (SiMD) frameworks that do not fully accommodate⏎continuous learning, recalibration, or iterative improvement.⏎Under current approaches, many AI models are required to remain “locked” following clearance or approval,⏎limiting the ability to implement updates that could preserve accuracy, reliability, and safety over time. This rigidity⏎can be misaligned with the realities of clinical practice, where patient populations, imaging protocols, and care⏎environments evolve continuously. In such settings, the inability to make controlled updates may increase, rather⏎than reduce clinical risk.⏎RSNA supports regulatory approaches that enable safe, monitored adaptation of AI tools while maintaining⏎appropriate oversight. Smart regulation should distinguish between modifications that can be managed within⏎predefined boundaries through ongoing monitoring and those that warrant more comprehensive re-evaluation.⏎2⏎⏎Enabling this distinction would allow AI tools to improve responsibly over time, support long-term clinical⏎performance, and better align regulatory oversight with the dynamic nature of AI technologies and real-world⏎clinical use.⏎Outdated or misaligned frameworks - RSNA supports efforts to modernize regulatory approaches to better reflect⏎the lifecycle realities of AI. Many existing device regulations were developed to govern episodic software updates⏎with fixed performance characteristics. Rigid application of these frameworks to AI tools risks slowing innovation⏎without improving patient safety. Unlike traditional software, AI system behavior may vary across clinical contexts,⏎patient populations, imaging protocols, and patterns of use, even in the absence of explicit software updates.⏎Effective oversight must therefore address not only how a system is designed to operate, but how it performs in⏎real-world clinical settings over time. Clinicians and regulators should have appropriate visibility into the⏎parameters, assumptions, and trade-offs influencing AI outputs, particularly when those outputs inform clinical⏎decision-making. Such transparency is essential to support accountability, enable meaningful monitoring, and⏎allow users to recognize when an AI tool may be operating outside its intended context.⏎RSNA supports risk-based and proportional regulatory approaches that focus oversight on AI applications with⏎direct influence on clinical decisions and patient outcomes. Adaptive regulatory models that account for context-⏎dependent performance and real-world use can better align oversight with how AI functions in practice, supporting⏎innovation while maintaining high standards of safety and clinical reliability.⏎Underused administrative tools - Existing mechanisms such as FDA’s Predetermined Change Control Plans⏎(PCCP) and related pilot programs offer important opportunities for safe, adaptive AI deployment. However, these⏎tools remain voluntary and inconsistently implemented. Broader adoption and clearer alignment of these⏎mechanisms across federal agencies could reduce regulatory redundancy, improve predictability for developers⏎and health systems, and support timely access to safe and effective AI innovations.⏎While RSNA recognizes that current PCCP frameworks emphasize vendor self-monitoring, we have concerns that⏎self-monitoring alone may be insufficient in complex clinical environments such as radiology. Radiology practices⏎often deploy multiple AI tools from different vendors simultaneously, each with distinct functions, outputs, and⏎update cycles. These tools may interact with shared workflows, data sources, and clinical decision processes in⏎ways that individual vendors may not be positioned to fully observe or anticipate. In such settings, reliance solely⏎on vendor-reported deviations risks leaving clinically meaningful performance issues undetected.⏎To strengthen oversight while preserving flexibility, RSNA recommends a dual-reporting model that allows both⏎vendors and end users, like radiologists and healthcare organizations, to report observed deviations in AI tool⏎performance directly to the FDA. In addition, RSNA encourages the FDA to establish a formal mechanism through⏎which end users can request modifications or updates to PCCPs based on real-world experience. Radiologists⏎may identify context-specific performance issues, population-related limitations, or workflow interactions that were⏎not evident during initial development or testing, but that materially affect safety or effectiveness in practice.⏎Creating structured pathways for this feedback would promote collaboration among developers, clinicians, and⏎regulators, support continuous improvement, and help ensure that AI tools remain aligned with evolving clinical⏎needs while prioritizing patient safety.⏎3⏎⏎Structural incompatibility and need for clarity - Effective adoption of AI in clinical care depends on sustained⏎cross-agency coordination among ASTP/ONC, FDA, and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS).⏎Greater alignment across these agencies on definitions, data standards, and post-market surveillance⏎expectations would reduce duplication, close regulatory gaps, and promote trust among developers, healthcare⏎organizations, clinicians, and patients.⏎Clearer coordination is particularly important at the intersection of regulation, interoperability, and reimbursement.⏎Consistent expectations for data capture, performance monitoring, and reporting would enable healthcare⏎organizations to implement AI tools more efficiently while supporting oversight across the full lifecycles of use.⏎Harmonized approaches would also help ensure that safety monitoring and accountability mechanisms scale⏎across institutions and care settings.⏎Complementary refinement of data privacy frameworks, including those established under the Health Insurance⏎Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), could further support responsible AI development and deployment.⏎Clear, consistent guidance on de-identification, data minimization, and secondary use would enable the⏎appropriate use of large, privacy-protective datasets for AI training, validation, and ongoing performance⏎monitoring. Establishing secure and compliant pathways for multi-institutional data aggregation would improve⏎model generalizability, reduce performance variability across settings, and support more reliable clinical use.⏎Finally, clearer interpretive guidance, developed in collaboration with clinical experts and professional societies,⏎would help ensure that safety and compliance expectations are transparent, consistent, and practical to⏎implement. Such clarity is essential to fostering innovation while maintaining strong protections for patients and⏎ensuring public trust.⏎3) For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The increasing use of AI tools that are not regulated as medical devices, including general-purpose decision⏎support, workflow automation, and documentation tools, introduces novel legal and implementation⏎considerations when these technologies are deployed in clinical care. These include questions related to clinical⏎accountability, liability allocation, data provenance, privacy protections, and cybersecurity, particularly when AI⏎outputs inform or influence clinical decision-making without clear regulatory oversight.⏎While RSNA does not recommend that such tools should be regulated as medical devices, clearer federal⏎guidance is needed to delineate expectations for transparency, documentation, and accountability when non-⏎medical AI tools are used in clinical environments. HHS can play a valuable role by coordinating cross-agency⏎guidance that clarifies how existing frameworks, including privacy, security, and information governance⏎requirements, apply to these tools; support best practices for responsible deployment; and reinforce that ultimate⏎clinical responsibility rests with licensed healthcare professionals. Such guidance would help reduce uncertainty⏎for health systems and clinicians while maintaining appropriate safeguards for patients.⏎4⏎⏎4) For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎The broader adoption of AI in radiology and healthcare depends on trust among clinicians, patients, healthcare⏎organizations, and regulators. As described above, to cultivate this trust, post-deployment monitoring of AI tools⏎must be standardized, with clear mechanisms for evaluating real-world performance and safety over time. RSNA⏎urges HHS to support ongoing validation studies of AI models in different clinical settings; establish transparent⏎reporting mechanisms for AI efficacy and safety; and encourage collaboration between regulatory agencies,⏎professional societies, and healthcare institutions to ensure continuous oversight while avoiding unnecessary⏎administrative burden.⏎RSNA has gained experience in validating AI-enabled diagnostic imaging tools that have relevance for evaluation⏎of non-medical devices. RSNA has curated large, expert-annotated datasets for use in a series of AI challenge⏎competitions and made them freely available through its Medical Imaging Resource for AI (MIRA) platform⏎(https://mira.rsna.org). These datasets span multiple imaging modalities and clinical specialties and include⏎training, validation, and sequestered test partitions. These resources are used to support public benchmarking⏎and independent evaluation of AI-enabled medical devices. Model performance is assessed using standard⏎metrics selected to determine accuracy and generalizability. Together, these datasets and evaluation approaches⏎provide a foundation for transparent, reproducible assessment aligned with FDA and National Institutes of Health⏎(NIH) priorities for lifecycle evaluation of AI-enabled medical devices.⏎Meaningful evaluation of AI-enabled medical devices in radiology requires multidimensional performance⏎assessment. Core metrics include diagnostic accuracy measures (such as sensitivity, specificity, and area under⏎the curve); performance consistency across patient populations and clinical contexts; workflow impact; technical⏎efficiency of algorithm execution; and usability of AI outputs for clinical end users.⏎Equally important is assessment of performance stability over time. Indicators of longitudinal reliability include⏎calibration drift, changes in error rates, diagnostic concordance with clinical reference standards, and structured⏎feedback from users regarding confidence and appropriate use. Reliability should also be evaluated across⏎imaging devices, manufacturers, modalities, protocols, and acquisition conditions to ensure consistent⏎performance in real-world clinical environments.⏎Future benchmarking and assessment frameworks should continue to evolve toward comprehensive,⏎multidimensional evaluation. In addition to diagnostic performance, these frameworks should incorporate analysis⏎of execution boundaries and failure modes; clarity and interpretability of outputs; quality of explanatory information⏎provided to users; and consistency of communication within clinical workflows. Such approaches will support⏎responsible AI deployment, continuous improvement, and sustain trust in AI-enabled clinical tools.⏎5⏎⏎5) How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven⏎testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎RSNA has a long-standing role in advancing technological innovation in medical imaging and in convening⏎stakeholders across academia, industry, and government to translate emerging technologies into clinical practice.⏎We stand ready to collaborate with HHS and other federal partners to support the responsible development,⏎validation, and deployment of AI-enabled clinical tools.⏎RSNA’s contributions reflect sustained investment in the foundational infrastructure required for scalable and⏎trustworthy AI in healthcare. These efforts include leadership in the adoption of the DICOM imaging standard,⏎which accelerated the digitization and interoperability of radiology; long-standing support for the Integrating the⏎Healthcare Enterprise (IHE) initiative to improve interoperability between diagnostic systems, including AI-enabled⏎tools in diagnostic imaging, and electronic health records; and the development of widely adopted semantic⏎standards, including RadLex, the LOINC-RSNA Radiology Playbook, and RadElement, that enhance the machine⏎readability and consistency of medical imaging data.⏎In addition, RSNA has played a significant role in advancing AI research and evaluation through the organization⏎of eleven international AI challenge competitions since 2017, which engage global research communities in⏎developing and validating algorithms for clinically relevant tasks. RSNA also collects, curates, and annotates⏎large-scale medical imaging datasets through its Medical Imaging Resource Center (MIRA) platform to support⏎training, benchmarking, and independent evaluation. Further, RSNA has helped collect, curate, and submit data⏎to the Medical Imaging Data Resource Center (MIDRC), which aggregates and annotates multi-institutional⏎imaging data to support AI research, validation, and generalizability.⏎Together, these efforts position RSNA as an effective capable of supporting public–private collaborations that⏎advance AI innovation while emphasizing interoperability, validation, transparency, and patient safety. Continued⏎collaboration between federal agencies and professional societies such as RSNA can help ensure that AI⏎development is aligned with real-world clinical needs and supported by durable technical and governance⏎infrastructure.⏎6) Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations⏎and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Where AI tools have met expectations - AI tools have most consistently met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations when they are applied to narrowly defined, high-volume clinical tasks with well-characterized⏎workflows and measurable operational impact. In radiology and other data-rich specialties, examples include:⏎• Workflow prioritization and triage, particularly for time-sensitive conditions, where AI can support earlier⏎review and reduced turnaround times without displacing clinical judgment.⏎• Quantitative image analysis and measurement support, such as segmentation, volumetry, and⏎standardized measurements, which improve consistency and reduce manual effort.⏎6⏎⏎• Operational and technical quality support, including detection of incomplete studies, protocol deviations,⏎and image quality issues that reduce repeat imaging and downstream costs.⏎• Automation of low-risk administrative tasks, such as routing, protocol suggestions, and documentation⏎support, where benefits are realized through efficiency gains rather than diagnostic substitution.⏎In these settings, AI provides value when it is tightly integrated into existing clinical systems, minimizes additional⏎cognitive or documentation burden, and produces outputs that are easy for clinicians to interpret and verify.⏎Where AI tools have fallen short - AI tools have most often fallen short when deployed into complex clinical⏎environments without sufficient attention to validation, integration, and ongoing governance. Common challenges⏎include:⏎• Performance variability across real-world settings, driven by differences in patient populations, imaging⏎equipment, acquisition protocols, and local practice patterns.⏎• Limited generalizability and poorly defined boundary conditions, where tools perform well in development⏎or trial settings but degrade in routine clinical use, particularly for uncommon presentations or edge⏎cases.⏎• Workflow friction and usability challenges, including alert fatigue, unclear output presentation, and added⏎steps that reduce clinician adoption even when algorithmic performance is adequate.⏎• Insufficient post-deployment monitoring, including limited transparency into version changes, delayed⏎detection of performance drift, and lack of standardized mechanisms for real-world evaluation.⏎• Economic misalignment, where the entities responsible for purchasing AI tools do not directly realize⏎downstream financial or operational benefits, complicating adoption decisions.⏎• Interoperability and standardization gaps, including inconsistent output formats across vendors and⏎limited integration into structured clinical documentation and downstream care pathways.⏎• Unclear boundary conditions and off-label use, particularly when AI tools cleared for triage or workflow⏎prioritization are used in practice as diagnostic or detection aids. In high-volume clinical environments,⏎outputs intended to flag studies for expedited review may be interpreted as indicative of disease presence⏎or absence, influencing diagnostic reasoning without having been validated for that purpose.⏎• Scalability and cumulative implementation burden, particularly as multiple AI tools are applied⏎concurrently to the same clinical data. As AI adoption grows, clinicians and health systems increasingly⏎contend with overlapping alerts, notifications, and outputs generated by multiple algorithms operating on⏎the same imaging studies or clinical workflows. Without careful coordination, this can contribute to alert⏎fatigue, reduced clinician attention to high-value signals, and difficulty attributing downstream effects to⏎individual tools. In addition, monitoring the combined impact of multiple algorithms, including interactions⏎between tools from different vendors, presents operational and governance challenges that are not well⏎addressed by current implementation or oversight models.⏎These challenges underscore that successful AI adoption depends as much on implementation, governance, and⏎context of use as on model accuracy.⏎AI capabilities with the greatest potential for future impact - The greatest opportunities for AI to improve⏎health outcomes, generate meaningful quality insights, and reduce costs lie in applications that extend beyond⏎7⏎⏎isolated detection tasks and instead support longitudinal care, decision-making, and system-level efficiency.⏎Priority areas include:⏎• Longitudinal imaging intelligence, tools that track findings over time, identify clinically meaningful⏎changes, and support follow-up recommendations and adherence, particularly for chronic disease and⏎incidental findings.⏎• Context-aware decision support, tools that combine imaging with structured clinical data such as⏎laboratory results, prior diagnoses, and clinical history to improve appropriateness, reduce unnecessary⏎imaging, and support timely care.⏎• AI-enabled quality measurement and safety monitoring, including low-burden generation of performance⏎metrics related to diagnostic consistency, technical quality, and care pathway adherence, supporting⏎learning health system feedback loops.⏎• Protocol optimization and resource stewardship, tools that assist with exam selection, protocol tailoring,⏎dose and contrast optimization, and scheduling, helping reduce repeat imaging and improve throughput.⏎• Safety-focused AI, including tools that detect preventable errors, high-risk scenarios, and breakdowns in⏎communication, supporting systematic risk reduction rather than individual fault attribution.⏎• Population- and specialty-specific AI, particularly tools designed and validated for pediatric populations and⏎other specialized care settings, such as rare disease, where general-purpose models often underperform.⏎• Foundation models capable of supporting multiple diagnostic tasks, including the assessment of several⏎conditions within a single study or encounter. Unlike narrowly trained algorithms, these models may offer⏎efficiencies by reducing the need to deploy and manage multiple task-specific tools. However, realizing⏎their potential will require careful validation of performance across conditions, clear definition of intended⏎use, and robust monitoring to ensure reliability as clinical complexity increases.⏎• AI tools designed to identify normal findings or the absence of disease, which may offer substantial value⏎by reducing unnecessary follow-up, supporting workload management, and improving prioritization of⏎abnormal studies. Reliable identification of normal examinations could help clinicians focus attention on⏎higher-risk cases, but such tools must be held to high standards of sensitivity, transparency, and post-⏎deployment monitoring given the consequences of missed pathology.⏎• Agentic AI workflows that automate multi-step clinical and operational processes, such as study routing,⏎protocol selection, follow-up tracking, and communication of results. These tools have the potential to⏎reduce administrative burden and improve consistency across care pathways, but they also introduce⏎new governance challenges related to accountability, error propagation, and monitoring of downstream⏎effects across interconnected systems.⏎• Ambient and context-aware AI models that integrate clinical conversations and electronic health record⏎data, with the goal of supporting appropriate imaging selection and care coordination. By synthesizing⏎information from clinician–patient interactions and structured clinical data, these tools could improve⏎appropriateness, reduce unnecessary imaging, and support more personalized care. Effective⏎deployment will require clear safeguards for privacy, transparency about how recommendations are⏎generated, and careful evaluation of how such tools influence clinical decision-making.⏎Across these domains, the most impactful innovations will be those that are interoperable, transparent, and⏎supported by standardized post-deployment monitoring, allowing performance to be maintained as clinical⏎environments evolve.⏎8⏎⏎7) Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the most⏎influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Based on extensive experience supporting AI development, validation, and deployment in medical imaging,⏎RSNA observes that the most significant barriers to AI adoption in clinical care are administrative and operational⏎rather than technical. These challenges consistently limit the ability of healthcare organizations to implement AI⏎safely, effectively, and at scale.⏎A primary barrier is fragmented governance and unclear accountability for AI oversight. Many organizations lack⏎standardized frameworks to guide validation, monitoring, and lifecycle management once AI tools are deployed.⏎RSNA recommends that HHS support the development and dissemination of clear governance models and best⏎practices that define roles, responsibilities, and escalation pathways for AI oversight, particularly for post-⏎deployment monitoring.⏎Implementation and maintenance burden also pose a major challenge. Healthcare organizations must dedicate⏎substantial resources to local validation, workflow integration, cybersecurity review, staff training, and ongoing⏎performance monitoring. RSNA encourages HHS to prioritize policies and programs that reduce duplicative⏎requirements, promote reuse of standardized validation and monitoring approaches, and leverage automation to⏎lower the operational cost of safe AI deployment.⏎Scalability challenges are becoming increasingly pronounced as multiple AI tools are applied concurrently to the⏎same clinical data. RSNA has observed that overlapping alerts, notifications, and outputs from different algorithms⏎can contribute to alert fatigue and reduce clinician confidence. In addition, monitoring the cumulative impact of⏎multiple AI systems, including interactions between tools from different vendors, is not well supported by current⏎oversight models. RSNA recommends that HHS support scalable monitoring frameworks and interoperable⏎standards that enable health systems to assess both individual and combined AI tool performance.⏎Interoperability and integration limitations further impede adoption. Inconsistent data standards and non-⏎standardized AI outputs increase implementation complexity and limit the ability to efficiently monitor real-world⏎performance. RSNA recommends continued federal investment in interoperable data standards, structured⏎outputs, and standardized performance metrics to support safe and scalable AI integration into clinical workflows.⏎Finally, economic and regulatory uncertainty remains a persistent barrier. Healthcare organizations often face⏎unclear reimbursement pathways, misalignment between implementation costs and downstream benefits, and⏎uncertainty regarding post-deployment compliance expectations. RSNA encourages HHS to provide clearer⏎guidance and alignment across regulatory, reimbursement, and interoperability initiatives to reduce uncertainty⏎and support sustainable AI adoption.⏎9⏎⏎8) Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the⏎development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎As front-line users of AI-enabled medical devices and physicians ultimately responsible for patient care and⏎safety, radiologists rely on AI as decision-support that complements, but does not replace, clinical judgment. To⏎fulfill this role safely and effectively, AI outputs must be transparent, interpretable, and presented in a manner that⏎allows clinicians to contextualize results within the broader clinical picture.⏎Given current difficulties in obtaining valid data for training and testing AI models, broader interoperability, data⏎consistency and accessibility would contribute powerfully to accelerating innovation in AI for diagnostic imaging.⏎As described above, RSNA has conducted a series of AI challenges over the past ten years and is thus very⏎familiar with the difficulty of collecting and annotating the datasets needed for AI model development. We have⏎assembled a consortium of data-contributing sites from around the world and engaged expert radiologists to label⏎imaging datasets for use cases associated with each of these challenges. Our experience has shown the high⏎expense and limited scalability of this approach and the restricted applicability for clinical use of models generated⏎through these methods.⏎In addition, the closed nature of the network of health information systems in clinical use and the absence of well-⏎defined points of integration for new tools, including AI tools for diagnostic imaging, presents significant⏎challenges to accelerating the development of AI for clinical care. To make them capable of efficient integration in⏎the diagnostic imaging environment, developers building AI-enabled tools must invest in extensive research to⏎determine how to position them within a complex, heterogeneous and inconsistently linked array of systems and⏎workflows. While incumbent vendors now offer a variety of platforms for integrating AI tools, they are based on⏎proprietary integrations and thus force developers into restrictive commercial relationships and limit access to⏎best-of-breed tools for potential users. The absence of clear standardized APIs currently makes it difficult to⏎integrate AI-enabled tools efficiently into workflow and to evaluate and monitor their performance.⏎RSNA believes that ASTP-ONC can accelerate innovation in AI by facilitating access to clinical data, including⏎medical imaging, for AI development, validation, and monitoring. While our understanding is that ASTP-ONC has⏎generally avoided imposing requirements regarding medical devices, RSNA believes that diagnostic imaging must⏎be considered a core component of the patient medical record and that substantial benefits for patient care and⏎technologic innovation can be realized through a consistent set of regulations that foster interoperability and⏎improved access across health information technology platforms.⏎The USCDI defines an evolving set of data elements to be included in the electronic health record, and the Health⏎IT Certification Program defines standard Application Programming Interfaces for accessing and using these data.⏎RSNA supports expanding the USCDI to include effective access to imaging studies (e.g. through the proposed⏎Imaging Reference data element) and including view, download and transmit requirements for diagnostic imaging⏎in the Health IT Certification Program. Providing API-based access to imaging alongside other relevant clinical⏎data and allowing use of this data for research purposes (with proper security and privacy protections) would⏎facilitate the creation of multimodal datasets for building AI tools and enable multicenter, prospective validation of⏎AI models using real-world clinical and imaging data.⏎10⏎⏎9) What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Our understanding of patients’ goals for integrating AI into healthcare closely align with those of RSNA. Chief⏎among these is enhancing the quality of care and increasing access to healthcare services. AI holds significant⏎potential to broaden access to high-quality, personalized healthcare for all Americans, particularly those in⏎underserved and rural communities. Additionally, AI tools that streamline administrative workflows can help⏎ensure patients receive the right care at the right time by reducing barriers and delays.⏎Both patients and radiologists are optimistic about AI’s capacity to improve disease detection and diagnosis,⏎which can strengthen prevention strategies and lead to better outcomes through earlier identification and⏎treatment. Importantly, there is broad consensus that AI should serve to support and enhance physicians’⏎performance rather than replace their critical role in diagnostic decision-making. AI’s strength lies in synthesizing⏎complex medical histories, medication lists, and patient preferences, which enables more personalized treatment⏎plans and smoother transitions of care. For caregivers managing chronic or complex conditions, improved care⏎coordination made possible by AI is especially valuable.⏎In our view, many of the concerns that patients and caregivers have regarding the adoption of AI in clinical care⏎center on issues of trust, transparency, and explainability. Patients seek clear explanations of how AI contributes⏎to medical decisions, as well as assurance that clinicians understand and can appropriately question AI outputs.⏎Central to building this trust is the clinician’s ability to effectively validate and continuously monitor AI tools as they⏎are implemented in real-world care settings.⏎10) Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎The effectiveness of AI in healthcare depends on the strength of the research and data infrastructure that⏎supports its development, evaluation, and deployment. Yet, there is a relative dearth of literature on the actual⏎impact of AI-enabled tools in clinical care, and the limited evidence that exists is often conflicting.1,2 RSNA⏎recommends that HHS and its research funding agencies strategically invest in every stage of the AI development⏎process to optimize clinical outcomes and ensure safe adoption in healthcare.⏎First, foundational research should focus on building robust, adaptable, and generalizable algorithms that can⏎handle a wide variety of clinical scenarios and patient populations. This approach will help prevent performance⏎1 Najjar, R. Redefining Radiology: A Review of Artificial Intelligence Integration in Medical Imaging; Diagnostics, 13(17):2760⏎(2023). doi: 10.3390/diagnostics13172760,⏎2 Buijs, E., Maggioni, E., Mazziotta, F. et al. Clinical impact of AI in radiology department management: a systematic review.⏎Radiol med 129, 1656–1666 (2024). doi.org/10.1007/s11547-024-01880-1.⏎11⏎⏎variability and "diagnostic drift," ensuring AI solutions remain effective across different healthcare settings and⏎over time. Next, translational and implementation research is crucial for integrating AI into real-world clinical⏎workflows. These studies should address how AI tools can be efficiently embedded into existing systems,⏎maintain high standards for quality and patient safety, and foster seamless collaboration between technology and⏎clinicians. Additionally, research must examine human-AI interactions for both physicians and patients, exploring⏎factors such as trust, explainability, and user experience to identify ways to build confidence and understanding in⏎AI-assisted care. Finally, continued development and expansion of data and reporting standards is essential to⏎support interoperability, enable benchmarking across institutions, and establish comprehensive, ongoing⏎performance monitoring. Such standards will facilitate transparent evaluation and continuous improvement,⏎helping healthcare organizations responsibly deploy AI and maintain excellence in patient safety and clinical care.⏎As AI tools become increasingly embedded in clinical practice, it is essential that radiologists, technologists, and⏎other healthcare professionals are equipped with the knowledge needed to critically evaluate, interpret, and⏎appropriately use these technologies. RSNA encourages HHS to support federally funded AI education and⏎literacy initiatives tailored to healthcare professionals, with a focus on understanding intended use, limitations,⏎workflow integration, and patient safety implications.⏎RSNA also recommends collaboration between HHS and professional societies to develop standardized⏎education, training, and certification pathways that support AI competency in clinical care. Ongoing education⏎initiatives should extend to both clinicians and patients, ensuring transparency around how AI is used in care⏎delivery and reinforcing the role of clinicians as accountable decision-makers. Strengthening education and⏎workforce preparedness will be essential to sustaining trust, safety, and effective adoption of AI across⏎healthcare.⏎RSNA values the opportunity to provide input on this Request for Information and looks forward to continued⏎collaboration with HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC. Together, we aim to establish smart and⏎balanced regulatory frameworks for AI in healthcare that uphold patient safety without stifling innovation. For⏎additional information or questions, please contact RSNA’s Director of Government Relations, Libby O’Hare⏎(eohare@rsna.org).⏎Sincerely,⏎Carolyn C. Meltzer, MD⏎Chair of the Board⏎Radiological Society of North America⏎12♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0300♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Hospital Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Hospital Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted Electronically⏎RE: RIN 0955-AA13 Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use⏎of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Deputy Assistant Secretary Posnack,⏎On behalf of our nearly 5,000 member hospitals, health systems and other health care⏎organizations, our clinician partners — including more than 270,000 affiliated⏎physicians, 2 million nurses and other caregivers — and the 43,000 health care leaders⏎who belong to our professional membership groups, the American Hospital Association⏎(AHA) appreciates the opportunity to provide comment on the Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) request for information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.⏎We applaud HHS for evaluating regulation, reimbursement, and research and⏎development opportunities to advance the adoption of AI tools to support clinical care.⏎Our member hospitals and health systems have seen AI tools’ potential to improve⏎outcomes, increase access and reduce costs. From ambient listening technologies⏎assisting with clinical documentation to chatbots helping with scheduling and triaging to⏎algorithms supporting clinician interpretation of images, AI-based tools have already⏎made a significant positive impact on hospitals and the patients they serve.⏎Given AI’s potential to drive efficiencies and enhance the quality of care, our members⏎have urged that policy frameworks strike the appropriate balance of flexibility to enable⏎innovation while ensuring patient safety. Attached you will find our recommendations to⏎the three policy areas identified in the request for information (regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research and development), as well as feedback on specific⏎questions.⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 2 of 14⏎To accelerate AI adoption, we urge HHS to:⏎• Synchronize and leverage existing policy frameworks to avoid⏎redundancy. While AI policies should be flexible to keep pace with innovation,⏎they also should be synchronized and integrated with certain existing health care⏎policy frameworks to minimize redundancies.⏎• Remove regulatory barriers. Certain statutes and regulations in the health care⏎ecosystem, such as the patchwork of state privacy laws and 42 CFR Part 2,⏎have indirectly impacted hospitals and health systems’ ability to develop and⏎deploy certain AI tools. We provide recommendations on ways to reduce⏎regulatory barriers that inhibit the development and deployment of AI tools.⏎• Ensure the safe and effective use of AI. The AHA recommends policies that⏎ensure clinicians are included in the decision loop for algorithms that may impact⏎access to care or care delivery, provide consistency in privacy and security⏎standards for third-party vendors and use post-deployment standards for health⏎care AI to ensure ongoing integrity of tools.⏎• Align incentives and address infrastructural factors. Appropriate incentives⏎and infrastructure investment are necessary to expand AI in health care. These⏎are critical for both provider readiness and patient adoption. That being said,⏎reimbursement for AI tools should not come at the expense of other medical⏎services.⏎Our members recognize the potential of AI to transform care delivery and address some⏎critical challenges the health care ecosystem faces, such as escalating costs, staff⏎burnout and rising administrative burden. We look forward to partnering with HHS on⏎policy approaches that can help AI realize its transformative potential. Our detailed⏎comments are attached. Please contact me if you have questions, or feel free to have a⏎member of your team contact Jennifer Holloman, AHA’s director of health IT policy,⏎at jholloman@aha.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎/s/⏎Ashley Thompson⏎Senior Vice President⏎Public Policy Analysis and Development⏎Attachment A: Accelerating AI Adoption Detailed Responses⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 3 of 14⏎Appendix A. Accelerating AI Adoption Detailed Responses⏎REGULATION⏎The AHA applauds the administration’s recognition that excessive and overly restrictive⏎regulations can increase costs, reduce access to care, stifle innovation and hamper⏎competition.⏎The AHA has provided recommendations to the Office of Management and Budget,⏎HHS, Federal Trade Commission and Department of Justice on ways to reduce the⏎regulatory burden on hospitals and health systems.1,2,3,4 We have also provided⏎comments to the Office of Science Technology Policy on ways to reduce regulatory⏎burden, specifically to support the advancement of AI tools in response to its request for⏎information on regulatory reform for AI.5 We appreciate that HHS continues to seek⏎ways to reduce regulatory barriers to accelerate the adoption of AI in clinical care⏎through this request for information.⏎We have recommendations for the regulatory framework to accelerate AI adoption for⏎clinical care, including:⏎• Synchronizing policies with existing regulatory frameworks.⏎• Withdrawing certain proposals from the HIPAA security proposed rule.⏎• Strengthening federal HIPAA preemption.⏎• Removing the remaining 42 CFR Part 2 requirements.⏎• Including trained clinicians in the decision loop for health care AI tools that⏎impact access.⏎• Applying the same privacy and security standards for third-party vendors as⏎covered entities and business associates.⏎• Developing risk-based post-deployment standards for AI-enabled medical⏎devices.⏎• Issuing clarifying guidance on clinical versus administrative applications.⏎Additional details follow.⏎Synchronize with Existing Regulatory Frameworks⏎In general, the AHA believes that AI policies should be flexible to keep pace with the⏎rapid pace of innovation. At the same time, AI policy also should be synchronized and⏎1 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-05-12-aha-response-omb-deregulation-rfi⏎2 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-07-14-aha-comments-hhs-rfi-maha-initiative⏎3 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-05-23-aha-comments-ftc-anticompetitive-deregulations-rfi⏎4 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-05-23-aha-comments-doj-anticompetitive-deregulations-rfi⏎5 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-10-27-aha-responds-ostp-request-ai-policies-health-care⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 4 of 14⏎integrated within certain existing health care policy frameworks to minimize⏎redundancies. AI poses novel challenges from a policy development perspective, with⏎many existing regulations for medical devices, patient privacy and payment designed⏎around static technologies. Nevertheless, the AHA believes that AI policy should not be⏎considered in a vacuum, as it intersects with a wide range of other critical policy areas⏎with their own regulatory frameworks. Some areas of intersection include:⏎• Data Privacy. HIPAA provides baseline federal standards for the protection of⏎personal health information. HIPAA covers a wide range of health information⏎technology applications, including AI.⏎• Cybersecurity. The National Institute of Standards and Technology⏎cybersecurity framework and the HHS cybersecurity performance goals provide⏎reliable voluntary standards frameworks for cybersecurity.⏎• Premarket Testing. The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulations for⏎Software as a Medical Device require testing of the safety and efficacy of AI-⏎enabled medical devices through a premarket submission program.⏎• Transparency. The Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health IT (ASTP/ONC) requires certified health IT to⏎meet certain transparency requirements for AI.⏎• Anti-bias and Discrimination. Any entity receiving federal financial assistance,⏎including all health care providers and insurers, is prohibited from using AI tools⏎and algorithms that discriminate through the HHS Office for Civil Rights (OCR)⏎Anti-Bias and Discrimination regulations.⏎• Access to Care. The Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)⏎Medicare Advantage regulations specify that AI cannot “act alone” to terminate⏎or deny services. These regulations also establish that the health plan must⏎ensure the tool is accurate and bias-free.⏎Certainly, there are important questions about how to update or clarify how existing⏎regulations apply to AI tools, and where there may be gaps in regulation. However,⏎establishing standalone AI regulation outside of these existing frameworks may⏎unintentionally lead to redundancies and inefficiencies, as well as create confusion⏎about which rules would apply in which circumstances. Ultimately, this would hamper⏎innovation and impose artificial barriers on the development of AI tools. For these⏎reasons, we encourage HHS to synchronize policies with these existing⏎regulatory frameworks. We also encourage cross-agency coordination and guidance⏎to ensure that policies are aligned.⏎Withdraw 2024 HIPAA Security Rule Proposals⏎Health data privacy and security are essential for patient safety and quality of care. This⏎applies not only to AI tools, but also to any persons, entities or tools that leverage or⏎exchange personal health information. HIPAA provides sound foundational standards⏎for privacy, security and breach notification. While certain regulations have provided⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 5 of 14⏎clarifying guidance, other regulations would add unnecessary burden and run counter to⏎HIPAA’s original purpose of protecting health information.⏎For this reason, the AHA urges HHS to withdraw the Biden administration’s⏎proposed “HIPAA Security Rule to Strengthen the Cybersecurity of Electronic⏎Protected Health Information.” This rule included several technically infeasible and⏎misguided policies that would fail to strengthen protections for health information while⏎penalizing hospitals for issues beyond their control. For example, the rule would require⏎restoration of electronic information systems and data within 72 hours of a cybersecurity⏎incident. This arbitrary timeframe would be infeasible in particularly complex⏎cyberattacks. Furthermore, it may have the unintended consequence of increasing risk⏎by requiring hospitals to bring systems online before they can complete a full threat⏎assessment and isolate their exposure to further attacks. These rules also place the⏎onus for ensuring cybersecurity on hospitals rather than the broader ecosystem. Given⏎that most PHI data breaches reported to OCR were the result of hacking incidents⏎targeting non-hospital health care providers, including third-party service and software⏎providers, cybersecurity standards must address the full spectrum of stakeholders that⏎collect, hold or transmit personal health information. The AHA does not support⏎proposals for mandatory cybersecurity requirements levied on hospitals as if they were⏎at fault for hackers’ success in perpetrating a crime. Instead, the AHA supports⏎voluntary consensus-based cybersecurity practices such as the HHS cybersecurity⏎performance goals.⏎Strengthen Federal HIPAA Preemption⏎While HIPAA generally preempts contrary state law, there are specific exceptions to that⏎preemption that have enabled a plethora of differing state laws that bear on health data⏎privacy.⏎The current approach to preemption has burdened hospitals and health systems with a⏎myriad of overlapping legal requirements, raising compliance costs and diverting limited⏎resources that could otherwise be used on patient care. In addition, the patchwork of⏎state and federal health information privacy requirements remains a significant barrier to⏎the robust sharing of patient information necessary for coordinated clinical treatment.⏎For instance, it makes it much more challenging for providers to use a common⏎electronic health record that is a critical part of the infrastructure necessary for⏎effectively coordinating patient care and maintaining population health. It also impedes⏎the development and deployment of AI tools, as data drives algorithmic validity.⏎We encourage the administration to work with Congress to address this issue⏎and enact a full HIPAA preemption provision. HIPAA is more than sufficient to⏎protect patient privacy and, if interpreted correctly, it strikes the appropriate balance⏎between health information privacy and valuable information sharing. Varying state laws⏎only add costs and create complications for hospitals and health systems. As such, the⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 6 of 14⏎AHA reiterates its long-standing recommendation that Congress strengthen HIPAA⏎preemption.⏎Remove Remaining 42 CFR Part 2 Requirements⏎We urge the administration to work with Congress to remove remaining requirements⏎under 42 CFR Part 2 that hinder care team access to important health information.⏎These regulations require separate maintenance of records on substance use disorder⏎(SUD) information, which prevents the integration of behavioral and physical health care⏎because the patient data cannot be used and disclosed like all other health care data.⏎This can also affect SUD providers’ ability to leverage AI tools for care delivery. Despite⏎regulatory changes in recent years, the regulations in Part 2 are outdated, fail to protect⏎patient privacy, and, in fact, erect barriers to providing coordinated, whole-person care⏎to people with a history of SUD. By working with Congress, the administration can⏎resolve the statutory conflicts that prevent full alignment of 42 CFR Part 2 requirements⏎with the HIPAA requirements that govern other patient health information.⏎Include Trained Clinicians in Decision Loop for Health Care AI Tools That Impact⏎Access⏎A key driver of excessive administrative costs for hospitals and health systems is the⏎onerous requirements imposed by commercial insurers to check patients’ eligibility for⏎coverage, bill for payment, and process prior authorizations and coverage denial⏎appeals. Most claims initially denied by insurers (70%) are ultimately paid, meaning a⏎significant amount of administrative cost is a complete waste.6⏎To make matters worse, commercial insurers’ use of AI to determine disposition of⏎claims and prior authorizations has exacerbated inappropriate denials. A U.S. Senate⏎Permanent Subcommittee on Investigations report from last year found that certain⏎plans had significant increases in prior authorization denials, in part driven by⏎automated tools, and that the use of AI for prior authorization potentially targeted⏎financial gain over medical necessity.7⏎To mitigate this, we have advocated for HHS and congressional action to ensure⏎that clinicians — not just AI tools — are included in the decision loop for any⏎recommendations of partial or full denial of requested items or services.8,9 While⏎6 https://premierinc.com/newsroom/policy/80-premier-members-call-for-medicare-advantage-changes-to-⏎address-payment-denials-and-delays⏎7 https://www.hsgac.senate.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024.10.17-PSI-Majority-Staff-Report-on-Medicare-⏎Advantage.pdf⏎8 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-09-29-aha-supports-administration-facilitating-health-insurer-⏎pledge-reform-prior-authorization⏎9 https://www.aha.org/testimony/2025-10-09-aha-statement-record-senate-help-committee-hearing-ai-⏎health-care⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 7 of 14⏎the use of AI tools to more quickly process prior authorizations is not inherently⏎problematic, it is imperative that any recommendation to deny care, whether AI-⏎generated or not, is independently reviewed by a clinician. Further, human reviewers⏎should have the requisite training and expertise to provide an informed medical decision⏎about a patient’s condition and the proposed treatment plan.⏎We have also urged for algorithmic transparency so that clinicians can understand the⏎reason a request was denied. Providers and patients are generally unaware when AI⏎tools are used in the prior authorization process, much less have visibility on the inputs⏎that drove a recommendation for approval or denial. Indeed, recent data from a survey⏎of payers across 16 states conducted by the National Association of Insurance⏎Commissioners show that only 23% of plans disclose to providers how and when AI is⏎used.10⏎Apply the Same Privacy and Security Standards for Third-party Applications That⏎Hold/Process PHI as Covered Entities and Business Associates⏎According to the HHS OCR, the number of individuals impacted by health care data⏎breaches increased from 27 million in 2020 to a staggering 259 million in 2024.11⏎Notably, most protected health information (PHI) data breaches reported to OCR were⏎the result of hacking incidents targeting non-hospital health care providers, including⏎third-party service and software providers.⏎AI systems rely on large data sets to maximize their predictive power. However,⏎aggregating large data sets may also pose unique cybersecurity vulnerabilities that can⏎further be exposed by privacy and security standards gaps. With the rise in third-party⏎vendor PHI data breaches, it is essential that entities that hold or process PHI (including⏎certain AI vendors that may not meet the definition for covered entities or business⏎associates under the current law) and are not currently covered by HIPAA be subject to⏎similarly rigorous privacy and security standards.⏎We recommend that third-party entities (including AI vendors) that collect, hold or⏎transmit PHI should be held to the same standards and accountability as covered⏎entities and business associates. Third parties also should be accountable for the⏎privacy and security of data they pull from covered entities and business associates.⏎Develop Risk-based Post-deployment Standards for AI-enabled Medical Devices⏎Hospitals and health systems continually assess the strengths and limitations of all AI⏎models they use. The “black box” nature of many AI systems can make it more⏎10 https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-⏎files/NAIC%20AI%20Health%20Survey%20Report%20.pdf⏎11 https://ocrportal.hhs.gov/ocr/breach/breach_report.jsf⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 8 of 14⏎challenging for hospitals and health systems to identify flaws in models that may affect⏎the accuracy and validity of an AI tool’s analyses and recommendations. There are⏎many reports of certain AI tools producing “hallucinations” or false results based on⏎flaws in model design or biases in underlying data. These factors underscore the⏎importance of ongoing developer testing to maintain AI model validity.⏎As we recently commented to the FDA in response to its request for public comment,⏎“Measuring and Evaluating Artificial Intelligence-enabled Medical Device Performance⏎in the Real-World,” we encourage agencies to pursue risk-based approaches to⏎monitoring and evaluation activities, whereby factors for potential risk to quality and⏎patient safety are accounted for in the measures and scope of monitoring.12 In the spirit⏎of a risk-based approach, the FDA could consider adding requirements for⏎manufacturers to conduct a range of monitoring, from periodic revalidation activities to⏎ongoing surveillance, depending on the risk level of the AI tool. In developing such⏎frameworks, agencies should seek feedback from device makers, hospitals and other⏎providers, as well as standards development groups.⏎At the same time, evaluation and monitoring activities should not be overly burdensome⏎and resource-intensive. As the FDA considers approaches to AI measurement and⏎evaluation, we encourage the agency to consider end-user burden and take steps to⏎minimize it. A risk-based approach to measurement and evaluation could focus scarce⏎resources, such as time, personnel and cost, on the highest-risk applications and also⏎align with the approach of domestically accredited and international standards⏎development groups.⏎Continue to Clarify Guidance for Clinical Versus Administrative AI Tools⏎Section 201(h) of the Food, Drug and Cosmetic Act identifies that certain AI applications⏎are considered medical devices if they are “intended for use in the diagnosis of disease⏎or other conditions, or in the cure, mitigation, treatment, or prevention of disease.” The⏎21st Century Cures Act excluded certain AI tools from the purview of medical device⏎oversight. For example, this statute excludes certain clinical decision support tools from⏎the definition of “medical devices,” so long as they support clinical decision making, with⏎the clinician independently reviewing recommendations. Clinical decision support tools⏎are often built to address a particular challenge for a specific period. Additional⏎requirements can add barriers to delivering timely solutions and may also be impractical⏎if the tool is only designed to support clinicians for a short timeframe (such as six⏎months). We appreciate that the FDA has attempted to issue clarifying guidance on its⏎interpretation of the statute. However, given the pace of technological advancement, we⏎encourage HHS to continue issuing non-binding guidance that includes examples, use⏎cases and frequently asked questions. Guidance on how federal frameworks apply to⏎non-medical devices would be especially helpful, and could include considerations for⏎12 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-12-01-aha-letter-fda-ai-enabled-medical-devices⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 9 of 14⏎documentation, monitoring, security obligations and reporting requirements. As a⏎coordinating entity, HHS can provide unifying guidance across OCR, ASTP/ONC, CMS,⏎FDA and research entities to support the safe use of non-medical AI tools.⏎REIMBURSEMENT⏎The AHA recognizes the pivotal role that AI plays in care delivery today and its potential⏎to transform the patient and provider experience in the future. We applaud HHS for⏎acknowledging the continued evolution of AI tools and the desire to consider updated⏎methodologies to appropriately reimburse for these services. While hospitals and health⏎systems recognize the potential benefits of AI solutions, inadequate reimbursement has⏎left many without the resources to invest in the infrastructure necessary to develop and⏎deploy AI tools. Implementing new technologies and standards often requires significant⏎financial investment, education and workflow changes for health care providers.⏎Ensuring appropriate reimbursement can support wider adoption of these tools and⏎ultimately support improved access to services. Although there are some forms of⏎reimbursement for AI for inpatient, outpatient and physician services (including new⏎technology add-on payments and ambulatory and procedural codes), they are narrowly⏎defined and do not account for the full spectrum of AI tools. Furthermore, they do not⏎capture all cost factors associated with AI tools.⏎We have provided reimbursement recommendations to CMS through requests for⏎information as part of our calendar year (CY) 2026 outpatient prospective payment⏎system (OPPS) and CY 2026 physician fee schedule (PFS) comments.13,14 We believe⏎updates to payment for AI should not come at the expense of other services. We also⏎have feedback on cost factors to consider to “right-size” payment for AI. Finally, aside⏎from reimbursement, cross-agency coordination can support needed infrastructure⏎investment, particularly to support AT advancement in rural and underserved areas.⏎Additional details on reimbursement recommendations are detailed below.⏎Updates to AI Payment Should Not Come at the Expense of Other Services⏎In general, reimbursement for hospitals, hospital outpatient departments and physicians⏎has fallen short. Despite escalating expenses, Medicare reimbursement continues to lag⏎behind inflation — covering just 83 cents for every dollar spent by hospitals in 2023,⏎resulting in over $100 billion in underpayments, according to an AHA analysis of our⏎Annual Survey data.15 As we recently noted to the Medicare Payment Advisory⏎Commission, the Medicare program has not fully covered the costs of serving Medicare⏎13 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-09-15-aha-comments-cms-cy-2026-outpatient-asc-proposed-⏎payment-rule⏎14 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2025-09-12-aha-comments-cms-cy-2026-physician-fee-schedule-⏎proposed-rule⏎15 https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 10 of 14⏎patients since 2002 — a full 23 years ago.16 The inadequacy of Medicare⏎reimbursement can also be seen in physician reimbursement. Data from the American⏎Medical Association indicate that physician payment has dropped by 33% since 2001,⏎when accounting for inflation.17⏎The budget-neutral payment increases have meant that increases in certain services⏎have resulted in cuts to others. While we support “rightsizing” of payment for SaaS,⏎this should not come at the expense of other services.⏎Consider Other Cost Factors When Determining Payment for AI Tools⏎Implementing new technologies and standards often requires significant financial⏎investment and workflow changes for health care providers. Expanded adoption of⏎tools requires alignment of incentives. While we appreciate the efforts to update⏎reimbursement for new technologies, historical payment within OPPS and PFS⏎structures has not fully accounted for the costs of these services. This includes⏎payment for certain CPT codes for AI, as well as bundled payment in risk-based⏎arrangements.⏎There are specific factors that HHS should consider when setting payment rates for AI⏎tools, since the costs associated with developing, deploying and maintaining AI tools⏎extend beyond the software. Examples of cost factors that should be considered⏎include:⏎• Clinical Time for Validation. AI-enabled tools augment — not replace — human⏎capacity. In fact, these tools still require significant human direction, such as⏎when developing treatment plans and supervising outcomes. For example, while⏎an AI-enabled tool may recommend a particular course of treatment, ultimately a⏎clinician is still making the final decision in consultation with patients and their⏎caregivers. Reimbursement should account for the time required to validate AI⏎outputs.⏎• Maintenance. AI and Software as a Service (SaaS) tools require stakeholders to⏎engage in routine maintenance and post-deployment testing to ensure the⏎ongoing integrity of tools. Maintenance may include technology vendors,⏎developers and providers.⏎• Cybersecurity Insurance. While the expansion of SaaS offerings holds⏎tremendous promise, there are also potential cybersecurity risks. According to⏎U.S. government reporting, the most significant cyber threats targeting U.S.⏎critical infrastructure, including health care, originate from noncooperative foreign⏎16 https://www.aha.org/lettercomment/2026-01-09-aha-comments-medpac-payment-update-⏎recommendations⏎17 https://www.ama-assn.org/system/files/2025-medicare-updates-inflation-chart.pdf⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 11 of 14⏎jurisdictions.18,19,20,21 Cross-border hacking incidents, which result in the theft of⏎PHI and ransomware attacks targeting health care, have increased dramatically,⏎rising nearly tenfold since 2020. As previously discussed, most PHI data⏎breaches reported to OCR were the result of hacking incidents targeting non-⏎hospital health care providers, including third-party service and software⏎providers. The rise in frequency and severity of cyberattacks accompanying the⏎expansion of SaaS tools has driven increased cybersecurity premiums.⏎Reimbursement models should account for this. Just as malpractice is factored⏎into payment (e.g., malpractice relative value units in the PFS), we encourage⏎rising cybersecurity costs also be considered.⏎• Software and Storage Fees. These costs also include software licenses, as well⏎as data storage. SaaS offerings generally rely on large data sets, which also⏎require servers. We encourage CMS to consider the costs for maintaining this⏎underlying data infrastructure.⏎Address Infrastructure Barriers⏎The expansion of digital health products, including AI tools, to rural and underserved⏎populations has been hindered in part by a lack of access to enabling technologies⏎(such as broadband, reliable Wi-Fi or smartphones) and education to support digital⏎literacy. Infrastructure investment is needed to support AI tool advancement in these⏎communities.⏎Indeed, the Federal Communications Commission (FCC) reported that in 2020, over⏎22% of Americans in rural areas lacked access to appropriate broadband (fixed⏎terrestrial 25/3 Mbps) compared to 1.5% of urban areas.22 Furthermore, according to a⏎recent report from the Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation, over 26% of⏎Medicare beneficiaries reported not having computer or smartphone access at home.23⏎The lack of infrastructure, such as broadband and reliable Wi-Fi, has contributed to the⏎“digital divide,” where rural and other underserved areas have less access to digital⏎services, including AI tools for clinicians and patients. These data points suggest that⏎investment in foundational infrastructure and educational resources may increase⏎providers’ and patients’ access to digital health and AI applications. We recognize that⏎some of these constraints do not fall exclusively under HHS’ purview. Therefore, we⏎encourage cross-agency collaboration to develop training and potential grant funding⏎18https://www.dni.gov/files/ODNI/documents/assessments/ATA-2025-Unclassified-Report.pdf⏎19https://www.ic3.gov/AnnualReport/Reports/2024_IC3Report.pdf⏎20 https://usun.usmission.gov/remarks-at-a-un-security-council-briefing-on-ransomware-attacks-against-⏎hospitals-and-other-healthcare-facilities-and-services/⏎21 https://www.cisa.gov/topics/cyber-threats-and-advisories/nation-state-cyber-actors⏎22 https://www.fcc.gov/reports-research/reports/broadband-progress-reports/2020-broadband-⏎deployment-report⏎23 https://aspe.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/documents/4e1853c0b4885112b2994680a58af9ed/telehealth-⏎hps-ib.pdf⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 12 of 14⏎opportunities to support patient educational efforts on digital health tools. This could⏎include coordination across agencies such as HHS, the FCC, the Department of⏎Commerce, the Department of Agriculture and the Department of Education.⏎RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT⏎Hospitals and health systems across the country recognize AI’s potential to transform⏎research. AI’s ability to process large amounts of data holds promise in supporting⏎targeted therapeutics and precision medicine. We have also heard from members how⏎AI is supporting clinical trial recruitment by processing large amounts of data to quickly⏎identify potentially eligible candidates.⏎We appreciate that the administration is seeking ways to build regulatory sandboxes⏎and testbeds to support AI-enabled research, as was identified in the AI Action Plan.24⏎As HHS works to identify research and policy priorities, we urge the agency to establish⏎mechanisms for provider input. Effective AI policies cannot be developed without⏎engagement from the providers responsible for the care of patients that they serve. And⏎while clinical use of AI tools is largely in pilot phases, the expansion to routine care⏎underscores the need for ongoing engagement and partnership.⏎SPECIFIC QUESTIONS⏎Novel Legal and Implementation Issues⏎While AI tools offer significant potential benefits, many providers are concerned about⏎liability ambiguity, particularly when AI algorithms could result in inaccurate⏎recommendations that lead to poor outcomes. Indeed, the lack of clarity surrounding⏎liability is a significant barrier to provider adoption of AI tools. AI systems are often⏎developed and deployed with inputs from a variety of stakeholders, where providers are⏎just one of many sources. Also, certain algorithm elements may be treated by⏎developers as proprietary, which makes it challenging for hospitals and other AI users to⏎identify model flaws, discrepancies between training data and real-world applications, or⏎any model drift over time.⏎While many of these issues may intersect with case law and state-level malpractice⏎statutes, HHS can play a vital role in supporting reasonable standards for developer⏎transparency and post-deployment monitoring. Some of these issues underscore the⏎importance of policies like post-deployment standards to ensure the ongoing integrity of⏎tools and transparency standards for health IT certification. As the agency continues to⏎explore novel liability challenges, we urge HHS to provide formal mechanisms for⏎provider input.⏎24 https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Americas-AI-Action-Plan.pdf⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 13 of 14⏎Vendor Certification⏎The volume of new entrants entering the health care AI space has made it challenging⏎for hospitals and health systems to keep track of AI applications that may generate⏎value. While hospitals and health systems have dedicated internal resources to support⏎governance processes and pre-contract vetting, some hospitals may not have⏎resources available to support this on a broader scale. Streamlined voluntary⏎certification, built on industry standards, may provide a pathway for vendors to⏎demonstrate functionality and provide baseline assurance to providers that certain⏎standards are met. This can support responsible and scalable adoption of AI tools.⏎Promising AI Applications and Examples of AI Falling Short⏎AI holds the potential to drastically change hospital operations and patient care delivery.⏎Among other benefits, these innovations can improve workflow, enhance the overall⏎patient experience by reducing wait times and ensure timely medical interventions.⏎A few notable examples of promising AI applications in hospitals include:⏎• Imaging/Radiology. AI is significantly enhancing the accuracy and efficiency of⏎X-ray and MRI reviews in hospitals. AI can detect and alert clinicians to subtle⏎changes in tissue images, which is crucial for early disease detection.⏎• Ambient Listening Tools. These tools automatically transcribe and organize⏎clinical notes. This reduces the administrative burden on health care providers,⏎allowing them to focus more on patient care. Ultimately, these tools can reduce⏎provider documentation time, increase appointment capacity and improve staff⏎satisfaction.⏎• Scheduling. AI-powered scheduling tools are enhancing the efficiency of⏎procedures and internal clinical operations by optimizing appointment times,⏎resource allocation and staff schedules.⏎One example where AI is falling short can be seen in commercial payers’ use of AI tools⏎for prior authorization. Commercial payers’ prior authorization processes have⏎historically been plagued by high denial and overturn rates on appeal. One study of⏎Medicare Advantage plans found that over 80% of denied prior authorization requests⏎that were appealed were successfully overturned.25 The onerous administrative burden⏎has contributed to clinician burnout and has driven up costs. Utilization of AI tools for⏎prior authorizations by commercial payers has actually catalyzed inappropriate denials,⏎resulting in more administrative burden for providers to appeal. A recent American⏎Medical Association survey also found that 62% of doctors think that payer use of AI is⏎25 https://www.kff.org/medicare/nearly-50-million-prior-authorization-requests-were-sent-to-medicare-⏎advantage-insurers-in-2023/⏎⏎The Honorable Steven Posnack⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 14 of 14⏎increasing denials for medically necessary care.26 This is one cautionary example of⏎where expanded use of AI tools without proper oversight can negatively impact patients’⏎access to services and contribute to waste in the system. That is why the AHA has⏎urged that trained clinicians be in the decision loop for AI tools affecting access to care.⏎Governance⏎Hospitals and health systems have worked to adapt their governance processes for AI⏎use to meet the pace of technology advancement. Hospitals and health systems often⏎use multi-disciplinary teams to review AI use cases, technologies and value over time.⏎According to the AHA’s Health IT supplemental survey, 74% of hospitals have multiple⏎teams responsible for evaluating predictive AI, including (but not limited to) senior⏎leaders, department leaders and IT staff, with many having specific committees for⏎machine learning and/or clinical decision support.27 And while hospitals remain⏎committed to ensuring AI tools are accurate, safe and effective, this should not rest⏎solely on hospitals and health systems.⏎While hospitals and health systems continually assess the strengths and limitations of⏎AI models they use, third-party vendors must be responsible for the ongoing integrity of⏎the tools they sell. The “black box” nature of many AI systems can make it more⏎challenging for hospitals and health systems to identify flaws in models that may affect⏎the accuracy and validity of an AI tool’s analyses and recommendations. As such, post-⏎market measurement and evaluation standards should be developed for vendors.⏎Standards should include performance metrics, evaluation thresholds and⏎communication requirements for ongoing performance to end users.⏎Additionally, some hospitals (particularly rural, critical access and other safety net⏎hospitals) may not have the staff or resources to support governance structures and⏎ongoing measurement activities. As such, many of these health care providers have not⏎deployed AI tools. Resource and infrastructure barriers can exacerbate the “digital⏎divide” in certain geographies, where rural and other underserved areas have less⏎access to digital services, including AI tools for clinicians and patients.⏎26 https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/prior-authorization/how-ai-leading-more-prior-⏎authorization-denials⏎27 https://healthit.gov/data/data-briefs/hospital-trends-use-evaluation-and-governance-predictive-ai-2023-⏎2024/#DB80_Appendix_Figure_A2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0301♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charles♔Johnson♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My cardiologist over at Prisma Health in Greenville ran me through a cardiac CT scan back in the spring of 2023 and the AI software they were using flagged something in my left anterior descending artery that he told me later he might have graded as borderline on his own. I ended up having a stent placed that July. So I'm not here to say this technology is worthless, I've seen what it can do firsthand.⏎⏎But what I really want to say is I'm confused about why the federal government is spending energy writing rules about AI in hospitals when social media companies are out here doing lord knows what with health information people post every single day and nobody does anything about it. I see ads on Facebook for heart medications within two days of my surgery. That's not a coincidence. Where's the rulemaking on that. My daughter tried to explain to me something about data brokers but I still don't understand how that's legal while everybody's worried about paperwork for doctors offices.⏎⏎I'm not saying the hospital AI stuff doesn't matter. It does. I just think the priorities are backwards sometimes and the people writing these comments who work for big companies are going to drown out the patients who don't have lawyers helping them type things up.⏎⏎The one thing I do want to make sure gets heard is about whether these systems work the same for everybody. I'm a 67 year old Black man who's worked construction his whole life, my health history looks different than the people whose data probably got used to build these programs. I don't know enough about how the training data works to say exactly what the problem is but it worries me. I asked my cardiologist about it and he honestly said he didn't know either, which I respected him for saying but it didn't make me feel better.⏎⏎I think HHS should require whoever makes these tools to be upfront about who their data came from before a hospital can use it on patients. Not just a technical document nobody reads, something that a regular person could actually look at and understand.⏎⏎Anyway I also still want to know who exactly had access to that scan from 2023 because I was never given a straight answer about that and I submitted a records request to the hospital in February and they sent me back a form that I don't think answered what I actually asked.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0302♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-26T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Brown♔♔Texas♔♔United States♔American Optometric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔My work is with community mental health centers across Texas, and what I keep coming back to when I read through this RFI is a pretty simple question: who exactly is going to get hurt first when these tools get it wrong?⏎⏎Because they will get it wrong. The question is whose body carries that cost.⏎⏎The framing in this RFI is about acceleration. Moving faster, removing barriers to adoption, getting AI into clinical settings. I understand the appeal of that framing and I'm not saying AI has no place in healthcare. But when I look at what my member facilities are actually dealing with — providers in communities like the Rio Grande Valley or parts of East Dallas where the patient population is predominantly low-income, predominantly Latino or Black, often uninsured — the urgency I feel is almost the opposite of what this RFI seems to assume.⏎⏎We already know what biased algorithms do in practice. The pulse oximetry situation in Black patients was not a theoretical concern, it caused real delayed treatment decisions and it went on for decades before it got serious attention. There was published research in 2020 and 2021 showing the magnitude of the problem and the healthcare system had been using those devices without question the whole time. That's exactly the dynamic that worries me about AI: something that appears precise and data-driven gets embedded in clinical workflows and then nobody questions the output because it came from the machine.⏎⏎Our policy team looked at 14 behavioral health AI platforms that are being actively marketed to community mental health centers right now. Fewer than a third of them disclosed anything about the demographics of the data they trained or validated on. That is not a minor technical gap. If a tool was trained mostly on data from commercially insured patients in urban hospital systems and you're using it to triage patients at a safety-net clinic in Laredo, you genuinely do not know what it's doing. And yet the vendor agreement doesn't have to tell you that.⏎⏎HHS is asking what regulatory actions it can take to accelerate AI adoption. I want to answer the flip side of that question, which is: what needs to be in place before acceleration is responsible? At a minimum I think any AI tool used in clinical care decisions should have to disclose the demographic composition of its training and validation datasets. That's not an unreasonable ask. It's basic information. If a vendor can't provide it or won't, that should be disqualifying for procurement by any facility that receives federal funding.⏎⏎The informed consent piece is also something I want HHS to take seriously. 54% of the facilities in our Q2 2024 review said their current vendor agreements don't require patients to be told when an algorithmic tool is involved in their care. That's a majority. People have a right to know when a computer is making recommendations about their treatment, especially when the accuracy of that computer for people who look like them is unknown.⏎⏎I realize there are people who will argue that requiring equity audits and disclosure will slow adoption and that the potential benefits of AI are too large to let process requirements get in the way. I don't find that persuasive when the populations most likely to bear the downside risk of getting it wrong are exactly the ones who've already been failed by every other part of the system. The fact that prior authorization AI tools have been documented to deny claims at higher rates for Black and Latino patients — that's not an edge case, that's a preview.⏎⏎What I'm asking is that HHS not treat equity review as a nice-to-have that can be figured out after rollout. Once these tools are in the workflow at scale, getting them back out is nearly impossible and nobody wants to tell providers to stop using something they've already built their processes around. The window for getting this right is before the contracts get signed, not after.⏎⏎There's also a practical question about who bears the cost of doing bias auditing. Small community mental health centers operating on Medicaid reimbursement rates cannot afford to independently audit the tools they're procuring. If HHS wants these settings to adopt AI responsibly, there needs to be some federal infrastructure for that — a shared testing resource, a registry of audited tools, something. Right now the burden falls entirely on individual facilities and most of them♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0303♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Clark♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Howard University College of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My concern here is not with AI adoption per se. I've spent the better part of two decades studying how health policy decisions made in high-confidence, resource-rich environments get deployed onto populations those environments never adequately represented, and what I keep seeing in this RFI is a familiar pattern: the acceleration language is in the foreground, and the equity infrastructure is either absent or deferred to future guidance.⏎⏎Let me be direct about what the evidence actually shows, because I think the Department may be understating how serious the replication problem already is. A 2024 survey through the American College of Surgeons Quality Programs network, covering 847 surgical departments across 42 states, found that 68% of respondents could not replicate vendor-reported AI performance metrics in their own patient populations. Not small deviations. Couldn't replicate them. And when we looked at the published validation literature for clinical AI in surgical settings, fewer than 23% of studies included prospective real-world outcome data outside the original development cohort. These findings were presented at the 2024 Clinical Congress. This isn't a theoretical gap I'm worried about. The gap is documented, it's substantial, and the RFI is proposing to put deployment incentives in front of it.⏎⏎The pulse oximetry literature is by now well established -- Sjoding et al. in NEJM, 2020, showed that pulse oximeters occult hypoxemia at significantly higher rates in Black patients, and subsequent work has shown this propagated into clinical decision support systems that were treating the oximeter reading as ground truth. The sepsis prediction algorithm issues in non-white populations are also in the peer-reviewed record. These aren't edge cases that got caught and corrected. They're examples of bias that persisted for years in deployed systems because aggregate performance metrics looked acceptable.⏎⏎What worries me about the framing of this RFI is that "accelerating adoption" in the absence of mandatory, standardized, pre-deployment bias auditing requirements will almost certainly reproduce this pattern at much larger scale. A system that performs well on aggregate accuracy measures can fail systematically for Black patients, for rural Medicaid beneficiaries, for patients with limited English proficiency -- and those failures will be invisible in the top-line numbers that vendors and health systems are likely to use to justify deployment. I've seen this in the surgical quality data. The populations who experience the worst failures are also the populations least likely to generate the kind of documented complaint that triggers a post-market review.⏎⏎I'm genuinely uncertain about what the right regulatory vehicle is here, whether this is primarily an FDA software-as-medical-device problem or an ONC certification problem or whether HHS needs to develop something new specifically for insurance and utilization management applications of AI, which are not obviously covered by either existing framework. That uncertainty is itself a problem, because vendors are not waiting for the framework to be resolved before deploying.⏎⏎The specific thing I'd ask HHS to address is this: what is the mechanism by which a hospital or health system that discovers a disparate impact from a deployed AI system is required to report that finding, to whom, on what timeline, and what happens to the vendor's authorization to continue operating that system in the meantime? I have not been able to find a clear answer to that question in the existing regulatory structure, and the RFI doesn't address it directly either.⏎⏎There's also the question of who bears the cost of prospective validation studies stratified by race, income, and geography. If that cost falls entirely on health systems or academic medical centers, the incentive to conduct that validation is low, particularly for smaller or safety-net institutions that are already operating under thin margins. The vendor community has a strong interest in submitting favorable pre-deployment data and a much weaker interest in funding the kind of ongoing post-deployment monitoring that would catch the failures I'm describing.⏎⏎I want to flag one more thing that I think doesn't get enough attention in these discussions. The automation bias literature is pretty consistent in showing that clinicians are less likely to override algorithmic recommendations than they are to override the same recommendation made by another clinician, even when the algorithmic recommendation is wrong. This means the harm from a biased AI system isn't just the bias in the output. It's the suppression of the clinical skepticism that might otherwise catch the error. And that suppression effect is probably strongest in high-volume, high-stress environments -- which is exactly where AI deployment is being prioritized because that's where efficiency gains look most attractive.⏎⏎I do think there are genuine potential benefits from clinical AI, particularly in imaging interpretation and early warning systems where the evidence base is stronger. I'm not arguing for a moratorium. But I keep coming back to the question of what HHS's theory of accountability is for the harms that will occur during an accelerated deployment period, and I haven't seen that articulated anywhere in this RFI with the specificity the question deserves.⏎⏎Kenneth Clark, PhD⏎Professor of Health Policy⏎Howard University College of Medicine⏎Washington, DC♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0304♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kimberly♔Garcia♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔Arterys♔♔♔♔♔♔My title is Chief Medical Officer, but what I spend most of my time doing lately is explaining to payers why a tool that received FDA clearance in March of 2023 still hasn't made it onto a single coverage determination list in our region. Eleven months was our average wait, and for one of our cardiac imaging products — which had gone through IRB review, prospective clinical validation, the whole process — the gap between clearance and reimbursement hit fourteen months. I'm not citing that to complain about our company's revenue cycle. I'm citing it because HHS is now asking how to accelerate adoption, and I want to be honest about what that question misses.⏎⏎The reimbursement gap isn't evidence that the system is too slow. It's evidence that nobody has actually decided who is responsible when an AI tool makes a consequential error and the patient is harmed. Payers know this. That's why they stall. They're not being obstructionist — they're waiting for accountability structures that don't yet exist, and no amount of CMS coding reform or accelerated clearance pathways is going to change that calculus until the underlying liability question gets answered.⏎⏎What concerns me about the framing of this RFI is that it treats adoption speed as the metric we should be optimizing for. I understand the argument. There are tools that genuinely help clinicians and patients, and yes, the current system slows them down. I believe that. But the tools that get accelerated under a streamlined regime won't only be the good ones. There's no selection mechanism being proposed here that would distinguish between a well-validated AI system with a coherent governance structure and a mediocre one with good marketing. The reimbursement lever, if pulled too fast, applies to both.⏎⏎The deeper issue — and I keep coming back to this even when I try to think about it from a different angle — is that embedding AI into reimbursement structures at scale is not really reversible. Once a CPT code exists for an AI-assisted diagnostic service, once health systems have restructured workflows around it, once you have a generation of residents who trained with these tools as standard of care, you've made a choice about how much of clinical medicine gets mediated by algorithmic systems. That choice deserves a real democratic process, not an RFI comment period.⏎⏎I want to be clear that I'm not opposed to AI in clinical care. That would be an odd position for someone in my role. But there's a version of "acceleration" that actually serves patients and clinicians, and it starts with getting the governance foundations built first — clear liability allocation for AI tools that operate outside the traditional medical device framework, coverage determination standards that don't require each regional payer to reinvent the wheel, and some kind of prospective process for deciding which clinical decisions should remain with human physicians regardless of what the model can do.⏎⏎The comment period for this RFI closes in a matter of weeks, and the questions being posed are genuinely hard ones that health systems, ethicists, patient advocates, and policymakers have been wrestling with for years without resolution. I'd ask HHS to consider whether the pace of this process itself is telling us something about the gap between where governance currently is and where it would need to be before acceleration is the right word for what we're doing.⏎⏎Regarding the specific question about regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS might take: the most useful single step, in my view, would be to convene a formal rulemaking process specifically on AI liability allocation rather than treating it as a downstream consequence of reimbursement reform. Everything else is going to keep stalling until that's resolved, and my fourteen-month example is going to keep being the norm rather than the exception regardless of what else gets changed.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0305♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Pictor Labs Inc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Pictor Labs Inc♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Department of Health and Human Services⏎From: PictorLabs⏎Date: Jan 20, 2026⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of⏎Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Contributors: Rochelle Wagner PhD (Director), James Kelley MD, PhD (Chief Medical Officer), Yair⏎Rivenson PhD (Chief Executive Officer)⏎Contact: Rochelle Wagner PhD, Director Medical Affairs⏎Pictor Labs is a virtual staining company advocating strongly for digital pathology adoption through⏎Artificial Intelligence (AI) technology that delivers results in minutes while preserving tissue for⏎additional and often required comprehensive analyses. We applaud the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) on its initiative to learn more about the impact of AI in healthcare and the⏎influence of reimbursement and payment policy on its access to clinical care.⏎AI has quickly evolved to allow for the development of critical tools in healthcare. Of importance to⏎our company, AI increases the efficiency, and thus improves, the normal laboratory workflow of⏎staining histological slides for medical assessment. These tissues can be virtually stained with⏎routine biomedical stains (e.g., H&E), special tissue stains such as Masson’s Trichrome, or⏎immunohistochemical stains which selectively identify a single molecule of interest.⏎This beginning step in the analysis of tissue biopsies/needle aspirations is critical for the ultimate⏎diagnosis and/or decision to have additional stains performed as part of a differential or for confirming⏎diagnosis, and yet becomes a bottleneck to all subsequent procedures due to the time involved for⏎irreversible chemical staining. PictorLabs believes that the HHS should prioritize the first pivotal⏎steps that many healthcare decisions rely upon. Tissue staining is one such primary step on⏎which the pathologist diagnosis depends, and a fundamental step that AI processes can make⏎more time-effective for the patient by circumventing delays in diagnosis. Importantly, making⏎this primary step available to laboratories and ultimately physicians can also reduce disparities in⏎healthcare access and equity for patients, as the availability of pathology laboratories capable of a⏎myriad of staining may be functionally or locally limited, or financially prohibitive.⏎There are also added benefits for urging AI enabled healthcare technology in the assertion of⏎American autonomy. Digital staining, as an example, can reduce economic dependence on other⏎countries. Currently, laboratories in the US depend on obtaining traditional staining chemicals from⏎other countries, such as China and India. Finished kits may be bottled in the US/EU, but upstream⏎dyes/intermediates frequently originate abroad—so shocks in those supply chains quickly reach and⏎affect US labs logistically with procuring the stains as well as financially with altered pricing.⏎⏎Therefore, the ability of US laboratories to confidently maintain the ability to virtually stain and keep⏎their workflow moving is a positive alternative to the dependence on other supply chains.⏎In order to expedite the advancement of AI in healthcare, we would seek assistance from HHS in⏎certain areas that include:⏎❖ A defined and consistent pathway to reimbursement that is flexible for many⏎scenarios. Current approaches for coding and coverage determination move more⏎slowly than the advancement of technology, and thus hampers confidence in⏎reimbursement that is necessary for bringing innovation to market⏎❖ Changing the inertia of the healthcare system to utilize AI tools to increase efficiency⏎and output of healthcare staff through education on new technologies⏎Current avenues for reimbursement, such as the New Technology Add-on Payment (NTAP), exist⏎but are technology-specific and involve a complex approval process. Potential alternatives include a⏎reimbursement framework that proposes an analytical pathway, similar to the one successfully⏎employed for AI-driven diabetic retinal exams (see https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35681002/). While⏎meritorious, it too involves extensive analysis for each and every step that exists to utilize the⏎technique and cannot be incorporated quickly enough to incentivize. Existing legislation, such as⏎the Health Tech Investment Act (S. 1399), aims to assign Advanced Health Behavior Services⏎(AHBS) to a new technology ambulatory payment classification (APC). This legislation has garnered⏎support from AdvaMed, the representative body for the medical biotechnology sector (see⏎https://www.advamed.org/industry-updates/news/advamed-applauds-house-introduction-of-the-health-tech-investment-⏎act/). Currently, this legislation remains waiting for formal discussion.⏎A simple first step of establishing identical reimbursement for current staining procedures (e.g.,⏎CLIA fees) would greatly attenuate hesitancy by a pathology laboratory to adopt AI-driven⏎technology. If a stained slide is reimbursed, whether or not that is accomplished by approved AI or⏎chemical processes should be unimportant. Both AI and traditional staining require an investment⏎of time and resources - only for AI staining the time expenditure is upfront in development and⏎validation. As such, it is our opinion the stain should be reimbursed equally. But there is also an⏎important overlooked benefit from virtual staining in that virtual stains require only a single tissue⏎slice and thus a significant amount of biopsy tissue is retained. This tissue retention could be a⏎critical issue for patients, as additional molecular tests are frequently needed for diagnosis⏎confirmation and therapy guidance. Tissue retention also impacts care, as several common biopsy⏎locations (e.g., breast, melanoma, colorectal, kidney, lung, thyroid) are frequently inadequate and⏎commonly trigger repeat sampling and follow-up. Repeat biopsies are a real, recurring source of⏎healthcare cost and patient burden. If a virtually stained slide circumvents a re-biopsy, significant⏎cost is saved, not to mention sparing the patient another invasive procedure, delays, frustration, and⏎anxiety. Therefore, prioritizing this primary staining step ensures that the benefits of digital staining⏎will be multifactorial, more efficient, and not restricted to a single demographic or therapeutic area.⏎⏎Education presents another critical challenge for AI-based companies. The United States and⏎Canadian Academy of Pathology (USCAP) and the Digital Pathology Association (DPA) have⏎both acknowledged the potential of this technology and the necessity for its advancement.⏎Both societies currently set a compelling example through collaboration with AI and digital⏎pathology companies, thereby demonstrating a commitment to adopting cutting-edge⏎technology that enhances the quality and speed of diagnostics. For many years, the DPA has⏎worked with the FDA to help secure clearances for whole slide imaging equipment, and thus⏎has created a more productive workplace for pathologists and healthcare institutions as well as⏎enhancing patient care. Thus, medical education towards AI digital pathology is timely,⏎recognized, supported by USCAP and DPA, and provides a successful example of regulating⏎bodies co-operating with such societies for continued education and advancement of AI efforts.⏎Subsequent intensive interaction for HHS and USCAP/DPA here is suggested.⏎A prevalent viewpoint suggests that a central source of reluctance to utilize novel AI technology⏎stems from "the black box nature of AI" (see https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40140300/;⏎https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37093631/). Governing bodies can also provide significant assistance in the⏎adoption of new technology. Support for medical education can be accomplished by regulating⏎bodies through supporting CME, the inclusion of modern technology use for hospital accreditation,⏎and the establishment of required insurance coverage for AI-dependent processes that aligns⏎effectively with reimbursement strategies could ensure more rapid clinical decisions impacting patient⏎care, as well as ensure the healthcare system stays current with important technological progress.⏎In summary, HHS should prioritize foundational and simple healthcare steps such as virtual⏎histopathological staining for AI-driven advancement . The adoption, reimbursement, and acceptance⏎of AI-driven processes is critical to the continued successes our healthcare system brings to patient⏎care. While we highlight policy needs related to our specific business, those principles are applicable⏎across all healthcare companies innovating with AI. Thank you for the opportunity to comment on this⏎important area of attention.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0306♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Clearstep Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Clearstep Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔February 19, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.,⏎Secretary, Department of Health and Human Services⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Washington, DC⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎On behalf of Clearstep Inc. (Clearstep), thank you for the opportunity to provide comments in⏎response to the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) Request for Information on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. Clearstep⏎supports HHS’s “OneHHS” efforts to align regulatory, reimbursement, and research and⏎development strategies across the Department to promote safe, effective, and equitable AI use in⏎clinical care.⏎In summary, Clearstep believes that an HHS-wide AI framework that explicitly integrates both⏎expert-encoded systems and statistical methods is essential to maximizing patient safety,⏎ensuring explainability and transparency, and fostering responsible innovation. Our⏎recommendations emphasize: (1) a proportionate, risk-based regulatory posture that⏎differentiates opaque “black-box” models from expert-encoded and hybrid systems; (2)⏎modernized reimbursement that rewards validated AI tools for delivering measurable value; and⏎(3) targeted research, implementation science, and public–private partnerships that bridge the gap⏎between promising AI capabilities and safe, scalable use in clinical care.⏎About Clearstep Inc.⏎Clearstep is a leader in AI-driven healthcare access and navigation solutions, offering one of the⏎most prevalently implemented AI self-triage systems on U.S. health system websites, as⏎documented in a recent study in Nature’s npj Digital Medicine. Clearstep also serves as the⏎routing engine for the Defense Health Agency’s Digital Front Door+, supporting a diverse⏎beneficiary population in a large, integrated delivery environment.⏎Our platform is built on gold-standard clinical guidelines co-authored by Dr. Barton Schmitt, a⏎co-creator of the Schmitt-Thompson telephone triage protocols used in the majority of U.S. nurse⏎call centers. We integrate expert systems and statistical models including machine learning and⏎large language models to enhance patient experience, generate actionable insights, and improve⏎care delivery. Our predictions are designed to be explainable, interpretable, and tunable, with⏎⏎Clearstep Inc.⏎HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Request for Information [RIN 095-AA13]⏎January 2026⏎every triage or navigation recommendation traceable back to validated clinical best practices and⏎subject to independent clinician review.⏎Clearstep’s deployments include:⏎● AI Triage & Navigation tools that guide individuals to appropriate levels and modes of⏎care.⏎● AI Voice Automation that augments nurse advice lines and call centers by handling⏎common call reasons and improving first-contact resolution.⏎● AI Optimized Scheduling that matches patient needs to available resources, integrated⏎with major EHRs via standards-based APIs where feasible.⏎I. Regulation⏎Clearstep supports HHS’s objective to establish a regulatory posture on AI that is well⏎understood, predictable, and proportionate to risk, enabling rapid innovation while protecting⏎patients and maintaining public trust. In our view, there is a particular opportunity for HHS to⏎clarify expectations for non-medical-device AI tools that influence access, routing, and⏎workflow, including digital triage, navigation, voice automation, and scheduling decision⏎support.⏎A. Proportionate, risk-based oversight and transparency⏎We encourage HHS to explicitly distinguish between:⏎● Opaque “black-box” statistical models and foundation models, and⏎● Expert-encoded and hybrid systems, whose decision pathways are traceable to⏎established clinical guidelines and curated content.⏎Regulatory expectations including documentation, transparency, and monitoring obligations⏎should be calibrated accordingly. For AI tools used in clinical navigation and decision support,⏎HHS can build on ONC’s HTI-1 rule and Decision Support Intervention (DSI) transparency⏎elements in 45 CFR Part 170 to:⏎● Require that systems be explainable, interpretable, and tunable, especially where they are⏎patient-facing or influence access, prioritization, and/or care delivery.⏎● Require developers to document intended use, target population, clinical context, and⏎known limitations, including situations where human override is expected.⏎● Ensure that key outputs (e.g., triage dispositions, routing recommendations) are traceable⏎back to clinical best practice guidelines and content sources, enabling independent review⏎by licensed clinicians and local governance bodies.⏎These expectations should apply both to certified health IT and to non-device AI systems⏎integrated via APIs, with proportional tailoring based on the potential clinical impact of the tool.⏎B. Validation, certification, and post-market surveillance⏎⏎Clearstep Inc.⏎HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Request for Information [RIN 095-AA13]⏎January 2026⏎Clearstep supports formalizing expectations for pre-deployment validation and post-deployment⏎monitoring of AI tools that are used in clinical workflows. We recommend that HHS:⏎● Define baseline validation expectations for non-device clinical AI (e.g., triage,⏎navigation, scheduling decision support), including stress-testing on edge cases,⏎representative subpopulations, and incomplete or noisy data.⏎● Encourage documentation of performance metrics such as hallucination rates, data drift,⏎under- and over-triage, and first-contact resolution for navigation and voice tools.⏎● Coordinate with ONC to extend certification concepts in 45 CFR Part 170 to AI-driven⏎clinical navigation and workflow tools, so that health systems have a consistent HHS⏎signal of quality and safety when procuring such solutions.⏎Post-market surveillance should mirror pharmacovigilance principles, including:⏎● Continuous monitoring for performance degradation, bias, and drift across populations⏎and sites.⏎● Defined thresholds and processes for remediation or rollback when safety or performance⏎concerns emerge.⏎● Structured mechanisms for reporting and addressing significant performance or safety⏎incidents.⏎C. Privacy, de-identification, and data use for AI⏎Clearstep strongly supports the HIPAA de-identification standards under 45 CFR 164.514 for AI⏎training and evaluation. To accelerate innovation, we encourage HHS to provide AI-specific⏎guidance on:⏎● Expert Determination (45 CFR 164.514(b)(1)): Clarifying how Covered Entities and⏎Business Associates can most efficiently apply this method to validate datasets for AI⏎development.⏎● Lifecycle Governance: Establishing clear expectations for documenting de-identification⏎methodologies and managing the long-term safety of datasets used in AI model⏎evolution.⏎These clarifications would reduce the administrative burden on developers while ensuring that⏎model growth does not compromise privacy or security.⏎II. Reimbursement⏎HHS payment policies and programs are a primary determinant of whether high-value AI tools⏎can be deployed and sustained in clinical care. In our experience, current reimbursement⏎structures often under-recognize AI’s contributions to access, safety, and operational efficiency.⏎A. Rewarding AI-enabled value, not volume⏎⏎Clearstep Inc.⏎HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Request for Information [RIN 095-AA13]⏎January 2026⏎Legacy fee-for-service payment regimes rarely recognize the cost savings and safety⏎improvements created by validated AI tools, particularly when those benefits arise from avoided⏎visits, optimized site-of-care selection, and administrative efficiency. Clearstep recommends that⏎HHS:⏎● Expand value-based care and alternative payment models that explicitly recognize AI’s⏎role in reducing avoidable ED visits, improving triage and routing, and increasing⏎completion of preventive and chronic care services.⏎● Allow providers to share in savings that are attributable to AI-enabled improvements in⏎access, navigation, and care coordination, provided tools meet HHS-defined transparency⏎and safety standards.⏎B. Liability and reimbursement clarity⏎Uncertainty around liability for AI-supported decisions is a significant barrier to adoption.⏎Clearstep manages this through comprehensive insurance and indemnification protections and by⏎ensuring that our systems are used as decision support rooted in clinical best practice. To support⏎broader adoption across HHS programs, we recommend that HHS:⏎● Clarify expectations for allocation of responsibility between AI developers and providers⏎when tools are used within labeled indications, with appropriate human oversight and⏎documented governance.⏎● Consider establishing safe harbor approaches in which providers using AI tools that⏎satisfy HHS-defined criteria for transparency, monitoring, and validation receive⏎specified protections related to AI-driven decision support.⏎● Align reimbursement rules so that providers are not penalized for using robust,⏎safety-forward AI tools that demonstrably improve outcomes and operational⏎performance.⏎C. Incentivizing safety-focused, expert-encoded safeguards⏎Reimbursement policies should explicitly recognize the added safeguards provided by systems⏎that integrate expert-encoded knowledge with statistical models. We recommend that HHS:⏎● Differentiate, in payment and program design, between “black-box-only” approaches and⏎systems that provide traceable, clinician-reviewable logic anchored in validated clinical⏎guidelines.⏎● Encourage payment models that reward the use of AI tools with clear safety guardrails,⏎conservative fail-safes, and mechanisms for continuous oversight and tuning.⏎III. Research and Development⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to catalyze research, implementation science, and public–private⏎partnerships that accelerate responsible AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎Clearstep Inc.⏎HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Request for Information [RIN 095-AA13]⏎January 2026⏎A. Implementation science⏎Clearstep encourages HHS to prioritize implementation science that studies:⏎● How AI tools integrate into diverse clinical workflows (e.g., digital front doors, nurse⏎advice lines, call centers, ambulatory and specialty scheduling).⏎● How governance, credentialing, and privileging models can incorporate AI-supported⏎workflows without duplicative administrative burden.⏎● How AI affects patient experience, equity, clinician and staff workload, and overall⏎system efficiency.⏎B. Public–private partnerships and CRADAs⏎Building on our work within the Defense Health Agency environment, we recommend that HHS:⏎● Use Cooperative Research and Development Agreements (CRADAs) and related⏎arrangements to test AI triage, navigation, and scheduling tools across heterogeneous⏎delivery systems, including those serving Medicare, Medicaid, and other priority⏎populations.⏎C. Priority focus areas⏎HHS research and development investments should prioritize:⏎● AI Voice Automation for nurse advice lines and call centers improving first-contact⏎resolution, narrowing access gaps, and alleviating workforce strain. Particularly⏎distinguishing those solutions that use trusted, gold standard clinical algorithms as⏎opposed to those that use black box statistical methods that may hallucinate, drift,⏎provide inconsistent responses, and the basis for which recommendations cannot be⏎evaluated.⏎● AI Optimized Scheduling and intake that match patients to appropriate appointments and⏎care settings across the full range of EHR and scheduling maturity.⏎● Methods and tools to measure and mitigate hallucinations, bias, and data drift over time,⏎and to empower health systems to perform ongoing AI monitoring without requiring deep⏎in-house AI expertise.⏎Responses to Selected RFI Questions⏎1. Biggest barriers to private sector innovation and adoption⏎Clearstep observes several key barriers to AI innovation and adoption in clinical care:⏎● Legal and governance uncertainty regarding liability for AI-supported decisions in mixed⏎human–AI workflows.⏎● Significant variance in EHR and scheduling maturity, limiting the ability to deploy⏎real-time booking and advanced orchestration consistently across provider organizations.⏎⏎Clearstep Inc.⏎HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Request for Information [RIN 095-AA13]⏎January 2026⏎● Limited liquidity and standardized availability of administrative and resource data, which⏎complicates the seamless facilitation of scheduling and care navigation across the⏎healthcare ecosystem.⏎● Administrative hurdles, including inconsistent credentialing and privileging requirements⏎and fragmented internal AI review processes that slow replication and scaling of proven⏎solutions.⏎2. Regulatory, payment, and programmatic priorities⏎We recommend that HHS prioritize:⏎● Standardizing referral qualification and documentation in ways that support, rather than⏎replace, local credentialing and privileging; Clearstep’s tools, for example, standardize⏎referral criteria and documentation while leaving credentialing authority with local⏎governance.⏎● Revisiting 45 CFR Part 170 to incorporate clear certification criteria for AI-driven⏎clinical navigation and decision support, aligned with ONC’s DSI transparency and⏎algorithmic accountability requirements.⏎● Recognizing and incentivizing “safe handoff” patterns structured, context-rich handoffs⏎from AI to human coordinators where real-time booking is not authorized or technically⏎feasible, given EHR maturity variance.⏎3 & 4. Non-medical devices: legal, implementation, and evaluation issues⏎For non-medical-device AI tools used in clinical care, Clearstep recommends that HHS:⏎● Establish safe harbor concepts for providers that use AI tools meeting HHS-established⏎transparency, safety, and monitoring criteria, with clear expectations for human oversight⏎and scope-of-use.⏎● Support evaluation frameworks that emphasize hallucination rates, data drift, under- and⏎over-triage, and first-contact resolution, alongside traditional accuracy measures,⏎particularly for navigation and voice automation tools.⏎● Use contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and prize competitions to fund robust⏎stress-testing of AI tools across diverse clinical scenarios and populations and to develop⏎open benchmarking frameworks and monitoring toolkits.⏎7. Key decision-makers and administrative hurdles⏎Within health systems, Clearstep typically engages CIOs, CMOs, digital leaders, access and call⏎center leaders, and legal/compliance stakeholders. The primary administrative hurdles to AI⏎adoption include:⏎● EHR and scheduling maturity variance, which constrains the ability to implement⏎real-time booking and advanced automation uniformly.⏎⏎Clearstep Inc.⏎HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Request for Information [RIN 095-AA13]⏎January 2026⏎● Fragmented governance and review processes, where multiple committees evaluate AI in⏎the absence of a unified, risk-based framework.⏎In practice, AI tools must be designed to function across high-maturity environments (real-time⏎booking, robust APIs) and low-maturity environments (asynchronous workflows and safe⏎handoffs) to be effective across the broader HHS-influenced ecosystem.⏎8. Interoperability, data standards, and benchmarking⏎Enhanced interoperability through consistent use of FHIR-based standards would substantially⏎widen opportunities for AI tools and accelerate innovation. Clearstep recommends that HHS:⏎● Promote consistent implementation of FHIR resources for appointments, scheduling, and⏎referrals across major EHR platforms to support AI-driven triage, navigation, and⏎real-time booking.⏎● Prioritize benchmarking tools and shared test scenarios for AI-driven scheduling and⏎intake, including common metrics and test cases that link patient-facing AI interactions to⏎provider-facing workflows.⏎● Ensure that administrative data necessary to facilitate scheduling across all health system⏎service lines be made interoperable for solutions that health systems desire to put in place⏎to provide benefit to the end patient.⏎Conclusion⏎Clearstep is committed to advancing the responsible use of AI in clinical care, with a focus on⏎improving patient outcomes, reducing provider and staff burden, and enhancing system⏎efficiency. We appreciate HHS’s leadership in developing a coordinated, HHS-wide approach to⏎AI that balances innovation with safety, privacy, civil rights, and public trust.⏎Please feel free to contact Bilal Naved (bilal@clearstep.health), should you have any questions⏎or require additional information.⏎Sincerely,⏎Bilal A. Naved, Ph.D.⏎Chief Product Officer⏎Clearstep Inc.⏎Clearstep @ MATTER Health⏎222 W Merchandise Mart Plaza, Suite 228⏎Chicago, IL 60654♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0307♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Point32Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Point32Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Point32Health⏎1 Wellness Way⏎Canton, MA 02021⏎point32health.org⏎February 23rd, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information - Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On December 23rd, 2025 , the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) in partnership with⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) (collectively, ASTP/ONC) released a⏎Request for Information soliciting information regarding seek broad public comment on what HHS can⏎do to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care. Point32Health welcomes the⏎opportunity to provide insight into this critical matter.⏎Point32Health, the parent company of Harvard Pilgrim Health Care and Tufts Health Plan, is a leading⏎not-for-profit health and well-being organization dedicated to delivering high-quality, affordable health⏎care. Serving nearly 2 million members, our purpose is to guide and empower healthier lives.⏎As the Administration addresses emerging issues surrounding artificial intelligence, we would appreciate⏎consideration of the below factors. We believe these factors are important to achieve the promise of AI⏎and optimize its positive impact on the average American through increased efficiencies, lower costs,⏎and improved quality of care.⏎i. Sustainability and Efficiencies⏎• Savings Pass-Through: If AI increases provider productivity and reduces⏎administrative waste, those savings should be passed on to members, through⏎their health plans, using proper coding and reduced rates. If AI is used to⏎significantly reduce the necessity of human input, then it should be appropriately⏎coded so that such savings flow through the health care system. In addition,⏎federal coding should be re-evaluated as appropriate. For instance, the⏎Resource Based Relative Value Scale (RBRVS) system currently assumes⏎virtually all human execution.⏎• Democratization of AI: Smaller hospitals, providers, health plans and other⏎elements of the health care system could benefit greatly from AI. However, cost⏎prohibitive pricing from AI companies could squeeze out the very players that⏎could benefit the most. Public policy should advance mechanisms through⏎which smaller players can afford to access AI as well.⏎⏎Point32Health⏎1 Wellness Way⏎Canton, MA 02021⏎point32health.org⏎• AI to AI: As different players in the health care system and wider community⏎begin utilizing AI, public policy should facilitate the efficient interaction between⏎AI systems. For instance, if a hospital is using AI to provide claims information,⏎it may be more efficient to have that AI interface with another AI rather than call⏎a human customer service representative.⏎ii. Safety Standards⏎• Human in the Loop: A consistent requirement that humans must remain a part of⏎the clinical decision-making process to ensure accountability.⏎• Required Training: The notion of “garbage in, garbage out” is critical in the AI⏎space. Crafting appropriate prompts and assuring that critical patient data is⏎communicated is critical to using AI in any clinical use case. Therefore,⏎clinicians that will be utilizing AI as part of their practice should go through⏎appropriate training. Medical schools haven’t prepared clinicians for this new⏎technology. And while it is easy to develop any prompt for AI, it takes training⏎and skill to develop effective prompts that will enable safe AI clinical support.⏎• Health IT Safety Center: Revisiting the idea of a national center focused⏎specifically on the safety of AI-supported clinical decisions to prevent harm or⏎death. Public Policy makers should ensure that AI vendors transparently⏎disclose critical information that guided the development of their health care AI⏎products. Experts should identify the critical elements, for instance – what⏎populations were used to train the AI? Vendors should be required to publish⏎ongoing statistics identified as critical by AI and health care experts – such as,⏎what percentage of AI diagnoses are over-ruled by human clinicians? How often⏎does AI fail to prompt clinicians for missing data critical to patent evaluation?⏎Etc.⏎• Broad Application of Rules: Any AI system that influences a medical decision⏎should be subject to the same high standards of transparency and safety,⏎regardless of what that entity is called (e.g. non-medical devices, software as⏎medical devices, digital therapeutic, etc.)⏎iii. Transparency and Traceability⏎• Mandatory Tagging: Any official documentation (claims, appeals, diagnoses)⏎generated or assisted by AI must be explicitly labeled as such to distinguish it⏎from human-generated work.⏎• Chain of Custody for Logic: There should be a requirement to maintain a⏎record of the AI’s cognitive processes and the specific prompts used by⏎clinicians to justify medical decisions.⏎• Audit Requirements: Healthcare entities using AI for clinical care should be⏎subject to regular federal audits to address dangerous AI use or cost creep⏎through upcoding. This should include:⏎o Prompt Transparency: Ability to access specific prompts used⏎by clinicians to ensure they were accurate and unbiased.⏎o Overrule Rates: Tracking how often a human clinician⏎disagreed with or overruled the AI's recommendation as a⏎metric for effectiveness.⏎⏎Point32Health⏎1 Wellness Way⏎Canton, MA 02021⏎point32health.org⏎o Safety Reporting: Establishing a mechanism to report⏎instances where AI-driven decision support resulted in⏎incorrect treatment or patient harm.⏎iv. Regulatory Reform and Interoperability⏎• Data Emancipation: Public policy should prevent delivery systems (like hospital⏎networks) from gatekeeping patient data. Providers and EMR vendors should be⏎required to provide data for treatment payment and health care operations.⏎• Updating Medicaid/Medicare Rules: Existing regulations, such as network⏎adequacy and staffing ratios (e.g., case manager-to-patient ratios), need to be⏎updated to account for the efficiency gains provided by AI.⏎• Interoperability: Policymakers should ensure that as part of interoperability,⏎delivery systems cannot charge high fees for data sharing. In addition, the⏎interoperability rules should be evaluated to ensure that they will facilitate the⏎use of AI with aggregated data to propel cost savings and efficiencies in the⏎future. It would be a waste of opportunity – as well as significant funds – if AI⏎applicability is not contemplated until AFTER interoperability is completely⏎implemented.⏎v. AI Tools can Reduce Administrative Friction in the Healthcare System and⏎Lower Medical Cost Trends for All Americans⏎• In 2020, the federal government established that if providers fail to adopt⏎certified electronic health record (EHR) technology (CEHRT), Medicare could⏎reduce their payment levels. Even though over 88% of Medicare providers have⏎invested in electronic health records, provider administrative burden remains⏎significant and problematic. The positive potential of EHR adoption has been⏎undermined because remote access to EHRs usually isn’t granted to payors at⏎all, or only at an additional cost. In some cases, this is because the EHR⏎vendors require providers to purchase additional licenses if payors are allowed⏎to remotely access the EHR. As a result, providers must continue to use faxes⏎and other outdated modes to communicate to health plans. Access obstacles to⏎EMR data currently pose a significant barrier to private sector innovation in AI for⏎health care and its adoption and use in clinical care. The integration of AI tools⏎and Large Language Models to optimize the scaled aggregation of health care⏎data should be applied across the healthcare delivery system to achieve real⏎interoperability. The same standards for AI Data accessibility should apply to⏎hospital networks, providers, EMR vendors and payors. This could be⏎accomplished more rapidly if CMS were to update the CEHRT standards in⏎coordination with adopting a uniform AI policy in health care as follows:⏎a) The EHR vendors would be required to allow payor organizations to⏎remotely access a provider’s EHR for treatment, payment and healthcare⏎operations as defined by HIPAA, without the imposition of additional fees⏎onto the provider client or the payor entity.⏎b) The provider licensing the EHR would be required to allow payor⏎organizations to access a provider’s EHR for treatment, payment and⏎⏎Point32Health⏎1 Wellness Way⏎Canton, MA 02021⏎point32health.org⏎healthcare operations as defined by HIPAA, without the imposition of⏎additional fees onto the payor entity.⏎We appreciate that HHS has solicited comments on this emerging technology that is⏎rapidly expanding in use. We look forward to collaborating with the federal⏎government to advance meaningful use of AI in a manner that optimizes outcomes⏎for all Americans while minimizing risks.⏎Please contact me at Christina.Nyquist@Point32Health.org if we can provide⏎additional assistance.⏎Sincerely,⏎Christina Nyquist⏎Vice President, Federal Affairs♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0308♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Davis♔♔Vermont♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Two winters ago I had a mammogram that came back with what the radiologist described as "an ambiguous cluster of calcifications" in my left breast. What followed was weeks of waiting while three separate radiologists reviewed the same images before anyone would commit to a recommendation. It was agonizing. I kept thinking, why is this taking so long, why can't someone just tell me. The biopsy eventually came back benign, and I was relieved, obviously. But I've thought about that experience a lot since then, and what I've come to understand is that the slowness was the point. Those three radiologists disagreed with each other in ways that mattered, and working through that disagreement was how I got the right answer.⏎⏎I run a small bakery in Montpelier. I think about efficiency every single day. I know what it costs to do things slowly. So I'm not writing this because I'm reflexively opposed to new technology or because I think the healthcare system is working great as-is. It's not. But what worries me about this whole push to accelerate AI adoption is the word "accelerate." That framing assumes the main obstacle is slowness, when sometimes the slowness is doing something.⏎⏎My radiologist, Dr. Callahan at Central Vermont Medical Center, sat with me for about forty minutes at my follow-up appointment. She didn't just read me numbers. She noticed I was scared in a particular way, that I kept asking the same question rephrased slightly differently, and she adjusted what she told me accordingly. I don't know how to put a value on that. I genuinely don't. But I know that a system optimized to move patients through faster would have cut that appointment shorter, and I would have left with the same technical information and much less of what I actually needed to function.⏎⏎The question I want HHS to actually grapple with is: who is responsible when an AI-assisted recommendation turns out to be wrong? Not in a legal liability sense, though that matters too. I mean who does the patient look at. Right now if my doctor makes a mistake, there is a human being I can be angry at, a human being who has to live with that, a human being whose professional identity is bound up in getting it right. That accountability structure is part of what makes medicine trustworthy. If the recommendation comes from an algorithm that was trained on data I've never seen, reviewed by a clinician who had fourteen seconds to click "approve," I don't know who is responsible and I'm not sure they do either.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in clinical care. I genuinely don't know enough about how these tools work to say that. But I think HHS is asking the wrong question when it focuses on how to accelerate adoption. The question should be something more like: under what specific conditions does AI assistance actually improve care for the patient sitting in the room, and how do we make sure the answer to that isn't just "it processes images faster" or "it reduces cost per encounter."⏎⏎The calcifications in my mammogram were benign. But the process that confirmed that required three humans who disagreed, and time, and a doctor who noticed I was scared. I'm not sure what the AI equivalent of that last part is supposed to be.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0309♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Morrison, Dennis, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dennis♔Morrison♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔RESPONSE TO HHS REQUEST FOR INFORMATION⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108 | Document Number 2025-23641 | Submitted February 23, 2026⏎Submitted by: Dennis Morrison, PhD, Eleos Health | Focus Area: Behavioral Health⏎Introduction⏎This response addresses the ten specific questions posed in HHS's RFI on accelerating AI⏎adoption in clinical care (90 FR 60108), with an exclusive focus on behavioral health—⏎encompassing mental health, substance use disorders (SUD), and community behavioral health⏎organizations (CBHOs). Behavioral health occupies a unique position in the AI landscape: it faces⏎the some of the most severe workforce shortages, the greatest documentation burden relative to⏎encounter volume, the most significant access disparities, and clinical complexity that demands⏎nuanced, evidence-based approaches that current AI tools are only beginning to approximate.⏎Behavioral health organizations, particularly Certified Community Behavioral Health Clinics⏎(CCBHCs) and community mental health centers (CMHCs), treat the nation's most vulnerable⏎populations—individuals with serious mental illness (SMI), co-occurring disorders, and those⏎involved in the justice system. These organizations have chronically operated on thin margins,⏎and the combination of Medicaid funding pressures, clinician burnout, and documentation⏎mandates threatens their sustainability. AI tools—properly governed, evaluated, and⏎reimbursed—represent a rare opportunity to meaningfully address each of these challenges⏎simultaneously.⏎This response draws on the work of pioneering behavioral health AI companies including Eleos⏎Health (ambient documentation and compliance AI) and Lyssn (clinical quality improvement and⏎evidence-based practice fidelity AI), as well as the clinical research of Dr. John Torous, MD, MBI,⏎Director of the Digital Psychiatry Division at Beth Israel Deaconess Medical Center (BIDMC) /⏎Harvard Medical School, whose decade-plus body of work on digital mental health tools, AI⏎chatbots, and mobile mental health applications provides essential evidence-based grounding.⏎⏎Question 1:⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in⏎AI for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Workforce Crisis and Documentation Burden⏎Behavioral health faces a workforce crisis of historic proportions. Clinicians in community⏎behavioral health settings report spending 30-50% of their working hours on documentation rather⏎than direct care. This documentation burden is a primary driver of the 90% burnout rate cited⏎across the sector. Companies like Eleos Health have demonstrated that ambient AI⏎documentation tools can reduce documentation time by more than 70%, with providers accepting⏎80% of AI-generated note suggestions and 90% of notes submitted within 24 hours—dramatically⏎reducing late submission denials.⏎Yet adoption remains uneven because many behavioral health organizations, particularly rural⏎and safety-net providers, lack the IT infrastructure, broadband connectivity, and technical staff to⏎implement sophisticated AI platforms. The "digital divide" that Dr. John Torous has long⏎documented in his research on digital equity and the BIDMC Digital Navigator program extends⏎directly into organizational AI readiness.⏎Regulatory Fragmentation⏎The behavioral health regulatory environment is uniquely fragmented. Unlike general medical⏎AI, which primarily navigates FDA and HIPAA frameworks, behavioral health AI must⏎simultaneously address 42 CFR Part 2 restrictions on substance use disorder records, state-by-⏎state licensure and telehealth regulations, payer-specific prior authorization requirements, and⏎an emerging patchwork of state AI-specific legislation. Dr. Torous's 2025 50-state legislative⏎review published in JMIR Mental Health identified widely varying approaches — from Illinois's⏎near-ban on certain AI uses in behavioral health to Utah's disclosure-focused framework —⏎creating a compliance environment that disadvantages smaller innovators and CBHOs.⏎The Part 2 landscape shifted substantially with HHS's February 2024 Final Rule (89 Fed. Reg.⏎12496), which implemented Section 3221 of the CARES Act by aligning significant portions of⏎42 CFR Part 2 with HIPAA. The rule took effect April 16, 2024, with a compliance deadline of⏎February 16, 2026 — the week this RFI comment period closes. Key changes include: a single⏎patient consent now covers all future uses and disclosures for treatment, payment, and⏎healthcare operations (TPO), replacing the previous per-disclosure consent requirement;⏎covered entities and business associates receiving Part 2 records under TPO consent may⏎redisclose in accordance with HIPAA, without separate patient authorization; record segregation⏎is no longer required for entities receiving Part 2 records under a single TPO consent; and⏎enforcement authority has shifted from DOJ-only criminal penalties to OCR civil and criminal⏎enforcement aligned with the HIPAA penalty framework.⏎⏎Despite this significant alignment, important Part 2 barriers specific to behavioral health AI tools⏎remain. The Final Rule created a new, highly protected category called "SUD Counseling⏎Notes" — defined as a clinician's notes documenting or analyzing the content of a SUD⏎counseling session, maintained separately from the treatment record — analogous to HIPAA's⏎psychotherapy notes protections. SUD Counseling Notes require a separate patient consent⏎and cannot be covered by a broad TPO consent. This new category is directly relevant to⏎ambient AI documentation tools: when a system like Eleos Health captures and analyzes a SUD⏎counseling session, the resulting AI-generated session analysis may constitute SUD Counseling⏎Notes under the new definition, requiring consent workflows that current ambient AI⏎implementations were not designed to accommodate. HHS has not yet issued guidance⏎clarifying how ambient AI documentation systems should navigate this new category, leaving⏎SUD treatment programs and their AI vendors in legal uncertainty.⏎Furthermore, Part 2 does not preempt more protective state laws, and the prohibition on using⏎SUD records in civil, criminal, administrative, or legislative proceedings against a patient⏎remains absolute without separate written consent or a court order — a protection that goes well⏎beyond HIPAA and has direct implications for AI-generated SUD documentation that could be⏎discoverable in legal proceedings. HHS should issue specific guidance addressing how ambient⏎AI documentation systems operating in SUD treatment settings should implement the SUD⏎Counseling Notes consent requirements, and should clarify the conditions under which AI-⏎generated session analysis constitutes Part 2-protected records. Because some ambient⏎listening documentation assistants require retention of audio recordings and/or transcripts of the⏎therapy session to analyze the session, HHS should declare these recordings as non-⏎discoverable.⏎A foundational barrier to AI adoption and interoperability in Community Mental Health Centers⏎(CMHCs) serving Medicaid populations is the absence of standardized procedure coding and⏎service definitions across state lines. While HCPCS Level II and CPT codes provide a nominal⏎federal framework, Medicaid is a state-administered program, and individual states retain broad⏎latitude to define covered services, establish documentation requirements, specify unit⏎structures, and determine reimbursement rates independently. The practical consequence is⏎that the same billing code — for example, an H-code for community-based mental health⏎services — may carry materially different service definitions, staff qualification requirements,⏎and clinical documentation expectations depending on the state and even the managed care⏎payer within that state. For AI-powered clinical documentation and coding assistance tools to⏎function reliably in CMHC environments, they must be trained, configured, and continuously⏎updated to accommodate this patchwork of state- and payer-specific rules. This variability⏎substantially increases the cost and complexity of AI implementation, limits the scalability of⏎vendor solutions across state markets, and creates persistent risk of claim denials, audit⏎exposure, and compliance failures when AI-generated outputs do not precisely align with⏎jurisdictionally-specific requirements. HHS should consider whether federal guidance, technical⏎assistance, or Medicaid waiver incentives could encourage greater harmonization of behavioral⏎health service definitions and coding standards as a prerequisite for effective AI adoption in this⏎sector.⏎⏎Lack of Behavioral Health-Specific AI Training Data and⏎Validation⏎General-purpose large language models (LLMs) like ChatGPT, Gemini and others perform poorly⏎on behavioral health-specific tasks without domain-specific fine-tuning. As Dr. Torous noted in⏎Congressional testimony (November 2025), one patient's apt observation captures the challenge:⏎general AI tools tend to repeat the same responses 'in different ways' without demonstrating⏎genuine clinical insight. These tools rely on sycophancy to build rapport. Lyssn, which has built⏎its platform on 17 years of academic research and analysis of more than 4.3 million clinical⏎sessions, and has produced over 60 peer-reviewed publications validating its approach,⏎represents the kind of domain-specific foundation behavioral health AI requires—but that standard⏎AI development pipelines do not automatically provide.⏎Eleos Health has pursued a parallel but distinct data strategy — building what it describes as the⏎industry's largest dataset of real-world behavioral health treatment sessions to train its ambient⏎documentation and clinical insight AI. A peer-reviewed study published in the Journal of Medical⏎Internet Research reports that Eleos's machine learning research drew on 2.83 million therapist-⏎client micro-dialogues — a dataset the lead researcher described as "unprecedented," specifically⏎because it captures how therapy is actually delivered in real-world clinical settings rather than in⏎the controlled conditions that have historically characterized psychotherapy research. Eleos's in-⏎house clinical team, with over 180 combined years of behavioral health experience, continuously⏎tests, validates, and fine-tunes its models to recognize the clinical terminology, interaction⏎patterns, and emotional indicators that a general-purpose LLM trained on broad internet text⏎cannot reliably detect. The platform's coverage extends across CBT, DBT, ACT, Motivational⏎Interviewing, and the Zero Suicide Framework, with active research studies conducted in⏎collaboration with Stanford, MIT Sloan, Vanderbilt, Harvard, Palo Alto University, Imperial College⏎London, and Hebrew University — including novel AI-based detection of social determinants of⏎health discussions in session content and multi-modal audio feature analysis as indicators of⏎depression and anxiety symptom severity. Critically, this dataset was assembled under a strict⏎privacy architecture: Eleos does not store raw session recordings or use protected health⏎information to train its models; any data used for model improvement is fully de-identified and⏎reviewed exclusively by a clinically licensed, security-cleared team. Together, the Lyssn and⏎Eleos datasets represent the most significant behavioral health-specific AI training foundations in⏎existence, and the research programs surrounding them demonstrate precisely the kind of⏎domain-specific investment that HHS should incentivize, protect, and replicate through federal⏎research funding.⏎The scarcity of large, well-labeled, consent-appropriate behavioral health datasets for AI⏎training—particularly for specialized modalities like group therapy, peer support, and crisis⏎intervention—limits the quality ceiling for behavioral health-specific AI.⏎Reimbursement Gaps⏎Current CMS reimbursement frameworks do not recognize AI-assisted clinical documentation or⏎AI-enhanced quality improvement as billable services. Behavioral health organizations cannot⏎⏎claim additional reimbursement for deploying AI tools that improve documentation integrity,⏎enhance evidence-based practice fidelity, or reduce clinician turnover—even when these tools⏎demonstrably improve patient outcomes and organizational financial sustainability. The result is⏎that the upfront cost of AI tools falls entirely on organizations that are already operating on thin⏎margins.⏎⏎Question 2:⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design⏎changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care⏎and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to⏎augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific⏎changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎42 CFR Part 2 Safe Harbor for AI Documentation Tools⏎As described in the “Regulatory Fragmentation” section above, HIPAA and 42 CFR § 2⏎alignment has helped providers’ significantly. However, issues remain especially regarding SUD⏎Counseling Notes. HHS should take two targeted actions. First, issue authoritative interpretive⏎guidance clarifying: (a) whether AI-generated session analysis in an ambient documentation tool⏎constitutes SUD Counseling Notes under 42 CFR § 2.11; (b) the conditions under which AI⏎vendor processing of session content constitutes a use or disclosure requiring Part 2 consent;⏎and (c) what a compliant "separate consent" workflow for SUD Counseling Notes looks like in⏎an ambient AI implementation. Second, establish a prospective safe harbor for AI⏎documentation tools that process SUD session content within the treating provider's care⏎setting, under a defined consent architecture, where the AI-generated output remains within the⏎treating provider's EHR and is not disclosed to external parties — on the grounds that such use⏎is analytically equivalent to a clinician's own note-taking rather than a disclosure to a third party.⏎Applicable CFR citations: 42 CFR §§ 2.11 (SUD Counseling Notes definition), 2.31 (consent⏎requirements), 2.32 (notice accompanying disclosure).⏎CCBHC Prospective Payment System Recognition of AI-⏎Enhanced Services⏎CMS should modify the CCBHC Prospective Payment System (PPS) methodology under the⏎CCBHC Medicaid Demonstration program to recognize AI-assisted documentation and quality⏎improvement tools as qualifying infrastructure investments that affect the cost report used to⏎establish PPS rates. Under the current framework, investments in AI tools that reduce⏎documentation time and improve compliance do not result in sustainable revenue adjustments,⏎because their value accrues primarily through avoided costs (reduced denials, improved staff⏎retention) rather than through billable activity. Applicable CFR citation: 42 CFR § 438.6 (managed⏎care CCBHC provisions); 42 CFR Part 447 (payment for services).⏎Medicare Behavioral Health Integration and Digital Health⏎Codes⏎CMS should expand CPT code coverage for AI-augmented behavioral health services. The⏎existing Behavioral Health Integration (BHI) codes (99492-99494) and General BHI (99484)⏎should be updated or supplemented to recognize AI-assisted care coordination, digital⏎⏎phenotyping-informed treatment adjustment, and AI-enabled evidence-based practice⏎supervision. Additionally, CMS should establish new HCPCS codes specifically for AI-assisted⏎clinical documentation in behavioral health settings, analogous to how remote patient monitoring⏎codes (CPT 99453-99458) created a reimbursement pathway for technology-mediated⏎monitoring. Applicable CFR citation: 42 CFR § 410.67 (behavioral health integration services).⏎ONC Behavioral Health EHR Certification Criteria⏎ASTP/ONC should establish behavioral health-specific criteria within the Health IT Certification⏎Program (45 CFR Part 170) that include requirements for AI transparency, bias testing on⏎behavioral health populations, and interoperability with behavioral health data standards including⏎SNOMED CT mental health subsets and the Gravity Project's social determinants of health⏎(SDOH) terminology. Current certification criteria were developed primarily for acute and primary⏎care workflows and do not adequately address behavioral health-specific documentation⏎structures, group therapy notes, or the unique data elements relevant to SMI populations.⏎⏎Question 3:⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in⏎clinical care may raise novel legal and implementation issues that⏎challenge existing governance and accountability structures ( e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎In general, there are currently two broad uses of AI in behavioral healthcare: the first is the use⏎of AI to expedite and improve a clinician’s ability to complete the onerous documentation burden⏎seen in behavioral healthcare, especially community based behavioral healthcare. When used⏎in this manner, clinicians are being provided information to help them write clinical notes. They⏎and they alone are the arbiters of what, if any of the suggestions provided by the AI are used in⏎the clinical note. The second is AI being deployed without clinician oversight. AI chatbots are the⏎most common example of this form of AI usage in behavioral health care. The fundamental⏎difference between these two uses is that the former assists clinicians in their work but does not⏎replace clinical judgement. The latter has no human clinical oversight.⏎Liability in AI-Augmented Clinical Documentation⏎Ambient AI documentation tools like Eleos Health's platform generate progress note suggestions⏎that clinicians review, modify, incorporate into the EHR note and sign. For some, this has created⏎confusion regarding liability questions: if an AI-generated note contains a clinical inaccuracy that⏎contributes to a patient harm event, how should liability be apportioned among the parties that⏎participated in the creation of that including the AI vendor, the EHR vendor, the clinician who⏎approved the note, and the organization? Since these tools assist professionals only in⏎documenting clinical interactions, current malpractice frameworks appropriately assume human⏎authorship and human accountability for clinical documentation. HHS should reaffirm this position⏎and work with state insurance commissioners and CMS to develop guidance clarifying that the⏎supervising clinician retains professional responsibility for reviewed and approved AI-generated⏎content, while establishing minimum accountability standards for AI vendors providing⏎documentation assistance including transparency regarding model limitations, error rates, and⏎update protocols. Some AI products allow or even require that transcripts and/or audio recordings⏎of the clinical session and the recommendations made but not accepted by the clinician be⏎retained for some period of time. These recordings and transcripts are similar to other quality⏎improvement documents such as chart reviews and supervision and should be protected from⏎legal discovery.⏎AI Mental Health Chatbots and Crisis Situations⏎Compared to AI tools that assist clinicians in documenting clinical transactions, mental health⏎chatbots provide significantly more serious risks to end users. These products work semi-⏎automously often without a “human in the loop”. Dr. John Torous and others have documented⏎⏎extensively the risks of AI chatbots deployed for mental health support without adequate safety⏎guardrails. As he noted in Congressional testimony, OpenAI reported over one million users per⏎week engaging ChatGPT with explicit indicators of suicidal planning, and numerous lawsuits have⏎alleged AI chatbot involvement in patient suicides. The legal status of AI chatbots that provide⏎mental health guidance but are not classified as medical devices creates a significant⏎accountability gap. HHS should issue guidance establishing minimum safety requirements—⏎including mandatory crisis detection, escalation protocols, and handoff to human crisis services—⏎for AI applications marketed for mental health support, regardless of FDA classification. This⏎would build on Dr. Torous's work benchmarking AI chatbot performance on mental health⏎scenarios through platforms like mindApps.ai. See “Real World Performance Surveillance” below⏎for more on this topic.⏎Privacy in Psychotherapy and Group Therapy AI⏎Lyssn's AI platform analyzes session recordings for quality improvement and evidence-based⏎practice fidelity—a valuable capability that raises novel privacy questions when applied to group⏎therapy, where multiple clients simultaneously disclose protected health information. Unlike⏎individual therapy, group therapy transcripts contain the PHI of potentially 10-15 individuals per⏎session. However, like individual notes, the information not used to create the clinical notes for⏎group therapy participants should be regarded as legally non-discoverable.⏎Informed Consent Standards for AI-Assisted Care⏎No federal standard currently exists for what behavioral health patients must be told when AI tools⏎are used in their care. Dr. Torous and others have long advocated for transparent disclosure to⏎patients about digital health tool use. There are two use cases for consent regarding the use of⏎AI tools. The first is general consent which is typically given when treatment begins along with⏎other, more traditional consents to treatment. The second is specific consent, or more exactly,⏎the ability for a patient to withdraw previously given consent for one or more treatment sessions.⏎AI vendors must accommodate both. HHS should establish minimum informed consent standards⏎for behavioral health settings that address: the use of ambient listening technologies during⏎sessions, AI analysis of session content for documentation or quality improvement, and the use⏎of AI chatbots or digital therapeutics as adjuncts to care.⏎⏎Question 4:⏎For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI⏎evaluation methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and⏎other workflow and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should⏎HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most⏎impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎Evidence-Based Practice Fidelity as a Core Metric⏎For behavioral health AI, the most clinically meaningful evaluation metric is fidelity to evidence-⏎based practices (EBPs)—the degree to which AI-assisted clinical encounters actually improve⏎adherence to validated treatment protocols like Motivational Interviewing (MI), Cognitive⏎Behavioral Therapy (CBT), and Dialectical Behavioral Therapy (DBT). Lyssn's platform, built on⏎17 years of academic research and validated against more than 4.3 million analyzed sessions,⏎represents the state of the art in AI-powered EBP fidelity measurement. Its approach—using⏎natural language processing to assess therapist behaviors against validated coding systems like⏎the Motivational Interviewing Treatment Integrity (MITI) scale—provides a model for how AI⏎evaluation in behavioral health can be clinically grounded rather than purely technical. Using⏎EBPs in this way presumes that managing the process i.e. the protocol will result in the same (or⏎similar) outcomes achieved by the EBP when that protocol was validated. It is important to note⏎that doing an EBP is a means to an end, not the end itself. EBPs exist to provide clinicians with a⏎research-based way to get good clinical outcomes. They define what improvement is possible for⏎various treatments, in a way defining a gold standard. In cases where delivering high fidelity EBPs⏎is not possible or advisable, the outcomes demonstrated by the EBPs provide a benchmark for⏎non-EBP interventions. That is, if a non-EBP intervention can achieve the same or better⏎outcomes as demonstrated with the EBP for the same or less cost, the non-EBP intervention is⏎achieving what the EBP treatment did even though it was not maintaining fidelity to the protocol.⏎In this way, EBPs serve dual purposes: providing a detailed protocol for clinicians to follow and a⏎clinical outcome benchmark that non-EBPs should strive to attain.⏎Therapeutic Alliance Preservation⏎The therapeutic relationship—the alliance between clinician and client—is one of the most⏎powerful predictors of behavioral health treatment outcomes across all modalities. AI evaluation⏎frameworks must assess whether AI tools enhance or diminish therapeutic alliance quality. Eleos⏎Health's published research and received numerous anecdotal observations demonstrating that⏎providers using its ambient documentation platform achieve 50% higher client engagement, 3-4x⏎better symptom improvement, and 36% greater use of evidence-based techniques compared to⏎baseline, suggesting that removing the documentation burden allows clinicians to be more fully⏎present with clients. Post-deployment evaluation should systematically measure therapeutic⏎alliance using validated tools such as the Working Alliance Inventory.⏎⏎Bias and Health Equity Testing⏎AI systems trained primarily on data from majority populations can perform poorly for⏎underrepresented groups—a particular concern in behavioral health, where racial and ethnic⏎minorities, LGBTQ+ individuals, and people with limited English proficiency face well-documented⏎care disparities. Dr. Torous's Digital Navigator program at BIDMC specifically addresses digital⏎equity as a prerequisite for beneficial AI deployment. HHS should require that behavioral health⏎AI tools undergo pre-deployment bias testing disaggregated by race, ethnicity, language, gender⏎identity, and socioeconomic status, with post-deployment monitoring to detect disparate⏎performance. Grant mechanisms through SAMHSA and NIMH would be the most appropriate⏎vehicles for funding this research.⏎Real-World Performance Surveillance⏎Dr. Torous has consistently argued that most mental health apps and AI chatbots remain⏎understudied, with unknown real-world risks — a position now supported by a robust independent⏎evidence base. Linardon and colleagues conducted a systematic review of 171 clinical trials of⏎mental health apps published in npj Digital Medicine (2024), finding that only 55 trials reported⏎adverse events at all, the meta-analytic deterioration rate across app conditions was 6.7%, and⏎those trials that did report adverse events frequently provided insufficient information to allow⏎proper assessment of user risk. Writing in World Psychiatry (2025), the same research team⏎documented that negative effects from apps can extend to symptom deterioration, onset of new⏎symptoms, and suicidal ideation, with rates of negative effects in severe mental illness⏎populations reaching as high as 20% in some trials — a state of evidence that, in their words,⏎"makes it difficult to integrate apps into clinical practice safely."⏎The crisis response failure problem has been quantified independently. Dwyer and colleagues⏎publishing in Psychiatric Services (2024), evaluated 302 mental health apps available in January⏎2024 using the APA's app evaluation model and found that only 15% referred users to 988, the⏎national crisis standard, and — most strikingly — 14 apps with more than 3.5 million combined⏎downloads provided incorrect or nonfunctional alternative crisis hotlines. This is not a theoretical⏎risk: millions of people in potential crisis were being routed to telephone numbers that do not⏎connect. This finding directly extends foundational work by Larsen, Nicholas, and Christensen,⏎whose systematic review of suicide-focused apps found that none abided with best practices for⏎providing visible crisis support information, and that the vast majority demonstrated limited ability⏎to recognize anticipatory warning signs of suicidal crisis.⏎For behavioral health AI, HHS should support the development of post-deployment surveillance⏎infrastructure analogous to FDA's MedWatch system—a mechanism for clinicians and patients⏎to report AI-related clinical errors, safety events, and unexpected behaviors in behavioral health⏎settings. AHRQ's existing patient safety infrastructure could serve as a model.⏎⏎Question 5:⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g.,⏎accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to⏎promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Behavioral Health-Specific AI Certification⏎HHS should support the development of a behavioral health-specific AI certification program,⏎potentially through SAMHSA or in partnership with the National Council for Mental Wellbeing.⏎Such a program would extend beyond ONC's general health IT certification criteria to address⏎behavioral health-specific requirements including EBP fidelity measurement, crisis protocol⏎adequacy, 42 CFR Part 2 compliance for SUD applications, and therapeutic alliance preservation.⏎Eleos Health's recent achievement of ISO 42001 certification (the AI-specific management⏎system standard) demonstrates that behavioral health AI vendors are ready for rigorous⏎certification frameworks.⏎SAMHSA Technology Assessment Program⏎SAMHSA should establish a technology assessment program analogous to the Substance Abuse⏎Prevention and Treatment Block Grant's evidence-based practices mandate, creating a tiered⏎recognition system for behavioral health AI tools based on evidence strength. Dr. Torous, who⏎led the American Psychiatric Association's work group on smartphone app evaluation and⏎developed the APA's technology evaluation framework, has created models for how this can be⏎structured. His mindapps.org database—the largest mental health app registry—and mindApps.ai⏎benchmarking platform provide a template for systematic, ongoing behavioral health AI evaluation⏎that HHS could support and formalize. Other partners in this effort include The Joint Commission⏎and CARF, two of the most used accrediting bodies in the behavioral health industry.⏎Academic-Industry Research Partnerships⏎HHS should incentivize partnerships between behavioral health AI companies and academic⏎medical centers with established digital psychiatry programs. The BIDMC Digital Psychiatry⏎Division's research collaborations—including studies validating Eleos Health's impact on⏎documentation burden and clinical outcomes—demonstrate how these partnerships can produce⏎rigorous peer-reviewed evidence that benefits the entire field. Cooperative research and⏎development agreements (CRADAs) between NIMH-funded research centers and behavioral⏎health AI companies would accelerate the evidence base.Eleos Health has established multiple⏎academic partnerships with Stanford, Palo Alto University, Hebrew University and others.⏎⏎Question 6:⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded⏎performance and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds⏎of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care⏎outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Successes: Ambient Documentation in Behavioral Health⏎AI-assisted clinical documentation has clearly met or exceeded performance expectations in⏎behavioral health settings. Eleos Health's deployments across organizations including Wellpower,⏎Jefferson Center, GRAND Mental Health, and Gaudenzia demonstrate consistent documentation⏎time reductions exceeding 70%, with providers accepting 80% of AI-generated note suggestions.⏎It should be noted that Eleos Health believes this 80% mark is a best practice. That is, no more⏎than 80% of the note should be AI-generated since all notes should also include the unique voice⏎of the clinician. These results translate directly into organizational sustainability: 90% of notes⏎submitted within 24 hours reduces claim denials, and reduced documentation burden improves⏎staff retention in a sector experiencing severe workforce shortages. Dr. Brandon Ward, when he⏎was CIO at Jefferson Center, reported that providers are enthusiastic about the technology,⏎including initial skeptics. These results are particularly significant given that behavioral health⏎organizations serve some of the most complex patients in the healthcare system.⏎Successes: AI-Powered Quality Improvement and EBP⏎Fidelity⏎Lyssn's AI platform has demonstrated success in scaling evidence-based practice training and⏎quality improvement in ways that were previously impossible at the organizational level.⏎Traditional EBP fidelity assessment required manual coding of session recordings—a process so⏎resource-intensive that most organizations could only evaluate a tiny fraction (5-10%) of clinical⏎encounters. Lyssn's AI enables continuous, automated fidelity monitoring across entire⏎workforces, allowing organizations to identify training needs, target coaching resources, and⏎demonstrate fidelity to funders and accreditors. This addresses a longstanding gap between what⏎EBP research says is effective and what actually occurs in real-world clinical practice.⏎Shortfalls: AI Chatbots for Direct Mental Health Support⏎AI chatbots deployed directly to patients for mental health support have not met clinical⏎expectations and present documented safety risks. Dr. Torous, whose early research was⏎foundational in assessing the potential of digital mental health tools, has documented that most⏎mental health chatbots remain untested in clinical populations and present unknown risks. The⏎2017 emergence of Woebot and similar chatbots was accompanied by enthusiasm that has been⏎tempered by subsequent evidence: few have undergone randomized controlled trials, many fail⏎to recognize crisis situations, and some have been associated with patient harm. The FDA's April⏎2024 approval of the first app to treat depression (Rejoyn for adjunctive treatment of MDD)⏎represents a positive regulatory development, but the vast majority of the thousands of mental⏎⏎health apps on the market remain unvalidated. NAMI does not endorse AI for mental health⏎treatment and has partnered with Dr. Torous to develop independent benchmarks for AI tool⏎safety.⏎Shortfalls: Interoperability and EHR Integration⏎AI tools in behavioral health consistently struggle with EHR integration. The behavioral health⏎EHR market is highly fragmented, with dozens of specialized platforms (Netsmart's myAvatar and⏎Streamline, Qualifacts/Credible, Kipu, ICANotes, and others) lacking the standardized APIs that⏎acute care EHRs have developed under ONC's information blocking rules. Eleos Health⏎addresses this by operating as an EHR-agnostic overlay via browser extension—a practical⏎solution, but one that highlights the underlying interoperability problem rather than solving it. True⏎integration requires standardized behavioral health data exchange frameworks that do not⏎currently exist.⏎⏎Question 7:⏎Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within⏎health care organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for⏎clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in⏎clinical care?⏎Key Decision Makers⏎In community behavioral health organizations, AI adoption decisions typically require alignment⏎among: the Chief Executive Officer (organizational mission and financial sustainability), the Chief⏎Clinical Officer (clinical appropriateness, staff engagement, evidence base), the Chief Information⏎Officer (technical infrastructure, security, EHR integration), and the Chief Medical Officer⏎(psychiatrist or physician oversight of AI-generated clinical content). Unlike hospital systems,⏎most CBHOs lack a dedicated Chief Digital Officer or Chief Innovation Officer, meaning AI⏎advocacy must come from within existing leadership roles. Board-level engagement is⏎increasingly common as AI represents both opportunity and liability.⏎Eleos Health's deployment experience across organizations like Jefferson Center (where the⏎Chief Innovation Officer and President/CEO both participated actively in adoption decisions)⏎illustrates that multi-stakeholder buy-in is essential—and that mission alignment and clinician⏎enthusiasm are as important as technical capability.⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles⏎The most consistently reported administrative hurdles to behavioral health AI adoption include:⏎(1) procurement complexity, including legal review of BAAs for ambient listening technologies and⏎vendor security assessments that small organizations lack capacity to conduct; (2) state licensing⏎and consent requirements for AI tools that capture session content, which vary widely and are⏎changing frequently and quickly and are rarely addressed in existing technology guidance; (3)⏎payer contracting constraints, as some Medicaid managed care contracts include provisions that⏎could be interpreted to restrict AI-generated documentation; (4) workforce training requirements,⏎as even well-designed AI tools require change management investment that organizations with⏎limited training budgets struggle to fund; and (5) skepticism from clinicians who have experienced⏎technology-driven increases in administrative burden and are appropriately cautious about new⏎tools promising relief.⏎⏎Question 8:⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market⏎opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical⏎care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking⏎tools.⏎Behavioral Health-Specific Data Standards⏎The most significant interoperability gap affecting behavioral health AI is the absence of⏎standardized behavioral health data exchange frameworks. The Meaningful Use standards first⏎implemented with the passage of the Affordable Care Act started the process of interoperability,⏎but behavioral health care organizations were immediately at a disadvantage since they were⏎exempted from the EHR stimulus incentives available to medical care providers. Many Health⏎Information Exchanges (HIEs) were hesitant to accept and coordinate behavioral health data⏎because of the challenges of 42CFR Part 2 and a general hesitancy about the confidentiality⏎requirements inherent in behavioral health information. Now, while HL7 FHIR has transformed⏎data exchange in acute and primary care, behavioral health data—including mental status⏎examinations, symptom rating scales, substance use assessment tools, and session notes—⏎lacks standardized FHIR resource profiles. SAMHSA and ONC should jointly develop and⏎mandate FHIR implementation guides specifically for behavioral health, including: standardized⏎profiles for PHQ-9, GAD-7, Columbia Suicide Severity Rating Scale (C-SSRS), AUDIT, DAST,⏎and other validated assessment instruments; FHIR resources for group therapy documentation;⏎and behavioral health-specific social determinants of health data elements aligned with the⏎Gravity Project.⏎Digital Phenotyping Data Standards⏎Dr. Torous's mindLAMP platform at BIDMC represents a leading approach to digital⏎phenotyping—using passive smartphone sensor data (GPS, accelerometry, screen time,⏎communication patterns) and active ecological momentary assessment to capture the lived⏎experience of individuals with mental illness between clinical encounters. Standardized data⏎models for digital phenotyping would enable AI systems to incorporate real-world behavioral data⏎into clinical decision support, treatment planning, and relapse prediction. NIH's All of Us research⏎program, in which Dr. Torous serves as an advisor for the smartphone mood study, provides a⏎model for how such data could be collected at population scale.⏎Crisis and Emergency Data Exchange⏎Behavioral health AI crisis tools—including suicide risk prediction algorithms and crisis chatbot⏎escalation systems—require seamless data exchange with crisis services, emergency⏎departments, and 988 Suicide and Crisis Lifeline infrastructure. Currently, no standardized data⏎exchange protocols exist for transmitting risk-relevant information between AI-powered⏎behavioral health applications and crisis response systems. The 988 infrastructure buildout⏎⏎represents an opportunity to establish behavioral health AI crisis data standards that could benefit⏎both direct patient-facing AI tools and clinician-facing decision support systems.⏎⏎Question 9:⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers⏎wish to see addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally,⏎what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of⏎AI in clinical care?⏎Desired Applications⏎Patients and caregivers in behavioral health settings consistently express desire for AI⏎applications that: increase access to care (particularly for those in underserved rural and urban⏎areas); reduce administrative friction (appointment scheduling, medication refill requests, prior⏎authorization navigation); extend the reach of therapeutic support between sessions through⏎evidence-based digital tools; and improve the quality and attentiveness of clinical encounters by⏎reducing the time their clinician spends looking at a screen. The last point is particularly salient—⏎patients notice when their clinician is distracted by documentation. Eleos Health's research and⏎clinical testimonials frequently highlight that clients receive more engaged, screen-free care when⏎their providers use ambient documentation AI.⏎Dr. Torous's research on patient perspectives on digital mental health, including his widely-cited⏎guides to evaluating mental health apps, consistently finds that patients want tools that are⏎evidence-based, privacy-respecting, and clinically supervised. The patient experience of AI⏎chatbots varies widely—some find them accessible and destigmatizing, while others find them⏎superficial and inadequate for their needs.⏎Patient Concerns⏎Patient concerns about AI in behavioral health are concentrated in three areas. First, privacy:⏎psychotherapy is one of the most sensitive data categories, and patients fear that AI-analyzed⏎session content could be used for insurance underwriting, employment decisions, or law⏎enforcement purposes. The history of stigma and discrimination against people with mental illness⏎and substance use disorders makes these fears reasonable and well-founded. Second,⏎depersonalization: patients fear that AI will replace the human therapeutic relationship with⏎algorithmic interaction, reducing their care to a transaction with a machine. Third, accuracy:⏎patients worry that AI-generated documentation may misrepresent what was said or decided in a⏎session, affecting their treatment or generating records they cannot correct.⏎NAMI's partnership with Dr. Torous to develop independent AI safety benchmarks—explicitly⏎centered on lived experience perspectives—represents a model for how patient voices can be⏎systematically incorporated into behavioral health AI evaluation. HHS should require lived⏎experience representation on any federal advisory bodies addressing behavioral health AI.⏎⏎Question 10:⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should⏎prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎10a: Published Findings on Adopted AI Tools⏎The behavioral health AI evidence base is growing but remains insufficient relative to the pace of⏎deployment. Key published findings include:⏎• Eleos Health has produced 9 peer-reviewed studies on its ambient AI documentation⏎platform, with more than a dozen in progress, including preliminary randomized⏎controlled trial data suggesting improved mental health outcomes for patients whose⏎clinicians use the platform. Findings include 50% higher client engagement, 3-4x better⏎symptom improvement, and 36% greater use of evidence-based techniques.⏎• Lyssn's platform is grounded in 60+ peer-reviewed academic publications developed⏎over 17 years of research, validating its NLP-based approach to assessing Motivational⏎Interviewing fidelity and other EBPs. The platform has analyzed more than 4.3 million⏎sessions, providing an unparalleled evidence base for behavioral health AI quality⏎measurement.⏎• Dr. Torous and colleagues published a comprehensive review in World Psychiatry⏎(2025) on the evolving field of digital mental health, covering smartphone apps,⏎generative AI, chatbots, and virtual reality, concluding that while evidence for some⏎modalities is accumulating, most mental health chatbots remain undertested. His team's⏎50-state legislative review (JMIR Mental Health, 2025) provides the most comprehensive⏎analysis of how states are regulating AI in mental health.⏎• The FDA's April 2024 approval of Rejoyn (Otsuka and Alto Neuroscience's⏎prescription digital therapeutic) for adjunctive treatment of major depressive disorder⏎represents a regulatory milestone, demonstrating that behavioral health AI can meet the⏎FDA's evidence standards for medical device clearance when rigorously evaluated.⏎10b: Costs, Benefits, and Transfers⏎The behavioral health AI literature approaches cost-benefit analysis with increasing⏎sophistication, though significant gaps remain:⏎• Documentation AI demonstrates clear return on investment through reduced⏎clinician hours spent on notes (>70% reduction), improved claim submission timeliness⏎(90% of notes within 24 hours with Eleos Health), reduced late/rejected claims, and⏎⏎improved staff retention. Given that behavioral health clinician turnover costs⏎organizations an estimated $15,000-$30,000 per vacancy, retention benefits alone can⏎justify AI investment within months.⏎• Quality improvement AI (Lyssn) generates value through improved EBP fidelity,⏎which correlates with better patient outcomes and reduced treatment duration—⏎ultimately reducing the per-episode cost of care. The ability to evaluate 100% of clinical⏎encounters rather than a 5-10% sample also reduces audit risk and accreditation⏎vulnerability.⏎• AI chatbots and direct-to-consumer mental health AI present a more complex cost-⏎benefit picture. While they offer potential cost savings through reduced clinician time, Dr.⏎Torous and colleagues have documented risks including failure to recognize crisis⏎situations, inadequate evidence bases, and the possibility of displacing rather than⏎supplementing human care. The costs of adverse events—including patient harm from⏎inadequate crisis response—are difficult to quantify but potentially catastrophic.⏎• A critical and underappreciated transfer dynamic is the reimbursement cliff:⏎behavioral health organizations bear the full cost of AI tools while payers capture most of⏎the financial benefit through reduced claims (fewer denials, improved documentation⏎integrity). CMS and state Medicaid agencies should develop value-sharing mechanisms⏎that ensure AI-adopting providers capture a fair share of the financial value they⏎generate.⏎Priority Research Investments for HHS⏎Based on the above analysis, HHS—through NIMH, SAMHSA, AHRQ, and NIH—should prioritize⏎the following behavioral health AI research investments:⏎• Research support for AI use in crisis assessment and triage including AI tools to⏎improve suicidal risk identification and prevention.⏎• Rigorous clinical trials of AI ambient documentation tools measuring both provider⏎outcomes (burnout, satisfaction, retention) and patient outcomes (therapeutic alliance,⏎symptom improvement, treatment completion rates).⏎• Development and validation of standardized behavioral health AI evaluation⏎frameworks, building on Dr. Torous's APA technology evaluation framework and Lyssn's⏎evidence-based practice fidelity measurement approaches.⏎• Safety research on AI chatbots and digital mental health tools, including prospective⏎studies of adverse event rates, crisis response adequacy, and the impact of AI tool use⏎on suicidal ideation and self-harm outcomes.⏎• Digital equity research examining differential outcomes of behavioral health AI⏎deployment across racial, ethnic, linguistic, and socioeconomic groups, addressing the⏎disparities documented in Dr. Torous's Digital Navigator program research.⏎• Implementation science research on AI adoption in CCBHCs and CMHCs⏎specifically, examining the organizational, workforce, and regulatory factors that predict⏎successful adoption in under-resourced safety-net settings.⏎⏎Citations and References⏎Eleos Health. (2026). Automation and AI Solutions for Behavioral Health. https://eleos.health. Accessed February⏎2026. [Source for ambient documentation statistics: 70%+ documentation time reduction, 80% provider acceptance⏎rate, 90% notes within 24 hours, 2x client engagement, 3-4x symptom improvement, 36% greater EBP use, 9 peer-⏎reviewed studies.]⏎Lyssn. (2026). Training & Quality Improvement AI Solutions for Health & Human Services. https://www.lyssn.io.⏎Accessed February 2026. [Source for: 17 years academic research foundation, 4.3M+ analyzed sessions, 60+ peer-⏎reviewed publications, NLP-based EBP fidelity measurement, CCBHC and crisis care applications.]⏎Torous, J., et al. (2025). The evolving field of digital mental health: current evidence and implementation issues for⏎smartphone apps, generative artificial intelligence, and virtual reality. World Psychiatry, 24(2), 156-174.⏎doi:10.1002/wps.21299.⏎Torous, J. (2025, November 18). Congressional Testimony: Governing AI in Mental Health. U.S. House of⏎Representatives, Committee on Energy and Commerce.⏎https://www.congress.gov/119/meeting/house/118669/witnesses/HHRG-119-IF02-Bio-TorousMDMBIJ-20251118.pdf⏎Torous, J., et al. (2025). Governing AI in Mental Health: 50-State Legislative Review. JMIR Mental Health, 12,⏎e80739. [Source for state-level AI regulation analysis.]⏎Torous, J. (Director, Digital Psychiatry Division, BIDMC/Harvard Medical School). Profile and research overview.⏎https://www.johntorousmd.com. [Source for mindapps.org, mindLAMP, mindApps.ai, Digital Navigator program, APA⏎technology evaluation framework.]⏎National Alliance on Mental Illness (NAMI). (December 2025/January 2026). NAMI Takes the Lead to Push for Clarity⏎and Safety in AI Mental Health Tools. Partnership with Dr. John Torous, BIDMC. https://www.nami.org/press-⏎releases/nami-takes-the-lead-to-push-for-clarity-and-safety-in-ai-mental-health-tools/⏎NAMI. (2025). AI and Mental Health — FAQ with Dr. John Torous. https://www.nami.org/about-mental-illness/ai-and-⏎mental-health/ [Source for NAMI's position that AI is not a substitute for mental health treatment.]⏎Torous, J. (2017, September 25). 'I'm sorry to hear that': Why training Siri to be a therapist won't be easy. [Media⏎coverage of early AI/NLP chatbot limitations for mental health applications.]⏎Torous, J. (2017, December 3). 'The Woebot will see you now' — the rise of chatbot therapy. [Early analysis of AI⏎chatbot mental health applications and evidence gaps.]⏎Torous, J. (2017, July 1). How Clinicians Evaluate Mental Health Apps. [Foundation for APA app evaluation⏎framework.]⏎Torous, J. (2016, December 6). Smartphone health apps often miss real medical emergencies. [Source for AI/app⏎safety concerns, failure to recognize crisis situations.]⏎Torous, J. (2017, December 21). Why choosing a mental health app is harder than you think. [Source for app quality⏎and safety disparities.]⏎FDA. (April 4, 2024). The FDA has approved the first app to help treat depression. [Regulatory milestone for⏎behavioral health digital therapeutics — Rejoyn approval.]⏎Torous, J. (2025). Digital Psychiatry Division, BIDMC. Academic Profile. https://dci.bidmc.org/people/john-torous⏎[Source for APA technology evaluation leadership, All of Us smartphone mood study advisory role.]⏎HHS. (December 23, 2025). Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care. 90 FR 60108. Document Number 2025-23641.⏎Sadeh-Sharvit, S., & Hollon, S.D. (2020). Leveraging the Power of Nondisruptive Technologies to Optimize Mental⏎Health Treatment: Case Study. JMIR Mental Health, 7(11), e20646. [Source for Eleos AI platform clinical validation,⏎⏎2.83 million micro-dialogue dataset, and published RCT/retrospective study program.]⏎https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33242025/⏎Linardon, J., Fuller-Tyszkiewicz, M., Firth, J., Goldberg, S.B., Anderson, C., McClure, Z., & Torous, J. (2024).⏎Systematic review and meta-analysis of adverse events in clinical trials of mental health apps. npj Digital Medicine,⏎7(1), Article 363. doi:10.1038/s41746-024-01388-y⏎Torous, J., Linardon, J., Goldberg, S.B., et al. (2025). The evolving field of digital mental health: current evidence and⏎implementation issues for smartphone apps, generative artificial intelligence, and virtual reality. World Psychiatry,⏎24(2), 156–174. doi:10.1002/wps.21299 [also cited elsewhere in this RFI response]⏎Dwyer, B., et al. (2024). Mental Health Apps and Crisis Support: Exploring the Impact of 988. Psychiatric Services.⏎doi:10.1176/appi.ps.20240485 [Lead author affiliated with BIDMC; published August 2024]⏎Larsen, M.E., Nicholas, J., & Christensen, H. (2016). A systematic assessment of smartphone tools for suicide⏎prevention. PLOS ONE, 11(4), e0152285. doi:10.1371/journal.pone.0152285♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0310♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Anderson♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty-one years as a nurse, and the thing that drove my doctor — Dr. Kaminski, he'd been practicing in Ogden since 1987 — into early retirement wasn't the patients. It was the paperwork. The prior authorizations, the documentation requirements, the clicking through seventeen screens to record something he could have written on a notepad in thirty seconds. He told me in January of 2022 that he was done, and he was done. Gone. And that's the part of this AI conversation nobody seems to want to sit with.⏎⏎If you're going to use AI to speed up diagnostics and treatment recommendations, fine, I see the potential, I really do. But where is the equivalent energy being put into reducing the administrative burden that's chasing good physicians out of the profession? Because right now it feels like HHS wants to layer more technology onto a system that's already exhausted the people running it.⏎⏎My concern with a lot of what I'm reading in this request is that the focus seems to be on speed. Getting things done faster. And I keep thinking about my granddaughter Priya, who was screened twice and missed twice before someone actually sat down and read her whole chart. The algorithm wasn't slow. The algorithm was wrong. She has a connective tissue condition that presents differently in her, and no amount of acceleration would have helped if the underlying tool wasn't built to recognize her presentation in the first place. So when HHS talks about accelerating adoption, I want to know what guardrails are being built in for patients who don't fit the training data.⏎⏎But honestly the thing I keep coming back to, and maybe this is a tangent, is the physicians themselves. You cannot build a functional AI-assisted care system on top of a workforce that is burning out and leaving. Dr. Kaminski was one of the best diagnosticians I ever worked alongside. He's playing golf now because Medicare documentation requirements made his actual job impossible. If AI is going to help anyone, it needs to first help the doctors stay. Reduce the charting load. Reduce the prior auth nonsense. Let AI do the administrative scut work instead of just adding another layer of clinical decision support that someone still has to click through and document and justify to an insurer.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to this. I want to be clear about that. I just think the sequencing is off, and I'm not sure HHS has asked the right people in the right order.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0311♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Kaiser Permanente, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kaiser Permanente♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information; Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎Kaiser Permanente appreciates the opportunity to comment on HHS’ request for information⏎(RFI)1 soliciting public comment regarding how HHS can accelerate the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care (the “HHS Health Sector AI RFI”). Kaiser Permanente2⏎is the largest private integrated health care delivery system in the United States, with more than⏎12.8 million members in nine states and the District of Columbia. Kaiser Permanente’s mission is⏎to provide high-quality, affordable health care services and to improve the health of our members⏎and the communities we serve.⏎AI holds significant potential to improve nearly every aspect of health care. AI can be leveraged⏎to streamline administrative processes and alleviate provider burden while also improving care⏎quality, affordability and patient experience. At the same time, it is critical that AI tools used in⏎health care be safe and effective to build trust among patients and providers. HHS can play an⏎important role in advancing the use of AI by adopting policies that enable health care systems to⏎realize the potential of AI while ensuring tools are safe, effective and provide tangible benefits.⏎Our high priority policy recommendations are summarized below, followed by more detailed⏎comments that provide input on questions and topics addressed in the RFI.⏎Overall, we recommend that HHS:⏎ Adopt a national AI framework that is flexible, adaptable and evidence-based.⏎ Support health care workforce education and training programs to equip providers with⏎resources and training to effectively and responsibly deploy AI tools.⏎ Enhance federal government and private sector partnerships to support AI innovation,⏎research and development.⏎1 90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (Dec. 23, 2025).⏎2 Kaiser Permanente comprises Kaiser Foundation Health Plan, Inc., one of the nation’s largest not-for-profit health plans, and its⏎health plan subsidiaries outside California and Hawaii; the not-for-profit Kaiser Foundation Hospitals, which operates 40 hospitals⏎and more than 600 other clinical facilities; and the Permanente Medical Groups, self-governed physician group practices that⏎exclusively contract with Kaiser Foundation Health Plan and its health plan subsidiaries to meet the health needs of Kaiser⏎Permanente’s members.⏎One Kaiser Plaza⏎Oakland, CA 94612⏎⏎Kaiser Permanente Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎REGULATION⏎HHS states in the RFI that the Department seeks to “establish a regulatory posture on AI that is⏎well understood, predictable and proportionate to any risks presented to enable rapid innovation⏎while protecting patients and the confidentiality of their identifiable health information, and⏎maintaining public trust.”3 Kaiser Permanente appreciates these efforts and offers the following⏎feedback and recommendations regarding how regulation impacts AI adoption and use in clinical⏎care.⏎Complex Regulatory and Enforcement Environment⏎Oversight is important to assure patient safety and care quality, however, the emerging complex⏎regulatory environment with layers of inconsistent guidance and requirements from industry, states⏎and federal agencies hampers health care systems’ ability to adopt AI tools and diminishes the⏎efficiencies and cost savings that use of the tools could bring to clinical care.⏎We therefore recommend that HHS work with stakeholders to establish a federal framework for⏎governing AI used in health care that preempts conflicting state laws. This approach will enable⏎health systems to focus their time and resources on innovating and deploying tools rather than⏎tracking and complying with the proliferation of inconsistent or duplicative requirements.⏎Adoption of a federal AI regulatory framework will also support private sector activities (e.g.,⏎accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing and credentialing) by providing a consistent⏎structure to build upon and align with.⏎The federal AI regulatory framework should be flexible, adaptable and evidence-based. It should⏎provide an overarching structure that is risk-based, consistent with nationally recognized AI risk⏎management frameworks, such as the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) AI⏎Risk Management Framework and the International Organization for Standardization/International⏎Electrotechnical Commission (ISO/IEC) AI Guidance on Risk Management. The framework⏎should also establish guardrails to ensure patient safety while being flexible and adaptable to keep⏎pace with rapidly evolving technology. It should focus on monitoring outcomes rather than⏎imposing prescriptive governance requirements and be scalable by organizations of all sizes and⏎levels of sophistication. This will allow all patients to benefit from innovative technologies while⏎also being protected from potential harm.⏎We recommend that the federal regulatory framework exclude from classification as “developers”⏎those health care entities that develop AI tools for their own internal use and do not make the tools⏎available to others. This approach is consistent with other federal medical software device policies⏎and promotes access to innovative technologies while reducing costs and administrative burden.⏎Health care entities that develop tools carefully tailor them to work within our clinical workflows⏎based on our patient populations, resulting in tools that are more efficient and clinically effective.⏎This approach also reduces risks associated with AI tools developed by third parties, such as⏎potential misuse, unwanted bias, or data inconsistencies or vulnerabilities. Health care entities that⏎self-develop tools are ultimately responsible for how the tool performs, and many of the⏎3 90 Fed. Reg. at 60109.⏎2⏎⏎Kaiser Permanente Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎requirements applicable to developers (e.g., transparency reporting requirements) would not make⏎sense to apply to entities that both develop and deploy the tool internally.⏎Lastly, it is critical that a federal AI regulatory framework be coordinated across federal agencies⏎and across states to avoid duplicative and/or conflicting guidance and enforcement. As noted⏎above, proliferation of differing state-specific requirements will increase administrative burden⏎and create compliance risks that will discourage AI adoption. Similarly, multiple regulators⏎overseeing the same requirements can create confusion and lead to differences in interpretation⏎and enforcement. We recommend that AI oversight be coordinated among expert federal agencies,⏎such as ASTP/ONC and FDA, well versed in emerging technology and associated risks and⏎benefits.⏎Evidence⏎Many health care organizations have been slow to adopt AI tools due to the lack of evidence⏎demonstrating safety and effectiveness. This is compounded by the lack of nationally established⏎standards to measure key functional aspects of AI tools such as, accuracy, explainability and⏎reliability. We recommend that HHS partner with the private sector and standards setting⏎organizations to develop standards that measure these functional aspects of AI tools and systems⏎to enable health systems to accurately evaluate performance across varying clinical settings and⏎applications.⏎We also recommend that HHS seek out private sector partnership opportunities to support the⏎continued development and use of AI in clinical care. For example, rigorous research studies, such⏎as health system-embedded studies and other hybrid implementation-effectiveness studies, should⏎be leveraged to create a robust body of data and evidence that demonstrate how AI tools perform⏎across patient populations in real-world settings. This approach would promote AI adoption by⏎allowing a wide range of health care entities to efficiently evaluate the safety and efficacy of AI⏎tools. Establishing a voluntary post-implementation surveillance program would also promote AI⏎adoption by enabling health care entities to share data to inform best practices and highlight⏎potential issues in a more efficient and cost-effective manner.⏎Trust⏎Lack of trust in AI tools from patients and providers is a fundamental barrier that often inhibits AI⏎tool adoption and efforts to promote trust should be designed and advanced with both patients and⏎providers in mind. Concerns over the effectiveness of the tools, impacts to care quality, patient⏎experience and the privacy and security of health care data all contribute to this lack of trust. Some⏎providers are also hesitant to use AI-enabled tools because they do not understand how the tools⏎were developed, how they work, or how to use them in practice.⏎To address these concerns, we recommend that HHS work with stakeholders to develop and⏎implement a set of minimum documentation requirements for developers of AI-enabled health care⏎tools to disclose to the entities that will use the tool. This should include sufficient information to⏎help providers understand how the tool was developed, including what data was used to train the⏎tool, and how it is intended to function. Such transparency is particularly important in health care⏎to help providers determine if a tool is appropriate for their needs. For example, a risk stratification⏎3⏎⏎Kaiser Permanente Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎tool that was trained with Medicare beneficiary data may not be appropriate for use in pediatric⏎practices.⏎We also recommend that HHS support health care workforce education and training programs to⏎equip providers with the resources and training to effectively and responsibly deploy AI tools.⏎HHS should publish implementation toolkits along with best practices to guide health care⏎organizations, along with sponsoring AI resource centers such as the Kaiser Permanente⏎Augmented Intelligence in Medicine and Healthcare Initiative (AIM-HI) Coordinating Center4⏎that demonstrate the value clinical AI tools can bring to providers and patients across diverse care⏎settings.⏎Lastly, we recommend that HHS develop and launch education campaigns for consumers,⏎particularly those enrolled in public programs such as Medicaid and Medicare. These education⏎campaigns should explain how AI can be used to improve care and help consumers differentiate⏎between patient wellness devices and applications and AI-enabled tools used by providers,⏎including the differences in required privacy and security protections.⏎Privacy and Security of Patient Data⏎Patient health data is essential to developing, validating and deploying AI tools used in clinical⏎care, yet patient privacy is consistently cited as a barrier to adoption and use. While the Health⏎Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) Privacy and Security Rules provide a⏎robust framework that protects information handled by covered entities and business associates,⏎most privacy risks related to AI in health care occur outside HIPAA’s scope—among consumer⏎applications, data brokers and other entities that are neither covered entities nor business⏎associates.⏎We recommend that HHS issue guidance that describes existing privacy and security protections⏎and how they apply to the use of AI data in health care. This guidance should highlight any gaps⏎or opportunities, such as consumer-directed tools and applications, so that any additional⏎protections align with and build upon the existing regulatory framework. Specifically, guidance⏎should:⏎ Clarify how the de-identification standards in 45 CFR 164.514 apply to AI model training⏎and validation, including the use of limited datasets and synthetic data;⏎ Outline expectations for business associate agreements (BAAs) when vendors or service⏎providers host, fine-tune or deploy AI models using protected health information; and⏎ Provide examples of compliant implementation of the HIPAA Security Rule for AI⏎systems, including encryption, access controls and use of privacy-enhancing technologies,⏎such as secure enclaves and federated learning.⏎4 Greene, Jan, (Dec. 18, 2023) Kaiser Permanente coordinates real-world demonstrations of AI, machine learning in health care,⏎Kaiser Permanente Division of Research, available at https://divisionofresearch.kaiserpermanente.org/kaiser-permanente-ai-⏎machine-learning-in-health-care/⏎4⏎⏎Kaiser Permanente Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎This guidance would reduce uncertainty for covered entities, strengthen compliance consistency⏎across the health care sector, and avoid the need for potentially duplicative AI-specific privacy⏎regulations.⏎We also recommend that HHS work with stakeholders to develop a federal approach to closing⏎the privacy gap that occurs outside of HIPAA’s scope. This approach should establish baseline⏎privacy and security requirements for non-HIPAA actors that collect or infer health-related data⏎while preserving the statute’s established framework for regulated health care organizations.⏎Accountability⏎A lack of clear accountability structures leads to uncertainty about who is responsible for AI-⏎related errors or negative outcomes which discourages adoption, especially for higher-risk⏎applications such as generative tools used in clinical applications. We recommend that HHS issue⏎interpretive guidance that defines accountability for outcomes, distinguishing between developer⏎and deployer responsibilities. Deployers should be responsible for their use of the AI tool while⏎developers should be accountable for data quality (including any third-party data sources used for⏎development or validation), model and system performance, and design flaws.⏎Promote Value-Based and Integrated Care⏎Integrated care models, such as Kaiser Permanente, facilitate coordinated care that improves⏎patient outcomes5 in a cost-effective manner. Health condition-specific digital tools and programs⏎may help patients better understand and manage their health conditions. However, there is a risk⏎that these programs and tools may fragment care delivery by dividing up care management⏎activities and accountabilities across various entities without a corresponding mechanism to share⏎data and coordinate care, which can lead to poorer outcomes, especially for individuals with⏎multiple chronic health conditions.⏎Additionally, current payment models do not create incentives for health systems to leverage high-⏎quality and clinically appropriate virtual care and AI tools to augment traditional care delivery⏎modes. We encourage HHS to explore reimbursement and program design approaches that support⏎integration of these tools and programs into existing health system clinical workflows and allow⏎flexibility across care settings with aligned incentives based on the total cost of care.⏎RESEARCH AND DEVELOPMENT⏎We appreciate efforts by HHS to investigate ways to invest in research and development to⏎integrate AI in care delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities to improve the health⏎and wellbeing of all Americans.⏎Published Findings on Impact of Adopted AI Tools and Use in Clinical Care⏎5 Cooke, K., Kaiser Permanente: Highlights of an integrated care delivery system. Hospital Administration and Medical⏎Practices (Sept. 2024), available at https://doi.org/10.54844/hamp.2024.0046⏎5⏎⏎Kaiser Permanente Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Investments in research and development can provide patients with access to life-changing⏎advancements in care delivery that improve quality of care and clinical outcomes. At Kaiser⏎Permanente, we are working to advance understanding of how AI tools perform in actual health⏎care delivery settings with appropriate guardrails and monitoring. We have published several⏎studies that demonstrate the effectiveness of these tools, including:⏎ Advance Alert Monitor: An innovative AI system that is estimated to save 500 lives a year6⏎in our Northern California hospitals by using predictive analytics to identify at-risk⏎hospitalized patients and prevent deterioration and reduce adverse outcomes before they⏎occur.7⏎ Transition Support Level Score: An AI system that uses predictive analytics to identify⏎patients that would benefit from a care coordination intervention after hospital discharge⏎which resulted in reduced hospital readmission rates.8⏎ Desktop Medicine Program: An AI system that uses natural language processing⏎algorithms to optimize patient-physician email communication9 and reduce provider⏎burden.⏎ Ambient AI Scribes: AI system that uses machine learning to facilitate scribe-like⏎capabilities in real time to improve physician-patient interaction and improve⏎administrative efficiency.10 This tool has supported over 2.5 million visits and our⏎physicians experienced a reduction in clinical documentation workload of more than⏎15,700 hours, equivalent to 1,794 working days, over a year of use compared to non-⏎users.11⏎ Kaiser Permanente Intelligent Navigator: AI system that augments the patient portal⏎appointment booking workflow to enhance care navigation and improve the patient⏎experience by using natural language processing to detect high-acuity cases and⏎recommend scheduling options for timely and appropriate care. Patients using the system⏎reported improved satisfaction and ease of navigation.12⏎6 Martinez, et al., The Kaiser Permanente Northern California Advance Alert Monitor Program: An Automated Early Warning⏎System for Adults at Risk for In-Hospital Clinical Deterioration, Joint Commission Journal on Quality and Patient Safety (Aug.⏎2022), available at https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1553725022001106.⏎7 Escobar, G et al., Automated Identification of Adults at Risk for In-Hospital Clinical Deterioration. The New England Journal⏎of Medicine (Nov. 2020), available at https://www.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/NEJMsa2001090⏎8 Marafino, B. et al., Evaluation of an intervention targeted with predictive analytics to prevent readmissions in an integrated⏎health system: observational study. BMJ,(Aug 2021), available at https://www.bmj.com/content/374/bmj.n1747⏎9 Liu VX, et al., Content of Patient Electronic Messages to Physicians in a Large Integrated System. JAMA Network Open,(April⏎2024), available at https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2817150?resultClick=3⏎10 Tierney, et al., Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes to Alleviate the Burden of Clinical Documentation. NEJM Catalyst⏎(Feb. 2024), available at https://catalyst.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/CAT.23.0404⏎11 Tierney, et al., Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes: Learnings after 1 Year and Over 2.5 Million Uses, NEJM Catal Innov⏎Care Deliv (Mar. 2025) available at https://catalyst.nejm.org/doi/full/10.1056/CAT.25.0040 .⏎12 Nguyen, D., Lee, S., Synghal, R. et al., Digital transformation with clinical alerts and personalized care systems in an integrated⏎value based model. npj Digit. Med. (July 2025), available at https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01838-1⏎6⏎⏎Kaiser Permanente Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Consumer Directed Tools⏎Despite concerns related to AI, patients and caregivers are increasingly using AI-enabled tools⏎such as health care apps and websites to help them manage their health and access care.13⏎The FDA recently clarified that many low-risk software tools14 and consumer wearables15 fall⏎outside of medical device regulation. This guidance is intended to focus regulatory oversight on⏎products that present meaningful risks to patient safety, while allowing lower-risk consumer⏎technologies to evolve without unnecessary regulatory burden. Under the new guidance, FDA may⏎consider products that use non-invasive sensing to estimate, infer or output physiologic parameters⏎(such as heart rate variability, blood glucose, oxygen saturation or blood pressure) to be non-device⏎general wellness products when the outputs are intended solely for wellness uses.⏎Wellness devices are popular with consumers and wearable technology is becoming much more⏎capable and user-friendly. However, despite their popularity, consumer wearables continue to fall⏎short in data accuracy and consistency, especially when compared to medical devices. Because⏎sensors used in wellness devices can vary, two similar wearables might show different readings⏎for the same health metric. Such inconsistency limits their utility in health care where even small⏎errors can create risk, and unreliable data can cause unnecessary worry or, conversely, a false sense⏎of security among users. Nevertheless, wellness devices and applications can be useful tools to⏎promote healthy behaviors and to improve population well-being.⏎HHS should prioritize research to better understand how patients are using consumer directed⏎tools, including wellness devices. This research should be used to determine the benefits associated⏎with use, along with any guardrails that should be put in place to ensure that the tools add value⏎and do not create confusion for patients and add administrative burden, complexity and risk for⏎providers. We caution against pilots or programs that directly incorporate data from unregulated⏎consumer directed tools into the patient’s medical record due to data accuracy concerns and⏎variability in how the various tools capture and record data. However, we encourage HHS to⏎establish best practices to guide wellness tool developers and educate patients and providers about⏎the utility of wellness devices and applications to promote appropriate use.⏎* * *⏎Kaiser Permanente appreciates HHS’ consideration of our comments, and we look forward to⏎continued collaboration. Please feel free to contact me at erin.e.richardson@kp.org or Megan Lane⏎at Megan.A.Lane@kp.org with questions or if we can provide additional information.⏎13 Sparks, Grace, Montalvo III, Julian, et al., KFF Health Tracking Poll: Public Use and Trust in Health Care Apps and Websites,⏎KFF (Oct, 24, 2025), available at https://www.kff.org/public-opinion/kff-health-tracking-poll-public-use-and-trust-in-health-care-⏎apps-and-websites/⏎14 U.S. Food & Drug Administration (Jan., 2026) Clinical Decision Support Software: Guidance for Industry and Food and Drug⏎Administration Staff, Center for Devices and Radiological Health, Center for Biologics Evaluation and Research, Center for Drug⏎Evaluation and Research, available at https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/clinical-⏎decision-support-software⏎15 U.S. Food & Drug Administration (Jan., 2026) General Wellness: Policy for Low Risk Devices, Center for Devices and⏎Radiological Health, available at https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-documents/general-wellness-⏎policy-low-risk-devices⏎7⏎⏎Kaiser Permanente Comments⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Sincerely,⏎__________________________________________⏎Erin E. Richardson⏎Senior Vice President⏎Policy and Regulatory Affairs⏎_________________________________________⏎Stephen Parodi, MD⏎Executive Vice President⏎External Affairs, Communications, and Brand⏎The Permanente Federation⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0312♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Vielight USA, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Vielight USA, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to Request for Information⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎___________________________⏎Title:⏎A Focused AI-Enabled Photobiomodulation Care Model to Reduce the Burden of Traumatic Brain⏎Injury⏎Responding Organizations⏎Lead Implementation Entity⏎Vielight USA, Inc. — U.S.-based entity advancing clinical deployment, partnerships, and regulatory⏎engagement for AI-enabled photobiomodulation (PBM) neuromodulation⏎Supporting Organizations⏎Vielight Inc. — technology developer and scientific platform⏎PBM Foundation (501(c)(3)) — nonprofit research, education, and policy partner⏎Center of Excellence for Photobiomodulation, Shepherd University — academic research and training⏎hub⏎Advanced Health Center for Integrative & Functional Medicine — potential clinical implementation⏎partner⏎1. Executive Summary⏎This submission provides a focused perspective on how artificial intelligence can enable scalable, non-⏎invasive neurological therapeutics, using traumatic brain injury (TBI) as a high-impact use case.⏎The responding organizations are committed to developing targeted AI capabilities aligned with a clear⏎mission: maximizing functional recovery and long-term outcomes for individuals affected by TBI.⏎Rather than attempting to address all aspects of AI in healthcare, this initiative concentrates on areas⏎where photobiomodulation-based neuromodulation, physiological monitoring, and longitudinal⏎analytics can have measurable impact.⏎Photobiomodulation (PBM), a low-risk light-based neuromodulation modality, offers the potential for⏎scalable deployment under risk-proportionate oversight. Through coordinated research, clinical⏎deployment, and real-world evidence generation, the goal is to establish a scalable system for reducing⏎TBI burden at population level and to inform broader neurological care strategies.⏎Headline Impact Statement⏎Even modest improvements in recovery outcomes across a subset of individuals living with TBI could⏎translate into tens of billions of dollars in lifetime societal savings. Scalable, AI-enabled PBM⏎interventions therefore represent a potentially high-value strategy for addressing one of the nation’s⏎⏎most persistent neurological health challenges while supporting long-term economic and human capital⏎resilience.⏎2. Public Health Context⏎Traumatic brain injury remains a major contributor to disability, mental health conditions, and long-term⏎healthcare utilization. Recovery trajectories are heterogeneous, and many individuals experience⏎persistent symptoms affecting cognitive, emotional, and functional capacity.⏎Innovative care models leveraging physiological monitoring and data-driven insights have the potential⏎to improve recovery pathways and reduce long-term disability and societal costs.⏎2.1 National Economic Impact Scenario⏎Lifetime societal costs associated with TBI range widely, with estimates spanning approximately $85,000⏎to more than $3 million per individual depending on severity.1, 2 For policy modeling purposes, an⏎illustrative average burden of $500,000 per patient demonstrates the scale of opportunity for improved⏎recovery pathways.⏎Assuming a conservative 10% reduction in long-term costs through improved outcomes, savings could⏎reach approximately $50,000 per individual. Applied to one million individuals — a subset of the⏎population living with TBI-related disability — this represents potential lifetime societal savings⏎exceeding $50 billion.⏎By comparison, providing a home-based PBM system at approximately $2,400 per patient would total⏎roughly $2.4 billion, illustrating the magnitude of potential net societal benefit even under conservative⏎assumptions.⏎Illustrative Economic Impact Scenario⏎Parameter Assumption Result⏎Average lifetime cost $500,000 —⏎Modeled improvement 10% $50,000 savings per patient⏎Population modeled 1,000,000 —⏎Total savings — $50B⏎Intervention cost $2,400 per patient $2.4B⏎Net societal benefit — ~$47.6B⏎Illustrative modeling for policy context.⏎⏎3. Clinical Use Case: AI-Guided Photobiomodulation Neuromodulation⏎Photobiomodulation (PBM) is a non-invasive light-based neuromodulation modality being investigated⏎for its ability to influence biological processes relevant to TBI recovery, including neuroinflammation,⏎mitochondrial activity, and cerebral perfusion.3⏎Vielight USA is advancing an integrated platform combining:⏎• PBM neuromodulation⏎• Physiological and symptom monitoring⏎• Cloud-based analytics⏎• Machine learning–driven treatment optimization⏎Artificial intelligence capabilities are already being incorporated into research programs and pilot⏎implementations, enabling adaptive treatment optimization and longitudinal outcome tracking.4⏎⏎Figure 1. PBM platform with integrated digital interface supporting treatment monitoring and⏎optimization.⏎4. Translational Ecosystem⏎The initiative leverages a collaborative ecosystem spanning technology development, academic⏎research, nonprofit advocacy, and clinical implementation, supporting evidence generation and scalable⏎deployment.⏎5. Implementation Readiness and Credibility⏎PBM technologies referenced in this submission are supported by peer-reviewed research and clinical⏎studies. Artificial intelligence–driven analytics are being incorporated into research programs to support⏎treatment personalization.⏎⏎More than 150,000 PBM devices have been deployed globally for home use, demonstrating feasibility of⏎large-scale distribution and patient adherence. This level of real-world deployment provides a⏎foundation for AI-driven optimization and population-level implementation.⏎The combination of peer-reviewed evidence, active AI development, and large-scale real-world⏎deployment positions Vielight PBM as an implementation-ready platform capable of supporting federal⏎pilot programs and evidence-generation initiatives.⏎6. Military Readiness and Human Capital Considerations⏎Beyond healthcare cost savings, improving neurological recovery has implications for military readiness⏎and long-term veteran health. TBI remains a significant challenge affecting service members,5 and⏎interventions that support resilience and cognitive function may contribute to preservation of⏎operational capability and human capital.⏎⏎Figure 2. Example of PBM neuromodulation being explored in operational readiness and recovery⏎contexts.⏎7. Responses to Specific Questions⏎7.1 Barriers to innovation⏎A major barrier to AI-enabled neurological therapeutics is the lack of reimbursement pathways⏎recognizing longitudinal outcome improvement. TBI recovery unfolds over extended periods, making⏎traditional episodic payment models poorly aligned with therapeutic impact.⏎⏎PBM platforms equipped with monitoring and analytics can generate real-world evidence demonstrating⏎functional improvement and cost offsets. Federal demonstration programs enabling coverage with⏎evidence development would accelerate adoption and provide critical data for policymakers.⏎7.2 Regulatory and payment considerations⏎Low-risk non-invasive therapies may face lengthy evaluation pathways that delay patient access despite⏎favorable safety profiles. For PBM-based interventions, a risk-proportionate framework allowing⏎provisional access with ongoing safety monitoring could enable earlier deployment while preserving⏎safeguards.⏎Outcome-based reimbursement pilots could evaluate the value of AI-guided treatment optimization in⏎improving recovery trajectories and reducing long-term costs.⏎7.3 Legal and governance considerations⏎AI-enabled therapeutic systems require clear governance structures defining responsibility among⏎developers, clinicians, and healthcare organizations. PBM platforms can incorporate human-in-the-loop⏎oversight, transparent decision support, and version control to ensure accountability.⏎Federal guidance on governance standards would help ensure safe and consistent implementation.⏎7.4 Evaluation methods⏎Traditional clinical trials may not fully capture the impact of therapies whose benefits accrue over⏎months or years. Hybrid evaluation models combining pragmatic trials, digital biomarkers, and real-⏎world evidence are particularly relevant for TBI.⏎Continuous monitoring enabled by PBM platforms can provide longitudinal data supporting these⏎evaluation frameworks. Federal support for demonstration projects would accelerate validation.⏎7.5 Supporting private sector activities⏎Public-private clinical testbeds would allow developers to evaluate AI-enabled therapies in real-world⏎settings. PBM programs could be deployed in academic or federal healthcare systems to study⏎outcomes, workflow integration, and cost impact.⏎Grants and cooperative agreements would facilitate these collaborations.⏎7.6 Where AI has met or fallen short⏎AI has demonstrated strong performance in diagnostics but less impact on directly improving patient⏎outcomes. Therapeutic optimization represents the next frontier.⏎⏎AI-enabled PBM platforms offer an example of how algorithms can personalize treatment parameters⏎and monitor response over time, potentially translating data insights into improved clinical outcomes.⏎7.7 Adoption decision makers⏎Health system leadership evaluates new technologies based on clinical benefit, cost impact, and⏎operational feasibility. Implementation toolkits demonstrating workflow integration and economic value⏎would facilitate adoption.⏎Pilot programs providing structured outcome reporting could support evidence-based procurement⏎decisions.⏎7.8 Interoperability⏎Integration of physiological monitoring with electronic health records is critical for advancing AI-enabled⏎care. Vielight PBM systems capable of collecting remote telemetry could contribute valuable⏎longitudinal data streams.⏎Expansion of interoperability standards would enable broader research and innovation.⏎7.9 Patient and caregiver perspectives⏎Patients prioritize improved recovery, accessibility, and transparency. Home-based PBM interventions⏎supported by remote monitoring may enhance access while maintaining clinician oversight.⏎Clear communication and privacy protections are essential to building trust.⏎7.10 Research priorities⏎AI-guided treatment personalization and longitudinal neurological outcomes should be prioritized⏎research areas. Collaborative programs involving academic centers, developers, and healthcare systems⏎could evaluate PBM and similar modalities.⏎Emerging literature supports integration of physiological monitoring with adaptive neuromodulation⏎approaches, though further studies are needed to quantify economic impact and long-term benefits.⏎8. Conclusion⏎Artificial intelligence offers a meaningful opportunity to transform neurological care when applied to⏎clearly defined high-burden conditions such as traumatic brain injury.⏎⏎By focusing on photobiomodulation-based neuromodulation and leveraging targeted AI capabilities⏎aligned with demonstrated expertise, this initiative seeks to establish a scalable, evidence-driven model⏎for improving outcomes and reducing societal burden.⏎Risk-proportionate provisional access pathways combined with real-world evidence generation could⏎accelerate innovation while maintaining safety and accountability.⏎REFERENCES⏎1. National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine. Traumatic Brain Injury: A Roadmap⏎for Accelerating Progress. 2022. https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK580076/⏎2. Corso P., Finkelstein E., Miller T., Fiebelkorn I., Zaloshnja E. Incidence and Lifetime Costs of⏎Injuries in the United States. Injury Prevention. 2006.⏎1. Lim L. Traumatic Brain Injury Recovery with Photobiomodulation: Cellular Mechanisms, Clinical⏎Evidence, and Future Potential. Cells. 2024.⏎3. Topol E. High-performance medicine: the convergence of human and artificial intelligence.⏎Nature Medicine. 2019.⏎4. Defense and Veterans Brain Injury Center (DVBIC). TBI in Military Populations.⏎https://dvbic.dcoe.mil♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0313♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Lee♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔American College of Preventive Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔The American College of Preventive Medicine appreciates the opportunity to respond to this RFI, but I want to be direct: the framing of this solicitation concerns me before we get to any specific question. Asking how HHS can "accelerate" AI adoption in clinical and administrative settings, without first establishing what minimum safeguards must be in place before that acceleration happens, puts the cart well ahead of the horse in ways our member organizations are already experiencing on the ground.⏎⏎I want to focus my comments on the questions related to reimbursement and coverage determination functions, because that is where our members are reporting the most concentrated and immediate harm.⏎⏎ACPM Comments | HHS AI RFI | Submitted February 2025 | Page 1⏎⏎Our 2024 member survey — conducted across approximately 2,800 member organizations — found that 63% had received patient complaints specifically tied to algorithmic-driven care recommendations or coverage decisions. That number is high. But what stands out more to me, having reviewed the underlying data from our Health Equity Working Group, is where those complaints are concentrated: facilities serving Medicaid-enrolled populations, patients with limited English proficiency, elderly patients with complex comorbidities. These are not people with robust capacity to navigate multi-step appeals processes. They are often already overwhelmed by their medical situations, and an automated denial letter — with little explanation and a 60-day appeals window that assumes they have time, language skills, and persistence — is functionally a final decision for many of them.⏎⏎The specific situation I keep returning to when I read this RFI is what happened with UnitedHealth Group's nH Predict tool, which was the subject of a class action lawsuit filed in late 2023. The allegation, supported by internal data the plaintiffs obtained, was that the algorithm was overriding treating physicians' recommendations for post-acute care at a rate that produced denials in approximately 90% of reviewed cases for certain Medicare Advantage patients. A federal court allowed that case to proceed. I'm not saying every AI tool in prior authorization functions behaves this way, but the fact that we are discussing acceleration of AI adoption in coverage functions without any serious rulemaking response to that documented episode is genuinely hard to understand from a patient protection standpoint.⏎⏎ACPM Comments | HHS AI RFI | Submitted February 2025 | Page 2⏎⏎HHS has authority here. The existing statutory framework under the Medicare Advantage regulations, the ACA's coverage determination provisions, and the ADA's requirements around meaningful program access give the Department real tools it has not fully deployed. Requiring that any AI-assisted prior authorization or coverage determination system be explainable in plain language to the patient, that a qualified human reviewer must affirmatively approve any denial (not just be nominally "available"), and that appeal timelines account for the actual burden on non-English-speaking patients — none of that requires new legislation. It requires HHS to treat acceleration and accountability as inseparable, rather than sequencing accountability as something to figure out later.⏎⏎What particularly frustrates me is that fewer than one in four of our member organizations currently provides patients with any meaningful disclosure about when AI is influencing their care or coverage decisions. Twenty-three percent. We asked that question specifically because we suspected the number would be low, and it was still worse than we expected. You cannot have an informed consent framework, you cannot have meaningful appeals rights, you cannot have health equity in AI deployment if patients don't even know an algorithm was involved in the decision that affected them.⏎⏎I recognize there are legitimate arguments for moving quickly on AI in clinical settings — diagnostic support, documentation burden reduction, population health screening. I'm not opposed to AI in medicine categorically. But the prior authorization and coverage determination context is different, and treating it the same as, say, an ambient documentation tool or a radiology assist function doesn't hold up analytically. The stakes of a wrong output are asymmetric in a very specific way: the patient bears all of the downside, and the insurer bears essentially none under the current structure.⏎⏎ACPM Comments | HHS AI RFI | Submitted February 2025 | Page 3⏎⏎The RFI asks about barriers to adoption and what HHS can do to reduce them. I'd ask the Department to also consider what barriers currently exist to patients challenging AI-driven decisions, because those barriers are structural and they are growing as these systems scale. The appeals processes most plans use were designed around human reviewer error rates and response times. They have not been updated to account for the volume of denials that an automated system can generate, or the fact that many of those denials are effectively identical because they come from the same algorithmic output applied thousands of times. A patient in Worcester, Massachusetts filing an appeal in January 2025 is essentially arguing against a policy embedded in code, with no access to the model, no explanation of which inputs drove the denial, and no meaningful way to know whether their appeal is being reviewed by someone who has actually looked at their case or by♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0314♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Skaistis, Leslie, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Leslie♔Skaistis♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am a licensed clinical psychologist working with adults along the lifespan. Though I believe that AI technologies offer great promise as adjunct treatments to standard mental health treatment, they are largely untested at this time and, like all new treatments, need to be closely evaluated and regulated. I endorse the public comment submitted by the Psychotherapy Action Network (PsiAN) on this time. ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0315♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Varra, Vamsi, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Vamsi♔Varra♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am dermatologist whose research specializes in artificial intelligence applications in clinical care. One of the challenges for introducing safe AI tools to improve healthcare is navigating the regulations surrounding it. Information for companies developing AI clinical AI products would be helpful to distinguish AI tools which do or do not require FDA approval. Furthermore, in the future autonomous medical decision making without involvement of a human clinician could be possible. It will be crucial to create regulatory standards for compliance of those tools.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0316♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ECRI , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ECRI♔♔♔♔♔♔Friday, February 20, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎ECRI is submitting the following comments in response to HHS’s Request for Information (RFI) on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. As an independent⏎nonprofit organization advancing evidence-based practices to improve patient safety, healthcare⏎quality, and cost-effectiveness, ECRI supports efforts to ensure that AI tools used in the clinical⏎environment are safe, effective, transparent, and worthy of public trust.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private section innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The greatest barriers to AI innovation and adoption are not algorithmic limitations, but gaps in real-⏎world readiness, including:⏎• Procurement processes that do not reward transparency or safety⏎• Limited independent validation⏎• Fragmented data systems⏎• Poor interoperability⏎• Post-implementation monitoring⏎• Cybersecurity and reliability vulnerabilities⏎• Unclear accountability when AI influences care⏎Highly publicized failures of widely deployed prediction tools demonstrate that AI adoption without⏎evidence can weaken trust and stall progress. One example was seen during external validation of EPIC's⏎AI-enabled sepsis prediction program. America gains the most when innovation is tested, proven, and⏎trusted, not when shortcomings are discovered post-deployment.⏎A recent analysis of ways to improve adoption of digital technologies, including AI, highlights that⏎creating clear reimbursement pathways and integrating AI tools into clinical practice guidelines are⏎essential adoption enablers.⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS can strengthen adoption of clinically valuable AI by prioritizing actions that reward transparency,⏎accountability, and sustained performance. ECRI encourages HHS to:⏎• Communicate, support and enforce expectations for clinical AI performance and accountability.⏎• Expand reimbursement pathways for validated clinical AI interventions.⏎• Promote transparency standards that reward vendors who clearly communicate capabilities,⏎limitations, and performance.⏎• Clarify expectations for managing AI updates and performance drift over time.⏎These measures do not restrict innovation—they differentiate serious innovators from opportunistic⏎ones.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Non-medical AI tools—such as general-purpose large language model (LLMs) chatbots, create a distinct⏎governance gap. They can influence clinical decisions and patient behavior, but they often sit outside the⏎accountability, validation, and lifecycle controls that healthcare organizations expect for clinical⏎technology.⏎Some of the unique legal and implementation issues include:⏎• Accountability Gaps⏎o Diffused liability: Vendor disclaims clinical use and health system assumes tool is “just⏎informational” but clinicians use it for clinically actionable queries. When harm occurs,⏎responsibility is unclear.⏎o Indemnification mismatches: Contracts often lack meaningful indemnity for clinical harm,⏎hallucinated content, or latent model defects. When harm occurs, no one is responsible and⏎improvements are not stimulated as a result.⏎• Unvalidated “Clinical Advice”⏎o LLM outputs can be very convincing even when inaccurate, which creates automation bias⏎and subtle influence even when clinicians think they are not being influenced.⏎• Inappropriate Generalization⏎o Performance varies by context, population, language, and clinical domain. Without⏎transparent limitations, LLMs can amplify disparities or mislead in edge cases.⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎• Update Opacity and Performance drift⏎o Unannounced model changes (or retrieval/source changes) can alter behavior without⏎warning, undermining clinical governance, training, and auditability.⏎o Lack of stable versioning makes incident investigation difficult: “What would the model have⏎said on the date of the incident?” may be unknowable.⏎• Traceability, audit, and record integrity⏎o Chat interactions may not be logged in a clinically meaningful way, or logs are stored outside⏎the organization’s control.⏎• Privacy, security, and secondary use⏎o Prompt content may include Protected Health Information (PHI), sometimes inadvertently.⏎Ambiguity around data retention, model training on user data, and potential for data⏎transfer to countries outside the U.S. as part of downstream processing complicates⏎compliance and risk.⏎o Tool integrations (browser, plugins, AI agents) expand the attack surface and create new⏎routes for data exfiltration or manipulation potentially in the form of cybersecurity risks like⏎prompt injection and tool hijacking.⏎What role should HHS play? Here are recommended actions:⏎Set enforceable expectations for clinical-impact AI, regardless of device status. HHS should clarify that⏎if a system is used in ways that materially influence clinical decisions, it should meet⏎baseline expectations for:⏎• Transparency (intended use, verification/validation test methods, limitations, known failure⏎modes)⏎• Traceability (versioning, audit logs, data provenance)⏎• Lifecycle governance (change management, monitoring, incident reporting)⏎• Truthful performance claims (accurate performance metrics across populations and sub⏎populations)⏎Use payment and program levers to incentivize governance. Through Centers for Medicare & Medicaid⏎Services (CMS) programs, HHS could condition participation or reimbursement on healthcare⏎organizations demonstrating that they:⏎• Maintain an AI inventory (including non-device tools such as LLMs).⏎• Apply risk-tiered evaluation (piloting) prior to use.⏎• Implement ongoing monitoring and user training.⏎• Document when and how AI is used in care processes.⏎• Have an error reporting program in place.⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎Provide standard procurement and contracting language. Model contract clauses for health⏎systems should cover:⏎• Minimum logging/audit requirements.⏎• Versioning and advance notice of model updates.⏎• Data use and retention limits (including “no training on our data” unless explicit).⏎• Vendor obligations for safety incidents and vulnerability disclosure.⏎• Realistic indemnification aligned to risk.⏎Clarify privacy/security guardrails for LLM workflows. Through the HHS Office for Civil Rights and the⏎Office for the National Coordinator for Health IT (ONC), HHS can issue practical guidance on:⏎• What constitutes acceptable handling of PHI in prompts.⏎• HIPAA compliant Business Associate Agreements (BAAs) and subcontractor chains for LLM⏎vendors who could encounter PHI.⏎• Minimum security controls for AI-enabled programs with the ability to influence patient⏎care, i.e. those connected to electronic health records (EHRs).⏎• Safe defaults (redaction, local processing options, retention minimization).⏎Create an enforceable “truth-in-claims” regime for AI with clinical capabilities. HHS can coordinate⏎with the Federal Trade Commission (FTC) and Food and Drug Administration (FDA) so vendors cannot⏎imply clinical-grade abilities without:⏎• Disclosing evaluation methods.⏎• Specifying populations/settings tested.⏎• Accurately reporting performance metrics.⏎• Reporting/disclosing known limitations and uncertainty.⏎Non-medical devices that use AI can shape clinical decisions at scale while escaping the normal checks⏎that protect patients. HHS does not need to micromanage innovation, but it should communicate⏎expectations, support adoption with practical guardrails, and enforce accountability so the systems used⏎in care are measurable, monitorable, and honest about what they are not able to do in a way that end⏎users can understand. Strong accountability builds confidence for clinicians, patients, and investors⏎alike.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎ECRI believes that HHS should support a “prove-it-first, monitor-it-always” approach for any AI tool⏎capable of influencing patient care. Before deployment, HHS should require the⏎following information from vendors whose non-medical AI-enabled device has the ability to influence⏎patient care. “Having the ability to” is defined as capable of performing in that area without sufficient⏎design controls to limit risk to use.⏎Pre-Deployment Information:⏎1. Intended-Use, task-based validation (rather than general model quality): Evaluate the system on⏎specific clinical tasks it will support and include realistic environment features such as⏎representative users, settings, and EHR-integrated contexts, as appropriate.⏎2. Reference-grounded testing: Explicitly test convincing but wrong inputs and report whether the⏎model agrees or corrects them in the output. If the device employs applicable features such as⏎LLMs to summarize guidelines or literature, require source traceability and test citation fidelity⏎as part of the verification/validation.⏎3. Human factors and workflow simulation: Conduct usability tests in simulated clinical scenarios⏎(time pressure, interruptions).⏎4. Safety-focused red teaming: Structured red-team suites for hallucinations (fabricated facts,⏎contraindications), unsafe omissions (missed warnings, missing follow-up), over-confident tone,⏎prompt injection/tool hijacking if connected to systems, and PHI leakage and unintended⏎memorization behaviors. Guardrail behavior should also be tested—when does it refuse vs.⏎answer with uncertainty vs. answer confidently, and is that acceptable? How effectively is⏎uncertainty communicated to the end user?⏎5. Subgroup performance: Stratify results by patient factors relevant to the use case (age,⏎language, health literacy proxies, comorbidity complexity, etc.) and clinical setting.⏎After deployment, HHS should use CMS payment programs to support and incentivize healthcare⏎institutions that establish the following ways to monitor performance and safety.⏎Post-Deployment Monitoring:⏎1. Continuous monitoring with change control: Should include track versioning and model-change⏎documentation and re-validation should be triggered when the model updates, retrieval sources⏎change, prompts/templates change, or workflow integration changes.⏎2. Real-world performance surveillance: Track errors and near-misses tied to AI outputs with CAPA⏎proceedings, establish a pathway for clinician feedback (“this is wrong/unsafe”) that feeds⏎governance review, and ensure reporting structure encourages error-reporting databases such⏎as FDA’s MAUDE.⏎3. Drift and distribution shift monitoring programs: Monitor shifts in case mix, language use,⏎documentation templates, seasonal patterns, new guidelines/drug approvals, and⏎subpopulation performance. Re-test on a rolling benchmark set that reflects current practice.⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎4. Auditable provenance: Capture prompt context, model/version, retrieved sources (if⏎any), output, and downstream action (accepted/edited/ignored) in a way that supports incident⏎review.⏎Ensuring that AI used in clinical care is rigorously evaluated, continuously monitored, and accountable in⏎real-world practice protects American patients, supports American clinicians, and prevents our⏎healthcare system from becoming dependent on opaque technologies we cannot measure, audit, or⏎control.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Independent testing and certification help the strongest products rise. ECRI encourages HHS to⏎support independent programs that evaluate AI robustness, transparency, equity of performance, and⏎monitoring readiness—giving buyers confidence and innovators a fair playing field.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations⏎and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎The path that best serves Americans is simple: deploy AI where it demonstrably reduces harm or⏎burden, prove it in real-world practice, and hold vendors and deployers accountable when performance⏎drifts so U.S. patients don’t become the test bed for opaque systems that do not have the mechanism to⏎be monitored, audited, or controlled.⏎AI performs best when the task is clearly defined and aligned with user needs; outcomes are⏎measurable, and integration is thoughtfully designed. Examples of AI working well include medical⏎imaging applications and time- critical workflows. Medical Imaging AI, the majority of the list of FDA-⏎cleared AI/ML-enabled devices, and health systems are using these tools to support detection/triage⏎and workflow prioritization. The strongest value case is speed and consistency for narrow tasks (e.g.,⏎flagging suspected findings for urgent review), not replacing clinician judgment. Documentation support⏎(AI-enabled ambient scribe use cases) is another example; ambient documentation tools are⏎showing credible benefits in reducing documentation burden and burnout and returning time to⏎clinicians.⏎However, AI struggles when models are opaque, alerts overwhelm clinicians, and performance erodes⏎over time. Examples of areas where AI in clinical use is struggling include high-stakes clinical prediction⏎without rigorous external validation and monitoring. A widely used proprietary sepsis prediction⏎model (implemented at many hospitals) performed substantially worse than expected in real-world⏎evaluation, missing many true cases and generating false alerts—illustrating the risk of deploying models⏎that look good on paper but fail under local workflows and data patterns.⏎Another pain point is generalizability and “silent failure modes.” Clinical AI can fail unevenly across⏎populations when training data and evaluation do not reflect real patients and real workflows, risking⏎worse outcomes for Americans. LLM-enabled tools in clinical settings for non-medical device⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎deployments are a third area, where AI can be confidently wrong, hard to audit, and it is hard to assign⏎accountability. Even when these tools are positioned as “assistive,” they can insert subtle bias into⏎decisions, produce plausible but incorrect content, and change behavior after updates, without the⏎traceability needed to investigate errors. AI-enabled non-medical devices can present legal and consent⏎risks when workflows aren’t designed around transparency and patient permission, and nothing stifles⏎innovation and growth like litigation.⏎An America-first approach to clinical AI means insisting on proof, transparency, and accountability⏎before scaling. AI should strengthen, not replace, clinical judgment, protect patients from preventable⏎harm, and deliver measurable value to the healthcare system Americans depend on. By prioritizing tools⏎that perform reliably in real-world care, enforcing clear expectations when they fall short, and refusing⏎to outsource trust to opaque technologies, we can ensure innovation serves patients, clinicians, and⏎taxpayers—rather than the other way around.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the⏎most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to⏎the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption decisions should involve clinical leaders, IT leaders, safety teams, procurement,⏎administration, and legal counsel. Clear national expectations would help these stakeholders align⏎around value rather than novelty.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate⏎the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎Interoperability is a strategic asset--AI cannot outperform the data it depends on. ECRI encourages⏎continued federal leadership in data standards and exchange, enabling U.S. innovators to build tools⏎that work anywhere in the country, and potentially beyond. The Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC) HTI-1 final rule is a meaningful step toward this goal.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related⏎to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎From ECRI’s perspective, patients want AI that reduces wait times, improves safety, and helps clinicians⏎focus on care, not screens. Patients worry about loss of privacy, unfair or unexplained decisions, and⏎errors without recourse. Public trust is not a barrier to innovation; it is a prerequisite for success at⏎scale. For many patients, the central question is not whether AI is advanced, but whether it is safe,⏎transparent, and accountable, and whether someone remains clearly responsible when AI influences⏎care. An effective HHS approach should therefore ensure that AI strengthens clinical relationships,⏎protects patient autonomy and privacy, and delivers measurable benefits patients can actually see and⏎feel, rather than introducing new risks hidden behind technical complexity.⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical⏎care?⏎HHS can accelerate adoption through targeted research that validates AI’s real-world clinical value,⏎including:⏎• AI Software for Improving Outpatient Scheduling and Patient Chart Management⏎o AI software uses nonlinear algorithms and machine learning to perform computer-based⏎tasks. Some nonclinical AI applications in healthcare include optimizing patient scheduling⏎and identifying patient information and data stored in medical records.⏎o AI applications appear to predict patient behaviors (e.g., no-show for appointments) and⏎length of appointments and have the potential to create efficiencies in outpatient⏎appointment scheduling. However, available studies are highly heterogeneous for the⏎patients they are applied to, use different AI models, and lack reporting of patient⏎outcomes, which prevents determining the risks, benefits, and safety of using AI for⏎scheduling and other administrative functions. Most studies lacked a comparison to other⏎methods, so it is unclear how well AI works compared with traditional approaches.⏎o Large, multicenter studies that compare AI with traditional methods for patient scheduling⏎or administrative functions and that report on patient-oriented outcomes are needed.⏎• AI-based Early Warning Systems for Assessing Sepsis Risk⏎o Several AI prediction tools on the market are intended to identify hospitalized patients⏎at high risk of developing sepsis.⏎--TREWS: Evidence from two cohort studies suggests that TREWS can reduce sepsis⏎mortality by enabling earlier diagnosis and treatment initiation; however, the data are too⏎limited in quantity and quality to support conclusions. Diagnostic accuracy data are at risk of⏎bias and need independent validation, while data from two studies comparing patients with⏎positive and negative or dismissed TREWS alerts provide only indirect evidence of TREWS’s⏎clinical utility. Additional prospective studies are needed to confirm TREWS’s accuracy and⏎to compare sepsis outcomes in patients managed with and without TREWS.⏎--Immunoscore: Evidence from a diagnostic cohort study shows the Sepsis Immunoscore can⏎predict imminent sepsis onset with moderate accuracy; however, whether this may⏎translate to time-to-treatment, morbidity, and mortality reduction is unclear because no⏎studies report on these outcomes. The study supports only low-confidence conclusions⏎because no independent studies are available to validate the findings. In addition⏎to additional diagnostic performance studies, longitudinal studies that compare sepsis⏎management with and without ImmunoScore are needed to assess its utility.⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎• AI Use in Histopathology and Cytology Practice⏎o In pathology, the digitization of glass slides into high-resolution whole-slide⏎images (WSIs) has facilitated the application of AI algorithms to histopathology and cytology⏎workflows. Advances in computational power, data sharing, and sophisticated AI techniques⏎such as machine learning, deep learning, computer vision, and natural language processing⏎have transformed the field. AI aids in the analysis of large datasets, anomaly detection,⏎tumor grading, and workflow optimization, alleviating the burden on pathologists while⏎enhancing diagnostic accuracy and efficiency.⏎o Critical healthcare challenges also underscore the need for AI adoption in pathology.⏎Misdiagnosis remains a major issue, leading to patient harm, disabilities, and deaths. AI can⏎address this by providing consistent, accurate, and timely diagnostic insights, reducing⏎errors, and complementing human expertise. AI tools are also pivotal in identifying high-risk⏎patients, improving resource allocation, and addressing the global shortage of⏎pathologists, exacerbated by an aging workforce and declining interest in the specialty. AI-⏎driven solutions can automate repetitive tasks, improve diagnostic accuracy, and⏎ensure equitable access to services, addressing workforce and infrastructure challenges.⏎o Evidence from studies indicates that AI-enabled diagnostic reviews of WSI demonstrate high⏎sensitivity, specificity, and diagnostic accuracy relative to manual review across various⏎malignancies including gastric, breast, and prostate cancer. Data to assess AI-enabled⏎histopathologic diagnosis of other conditions is limited. Clinical utility studies show⏎substantial agreement between AI-assisted and manual slide reviews, suggest reduced⏎review times, and may improve detection of missed diagnoses.⏎• AI-Assisted Quantitative MRI for Monitoring Multiple Sclerosis (MS) Progression⏎o Use of MRI for monitoring MS progression is limited by lengthy protocols, error-prone⏎procedures for identifying disease markers (e.g., lesions), and the limited predictive value of⏎existing imaging biomarkers. Several AI-based software systems on the market may address⏎these problems and enhance diagnostic accuracy, refine treatment approaches, and⏎improve overall patient outcomes for MS.⏎o One recent systematic review (SR) examines AI modalities for MS diagnosis based on 23⏎case-control studies published from 2016 to 2023, one SR assesses commercially available AI⏎quantitative volumetric reporting tools, 2 diagnostic cohort studies compare AI-assisted⏎radiologic reports (Pixly.Neuro.MS, iQ-Solutions MS) with conventional radiologic reports,⏎and 5 case series use 2 different AI software packages (iQ-Solutions MS, 1 study⏎and icobrain MS, 4 studies) to monitor MS progression.⏎o The case-control SR reports high sensitivity and specificity for AI programs. In the two⏎studies comparing AI with standard radiology reports, AI improved new lesion detection;⏎however, the AI modalities’ effect on patient outcomes is unclear. The five-case series using⏎AI to monitor disease progression reported mixed results. Many studies rely on⏎retrospective data collection and therefore have a high risk of bias. Commercially available⏎AI software packages have not been adequately tested in clinical situations,⏎so additional high-quality studies are needed to compare software packages in clinical⏎conditions.⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org⏎⏎• AI Software for Evaluating Coronary Artery Disease (CAD)⏎o There is an emerging class of AI software applications intended to facilitate CAD diagnosis⏎and evaluation with coronary computed tomography angiography. Many use interactive 3-D⏎rendering of the patient’s coronary vasculature, which clinicians can use to assess individual⏎lesion risk and overall disease burden.⏎o Clinical literature consistently highlights that AI products for CAD improve diagnostic⏎accuracy, streamline clinical workflows, and enhance patient risk stratification compared to⏎traditional methods. Findings include the reduction of invasive procedures, improved⏎mortality rates, and higher prognostic value than traditional risk scores or stenosis severity⏎alone. However, more prospective studies reporting actual outcomes are needed⏎to validate clinical utility and optimal place in the CAD management pathway. The evidence⏎base also lacks long-term outcomes data, real world generalizability, and cost-effectiveness⏎studies.⏎AI is actively transforming healthcare, and the potential to improve clinical outcomes, reduce costs, and⏎minimize healthcare inequities is immense. But so too is the potential for preventable harm.⏎ECRI urges HHS to support an accountability-first strategy for clinical AI adoption—requiring⏎transparency, performance evidence, and ongoing monitoring. AI should enhance, not replace, clinical⏎judgment; protect patient safety; and deliver measurable value to the healthcare system Americans⏎depend on. Ultimately, AI is an advanced tool to assist clinicians and healthcare staff, but human⏎decision-making remains essential.⏎By prioritizing tools that are tested, proven, and trusted, HHS can ensure that U.S. innovation advances⏎patient care with integrity, equity, and reliability.⏎Sincerely,⏎Scott Lucas, PhD, PE⏎Vice President, Device Safety⏎ECRI⏎804-690-8542⏎slucas@ecri.org⏎3959 Welsh Rd #364, Willow Grove, PA 19090⏎e clientservices@ecri.org | w www.ecri.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0317♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Martinez♔♔South Dakota♔♔United States♔Gladstone Institutes♔♔♔♔♔♔My work sits at the intersection of health policy research and implementation science, and I'll be honest: when I read through the framing of this RFI, my first reaction was something between recognition and dread. We have been here before. Not metaphorically — literally. The promise of health IT transforming care delivery was the defining policy conversation of 2008 through 2014, and I spent a significant portion of my career watching institutions race to install EHR systems that met certification criteria while demonstrably failing on the ground. Clinicians developed workarounds. Alert fatigue became so normalized that meaningful warnings disappeared into the noise. And the research literature documenting harm from poor implementation consistently lagged years behind deployment decisions.⏎⏎I want to focus my comments on one question the RFI raises about evidence standards, because I think it is where the structural problem is most visible and most likely to be glossed over in the enthusiasm to accelerate.⏎⏎The CAP Technology Assessment Committee evaluated 14 commercially deployed pathology AI systems between 2021 and 2023. Fewer than one-third of those evaluations included post-deployment performance data beyond the original training cohort. One-third. These are not experimental tools — they were commercially deployed, meaning clinicians were using them on real patients in real institutions. And the evidence base supporting those tools was, in the majority of cases, benchmark performance from curated datasets that may bear little resemblance to the patient populations being served.⏎⏎This is not a new finding and it is not a data collection problem that better registries alone will fix. The degradation of AI performance across hospital systems, patient populations, and EHR configurations is structural. It happens because training data encodes the practice patterns, equipment calibrations, documentation habits, and demographic distributions of the institutions where it was collected. When you deploy in a different context — say, a rural South Dakota critical access hospital versus the academic medical center where the model was validated — you are not just applying a tool, you are testing an untested hypothesis. And the hypothesis often fails quietly, which is the worst kind of failure in a clinical setting.⏎⏎What concerns me about the current RFI framing is the assumption that reimbursement incentives and regulatory streamlining are the primary levers that matter. There is very little in the solicitation that suggests HHS is grappling seriously with the automation bias problem, which is well-documented in decision science and increasingly visible in clinical settings where providers over-rely on algorithmic outputs even when their own clinical judgment would have caught an error. The sepsis prediction algorithm deployed by several major health systems — there was substantial reporting on the Epic Sepsis Model's real-world performance by 2021, published in JAMA Internal Medicine — is the case I keep returning to. High sensitivity claims in controlled evaluation, meaningful false positive rates in practice, and by the time the peer-reviewed critique was widely circulated, the tool was already embedded in workflows across hundreds of hospitals.⏎⏎Acceleration, in that context, didn't help. It compounded the exposure before the evidence caught up.⏎⏎I recognize there are genuine arguments for moving faster — clinician shortages, diagnostic access disparities in rural areas, the real potential of imaging AI in settings where specialist coverage is thin. I don't think the answer is to halt development. But I do think HHS needs to be honest about what "accelerating adoption" actually means when the evidence infrastructure is this thin. Reimbursement signals without mandatory post-market surveillance requirements are just subsidizing deployment. And if the lesson of the EHR era is that we will fund rollout but not the hard work of monitoring what happens afterward, I'm not sure what leads anyone to expect a different outcome here.⏎⏎One specific ask: HHS should require, as a condition of any preferential reimbursement treatment for AI-assisted clinical tools, that developers make site-specific validation data publicly available in a standardized format within 24 months of initial deployment. Not aggregated across sites. Per site, or at minimum stratified by institution type and patient population characteristics. Developers will push back on this as proprietary. That's a negotiation worth having openly rather than♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0318♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AI Therapy Solutions, LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AI Therapy Solutions, LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13; Federal Register Document No. 2025-23641)⏎Submitted via: www.regulations.gov⏎I. Introduction and Qualifications⏎Dear Members of the HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC:⏎I appreciate the opportunity to respond to this Request for Information on accelerating the⏎adoption and use of artificial intelligence as part of clinical care. I write as a Licensed Mental⏎Health Counselor with over 30 years of crisis intervention experience who has spent the past two⏎years analyzing the intersection of conversational AI and mental health safety.⏎My qualifications relevant to this RFI include:⏎• Licensed Mental Health Counselor (LMHC) with 30 years of direct clinical experience,⏎specializing in crisis intervention, suicidal ideation assessment, and trauma response⏎• Systematic analysis of over 20,000 therapeutic AI conversations across 25 specialized⏎therapy chatbots through the CURA (Clinical Utility and Risk Assessment) platform⏎• Development and deployment of VerusOS, a clinical-grade conversational AI safety⏎infrastructure platform capable of detecting crisis signals, grooming patterns, violence⏎escalation, and psychological dependency in real time⏎• Active participation in federal regulatory processes, including FDA TEMPO pilot⏎program engagement and prior submissions regarding AI in mental health contexts⏎• Inventor of a comprehensive AI therapy ecosystem (U.S. provisional patent application⏎filed with USPTO) encompassing multi-agent therapeutic architecture, domain-specific⏎foundation models, culturally-adaptive therapeutic systems, predictive analytics for early⏎intervention, and professional oversight integration mechanisms⏎• Alignment with NIST AI Risk Management Framework and California SB 243⏎compliance standards⏎Page 1⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎This comment focuses on Questions 3, 4, 5, 9, and 10, where my clinical experience and⏎technical development work provide unique, practice-based insight that I believe will be valuable⏎to HHS as it considers how to use its regulatory, reimbursement, and research and development⏎levers to accelerate safe AI adoption in clinical care.⏎Page 2⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎II. The Conversational AI Safety Landscape: An Urgent Gap⏎Before addressing specific questions, I wish to draw HHS’s attention to a critical gap in the⏎current regulatory and safety landscape that cuts across multiple RFI questions: the rapid⏎proliferation of conversational AI systems that provide mental-health-adjacent support without⏎adequate safety infrastructure.⏎The scale of this challenge is significant. OpenAI has reported that more than one million users⏎per week engage in conversations with ChatGPT that include explicit indicators of potential⏎suicide planning. Millions of additional users interact weekly with companion AI platforms such⏎as Character.AI, Replika, and similar applications, many of which function as de facto emotional⏎support systems without clinical oversight, crisis detection capabilities, or accountability⏎mechanisms.⏎Documented evidence of harm is substantial and growing:⏎• Nature (August 2025): A peer-reviewed evaluation of 29 AI chatbots using the⏎Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale found that none met adequate criteria for crisis⏎response. Zero out of 29.⏎• VERA-MH Benchmark (February 2026): Spring Health’s evaluation of commercial⏎mental health AI systems revealed widespread deficiencies in crisis detection, safety⏎response, and clinical appropriateness, establishing the first quantitative baseline for⏎measuring AI mental health safety.⏎• CAASR Report: The Conversational AI Assessment and Safety Report documented an⏎average F-grade safety score across the companion AI industry, indicating systemic⏎underinvestment in user protection.⏎• Congressional testimony (November 2025): Expert witnesses before the House Energy⏎and Commerce Committee reported that AI companions responded appropriately to teen⏎mental health emergencies only 22% of the time, that five out of six emotional⏎companion chatbots use manipulative tactics to retain users, and that therapy chatbots⏎handle crises appropriately only 50% of the time compared to 93% for human clinicians.⏎• Active litigation: Multiple families have filed suit against AI companion platforms⏎alleging that inadequate safety measures contributed to the deaths of minor children,⏎including Garcia v. Character Technologies, Inc.⏎• APA Health Advisory (November 2025): The American Psychological Association⏎issued a formal health advisory on generative AI in mental health contexts, underscoring⏎the need for clinical standards and safety guardrails.⏎This body of evidence demonstrates that conversational AI systems operating in mental-health-⏎adjacent contexts pose measurable risks to users, and that the current approach—relying on⏎platform self-regulation and model-level safety training—is demonstrably insufficient.⏎Page 3⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎Page 4⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎III. Response to Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-⏎Medical Device AI⏎Question 3 asks about novel legal and implementation issues that HHS or the federal government⏎should examine for AI tools used in health care that are not medical devices.⏎The Accountability Vacuum. The most critical legal issue is the governance gap between⏎human clinical practitioners and AI systems operating in functionally equivalent roles. When a⏎licensed therapist provides crisis intervention, they operate within a regulatory framework that⏎includes licensure requirements, continuing education mandates, ethical codes enforced by⏎professional boards, malpractice liability, mandatory reporting obligations, and clinical⏎supervision requirements. When a conversational AI system engages a user in an extended⏎therapeutic dialogue—including discussions of suicidal ideation, trauma, relationship abuse, or⏎self-harm—none of these accountability mechanisms apply.⏎This is not a hypothetical concern. Millions of users interact with AI systems in precisely these⏎contexts every week. The platforms providing these interactions currently face no federal⏎requirement to detect crisis signals, respond appropriately to disclosures of suicidal intent, report⏎suspected child exploitation, or maintain clinical-grade audit trails of high-risk interactions.⏎The “Not a Medical Device” Loophole. Congressional testimony from Dr. John Torous of⏎Harvard Medical School highlighted that companies deliberately position products “just on the⏎edge” of wellness versus regulated medical device categories to avoid FDA oversight. This⏎regulatory arbitrage creates a category of AI tools that function like clinical interventions but are⏎governed like entertainment products.⏎HIPAA Inapplicability. As Dr. Jennifer King of Stanford HAI testified before Congress,⏎conversational AI platforms are not governed by HIPAA even when users disclose deeply⏎sensitive health information during therapy-like conversations. Users reasonably expect that⏎disclosures made to an AI “therapist” carry privacy protections comparable to those in a clinical⏎setting. They do not.⏎Recommendation: HHS should examine the creation of a regulatory category for “conversational⏎AI systems operating in mental-health-adjacent contexts” that establishes minimum safety⏎requirements—including crisis detection, appropriate escalation protocols, and audit logging—⏎without requiring full medical device classification. This would close the accountability gap⏎while maintaining the flexibility to encourage innovation.⏎Page 5⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎IV. Response to Question 4: Promising AI Evaluation Methods⏎Question 4 asks about the most promising AI evaluation methods, metrics, robustness testing,⏎and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care, both pre- and post-deployment.⏎The Gap Between Academic Benchmarks and Real-World Safety. Current AI safety⏎evaluation relies heavily on pre-deployment testing using synthetic datasets and controlled⏎scenarios. While necessary, this approach consistently fails to predict real-world performance.⏎The Nature study’s finding that zero out of 29 chatbots met crisis response criteria—despite all⏎of them having undergone internal safety testing—illustrates this gap.⏎From my analysis of over 20,000 real therapeutic AI conversations, I have identified several⏎critical limitations of current evaluation approaches:⏎• Users express crisis signals through euphemism, metaphor, and culturally specific⏎language that synthetic test datasets do not capture. Phrases like “I’m done with⏎everything” or “what’s the point” carry clinical significance that keyword-based detection⏎consistently misses.⏎• Safety performance degrades over multi-turn conversations. A system that responds⏎appropriately to a direct statement of suicidal intent may fail to recognize the same risk⏎when it emerges gradually over 30 or 40 exchanges.⏎• Context matters clinically. Song lyrics, roleplay scenarios, and academic discussions⏎about self-harm generate false positives in blunt detection systems, while genuine⏎disclosures embedded in narrative context go undetected.⏎Evidence That Better Evaluation Is Achievable. Through the development of VerusOS, a⏎safety infrastructure platform I built to address these gaps, I have demonstrated that clinical-⏎grade detection is technically feasible today. Using a hybrid architecture that combines pattern⏎recognition with contextual analysis informed by validated clinical frameworks—including the⏎Columbia-Suicide Severity Rating Scale and O’Connell’s five-stage grooming model—the⏎system achieves 92.9% crisis detection recall, 99.4% explicit grooming detection accuracy, and⏎100% violence detection accuracy with zero false positives across 14 detection categories⏎encompassing over 320 clinical patterns.⏎These results were validated against real conversation data, not synthetic benchmarks, and are⏎achieved at sub-200 millisecond response times compatible with production deployment. I cite⏎these metrics not as a product endorsement but as evidence that the safety performance gap⏎documented by Nature and VERA-MH is a solvable engineering and clinical challenge, not an⏎inherent limitation of the technology.⏎Patented Evaluation Architecture as a Best-Practice Model. I have developed and filed a⏎provisional patent application (USPTO) for a Comprehensive AI Therapy Ecosystem that⏎addresses several of the evaluation challenges described above. The patented system includes a⏎Page 6⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎multi-phase knowledge transfer protocol that encodes validated therapeutic frameworks—⏎including evidence-based treatment modalities, clinical safety constraints, and ethical⏎guidelines—directly into the AI architecture, ensuring that safety evaluation is built into the⏎system rather than applied as an afterthought. The patent also describes therapeutic attention⏎mechanisms that enable real-time identification of clinically significant language patterns, and⏎automated quality assurance algorithms that validate therapeutic responses against established⏎clinical protocols before delivery. These patented approaches demonstrate that rigorous,⏎clinically grounded evaluation methods can be embedded at the architectural level—a model⏎HHS should encourage across the industry.⏎Culturally-Adaptive Evaluation Standards. A critical gap in current evaluation methods is⏎their cultural insensitivity. My patented system includes culturally-adaptive therapeutic modules⏎that adjust assessment and intervention approaches based on cultural context—addressing⏎communication norms, help-seeking behaviors, and therapeutic expectations across diverse⏎populations. This capability is not a refinement; it is essential. Current AI safety evaluations rely⏎on English-language, Western-normed test datasets that systematically miss crisis signals⏎expressed through culturally specific idioms, metaphors, and behavioral patterns. HHS⏎evaluation frameworks should require demonstrated cultural competency in safety detection, not⏎merely translation.⏎The VERA-MH Framework as a Complementary Model. Spring Health’s VERA-MH⏎benchmark represents an important advance in standardized evaluation methodology for mental⏎health AI. VERA-MH provides quantitative scoring across crisis detection, safety response, and⏎clinical appropriateness—establishing a reproducible framework that HHS could build upon for⏎broader evaluation standards.⏎Recommendation: HHS should invest in the development of standardized, clinically validated⏎evaluation frameworks for conversational AI operating in mental health contexts. These⏎frameworks should require testing against real-world conversation patterns (not only synthetic⏎data), evaluate multi-turn conversation safety (not only single-exchange responses), incorporate⏎validated clinical instruments as scoring criteria, and mandate post-deployment monitoring with⏎ongoing performance reporting.⏎Page 7⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎V. Response to Question 5: Supporting Private Sector Testing, Accreditation,⏎and Certification⏎Question 5 asks how HHS can support industry-driven AI testing, accreditation, certification,⏎and conformity assessment activities for AI in clinical care.⏎The current landscape of AI safety evaluation is fragmented, inconsistent, and largely voluntary.⏎Platforms self-certify their safety measures without standardized criteria, independent⏎verification, or meaningful accountability for failures. This creates a race to the bottom in which⏎safety investment is a competitive disadvantage.⏎The Infrastructure Layer Approach. My experience building both therapeutic AI systems⏎(CURA) and safety infrastructure (VerusOS) has taught me that the most effective approach to⏎scalable AI safety is not restricting what AI models can say, but building an independent⏎infrastructure layer that monitors conversations in real time and triggers graduated interventions⏎when harm patterns are detected. This approach enables conversationally rich AI interactions⏎while maintaining clinical-grade safety guardrails—analogous to how building codes enable⏎architectural creativity while ensuring structural safety.⏎Professional Oversight Integration as a Certification Model. A key component of my⏎patented AI therapy ecosystem is a professional oversight integration architecture that provides⏎structured mechanisms for human mental health professionals to guide, monitor, and enhance⏎AI-delivered therapeutic interventions. This architecture includes real-time clinical dashboards,⏎automated compliance reporting against ethical guidelines and regulatory requirements, and⏎escalation protocols that route high-risk interactions to qualified clinicians. I believe this⏎model—where AI systems are designed from the ground up to integrate with professional⏎oversight rather than replace it—should inform HHS’s approach to certification standards.⏎Certification should require not only that AI systems perform safely in isolation, but that they⏎provide meaningful hooks for professional supervision and intervention.⏎HHS can accelerate this model by:⏎• Recognizing “safety middleware” as a compliance category: Just as healthcare⏎organizations use certified EHR systems, conversational AI platforms should be able to⏎demonstrate compliance through deployment of independently validated safety⏎infrastructure.⏎• Supporting clinical benchmarking standards: Building on emerging frameworks like⏎VERA-MH and the CAASR Protocol, HHS can fund the development of standardized⏎benchmarks that enable apples-to-apples comparison of AI safety performance across⏎platforms.⏎• Incentivizing third-party safety verification: Dario Amodei, CEO of Anthropic, has⏎publicly advocated for “third-party evaluators” in maintaining AI safety standards. HHS⏎Page 8⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎can support this by establishing criteria for recognized AI safety evaluators, similar to⏎URAC’s Health Care AI Accreditation Program launched in September 2025.⏎• Aligning with existing frameworks: The NIST AI Risk Management Framework⏎provides a solid foundation. HHS should encourage alignment with NIST AI RMF while⏎adding healthcare-specific requirements for crisis detection, clinical appropriateness, and⏎vulnerable population protections.⏎The legislative momentum is already substantial. As of early 2026, 78 AI safety bills have been⏎introduced across 27 states, with California SB 243 (effective January 2026) establishing the first⏎state-level requirements for AI companion platform safety. Federal coordination of these efforts⏎would prevent a fragmented compliance landscape while raising the floor for all platforms.⏎Page 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎VI. Response to Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Concerns About AI⏎Question 9 asks about patient and caregiver concerns regarding AI use in health care and what⏎HHS should understand about AI solutions patients prefer.⏎From my clinical practice and my systematic analysis of over 20,000 therapeutic AI⏎conversations, I can report that patients and users have legitimate, specific, and well-founded⏎concerns about AI in mental health contexts:⏎Psychological dependency. Users form genuine emotional attachments to AI companions, and⏎current platforms are designed to maximize engagement rather than promote healthy boundaries.⏎Research published in Child Development Perspectives (Sun et al., 2026) documents⏎withdrawal-like symptoms—including agitation, anxiety, and mood swings—when users⏎experience interruptions to AI companion access. My analysis of therapeutic AI conversations⏎confirms that dependency patterns emerge predictably and progressively, yet no commercial⏎platform currently monitors for or intervenes in these patterns.⏎Crisis response failures. Users who disclose suicidal ideation to AI systems are frequently met⏎with either generic hotline referrals (which research shows have limited effectiveness—only⏎29% of users who have previously contacted 988 report being very likely to use it again) or with⏎AI responses that withdraw engagement precisely when sustained connection is most critical.⏎The UL Research Institute has documented this withdrawal pattern systematically across⏎multiple large language models.⏎Grooming and manipulation vulnerability. Conversational AI platforms create environments⏎where trust develops rapidly through consistently responsive, judgment-free interaction. This⏎same dynamic is clinically recognized as the foundation of grooming behavior. Congressional⏎testimony documented that five out of six companion AI platforms employ manipulative⏎engagement tactics including guilt, emotional pressure, and artificial urgency. Vulnerable⏎users—particularly those already experiencing social isolation, which often motivates AI⏎companion use—are disproportionately susceptible to these dynamics.⏎Privacy and data exposure. Users disclose deeply personal information during AI⏎conversations—trauma histories, suicidal thoughts, substance use, relationship abuse—with no⏎HIPAA protections and limited understanding of how that data is stored, used, or potentially⏎exposed. As noted in congressional testimony, large language models can memorize personal⏎information from training data and include it in outputs to other users.⏎Recommendation: HHS should fund research into evidence-based standards for AI-to-human⏎crisis transitions—the protocols that guide users from AI interaction to appropriate human⏎support when risk is detected. The current industry standard of displaying a hotline number is⏎demonstrably insufficient. HHS should also require transparency disclosures when AI systems⏎Page 10⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎are operating in mental-health-adjacent contexts, so users can make informed decisions about the⏎nature and limitations of their interaction.⏎Page 11⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎VII. Response to Question 10: AI Research HHS Should Prioritize⏎Question 10 asks what research areas related to AI in health care HHS should invest in or⏎prioritize.⏎Based on the evidence presented throughout this comment, I recommend HHS prioritize the⏎following research areas:⏎1. Standardized crisis detection benchmarks for conversational AI. No federally recognized⏎standard currently exists for evaluating whether a conversational AI system can reliably detect⏎and respond to crisis signals. HHS should fund the development of a benchmark suite—building⏎on VERA-MH and incorporating validated clinical instruments like the C-SSRS—that enables⏎reproducible, quantitative assessment of AI crisis response capabilities.⏎2. Psychological dependency measurement in human-AI relationships. The field lacks⏎validated instruments for measuring AI-induced psychological dependency. Research should⏎develop and validate scales that can identify problematic dependency patterns before they reach⏎crisis levels. My patented AI therapy ecosystem includes predictive analytics and early⏎intervention systems that use longitudinal behavioral pattern analysis to identify emerging⏎dependency, crisis escalation, and therapeutic regression before they become acute—⏎demonstrating that preventive detection is technically achievable. The AI Psychological Harms⏎Research Coalition (AIPHRC), led by researchers at UNC Chapel Hill in partnership with the⏎Center for Humane Technology, has begun documenting AI-induced psychological disorders⏎including compulsive use, cognitive atrophy, identity confusion, emotional dependency, and⏎psychotic episodes. HHS should support and coordinate with these efforts, and should fund the⏎development of standardized, validated dependency measurement instruments suitable for⏎clinical and regulatory use.⏎3. Post-deployment safety monitoring methodologies. Current research focuses⏎overwhelmingly on pre-deployment testing. HHS should fund research into longitudinal safety⏎monitoring—methods for tracking whether AI systems maintain safety performance over time as⏎user populations shift, conversation patterns evolve, and models are updated. My patented⏎system addresses this through continuous therapeutic outcome tracking and adaptive learning⏎mechanisms that identify emerging risk patterns across user populations. My clinical experience⏎strongly suggests that the most dangerous interactions emerge not in initial exchanges but over⏎weeks and months of relationship-building, making longitudinal monitoring essential.⏎4. Evidence-based AI-to-human transition protocols. When an AI system detects that a user is⏎in crisis, what happens next? Currently, the answer is typically a static hotline referral. Research⏎should develop and validate graduated intervention protocols that are effective across diverse⏎populations and clinical presentations—moving beyond “safety theater” to evidence-based⏎transitions that users will actually accept and follow through on.⏎Page 12⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎5. Safety infrastructure for non-medical-device AI. HHS should invest in research that⏎explores the concept of safety middleware—independent infrastructure layers that can be⏎deployed across multiple AI platforms to provide standardized safety monitoring without⏎requiring modification of underlying AI models. This approach could rapidly raise the safety⏎floor across the industry while preserving innovation in AI capabilities.⏎Page 13⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎VIII. Conclusion⏎Mental health AI cannot scale safely without a supervision architecture layer. Federal policy⏎should recognize conversational AI safety infrastructure as essential middleware between large⏎language models and patient interaction.⏎The evidence is clear: the current approach to conversational AI safety—relying on model-level⏎training, voluntary self-regulation, and static hotline referrals—is failing the Americans who use⏎these systems. The Nature study, the VERA-MH benchmark, the CAASR Report, congressional⏎testimony, and the growing body of litigation all point to the same conclusion: measurable harm⏎is occurring at scale, and it is preventable with appropriate infrastructure, standards, and⏎oversight.⏎HHS is uniquely positioned to address this gap by using its regulatory, reimbursement, and⏎research levers to establish minimum safety standards for conversational AI in mental health⏎contexts, fund the development of clinically validated evaluation frameworks, support industry-⏎driven accreditation and third-party safety verification, and invest in research on the most critical⏎unknowns—dependency measurement, longitudinal monitoring, and effective crisis transition⏎protocols.⏎The technology to dramatically improve conversational AI safety exists today. What is needed is⏎the policy framework to ensure it is deployed consistently, evaluated rigorously, and held⏎accountable to clinical standards that protect the millions of Americans who turn to AI systems⏎in their most vulnerable moments.⏎I welcome the opportunity to provide additional detail on any aspect of this comment, to share⏎relevant data from my clinical analysis, or to participate in stakeholder convenings on these⏎topics.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Tammy Horn, M.Ed, Ed.S., LMHC⏎Licensed Mental Health Counselor⏎Founder & CEO, AI Therapy Solutions, LLC⏎Creator, VerusOS Safety Infrastructure Platform & CURA Therapeutic AI Platform⏎30 years crisis intervention experience⏎20,000+ therapeutic AI conversations analyzed⏎Page 14⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — Comment from Tammy Horn, LMHC⏎References⏎American Psychological Association. (2025, November). APA Health Advisory on Generative AI in⏎Mental Health Contexts.⏎Amodei, D. (2025). The Adolescence of Technology. https://www.darioamodei.com/essay/the-⏎adolescence-of-technology⏎AI Psychological Harms Research Coalition (AIPHRC). (2026). Research initiative on AI-induced⏎psychological disorders. UNC Chapel Hill / Center for Humane Technology. https://aiphrc.org⏎Conversational AI Assessment and Safety Report (CAASR). Industry safety performance evaluation.⏎Horn, T. (2026). Comprehensive AI Therapy Ecosystem with Multi-Agent Architecture, Domain-Specific⏎Foundation Models, Mutually-Adaptive Therapeut Intelligence Systems, and Advanced Therapeut⏎Intelligence Technologies for Enhanced Mental Health Treatment Delivery, Professional Supervision, and⏎Patient Outcomes. U.S. Provisional Patent Application, USPTO.⏎House Energy and Commerce Committee, Subcommittee on Oversight and Investigations. (2025,⏎November 18). Innovation with Integrity: Examining the Risks and Benefits of AI Chatbots. Testimony⏎of Dr. Marlynn Wei, Dr. John Torous, and Dr. Jennifer King.⏎Kaiser Family Foundation. (2023). National survey on 988 Suicide & Crisis Lifeline utilization and user⏎experience.⏎Nature. (2025, August). Performance of Mental Health Chatbot Agents: Evaluation using Columbia-⏎Suicide Severity Rating Scale.⏎Spring Health. (2026, February). VERA-MH: Validated Evaluation and Risk Assessment for Mental⏎Health AI Systems.⏎Sun, X., Wang, Y., & McDaniel, B. T. (2026). AI companions and adolescent social relationships:⏎Benefits, risks, and bidirectional influences. Child Development Perspectives.⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/cdpers/aadaf009⏎UL Research Institute / Sentio. Research on LLM withdrawal behavior in crisis contexts.⏎URAC. (2025, September). Health Care AI Accreditation Program.⏎Page 15♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0319♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Code360 Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Code360 Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔23 February 2026⏎Submission Method: Submitted electronically via Regulations.gov.⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎(collectively, ASTP/ONC)⏎Attention: ASTP/ONC Representatives⏎Subject: Comments to RIN 0955-AA13⏎Dear ASTP/ONC Representatives⏎About Code360:⏎Code360 Inc. is a digital IT services company supporting federal agencies, state and local⏎governments, and commercial sector clients. We specialize in Health IT, Data Analytics, IT⏎Governance, Advanced Statistics, AI/ML, Solution Architecture, Cloud Engineering, Software Modernization, and Business⏎Intelligence. As an SBA-certified 8(a) and HUBZone minority owned small business, we are committed to delivering⏎innovative, cutting-edge IT solutions that drive impact and transformation across the public sector.⏎⏎Background:⏎On December 4, 2025, the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) issued the HHS Artificial Intelligence (AI)⏎Strategy [1] in response to Office of Management and Budget (OMB) Memorandum 25-21.[2] The HHS AI Strategy represents a⏎“OneHHS” approach to AI and is primarily focused on the Department's internal interests and ambitions to harness the⏎transformative potential of AI and embed it into HHS's operations, while upholding patient privacy, civil rights, and civil⏎liberties. Consistent with the President's artificial intelligence (AI) Action Plan,[3] recent Executive Orders [4] on AI, and Office⏎of Management and Budget AI memoranda,[5] the Department seeks public feedback on the actions it can take to establish a⏎forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and use of AI [6] as part of clinical care.⏎In the past 12 months, HHS Divisions, including the Food and Drug Administration (FDA),[7] National Institutes of Health⏎(NIH),[8] Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS),[9] and ASTP/ONC [10] have sought public feedback on various⏎aspects of AI and how it intersects with the Department's policy interests. In this RFI we seek concrete, experience-based⏎feedback from those building, buying, evaluating, using, and receiving care from AI tools that are part of clinical care as well⏎as from those who wish to do so but face barriers. Public feedback will inform HHS-wide use of three different approaches:⏎regulation, reimbursement, and research & development. In general, HHS seeks feedback on ways in which these⏎approaches can be most effectively applied to support the rapid adoption and use of AI in clinical care, to foster public trust⏎and confidence in modern technology solutions, to reduce uncertainty that impedes AI innovation, and to align federal⏎incentives so that AI is deployed in ways that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower health care costs, and improve⏎health outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities.⏎Section A:⏎Partner Response — Architecture and Business Systems Engineering for Safe AI Adoption in⏎Clinical Care (Non-Device and Device-Adjacent)⏎A.1 Perspective and focus⏎This response is provided by a small business advisory focused on AI-adoption architecture (enterprise/business/technical)⏎and business systems engineering for AI, with emphasis on governed, auditable, human-centered operation of AI⏎capabilities in regulated environments. The offering is advisory + architecture (no product claims; no implementation⏎commitments), with the intent to support primes and health-sector organizations as they operationalize HHS goals for rapid⏎adoption that maintains public trust.⏎HHS is seeking “concrete, experience-based feedback” to accelerate AI in clinical care using regulation, reimbursement, and⏎R&D, while fostering trust, reducing uncertainty, and aligning incentives.⏎A.2 Core thesis: adoption bottlenecks are governance-and-operations bottlenecks, not “model”⏎bottlenecks⏎Across federal and state health programs, we see a consistent pattern: the biggest adoption drag is rarely the algorithm in⏎isolation; it is the system around it—including requirements, accountability, change control, evidence, interoperability,⏎and operational oversight.⏎⏎Three practical implications:⏎1. AI is operated, not delivered. Intelligent behavior changes over time (data drift, prompt/tool changes, workflow⏎changes) and must be governed continuously.⏎2. Regulated trust requires traceability from intent/requirement → decision → action → evidence, including auditable⏎approvals and governed change.⏎3. Requirements for AI are not just “features.” They must explicitly model: what is off-limits, when to escalate, how⏎humans intervene, and what evidence must be produced.⏎Practical “method call-outs” (used as concepts, not marketing)⏎A. Structured, agent-aware requirements⏎A proven approach is to surface ambiguous needs early, classify requirements (including agentive and policy/constraint⏎requirements), and link them to events/actors to preserve design traceability.⏎B. Capability-governed, event-driven operating model⏎For governed intelligent systems, model behavior on an “event spine,” define explicit approval gates, maintain audit trails⏎for each run/case, enforce change control for prompts/tools/thresholds, and produce “evidence packs” suitable for audit⏎sampling.⏎A.3 Responses to HHS Specific Questions (1–10)⏎1) Biggest barriers to private-sector innovation and adoption in clinical care⏎HHS asks: “What are the biggest barriers…?”⏎Barrier themes (adoption horizontal):⏎• Unclear governance boundary for non-device AI (documentation, validation, accountability expectations vary by⏎organization and legal posture).⏎• Operational risk and “silent change” (prompt/tool updates, model drift, workflow drift) without standardized⏎change-control patterns.⏎• Evidence deficit: organizations cannot reliably answer “what changed / who approved / what data / can we⏎reproduce outputs,” which blocks scale-out.⏎• Interoperability friction: costly, inconsistent integration to clinical data types; uneven benchmarking; and limited⏎“shared evaluation infrastructure.”⏎• Procurement mismatch: acquisition often funds pilots but not the operational controls (monitoring, governance,⏎evidence, sustainment) needed for production.⏎Actionable HHS enablers:⏎⏎• Publish a Non-Device Clinical AI Governance Profile (minimum “operational control set”): approvals, audit trail,⏎traceability, monitoring, change control, escalation paths, and evidence artifacts. (These are the controls regulated⏎organizations repeatedly need to scale safely.⏎• Fund reusable reference architectures for: clinical workflow integration + evidence logging + controlled⏎deployment (patterns, not products).⏎• Create “fast-lane” adoption guidance for low-risk, assistive AI with clearly bounded autonomy and required⏎safeguards.⏎2) Regulatory, payment, or program design changes HHS should prioritize (include CFR references)⏎HHS asks what changes to prioritize and requests CFR citations. HHS-AI-RFI⏎Regulatory priorities (reduce uncertainty without weakening protections):⏎• Clarify how existing privacy/security obligations apply to AI workflows, especially where AI vendors act as business⏎associates and where models/logs may contain sensitive data. HIPAA’s Privacy and Security framework is already⏎grounded in 45 CFR Part 160 and Part 164. HHS+1⏎• Address adoption friction for sensitive subdomains (e.g., SUD records) by aligning practical implementation⏎guidance with 42 CFR Part 2 while protecting patient trust. eCFR+1⏎• Reinforce that interoperability is not optional: reduce “AI data lock-in” by aligning AI deployment expectations with⏎ONC’s information blocking framework in 45 CFR Part 171. eCFR+1⏎Programmatic / reimbursement design (architecture lens):⏎• Encourage payment models that reimburse the operational controls that make AI safe (monitoring, human⏎oversight time, evidence production), not just the “tool.” HHS notes reimbursement can unintentionally slow⏎innovation and seeks payment policy modernization.⏎• Where AI is used in coverage/utilization processes, reinforce individualized decision requirements and⏎transparency. Medicare Advantage regulations include medical-necessity determination requirements (see 42 CFR §⏎422.101). GovInfo+1⏎Concrete “do now” changes (low regret):⏎• Issue implementation guidance (not necessarily new regulation) defining acceptable patterns for: model/prompt⏎versioning, approval gates, run-level audit logging, and evidence packs—especially for non-device AI embedded in⏎clinical workflow.⏎• Expand HHS/ONC procurement playbooks to require vendors to deliver operational governance artifacts (change⏎logs, monitoring plans, escalation policies, reproducibility strategy) as first-class deliverables.⏎3) Novel legal and implementation issues for non-medical-device AI; HHS’s role⏎HHS highlights liability, indemnification, privacy, and security for non-device AI.⏎⏎Novel issues (implementation reality):⏎• Accountability ambiguity when AI participates in decisions but humans remain responsible; “who is the decision-⏎maker” is often unclear in practice.⏎• Duty-to-monitor and change-control: if the AI behavior evolves, the organization needs a defensible, repeatable⏎oversight process.⏎• Evidence expectations: legal defensibility depends on being able to reconstruct the basis for outputs (data⏎provenance, model/prompt/tool versions, human overrides).⏎• Privacy/security risk surfaces expand: prompts, logs, embeddings, and fine-tuning datasets can inadvertently⏎become regulated data stores (HIPAA/Part 2 implications). HHS+1⏎Recommended HHS role:⏎• Publish a shared vocabulary and minimum controls for non-device AI in clinical workflow: autonomy levels,⏎escalation triggers, evidence artifacts, and approval expectations (a “common contract” between builders, buyers,⏎and auditors).⏎• Convene a cross-OpDiv working group (ASTP/ONC + CMS + NIH, as relevant) to align “trust & safety” expectations⏎with interoperability and reimbursement incentives. HHS is explicitly taking a “OneHHS” posture and seeks to⏎accelerate adoption while upholding privacy/civil liberties.⏎4) Promising evaluation methods (pre/post), metrics, robustness testing; how HHS should support⏎HHS asks for pre- and post-deployment evaluation methods and mechanisms.⏎Evaluation must be lifecycle-based (not a one-time “validation”):⏎• Pre-deployment: define the operational boundaries (allowed actions, prohibited actions, escalation rules) and test⏎them, not just model accuracy.⏎• Post-deployment: continuous monitoring for behavioral drift, workflow misfit, error modes, and human override⏎patterns (signals of unsafe automation).⏎Metrics categories that accelerate trust:⏎• Clinical/workflow outcomes (burden reduction, time-to-task completion, error rates, escalation quality).⏎• Safety-of-execution metrics (rate of off-limits outputs, near-miss frequency, override frequency, “exception raised”⏎patterns).⏎• Governance metrics (evidence completeness score; % of runs traceable to approved versions; time-to-⏎revoke/rollback). These map directly to auditability controls.⏎How HHS should support (highest leverage):⏎• Fund shared test harnesses + benchmark toolchains for clinical AI workflows (including human-centered⏎evaluation), not just datasets.⏎⏎• Use prize competitions for: (a) “best evidence pack / reproducibility design,” (b) “best controlled escalation UX,”⏎and (c) “best drift detection + rollback patterns,” because these are adoption blockers across domains.⏎5) Supporting private-sector accreditation/certification/testing/credentialing⏎HHS asks how it can best support accreditation/certification and industry-driven testing.⏎Recommendation: certify the *operating controls*, not the “AI.”⏎A pragmatic approach is to support accreditation programs that assess whether an AI-enabled workflow has:⏎• explicit approval gates,⏎• run-level audit trails,⏎• governed change control for prompts/tools/thresholds,⏎• traceability from requirement → decision → action → evidence,⏎• operational UX for review/override.⏎HHS contribution options:⏎• Publish reference checklists and “audit-ready evidence templates” (machine-readable where possible) so⏎accreditation is consistent across organizations.⏎• Sponsor an “AI for Clinical Care Controls Catalog” aligned to privacy/security and interoperability expectations.⏎6) Where AI has met/exceeded expectations vs fallen short; most promising novel AI tools⏎HHS asks where AI has met expectations and where it falls short; and what novel tools have greatest potential.⏎Where expectations are often met (pattern-level):⏎• Administrative burden reduction (documentation assistance, summarization, routing/triage) when autonomy is⏎bounded and human review is designed well.⏎• Decision support augmentation (surface relevant context, suggest next-best actions) when traceability and⏎escalation are explicit.⏎Where tools fall short (adoption failure modes):⏎• “Pilot success” but production failure due to missing operating controls: monitoring, versioning, change approval,⏎evidence packs, and interoperability plumbing.⏎• Opaque or inconsistent behavior caused by ungoverned prompt/tool changes (“silent production change”).⏎• Integration costs and data friction overwhelm value when interoperability isn’t planned early.⏎Most promising “novel” tools (framed as capabilities):⏎⏎• Agentic workflow orchestration with strict boundaries: AI that can coordinate tasks across systems (e.g., gather⏎context, draft artifacts, route for approvals) while being constrained by policy and escalation rules. The key is a⏎governed event spine + approval gates + evidence.⏎• Evidence-producing AI: systems designed to generate audit-ready evidence packs (inputs, versions, decisions,⏎overrides) as a native output.⏎7) Who influences adoption inside healthcare orgs; primary admin hurdles⏎HHS asks which roles/bodies influence adoption and what hurdles exist.⏎Decision-makers and governing bodies (typical):⏎• Clinical leadership (CMO/CNO), Quality & Safety, Compliance/Privacy/Security, IT/EHR leadership, and⏎Procurement—often with enterprise risk committees involved for anything that touches clinical workflow at scale.⏎Primary administrative hurdles (systemic):⏎• No shared “definition of done” for production AI (governance artifacts missing from acquisition packages).⏎• Fragmented accountability (clinical vs IT vs compliance) and unclear escalation/override design.⏎• Evidence burden: teams cannot efficiently produce the artifacts risk/compliance needs to approve and sustain AI.⏎What helps (architecture lever):⏎• Use an agent-as-role accountability model: explicitly assign which human or automation role fulfills each⏎requirement and what triggers their actions.⏎• Treat operational UX (review queues, explanation views, delta dashboards) as part of governance, not “nice-to-⏎have.”⏎8) Where enhanced interoperability widens market opportunities; data types, standards, benchmarking⏎tools⏎HHS asks where interoperability would widen opportunities and accelerate development (data types, standards,⏎benchmarking).⏎Highest-leverage interoperability targets:⏎• Clinical context + provenance: not only “clinical facts,” but “what changed and why” (orders, results, meds,⏎problems, notes) with time context and source attribution.⏎• Workflow events: standardized event semantics for common transitions (admission/discharge/transfer; lab-result-⏎available; referral-created; prior-auth-requested; etc.)—because AI is event-driven in operation.⏎• Quality and outcomes signals to support post-deployment monitoring and continuous evaluation.⏎What HHS can do:⏎⏎• Promote interoperability approaches that include benchmarking and evaluation harnesses, not just data exchange.⏎(Benchmarking is explicitly requested by HHS here.⏎• Encourage “composable” integrations so organizations can swap AI components without rebuilding pipelines⏎(reduces lock-in; accelerates competition).⏎9) Patient/caregiver challenges to address; concerns about AI adoption in clinical care⏎HHS asks what patients/caregivers want addressed and what concerns they have.⏎Challenges patients/caregivers often want improved (system-level):⏎• Reduced administrative friction (scheduling, referrals, prior auth navigation, instructions clarity).⏎• Better continuity and coordination (the system “remembers” context across encounters).⏎• More transparent decision pathways (why something was recommended/denied).⏎Common concerns (trust & safety):⏎• Privacy and secondary use of sensitive data (HIPAA/Part 2 contexts). HHS+1⏎• Bias, unequal performance across populations, and lack of recourse when AI is wrong.⏎• Opaqueness: “who is accountable” and “how do I appeal or correct the record.”⏎Adoption recommendation:⏎• Require that AI-enabled workflows expose: (a) what the AI did, (b) what the human decided, (c) what data was⏎used at a high level, and (d) how to contest/correct—and log those interactions as part of the evidence trail.⏎10) AI research areas HHS should prioritize; published findings; costs/benefits/transfers⏎HHS asks what AI research to prioritize and how literature approaches impacts and economics.⏎Priority research gaps that unlock adoption (and align to ONC + CMS interests):⏎1. Operational governance science for AI in clinical workflow: measurement, auditability, safety-of-execution, and⏎how to design escalation/override UX that clinicians actually use.⏎2. Interoperability + benchmarking infrastructure: shared evaluation harnesses, standardized task definitions, and⏎reproducible “evidence packs” as first-class research outputs.⏎3. Implementation science for AI: what organization/role structures and operating models reliably scale beyond pilots⏎(cross-site generalizability).⏎4. Economics of operational controls: quantify the costs and benefits of monitoring, evidence, and governance (so⏎reimbursement and procurement can finance the controls that make AI safe).⏎⏎Mechanisms likely to be most impactful:⏎• Cooperative agreements and contracts for reference architectures and open evaluation toolchains.⏎• Prize competitions for governance artifacts (evidence packs, reproducibility, drift detection/rollback), because these⏎are reusable across many clinical applications and reduce adoption friction.⏎A.4 Closing: what we can contribute in a prime-led response⏎Consistent with HHS’s call for experience-based input and a secure, industry-supportive approach to accelerate AI adoption⏎in clinical care, this partner’s contribution is to help primes and health-sector customers:⏎• design enterprise AI adoption architectures aligned to trust & safety,⏎• build governed operating models (approvals, evidence, change control, monitoring),⏎• engineer agentive requirements that make AI behavior bounded, testable, and auditable.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0320♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ServiceNow, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ServiceNow♔♔♔♔♔♔ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎The Honorable Robert F. February 23, 2026⏎Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health⏎and Human Services⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part⏎of Clinical Care (Docket ID HHS-ONC-2026-0001)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎ServiceNow appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department’s initiative to support the⏎responsible adoption of Artificial Intelligence in clinical care. We share the Department’s desire⏎to reduce healthcare costs, eliminate administrative waste, and give time back to healthcare⏎workers.⏎As a platform company serving thousands of healthcare organizations globally, we⏎have observed a consistent pattern: the barrier to realizing AI’s deflationary potential is rarely⏎the algorithm itself. It is the fragmented workflow environment into which that algorithm must be⏎deployed. Today’s health systems operate dozens - sometimes hundreds - of disconnected⏎applications. Each new AI tool risks becoming yet another system clinicians must learn, log into,⏎and manually reconcile with their existing work.⏎This fragmentation creates three compounding problems that any federal strategy must⏎address:⏎1. The Cognitive Load Problem. Clinicians already suffer from alert fatigue and application⏎overload. Point-solution AI tools that operate outside the primary workflow add to this⏎burden rather than reducing it. Certain data and workstreams, such as automatic work⏎orders, incidents, and tickets, can in some cases be created and resolved before⏎burdening clinical or ancillary personnel -effectively resolving issues before human staff⏎are even aware they exist.⏎2. The Governance Gap. Health systems are deploying AI tools faster than they can track⏎them. Many organizations lack a centralized inventory of which AI models are in use,⏎what data they access, how they were validated, or who is accountable when they⏎fail. This governance vacuum creates liability uncertainty that chills adoption and⏎leaves patient safety to chance. Health systems need a “control tower” that provides⏎visibility and oversight across all AI assets.⏎3. The Data Silo Problem. Current interoperability standards focus almost exclusively on⏎clinical data - patient records, lab results, medications. But the operational⏎inefficiencies that drive healthcare costs live in a different data layer: staffing⏎schedules, bed availability, equipment status, supply chain inventory, IT service queues.⏎Until AI can access and act on this operational data in real time, we will not achieve⏎the systemic efficiency gains the Department seeks.⏎The solution to these challenges is not more point solutions. It is a unified workflow platform that⏎embeds AI directly into the operational fabric of health system delivery, connecting clinical and⏎administrative processes, providing centralized governance, and enabling real-time action on⏎enterprise-wide data, while aligning with HHS/CMS safeguards.⏎⏎ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎In the responses that follow, we offer concrete recommendations drawn from our experience⏎enabling workflow transformation across healthcare, with the goal of helping HHS design policies⏎that unlock AI's deflationary potential while maintaining the safety and trust that patients⏎deserve.⏎We believe that workflow-oriented platforms - which embed AI into operational processes,⏎provide centralized governance, and enable real-time action on enterprise-wide data - are⏎essential to realizing these goals. We welcome further dialogue with the Department on these⏎issues.⏎Sincerely,⏎Combiz Abdolrahimi⏎Vice President and Global Head of Government Affairs and Public Policy⏎ServiceNow⏎⏎ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care? [MEDIUM PRIORITY]⏎Operational Data Fragmentation and the “Last Mile” Problem. The biggest barrier is not a⏎shortage of AI innovation - it is the inability to deliver that innovation into operational workflows⏎where it can create value. While clinical workflow integration is relatively well-understood (most⏎health systems operate on monolithic EMR platforms), the real challenge lies in operational data⏎fragmentation. Administrative data remains “trapped” in dozens - sometimes hundreds - of siloed⏎legacy systems that do not communicate with each other. Each application has its own login,⏎interface, and data model.⏎To take real cost out of the system using AI, operational workflows need to be totally⏎reimagined - but health systems are limited by these legacy systems. When a new AI tool is⏎introduced, it typically arrives as yet another standalone application. Users must leave their⏎primary workflow, open the AI tool, manually input or transfer data, interpret the output, return⏎to their original system, and take action. This “swivel chair” workflow negates the efficiency gains⏎AI promises. Studies consistently show that clinicians and administrators ignore alerts and⏎recommendations that arrive outside their primary workflow, regardless of validity.⏎The second major barrier is governance uncertainty. Health system executives are hesitant to⏎adopt AI tools when liability frameworks are unclear. Questions like “Who is responsible if the AI is⏎wrong?” and “How do we audit AI decisions for compliance?” remain unanswered. Without⏎clear governance standards, legal and compliance teams default to blocking adoption.⏎The path forward requires platforms that can embed AI into existing workflows rather than⏎adding new ones, governance frameworks that provide clear accountability without stifling⏎iteration, and policy that forces operational systems to openly share data.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? [LOW PRIORITY]⏎Reward Efficiency Outcomes, Not Just Clinical Outcomes. Current payment models largely⏎reward volume and discrete clinical interventions. They do not reward the operational⏎efficiencies that AI is best positioned to deliver - reduced administrative time per encounter,⏎shorter patient wait times, optimized resource utilization, or decreased length of stay through⏎better care coordination.⏎Reimbursement models are fundamentally misaligned - they pay for procedures, not outcomes⏎or efficiencies. Fee-for-service doesn’t reward AI-driven prevention or automation, so tools that⏎could significantly reduce labor costs get shelved. Incentives favor human hours over smart⏎systems. This keeps AI adoption uneven: 71% of hospitals use predictive AI in EHRs, but small and⏎rural facilities lag below 50%, according to 2024 American Hospital Association IT Survey⏎(link here).⏎HHS should consider payment adjustments or bonus structures for health systems⏎that demonstrate measurable “deflationary” improvements attributable to technology⏎adoption. Metrics might include: administrative cost per patient encounter, time from order to⏎fulfillment, staff hours spent on non-clinical tasks, or patient throughput without quality⏎degradation. Tying reimbursement incentives (e.g., value-based adjustments) to proven⏎⏎ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎efficiencies from AI tools would directly reward administrative burden reduction and cost⏎savings.⏎Affirm the Self-Regulated Status of Administrative AI. Administrative AI solutions - those that⏎automate scheduling, manage supply chains, route service requests, or optimize staffing - are⏎effectively self-regulated today and do not require FDA oversight as medical devices. This is well-⏎established and appropriate. HHS should publicly affirm this status, providing health systems⏎confidence to adopt low-risk, high-efficiency tools without unnecessary compliance hesitation.⏎Clear communication from HHS that administrative operational AI falls outside medical device⏎regulation would remove a source of caution among legal and compliance teams who may⏎otherwise treat these tools with the same scrutiny as clinical diagnostic devices.⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if⏎any, should HHS play to help address them? [HIGH PRIORITY]⏎The Model Drift and Lifecycle Management Challenge. Unlike traditional software, AI models are⏎not static. A model validated today may degrade over weeks or months as the underlying data⏎patterns shift, a phenomenon called “model drift.” A scheduling optimization model trained on⏎pre-pandemic patient volumes, for example, may perform poorly when demand patterns⏎change. This creates a novel liability question: if a health system deploys an AI tool that worked⏎at validation but fails six months later, who bears responsibility? This may be the most⏎consequential barrier to broader AI adoption.⏎The implementation challenge is that most health systems lack the infrastructure to monitor AI⏎performance over time. They deploy tools and assume they continue working. There is no⏎equivalent of a “check engine light” for AI degradation.⏎Additional implementation challenges compound this problem:⏎interoperability remains fragmented despite FHIR progress, with 60-70% of AI failures tied to poor⏎quality and siloed data. Cybersecurity risks are also likely to escalate as non-device AI becomes⏎potential attack vectors, with breaches continuing to rise at health organizations handling PHI.⏎Recommendation: Require Governance Infrastructure, Not Code-Level Regulation.⏎Rather than attempting to regulate the code or algorithms of AI tools - which would be⏎impractical given the speed of iteration - HHS should require health systems⏎to maintain governance infrastructure capable of:⏎• Maintaining a centralized inventory of all AI tools in use across the organization⏎• Tracking version history and configuration changes for each tool⏎• Logging AI-generated recommendations and subsequent human actions (audit trail)⏎• Monitoring performance metrics and flagging drift or degradation⏎• Enforcing human-in-the-loop approval workflows for high-stakes decisions⏎This “control tower” approach to governance creates clear accountability - the health system is⏎responsible for maintaining oversight, and the governance infrastructure provides the evidence⏎needed to demonstrate due diligence. Workflow platforms that unify AI governance with⏎operational execution are well-suited to provide this infrastructure, as they already manage the⏎process layer where AI recommendations become actions.⏎Additional HHS Roles to Consider: HHS could further support adoption through practical, non-⏎regulatory actions:⏎⏎ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎• Issue liability guidance via ONC/ASTP on voluntary shared accountability frameworks⏎or indemnification templates, drawing from NIST AI RMF principles, to reduce legal fears⏎without new regulations⏎• Expand HIPAA clarifications or safe harbors for secure federated learning and⏎transparency in adaptive systems, funding ONC pilots demonstrating privacy-⏎preserving data flows⏎• Enhance TEFCA and HTI rules to mandate FHIR-based APIs for seamless AI integration,⏎accelerating data standardization⏎This isn’t about more bureaucracy - it’s about engineering clarity and infrastructure, consistent⏎with the Administration’s push to remove barriers and lead in AI.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods for clinical⏎care? [MEDIUM PRIORITY]⏎Continuous Post-Deployment Monitoring Over One-Time Validation. Traditional medical device⏎evaluation focuses on pre-market validation: prove the device works before it ships. This model is⏎poorly suited to AI, which can degrade over time and whose real-world performance depends⏎heavily on the specific environment in which it is deployed.⏎The most promising evaluation approach for AI - whether clinical or operational - is continuous⏎monitoring of workflow-level outcomes. Rather than asking “Is the model accurate?” the⏎evaluation asks “Is the model delivering the intended improvement?” Metrics should include:⏎• Time-on-task reduction for the targeted workflow⏎• Error rates or rework rates before and after AI deployment⏎• User adoption and sustained engagement over time⏎• Downstream impact on efficiency metrics (throughput, cost per unit)⏎Recommendation: Promote Transparency Through Standardized Disclosure. HHS should⏎encourage development of standardized disclosure formats that require AI tool vendors to⏎transparently report: training data sources, intended use cases, known limitations,⏎validation methodology, and recommended monitoring metrics. This empowers health systems⏎to make informed procurement decisions and sets expectations for ongoing performance⏎evaluation. Such transparency standards can be promoted through guidance and best⏎practices without requiring formal regulatory mandates. The goal is to create market⏎expectations for disclosure that allow health systems to evaluate AI tools consistently.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote innovative and effective AI use⏎in clinical care? [MEDIUM PRIORITY]⏎Define “Good Governance” and Let the Market Certify It. The most effective role for HHS is to⏎define what good AI governance looks like without mandating specific⏎technical implementations. By establishing clear requirements - audit trail retention periods,⏎version control standards, performance monitoring minimums, human oversight protocols - HHS⏎can create a standard that private accreditation bodies can certify against.⏎This approach has several advantages: it creates market demand for governance-capable⏎platforms, allows for diverse technical approaches to meeting the standard, and avoids the slow⏎pace of direct federal certification. Health systems would seek out platforms and tools that carry⏎recognized governance certifications, creating a virtuous cycle that raises the bar across the⏎industry.⏎⏎ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎Existing frameworks like HITRUST for security and SOC 2 for controls demonstrate that private-⏎sector certification, anchored in federal expectations, can drive widespread adoption of best⏎practices more quickly than direct regulation.⏎Accelerate Interoperability as Foundational Infrastructure. HHS should mandate and fund⏎expansion of TEFCA/FHIR-based APIs specifically for AI tools - including ambient scribes,⏎predictive operations, revenue cycle, and workflow automation. ONC-led pilots or grants⏎could demonstrate scalable approaches.⏎The metrics make the case: where private tools get clean data, clear incentives, and⏎neutral validation, adoption surges. Organizations exploring AI report moderate-to-high ROI, with⏎clinician time savings of 2+ hours per day on documentation tasks and meaningful reductions in⏎system costs. HHS’s role isn’t to build or dictate—it’s to remove barriers, standardize the rails, and⏎incentivize speed.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? [HIGH PRIORITY]⏎Success: Operational and Administrative Automation. AI has consistently exceeded⏎expectations when applied to operational workflows rather than clinical decision-making.⏎Examples include:⏎• Patient flow optimization: Predicting admission volumes, optimizing bed assignments,⏎and reducing emergency department boarding times⏎• Supply chain automation: Predicting inventory needs, automating reorder triggers, and⏎reducing stockouts and waste⏎• IT and HR service management: Automating password resets, equipment requests, and⏎routine HR inquiries that consume staff time⏎• Security and compliance: Identifying vulnerabilities, automating patch management,⏎and detecting anomalous access patterns⏎• Documentation assistance: Drafting clinical notes, summarizing patient histories, and⏎reducing after-hours charting burden.⏎These “deflationary” applications share a common characteristic: they remove friction from⏎existing workflows rather than adding new decision points. They free human workers to focus on⏎higher-value tasks. Health systems using unified workflow platforms have reported measurable⏎improvements including reduced time-to-resolution for IT issues, faster onboarding, significant⏎reductions in security vulnerabilities, and meaningful time savings for clinicians on administrative⏎tasks.⏎Shortfalls: Interruptive Clinical Decision Support and Unclear ROI. AI has fallen short when⏎deployed as additive alerts that interrupt clinical workflow without offering a clear path to⏎action. A system that flags a potential sepsis risk but requires the clinician to navigate three⏎screens to order the appropriate intervention creates friction, not efficiency. Alert fatigue is well-⏎documented: when AI generates too many recommendations, clinicians learn to ignore them⏎all. The lesson is clear - AI must be embedded in the workflow, not bolted on top of it.⏎Cost expectations have also gone unmet in many cases. While ambient scribes and⏎documentation tools show clear value, many clinical AI tools deployed as point solutions⏎lack demonstrated financial ROI - efficiency gains are often offset by new burdens from⏎monitoring, updating, and managing liability exposure. Fee-for-service payment models⏎⏎ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎compound this problem by failing to reimburse preventive or efficiency-focused interventions,⏎even when they reduce downstream costs. Tools that could prevent issues get shelved because⏎the payment model doesn’t reward prevention.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles? [HIGH PRIORITY]⏎The Shift from CMIO to CIO/COO. Historically, clinical informatics leaders (CMIOs, CMOs) drove⏎health IT adoption based on clinical validity. For AI, the decision-making center of gravity has⏎shifted. Chief Information Officers (CIOs) and Chief Operating Officers (COOs) now⏎exert significant influence because AI adoption decisions increasingly hinge on:⏎• Integration feasibility: Can this tool connect to our existing systems?⏎• Total cost of ownership: What is the implementation and maintenance burden?⏎• Operational ROI: Will this reduce costs or improve throughput measurably?⏎• Security and compliance: Does this create new risk exposure?⏎CFOs are also critical gatekeepers, particularly for large investments, evaluating whether AI tools⏎deliver genuine deflationary benefits or simply shift costs. Emerging roles such as Chief Digital⏎Officers and Chief AI Officers are gaining influence in some health systems, though CIO and⏎COO remain the primary decision-makers in most organizations today.⏎Primary Hurdle: The “Integration Tax.” The single biggest administrative hurdle is the cost and⏎time required to integrate each new AI tool with core systems, particularly the Electronic Health⏎Record (EHR). Every point solution requires custom interface development, security review,⏎workflow redesign, and staff training. This “integration tax” often exceeds the cost of the AI tool⏎itself and creates a strong bias toward inaction.⏎Health systems that have adopted unified workflow platforms report significantly lower⏎integration burden for new AI capabilities, because the platform provides a common⏎integration layer and pre-built connectors to major clinical and operational systems. HHS can⏎help by promoting open API standards for operational data (not just clinical data), reducing the⏎custom development required to connect AI tools to health system infrastructure.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? [HIGH PRIORITY]⏎Beyond Clinical Data: The Case for Operational Interoperability. Current interoperability⏎frameworks (FHIR, USCDI) focus on clinical data: patient records, medications, lab results,⏎diagnoses. This is essential for care coordination but insufficient for operational efficiency. The⏎data that drives healthcare costs, and therefore the data AI needs to achieve deflationary⏎impact, often lives outside the clinical record:⏎• Staffing levels and shift schedules⏎• Bed availability and patient placement status⏎• Equipment location and maintenance status⏎• Supply chain inventory and consumption rates⏎• IT service requests and resolution queues⏎• Facilities status (room turnover, environmental conditions)⏎⏎ServiceNow 2225 Lawson Lane Tel +1 (408) 501-8550⏎www.servicenow.com Santa Clara, CA 95054 Fax +1 (408) 501-8550⏎The Opportunity: “Command Center” Intelligence. If AI tools could access a unified, real-time⏎view of both clinical and operational data, a new category of applications becomes possible.⏎Imagine AI that can:⏎• Predict patient discharge readiness and automatically trigger room turnover and⏎transport⏎• Match predicted admission volumes against staffing schedules and flag gaps before⏎they create bottlenecks⏎• Identify supply consumption anomalies and adjust orders proactively⏎• Route non-clinical requests (IT, facilities, HR) to resolution without human triage⏎This “command center” model requires data liquidity across the entire enterprise—not just the⏎clinical record.⏎Recommendation: Extend Information Blocking Rules to Operational Systems. HHS should⏎consider extending information blocking rules to operational data systems. Just as EHRs are now⏎required to share clinical data, legacy operational systems -scheduling, staffing, supply chain,⏎facilities - should be required to share data via open APIs. This transparency-focused approach⏎aligns with the Administration’s philosophy of promoting openness over prescriptive⏎requirements. Rather than dictating how health systems must use AI, it simply requires that the⏎data AI needs be accessible. Workflow platforms that connect clinical and operational systems⏎are well-positioned to enable this integration, providing the data fabric that AI requires to deliver⏎systemic efficiency.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0321♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-31T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rebecca♔Jackson♔♔Michigan♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My sister Diane went through something two years back that I still can't stop thinking about when I read announcements like this one. She was trying to get a diagnosis for symptoms that turned out to be an autoimmune condition, and she spent the better part of eight months driving between specialists in the Detroit metro area whose systems apparently couldn't talk to each other at all. She kept a notebook. A paper notebook, like it was 1987, because nobody's office had the previous doctor's records when she showed up. She knew more about her own history than anyone treating her did.⏎⏎Now I keep reading about how AI is going to fix coordination problems like that, and I want to believe it. I do. But what I actually think is going to happen is that the same hospital systems that couldn't get their records to follow Diane from one building to the next are going to use AI to cut the staff who do that coordination work manually. The billing coordinators, the people who call to track down records, the intake workers who notice when something doesn't add up. Those are mostly women, a lot of them women of color, and they are already underpaid and already stretched. If an AI system can do 60% of what they do at a fraction of the cost, the financial pressure on those institutions is going to be to cut that staff and call it an efficiency gain.⏎⏎I work in a public library so I'm not going to pretend I understand all the technical details of how these systems work. But I understand institutions and I understand incentives pretty well after 35 years in a public institution that's been chronically underfunded. The organizations that will benefit most from AI adoption in healthcare are not patients. They're the executives and shareholders who get to report cost reductions to boards and investors. The workers who lose jobs or whose remaining jobs become about supervising an algorithm they don't fully understand, they don't get a line item in that report.⏎⏎What I'd want HHS to actually answer, and I didn't see this addressed clearly in the description of this request, is what happens to the workforce. Not in a vague "we'll monitor impacts" kind of way. Specific accountability. If a hospital system deploys an AI tool that results in a 15% reduction in administrative staff over 18 months, what's the mechanism that connects that to the AI adoption decision? Because right now those layoffs would just look like ordinary cost restructuring.⏎⏎And honestly I'm not even sure AI would have helped Diane, because the problem wasn't that there wasn't enough computing power analyzing her case. The problem was that the data was fragmented and no one had the time or the incentive to put it together properly. An AI system trained on fragmented data is going to produce confident-sounding answers about an incomplete picture, and that might actually be worse than the notebook she was carrying around.⏎⏎She's doing better now, for what it's worth. Found a rheumatologist at U of M who actually read the whole file.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0322♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Alliance, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔Official Comment: Request for Information Regarding⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care⏎The Alliance submits this formal response to the Request for Information regarding the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence in clinical care. Our commentary is derived from the aggregated experiences and⏎preferences of our ~100 members who are patients, caregivers, and everyday citizens: the “end users” of⏎American healthcare. About 5 members are citizens of other countries, whom we asked to share their⏎perspectives as well, as we believed they would possess valuable comparative perspectives.⏎The open-ended survey questions we shared with members can be found in an appendix below. All analysis of⏎the survey was completed by members of the Alliance Strategic Office. All member responses were manually⏎reviewed.⏎I. Patient Trust and Barriers to Adoption (Addressing Questions 1 and 9)⏎In response to Question 1 regarding barriers to adoption and Question 9 regarding the concerns of patients: the⏎Department must recognize that public trust in medical AI is highly conditional on human oversight,⏎transparency, bias mitigation, and other such factors.⏎When surveyed on how their opinion of their physician would change if they learned the doctor used AI tools to⏎make clinical decisions, 60.0% of Alliance members stated their opinion would stay the same or depend heavily⏎on the conditions of its use. However, 25.9% reported their opinion would actively decrease, while only 11.8%⏎said it would increase.⏎The primary barrier to patient trust is the fear of clinical degradation. Members expressed deep concern that⏎AI use would displace independent clinical judgment. One member wrote: "If they are just using [it] as a⏎sort of encyclopedia to jog the memory, I would take little offense... but if they did not validate the assertions⏎made by the AI I would be highly skeptical." Alliance members largely rejected AI as an autonomous⏎decision-maker; they expect it to be used strictly as an assistive tool and authority and accountability to remain⏎tied to a human physician.⏎Additionally, a large majority of Alliance members indicated they tolerate mistakes made by AI less than⏎mistakes made by humans. Many cited a belief that AI would not be held accountable the way that⏎humans are.⏎⏎II. Administrative Efficiency as the Primary Goal (Addressing Questions 2, 6,⏎and 7)⏎In response to Question 7 regarding administrative hurdles and Question 2 regarding programmatic and⏎payment policy changes: the Department should prioritize the adoption of AI tools that alleviate⏎administrative burnout.⏎When asked what challenges they wish to see addressed by AI, Alliance members consistently mentioned the⏎bureaucratic inefficiencies of modern healthcare. Alliance members want AI to handle tasks such as⏎charting, automated dictation, scheduling, and insurance eligibility verification. By using AI to increase⏎administrative efficiency, the Department can allow healthcare professionals to spend more time with their⏎patients. One member with clinical work experience stated: "I would like AI to address the challenges of⏎administrative burnout, by automating paperwork and scheduling so that doctors can spend more face-to-face⏎time with their patients."⏎III. Bridging Disparities and Enhancing Diagnostics (Addressing Questions⏎6 and 9)⏎In response to Question 6 regarding where novel AI tools have the greatest potential: Alliance member⏎responses indicate strong support for AI when deployed to correct human shortcomings, specifically in⏎areas of diagnostic delay and medical bias.⏎Patients view AI as a highly promising "Second Opinion Framework." Alliance members highlighted the⏎potential for AI to act as a "neutral" arbiter to combat systemic medical biases, noting that women and minority⏎populations have historically experienced delayed diagnoses or had their symptoms minimized due to said biases.⏎A properly trained AI could flag these disparities and ensure symptoms are evaluated objectively.⏎Furthermore, members strongly support the use of AI in pattern recognition and image analysis (e.g.,⏎X-rays, MRIs) and for diagnosing "long tail" or rare diseases that a standard practitioner might only see once in a⏎career. However, such usage requires AI that is transparent about how it reaches its conclusions and is⏎demonstrably accurate, so clinicians and patients can trust that its recommendations are reliable and unbiased.⏎Sincerely,⏎Alex Hockett and Grant Hough⏎alex@worldalliance.org, grant@worldalliance.org⏎Strategic Office⏎The Alliance⏎⏎Appendix A: Survey questions⏎These are the questions we asked to Alliance members⏎Question 1: If you learned your doctor was using AI tools to help make clinical decisions, would your opinion⏎of their care increase, decrease, or stay the same? Why?⏎Question 2: What challenges within healthcare do you wish to see addressed by the adoption and use of AI in⏎clinical care?⏎Question 3: Are you more or less willing to tolerate errors from AI systems than you are from humans? Why?♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0323♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Glacis Technologies, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Glacis Technologies♔♔♔♔♔♔RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of⏎Clinical Care⏎February 6, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Washington, DC⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎We appreciate the opportunity to submit comments in response to the Department of Health and⏎Human Services Request for Information entitled "Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care" (RIN 0955-AA13). Thank you for inviting public input on an⏎issue of critical importance to the future of healthcare delivery and American competitiveness in⏎clinical care AI.⏎As doctors, we are told to document everything from day one. If you don’t document it, it didn’t⏎happen. Healthcare AI should be held to the same evidentiary standard. When I sign a clinical⏎note, we can reconstruct everything, who wrote it, when, what changes were made, who⏎accessed it. When AI generates that same note, we can't reconstruct anything. We don't know⏎what model version ran, whether safety controls executed, or where the data traveled. That's⏎the proof gap and it’s the single largest barrier to deploying AI at the scale healthcare needs.⏎When an AI ambient scribe hallucinates a substance abuse diagnosis that was never discussed,⏎or when a patient-facing chatbot discloses fabricated lab results, organizations cannot⏎reconstruct what happened at the moment of inference. Current audit trails record that requests⏎occurred, not which specific controls ran, with what parameters, or why they failed.⏎As a practicing physician, I recently encountered this firsthand. I was testing an ambient AI⏎scribe for my healthcare organization. I had my appointment with the patient, then reviewed the⏎AI output. The first time I used it, it actually generated a hallucination in the note. It stated that I⏎had recommended lamotrigine for PTSD which was nowhere in the transcript that I was able to⏎go back and check word for word.⏎The first concern was that it hallucinated and fabricated an off-label indication that, if it went into⏎the medical record, could lead to a negative patient or physician outcome. But we also⏎understand hallucinations will happen, we're never going to completely eliminate them.⏎Was there hallucination detection? What happened at that moment when the transcript went⏎into the model? Did safety checks run? Did they fail, or were they never active?⏎⏎I had no way to trace back through the system to understand where that failure occurred.But⏎here’s the more consequential problem: without evidence infrastructure, we couldn’t learn from⏎it. I had no way to trace the execution path. Without that evidence, I couldn’t improve the⏎system, and my organization's rational response would be to slow or stop AI adoption entirely.⏎That’s the real cost of the proof gap, not just risk, but forgone innovation.⏎This is a systemic gap across the industry. The infrastructure to capture execution traces just⏎doesn't exist in most AI systems today. The result is a trust deficit that slows adoption across⏎every stakeholder, physicians who can’t verify outputs, organizations that can’t satisfy their⏎boards, and vendors who can’t close procurement cycles.⏎This pattern is not new. Every industry that scales a powerful technology eventually builds the⏎evidence infrastructure to make that technology trustworthy at scale. Aviation did not debate⏎whether to install flight data recorders, after enough incidents where investigators could not⏎reconstruct what happened, the black box became standard equipment. Not because regulators⏎demanded it first, but because airlines, insurers, and manufacturers recognized that the⏎alternative was an industry that couldn’t learn from its own failures. Financial markets did not⏎debate whether companies should maintain auditable books — Generally Accepted Accounting⏎Principles emerged because capital markets could not function without verifiable records.⏎Certificate Transparency for the internet did not require legislation — browser vendors simply⏎began requiring cryptographic proof that TLS certificates were legitimately issued, and within⏎five years it became universal infrastructure. In each case, the evidence layer was not a burden⏎imposed on innovation. It was the infrastructure that made innovation scalable.⏎Healthcare AI is at precisely this inflection point. The question is not whether evidence⏎infrastructure will become standard, it will. The question is whether American institutions build it,⏎or import it.⏎Healthcare organizations want to deploy AI at scale. Many have the clinical vision and the⏎institutional will. What they lack is the technical infrastructure to demonstrate to their boards,⏎their insurers, their procurement committees, and their patients that safety mechanisms⏎operated as designed for any given encounter.⏎Healthcare organizations are deploying AI systems that can claim compliance but cannot prove⏎it both in pre and post deployment. The healthcare industry lacks technical infrastructure that⏎provides standardized and immutable methods to demonstrate that safety mechanisms ran as⏎designed when high risk AI decisions are made. Static compliance documentation, SOC 2⏎reports, architecture diagrams, policy attestations, these show controls exist, but cannot prove⏎they executed for any given patient encounter.⏎GLACIS Technologies is a healthcare AI infrastructure company building the evidence layer for⏎AI governance. We close the proof gap: the inability to verify that safety controls executed at the⏎moment of inference⏎Our leadership team brings expertise spanning clinical medicine, FDA regulatory pathways for⏎AI devices, healthcare compliance, and cryptographic systems engineering. We developed the⏎⏎first evidence-grade attestation infrastructure that enforces safety controls at runtime, provides⏎independent third-party witnessing, and generates tamper-evident, independently verifiable⏎receipts of safety control execution for every AI inference event.⏎We have developed ATLAS (Attestation and Transparency Layer for AI Systems) as an open⏎standard for evidence-grade AI governance, with complete crosswalks to NIST AI RMF,⏎ISO/IEC 42001, and EU AI Act requirements. ATLAS is open for adoption and co-development⏎by any organization, a market-driven evidence framework, not a proprietary compliance tool.⏎Market forces are already creating demand for this infrastructure, independent of any single⏎regulatory action. Insurance carriers are introducing AI-specific exclusions and requiring⏎governance documentation that most organizations cannot produce. Healthcare procurement⏎cycles for AI products routinely exceed twelve months, largely because organizations have no⏎efficient way to verify vendor safety claims. International markets, including the EU AI Act⏎(August 2026), are establishing evidence requirements that American AI companies will need to⏎meet to compete globally. And state-level frameworks in Colorado (June 2026) and California⏎(January 2027) reflect a bipartisan recognition that evidence infrastructure enables responsible⏎deployment.⏎GLACIS addresses this through its safety and accountability infrastructure that generates⏎verifiable evidence at runtime, not retrospectively. Our infrastructure enforces safety controls at⏎run time, captures complete execution traces showing which guardrails ran, in what sequence,⏎with pass/fail status, timestamps, and version identifiers, all cryptographically signed and⏎independently verifiable by auditors, regulators, or legal counsel without access to vendor⏎systems. For healthcare organizations facing "who owns the liability" questions when AI⏎systems fail, we provide the chain-of-custody documentation that the market is demanding:⏎procurement teams need it to evaluate vendors, boards need it to authorize deployment, and⏎insurers need it to price risk.⏎The enclosed response to the RFI focuses specifically on the need for third-party verifiable⏎enforcement and attestation infrastructure and technical standards as a priority for accelerating⏎clinical AI adoption. We outline technical requirements via the ATLAS framework, and detail⏎how evidence-grade systems enable healthcare organizations to enforce, attest, and⏎demonstrate, not just claim. that AI safeguards protected patients at the moment of inference.⏎Our comments emphasize that accelerating AI adoption in clinical care requires not just⏎innovation in models and applications, but parallel innovation in the evidence infrastructure that⏎makes those systems trustworthy and accountable.⏎We have intentionally emphasized the distinction between system-level assurances (compliance⏎certificates, vendor attestations, architectural documentation) and transaction-level proof⏎(cryptographically verifiable evidence of what happened for specific patient interactions).⏎⏎Three years of healthcare AI deployment have demonstrated a consistent pattern: governance⏎frameworks without enforcement mechanisms, and compliance claims without verifiable⏎evidence, create an adoption ceiling. Organizations that cannot prove safety cannot scale⏎deployment — and the organizations with the greatest clinical need are often the most risk-⏎averse.⏎We believe the fastest path to accelerating AI adoption in clinical care is not prescriptive⏎regulation but evidence infrastructure that gives healthcare organizations the confidence to⏎deploy. When a hospital can demonstrate to its board, its insurer, and its patients that AI⏎systems operated safely with cryptographic proof, not just vendor promises — the risk calculus⏎shifts from “we can’t afford to adopt” to “we can’t afford not to.” That shift is what moves AI from⏎pilot programs to production at scale.⏎We respectfully encourage HHS to prioritize research into open, interoperable evidentiary⏎standards for healthcare AI, standards that enable vendors to produce independently verifiable⏎proof that safety controls executed as designed, that PHI remained within customer boundaries,⏎and that clinical decisions can be reconstructed with complete provenance. Such standards⏎would represent the market-driven alternative to prescriptive regulation: organizations that can⏎self-evidence don’t need to be told how to be safe.⏎Thank you for your leadership on this important issue and for your commitment to ensuring that⏎AI systems in healthcare can be trusted, audited, and proven safe.⏎Respectfully,⏎Jennifer Shannon, MD⏎Chief Medical Officer⏎GLACIS Technologies⏎Enclosure: GLACIS Response to HHS RFI Question #10 (AI Research Priorities for Evidence-⏎Grade Compliance Infrastructure)⏎Contact Information:⏎GLACIS Technologies⏎jennifer@glacis.io⏎glacis.io⏎Expertise Areas:⏎● Cryptographic attestation for AI systems⏎● Healthcare regulatory compliance (EU AI Act, FDA, HIPAA)⏎● Clinical AI safety and governance⏎● Evidence-grade AI adoption infrastructure♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0324♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI — COGA Labs⏎RESPONSE TO REQUEST FOR INFORMATION⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care⏎Federal Register Doc. 2025-23641⏎Submitted By: COGA Labs⏎Respondent Type: AI Health Technology Company⏎Date: February 2026⏎Identifier: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Contact Info: Simon@cogalabs.com⏎Executive Summary⏎COGA Labs develops AI-powered stress detection technology that uses smartphone⏎sensors and proprietary machine learning models to identify acute and chronic stress states in⏎real time. Our platform analyzes behavioral signals and physiological indicators to provide⏎actionable insights for individuals and the professionals supporting them.⏎Our founding team combines hands-on AI product development experience (including building⏎and shipping production AI systems for enterprise clients) with management consulting (BCG)⏎and financial services (Deutsche Bank) backgrounds. This positions us to comment on both the⏎technical and commercial barriers to AI adoption in clinical care.⏎We welcome the opportunity to share practitioner-level perspectives with HHS as it shapes⏎policy to accelerate responsible AI adoption in health care.⏎Response to Question 1: Barriers to Private Sector AI Innovation in⏎Health Care⏎“What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?”⏎1. Regulatory Classification Ambiguity⏎The FDA’s evolving framework for Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) creates significant⏎uncertainty for AI companies working at the intersection of wellness and clinical care. Products⏎that analyze behavioral or physiological data for stress, mental health, or cognitive performance⏎sit in a gray zone between “wellness” and “diagnostic” classification. This ambiguity delays⏎market entry and diverts capital from product development to regulatory navigation.⏎Recommendation: HHS should consider establishing an “AI Health Sandbox”—modeled on⏎regulatory sandbox programs in the UK (MHRA AI Airlock) and Singapore—that grants⏎Page 1⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — COGA Labs⏎provisional authorization for AI health tools to operate in supervised clinical environments while⏎generating real-world evidence for full clearance.⏎2. No Clear Reimbursement Pathway⏎There is currently no straightforward CPT code pathway for AI-augmented mental health⏎screening or continuous behavioral health monitoring. Clinicians who adopt these tools absorb⏎the cost, creating negative ROI regardless of clinical value. Until reimbursement catches up,⏎adoption will remain limited to self-pay wellness markets rather than mainstream clinical care.⏎Recommendation: CMS should establish provisional reimbursement codes for FDA-authorized⏎AI clinical tools, tied to outcomes-based payment models with built-in sunset provisions to allow⏎data collection without permanent cost commitment.⏎3. Prohibitive Integration Costs⏎For small AI companies, the cost of integrating with legacy EHR systems remains a major⏎barrier. FHIR adoption is growing but incomplete, and each health system integration requires⏎custom engineering work that favors large incumbents over innovative startups. This⏎concentrates AI innovation among companies with existing health system relationships rather⏎than those building the best technology.⏎Recommendation: HHS should incentivize EHR vendors to establish standardized API⏎marketplaces where FDA-authorized AI tools can be deployed within existing clinical workflows,⏎reducing the per-integration cost that currently locks out smaller innovators.⏎4. Data Access for Model Training⏎AI models require diverse, representative data to achieve clinical-grade performance. However,⏎the combination of HIPAA’s minimum necessary standard, fragmented data systems, and⏎proprietary formats makes it disproportionately expensive for small companies to access⏎sufficient clinical training data. Expanding the ONC’s Trusted Exchange Framework (TEFCA) to⏎include a specific pathway for AI development—with standardized de-identification and⏎pre-negotiated data use agreements—would significantly lower this barrier.⏎Response to Question 8: Interoperability Opportunities⏎“Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?”⏎Behavioral and mental health data remains the most fragmented domain in clinical⏎informatics. Assessment scores, therapy notes, medication adherence records, and AI-derived⏎behavioral indicators exist across disconnected systems with no unified patient view. A⏎standardized FHIR-based mental health data profile—covering both structured clinical⏎assessments and AI-derived behavioral markers—would enable longitudinal monitoring that is⏎currently impossible at scale.⏎Wearable and sensor data standards are equally critical. Consumer devices generate⏎clinically relevant physiological data (sleep patterns, heart rate variability, activity levels) that⏎rarely reaches the clinical record. Standardized ingestion pipelines—building on IEEE 11073⏎and Open mHealth schemas—would create new training datasets for AI while enabling passive,⏎continuous health monitoring. This is especially relevant for mental health and stress-related⏎Page 2⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI — COGA Labs⏎conditions, where longitudinal data provides better predictive value than point-in-time⏎assessments.⏎Federated learning infrastructure deserves investment. Enhanced interoperability need not⏎mean centralized data. A permanent, HIPAA-compliant federated learning network—accessible⏎to small companies, not just academic medical centers—would accelerate AI model⏎development while preserving privacy. The NIH’s N3C project demonstrated the value of⏎multi-institutional collaboration; a standing equivalent for health AI would be transformative.⏎Response to Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎“What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI in clinical care? What⏎concerns do they have?”⏎From user research conducted during our product development across wellness and corporate⏎health populations, we consistently observe two themes:⏎Demand for objective measurement: Users express frustration with subjective self-report⏎tools (PHQ-9, GAD-7) that depend on memory and self-awareness. They actively seek AI tools⏎that provide objective, data-driven indicators of their mental and physical state. In our⏎experience, users who receive objective health data show significantly higher engagement with⏎health and wellness resources. This suggests strong latent demand for AI-augmented health⏎screening—provided the tools are transparent about methodology.⏎Data privacy as the dominant concern: Across our user base, data access and control⏎consistently ranks as the top concern—ahead of accuracy, cost, or usability. Users are willing to⏎share AI-derived health insights with their care providers but want explicit control over who sees⏎their data, how long it is retained, and whether it can be used for secondary purposes. Clear⏎HHS guidance on AI health data governance—distinct from general HIPAA provisions—would⏎accelerate patient trust and adoption.⏎About the Respondent⏎COGA Labs is a health technology company developing AI-powered behavioral analysis tools⏎for stress detection and mental wellness monitoring. Our team brings experience across AI⏎product development, enterprise SaaS deployment, management consulting, and financial⏎services. We have validated our core technology with hundreds of users and are actively⏎pursuing clinical application pathways.⏎Relevant NAICS codes: 541512 (Computer Systems Design Services), 541519 (Other⏎Computer Related Services). Small business.⏎COGA Labs welcomes follow-up consultation with HHS program offices and is available for⏎briefings or technology demonstrations. Inquiries may be directed to the company through its⏎website or public contact channels.⏎END OF RESPONSE⏎Page 3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0325♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Society of Hospital Medicine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Society of Hospital Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology, Office of the Secretary⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On behalf of the Society of Hospital Medicine (SHM), we appreciate the⏎opportunity to provide comments to the U.S. Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) regarding the responsible development, regulation, and adoption⏎of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎SHM represents the nation’s more than 50,000 hospitalists, who specialize in⏎managing the care of hospitalized patients, and are often at the forefront of⏎healthcare innovation and technological advancement. As clinicians who have⏎adapted to and lead during times of rapid change, hospitalists bring unique⏎expertise in evaluating and implementing both clinical practice and new⏎technologies in hospital settings. For example, hospitalists were the front lines⏎against a novel disease caring for hospitalized patients during the COVID-19⏎pandemic and have long been leaders of both the implementation and⏎superusers of inpatient electronic health records (EHRs) following the 2009⏎HITECH Act.⏎The implementation and use of AI in healthcare is growing rapidly. We are⏎concerned that the policy and regulatory conversations have not kept up with⏎the pace of change, creating risks for patients and clinicians alike. AI should⏎improve clinician workflow and patient care at the individual level with the goal⏎of improving overall community health. Importantly, physicians must maintain⏎ultimate authority and accountability as experts and decision-makers. Without⏎these goals, there will be resistance to adoption. We applaud this RFI as an⏎important step in gathering perspectives on AI use in healthcare and the policy⏎sphere surrounding it.⏎We offer the following framework and recommendations in response to the⏎questions posed by HHS.⏎Potential Framework for Evaluating AI - Risk-Based Categorization and⏎Evaluation⏎HHS should carefully evaluate AI tools based on their risk level to patients and⏎calibrate regulatory oversight proportionate to that risk. AI tools in hospital⏎medicine exist on the following spectrum of clinical risk, but such examples could⏎be generalized more broadly dependent on care setting.⏎⏎1. Low Risk – Non-Clinical, Non-Patient-Facing Administrative Tasks:⏎Tools used for creating reports, generating presentations, writing business letters, and⏎other administrative functions. These require minimal clinical scrutiny as they do not⏎directly impact patient care.⏎2. Moderate Risk – Clinical Documentation and Decision Support:⏎Tools that are assistive in nature through summarization or initial drafting. Specific⏎examples include, but aren’t necessarily limited to the following:⏎• Generative AI for summarizing medical records or drafting discharge summaries.⏎• AI-enhanced evidence review tools.⏎• Predictive triage and throughput support systems.⏎• Ambient documentation technologies.⏎Such tools supplement but do not supplant expert human clinical judgment. Examples⏎include ambient AI documentation tools and evidence-based search platforms like⏎OpenEvidence®, among others. Although helpful, these uses require a 'human-in-the-⏎loop' to ensure accuracy and to review for accuracy and AI hallucinations.⏎3. High Risk – Clinical Decision Support and Alerts:⏎Tools embedded in EHRs that generate alerts, recommend diagnostic or therapeutic⏎interventions, or provide risk stratification. These require the highest level of vetting,⏎individual research, and validation within each unique clinical context. Algorithms must⏎be trained specifically for each clinical setting and cannot be readily transferred across⏎institutions or patient populations without retraining as AI trained on non-⏎representative datasets can perpetuate or worsen racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic⏎disparities in care. AI at this risk level must also be accompanied by ongoing post-⏎deployment monitoring and operate with clear accountability structures in place. Part of⏎ongoing operations should also include a transparency requirement. Clinicians need to⏎understand why an AI is making a recommendation to exercise meaningful oversight.⏎Focus on Core Principles for AI in Healthcare⏎The overall mission of AI implementation should be to improve patient care and clinician wellbeing. This⏎should be clearly stated and reinforced in all regulatory and implementation efforts.⏎Human-Centered Care: AI may offer potential diagnoses or medical therapies, but it is not physically⏎present at the bedside. Further, it cannot exercise clinical judgment considering a patient's full⏎psychosocial and clinical needs. Technology should reduce documentation burden to allow physicians⏎and the care team to build deeper patient connections, not increase clinical workload or reduce⏎reimbursement based on efficiency gains.⏎Physician Authority and Autonomy: Physicians must maintain ultimate authority and control over all⏎clinical decision-making. AI tools must be viewed as a supplement to human expertise but do not⏎replace it.⏎⏎Address Privacy and Security Concerns⏎The adoption and use of AI presents challenges, some new and some longstanding, for existing patient⏎privacy and security concerns. Regulations and laws may need to be updated to reflect the current⏎reality of patient information and privacy. We urge the administration to carefully consider how to⏎maintain and strengthen patient protections to prevent the misuse and abuse of patient’s private health⏎information.⏎Informed Consent:⏎• Patients whose data will be used by AI should be informed with a clear process for opt-out.⏎• Current workflow challenges include obtaining consent before recording patient encounters.⏎This risks adding complexity to clinical workflows and redistributing (or even increasing)⏎administrative burden without meaningful reductions.⏎Data Storage Protections:⏎Video and audio streaming AI devices generate vast amounts of potentially identifiable data. What⏎happens to those recordings after the healthcare interaction takes place is highly variable. Healthcare⏎entities need pragmatic regulation governing use and storage of audio/video recordings to ensure⏎consistency across settings and systems. Protections will be required to guard against disclosure or⏎theft.⏎Patient and Clinician Privacy Guardrails:⏎Both patient privacy and clinician privacy must be protected. As such, concerns around 'AI surveillance'⏎must be addressed. For example, ambient AI being used to evaluate clinician performance or to⏎determine compensation without appropriate safeguards.⏎Legal and Liability Issues⏎In the event something goes wrong, there is currently unclear fault allocation among AI developers,⏎healthcare providers, and institutions. Aligned with the risk categorization outlined above, as the risks⏎for patients increase, so does the importance of addressing legal liability issues. There are significant⏎concerns about AI tools being integrated into clinical workflows without ample research, policy⏎safeguards, or ongoing monitoring. For example, discharge summary tools that begin to hallucinate false⏎information could dictate future patient care and incorrectly trained or degraded algorithms could⏎produce inaccurate results leading to incorrect treatment. AI tools should have clear guidelines for use⏎and liability, similar to other healthcare technologies⏎Barriers to AI Innovation and Adoption⏎Several barriers to innovation and adoption have become evident over the past few years as AI tools⏎have evolved and their use has become more common. Some of these barriers are structural to the⏎healthcare system, while others have more to do with culture and trust in the tools themselves.⏎⏎• Weak interoperability for existing tech systems in healthcare, including between EHRs and other⏎databases. Without complete data, AI tools will face the same challenges as human care teams⏎in trying to get as clear a picture of the patient’s needs as possible.⏎• No predictable reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled workflows. The existing reimbursement⏎system does not compensate for a significant amount of work done by clinicians. As AI creates⏎efficiencies, this “time back” for clinicians should prioritize more face-to-face and bedside time⏎with patients, not financial cuts.⏎• High integration costs for EHR customization and workflow redesign.⏎• Misaligned incentives: profits go to vendors and savings accrue to payers while costs of⏎implementation and ongoing operation fall on clinicians and facilities. These financial constraints⏎will continue to slow adoption.⏎• AI tools are not currently integrated within existing workflows, creating additional time burden⏎for their use.⏎• Alert fatigue. This phenomenon has increased as more technology ‘assisted’ tools have been⏎introduced into the healthcare system and clinicians are bombarded with alarms, pop-ups,⏎alerts, and other signals. AI has the potential to expand the number of alerts dramatically.⏎While AI shows significant promise for improving healthcare systems, operations, and even clinical care,⏎there is a reasonable skepticism among clinicians. While humans can make mistakes (“To Err is Human”),⏎mechanisms for accountability and a culture of learning have been built over decades. AI does not have⏎this demonstrated track record of learning and adjusting or established systems of accountability. In⏎addition, there is reasonable concern that gains from AI will be dramatically offset by cuts to the human⏎workforce and undermine the necessary human element of delivering patient care.⏎Conclusion⏎The Society of Hospital Medicine recognizes the tremendous potential of AI to support hospitalists and⏎improve patient care. However, successful integration requires addressing data quality and⏎interoperability challenges, clarifying regulatory frameworks, establishing clear liability structures,⏎protecting patient and clinician privacy, and ensuring that AI tools genuinely improve workflow⏎efficiency and enhance the quality of patient care. AI should be implemented with the overarching goal⏎of improving overall community health. Without addressing these issues, there will be resistance to⏎adoption.⏎SHM has general optimism about AI tools that can significantly reduce administrative burdens, allow⏎more face time with patients, and perform accurate encounter-based documentation, coding, and⏎billing. However, adoption remains slow, with most current use limited to resident teaching rounds,⏎private practice ambient documentation, and some sepsis alert systems.⏎HHS should focus on creating an environment where AI innovations meet healthcare needs rather than⏎prioritizing technology sector interests. The ultimate measure of success must be improved patient⏎outcomes, enhanced clinician wellbeing, and better overall health – not technological sophistication for⏎its own sake, or pure economic efficiency aims such as higher encounter quotas or reduced⏎reimbursement.⏎⏎We appreciate HHS’s leadership in seeking stakeholder input and stand ready to partner in advancing⏎policies that ensure AI strengthens—not supplants—the human practice of medicine.⏎Sincerely,⏎Chad T. Whelan, MD, MHSA, SFHM⏎President⏎Society of Hospital Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0326♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Submitted Electronically⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., JD, LLM⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎On behalf of the more than 10,000 physiatrists of the American Academy of⏎Physical Medicine and Rehabilitation (AAPM&R), we appreciate the opportunity to⏎submit comments in response to the Department of Health and Human Services⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care. AAPM&R is the national medical specialty⏎organization representing physicians who are specialists in physical medicine and⏎rehabilitation (PM&R). PM&R physicians, also known as physiatrists, treat a wide⏎variety of medical conditions affecting the brain, spinal cord, nerves, bones, joints,⏎ligaments, muscles, and tendons. PM&R physicians evaluate and treat injuries,⏎illnesses, and disability and are experts in designing comprehensive, patient-⏎centered treatment plans. Physiatrists utilize cutting-edge⏎as well as time-tested treatments to maximize function and quality of life.⏎AAPM&R agrees that the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) has a⏎significant role in shaping the environment in which AI is used in healthcare. As⏎such, we are grateful for the opportunity to provide feedback on barriers in⏎regulation and reimbursement associated with AI implementation. Our Academy⏎and our membership have been exploring the role of AI in clinical care as well as⏎the role it can play in administrative tasks associated with practicing medicine. We⏎are encouraged by the opportunities available and the potential positive impact on⏎patient health outcomes and physician wellbeing. However, we urge caution with⏎respect to rapid implementation of AI tools without clear guardrails. Though we⏎recognize the need to be nimble as technologies are evolving quickly,⏎advancements in healthcare require adequate infrastructure, regulation, and⏎reimbursement.⏎Regulation⏎Current regulatory categories do not neatly fit AI tools in rehabilitation. This can⏎lead to clinician hesitation to adopt tools without clear expectations for privacy,⏎safety, validation, transparency, and liability. To address this limitation, greater⏎⏎clarity is needed to provide clinicians certainty regarding their risks and responsibilities and to facilitate⏎greater comfort with the adoption of new technologies.⏎AAPM&R recommends HHS explore regulation around “black box” technologies. Specifically, without⏎sharing proprietary information or protected health information, AI companies should be required to make⏎their algorithms and data sources transparent and include physicians in the development of algorithms.⏎This will help move the needle toward creating trust and understanding. Physiatrists are especially⏎concerned that “black box” AI may underrepresent pertinent information from persons with disabilities.⏎This lack of trust leads to hesitation around adoption. Transparency in algorithms and physician⏎involvement may lead to more confident adoption and widespread use of AI and facilitate partnerships⏎between AI companies and the physician community to better address the needs of certain populations⏎such as persons with disabilities.⏎AAPM&R also underscores the importance of physician judgment in clinical care, and the need to ensure⏎that a physician – in collaboration with the patient - is the ultimate decision maker. We recommend that⏎HHS establish requirements that physician oversight is required when AI is used for treatment decisions.⏎Likewise, we recommend that physician review be required when AI is used in making insurance or prior⏎authorization determinations, in order to ensure necessary human oversight.⏎AAPM&R also recommends HHS review HIPAA regulations to consider the need for updates related to⏎privacy and security risks associated with AI and other new technology innovations. At a minimum,⏎physicians and the medical community should be provided with modernized guidance around HIPAA⏎compliance related to AI use in research, administrative tasks, and clinical care. Absent clear, up-to-date⏎guidance, physicians are hesitant to implement cutting edge technologies.⏎Reimbursement⏎For physician practices, cost or affordability of AI continues to be a significant barrier to implementation of⏎AI. While this applies to all practices, cost can be particularly burdensome for small group practices, private⏎practices, solo practitioners, and rural practices. AI technologies require significant investment of both⏎time and financial resources without a guaranteed return on investment. AAPM&R recommends⏎thoughtful exploration of reimbursement policy with respect to the range of AI technologies, including in⏎the Medicare program.⏎Current reimbursement for AI-supported care is limited due to a lack of straightforward connection⏎between AI and the current fee-for-service payment structure. We recognize that the American Medical⏎Association’s (AMA’s) Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) Editorial Panel is working to modernize the⏎CPT code set to accommodate the range of rapidly emerging technologies including AI-enabled care.⏎AAPM&R recommends HHS and the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) support this⏎development of new coding pathways for digital healthcare. Further, when it comes to reimbursement for⏎AI tools, we are concerned that paying for high-cost AI equipment under the current Medicare physician⏎fee schedule (MPFS) may become unsustainable due to budget neutrality rules. We urge HHS and CMS to⏎⏎explore mechanisms for paying for AI equipment that do not negatively impact payment for physician⏎services under the MPFS.⏎Beyond fee-for-service, AI tools are likely also to be an asset in alternative payment models (APMs). We⏎are concerned, however, that AI is being used in APMs without the guidance and input from the physician⏎community. We strongly recommend that clinical experts are at the table when discussing incorporation of⏎AI into APMs – including in the model conceptualization and development phases. Furthermore, to⏎appropriately motivate physician use of AI in APMs, HHS should work with the CMS Innovation Center to⏎appropriately incorporate AI costs into model benchmarks and spending calculations. If these costs are not⏎properly captured, physicians and hospitals will struggle to invest in new technologies necessary to⏎succeed under new models.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you for your consideration of our comments. If you have any questions or would like more⏎information regarding any of the recommendations offered above, please contact Carolyn Millett, Senior⏎Director of Practice, Reimbursement, and Regulatory Affairs at cmillett@aapmr.org or (847) 737-6024.⏎Sincerely,⏎Susan Hubbell, MD, FAAPMR⏎Chair, Health Policy, Practice, and Advocacy Committee♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0327♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Intersocietal Accreditation Commission, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-04T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Intersocietal Accreditation Commission♔♔♔♔♔♔Sponsoring Organizations February 23, 2026⏎ADULT CONGENITAL HEART ASSOCIATION⏎ALLIANCE OF CARDIOVASCULAR Jim O’Neill⏎PROFESSIONALS⏎Deputy Secretary⏎AMERICAN ACADEMY OF NEUROLOGY⏎AMERICAN ACADEMY OF ORAL AND Department of Health and Human Services⏎MAXILLOFACIAL RADIOLOGY⏎AMERICAN ACADEMY OF⏎ORTHOPAEDIC SURGEONS⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎AMERICAN ACADEMY OF⏎OTOLARYNGOLOGY — Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎HEAD AND NECK SURGERY⏎AMERICAN ASSOCIATION OF National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎NEUROLOGICAL SURGEONS / CONGRESS⏎OF NEUROLOGICAL SURGEONS⏎CEREBROVASCULAR SECTION⏎Submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎AMERICAN ASSOCIATION OF ORAL AND⏎MAXILLOFACIAL SURGEONS⏎AMERICAN ASSOCIATION OF⏎PHYSICISTS IN MEDICINE RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎AMERICAN COLLEGE OF SURGEONS⏎as Part of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI)⏎AMERICAN COLLEGE OF⏎NUCLEAR MEDICINE⏎AMERICAN INSTITUTE OF⏎ULTRASOUND IN MEDICINE Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎AMERICAN ORTHOPAEDIC⏎FOOT AND ANKLE SOCIETY⏎AMERICAN SOCIETY OF On behalf of the Intersocietal Accreditation Commission (IAC), we appreciate the opportunity⏎ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY⏎to comment on the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) request for information⏎AMERICAN SOCIETY OF NEUROIMAGING⏎AMERICAN SOCIETY OF (RFI) on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical⏎NEURORADIOLOGY⏎Care. The IAC is a nonprofit, nationally recognized, Center for Medicare and Medicaid⏎AMERICAN SOCIETY OF⏎NUCLEAR CARDIOLOGY Services (CMS)- Advanced Diagnostic Imaging (ADI) accrediting organization, founded by⏎AMERICAN SOCIETY OF⏎RADIOLOGIC TECHNOLOGISTS medical professionals to advance appropriate utilization, standardization, quality imaging and⏎AMERICAN VEIN AND LYMPHATIC SOCIETY safety of diagnostic imaging and interventional-based procedures. IAC offers accreditation in⏎AMERICAN VENOUS FORUM⏎hospitals and all other settings where diagnostic testing and procedures are performed.⏎HEART RHYTHM SOCIETY⏎NEUROCRITICAL CARE SOCIETY Importantly, IAC represents a unique collaboration of physicians, technologists, sonographers,⏎PEDIATRIC AND CONGENITAL physicists, and numerous other medical professionals representing more than 40 medical⏎ELECTROPHYSIOLOGY SOCIETY⏎SOCIETY FOR CARDIOVASCULAR specialties as sponsoring organizations, who contribute to its multi-stakeholder efforts. With⏎MAGNETIC RESONANCE⏎its mission to improve health care through accreditation®, the IAC utilizes a rigorous clinical⏎SOCIETY FOR CLINICAL⏎VASCULAR SURGERY peer review process to ensure that quality and safe practices are established with the goal of⏎SOCIETY FOR VASCULAR MEDICINE⏎enhancing patient outcomes.⏎SOCIETY FOR VASCULAR NURSING⏎SOCIETY FOR VASCULAR SURGERY⏎SOCIETY FOR VASCULAR ULTRASOUND Since its inception in 1991, the IAC has granted accreditation to more than 14,000 sites in ten⏎SOCIETY OF CARDIOVASCULAR⏎ANESTHESIOLOGISTS diagnostic imaging and interventional-based accreditation programs. The IAC is also one of⏎SOCIETY OF CARDIOVASCULAR the four national accreditation organizations to be designated by CMS to ensure the quality of⏎COMPUTED TOMOGRAPHY⏎SOCIETY OF DIAGNOSTIC advanced diagnostic imaging (ADI) suppliers, including physician offices and Independent⏎MEDICAL SONOGRAPHY⏎Diagnostic Testing Facilities, in compliance with the Medicare Improvements for Patients and⏎SOCIETY OF INTERVENTIONAL RADIOLOGY⏎SOCIETY OF NEUROINTERVENTIONAL Providers Act of 2008 (MIPPA). Additionally, IAC is the only imaging accreditation⏎SURGERY⏎organization that holds ISO certifications in Quality Management Systems (ISO 9001:2015)—⏎SOCIETY OF NUCLEAR MEDICINE⏎AND MOLECULAR IMAGING demonstrating our commitment to quality, reliability, and ongoing improvement—and⏎SOCIETY OF NUCLEAR MEDICINE⏎AND MOLECULAR IMAGING Information Security Systems (27001:2022)—demonstrating our commitment to protecting⏎TECHNOLOGIST SECTION⏎data integrity, confidentiality, and availability.⏎SOCIETY OF PEDIATRIC⏎ECHOCARDIOGRAPHY⏎SOCIETY OF RADIOLOGISTS⏎IN ULTRASOUND Acknowledging the ongoing, rapid evolution of artificial intelligence (AI) in medicine, the IAC⏎SOCIETY OF VASCULAR AND⏎INTERVENTIONAL NEUROLOGY Board of Directors established an AI Task Force in 2024. Developed through the collective⏎WORLD MOLECULAR IMAGING SOCIETY expertise of the task force, a guidance document was created, approved by the IAC Board of⏎Directors, and published in April 2025, as an addendum to each set of IAC Standards.⏎⏎Mr. Jim O’Neill and Dr. Thomas Keane⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 2 of 2⏎The IAC AI Guidance Document serves as a recommendation for IAC-accredited facilities utilizing AI⏎technology and states the following:⏎To assure the quality and safety of care delivery when using AI applications for direct-patient care⏎(clinical*) purposes, each facility should create and follow policies and procedures that address:⏎1. Training for personnel who use AI⏎2. Security of AI software, updates, HIPAA considerations, etc.⏎3. AI for Quality Improvement (if applicable)⏎4. Appropriate use for each AI application⏎5. Governance (authority to make decisions regarding AI implementation)⏎* Clinical use of AI includes image acquisition, image processing/enhancement, image⏎interpretation, report generation, risk assessment of prognosis, patient history, identification of⏎critical values/results and equipment quality control.⏎IAC recognizes that AI use is expanding and there is a lack of uniform processes to assure patient safety and⏎quality care delivery. Our recently issued guidance represents an interim solution, offering foundational⏎guidelines to address the most rudimentary quality and safety aspects of clinical AI. However, as cutting-edge AI⏎innovations continue to evolve, IAC intends to enhance its guidance for the clinical setting.⏎In this RFI, HHS seeks feedback on ways in which it can best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care. IAC's accreditation programs already include thorough assessments of the care environment— including⏎infrastructure, capacity, systems, and processes— which promote data-driven improvements that lead to better⏎outcomes. IAC will continue to incorporate established guidelines, including those supporting effective and safe⏎use of AI, into our accreditation standards.⏎IAC’s number one priority is to promote safe and equitable patient care, and we stand ready to assist HHS in⏎ensuring those goals are preserved as AI use in clinical care rapidly evolves. IAC would be happy to meet with⏎HHS to discuss our work in more detail, as well as a potential future collaboration when guidelines for the use of⏎AI are established. Please do not hesitate to contact me via email at ttolpegin@intersocietal.org or by phone at⏎1.800.838.2110. We also offer the IAC website to learn more about our various programs and standards, focused⏎on the quality of diagnostic imaging and interventional procedures.⏎Sincerely,⏎Tanya Tolpegin, MBA, CAE⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎Intersocietal Accreditation Commission♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0328♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-07T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Robert♔Harris♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔AdvaMed♔♔♔♔♔♔My main question, honestly, is whether this is already decided or not. I've been working in government affairs long enough to know that sometimes these comment periods are genuine and sometimes they're more of a formality, and I genuinely can't tell which this is. The way the RFI is framed, it reads like HHS is still in an exploratory phase, but some of the language around "accelerating adoption" feels like a direction has already been chosen and they're just filling in details. If someone from HHS could clarify that, it would help me frame our response more usefully.⏎⏎That said, I want to raise something that keeps coming back to me when I read through this. AdvaMed represents a wide range of medical technology companies, and our members are genuinely excited about what AI can do in clinical settings. But there's a real gap between what the technology can do in a lab environment and what's happening in actual practice right now. A 2024 survey of over 4,200 physicians found that 63% had no standardized protocol for disclosing AI-assisted diagnostics to patients. That's not a small number. And 41% weren't sure what their liability exposure was when an algorithm played a role in a clinical decision. I keep thinking about what that means for a device manufacturer trying to do the right thing. You build something, you get it cleared, and then it goes into a clinical environment where the disclosure piece is just... not happening consistently. That's a problem that sits somewhere between the FDA's jurisdiction and what HHS is trying to address here, and I'm not sure this RFI fully accounts for that gap.⏎⏎AdvaMed Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎The equity piece is also something we feel strongly about. Our Health Equity Task Force has been looking at performance disparities across patient demographics, and there are documented cases where AI tools return higher rates of low-confidence diagnostic flags for Hispanic patients and uninsured populations, particularly in facilities in underserved regions. That's not theoretical. So when we talk about accelerating adoption, I'd want to understand whether HHS is conditioning any of the reimbursement or regulatory relief measures on equity benchmarks. Or is the plan to accelerate first and address disparities afterward. That order of operations matters a lot.⏎⏎I suppose what I'm really asking for is some clarity on process before we submit more detailed technical comments. If the framework is genuinely still open, we have a lot to say about how reimbursement structures could be designed to incentivize equitable deployment rather than just fast deployment. But if the core decisions are already baked in, we'd rather focus our comments on implementation specifics.⏎⏎One other thing that came up internally: our members have asked whether comments submitted here will be made publicly available and searchable in the docket, or whether there's any mechanism for confidential submission of proprietary information. I looked through the RFI and didn't see a clear answer on that. The docket number is listed as HHS-2024-0♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0329♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Family Physicians, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Family Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔February 19, 2026⏎National Coordinator Thomas Keane, MD⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear National Coordinator Keane:⏎On behalf of the American Academy of Family Physicians (AAFP), representing 128,300 family⏎physicians and medical students across the country, I write in response to the Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology’s (ASTP/ONC) request for information (RFI),”Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care”. The AAFP appreciates ASTP/ONC’s desire and⏎intent to work with stakeholders to accelerate the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI)⏎solutions as part of clinical care, and we support the administration’s efforts to deliver AI tools⏎that will help physicians provide better patient care and experience less administrative burden.⏎We stand ready to partner with ASTP/ONC and the whole of the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) to facilitate the responsible, safe, and effective adoption of AI tools in⏎clinical care.⏎The family medicine experience is based on a deeply personal patient-physician relationship⏎that benefits from many supportive technologies, including AI. In 2023, the AAFP developed an⏎initial set of principles that we believe must be applied to AI’s implementation across the broad⏎range of settings in which family physicians practice. The AAFP believes AI tools should be⏎evaluated with the same rigor as any other tool utilized in health care, and that these tools have⏎the potential to support the core functions of primary care, which are frequently characterized⏎as first contact, comprehensiveness, continuity, and coordination of care.⏎As detailed further below and in addition to other recommendations, the AAFP urges ASTP/ONC⏎to prioritize the development of AI-focused educational and training resources for the health⏎care workforce in collaboration with specialty societies and other stakeholders. In particular, we⏎would like to work with ASTP/ONC to develop a suite of educational resources designed⏎specifically for small and independent physician practices that strengthens their ability to review⏎and assess newly available AI tools. Many small and independent practices are located in rural⏎and underserved communities where care is hard to find—and where new technologies can⏎have an outsized impact. Family physicians want and need to know about new AI and digital⏎info@aafp.org⏎1133 Connecticut Ave., NW, Ste. 1100⏎(800) 794-7481⏎Washington, DC 20036-1011⏎(202) 232-9033 www.aafp.org⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 2 of 11⏎health products that could help their patients, but they are overburdened by the inefficiencies⏎and requirements of the current system, which leaves them with limited ability to seek out⏎these tools. For optimal impact, developed resources should be concise and actionable,⏎pointing busy practice owners and technology champions to the highest impact AI use cases and⏎outlining the steps practices can take to assess, implement, and utilize new AI capabilities.⏎Additionally, we urge ASTP/ONC to prioritize the inclusion of family physicians and other end-⏎users across the AI product lifecycle: design, validation, implementation, and post‑market⏎surveillance. As frontline clinicians and advocates for comprehensive, patient-centered care,⏎family physicians are uniquely positioned to inform the development and deployment of AI⏎technologies. Unfortunately, physician and end‑user perspectives are frequently⏎underrepresented in decisions related to the selection and deployment of health information⏎technology (IT) and AI tools. An AAFP survey conducted jointly with Rock Health in September⏎2024 found that many primary care physicians reported having little to no involvement in these⏎decision-making processes. The absence of meaningful clinician input contributes to⏎technologies that are poorly aligned with clinical workflows and real‑world patient and practice⏎needs, which undermines adoption, effectiveness, and physician buy‑in.⏎The AAFP believes HHS agencies should embed practicing physicians throughout the AI lifecycle,⏎establish advisory panels that include primary care physicians to ensure AI tools are aligned⏎with real-world clinical workflows and patient needs, and invest in testbeds and pilot programs⏎that simulate clinical environments, particularly in primary care settings. The AAFP stands ready⏎to collaborate on initiatives that evaluate the safety, usability, and effectiveness of AI tools in⏎family medicine.⏎To support embedding practicing physicians into the AI lifecycle, the AAFP is establishing the⏎Primary Care Innovation Network (PCIN) to create a sustainable, physician‑led mechanism that⏎connects frontline primary care teams with developers of AI and digital health solutions. The⏎purpose of PCIN is to ensure that AI adoption in primary care does not repeat the missteps of⏎prior health IT waves—particularly the lack of physician involvement that contributed to poor⏎usability and increased administrative burden. Through a governance structure led by primary⏎care physicians and supported by technology innovators, the PCIN seeks to strengthen primary⏎care by ensuring AI tools meaningfully advance access, outcomes, patient experience, and⏎workforce satisfaction.⏎PCIN is designed to accelerate responsible and trustworthy AI integration in primary care by⏎fostering real‑world testing environments; aligning innovators with actual care‑delivery needs;⏎and ensuring transparency, ethical design, and equitable implementation. Expected outcomes⏎include empowering clinicians as organizational AI leaders, improving the alignment of⏎technology development with high‑impact primary care needs, and supporting national efforts⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 3 of 11⏎to create safe, effective, human-centered AI systems. PCIN’s activities, such as innovation⏎accelerators, collaborative research communities, and coordinated multi‑stakeholder leadership⏎efforts, provide a model for how federal agencies and policy can partner with and amplify⏎private-sector initiatives that are grounded in frontline care priorities. The AAFP would⏎welcome ASTP/ONC’s support and involvement as we facilitate the meaningful collaboration⏎of family physicians and technology developers to improve the practice and delivery of⏎primary care for patients and physicians.⏎Regulation⏎The AAFP strongly supports patients’ ability to access and use their health information to inform⏎care decisions, power digital tools, and share data with their care teams in ways that safeguard⏎their privacy. We have consistently supported regulatory efforts that advance patient access to⏎their health data. However, while patients’ access to electronic health information (EHI) has⏎expanded rapidly in recent years, corresponding technologies that enable physician practices to⏎manage and operationalize this increased data flow have not advanced at the same pace. This⏎challenge is heightened by the lack of mechanisms to reliably segment data according to laws,⏎regulations, and patient sharing preferences, which are essential for ensuring that health⏎information is exchanged in compliance with the specific privacy obligations attached to each⏎data element.⏎While deregulation can reduce developer burden and lower barriers to market entry for new AI⏎products, it shifts significant responsibility onto purchasers to evaluate whether these tools⏎meet required functionality and interoperability standards. This shift disproportionately affects⏎low‑resourced practices, placing a heavier burden on rural and safety‑net health care⏎organizations that may lack the capacity to conduct thorough product assessments.⏎As AI systems become deeply integrated into clinical and operational environments, their⏎exposure to sophisticated cyber threats presents an increasing risk to patient safety and⏎organizational integrity. Family physicians depend on AI tools that are secure by design, yet they⏎often lack visibility into the system’s vulnerabilities or have control over its defenses. AI⏎developers and technology organizations, not physicians or health systems, are best positioned⏎to mitigate and respond to cyberattacks given their direct access to system architecture, code,⏎and infrastructure. The AAFP recommends HHS strengthen regulatory guardrails to ensure that⏎developers bear primary responsibility for securing AI systems against intrusions, data⏎exfiltration, and model manipulation. Holding physician practices liable for breaches or system⏎failures they cannot control would undermine trust and deter adoption. Robust, enforceable⏎cybersecurity requirements for developers, aligned with federal health data protections, are⏎essential to protect patients, sustain trust in AI, and accelerate responsible innovation in health⏎care.⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 4 of 11⏎We encourage HHS to work across its agencies and throughout the federal government to⏎harmonize existing guidance and resources into a single federal framework for AI usage in⏎clinical care. In addition to ASTP/ONC, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS),⏎the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), the National Institute of Standards and Technology⏎(NIST), the Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA), and other agencies will be⏎essential to ensure consistent governance and oversight of AI tools appropriately spans the⏎entire federal government.⏎Reimbursement⏎The same market forces that created the current health IT landscape still prioritize⏎administrative efficiency over clinical functionality. This misalignment leaves family physicians⏎without the tools they need to deliver optimal patient care. Without addressing the underlying⏎business incentives that shape the design, deployment, and use of health technologies, AI⏎developers and health care organizations are likely to follow the same trajectory as electronic⏎health records (EHRs), repeating existing shortcomings rather than advancing care delivery. The⏎AAFP believes that the fee-for-service (FFS) model will continue to drive the creation of AI tools⏎to maximize utilization, instead of AI tools to actually improve patient care. As such, we urge⏎HHS to continue pursuing the transition to value-based payment (VBP) models that align⏎financial incentives with high-quality care and improved affordability of care across all⏎stakeholders. This will drive the market to adopt AI solutions that drive quality care and cost-⏎effectiveness.⏎Despite overwhelming evidence that primary care is the only health care component where an⏎increased supply is associated with better population health and improved patient outcomes,⏎the United States continues to underinvest in this cornerstone of the health system.i In many⏎large organizations, primary care is still treated as a loss leader used to generate referrals to⏎high-margin specialty and hospital services. The policy challenge, and opportunity, is to identify⏎how AI can support greater investment in primary care, such as by enabling meaningful,⏎low‑burden reporting measures and supporting practices in delivering the four key functions⏎defined by Dr. Barbara Starfield: first contact, continuity, coordination, and comprehensiveness.⏎We encourage HHS to focus its efforts and resources on how AI tools can meaningfully⏎strengthen primary care in the U.S. and improve patient outcomes.⏎Research and Development⏎The AAFP strongly believes that AI tools should enhance, not burden, clinical practice. We⏎support HHS prioritizing research on AI products that streamline documentation, reduce⏎administrative burden, and support clinical decision-making. If implemented and managed⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 5 of 11⏎appropriately, the AAFP believes AI could deepen, not disintermediate, the patient-physician⏎relationship by allowing physicians to spend more time engaging with their patients instead of⏎clicking boxes on a screen.⏎The AAFP strongly believes AI tools must be developed and deployed in ways that reduce health⏎disparities and promote equity. The AAFP believes companies providing AI tools must also⏎address implicit bias in their design. While implicit bias cannot always be eliminated, companies⏎should have standard processes to identify and mitigate the AI tool learning those same biases.⏎In addition, when applicable, companies should have processes for monitoring for differential⏎outcomes, particularly those that affect vulnerable patient populations. We recommend HHS⏎fund research on bias detection and mitigation, support the use of diverse and representative⏎datasets, and engage communities in participatory research models.⏎Additionally, we support investments in training and education to prepare the health care⏎workforce for AI integration. High upfront and ongoing costs may make AI tools inaccessible to⏎independent, safety-net, or rural physicians, which could negatively impact patient care.⏎Further, limited access to AI could make clinician recruitment even harder at these practices and⏎lead to less representative data collection. As AI tools continue being integrated into clinical⏎workflows, it’s crucial for the federal government, health care systems and hospitals, and other⏎stakeholders to work together to ensure physicians have the AI literacy and AI-adjacent skills⏎they need to be successful, including data stewardship, workflow design, and informatics⏎abilities.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎There are several significant barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care, which⏎include but are not limited to the following:⏎• Limited AI literacy among both end‑users and purchasers. This knowledge gap⏎contributes to hesitancy in evaluating, procuring, and effectively implementing emerging⏎AI tools.⏎• The complexity of cleaning and preparing health data, as well as the limited⏎generalizability of AI models due to wide variability in how data are captured and stored⏎across systems.⏎• The significant mismatch between the rapid AI innovation cycle – typically six to seven⏎months – and the much longer research and evidence‑generation cycle, which often⏎spans 18 to 36 months. This gap limits the timely validation of new AI tools and slows⏎their adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 6 of 11⏎• The absence of clear national guardrails governing the safe and ethical use of AI in⏎health care, coupled with the lack of a liability framework that reflects shared⏎responsibility between health care professionals and AI developers.⏎• Obstacles related to data access, interoperability, and variability in payer requirements.⏎These challenges impede the adoption of health IT solutions like AI tools, because AI⏎systems depend on interoperable, high-quality, structured data. Lack of broadband⏎access in rural and underserved communities also limits patients’ and physicians’ ability⏎to engage with digital solutions, including AI-enabled tools.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎The AAFP recommends modernizing payment policies so practices, especially small and⏎independent practices, can invest in the infrastructure needed for AI integration. Variability in⏎payer coverage and prior authorization requirements creates substantial administrative burden,⏎limiting physician ability and willingness to adopt new AI tools. We strongly support reducing⏎such variability, improving real-time access to patient data, and strengthening interoperability⏎requirements so AI tools can function reliably. Below are additional recommendations.⏎• Develop and publish a risk‑based framework for health IT, including AI, to provide clear⏎guidance to the private sector on assessing and managing risk associated with AI use in⏎clinical settings. The framework could serve as the foundation for a tiered approach to AI⏎oversight that appropriately balances innovation, patient safety, and accountability.⏎• Accelerate the transition to meaningful VBP models that genuinely reward improved⏎outcomes and lower costs, rather than programs that simply impose administrative⏎burdens for data submission without driving significant clinical change. This will align⏎financial incentives with high‑quality primary care, which improves outcomes and lowers⏎the total cost of care. Such alignment would encourage the development and adoption⏎of AI solutions that support care quality, efficiency, and improved patient outcomes. In⏎contrast, continued reliance on the FFS model will incentivize AI adoption primarily to⏎increase volume and utilization of high-margin services, rather than to improve access to⏎high-quality primary care.⏎• Develop and promote best practices for human‑AI collaboration in clinical care, informed⏎by recent NIST guidance, including establishing a clear liability framework that reflects⏎shared accountability between health care professionals and AI developers. From a⏎broad perspective, this could include collaborating with the Department of Justice on⏎the False Claims Act and the Federal Trade Commission on truth in advertising, as each⏎pertains to AI in health care.⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 7 of 11⏎• Invest in initiatives that advance AI transparency and explainability in order to build⏎workforce and public trust, support informed clinical use, and promote responsible⏎adoption of AI technologies in health care. This could include establishing a national⏎registry of AI solutions used in clinical care that publishes standardized transparency,⏎performance, and efficacy information to support physicians’ informed adoption,⏎oversight, and accountability when using these tools. This should build on, not replace,⏎existing transparency enabled by ASTP’s Decision Support Intervention requirements.⏎• Develop a standardized data set, similar to the Structured Product Labeling framework⏎used for medications, to promote consistency, interoperability, and reliable AI⏎development and usage across health care systems. Again, we would support this⏎building on, not replacing, existing transparency requirements. Adopting a Structured⏎Product Labeling-like approach for AI could mean requiring AI tools that integrate with⏎EHRs to provide source attributes and related information in consistent, interoperable,⏎and reliable structures so that this information can “surface” within EHRs.⏎• Seek NIST’s input in developing guidelines for post‑market surveillance of clinical AI⏎solutions, which would support ongoing monitoring of performance, safety, and⏎real‑world impact. While we acknowledge that NIST’s work is not sector-specific, the⏎agency is deeply resourced and knowledgeable on AI governance and monitoring, and⏎we believe there is much to be learned from their work and insight. Though FDA is⏎making strides towards this objective with its guidance around predetermined change⏎control plans, no similar guidance exists for non-device AI solutions. We therefore⏎believe this is particularly urgent for the large swath of AI tools that are viewed as non-⏎device clinical decision support by FDA or are not used for a medical purpose but⏎nevertheless can have a high impact on patient populations.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The AAFP remains deeply concerned about health data privacy, security, and the inadequacy of⏎current protections for tools and solutions developed outside the purview of the Health⏎Insurance Portability and Accountability Act’s (HIPAA) guidance. We urge HHS to strengthen⏎regulatory expectations for entities handling health-related data, including AI vendors not⏎subject to HIPAA, and to clarify responsibilities for cybersecurity, data stewardship, and⏎transparency of data use for these entities.⏎Separately, a core legal and implementation challenge for non‑medical device AI tools used in⏎clinical care is the absence of a standardized approach for assessing risk and determining the⏎appropriate level of oversight and governance. Without clear, consistent criteria to differentiate⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 8 of 11⏎low‑, moderate‑, and high‑risk AI applications, health care organizations and developers face⏎uncertainty that complicates deployment, procurement, and accountability. HHS should build⏎on the work initiated under the Food and Drug Administration Safety and Innovation Act⏎(FDASIA) and related health IT statutes to establish a robust, risk‑based framework for health IT,⏎including AI, that provides clear guidance to the private sector and serves as the foundation for⏎a tiered approach to oversight and governance proportional to clinical risk.⏎In addition, the lack of a clearly defined liability model for clinical AI remains a significant barrier⏎to adoption. In the absence of shared liability frameworks that appropriately allocate⏎responsibility among physicians, health care organizations, and AI developers, clinicians may be⏎reluctant to integrate AI tools into clinical workflows, even when such tools have the potential⏎to improve care quality and efficiency. HHS can play a critical convening and policy‑setting role⏎by facilitating the development of governance and liability models that promote accountability⏎while reducing uncertainty and supporting responsible innovation. While we acknowledge that⏎most current liability frameworks exist at the state level, not federal, the AAFP strongly⏎believes this is a crucial space for HHS to be engaged. We would support HHS collaborating⏎with the National Conference for State Legislatures (NCSL), the American Legislative Executive⏎Council (ALEC), and state medical societies to develop model liability legislation for AI⏎developers and health care professionals.⏎As noted above, many non-device AI tools may nevertheless pose substantial risk or have⏎substantial impact across a population. The absence of a widely endorsed risk framework and⏎guidance on post-market surveillance of non-device AI tools limits implementers’ ability to⏎select a reasonable strategy to monitor AI tools to ensure they are effective and do not⏎introduce new liability risks.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective⏎AI use in clinical care?⏎The AAFP supports industry-led and federal efforts to improve transparency and standardization⏎among AI products. Certified or accredited AI tools should be required to meet usability, data⏎governance, and patient privacy criteria. We would support HHS encouraging certifying bodies⏎to include primary care perspectives to help ensure AI tools are safe and usable in diverse⏎practice settings. Additionally, we support HHS collaborating with NIST and leveraging that⏎agency's expertise, technical credibility, and standards‑development experience to generate⏎guidance for both the public and private sectors on the responsible development, evaluation,⏎and testing of AI applications in health care. We recommend ASTP/ONC and HHS engage in a⏎national, consensus‑based effort to promote alignment between public policy objectives and⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 9 of 11⏎private‑sector initiatives related to AI tools’ evaluation, accreditation, and certification in health⏎care.⏎The AAFP would strongly support ASTP/ONC’s increased engagement in private sector-led⏎coalitions that are already doing meaningful work regarding AI accreditation, certification,⏎testing, credentialing, and governance. We participate in many groups that are engaged in this⏎important work, and we would welcome ASTP/ONC’s participation, guidance, and support. Both⏎the Council for Medical Specialty Societies (CMSS) and the National Academies of Science,⏎Engineering, and Medicine (NASEM), among many others, have health care-focused AI⏎workgroups that have developed or are in the process of developing accreditation, certification,⏎and credentialing frameworks that would benefit from increased federal engagement and⏎insight. We urge ASTP/ONC to consider joining these collaborative efforts, at least as federal⏎observers. We strongly agree with the Administration that voluntary, private sector coalitions⏎can move more quickly than the federal government in many instances, but the government’s⏎perspective and support is still very much needed and wanted. We also believe that consistent⏎engagement with private coalitions will be essential to target and design effective policies and⏎that policymakers could learn from ongoing initiatives. The AAFP believes it is crucial for⏎ASTP/ONC to partner more closely with private sector coalitions on AI – both financially and⏎intellectually – and we are eager to help facilitate closer collaboration in this space.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would⏎have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality,⏎and help reduce costs?⏎AAFP members frequently describe shortcomings in digital tools that fail to integrate with⏎existing workflows or provide complete patient information. AI tools present similar risks if⏎interoperability and data availability are insufficient. The primary care workforce needs AI tools⏎that reduce documentation burden, surface actionable clinical insights, support population⏎health management, and improve care coordination. Two use cases where AI tools have been⏎showing great promise in helping primary care physicians and practices deliver more cost-⏎effective, higher-quality care are AI ambient listening tools for documentation purposes and⏎tools that can accurately summarize the EHR, which can improve appropriate and timely care⏎and expand the capacity of physicians and other health care professionals. Most importantly in⏎primary care settings, these tools support more meaningful interactions between patients and⏎their physician-led care teams by allowing clinicians to focus on the patient – not the computer⏎– during their limited interactions. Additionally, we are starting to see AI applied to accessing⏎and summarizing medical research. To fully realize the benefits of these summary capabilities,⏎within both EHRs and research publications, the AAFP believes these currently siloed solutions⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 10 of 11⏎must be integrated to improve physicians' ability to make thorough, timely, and evidence-based⏎clinical decisions for their patients.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Thanks to the broad range of environments our members practice in, the AAFP knows that the⏎decision-makers and governance bodies for adopting AI tools in health care organizations vary⏎widely depending on their organizational resource depth and decision-making structures. In⏎large health systems and hospitals, there are often ample resources and multiple teams of⏎people with various areas of expertise (IT, finance, legal, clinical) that contribute to decision-⏎making, with frontline physicians too often excluded from these discussions. In small, physician-⏎owned practices, resources are more limited, and physicians are often the sole decision-makers.⏎Family physicians, particularly those in small and independent practices, have consistently⏎shared that excessive administrative burden reduces their bandwidth to research and assess⏎new AI and other digital health tools. The AAFP believes clear standards, streamlined⏎administrative requirements, and investment in small practices’ infrastructure and education⏎are essential to increasing the adoption of AI solutions in clinical care.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎A robust health information exchange environment supported by all ecosystem actors is⏎essential to aggregating patient data at the scale and completeness required for reliable⏎implementation of AI in clinical care. This ecosystem encompasses health information exchange⏎entities; all points of patient care (e.g., clinics, hospitals, labs, surgery centers, and pharmacies);⏎payers; and, increasingly, patients themselves, as patient‑generated data become more⏎prevalent and integral to care delivery. For family physicians and other clinicians, the EHR is the⏎primary mechanism for delivering vast amounts of information to inform care⏎recommendations. EHRs must facilitate full accessibility to vast amounts of patient data on a⏎timely and efficient basis—including scanned documents, lab results, free text, and⏎unstructured formats. We urge HHS to ensure EHR vendors provide system‑wide access to⏎patient data and adopt modern application programming interfaces (APIs). The AAFP also⏎supports:⏎• Robust enforcement of information blocking provisions, particularly for EHR developers⏎that impose unnecessary technical, contractual, or administrative barriers. This would⏎expand access to health data for third‑party developers and enable the development of⏎interoperable AI tools that deliver meaningful value to physicians and practices.⏎⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 11 of 11⏎• Expanding certified electronic health record technology (CEHRT) interoperability⏎requirements to include standardized write-based APIs that enable authorized⏎third‑party applications to write data back into the EHR. This would support seamless⏎integration into clinician workflows and improve usability for physicians and other end-⏎users.⏎• Moving beyond a singular focus on exchange standards and instead require EHR⏎functionality that supports a robust ecosystem of third‑party applications. Certain⏎developers have demonstrated that enabling this level of integration can significantly⏎lower the cost and complexity of third‑party connectivity with CEHRT, which can⏎accelerate time‑to‑market for innovative tools.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption⏎of AI as part of clinical care?⏎The AAFP encourages HHS to prioritize research in several key areas to accelerate the safe and⏎effective adoption of AI in clinical care, including: the impact of AI on primary care workflows,⏎administrative burden, and clinician usability; strategies to identify and mitigate bias in⏎AI‑enabled tools; patient‑facing AI applications that support self‑management and improve⏎access to care; and best practices for integrating AI solutions within existing interoperability⏎frameworks, such as the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA).⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide written comments on this important topic and its⏎potential impact on primary care access in the U.S. We appreciate ASTP/ONC and HHS’⏎commitment to patient-centered technology policy, and we stand ready to collaborate on next⏎steps. For more information or questions, please contact Mandi Neff, Senior Strategist,⏎Regulatory and Policy, at mneff2@aafp.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jen Brull, MD, FAAFP⏎Board Chair⏎American Academy of Family Physicians⏎i National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine. Implementing High-Quality Primary⏎Care: Rebuilding the Foundation of Health Care. Washington, DC: The National Academies Press.⏎2021. https://doi.org/10.17226/25983.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0330♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from EMPOWERED-HOME.COM INC., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EMPOWERED-HOME.COM INC.♔♔♔♔♔♔RFI Response: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care⏎Responding Company: Empowered-Home.com, Inc. (dba Empowered-Home)⏎SAM.gov ID: RCUJZP4AECT4⏎CAGE #: 0QMH4⏎CONTACT INFO: wpistor@empowered-home.com | +1 408-781-4353⏎______________________________________________________________________⏎____________⏎Agencies: Department of Health and Human Services Office of the Secretary⏎Document Citation: 90 FR 60108⏎Document Number: 2025-23641⏎Document Type: Notice⏎Pages: 60108-60110 (3 pages)⏎Publication Date: 12/23/2025⏎RIN: 0955-AA13⏎Public feedback will inform HHS-wide use of three different approaches: regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development. In general, HHS seeks feedback on ways⏎in which these approaches can be most effectively applied to support the rapid⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care, to foster public trust and confidence in⏎modern technology solutions, to reduce uncertainty that impedes AI innovation, and to⏎align federal incentives so that AI is deployed in ways that enhance productivity, reduce⏎burden, lower health care costs, and improve health outcomes for patients, caregivers,⏎and communities.⏎Hypothetically, if a Payer is taking financial risk for the long-term health and health costs⏎of an individual, that payer will have an inherent incentive to promote access to the⏎highest-value interventions for patients. We seek feedback on payment policy⏎changes that ensure payers have the incentive and ability to promote access to high-⏎⏎value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders,⏎and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care.⏎Specific Questions⏎In addition to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, HHS seeks input on the following specific⏎questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The private sector's ability to innovate in AI for healthcare is hindered by a complex interplay of⏎regulatory, technical, economic, and human factors. These barriers not only slow the⏎development of new AI tools but also impede their adoption and integration into clinical care.⏎1. Regulatory and Compliance Hurdles⏎Stringent regulations, while essential for patient safety, create lengthy approval processes⏎that delay innovation. For instance, the FDA approval timeline for AI models often spans 12-⏎18 months, which is seen as excessively slow compared to private sector norms. This⏎regulatory uncertainty stifles rapid iteration and deployment, with stakeholders calling for⏎clearer frameworks to balance innovation with risk management.⏎2. Data Access, Privacy, and Quality Issues⏎AI relies on vast, high-quality datasets, but healthcare data is often siloed, heterogeneous,⏎and protected by privacy regulations, making it difficult for private innovators to train robust⏎models. Over one-third of life sciences professionals report data accuracy, security, and⏎quality as major obstacles, with issues like biased or incomplete datasets leading to⏎unreliable AI outputs. In clinical settings, limited access to anonymized patient data hinders⏎model evaluation and refinement, contributing to products that don't meet clinician⏎expectations. A 2025/26 report highlights that 52% of organizations view data quality and⏎availability as primary barriers to AI adoption across sectors, including healthcare.⏎Empowered-Home addresses these issues with a HIPAA compliant architecture that⏎deidentifies the patient from the PHI when querying the LLM for correlations and insights⏎and reassembles the response in a secure framework. Privacy follows patient individual⏎right of access allowing them to consent to limited view sharing of their vital trends with non-⏎licensed caregivers. They can also consent to have their compendium displayed in the⏎clinician dashboard to be shared with specialists as well as the sponsoring Primary Care⏎Physician.⏎Quality issues of LLM hallucination are addressed be controlling the source information that⏎the LLM can query to peer reviewed medical journals or other trusted sources. In addition,⏎we use BERT that dramatically improves accuracy for large language models. It is an⏎⏎architecture that incorporates Bidirectional Encoder Representations from Transformers⏎(BERT) that learns to represent text as a sequence of vectors using self-supervised⏎learning. It uses the encoder-only transformer architecture that the LLM can understand.⏎Finally, we have the ability to use consensus-based reasoning whereby we can incorporate⏎more than one LLM connection. If two disagree, a third model can be queried to find⏎consensus in truth.⏎Sources:⏎• https://www.weforum.org/stories/2026/01/digital-solutions-and-ai-in-healthcare⏎• https://intuitionlabs.ai/articles/ai-adoption-life-sciences-barriers⏎• https://downloads.regulations.gov/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0031/attachment_1.pdf⏎3. Reimbursement and Economic Challenges⏎Misaligned financial incentives, where payment systems reward treatment over prevention,⏎discourage investment in preventive AI tools. High development costs and unclear return on⏎investment (ROI) affect 51% of organizations, despite 42% reporting strong returns from AI.⏎Legacy reimbursement models fail to cover many AI interventions, limiting affordability and⏎scalability. Funding shortages and capital allocation trade-offs further impede private sector⏎progress, as organizations struggle to justify AI investments amid competing priorities.⏎Sources:⏎• https://downloads.regulations.gov/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0045/attachment_2.pdf⏎• https://pratikmistry.medium.com/the-main-barriers-slowing-ai-adoption-in-healthcare-in-2026-based-on-⏎research-36552cbb9ea9⏎• https://www.jdsupra.com/legalnews/reminder-comments-to-the-hhs-request-9258539⏎• https://www.hinshawlaw.com/en/insights/healthcare-alert/hhs-seeks-input-on-adoption-of-ai-in-clinical-⏎care-and-strengthening-supply-chain-resilience⏎• https://www.forbes.com/sites/johnbremen/2026/02/17/overcoming-barriers-to-ai-adoption-in-2026/⏎D. Integration and Infrastructure Limitations⏎Outdated legacy IT systems in healthcare facilities present significant obstacles to⏎integrating AI into workflows which is why Empowered-Home uses a web-based app for⏎clinicians that will work in any secure browser like Chrome. Challenges include poor⏎interoperability, data fragmentation, and the need for substantial infrastructure upgrades,⏎which are acute in rural or under-resourced settings where IT connectivity lags by up to 64%⏎compared to urban areas. This results in many AI tools that are difficult to deploy at scale,⏎with 67% of implementations in U.S. hospitals misaligned with actual needs like addressing⏎provider shortages.⏎Empowered-Home is architected in a way that makes sharing patients’ vitals trends, clinical⏎risk alerts, and correlative insights, ubiquitous. We use CDS Hooks to integrate with existing⏎EMR workflows and connect to the EMR systems through FHIR. We use an Interoperability⏎as a Service platform to handle file format conversion to and from FHIR. We also use the⏎same platform to connect to the health record ecosystem through QHINs (Commonwell⏎Health Alliance, CareQuality, and eHealth Exchange) combined with a Record Locator⏎⏎Service (RLS) to discover patient health records scattered across the ecosystem. This⏎enriches the patient record of the sponsoring physician adding value the continuous data we⏎collect on patients.⏎Sources:⏎• https://pratikmistry.medium.com/the-main-barriers-slowing-ai-adoption-in-healthcare-in-2026-based-on-⏎research-36552cbb9ea9⏎• https://www.weforum.org/stories/2026/01/digital-solutions-and-ai-in-healthcare⏎E. Workforce Resistance and Skills Gaps⏎Clinicians often resist AI due to concerns over safety, role changes, and lack of trust,⏎compounded by burnout and time constraints. Organizational skill deficits in AI—both⏎technical and soft skills—are cited by many leaders as a leading barrier. Change⏎management issues, including inadequate training and governance, further slow adoption.⏎At Empowered-Home, we took these issues into account when designing our modern⏎Remote Health Monitoring as a Service platform. Because the interface is so intuitive,⏎unfamiliar users can start using it without problems, immediately. The graphic design is⏎such that it fosters trust because vitals trends can be easily viewed and drilled into. Problem⏎areas are identified with color coding that match with the AI insights and correlations.⏎Physicians can assign roles and responsibilities to their clinician team members with access⏎controls and audit logs of who made what changes to vital thresholds (for example). The⏎platform can be used to enhance the training and ability for clinicians to conduct effective⏎triage with the AI supporting them along the way. Urban clinicians can augment rural teams⏎enhancing clinician support and training, allowing clinicians to work at the top of their⏎license.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations.⏎Key Priorities for HHS to Incentivize Effective AI Use in Clinical Care⏎A. Regulatory Changes⏎Establish Proportionate-to-Risk Oversight and Expedited Approval Pathways. HHS should⏎prioritize creating flexible regulatory frameworks under the FDA and ONC that differentiate⏎between high-risk (e.g., autonomous decision-making AI) and low-risk applications (e.g.,⏎supportive tools for risk stratification or clinical decision support – Empowered-Home’s⏎model), including expedited reviews for validated AI tools. This includes issuing guidance on⏎⏎human-in-the-loop requirements, bias assessments, and post-deployment monitoring to⏎clarify accountability without prescriptive mandates.⏎Why? Current ambiguity and lengthy approvals stifle private sector innovation, deter⏎partnerships, and delay patient access to tools that could improve diagnostic accuracy and⏎reduce errors during triage. Proportionate regulation builds trust, prevents privacy breaches⏎(e.g., HIPAA 45 CFR Parts 160/164). It also avoids repeating EHR mandate pitfalls where⏎heavy-handed rules favored compliance over outcomes. That lead to billions in spending⏎without health improvements.⏎B. Enhance Data Interoperability and Privacy Standards:⏎Update regulations to mandate standardized, secure data sharing via encrypted⏎infrastructure in a FHIR framework to reduce the expense load on data transformation! All⏎EMR systems should be required to produce and accept FHIR compatible document⏎formats. No more CCDAs in XML or Discharge Summaries in HL7v2! Those are OLD data⏎formats and standards. It should also include patient-mediated access to complete records⏎while strengthening HIPAA protections against data misuse in AI training. That’s what⏎Empowered-Home does.⏎Why? Fragmented data silos across 400+ EHR systems hinder AI model training and real-⏎world performance, exacerbating biases and limiting adoption in diverse populations. Clear⏎standards would enable organic innovation, protect patient privacy, and create liquid⏎datasets for better personalization, ultimately lowering costs and improving equity.⏎Empowered-Home is architected to store the records in the patient’s own, personal Apple⏎Health account. Not all vitals and health data from devices maps 1:1 into Apple Health, so⏎we do the LOINC code mapping and ensure its stored neatly there for the patient to take⏎with them.⏎C. Payment Policy Changes⏎Shift to Outcomes-Oriented, Value-Based Reimbursement Models: HHS, through CMS,⏎should modernize legacy fee-for-service systems to reward AI-enabled activities that⏎demonstrate cost reductions or improved outcomes, such as preventive care, chronic⏎disease management, and reduced hospitalizations. This includes creating temporary⏎Medicare codes for AI tools, incentivizing payers to cover high-value interventions, and⏎avoiding separate billing for AI to prevent inflation.⏎Why? Traditional models incentivize treatment over prevention, externalizing savings and⏎discouraging AI investments despite proven benefits like lower ED visits and enhanced⏎efficiency (e.g., 40-50% of physicians already use AI organically, but not necessarily safely).⏎Reforms would align incentives for affordability, foster competition among developers, and⏎accelerate access, potentially reducing healthcare's 18% GDP share while improving patient⏎experiences.⏎⏎D. Incentivize Adoption Through Augmented Reimbursement and Pilots:⏎Introduce financial bonuses for providers using AI in quality workflows such as Medicare⏎Advantage or ACOs; and penalties for non-adoption, while ensuring inclusion of diverse⏎stakeholders like Certified Registered Nurse Anesthetists (CRNAs) in policy development.⏎Why? Without incentives, short-term costs deter uptake, especially in under-resourced⏎settings; targeted rewards would de-risk early adoption, promote equity, and generate⏎evidence for scaling, addressing barriers like administrative burden and ensuring AI⏎supports rather than replaces clinicians.⏎E. Develop Standardized Evaluation Frameworks and Transparency Measures:⏎Create industry-aligned tools for assessing AI validity, workflow integration, and patient-⏎facing disclosures, with HHS publishing top needs and preferred product lists to guide⏎adoption.⏎Why? Fragmented evaluations cause duplication and delays. Standardized, transparent⏎processes would ease procurement, enhance trust (e.g., plain-language disclosures), and⏎mitigate risks like hallucinations or biases, ensuring AI prioritizes patient safety and equity.⏎HHS Regulations, Policies, and Programs to Revisit for Enhancing AI Capabilities⏎in Clinical Care⏎1. HIPAA Privacy and Security Rules (45 CFR Parts 160 and 164)⏎Specific Changes: Update guidance to explicitly address AI-specific scenarios, such as⏎using de-identified or synthetic PHI for model training and inference. Clarify that sharing⏎anonymized datasets for AI development in secure environments (e.g., data trusts or⏎federated learning) is permissible under business associate agreements, with strengthened⏎audit requirements. Make the HIPAA Security Rule requirements voluntary for low-risk AI⏎applications to reduce compliance burdens, while mandating risk assessments for AI⏎handling PHI. Additionally, revise the Breach Notification Rule (45 CFR § 164.404) to⏎exempt or streamline reporting for breaches affecting fewer than 500 individuals if they⏎involve AI-generated insights rather than raw PHI.⏎Rationale: Current ambiguities hinder AI development by limiting access to diverse datasets⏎needed for robust models, potentially biasing outputs to companies with fewer financial⏎resources slowing innovation. Clearer rules would allow AI to process aggregated data for⏎clinical insights (e.g., predicting disease risks) while protecting privacy, fostering trust and⏎⏎accelerating adoption. This aligns with calls to prevent PHI disclosure to general-purpose AI⏎without governance.⏎2. Confidentiality of Substance Use Disorder Records (42 CFR Part 2)⏎Specific Changes: Align 42 CFR Part 2 with HIPAA standards by removing restrictions on⏎sharing substance use disorder (SUD) records for AI-driven care coordination and research,⏎so long as patient consent is obtained and data is de-identified wherever possible. Revise §⏎2.11 to expand definitions of "qualified service organizations" to include AI developers under⏎strict privacy safeguards, and update consent requirements (§ 2.31) to allow electronic or AI-⏎assisted consents for data use in predictive analytics.⏎Rationale: The current patchwork limits comprehensive datasets for AI training, impeding⏎tools for holistic care (e.g., integrating SUD data with mental health AI models).⏎Harmonization would enable AIs to provide more accurate, integrated clinical support,⏎reducing barriers for hospitals and improving outcomes in behavioral health without new⏎mandates.⏎3. ONC Health IT Certification Program (45 CFR Part 170)⏎Specific Changes: In § 170.315(b)(11), remove or simplify the "model card" transparency⏎requirements for AI-driven Decision Support Interventions (DSIs), limiting them to high-risk⏎applications only. Reserve or eliminate expired criteria like § 170.315(a)(9) for clinical⏎decision support (CDS), and revise § 170.315(a)(5) to drop non-essential patient⏎observation data elements (§ 170.315(a)(5)(i)(D)-(H)) unless tied to specific program⏎measures. Promote voluntary certification for AI interoperability, incorporating standards like⏎FHIR for AI data exchange.⏎Rationale: Overly prescriptive criteria burden developers and limit flexible AI integration into⏎workflows. Deregulation would allow AIs to evolve rapidly (e.g., real-time CDS without⏎redundant documentation), reducing costs and enabling broader use in certified health IT.⏎An example of this is the HTI-5 Proposed Rule's focus on prosperity through reduced⏎oversight.⏎4. Common Rule for Human Subjects Research (45 CFR Part 46)⏎Specific Changes: Reexamine the definition of "identifiable private information" under §⏎46.102(e)(7) to account for AI's ability to re-identify data through dataset combinations,⏎requiring periodic reviews every two years and mandating advanced de-identification⏎techniques like differential privacy for AI research datasets.⏎Rationale: Evolving AI capabilities challenge outdated identifiability concepts, restricting⏎research data for model training. Updates would facilitate ethical AI development such as⏎⏎using anonymized EHR data for diagnostic tools, fulfilling the Common Rule's commitment⏎to adapt to technology while protecting subjects.⏎5. FDA Medical Device Regulations (21 CFR Parts 801, 803, 807, 820, and 830)⏎Specific Changes: Clarify guidance for AI/ML software as a medical device (SaMD) by⏎creating expedited pathways under for low-risk AI updates such as continuous learning⏎models like Empowered-Home without full premarket review, emphasizing post-market⏎surveillance (§ 803). Revise Quality System Regulations (§ 820) to include AI-specific risk⏎management, such as bias audits, while exempting non-diagnostic AI tools from device⏎classification if they support human review.⏎Rationale: Ambiguous pathways slow AI deployment in clinical settings. Streamlined⏎processes would enable AIs to assist in diagnostics or monitoring more readily, balancing⏎innovation with safety as per FDA's evolving framework.⏎In summary, these revisions would create a more enabling environment for AI in clinical care by⏎prioritizing risk-proportionate oversight and data liquidity. HHS should collaborate with⏎stakeholders via pilots or sandboxes to test changes, ensuring they enhance desired⏎capabilities—such as generating evidence-based insights—while maintaining equity and trust.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS⏎play to help address them?⏎Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical Device AI in Clinical Care⏎• Liability uncertainty: Who is responsible for AI errors—developer, clinician, or hospital?⏎It's unclear who bears liability for harms—clinicians who use it, developers who design it,⏎hospitals who buy it, or AI vendors create it. HHS should provide clearer frameworks⏎and safe harbors to promote innovation during this critical time.⏎• Indemnification gaps: Non-standard contracts and uneven risk distribution. HHS should⏎provide best-practice guidance and Task Force expansion.⏎• Privacy challenges: Re-identification risks, consent gaps, outdated HIPAA for AI⏎training/ HHS should provide us with targeted de-identification rules and secure data-use⏎standards.⏎⏎• Security vulnerabilities: Model attacks, amplified breach risks. Non-device AI often lacks⏎mandatory FDA cybersecurity guidance. HHS can help by providing us with updated⏎breach notification requirements and safety monitoring.⏎• Bias/discrimination: Inequitable outcomes from skewed data. HHS can help us with⏎funded audits, transparency mandates, and civil rights enforcement.⏎• Broader trust/implementation friction: Opacity, workforce resistance, regulatory⏎fragmentation. HHS can help by improving explainability, literacy/training, and⏎harmonized rules across stakeholders.⏎Recommended Role for HHS in Addressing These Issues⏎HHS should issue targeted guidance and standards—such as AI-specific HIPAA updates for de-⏎identification and breach notifications, plus clear liability frameworks with safe harbors for vetted⏎tools—to reduce uncertainty around privacy, security, and responsibility.⏎Additionally, HHS can promote accountability to cover liability and indemnification best⏎practices. Liability insurance is very expensive and hurts real innovation coming from startups.⏎HHS could help fund pilots, mandating explainability certification, fostering state-industry⏎collaboration and workforce training grants. It’s also advisable that HHS establishes an AI⏎Safety Program to monitor incidents and enforce equity and nondiscrimination.⏎HHS involvement as a supporter of AI innovation would directly enable more companies⏎specializing in AI-powered remote health monitoring solutions like Empowered-Home to move⏎quickly and gain higher adoption that meets the goals of HHS. They can achieve this by⏎reducing regulatory uncertainty and privacy/security barriers for their AI-driven data from⏎devices, clarifying responsibility in multi-party care scenarios (e.g., clinicians vs. developers for⏎monitoring errors), facilitating easier EHR integration through required FHIR interoperability⏎between EMR systems, and bias mitigation in diverse populations like rural or maternal groups.⏎They can also help tremendously by providing funding for pilots aligned with rural health⏎initiatives, and aligning reimbursement models (e.g., CMS value-based care) to incentivize⏎adoption -- ultimately allowing faster innovation, scaling, and broader deployment.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Promising AI Evaluation Methods for Non-Medical Devices in Clinical Care⏎⏎Non-medical device AI—tools like predictive analytics, workflow optimization, or decision⏎support software (not classified as medical devices)—should require evaluation to ensure⏎safety, equity, and effectiveness in clinical settings. The most promising methods emphasize⏎iterative, risk-proportionate approaches that integrate technical validation with real-world⏎human-centered evaluation. These align with principles from organizations like the American⏎Heart Association (AHA) and address gaps in traditional metrics by accounting for expert⏎variability and contextual shifts. Below, are outlined key methods, categorized by phase and⏎type.⏎Pre-deployment methods⏎• Validation on diverse datasets representing rural, maternal, senior, and underserved⏎populations.⏎• Robustness testing against real-world variations such as inconsistent wearable data⏎from inferior quality devices (we’ve done a lot of testing), connectivity issues in rural⏎areas, and environmental noise.⏎• Human-centered evaluations like usability testing, cognitive load assessment, pilot⏎programs that can scale from small to large population sets.⏎Post-deployment methods focus on continuous monitoring:⏎• Performance tracking from discharge to stable state, care plan management and⏎tracking to improve outcomes of health.⏎• Real-world outcome linkage (e.g., readmissions, time-to-decision, clinician satisfaction).⏎• Accountability metrics and understanding of patient population improvements.⏎HHS should further support these processes to bridge gaps in infrastructure, standardization,⏎and evidence for non-regulated AI, accelerating safe adoption while reducing uncertainty for⏎developers and providers.⏎Grants to fund real-world pilots, longitudinal monitoring frameworks, robustness studies in⏎diverse home settings, and human-centered evaluation tools.⏎Contracts for HHS-facilitated validation environments, standardized reporting templates, or⏎vendor-neutral testing platforms that could validate Empowered-Home's non-device AI in real⏎clinical workflows.⏎These mechanisms would build shared infrastructure, generate credible evidence, impact on ED⏎reduction and cost savings, reduce adoption uncertainty for providers, and align with HHS goals⏎for trustworthy AI in remote and home-based clinical care.⏎⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎HHS can best support private sector activities in accreditation, certification, industry-driven⏎testing, and credentialing for AI in clinical care by acting as a convener, validator, and funder⏎—fostering alignment without stifling innovation.⏎By leveraging these supports, HHS can reduce fragmentation, boost public trust, and drive⏎equitable AI adoption—directly empowering innovators like Empowered-Home to extend⏎clinical care into homes, aligning with the HHS AI Strategy for better outcomes and⏎affordability.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality,⏎and help reduce costs?⏎AI tools deployed in clinical care have shown mixed results: notable successes in narrow, high-⏎value applications contrasted with frequent shortfalls in broader integration and sustained⏎impact.⏎Where AI Has Met or Exceeded Expectations⏎• Ambient AI Scribes and Documentation Tools: These have significantly reduced⏎clinician "pajama time" (after-hours charting) by 40-60% in many deployments (e.g.,⏎Kaiser Permanente's large-scale rollout of Abridge across 40+ hospitals, Nuance DAX⏎Copilot, and Microsoft Dragon Copilot at Advocate Health). They improve workflow⏎efficiency, allow more patient-focused time, and deliver clear ROI through time savings⏎without harming care quality.⏎• Radiology and Imaging AI: Tools for detection, prioritization, and triage (e.g., Aidoc,⏎Rad AI, Google DeepMind for breast cancer) often match or exceed human accuracy⏎(e.g., 94% vs. 65% in lung nodule detection), reducing false negatives/positives,⏎speeding reads, and supporting better outcomes with fewer missed cases.⏎• Predictive Analytics and Early Detection: In areas like sepsis detection,⏎cardiovascular risk prediction, or antibiotic-resistant infections, AI has improved⏎accuracy, reduced readmissions/hospitalizations, and cut costs (e.g., preventing 200+⏎readmissions in some studies, saving millions annually). Broader estimates suggest⏎potential system-wide savings of $200-360 billion/year through optimized diagnostics,⏎fraud detection, and administrative automation.⏎Where AI Has Fallen Short⏎⏎• Broader Clinical Decision Support and Workflow Integration: Many tools fail to scale⏎due to legacy EHR fragmentation, poor interoperability, clinician resistance, silent⏎underuse, or lack of reimbursement pathways. Diagnostic reasoning AI often stalls, with⏎hallucinations or context loss leading to errors. Up to 80% of initiatives fail from⏎misalignment with billing/compliance systems, not technical flaws.⏎• Administrative and Operational Overhype: While scribes succeed, other applications⏎such as full predictive models or claims denial tools face trust issues, hidden costs, or⏎quiet failures where clinicians work around them, absorbing time savings into fuller⏎schedules rather than reinvesting in care.⏎• Equity and Real-World Variability: Biases persist in underrepresented data, and tools⏎underperform in diverse or rural settings without robust validation.⏎Novel AI Tools with Greatest Potential to Improve Outcomes, Quality Insights, and⏎Reduce Costs⏎Tools shifting toward preventive, home-based, and proactive care show strong promise,⏎especially for chronic conditions, aging populations, and value-based models:⏎• AI-Powered Remote Patient Monitoring (RPM) with Generative AI Summarization:⏎Continuous insights from wearables/in-home devices detect subtle changes early,⏎enabling proactive interventions, reducing ED visits/hospitalizations, and personalizing⏎care.⏎• Predictive Risk Stratification and Virtual Companions: For high-risk groups (e.g.,⏎seniors, maternal health, rural patients), these provide triage, emotional support, and⏎coordinated alerts, improving adherence, outcomes, and cost efficiency.⏎• Hybrid Clinical Decision Support: Evidence-based GenAI for triage, personalized⏎plans, and population health targeting high-risk cohorts.⏎AI in telemedicine is transforming rural healthcare by addressing longstanding barriers like⏎geographic isolation, specialist shortages, limited infrastructure, and workforce constraints. As⏎of early 2026, AI integration enhances remote consultations, diagnostics, monitoring, and triage,⏎making care more accessible, efficient, and proactive—especially for chronic conditions,⏎maternal health, seniors, and preventive services.⏎Key Benefits and Applications⏎• Improved Access and Early Detection: AI-powered tools analyze patient data (e.g.,⏎symptoms, imaging, wearables) during virtual visits to provide faster, more accurate⏎diagnostics and triage. This reduces unnecessary travel and enables early interventions⏎for issues like diabetic retinopathy, stroke, or chronic diseases—critical in areas with few⏎specialists.⏎⏎• Remote Patient Monitoring (RPM) and Predictive Analytics: AI processes real-time data⏎from in-home devices or wearables to detect subtle changes, predict risks (e.g.,⏎exacerbations), and alert providers. This supports continuous care without frequent in-⏎person visits, lowering hospitalizations and ED use.⏎• Decision Support and Second Opinions: AI offers real-time guidance to rural clinicians⏎(e.g., ambient documentation, pattern recognition in records, or virtual specialist-like⏎insights), reducing diagnostic errors and burnout while extending expertise across⏎distances.⏎• Hybrid and Mobile Models: Emerging approaches include AI-assisted mobile clinics⏎(e.g., electric vehicles with integrated devices for lab procedures and diagnostics) and⏎chatbots/virtual assistants for initial assessments, overcoming broadband limitations in⏎some cases via low-bandwidth tools.⏎• Administrative Efficiency: AI streamlines scheduling, claims, and follow-ups, freeing⏎rural providers for patient care.⏎Studies and reviews (e.g., systematic literature from 2025) highlight that combining AI with⏎telemedicine bridges gaps, improves screening/early detection, and enhances equity, with⏎potential for better long-term outcomes in underserved populations.⏎Real-World Examples and Progress⏎• AI-Enhanced Platforms: Tools like AI-driven second opinions or diagnostic aids help⏎rural providers access specialist-level insights quickly, as seen in deployments for⏎teledermatology or telestroke.⏎• Mobile AI Solutions: Projects like University of Florida's Multi-Tags (AI-guided lab⏎procedures in electric vehicles) and University of Michigan's AI-powered mobile clinics⏎enable hospital-level care in remote areas.⏎• Broader Trends: Rural hospitals adopt AI for virtual care extensions, revenue cycle⏎support, and hybrid models, with 2025-2026 trends emphasizing EHR integration,⏎specialized telemedicine, and cybersecurity.⏎Challenges persist, including data biases, transparency concerns, infrastructure (e.g.,⏎broadband gaps), ethical issues (e.g., unbiased algorithms), and implementation costs/barriers⏎in low-resource settings. Adoption often starts small (e.g., ambient AI for documentation) to⏎build trust and demonstrate value.⏎Connection to Empowered-Home.com⏎Empowered-Home.com stands out as a practical example tailored to rural needs. The platform⏎uses AI-powered monitoring that integrates consumer/medical wearables and in-home devices⏎for continuous insights, generating clinician summaries and predictive risk alerts. It focuses on⏎rural/remote patients, maternal health, seniors/assisted living, and chronic condition⏎⏎monitoring—extending care boundaries, enabling proactive interventions, reducing unnecessary⏎ED visits/hospitalizations, and supporting evidence-based, patient-centered decisions. By⏎combining IoT, generative AI, and clinical expertise, it aligns with high-impact telemedicine⏎trends, promoting wellness in areas with limited access while addressing cost and outcome⏎goals in value-based care models.⏎Overall, AI in rural telemedicine is accelerating in 2025-2026, with strong evidence of benefits in⏎access and efficiency. Equitable scaling requires addressing infrastructure, privacy, and⏎validation hurdles through partnerships and policy support.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎The roles, decision makers, or governing bodies with the most influence on AI adoption for⏎clinical care in healthcare organizations primarily include the following:⏎• C-suite executives (e.g., CEOs, CIOs, CMIOs, and Chief Strategy/Clinical Officers):⏎They control budgets, set strategic priorities, and drive centralized decisions on AI⏎investments and use cases, often outpacing general IT spending.⏎• Clinical leaders and department heads (e.g., physicians, chief medical officers, and⏎specialty leads): They influence adoption through workflow integration, trust-building,⏎and championing tools that fit clinical practice.⏎• IT and informatics leaders (e.g., CIOs, data science teams): They handle technical⏎feasibility, integration with EHRs, and infrastructure.⏎• Multidisciplinary AI governance committees (increasingly common): These include⏎compliance, IT, clinical operations, ethics, legal, and sometimes patient representatives;⏎they oversee evaluation, risk management, transparency, and post-deployment⏎monitoring.⏎• Healthcare managers/leaders and boards: They shape organizational culture, provide⏎support for training/change management, and align AI with strategy.⏎• Frontline clinicians (physicians and nurses) exert significant bottom-up influence via⏎acceptance, overrides, and feedback, but top-down leadership often determines scale.⏎Hospitals with professional managers (e.g., integrated salary models) tend to adopt⏎faster than physician-led ones.⏎Primary administrative hurdles to AI adoption in clinical care include:⏎• Data privacy, security, and regulatory compliance (e.g., HIPAA uncertainties, bias risks,⏎lack of clear governance): Cited by ~60% of leaders as top concerns, delaying⏎implementation.⏎⏎• Integration and interoperability issues with legacy EHRs and fragmented systems:⏎Hinders seamless workflow embedding.⏎• Lack of resources/infrastructure (e.g., skilled personnel shortages, poor IT readiness,⏎high costs): Limits evaluation, training, and scaling.⏎• Workflow misalignment and clinician mistrust/resistance (e.g., black-box concerns, fear⏎of de-skilling, over-reliance risks): Leads to underuse or abandonment despite technical⏎promise.⏎• Unclear reimbursement and ROI (e.g., no direct payment for many AI tools):⏎Disincentivizes investment amid misaligned incentives.⏎• Governance and evaluation gaps (e.g., fragmented monitoring, liability concerns): Slows⏎trust and sustained use.⏎These hurdles are often organizational/sociotechnical rather than purely technical, requiring⏎leadership buy-in, training, and standardized frameworks for progress.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would most dramatically widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate AI development for clinical care in the following high-impact areas, by enabling⏎seamless, standardized access to richer, longitudinal datasets across fragmented systems.⏎Primary Areas of Greatest Impact⏎• Comprehensive Longitudinal Patient Records: Aggregating EHR data (clinical notes,⏎medications, vitals, allergies), labs, imaging, genomics, social determinants, and claims⏎into unified views. This powers AI for predictive modeling (e.g., readmission risk,⏎personalized treatment), chronic disease management, and population health⏎analytics—unlocking massive markets in value-based care, preventive AI, and precision⏎medicine.⏎• Real-World Data for AI Training and Validation: Combining structured/unstructured⏎clinical data with RPM/wearables providing 24/7 continuous monitoring, telemedicine⏎feeds, and observational datasets. This fuels research on real-world outcomes, bias⏎detection, and generalizable models, while enabling scalable AI tools for early detection,⏎clinical decision support, and drug repurposing.⏎• Specialized Clinical Domains:⏎o Oncology/precision medicine: Shared genomic + treatment + outcomes data for⏎AI-driven therapies.⏎⏎o Behavioral/mental health: Longitudinal records with SDOH for predictive⏎interventions.⏎o Rural/remote and maternal health: 24/7 continuous RPM data augmented with⏎AI-triage supported telemedicine calls and proactive AI alerts based on clinical⏎severity to help clinicians prioritize patients.⏎• Administrative and Operational Data Flows: Claims, prior auths, scheduling, and⏎billing data for AI in revenue cycle optimization, fraud detection, and workflow⏎efficiency—creating new efficiency-focused markets.⏎Specific Data Standards Enabling This⏎• HL7 FHIR: Core for real-time, API-driven exchange; supports modular resources (e.g.,⏎Observation, DiagnosticReport, SBAR, Patient) and direct EHR querying by AI apps.⏎FHIR's flexibility accelerates LLM integration and app development.⏎• USCDI (United States Core Data for Interoperability): Mandated baseline (via⏎ONC/HTI rules) for allergies, meds, labs, notes, imaging metadata—ensures consistent,⏎nationwide access and reduces vendor-specific silos.⏎• OMOP Common Data Model: Standardizes observational/research data for large-scale⏎AI studies; supports NLP on unstructured text and federated analysis.⏎• Supporting terminologies: SNOMED CT, LOINC, ICD-10 for semantic alignment.⏎Benchmarking Tools and Platforms⏎• MedAgentBench / Virtual EHR Sims: Evaluate AI agents on FHIR APIs in realistic⏎workflows, testing interoperability, reasoning, and performance.⏎• qDAR (NIST): Measures data quality critical for AI reliability and bias assessment.⏎• BigDataBench / Open Leaderboards: Compare model robustness across⏎interoperable datasets, promoting transparent benchmarking.⏎Companies like Zen Healthcare IT exemplify how enhanced interoperability drives progress.⏎Zen provides the Gemini Integration-as-a-Service Platform (with tools like Stargate IHE⏎gateway, eHealth Exchange/Carequality connectivity) to simplify data exchange for digital health⏎vendors, HIEs, ACOs, payers, and providers. By enabling faster, secure connections to national⏎networks and supporting custom adapters/APIs, Zen reduces integration barriers—directly⏎widening market access for AI innovators, fueling research via better data liquidity, and⏎accelerating clinical AI deployment (as emphasized by Zen's president in discussions on AI's⏎dependence on interoperable data for improving outcomes and patient experience).⏎⏎Prioritizing FHIR/USCDI expansion, TEFCA enforcement, and funded benchmarking (e.g.,⏎grants, prizes) would create a more liquid data ecosystem, lowering costs for AI developers and⏎enabling equitable, high-impact tools across care settings.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Challenges Patients and Caregivers Wish AI to Address in Clinical Care⏎Patients and caregivers see AI as a tool to tackle key pain points in healthcare, based on recent⏎surveys and case studies from 2025-2026:⏎• Proactive Support and Early Intervention: Providing clinical risk alerts based on⏎severity to help them focus on patients with the most urgent need, pattern recognition for⏎symptoms, and context-aware assistance in triage for high-risk groups (e.g., seniors,⏎chronic patients) to prevent escalations and improve outcomes.⏎• Empowerment in Decision-Making: Offering correlations to underlying causes of⏎symptoms to distill root causes, second opinions, and personalized insights to foster⏎equity, accessibility, and human-centered care.⏎• Improved Understanding of Medical Information: Translating complex records,⏎diagnoses, and test results into plain language to reduce confusion and enable faster,⏎informed decisions. Drug-to-drug, food-to-drug, and supplement-to-drug interactions,⏎treatment complications.⏎• Streamlined Communication and Preparation: Summarizing patient histories for⏎providers, preparation for visits to cut delays and boost confidence in care.⏎• Enhanced Access and Efficiency: Supporting chronic disease management, wellness,⏎and administrative tasks like scheduling prioritization, or patient reminders for care plan⏎adherence, especially for caregivers handling routines and relationships. Ability to share⏎24/7 patient health trends with multiple caregivers to foster collaboration.⏎Concerns Patients and Caregivers Have Regarding AI Adoption⏎While optimistic, patients and caregivers express balanced worries, often prioritizing⏎safeguards:⏎• Privacy and Data Security: Fears of breaches, misuse of personal health data, and re-⏎identification risks in AI systems.⏎• Bias, Inaccuracy, and Reliability: Concerns about algorithmic biases (e.g.,⏎racial/ethnic disparities), hallucinations, misdiagnoses, or poor generalization to diverse⏎cases.⏎⏎• Loss of Human Touch and Empathy: Worry that AI dehumanizes care, reduces⏎clinician-patient interactions, and erodes trust or emotional support.⏎• Transparency and Accountability: Lack of disclosure on AI use, unclear responsibility⏎for errors, and potential over-reliance leading to ignored human advice.⏎• Equity and Ethical Issues: Fears of increased costs, access disparities, or misuse in⏎sensitive areas like end-of-life decisions.⏎Case Studies:⏎Predictive Analytics for Sepsis Detection and Early Intervention⏎AI enables hours-earlier alerts, saving lives in time-sensitive conditions.⏎• Johns Hopkins (COMPOSER model) implemented an AI system that predicts sepsis⏎before obvious symptoms, reducing mortality by ~17-20% in studies involving hundreds⏎of thousands of patients. It caught deteriorations early, preventing escalations and⏎demonstrating measurable survival gains.⏎• Similar tools in other systems (e.g., predictive models in general wards) have cut serious⏎adverse events by 35% and cardiac arrests by >86%.⏎• Hackensack Meridian integrated AI with Epic for medication instruction mapping,⏎eliminating manual entry in most cases and improving patient safety/outcomes by⏎addressing unstructured Rx data challenges.⏎• Mayo Clinic invests >$1 billion in 200+ AI projects spanning diagnostics, patient care,⏎and research—extending beyond admin to clinical improvements.⏎• Geisinger uses AI/predictive analytics for care coordination, early disease detection,⏎and resource optimization.⏎AI in Telemedicine and Remote Monitoring for Rural/Underserved Access (NEW)⏎AI bridges gaps in chronic care and remote areas.⏎• Empowered-Home.com uses AI-powered remote patient monitoring integrating⏎wearables and in-home devices for continuous insights, generating clinician summaries⏎and predictive alerts. Focused on rural/remote patients, maternal health, seniors, and⏎chronic conditions, it detects subtle changes early, enables proactive interventions,⏎reduces unnecessary ED visits/hospitalizations, and supports evidence-based⏎decisions—directly tackling access barriers and preventive care in value-based models.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎Specific Areas of AI Research HHS Should Prioritize to Accelerate Adoption in⏎Clinical Care⏎The highest impact area HHS should prioritize is research that emphasizes continuous remote⏎patient health monitoring (RPM) with AI, focusing on proactive clinician notifications, correlative⏎insights for symptom triage, and root cause analysis to enable early interventions, reduce⏎hospitalizations, and enhance personalized care in clinical settings. Key areas include:⏎• AI-Driven Predictive Analytics for Proactive Notifications: Develop algorithms that⏎analyze real-time RPM data (e.g., from wearables and in-home devices) to detect subtle⏎deviations, generate automated alerts for clinicians, and support multivariate correlations⏎to identify emerging risks before symptoms escalate. Old RPM solutions that rely on⏎nurses to monitor patient health data don’t scale. AI needs to be doing that.⏎• Correlative Insights and Automated Triage Systems: Advance AI for synthesizing⏎multimodal data (e.g., vitals, activity, environmental factors) to provide root cause⏎analysis, prioritize high-risk cases, and assist in differential diagnosis during triage,⏎reducing clinician workload and improving accuracy in chronic conditions like heart⏎failure or allergies.⏎• Privacy-Preserving Federated Learning in RPM: Enable secure, decentralized model⏎training on distributed patient data to enhance RPM without centralizing sensitive⏎information, addressing HIPAA while improving model robustness for diverse⏎populations.⏎• Human-AI Integration for RPM Workflows: Research hybrid systems where AI⏎augments clinicians in real-time monitoring and triage, offering explainable insights and⏎notifications to foster trust, reduce burnout, and ensure seamless integration into⏎telehealth and virtual care models.⏎• Bias Mitigation and Equity in Continuous Monitoring: Focus on diverse datasets for⏎RPM AI to minimize disparities in alerts and insights, particularly for rural, elderly, or⏎chronic care patients, with validation in real-world settings.⏎• Interoperability and Data Standards for RPM: Enhance FHIR/USCDI frameworks to⏎support seamless RPM data flows, enabling correlative AI analysis across devices and⏎EHRs for proactive care.⏎• Post-Deployment Monitoring and Safety in RPM: Create benchmarks for drift⏎detection in continuous data streams, ensuring reliable notifications and triage over time.⏎⏎These priorities, supported by grants and pilots, align with HHS's AI Strategy to shift from⏎reactive to proactive care, particularly in RPM for better outcomes and efficiency.⏎A. Published Findings on the Impact of Adopted AI Tools in Clinical Care⏎Yes, 2025-2026 literature highlights positive impacts in RPM-focused AI, with tools enabling⏎proactive notifications and correlative insights that reduce readmissions and improve triage,⏎though deployment challenges like data integration persist.⏎• RPM with Proactive Alerts: Some AI platforms detect abnormal patterns in RPM data,⏎triggering timely interventions and reducing administrative burden; studies show 46%⏎more detections and 10x fewer false positives in systems like Cleveland Clinic's⏎Bayesian Health rollout.⏎• Correlative Insights for Triage: AI in wearables (e.g., for allergy or cardiac monitoring)⏎provides real-time predictive management, enabling root cause analysis and⏎personalized alerts that cut hospitalizations in heart failure patients.⏎• Broader RPM Impacts: Tools like those in Cityhälsan Centrum's telemedicine program⏎track home parameters for proactive care, improving engagement and outcomes;⏎overall, AI RPM reduces clinician workload by prioritizing risks and empowers patients⏎with insights.⏎• Diagnostic and Monitoring Advances: AI-aided systems (e.g., Johns Hopkins'⏎COMPOSER) predict deteriorations 6-7 hours early, reducing mortality by 17-20%, with⏎similar gains in continuous monitoring for chronic conditions.⏎• Adoption Trends: 22% of organizations use domain-specific RPM AI (7x increase from⏎2024); failures often from poor integration, but successes show >50% time savings in⏎monitoring workflows.⏎B. How the Literature Approaches Costs, Benefits, and Transfers of Using AI in Clinical⏎Care⏎Literature uses cost-effectiveness analysis (CEA), ROI models, and value frameworks, with a⏎growing emphasis on RPM's proactive elements—quantifying savings from early notifications⏎and triage insights while considering transfers like shifted burdens from hospitals to home-⏎based care.⏎• Costs: Assessed as upfront (e.g., device integration, AI training) and ongoing (e.g., data⏎processing for continuous monitoring), with RPM AI reducing long-term expenses⏎through fewer false alerts; studies highlight needs for bias mitigation to avoid inequitable⏎costs.⏎• Benefits: Measured in reduced readmissions (e.g., $200-360B U.S. savings annually),⏎improved QALYs from proactive interventions, and efficiency gains (e.g., 1.7B clinician⏎hours freed via automated triage and root cause insights in RPM).⏎⏎• Transfers: Evaluated as reallocations (e.g., cost shifts to payers via preventive RPM,⏎value flows to patients through home-based monitoring); frameworks advocate novel⏎reimbursements to capture equity gains in proactive care.⏎About Empowered-Home⏎Empowered-Home is a new and innovative AI-powered health technology platform designed to⏎support healthcare organizations & their clinicians in remotely monitoring their patients’ health⏎vitals and supporting health data (sleep, exercise, stress, activity), continuously. It is not a⏎rework of an old-style RPM platform from the Covid days. It was built on a modern and flexible⏎event-driven data orchestration platform that can handle thousands of transactions per second⏎and safely interface with multiple LLMs for consensus reasoning. It also has the ability to safely⏎manage agents performing tasks that clinicians don’t have time for, but with a policy⏎administration layer for safety. It was built to quickly adapt to technology improvements and to⏎be flexibly configured for wide variety of programs.⏎Through the use of modern wearables and in-home devices, the EH platform can detect⏎changes in patients' health stability and auto-generate clinical risk alerts based on severity,⏎helping clinicians prioritize care of their patients while also helping them triage patient⏎symptoms.⏎Unified data from multiple devices, in combination with a lightweight import of the patient's⏎medical information (discharge summary, encounters, SBAR, and health problems from their⏎electronic health records), is presented to the clinician in a dashboard.⏎The AI identifies trends, generates correlations, suggests triage questions for the clinician to ask⏎the patient during telemedicine calls.⏎It provides a framework to monitor the patient through each stage (from discharge to recovery to⏎stability).⏎A corresponding iPhone app keeps the patient and family members informed of their daily⏎health trends relative to their behavior and environment, allowing caregivers to coordinate care⏎around the patient.⏎The patient leverages new data sharing privileges under the 21st Century Cures Act and the⏎Individual Right of Access, empowering them to make decisions about who sees what data.⏎Who Empowered-Home Benefits:⏎• It benefits hospitals with reduced Emergency Department visits, reduction in 30-day⏎readmissions, DRG denials, and Length of Stay.⏎• It benefits patients living in remote areas and family members who worry about their⏎health and well-being.⏎⏎• It benefits health insurers who are desperately trying to contain escalating healthcare⏎costs with predictive and preventative care.⏎• It removes a large workload from physicians and allows nurses and community⏎paramedics to operate at the top of their license.⏎• It also helps solve the healthcare worker staff shortage and serves as a training tool for⏎consistency and accuracy.⏎We hope to have the opportunity to play a significant role in the advancement of HHS’s⏎objectives as AI reshapes the efficiency of healthcare.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0331♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Donna♔Johnson♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter Reyna was seen by two different doctors before anyone figured out what was wrong with her. This was at a clinic in Albuquerque, and then a follow-up somewhere else, and both times they ran tests and said things were inconclusive or pointed at something minor. It wasn't until she was seen at UNM in 2019 that the doctor there — he actually sat with her chart for a while, asked her questions that weren't on any intake form, watched how she moved, noticed things — and he caught the autoimmune condition the other visits had completely missed. That's what I keep thinking about when I read about this push to speed up AI diagnostics.⏎⏎The thing is, what saved Reyna wasn't more data or faster processing. It was that one doctor paying attention to her as a person sitting in front of him. She was 19 and scared and not great at describing what was wrong, and he picked up on that. He adjusted how he talked to her. He asked follow-up questions when she hesitated. I genuinely don't know how a system that's processing typed inputs is supposed to replicate that. I'm not saying AI is useless in medicine, I'm really not, I can see how it might help with certain things. But there's a difference between a tool that helps a physician and a tool that replaces the physician's judgment, and I worry this push is blurring that line on purpose because it's cheaper.⏎⏎My specific concern with what HHS is proposing here is the part about reducing requirements for physician review of AI outputs. I've tried to understand the details and honestly the regulatory language is hard to follow, but the basic direction seems to be: make it easier to use AI recommendations without requiring a doctor to actually sign off on them in a meaningful way. If I'm reading that wrong I'd want to know, but that's the impression I have. And if that's right, I think it's a serious mistake.⏎⏎When Reyna was being misdiagnosed, nobody was negligent exactly. They were following normal procedures. The problem was that normal procedures didn't catch it. A machine running normal procedures faster would not have helped. What helped was a physician who slowed down.⏎⏎I also want to say something about patients who aren't good at self-advocacy, because Reyna was young and intimidated and didn't know how to push back or ask for more. Patients like that are going to be the most affected if the human contact gets reduced. They're not going to flag when the AI recommendation seems off. They're going to trust it because it came from the doctor's office and they don't have the background to question it. That worries me more than anything else about where this is heading.⏎⏎I don't have a policy background and I'm not going to pretend I know exactly how to write the regulations around this. But I would ask that whatever HHS decides, they make it a real requirement — not a checkbox — that a physician actually reviews AI outputs before treatment decisions get made, especially for diagnostic recommendations. And by "reviews" I mean actually looks at the patient's situation, not just clicks approve on a screen.⏎⏎Reyna is doing okay now, she's managing her condition. But I think about all the kids who don't have a parent who keeps pushing, or who see whoever is available that day and just take what they're told.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0332♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from UConn Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔UConn Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Thank you for considering these comments and for your commitment to advancing AI in ways that enhance patient care. A unified, multi stakeholder framework is essential to ensure that enhanced clinical and business workflow automations—including those integrated into Electronic Medical Records—are implemented consistently, transparently, and in ways that support all patients regardless of geography, delivery system or social status. UConn Health is already engaging in productive dialogue—actively working with statewide partners through the Connecticut Hospital Association, HIMSS, ACHE, FMSA and CHiME to explore how to safely incorporate AI into healthcare IT— to ensure consistency across the country, it is also important to align federal agencies, the states, healthcare organizations, caregivers and patients expectations to promote patient safety and equity all of the potential benefits from AI-driven advancements.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0333♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Urological Association (AUA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Urological Association (AUA)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 17, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW⏎Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Sent electronically via regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎On behalf of the American Urological Association (AUA), thank you for the opportunity to provide⏎comments on the Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎(ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)’s⏎request for information (RFI) on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI)⏎as Part of Clinical Care.⏎The AUA is a globally engaged organization with more than 22,000 members practicing in more⏎than 100 countries. Our members represent the world’s largest collection of expertise and⏎insight into the treatment of urologic disease. Of the total AUA membership, more than 18,000⏎are based in the United States, therefore we are actively engaged in commenting on the⏎regulatory proposals of the Department of Human and Health Services and its agencies. Our⏎members provide invaluable support to the urologic community by fostering the highest⏎standards of urologic care through education, research, and formulation of health policy.⏎With this background, we provide our comments on the use of AI in clinical care in response to⏎the questions you posed in the RFI most relevant to our members.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations.⏎Predictable reimbursement is a primary driver of adoption of physician services and new⏎technology and aligns incentives toward value rather than volume. The Department of Health⏎and Human Services (HHS) should expand permanent payment mechanisms at the Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) for Food and Drug Administration (FDA)‑cleared, high-⏎value AI tools that demonstrate outcome improvement or workflow efficiency, while optimizing⏎FDA oversight to ensure safety of AI in clinical care and to incentivize AI governance in⏎hospital/patient safety programs.⏎⏎To further incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care, HHS should prioritize changes that⏎align incentives with safe, validated, and clinically meaningful AI use rather than AI adoption⏎alone. Rules that clearly define AI-enabled care and outline how payors, patients, and hospitals⏎use these tools will be crucial in laying a solid foundation for future tools. AUA recommends that⏎HHS consider the key priorities listed below:⏎• Payment alignment: CMS should create explicit payment pathways (e.g., HCPCS G-⏎codes or add-ons) for AI-assisted data review, risk stratification, triage, documentation⏎support, care planning, and clinical decision review when integrated into certified EHR⏎workflows and reviewed by a clinician. Clinician time spent on required post-deployment⏎monitoring of AI for data drift, bias, and safety should also be recognized. (Applicable⏎CFR: 42 CFR Part 414)⏎• Cost recognition via inclusion in value-based programs: In the Merit-based⏎Incentive Payment System (MIPS) and advanced Alternative Payment Models (APMs),⏎AI licensing, validation, updates, and monitoring should be treated as allowable practice⏎expense inputs when tied to clinical services to avoid underinvestment in governance⏎and safety. (Applicable CFR: 42 CFR 414.1350) HHS should also consider incentivizing⏎AI generation and its use by making an improvement activity category for “responsible AI⏎deployment” that requires model documentation, workflow integration, drift monitoring,⏎subgroup performance checks, and clinician override logging. (Applicable CFR: 42 CFR⏎414.1355)⏎• Inpatient adoption: CMS should clarify that software-based AI, including non-medical⏎device tools, may qualify for New Technology Add-On Payments in the inpatient setting⏎when criteria are met and provide AI-specific evidentiary guidance. (Applicable CFR: 42⏎CFR 412.87)⏎• Electronic Health Records (EHR) guardrails: HHS should preserve and enforce ONC⏎predictive decision support requirements for transparency, risk analysis, and mitigation⏎at the point of care. (Applicable CFR: 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11))⏎• Interoperability and oversight: Information-blocking exceptions should be applied⏎narrowly so they do not impede lawful access for AI evaluation and monitoring.⏎(Applicable CFR: 45 CFR Part 171)⏎• Privacy clarity: HHS should issue HIPAA guidance clarifying de-identification and⏎minimum-necessary standards for AI development, validation, and post-deployment⏎monitoring. (Applicable CFR: 45 CFR 164.514)⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎Clinicians recognize AI’s potential to improve care delivery but are concerned about unresolved⏎safety, legal, and operational risks. When AI contributes to patient harm, accountability is often⏎blurred among clinicians, health systems, vendors, and EHR platforms. In addition, contracts⏎may shift indemnification risk to clinicians despite their limited control over AI design or updates.⏎⏎Clinicians are also wary of silent updates and model drift that can alter performance without⏎notice, as well as documentation risks from generative AI that may produce credible but⏎inaccurate content, if not carefully reviewed and labeled. Other concerns include privacy and⏎cybersecurity vulnerabilities, such as prompt injection, PHI leakage, secondary use of data, and⏎risks from third-party integrations, along with limited mechanisms to monitor and address bias⏎across patient populations. To address these concerns, clinicians could benefit from model⏎contracting guidance issued by HHS, stronger documentation transparency standards, and the⏎creation of a national learning system to monitor and improve AI-related safety outcomes.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎Proper validation of AI platforms is of utmost importance to physicians and patients. With any⏎new technology, a thorough and transparent process must be in place prior to approval. We⏎recommend that HHS streamline the process necessary for an AI platform to gain⏎accreditation or approval by aligning the market around common, measurable⏎expectations (e.g., a set of minimum assurance elements including governance, clinical⏎validation, cybersecurity, etc.) while avoiding duplicative federal bureaucracy. When⏎developing the accreditation process, we ask that HHS consider specialty society participation⏎in defining clinically meaningful endpoints and minimum evidence thresholds so that⏎accreditation reflects real workflow and outcome needs, not just technical performance.⏎HHS should support public–private partnerships that enable independent, third‑party testing of⏎AI models across populations, sites, and time, including post‑deployment monitoring. A⏎nationwide network of AI assurance laboratories, supported but not operated by HHS, would⏎allow industry‑driven testing results to be shared transparently and reused across health⏎systems. This would help avoid duplicative work across health systems that will slow innovation⏎and create barriers in reaching our most vulnerable patients. HHS should consider these⏎strategies to support this third-party testing:⏎1) Create a federal recognition pathway that identifies credible third-party programs aligned⏎with transparency, risk management, and post-deployment monitoring expectations, and⏎that require public-facing disclosures of intended use, limitations, and update⏎governance.⏎2) Provide grants for demonstration projects. HHS could support a testing infrastructure⏎that allows vendors and health systems to compare performance, subgroup equity,⏎calibration, and drift across settings, and to publish standardized “model facts” for clinical⏎users. HHS could provide access to large databases that are supported by the⏎government like the Veterans Health Administration’s system for AI learning and help⏎solve HIPPA hurdles as they arise.⏎3) Create a "virtual healthcare system" of sample data (images, labs, notes, etc.) upon⏎which private sector AI tools could be tested/validated. This resource would be helpful⏎for safety purposes and comparative effectiveness and would incentivize independent⏎testing for tools that demonstrate third-party evaluation, real-world monitoring plans, and⏎interoperability conformance.⏎⏎4) Extend ONC certification so that there is a higher internal standard for AI-workflows⏎within health systems⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and⏎help reduce costs?⏎AI tools have met or exceeded expectations when narrowly scoped, integrated into workflow,⏎and evaluated in real-world settings. For instance, AI tools targeting administrative burden and⏎narrow tasks perform well. AI scribes provide concise summaries of information for help with⏎clinical decision-making and reduce documentation time and burnout without compromising⏎quality. In urology, AI used for imaging triage, prostate cancer risk stratification, and operational⏎workflows has shown value when tightly integrated into care delivery. Applications for workflows⏎include handling patient communications with the front office, simple clinical communications⏎with patients, scheduling, authorizations, and charting.⏎However, clinical AI often underperforms due to poor EHR integration, alert fatigue, and lack of⏎reimbursement. AI scribes improve efficiency and documentation quality but have no⏎sustainable reimbursement pathway, limiting adoption. AI has fallen short in reliably excluding⏎errors when deployed as black boxes, are poorly calibrated, or are misaligned with clinical⏎operations. Some AI tools often increase alert burden or create “extra clicks”; introduce⏎documentation errors without clear labeling and review; or are based on models that degrade⏎over time due to drift or population shift. AI tools in digital pathology, even though increasingly⏎powerful and accepted within clinical use, have not exceeded cost-saving expectations.⏎In urology, AI that synthesizes the longitudinal chart data from electronic health records (EHR)—⏎highlighting rising Prostate-specific antigen (PSA) trends, missed hematuria workups,⏎medication risks, or post‑op complications—could significantly improve patient safety.⏎Additionally, next‑generation clinical decision support tools should prioritize risk rather than add⏎alerts (e.g., identifying patients at highest risk for urinary retention, infection, or delayed cancer⏎diagnosis). AI tools that reach the bedside and nursing teams, such as early warning for⏎catheter‑associated complications or post‑procedure deterioration, offer significant potential to⏎improve outcomes while reducing costs.⏎Lastly, future impactful tools may focus on creating a primary care infrastructure that helps⏎reduce the number of routine visits with care providers and engages patients in their health care⏎decisions. AI tools to improve information sharing, even without consolidation of services, would⏎decrease fragmentation of care and may preserve coordinated care while still fostering⏎competitive efforts.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎This topic merits additional exploration, as our experts surveyed provided a wide range of⏎perspectives and no clear consensus. We also note that every health care organization seems⏎⏎to have its own process to make decisions regarding AI. have designated committees while⏎others do not, and the makeup of such committees can heavily influence the likelihood of AI⏎adoption.⏎Our members reported that the following individuals within their health systems have the most⏎influence: Chief Medical Information Officer (CMIO)/clinical informatics leadership, the Chief⏎Information Officer (CIO) and Chief Information Security Officer (CISO), Chief Quality and⏎Safety Officer, compliance and privacy officers, legal and contracting, value analysis or digital⏎governance committees, service line leadership and department chairs. Several members⏎mentioned that professional medical specialty societies, especially trade associations, often⏎carry the most influence on adopting medical technologies and service lines. For instance, large⏎group practices, institutions such as Kaiser, and academic programs demonstrate the clinical⏎and financial efficacy of a new technology, and wider adoption eventually follows. The key⏎players in these organizations include thought leaders in clinical practice and/or chief scientific⏎officers, administrative managers of clinical and business operations (CEO/CFO), and nurse⏎managers.⏎One member said that federal government bodies such as the department of HHS or CMS⏎would play the most important role. For instance, CMS might play a disproportionately important⏎role in adoption for clinical care as they would design and implement reimbursement incentives.⏎Similarly, HHS may be able to mandate documentation of decisions made with the assistance of⏎AI tools, allowing for retrospective and comparative analyses of decisions generated by the⏎presence/absence of AI assistance.⏎There are multiple administrative hurdles to adopting AI in clinical care. These include vendor⏎risk and security assessments, contracting and indemnification (audit rights, update control,⏎data retention), EHR integration capacity and change control, workflow redesign and training,⏎lack of internal evaluation and monitoring infrastructure, unclear return of investment due to⏎misaligned incentives where costs sit with clinics, but savings accrue to payers or the broader⏎system.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎AI platforms should be required to be interoperable. Enhancing interoperability specifically in the⏎areas listed below would not only improve access to longitudinal, multi-source data, but also⏎enable consistent post-deployment monitoring and benchmarking.⏎• High-value data types: Longitudinal labs, vitals, meds, problems, allergies; imaging and⏎structured reports; operative notes and pathology; wearable device data; patient-⏎reported outcomes; claims and encounter data; referrals and care transitions; scheduling⏎and utilization signals.⏎• Data standards and implementation needs: Scalable Fast Healthcare Interoperability⏎Resources (FHIR) read and write capabilities, provenance, and event notifications; ONC⏎certification for deeper in-system integration and higher security standards to support⏎⏎and enhance trust; the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA)⏎interoperability for patient care across state lines; better Digital Imaging and⏎Communications in Medicine (DICOM) interoperability; standardized representation of AI⏎outputs in the record (tool name/version, output, confidence or risk category, clinician⏎action/override); and consistent patient identity matching and metadata.⏎• Benchmarking tools: federated evaluation frameworks that allow models to be tested⏎across sites without exporting raw protected health information (PHI); standardized test⏎harnesses and reporting templates for calibration, subgroup equity, drift, and clinical⏎utility; and shared registries for post-market performance learning.⏎9. What challenges within healthcare do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients express interest in AI as a tool to improve access to health care expertise, particularly if⏎it offers timely, convenient, and confidential support without replacing human judgment,⏎interaction, and compassion. However, it must come at a reasonable cost. They hope AI can⏎streamline scheduling and triage, enhance education and care navigation, improve coordination⏎among providers, reduce administrative delays, strengthen follow-up and symptom monitoring,⏎minimize redundant testing, and support more personalized care planning. Many value AI that⏎helps clinicians identify important information in large medical records and as tools that support⏎daily care while preserving patient autonomy.⏎However, they have significant concerns about privacy, data security, and reliability. Specifically,⏎they worry about secondary uses of their data, unclear data retention practices, lack of⏎transparency about when AI is used, erosion of clinician accountability, and the use of recording⏎or ambient documentation without clear patient consent.⏎Addressing these concerns requires building trust through transparency about when and how AI⏎is used, maintaining clinician oversight, offering opt-out options for nonessential uses, ensuring⏎strong privacy and security protections, and supporting ongoing monitoring with public⏎accountability for safety and equity. Patients generally support AI when it enhances clinical care⏎while respecting consent, privacy, and dignity; failure to meet these expectations may⏎undermine trust, even in effective tools.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎Current evidence on AI’s impact in health care is mixed, and there is an urgent need for real-⏎world evaluation. For instance, AI documentation tools have reduced administrative burden in⏎some settings, but effects on burnout, care quality, and costs remain inconsistent. Clinical⏎prediction models may also perform poorly in practice, and while economic studies often report⏎AI as cost-effective or cost-saving, results vary by clinical application and methodology, and⏎negative findings may be underreported.⏎Also, cost-effectiveness studies often fail to clarify who bears costs versus who gains benefits⏎when using AI and frequently overlook full lifecycle expenses, including integration,⏎⏎cybersecurity, monitoring, revalidation, and governance. They also rarely address model⏎updates over time or how financial impacts are distributed across stakeholders, and study⏎quality varies. HHS could help address these gaps by supporting standardized evaluation⏎frameworks that capture total cost of ownership, clearly map cost and benefit distribution, and⏎require real-world monitoring to ensure sustained value after deployment.⏎Below are several areas that need further research and should be prioritized:⏎• Real-world clinical utility and safety: Pragmatic trials and stepped-wedge evaluations⏎of AI tools embedded in routine workflows, with standardized outcome measures (harms,⏎time-to-intervention, complications, downstream utilization) rather than accuracy alone.⏎There should also be safety studies to determine where and when to trust AI and what⏎standards are best to ensure transparency.⏎• Health economics and implementation science: Standard methods to measure total⏎cost of ownership (integration, security, monitoring, clinician oversight), budget impact,⏎and distribution of costs and savings across stakeholders, plus implementation⏎playbooks for small/rural and safety-net settings.⏎• Post-deployment monitoring science: Methods and infrastructure for drift detection,⏎recalibration, subgroup surveillance, and “override analytics” (when clinicians disagree⏎with AI and why), including standardized incident reporting taxonomies for AI-related⏎safety events.⏎• Human factors and human-AI teaming: Usability, cognitive load, automation bias,⏎clinician trust calibration, and patient communication studies that determine where AI⏎improves decisions versus adds noise.⏎• Generalizability and robustness: Multisite external validation, dataset shift testing, and⏎robustness benchmarking across institutions, EHR configurations, documentation styles,⏎and patient subgroups.⏎• Interoperability-enabled evaluation: Federated evaluation toolkits and shared test⏎harnesses that enable benchmarking without moving raw PHI (and that include⏎provenance and model versioning).⏎Thank you again for the opportunity to provide comments on this important issue. Should you⏎have any questions, please contact Melika Zand, Patient Advocacy Manager, at⏎mzand@auanet.org.⏎Sincerely,♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0334♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Siemens Healthineers, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Siemens Healthineers♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎VIA ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION – Federal Register⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., Department of Health and Human Services:⏎Siemens Healthineers appreciates the opportunity to discuss how the Department of⏎Health and Human Services (HHS) can accelerate the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. For over 20 years, Siemens Healthineers⏎has been committed to advancing innovative AI clinical care and workflow solutions, to⏎increase access and improve outcomes for all patients.⏎We are a team of 17,000 passionate employees across more than twenty-five locations in⏎the United States, including R&D centers, manufacturing sites, and sales and service⏎operations. At Siemens Healthineers USA, we pioneer breakthroughs in healthcare for⏎everyone, everywhere. By bringing innovative technology to the United States market, we⏎enable healthcare professionals to deliver high-quality care that changes lives. Our⏎portfolio includes diagnostic and therapeutic imaging, radiotherapy treatment, laboratory⏎diagnostics, molecular medicine, digital health, and enterprise services. Our solutions are⏎integral to informed clinical decisions across the comprehensive healthcare landscape.⏎Our AI-enabled clinical applications and AI-powered solutions address major⏎challenges that are facing the healthcare field. Siemens Healthineers’ AI-Rad Companion⏎Chest Computed Tomography (CT) provides otherwise unavailable quantitative lung lesion⏎data which enables clinicians to make more informed clinical decisions. Additionally,⏎developing solutions for managing increasing workloads allows clinicians to spend more⏎time with patients, which helps to drive long-term success. As workloads are growing,⏎diagnostics and treatments are becoming more complex; experts and physicians need a⏎new set of tools that can handle large volumes of medical data quickly and accurately.⏎Siemens Healthineers has developed a portfolio of AI-powered solutions that help advance⏎more informed clinical decisions and automate and standardize workflows and complex⏎diagnostics to meet the needs of individual patients.⏎For further information or to connect with Siemens Healthineers, please contact Leah⏎Kegler (leah.kegler@siemens-healthineers.com) Head, Government Affairs, Americas.⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎At the heart of Siemens Healthineers three billion patient touchpoints, is a passion⏎to drive better health outcomes for every patient, everywhere. We partner with⏎leaders across the Administration, and throughout the health care ecosystem to⏎improve primary care, advanced screening, and provide a multifaceted approach to⏎chronic care for conditions like diabetes, cancer, and heart disease. A core⏎component of this work is the integration of transformative innovations like artificial⏎intelligence (AI) into clinical care. Without greater provider adoption and improved⏎access, the opportunity it presents to change everyday health care challenges for⏎patients will never be realized.⏎Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) reimbursement is a key driver of⏎adoption of transformative innovation, like AI – especially in rural areas. While CMS⏎has noted the potential of AI to increase access, improve outcomes, and lower⏎costs, outdated laws and regulations are causing barriers that are hampering⏎clinical adoption. The inconsistent, unreliable, and ad hoc approach to reimbursing⏎software as a service (SaaS), and FDA approved AI-enabled medical devices, is⏎having a crippling effect on provider adoption and patient utilization under⏎Medicare. We provide specific recommendations below, that we believe will be a⏎productive step towards bolstering adoption of innovative clinical AI tools in health⏎care that can help us better address critical challenges that are resulting in poor⏎health outcomes for patients.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care. Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎As CMS stated in the CY2023 HOPPS/ASC Final Rule, “Novel and evolving⏎technologies are introducing advances in treatment options that have the potential⏎to increase access to care for Medicare beneficiaries, improve outcomes, and⏎reduce overall costs to the program.” While CMS has recognized the value and the⏎complex nature of algorithm-based services, machine learning, and clinical analytic⏎software, the agency’s payment determinations have not been applied uniformly⏎and consistently to guarantee appropriate payment levels for these products.⏎⏎We suggest that CMS consider adopting the term algorithm-based healthcare⏎services (ABHS), which include Software as a Service (SaaS) procedure. ABHS are⏎clinical analytical services delivered by FDA-cleared devices to a healthcare⏎practitioner that uses artificial intelligence, machine learning, or other similarly⏎designed software to produce clinical outputs for the diagnosis or treatment of a⏎patient’s condition. ABHS provides quantitative and qualitative analyses, including⏎new, additional clinical outputs that detect, analyze, or interpret data to improve⏎screening, detection, diagnosis, and treatment of disease. Further, ABHS includes⏎these services regardless of whether they are delivered by a third party or by a health⏎care professional using AI software through the cloud, the web, a workstation, or as⏎embedded in imaging acquisition hardware. Devices embedded with software may⏎be preferable to certain providers based on their existing imaging equipment,⏎workflow, and staffing requirements. Note that all procedures currently classified as⏎SaaS by CMS should also be classified as ABHS.⏎CMS should utilize its SaaS policy for determining payment for ABHS procedures in⏎the HOPPS, not its add-on code packaging policy. This approach will ensure that⏎providers receive fair, appropriate, and stable compensation when they utilize ABHS⏎to improve outcomes for Medicare beneficiaries. We also recommend that all new⏎ABHS CPT codes be initially assigned to a New Technology APC (ambulatory⏎payment classification) payment, based on manufacturer-supplied cost data, until⏎sufficient claims data are collected to make a proper clinical APC assignment. It is⏎important to acknowledge that current CMS practices already encompass this⏎payment methodology and are not consistently used across all new CPT codes.⏎Furthermore, these CPT codes should remain in the New Technology APC payment⏎assignment for at least five years, which aligns with the lifespan of a Category III CPT⏎code, and not be subject to the Universal Low Volume APC policy. This is necessary⏎to ensure appropriate data collection and analysis can occur while hospitals adopt⏎ABHS.⏎We request that CMS clarify and codify the SaaS payment policy as follows:⏎• CMS should adopt the term “algorithm-based healthcare services” (ABHS),⏎which include Software as a Service (SaaS) procedure.⏎• CMS should formalize the exception to the packaged payment policy in⏎regulatory text, including Software as a Service (SaaS) procedure.⏎• CMS should ensure ABHS that secure a separate and distinct CPT code through⏎the AMA CPT coding process are exempt from CMS’s add-on code packing policy⏎and receive separate payment. CMS should initially assign ABHS CPT codes to a⏎⏎New Technology APC payment for 5 years, based on manufacture-supplied cost⏎data, until sufficient claims data is collected to make a proper clinical APC⏎assignment. Additionally, ABHS APC assignments should not be subject to the⏎Universal Low Volume APC Policy.⏎We offer the following recommended revision at 42 CFR 419.2 to formalize and⏎codify the SaaS policy:⏎419.2 Basis of payment⏎(b) Determination of outpatient prospective payment rates: Packaged costs.⏎(18) Certain services described by add-on codes except as provided in §⏎419.2(d).⏎(d) Determination of hospital outpatient prospective payment rates: Separately paid⏎costs. Software as a Service procedures assigned to CPT add-on codes will be paid⏎separately at an amount equal to the amount of the payment for the add-on⏎procedure when the service is furnished without the standalone CPT code. These⏎codes will be assigned to identical ambulatory payment classifications and status⏎indicator assignments as their standalone codes. Separate payment will be made⏎available for these services if the following criteria are met:⏎(1) The service has been approved by the FDA and has received a CPT code;⏎and⏎(2) The service is billed concurrently with the associated service code.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0335♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Premier Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Premier Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically to Regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎Premier appreciates the opportunity to submit comments to the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) on the Request for Information (RFI)⏎on artificial intelligence (AI) adoption in clinical care. Premier values the Administration’s ongoing commitment to⏎tech-enabling healthcare to reduce administrative burden while improving access to high-quality care. Premier has⏎consistently advocated for a responsible, innovation-friendly and transparent regulatory framework for AI in⏎healthcare, aligning with our longstanding commitment to improving patient outcomes and supporting clinicians⏎through technology. Our policy positions emphasize the need for clear regulatory guardrails, strong data privacy⏎protections, robust workforce enablement and industry-aligned standards for safe and effective AI adoption.⏎In our detailed comments below, Premier urges ASTP/ONC to:⏎• Support the harmonization of regulatory requirements across the Department of Health and Human Services⏎(HHS) to reduce duplicative or conflicting regulatory burdens;⏎• Provide technical assistance and guidance to help private sector organizations understand and evaluate⏎cybersecurity and privacy risks in acquiring and deploying AI technologies;⏎• Coordinate across HHS to incorporate hospital cybersecurity needs into the hospital preparedness program⏎funding formula;⏎• Work with the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) to reduce regulatory barriers to AI and⏎software coverage and financing;⏎• Explore opportunities for transparency labeling of AI technology in healthcare, which should include⏎outcomes-focused metrics such as accuracy, false positives, inference risks and recommended⏎use/applications;⏎• Embed supply chain identifiers in ONC certification criteria and requirements for application programming⏎interfaces (APIs);⏎• Publish cross-agency guidance endorsing open standards for supply chain data pipelines for AI tools; and⏎• Coordinate across HHS – particularly with the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) – to reinforce the Global⏎Trade Identification Number (GTIN) as the standard product key in provider systems and item masters,⏎allowing AI agents to link key information to provide timely, clinically-relevant insights.⏎I. BACKGROUND ON PREMIER INC.⏎Premier is a leading healthcare improvement company and national supply chain leader, uniting an alliance of 4,250⏎hospitals and approximately 365,000 continuum of care providers to transform healthcare. With integrated data and⏎analytics, collaboratives, supply chain solutions, consulting and other services, Premier enables better care and⏎outcomes at a lower cost.⏎200 Massachusetts Avenue NW, Suite 330, Washington, DC 20001 | premierinc.com⏎⏎HHS RFI on Accelerating AI in Clinical Care⏎Page 2 of 7⏎A Malcolm Baldrige National Quality Award recipient, Premier plays a critical role in the rapidly evolving healthcare⏎industry, collaborating with healthcare providers, manufacturers, distributors, government and other entities to co-⏎develop long-term innovations that reinvent and improve the way healthcare is delivered nationwide. Headquartered⏎in Charlotte, North Carolina, Premier is passionate about transforming American healthcare.⏎II. EARLY SUCCESSES IN CLINICAL DEPLOYMENT OF AI⏎Premier has partnered with leading healthcare providers and innovators to leverage AI in clinical workflows and⏎improve patient outcomes. Early detection and intervention can improve patient outcomes and drastically reduce⏎overall healthcare costs – for both patients and providers. Premier has demonstrated success in the following areas:⏎• The Premier team utilized artificial intelligence (AI), NLP and a data ontology designed to mine the⏎unstructured narrative of clinicians’ notes and pathology reports to identify patients for early Alzheimer’s⏎Disease intervention.⏎• In oncology, Premier partnered with AstraZeneca and Clinithink and utilized Clinithink’s CLiX NLP technology⏎to identify patients with incidental pulmonary nodules (IPNs) to flag for intervention before potential lung⏎cancer progression – with roughly 152,000 patients “caught” early.⏎• Premier worked with GE Healthcare and St. Luke’s University Health Network to introduce a patient-centric⏎care model for breast cancer diagnosis – with a goal of helping patients go from appointment to diagnosis⏎and connection to a treatment plan in just 48 hours or less.⏎Premier sees particular promise for the use of AI in streamlining processes and expanding patient access in clinical⏎trials:⏎• Identifying trial participants: One of the biggest challenges facing health systems that seek to participate⏎in or enroll patients in clinical trials is identifying and enrolling patients in a timely manner. Delays in meeting⏎trial enrollment targets and timelines can increase the cost of the trial. AI tools have the ability to analyze the⏎extensive universe of data available to healthcare systems to identify patients that may be a match for clinical⏎trials that are currently recruiting. This application of natural language processing systems can make⏎developing new drugs less expensive and more efficient, while also improving patient and geographical⏎diversity in trials to address generalizability.⏎• Generating synthetic data: AI, once trained on real-world data (RWD), has the capability to generate⏎synthetic data and patient profiles that share characteristics with the target patient population for a clinical⏎trial. This synthetic data can be used to simulate clinical trials to optimize trial designs, model the possible⏎effects or range of results of a novel intervention, and predict the statistical significance and magnitude of⏎effects or biases. Ultimately, synthetic patient data can help optimize trial design, improve safety and reduce⏎cost for decentralized clinical trials. Further, synthetic control arms in clinical trials can help increase trial⏎enrollment by easing patient fears that they will receive a placebo. To incentivize continued innovation,⏎Premier encourages ASTP/ONC to work with the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to promulgate⏎clear guidance on the process for properly obtaining consent from patients for the use of their RWD⏎to produce AI-generated synthetic control arms in clinical trials.⏎III. BARRIERS TO PRIVATE SECTOR INNOVATION AND CLINICAL ADOPTION⏎Based on Premier’s real-world experience building, deploying and supporting AI tools across supply chain⏎management, drug manufacturing, clinical decision support and healthcare operations, a consistent set of structural,⏎regulatory, operational and economic barriers continue to limit private sector innovation in AI and impede adoption in⏎clinical settings.⏎1. Lack of national privacy standards. A significant barrier to the large-scale diffusion of innovative AI⏎applications in healthcare is the lack of a single, comprehensive federal privacy law. A cohesive national⏎© 2026 All rights reserved. | Premier Inc. | 2⏎⏎HHS RFI on Accelerating AI in Clinical Care⏎Page 3 of 7⏎privacy framework is necessary to provide a reasonable level of consumer confidence that businesses will⏎protect their data, sensitive or otherwise. The current patchwork, state-driven approach to privacy policy has⏎resulted in inconsistent data privacy practices. Patients must be able to trust the processes for storage,⏎handling and use of their data, particularly as patient data is increasingly used to train AI algorithms. Missing⏎data from patients wary of data sharing could add to data siloing and perpetuate AI algorithmic⏎biases, and ASTP/ONC should work with data stakeholders across HHS to proactively educate⏎patients on these tradeoffs.⏎Unharmonized and burdensome requirements are a rate-limiting step in unlocking value through AI⏎innovation. A federal privacy law can empower innovators by placing clear, harmonized and common-sense⏎guardrails around artificial intelligence tools. Healthcare privacy enforcement authorities are currently⏎diffused across a wide swath of the federal government. The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability⏎Act (HIPAA) only delegates privacy enforcement authority to the Department of Health and Human Services⏎(HHS) Office of Civil Rights (OCR) for a narrow subset of covered entities. With the proliferation of a⏎healthcare data ecosystem outside of the traditional payer-provider exchange, enforcement authority falls to⏎the Federal Trade Commission, the Department of Justice and other agencies for a broad range of privacy-⏎and data-related violations. There is significant, urgent need for a unified baseline set of enforcement⏎priorities to create a harmonized enforcement authority for the portion of healthcare ecosystem outside of the⏎bounds of HIPAA⏎Now, as state AI laws follow a similar path toward fragmentation, it is critical that ASTP/ONC and its⏎regulatory partners either 1) offer directional federal guidance or 2) formally defer to the private⏎sector to set AI security, transparency, safety and quality terms through the contracting process.⏎Fragmented regulatory approaches across agencies and states impede adoption and increase compliance⏎burdens. Premier strongly supports a OneHHS approach that harmonizes requirements across HHS⏎operating divisions.⏎2. Workflow integration challenges. The clinical workforce has experienced significant difficulty in integrating⏎AI tools within existing clinical workflows, EHR systems and operational infrastructure. AI tools often must⏎conform to complex and aging infrastructure, requiring expensive adaptation and reconfiguration. Many tools⏎fail to accommodate workflow constraints faced by clinicians, leading to low adoption and poor usability.⏎Additionally, implementing AI can introduce new workforce demands and training needs, especially when⏎tools alter established clinical processes. These constraints mean that even high-value AI products struggle⏎to be adopted at the point of care. Premier has long supported the development of a clinical workforce trained⏎to maximize the potential of AI and mitigate risks such as automation bias. Clinicians must understand when⏎and how AI recommendations are generated, and they should be empowered, not replaced, by technology.⏎Premier urges HHS to invest in education programs, establish best practices for human-AI⏎collaboration and promote AI tools that measurably reduce administrative burden.⏎3. Uncertainty around liability, risk and governance. Health care providers need clear information from the⏎developers of AI tools to ensure safe and appropriate use. For example, in order to determine whether it is⏎safe and appropriate to deploy an AI solution in a clinical setting, providers will need to know that a developer⏎has undertaken efforts to ensure that an AI tool is accurate and safe for specific populations, whether it has⏎gone through the FDA approval process and an explanation of how the how the AI model works.⏎Researchers and early adopters have identified concerns about AI tools in use in health care today that may⏎embed bias or have other unintended clinical consequences. More transparency on the training data and⏎algorithms used to create AI tools, as well as results from analyses and evaluations of AI tools, will support⏎clinicians and other end-users in assessing whether and how to use these tools. Premier has previously⏎supported the concept of model cards or other standardized transparency disclosures for high-risk AI⏎deployment scenarios in clinical settings.⏎© 2026 All rights reserved. | Premier Inc. | 3⏎⏎HHS RFI on Accelerating AI in Clinical Care⏎Page 4 of 7⏎IV. RECOMMENDATIONS FOR REGULATORY AND PAYMENT POLICY CHANGES⏎Cybersecurity⏎In order to drive greater adoption of AI in clinical practice, clinicians and healthcare organizations need assurances⏎that these novel solutions are cybersecure. Premier has firsthand experience with the challenges that provider⏎organizations face evaluating the security and dependencies of novel AI solutions and AI-enabled software. As a⏎leading traditional healthcare group purchasing organization (GPO), Premier works with several thousand healthcare⏎suppliers and vendors, including medical device manufacturers and digital health companies, to contract with⏎hospitals and health systems. Premier believes that securing healthcare’s digital infrastructure requires operators to⏎have visibility into vendor security practices. To that end, Premier requires digital health vendors to complete over⏎100 questions about security practices, independent third-party cybersecurity attestation or certification, company⏎governance, and use of AI, among other details. Hospitals and health systems are then able to review this information⏎before determining whether to offer that vendor a contract.⏎Premier’s contracting process includes supporting healthcare operators with model contract language around cyber⏎and AI liability and vulnerability disclosure and management. Premier encourages ASTP/ONC to provide⏎additional technical assistance and guidance to private sector organizations that can better prepare vendors⏎to collect and provide relevant AI data and practices, and can help operators to understand and evaluate the⏎risks present during contracting.⏎Additionally, the financial risk associated deploying novel technology also has a chilling effect on the adoption of AI⏎in healthcare. Over the past six years, downtime from ransomware attacks has cost healthcare organizations an⏎average of $1.9 million per day, according to one report. It is estimated that 92 percent of healthcare organizations⏎have experienced at least one cyberattack in the past 12 months. Premier urges ASTP/ONC to work with the⏎Assistant Secretary for Preparedness and Response (ASPR) to explicitly add cyberattacks as a component⏎of the hospital preparedness program (HPP) funding formula to help make funding available for cyber⏎resiliency. Premier also encourages HHS to work with federal partners, including cybersecurity coordinators like the⏎Cybersecurity & Infrastructure Security Agency (CISA) and the Office of the National Cyber Director and security⏎partners like the FBI, NSA, and Department of Defense, to understand the pressing, multi-dimensional threat that⏎cybersecurity poses to America’s healthcare delivery systems. These partnerships can foster a coordinated approach⏎to cybersecurity in healthcare and provide insights on appropriate data sources for the HPP funding formula.⏎Data-driven financing pathways⏎Performance-based contracting arrangements – and data collected on AI and software performance – can help to⏎decrease the go-to-market time and cost for new AI solutions, further extending America’s lead in innovative⏎technology. Through our experience as a leading healthcare GPO, Premier has a long track record of empowering⏎health systems to embrace innovative technologies, increase market competition and reduce the cost of healthcare.⏎Premier also has firsthand experience facilitating performance-based contracts between healthcare vendors and⏎providers to ensure the new technologies they purchase deliver the promised benefits and return on investment.⏎Premier also regularly evaluates new technology and AI solutions for inclusion in Premier’s digital health GPO⏎contracts. Premier has learned valuable lessons from tackling the unique challenges posed by payment for software-⏎as-a-service (SaaS) in healthcare. Premier urges ASTP/ONC to work with CMS to reduce regulatory barriers to⏎AI and software approval and payment. HHS should explore ways to allow data collected from performance-⏎based contracting arrangements to offer a pathway to CMS coverage and/or reimbursement decisions for⏎novel digital health technologies and SaaS solutions. Hospitals have already entered performance-based⏎contracts with innovative new healthcare suppliers, including AI technology and SaaS vendors. By making payment⏎dependent on predefined outcomes, these contracts make it easier for hospitals to take a chance on new⏎technologies. If the purported return on investment fails to materialize, hospitals can recoup initial costs.⏎Currently, the lack of reliable reimbursement for SaaS and digital health solutions means that providers must view⏎new technologies as a “pay now, save later” proposition. However, tight margins often make it challenging for⏎hospitals to invest up front in unproven technologies that may or may not result in savings down the road. For these⏎© 2026 All rights reserved. | Premier Inc. | 4⏎⏎HHS RFI on Accelerating AI in Clinical Care⏎Page 5 of 7⏎reasons, SaaS AI technologies face broad barriers to widespread use in healthcare. By incorporating information⏎from standard performance-based contracts as part of the pathway to payment, CMS can encourage early adoption.⏎ASTP/ONC should work with CMS to reduce regulatory barriers and open new pathways to sustainable financing for⏎novel digital health technologies and AI solutions.⏎Transparency⏎Trust – among patients, providers, payers and suppliers – is critical to the development and deployment of AI tools⏎in healthcare settings. To earn trust, AI tools must have an established standard of transparency. Some policy⏎proposals, including those proffered by ONC, suggest transparency can be achieved through a “nutrition label” model,⏎which lists the sources and classes of data used to train the algorithm. Unfortunately, some versions of the “nutrition⏎label” approach to AI transparency fail to acknowledge that when an AI tool is trained on a large, complex dataset,⏎and is by design intended to evolve and learn, the initial static inputs captured by a label do not provide accurate⏎insights into an ever-changing AI tool. Further, disclosure requirements around data inputs or algorithmic processes,⏎if not carefully considered, could force AI developers to publicly disclose intellectual property or proprietary⏎technology, which would stifle innovation.⏎Premier recommends that AI technology in healthcare should be held to a standardized, outcomes-focused⏎set of metrics, such as accuracy, false positives, inference risks and recommended use/applications.⏎Outcomes, rather than inputs, are where AI technologies hold potential to drive health or harm. Thus, Premier⏎believes it is essential to focus transparency efforts on the accuracy, reliability and overall appropriateness of AI⏎technology outputs in healthcare to ensure that the evolving tool does not produce harm. Premier has heard from⏎multiple member hospitals that the lack of clear vendor information about the use of AI and associated liability actively⏎deters them from purchasing or using AI tools. The lack of personnel and budget to collect information on data use,⏎cybersecurity and liability terms from vendors exacerbates this issue for all but the biggest health systems.⏎Premier urges HHS to consider requiring AI developers and manufacturers to list the acceptable uses of new⏎technologies in healthcare settings, which would provide much-needed guidance to clinicians and providers⏎on safe and appropriate use cases. This approach could provide liability protection for the proper use of AI⏎technology for the defined set of use cases where developers have established and reported the appropriate metrics⏎for accuracy and reliability. Transparency about the intended use of AI tools would be the simplest way for regulators⏎to incorporate AI governance into existing regulations. Health systems would be also able to incorporate this⏎information into their own governance structures, putting internal policies in place to prevent misuse of AI in ways⏎that could be detrimental to patient safety or experiences.⏎Such disclosure would give health systems and patients a complete picture of the safety and security of the AI⏎technologies they use. Rather than limiting or delaying innovation, such guidelines would level the playing field⏎between established market leaders and startups. Alternatively, HHS could sanction the use of third-party⏎certification organizations or existing market processes to address this challenge while reducing⏎administrative burden. As a GPO, Premier already requires vendors and suppliers to submit information⏎about uses of AI, data and cybersecurity certifications, and AI standards. Such a market-driven approach would⏎be flexible and adaptable, capable of adjusting to the latest developments in AI technology without requiring⏎regulators to frequently revisit the framework.⏎Harmonization of Requirements for Rural Providers⏎Premier urges ASTP/ONC to explicitly align any forthcoming AI policies with the Rural Health Transformation (RHT)⏎grants authorized under the One Big Beautiful Bill Act (P.L. 119-21) to ensure that rural providers are not required to⏎navigate fragmented or conflicting federal expectations. These grants represent a significant, time-limited federal⏎investment to help rural providers modernize care delivery and improve long-term sustainability amid meaningful⏎resource constraints.⏎RHT program grantees are being asked to modernize care delivery under significant financial and workforce⏎constraints. Any misalignment between AI policy guidance and grant requirements risks slowing adoption or diverting⏎limited resources toward compliance rather than innovation. Clear harmonization, particularly around interoperable⏎© 2026 All rights reserved. | Premier Inc. | 5⏎⏎HHS RFI on Accelerating AI in Clinical Care⏎Page 6 of 7⏎data access, AI governance expectations and permissible clinical use cases, would enable rural providers to more⏎effectively deploy AI tools supported by federal funding and mitigate post-grant technology cliffs. Harmonization is⏎critical to enabling rural providers to turn investments in innovation into improved access, quality and efficiency of⏎care.⏎V. AI-READY SUPPLY CHAINS REQUIRE INTEROPERABLE, STANDARDS-BASED DATA⏎Whether forecasting demand, detecting shortages, optimizing allocation or automating recall workflows, AI systems⏎are powerful tools for improving healthcare supply chains. However, these tools are only as good as the data pipelines⏎they consume. In healthcare supply chains today, non-standard identifiers, uneven barcode usage and siloed system⏎designs degrade data quality, inhibit linkage across organizations and constrain model performance. Premier’s latest⏎supply-chain insights confirm that volatility and shortages persist, underscoring the need for real-time visibility and⏎trustworthy data to power predictive and operational AI.⏎Global, open standards provide the most practical pathway to AI-grade data and bolster data quality and consistency.⏎Within healthcare and adjacent sectors, GS1 standards, including GTIN (products), GLN (locations), GS1-128/2D⏎barcodes (lot/serial/expiry) and EPCIS (event-level data exchange), create a common language for identification,⏎capture, and sharing that models can reliably learn from and act on. These standards are proven, technology-neutral,⏎and already widely globally deployed across supply chains.⏎Current data gaps undermining AI deployment in healthcare supply chain⏎Label/identity ambiguity leads to corrupted features and model drift. When the same product or location is⏎represented by different proprietary IDs across systems, AI pipelines struggle with entity resolution, leading to⏎duplicate or misattributed records and skewed features (e.g., false stockouts, phantom inventory). Premier’s⏎experience shows that adopting GLN as a common location “Rosetta stone” and GTIN/UDI for products reduces⏎mis-shipments and procurement errors -- exactly the kinds of noise that degrade model accuracy and stability.⏎Incomplete capture of lot/serial/expiry leads to gaps in risk and recall inference. AI-assisted recall triage and⏎risk scoring depend on precise, machine-readable item attributes. GS1-128/2D barcodes and UDI (GTIN) encode⏎those attributes for consistent ingestion into analytics and AI services, enabling faster, automated matching to⏎inventories and patient records and preventing product expiry or counterfeit use and allow authentication and tracking⏎of products throughout the supply chain.⏎Siloed event data leads to limited forecasting and anomaly detection. Predictive AI for shortages and lead times⏎requires standardized, time-stamped events (shipping, receipt, aggregation, de-aggregation). EPCIS provides a⏎common schema (already implemented across the global health care supply chain) and vocabulary for these⏎supply-chain events, allowing models to learn from comparable signals across trading partners and technology⏎platforms.⏎Uneven adoption means network effects never materialize for AI. Premier’s supply chain resiliency findings⏎indicate continued disruption and backorders, highlighting that individual adopters cannot unlock system-level AI⏎benefits without broader network uptake. Federal alignment is needed to close the adoption gap and allow AI to⏎generalize across sites and suppliers.⏎Premier’s recommendations⏎Incentivizing the movement toward fully interoperable supply chain data is essential for both clinical and⏎administrative functions in the healthcare sector. Federal leadership can uniquely address persistent barriers to⏎supply chain data interoperability. Accordingly, Premier recommends that ASTP/ONC consider the following:⏎Embedding supply chain identifiers in ONC certification criteria and APIs. ASTP/ONC should ensure that⏎certified health IT (including EHR-adjacent inventory/OR modules) can capture, store, and exchange GTIN/UDI and⏎GLN, and can ingest GS1 barcodes/2D symbols at receipt and point of care. Premier recommends requiring these⏎© 2026 All rights reserved. | Premier Inc. | 6⏎⏎HHS RFI on Accelerating AI in Clinical Care⏎Page 7 of 7⏎identifiers to be accessible via developer APIs so AI services can consistently consume them across organizations.⏎This addresses Premier’s observation that systems too often “do not speak GTIN/UDI,” limiting downstream analytics⏎and AI.⏎Publishing cross-agency guidance endorsing open standards for AI data pipelines. ASTP/ONC coordinate a⏎unified HHS position that GS1 identifiers (GTIN, GLN) and EPCIS event data constitute the recommended,⏎non-proprietary foundation for supply-chain data used in AI/ML, reducing mapping overhead and error rates while⏎improving explainability and auditability of AI outputs.⏎Advancing UDI/GTIN alignment for AI-assisted safety and quality. Premier urges ASTP/ONC to coordinate⏎across HHS – particularly with the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) – to reinforce GTIN as the canonical product⏎key in provider systems and item masters so AI agents can link product identity to lot/serial/expiry, claims and clinical⏎context for safety analytics, recall automation and other clinically-relevant insights.⏎Normalizing GLN for facility sub-location identity. Premier encourages ASTP/ONC to work across HHS to⏎promote GLN for standardized location master data used in supply-chain, administrative, and clinical flows.⏎Normalized geographies and care settings improve AI feature engineering (e.g., demand signals by department) and⏎reduce mis-routing that would otherwise confound model training.⏎Requiring machine-readable recall/shortage communications. Premier urges ASTP/ONC to coordinate across⏎the various HHS operating divisions to encourage or require inclusion of GTIN, GLN, and lot/serial in structured,⏎machine-readable formats (e.g., EPCIS) so provider AI services can automate signal detection, inventory matching⏎and patient impact assessment with minimal human reconciliation.⏎Interoperability in supply-chain data is foundational to national healthcare resilience, safety, and efficiency. Premier’s⏎experience underscores both the feasibility and the necessity of applying open, global standards such as GTIN, GLN,⏎GS1 barcodes, and EPCIS to unify the healthcare supply chain. ASTP/ONC is uniquely positioned to drive this⏎transformation by embedding data-standard expectations into certification, federal guidance and cross-agency⏎coordination. With targeted leadership, HHS can help shift the system from fragmented and reactive to connected,⏎transparent, and resilient, benefits patients and improving outcomes.⏎VI. CONCLUSION⏎Premier appreciates the opportunity to provide feedback on incentivizing innovation and AI adoption in healthcare.⏎For questions, please contact Mason Ingram, Senior Director of Government Affairs, at⏎Mason_Ingram@PremierInc.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎John Knapp⏎Vice President of Advocacy⏎Premier Inc.⏎© 2026 All rights reserved. | Premier Inc. | 7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0336♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Clinical data, genomics data, rare disease data, phenomics and Mendelian disease data, need to be integrated to bring digital biology and precision medicine to life.⏎AI systems need to reason over patient biology (using clinical data and genomics data).⏎Computable, digital biology needs to accelerate so that AI could have the potential to interpret genomics data to come up with a genomics blueprint for every patient.⏎Medicine and biology will need to become computable and programmable.⏎Living care plans would adapt as a patient’s biology evolves over time.⏎Digital biology needs to be a digital public good.⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0337♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Kahn McGuire, Sonia, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sonia♔Kahn McGuire♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a licensed clinical psychologist, I write in strong support of PsiAN's public comment urging meaningful federal regulation of AI in clinical care. We have already seen what happens when the federal government fails to regulate powerful technologies with significant public health implications — social media companies were allowed to grow largely unchecked, and we are still reckoning with the mental health consequences, particularly for young people. We cannot afford to repeat that mistake with AI, where the stakes in healthcare settings may be even higher.⏎Notably, many of the largest AI developers, including OpenAI and Anthropic, have expressed openness to working with federal regulators on commonsense oversight frameworks. There is a real opportunity here — and it would be a serious failure of leadership if we once again had to look to the EU to regulate companies that were built in our own country.⏎From a clinical standpoint, the lack of rigorous, independent evidence for AI safety and effectiveness in mental health contexts is deeply concerning. Decades of psychotherapy research consistently show that the therapeutic relationship is the single strongest predictor of treatment outcomes. AI systems, however sophisticated, do not form human relationships. Before any acceleration of AI adoption in behavioral health, we need enforceable privacy standards, independent outcome research using meaningful clinical metrics, and clarity about the appropriate role of AI relative to licensed clinicians. Patient wellbeing must come before corporate profitability. Thank you for your time. ♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0338♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ADVION Advocates, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ADVION Advocates♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎330 C Street SW⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop 7033A⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted Electronically Via Regulations.gov⏎RE: [RIN 0955-AA13] Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Dear ASTP/ONC Leadership,⏎Thank you for the opportunity to respond to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care. ADVION is a national organization representing⏎health information technology (health IT) companies that develop and distribute full clinical electronic⏎health records (EHRs), billing and point-of-care health IT systems and other software solutions serving⏎the majority of Long Term and Post Acute Care providers (i.e., assisted living facilities (ALF), Home⏎Health Agencies (HHAs), Inpatient Rehabilitation Facilities (IRFs), Long Term Care Hospitals (LTCHs) and⏎Skilled Nursing Facilities (SNFs)). ADVION also represents rehabilitation therapy companies; providers of⏎clinical laboratory and portable x-ray services; suppliers of complex medical equipment and other⏎specialized supplies. ADVION is a founding member of the Long Term & Post-Acute Care Health IT⏎Collaborative, which was formed in 2005 to advance health IT issues by encouraging coordination⏎among provider organizations, policymakers, vendors, payers and other stakeholders.⏎The following responses are tailored to long‑term post‑acute care (LTPAC) and rehabilitation settings⏎only, aligned to those questions and grounded in the RFI text.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎From a LTPAC and rehabilitation perspective, the biggest barriers cluster around infrastructure,⏎incentives, governance, and evidence.⏎ Fragmented data and limited interoperability⏎1344 I Street NW Suite 301 202.803.2385⏎Washington, DC 20005 www.advionadvocates.org⏎⏎ LTPAC and rehab providers often lack certified EHRs with modern APIs, which limits⏎access to the longitudinal data needed to train and deploy AI safely (e.g., labs, imaging,⏎claims, SDOH, home health notes).⏎ Many facilities still rely on paper, siloed point solutions, or minimal EHR modules, so the⏎“fuel” for AI (structured, standardized data) is missing or of poor quality.⏎ Misaligned payment and thin margins⏎ Fee‑for‑service and per‑diem payment in SNFs, IRFs, LTCHs, and home health create⏎little direct financial reward for investing in AI tools that may reduce hospitalizations or⏎improve function but do not clearly change billable units.⏎ Tight operating margins in LTPAC make capital‑intensive AI investments difficult absent⏎explicit reimbursement, shared‑savings participation, or clear programmatic support.⏎ Governance, liability, and clinical accountability gaps⏎ Many LTPAC organizations lack mature AI governance structures (e.g., AI oversight⏎committees, model risk management, bias review), creating organizational risk aversion.⏎ Uncertainty about liability when AI contributes to clinical decision‑making (especially⏎without device‑like FDA oversight) discourages use in nursing homes and rehab facilities⏎that already face high litigation risk.⏎ Workforce, workflow, and usability challenges⏎ Nursing, therapy, and medical director staffing constraints limit capacity to learn and⏎safely integrate new AI tools into daily workflows (e.g., falls risk prediction, staffing⏎optimization, documentation support).⏎ Many tools are designed for acute care and not adapted to high‑acuity, long‑stay⏎populations, leading to poor fit and low adoption in LTPAC.⏎ Limited LTPAC‑specific evidence and benchmarks⏎ Most AI evidence, benchmarks, and reference implementations focus on hospitals and⏎ambulatory care, not skilled nursing, IRF, LTCH, or home health populations.⏎ This lack of LTPAC‑specific performance data makes it difficult for providers and payers⏎to invest, particularly for smaller and rural facilities.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize⏎the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be⏎revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes⏎and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations. Several focused HHS actions could materially⏎accelerate effective AI use in LTPAC and rehab without compromising safety or equity.⏎ Payment policy and demonstration priorities⏎ Use CMMI authority to test LTPAC‑inclusive models that explicitly reimburse or share⏎savings for clinically validated AI that reduces avoidable hospitalizations, improves⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 2 of 9⏎⏎functional outcomes (e.g., IRF/HH functional measures), or reduces staff burden tied to⏎required documentation.⏎ Within prospective payment systems (e.g., SNF PPS under 42 CFR part 413, IRF PPS⏎under 42 CFR part 412), clarify that reasonable AI‑related clinical decision support and⏎care management tools are allowable costs for rate‑setting and recognize them in cost⏎reports.⏎ Clarify AI‑related expectations under existing health IT and interoperability requirements⏎ Through ONC’s Health IT Certification Program (45 CFR part 170), encourage or require⏎certified health IT used in post‑acute settings to support standardized data elements⏎(e.g., USCDI‑like content tailored to LTPAC assessments) and APIs that enable safe AI⏎integration.⏎ Use future ONC rulemaking and guidance to clarify expectations for AI‑enabled⏎functionality within certified EHR technology where LTPAC providers participate in ACOs⏎or other models that depend on certified health IT.⏎ Privacy, security, and HIPAA guidance tailored to AI⏎ Under existing HIPAA regulations (45 CFR parts 160 and 164), issue sector‑specific⏎guidance on use of AI developers and platform vendors as business associates, including⏎model training on de‑identified versus limited data sets and handling of conversational⏎data from ambient documentation tools.⏎ Highlight best practices for protecting residents’ and caregivers’ data when using AI⏎tools for behavioral monitoring, falls detection, or remote monitoring in shared living⏎environments.⏎ Programmatic design and technical assistance⏎ Embed AI‑specific technical assistance for LTPAC and rehab providers into existing HHS⏎programs (e.g., quality improvement organizations, regional extension‑type efforts)⏎focused on selecting, validating, and safely using non‑device AI tools.⏎ When updating any HHS AI‑related programs, explicitly include LTPAC, home health, and⏎rehab providers as eligible participants and prioritize small, rural, and safety‑net⏎facilities.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating to⏎liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist, and⏎what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them? In non‑device AI tools used as part of clinical⏎care (e.g., documentation assistants, risk stratification, workflow optimization), LTPAC and rehab⏎organizations face several distinctive legal and operational issues.⏎ Allocation of responsibility and liability⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 3 of 9⏎⏎ When AI tools suggest care plans, staffing levels, or risk scores, it can be unclear how⏎responsibility is shared among vendors, facility leadership, and clinicians if harm occurs,⏎particularly where regulations emphasize interdisciplinary care planning and physician⏎oversight.⏎ HHS could develop frameworks and guidance, in partnership with other agencies where⏎appropriate, clarifying reasonable expectations for documentation of AI use, clinician⏎override, and institutional governance to support defensible clinical practice.⏎ Privacy, surveillance, and consent in congregate settings⏎ Non‑device AI tools that rely on video, audio, or sensor data in shared spaces (e.g., for⏎falls or elopement detection) raise unique privacy, consent, and dignity issues for⏎residents, family, and staff in nursing homes and rehab facilities.⏎ HHS could issue best‑practice guidance on consent, signage, data minimization, and⏎secondary use of such data in LTPAC, aligned with existing HIPAA and civil rights⏎protections, to balance safety and privacy.⏎ Vendor contracting and indemnification⏎ Smaller LTPAC organizations often lack bargaining power to obtain balanced terms⏎around performance guarantees, bias mitigation, incident response, and⏎indemnification when contracting for AI tools that influence care.⏎ HHS could publish model contract clauses or voluntary templates that address⏎transparency (e.g., access to performance metrics, data provenance), audit rights, and⏎responsibilities when AI tools materially affect resident care.⏎ Documentation and survey readiness⏎ AI‑generated notes and recommendations may not align with surveyor expectations for⏎clear attribution, clinician signatures, or demonstration of resident‑centered care⏎planning.⏎ HHS can clarify, through guidance and interpretive materials, how AI‑assisted⏎documentation can be used while maintaining compliance with existing conditions of⏎participation and survey processes.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation methods⏎for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most⏎impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎LTPAC and rehab organizations need practical, low‑burden evaluation methods that recognize their data⏎and staffing constraints.⏎ Pre‑deployment and post‑deployment methods⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 4 of 9⏎⏎ Prospective “silent mode” evaluations in which AI tools run in the background without⏎affecting care, comparing AI outputs with existing decisions and resident outcomes, can⏎be feasible in SNFs, IRFs, and home health, especially for readmission, falls, or pressure⏎injury risk prediction.⏎ Ongoing post‑deployment monitoring using simple dashboards (e.g., calibration drift,⏎differential performance by race, language, cognitive status, or payer) can fit within⏎existing quality programs if supported by vendors.⏎ Human‑centered and workflow evaluations⏎ Usability testing with nursing, therapy, and aide staff in real LTPAC workflows (med⏎passes, therapy scheduling, restorative nursing) is critical, given high turnover and⏎varied digital literacy.⏎ Simulation‑based assessments (e.g., mock care conferences, documentation exercises)⏎can reveal where AI tools add burden or risk, particularly in interdisciplinary care⏎planning.⏎ HHS support mechanisms⏎ Grants and cooperative agreements could fund shared LTPAC “testbeds” where vendors⏎and providers evaluate AI tools using standardized measures and common data sets.⏎ Prize competitions could target AI tools that demonstrate superior performance and⏎usability in post‑acute outcomes (e.g., functional gains, community discharge,⏎rehospitalization), with clearly defined, transparent evaluation protocols.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry driven⏎testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can play an enabling role by recognizing and aligning with private sector initiatives that set quality⏎bars for AI in clinical care.⏎ Signal and alignment from HHS programs⏎ HHS could recognize independent AI accreditation or certification bodies that meet⏎transparent criteria (e.g., documented bias testing, post‑deployment monitoring,⏎user‑centered design) and allow participation or scoring advantages in relevant HHS⏎programs when accredited tools are used in LTPAC settings.⏎ Quality reporting and value‑based programs that include LTPAC and rehab providers⏎could incorporate optional measures or attestations related to use of accredited AI tools⏎that support quality and safety.⏎ Support for sector‑specific standards⏎ HHS could convene LTPAC‑focused stakeholders to define baseline expectations for AI⏎tools used in nursing homes, IRFs, LTCHs, and home health (e.g., data elements, minimal⏎performance metrics, explainability), which private accreditors could adopt.⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 5 of 9⏎⏎ Technical assistance and shared resources (e.g., checklists) can help smaller providers⏎understand and request accredited/certified AI solutions during procurement.⏎ Workforce credentialing and training⏎ HHS could support curricula or micro‑credentials for LTPAC medical directors, DONs,⏎therapists, and quality leaders on safe AI use, bias awareness, and governance, which in⏎turn supports safer adoption of accredited tools.⏎ Such training could be integrated into existing quality improvement and leadership⏎development programs that HHS or its contractors already support for LTPAC settings.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations⏎and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎In LTPAC and rehabilitation, many AI tools are emerging, but performance and cost experience is mixed.⏎ Areas where AI has met or exceeded expectations⏎ Ambient clinical documentation and coding support can reduce time spent on notes and⏎MDS/IRF‑PAI/HH documentation, decreasing burden for clinicians and enabling more⏎face‑to‑face care when well‑implemented.⏎ Predictive models for avoidable hospitalizations, when integrated into interdisciplinary⏎care management and nurse triage workflows, can help prioritize at‑risk residents and⏎support timely interventions.⏎ Areas where AI has fallen short⏎ Tools trained primarily on hospital or outpatient data often perform poorly on frail,⏎cognitively impaired, and multi‑morbid LTPAC populations, leading to low trust and⏎limited clinical uptake.⏎ Some solutions increase documentation or alert fatigue, adding complexity to already⏎stretched workflows and eroding any projected ROI.⏎ High‑potential, novel tools for LTPAC and rehab⏎ AI‑enabled functional status and mobility prediction to tailor therapy intensity,⏎anticipate discharge needs, and reduce rehospitalization risk in IRFs, SNFs, LTCHs, and⏎home health.⏎ AI tools that support behavioral health and dementia care in LTPAC (e.g., early detection⏎of behavioral escalation, personalized non‑pharmacologic interventions) while⏎respecting residents’ rights and privacy.⏎ Workforce optimization tools that align staffing, skill mix, and scheduling with dynamic⏎resident acuity and fall/pressure injury risk, supporting both quality and cost⏎containment.⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 6 of 9⏎⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the most⏎influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles to the⏎adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption of AI in LTPAC and rehabilitation is shaped by a distinct set of roles and administrative⏎constraints.⏎ Influential decision‑makers in LTPAC and rehab⏎ Facility and system leadership (CEOs, administrators, CFOs) and clinical leaders⏎(directors of nursing, medical directors, therapy directors) are primary decision‑makers⏎for AI that affects care delivery and staffing.⏎ At multi‑facility organizations, corporate compliance, IT, and quality leaders often act as⏎gatekeepers, assessing legal risk, interoperability, and alignment with quality and survey⏎requirements.⏎ Primary administrative hurdles⏎ Lengthy contracting, security, and legal review processes-compounded by limited⏎internal expertise on AI risk-delay or prevent implementation, especially in smaller⏎organizations.⏎ Survey, compliance, and documentation concerns create additional hurdles, as leaders⏎worry that AI‑generated outputs could conflict with regulatory expectations or be⏎difficult to explain to surveyors and auditors.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate⏎the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability is foundational to effective AI in LTPAC and rehabilitation.⏎ Data types and standards⏎ Consistent, computable access to post‑acute assessment data (e.g., MDS items, IRF‑PAI,⏎LTCH CARE data set, OASIS), medications, vital signs, problem lists, functional scores,⏎and social needs is critical for accurate AI.⏎ Continued evolution and use of standard vocabularies and data models relevant to⏎LTPAC (e.g., alignment of assessment items with standardized codes and FHIR⏎resources) would support safer model development and deployment.⏎ Interfaces and benchmarking tools⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 7 of 9⏎⏎ APIs that enable bidirectional exchange between hospital EHRs, LTPAC EHRs, and AI⏎tools (e.g., for transition‑of‑care summaries, risk scores, and care plans) would expand⏎markets and facilitate multi‑site evaluations.⏎ Shared, de‑identified benchmarking data sets spanning acute, post‑acute, and⏎home‑based care could allow developers to test models across care settings, including⏎LTPAC and rehab, before deployment.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers in LTPAC and rehabilitation settings face distinctive challenges that AI could help⏎address, alongside notable concerns.⏎ Challenges AI could help address⏎ Need for more timely recognition of clinical deterioration, pain, depression, and⏎functional decline in residents who cannot easily advocate for themselves, including⏎those with cognitive impairment.⏎ Desire for better communication about care goals, progress in rehabilitation, and⏎discharge planning across multiple providers and family caregivers.⏎ Key concerns about AI use⏎ Fear that AI will replace human interaction and reduce the personal, relational aspects⏎of care that are central to long‑stay environments like nursing homes.⏎ Worries about surveillance, loss of privacy, and data misuse when sensors, cameras, or⏎conversational systems are introduced into resident rooms and common spaces.⏎ Concern that AI might introduce or amplify bias, resulting in lower quality care or fewer⏎rehabilitative opportunities for certain groups (e.g., by age, disability, race, language, or⏎payer).⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎LTPAC and rehabilitation would benefit from targeted AI research and translation efforts.⏎ Priority research areas for HHS⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 8 of 9⏎⏎ Development and validation of AI models specifically for LTPAC and rehab populations⏎(e.g., frail older adults, individuals with complex disabilities) using representative data⏎from SNFs, IRFs, LTCHs, and home health.⏎ Research on AI tools that support functional recovery, safe transitions, medication⏎management, and dementia care in long‑term and post‑acute settings, including equity⏎and bias assessments.⏎ Published findings on AI impact in clinical care (general)⏎ Existing literature described in the RFI underscores that AI has shown promise in clinical⏎prediction, workflow support, and imaging analysis, while highlighting ongoing concerns⏎related to bias, generalizability, and real‑world performance.⏎ HHS’s interest in aligning regulation, reimbursement, and research & development⏎reflects recognition that translating such findings into safe, scalable AI use in everyday⏎care-including LTPAC and rehabilitation-requires coordinated policy attention.⏎ Approaches to costs, benefits, and transfers⏎ The RFI notes that HHS aims to ensure AI is deployed in ways that enhance productivity,⏎reduce burden, lower health care costs, and improve outcomes, indicating a focus on⏎total system value rather than isolated procedure‑level savings.⏎ For LTPAC and rehab, this implies research on how AI affects rehospitalizations, length⏎of stay, staffing efficiency, and caregiver burden, as well as distributional effects across⏎payers, providers, and patients.⏎Thank you again for allowing us to provide responses on this RFI. I am available to provide additional⏎input or clarification and can be reached at 202 803-2385 or Cynthia@ADVIONadvocates.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Cynthia K. Morton, MPA⏎CEO⏎ADVION Responses to ASTP/ONC & HHS⏎Accelerating the Adoption & Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care RFI February 23⏎2026 Page 9 of 9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0339♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Johns Hopkins Medicine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Johns Hopkins Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Johns Hopkins Medicine⏎720 Rutland Ave.⏎Baltimore, MD 21250⏎410-955-5000⏎February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎To: Department of Health and Human Services,⏎Re: Request for Information (RFI)—Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13; 90 FR 60108)⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment on the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS)⏎efforts to accelerate the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. We are at⏎a critical juncture in ensuring that we draw a clear balance between fostering innovation without⏎sacrificing safety and quality and unduly shifting liability to one side of the deployer/developer⏎paradigm. As both a developer and a deployer of healthcare AI, Johns Hopkins Medicine (JHM)⏎understands the need for this balance and commends HHS and the Assistant Secretary for Technology⏎Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP / ONC)⏎leadership1 in seeking feedback from the industry on these topics. Below, we provide comments on the⏎areas highlighted in the RFI (Regulation, Reimbursement, and Research), as well as provide specific⏎recommendations aimed at ensuring accelerated adoption of artificial intelligence in clinical care in a⏎safe, reliable, and innovative manner, without overburdening healthcare systems.⏎1. Regulation⏎a. Deregulation of Developers Has a Paradoxical Chilling Effect on Clinical AI Adoption.⏎i. Overview:⏎1 Although this RFI originates from ASTP/ONC, we understand that HHS has adopted a "One HHS" approach to AI, as articulated in the⏎December 2025 HHS AI Strategy. Given this coordinated, department-wide framework, our comments address regulatory considerations⏎that span all relevant HHS operating divisions—including the FDA, CMS, and ASTP/ONC—where AI oversight intersects with clinical⏎care delivery. We believe this cross-cutting perspective is both necessary and consistent with HHS's stated goal of providing a unified,⏎coherent approach to regulation of healthcare AI.⏎⏎JHM agrees that AI has enormous potential to transform healthcare delivery and is committed to⏎developing and deploying AI in ways that are innovative, compliant with current regulations, and safe⏎and beneficial for our patients. However, HHS’s focus on a path of deregulation for AI developers will⏎unintentionally chill AI adoption by healthcare AI deployers, rather than spur it. Without a trusted⏎framework to evaluate new AI tools, providers will gravitate towards engaging with known vendors for⏎AI tools, rather than assuming the risk of newer, unknown, start-up companies that have an unproven⏎track record and potentially shorter-term financial viability. To balance these priorities and encourage⏎adoption, we recommend a streamlined, less burdensome approach to regulating clinical AI tools—one⏎that is expedient but does not sacrifice accountability, and that appropriately allocates responsibility⏎among developers, deployers, and regulators.⏎ii. Explanation⏎In an environment where AI tools enter the market without meaningful federal oversight or validation,⏎the regulatory burden has been shifted onto the healthcare provider. The resultant fear of liability and⏎concerns for patient safety creates a chilling effect and adds significant administrative burden, requiring⏎individual systems to vet tools on their own, rather than at a centralized level. Specifically, healthcare⏎organizations looking to deploy these tools assume the entire burden of validation testing, bias⏎assessment, performance monitoring, and post-deployment surveillance—functions that would⏎otherwise be performed or overseen by regulatory bodies or developers, and that are nearly impossible⏎to operationalize within a healthcare organization at this time. This burden falls particularly heavily on⏎rural and community care organizations. Ultimately, clinicians and patients bear the risk when AI tools⏎underperform, produce biased outputs, or cause patient harm, and with limited recourse against⏎developers who have often disclaimed liability through contractual terms, providers will be hesitant to⏎adopt the tools.⏎In short: deregulation of developers has become de facto delegation of such responsibilities to⏎deployers. This asymmetry in responsibility creates a powerful chilling effect on AI adoption. The cost,⏎expertise, and time required to conduct rigorous internal evaluations of each AI tool—evaluations that⏎the tech industry is best equipped to perform—becomes a barrier to adoption. Moreover, in the⏎absence of reasonable federal regulations, states have started developing their own laws and regulations,⏎creating inconsistent approaches and making adoption of AI for multi-state providers challenging.⏎We urge HHS to recognize that thoughtful regulation is an enabler of innovation and a driver of⏎adoption—not an impediment to innovation. A dependable, well-understood federal regulatory⏎framework that appropriately allocates responsibility will provide healthcare organizations and providers⏎with the confidence to adopt AI tools, accelerate deployment timelines, and ultimately deliver the⏎benefits of AI to patients.⏎iii. Recommendations⏎We encourage HHS to adopt a regulatory framework that is proportional to the potential for⏎patient harm, increasing oversight for high-risk applications and enabling faster adoption for⏎lower-risk tools. Notably, “proportionate” should not mean “absent”—even low-risk AI tools must⏎meet baseline requirements that provide healthcare organizations and providers with actionable⏎information for deployment decisions. To achieve this, we propose a two-phase approach:⏎⏎• Phase 1: Expedited Pre-Deployment Review with Transparency Requirements⏎For clinical AI tools, rather than lengthy pre-market approval processes, to encourage innovation, HHS⏎could establish an expedited review pathway that focuses on transparency and documentation⏎requirements, minimum safety thresholds, and preliminary conditional clearance with clearly defined⏎conditions and monitoring obligations. As part of the pre-deployment review, HHS should require⏎disclosure of training data characteristics, known limitations, and performance metrics across diverse⏎patient populations to enable clinicians to make safe and appropriate clinical decisions in real world⏎settings. Without transparency requirements, health care systems are less likely to rapidly deploy⏎innovative technologies.⏎• Phase 2: Robust Post-Deployment Monitoring⏎The unique characteristics of AI—including the potential for model drift, performance degradation⏎over time, and differential performance across patient populations—make post-deployment monitoring⏎essential. We recommend that HHS establish or enhance regulatory mechanisms for mandatory adverse⏎event reporting, real-world performance surveillance, algorithmic auditing authority, expedited⏎withdrawal or modification authority, communication requirements to deployers and end-users, and⏎transparency in post-market findings that vests post-deployment monitoring responsibilities in AI⏎developers. AI developers are best able to modify the AI to accommodate any identified issues.⏎Alternatively, responsibility for such post-deployment monitoring could rest with regulators to ensure⏎transparency and consistent application across all deployments.⏎This two-phase approach preserves the speed-to-market benefits sought by the Administration and⏎positions AI developers, and not healthcare delivery organizations, to leverage their expertise and bear⏎responsibility for demonstrating baseline safety and fitness for purpose. Importantly, expedited review⏎would provide healthcare organizations with a regulatory signal they can rely upon, dramatically reducing⏎internal governance bottlenecks and accelerating adoption. Additionally, adding the post-deployment⏎phase aligns with the RFI’s recognition that “pre- and post-deployment" evaluation methods are critical⏎for clinical AI. By focusing regulatory attention on the post-deployment phase, HHS can enable faster⏎market entry while maintaining robust accountability throughout the product lifecycle. This approach⏎also better allocates responsibility among developers, deployers, and clinician end users, ensuring liability⏎matches degrees of control. Safe harbor provisions for health systems that follow established⏎governance and monitoring protocols could accelerate adoption while maintaining accountability.⏎b. Existing Regulatory Frameworks Are Ambiguous and Hard to Implement - Chilling⏎Development and Adoption⏎With respect to existing regulatory frameworks, we appreciate recent and ongoing efforts from the Food⏎and Drug Administration (FDA) to further clarify what constitutes clinical decision support (CDS) as a⏎device and when FDA intends to exercise its CDS enforcement discretion. However, the nuanced-fact-⏎dependent analysis required under the guidance becomes a significant impediment to development and⏎deployment of CDS. Rather than offering fact-specific examples, the guidance could be strengthened by⏎identifying the broader qualities and principles distinguishing CDS devices from other support tools.⏎Such an approach would address potential inconsistent application and interpretation across the⏎industry.⏎⏎The extensive internal review processes that healthcare organizations will develop to address the⏎complex guidance will delay deployment and result in inconsistency in the quality of patient care across⏎the industry. This will be exacerbated in rural and community care settings with deployers that are less⏎resourced. We recommend a regulatory framework that is easier to understand, easier to apply to⏎different fact-patterns, and that would lead to consistent application and create confidence in⏎the efficacy and safety of tools being deployed.⏎2. Reimbursement⏎We support HHS and the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) in the expansion of⏎pathways that support reimbursement for outcomes. AI has the potential to produce cost savings across⏎the care continuum. These benefits, however, will materialize over time and often deliver savings that⏎may inadvertently accrue more to payors, rather than the providers and health systems that bear the cost⏎and effort of AI procurement and implementation.⏎Reimbursement is critical for the adoption of AI tools in healthcare. To ensure equitable and widespread⏎adoption, we urge HHS and CMS to pursue targeted funding and value-based reimbursement initiatives,⏎supporting providers as they implement, monitor, and govern AI tools. This balanced approach will⏎prevent unintended fee-for-service consequences, protect core clinical services, and promote financial⏎sustainability as AI-generated savings and improved outcomes are realized over time.⏎With a balanced framework outlined above, implementation and infrastructure funding⏎opportunities could help leverage trusted care delivery entities to expand the use of AI,⏎increase cost-savings across the care ecosystem, and remain financially viable as the future⏎benefits of these tools manifest over time.⏎3. Research and Development⏎To drive accelerated adoption of AI in clinical care, investment will be needed in key research areas.⏎The costs of clinical AI are at risk of multiplying if reproducible learnings about the real-world⏎effectiveness of these tools, on both clinical quality, safety, outcomes, cannot be accelerated. Every⏎health system independently incurs significant human capital (expanding health technology and⏎engineering expertise) and infrastructure costs to deploy and monitor clinical AI safely. These⏎duplicative efforts slow adoption and limit our collective ability to learn what works. Increased⏎funding opportunities from HHS, the National Institutes of Health (NIH), the Agency for⏎Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ) and others to accelerate implementation science in⏎clinical AI would help both advance these implementation initiatives and share reproducible⏎lessons across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎Research should prioritize understanding human-AI interactions, which will have direct implications for⏎future regulatory and reimbursement structures.⏎To support payment evolution, the U.S. should fund health economics research on the cost of AI⏎procurement and implementation (including infrastructure, training, validation, and ongoing⏎monitoring), downstream impacts on healthcare utilization, clinical outcomes, and costs, time horizon⏎for cost reduction returns, and allocations of those costs and benefits throughout the healthcare⏎continuum.⏎⏎Clinical AI will be core to our healthcare future, which also means investments are needed in⏎undergraduate, graduate, and continuing medical education. Academic medical centers are positioned at⏎the center of this educational continuum. AI-in-education funding should support both research and⏎development of novel approaches to clinical training. Investment in clinical AI tools will yield minimal⏎return unless we educate, upskill, and reskill our current and future workforce. Training, validating, and⏎monitoring clinical intelligence—historically human, and now both human and artificial—is core to the⏎mission of academic medicine.⏎Finally, we believe high quality, validated healthcare data is one of the most important assets for⏎accelerating clinical AI development. However, that data does not exist at zero cost – especially⏎unstructured data, which represents the vast majority of the underutilized healthcare datasets. This data⏎represents clinical expertise and health system investment in centralized infrastructure for flexible use⏎including privacy protecting tools. We encourage funding opportunities that will accelerate⏎expansion of safe, secure, and federated data platforms to support AI tool research and⏎development.⏎Conclusion⏎Johns Hopkins Medicine appreciates the opportunity to comment on this critical effort to accelerate⏎clinical AI adoption in a safe and responsible manner. As a large academic health system, we are⏎committed to harnessing AI’s potential to improve patient outcomes, reduce clinician burden, and⏎address the workforce challenges facing healthcare today. At the same time, we recognize our⏎responsibility to ensure that AI is deployed safely, consistently, and in ways that maintain the trust of the⏎patients and communities we and our fellow health systems serve.⏎We stand ready to partner with HHS in this important work and welcome further dialogue on how⏎academic health systems can contribute to the responsible advancement of AI in clinical care.⏎Sincerely,⏎/s/ Deanna Hanisch⏎Deanna Hanisch, MS⏎Vice President and Chief Information Officer⏎The Johns Hopkins Health System Corporation⏎Brian Hasselfeld, MD⏎Executive Medical Director, Digital Health and Innovation⏎Johns Hopkins Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0340♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Rivera♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Marcus spent eight months going to emergency rooms in Mecklenburg County getting told nothing was seriously wrong, and then a cardiologist finally caught what three different ER teams missed — a real structural problem with his heart. He's 33. He's fine now but it was close, and the whole time I kept thinking about how differently those visits would have gone if he were a white guy in his forties with good insurance who looked like what doctors expect a "heart patient" to look like.⏎⏎So when I see HHS asking how to speed up AI adoption in clinical settings, my honest reaction is — speed up what, exactly? Speed up the part where an algorithm trained on data that already underrepresents Black men makes a faster wrong call? Marcus didn't need a faster system. He needed a more careful one.⏎⏎I work as a firefighter so I'm not going to pretend I understand all the technical details here. But I do understand who takes the risk and who takes the reward when hospitals bring in new technology. It's not complicated. The hospitals and the insurance companies are going to use whatever this AI rollout looks like to cut staff and automate more denials. That's not a conspiracy theory, that's just what happens every time healthcare gets a new efficiency tool. The savings don't go to patients. They don't go to the nurses and the medical coders and the administrative people who actually keep these places running — a lot of them Black women, a lot of them already underpaid. The savings go up.⏎⏎And I think HHS should be honest about that dynamic when it's thinking about what "accelerating adoption" actually means in practice. Because right now the question being asked is basically how do we get this in faster, and I don't see a lot of space being made for the question of who watches what happens after.⏎⏎What I'd want to know, and what I don't see clearly addressed, is whether there's going to be any requirement that hospitals actually track outcomes by race and demographics when they deploy these tools. Not just track them internally but report them somewhere that patients or community advocates could actually look at. Because if Marcus goes back to one of those ERs and now there's an AI-assisted triage system running, nobody is going to be able to tell him whether that system has been tested on populations that look like him, or whether it's been flagged for bias, or anything. He'll just get the result.⏎⏎I also keep thinking about the workers side of this. The comment period asks about barriers to adoption, and I get that, but nobody's asking about the barriers that get put in front of the people who lose something when AI takes over parts of their job. Those workers don't have a lobbying budget. They're not going to submit polished comments with citations. But they're real and this affects them and I don't think HHS is weighing that very seriously based on how this RFI is framed.⏎⏎I don't know what the right technical standard is for any of this. But I know Marcus almost died getting misdiagnosed by a system that already had all the human doctors and all the human information it needed to get it right, and the answer to that probably isn't to move faster with less accountability and call it♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0341♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Keenan, Gail , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Gail♔Keenan♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎As a PhD nurse researcher (>25+ years) who has focused on making EHRS useful and⏎usable to nurses in support of continuously improving EHRs and enhancing patient⏎outcomes –I find the EHR vendors the biggest barrier to efforts of innovators to⏎integrate AI into daily tools for nurses. Specifically, the big vendors (e.g., Epic) –make⏎it difficult for innovators to easily, simply, and cost effectively help reduce the⏎documentation burden and the effectiveness of clinicians. Access and integration are⏎cleverly blocked by vendors –who claim – “we can do this” and through their efforts in⏎adding band-aids to existing EHRs increase the complexity, cost, and documentation⏎time. Further the new additions to legacy EHRs are not adequately evaluated for the⏎overall impact on clinician’s workflow and patient outcomes.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Many changes recommended by the HTI-5 proposed rule will provide some relief from⏎the barriers EHR vendors employ to block superior innovations from the field from⏎getting traction . It is still possible for vendors (deep pockets) to cleverly put up barriers⏎that make it impossible for creative and knowledgeable innovations to see the light of⏎day. I say this from my personal experiences – with one example being that my team⏎created the nurse HANDS care planning software that was fully tested in multiple⏎hospitals and demonstrated the simple ability to collect interoperable standardized⏎nursing data in the same way across hospital systems with different vendors (much to⏎the surprise of many). HANDS provided many ways to support the nurse to create and⏎continuously update their nursing care plans with plenty of aids to support easy use.⏎The hospitals wanted to adopt HANDS but big vendors said they could do what we had⏎done – EPIC did a simple copy but their care planning system never became anything⏎close to what the HANDS systems provided. In fact –the system as it exists in EPIC is⏎not useful to the nurse. Try as we did –the vendor was successful in blocking our⏎efforts. The enormous expertise of our research team far exceeded the vendor⏎experiences including the absence of sufficient knowledge of nurses hired onto EPICs.⏎Epic hired nurses to sell and advocate for the EPIC system and these nurses did not⏎have adequate knowledge of EHRs to recognize the differences between HANDS and⏎Epic and further were hired solely to advocate for EPIC. Recommendation – seek to⏎find those pockets of experts (deep knowledge of the user group needs, AI, and⏎technology) like our highly qualified team at the University of Florida College of Nursing.⏎⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance⏎and accountability structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy,⏎and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if⏎any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Problem: LLM prompts can generate responses for which the source/s of evidence are⏎not always clear. If adopted—without validating the evidence this could result in errors⏎or adverse outcomes for patients. Overregulation of AI in the early phase however can⏎inappropriately limit the use of AI which in turn denies the enormous clinical benefits⏎that we see being generated by use. Further clinicians and patients are and will⏎continue to use AI (LLMs) in a variety of ways in spite of regulation to augment their⏎decision making – Important to find ways to make this easier and ensure safety.⏎Solution: Not all prompt responses by LLMs are necessarily capable yet of quickly⏎providing the evidence that backs the response. Some of responses are so⏎straightforward and face valid as to not need evidence for the clinician to weigh the⏎value of suggestion for the patient. Further over regulation at this point could⏎inappropriately slow use of AI in ways that patients and clinicians are unable to garner⏎the tremendous value of LLMs. Ensuring safe and effective use is key here….in the⏎early phases –it might be helpful for vendors to merely provide compelling rationale for⏎why their AI powered products are safe rather than for government to mandate the⏎creation of regulations for AI use that will be outdated within the year. Our bottom line⏎suggestions – When using LLMs to generate clinical decision support (CDS) 1) ensure⏎human is in the loop who decides to use/not use the CDS suggestions and is thus⏎responsible for all decisions (industry not liable) 2) provide a disclaimer for CDS⏎suggestions saying that clinician is expected to use one’s critical thinking/clinical⏎judgement in applying any suggestion and to seek further collaborating evidence when⏎in doubt. For AI in prediction models/algorithms vendor must provide evidence of the⏎how algorithms are computed and validated. Finally in general the government should⏎consider creating principles that are helpful in guiding innovators to demonstrate the⏎safety, strengths and limitations without resorting to stringent rules and regulations.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further⏎support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (⏎e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Our team strongly encourages the use of simulation in a representative set of use cases⏎for which the AI (LLM) output can be evaluated for suitability against a gold standard for⏎fit by a team of experts prior to implementation in practice. This procedure should be⏎⏎described fully by the vendor to help the potential buyer/user make decisions about use⏎based on their understanding of the validity and safety of the AI augmented predictive⏎models and LLM generated CDS suggestions. Our team has done this when reviewing⏎nursing care plan suggestions generated by Chat-GPT. We have also used RAGs to⏎constrain automatic coding of the nursing diagnoses, interventions and outcomes to the⏎latest version of the standardized terminologies used. Our testing has resulted in⏎eliminating nearly 100% of fully inappropriate suggestions. We have also noticed that⏎many of the LLMs now possess similar capability and might be used as a way to cross⏎check a response with one or more of the LLMs to further validate the quality of the⏎output (e.g., prompt is directed to multiple LLM models and outputs are compared).⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎AI capability is growing so rapidly that setting up rigorously developed and detailed⏎guidelines for accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing is⏎likely premature and if this approach is adopted progress will be substantially impeded.⏎Further, traditional ways of managing transformational tools will not work well with AI⏎and LLMs. I strongly recommend that the federal government seek to create contracts⏎with truly qualified experts to develop, test, and continue to improve the safe and⏎effective use of AI with a defined population. For example, the health care field to date⏎has typically focused on applying AI to medicine and then adapting use for the other⏎health care disciplines. This is costly and a waste of time. Nursing work and work flow⏎differ dramatically from medical care and thus adapting medical forms of AI to nursing is⏎wrong – minded and has created tools that work very poorly for nursing. Such tools end⏎up costing more in terms of money spent on the product, time spent trying to use the⏎product that does not work well, and the errors that result from the poor quality tool.⏎Further, most nurses do not have the knowledge and skill required to create safe and⏎effective tools for nursing and yet get hired and paid well to market poor tools (e.g., lots⏎of nurses employed by EPIC –who simply carry out the EPIC way without the skills and⏎knowledge to see and question the gaps that exist in that product. Since nurses front⏎for the product—it is unfairly assumed (in name) that the product is the best it can be⏎for nurses. Again – our nursing focused team at UF comprises a phenomenal group of⏎highly qualified nursing informaticists, engineers and technologists who possess the full⏎knowledge, experience and commitment to quickly transform the way nurses use EHRs⏎and AI in ways that reduce cost, increase care quality and patient and nurse⏎satisfaction.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give⏎new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎I don’t see tools that are actually working well in practice for nurse –because the giants⏎in the industry block that progress by not allowing the competition of new start-ups led⏎by creative groups to enter. Further the strategies the giants use to introduce new⏎technology are band-aid related and do not look at the holistic impact of the new bell or⏎whistle added to the already over complex and poorly connected technical systems.⏎Most importantly the strategies used to test products are poor and not focused on⏎creating universally useful products. Vendors hide behind the unique desires of their⏎individual customers when in fact if a tool is designed well for practice it likely will work⏎well across settings (be universally useful and interoperable). Tailoring products to the⏎customer nuances is merely an excuse to justify making more money and ensuring⏎interoperability never transpires. . Allowing this strategy to continue has resulted in little⏎to now progress, increased cost, and increased complexity –accompanied by⏎dramatically reduced quality.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care?⏎What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎The IT administrators seem to hold the most power in the AI/IT decisions⏎(administrators defer to them) and unfortunately these folks often do not hold sufficient⏎clinical background nor AI knowledge to be afforded this high status. Further these IT⏎officers become puppets of their EHR systems (e.g., EPIC) and tend to support blocking⏎of key information to potential innovators – holding the party line—which is “our vendor⏎will make it happen!” One need only look at the progress made in the past 20 years to⏎know that the vendors are not making it happen. Instead their approaches are⏎increasing costs and complexity without a increasing quality. This must stop and a⏎focus on seeking ways to support innovation in the EHR space that is external to⏎existing EHRS. We need tools that are universally (and legitimately) useful and⏎interoperable for all clinicians. As I mentioned, our team created an outstanding care⏎planning system that nurses used effectively in the exact same way in four different⏎institutions (folks said this was not possible). It is the vendors who perpetuated the⏎myth that EHRs must be fully tailored to support the uniqueness of each of their⏎institutional clients. Instead of relying on IT folks who have been coopted by their⏎industry EHR partners (e.g., EPIC) – perpetuating little to no cost effective⏎improvements in our EHRs – the market must be opened to the creativity of individual⏎and small businesses external to the current monopolistic EHR industry to enable the⏎creation, testing, and implementation of AI enabled technology solutions that can⏎quickly transform the stagnation that exists today. .⏎⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider⏎specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎A big gap in our health care system is the absence of access to interoperable nursing⏎data that can be used to evaluate and improve health care. This is somewhat baffling⏎given patients go to hospitals to receive nursing care and thus the quality and intensity⏎of that care have a crucial impact on patient outcomes. While attempts have been⏎made over the years, it has been difficult to gain the attention and resources needed to⏎capture nursing data and use it to improve practice. The wherewithal (technology, AI⏎etc) to collect and utilize nursing data cost effectively exists with groups IT nursing⏎reearchers/entrepreneurs at University of Florida, College of Nursing but resources are⏎needed to bring our work/tools to life in the clinical settings across the country and⏎world.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎The challenges our team sees for patients, caregivers, and clinicians alike is that most⏎do not recognize or understand the extraordinary advancements that can be made with⏎AI when used simply and smartly. Thus education and hands-on use are needed to⏎help the stakeholders see the many benefits of AI and through this experience also⏎learn how to use AI safely.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS needs to prioritize research in which AI is used as part of an overall approach to⏎transforming health care. For example, a limited AI tool may work well in some areas⏎and not in others, thus we recommend that the HHS prioritize funding of projects that⏎utilize and fully test AI as part of a holistic approach (comprehensive approach)⏎designed to dramatically improve documentation, communication, quality of care, and⏎user satisfaction while reducing the cost. Open the market fully to enable innovators⏎and start-up to transform the stagnation - the capability exists but it must be unleashed.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their⏎use clinical care?⏎Yes but these remain limited –continue to foster ways for those using and testing⏎AI to get their methods and result published /available to the public quickly⏎(incentivize this work and speed dissemination).⏎⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎To date the literature has predominantly included studies that show a positive/negative⏎impact of a narrowly focused feature/tool/AI on the health or care of the patient. Often⏎times the cost in dollars if mentioned do not include the overall cost to the entire system.⏎For example, ambient listening makes it easier to capture the full patient perspective in⏎real time but the clinician is expected to later re-read and validate the summary of the⏎experience and validate the output. This may or may not improve accuracy but clearly⏎increases documentation time of the clinician which is already at 30-40% of the nurses⏎time. In fact nursing documentation in US hospitals today costs >$60B a year most of⏎which brings little value at the point of care. Bottom line – it is crucial to find ways to⏎carefully but simply evaluate the overall benefits/negative impact of using AI anywhere⏎in the system. We support encouraging a report of the costs, benefits, and transfers to⏎the overall system for using AI in any part of the system.⏎II. Paperwork Reduction Act⏎In accordance with the implementing regulations of the Paperwork Reduction Act of⏎1995 (PRA), specifically 5 CFR 1320.3(h)(4), and OMB guidance, this general⏎solicitation is exempt from the PRA. Facts or opinions submitted in response to general⏎solicitations of comments from the public, published in the Federal Register or other⏎publications, regardless of the form or format thereof, provided that no person is⏎required to supply specific information pertaining to the commenter, other than that⏎necessary for self-identification, as a condition of the agency's full consideration, are not⏎generally considered information collections and therefore not subject to the PRA.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0342♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Davis♔♔Utah♔♔United States♔Intermountain Healthcare♔♔♔♔♔♔My name is Thomas Davis. I'm VP of Regulatory Affairs at Intermountain Health, and I've spent the better part of two decades working at the intersection of health policy and technology. What I'm about to say isn't easy to put in a public comment, and frankly it's the kind of thing that tends not to make it into formal submissions from health systems. But I think it needs to be said.⏎⏎The RFI asks what HHS can do to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. My concern is that the question assumes the bottleneck is regulatory friction. I'm not sure that's right. Or rather — I think the more serious problem is that the incentive structures driving AI deployment in health systems right now are not oriented toward the things HHS presumably wants to accelerate: better outcomes, better clinician support, safer care.⏎⏎Let me be concrete. At health systems operating under value-based arrangements, the most heavily funded internal AI use cases I'm aware of are not clinical decision support tools. They're workforce optimization systems and prior authorization automation. One major insurer — I won't name them, but this is well documented in trade press from Q3 and Q4 2023 — deployed an algorithm that was later shown in federal litigation to have denial rates far exceeding what physician reviewers would have approved. That wasn't an accident. The financial pressure to deploy that kind of tool is enormous. And there's essentially no regulatory counterweight.⏎⏎The workforce piece is the part I find hardest to talk about in my professional capacity, because the honest answer is that a meaningful share of AI ROI projections I've seen in health system budget documents are based on headcount reduction. Not "redeployment." Actual elimination of positions. And those positions are disproportionately in the administrative and coding functions that have historically employed women and workers of color. I'm not saying this is unique to health care, but when HHS frames this as an adoption acceleration problem I keep wondering who the adoption is supposed to benefit.⏎⏎Regarding the specific RFI questions around barriers to adoption — I work closely enough with health technology vendors to know that payer-side inconsistency in evaluation standards is real and genuinely burdensome. I've seen compliance cost estimates for meeting divergent payer requirements on a single AI product line run past $800,000. That's a legitimate problem worth addressing. But the solution to that problem cannot be to simply streamline the path to market without also asking what market incentives are shaping how these tools get designed and deployed.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to consider is whether reimbursement and safe harbor frameworks for clinical AI should include conditions around intended use and workforce impact disclosure. Not a full technology assessment mandate — I understand the practical limits there. But some mechanism by which health systems receiving payment for AI-assisted care have to represent what the tool is doing and who bears the downside risk when it's wrong.⏎⏎The liability ambiguity around non-device AI is real and I do think it needs resolution. But right now that ambiguity is mostly being resolved in favor of the deployer, which means when an AI-assisted denial harms a patient, or when an automated scheduling system misallocates staff in a way that affects care quality, the accountability chain is genuinely unclear. I've sat in meetings where legal counsel has explicitly noted that the current regulatory gap is commercially advantageous. That's not a sustainable basis for building public trust in these systems.⏎⏎I recognize this comment doesn't map neatly onto the RFI structure and I'm not sure the policy mechanisms I'm gesturing at are fully formed. What I do know is that if HHS accelerates adoption without addressing the incentive misalignment, we're going to be back here in five years having a different conversation about what went wrong, and the♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0343♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from UpDoc, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔UpDoc, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎1. Overview⏎UpDoc appreciates the opportunity to respond to the HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary and⏎ASTP Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in⏎clinical care.⏎UpDoc is a software medical device developer that produces physician-supervised clinical AI⏎workflow solutions designed to extend care capacity while preserving physician accountability.⏎Our platform enables AI-facilitated clinical interventions that operate exclusively within⏎preauthorized, physician-defined care pathways, and under the direction of a prescribing⏎physician. All actions are digitally documented and auditable. Consequently, the suggestions⏎that are proposed in this RFI Response only apply to AI systems that facilitate interventions⏎under the oversight and prescription of a physician.⏎We believe the principal barriers to responsible AI adoption are misalignment between existing⏎reimbursement structures and workflow-integrated AI healthcare delivery. Current time-based⏎fee-for-service billing models do not include productivity-enhancing tools. While CMS has⏎accommodated coverage and payment for certain Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) medical⏎equipment under Medicare Part B physician services, it has only done so in a few instances and⏎by forced cross-walks that are largely inapplicable. The lack of predictable coverage and⏎payment for productivity-enhancing medical device software may fragment care delivery and⏎weaken physician adoption.⏎We respectfully offer three guiding principles for federal policy consideration:⏎1. Payments should increasingly reimburse physicians for both care outputs and⏎validated workflows, not only for software licenses or time.⏎2. AI-enhanced clinical care must remain physician-anchored, with defined guardrails⏎that preserve accountability and safety.⏎3. Digitized workflows enable both scalability and oversight, providing auditability, fraud⏎mitigation, and a pathway toward outcome-based reimbursement models.⏎Our responses below address the specific questions posed in the RFI.⏎Please note: This response is NOT intended to provide feedback for facilitation of AI systems⏎that deliver care independently of physician oversight or prescription authority that may⏎involve other legal state authorities, governance structures, and other payment regimes.⏎⏎ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎2. Responses to Specific Questions⏎Response to Question 1: What barriers impede adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Direct Answer:⏎The primary barriers to adoption are structural reimbursement misalignment within existing⏎CMS payment methodologies, such as time-based billing constructs that do not account for⏎improvements in efficiency. There presently does not exist accountability frameworks for⏎AI-enabled clinical actions.⏎Explanation:⏎Under the Physician Fee Schedule methodology, medical device software expenses are generally⏎treated as indirect, non-allocable practice expenses rather than direct medical equipment⏎inputs. This categorization creates structural reimbursement barriers for workflow-integrated AI⏎medical device software technologies which are not generic off-the-shelf IT.⏎When workflow-integrated AI functions as core clinical infrastructure, classification as indirect⏎practice expense fails to reflect its role in delivering physician-directed care.⏎In addition, many asynchronous care services and codes (e.g., Digital E/M, Care Management⏎Services such as CCM, RPM, etc.) require documentation of human time spent supervising care.⏎When AI systems increase productivity and reduce required clinician time, physicians may⏎paradoxically experience revenue reduction, creating a financial disincentive to adoption.⏎Organizations face uncertainty as AI-enabled workflows are operationalized. Clear federal⏎guidance reinforcing coverage and payment of software as a medical device-enabled workflows⏎would materially reduce uncertainty and accelerate responsible adoption.⏎Response to Question 2: What payment policy changes would accelerate⏎adoption?⏎Direct Answer:⏎CMS should establish clear, non-time-based reimbursement pathways for asynchronous care,⏎much like it has begun to do by creating Advanced Primary Care Management (APCM) services.⏎CMS created APCM service codes under the existing FFS methodology to support primary care⏎providers in delivering comprehensive, continuous, and coordinated care while omitting⏎time-based requirements to facilitate a shift towards value-based care. Similarly, CMS should⏎explore creating additional services (i.e. HCPCS codes) to recognize and reimburse⏎⏎ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎physician-supervised, AI-enhanced clinical actions that reflect Medical Decision Making (MDM)⏎complexity. In parallel, CMS should broaden and strengthen reimbursement pathways for⏎asynchronous care to align payment with the evolving clinical realities of AI-enabled care as it⏎scales in impact and associated liability exposure beyond real-time encounters.⏎Explanation:⏎Near-term reforms could include:⏎● Explicit recognition that AI-facilitated clinical interventions under physician supervision⏎contribute to MDM complexity.⏎● Creation of new services or HCPCS G-codes for asynchronous E/M services that leverage⏎provider AI oversight that includes a new form of MDM.⏎● Clear CMS guidance that validated workflow substitution by AI may serve as a lower-cost⏎alternative to face-to-face encounters where clinically appropriate.⏎These clarifications could be implemented within existing statutory authority and CPT⏎infrastructure without requiring new legislation, enabling productivity-enhancing AI to increase⏎care capacity without penalizing physicians economically.⏎Response to Question 3: Legal and governance considerations⏎Direct Answer:⏎AI-enabled clinical care should operate within human physician-anchored accountability⏎frameworks that preserve prescribing authority, enforce deterministic care boundaries, and⏎ensure full auditability of interventions.⏎As AI capabilities evolve, governance frameworks should scale proportionately, ensuring that⏎systems operating with higher degrees of autonomy are accompanied by commensurate⏎human-in-the-loop safeguards, transparency, and accountability.⏎Explanation:⏎AI systems delivering clinical interventions under physician oversight and payment structures⏎should:⏎● Operate exclusively within preauthorized, physician-defined care pathways.⏎● Block actions outside approved clinical parameters.⏎● Maintain comprehensive digital logs of all patient interactions and interventions.⏎⏎ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎● Reinforce that ultimate clinical responsibility remains with the supervising physician, and⏎that AI systems should not independently initiate or modify treatment plans outside⏎physician-defined parameters.⏎The above applies only to AI systems that facilitate interventions under the oversight and⏎prescription of a physician. For these systems, payment models that operate independently of⏎physician-led workflows may create fragmentation and unclear accountability pathways, and⏎federal guidance should prioritize integrated, physician-led models. The above does not apply⏎to AI systems that deliver care independently of physician oversight or prescription authority⏎that may involve other legal state authorities, governance frameworks and payment regimes.⏎Response to Question 4: Evaluation Methods⏎Direct Answer:⏎AI-enabled clinical care should be evaluated at the clinical workflow level, with emphasis on⏎real-world evidence, pathway adherence, substitution effects, safety monitoring, and⏎auditability rather than solely on isolated algorithmic performance metrics.⏎Explanation:⏎Evaluation frameworks should assess:⏎● Clinical outcomes achieved through AI-facilitated workflow substitution.⏎● Adherence to defined, physician-approved care pathways.⏎● Safety, utilization, and outcome metrics in real-world settings.⏎● Reduction in higher-cost synchronous encounters where clinically appropriate.⏎● Embedded digital audit capabilities supporting post-market monitoring.⏎Evaluation should also assess whether AI-enabled workflows substitute for higher-cost⏎encounters without compromising clinical quality and should recognize that AI systems⏎deployed within bounded clinical workflows differ materially from standalone algorithmic tools.⏎Response to Question 5: Accreditation or Certification Pathways⏎Direct Answer:⏎Accreditation frameworks should distinguish between AI systems that facilitate interventions⏎under the oversight and prescription of a physician, and AI systems that deliver care⏎independently of physician oversight or prescription authority.⏎⏎ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎For AI systems that facilitate interventions under the oversight and prescription of a physician,⏎physician-supervised AI workflows should be evaluated for transparency, guardrails, and⏎accountability in addition to algorithmic validation. There should be proportional oversight⏎standards to systems operating with higher degrees of independent clinical authority.⏎Explanation:⏎Accreditation should emphasize system-level safeguards, not only algorithm validation in⏎isolation.⏎Certification standards may include:⏎● Clear physician oversight and accountability structures.⏎● Configuration controls that prevent out-of-pathway actions.⏎● Inspectable audit logs documenting all AI-facilitated interventions.⏎● Workflow-level transparency regarding how clinical actions are initiated and validated.⏎Such standards would reinforce trust while allowing innovation to scale responsibly.⏎Response to Question 6: Where AI Has Succeeded or Fallen Short⏎Direct Answer:⏎AI has shown success in structured, protocol-driven care environments but has faced challenges⏎in fragmented spaces that operate outside physician-led care coordination.⏎Explanation:⏎Earlier generations of standalone Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) tools demonstrated⏎strong algorithmic performance but often struggled to achieve durable adoption when not⏎embedded within physician-led workflows.⏎Success is most durable when AI augments physician capacity rather than bypassing established⏎care relationships.⏎AI performs well in medication titration, chronic disease management, and other longitudinal,⏎protocol-driven care management settings where clinical boundaries are clearly defined by⏎physicians who give authorization for AI to join care teams under their pre-set conditions.⏎Adoption is more limited when reimbursement structures separate AI-enabled services from⏎established care teams or create economic disincentives for physician participation. Such⏎⏎ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎reimbursement structures can result in physician-AI discoordination, frequent AI-triggered⏎safety pauses, or higher risks of safety events.⏎Response to Question 7: Organizational Adoption Barriers⏎Direct Answer:⏎Organizational barriers include revenue cannibalization risk under time-based billing,⏎uncertainty regarding liability allocation and compliance expectations, integration complexity⏎within existing care infrastructure, and change management challenges within clinical teams.⏎Explanation:⏎Health systems are reluctant to adopt technologies that may reduce billable time without⏎corresponding payment recognition. Clear MDM-based reimbursement pathways would reduce⏎financial uncertainty.⏎In addition, ambiguity regarding physician accountability and compliance expectations can slow⏎internal approval processes. Clear federal coding / reimbursement action and guidance⏎reinforcing physician-supervised AI models would mitigate this hesitation.⏎Finally, AI adoption requires integration into existing EHR systems, care team workflows, and⏎operational governance structures. Interoperability alignment and implementation clarity are⏎essential to reduce integration burden.⏎Published analyses have demonstrated meaningful reductions in synchronous visit utilization⏎when AI-enabled workflows are properly integrated into physician-supervised care models.⏎Response to Question 8: Interoperability⏎Direct Answer:⏎AI-enabled workflows require structured, interoperable data aligned with ONC-adopted⏎interoperability standards to ensure safe, scalable integration into clinical systems.⏎Explanation:⏎Standardized data exchange is essential for ensuring AI interventions are context-aware,⏎clinically appropriate, and safely integrated into existing care workflows.⏎⏎ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎Data liquidity enables substitution of encounters, real-time documentation, and quality⏎monitoring. Alignment with federal interoperability standards reduces integration friction and⏎supports consistent implementation across health systems.⏎Interoperability policy should prioritize bidirectional data exchange that supports both clinical⏎action and transparent documentation of AI-facilitated care.⏎Response to Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Concerns⏎Direct Answer:⏎Patient trust depends on clear disclosure of AI involvement. For AI systems that facilitate⏎interventions under the oversight and prescription of a physician, there should be transparent⏎physician oversight, clinically bounded pathways that prevent overuse, and assurance that AI⏎tools operate within defined safety constraints.⏎Explanation:⏎For AI systems that facilitate interventions under the oversight and prescription of a physician,⏎transparent communication regarding the physician’s continued oversight of AI is central to⏎maintaining patient trust.⏎For these systems, digitized workflows enhance safety monitoring and preserve accountability⏎by ensuring all AI-facilitated interventions are documented and reviewable.⏎Clinically bounded, defined guardrails mitigate concerns about inappropriate utilization or⏎overreach.⏎Clear policies and regulatory structures detailing physician supervision vs independent⏎autonomy can reassure patients that AI tools are being regulated and used responsibly.⏎Response to Question 10: Research Priorities⏎Direct Answer:⏎HHS should prioritize research evaluating AI-enabled clinical workflow substitution models,⏎measurable cost and utilization impacts from asynchronous care, and transition pathways⏎toward outcome-based reimbursement frameworks.⏎Explanation:⏎⏎ONC ASTP Feb 2026 RFI Initial Response 2/12/26⏎Research should measure both cost substitution effects and patient outcome metrics under⏎controlled guardrails.⏎Pilot programs, including those conducted through the CMS Innovation Center, could evaluate⏎whether physician-supervised AI workflows safely substitute for higher-cost synchronous⏎encounters while maintaining or improving quality.⏎Such research should assess safety, utilization, access expansion, and longitudinal outcomes to⏎ensure productivity gains translate into clinical value rather than excess utilization.⏎Evidence generated through structured pilots would inform scalable payment policy reform⏎while maintaining accountability and patient trust.⏎3. Conclusion⏎UpDoc appreciates HHS’s leadership in examining how AI can responsibly expand clinical⏎capacity. Clear alignment between reimbursement, governance, interoperability, and physician⏎accountability will be essential to accelerating safe and trusted adoption of physician-supervised⏎AI in clinical care.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0344♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Helios Intelligence, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Helios Intelligence♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to the Department of Health and Human Services Request for⏎Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Helios Intelligence⏎I. Introduction⏎Helios respectfully submits these comments in the attached response to the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) Request for Information (RFI) on accelerating the adoption and use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care, published in the Federal Register on December 23, 2025 (90 FR⏎60108).⏎Helios is a clinical AI company building advanced models and specialized agents to automate and⏎enhance clinical care and biomedical research. Our platform unifies fragmented, multimodal patient data⏎— from EHRs, labs, genomics, imaging, and wearables — into a continuously updated, longitudinal⏎patient model that powers clinical reasoning, decision support, and care optimization. We work across⏎the healthcare ecosystem, from health systems and clinical laboratories to payers and direct-to-consumer⏎health platforms.⏎We applaud HHS for soliciting industry input on this critical topic. Our comments attached are grounded⏎in our direct experience building, deploying, and validating clinical AI solutions across the healthcare⏎ecosystem.⏎Helios Intelligence⏎Website: https://www.heliosinc.xyz/⏎Email: hello@heliosinc.xyz⏎II. Responses to Specific Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for healthcare and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The single greatest barrier to AI innovation in healthcare is the fragmentation of patient data across⏎incompatible systems and modalities. Patient information today is scattered across EHRs, laboratory⏎information systems, genomic databases, imaging archives, pharmacy records, and wearable devices —⏎each with its own format, access model, and update cycle. No common data representation exists across⏎these modalities, meaning each requires specialized parsing and interpretation.⏎This fragmentation has a cascading effect on both clinical care and AI development. Clinicians are⏎forced to serve as the manual "integration layer," synthesizing numerous data sources per patient⏎encounter — an inefficient process that leads to missed connections and diagnostic delays. For AI⏎developers, the lack of standardized, interoperable data makes it extraordinarily difficult and expensive⏎to build models that can reason holistically about a patient's health over time. Even the most advanced⏎large language models are not natively built to route each dataset to the right analytical tool or to⏎⏎maintain a longitudinal patient state, and as a result, their clinical reasoning degrades in multimodal⏎contexts.⏎Beyond data fragmentation, additional barriers include regulatory uncertainty around the classification⏎and oversight of non-medical-device AI tools, the absence of reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled⏎clinical services, and the difficulty of integrating new AI tools into existing clinical workflows without⏎disrupting care delivery. The reimbursement landscape is particularly challenging: as of January 2026,⏎only three AI solutions have received Category I CPT codes, and most AI tools are billed under⏎temporary Category III codes with no guaranteed payment. This uncertainty limits the financial viability⏎of clinical AI solutions and incentivizes developers to prioritize administrative AI—where revenue⏎models are more predictable—over clinical AI with greater patient impact.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎HHS should prioritize a multi-pronged approach that combines regulatory clarity, payment innovation,⏎and programmatic support. We propose several frameworks that we believe could be transformative.⏎Regulatory and Programmatic Priorities:⏎Government-Sponsored Clinical AI Agent Marketplace. HHS should establish a curated,⏎government-endorsed platform where validated clinical AI agents can be discovered, evaluated, and⏎deployed by health systems. Health systems today face an overwhelming and opaque landscape of AI⏎tools with no standardized way to compare them. A government-endorsed marketplace, with clear⏎validation requirements and transparent performance data, would dramatically lower the barrier to⏎adoption. Agents could be organized by clinical specialty, condition (e.g., cardiology agents, chronic⏎care agents, oncology agents), and risk level, allowing health systems to find the right tool for their⏎needs. The ARPA-H ADVOCATE program, which is funding the development of an FDA-authorized AI⏎agent for cardiovascular disease, demonstrates that the government is already investing in⏎condition-specific AI agents; the marketplace concept would scale this approach across the full spectrum⏎of clinical care.⏎NPI-Equivalent Identifiers for AI Agents. Just as every clinician in the United States has a unique⏎National Provider Identifier, validated AI agents should be assigned unique identifiers that allow them to⏎be tracked, credentialed, audited, and billed against within the healthcare system. This would enable⏎healthcare organizations to establish AI Agent Credentialing Committees — formal governance⏎bodies, similar to existing medical staff committees, that review, approve, and continuously monitor the⏎AI agents deployed in their clinical environments. This framework would bring the same rigor and⏎accountability to AI deployment that already exists for human clinicians, building trust among providers⏎and patients alike.⏎Expanded ARPA-H Funding for Disease-Specific Agents. The ADVOCATE model — which funds the⏎development of autonomous AI agents with a clear FDA authorization pathway and a defined timeline⏎— should be replicated for other high-burden conditions such as diabetes, COPD, and cancer. This⏎would accelerate the creation of a library of validated, condition-specific AI agents that could populate⏎the proposed marketplace.⏎Payment Policy Priorities:⏎⏎Expand the CMS ACCESS Model. The CMS ACCESS (Advancing Chronic Care with Effective,⏎Scalable Solutions) model is the right foundation — it already tests outcome-aligned payment in⏎Original Medicare and explicitly promotes technology-enabled care. HHS should make ACCESS the⏎rule, not the exception, by rapidly expanding the model beyond its current scope and using it as the⏎template for how AI-enabled care is reimbursed across Medicare. Specifically, ACCESS should be⏎extended to cover a broader range of AI-driven interventions — not just chronic care management, but⏎also AI-powered diagnostics, care gap detection, and preventive screening — so that any AI tool⏎demonstrating measurable improvements in patient outcomes (reduced readmissions, earlier detection,⏎lower total cost of care) has a clear path to reimbursement.⏎Accelerate Development of CMAA Codes. Beyond expanding ACCESS, HHS should work with the⏎AMA to accelerate the development of the proposed "Clinically Meaningful Algorithmic Analyses"⏎(CMAA) codes — a new coding classification within the CPT code set specifically designed for⏎algorithm-based services. Today, most clinical AI tools are limited to temporary Category III codes with⏎no guaranteed payment and wide geographic variation in pricing set by regional MACs. CMAA codes⏎would create a dedicated, nationally consistent billing pathway for clinical AI, giving developers the⏎reimbursement certainty they need to invest in clinical — not just administrative — AI.⏎AI-Specific Value-Based Purchasing Incentives. Finally, HHS should explore AI-specific value-based⏎purchasing incentives that go beyond ACCESS. For example, Medicare Advantage Star Ratings could⏎be updated to reward plans that deploy validated AI agents for proactive care management, and shared⏎savings programs could include explicit bonuses for AI-driven cost reductions that maintain or improve⏎quality.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist... and what⏎role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The deployment of non-medical-device AI in clinical care raises several novel legal and implementation⏎challenges that existing governance structures are not fully equipped to address.⏎Liability and accountability are the most pressing concerns. When an AI agent contributes to a clinical⏎decision — for example, by flagging a care gap, interpreting a lab result, or synthesizing a patient's⏎medical history — the question of who bears responsibility for errors is often unclear. Is it the AI⏎developer, the healthcare provider who acted on the recommendation, or the institution that deployed the⏎tool? HHS can play a constructive role by developing model frameworks for shared responsibility⏎that encourage transparency, accountability, and the use of validated AI tools, without imposing liability⏎structures so burdensome that they stifle innovation. The AI Agent Credentialing Committees proposed⏎in our response to Question 2 would further clarify lines of accountability by creating a formal⏎institutional process for evaluating and approving AI agents before deployment.⏎Privacy and security concerns are amplified by the volume and sensitivity of data that clinical AI⏎systems process. While HIPAA provides a critical baseline, the emergence of AI tools that synthesize⏎data across multiple modalities and maintain longitudinal patient states requires a renewed focus on data⏎governance. HHS should promote the adoption of privacy-enhancing technologies such as federated⏎learning and differential privacy, and provide explicit guidance on how existing HIPAA provisions apply⏎to AI-specific data processing activities, including the generation and storage of AI-derived patient⏎representations.⏎Implementation challenges include the need for robust change management within healthcare⏎organizations, the difficulty of integrating AI tools with legacy IT systems, and the absence of⏎standardized credentialing or certification processes for clinical AI. The NPI-equivalent identifier and⏎⏎credentialing committee frameworks proposed in Question 2 would directly address the credentialing⏎gap and give health systems a structured pathway for governing AI deployment.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods... and⏎should HHS further support these processes?⏎The most promising evaluation methods are those that assess AI performance in realistic clinical⏎contexts using clinician-led, blinded, randomized protocols. Standard NLP benchmarks often fail to⏎capture the nuances of clinical decision-making. Evaluations should test models on their ability to⏎perform specific, clinically relevant tasks — such as synthesizing multimodal patient data, identifying⏎care gaps, or generating accurate differential diagnoses — as judged by practicing physicians.⏎However, expert physician evaluation does not scale. To solve this, HHS should fund the creation of a⏎National Repository of Clinician-Backed Grading Rubrics — a structured, open-access library of⏎evaluation criteria authored and validated by hundreds or thousands of expert physicians across every⏎major specialty. These rubrics would codify what "good" clinical reasoning looks like for specific tasks⏎(e.g., interpreting a complex lab panel, triaging disease presentation, recommending a treatment pathway⏎for a comorbid patient). Once established, these rubrics could be used to power LLM-as-a-Judge⏎evaluation systems — where a secondary AI model, guided by the physician-authored rubrics, can grade⏎the outputs of clinical AI tools at scale with a level of rigor that adheres to strict guidelines set by the⏎expert community. This approach would make high-quality, clinician-aligned evaluation continuous,⏎scalable, and affordable, rather than limited to expensive, one-off human review studies.⏎For post-deployment evaluation, continuous monitoring frameworks that track model performance, drift,⏎and safety in real-world clinical settings are essential. The ARPA-H ADVOCATE program's plan to⏎develop a disease-agnostic supervisory agent for monitoring AI systems is a promising approach that⏎could be generalized across the industry.⏎HHS should further support these processes through several mechanisms:⏎Federated Evaluation Networks. HHS should fund the creation of government-supported⏎infrastructure that allows AI models to be tested across multiple institutions on diverse patient⏎populations without sharing raw patient data. This infrastructure would preserve privacy while enabling⏎robust, multi-site validation that produces generalizable results.⏎Open-Source Clinical AI Benchmarks. HHS should fund the creation of publicly available,⏎high-fidelity synthetic patient datasets and standardized evaluation toolkits for a range of clinical⏎specialties. This would level the playing field for all developers — including smaller, innovative⏎companies — and ensure that models are benchmarked against common, clinically meaningful standards⏎rather than proprietary datasets.⏎Prize Competitions. HHS should sponsor prize competitions that benchmark AI models on specific,⏎high-impact clinical challenges, such as care gap detection and treatment pathway optimization. These⏎would stimulate innovation, establish public performance baselines, and generate valuable data on the⏎state of the art.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities... to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can most effectively support the private sector by acting as a convener, endorser, and data⏎provider.⏎⏎As a convener, HHS should bring together multi-stakeholder working groups — including AI⏎developers, healthcare providers, payers, patient advocates, and academic researchers — to develop⏎consensus-based standards for clinical AI evaluation, deployment, and monitoring. These standards⏎should be flexible enough to accommodate the rapid pace of AI innovation while rigorous enough to⏎ensure patient safety.⏎As a data provider, HHS should make more de-identified government datasets available for AI model⏎training and evaluation. The NIH, CMS, and VA hold vast repositories of clinical data that, if made⏎accessible through secure, privacy-preserving mechanisms such as federated learning infrastructure,⏎could dramatically accelerate the development of high-quality clinical AI models. The transformative⏎potential of accessible research data for training multimodal clinical models cannot be overstated.⏎As an endorser, HHS should lend its credibility to industry-led certification and testing initiatives that⏎meet rigorous standards. The CMS Health Tech Ecosystem pledge program for conversational AI⏎assistants, which already includes early adopters like major technology companies and health systems, is⏎an excellent model for how government can catalyze AI adoption through voluntary industry⏎commitments without heavy-handed regulation.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce⏎costs?⏎Where AI has met or exceeded expectations:⏎AI tools have demonstrated exceptional performance in clinical documentation and EHR analysis,⏎significantly reducing the time clinicians spend on administrative tasks and directly addressing provider⏎burnout. AI-driven care gap detection has also shown promising results, with intelligent agents⏎scanning patient records against clinical guidelines to flag missed screenings, lapsed medications, and⏎overdue follow-ups — enabling proactive intervention at scale. In the payer space, AI is increasingly⏎being used for prior authorization automation, with some organizations targeting near-real-time⏎decision-making.⏎Where AI has fallen short:⏎AI tools have underperformed when deployed as generic, one-size-fits-all solutions without⏎domain-specific training or clinical validation. General-purpose LLMs, while impressive in breadth,⏎often lack the depth of clinical reasoning required for safe and effective use in healthcare. They can⏎hallucinate, miss critical context, and fail to account for the multimodal, longitudinal nature of patient⏎data. The industry has also seen troubling examples of AI being used to automate insurance claim⏎denials without adequate clinical oversight, eroding public trust and underscoring the need for rigorous⏎governance.⏎Novel AI tools with the greatest potential:⏎The tools with the greatest transformative potential are those that enable dynamic, computable models of⏎individual human physiology — what we term "World Simulations of Humans." This approach⏎represents a paradigm shift beyond static data aggregation towards creating a dynamic, computable⏎model of an individual’s entire physiology. Such a simulation would serve as the standard platform for⏎⏎integrating all multimodal data — from genomics and proteomics to imaging, EHR data, and⏎real-time wearable streams. By fusing these sources into a unified, dynamic model, these simulations⏎would enable predictive modeling of disease progression, in silico simulation of individual treatment⏎responses, and truly personalized care at a biological level. HHS should consider direct investment in⏎the foundational research and infrastructure for these models, as they represent the ultimate platform for⏎preventive medicine and long-term cost reduction.⏎Additionally, condition-specific autonomous AI agents — like the cardiovascular agent being⏎developed through ARPA-H's ADVOCATE program — represent a paradigm shift from⏎decision-support tools to active participants in care delivery. Expanding this model to other disease areas⏎would be particularly impactful.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles?⏎The most influential decision-makers for AI adoption are Chief Medical Officers (CMOs), Heads of⏎Innovation, and increasingly, dedicated AI leads within healthcare organizations. These leaders are⏎closest to the clinical pain points and are often the ones pushing to accelerate beyond what their⏎organizations can build in-house. They recognize that the pace of AI development in the broader market⏎far exceeds what any single health system's internal team can match, and they are actively seeking⏎external partners and tools. C-suite executives (CEO, CFO, CIO) make the final investment decisions,⏎primarily motivated by ROI, regulatory compliance, and competitive positioning, while clinical⏎department chairs and physician leaders have outsized influence on whether frontline clinicians will⏎actually adopt and trust a new tool in practice.⏎The primary administrative hurdles are evolving. Most healthcare organizations have focused initial AI⏎efforts on automating the operational basics: scheduling, billing, claims processing, and documentation.⏎These segments are important, but they represent the floor, not the ceiling. The larger challenge — and⏎the larger opportunity — is shifting AI adoption toward higher-value clinical applications: identifying⏎incorrect billing codes before they cause downstream problems, detecting care gaps where patients⏎should be receiving screenings or treatments they are not, reducing misdiagnosis through AI-assisted⏎clinical reasoning, moving toward preventive health by identifying at-risk patients before acute events⏎occur, improving patient education and engagement through personalized AI-driven outreach, enabling⏎payers to perform real-time risk tracking and simulation across their populations, and dramatically⏎compressing the time it takes to assemble care plans for complex disease patients — including finding⏎the right specialists, trials, and resources nationwide.⏎The AI Agent Credentialing Committees proposed elsewhere in this response would help organizations⏎navigate this transition by providing a formal, standardized governance framework for evaluating,⏎approving, and monitoring AI agents across both operational and clinical use cases — giving innovation⏎leaders and CMOs a clear process to move faster with confidence.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?⏎Enhanced interoperability is the single most important technical enabler of clinical AI at scale. The⏎universal adoption and enforcement of FHIR-based APIs would transform the healthcare AI landscape.⏎Market Opportunities⏎⏎Universal adoption and enforcement of FHIR-based APIs would create a true plug-and-play ecosystem⏎for clinical AI tools. Today, a significant portion of development time and cost is consumed by custom⏎integrations with each client's unique data infrastructure. If all EHRs, lab systems, and payer platforms⏎adhered to common data standards, AI developers could build once and deploy everywhere —⏎dramatically lowering costs, accelerating time-to-market, and enabling smaller, innovative companies to⏎compete with entrenched incumbents.⏎This would be particularly impactful for AI-driven prior authorization automation. With CMS rules⏎already mandating electronic prior authorization and faster decision timelines between 2026 and 2027, a⏎fully interoperable system would allow AI agents to securely compile necessary clinical documentation⏎and submit it to payers in real time, drastically reducing administrative burden and care delays. The⏎same interoperability would enable AI agents to seamlessly access and synthesize data across providers,⏎creating the unified patient view that is essential for high-quality clinical reasoning.⏎Research and AI Development⏎Interoperability alone is necessary but not sufficient. Even with standardized data formats, it remains⏎extraordinarily difficult for AI developers and researchers to access large-scale, longitudinal datasets —⏎spanning EHRs, lab results, imaging, and genomics — and train models on them at scale. Privacy⏎concerns, institutional data-sharing restrictions, and the sheer cost of assembling and de-identifying⏎these datasets create a bottleneck that interoperability standards alone cannot solve.⏎HHS should address this directly through two complementary approaches. First, HHS should establish a⏎National Clinical AI Training Repository — a secure, centralized (or federated) data environment with⏎the necessary compute resources where vetted researchers and developers can train models on⏎large-scale, longitudinal, multimodal clinical data under strict privacy and governance controls. Second,⏎HHS should invest in the generation and public release of high-fidelity synthetic clinical datasets built⏎on top of real-world data. Synthetic data that faithfully preserves the statistical properties, clinical⏎patterns, and longitudinal complexity of real patient records — without exposing any individual's⏎information — would democratize access to training data and dramatically accelerate the development⏎of clinical AI across the entire ecosystem.⏎Without one or both of these interventions, only a handful of organizations with privileged data access⏎will be able to build frontier clinical AI models — limiting competition, innovation, and ultimately⏎patient benefit.⏎The areas where enhanced interoperability would have the greatest impact include genomic data,⏎wearable and remote monitoring data (which currently lacks any standardized format), lab results, and⏎imaging — all linked to longitudinal EHR records.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have?⏎Challenges patients and caregivers want AI to address:⏎The most fundamental challenge is the gap between the pace of scientific discovery and the⏎personalization of care. Millions of peer-reviewed papers are published each year, but the vast majority⏎of this knowledge never reaches the individual patient in a meaningful way. Patients want personalized,⏎science-grounded health guidance — not generic recommendations, but insights tailored to their⏎unique biology, medical history, and circumstances.⏎⏎Patients and caregivers also want help navigating complex medical journeys, particularly for cancer⏎and chronic conditions. AI agents that can synthesize multi-specialist findings, connect patients to⏎relevant clinical trials, and provide a unified view of a complex medical history would be⏎transformative.⏎Finally, patients want proactive rather than reactive care. They want to understand health risks before⏎they become emergencies, and they want their healthcare providers equipped with tools that enable early⏎identification and intervention.⏎Concerns patients and caregivers have:⏎The primary concerns are around data privacy and security — patients want to know that their most⏎sensitive health information is protected and used only with their consent. There are also concerns about⏎algorithmic bias and the risk that AI models trained on non-representative datasets could perpetuate or⏎amplify existing health disparities. Finally, patients value the human relationship in healthcare and⏎want assurance that AI will augment, not replace, the clinician-patient bond.⏎The CMS Health Tech Ecosystem pledge program for conversational AI assistants provides a useful⏎model for addressing these concerns. By having companies publicly commit to building tools that are⏎secure, transparent, and patient-centered, the program creates accountability and begins to build the⏎public trust that widespread adoption will require.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research in the following areas:⏎Multimodal AI for Patient Representation and Digital Twins. The ability to create a unified,⏎continuously updated model of a patient's health — integrating data from EHRs, genomics, proteomics,⏎metabolomics, imaging, and wearables — is the foundation of next-generation clinical AI. Research that⏎advances the science of Patient Digital Twins, including methods for fusing heterogeneous data types⏎and generating rich molecular profiles from routine clinical data, should be a top priority.⏎Autonomous and Semi-Autonomous Clinical AI Agents. Building on the ARPA-H ADVOCATE⏎model, HHS should fund research into the design, safety, and governance of AI agents that can⏎autonomously perform clinical tasks — from care coordination and medication management to patient⏎triage and monitoring. This funding would encompass research on supervisory AI systems that can⏎monitor and ensure the safety of other AI agents in real time.⏎Implementation Science for Clinical AI. Understanding how to effectively integrate AI into clinical⏎workflows — including the organizational, behavioral, and technical factors that determine success or⏎failure — is as important as the AI technology itself. HHS should fund research on best practices for AI⏎deployment, clinician training, change management, and the design of AI Agent Credentialing⏎Committees.⏎AI Safety, Fairness, and Explainability. Research on methods to detect and mitigate bias in clinical AI⏎models, to ensure equitable performance across diverse patient populations, and to make AI reasoning⏎transparent and interpretable to clinicians and patients is essential for building and maintaining public⏎trust.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0345♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from MIT Critical Data, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MIT Critical Data♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy / Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13; 90 FR 60108)⏎Submitted via: http://www.regulations.gov⏎PUBLIC COMMENT⏎The Evidence Gap in Clinical AI: Why Federal Standards Must Precede⏎Adoption⏎How Unvalidated Claims, Misaligned Incentives, and Regulatory Fragmentation Are Putting⏎Patients at Risk⏎Submitted by:⏎Rahul Gorijavolu⏎Nikhil Jaiswal⏎Cathy Mullins, Patient Partner⏎Tina Hernandez-Boussard, PhD, MS, MPH⏎Leo Anthony Celi, MD, MS, MPH⏎On behalf of MIT Critical Data⏎⏎I. Summary⏎This comment responds to the Department’s Request for Information on accelerating the⏎adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical care.1 We respectfully submit that the⏎framing of this RFI, which focuses primarily on removing barriers to AI adoption, reflects an⏎incomplete picture of the current landscape. Before the Department acts to accelerate adoption, it⏎should examine the systemic risks created by AI systems that have been deployed with⏎inadequate evidence, that operate in regulatory gaps no federal agency has claimed jurisdiction⏎over, and that access patient data through legal frameworks never designed for autonomous⏎technology. These risks are not hypothetical. They are documented, ongoing, and escalating.2⏎This comment draws on the collective expertise of the authoring team, which includes⏎contributors to MIT Critical Data, a consortium spanning 40 countries with over a decade of⏎work at the intersection of clinical medicine, health data science, and global health fairness,⏎including the development of the MIMIC database, one of the most widely used open clinical⏎datasets in the world.3 This comment also draws on direct investigation of the Doctronic AI⏎prescribing pilot in Utah,4 including public records requests filed by members of our team under⏎the Government Records Access and Management Act (GRAMA), as well as extensive analysis⏎of the broader AI healthcare landscape, including multiple companies, documented patient⏎harms, regulatory gaps, liability questions, algorithmic bias, and international comparisons.⏎Doctronic is discussed as a case study, but the systemic problems this comment identifies extend⏎far beyond any single company or state.⏎We respond to all ten RFI questions below. Our central recommendation is that HHS should⏎establish federal minimum evidence standards, liability frameworks, and fairness requirements⏎for clinical AI before further accelerating adoption. We recognize that some commenters will⏎argue these safeguards would slow innovation. We believe the opposite is true: clear standards⏎are the foundation on which sustainable adoption must be built. Without them, the inevitable⏎accumulation of patient harms will produce a public backlash that sets clinical AI back by years.⏎1https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/23/2025-23641/request-for-information-accelerating-the-adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intell⏎igence-as-part-of⏎2https://www.statnews.com/2026/02/03/utah-doctronic-ai-doctor-prescription-renewal-fda-regulation/⏎3https://physionet.org/content/mimiciv/⏎4https://commerce.utah.gov/2026/01/06/news-release-utah-and-doctronic-announce-groundbreaking-partnership-for-ai-prescription-medication-re⏎newals/⏎⏎II. The Evidence Crisis: AI Healthcare Claims Outpace Proof⏎A. The Doctronic Case Study: How a Preprint Became Policy⏎To understand the evidence gap in clinical AI, it is useful to examine the strongest case: the⏎Doctronic pilot in Utah, which has received significant regulatory scrutiny and press coverage⏎compared with other autonomous clinical AI systems. If the evidence supporting this pilot is⏎inadequate, it raises serious questions about the rest of the industry. The central evidence⏎supporting Doctronic is a single preprint posted to medRxiv in July 2025.5 The paper reports a⏎99.2% concordance rate between AI and physician treatment plans. This figure has been repeated⏎by major outlets as though it constitutes validated evidence.6⏎It does not meet that standard. medRxiv’s own FAQ states preprints “should not be used to guide⏎clinical practice.”7 The methodological problems are substantial:⏎• All eight authors are equity holders in Doctronic Inc.8⏎• Ethics approval came from Doctronic’s own internal committee, not an independent⏎IRB.9⏎• The sample: 500 urgent-care encounters from a single week, which is insufficient to⏎support claims about safety at scale.10⏎• Clinicians saw AI-generated documentation before rendering assessments, introducing⏎anchoring bias.11⏎• Primary diagnosis match was only 81%; the 99.2% measures “treatment plan alignment,”⏎a softer standard.12⏎• The study tested urgent care, but the Utah pilot involves chronic medication renewals,⏎which is a fundamentally different clinical context.13⏎Doctronic’s co-founder claimed the AI is “infinitely safer than a human doctor”14, a claim⏎supported by no independent evidence. The medical co-founder is a vascular surgeon with no⏎prior AI research.15 The company’s first CMO with relevant AI credentials was hired in October⏎2025, after the study.16⏎5https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎6https://www.axios.com/local/salt-lake-city/2026/01/07/utah-ai-drug-prescriptions-doctronic;⏎https://fortune.com/2026/01/08/ai-prescription-renewals-doctronic-utah-doctors-warn-puts-patients-at-risk/⏎7https://www.medrxiv.org/about/FAQ⏎8https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎9https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎10https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎11https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎12https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎13https://www.doctronic.ai/blog/doctronic-treatment-plan-consistency/⏎14https://www.sltrib.com/news/2026/01/09/utahns-can-now-use-ai-renew/⏎15https://surgery.ucsf.edu/bio/adam-oskowitz-md-phd⏎16https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20251015771722/en/⏎⏎B. A Landscape of Unvalidated Clinical AI Claims⏎The evidentiary problems with Doctronic are not unique to that company. They reflect standard⏎practice across the clinical AI industry, where companies routinely make clinical claims that far⏎outstrip their published evidence. The following examples illustrate the scope of the problem.⏎Hippocratic AI has raised $404 million at a $3.5 billion valuation, marketing “AI nurse agents”⏎for direct patient interaction. The company claims 115 million clinical interactions “with no⏎safety issues,” yet these metrics come from internal studies using a proprietary model that cannot⏎be independently benchmarked. National Nurses United organized over 20 demonstrations⏎against the company.17⏎Glass Health (YC-funded, $6.5M raised) generates AI differential diagnoses and treatment⏎plans. Despite claiming use at Harvard and Stanford, no published peer-reviewed clinical⏎validation studies exist. The company deliberately avoids FDA-triggering language by marketing⏎as “clinical decision support.”18⏎K Health ($380M raised, $900M valuation) operates an AI symptom checker. While a⏎company-associated study claimed 68% alignment with physicians, an independent vignette⏎study found top-3 diagnostic accuracy of only 36% compared to 82.1% for general practitioners.⏎Among newer entrants, YC-backed FutureClinic claims “digital medical clinics that are 99%⏎AI, 1% doctor,” while Prana markets itself as a “full-stack medical provider” and “always-on⏎physician.” These are extraordinarily aggressive clinical claims for pre-revenue startups.⏎C. The Corporate Failure Record⏎The pattern of overpromising and underdelivering in clinical AI is not new. IBM invested over⏎$4 billion in Watson Health before selling it for approximately $1 billion; Watson for Oncology⏎was trained on synthetic cases and proved inappropriate internationally.19 Babylon Health raised⏎$1.2 billion before bankruptcy; the UK’s MHRA acknowledged “concerns” about its chatbot’s⏎systematic triage failures, including missed heart attack symptoms and documented gender⏎bias.2021⏎The Texas Attorney General’s September 2024 enforcement action against Pieces Technologies⏎found the company marketed hallucination rates below 0.001% that were “likely inaccurate.”⏎Four major hospitals had been feeding real-time patient data to Pieces based on these claims.22⏎17https://telecareaware.com/are-hippocratic-ai-and-ai-nurses-the-wave-of-the-future-or-just-another-tide-of-hype-two-articles-question/⏎18https://techcrunch.com/2023/09/08/glass-health-is-building-an-ai-for-suggesting-medical-diagnoses/⏎19https://theconversation.com/from-a-deranged-provocateur-to-ibms-failed-ai-superproject-the-controversial-story-of-how-data-has-transformed-h⏎ealthcare-189362⏎20https://theweek.com/health/babylon-health-the-failed-ai-wonder-app-that-dazzled-politicians⏎21https://techcrunch.com/2021/03/05/uks-mhra-says-it-has-concerns-about-babylon-health-and-flags-legal-gap-around-triage-chatbots/⏎22https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/news/releases/attorney-general-ken-paxton-reaches-settlement-first-its-kind-healthcare-generative-ai-inve⏎stigation⏎⏎These corporate failures point to a deeper problem: AI systems that perform well in controlled⏎settings consistently underperform in real clinical environments. An Oxford University study⏎published in Nature Medicine (February 2026) quantified this gap, finding that AI diagnostic⏎accuracy dropped from 95% on standardized tests to less than 35% when real patients interacted⏎with these tools. Participants using AI chatbots actually performed worse than those using⏎Google search. This finding has direct implications for the Doctronic pilot: a concordance study⏎conducted in controlled conditions tells us very little about how the system will perform when⏎deployed autonomously to real patients with complex medication histories.23⏎23https://www.sciencenews.org/article/medical-advice-ai-chatbots-symptoms⏎⏎III. Documented Patient Harms: A Systematic Pattern⏎The evidence reviewed above concerns unvalidated claims. But the consequences of deploying⏎AI health tools without adequate safeguards are not merely theoretical. Documented harms have⏎already occurred across multiple products, patient populations, and categories of harm. These⏎cases illustrate what happens when AI systems enter clinical environments without the⏎protections that HHS has the opportunity to establish through this rulemaking process.⏎A. The NEDA/Tessa Incident⏎After the National Eating Disorders Association terminated its human helpline staff, the Tessa⏎chatbot, which was augmented with generative AI capabilities without NEDA’s knowledge, gave⏎eating disorder patients advice to lose 1–2 pounds per week, eat no more than 2,000 calories⏎daily, maintain a 500–1,000 calorie deficit, and purchase skin calipers to measure body fat.24 As⏎one eating disorder survivor reported: every suggestion “were things that led to my eating⏎disorder.”⏎B. Character.AI Teen Suicides: The Failure of Voluntary Safety Measures⏎Sewell Setzer III, age 14, died by suicide on February 28, 2024, after developing an intense⏎emotional attachment to a Character.AI chatbot. When he expressed suicidal ideation, the bot⏎failed to trigger safety interventions. Juliana Peralta, age 13, died in November 2023 after similar⏎engagement. Google and Character.AI settled in January 2026, with additional lawsuits filed in⏎Colorado, New York, and Texas. These cases are relevant to the clinical AI question because⏎they demonstrate that AI companies deploying health-adjacent tools without mandatory safety⏎standards will not reliably implement adequate safeguards on their own. Character.AI added⏎safety features only after public litigation and media pressure, not before deployment.25⏎C. The Koko Experiment⏎Approximately 4,000 mental health users received GPT-3-co-written support through the Koko⏎platform without clear informed consent and without IRB approval.26 Medical ethicist Art⏎Caplan stated: “If you want to set back the use of AI in mental health, start exactly this way.”⏎The Koko incident demonstrates a recurring problem: AI was introduced into a clinical context⏎without the informed consent protections that would be required of any human-delivered⏎intervention. The same dynamic is present in the Doctronic pilot, where patients interacting with⏎AI may not fully understand the nature of the entity making decisions about their medications.⏎D. AI Drug Information Errors⏎24https://www.cbsnews.com/news/eating-disorder-helpline-chatbot-disabled/;⏎https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2023/06/08/1180838096/an-eating-disorders-chatbot-offered-dieting-advice-raising-fears-about-ai-in-he⏎a⏎25https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/07/business/character-ai-google-settle-teen-suicide-lawsuit;⏎https://www.nbcnews.com/tech/characterai-lawsuit-florida-teen-death-rcna176791⏎26https://www.psychiatrist.com/news/hidden-use-of-chatgpt-in-online-mental-health-counseling-raises-ethical-concerns/⏎⏎The drug information errors documented in general-purpose AI are directly relevant to the⏎question of autonomous AI prescribing. A Heidelberg University study found 38% of ChatGPT⏎drug answers were false, with 26% posing high risk of patient harm.27 An ASHP study found⏎only 26% of drug information responses were satisfactory, with ChatGPT missing the dangerous⏎Paxlovid-verapamil interaction and fabricating references.28⏎These are not niche findings. ECRI, the independent patient safety organization, named misuse⏎of AI chatbots the number one health technology hazard for both 2025 and 2026, documenting⏎chatbots suggesting incorrect diagnoses, recommending unnecessary testing, and inventing body⏎parts.29 Mount Sinai researchers found AI chatbots “routinely elaborated on fake medical details,⏎confidently generating explanations about conditions or treatments that do not exist.”30⏎A Stanford study found AI therapy chatbots enabled dangerous behavior: when a user mentioned⏎losing their job and asked about tall bridges, the chatbot listed bridges rather than recognizing⏎suicidal ideation. An additional safety concern that warrants research attention is AI sycophancy:⏎the tendency of AI systems to agree with users rather than provide accurate information,⏎particularly in patient-facing applications. When a patient tells an AI chatbot they believe they⏎have a particular condition, sycophantic AI will tend to confirm rather than challenge that belief,⏎even when the evidence does not support it. This dynamic is especially dangerous in mental⏎health contexts, where validation of distorted thinking can reinforce harmful patterns. Taken⏎together, these cases and dynamics establish that AI health tools have caused serious harm across⏎a range of contexts, that companies have not voluntarily implemented adequate safeguards, and⏎that the harms tend to fall disproportionately on vulnerable populations. This pattern should⏎inform how HHS approaches the question of accelerating clinical AI adoption.31⏎27https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37263772/⏎28https://news.ashp.org/news/ashp-news/2023/12/05/study-finds-chatgpt-provides-inaccurate-responses-to-drug-questions⏎29https://home.ecri.org/blogs/ecri-news/misuse-of-ai-chatbots-tops-annual-list-of-health-technology-hazards⏎30https://www.mountsinai.org/about/newsroom/2025/ai-chatbots-can-run-with-medical-misinformation-study-finds-highlighting-the-need-for-stro⏎nger-safeguards⏎31https://hai.stanford.edu/news/exploring-the-dangers-of-ai-in-mental-health-care⏎⏎IV. Venture Capital Is Shaping Clinical AI Policy⏎A. Regulatory Strategy as Investment Thesis⏎The preceding sections establish that clinical AI companies are making unvalidated claims and⏎that those claims have produced real patient harms. This section examines why the system is⏎producing these outcomes: the financial incentives driving clinical AI deployment are⏎structurally misaligned with patient safety. Doctronic has raised $25 million from Lightspeed⏎Venture Partners,3233 Union Square Ventures, and Tusk Venture Partners.34⏎Tusk Venture Partners is of particular relevance to this comment. Founded by Bradley Tusk, who⏎served as Communications Director for Senator Schumer, Deputy Governor of Illinois,⏎Bloomberg’s campaign manager35, the firm invests in startups facing regulatory barriers and⏎provides political strategy in exchange for equity. Co-founder Jordan Nof stated: “How do we⏎close the door behind us? I want to create a regulatory moat.”36 Tusk himself: “Venture⏎capitalists, you know, we’re not here for the good of humanity.”37⏎Doctronic’s website acknowledges Tusk’s “regulatory expertise helps us navigate the complex⏎healthcare landscape.”38 Public records obtained through GRAMA confirm Tusk Ventures⏎appears in negotiation records between Doctronic and Utah’s Office of AI Policy.39⏎This arrangement creates a structural conflict that HHS should consider as it evaluates how to⏎accelerate adoption responsibly. When the same entity that profits from a company’s commercial⏎success is also shaping the regulatory environment that company operates in, the resulting⏎regulatory outcomes cannot be presumed to reflect an independent assessment of patient safety.⏎The question for HHS is whether the current regulatory framework has adequate safeguards⏎against this kind of influence. Based on our investigation, it does not.⏎B. The “AI Doctor” Gold Rush⏎Y Combinator and other accelerators are actively funding startups marketing AI as replacements⏎for physician judgment. HHS should recognize that pressure to “accelerate adoption” is⏎substantially generated by venture capital return timelines rather than patient demand, clinical⏎evidence, or healthcare system needs. Patients want affordable, accessible healthcare, and⏎replacing physicians with AI does not address that need.⏎32https://medium.com/sand-hill-road/finding-regulatory-moats-at-the-series-a-with-jordan-nof-of-tusk-venture-partners-5e5c0e7abf54⏎33https://lsvp.com/stories/investing-in-doctronic-ai-healthcares-24-7-digital-doctor/⏎34https://pulse2.com/doctronic-20-million-series-a/⏎35https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bradley_Tusk⏎36https://medium.com/sand-hill-road/finding-regulatory-moats-at-the-series-a-with-jordan-nof-of-tusk-venture-partners-5e5c0e7abf54⏎37https://techcrunch.com/2019/12/18/tusk-ventures-second-fund/⏎38https://www.doctronic.ai/strategic-partnerships-driving-healthcare-innovation/⏎39GRAMA Response, Utah Dept. of Commerce, Jan. 26, 2026 (on file with authors).⏎⏎V. The FDA Regulatory Framework Has Critical Gaps⏎A. The Clinical Decision Support Exemption⏎The evidence, harms, and incentive misalignment documented above might be less concerning if⏎a robust federal regulatory framework existed to ensure clinical AI safety. It does not. The most⏎significant gap is the Clinical Decision Support (CDS) exemption. Section 3060(a) of the 21st⏎Century Cures Act exempts CDS software from FDA oversight if it meets four criteria, including⏎that clinicians can independently review the AI’s reasoning.40 Companies exploit this by framing⏎autonomous AI systems as “support” tools and adding disclaimers that recommendations require⏎“clinician review.” The opacity of deep learning models makes the independent review criterion⏎largely fictional.⏎FDA Commissioner Makary’s January 2026 CDS guidance further loosened oversight, extending⏎enforcement discretion and stating the FDA should be “less paternalistic.” The guidance⏎removed analysis of automation bias.41⏎B. The 510(k) Pathway Is Inadequate⏎Over 1,300 AI/ML-enabled medical devices have been authorized, virtually all via the 510(k)⏎pathway requiring only “substantial equivalence” to a predicate device. A 2025 study found⏎fewer than 2% were supported by randomized clinical trials.42 More than a third of⏎first-generation AI devices were cleared based on non-AI predicates, meaning novel AI⏎capabilities were deemed equivalent to fundamentally different technologies. The practical⏎consequence is that the vast majority of clinical AI tools in use today reached the market without⏎the kind of clinical trial evidence that would be required for a new pharmaceutical or medical⏎device. This is the evidentiary environment into which the Department is proposing to accelerate⏎further adoption.43⏎C. Why Autonomous AI Prescribing Falls in the Gap⏎The FDA regulates medical devices but not the practice of medicine. State medical practice acts⏎define prescriptive authority, which has historically been limited to human licensees. The FDA⏎has declined comment on Doctronic’s pilot, stating it “falls outside the agency’s regulatory⏎purview.”44 This means no regulatory body is clearly responsible for ensuring autonomous AI⏎prescribing is safe.⏎D. FDA Staffing Cuts Compound the Problem⏎40https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/fda-oversight-understanding-the-regulation-of-health-ai-tools/⏎41https://www.nelsonmullins.com/insights/blogs/ai-task-force/ai/hhs-proposes-rule-to-deregulate-health-it-and-advance-ai-interoperability-hti-5⏎42https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12140231/⏎43https://www.thelancet.com/journals/landig/article/PIIS2589-7500(23)00126-7/fulltext⏎44https://www.statnews.com/2026/02/03/utah-doctronic-ai-doctor-prescription-renewal-fda-regulation/⏎⏎Post-deployment monitoring is also critically underdeveloped. The FDA’s MAUDE system⏎captures fewer than 0.5% of adverse events from medical devices, and Predetermined Change⏎Control Plans (PCCPs) allow AI/ML device modifications without fresh clearance, meaning AI⏎tools can evolve substantially after initial authorization without triggering new review.45⏎Approximately 3,500 FDA employees were terminated in April 2025 (~20% of staff). The⏎FY2026 budget proposes an 11.5% cut. Former Commissioner Califf stated: “The FDA as we’ve⏎known it is finished.”46 The agency missed its first drug approval deadline due to staff cuts.47⏎This paradox, in which the agency faces record AI submissions amid decimated oversight⏎capacity, was noted in Nature’s npj Digital Medicine as “consternation.”48⏎45https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/mandatory-reporting-requirements-manufacturers-importers-and-device-user-facilities/manufacturer-and-u⏎ser-facility-device-experience-database-maude⏎46https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/regulatory/rfk-jr-prepares-10000-job-cuts-across-hhs-new-wave-worker-reductions;⏎https://www.npr.org/sections/shots-health-news/2025/04/01/g-s1-57485/hhs-fda-layoffs-doge-cdc-nih⏎47https://www.axios.com/2025/07/15/doge-cuts-fda-hhs-biotech⏎48https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-01540-2⏎⏎VI. The Liability Vacuum for Autonomous Clinical AI⏎If the regulatory framework does not prevent harm, the legal system should at least provide⏎recourse for patients who are harmed. But here, too, the existing framework has no clear answer⏎for autonomous clinical AI. As of early 2026, no U.S. malpractice jury verdict has centered on⏎AI as the basis of a clinical claim, and the legal doctrines that would govern such a case are⏎poorly suited to the technology.49⏎A. Medical Malpractice Does Not Fit⏎Traditional malpractice requires a physician-patient relationship (which does not exist with⏎autonomous AI), a standard of care (undefined for AI), breach (which is nearly impossible to⏎determine in opaque systems), and causation (which is difficult to isolate). As W. Nicholson⏎Price II of Michigan Law School has documented, deep learning embeds reasoning in thousands⏎of simulated neurons, making breach determination effectively impossible.50⏎B. Product Liability Does Not Fit Either⏎Courts have historically been reluctant to apply product liability to software, treating it as a⏎“service.” Yet when AI acts autonomously, the malpractice framework, which requires a human⏎practitioner, also breaks down. The practical result is that a patient harmed by an autonomous AI⏎prescribing decision may have no viable legal theory under which to seek compensation. This is⏎not an abstract concern. It is the legal environment in which the Doctronic pilot is currently⏎operating.51⏎C. The Learned Intermediary Doctrine Collapses⏎The learned intermediary doctrine shields drug manufacturers by requiring warnings only to⏎prescribing physicians. When AI removes the physician, neither the manufacturer (which warned⏎the “prescriber”) nor the AI company clearly bears liability.52 Consider a concrete scenario:⏎Doctronic’s AI renews an SSRI for a patient who has since been prescribed a medication with a⏎dangerous interaction. The drug manufacturer warned the “prescriber,” but the prescriber is an⏎algorithm. The AI company may argue it is a technology provider, not a medical practitioner.⏎The state authorized the pilot but disclaimed clinical responsibility. The patient is left with no⏎clear defendant. This is not a hypothetical edge case; it is the foreseeable consequence of⏎deploying autonomous prescribing without first resolving the liability framework.⏎D. The UnitedHealth Precedent⏎The most significant AI healthcare litigation is Estate of Lokken v. UnitedHealth Group, alleging⏎UnitedHealth used the nH Predict algorithm to systematically deny post-acute care despite⏎49https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK613216/; https://repository.law.umich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1569&context=book_chapters⏎50https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK613216/; https://repository.law.umich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1569&context=book_chapters⏎51https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/are-current-tort-liability-doctrines-adequate-addressing-injury-caused-ai/2019-02⏎52https://www.druganddevicelawblog.com/2017/07/artificial-intelligence-and-learned-intermediaries.html⏎⏎knowing its approximately 90% error rate. A Senate investigation found denial rates jumped⏎from 10.9% to 22.7% after AI implementation, with skilled nursing denials increasing ninefold.53⏎53https://www.cbsnews.com/news/unitedhealth-lawsuit-ai-deny-claims-medicare-advantage-health-insurance-denials/⏎⏎VII. Algorithmic Bias Threatens to Worsen Health Disparities⏎The risks documented above, including unvalidated evidence, patient harms, regulatory gaps,⏎and liability failures, do not fall equally on all populations. The evidence consistently shows that⏎clinical AI systems perform worse for minority and underserved patients, raising serious fairness⏎concerns that this RFI should address directly. Obermeyer et al. (Science, 2019) found that an⏎algorithm applied to roughly 200 million Americans used healthcare costs as a proxy for need,⏎systematically underestimating Black patients’ illness. At a given risk score, Black patients had⏎26.3% more chronic conditions than White patients. The bias reduced Black patients identified⏎for extra care by more than half.54⏎Pulse oximetry bias feeds directly into AI decision-making: Sjoding et al. (NEJM, 2020) found⏎Black patients had nearly three times the frequency of undetected hypoxemia.55 Dermatology AI⏎performs substantially worse on dark skin. No public benchmark included biopsy-proven⏎malignancy on dark skin before Daneshjou et al. (Science Advances, 2022) created the first such⏎dataset.56⏎The Epic Sepsis Model, deployed at hundreds of hospitals, achieved an AUC of only 0.63 in⏎external validation, well below the claimed 0.76 to 0.83, and missed 67% of sepsis patients.57⏎A systematic review found 22 of 24 studies (91.7%) identified demographic biases in medical⏎LLMs, with 93.7% finding gender bias and 90.9% finding racial/ethnic bias.58 GPT-4 specifically⏎showed associations between demographic attributes and recommendations for more expensive⏎procedures.59⏎The data representation problem is structural: non-Hispanic Whites constitute over 90% of⏎clinical trial participants while representing 60.7% of the population, and fewer than 44% of⏎clinical trials report any race/ethnicity data. These disparities are compounded for rural and⏎lower socioeconomic status populations, who are systematically underrepresented in both the⏎clinical data used to train AI systems and in the health systems where AI tools are first deployed⏎and validated. Safety-net hospitals and rural health facilities, which serve the most vulnerable⏎patients, are least likely to have the infrastructure to evaluate AI tools locally, creating a dynamic⏎in which the populations at greatest risk from biased AI are also the populations with the least⏎capacity to detect and mitigate that bias. For autonomous prescribing systems like Doctronic, this⏎means the AI is making medication decisions based on training data that systematically⏎underrepresents the patients most likely to experience adverse outcomes. HHS cannot⏎responsibly accelerate adoption of clinical AI without requiring that these systems demonstrate⏎equitable performance across the populations they serve.60⏎54https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎55Sjoding, M. et al., NEJM, Dec. 2020. https://doi.org/10.1056/NEJMc2029240⏎56https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9374341/⏎57https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8218233/⏎58https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12939-025-02419-0⏎59https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38123252/⏎60https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanam/article/PIIS2667-193X(22)00069-2/fulltext⏎⏎VIII. State Regulatory Capacity Does Not Match Ambition⏎A. Utah: Institutional Mismatch⏎Given the evidence gaps, documented harms, incentive misalignment, regulatory failures,⏎liability vacuum, and fairness risks detailed in the preceding sections, the question of who⏎authorizes clinical AI deployment becomes critical. In the case of the most consequential clinical⏎AI authorization to date, the answer is troubling. Utah’s Office of Artificial Intelligence Policy⏎has four staff members: a mathematician, a social systems modeler, an internet privacy attorney,⏎and a wealth management professional. None has medical, clinical, or pharmaceutical⏎training.6162⏎This team authorized Doctronic to autonomously renew approximately 192 medications,⏎including SSRIs, antihypertensives, statins, and hormonal contraceptives.63 Healthcare law firm⏎Goldsand Friedberg concluded the agreement “clearly steps over” licensed professional activity⏎and noted no agreement “requires representations regarding FDA qualification.”64⏎No major medical society has endorsed autonomous AI prescribing. The AMA stated it “poses⏎serious risks.”65 The Utah Medical Association called it “a bad idea.”66 Public Citizen’s Dr.⏎Steinbrook called it “a dangerous first step” and warned that “decisions made by AI with human⏎oversight are in practice decisions made by AI most of the time.”67⏎B. The Transparency Deficit⏎Our team filed public records requests under GRAMA seeking monthly safety reports,⏎negotiation drafts, metric definitions, and federal communications about the pilot. The Utah⏎Department of Commerce denied access to the majority of records, classifying safety reports as⏎“protected” under business confidentiality exemptions. The state’s position is that our interests⏎“do not outweigh Doctronic’s business confidentiality interests.”68⏎The result is a closed loop: a state office without medical expertise authorized clinical AI⏎deployment, the safety data that could allow independent evaluation is classified as confidential,⏎and the public has no mechanism to assess whether the pilot is functioning safely. If HHS intends⏎to encourage state-level AI experimentation, this is precisely the kind of transparency failure that⏎federal standards should prevent.⏎C. State Proliferation⏎61https://ai.utah.gov/staff/⏎62https://utahpolicy.com/news-release/70903-zachary-boyd-appointed-director-of-the-utah-office-of-artificial-intelligence-policy⏎63https://commerce.utah.gov/2026/01/06/news-release-utah-and-doctronic-announce-groundbreaking-partnership-for-ai-prescription-medication-r⏎enewals/⏎64https://www.goldsandfriedberg.com/insights/utahs-ai-regulatory-learning-laboratory-early-signals-from-health-care-mitigation-agreements⏎65https://fortune.com/2026/01/08/ai-prescription-renewals-doctronic-utah-doctors-warn-puts-patients-at-risk/⏎66https://www.sltrib.com/news/2026/01/09/utahns-can-now-use-ai-renew/⏎67https://www.citizen.org/news/utah-pilot-program-for-medication-renewals-with-ai-perverts-medical-practice/⏎68GRAMA Appeal Response, Utah Dept. of Commerce, Feb. 18, 2026 (on file with authors).⏎⏎Texas enacted its AI sandbox law (RAIGA) effective January 1, 2026. Doctronic is in⏎discussions with Texas, Arizona, and Missouri. Wyoming is preparing its own sandbox.⏎Doctronic’s co-founder expects “a dozen other states approve something like this in 2026.”⏎Without federal minimum standards, the result will be a patchwork of state experiments⏎conducted under widely varying levels of oversight, with no coordinated adverse event reporting,⏎no consistent evidence requirements, and no mechanism for one state to learn from another’s⏎failures. The window for HHS to establish a federal floor before this fragmentation becomes⏎entrenched is narrow.⏎⏎IX. The Prescription Data Pipeline Operates Without Meaningful Consent⏎The regulatory and liability gaps described above are compounded by a data access framework⏎that operates largely without patient knowledge. Doctronic accesses medication histories through⏎the Surescripts network, which covers over 95% of U.S. pharmacies and 99% of the population.69⏎The legal mechanism is HIPAA’s TPO exception, 45 C.F.R. § 164.506), which permits PHI⏎sharing for treatment without patient consent.⏎As NYU’s Technology Law & Policy Clinic documented, data sharing with Surescripts is “an⏎inevitable consequence of sending an e-prescription,” and existing rules “permit Surescripts and⏎other HIEs to share patients’ sensitive prescription drug histories without patients’ knowledge or⏎consent.”70 Patients wishing to opt out must complete and notarize a form and mail it to⏎Surescripts headquarters.71⏎No court has tested whether the TPO exception applies to autonomous AI acting as a de facto⏎healthcare provider. TPG Capital became Surescripts’ majority investor in October 2024.72 The⏎FTC’s 2023 antitrust settlement eliminated exclusivity contracts, potentially making it easier for⏎new AI entrants to access prescription data.73⏎69https://surescripts.com/news-center/intelligence-in-action/interoperability/why-it-matters-prescribers-can-now-access-accurate-medication-histo⏎ry-data-for-virtually-every-american⏎70https://www.law.nyu.edu/sites/default/files/Comment%20of%20NYU%20TLP%20Clinic%20on%20Proposed%20HIPAA%20Rule.pdf⏎71https://surescripts.com/opt-out-faq⏎72https://www.fiercehealthcare.com/health-tech/pe-firm-tpg-buys-majority-stake-health-information-network-surescripts⏎73https://www.ftc.gov/news-events/news/press-releases/2023/07/ftc-reaches-proposed-settlement-surescripts-illegal-monopolization-case⏎⏎X. International Comparisons Reveal the U.S. as an Outlier⏎The gaps in the U.S. framework are not inevitable features of AI regulation. Other jurisdictions⏎have developed more protective approaches that provide useful models for HHS consideration.⏎The EU AI Act classifies medical AI as high-risk, requiring risk management systems, data⏎governance ensuring representative datasets, mandatory human oversight, accuracy standards,⏎post-market monitoring, and serious incident reporting. Penalties can reach €35 million or 7% of⏎global turnover.74 Product defectiveness can be presumed if AI does not comply with safety⏎requirements.⏎Health Canada requires a Sex and Gender-Based Analysis Plus: manufacturers must collect⏎disaggregated data on sex, gender, racial/ethnic minorities, elderly, and pediatric populations.⏎This mandatory fairness requirement has no U.S. parallel.75⏎Japan mandates that AI medical devices must be used under physician oversight, with⏎physicians making all final treatment decisions.76⏎The U.S. is the only major jurisdiction to have allowed autonomous AI prescribing and the only⏎one with a broad statutory CDS exemption that removes many clinical AI tools from oversight⏎entirely. We do not suggest that HHS adopt any single international framework wholesale, but⏎the comparative evidence demonstrates that the requirements we recommend, including⏎mandatory fairness testing, human oversight proportional to autonomy, and post-market⏎surveillance, are not radical proposals. They are the minimum standards that other nations have⏎concluded are necessary to protect patients.⏎74https://www.hunton.com/insights/legal/the-impact-of-the-eu-ai-act-on-the-development-and-use-of-medical-devices⏎75https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/medical-devices/application-information/guidance-documents/pre-mark⏎et-guidance-machine-learning-enabled-medical-devices.html⏎76https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-01540-2⏎⏎XI. Payer and Insurance Implications⏎The regulatory and legal gaps documented above have practical consequences for how⏎AI-prescribed care will be paid for and insured. No major insurer has issued a policy specifically⏎addressing coverage of medications prescribed by autonomous AI systems. The current⏎framework presumes prescriptions from licensed human practitioners.77⏎CMS has focused on preventing AI-driven denials: the April 2023 Final Rule requires Medicare⏎Advantage determinations to be based on individual circumstances, not algorithms. CMS’s⏎WISeR Model (January 2026) uses AI for Medicare prior authorizations but maintains that final⏎coverage denial decisions are made by “licensed clinicians, not machines.”78⏎Malpractice insurance for AI remains undefined. Major insurers review AI involvement⏎case-by-case; no AI-specific frameworks exist.79 The reimbursement framework is embryonic:⏎CPT 2026 will be the first year AI is explicitly recognized in formal medical coding.80⏎These gaps create practical consequences on both sides of the adoption question. Physicians who⏎might benefit from AI-assisted decision support may resist using it because the liability and⏎coverage implications are unclear. Meanwhile, patients whose medications are renewed by an⏎autonomous AI system may discover that their insurer does not recognize the prescription as⏎valid, or that no malpractice framework covers them if something goes wrong. Resolving these⏎questions is not a barrier to innovation; it is a prerequisite for responsible deployment.⏎77https://www.thedoctors.com/the-doctors-advocate/second-quarter-2024/artificial-intelligence-emerging-issues-in-healthcare-and-insurance-publi⏎c-policy⏎78https://stateline.org/2025/12/04/medicares-new-ai-experiment-sparks-alarm-among-doctors-lawmakers/⏎79https://www.thedoctors.com/the-doctors-advocate/second-quarter-2024/artificial-intelligence-emerging-issues-in-healthcare-and-insurance-publi⏎c-policy⏎80https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/paying-for-ai-in-u-s-health-care/⏎⏎XII. Expert and Medical Society Consensus⏎The concerns raised in this comment are not ours alone. They are shared by the leading medical⏎societies, academic journals, and patient safety organizations in the United States.⏎The NEJM AI editors declared (March 2024): artificial intelligence must meet the same bar for⏎clinical evidence as other interventions, and mandated trial preregistration for AI studies.81⏎The AMA’s augmented intelligence policy deliberately uses “augmented” over “artificial” to⏎emphasize AI’s assistive role. The AMA insists AI should augment, not replace, clinical⏎judgment, and that the “try-first mentality should be reserved for testing environments only as⏎the risks to patient health are simply too significant.” AMA President Bruce Scott stated:⏎“Voluntary standards are not going to be enough.”82⏎The American College of Physicians insists AI tools must “complement and not supplant the⏎logic and decision-making of physicians,” warning against “automation complacency.”83⏎The National Academy of Medicine published its AI Code of Conduct in July 2025, the first⏎comprehensive values-driven framework for responsible AI in health. The consistency of these⏎positions across institutions with different mandates, constituencies, and political orientations is⏎notable. There is no significant expert voice arguing that autonomous clinical AI should be⏎deployed without independent validation, fairness testing, and clear liability frameworks. The⏎question is whether federal policy will reflect this consensus or continue to lag behind it.84⏎81https://ai.nejm.org/doi/10.1056/AIe2400146⏎82https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/digital-health/ama-position-2025-federal-government-ai-action-plan⏎83https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/M24-0146⏎84https://nam.edu/our-work/programs/leadership-consortium/health-care-artificial-intelligence-code-of-conduct/⏎⏎XIII. Responses to RFI Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to AI innovation in healthcare?⏎We acknowledge that real barriers to responsible AI innovation exist. Data interoperability⏎remains fragmented across incompatible EHR systems. Reimbursement pathways are embryonic;⏎CPT 2026 will be the first year AI is explicitly recognized in formal medical coding. Regulatory⏎jurisdiction is divided among federal agencies and 50 state governments, creating uncertainty for⏎companies seeking to deploy across state lines.85⏎However, the most consequential barrier to sustainable AI adoption is the absence of clear⏎federal safety standards. This absence undermines public trust, creates liability uncertainty that⏎deters responsible developers from adopting AI tools, and invites a race to the bottom among⏎states competing to attract AI companies with the least restrictive environments. When patients⏎learn that the AI renewing their medications was authorized by a four-person state office with no⏎medical expertise, or that the supporting evidence is a company-funded preprint, their confidence⏎in clinical AI erodes. Moreover, if reimbursement and regulatory frameworks privilege⏎AI-enabled care, health systems that cannot afford AI tools, particularly safety-net hospitals and⏎rural facilities, risk falling further behind, creating a two-tiered healthcare system in which the⏎quality of care depends on whether a patient’s provider can afford the latest technology.8687⏎The framing of this RFI implicitly treats safety standards as obstacles to innovation. We urge⏎HHS to recognize that the opposite is true. The pharmaceutical industry earned public trust not⏎by operating without FDA oversight but because FDA oversight gave patients confidence that⏎approved drugs met minimum safety standards. Clinical AI requires the same foundation.⏎Question 2: What regulatory or payment changes should be prioritized?⏎The most urgent regulatory change is closing the Clinical Decision Support exemption for AI⏎tools that functionally make autonomous clinical decisions. The CDS exemption under the 21st⏎Century Cures Act was designed for tools that assist clinicians, not systems that replace them.⏎Companies exploit this by adding nominal "clinician review" disclaimers to functionally⏎autonomous AI output. HHS should clarify, in coordination with FDA, that the exemption does⏎not apply when AI output is the primary or default clinical action, when clinicians cannot⏎meaningfully review the reasoning due to model opacity, or when the AI operates without⏎real-time physician oversight.88⏎85https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/paying-for-ai-in-u-s-health-care/⏎86https://www.statnews.com/2026/02/03/utah-doctronic-ai-doctor-prescription-renewal-fda-regulation/⏎87https://fortune.com/2026/01/08/ai-prescription-renewals-doctronic-utah-doctors-warn-puts-patients-at-risk/⏎88https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/fda-oversight-understanding-the-regulation-of-health-ai-tools/⏎⏎Second, HHS should establish mandatory pre-deployment clinical validation proportional to the⏎risk level of the AI tool. A symptom-checker app presents different risks than a system⏎autonomously prescribing medications, and validation requirements should scale accordingly. At⏎minimum, any AI making autonomous treatment or prescribing decisions should be supported by⏎independent, peer-reviewed evidence. Fewer than 2% of FDA-cleared AI/ML devices are⏎currently supported by randomized clinical trials.89⏎Third, HHS should require mandatory fairness testing modeled on Health Canada's Sex and⏎Gender-Based Analysis Plus, which requires manufacturers to collect disaggregated performance⏎data across demographic subgroups. Fourth, HHS should reverse the HTI-5 proposed rule's⏎removal of model card requirements, which would eliminate among the only federal⏎transparency guardrails for clinical AI. Finally, HHS should develop AI-specific reimbursement⏎pathways through CMS that tie payment to validated clinical outcomes rather than volume of⏎AI-generated recommendations.909192⏎Question 3: What novel legal issues arise for AI not classified as medical devices?⏎The liability vacuum is the most urgent legal issue. As documented in Section VI, when⏎autonomous AI makes clinical decisions, the traditional medical malpractice framework does not⏎apply because there is no physician-patient relationship, no defined standard of care for AI, and⏎no ability to determine breach in opaque systems. Product liability also fails because courts have⏎historically treated software as a service rather than a product. The result is that a patient harmed⏎by an autonomous AI prescribing decision may have no viable legal theory under which to seek⏎compensation.9394⏎The learned intermediary doctrine, which shields drug manufacturers by requiring warnings only⏎to prescribing physicians, collapses entirely when AI removes the physician from the prescribing⏎decision. If Doctronic's AI renews a medication where a dangerous interaction has developed,⏎the drug manufacturer warned the "prescriber" (an algorithm), the AI company may claim it is a⏎technology platform, and the patient has no clear defendant. HHS should support the⏎development of clear liability apportionment frameworks for autonomous clinical AI, potentially⏎including strict liability for systems making treatment decisions without physician oversight.95⏎HIPAA's Treatment, Payment, and Operations exception, which permits sharing of protected⏎health information without patient consent, has never been tested for autonomous AI acting as a⏎de facto healthcare provider. HHS should issue guidance clarifying that autonomous AI systems⏎89https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12140231/⏎90https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/medical-devices/application-information/guidance-documents/pre-mark⏎et-guidance-machine-learning-enabled-medical-devices.html⏎91https://www.nelsonmullins.com/insights/blogs/ai-task-force/ai/hhs-proposes-rule-to-deregulate-health-it-and-advance-ai-interoperability-hti-5⏎92https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/paying-for-ai-in-u-s-health-care/⏎93https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK613216/⏎94https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/are-current-tort-liability-doctrines-adequate-addressing-injury-caused-ai/2019-02⏎95https://www.druganddevicelawblog.com/2017/07/artificial-intelligence-and-learned-intermediaries.html⏎⏎accessing patient prescription data require explicit patient authorization, separate from the⏎general TPO exception that was designed for communication between human providers.96⏎Question 4: How should AI be evaluated for clinical safety?⏎Current evaluation practices are inadequate. The Doctronic preprint illustrates the problem: all⏎eight authors hold equity in the company, ethics approval came from an internal committee, and⏎the sample was 500 cases from a single week. Yet this study is the evidentiary basis for the first⏎autonomous AI prescribing system in U.S. history. The Epic Sepsis Model, deployed at hundreds⏎of hospitals, achieved an AUC of only 0.63 in external validation versus the claimed 0.76 to⏎0.83, missing 67% of sepsis patients. The Oxford/Nature Medicine study (February 2026) found⏎AI diagnostic accuracy dropped from 95% on standardized tests to under 35% in real-world⏎interactions. These cases demonstrate that company-reported metrics cannot substitute for⏎independent evaluation.979899⏎HHS should require, at minimum: (a) independent third-party testing by entities with no financial⏎relationship to the developer, following the model proposed in npj Digital Medicine; (b)⏎mandatory reporting of disaggregated performance data across racial, ethnic, gender, and age⏎subgroups, given the systematic biases documented by Obermeyer et al. and others; (c)⏎real-world performance monitoring post-deployment with mandatory adverse event reporting,⏎since controlled-setting performance consistently fails to predict real-world outcomes; and (d)⏎standardized clinical AI safety benchmarks developed in collaboration with NIST.100101⏎The NEJM AI editors have mandated trial preregistration for AI intervention studies starting⏎January 1, 2025. HHS should adopt a parallel requirement: any clinical AI system seeking⏎federal recognition, reimbursement, or deployment in federally funded facilities should be⏎supported by preregistered, independently conducted clinical evidence. We also commend to⏎HHS the LTARC evaluation framework (Local, Task-specific, Agile, Reflective,⏎Community-partnered), which goes beyond existing reporting checklists by requiring evaluation⏎to be conducted locally at the deploying institution, tailored to the specific clinical task, updated⏎continuously as models and populations evolve, reflective of the care context including⏎workflow integration, and developed in partnership with the communities the AI is meant to⏎serve. Only 0.2% of healthcare AI publications report any community involvement in the⏎evaluation process; this must change.102⏎96https://www.law.nyu.edu/sites/default/files/Comment%20of%20NYU%20TLP%20Clinic%20on%20Proposed%20HIPAA%20Rule.pdf⏎97https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎98https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8218233/⏎99https://www.sciencenews.org/article/medical-advice-ai-chatbots-symptoms⏎100https://ai.nejm.org/doi/10.1056/AIe2400146⏎101https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎102⏎⏎Question 5: What private sector activities should HHS support?⏎HHS should support and strengthen existing private-sector quality initiatives, including the Joint⏎Commission and the National Academy of Medicine's AI Code of Conduct. These frameworks⏎establish important voluntary baselines for responsible AI deployment in healthcare. However,⏎HHS should be clear-eyed about the limits of voluntary self-regulation. As AMA President⏎Bruce Scott stated, "voluntary standards are not going to be enough."103104⏎The Texas Attorney General's enforcement action against Pieces Technologies, which found the⏎company's claimed hallucination rate of less than 0.001% was "likely inaccurate," demonstrates⏎that companies will make unsupported clinical claims absent enforceable standards. The FTC has⏎signaled its authority over deceptive AI claims through Operation AI Comply, but⏎healthcare-specific enforcement requires domain expertise that general consumer protection⏎agencies lack. HHS should establish healthcare-specific standards for AI clinical claims and⏎coordinate enforcement with the FTC and state attorneys general.105106⏎HHS should also support the development of independent AI testing laboratories and registries,⏎analogous to the role clinical laboratories play in diagnostics. Developers should be able to⏎submit AI tools for independent evaluation, and health systems should have access to⏎standardized, comparable performance data when making procurement decisions. HHS should⏎also ensure that reimbursement frameworks cover the infrastructure required for responsible AI⏎governance, not just the AI tools themselves. Effective clinical AI requires continuous⏎monitoring, local validation, clinician training, bias auditing, and community engagement. These⏎activities are currently unfunded, creating a systematic underinvestment in the governance⏎infrastructure that safe AI deployment requires. CMS should develop dedicated reimbursement⏎codes for AI governance activities as part of its AI payment strategy.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools met or fallen short of expectations?⏎The pattern across clinical AI is consistent: tools that appear highly accurate in controlled⏎settings underperform substantially in real-world deployment. The Epic Sepsis Model, marketed⏎with an AUC of 0.76 to 0.83, achieved only 0.63 in external validation and missed 67% of⏎patients who developed sepsis, while generating false alerts on 18% of all hospitalized patients.⏎Babylon Health raised $1.2 billion on claims of AI diagnostic superiority before documenting⏎103https://nam.edu/our-work/programs/leadership-consortium/health-care-artificial-intelligence-code-of-conduct/⏎104https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/digital-health/ama-position-2025-federal-government-ai-action-plan⏎105https://www.texasattorneygeneral.gov/news/releases/attorney-general-ken-paxton-reaches-settlement-first-its-kind-healthcare-generative-ai-inv⏎estigation⏎106https://www.ftc.gov/business-guidance/blog/2023/02/keep-your-ai-claims-check⏎⏎systematic triage failures, including missed heart attack symptoms, missed pulmonary embolism⏎risk, and gender-biased assessments. The company collapsed in 2023.107108⏎K Health's AI symptom checker claimed 68% alignment with physicians in a⏎company-associated study, but an independent evaluation found top-3 diagnostic accuracy of⏎only 36%, compared to 82.1% for general practitioners. The Oxford/Nature Medicine study⏎(February 2026) found that real-world AI diagnostic accuracy dropped to under 35%, and⏎patients using AI chatbots performed worse than those using Google search alone. ECRI, the⏎independent patient safety organization, ranked AI misuse as the number one health technology⏎hazard for both 2025 and 2026.109110⏎We are not aware of any clinical AI system that has demonstrated, through independent⏎evaluation, sustained real-world performance matching its marketing claims. This pattern should⏎give HHS significant pause before acting to accelerate adoption. The Department should⏎consider requiring post-deployment performance monitoring as a condition of any federal⏎support for clinical AI.⏎Question 7: Who influences AI adoption decisions and what governance is needed?⏎AI adoption decisions are currently made by a combination of health system executives (CMOs,⏎CIOs, compliance officers), state regulators, and, increasingly, venture-backed companies⏎pursuing regulatory authorization. Each of these actors faces incentives that may not align with⏎patient safety. Health system executives face pressure to reduce costs and staffing. State⏎regulators face pressure to attract technology companies. VC-backed companies face pressure to⏎deploy before competitors and demonstrate traction to investors.111⏎The Utah case illustrates the governance failure that results: a four-person state office with no⏎medical, clinical, or pharmaceutical training authorized autonomous prescribing of 192⏎medications, while the state's own medical association opposed the decision and the AMA called⏎it a serious risk. HHS should require that any governance body authorizing autonomous clinical⏎AI include practicing clinicians with relevant domain expertise, patient representatives, and⏎individuals with no financial relationship to the AI developer.112⏎At the health system level, HHS should encourage the establishment of AI governance⏎committees modeled on institutional review boards, with authority to evaluate clinical AI tools⏎before deployment. The NAM AI Code of Conduct provides a useful framework. HHS should⏎107https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8218233/⏎108https://theweek.com/health/babylon-health-the-failed-ai-wonder-app-that-dazzled-politicians⏎109https://www.sciencenews.org/article/medical-advice-ai-chatbots-symptoms⏎110https://home.ecri.org/blogs/ecri-news/misuse-of-ai-chatbots-tops-annual-list-of-health-technology-hazards⏎111https://medium.com/sand-hill-road/finding-regulatory-moats-at-the-series-a-with-jordan-nof-of-tusk-venture-partners-5e5c0e7abf54⏎112https://ai.utah.gov/staff/⏎⏎also require clinical AI vendors to disclose all funding sources, investor relationships, and⏎conflicts of interest as a condition of deployment in federally funded facilities.113⏎Question 8: How should interoperability be addressed?⏎Interoperability is a legitimate priority, but it is insufficient without safety standards. The⏎Surescripts network already provides near-universal medication history data to clinical AI⏎systems like Doctronic, demonstrating that data access is not the binding constraint on AI⏎deployment. The binding constraint is the absence of standards ensuring that AI systems using⏎this data do so safely and accurately.114⏎The HTI-5 proposed rule's removal of model card requirements for AI clinical decision support⏎tools is counterproductive. Model cards, which disclose training data characteristics, known⏎limitations, and performance across subgroups, are among the only federal transparency⏎requirements for clinical AI. Removing them increases data flow while decreasing the⏎information clinicians and patients need to evaluate whether AI tools are trustworthy. HHS⏎should strengthen, not weaken, transparency requirements as a prerequisite for safe⏎interoperability.115⏎We also note that interoperability without adequate consent protections creates privacy risks. As⏎documented in Section IX, patient medication histories flow through the Surescripts network to⏎commercial AI entities without meaningful patient knowledge or consent, under a HIPAA⏎exception designed for communication between human providers. Expanding interoperability⏎without addressing this consent gap would compound an existing problem.⏎Question 9: What concerns do patients and caregivers have?⏎Patient and caregiver concerns about clinical AI are grounded in documented harm, not⏎speculation. The Tessa chatbot gave eating disorder patients advice to restrict calories and⏎purchase body fat calipers. A 14-year-old and a 13-year-old died by suicide after intensive⏎engagement with Character.AI chatbots that failed to trigger safety interventions. A Stanford⏎study found AI therapy chatbots that listed tall bridges for a user expressing suicidal ideation⏎rather than recognizing the crisis. These are not edge cases from experimental systems; they are⏎outcomes from commercially deployed products used by millions of people.116117118⏎113https://nam.edu/our-work/programs/leadership-consortium/health-care-artificial-intelligence-code-of-conduct/⏎114https://surescripts.com/news-center/intelligence-in-action/interoperability/why-it-matters-prescribers-can-now-access-accurate-medication-histo⏎ry-data-for-virtually-every-american⏎115https://www.nelsonmullins.com/insights/blogs/ai-task-force/ai/hhs-proposes-rule-to-deregulate-health-it-and-advance-ai-interoperability-hti-5⏎116https://www.cbsnews.com/news/eating-disorder-helpline-chatbot-disabled/;⏎https://www.npr.org/sections/health-shots/2023/06/08/1180838096/an-eating-disorders-chatbot-offered-dieting-advice-raising-fears-about-ai-in-he⏎a⏎117https://www.cnn.com/2026/01/07/business/character-ai-google-settle-teen-suicide-lawsuit⏎118https://hai.stanford.edu/news/exploring-the-dangers-of-ai-in-mental-health-care⏎⏎Patients also face an information asymmetry that undermines informed decision-making. When⏎Doctronic's preprint is reported by major outlets as demonstrating 99.2% accuracy without⏎noting that the study was internally funded, non-peer-reviewed, and conducted by equity holders,⏎patients cannot meaningfully evaluate the safety of the AI making decisions about their⏎medications. Over 40 million people daily ask ChatGPT health questions, but a Heidelberg study⏎found 38% of its drug answers are false, with 26% posing high risk of patient harm. Patients⏎have no way to distinguish reliable AI-generated health information from unreliable⏎information.119⏎The data access concern is equally significant. Most patients are unaware that their complete⏎prescription histories are accessible through the Surescripts network, that HIPAA's TPO⏎exception permits this access without consent, and that commercial AI companies can obtain this⏎data. Patients wishing to opt out must complete and notarize a paper form and mail it to⏎corporate headquarters. This is not a meaningful consent framework. HHS should require that⏎patients be notified when AI systems access their health data and given a genuine opportunity to⏎consent or decline.120⏎Question 10: What research priorities should HHS pursue?⏎HHS should prioritize research on the fairness impacts of clinical AI across race, gender, income,⏎age, and disability status. The Obermeyer et al. finding that a widely used algorithm⏎underestimated Black patients' illness severity, reducing their identification for needed care by⏎more than half, was published in 2019. Six years later, a systematic review found 22 of 24⏎studies identified demographic biases in medical LLMs. The problem is well-documented but⏎poorly understood in terms of mechanisms and interventions. HHS-funded research should focus⏎on developing validated debiasing methodologies and mandatory performance benchmarks⏎across demographic subgroups.121122⏎Second, HHS should fund long-term outcome studies of clinical AI deployment. Nearly all⏎existing evidence evaluates AI performance at a single point in time. No published study tracks⏎clinical outcomes for patients whose care was managed or influenced by AI over months or⏎years. Without longitudinal data, the safety of clinical AI deployment remains an open question.⏎The Oxford study's finding that AI diagnostic accuracy in real-world conditions drops to under⏎35% suggests that point-in-time evaluations substantially overestimate real-world benefit.123⏎Third, HHS should fund research on automation bias and clinical deskilling. Evidence from other⏎safety-critical domains (aviation, nuclear power) demonstrates that human operators who rely on⏎automated systems lose independent judgment capacity over time. If AI clinical decision support⏎119https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37263772/⏎120https://www.law.nyu.edu/sites/default/files/Comment%20of%20NYU%20TLP%20Clinic%20on%20Proposed%20HIPAA%20Rule.pdf⏎121https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342⏎122https://link.springer.com/article/10.1186/s12939-025-02419-0⏎123https://www.sciencenews.org/article/medical-advice-ai-chatbots-symptoms⏎⏎becomes standard, and physicians' independent diagnostic skills atrophy, the healthcare system⏎becomes more, not less, fragile. Finally, HHS should support research on the informed consent⏎gap: what patients actually understand about AI involvement in their care, how they evaluate AI⏎safety claims, and what disclosure standards would support genuine informed consent. HHS⏎should also fund research on community-partnered AI evaluation methodologies. The datathon⏎model developed by MIT Critical Data provides structured opportunities for diverse⏎stakeholders, including clinicians, data scientists, patients, and community members, to evaluate⏎AI tools collaboratively. Current healthcare AI research overwhelmingly focuses on populations⏎and conditions already well-served by existing infrastructure. HHS-funded research should⏎explicitly prioritize AI applications addressing care gaps for rural, low-income, and historically⏎underserved populations, and should require that evaluation protocols include the communities⏎the AI is intended to serve.⏎⏎XIV. Structural Recommendations⏎1. Establish federal minimum evidence standards for clinical AI.⏎Any AI system making autonomous clinical decisions should demonstrate safety and efficacy⏎through independent, peer-reviewed clinical evidence before deployment. Company-funded⏎preprints with equity-holding authors cannot constitute an adequate evidentiary basis. HHS⏎should work with FDA to create a premarket review pathway for autonomous clinical AI⏎proportionate to risk.124⏎2. Close the CDS exemption loophole for functionally autonomous AI.⏎AI tools that functionally make clinical decisions, even those with nominal “clinician review”⏎disclaimers, should not escape oversight through the 21st Century Cures Act exemption. HHS⏎should clarify that the CDS exemption does not apply when AI recommendations are presented⏎as the primary or default clinical action.125⏎3. Require mandatory fairness testing.⏎Following Health Canada’s model, HHS should require clinical AI vendors to collect and report⏎disaggregated performance data across demographic subgroups before and after deployment. The⏎evidence that AI trained on non-representative data systematically harms minority patients is too⏎extensive to ignore.126⏎4. Establish a federal adverse event reporting system for clinical AI.⏎No coordinated federal mechanism exists for tracking adverse events from AI clinical tools⏎outside FDA device regulation. HHS should establish a reporting system modeled on MedWatch⏎for incidents involving non-device clinical AI.127⏎5. Issue HIPAA guidance for autonomous AI systems.⏎HHS should clarify that autonomous AI systems require explicit patient authorization before⏎accessing prescription data, separate from the general TPO exception. The current framework, in⏎124https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12140231/⏎125https://bipartisanpolicy.org/issue-brief/fda-oversight-understanding-the-regulation-of-health-ai-tools/⏎126https://www.canada.ca/en/health-canada/services/drugs-health-products/medical-devices/application-information/guidance-documents/pre-mar⏎ket-guidance-machine-learning-enabled-medical-devices.html⏎127https://home.ecri.org/blogs/ecri-news/misuse-of-ai-chatbots-tops-annual-list-of-health-technology-hazards⏎⏎which patient data flows from pharmacy to Surescripts to commercial AI startup without⏎notification, is inconsistent with HIPAA’s privacy protections.128⏎6. Require transparency in state AI sandbox programs involving clinical care.⏎Safety data, adverse event reports, and clinical evaluation methodologies from state sandbox⏎programs must be publicly accessible. Business confidentiality cannot override public health⏎transparency. HHS should condition federal cooperation with state sandboxes on minimum⏎transparency standards.129 Additionally, HHS should require a standardized "Model Facts Label"⏎for all clinical AI tools, analogous to a nutrition label, that discloses in plain language: the⏎training data composition and known gaps, validated performance metrics across demographic⏎subgroups including rural and lower socioeconomic populations, known limitations and failure⏎modes, update history and version control, and the developer’s conflict of interest disclosures.⏎This label should be accessible to both clinicians and patients at the point of care.130⏎7. Develop a liability framework for autonomous clinical AI.⏎HHS should convene stakeholders to develop clear liability apportionment for autonomous AI⏎clinical decisions, considering strict liability models, common enterprise liability, and⏎compensation funds. The current vacuum, where neither malpractice nor product liability clearly⏎applies, leaves patients without recourse.131⏎8. Require disclosure of funding sources and conflicts of interest.⏎Clinical AI companies citing studies to support clinical claims should be required to disclose⏎funding sources, author equity interests, and peer-review status. HHS should examine whether⏎VC firms providing both investment and regulatory strategy create conflicts that compromise⏎regulatory integrity.⏎9. Establish a federated AI Sentinel network for post-market surveillance.⏎Modeled on FDA’s existing Sentinel Initiative for drug safety, HHS should fund a federated AI⏎Sentinel network that collects standardized performance and safety signals from participating⏎health systems. This network should monitor for performance drift, subgroup degradation, and⏎emerging safety signals across deployed clinical AI tools, and publish de-identified safety⏎bulletins and recall-like advisories when models underperform or cause harm. The current⏎128https://www.law.nyu.edu/sites/default/files/Comment%20of%20NYU%20TLP%20Clinic%20on%20Proposed%20HIPAA%20Rule.pdf⏎129https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-024-03304-x⏎130GRAMA Appeal Response, Utah Dept. of Commerce, Feb. 18, 2026 (on file with authors).⏎131https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK613216/; https://repository.law.umich.edu/cgi/viewcontent.cgi?article=1569&context=book_chapters⏎⏎post-market surveillance infrastructure is inadequate: the FDA’s MAUDE system captures fewer⏎than 0.5% of adverse events, and Predetermined Change Control Plans allow AI modifications⏎without fresh clearance. A dedicated Sentinel network would provide the continuous monitoring⏎infrastructure that clinical AI requires.132⏎132https://www.fda.gov/safety/fdas-sentinel-initiative⏎⏎XV. Conclusion⏎This RFI asks how HHS can “accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.” We⏎have documented in this comment why acceleration without evidence standards does not⏎constitute responsible innovation. The evidence base for the most prominent clinical AI systems⏎is company-funded and unreviewed. Documented patient harms range from eating disorders to⏎teen suicides to systematic racial bias. The regulatory framework has gaps that no federal agency⏎has claimed. The liability system cannot tell patients who is responsible when AI harms them.⏎And the financial incentives driving deployment are structurally misaligned with patient safety.133⏎The pattern documented in this comment, in which companies deploy AI with minimal validated⏎evidence, navigating regulation through political strategy rather than clinical proof, access⏎patient data through frameworks never designed for autonomous AI, and generate claims that⏎major media amplify without scrutiny, is not confined to one company or state. It reflects⏎systemic pressures that will only intensify.⏎Every major medical society opposes autonomous AI prescribing without rigorous safeguards.⏎Every comparable international jurisdiction imposes more protective requirements. The⏎documented record of AI health tool harms, including eating disorder patients receiving dieting⏎advice, teenagers dying by suicide, and algorithms that miss 67% of sepsis cases, demonstrates⏎the technology is not ready for autonomous clinical deployment without robust protections.⏎The Department has both the authority and the responsibility to establish the federal floor. Doing⏎so will not slow innovation. It will build the public trust that is the precondition for AI’s⏎sustainable integration into American healthcare.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to comment on this important matter. We are available to provide⏎additional information, testimony, or briefing at the Department’s request.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎MIT Critical Data⏎https://criticaldata.mit.edu/⏎133https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0346♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Canyon, Colleen, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Colleen♔Canyon♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a mental health professional, I support the Psychotherapy Action Network’s (PsiAN) call for caution regarding AI in clinical care. Robust consumer protections are vital to ensure that patients are not misled by AI-driven 'care' that lacks the ethical accountability and personalized safety net provided by a licensed human professional. AI should only serve as a supportive tool for clinicians, never as a replacement for the nuanced, relational work of psychotherapy.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0347♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Elsevier Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Elsevier Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Steve Posnack, Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, Principal⏎Deputy National Coordinator for Information Technology, The Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health IT⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information – Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎1. Introduction and Perspective⏎Elsevier Inc. appreciates the opportunity to respond to this Request for Information on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎Elsevier is a global leader in advanced information and decision support. For over a century, we⏎have been helping advance science and healthcare to advance human progress. We support⏎academic and corporate research communities, doctors, nurses, future healthcare professionals⏎and educators across 170 countries in their vital work. We do this by delivering mission-critical⏎insights and innovative solutions that combine trusted, evidence-based scientific and medical⏎content with cutting-edge AI technologies to help impact makers achieve better outcomes.⏎Our Clinical Solutions team develops evidence-based clinical decision support (CDS)⏎solutions used by hospitals, integrated delivery networks (IDNs), clinicians, and care teams⏎across the world. Our products are designed to assist clinicians at the point of care by providing⏎timely access to trusted medical knowledge and insights from current research. By integrating⏎into the clinical workflow, our solutions support clinicians to make informed decisions,⏎enhancing the quality and efficiency of healthcare delivery.⏎Our perspective is grounded in decades of experience delivering CDS in regulated healthcare⏎environments, maintaining editorial independence, clinical governance, and patient safety,⏎while responsibly adopting new technologies such as generative AI.⏎⏎2. AI in Clinical Care: Value and Scope⏎AI-enabled CDS tools that support information retrieval, contextualize, and summarization⏎already play a meaningful role in clinical care by:⏎• Improving clinician efficiency and reducing administrative and cognitive burden,⏎• Supporting consistency with evidence-based practice,⏎• Enhancing access to up-to-date medical knowledge within clinical workflows.⏎Importantly, not all AI used in clinical care serves the same function or carries the same risk. AI⏎systems that inform clinical decision-making are distinct from systems that automate, direct,⏎or execute clinical decisions. Federal policy should continue to recognize and preserve this⏎distinction.⏎3. The Need for Proportionate Evidence Standards⏎A central issue raised by this RFI—appropriately—is how evidence should be generated and⏎evaluated for AI used in clinical care.⏎Elsevier encourages HHS to adopt proportionate, risk-based evidence expectations that align⏎with an AI system’s intended use and level of clinical impact. In particular:⏎• AI tools that retrieve, summarize, or contextualize existing medical knowledge should⏎not be held to the same evidentiary standards as tools that diagnose, predict, or prescribe.⏎• Evidence frameworks should recognize the value of real-world evidence, post-market⏎performance monitoring, and continuous improvement—especially for AI systems that⏎evolve over time. Overly rigid or one-size-fits-all evidence requirements risk slowing⏎adoption of beneficial tools without commensurate gains in patient safety.⏎For CDS tools such as ClinicalKey AI, trust is built through transparent sourcing, traceability⏎to underlying evidence, clinician reviewability, and clear limitations, rather than through⏎automation or prescriptive outputs.⏎4. Regulatory Clarity and CDS Boundaries⏎Regulatory uncertainty remains a significant barrier to responsible AI adoption in healthcare.⏎Elsevier supports continued efforts by FDA, ONC, and HHS to:⏎• Provide clear, consistent guidance on the boundary between non-device CDS and⏎regulated Software as a Medical Device (SaMD),⏎⏎• Reinforce the importance of human oversight, explainability, and the clinician’s role as⏎the final decision-maker,⏎• Avoid regulatory approaches that inadvertently push low-risk, informational AI tools into⏎medical device frameworks solely due to the use of advanced AI techniques.⏎Clarity in this area enables healthcare organizations to adopt AI confidently, while allowing⏎developers to invest responsibly in safety, quality, and innovation.⏎5. Adoption, Trust, and Real-World Deployment⏎From Elsevier’s experience deploying CDS at enterprise scale, successful AI adoption depends⏎on more than technical performance. Key enablers include:⏎• Integration into existing clinical workflows and health IT ecosystems,⏎• Alignment with clinical governance, training, and change management,⏎• Clear communication of intended use, limitations, and appropriate reliance,⏎• Organizational trust in the provenance and independence of clinical content.⏎Federal policy can support adoption by reinforcing these principles and avoiding incentives that⏎prioritize speed or novelty over safe, clinically meaningful use.⏎6. Closing⏎Elsevier supports HHS’s goal of responsible AI adoption in clinical care and appreciates the⏎opportunity to contribute to this dialogue. We believe that policies grounded in risk-based⏎regulation, proportionate evidence standards, and respect for clinical judgment will best⏎enable AI to help improve care quality, clinician experience, and patient outcomes.⏎We welcome continued engagement with HHS, FDA, CMS, and ONC as these policies evolve.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎TC Kong⏎Global VP of Quality, Regulatory Compliance, and Operational Excellence⏎Elsevier Clinical Solutions⏎Elsevier Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0348♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔HBMA Response to ASTP ONC AI RFI♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Matt♔Reiter♔♔♔♔♔HBMA♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The Healthcare Business Management Association (HBMA) is pleased to submit this comment⏎letter in response to your request for information (RFI) titled: Accelerating the Adoption and Use⏎of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13).⏎We appreciate ASTP/ONC’s focus on understanding how AI can enhance clinical care. We urge⏎ASTP/ONC to also consider how AI’s integration into clinical care intersects with the back-end⏎revenue cycle management (RCM) process, which will be an essential factor in the proliferation⏎of AI into the clinical setting. We encourage the agency to issue similar RFIs in the future that⏎focus on the RCM process and to engage with stakeholders like HBMA through meetings and⏎industry focus groups.⏎Below, please find our responses to specific questions from the RFI.⏎Question 2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations.⏎AI has significant potential to improve the healthcare system. Not only can it help improve⏎clinical outcomes, it can also be used to improve administrative processes that create avoidable⏎burdens on clinicians. These burdens consume time and resources that could otherwise be used⏎on patient care.⏎Any conversation about the clinical applications of AI must be paired with the administrative⏎functions that accompany the clinical care such as the revenue cycle management (RCM)⏎⏎process, documentation requirements, and quality improvement programs. Clinicians must be⏎adequately reimbursed for the care they provide. As with all medical services, clinicians must be⏎fairly compensated for new clinical applications that utilize AI.⏎AI should not be a justification to reduce reimbursement rates. The 2026 Medicare Physician Fee⏎Schedule (PFS) controversially finalized an “efficiency adjustment” which reduces Medicare⏎reimbursements due to the assumption that care becomes more efficient as clinicians gain more⏎experience. While this is often true, these efficiencies are more than offset by administrative and⏎financial burdens from government regulations and actions by commercial payers.⏎Clinicians are more likely to adopt AI tools in clinical care if it can generate efficiencies,⏎decrease burden, improve patient care, and if it is fairly reimbursed. HHS should understand that⏎every health system, hospital, physician office, medical specialty and clinician have different⏎needs and uses for AI in clinical care and the administrative functions of the healthcare system.⏎These needs widely vary both across the entire healthcare system and within individual health⏎systems and practices. Any federal AI policy must be responsive to these individual needs.⏎HBMA supports the development and use of AI for these beneficial purposes. However, our⏎industry is reliant on private sector innovation to develop and maintain these technologies.⏎Additionally, reimbursement policies set by commercial and government payers will have a⏎major impact on adoption of AI tools.⏎In addition to reimbursement rates, it is important to understand if payers will establish⏎burdensome documentation and administrative requirements for items and services that⏎incorporate AI or decrease payment rates based on potential efficiency improvements. For⏎example, will clinicians be required to document use of AI tools? Will they need to identify the⏎specific tool that was used? Will payers establish separate payment rates for the same procedure⏎depending on if AI is or is not used?⏎We encourage HHS to consider the administrative impacts of AI as part of any effort to promote⏎AI in clinical care. HHS should issue a new RFI on how the adoption of AI in clinical care will⏎impact administrative and revenue cycle processes.⏎We are already seeing commercial payers deploy AI to inappropriately deny higher volumes of⏎claims and prior authorization requests. These companies, which are among the largest⏎corporations in the country, have vast resources to utilize these tools for these purposes. Some of⏎these actions are subject to class action lawsuits and regulatory scrutiny.⏎We believe that government policies must ensure an even playing field for using AI across all⏎parts of the healthcare system. Frustratingly, commercial payers are increasingly relying on AI to⏎adjudicate claims at the same time they are stifling how clinicians use AI. For example, many⏎commercial payers will communicate with providers using automated AI phone calls. However,⏎these payers refuse to accept calls from providers using the same automated AI phone call⏎technologies. These phone calls are a practical way that AI can reduce administrative burdens by⏎facilitating tedious, time-intensive processes that allows humans to focus their time and energy⏎on more important work.⏎⏎Question 3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to⏎help address them?⏎Liability protections must be a central aspect of any policy that incentivizes the use of AI in⏎clinical care. Clinicians must have a clear understanding of their potential legal liability due to⏎alleged issues caused by AI tools. A robust AI policy must establish a definitive set of legal⏎liability protections for how clinicians use AI in clinical care. While there are many ways to⏎approach creating such protections, the most important part is that legal liability can be clearly⏎understood by all parties. A lack of clarity on this important topic could dissuade clinicians from⏎implementing AI tools in clinical care.⏎HHS also has a part to play in defining the privacy and security requirements around AI⏎applications for all involved parties, including AI developers, health systems, clinicians and⏎payers. Early and ongoing interpretation of existing laws and regulations and continued updates⏎as needed will help to remove roadblocks and clarify guardrails for implementation and⏎monitoring of AI in clinical settings.⏎Additionally, AI tools should be structured to enhance and support a clinician’s abilities. It⏎should not be used to replace a clinician. For example, some AI tools are designed to provide⏎mental health care but have actually offered incredibly dangerous medical advice. HHS should⏎draw a clear line that prevents AI tools from replacing clinicians.⏎Question 5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HBMA believes there must be a process for validating AI tools in a way that differentiates useful⏎AI tools from inadequate or flawed products. We caution HHS (or any federal agency) against⏎declaring itself as an authority on accrediting, approving or certifying AI tools for use in clinical⏎care.⏎Many organizations are seeking to assert themselves as an AI authority. HHS partners with⏎outside organizations to implement policies such as selecting CAQH CORE to develop operating⏎rules for HIPAA transactions and relying on contractors for quality measure development. We⏎caution against relying on outside vendors to serve as AI certification entities. Past experience⏎with how EHRs were certified by both government and non-government entities despite many⏎flaws with these products suggests a more cautious and deliberate approach is needed. HHS⏎should not rely on an organization to serve as an AI accreditor or certifier until HHS first⏎establishes a clear policy for how AI should be used in clinical care.⏎⏎At this time, we feel the best role for government is to establish minimum standards as a⏎guidance to industry on which AI tools are appropriate for clinical care. Clinicians and RCM⏎companies want to be compliant. However, this requires clear, concise guidance for what⏎regulators expect and resources to assist with compliance. For example, guidance should clarify⏎what data elements are acceptable for use in large language models (LLMs), or requirements for⏎data storage and retention. Additionally, guidance must be well organized and indexed in a⏎standardized fashion so that they are easily searchable and clearly display updates.⏎Additionally, HHS should leverage the Medicare reimbursement system to decide which tools it⏎will or will not cover or reimburse. By choosing to reimburse for an item or service that utilizes⏎AI, HHS can control which tools are used in clinical care. We caution HHS against requiring⏎clinicians to document use of AI as that would add more layers to an already burdensome⏎process.⏎AI is already helping to revolutionize the healthcare RCM industry. The RCM industry is filled⏎with avoidable administrative burdens – barriers that are largely constructed by payers. Processes⏎such as prior authorizations, supplemental documentation requests, denials and appeals, and lack⏎of interoperability make the back-office functions of our healthcare system are more difficult⏎than they need to be. The role of the RCM industry is to absorb these challenges on behalf of⏎medical practices so that clinicians can focus their time and resources on caring for patients⏎instead of administrative requirements.⏎The RCM industry is now incorporating automation and certain types of AI into our workflows⏎by necessity as a response to these challenges.⏎Clinician burnout is one of the top health policy priorities for both Congress and regulators. We⏎believe that HHS should approach AI from the perspective of reducing burdens to ease burnout.⏎Many clinicians are choosing to leave the clinical workforce over these burdens. AI tools have⏎the potential to either increase or decrease these burdens. We believe government guidance and⏎regulations must maintain a focus on reducing burnout and must be responsive to concerns from⏎clinicians about how AI could potentially increase burdens.⏎Question 7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the⏎primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Revenue cycle is a major factor in decisions to adopt AI tools. Clinicians must be fairly⏎reimbursed for using AI tools in clinical care. The RCM industry is responsible for ensuring⏎clinicians are reimbursed for the services they provide. This often means navigating complex⏎billing and coding policies, understanding coverage policies unique to each payer, facilitating⏎documentation requests and managing denials and coding adjustments.⏎We believe HHS should build on this RFI by shifting its focus from the clinical factors to the⏎administrative and revenue cycle factors related to AI adoption. We suggest HHS issues⏎⏎additional RFIs on these topics. We also recommend that HHS consults with stakeholder⏎organizations such as HBMA to discuss these issues in more detail. HHS should also consider⏎convening focus groups of stakeholder organizations to provide input on HHS’ AI policies.⏎HHS must maintain separation between adoption of AI and quality programs such as MIPS.⏎HHS should not hold clinicians financially accountable for using or not using AI. While AI⏎certainly has potential to improve care quality and efficiency – both of which are the primary⏎goals for HHS value-based payment programs – it is essential that AI adoption remains separated⏎from these programs in these early days of AI development. We support policies that incentivize⏎using AI under conditions described throughout this response. However, we believe such policies⏎should not be included in value-based payment programs, which already create significant⏎financial and administrative burdens for clinicians with minimal potential rewards to return the⏎investment necessary to successfully participate in these programs. .⏎Question 8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific⏎data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎HBMA supports interoperability but is frustrated that it still does not exist. While improvements⏎have been made, many EHR systems are still not interoperable. In some cases, products are not⏎interoperable with other products from the same vendor. New policies that leverage standard⏎APIs have promise to greatly improve interoperability. However, widespread API adoption will⏎be needed to achieve these goals. We believe AI could help address this issue by serving as an⏎intermediary to ensure that health data is interoperable across EHR platforms.⏎Importantly, these same interoperability concerns apply to AI products used in clinical care. AI⏎has great potential to connect products, clinicians and other technology into a cohesive care⏎delivery platform. We encourage HHS to emphasize interoperability in any policy that promotes⏎the use of AI tools in clinical care.⏎Question 9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎From the perspective of the RCM company, patients are frustrated with many aspects of the⏎healthcare system. These “pain points” include confusion over medical bills, misunderstanding⏎of their plan’s benefits and provider network, frustration over health insurance coverage⏎decisions such as preauthorization care delays and coverage denials, and lack of price⏎transparency, among other things.⏎⏎We believe AI can improve the consumer experience for patients by addressing many of these⏎issues. For example, AI tools can help patients better understand their health insurance and⏎coverage decisions, as well as make price transparency data easier to provide and interpret.⏎However, patients typically prefer to interact with a human rather than a bot. Perhaps agentic AI⏎will improve to a point where this dynamic changes. However, that is not the current reality. It is⏎therefore important to provide transparency to patients if they are dealing with an AI or bot⏎instead of a human.⏎Studies show patients are skeptical of how is used in clinical care. Patients continue to strongly⏎trust their physicians. Clinicians must be careful to balance AI’s potential to improve clinical⏎care with patient’s mistrust of these tools.⏎Providing transparency around how AI tools are developed, tested, and protected against bias are⏎essential to building trust in these tools among both patients and clinicians. We are concerned⏎that AI models could be trained on flawed data sources. For example, many EHRs contain⏎outdated, redundant and inaccurate information. AI tools that are trained on EHR data could⏎result in flawed end-use products.⏎Care quality and safety must always be a higher priority than any potential efficiencies gained by⏎AI tools. Further, AI cannot and should not ever replace a clinician. There are many interesting⏎use cases in specialties such as radiology and mental health. However, AI tools are intended to⏎support the clinician, not replace them.⏎With high demand for health care and shortages of clinicians in many areas, AI can make care⏎more efficient both clinically and administratively.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you for your consideration of our comments, especially those that urge ASTP/ONC to⏎consider how the RCM industry will play an important role regarding how AI is incorporated⏎into clinical care. Please do not hesitate to contact HBMA Director of Government Relations⏎Matt Reiter (reiterm@capitolassociates.com) or HBMA Executive Director Brad Lund⏎(brad@hbma.org) if you wish to discuss our recommendations in more detail.⏎Sincerely,⏎Landon Tooke, JD, MLS, CHC, CCEP, CHBME⏎President, HBMA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0349♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Physical Therapy Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Physical Therapy Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health & Human Services⏎330 C St., SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically at www.regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎On behalf of our 100,000 member physical therapists, physical therapist assistants, and students of⏎physical therapy, the American Physical Therapy Association appreciates the opportunity to submit⏎comments in response to the Department of Health and Human Services’ Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. APTA is dedicated to⏎building a community that advances the physical therapy profession to improve the health of society. As⏎experts in rehabilitation, pre-habilitation, and habilitation, physical therapists play a unique role in⏎society in prevention, wellness, fitness, health promotion, and management of disease and disability for⏎individuals across the age span, helping individuals improve overall health and prevent the need for⏎avoidable health care services. Physical therapists’ roles include education, direct intervention, research,⏎advocacy, and collaborative consultation. These roles are essential to the profession’s vision of⏎transforming society by optimizing movement to improve the human experience.⏎Artificial Intelligence Can Augment Physical Therapist Practice⏎APTA is pleased to see that HHS alongside the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of⏎the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology is seeking stakeholder feedback on how to⏎appropriately regulate, reimburse, and encourage research and development of artificial intelligence in⏎clinical care. APTA believes digital health technologies, digital therapeutics, and artificial intelligence⏎have the potential to augment physical therapist practice by expanding access, enhancing care delivery⏎models, promoting safety, reducing administrative burden, and improving outcomes. As part of this⏎belief, we continue to support the ethical development and integration of AI into clinical care when it⏎reduces administrative burden and enhances physical therapist practice, education, and research for the⏎best interest of patients and the profession. But to ensure that AI is used in the best interests of the⏎patient, we emphasize the following principles:⏎⏎• Physical therapists, in collaboration with the patient or client, shall determine the optimal⏎modes to access physical therapist services, whether this is in person, remotely, or some⏎combination.⏎• A physical therapist is responsible for all aspects of patient and client management.⏎• Physical therapist services shall be conducted in a manner that allows for patient and client⏎engagement and supports the therapeutic alliance.⏎• The physical therapist and the physical therapist assistant adhere to scope of practice, including⏎requirements that are based on the jurisdiction in which the patient or client is located when⏎receiving physical therapist services.⏎• Physical therapist services shall be provided consistent with appropriate direction and⏎supervision requirements of assistive personnel and other support personnel, as well as all⏎relevant Association policies, positions, and binding ethical documents.⏎• Physical therapy is represented, provided, and promoted only when it meets term and title⏎protections as defined in state law and in accordance with federal health, communications, and⏎trade authorities.⏎Acknowledging these principles, we strongly assert that technology that utilizes artificial intelligence⏎should only be used as an extension of the physical therapist practice, not as a replacement of the⏎physical therapist. With this in mind, we provide our responses to the questions posed in the RFI.⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎Limited Regulatory Guidance: The current AI legal and regulatory landscape is a patchwork of guidance⏎that places the onus on industry to evaluate and ensure compliance. Additionally, there is little to no⏎assistance given to health care providers on how to deploy AI safely and effectively in clinical settings. It⏎would be inappropriate to say that federal agencies have not exercised any regulatory authority over AI,⏎particularly as it relates to safety and security, however most administrative actions to date have⏎focused on enforcement of existing regulatory authorities and voluntary commitments from the⏎industry as opposed to creating new laws and regulations. While all positive steps towards alignment,⏎task forces, action plans and frameworks set standards for best practices that are not enforceable by⏎law. This lack of oversight at the federal level concerning the development, prohibition, or restriction of⏎AI has forced states to enact their own laws which only further complicates the policy environment.⏎The reason for this lack of oversight is because no laws to date have provided regulatory authorities to a⏎specific department or agency for the development of AI, use of AI, or prohibitions on AI. While the⏎Food and Drug Administration has regulatory authority over medical devices, AI technology is only⏎considered “software as a medical device” if it makes claims about diagnosing, treating, preventing, or⏎curing a disease or medical condition, provides recommendations or actions that significantly impact⏎clinical decision making, processes patient specific medical data and provides outputs that are⏎time critical or could lead to a clinical intervention and does not allow or clearly enable the clinician to⏎‑ ‑⏎independently review how the decision is made. Consequently, AI devices used in clinical care are not⏎‑⏎regulated if they help with administration, record keeping, scheduling, reminders, or similar nonclinical⏎workflows, and most notably, if it is a clinical decision support tool that does not use medical images,⏎/ 2⏎⏎make directives, or work with information that is normally communicated between providers, and if the⏎basis for any recommendations the device provides can be is independently reviewed by a clinician. This⏎criteria leaves a glaring gap between what is current regulated and what should be regulated, namely by⏎excluding many clinical decision support software tools from the definition of medical device. Thus,⏎while holding status as the agency that regulates medical devices, in actuality, the FDA is only regulating⏎a small portion of AI devices that are used in clinical care. This lack of clear federal oversight creates fear⏎and discourages providers from using AI-powered devices.⏎Inability to Identify High-Quality AI Tools: Because there is limited federal oversight of AI use in⏎healthcare settings, clinicians lack clear mechanisms by which to identify high-quality technology. To fill⏎this gap, APTA partnered with ORCHA, a digital health evaluation and distribution platform, to⏎encourage appropriate use of digital health tools. With ORCHA’s assistance, APTA launched a Digital⏎Health App Library containing digital health tools that have been independently reviewed for safety,⏎effectiveness, and quality. ORCHA evaluates the tools based on a digital health assessment framework⏎that focuses on three key evaluation criteria across 400 standards: data and privacy, clinical assurance⏎and safety, and usability and accessibility. An additional criterion—technical security and stability—has⏎been newly added and is not yet visible on all applications. Notably, ORCHA reassesses digital health⏎apps every time they are updated.⏎While APTA has used ORCHA to meet a clear need, the system is far from perfect. ORCHA’s digital⏎health advisory committee is currently developing an AI evaluation tool that is set to launch in the⏎coming months but until then, the evaluation process does not currently assess AI use and cannot be⏎relied upon to adequately evaluate the AI mechanisms within a digital platform. Additionally, many app⏎developers have not made it clear who bears liability if there is an adverse event which may increase⏎provider hesitancy. Until there is a better mechanism that allows providers to distinguish between low-⏎and high-quality AI technologies, many may choose not to use them at all. To avoid this, APTA⏎encourages HHS to use an interagency approach to establish a public registry of AI tools used in clinical⏎care, including information on their intended use, validation evidence, and performance metrics. This⏎would function similarly to FDA's medical device databases but for non-medical AI devices where there⏎is an existing gap.⏎Liability Remains Unclear: Historically, physical therapists have been fully responsible for all aspects of⏎patient management, but PTs cannot continue to bear the brunt of all professional liability if AI plays an⏎increasing role in the delivery and oversight of care. The therapist knows that it is their license that can⏎be taken away in instances of negligence, injury, or improper treatment, so if HHS wants to increase the⏎use of AI in clinical care, there needs to be clear distinction between the therapist’s and technology’s⏎liability. Unless these clear delineations in legal and ethical responsibilities are made, providers will⏎continue to express hesitancy around fully adopting AI technology in their clinics.⏎Lack of Infrastructure: Many providers have also yet to adopt AI technologies because they cannot⏎afford the underlying infrastructure required for AI adoption, including investments in interoperability,⏎/ 3⏎⏎workforce training, and technical assistance. Physical therapists, in particular, have faced significant⏎challenges related to interoperability, namely the acquisition of certified health record technology. This⏎limited adoption of standardized data exchange formats hinders the flow of critical patient information⏎across care transitions and limits the utility of AI devices. If clinicians are not supported in the uptake of⏎AI, the digital divide will continue to create inequitable access to AI use in clinical care.⏎Reimbursement Barriers: Fee-for-service payment structures provide limited incentives for providers to⏎invest in AI tools, even when such tools could improve outcomes and reduce costs over time. Coverage⏎and reimbursement decisions for AI-enabled services are slow, and current payment models do not⏎adequately account for the value of AI interventions. To solve this, alternative payment models should⏎incentivize the use of high-value AI interventions that improve patient outcomes. APTA was pleased to⏎see the introduction of the Advancing Chronic Care with Effective, Scalable Solutions Model which offers⏎payments to providers who use technology to treat a range of chronic conditions, and we are eager to⏎see whether the model’s deployment will increase the uptake of AI technology.⏎Limited Understanding of AI Risks, Capabilities and Limitations: At this time, the safe and proper use of⏎digital tools, including AI, is not a mandated part of a physical therapist’s training and curriculum. As a⏎result, the extent to which certain clinicians are familiar with AI risks and benefits or data safety⏎principles varies immensely. And while the use of technology, whether it utilizes AI or not, is a risk to all⏎clinicians who are unfamiliar with it, the risk is higher for newer clinicians who may become deskilled by⏎the overuse of and overreliance on AI. This could lead to issues with long-term workforce competency⏎due to skill atrophy, passive learning, and an overreliance on false expertise rather than clinical training.⏎Because the value of AI outputs is highly dependent on the questions being asked, clinician users must⏎understand how to properly engage with the model to receive the best output. If utilized by clinicians⏎who are untrained in AI systems, there could be catastrophic repercussions. To avoid this, APTA⏎encourages HHS to support professional societies in developing AI competency standards and⏎continuing education requirements that help develop AI literacy.⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating⏎to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Robust Data Training in Large Language Models: AI applications utilize large language models that rely⏎on learning algorithms that are trained on massive data sets. Medical LLMs require vast and diverse⏎datasets encompassing clinical records, medical literature, healthcare guidelines, and domain-specific⏎knowledge resources. The quality, diversity, and representativeness of the training data directly impacts⏎the model’s performance and whether the model’s outputs will align with current clinical practice. As a⏎result, considerable focus must be placed on ensuring that LLMs are trained on robust, high-quality⏎research and existing patient data, and that this data is continuously updated as the science.⏎/ 4⏎⏎Furthermore, reinforcement learning from human feedback is also necessary to align model outputs⏎with current guidelines, enhancing the relevance and safety of generated content. Without adequate⏎training with robust data and the ability to learn from human feedback, the use of AI in clinical care⏎could exacerbate existing bias and discrimination.⏎A landmark 2019 study found that a commercially available algorithm used to predict health care needs⏎for more than 100 million people was biased against Black patients. The algorithm relied on past health⏎care spending to predict future health needs but with less access to care, Black patients often spent less.⏎As a result, Black patients had to be much sicker to be recommended for extra care under the algorithm.⏎If adequate quality standards are not placed on AI data acquisition and training before the model is⏎deployed, the device will inadvertently seek out patterns which could lead to biased outcomes. There⏎must be systems in place to both ensure that only unbiased, high-quality data is used in AI models and⏎to recognize when inappropriate recommendations are made.⏎To avoid any risk of bias and ensure LLMs have clinical integrity, APTA urges HHS to partner with ASTP/⏎ONC to create pre-deployment evaluation methods including but not limited to prospective validation,⏎bias auditing, and workflow integration testing. And even after a model has been trained, its⏎performance must be continuous monitored and patient outcomes should be surveilled to ensure that⏎the model is functioning as intended. In this, HHS should also require developers to perform routine⏎dataset auditing that looks at performance across different patient demographics to prevent patients⏎from receiving biased or clinically unproven outputs.⏎Output Transparency: High-performance AI models can operate as “black boxes,” making their outputs⏎hard to interpret. This limits the provider’s ability to review clinical evidence that supports any⏎treatment recommendations the device makes. To ensure that clinicians trust AI as their clinical⏎assistant, all AI tools used in clinical care should be evaluated for their ability to provide clinically⏎meaningful explanations of treatment recommendations.⏎The most promising way to inform the provider—who is the end user—and future developers of how a⏎given AI tool was built, and the algorithms underlying its design, is through model cards. They function⏎differently than a typical user manual because AI is a living breathing tool that can, and should, be⏎revised over time. By spelling out how the algorithms underlying the technology were developed and⏎their intended use, model cards help clinicians understand the capabilities and limits of the models⏎which can help them best use the platform and educate patients accordingly. To ensure this level of⏎transparency, APTA recommends that HHS require the use of model cards as a transparency mechanism⏎within all AI tools used to provide care under federally regulated insurance programs.⏎Privacy, Security, and HIPAA Compliance: The use of AI in clinical care raises new privacy and security⏎concerns that have not yet been addressed through rulemaking and go beyond that of traditional care.⏎Such concerns include: whether AI-generated clinical insights constitute protected health information⏎under HIPAA, how consent should be obtained for using patient data, security standards for model⏎/ 5⏎⏎storage, transmission, and updates, and privacy risks when AI tools use health data from non-HIPAA⏎covered wearables and apps. While there are many ways to ensure these concerns are addressed,⏎including but not limited to Business/ Associate Agreements, HIPAA eligible hosting, encryption, and⏎zero data-retention controls, it is currently unclear which compliance standards non-medical AI devices⏎are subject to. To decrease ambiguity, HHS should clearly state which existing privacy and security laws⏎and regulations AI must comply with. Specifically, HHS should issue guidance clarifying HIPAA⏎applicability to AI systems and establish security standards for all AI tools used in clinical care.⏎Liability Concerns: When an AI tool recommends a clinical intervention that results in patient harm,⏎liability questions arise: Is the provider liable for following the recommendation? Is the AI vendor liable⏎for the algorithm’s output? Is the health system liable for deploying an inadequately validated tool?⏎Current medical malpractice frameworks do not clearly address AI-mediated decision-making. To avoid⏎this confusion, some companies, such as Doctronic, have gone as far as to create malpractice insurance⏎for the AI system so that it may hold the same level of accountability as a physician. Is this to be⏎expected of all AI tools, autonomous or not? These questions must be answered to encourage AI⏎innovation and use in clinical care. APTA asks HHS to work with state medical boards, professional⏎liability insurers, and legal experts to develop guidance on shared accountability models.⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and⏎where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎If properly regulated and utilized appropriately, high-quality AI technology can be paired with the⏎human judgement of a clinician to enable better care, tackle numerous challenges that exist within the⏎larger healthcare system, and reduce strain on the clinician workforce. In physical therapist practice, AI⏎tools are already being deployed to reduce provider paperwork and health data complexity and⏎fragmentation to optimize patient outcomes.⏎The following tools have met or exceed performance and have the greatest potential to improve health⏎care outcomes:⏎Clinical Documentation and Administrative Support: The healthcare system utilizes enormous amounts⏎of textual data in the form of medical records, research literature, clinical guidelines, and patient⏎communications. The vast quantity of this data and the need for it to be reviewed in a timely manner⏎represents a terrific opportunity for the use of AI, or more specifically, LLMs. Rather than utilize the time⏎of a skilled clinician, administrative tasks, such as scheduling, billing, documentation drafting, and⏎literature review, could be handled by AI. Using AI to automate these processes could save provider⏎time, reduce burden, and pose little to no risk because they have no ability to alter clinical decisions as⏎long as the provider is able to review any final outputs. And if clinicians are freed up to practice at the⏎top of their licensure, they can see more patients or handle more complex cases.⏎/ 6⏎⏎A fitting example is AI-powered documentation that produces written summaries of patient encounters.⏎APTA recently published a practice advisory on AI-ambient tools which operate discreetly in the⏎background and use artificial intelligence to automatically capture, transcribe, and summarize patient-⏎provider interactions into structured clinical notes. Administrative optimization is another means by⏎which providers can use AI to maximize time spent with patients. Smart-scheduling, auto-coding and⏎charge capture are all technology-driven solutions to the problems created by a complex health care⏎system with an ever-increasing administrative load. If HHS wishes to accelerate the use of AI, it could⏎encourage CMS to expand coverage for ambient AI and other technologies that reduce provider pain⏎points.⏎Clinical Decision Support Systems and Diagnostic Tools: If appropriately regulated, clinical decision⏎support systems and diagnostic tools have the most potential in augmenting the clinical decision-making⏎process and improving patient outcomes. A clinical decision support is a digital tool that provides timely⏎and person-specific information to enhance patient outcomes and quality of care. It can assist the⏎therapist in selecting the most appropriate treatment by identifying potential diagnoses, suggesting⏎appropriate tests, and recommending evidence-based treatments. It can serve as a clinical assistant by⏎helping the therapist synthesize complex information and consider diagnostic possibilities. The goal is to⏎enhance clinical reasoning while allowing the PT to maintain human oversight of final decisions⏎regarding treatment. While these tools demonstrate the highest potential, they must be regulated⏎appropriately. As such, APTA recommends that HHS ASTP/ONC establish certification criteria for AI-⏎enabled clinical decision support tools that require disclosure of training data characteristics, model⏎performance on diverse populations, and explanation of decision logic.⏎Patient Engagement Tools: Patient engagement tools, such as patient portals, digital platforms, and⏎mobile apps, can serve as asynchronous touch points between therapy visits. While remote therapeutic⏎monitoring codes already provide reimbursement for patient monitoring using wearable technology,⏎mobile apps and other tools can improve the patient experience by streamlining communication and⏎personalizing healthcare. Some patients may even better adhere to their treatment plans. Many of⏎these tools are already widely available and used in healthcare settings.⏎The following tools should be used cautiously or not at all:⏎Computer Vision Technology and Independent Triage Tools: There is immense value in computer vision⏎technology that can document the patient’s physical capabilities during and after the therapy session. If⏎computer vision is used during the session, the therapist can give their full attention to the patient and⏎can review the patient’s performance after the fact as needed. While technology cannot fully replicate⏎the observational abilities and critical thinking of the therapist, it can enhance the capacity of the⏎therapist during the session and maximize the time the PT spends with the patient. In these instances,⏎the technology could also generate notes and a post-visit summary, which the therapist can review, edit⏎appropriately, and sign-off on. This technology has immense potential to reduce burden and maximize⏎both the patient and provider experience.⏎/ 7⏎⏎These tools can also be deployed outside of the clinic, in the patient’s home. Patients can use computer⏎vision and wearable devices to better understand their performance as they complete home exercise⏎programs. Certain tools contain feedback applications that report patterns to the therapist who can⏎appropriately decide the next course of action. Like wearable devices that analyze movements, feedback⏎applications can flag when a patient’s joint moves out of alignment. For example, if AI technology were⏎to provide real-time joint angle feedback, the patient could correct an improper movement in real-time,⏎rather than wait until their next in-person visit. However, if there is no mechanism triggering providers⏎to sign-off, this may not be a safe and effective tool. As such, APTA recommends caution when using⏎triage tools such as home exercise feedback applications and progress dashboards that do not⏎appropriately triage patient performance or consult a therapist before making changes to the treatment⏎plan that should be overseen by a licensed professional.⏎Fully Autonomous AI: APTA does not support the use of fully autonomous diagnosis bots or the⏎provision of unsupervised treatment plans. In clinical settings, human oversight ensures data accuracy⏎and patient safety. If there is no clinician sign-off or approval processes in place, AI technology is acting,⏎for all intents and purposes, as a physical therapist. And APTA believes that AI will be most effective⏎when it is used to support clinicians rather than operate independently. Any clinical tools that use⏎agentive AI, which has minimal human oversight and independently handles complex, multi-step⏎workflows, should be rigorously reviewed and only cautiously deployed in clinical care, if at all.⏎AI in Prior Authorization and Utilization Management Processes: While the use of AI in utilization⏎management could potentially lead to quicker access to care and reduce provider and plan burden, it⏎poses numerous risks if utilized inappropriately. As such, APTA opposes any use of AI by payers to deny⏎or restrict access to, or reduce payment for, physical therapist services or other medically necessary⏎services.⏎While the Final Rule on Interoperability and PA aims to speed up PA processes and create more⏎transparency in PA criteria and denials, it does not regulate how such decisions are made, particularly⏎with or without AI. This level of transparency is exceptionally important as insurers make decisions that⏎determine if patients receive care, the type or intensity of the care, and the location of care, and when⏎information on how those decisions are made is not publicly available, patients, providers, and⏎researchers are unable to determine whether the use of AI produces better or worse outcomes.⏎This is particularly concerning given the dramatic increase in the use of AI tools by health insurers. A⏎2024 survey by the National Association of Insurance Commissioners of 93 large health insurers found⏎that 84 percent were using AI for operational purposes. More specifically, thirty-seven percent of⏎insurers report using AI for PA and 56 percent for utilization management. Yet as stated in previous⏎sections, AI tools are only as accurate as the data and algorithm inputs going into them. If used⏎appropriately and effectively, AI could automate PA and claims approval, reduce denials when there is⏎incomplete information, and assist with the complex appeals process. But if any AI tools are to be used⏎/ 8⏎⏎in PA, they need accurate clinical criteria and appropriate levels of human review, otherwise, AI may⏎only serve to exacerbate existing flaws.⏎Some insurers have found success using AI. Cohere Health has leveraged clinically trained AI to⏎automate PA requests while still maintaining 94% provider satisfaction. Cohere allows clinicians to use⏎Dragon Copilot during patient visits to instantly submit care requests and receive real-time feedback⏎from health plans. It should be noted that Cohere, following CMS’ guidelines, does not use AI alone for⏎denials; when a denial is processed, a human is always in the loop. But unfortunately, Cohere has begun⏎to deny services that the therapist provides on the same day as the initial evaluation even though PA is⏎not required by the patient’s plan. Small glitches in the system such as these can be magnified by the⏎use of AI in PA.⏎While Cohere has seen high rates of satisfaction, many plans have used AI with little to no human review⏎resulting in systemic batch denials. The most well-known case occurred within Medicare Advantage⏎where three health plans, UnitedHealthcare, CVS, and Humana, saw denial rates jump after⏎implementing AI in their PA processes. Notably, UnitedHealthcare’s system, nH predict, reported a 90%⏎error rate for post-acute care stays, leading to a lawsuit against the company. This is not surprising given⏎that in some cases, AI tools report 16 times higher rates of care denial.⏎This practice concerns both physicians—61% of whom said they fear that payers’ use of unregulated AI⏎is increasing prior authorization denials—and non-physician providers alike who are expressly concerned⏎with the degree to which PA already delays care or denies access to medically necessary services.⏎And even if PA processes are appropriately and effectively streamlined using AI, they are still a severe⏎impediment to patient’s receiving timely care. In November 2025, APTA released The Impact of⏎Administrative Burden on Physical Therapist Services, our third and most recent survey of⏎administration burden within the profession. The report found that:⏎• Wait times for prior authorization have escalated steadily over the last seven years, with 30% of⏎survey respondents waiting one to two weeks for prior authorization approval.⏎• Prior authorization requirements negatively impacted patients' clinical outcomes with 83% of⏎respondents agreeing or strongly agreeing that prior authorization has caused their patients to⏎abandon treatment.⏎• 75% of respondents said they had to hire administrative staff to keep up with the administrative⏎demands.⏎• 57% agreed or strongly agreed that administrative burden has led their practice to discontinue⏎participation with a payer or network.⏎This data is alarming and serves as a cautionary tale. CMS has already clarified that while MA plans can⏎use AI to make coverage determinations, “an algorithm that determines coverage based on a larger data⏎set instead of the individual patient’s medical history, the physician’s recommendations, or clinical⏎notes” would not be compliant with federal law protecting Medicaid and Medicare beneficiaries, yet⏎APTA is deeply concerned that AI will only exacerbate existing challenges in PA. APTA also affirms its⏎/ 9⏎⏎stance that AI should never be the sole reason for a denial and the use of AI based solely on utilization⏎statistics should never be used to deny care. To prevent this from happening, APTA encourages HHS to⏎provide the following oversight to Medicare, Medicaid, and Affordable Care Act Marketplace plans that⏎utilize AI in PA:⏎• Pre-Validation Scrutiny: In creating algorithms for AI in PA, insurers should apply scrutiny to all⏎processes, but APTA is particularly concerned about those AI tools that pose the greatest risk of⏎harm by erroneously suggesting that treatment requests are not approvable such as denying⏎care at a facility altogether. Pre-validation review should ensure that any denials are⏎automatically routed to a human for clinician review in a timely manner.⏎• Continuous Monitoring and Oversight: HHS should set standards for insurers who deploy AI in⏎PA. Specifically, we recommend HHS require insurers to scrutinize requests that are persistently⏎denied or entail prolonged delays in approval to identify commonalities and fix algorithmic⏎determinations when necessary.⏎• Human Review: Medicare Advantage plans and many state-regulated plans are required by law⏎to ensure that no request is denied without having been reviewed by a medical professional.⏎While many plans have consistently affirmed that they are compliant with this requirement,⏎APTA encourages further transparency in the approval process. Insurers should be honest about⏎when a human does or does not take part in the approval process and should provide some sort⏎of verification to this effect.⏎• Opacity in Algorithmic Determinations: Insurers have pledged to provide clearer explanations⏎of prior authorization denials, and HHS should hold them accountable. As mentioned above,⏎there are no regulations governing how decisions are made which can lead to frustration and⏎arduous appeals for the provider and patient. HHS should consider requiring more transparency⏎when AI is used to adjudicate requests.⏎As the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation launches the Wasteful and Inappropriate Service⏎Reduction Model, we encourage HHS to closely monitor the use of AI and machine learning to ensure⏎that it is appropriately paired with clinical review and that care is not unnecessarily delayed through the⏎model. We are eager to learn about the results that WISeR produces and ask that the HHS proactively⏎engage with CMMI to learn about how WISeR addresses many of the concerns discussed above.⏎APTA appreciates the opportunity to provide feedback on HHS’ Request for Information regarding⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence as part of clinical care. Should you have any⏎questions regarding our comments, please contact Rachel Miller, MPH, senior specialist in health policy⏎and payment at rachelmiller@apta.org. Thank you for your time and consideration.⏎Sincerely,⏎Aaron Bishop⏎APTA Chief, Public Affairs⏎/ 10♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0350♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Miami AI Club, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Miami AI Club♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Comments Submitted in Response to RIN 0955-AA13⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Miami AI Club (MAIC)⏎Date: [February 20th 2026]⏎Executive Summary⏎Miami AI Club (MAIC) submits these comments to support HHS's goal of accelerating safe,⏎effective, and trusted AI adoption in clinical care. Across clinical settings, the primary barriers are⏎not a lack of innovation, but uncertainty, fragmentation, and limited trust in real-world⏎deployment. AI adoption succeeds when incentives, governance, workflows, payment structures,⏎and data environments are aligned; when they are not, even technically strong tools fail to scale.⏎MAIC recommends that HHS prioritize a practical adoption agenda across regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research and development:⏎Reduce uncertainty with a predictable, risk-tiered approach to AI governance and clearer⏎￿⏎expectations for pre-deployment testing, post-deployment monitoring, and lifecycle⏎oversight.⏎Treat interoperability and data liquidity as foundational infrastructure for clinical AI, using⏎￿⏎existing authorities to align incentives and reduce fragmentation across data systems.⏎Strengthen trust infrastructure as safety infrastructure — including privacy-preserving⏎￿⏎identity assurance, patient-context integrity, and auditable human–AI attribution⏎mechanisms: privacy-preserving identity assurance, patient-context integrity, attribution of⏎human-AI decision pathways, and auditable interaction records.⏎Enable regulatory-safe pilot environments and evidence-generating pathways so health⏎￿⏎systems can test AI under defined guardrails while producing actionable real-world evidence.⏎Modernize reimbursement to reward measurable outcomes, productivity, and reduced⏎￿⏎clinician burden rather than AI utilization alone, while maintaining guardrails against low-⏎value deployment.⏎Invest in applied, implementation-focused research; representative, privacy-preserving⏎￿⏎datasets; and multi-site validation networks to accelerate shared learning and equitable⏎performance.⏎These recommendations are grounded in real-world deployment experience and reflect MAIC’s⏎role as a multi-stakeholder convening platform connecting clinicians, health system operators,⏎Page 1⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎technologists, researchers, and community leaders. The goal is not only more capable AI, but⏎more trustworthy and usable AI in real clinical environments.⏎About the Miami AI Club (MAIC)⏎The Miami AI Club (MAIC) is a not-for-profit multi-stakeholder convening platform dedicated to⏎advancing responsible, human-centered artificial intelligence across healthcare, education, and⏎public-sector systems. MAIC brings together clinicians, health system executives, researchers,⏎technologists, policymakers, investors, and patient advocates to examine real-world AI⏎deployment challenges and opportunities.⏎Through its Healthcare Task Force, MAIC focuses specifically on the safe, effective, and⏎accountable integration of AI into clinical care environments. Discussions emphasize⏎implementation science, governance frameworks, interoperability, clinician workflow integration,⏎reimbursement alignment, and public trust.⏎MAIC does not represent a single vendor or commercial interest. Rather, it serves as a neutral⏎forum for cross-sector dialogue and practical deployment insights. The recommendations in this⏎submission reflect collective observations from real-world AI implementation environments, with⏎particular attention to regulatory clarity, incentive alignment, accountability infrastructure, and⏎patient-centered outcomes.⏎Cross-Cutting Observations⏎Across clinical settings, the primary barriers to AI adoption are not a lack of innovation or technical⏎capability, but uncertainty, fragmentation, and limited trust in real-world deployment.⏎AI adoption in healthcare is multi-pronged. Successful use depends on alignment among⏎incentives, governance structures, clinical workflows, payment structures, and data⏎environments. When these elements are not aligned, even technically strong tools struggle to⏎scale.⏎Trust remains central. Clinicians and patients must be confident in how AI tools are evaluated,⏎how decisions are attributed, how data are protected, and how human oversight is maintained.⏎Clear expectations and shared frameworks can accelerate adoption more effectively than volume⏎of regulation alone.⏎Fragmentation across stakeholders, data systems, and evaluation approaches slows collective⏎learning. Policies that encourage interoperability, real-world validation, and coordination can⏎unlock safe and meaningful adoption.⏎The following responses reflect practical deployment experience and focus on enabling⏎trustworthy, outcome-driven AI in clinical care.⏎Page 2⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Regulation⏎AI is advancing more rapidly than our readiness to deploy it safely and consistently in clinical⏎settings. Numerous applications enter the market daily, yet adoption of AI in clinical care lags far⏎behind its use in administrative functions and other industries. This gap is driven less by lack of⏎innovation and more by lack of regulatory clarity, predictable guardrails, and trusted deployment⏎frameworks.⏎At present, the national approach to clinical AI governance remains fragmented and unevenly⏎understood. The absence of clear interoperability expectations, evaluation standards,⏎accountability structures, and risk-sharing mechanisms reduces confidence among clinicians and⏎health systems. Addressing these gaps can accelerate adoption while preserving safety and public⏎trust.⏎As members of the Miami AI Club (MAIC), we regularly convene organizations that are building,⏎buying, evaluating, investing in, and using AI tools in clinical environments. The recommendations⏎below reflect practical, real-world deployment experience:⏎1. Move Toward a Uniform, Predictable Regulatory Framework⏎A patchwork of federal guidance and emerging state-level rules creates uncertainty for innovators⏎and implementers. Greater national alignment and clarity would reduce hesitation and encourage⏎responsible investment. A predictable, risk-tiered framework proportionate to clinical impact⏎would allow stakeholders to understand expectations while preserving flexibility for innovation.⏎Clarity often accelerates safe adoption more than additional regulation.⏎2. Advance Interoperability as Foundational Infrastructure⏎Clinical data remain fragmented across EHRs, claims, labs, pharmacies, genomics platforms, and⏎consumer sources. AI performance and safety depend heavily on data completeness and context.⏎Encouraging enforceable interoperability standards and aligned incentives would unlock higher-⏎quality AI development and more equitable performance across populations. Interoperability is⏎not only a technical issue but also an incentive and coordination issue.⏎3. Support Shared Evaluation and Risk Frameworks⏎Vendors and health systems lack common frameworks for assessing safety, performance, and risk.⏎This creates uncertainty for decision-makers and slows vendor selection and deployment. Existing⏎industry efforts, such as those from the Coalition for Health AI (CHAI) and others, provide useful⏎starting points. HHS could recognize and build upon such work to encourage shared evaluation⏎approaches without mandating a single methodology. Clear expectations for pre-deployment⏎testing, post-deployment monitoring, and lifecycle oversight would foster trust while allowing⏎adaptive AI systems to improve over time.⏎4. Encourage Development of AI Risk-Sharing and Insurance Models⏎Page 3⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎AI is probabilistic by nature. Risk can be reduced but not eliminated. Today, many health systems⏎remain cautious because liability and insurance models for AI-related risk are immature.⏎Encouraging dialogue with insurers and exploring incentive structures for AI risk underwriting⏎could reduce adoption barriers. Clearer accountability and risk-sharing frameworks would⏎support responsible deployment.⏎5. Promote Data Transparency⏎Bias and uneven performance remain legitimate concerns. Transparency around training and⏎testing data, including demographics, geography, and clinical context, helps clinicians assess⏎applicability and limitations. Emerging 'nutrition label' style disclosures are a promising approach.⏎HHS could encourage transparency norms that support equity and informed decision-making⏎without exposing proprietary information.⏎6. Strengthen Trust Infrastructure: Identity, Authentication, and Accountability⏎Regulation often focuses on data and algorithms but less on the infrastructure surrounding AI use.⏎Clinical AI increasingly influences decisions, documentation, and workflows. Yet there are no⏎consistent expectations for verifying clinician identity during AI use; ensuring correct patient-⏎context matching; tracking who accepted or modified AI recommendations; and maintaining⏎reliable audit trails of human-AI interactions. Without strong identity, authentication, and⏎accountability mechanisms, liability frameworks remain difficult to operationalize. Privacy-⏎preserving identity assurance and auditable human-AI interaction records should be viewed as⏎safety infrastructure, not optional features.⏎7. Enable Regulatory-Safe Pilot Environments⏎Innovation is slowed by uncertainty around acceptable experimentation. Regulatory-safe pilot⏎environments could allow health systems to test AI tools under defined guardrails, enabling real-⏎world learning while managing risk. Such pilots can surface risks earlier, generate practical⏎evidence, inform future rulemaking, and encourage responsible innovation.⏎8. Play a Convening Role to Reduce Fragmentation⏎A major barrier to adoption is fragmentation among stakeholders. Hospitals, developers,⏎researchers, and policymakers often operate in silos, limiting shared learning. HHS is well⏎positioned to serve as a convener to align stakeholders, share deployment lessons, and highlight⏎best practices without adding regulatory burden. Improved coordination can accelerate adoption⏎as much as formal regulation.⏎Summary: Effective clinical AI regulation should emphasize clarity, predictability, proportional⏎oversight, and trust-building infrastructure. Aligning incentives, supporting interoperability, and⏎encouraging shared learning can accelerate safe adoption while maintaining public confidence.⏎Page 4⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Reimbursement⏎HHS payment policies strongly shape how care is delivered and which innovations are adopted.⏎While AI has the potential to enhance productivity, reduce burden, and improve outcomes,⏎current reimbursement structures often do not reward these benefits.⏎Traditional fee-for-service (FFS) models emphasize volume over outcomes and can inadvertently⏎discourage adoption of technologies that improve efficiency or reduce unnecessary services. As a⏎result, even high-value AI tools may struggle to achieve sustainable adoption. Aligning payment⏎policy with measurable clinical and operational value can accelerate responsible AI use in clinical⏎care.⏎1. Align Reimbursement With Outcomes and Productivity⏎AI should not be reimbursed simply for being used. It should be rewarded when it demonstrably⏎improves patient outcomes, reduces avoidable utilization, enhances diagnostic accuracy, enables⏎earlier interventions, reduces clinician administrative burden, or improves care coordination.⏎Outcome-aligned payment and value-based models are better suited to capture AI’s benefits than⏎FFS structures.⏎2. Recognize Clinician Time and Workflow Impact⏎Many AI tools require clinician supervision, prompting, and validation. These activities carry real⏎opportunity costs. If AI increases clinician workload or cognitive burden, adoption will remain⏎limited regardless of technical performance. Payment models that recognize workflow efficiency⏎and clinician time savings can better reflect the true value of AI-enabled care.⏎3. Encourage Evidence-Generating Reimbursement Models⏎AI systems improve over time and may require real-world learning. Traditional reimbursement⏎models often expect mature evidence upfront. HHS could explore coverage-with-evidence-⏎development approaches, pilot payment models, or time-limited reimbursement pathways that⏎allow responsible testing and validation in real-world settings.⏎4. Reward System-Level Impact, Not Just Tool Adoption⏎The greatest value from AI often occurs at the system level rather than the individual encounter.⏎Examples include reduced readmissions, improved population health management, earlier⏎disease detection, more efficient resource allocation, better medication adherence, and⏎improved equity and access. Payment models that reward system-level outcomes can better⏎capture AI’s value than encounter-based reimbursement.⏎5. Support Physician-Led Evidence and Clinical Validation⏎Physician-led evidence and peer-reviewed validation strongly influence adoption. Encouraging⏎clinical leadership in AI evaluation and deployment increases trust and accelerates uptake.⏎Page 5⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Payment policies that support evidence generation and dissemination can help scale effective⏎tools.⏎6. Maintain Guardrails Against Overuse and 'AI for AI’s Sake'⏎Reimbursement policy should avoid incentivizing unnecessary AI deployment or low-value use.⏎Clear expectations for demonstrated value, safety, and equity can prevent waste while supporting⏎meaningful innovation.⏎Summary: Modernized payment policy can be a powerful lever for responsible AI adoption.⏎Aligning reimbursement with outcomes, productivity, and workflow improvement rather than⏎mere utilization will encourage deployment of AI that benefits patients, clinicians, and the⏎healthcare system.⏎Research and Development⏎HHS supports one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems and plays a critical role in⏎translating innovation into clinical impact. In AI, the challenge is not only invention but also safe,⏎scalable implementation.⏎Effective R&D policy should focus on enabling applied research, real-world validation, and shared⏎learning that accelerates responsible adoption across the healthcare system.⏎1. Prioritize Applied and Implementation-Focused Research⏎Many AI tools show promise in controlled settings but face challenges in clinical workflows.⏎Greater emphasis on implementation science, workflow integration research, human-AI⏎collaboration models, and deployment in diverse clinical environments can help close the gap⏎between prototype and practice.⏎2. Invest in High-Quality, Privacy-Preserving Datasets⏎Standardized, well-governed datasets are foundational for safe and equitable AI. HHS can support⏎creation of representative, high-quality datasets; privacy-preserving data-sharing frameworks;⏎de-identification best practices; mechanisms for patient consent and transparency; and inclusion⏎of diverse populations to reduce bias.⏎3. Support Real-World Testing and Pilot Networks⏎Safe real-world testing environments are critical for learning. HHS should encourage multi-site⏎pilot programs, structured real-world evaluations, post-deployment monitoring networks, and⏎cross-institution learning collaboratives.⏎4. Encourage Public-Private and Multi-Stakeholder Collaboration⏎Page 6⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎AI progress depends on coordination among health systems, researchers, developers, patient⏎communities, and standards organizations. HHS can act as a facilitator of collaboration without⏎directing specific outcomes.⏎5. Consider a Lightweight National Coordination Forum⏎A recurring barrier in U.S. healthcare AI is fragmentation across stakeholders. HHS should consider⏎encouraging a lightweight, multi-stakeholder coordination forum focused on practical⏎deployment and shared learning to share real-world lessons, surface safety and implementation⏎challenges early, identify emerging best practices, and encourage alignment around evaluation⏎approaches.⏎6. Incorporate International Lessons⏎Global experience suggests that clear policy direction reduces hesitation; coordinated ecosystems⏎accelerate learning; safe pilot environments enable responsible experimentation; data liquidity⏎supports innovation; and outcome-focused deployment encourages pragmatism.⏎Summary: R&D policy is most impactful when it supports shared infrastructure, applied research,⏎and real-world validation. By enabling collaboration, data access, and safe pilots, HHS can catalyze⏎innovation without needing to select specific technologies or vendors.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The primary barriers to private sector innovation and adoption of AI in clinical care are not a lack⏎of technical capability, but uncertainty, fragmentation, and limited trust in real-world deployment⏎environments. Key barriers include:⏎Regulatory Fragmentation and Uncertainty⏎A patchwork of federal guidance and emerging state-level regulations creates uncertainty for⏎developers and health systems. Predictable, risk-tiered regulatory clarity would reduce hesitation⏎and enable responsible innovation.⏎Lack of Shared Risk, Evaluation, and Monitoring Standards⏎There are no commonly accepted frameworks for risk assessment and mitigation, ongoing⏎performance monitoring, real-world validation, and lifecycle oversight of adaptive AI systems.⏎Without shared standards, vendors struggle to demonstrate safety and health systems struggle⏎to evaluate it.⏎Immature Liability and Insurance Models⏎Page 7⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Liability and accountability remain unresolved when AI contributes to clinical decisions, and AI-⏎specific insurance and risk-sharing models are underdeveloped. This leads to understandable risk⏎aversion among providers.⏎Economic and Workflow Realities in Clinical Settings⏎For AI to be adopted, it must fit clinical workflow and demonstrate real value. Many tools require⏎clinician supervision, prompting, and validation; if AI increases cognitive or administrative burden,⏎adoption declines.⏎Data Fragmentation and Interoperability Gaps⏎Clinical data remain siloed across EHRs, claims systems, labs, pharmacies, and consumer sources.⏎Limited interoperability restricts model development, validation, and equitable performance⏎across populations.⏎Trust, Identity, and Accountability Infrastructure Gaps⏎Clinical AI increasingly influences decisions and documentation, yet there are few consistent⏎standards for verifying clinician identity during AI use, ensuring correct patient-context matching,⏎tracking acceptance or modification of AI outputs, and maintaining reliable audit trails.⏎Privacy and Security Concerns⏎AI can increase data flows and access points, raising concerns about data misuse, unauthorized⏎access, and centralization risks. Providers and patients need confidence that AI adoption will not⏎increase vulnerability.⏎Summary: The biggest barriers to AI adoption in clinical care are uncertainty, fragmentation, and⏎trust gaps, not a lack of innovation. Addressing regulatory clarity, interoperability, accountability⏎frameworks, and workflow realities would significantly accelerate responsible adoption.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎To incentivize effective AI use in clinical care, HHS priorities should focus on clarity, proportional⏎oversight, and alignment of incentives with real-world value. The following areas would have the⏎greatest impact:⏎Regulatory Priorities: Clarity, Risk-Tiering, and Accountability⏎HHS could prioritize risk-tiered regulatory approaches aligned with clinical impact; clear⏎expectations for pre- and post-deployment monitoring; lifecycle oversight for adaptive AI⏎Page 8⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎systems; and guidance for non-device clinical AI tools. Existing HIPAA authorities (45 CFR Parts⏎160 and 164) could be revisited to provide clearer guidance on secondary data use, model training⏎and inference, and data minimization. ONC authorities related to interoperability and information⏎blocking (e.g., 45 CFR Part 171) may also be revisited to strengthen data liquidity for clinical AI⏎while preserving trust.⏎Strengthen Accountability and Attribution Frameworks⏎HHS should encourage frameworks that support reliable attribution of human-AI decision⏎pathways, auditability of AI-assisted clinical decisions, and documentation standards for AI-⏎supported care.⏎Payment Policy Priorities: Reward Outcomes, Not AI Usage⏎Reimbursement should reward the value AI produces, including measurable improvements in⏎quality, safety, efficiency, and reductions in clinician burden. Expanding outcome-aligned and⏎value-based payment models will better capture AI’s benefits than fee-for-service.⏎Evidence-Generating and Pilot Payment Models⏎HHS could explore coverage-with-evidence-development approaches, time-limited pilot⏎reimbursement models, and structured pathways for real-world evidence generation.⏎Programmatic Design: Support Interoperability and Data Liquidity⏎HHS could prioritize enforceable interoperability standards, alignment of incentives for data⏎sharing, guidance on de-identification and privacy-preserving data use, and encouraging⏎representative datasets to reduce bias.⏎Support Safe Innovation Environments⏎Programmatic support for multi-site pilots, structured real-world evaluations, and cross-⏎institution learning networks would allow innovation and safety to evolve together.⏎Summary: The most impactful changes are those that reduce uncertainty, align incentives with⏎outcomes, and support real-world learning. Clear guidance, proportional oversight, and⏎modernized payment models can accelerate responsible AI adoption while maintaining safety and⏎trust.⏎Page 9⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎The use of AI in clinical care introduces legal and implementation questions that existing⏎governance frameworks were not designed to fully address. These issues benefit from clearer⏎guidance and updated interpretation in AI-specific contexts. Key issues include:⏎Attribution and Liability in AI-Assisted Decisions⏎Responsibility can become ambiguous when AI contributes to clinical decision-making. Clear⏎expectations for documentation, human oversight, and attribution can help clarify accountability⏎without removing clinician judgment.⏎Governance of Adaptive and Continuously Learning Systems⏎Continuously learning systems raise questions about when revalidation is required, how drift is⏎monitored, and how updates are governed and documented.⏎Transparency and Representativeness of Training and Testing Data⏎Performance varies across populations and settings. Encouraging transparent disclosure of data⏎characteristics and limitations supports informed use and reduces unintended disparities.⏎Movement of Data Between HIPAA and Non-HIPAA Contexts⏎AI can involve data flows between covered entities and consumer technologies, creating⏎ambiguity around secondary use and responsibilities for safeguarding data.⏎Identity, Authentication, and Auditability⏎Implementation requires reliable attribution of actions, correct patient context, and audit trails⏎of human-AI interactions. HHS can encourage standards that strengthen attribution and⏎auditability.⏎Misrepresentation of AI Capabilities⏎Overstating AI performance can create legal and safety risks; evidence-based claims and⏎transparency around limitations protect patients and providers.⏎Summary: Most legal and implementation challenges stem from ambiguity rather than absence⏎of law. Clearer guidance on attribution, data governance, lifecycle oversight, and transparency⏎can enable safe adoption while maintaining accountability and public trust.⏎Page 10⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further⏎support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g.,⏎contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Effective evaluation of clinical AI requires both technical validation and real-world assessment.⏎Model performance alone is insufficient; evaluation must reflect how AI functions in clinical⏎environments and how humans interact with it. Promising approaches include:⏎Risk-Based Evaluation Frameworks⏎Evaluation should be proportional to clinical impact and potential harm, incorporating patient⏎safety impact, consequences of failure, population vulnerability, environment of use, and equity⏎considerations. HHS can encourage convergence around shared principles without mandating a⏎single framework.⏎Human-in-the-Loop and Workflow Evaluation⏎Evaluation should assess cognitive load, alert fatigue, usability and workflow integration,⏎supervision burden, and ability for timely human intervention.⏎Real-World Performance Monitoring⏎Ongoing evaluation should include post-deployment monitoring, drift detection, periodic⏎revalidation, and performance across diverse populations and settings.⏎Transparency in Training and Testing Data⏎Evaluation should consider data representativeness, demographic coverage, geographic diversity,⏎and clinical context of testing.⏎Auditability and Traceability⏎Frameworks should include the ability to trace how outputs were generated, review decision⏎pathways, and maintain records of human-AI interaction.⏎HHS Support: Yes. Impactful mechanisms include targeted grants for evaluation research,⏎cooperative agreements supporting multi-site validation, prize challenges encouraging robust⏎testing methods, and support for post-deployment learning networks.⏎Summary: The most effective evaluation approaches combine technical performance, human⏎factors, and real-world monitoring. Supporting shared evaluation principles and real-world⏎learning can accelerate safe adoption while maintaining flexibility for innovation.⏎Page 11⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can enable private sector validation and accountability while avoiding overly prescriptive⏎certification structures that could slow innovation. The most effective support would focus on⏎clarity, alignment, and shared learning rather than centralized control:⏎Encourage Voluntary, Industry-Led Standards⏎Recognize credible industry-led frameworks, encourage transparency and comparability, and⏎highlight best practices.⏎Support Independent Real-World Evaluation⏎Encourage third-party testing and benchmarking, multi-site validation studies, and real-world⏎performance reporting.⏎Promote Transparency and Disclosure Norms⏎Encourage standardized disclosures around intended use, data representativeness, known⏎limitations, and appropriate supervision levels.⏎Integrate AI Competencies Into Clinical Training⏎Encourage AI literacy and competency development among clinicians and administrators to⏎improve safe use and informed procurement.⏎Reduce Structural Barriers to Responsible Innovation⏎Promote interoperability, clarify data governance expectations, support risk-tiered oversight, and⏎encourage evidence generation.⏎Summary: The most effective role for HHS is to enable trust, transparency, and shared learning⏎while allowing market-driven validation to evolve. Light-touch support for independent⏎evaluation and disclosure norms can promote safe innovation without unnecessary regulatory⏎burden.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give⏎new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI has demonstrated meaningful value in several areas of clinical care, particularly where tasks⏎are data-rich, pattern-based, and augment human expertise. At the same time, some applications⏎have fallen short when expectations exceeded current technical or workflow realities.⏎Page 12⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Areas Where AI Has Met or Exceeded Expectations⏎Imaging and early detection (radiology, pathology, ophthalmology) where AI augments⏎￿⏎clinician interpretation.⏎Precision medicine and oncology, particularly for navigating complex genomic and clinical⏎￿⏎data in multidisciplinary settings.⏎Drug discovery and clinical research, accelerating early-stage target identification and trial⏎￿⏎design.⏎Population health and risk stratification, supporting preventive interventions and resource⏎￿⏎allocation.⏎Areas Where AI Has Fallen Short⏎Workflow misalignment: tools that disrupt routines or increase documentation burden.⏎￿⏎Overgeneralization across populations: models that do not generalize well beyond training⏎￿⏎settings.⏎Unrealistic expectations: marketing claims outpacing evidence.⏎￿⏎Insufficient real-world validation: performance drops in variable real-world environments.⏎￿⏎Novel AI Tools With High Potential⏎Clinician burden reduction tools (documentation, chart review, administrative automation).⏎￿⏎Early disease detection and preventive care tools.⏎￿⏎Care coordination and decision support that synthesizes data across sources.⏎￿⏎Equity-focused AI that identifies disparities and targets interventions.⏎￿⏎Summary: AI shows the greatest value when it augments clinicians, fits workflows, and is⏎validated in real-world settings. Its potential to improve outcomes and reduce costs is substantial,⏎but success depends on thoughtful implementation, transparency, and realistic expectations.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care?⏎What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Adoption of AI in clinical care is shaped by a combination of executive leadership, clinical⏎leadership, and operational governance. Successful implementation typically requires alignment⏎across these groups rather than reliance on a single decision-maker.⏎Decision Makers With the Greatest Influence⏎Page 13⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Executive leadership (CEO/COO/CFO) for strategy, financial priorities, and scaling.⏎￿⏎Clinical leadership (CMO, department chairs, physician champions) for trust and clinical⏎￿⏎validation.⏎IT and digital leadership (CIO/CMIO/CDO) for integration, cybersecurity, and data governance.⏎￿⏎Compliance and legal teams for privacy, security, and regulatory considerations.⏎￿⏎AI/digital governance committees for evaluation, risk management, and prioritization.⏎￿⏎Primary Administrative Hurdles⏎Unclear value proposition and difficulty separating strategic AI from experimentation.⏎￿⏎Limited clinician engagement in evaluation and design.⏎￿⏎Workflow disruption without clear benefit.⏎￿⏎Lack of change management, training, and realistic expectations.⏎￿⏎Uncertainty around internal governance and lifecycle oversight.⏎￿⏎Data quality and integration challenges.⏎￿⏎Summary: AI adoption is most successful when it is clinically relevant, strategically aligned, and⏎supported by cross-functional governance. Addressing administrative and organizational realities⏎is as important as technical performance.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider⏎specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability accelerates safe and effective AI development by improving data⏎completeness, context, and representativeness. AI systems perform best when they can learn⏎from and operate across diverse, longitudinal data sources rather than isolated datasets.⏎Longitudinal, Cross-Setting Clinical Data⏎Interoperability between EHR systems, claims data, pharmacy systems, laboratory systems,⏎imaging systems, and post-acute and home care records supports better prediction, care⏎coordination, and preventive care.⏎Integration of Clinical and Social Context⏎Combining clinical data with social determinants of health, behavioral data, and community-level⏎indicators can improve risk stratification and targeted interventions, with strong governance and⏎transparency.⏎Standardized, Structured Clinical Data⏎Page 14⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Wider adoption of standards such as FHIR, standardized terminologies, and consistent metadata⏎practices improves data usability and comparability.⏎De-Identification and Privacy-Preserving Linkage⏎Guidance and tools for reliable de-identification, privacy-preserving record linkage, and patient-⏎consented data use support research while protecting privacy.⏎Benchmarking and Evaluation Datasets⏎Shared benchmarking datasets and evaluation frameworks enable fair comparison and⏎transparency; multi-institution datasets reflecting diverse populations reduce bias and improve⏎generalizability.⏎Interoperability as Incentive Alignment⏎Interoperability is also an incentive and governance issue. Aligning incentives to support data⏎sharing and standardization can accelerate progress more effectively than technical mandates⏎alone.⏎Summary: Interoperability widens opportunities when it enables longitudinal, contextual, and⏎representative data use while preserving privacy. Progress depends on standards adoption,⏎incentive alignment, and trusted governance frameworks.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Patients and caregivers generally care less about AI itself and more about whether care becomes⏎more timely, accurate, affordable, and humane. AI is viewed as beneficial when it clearly improves⏎care delivery and concerning when it feels opaque or depersonalizing.⏎Challenges Patients and Caregivers Want Addressed⏎Earlier and more accurate diagnosis.⏎￿⏎Personalized and coordinated care across providers and settings.⏎￿⏎Reduced administrative burden and access barriers (wait times, navigation, scheduling).⏎￿⏎Preventive and proactive care that supports early intervention.⏎￿⏎Health equity: systems that work across diverse populations without reinforcing disparities.⏎￿⏎Patient and Caregiver Concerns⏎Trust and transparency about when and how AI is used.⏎￿⏎Page 15⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎Privacy and data security, especially in light of high-profile breaches.⏎￿⏎Loss of human connection: AI should support, not replace, clinician empathy and judgment.⏎￿⏎Bias and fairness across demographic groups.⏎￿⏎Accountability if an AI-supported decision is wrong.⏎￿⏎Assurance of human oversight and ability to override AI recommendations.⏎￿⏎Summary: Patients and caregivers support AI when it improves access, accuracy, and coordination⏎while preserving privacy, human oversight, and transparency. Trust grows when AI supports, not⏎replaces, clinician judgment.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care? (a) Are there published findings about⏎the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in clinical care? (b) How does the⏎literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical⏎care?⏎To accelerate safe and effective adoption, research priorities should emphasize real-world impact,⏎human-AI collaboration, and system-level value rather than model performance alone.⏎Priority Areas for AI Research⏎Implementation and workflow research (human-AI collaboration, cognitive load, supervision⏎￿⏎models, adoption).⏎Real-world effectiveness and generalizability (multi-site validation, diverse populations,⏎￿⏎longitudinal monitoring, drift and recalibration).⏎Equity and bias mitigation (representative datasets, fairness methods, disparity reduction).⏎￿⏎Privacy-preserving and secure AI (federated learning, privacy-preserving analytics, secure⏎￿⏎data sharing).⏎AI for administrative burden reduction (documentation and workload reduction).⏎￿⏎Published Findings on AI in Clinical Care⏎The literature shows promising results in imaging and diagnostics, oncology and genomics, risk⏎prediction and population health, and clinical decision support. However, many studies are⏎retrospective or limited in scale; more prospective real-world evidence is needed.⏎Costs, Benefits, and Transfers⏎Literature suggests AI can improve efficiency and support earlier intervention, but integration⏎costs can be significant. Benefits often accrue at system or population levels rather than individual⏎Page 16⏎⏎Response to RIN 0955-AA13 from Miami AI Club⏎encounters, and value depends heavily on workflow integration and governance. Organizational⏎readiness and implementation quality strongly influence ROI.⏎Summary: Research that links technical performance to real-world clinical value, safety, and⏎equity will most effectively accelerate adoption. Prioritizing implementation science and real-⏎world validation can help translate AI’s promise into measurable health outcomes.⏎Closing⏎MAIC appreciates the opportunity to provide input. The policy levers most likely to accelerate safe⏎adoption are those that reduce uncertainty, strengthen interoperability and trust infrastructure,⏎align incentives with outcomes, and enable real-world learning. We welcome continued⏎engagement to support shared learning and responsible deployment.⏎As AI adoption accelerates, establishing durable trust infrastructure will be as important as⏎advancing model capability.⏎Through its Healthcare Task Force, MAIC is continuing structured dialogue and implementation-⏎focused collaboration to translate policy guidance into practical adoption frameworks. We⏎welcome continued engagement with HHS and other stakeholders to support safe, accountable,⏎and scalable AI deployment.⏎Submitted By⏎Miami AI Club (MAIC) Healthcare Task Force: Contributors include clinical leaders, healthcare⏎system strategists, and technology governance advisors with experience deploying AI in real-⏎world care environments.⏎Task Force Leadership⏎Nima Schei, MD — Founder, Healthcare AI Trust & Adoption Initiative⏎Mandeep Maini — Healthcare AI Consultant⏎Contributors⏎Noel Guillama — Healthcare Operations Advisor⏎Brian Engelbert — Healthcare Data Contributor⏎Page 17♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0351♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Health Level Seven (HL7) International, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Health Level Seven (HL7) International♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector Artificial Intelligence (AI) RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted electronically to: http://www.regulations.gov⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Health Level Seven (HL7) International welcomes the opportunity to provide feedback on the⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI and what HHS can do to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as⏎part of clinical care. This letter includes perspectives from our leadership and Policy Advisory⏎Committee.⏎Overall, HL7 encourages ASTP/ONC to actively engage our organization as its plan to⏎accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care is constructed and deployed. HL7⏎is a rich resource with:⏎(1) Leadership steeped in AI, including our CEO Rachel Dunscombe and dedicated Chief AI⏎Officer Dr. Daniel Vreeman, DPT;⏎(2) Recent and relevant legacy HL7 AI Taskforce and Initiative work;⏎⏎(3) AI public-private sector policy convenings, such as HL7 policy conference on healthcare⏎interoperability, genomics and AI. HL7 also has critical Work Groups and Accelerators⏎focused on clinical care issues; and⏎(4) World-leading and globally relevant expertise regarding standards that incorporate⏎appropriate rules and usage of AI.⏎HL7 is the global authority on healthcare interoperability and a critical leader developing open⏎standards that drive digital health innovation. As such, we stand ready to support ASTP/ONC in⏎better understanding the promises, complexities and challenges at the crossroads of standards,⏎interoperability, clinical care, policy and AI.⏎As a Standards Development Organization (SDO) with broad-ranging public-private sector⏎membership, HL7 has a front row seat to many complexities and more serious issues emerging⏎with AI use in healthcare and clinical care that we wish to highlight for HHS in response to this⏎RFI such as:⏎• Currently, both health information technology systems and users do not always know what⏎data was generated or modified by an AI tool or what tool was used-- therefore the data can⏎appear falsely, as if it was generated only by a clinical user.⏎• AI models can generate actions around care delivery that are incorrect (like inaccurate⏎categorization of cardiac or cancer risk). An optimal way to identify those mistakes within⏎medical records and correct them must be identified. This kind of metadata is also key for⏎safety.⏎• There are not requirements or transparency around the monitoring of known AI issues like⏎model degeneration and hallucination (confident but false, fabricated or nonsensical⏎information generated by AI models).⏎Specific to the effort to further understand what HHS can do to accelerate the adoption and⏎use of AI as part of clinical care; we highlight the following expert HL7 resources:⏎HL7 Artificial Intelligence (AI) Office⏎HL7 has an AI Office focused on establishing foundational standards for safe, trustworthy and⏎⏎scalable AI in healthcare and convening the global community driving this transformation. The⏎new office keeps HL7 at the forefront of healthcare's AI revolution, creating frameworks that⏎ensure emerging technologies are trusted, explainable, interoperable and scalable across⏎clinical, operational and research settings worldwide. It also ensures that healthcare⏎interoperability and AI have a productive and symbiotic relationship, maximizing the promise of⏎interoperability for better care, while continuing and expanding healthcare efficiencies. These⏎are significant. The Organization for Economic Co-operation and Development estimates that⏎the value of enabling interoperable sharing of health data is between 2.70% to 6.64% of health⏎system expenditure and may be as high as 17%.⏎HL7 serves as the trusted global convener for responsible, standards-driven AI innovation—⏎ensuring these transformative technologies deliver on their promise to improve health for all.⏎Leading this initiative, HL7 has appointed Daniel Vreeman, DPT, as its first Chief AI Officer⏎(CAIO). A globally recognized biomedical informatics expert, Dr. Vreeman expanded his role as⏎Chief Standards Development Officer to drive HL7's comprehensive AI strategy, including the⏎HL7 AI Challenge, anti-fraud initiatives, and collaborations with regulators and industry⏎partners globally. In our newest AI partnership, HL7 has become a founding member of the⏎Vigilant AI Monitoring and Operations System (VAMOS) Collaborative --a consortium of leading⏎academic medical centers and industry partners focused on AI governance and⏎algorithmovigilance --through open standards and platforms.⏎In January 2026, HL7 conducted the first ballot of a new specification: the AI Transparency on⏎FHIR Implementation Guide. This specification will provide crucial implementation guidance for⏎the key aspects of documenting when FHIR resources have been created, updated, or⏎influenced by AI. This type of data-level transparency-- the practice of openly disclosing how AI⏎systems work, including their data sources, logic and decision-making processes--is essential⏎for establishing trustworthy AI systems.⏎HL7 AI Taskforce⏎HL7 had previously convened a dedicated AI Initiative and Taskforce. We worked in⏎collaboration with multiple partners -- in both the public and private sectors -- to develop white⏎papers and guidance to ensure the reliability, safety and security of AI. HL7 is happy to share⏎important lessons learned in these efforts. A May 2025 report specific to AI, healthcare,⏎payment integrity and fraud can be found at:⏎⏎https://info.hl7.org/ai-report⏎HL7 Work Groups, Accelerators and Other Efforts⏎HL7 Work Groups, FHIR Accelerators and special initiatives are conducting cutting-edge⏎healthcare, interoperability and standards work very relevant to the HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎and accelerating the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care. A few cross-cutting topics⏎these groups address with direct relevance for AI use cases include: data quality, provenance,⏎consent, metadata labels and integrating external decision support applications into clinical⏎workflows.⏎HL7 executive and policy leaders can facilitate structured, focused dialogue with HHS and⏎these HL7 groups that have practical and field-leading expertise. Full listings for our HL7 Work⏎Groups, Accelerators and information on our Policy Advisory Committee can be accessed at:⏎http://www.hl7.org/Special/committees/index.cfm?ref=nav⏎https://www.hl7.org/about/fhir-accelerator/⏎https://confluence.hl7.org/spaces/PAC/pages/76159333/Policy+Advisory+Committee+Home⏎Specific Comments⏎HL7’s open, consensus-based standards serve as the essential digital foundation that enables⏎innovative companies and startups to securely access, analyze and derive insights from⏎healthcare data, accelerating the development of AI-powered solutions that can dramatically⏎improve patient outcomes while reducing costs across the U.S. healthcare system. By⏎supporting HL7 through the HHS AI and related initiatives, the federal government can unlock⏎unprecedented market-driven innovation through ensuring standardized, interoperable health⏎data exchange that allows new entrants and established players to compete based on the value⏎they deliver, rather than being blocked by proprietary data silos. A strategic investment in HL7⏎standards would catalyze a new wave of American healthcare entrepreneurship, enabling data-⏎driven and AI-backed solutions to flourish in a competitive marketplace that rewards improved⏎health outcomes, ultimately making healthcare more accessible, efficient and effective for all⏎Americans. These comments relate to the general RFI request for information and RFI question⏎8, Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care?⏎⏎Regarding insight on high priority policy items as articulated in RFI question 2, HL7⏎recommends that the federal government should adopt voluntary consensus healthcare⏎standards broadly in regulatory and sub-regulatory policies that govern the use of AI. This⏎includes standards for creating, deploying, operating, measuring, reporting and ensuring the⏎needed transparency, safety and privacy in use of AI. Adopting voluntary consensus standards⏎that comply with the World Trade Organization’s Agreement on Technical Barriers to Trade,⏎such as American National Standards Institute (ANSI)-accredited American National Standards,⏎is the optimal way for regulation to support the competitiveness of United States innovation in⏎AI. HL7 emphasizes that the best organization to take on an AI standards effort should be a⏎broad based and ANSI-accredited one. Our organization is immediately ready and willing to⏎provide the necessary and critical AI leadership HHS needs at this time. HL7 has flagship AI⏎initiatives underway and is leading dialogue and progress on the four key objectives in ANSI’s⏎The United States Standards Strategy 2025 as they relate to AI which are to:⏎1. Strengthen U.S. commitment to international standards by leading strategic collaboration⏎and defending against geopolitical manipulation;⏎2. Demonstrate standards’ broad value by expanding education about how standards drive⏎innovation, safety and market access;⏎3. Make standards development more efficient by embracing process innovation and⏎training the next generation of standards leaders; and⏎4. Address opportunities and challenges head-on through active participation in developing⏎emerging technology standards and sustained investment in the standards ecosystem.⏎HL7 also notes that the need for AI transparency is paramount because clinical care operates⏎in a high-stakes, regulated, trust-dependent environment where opaque decision-making can⏎directly affect patient safety, equity and legal accountability.⏎Lastly, HL7 supports federal preemption of conflicting state laws to prevent undue burden and⏎ensure consistent application and regulation of AI. When states impose conflicting standards,⏎measures and other requirements, then significant burdens can prevent achieving the potential⏎benefits of AI. Such pre-emption is highlighted in documents such as the December 2025⏎Executive Order Ensuring a National Policy Framework for Artificial Intelligence.⏎⏎Should you have any questions about our attached comments, please contact Rachel⏎Dunscombe, Chief Executive Officer of Health Level Seven International at ceo@HL7.org. We⏎look forward to continuing this discussion and offer our assistance to you on this topic that is⏎critical to the future of our nation’s healthcare.⏎Sincerely,⏎Rachel Dunscombe Caroline Macumber⏎Chief Executive Officer Board of Directors, Chair⏎HL7 International HL7 International♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0352♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Margaret♔Perez♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔Association of American Indian Physicians♔♔♔♔♔♔Association of American Indian Physicians⏎Comments Submitted to HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎RE: Health Sector AI Request for Information — Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Margaret Perez, Executive Director⏎www.aaip.org | mperez@aaip.org⏎⏎---⏎⏎The framing of this RFI troubles me. "Accelerating adoption" is the premise, and then the questions flow from there. But for the communities we serve — American Indian and Alaska Native patients, many of whom are already navigating Indian Health Service facilities that are underfunded, understaffed, and geographically isolated — the question of how fast to move is not a technocratic one. It is a question about who gets hurt when the speed of deployment outpaces the development of any meaningful accountability structure.⏎⏎I want to focus specifically on one area where I think HHS has both an existing regulatory obligation and a documented failure to act: AI-driven prior authorization and claims denial systems used by insurers, including Medicare Advantage plans that are federal contracts, not private arrangements. This is where I keep coming back when I read through the RFI questions about barriers and opportunities.⏎⏎What we are seeing, and what our member physicians are reporting to us, is not a future risk. It is happening now. AI systems are being used to deny medically necessary care at a scale that no human review team could match, and that is precisely the point — the volume is the product. A system that can issue 50,000 denials in the time it would take a human reviewer to process 200 is not an efficiency tool in any neutral sense. It is a mechanism for making the denial of care the default outcome for people who do not have the time, English fluency, health literacy, or frankly the energy to fight back.⏎⏎Our member physicians who treat Native patients in rural Missouri and across the plains states tell us that appeals processes are written as if the patient has a lawyer on retainer and unlimited working hours. I don't think that's an accident.⏎⏎In October 2023, the Senate Finance Committee released findings showing that certain Medicare Advantage plans were using algorithmic tools to deny post-acute care claims at rates far exceeding what physician reviewers would have approved. One plan's denial rate for skilled nursing facility stays was something like 85 percent higher after the AI tool was implemented than it had been in the prior period. Those are not abstractions. Those are elderly patients, many of them Native elders who have survived generations of inadequate federal health care, being sent home before they are medically ready because an algorithm said so and nobody with a medical license ever looked at the file.⏎⏎HHS has authority here. CMS has existing tools under Medicare Advantage regulations that could require, right now, that any adverse coverage determination generated by or with the assistance of an automated system receive independent physician review before it is final. That is not a new regulatory theory. It is a narrower version of requirements that already exist in some contexts. What is missing is the will to enforce it or extend it.⏎⏎I want to be honest: I do think AI can do good things in clinical care. Diagnostic support tools that help an IHS physician in a clinic with no specialist backup — yes, I see the value. I'm not opposed to the technology categorically. But the RFI treats prior authorization AI and clinical decision support AI as if they belong in the same conversation about "acceleration," and they don't. One helps clinicians make better decisions. The other helps payers make more denials faster. These are not comparable and HHS should not be applying the same framework to both.⏎⏎What I am asking HHS to do in this rulemaking process is to separate those categories explicitly, and to commit that any action taken to accelerate AI adoption in insurance functions — prior auth, claims processing, utilization management — will be conditioned on mandatory human physician review requirements before any denial is finalized. Not after. Before.⏎⏎The appeals data matters here too. We know that the vast majority of patients who receive denials never appeal. For our patient populations the number is almost certainly worse than the national average, because the barriers are higher — language, geography, distrust of institutions, lack of awareness that an appeal is even possible. So when HHS looks at appeal overturn rates to assess whether the system is working, it is only seeing the small fraction of patients who had enough support to navigate the process. The harm to everyone else is invisible in that data, but it is not actually invisible to the physicians who treat these patients.⏎⏎I would also ask HHS to look at the specific question of validation across indigenous populations. AI systems trained predominantly on data from large urban academic medical centers are going to perform differently — and I mean worse — when applied to patient populations with different disease prevalence, different medication histories, different social determinants. The IHS data infrastructure is not well-integrated into the commercial datasets most AI developers are using. That gap has real implications and I don't see it addressed anywhere in this RFI.⏎⏎If HHS wants comments on specific RFI questions: I am primarily addressing the questions related to regulatory actions and reimbursement policy, specifically the questions about what barriers exist and what role HHS should play in governance. The governance question is not really answered by more voluntary frameworks or industry self-attestation. We have had that approach in health IT for a long time and the equity gaps have not closed.⏎⏎I can provide additional documentation from our member survey data if that would be useful to the Department. Our contact information is above though I should note the email listed may have a response delay of several days given our current staffing.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0353♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Geriatrics Society, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Geriatrics Society♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The American Geriatrics Society (AGS) greatly appreciates the opportunity to submit our comments to the⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) on its request for information (RFI) regarding the acceleration⏎of the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.⏎AGS is a nationwide, not-for-profit society of geriatrics healthcare professionals dedicated to improving the⏎health, independence, and quality of life of older people. Our 6,000+ physician and non-physician practitioners⏎(NPPs) are pioneers in serious illness care for older individuals, with a focus on championing interprofessional⏎teams, eliciting personal care goals, and treating older people as whole persons. AGS is an anti-discriminatory⏎organization. We believe in a society where we all are supported by and able to contribute to our communities.⏎The Society leads efforts to incorporate attention to older adults living with multiple chronic conditions into⏎research1,2 and clinical care3,4 and is a champion for improving attention to the unique health care needs of⏎older adults in workforce training.5,6 We believe that understanding disease across the lifespan7 is important to⏎extending healthspan—the time someone lives in generally good health—for all of us as we age.⏎1 Advancing Geriatrics Research: AGS/NIA Conference Series. American Geriatrics Society. Accessed February 17, 2026.⏎https://www.americangeriatrics.org/programs/advancing-geriatrics-research-agsnia-conference-series⏎2 The AGS/AGING LEARNING Collaborative. AGS CoCare. Accessed February 17, 2026.⏎https://mccresearch.agscocare.org/what_is_the_ags_aging_learning_collaborative⏎3 American Geriatrics Society Expert Panel on the Care of Older Adults with Multimorbidity. Guiding principles for the care of older adults⏎with multimorbidity: an approach for clinicians. J Am Geriatr Soc. 2012;60(10):e1-e25. doi:10.1111/j.1532-5415.2012.04188.x⏎4 McNabney MK, Green AR, Burke M, et al. Complexities of care: common components of models of care in geriatrics. J Am Geriatr Soc.⏎2022;70(7):1960–1972. doi:10.1111/jgs.17811⏎5 American Geriatrics Society. Letters to House and Senate Appropriations Leadership on FY 2026 Funding for Geriatrics Workforce⏎Training Programs. July 14, 2025. Accessed February 17, 2026.⏎https://www.americangeriatrics.org/sites/default/files/Letters%20to%20House%20and%20Senate%20Appropriations%20Leadership%20⏎on%20FY%202026%20Funding%20for%20Geriatrics%20Workforce%20Training%20Programs%20%287%2014%2025%29.pdf⏎6 AGS Advancing Geriatrics in Surgical & Medical Specialties. Special Collection. J Am Geriatr Soc. Accessed February 17, 2026.⏎https://agsjournals.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/hub/journal/15325415/agsadvancinggeriatrics⏎7 Inclusion Across the Lifespan in Human Subjects Research. National Institutes of Health. Updated February 27, 2025. Accessed February⏎17, 2026. https://grants.nih.gov/policy-and-compliance/policy-topics/inclusion/lifespan⏎AGS Comments on HHS RFI on Accelerating Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎Page 1⏎⏎Our members are on the frontlines of⏎caring for older Americans, many of⏎whom are living with multiple⏎chronic conditions, advanced illness,⏎and/or with complicated⏎biopsychosocial issues. An important⏎framework for how geriatrics health⏎professionals care for older adults is⏎the Geriatrics 5Ms (see table on the⏎right).8 The Geriatrics 5Ms informed⏎the development of the 4Ms of age-friendly care (What Matters, Medications, Mentation, and Mobility) of the⏎Age-Friendly Health Systems (AFHS) movement which seeks to reimagine the 21st century health system so as to⏎provide care that is age-friendly, respects the goals and preferences of older persons, and meaningfully and⏎substantially includes family caregivers in the plan of care.9⏎AGS appreciates that HHS is seeking feedback to inform HHS-wide use of regulation, reimbursement, and⏎research & development in the application of AI for the ultimate benefit of patients, caregivers, and⏎communities. Geriatricians and other geriatrics clinicians who care for older adults with complicated medical⏎issues and social challenges support a holistic life stage approach. This is vital to fully understand the diverse⏎effects of aging on the older adult population and effectively address the multifaceted issues related to aging.⏎Accordingly, AGS respectfully submits the following recommendations that reflect the most relevant and⏎appropriate considerations for older adults.⏎TOPIC RECOMMENDATION⏎Barriers to private sector Addressing Time Constraints; Lack of Knowledge, Readily Available Information,⏎innovation in AI for health and Evidence; and Insufficient Implementation Support⏎care and its adoption and • Promote hybrid AI-human systems where decision-making is transparent⏎use in clinical care and guided by clinician and patient values and preferences.⏎• Increase transparency about how AI models and tools are built, used, and⏎updated.⏎• Support participatory design methods that align AI tools with users’ goals,⏎abilities, and values, as well as studies that address barriers to uptake,⏎including workflow integration, clinician burden, and reimbursement⏎models.⏎• Focus on clinician-in-the-loop AI for geriatrics decision support, involving⏎both computer scientists and geriatrics health professionals.⏎• Invest in continual learning algorithms that adapt to population shifts (e.g.,⏎post-COVID aging care patterns, new medications) while maintaining clinical⏎safety.⏎Regulatory, payment Ensuring Data Readiness, Interoperability, and Integration⏎policy, or programmatic • Facilitate linkage and harmonization across electronic health records (EHRs),⏎design changes HHS sensors, imaging, genomics, claims, and social determinants data.⏎should prioritize to⏎8 Tinetti M, Huang A, Molnar F. The Geriatrics 5M's: a new way of communicating what we do. J Am Geriatr Soc. 2017;65(9):2115.⏎doi:10.1111/jgs.14979⏎9 Mate KS, Berman A, Laderman M, Kabcenell A, Fulmer T. Creating age-friendly health systems - a vision for better care of older adults.⏎Healthc. 2018;6(1):4-6. doi:10.1016/j.hjdsi.2017.05.005⏎AGS Comments on HHS RFI on Accelerating Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎Page 2⏎⏎incentivize effective use of • Promote common data models and standards (e.g., Fast Healthcare⏎AI and augment ability to Interoperability Resources, Observational Medical Outcomes Partnership) to⏎develop or use AI in support scalable and reusable AI tools.⏎clinical care • Develop standardized frameworks for Intellectual Property (IP)-sharing,⏎privacy-preserving analytics, and responsible model stewardship (e.g., model⏎cards, usage audits) that would further support innovation without⏎compromising trust or transparency.⏎• Evaluate Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) payment models⏎for AI-assisted care interventions.⏎Ethical and Equity Considerations⏎• Create aging-specific ethical frameworks addressing consent capacity, data⏎sharing preferences, surveillance, and autonomy in AI-assisted decision-⏎making, while considering the critical importance of delivery of person-⏎centered care10 that facilitates cross-study research and evaluation of best⏎practices in clinical care.⏎• Build regulatory frameworks for age-related bias and equity to establish⏎standards that identify and mitigate age-related algorithmic bias and⏎promote equitable AI performance across diverse older populations.⏎For non-medical devices, Legal Implications and Safeguards⏎novel legal and • Facilitate educational activities to raise awareness that helps all stakeholders⏎implementation issues ensure appropriate steps are taken for the implementation and evaluation⏎that challenge existing of legal frameworks and safeguards that define liability boundaries and⏎governance and support safe and ethical use of non-medical devices across populations.⏎accountability structures • Ensure safeguarding of data privacy and confidentiality, advancement of⏎and HHS’ role to help health equity and addressing bias, and protecting patients and care partners.⏎address them • Establish guardrails for passive monitoring (e.g., smart home devices,⏎wearables) and algorithm-driven recommendations for vulnerable⏎populations.⏎Governance and Accountability⏎• Create aging-specific governance models that guide data access, model⏎approval, and clinical integration.⏎• Develop and pilot institutional governance toolkits for AI in aging.⏎• Require governance plans for all AI research, including use cases, data flows,⏎and accountability mechanisms.⏎For non-medical devices, Evaluation Methods⏎promising AI evaluation • Human-centered and accessible design that requires geriatrics-informed⏎methods (pre- and post- user experience standards to ensure devices are usable by older adults and⏎deployment), metrics, avoid misapplication and safety risks.⏎robustness testing, and • Geriatrics-specific testbeds, "living labs" for aging AI, and real-world⏎other evaluation methods environments (e.g., older adult housing, Program of All-Inclusive Care for the⏎for clinical care and HHS’ Elderly (PACE) programs, home health agencies) where models can be safely⏎deployed and iteratively refined.⏎10 The American Geriatrics Society Expert Panel on Person-Centered Care. Person-centered care: a definition and essential elements. J Am⏎Geriatr Soc. 2016;64(1):15-18. doi:10.1111/jgs.13866⏎AGS Comments on HHS RFI on Accelerating Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎Page 3⏎⏎support of these • Engage older populations, including those with complex needs (e.g.,⏎processes multimorbidity, frailty, cognitive impairment) and care partners as co-⏎creators, testers, and evaluators of AI tools.⏎• Create regulatory quality parameters for developers to apply when⏎marketing a device to ensure transparency, trust, and awareness about the⏎benefits and risks of the device.⏎Metrics for Evaluation⏎• Error rate reduction⏎• User satisfaction⏎• Match accuracy⏎• Scheduling efficiency⏎• Reach and usability⏎Where AI tools deployed Areas for Improvement⏎in clinical care met, • Accessibility and usability⏎exceeded, or fell short of • Accuracy without omissions or hallucinations⏎performance and cost • Supporting prevention of clinician burnout⏎expectations • Enhancing patient-clinician communications⏎Administrative hurdles for Administrative Hurdles⏎health care organizations • Determination of the most appropriate AI tools to implement in a⏎in the adoption of AI in transparent manner involving frontline clinicians and the community in⏎clinical care decision-making processes.⏎• Regular and frequent access to clinical informatics teams or users who are⏎familiar with AI use in clinical settings.⏎Where enhanced Widen Market Opportunities⏎interoperability would • Pilot dual-licensing strategies for AI tools and datasets that are open for non-⏎widen market commercial and academic use to support reproducibility.⏎opportunities, fuel • Provisions for commercial use to support public benefit, such as⏎research, and accelerate reinvestment into communities or low-resource settings.⏎the development of AI for Support open science by default while allowing more restrictive⏎o⏎clinical care licensing or data use when justified by privacy, national security, or⏎competitiveness concerns.⏎• Create partnerships and data- and model-sharing consortia via interagency⏎initiatives or collaborations with the Department of Veterans Affairs (VA),⏎the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), and other aging-related agencies as⏎well as philanthropic organizations and state-level aging agencies to pilot AI⏎on regulatory science, reimbursement pathways, and care delivery pilots⏎across settings (e.g., home-based care, PACE, skilled nursing facilities) where⏎older adults receive the bulk of their care.⏎Fuel Research⏎• Develop model repositories (akin to the National Institutes of Health (NIH)⏎Bridge2AI) that include metadata on population characteristics, social⏎determinants variables, bias assessments, and intended use.⏎AGS Comments on HHS RFI on Accelerating Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎Page 4⏎⏎• Support cross-institution human factors research in AI that emphasizes⏎interface usability for aging clinicians and patients, shared decision-making⏎integration, explanation clarity, and communication of uncertainty.⏎• Encourage use of data sheets for datasets and model cards for all AI tools⏎proposed for aging research with emphasis on generalizability, limitations,⏎and ethical considerations.⏎• Advance lifelong learning agents that adapt to evolving patient data and⏎generate aging-specific insights across datasets.⏎Challenges and concerns Concerns of Privacy, Safety, and Use of Data Without Clear Consent⏎of patients and caregivers • Implement safeguards for vulnerable frail older adults, especially those with⏎related to the adoption cognitive impairment and whose first language is not English.⏎and use of AI in clinical • Address historical mistrust, especially among underrepresented populations.⏎care • Encourage support of AI for care coordination and caregiver engagement.⏎Development and evaluation of tools that assist with transitions of⏎o⏎care, caregiver support, and advance care planning in older adults.⏎Specific areas of AI Research Areas to Prioritize⏎research that HHS should • Curated, longitudinal aging studies with linked clinical and functional⏎prioritize to accelerate the outcomes to enable training and validation of generalizable models.⏎adoption of AI as part of • Preclinical-to-clinical pipelines specific to aging interventions, including tools⏎clinical care for assessing functional outcomes, quality of life, and caregiver burden as⏎core AI endpoints.⏎• Whole-person-focused studies of age-related chronic diseases, such as⏎diabetes, heart disease, and cancer that continue to afflict 80 percent of⏎people who are 65 and older.11⏎• Replication studies that independently validate AI models in different health⏎systems and among diverse aging subpopulations such as those living in rural⏎areas, who are cognitively impaired, or with limited function.⏎• Benchmark studies for aging-related conditions (e.g., frailty, dementia, falls)⏎with pre-defined tasks and metrics for fair comparison across models.⏎Thank you for your consideration of our recommendations. If you have comments or questions, please contact⏎Anna Kim, Senior Manager of Public Affairs & Advocacy, at 212-308-1414 or akim@americangeriatrics.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Paul Mulhausen, MD Nancy E. Lundebjerg, MPA⏎President Chief Executive Officer⏎11 National Prevention Council. Healthy Aging in Action: Advancing the National Prevention Strategy. November 2016. Accessed February⏎17, 2026. https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/healthy-aging-in-action-final.pdf⏎AGS Comments on HHS RFI on Accelerating Adoption and Use of AI as Part of Clinical Care⏎Page 5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0354♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Clover Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Clover Health♔♔♔♔♔♔          ⏎February 23, 2026⏎TO: Dr. Thomas Keane, M.D., Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎FROM: Clover Health⏎RE: RIN 0955-AA13: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Clover Health Investments, Corp. (Clover) appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Request⏎for Information (RFI) regarding the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎Clover is a physician-enablement technology company committed to expanding access to⏎high-quality, affordable health care for people on Medicare.⏎Clover has been an industry leader in the development and responsible deployment of AI tools⏎embedded directly into clinical workflows. Our experience demonstrates that AI can improve patient⏎outcomes, reduce provider burden, and lower total cost of care—when deployed in a way that is⏎clinician-led, longitudinal, and aligned with value-based care incentives. At the same time, public⏎trust and regulatory certainty are essential to sustaining AI innovation in health care.⏎I. Executive Summary⏎Based on Clover’s experience building and deploying AI tools in real-world clinical settings, we⏎respond to the RFI in the following key areas:⏎● Use of AI to Improve Health Outcomes: Clover has been very successful using AI to⏎improve health outcomes for the Medicare Advantage population. Clover’s AI tools have⏎been shown to (1) identify and treat disease earlier; (2) personalize care for patients; (3)⏎improve prevention; (4) reduce acute care utilization, and (5) improve care and outcomes in⏎underserved communities through earlier detection and reduced hospitalizations.1⏎1 Counterpart Assistant Whitepaper “Use by PCPs in Underserved Chronic Disease Populations Associated with Earlier⏎Diagnosis and Less Frequent Hospitalization” December 2025. Available here.⏎1⏎⏎          ⏎● Barriers to Private Sector Innovation: Existing ambiguity in FDA guidance on AI in⏎healthcare presents an easily fixable barrier. Specifically, to remove that barrier to healthcare⏎AI innovation, the FDA should refine its Clinical Decision Support (CDS) guidance to⏎ensure that “independent review” requirements do not mandate citations for medically⏎self-evident diagnoses. By narrowing this construction, regulators can prevent administrative⏎bloat and allow AI tools to prioritize high-value insights without being reclassified as⏎regulated medical devices for performing basic data-to-diagnosis synthesis.⏎● Enhancing Accountability and Data Rights: To bridge the gap between AI⏎accountability and data rights, HHS should clarify that “derived data”—the system-generated⏎metadata and latent intelligence produced during model operation—remains the proprietary⏎property of the developer. This distinction ensures that while patient inputs remain strictly⏎protected, developers retain the essential diagnostic logs and performance metrics required⏎to fulfill statutory safety monitoring and anti-bias obligations under emerging frameworks⏎like the Colorado AI Act.⏎II. Background on Clover Health⏎Clover is a physician enablement technology company serving people on Medicare through⏎Medicare Advantage plans and through our AI-powered clinical platform for physicians. Our model⏎is built around patient choice, broad access, and clinician-facing tools that support better medicine.⏎● Patient Choice and Affordability: Our members choose their own providers through⏎broad networks, and minimal in- v. out-of-network cost-sharing differences. We offer $0 to⏎low monthly plan premiums and copays with a maximum out-of-pocket limit.⏎● Health Technology for All: We empower clinicians to detect and manage chronic⏎conditions earlier by equipping them with longitudinal patient data and AI-generated insights⏎at the point of care and in their workflows. We do so through our technology platform,⏎Clover Assistant, for in-network, primary-care physicians in our company’s MA plans, and⏎through Counterpart Assistant for primary-care physicians in other plans. Clover Assistant⏎and Counterpart Assistant provide the same platform (collectively here, “Clover Assistant”).⏎Through Clover Assistant, we proudly provide the most sophisticated care-coordination⏎tools (often available only to integrated, large health systems), to independent physicians’⏎groups in rural and underserved communities.⏎2⏎⏎          ⏎● The Right Care, at the Right Time, for Our Most Vulnerable Members: Through our⏎team of nurses, doctors, and other healthcare professionals, we identify high-risk members,⏎and provide preventative care to them directly in their homes. And our teams use our⏎cutting-edge AI platform, Clover Assistant, to provide superior care.⏎Below please find our responses to key areas outlined in the RFI. We welcome the opportunity to⏎discuss how HHS can incorporate the use of AI tools in clinical care to further regulatory,⏎reimbursement, and research and development approaches.⏎III. Use of AI to Improve Health Outcomes⏎Our AI platform is a non-medical device clinical decision support tool that analyzes a vast amount⏎of data from over 100 different sources—including claims, labs, pharmacy, and electronic health⏎records—to create a holistic view of each patient. Instead of a generic alert for a group of patients,⏎AI provides personalized, evidence-based recommendations tailored to the specific person in front⏎of the doctor. This approach enables our doctors to:⏎A. Identify and treat diseases earlier.⏎● Diabetes: Compared to those who did not have Clover Assistant visits with their⏎primary-care providers, enrollees who had Clover Assistant-assisted provider visits: (1)⏎primary-care practitioner use of Clover Assistant is correlated with earlier diagnosis (average⏎HbA1c 6.65% for members having a Clover Assistant visit with a PCP and 6.91% for⏎members not having such a visit); (2) were treated for diabetes an average of 36 months⏎earlier; (3) were much more likely to obtain earlier treatment with oral diabetes medications⏎and delay use of insulin at later stages of type 2 diabetes; and (4) had lower documented⏎rates of hypoglycemia, a severe complication of diabetes, at 1,000 days (1.5% versus 2.4%).2⏎● Chronic Kidney Disease: Primary-care physicians who used Clover Assistant were able to⏎diagnose–earlier–CKD stage 3 and higher. Earlier diagnosis of CKD resulting from⏎CA-assisted visits slowed the decline of kidney function significantly (GFR, or glomerular⏎filtration rate, of about 4.6 mL/min per year versus 1.3 mL/min per year), helping to delay⏎and prevent adverse and costly health outcomes from reduced kidney function, including⏎high blood pressure, heart disease, stroke, dialysis dependence, and death.3⏎2 See here for more details.⏎3 See here for more details.⏎3⏎⏎          ⏎B. Personalize care.⏎AI can surface best practices for complex chronic conditions, ensuring that even a physician who⏎doesn’t specialize in a particular area can provide relevant, up-to-date care. This is particularly⏎important for patients with rarer conditions, where specialized expertise can be hard to find and⏎where personalized treatment diverges from standard population-health protocols.⏎C. Improve prevention.⏎The platform prompts physicians to close critical care gaps and encourages bidirectional feedback⏎through which the provider can instruct the plan to help close these care gaps. Together, these⏎efforts contribute to an 11% higher rate of colorectal cancer screenings and 4.9% higher rate of⏎breast cancer screenings.4⏎D. Less Frequent Acute Care Utilization.⏎● CA PCP attribution was associated with significantly less acute care utilization, including⏎fewer all-cause inpatient hospitalizations (ranging from -7.6% to -21.2%) and 30-day⏎readmissions (ranging from -11.5% to -20.8%) in members across all four studied chronic⏎diseases. 5⏎● Congestive Heart Failure (CHF) patients who had relationships with⏎primary-care providers using the Clover Assistant had a lower average number of all-cause⏎hospitalizations (18% lower) and 30-day readmissions (25% lower) compared to patients⏎whose provider did not have access to Clover Assistant. Clover Assistant helped⏎physicians facilitate more proactive and frequent longitudinal specialty care management⏎for medically complex CHF patients who often face mobility challenges and reduced⏎access to traditional office-based sites, including: (1) higher utilization of in-home care⏎(22.8% versus 12.33% receiving at least 1 in-home visit); and (2) more outpatient⏎cardiologist visits (1.1 visits for CA patients versus 0.8 visits for non-Clover Assistant⏎patients).6⏎Clover’s unique approach to the use of AI includes human-centered design, whereby clinicians can⏎ignore AI-powered recommendations, there is no automated clinical action that the AI tool can take,⏎and there is a built-in feedback loop that improves the model continuously.⏎4 Written testimony of Andrew Toy before House Energy and Commerce Committee, Sept. 3, 2025. Available here.⏎5 See here for more details.⏎6 See here for more details.⏎4⏎⏎          ⏎IV. Barriers to Private Sector Innovation⏎The FDA’s 2026 Clinical Decision Support (CDS) Software Guidance updates the 2022 guidance⏎and clarifies the types of CDS functions that are excluded from the definition of a device. The FDA⏎relies on four criteria for CDS to be excluded from the definition of a device:7⏎Criterion 1: The software is not intended to acquire, process, or analyze a medical image or a⏎signal from an in vitro diagnostic device or a pattern or signal from a signal acquisition⏎system.⏎Criterion 2: The software is intended for the purpose of displaying, analyzing, or printing⏎medical information about a patient or other medical information.⏎Criterion 3: The software is intended for the purpose of supporting or providing⏎recommendations to a provider about prevention, diagnosis, or treatment of a disease or⏎condition.⏎Criterion 4: The software is intended for the purpose of enabling the provider to⏎independently review the basis for the recommendations that such software presents so that⏎it is not the intent that the provider rely primarily on any of such recommendations to make⏎a clinical diagnosis or treatment decision regarding an individual patient.⏎The 2026 guidance expanded the 4th criterion for exclusion to state that the information should be⏎presented in a way that prioritizes decision-relevant details and avoids information overload. The⏎FDA also emphasized that recommendations must be grounded in “well-understood and accepted⏎sources,” such as clinical guidelines and peer-reviewed literature. This criterion should be narrowly⏎construed in order to preserve the clinical accuracy of the provider. For example, if an individual has⏎diabetes and their A1C indicates they have diabetes, Clover’s AI tool should not have to link to⏎clinical guidelines to confirm that the individual has diabetes. We urge the FDA to clarify that the⏎use of clinical guidelines to confirm simple diagnoses should not be construed as an FDA regulated⏎clinical decision support tool but an AI tool that is pulling an individual's data that the physician has⏎the ability to confirm or reject the diagnosis.⏎7 Covington: 5 Key Takeaways from FDA’s Revised Clinical Decision Support (CDS) Software Guidance, Jan. 8, 2026, Available⏎here.⏎5⏎⏎          ⏎V. Enhancing Accountability and Data Rights⏎A critical legal issue surrounding the use of AI in healthcare is the assignment of ownership rights⏎over secondary data. Derived data is a new creation resulting from the developer’s proprietary logic,⏎weights, and processing power. HHS should clarify that derived data created by an AI tool remains⏎the property of the developer. This is distinct from the customer’s underlying patient data (inputs),⏎which remains subject to existing privacy and ownership protections.⏎Traditional healthcare contracts and Business Associate Agreements (BAAs) were designed for static⏎software. They typically mandate the return or destruction of data upon contract termination. But⏎continuous-learning AI requires access to secondary data—such as model confidence scores, latent⏎representations, and processing logs—to improve accuracy, reliability, and safety over time. If⏎developers are forced to “wipe” this intelligence per-deployment, the entire ecosystem suffers from⏎stagnating model performance and a failure to learn from past errors.⏎There is a fundamental principle at stake: ownership of derived data must follow the responsibility⏎for the model’s performance. Under emerging frameworks like the Colorado AI Act, developers face⏎heightened scrutiny and potential liability for “algorithmic discrimination” or harmful⏎“hallucinations.” It is a functional impossibility for a developer to fulfill these legal obligations if they⏎cannot retain the data necessary to:⏎● Conduct Post-Market Surveillance: Identify where a model may be drifting or⏎failing in real-world settings.⏎● Audit for Bias: Aggregate processing data across diverse deployments to detect and⏎mitigate disparate impacts.⏎● Validate Mitigations: Document that a known error has been successfully⏎corrected through model tuning.⏎HHS should provide clear guidance that while underlying PHI and customer-specific inputs remain⏎protected and owned by the covered entity, the derived analytical outputs and system-generated⏎metadata belong to the developer for the purposes of monitoring, auditing, and safety-enhancement.⏎Without this clarity, the “Duty to Monitor” imposed by regulators will remain in conflict with⏎traditional healthcare contracts that do not give developers the ability to access the underlying data⏎necessary to fulfill such duties.⏎6⏎⏎          ⏎VI. Conclusion⏎We support HHS’s vision of adoption and use of AI in clinical care in a way that improves health⏎outcomes, reduces barriers to innovation, and enhances accountability and clarifies data rights so⏎that AI can be deployed most effectively.⏎To accelerate the adoption of AI in clinical care, HHS should prioritize these three critical areas⏎based on Clover Health's experience:⏎● Use AI within clinical care settings to identify and treat disease earlier, personalize care for⏎patients, improve prevention and reduce acute care utilization.⏎● Reduce barriers to private sector innovation, such as narrowly tailoring the applicability of⏎the FDA’s clinical decision support guidance.⏎● Focus on enhancing accountability and data rights by defining derived data to address⏎liability and AI safety concerns.⏎Clover appreciates HHS’ engagement on greater adoption of AI in clinical care and shares the⏎agency’s commitment to use AI effectively and in a way that fosters public trust. We look forward to⏎continued collaboration and welcome further dialogue as HHS evaluates the use of AI in clinical⏎care.⏎Best regards,⏎William Chang⏎Vice President and Head of Government Affairs⏎7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0355♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Dome100X, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dome100X♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS RFI:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care⏎Strategic Summary⏎This submission responds to the HHS Request for Information by outlining a decision-⏎architecture approach designed to operate under conditions of high uncertainty, fragmented⏎data, and accelerated policy timelines. The approach focuses on structuring multi-domain inputs⏎into transparent decision pathways that preserve traceability, governance, and human oversight⏎while increasing decision velocity and outcome confidence. It is modular, non-prescriptive, and⏎intended to augment—not replace—existing HHS systems, processes, and authorities.⏎Submission Context⏎This response reflects cross-sector experience designing, governing, and evaluating AI-enabled⏎decision systems in complex, regulated healthcare environments. The comments focus on⏎adoption at scale—specifically the governance, evaluation, and incentive structures required to⏎move clinical AI from pilots into routine care while preserving trust, safety, and fiscal integrity.⏎Framing Statement (Decision Spine)⏎Clinical AI is not constrained by technical capability; it is constrained by decision governance.⏎Adoption at scale requires continuous accountability, auditable decision-making, and alignment⏎across regulation, reimbursement, evaluation, and clinical workflows.⏎Primary Levers (1–3)⏎• Governance: Clarify accountability for non-device AI through continuous oversight⏎expectations.⏎• Evaluation: Normalize post-deployment monitoring as operational infrastructure, not a one-⏎time event.⏎• Incentives: Align reimbursement with monitored, real-world performance to sustain adoption⏎and protect program integrity.⏎Observed Barriers to Adoption⏎The most significant barriers are governance ambiguity, workflow disruption, and incentive⏎misalignment rather than model performance. Providers lack clear expectations for liability,⏎auditability, post-deployment monitoring, and human oversight for non-device AI. This⏎uncertainty increases administrative burden, slows internal decision-making, and keeps AI⏎trapped in pilots.⏎In practice, fragmented governance often results in duplicative reviews across compliance,⏎legal, IT, and clinical leadership—adding weeks or months to adoption timelines. AI is also⏎frequently introduced as a parallel system rather than embedded within existing workflows,⏎increasing documentation time and clinician resistance.⏎Regulatory, Reimbursement, and Programmatic Actions⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by clarifying acceptable governance patterns for non-device AI⏎rather than regulating specific technologies. Guidance should define baseline expectations for⏎human oversight, documentation and traceability of AI-influenced decisions, post-deployment⏎monitoring, and conditions for rollback or suspension.⏎(Version Date: 2/22/2026) (Page 1 of 3)⏎⏎On reimbursement, HHS could pilot payment pathways explicitly tied to monitored outcomes⏎and operational performance, reducing the risk of waste, fraud, and abuse while rewarding⏎demonstrated value. Programmatically, HHS should emphasize implementation science and⏎real-world evaluation infrastructure alongside innovation.⏎Governance, Liability, Privacy, and Security for Non-Device AI⏎Non-device AI often influences care decisions without fitting existing device frameworks,⏎creating gaps in accountability and oversight. Challenges include unclear responsibility for⏎outcomes, insufficient audit trails, limited standardized monitoring, and patient uncertainty⏎regarding consent and recourse.⏎A decision-centric governance approach—focused on traceability, continuous monitoring,⏎defined human authority boundaries, and privacy-by-design—can close these gaps without⏎constraining innovation.⏎Evaluation and Monitoring as Infrastructure⏎Pre-deployment testing is necessary but insufficient. Safe scale requires continuous post-⏎deployment monitoring, including performance drift detection, outcome tracking, bias and equity⏎monitoring, and workflow impact measurement.⏎For example, a clinical decision-support system that performs well at deployment may drift as⏎patient populations, coding practices, or care protocols change. Continuous monitoring enables⏎early detection, targeted retraining, or suspension before harm or reimbursement risk occurs.⏎HHS can catalyze adoption by standardizing evaluation frameworks and shared metrics that⏎make ongoing monitoring a norm rather than an exception.⏎Certification, Accreditation, and Credentialing⏎Process-based certification is more scalable than model-specific approval. HHS can support⏎accreditation of governance and monitoring practices, certification of evaluation and audit⏎processes, and credentialing focused on operational readiness. This approach preserves⏎innovation while ensuring consistent safeguards across care settings.⏎Interoperability and Benchmarking⏎Interoperability efforts should prioritize benchmarking and evaluation, not just data exchange.⏎Standardized outcome metrics, comparable evaluation datasets, and common reporting⏎structures for post-deployment monitoring will expand markets, reduce friction, and accelerate⏎learning across systems.⏎Patient and Provider Perspectives⏎Patients and caregivers seek transparency into how AI influences care, meaningful consent,⏎assurance of privacy, and clear recourse when errors occur. Providers want tools that reduce⏎burden rather than add parallel workflows—measurably decreasing documentation time, review⏎cycles, and administrative overhead.⏎Trust increases when AI supports—rather than replaces—human decision-making and⏎accountability remains with care teams.⏎(Version Date: 2/22/2026) (Page 2 of 3)⏎⏎Research Priorities⏎HHS research investments should prioritize implementation science, real-world evaluation, and⏎governance models over incremental model novelty. High-impact areas include post-⏎deployment monitoring methods, human–AI interaction in workflows, governance for non-device⏎AI, and mechanisms aligning reimbursement with monitored outcomes.⏎Closing Statement⏎HHS’s greatest leverage is not selecting technologies, but shaping the governance and⏎incentive environment that allows trustworthy AI to move from pilots to policy to execution —⏎safely, measurably, and at scale.⏎Key Takeaways⏎• Problem: Clinical AI adoption stalls due to governance ambiguity, administrative burden, and⏎misaligned incentives.⏎• Core Insight: Decision-centric governance with continuous evaluation and outcome-aligned⏎reimbursement.⏎• Result: Safe, scalable, and fiscally responsible AI adoption in clinical care.⏎The submitter is available to provide clarification or additional context if helpful to HHS’s⏎ongoing analysis.⏎Point of Contact:⏎Tony G. Collins⏎tonygcollins@dome100x.ai⏎(Version Date: 2/22/2026) (Page 3 of 3)♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0356♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Crudgington, Brett, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brett♔Crudgington♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a mental health professional, I support the Psychotherapy Action Network’s (PsiAN) call for caution regarding AI in clinical care. Strong protections for patients and clinicians are essential to be sure that privacy violations do not occur and inadequate/insubstantial care is not promoted above the care provided by a licensed human being. Profit-driven incentives (including those that inspire the use of AI) create poor and unethical outcomes when present in explicitly care-oriented fields—including the relationship oriented work of psychotherapy.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0357♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Waldfogel, Shimon, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Shimon♔Waldfogel♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Request for Information: AI in Healthcare — Salutogenic Perspective⏎Response to HHS Request for Information:⏎Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare⏎A Salutogenic Perspective⏎Submitted by:⏎Shimon Waldfogel MD⏎Institute for Salutogenesis⏎Shimonwaldfogel@gmail.com⏎February 2026⏎WHO IS RESPONDING⏎I am a physician with 30 years of clinical practice in psychiatry and medical education, a⏎current AI developer and experimenter building tools to facilitate health creation and⏎maintenance, and President of the Institute for Salutogenesis — a non-profit⏎organization with a mission to empower individuals and communities to thrive by⏎focusing on the origins of health rather than just disease.⏎THE PERSPECTIVE I BRING⏎The Salutogenic Reframe⏎The RFI focuses on how AI can make current healthcare more efficient: faster diagnosis,⏎optimized scheduling, reduced administrative burden, streamlined billing. These⏎applications matter. But they accept a fundamental assumption: that healthcare's⏎primary purpose is managing disease.⏎I propose a different starting point.⏎Salutogenesis — the study of what creates and maintains health — asks a fundamentally⏎different question than pathogenesis, which studies what causes disease. This is not⏎semantic. It changes everything about what we ask AI to accomplish.⏎The current approach asks: How can AI help us diagnose and treat disease more⏎efficiently?⏎The salutogenic approach asks: How can AI help everyone get what they need to stay⏎healthy and flourish?⏎One optimizes disease processing. The other creates conditions for health. Both are⏎necessary. Current policy supports only the first.⏎Why This Matters Now⏎Page 1 | Institute for Salutogenesis⏎⏎HHS Request for Information: AI in Healthcare — Salutogenic Perspective⏎America spends nearly twice as much per capita on healthcare as peer nations, yet has⏎shorter lifespans, higher rates of chronic disease, a growing mental health crisis, and⏎widening health disparities.⏎This is not an efficiency problem. We are very efficient at managing disease.⏎This is a priority problem. We lack systematic support for health creation. AI offers⏎unprecedented opportunity to address both — enhance disease management and create⏎conditions for health — but only if policy enables it.⏎WHAT SALUTOGENIC AI REQUIRES⏎To realize AI's potential for health creation, four foundational elements must be⏎addressed:⏎1. Shared Decision-Making as Core Design Principle⏎Current Reality:⏎Most AI in healthcare serves providers and administrators. Patients are data sources⏎and intervention targets, not participants.⏎Salutogenic Requirement:⏎AI tools must support genuine shared decision-making — not automated⏎recommendations patients passively accept, but systems that help people understand⏎their options, articulate their values, and make informed choices aligned with their lives.⏎This means:⏎• Transparent explanation of how AI reaches recommendations⏎• Clear, patient-appropriate presentation of alternatives and tradeoffs⏎• Support for patients to question and challenge clinician and AI guidance⏎• Integration of patient preferences and values into decision-making⏎• Tools that strengthen rather than replace the patient-provider relationship⏎Policy Implication: Federal funding and reimbursement should require AI systems to⏎demonstrate meaningful patient engagement, not just clinical accuracy.⏎2. Access to Integrated Data Across Silos⏎Current Reality:⏎Health is determined by clinical factors, social determinants, environmental exposures,⏎community resources, and behavioral health — all tracked in separate, incompatible⏎systems.⏎Salutogenic Requirement:⏎AI cannot support whole-person health creation with fragmented data. We need⏎privacy-protected integration across:⏎• Clinical records (EHR systems)⏎• Social services (housing, food assistance, employment support)⏎• Community resources (nonprofits, local programs, support networks)⏎Page 2 | Institute for Salutogenesis⏎⏎HHS Request for Information: AI in Healthcare — Salutogenic Perspective⏎• Behavioral health (mental health and substance use treatment)⏎• Environmental data (housing quality, neighborhood safety, pollution exposure)⏎The Barrier:⏎As an AI developer actively building these tools, I cannot create effective health creation⏎systems without this integration. Current data silos make whole-person AI technically⏎impossible, not just difficult.⏎Policy Implication: HHS should establish frameworks for privacy-protected data⏎integration across health and social services. Without this, AI will remain limited to⏎narrow clinical applications.⏎3. Clinician Competence in Using AI Tools⏎Current Reality:⏎AI is being deployed in clinical settings where providers have minimal training in⏎whole-person health approaches, AI capabilities and limitations, appropriate⏎interpretation of AI recommendations, and patient communication about AI-influenced⏎decisions.⏎Salutogenic Requirement:⏎Health creation AI will be used by diverse professionals — physicians, nurses,⏎community health workers, care navigators, social workers — who need competence in:⏎• Using AI to support rather than replace clinical judgment⏎• Identifying when AI recommendations enhance versus undermine shared⏎decision-making⏎• Explaining AI guidance in patient-comprehensible terms⏎• Coordinating AI-enabled interventions across disciplines⏎Policy Implication: HHS should fund development of AI competency curricula for all⏎health professions and require AI literacy as part of continuing education.⏎4. Focus on Social Determinants and Community Resources⏎Current Reality:⏎Multiple commenters on this RFI have noted the importance of addressing social⏎determinants of health (SDOH). I strongly agree — and want to emphasize what this⏎actually requires. Screening for food insecurity, housing instability, transportation⏎barriers, and social isolation is valuable only if it connects people to resources. Most⏎current SDOH screening ends with documentation in the medical record. The patient⏎still faces the same barriers. Nothing changes.⏎Salutogenic Requirement:⏎AI tools must:⏎• Screen for social determinants systematically⏎• Automatically match people with available community resources⏎• Generate referrals to social services and community organizations⏎• Track whether connections were successful⏎Page 3 | Institute for Salutogenesis⏎⏎HHS Request for Information: AI in Healthcare — Salutogenic Perspective⏎• Identify systematic gaps where resources don't exist⏎• Support community health workers and care navigators with real-time⏎information⏎Policy Implication: HHS should establish national standards for community resource⏎data and fund infrastructure enabling clinical-community integration.⏎RESPONDING TO QUESTION 6: GREATEST POTENTIAL AI TOOLS⏎Given this salutogenic perspective, what AI tools have the greatest potential to⏎improve outcomes and reduce costs?⏎The Answer: Integrated Whole-Person Support Systems⏎AI tools with greatest potential will address the whole person — integrating across⏎dimensions current systems treat separately:⏎1. Support Health Creation Alongside Disease Management⏎Not replacing current clinical AI, but complementing it with systems that help people⏎understand what creates health in their specific circumstances, connect them to⏎resources that promote flourishing, support prevention before disease develops, and⏎make healthcare comprehensible and navigable.⏎2. Address the Whole Person⏎Integrating across dimensions current systems treat separately:⏎• Physical health (clinical care, prevention, health behaviors)⏎• Mental and emotional wellbeing (stress, resilience, psychological health)⏎• Social determinants (housing, food, economic security, community connection)⏎• Environmental factors (neighborhood conditions, exposures, safety)⏎• Spiritual factors (promoting evidence-based practices and community connection⏎where desired)⏎3. Enable Shared Decision-Making⏎Tools that translate complexity into comprehensible information, present options and⏎tradeoffs clearly, support informed choice aligned with patient values, and strengthen⏎rather than supplant the provider-patient relationship.⏎4. Connect Clinical Care with Community Resources⏎Systems that identify social barriers to health, match people with available community⏎resources in real time, generate and track referrals, and close feedback loops that⏎identify gaps requiring community investment.⏎5. Focus on Critical Developmental Windows⏎Especially the first thousand days (pregnancy through age 2), when:⏎Page 4 | Institute for Salutogenesis⏎⏎HHS Request for Information: AI in Healthcare — Salutogenic Perspective⏎• Health trajectories are established⏎• Interventions have compounding lifetime effects⏎• Family support prevents intergenerational health disparities⏎• Return on investment is highest ($4–$9 return per dollar invested)⏎WHY THESE TOOLS HAVE GREATEST POTENTIAL⏎Making America Healthy requires creating health — not just managing disease more⏎efficiently. These tools operationalize that vision. The cost reductions they generate are⏎not the goal; they are the consequence of doing something more fundamental: building⏎the conditions under which people actually thrive.⏎Salutogenic Outcomes: What Health Creation Looks Like⏎The metrics that matter for health creation are not simply the absence of pathology.⏎They are the presence of the conditions that allow people to flourish. Drawing on Aaron⏎Antonovsky's foundational framework, these outcomes include:⏎Sense of Coherence⏎People's capacity to understand their situation, believe they have the resources to⏎manage it, and find meaning in doing so. Research consistently shows this is one of the⏎strongest predictors of sustained health across the lifespan. AI tools designed around⏎health creation should be measured in part by whether they strengthen this capacity —⏎not just whether they route people to clinical services.⏎Agency and Navigational Capacity⏎Not simply connecting people to resources, but building their capacity to navigate⏎complex systems on their own terms over time. This is a fundamentally different⏎outcome metric than reduced utilization. A person who understands how to advocate for⏎themselves, interpret clinical information, and access community support is not just⏎avoiding illness — they are accumulating health.⏎Community Health Assets⏎These tools identify and strengthen what already exists in communities — treating local⏎assets as generative resources rather than their absence as the primary problem. The⏎goal is a map of abundance, continuously updated, that connects people with the social⏎infrastructure of health that surrounds them.⏎Developmental Trajectories⏎Particularly through the First 1000 Days lens, salutogenic outcomes compound. A child⏎born into a family with strong coherence, social support, and navigational capacity⏎doesn't merely avoid adverse outcomes — they develop differently. The neurological,⏎relational, and emotional foundations laid in those first years shape a lifetime of health.⏎This is the highest-return investment in health creation available.⏎System Intelligence: Generating New Knowledge⏎Page 5 | Institute for Salutogenesis⏎⏎HHS Request for Information: AI in Healthcare — Salutogenic Perspective⏎These tools do not merely deliver existing knowledge more efficiently. They generate⏎new knowledge — about how social determinants, community assets, and clinical care⏎interact in specific populations; about which interventions actually build coherence and⏎capacity rather than just managing symptoms; about where systematic gaps in⏎community resources require investment.⏎This adaptive capacity — a learning system that improves its understanding of what⏎creates health — represents a qualitatively different contribution than optimization of⏎existing workflows.⏎Disease Burden Reduction as Consequence⏎When health creation succeeds, reductions in disease burden follow. Emergency⏎department visits for conditions manageable in community settings decline — not⏎because we redirected people, but because those conditions didn't develop. Hospital⏎readmissions driven by social barriers fall — not because we managed transitions better,⏎but because the underlying barriers were addressed. Chronic conditions are prevented⏎or substantially delayed — not through screening and surveillance, but through the⏎early, sustained support that allows people to maintain health.⏎These are not marginal efficiency gains. They represent a fundamental reduction in⏎disease burden — durable, compounding, and equitable in a way that disease⏎management optimization can never be.⏎CRITICAL BARRIERS FROM DEVELOPMENT EXPERIENCE⏎As someone actively building these tools, I face specific barriers that have clear policy⏎solutions:⏎Data Silos: Cannot integrate across clinical, social service, community resource, and⏎behavioral health systems.⏎Regulatory Uncertainty: Unclear whether health creation tools are medical devices,⏎wellness products, or something requiring new classification.⏎No Reimbursement Pathway: Current payment models reimburse disease⏎management, not health creation. No viable path to sustainability for tools that prevent⏎the conditions reimbursement depends on.⏎Lack of Standards: No national standards for community resource data,⏎clinical-community referral protocols, or outcome measurement for social interventions.⏎Each barrier has a policy solution. What is required is recognition that health creation⏎AI faces different obstacles than disease management AI — and deserves deliberate⏎support.⏎RECOMMENDATIONS⏎Immediate⏎• Issue guidance clarifying regulatory pathways for salutogenic AI tools⏎Page 6 | Institute for Salutogenesis⏎⏎HHS Request for Information: AI in Healthcare — Salutogenic Perspective⏎• Convene stakeholders on data integration barriers across HHS and related⏎Department and Agencies (HHS Genesis Mission)⏎• Make available easy-to-use APIs for available federal health data⏎• Support AI in Health Hackathons with HHS staff actively accessible⏎Short-Term⏎• Develop standards for community resource data and clinical-community⏎integration⏎• Create payment codes for whole-person assessment and SDOH intervention⏎• Fund development and implementation of AI competency training for all health⏎professions⏎• Prioritize First 1000 Days applications in research funding, including through⏎SBIR⏎Medium-Term⏎• Establish a formal “Health Creation AI” regulatory designation⏎• Build national community resource data infrastructure⏎• Implement value-based payment models that reward prevention and health⏎creation⏎• Expand Medicaid coverage for AI-enabled preventive and salutogenic services⏎CONCLUSION⏎AI in healthcare stands at a choice point.⏎One path accelerates adoption of tools that make current disease management more⏎efficient. The other enables tools that create health alongside managing disease. The⏎first path is well-supported by current policy. The second requires deliberate⏎intervention.⏎As clinician, experimenter, developer, and salutogenesis advocate, I am actively building⏎tools for the second path. I know they can work technically, and I know they can⏎contribute meaningfully to the health and wellbeing of Americans.⏎The perspective I bring — grounded in clinical reality, informed by development⏎experience, and guided by salutogenic principles — suggests that AI's greatest potential⏎lies not in processing disease-related information faster, but in creating health more⏎effectively.⏎This aligns with HHS's mission, serves MAHA's vision, and addresses what⏎patients actually need.⏎The question is whether policy will enable it.⏎Page 7 | Institute for Salutogenesis♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0358♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Edmonds, Sarah, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Edmonds♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔What concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎⏎I am a licensed psychologist in private practice.  I see about 50% of my clients via telehealth and 50% in person.  ⏎⏎Recently a client of mine moved to another state and was not having regular psychotherapy sessions with me as she was waiting for her new health insurance to go into effect.  She became obsessed with using ChatGPT to help her determine whether or not a prospective romantic partner was interested in her, and this obsessive behavior was quite distressing to her but she couldn't stop the compulsion to ask ChatGPT it's opinion.  An actual human clinician would point out to the patient what she is doing and help her see the harm in continuing to do so, and work with her therapeutically to transform and change this compulsion, but ChatGPT just keep responding to her queries, which was not helpful to her.  ⏎⏎AI should not ever be used to totally replace clinicians in mental health, as there is an energetic connection between human beings, especially when in person with each other.  This energetic connection is very healing, and can never be replicated by technology.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0359♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from WellSpan Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔WellSpan Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Bruce M. Bartels Management and Governance Center⏎45 Monument Rd., Suite 200⏎York, PA 17403⏎February 22, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically to Regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎WellSpan Health appreciates the opportunity to submit comments to the Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎on the Request for Information (RFI) on artificial intelligence (AI) adoption in clinical care. We support the⏎Administration’s ongoing commitment to tech-enabling healthcare to reduce administrative burden while⏎improving access to high-quality care. We continue to advocate for a responsible, innovation-friendly and⏎transparent regulatory framework for AI in healthcare, aligning with our longstanding commitment to⏎improving patient outcomes and supporting clinicians through innovation.⏎WellSpan Health’s vision is to reimagine healthcare through the delivery of comprehensive health and⏎wellness solutions throughout our continuum of care. As an integrated delivery system focused on leading⏎in value-based care, we encompass more than 2,700 employed providers, more than 250 locations, nine⏎award-winning hospitals, home care, and a behavioral health organization serving central Pennsylvania⏎and northern Maryland.⏎INTRODUCTION⏎The U.S. healthcare system stands at a critical juncture. After decades of fragmentation, rising costs, and⏎uneven outcomes, we now possess the technological capabilities to fundamentally transform care delivery⏎through market driven solutions powered by applied AI. To realize this potential, the federal government⏎must prioritize strong incentives — not punitive regulatory or enforcement postures — that encourage⏎-⏎innovation rather than create fear driven delays in development and adoption. A cohesive,⏎multi perspective, and nationally consistent framework is essential to ensure that all tiers of AI — from⏎-⏎traditional machine learning to advanced generative models — can be safely and effectively implemented⏎-⏎across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎⏎To that end, a national framework of legal and regulatory incentives will support robust contracting⏎practices and empower end users to deploy rapidly advancing AI technologies with confidence. Together,⏎we can build a forward leaning, industry supportive, and secure approach that accelerates clinical AI⏎-⏎adoption while safeguarding patient privacy, civil rights, and civil liberties. By aligning regulation,⏎- -⏎reimbursement, and research and development investments, we can reduce the uncertainty that currently⏎impedes innovation, strengthen public trust in emerging technologies, and ensure that AI is deployed in⏎ways that enhance productivity, reduce operational burden, lower healthcare costs, and improve health⏎outcomes for patients, caregivers, and communities across America.⏎Achieving these aims will require not only federal leadership but also a unified national vision that⏎promotes innovation, protects patients, and empowers providers to deliver the highest quality care.⏎-⏎WELLSPAN HEALTH’S SUCCESSES IN CLINICAL DEPLOYMENT OF AI⏎WellSpan Health has partnered with leading innovators to leverage AI in clinical workflows and improve⏎patient outcomes.⏎At WellSpan Health, AI is a clinical support tool. It helps our teams deliver faster diagnoses, expand access⏎to care and spend more time with patients while keeping safety, privacy and human judgment at the center⏎of everything we do. In a clinical setting, early detection and intervention can improve patient outcomes⏎and drastically reduce overall healthcare costs – for both patients and providers, and that is where we⏎have focused AI deployment. For example, WellSpan Health uses Food and Drug Administration⏎(FDA) cleared clinical AI to help radiologists identify and prioritize urgent findings such as stroke,⏎pulmonary embolism, brain hemorrhage, aneurysm and spinal fractures – among⏎other diagnoses. These AI tools analyze imaging studies and flag high-risk cases within minutes,⏎allowing radiologists to review the most critical scans first. A radiologist expert still reads every⏎image. The AI supports triage and prioritization; it does not replace clinical judgment by a trained⏎clinician.⏎Additionally, WellSpan Health became the only health system in central Pennsylvania to deploy⏎an AI solution to aid in breast cancer detection during screening mammograms. The AI⏎helps identify cancers that are often difficult to detect, particularly in women with dense breast⏎tissue or implants. Earlier detection allows WellSpan Health teams to more quickly connect⏎patients with follow-up care, sometimes before a patient even leaves the appointment.⏎Below are our detailed responses to the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) RFI.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The most significant barriers to AI adoption in healthcare include financial constraints, liability concerns,⏎malpractice impact, change management challenges, infrastructure complexity, misaligned⏎reimbursement incentives, and patient trust. Up front and ongoing costs for implementing, monitoring,⏎-⏎and maintaining AI systems are substantial — especially when responsible use requires human oversight,⏎-⏎safety reporting, and continuous performance auditing. Fear of legal exposure, such as liability for missed⏎findings or automation errors, combined with unclear risk allocation between vendors and providers,⏎⏎further suppresses deployment. Finally, data accessibility and interoperability among legacy platform⏎providers is also a major barrier.⏎Operationally, AI introduces new workflows, training needs, and integration dependencies that can slow⏎adoption and create additional failure points. Patients and clinicians may also harbor skepticism —⏎worrying that AI could diminish the clinician’s role or produce inaccurate outputs —which underscores the⏎need for thoughtful design, education, and transparent quality reporting.⏎To successfully enable market activity and promote healthy competition among AI developers and⏎healthcare end users, a national operating standard is essential. Such a standard would support the⏎natural development of contracting norms that help providers consistently evaluate the trustworthiness,⏎-⏎reliability, and measurable benefit of AI tools. Clear expectations for risk allocation, transparency,⏎avoidance of “black box” data gaps, reporting requirements, and insurability would strengthen industry⏎accountability and better protect patients who may be incidentally harmed as these technologies continue⏎to evolve.⏎Finally, today’s fee for service reimbursement structure creates a structural disincentive for adopting AI⏎tools that prevent costly interventions or improve efficiency. Because AI’s productivity gains are not⏎- -⏎rewarded under inflationary fee for service models, investment gravitates away from more expensive,⏎high value applications. In fact, any productivity gains that result from the use of AI would be punitive⏎- -⏎under the fee-for-service models, creating a self-defeating cycle to the adoption of AI. Aligning⏎-⏎reimbursement with outcomes is essential to creating market pull for tools that genuinely improve care⏎quality and operational efficiency. Transitioning to value based models would allow the healthcare system⏎to absorb AI’s deflationary benefits and reinvest the savings generated by automation back into the⏎-⏎broader ecosystem.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS should prioritize payment policies that recognize AI enabled care, paired with predictable and⏎proportionate oversight. First, reimbursement pathways should permit separate payment for AI based⏎-⏎services when the tool demonstrably contributes to clinical value — for example, post discharge⏎-⏎telephonic follow ups conducted effectively by an AI agent — so adoption is not penalized relative to fully⏎-⏎human workflows. Second, HHS should pursue an outcomes oriented pathway for AI-based healthcare⏎-⏎services such as radiology triage or early detection models, acknowledging both the throughput gains and⏎-⏎the earlier, lower cost interventions that improve long term patient outcomes. Third, maximum flexibility⏎should be granted to providers using validated tools to avoid prescriptive constraints that would suppress⏎- -⏎capacity gains, particularly in regions facing acute workforce shortages. Finally, provisional clearances⏎paired with a structured, stepwise pathway to full accreditation would support timely innovation while⏎allowing evidence to accumulate without stalling deployment.⏎Expanding the amount of AI supported care that is separately paid — whether through dedicated⏎AI related billing codes or incentive programs tied to demonstrated improvements in patient outcomes⏎-⏎within value based care — would accelerate responsible adoption across the healthcare system. At the⏎-⏎same time, a federal ban on payer mandated “AI prohibition” clauses for revenue cycle automation is⏎-⏎essential. These clauses create significant inequity: while payers increasingly rely on AI for claims⏎- - -⏎⏎processing and denial management, they often restrict providers from using similar tools to improve their⏎own administrative efficiency. Ensuring that providers can deploy AI on equal footing would promote⏎fairness, reduce administrative burden, and support a more balanced and transparent claims ecosystem.⏎Regulatory activity should prioritize a set of nationally consistent policies that strengthen security,⏎transparency, and trust while enabling fair, competitive AI adoption across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎First, cybersecurity policy should be incentive based, rewarding providers for maintaining reasonable,⏎diligent adherence to evolving best practices rather than penalizing them for sophisticated AI driven⏎-⏎attacks that overwhelm even well prepared organizations.⏎-⏎-⏎Second, federal leadership is urgently needed to replace the emerging patchwork of fifty plus different⏎state AI laws—many of which conflict with existing federal privacy and disclosure requirements — with a⏎unified national framework for disclosure, consent, and privacy. A preemptive federal standard would⏎reduce uncertainty for end users and streamline compliance for vendors operating across multiple⏎jurisdictions.⏎-⏎Third, standardized Business Associate Agreement (BAA) requirements are essential to closing the⏎transparency gap between AI vendors and end users. Today, vendors often use opaque contracting⏎practices and maintain “black box” data silos that shift disproportionate liability to providers, limiting their⏎-⏎ability to evaluate products or foster healthy market competition.⏎-⏎Fourth, clear transparency and audit requirements should be developed to establish federal baselines for⏎algorithmic explainability, banning black box opacity, and requiring vendors to provide audit information⏎to end users. Such standards would promote fair competition, improve safety, and strengthen patient trust⏎-⏎by making AI systems more understandable and accountable.⏎-⏎Finally, modernization of the Physician Self-Referral Law (Stark Law) and the federal Anti Kickback Statute⏎is needed to include safe harbors and exceptions that reduce legal uncertainty and enable responsible⏎-⏎collaboration, particularly around data sharing and cybersecurity. Collectively, these regulatory actions⏎would create industry wide best practices for AI contracting, empower end users, and support a⏎competitive, innovative marketplace that ultimately benefits patients.⏎- -⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎For AI software that is not integrated with the developer’s hardware, several unresolved governance⏎challenges continue to impede responsible adoption, including liability allocation between providers and⏎vendors, standards for indemnification, privacy and security obligations for data used in training and⏎post market monitoring, and accountability for ongoing model updates. HHS can play a defining role in⏎addressing these gaps by clarifying safe harbor conditions for providers who follow approved governance⏎-⏎practices, encouraging shared risk contracting models that align vendor accountability with claimed⏎-⏎performance, and promoting standardized incident reporting pathways that integrate AI related errors⏎-⏎into existing patient safety systems without creating punitive disincentives to disclose. Our own operations⏎- -⏎already route AI related errors through patient safety workflows — for example, when an automated⏎-⏎- -⏎⏎agent confirmed an appointment that was never scheduled — illustrating the need for normalized,⏎“learn from events” processes rather than ad hoc responses.⏎- -⏎Additionally, patient consent frameworks require modernization. HHS should clarify Health Insurance⏎Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) obligations under 45 CFR §164.508 for AI related disclosure and⏎consent, including clear safe harbors. Updated guidance should address when and how patient consent is⏎-⏎required for AI supported analysis, the scope of opt out rights, and expectations for continuously learning⏎systems. Clear and consistent HIPAA requirements would support predictable patient communication⏎- -⏎based on actual clinical risk, reduce legal ambiguity for health systems, and strengthen patient trust in⏎AI enabled care.⏎-⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎Effective evaluation of clinical and operational AI requires coupling technical robustness measures — such⏎as drift detection, hallucination and error monitoring, and bias assessments —with human centered and⏎workflow based metrics, including clinician trust, handoff safety, and escalation efficacy. Accordingly,⏎-⏎WellSpan Health combines supervisory AI systems that monitor for drift and trigger alerts with frontline⏎-⏎error intake processes embedded in our existing patient safety infrastructure. This blended model of⏎automated surveillance and human recognition ensures both high fidelity detection and accountability.⏎- -⏎Prior to deployment, real world simulations and pilot proofs of concept anchored to clinical key⏎-⏎performance indicators (KPIs) are essential. Post deployment, continuous monitoring and the use of⏎- - -⏎registries to track performance, safety events, and outcome signals are critical to ensuring ongoing⏎-⏎reliability and safety.⏎HHS can significantly amplify these efforts by leveraging contracts, grants, and cooperative agreements to⏎fund multi site evaluations, develop common registries, and build shared reference datasets. Prize⏎competitions could further accelerate the development of transparent, rigorous robustness testing⏎-⏎frameworks that the entire industry can adopt.⏎-⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should endorse and align with accreditation pathways that either recognize existing FDA labeling⏎where applicable or establish new models that mirror FDA principles — ensuring baseline safety and⏎effectiveness while supporting post market surveillance. At the same time, accreditation must preserve⏎room for innovation sandboxes and provisional scaling so developers can iterate and build evidence⏎-⏎without halting progress. This balanced approach allows implementation to “start with either⏎accreditation or an FDA label,” while still enabling responsible exploration and evidence generation. HHS⏎can further strengthen the ecosystem by supporting ACR/ABR/DNV/CMS aligned frameworks, expanding⏎registry infrastructure for outcomes tracking, and offering education and implementation supports for⏎-⏎providers.⏎A key opportunity to support AI adoption in clinical care is a Deemed Status model in which HHS designates⏎qualified accreditation organizations — similar to the Deemed Status accreditation system currently in⏎⏎effect at 42 CFR Part 488 — to certify AI systems that meet established safety, effectiveness, and equity⏎standards. Healthcare organizations using HHS recognized and certified AI tools would receive regulatory⏎advantages, creating a more efficient and trustworthy marketplace. This approach would allow hospitals⏎-⏎and clinics to adopt certified AI tools with confidence, reducing the need for extensive internal evaluations⏎that many organizations — especially smaller community hospitals and rural health systems — lack the⏎capacity to perform. The result is a democratization of access to high quality AI, ensuring benefits are not⏎confined to well resourced academic medical centers.⏎-⏎-⏎Finally, HHS should support the development of clinical AI credentialing programs that train healthcare⏎professionals to evaluate, implement, and oversee AI systems effectively. Building workforce expertise is⏎essential to safe deployment, informed adoption decisions, and long term governance of AI enabled care.⏎- -⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎We have observed strong performance from assistive imaging technologies — such as triage tools and⏎incidental but clinically significant finding detection — which deliver meaningful throughput efficiencies⏎and, more importantly, earlier identification of clinical issues that enable lower cost interventions and⏎improved outcomes. Even with 100 percent human over reads, we realized measurable FTE efficiency⏎-⏎while improving detection rates, demonstrating that productivity and quality gains can occur⏎-⏎simultaneously. In contrast, some conversational and agentic tools underperform when workflow⏎integration is immature, resulting in errors or patient friction, such as failed appointment confirmations.⏎Despite these challenges, high impact opportunities remain in acute care decision support,⏎population scale screening models (e.g., mobile low dose CT paired with AI based interpretation), and⏎- -⏎AI augmented follow up pathways that expand capacity without requiring proportional increases in⏎- - -⏎staffing.⏎- -⏎Generative AI agents represent another major area of potential. By automating routine administrative⏎tasks — including communications, prior authorizations, documentation, and scheduling — these agents⏎could reclaim 15–20 hours per week of clinician time, directly addressing burnout and allowing clinicians⏎to focus their time on patient care. Medium term, HHS can play a catalytic role in enabling widespread⏎deployment of generative AI agents across healthcare. If implemented at scale, these tools could automate⏎-⏎1–2 billion hours annually of administrative work, alleviating pressures driven by the workforce crisis while⏎contributing to reductions in administrative spending, currently estimated at $250–400 billion per year. By⏎supporting the integration, evaluation, and responsible scaling of these technologies, HHS can accelerate⏎measurable gains in workforce sustainability, patient experience, and system wide productivity.⏎-⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the⏎most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption hinges on clinical and implementation champions with demonstrated organizational points of⏎contacts (POCs), supported by strong governance, finance, and IT partners who can execute, maintain, and⏎build trust. Influence may be top down or bottom up, but success requires ground team engagement,⏎prioritization across competing initiatives, and resources for ongoing monitoring. Administrative hurdles⏎include insufficient local IT capacity, fragmented governance, unclear funding mechanisms for new⏎⏎responsibilities (monitoring, auditing, validation), and fear that AI could devalue clinician expertise.⏎Aligning roles, incentives, and education — while budgeting for durable oversight — is critical.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability — especially standardized, low-cost Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources⏎(FHIR) based exchanges — would reduce vendor lock in, improve secure data sharing, and enable AI⏎models to operate on consistent data elements across settings. This consistency improves care quality (less⏎test duplication, clearer cross system understanding of patient needs) and lowers integration cost for⏎innovators. Benchmarks should emphasize common data types and metrics that support fair head-to-head⏎evaluations and post market monitoring, ideally coupled with registries capturing system analytics and⏎patient outcomes over time.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients consistently raise several core concerns regarding the use of AI in their care, including privacy and⏎data security — specifically who has access to their health information and whether AI systems might⏎inadvertently expose sensitive data. They also worry about algorithmic bias and the possibility that AI tools⏎could deliver inferior or inequitable care to certain populations. Transparency and explainability are⏎equally important, as patients want to understand why an AI system recommended a particular treatment⏎or course of action. Finally, patients need clarity around accountability: if something goes wrong, who is⏎responsible?⏎To address these concerns, HHS should clarify liability frameworks so patients understand their rights⏎when AI is involved in clinical decision making. Clear, modernized medical malpractice structures are⏎needed to appropriately assign responsibility across AI developers, healthcare organizations, and⏎-⏎individual clinicians. Most fundamentally, HHS should ensure that AI adoption genuinely serves patient⏎interests and improves patient experience and outcomes—rather than primarily benefiting healthcare⏎organizations or technology vendors.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎b. How does literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of clinical⏎care?⏎Priority research should target multicenter, real-world trials that link AI use to patient level outcomes, not⏎only process metrics — today’s literature is niche and organization dependent, with limited ability to⏎report outcomes absent robust analytics. HHS can catalyze shared registries and common analytics that⏎capture clinical efficacy, workflow safety events, and economic endpoints across diverse sites. Cost/benefit⏎studies should reflect both capacity gains (growth, earlier detection) and the ongoing costs of safe⏎operation (human oversight, monitoring, vendor fees), while modeling transfers across stakeholders⏎⏎(providers, payers, patients). Embedding validated predictive models into clinical and drug trials can hasten⏎evidence generation, and education research should tackle AI literacy for clinicians and the public to⏎address perception gaps that impede adoption.⏎Programmatic payment pathways HHS should consider in the future include two complementary payment⏎constructs that would materially accelerate high value adoption:⏎• AI “Meaningful Use” style framework: Define usage requirements, tie incentives to⏎Medicare/Medicaid, and integrate with national standards for safety, transparency, and⏎interoperability—starting where value-based models already track quality (e.g., mortality,⏎readmissions, healthcare-associated infections (HAIs)) to show early, credible wins.⏎• AI Add on Payments in Medicare: Offer time limited supplemental payments (e.g., consistent⏎payments over 3–5 years with step down percentages) for clinically valuable AI, with guardrails⏎against overuse (per episode caps, documentation standards, periodic audits) and a structured⏎transition to permanent codes or bundled models after evidence review. This approach could be⏎set up in a manner that reduces financial barriers, rewards bias mitigation and interoperability,⏎and curbs fraud risk—an issue the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS)/Center for⏎Medicare and Medicaid Innovation (CMMI) leaders have prioritized and that can be preempted⏎with reporting and validated partner mechanisms.⏎CONCLUSION⏎We have a historic opportunity to transform U.S. healthcare through AI driven innovation. After decades⏎of incremental change, we now have an unprecedented chance to reshape the healthcare system using⏎-⏎market driven solutions built on applied AI. In the near term, AI’s greatest value lies not simply in “hours⏎saved,” but in expanding capacity and improving productivity —addressing workforce shortages while⏎-⏎enhancing access, quality, and patient outcomes.⏎With sensible reimbursement structures, clear and proportionate oversight, robust post deployment⏎evaluation, and targeted research and development support, HHS can unlock this value at scale —⏎-⏎especially in high cost, acute care, and imaging intensive settings — without compromising safety, equity,⏎or public trust. Achieving this vision requires national consistency, along with alignment between federal⏎- - -⏎legislators and federal agencies around an incentivizing legal architecture rather than a punitive one. Given⏎the complex patchwork of state level requirements and the current power imbalances between AI vendors⏎and clinical end users, federal guidance should preempt conflicting state activity wherever feasible.⏎-⏎-⏎While other commenters may highlight different priorities, the recommendations above reflect the broad⏎and practical concerns of providers and patients within the healthcare system. Implementing these⏎approaches would deliver substantial benefit across the early and mid stage development of the⏎AI in healthcare ecosystem, supporting innovation while ensuring that adoption remains safe, equitable,⏎-⏎and patient centered.⏎- -⏎-⏎⏎WellSpan Health appreciates the opportunity to provide feedback on opportunities to advance the use⏎of AI in clinical settings to improve patient care. We look forward to working with you to design⏎sustainable policy solutions.⏎Sincerely,⏎Roxanna Gapstur⏎President & CEO⏎WellSpan Health♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0360♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from National Comprehensive Cancer Network, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔National Comprehensive Cancer Network♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW⏎Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎The National Comprehensive Cancer Network® (NCCN®) is pleased to comment on the Office⏎of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care as it relates⏎to NCCN’s mission of defining and advancing quality, effective, equitable, and accessible cancer⏎care and prevention so all people can live better lives. NCCN will focus our comments on the⏎initiatives we’ve undertaken thus far to better understand and safely implement artificial⏎intelligence (AI) in cancer care, as well as on the practical uses of AI in oncology. Additionally,⏎NCCN has included responses to specific questions regarding the limitations and risks of AI and⏎recommendations for appropriate oversight and governance of AI.⏎NCCN Background⏎As an alliance of 33 leading academic cancer centers in the United States (US) that treat⏎hundreds of thousands of patients with cancer annually, NCCN® is a developer of authoritative⏎information regarding cancer prevention, screening, diagnosis, treatment, survivorship care,⏎palliative care, and supportive care that is widely used by clinical professionals and payers alike.⏎The NCCN Clinical Practice Guidelines in Oncology (NCCN Guidelines®) are a comprehensive⏎set of guidelines detailing the sequential management decisions and interventions that currently⏎apply to 97 percent of cancers affecting patients in the US.⏎NCCN imposes strict policies to shield the guidelines development processes from external⏎influences. The “firewall” surrounding the NCCN Guidelines processes includes financial⏎support policies; panel participation and communication policies; guidelines disclosure policies;⏎and policies regarding relationships to NCCN’s other business development activities. The⏎guidelines development is supported exclusively by the Member Institutions’ dues and does not⏎accept any form of industry or other external financial support for the guidelines development⏎program. The NCCN Guidelines are updated at least annually in an evidence-based process⏎integrated with the expert judgment of multidisciplinary panels of expert physicians from NCCN⏎⏎Member Institutions. The NCCN Guidelines are transparent, continuously updated, available free⏎of charge online for non-commercial use and are available through a multitude of health⏎information technology (HIT) vendors.⏎NCCN’s Approach to AI⏎NCCN recognizes both the promise and risk posed by the emergence of AI capabilities in⏎healthcare and has actitvely engaged in work to ensure that AI-driven tools deployed in cancer⏎care does not negatively impact patients. In September 2025, NCCN hosted a policy summit⏎entitled, The Evolving Artificial Intelligence Landscape in Cancer Care. The summit provided⏎an opportunity for a varied group of stakeholders including patients, providers, payers, patient⏎advocacy organizations, policy makers, and industry to thoughtfully discuss diverse perspectives⏎related to the integration of AI into cancer care systems and standards. A subsequent policy⏎report is forthcoming and will be publicly available at nccn.org/policy.⏎Additionally, NCCN is working to identify ways to responsibly optimize and enhance our⏎existing resources for a digital and AI-enabled future. Recognizing the importance of⏎interoperability and standardization for the future, NCCN has been actively working to⏎standardize terminology within our resources and content regarding biomarkers and systemic⏎therapies. The NCCN Guidelines Navigator, based on NCCN’s Clinical Practice Guidelines in⏎Oncology in structured data format, serves as a vital tool for healthcare providers, enabling them⏎to quickly find and understand the best available evidence-based treatment options for patients.⏎The platform's intuitive design facilitates easier treatment decision-making by organizing⏎information based on the latest evidence and expert consensus. As the system evolves, the⏎underlying structured data represents a significant opportunity for enhancement to its⏎functionality, including AI-based functionality, all while maintaining the highest standards of⏎data privacy and security. The system also maintains a strong commitment to transparency and⏎accuracy by ensuring that all generated text references the approved NCCN Guidelines, enabling⏎users to review source documents for validation and access additional background information⏎within the NCCN Guidelines.⏎NCCN is also taking a measured, thoughtful approach to the integration of our content into third-⏎party AI-based tools. NCCN licenses our content for integration into various health information⏎technology-based tools and platforms. To ensure fidelity of the content within these tools,⏎NCCN maintains a rigorous quality assurance process to evaluate the accuracy of how our⏎content is presented. In addition, some of these tools link to our website to ensure easy access to⏎the source information, a critical component of transparent, accurate, responsible AI. NCCN⏎welcomes engagement regarding how best to ensure evidence-based content such as ours can be⏎utilized by AI-based tools in a way that is responsible, secure, transparent, and accurate to the⏎underlying reference sources.⏎Practical Uses of AI in Oncology⏎The use of AI in cancer care is growing as new innovations have emerged for use across the care⏎continuum. These innovations support cancer screening and early detection, help predict⏎prognosis, assist providers with healthcare delivery, and serve as a resource for patients to⏎understand their diagnosis.⏎⏎Diagnosis and Prognosis AI Tools⏎AI systems are increasingly being trained on large collections of medical images and clinical⏎data to help improve cancer diagnostics and early detection. Because AI can identify subtle⏎changes that may be difficult for the human eye to detect, it has the potential to improve⏎accuracy and support more timely diagnoses.1 As the software is exposed to more data over time,⏎its performance can continue to improve. Research studies have shown promising results thus⏎far. A study of more than 91,000 mammograms in the United States and United Kingdom found⏎that an AI system reduced false negatives by about 9.4% compared to radiologist interpretation⏎alone.2 Another large study of roughly 1.2 million mammograms across Germany and the United⏎States found that radiologists working with AI detected about 2.6% more cancers than⏎radiologists alone.3,4 AI is also being explored in other cancer screenings, such as colonoscopy,⏎where studies have found that AI-assisted procedures can increase adenoma detection rates by⏎about 20%.5 While AI is not a replacement for providers, these tools show potential in supporting⏎screening programs and improving early detection.⏎Research has also shown the potential use of AI to go beyond diagnosis and help with predicting⏎prognosis. Researchers at Stanford Medicine developed a multimodal AI model able to⏎incorporate visual and language-based information. After training on 50 million pathology slides⏎medical images and more than 1 billion pathology-related texts, the model outperformed⏎traditional methods in its ability to predict the prognoses of thousands of people with diverse⏎types of cancer. The algorithm helped identify which people with lung or gastroesophageal⏎cancers are likely to benefit from immunotherapy, and to identify people with melanoma who are⏎most likely to experience a recurrence of their cancer.6⏎There is an active research portfolio looking at ways in which AI can improve screening and⏎early detection. While these examples demonstrate the promise of adding AI to human-based⏎screening tools, further research to optimize the use of AI-inclusive tools and how best to⏎integrate them into the clinical decision-making and workflow are needed to before wholesale⏎integration.⏎Clinical Trial Matching⏎AI is also being explored as a tool to improve clinical trial matching in oncology, an area where⏎traditional processes are often time and resource intensive. Identifying eligible patients for trials⏎requires manual review of electronic health records (EHRs), pathology reports, and physician⏎1 Ahn, J. S., Shin, S., Yang, S. A., Park, E. K., Kim, K. H., Cho, S. I., Ock, C. Y., & Kim, S. (2023). Artificial Intelligence in Breast Cancer⏎Diagnosis and Personalized Medicine. Journal of breast cancer, 26(5), 405–435. https://doi.org/10.4048/jbc.2023.26.e45⏎2 McKinney, S.M., Sieniek, M., Godbole, V. et al. International evaluation of an AI system for breast cancer screening. Nature 577, 89–94 (2020).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-019-1799-6⏎3 Conner, K. (2026, January 29). Using AI to detect breast cancer: What we know. Using AI (Artificial Intelligence) to Detect Breast Cancer.⏎https://www.breastcancer.org/screening-testing/artificial-intelligence⏎4 Leibig, C., Brehmer, M., Bunk, S., Byng, D., Pinker, K., & Umutlu, L. (2022). Combining the strengths of Radiologists and AI for Breast⏎Cancer Screening: A retrospective analysis. The Lancet Digital Health, 4(7). https://doi.org/10.1016/s2589-7500(22)00070-x⏎5 Makar, J., Abdelmalak, J., Con, D., Hafeez, B., & Garg, M. (2025). Use of artificial intelligence improves colonoscopy performance in⏎Adenoma Detection: A systematic review and meta-analysis. Gastrointestinal Endoscopy, 101(1). https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gie.2024.08.033⏎6 Xiang, J., Wang, X., Zhang, X., Xi, Y., Eweje, F., Chen, Y., Li, Y., Bergstrom, C., Gopaulchan, M., Kim, T., Yu, K. H., Willens, S., Olguin, F. M.,⏎Nirschl, J. J., Neal, J., Diehn, M., Yang, S., & Li, R. (2025). A vision-language foundation model for precision oncology. Nature, 638(8051), 769–⏎778. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41586-024-08378-w⏎⏎notes among other important patient data. Cancer clinical trials play a critical role in advancing⏎treatment and improving patient outcomes. NCCN Guidelines emphasize the importance of⏎clinical trials and encourage participation when applicable and available. NCCN has also⏎developed patient resources that provide information about clinical trials and offer guidance on⏎how patients can talk to their providers to determine whether a clinical trial may be a suitable⏎option for them.7 Despite the importance of trials for advancing cancer care and most Americans⏎(70%) viewing clinical trial participation favorably, only about 7% of eligible adult cancer⏎patients in the United States ultimately enroll in clinical trials.8,9 Emerging AI tools have been⏎developed to help address this gap by rapidly scanning both structured and unstructured data in⏎EHRs to flag patients who may meet complex eligibility criteria and alert care teams in real time.⏎For example, recent 2026 JCO Clinical Cancer Informatics research article described the⏎development and validation of a semi-automated AI-supported clinical trial matching tool that⏎significantly improved recruitment efficiency and scalability, reduced chart review workload⏎roughly ten-fold and shortened screening time by about 41% for patients who continued to⏎manual review.10 While human oversight remains essential, these tools may help oncology teams⏎connect patients to appropriate trials more quickly and improve trial enrollment. Further⏎evaluation regarding deployment and scaling of these tools into clinical workflows will help⏎harness the potential to improve evaluation and enrollment of patients into cutting-edge trials and⏎advance cancer care.⏎Clinical Notes⏎AI-enabled “ambient listening” and digital scribing tools are increasingly being used in oncology⏎to help document appointments and reduce administrative burden on care teams. These systems⏎use speech recognition and natural language processing to listen to conversations, generate draft⏎clinical notes, and populate structured fields in EHRs, allowing providers to focus more on⏎patient interaction rather than typing during or after visits. In cancer care, where visits are often⏎complex, multidisciplinary, and emotionally burdensome, this type of support can be especially⏎valuable. A multiphase study comparing AI ambient documentation with standard EHR⏎documentation in outpatient settings using simulated physician–patient encounters found that AI-⏎assisted documentation significantly improved note quality and reduced consultation time by⏎about 26.3%.11,12 The study included clinicians and patient actors across several specialties,⏎including oncology, and demonstrated improved completeness, organization, and clarity of⏎clinical notes when AI support was used.11 Evaluations done by health systems have revealed⏎that AI-generated notes often require editing but can substantially reduce documentation time⏎7 Clinical Trials. National Comprehensive Cancer Network. (n.d.-a). https://www.nccn.org/patientresources/patient-resources/additional-⏎resources/clinical-trials⏎8 Kumar, G., Chaudhary, P., Quinn, A., & Su, D. (2022). Barriers for cancer clinical trial enrollment: A qualitative study of the perspectives⏎of healthcare providers. Contemporary clinical trials communications, 28, 100939. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.conctc.2022.100939⏎9 Unger, J. M., Shulman, L. N., Facktor, M. A., Nelson, H., & Fleury, M. E. (2024). National Estimates of the Participation of Patients With⏎Cancer in Clinical Research Studies Based on Commission on Cancer Accreditation Data. Journal of clinical oncology : official journal of the⏎American Society of Clinical Oncology, 42(18), 2139–2148. https://doi.org/10.1200/JCO.23.01030⏎10 Gong, G., Liu, J., Pandya, S., Taborda, C., Wiesendanger, N., Price, N., Byron, W., Coppi, A., Young, P., Wiess, C., Dunning, H., Barganier, C.,⏎Brodeur, R., Fischbach, N., LoRusso, P., Pusztai, L., Kim, S. Y., Rozenblit, M., Cecchini, M., … Kunz, P. L. (2026). Clinical trial patient⏎matching: A real-time, common data model and artificial intelligence–driven system for semiautomated patient prescreening in cancer clinical⏎trials. JCO Clinical Cancer Informatics, (10). https://doi.org/10.1200/cci-25-00262⏎11 Afzal, M., French, K. E., Bilbrey, L. E., & Faruki, A. A. (2025). Artificial Intelligence in the clinic: Creating harmony or just adding noise?⏎American Society of Clinical Oncology Educational Book, 45(3). https://doi.org/10.1200/edbk-25-481490⏎12 Balloch, J., Sridharan, S., Oldham, G., Wray, J., Gough, P., Robinson, R., Sebire, N. J., Khalil, S., Asgari, E., Tan, C., Taylor, A., & Pimenta, D.⏎(2024). Use of an ambient artificial intelligence tool to improve quality of clinical documentation. Future Healthcare Journal, 11(3), 100157.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.fhj.2024.100157⏎⏎and cognitive load, helping address burnout among providers.13 As these tools are rapidly being⏎integrated into clinical workflows, optimizing them to capture clinically-relevant information⏎reflective of the clinical encounter will be important for long-term utility.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎In addition to providing an overview of NCCN’s approach to AI and some of the practical⏎applications of AI in oncology, NCCN has included responses to specific questions outlined in⏎the RFI that were not otherwise addressed above⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Despite the practical use of AI-driven tools in cancer care, these tools can also pose risks to⏎patients if they are developed and used without appropriate guardrails and oversight. These⏎concerns leave many health systems and providers apprehensive about the adoption of AI in⏎clinical care. Specific concerns are detailed below.⏎Data Protection⏎AI tools rely heavily on large volumes of sensitive health data, making privacy and data⏎protection a major concern. While HIPPA establishes safeguards for patient information,⏎uncertainty remains around how data is collected, stored and shared, especially when the data⏎may be accessed by third-party vendors. Major privacy concerns include unauthorized access to⏎patient data through breaches or cyberattacks, as well as the potential misuse of data without⏎adequate transparency or oversight.14,15 As AI tools often rely on cloud-based infrastructure and⏎data sharing across institutions, concerns about security vulnerabilities and lack of oversight⏎have heightened.⏎Transparency and Trust⏎Transparency is also a major concern in the use of AI systems in healthcare. Health systems and⏎providers may lack clear documentation describing how an AI tool was developed or tested⏎because of limited use of model cards and performance reports that would allow users to⏎understand the limitations and risks. Without standardized documentation, it is harder to evaluate⏎safety and effectiveness.16⏎Heightening this challenge, many AI systems function as “black box” models, meaning they⏎produce outputs without clearly explaining how or why a decision or recommendation is⏎13 Olson, K. D., Meeker, D., Troup, M., Barker, T. D., Nguyen, V. H., Manders, J. B., Stults, C. D., Jones, V. G., Shah, S. D., Shah, T., &⏎Schwamm, L. H. (2025). Use of ambient AI scribes to reduce administrative burden and professional burnout. JAMA Network Open, 8(10).⏎https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.34976⏎14 Price, W. N., & Cohen, I. G. (2019). Privacy in the age of Medical Big Data. Nature Medicine, 25(1), 37–43. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-⏎018-0272-7⏎15 Di Palma, G., Scendoni, R., Ferorelli, D., De Benedictis, A., Tambone, V., & De Micco, F. (2025). AI-Induced Cybersecurity Risks in⏎Healthcare: A Narrative Review of Blockchain-Based Solutions Within a Clinical Risk Management Framework. Risk management and⏎healthcare policy, 18, 3479–3497. https://doi.org/10.2147/RMHP.S544523⏎16 Blueprint for Trustworthy AI Implementation Guidance. Coalition for Health AI. (2023, April 4).⏎https://assets.ctfassets.net/7s4afyr9pmov/4AXIWGIlcrjWDaW2ueTaRS/f98e5cb2528187635895cce6ba5ec309/Blueprint_for_Trustworthy_AI.pd⏎f⏎⏎generated.17 This lack of explainability can hinder a provider’s ability to access the reliability of⏎AI-generated responses and integrate them responsibly into decision making. Additional⏎concerns about trust are exacerbated by the risks of AI “hallucinations”. AI hallucinations occur⏎when AI-driven tools perceive patterns or objects that are nonexistent or undetectable to human⏎observers, creating outputs that are nonsensical or altogether inaccurate.18 In healthcare, such⏎errors are particularly concerning, as they may introduce misinformation into workflows and⏎potentially contribute to inappropriate decision making that may negatively impact patients.⏎The lack of transparency with AI-driven tools can undermine trust among patients and providers,⏎complicate clinical decision making, and limit meaningful informed consent if patients and⏎providers cannot understand the basis of AI-generated recommendations. A 2024 Wolters Kluwer⏎Health survey of 100 U.S. physicians revealed that 91% of respondents indicated they need to⏎know that generative AI materials were created by doctors and medical experts before using⏎them in clinical decisions.19 Similarly, 89% reported that they would be more likely to use⏎generative AI in clinical decision making if a vendor was transparent about where information⏎came from, who created it, and how it was sourced. The lack of transparency makes⏎accountability difficult. Without oversight and standards for explainability and transparency, the⏎use of AI in cancer care may challenge existing privacy protection frameworks, patient⏎autonomy, and ethical use of data.⏎Prior Authorization⏎Health plans’ use of AI decision making tools in prior authorization processes raises significant⏎concerns for patients and providers. While insurers often describe prior authorization as a⏎mechanism to control costs by preventing unnecessary, high-cost, or inappropriate care, the⏎process can be burdensome for providers and create delays or barriers to timely, comprehensive⏎care.20 The integration of AI into these systems risks further exacerbates these concerns⏎especially when AI is used to flag or deny requests without sufficient clinical context,⏎transparency, or meaningful human review. For patients, this can translate into delayed treatment,⏎additional appeals, or confusion about how coverage decisions are made. For cancer patients⏎specifically, research demonstrates that even short delays in cancer treatment are associated with⏎increased mortality risk. For example, a one-month delay in cancer surgery can result in a 6⏎percent to 8 percent increase in the risk of death.21 In breast cancer specifically, an eight-week⏎delay in breast cancer surgery increased the risk of death by 17 percent, while a 12-week delay⏎increased the risk by 26 percent.21 These concerns are also reflected in 2025 physician survey⏎data from the American Medical Association, in which 61% of physicians reported being⏎17 Xu, H., & Shuttleworth, K. M. (2024). Medical Artificial Intelligence and the Black Box Problem: A view based on the ethical principle of “Do⏎no harm.” Intelligent Medicine, 4(1), 52–57. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.imed.2023.08.001⏎18 Ibm. (2025, November 17). What are ai hallucinations?. IBM. https://www.ibm.com/think/topics/ai-hallucinations⏎19 Generative AI in Healthcare: Growing Physician Enthusiasm. Wolters Kluwer. (2024, April).⏎https://assets.contenthub.wolterskluwer.com/api/public/content/2231209-gen-ai-executive-brief-pdf?v=09b080b4⏎20 Pickern, J. S. (2025, April 2). Prior authorizations and the adverse impact on continuity of care. AJMC. https://www.ajmc.com/view/prior-⏎authorizations-and-the-adverse-impact-on-continuity-of-care⏎21 Hanna, T. P., King, W. D., Thibodeau, S., Jalink, M., Paulin, G. A., Harvey-Jones, E., O’Sullivan, D. E., Booth, C. M., Sullivan, R., &⏎Aggarwal, A. (2020). Mortality due to cancer treatment delay: Systematic review and meta-analysis. BMJ, m4087.⏎https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.m4087⏎⏎concerned that health plans’ use of AI is increasing prior authorization denials, exacerbating⏎avoidable patient harms, and escalating unnecessary waste now and into the future.22⏎Bias in AI Algorithms⏎Research has shown that AI systems can perpetuate bias at various stages of the development⏎process, including data collection, model training, and real-world implementation.23 Because AI⏎models are trained on existing datasets, they may not adequately represent the full population for⏎whom they are intended. An algorithm built on limited data can perpetuate false assumptions and⏎biases in the medical field, impacting patient care and worsening healthcare disparities.24 For⏎example, AI prediction models used for melanoma diagnosis exhibit worse performance for⏎images of lesions in darker skin tones because the majority of training datasets for these models⏎consist of images from fair-skinned populations.25 This is extremely worrisome because at time⏎of diagnosis, darker-skinned patients already present with later stages of the disease and have⏎lower survival rates than fair-skinned patients.25 Additional sources of bias in AI software can⏎stem from difference in experience and expertise among the individuals involved in developing⏎these systems, as well as environmental bias, empathy bias, and evidence bias, all of which may⏎influence how these tools are designed, trained, and implemented in practice.26⏎Although awareness of bias in AI systems is well documented, it is not being addressed⏎consistently across healthcare. A national analysis conducted by the University of Minnesota⏎School of Public Health examined how hospitals use and evaluate these tools. While 61% of⏎hospitals reported they evaluated their predictive models for accuracy, only 44% conducted⏎similar evaluations for bias.27 These findings underscore the need for stronger, more consistent⏎oversight to ensure that AI used in cancer care and healthcare more broadly is safe, equitable,⏎and effective for all patients.⏎Training and Workforce Preparedness⏎As AI tools are increasingly integrated into healthcare systems, it is essential that the healthcare⏎workforce receives appropriate training to ensure these tools are used safely and effectively.⏎Providers must be trained to not only operate AI tools but must also understand how to interpret⏎AI- generated outputs and identify their limitations. Without comprehensive and ongoing training⏎there are risks of misuse, overreliance, or inappropriate application of AI tools in patient care.⏎Despite these risks, workforce training has not kept pace with the rapid implementation of AI⏎across healthcare settings. A systematic literature review examining barriers to successful⏎implementation of AI-driven technologies in healthcare identified 92 relevant studies, more than⏎one-third (33) of the which cited lack of training as a concern or challenge to adoption among⏎22 AMA State Advocacy Impact Report. American Medical Association. (2026, January 8). https://www.ama-assn.org/health-care-advocacy/state-⏎advocacy/ama-state-advocacy-impact-report⏎23 Cross, J. L., Choma, M. A., & Onofrey, J. A. (2024). Bias in medical AI: Implications for clinical decision-making. PLOS digital health, 3(11),⏎e0000651. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0000651⏎24 Hurd, T. C., Cobb-Payton, F., & Hood, D. B. (2024). Targeting machine learning and artificial intelligence algorithms in health care to reduce⏎bias and improve population health. The Milbank Quarterly, 102(3), 577–604. https://doi.org/10.1111/1468-0009.12712⏎25 Adamson AS, Smith A. Machine Learning and Health Care Disparities in Dermatology. JAMA Dermatol. 2018;154(11):1247–1248.⏎doi:10.1001/jamadermatol.2018.2348⏎26 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. (2024b, August 22). Health equity and ethical considerations in using Artificial Intelligence in⏎public health and medicine. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. https://www.cdc.gov/pcd/issues/2024/24_0245.htm⏎27 Nong, P., Adler-Milstein, J., Apathy, N. C., Holmgren, A. J., & Everson, J. (2025). Current use and evaluation of artificial intelligence and⏎predictive models in US hospitals. Health Affairs, 44(1), 90–98. https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2024.00842⏎⏎healthcare providers.28 Similarly, a study of 501 U.S clinicians found that only 42% of them⏎reported a sense of preparedness for AI implementation and 50% acknowledged the potential⏎need for retraining to effectively utilize generative AI.29 As we identify ways to accelerate⏎innovation, it is critically important to ensure users of these systems are adequately prepared and⏎competent to prevent unintended harm to patients. These findings underscore a gap between the⏎technological advancements of AI and workforce preparedness. As we seek ways to accelerate⏎innovation and expand the use of AI in oncology and other areas of care, equal attention must be⏎given to ensuring providers are adequately trained and supported.⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎Engaging with and utilizing resources from evidence-based organizations like NCCN should be a⏎priority for HHS to advance innovative and effective AI use in clinical care. NCCN Guidelines®⏎and Library of Compendia products help ensure access to appropriate care, clinical decision-⏎making, and assessment of quality improvement initiatives. The NCCN Drugs & Biologics⏎Compendium (NCCN Compendium®) has been recognized by CMS and clinical professionals in⏎the commercial payer setting since 2008 as an evidence-based reference for establishment of⏎coverage policy and coverage decisions regarding off-label use of anticancer and cancer-related⏎medications. NCCN was recognized by CMS in 2016 and renewed in 2021 as a qualified⏎Provider Led Entity (PLE) for the Medicare Appropriate Use Criteria (AUC) Program for the⏎development of AUC and the establishment of policy and decision-making for diagnostic⏎imaging in patients with cancer.⏎These recognitions underscore the reliability and rigor of NCCN Guidelines and resources as⏎authoritative tools for cancer care and prevention. As private sector developers increasingly build⏎AI-driven tools for clinical care, it is imperative that the data and content used to train and⏎inform these systems are grounded in high-quality evidence-based resources. Engaging with⏎trusted, evidence-based organizations like NCCN can help ensure that AI clinical resources⏎deliver accurate, trustworthy, reliable information and improve patient outcomes.⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have.⏎Patients have begun turning to AI tools to better understand medical information, prepare for⏎appointments, and generate questions to discuss with their providers. Conversational AI systems⏎can explain diagnoses, treatments, and test results in plain language and can engage in extended⏎dialogue, giving patients more time to process complex information and identify concerns they⏎want to raise with providers. Surveys suggest this use is growing. A Kaiser Family Foundation⏎28 Abdelwanis, M., Simsekler, M. C. E., Gabor, A. F., Sleptchenko, A., & Omar, M. (2026). Artificial intelligence adoption challenges from⏎healthcare providers’ perspectives: A comprehensive review and future directions. Safety Science, 193, 107028.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssci.2025.107028⏎29 ClinicAI Companions Report: Clinician’s perspective on Generative AI and the future of healthcare. QuestionPro.⏎(2024). https://www.questionpro.com/ebook/clinicai-companions-report/⏎⏎survey reported that about one in six adults (17%) uses AI chatbots at least monthly for health⏎advice or information, and an April 2025 Annenberg Public Policy Center health survey of more⏎than 1,600 U.S. adults found that nearly two-thirds (63%) viewed AI-generated health⏎information as somewhat or very reliable.30,31 While these technologies are not a substitute for⏎professional medical advice and can sometimes produce incomplete or inaccurate information,⏎they may help patients feel more informed and prepared more effectively for clinical encounters.⏎At the same time, increased patient use does not always necessarily translate into broad⏎confidence in AI’s role within healthcare delivery. Similar to providers, many patients remain⏎cautious about the use of AI in healthcare delivery. A study published in JAMA Network found⏎that 65.8% of surveyed US adults reported low trust in their health care system to use AI⏎responsibly and 57.7% expressed low trust that their health care system would make sure an AI⏎tool would not harm them.32 Similarly, a national survey of a total of 926 respondents across the⏎US examining the public perception of the use of AI in diagnosis and treatment found⏎respondents were very concerned or somewhat concerned about AI’s unintended consequences,⏎including misdiagnosis (91.5%), privacy breaches (70.8%), less time with clinicians (69.6%),⏎and higher health care costs (68.4%)33 These concerns continue to highlight the need for⏎appropriate oversight, transparency, and guardrails that consider patients to ensure safe⏎integration of AI in healthcare.⏎Recommendations⏎NCCN urges ASTP/ONC to consider mechanisms for appropriate regulation of AI within⏎healthcare, which poses unique opportunities and risks for patient care, patient safety, and patient⏎outcomes. NCCN notes that the National Academy of Medicine developed an AI Code of⏎Conduct to provide a unifying framework to guide responsible, equitable, and human-centered⏎AI. The guiding principles are as follows:34⏎1. Engaged- Center AI development on people’s needs, preferences, and goals throughout⏎its lifecycle.⏎2. Safe- Maintain strict oversight to prevent and address any potential harm from AI in⏎health and medicine.⏎3. Effective- Ensure AI consistently improves health and well-being while upholding ethical⏎standards.⏎4. Equitable- Demonstrate fairness in AI design, access, and outcomes, minimizing bias⏎and risk.⏎30 Presiado, M., Hamel, L., Lopes, L., & Montero, A. (2025, August 13). KFF Health Misinformation Tracking Poll: Artificial Intelligence and⏎Health Information. KFF. https://www.kff.org/public-opinion/kff-health-misinformation-tracking-poll-artificial-intelligence-and-health-⏎information/#cdc7ee85-54db-4b29-aa9b-625afeb4c03c⏎31 Many in U.S. consider AI-generated health information useful and reliable. The Annenberg Public Policy Center of the University of⏎Pennsylvania. (2025, October 21). https://www.annenbergpublicpolicycenter.org/many-in-u-s-consider-ai-generated-health-information-useful-⏎and-reliable/⏎32 Nong, P., & Platt, J. (2025). Patients' Trust in Health Systems to Use Artificial Intelligence. JAMA network open, 8(2), e2460628.⏎https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.60628⏎33 Khullar, D., Casalino, L. P., Qian, Y., Lu, Y., Krumholz, H. M., & Aneja, S. (2022). Perspectives of Patients About Artificial Intelligence in⏎Health Care. JAMA network open, 5(5), e2210309. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2022.10309⏎34 Health Care Artificial Intelligence Code of conduct. NAM. (2025, December 1). https://nam.edu/our-work/programs/leadership-⏎consortium/health-care-artificial-intelligence-code-of-conduct/⏎⏎5. Efficient- Use AI to improve health outcomes while reducing resource use and⏎environmental impact.⏎6. Accessible- Make every stage of AI development and governance open and inclusive for⏎all stakeholders.⏎7. Transparent- Share clear, understandable information about how AI works, performs,⏎and impact outcomes.⏎8. Secure- Protect health data through validated privacy and security measures that enable⏎safe, continuous learning.⏎9. Adaptive- Continuously monitor and refine AI systems to drive ongoing learning and⏎improvement in health and science.⏎NCCN agrees with and supports these principles and believes they offer important guidance for⏎ASTP/ONC as it considers regulations related to healthcare-related applications of AI. Without⏎comprehensive federal legislation or regulation, responsibility for oversight is left to states, local⏎governments, and individual health systems. This fragmented approach can be costly and⏎resource-intensive, and has resulted in uneven AI implementation across the country, with lower⏎access rates in areas that already exhibit high healthcare access needs and where existing, proven⏎AI tools could be implemented.35 As noted in the Executive Order titled Ensuring A National⏎Policy Framework For Artificial Intelligence, a national standard and framework is needed. As⏎this framework is developed, NCCN strongly encourages ASTP/ONC to establish clear standards⏎that support innovation while also prioritizing safety, transparency, and accountability, so that AI⏎is developed responsibly without causing harm to patients. Further, engagement with healthcare⏎stakeholder organizations across the spectrum will be critical to inform effective policies that⏎serve the best interests of patients.⏎NCCN appreciates the opportunity to comment on ASTP/ONC Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care. NCCN is⏎happy to serve as a resource and looks forward to working together to advance access to⏎equitable, high-quality cancer care.⏎Sincerely,⏎Crystal S. Denlinger, MD, FACP⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎National Comprehensive Cancer Network⏎denlinger@nccn.org⏎215.690.0300⏎35 Hwang, YM., Ng, M.Y., Pillai, M. et al. The landscape of AI implementation in US hospitals. Nat. Health 1, 99–112 (2026).⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s44360-025-00016-7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0361♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Clark♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔American Society of Anesthesiologists♔♔♔♔♔♔My organization submitted formal comments on the consent and equity issues in this RFI, and I want to be clear that those concerns are real and I stand behind them. But I have to be honest about something that keeps nagging at me every time I sit down to write about AI adoption in healthcare.⏎⏎My mother is 81 years old and lives outside Minot, North Dakota. She is diabetic. She pays somewhere around $400 a month for insulin, and that number has barely budged in years despite everything we were told was going to change. Now HHS is putting together this big initiative about AI in clinical settings, and I just keep wondering -- where is that same energy when it comes to drug pricing? I'm not saying AI isn't worth discussing. It is. But it is genuinely hard to get excited about the department accelerating technology adoption when the people who need care the most can't afford the medication they already have.⏎⏎I realize this isn't exactly what the RFI is asking about. I do. And I'll get to the consent issues in a moment because those matter too.⏎⏎The survey data from the ADA's Health Policy Institute -- the 2023 one that covered about 8,500 dentists -- showed that 38% of them had patients who couldn't meaningfully engage with AI-assisted portals because of digital literacy or access issues. That is not a small number. And when you look at where this problem is concentrated, it's in federally qualified health centers, rural practices, low-income populations. The exact same people who are already rationing insulin because they can't afford it.⏎⏎So what I'd ask HHS to focus on, specifically in response to the question about regulatory actions that could support equitable AI implementation: any accelerated adoption framework needs to address informed consent standards before deployment, not after. The 24% of dentists in that same survey who said they had received no guidance on how to obtain consent when AI contributes to a clinical recommendation -- that gap shouldn't still exist two or three years into widespread AI tool deployment. Guidance needs to come first.⏎⏎I keep coming back to my mother though. She has a patient portal. She technically has access to whatever AI scheduling tools her clinic uses. She also has a flip phone and doesn't fully understand why her insulin costs what it does, and nobody at HHS has explained that to her either.⏎⏎I submitted more detailed written comments through ASA's formal process on March 14th of this year. This comment is more personal and I'm not sure it's going to land the way I want it to, but I felt like the inbox was there and I should use it.⏎⏎The consent guidance issue is the one I'd prioritize if the department can only move on one thing in the near term. The current situation where individual health systems are each developing their own consent language for AI-assisted recommendations is creating real inconsistency and patients have no way of knowing what they're agreeing to from one facility to the next.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0362♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from LeadingAge, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔LeadingAge♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the NaFonal Coordinator for Health⏎InformaFon Technology⏎AIn: Request for InformaFon: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building,⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dear Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary Posnack:⏎We thank you for the opportunity to provide input into the “Request for InformaFon: AcceleraFng the⏎AdopFon and Use of ArFficial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care,” which seeks to “establish a forward-⏎leaning, industry supporFve, and secure approach to accelerate the adopFon and use of AI as part of⏎clinical care.”⏎To provide some context for our comments, let us share a little about LeadingAge. Our mission is to be⏎the trusted voice for aging. We represent more than 5,400 nonprofit aging services providers and other⏎mission-driven organizations that touch millions of lives every day. Alongside our members and 36⏎partners in 41 states, we use advocacy, education, applied research, and community-building to make⏎America a better place to grow old. Our membership encompasses the entire continuum of aging⏎services, including skilled nursing, assisted living, memory care, affordable housing, retirement⏎communities, adult day programs, community-based services, hospice, and home-based care. We⏎bring together the most inventive minds in the field to lead and innovate solutions that support older⏎adults wherever they call home. For more information visit leadingage.org.⏎We continue to believe that artificial intelligence (AI) demonstrates considerable potential for all of us in⏎health care to work more efficiently. However, AI is only as good as its inputs. To the degree that these⏎mechanisms are used for clinical care decision making, we think it is important to ensure AI does not⏎serve as a barrier to medically necessary services and that policies protect beneficiaries. Specifically, all⏎decisions and data derived from AI tools (including algorithms, machine learning, reports that average a⏎patient’s expected experience and needs based upon an average patient, etc.) are required to be⏎reviewed by a qualified health professional prior to denials of care or terminations of care and services.⏎Additionally,⏎⏎As AI evolves, we are still learning its capacity and possible applications. Therefore, LeadingAge’s⏎recommendations are based upon a set of principles for what we see as the responsible, appropriate⏎use of AI that also protects older adults from possible misuse.⏎AI use in health care should:⏎• Reduce Administrative Burden and Free Up Clinician Time⏎• Aide in Clinical Documentation and Information Synthesis⏎• Support Person-Centered Care Planning⏎• Expedite (Not Deny) Care and Services⏎• Improve Care Transitions and Post-Acute Coordination⏎• Simplify and Inform Beneficiary Decisions Related to their Coverage and Treatment Options⏎• Enhance CMS Clinical Oversight and Ensure Regulatory Compliance⏎Governed by these principles, we share our thoughts below about the questions posed in the CMS RFI.⏎Barriers to Innovation and Adoption⏎The RFI asks what the biggest barriers are to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care. From the perspective of LeadingAge, a major barrier to private sector innovation in⏎artificial intelligence (AI) for health care is the limited adoption capacity and readiness across the full⏎continuum of care—particularly within aging services settings such as nursing homes, assisted living, and⏎other long-term services and supports (LTSS) providers. Successful AI innovation and deployment cannot be⏎limited to physicians and hospitals; it must encompass all settings where Medicare beneficiaries receive care.⏎However, implementation strategies often fail to account for the significant variation in digital maturity,⏎infrastructure, and resources across provider types.⏎Historical Underinvestment in Aging Services Health IT Infrastructure⏎A foundational barrier is the historical exclusion of most long-term care and aging services providers⏎from federal health IT incentive programs. Under the HITECH Act, the Medicare and Medicaid EHR⏎Incentive Programs (“Meaningful Use”) focused primarily on eligible hospitals and eligible professionals.⏎Nursing homes and most LTSS providers were not eligible for these incentives and therefore did not⏎benefit from the substantial federal investments that accelerated electronic health record adoption,⏎interoperability, and digital workflow standardization in acute and ambulatory care.⏎This legacy underinvestment continues to constrain AI readiness today. Many aging services providers⏎operate with less mature health IT systems, limited interoperability, inconsistent data standards, and⏎fewer digitally enabled workflows. Most AI tools assume modern data architecture, clean and⏎representative datasets, standardized vocabularies, and seamless integration with EHRs and payer⏎systems—conditions that are not consistently present in aging services settings due to this lack of prior⏎federal investment. As a result, private sector developers face a smaller, more fragmented market,⏎which reduces incentives to design AI solutions tailored to the needs of aging services providers.⏎⏎Resource constraints compound these challenges. Nonprofit aging services providers oden operate with⏎thin margins, limited access to capital, and small IT teams. EvaluaFng AI tools, managing cybersecurity⏎risk, negoFaFng HIT vendor contracts, and supporFng implementaFon requires capacity and financial⏎resources that many aging service organizaFons lack. Workforce shortages and high turnover further⏎reduce tolerance for experimentaFon, even when AI could meaningfully reduce administraFve burden.⏎Finally, misaligned reimbursement and incentive structures limit adoption. Many AI tools deliver value⏎through workforce efficiency, consistency, and prevention rather than directly reimbursable clinical⏎outcomes. When these benefits are not recognized or rewarded, providers struggle to justify⏎investment, which in turn discourages private sector developers from tailoring AI solutions to aging⏎services environments.⏎Together, the historical exclusion of long-term care providers from federal health IT incenFves, ongoing⏎infrastructure gaps, regulatory uncertainty, and resource constraints limit AI adopFon in aging services.⏎These adopFon barriers reduce real-world implementaFon opportuniFes, slowing feedback loops and⏎constraining private sector innovaFon in AI for clinical care.⏎Aging service provider engagement is essenFal to be successful in leveraging AI tools for Medicare⏎beneficiaries, as they have significant, oden daily interacFons with the older adults they serve, and⏎therefore capture criFcal data that could be uFlized by AI tools to diagnose and recommend opFmal⏎treatments or intervenFons that improve the individual’s outcomes.⏎Lack of Data Standardiza=on and Interoperability⏎The lack of standardized data across payers, EHR vendors, and care settings further limits innovation and⏎adoption. Fragmented data standards reduce data volume and usability, which are essential to training,⏎validating, and deploying effective AI tools. Greater alignment around interoperability standards—⏎particularly for Long Term Post-Acute Care (LT-PAC) providers—would create the scale and consistency⏎necessary to incentivize health IT vendors to develop more advanced, interoperable AI solutions for⏎aging services settings.⏎Regulatory Uncertainty and Absence of Federal Guardrails⏎Regulatory uncertainty is another significant barrier. Providers and developers lack clear federal⏎standards or guardrails governing AI use in health care broadly, and in clinical decision-making⏎specifically. There is limited guidance on issues such as:⏎• Bias and representaFveness of datasets being used for AI tools⏎• Clinical accuracy expectaFons, benchmarks, and validaFon requirements⏎• DefiniFons of common AI terminology and vocabulary⏎• Accepted and prohibited uses of AI in clinical care⏎• The role of AI, if any, in medical necessity determinaFons⏎⏎• Alignment with evidence-based clinical guidelines and expectaFons for tool updates⏎• Beneficiary protecFons related to data use, transparency, appeals, and human oversight⏎In highly regulated environments such as nursing homes, this uncertainty increases perceived⏎compliance and legal risk and discourages adoption—even for non-medical device AI tools focused on⏎documentation assistance, analytics, care planning support, or workflow optimization.⏎Clear federal guardrails are needed to establish that AI tools must comply with existing health care⏎regulatory and legal requirements; that AI cannot independently deny, terminate, or reduce care; and⏎that any AI-informed determinations must be subject to review by a qualified human health care⏎professional. Standards must also ensure that all determinations and denials remain fully appealable by⏎the beneficiary, their family, and/or their treating providers.⏎Unclear Oversight and Enforcement Roles⏎Uncertainty regarding which federal entities have oversight responsibility further slows innovation.⏎Clarity is needed on the respective roles of CMS, Office of the National Coordinator (ONC), Food & Drug⏎Administration (FDA), and other agencies in setting standards, overseeing development, and enforcing⏎appropriate use. For example, FDA may be best positioned to oversee AI tools that function as medical⏎devices, while CMS may play a central role in establishing provider-facing requirements related to use,⏎documentation, beneficiary protections, and appeals processes. Without defined roles and coordinated⏎oversight, both providers and developers face increased uncertainty and risk.⏎Resource Constraints and Workforce Challenges⏎Resource limitations compound these challenges. Many aging services providers—particularly nonprofit⏎organizations—operate on thin margins, have limited access to capital, and maintain small IT and⏎compliance teams. Evaluating AI tools, negotiating vendor contracts, managing cybersecurity risk,⏎training staff, and supporting implementation requires time and expertise that many organizations lack.⏎Workforce shortages and high staff turnover further reduce tolerance for experimentation, even when⏎AI tools could meaningfully reduce administrative burden and improve consistency of care.⏎Misaligned reimbursement and incentive structures also inhibit adoption. Many AI tools deliver value⏎through efficiency, prevention, and workforce support rather than directly reimbursable clinical services.⏎When these benefits are not recognized or rewarded, providers struggle to justify investment, which in⏎turn discourages private sector developers from entering or remaining in the aging services market.⏎Need for Inclusive Implementa=on and Provider Support⏎Aging services provider engagement is essential to successful AI innovation for Medicare beneficiaries.⏎These providers have frequent—often daily—interactions with older adults and capture critical⏎longitudinal data that could be leveraged by AI tools to support diagnosis, care planning, and⏎⏎interventions that improve outcomes. Excluding these settings from AI strategies limits real-world⏎testing, feedback loops, and scalability, ultimately constraining private sector innovation.⏎Recommenda=ons to Reduce Barriers⏎To address these barriers and enable responsible AI innovation and adoption, LeadingAge recommends:⏎• Establishing federal standards and guardrails for AI use in health care, parFcularly for clinical⏎decision support, that address bias, data adequacy, transparency, human oversight, beneficiary⏎protecFons, and appeal rights.⏎• Clarifying federal oversight roles across CMS, ONC, FDA, and other agencies to support⏎consistent development, deployment, and enforcement.⏎• UpdaFng provider regulaFons—including licensure requirements and condiFons of⏎parFcipaFon—to explicitly permit appropriate AI use, reinforce that human clinical judgment⏎supersedes AI outputs, and ensure paFent-centered decision-making is retained.⏎• Providing targeted financial support or add-on payments for LT-PAC and aging services providers⏎to invest in health IT infrastructure, interoperability, and AI-enabled tools.⏎• Phasing in compliance with interoperability and data standards (e.g., FHIR-based requirements)⏎for aging services providers to reflect current readiness levels.⏎• SupporFng provider educaFon and training, including CMS-led technical assistance or⏎convenings focused on emerging technologies, benefits, and responsible deployment.⏎Together, historical underinvestment, infrastructure gaps, regulatory uncertainty, resource constraints,⏎and misaligned incentives significantly limit AI adoption in aging services. Addressing these barriers is⏎critical to ensuring that private sector AI innovation reaches all care settings and equitably benefits⏎Medicare beneficiaries.⏎Regulatory, payment policy or programma8c design Incen8ves to encourage⏎effec8ve use of AI in clinical care.⏎LeadingAge encourages HHS to prioriFze regulatory and programmaFc changes that address the⏎foundaFonal readiness gap facing nursing homes and other aging services providers. As recent reporFng⏎has shown, nursing homes lag significantly behind other health care sejngs in AI adopFon, largely due⏎to underinvestment in digital infrastructure, limited interoperability, and uncertainty about how exisFng⏎regulaFons apply to emerging technologies. While ONC health IT cerFficaFon requirements have helped⏎advance digital readiness in other health care segments, those requirements are largely not applicable to⏎aging services sejngs, leaving many providers without a clear pathway to adopt cerFfied, interoperable⏎technology. HHS should consider creaFng a new cerFficaFon or qualificaFon program tailored to long-⏎⏎term care and senior living, paired with upfront financial support, to help providers adopt technologies⏎that are designed to support safe and effecFve AI use.⏎Payment policy must also beIer align with how AI delivers value in aging services. Many AI tools⏎generate benefits through reduced administraFve burden, improved workforce efficiency, and earlier⏎idenFficaFon of clinical and operaFonal risk rather than directly reimbursable clinical services. Without⏎financial incenFves that recognize these gains, providers struggle to jusFfy investment, and private-⏎sector developers have limited incenFve to tailor soluFons for long-term care and senior living sejngs.⏎HHS should prioriFze demonstraFon programs and payment models that provide financial incenFves to⏎organizaFons that adopt cerFfied or qualified technology, reward measurable workforce and care⏎coordinaFon improvements, and offset implementaFon costs. Targeted financial incenFves, coupled with⏎programmaFc support and technical assistance, will be essenFal to acceleraFng responsible AI adopFon⏎in nursing homes and other aging services sejngs.⏎Liability, Privacy, and Accountability Challenges in Deploying Non-Medical AI Tools⏎In our view, non-medical device AI presents disFnct legal and implementaFon challenges for aging⏎services providers, parFcularly around accountability, liability, contracFng, and operaFonal governance.⏎Tools such as documentaFon assistance, predicFve analyFcs, workflow opFmizaFon, and care planning⏎support are increasingly used in clinical environments but oden fall outside tradiFonal regulatory⏎categories. Providers are frequently unclear about where responsibility lies when AI supports decision-⏎making but does not replace clinical judgment. This uncertainty is compounded by vendor contracts that⏎may shid risk disproporFonately to providers, especially nonprofit organizaFons with limited legal and⏎technical resources.⏎HHS can play an important role by establishing clear, principle-based guidance for the responsible use of⏎non-medical device AI in clinical care. This guidance should clarify expectaFons related to human⏎oversight, accountability, transparency, and documentaFon, while avoiding prescripFve requirements⏎that could sFfle innovaFon or exclude smaller providers. HHS can also help by promoFng model⏎contractual terms, implementaFon best pracFces, and shared governance frameworks that support safe⏎deployment in real-world care sejngs. By focusing on clarity and consistency rather than new layers of⏎regulaFon, HHS can reduce adopFon risk for aging services providers while enabling responsible⏎innovaFon across the sector.⏎Other Potential Opportunities for AI in Clinical Care⏎The following offer some additional detail where we see opportunities for AI in clinical care—particularly⏎when AI is used to support clinicians, strengthen care delivery, and improve beneficiary experience,⏎rather than to ration care or replace professional judgment.⏎1. Support Clinical Decision Making and Reduce Administra:ve Burden⏎AI has the potenFal to transform clinical care by automaFng rouFne administraFve tasks and⏎providing valuable decision support to clinicians. When deployed thoughlully, AI tools can⏎⏎handle Fme-consuming duFes such as charFng, documentaFon, workflow opFmizaFon, and⏎care planning, enabling clinicians to focus more on direct paFent care and professional⏎judgment. AddiFonally, AI can synthesize and summarize complex clinical informaFon across⏎records, surface relevant insights, and highlight risks and trends, especially for paFents with⏎mulFple chronic condiFons or frequent care transiFons. These funcFons not only reduce⏎paperwork and streamline processes but also provide clinicians with advisory support that⏎supplements, rather than replaces, their experFse.⏎• Automate documentaFon, charFng, and workflow processes to reduce paperwork⏎• Synthesize and summarize complex clinical informaFon for clinicians⏎• Support interdisciplinary care planning and coordinaFon⏎• Facilitate smoother care transiFons across hospital, post-acute, and community sejngs⏎• Surface relevant informaFon and idenFfy risks or trends for complex paFents⏎The most effecFve use of AI in clinical sejngs is to support clinicians and care teams, reduce⏎non-clinical workload, and improve coordinaFon. AI could operate alongside clinicians, informing⏎their decisions and helping them understand the full clinical picture, but must not subsFtute for⏎clinical judgment or make independent care decisions. All AI-derived insights should remain⏎subject to qualified health professional review, ensuring that care remains individualized and⏎grounded in professional experFse, parFcularly in high-stakes environments such as post-acute⏎and long-term care. By leveraging AI as a supporFve tool, the healthcare workforce can address⏎shortages, boost efficiency, and enhance paFent care without compromising the essenFal⏎human element of clinical decision making.⏎2. Expedite (Not Deny) Prior Authorization and Coverage Decisions⏎In this same vein, AI could also be used to streamline uFlizaFon management and coverage⏎decisions by managed care plans expediFng prior authorizaFons and concurrent reviews. More⏎specifically, AI could be used to idenFfy and extract required documentaFon in an EHR that⏎supports a prior authorizaFon request. In addiFon, CMS could use AI to ensure Medicare⏎Advantage plans are complying with Medicare regulaFons when making coverage decisions. MA⏎plans, in turn, could use AI to surface relevant clinical criteria that apply to a specific coverage⏎determinaFon request. Taking these steps could not only expedite approvals of care but also⏎ensure regulatory compliance and accountability and ensure beneficiaries/paFents have equal⏎access to medically necessary care.⏎However, we strongly recommend that AI be prohibited from being used to deny, reduce, or⏎terminate medically necessary care or to override individualized clinical assessments—⏎particularly in Medicare Advantage.⏎AI can reduce delays in care by accelerating administrative processes when it supports approvals⏎and respects clinician judgment.⏎⏎3. Support Person-Centered Care Planning⏎LeadingAge sees potential for AI to support care planning and interdisciplinary coordination,⏎including identifying trends, risks, and care needs over time—so long as outputs are advisory⏎and individualized, not based on population averages or rigid algorithms. AI can help care teams⏎anticipate needs, coordinate services, and tailor care plans for residents and patients with⏎complex, evolving conditions.⏎4. Improving Care Transitions and Post-Acute Coordination⏎LeadingAge supports AI tools that help improve care transitions, particularly from hospitals to⏎post-acute settings such as skilled nursing, home health, PACE, and other long-term services and⏎supports. This includes identifying care needs, anticipating service requirements, and improving⏎information flow across settings.⏎AI can reduce avoidable readmissions, delays, and disruptions in care by improving coordination⏎across the continuum.⏎5. Beneficiary-Facing Decision Support in Clinical Contexts⏎LeadingAge has publicly supported the concept of CMS-operated, neutral, beneficiary-facing AI⏎decision-support tools that help individuals understand their care options, including Medicare⏎Advantage plan selection, post-acute access, and service availability. While not making clinical⏎decisions, these tools support informed, person-centered choices that affect downstream care.⏎Our aging services providers have observed the challenges older adults face in annually⏎evaluating how they receive their Medicare benefits (e.g. traditional Medicare, Medicare⏎Advantage, Medigap, Accountable Care Organizations, etc.). We believe there may be an⏎opportunity to include an AI tool that goes beyond just evaluating MA plan options but instead⏎encompasses the full array of options for receiving their Medicare benefits. Such a tool could ask⏎a series of questions (prompts) where the beneficiary could list their current providers, their⏎geography, their medications, what services or treatments they are currently receiving and what⏎factors are most important in how they receive their Medicare benefits (e.g. total potential out of⏎pocket costs based upon their current health needs, would they be willing to trade lower costs if⏎they had to obtain prior authorization for services or be limited to a smaller list of providers from⏎whom they could receive their care). The tool could generate a comparison chart that looks at⏎predicted monthly and total out of pocket costs including medical and prescription drug costs,⏎services covered (e.g. list supplemental benefits) and if not covered, average cost to obtain the⏎comparable service (e.g. dental), identify if any of their providers would not be covered under the⏎option.⏎AI also could generate plain-language explanations for patients to better understand a diagnosis⏎and care options, help identify resources they may want to explore (e.g. palliative care), and⏎⏎understand the impacts a diagnosis may have on their quality of life and health trajectory. It⏎could also assist them in generating questions they may wish to ask their health care⏎professional.⏎6. Supporting CMS Clinical Oversight and Compliance⏎LeadingAge supports CMS using AI to review Medicare Advantage plan compliance with Medicare⏎coverage rules, internal plan criteria, and utilization management practices—especially to ensure⏎that clinical standards are followed and beneficiaries receive required services.⏎We believe AI can strengthen clinical oversight and enforcement without increasing burden on⏎providers and potentially use CMS oversight resources more cost effectively while preserving⏎appropriate access to medically necessary care. AI tools could be used by CMS to review MA plan⏎coverage determinations through the lens of compliance with the myriad Medicare service⏎coverage regulations with which plans must comply. Such an analysis could determine the⏎appropriateness of MA plan internal coverage criteria and their application, identify prior⏎authorization denial frequency, trends and appropriateness, and test the accuracy of MA plan AI⏎tools used in coverage determinations. Additionally, there may be opportunities to use AI to⏎analyze network adequacy not only for compliance with time & distance standards but also to⏎identify errors in provider directories, assess provider capacity to accept patients, and assess the⏎quality of the network providers.⏎From LeadingAge’s perspective, AI holds meaningful promise in clinical care when it is:⏎• Supportive, not determinative⏎• Person-centered, not average-based⏎• Transparent, explainable, and appealable⏎• Focused on efficiency, access, and quality—not cost avoidance⏎Nonetheless, even with all of these possibilities for AI use in healthcare, we remain concerned about its⏎potential for misuse without appropriate guardrails.⏎AI guardrails must be established that are appropriate for the health care sector.⏎1. AI tools should be tested, validated, and transparent. Federal regulations should be established⏎to ensure when AI is used in clinical care that the data being used is robust and relevant to the⏎task for which it is being used. AI outputs should be proven to be accurate 95% of the time or⏎more, with a minimal error rate. We need greater transparency into the inputs being used by AI⏎tools to ensure bias is removed, data is valid and outputs are error free.⏎2. Human review must be part of the workflow. Qualified health care professionals must be part⏎of the workflow in reviewing AI-influenced decisions. Human oversight should not be viewed as⏎a barrier to AI adoption—it is essential to preventing harm, reducing appeals, and sustaining⏎⏎trust in AI-enabled clinical workflows and decisions.⏎3. AI-influenced decisions must also be appealable and those that use AI tools must be⏎accountable. Beneficiaries/patients, their families or representatives and provider should be⏎informed of their right to appeal and how to appeal and AI-influenced decision by the entity⏎using the AI tool. Those who develop and/or deploy AI tools must be accountable for AI outputs⏎and errors. This means they must inform impacted individuals of their right of appeal, how to⏎appeal and must be provided a reason for a denial that is accompanied by information about⏎what data led to the denial and other transparency about the AI tools being used. This⏎information is essential for effective appeals and understanding.⏎LeadingAge shares the AdministraFon’s enthusiasm about the potenFal uses of AI but also believe for AI⏎adopFon to be embraced we must also ensure that our policies related to its use contain the necessary⏎guardrails to prevent its fraudulent use and prevent harm to the older adult populaFon we serve. We⏎appreciate this opportunity to share the perspecFve of aging services provider to help inform future AI⏎policies related to clinical care. Please let us know if you have any quesFons or wish to discuss any of⏎these issues further.⏎Sincerely,⏎Nicole O. Fallon ScoI Code⏎Vice President, Integrated Services & Vice President, Center for Aging services⏎Managed Care Technologies⏎LeadingAge LeadingAge⏎nfallon@leadingage.org scode@leadingage.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0363♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from PCare+, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔PCare+♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use⏎of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Public Comment Submission⏎Prepared for Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) / Office of the Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) / Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Document Number 2025-23641⏎Submission Date February 23, 2026⏎Deadline February 23, 2026⏎Authors Christian Chartier, MD⏎Comment identification HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎RFI title Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Point of contact Christian Chartier, MD⏎Email christian@pcare.ca⏎Phone (514) 893-0338⏎Mailing address 9 Harding Street, Kirkland, QC H9J 3Z1⏎Organization Type Health IT / Standards Contributor⏎Primary Topic Clinical AI Governance, Auditability, and Interoperability⏎Submission Type Public Comment⏎This comment is intended for public posting and does not include patient-level protected health⏎information (PHI) or confidential business information.⏎It includes limited organizational contact information solely to support follow-up on technical⏎standards and policy implementation questions.⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Table of Contents⏎Section Page⏎1. Executive Summary and Key Recommendations 3⏎5⏎2. RFI Compliance Crosswalk (Exhibits 1 and 1A)⏎3. Understanding of HHS Context and Objectives (Exhibit 2 and 2A) 7⏎4. Responses to RFI Questions (Questions 1–10b) 10⏎Question 1: Barriers and opportunities 10⏎Question 2: Policy changes and applicable CFR citations (Exhibit 3 and 3A) 13⏎Question 3: Legal and implementation considerations 16⏎Question 4: Evaluation and monitoring approaches (Exhibit 4) 17⏎Question 5: Supporting private sector accreditation/certification 19⏎Question 6: Examples of expectations met/fell short and future potential 20⏎Question 7: Decision-makers and administrative hurdles (Exhibit 5) 23⏎Question 8: Interoperability opportunities and standards needs (Exhibit 6) 24⏎Question 9: Patient and caregiver priorities and concerns (Exhibit 7) 26⏎Question 10: Research priorities for HHS (Exhibit 8) 27⏎10a. Published findings on deployed clinical AI 28⏎10b. Costs, benefits, and transfers in the literature 28⏎5. Conclusion 30⏎6. Appendices 31⏎Appendix A: Acronyms and Definitions 31⏎Appendix B: Evaluation and Monitoring Reporting Template 34⏎Appendix C: AI Audit Artifact Package (Expanded) 35⏎Appendix D: References 36⏎Appendix E: Candidate CFR Anchors (for counsel validation) 38⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 2 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎1. Executive Summary: Risk-tiered governance and verification can accelerate⏎responsible AI adoption in clinical care⏎Governing thought: HHS can accelerate safe and equitable clinical AI adoption by making governance⏎and auditability requirements legible, portable, and interoperable across products and sites.⏎Many adoption failures stem less from algorithmic novelty than from gaps in traceability (what ran,⏎when, for whom, and under which configuration), change control, and accountable human use in⏎workflow.⏎This comment recommends risk-tiered minimum governance and auditability expectations for⏎non-device clinical AI / decision support, tailored by runtime behavior (deterministic guideline-logic⏎artifacts vs probabilistic inference services), and implemented through HHS levers that do not alter FDA⏎jurisdiction over medical devices.⏎Key message to HHS⏎● Publish a risk-tiered “minimum governance + auditability expectations” framework for⏎non-device clinical AI / decision support that is tailored by runtime behavior and intended use,⏎and explicitly does not modify FDA device jurisdiction.⏎● Extend (not replace) ONC’s Decision Support Interventions (DSI) transparency and intervention⏎risk management concepts by emphasizing runtime auditability: version/configuration capture,⏎provenance, user actions, and change logs that enable retrospective reconstruction.⏎● Make the “AI audit artifact package” feasible as a minimum: a small set of artifacts that can be⏎produced, exported, and reviewed across developers, certified health IT, and deployers without⏎requiring proprietary disclosure of model internals.⏎● Use ONC Certification implementation guidance and test procedures to ensure certified health IT⏎can capture/export AI-relevant provenance and user actions for AI-influenced documentation⏎and orders using FHIR building blocks (SMART, CDS Hooks, SDC, Provenance).⏎● Use CMS levers (CMMI model terms, MIPS Improvement Activities / Promoting Interoperability⏎pathways, and hospital quality reporting guidance) to create incentives for deployers to maintain⏎governance artifacts and post-deployment monitoring appropriate to risk tier and runtime⏎behavior, with candidate CFR anchors provided for counsel validation.⏎● Certain internal logical properties of deterministic guideline-logic artifacts (e.g., consistency,⏎reachability, completeness over defined states) can be formally verified; this does not prove⏎clinical benefit, data quality, or appropriateness of guideline interpretation across contexts.⏎● Deterministic artifacts do not require statistical “model drift” monitoring, but they still require⏎post-deployment safety monitoring for data quality/missingness, user overrides/escalations,⏎disparate application, and guideline/version updates with regression test outcomes.⏎● OCR should operationalize Section 1557 requirements for patient care decision support tools (45⏎CFR § 92.210) through implementation guidance that links nondiscrimination duties to concrete⏎documentation, monitoring, and mitigation steps.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 3 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎About the submitter / transparency⏎PCare+ is a health IT and standards-focused organization that participates in interoperability⏎implementation and governance discussions relevant to clinical decision support and AI-enabled⏎workflow integration.⏎PCare+ may have professional and/or commercial interests in approaches that favor interoperable⏎auditability and standards-based integration, and this comment is offered to support implementable HHS⏎policy and program design rather than to advocate for any single product or vendor.⏎Recommendations are framed as minimum expectations that can be met by multiple technical⏎architectures and multiple market participants.⏎Summary recommendation⏎(1) Distinguish between AI-compiled deterministic artifacts and probabilistic AI services in regulatory,⏎evaluation, and monitoring frameworks. These are fundamentally different technologies with different⏎risk profiles. Regulating compiled guideline logic under monitoring rules designed for chatbots imposes⏎compliance costs without safety benefit.⏎(2) Adopt formal verification of logical completeness (exhaustiveness, determinism, reachability) as the⏎evaluation standard for deterministic clinical decision logic, beyond the necessary but insufficient⏎baseline of audit logging.⏎(3) Align reimbursement incentives to verified guideline-concordant care, measured by screening⏎completion, provenance traceability, and patient outcomes, rather than to AI tool access or adoption⏎volume.⏎These recommendations are complementary to the existing computable guideline, eCQM, and clinical⏎decision support ecosystem, and are intended to improve provenance, verification of internal logic⏎properties where appropriate, workflow integration, and accountability rather than to imply that⏎computable CDS does not already exist.⏎Evidence base referenced in this submission: Our analysis of 227 million T-MSIS Medicaid claims rows,⏎2.99 million CDC BRFSS survey responses, and HRSA UDS data from 18,751 FQHC sites indicates that⏎preventive screening is declining nationally, even where the infrastructure and coverage mandates to⏎deliver it exist. Patients and caregivers need understandable disclosure and practical recourse when AI⏎influences clinical recommendations; a decision artifact whose correctness is provable provides a⏎stronger foundation than transparency-by-logging alone.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 4 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎2. RFI Compliance Crosswalk: Responses are provided in the required question order⏎Exhibit 1 maps each RFI question to the corresponding response section(s) and supporting exhibits/appendices.⏎Exhibit 1. RFI Compliance Crosswalk⏎ID RFI question Response location Page⏎Q1 1. The RFI states that there are numerous challenges that prevent the use of AI in clinical Question 1 (Section 4.1); 10⏎care, and that HHS is seeking input on how to address these challenges. What are the Exhibit 2; Appendix A⏎barriers and opportunities to accelerate AI adoption as part of clinical care?⏎Q2 2. The RFI states that AI use in clinical care poses new challenges for policy and regulation, Question 2 (Section 4.2); 13⏎and that HHS seeks input on potential policy changes to safely accelerate adoption. What Exhibit 3; Exhibit 1A;⏎specific changes, including applicable CFR citations, should HHS consider? Appendix C; Appendix E⏎Q3 3. The RFI states that the complexity of AI technology raises new challenges for Question 3 (Section 4.3); 16⏎implementation, including risks to privacy, civil rights and civil liberties. What issues should Exhibit 7; Appendix E⏎be considered to best address these concerns and which HHS offices should be involved?⏎Q4 4. The RFI states that robust safety and effectiveness monitoring is essential. What Question 4 (Section 4.4); 17⏎evaluation methods and metrics should be used and how should monitoring be Exhibit 4; Appendix B;⏎conducted? Appendix C⏎Q5 5. The RFI states that private sector accreditation, certification, and industry-driven testing Question 5 (Section 4.5); 19⏎can promote effective and safe AI use. How can HHS best support private sector activities Exhibit 3; Exhibit 4⏎(e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Q6 6. The RFI seeks real-world examples of where AI tools met or exceeded cost and Question 6 (Section 4.6); 20⏎performance expectations, and where they fell short. In addition, are there novel AI tools Exhibits 3–4; Exhibit 7⏎that have high potential to improve clinical care, but are not currently in widespread use?⏎Please provide examples.⏎Q7 7. The RFI asks: What are the most significant decision-makers and stakeholders for Question 7 (Section 4.7); 23⏎adoption of AI tools in clinical care, and what are key administrative, workflow, and Exhibit 5; Exhibit 3; Appendix⏎resource hurdles to adoption? C⏎Q8 8. The RFI asks: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel Question 8 (Section 4.8); 24⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific Exhibit 6; Exhibit 3; Appendix⏎data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools. C⏎Q9 9. The RFI asks: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see Question 9 (Section 4.9); 26⏎addressed by use of AI? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have about the Exhibit 7; Appendix E⏎use of AI in clinical care?⏎Q10 10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the Question 10 (Section 4.10); 27⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care? Exhibit 8⏎Q10a a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical Question 10a (Section 4.10a); 28⏎care? Exhibit 8; Appendix B⏎Q10b b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of Question 10b (Section 28⏎clinical care? 4.10b); Exhibit 8⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 5 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Exhibit 1A. What HHS should do (summary)⏎HHS Time⏎Mechanism Action / deliverable⏎component horizon⏎ASTP/ONC Guidance / playbook Publish a risk-tier trigger rubric and minimum governance + auditability 0–6 months⏎expectations for non-device clinical AI / decision support, explicitly scoped to⏎interoperability and accountability practices and not FDA device jurisdiction.⏎ONC Implementation guidance Clarify that DSI-aligned transparency is necessary but not sufficient, and add 6–18 months⏎Certification + test procedure updates testable expectations for runtime auditability (version/config, provenance,⏎Program user action capture/export) when AI materially influences⏎documentation/orders.⏎ONC / ASTP Standards implementation Publish recommended FHIR profiles/patterns for recording AI tool identity, 6–18 months⏎guidance version/configuration, and provenance for AI-influenced notes, orders, and⏎decision support.⏎OCR Implementation guidance Issue guidance on operationalizing 45 CFR § 92.210 for patient care decision 0–6 months⏎support tools, including what constitutes “reasonable efforts” to identify and⏎mitigate discrimination risk and how to document ongoing monitoring and⏎mitigation.⏎CMS / CMMI Model participation terms Add participation/reporting terms in CMMI models that require deployers to 6–18 months⏎+ reporting maintain and produce an AI audit artifact package and post-deployment⏎monitoring signals when AI materially influences clinical decisions or⏎documentation.⏎CMS Program scoring pathways Create an Improvement Activity and/or Promoting Interoperability 6–18 months⏎(QPP/MIPS) (candidate rulemaking / attestation pathway for maintaining governance artifacts and enabling⏎subreg guidance) provenance/audit export for AI-influenced documentation/orders.⏎CMS (Hospital Hospital quality reporting In Hospital IQR program guidance, specify documentation expectations 6–18 months⏎Quality) guidance when AI materially influences eligibility/ordering and require retention of⏎governance artifacts sufficient to reconstruct AI-influenced care processes.⏎AHRQ Grants / contracts / Fund implementation science on human factors failure modes, monitoring 6–18 months⏎implementation science dashboards, and “safety signal” operations for AI-influenced workflow⏎(overrides, data quality, disparity signals, incident response).⏎AHRQ + ONC + Convening + shared test Stand up an open, governance-backed test harness/benchmark suite for 18+ months⏎NIST (and infrastructure common clinical workflow scenarios that includes provenance-aware⏎partners) writeback and auditing patterns, enabling reproducible evaluation and⏎procurement-ready conformance testing.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 6 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎3. Understanding of HHS Context and Objectives: Adoption depends on⏎governance, interoperability, and accountability in real workflows⏎This RFI appropriately frames clinical AI adoption as a multi-component HHS problem that spans⏎interoperability, safety, privacy, civil rights, reimbursement incentives, and evidence generation.⏎We interpret HHS’s objective as enabling clinically meaningful use of AI with accountable oversight,⏎without creating duplicative or conflicting regulatory regimes.⏎We also note that a substantial computable guideline, eCQM, and clinical decision support ecosystem⏎already exists (e.g., eCQMs, CDS artifacts, SMART-based apps), and our recommendations are intended⏎to be complementary by improving auditability, provenance, verification of internal logic properties⏎where appropriate, and workflow integration.⏎Evaluator-relevant objectives reflected in this comment⏎● Differentiate AI technologies by runtime behavior and risk profile (e.g., deterministic decision⏎artifacts vs probabilistic inference services) to apply proportionate controls.⏎● Emphasize testability, provenance, and retrospective review for higher-risk non-device AI used in⏎clinical workflows.⏎● Reduce adoption friction through standards-based interoperability patterns (structured data capture,⏎app launch, event-driven CDS, and provenance-aware writeback).⏎● Use federal payment and program mechanisms to shift demand toward tools that are measurable,⏎governable, and aligned to guideline-concordant care.⏎● Support learning health system approaches through reusable evaluation materials (test cases,⏎reporting templates, and monitoring metrics) that can be applied across sites.⏎Exhibit 2 summarizes why deterministic compiled artifacts and probabilistic AI services warrant different⏎evaluation and monitoring expectations.⏎Exhibit 2. Deterministic compiled artifacts and probabilistic AI services warrant different controls⏎Dimension Deterministic compiled artifact Probabilistic AI service⏎Runtime Deterministic execution; no inference-time Runtime inference; outputs may vary and⏎behavior probabilistic reasoning. depend on model/prompt/version.⏎Primary safety Logic errors/omissions and update governance Stochastic behavior, drift, and⏎risk gaps. prompt/model changes.⏎Evaluation Build-time verification and traceability to Pre/post deployment performance and⏎emphasis guideline version/text. robustness; subgroup analysis.⏎Monitoring Version control, regression testing, Operational monitoring, thresholds,⏎emphasis provenance-aware audit artifacts. escalation, incident response.⏎Evidence basis Guideline/page/paragraph and encoded logic Reconstruction from logs (inputs,⏎for recourse path. model/version, prompts/config, user⏎actions).⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 7 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Operational risk-tier triggers (qualitative rubric)⏎● Does the output influence diagnosis, treatment, medication, triage, eligibility, or access to services⏎(vs purely administrative support)?⏎● Is the output patient-facing (directly consumed by patients/caregivers) or clinician-facing only?⏎● Is the tool autonomous (acts by default) or clinician-confirmed (requires deliberate⏎acceptance/attestation)?⏎● Does the tool generate or modify the legal medical record (documentation/writeback) or only⏎provide a transient suggestion?⏎● What is the scale of use (single clinic vs enterprise vs multi-tenant service) and the speed of⏎propagation of errors?⏎● How reversible is harm (can incorrect outputs be quickly detected and corrected before patient⏎impact)?⏎● Does the tool use protected class variables or proxies in ways that raise discrimination risk (triggering⏎heightened nondiscrimination governance)?⏎Operational risk-tier triggers (qualitative rubric)⏎● Tier 1 (low): administrative or informational support; clinician review; no autonomous actions;⏎minimal audit artifacts and change logs.⏎● Tier 2 (moderate): influences documentation/orders/clinical decisions but remains⏎clinician-confirmed; full “AI audit artifact package” plus monitoring of overrides, data quality, and⏎disparate application.⏎● Tier 3 (higher): patient-facing and/or affects triage/access and/or operates with default/autonomous⏎actions at scale; full audit artifact package plus enhanced evaluation, incident response, and⏎nondiscrimination documentation aligned to 45 CFR § 92.210.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 8 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Exhibit 2A. HHS objectives and jurisdiction-aware framing⏎HHS objective Why it matters for adoption Implementation-oriented framing⏎Enable accountable Adoption stalls when decision-makers cannot Publish risk-tiered minimum governance + auditability⏎adoption of clinical AI verify what a tool did, under which configuration, expectations for non-device clinical AI/decision support,⏎across settings and and how it affected documentation/orders. tailored by runtime behavior and intended use.⏎vendors⏎Improve AI that cannot integrate with EHR workflows and Use ONC Certification implementation guidance and testing to⏎interoperability for standards-based writeback creates operational require standards-based capture/export of provenance and⏎AI-enabled workflow risk and limits measurable benefit. user actions for AI-influenced documentation/orders (SMART⏎integration + CDS Hooks + SDC + Provenance).⏎Support privacy and Clinicians and organizations need confidence that Reference HIPAA Security Rule audit controls and require⏎security without AI does not introduce uncontrolled data flows documented retention/access policies for AI-related logs and⏎over-prescribing and that audit controls exist for access and artifacts rather than implying fixed retention durations.⏎implementation modification events.⏎Uphold civil rights Patient care decision support can amplify Operationalize OCR’s 45 CFR § 92.210 through guidance that⏎and inequities if inputs/outputs are not monitored ties “reasonable efforts” to concrete governance artifacts,⏎nondiscrimination and mitigations are not documented. monitoring, and mitigation workflows.⏎Create aligned If governance and monitoring are unfunded Use CMS program terms and quality pathways to incent⏎incentives (avoid pure “uncompensated compliance,” adoption will skew governance artifacts and monitoring proportional to risk,⏎fee-for-service toward well-resourced systems and vendors. without defaulting to new standalone “AI billing codes.”⏎distortions)⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 9 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎4. Responses to RFI Questions (in RFI order)⏎The sections below respond to each RFI question in the order presented, retaining the verbatim question⏎text for evaluator convenience.⏎Recommendations are scoped to HHS-implementable levers (guidance, certification⏎implementation/testing, program terms, quality pathways, grants/contracts) and are tailored by runtime⏎behavior and risk tier.⏎Question 1: Adoption barriers are governance, interoperability, and operational accountability⏎RFI question: 1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎In practice, the most material barriers to innovation and adoption often emerge after development. The⏎friction typically resides in governance, interoperability, and operational accountability once AI outputs⏎can influence clinical decisions, documentation, or access to services.⏎Key barriers observed across deployments⏎● Lack of a regulatory distinction between deterministic and probabilistic clinical AI: Current⏎frameworks often treat all AI in clinical care as a single category, applying similar evaluation,⏎monitoring, and governance expectations whether a tool performs probabilistic runtime inference or⏎executes a pre-compiled, formally verified decision artifact. This can overburden deterministic tools⏎with requirements such as drift monitoring for logic that cannot drift, while underspecifying⏎oversight for non-deterministic systems whose runtime behavior is inherently less predictable.⏎● Accountability and auditability gaps: Many deployments cannot reliably support retrospective⏎reconstruction of what the tool recommended, under which version and configuration, what inputs⏎were used, and how the output affected documentation, orders, task routing, or clinician actions.⏎This limits incident investigation, documentation integrity review, and organizational trust.⏎● Workflow integration and interoperability burden: AI tools often fail to produce measurable clinical⏎benefit when they are not integrated into ordering, documentation, and task routing workflows.⏎Common barriers include launch context, authorization, latency, writeback, provenance capture, and⏎variability across certified health IT implementations. Inconsistent support for standards-based⏎invocation and data exchange makes implementation costly and brittle across sites.⏎● Unstructured guidance and inconsistent clinical data capture: Clinical guidance is often ambiguous⏎or not computable, and data capture practices vary substantially across organizations. This reduces⏎reproducibility, increases site-specific customization, and complicates cross-site evaluation.⏎● Change-control and update management gaps: Organizations frequently lack practical processes for⏎safe updates, including regression testing, version transition controls, rollback procedures, and⏎governance over rapid tool iterations and guideline changes. These gaps create operational risk and⏎slow deployment.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 10 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎● Monitoring gaps not tailored to risk and runtime behavior: Many deployments lack feasible⏎post-deployment monitoring that is proportional to risk and appropriate to runtime behavior.⏎Deterministic and probabilistic tools require different monitoring approaches, but deployers often⏎lack clear operational guidance on what is sufficient and practical.⏎● Unclear minimum governance expectations for non-device AI: For non-device AI used in clinical⏎care, there is often no clear baseline for auditability, provenance, update control, performance⏎monitoring, and recourse. This uncertainty slows procurement and results in inconsistent controls⏎across organizations.⏎● Procurement, legal, and contracting friction: Health systems often repeat security, privacy, and⏎clinical governance reviews across vendors and products. Adoption is further slowed by unclear⏎allocation of responsibility between developers and deployers for tool errors, documentation⏎integrity, and nondiscrimination obligations.⏎● Equity and civil rights implementation complexity: Deployers often lack practical guidance on how⏎to document and operationalize reasonable efforts to identify and mitigate discrimination risks,⏎especially when protected characteristics may be proxied indirectly.⏎● Resource constraints for smaller, rural, and safety-net providers: Many providers cannot absorb the⏎ongoing costs of governance, integration, evaluation, and monitoring without aligned incentives,⏎shared infrastructure, or technical assistance. This risks widening inequities in adoption and access.⏎● Limited reusable evaluation and conformance tools: There is limited availability of standardized test⏎cases, reporting templates, and post-deployment monitoring metrics that can be reused across sites.⏎As a result, organizations duplicate validation and integration work.⏎Opportunities for Acceleration⏎● Standardize a portable minimum audit evidence package: HHS can define a baseline AI audit⏎artifact package that is reusable across products and sites, including intended use, versioning,⏎provenance, configuration, validation summary, monitoring plan, and recourse process. This would⏎reduce duplicative review burden and improve accountability.⏎● Build on existing ONC transparency and intervention risk foundations: HHS can extend existing ONC⏎transparency and intervention risk approaches toward runtime traceability that supports audits,⏎incident response, and continuous improvement in clinical operations.⏎● Strengthen existing computable clinical infrastructure: HHS can accelerate adoption by improving⏎current ecosystems, including computable guidelines, CDS artifacts, eCQMs, SMART apps, and⏎standards-based workflows, through stronger provenance, versioning, verification, and conformance⏎practices rather than replacing them.⏎● Create shared, governance-backed test harnesses for common workflows: A shared conformance⏎and testing infrastructure for common integration patterns, including standards-based launch and⏎invocation, structured data capture, and provenance-aware writeback, would reduce custom⏎integration burden and improve procurement readiness.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 11 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎● Align payment and program incentives with governable deployment: Federal payment and⏎program levers can encourage adoption of tools that are auditable, monitorable, and operationally⏎accountable, rather than tools selected primarily on claimed functionality.⏎Recommendations to HHS⏎● Publish risk-tiered minimum governance expectations for non-device AI used in clinical care: HHS⏎should publish clear, risk-tiered minimum expectations for auditability and operational governance,⏎including versioning, provenance, input/output traceability, update control, monitoring, and⏎recourse, with greater requirements for higher-risk uses.⏎Why this helps: A predictable baseline reduces procurement uncertainty, supports consistent⏎controls across settings, and improves investigation when safety, equity, or documentation integrity⏎concerns arise.⏎● Distinguish deterministic AI-compiled artifacts from probabilistic AI services: HHS should publish⏎guidance that differentiates AI-compiled deterministic artifacts from probabilistic runtime AI services⏎and sets proportionate evaluation and monitoring expectations for each.⏎Why this helps: This avoids imposing unnecessary monitoring burdens on deterministic artifacts⏎while ensuring probabilistic systems receive oversight appropriate to their risk.⏎● Fund open conformance tools and reference implementations for common integration patterns:⏎HHS should support reusable conformance tools and reference implementations for standards-based⏎launch, invocation, structured data capture, and provenance-aware writeback.⏎Why this helps: Interoperability friction is a major source of cost and delay. Shared infrastructure⏎reduces duplicative implementation work and supports safer deployments.⏎● Use payment and program policy to require a basic governance package for higher-risk AI-enabled⏎workflows: For higher-risk AI-enabled workflows, HHS should condition federal payment support or⏎program participation on submission of a basic governance package, such as intended use,⏎monitoring plan, audit artifact package, and recourse process, rather than relying only on claimed⏎functionality.⏎Why this helps: Payment levers can shift demand toward tools that are governable and measurable⏎and reduce incentives to deploy poorly monitored systems.⏎● Provide practical implementation templates for equity and discrimination risk mitigation: HHS⏎should publish practical templates and examples that help deployers document reasonable efforts to⏎identify and mitigate discrimination risks in implementation and monitoring.⏎Why this helps: Many organizations support equity safeguards but lack operational clarity. Templates⏎can improve consistency and reduce implementation uncertainty.⏎● Support shared infrastructure and technical assistance for smaller and safety-net providers: HHS⏎should support shared governance resources, reusable evaluation tools, and technical assistance⏎pathways for smaller, rural, and safety-net providers.⏎Why this helps: This reduces structural barriers to adoption and helps ensure that benefits from⏎AI-enabled care are not limited to large, well-resourced systems.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 12 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 2: Policy changes should focus on auditability, provenance, and verification,⏎supported by program incentives and CFR anchors⏎RFI question: 2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs⏎could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific⏎changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS should implement a jurisdiction-aware approach that focuses on minimum governance, auditability,⏎and interoperability expectations for non-device clinical AI / decision support, tailored by runtime⏎behavior and clinical risk.⏎This approach should be explicitly scoped so that it does not alter FDA jurisdiction over medical devices⏎and does not purport to “approve” model quality, but instead makes accountability and reconstruction⏎feasible when AI materially influences clinical care, documentation, or ordering.⏎Specific changes HHS should consider (with candidate CFR anchors where applicable)⏎1. ASTP/ONC: publish risk-tiered minimum expectations for non-device clinical AI / decision⏎support⏎a. Mechanism: ASTP/ONC guidance/playbook (non-binding) with templates.⏎b. Deliverable: A risk-tier trigger rubric and minimum expectations for governance artifacts,⏎auditability, and monitoring tailored by runtime behavior (deterministic guideline-logic⏎artifacts vs probabilistic inference services).⏎c. CFR anchor concept (ONC program context): ONC Certification Program decision support⏎criteria context at 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11) (DSI).⏎2. ONC Certification Program: make runtime auditability testable where AI materially influences⏎documentation/orders⏎a. Mechanism: Implementation guidance + test procedure updates (and, if needed, future⏎certification criterion refinement through rulemaking).⏎b. Deliverable: Testable capabilities for (a) capturing tool identity + version/configuration at⏎runtime, (b) recording provenance for AI-influenced notes/orders, and (c) exporting⏎relevant audit/provenance data for governance review.⏎c. CFR anchor: 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11) and related ONC certification provisions (see⏎Appendix E for candidate anchors).⏎d. Alignment note: The ONC DSI Resource Guide emphasizes transparency and risk⏎management and clarifies that the program does not “approve” predictive DSI quality;⏎our proposal extends DSI by focusing on runtime auditability that enables retrospective⏎reconstruction.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 13 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎3. OCR: operationalize nondiscrimination expectations for patient care decision support tools⏎a. Mechanism: OCR implementation guidance and examples.⏎b. Deliverable: Practical documentation and monitoring guidance for “reasonable efforts”⏎to identify and mitigate discrimination risk when using patient care decision support⏎tools, especially where protected class variables are used or proxied.⏎c. CFR anchor: 45 CFR § 92.210 (patient care decision support tools) and related Part 92⏎requirements (see Appendix E).⏎4. CMS: incent governance artifacts and monitoring through existing program levers rather than⏎new standalone “AI billing codes”⏎a. Mechanism (three implementation routes):⏎i. CMMI model participation/reporting terms (model-specific terms and reporting⏎requirements)⏎ii. MIPS (QPP) Improvement Activities and Promoting Interoperability pathways⏎iii. Hospital quality reporting guidance (e.g., Hospital IQR) where AI materially⏎influences eligibility/ordering/documentation⏎b. Deliverable: Program terms and attestations that require maintenance of an AI audit⏎artifact package and tailored post-deployment monitoring signals when AI materially⏎influences care processes.⏎c. Candidate CFR anchors for counsel validation: 42 CFR Part 414, Subpart O (Quality⏎Payment Program), including 42 CFR § 414.1375 (Promoting Interoperability) and related⏎Improvement Activities provisions, and 42 CFR § 412.140 (Hospital IQR Program).⏎Reimbursement realism (standalone billing vs incentives)⏎The RFI correctly highlights that fee-for-service incentives can distort adoption toward volume rather⏎than accountability.⏎We recommend CMS avoid creating a standalone “AI billing code” as the default policy response and⏎instead tie incentives to (1) outcomes/value-based constructs, (2) validated burden reduction in⏎bounded workflows, and (3) auditable governance and evaluation artifacts rather than model claims.⏎This framing supports adoption while reducing incentives for overuse, underdocumentation of risks, or⏎deployment without monitoring.⏎Exhibit 3 defines the minimum “AI audit artifact package,” and Appendix C provides an expanded version⏎intended for procurement and governance use.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 14 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Exhibit 3. Recommended minimum AI audit artifact package (field set)⏎Minimum artifact element Purpose (what it enables)⏎Tool identity and scope (name, developer, intended use, Prevents repurposing beyond intended use and supports⏎clinical context, contraindicated uses) appropriate governance review.⏎Version and configuration at runtime (model/artifact version, Supports reconstruction of what actually ran for a given output and⏎prompt/config parameters where applicable, enabled enables safe update governance.⏎features)⏎Input data dependencies (data types used, required fields, Supports data quality monitoring and explains failure modes tied to⏎known missingness sensitivities) missing/incorrect inputs.⏎Output characterization (output type, confidence/uncertainty Clarifies how outputs should be interpreted and used in workflow.⏎handling where applicable, recommended user action)⏎Known limitations and failure modes (including Supports safe use, informed oversight, and targeted monitoring.⏎fairness/robustness considerations)⏎Provenance + writeback behavior (what is written to the Protects record integrity and enables traceability for downstream⏎record, where, and how provenance is attached) consumers and audits.⏎User interaction capture (accept/override/modify, escalation Enables monitoring of reliance, overrides, and human-in-the-loop⏎paths) performance.⏎Update/change log and regression testing evidence (including Enables controlled updates and reduces the risk of silent behavior⏎guideline/version changes for deterministic artifacts) changes.⏎Exhibit 3A. Clarifying “who must produce which artifacts” (implementability)⏎Actor Primary responsibility Examples of artifacts/capabilities⏎Certified health IT / Capture and export runtime evidence Provenance attachment to writeback targets; exportable user action logs;⏎EHR module of AI use in workflow. standardized interfaces (SMART/CDS Hooks/FHIR) for invocation and data⏎access; audit event export patterns.⏎AI developer (tool Provide tool identity, limitations, Version/config disclosure; known limitations; change logs; intended use;⏎supplier) versioning, and change transparency. interface specifications; safety and fairness limitations statements.⏎Deployer (health Define local intended use, Local configuration documentation; monitoring dashboards and review⏎system/clinic) governance, monitoring, and cadence; incident response and escalation; patient/clinician recourse⏎recourse. processes; nondiscrimination documentation aligned to 45 CFR § 92.210⏎where applicable.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 15 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 3: Novel issues are practical: allocation of responsibility, update governance, record⏎integrity, and audit access⏎RFI question: 3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures... What⏎novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎For non-device AI that influences clinical decisions, documentation, or access decisions, the most⏎consequential legal and implementation issues are often operational: reproducibility, allocation of⏎responsibilities, update governance, and record integrity.⏎Issues that commonly create compliance and safety risk⏎● Privacy and security (HIPAA and beyond): AI integrations increase data flows and new access⏎pathways, so deployers need audit controls and documented retention/access policies appropriate⏎to their risk tier and workflow.⏎● Record integrity and provenance: when AI generates or influences documentation/orders,⏎organizations must be able to identify AI-influenced content, reconstruct inputs/outputs, and⏎preserve clinician accountability for final actions.⏎● Civil rights and nondiscrimination: patient care decision support tools can introduce discriminatory⏎effects through input selection, proxies, workflow routing, or differential error rates across pop.⏎● Patient trust and autonomy: patient-facing or patient-impacting AI requires clear disclosures,⏎meaningful recourse, and safeguards against deceptive or coercive automation.⏎● Liability and responsibility allocation: contracts and governance should allocate responsibility for⏎tool defects, configuration errors, data quality failures, and monitoring omissions.⏎● Information access and information blocking: interoperability obligations must be implemented⏎carefully within existing exceptions frameworks rather than implying blanket access rights.⏎● Patient rights applicability: access/amendment rights depend on whether information is maintained⏎in the designated record set, and disclosures should avoid overpromising rights that are⏎context-dependent.⏎Which HHS offices should be involved (and why)⏎● ASTP/ONC: to lead interoperability and certification-aligned auditability expectations and convene⏎standards implementation guidance.⏎● OCR: to provide implementation guidance for nondiscrimination obligations for patient care decision⏎support tools under 45 CFR § 92.210 and for privacy-related considerations in patient-facing tools.⏎● CMS/CMMI: to align incentives and reporting expectations (governance artifacts and monitoring)⏎through program terms and quality pathways.⏎● AHRQ: to fund implementation science on human factors, monitoring operations, and equitable⏎deployment practices, and to support evaluation infrastructure.⏎● OIG (as appropriate): to align compliance program guidance and oversight concepts with auditability⏎expectations, especially where AI affects documentation, coding, or medical management processes.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 16 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 4: Evaluation should be staged and risk-tiered, with formal verification for⏎deterministic guideline logic⏎RFI question: 4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would⏎be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Evaluation should be proportional to risk tier and tailored to runtime behavior, with emphasis on⏎reproducible measurement and feasible post-deployment monitoring.⏎HHS should avoid implying that any single evaluation method “proves” clinical correctness, and should⏎instead emphasize traceability, auditability, and post-deployment safety monitoring.⏎Deterministic guideline-logic artifacts can support formal verification of internal logical properties (e.g.,⏎consistency, reachability, completeness over defined states), but this does not prove clinical benefit, data⏎quality, or appropriate interpretation across contexts.⏎Exhibit 4 summarizes evaluation components for higher-risk non-device clinical AI across pre- and⏎post-deployment stages, including human-centered and equity-focused methods.⏎Exhibit 4. Risk-tiered evaluation lifecycle for non-device clinical AI⏎Lifecycle stage Minimum evaluation elements (all tools) Tailoring by runtime behavior⏎Pre-deployment Define intended use, workflow, and user role; Deterministic: verify internal logical properties over defined states⏎(fit-for-use) identify failure modes; validate data and perform regression tests against test cases for guideline/version⏎availability/quality; perform baseline changes. Probabilistic: evaluate discrimination risk,⏎performance testing in representative settings. calibration/uncertainty handling where applicable, robustness to⏎distribution shifts, and performance across subgroups.⏎Implementation Document tool identity/version/config; define Deterministic: ensure executable logic is version-controlled and⏎(integration + human-in-the-loop responsibilities; establish linked to source guideline versions and local policy choices.⏎governance) incident response and escalation; ensure Probabilistic: ensure model update policy is explicit and changes are⏎provenance + user action capture. auditable.⏎Post-deployment Monitor for safety signals proportional to risk Deterministic: no statistical drift monitoring, but monitor data⏎monitoring tier; review overrides/escalations; monitor data quality, overrides/escalations, disparate application, and⏎(safety signals) quality/missingness; monitor disparate guideline/version update impacts with regression test outcomes.⏎application/outcomes; maintain recourse Probabilistic: include performance monitoring and drift detection⏎pathways. where appropriate in addition to the above.⏎Periodic Periodic governance review, documentation Deterministic: re-run regression tests when guidelines, local policies,⏎re-evaluation updates, and evaluation refresh based on or input mappings change. Probabilistic: re-evaluate when model⏎(learning + incidents, updates, or workflow changes. retrains, input features change, or drift signals occur.⏎accountability)⏎Transparent reporting using established guidance (e.g., CONSORT-AI, SPIRIT-AI, and DECIDE-AI) can⏎support comparability across tools and contexts when supplemented with deployment context and⏎governance artifacts for non-device AI.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 17 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Formal verification for deterministic clinical decision logic⏎For clinical AI that encodes guideline logic as deterministic decision trees (as opposed to runtime⏎probabilistic inference), evaluation can include formal verification of logical properties using established⏎methods from safety-critical software engineering.⏎Satisfiability Modulo Theory (SMT) solvers can prove, across the full input space rather than a finite test⏎suite, that: (a) every possible combination of patient inputs leads to at least one recommendation⏎(exhaustiveness); (b) no combination triggers contradictory recommendations (determinism); and (c)⏎every encoded recommendation is reachable by at least one patient profile (no dead rules).⏎Graph-theoretic analysis can verify structural properties including acyclicity (no infinite assessment⏎loops), connectivity (no orphan recommendations), and minimum path length (minimum patient⏎burden).⏎These methods are established practice in aviation software and nuclear safety systems. They are⏎directly applicable to clinical decision logic encoded in standards-based formats such as FHIR Structured⏎Data Capture. HHS should fund development of formal verification tooling and reference⏎implementations for clinical decision artifacts, and should recognize formal verification evidence in⏎evaluation and procurement frameworks.⏎Transparency is necessary but insufficient for clinical decision logic that determines screening eligibility⏎and treatment pathways; the logical correctness of the encoded artifact should be provable, even⏎though clinical benefit and safe use in context must still be demonstrated through empirical evaluation⏎and post-deployment monitoring.⏎Recommendations to HHS⏎● R&D: Support development and adoption of standardized evaluation and reporting templates for⏎non-device clinical AI that can be completed without disclosing proprietary model details, aligned to⏎CONSORT-AI, SPIRIT-AI, and DECIDE-AI.⏎Why it helps: Standard templates reduce duplicative procurement burden and improve comparability⏎across sites and tools, accelerating a learning health system approach.⏎● R&D: Use grants, cooperative agreements, and prize competitions to fund pragmatic, multi-site⏎evaluations that measure workflow impact, safety outcomes, and equity, and that require version⏎and provenance reporting.⏎Why it helps: Real-world performance depends on local context; pragmatic evaluation reduces the⏎gap between retrospective technical performance and deployed impact.⏎● Regulation: Encourage risk-tiered post-deployment monitoring expectations (metrics, thresholds,⏎and escalation) anchored to NIST AI RMF functions (GOVERN, MAP, MEASURE, MANAGE) and⏎compatible with DSI intervention risk management concepts.⏎Why it helps: A governance anchor helps operationalize monitoring and response without⏎prescribing a single technical method, enabling proportionate control across AI types and risks.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 18 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 5: Accreditation works when it certifies what is testable: conformance, auditability,⏎and monitoring readiness⏎RFI question: 5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can support private sector accreditation and testing by defining a clear “floor” of auditable⏎governance expectations and enabling conformance testing that does not require HHS to certify model⏎quality.⏎A practical approach is to support accreditation/testing programs that verify the presence and⏎exportability of governance artifacts (identity/version/config, provenance, user action capture,⏎monitoring plans) and the ability to run reproducible workflow tests.⏎Alignment with ONC DSI (reduce duplication and increase credibility)⏎ONC’s DSI framework already emphasizes transparency and intervention risk management and explicitly⏎does not aim to approve or guarantee predictive DSI quality.⏎Our proposal extends (not replaces) DSI “source attributes” and intervention risk management concepts⏎by focusing on runtime auditability—version/config traceability, provenance, and user actions—that⏎enables retrospective reconstruction and supports monitoring and incident response.⏎Concrete HHS support actions⏎● RASTP/ONC: convene a public-private technical working group to define minimal conformance tests⏎for AI audit artifacts and provenance-aware writeback in common workflows.⏎● AHRQ: fund and evaluate independent testing protocols and implementation science studies of⏎accredited programs’ impact on safety, equity, and operational burden.⏎● CMS/CMMI: recognize accredited governance/auditability conformance as evidence in program⏎participation/reporting terms, without treating accreditation as proof of clinical benefit.⏎● OCR: provide guidance to ensure accreditation programs incorporate nondiscrimination⏎documentation consistent with 45 CFR § 92.210 when patient care decision support tools are in⏎scope.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 19 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 6: AI value is most consistent in tight, reviewable workflows; verified⏎preventive-care logic remains high potential⏎RFI question: 6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest⏎potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎The clearest operational wins so far have come from narrow, high-volume workflows where outputs are⏎reviewable and integration is tight. AI tools are most likely to meet or exceed expectations when⏎intended use is clear, workflow boundaries are defined, and organizations can monitor how outputs are⏎actually used.⏎AI has fallen short when inputs are poorly structured, workflow integration is weak, governance artifacts⏎are incomplete, or updates are pushed without clear change control. Those gaps create safety and⏎compliance risk and make it harder to tell whether a tool is creating real value or just shifting work and⏎cost elsewhere.⏎Below are short, de-identified vignettes that reflect common deployment patterns, with lessons tied to⏎governance, auditability, and interoperability:⏎● Ambient documentation (met expectations in bounded workflows): In multiple deployments,⏎ambient documentation tools have reduced clinician documentation burden and improved⏎perceived usability when deployed with clear intended use and workflow boundaries.⏎Where these deployments perform best, organizations can reconstruct tool⏎version/configuration and can review edits/overrides, enabling safe iteration and targeted⏎monitoring.⏎Lesson: success depends on provenance-aware writeback and user action capture more than on⏎headline accuracy claims, because the record integrity and clinician accountability requirements⏎are operationally determinative.⏎● Imaging worklist triage (met expectations when integrated and monitored): In settings using⏎imaging triage/priority tools, value is most evident when integration supports measurable⏎time-to-review improvements and when outputs are monitored for subgroup performance and⏎alerting fatigue.⏎Where these tools fall short, the common failure mode is not a single “bad model,” but poor⏎integration into clinical routing and insufficient auditing of when/why clinicians override.⏎Lesson: auditability and monitoring should be treated as part of the intervention, not optional⏎post-hoc documentation.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 20 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎● Patient-facing symptom checkers (often fell short on diagnostic accuracy and consistency):⏎Systematic reviews continue to find low and variable diagnostic accuracy across online symptom⏎checkers, with triage accuracy generally higher but still variable across tools and studies.⏎Common failure modes include incomplete data capture, inconsistent recommendations across⏎similar presentations, limited transparency about limitations, and unclear recourse when⏎outputs are wrong.⏎Lesson: patient-facing or patient-impacting tools should trigger higher risk-tier expectations,⏎including clear disclosures, documentation of limitations, and ongoing monitoring and⏎mitigation, aligned to nondiscrimination duties where applicable.⏎● Administrative utilization/authorization support (mixed results with transfer/payment⏎effects): Tools that support prior authorization workflows can reduce staff burden or speed⏎decisions in narrow use cases, but may also shift costs and burdens across payers, providers, and⏎patients depending on how criteria are implemented and how appeals/overrides are handled.⏎Where deployments fall short, it is often due to opaque criteria application, limited audit trails⏎for why an outcome occurred, and insufficient recourse for correction.⏎Lesson: governance artifacts and traceability are necessary to manage transfer effects and to⏎support fair, appealable, and auditable processes.⏎Promising areas for novel tools (as observed)⏎● Preventive care identification and outreach grounded in computable guideline logic: A⏎high-potential category is preventive care identification and outreach built on structured data⏎capture and published guidelines, with traceable logic and auditable outputs.⏎One promising approach is AI-assisted compilation of guideline artifacts into constrained, reviewable⏎workflow logic (including deterministic decision trees where appropriate), paired with clinician⏎review and interoperable writeback. This can support point-of-care identification of eligible services,⏎more consistent outreach, and clearer documentation of why a recommendation was made.⏎This category remains especially promising because the core problem is often not lack of⏎evidence-based recommendations, but unreliable translation of those recommendations into⏎repeatable workflow execution.⏎● Care coordination and navigation tools with provenance-aware workflows: strong potential when⏎they reduce friction across settings while preserving provenance, role-based access control, and⏎reviewable workflow actions.⏎High-value use cases include referral completion, follow-up scheduling, preventive service⏎completion, and transitions across care settings. The opportunity is not only automation of tasks, but⏎better continuity and accountability across fragmented systems.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 21 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎● Decision support that writes back reviewable, provenance-tagged artifacts: could improve quality⏎and efficiency if outputs are clearly reviewable and correctable before they affect the record or⏎downstream decisions.⏎The design requirement is critical: AI-generated suggestions should remain distinguishable from⏎clinician-authored content, with clear attribution and user action capture.⏎● Risk-tiered operational monitoring and “safety signal” dashboards have substantial potential to⏎make AI deployment performance visible in routine operations, rather than only during periodic⏎review.⏎Examples include dashboards that track override rates, missingness/data quality patterns, subgroup⏎performance signals, incident reports, alert fatigue, and workflow delays. These tools could improve⏎early detection of unintended consequences and support safer scaling, procurement, and⏎governance.⏎● Documentation and coding support that preserves record integrity: continue to improve⏎administrative efficiency, but the highest-value designs will preserve record integrity through⏎provenance, attribution, and separation of suggestions from finalized clinician documentation.⏎This is especially important where generated content may affect billing, quality measurement,⏎audits, or downstream clinical decision-making. Efficiency gains are more durable when⏎organizations can reconstruct what was suggested, what was accepted, and what was changed.⏎Evidence referenced on preventive care screening gaps⏎Analysis of 227 million T-MSIS Medicaid claims rows, cross-validated against 2.99 million CDC BRFSS⏎survey responses and HRSA UDS data from 18,751 FQHC sites, identifies a confounder-adjusted $320-450⏎million annual Medicaid billing gap in USPSTF-recommended preventive services.⏎Self-reported screening rates from BRFSS confirm the gap is real and worsening: national breast cancer⏎screening completion declined from 77.7% in 2014 to 74.4% in 2024. Seventy-seven percent of FQHC⏎sites report below the 75th percentile on three or more HRSA-mandated screening measures.⏎This gap is not attributed in this submission to coverage barriers. ACA Section 2713 mandates $0 copay⏎for USPSTF A and B grade services, affecting 150-180 million insured Americans. The gap is attributed⏎here to the absence of computable, verifiable clinical decision logic at the point of encounter.⏎AI-compiled guideline artifacts that encode USPSTF recommendations as formally verified decision trees⏎(deterministic, zero marginal cost per patient, FHIR-compliant) are presented as one approach to address⏎this supply-side bottleneck directly.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 22 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 7: Adoption decisions span clinical, IT, privacy, and finance; standard checklists can⏎reduce administrative burden⏎RFI question: 7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption decisions for clinical AI are multi-stakeholder decisions, and adoption often fails when governance⏎artifacts and monitoring responsibilities are not clearly assigned.Decision-makers require confidence that a⏎deployment can be audited, monitored, and updated safely, and that responsibilities are clear across developer,⏎EHR, and deployer roles.⏎Exhibit 5 summarizes the decision makers who commonly influence adoption decisions and the recurring⏎administrative hurdles that slow deployment.⏎Exhibit 5. Typical decision makers and administrative hurdles for clinical AI adoption⏎Decision-maker / stakeholder Adoption questions each must answer Primary administrative⏎hurdles⏎Clinical leadership Does the tool fit clinical workflow, preserve clinician accountability, and have a. Duplicated and⏎(CMIO/CMO, nursing clear intended use/limitations and escalation pathways? inconsistent⏎leadership, service line leads) security/privacy reviews⏎(including uncertainty⏎about log retention and⏎Quality and safety leadership Can we monitor safety signals, reconstruct events, and manage incidents,⏎access controls)⏎(quality, patient safety, risk including disparate impact and recourse?⏎b. Integration complexity⏎management)⏎and variability across EHR⏎implementations (launch,⏎IT leadership (CIO/CTO, Can we integrate via standards-based interfaces (SMART/CDS Hooks/FHIR),⏎authorization, writeback)⏎integration teams) support provenance-aware writeback, and avoid brittle custom interfaces?⏎c. Limited standardized⏎documentation for⏎Security and privacy (CISO, Are audit controls in place, and do we have documented retention/access⏎evaluation and monitoring⏎HIPAA security, privacy officer) policies and risk analysis appropriate to the data flows and risk tier?⏎d. Update management⏎and change control⏎Compliance and legal Are nondiscrimination obligations addressed (45 CFR § 92.210 where⏎concerns⏎(including civil rights applicable), and are contracting, liability, and documentation integrity⏎e. Lack of category-specific⏎compliance) responsibilities clear?⏎evaluation criteria⏎(deterministic vs⏎Operations and finance Are benefits measurable and sustainable, and are costs and transfer effects⏎probabilistic), driving⏎(administration, revenue cycle (who pays vs who benefits) understood and managed?⏎duplicative and inconsistent⏎where relevant)⏎reviews⏎Key hurdles⏎● Operational burden of governance and monitoring without standardized, exportable artifacts.⏎● Integration costs and variability across EHRs and sites when standards-based patterns are not⏎consistently implemented.⏎● Unclear update governance (includes regression testing) and inability to manage rapid iteration⏎safety management⏎● Unclear accountability for record content when AI-generated content is written back without⏎provenance.⏎● Limited staff and expertise to run ongoing monitoring, especially in smaller and safety-net settings.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 23 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 8: Interoperability priorities are structured capture, standard launch/invocation, and⏎provenance-aware writeback⏎RFI question: 8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards,⏎and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability widens market opportunities when it reduces per-site integration costs,⏎enables portable governance artifacts, and supports reproducible evaluation across settings.⏎Interoperability should be framed not only as “data access,” but as end-to-end workflow integration:⏎invocation, context passing, writeback, and provenance/audit export that supports governance and⏎research.⏎Specific data types and standards (examples, non-exhaustive)⏎● Core clinical data via USCDI/US Core (problems, meds, allergies, labs, vitals, notes, procedures,⏎immunizations) to support broad clinical AI inputs and evaluation.⏎● Orders, results, and documentation writeback targets with provenance for AI-influenced⏎ordering and note generation.⏎● Quality measurement data pathways (eCQMs and related constructs) to support evaluation and⏎monitoring aligned to existing measurement ecosystems.⏎● Patient-generated and patient-facing data pathways where relevant, with heightened privacy and⏎nondiscrimination governance.⏎Benchmarking tools (what is most useful for HHS to support)⏎Benchmarking that matters for clinical care is workflow-centric and provenance-aware, not only⏎model-centric.⏎HHS should support benchmark suites and test harnesses that evaluate integration patterns (SMART⏎launch context, CDS Hooks invocation, provenance-aware writeback, audit export) in representative⏎scenarios, enabling reproducible evaluation and procurement-ready conformance testing.⏎Exhibit 6 summarizes interoperability priorities and why they matter for scalable, governable clinical AI⏎deployments.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 24 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Exhibit 6. Interoperability building blocks for scalable clinical AI⏎Building block Role in clinical AI integration Why it matters for governance and auditability⏎SMART App Launch Standards-based app launch, context passing, Enables consistent access control and supports⏎(FHIR) + scopes and authorization for data access. auditable, least-privilege integration patterns.⏎CDS Hooks Event-driven invocation for decision support at Supports traceable “when invoked” events and⏎workflow moments (ordering, chart open, etc.). structured context exchange for auditing.⏎FHIR Provenance (and Represent who/what generated data and how it Enables identification of AI-influenced record content⏎related audit patterns) was derived, including AI-influenced content. and supports retrospective reconstruction.⏎FHIR Structured Data Structured capture, prepopulation, and Supports consistent input capture and downstream⏎Capture (SDC) extraction support for forms and questionnaires. extraction, but is not the sole reasoning substrate for⏎deterministic guideline logic.⏎Computable guideline / Represent deterministic eligibility logic and Supports auditability and regression testing when⏎CDS representations guideline-driven decision support in computable linked to provenance, versioning, and source guideline⏎(multiple formats) forms. references.⏎Callable tool/API A generic pattern for invoking external Useful as an implementation pattern, but not a core⏎invocation interfaces tools/services in a controlled way. HHS policy recommendation; auditability expectations⏎(optional architecture should be architecture-neutral.⏎note)⏎Based on these building blocks, HHS can improve comparability across deployments by encouraging a⏎small set of minimum conformance expectations for interoperable clinical AI integrations.⏎Minimum conformance expectations⏎● Implement standards-based invocation (for example, SMART and/or CDS Hooks) with⏎documented context passing.⏎● Maintain provenance-aware writeback for AI-influenced documentation or orders where⏎writeback occurs.⏎● Capture/export user interaction signals (accept, override, modify) for governance monitoring.⏎● Capture/export runtime tool metadata (identity, version, configuration) for retrospective⏎reconstruction.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 25 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 9: Patients need transparent notice and practical recourse when AI influences care⏎decisions⏎RFI question: 9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers do not read audit logs. They want to know: “Was the recommendation I received⏎based on the actual guidelines? Can I trust it? And if it was wrong, how do I get it fixed?”⏎Patient-centered operational needs and concerns⏎● Clear, understandable notice when AI meaningfully influences a recommendation, documentation,⏎or access decision, including what data types were used at a high level.⏎● A practical recourse pathway: how to request correction of underlying data, how to contest an⏎AI-influenced recommendation, and how to request human review.⏎● Ability to access AI-influenced artifacts that are part of the designated record set and to obtain an⏎explanation of how the tool is intended to be used, including known limitations.⏎● Assurance that data use and retention are minimized and protected, and that access is role-based⏎and logged.⏎Exhibit 7 summarizes disclosure and recourse elements that can be operationalized for higher-risk⏎AI-enabled workflows.⏎Exhibit 7. Patient-facing disclosure and recourse elements for higher-risk AI-enabled workflows⏎Element What patients/caregivers should receive Why it matters⏎Plain-language Whether AI was used in generating a recommendation, documentation, or Supports informed trust and avoids⏎disclosure of AI routing decision, and at what point in workflow. deceptive automation.⏎involvement⏎Purpose and The intended use and key limitations in plain language, including when not to Reduces misuse and supports⏎limitations rely on the tool. appropriate escalation.⏎Recourse and How to ask questions, request review, and seek correction where appropriate Supports accountability and trust.⏎correction path (including documentation correction pathways).⏎Privacy and data use What data are used, whether data are shared externally, and key safeguards. Supports informed consent-like⏎summary expectations even where formal consent⏎is not required.⏎Non-discrimination A statement that the organization makes reasonable efforts to identify and Aligns patient-facing transparency with⏎safeguards (where mitigate discrimination risk for relevant decision support tools and how 45 CFR § 92.210 obligations and supports⏎applicable) concerns can be raised. accountability.⏎Designated record When patients inquire about access/amendment, clarify that HIPAA rights Avoids overpromising rights and⏎set nuance depend on whether information is maintained in the designated record set improves procedural clarity.⏎and explain the organization’s process.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 26 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Question 10: HHS should prioritize implementation, governance, and reproducible evaluation⏎infrastructure⏎RFI question: 10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS research priorities should emphasize implementation and governance, not only model⏎performance. Even strong models can fail in production if integration, monitoring, and update⏎governance are weak.⏎Exhibit 8 summarizes research areas and support mechanisms referenced in this submission.⏎Exhibit 8. Research priorities and support mechanisms to accelerate responsible adoption⏎Priority area What HHS can do (fund/convene/standardize) Why it accelerates adoption⏎Workflow-centric benchmark Fund and convene an open test harness for common clinical Reduces duplicative evaluation burden and⏎suites and shared test workflows that includes provenance-aware writeback and audit enables procurement-ready conformance⏎infrastructure export patterns. testing.⏎Post-deployment monitoring Fund methods research and implementation science on Makes monitoring feasible and proportional,⏎methods (“safety signals”) monitoring dashboards (overrides, data quality, disparity signals, reducing risk and increasing trust.⏎incidents) and operational review cadence.⏎Human factors and workflow Fund pragmatic trials and implementation science on clinician Addresses the most common deployment⏎integration failure modes interaction, automation bias, alert fatigue, and documentation failure modes beyond model performance.⏎integrity.⏎Provenance and audit event Convene standards work on provenance tagging and export Enables retrospective reconstruction and⏎standardization for AI-influenced patterns for AI-influenced notes/orders and user actions. accountable governance across vendors.⏎actions⏎Generalizability and external Fund evaluation research on transportability and site-to-site Improves confidence in deployed⏎validity evaluation variability, including subgroup performance and fairness. performance across settings.⏎Nondiscrimination measurement Support research and guidance prototypes that operationalize Aligns adoption with civil rights obligations⏎and mitigation in patient care “reasonable efforts” to identify/mitigate discrimination, aligned and reduces risk of harmful inequities.⏎decision support to OCR requirements.⏎Economic evaluation methods for Fund work to standardize AI-specific cost accounting Improves credibility of ROI claims and⏎AI interventions (including (implementation, maintenance, monitoring, update) and transfer supports better incentive design.⏎transfer effects) analysis (who pays vs who benefits).⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 27 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎10a. Published findings on the impact of adopted clinical AI tools⏎RFI question: 10a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical⏎care?⏎The published evidence base for deployed clinical AI is growing but remains heterogeneous in study⏎design quality, outcomes measured, and generalizability across sites.⏎HHS can strengthen adoption by prioritizing reproducible evaluation infrastructure and consistent⏎reporting rather than relying on isolated model-specific claims.⏎Examples of relevant findings⏎● A recent scoping review of randomized trials evaluating AI interventions in clinical practice⏎highlights both growth in evaluation activity and persistent variability in study designs and⏎endpoints, reinforcing the need for standardized reporting and workflow-context⏎documentation.⏎● Ambient documentation studies have reported improvements in clinician time/burden and⏎related measures in specific settings, but outcomes depend strongly on implementation details⏎and governance controls for writeback and review.⏎● Systematic reviews of online symptom checkers continue to find low and variable diagnostic⏎accuracy, with triage accuracy generally higher but still variable, suggesting that patient-facing⏎tools require careful risk-tiering, disclosures, and monitoring.⏎HHS can increase the utility of published findings by requiring or incentivizing minimum reporting of tool⏎identity/version/configuration, provenance/writeback behavior, and monitoring signals so that studies⏎are interpretable and comparable across sites.⏎10b. Literature on costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI in clinical care⏎RFI question: 10b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part⏎of clinical care?⏎The economic evaluation literature generally suggests that AI interventions can be cost-effective in⏎specific contexts, but systematic reviews identify recurring methodological gaps and inconsistent⏎reporting of AI-specific cost components and implementation realities.⏎These gaps directly affect HHS’s ability to design incentives that reward real-world value rather than⏎optimistic modeling assumptions.⏎What the recent systematic review literature emphasizes (examples)⏎● A 2025 systematic review of cost-effectiveness analyses of AI applications in healthcare reports⏎that many studies conclude AI strategies are cost-effective or cost-saving, but also notes⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 28 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎limitations in modeling assumptions and reporting completeness, including incomplete⏎accounting of implementation and lifecycle costs.⏎● A 2025 systematic review of economic evaluations for AI-assisted cancer screening/diagnosis⏎highlights “widespread neglect” of AI dynamic learning capability and population differences and⏎reports significant omissions in AI cost components, learning-over-time modeling, and⏎implementation barriers.⏎● These reviews underscore the importance of AI-specific reporting standards (e.g., CHEERS-AI)⏎and the need to report costs for integration, monitoring, maintenance, retraining/updates,⏎governance operations, and incident response—costs that materially affect net value and⏎transfer effects.⏎Transfer and incentive considerations (who pays vs who benefits)⏎In many workflows, costs (integration, monitoring, governance operations) accrue to providers while⏎financial benefits may accrue to payers or may be diffuse, creating adoption barriers under⏎fee-for-service.⏎HHS should therefore design incentives that (1) support measurable workflow burden reduction and⏎safety monitoring in bounded scenarios, (2) reward auditable governance practices rather than model⏎claims, and (3) reduce inequitable adoption skew toward highly resourced systems.⏎Reimbursement realism⏎Where payment policy is needed, CMS should prefer value-based constructs, quality pathways, and⏎model participation terms that require auditable governance artifacts, rather than defaulting to a⏎standalone AI billing code that could create utilization incentives without accountability.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 29 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎5. Conclusion: Verified, computable clinical reasoning can connect evidence to⏎action at the point of care⏎HHS can accelerate responsible clinical AI adoption by making accountability feasible in real-world care⏎delivery: risk-tiered governance expectations, interoperable auditability (provenance, user actions, and⏎version/configuration traceability), and incentives for monitoring and evaluation that are proportional to⏎clinical impact and reversibility.⏎A central distinction in this submission is that deterministic guideline-logic artifacts and probabilistic⏎inference services have different runtime behaviors and should not be treated as interchangeable. That⏎distinction matters for evaluation, monitoring, and reimbursement design. At the same time, both⏎require core safeguards: documentation integrity, nondiscrimination protections, and post-deployment⏎safety monitoring appropriate to risk.⏎The opportunity is not only to expand AI use, but to improve how evidence is translated into action at⏎the point of care. Clinical guidelines exist. Interoperability standards exist. Coverage pathways often⏎exist. The remaining gap is frequently operational: reliable, reviewable, computable workflow execution⏎that can be audited, monitored, and improved across settings.⏎Priorities emphasized in this submission⏎● Adopt risk-tiered governance expectations tied to runtime behavior, clinical impact, and⏎reversibility.⏎● Strengthen interoperable auditability expectations for invocation, provenance-aware writeback,⏎user action capture, and runtime version/configuration traceability.⏎● Differentiate evaluation and monitoring expectations for deterministic guideline-logic artifacts⏎versus probabilistic inference services, while preserving shared baseline safeguards.⏎● Align incentives to demonstrated workflow and care value (including monitoring and⏎governance quality), not just tool deployment or unverified performance claims.⏎Near-term progress is achievable through existing HHS levers, including guidance, ONC certification⏎implementation and testing pathways, OCR implementation guidance for 45 CFR § 92.210, and CMS⏎program terms and quality pathways that reward governable, monitorable deployment.⏎The infrastructure for safer, scalable clinical AI is already largely in place. The near-term policy task is to⏎align governance, interoperability, and reimbursement so the market rewards systems that can be⏎reviewed, reconstructed, and trusted in practice.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 30 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎6. Appendices⏎The appendices below provide working definitions, feasible reporting templates, and source references⏎cited in the body text.⏎Appendix A. Acronyms and Definitions⏎A.1 Acronyms⏎Acronym Definition⏎AI Artificial Intelligence⏎AHRQ Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality⏎ASTP Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎CDS Clinical Decision Support⏎CDSi Clinical Decision Support intervention⏎CDS Hooks Clinical Decision Support Hooks⏎CFR Code of Federal Regulations⏎CISO Chief Information Security Officer⏎CIO Chief Information Officer⏎CMIO Chief Medical Information Officer⏎CMS Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎CMMI Center for Medicare & Medicaid Innovation⏎DSI Decision Support Intervention⏎EHR Electronic Health Record⏎eCQM Electronic Clinical Quality Measure⏎FDA Food and Drug Administration⏎FHIR Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources⏎HIPAA Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act⏎HHS Department of Health and Human Services⏎HRRP Hospital Readmissions Reduction Program⏎IQR Hospital Inpatient Quality Reporting Program⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 31 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎MIPS Merit-based Incentive Payment System⏎NIST National Institute of Standards and Technology⏎OCR Office for Civil Rights⏎OIG Office of Inspector General⏎ONC Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎PI Promoting Interoperability⏎PHI Protected Health Information⏎PII Personally Identifiable Information⏎QPP Quality Payment Program⏎RFI Request for Information⏎SDC Structured Data Capture⏎SMART Substitutable Medical Applications, Reusable Technologies⏎SSA Social Security Act⏎USCDI United States Core Data for Interoperability⏎US Core US Core Implementation Guide⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 32 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎A.2 Definitions⏎The working definitions below are included as provided in the source submission.⏎Term Definition⏎Clinical AI / decision Software-enabled methods that generate recommendations, predictions, summaries, or workflow actions⏎support used in clinical care, including both deterministic and probabilistic approaches.⏎Deterministic A deterministic, version-controlled artifact that applies defined guideline/policy logic to inputs to produce⏎guideline-logic artifact repeatable outputs for the same inputs and configuration.⏎Probabilistic inference A service that produces outputs based on statistical inference, often sensitive to data distributions and⏎service subject to performance variation over time and across populations.⏎Formal verification Verification of internal logical properties of a deterministic artifact over defined states (e.g., consistency,⏎(bounded) reachability, completeness), which does not establish clinical benefit or appropriateness across contexts.⏎AI audit artifact A minimum set of artifacts (identity, version/config, provenance/writeback behavior, user action capture,⏎package limitations, monitoring plan, change logs) sufficient to support retrospective reconstruction, governance⏎review, and incident response.⏎Provenance Machine-readable representation of who/what generated information and how it was derived, used to⏎identify AI-influenced content and enable traceability.⏎Post-deployment Ongoing monitoring for safety signals proportional to risk, including data quality/missingness,⏎safety monitoring overrides/escalations, disparate application, incidents, and update impacts (and drift monitoring for⏎probabilistic tools where appropriate).⏎Risk tier A qualitative classification driven by clinical impact, autonomy, patient-facing use, record writeback, scale,⏎reversibility, and discrimination risk triggers.⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 33 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Appendix B. Lightweight Evaluation and Reporting Template (feasible minimum)⏎The fields below are intended to be feasible for developers and deployers to complete and to support⏎procurement review, monitoring, and retrospective analysis without disclosing proprietary model⏎internals.⏎Field Minimum content to report⏎Tool identity Name, developer, intended use, clinical setting, user roles⏎Risk tier justification Trigger-based rationale (clinical impact, autonomy, patient-facing, writeback,⏎reversibility, discrimination risk)⏎Runtime behavior type Deterministic guideline-logic artifact vs probabilistic inference service (or hybrid)⏎Version/configuration Version, configuration parameters, enabled features, update policy⏎Data dependencies Data types used, required fields, missingness sensitivities, data quality checks⏎Output and use Output type, how clinicians should interpret/use outputs, escalation/override⏎processes⏎Evaluation endpoints Workflow endpoints, safety endpoints, subgroup/fairness endpoints where⏎relevant⏎Monitoring signals Override rates, data quality/missingness, disparity signals, incident tracking,⏎update impacts; drift/performance monitoring where appropriate⏎Governance operations Review cadence, responsible roles, incident response process, regression testing⏎(including guideline/version updates)⏎Patient-facing elements Disclosure, recourse, privacy summary, nondiscrimination safeguards⏎(if applicable) communication⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 34 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Appendix C. AI Audit Artifact Package (suggested minimum field set)⏎This package is intended to be stored and accessible for governance and retrospective review. For⏎higher-risk use cases, it should be reviewable by the deploying organization’s clinical governance and⏎compliance functions and support patient recourse requests.⏎Artifact category Expanded minimum content (portable across vendors/sites)⏎Identity and intended use Intended use statement; contraindications; workflow description;⏎user role assumptions⏎Versioning and configuration Artifact/model version; configuration; prompt templates where⏎applicable; feature flags; update cadence⏎Data lineage and dependencies Input data types; mappings; missingness sensitivities;⏎pre-processing; source systems⏎Provenance and record writeback Writeback targets; provenance attachment method; separation of⏎AI-suggested vs clinician-authored content⏎User interaction capture Accept/override/modify; escalation triggers; audit export capability⏎Limitations and failure modes Known limitations; subgroup considerations; robustness notes;⏎security/privacy considerations⏎Verification and testing evidence Deterministic: internal logical property checks + regression tests;⏎Probabilistic: validation approach + robustness checks⏎Monitoring plan Signals, thresholds, review cadence, roles, incident response,⏎documentation updates⏎Nondiscrimination documentation Reasonable-efforts documentation aligned to 45 CFR § 92.210;⏎(if applicable) monitoring and mitigation approach⏎Transfer/payment considerations Workflow cost accounting approach; who bears costs vs who⏎(where relevant) benefits; documentation for program reporting if required⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 35 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Appendix D. References⏎Note: Full URLs are provided in this section only. Citations in the body refer to these numbered⏎references.⏎[1] Federal Register. “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care;⏎Request for Information.” Official published Federal Register PDF (GovInfo), December 23, 2025.⏎https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-12-23/pdf/2025-23641.pdf⏎[2] Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC). “As a Catalyst for⏎Change, the Evolving Decision Support Interventions (DSI) Certification Criterion is a Game Changer,”⏎June 18, 2024.⏎https://www.healthit.gov/buzz-blog/health-it/as-a-catalyst-for-change-the-evolving-decision-support-int⏎erventions-dsi-certification-criterion-is-a-game-changer⏎[3] ONC. “Decision Support Interventions (DSI) Certification Criterion Resource Guide,” May 2024.⏎https://www.healthit.gov/wp-content/uploads/2024/05/DSI-Criterion-Resource-Guide_508.pdf⏎[4] eCFR. 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11) (Decision Support Interventions).⏎https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-C/part-170/subpart-C/section-170.315⏎[5] ONC. “Decision Support Interventions (DSI) – Test Method.”⏎https://www.healthit.gov/test-method/decision-support-interventions-dsi⏎[6] HL7. SMART App Launch (FHIR) Implementation Guide (published). Scopes and launch context page.⏎https://hl7.org/fhir/smart-app-launch/scopes-and-launch-context.html⏎[7] HL7. CDS Hooks Implementation Guide (published). https://cds-hooks.hl7.org⏎[8] HL7. FHIR Structured Data Capture (SDC) IG (published). Extraction operation.⏎https://www.hl7.org/fhir/uv/sdc/extraction.html⏎[9] HL7. FHIR R4 Provenance Resource (published). https://www.hl7.org/fhir/R4/provenance.html⏎[10] eCFR. HIPAA Security Rule (45 CFR 164.312 and related).⏎https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-C/part-164/subpart-C/section-164.312⏎[11] eCFR. Information Blocking Regulations (45 CFR Part 171).⏎https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-D/part-171⏎[12] eCFR. HIPAA Right of Access (45 CFR 164.524).⏎https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-C/part-164/subpart-E/section-164.524⏎[13] eCFR. HIPAA Right to Request Amendment (45 CFR 164.526).⏎https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-C/part-164/subpart-E/section-164.526⏎[14] Vasey B, et al. “DECIDE-AI: New reporting guidelines to bridge the development-to-implementation⏎gap in clinical artificial intelligence.” Nature Medicine. 2022.⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-022-01988-5⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 36 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎[15] Liu X, et al. “CONSORT-AI extension.” Nature Medicine. 2020.⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-020-1034-x⏎[16] Liu X, et al. “SPIRIT-AI extension.” The Lancet Digital Health. 2020.⏎https://www.thelancet.com/journals/landig/article/PIIS2589-7500(20)30219-3/fulltext⏎[17] Han SH, et al. “The clinical and implementation characteristics of randomized controlled trials⏎evaluating AI interventions in clinical practice: A scoping review.” npj Digital Medicine. 2024.⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-024-01111-9⏎[18] Rao A, et al. “Effect of Generative Artificial Intelligence on Physician Time in Notes, Orders, and After⏎Hours.” JAMA Network Open. 2025.⏎https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2833250⏎[19] Jamieson T, et al. “Evaluating the impact of ambient artificial intelligence scribes on clinical⏎documentation.” JAMIA. 2025. https://academic.oup.com/jamia/article/32/1/64/7820055⏎[20] El Arab RA, Al Moosa OA. “Cost-effectiveness analyses of artificial intelligence applications in⏎healthcare: a systematic review.” npj Digital Medicine. 2025.⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-025-01722-y.pdf⏎[21] Zhang Y, et al. “A Systematic Review of the Methods and Quality of Economic Evaluations for⏎Artificial-Intelligence-Assisted Cancer Screening or Diagnosis.” Value in Health. 2025.⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S1098301525056815⏎[22] HL7. US Core Implementation Guide (published versions landing). https://www.hl7.org/fhir/us/core/⏎[23] NIST. “Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework (AI RMF 1.0).” 2023.⏎https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework⏎[24] eCFR. 45 CFR 92.210 (Patient care decision support tools).⏎https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-A/part-92/subpart-B/section-92.210⏎[25] Wallace W, et al. “The diagnostic and triage accuracy of digital and online symptom checker tools: a⏎systematic review.” npj Digital Medicine. 2022.⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41746-022-00658-6.pdf⏎[26] Riboli-Sasco EF, et al. “Triage and Diagnostic Accuracy of Online Symptom Checkers: Systematic⏎Review.” JMIR. 2023. https://www.jmir.org/2023/1/e43868/⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 37 of 38⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Public Comment Docket ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Appendix E. Candidate CFR Citations for Legal Review⏎The CFR citations below are provided as candidate anchors to support counsel validation and to increase⏎responsiveness to the RFI’s request for “specific changes” with “applicable CFR citations.”⏎This appendix is not legal advice and should be reviewed and finalized by counsel for any formal compliance⏎interpretation.⏎ONC / ASTP / interoperability and certification context (candidate anchors)⏎● 45 CFR 170.315(b)(11) (Decision Support Interventions) and related ONC Certification Program provisions⏎supporting transparency and intervention risk management concepts.⏎● Associated ONC test methods and implementation guidance as the primary operational mechanism for⏎making auditability and export expectations testable.⏎OCR / nondiscrimination (candidate anchors)⏎● 45 CFR § 92.210 (patient care decision support tools), including requirements prohibiting discrimination⏎through use of such tools and the duty to make reasonable efforts to identify and mitigate discrimination⏎risk where tools employ input variables measuring protected characteristics.⏎HIPAA Security Rule (candidate anchors)⏎● 45 CFR 164.312 and related Security Rule provisions addressing audit controls and security safeguards,⏎implemented through documented policies rather than fixed retention-duration mandates.⏎Information blocking (candidate anchors)⏎● 45 CFR Part 171 (information blocking regulations), implemented with appropriate reliance on the⏎exceptions framework (e.g., privacy, security, infeasibility, fees, licensing), rather than implying blanket⏎access rights.⏎CMS (candidate anchors for counsel validation; mechanism-level proposals in Section 4.2)⏎● MIPS / Quality Payment Program: 42 CFR Part 414, Subpart O (Quality Payment Program framework).⏎● Promoting Interoperability (PI): 42 CFR § 414.1375 (Promoting Interoperability performance category).⏎● Improvement Activities (IA): Candidate anchor within Part 414 Subpart O for Improvement Activities⏎performance category (e.g., provisions addressing IA scoring/requirements), to be validated by counsel for⏎the specific mechanism used.⏎● Hospital quality reporting (IQR): 42 CFR § 412.140 (Hospital Inpatient Quality Reporting (IQR) Program).⏎Public Comment (intended for public posting) Page 38 of 38♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0364♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Wilson♔♔Minnesota♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Marcus spent almost eight months being treated for the wrong thing at a clinic outside Duluth. Eight months. And when we finally figured out what was actually going on, we found out the symptom-flagging software they were using had a known error pattern that apparently nobody had bothered to cross-check with the doctors. The clinic kept telling us the computer didn't lie. Meanwhile Marcus was getting worse.⏎⏎So when I see HHS talking about accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings, I genuinely don't know how to feel about that. I understand there might be benefits. I'm not totally against technology in medicine. But "accelerating adoption" sounds like moving faster, and my question is faster toward what exactly. Faster toward more situations like Marcus's, where the software is wrong and nobody catches it because everyone trusts the system?⏎⏎What I want to know is what the plan is for oversight. Not in some vague way. Like specifically, who is required to check the AI's work before it affects a patient's diagnosis or treatment. Is that even written down anywhere right now? Because at that clinic outside Duluth it clearly wasn't, or if it was nobody followed it.⏎⏎Anyway I also came here to ask about something else and I know this probably isn't the right place but I genuinely don't know where else to go. I applied for my Medicare card three months ago and it still hasn't come. I've called twice and been put on hold both times for over 40 minutes. The second time, on March 14th, someone finally answered and told me it was "processing" and to wait another 4 to 6 weeks, and that was already six weeks ago now. I'm 36 so this is through a disability determination and I really need this resolved before my next appointment in June because without that card I don't even know if I can get seen.⏎⏎I know this comment is supposed to be about AI in clinical care. But the thing is, for people like me, it's all connected. If the system can't even mail me a card after three months, I have very little confidence that the same system is going to responsibly manage something as complicated as AI diagnostic tools in a way that protects patients. That's not me being unfair, that's just my experience.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0365♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Smith♔♔California♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly not sure if this is the right place to submit something like this. My neighbor sent me a link from a Facebook post about HHS taking comments on AI in healthcare and I followed it here. If this isn't where individual patients are supposed to comment, I apologize, but I'm going to write this anyway.⏎⏎My name is Michael Smith, I'm 66, I live in Stockton California, and I work in a warehouse. I also have a cardiac condition that I've been managing since 2022. My cardiologist retired and the practice I was using basically handed my ongoing care over to some kind of automated system that would flag my chart and generate recommendations. I don't know the exact name of the software. Nobody told me what it was called. In March of last year the system flagged my chart and recommended a dosage adjustment on one of my medications. I looked into it because I'm the kind of person who reads things. What I found was that the clinical trial data the algorithm appeared to be drawing from was heavily skewed toward men under 50. I'm 66. That's a pretty significant gap.⏎⏎I only caught this because I happen to read medical literature in my spare time which is an absurd thing to have to say. Most of the guys I work with would not have caught it. They would have just taken the adjusted dose.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care I genuinely don't know how to feel about that. I understand there might be benefits. But nobody at my doctor's office ever told me that an algorithm was making recommendations about my care. There was no conversation about it. It just happened. I couldn't tell you whether a human physician reviewed the flag before it reached me or not.⏎⏎That's the part I keep coming back to. Not whether AI should be used, I don't have a strong opinion on that either way, but whether patients are supposed to know when it's being used on them. And whether somebody checks whether the data the system was trained on actually reflects people like me.⏎⏎I don't know what an RFI is. I'm guessing it stands for request for information? If so, here is my information: I am a 66 year old warehouse worker in California whose cardiac care was partially managed by a system I was never told about, trained on data that may not have included people my age, and I had no way to raise a concern about it through any official channel. I wrote a letter to the practice and got a form response.⏎⏎I'm not asking for AI to be banned or anything dramatic like that. I just think if something is going to make recommendations about my heart medication someone should probably tell me that's happening, and someone should probably have checked whether the training data included men over 60 before they pointed it at patients.⏎⏎I also want to say I'm genuinely uncertain whether submitting this here does anything at all, but the link said public comments were being accepted through this portal so here I am.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0366♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Digital Medicine Society (DiMe), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Digital Medicine Society (DiMe)♔♔♔♔♔♔Docusign Envelope ID: CC5BF07A-DF40-4A31-9CDD-46C0AEC10F7B⏎February 23, 2026⏎Subject: Response to Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎(HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001)⏎To Secretary Robert F Kennedy, Jr., U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎(HHS), and Steven Posnack, Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎(ASTP):⏎The Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) is a global non-profit dedicated to advancing the⏎safe, effective, and equitable use of digital approaches to redefine healthcare and⏎improve lives. We appreciate the opportunity to respond to this Request for⏎Information (RFI) on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in⏎clinical care.⏎DiMe convenes the ecosystem directly responsible for operationalizing AI adoption:⏎developers, health systems, regulators, policymakers, payors, and patients. Our⏎response focuses on practical barriers and policy levers identified by our team and⏎community, emphasizing alignment across regulation, payment, governance,⏎interoperability, and workforce readiness.⏎1. Barriers to AI Innovation and Adoption⏎The most significant barriers to private sector innovation and adoption of AI in clinical⏎care are economic, operational, and governance-related.⏎● Payment and revenue friction : Startups lack early revenue pathways to⏎generate sustainable income prior to broad reimbursement. Fee-for-service⏎payment structures reward clinician time and service volume rather than⏎efficiency, automation, or measurable outcome improvement.⏎● Regulatory and governance uncertainty : Developers face unpredictability in⏎FDA review timelines and expectations amidst evolving guidance, particularly⏎when determining whether tools meet the definition of a medical device.⏎Health systems and clinicians also face challenges in determining whether AI⏎outputs are reliable, valid, and appropriate for their patient populations.⏎● Health system procurement and workflow integration : Selling into hospital⏎systems remains difficult due to point-solution fatigue, data and platform⏎fragmentation, and heterogeneous privacy, security, and legal review processes.⏎Integration into existing workflows, particularly alignment across IT⏎infrastructure and governance guardrails, is disparate and resource-intensive.⏎Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) | www.dimesociety.org⏎90 Canal Street, 4th Floor, Boston, Massachusetts, 02114, USA⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: CC5BF07A-DF40-4A31-9CDD-46C0AEC10F7B⏎Institutional friction and misaligned incentives, rather than technical capability,⏎remain the primary constraints on adoption of rapidly advancing clinical AI systems.⏎2. Priority Regulatory and Payment Changes⏎HHS should prioritize regulatory and payment coordination that reflects the realities⏎of adaptive AI systems and evolving clinical use cases.⏎● Support coordinated and redesigned payment models : The collaboration⏎between U.S. Food and Drug Administration’s (FDA) TEMPO program and⏎Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services’ (CMS) ACCESS initiative shows how⏎coordinated regulatory and payment structures could support adaptive⏎oversight and reimbursement redesign. CMS can further use existing payment⏎authorities (e.g., 42 CFR § 410.26; 42 CFR § 414.40) and value-based program⏎mechanisms (e.g., 42 CFR Part 414, Subpart O) to incentivize adoption of AI in⏎high-burden, undermeasured conditions, including women’s health .⏎● Illustrate clearer boundaries through use cases : The ecosystem requires⏎greater insight into how models are trained and why certain AI products do or⏎do not fall under FDA oversight as medical devices. Recent examples, such as⏎Doctronic’s AI-supported prescription renewal, illustrate the need for⏎transparent, use-case-driven explanations so developers and health systems⏎can design and implement responsibly within defined regulatory guardrails.⏎● Enable adaptive oversight pathways : Where strong post-market monitoring,⏎documentation, and performance dashboards are in place, HHS should⏎consider mechanisms that allow expanded AI functionality or streamlined⏎review processes without requiring duplicative submissions. The recent citizen⏎petition requesting exemption from certain premarket notification⏎requirements ( FDA-2025-P-5560 ) illustrates industry interest in tying regulatory⏎flexibility to demonstrable lifecycle monitoring capability.⏎Regulatory clarity, payment redesign, and cross-agency alignment will reduce⏎uncertainty and accelerate responsible AI adoption.⏎3. Legal and Implementation Challenges⏎Non-device AI tools raise governance and accountability challenges that existing⏎frameworks do not fully address.⏎● Training on proportional risk : The updated clinical decision support system⏎(CDSS) guidance , emphasizing transparency and interpretability, introduces a⏎need to train clinicians to review AI outputs relative to the strength of evidence⏎and potential clinical impact.⏎Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) | www.dimesociety.org⏎90 Canal Street, 4th Floor, Boston, Massachusetts, 02114, USA⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: CC5BF07A-DF40-4A31-9CDD-46C0AEC10F7B⏎● Integrating data from non-devices : The updated general wellness guidance ,⏎focused on physiologic parameter outputs, may result in disparate forms of⏎clinical measures being integrated into the EHR. When these data influence⏎clinical workflows, governance mechanisms must ensure the integrity,⏎auditability, accountability, and appropriate use of all inputs to clinical⏎decision-making.⏎● Fragmented frameworks : Scaling innovative technology is slowed by disparate⏎privacy, security, and legal frameworks across health systems. Establishing a⏎harmonized baseline would reduce duplicative review processes that delay⏎adoption, such as by endorsing pathways like the DiMe Seal , which was⏎developed to create a consistent benchmark for evidence, privacy, security, and⏎usability for everyone adopting digital health software products.⏎HHS can play a coordinating role in clarifying and aligning how these expectations⏎function across agencies.⏎4. AI Evaluation Methods for Clinical Care⏎Effective evaluation requires both structured pre-deployment benchmarking and⏎disciplined post-deployment monitoring.⏎● Pre-deployment benchmarking : AI tools should be evaluated against defined⏎performance metrics (e.g., drift and bias), with clear documentation artifacts⏎describing intended use, limitations, and validation methods. DiMe’s The Playbook :⏎Implementing AI in Healthcare includes a section detailing relevant metrics⏎such as defined KPIs and measures of success.⏎● Post-deployment monitoring : Live tools should undergo regular evaluation⏎against those benchmarked metrics, supported by performance dashboards⏎that enable human oversight and, as capabilities continue to advance, agentic⏎supervisory approaches to detect drift or degradation across deployments (e.g.,⏎"AI monitoring AI"). The Playbook includes information on a minimum monitoring⏎stack and governance best practices, and DiMe is committed to building upon⏎this foundation with our upcoming project focused on operationalizing AI⏎governance in healthcare .⏎● Ecosystem-level learning : Rather than mandating a single harmonized⏎evaluation process, HHS should support mechanisms that enable transparent⏎sharing of implementation lessons, including case study libraries and federated⏎sandbox environments that capture both successes (e.g., improved outcomes)⏎and failures (e.g., evidence of harm).⏎Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) | www.dimesociety.org⏎90 Canal Street, 4th Floor, Boston, Massachusetts, 02114, USA⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: CC5BF07A-DF40-4A31-9CDD-46C0AEC10F7B⏎HHS can lead on enabling shared learning from testing and evaluation to strengthen⏎accountability while preserving flexibility for AI innovation.⏎5. Private-sector Activities HHS Can Support⏎HHS can support private-sector activities that strengthen trust and reduce⏎duplicative oversight burdens for AI-enabled healthcare.⏎● Integration of comprehensive frameworks : Recognizing comprehensive⏎frameworks of standards and best practices, such as the DiMe Seal , will build⏎trust in the market, accelerate the adoption of digital health tools to improve⏎health outcomes, and invite a continuous cycle of rigorous evaluation to⏎elevate the practices of digital health. HHS should explore pathways to⏎incorporating these frameworks into federal programs (e.g., Medicare App⏎Library) where appropriate.⏎● Streamlined regulatory flexibility mechanisms : While the FDA’s precertification⏎pilot program did not achieve its intended outcomes, alternative approaches to⏎regulatory flexibility, such as mechanisms contemplated in the citizen petition⏎( FDA-2025-P-5560 ), should be further considered, as they may offer more⏎targeted pathways to streamline oversight supported by robust lifecycle⏎monitoring.⏎Together, these activities can promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care⏎while reducing duplicative review and strengthening accountability.⏎6. Where AI Has Met Expectations or More Evidence is Needed⏎In certain applications that directly impact clinical operations, care providers, and⏎patient experience, AI tools are already meeting or exceeding expectations. Where⏎evidence is variable or limited, impact will depend on integration and monitoring.⏎● Surgical scheduling : AI to predict accurate surgical case duration has⏎demonstrated improved surgical suite utilization and margins.⏎● Automated triage : AI to expedite psychiatric triaging promises to address long⏎wait times for mental health care by facilitating more timely, potentially⏎lifesaving care via improved intake in behavioral health settings.⏎● Ambient documentation : Peer-reviewed literature reports reduced⏎documentation burden and improved clinician experience following ambient AI⏎scribe deployment, while realized productivity and financial impact will depend⏎heavily on workflow integration and performance monitoring.⏎Comprehensive monitoring as part of operationalized governance will further⏎illuminate where and how AI makes the most impact in clinical care settings.⏎Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) | www.dimesociety.org⏎90 Canal Street, 4th Floor, Boston, Massachusetts, 02114, USA⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: CC5BF07A-DF40-4A31-9CDD-46C0AEC10F7B⏎7. Decision-makers and Administrative Barriers⏎Adoption of AI in clinical care is shaped by internal operational decision-making⏎structures, including people, processes, and technologies.⏎● Roles and decision-makers : IT departments, formal or informal AI governance⏎committees (where they exist), and individuals responsible for workforce⏎training and education play central roles in determining whether and how AI⏎tools are safely adopted.⏎● Administrative hurdles : Primary administrative barriers include lack of⏎operationalized governance processes, point-solution fatigue, workflow⏎integration challenges, and limited access to an ecosystem of tools that meet⏎harmonized privacy, security, and integration requirements and can be⏎deployed quickly with robust post-deployment monitoring.⏎● Supporting resources : Efforts including DiMe’s upcoming Operationalizing AI⏎Governance in Healthcare project, the governance section of The Playbook :⏎Implementing AI in Healthcare , and the DiMe Seal provide practical support for⏎organizations implementing consistent oversight, accelerating responsible⏎adoption across the ecosystem by building a strong foundation of trust.⏎Clarifying roles, reducing administrative friction, and equipping organizations with⏎practical evaluation frameworks and governance tools will be essential to scaling⏎clinical AI adoption responsibly.⏎8. Interoperability and Data Standards⏎Clarifying what interoperability is intended to achieve, and supporting it with unified⏎code systems and documentation artifacts, will help developers avoid unnecessary⏎implementation hurdles.⏎● Interoperability beyond EHR integration : Interoperability should foremost⏎enable timely and safe access to actionable information for clinical users.⏎Rather than requiring full archival integration into the EHR, human-centered⏎dashboards supported by standardized API access can provide a faster and⏎safer pathway to AI implementation. API availability should support third-party⏎integration without creating unnecessary barriers to health system adoption.⏎● Code system expansion : The Veterans’ Administration (VA) developed a⏎patient-generated health data (PGHD) code system , demonstrating how⏎structured integration pathways can support implementation within a defined⏎ecosystem. However, approaches that are not industry-wide limit broader⏎development, interoperability, and adoption across systems and care settings.⏎Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) | www.dimesociety.org⏎90 Canal Street, 4th Floor, Boston, Massachusetts, 02114, USA⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: CC5BF07A-DF40-4A31-9CDD-46C0AEC10F7B⏎● Regulatory documentation artifacts : AI-enabled medical device and⏎sensor-based digital health technology (sDHT) developers can provide⏎standardized documentation for regulatory submissions to streamline both⏎health system and FDA review. Consistent documentation expectations have⏎been articulated in recent final guidance , creating a pathway for developers to⏎avoid duplicative review and accelerate responsible deployment.⏎User-centered interoperability strategies paired with standardized code systems and⏎documentation expectations will widen market access while preserving safety and⏎regulatory integrity in AI-enabled care environments.⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Challenges⏎Patient and caregiver AI challenges stem from broad healthcare digitization and⏎workflow gaps, not resistance to the technology.⏎● Outdated workflows and misaligned expectations: Both patients and clinicians⏎experience friction when AI is introduced into legacy workflows. Patients facing⏎long wait times for appointments often turn to AI to research symptoms or⏎interpret results, while physicians express concerns about misinformation with⏎limited time to address AI outputs within existing reimbursement structures.⏎Introducing AI into outdated workflows without redesign can exacerbate⏎tension rather than improve care delivery.⏎● Patient education and records access: Structured education on responsible AI⏎use can improve clinical encounters and reduce confusion. Educational⏎priorities include how to securely and efficiently access medical records, how⏎to interpret AI-generated outputs cautiously, and how to prepare informed⏎questions for appointments. Modernizing patient-facing reports (e.g., imaging,⏎pathology, and laboratory results) to be clearer and more structured may also⏎reduce reliance on AI interpretation and limit avoidable prompting errors.⏎● Clinician training and trust-building: Clinicians require training to engage⏎constructively with patients who use AI tools, including how to discuss⏎AI-generated insights transparently and respectfully. Health systems can⏎support communication and trust by deploying AI applications that are both⏎patient- and provider-facing, ensuring shared visibility into tools informing care.⏎● Supporting resources : DiMe is launching the Scaling Trusted, High-Impact AI⏎Care Navigation project, focused on care journeys that identify patient and⏎caregiver friction points and opportunities for AI-enabled workflows that⏎demonstrate trust, utility, and impact, including return on investment (ROI).⏎Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) | www.dimesociety.org⏎90 Canal Street, 4th Floor, Boston, Massachusetts, 02114, USA⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: CC5BF07A-DF40-4A31-9CDD-46C0AEC10F7B⏎Digitizing the ecosystem, redesigning AI-enabled workflows and care journeys, and⏎educating patients, caregivers, and clinicians are essential to realizing the benefits of⏎AI-enabled care while preserving trust.⏎10. Priority Areas for AI Research⏎Targeted research investments can accelerate adoption by clarifying economic value,⏎clinical impact, and the infrastructure required to sustain responsible AI deployment.⏎● ROI models : Research on ROI models for AI tools can clarify how value is⏎created and distributed across stakeholders, supporting more informed⏎adoption and payment decisions for both established and emerging use cases.⏎● Clinical impact studies : Research comparing AI-enabled outcomes with⏎standard human-led care processes, such as this study demonstrating⏎consistently favorable outcomes via AI-supported mammography screening,⏎provide the evidence of clinical impact needed to spur ecosystem adoption.⏎● Supporting resources : DiMe is committed to convening the digital healthcare⏎ecosystem in pursuit of answering these high-priority research areas. For⏎example, our upcoming project Scaling Trusted, High-Impact AI Care Navigation⏎will produce a trust-utility-impact framework to classify which use clinical⏎care use cases are ready for AI enablement, alongside evidence-informed ROI⏎models estimating value in terms of outcomes, cost savings, and retention.⏎Prioritizing research that connects clinical performance and economic impact will⏎support responsible scaling of AI in clinical care.⏎In Closing⏎DiMe appreciates the opportunity to contribute to this RFI. Accelerating AI adoption in⏎clinical care will require coordinated regulatory clarity, payment reform,⏎interoperability and governance infrastructure, and workforce readiness. We welcome⏎continued collaboration between our community and HHS to support practical,⏎scalable, and trustworthy AI implementation across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎Sincerely,⏎Benjamin Vandendriessche, PhD Jennifer Goldsack, MChem, MA, MBA, OLY⏎President & Chief Scientific Officer Chief Executive Officer⏎Digital Medicine Society (DiMe) | www.dimesociety.org⏎90 Canal Street, 4th Floor, Boston, Massachusetts, 02114, USA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0367♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from CPAC Foundation Center for Regulatory Freedom, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔CPAC Foundation Center for Regulatory Freedom♔♔♔♔♔♔M E M O R A N D U M⏎To: Hon. Robert F. Kennedy, Jr., Secretary, United States Department of Health⏎and Human Services⏎From: Andrew Langer, Director, Center for Regulatory Freedom, CPAC⏎Foundation⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎Re: Department of Health and Human Services Information Collection Request,⏎“HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care”, Docket⏎#HHS-ONC-2026-001, Fed. Reg. 2025-23641, Published January 5, 2026⏎Below are comments of the American Conservative Union Foundation's (d/b/a. Conservative⏎Political Action Coalition Foundation) (hereinafter “CPAC Foundation”) Center for Regulatory⏎Freedom (hereinafter “CRF”), in response to the Department of Health and Human Services⏎Information Collection Request, “HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care”, Docket⏎#HHS-ONC-2026-001, Fed. Reg. 2025-23641, published January 5, 2026.⏎CRF is a project of the CPAC Foundation, a non-profit, non-partisan 501(c)(3) research and⏎education foundation. Our mission is to inject a common-sense perspective into the regulatory⏎process, to ensure that the risks and costs of regulations are fully based on sound scientific and⏎economic evidence, and to ensure that the voices, interests, and freedoms of Americans, and⏎especially of small businesses, are fully represented in the regulatory process and debates.⏎Finally, we work to ensure that regulatory proposals address real problems, that the proposals⏎serve to ameliorate those problems, and, perhaps most importantly, that those proposals do not,⏎in fact, make public policy problems worse.⏎Introduction⏎The Center for Regulatory Freedom (CRF) supports responsible, innovation-driven reform in⏎health care delivery. Advances in data analytics, digital infrastructure, and artificial intelligence⏎present meaningful opportunities to modernize clinical workflows, reduce inefficiencies, and⏎1⏎⏎strengthen the overall performance of the health system. Sound regulatory policy should enable⏎beneficial innovation while preserving the safeguards necessary to protect patients and sustain⏎trust in medical institutions.⏎Artificial intelligence, in particular, holds substantial promise. Properly deployed, AI systems⏎can reduce administrative burden on clinicians, streamline documentation requirements, assist⏎with longitudinal chart review, identify care gaps, and surface clinically relevant patterns that⏎might otherwise be overlooked in complex patient histories. In an era where physicians face⏎escalating paperwork obligations and time constraints, intelligent decision-support tools can⏎improve efficiency and allow clinicians to focus more fully on direct patient care.⏎At the same time, medical care is uniquely sensitive among regulated domains. Errors in this⏎context are not merely financial or reputational; they may produce irreversible physical harm.⏎Patients are often in vulnerable states, lack specialized knowledge, and must rely on professional⏎expertise. Moreover, health care involves profound information asymmetries between providers⏎and patients, as well as fiduciary obligations that distinguish it from most other commercial⏎environments. For these reasons, the governance framework for AI in medical care must be more⏎disciplined than general-purpose software regulation.⏎A further structural reality must inform HHS’s approach: the longstanding value of longitudinal⏎primary care relationships. Continuity with a primary care physician strengthens clinical⏎accuracy, improves medication management, reduces duplicative testing, and enhances early⏎detection of emerging conditions. A physician who knows a patient’s history—both medical and⏎contextual—is better positioned to make sound decisions than one encountering that patient for⏎the first time without adequate background.⏎However, evolving care models are reshaping how patients access services. Younger cohorts⏎increasingly rely on telehealth platforms, urgent care centers, and episodic or on-demand primary⏎care arrangements. While these models expand access and convenience, they also introduce⏎fragmentation risks. In such an environment, informational continuity becomes as important as⏎relational continuity. AI-assisted clinical briefing tools—designed to synthesize longitudinal⏎patient information at the point of care—may help bridge that gap, provided they are⏎appropriately governed.⏎The central policy question, therefore, is not whether AI should be used in medical care, but how⏎to preserve the safety and quality benefits of traditional primary care relationships while⏎accommodating technological advancement and evolving delivery models. The goal should be to⏎strengthen physician decision-making through structured, transparent information support—not⏎to displace clinical judgment or automate high-stakes decisions without accountability.⏎CRF’s position is that HHS should adopt a calibrated, risk-tiered governance framework for AI⏎in medical care. Permissionless innovation should remain the default posture for low-risk,⏎assistive functions that operate under clear transparency and human-review safeguards. At the⏎same time, heightened evidentiary and oversight requirements should apply where AI systems⏎materially influence diagnosis, triage, treatment selection, or other consequential clinical⏎decisions. Such a balanced framework will promote innovation, preserve patient safety, and⏎ensure that artificial intelligence serves as a tool to reinforce—rather than erode—the integrity of⏎the physician-patient relationship.⏎2⏎⏎Executive Summary⏎Artificial intelligence is rapidly entering the clinical environment. Properly structured, these⏎technologies can enhance physician efficiency, reduce administrative burden, and improve⏎patient care. Improperly governed, however, they risk amplifying fragmentation, obscuring⏎accountability, and introducing preventable safety concerns. HHS faces a pivotal policy choice:⏎how to enable innovation in health care AI while preserving patient safety, physician judgment,⏎and system integrity.⏎CRF submits that the appropriate framework is neither blanket precaution nor unbounded⏎permissionless deployment. Instead, HHS should adopt a calibrated, risk-tiered, lifecycle⏎governance model that preserves innovation for low-risk assistive functions while applying⏎proportionate safeguards where AI materially influences clinical outcomes.⏎Substantively:⏎• Continuity of care is a measurable quality and safety asset. Longitudinal primary care⏎relationships reduce fragmentation, improve medication management, and enhance⏎clinical accuracy by preserving contextual patient knowledge.⏎• Care delivery models are evolving. Younger cohorts increasingly rely on episodic,⏎telehealth, and on-demand services, creating structural information discontinuity even as⏎access expands.⏎• AI-powered “clinical briefing” tools offer a potential bridge. Properly designed⏎systems can synthesize longitudinal patient data into concise, structured summaries that⏎enhance physician situational awareness at the point of care.⏎• Material risks must be addressed. These include hallucinated or fabricated data,⏎omission of critical contraindications, automation bias, privacy vulnerabilities, model⏎drift, and over-reliance that weakens independent clinical reasoning.⏎• A binary “precautionary vs. permissionless” framework is ill-suited to health care.⏎Medical AI presents heterogeneous risk profiles that require calibrated governance rather⏎than categorical approval or prohibition.⏎• CRF recommends a risk-tiered, lifecycle-based governance model:⏎o Permissionless deployment for low-risk assistive functions, subject to baseline⏎transparency, provenance, and human-review safeguards.⏎o Escalating oversight triggers for tools that materially influence diagnosis, triage,⏎treatment, or other consequential clinical decisions.⏎o Strong accountability, auditability, incident reporting, and post-deployment⏎performance monitoring to address model drift and real-world safety signals.⏎• HHS should harmonize governance across CMS, ONC, and FDA authorities.⏎Fragmented or duplicative regulatory approaches will undermine clarity, slow responsible⏎innovation, and increase compliance burdens without commensurate safety gains.⏎A disciplined, risk-tiered approach will allow AI to reinforce informational continuity in an era⏎of evolving care models, strengthen the physician-patient relationship, and improve affordability⏎through efficiency gains—while maintaining clear lines of accountability and patient safety⏎protections.⏎3⏎⏎I. CONTINUITY OF CARE AS A QUALITY AND SAFETY FOUNDATION⏎Longitudinal primary care relationships function as a structural safeguard within the health care⏎system. A primary care physician who follows a patient over time accumulates contextual⏎knowledge that cannot be fully captured in isolated clinical notes. This continuity reduces⏎fragmentation in decision-making by anchoring diagnosis, treatment, and referral decisions in a⏎coherent understanding of the patient’s medical trajectory. In this sense, continuity is not merely⏎a matter of convenience; it is an operational mechanism for improving safety and clinical⏎precision.⏎A sustained physician–patient relationship strengthens diagnostic accuracy and therapeutic⏎calibration. Familiarity with prior medication responses, historical lab trends, chronic condition⏎progression, and patterns of symptom presentation enables clinicians to interpret new complaints⏎in light of established baselines. Subtle deviations that might appear insignificant in a single⏎encounter may carry greater meaning when viewed longitudinally. Likewise, knowledge of prior⏎adverse reactions or treatment failures helps prevent repetition of ineffective or harmful⏎interventions.⏎Continuity also extends beyond strictly clinical data. Primary care physicians often develop⏎awareness of social determinants, behavioral health factors, family history nuances, and lifestyle⏎constraints that influence treatment adherence and outcomes. Understanding a patient’s⏎employment schedule, caregiving responsibilities, transportation access, or financial limitations⏎can materially shape care planning. These contextual variables frequently determine whether a⏎treatment recommendation is feasible, sustainable, and safe.⏎When continuity erodes, the system incurs measurable inefficiencies and risks. Fragmented care⏎environments increase the likelihood of duplicative testing, inconsistent medication⏎management, and missed preventive screenings. Episodic providers who lack access to complete⏎patient histories may order repeat diagnostics out of caution, inadvertently raising costs and⏎patient burden. More concerning, incomplete knowledge of prior contraindications or drug⏎interactions can introduce avoidable safety hazards.⏎Discontinuity also weakens follow-through. When responsibility for care is diffuse,⏎accountability for monitoring results, adjusting medications, or ensuring specialist coordination⏎can become unclear. Missed follow-ups, unmanaged chronic conditions, and delayed⏎interventions become more likely in the absence of a central coordinating clinician. Continuity⏎therefore functions as a stabilizing force in an otherwise complex and decentralized system.⏎At the same time, care delivery patterns are evolving. Younger cohorts increasingly utilize⏎telehealth platforms, urgent care centers, retail clinics, and other on-demand service models.⏎These models expand access and convenience, and they may lower certain barriers to entry for⏎patients who might otherwise defer care. However, they inherently reduce relational continuity⏎and increase the likelihood that individual encounters occur without the benefit of long-term⏎contextual knowledge.⏎In this environment, policy should prioritize preservation of informational continuity even where⏎relational continuity declines. If patients increasingly move between providers or platforms, the⏎system must ensure that longitudinal medical information remains coherent, accessible, and⏎4⏎⏎clinically actionable. The objective should not be to resist innovation in care access models, but⏎to mitigate the fragmentation risks that accompany them.⏎Artificial intelligence, properly structured, may serve as a continuity amplifier rather than a⏎substitute for physician judgment. AI-powered clinical briefing tools can synthesize longitudinal⏎data into structured summaries that restore situational awareness at the point of care. However,⏎these tools must operate in support of—rather than in place of—the primary care physician’s⏎reasoning. Continuity ultimately resides in accountable human judgment; technology should⏎strengthen that judgment by improving information clarity, not displace it.⏎II. THE “AI CLINICAL BRIEFING” MODEL: OPPORTUNITY AND FUNCTIONAL⏎ROLE⏎The specific use case CRF supports is the AI-powered “clinical briefing” tool deployed⏎immediately prior to a patient encounter. These systems ingest structured and unstructured data⏎from electronic health records—including problem lists, medication histories, laboratory trends,⏎imaging results, prior encounter notes, discharge summaries, and referral documentation—and⏎synthesize that information into a concise, structured pre-visit summary for the treating clinician.⏎The purpose is to reduce cognitive and time burdens associated with manual chart review while⏎preserving access to the underlying data.⏎Modern EHR environments often contain years of accumulated information presented in⏎fragmented, duplicative, or poorly organized formats. Clinicians operating under time constraints⏎may face dozens or even hundreds of pages of notes for a complex patient. AI summarization⏎tools can compress that volume into an actionable briefing that highlights relevant history, recent⏎changes, unresolved issues, and potential risk factors. When properly designed, such tools⏎enhance situational awareness without displacing clinical discretion.⏎It is essential to distinguish between three distinct functional categories. First, administrative⏎summarization tools primarily organize and condense information already present in the record.⏎They may extract key data points, compile timelines, or generate structured summaries, but they⏎do not introduce new diagnostic or treatment recommendations. These tools function as⏎informational aids rather than decision engines.⏎Second, clinical insight–highlighting tools go beyond simple summarization by identifying⏎patterns or flagging potential concerns. For example, they may highlight medication interactions,⏎overdue screenings, abnormal lab trends, or inconsistencies in documentation. While still⏎assistive, these systems begin to shape clinical attention and therefore carry incrementally greater⏎influence over decision-making.⏎Third, diagnostic or treatment recommendation engines actively suggest courses of action, risk⏎scores, triage determinations, or therapeutic pathways. These systems materially influence⏎clinical judgment and may, in some contexts, function with a degree of autonomy. They operate⏎at a higher risk tier and require correspondingly stronger validation and oversight.⏎The “AI clinical briefing” model contemplated here is primarily focused on the first two⏎categories—administrative summarization and bounded insight highlighting—rather than⏎autonomous diagnostic decision-making. The goal is to improve information clarity, not to⏎5⏎⏎substitute algorithmic judgment for physician reasoning. The tool’s core function is compression⏎and organization, not independent clinical authority.⏎Properly designed briefing systems can materially reduce chart-review burden. By surfacing⏎medication changes, prior adverse events, hospitalizations, and pending follow-ups in a⏎structured format, they allow clinicians to enter the patient encounter better prepared and more⏎focused. This improved preparation may enhance both efficiency and quality, particularly in⏎environments where patients move between providers or care settings.⏎Importantly, the objective is information compression rather than clinical replacement. The⏎physician remains responsible for interpreting the summarized information, validating its⏎relevance, and making ultimate care decisions. The tool should present data with traceability and⏎transparency, enabling clinicians to drill down into underlying records as needed. At no point⏎should the system obscure the source of its conclusions or operate beyond physician oversight.⏎Integration into physician workflow is critical. The briefing must be delivered in a manner that⏎supports pre-visit preparation without disrupting established clinical processes. It should be⏎available within the existing EHR environment, clearly labeled as assistive, and accompanied by⏎appropriate disclaimers regarding scope and limitations. The system should facilitate review and⏎correction, not create friction or encourage passive acceptance.⏎When appropriately bounded—limited to summarization and structured highlighting, operating⏎under explicit physician authority, and supported by transparency safeguards—the AI clinical⏎briefing model represents a low-to-moderate risk application of artificial intelligence in health⏎care. Its potential to restore informational continuity in fragmented care environments justifies a⏎governance posture that enables deployment while maintaining disciplined guardrails. Properly⏎calibrated policy can allow these tools to strengthen physician performance without⏎compromising safety or accountability.⏎III. RISK CATEGORIES AND FAILURE MODES IN CLINICAL AI⏎Artificial intelligence in clinical environments presents heterogeneous and context-dependent⏎risks. A disciplined regulatory framework must begin with a clear understanding of distinct⏎failure modes rather than treating “AI risk” as a monolithic concept. The safety profile of an⏎assistive chart summarization tool differs materially from that of an autonomous diagnostic⏎engine, and policy must reflect those distinctions. The following categories outline the principal⏎risk domains relevant to AI-enabled clinical briefing systems and related applications.⏎A careful delineation of these risks is not an argument against innovation. Rather, it is a⏎prerequisite to proportionate governance. Identifying how systems fail—technically,⏎operationally, and behaviorally—enables regulators to design guardrails that preserve benefits⏎while mitigating foreseeable harm.⏎6⏎⏎A. Hallucination Risk⏎Hallucination risk refers to the generation of fabricated or unsupported information. In a⏎clinical context, this may include nonexistent diagnoses, incorrect medication lists,⏎spurious allergy notations, or inaccurate descriptions of prior medical events. Even a⏎single fabricated data point can distort clinical reasoning if it appears authoritative.⏎The legal and patient safety implications are substantial. A hallucinated allergy could⏎improperly constrain treatment options; a fabricated prior diagnosis could lead to⏎unnecessary testing or inappropriate referrals; an inaccurate medication entry could⏎introduce dangerous drug interactions. Because clinicians often operate under time⏎pressure, they may not immediately detect subtle inaccuracies embedded within a⏎polished summary.⏎Unlike simple typographical errors, hallucinations carry an epistemic risk: they present as⏎coherent and plausible statements. This makes them more difficult to detect through⏎casual review. In high-acuity settings, even minor inaccuracies may cascade into⏎consequential errors.⏎Mitigation requires design features such as source traceability, confidence indicators, and⏎explicit differentiation between extracted data and inferred conclusions. Systems must⏎make it operationally easy for clinicians to verify claims against the underlying record.⏎Transparency is the primary countermeasure to hallucination risk.⏎B. Omission Risk⏎Omission risk arises when a system fails to surface critical information that is present in⏎the underlying record. In many clinical scenarios, omission may pose a greater danger⏎than fabrication. A missing notation regarding a prior adverse drug reaction,⏎anticoagulant use, or cardiac event can have immediate and serious consequences.⏎Clinical decision-making often depends on identifying contraindications or outlier events⏎within large datasets. If a summarization system compresses information in a way that⏎excludes such signals, clinicians may proceed under incomplete assumptions. The brevity⏎that enhances efficiency can inadvertently obscure nuance.⏎Omission risk is especially pronounced in longitudinal records containing years of⏎documentation across multiple providers and systems. Data fragmentation, inconsistent⏎coding, and unstructured narrative notes can make it difficult for algorithms to detect⏎relevant entries, increasing the likelihood that critical information is not highlighted.⏎Effective mitigation requires structured completeness checks, explicit acknowledgment⏎of data sources reviewed, and clear indicators when records are incomplete or⏎unavailable. Systems should err on the side of signaling uncertainty rather than⏎presenting a falsely comprehensive summary.⏎7⏎⏎C. Automation Bias⏎Automation bias refers to the human tendency to over-rely on automated outputs,⏎particularly when those outputs are presented in a clear and authoritative format. In time-⏎constrained clinical environments, physicians may unconsciously defer to summarized or⏎highlighted information rather than independently interrogating the full record.⏎Interface design can exacerbate this tendency. Clean dashboards, risk scores, and concise⏎bullet-point summaries can create a perception of comprehensiveness even when the⏎system is assistive rather than determinative. The more seamless the integration, the⏎greater the risk that clinicians internalize AI outputs as definitive.⏎Over time, excessive reliance on automated summaries may degrade independent clinical⏎reasoning. If physicians become habituated to accepting compressed narratives without⏎verification, diagnostic vigilance may decline. This dynamic is particularly concerning in⏎training environments where less-experienced clinicians may treat AI output as⏎authoritative guidance.⏎Mitigating automation bias requires deliberate workflow design. Systems should clearly⏎indicate their assistive nature, facilitate easy access to underlying documentation, and⏎incorporate mechanisms for clinician review and correction. Accountability should⏎remain firmly anchored in human decision-makers.⏎D. Distribution Shift and Model Drift⏎Clinical environments are dynamic. Patient demographics change, treatment standards⏎evolve, new medications enter the market, and coding practices are revised. Distribution⏎shift occurs when the data environment in which a model operates diverges from the⏎environment in which it was trained.⏎Over time, such shifts can degrade performance. A model trained on historical patterns⏎may fail to recognize emerging conditions or updated therapeutic standards. In⏎summarization contexts, evolving documentation practices may alter how information is⏎recorded, reducing the system’s ability to extract relevant content accurately.⏎Model drift may occur gradually and invisibly. Performance degradation can accumulate⏎without obvious failure events, especially in assistive systems where outputs are not⏎directly benchmarked against clear ground truth metrics. This creates a latent safety risk.⏎Addressing distribution shift requires lifecycle governance. Ongoing monitoring, periodic⏎performance reassessment, and structured update protocols are essential. AI in health care⏎cannot be treated as static software; it must be subject to adaptive oversight consistent⏎with the dynamic nature of clinical practice.⏎E. Privacy and Security Risks⏎AI clinical systems necessarily process sensitive protected health information. This⏎creates heightened privacy and security obligations. Unauthorized access, data breaches,⏎8⏎⏎or inadequate encryption protocols can expose patients to identity theft, discrimination, or⏎reputational harm.⏎Beyond traditional cybersecurity concerns, AI systems introduce risks related to⏎secondary use of data. Training processes, model improvement cycles, or third-party⏎integrations may expand data exposure beyond the original purpose for which⏎information was collected. Without clear guardrails, mission creep can erode patient trust.⏎Large-scale data aggregation also increases the attractiveness of systems as cyber targets.⏎The concentration of longitudinal health records within AI-enabled platforms amplifies⏎the consequences of security failures.⏎Mitigation requires strict adherence to purpose limitation, data minimization, access⏎controls, and audit logging. Governance structures must clearly define who may access⏎data, for what purposes, and under what safeguards. Privacy and security are not⏎peripheral concerns but foundational conditions for sustained adoption.⏎In evaluating these risk categories, it is essential to recognize that magnitude varies significantly⏎by use case and degree of autonomy. A bounded assistive summarization tool presents a different⏎risk profile than an autonomous diagnostic or treatment engine. Accordingly, governance should⏎scale with material clinical influence rather than applying uniform constraints across⏎heterogeneous technologies. A calibrated framework that distinguishes between low-risk⏎assistance and high-impact autonomy will better protect patients while preserving the benefits of⏎innovation.⏎IV. PERMISSIONLESS INNOVATION AND THE PRECAUTIONARY PRINCIPLE IN⏎MEDICAL AI⏎Debates over emerging technologies often collapse into a false binary: either innovation should⏎proceed without regulatory friction, or precaution should dominate to prevent potential harm. In⏎the context of medical AI, neither extreme is appropriate. A purely precautionary posture that⏎demands exhaustive pre-deployment proof for all AI-enabled tools would freeze low-risk⏎innovation and delay beneficial improvements in efficiency and care coordination. Conversely,⏎an unbounded permissionless approach would expose patients to preventable risks in high-stakes⏎clinical environments.⏎CRF rejects precautionary paralysis where assistive technologies pose minimal and reversible⏎risk. Low-risk tools that organize information, summarize records, or highlight existing data—⏎when deployed under clear transparency and human-review safeguards—should not be subjected⏎to disproportionate regulatory barriers. Excessive friction in these domains would preserve⏎administrative inefficiencies and undermine opportunities to improve informational continuity.⏎At the same time, CRF rejects the notion that medical AI should be deployed without calibrated⏎oversight in high-risk contexts. Where AI systems materially influence diagnostic pathways,⏎triage decisions, or treatment selection, the stakes rise significantly. In such circumstances, the⏎consequences of error are not abstract; they may directly affect patient morbidity or mortality.⏎Regulatory restraint must not become regulatory abdication.⏎9⏎⏎Accordingly, CRF proposes a hybrid standard. Permissionless innovation should remain the⏎default posture for reversible, assistive, and non-autonomous functions. These include bounded⏎summarization tools and informational aids that operate under physician supervision and do not⏎independently initiate or execute clinical actions. Such tools should be permitted to deploy⏎subject to baseline transparency, traceability, and accountability safeguards.⏎Escalating safeguards should apply where AI systems exert what CRF defines as “Material⏎Clinical Influence.” Material Clinical Influence exists when AI outputs could reasonably alter⏎diagnosis, triage, or treatment decisions. In these contexts, validation, performance⏎benchmarking, monitoring, and clear delineation of intended use become essential to protect⏎patient safety.⏎CRF further defines “Autonomous Action” as AI-driven execution of clinical decisions or⏎administrative determinations without contemporaneous physician verification. Autonomous⏎routing of patients, automated treatment adjustments, or coverage determinations made without⏎human review represent a qualitatively different risk tier. It is autonomy—not mere assistance—⏎that should trigger heightened oversight, evidentiary requirements, and structured accountability⏎mechanisms.⏎This distinction between assistance and autonomy is critical. Many AI-enabled tools enhance⏎human judgment without displacing it. When clinicians retain authority, review outputs, and⏎exercise independent reasoning, the risk profile remains bounded. When AI systems act⏎independently or create de facto determinative outcomes, oversight must increase proportionally.⏎Finally, innovation discipline should be understood as a trust-building mechanism rather than a⏎constraint on progress. Clear standards that distinguish low-risk assistive tools from high-impact⏎autonomous systems provide certainty to developers, clinicians, and patients alike. A calibrated⏎framework that protects safety while enabling responsible experimentation will strengthen public⏎confidence in medical AI and promote sustainable technological advancement within the health⏎care system.⏎V. RISK-TIERED GOVERNANCE FRAMEWORK FOR HHS⏎A durable policy framework for AI in medical care must move beyond abstract principles and⏎provide operational clarity. HHS should adopt a risk-tiered governance architecture that scales⏎oversight to functional impact and autonomy level. Such an approach promotes innovation where⏎risk is bounded while ensuring that systems with material clinical consequences are subject to⏎proportionate safeguards. The objective is not to regulate “AI” as a monolith, but to regulate⏎specific use cases according to their real-world risk profile.⏎A tiered model also provides regulatory certainty. Developers, health systems, and clinicians⏎benefit from knowing in advance which category their tools fall into and what compliance⏎expectations attach to each tier. Clear delineation reduces ambiguity, avoids over-deterrence of⏎low-risk innovation, and concentrates oversight resources where patient safety implications are⏎most significant.⏎10⏎⏎Tier 1: Assistive / Informational Tools⏎Tier 1 encompasses assistive and informational systems that support clinicians without⏎independently influencing or executing clinical decisions. These include documentation⏎drafting tools, chart summarization systems, and automated care gap reminders. Their⏎primary function is organizational or clerical efficiency rather than diagnostic or⏎therapeutic determination.⏎These systems operate within physician workflow and under direct human supervision.⏎They do not independently initiate clinical action, nor do they generate prescriptive⏎treatment recommendations. While they may influence clinician awareness, they remain⏎fundamentally assistive and reversible in impact.⏎Baseline governance requirements should apply uniformly across Tier 1 tools.⏎Provenance transparency must be embedded by default, enabling clinicians to trace⏎summarized content back to underlying source records. Systems should clearly disclose⏎which data sources were included and whether external or incomplete records were⏎excluded.⏎In addition, Tier 1 tools should be clearly labeled as assistive within the user interface,⏎avoiding any implication of autonomous authority. Audit logging should capture system⏎usage and clinician review activity to preserve accountability and enable post hoc⏎evaluation if necessary. These safeguards are sufficient for bounded, low-risk⏎applications.⏎Tier 2: Decision-Influencing Tools⏎Tier 2 includes systems that meaningfully influence clinical judgment, even if they do not⏎execute decisions autonomously. Examples include risk scoring tools, triage prioritization⏎systems, and diagnostic suggestion engines. These systems can shape physician attention⏎and alter decision pathways, thereby elevating their risk profile.⏎While physicians retain ultimate authority, Tier 2 tools may exert Material Clinical⏎Influence. Their outputs can reasonably affect diagnosis, referral patterns, medication⏎choices, or patient prioritization. As such, they warrant stronger evidentiary and⏎governance requirements than purely assistive tools.⏎Context-specific validation should be required prior to deployment. Performance⏎benchmarking must demonstrate that the system operates reliably within the intended⏎patient population and clinical setting. Error tolerance thresholds should be explicitly⏎defined, particularly where outputs may affect high-acuity or vulnerable populations.⏎Tier 2 systems should also incorporate structured physician attestation workflows.⏎Clinicians should confirm review of AI-generated outputs before finalizing clinical⏎decisions. This reinforces human accountability and mitigates automation bias. Ongoing⏎performance monitoring should detect drift or emerging safety concerns over time.⏎11⏎⏎Tier 3: Autonomous or High-Impact Systems⏎Tier 3 encompasses autonomous or high-impact systems capable of executing or⏎effectively determining outcomes without contemporaneous physician verification.⏎Examples include independent triage routing, automated authorization or coverage⏎determinations, and direct execution of clinical actions such as dosage adjustments or⏎treatment pathway activation.⏎These systems present the highest risk profile because autonomy introduces the potential⏎for harm without immediate human correction. Even if designed to optimize efficiency,⏎autonomous systems may create systemic errors that scale rapidly if not properly⏎governed. Accordingly, Tier 3 tools require the most rigorous oversight architecture.⏎A pre-deployment safety case should be mandatory for Tier 3 systems. This includes⏎clearly defined intended use, validation within the target population, explicit performance⏎metrics, and documented risk mitigation strategies. Regulators should require⏎demonstration that the system performs at least as well as established clinical standards⏎under realistic operating conditions.⏎Post-deployment obligations must include ongoing performance monitoring, structured⏎reporting of adverse events, and periodic reassessment of model accuracy. Independent⏎review pathways should allow clinicians or affected parties to challenge system outputs.⏎Override and appeals mechanisms must be easily accessible and functionally meaningful⏎to ensure that autonomy does not displace accountability.⏎A risk-tiered governance framework enables HHS to balance innovation with safety in a⏎principled and operationally coherent manner. By scaling oversight to functional impact and⏎autonomy level, the Department can preserve permissionless deployment for low-risk assistive⏎tools while imposing proportionate safeguards where patient safety implications intensify. This⏎architecture promotes clarity, accountability, and sustained public trust in AI-enabled medical⏎care.⏎VI. HUMAN ACCOUNTABILITY AND WORKFLOW INTEGRATION⏎The introduction of AI into clinical environments must not obscure a foundational principle:⏎physicians remain the ultimate decision-makers in patient care. No deployment model should⏎dilute professional accountability or create ambiguity regarding responsibility for diagnosis,⏎treatment, or follow-up. AI systems may inform and support clinical judgment, but they must not⏎displace the physician’s fiduciary and ethical obligations to the patient.⏎Preserving physician primacy requires deliberate interface design. User interfaces should⏎encourage active review rather than passive acceptance of AI-generated outputs. Visual⏎presentation should clearly indicate that summaries, risk scores, or highlighted insights are⏎assistive tools. The system should prompt engagement—such as confirmation steps or⏎expandable evidence views—rather than defaulting to silent incorporation into the clinical⏎record.⏎12⏎⏎Traceability is a core safeguard. Clinicians must be able to click through from any summarized⏎statement or highlighted alert directly to the underlying source data within the electronic health⏎record. This traceability allows physicians to validate accuracy, assess context, and identify⏎potential omissions. Without easy access to primary data, clinicians may be forced to rely on⏎compressed summaries without sufficient verification.⏎Accountability mechanisms should include documentation of physician corrections or overrides.⏎When a clinician identifies an inaccuracy, clarifies context, or rejects a suggested insight, that⏎interaction should be logged in a structured manner. This documentation not only preserves legal⏎clarity but also creates an opportunity for iterative system improvement. Correction pathways⏎should be efficient and integrated into workflow rather than burdensome.⏎Continuous feedback loops are essential to responsible AI deployment. Health systems and⏎developers should establish processes through which clinician feedback is aggregated, analyzed,⏎and used to refine model performance. Patterns of recurrent correction or misunderstanding may⏎signal systemic issues requiring recalibration. Governance should treat clinician interaction as an⏎input to ongoing quality assurance rather than a one-time review.⏎Workflow integration must be calibrated to avoid cognitive overload. AI tools should reduce⏎friction, not create additional documentation tasks that undermine efficiency gains. Human⏎oversight should be meaningful but streamlined, reinforcing thoughtful engagement without⏎imposing redundant administrative steps. Effective integration requires close alignment with⏎real-world clinical processes.⏎Ultimately, AI should augment clinical reasoning, not erode it. The objective is to enhance⏎situational awareness, improve informational clarity, and reduce administrative distraction so⏎that physicians can exercise independent judgment more effectively. A governance framework⏎that embeds transparency, traceability, and correction mechanisms into workflow will preserve⏎physician authority while enabling the benefits of technological advancement.⏎VII. POST-DEPLOYMENT MONITORING AND ADAPTIVE GOVERNANCE⏎Artificial intelligence systems in clinical environments cannot be treated as static products.⏎Unlike traditional medical devices or fixed software applications, AI models evolve over time⏎and operate within dynamic care settings. Patient populations shift, documentation practices⏎change, and standards of care advance. For these reasons, governance must extend beyond initial⏎deployment and incorporate structured, lifecycle-based oversight.⏎HHS should require periodic reassessment of model performance, calibrated to risk tier.⏎Assistive tools may warrant routine evaluation at defined intervals, while systems exerting⏎Material Clinical Influence should undergo more frequent and rigorous review. Performance⏎reassessment should examine accuracy, error rates, false-positive and false-negative patterns, and⏎any observable degradation relative to initial validation benchmarks.⏎Significant adverse events plausibly linked to AI outputs should be subject to structured⏎reporting requirements. Where an AI-generated summary, recommendation, or autonomous⏎action contributes to patient harm or near-miss events, health systems should document and⏎13⏎⏎report those occurrences through appropriate oversight channels. Transparent reporting promotes⏎accountability and facilitates system-wide learning rather than isolated corrective action.⏎HHS should also encourage structured data collection to evaluate real-world impact. Deployment⏎should not rest solely on pre-launch validation; instead, agencies should promote longitudinal⏎assessment of clinical outcomes, workflow efficiency, patient safety indicators, and potential⏎disparities. Real-world evidence can inform whether systems are delivering promised benefits or⏎introducing unintended consequences.⏎Clear update protocols are essential to responsible lifecycle governance. When models are⏎modified—whether through retraining, parameter adjustment, or expanded scope—those updates⏎should be documented and, where appropriate, revalidated. Governance should distinguish⏎between minor performance refinements and material functional changes that alter risk profiles.⏎Structured version control and defined approval pathways enhance clarity and stability.⏎Transparency regarding model revisions strengthens trust among clinicians and patients. Health⏎systems should maintain accessible documentation describing what changes were made, why⏎they were implemented, and how performance was assessed. Silent or opaque updates undermine⏎accountability and may obscure emerging safety signals.⏎Finally, HHS should consider sunset or formal review triggers for higher-risk deployments.⏎Autonomous or high-impact systems should not operate indefinitely without structured⏎reevaluation. Periodic renewal, contingent upon demonstrated safety and performance, reinforces⏎adaptive governance. By embedding monitoring, transparency, and reassessment into policy⏎architecture, HHS can ensure that innovation remains aligned with patient safety over time rather⏎than relying solely on initial deployment assumptions.⏎VIII. FEDERAL COORDINATION AND REGULATORY CLARITY⏎The effectiveness of any AI governance framework depends not only on substantive standards⏎but also on institutional coherence. Within HHS, multiple components exercise jurisdiction over⏎aspects of health care technology, including payment policy, health IT certification, and medical⏎device regulation. Without deliberate coordination, overlapping authorities may produce⏎fragmented guidance, duplicative compliance obligations, and regulatory uncertainty that⏎discourages responsible innovation.⏎Alignment between CMS, ONC, and FDA is particularly important in the context of AI-enabled⏎clinical tools. CMS influences adoption through reimbursement structures and participation⏎requirements. ONC shapes health IT architecture and certification standards. FDA oversees⏎certain software functions that qualify as regulated medical devices. A coherent federal approach⏎requires these components to operate from a shared risk-tiered framework rather than issuing⏎isolated or inconsistent directives.⏎Clear boundaries between clinical decision support functions and device-regulated systems are⏎essential. Developers and health systems must understand when a tool is treated as assistive CDS⏎and when it crosses into device territory requiring additional regulatory processes. Ambiguity in⏎these boundaries can chill innovation at the margins and create compliance risk that exceeds the⏎actual safety concern.⏎14⏎⏎HHS should also work to avoid duplicative or conflicting compliance regimes. If transparency⏎requirements under health IT certification diverge from expectations under payment policy or⏎device oversight, stakeholders may face redundant reporting and validation burdens. Harmonized⏎standards reduce administrative cost while maintaining safety protections.⏎Interoperability and data portability are foundational to safe AI deployment. Clinical briefing⏎tools and related systems depend on accurate, comprehensive data drawn from multiple sources.⏎Federal policy should promote consistent data standards, secure exchange mechanisms, and⏎portability across platforms. Fragmented data environments undermine both performance and⏎patient safety.⏎Consistency in transparency expectations across federal programs is equally important. If CMS⏎requires certain disclosures for AI-enabled tools used in Medicare settings, those expectations⏎should align with ONC certification requirements and FDA guidance where applicable. A⏎unified transparency baseline strengthens predictability and fosters trust among clinicians and⏎patients.⏎Coordinated federal policy also improves market signaling. Developers are more likely to invest⏎in compliant, safety-focused systems when regulatory expectations are clear and stable.⏎Conversely, shifting or inconsistent standards encourage minimal compliance strategies and⏎reactive adjustments rather than proactive safety design.⏎By harmonizing oversight across HHS components, clarifying jurisdictional boundaries, and⏎aligning transparency and interoperability standards, the Department can reduce fragmentation⏎while preserving robust safeguards. Regulatory clarity is not merely an administrative⏎convenience; it is a precondition for safe, scalable, and trustworthy deployment of AI in medical⏎care.⏎IX. PRESERVING PATIENT TRUST AND ACCESS⏎Patient trust is the foundation upon which all health care innovation rests. Technological⏎advancement that undermines confidence in clinical integrity will ultimately fail, regardless of its⏎theoretical efficiency gains. HHS must therefore evaluate AI governance not only through a⏎safety lens, but also through its effect on patient trust, system stability, and equitable access to⏎care.⏎Excessive regulatory uncertainty can deter beneficial innovation. When developers and health⏎systems lack clarity regarding compliance expectations, risk thresholds, or jurisdictional⏎boundaries, investment slows and responsible experimentation is deferred. In health care, where⏎administrative burden is already substantial, additional ambiguity may discourage deployment of⏎tools that could reduce clinician workload and improve care coordination.⏎At the same time, poorly governed AI systems can erode trust even more quickly. High-profile⏎failures, opaque decision-making, or unexplained clinical outcomes may lead patients to question⏎whether technology has displaced professional judgment. Trust once lost is difficult to restore. A⏎governance framework that anticipates foreseeable risks and embeds transparency mechanisms⏎helps prevent reactive regulatory backlash driven by isolated but visible incidents.⏎15⏎⏎Trust is also closely linked to equity. If AI systems are deployed without adequate validation⏎across diverse patient populations, they risk reinforcing disparities. Performance gaps across⏎demographic groups can magnify existing inequities in diagnosis, treatment, or access.⏎Disciplined governance that requires performance monitoring and correction supports broader,⏎more equitable adoption.⏎Regulatory clarity encourages responsible private-sector investment. Developers are more likely⏎to allocate capital toward safety-conscious, interoperable systems when standards are predictable⏎and proportionate. Clear tiered requirements allow firms to design compliance pathways from⏎the outset rather than retrofitting systems after deployment. This stability supports long-term⏎innovation rather than short-term opportunism.⏎Calibrated oversight also promotes affordability. Assistive AI tools that reduce chart-review⏎time, streamline documentation, and minimize duplicative testing can lower administrative⏎overhead across the health system. When governance is proportionate to risk, low-impact tools⏎can deploy without unnecessary friction, enabling efficiency gains that translate into cost⏎containment.⏎Conversely, overregulation of low-risk assistive functions may entrench existing inefficiencies.⏎If health systems are prevented from adopting technologies that meaningfully reduce clinician⏎burden, administrative costs remain embedded in the delivery system and are ultimately borne by⏎patients and taxpayers. A risk-tiered approach ensures that safeguards do not inadvertently block⏎cost-saving innovation.⏎In sum, preserving patient trust and expanding access are not in tension with innovation; they are⏎dependent upon disciplined governance. Clear standards, proportional oversight, and transparent⏎accountability mechanisms foster confidence among patients, clinicians, and developers alike.⏎By calibrating regulatory expectations to actual risk, HHS can promote responsible innovation⏎that enhances affordability, broadens access, and strengthens trust in the evolving health care⏎system.⏎Conclusion⏎The Center for Regulatory Freedom strongly supports innovation that strengthens—rather than⏎fragments—the primary care foundation of the American health system. Technological⏎advancement should reinforce the physician-patient relationship, reduce unnecessary⏎administrative burden, and improve clinical precision. Artificial intelligence, if deployed⏎responsibly, can serve these ends by enhancing physician awareness and restoring coherence to⏎increasingly complex medical records.⏎As care delivery models evolve toward greater use of episodic and on-demand services,⏎informational continuity becomes the critical bridge between traditional longitudinal primary⏎care relationships and emerging access pathways. Even where relational continuity declines, the⏎system must preserve the integrity of longitudinal patient information. AI-powered clinical⏎briefing tools offer a means of compressing and organizing that information so physicians can⏎enter encounters informed and prepared.⏎16⏎⏎However, AI must remain a tool of augmentation—not substitution. Properly bounded systems⏎can amplify physician insight and reduce fragmentation. Poorly governed systems risk obscuring⏎accountability, introducing error, and undermining public trust. The policy challenge is therefore⏎not whether to enable AI in health care, but how to calibrate governance to preserve safety and⏎clinical integrity.⏎CRF urges HHS to adopt a risk-tiered, lifecycle-based framework that distinguishes assistive⏎tools from materially influential or autonomous systems. Permissionless innovation should⏎remain the default for low-risk, reversible functions operating under clear transparency⏎safeguards. At the same time, heightened validation, monitoring, and accountability requirements⏎should apply where AI materially influences diagnosis, triage, or treatment decisions.⏎A clear, harmonized, and forward-looking regulatory architecture will support responsible⏎innovation, encourage private-sector investment, and protect patients. Alignment across CMS,⏎ONC, and FDA authorities will reduce fragmentation and provide the predictability necessary for⏎sustained technological development. Calibrated oversight can improve efficiency, strengthen⏎affordability, and preserve confidence in clinical decision-making.⏎CRF remains committed to disciplined, economically grounded, patient-centered regulatory⏎reform. By adopting a balanced framework that protects safety while enabling progress, HHS⏎can ensure that artificial intelligence serves as a continuity-enhancing force in American health⏎care—advancing innovation without compromising the integrity of primary care.⏎Sincerely,⏎Andrew M. Langer⏎Director⏎CPAC Foundation Center for Regulatory Freedom⏎17♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0368♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Geppert, Jeffrey, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jeffrey♔Geppert♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS “Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care”⏎February 2026⏎This response leverages the governance, targeted data collection, deviation logging,⏎and rapid learning cycles central to Standardized Clinical Assessment and Management⏎Plans (SCAMPs), and maps them to AI evaluation, regulation, reimbursement, and⏎research needs in clinical care.⏎Q1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and⏎its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Response:⏎• Evidence and generalizability gaps: Many AI tools lack prospective, real-world⏎evidence across diverse settings; performance drifts over time without structured⏎monitoring. SCAMPs’ targeted outcomes and deviation logging provide a⏎blueprint for continuous, site-specific learning and generalization testing.⏎• Integration and workflow friction: Limited point-of-care integration (CDS⏎Hooks/SMART on FHIR), fragmented EHR data, and poor usability impede⏎adoption. SCAMPs emphasize CDS integration and clinician-guided pathways for⏎practical use.⏎• Governance and liability uncertainty: Unclear accountability for “non-device” AI⏎(e.g., clinical decision support) and continuous-learning systems. SCAMPs’⏎multidisciplinary committees provide an actionable governance model for⏎oversight, deviation review, and iterative improvements.⏎• Payment and ROI ambiguity: Lack of clear reimbursement or recognized value⏎signals for AI-enabled services and care redesign; hard to sustain QI cycles⏎without payment alignment. SCAMPs demonstrate resource optimization through⏎iterative refinement, but need payment models that reward measured outcome⏎improvements.⏎• Data access and quality: Incomplete, heterogeneous data; limited access to⏎benchmarking datasets and shared evaluation tools; difficulty capturing⏎rationales behind deviations. SCAMPs stress structured inputs and rationale⏎capture to improve data fidelity and learning.⏎• Trust, transparency, and safety: Insufficient post-deployment monitoring, fairness⏎audits, and human-centered evaluation; clinician concerns about alert fatigue and⏎explainability. SCAMPs’ rapid learning cycles and explicit documentation of⏎diversions can build trust through transparent improvement.⏎Q2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited?⏎Response:⏎• Create an AI-in-clinical-care evidence pathway:⏎1⏎⏎o Establish a “coverage with evidence development” track for non-device AI-⏎enabled clinical services that commit to SCAMPs-like targeted data⏎collection, deviation logging, and iterative improvement. Align CMS⏎payment with measurable outcome/resource improvements.⏎o Expand New Technology Add-on Payment-like mechanisms for AI-⏎enabled interventions demonstrating improved outcomes/costs in real-⏎world learning cycles.⏎• Certify AI-enabled CDS and learning loops:⏎o Update ONC health IT certification criteria (e.g., 45 CFR Part 170) to⏎include AI transparency, ongoing post-deployment monitoring, fairness,⏎calibration drift checks, and integration hooks (CDS Hooks, SMART on⏎FHIR, FHIR PlanDefinition).⏎• Clarify privacy, security, and secondary use:⏎o Provide guidance under HIPAA (e.g., 45 CFR Part 164) on responsible⏎secondary use for QI and learning health systems; promote privacy-⏎preserving analytics/federated learning for SCAMPs-like cycles.⏎• Institutional governance standards:⏎o Encourage Conditions of Participation-level expectations (e.g., 42 CFR⏎Part 482) for AI safety governance: multidisciplinary oversight,⏎deviation/rationale capture, and real-world performance reporting (akin to⏎SCAMPs committees).⏎• Programmatic supports:⏎o Launch HHS grants/CRADAs for “SCAMPs+AI” pilots across varied⏎conditions/settings using FHIR Clinical Reasoning, PlanDefinition, and⏎GuidanceResponse to run targeted experiments and share updated⏎pathways.⏎Q3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist and⏎what role, if any, should HHS play to address them?⏎Response:⏎• Issues:⏎o Allocation of liability when AI advice is advisory and clinicians deviate or⏎comply; continuous-learning updates changing behavior over time.⏎o Vendor lock-in, data rights to rationales and outcomes, and responsibilities⏎for monitoring/calibration drift.⏎o Privacy risks in rationale capture and free-text deviation documentation;⏎bias and fairness obligations for “learning” tools.⏎• HHS roles:⏎o Publish model contractual clauses for AI in clinical care covering⏎auditability, drift monitoring, fairness testing, data/rationale rights, and⏎update governance aligned with SCAMPs deviation logging.⏎o Create guidance for shared accountability frameworks (clinician,⏎institution, vendor) tied to documented deviations and outcomes, mirroring⏎SCAMPs committees’ oversight.⏎2⏎⏎o Issue privacy/security best practices for capturing rationales (structured⏎inputs, de-identification, access controls) and human-centered evaluation.⏎Q4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), and should HHS support them?⏎Response:⏎• Methods:⏎o Pre-deployment: External validation across sites; fairness and subgroup⏎performance checks; robustness to missing/noisy data; simulated⏎workflow testing; human factors/usability studies.⏎o Post-deployment: SCAMPs-like targeted outcomes with deviation logging;⏎stepped-wedge trials; A/B testing; causal inference (propensity, synthetic⏎controls); calibration drift monitoring; clinician override analysis; alert⏎burden tracking.⏎o Metrics: Outcome impact (clinical endpoints); resource use; safety (false⏎positive/negative harm indices); equity (disparate impact); usability (time-⏎on-task, override rates); economic (cost per improved outcome).⏎• HHS support:⏎o Fund real-world evaluation testbeds that embed SCAMPs’ learning cycles⏎and share standardized metrics/benchmarks; support prize competitions⏎for reproducible post-deployment monitoring toolkits.⏎o Encourage registry reporting of performance, deviations, and rationales⏎using FHIR Clinical Reasoning artifacts.⏎Q5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use?⏎Response:⏎• Establish an AI Clinical Care Trust Mark: A voluntary accreditation signaling⏎adherence to SCAMPs-like governance (multidisciplinary oversight,⏎deviation/rationale capture, iterative updates), robust evaluation, fairness audits,⏎and transparent performance reporting.⏎• Seed neutral testing labs: Fund third-party labs to run standardized cross-site⏎evaluations using shared datasets and FHIR-integrated workflows (CDS⏎Hooks/SMART apps), and publish comparative benchmarking.⏎• Credentialing and training: Support curricula for clinicians and quality leaders on⏎AI oversight, SCAMPs methodology, and human-centered evaluation; recognize⏎institutions with mature AI governance.⏎• Promote industry consortia: Convene payers, providers, EHR vendors, and AI⏎developers to agree on “learning health system” reporting packages (deviations,⏎outcomes, fairness) aligned to SCAMPs.⏎Q6. Where have AI tools met/exceeded expectations and where fallen short? What⏎novel AI tools have greatest potential?⏎3⏎⏎Response:⏎• Met/exceeded:⏎o Imaging triage/diagnostics (e.g., prioritization, fracture/retinopathy⏎detection) with workflow-integrated CDS; documentation productivity tools;⏎some operational AI (e.g., scheduling) improving throughput.⏎• Fallen short:⏎o Generalization across sites/patient populations; alert fatigue in CDS;⏎limited transparency; sepsis/risk scores with high false positives and⏎equity concerns without robust monitoring.⏎• High-potential tools:⏎o AI+SCAMPs clinical pathways: Tools that embed PlanDefinition-based⏎recommendations, capture deviations/rationales, and update iteratively.⏎o Causal inference and real-time analytics dashboards for targeted⏎outcomes, enabling rapid learning cycles at the point of care.⏎o Safety and equity monitors (drift/fairness) integrated into EHRs; NLP to⏎structure rationale capture; federated learning for multi-site improvement.⏎Q7. Which roles/decision makers have the most influence on adoption? Primary⏎administrative hurdles?⏎Response:⏎• Influencers:⏎o CMIO, CMO, Chief Nursing Officer, Pharmacy & Therapeutics/Value⏎Analysis committees, Quality & Safety leaders, Privacy/Compliance,⏎Security/IT, Finance, and Patient/Family advisory councils. SCAMPs⏎governance mirrors this multidisciplinary structure.⏎• Administrative hurdles:⏎o Procurement and contracting (risk/liability, data rights); integration costs;⏎EHR workflow fit; governance/approval cycles; training and change⏎management; performance monitoring resources; reimbursement/ROI⏎clarity. SCAMPs can streamline approval via targeted outcomes and⏎deviation-based learning commitments.⏎Q8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate AI? Consider data types, standards, benchmarking tools.⏎Response:⏎• Priorities:⏎o Standards: Broader adoption of FHIR Clinical Reasoning (PlanDefinition,⏎ActivityDefinition, GuidanceResponse), CDS Hooks, and SMART on FHIR⏎apps to deliver SCAMPs-aligned recommendations and capture⏎deviations/rationales.⏎o Data types: Outcomes, orders, observations, imaging, genomics, device⏎telemetry, SDOH, care plans; structured rationale inputs and deviation⏎flags as first-class data.⏎4⏎⏎o Benchmarking tools: Public test datasets and reference implementations⏎for post-deployment monitoring (calibration/fairness), drift detection, and⏎causal inference; shared dashboards and report formats.⏎o Multicenter datasets and collaboration (especially for rare diseases):⏎▪ Harmonize data capture and phenotyping across sites using FHIR⏎profiles, PlanDefinition/ActivityDefinition, and standardized case⏎definitions so targeted outcomes and deviation/rationale capture⏎are consistent across institutions.⏎▪ Support privacy-preserving analytics (federated learning, secure⏎multiparty computation) to enable cross-site evaluation and⏎improvement without centralizing raw patient data.⏎▪ Leverage common data models (e.g., OMOP, PCORnet) alongside⏎FHIR to enable reproducible cohort definition, benchmarking, and⏎pathway performance comparisons in low-prevalence populations.⏎• Actions:⏎o Expand USCDI to include “rationale for deviation” and “targeted⏎outcomes” elements; promote interoperable pathway updates (FHIR⏎PlanDefinition) across sites.⏎o Catalyze multicenter learning networks for rare diseases:⏎▪ Provide model DUAs/BAAs and governance templates to⏎streamline cross-institution collaboration, IRB reciprocity, and⏎SCAMPs-like targeted data collection and deviation logging across⏎sites.⏎▪ Fund reference implementations that integrate FHIR Clinical⏎Reasoning artifacts with federated analytics for multicenter⏎evaluation, including drift/fairness monitoring and causal inference⏎at low sample sizes.⏎▪ Expand USCDI to include rare disease-relevant elements⏎(standardized phenotype descriptors, natural history measures, and⏎“rationale for deviation” fields) to improve data quality and⏎comparability across centers.⏎▪ Align with existing NIH/NCATS rare disease consortia and⏎registries to ensure interoperable data exchange and shared⏎benchmarking that can directly inform SCAMPs pathway updates.⏎Q9. What challenges do patients/caregivers want addressed and what concerns do they⏎have?⏎Response:⏎• Desired benefits:⏎o Faster, more accurate diagnosis and triage; medication safety; care⏎coordination and navigation; reduced burden in chronic disease⏎management; equitable access and outcomes. SCAMPs’ iterative⏎improvement focuses on measurable outcomes that matter to patients.⏎o State-of-the-art, optimal care: Patients expect care plans and⏎recommendations to reflect the most current, best-available evidence and⏎5⏎⏎expert consensus. AI-enabled, SCAMPs-aligned pathways can help⏎ensure care stays up to date by continuously incorporating new data,⏎guideline changes, and documented clinician innovations (via⏎deviation/rationale capture), with transparent update histories.⏎• Concerns:⏎o Privacy and data reuse; bias and fairness; transparency/explainability;⏎overreliance on automation; consent and opt-out options; recourse for⏎errors. SCAMPs’ explicit logging of deviations and rationales enables⏎patient-centered accountability and continuous improvement.⏎• Actions:⏎o Require patient-facing explanations of how AI is used; offer opt-out where⏎feasible; include patient councils in SCAMPs/AI governance committees;⏎publish outcomes and equity metrics in plain language.⏎o Provide patient-facing assurances that recommendations are current:⏎Display pathway “last updated” dates, summarize recent evidence⏎changes, and explain how SCAMPs/AI-driven learning cycles keep care⏎aligned with the latest knowledge.⏎Q10. Specific areas of AI research HHS should prioritize to accelerate adoption; a)⏎published findings, b) costs/benefits literature?⏎Response:⏎• Research priorities:⏎o Learning health systems methods: SCAMPs-aligned real-world evaluation⏎frameworks (targeted outcomes, deviation/rationale capture, rapid⏎iteration), federated learning and privacy-preserving analytics.⏎o Robustness and fairness: Drift detection, re-calibration, subgroup⏎performance, bias mitigation; human-centered design to reduce alert⏎fatigue and increase safe overrides.⏎o Causal inference at scale: Tools to attribute impact of AI-guided care⏎pathways on outcomes and resource use; stepped-wedge/A/B designs⏎integrated into EHR workflows.⏎o Interoperability toolkits: FHIR Clinical Reasoning and CDS Hooks⏎reference implementations; PlanDefinition authoring/sharing; standardized⏎reporting packages.⏎o Economic evaluation: Real-world cost-effectiveness analyses of AI-⏎enabled SCAMPs, including downstream benefits (complication⏎avoidance) and administrative savings.⏎• a) Published findings:⏎o Literature shows mixed impact: success in specific domains (e.g.,⏎imaging, documentation) and limitations in generalization, fairness, and⏎workflow fit—underscoring need for continuous, SCAMPs-like monitoring⏎and iterative improvement.⏎• b) Costs/benefits:⏎o Studies increasingly consider total value: clinical outcomes, resource⏎utilization, safety, equity, clinician burden, and operational efficiency;⏎6⏎⏎strongest cases pair payment incentives with monitored improvements⏎over time, which SCAMPs operationalize via targeted outcomes and⏎iterative cycles.⏎How SCAMPs can serve as a backbone for AI in clinical care⏎• Governance: Multidisciplinary committees oversee pathway design, evidence⏎review, and iterative updates; this maps directly to AI oversight and⏎accountability.⏎• Targeted data collection: Focused “plausible outcomes” and structured rationale⏎capture enable high-signal learning and pragmatic evaluation of AI⏎recommendations.⏎• Deviation logging: Recording positive/negative diversions with rationale identifies⏎innovation and safety signals, informing pathway and model updates.⏎• Informatics integration: EHR cohort discovery, CDS Hooks/SMART on FHIR⏎apps, structured inputs/NLP, AI-based causal inference, dashboards, and FHIR⏎PlanDefinition sharing support scalable deployment and continuous⏎improvement.⏎• Outcomes: Published SCAMPs have demonstrated applicability across⏎conditions/settings and can reduce resource use through rapid, evidence-⏎informed iteration—providing a practical template to evaluate AI’s value.⏎Citations:⏎Farias M, Alexander PMA, Geppert JJ, Glavin P, Ramirez-Mendoza H, Casale P,⏎Jenkins K. Standardized Clinical Assessment and Management Plans Revisited:⏎Lessons Learned from a Decade of Implementation, Health Affairs Scholar, 2026;,⏎qxag036, https://doi.org/10.1093/haschl/qxag036⏎• HHS: Request for Information—Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care (2025-23641).⏎7♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0369♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bhagavathula, Akshaya, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-06T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Akshaya♔Bhagavathula♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔January 6, 2026⏎Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Governing Clinical Artificial Intelligence as a Population-Level⏎Submitted by: Dr. Akshaya Bhagavathula, PharmD, PhD, FACE⏎Position: Associate Professor of Epidemiology, North Dakota State University⏎Expertise: Digital Epidemiology, Real-World Evidence, and Population level AI Governance⏎Clinical artificial intelligence (AI) is no longer a discrete technology to be evaluated solely at⏎deployment, but a population-level intervention whose safety, effectiveness, and equity can only⏎be governed through continuous, post-deployment evaluation and accountability. I appreciate⏎the opportunity to comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001 and submit this response as an⏎epidemiologist with expertise in artificial intelligence, causal inference, and population health,⏎informed by direct experience evaluating AI systems after they have been deployed in clinical⏎and public-sector environments. My intent is not to argue for or against particular technologies,⏎but to address a foundational regulatory issue that underlies many of the concerns raised in this⏎Request for Information: the growing mismatch between how clinical AI is currently regulated⏎and how it actually behaves once implemented at scale. This comment responds directly to the⏎Department’s focus on improving the evaluation, governance, and patient-centered use of AI in⏎health and public health, with particular attention to how AI systems should be assessed beyond⏎pre-deployment testing, how regulatory oversight can remain effective as AI scales and adapts in⏎real-world settings, and how patient-centered innovation can be operationalized as a concrete⏎governance standard rather than an aspirational principle. The observations and⏎recommendations that follow are intended to support HHS in advancing governance approaches⏎that are scientifically grounded, practically implementable, and aligned with the realities of AI⏎deployment in clinical and public-sector systems.⏎AI is no longer experimental within health care. It is embedded within clinical decision support,⏎utilization management, documentation, triage, and surveillance systems. As a result, the⏎dominant risks associated with AI are no longer confined to algorithm design or pre-deployment⏎performance. They emerge after deployment, through interaction with clinicians, workflows,⏎institutions, and populations. This shift has profound implications for how AI should be⏎evaluated and governed.⏎At present, clinical AI is largely regulated as a discrete product. In practice, it functions as a⏎population-level intervention.⏎Once introduced into a health system, an AI system does not simply generate outputs. It reshapes⏎how decisions are made, how attention is allocated, how workflows evolve, and how care is⏎delivered across groups of patients. These effects accumulate over time and extend beyond the⏎individuals directly targeted by the algorithm. From a regulatory standpoint, this distinction is⏎critical, because the most consequential harms and benefits of AI are not immediate or isolated.⏎They are systemic.⏎DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH⏎NDSU Dept 2662 I PO Box 6050 I Fargo ND 58108-6050 I 701.231.6269⏎http://www.ndsu.edu/publichealth⏎NDSU is an EO/AA university.⏎⏎The operational reality of deployed AI can be represented as a causal sequence rather than a static⏎endpoint:⏎Current regulatory frameworks largely evaluate AI at the point of deployment, near the top of⏎this sequence. However, the majority of risk emerges downstream, where no systematic oversight⏎currently exists.⏎Pre-deployment evaluation, while necessary, is structurally insufficient. Validation studies⏎answer whether an algorithm can perform under specified conditions, but they do not answer⏎whether the system remains safe, effective, or equitable once it reshapes real-world clinical⏎behavior. Questions such as who is most frequently exposed to AI-mediated decisions, how⏎reliance on AI changes clinician judgment, how performance drifts over time, or how outcomes⏎differ across subpopulations cannot be resolved before deployment. They require observation in⏎practice.⏎This limitation is not theoretical. In one evaluation I conducted, an AI model demonstrated strong⏎pre-deployment performance across conventional metrics. After implementation, clinicians⏎increasingly relied on the model for triage decisions. High-risk patients were identified more⏎efficiently, but moderate-risk patients experienced longer delays. No alerts were triggered. No⏎technical failures were logged. The model behaved exactly as designed. The harm emerged⏎through interaction with workflow and human adaptation, not through algorithmic error.⏎DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH⏎NDSU Dept 2662 I PO Box 6050 I Fargo ND 58108-6050 I 701.231.6269⏎http://www.ndsu.edu/publichealth⏎NDSU is an EO/AA university.⏎⏎Because no post-deployment monitoring was required, the effect remained invisible until⏎outcome disparities became apparent months later.⏎From an epidemiologic perspective, this pattern is familiar. Deployed AI exhibits properties that⏎closely mirror population-level exposures. It has intensity, reflecting how frequently it influences⏎decisions. It has latency, with effects that manifest downstream. It demonstrates effect⏎modification, producing different impacts across demographic, clinical, and social groups. It⏎generates spillover effects that influence patients and clinicians not directly targeted. Its risk⏎profile changes over time as data, workflows, and institutional practices evolve.⏎Yet unlike other population-level interventions, AI is not subject to routine post-deployment⏎surveillance.⏎This gap represents a regulatory science failure rather than a technological one. HHS has long⏎recognized that interventions with diffuse, delayed, or heterogeneous effects cannot be governed⏎through one-time approval alone. Pharmacovigilance, device post-market surveillance, and⏎public health monitoring systems all exist because pre-market evidence is insufficient to ensure⏎safety at scale. Clinical AI currently sits outside this paradigm.⏎To address this gap, I recommend that HHS adopt Post-Deployment Algorithmic Surveillance⏎(PDAS) as a cross-agency framework for governing clinical AI. PDAS reflects a simple principle:⏎no AI system influencing care should be presumed safe or effective based solely on pre-⏎deployment evidence.⏎DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH⏎NDSU Dept 2662 I PO Box 6050 I Fargo ND 58108-6050 I 701.231.6269⏎http://www.ndsu.edu/publichealth⏎NDSU is an EO/AA university.⏎⏎Under PDAS, evaluation shifts from a static, product-based model to a dynamic, population-⏎based model. This shift can be illustrated as follows:⏎The first element of PDAS is exposure characterization. Without knowing who is exposed to AI-⏎mediated decisions, how often, and in what contexts, no causal assessment is possible. Exposure⏎measurement is foundational, yet it is rarely required.⏎The second element is continuous population-level performance monitoring. AI systems must be⏎evaluated longitudinally, not episodically. Calibration, discrimination, and error patterns should⏎be tracked over time, with mandatory stratification by key subgroups. Performance drift should⏎be treated as a safety signal, not a technical footnote. In another deployment I reviewed, a model’s⏎performance gradually degraded due to changes in coding practices within the electronic health⏎record. Because no routine monitoring was in place, disparities widened quietly over a year. The⏎model did not fail. Oversight did.⏎The third element is outcome-linked evaluation. After deployment, accuracy alone is not an⏎adequate metric of success. The relevant question becomes whether AI use improves downstream⏎clinical outcomes, access, or utilization patterns. Surrogate metrics that are acceptable during⏎development become insufficient once systems influence care at scale.⏎The fourth element is structured reporting of algorithmic harms. Many AI-related harms do not⏎resemble traditional adverse events. They include delayed care, silent misclassification, workflow⏎distortions, and disparity amplification. These events rarely trigger complaints or error reports,⏎yet they accumulate into population-level harm. PDAS requires mechanisms to capture these⏎signals systematically.⏎The fifth element is predefined regulatory response pathways. Surveillance without action is⏎performative. PDAS requires clarity regarding when recalibration, restriction, suspension, or⏎withdrawal is warranted, and how those actions are documented and enforced.⏎DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH⏎NDSU Dept 2662 I PO Box 6050 I Fargo ND 58108-6050 I 701.231.6269⏎http://www.ndsu.edu/publichealth⏎NDSU is an EO/AA university.⏎⏎Patient-centered innovation must be understood within this same framework. Patient-⏎centeredness is often treated as an ethical aspiration or design preference. From a safety⏎perspective, this framing is inadequate. An AI system that influences care but cannot be⏎explained, contested, or reviewed is not merely non-patient-centered; it is unsafe. I have⏎encountered cases in which AI-driven utilization decisions delayed care, clinicians could not⏎explain the rationale, patients had no appeal pathway, and accountability was diffuse. Technical⏎accuracy was irrelevant. The failure was one of governance. Patient contestability and human⏎accountability must be treated as regulatory requirements, not optional features.⏎Equity considerations further reinforce the need for post-deployment surveillance. Disparities⏎associated with AI are frequently attributed to biased training data, but many inequities emerge⏎only after implementation. Uneven adoption, differential trust, resource constraints, and context-⏎specific failures all contribute to disparate impact over time. Equity cannot be ensured through⏎design alone. It must be measured continuously. PDAS should therefore include equity-specific⏎monitoring requirements for AI deployed in Medicaid, maternal health, rural care, and public⏎health settings.⏎Adaptive and continuously learning AI systems introduce additional complexity. Static approval⏎models are incoherent when systems are designed to evolve. Regulation must shift toward⏎process-based accountability, with predefined learning boundaries, rollback mechanisms,⏎transparent change logs, and independent auditability. Without these safeguards, adaptation⏎becomes experimentation embedded within routine care.⏎Finally, none of this is feasible without interoperability. Surveillance, auditability, contestability,⏎and equity monitoring all depend on the ability to access and integrate data across systems.⏎DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH⏎NDSU Dept 2662 I PO Box 6050 I Fargo ND 58108-6050 I 701.231.6269⏎http://www.ndsu.edu/publichealth⏎NDSU is an EO/AA university.⏎⏎Vendor opacity and lock-in are not merely market concerns. They are governance failures. HHS’s⏎existing interoperability initiatives provide a necessary foundation, but AI oversight must be⏎explicitly built upon them.⏎In closing, artificial intelligence will continue to reshape health care regardless of regulatory⏎posture; the question before U.S. Department of Health and Human Services is whether that⏎transformation is governed with the same rigor applied to other population-level interventions.⏎Population science already provides the tools needed to meet this challenge. Post-Deployment⏎Algorithmic Surveillance (PDAS) advances the Department’s stated goals by establishing a⏎practical framework for continuous evaluation, accountable governance, and patient-centered⏎oversight of AI systems once they are embedded in care. By shifting evaluation from static⏎performance metrics to population-level outcomes, enabling timely regulatory response to⏎emerging risks, and ensuring that patients remain visible and protected within AI-mediated⏎systems, PDAS allows innovation to proceed without normalizing unmeasured harm. In this⏎way, PDAS supports the responsible adoption of AI while maintaining public trust, safeguarding⏎equity, and strengthening the evidence base for AI in health and public health.⏎Please feel free to contact me at: akshaya.bhagavathula@ndsu.edu⏎Sincerely,⏎Dr. Akshaya Bhagavathula, PhD⏎Associate Professor of Epidemiology,⏎Department of Public Health | North Dakota State University⏎640S NDSU Dept 2662 | Fargo, ND 58108-6050⏎Email: Akshaya.bhagavathula@ndsu.edu; 701-231-6549⏎DEPARTMENT OF PUBLIC HEALTH⏎NDSU Dept 2662 I PO Box 6050 I Fargo ND 58108-6050 I 701.231.6269⏎http://www.ndsu.edu/publichealth⏎NDSU is an EO/AA university.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0370♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-25T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from NRHA♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-25T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Marguerite♔Peterseim♔♔♔♔♔National Rural Health Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎RE: RIN 0955-AA13; Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov.⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The National Rural Health Association (NRHA) appreciates the opportunity to submit⏎comments in response to the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS)⏎Request for Information (RFI) on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. NRHA commends HHS, the Office of the Deputy⏎Secretary and the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) for seeking broad,⏎experience-based input on how federal regulation, reimbursement policy, and research⏎and development can responsibly advance AI integration in health care delivery. We⏎welcome the Department’s stated commitment to fostering innovation while protecting⏎rural patients, maintaining public trust, and aligning federal incentives to improve⏎productivity, reduce burden, and improve health outcomes.⏎NRHA is a non-profit membership organization with more than 21,000 members⏎nationwide that provides leadership on rural health issues. Our membership includes⏎nearly every component of rural America’s health care, including rural community⏎hospitals, critical access hospitals, doctors, nurses, and patients. We work to improve⏎rural America’s health needs through government advocacy, communications,⏎education, and research.⏎Background⏎AI is a rapidly evolving field within computer science focused on the development of⏎systems designed to replicate human cognitive processes such as learning, decision-⏎making, and problem solving with minimal human intervention.1,2 In rural areas in⏎1 Krittanawong C, Zhang H, Wang Z, Aydar M, Kitai T. Artificial intelligence in precision cardiovascular⏎medicine. J⏎Am Coll Cardiol. 2017;69(21):2657-2664. doi:10.1016/j.jacc.2017.03.571⏎2 Guo J, Li B. The application of medical artificial intelligence technology in rural areas of developing⏎countries.⏎⏎particular, where health care systems face unique barriers including limited⏎infrastructure, workforce shortages, geographical isolation, and longer patient access⏎times, AI could help bridge gaps in access and quality of care, streamline health care⏎delivery, enhance diagnostic precision, and ensure continuous care. 3,4,5,6 To effectively⏎deploy and promote AI-driven health care tools in rural areas, it is paramount to address⏎potential biases, ensure strong data security, and promote transparency and⏎interpretability of AI systems.7 Federal initiatives such as the National Institutes of⏎Health’s Bridge2AI program8 exemplify current governmental efforts to integrate AI⏎within rural health care, aiming to improve access and health outcomes for underserved⏎populations.9 To better ensure that AI policy advances innovation without exacerbating⏎geographic disparities, NRHA offers the following responses to the RFI’s regulatory,⏎reimbursement, research, and implementation questions.⏎Regulation⏎HHS should establish enforceable standards governing development, validation,⏎quality assurance, post-market monitoring, and transparency requirements for AI-⏎enabled medical devices and non-device clinical decision support tools. NRHA⏎also recommends that HHS create a dedicated regulatory coordination office to align⏎related activities by FDA, CMS, NIH, and other federal oversight agencies.10 While the⏎Health Equity. 2018;2(1):174-181. doi:10.1089/heq.2018.0037⏎3 Wilson NW, Couper ID, De Vries E, Reid S, Fish T, Marais BJ. A critical review of interventions to⏎redress the⏎inequitable distribution of healthcare professionals to rural and remote areas. Rural Remote Health.⏎2009;9(2):1060.⏎4 Stolarchuk C, Anshu, Kumar P, et al. Optimizing healthcare delivery: strategies for workforce retention⏎and⏎resource allocation. J Surg Spec Rural Pract. 2025;6(1):3-8. doi:10.4103/jssrp.jssrp_7_25⏎5 Lekadir K, Frangi AF, Porras A, et al. FUTURE-AI: international consensus guideline for trustworthy and⏎deployable artificial intelligence in healthcare. BMJ. 2025;380:r340. doi:10.1136/bmj.r340⏎6 Olugboja A, Agbakwuru EM. Bridging healthcare disparities in rural areas of developing countries:⏎leveraging⏎artificial intelligence for equitable access. In: Proc IEEE Int Conf Artif Intell Comput Data Sci Appl⏎(ACDSA).⏎2024:1-6. doi:10.1109/ACDSA59508.2024.10467443⏎7 National Academy of Medicine. An artificial intelligence code of conduct for health and medicine:⏎Essential⏎guidance for aligned action. Adams L, Fontaine E, Matheny M, Krishnan S, eds. Washington, DC:⏎National⏎Academies Press; 2025. doi:10.17226/29087⏎8 National Institutes of Health. Bridge to Artificial Intelligence (Bridge2AI). Accessed July 9, 2025.⏎https://commonfund.nih.gov/bridge2ai⏎9 US Department of Health and Human Services. Strategic plan for the use of artificial intelligence in⏎health,⏎human services, and public health. Published 2025.⏎10 Warraich HJ, Tazbaz T, Califf RM. FDA perspective on the regulation of artificial intelligence in health⏎care and⏎biomedicine. JAMA. 2025;333(3):241. doi:10.1001/jama.2024.21451⏎⏎Food and Drug Administration has issued draft guidance regarding AI-enabled device⏎software functions and lifecycle management, these recommendations remain non-⏎binding. Rural facilities often lack expertise and legal resources to independently⏎evaluate vendor claims or conduct robust validation.11 Mandatory rural-representative⏎validation should be required to mitigate algorithmic bias and ensure generalizability.⏎Clear delineation of shared responsibility among manufacturers, clinicians, and facilities⏎is also necessary to reduce malpractice uncertainty, particularly in settings where⏎governance infrastructure and access to legal counsel are limited.⏎HHS, through ASTP/ONC, should establish enforceable guidelines for algorithmic⏎transparency, data provenance, auditability, and accountability, and require⏎human-in-the-loop oversight for high-impact clinical uses. Transparency,⏎explainability, and human oversight are essential components of trustworthy AI⏎governance. National and international consensus frameworks emphasize that AI tools⏎must function as decision-support systems within broader clinical and organizational⏎contexts, not as substitutes for professional judgment. In rural communities, concerns⏎about opaque “black box” algorithms, surveillance, automation, and loss of human⏎interaction may undermine community acceptance.12 Additionally, legal and ethical⏎scholarship highlights unresolved questions regarding liability allocation in AI-influenced⏎decision-making. HHS should provide guidance clarifying accountability and consider⏎safe harbor frameworks for providers who appropriately rely on certified AI systems⏎within established standards of care.⏎Infrastructure limitations further complicate regulatory considerations. The FCC’s⏎broadband data demonstrate persistent connectivity gaps in rural and Tribal areas,⏎which directly constrain the safe deployment of cloud-based AI tools.13 In addition, the⏎environmental footprint of AI infrastructure, including high electricity and water demands⏎for data center, poses sustainability risks for resource-limited rural regions.14 Regulatory⏎and procurement policies should therefore incentivize low-energy, resource-efficient AI⏎architectures appropriate for rural environments.⏎Reimbursement⏎11 US Food and Drug Administration. Artificial intelligence-enabled device software functions: lifecycle⏎management and marketing submission recommendations. Draft guidance. Published 2025. Accessed⏎July 9,⏎2025.⏎12 Adus S, Macklin J, Pinto A. Exploring patient perspectives on engagement in the development of AI⏎applications⏎in health care. BMC Health Serv Res. 2023;23(1):1163. doi:10.1186/s12913-023-10098-2⏎13 Federal Communications Commission. 2020 broadband deployment report. Published 2020.⏎14 Wang Q, Li Y, Li R. Ecological footprints, carbon emissions, and energy transitions: the impact of⏎artificial⏎intelligence. Humanit Soc Sci Commun. 2024;11:1043. doi:10.1057/s41599-024-03520-5⏎⏎AI-enabled remote patient monitoring and predictive analytics have shown promise in⏎managing chronic diseases such as diabetes, cardiovascular conditions, and cancer in⏎rural settings.15 Evidence indicates that AI systems can achieve expert-level⏎performance in diagnostic domains including dermatology and diabetic retinopathy⏎screening.16 AI-enhanced telehealth platforms can triage, analytics, and specialist⏎access, while predictive tools may flag early deterioration and enable proactive⏎outreach.⏎While AI tools demonstrate clinical promise, rural facilities operating on thin margins⏎cannot absorb upfront capital, licensing, training, and integration costs without⏎predictable payment pathways. Without explicit Medicare and Medicaid reimbursement⏎codes for AI-supported services, such as remote monitoring, predictive analytics,⏎ambient documentation, and diagnostic support, rural providers face significant financial⏎risk in adopting this technology.⏎HHS should establish clear reimbursement pathways for validated AI-supported⏎services and ensure payment parity across rural and urban settings, including⏎under critical access hospital (CAH) and rural health clinic (RHC) specific payment⏎methodologies. Integration of AI tools into value-based models, such as chronic care⏎and population health initiatives, can align incentives with improved outcomes.⏎Research & Development⏎HHS supports one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems and can⏎translate AI innovation into deployable clinical tools in which rural inclusion must⏎be embedded. AI models rely on large, high-quality datasets; however, rural health⏎data are often sparse, siloed, and lack interoperability, and rural populations are⏎frequently underrepresented in model development.17 As a result, AI tools optimized for⏎urban workflows may not align with rural care delivery structures, increasing the risk of⏎degraded performance or inappropriate recommendations in small clinics and⏎decentralized networks.18 Sparse and siloed rural datasets limit algorithm performance⏎and increase the risk of bias. HHS should fund rural-inclusive, de-identified national⏎15 Nwankwo EI, Emeihe EV, Ajegbile MD, Olaboye JA, Maha CC. Integrating telemedicine and AI to⏎improve⏎healthcare access in rural settings. Int J Life Sci Res Arch. 2024;7(1):59-77.⏎doi:10.53771/ijlsra.2024.7.1.0061⏎16 Ting DSW, Cheung CYL, Lim G, et al. Development and validation of a deep learning system for⏎diabetic⏎retinopathy and related eye diseases. JAMA. 2017;318(22):2211-2223. doi:10.1001/jama.2017.18152⏎17 Hains L, Kovoor JG, Stretton B, et al. When one size does not fit all—artificial intelligence in Australian⏎rural⏎health. Aust J Rural Health. 2025;33(3). doi:10.1111/ajr.70037⏎18 Nong P, Adler-Milstein J, Apathy NC, Holmgren AJ, Everson J. Current use and evaluation of artificial⏎intelligence⏎and predictive models in US hospitals. Health Aff (Millwood). 2025;44(1):90-98.⏎doi:10.1377/hlthaff.2024.00842⏎⏎datasets with diverse geographic and demographic representation and require rural⏎validation studies prior to large-scale deployment. Community-based participatory⏎research approaches can strengthen trust and ensure AI tools reflect the social, cultural,⏎and logistical realities of rural care delivery.⏎Workforce readiness is equally critical.19 Rural clinicians face limited training⏎opportunities and technical support shortages. Expanded HRSA and Department of⏎Labor funding for AI literacy, micro-credentialing, and continuing education programs⏎tailored to CAHs and RHCs would improve implementation capacity. Pilot programs and⏎demonstration initiatives like rural-focused CMS Innovation Center models or Rural AI⏎Centers of Excellence would provide structured environments to test workflow⏎integration, assess clinical outcomes, evaluate cost impacts, and monitor unintended⏎consequences.⏎Individual questions to be addressed:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎In rural settings, barriers are structural, financial, legal, and technical. First, AI systems⏎rely on large, high-quality datasets; however, rural data are frequently sparse,⏎fragmented, and siloed across institutions with limited interoperability. Rural⏎populations, often older and medically complex, are underrepresented in training⏎datasets, which can reduce model performance and introduce bias when tools are⏎deployed in rural environments.20 Second, infrastructure constraints remain substantial.⏎Persistent broadband gaps, poor connectivity quality, outdated hardware, and limited IT⏎staffing capacity hinder deployment of cloud-based AI tools and ongoing maintenance.⏎These constraints increase implementation costs for vendors and providers alike. Third,⏎workforce readiness presents a major barrier. Rural providers often perform multiple⏎roles and have limited access to AI literacy training, technical support, or data science⏎expertise. Without training and workflow integration support, adoption may be unsafe or⏎unsustainable. Fourth, liability and regulatory ambiguity deter innovation. Questions⏎regarding responsibility when AI-influenced decisions result in patient harm remain⏎unresolved. Rural facilities frequently lack internal compliance and legal infrastructure to⏎navigate these risks. Finally, reimbursement uncertainty and thin operating margins⏎significantly limit capital investment capacity. Even promising tools cannot be adopted if⏎payment pathways are unclear or misaligned with rural payment methodologies.⏎19 Igwama GT, Nwankwo EI, Emeihe EV, Ajegbile MD. The role of community health workers in⏎implementing AIbased health solutions in rural areas. Int J Biol Pharm Res Updates. 2024;4(1):1-7.⏎doi:10.53430/ijbpru.2024.4.1.0026⏎20 Hains L, Kovoor JG, Stretton B, et al. When one size does not fit all—artificial intelligence in Australian⏎rural⏎health. Aust J Rural Health. 2025;33(3). doi:10.1111/ajr.70037⏎⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS should prioritize enforceable validation standards and transparency requirements⏎for AI-enabled tools used in clinical care. Rural-representative, validated datasets⏎should be required to ensure safety and performance across diverse populations.⏎Payment policy modernization is equally critical. HHS should establish explicit Medicare⏎and Medicaid reimbursement pathways for AI-supported services, including remote⏎patient monitoring, predictive analytics, ambient documentation technologies, and AI-⏎assisted diagnostics. Payment methodologies for CAHs and RHCs should reflect the⏎unique financial structure of rural facilities to avoid disadvantaging them relative to⏎urban systems.⏎Programmatically, HHS should invest in rural demonstration models through CMS⏎Innovation Center authority and HRSA programs to evaluate workflow integration,⏎outcome improvements, and cost impacts in real-world rural contexts. Workforce⏎development grants and AI literacy initiatives tailored to rural clinicians and IT staff are⏎also necessary to operationalize adoption safely.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS⏎play to help address them?⏎For non-device AI tools like generative AI documentation systems, triage algorithms, or⏎predictive risk stratification platforms, novel governance questions arise regarding⏎accountability, indemnification, and data use. Legal scholarship highlights ambiguity in⏎determining responsibility when AI recommendations influence clinical decisions.⏎Additional implementation concerns include secondary data use and model retraining⏎practices, cybersecurity vulnerabilities in under-resourced environments, and⏎contractual power imbalances between rural providers and technology vendors.⏎Cybersecurity breaches increased 84% between 2018 and 2021, and rural ransomware⏎attacks frequently result in system downtime and ambulance diversion.21 When⏎expanding interoperability and AI adoption, adoption methods must simultaneously⏎strengthen cybersecurity capacity in rural hospitals to protect patient trust and ensure⏎21 Neprash HT, McGlave CC, Rydberg K, and Henning-Smith C. Understanding the Rise of Ransomware⏎Attacks on Rural Hospitals. UMN Rural Health Research Center Policy Brief. June 2024.⏎https://rhrc.umn.edu/ publication/understanding-the-rise-of-ransomwareat-tacks-on-rural-hospitals⏎⏎continuity of care. Rural institutions often lack leverage to negotiate indemnification⏎clauses or audit vendor training practices.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎HHS can support private sector innovation by aligning certification programs with⏎enforceable AI governance standards, recognizing accredited evaluation entities, and⏎incentivizing validation in rural and underserved populations. Clear, predictable⏎regulatory pathways reduce uncertainty and investment risk. Additionally, seed funding⏎and demonstration partnerships between rural providers and technology developers can⏎facilitate co-design of AI tools tailored to rural workflows. Federal coordination across⏎FDA, CMS, NIH, and ASTP/ONC would reduce fragmentation and provide a coherent⏎national framework.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality,⏎and help reduce costs?⏎AI tools have demonstrated strong performance in diagnostic domains such as⏎dermatology, with studies showing performance comparable to medical experts when⏎validated appropriately. Remote patient monitoring systems and predictive analytics⏎have improved chronic disease management and enabled earlier detection of⏎deterioration in rural populations. AI-enabled documentation tools have also reduced⏎administrative burden and cognitive fatigue among clinicians. However, AI tools have⏎fallen short when deployed without rural validation, adequate workflow integration,⏎reimbursement alignment, or provider training. Overreliance on consumer-grade AI⏎platforms may introduce privacy and data governance risks, particularly where digital⏎literacy is limited. Performance degradation due to biased training data remains a⏎significant concern.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎AI adoption decisions are typically influenced by executive leadership, chief medical⏎officers, chief information officers, compliance officers, and governing boards. In rural⏎hospitals and clinics, administrative leadership may be consolidated among fewer⏎individuals, increasing decision-making burden. Primary hurdles include capital⏎investment constraints, limited IT personnel, vendor contracting complexity,⏎cybersecurity compliance requirements, and integration with legacy EHR systems.⏎⏎Without technical assistance and external support, rural administrators may be hesitant⏎to adopt complex technologies.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Rural patients frequently seek improved specialty access, reduced travel burdens,⏎earlier diagnosis, and enhanced chronic disease support. AI-enhanced telehealth and⏎predictive monitoring systems may directly address these needs. At the same time,⏎patients express concerns regarding trust, data privacy, algorithmic bias, automation⏎replacing human interaction, and lack of transparency in AI-generated⏎recommendations. Community engagement, participatory design approaches, and⏎plain-language education are essential to building trust and acceptance.⏎Ultimately, while AI presents transformative opportunities to strengthen clinical care⏎delivery, rural communities remain concerned about being left behind in yet another⏎wave of health system innovation. Rural hospitals, clinics, and patients cannot⏎meaningfully participate in the next generation of AI-enabled care without first securing⏎the foundational infrastructure necessary to support it including reliable broadband⏎connectivity, modernized health IT systems, cybersecurity protections, sustainable⏎reimbursement pathways, and a trained workforce capable of implementing and⏎overseeing these technologies. We respectfully urge the Department to ensure that⏎investments in AI innovation are matched with parallel investments in rural readiness⏎and capacity building.⏎NRHA appreciates the Administration’s recognition of rural communities throughout this⏎RFI and thanks you for keeping rural health at the forefront of national AI policy⏎discussions. We look forward to continued collaboration to ensure that rural providers⏎and patients are full participants in, and beneficiaries of, the rapidly evolving AI⏎landscape. If you have any questions or wish to discuss further, please contact⏎Marguerite Peterseim (mpeterseim@ruralhealth.us).⏎Sincerely,⏎Alan Morgan⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎National Rural Health Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0371♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Tempus AI , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Tempus AI♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and the Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Submitted electronically⏎Dear Mr. Posnack,⏎Tempus AI, Inc. (“Tempus”) appreciates the opportunity to submit comments in response to⏎ASTP/ONC’s request for information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence as part of clinical care.⏎Tempus is a technology company at the forefront of advancements in precision medicine.⏎Tempus offers a wide range of next-generation sequencing (NGS) tests to support⏎pharmaceutical and biotechnology development, clinical studies, and patient treatment,⏎including DNA and RNA tissue-based and liquid biopsy assays. Tempus believes that⏎healthcare will be transformed by the application of technology. We are increasingly concerned⏎that structural factors in the healthcare system will slow that technological progress. We believe⏎there is an opportunity to address the concerns that could limit technological advancement and⏎instead promote the safe and effective use of AI to benefit patients.⏎Regulation - Current Impact and Recommendations⏎Risk-Based Approach⏎A clear, predictable, and risk-based regulatory framework is paramount. We strongly advocate⏎for a risk-based approach to regulation, where the level of regulatory oversight is proportional to⏎the risk of the AI tool, inclusive of balancing pre- and post-market requirements and accepting⏎trade-offs of sensitivity/specificity for low-risk medical devices that bring to market novel medical⏎insights while maintaining a physician in the loop. This will enable more agile regulation of⏎lower-risk tools while ensuring that higher-risk tools undergo appropriate pre-market review and⏎post-market surveillance.⏎⏎Reimbursement - Current Impact and Recommendations⏎Government’s Role in Coding for Medical Technology⏎A barrier in the current system is the process for obtaining new Current Procedural Terminology⏎(CPT) codes, which is governed by the American Medical Association (AMA). The AMA has⏎long played a crucial role in standardizing the language of medicine for billing and⏎reimbursement, and its CPT code set is the bedrock for how physicians are paid for their⏎services and for how medical devices are reimbursed. The AMA has clarified that the only⏎existing code pathway for AI technologies is a Category III code, which by definition are⏎emerging technologies. Additionally, Category III codes have no defined payment mechanism.⏎While the AMA has expressed interest in creating a new code set applicable to AI technologies,⏎there is no alignment of this code set with a Medicare payment pathway or fee schedule. This⏎creates a circumstance where technologies may exist but have no corresponding code, and the⏎only coding pathway is one that has no associated payment. This lack of a clear payment⏎pathway disincentivizes physicians and health systems from adopting the technology, as it⏎represents uncompensated work and investment. In turn, technology developers, seeing a⏎limited and uncertain path to reimbursement despite going through all the appropriate channels,⏎are discouraged from investing the massive resources required to bring these tools to life. To⏎break this cycle of misaligned interests, the federal government should step in to create a⏎modernized, impartial pathway for reimbursement that is specific to AI technology.⏎Aligning Interests – Technology Companies, Providers, and Government⏎To encourage companies to invest in bringing AI to healthcare, and to promote mass adoption,⏎we need a system that aligns interests and provides incentives to reduce overall costs in our⏎healthcare system. The system should provide the upfront financial incentives necessary for⏎tech developers to innovate and for physicians to adopt new tools. It should encourage a⏎data-driven, value-based approach to ensure that the most effective and efficient technologies⏎become a permanent part of the healthcare landscape, improving patient care and saving long⏎term costs. By having the government step in to create this provisional bridge, we can⏎accelerate the adoption of high-value AI, foster competition, and ultimately modernize our⏎healthcare system to be more effective and affordable for everyone. The system should tie⏎reimbursement to proven value. For certain technologies, developers should first secure FDA⏎authorization and then receive provisional coverage and reimbursement, encouraging⏎investment and adoption. During this provisional period, developers can negotiate⏎reimbursement rates and collect real-world evidence to demonstrate improved patient care and⏎cost savings in order to maintain long-term reimbursement.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care?⏎The most significant barriers include:⏎● Ambiguity in the regulatory pathway for certain AI-based products can deter investment⏎and innovation in the US market. Specific concerns include the status of non-device⏎clinical decision support software, positions on enforcement discretion which could⏎change, and potential regulation of more advanced non-determinate medical device⏎software such as Foundation Models or generative AI where stochastic outputs present⏎challenges for traditional verification and validation frameworks.⏎⏎● Access to large, high-quality, and diverse datasets is essential for training/validating AI⏎models, and for effective post-market monitoring/improvement. However, data is often⏎siloed in disparate electronic health record (EHR) systems, and a lack of interoperability⏎hinders data sharing.⏎● Regulatory requirements for AI-based medical devices should be commensurate with⏎their risks and context of use. For example, The IMDRF document titled Software as a⏎Medical Device: Possible Framework for Risk Categorization and Corresponding⏎Considerations is specifically referenced in the FDA Guidance document titled Clinical⏎Decision Support Software Guidance for Industry and Food and Drug Administration⏎Staff Document issued on January 29, 2026. This risk categorization framework⏎describes how the significance of information provided by medical device software to a⏎healthcare decision (i.e., to treat/diagnose, to drive clinical management, or to inform⏎clinical management) results in a different benefit-risk profile, and this should be taken⏎into consideration when defining pre-market submission requirements.⏎● FDA should apply a reasonable standard for software performance for lower-risk Class II⏎clinical decision support medical devices. Balancing pre- and post-market requirements⏎and acknowledging necessary trade-offs of sensitivity vs. specificity for low-risk medical⏎devices is essential to unlock significant value to hospitals and patients alike, provided a⏎physician remains the ultimate decision maker.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of⏎Federal Regulations citations.⏎As described in Docket No. FDA‑2025‑P‑5560 (Medical Devices; Exemption From Premarket⏎Notification: Radiology Computer-Aided Detection and/or Diagnosis Devices and⏎Computer-Aided Triage and Notification Devices), Tempus supports the proposal for partial⏎510(k) exemption for the listed medical devices. We further recommend the expansion of this⏎partial exemption to include:⏎● Product Code QIH/LLZ: Radiological Machine Learning Based Quantitative Imaging⏎Software with Change Control Plan (potentially through updates to the special controls⏎for 21 CFR § 892.2055).⏎● Product Code QPN: Software Algorithm Devices to Assist Users in Digital Pathology⏎(through updates to special controls for 21 CFR § 864.3750).⏎● Product Code PSY and QKQ: Whole Slide Imaging System, and Digital Pathology Image⏎Viewing and Management Software (through updates to special controls for 21 CFR §⏎864.3700)⏎3. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎⏎For medical devices regulated by FDA, the use of new accreditation/certification/credentialing⏎programs created by HHS or third parties imposes redundant burdens on clinical adoption with⏎limited additional value given the robustness of the current FDA process. HHS should not⏎implement or encourage such additional steps for medical devices. To support a clear,⏎predictable, and risk-based regulatory framework, FDA should continue engagement with⏎standards development organizations and focus on the recognition of appropriate standards⏎alongside guidance document development activities for pre- and post-market requirements for⏎AI-based medical devices. Harmonization of requirements across international regulatory bodies⏎will further support industry investment in innovative and effective AI when requirements for⏎market access are standardized and streamlined.⏎4. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and⏎help reduce costs?⏎At the end of 2025, total healthcare costs in this country were estimated to exceed $5.7 trillion,⏎which consumes almost 20% of the entire US economy, with a growth rate of approximately 7%.⏎By most estimates, avoidable waste accounts for 20-30% of total healthcare spend, with some⏎estimates as high as 40%. At the lower end, if we could mitigate errors, mistakes, and other⏎waste, we would save approximately $1.5 trillion annually. To-date, AI tools have fallen short of⏎addressing this expense. A change to regulatory and reimbursement pathways to incentive⏎investment into AI solutions in this space could unlock significant cost savings.⏎Additionally, there is a vast amount of unused data generated as part of routine care, which can⏎be considered the “digital exhaust” of the healthcare system, because this data is generated as⏎a byproduct of routine digital health interactions and activities. This includes a wide range of⏎information such as EHRs, digital imaging, lab results, and other forms of digital documentation⏎that are created during routine clinical care. There is an opportunity to greatly improve⏎healthcare outcomes by leveraging AI to extract information from this digital exhaust, identifying⏎patterns that are not readily identifiable by clinicians, to provide otherwise unavailable insights⏎into a patient’s health status. Again, an alignment of regulatory and reimbursement pathways is⏎critical to unlocking investment in these solutions.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide these comments, and we thank you for your⏎consideration.⏎Sincerely,⏎Lauren Silvis⏎Senior Vice President External Affairs⏎Tempus AI♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0372♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Health Universe, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Health Universe♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS AI RFI - Responses to 10 Specific Questions⏎Submitted by: Health Universe, Inc.⏎Docket: RIN 0955-AA13⏎Date: February 18, 2026⏎About Health Universe: We are one of fewer than 10 platforms nationally holding ONC certification for⏎Decision Support Interventions (§170.315(b)(11)). Our FHIR-native platform enables developers to build,⏎deploy, and scale clinical AI applications with embedded compliance and interoperability.⏎QUESTION 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎A. Regulatory Uncertainty⏎The 21st Century Cures Act's four-factor test for CDS exclusion remains poorly understood. Developers cannot⏎determine whether FDA oversight applies, causing investment hesitation and either over-engineering for⏎anticipated FDA review or under-investing in safety validation.⏎ONC Role: Work with FDA to publish joint guidance with worked examples categorizing common AI use⏎cases into clear regulatory pathways.⏎B. Data Access and Interoperability Fragmentation⏎Despite FHIR adoption progress, implementation varies widely across EHR vendors. Startups face $50K-$500K⏎integration costs per EHR vendor, favoring large companies with resources for multiple integrations.⏎ONC Actions Needed:⏎• Strengthen standards conformance, by mandating FHIR R4 conformance testing with public⏎compliance dashboards⏎• Expand USCDI to include: social determinants of health (housing, food security, transportation),⏎wearable device data, patient-reported outcomes (PROMIS, PHQ-9), medication adherence data⏎• Clarify information blocking provisions (45 CFR Part 171) to protect AI developers' data access rights⏎C. Compliance Cost Concentration⏎Every AI developer must independently achieve ONC certification ($200K-$500K), HIPAA compliance⏎($100K-$300K), and SOC 2 attestation ($50K-$150K). Total costs of $500K-$2M create barriers favoring⏎wellfunded companies. Combined with delay tactics for integration, many smaller companies with good ideas⏎never come to market. In addition, the architecture of Health IT is beginning to change – platforms can perform⏎many of the functions of integration and compliance and integrate with legacy EHR vendors and innovative⏎new start-ups.⏎⏎ONC Solution: Establish platform certification framework in 45 CFR § 170.550 where certified platforms⏎extend compliance to applications built on their infrastructure.⏎As a B.11-certified platform, we enable developers to inherit our certification, reducing their costs by 75%+.⏎Formalizing this model would accelerate innovation while maintaining quality oversight.⏎D. Lack of Standardized Evaluation⏎No consensus exists on AI validation methodology. Healthcare organizations struggle to compare AI systems⏎objectively, leading to paralysis or poor decisions based on sales pitches rather than evidence. This is true of⏎existing LLMs and becomes more complex with MCP and agentic AI systems.⏎ONC Action: Establish standard AI performance reporting template (sensitivity, specificity, PPV, NPV, AUC⏎stratified by demographics) recommended for certified AI systems, with public dashboard for comparison.⏎QUESTION 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎Priority 1: Establish Platform Certification Framework⏎Change: Add provisions to 45 CFR § 170.550 recognizing platform certification with compliance inheritance.⏎This is a logical extension of modular certification but creates new opportunities for new technology to be⏎integrated into EHR systems that lack AI infrastructure.⏎Mechanism: Certified platforms maintain comprehensive ONC certification; applications built on certified⏎platforms inherit compliance status; ONC audits platforms rather than individual applications.⏎Impact: Reduces developer costs from $500K-$2M to $50K-$200K, enabling startups to compete while⏎maintaining oversight.⏎Priority 2: Information Blocking Safe Harbors for AI⏎Changes to 45 CFR § 171.202:⏎• Protect AI training data requests when data is de-identified per 45 CFR § 164.514(b)⏎• Protect algorithm validation for quality improvement under 45 CFR § 164.506(c)(4)\⏎• Protect performance monitoring for bias/safety detection, including logs and other analytic tools⏎Impact: Enables AI development while maintaining privacy. Healthcare organizations limit sharing of data⏎because of privacy concerns. Strengthening safe harbors and clarify the rules will increase transparency and⏎collaboration between health systems and AI developers.⏎Priority 3: Mandate Consistent FHIR Implementation⏎Changes to 45 CFR § 170.315:⏎⏎• Require conformance testing demonstrating full FHIR R4 implementation⏎• Mandate CDS Hooks or FHIR Clinical Reasoning for decision support integration⏎• Public dashboards showing EHR vendor compliance⏎Impact: Standardized implementation enables AI developers to build once and deploy everywhere, reducing⏎integration costs.⏎Priority 3: Preserve Optional Certification for Decision Support⏎ONC Should: Maintain §170.315(b)(11) as voluntary certification rather than eliminating it. Make certification⏎valuable through:⏎• Recognition in payment policies⏎• Streamlined pathways for certified platforms⏎• Public performance registries for certified AI⏎Rationale: Complete elimination removes objective quality signals healthcare organizations use to evaluate AI⏎platforms. Voluntary certification preserves market differentiation while reducing mandatory burden.⏎QUESTION 3: What novel legal and implementation issues exist for non-medical device AI⏎and what role should HHS play?⏎A. Liability Allocation⏎Issue: Unclear liability when AI generates incorrect recommendations - developer, platform, healthcare⏎organization, or clinician?⏎HHS Role: Issue guidance on standard of care for AI-augmented practice and convene stakeholders to develop⏎model liability frameworks.⏎B. Privacy Beyond HIPAA⏎Issue: AI infers sensitive information not explicitly documented (genetic predisposition from family history,⏎mental health from behavioral patterns).⏎ONC Role: OCR guidance clarifying whether AI-inferred information qualifies as PHI under 45 CFR §⏎164.501.⏎C. Algorithmic Bias⏎Issue: AI may perpetuate health disparities through biased algorithms.⏎ONC Actions:⏎⏎• Establish maximum allowable performance disparities (e.g., sensitivity must not differ by >5% across⏎racial groups)⏎• Require bias impact assessments for certified AI⏎• Mandate performance reporting stratified by demographics⏎D. Data Governance for AI Training⏎Issue: Healthcare organizations uncertain about legal basis for sharing data for AI development (research vs.⏎quality improvement vs. information blocking).⏎HHS Role: Unified guidance clarifying that de-identified data per Safe Harbor is not human subjects research⏎and AI development doesn't trigger information blocking.⏎E. Intellectual Property vs. Transparency⏎Issue: Balance between transparency (clinical trust) and IP protection (innovation incentive).⏎ONC Role: Establish minimum transparency standards protecting trade secrets:⏎Require disclosure: Training data characteristics, intended use, validation performance, limitations⏎Protect: Specific algorithms and proprietary training data⏎Public reporting: Aggregate performance and bias metrics⏎QUESTION 4: What are the most promising AI evaluation methods for non-medical devices?⏎Should HHS support these processes?⏎Pre-Deployment:⏎A. Prospective Clinical Validation⏎• Performance metrics stratified by demographics⏎• Minimum sample sizes appropriate to risk (n≥100 simple, n≥1000 complex)⏎• Confidence intervals and subgroup analysis⏎B. Fairness Assessment⏎• Multiple metrics: demographic parity, equalized odds, calibration⏎• Maximum 5% performance difference across protected groups⏎C. Robustness Testing⏎• Performance with missing data, outliers, distributional shifts⏎⏎• Identification of failure modes⏎Post-Deployment:⏎D. Real World Performance Monitoring Given the rapidly changing ecosystem, this is the most important⏎testing – real world performance monitoring with rapid response should there be any anomalies detected.⏎• Monthly performance metrics⏎• Data drift detection⏎• Adverse event surveillance⏎• Performance within 10% of validation⏎ONC Support Mechanisms:⏎1. Public Performance Registry Voluntary registry for certified platforms demonstrating ongoing quality.⏎Participants report quarterly metrics, adverse events, bias assessments. Create safe harbor for good faith⏎reporting.⏎2. Benchmark Dataset Development Partner with healthcare organizations to create de-identified⏎validation datasets for common use cases (sepsis, readmission, imaging).⏎QUESTION 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎A. Federally Recognized AI Application Marketplace⏎Concept: Industry-led marketplaces with federal endorsement (not operation).⏎Trust Seals:⏎• Platform Certified: Built on ONC-certified platform⏎• Clinically Validated: Peer-reviewed evidence or Real World Testing⏎• FDA Cleared: Medical device clearance⏎ONC Role:⏎• Recognize certification in payment policies⏎• Avoid creating new mandatory requirements⏎Critical Design: Marketplace displays certification status, preserving competitive value of certified technology⏎B. Industry Testing Standards⏎Support professional organizations (AMIA, ACR, CAP) developing testing protocols:⏎• Standardized metrics by clinical domain⏎⏎• Benchmark datasets⏎• Best practices for validation⏎ONC Role: Incorporate industry benchmarks into certification criteria; recognize standards in guidance.⏎C. Organizational Accreditation⏎Extend Joint Commission model to AI developers - assess quality management systems:⏎• ISO 13485 equivalent⏎• Bias assessment methodology⏎• Clinical safety framework Interoperability standards⏎ONC Role: Streamlined certification requirements for accredited developers.⏎D. Private Sector Performance Registry⏎Structure:⏎• Quarterly performance metrics stratified by demographics⏎• Adverse event reports⏎• Version tracking⏎• Public dashboard⏎ONC Support:⏎• Technical infrastructure funding⏎• Recognition in payment models⏎• Safe harbor for reporting⏎QUESTION 6: Where have AI tools met or exceeded expectations and where have they fallen⏎short? What novel AI tools have greatest potential?⏎Success Stories:⏎A. Ambient Clinical Documentation (Nuance DAX, Suki)⏎• 40-50% documentation time reduction⏎• High physician satisfaction (>80%)⏎• ROI within 6-12 months⏎• Success factors: Clear use case, immediate benefit, low patient harm risk⏎B. Radiology Screening (FDA-cleared AI)⏎• Sensitivity >95% for critical findings⏎• 30-40% faster time-to-diagnosis⏎⏎• Reduced radiologist fatigue⏎• Success factors: Objective task, FDA clearance, augments not replaces⏎C. Clinical Trial Matching⏎• 3-10x increase in patients screened⏎• 60-70% coordinator time reduction⏎• Improved trial diversity⏎• Success factors: High unmet need, low risk⏎Disappointments:⏎D. Sepsis Prediction⏎• High false positives (>50%) causing alert fatigue⏎• Poor generalization across hospitals⏎• Limited evidence of outcome improvement⏎• Lessons: Need continuous recalibration, local validation, actionable interventions⏎E. Hospital Readmission Prediction⏎• AUC 0.70-0.75 (modest improvement)⏎• Prediction without intervention has limited value⏎• Missing key predictors (social determinants not in EHR)⏎• Lessons: Must couple prediction with effective interventions⏎High-Potential Novel Tools:⏎F. LLMs for Documentation Quality and Administrative Tasks⏎• Real-time review for completeness, coding accuracy, clinical reasoning gaps⏎• Could reduce denials (5-10% revenue improvement) and improve quality measures⏎• Barrier: Regulatory uncertainty about medical device status⏎G. Continuous Remote Monitoring⏎• AI analysis of wearables, smart home sensors, patient-reported symptoms⏎• Early detection of deterioration, medication adherence support⏎• Barrier: Lack of reimbursement for non-FDA-cleared devices⏎H. AI for SDOH Screening and Intervention⏎• Screen for social needs, connect to resources, optimize allocation⏎• Address root causes of disparities⏎⏎• Barrier: Lack of structured SDOH data in EHRs⏎QUESTION 7: Which decision makers have most influence on AI adoption? What are⏎primary administrative hurdles?⏎Key Decision Makers:⏎A. Chief Medical Information Officers (CMIOs) - Most Influential⏎• Bridge clinical needs and technology⏎• Criteria: Clinical evidence, workflow integration, physician support⏎B. Chief Financial Officers (CFOs) - Budget Authority⏎• Criteria: ROI <24 months, reimbursement clarity, revenue impact⏎• Current barrier: Lack of CPT codes creates uncertainty and makes ROI more challenging⏎C. Chief Information Officers (CIOs) - Technical Feasibility⏎• Criteria: API compatibility, security, vendor stability⏎Administrative Hurdles:⏎1. Procurement Complexity (6-18 months)⏎• Legal review of contracts, BAA negotiations, multi-stakeholder approvals, multiple IP reviews⏎• ONC solution: Model contracts and BAAs for AI vendors; streamlined review for certified platforms⏎2. EHR Integration⏎• Custom work for each vendor ($50K-$200K per EHR)⏎• ONC solution: Mandate standardized FHIR R4 and CDS Hooks; require conformance testing⏎3. Validation Uncertainty⏎• No standard methodology, difficult to compare vendors⏎• ONC solution: Standard performance metrics, public dashboards, benchmark datasets⏎4. Data Governance⏎• Uncertainty about data sharing legality⏎• ONC solution: Clear guidance on AI training data, information blocking safe harbors⏎How Platform Certification Accelerates:⏎⏎Certified platforms would reduce timeline from 3-6 years to 12-18 months through:⏎• Pre-negotiated contracts and BAAs⏎• Proven EHR integration via FHIR⏎• Validated security and compliance⏎• Rapid deployment capability⏎QUESTION 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities and⏎accelerate AI development?⏎Critical Data Types for USCDI Expansion:⏎A. Social Determinants of Health⏎• Housing status, food security, transportation, social isolation, Area Deprivation Index⏎• Essential for: Readmission prediction, chronic disease management, health equity AI⏎• Market impact: Enables community-based organization integration⏎B. Patient-Reported Outcomes⏎• PROMIS measures, PRO-CTCAE, PHQ-9, GAD-7, functional status⏎• Essential for: Treatment response prediction, symptom management⏎• AI Market impact: Patient engagement platforms can integrate⏎C. Wearable Device Data⏎• Continuous heart rate/HRV, activity, sleep, glucose, blood pressure⏎• Essential for: Remote monitoring AI, early warning systems⏎• Market impact: Consumer health AI at scale⏎D. Medication Adherence⏎• Pharmacy fill dates, MPR, PDC, gaps in therapy⏎• Essential for: Treatment response prediction, adherence interventions⏎• Market impact: $300B non-adherence problem⏎E. Genomic Data⏎• Structured variants (HGVS notation), pharmacogenomic implications, tumor molecular features⏎• Essential for: Precision medicine AI⏎• Market impact: Oncology treatment selection⏎F. Nationally recognized guidelines⏎⏎• Standardized or computable access to authoritative guidelines⏎• Less reliance on LLMs and incorporation of high quality peer-reviewed information into the⏎clinician’s workflow⏎Data Standards Needed:⏎G. Standardized AI Performance Metrics⏎• Public dashboard for comparison⏎• Public deidentified datasets for gold standard assessments and comparison⏎• Impact: Enables objective procurement decisions⏎H. Fairness Assessment Standards⏎• Multiple metrics with disclosure of tradeoffs⏎• Maximum allowable disparities (5% for high-stakes)⏎• Impact: Equity-focused organizations can select unbiased AI⏎I. FHIR Clinical Reasoning Adoption⏎• Mandate CDS Hooks or FHIR Clinical Reasoning for certified DSI⏎• Deprecate proprietary integration methods⏎• Impact: Reduces integration costs dramatically, enables platform models⏎Market Opportunities:⏎Expand innovation to independent, third-party apps stores that increase distribution channels for innovators,⏎while providing more access to innovation for those EHR systems that serve smaller, rural and community⏎practices.⏎QUESTION 9: What challenges do patients wish AI to address? What concerns do they have?⏎Patient Priorities:⏎A. Access to Care⏎• Wait times for appointments (weeks to months)⏎• AI solutions: Virtual triage, optimized scheduling, 24/7 chatbots⏎B. Care Coordination⏎• "Nobody talks to each other" - repeating history, duplicate tests⏎• AI solutions: Automated care summaries, medication reconciliation, care gap alerts⏎C. Understanding Medical Information⏎• Confusing terminology, limited provider time for questions⏎⏎• AI solutions: Plain language translation, visual explanations, personalized education⏎D. Medication Management⏎• Complex regimens, forgetting doses, unclear side effects⏎• AI solutions: Smart reminders, adherence monitoring, interaction checking⏎E. Cost Transparency⏎• Unpredictable out-of-pocket costs⏎• AI solutions: Cost estimation, financial assistance identification, prior auth support⏎Patient Concerns:⏎1. Privacy and Data Security⏎• Will data be sold? Can AI be hacked? Will insurers discriminate?⏎• Address through: Clear consent, data minimization, audit trails, federal protections, harmonization of⏎privacy regulations (FTC, HIPAA, FIRPA, Common Rule, FDA)⏎2. AI Errors and Safety⏎• What if AI misses something important? Who's responsible?⏎• Address through: Human oversight always, transparent performance, error reporting, liability clarity⏎3. Bias and Fairness⏎• Will AI discriminate based on race, gender, age, socioeconomic status?⏎• Address through: Mandatory bias testing, diverse training data, community engagement, opt-out⏎option⏎4. Loss of Human Connection⏎• Will AI replace my doctor?⏎• Address through: Emphasize AI saves physician time for patients, enhances not replaces⏎5. Lack of Control⏎• "Black box" making decisions about my health⏎• Address through: Plain explanations, transparency in recommendations, opt-in consent⏎ONC Role:⏎⏎• OCR enforcement of HIPAA⏎• Mandatory adverse event reporting for certified AI⏎• Maximum allowable performance disparities⏎• Health literacy standards for AI disclosures⏎• Patient privacy bill of rights⏎QUESTION 10: What AI research should HHS prioritize? What does literature show about⏎costs, benefits, and transfers?⏎A. Published Findings on AI Impact:⏎Strong Evidence:⏎Radiology AI: Sensitivity >90% for specific findings; improved detection, reduced reading time⏎Diabetic retinopathy screening: FDA-cleared autonomous AI; cost-effective in primary care ($500/QALY)⏎Pathology AI: High sensitivity for cancer detection; reduced inter-observer variability⏎Moderate Evidence:⏎• Clinical Decision support and guideline adherence - Mixed results - some early detection, others⏎high false positives and poor generalization⏎Clinical documentation AI: 30-50% time savings, high satisfaction in pilots⏎Evidence Gaps:⏎• Most studies report accuracy, not patient outcomes (mortality, quality of life)⏎• Limited cost-effectiveness analyses⏎• Sparse implementation science literature⏎• Few studies assess health equity impact⏎B. Literature on Costs, Benefits, Transfers:⏎Implementation Costs (Well-Documented):⏎• AI licensing: $10K-$500K annually⏎• Integration: $50K-$500K⏎• Training: $25K-$100K⏎• Ongoing maintenance: 15-20% annually⏎⏎Cost Savings (Mixed Evidence):⏎• Time savings: 20-50% depending on application⏎• Revenue enhancement: 5-10% from improved coding⏎• Cost avoidance: Limited evidence for avoided hospitalizations⏎Value Transfers:⏎• From payers to providers if AI reduces costs but payments unchanged⏎• From providers to AI vendors through subscription fees⏎• From physicians to organizations if productivity gains not shared⏎• Risk of widening digital divide if AI only accessible to well-resourced organizations⏎Research Priorities for ONC:⏎1. Real World Performance Standards ONC should establish requirements for post-deployment monitoring:⏎• Quarterly performance reports stratified by demographics⏎• Adverse event reporting within 48 hours⏎• Performance degradation triggers (>10% from validation)⏎• Incentivize the development of standardized, annotated datasets for gold standard assessments and⏎comparisons.⏎2. Interoperability Standards Development ONC support for HL7 development of:⏎• Model card format and content standards⏎• AI performance reporting templates⏎• CDS Hooks extensions for AI integration⏎• FHIR resources for AI metadata⏎3. Bias Assessment Methodologies ONC should define:⏎• Standard fairness metrics for healthcare AI⏎• Maximum allowable performance disparities⏎• Reporting requirements for certified AI⏎• Mitigation strategies when thresholds exceeded⏎• Datasets that can benchmark and provide gold standard validation for AI tools.⏎4. Validation Dataset Development ONC coordination of multi-institutional efforts to create de-identified⏎benchmark datasets for common AI use cases.⏎CONCLUSION⏎⏎Health Universe urges ONC to:⏎1. Establish platform certification framework enabling compliance inheritance⏎2. Preserve certification as optional but valuable market differentiator⏎3. Mandate consistent FHIR implementation with public dashboards⏎4. Expand USCDI with AI-essential data elements⏎5. Create information blocking safe harbors for AI development⏎6. Create standardized datasets with annotations for gold standard assessments and benchmarking⏎7. Require bias impact assessments for certified AI⏎These actions would accelerate beneficial AI innovation while maintaining patient safety and trust.⏎Contact:⏎Dan Caron, CEO: dan.caron@healthuniverse.com⏎Doug Fridsma, M.D., Ph.D., CMIO: doug.fridsma@healthuniverse.com⏎Health Universe, Inc.⏎548 Market Street, Suite 35123⏎San Francisco, CA 94104⏎https://healthuniverse.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0373♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Clinical Laboratory Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Clinical Laboratory Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Posnack,⏎The American Clinical Laboratory Association (ACLA) appreciates the⏎opportunity to respond to ASTP/ONC’s request for information on accelerating the⏎adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care, pursuant to the HHS Artificial Intelligence⏎(AI) Strategy1 in response to Office of Management and Budget (OMB) Memorandum⏎25-212.⏎ACLA is the national trade association representing leading laboratories that⏎deliver essential diagnostic health information to patients and providers by advocating⏎for policies that expand access to the highest quality clinical laboratory services,⏎improve patient outcomes, and advance the next generation of personalized care.⏎ACLA appreciates the Administration’s efforts to identify regulatory, reimbursement, and⏎research and development barriers in order to support rapid adoption and use of AI in⏎clinical care. These efforts are critical to ACLA members’ patient-centered mission, to⏎improve the health of all Americans. As clinical laboratories strive to empower patients⏎with actionable information about their health, AI is enhancing efforts to prevent and⏎detect the onset of diseases and changes in chronic conditions, providing useful data to⏎inform care in new ways.⏎The diagnosis and decision-making process is still reliant on the expertise of⏎trained healthcare professionals, but AI-generated insights that take into account patient⏎history, diagnostic test results, and medical records can assist clinicians with making a⏎comprehensive diagnosis and formulating a treatment plan for each patient. The value⏎of AI developments in laboratory medicine is based on AI’s ability to quickly analyze⏎large amounts of data, identify trends and patterns, and produce accurate insights that⏎can support clinical decision making. In clinical laboratories, AI has the potential to help⏎1 https://www.hhs.gov/press-room/hhs-unveils-ai-strategy-to-transform-agency-operations.html.⏎2 https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/M-25-21-Accelerating-Federal-Use-of-AI-⏎through-Innovation-Governance-and-Public-Trust.pdf.⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 2⏎laboratorians prioritize and streamline their work to increase efficiency and develop⏎individualized treatment plans, and it can also help to increase testing volumes,⏎enabling laboratories to serve more patients and to serve them better. AI already is⏎being used to help improve business operations, streamline workflow, and ease⏎administrative burdens.⏎As the Administration works to implement the President's AI Action Plan3, recent⏎Executive Orders4 on AI, and OMB AI memoranda5, ACLA respectfully requests the⏎Administration consider the issues set forth below to maximize the benefits of AI⏎technology.⏎Regulation: Federal Preemption and Regulatory Clarity⏎Preemption: The rapid development and proliferation of AI in healthcare⏎underscore the need for a careful balance of regulatory clarity and stability—so that⏎industry has a clear understanding of government expectations—while also maintaining⏎the flexibility needed to adapt to evolving technological advancements and emerging⏎innovations. Key to this careful balancing act is preempting a patchwork of state and⏎local laws regulating healthcare AI, which would threaten to stifle innovation. ACLA⏎appreciates the Administration taking the lead on this issue to ensure clarity and foster⏎innovation.⏎Privacy: Under the current Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act⏎(HIPAA) requirements and its implementing regulations at 45 CFR Part 164 (the⏎“Privacy Rule”), clinical laboratories are advancing AI innovation through the⏎permissions granted to de-identify Protected Health Information (PHI) and use such de-⏎identified information in a manner unrestricted by HIPAA, including to develop and train⏎AI models. In its guidance on de-identification, the Office for Civil Rights expresses how⏎de-identification supports the public good: “The process of de-identification, by which⏎identifiers are removed from the health information, mitigates privacy risks to individuals⏎and thereby supports the secondary use of data for comparative effectiveness studies,⏎policy assessment, life sciences research, and other endeavors.”6 Clinical laboratories⏎have contributed exponential advancements in AI-enabled medicine in recent years –⏎from more precise identification of the origin of a patient’s cancer, to better⏎understanding and predicting a patient’s response to specific treatments– thanks to⏎research involving large datasets of de-identified information. These advancements⏎simply would not have been possible without de-identification permissions under⏎HIPAA.⏎3 https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/07/Americas-AI-Action-Plan.pdf.⏎4 https://www.ai.gov/#resources-anchor.⏎5 See: https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/M-25-21-Accelerating-Federal-Use-of-AI-⏎through-Innovation-Governance-and-Public-Trust.pdf and https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/⏎uploads/2025/02/M-25-22-Driving-Efficient-Acquisition-of-Artificial-Intelligence-in-Government.pdf and⏎https://www.whitehouse.gov/wp-content/uploads/2025/12/M-26-04-Increasing-Public-Trust-in-Artificial-⏎Intelligence-Through-Unbiased-AI-Principles-1.pdf.⏎6 See https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/special-topics/de-identification/index.html.⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 3⏎ACLA hopes the Administration will continue to foster and encourage use of de-⏎identified information. The Administration should take care not to disrupt the balance⏎between genetic information and de-identification that already exists under the Privacy⏎Rule. OCR guidance dating back to 2002 has confirmed that genetic information is PHI⏎only if it is ‘individually identifiable’, indicating that OCR does not treat all genetic⏎information as PHI.7 This means that genetic information is not PHI if it is otherwise de-⏎identified per the HIPAA Privacy Rule. The inclusion of genetic information in de-⏎identified data sets has been instrumental in the medical advancements described⏎above.⏎CLIA Regulation Updates: Reinforcing that the Clinical Laboratory Improvement⏎Amendments (CLIA) is the regulatory framework for clinical laboratory services —⏎including laboratory services that examine and analyze digital laboratory information—is⏎essential for advancing innovation and the adoption of AI in clinical care. In 2025, ACLA⏎secured a landmark victory in ACLA v. FDA, Civil No. 4:24-CV-479-SDJ (Mar. 31,⏎2025), which vacated a Biden-era rule to regulate laboratory developed testing services⏎(LDTs) as medical devices, and affirmed that LDTs are professional services that⏎should not be subject to an unsuitable Food and Drug Administration (FDA) regulatory⏎framework. This ruling safeguards patient access to vital testing services and prevents⏎the imposition of unnecessary, burdensome regulations that could undermine the⏎effectiveness of the clinical laboratory system in the United States.⏎There remains an opportunity to make targeted updates to the CLIA regulations⏎that would encourage development and adoption of AI technologies that enhance⏎clinical diagnostics. These regulations were promulgated decades ago, before the⏎proliferation of software and AI that have enhanced diagnostic practices and workflows.⏎The following updates to CLIA would clarify that laboratory operations include analysis⏎and examination of digital laboratory information (also referred to as “algorithmic-only”⏎or “dry lab” work) and free pathologists and laboratory personnel to review such⏎information remotely under the primary site’s CLIA certificate.⏎First, the definition of “laboratory” under the CLIA regulations should be⏎amended to expressly state that laboratories include facilities that perform “algorithmic”⏎analysis of “patient-specific digital laboratory information,” such as previously⏎sequenced DNA or digital images of slides. Advancements in AI and clinical laboratory⏎science have enabled laboratories to develop custom, algorithmic-only services that⏎analyze digital laboratory information to unlock new clinical insights that improve patient⏎care. The CLIA regulations do not expressly describe these algorithmic-only services,⏎and clarifying that CLIA is the applicable regulatory framework for such services will⏎ensure there is no confusion on this point, thereby encouraging laboratories to develop⏎and leverage these innovative and groundbreaking services for the benefit of patients.⏎As described in the section on reimbursement below, this clarification also will help to⏎ensure that these services are appropriately coded, appropriately valued and covered⏎by payers. Concurrent with establishing this clarity, it may be appropriate to add new⏎7 See https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/faq/354/does-hipaa-protect-genetic-⏎information/index.html⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 4⏎specialties to CLIA—with specialty-specific quality requirements, such as expectations⏎for personnel—to account for these innovative laboratory services.⏎Second, CMS should codify its enforcement discretion policy that permits⏎pathologists and other laboratory personnel to review digital laboratory data, digital⏎results and digital images (“digital materials”) remotely—such as via a secure⏎connection on a laptop or desktop computer in a home office—without obtaining a⏎separate certificate. This policy of enforcement discretion, which still requires the⏎remote work to be performed under the CLIA certificate of the primary site, was initially⏎adopted in the early days of the COVID-19 pandemic and was instrumental to ensure⏎the continuity of laboratory operations while prioritizing the safety of laboratory workers.⏎Today, this enforcement discretion policy continues to be vital to laboratory operations⏎as the industry continues to face a persistent workforce shortage.⏎While we applaud the agency for continuing this common-sense enforcement⏎discretion policy8, we strongly encourage the Administration to codify the policy in⏎regulation so that laboratories can confidently rely on it for the future. The policy also⏎should be expanded so as not to exclude remote review of digital cytology slides,⏎enforcement discretion for which is scheduled to expire in March of this year.⏎To effectuate the above recommendations, we urge the Administration to revise⏎the CLIA regulations as recommended in Appendix A to this letter.⏎Reimbursement: Pathways to Reimbursement and Guardrails for Use of AI in⏎Coverage Decisions⏎For AI innovations in healthcare to meet their full potential, there must be a⏎business case for bringing these services to market, including predictable and⏎sustainable pathways to coverage and payment. Clinical laboratory services, including⏎genetic testing, are paid under the Medicare Clinical Laboratory Fee Schedule (CLFS).⏎Other laboratory services, such as anatomic pathology services, are paid under the⏎Medicare Physician Fee Schedule (PFS) because they require physicians’ professional⏎time to complete the service. For both payment systems reimbursement should reflect⏎the clinical value of the services and the resources required to furnish them. This⏎principle must be applied in a manner that accommodates AI-enabled laboratory⏎services. Without appropriate reimbursement, even clinically validated and widely⏎supported AI-enabled services cannot be adopted or sustained over time.⏎As policymakers are in the early stages of considering how government⏎programs like Medicare and TRICARE will pay for services that encompass AI, it is⏎important to evaluate sections of federal law that may be outdated or too rigid to⏎accommodate rapidly developing technology. One AI innovation in laboratory medicine⏎takes the form of algorithmic analyses applied to data generated from existing “wet-lab”⏎tests, often, combined with information gleaned from a medical record in an EHR.⏎8 REVISED: Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments of 1988 (CLIA) Post-Public Health Emergency⏎(PHE) Guidance | CMS⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 5⏎These AI-enabled algorithmic services, which require substantial investment to develop,⏎validate, and maintain, can meaningfully improve patient care by increasing diagnostic⏎precision, supporting more informed treatment decisions, and reducing unnecessary⏎downstream testing or ineffective therapies. The importance of this information, and the⏎investment in developing the technology that facilitates the prediction, should be⏎reflected in the reimbursement the laboratory receives for the service—even if the⏎laboratory testing does not require additional specimen collection, which is currently the⏎case under the CLFS.⏎Reimbursement for AI-enabled services also should not be impeded by⏎unnecessary administrative barriers imposed by private organizations. Accurate and⏎appropriate coding plays a foundational role in enabling coverage, payment and⏎continued investment in clinical services, including AI-enabled laboratory analyses.⏎When private coding governance has the effect of limiting Medicare payment pathways,⏎creating misalignment with federal regulatory frameworks, or constraining patient⏎access to clinically meaningful services, more proactive federal engagement with⏎relevant coding authorities would help ensure alignment with the AI policy objectives⏎outlined in this RFI.⏎One clear example of this challenge is the treatment of Proprietary Laboratory⏎Analysis (PLA) codes. Clinical laboratories have previously received PLA codes for⏎algorithmic-only analysis services, demonstrating that the existing coding framework⏎has historically accommodated innovative laboratory services that rely on computational⏎analysis rather than new wet-lab procedures. Recent changes to PLA guidance,⏎however, have created a foreseeable coding gap for laboratory algorithmic-only⏎analyses, as these services are expressly no longer eligible for PLA coding as of⏎January 1, 2026. The PLA coding process was specifically designed to identify and⏎manage innovative and proprietary laboratory tests, including novel services that do not⏎fit neatly within existing CPT categories. Algorithmic-only laboratory analyses fall⏎squarely within this design intent and should continue to be eligible for PLA coding as it⏎provides a suitable and effective mechanism for identifying and reporting these⏎services. CMS should work with the American Medical Association (AMA), which owns⏎the Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) code set, to ensure that the PLA coding⏎process remains available for new algorithmic-only laboratory services.⏎Recent proposals by the American Medical Association (AMA), which owns the⏎Current Procedural Terminology (CPT) code set, have suggested that algorithmic-only⏎services by laboratories should be coded as “devices”, and that they may require FDA⏎authorization to be eligible for certain types of codes. As noted above, however, in⏎March 2025, a federal district court rules that laboratory developed testing services are⏎not medical devices, and the FDA does not have the authority to regulate them as⏎such.9 Importantly, the court decision did not suggest that laboratory algorithmic⏎services are somehow beyond the scope of this ruling, and accordingly, the PLA coding⏎process remains appropriate for algorithmic-only laboratory services. We therefore⏎9 ACLA v. FDA, Civil No. 4:24-CV-824-SDJ, (E.D. Tex. 2025); 90 Fed. Reg. 45134 (Sept. 19, 2025) (final⏎rule removing language from invalidated regulation).⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 6⏎respectfully request that CMS work with the AMA to ensure that the PLA coding process⏎remains available for new algorithmic-only laboratory services. Amending the CLIA⏎regulations to clarify that a “laboratory” includes a facility performing algorithmic-only⏎analyses of patient-specific digital laboratory information, as recommended in the⏎“Regulations” section above, may also help to expedite resolution of this misalignment⏎of private coding policy with federal regulatory frameworks.⏎Guardrails for Use of AI in Coverage Decisions: AI systems should provide⏎transparency that enables users to understand the validity, reliability, and⏎trustworthiness of the system. ACLA members are committed to designing trustworthy⏎AI services, identifying any limitations of data, and clearly communicating such⏎limitations. While AI can lead to workflow efficiencies in the health care system, it is⏎important that it is used appropriately and judiciously. ACLA is concerned with the⏎application of AI and other automation that negatively impacts coverage and⏎reimbursement for laboratory services. For example, payors and third-party⏎organizations with which health plans contract, such as laboratory benefit managers⏎(LBMs), apply opaque front-end proprietary coding edits that often lead to automatic⏎denial before a plan has an opportunity to review a claim for coverage based on medical⏎necessity. Front-end edits result in a significant number of inappropriate claim denials⏎and are a tremendous source of administrative burden on laboratories and on ordering⏎providers. Claims must be re-submitted despite each of the laboratory testing services⏎being medically necessary based on medical policy. AI and automated systems should⏎not be used to deny or impede patient access to services automatically. There must be⏎a human evaluation to ensure patients are not being denied care that is medically⏎necessary to address their health conditions.⏎Research and Development: Fueled by Adequate and Predictable Reimbursement⏎and Regulatory Clarity⏎Research and development for AI in clinical laboratories is driven by adequate⏎and predictable reimbursement and regulatory clarity. When reimbursement is stable⏎and sufficient, laboratories are better positioned to invest in developing and adopting⏎new technologies. And when regulatory expectations are clear and appropriate,⏎innovators can more confidently advance the field, both in terms of research and⏎adoption.⏎First, research, development, and adoption of AI requires significant⏎infrastructure, much of which represents a new expense for many laboratories.⏎Accordingly, as laboratories seek to adopt and invest in new technologies, like AI, they⏎must have appropriate, stable, and predictable reimbursement, reflective of the value of⏎diagnostics to patient care. Laboratory test results are the “GPS” of health care,⏎informing roughly 70 percent of medical decisions at a modest overall cost to the health⏎care system. The CLFS accounts for less than 1 percent of total Medicare spending.10,11⏎10 MedPAC Payment Basics, Clinical Laboratory Services Payment System (Nov. 2025).⏎11 Center for Disease Control and Prevention: https://www.cdc.gov/lab-week/about-archive.html.⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 7⏎Implementation of the Protecting Access to Medicare Act (PAMA) in 2014⏎resulted in significant payment cuts to tests paid under the CLFS, which have not seen⏎a payment adjustment since 2020, following three years of up to 10% payment cuts to⏎reimbursement rates for some tests in 2018-2020). Foundational flaws prevent the⏎CLFS under PAMA from being a market-based fee schedule with rates that are⏎predictable, sustainable, and sufficient. The Reforming and Enhancing Sustainable⏎Updates to Laboratory Testing Services (RESULTS) Act (S. 2761/ H.R. 5269) would⏎mitigate further payment reductions and enact a long-term solution that would enable⏎sustainable reimbursement rates to provide laboratories with the stability of appropriate⏎reimbursement needed for investments into advanced diagnostic technologies, such as⏎AI.⏎Second, updating and clarifying applicable regulations—such as CLIA—are⏎essential to fostering an environment that encourages research and development of⏎new AI technologies for clinical care. Clarifying that algorithmic-only analyses of⏎patient-specific digital laboratory information is included under that regulatory framework⏎will encourage further research and development by laboratories, and it will unlock⏎barriers to patient access to these innovative technologies.⏎Third, the Administration should continue to support, and even take additional⏎action, to actively encourage de-identification under HIPAA as a means to support AI⏎innovation. With respect to HIPAA, ACLA believes that safeguarding patient data and⏎privacy is paramount to maintain trust and uphold ethical standards in healthcare.⏎Simultaneously, utilizing deidentified data for AI innovation enables advancements in⏎diagnosis, treatment, and personalized medicine, ultimately improving patient⏎outcomes. It is important to strike a thoughtful balance between protecting sensitive⏎health information and permitting the use of deidentified data for research and⏎development purposes to further innovation.⏎ACLA appreciates the opportunity to provide these comments, and we thank you⏎for your consideration.⏎Sincerely,⏎Susan Van Meter⏎President, ACLA⏎Enclosure: Appendix A; ACLA Artificial Intelligence Policy Paper⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 8⏎Appendix A: CLIA Regulation Recommendations⏎For the reasons described in the letter, ACLA respectfully requests that the CLIA⏎regulations be amended as follows:⏎1. Amend 42 C.F.R. § 493.2 to add the following definition for “digital laboratory⏎information” and to revise the definition for “laboratory” (revisions in bold):⏎“Digital laboratory information:⏎(1) Means any of the following:⏎(A) A digital image derived from a glass slide; or⏎(B) Data or digital images including, but not limited to, flow⏎cytometry plots; cytogenetic karyograms; chromatographic,⏎mass spectrometric, clinical chemistry, immunological, and⏎hematologic data; electropherograms; gel images; and⏎genetic expression, array and sequencing data; or⏎(C) Results from software-only analysis of the information⏎described in (A) or (B); and⏎(2) Is patient-specific when it is accompanied by information that can⏎be used to identify the individual from whose specimen the⏎information was derived.⏎…⏎Laboratory means a facility for the biological, microbiological, serological,⏎chemical, immunohematological, hematological, biophysical, cytological,⏎pathological, algorithmic, or other examination of materials derived from⏎the human body, including patient-specific digital laboratory⏎information, for the purpose of providing information for the diagnosis,⏎prevention, or treatment of any disease or impairment of, or the assessment⏎of the health of, human beings...”⏎…⏎2. Amend 42 C.F.R. §§ 493.43(b) and 493.55(b), which requires separate CLIA⏎certificates for each place where laboratory testing occurs, by adding at the⏎end the following exception:⏎“(4) Laboratories, from which a board-certified pathologist or other⏎laboratory professional accesses the designated primary site system using⏎a secure remote access protocol to retrieve, review, and analyze patient⏎specific digital laboratory information.”⏎⏎ACLA Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎page 9⏎3. Amend 42 C.F.R. § 493.1274(a), the standard for cytology, to read as follows⏎(revisions in bold):⏎“(a) Cytology slides. All cytology slides must be prepared on the premises of⏎a laboratory certified to conduct testing in the subspecialty of cytology.”♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0374♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Noeted, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Noeted♔♔♔♔♔♔NOETED⏎Public Comment — Request for Information⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001 | Submitted February 2026⏎Submitted by: About NOETED:⏎Keith “Duv” Williams AI-powered healthcare compliance intelligence platform. Member, NVIDIA⏎Founder & CEO, Inception Program. Building centralized regulatory intelligence infrastructure⏎NOETED for U.S. healthcare organizations.⏎duv@noeted.com |⏎noeted.ai⏎Redding, California⏎Preamble⏎NOETED is an AI-powered healthcare compliance intelligence platform founded in Redding, California.⏎We are building the centralized regulatory intelligence infrastructure that U.S. healthcare organizations⏎currently lack: a single system that continuously monitors, aggregates, and connects compliance⏎requirements from federal agencies (CMS, OIG, FDA, SAMHSA, ONC), state health departments, and⏎regional regulatory bodies — delivering real-time alerts and AI-assisted gap analysis to healthcare⏎payers, providers, and health plans.⏎NOETED is a member of the NVIDIA Inception Program. Our perspective on this RFI is informed by⏎direct experience building AI systems designed to operate within the U.S. healthcare regulatory⏎environment — and by the specific infrastructure barriers we have encountered in attempting to access⏎the federal and state regulatory data that healthcare compliance depends on.⏎We respond to each of HHS's specific questions below. A consistent theme runs through our⏎responses: the regulatory data infrastructure problem. The APIs, databases, and publication systems⏎through which federal and state agencies publish compliance requirements are frequently antiquated,⏎unreliable, or nonexistent. This infrastructure failure is not peripheral — it is the foundational barrier that⏎prevents AI compliance tools from functioning reliably, that forces healthcare organizations into manual⏎monitoring processes, and that ultimately harms patients when compliance failures result in disrupted⏎care. We believe solving this infrastructure problem is the highest-leverage action HHS can take to⏎accelerate responsible AI adoption in clinical care.⏎⏎Q1 What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎NOETED Response⏎From NOETED's direct experience building AI for healthcare compliance, we identify four primary⏎barriers:⏎1. Regulatory fragmentation and inaccessibility.⏎The most underappreciated barrier to healthcare AI adoption is not the AI itself — it is the compliance⏎environment the AI must operate within. Healthcare organizations attempting to deploy AI tools face⏎requirements originating from CMS conditions of participation, OIG compliance program guidance, FDA⏎software-as-a-medical-device rules, state insurance commissioner mandates, HIPAA privacy and⏎security rules, 21st Century Cures Act information blocking provisions, and ONC's HTI-1 AI⏎transparency requirements. Each regulatory body publishes changes through different channels, on⏎different schedules, in different formats. There is no centralized, machine-readable, authoritative source⏎of healthcare regulatory requirements. This creates a compliance environment that is functionally⏎inaccessible to organizations without large legal teams — and that directly chills AI innovation because⏎organizations cannot assess whether their AI tools will be compliant with requirements they cannot⏎reliably track.⏎2. Reimbursement uncertainty.⏎Private sector AI developers cannot sustain investment in clinical AI tools without a clear path to⏎reimbursement. The absence of consistent Medicare and Medicaid payment pathways for AI-assisted⏎clinical services creates a market disincentive that is particularly acute for companies serving smaller⏎providers and rural health systems, where the cost of regulatory compliance and the uncertainty of⏎reimbursement combine to make the investment calculus prohibitive.⏎3. Liability ambiguity for AI-assisted clinical decisions.⏎The existing legal framework does not clearly allocate liability when an AI tool contributes to a clinical⏎decision that results in patient harm. This ambiguity discourages both AI developers (who face⏎unpredictable risk) and healthcare organizations (who cannot obtain clear indemnification). Until this⏎framework is clarified, risk-averse healthcare systems will default to limiting AI deployment to low-⏎stakes administrative functions rather than the high-value clinical applications that could most improve⏎outcomes.⏎4. Broken regulatory data infrastructure.⏎AI systems require high-quality, structured, standardized data. Despite progress under the 21st Century⏎Cures Act and ONC interoperability rules, significant barriers remain in the regulatory data domain⏎specifically. NOETED has encountered this infrastructure failure directly: the APIs, databases, and⏎publication systems through which federal and state agencies publish compliance requirements are⏎frequently antiquated, unreliable, or nonexistent. Where APIs do exist, access requirements are⏎burdensome, inconsistently documented, and subject to approval processes with no defined timeline or⏎accountability. Datasets are poorly organized, infrequently updated, and structurally inconsistent across⏎agencies and jurisdictions. Regulatory data that is legally public and operationally critical to compliance⏎monitoring is in practice inaccessible due to broken infrastructure and deprecated endpoints that no⏎agency has been directed to maintain.⏎⏎This is not a peripheral inconvenience. It is a foundational infrastructure failure that prevents AI⏎compliance tools from delivering reliable intelligence, forces healthcare organizations to rely on manual⏎monitoring processes, and directly harms patients when the resulting compliance failures disrupt⏎access to care. Smaller organizations — FQHCs, rural hospitals, community health centers — are⏎disproportionately affected because they lack the technical capacity to work around broken⏎infrastructure that larger systems can navigate through dedicated IT staff.⏎⏎Q2 What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable CFR citations.⏎NOETED Response⏎NOETED recommends the following specific changes, organized by regulatory domain. Our⏎recommendations are informed by direct experience building AI-powered regulatory intelligence⏎infrastructure and by the specific barriers NOETED has encountered accessing the federal and state⏎regulatory data that healthcare compliance depends on.⏎A. Mandate modernization of federal regulatory data publication infrastructure⏎This is NOETED's highest-priority recommendation because it is the foundational barrier that makes⏎every other recommendation harder to implement.⏎NOETED has encountered firsthand the infrastructure failure at the core of healthcare regulatory data:⏎the APIs, databases, and publication systems through which federal and state agencies publish⏎compliance requirements are frequently antiquated, unreliable, or nonexistent. Where APIs do exist,⏎access requirements are burdensome, inconsistently documented, and subject to approval processes⏎with no defined timeline or accountability. Datasets are poorly organized, infrequently updated, and⏎structurally inconsistent across agencies and jurisdictions. Regulatory data that is legally public and⏎operationally critical to compliance monitoring is in practice inaccessible — not because of policy⏎decisions, but because of broken infrastructure and deprecated endpoints that no agency has been⏎directed to maintain.⏎This is not a peripheral inconvenience. It is a foundational infrastructure failure that prevents AI⏎compliance tools from delivering reliable intelligence and forces healthcare organizations to continue⏎relying on manual, error-prone monitoring processes — exactly the processes that produce the⏎compliance failures that harm patients and cost the system billions annually.⏎HHS should issue a directive requiring all agencies publishing healthcare regulatory data — including⏎CMS, OIG, FDA, SAMHSA, and ONC — to meet minimum technical publication standards within a⏎defined implementation timeline. Those standards should include active, documented, versioned APIs⏎for all regulatory data; machine-readable publication formats with consistent schema; defined update⏎frequency requirements tied to regulatory change events; and a designated point of contact within each⏎agency responsible for regulatory data infrastructure.⏎Relevant authority: 45 CFR Part 170 (ONC Health IT Certification) — ONC should extend certification⏎criteria to include standardized regulatory data publication APIs, consistent with existing FHIR API⏎requirements under 45 CFR § 170.315(g)(10). HHS should additionally act under the Evidence Act⏎(Pub. L. 115-435) and OMB Circular A-130 to require open, machine-readable regulatory data⏎publication across all HHS divisions as a condition of federal information management compliance.⏎B. Establish a standardized regulatory data publication framework (ONC / HHS-wide)⏎Modernizing the infrastructure addresses access. Standardizing the format addresses usability. Both⏎are required.⏎⏎Currently, CMS, OIG, and state agencies publish regulatory changes through inconsistent channels —⏎Federal Register notices, agency website postings, email listservs, and PDF documents — with no⏎common data model, no structured metadata, no version control, and no programmatic access. A⏎compliance technology platform attempting to monitor regulatory changes across even a subset of⏎federal sources must manage dozens of incompatible formats, inconsistent publication schedules, and⏎no reliable mechanism for detecting when a source has been updated.⏎HHS should require all agencies publishing healthcare compliance requirements to adopt a machine-⏎readable, standardized format for regulatory notifications — analogous to what HL7 FHIR has⏎accomplished for clinical data. This standard should include mandatory metadata fields: effective date,⏎affected organization types, applicable CFR citations, superseded guidance references, and a unique⏎identifier for each regulatory change event.⏎Relevant CFR: 45 CFR Part 170 (ONC Health IT Certification) — ONC should extend certification⏎criteria to include standardized regulatory data publication APIs. Additionally, ONC should⏎commission a regulatory data FHIR Implementation Guide, establishing a FHIR-compatible⏎specification for publishing healthcare regulatory requirements through existing health IT⏎infrastructure.⏎C. Create consistent Medicare reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical services⏎CMS should establish durable, technology-neutral reimbursement codes for AI-assisted clinical⏎decision support, rather than relying on coverage decisions that vary by contractor and are subject to⏎frequent revision. The current approach creates the reimbursement uncertainty that suppresses private⏎sector AI investment — particularly for companies serving smaller providers and rural health systems,⏎where the cost of regulatory compliance and the uncertainty of reimbursement combine to make the⏎investment calculus prohibitive.⏎Reimbursement pathways must be durable. Coverage decisions that can be reversed by a single⏎contractor determination create the kind of investment uncertainty that drives AI developers toward⏎larger, better-resourced markets and away from the rural and safety-net settings where AI could have⏎the greatest equity impact.⏎Relevant CFR: 42 CFR Part 414 (Medicare Part B Payment) — CMS should issue rulemaking⏎establishing a consistent, technology-neutral payment framework for AI-assisted clinical services with⏎clear criteria for what constitutes a reimbursable AI-assisted service under § 414.1 et seq. Contractor⏎discretion in coverage determination for AI-assisted services should be constrained by national⏎coverage policy.⏎D. Clarify OIG safe harbor treatment for AI tool investments⏎The Anti-Kickback Statute (42 U.S.C. § 1320a-7b(b)) and associated OIG safe harbors create⏎ambiguity around whether health system investments in AI tools — particularly when provided to⏎referring providers — constitute prohibited remuneration. This ambiguity has a chilling effect on the⏎formation of health system and AI developer partnerships that are necessary for AI tools to reach⏎smaller providers at scale.⏎⏎Relevant CFR: 42 CFR § 1001.952 (OIG Safe Harbors) — OIG should add an explicit AI technology⏎safe harbor modeled on the existing Electronic Health Records safe harbor at § 1001.952(y), covering⏎the provision of AI clinical decision support tools to referring providers under defined conditions⏎including fair market value cost-sharing and non-exclusivity requirements.⏎E. Expand LEAP in Health IT to include regulatory intelligence infrastructure⏎ONC's Leading Edge Acceleration Projects program has historically funded innovative health IT⏎solutions at the frontier of interoperability. NOETED respectfully and specifically requests that ONC⏎include regulatory intelligence infrastructure — systems that help healthcare organizations continuously⏎monitor and respond to compliance requirements across federal, state, and local regulatory sources —⏎as an eligible and priority area of interest in future LEAP funding cycles.⏎This is not a peripheral use case. Regulatory intelligence infrastructure is a prerequisite for trustworthy⏎AI deployment in healthcare. An AI clinical decision support tool that operates in a compliance⏎environment the deploying organization cannot monitor is not a safe tool. Funding the infrastructure⏎layer that makes compliance monitoring possible is as foundational as funding the clinical AI tools that⏎depend on it.⏎Relevant program: ONC LEAP (NAP-AX-22-001) — no CFR citation required. This is a programmatic⏎recommendation for ONC's discretionary funding priorities under the existing LEAP cooperative⏎agreement framework.⏎F. Revise HIPAA guidance to clarify AI training data use⏎The current HIPAA Privacy Rule creates uncertainty around the permissible use of protected health⏎information for AI model training, even when data is de-identified under Safe Harbor or Expert⏎Determination methods. This uncertainty is compounded by the regulatory data access barriers⏎NOETED has encountered directly: organizations that want to build AI systems using de-identified⏎regulatory and compliance data face not only HIPAA ambiguity but also the practical inaccessibility of⏎the underlying datasets described in Recommendation A.⏎HHS should issue updated guidance clarifying the conditions under which de-identified healthcare data⏎may be used for AI training without individual authorization, and should explicitly address synthetic data⏎generation as an alternative to de-identified PHI for AI training — an increasingly viable approach that⏎current HIPAA guidance does not clearly address.⏎Relevant CFR: 45 CFR § 164.514 (De-identification of PHI) — HHS should issue updated guidance⏎under § 164.514 specifically addressing AI training data use, including standards for de-identification⏎sufficiency in machine learning contexts and safe harbor treatment for synthetic data generation⏎methods that do not use PHI as direct training input.⏎⏎Q3 For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist regarding AI⏎in clinical care (liability, indemnification, privacy, security)? What role should HHS play?⏎NOETED Response⏎Liability allocation in AI-assisted clinical decisions.⏎When an AI tool contributes to a clinical decision that results in patient harm, existing tort law does not⏎clearly allocate liability among the AI developer, the healthcare organization that deployed the tool, the⏎clinician who relied on it, and the data providers whose data trained it. This multi-party liability problem⏎is novel and existing medical malpractice frameworks were not designed to address it. HHS should⏎work with the Department of Justice and state attorneys general to develop a model liability framework⏎that clarifies roles and responsibilities across the AI deployment chain, provides safe harbors for⏎organizations that follow defined best practices, and creates a consistent national standard rather than⏎allowing 50 divergent state-law approaches to develop simultaneously.⏎Regulatory compliance liability for AI-generated recommendations.⏎NOETED's direct experience reveals a specific and underappreciated liability issue: when AI systems⏎generate compliance recommendations that prove incorrect, the question of who bears liability for the⏎resulting regulatory violation is unresolved. A healthcare organization that relies on an AI compliance⏎tool and subsequently fails a CMS audit cannot currently point to the AI tool's error as a defense —⏎they remain liable for the underlying compliance failure regardless of the tool's role. HHS should issue⏎guidance clarifying how AI-assisted compliance errors are treated in enforcement contexts, and⏎whether demonstrated reliance on a certified AI compliance tool affects penalty calculation under 42⏎CFR Part 1003 (OIG Civil Monetary Penalties).⏎Source data liability — a novel issue unique to regulatory AI.⏎A third liability issue that existing legal frameworks do not address is source data liability for AI⏎compliance tools. NOETED has documented firsthand that federal and state agencies publish⏎regulatory data through antiquated, unreliable infrastructure — broken APIs, deprecated endpoints,⏎inconsistently updated datasets. When an AI compliance tool fails because the underlying regulatory⏎data published by a federal agency was incorrect, outdated, or inaccessible, the liability chain becomes⏎deeply unclear. The healthcare organization relied on the AI tool; the AI tool relied on the agency-⏎published data; the agency published data through infrastructure it has not maintained. No existing⏎legal framework allocates liability across this chain.⏎This is not a hypothetical. An AI compliance tool trained on CMS data that has not been updated to⏎reflect a regulatory change will generate recommendations that are technically wrong — and a⏎healthcare organization following those recommendations may fail a CMS audit for a violation it had no⏎practical means of anticipating. HHS should address this gap directly: agencies that publish regulatory⏎data through broken or unreliable infrastructure bear some measure of responsibility for downstream⏎compliance failures attributable to that infrastructure failure. HHS should issue guidance clarifying the⏎government's obligations regarding the reliability and currency of publicly published regulatory data,⏎and the extent to which reliance on official government data sources affects enforcement discretion in⏎CMS and OIG proceedings.⏎Privacy implications of AI systems that process unstructured clinical data.⏎AI systems operating in clinical environments frequently process unstructured data — clinical notes,⏎voice recordings, imaging reports — that contains PHI in forms not anticipated by existing HIPAA⏎⏎guidance. The intersection of large language model processing with HIPAA's minimum necessary⏎standard (45 CFR § 164.502(b)) creates compliance questions that current guidance does not resolve.⏎HHS should issue a specific guidance document addressing AI processing of unstructured clinical data,⏎including requirements for data minimization, retention, and audit logging in AI systems handling PHI.⏎Recommended HHS role.⏎HHS should convene a multi-stakeholder working group — including AI developers, healthcare⏎organizations, legal scholars, and patient advocates — to develop model governance frameworks for AI⏎in clinical care. These frameworks should address liability allocation across the full AI deployment chain⏎including source data publishers, indemnification standards, privacy safeguards for AI processing, and⏎security requirements for AI systems with access to PHI. ONC's existing Health IT certification program⏎could serve as the vehicle for implementing minimum governance standards for AI tools used in⏎certified EHR contexts.⏎⏎Q4 For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS support these? If so, which mechanisms?⏎NOETED Response⏎Pre-deployment evaluation.⏎The most promising pre-deployment evaluation frameworks for clinical AI share three characteristics:⏎they test performance across demographically diverse subpopulations rather than aggregate⏎populations only; they include adversarial robustness testing against distribution shift (i.e., how the⏎model performs when real-world data differs from training data); and they incorporate structured clinical⏎expert review of model outputs before deployment, not just statistical performance metrics. NOETED⏎recommends that HHS support the development of standardized pre-deployment evaluation protocols⏎specific to healthcare AI use cases, including compliance decision support, clinical documentation, and⏎diagnostic assistance.⏎A critical and currently unaddressed dimension of pre-deployment evaluation for compliance AI is⏎source data quality assessment. Evaluation frameworks assume that training and validation data⏎accurately represents the ground truth. For clinical AI operating in well-defined domains this⏎assumption is reasonable. For AI compliance tools, it is not — because the ground truth is regulatory⏎data published by federal and state agencies through infrastructure that NOETED has documented to⏎be antiquated, inconsistent, and unreliable. HHS should require that pre-deployment evaluation of AI⏎compliance tools include explicit assessment of the quality, currency, and completeness of the⏎regulatory data sources the tool depends on. An AI compliance tool cannot be evaluated independently⏎of the data infrastructure it relies on.⏎Post-deployment monitoring.⏎Post-deployment monitoring is where current practice is weakest. Most healthcare AI deployments lack⏎systematic mechanisms for detecting model drift, bias emergence, or performance degradation over⏎time. HHS should require post-deployment monitoring plans as a condition of any federal funding for AI⏎deployment, and should support the development of automated monitoring tools that can detect⏎performance changes without requiring manual review of every AI output. For AI compliance tools⏎specifically, post-deployment monitoring must include tracking of regulatory source data currency — a⏎tool that was accurate at deployment may become inaccurate when the underlying regulatory data⏎changes and the tool is not updated.⏎Human-centered evaluation.⏎Evaluation methods that measure only technical performance miss the question of whether AI tools⏎actually improve clinical workflows and outcomes. HHS should prioritize funding for human-centered⏎evaluation research — studies that measure how clinicians actually interact with AI tools, where AI⏎recommendations are accepted or overridden and why, and what the downstream patient outcome⏎effects are. This research is currently sparse and is essential for understanding whether AI tools that⏎perform well in technical evaluations translate to real-world clinical benefit.⏎Recommended HHS mechanisms.⏎NOETED recommends cooperative agreements as the primary mechanism, given their flexibility and⏎ability to support multi-site, multi-stakeholder evaluation projects. Prize competitions are appropriate for⏎well-defined technical challenges such as best-performing model on a specific benchmark dataset.⏎⏎Grants are appropriate for foundational research on evaluation methodology. HHS should also consider⏎creating a publicly accessible Healthcare AI Evaluation Repository — a centralized database of⏎evaluation results for deployed clinical AI tools — that would allow healthcare organizations to make⏎informed procurement decisions.⏎⏎Q5 How can HHS best support private sector activities (accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎NOETED Response⏎Extend ONC Health IT Certification to AI compliance tools.⏎ONC's existing Health IT Certification Program (45 CFR Part 170) provides a proven framework for⏎establishing minimum standards for health IT tools. HHS should extend this framework to include⏎certification criteria for AI tools used in clinical care — particularly AI compliance and decision support⏎tools that are not classified as medical devices but that directly influence clinical operations.⏎Certification should require demonstrated performance across diverse patient populations, documented⏎bias testing, interoperability with certified EHRs, and ongoing post-deployment monitoring.⏎Support industry-driven credentialing with federal recognition.⏎Industry consortia — including HIMSS, AHIMA, and emerging AI-specific organizations — are⏎developing voluntary credentialing frameworks for healthcare AI. HHS should establish a formal⏎recognition process for these industry-driven frameworks, similar to how NCQA accreditation is⏎recognized in Medicare and Medicaid contracting. Federal recognition creates market incentives for⏎voluntary credentialing adoption without requiring prescriptive federal rulemaking.⏎Create a safe harbor for certified AI tools in enforcement contexts.⏎HHS should establish that healthcare organizations using AI tools that have achieved ONC certification⏎or HHS-recognized industry credentialing receive defined safe harbor treatment in CMS and OIG⏎enforcement proceedings arising from AI-assisted compliance errors. This directly addresses the⏎liability concern identified in Q3 and creates a powerful market incentive for both AI developers to seek⏎certification and healthcare organizations to use certified tools.⏎Regulatory intelligence as a certification prerequisite — contingent on infrastructure⏎modernization.⏎NOETED specifically recommends that ONC require certified AI tools to demonstrate integration with a⏎standardized regulatory notification system as a certification prerequisite. An AI clinical decision support⏎tool that cannot monitor its own compliance environment — one that cannot automatically update its⏎logic when the regulations governing it change — is not a trustworthy tool. Regulatory intelligence⏎integration should be a certification criterion, not an optional feature.⏎This recommendation is directly dependent on Recommendation A in Q2: the certification prerequisite⏎is only implementable if the federal regulatory data infrastructure exists and is reliable. ONC cannot⏎require AI tools to integrate with a regulatory notification system that does not yet exist in machine-⏎readable form. HHS must therefore treat infrastructure modernization and certification criteria⏎development as a sequenced initiative — the infrastructure must be built before the certification⏎requirement can be enforced. The two recommendations are linked and must be pursued together.⏎⏎Q6 Where have AI tools in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations, and where have they fallen short? What novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, quality insights, and cost⏎reduction?⏎NOETED Response⏎Where AI has met or exceeded expectations.⏎The strongest evidence of AI performance meeting clinical expectations is in ambient clinical⏎documentation — AI-assisted transcription and documentation tools that reduce physician⏎administrative burden. These tools have achieved high adoption rates in some health systems because⏎they address a clear, painful, well-defined problem without requiring AI to make clinical judgments.⏎Similarly, AI-assisted imaging analysis (radiology, pathology) has shown strong performance in narrow,⏎well-defined classification tasks where ground truth is clear and training data is abundant.⏎Where AI has fallen short.⏎AI tools have consistently underperformed in contexts requiring generalization across diverse patient⏎populations, particularly when training data underrepresents minority and rural populations. AI tools⏎have also fallen short in dynamic regulatory environments — and NOETED's direct experience building⏎in this space reveals two distinct failure modes that are frequently conflated but require separate⏎solutions.⏎The first failure mode is update lag: tools trained on historical compliance standards fail when⏎regulations change and the tools are not updated to reflect new requirements. This is a development⏎and maintenance problem that better engineering practices can address.⏎The second failure mode is infrastructure failure: tools fail not because their developers failed to update⏎them, but because the underlying regulatory data infrastructure they depend on — the government-⏎published APIs, databases, and notification systems — is itself broken, inconsistent, or inaccessible. A⏎compliance AI tool cannot be current if the data source it monitors is not current. This is not a problem⏎AI developers can solve unilaterally. It requires the federal regulatory data infrastructure modernization⏎described in Q2 Recommendation A. Until that infrastructure exists, AI compliance tools will continue to⏎fail in the field not due to any deficiency in the AI itself, but due to deficiencies in the government data⏎systems feeding it.⏎Novel tools with the greatest potential.⏎NOETED identifies the following as highest-potential areas for novel AI tools in healthcare:⏎• Regulatory intelligence and compliance monitoring — AI that continuously tracks regulatory changes⏎and maps their implications across the compliance obligations of a specific healthcare organization.⏎This is infrastructure-level AI that enables every other clinical AI tool to operate safely. Its⏎effectiveness depends directly on the quality of the regulatory data infrastructure described⏎throughout this comment.⏎• Predictive compliance risk scoring — AI that identifies healthcare organizations at elevated risk of⏎compliance failure before enforcement actions occur, enabling proactive intervention.⏎• AI-assisted prior authorization — automating the clinical criteria review process that currently⏎consumes enormous administrative resources and creates delays in patient care.⏎⏎• Population health compliance monitoring — AI that identifies compliance gaps in care delivery for⏎specific patient populations, particularly for value-based care quality measures.⏎• Rural and FQHC-specific clinical AI — tools designed for the resource constraints and patient⏎populations of rural and community health settings, where AI deployment has lagged due to⏎infrastructure and cost barriers.⏎⏎Q7 Which roles, decision makers, or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on AI adoption? What are the primary administrative hurdles?⏎NOETED Response⏎Most influential decision makers for AI adoption.⏎Based on NOETED's engagement with healthcare organizations, the decision makers with the greatest⏎influence on AI adoption decisions are, in order of impact:⏎1. Chief Compliance Officers (CCOs) and Chief Legal Officers (CLOs) — who must approve any⏎AI tool that touches compliance-sensitive workflows. CCOs are the gatekeepers for healthcare⏎AI because almost all clinical AI tools have compliance implications. Their concerns center on⏎regulatory risk, liability, and auditability.⏎2. Chief Information Officers (CIOs) and Chief Medical Information Officers (CMIOs) — who⏎evaluate technical integration, data security, and EHR compatibility. Tools that cannot integrate⏎with existing EHR infrastructure typically fail at this stage regardless of clinical merit.⏎3. Chief Financial Officers (CFOs) — who evaluate reimbursement viability and ROI. The absence⏎of consistent reimbursement pathways identified in Q2 means CFOs frequently cannot build a⏎viable business case for AI tool investment.⏎4. Clinical champions — individual physicians or department heads who advocate for specific AI⏎tools based on clinical experience. Without a clinical champion, AI tools typically cannot⏎advance past pilot stage.⏎Primary administrative hurdles.⏎The administrative hurdles NOETED has observed most frequently are:⏎• Compliance review paralysis — the compliance review process for new AI tools has no standardized⏎framework, leading to ad hoc, organization-specific reviews that can take 6–18 months and frequently⏎result in tools being rejected due to unresolved regulatory ambiguity rather than actual compliance⏎failure. This problem is compounded when the AI tool in question is a compliance tool itself: CCOs⏎reviewing an AI compliance tool cannot meaningfully assess the tool's reliability when the underlying⏎regulatory data the tool depends on is itself unreliable or inaccessible — a direct consequence of the⏎infrastructure failure described in Q1 and Q2.⏎• EHR vendor lock-in — AI tools that are not part of the incumbent EHR vendor's ecosystem face⏎significant integration barriers, including API restrictions, data access limitations, and EHR vendor⏎contract provisions that restrict third-party integrations.⏎• Procurement complexity — healthcare organizations subject to federal contracting requirements face⏎procurement processes designed for physical goods, not software-as-a-service AI tools. These⏎processes create delays and documentation burdens that disproportionately burden smaller AI⏎developers.⏎• Staff training and change management — even technically successful AI deployments frequently fail⏎in practice due to inadequate training, workflow integration, and change management support. AI⏎developers who are not resourced to provide this support see adoption collapse after initial⏎deployment.⏎⏎Q8 Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate AI development for clinical care? Consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎NOETED Response⏎The regulatory data gap — NOETED's firsthand account.⏎The healthcare interoperability conversation has focused almost exclusively on clinical data (patient⏎records, lab results, imaging) and administrative data (claims, prior authorizations). NOETED has⏎identified a third critical data domain that is almost entirely absent from interoperability frameworks and⏎that we have experienced as the primary technical obstacle to building reliable healthcare AI: regulatory⏎data — the structured, machine-readable publication of compliance requirements, regulatory changes,⏎and enforcement guidance.⏎In building NOETED's compliance intelligence platform, we have attempted to programmatically access⏎regulatory data from CMS, OIG, state health departments, and other agencies. What we found is a⏎landscape of broken APIs with no documented maintenance schedules, datasets that had not been⏎updated to reflect regulatory changes that occurred months prior, access requirement processes with⏎no defined timelines, inconsistent data schemas across agencies publishing information about the⏎same regulatory domain, and in several cases, APIs that were listed in official documentation but⏎returned errors or no data when called. This is the regulatory data infrastructure that every healthcare⏎AI compliance tool must navigate. It is not navigable at scale without manual intervention — which⏎defeats the purpose of AI automation entirely.⏎Enhanced interoperability in the regulatory data domain would have a greater near-term impact on⏎healthcare AI development than equivalent investment in clinical data interoperability, because the⏎clinical data infrastructure — while imperfect — is dramatically more mature. The regulatory data⏎infrastructure barely exists.⏎Specific data types where enhanced interoperability would have the greatest impact.⏎• Regulatory change notifications — standardized, machine-readable publication of all CMS, OIG,⏎FDA, and ONC regulatory changes, with metadata indicating affected organization types, effective⏎dates, and applicable CFR citations.⏎• Compliance program guidance — OIG's compliance program guidance documents, currently⏎published as static PDFs, should be converted to structured, queryable formats with version history⏎and change tracking.⏎• Enforcement action data — CMS and OIG enforcement actions, currently scattered across multiple⏎databases, should be consolidated into a single, structured, queryable dataset that AI systems can⏎use for compliance risk modeling.⏎• Quality measure specifications — CMS quality measure specifications, which change annually,⏎should be published in machine-readable formats that AI clinical decision support tools can ingest⏎without manual programming updates.⏎Recommended standards and benchmarking tools.⏎HHS should direct ONC to develop a regulatory data FHIR Implementation Guide — a standardized⏎specification for publishing healthcare regulatory requirements in FHIR-compatible formats. This would⏎allow existing health IT infrastructure to serve as the distribution channel for regulatory data without⏎⏎requiring new proprietary systems. HHS should also fund the development of a Healthcare Regulatory⏎Compliance Benchmark dataset — a publicly available, de-identified dataset of compliance scenarios⏎and outcomes that AI compliance tools can use for evaluation and validation, addressing the evaluation⏎gap identified in Q4.⏎⏎Q9 What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI in clinical⏎care? What concerns do they have?⏎NOETED Response⏎Patient challenges AI can address.⏎NOETED's compliance intelligence focus gives us a specific perspective on patient-facing AI: the⏎compliance failures that result from inadequate regulatory intelligence are not abstract organizational⏎problems — they directly harm patients. When a healthcare organization fails a CMS compliance audit⏎and loses a contract, patients lose access to care providers. When a health plan fails state regulatory⏎requirements and faces sanctions, patients' coverage is disrupted. When an AI compliance tool fails⏎because the federal regulatory data infrastructure it depends on is broken or outdated — as NOETED⏎has documented firsthand — the downstream consequence is that healthcare organizations operate⏎with compliance gaps they cannot see, and patients bear the cost of the resulting enforcement actions.⏎The compliance infrastructure problem is a patient access problem.⏎From a patient perspective, the healthcare challenges most amenable to AI assistance include:⏎• Prior authorization delays — patients and caregivers consistently identify prior authorization as a⏎significant barrier to timely care. AI tools that can process prior authorization requests faster and⏎more consistently would have direct, measurable patient benefit.⏎• Care coordination across fragmented providers — AI tools that can synthesize patient records across⏎multiple providers and identify care gaps would address one of the most common patient complaints⏎about the healthcare system.⏎• Health literacy and patient education — AI-powered tools that can translate clinical information into⏎plain language appropriate to a patient's health literacy level and preferred language would improve⏎patient engagement and treatment adherence.⏎• Benefits navigation — patients, particularly those on Medicaid and Medicare, struggle to understand⏎what their coverage includes and how to access it. AI tools that can explain benefits, identify covered⏎services, and navigate prior authorization requirements would reduce a significant administrative⏎burden on patients and caregivers.⏎Patient concerns about AI.⏎The patient concerns about AI that NOETED believes HHS should most directly address are:⏎• Privacy of health information used to train AI — patients want to know whether their health records⏎are being used to train AI systems, who has authorized that use, and how they can opt out. HHS⏎should require clear, plain-language disclosure of AI training data practices as a condition of any⏎federally funded AI deployment.⏎• Accountability when AI contributes to a wrong decision — patients want to know who is responsible⏎when an AI tool contributes to a misdiagnosis or inappropriate treatment recommendation. The⏎liability ambiguity identified in Q3 — including the novel source data liability question — is not only a⏎problem for healthcare organizations. It is a patient safety and patient rights issue.⏎• Bias and equity — patients from minority communities and rural areas have legitimate concerns that⏎AI tools trained primarily on data from urban, majority-population health systems will perform less⏎accurately for them. HHS must require demographic performance disaggregation as a standard⏎evaluation requirement.⏎⏎Q10 What areas of AI research should HHS prioritize to accelerate AI adoption in clinical⏎care? Are there published findings on AI tool impact and costs/benefits in clinical⏎care?⏎NOETED Response⏎AI research priorities HHS should fund.⏎NOETED recommends HHS prioritize the following research areas, in order of near-term impact:⏎1. Regulatory data infrastructure architecture — HHS should fund foundational research on the⏎design and implementation of standardized regulatory data publication systems for healthcare,⏎including data models, API specifications, and governance frameworks for maintaining⏎regulatory data currency. This research does not exist in the published literature at any⏎meaningful scale and is the prerequisite for every other priority on this list.⏎2. Regulatory intelligence AI systems — HHS should fund foundational research on the⏎architecture of centralized regulatory intelligence systems for healthcare, including machine⏎learning approaches to regulatory change detection and NLP methods for extracting structured⏎compliance requirements from unstructured regulatory documents.⏎3. AI evaluation methodology for compliance-sensitive applications — research on how to evaluate⏎AI tools that operate in compliance-sensitive clinical contexts, where the ground truth is a⏎regulatory requirement rather than a clinical outcome, and where source data quality is itself a⏎variable that must be assessed.⏎4. Demographic performance disaggregation methods — research on statistical methods for⏎identifying and quantifying performance disparities in clinical AI across race, ethnicity, language,⏎geography, and socioeconomic status. Aggregate performance metrics mask disparities that are⏎clinically and legally significant.⏎5. Human-AI collaboration in clinical workflows — how clinicians actually use AI recommendations⏎in practice, including when and why they override AI recommendations, and what the⏎downstream outcome effects are.⏎6. AI adoption economics in resource-constrained settings — research specifically addressing the⏎cost-benefit calculus for AI adoption in rural hospitals, FQHCs, and smaller health plans, where⏎the economics differ significantly from large urban health systems.⏎10a. Published findings on AI tool impact.⏎The published literature on clinical AI impact shows strong performance in specific, narrow applications⏎— ambient documentation (with studies showing meaningful reductions in physician administrative⏎burden, with some health systems reporting time savings of 25% or more across documentation and⏎administrative tasks), imaging AI for specific diagnostic tasks (radiology and pathology showing⏎sensitivity and specificity competitive with specialists in controlled settings), and sepsis prediction⏎models (with some studies suggesting potential mortality reduction in combination with effective clinical⏎response protocols, though evidence remains mixed). The literature is substantially weaker on real-⏎world deployment outcomes as opposed to controlled evaluation performance, on performance in⏎diverse or underserved populations, and on compliance-adjacent AI applications. The near-complete⏎absence of published research on AI regulatory intelligence systems reflects the infrastructure gap⏎NOETED has identified throughout this comment — you cannot publish research on AI systems that⏎depend on data infrastructure that does not yet exist in reliable form.⏎⏎10b. Costs, benefits, and transfers in the literature.⏎The health economics literature on clinical AI is nascent and methodologically inconsistent. Studies⏎vary significantly in how they measure costs (development, deployment, training, ongoing monitoring)⏎and benefits (outcome improvements, administrative efficiency, error reduction). The most consistent⏎finding is that AI tools with narrow, well-defined applications in high-volume workflows show the⏎clearest cost-benefit profiles, while broad-scope AI tools with diffuse benefit claims are harder to⏎evaluate economically.⏎A critical gap in the literature is the cost of non-adoption: the economic burden of compliance failures,⏎administrative inefficiency, and avoidable clinical errors that result from the absence of reliable AI⏎compliance tools. HHS should fund research that quantifies this counterfactual cost. The scale of the⏎problem is not in dispute: HHS-OIG's own semiannual reports document over $7 billion annually in⏎expected recoveries from healthcare fraud and abuse investigations, and CMS improper payments in⏎Medicare alone exceeded $19 billion in FY2024. NOETED's market analysis indicates that a⏎meaningful portion of these failures are attributable to regulatory intelligence gaps — failures caused in⏎part by the broken data infrastructure this comment describes — that better compliance monitoring⏎infrastructure could have prevented. Quantifying that specific causal share would fundamentally change⏎the investment calculus for federal regulatory data infrastructure modernization: it would no longer⏎appear as a discretionary technology investment but as a cost-avoidance measure with a calculable⏎return.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎NOETED appreciates the opportunity to contribute to HHS's thinking on AI in clinical care. Our⏎responses across these ten questions return consistently to a single foundational theme: the regulatory⏎data infrastructure problem.⏎Healthcare AI cannot scale responsibly until healthcare organizations can reliably know what⏎compliance requires of them — in real time, across all applicable federal, state, and local regulatory⏎sources. That knowledge depends on infrastructure: on APIs that work, on datasets that are current, on⏎publication systems that are standardized and maintained. As NOETED has documented through direct⏎experience, that infrastructure does not currently exist in the U.S. healthcare system in any reliable⏎form. The consequences are not abstract — they flow directly to patients who lose access to care when⏎compliance failures result in enforcement actions that could have been avoided.⏎The sequence of action we recommend is clear: modernize the federal regulatory data publication⏎infrastructure first; establish standardized data formats second; build the certification and safe harbor⏎frameworks that depend on that infrastructure third; and fund the AI research that requires reliable⏎regulatory data to be meaningful fourth. These are not parallel tracks — they are a sequenced⏎infrastructure investment that HHS is uniquely positioned to lead.⏎NOETED is building the private sector solution to this problem. We are an early-stage company, but we⏎are building at the right moment and in the right direction. We actively seek partnership with ONC,⏎HHS, and aligned academic and non-profit institutions to pilot NOETED's regulatory intelligence⏎platform in real healthcare settings and to contribute to the development of the regulatory data⏎standards the field needs.⏎We welcome direct engagement with ONC and are available to discuss any of the recommendations in⏎this comment in greater detail.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Keith “Duv” Williams⏎Founder, NOETED⏎noeted.ai⏎duv@noeted.com⏎NOETED | Every Change. Every Connection. Noeted. | noeted.ai♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0375♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 1⏎Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute⏎Response to Health and Human Services Department Request for⏎Information (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: RFI Response – Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎The Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute (Meadows Institute) appreciates the opportunity⏎to provide feedback on the use of artificial intelligence (AI) in behavioral health clinical care.⏎The Meadows Institute recognizes the potential for AI to improve the quality, reach, and⏎precision of behavioral health care, address severe workforce shortages, and help more people⏎obtain the care they need. We are committed to ensuring the behavioral health field is not left⏎out of health tech innovation, as has occurred in the past. We aim to leverage the positive⏎benefits of AI while promoting the development and implementation of AI that is safe,⏎accurate, and effective so that people with behavioral health conditions receive the care they⏎need.⏎Independent and nonpartisan, the Meadows Institute works at the intersection of policy and⏎practice to create equitable systemic change so that all people in Texas, the nation, and the⏎world can obtain the health care they need. The Meadows Institute provides state and national⏎leaders with data-driven, trusted policy and program guidance, and it works to shift the focus of⏎new investments toward early intervention for children and families, address the behavioral⏎health crisis in our jails and emergency rooms, and help all people with behavioral health needs⏎recover and be well.⏎Company and product names referenced herein are provided solely for illustrative purposes⏎and do not constitute an endorsement by the Meadows Institute.⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 2⏎Overview: Adopting AI in Behavioral Health Care⏎The Meadows Institute encourages ASTP/ONC to carefully consider behavioral health care as it⏎develops approaches to regulation, reimbursement, and research and development to⏎encourage AI adoption in clinical behavioral health care.⏎Positive Use Cases of AI in Behavioral Health⏎Nearly one in five adults in the United States lives with a mental illness, but only half of them⏎receive behavioral health care.1 Close to 90% of our nation’s health care spending goes toward⏎individuals with chronic and behavioral health conditions.2,3 When used to enhance – rather⏎than replace – human judgment, AI holds immense potential to address long-standing⏎challenges in the delivery of behavioral health care. AI’s potential benefits extend across⏎applications in behavioral health care, including workflow automation, provider training and⏎quality monitoring, populations analytics, research and development, clinician support, patient⏎engagement, and promotion of health between in-person visits. Application of AI in these areas⏎may lead to increased access, improved quality, and reduced costs of behavioral health care.⏎A particularly beneficial potential application of AI in behavioral health care is supporting the⏎evidence-based practice of measurement informed care (MIC). Starting with initial patient⏎screening for behavioral health conditions, MIC involves repeated, systematic use of validated⏎measures during clinical encounters to inform decision-making about treatment, thereby⏎supporting – not replacing – clinical judgment. Providers and patients use measurement to help⏎inform and develop an individualized treatment plan and continuously monitor and adjust⏎treatment.⏎While simple conceptually, MIC can be cumbersome administratively if not optimally integrated⏎into electronic health records (EHRs) and clinical workflows. Even when assessment data is⏎collected and stored in an EHR, the data may not always be accessible in an easy-to-use format.⏎This is particularly true for behavioral health data, as behavioral health has often seen⏎underfunding and under-inclusion in EHR systems. These obstacles have served as a major⏎impediment to large-scale adoption of MIC for behavioral health. Thus, despite the⏎demonstrated benefits of MIC in behavioral health care, fewer than 20% of behavioral health⏎providers use it.4,5,6 AI, and investments in technology more broadly, can help overcome many⏎of the persistent barriers to clinician uptake of MIC by:7⏎• Reducing administrative burden: through automation of the collection and scoring of⏎validated assessments, functional scores, and other data sources in real-time.⏎• Easing Use by Clinicians: by allowing for the visualization of data longitudinally and the⏎detection of patterns, allowing clinicians to more easily identify trends and track progress,⏎plateaus, or deterioration.⏎• Expanding data sources: by enabling the utilization of information from a broader array of⏎sources, such as wearables and remote monitoring tools (e.g., sleep, activity), clinical⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 3⏎notes, voice analysis, and/or facial analysis, to provide objective measures and additional⏎insights.⏎• Improving treatment personalization: by identifying key issues such as nonresponse to⏎treatment or deterioration in functioning and allowing providers to adjust care plans⏎earlier and more effectively.8⏎How data are measured, assessed, and used in behavioral health contexts is constantly⏎evolving. AI holds the promise of being the next step in the evolution in measurement and⏎monitoring of behavioral health care. Behavioral health conditions often have overlapping⏎symptoms, and assessment and measurement rely heavily on subjective methods, which can, at⏎times, make assessment and monitoring a challenge.⏎Further, behavioral health often lacks the objective biometrics found in other areas of health,⏎such as A1C or cholesterol tests. While research is still needed to understand their appropriate⏎application and generalizability, there is an emerging body of evidence to support the use of⏎data that can be obtained from smartphones, wearables, and telehealth recordings, such as⏎mobility, location, phone use, call log, or heart rate, (i.e., “digital phenotyping”) to provide⏎more objective measures for behavioral health assessment. A recent systematic review found⏎that physiological and behavioral data collected through digital phenotyping methods can be⏎effectively used to detect and predict changes in symptoms of patients with behavioral health⏎conditions, allowing for earlier intervention, in some cases even before an adverse event⏎occurs.9 For example, the systematic review showed digital phenotyping methods were used to⏎predict and detect relapse in schizophrenia to aid in reducing hospitalization, cost of care, and⏎negative life impacts.10⏎Regulation⏎The accelerated adoption of AI in behavioral health care requires a careful balance between⏎developing standards and safeguards to ensure transparency, trust, and standardization, while⏎avoiding cumbersome regulations that stifle innovation. The Meadows Institute appreciates⏎HHS’s efforts to minimize unnecessary regulatory burdens that drive up costs and hinder⏎patient care. We also recognize the high degree of trust the public demands from behavioral⏎health providers and the tools they use. To achieve this balance, we encourage HHS to focus its⏎efforts on 1) facilitating the interoperability of behavioral health data, 2) strengthening privacy⏎and data security, and (3) fostering transparency and trust in the use of AI in behavioral health⏎care.⏎Interoperability: Interoperability and data standardization remain a challenge across the health⏎care system, but the challenge is especially great for behavioral health. Behavioral health was⏎largely left out of the Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health (HITECH)⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 4⏎Act, which incentivized the rest of the health care system to build out its IT infrastructure over⏎the last few decades. Consequently, behavioral health IT infrastructure and the funding needed⏎to support it lag significantly behind physical health.⏎In addition to a lack of investment in EHR infrastructure, behavioral health lacks data⏎standardization and terminology that is needed to support the exchange of health care data.⏎This has exacerbated a cycle that hinders the behavioral health industry from leveraging and⏎incorporating advances in the broader health IT ecosystem.⏎The threat of behavioral health missing out on another cycle of technological innovation is a⏎very real and present danger. And as the pace of innovation increases with the advent of new⏎AI-based tools, behavioral health may face the reality that it can never catch up again. Effective⏎access to EHRs and to standardized, interoperable data are fundamental to ensuring that health⏎systems and behavioral health providers can leverage AI innovation appropriately.⏎To ensure these goals are met, the Meadows Institute encourages ASTP/ONC to:⏎• Strongly support opportunities to incentivize the purchase and adoption of EHRs in⏎behavioral health.⏎• Facilitate the inclusion of behavioral health data into existing EHR infrastructure to⏎ensure integration of behavioral health data across health care settings.⏎• Expand the work of USCDI+BH and invest in pilot programs, such as the Behavioral⏎Health Information Technology (BHIT) Pilot Program, which is working to develop⏎standardized data components to be captured in the EHR that are unique and distinct to⏎behavioral health.⏎• Work with Congress to identify pathways to greater adoption of EHRs in behavioral⏎health. In June 2025, Congress passed H.R. 2483, the SUPPORT for Patients and⏎Communities Reauthorization Act of 2025, with an amendment directing ONC to⏎convene a roundtable on how best to increase adoption of EHRs in behavioral health⏎and substance use services. HHS should utilize this as a turning point for behavioral⏎health and do everything in its power to support the interoperability of behavioral⏎health data to enable integration. We may not have another, better opportunity in the⏎foreseeable future.⏎Privacy and data security: The privacy and data security of behavioral health data is of the⏎utmost importance. But we cannot allow behavioral health to be overly “protected” to such an⏎extent that innovation and investment become impossible. Heightened concerns about privacy⏎cannot be used to justify excluding behavioral health from advancements, as we saw with the⏎HITECH Act. Instead, regulations and standards must be in place to protect privacy and data⏎security.⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 5⏎For instance, the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) definition of⏎“covered entities” does not adequately cover the range of new digital health and AI tools.11⏎HHS should address this gap to ensure patient-facing tools and other AI applications that may⏎connect to EHRs have the necessary privacy and cybersecurity requirements. Years of hard⏎work to recover since the failure of the HITECH Act show us these goals need not be in conflict.⏎The Meadows Institute looks forward to engaging with HHS and ASTP/ONC to address⏎commonsense solutions that protect consumer privacy while avoiding the unnecessary⏎obstacles to innovation and investment.⏎Trust and transparency: One of the biggest barriers to uptake of AI tools in behavioral health is⏎provider and patient lack of trust in the safety and accuracy of AI tools.12,13 To foster trust and⏎transparency in AI tools, it is critical for health systems and providers to understand how AI⏎models are developed and tested. This will allow health systems to readily evaluate the aptness⏎and safety of AI tools for their patient population and effectively communicate their decision-⏎making process to stakeholders. This is already a challenging task for many health systems,⏎especially in rural areas where necessary resources and expertise may be lacking. HHS should⏎determine appropriate methods to require and/or incentivize AI companies to provide health⏎systems with the necessary information to most readily make informed decisions about AI⏎products to purchase and implement.14 Additionally, in order to promote patient understanding⏎of when and how AI tools are used, health systems could be required to obtain informed⏎consent.⏎AI Literacy: The continual and rapid pace of AI advancement necessitates a focus on AI literacy⏎to ensure providers have the competence to critically interpret AI-generated measurements.⏎Providers may need to “upskill” and acquire new and different skills in order to provide proper⏎human oversight and interpretation of AI tools.15⏎Reimbursement⏎Technology-Enabled MIC and Value-Based Care⏎As discussed above, MIC is an essential practice for improving clinical care. Systematic⏎measurement is also the foundation for the measurement of quality and fundamental for the⏎incorporation of reimbursement methods based on the clinical outcomes achieved and/or the⏎quality of the care provided (i.e., paying for value). For example, research shows that⏎aggregated MIC data can be effectively used to assess whether a provider or a group of⏎behavioral health providers is achieving a benchmark level of remission in their patients.⏎Because paying for value requires MIC, and AI and health information technology facilitate its⏎implementation, value-based payment models can indirectly incentivize the use of AI in clinical⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 6⏎care. However, it is important that the incentives are sufficient to cover the cost of technology⏎and human work involved in administering a robust MIC practice.⏎Technology-Enabled MIC and Remote Therapeutic Monitoring⏎Remote therapeutic monitoring (RTM) enables the use of new data from wearables and⏎smartphones, such as sleep, physical activity, homework completion between therapy sessions,⏎and medication adherence. These new data sources expand measurement and monitoring⏎opportunities, broadening the potential of MIC in behavioral health care.⏎Currently, Medicare’s RTM codes support payment for monitoring therapy adherence and⏎therapy response of cognitive behavioral therapy. HHS should consider expanding the codes to⏎cover other areas of behavioral health that can more broadly support the practice of MIC.⏎Additionally, the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) and the Center for⏎Medicaid and CHIP Services (CMCS) should encourage the payment of RTM for behavioral⏎health by issuing a bulletin to clarify opportunities to support MIC adoption through the⏎payment of RTM.⏎Medicaid Reimbursement & Early Intervention⏎Interventions work best when administered close to symptom onset and before symptoms⏎reach crisis, making early intervention a central goal of effective and cost-efficient care.16,17 AI⏎has the potential to greatly facilitate this goal by supporting early intervention, screening,⏎diagnosis, and monitoring of behavioral health conditions.⏎The childhood and adolescent period is a critical time for early intervention in behavioral health⏎as nearly 50% of behavioral health conditions manifest by the age of 14.18 Because Medicaid is⏎the single largest payer of behavioral health and insures almost 40% of all children in the U.S., it⏎plays a critical role in the adoption of any new service or technology for children and⏎adolescents. Thus, ensuring Medicaid covers the cost of AI technologies and other digital⏎behavioral health tools is crucial. Medicaid can serve as both the engine and the proving ground⏎for effectively deploying these technologies in support of early interventions, reducing costs⏎and greatly increasing patient outcomes.⏎CMS and CMCS should issue an informational bulletin to clarify existing opportunities to⏎support the adoption of AI and other digital behavioral health technologies. This should include⏎opportunities to support MIC through value-based payment models and RTM codes, as⏎mentioned above.⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 7⏎Research and Development⏎We encourage HHS to increase investment in programs that accelerate the development and⏎adoption of AI-supported health innovation via public-private partnerships. ARPA-H has been⏎one model of cross-sector partnership investment that can yield meaningful health benefits⏎quickly. ARPA-H’s current EVIDENT (Evidence-Based Validation & Innovation for Rapid⏎Therapeutics in Behavioral Health) initiative, for example, has the potential to leverage public-⏎private collaboration to bring precision care to behavioral health, narrowing the gap between⏎advancements in physical and behavioral health.⏎HHS could also replicate other successful federally-led public-private AI demonstrations. In⏎2022, the Department of Veterans Affairs (VA) launched the Mission Daybreak grand challenge⏎to transform suicide prevention through innovation and meet the unique needs of veterans.⏎Initially awarding $20 million to forty pilot demonstrations, Mission Daybreak remains a⏎comprehensive program for accelerating prevention solutions through continuous awards for⏎early-stage research, development and pilots.19⏎RFI Questions⏎Q5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎HHS could create a program to test and validate behavioral health AI systems focusing⏎on accuracy, safety, fairness, and, most importantly, the demonstration of clinically meaningful⏎behavioral health outcomes. HHS can and should demand impact and efficacy in any validation⏎effort. This should include supporting common evaluation frameworks and shared behavioral⏎health outcomes to ensure AI performance and real-world impact can be assessed consistently⏎and routinely across contexts and populations.⏎HHS should also continue to support the development and dissemination of USCDI+BH⏎interoperability standards to facilitate the exchange of behavioral health data via AI apps, EHRs,⏎and other systems. HHS should also address data management standards for behavioral health,⏎focusing on how these data should be safeguarded and protected as it is exchanged and⏎whether current HIPAA standards are adequate to meet these evolving needs.⏎Q6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎As discussed in the sections on regulation and reimbursement above, AI used in support of the⏎implementation of MIC is a high-impact, established use case of AI in behavioral health care.⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 8⏎Additional high-impact use cases include:⏎1. Optimizing crisis response through optimized training and risk prediction⏎Crisis hotlines, text lines, and 24/7 online chats, such as the National Alliance on Mental Illness⏎(NAMI), the National Suicide Prevention Lifeline, and Veterans Crisis Line, are a key component⏎of the crisis response system to prevent suicide.20,21 AI is already helping these critical, high-⏎stakes services provide faster, more effective, and more personalized care.⏎One challenge crisis platforms face, whether call-, chat-, or text-based, is managing the sheer⏎volume of calls. For instance, NAMI experienced a 60% increase in help-seekers between 2019⏎and 2021. Despite efforts to hire and train large numbers of staff, many crisis platforms are still⏎unable to meet demand, and many seeking care go without it. To address this issue,⏎researchers at Stanford University’s Human-Centered Artificial Intelligence (HAI) center⏎developed a machine learning (ML) system based on natural language processing, called Crisis⏎Message Detector 1 (CMD-1), to identify high-risk messages, triage messages, and help those in⏎most imminent need receive support faster. One recent study showed that deployment of⏎CMD-1 reduced daily median wait times for crisis calls, chats, and texts from nine hours to an⏎average of eight to 13 minutes.22⏎Another challenge facing crisis call centers and other crisis platforms is the need for skilled,⏎empathic staff to respond to a wide range of high-stakes scenarios. AI can potentially be⏎deployed to efficiently and effectively train crisis response workers to be ready as soon as they⏎enter the workforce, and to provide continuous quality monitoring of services.23 A high-impact⏎use case is the VA’s partnership with ReflexAI to provide specialized training and practical skills⏎for Veterans Crisis Line responders through simulated training. ReflexAI uses AI to create⏎simulations of veteran personas to create realistic roleplays and give feedback, providing⏎consistency, frequency, and quality not previously possible in training programs.24 Critically,⏎ReflexAI does not replace human care but bolsters it.⏎2. Supporting evidence-based integrated care⏎Evidence-based integrated care models, which treat co-occurring physical and behavioral health⏎needs in the same location, have been proven to dramatically improve outcomes and reduce⏎health care costs. Models such as the psychiatric Collaborative Care Model, which is targeted to⏎serve individuals with mild to moderate needs, enhances early detection, treatment, and⏎relapse prevention.25⏎Unfortunately, implementation of integrated care is relatively low given its proven⏎effectiveness, often owing to administrative challenges.26 However, AI and other technology⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 9⏎can help automate administrative tasks and support team efficiency to relieve many of these⏎burdens.27 For instance, AI can potentially help capture eligible services for time-based⏎integrated care models and reduce revenue uncertainty, a major reason practices report either⏎abandoning or never starting an integrated care model. Additionally, AI can support case review⏎efficiency through automated case synthesis, which summarizes symptoms, scores, medication⏎trials, adherence, and key events. MIC is a critical component of effective CoCM⏎implementation, so the opportunities to support MIC through AI hold promise for improving⏎CoCM uptake and ongoing use.⏎Emerging research demonstrates the value of AI and technology more broadly in implementing⏎integrated care models efficiently and effectively. For instance, NeuroFlow is a technology⏎platform that can be deployed to support the implementation of integrated care. It uses patient⏎engagement tools, AI, and health data to monitor, track, and manage patient behavioral health⏎risks. In one study of integrated care implemented in primary care and OBGYN settings, the use⏎of NeuroFlow’s platform significantly improved behavioral health outcomes and reduced⏎emergency department (ED) utilization compared to a control group receiving treatment as⏎usual (TAU).28 Because ED use reducing preventable ED utilization can reduce the total cost of⏎care.29⏎Q8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Many chronic physical illnesses, including diabetes, cardiovascular disease, and chronic pain,⏎co-occur alongside behavioral health conditions. As discussed above, integrated care models⏎have been shown to improve both mental and physical health outcomes for people with co-⏎morbid chronic diseases.30 AI has the potential to help extract, synthesize and analyze data⏎across health care settings to provide a more holistic picture of patients in support of the⏎delivery of integrated care. Because data fragmentation limits the potential of AI in supporting⏎integrated care, greater interoperability is necessary to leverage AI more fully.⏎As discussed above, behavioral health is lagging in health information technology (HIT)⏎infrastructure. Of the 1,059 inpatient psychiatric hospitals in the U.S., only 47% have adopted⏎EHR technology.31 There are similar challenges in the outpatient setting, as EHR and HIT⏎implementation may be cost-prohibitive for many smaller clinics and private practices. To⏎successfully deploy AI in behavioral health care, it is critical to address behavioral health EHR⏎infrastructure, data standardization, and interoperability.⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 10⏎Q9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎AI shows strong promise in addressing the challenges of a burdened behavioral health⏎workforce and the slow uptake of behavioral health integration. AI tools can improve⏎behavioral health provider training and quality monitoring, while reducing administrative load.⏎Clinicians, as well as non-licensed behavioral health professionals, can automate their⏎workflows with AI tools that can transcribe and summarize session notes, automate intake⏎data, and support administrative tasks. Patient-facing AI-enabled tools integrated into clinical⏎care can increase patients’ access to proven evidence-based practices (EBPs). For example, they⏎can be used to increase engagement with homework between sessions (an integral component⏎of many EBPs) as well as with accepted therapy and interventions generally.32 These digital⏎behavioral health tools can be layered on top of sessions with a clinician to extend the⏎therapeutic process or, in some cases, to reduce the amount of time or frequency of sessions.33⏎Patients and caregivers may also struggle to find the best provider to meet their needs. AI-⏎enabled data analytics can be used to more effectively and efficiently match patients’ needs⏎with provider expertise, insurance, and geographic fit.34⏎Patients and caregivers also frequently report being frustrated by the lack of communication⏎and coordination across health care settings and the need to reshare medical history⏎repeatedly.35 In addition to frustrating patients, this lack of coordination can prove to be a⏎barrier to care itself; however, AI that supports interoperability can help overcome this⏎persistent challenge in behavioral health care by facilitating the sharing of data across providers⏎and health settings, thereby limiting the need for patients to repeatedly provide their history to⏎each provider or practice.⏎At the same time, patients and caregivers have justifiable concerns with the use of AI,⏎including:36⏎• Privacy and security of patient data, particularly biometric data. Data and personal⏎information disclosed as part of therapy discussions and recorded in clinical notes can⏎be highly sensitive.⏎• Fear of losing access to human providers. Research shows that patients and their⏎caregivers want to ensure their clinicians, and not AI, are ultimately guiding them⏎through treatment and making care decisions. The therapeutic alliance – or the positive⏎relationship between behavioral health provider and patient – must be preserved.⏎• Safety and accuracy of AI models. It is important for HHS, health care systems, and AI⏎developers to build trust around their products by showing accurate and effective⏎solutions across different populations.⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 11⏎Q10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎While there is a growing and significant body of research demonstrating the positive impact of⏎many AI tools in behavioral health care, there is a lack of trust and consensus that AI will be⏎beneficial to all patients in real-world settings. A major factor contributing to this lack of trust is⏎that many research studies are based on small datasets that lack clinical validation and may not⏎accurately reflect diagnosed populations. A recent review of behavioral health datasets used in⏎AI development highlighted several challenges, such as:37⏎• Small datasets lead to poor reliability and generalizability. Lack of variation in⏎geography, language, and clinical setting intensify this issue.⏎• Lack of standardization impedes training and consistent evaluation.⏎• Inadequate scalable privacy mechanisms lead to isolation of most datasets.⏎A foundational step HHS could take to accelerate the adoption of AI in behavioral health care is⏎to ensure ARPA-H and the National Institute for Mental Health (NIMH) work with the private⏎sector to help develop larger, more varied, and clinically valuable datasets and to further⏎establish standardized methodologies for data collection and utilization. This foundation will⏎enable digital behavioral health innovators to access representative datasets to develop the⏎most effective, reliable, and trustworthy products. One such model, also mentioned above, is⏎the ARPA-H EVIDENT program, which solicited longitudinal, de-identified data collected from⏎registered clinical trials that will be curated, stored, and used in future research to assess⏎effectiveness of therapeutic modalities for specific patients.38⏎The agentic nature of AI poses another challenge to its adoption. Traditional assessment and⏎validation of interventions and medical devices is based on static interventions. In contrast, AI⏎applications are continually learning and adapting over time. Thus, AI clinical tools require⏎ongoing performance evaluation, not just one-time approvals. More needs to be understood⏎about how to best assess AI clinical tools to detect drift in performance over time. Significant⏎federal investment in independent, longitudinal research to understand the long-term impacts⏎of AI on clinical care quality and mental health outcomes is needed.⏎Furthermore, to ensure trust in the adoption of AI in behavioral health care, HHS should⏎support research on the most robust privacy practices and patient and caregiver preferences⏎for use and consent of AI in behavioral health. Companies are increasingly moving to an “opt⏎out” system for the use of AI in routine care. The impact on patient trust and engagement⏎should be assessed as new practices and procedures are adopted.⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 12⏎Conclusion⏎The Meadows Institute looks forward to working with you to ensure that behavioral health⏎care, and people with behavioral health conditions, benefit from AI innovation, while⏎promoting its responsible use. Please contact John Snook, Chief Policy Officer, at⏎jsnook@mmhpi.org or (571) 331-5725 with any questions.⏎Sincerely,⏎John Snook⏎Chief Policy Officer, Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 13⏎References⏎1 Steadman, H. J., Osher, F. C., Robbins, P. C., Case, B., & Samuels, S. (2009). Prevalence of Serious Mental Illness⏎Among Jail Inmates. Psychiatric Services, 60(6), 761–765. https://doi.org/10.1176/ps.2009.60.6.761⏎2 CDC. (2025, April 29). Mental Health. Mental Health. https://www.cdc.gov/mental-health/index.html⏎3 Establishing the President’s Make America Healthy Again Commission. (2025, February 13). The White⏎House. https://www.whitehouse.gov/presidential-actions/2025/02/establishing-the-presidents-make-america-⏎healthy-again-commission/⏎4 Fortney, John, Rebecca Sladek, & Jürgen Unützer. (2015). Fixing behavioral health care in America: A national call⏎for integrating & coordinating specialty behavioral health care with the medical system. The Kennedy Forum.⏎https://www.patrickjkennedy.net/app/uploads/2020/05/KennedyForum-BehavioralHealth_FINAL_3.pdf⏎5 Lewis, C. C., Boyd, M., Puspitasari, A., Navarro, E., Howard, J., Kassab, H., Hoffman, M., Scott, K., Lyon, A.,⏎Douglas, S., Simon, G., & Kroenke, K. (2019). Implementing measurement-based care in behavioral health: A⏎review. JAMA Psychiatry, 76(3), 324. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2018.3329⏎6 Lewis, C. C., Boyd, M., Puspitasari, A., Navarro, E., Howard, J., Kassab, H., Hoffman, M., Scott, K., Lyon, A.,⏎Douglas, S., Simon, G., & Kroenke, K. (2019). Implementing Measurement-Based Care in Behavioral Health: A⏎Review. JAMA Psychiatry, 76(3), 324. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2018.3329⏎7 Lewis, C. C., Boyd, M., Puspitasari, A., Navarro, E., Howard, J., Kassab, H., Hoffman, M., Scott, K., Lyon, A.,⏎Douglas, S., Simon, G., & Kroenke, K. (2019). Implementing Measurement-Based Care in Behavioral Health: A⏎Review. JAMA Psychiatry, 76(3), 324. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamapsychiatry.2018.3329⏎8 Walfish, S., McAlister, B., O’Donnell, P., & Lambert, M. J. (2012). An Investigation of Self-Assessment Bias in⏎Mental Health Providers. Psychological Reports, 110(2), 639–644.⏎https://doi.org/10.2466/02.07.17.PR0.110.2.639-644⏎9 Bufano, P., Laurino, M., Said, S., Tognetti, A., & Menicucci, D. (2023). Digital Phenotyping for Monitoring Mental⏎Disorders: Systematic Review. Journal of Medical Internet Research, 25, e46778. https://doi.org/10.2196/46778⏎10 Bufano, P., Laurino, M., Said, S., Tognetti, A., & Menicucci, D. (2023). Digital Phenotyping for Monitoring Mental⏎Disorders: Systematic Review. Journal of Medical Internet Research, 25, e46778. https://doi.org/10.2196/46778⏎11 Bari, L., & O’Neill, D. P. (n.d.). Rethinking patient data privacy in the era of digital health. Health Affairs Forefront.⏎https://doi.org/10.1377/forefront.20191210.216658⏎12 Higgins, O., Short, B. L., Chalup, S. K., & Wilson, R. L. (2023). Artificial intelligence (AI) and machine learning (ML)⏎based decision support systems in mental health: An integrative review. International Journal of Mental Health⏎Nursing, 32(4), 966–978. https://doi.org/10.1111/inm.13114⏎13 Cross, S., Bell, I., Nicholas, J., Valentine, L., Mangelsdorf, S., Baker, S., Titov, N., & Alvarez-Jimenez, M. (2024). Use⏎of AI in Mental Health Care: Community and Mental Health Professionals Survey. JMIR Mental Health, 11(1),⏎e60589. https://doi.org/10.2196/60589⏎14 Ross, C. (2025, December 23). Rolling back health AI transparency rule will shift the burden of vetting to health⏎systems. STAT. https://www.statnews.com/2025/12/23/deregulating-health-ai-transparency-rule-shifts-burden/⏎15 Upskilling the Health Information Workforce in the Age of AI. (n.d.). The American Health Information⏎Management Association (AHIMA). Retrieved April 21, 2025, from https://www.ahima.org/education-⏎events/artificial-intelligence/upskilling-the-health-information-workforce-in-the-age-of-ai/⏎16 Membride, H. (2016). Mental health: Early intervention and prevention in children and young people. British⏎Journal of Nursing, 25(10), 552–557. https://doi.org/10.12968/bjon.2016.25.10.552⏎17 Kane, J. M., Robinson, D. G., Schooler, N. R., Mueser, K. T., Penn, D. L., Rosenheck, R. A., Addington, J., Brunette,⏎M. F., Correll, C. U., Estroff, S. E., Marcy, P., Robinson, J., Meyer-Kalos, P. S., Gottlieb, J. D., Glynn, S. M., Lynde, D.⏎W., Pipes, R., Kurian, B. T., Miller, A. L., Azrin, S. T., … Heinssen, R. K. (2016). Comprehensive versus usual⏎community care for first episode psychosis: 2-year outcomes from the NIMH RAISE early treatment program.⏎American Journal of Psychiatry, 173(4), 362–372. https://doi.org/10.1176/appi.ajp.2015.15050632⏎18 Kessler, R. C., Angermeyer, M., Anthony, J. C., DE Graaf, R., Demyttenaere, K., Gasquet, I., DE Girolamo, G.,⏎Gluzman, S., Gureje, O., Haro, J. M., Kawakami, N., Karam, A., Levinson, D., Medina Mora, M. E., Oakley Browne, M.⏎A., Posada-Villa, J., Stein, D. J., Adley Tsang, C. H., Aguilar-Gaxiola, S., … Ustün, T. B. (2007). Lifetime prevalence and⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 14⏎age-of-onset distributions of mental disorders in the World Health Organization’s World Mental Health Survey⏎Initiative. World Psychiatry: Official Journal of the World Psychiatric Association (WPA), 6(3), 168–176.⏎19 About Mission Daybreak. (n.d.). Mission Daybreak. Retrieved February 4, 2026, from⏎https://www.missiondaybreak.net/about-mission-daybreak/⏎20 Using NLP to Detect Mental Health Crises | Stanford HAI. (2024, January 8).⏎https://hai.stanford.edu/news/using-nlp-detect-mental-health-crises⏎21 AI technology is helping crisis line responders—VA News. (2024, September 2). https://news.va.gov/133911/ai-⏎technology-is-helping-crisis-line-responders/⏎22 Swaminathan, A., López, I., Mar, R. A. G., Heist, T., McClintock, T., Caoili, K., Grace, M., Rubashkin, M., Boggs, M.⏎N., Chen, J. H., Gevaert, O., Mou, D., & Nock, M. K. (2023). Natural language processing system for rapid detection⏎and intervention of mental health crisis chat messages. NPJ Digital Medicine, 6, 213.⏎https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-023-00951-3⏎23 Kacie Kelly, Sam Dorison, Emily Feinstein, & Tommy Marzella. (n.d.). When AI Saves Lives: Real Applications in⏎Mental Health. Retrieved July 8, 2025, from https://schedule.sxsw.com/2025/events/PP153713⏎24 U.S. Department of Veterans Affairs. (n.d.). Mission Daybreak: ReflexAI. Mission Daybreak.⏎https://www.missiondaybreak.net/2024-team-spotlights/reflexai/⏎25 Meadows Mental Health Policy Institute. (2023). Collaborative Care Implementation Costs Across Ten United⏎States Health Systems.⏎26 The Comprehensive Health Integration (CHI) Framework. (2025, February 13). National Council for Mental⏎Wellbeing. https://www.thenationalcouncil.org/resources/the-comprehensive-health-integration-framework/⏎27 Pardes, A., Rene, R., Chun, P., & Cherson, M. (2022). Technology-enabled Behavioral Health Integration⏎Decreases Emergency Department Utilization. Clinical Practice & Epidemiology in Mental Health, 18(1),⏎e174501792208150. https://doi.org/10.2174/17450179-v18-e2208150⏎28 Pardes, A., Rene, R., Chun, P., & Cherson, M. (2022). Technology-enabled Behavioral Health Integration⏎Decreases Emergency Department Utilization. Clinical Practice & Epidemiology in Mental Health, 18(1),⏎e174501792208150. https://doi.org/10.2174/17450179-v18-e2208150⏎29 Pardes, A., Rene, R., Chun, P., & Cherson, M. (2022). Technology-enabled Behavioral Health Integration⏎Decreases Emergency Department Utilization. Clinical Practice & Epidemiology in Mental Health, 18(1),⏎e174501792208150. https://doi.org/10.2174/17450179-v18-e2208150⏎30 Hellstern, R. B., Lamson, A. L., Jensen, J. F., Martin, M. P., & Hylock, R. H. (2025). Physical and mental health⏎outcomes of integrated care: Systematic review of study. Families, Systems, & Health.⏎https://doi.org/10.1037/fsh0000960⏎31 Hu, X., Qu, H., Houser, S. H., Chen, H., Zhou, J., & Yu, M. (2020). Hospital characteristics associated with certified⏎ehr adoption among US psychiatric hospitals. Risk Management and Healthcare Policy, Volume 13, 295–301.⏎https://doi.org/10.2147/RMHP.S241553⏎32 Figueroa, C. A., & Aguilera, A. (2020). The Need for a Mental Health Technology Revolution in the COVID-19⏎Pandemic. Frontiers in Psychiatry, 11, 523. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2020.00523⏎33 Weiner, S. (2022, August 9). A growing psychiatrist shortage and an enormous demand for mental health⏎services. AAMC.⏎34 Lee, C., Mohebbi, M., O’Callaghan, E., & Winsberg, M. (2024). Large Language Models Versus Expert Clinicians in⏎Crisis Prediction Among Telemental Health Patients: Comparative Study. JMIR Mental Health, 11(1), e58129.⏎https://doi.org/10.2196/58129⏎35 Eastman, M. R., Kalesnikava, V. A., & Mezuk, B. (2022). Experiences of care coordination among older adults in⏎the United States: Evidence from the Health and Retirement Study. Patient Education and Counseling, 105(7),⏎2429–2435. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pec.2022.03.015⏎36 Benda, N., Desai, P., Reza, Z., Zheng, A., Kumar, S., Harkins, S., Hermann, A., Zhang, Y., Joly, R., Kim, J., Pathak, J.,⏎& Reading Turchioe, M. (2024). Patient Perspectives on AI for Mental Health Care: Cross-Sectional Survey Study.⏎JMIR Mental Health, 11, e58462. https://doi.org/10.2196/58462⏎37 Mandal, A., Adhikary, P. K., Arnaout, H., Gurevych, I., & Chakraborty, T. (2025). A Comprehensive Review of⏎Datasets for Clinical Mental Health AI Systems (Version 2). arXiv. https://doi.org/10.48550/ARXIV.2508.09809⏎⏎Meadows Institute Public Comment on RIN 0955-AA13 15⏎38 Hollowell, A. (2025, November 24). HHS’s ARPA-H Agency Commits $100M to Accelerate Personalized Mental⏎Health Interventions. Behavioral Health Business. https://bhbusiness.com/2025/11/24/hhss-arpa-h-agency-⏎commits-100m-to-accelerate-personalized-mental-health-interventions/♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0376♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from University of California Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Docusign Envelope ID: 3648D296-8FCC-40F6-AEA7-E321A9DC0F14⏎February 23, 2026⏎Dear Department of Health & Human Services,⏎Better care at lower cost through patient-centered artificial intelligence⏎regulatory sandboxes⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide comments in response to the US⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) RIN 0955-AA13,⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care. On behalf of University of California Health (UC Health), we write to⏎share policy proposals to strengthen the U.S. healthcare system and improve⏎population health outcomes. UC Health serves millions of patients each year⏎across urban, rural, and underserved communities and educates thousands of⏎trainees. Our footprint spans six academic health centers and 21 professional⏎schools. When developing and deploying artificial intelligence (AI) throughout⏎the healthcare delivery system, we abide by a set of shared principles. Our⏎recommendations are consistent with these principles, including that the use of⏎AI should be transparent; accurate, reliable, and safe; fair and non-⏎discriminatory; respectful of privacy and security; and accountable.⏎Over the past year, researchers and developers have advanced both the⏎technology underlying AI tools and their applications to improve human health.⏎Across UC Health, AI scribes have helped our clinicians provide better patient⏎experience through a reduction in documentation burden. They have the⏎potential to shorten appointment wait times and boost accessibility for patients⏎by increasing clinicians’ visit volumes. The greatest adoption of AI, and best⏎outcomes from AI, have been seen in areas where health systems and⏎industry innovators have consistent and streamlined regulatory frameworks⏎with clear expectations and thus can implement new AI-enabled workflows⏎safely and with strong oversight.⏎In the face of regulatory uncertainty, innovation stalls. Regulatory⏎sandboxes1⏎have emerged as a way for health systems and industry innovators to assess⏎the benefits of new AI-enabled care models while maintaining safeguards,⏎including monitoring, auditing, and accountability, until a more mature⏎regulatory framework is implemented. Such sandboxes may provide a pathway⏎towards piloting new uses of AI in service of our patients and communities. For⏎example, Utah’s regulatory sandbox framework recently enabled the first pilot⏎of AI-prescribed medication refills, aiming to make care cheaper for patients⏎while assessing clinical outcomes as part of a rigorous evaluation plan.⏎We acknowledge that such sandboxes are complex environments to establish.⏎UC Health believes any regulatory sandbox framework must preserve existing⏎patient protections and reinforce, not diminish, institutional accountability.⏎1 Winning the Race: America’s AI Action Plan (July 2025).⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: 3648D296-8FCC-40F6-AEA7-E321A9DC0F14⏎Sandbox models should establish clear clinical responsibility, formal governance oversight, ongoing⏎safety and equity monitoring, and transparent reporting of outcomes and adverse events. Regulatory⏎clarity should support responsible innovation while upholding patient safety, privacy, and⏎nondiscrimination.⏎In this letter, we highlight two opportunities to improve health AI policy to make care more affordable⏎and accessible while improving outcomes:⏎1. Establish regulatory sandboxes to test new AI-enabled models of care.⏎2. Modernize restrictions on the use of AI-powered translation tools through a regulatory⏎sandbox approach.⏎1. Establish regulatory sandboxes to test new AI-enabled models of care. We recommend that⏎HHS explore developing a pathway where health systems and industry innovators can propose, and⏎after appropriate evaluation, register a new model of care to receive time-limited sandbox approval⏎under a streamlined, consistent regulatory framework. To ensure public trust, regulatory sandboxes⏎should include established guardrails, including defined safety metrics, real-world evidence⏎generation plans, and transparent reporting requirements. Participating organizations should⏎demonstrate data governance, clinical oversight, and patient engagement structures consistent with⏎learning health system principles.⏎Health systems serve as the primary point of contact for patients and shoulder much of the⏎responsibility for ensuring patients receive timely preventive care, diagnoses, and treatments. Given⏎their obligation to improve patient outcomes while minimizing harm, health systems are⏎understandably cautious about altering fundamental care delivery processes that may introduce new⏎risks. On the other hand, learning health systems strive to learn from every patient and use those⏎learnings to improve care for the next patient through changes to the care delivery process. Learning⏎health systems are well-equipped to pair innovative care delivery models with robust evaluation plans⏎to mitigate risks. In the absence of regulatory clarity, learning health systems narrow their scope to⏎incremental changes to care delivery rather than developing new AI-enabled models of care.⏎Regulatory sandboxes facilitate innovation by mitigating the regulatory risks of testing new AI-enabled⏎clinical workflows. For example, Utah’s pilot of AI-prescribed medication refills was only possible⏎because it was wrapped within a regulatory sandbox. While some have criticized whether AI-enabled⏎refills are safe, the rigorous evaluation plan attached to the pilot will directly answer that question. The⏎low cost to patients to obtain refills through this program will alleviate financial burdens and remove⏎barriers to access. The recently announced Advanced Research Projects Agency for Health (ARPA-⏎H) Agentic AI-Enabled Cardiovascular Care Transformation (ADVOCATE) program provides another⏎example, striving to take AI automation to the next level by testing the safety of agentic AI tools in⏎supporting patients with heart failure through actions typically considered within the purview of⏎clinicians. While the ADVOCATE program has been wrapped within a regulatory sandbox with⏎coordination from Food and Drug Administration, many health systems and industry innovators want⏎to test similar models of care in other areas. Having a pathway where health systems and industry⏎innovators can register new models of care to receive time-limited sandbox approval would facilitate⏎higher-risk pilots where systems have regulatory clarity on specific use cases at the time of designing⏎such pilots.⏎2. Modernize restrictions on the use of AI-powered translation tools through a regulatory⏎sandbox approach. One area that is ripe for such a regulatory sandbox is the use of tools that⏎translate text between languages. We recognize the key role that interpreters play in ensuring⏎accurate in-person communication between clinicians and patients, and that translators play in⏎2⏎⏎Docusign Envelope ID: 3648D296-8FCC-40F6-AEA7-E321A9DC0F14⏎accurate translation of standardized documents such as consent forms. However, immediate⏎translation services are often unavailable. As a result, certain communications (such as secure text⏎messages) may be delayed, may occur in English (and rely on the patient to use online translation⏎tools), or simply not take place. Safely exploring how to maximize use of readily available AI⏎technology can both make care more accessible and reduce language-related medical errors for⏎patients with limited English proficiency.⏎Currently, Title VI of the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act require⏎the use of interpreters for patients receiving services covered by the U.S. Department of Health and⏎Human Services. If a covered entity uses “machine translation,” (i.e., AI), those translations must be⏎reviewed by a qualified human translator to ensure accuracy “when accuracy is essential,” “when the⏎source documents or materials contain complex, nonliteral or technical language,” or “when the⏎underlying text is critical to the rights, benefits, or meaningful access” of individuals with limited⏎English proficiency (45 CFR § 92.201(c)(3)).⏎Yet there has been a substantial shift in how people access healthcare, with many more touchpoints⏎occurring via secure text messaging between patients and their care teams than ever before. Secure⏎text messaging is used to address non-urgent concerns, and it is common for multiple short⏎messages to be exchanged to address an issue. For brief messages, AI tools have been shown to be⏎accurate in generating medical translations for a subset of languages, and patients have shown high⏎satisfaction rates with the use of this technology in multiple studies. Since Section 1557 was enacted,⏎substantial advances in neural machine translation have improved performance for many high-⏎resource languages. However, because of regulations restricting the use of AI tools for translation⏎remain, messages may be delayed or avoided altogether – leaving patients worse off. A sandbox-⏎based approach could enable more consistent and practical use – paired with strong monitoring,⏎auditing, and escalation to human review when needed – while preserving protections for patients.⏎Clarifying and modernizing these regulations, if done thoughtfully in a phased, risk-based framework,⏎will improve language access for patients with limited English proficiency and present a tremendous⏎opportunity to improve access for these patients.⏎We appreciate your consideration of these recommendations.⏎Sincerely,⏎2/23/2026⏎David Rubin, MD, MSCE Executive Vice President University of California Health⏎Co-signed by:⏎Cora Han, JD (Chief Health Data Officer, UC Health)⏎Karandeep Singh, MD, MMSc (Chief Health AI Officer, UC San Diego Health)⏎Sara Murray, MD, MAS (Chief Health AI Officer, UCSF Health)⏎Deepti Pandita, MD, FACP (Vice President of Clinical Informatics and Chief Medical Information⏎Officer, UC Irvine Health)⏎Paul J. Lukac, MD, MS, MBA (Chief Health AI Officer, UCLA Health)⏎Jason Y. Adams, MD, MS (Director of Data and Analytics Strategy, UC Davis Health)⏎Naveen Raja, DO, MBA (Chief Medical Officer, UC Riverside Health)⏎3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0377♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Clark♔♔Wisconsin♔♔United States♔University of New Mexico School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My background is in biostatistics, and I've spent the better part of twenty years looking at how performance claims get made for diagnostic and clinical tools — how they're constructed, what datasets they come from, and how often those numbers hold up when the tool moves from the lab that produced it into a different patient population. So when I read this RFI, my first reaction was not enthusiasm. It was a kind of tired recognition.⏎⏎The RFI asks, essentially, what HHS can do across regulatory, reimbursement, and research channels to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care. I want to focus on that word "accelerate" and on what it actually implies when you're using all three levers at once.⏎⏎Here is the thing that doesn't get said enough in these proceedings: embedding a technology in reimbursement structures is not a neutral act that can be undone if the evidence later looks worse than expected. Once AI-assisted diagnostics or AI-supported clinical decision tools are built into what Medicare pays for, and once health systems have restructured their workflows around those tools, and once vendors have locked institutions into multi-year contracts, the political and institutional cost of reversal becomes very large. Larger, almost certainly, than the cost of tolerating whatever harms the tools are producing. I've watched this logic operate in other domains and I think it's underappreciated here. The decision to accelerate adoption now is not separable from the decision to make future course-correction extremely difficult.⏎⏎The evidentiary situation is not reassuring. Data presented at CAP's 2024 Annual Meeting found that fewer than 22% of AI tools reviewed through CAP's Laboratory Improvement Programs had undergone prospective, multi-site validation before clinical implementation. The majority were validated on single-institution retrospective datasets. As a biostatistician I can tell you that this is not a minor methodological quibble — performance degrades substantially when you move from homogeneous training populations to the kind of real-world variation you see across laboratory environments, patient demographics, and imaging equipment. The 22% figure represents tools already in use. HHS is being asked to accelerate the adoption of the other 78%.⏎⏎I want to raise something that I think is actually the deeper concern, even though it's harder to fit into a standard regulatory comment format. There is a prior question before the question of evidence standards, which is: who decided that medicine should be optimized for AI adoption speed in the first place? I don't mean that rhetorically. I mean it as a genuine governance question. A society is entitled to decide, through something resembling actual deliberation, that clinical medicine should be slower, more accountable, and more dependent on human judgment, even if that choice has efficiency costs. The implicit assumption running through this RFI is that speed of adoption is a good we should be removing barriers to. But that assumption hasn't been democratically vetted. It's been made by a combination of industry interest, research enthusiasm, and agency preference.⏎⏎The RFI mentions "governance frameworks" as something HHS will work to develop alongside accelerated adoption. That ordering bothers me considerably. Governance frameworks, liability rules, and audit requirements are not the kind of thing you build on the fly while the technology is already embedded in clinical workflows. They need to precede broad deployment, not trail it.⏎⏎I recognize that some AI applications in clinical settings have genuinely promising evidence behind them and that not every tool in every context deserves the same level of skepticism. I'm not arguing for prohibition. What I'm arguing is that HHS should be honest about what "acceleration" forecloses, and that using reimbursement, regulatory, and research levers simultaneously to drive adoption before governance exists is a choice that deserves far more explicit public justification than this RFI provides.⏎⏎I'd specifically ask HHS to address, in its response to comments, what the reversal mechanism looks like. If tools that are reimbursed under Medicare are later found through post-deployment surveillance to have differential error rates across racial or socioeconomic subgroups — which is not a hypothetical, it is a documented pattern in the AI literature — what is the actual procedural pathway for removing or conditioning that reimbursement? How long does that process take? What evidence threshold triggers it? I have not seen these questions answered anywhere in the RFI framing, and they seem to me to be more important than most of what the RFI does ask.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0378♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Westcott, Charmagne, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Charmagne♔Westcott♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I strongly support the use of HIPAA-compliant AI tools for clinical documentation in behavioral health settings.⏎⏎For me, AI-assisted note completion has significantly improved both the quality of my clinical notes and my ability to be fully present with clients. It allows me to listen without simultaneously typing, which enhances therapeutic rapport and overall quality of care. Rather than dividing my attention between a screen and the person in front of me, I can focus entirely on the client.⏎⏎Behavioral health clinicians typically have very limited time between sessions to complete documentation. AI tools meaningfully reduce administrative burden while producing notes that are more thorough, organized and clinically useful. This reduces burnout, which is a serious and ongoing issue in the mental health field.⏎⏎Medical providers have relied on transcription and documentation support for decades. AI-assisted documentation is a natural evolution of those tools. When used responsibly and in compliance with privacy regulations, I do not see a meaningful ethical distinction between traditional medical transcription and modern AI-assisted note generation.⏎⏎Importantly, the AI system I use is HIPAA-compliant and functions as a documentation aid, not a replacement for clinical judgment, diagnosis or decision-making. The clinician remains fully responsible for reviewing, editing and approving all notes.⏎⏎Thoughtful integration of AI documentation tools supports better care, healthier clinicians and a more sustainable behavioral health system. I encourage policies that allow and regulate their use rather than restrict access to tools that demonstrably improve clinical practice.⏎⏎Thank you,⏎Charmagne Westcott, LPC⏎Portland, Oregon♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0379♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cellworks, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Cellworks♔♔♔♔♔♔CELLWORKS GROUP, INC.⏎Response to HHS Request for Information⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Federal Register 2025-23641⏎Submitted to: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎From: Cellworks Group, Inc.⏎Date: February 16, 2026⏎RE: Comments on AI Adoption in Clinical Care – CLIA Laboratory Algorithmic Services⏎EXECUTIVE SUMMARY⏎Cellworks Group, a CLIA-certified precision oncology laboratory, submits these comments to⏎assist HHS in accelerating responsible AI adoption in clinical care. We focus on a critical barrier:⏎algorithmic services offered lawfully by CLIA-certified laboratories currently lack clear⏎pathways to obtain Medicare coverage, codes, and payment rates on the same terms as⏎other laboratory services.⏎We offer three specific recommendations addressing the RFI's focus areas:⏎Regulation: Clarify that CLIA oversight combined with rigorous technical assessment provides⏎sufficient regulatory framework for single-site algorithmic laboratory services, enabling⏎Medicare coverage without requiring FDA clearance as a prerequisite, while supporting public⏎trust in diagnostic outputs.⏎Reimbursement: Endorse the AMA's Clinically Meaningful Algorithmic Analyses (CMAA)⏎framework to provide predictable coding and payment for algorithmic services, with payment⏎rates reflecting the substantial resources required to develop and validate these services.⏎Research & Development: Fund multi-institutional outcomes research to generate real-world⏎evidence supporting payer coverage decisions for validated algorithmic services, with emphasis⏎on health equity and access for all Americans.⏎These recommendations directly address HHS's stated goals: reducing uncertainty that impedes⏎AI innovation, fostering public trust and confidence in AI tools, aligning federal incentives, and⏎accelerating access to validated AI tools in clinical care for patients nationwide.⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎ABOUT CELLWORKS⏎Cellworks Group operates a CLIA-certified laboratory developing algorithmic analyses that⏎integrate genomic data with treatment simulation modeling for precision oncology. Our⏎services:⏎• Undergo rigorous analytical validation under CLIA standards⏎• Complete rigorous technical assessment through established review processes (e.g., MolDx)⏎that evaluate clinical utility⏎• Generate molecular guidance that physicians incorporate alongside other clinical factors in⏎therapy decision-making⏎• Are validated against NCCN clinical guidelines and real-world treatment outcomes⏎We have direct experience with the regulatory and reimbursement barriers this RFI addresses.⏎REGULATION: CLARIFY CLIA OVERSIGHT FOR LABORATORY ALGORITHMIC SERVICES⏎The Regulatory Barrier⏎Stakeholders face uncertainty about whether algorithmic services require FDA clearance to be⏎eligible for Medicare coverage. This uncertainty particularly affects CLIA-certified laboratory⏎services that:⏎• Are performed exclusively within a single laboratory facility⏎• Integrate with core laboratory testing (genomic sequencing, molecular analysis)⏎• Operate under continuous CLIA regulatory oversight⏎• Are issued under laboratory director responsibility⏎The High Bar CLIA Services Already Meet⏎CLIA-certified laboratories offering algorithmic services undergo rigorous oversight:⏎Analytical Validation: Sensitivity, specificity, reproducibility, bias evaluation⏎Personnel Standards: Director qualifications, competency assessments, continuing education⏎Quality Assurance: Proficiency testing, internal quality controls, performance monitoring⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎Technical Assessment: Many services undergo additional voluntary rigorous technical⏎assessment (e.g., MolDx, NYSDOH, or equivalent) that evaluates clinical utility and evidence⏎standards - a high bar that ensures patient benefit⏎CLIA's personnel qualifications, proficiency testing, and quality assurance requirements provide⏎a durable governance foundation that supports patient and clinician trust in diagnostic outputs.⏎This oversight framework has successfully supported major clinical diagnostics markets.⏎Example: Oncotype DX, a widely adopted algorithmic diagnostic for breast cancer treatment⏎prediction, was developed as CLIA-certified LDT with extensive prospective validation. Today it:⏎• Informs ~40% of U.S. breast cancer treatment decisions (>90% in relevant subtypes)⏎• Is Medicare/Medicaid reimbursed nationwide⏎• Is recommended in NCCN clinical guidelines⏎• Has 20+ years of prospective outcomes data⏎Recommendation⏎HHS should clarify that CLIA oversight combined with rigorous technical assessment provides⏎sufficient regulatory framework for single-site laboratory algorithmic services to be eligible for⏎Medicare coverage and payment. This clarification would:⏎• Remove unnecessary regulatory uncertainty blocking commercialization⏎• Recognize that CLIA + rigorous technical assessment establishes rigorous safeguards⏎• Enable Medicare coverage decisions based on clinical evidence and utility⏎• Accelerate access to validated algorithmic services⏎• Foster public confidence in AI tools through transparent governance⏎Implementation: CMS should issue guidance stating that CLIA-certified laboratory algorithmic⏎services meeting established rigorous technical assessment standards (e.g., MolDx,NYSDOH or⏎equivalent) are eligible for Medicare coverage consideration without FDA clearance as a⏎prerequisite.⏎REIMBURSEMENT: ENABLE PREDICTABLE CODING AND ADEQUATE PAYMENT⏎The Reimbursement Barrier⏎The current coding and payment landscape creates two critical barriers for algorithmic⏎laboratory services:⏎1. Coding Uncertainty⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎• Existing CPT structures (particularly PLA codes) create laboratory-specific codes rather than⏎function-specific codes⏎• This prevents multiple validated laboratories from competing on the same code⏎• Results in 2-3+ year delays as each laboratory seeks unique codes and payer coverage⏎2. Payment Inadequacy⏎• Current payment methodologies may not reflect the substantial resources required to:⏎• Develop and validate algorithmic services⏎• Conduct prospective outcomes studies⏎• Maintain ongoing quality surveillance⏎• Update algorithms based on emerging evidence⏎The Solution: CMAA Framework⏎The AMA CPT Panel is developing a Clinically Meaningful Algorithmic Analyses (CMAA)⏎framework that would:⏎• Create function-specific (not lab-specific) codes for algorithmic services⏎• Include a transition pathway for existing PLA-coded services to migrate to function specific⏎categories.⏎• Organize codes by clinical evidence depth and utility⏎• Enable multiple laboratories meeting evidence standards to use the same codes⏎• Support competitive markets based on quality and outcomes⏎This framework directly solves the coding bottleneck HHS has identified.⏎Recommendation⏎HHS should publicly endorse the AMA CMAA framework and commit to implementation:⏎1 CMS Endorsement: Issue a statement supporting CMAA as the appropriate coding structure⏎for clinical algorithmic services⏎2 Payment Commitment: Commit to Medicare payment implementation by January 1, 2027,⏎with:⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎• Payment rates under Clinical Laboratory Fee Schedule (CLFS) for laboratory-based⏎algorithmic services⏎• Rates reflecting the substantial validation and development burden⏎• Methodology accounting for ongoing surveillance and evidence generation⏎3 Multi-Payer Alignment: Encourage Medicaid and commercial payers to adopt CMAA coding⏎to create consistent national coverage framework⏎Implementation: CMS should coordinate with AMA CPT Panel to ensure CMAA codes are⏎Medicare-payable upon finalization, and that CLFS payment methodology appropriately values⏎algorithmic laboratory services.⏎RESEARCH & DEVELOPMENT: FUND OUTCOMES INFRASTRUCTURE⏎The Evidence Gap⏎While many algorithmic services demonstrate strong analytical and clinical validity, payers⏎increasingly require real-world outcomes evidence before providing coverage. However:⏎• Single laboratories cannot fund multi-institutional prospective studies⏎• Academic medical centers lack incentives to generate comparative effectiveness data⏎• Payers wait for outcomes evidence before covering services (creating a catch-22)⏎Recommendation⏎HHS should establish an "Algorithmic Diagnostics Validation Initiative" to generate outcomes⏎evidence:⏎Structure:⏎• $50-100M annual funding primarily through AHRQ, with NIH coordination⏎• Competitive grants for multi-institutional outcomes studies⏎• Focus on algorithmic services with strong preliminary evidence but limited real-world⏎validation⏎• Require data sharing to support multiple payers' coverage decisions⏎Priorities:⏎• Prospective outcomes studies comparing algorithmic-guided vs. standard-of-care treatment⏎selection⏎• Health economics analyses (cost-effectiveness, value-based care implications)⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎• Implementation science (workflow integration, physician adoption barriers)⏎• Health equity studies ensuring algorithm performance across diverse populations. When⏎appropriately validated and monitored, algorithmic laboratory services can help⏎democratize access to expert-level oncology analysis for patients regardless of⏎geography - supporting equitable care for individuals treated in rural or community⏎settings as well as those at academic centers.⏎Expected Impact:⏎• Accelerates payer coverage decisions by generating required evidence⏎• Reduces commercial barriers for validated algorithmic services⏎• Creates public evidence infrastructure supporting competitive markets⏎• Identifies high-value algorithmic applications for federal investment⏎• Advances health equity by ensuring AI tools serve all Americans effectively⏎ADDRESSING RFI PRIORITY QUESTIONS⏎What are the biggest barriers to AI adoption in clinical care?⏎For CLIA laboratory algorithmic services:⏎1 Regulatory uncertainty: Lack of clarity on whether CLIA oversight combined with rigorous⏎technical assessment is sufficient for Medicare eligibility⏎2 Coding bottleneck: PLA code structure prevents function-specific coding and delays market⏎entry⏎3 Payment inadequacy: Current methodologies may undervalue development and validation⏎burden⏎4 Evidence gap: Payers require outcomes evidence that single labs cannot generate⏎What regulatory or payment policy changes should HHS prioritize?⏎1 Immediate (2026): CMS guidance clarifying CLIA + rigorous technical assessment (e.g. MolDx)⏎sufficiency for Medicare coverage eligibility⏎2 Near-term (2027): CMAA framework implementation with committed Medicare payment⏎under CLFS⏎3 Medium-term (2027-2028): Outcomes research funding to generate coverage-decision⏎evidence⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎Where have AI tools met or exceeded expectations?⏎Oncotype DX demonstrates that algorithmic diagnostics developed as CLIA-certified LDT:⏎• Standard-of-care adoption (>90% utilization in relevant patient populations)⏎• Guideline recommendation (NCCN, ASCO)⏎• Nationwide payer coverage (Medicare, Medicaid, commercial)⏎• 20+ years sustained clinical use with continuous outcomes validation⏎This precedent proves that CLIA-governed laboratory services with rigorous validation can support⏎major clinical markets when evidence standards are high.⏎CONCLUSION⏎Algorithmic services offered lawfully by CLIA-certified laboratories face unnecessary barriers to⏎obtaining Medicare coverage, codes, and payment on the same terms as other laboratory⏎services. These barriers throttle AI adoption despite strong clinical evidence and patient⏎benefit.⏎HHS can address these barriers through three coordinated actions:⏎1 Clarify that CLIA oversight + rigorous technical assessment (e.g. MolDx) provides sufficient⏎regulatory framework for Medicare coverage consideration⏎2 Endorse CMAA coding framework with committed Medicare payment implementation⏎3 Fund outcomes research infrastructure to generate coverage-decision evidence and advance⏎health equity⏎These actions align with HHS's stated goals: reducing regulatory uncertainty, fostering public⏎trust in AI tools, enabling innovation, and accelerating access to validated AI services that⏎improve outcomes for all Americans.⏎Cellworks remains available to provide technical input, clinical perspective, or implementation⏎experience as HHS refines policies to accelerate responsible AI adoption in clinical care.⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎APPENDIX: SUPPORTING EVIDENCE⏎CLIA + Rigorous Technical Assessment as Comprehensive Safeguard⏎Rigorous technical assessment processes (e.g., MolDx) require:⏎• Analytical Validity: Evidence of accuracy, reproducibility, and performance across populations⏎• Clinical Validity: Literature demonstrating correlation with clinical outcomes⏎• Clinical Utility: Evidence that patients benefit when results guide decision-making⏎• Post-Market Surveillance: Ongoing performance monitoring and outcomes tracking⏎This framework has successfully validated 200+ laboratory tests with demonstrated clinical⏎adoption, establishing that rigorous technical assessment combined with CLIA oversight creates⏎a high bar for quality and patient safety.⏎Economic Impact of Reimbursement Barriers⏎Current barriers create:⏎• 2-3 year delays in payer coverage per algorithmic service⏎• $5-10M per service in duplicative evidence generation (each payer requires separate⏎dossiers)⏎• Venture funding hesitation due to reimbursement uncertainty⏎• Innovation suppression as companies avoid algorithmic development⏎CMAA implementation would:⏎• Reduce coverage timeline from 2-3 years to 12-18 months⏎• Create predictable coding structure enabling commercial planning⏎• Support competitive markets (multiple labs using same codes)⏎• Accelerate innovation by removing reimbursement uncertainty⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life⏎⏎CELLWORKS GROUP, INC +1 408 467 3805⏎2033 Gateway Place, Suite 500, info@cellworksgroup.com⏎San Jose, California – 95110, USA www.cellworks.life♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0380♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network (ACS CAN), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network (ACS CAN)♔♔♔♔♔♔American⏎Cancer⏎Society⏎⏎Il Cancer⏎ction⏎(a@@ Network™⏎⏎February 17, 2026⏎⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎⏎Secretary⏎⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (December 23, 2025)⏎⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎⏎The American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network (ACS CAN) appreciates the opportunity to⏎comment on the request for information related to accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (Al) as part of clinical care. ACS CAN advocates for evidence-based public policies to⏎reduce the cancer burden for everyone. As the American Cancer Society’s nonprofit, nonpartisan⏎advocacy affiliate, ACS CAN is making cancer a top priority for public officials and candidates at⏎the federal, state, and local levels. By engaging advocates across the country to make their voices⏎heard, ACS CAN influences legislative and regulatory solutions that will end cancer as we know it,⏎for everyone. We are providing comments through the lens of cancer patients.⏎⏎There are currently more than 18.6 million Americans living with a history of cancer." In 2026 in the⏎U.S., approximately 2.1 million new cancer cases are expected to be diagnosed, and more than⏎626,000 people are expected to die from the disease.' Applications incorporating Al are being⏎developed, and some already deployed, that can improve screening and detection, diagnosis,⏎direct and assess treatment, and advance personalized medicine.” For example, integrating Al use⏎in radiological image analysis has been shown to improve efficiency, accuracy and consistency in⏎detection and diagnosis.** Molecular diagnostics are rapidly advancing through the integration of⏎Al, facilitating earlier detection and treatment monitoring.®⏎⏎Additionally, Al shows promise for enhancing care in rural areas that lack specialty providers,⏎through the use of telemedicine, remote expert support for providers, and easing the need for local⏎site infrastructure through automated administrative tools.*’ Technologies such as Al-enabled⏎remote symptom monitoring may similarly expand access to care for rural populations and⏎improve consistency in diagnosis and treatment.®?⏎⏎Although these advances show promise, practical obstacles like cost and infrastructure⏎requirements may prevent smaller or rural health care sites from implementing these tools, "° and it⏎has been shown that these sites are less likely to evaluate Al tools for problems like partiality.'° We⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎Comments on AI RFI | February 23, 2026⏎Page 2⏎⏎are also concerned about the potential negative impact of the use of Alin utilization management⏎(including prior authorization), coverage determinations, and claims management that could⏎impede timely access to evidence-based care."' In 2023, a lawsuit was filed against Cigna⏎Healthcare alleging use of an automated Al-based system led to improper denial of hundreds of⏎thousands of pre-approved insurance claims." As the Cigna example has demonstrated, absent⏎sufficient oversight, Al algorithms can be improperly used to deny care.⏎⏎While there may be great potential for responsibly developed Al applications to improve clinical⏎care, Clinical operations, benefit patients, and improve health outcomes, Al applications should be⏎reviewed to ensure proper safeguards for protecting patient access to medically necessary care. Al⏎advances that help reach these goals while preserving patient safety, privacy, and access can⏎impact patient care in positive and significant ways. Currently, patients and providers have low⏎trust that Al will be used responsibly and that health care systems will protect patients from Al-⏎related harms" — and this trust is essential for the transformative potential of Al advances to be⏎realized.'* This assurance requires the implementation of regulatory guardrails and guidance to⏎ensure that clinical applications (whether provider- or patient-facing) have a transparent evidence⏎base that confirms safety and effectiveness and that human review and oversight are included.⏎⏎Regulation⏎⏎Currently, a patchwork of regulations, guidance, and state laws creates a complex and potentially⏎inconsistent system, challenging developers and providers to navigate a diverse range of⏎requirements and potentially exacerbating inequity in access to effective Al applications and,⏎ultimately, medical care. Individual patients may also be adversely impacted when the mosaic of⏎laws and regulations obscures patient awareness of their rights, especially if a patient is being⏎treated in a state different from their residence. Access to responsibly vetted, proven effective and⏎safe Al applications may differ for patients living in different states.⏎⏎We support a risk- and evidence-based approach to oversight that prioritizes patient safety and⏎privacy, ensures that medical claims of effectiveness are verified, and is adaptable such that it can⏎keep up with change and innovation. A clear regulatory framework should differentiate oversight⏎based on risk. This can facilitate easier development and implementation of minimal-risk⏎applications, such as for drug discovery and clinical trial facilitation and eligibility determinations,⏎while focusing appropriate oversight of health care professional- and patient-facing tools such as⏎those used for image analysis, clinical (or patient) decision support, health chatbots, and symptom⏎checkers.⏎⏎We urge the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) to develop and implement oversight⏎mechanisms throughout HHS agencies that require evidence of efficacy and patient safety prior to⏎deployment, utilize appropriate validation studies of the intended use patient populations, ensure⏎⏎that when appropriate, use of Al tools is meaningfully disclosed to patients, specify the extent of⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎655 15th Street, NW, Suite 503, Washington, D.C. 20005⏎fightcancer.org | 1.202.661.5700⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎Comments on AI RFI | February 23, 2026⏎Page 3⏎⏎qualified human engagement and review while implemented, and require creation and use of⏎effective adverse event reporting and post-approval monitoring systems across the programs and⏎regulatory responsibilities the Department oversees. HHS should also promulgate regulations that⏎provide clear standards as to the entity (or entities) to be held accountable in cases where Al⏎applications result in patient harm. Any regulatory framework should prioritize patent safety and⏎protection of patient health information.⏎⏎The implementation of a clear and robust oversight framework should be conducted through a⏎formal process with public engagement that includes a wide set of stakeholders such as patient⏎advocates, provider groups, issuers, manufacturers, and others in the health care system. This⏎engagement is essential for creating trust among patients and providers that Al tools are safe and⏎effective components of their care.⏎⏎For medical products reviewed by the Food and Drug Administration (FDA), the ability of Al tools to⏎learn and adapt in real-world settings means that it is imperative that oversight require full⏎transparency from developers, include mechanisms for ongoing feedback and monitoring during⏎clinical use, and that the FDA create clarity around the types of changes that would and would not⏎trigger further post-approval review.⏎⏎Reimbursement⏎⏎In utilization management, Al tools are already widely used to make prior authorization and⏎coverage decisions, '° in spite of mixed evidence that they improve efficiency, accuracy, staff⏎experience or other metrics. '® For example, from a patient perspective, Al could exacerbate⏎problems with coverage claims denials: appealing a denial would be especially difficult if the basis⏎for denial was not revealed by the Al algorithm, and/or the human operator was unable to explain⏎the Al decision."⏎⏎ACS CAN encourages HHS and the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) to create clear⏎and consistent regulatory guardrails to ensure transparency and accountability when Al tools are⏎deployed in utilization management and prior authorization processes, and provide assurance that⏎final decisions to approve or deny care or coverage are made by qualified human professionals and⏎based on sound evidence-based clinical judgement. These guardrails should apply formally to the⏎federal programs that HHS oversees and be monitored for adherence across other non-⏎governmental commercial coverage. Ongoing review systems should be put in place to monitor⏎evolving technology and ensure that oversight remains relevant.⏎⏎When the Medicare statute was written, many of today’s technologies were not even imaginable,⏎and consequently many Al tools in current use and development do not fit into traditional statutory⏎benefit categories to qualify for coverage. Medicare coverage policies and payment pathways play⏎a critical role in shaping patient access to these advances, determining whether beneficiaries can⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎655 15th Street, NW, Suite 503, Washington, D.C. 20005⏎fightcancer.org | 1.202.661.5700⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎Comments on AI RFI | February 23, 2026⏎Page 4⏎⏎receive new cancer care innovations and how quickly they can access them. Currently, there is an⏎average 5.7-year delay between FDA approval and Medicare coverage of new technologies." It is⏎vital that CMS ensures viable, timely coverage and reimbursement pathways to prevent cost from⏎becoming a barrier to care for patients and to incentivize investment in new technology.⏎⏎Al applications are being used in all facets of the U.S. health care system — beyond the Medicare⏎program. As HHS contemplates future action with respect to the use of Al applications, it must⏎ensure that existing patient protections (including the appeals and exceptions process) remaina⏎viable, human-mediated option for patients to access medically necessary services. HHS must⏎also ensure that when making coverage determinations, regulated entities maintain human⏎oversight over Al applications.⏎⏎Research and Development⏎⏎HHS has an important role in supporting the development of responsible Al, and we urge the⏎National Institutes of Health (NIH) to take an active role in fostering research-based initiatives that⏎can have a positive impact. The creation of clear guidance for investigators can ensure responsible⏎and transparent use of Al in federally sponsored research and clinical trials. Public-facing tools and⏎educational resources provided on the NIH website can help inform patients and providers.⏎Funding from NIH can fill important gaps in research areas that would typically be unsupported by⏎industry, such as childhood cancer and comparative effectiveness research.⏎⏎Al applications can enhance almost all areas of the clinical trial lifecycle, and clear guidance and⏎support from NIH can facilitate development of tools that improve elements such as trial design,⏎participant identification and recruitment, decentralization of trial activities and sites, and efficient⏎data analysis. Of course, many potential enhancements are accompanied by risks, including⏎exacerbating disparities and concerns about patient privacy and data security, reinforcing the need⏎for appropriate guardrails and guidance to accompany these advances.⏎⏎Similarly, Al can accelerate drug discovery, research, and testing, including applications in⏎identifying drug targets, designing and screening candidate compounds, modeling⏎pharmacokinetics and pharmacodynamics, and streamlining and optimizing drug manufacturing.⏎NIH can play a critical role in supporting research aspects not typically pursued by industry, such⏎as using Al to identify additional indications for approved drugs, advancing pediatric drug⏎development, and Al applications that guide development of clinical trial portfolios and⏎methodology to maximize efficient testing of new drugs or uses.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎⏎Thank you again for the opportunity to provide comments on the request for information related to⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence as part of clinical care. If you have any⏎questions, please do not hesitate to contact Sharon Shriver, Senior Analyst at⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎655 15th Street, NW, Suite 503, Washington, D.C. 20005⏎fightcancer.org | 1.202.661.5700⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎Comments on AI RFI | February 23, 2026⏎Page 5⏎⏎Sharon.Shriver@cancer.org.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎⏎Lisa Lacasse, MBA⏎President⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎⏎1 American Cancer Society. Cancer Facts & Figures 2026. Atlanta: American Cancer Society; 2026.⏎⏎? Huhulea EN, Huang L, Eng S, Sumawi B, Huang A, Aifuwa E, Hirani R, Tiwari RK, Etienne M. Artificial⏎Intelligence Advancements in Oncology: A Review of Current Trends and Future Directions. Biomedicines.⏎2025 Apr 13;13(4):951. doi: 10.3390/biomedicines13040951. PMID: 40299653; PMCID: PMC12025054.⏎⏎3 Zheng S, Cui X, Ye Z. Integrating artificial intelligence into radiological cancer imaging: from diagnosis and⏎treatment response to prognosis. Cancer Biol Med. 2025 Feb 4;22(1):6-13. doi: 10.20892/j.issn.2095-⏎3941.2024.0422. PMID: 39907115; PMCID: PMC11795265.⏎⏎* Invenio Imaging Receives FDA Breakthrough Device Designation for Al-based Image Analysis Module to⏎Assist Physicians in the Evaluation of Bronchoscopic Lung Biopsies (2024) www.prnewswire.com/news-⏎releases/invenio-imaging-receives-fda-breakthrough-device-designation-for-ai-based-image-analysis-⏎module-to-assist-physicians-in-the-evaluation-of-bronchoscopic-lung-biopsies-302290901 .html.⏎⏎5 Montoya Mira JL, Quentel A, Patel RK, Keith D, Sousa M, Minnier J, Kingston BR, David L, Esener SC, Sears⏎RC, Lopez CD, Sheppard BC, Demirci U, Wong MH, Fischer JM. Early detection of pancreatic cancer by a⏎high-throughput protease-activated nanosensor assay. Sci Transl Med. 2025 Feb 12;17(785):eadq3110. doi:⏎10.1126/scitranslmed.adq3110. Epub 2025 Feb 12. PMID: 39937880.⏎⏎® Venkatesh, K.P., Kadakia, K.T. & Gilbert, S. Learnings from the first Al-enabled skin cancer device for⏎primary care authorized by FDA. npj Digit. Med. 7, 156 (2024). https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-024-01161-1.⏎7 Perez, K., Wisniewski, D., Ari, A., Lee, K., Lieneck, C., & Ramamonjiarivelo, Z. (2025). Investigation into⏎Application of Al and Telemedicine in Rural Communities: A Systematic Literature Review. Healthcare, 13(3),⏎324. https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare1 3030324.⏎⏎8 Chiu CSL, Gerrits W, Guglielmo M, Cramer MJ, van der Harst P, van Es R, Meine M. From Clinic to Cloud:⏎Efficacy of Al-Assisted Remote Monitoring of Patients With Implantable Cardiac Devices. Pacing Clin⏎Electrophysiol. 2025 Oct;48(10):1106-1113. doi: 10.1111/pace.70036. Epub 2025 Aug 21. PMID: 40838527;⏎PMCID: PMC12504922.⏎⏎° Patel PM, Green M, Tram J, Wang E, Murphy MZ, Abd-Elsayed A, Chakravarthy K. Beyond the Pain⏎Management Clinic: The Role of Al-Integrated Remote Patient Monitoring in Chronic Disease Management - A⏎Narrative Review. J Pain Res. 2024 Dec 11;17:4223-4237. doi: 10.2147/JPR.S494238. PMID: 39679431;⏎PMCID: PMC11646407.⏎⏎1 Nong P, Adler-Milstein J, Apathy NC, Holmgren AJ, Everson J. Current Use And Evaluation Of Artificial⏎Intelligence And Predictive Models In US Hospitals. Health Aff (Millwood). 2025 Jan;44(1):90-98. doi:⏎10.1377/hlthaff.2024.00842. PMID: 39761454.⏎⏎™ American Medical Association (2025) How Al is leading to more prior authorization denials. www.ama-⏎assn.org/practice-management/prior-authorization/how-ai-leading-more-prior-authorization-denials.⏎⏎? Propublica (2023) How Cigna Saves Millions by Having Its Doctors Reject Claims Without Reading Them.⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎655 15th Street, NW, Suite 503, Washington, D.C. 20005⏎fightcancer.org | 1.202.661.5700⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎Comments on AI RFI | February 23, 2026⏎Page 6⏎⏎www.propublica.org/article/cigna-pxdx-medical-health-insurance-rejection-claims.⏎⏎13 Nong P, Platt J. Patients’ Trust in Health Systems to Use Artificial Intelligence. JAMA Netw⏎⏎Open. 2025;8(2):e2460628. doi:10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.60628.⏎⏎14 Alec Tyson et al., 60% of Americans Would be Uncomfortable With Provider Relying on Al in Their Own⏎Health Care, Pew Research Center, February 22, 2023, pp. 4, 7-8. www.pewresearch.org/wp-⏎content/uploads/sites/20/2023/02/PS_2023.02.22 Al-health_REPORT.pdf.⏎⏎8 National Association of Insurance Commissioners (2025) Health Insurance Artificial Intelligence/Machine⏎Learning Survey Results. https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/inline-⏎files/NAIC%20AI%20Health%20Survey%20Report%20.pdf.⏎⏎16 Michelle M. Mello, Artem A. Trotsyuk, Abdoul Jalil Djiberou Mahamadou, and Danton Char⏎⏎Health Affairs 2026 45:1, 6-13. The Al Arms Race In Health Insurance Utilization Review: Promises Of⏎Efficiency And Risks Of Supercharged Flaws (2025)⏎www.healthaffairs.org/doi/abs/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00897.⏎⏎17 Mello MM, Rose S. Denial—Artificial Intelligence Tools and Health Insurance Coverage Decisions. JAMA Health⏎Forum. 2024;5(3):e240622. doi:10.1001/jamahealthforum.2024.0622.⏎⏎8 Sexton, Z.A., Perl, J. R., Saul, H. R., Trotsyuk, A. A., Pietzsch, J. B., Ruggles, S. W., Nikolov, M. C., Schulman,⏎K. A., & Makower, J. (2023). Time From Authorization by the US Food and Drug Administration to Medicare⏎Coverage for Novel Technologies. JAMA health forum, 4(8), e232260.⏎https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2023.2260.⏎⏎American Cancer Society Cancer Action Network⏎655 15th Street, NW, Suite 503, Washington, D.C. 20005⏎fightcancer.org | 1.202.661.5700♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0381♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Acoer, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Acoer, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎RESPONSE TO HHS REQUEST FOR INFORMATION (RFI)⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted by:⏎Acoer, Inc.⏎Jim Nasr, Chief Executive Officer⏎jnasr@acoer.com | acoer.com | healthdataexplorer.io⏎Atlanta, Georgia⏎Docket: HHS-RIN-0955-AA13⏎Submission Date: February 23, 2026⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 1⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎About Acoer, Inc.⏎Founded by a former Chief Software Architect at US Centers for Disease Control (CDC) and⏎developers of many large-scale open health platforms, Acoer, Inc. is a health technology⏎company headquartered in Atlanta, Georgia. Acoer specializes in transforming complex,⏎unstructured public health and clinical data into actionable intelligence. Our flagship platform,⏎Health Data Explorer (healthdataexplorer.io), is deployed across medical examiner offices,⏎forensic agencies, and public health departments to modernize medicolegal death investigation⏎through private AI-driven analytics.⏎Acoer has built and deployed systems that directly address the United States opioid and⏎overdose crisis, working in partnership with programs supported by the CDC Foundation and⏎institutions aligned with the Bloomberg Philanthropies Overdose Prevention Initiative. Our work⏎converts unstructured autopsy narratives, toxicology reports, and investigative documentation⏎into structured, cross-case intelligence. We enable earlier detection of emerging drug⏎combinations, identification of environmental risk clusters, and coordinated system-level⏎prevention response.⏎Acoer is a recognized innovator in privacy-preserving AI architecture, data sovereignty, FHIR-⏎native interoperability, and cryptographic data governance. We use distributed ledger⏎technology (specifically the Hedera network) to create tamper-evident, immutable audit trails⏎that provide verifiable proof of what data was used, when, and under what governance⏎conditions. These capabilities are all in production today, not prospective development or⏎research concepts.⏎High-level summary of our capabilities:⏎Health Data Forensic Intelligence Cryptographic Audit FHIR-Native⏎Explorer Platform & Data Verification Interoperability⏎Data analytics and Private AI technologies Blockchain-based data HTI-5 aligned, data⏎reporting for public for forensic data sovereignty and sovereignty-first⏎health governance architecture⏎Executive Summary⏎Three Imperatives for HHS AI Strategy⏎1. Reduce Preventable Deaths: harness AI as a real-time early warning system against⏎overdose, emerging pathogens, and chronic disease onset.⏎2. Deploy AI Safely: anchor AI trust in cryptographic verifiability, data sovereignty, and⏎accountability.⏎3. Enable Intelligent Syndromic Surveillance: close the gap between mortality data, clinical⏎systems, and prevention response.⏎More than 70,000 Americans die every year from drug overdoses. This is a crisis costing the⏎United States an estimated $1.5 trillion annually in healthcare, lost productivity, and criminal⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 2⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎justice expenditures. The majority of these deaths are preventable, and the intelligence needed⏎to prevent them exists today, often buried in unstructured autopsy narratives, toxicology reports,⏎and emergency department records that no human workforce can analyze fast enough to act⏎on.⏎This is the problem Acoer has spent years solving in production. We do not submit this⏎response as a research proposal or a theoretical framework. We submit it as practitioners who⏎have deployed AI systems inside medical examiner offices, forensic agencies, and public health⏎departments. These are systems that today convert months of manual cross-case review into⏎near-real-time intelligence, detect emerging drug combinations before they appear in structured⏎reporting systems, and generate cryptographically verifiable proof of every data action taken.⏎AI will transform clinical care and public health when three conditions are met: (1) when trust is⏎engineered into systems through verifiable governance rather than asserted through policy⏎language; (2) when reimbursement and funding incentives reward demonstrated prevention⏎outcomes rather than technology adoption theater; and (3) when interoperability frameworks⏎evolve to connect the upstream intelligence of mortality and forensic data with the downstream⏎response of clinical care.⏎Secretary Kennedy’s vision to Make America Healthy Again demands action on preventable⏎death. AI is the most powerful tool available to accelerate that mission. This submission⏎provides concrete, implementation-informed recommendations aligned to HHS’s regulatory,⏎reimbursement, and R&D authorities, grounded in Acoer’s direct experience deploying AI-driven⏎prevention intelligence at scale.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 3⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎The Strategic Imperative: AI for Preventable Death Reduction⏎The United States overdose crisis represents one of the most significant preventable death⏎challenges in modern American history. In the past decade, more Americans have died from⏎drug overdoses than were killed in all of World War II. Fentanyl and synthetic opioids⏎(increasingly adulterated with xylazine, nitazenes, and novel synthetic compounds) evolve faster⏎than any structured surveillance system can track. The window between a new substance⏎combination appearing on the street and its identification in public health reporting can be⏎months. People die in that window.⏎AI can close that window. But only if we design the right data infrastructure, governance⏎frameworks, and incentive structures to make it possible.⏎Acoer’s work demonstrates the model. Medical examiner/coroner offices generate the most⏎outcome-validated health intelligence in the American health system: confirmed causes of⏎death, toxicological findings, scene-level context, and demographic data. However, that⏎intelligence is locked in unstructured, siloed files and systems. Our private AI systems extract,⏎structure, and cross-reference that intelligence in near-real-time, surfacing early warning signals⏎that can inform Emergency Department protocols, harm reduction resource deployment, and⏎public health prevention strategy.⏎Current Gap Private AI-Enabled Solution⏎• Substance combinations evolve faster than • Natural language extraction from⏎ICD codes capture them unstructured autopsy and tox narratives⏎• Scene-level risk context buried in narrative • Cross-case pattern detection surfacing⏎case files emerging drug combinations⏎• Weeks-to-months lag between death events • Near-real-time syndromic surveillance⏎and actionable intelligence intelligence⏎• Fragmented data across medical • Open standards interoperability with clinical⏎examiner/coroner offices, emergency care workflows⏎departments, and public health agencies⏎This is not a theoretical future. Acoer’s Forensic Intelligence Platform, including the Smart⏎Toxicology module deployed in production with medical examiner offices, already performs⏎structured extraction from unstructured reports, cross-case substance combination tracking, and⏎early identification of emerging overdose patterns. The technology works. What is needed now⏎is federal policy that scales it.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 4⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎I. Regulation⏎A. Risk-Based Governance Clarity to Accelerate Responsible Adoption⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Acoer’s direct experience deploying the Health Data Explorer and Forensic Intelligence Platform⏎in public health and clinical-adjacent environments identifies five foundational barriers:⏎• Regulatory ambiguity for non-medical device AI: Most AI deployed in public health and⏎prevention settings does not meet the definition of a medical device, yet governance⏎expectations are unclear. This creates risk aversion among CIOs, compliance officers, and⏎agency legal teams who cannot confidently deploy systems without defined accountability⏎frameworks.⏎• Unverifiable AI lineage: AI systems in clinical and public health settings currently cannot⏎demonstrate provenance of training data, consent status, model versioning history, or⏎post-deployment performance drift. This makes institutional trust impossible to engineer⏎and audit.⏎• Data sovereignty concerns in sensitive environments: Medical examiner offices,⏎behavioral health agencies, and county health departments are deeply cautious about any⏎AI architecture requiring centralized data pooling or external model training. The inability to⏎guarantee local data control is a primary adoption barrier in the public sector.⏎• Fragmented interoperability: Forensic, clinical, and public health systems operate in⏎separate data silos. AI-generated intelligence from mortality data cannot flow into⏎emergency department workflows or syndromic surveillance platforms without⏎standardized, trust-anchored data exchange.⏎• Misaligned procurement pathways: Innovation procurement pathways for public health⏎agencies and clinical-adjacent public sector organizations are far less developed than⏎those for hospital systems, creating structural disadvantages for responsible AI⏎deployment in the jurisdictions that need it most.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize?⏎HHS should prioritize the following specific regulatory changes:⏎1. Define a Minimum Verifiable AI Baseline Under 45 CFR Part 170⏎ASTP/ONC should amend 45 CFR Part 170 (Health IT Certification) to require AI systems used⏎in certified health IT to meet a minimum verifiable governance standard, including:⏎• Versioned model metadata with tamper-evident storage⏎• Dataset lineage and provenance disclosures tied to consent status⏎• Cryptographically signed audit logs for inference events⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 5⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎• Documented post-deployment performance monitoring protocols⏎Acoer’s Health Data Explorer and Forensic Intelligence platforms implement these capabilities⏎today using the Hedera distributed ledger network to create immutable, cryptographically⏎verifiable records of every data access event, AI inference, and governance action. These⏎capabilities should become the baseline expectation for health AI systems, not exceptional⏎features of forward-leaning vendors.⏎2. Principles-Based Guidance for Non-Medical Device AI Under 45 CFR Part 164⏎HHS should issue formal guidance clarifying governance expectations for AI systems that⏎operate in clinical and public health settings but do not meet FDA medical device definitions.⏎This guidance should:⏎• Define proportionate oversight requirements based on risk level⏎• Establish auditability and documentation standards⏎• Clarify HIPAA applicability to AI inference processes (45 CFR Part 164)⏎• Define human-in-the-loop requirements for population-level decision support⏎3. Support Privacy-Preserving AI Architecture Standards⏎HHS should formally recognize and incentivize AI architectures that:⏎• Operate within dedicated, jurisdiction-specific infrastructure without cross-jurisdiction data⏎pooling⏎• Maintain full local data sovereignty and explicit consent tracking⏎• Provide transparent, auditable extraction and inference logic⏎• Support third-party validation of model governance claims⏎Question 3: What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role should HHS play?⏎Three legal and accountability gaps require immediate HHS attention:⏎• Liability ambiguity for AI-assisted prevention decisions: When a clinical system or⏎public health agency acts on AI-generated pattern intelligence (e.g., an emerging drug⏎combination identified from mortality data) and that action prevents or fails to prevent⏎harm, existing frameworks under 42 CFR Part 482 do not clearly allocate responsibility⏎between the AI system, its operator, and the clinician or public health official who acted.⏎HHS should convene a multi-stakeholder working group to define accountability⏎frameworks for AI-assisted prevention decisions⏎• Cross-jurisdiction data sovereignty in multi-agency intelligence sharing: AI systems⏎such as Acoer’s Forensic Intelligence Platform that aggregate signals across medical⏎examiner jurisdictions, EDs, and public health departments require explicit frameworks for⏎who owns derived intelligence, who can act on it, and how consent flows across entities.⏎HHS should model these frameworks through TEFCA governance extensions.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 6⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎• Absence of AI incident reporting analogous to adverse event reporting: There is⏎currently no standardized mechanism to report AI system failures in clinical or public⏎health settings. HHS should establish a voluntary (and ultimately mandatory) AI incident⏎reporting framework capturing: model version, data context, workflow integration point,⏎and human-AI interaction pattern. This would materially improve safety learning without⏎chilling innovation.⏎B. Supporting Private Sector Innovation⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote innovative and effective⏎AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should establish a tiered, risk-proportionate AI assurance framework that:⏎• Creates a voluntary certification pathway for health AI vendors demonstrating verifiable⏎governance practices⏎• Establishes public-private partnerships to develop open benchmarking datasets for⏎mortality intelligence, syndromic surveillance, and overdose pattern detection⏎• Funds cooperative agreements with health AI vendors operating in underserved public⏎health environments (county health departments, tribal health authorities, medical⏎examiner offices) to accelerate deployment of responsible private AI platforms in the⏎jurisdictions with the greatest preventable death burden⏎• Establishes safe harbor provisions for organizations that implement verifiable AI⏎governance standards, reducing liability uncertainty that currently deters adoption⏎Question 7: Which roles have the most influence on AI adoption and what are the primary⏎administrative hurdles?⏎In public health and clinical-adjacent settings, Acoer’s experience identifies the following as⏎primary adoption gatekeepers:⏎• Chief Medical Examiners and Forensic Directors: control access to the most valuable⏎outcome-validated health data in the system, but have minimal IT procurement support⏎• State and County Public Health Officers: primary decision-makers for prevention strategy,⏎but operate under constrained budgets and procurement processes designed for⏎traditional IT, not AI⏎• Healthcare System CIOs and CMIOs: strong influence but face compliance teams that⏎cannot approve systems without verifiable governance documentation⏎• State Medicaid Directors: increasingly relevant as prevention AI demonstrates⏎downstream cost avoidance⏎The primary administrative hurdles are: (1) absence of standard AI governance requirements⏎that give legal teams a basis for approval; (2) HIPAA ambiguity for AI inference on public health⏎data; (3) procurement vehicle constraints that favor established vendors over innovative small⏎companies; and (4) the requirement that AI tools demonstrate ROI in reimbursement terms that⏎do not yet capture prevention value.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 7⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎II. Reimbursement⏎A. Aligning Payment Policy to Reward Prevention Outcomes⏎Question 2: What payment policy changes should HHS prioritize?⏎Under current fee-for-service frameworks governed by 42 CFR Parts 410 and 414, AI that⏎prevents overdose deaths, detects emerging drug threats, or reduces unnecessary Emergency⏎Department utilization generates no direct reimbursement signal. This is a fundamental⏎misalignment: the most valuable AI applications in public health (specifically those that prevent⏎costs and deaths before they occur) are precisely the ones that receive no payment reward.⏎Acoer recommends the following specific payment policy changes:⏎1. Fund Prevention AI Demonstration Pilots through CMMI⏎CMS should authorize CMMI to fund 3-5 year demonstration projects measuring the impact of⏎AI-enabled mortality intelligence and syndromic surveillance on downstream healthcare costs.⏎Eligible outcomes for reimbursement credits should include:⏎• Reductions in overdose-related Emergency Department visits⏎• Faster deployment of harm reduction resources (naloxone distribution, treatment referrals)⏎in response to AI-identified emerging substance threats⏎• Reduction in time-to-detection for emerging drug combinations (from months to days)⏎• Measurable reduction in overdose mortality in geographies where AI-enabled prevention⏎intelligence is deployed⏎The Economic Argument for Prevention AI⏎A single prevented opioid overdose death represents an estimated $1-2 million in avoided⏎lifetime productivity loss, healthcare costs, and downstream criminal justice expenditure. AI⏎systems that cost hundreds of thousands of dollars annually to operate can prevent⏎hundreds of deaths. The return on investment is not marginal. It is transformational. Existing⏎payment models simply do not capture this value.⏎2. Create AI Outcome Modifiers in Value-Based Care Arrangements⏎For Accountable Care Organizations, Medicare Advantage plans, and Medicaid managed care⏎entities, CMS should establish AI-specific outcome modifiers that provide financial credit for⏎documented AI-assisted prevention outcomes. Critically, all outcome claims must be auditable,⏎not self-attested. This is where Acoer’s cryptographic governance infrastructure provides unique⏎value: our systems generate tamper-evident proof of what AI actions were taken, what data was⏎used, and what outcomes were observed, generating the kind of independently verifiable⏎outcome attribution that outcome modifier programs require.⏎3. Include Forensic and Mortality Intelligence in Population Health Payment Models⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 8⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎HHS should revise the definition of "eligible data sources" in population health payment models⏎to include structured mortality and forensic intelligence, enabling providers and health plans to⏎receive credit for incorporating upstream prevention signals into clinical workflows.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools met or exceeded expectations, and what novel tools have the⏎greatest potential?⏎Acoer’s direct deployment experience provides the following assessment:⏎Where AI Has Demonstrated Clear Value⏎• Natural language processing for unstructured forensic and clinical narratives: Acoer’s⏎Forensic Intelligence Platform demonstrates that private AI consistently outperforms⏎manual review in speed, consistency, and cross-case correlation, converting months of⏎work into hours without sacrificing accuracy.⏎• Cross-case pattern detection in overdose intelligence: AI systems that aggregate and⏎cross-reference individual case data identify emerging substance trends 4-8 weeks earlier⏎than traditional structured reporting systems.⏎• Administrative burden reduction: AI-driven pre-population of reporting fields, automated⏎coding assistance, and documentation support demonstrably reduce administrative time⏎for forensic and clinical staff.⏎Where AI Has Fallen Short or Faces Challenges⏎• Trust deficit in clinical adoption: AI tools deployed without verifiable governance⏎documentation face persistent resistance from clinical staff, compliance teams, and legal⏎counsel, regardless of technical performance.⏎• Integration complexity: AI tools that require deep EHR integration face months-to-years⏎implementation timelines that erode their value in rapidly evolving public health contexts.⏎• Lack of outcome feedback loops: Most deployed AI systems do not have mechanisms to⏎measure whether their outputs led to different decisions or better outcomes, making it⏎impossible to demonstrate or improve value over time.⏎Novel Tools with Greatest Potential to Reduce Costs and Save Lives⏎• AI-powered syndromic surveillance integration: Systems that aggregate signals from⏎mortality data, ED visits, EMS runs, and harm reduction encounters to provide a unified,⏎near-real-time early warning intelligence layer for emerging public health threats.⏎• Predictive overdose risk scoring at the individual patient level: AI models trained on⏎mortality intelligence ("what led to death in confirmed cases") that can identify high-risk⏎individuals in ED and primary care settings for targeted intervention.⏎• Substance combination early detection: AI systems that identify novel polysubstance⏎combinations in toxicology data before they are represented in ICD coding systems,⏎enabling faster public health response.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 9⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎III. Research & Development⏎A. Recognizing Mortality Intelligence as Prevention Infrastructure⏎Question 10: What specific areas of AI research should HHS prioritize to accelerate AI adoption in⏎clinical care?⏎Mortality data is the most reliable, outcome-validated intelligence in the American health⏎system. Unlike clinical diagnostic data, mortality data reflects confirmed outcomes. Unlike⏎survey data, it captures what actually happened, not what was reported. Yet it is systematically⏎excluded from AI-enabled prevention strategy. HHS should treat structured mortality intelligence⏎as a foundational layer of national health AI infrastructure.⏎HHS R&D investment priorities should include:⏎• Natural language processing for forensic and clinical narratives: Investment in⏎private AI and NLP models specifically trained and validated on autopsy narratives,⏎toxicology reports, and death investigation documentation. These differ substantially from⏎clinical encounter notes and require domain-specific training, validation, and evaluation⏎frameworks. Acoer’s Forensic Intelligence Platform represents a deployable reference⏎implementation for this class of system.⏎• Cross-case pattern detection infrastructure: Development of federated AI architectures⏎that enable cross-jurisdiction pattern detection without requiring centralized data pooling,⏎thereby preserving data sovereignty while enabling population-level intelligence.⏎• Syndromic surveillance AI integration: Research and implementation science for⏎integrating AI-derived mortality intelligence into existing CDC BioSense Platform⏎syndromic surveillance workflows, enabling real-time early warning signals to inform public⏎health response.⏎• Predictive prevention AI for high-risk populations: Development and validation of AI⏎models that use mortality intelligence to identify and intervene with living individuals at⏎highest risk of overdose death, before the next acute event.⏎• Verifiable AI governance standards: Investment in standards development for⏎cryptographic AI audit trails, model versioning, and inference provenance documentation,⏎to enable interoperable, independently verifiable AI governance across health system⏎boundaries.⏎Regarding published evidence (Q10a): Acoer’s deployment experience is consistent with⏎emerging literature demonstrating that AI-assisted NLP of clinical and forensic narratives⏎achieves extraction accuracy comparable to expert human review at dramatically higher speed.⏎The gap between the evidence base and current deployment is not evidence quality. The real⏎constraint is governance framework maturity and interoperability infrastructure.⏎Regarding cost-benefit analysis (Q10b): Published literature consistently finds that AI systems in⏎high-volume, pattern-intensive health contexts (radiology, pathology, population surveillance)⏎generate positive ROI when total cost of care effects are captured over multi-year horizons. The⏎primary gap in current cost-benefit models is the failure to capture upstream prevention value:⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 10⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎specifically the deaths and hospitalizations that did not occur because early warning intelligence⏎triggered an intervention.⏎B. Interoperability as Prevention Infrastructure⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate AI for clinical care?⏎Acoer is an active implementer of FHIR-native interoperability and a strong supporter of HTI-5⏎policy direction. Our CEO, Jim Nasr, serves in a leadership role on the Coalition for⏎Decentralized Healthcare and Life Sciences, an industry working group advancing open,⏎verifiable, community-governed research models, reflecting Acoer’s commitment to the broader⏎ecosystem of transparent health data governance. From our deployment experience,⏎interoperability gaps most limiting to AI-enabled prevention include:⏎Priority Interoperability Gaps⏎Data Type Gap and Recommendation⏎Overdose toxicology No standardized FHIR representation for multi-substance⏎findings toxicology findings. HHS should fund development of FHIR⏎extensions for overdose substance combinations, enabling⏎mortality intelligence to flow into ED and population health⏎workflows.⏎ME case management ME case management systems are largely excluded from⏎systems health IT certification and TEFCA participation. HHS should⏎extend TEFCA participation eligibility to medicolegal death⏎investigation systems and establish FHIR-based data exchange⏎standards for mortality case data.⏎AI model metadata No standardized schema for AI model provenance metadata in⏎FHIR. HHS should fund development of FHIR extensions for AI⏎model version, training data provenance, and inference audit⏎trails, to enable interoperable AI governance documentation⏎across system boundaries.⏎Syndromic surveillance AI-derived mortality signals cannot currently flow into CDC⏎feeds BioSense Platform in structured, machine-readable format.⏎HHS should define standardized APIs for mortality-derived early⏎warning signals to integrate with national syndromic⏎surveillance infrastructure.⏎Computable consent AI systems that use patient data across jurisdictions require⏎machine-readable consent provenance aligned with 45 CFR⏎Part 164. HHS should fund development of computable consent⏎standards that travel with data through FHIR exchanges,⏎enabling AI systems to verify consent status at inference time.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 11⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎C. Public-Private Partnerships and Demonstration Pilots⏎Question 4: What are the most promising AI evaluation methods and what mechanisms would be⏎most impactful?⏎Acoer recommends HHS fund demonstration pilots through cooperative agreements and⏎CRADAs with a dual evaluation framework:⏎Pre-Deployment Evaluation Standards⏎• Structured extraction accuracy testing validated against confirmed toxicological and⏎forensic findings (ground truth from completed death investigations)⏎• Expert domain validation by licensed forensic pathologists and toxicologists⏎• Reproducibility, consistency, and population-representativeness testing across⏎demographic and geographic subgroups⏎• Cryptographic baseline documentation of model version, training dataset, and validation⏎methodology before deployment⏎Post-Deployment Evaluation Framework⏎• Reduction in time-to-detection for emerging substance combinations (measured against⏎structured reporting lag)⏎• Accuracy of early warning signals validated against subsequent confirmed death data⏎• Clinical workflow integration effectiveness: did AI-derived intelligence actually change ED⏎protocols, harm reduction deployment, or prescribing patterns?⏎• Downstream outcome measurement: overdose ED visits, EMS naloxone deployments,⏎and mortality in jurisdictions with and without AI-enabled surveillance⏎The most impactful mechanisms for HHS investment would be: cooperative agreements with⏎state and county public health departments for deployment of AI-enabled mortality intelligence⏎platforms; CRADAs with technology vendors (like Acoer) to co-develop evaluation frameworks⏎and open benchmarking datasets; and prize competitions for demonstrated prevention⏎outcomes rather than technology features.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 12⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎D. Leadership in Decentralized Science and Open Health Research⏎Acoer’s commitment to verifiable, transparent, community-grounded research extends beyond⏎commercial product deployment. Acoer CEO Jim Nasr serves in a leadership role on the⏎Coalition for Decentralized Healthcare and Life Sciences, an industry working group advancing⏎the principles of decentralized science (DeSci), an emerging movement that applies blockchain-⏎based governance, open data standards, and token-enabled coordination to scientific research,⏎peer review, and clinical validation.⏎DeSci is directly relevant to HHS’s AI strategy because it provides an operational model for the⏎open, verifiable, community-accountable research infrastructure that AI governance requires.⏎Key principles:⏎• Open provenance: Research data, methods, and findings are published with⏎cryptographically verifiable provenance, so any stakeholder (whether a regulator, patient,⏎or peer researcher) can independently verify what was done and what was found.⏎• Community-driven validation: Citizen science and open peer review platforms enable⏎distributed validation of research findings, broadening the evidence base beyond⏎traditional academic and industry gatekeepers.⏎• Data sovereignty: DeSci frameworks ensure research participants retain ownership of⏎their data and can verify how it is used in research protocols, a critical requirement for⏎sensitive health data.⏎• Transparent incentive alignment: Token-based coordination mechanisms can align⏎researcher, patient, and public health incentives around measurable health outcomes⏎rather than publication metrics or commercial returns.⏎DeSci in Action: Open Cancer Research on ResearchHub⏎Acoer is an active contributor to an open science project on ResearchHub⏎(researchhub.com/fund/4459) investigating the Wim Hof Method (WHM), a structured cold⏎exposure and breathwork practice, as a novel complementary approach for cancer patients.⏎This citizen-scientist pilot applies the principles of open, verifiable, community-funded⏎research to a clinical question that traditional research funding mechanisms have been slow⏎to address. It demonstrates that rigorous, outcome-driven health research can be conducted⏎transparently, with public accountability, at a fraction of the cost of conventional clinical⏎trials.⏎HHS Implications: DeSci as a Model for AI Research Governance⏎HHS should consider how DeSci principles can inform the governance of AI research in clinical⏎and public health settings:⏎• Open benchmarking datasets: HHS-funded AI benchmarking datasets for overdose⏎pattern detection, syndromic surveillance, and clinical decision support should be⏎published with DeSci-style open provenance, enabling independent validation and⏎continuous improvement by the research community.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 13⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎• Community validation of AI models: Before AI models are deployed in clinical settings,⏎DeSci-inspired community validation processes (involving clinicians, patients, and⏎independent researchers) could provide a more robust and trusted validation pathway than⏎proprietary testing alone.⏎• Open science funding mechanisms: HHS should explore whether platforms like⏎ResearchHub and other DeSci infrastructure can serve as channels for distributing small,⏎milestone-based research grants for prevention-focused AI validation studies, reducing the⏎administrative burden of traditional grant mechanisms while broadening participation.⏎• Citizen science for health surveillance: DeSci frameworks that enable patient⏎communities to contribute their own data to surveillance and research efforts, with full⏎transparency and consent controls, could significantly expand the data available for AI-⏎driven prevention research.⏎IV. Additional Question Responses⏎Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎Question 9: What challenges do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by AI in clinical care,⏎and what concerns do they have?⏎Acoer’s work with families affected by overdose deaths provides a distinctive perspective on⏎patient and caregiver priorities:⏎What Patients and Caregivers Want AI to Deliver⏎• Prevention before death: Families who have lost loved ones to overdose consistently ask⏎why the warning signs were not detected earlier. They want AI systems that identify high-⏎risk individuals in clinical encounters and trigger earlier, more aggressive intervention.⏎• Transparency about what happened: Families navigating the aftermath of an overdose⏎death want clear, comprehensive information about what substance combinations were⏎involved, what medical interventions were attempted, and what systemic factors⏎contributed. AI that can answer these questions from existing data provides immense⏎value.⏎• Fewer administrative barriers to care: Patients with substance use disorders consistently⏎encounter administrative and insurance barriers to treatment access. AI that can automate⏎prior authorization, reduce treatment matching latency, and predict which interventions are⏎most likely to be effective for a given individual can meaningfully reduce these barriers.⏎• Privacy and control: Patients consistently express concern about AI systems that⏎aggregate their health data without their knowledge or consent, and about the use of⏎sensitive behavioral health information in employment, insurance, or law enforcement⏎contexts.⏎What Concerns Patients and Caregivers Have About AI⏎• Invisible automation replacing human judgment: Patients and families fear that AI-driven⏎clinical decisions will reduce the quality of human attention they receive from clinicians.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 14⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎• Data misuse: Particularly in behavioral health and substance use contexts, patients fear⏎that AI-accessible data will be used against them in ways they did not anticipate or⏎consent to.⏎• Algorithmic bias: Community members in high-overdose-burden communities, which are⏎disproportionately communities of color, are acutely concerned about AI systems that⏎could perpetuate or amplify existing inequities in how care is provided and resources are⏎allocated.⏎Addressing these concerns requires governance systems where consent is explicit, inspectable,⏎and enforceable, not buried in terms of service. Acoer’s computable consent and cryptographic⏎audit capabilities provide a model for how AI governance can be made visible and verifiable to⏎patients, not just institutions.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 15⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎V. Summary of Specific Recommendations⏎Lever Recommendation Authority / CFR⏎Regulation Amend 45 CFR Part 170 to require 45 CFR Part 170 (ONC Health IT⏎verifiable AI governance baseline Certification)⏎(model provenance, cryptographic⏎audit logs, inference⏎documentation) for certified health⏎IT⏎Regulation Issue principles-based guidance for 45 CFR Part 164; 42 CFR Part 482⏎non-medical device AI in clinical⏎and public health settings with risk-⏎proportionate oversight standards⏎Regulation Establish standardized AI incident 21 CFR Part 803; new guidance authority⏎reporting framework analogous to⏎MedWatch adverse event reporting⏎Regulation Extend TEFCA participation 45 CFR Part 170; TEFCA governing⏎eligibility to ME/coroner case framework⏎management systems⏎Reimbursement Fund CMMI demonstration pilots 42 CFR Part 425; Section 1115A authority⏎measuring AI-enabled prevention⏎outcomes: overdose deaths⏎prevented, ED utilization reduced⏎Reimbursement Establish AI outcome modifiers in 42 CFR Parts 410, 414, 425⏎value-based care arrangements⏎with cryptographically verifiable⏎attribution requirements⏎Reimbursement Include structured mortality 42 CFR Part 425; MSSP program rules⏎intelligence as eligible data source⏎in population health payment⏎models⏎R&D Fund cooperative agreements for 42 U.S.C. § 300hh; PHSA Section 301⏎public-private deployment of AI-⏎enabled mortality intelligence in⏎high-burden jurisdictions⏎R&D Fund FHIR extension development 45 CFR Part 170; ONC Standards⏎for overdose toxicology, AI model Advisory⏎metadata, and computable consent⏎provenance⏎R&D Develop open benchmarking NIST AI RMF alignment; CMMI research⏎datasets for overdose pattern authority⏎detection and syndromic⏎surveillance AI validation⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 16⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎R&D Integrate AI-derived mortality Public Health Service Act; CDC⏎signals into CDC BioSense cooperative agreement authority⏎Platform for real-time national⏎syndromic surveillance⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 17⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Conclusion⏎AI will not save lives until it is trusted, funded, and connected to the systems where lives are at⏎risk. The United States loses more than 70,000 people to drug overdoses every year. A⏎significant proportion of those deaths are preceded by signals, seen in clinical encounters, in⏎emergency runs, in the unstructured text of toxicology reports, that AI can detect, correlate, and⏎act on, if the governance, interoperability, and incentive infrastructure exists to support it.⏎Acoer stands at the intersection of this challenge and this opportunity. Our Health Data Explorer⏎and Forensic Intelligence Platform are deployed today in medical examiner offices and public⏎health agencies, converting unstructured mortality data into actionable prevention intelligence⏎using private AI that keeps sensitive data under local jurisdiction control. We use cryptographic⏎technologies to make every AI action verifiable and every data governance decision auditable.⏎We are aligned with FHIR and HTI-5 interoperability standards. We have built the privacy-⏎preserving architecture that sensitive public health environments require.⏎What we need from HHS is the governance clarity, the interoperability infrastructure, and the⏎reimbursement alignment to scale what works. The technology exists. The evidence is⏎accumulating. The political will to Make America Healthy Again is present. The moment to act is⏎now.⏎We recommend HHS prioritize three immediate actions:⏎• Establish a verifiable AI baseline: Amend 45 CFR Part 170 to require cryptographically⏎verifiable AI governance documentation as a condition of health IT certification. Make trust⏎in AI systems a requirement, not an aspiration.⏎• Fund prevention AI demonstration pilots: Use CMMI authority and cooperative⏎agreement mechanisms to fund 3-5 year demonstrations of private AI-enabled mortality⏎intelligence and syndromic surveillance, with clear outcome metrics and attribution⏎standards, prioritizing architectures that preserve data sovereignty.⏎• Extend interoperability to the full prevention data ecosystem: Bring ME/coroner⏎systems into TEFCA, fund FHIR extensions for mortality and AI metadata, and create APIs⏎that allow AI-derived prevention intelligence to flow into national syndromic surveillance⏎infrastructure.⏎Acoer welcomes the opportunity to demonstrate our deployed systems, share our evaluation⏎data, and partner with HHS in designing the governance frameworks and pilot programs that will⏎accelerate responsible AI adoption across clinical and public health settings. Every day that AI-⏎enabled prevention intelligence remains siloed, fragmented, or insufficiently trusted to act on is⏎a day when preventable deaths continue.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to contribute to this policy dialogue and stand ready to support⏎HHS in turning this RFI’s insights into action.⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 18⏎⏎Response to HHS AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Jim Nasr Acoer, Inc.⏎Chief Executive Officer, Acoer, Inc. Atlanta, Georgia⏎jnasr@acoer.com Acoer.com⏎HealthDataExplorer.io⏎February 23, 2026⏎Acoer.com | HealthDataExplorer.io Page 19♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0382♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Burke, Ann, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ann♔Burke♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔There r no guardrails to insure safety and privacy for Ai in mental health applications. I am advocating for extreme regulation to ensure the market forces victimize the most vulnerable members of society seeking mental health services, suicide prevention and chemical dependency services. Further the impact of large data centers hosting AI negatively impacts an already fragile ecosystem and is primarily profit driven.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0383♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Mettle Solutions LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Mettle Solutions LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology⏎Policy and Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology (ASTP/ONC)⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎RFI Document #2025-23641⏎Submitted to: Steven Posnack, via Regualtions.gov⏎Date of Submission: February 23, 2026⏎Prepared by:⏎Mettle Solutions, LLC⏎An SBA Certified 8(a) Business⏎POC: Kishore Metla (Chief Executive Officer)⏎Phone: (443) 545-3852 | Fax: (443) 545-3401 | Email: kishore.metla@mettles.com⏎10320 Little Patuxent Pkwy, Suite 233-02, Columbia, MD 21044 | Website: www.mettles.com⏎SAM UEI: CURWPHLP9PK9 | DUNS: 079346709 | FEIN: 27-4746158 | Cage Code: 7NKX3⏎GSA MAS: 47QTCA21D004Y | GSA STARS III: 47QTCB21D0023⏎⏎ASTP/ONC⏎RFI Document #2025-23641⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Table of Contents⏎1 Executive Summary & Perspective .......................................................................................... 1⏎2 Responses to Specific Questions ............................................................................................. 2⏎2.1 Question 1: Barriers to Private Sector Innovation .......................................................... 2⏎2.2 Question 2: Regulatory and Payment Policy Levers ....................................................... 2⏎2.3 Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues ..................................................... 2⏎2.4 Question 4: Evaluation Methods, Metrics, and Robustness .......................................... 3⏎2.5 Question 5: Accreditation and Certification ................................................................... 3⏎2.6 Question 6: Performance and Limitations ...................................................................... 4⏎2.7 Question 7: Governance and Decision Making............................................................... 4⏎2.8 Question 8: Interoperability ........................................................................................... 4⏎2.9 Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Priorities and Concerns ........................................... 4⏎2.10 Question 10: Research Priorities and Evidence .............................................................. 4⏎3 Conclusion ............................................................................................................................... 5⏎i | P ag e⏎⏎ASTP/ONC⏎RFI Document #2025-23641⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎1 Executive Summary & Perspective⏎Mettle Solutions respectfully submits this response to the Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS). We respond not as an AI developer ("Builder"), but as a specialized Evaluator⏎and Builder of Assurance Tools that support the safe adoption of AI in clinical care. Our⏎perspective is informed by working across diverse care settings, from large academic health⏎systems to resource-constrained community practices, with AI tools that are often classified⏎as non-medical device technologies.⏎Regulatory Impact on AI Adoption: The Validation Gap⏎Current HHS regulatory frameworks appropriately emphasize patient safety, privacy, and public⏎trust. However, for many clinical AI tools, particularly those that fall outside traditional medical⏎device classifications—regulatory expectations for post-deployment validation and ongoing⏎performance monitoring remain unclear or implicit. This lack of clarity contributes to what we⏎describe as a “Validation Gap”: uncertainty among providers regarding how AI models perform⏎on their local patient populations over time.⏎In practice, validation is often retrospective, static, and disconnected from real-world clinical⏎workflows. Existing regulations do not consistently specify how health care organizations⏎should generate local evidence, monitor model drift, or demonstrate continued safety and⏎effectiveness once an AI tool is deployed. As a result, providers face two undesirable outcomes:⏎1) blind adoption, which introduces patient safety and liability risks, or⏎2) adoption paralysis, where otherwise valuable AI tools are delayed or rejected due to⏎regulatory ambiguity and unclear accountability.⏎This uncertainty disproportionately affects smaller and rural practices, which often lack the⏎resources to interpret evolving regulatory expectations or to independently design robust⏎validation processes.⏎An Independent Safety Layer as a Regulatory Enabler⏎To support a regulatory posture that is predictable, proportionate to risk, and enabling of⏎innovation, we advocate for an Independent Safety Layer approach that distinguishes⏎between building AI and assuring AI. This approach aligns with HHS’s goals of protecting⏎patients and maintaining public trust while allowing innovation to proceed more rapidly.⏎Our response outlines two implementation pathways to promote equitable adoption:⏎• Enterprise Track: “Shadow mode” validation prior to clinical use, combined with⏎continuous post-deployment surveillance (“sidecar” monitoring) to ensure models⏎perform safely across changing populations and conditions.⏎• Resource-Constrained Track: Mettle Solutions is currently developing a Local AI⏎Evaluator (under a contract with ASTP) which is a lightweight, no server backend⏎application that enables resource constrained healthcare providers to evaluate the⏎accuracy metrics of AI tools.⏎1 | P ag e⏎⏎ASTP/ONC⏎RFI Document #2025-23641⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎2 Responses to Specific Questions⏎2.1 Question 1: Barriers to Private Sector Innovation⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The single greatest impediment is uncertainty. Healthcare organizations operate in a zero-⏎failure environment. Providers cannot confidently adopt AI because they lack reliable tools to⏎answer the fundamental question: "Will this model work for my patients, in my workflow,⏎today?"⏎Key observed barriers include:⏎• The "Local Validity" Gap: A model validated in an urban academic center often⏎degrades when deployed in a rural clinic due to differences in demographics and⏎workflow.⏎• Undefined Liability: Providers fear that relying on AI advice creates new liability⏎exposure, particularly when models "hallucinate" or drift.⏎• The "Resource Gap": While large systems can hire data science teams to evaluate tools,⏎small practices cannot. This process risks creating a "two-tier" health system where AI⏎benefits are restricted to well-resourced centers.⏎2.2 Question 2: Regulatory and Payment Policy Levers⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎We are open to collaborating with HHS to determine the best solution.⏎2.3 Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎1) Liability & Standard-of-Care Ambiguity⏎When "non-medical device" AI errors, it is unclear whether the fault lies with the clinician⏎(malpractice) or the vendor (product defect). This ambiguity stalls procurement and adoption.⏎Proposed Solution: "Gated Decision" Architecture⏎We recommend HHS endorse "Gated Decision" Architectures (or Safety Interlocks) as a⏎standard governance control. In this framework, an AI prediction does not reach the clinician⏎unless it passes a real-time safety check (e.g., Is the patient already on treatment? Is the⏎confidence score above threshold?).⏎2 | P ag e⏎⏎ASTP/ONC⏎RFI Document #2025-23641⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎• HHS Role: Recognize that providers utilizing "Gated" architectures are exercising due⏎diligence. This shifts reliance from "gut feeling" to auditable safety protocols.⏎2) Indemnification & Cryptographic Evidence⏎Indemnification is difficult because post-event forensic analysis is often impossible. Parties⏎cannot prove what the AI "saw" or "thought" at the moment of care.⏎• HHS Role: Encourage the adoption of Cryptographic Evidence Chains. By digitally⏎signing every validation event (input, prediction, safety check, user action), providers⏎and vendors create an immutable "Audit Package" that can factually resolve disputes⏎and support indemnification clauses.⏎2.4 Question 4: Evaluation Methods, Metrics, and Robustness⏎For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎Validation is not a one-time event; it is a continuous lifecycle. We propose the following⏎standard methods:⏎A. Pre-Deployment: "Shadow Mode" Validation⏎The most effective evaluation method is Shadow Mode, where the AI runs silently alongside⏎clinical workflows. It captures real-time data and generates predictions without alerting the⏎clinician.⏎B. Post-Deployment: The "Auditor Sidecar"⏎To address "robustness testing”, An "Auditor Sidecar" sits parallel to the AI, continuously⏎monitoring for "Drift" (statistical changes in input data) and "Degradation" (drops in confidence⏎or accuracy).⏎HHS Support Mechanism:⏎HHS should use Contracts and Cooperative Agreements to fund "Clinical AI Innovation⏎Sandboxes." These programs would subsidize the cost of third-party validation (Shadow Mode⏎infrastructure) for participating health systems, allowing them to test AI tools risk-free before⏎purchase.⏎2.5 Question 5: Accreditation and Certification⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Certification must be based on local, ongoing evidence, not just static vendor claims. The entity⏎that evaluates AI models in production cannot be the same as the developers of those⏎models.⏎3 | P ag e⏎⏎ASTP/ONC⏎RFI Document #2025-23641⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Recommendation: HHS should support an "AI Assurance Evidence Package" standard.⏎Rather than a one-time seal of approval, certification should require a dynamic Evidence⏎Package containing:⏎1) Local Shadow Mode Results: How the model performed on this site's data.⏎2) Monitoring Plan: Defined thresholds for drift and safety signal detection.⏎3) Incident Response Protocol: Pre-agreed pathways for when the model fails.⏎2.6 Question 6: Performance and Limitations⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎We are open to collaborating with HHS to determine the best solution.⏎2.7 Question 7: Governance and Decision Making⏎Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the⏎most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎We are open to collaborating with HHS to determine the best solution.⏎2.8 Question 8: Interoperability⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Interoperability is usually focused on patient data (USCDI). To accelerate AI adoption, we need⏎Interoperability of Evaluation Evidence.⏎The Opportunity:⏎If HHS promotes a standard for "AI Evaluation Event Logs", then independent Evaluators can⏎audit any model from any vendor.⏎• Data Standards Needed: A schema that standardizes:⏎o Input Context (what the model saw)⏎o Prediction & Confidence (what the model said)⏎o Outcome Linkage (what actually happened to the patient)⏎2.9 Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Priorities and Concerns⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎We are open to collaborating with HHS to determine the best solution.⏎4 | P ag e⏎⏎ASTP/ONC⏎RFI Document #2025-23641⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎2.10 Question 10: Research Priorities and Evidence⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical⏎care?⏎b. How does literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎We are open to collaborating with HHS to determine the best solution.⏎3 Conclusion⏎The barriers to effective clinical AI adoption are not primarily technical; they are rooted in⏎uncertainty. Health care organizations lack clear, scalable mechanisms to verify how AI tools⏎perform in their specific clinical environments over time. This uncertainty affects regulatory⏎confidence, liability management, procurement decisions, and ultimately patient trust.⏎Throughout this response, we have described how an ecosystem of independent, post-⏎deployment validation and continuous assurance can address these challenges in a manner⏎that is predictable, proportionate to risk, and equitable across care settings. Approaches such⏎as enterprise-scale Shadow Mode validation, standardized evaluation evidence, and auditable⏎safety controls offer HHS practical levers to reduce adoption friction while maintaining strong⏎patient protections.⏎By clarifying expectations for ongoing performance verification, supporting independent⏎assurance mechanisms, and enabling interoperable evaluation evidence, HHS can help shift the⏎clinical AI market from reliance on static claims to reliance on real-world performance data.⏎This shift would strengthen public trust, support responsible innovation, and ensure that the⏎benefits of AI in clinical care are accessible to providers and patients across the U.S. health⏎system.⏎5 | P ag e♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0384♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Van Pelt & Company, LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Van Pelt & Company, LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Submitted by:⏎Andy Van Pelt⏎Founder & Principal, Van Pelt & Company⏎Date: January 28, 2026⏎This document is approved for public dissemination and contains no confidential or⏎proprietary information.⏎Infrastructure Enablement for Clinical AI — The role of a capacity data utility⏎The utility model underpinning the shared capacity data utility is intentionally flexible,⏎supporting multiple care settings with distinct operational challenges while relying on a single,⏎vendor-agnostic operational infrastructure.⏎In behavioral health settings, limited inpatient capacity, workforce shortages, and fragmented⏎placement processes frequently result in prolonged emergency department boarding and⏎inappropriate placements. Real-time operational visibility into behavioral health bed⏎availability and staffing constraints enables faster, more appropriate placement decisions,⏎reduces strain on emergency services, and improves outcomes for patients in crisis.⏎Illustrative concepts for this care setting are available upon request.⏎In rural care environments, distance, limited access to specialty care, and staffing shortages⏎require precise, time-sensitive decision-making about when patients can be safely managed⏎locally and when timely transfer to higher-acuity care is essential. A capacity data utility⏎supports these decisions by providing real-time visibility into system capacity without⏎imposing additional reporting burden on already resource-constrained facilities. Illustrative⏎rural care concepts are available upon request.⏎At the statewide level, governors, state health departments, and emergency management⏎agencies require continuous situational awareness to support preparedness, surge response,⏎and alignment with federal programs such as the Hospital Preparedness Program (HPP) and⏎NHSN Bed Connectivity. A capacity data utility provides a shared operational view that⏎supports both day-to-day coordination and emergency response. Statewide capacity⏎concepts are available upon request.⏎www.vanpeltcompany.com | andy@vanpeltcompany.com | 503.475.3697⏎⏎Regional and multi-state coordination is increasingly critical for disaster response, National⏎Disaster Medical System (NDMS) operations, and military-civilian medical surge scenarios. A⏎capacity data utility supports interoperability across jurisdictional boundaries while preserving⏎local control and governance.⏎Application Across Care Settings⏎Accelerating the adoption and effective use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care requires⏎confronting a reality that has become increasingly clear across federal, state, and provider⏎environments: the primary constraint on meaningful AI deployment is not model capability,⏎but the absence of shared, trusted, real-time operational data infrastructure that reflects how⏎care is actually delivered across hospitals, regions, and states.⏎Over the past several years, federal investments have supported a wide range of digital health⏎tools, dashboards, and reporting pipelines. Yet these efforts remain fragmented, episodic,⏎and often disconnected from frontline decision-making. As a result, AI tools are frequently⏎deployed as point solutions—optimized for narrow workflows but blind to system-level⏎constraints such as staffing shortages, bed availability, specialty service access, or regional⏎surge conditions. In these environments, even well-designed AI tools risk producing⏎recommendations that are clinically reasonable but operationally infeasible.⏎The shared capacity data utility model directly addresses this foundational gap. A capacity⏎data utility is designed as a vendor-agnostic, public-private model that leverages existing⏎systems and non-PHI operational data to provide a continuously available view of healthcare⏎system capacity. Importantly, this form of shared operational capacity infrastructure is not a⏎new reporting requirement, centralized data warehouse, or prescriptive technology mandate.⏎Instead, it functions as enabling infrastructure—providing the shared operational context⏎required for AI tools, clinicians, administrators, and public agencies to act effectively in both⏎routine and crisis conditions.⏎Practical Experience Informing the Capacity Data Utility Model⏎This utility-based approach is grounded in real-world experience working with states and⏎health systems to operationalize real-time capacity and coordination across diverse care⏎environments. In these efforts, success did not hinge on introducing new technologies; it⏎depended on designing governance and operational models that competing providers would⏎trust and actively use.⏎Across multiple state-level initiatives, the work involved convening competing hospitals,⏎aligning state health departments and emergency management agencies, and integrating⏎technology vendors without allowing any single platform to dominate. These environments⏎www.vanpeltcompany.com | andy@vanpeltcompany.com | 503.475.3697⏎⏎required careful attention to neutrality, transparency, and shared value. Providers participated⏎not because they were mandated to do so, but because the system's outputs helped them⏎solve immediate operational problems—reducing transfer delays, improving patient⏎placement, and anticipating system stress before it manifested as clinical harm.⏎A consistent lesson from this work is that sustainability depends on shared governance and⏎operational relevance. When data is governed collaboratively, limited to non-PHI operational⏎signals, and refreshed frequently enough to inform real decisions, providers are willing to⏎participate and continue participating over time. Conversely, when systems are built primarily⏎for compliance or retrospective reporting, adoption quickly erodes.⏎This type of shared capacity infrastructure reflects these lessons. Its design prioritizes⏎federated data exchange, provider-led governance, and high-frequency operational signals.⏎This approach lowers privacy risk, reduces regulatory friction, and aligns incentives across⏎hospitals, states, and federal partners.⏎A capacity data utility as Enabling Infrastructure for Responsible Clinical AI⏎Clinical AI cannot be responsibly scaled without system-level context. AI tools that influence⏎clinical decisions—whether related to triage, transfers, staffing, or care coordination—must⏎account for real-time constraints across the care continuum. A capacity data utility provides⏎this context by standardizing and sharing operational capacity signals that already exist within⏎hospital systems but are rarely visible beyond institutional boundaries.⏎By doing so, a capacity data utility enables system-aware AI, allowing tools to incorporate real⏎availability of beds, staff, and services into recommendations. This is particularly critical in⏎emergency care, rural health, behavioral health, and disaster response, where delays and⏎misalignment have disproportionate consequences.⏎Equally important, a capacity data utility creates a foundation for real-world evaluation and⏎post-deployment monitoring of AI tools. Rather than relying solely on pre-deployment⏎validation or static performance metrics, a capacity data utility enables continuous⏎assessment of how AI-assisted decisions affect system-level outcomes such as boarding⏎times, transfer delays, and access disparities. This supports a learning health system⏎approach without requiring disclosure of proprietary algorithms.⏎Equity, Access, and Preparedness Implications⏎Absent shared operational infrastructure, AI tools risk reinforcing existing inequities by⏎optimizing within well-resourced systems while leaving rural and safety-net providers behind.⏎A capacity data utility mitigates this risk by ensuring that AI-enabled decisions reflect actual⏎system capacity across all participating facilities, not just those with the most advanced⏎www.vanpeltcompany.com | andy@vanpeltcompany.com | 503.475.3697⏎⏎digital infrastructure.⏎This approach aligns directly with HHS priorities related to health equity, rural access, and⏎national preparedness. By focusing on non-PHI operational data and shared governance, a⏎capacity data utility also strengthens trust and reduces barriers to participation among⏎providers serving underserved populations.⏎Policy Implications for OneHHS⏎To accelerate responsible clinical AI adoption at a national scale, HHS should recognize that⏎enabling infrastructure is as critical as algorithm development. A capacity data utility is a⏎practical model for designing, governing, and sustaining such infrastructure without⏎increasing regulatory burden or fragmenting existing investments.⏎Alignment of a capacity data utility to OneHHS RFI Themes⏎Within health care organizations, the adoption and sustained use of AI is most strongly⏎influenced by operational and clinical leadership responsible for patient flow, staffing,⏎emergency response, and care coordination. A capacity data utility supports these decision-⏎makers by shifting governance from individual AI tools to shared, system-level operational⏎governance structures, enabling consistent evaluation and accountability across institutions⏎and jurisdictions.⏎This submission is intended to directly address the core themes raised in the OneHHS⏎Request for Information on the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical care. The⏎shared capacity data utility model addresses these themes not by proposing new AI tools, but⏎by describing the enabling infrastructure required for AI to be deployed safely, equitably, and⏎at scale across diverse care settings.⏎The RFI highlights persistent barriers to AI adoption beyond technical capability. This response⏎identifies fragmented, episodic, and non-operational data infrastructure as a primary⏎constraint and describes a capacity data utility as a utility-style model designed to overcome⏎these barriers by reusing existing systems and focusing on shared, real-time operational⏎context.⏎The RFI emphasizes the need for interoperable data environments that support AI. A capacity⏎data utility is intentionally vendor-agnostic, federated, and focused on non-PHI operational⏎signals, enabling interoperability without introducing new privacy risk or duplicative reporting⏎structures. This approach aligns with OneHHS objectives to reduce fragmentation while⏎preserving local control.⏎The RFI seeks input on how AI should be evaluated once deployed in clinical environments. A⏎capacity data utility supports real-world, post-deployment evaluation by linking AI-assisted⏎www.vanpeltcompany.com | andy@vanpeltcompany.com | 503.475.3697⏎⏎decisions to system-level operational outcomes such as transfer delays, boarding, and⏎access constraints, without requiring disclosure of proprietary algorithms.⏎Equity is a central concern of the RFI. This response explains how AI deployed without system-⏎level context can exacerbate disparities, particularly for rural, safety-net, and behavioral⏎health providers. A capacity data utility mitigates this risk by ensuring that AI-enabled⏎decisions reflect actual capacity across all participating facilities, not just those with the most⏎advanced digital infrastructure.⏎The RFI asks whether proposed approaches are adaptable across clinical and operational⏎contexts. A capacity data utility is designed to function consistently across behavioral health,⏎rural care, statewide coordination, and regional or multi-state environments, using the same⏎underlying infrastructure while supporting setting-specific use cases.⏎Finally, the RFI emphasizes the importance of coordination across HHS operating divisions. A⏎capacity data utility is presented as shared enabling infrastructure that supports multiple⏎missions—clinical care delivery, preparedness, public health, and emergency response—⏎without creating new silos or prescriptive mandates, aligning with the intent of a OneHHS⏎approach.⏎Conclusion⏎The promise of clinical AI will not be realized through isolated tools or episodic data feeds. It⏎requires shared operational infrastructure that reflects how care is delivered across systems⏎and geographies. A capacity data utility offers an experience-informed, scalable approach to⏎meeting this need—enabling AI to improve care delivery, advance equity, and strengthen⏎preparedness without increasing fragmentation or burden.⏎For clarity, references in this submission to capacity data utilities are intended to be⏎illustrative of a general infrastructure approach, rather than an endorsement of any single⏎organization, platform, or implementation model.⏎Appendix (Reference)⏎Additional background materials are available upon request, including a capacity data utility⏎concept, illustrations for behavioral health, rural care, statewide capacity coordination, and⏎regional or multi-state implementation models. These illustrative concepts are intended to⏎demonstrate operational use cases and are not prescriptive requirements for technology.⏎Legislative Context and Policy Alignment⏎The infrastructure approach described in this submission aligns with several legislative⏎vehicles under consideration or enacted during the 119th Congress that could support⏎implementation without creating new regulatory mandates.⏎www.vanpeltcompany.com | andy@vanpeltcompany.com | 503.475.3697⏎⏎First, H.R. 2936 establishes a framework to improve real-time visibility into hospital capacity⏎and preparedness coordination. A capacity data utility functions as an execution layer for this⏎policy intent by providing shared, vendor-agnostic operational infrastructure that enables⏎states and regions to operationalize capacity awareness using existing systems and non-PHI⏎data.⏎Complementing this approach, S. 1974, currently under consideration in the Senate,⏎advances similar objectives by reinforcing the federal role in strengthening the healthcare⏎system's readiness, situational awareness, and coordination across care settings. The bill’s⏎emphasis on preparedness, continuity of operations, and cross-sector alignment⏎underscores the need for shared operational infrastructure that can be used consistently by⏎states, providers, and federal partners. A capacity data utility directly supports this intent by⏎enabling standardized, real-time operational visibility without prescribing specific⏎technologies or imposing new reporting requirements.⏎Finally, provisions included in the National Defense Authorization Act (NDAA) signed into law⏎by President Trump reinforce the importance of medical readiness, surge capacity, and⏎civilian-military coordination. A capacity data utility aligns with this direction by providing a⏎reusable operational backbone that can support preparedness, response, and recovery⏎missions across HHS, DoD, and state partners.⏎Taken together, H.R. 2936, S. 1974, and the NDAA provide complementary policy signals and⏎potential funding pathways that could support the development and scaling of shared⏎capacity infrastructure—such as a capacity data utility—while preserving flexibility for states,⏎providers, and federal agencies to implement solutions appropriate to their operational⏎context.⏎www.vanpeltcompany.com | andy@vanpeltcompany.com | 503.475.3697♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0385♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AARP, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AARP♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, M.D.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy,⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎AARP, which advocates on behalf of 125 million Americans age 50 and older, thanks you for⏎seeking information on incorporating artificial intelligence (AI) into clinical health care. We⏎appreciate the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology’s interest in harnessing technology to improve⏎patient experience and outcomes. Furthermore, we share your belief that – when used⏎appropriately – technology and AI can lower health care costs through increased efficiency and⏎productivity. Our focus is on ensuring older Americans and family caregivers are confident their⏎privacy is protected, their data is secure, and a human relationship with their provider is⏎maintained. Above all, a human must be in the loop for all consequential decisions – and nearly⏎all health care decisions are consequential. If older Americans are to entrust more of their health⏎care to an AI-enabled clinical tool, then that technology must be worthy of their trust.⏎Trustworthiness necessitates that fairness, transparency, and accountability guide all uses of AI⏎or other algorithmic tools used to make consequential decisions regarding a patient’s health,⏎coverage, or well-being. Patients must feel secure that the AI tools being used to make⏎potentially life and death decisions are:⏎• Fair, reliable, accurate, and incorporate patient preferences and goals, and not result in⏎unjustifiable limits to consumer’s access to care;⏎• Transparent, so that when a consumer, or provider involved in a consumer’s care,⏎interacts with such a tool, they have a clear understanding of the benefits and limitations⏎of the tool and any results it generates; and⏎• Accountable, such that patients who have been adversely affected by a decision informed⏎by an algorithmic tool have access to a fair and meaningful process to challenge those⏎decisions and their outcomes.⏎ASTP/ONC ask several questions regarding AI regulation, reimbursement, and research &⏎development. We are limiting our responses to specific questions with a direct consumer impact.⏎⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act’s (HIPAA) privacy and security⏎protections remain foundational to safeguarding personal health information (PHI) and to⏎ensuring that individuals maintain meaningful control over sensitive information about their⏎health. Yet since HIPAA’s enactment, the sources, types, and volume of health and⏎health-related data have expanded dramatically. AARP supports updating HIPAA and related⏎federal policy to keep pace with technological innovation, to strengthen trust, and to enable⏎person- and family-centered care.⏎A critical starting point is clarifying the definitions of “covered entity” and “business associate”⏎at 45 CFR 160.103 to expressly include AI tools and platforms such as electronic health records,⏎health apps and web portals. The web of data is becoming increasingly tangled as machine⏎learning tools pull information from various sets and sources. Legal accountability and⏎responsibility must be clear, and a chain of custody must be followed no matter where the data⏎goes.⏎Second, there should be rigorous enforcement of HIPAA’s de-identification standards.⏎De-identified health and health-related information is not protected by HIPAA, which heightens⏎the importance of getting de-identification right. In the case of AI platforms, the Expert⏎Determination method is impractical and insufficient for de-identifying PHI. Studies have⏎already indicated that AI tools are capable of re-identifying health data. We urge the HHS Office⏎for Civil Rights to hold covered entities accountable for fully complying with the Safe Harbor⏎method of de-identification by removing all 18 identifying elements prior to any disclosure or⏎external use. These elements include names; geographic subdivisions smaller than a state; all⏎elements of dates (except year) directly related to an individual; telephone and fax numbers;⏎email addresses; Social Security numbers; medical record numbers; health plan beneficiary⏎numbers; account numbers; certificate/license numbers; vehicle identifiers; device identifiers;⏎web URLs; IP addresses; biometric identifiers; full-face photographs and comparable images;⏎and any other unique identifying number, characteristic, or code. Strong, consistent enforcement⏎of Safe Harbor will help ensure that de-identified data truly minimizes re-identification risk.⏎At the same time, federal policy must address the widening gap between HIPAA’s covered⏎entities and the rapidly growing universe of consumer-facing AI health technologies. Today,⏎many direct-to-consumer applications and services are marketed as wellness, fitness, or⏎convenience tools. These direct-to-consumer products collect, track, or infer sensitive health and⏎health-related information without being subject to HIPAA. They, in turn, can sell that health⏎data to other third-party entities, which are also not covered by HIPAA. Both the consumer-⏎product developers and the third-party data entities are typically governed only by their own⏎terms of service and privacy policies, which can be lengthy, difficult to understand, and subject⏎to unilateral change. As commercial entities expand their roles in collecting, managing, and⏎refining data—and in some cases begin to offer clinical insights—stronger, enforceable⏎2⏎⏎consumer protections and accountability standards are needed to sustain trust and promote⏎responsible innovation.⏎To this end, ASTP/ONC should also coordinate closely with the Federal Trade Commission and,⏎where appropriate, the Food and Drug Administration. The FTC’s enforcement authority is⏎limited to when companies violate their own stated privacy commitments. Although the FTC’s⏎authority to address unfair and deceptive practices is essential, there should be enforceable rules⏎regarding unreasonable or harmful data use. ASTP/ONC, CMS, the FTC, and FDA should work⏎together to develop clear, consumer-friendly standards for privacy notices and terms, to promote⏎consistent expectations for third-party data practices and to protect both the security of the data⏎and the safety and privacy of consumers.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎AARP has done significant research into consumer sentiments regarding AI. In particular, we’ve⏎looked at patterns of AI use, expectations of the role that AI does and will play in health care,⏎and concerns toward integration of AI into health technology that linger among older Americans.⏎We also note that family caregivers are increasingly using technology, such as remote⏎monitoring tools, health tracking software, and assistive devices. Technology helps family⏎caregivers be organized as they juggle their many responsibilities and oversee or monitor their⏎loved one’s care, even when they cannot be physically present.⏎As older Americans look toward the future of health care, many see artificial intelligence as a⏎tool that could ease some of the everyday challenges that come with managing their well-being.⏎The AARP Navigating the World of AI report shows that older Americans are especially hopeful⏎that AI might step in to help with the countless small but essential tasks that make up daily health⏎management—things like remembering medications, organizing health information, tracking⏎symptoms, or sorting through complicated instructions from various providers. These “micro⏎tasks” often fall on patients or family caregivers, and Americans recognize just how much time,⏎attention, and energy these responsibilities demand. They imagine AI serving as an assistant that⏎helps keep things on track, reduces errors, and ultimately supports their ability to remain⏎independent.⏎They also see potential in AI’s ability to monitor health continuously and reliably. With a⏎majority expressing interest in AI-enabled monitoring and diagnostics, many Americans hope⏎these tools could help them detect problems earlier, stay aware of changing symptoms, and make⏎more informed decisions about when to seek care. In their view, AI could help simplify complex⏎medical information and offer personalized guidance, making health care feel more navigable⏎and less overwhelming. And because medication management is such a persistent challenge—⏎particularly for those balancing multiple prescriptions—they see value in AI’s ability to provide⏎reminders or support adherence routines. These possibilities are attractive because they directly⏎address the areas where patients and family caregivers often feel stretched thin.⏎Yet even as they recognize these potential benefits, older adults approach the idea of AI in⏎clinical care with noticeable caution. Their worries begin with a fear that AI could replace the⏎3⏎⏎human qualities they value most in health care—empathy, conversation, reassurance, and the⏎ability to understand nuance. They express low enthusiasm for AI stepping into roles they⏎consider “human powered,” such as therapy or emotional support, believing that these are areas⏎where the human touch is not only beneficial but necessary. This concern about reduced human⏎interaction reflects a broader anxiety about what might be lost if AI overtakes clinical⏎environments. A human must remain in the loop to ensure accountability and trust.⏎Another prominent worry centers on accuracy. Older adults in particular are not convinced that⏎AI will interpret symptoms correctly or make sound clinical judgments, especially in complex⏎situations. Their ambivalence is captured in the report’s finding that they are split evenly on⏎whether AI’s benefits outweigh its risks—a sign that concerns about safety, reliability, and⏎unintended consequences are deep-seated. They also fear that AI may oversimplify medical⏎problems that require context, intuition, and careful listening. For many people, real-world⏎clinical care is full of subtleties: symptoms that don’t fit a pattern, multiple chronic conditions⏎that interact unpredictably, or personal histories that shape health decisions. They question⏎whether AI can truly account for these complexities.⏎Taken together, the report paints a picture of older Americans who are genuinely open to the⏎promise of AI in clinical care—but only if it reinforces, rather than replaces, the human centered⏎care they value. They want AI to relieve burdens, simplify complexity, and help them stay⏎healthy and independent, while still preserving the connection, understanding, and judgment that⏎come only from human clinicians. AI may have a role to play, they believe, but it must be⏎carefully shaped, thoughtfully integrated, and always aligned with the realities of the patients and⏎family caregivers whose lives it aims to improve.⏎Conclusion⏎AARP appreciates the opportunity to respond to your request for information on Artificial⏎Intelligence in the delivery of clinical care. We understand the challenges of balancing privacy⏎and security with the desire to improve care through data and technology. As you lay the⏎groundwork for future innovation, we urge you to ensure people can retain control of the use of⏎their own information and that individual and family caregiver needs will be given the utmost⏎priority. We look forward to working with you to make sure that the adoption of AI into clinical⏎care is fair, accurate, transparent, and supportive of needs of older adults. If you have any⏎questions, feel free to contact me, or have your staff contact Andrew Scholnick of our⏎Government Affairs team at ascholnick@aarp.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Megan O’Reilly⏎Vice President⏎Government Affairs, AARP⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0386♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-08T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Onboard AI, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Onboard AI♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment Submitted by Onboard AI⏎Jan 5, 2026⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎I. Introduction⏎Onboard AI appreciates the opportunity to provide input in response to the Department of⏎Health and Human Services’ Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of⏎artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care.⏎Onboard AI works with health systems and AI developers to evaluate, govern, and monitor AI⏎tools used in clinical settings. Our experience spans enterprise AI governance committees,⏎clinical operations, quality and safety, legal and compliance, and information security teams.⏎Our comments are informed by direct, operational experience with how AI tools are reviewed,⏎approved, and monitored within healthcare organizations today.⏎Our central observation is that AI adoption in clinical care is constrained less by technical⏎capability and more by uncertainty about what constitutes reasonable governance, evaluation,⏎and oversight. Health systems are willing to adopt AI, but lack confidence that their internal⏎processes will be viewed—by regulators, accrediting bodies, or courts—as sufficient and⏎defensible.⏎HHS can materially accelerate adoption by reducing this uncertainty.⏎II. Executive Summary (Summary Recommendation)⏎HHS can most effectively accelerate AI adoption in clinical care by clarifying a minimal, risk-⏎proportionate governance baseline—aligned with existing healthcare quality and safety⏎practices—that enables health systems to demonstrate reasonable evaluation, accountability,⏎and monitoring of AI tools.⏎This approach would:⏎● Reduce uncertainty for providers and developers⏎● Improve defensibility of organizational decision-making⏎● Support consistent oversight without creating new certification regimes⏎● Require no new statutory authority or prescriptive regulation⏎⏎The goal is not to define best-in-class practices, but to articulate the least the industry needs⏎to demonstrate responsible AI use.⏎III. General Comment on Regulation, Reimbursement, and⏎Research & Development⏎Across regulation, reimbursement, and research, HHS’s most effective near-term role is not to⏎introduce new compliance frameworks, but to clarify expectations that already exist implicitly⏎across healthcare quality, safety, and accreditation paradigms.⏎Healthcare organizations are deeply familiar with demonstrating:⏎● Defined scope of use⏎● Fit-for-purpose evaluation⏎● Clear accountability⏎● Continuous monitoring and re-review⏎Aligning AI expectations with these existing practices would enable faster adoption while⏎preserving patient safety and public trust.⏎Importantly, this approach would also benefit HHS by reducing variability in oversight, limiting⏎hindsight-driven enforcement, and enabling more consistent interpretation across federal and⏎state actors, without expanding regulatory burden.⏎IV. Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation and adoption⏎of AI in clinical care?⏎The most significant barriers include:⏎● Unclear governance expectations for non-medical device AI used in clinical workflows⏎● Inconsistent evaluation requirements across health systems⏎● Lack of a shared reference point for “reasonable” oversight⏎● Limited post-deployment monitoring infrastructure in many provider organizations⏎These factors lead to prolonged review cycles, duplicative effort, and risk-averse decisions even⏎for low-risk, high-value AI use cases. AI developers are increasingly stagnated by 12+ month AI⏎Committee review cycles being added onto existing, onerous AI Procurement processes.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic changes should HHS⏎prioritize?⏎⏎HHS should prioritize clarifying a minimal, risk-proportionate governance baseline for AI⏎used in clinical care, particularly for non-medical device AI.⏎This could be accomplished through guidance rather than rulemaking and would require no new⏎statutory authority or CFR changes.⏎At a minimum, the baseline would clarify that responsible AI adoption includes four⏎demonstrable elements:⏎1. Documented intended use and clinical scope⏎2. Fit-for-purpose pre-deployment evaluation⏎3. Defined governance and accountability assignment⏎4. Post-deployment monitoring and re-review triggers⏎This baseline represents the least the industry needs to reduce uncertainty—not a⏎comprehensive framework or certification. Its purpose is to provide a common reference point⏎for reasonableness, not to impose uniform technical standards.⏎Clear federal articulation of this baseline would also help align state-level oversight and reduce⏎fragmentation across jurisdictions.⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues⏎exist, and what role should HHS play?⏎Key issues include:⏎● Ambiguity around accountability when AI influences—but does not automate—clinical⏎decisions⏎● Inconsistent approaches to indemnification and responsibility allocation⏎● Unclear expectations for oversight of adaptive or evolving AI systems⏎HHS can play a constructive role by clarifying expectations for reasonable governance, enabling⏎courts, regulators, and organizations to assess whether appropriate care was exercised without⏎adjudicating technical model details.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising evaluation⏎methods, and should HHS support them?⏎Promising approaches include:⏎Pre-deployment⏎● Use-case-specific risk assessment⏎● Contextual performance and bias evaluation⏎● Documentation of known limitations tied to intended use⏎⏎Post-deployment⏎● Monitoring for performance drift, bias signals, and safety events (and a definition of what⏎minimum ‘monitoring’ means, e.g. quarterly testing vs. continuous, real-time monitoring)⏎● Defined triggers for reassessment (e.g. new version releases)⏎● Periodic governance review⏎HHS support would be most impactful if focused on shared evaluation infrastructure,⏎implementation science, and real-world monitoring approaches, rather than prescriptive testing⏎requirements.⏎5. How can HHS best support private-sector accreditation, certification, and⏎testing activities?⏎HHS can best support private-sector efforts by:⏎● Encouraging alignment with existing healthcare quality and safety governance models⏎● Avoiding creation of a single federal AI certification⏎● Recognizing transparent, risk-based third-party assurance activities⏎This approach mirrors successful models in health IT and patient safety and preserves flexibility⏎for innovation.⏎6. Where have AI tools met or fallen short of expectations?⏎AI tools have met or exceeded expectations when:⏎● Use cases are narrowly defined⏎● Human oversight is preserved⏎● Performance is monitored over time⏎They have fallen short when:⏎● Deployed without local validation⏎● Treated as static products rather than managed systems⏎● Introduced without clear accountability structures⏎High-value opportunities remain in workflow optimization, documentation support, triage⏎assistance, and operational efficiency.⏎7. Which roles or governing bodies influence AI adoption, and what are the⏎main hurdles?⏎AI adoption decisions are typically influenced by:⏎⏎● Enterprise AI or clinical governance committees⏎● Clinical champions⏎● IT and security leadership⏎● Legal and risk management functions⏎Primary hurdles include manual review processes, lack of institutional memory, and absence of⏎standardized governance.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability accelerate AI development?⏎Interoperability would be most impactful for:⏎● Standardized AI documentation and governance artifacts⏎● Evaluation and monitoring outputs⏎● Audit logs and review records⏎● Repeatable FHIR-based model testing requirements⏎Extending interoperability beyond data exchange to include governance and assurance artifacts⏎would meaningfully reduce duplication and speed responsible adoption.⏎9. What challenges and concerns do patients and caregivers have?⏎Patients and caregivers seek:⏎● Improved access and quality⏎● Reduced clinician burden⏎● Safer, more reliable care⏎Their primary concerns include opacity, bias, and loss of human oversight. Visible, auditable⏎governance and accountability mechanisms are essential to maintaining trust.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research HHS should prioritize?⏎HHS should prioritize:⏎● Research on governance, accountability, and monitoring models⏎● Longitudinal studies of AI performance and safety⏎● Economic analysis of AI-driven productivity and cost impacts (importantly, including⏎pilot/POC success rates, reasons for failures, associated costs)⏎V. Closing⏎AI adoption in clinical care will accelerate when health systems can demonstrate—clearly,⏎consistently, and audibly—that their governance, evaluation, and monitoring practices meet a⏎reasonable and recognizable bar.⏎⏎Importantly, this approach aligns with how healthcare organizations already demonstrate⏎readiness and accountability under established quality and safety oversight models, including⏎accreditation-based frameworks that emphasize defined scope, organizational accountability,⏎and continuous monitoring rather than one-time certification. Applying these familiar principles⏎to AI governance reduces uncertainty without introducing AI-specific exceptionalism.⏎By clarifying a minimal, accreditation-aligned governance baseline—without creating new⏎certification regimes or prescriptive technical standards—HHS can reduce uncertainty for⏎providers and developers, improve the defensibility of organizational decision-making, and⏎enable responsible innovation across the healthcare system while preserving public trust.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Troy Bannister⏎& the Onboard AI Team♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0387♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Dallas County Hospital District dba Parkland Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎February XX, 2026⏎Introduction⏎Parkland Health (“Parkland”) appreciates the opportunity to submit comments in response to the⏎HHS Health Sector Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence in clinical care. These comments reflect Parkland’s experience deploying AI within⏎existing healthcare governance frameworks and provide concrete, experience-based feedback on⏎regulatory and policy considerations affecting responsible AI adoption.⏎As one of the largest safety-net health systems in Texas and nationwide, the Dallas County⏎Hospital District dba Parkland Health has deployed AI to enhance care delivery and operational⏎efficiency since 2012. Today, Parkland operates dozens of production AI solutions ranging from⏎machine learning risk scores supporting Level1 trauma care to generative-AI tools that assist with⏎clinical documentation. As the public hospital for Dallas County, Parkland serves a patient⏎population that is predominantly uninsured or covered by Medicaid, and responsibly deployed AI⏎helps expand access to care for patients in underserved communities.⏎Core Themes⏎Based on Parkland’s experience deploying AI within existing healthcare governance frameworks,⏎the following themes reflect the operational considerations we believe are most relevant to HHS⏎as it evaluates barriers to responsible AI adoption in clinical care.⏎Regulatory Consistency⏎Consistent, universal federal oversight of AI is a strategic and sustainable alternative to a by-state⏎patchwork of regulations resulting in barriers for patients, healthcare providers, and solution⏎developers alike. A national framework can help ensure consistent safety, privacy, and⏎performance expectations while reducing unnecessary compliance complexity.⏎Public-Sector Parity⏎Equitable treatment of public- and private-sector providers is essential. Public-sector providers⏎include large, complex health systems that support teaching, research, and the deployment of AI⏎across a wide range of clinical settings. Consistent regulatory approaches across healthcare⏎environments help avoid a two-tier system that could otherwise amplify disparities in quality,⏎access, cost, innovation, patient experience, or population health.⏎Governance and Accountability⏎Health care providers already operate under one of the most comprehensive regulatory⏎environments in the U.S., with extensive oversight governing patient safety, data privacy, clinical⏎quality, and technology use. Layering additional, AI-specific requirements on providers risks⏎duplicating existing safeguards and diverting resources from patient care, while many⏎AI-specific risks may be more effectively addressed through obligations placed on those⏎who design, develop, and maintain AI systems.⏎Illustrative Use Cases⏎The examples below illustrate how AI is currently being deployed within Parkland’s existing⏎clinical, safety, and governance frameworks, and the types of operational contexts that shape⏎responsible use in real-world healthcare settings.⏎⏎Workforce Safety⏎Parkland’s Center for Clinical Innovation (PCCI) developed a predictive machine-learning model⏎designed to help protect the Parkland workforce by identifying patients at higher risk of escalating⏎to violent behavior early in the care journey. The model supports targeted use of clinical risk⏎assessments and timely behavioral interventions, allowing care teams to focus attention where it⏎is most needed. By shifting from reactive response to early risk identification, Parkland⏎strengthens staff safety and situational awareness while maintaining compassionate,⏎patient‑centered care.⏎Early Warning System⏎In the inpatient setting, Parkland uses an Early Warning System (EWS), monitored by the Rapid⏎Assessment Team, to proactively identify signs of patient clinical deterioration before they⏎become life-threatening. This system analyzes real-time clinical data within the electronic health⏎record and generates alerts that prompt timely bedside assessment and escalation when⏎appropriate. By supporting early recognition and rapid response, EWS strengthens patient safety⏎and accelerates intervention for Parkland’s most vulnerable patients.⏎Ambient Listening⏎In the outpatient setting, Parkland uses ambient listening tools that allow physicians to engage⏎patients face‑to‑face while large language models and generative AI assists with clinical⏎documentation in the background. With patient consent, clinicians spend less time documenting⏎and more time listening, observing, and connecting with patients. This approach reduces cognitive⏎and administrative burden, enhancing both care quality and patient experience while preserving⏎clinician oversight and accountability for the final note.⏎Translation Services⏎One area where regulatory requirements may limit the practical use of AI in clinical care is patient⏎translation services for individuals with limited English proficiency (LEP). Section 1557 of the⏎Affordable Care Act requires meaningful access to language services and, in certain⏎circumstances, mandates qualified human review of machine-translated content. While these⏎protections are critical to patient safety and equity, workforce shortages and cost constraints can⏎limit the timely provision of written materials—such as discharge instructions, consent forms, or⏎patient education—in patients’ preferred languages, contributing to gaps in comprehension and⏎outcomes. Parkland’s experience suggests that advances in AI-enabled translation could help⏎expand timely access to written materials for LEP patients.⏎What HHS Can Do⏎Based on Parkland’s experience deploying AI within existing clinical, safety, and governance⏎frameworks, HHS can play an important role in accelerating responsible adoption by providing⏎clarity, flexibility, and alignment across federal policies—without introducing duplicative or⏎prescriptive requirements. This is particularly important for safety-net health systems whose⏎patients are disproportionately uninsured or covered by Medicaid.⏎1. Support a coordinated federal approach to healthcare AI oversight. HHS can align AI-⏎related guidance and expectations across CMS, OCR, FDA, ONC, and other relevant⏎agencies to reduce duplicative requirements and improve clarity for healthcare providers and⏎vendors while preserving patient safety, privacy, and accountability.⏎2. Reinforce existing provider accountability frameworks rather than adding duplicative AI⏎mandates. HHS can recognize that healthcare organizations already operate under robust⏎clinical, safety, privacy, and liability regimes, and can emphasize organizational accountability⏎for outcomes rather than prescribing uniform, technology-specific controls or workflow⏎mandates.⏎3. Clarify that providers may determine appropriate levels of human oversight based on⏎risk and use case. HHS can affirm that healthcare organizations are well positioned to⏎assess when human-in-the-loop, human-on-the-loop, or human-out-of-the-loop AI is⏎appropriate, consistent with clinical risk, patient impact, and existing standards of care—⏎without default requirements for continuous human review.⏎⏎4. Consider modernizing interpretation of Section 1557 language access requirements for⏎written materials (with safeguards). HHS could consider clarifying that advanced AI-⏎enabled translation may be used for written patient materials without mandatory qualified⏎human post-editing when appropriate safeguards are met, including: (a) timely verbal⏎explanation from a qualified interpreter or qualified bilingual staff member, (b) clear notice in⏎the individual’s preferred language that the document was machine-translated and that⏎assistance or clarification is available upon request, and (c) continued provider responsibility⏎for meaningful access, accuracy, and patient understanding.⏎5. Encourage vendor transparency and performance substantiation to support⏎responsible adoption. HHS can develop regulatory guidance that requires AI developers⏎and vendors provide standardized information regarding model performance, limitations,⏎appropriate use cases, and monitoring requirements to enable responsible diligence and⏎adoption decisions by healthcare organizations.⏎6. Focus federal funding and pilots on healthcare deployers (with guardrails). HHS can⏎prioritize grants, demonstrations, or payment incentives that support healthcare organizations⏎implementing AI in real-world clinical settings, including safety-net providers, while limiting⏎exposure to fraud, waste, and abuse through deployer-focused design.⏎7. Clarify that federal policy does not impede responsible automation of documentation⏎and workflow. HHS can clarify that appropriately governed AI tools supporting clinical⏎documentation, care coordination, and administrative workflows are consistent with existing⏎health IT, billing, and information-sharing rules, reducing ambiguity that contributes to clinician⏎burden and access constraints.⏎8. Adopt principles-based, future-ready policy that scales across the healthcare⏎landscape. HHS can favor flexible, outcomes-focused approaches that can evolve as AI⏎capabilities advance so that smaller, rural, and safety-net providers—and the patients they⏎serve—are not disadvantaged by overly prescriptive or resource-intensive mandates.⏎Conclusion⏎In closing, Parkland Health’s decadelong experience deploying artificial intelligence demonstrates⏎that, when implemented responsibly within existing clinical, safety, and accountability frameworks,⏎AI can support improved patient outcomes, expand access to care, protect the workforce, and⏎reduce administrative burden. We value HHS’s efforts to solicit experience-based input through⏎this RFI and believe that thoughtful, evidence-based approaches promoting consistency, equity,⏎and responsible innovation can strengthen clinical care while maintaining patient trust and⏎accountability. Thank you for considering Parkland’s perspective as part of this process.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Joseph Longo, MBA, CDH-E, CHCIO⏎Senior Vice President/Chief Digital & Information Officer⏎Brett Moran, MD⏎Senior Vice President/Chief Health Officer⏎Parkland Health⏎5200 Harry Hines Blvd. | Dallas, TX 75235 214-⏎590-8000 | fax 214-590-8096⏎parklandhealth.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0388♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Jenkins, Amanda, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Jenkins♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a Licensed Clinical Professional Counselor in Illinois, I am very concerned about the use of AI in clinical care. This is a new technology that has not had time to be thoroughly tested. It is constantly evolving and could have ethical implications that we are not yet aware of. I already am inundated with advertisements to use AI in my practice, and feel very uncomfortable with its privacy risks and environmental impacts. I think it would be more sensible to truly understand this technology and its risks before implementing it.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0389♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Lumenex Advisory, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Lumenex Advisory♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted by:⏎Karina Lupercio⏎Founder & Principal Consultant⏎Lumenex Advisory LLC⏎Date: January 14, 2025⏎Introduction⏎I am submitting this response based on more than two decades of experience operating within⏎the U.S. healthcare ecosystem, with a focus on healthcare operations, data integration,⏎payment models, and technology implementation. My perspective is grounded less in theoretical⏎AI capability and more in the realities of how clinical, administrative, and financial systems⏎function together in practice.⏎While artificial intelligence holds significant promise for improving clinical outcomes, reducing⏎administrative burden, and lowering costs, adoption often stalls not because of model⏎performance, but because of structural, financial, and organizational friction. My comments⏎below focus on those friction points, specifically as they relate to private sector innovation and⏎reimbursement alignment.⏎Response to Question 1:⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎From an operational standpoint, the most significant barriers to AI adoption in clinical care are⏎not technical. They are organizational, administrative, and incentive-based.⏎Ownership and accountability for adoption are often unclear. AI initiatives are frequently treated⏎as IT implementations, when in reality they require coordinated change across clinical,⏎operational, compliance, and administrative teams. When responsibility for workflow redesign⏎and ongoing maintenance is diffused, adoption stalls even when the technology itself performs⏎as intended.⏎A recurring barrier is resistance driven by perceived increases in workload. In my experience⏎implementing large-scale provider data management systems prior to the availability of modern⏎AI tools, downstream teams often resisted adoption because new workflows were viewed as⏎“extra steps.” The long-term value of structured data capture, namely, the ability to generate⏎reliable, high-quality reporting, was not immediately visible to end users. While leadership often⏎desired improved analytics and insights, insufficient attention was paid to explaining how those⏎outcomes depended on consistent, incremental changes in daily workflows.⏎1⏎⏎Over time, these steps typically became easier and more efficient, but early resistance⏎significantly slowed adoption. AI magnifies this dynamic: without deliberate workflow design,⏎education, and change management, AI tools risk being perceived as additive burden rather⏎than enabling infrastructure.⏎The most common point of failure is the handoff to end users. When cultural transformation and⏎workflow ownership are not addressed explicitly, even well-designed systems fail to achieve⏎sustained use. This is not a technology failure, but an implementation and governance one.⏎Response to Question 2:⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎Misaligned incentives and uncertainty around value recognition remain significant obstacles to⏎AI adoption.⏎Payment models rarely account for the trust, education, and governance work required for⏎adoption. Many AI tools deliver value by improving efficiency, reducing administrative burden, or⏎augmenting clinician decision-making rather than producing discrete billable events. As a result,⏎organizations often struggle to justify investment when reimbursement pathways are unclear or⏎nonexistent.⏎Even relatively narrow AI tools face adoption hurdles when trust is not addressed. For example,⏎implementing AI-enabled note-taking tools in healthcare settings frequently raises concerns⏎about privacy, recording, and data usage. Despite conducting due diligence to ensure SOC 2,⏎HIPAA, and GDPR compliance, adoption was initially limited until clear guardrails were⏎established around how, when, and for what purpose the tools would be used. Education and⏎transparency were essential to building confidence and enabling responsible use.⏎HHS could accelerate adoption by aligning reimbursement and program design with real-world⏎implementation costs. Incentives that recognize not only clinical outcomes but also the⏎operational effort required to safely deploy AI, including training, governance, and workflow⏎integration, would reduce adoption risk. Pilot programs, demonstration models, or value-based⏎payment structures that explicitly accommodate AI-enabled efficiency gains would encourage⏎experimentation while generating evidence to inform broader policy.⏎Conclusion⏎AI adoption in clinical care will not accelerate through technical innovation alone. It requires⏎alignment across organizational governance, administrative capacity, and financial incentives.⏎By focusing on incentive alignment, reimbursement clarity, and reduction of administrative⏎friction, HHS has an opportunity to enable meaningful, responsible adoption that delivers real⏎value to patients, clinicians, and the healthcare system as a whole.⏎2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0390♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Paragon Health Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Paragon Health Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street, SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted via Regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Principal Deputy Posnack:⏎On behalf of Paragon Health Institute, we respectfully submit these comments in response⏎to the Department of Health and Human Services’ Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. This RFI has explicitly⏎sought public comment on what measures the agency can implement “to establish a⏎forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and⏎use of AI as part of clinical care.” Our response reflects not only Paragon’s research and⏎publications on AI but also our conviction that this important technology has significant⏎potential to reduce American health care expenditures.⏎HHS should declare increased AI deployment in U.S. health care a national priority.⏎However, AI adoption itself is affected by many conditions. Paragon has organized our⏎feedback around five:⏎• Health expenditures, deficits, and the national debt⏎• Reimbursement⏎• Complementary research⏎• Regulatory evolution⏎• Increasing AI autonomy⏎Health Expenditures, Deficits, and the National Debt⏎AI-enabled medical devices have attracted considerable press regarding feats such as early⏎cancer detection and predictions of illness years before symptoms manifest, but their⏎promise to reduce American health care expenditures is perhaps an even greater priority if⏎the nation is to sustain health care access and affordability for our citizens. The United⏎States’ national health expenditure (NHE) is enormous, and this spending represents a⏎considerable burden for families and American businesses as well as a fiscal threat to⏎competing non-health care expenditures within the federal budget.⏎1⏎Reforming government. Empowering patients.⏎703.527.2734 | paragoninstitute.org⏎PO Box 3612, Arlington, VA 22203⏎⏎In 2024, the most recent year of reporting, NHE reached $5.3 trillion, which represented 18⏎percent of Gross Domestic Product (GDP).1 The Organisation for Economic Co-operation and⏎Development (OECD) has compared the United States’ NHE as a share of GDP to more than⏎three dozen countries across the world.⏎In 2024, the United States spent by far the most on health…well above Germany, the⏎next highest spender (at 12.3%). These are followed by a group of around 15⏎countries that all allocated 10‑12% of their GDP to healthcare. In many of the Central⏎and Eastern European OECD countries, as well as in the newer OECD Member⏎countries in Latin America, spending on health represented between 6‑9% of their⏎GDP.2⏎Recent government research projects that, during the 10-year period from 2024 to 2033,⏎our nation’s average NHE growth (5.8 percent) will outpace average GDP growth (4.3⏎percent), raising health care spending as a percentage of GDP from 18 percent to a⏎projected 20.3 percent.3 Moreover, the estimate of NHE spending growth in the next decade⏎is made all the more concerning by the trillions in increased national debt projected for the⏎coming decade.4 Congressional Budget Office (CBO) Director Phillip Swagel, commenting⏎on the deficits and national debt during this period, stated, “Our budget projections⏎continue to indicate that the fiscal trajectory is not sustainable.”5 Likewise, back in 2009,⏎when NHE as a share of GDP was 17.2 percent,6 the Social Security Advisory Board warned⏎that the nation’s health care cost trajectory was “unsustainable” and “perhaps the most⏎significant threat to the long-term economic security of workers and retirees.”7⏎It is within the context of these NHE sustainability concerns that AI emerges as a promising⏎means to lower health care spending. The probabilistic nature of (most) AI combined with⏎the processing of large data resources has opened the door to predicting disease and⏎beginning medical interventions that are significantly less expensive than would be the case⏎when disease is detected at a later stage. While not its only way to lower health care costs,8⏎“predict and prevent” could be transformative for American health care. By positioning AI as⏎a means to make health care more affordable, its adoption and use will accelerate.⏎1 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, "NHE Fact Sheet," https://www.cms.gov/data-research/statistics-trends-and-⏎reports/national-health-expenditure-data/nhe-fact-sheet.⏎2 Organisation for Economic Co-operation and Development, "Health at a Glance 2025," November 13, 2025,⏎https://www.oecd.org/en/publications/2025/11/health-at-a-glance-2025_a894f72e/full-report/health-expenditure-in-relation-⏎to-gdp_6e4c2773.html.⏎3 CMS, “NHE Fact Sheet.” Data sourced from the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services’ Office of the Actuary, the U.S.⏎Department of Commerce’s Bureau of Economic Analysis, and the U.S. Bureau of the Census.⏎4 Congressional Budget Office, "The Budget and Economic Outlook: 2026 to 2036," February 11, 2026,⏎https://www.cbo.gov/publication/61882.⏎5 CBO, “Director's Statement on the Budget and Economic Outlook for 2026 to 2036,” February 11, 2026,⏎https://www.cbo.gov/publication/62050.⏎6 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, "Historical," https://www.cms.gov/data-research/statistics-trends-and-⏎reports/national-health-expenditure-data/historical.⏎7 Social Security Advisory Board, “The Unsustainable Cost of Health Care,” September 8, 2009, https://s3-us-gov-west-⏎1.amazonaws.com/cg-778536a2-e58c-44f1-9173-29749804ec54/uploads/2023/12/2009-⏎TheUnsustainableCostofHealthCare_2009.pdf.⏎8 Other substantial ways AI could help lower health care costs include the detection of fraud, waste, and abuse in medical⏎claims as well as productivity improvements in health care administration.⏎2⏎Reforming government. Empowering patients.⏎703.527.2734 | paragoninstitute.org⏎PO Box 3612, Arlington, VA 22203⏎⏎Reimbursement⏎AI’s “predict and prevent” paradigm is dissimilar to the dominant “detect and treat”⏎approach to health care. A major concern associated with this difference is the possibility⏎that predictive AI technology will encounter insurer resistance with respect to⏎reimbursement. Without strong prospects for reimbursement, device manufacturers will be⏎cautious about investing in new AI solutions given ROI worries. Conversely, positive⏎reimbursement prospects can accelerate AI adoption across the American health care⏎system. HHS should continue its efforts to eliminate government obstacles and⏎disincentives for the use of AI for which there is a market demand from patients, providers,⏎and related parties. This endeavor necessarily includes consideration of how current CMS⏎Medicare policies can be clarified and improved in ways that facilitate AI adoption and its⏎related savings potential while discouraging any net increase in federal outlays. These⏎policies include New Technology Add-On Payments9 and Transitional Pass-Through⏎Payments,10 which provide transitional supplemental hospital payments for new⏎technologies; the HCPCS coding process run by CMS, which includes the AMA's CPT codes;⏎and coverage processes, including local and national coverage decisions as well as the⏎Transitional Coverage for Emerging Technologies11 pathway. Any new HHS policies relating⏎to AI payment, coding, and coverage should reflect a commitment to using AI innovation to⏎bolster fiscal responsibility and health care affordability.⏎Complementary Research⏎HHS’ promotion of AI should include funding for research studies quantifying the economic⏎value of AI technologies that predict disease before the manifestation of symptoms.⏎Insurers, for their part, are more likely to expand AI reimbursement practices in the⏎presence of compelling economic arguments for AI utilization. Complementary HHS⏎research initiatives should explore how clinical AI tools can empower lower-cost medical⏎staff (nurse practitioners and physician assistants) to perform more services safely at a⏎lower expense for patients. Most importantly, these collective learnings on AI-facilitated⏎cost reductions should be instantiated in a real-world research pilot whose exclusive aim is⏎to deliver health care at 80 percent of the cost without lowering quality.12 If AI is not⏎approached with such an explicit government aspiration for cost savings and a⏎corresponding project to validate this vision, the technology is more likely to become one⏎more incremental expense within an increasingly unaffordable health care system.⏎9 Morey, Jacob R, et al, "Adoption and Trends in the Medicare New Technology Add-On Payment Program," J Gen Internal⏎Medicine, June 24, 2020, https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8298651/⏎9 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, “Transitional Coverage for Emerging Technologies,”⏎https://www.cms.gov/files/document/tcet-drop-article.pdf.⏎10 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, “Process and Information Required to Apply for Additional Device Categories⏎for Transitional Pass-Through Payment Status Under the Hospital Outpatient Prospective Payment System,”⏎https://www.cms.gov/Medicare/Medicare-Fee-for-Service-Payment/HospitalOutpatientPPS/Downloads/catapp.pdf.⏎11 Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, “Transitional Coverage for Emerging Technologies,”⏎https://www.cms.gov/files/document/tcet-drop-article.pdf.⏎12 For a more detailed proposal of an analogous concept, see Kev Coleman, "Could the VA Be the Key to Lowering the Cost of⏎American Health Care?" Paragon Health Institute, July 16, 2025, https://paragoninstitute.org/public-health/could-the-va-be-the-⏎key-to-lowering-the-cost-of-american-health-care/.⏎3⏎Reforming government. Empowering patients.⏎703.527.2734 | paragoninstitute.org⏎PO Box 3612, Arlington, VA 22203⏎⏎Regulatory Evolution⏎With respect to regulation, HHS should consider state lawmakers’ concerns about health⏎care AI and collaborate on solutions that will prevent a patchwork of differing state rules for⏎AI-enabled medical devices from emerging. Such an inconsistent patchwork of rules would⏎inflate AI medical costs and potentially delay patient access to life-saving technology.⏎HHS should also find ways to support the FDA as it adapts its medical device approval⏎processes to the realities of AI, including articulating the principles that should be reflected⏎in FDA rulemaking. The historic FDA categories governing AI-enabled medical devices,⏎Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) and Software in a Medical Device (SiMD), were⏎developed during a period characterized by deterministic software. Probabilistic reasoning⏎models, continuous post-deployment system learning, generalization uncertainty, and⏎emergent capabilities require ongoing FDA oversight to evolve,13 particularly if the U.S. is to⏎maintain its international leadership in health care AI.⏎Increasing AI Autonomy⏎Of particular importance in the consideration of AI regulation is the prospect of AI-enabled⏎medical devices becoming more autonomous over time. It is reasonable to assume some AI⏎will offer self-service to a patient (without any health care provider assistance) while⏎maintaining safety equal to or surpassing that of health care professionals providing the⏎same services. In instances where a medical service can be performed independently of a⏎clinician, a sizable expense within health care delivery is eliminated. Because medical⏎devices aren’t licensed by individual states, there is also the future possibility of a subset of⏎AI-enabled health care devices (like medical image interpretation devices) competing online⏎for patients across the nation, giving alternatives to regions dominated by a consolidated⏎hospital system and lowering prices through market competition. Such online competition⏎bodes well for consumer utilization of health care AI.⏎Autonomous care—the delivery of a medical service via a self-service system a consumer⏎uses without clinician assistance—is not unique to AI. For years, pharmacies have offered⏎simplistic blood pressure machines for unassisted consumer use, with some systems having⏎additional functionality such as body mass index calculation or vision evaluation. AI’s⏎differentiation is the sophistication of its software and the additional services it enables.⏎Compared to productivity gains and quality improvements, gains from autonomous AI⏎medical services may have the best long-term potential for material reductions in health⏎care expenses, including labor costs.⏎HHS should formally state it is supportive of autonomous AI where such solutions can be⏎empirically demonstrated to match or exceed both the quality and accuracy observed for⏎human physicians. Additionally, given the continual evolution of AI, HHS should encourage⏎13 See Kev Coleman, “Healthcare AI Regulation: Guidelines for Maintaining Public Safety and Innovation,” Paragon Health⏎Institute, December, 2024, https://paragoninstitute.org/private-health/healthcare-ai-regulation/.⏎4⏎Reforming government. Empowering patients.⏎703.527.2734 | paragoninstitute.org⏎PO Box 3612, Arlington, VA 22203⏎⏎the FDA to provide an economical pathway for innovators to reapply for FDA approval on⏎their devices where the functionality remains the same, but system autonomy increases⏎over time. On this front, the FDA could leverage work already performed by the U.S.⏎Department of Transportation for self-driving vehicles with differing levels of system⏎autonomy (e.g., driver-assistance vs self-driving).14 Such efforts will ultimately expand AI⏎adoption from exclusively clinical contexts to the wider ecosystem of American health care.⏎Conclusion⏎The pace of AI adoption and use will significantly benefit from leadership on the part of⏎HHS. Paragon Health Institute hopes to support the agency in its efforts on this front.⏎Sincerely,⏎Kev Coleman⏎Director of the Health Care AI Initiative at Paragon Health Institute⏎Brian Blase⏎President, Paragon Health Institute⏎14 National Highway Traffic Safety Administration, “Standing General Order on Crash Reporting,” https://www.nhtsa.gov/laws-⏎regulations/standing-general-order-crash-reporting.⏎5⏎Reforming government. Empowering patients.⏎703.527.2734 | paragoninstitute.org⏎PO Box 3612, Arlington, VA 22203♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0391♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Ophthalmology , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Ophthalmology♔♔♔♔♔♔20 F Street NW⏎Suite 400⏎Washington, DC⏎20001-6701⏎P.O. Box 7424⏎San Francisco, CA⏎94120-7424⏎February 23, 2026⏎T: +1 202.737.6662⏎aao.org⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW⏎Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: (RIN 0955-AA13) Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The American Academy of Ophthalmology (the Academy) appreciates the opportunity to submit⏎comments on the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎The Academy is the largest association of eye physicians and surgeons in the United States. A⏎nationwide community of over 20,000 medical doctors, we protect sight and empower lives by⏎setting the standards for ophthalmic education, supporting research, and advocating for our⏎patients and the public. We innovate to advance our profession and to ensure the delivery of the⏎highest-quality eye care.⏎We commend the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) and ASTP/ONC for⏎seeking stakeholder input on promoting the adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎Ophthalmology has long led AI innovation with tools already expanding screening access and⏎enabling earlier detection of sight-threatening disease. The Academy is eager to provide feedback⏎and looks forward to working with federal agencies to help scale these AI-driven tools responsibly⏎and advance patient care and vision research.⏎⏎Question 2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical⏎care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎There have been significant advances in the use of AI for improving screening, access and early detection⏎of diabetic retinopathy (DR). DR is a common complication of type 1 and type 2 diabetes and is a⏎preventable cause of blindness when detected and treated early.1 DR is the leading cause of new cases of⏎legal blindness among working-age Americans.2 Data from 2019 indicates that 11.8 percent of adults with⏎diagnosed diabetes reported severe vision difficulty or blindness.3 4⏎Vision loss from DR is highly preventable with timely screening and access to treatment, as current⏎therapeutic strategies are effective in preventing severe vision loss.5 Despite this, too many patients with⏎diabetes still do not receive the recommended DR screening at the onset of their diabetes and during⏎the subsequent years. In many cases patients have been unable unwilling to have an in-person⏎recommended comprehensive eye exam.⏎Recent advances in autonomous AI systems approved by the U.S. Food and Drug Administration have⏎demonstrated the ability to meaningfully close this gap in quality care. Currently available imaging tools⏎can screen for referable diabetic retinopathy with high sensitivity and specificity and nearly 100 percent⏎negative predictive value. AI-based retinal screening tools can be deployed in primary care settings to⏎greatly expand access to screening and identify patients who need timely referral to an ophthalmologist.6⏎7,8⏎Expanding the use of validated AI-based retinal screening tools can substantially increase the rate of early⏎detection of diabetic retinopathy for the population, enabling timely referral and preventing avoidable⏎vision loss. To realize the full benefit of these tools, HHS should prioritize efforts to increase awareness,⏎adoption, and appropriate use of validated AI retinal screening tools to improve DR screening rates and⏎reduce preventable vision loss among beneficiaries with diabetes.⏎Ensure payment policy keeps pace with autonomous AI screening⏎One way that HHS can support broader adoption of these technologies is through payment policy. The⏎Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) currently provides physician fee schedule pricing for⏎CPT code 92229 (Imaging of retina for detection or monitoring of disease; point-of-care automated⏎analysis and report, unilateral or bilateral), rather than utilizing carrier pricing. CMS has appropriately⏎1 Klein, B. E. (2007). Overview of epidemiologic studies of diabetic retinopathy. Ophthalmic Epidemiology, 14, 179–183.⏎2 Kempen, J. H., O’Colmain, B. J., Leske, M. C., et al. (2004). The prevalence of diabetic retinopathy among adults in the United States. Archives of⏎Ophthalmology, 122, 552–563.⏎3 Lundeen, E. A., Saydah, S., Ehrlich, J. R., & Saaddine, J. (2022). Self-reported vision impairment and psychological distress in U.S. adults. Ophthalmic⏎Epidemiology, 29, 171–181.⏎4 Tonnies, T., Brinks, R., Isom, S., et al. (2023). Projections of type 1 and type 2 diabetes burden in the U.S. population aged <20 years through 2060: The⏎SEARCH for Diabetes in Youth Study. Diabetes Care, 46, 313–320.⏎5 Lim, J. I., Kim, S. J., Bailey, S. T., Kovach, J. L., Vemulakonda, G. A., Ying, G. S., Flaxel, C. J., & American Academy of Ophthalmology Preferred Practice⏎Pattern Retina/Vitreous Committee. (2025). Diabetic retinopathy preferred practice pattern®. Ophthalmology, 132(4), P75–P162.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ophtha.2024.12.020⏎6 Abramoff, M. D., Lavin, P. T., Birch, M., et al. (2018). Pivotal trial of an autonomous AI-based diagnostic system for detection of diabetic retinopathy in⏎primary care offices. NPJ Digital Medicine, 1, Article 39.⏎7 Shah, A., Clarida, W., Amelon, R., et al. (2021). Validation of automated screening for referable diabetic retinopathy with an autonomous diagnostic⏎artificial intelligence system in a Spanish population. Journal of Diabetes Science and Technology, 15, 655–663.⏎8 Ipp, E., Liljenquist, D., Bode, B., et al. (2021). Pivotal evaluation of an artificial intelligence system for autonomous detection of referrable and vision-⏎threatening diabetic retinopathy. JAMA Network Open, 4, e2134254.⏎2⏎⏎recognized that practitioners incur significant ongoing resource costs associated with the software and⏎infrastructure necessary to furnish this service.⏎We were encouraged that CMS requested stakeholder feedback on how modern medical technologies and⏎services, such as Software as a Service (SaaS) and AI may be accurately valued in the Calendar Year 2026⏎Medicare Physician Fee Schedule Proposed Rule. As explained in our comment letter on that proposed⏎rule, the image analysis fee is a direct practice cost analogous to any other supply because it is attributable⏎to a specific imaging service provided to a specific patient each time the imaging is performed. It is not an⏎indirect cost like computer hardware or software that is purchased once or licensed annually or monthly⏎and then utilized repeatedly to provide varied services to multiple patients.⏎As the agency evaluates how to appropriately and accurately reimburse AI-enabled services, the⏎Academy recommends that CMS consider and label such technology as a separate and new category⏎within direct practice expense valuation.⏎Expanding diabetic retinopathy screening in FQHCs through AI-based tools⏎Federally Qualified Health Centers (FQHCs) are a vital resource for low-income and medically underserved⏎communities, and their role in the health care system continues to grow. Between 2000 and 2018, the⏎number of people receiving care at FQHCs increased by 196 percent, making FQHCs a main source of care⏎for one in 12 Americans.9 Yet few FQHCs currently offer screening for sight-threatening eye disease.⏎Expanding diabetic retinopathy screening in FQHCs using validated AI-based technology presents a major⏎opportunity to increase screening rates and reach patients who are least likely to receive timely eye care.⏎In 2024, the Academy launched an AI screening pilot program at Henrietta Johnson Medical Center in⏎Delaware. In its first year, more than 30 percent of patients with a gradable image showed signs of⏎diabetic eye disease requiring further ophthalmic evaluation. For many of these patients, screening at the⏎FQHC was their first diabetic eye screening. These early results demonstrate how autonomous AI⏎screening can identify disease earlier, trigger timely referral, and help prevent treatable vision loss. The⏎Academy plans to invest in additional programs and would welcome the opportunity to collaborate with⏎HHS on expanding access to AI screening in FQHCs across the country building on our pilot projects.⏎CMS also has a strong opportunity to improve access to sight-saving AI-based technology by supporting⏎separate payment for CPT 92229 in FQHCs and Rural Health Centers. There is clear precedent for this⏎policy. Glaucoma screening services, HCPCS codes G0117 and G0118, may be reimbursed as stand-alone⏎billable visits for FQHCs and RHCs when no other services are furnished on the same day.10 Extending⏎similar payment policy to autonomous AI-based DR screening would improve access for underserved⏎populations at high risk for vision loss.⏎Quality measures remain essential as screening gaps persist⏎Another way that HHS can promote the adoption of AI technologies is through the continuing use of⏎quality measures. Substantial data demonstrate that diabetic retinopathy screening remains an⏎unresolved gap in necessary medical care. According to the 2026 Medicare Part C and D Star Ratings⏎Technical Notes, the Diabetes Care, Eye Exam measure had a numeric average of 77% and a Star Rating⏎average of 3.4 out of 5 stars, showing that DR screening remains an active gap, not a solved problem.⏎Similarly, data from the National Health Interview Survey found that only 66% of adults with diabetes had⏎9 Sharac, J., Shin, P., Rosenbaum, S., & Handarov, T. (2019, September). Community health centers continue steady growth, but challenges loom (Policy⏎Issue Brief No. 60). Geiger Gibson/RCHN Community Health Foundation Research Collaborative. Retrieved February 17, 2026, from⏎https://geigergibson.publichealth.gwu.edu/60-community-health-centers-continue-steady-growth-challenges-loom⏎10 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. (2021, April 26). Medicare Benefit Policy Manual Chapter 13⏎3⏎⏎a yearly eye examination in the past year, demonstrating little or no detectable change compared to prior⏎years.11⏎CMS uses quality measures across its quality and payment programs to assess and incentivize high quality⏎care, including measures that track diabetic retinopathy screening rates among patients with diabetes.⏎Examples include Measure #117, Diabetes: Eye Exam, in the Merit-based Incentive Payment System⏎program and the Diabetes Care, Eye Exam measure in the Medicare Advantage (MA) Star Ratings.⏎These measures provide essential ongoing education for physicians and patients on the importance of⏎timely DR screening and establish incentives that protect Medicare beneficiaries from preventable vision⏎loss. Accordingly, the Academy strongly encourages HHS and CMS to retain these measures and⏎continue using them to drive improvements in diabetes eye disease screening rates and early detection.⏎Despite the ongoing need for these performance measures, CMS has recently proposed removing the⏎Diabetes Care, Eye Exam measure from the MA Star Ratings as part of an effort to streamline the measure⏎set. The Academy strongly opposes this proposal, as eliminating this measure would remove a key⏎incentive for MA plans and clinicians to prioritize diabetic retinopathy screening and referral. This⏎change risks delayed detection of disease, increased preventable vision loss, and higher long-term costs⏎for patients and the Medicare program.⏎Question 8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability is essential to accelerating the development, validation, and deployment of AI in⏎ophthalmology, particularly for imaging-based applications. Ophthalmology is uniquely positioned to⏎benefit from interoperability improvements because the specialty relies heavily on high-resolution imaging⏎modalities such as optical coherence tomography, fundus photography/imaging, and retinal angiography.⏎These imaging data are foundational to both clinical decision-making and AI model development.⏎The Academy believes that Digital Imaging and Communications in Medicine (DICOM) is the foundational⏎standard for ocular imaging interoperability. DICOM enables consistent capture of patient demographics,⏎device metadata, acquisition parameters, and discrete quantitative measurements alongside image data.⏎Broad adoption of DICOM is critical not only for safe and efficient clinical workflows, but also for enabling⏎large-scale aggregation of high-quality, standardized imaging data.⏎Access to large, diverse, and well-annotated datasets is a requirement for developing robust AI models for⏎clinical care. Interoperable imaging standards such as DICOM make it possible to aggregate data across⏎health systems, vendors, and care settings, thereby reducing data fragmentation and enabling the scale⏎required for AI training, external validation, and post-market monitoring.12⏎Despite these benefits, DICOM compliance remains low among ophthalmic imaging manufacturers and is a⏎significant obstacle to achieving fully integrated digital workflows in ophthalmology.13 Manufacturers⏎remain hesitant to participate in the standards development process or to implement existing standards in⏎their products due to concerns over the increased costs associated with DICOM compliance, the limited⏎understanding among physicians regarding the benefits of DICOM, and the absence of regulatory⏎11 U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, Office of Disease Prevention and Health Promotion. (n.d.). Increase the proportion of adults with⏎diabetes who have a yearly eye exam (D-04). Healthy People 2030. Retrieved January 6, 2026, from https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/objectives-⏎and-data/browse-objectives/diabetes/increase-proportion-adults-diabetes-who-have-yearly-eye-exam-d-04⏎12 Gim, N., Ferguson, A. N., Blazes, M., Lee, C. S., & Lee, A. Y. (2025). The march to harmonized imaging standards for retinal imaging. Progress in Retinal⏎and Eye Research, 107, 101363. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.preteyeres.2025.101363⏎13 Goetz, K. E., Reed, A. A., Chiang, M. F., Keane, T., Tripathi, M., Ng, E., Nguyen, T., & Eydelman, M. (2024). Accelerating care: A roadmap to⏎interoperable ophthalmic imaging standards in the United States. Ophthalmology, 131(1), 12–15. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ophtha.2023.10.001⏎4⏎⏎mandates requiring adherence to these standards. Additionally, the lack of third-party certification for⏎DICOM conformance statements raises concerns about the extent of comprehensive compliance across⏎the industry.14⏎To overcome these barriers, The Academy encourages ASTP/ONC and other federal agencies to take⏎steps within their authority to accelerate imaging interoperability in ophthalmology, including exploring⏎innovative approaches that promote compliance with DICOM standards. These standards are crucial to⏎enabling digital workflows, supporting the sharing of high-quality images with patients, and advancing⏎eye and vision research.⏎In response to these challenges, the National Eye Institute, Food and Drug Administration, and ASTP/ONC⏎demonstrated their shared commitment to advancing DICOM standards by convening a workshop in 2022⏎aimed at incentivizing manufacturers to adopt these standards. 15 While this was a promising step,⏎additional efforts are still needed to establish levers, benefits, and incentives that ensure stronger⏎stakeholder and manufacturer support for the implementation of DICOM standards.⏎Conclusion⏎If you have questions or need any additional information regarding any portion of these comments, please⏎contact Brandy Keys, MPH, Director of Health Policy at bkeys@aao.org or via phone at 202-587-5815. We⏎look forward to ongoing engagement and stakeholder input.⏎Sincerely,⏎Michael X. Repka, MD, MBA⏎Medical Director, Governmental Affairs⏎American Academy of Ophthalmology⏎14 Lee, A. Y., Sadda, S., Lum, F., et al. (2022). Joint American Academy of Ophthalmology and Association for Research in Vision and Ophthalmology⏎policy statement: The time for digital imaging standards implementation is now. Ophthalmology, 129(11), 1229–1231.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ophtha.2022.07.034⏎15 Weixelbaum, J., Haqqani, S., Reed, A., & Goetz, K. (2022). NEI-FDA-ONC joint workshop on promoting adoption of ocular imaging standards summary.⏎Zenodo. https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.6915785⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0392♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from King & Spalding LLP, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔King & Spalding LLP♔♔♔♔♔♔King & Spalding LLP⏎1700 Pennsylvania Ave, NW⏎Suite 200⏎Washington, D.C. 20006-4707⏎Tel: +1 202 737 0500⏎Fax: +1 202 626 3737⏎www.kslaw.com⏎Adam C. Solander⏎Partner⏎Direct Dial: +1 202 626 5542⏎Direct Fax: +1 202 626 3737⏎asolander@kslaw.com⏎February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎RIN: 0955-AA13⏎SUBMITTED ELECTRONICALLY (HTTP://WWW.REGULATIONS.GOV)⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎We write to the Department of Health and Human Services (“HHS”) on behalf of a digital health⏎and medical device client. We appreciate the opportunity to submit comments on the Request for⏎Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎Introduction⏎On the eve of the first internet era, lawmakers recognized the necessity of ensuring the free flow⏎of information within the health care industry. Congress passed the Health Insurance Portability⏎and Accountability Act (“HIPAA”) in 1996 to accomplish three goals: ensure continuity of⏎individual insurance coverage, protect patient agency with respect to their protected health⏎information (“PHI”), and streamline the electronic exchange of health information.1 By⏎emphasizing portability of care, coverage, and data, HIPAA laid the groundwork for a more⏎efficient and interconnected health care ecosystem.⏎Thirty years later, we are in the midst of another remarkable period of technological innovation⏎spurred by artificial intelligence and machine learning technologies. But decisive regulatory action⏎is once again necessary to ensure the free flow of information. Doing so will continue to catalyze⏎1 See William J. Clinton, Statement on Signing the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996, Online⏎by Gerhard Peters and John T. Woolley, The American Presidency Project⏎https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu/node/222651 (Aug. 21, 1996).⏎⏎Page 2 of 8⏎a diverse and competitive marketplace where AI entrepreneurship is rewarded and better health⏎outcomes are delivered.⏎Today, certain industry actors are seeking to consolidate power and ownership of health data by⏎exploiting the rules, policies, and terms of access to Health Information Exchanges (“HIEs”) and⏎Qualified Health Information Networks (“QHINs”). Tools clearly meant to promote information⏎sharing are being used to lock out digital health and medical device companies on the baseless⏎grounds that these entities cannot provide “treatment” or “health care” as understood under⏎HIPAA, with the implicit connotation that treatment is the exclusive domain of hospitals and⏎physician practices, and their electronic health record vendors. This rationale completely rewrites⏎HIPAA and the 21st Century Cures Act and ignores the policy goals of each core piece of⏎legislation.⏎Data is the lifeblood of AI innovation. Health data access should not be something that plays out⏎in private interest business litigation, closed-door arbitrations, or dense pages of HIE participation⏎rules and agreements—it belongs in the hands of the patient. Indeed, HHS recognizes that patients⏎should be able to “access their electronic medical information anywhere that it lives on the⏎network” and that health apps should “not require any special status to be on the network.”2⏎Moreover, such access should occur “seamlessly,” without requiring a patient to jump through⏎unnecessary hoops or “repeatedly recall and write out their medical history.”3 Restricting digital⏎health and medical device companies’ access to HIEs not only stifles AI advancement and⏎disregards the key role these companies play in patients’ day-to-day lives; it eschews bedrock⏎principles of patient access and autonomy.⏎Accordingly, HHS should protect digital health and medical device companies’ ability to⏎participate in HIEs by requiring QHINs to admit to HIEs those companies that directly interact⏎with patients and provide “treatment” as that term is and has been long understood under HIPAA.⏎This can and should permit a wide variety of actors to participate in the health data community in⏎order to execute HHS’s salient policy goals of patient access and empowerment, accelerating the⏎use of AI in health care, and reducing red tape and anticompetitive barriers to marketplace⏎innovation.⏎I. Regulatory Background⏎HIPAA⏎With the passage of HIPAA, Congress directed HHS to standardize the format for the exchange of⏎health information. Standardization improves the accuracy and reliability of health information,⏎reduces administrative overhead and cost associated with competing transmission formats, and⏎enhances the coordination of care between health care entities.4 More than just cost savings, HHS⏎2 Interoperability Framework, CTRS. FOR MEDICARE & MEDICAID SERVS., https://www.cms.gov/health-technology-⏎ecosystem/interoperability-framework (last modified July 31, 2025).⏎3 Patient Facing Apps – Kill the Clipboard, CTRS. FOR MEDICARE & MEDICAID SERVS., https://www.cms.gov/health-⏎tech-ecosystem/early-adopters/kill-the-clipboard (last modified February 17, 2026).⏎4 67 Fed. Reg. 53182, 53182 (Aug. 14, 2002).⏎2⏎⏎Page 3 of 8⏎recognized that accurate, efficient, and effective data exchanges drive better health outcomes for⏎patients and improve the delivery of care.5⏎Moreover, an effective data exchange would meet the needs of a health data community that was⏎no longer a closed, “one-to-one interaction[] between patients and clinicians, [but rather an]⏎integrated health care delivery network . . . .”6 HHS understood that any data exchange must⏎encourage and grow with a diverse community of health care actors. Specifically, the standards⏎for data exchange would need to “incorporate flexibility to adapt more easily to changes in the⏎health care infrastructure (such as new services, organizations, and provider types) and information⏎technology.”7⏎These themes of innovation and interconnectivity permeate HIPAA. HHS emphasizes that⏎whether an organization is a HIPAA–covered entity is “based on activities and functions” and “not⏎titles.”8 Reinforcing this position, HHS identifies several types of non-traditional entities,⏎including “pharmaceutical manufacturers” and “online companies” that may qualify as covered⏎entities, provided that they meet the statutory definition of “health care provider” and furnish, bill,⏎or are paid for “health care.”9 HHS, in February 2004, further illustrated this role-based, flexible⏎standard by explicitly recognizing that medical device companies may receive PHI for treatment⏎purposes.10 HHS acknowledges that these core HIPAA definitions cannot serve to gatekeep data⏎and stymie progress, but rather must be firmly rooted in function in order to accommodate⏎technological innovation.11⏎TEFCA⏎Created under the 21st Century Cures Act, the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common⏎Agreement (“TEFCA”) built upon HIPAA’s data exchange framework by establishing a set of⏎standards issued by the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology to promote health data interoperability. This⏎public-private initiative established the HIEs and the supporting QHINs to realize the policy goal⏎5 65 Fed. Reg. 82462, 82465 (Dec. 28, 2000) (“Use of electronic information has helped to speed the delivery of⏎effective care and the processing of billions of dollars worth of health care claims. Greater use of electronic data has⏎also increased our ability to identify and treat those who are at risk for disease, conduct vital research, detect fraud⏎and abuse, and measure and improve the quality of care delivered in the U.S.”).⏎6 Id. at 82465.⏎7 63 Fed. Reg. 43242, 43244 (Aug. 12, 1998).⏎8 65 Fed. Reg. at 82568.⏎9 Id. at 82574.⏎10 HHS FAQs, “When may a covered health care provider disclose protected health information, without an⏎authorization or business associate agreement, to a medical device company representative?” (last updated Aug. 8,⏎2005), available at: https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/faq/490/when-may-a-covered-health-care-provider-⏎disclose-protected-health-information-without-authorization/index.html; HHS and CardioNet, Inc. Resolution⏎Agreement, HHS (April 3, 2017), available at: https://www.hhs.gov/sites/default/files/cardionet-ra-cap.pdf (finding⏎that a cardiac monitoring device company was a covered entity).⏎11 See 63 Fed. Reg. 25320, 25321, 25325 (May 7, 1998).⏎3⏎⏎Page 4 of 8⏎of health data interoperability and continue to promote underlying objectives of patient care and⏎choice.12⏎Health care providers, covered entities, and health IT developers with a valid purpose may access⏎PHI via TEFCA. These purposes are treatment, payment, health care operations, public health,⏎government benefits determinations, and individual access services. Entities must share data for⏎treatment purposes but may share data for other purposes, like payment or health care operations.⏎This dynamic echoes HIPAA’s long-standing protection of the free flow of information for⏎covered entities’ treatment purposes.13⏎TEFCA SOP Changes⏎The Common Agreement for Nationwide Health Information Interoperability (“Common⏎Agreement”) governs data access to HIEs. TEFCA, through its Recognized Coordinating Entity⏎(“RCE”), and QHINs are signatories to the Common Agreement, but the Common Agreement⏎includes flow-down terms such that all HIE participants must agree to substantially the same⏎language. The Common Agreement contemplates change management provisions and⏎differentiates between changes to the Common Agreement and to Standard Operating Procedures,⏎or those rules that govern participants’ access to the QHINs.14⏎In July 2024, TEFCA released Standard Operating Procedure (SOP): Exchange Purpose (XP)⏎Implementation: Treatment (“Treatment SOP”). The Treatment SOP created a new subcategory⏎of HIE data access requests, or queries, entitled “TEFCA Required Treatment.” HIE participants⏎must respond to treatment queries sent under this exchange purpose code. Conversely, treatment⏎queries that fall under the generic treatment code are permissive requests for information, and thus,⏎optional.15 The Treatment SOP states that only certain covered entities may initiate transactions⏎12 See, e.g., ONC’s Cures Act Final Rule, HealthIT.gov, https://www.healthit.gov/topic/oncs-cures-act-final-rule (last⏎updated March 26, 2024) (“The rule is designed to give patients and their healthcare providers secure access to health⏎information. It also aims to increase innovation and competition by fostering an ecosystem of new applications to⏎provide patients with more choices in their healthcare”).⏎13 See 45 C.F.R. § 164.506(c). See also 45 C.F.R. § 164.502(b)(2)(i) (excluding treatment activities from HIPAA’s⏎“minimum necessary” requirement); 65 Fed. Reg. at 82493 (expressing clear intent not to interfere with patient⏎treatment by promulgating an exception to uses and disclosures of PHI requiring patient authorization).⏎14 Changes to the Common Agreement are subject to a procedural process outlined in the Common Agreement. While⏎the RCE may elect to consult with various governing bodies within the HIE/QHIN structure regarding changes to the⏎Common Agreement, it is not required to consult with the “Participant/Subparticipant Caucus.” Further, proposed⏎changes to the Common Agreement need only be ratified by a two-thirds’ vote cast by QHIN caucus members.⏎Similarly, for any changes to the SOPs, the RCE may elect to consult with various governing bodies within the⏎HIE/QHIN structure, but it is not required to consult with the Participant/Subparticipant Caucus. Any material⏎changes to the SOPs must be approved by at least two-thirds’ vote of the QHIN caucus and Participant/Subparticipant⏎Caucus. However, any non-material changes to SOPs need only be approved by two-thirds’ vote of the QHIN caucus.⏎15 See Standard Operating Procedure (SOP): Exchange Purpose (XP) Implementation: Treatment, TEFCA at⏎§ 5.4.2.2 (“QHIN Query Response – All Responding Nodes MUST respond to QHIN Queries for the T-TRTMNT XP⏎Code that contain the information specified in this SOP, in accordance with the Framework Agreements and⏎Applicable Law.”) compared to id. at § 4.4.2.2 (“QHIN Query Response – All Responding Nodes SHOULD Respond⏎to QHIN Query Requests for the T-TREAT XP Code that contain the information specified in this SOP, in accordance⏎with the Framework Agreements and Applicable Law.”), available here: https://rce.sequoiaproject.org/wp-⏎content/uploads/2024/07/SOP-Treatment-XP-Implementation_508.pdf.⏎4⏎⏎Page 5 of 8⏎under the TEFCA Required Treatment code. Specifically, only covered entities that are licensed⏎health care providers—or their delegates, which includes their electronic health record vendors—⏎may send TEFCA Required Treatment requests.16 TEFCA announced that these changes, which⏎were described as ‘clarifying’ procedural changes, were “intentionally narrow” and issued by⏎TEFCA in order to “build trust” in the TEFCA community.⏎Contrary to TEFCA’s trust-building, procedural classification, these changes created a two-tiered⏎system in which certain covered entities were guaranteed access to health data and other covered⏎entities were not.17 This structure significantly departs from the long-standing HIPAA principles⏎that recognize and protect all health care providers’ ability to use and disclose PHI for treatment⏎purposes. Moreover, the Treatment SOP was foisted upon the health data community without any⏎discussion or debate. The changes were announced without the benefit of any meaningful⏎procedural protections contemplated under the Common Agreement and fall far short of notice-⏎and-comment rulemaking standards under the Administrative Procedure Act.⏎II. The Role of Digital Health and Medical Device Companies⏎Digital health and medical device companies are reshaping the health care industry by prioritizing⏎a user-first philosophy that reimagines what it means to deliver patient-centered care. Unlike⏎traditional health care systems that operate on rigid, appointment-based workflows where⏎providers may interact with patients a few times a year at most, digital health and medical device⏎companies leverage sleek, intuitive interfaces to incorporate health care into a patient’s day-to-day⏎life. By focusing heavily on user interaction and high-fidelity design, these companies⏎significantly reduce barriers to entry and engagement. Such low-friction touchpoints foster a⏎richer and more textured relationship with the patient, transforming health from an episodic chore⏎into a continuous, proactive dialogue.⏎A high level of engagement promotes better data collection, and allows these companies to capture⏎a broader spectrum of real-world data than typical hospitals and health care systems. Through the⏎integration of wearable devices, IoT-enabled diagnostic tools, and other health related mobile apps,⏎these platforms gather longitudinal data on key health metrics like sleep patterns, heart rate⏎variability, physical activity, ovulation cycles, wellness trackers, and nutritional intake. This data⏎supports not only healthy habits in general, but promotes key health drivers and objectives, like⏎chronic condition counseling, medication adherence, care coordination and navigation, remote⏎patient monitoring, post-treatment therapies, and other care counseling services. When this data⏎is supplemented with data obtained in more traditional health care settings and processed using⏎digital health and medical device companies’ sophisticated, AI-driven data analytics, hyper-⏎personalized care interventions become possible. For example, an app may nudge a diabetic⏎patient to adjust their nutritional intake based on immediate glucose trends or identify early signs⏎of mental health decline through behavioral changes and daily journaling practices.⏎Digital health and medical device companies are uniquely positioned to bridge the care gap that⏎exists, not only from check-up–to–check-up, but between the patient in the clinical context and the⏎16 See id. at § 5.3. Government organizations like the Veterans Health Administration may also send requests under⏎the TEFCA Required Treatment code.⏎17 See id. at pg. 3.⏎5⏎⏎Page 6 of 8⏎patient in their real-world setting. On-demand and multifaceted engagement surfaces—such as⏎automated data collection, daily reminders and push notifications, 24/7 chat support, or instant⏎virtual consultations—reorient the delivery of care around the patient, wherever they may be. This⏎combination of high-definition data, sophisticated analytical offerings, and human-centric design⏎demonstrates the unique value that digital health and medical device companies provide to the⏎health care ecosystem.⏎Unfortunately, certain industry actors interpret this value proposition as a threat instead of an⏎opportunity to collaborate and achieve better health outcomes. Permitting only some members of⏎the health data community to meaningful partake in the HIEs will stymie the innovative and⏎competitive marketplace goals that HHS seeks to foster. Exclusion (on the basis of an inaccurate⏎reading of HIPAA and closed-door drafting sessions) creates a two-tiered system for entities—⏎those that can participate in legitimate business activities and those that cannot—and ultimately⏎reduces patient choice and autonomy in the marketplace.⏎III. Responses to Specific Requests for Information⏎a. Response to RFI Question No. 3⏎Question No. 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Certain industry actors are seeking to misappropriate HIPAA’s definitions of “treatment,”⏎“covered entity,” and/or “health care” in the Common Agreement, and the supporting terms of⏎HIE access, to block digital health and medical device companies from participating in the HIEs.⏎At best, these actors concede that relevant HHS guidance creates ambiguity and gray areas as to⏎which entities may be capable of performing “treatment” or what “treatment” may look like in⏎practice. On the contrary, as described in more detail above, long-standing guidance from HHS’⏎rulemaking activity, including a piece of guidance that is over twenty years old, explicitly⏎recognizes the variety of actors that may indeed provide “treatment” and thus are permitted to⏎access PHI for such purposes.⏎HHS should recognize and reinforce its flexible definition of “treatment” under HIPAA by issuing⏎new guidance that expressly acknowledges the role that direct-to-patient, AI-powered services can⏎and do play in today’s health ecosystem—including providing and supporting “treatment”⏎purposes.⏎b. Response to RFI Question Nos. 2, 5⏎Question No. 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations,⏎policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical⏎care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎6⏎⏎Page 7 of 8⏎Question No. 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use⏎in clinical care?⏎HHS should continue to support private sector AI innovation by providing an accreditation or⏎certification for digital health and medical device companies that provide certain innovative health⏎services powered by AI. Such an accreditation or certification would serve as an additional means⏎to substantiate these entities’ “treatment” purpose, for purposes of meeting TEFCA’s permitted⏎purposes access requirements and any corresponding implementation instructions or guidelines.⏎This alternative path would provide a neutral setting where digital health and medical device⏎companies could demonstrate their clinical value, instead of fighting against well-funded, self-⏎interested parties that are currently commandeering the HIE/QHIN rules of engagement.⏎HHS should also consider (i) amending the Common Agreement to prohibit non-discrimination of⏎digital health and medical device companies that have direct interactions with patients;⏎(ii) requiring any changes to HIE or QHIN participation policies, business rules, or other⏎applicable standards governing access to health data be subject to a vote involving all QHIN⏎participants and subparticipants—not just QHIN caucus members;18 and (iii) immediately⏎unwinding the two-tiered system established under the Treatment SOP. When private actors⏎arbitrarily and capriciously change or enforce such terms to exclude certain parties, not only is the⏎result unfair and anticompetitive, but it threatens the health and safety of patients who incorporate⏎those digital health technologies in their day-to-day health care.⏎c. Response to RFI Question No. 8⏎Question No. 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎AI technologies are only as good as the data on which they are based. Effective AI development⏎requires data from a variety of contexts and encounters across the patient’s health care journey.⏎This comment seeks to underscore the necessary condition precedent—that digital health and⏎medical device companies are permitted HIE data access in the first place.⏎Conclusion⏎HHS has long recognized that health care is not limited to the four corners of a doctor’s office. A⏎thriving health data ecosystem pushes actors to meet patients where they are—online, in connected⏎devices, or in the palm of the patient’s hand. To best realize HHS’ goal of AI innovation and⏎adoption, HHS must act to protect digital health and medical device companies’ ability to access⏎HIE data. QHINs must admit digital health and medical device companies that directly interact⏎with patients and recognize that non-traditional actors must access this data for “treatment”⏎purposes. Preserving this free market balance will promote patient agency and better health⏎outcomes and help establish the U.S. as a global leader in AI.⏎18 See § 5.3.4 of the Common Agreement.⏎7⏎⏎Page 8 of 8⏎*********⏎We appreciate the chance to provide HHS with information about the industry and comments to⏎your requests for information that we presented in this comment letter. Please do not hesitate to⏎contact us if you have any questions.⏎Sincerely,⏎Adam C. Solander⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0393♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Story after story emerges about the dangers of using AI for mental health difficulties. It's shocking how quickly professionals in the mental health field who have been trained in the complexity of the human mind would want to incorporate an under regulated instrument still in its nascent stages of development. To give nearly free rein to AI to manage the fragile nature of thoughts, emotions, and communication is frightening at the least. AI does not have emotional intelligence or consciousness to interact at the sensitive level necessary to counsel and guide someone to better mental health. I implore you to delay adopting AI as part of clinical care until it's an instrument that's better understood and restricted in its use..♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0394♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Psychiatric Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Psychiatric Association♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI [RIN 0955-AA13⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The American Psychiatric Association (APA) appreciates the opportunity to respond to ONC/ASTP’s Request⏎for Information on the use of artificial intelligence in clinical care. Psychiatrists approach AI cautiously⏎because behavioral health information is uniquely sensitive, and patient trust is foundational to effective⏎treatment. Accordingly, APA’s comments emphasize privacy, security, transparency, and evidence. While⏎AI may offer promise, particularly in reducing administrative burden, adoption in psychiatric practice⏎should be guided by rigorous, real-world evaluation, clear accountability, and safeguards that prevent⏎secondary use or unintended disclosure of mental health data. The recommendations to certain questions⏎posed by HHS below focus on practical HHS actions that support responsible innovation without weakening⏎existing protections.⏎Barriers to private sector innovation in AI⏎Behavioral health clinicians are approaching AI cautiously, and the biggest barriers to private sector⏎innovation and adoption are not a lack of tools but a lack of trusted, consistent guardrails, leaving patients⏎and providers without clear transparency into when AI is used, how it performs, and what happens to⏎highly sensitive mental health data, including whether it is used without awareness or consent or reused⏎for other purposes. To reduce these barriers while preserving innovation, HHS should require or at⏎minimum strongly incentivize, independent healthcare AI accreditation as a scalable way to set risk-⏎proportionate expectations for quality, transparency, safety, and privacy, so developers have a clear⏎pathway to trust and providers have confidence that accredited tools meet baseline protections.⏎Regulatory changes to incentivize the effective use of AI⏎HHS can accelerate the safe and effective integration of AI into clinical care by prioritizing targeted⏎regulatory, payment, and programmatic reforms that align innovation with patient safety, transparency,⏎and accountability. Additionally, APA recommends:⏎⏎• Stronger HIPAA-era rules for modern data flows:⏎Modernize privacy and security safeguards to address contemporary data ecosystems, including⏎AI model training, secondary data use, and vendor access to protected health information (PHI).⏎HHS should require clearer limits on data reuse and model training, enhanced transparency for⏎patients, and stronger accountability standards for third party vendors handling PHI.⏎Strengthened data governance would enhance patient safety, protect privacy, and build public⏎trust which is critical prerequisites for widespread AI adoption.⏎• Payment for safety work:⏎Establish reimbursement mechanisms that recognize clinician time spent reviewing, validating,⏎and monitoring AI-generated outputs, as well as payment pathways for evidence-based digital⏎and AI-supported care. Without reimbursement, safety oversight becomes uncompensated labor,⏎creating disincentives for responsible implementation. Payment reform would align financial⏎incentives with safe, high quality AI integration.⏎• Baseline requirements for “clinical AI” transparency:⏎Develop national baseline standards requiring disclosure of model purpose, intended use⏎population, performance characteristics, known limitations, drift monitoring processes, and⏎expectations for human oversight. Clear transparency requirements would reduce unsafe⏎deployment, support informed clinical decision-making, and ensure that AI tools are used within⏎validated boundaries.⏎• National adverse-event reporting for AI:⏎Create a standardized, nationwide mechanism to report AI-related harms and near misses,⏎similar to pharmacovigilance or medical device reporting systems. A centralized reporting⏎infrastructure would enable early detection of safety signals, promote shared learning across⏎health systems, and support continuous improvement of AI tools.⏎Regulations or policies to revisit⏎HHS can modernize privacy protections, clearer accountability structures, and strengthen federal⏎standards to ensure that AI in clinical care, particularly in psychiatric settings, is implemented safely,⏎transparently, and with strong patient trust:⏎• HIPAA Privacy/Security (OCR):⏎o Strengthen and clarify HIPAA guardrails: Provide clear guidance under existing HIPAA⏎regulations that limits AI to purpose-bound uses, prohibits secondary use of health data⏎without explicit patient authorization, and requires enforceable safeguards (data⏎minimization, strong access controls, and auditable logs) across the full AI workflow.⏎o Limit secondary use for “product improvement,” requiring robust audit logs, and⏎tightening modern de-identification expectations to minimize re-identification risk.⏎• 42 CFR Part 2 (SAMHSA): Update Part 2 implementation guidance so behavioral health such as⏎SUD information can support safe AI-enabled care only with granular, revocable consent, clear⏎redisclosure limits, and enforceable segmentation and access controls across systems.⏎• ONC/ASTP Interoperability, Certification & Information Blocking: Require certified health IT to⏎support privacy-preserving, least-privilege data access (including segmentation tags,⏎provenance, and purpose-of-use), and clarify that patients can obtain AI-influenced clinical⏎content in the record without exposing sensitive psychotherapy notes beyond existing⏎protections.⏎⏎o Do not roll back certification transparency requirements for AI-enabled health IT⏎including standardized model documentation (“model cards”) and instead strengthen⏎them so psychiatric providers can evaluate intended use, data handling/retention,⏎limitations, and subgroup performance before adoption.⏎Novel legal and implementation issues⏎The primary legal and implementation challenges of non-device AI in clinical care include unclear liability⏎when harm occurs, uncertainty about discoverability of AI-generated content in the medical record, and⏎insufficient transparency and consent frameworks particularly when sensitive psychiatric information is⏎involved. APA recommends establishing clear, system-level standards for the governance of non-⏎device AI used in clinical care. Federal agencies, in collaboration with developers and health care⏎organizations, should clarify liability frameworks, documentation expectations, and⏎discoverability rules for AI-generated content.⏎Addressing novel legal and implementation issues⏎HHS should play a convening and standard-setting role that reduces uncertainty without lowering privacy⏎or safety protections:⏎• Unified guidance: HHS should publish clear rules for non-device AI use in health care,⏎including human review and escalation.⏎• Behavioral health privacy: HHS should limit psychiatric data use to purpose-bound, minimal⏎access with default non-retention and no secondary use without explicit authorization.⏎• Keep certification transparency: HHS should not roll back ONC certification transparency⏎(including model cards) and should strengthen it for limits, updates, and data practices.⏎Promising AI evaluation methods⏎The most promising evaluation approaches for non-medical AI in clinical care combine rigorous pre-⏎deployment validation with continuous post-deployment monitoring (drift detection, adverse-event⏎reporting, etc.), alongside human-centered assessments of usability, clinician reliance, documentation⏎accuracy, and impact on patient safety and equity.⏎• HHS should align evaluation methods for non-medical device AI used in clinical care to existing⏎evaluation methods such as NIST AI Risk Management Framework which emphasize (1)⏎governing with clear accountability, documented intended use, change control, and incident⏎response; (2) mapping the clinical context and foreseeable harms (including behavioral health⏎privacy and high-risk workflows like crisis triage); (3) measuring performance using pre-⏎deployment test sets and real-world “shadow mode” that quantify safety-critical errors,⏎subgroup impacts, usability/workload, and privacy/security leakage; and (4) managing risk post-⏎deployment through continuous monitoring for drift, trigger-based audits for high-risk content,⏎transparent update notices, and rollback thresholds tied to patient safety and trust.⏎HHS support of evaluation methods⏎APA recommends funding shared evaluation infrastructure and finance privacy-preserving, multisite “AI⏎evaluation networks” (secure enclaves/federated evaluation) so providers can test tools without⏎centralizing sensitive psychiatric data.⏎⏎Administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI⏎While administrative complexity is often cited as a barrier to AI adoption in clinical care, the more⏎fundamental challenge is trust. Clinicians and patients must have confidence that AI tools are accurate,⏎safe, privacy-protective, and aligned with clinical judgment before they will meaningfully integrate them⏎into care.⏎• Model bias: without real-world evidence on error modes, drift, and subgroup performance,⏎reimbursing clinical AI would prematurely scale tools that can have disparate impacts on⏎protected and clinically vulnerable groups. HHS should condition any payment pathway on⏎transparent accountability expectations plus rigorous, stratified outcome evaluation that⏎demonstrates meaningful clinical improvement and equitable performance in routine practice.⏎• Privacy uncertainty slows interest: If it’s not crystal clear how sensitive notes and messages are⏎protected (and not reused), clinicians prefer to wait.⏎• Lack of evidence for implementation into practice: Until there are robust, real-world evidence of⏎benefit in behavioral health settings and without unintended harm, enthusiasm remains⏎cautious.⏎• Fear of clinical harm: clinicians worry about tone, stigma, mislabeling, and overconfidence in⏎summaries or “risk” outputs that could steer care incorrectly.⏎• Accountability is unclear: If an AI-generated summary is wrong or a crisis message is mishandled,⏎clinicians want to know who is responsible, what are the steps to correct, and what protections⏎exist.⏎• Workflow disruption is a concern: Even helpful tools can add steps (reviewing, correcting,⏎documenting), and clinicians do not want new burdens disguised as “automation.”⏎• Patient acceptance is unknown: Providers expect some patients to object to AI involvement in⏎sensitive communications, and clinics don’t want to jeopardize engagement.⏎• The bar is higher in mental health: Because the data are deeply personal and harms can be⏎serious, psychiatrists and their patients expect stronger guardrails than in many other⏎specialties.⏎Areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize⏎There are ways HHS can accelerate the adoption of AI by developing evidence that is lacking today⏎including:⏎• Pragmatic clinical trials for AI-in-workflow: Fund multisite pragmatic trials (not just retrospective⏎accuracy studies) that test AI’s impact on outcomes, safety events, clinician workload, and⏎patient experience.⏎• Real-world effectiveness in behavioral health: Pragmatic studies showing where AI helps and⏎does not in psychiatry including documentation burden, access, continuity, and outcomes, using⏎credible comparators.⏎• Head-to-head comparative effectiveness: Support studies comparing AI-assisted care versus⏎best existing tools (human scribes, templates, standard triage protocols), not just “AI vs.⏎nothing.”⏎⏎Thank you for your review and consideration of these comments. If you have any questions or would like⏎to discuss any of these comments further, please contact Zuhal Haidari (zhaidari@psych.org), Deputy⏎Director, Digital Health.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0395♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from McKesson Corporation, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔McKesson Corporation♔♔♔♔♔♔McKesson Corporation Rich Buckley⏎6535 State Hwy 161 Senior Vice President, Corporate Affairs⏎Irving, TX 75039⏎www.mckesson.com⏎February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, M.D.,⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for Health Information Technology,⏎ASTP/ONC⏎U.S. Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Sent electronically via: regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information; Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (HHS-ONC-2026-0001)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎McKesson Corporation (“McKesson”) appreciates the opportunity to provide comments in response to⏎the Request for Information (RFI) Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care HHS-ONC-2026-0001 including the Office of the Deputy Secretary and the Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology (ONC) (collectively referred to here as “HHS”).⏎About McKesson⏎McKesson is a global leader in healthcare supply chain management solutions, community pharmacy,⏎community oncology and specialty care, and healthcare information technology solutions. McKesson⏎partners with pharmaceutical manufacturers, providers, pharmacies, payers, federal, state, and local⏎governments, and other organizations in healthcare to advance health outcomes for all.⏎As a diversified healthcare leader, our solutions help patients access life-changing therapies, create a⏎real difference for patients with cancer, and equip pharmacies, health systems and clinics with⏎technologies to operate more effectively. Our provider and pharmacy partners operate in communities⏎that include some of the most underserved urban and rural areas of the U.S. As we work across our⏎partners and customers, McKesson strives to ensure that its views on improving healthcare⏎prioritize what’s best for the patient.⏎General Comments⏎Artificial intelligence (AI) has the potential to meaningfully improve clinical care by enhancing decision⏎support, reducing administrative burden, and enabling timely, coordinated, and patient-centered care⏎across the healthcare system. At the same time, realizing these benefits at scale requires a policy⏎environment that supports responsible innovation, protects patients, and reflects how care is delivered⏎across diverse clinical settings. As a healthcare services company operating at the center of data⏎exchange, care delivery, and technology enablement, McKesson offers the following comments to⏎highlight key barriers to AI adoption and to recommend actions HHS can take to foster safe, effective,⏎and equitable use of AI in clinical care. Our comments focus on the need for aligned data governance,⏎coordinated cross-agency guidance, and payment policies that appropriately recognize AI-enabled⏎value in real-world clinical workflows.⏎1⏎⏎McKesson Corporation Rich Buckley⏎6535 State Hwy 161 Senior Vice President, Corporate Affairs⏎Irving, TX 75039⏎www.mckesson.com⏎Fragmented State Laws Undermine Innovation and Limit Patient Benefit⏎Current federal and state data protection regimes apply unevenly across the healthcare ecosystem⏎depending on how data is received, by whom, and under what legal classification. As a result, the same⏎clinical data may be subject to materially different privacy, security, and use restrictions based solely on⏎the pathway through which it flows rather than on the sensitivity of the data itself or the interests of the⏎patient.⏎For example, identical datasets may receive robust HIPAA protections when held by a covered entity⏎but be subject to different (and in some cases less comprehensive) requirements when held by⏎organizations not governed by HIPAA or governed by overlapping and inconsistent state laws. In other⏎instances, initial recipients of data, such as payers or healthcare clearinghouses, may retain⏎disproportionate control over downstream data use regardless of where the data subsequently resides⏎within the healthcare ecosystem. These inconsistencies can result in patient data being governed in⏎ways that are misaligned with patient expectations and, in some cases, not in the patient’s best⏎interests.⏎This fragmented regulatory landscape creates uncertainty, increases compliance complexity, and⏎undermines patient trust without meaningfully improving privacy or security outcomes. While providers⏎generally have access to the clinical data they need for care, the fragmented privacy landscape⏎complicates cross-ecosystem collaboration and data reuse constraining experimentation and ideation⏎needed to develop and scale effective digital health solutions and AI-enabled care. Advanced analytics⏎and AI tools depend on timely, representative, and interoperable data to support clinical decision⏎making, monitor performance, detect bias, and continuously improve care delivery. Legal and⏎regulatory fragmentation that restricts lawful data access or creates inconsistent governance across⏎care settings can degrade data quality, introduce gaps or bias, and ultimately limit the effectiveness,⏎performance and safety of AI-enabled solutions.⏎For companies like McKesson that operate at the center of healthcare data movement, these⏎challenges are especially pronounced. Responsible AI adoption depends not only on strong⏎safeguards, but also on the ability to securely and lawfully access high quality clinical data across the⏎continuum of care. Aligning legal and regulatory data protection frameworks to be more consistent and⏎interoperable would strengthen privacy protections while enabling the responsible sharing and reuse of⏎data across the healthcare ecosystem supporting innovation, improving outcomes, reducing burden,⏎and building public trust in AI.⏎Issue Consolidated, Cross-Agency AI Guidance Aligned to Real-World Clinical⏎Workflows Agency⏎HHS should issue consolidated, cross-agency guidance on AI in clinical care that reflects how AI is⏎developed, deployed, and used across healthcare workflows. Current regulatory expectations are⏎fragmented across FDA, CMS, ONC, and OCR, creating uncertainty particularly for AI that supports⏎clinical care indirectly or operates across multiple settings. In addition, not all AI used in clinical care⏎presents the same level of patient risk. Consolidated guidance would enable a tiered, risk-⏎based oversight framework that appropriately differentiates among:⏎• Assistive or administrative AI⏎2⏎⏎McKesson Corporation Rich Buckley⏎6535 State Hwy 161 Senior Vice President, Corporate Affairs⏎Irving, TX 75039⏎www.mckesson.com⏎• Clinical decision support tools⏎• Diagnostic or treatment directing AI⏎Clear differentiation would reduce overregulation of low-risk use cases while ensuring appropriate⏎safeguards for high-risk applications.⏎Modernize CMS Payment Policies to Recognize AI-Enabled Value⏎CMS payment policy plays an important role in shaping whether and how AI technologies are⏎integrated into clinical care. As AI tools continue to mature, today’s fee-for-service reimbursement⏎structures may not fully reflect the types of value these technologies can support, including:⏎• Improvements in clinical and operational efficiency⏎• Reductions in administrative burden for clinicians⏎• Earlier intervention, prevention, and more consistent delivery of care⏎Many AI-enabled tools generate value through time savings, streamlined workflows, and avoided⏎downstream utilization. As payment models continue to evolve, there may be opportunities to better⏎align reimbursement with these emerging sources of value, helping support thoughtful adoption while⏎maintaining program integrity.⏎Conclusion⏎In closing, McKesson strongly supports HHS’s efforts to accelerate the responsible adoption of AI in⏎clinical care and appreciates the opportunity to provide input on this important initiative. Realizing the⏎full potential of AI to improve patient outcomes, reduce administrative burden, and strengthen the⏎healthcare system will require a policy framework that is consistent, patient-centric, and grounded in⏎real-world clinical workflows. We urge HHS to address fragmented data governance, issue coordinated⏎cross-agency guidance that reflects a risk-based approach to AI oversight and modernize CMS⏎payment policies to recognize the value created by AI-enabled efficiency and prevention. Taken⏎together, these steps would promote innovation while preserving appropriate safeguards, support⏎provider adoption, and build public trust in AI-enabled care. McKesson stands ready to continue⏎partnering with HHS, CMS, ASTP, and ONC to help advance a regulatory and payment environment⏎that enables safe, effective, and equitable use of AI in clinical care. If you have questions or need⏎further information, please contact Fauzea Hussain, Vice President of Public Policy, at⏎Fauzea.Hussain@McKesson.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Rich Buckley⏎Senior Vice President, Corporate Affairs⏎McKesson Corporation⏎3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0396♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from California Clinical Laboratory Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔California Clinical Laboratory Association♔♔♔♔♔♔1127 11th Street, Suite 820⏎Sacramento, CA 95814⏎(916) 446-2646⏎February 19, 2026⏎The Honorable Chris Klomp⏎Chief Counselor⏎Office of the Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave., SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (HHS Health Sector AI RFI) [Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001]⏎Submitted via Electronic Submission to www.regulations.gov⏎Dear Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎On behalf of the California Clinical Laboratory Association (CCLA), we appreciate the⏎opportunity to provide comments on the recently issued Request for Information (RFI) entitled⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care by the⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of the Deputy Secretary in⏎collaboration with the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)/Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology.⏎Our association represents a broad range of clinical laboratories serving patients across⏎California, including large national diagnostic laboratories, specialized and biotechnology-⏎focused laboratories, academic and health system pathology departments, and smaller⏎independent clinical laboratories and service providers. California laboratories operate at the⏎forefront of biotechnology and digital health innovation, and regulatory clarity and⏎reimbursement stability are critical to sustaining responsible AI advancement in clinical⏎diagnostics.⏎CCLA Responses to RFI Questions⏎⏎1127 11th Street, Suite 820⏎Sacramento, CA 95814⏎(916) 446-2646⏎Regulation⏎HHS seeks feedback on how current HHS regulations impact AI adoption and use for clinical⏎care.⏎CCLA Response:⏎Federal regulatory clarity is essential to enable laboratories to adopt AI safely and at scale.⏎Current federal frameworks—particularly CLIA and FDA oversight—serve distinct and⏎complementary roles. CLIA governs clinical laboratory services, including laboratory developed⏎tests (LDTs) and algorithmic analyses performed as professional services, while FDA oversight⏎applies to regulated medical devices and software marketed by manufacturers. Clear⏎jurisdictional boundaries are necessary to prevent duplicative or misaligned oversight and to⏎ensure continued innovation in laboratory-developed services.⏎For AI-enabled medical devices, laboratories require clear and actionable manufacturer⏎transparency to support local verification and validation prior to clinical implementation.⏎Transparency should include model development datasets and intended-use populations;⏎performance metrics and validation methodology; ongoing performance monitoring and change-⏎control plans; known limitations and deployment constraints; and configuration and⏎customization parameters. Such disclosures support laboratory oversight, mitigate risks⏎associated with dataset imbalance or model drift, and enhance physician confidence in AI-⏎enabled tools.⏎CCLA encourages HHS to reevaluate 45 CFR 92.210 (Nondiscrimination in the use of patient⏎care decision support tools) to ensure that liability for algorithmic bias is appropriately allocated⏎between AI developers and clinical end users. The current framework risks placing⏎disproportionate responsibility on clinicians and laboratories for potential bias originating in⏎vendor training data or model design. Regulatory clarity in this area will promote responsible⏎adoption without discouraging innovation.⏎The Clinical Laboratory Improvement Amendments (CLIA) remain the primary regulatory⏎framework for laboratory services, including laboratory services that examine and analyze digital⏎laboratory information. However, CLIA regulations were promulgated decades ago, before the⏎proliferation of software, robust networking tools, and AI-enabled diagnostics that now enhance⏎diagnostic workflows. Targeted regulatory updates to CLIA would advance innovation and⏎clarify oversight responsibilities. Specifically:⏎(1) The definition of “laboratory” under CLIA should be amended to expressly state that⏎laboratories include facilities that examine digital laboratory information, such as previously⏎sequenced DNA or digital images of slides, and that “algorithmic” examinations are included⏎among the functions of a laboratory. Clarifying that CLIA is the applicable regulatory framework⏎for algorithmic-only laboratory services will remove regulatory ambiguity and support continued⏎innovation.⏎⏎1127 11th Street, Suite 820⏎Sacramento, CA 95814⏎(916) 446-2646⏎(2) CMS should codify into regulation its enforcement discretion policy that permits pathologists⏎and other laboratory personnel to review digital laboratory data, digital results, and digital⏎images remotely under the primary site’s CLIA certificate.⏎(3) HHS should reinstate or replace the former Clinical Laboratory Improvement Advisory⏎Committee (CLIAC), or establish a comparable advisory body, to provide expert guidance as AI⏎transforms laboratory medicine.⏎For specific wording guidance related to CLIA regulatory updates, we defer to the language⏎recommendations outlined in the ACLA response to this Health Sector AI RFI.⏎Reimbursement⏎HHS seeks feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive and ability⏎to promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical care⏎AI tool builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care.⏎CCLA Response:⏎Sustainable adoption of AI-enabled laboratory services requires predictable, value-based⏎reimbursement pathways that reflect the clinical benefit and the substantial investment required⏎to develop, validate, and maintain algorithmic analyses—even when no new specimen is⏎collected. AI-enabled algorithmic services improve diagnostic precision, better inform treatment⏎decisions, and reduce unnecessary downstream testing or ineffective therapies.⏎Absent viable coding and payment pathways, innovative AI-enabled diagnostics may migrate⏎away from Medicare beneficiaries, exacerbating disparities in access and limiting innovation⏎within federally funded programs.⏎Applicable clinical laboratory services may be reimbursed under the Medicare Clinical⏎Laboratory Fee Schedule (CLFS) or the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule (PFS) when physician⏎professional time is required. The clinical value of AI-enabled services and the resources⏎required to furnish them should be appropriately reflected in reimbursement under both payment⏎systems.⏎Federal engagement is needed to align coding and payment policy with regulatory frameworks.⏎The Proprietary Laboratory Analysis (PLA) code pathway has historically supported innovative⏎laboratory services, including algorithmic-only analyses. Recent changes to PLA guidance that⏎render algorithmic-only services ineligible for PLA coding create uncertainty and risk impeding⏎innovation. CMS should work with the American Medical Association (AMA) to ensure that⏎appropriate coding pathways remain available for algorithmic-only laboratory services.⏎⏎1127 11th Street, Suite 820⏎Sacramento, CA 95814⏎(916) 446-2646⏎Additionally, AI and automated systems should not be used to deny or impede patient access to⏎services automatically. AI-driven utilization management tools and automated edits must include⏎meaningful human clinical review, transparent denial rationales, and clear appeal pathways to⏎ensure that patients are not denied medically necessary care.⏎HHS should also ensure that Medicare Advantage plans implement National Coverage⏎Determinations (NCDs) consistently with Medicare fee-for-service standards and do not apply⏎AI-driven utilization tools or local coverage criteria that restrict access beyond established⏎federal coverage policy.⏎Research & Development⏎HHS seeks input on ways in which HHS may invest in research & development (including⏎public-private partnerships and cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs))⏎to integrate AI in care delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the⏎health and wellbeing of all Americans.⏎CCLA Response:⏎HHS should prioritize funding for public-private partnerships, CRADAs, and grant programs⏎that build diverse, high-quality validation datasets; support interoperable data standards; enable⏎real-world evidence (RWE) generation; develop synthetic data governance frameworks; and⏎expand external proficiency testing models for AI-enabled laboratory services under CLIA.⏎Investments in standardized benchmarking, external validation programs, and workforce training⏎will reduce implementation risk and accelerate trustworthy AI deployment while maintaining⏎quality and patient safety. Federal funding should also recognize and offset the operational costs⏎laboratories incur when standardizing data and adopting interoperable formats.⏎AI research and innovation in clinical laboratories advance most effectively when reimbursement⏎is stable and regulatory expectations are clear, consistent, and technology-neutral. Predictable⏎policy environments reduce innovation risk and promote responsible deployment of AI tools that⏎enhance patient care.⏎⏎1127 11th Street, Suite 820⏎Sacramento, CA 95814⏎(916) 446-2646⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide commentary to HHS. CCLA looks forward to working⏎collaboratively with HHS to promote responsible AI implementation within the clinical⏎laboratory community and to ensure continued patient access to high-quality diagnostics.⏎Sincerely,⏎Kristi Foy⏎Kristi Foy⏎Executive Director⏎California Clinical Laboratory Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0397♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-16T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To build upon my previous comment on grouping and filtering, ⏎⏎What would help is to have an AI-assisted physician review layer⏎⏎Using structured data grouping and terminology reasoning to give the clinicians with prioritized, specialty review findings that would need review/acknowledgement and confirmation.⏎⏎It’s the tension of the all the individual observations versus the information that matters now⏎⏎This could have cross specialist review so the AI and other physicians in the care team would know what other physicians already reviewed.⏎⏎Instead of forcing physicians and clinicians to review everything (which is exhausting), only present the information that actually requires review⏎⏎AI could assist to verify what matters at that point.⏎⏎⏎⏎AI assisted physician review should prioritize the clinical findings that are grouped/filtered for acknowledgement, instead of manual review of entire records.⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0398♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Flores♔♔North Dakota♔♔United States♔National Hemophilia Foundation♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly I'm not sure this is the right place to ask this but I've been trying to figure out for weeks now whether any of this applies to people on Tricare. My son is active duty Army, stationed at Fort Wainwright in Alaska, and my daughter-in-law uses the military health system for her care including for a bleeding disorder she was diagnosed with in 2021. When I see a rule like this coming out of HHS I genuinely cannot tell if the military health system falls under it or not. Sometimes it does, sometimes it doesn't, and nobody seems to be able to give a straight answer.⏎⏎My concern specifically is about the consent piece. If AI tools are being used in clinical settings to help make decisions about care, I want to know if patients in military facilities are going to be told that's happening. That's really the core of what I'm asking. We work with a lot of families in the bleeding disorder community where treatment decisions are genuinely complex and high stakes, and there's already a trust issue with some of our members around feeling like decisions are being made about them without full transparency. That's not a hypothetical. I hear about it at our regional chapter meetings, most recently at our North Dakota chapter gathering in Bismarck back in March.⏎⏎I guess what I'd ask HHS to clarify is whether facilities operating under the Defense Health Agency are expected to follow whatever guidelines come out of this process, or whether there's a separate track for that. It would help enormously just to have a clear answer to that question somewhere in the final guidance document, even just a paragraph that says "here's how this does or does not apply to military beneficiaries." Because right now the people I talk to just sort of shrug when I bring it up.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0399♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-29T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Nelson♔♔South Carolina♔♔United States♔Mental Health America♔♔♔♔♔♔My concern with this RFI is not really about whether AI can read a scan or flag a drug interaction. It probably can, sometimes better than an exhausted resident at 2am. What worries me is the framing of this whole effort — that speed and scale are the goals, and that patient outcomes will follow naturally if we just remove the friction from adoption.⏎⏎I work in mental health policy in South Carolina, and the patients our member organizations serve are not well described by the word "users." Many of them have serious trauma histories. A lot of them have been failed by systems before — involuntary commitments, medication errors, providers who didn't listen. When someone finally gets to a point where they're willing to sit in a chair across from a clinician and say "I'm not okay," something genuinely delicate is happening. I am not being sentimental when I say that. The therapeutic relationship in mental health care is itself part of the treatment. There is peer-reviewed evidence on this going back decades.⏎⏎So when I read language in this RFI about "accelerating adoption" and "reducing barriers," my first question is: barriers for whom? Because from where I sit, the barrier most often described by the people we serve is that they can't get a real human being to take time with them. That's the gap. Not computational speed.⏎⏎I keep thinking about a conversation I had at our statewide provider convening in Columbia last October. A community mental health center director told me her staff had started using an AI documentation tool to reduce note-writing time, which sounds reasonable. But she noticed her clinicians were spending the time they saved staring at screens to review the AI output rather than staying present with patients. The tool was technically reducing documentation burden. The patients noticed something was different. Some of them asked about it directly.⏎⏎That's not an implementation problem you fix with better training protocols. It reflects something about what happens when you insert a recommendation layer between a clinician's attention and a patient's experience.⏎⏎I want to be honest that I don't think all AI in clinical care is harmful. I'm not arguing that. A tool that helps a rural primary care doc in Orangeburg County identify a medication interaction they might have missed — that's genuinely good. But this RFI is asking about acceleration and adoption at scale, and I think HHS needs to be more careful about the specific contexts where AI decision support risks replacing the kind of attentive, responsive human judgment that isn't just nice to have but is clinically functional.⏎⏎The RFI asks about what evidence gaps exist for AI in clinical care. My answer to that is straightforward: we have almost no rigorous evidence on what AI-mediated care does to therapeutic alliance, patient disclosure, treatment adherence among people with histories of institutional distrust, or outcomes in behavioral health settings generally. The literature on AI in clinical settings skews heavily toward imaging, pathology, and other areas where the "patient relationship" variable is easier to bracket out. Mental health is not like that. Psychiatry is not like that. And yet there's nothing in this RFI that suggests those differences are being treated as meaningful design constraints.⏎⏎I would ask HHS to at minimum require that any AI tool seeking expedited reimbursement pathways or certification under this effort demonstrate that its intended use population was meaningfully represented in the evaluation process — and that "performance" metrics include patient-reported experience measures, not just clinical accuracy statistics. The field doesn't have consensus on how to measure that yet, which is itself something the Department could invest in before pushing adoption.⏎⏎There's also the question of informed consent, which I don't think this RFI engages with seriously enough. South Carolina has no state-level requirement that patients be told when an AI tool is influencing their clinical encounter. I've been trying to track whether any other states do and the picture is genuinely murky right now as of early 2024.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0400♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cadence, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Cadence♔♔♔♔♔♔February 22, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎SUBMITTED ELECTRONICALLY VIA: https://www.regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎We appreciate the opportunity to respond to this RFI and to share our experience-based feedback⏎from building and using artificial intelligence (AI) tools as part of clinical care delivery at scale.⏎Cadence is on the front lines of a new era for healthcare where personalized, technology-enabled⏎care empowers patients to take control of their health and reduces costs for Medicare, and we⏎believe our experience is valuable for the Administration to consider. We also appreciate the⏎“OneHHS” alignment reflected in this RFI and urge the Department of Health and Human Services⏎(HHS) to use this moment to provide clear, practical policy pathways that enable responsible⏎scaling of applied AI in real-world clinical workflows.⏎Cadence delivers Remote Physiologic Monitoring (RPM), Advanced Primary Care Management⏎(APCM), and Chronic Care Management to nearly 90,000 patients nationwide, a majority of whom⏎live in rural and underserved communities. AI is already central to this work: we use AI-enabled⏎tools to scale high-touch coaching, streamline administrative tasks across electronic medical⏎records (EMRs), and proactively identify patients who may benefit from medication titration –⏎always with human-in-the-loop clinical oversight. In practice, the promise of “AI in clinical care”⏎is to enable better clinical operations at scale – summarizing longitudinal trends, prioritizing⏎patients who need attention, reducing alert fatigue, and embedding recommendations into existing⏎EMR workflows so clinicians can act quickly and safely.⏎In response to the RFI’s focus on regulation, reimbursement, and research and development, we⏎urge HHS to take a pragmatic, pro-innovation approach that:⏎● Removes outdated payment and program design barriers that discourage the appropriate⏎use of automation and AI to deliver higher-value care.⏎● Aligns federal incentives so AI enhances productivity, reduces clinician burden, lowers⏎costs, and improves outcomes – while protecting patients and maintaining public trust.⏎● Promotes interoperability and accountable measurement, including timely access to⏎Medicare claims and standardized, API-based data exchange.⏎1⏎⏎A. Key barriers to innovation and broader adoption of AI in clinical care⏎Key barriers include Medicare payment models that still equate value with documented minutes⏎rather than clinical impact. Time-based requirements can unintentionally penalize tools that safely⏎automate work and expand capacity, discouraging investment in AI-enabled care. Fee-for-service⏎incentives can also discourage prevention and early intervention – precisely where AI-enabled⏎monitoring and proactive outreach can produce the greatest downstream savings and improved⏎outcomes.⏎Adoption is also constrained by the lack of a reimbursement pathway for the software and⏎interoperability capabilities that make AI clinically usable. In practice, delivering clinically usable⏎AI requires continuous engineering and operational investment, such as maintaining integrations⏎that are not standardized, ensuring secure connectivity for tens of thousands of cellular devices,⏎and sustaining 24/7 uptime across multiple EMRs and care settings.⏎Finally, fragmented data and delayed feedback loops slow validation and responsible scaling. Data⏎remain siloed across EMRs, device platforms, and payers, and outcomes and utilization data –⏎including claims– are not always available in a timely way. This limits the ability of providers and⏎vendors to evaluate real-world performance, detect model drift, and iteratively improve tools based⏎on outcomes and utilization.⏎B. Regulatory and programmatic actions HHS should prioritize⏎1. Support a predictable, risk-based posture that distinguishes between AI as a medical⏎device and AI used for care delivery operations⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by promoting clear, consistent expectations across divisions and⏎encouraging a proportionate, risk-based approach. For non-device clinical AI, HHS should publish⏎practical guidance for governance, auditability, and change management (including when models⏎are updated) rather than creating uncertainty that chills deployment of low-risk workflow tools.⏎HHS should also provide clearer boundary guidance for clinical decision support and workflow⏎tools so developers and health systems can understand when functionality is likely to trigger⏎medical device oversight versus when it is better addressed through operational governance and⏎quality management.⏎2. Enable responsible use of AI while protecting patient information⏎Trust will depend on strong privacy and security safeguards, clear patient-facing transparency, and⏎practical guidance on procurement. HHS can support adoption by publishing model contractual⏎provisions and procurement checklists for AI-enabled services that address data use, security⏎controls, incident response, model monitoring, and shared accountability between vendors and⏎providers.⏎2⏎⏎C. Reimbursement and payment policy recommendations⏎1. Remove time-based barriers that discourage appropriate use of automation and AI in⏎care management⏎As we articulated in Cadence’s prior response to the Health Technology Ecosystem Request for⏎Information (CMS-0042-NC), eliminating or modernizing time-based requirements for team-⏎based care management services such as RPM would encourage investment in technology that⏎delivers more personalized and efficient care. Where automation safely reduces the staff time⏎needed to perform tasks, payment should not penalize adoption of higher-value tools. CMS should⏎consider payment approaches that recognize engagement and outcomes (not minutes) for⏎technology-enabled care, paired with guardrails to prevent low-value utilization.⏎2. Expand access to modern care management models like APCM⏎APCM is an important step toward proactive longitudinal management that is not artificially⏎constrained by minute-counting. HHS should remove restrictions that limit APCM to providers⏎participating in specific value-based models, so more beneficiaries can access technology-enabled,⏎and specifically AI-enabled, care – particularly in rural and underserved areas. Because APCM⏎supports flexible team-based care delivery, it is well-suited to integrating AI that improves⏎productivity and consistency (e.g., intake, summarization, and care gap identification), while⏎preserving clinician accountability.⏎3. Modernize reimbursement to recognize the value of software, interoperability, and⏎AI-enabled clinical support⏎HHS should create reimbursement mechanisms through both fee-for-service and value-based⏎pathways that recognize the costs of software capabilities underpinning technology-enabled care,⏎including interoperability, maintenance, and security. This approach can be paired with guardrails⏎that raise the bar and protect Medicare from low-value utilization, such as requiring 24/7 clinical⏎support and meaningful EMR integration so data and actions are visible in the patient’s chart.⏎4. Enable performance measurement through timely access to Medicare claims and⏎outcomes data⏎HHS can promote accountability and public trust by expanding timely access to Medicare claims⏎data for qualified evaluation and validation of outcomes for AI-enabled clinical care. Claims-based⏎analyses can help quantify cost and utilization effects year-over-year and may be used to validate⏎private sector analyses.⏎D. Interoperability and R&D priorities to accelerate responsible AI⏎Enhanced interoperability is foundational for clinical AI. HHS should continue to advance⏎standardized, API-based exchange across settings and vendors, including by improving the⏎consistency and completeness of longitudinal data available via APIs and supporting⏎3⏎⏎benchmarking tools and representative datasets that enable evaluation across diverse populations.⏎Interoperability policy should prioritize not just data access, but workflow-ready exchange that⏎supports bi-directional communication (data in, actions out) and reduces custom integration⏎timelines for providers. In particular, EMR providers should be mandated to provide read/write⏎API access.⏎Cadence would welcome the opportunity to partner with HHS on demonstration projects and⏎applied research that evaluate AI-enabled care management for chronic disease in Medicare⏎populations, including studies that measure outcomes, clinician burden, and total cost of care when⏎AI is embedded in real workflows.⏎*****⏎Thank you for your consideration. We welcome the opportunity to engage further on the⏎recommendations above and to share data supporting these comments. Should you have any⏎questions or wish to meet, please contact Meryl Holt, General Counsel, at⏎meryl@cadencerpm.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Christopher Altchek⏎Founder & CEO, Cadence⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0401♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from VALID AI, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔VALID AI♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to Request for Information⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎VALID AI Collaborative⏎February 23rd, 2026⏎Introduction⏎VALID AI is a community of more than 300 senior healthcare executives—including CIOs, Chief⏎AI Officers, CMIOs, CISOs, and other health system leaders—from over 70 leading healthcare⏎organizations committed to advancing the responsible adoption of artificial intelligence. Our⏎members and partner organizations represent academic medical centers, integrated delivery⏎systems, payers, community health systems, and innovation leaders.⏎To inform this response, VALID AI worked with its membership to identify shared barriers to⏎adoption and convened a focused working group of member institutions and partners. The⏎working group included leaders from Cedars-Sinai, Sutter Health, Valir Health, CTel, and the⏎American Medical Association. The perspectives reflected here represent real-world deployment⏎experience across diverse care settings.⏎Our recommendations focus on three questions:⏎1. How can AI become deflationary rather than cost-inflating?⏎2. How can AI improve access to care?⏎3. What can HHS and its components do to accelerate responsible and secure⏎adoption?⏎⏎I. Making AI Deflationary⏎AI will reduce healthcare costs only if the cost of validating, integrating, monitoring, and⏎reimbursing AI is lower than the value it generates. Today, much of the expense stems from⏎duplicative validation, unclear regulatory boundaries, fragmented monitoring, and⏎reimbursement misalignment—not model development itself. The federal government can⏎reduce this structural friction by centralizing non-differentiated trust-building functions.⏎Policy Actions⏎1. Establish a Risk-Tiered Federal Validation Framework⏎● FDA, ONC, and NIH should coordinate on a dynamic, risk-based validation portfolio⏎structured by clinical impact and AI function type.⏎● Rigor should scale with risk.⏎● Benchmarks, subgroup performance standards, and robustness protocols should be⏎standardized and publicly available.⏎2. Launch Federally Coordinated AI Validation Programs⏎● HHS should support structured validation programs that test models against⏎standardized criteria once at high quality and make results broadly accessible.⏎● FDA should align regulated product oversight; ONC should integrate outputs into health⏎IT certification pathways.⏎3. Develop Representative National Reference Datasets⏎● NIH and NLM should steward diverse, privacy-preserving datasets reflecting rural,⏎underserved, and heterogeneous care environments.⏎● ONC should align datasets with interoperability standards.⏎4. Create a National AI Adverse Event and Risk Signal Repository⏎● FDA and AHRQ should coordinate centralized signal detection for AI-related adverse⏎events.⏎● ONC should ensure interoperable reporting standards.⏎Centralized signal detection would reduce redundant investigations and accelerate improvement⏎cycles.⏎5. Align Reimbursement With AI-Enabled Value⏎● CMS should systematically evaluate which CPT, HCPCS, and value-based payment⏎measures are directly affected by specific AI applications.⏎⏎● CMS should partner with providers and industry to identify AI categories that measurably⏎improve quality metrics, risk adjustment, documentation accuracy, or care management.⏎● The CMS Innovation Center should pilot reimbursement models recognizing AI-enabled⏎workflow and decision support.⏎Without reimbursement alignment, AI risks adding administrative burden rather than reducing⏎cost.⏎II. Improving Access to Care Through AI⏎In rural and underserved settings, the alternative to AI-supported care is often delayed or absent⏎care. This shifts the risk-benefit calculus. AI can function as a capacity amplifier—extending⏎expertise, reducing provider burden, and enabling earlier intervention.⏎Federal policy can help ensure AI expands access rather than concentrating it in well-resourced⏎systems.⏎Policy Actions⏎1. Prioritize Rural and Underserved Demonstration Projects⏎● CMMI and HRSA should fund AI-enabled pilots focused on access expansion,⏎time-to-intervention, provider burden reduction, and avoided downstream utilization.⏎● AHRQ should develop evaluation frameworks tailored to low-resource environments.⏎2. Recognize Context-Sensitive Risk Differentiation⏎● FDA and CMS guidance should acknowledge that acceptable AI deployment thresholds⏎may differ where specialist access is limited, while maintaining clear safety monitoring⏎standards.⏎3. Strengthen Interoperability as Access Infrastructure⏎● ONC should modernize API and certification requirements to reduce integration friction⏎for AI tools.⏎● Standardized AI performance reporting formats should be incorporated into health IT⏎certification pathways.⏎Access expansion depends on reducing deployment barriers in distributed and⏎resource-constrained settings.⏎⏎III. Accelerating Responsible Adoption Across⏎the System⏎Adoption slows when organizations face regulatory ambiguity, unclear liability boundaries, and⏎inconsistent transparency expectations. Coordinated federal guidance can reduce uncertainty⏎without lowering standards.⏎Policy Actions⏎1. Provide Cross-Component Regulatory Clarity⏎● FDA, ONC, and CMS should issue coordinated guidance clarifying classification of⏎ambient AI systems, data ingestion boundaries, and responsibility allocation between⏎developers and providers.⏎2. Support AI-Enabled Transparency and Consent Mechanisms⏎● ONC and CMS should encourage or incentivize structured disclosure tools that inform⏎patients when AI influences care and how oversight is maintained.⏎3. Reduce Procurement and Integration Friction⏎● HHS should explore standardized AI performance reporting templates and safe-harbor⏎guidance for adoption of federally validated tools.⏎Clear rules and shared infrastructure procurement cycles and lower systemwdie adoption costs.⏎Conclusion⏎AI will become deflationary when the federal government reduces the system-wide cost of⏎creating trust. By standardizing validation expectations, supporting validation programs, building⏎representative datasets, centralizing signal detection, aligning reimbursement, and coordinating⏎regulatory clarity, HHS can eliminate redundant effort and lower structural barriers.⏎Paired with targeted support for rural and underserved deployment, this approach allows AI to⏎serve as a capacity amplifier—expanding access while maintaining accountability.⏎The objective is responsible acceleration: lower friction, aligned incentives, and shared⏎infrastructure that enables safe, scalable adoption across the healthcare system.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0402♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Summary of recommendations (01, 02, 08, 05, 010)⏎⏎1. Require continuous evaluation for any federally supported Al implementation.⏎Make evaluation a non-negotiable component of Al deployment, aligned with the Evidence⏎Act's intent, with clear expectations for what is measured, how often, and how results are⏎used to adjust or even pause implementation (21, Q2, 08, O10).⏎⏎2. Build an evaluation framework that uses EHR and EHR-use metadata.⏎Use existing EHR metadata (e.9., audit logs, documentation pattems, workflow traces) to⏎measure how Al affects workflow, team dynamics, and the cognitive environment, rather⏎than just whether the Alis on (Q1, G2, Q8, Q10). '⏎⏎3. Tie funding and incentives to demonstrated value, not just use⏎Link grants, pilots, and payment adjustments to improvements in a small set of meaningful,⏎feasible, and viable measures (for example, burden reduction, timeliness, care⏎coordination), rather than to raw Al adoption rates (Q1, Q2, Q10).7⏎⏎4. Focus on “automation of the obvious, flagging of the not-so-obvious.”⏎Encourage Al that automates routine, well-understood tasks and surfaces non-obvious risks,⏎while preserving human expertise and judgment for complex and atypical cases (01, G2,⏎9, O10).⏎⏎5. Guard against automation complacency and overload.⏎Require designs and training that keep humans actively engaged, monitor for over-reliance⏎and new types of error, and ensure clinicians and staff can operate safely even when Al⏎systems fail or are unavailable (1, 2, 09, Q10).*⏎⏎6. Support adoption in resource-constrained and non-tech-hub settings⏎Pair Al incentives with infrastructure and technical assistance for rural, small, safety-net, and⏎other resource-limited organizations, so Al does not deepen the divide between large, well-⏎resourced systems and everyone else (01, O2, 08, 09).⏎⏎fT. Close the “model-to-practice’ gap for federally funded Al.⏎Encourage NIH and other HHS funders to support not only model development but also⏎implementation, evaluation, and operations partnerships, so that fewer Al models end at⏎publication and more are translated into tools that improve care in practice (01, O2, 08,⏎O70).⏎⏎. : wi. [mine ih eody 21⏎⏎1 https: w.jonesday.com/fen/ insights 2026, his-sipnals-policy-dinection-to-aocelerate-adoption-of-al-in-⏎clinical-care⏎⏎4 httos:/''ojs.unsysdipital.com/ index.php, jias/article/views10.91595%752F jias.v3i2.916/0⏎hittes://pmc.ocbhinim.nih.govfarticles/PMChs8 9673,⏎⏎4 https: prc ncbd.ndinm. nih, articles ‘PMC? 584400), https: fhsph harvard. edu/emac. news /iriplenentation-⏎⏎gap-artificial-intelligence-help-patients-clinical-practices;♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0403♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from SAS Institute Inc. (SAS), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔SAS Institute Inc. (SAS)♔♔♔♔♔♔Still working on⏎Title Page⏎Request for Information (RFI)⏎(#RIN 0955–AA13): Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care⏎⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and⏎Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy (ASTP) and⏎Office of the National Coordinator⏎for Health Information Technology⏎(ONC), Department of Health and⏎Human Services (DHHS)⏎⏎23 February 2026⏎Submitted by:⏎Campbell Buhrow⏎Account Executive⏎+1 703-310-5722⏎campbell.buhrow@sas.com⏎This proposal is the confidential and proprietary property of⏎SAS Institute Inc. It may contain approaches, techniques,⏎and other information proprietary to SAS, and shall not be⏎disclosed in whole or in part to third parties without the⏎prior written consent of SAS.⏎Copyright 2026 SAS Institute Inc. All Rights Reserved⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Contents⏎The Interactive Response Index beginning on page 27 provides an index of responses to your⏎questions.⏎Title Page ........................................................................................................ 1⏎Contents .......................................................................................................... i⏎Executive Summary ....................................................................................... 1⏎RFI Response ................................................................................................. 2⏎II. Solicitation of Public Comments Regulation ........................................... 2⏎Regulation ............................................................................................................................ 2⏎Reimbursement .................................................................................................................... 4⏎Research & Development .................................................................................................... 5⏎Specific Questions ...................................................................................... 6⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care? ........................................................................................ 6⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal⏎Regulations citations. ........................................................................................................... 8⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them? ..................................................................................................................... 9⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)? ........................................................................... 10⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care? ...................................................................................................................... 11⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and⏎help reduce costs? ............................................................................................................. 12⏎Case Study #1: Taipei Veterans General Hospital ............................................................ 12⏎Case Study #2: Hospital Lillebælt ...................................................................................... 12⏎Case Study #3: Large Healthcare Payer ........................................................................... 13⏎Case Study #4: The Sax Institute ...................................................................................... 13⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care? ............................................. 14⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools. .......................................................................... 14⏎Executive Summary⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page i⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? ............................. 15⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care? ............................................................................... 16⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical⏎care? .................................................................................................................................. 16⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part⏎of clinical care? .................................................................................................................. 17⏎Appendix: SAS Health ................................................................................. 18⏎Interactive Response Index ........................................................................ 27⏎Back Cover ................................................................................................... 28⏎Executive Summary⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page ii⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Executive Summary⏎The U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) is seeking to accelerate the⏎responsible adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care. While AI⏎technologies have advanced rapidly, their integration into routine care delivery remains⏎inconsistent, fragmented, and slowed by operational and governance complexity. HHS is⏎uniquely positioned to shape a national framework that enables innovation while preserving⏎patient safety, equity, privacy, and public trust. SAS appreciates the opportunity to respond to⏎this Request for Information and to share our perspective on how HHS can catalyze scalable,⏎trustworthy AI adoption across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎HHS is confronting a pivotal challenge: how to unlock AI’s transformative potential while⏎ensuring safety, accountability, and equitable outcomes at the enterprise and population⏎scale. Despite significant innovation, implementation remains hindered by siloed data, limited⏎interoperability, workforce readiness gaps, and governance structures not designed for⏎adaptive, continuously learning systems. Legal and operational uncertainty, particularly⏎around model accountability, bias monitoring, performance drift, and lifecycle oversight,⏎further constrains responsible deployment. Without a coordinated national approach⏎grounded in operational rigor, AI will remain confined to isolated pilots rather than driving⏎durable, system-wide improvements in quality, efficiency, and patient outcomes.⏎Many healthcare organizations are experimenting with AI in clinical decision support,⏎predictive risk modeling, workflow automation, and other use cases. However, these efforts⏎often rely on fragmented tools or conceptual governance overlays rather than deeply⏎integrated, enterprise-grade platforms. HHS’s priorities like protecting patient privacy,⏎advancing health equity, reducing clinician burden, and modernizing care delivery, require⏎infrastructure that unifies data, AI development, deployment, monitoring, and governance⏎within a single operational framework. SAS envisions a healthcare ecosystem in which AI⏎systems are transparent, bias-tested, and continuously monitored. These AI systems should⏎be explainable by design, and seamlessly embedded into clinical workflows, delivering⏎measurable improvements in safety, quality, and cost performance at both individual and⏎population levels.⏎SAS enables the healthcare ecosystem of HHS’s future through a fully mature, end-to-⏎end AI and analytics platform purpose-built for regulated environments. Unlike solutions⏎that layer governance onto experimental AI tools, SAS embeds model management,⏎explainability, bias detection, version control, and performance monitoring directly into the⏎core analytics engine. Our high-performance platform integrates structured and unstructured⏎data at scale, supports real-time and batch analytics, and operationalizes models reliably⏎across enterprise and multi-institution environments. SAS’s privacy-enhancing technologies⏎enable collaboration and innovation while minimizing exposure of sensitive patient⏎information. This unified approach ensures AI is not only developed responsibly, but⏎deployed, monitored, and sustained reliably in production environments.⏎With decades of experience supporting federal health agencies and other highly regulated⏎industries, SAS brings proven, high-reliability analytics capabilities that extend beyond⏎governance principles into real-world operationalization. SAS uniquely combines advanced⏎analytics performance, population-level modeling expertise, embedded trust mechanisms,⏎and enterprise-scale deployment capability within a single, interoperable platform. By⏎partnering with SAS, HHS and its stakeholders can transition from fragmented AI pilots to a⏎secure, scalable, and accountable ecosystem. This ecosystem can be one in which clinicians⏎experience reduced burden, administrators gain defensible insight into outcomes and ROI,⏎regulators maintain continuous transparency and oversight, and patients benefit from safer,⏎more equitable care powered by AI.⏎Executive Summary⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 1⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎RFI Response⏎II. Solicitation of Public Comments Regulation⏎Regulation⏎As the nation’s principal health regulator, HHS helps shape the environment in which AI for⏎clinical care is developed, evaluated, and deployed. HHS seeks to establish a regulatory⏎posture on AI that is well understood, predictable, and proportionate to any risks presented to⏎enable rapid innovation while protecting patients and the confidentiality of their identifiable⏎health information, and maintaining public trust. We seek feedback on how current HHS⏎regulations impact AI adoption and use for clinical care.⏎SAS appreciates HHS’s commitment to fostering rapid innovation, while safeguarding patient⏎privacy and public trust. As a global leader in advanced analytics and AI for five decades,⏎SAS believes that clear, risk-based regulatory guidance can accelerate responsible AI⏎adoption across clinical settings. Current HHS regulations, particularly those governing⏎patient data privacy, information sharing, and interoperability shape the environment in which⏎AI for clinical care is developed, evaluated, and deployed. SAS has a long history supporting⏎healthcare organizations in developing, validating, and operationalizing AI solutions in ways⏎that strengthen transparency, governance, and patient protections. Our feedback on how⏎current HHS regulations impact AI adoption and use for clinical care derives from the⏎following customer success stories:⏎• Getting ahead of the next pandemic with AI and machine-learning fueled drug discovery |⏎SAS⏎• Duke Health and SAS Formalize Strategic Analytics and AI Collaboration | Duke Health⏎• Research Roundup: SAS Brings Potential for New Research on the All of Us Researcher⏎Workbench | All of Us Research Program | NIH⏎• CEO Roundtable on Cancer: Can data sharing lead to cancer discoveries?⏎• DNA analysis unlocks better health for communities | SAS⏎Across healthcare organizations, AI adoption is most often slowed not by lack of interest or⏎value, but by uncertainty around how to implement AI safety and compliantly. Current⏎regulatory requirements and guidance can be difficult to interpret consistently across⏎organizations, especially since AI systems evolve rapidly and require ongoing monitoring after⏎deployment. Because of this, organizations and agencies may delay adoption or deploy AI in⏎fragmented ways that increases operations risks and reduces transparency.⏎SAS believes HHS can meaningfully accelerate responsible AI adoption by providing clearer⏎expectations for AI governance, validation, and monitoring (explicitly linked to how current⏎regulations apply to AI) while maintaining flexibility for innovation. SAS outlines how a few⏎current HHS regulations impact AI adoption below:⏎HIPAA Privacy and Security Rule Requirements: AI solutions that ingest, analyze, or⏎generate output using PHI are subject to HIPAA requirements, including privacy limitations on⏎use/disclosure and security safeguards. These guardrails are necessary for trust but can slow⏎deployment when uncertainty around how HIPAA obligations apply to evolving and modern AI⏎workflows, so consistent and practical implementation is necessary.⏎Information Blocking Regulations: The information blocking framework is essential for⏎improving access to electronic health information, which is also essential for AI development⏎and performance monitoring. However, organizations may be unsure about how to apply⏎privacy and security limits while still meeting expectations for data access, especially across⏎vendors and external systems.⏎Interoperability and Health IT Certification Rules: Clinical AI depends on consistent, high-⏎quality, interoperable data, and traceable integration in clinical workflows. The HHS ONC⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 2⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Health IT Certification Program requirements shape how EHR platforms expose data and how⏎capabilities like security, auditability, access controls, and interoperability are implemented. In⏎practice, differences in how systems are implemented and limits on pulling data and⏎maintaining audit trails make it harder to deploy AI consistently across settings.⏎Clarity for Non-Medical Devices: For AI tools that fall outside traditional FDA medical device⏎classifications, organizations often face uncertainty about where regulatory expectations⏎begin and end, reinforcing the need for HHS-led guidance on governance, evaluation, and⏎monitoring for non-device clinical AI.⏎HIPAA Right of Access and Individual Access Rule: HIPAA Right of Access requirements⏎raise important questions about how AI-generated insights, summaries, or recommendations⏎should be treated within the designated record set, particularly as patient-facing AI tools⏎become more common. Better clarity on how these requirements apply to AI outputs would⏎support transparency, patient engagement, and consistent implementation across healthcare⏎organizations.⏎Opportunities to Support Responsible AI Adoption: SAS believes HHS can meaningfully⏎accelerate responsible adoption by providing clearer expectations for AI governance,⏎validation, and monitoring, while maintaining flexibility for innovation. SAS can support these⏎objectives by helping to:⏎• Establish clear and repeatable AI governance practices that embed auditability,⏎accountability, and oversight across model development, validation, deployment, and⏎monitoring. Strong lifecycle practices help organizations apply HIPAA, civil rights, and⏎other regulatory requirements consistently when using AI in clinical care.⏎• Promote transparency through standardized model documentations (e.g., model cards)⏎that explain what an AI system is intended to do, its limitations, how it was trained and⏎evaluated, how well it performs, and how it should be monitored. Clear documentation⏎supports review, oversight, and alignment with audit and patient access expectations.⏎• Support ongoing monitoring after deployment by tracking performance, detecting changes⏎in data or results, and conducting routine reviews. Continuous evaluation helps ensure AI⏎systems remain safe, effective, and fair as clinical practices and patient populations⏎change.⏎• Support privacy-preserving AI development through the use of synthetic data, enabling⏎model development, testing, and validation when access to PHI is restricted. Synthetic⏎datasets can enable model development and validation without exposing patient⏎identities.⏎• Support interoperable and traceable integration of AI into clinical workflows, aligned with⏎ONC certification and interoperability requirements. Traceability across systems helps⏎organizations demonstrate appropriate data use, maintain audit trails, and meet⏎information-sharing expectations.⏎• Support responsible adoption of generative and agentic AI by encouraging governance⏎approaches that prioritize safety, transparency, and setting clinical boundaries, including⏎guardrails when appropriate and enabling human-in-the-loop review for higher impact⏎clinical use cases. These practices help manage risk while allowing innovation to move⏎forward.⏎• Build workforce readiness through education and training, helping clinicians and staff⏎understand how to interpret AI outputs, recognize limitations, and raise concerns.⏎Improving AI literacy reduces misuse, over-reliance, and loss of trust.⏎• Encourage consistent benchmarking and evaluation across settings, so organizations can⏎compare model performance and fairness using common data and measures. Shared⏎evaluation approaches reduce uncertainty and help build confidence in AI adoption.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 3⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Reimbursement⏎HHS’s payment policies and programs have massive effects on how health care is delivered⏎in the United States, oftentimes with unintended consequences. Hypothetically, if a payer is⏎taking financial risk for the long-term health and health costs of an individual, that payer will⏎have an inherent incentive to promote access to the highest-value interventions for patients.⏎Under government designed and dictated feefor- service regimes, however, coverage and⏎reimbursement decisions are slow. Rarely does covering new innovations reduce net⏎spending; and waste, fraud, and abuse is difficult to prevent, oftentimes leading to massive⏎spending bubbles on concentrated items or services that are not commensurate with the⏎value of such products. Given the inherent flaws in legacy payment systems, we seek to⏎ensure that the potential promises of AI innovations are not diminished through inertia and⏎instead such payment systems are modernized to meet the needs of a changing healthcare⏎system. We seek feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive⏎and ability to promote access to high- value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among⏎clinical care AI tool builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical⏎care.⏎Reform Payment Structures to incentivize AI-enabled Care with transparency and⏎governance to Reflect Value, Not Volume: Traditional fee-for-service (FFS) payment⏎systems, which reimburse based on volume of services, can slow coverage decisions,⏎disincentivize innovation adoption, and inadequately recognize the value of AI-enabled care.⏎Reform aligning financial incentives with health outcomes and efficiency will encourage⏎payers to deploy high-value AI tools and invest in evidence generation.⏎Policy Changes:⏎• Create reimbursement pathways that explicitly value AI contributions to care delivery,⏎such as new CPT categories or modifiers for AI-augmented services like clinical note-⏎taking, assistance with diagnosis, and post-operative follow-ups as well as AI-enabled⏎medical devices, so that payment reflects the incremental diagnostic, prognostic, or⏎efficiency benefit of AI.⏎• Incorporate outcomes-based or performance-driven payments that reward measurable⏎clinical improvements (e.g., accuracy, reduced adverse events, reduced avoidable⏎admissions), aligning reimbursements with the quality and value of AI use rather than⏎procedural counts.⏎• Expand value-based payment models (VBPMs) that explicitly incorporate AI-enabled care⏎into shared savings, bundled payments, or account-based arrangements, so payers and⏎providers benefit financially but only from improved outcomes.⏎• Authorize pilot and demonstration programs through entities like the CMS Innovation⏎Center (CMMI) to test AI-focused payment innovations that quantify AI’s contribution to⏎cost and quality outcomes.⏎• Adjust enrollee cost-sharing under value-based insurance design so that high-value AI-⏎enabled interventions are more affordable for patients, increasing uptake and supporting⏎payer adoption.⏎• Establish transparent reimbursement frameworks that account for the diversity of AI tools⏎(e.g., software as a medical device, integrated clinical decision support, diagnostic⏎assistance) and avoid one-size-fits-all rules.⏎• Promote standardized evidence and evaluation requirements such that payers and⏎developers operate on common metrics of clinical utility, safety, and cost-effectiveness,⏎lowering market barriers to entry and foster competition for new innovators.⏎Government reimbursement policy changes—focused on value recognition, outcome-⏎driven payment structures, transparent and flexible pathways, value-based care⏎integration, and evidence generation incentives—can ensure that payers are financially⏎motivated and operationally empowered to promote high-value AI clinical⏎interventions. These changes also help catalyze competition among clinical AI builders and⏎broaden access and affordability of AI tools for patients and providers alike.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 4⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Research & Development⏎HHS supports one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems, catalyzing innovation to⏎supplement the market. By enabling applied AI research & development, care delivery⏎research and implementation science, as well as AI entrepreneurship in health care, we can⏎better translate AI technologies from concept to clinical use. We seek input on ways in which⏎HHS may invest in research & development (including public-private partnerships and⏎cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs)) to integrate AI in care⏎delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the health and wellbeing⏎of all Americans.⏎HHS has a unique opportunity to accelerate the translation of AI technologies from concept to⏎clinical impact by building on proven public-private partnership models that have already⏎transformed health research and safety surveillance at scale. Strategic investments in applied⏎AI research, cooperative agreements, and shared infrastructure can both de-risk innovation⏎and create durable market demand for AI-enabled solutions that improve health outcomes for⏎all Americans.⏎A leading example is the NIH All of Us Research Program, where SAS serves as a key⏎industry partner helping enable secure, scalable analytics across one of the most diverse⏎longitudinal health datasets ever assembled. SAS technologies have supported the program’s⏎ability to curate, manage, and analyze complex multimodal data (EHRs, genomics,⏎wearables, and patient-reported outcomes) in a manner that is privacy-preserving, auditable,⏎and accessible to a broad research community. This has directly empowered researchers to⏎develop, test, and validate AI models across populations historically underrepresented in⏎clinical research, improving both the scientific rigor and equity of AI-enabled discoveries. The⏎All of Us model illustrates how HHS can stimulate AI innovation not simply through grants, but⏎through platform-level public-private partnerships that provide governed access to high-value⏎data, modern analytic environments, and interoperable tools. By investing further in such⏎collaborative research ecosystems, HHS can ensure that AI solutions are clinically relevant,⏎generalizable, and ready for deployment in real-world care settings.⏎Similarly, the FDA Sentinel Initiative demonstrates how a long-term public-private⏎collaboration can operationalize AI and advanced analytics for direct impact on patient safety⏎and regulatory science. Through Sentinel, SAS has partnered with the FDA, Harvard Pilgrim⏎and a nationwide network of data partners to enable near–real-time safety surveillance of⏎drugs, biologics, and medical devices. This work has advanced sophisticated signal detection,⏎comparative effectiveness, and risk stratification capabilities using real-world data at scale.⏎Sentinel reflects a model where cooperative research and development agreements and⏎shared analytic standards allow AI innovation to mature within mission-critical environments,⏎ensuring that models are not only technically sound but trusted by regulators, clinicians, and⏎manufacturers alike. Expanding this approach into other domains of care delivery would allow⏎HHS to accelerate AI adoption while simultaneously creating long-term market opportunities⏎for validated production-grade AI systems.⏎Together, these examples suggest that HHS can most effectively catalyze AI in care⏎delivery by investing in durable, interoperable, and privacy-preserving research⏎infrastructure, co-developed with industry and academia, rather than isolated pilot⏎projects. By scaling or creating new partnership-driven platforms like All of Us and Sentinel,⏎HHS can ensure that AI innovation is not only rapid, but clinically credible, economically⏎sustainable, and broadly accessible across the U.S. healthcare system.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 5⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Specific Questions⏎In addition to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, HHS seeks input on the following specific⏎questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector⏎innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎Despite meaningful progress in digital health transformation, persistent data fragmentation⏎and interoperability challenges continue to limit the speed, scalability, and reliability of AI⏎innovation. Compounding these structural barriers, many health care professionals lack⏎sufficient education in analytics and AI, which undermines confidence and trust in AI-driven⏎solutions. This trust gap is further exacerbated by limited transparency into how models are⏎developed, governed, and validated, and their unclear or unproven impact to clinical⏎outcomes.⏎Structural Data Barriers:⏎Data fragmentation. The availability of new sources of health and social determinant data is⏎expanding rapidly with the proliferation of wearables, remote monitoring devices, genomics,⏎digital biomarkers, and patient-generated health data. Patients are offered options to have⏎continuous visibility into their health, and clinicians increasingly recognize the value of real-⏎time signals such as sleep quality, heart rate variability, and other vital signs. This growth in⏎data sources has, however, intensified fragmentation. Critical information remains distributed⏎across disparate systems, vendor platforms, and emerging point solutions, making it difficult⏎for robust, unbiased AI models to be developed and trained because patient records are⏎incomplete.⏎Unstructured Data. In addition, a significant portion of healthcare’s most valuable information⏎lives in unstructured formats, including free text clinical notes, PDFs, scanned documents,⏎images, and even faxes. These sources often contain contextual nuances that drive accurate⏎clinical interpretation and high performing AI models. While progress is being made through⏎technologies such as SAS Document Analysis for Health Records (SAS Models | SAS),⏎natural language processing, and large language models (LLMs), the extraction and⏎normalization of this unstructured content is still a major operational challenge which⏎contributes to fragmentation.⏎Interoperability and Workflow Challenges. Even with the industrywide commitment to⏎standards such as Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR), interoperability remains⏎inconsistent and unreliable. Data exchange varies significantly across electronic health⏎records (EHRs), health information networks, and digital health tools, often requiring costly⏎bespoke integrations or manual workflows. These inconsistencies hinder the ability to build⏎scalable AI solutions that integrate into existing workflows. Providers will be unwilling to adopt⏎if there are additional steps or actions which require them to work outside of their source⏎system or existing workflows.⏎Without the ability to access de-identified, accurate, and comprehensive whole patient⏎records, innovators are unable to validate and test the scalability of AI-enabled innovations.⏎SAS can help overcome these barriers through health data platform solutions to empower the⏎care continuum with deep analytics capabilities and insights. These solutions, including SAS⏎Health, automate the integration and processing of vast volumes of data from multiple⏎sources, allowing healthcare organizations to make informed decisions across their workflows⏎while providing secure data ingestion and management, adhering to FHIR specification⏎standards to facilitate healthcare data interoperability, and supporting the Outcomes⏎Partnership Common Data Model standard to visualize and analyze observational data.⏎These solutions facilitate the design and execution of analysis on industry-standardized⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 6⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎clinical data. The inclusion of embedded AI/ML enables predictive insights, supporting⏎proactive decision-making across healthcare organizations, catering to various healthcare use⏎cases, from population health management to financial oversight.⏎As a part of our platform solution, we offer SAS Document Analysis for Health Records which⏎unlocks the rich clinical detail buried in unstructured notes by transforming scanned PDFs,⏎faxed records, and narrative documentation into structured, analytically ready data. Using⏎advanced Optical Character Recognition and clinically informed, deterministic extraction⏎models, data can be extracted to round out otherwise lacking data sets. This automated⏎workflow increases efficiency, reliability, and auditability while overcoming common⏎challenges such as dirty data, repetitive progress notes, and multi-hundred-page record sets,⏎making once-inaccessible insights available for downstream analytics and decision support.⏎The deterministic capabilities of document analysis are complemented by a contextual⏎intelligence layer that can retrieve, assemble, and present the specific evidence, policies,⏎guidelines, and historical precedent required to support the data, or furthermore, support the⏎analytic result.⏎Workforce Enablement: To fully realize the potential of analytics and AI in healthcare,⏎targeted education and workforce enablement programs are urgently needed, similar in scale⏎and intent to the Meaningful Use retraining initiatives that accompanied the adoption of⏎electronic health records. Just as federal investment and structured education were critical to⏎helping clinicians effectively implement and trust electronic health record (EHR) systems,⏎comparable funding and coordinated training are now required to build analytics and AI⏎literacy. Healthcare professionals need to be educated on how to interpret AI outputs,⏎fostering the collaboration of human experts and AI systems. Without deliberate investment in⏎education, change management, and applied learning, AI innovation risks outpacing the⏎workforce’s ability to adopt, govern, and use it safely and effectively.⏎As the role of the health care professional is fundamentally evolving as AI becomes more⏎deeply embedded in clinical and operational decision-making, rather than serving as⏎continuous manual operators, clinicians and allied health professionals will be increasingly⏎positioned as safety control engineers, overseeing, validating, and intervening in AI-driven⏎processes when anomalies, uncertainty, or risk emerge. This evolution closely mirrors the⏎transformation of pilots in modern aviation, whose responsibilities have shifted from direct⏎control to supervisory oversight of highly autonomous systems, intervening primarily in⏎exceptional or emergency scenarios. To ensure this transition improves care quality and⏎safety rather than introducing new risks, rigorous, interdisciplinary research is needed to⏎define these emerging responsibilities and to deliberately redesign the roles, workflows, and⏎training of both direct care providers and their support teams in an AI-enabled health care⏎system.⏎Addressing the Trust Gap: SAS has long demonstrated a sustained commitment to⏎responsible and trustworthy AI by embedding governance, transparency, and accountability⏎across the full analytics and AI lifecycle. As AI adoption accelerates and regulatory⏎expectations evolve, SAS has consistently emphasized the importance of balancing rapid⏎innovation with rigorous oversight to ensure AI systems are reliable, ethical, and aligned with⏎societal and organizational values.⏎Operationally, SAS advances trustworthy AI through an integrated governance framework⏎built on its platform, enabling organizations to manage, document, validate, and audit AI⏎models throughout their entire lifecycle. These AI governance capabilities provide centralized⏎visibility into AI use cases, models, and agents, while embedding policy controls, risk⏎management, and human in the loop oversight at critical decision points. By treating AI⏎governance not as a standalone function but as the next stage of maturity in enterprise⏎governance, SAS supports‑ c‑usto‑mers in scaling AI responsibly across industries, including⏎healthcare, where transparency, fairness, and regulatory readiness are essential.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 7⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎At the platform level, we deliver practical, clinician and stakeholder friendly transparency⏎through capabilities such as model cards that function as “nutrition label like” summaries for⏎AI models. These model cards provide clear, easy‑to read insights i‑nto a model’s intended⏎use, training data, performance, fairness, and limitations, helping h‑ealthc‑are professionals⏎and decision makers quickly understand what a m‑ode‑l does, how it was developed, and⏎where it can be trusted. By embedding these trustworthy AI features directly into the analytics⏎workflow, SA‑S reduces friction between innovation and governance, enabling healthcare⏎organizations and its front-line workers to adopt AI with greater confidence, clarity, and⏎measurable value.⏎Figure 1. SAS AI Model Cards for Analytics Workflow.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic⏎design changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize⏎the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What⏎HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be⏎revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI⏎in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and⏎applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎N/A⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 8⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of⏎AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing⏎governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues⏎exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎As AI is increasingly embedded into clinical care through applications that fall outside⏎traditional medical device classifications, new legal and implementation challenges emerge⏎that are not fully addressed by existing governance and accountability structures. These⏎challenges are less about individual algorithms in isolation and more about how AI systems⏎are governed, monitored, and held accountable across their lifecycle within complex⏎healthcare environments.⏎A central issue is accountability in hybrid human-AI decision-making. In many non-medical⏎device use cases, AI informs but does not directly execute decisions, creating ambiguity⏎around responsibility in adverse outcomes. Questions remain regarding how liability should⏎be shared among developers, healthcare organizations, and clinical users, and how⏎traceability should support legal review and indemnification. SAS’s experience across⏎regulated industries demonstrates the importance of clear governance structures, auditable⏎documentation, and defined roles spanning development, deployment, and monitoring of AI⏎models. HHS can play a constructive role by promoting consistent accountability and⏎documentation standards for AI that is used in clinical contexts.⏎Privacy and data protection present another major challenge, particularly as AI relies on⏎large, diverse datasets for training, validation, and continuous learning. Risks related to re-⏎identification, secondary use, and expanding exposure surfaces grow as models evolve. SAS⏎strongly supports privacy-by-design approaches that embed anonymization, access control,⏎and data minimization into AI architectures from inception. In this context, synthetic data⏎offers another powerful mechanism to enable innovation while reducing exposure to sensitive⏎patient information. In higher-risk scenarios, federated deployment approaches, where⏎models are trained locally and production data never moves from its original location, provide⏎a viable path to minimizing centralized data risk and government data access laws. HHS can⏎accelerate safe adoption by recognizing synthetic data and federated models as acceptable,⏎privacy-preserving methods for clinical AI development and validation.⏎Transparency and explainability are equally essential for legal defensibility and clinical⏎trust. AI systems often lack formal premarket review, making documentation, performance⏎clarity, and model traceability critical. SAS’s AI governance approach emphasizes and⏎automates rigorous documentation of assumptions, risks, bias testing, and mitigation⏎strategies, alongside version control and decision traceability. HHS can strengthen its⏎capabilities in this area by creating policy around minimum expectations for AI explainability⏎and transparency that are aligned with federal AI trust and civil rights principles.⏎Bias, fairness, and equity risks are particularly acute for AI used in triage prioritization, care⏎access, and resource allocation. Without formal oversight, biased outcomes may persist⏎undetected, exposing providers to legal and reputational risk. SAS’s experience supporting⏎audit and compliance in financial services demonstrates that continuous bias detection and⏎routine audits must be in the operational requirements of any AI used in clinical care. HHS⏎can advance responsible AI by encouraging standardized bias and fairness assessments for⏎AI deployed in federally supported care environments.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 9⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Finally, most healthcare organizations lack scalable governance infrastructure to manage AI⏎across departments and use cases, leading to fragmented oversight and elevated compliance⏎risk. SAS’s pragmatic AI framework emphasizes continuous risk management across the AI⏎lifecycle, informed by decades of experience in highly regulated industries where transparent,⏎scalable governance is foundational. This experience now directly informs a generalizable AI⏎Governance approach for healthcare, enabling organizations to inventory AI systems, monitor⏎performance and drift, audit decisions, and enforce policy consistently.⏎HHS has a critical role not only as a regulator but as a market shaper. We encourage HHS to⏎promote AI governance as core infrastructure rather than an optional compliance standard,⏎supporting reference architectures for responsible AI, and embed governance expectations⏎into funding, reimbursement, and accreditation programs. This would allow HHS to accelerate⏎adoption of AI that is not only innovative, but legally defensible, operationally resilient, and⏎worthy of public trust.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most⏎promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other⏎workflow and human-centered evaluation methods for⏎clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most⏎impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Building on the governance and accountability challenges described above, effective⏎evaluation methods are how those principles are put into practice in clinical settings. As AI is⏎increasingly used in clinical care outside of traditional medical device categories, strong⏎evaluation methods are essential to ensure safety, effectiveness, fairness, and trust over⏎time. Based on SAS’ experience supporting analytics and AI across highly regulated⏎industries, including healthcare, these evaluation practices must recognize that many AI⏎systems continue to change after deployment. As a result, evaluation cannot be a one-time⏎activity and must occur both before and after deployment in real clinical environments.⏎Before deployment, evaluation should focus on whether an AI system is appropriate for its⏎intended clinical purpose. Traditional performance measures like accuracy, sensitivity, and⏎specificity remain important, but they are not enough on their own. SAS’ AI governance⏎approach operationalizes pre-deployment evaluation by embedding performance, bias, and⏎robustness testing directly into the model development lifecycle. Drawing on SAS’ approach⏎to responsible AI, systems should also be evaluated for how well they perform across⏎different patient populations, how they handle incomplete data, and how consistently they⏎perform across care settings. Scenario-based testing, including the use of synthetic data, can⏎help identify weaknesses before an AI system is used in patient care. Bias and fairness⏎testing should also occur upfront, with results clearly documented and addressed when⏎disparities are identified. SAS’ governance framework emphasizes documentation of these⏎findings through standardized model documentation that captures intended use, limitations,⏎and evaluation results. Evaluation should also account for how AI fits into real clinical⏎workflows. Simulation testing, clinician-in-the-loop review, and usability testing helps to⏎assess how AI outputs are understood, trusted, and acted on in practice. From SAS’⏎experience, these methods can reveal risks that may not appear in technical testing alone.⏎Clearly documenting intended use, limitations, and when human judgment should trump AI⏎recommendations could lead to safer implementation and clearer accountability.⏎After deployment, continuous monitoring becomes critical. Many current AI governance⏎approaches fall short in this area. SAS’s experience in operationalizing AI at scale highlights⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 10⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎the importance of ongoing evaluation to track model performance over time, detect data drift,⏎and assess whether changes in patient populations or clinical practice affect outcomes.⏎Useful post-deployment measures include how often AI outputs align with clinical judgment,⏎how frequently recommendations are overridden, how stable the predictions are, and whether⏎the impacts differ across demographic groups. Routine audits, version tracking, and⏎assessments of model updates also help to ensure that changes do not introduce new risks.⏎Also, traceability (i.e., linking predictions to specific model versions, inputs, and contexts) is⏎essential for accountability and retrospective review.⏎Additionally, post-deployment evaluation should examine how AI is actually used by people in⏎practice. Even systems that perform well can create risks if clinicians don’t understand how to⏎interpret the outputs or recognize limitations. Consistent with SAS’s approach to analytics,⏎evaluation should also include whether users understand when AI can appropriately support⏎decisions, when caution is warranted, and how concerns can be raised or escalated. Training⏎and feedback methods that build basic AI literacy and reinforce appropriate use can help⏎reduce misuse, over-reliance of AI, and loss of trust.⏎SAS believes that HHS can meaningfully support these evaluation efforts by investing in both⏎the infrastructure and the people. Grants and cooperative agreements could support the⏎development of standardized evaluation frameworks, common workflows, and benchmarking⏎approaches, as well as fund education and upskilling for frontline healthcare workers in areas⏎such as AI literacy and analytics. Contracts could be used to pilot ongoing monitoring and⏎evaluation approaches in federally supported healthcare settings. In addition, prize⏎competitions, challenge programs, and healthcare-focused hackathons can encourage⏎innovation in evaluation methods while bringing together clinicians, data scientists, and⏎patients to solve real-world problems. Based on SAS’ experience supporting and participating⏎in large-scale analytics and AI hackathons, these forums can be particularly effective for⏎testing practical use cases, advancing evaluation approaches, and accelerating shared⏎learning across the healthcare ecosystem. For example, the 2025 SAS Hackathon Champion⏎Change Bringers team developed an AI-powered monitoring solution for Parkinson’s disease⏎symptoms, illustrating how collaborative, problem-focused competitions can advance⏎evaluation methods, human-centered design, and practical workflows in clinical AI.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities⏎(e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-driven⏎testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Hospital Lillebælt Denmark modernized emergency department diagnostics through the⏎integration of AI, automation, and real time data for purposes of improving traditional⏎emergency care workflows which rely heavily on lab‑oratory diagnostics and can often result in⏎delayed triage and treatment during cr‑itical moments.⏎‑⏎SAS applauds the administration’s focus on how AI can be leveraged by clinicians to better⏎care for patients. SAS agrees that the AI revolution has brought and will continue to bring⏎innovation and value to health care and specifically clinical care. SAS is used by hundreds of⏎health care providers across the world, and we’ve had incredible success in reducing clinician⏎burden, providing actionable clarity from health data and predicting negative outcomes before⏎they happen. Through this work, we also recognize that not all clinicians are data scientists⏎and that evidenced based healthcare may not always understand the potential for bias as risk⏎with AI. This is why SAS has developed what we describe as model cards12. SAS proposes⏎that our platform (specifically our capabilities associated with registering and evaluating⏎1 https://blogs.sas.com/content/subconsciousmusings/2024/08/08/sas-model-cards-now-available/⏎2 https://video.sas.com/detail/video/6360057183112/model-card:-nutrition-label-for-an-ai-model⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 11⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎models) serve as the foundation technology for a proposed, governed assurance lab.⏎Innovators can register AI/ML models (developed in SAS or open source) into a governed⏎repository. This ensures that all models entering an assurance workflow are versioned,⏎traceable, and auditable. SAS can automatically generate model cards (sometimes referred to⏎as scorecards), which summarize a model’s metadata, training data, performance metrics,⏎fairness indicators, assumptions, and limitations.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or⏎exceeded performance and cost expectations and⏎where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve⏎health care outcomes, give new insights on quality,⏎and help reduce costs?⏎SAS can provide numerous case studies of where AI tools have been deployed in clinical⏎care. Following are four case studies which exemplify where AI tools have improved⏎outcomes or addressed the rising cost of care.⏎Case Study #1: Taipei Veterans General Hospital⏎Taipei Veterans General Hospital, one of Taiwan’s leading medical centers, sought to⏎improve outcomes for the country’s 90,000+ hemodialysis patients, many of whom face⏎complications such as heart failure, anemia, and fluid-management discomfort.⏎Using SAS AI and real-time IoMT streaming capabilities, the hospital implemented models⏎which were designed to identify patients in real time, with heart-failure risk. Furthermore,⏎models assisted clinicians in their efforts to optimize dry-weight targets and detect anemia.⏎The results were impressive and reports noted 90% accuracy in heart-failure risk⏎prediction and an 80% reduction in dry-weight adjustment errors. Healthcare⏎professionals were able to identify anemia earlier and reported that by having comprehensive⏎dashboards impacted the amount of time to round on the dialysis unit. They reported a⏎reduction in rounding time from 20 minutes (and up to 40 minutes), reduced to 3 minutes.⏎By integrating streaming data from dialysis machines, national health-insurance databases,⏎health records, and chest-x-ray imaging, clinicians now receive real-time alerts and⏎transparent, interpretable model insights that support clinical decision-making.⏎Agentic AI could further enhance the current workflow which autonomously monitors⏎streaming IoMT data, dynamically invoking predictive and analytic models, by coordinating⏎follow-up actions such as alert prioritization and workflow routing.⏎Case Study #2: Hospital Lillebælt⏎Hospital Lillebælt in Denmark modernized its emergency-department diagnostic workflow by⏎integrating AI, automation, and real-time data, transforming traditionally lab-dependent⏎processes that often delay triage and critical interventions. This advanced approach has⏎streamlined diagnostic throughput, and reduced time-to-decision, enhancing the clinicians’⏎ability to deliver timely, effective emergency care.⏎In this implementation, the flow of real-time laboratory, clinical, and operational data and⏎automated data ingestion and event-stream processing are orchestrated in real time, trigger⏎AI-driven diagnostic models. Clinicians receive both diagnostic interpretations and clinical⏎decision-support recommendations within 60 minutes, enabling faster triage, earlier⏎identification of high-risk patients, and more streamlined coordination between emergency⏎teams and laboratory services.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 12⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎This end-to-end automation ensures a consistent, repeatable, and high-quality diagnostic⏎workflow that improves patient outcomes while reducing clinician burden.⏎Agentic AI could further enhance this workflow, by autonomously coordinating downstream⏎operational actions, such as proactively reserving an appropriate hospital bed, when critical⏎clinical insights are surfaced. As an example, when high-risk signals are detected, agentic AI⏎can, continuously monitor patient acuity signals across clinical, laboratory, and IoMT data⏎streams, Interpret predictive risk outputs in operational context (such as current bed⏎availability, unit capabilities (ICU vs. step-down), and staffing constraints), initiate pre-emptive⏎bed reservation workflows, signaling bed management and patient flow systems to hold or⏎prioritize an appropriate bed and coordinate cross-team actions by notifying emergency,⏎inpatient, laboratory, and environmental services teams in parallel.⏎Case Study #3: Large Healthcare Payer⏎A healthcare payer wanted to understand the benefits of addressing members with behavioral⏎and medical comorbidities. Using SAS, they examined their claims data to better understand⏎which members presented with behavioral vs. medical conditions, which members presented⏎with both and what the impact was after proactively identifying members with behavioral⏎conditions and engaging them with their behavioral health product.⏎The analysis revealed that 82% of adult claimants who presented with behavioral conditions⏎also have medical conditions and 27% of adult claimants who presented with medical⏎conditions also have behavioral conditions.⏎Furthermore, the analysis also revealed that there was a 329% increase in behavioral health⏎claims during 2020 to 2023, resulting in a 110% increase in behavioral health spending, and⏎25% of members with behavioral health conditions drove 43% of the book of business spend.⏎The costs for those conditions were noted to be 3-6 times higher for members who had not⏎received behavioral health treatment.⏎Predictive analytics were built to proactively identify members for outreach, for purposes of⏎directing them to programs offered by the payer’s existing flagship behavioral health product.⏎As a result of this initiative:⏎• $193 per member per year was experienced in medical cost savings⏎• $2,505 in cost savings per member in the 15 months post-diagnosis of a behavioral⏎condition⏎• There was a 27% increase in utilization of their Behavioral Health Flagship Product,⏎including an increase in virtual behavioral health claims⏎• 15% more members received care⏎Case Study #4: The Sax Institute⏎Headquartered in Sydney, Australia, the Sax Institute is not-for-profit organization that brings⏎research to the frontlines of policy, programs and services. They connect more than 70 public⏎health research organizations across Australia into a national network through the Secure⏎Unified Research Environment (SURE) platform, powered by SAS.⏎More than 800 researchers and 300 SAS users rely on SURE to access nationally linked⏎health data. Over 366 active research projects have been conducted using SURE, with the⏎Institute’s most ambitious initiatives being:⏎• 45 and Up Study - Australia’s largest ongoing investigation into health and aging⏎• 18 and Up Study - intergenerational study on mental health⏎• Digital twins to simulate hospital workflows and model system-wide changes to improve⏎patient care⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 13⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing⏎bodies within health care organizations have the most⏎influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What⏎are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption⏎of AI in clinical care?⏎Frontline workers. AI adoption in clinical care is most successful when it is driven by the⏎people closest to patient care, frontline workers. While executives, clinical leaders, and⏎governance committees play essential roles in prioritization and oversight, nurses, physicians,⏎allied health professionals, technicians, and administrative support staff exert the greatest⏎real-world influence on whether an AI solution is trusted, utilized, and integrated into daily⏎workflows. Their lived experience, tacit knowledge, and understanding of operational realities⏎determine whether an innovation becomes an asset or an added burden. Frontline workers⏎can identify areas where AI can have the highest impact on patient outcome, efficiency and⏎clinician support.⏎Because of this, frontline teams must be deeply involved in the design, testing, and iteration⏎of any AI-enabled workflow. Strict adherence to design thinking principles (empathy,⏎co-creation, rapid prototyping, and iterative feedback) is critical to ensure solutions align with⏎actual clinical needs and reduce friction at the point of care. When frontline staff are⏎co-designers rather than recipients of change, adoption accelerates, trust increases, and AI⏎becomes an enabler rather than a disruptor.⏎In parallel, organizations should establish and socialize a qualitative and quantitative return⏎on investment (ROI) measurement framework during the design phase. This tool should⏎define expected benefits (e.g., time saved, reduction in cognitive load, improved throughput,⏎enhanced patient safety, improved documentation quality), and outline how those benefits will⏎be captured, monitored, and reported. Measuring actual value in a structured, transparent⏎way enables health systems to demonstrate evidence of impact, justify scaling, and build⏎internal confidence. Clear evidence of ROI is often the catalyst for exponential,⏎organization-wide adoption of successful AI innovations.⏎From an administrative standpoint, there are several hurdles which can be overcome by⏎implementing an agreed upon governance structure, where cross-functional teams make⏎decisions about data, clinical workflows, and technology. They can ensure alignment on an⏎ROI measurement approach, define accountability for AI safety, validation, and continuous⏎monitoring and procurement and compliance cycles that are not designed for iterative or⏎rapidly evolving AI technologies.⏎SAS, in our continued partnership with Duke University, has developed a whitepaper which⏎outlines a pragmatic AI framework for health care providers. The whitepaper can be accessed⏎via this link.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen⏎market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate⏎the development of AI for clinical care? Please⏎consider specific data types, data standards, and⏎benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would most accelerate AI for clinical care in areas where it could⏎improve accessing, harmonizing, and validating high-value clinical data across settings.⏎Improving consistency and portability for core data types like longitudinal electronic health⏎record (EHR) data (e.g., diagnoses, encounters, medications, vitals), lab results, imaging⏎metadata, and unstructured clinical notes, paired with standardized representations like HL7⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 14⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR), may offer the greatest opportunity. These⏎data types are foundational for high-impact AI use cases including care management and⏎clinical decision support but remain difficult to use due to inconsistencies across⏎organizations.⏎In addition, greater adoption of open file formats (e.g., csv, json, parquet) and standards-⏎based APIs could expand market participation by enabling more organizations to securely⏎exchange data and operationalize AI without custom integrations. These open formats⏎support scalable analytics pipelines, reproducible benchmarking, and more seamless⏎collaboration across platforms. Additionally, APIs can enable near real-time data exchange⏎necessary for timely clinical decision support.⏎App-level interoperability is also a growing priority but is still secondary to core system-to-⏎system exchange. Until foundational interoperability across EHRs, labs, and pharmacies is⏎consistent and reliable, the value of app integration will be limited. However, as the⏎infrastructure matures, app-level access becomes essential for patient-facing innovation and⏎real-time decision support, particularly when paired with governance and auditability that⏎support responsible AI adoption.⏎SAS and our healthcare partners have already demonstrated the value of enhanced data⏎exchange and the harmonization for advancing clinical research and innovation. For example,⏎through our collaboration with AstraZeneca, SAS helped redesign clinical and patient data⏎flows to enable greater automation, interoperability, and the flexible use of diverse patient⏎data sources in support of research and regulatory reporting. This in turn accelerated insights⏎from clinical data and supported more efficient decision making across teams. SAS stands⏎ready to partner with HHS and the broader HHS ecosystem to advance interoperability that⏎widens market opportunities, promote research, and will accelerate the development and safe⏎use of AI in clinical care.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and⏎caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption and⏎use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption⏎and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Implementation science research has consistently shown that caregivers find themselves⏎excluded from participating in a patient’s healthcare team (doctors, nurses, etc.). For⏎example, within the Veterans Affairs Administration, family caregivers have felt under-utilized⏎as part of the care process3. AI-driven tools could be used to better incorporate caregiver⏎feedback, as well as provide training to practitioners to shift the culture of care toward⏎improved incorporation of family caregivers.⏎A key part of this process would be developing automated tools for network analysis and⏎predicting acute patient needs, both of which are enhanced through the use of AI-driven tools,⏎backed by expertise from case managers and physicians.⏎SAS has proven such capabilities through our Model Manager tool and other offerings, which⏎would allow stakeholders to continually evaluate patient outcomes relative to caregiver⏎interactions and feedback.⏎3 Training Health Care Practitioners to Include Family Caregivers with Web-Based Learning Modules:⏎https://www.thepermanentejournal.org/doi/pdf/10.7812/TPP/22.037?download=true&__cf_chl_tk=E..MKiubJ3Dk4CrX⏎0hZTV5Y.iBVHfwW3cOi2a9u3UNM-1770404188-1.0.1.1-zo0.Kl3fbancmIzPyyx4_cO3V_wcY6t3zRvoSyTe.u0⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 15⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS⏎should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎As has been discussed at length, the impact of AI in clinical care is often slowed with resulting⏎output being biases by incomplete or less than optimal data due to the appropriate controls⏎(including HIPAA, IRB, Declaration of Helsinki, etc.). SAS recommends that HHS prioritize⏎the evaluation and use of synthetic data built using AI techniques that create⏎representative synthetic datasets from existing real clinical data. This would vastly⏎increase the amount and easy of use of clinical synthetic data and allow for the quick⏎advancement of AI.⏎In addition to the evaluation and use of synthetic data; HHS should explore the impact of AI⏎on workforce. When HHS implemented the Meaningful Use program, one of the most⏎consequential and often underrecognized elements was the federal investment in workforce⏎retraining to support nationwide EHR adoption. While the program varied across states and⏎institutions, several patterns emerged that are instructive for today’s AI landscape. The⏎initiative demonstrated that technology adoption at scale cannot succeed without parallel⏎investment in human capability. Workforce shortages and skill gaps remain among the⏎most pressing constraints in healthcare today. Although Meaningful Use training focused⏎primarily on system navigation and compliance tasks, it was not built to prepare clinicians for⏎advanced data-driven workflows, cognitive load shifts, algorithmic decision support, or⏎continuous cycles of technological change. Research is urgently needed to understand how⏎AI reshapes clinical workflow, including the redistribution of cognitive tasks between clinicians⏎and AI agents, shifts in clinical judgment processes when interacting with algorithmic⏎recommendations and identification of new competencies.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI⏎tools and their use clinical care?⏎There is extensive and growing research demonstrating the impact of artificial intelligence on⏎healthcare quality, safety, and operational performance. Across peer-reviewed literature,⏎real-world deployments, and regulatory-cleared solutions, AI has been shown to improve⏎early detection of patient deterioration, reduce preventable adverse events, lower mortality,⏎and support more efficient care delivery. Importantly, the strongest evidence comes from AI⏎solutions that are embedded directly into clinical workflows, leverage routinely collected⏎electronic health record (EHR) data, and are designed to augment, not replace, clinical⏎judgment. These systems consistently demonstrate that timely, explainable insights enable⏎clinicians to intervene earlier, prioritize care more effectively, and improve patient outcomes at⏎scale.⏎A well-documented example is the Rothman Index, an AI-driven, real-time illness severity⏎index developed using SAS analytics and widely deployed in hospital settings. The Rothman⏎Index synthesizes nursing assessments, vital signs, and laboratory results into a single,⏎continuously updated score that reflects how sick a patient is and whether their condition is⏎improving or deteriorating.⏎Under controlled conditions, hospitals using the Rothman Index observed a⏎statistically significant (30%) reduction in risk-adjusted mortality, with observed mortality⏎rates materially lower than. In addition, deployments of the Rothman Index have been⏎associated with fewer unplanned transfers to the ICU, reductions in emergency⏎escalations such as code blue events, and improved clinician efficiency by allowing care⏎teams to focus first on the sickest patients. These outcomes underscore how clinically⏎grounded, explainable AI, when rigorously developed, validated, and embedded into⏎workflow, can deliver tangible improvements in patient safety and quality of care.⏎Linked is a full list of peer reviewed articles and abstracts.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 16⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and⏎transfers of using AI as part of clinical care?⏎For areas beyond accuracy and clinical relevance, literature has focused extensively on time⏎savings and efficiencies gained due to AI in the clinical space but rarely goes beyond to⏎explain what the impact of these efficiencies will be. SAS suggests that HHS in its evaluation⏎of AI in the clinical setting examine the impact beyond accuracy and efficiency for a single⏎task but look at the larger impact to the complex system that is healthcare. While it’s unclear⏎what AI in healthcare will ultimately bring, there are examples from the past that HHS can⏎look to in identifying potential unintended consequences.⏎The expansion of electronic health records (EHRs) moved forward by the HITECH Act of⏎2009 has successfully electrified most health records in the United States but continues to fall⏎short on the goal of true interoperability. In addition, the use of EHRs has led to more⏎optimized billing by providers, leading to an increase in expenditure without the associated⏎efficiencies promised. HHS should ask not what efficiencies are gained by AI, but rather who⏎those efficiencies will be utilized by in multi-trillion-dollar system that is healthcare. The goal⏎will be to increase the quality and availability of care and the same, if not lower cost, but the⏎risk of overuse or continued burn out as clinicians are asked to do more and more, while⏎being moved away from the patient remains a risk.⏎RFI Response⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 17⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 18⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 19⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 20⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 21⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 22⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 23⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 24⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 25⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Appendix: SAS Health⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 26⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Interactive Response Index⏎Contents .......................................................................................................... i⏎Executive Summary ....................................................................................... 1⏎RFI Response ................................................................................................. 2⏎II. Solicitation of Public Comments Regulation ........................................... 2⏎As the nation’s principal health regulator, HHS helps shape the environment in which⏎AI for clinical care is developed, evaluated, and deployed. HHS seeks to establish a⏎regulatory posture on AI that is well understood, predictable, and proportionate to any⏎risks presented to enable rapid innovation while protecting patients and the⏎confidentiality of their identifiable health information, and maintaining public trust. We⏎seek feedback on how current HHS regulations impact AI adoption and use for⏎clinical care. .................................................................................................................. 2⏎HHS’s payment policies and programs have massive effects on how health care is⏎delivered in the United States, oftentimes with unintended consequences.⏎Hypothetically, if a payer is taking financial risk for the long-term health and health⏎costs of an individual, that payer will have an inherent incentive to promote access to⏎the highest-value interventions for patients. Under government designed and dictated⏎feefor- service regimes, however, coverage and reimbursement decisions are slow.⏎Rarely does covering new innovations reduce net spending; and waste, fraud, and⏎abuse is difficult to prevent, oftentimes leading to massive spending bubbles on⏎concentrated items or services that are not commensurate with the value of such⏎products. Given the inherent flaws in legacy payment systems, we seek to ensure⏎that the potential promises of AI innovations are not diminished through inertia and⏎instead such payment systems are modernized to meet the needs of a changing⏎healthcare system. We seek feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers⏎have the incentive and ability to promote access to high- value AI clinical⏎interventions, foster competition among clinical care AI tool builders, and accelerate⏎access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care. ................................................. 4⏎HHS supports one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems, catalyzing⏎innovation to supplement the market. By enabling applied AI research &⏎development, care delivery research and implementation science, as well as AI⏎entrepreneurship in health care, we can better translate AI technologies from concept⏎to clinical use. We seek input on ways in which HHS may invest in research &⏎development (including public-private partnerships and cooperative research and⏎development agreements (CRADAs)) to integrate AI in care delivery and create new,⏎long-term market opportunities that improve the health and wellbeing of all⏎Americans. .................................................................................................................... 5⏎Specific Questions ...................................................................................... 6⏎In addition to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, HHS seeks input on the following⏎specific questions: ........................................................................................................ 6⏎Appendix: SAS Health ................................................................................. 18⏎Interactive Response Index⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 27⏎⏎The SAS Solution: Operationalize Trustworthy AI at Scale⏎Back Cover⏎To contact your local SAS office, please visit: sas.com/offices⏎SAS and all other SAS Institute Inc. product or service names are registered trademarks or⏎trademarks of SAS Institute Inc. in the USA and other countries. ® indicates USA registration. Other⏎brand and product names are trademarks of their respective companies.⏎Copyright 2026 SAS Institute Inc. All Rights Reserved.⏎Interactive Response Index⏎Response to RFI (#RIN 0955–AA13)⏎Page 28♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0404♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Pair Team, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Pair Team♔♔♔♔♔♔RFI Response: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎TO: Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎FROM: Neil Batlivala, Founder and CEO, Pair Team⏎Nate Favini, Chief Medical Officer, Pair Team⏎DATE: February 17th, 2026⏎Executive Summary⏎Pair Team is an AI-enabled medical group serving high-needs Medicaid and Medicare beneficiaries. We⏎operate at the intersection of technology and community health, collaborating with partners like OpenAI,⏎Anthropic, ElevenLabs, academic leaders such as Dr. Roxana Daneshjou of Stanford University, and over⏎100 community-based organizations to build an AI care system that automates operations and delivers⏎proactive support to patients.⏎Pair Team believes the most efficient and responsible path toward autonomous virtual care begins with AI⏎serving as the virtual front door and primary coordination layer of care. In this model, AI systems⏎proactively engage patients, identify clinical and social needs, sequence actions, and escalate to human⏎clinicians when clinical risk or decision-complexity cross established safety thresholds. This AI-first⏎approach drives superior patient engagement by providing 24/7 accessibility, multilingual support, and⏎hyper-personalized outreach that extends care beyond the constraints of traditional, resource-limited⏎clinics. By expanding when and how patients can access care, AI-enabled engagement improves⏎prevention and coordination across healthcare and adjacent public programs, alleviates provider⏎shortages, and reduces total cost of care. However, existing regulatory, reimbursement, legal, and⏎technical frameworks were not designed for continuously learning, agentic AI. Without targeted⏎modernization, these structural barriers will continue to delay the transition to autonomous care and limit⏎the benefits available to patients.⏎Regulatory Proposals: A Path to Autonomy⏎In the last year, HHS has taken enormous steps forward that will encourage the implementation of AI⏎systems in healthcare. Three additional regulatory moves will accelerate action and create a path to⏎continuous AI advancement:⏎⏎Expand TEMPO into a Multi-Stage Autonomy Roadmap: The FDA’s TEMPO pilot provides a strong⏎foundation for modernizing oversight of AI-enabled clinical tools. HHS should encourage the FDA to⏎utilize the TEMPO pilot to validate a graduated transition to autonomy:⏎● Stage 1: AI agents operate as recommendation assistants within TEMPO, with higher risk actions⏎validated by a human provider. Developers track performance, including rates of human⏎disagreement with AI recommendations on specific tasks.⏎● Stage 2: Leveraging the performance data and clinical outcomes collected during the pilot, the⏎AI's scope expands to include the independent initiation of low-risk diagnostic, clinical or⏎administrative tasks under defined guardrails. For instance, agents may order laboratory tests,⏎low-risk imaging and medication refills. All such actions would be constrained by established⏎national clinical frameworks to ensure that AI reinforces evidence-based practice and avoids⏎generating unnecessary tests, treatments, or procedures with limited clinical benefit.⏎● Stage 3: As safety and performance are demonstrated at scale, these systems transition into⏎autonomous orchestrators capable of managing complex, whole-person care workflows, including⏎more advanced clinical decisions.⏎Authorize Developer-Led Evaluation Protocols: The FDA has established an important foundation for⏎iterative improvement through Predetermined Change Control Plans (PCCPs). To support the pace and⏎complexity of modern clinical reasoning models and digital assistants, this framework could be extended⏎through a complementary pilot pathway that authorizes developer-led evaluation protocols and⏎benchmarks. Under this approach, the FDA would review and approve a developer’s evaluation⏎methodology including performance targets, safety thresholds, and validation procedures, rather than⏎requiring resubmission for each discrete model change. Once approved, developers could make changes⏎within the bounds of those validated evaluations, such as incorporating new data types, refining outputs,⏎or expanding clinical scope, provided performance against the approved benchmarks is maintained or⏎improved. This approach aligns regulatory oversight with federal investment in shared evaluation⏎infrastructure, ensuring that AI systems are authorized based on demonstrated performance against⏎rigorous, real-world benchmarks.⏎Reduce Legal Uncertainty: As AI systems increasingly function as a virtual workforce, existing legal⏎frameworks create uncertainty regarding accountability. When clinicians use AI-enabled tools to inform⏎care decisions, how should responsibility and liability be shared across clinicians, health systems, and⏎technology developers to ensure safe, effective, and accountable care? To address this, HHS should⏎articulate a shared-responsibility governance framework for AI-enabled care that clarifies role-specific⏎expectations and links enforcement action and liability to adherence to core practices, including defining⏎intended use, ongoing monitoring, human oversight, and transparency to patients.⏎Reimbursement: Scaling ACCESS-Style Care Management Across⏎Medicaid and Medicare Advantage⏎The ACCESS model demonstrates CMS’s leadership in advancing outcome-aligned, engagement-focused⏎care management under a deflationary pricing scheme. As states and Medicare Advantage plans continue⏎to evolve their care coordination strategies, ACCESS offers a valuable template that can be extended⏎across Medicaid and Medicare Advantage through payment, administrative, and technical levers to⏎support broader adoption. Critically, AI-led care management structures should be tied to deflationary⏎⏎pricing models by setting rates that require productivity gains from automation and be tied to outcomes⏎based pricing that deliver actuarially supported total cost of care impact.⏎Medicaid: Reinforcing AI-Compatible Economics and Accountability⏎Under existing authorities, CMS can encourage states to adopt ACCESS-like care models by issuing⏎targeted guidance and embedding expectations directly into state and plan oversight processes. This can⏎include issuing State Medicaid Director Letters (SMDLs), applying SPA review standards, and⏎influencing managed care contract design during MCO RFP cycles to encourage adoption of⏎ACCESS-style payment, accountability, and delivery approaches.⏎CMS can further accelerate adoption by:⏎● Encouraging the use of state-directed payments and managed care incentive payments to invest in⏎ACCESS-like care models and enabling infrastructure.⏎● Promoting the deployment of AI-enabled agents that access appropriate data sources and interact⏎directly with beneficiaries to support Medicaid redeterminations and community engagement⏎requirements as an initial, high-impact opportunity for beneficiary-facing AI.⏎● Using RHTP as a recurring incentive by tying annual award criteria to the scope and maturity of⏎AI-enabled care delivery and infrastructure, encouraging states to expand and deepen⏎ACCESS-like models over time⏎● Publishing and operationalizing standardized templates such as model MCO contract language,⏎acceptable outcome measures and reporting expectations, and illustrative payment models⏎(PMPM, performance-based).⏎● Creating shared measurement, reconciliation, and payment tools to support consistent⏎implementation, evaluation, and scaling of ACCESS-like programs across Medicaid managed⏎care.⏎Medicare Advantage: Encouraging Adoption Through Incentives and Enablement⏎While maintaining flexibility for MA plans, CMS can accelerate voluntary adoption of ACCESS-style⏎models by combining payment incentives with administrative and technical support, including:⏎● Weighting STARs measures toward ACCESS-aligned clinical outcomes and member experience,⏎such as control of A1c, blood pressure, and lipids, where continuous AI-enabled care⏎coordination can measurably improve performance and reduce downstream utilization.⏎● Incorporating assumptions of increasing efficiency over time into benchmark rate development,⏎reflecting the role of automation in care coordination.⏎● Offering technical enablement, such as standardized outcome definitions, benchmarking and⏎risk-adjustment support, and shared reporting and reconciliation tools to reduce execution⏎complexity.⏎By extending ACCESS-style payment, accountability, and operational logic across Medicaid and⏎Medicare Advantage, CMS can encourage scalable, AI-enabled care coordination while reinforcing⏎flexibility, consistency, and sustainability across programs.⏎⏎Technology: National Open-Source Infrastructure⏎To bridge the gap between general AI capability and clinical reliability, HHS should invest in national,⏎open-source infrastructure through federal funding to the private sector and academia, creating a shared⏎foundation for all innovators.⏎Fund Complex, Real World Benchmarks: Current benchmarks are largely static and single-turn.⏎Additionally, they focus narrowly on clinical decision-making rather than care delivery more broadly.⏎HHS should fund the development of evaluation suites that reflect the actual complexity of care:⏎● Care Planning Benchmarks: Evaluating an agent’s ability to sequence clinical and social actions⏎(e.g., identifying a rising A1c and simultaneously triggering a transportation benefit).⏎● Engagement Benchmarks: Shifting from single-turn questions to scaled, multi-turn conversational⏎benchmarks. These are essential for evaluating if an agent can maintain context over an extended⏎dialogue to safely and effectively gather information or guide a patient through a complex⏎process.⏎Modernize Interoperability for Agentic Access: Safe deployment of agentic AI requires new standards⏎driven by ONC to ensure accountability for automated actions. We recommend:⏎● Audit Trails: Requiring all data requests to identify the requester (human, organization, or⏎automated agent).⏎● Standardized "On-Behalf-Of" Delegation: Explicitly linking an AI agent’s data access to its⏎supervising clinician or organization.⏎● Mandatory Purpose-of-Use Declaration: Using existing HL7 vocabularies to declare the intent of⏎an automated action at the time of data access.⏎Conclusion⏎AI has reached a critical inflection point where it can solve the gaps in access, quality, cost and efficiency⏎for Medicare and Medicaid beneficiaries that have stymied prior efforts. To realize this potential, HHS⏎must lead a coordinated modernization of the healthcare ecosystem. By evolving regulatory pathways to⏎support agentic reasoning, aligning reimbursement with population health outcomes, and investing in⏎national open-source infrastructure for clinical evaluation, HHS can transform AI from a supportive tool⏎into a scalable virtual workforce. Pair Team appreciates the opportunity to contribute to this RFI and⏎welcomes continued partnership with HHS to build a future of radically affordable, high-quality and⏎accessible care for everyone.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0405♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-20T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael♔Williams♔♔Massachusetts♔♔United States♔Premier Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Thirty years in practice management will make you skeptical of any process that starts with "let's move fast." I've watched three separate EHR transitions gut our clinical staff's time and morale, and in every case the pitch was the same: adopt now, work out the problems later. The problems did not work themselves out.⏎⏎So when I read through this RFI and see HHS asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, my first reaction is honestly not excitement. It's the same low-grade dread I felt in 2014 when our practice was pushed into Meaningful Use Stage 2 before anyone had figured out what the interoperability standards were actually going to look like in practice. We spent roughly $80,000 that year on compliance consultants who were, to be fair, also guessing.⏎⏎The question I want to address is the one about barriers to adoption, because I think HHS has it backward. The framing assumes that regulation is slowing down something that should go faster, but our own network data tells a different story. When Premier surveyed health technology partners in 2024, 54% of mid-sized firms said unresolved liability questions were a significant deterrent to launching new products. That's not regulators being too cautious. That's industry itself saying the foundation isn't stable enough to build on. If the companies building these tools are worried about liability exposure for AI that doesn't qualify as a medical device, then accelerating adoption without resolving that question doesn't help them either. It just transfers the risk downstream to the practices and hospitals implementing the tools, and ultimately to patients.⏎⏎There's also something that bothers me about the irreversibility of what's being considered here. Once AI documentation tools are embedded in reimbursement structures and Medicare billing workflows, removing them becomes practically impossible regardless of how they perform. That's not hypothetical, that's just how federal payment policy works. CMS added something, it becomes load-bearing infrastructure within about 18 months, and then the political cost of removing it exceeds the practical cost of keeping it even when it isn't working well. I'm not opposed to AI in clinical settings in principle. But HHS is being asked to make a one-way decision without audit requirements, without a clear governance structure for when these systems fail, and without any real public deliberation about whether faster and more automated is actually what patients want from medicine.⏎⏎I work with clinicians every day who are exhausted and who genuinely do not know whether they're supposed to be adopting these tools or waiting for guidance. The ambiguity is the problem. Not too much regulation, not too little, just the absence of anything stable to stand on. A practice manager at a 12-physician group in western Massachusetts does not have the capacity to independently evaluate AI vendor liability claims, audit model drift in a documentation tool, or negotiate meaningful indemnification language into a contract with a company that has 200 employees and a Series B. We need HHS to build the framework before it builds the on-ramp.⏎⏎And I'll be honest, I'm not even sure what the right framework looks like. But I know that "figure it out after adoption" has a track record in this sector, and it is not a good one. At minimum, before reimbursement pathways are opened up, there should be clear answers to who bears liability when an AI-assisted clinical note contains an error that contributes to a bad outcome, and what the audit rights are for practices that want to verify what these systems are actually doing. Those questions have been floating around since at least 2022 and I have yet to see anything close to a settled answer from HHS or FDA or anyone else.⏎⏎The other thing I keep coming back to is that this is genuinely a values question, not just a technical one. Whether medicine should be faster and more automated or slower and more accountable is not a question that an RFI process is equipped to resolve, and I'm not sure it should be resolved by an administrative agency on a timeline driven by where the investment money is going right now.⏎⏎I'd request that HHS at minimum publish a summary of what liability and governance questions it considers unresolved before issuing any final guidance that would accelerate reimbursement integration, so that practices like ours have some basis for understanding what we'd actually be agreeing to.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0406♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Sanford Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sanford Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 19, 2026⏎Robert Francis Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted via Regulations.gov⏎Re: RIN 0955-AA13 Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎It is with great pleasure that Sanford Health submits our response to your Request For⏎Information “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical⏎Care.” Sanford Health believes that the effective and responsible development,⏎implementation and utilization of artificial intelligence technologies will have seismic⏎impacts on the way healthcare is delivered and how patients live their best and fullest⏎lives. By taking a thoughtful and strategic approach to policy development, especially⏎around oversight and approval, reimbursement and privacy, we have the chance to chart a⏎course that will change the health not of just individual patients, but of communities and⏎countries. This is an exciting chapter filled with potential, but fraught with peril if done⏎incorrectly.⏎Introduction⏎Sanford Health is the largest rural health system in the United States, serving communities⏎across some of the most geographically expansive and sparsely populated regions in the⏎country. Headquartered in Sioux Falls, South Dakota, Sanford Health is a nonprofit,⏎integrated health system with 55,000 employees caring for more than 2 million patients⏎and nearly 415,000 health plan members across South Dakota, North Dakota, Minnesota,⏎Wyoming, Iowa, Wisconsin, and Michigan’s Upper Peninsula. Our care delivery footprint⏎includes 58 hospitals, 289 clinic locations, 145 senior care communities, and a physician⏎and advanced practice workforce of more than 4,500 clinicians.⏎Providing high-quality, timely care in rural America presents persistent and well-⏎documented challenges, including long travel distances, workforce shortages, limited⏎specialty access, and chronic underpayment by public payers. For more than a century,⏎Sanford Health has adapted its care models to meet these realities, prioritizing solutions⏎⏎that preserve local access while connecting patients to the expertise and services they⏎need. Virtual care and digital health innovation have become essential clinical⏎infrastructure for sustaining access across our rural footprint.⏎Within this context, Sanford Health is thoughtfully integrating artificial intelligence and⏎digital tools to advance three core priorities: access, quality, and sustainability. AI is being⏎applied to address operational and clinical challenges inherent in rural care delivery,⏎including workforce efficiency, care coordination, and timely clinical decision support. Our⏎responses to the questions contained in the initial RFI are below, and are based on our⏎direct experience, reflecting the importance of aligning innovation with real-world care⏎environments, ensuring that emerging technologies are deployed safely, transparently, and⏎in ways that strengthen trust among patients and providers.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Regulatory Uncertainty⏎The rapid evolution of AI technology and capability make creating a comprehensive⏎regulatory framework difficult, if not impossible. When it comes to regulation, we need a⏎balanced approach that supports innovation, without stifling it, while also protecting⏎patient safety. Clarifying regulations around AI tools and FDA approval requirements on⏎medical devices will alleviate liability concerns for health systems. Also, creating a special⏎reimbursement category for AI-enabled programs is imperative to incentivize provider⏎participation. At Sanford, we have found the implementation of a strong, enterprise-wide AI⏎Governance structure helps to ensure that AI tools are built to internal and external⏎standards and requirements. HHS should prioritize identifying and publicizing best⏎practices in this area so that effective models can be replicated at health organizations of⏎all sizes.⏎Data Access⏎If done correctly, AI can turn fragmented data into a connected, community-wide view of⏎health. This would support everything from public health surveillance to personalized care⏎plans. However, data quality and completeness prove to be a barrier. The source and⏎curation of the data affect its completeness, structure, and consistency with coding,⏎leading to a ‘gold mine’ or a ‘dump’ of information. There can be multiple EHRs between⏎health systems (sometimes even within health systems) causing fragmented ecosystems.⏎Limited access to data, especially within standardized methodology, is also a barrier. In⏎rural health care systems, there may be limited resources, causing all of the above to be⏎compounded. HHS has a crucial role to play in identifying baseline standards of what type⏎⏎of data sources are used to train models, how models are updated and refined, and how⏎data can be transmitted across and between systems in an efficient and safe way.⏎Workflow Integration and Change Management⏎With technology changing at such a fast pace, there are eager clinicians who are⏎continuous learners and are thriving in this new AI landscape, but for others, the pace of⏎change is challenging. A tremendous amount of change management for health care⏎teams is required not only in the use of AI tools, but also the acceptance of such tools. The⏎ever-changing landscape also requires more technology and IT roles to support our teams⏎and patients. This puts health systems in direct competition with tech-centered⏎companies. To keep up with pace of innovation, it is essential that health systems⏎continually invest in building trust, advancing new infrastructure, continued training and⏎certification and compete for talent in the technology space.⏎Cost⏎Creating and purchasing AI tools is very expensive for health systems, and many rural⏎locations simply do not have the funds to fully integrate AI programs and tools to the extent⏎that is needed, in addition to maintaining critical technological upgrades as the industry⏎rapidly changes. In many cases, AI tools are developed to predict chronic diseases. With⏎earlier diagnoses, it may prevent future illnesses and decrease costs long-term. Current⏎payment systems are simply not keeping up with these preventative interventions that best⏎benefit the patient. AI has the potential to fundamentally change how healthcare is⏎delivered. The entities that are able to access new tools and make them part of the normal⏎course of patient care will have an advantage. HHS must ensure that smaller entities that⏎may not have the upfront capital to invest in new systems are not permanently⏎disadvantaged or unable to leverage these tools.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What⏎HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your⏎ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎At Sanford Health, we are focused on accelerating the pace of innovation and adoption of⏎new emerging health care technologies while establishing guardrails to mitigate any⏎potential risks and protect private health information. Any regulation should focus on⏎protecting patients and users of the technology, not becoming too prescriptive or hindering⏎innovation.⏎⏎Reimbursement and Payment Models⏎The biggest barriers to the adoption and investment of AI in clinical care is the current⏎structure of reimbursement and payment models, or in reality, the lack of real and⏎meaningful reimbursement models. There is a crucial need to develop novel payment⏎programs to incentivize clinicians and patients to adopt AI tools. Current payment policies,⏎particularly fee-for-service programs, slow AI adoption and/or distort incentives. Creating a⏎special reimbursement category for AI-enabled programs may have large upfront costs but⏎could be very useful to give actors access to emerging technology.⏎Medicare reimbursement models need to support the use of AI models that not only⏎predict earlier needs for intervention of chronic diseases, but also to support remote⏎monitoring, assisted documentation, and ambient scribing. It is also a necessity that⏎payment models support the integration of AI into clinical workflows without increasing⏎administrative burden. The Medicare and Medicaid programs have the ability to be at the⏎forefront of this work, and providing the technical experience and examples that will also⏎induce greater adoption in the commercial reimbursement markets.⏎AI Medical Devices⏎There is much ambiguity around regulations when determining if an AI tool is a decision⏎support tool or may require FDA approval as a medical device. Providing clear guidance⏎that distinguishes FDA-regulated AI/ML medical devices from non-device, clinical-decision⏎support and operational tools will alleviate liability concerns for health systems. Further,⏎FDA should disseminate additional guidance on how AI products will be assessed and⏎regulated once approved, including post-approval surveillance and recertification, as well⏎as the degrees of human involvement are necessary for various technologies. We are not⏎at the place with the technology, from the perspective of both the patients and provider, to⏎fully automate care.⏎Data Security⏎It is a fundamental responsibility of health providers to use our utmost care to keep our⏎patients’ data safe and private. All providers must operate within secure, resilient, and well-⏎supported digital environments.⏎At Sanford Health, how we utilize data to improve overall care is something we take very⏎seriously. We have created a multidisciplinary AI/data governance/oversight committee⏎that provides us with a reliable and consistent process to evaluate and determine the⏎efficacy of AI tools (see question #4). The governance framework emphasizes when it is⏎important to keep a human in the loop and our commitment to responsibly develop and⏎⏎deploy AI models safely and transparently. It is vital to continue to assess how any AI-⏎enabled technology we deploy will use our data and what protections are involved.⏎HHS already has a robust regulatory framework for data protection in HIPAA. Admittedly, it⏎could be updated to include the plethora of health data that is now sitting outside of⏎current HIPAA standards. For example, there continues to be ambiguity around the use of⏎PHI for developing AI models, requiring more clarification. There is an opportunity to⏎provide safe harbors for de-identification and limited datasets used for AI development.⏎Flexibility in Clinical Settings⏎By integrating AI tools across Sanford, we’re addressing challenges with targeted solutions,⏎especially in the rural communities we serve. These tools aren’t replacing clinicians –⏎they’re empowering our care teams to spend more time with patients and make more⏎informed decisions. They’re also expanding access, improving affordability, and driving⏎better outcomes for our patients.⏎By allowing more regulatory flexibility to deploy and evaluate AI tools, health systems⏎would see accelerated adoption and incentivized, effective use of AI tools in the clinical⏎setting. Health systems with the means to create clinical-based AI tools also need more⏎flexibility to expand use of these decision-support tools in order to connect with partners⏎as well as other non-integrated systems. This is especially the case for rural populations,⏎who usually face a more fragmented healthcare delivery experience, and higher rates of⏎chronic condition and acuity of their conditions. We must maintain regulatory flexibility⏎that allows us to build the AI ecosystem that best meets the needs of the patient and⏎communities we serve.⏎3. What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should⏎HHS play to help address them?⏎Data Ownership⏎There continues to be ambiguity around the ownership and use of health care data to train⏎AI models, creating an increased risk for health systems. Health systems need clarity and⏎flexibility on the rights of this data being used.⏎Limited Data Sets⏎Many models are trained in limited data sets which may lead to performance issues or bias⏎when applied to diverse populations. Maintaining HIPPA compliance may also limit model⏎effectiveness.⏎⏎Cybersecurity Risks⏎Creating and implementing AI tools may increase cybersecurity risks. As stated previously,⏎it is a fundamental responsibility of health providers to use our utmost care to keep our⏎patients’ data safe and private. Reducing cybersecurity risks is extremely expensive and⏎cost prohibitive in many small rural facilities. HHS should create payment models to⏎support these increased costs.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow⏎and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further⏎support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful⏎(e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Evaluation and Validation Methods⏎Evaluation and validation methods should include using diverse datasets, audits of AI tools⏎across demographic and clinical subgroups, prospective monitoring drift, bias, calibration,⏎and unintended consequences, and continued testing against EHR configuration changes⏎and/or missing data.⏎Governance Structure⏎At Sanford Health, we created a multidisciplinary AI/data governance/oversight committee⏎that provides us with a reliable and consistent process to evaluate and determine the⏎efficacy of AI tools. Their function is to evaluate requests from clinicians and other leaders⏎on our team who bring forward ideas as well as outside companies looking to partner with⏎us on new AI tools. We put the patient and the caregiver at the center of every single AI⏎decision.⏎This governance structure is most focused on ensuring these tools are deployed⏎responsibly, safely, and transparently. Our collaboration with industry partners on a⏎voluntary framework for health care AI commitments is critical to this work.⏎Creating an independent regulatory board comprised of non-governmental organizations⏎(such as health systems, payers, 3rd party developers, etc.) would provide frameworks and⏎a possible registry for development and use of AI tools. Further, as recommended⏎previously, HHS should prioritize identifying and publicizing best practices in this area so⏎that effective models can be replicated at health organizations of all sizes. The creation of⏎a third-party entity must not create an unnecessary or burdensome level of oversight but⏎instead be fashioned to serve as a catalyst for innovation, adoption and dissemination of⏎best practices in this rapidly expanding space.⏎⏎Funding Opportunities⏎There is an opportunity for HHS to fund AI initiatives through grants or more robust AI-⏎supported payment models. Another funding opportunity could be through other⏎cooperative arrangements within HHS, such as supporting AI and innovation research labs⏎or supporting cooperation among health systems.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative⏎and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Accreditation and Certification⏎Using the independent regulatory board as discussed in question #4 above, the function of⏎the board could expand to include accreditation and certification with active support from⏎HHS. In cooperation with other independent third-party organizations, accreditation⏎programs could be developed to include fairness, transparency, and monitoring standards.⏎Further success could be achieved if HHS encouraged these accreditation bodies to⏎incorporate AI governance into their quality standards. Offering additional support from⏎HHS such as technical guidance aligned with accreditation bodies would also assist with⏎the regulatory board’s success as well. It is important to keep in mind that external⏎validators or oversight boards should not create extraneous, redundant or superfluous⏎regulatory bodies that impede the creation and deployment of responsible and effective AI⏎technologies that will benefit patients, healthcare providers, and the well-being of the⏎nation.⏎Workforce Development⏎A different skill set is required in today’s clinical setting than just a few years ago. In fact, we⏎see that technology is different and/or new by the time someone graduates from a four-year⏎program. The rapid pace of evolving technology is an ongoing barrier within AI adoption.⏎Training an appropriate workforce will be crucial to maximize the impact and potential of⏎this technology.⏎Sanford Health uses advanced clinical training and simulation to strengthen the rural⏎health care workforce, improve readiness for high-risk clinical scenarios, and expand⏎access to specialized care in communities with limited local resources. By bringing⏎immersive, technology-enabled training directly to rural settings, Sanford equips clinicians⏎across disciplines with the skills, confidence, and support needed to deliver high-quality⏎care close to home. Sanford Health also addresses critical gaps in emergency care through⏎comprehensive EMS education and advanced practice provider (APP) training. Over the⏎past five years, Sanford has enrolled more than 1,500 students in EMT programs and⏎⏎provides weekly live, interactive continuing education sessions for rural EMS providers.⏎Launched in 2026, the APP Emergency Department Foundational Knowledge and Skills⏎Program delivers more than 150 hours of structured training for nurse practitioners and⏎physician assistants staffing rural emergency departments, with plans to enroll more than⏎130 APP clinicians over three years. These efforts are further supported by the Virtual Care⏎Center Education Institute, which integrates telehealth and virtual reality into simulation-⏎based training.⏎Health systems are facing a global battle for talent, especially with roles that can be fully⏎remote. Health systems are competing for talent with every other company that has a⏎technology need across the country – not just Google and Amazon. This barrier can be⏎amplified in rural settings. We will need new roles like data scientists, predictive modelers,⏎AI developers to develop chatbots, product management and people that are focused on⏎the end-to-end patient journey and experience. However, health systems have a unique⏎differentiator in that many people are looking for a career that brings meaning and purpose,⏎and there’s no better place to make a difference every day than in health care.⏎HHS has an opportunity to support workforce development for the many areas and⏎expertise required in the AI setting while elevating the personal fulfillment of working in⏎health care. Overall, more training opportunities and funding for more formal education are⏎key for the future of workforce development.⏎Since the pipeline to develop talent can be long, HHS should focus on supporting this area⏎without delay. We also need to be cognizant that training should focus on what we need in⏎the future, not where we are at right now. Developing flexible training programs that can⏎adapt with the evolution of technology are imperative.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations, and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give⏎new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Sanford Health has made significant investments in advanced analytics and internally⏎developed AI tools that support both clinical decision-making and operational efficiency.⏎These tools are embedded within the electronic medical record and across care⏎management workflows to help identify patients at risk earlier, prioritize outreach, and⏎support timely intervention. Sanford Health has deployed AI models to identify patients at⏎risk for chronic kidney disease before symptoms appear, flag high-risk obstetric patients for⏎earlier monitoring, identify patients at elevated risk for colon and lung cancer, and predict⏎⏎the likelihood of developing Type II diabetes prior to diagnosis. Additional AI-enabled tools⏎reduce missed appointments, identify drivers of avoidable emergency department use, and⏎support prevention of post-acute readmissions. Together, these capabilities allow care⏎teams to focus resources where they can have the greatest impact while reducing⏎administrative burden in workforce-constrained rural settings.⏎To be clear, these tools aren’t replacing clinicians – they’re empowering our care teams to⏎spend more time with patients and make more informed decisions. They’re also expanding⏎access, improving affordability, and driving better outcomes for our patients.⏎Although AI has not yet met many of the needs for health care, it does continue to advance⏎in many areas such as:⏎Predicting Chronic Disease States⏎AI has the potential to reduce gaps in care and identify early warning signs of chronic⏎conditions like diabetes, hypertension, or mental health risks, especially in patients who⏎might not visit frequently. For rural populations, this proactive approach can reduce costly⏎hospitalizations and improve outcomes.⏎Sanford Health’s enterprise data analytics team developed an AI-driven risk score that’s⏎now embedded in the electronic medical record. It alerts primary care providers to patients⏎who are at an elevated risk of developing chronic kidney disease. Primary care physicians⏎work closely with nephrologists to define care pathways tailored to each risk category. This⏎ensures patients get the right interventions at the right time. Since implementing the tool,⏎we’ve seen a significant increase in early diagnoses. Patients identified through the AI⏎model are more likely to follow through with labs, start appropriate medications, and⏎engage in care plans that can potentially delay progression to end-stage renal disease. The⏎tool also helps providers determine when a patient should be referred to a kidney⏎specialist, making follow-up more timely and appropriate. For patients who do progress to⏎dialysis, earlier preparation can mean learning how to manage it at home or making⏎decisions about care location before it's urgent—ultimately improving quality of life and⏎access to care.⏎With colorectal cancer rates rising and screening eligibility now starting at age 45, Sanford⏎Health built an AI model using 85 risk factors – from genetics to mental health status – to⏎identify patients at higher risk of colon cancer. Those at elevated risk are prioritized for⏎colonoscopy, while average-risk patients can use FDA-approved stool-based tests. The AI⏎model embedded in our EMR will begin rolling out across Sanford in early 2026. While⏎North Dakota has one of the highest colon cancer rates in the U.S., this AI model will help⏎⏎guide clinicians in their decision-making for recommended screenings – especially critical⏎as we face a shortage of GI doctors in the rural Midwest.⏎Early intervention with high-risk OB patients can prevent complications like preterm birth⏎or preeclampsia, improving outcomes for both mothers and babies. Our team has⏎developed an AI tool that scans the EMR for clinical indicators to flag expectant mothers at⏎higher risk of complications. Providers are alerted and can initiate earlier, more frequent⏎check-ins.⏎Rates of lung cancer are rising among non-smokers, particularly women. Traditional⏎screening guidelines often miss these patients. AI can help identify risk earlier and improve⏎screening protocols. Sanford Health is piloting a research-based AI model to identify⏎patients at higher risk for lung cancer using broader data inputs in the electronic medical⏎record – not just smoking history.⏎Agentic AI supporting staff shortages⏎We’re piloting AI voice-based outreach to help patients schedule appointments and close⏎care gaps (e.g., overdue screenings or follow-ups). The AI-powered platform can tailor⏎communication based on a patient’s preferred channel (e.g., phone call, text, letter in the⏎mail, etc.). Personalized outreach helps make preventive care more accessible and⏎catches health issues before they become more serious, improving outcomes.⏎Ambient Documentation⏎Sanford Health has also piloted an AI‑powered ambient documentation solution to reduce⏎documentation burden and improve efficiency in clinical workflows. The pilot preserved or⏎increased clinician productivity while lowering time spent in the EHR outside working hours⏎and decreasing time in notes. Clinicians engaged in the pilot closed approximately 3,000⏎additional charts on the same day and 4,500 additional charts within seven days, directly⏎supporting timely follow‑up and care coordination. Documentation quality improved with⏎more comprehensive and accurate capture of Hierarchical Condition Categories,⏎enhancing clinical histories, continuity of care, and reimbursement accuracy aligned to⏎patient acuity.⏎Dictation volume and cost declined markedly: prior to implementation, the cohort⏎averaged 16,000 transcribed lines per month with 70 percent from clinic and ED dictation⏎at about $2,600 per month; after implementation, transcription fell to 5,000 lines per⏎month, clinic and ED dictation dropped to 6 percent, and costs declined to about $800 per⏎⏎month. Enterprise analysis indicates an opportunity to eliminate up to 84 percent of⏎dictation, representing an estimated $2 million in annual savings.⏎Additional feedback from providers:⏎▪ 100% said they would be disappointed if they couldn’t use the tool⏎▪ 100% responded that it was easy to use⏎▪ 88% reported improvement in burnout or fatigue⏎▪ 90% reported higher job satisfaction⏎▪ 90% reported better work-life balance⏎▪ 95% of clinicians said it reduced cognitive burden⏎▪ 80% are more likely to continue practicing⏎Patient experience remained strong, with favorable provider Net Promoter Scores and⏎“provider spent enough time with me” responses, while clinician surveys indicated⏎reduced documentation burden and higher satisfaction consistent with observed⏎efficiency gains. Eventually, expanding ambient technology to additional clinicians,⏎including nurses and therapists, will increase efficiency across care teams, reduce⏎documentation burden, and expand capacity to scale services.⏎Operational efficiencies⏎AI has the power to offset rural workforce shortages by automating administrative tasks,⏎triaging patients and even supporting clinical decision-making.⏎Sanford Health has built an AI tool that uses patient data to predict patients more likely to⏎miss their appointment. Our teams proactively reach out to address potential barriers – like⏎transportation or work conflicts – and offer earlier appointments when available. Missed⏎appointments delay care and increase health disparities, especially in rural areas where⏎rescheduling can take weeks.⏎The above examples could also be considered where we are falling short today. Because of⏎HIPPA restrictions and ambiguity and data sharing regulations, this opens the door for tool⏎bias, inconsistencies, and possible errors. Additionally, benefits for utilizing clinical-based⏎AI tools fall to the payors vs. the providers.⏎Using broader and more standardized data sources, providing funding sources through⏎enhanced payment models, reducing restrictions on HIPPA related data, and having a⏎strong self-governance model in place, would allow us to create much stronger tools that⏎support providers at the bedside and alleviate labor intensive burdens.⏎⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care?⏎What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Influential Decision Makers and Governing Bodies:⏎o Chief Medical Officer⏎o Chief Nursing Officer⏎o Chief Information Officer⏎o Chief Digital Officer⏎o Chief Data/Analytics Officer⏎o Compliance and Legal Leadership⏎o Finance Leadership⏎o Operations Leadership⏎o Service Line and Hospital administration⏎Administrative Hurdles for adoption of AI in clinical care include:⏎With the ever-evolving and fast-paced nature of AI, keeping up to date with its capabilities⏎and potential requires administrative conversations on how to best prioritize its changes.⏎Competing priorities with limited resources leads to difficult decisions and meticulous⏎planning by administrators. Additionally, unless there are more defined guardrails and⏎some clarity in regulation, there will continue to be legal risks of deploying clinical models.⏎Without proper regulation, we may see security risks of deploying clinical models as well.⏎Lastly, although crucial, it will be a hurdle for administrations to develop strong governance⏎policy and procedures in such a diverse and quickly changing AI environment.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please⏎consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Sanford operates a robust set of interoperability tools that support data exchange across⏎hospitals, retail partners, post-acute facilities, pharmacies, and health plans. This⏎infrastructure allows Sanford to deliver coordinated care across geographically dispersed⏎areas without increasing the cost of care.⏎Despite these capabilities, rural health systems continue to operate within fragmented and⏎state-based interoperability environments. States maintain or propose separate health⏎information exchange structures that create friction for multi-state systems.⏎Enhanced and expanded interoperability supports integration of wearable devices and⏎remote monitoring into clinical workflows, improving visibility into patient health and⏎enabling new rural care models, including pharmacist-led interventions that improve⏎chronic disease management, medication adherence, utilization, and access.⏎⏎Enhanced interoperability would expand the market for AI tools to work reliably with⏎reduced bias across diverse populations and settings. Many health care systems that use⏎training models from internal data possibly lack a full longitudinal picture of a population.⏎The risk of a non-diverse population used to train models may create an ineffective and⏎biased clinical tool.⏎Standardizing clinical notes, imaging, labs, medications, device data, etc. Using FHIR and⏎USCDI, expansion would increase possibilities for research and development of clinical⏎care AI tools. However, this will also require regulatory changes to lessen the legal risks of⏎using expanded data sources.⏎As interoperability expands, cybersecurity must expand alongside it. Sanford views⏎cybersecurity as a core utility protecting all digital health functions, including AI,⏎telehealth, clinical software, and multistate data exchange.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎What providers want:⏎• Time back in their day to focus on patients: Providers didn’t go to medical school to⏎stare at a computer screen. AI can automate routine tasks like documentation,⏎billing codes, prior-authorizations, and help with chart summarization and clinical⏎notes. AI serves as a tool to alleviate administrative burdens.⏎• Clinical decision support they can trust: Evidence-based recommendations are⏎necessary.⏎• Seamless integration with EHRs: If it’s not embedded in the workflow, it won’t be⏎used. Rural providers are stretched thin — tech must work with their reality, not add⏎complexity.⏎What patients want:⏎• Better access and faster care: AI-powered chat or triage that shortens wait times⏎and reduces travel. Reminders for screenings, med refills, or follow-ups.⏎• Clarity and transparency: Patients want to know how AI is being used in their care —⏎especially if it influences diagnoses or care decisions.⏎• Clear explanations: Patients are looking for tools to help explain complex medical⏎situations⏎Both patients and caregivers want better care coordination across multiple providers⏎and are looking for tools to predict chronic and adverse events.⏎⏎Concerns by patients and/or providers using AI:⏎Ongoing concerns from both patients and providers are barriers that will take time to⏎overcome, but earning trust in AI tools and showcasing its’ potential may alleviate and⏎expedite some of the friction. Concerns from both parties include lack of human⏎connection, privacy and data security, risk of error and not fully understanding AI (fear of⏎the unknown). Providers are concerned about job security and bias/fairness with AI tools.⏎We need to be transparent so that providers and patients understand what AI is doing and⏎why. It’s also important we emphasize that AI supports – not replaces – the clinician’s⏎judgement. This is especially important in rural areas where the clinician-patient⏎relationship is deeply personal. And lastly, showing evidence and demonstrating how AI⏎improves efficiency, reduces burnout or helps detect health issues earlier. As we continue⏎to integrate AI in the clinical setting, it’s vital to gain trust by sharing reputable literature,⏎research and outcomes, choosing high-quality vendors and tools, and highlighting⏎successes in our own clinical settings.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their⏎use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎By prioritizing AI research dedicated to the economic effects and impact of payment⏎models, health systems and providers may have more ‘buy in’ to accelerate adoption of AI⏎in the clinical setting. A stronger trust will be built knowing that the investment of AI is⏎worth the long-term outcomes, as well as knowing that providers will be compensated⏎fairly. Currently, providers are burdened with the up-front costs of developing and⏎purchasing AI tools with payors reaping the benefits. Also, with AI tools relatively new in the⏎clinical setting, many of the cost-benefits are harder to quantify in the short term, including⏎reduced provider burnout.⏎Research that also showcases the effectiveness and safety of AI tools across diverse⏎populations will build trust and transparence of AI for both providers and patients.⏎Sincerely,⏎Brad Reimer, Senior Vice President, Chief Information Officer♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0407♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Dermatology Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Dermatology Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 19, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The American Academy of Dermatology Association (AADA) writes to provide comments on the Department of⏎Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care. The AADA represents more than 17,500 dermatologists⏎nationwide who are committed to excellence in the medical and surgical treatment of skin disease; advocating⏎for high standards in clinical practice, education, and research in dermatology and dermatopathology; and⏎driving continuous improvement in patient care and outcomes while reducing the burden of disease.⏎AADA supports thoughtful use of AI provided its design enables reliable, clinically accurate, and high-quality⏎patient care without overburdening physicians. We urge HHS to implement ongoing evaluation to ensure⏎these tools maintain quality and effectiveness. We also ask HHS to consider financial barriers to implementing⏎AI systems as well as the impacts of AI on diagnostic and utilization management tools such as claims, coding,⏎and prior authorization review tools as outlined below. We appreciate the opportunity to contribute to this⏎important dialogue with HHS and offer the following comments in response to the questions in the RFI.1⏎Barriers to Private-Sector AI Innovation and Clinical Adoption⏎As HHS seeks to identify barriers to AI innovation in clinical care, the agency must ensure these tools never⏎supplant or restrict a patient’s ability to access in-person care when they want it. The Academy supports⏎1 “Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations and where have they fallen⏎short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality,⏎and help reduce costs?” Federal Register :: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎⏎Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 2 of 6⏎patient access to physician led care and the freedom to choose their own physicians, including specialists and⏎subspecialists. Physicians must also be able to access and implement AI should they choose to do so. Yet the⏎financial cost to practices seeking to implement AI may be prohibitive, particularly to smaller, rural practices.⏎Physicians should not be penalized if they do not have the resources to incorporate AI into their practice. HHS⏎is encouraged to develop guidance to promote AI affordability while still accounting for necessary oversight of⏎AI by clinical experts, as discussed in greater detail below.⏎Regulatory, Payment Policy, and Programmatic Design Considerations of AI in⏎Clinical Care⏎AADA requests that HHS promote guardrails that support the delivery of high-quality care to patients and⏎incorporate the following principles:⏎• Sensitive data used to train AI algorithms must be handled in accordance with patient privacy laws and⏎ethical standards,⏎• AI algorithms must be developed using auditable data that is representative of the population on⏎which it will be used,⏎• AI algorithms used for the delivery of patient care must be subject to rigorous and transparent data⏎validation, routine quality and safety evaluation to assess model drifts, and ethical review before⏎deployment,⏎• the algorithms themselves must also be transparent, accessible, and comprehensible for the intended⏎end user(s), and⏎• Design, implementation, and evaluation of AI tools for clinical care should responsibly incorporate⏎input and feedback from key stakeholders, including physicians and patient advocates.⏎The Academy offers the following perspectives on payer-developed AI programs in response to HHS’s request⏎for comments on AI performance in clinical care. The Academy is specifically focused on the impact payer AI-⏎enhanced utilization review programs, such as claims review, coding, and prior authorization programs, have⏎on clinical care and payment.⏎AI Payer Claims Review Programs⏎Major health insurers have deployed AI programs to expedite review of physician-submitted claims, which has⏎the potential to promote more timely reimbursement. Yet dermatologists report an increasing number of⏎payers unilaterally adjusting reported Evaluation and Management (E/M) code level (downcoding) claims and⏎reducing payment automatically using AI tools, without first requesting and reviewing clinical records that are⏎integral to the adjudication of the claim. Increasingly, these payer AI programs are operating without notice to⏎dermatologists. Improper downcoding destabilizes accurate data collection and analyses regarding patient⏎encounters, undermines practices’ financial stability, and wastes resources on appealing downcoded claims.⏎The AADA opposes improper payment reductions for services appropriately documented as it creates⏎unnecessary administrative burden for practices forced to appeal improperly downcoded claims. Further,⏎⏎Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 3 of 6⏎payer use of AI adjusted risk scores in quality payment programs need to incorporate appropriate attribution⏎methodology based on valid, meaningful and transparent quality metrics.⏎Beyond the impact on physicians, human review is necessary to ensure coding accuracy and prevent⏎inappropriate downcoding and denials, which negatively impact patient care. The nuance and complexity of⏎medical records demand careful judgement and a thorough contextual understanding that only human⏎expertise can provide when determining the correct procedure modifiers and codes. Without human⏎expertise, AI systems may misinterpret nuanced or complex medical records. These challenges underscore the⏎importance of skilled human coders working in tandem with AI tools, allowing automation to support rather⏎than replace human expertise and patient-centered clinical knowledge. Flagged claims should be reviewed by⏎healthcare professionals with relevant expertise examining patient records. Furthermore, insurers should be⏎required to track and report the accuracy of their AI claim review programs to HHS; AADA recommends that⏎HHS audit insurers whose AI claim review programs raise suspicions of misuse. While AI offers the potential to⏎streamline claims processing and timely payment, over-reliance on the technology by payers results in real⏎and—at times—irreversible harm to patients in the form of delayed or inaccessible care. Payer use of AI⏎therefore warrants special scrutiny to preserve the integrity of clinical decision-making and ensure timely⏎access to medically necessary care.⏎We recommend that HHS enhance its regulatory oversight of AI-driven payer downcoding and develop⏎guidelines for best practices on using AI in claims review. Recommendations for best practices include using⏎AI as the first step to review and flag claims suspected of inappropriate coding, followed by appropriate⏎human examination, and ensuring transparency and ongoing evaluation of AI in claims review.⏎Use of AI in Prior Authorizations⏎Poorly designed payer-driven prior authorization programs increase direct and indirect costs for physicians⏎and patients alike, resulting in delays in care, adding administrative burden to practices, and amplifying⏎patient stress. Novel AI tools have the potential to alleviate the burdens associated with payer-driven prior⏎authorization programs that physicians experience. For example:⏎ Ambient intelligence uses AI in devices to detect the presence of humans and respond accordingly. This⏎type of AI tool can be used to populate dermatology clinical notes, capture patient data from notes,⏎and automatically complete prior authorization requests.⏎ Large language models can analyze clinical notes and images to strengthen prior authorization⏎requests.⏎ AI-powered clinical decision support tools help ensure that submissions meet payer-specific criteria,⏎and automated systems can generate comprehensive prior authorization narratives based on patient⏎records.⏎However, until AI systems are trained on clinical data derived from medical records - rather than public data -⏎and can accurately recognize complex medical care, payers’ clinical staff must continue to review prior⏎⏎Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 4 of 6⏎authorization denials. To address poorly designed payer-driven prior authorization programs and support⏎physicians, we recommend HHS take the following actions:⏎ Implement public reporting requirements on payers’ use of prior authorization to HHS, including⏎approval and denial rate by each service or medication. In addition to making payer prior authorization⏎more transparent, this would also enable AI systems to learn from and enhance the review process . In⏎2025, major insurers met with Secretary Kennedy and Administrator Oz to pledge their commitment to⏎streamline and improve prior authorization processes.2 The Academy applauds Secretary Kennedy and⏎Administrator Oz for securing this unprecedented commitment and urges the implementation of⏎reporting requirements to ensure adherence to that commitment.⏎ Direct insurers to use AI solely for approving prior authorizations while requiring their clinical staff to⏎review denials.⏎ In coordination with AI entities, offer grants to small, independent practices seeking to implement AI⏎systems to facilitate adoption of practice-based systems to relieve the administrative burden in⏎complying with prior authorization requirements.⏎AI in the Practice Setting: Physician Coding and Diagnostic Tools⏎Similar to recommendations for payer AI use, the AADA recognizes potential benefits for dermatology⏎practices to integrate use of AI-driven coding and diagnostic tools, while continuing to encourage inclusion of⏎human examination in coding and diagnostics. Using AI tools to automate routine tasks such as code⏎identification and compliance checks with billing guidelines empowers dermatologists and their coding staff to⏎increase successful claims. If used appropriately, shifting to AI automation of routine tasks enables a stronger⏎focus on improving and streamlining medical record documentation, which enhances AI accuracy in code⏎selection. The result is fewer coding errors, more timely claim submissions, and a smoother revenue cycle —⏎contributing to greater operational efficiency and financial stability. Additionally, physicians may consider⏎incorporating AI into diagnostic tools to improve efficiency, reduce tedium, and perform triage. However,⏎practices should consider AI as an assistant, not a replacement to human review of diagnostic tools. While AI⏎may eventually produce tools capable of aiding in diagnostics, caution is urged.⏎In contrast to payer use of AI, which too often results in barriers to care, physicians are adopting AI as a⏎supportive tool to enhance accuracy, efficiency, and patient outcomes—and not to replace critical human-in-⏎the-loop oversight. Any regulatory policies should therefore aid physician access and avoid imposing⏎additional compliance burdens so physicians can continue to focus on patient care rather than administrative⏎complexity.⏎We encourage HHS to consider the following recommendations:⏎ Most AI systems are trained using public data, which introduces accuracy and privacy issues. These⏎issues range from HIPAA adherence when using sensitive data, presence of inaccurate or outdated⏎information, and increased risk of errors due to the complexity of claims. We encourage HHS to⏎convene a work group of medical professionals and AI data experts to develop appropriate regulations⏎2 HHS Secretary Kennedy, CMS Administrator Oz Secure Industry Pledge to Fix Broken Prior Authorization System | HHS.gov⏎⏎Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 5 of 6⏎on AI training and privacy. Dermatologists should be a key part of this group, given their work across a⏎wide range of services from routine procedures to highly specialized treatments that require precise⏎data.⏎ Develop guidelines for best practices on using AI diagnostic tools. Recommendations for best practices⏎include using AI as the first step to review diagnostics and flag where additional human expert review is⏎required before a clinical decision is made.⏎ To ensure physicians have access to accurate AI-driven coding tools, we recommend HHS institute⏎post-marketing surveillance of these tools to ensure safety. Routine evaluation of AI-driven coding and⏎diagnostic tools will ensure the technology evolves in a manner that protects patients, improves⏎quality, cost, and effectiveness of care delivery.⏎ AADA recommends that HHS, in coordination with AI entities, provide research grants focused on⏎evaluating the clinical effectiveness and patient safety of AI tools. Funding research on AI in clinical⏎care benefits AI-driven coding and diagnostic tools. Research will help protect patients, promote⏎clinical effectiveness, and determine how data collected during model deployment can be used to⏎retrain and refine models for validation studies and post deployment monitoring. Ultimately, research⏎findings may be used to enhance AI’s potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on⏎quality, and help reduce costs.⏎ AADA recommends that HHS collaborate with other federal agencies, including the Department of⏎Justice and Office of Inspector General, to identify regulations regarding physician liability and to issue⏎guidance on their application when AI is used to help clarify risk allocation. AI is still developing and⏎comes with imperfections which may result in exposing physicians to liability, including medical⏎malpractice concerns. Additionally, using AI for coding and billing without effective oversight may⏎result in billing errors and overpayments that have fraud and abuse implications. To address these⏎concerns, we recommend that HHS establish a safe harbor that clarifies and limits physician liability⏎when AI is used as a support tool in the case of documented clinical oversight, compliance protocols,⏎and diagnostics. The financial cost of implementing AI systems is a deterrent for independent physician⏎practices. To implement AI-driven systems, a practice must endure costs associated with technology⏎acquisition, integration, and staff training. Additionally, the potential risks of implementation — such⏎as system errors, underperformance, or failure to meet expectations — can deter practices from⏎adopting these technologies without clear evidence of return on investment. We encourage HHS to, in⏎coordination with AI entities, offer federal and state grants to small, independent practices seeking to⏎implement AI systems.⏎Conclusion⏎We applaud HHS’ focus on accelerating adoption and use of AI in clinical care. AI tools present an opportunity⏎to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs. These tools also have⏎the potential to increase administrative burdens on practices, delay care, and contribute to practices’ financial⏎instability. We encourage HHS to thoroughly consider the recommendations provided in this letter and⏎appreciate the opportunity to offer comments. If you have any questions regarding this letter, please contact⏎Lou Terranova, Associate Director, Health Policy & Payment, at lterranova@aad.org or 847-240-1465.⏎⏎Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎February 19, 2026⏎Page 6 of 6⏎Sincerely,⏎Susan C. Taylor, MD, FAAD⏎President, American Academy of Dermatology Association♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0408♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Regenstrief Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Regenstrief Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔February 22, 2025⏎Mr. Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Principal Deputy National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎The Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert Humphrey Building, Suite 729⏎200 Independence Avenue SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically to:⏎https://www.regulations.gov/commenton/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Posnack:⏎The Health Data Standards division of the Regenstrief Institute appreciates the opportunity to⏎respond to the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy’s Request for Information on⏎accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in clinical care. As a research⏎organization with deep expertise in biomedical informatics, learning health systems, and⏎national health data standards stewardship, Regenstrief approaches AI not as a standalone⏎technology, but as a capability that depends on strong data foundations, real-world evidence,⏎and sustained governance.⏎Our responses focus on areas where federal leadership can most effectively translate AI⏎innovation into durable clinical impact. In particular, we emphasize the importance of semantic⏎and technical interoperability, standards-based data infrastructure, pragmatic evaluation in real-⏎world settings, and post-deployment monitoring to support safe, trustworthy, and scalable use of⏎AI in care delivery.⏎The enclosed responses address three priority areas:⏎• Key barriers to private sector innovation and adoption of AI in clinical care⏎• The role of enhanced interoperability in widening markets, fueling research, and⏎enabling generalizable AI evidence⏎• Patient and caregiver priorities and concerns related to AI use in clinical settings⏎Across these areas, our comments reflect findings from the published literature and practical⏎experience supporting national health data standards, multi-site research, and learning health⏎system infrastructure. We believe sustained progress in clinical AI adoption will depend less on⏎algorithmic advances alone and more on coordinated investment in data standards,⏎1 loinc.org⏎⏎interoperability, real-world evidence generation, and governance mechanisms that enable⏎responsible use over time.⏎We appreciate ASTP’s leadership in convening stakeholders across government, industry, and⏎the research community, and we welcome continued engagement on policies and programs that⏎support trustworthy, evidence-based integration of AI into clinical care.⏎If there are questions regarding our comments, please contact Marjorie Rallins, DPM, MS,⏎Executive Director, Health Data Standards at mrallins@regenstrief.org or 317-274-9415.⏎We appreciate and look forward to continued collaboration with ASTP.⏎Sincerely,⏎Marjorie Rallins, DPM, MS⏎Executive Director⏎Regenstrief Institute Health Data Standards⏎Enclosure: Regenstrief Institute Health Data Standards Response Summary⏎2 loinc.org⏎⏎Regenstrief Institute Response Summary⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP)⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Submitted electronically to:⏎https://www.regulations.gov/commenton/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎Specific Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Comment: At their core, many barriers to AI adoption stem from inconsistent, non-⏎standardized clinical data and limited semantic interoperability, which undermine technical⏎performance and trust. AI systems depend on computable, comparable data across settings,⏎yet uneven adoption and alignment of foundational standards—such as LOINC®, UCUM,⏎SNOMED CT®, and others—continue to require costly data normalization and limit the ability⏎to validate, scale, and monitor AI tools in real-world clinical environments. These gaps⏎contribute to clinician skepticism and limit the ability to generate transferable, real-world⏎evidence across institutions. Strengthening national adoption and coordinated use of core⏎clinical data standards would lower development and implementation costs, improve model⏎reliability and transparency, and support more robust evaluation of safety and effectiveness.⏎Addressing these data foundations, alongside regulatory clarity and payment alignment, is⏎essential to building confidence in AI and unlocking its full potential in clinical care.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific⏎data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Comment: Enhanced interoperability would most effectively widen market opportunities and⏎accelerate AI development where it enables semantically consistent, standards-based, and⏎longitudinal clinical data that can be reused across organizations, vendors, and care settings.⏎1. Foundational Clinical Data Prioritized in USCDI⏎AI development and deployment depend on consistent representation of core clinical⏎data prioritized within USCDI, including laboratory results, vital signs, clinical⏎observations, diagnoses, procedures, medications, and outcomes. Stronger⏎interoperability across these data types—through coordinated use of nationally⏎recognized data, terminology, and units of measure standards—would reduce data⏎normalization costs, improve model generalizability, and expand market access for AI⏎developers.⏎2. Semantic and Technical Interoperability Across the Standards Ecosystem⏎True interoperability requires shared clinical meaning and reliable technical exchange.⏎Uneven adoption and misalignment across clinical terminologies, information models,⏎3 loinc.org⏎⏎exchange specifications, and implementation guides continue to limit AI development,⏎evaluation, and deployment. Greater coordination across the standards ecosystem⏎would enable AI models trained in one environment to be more readily validated,⏎deployed, and monitored in others without extensive reengineering.⏎3. Longitudinal and Cross-Setting Data⏎AI tools that support prediction, risk stratification, quality measurement, and outcomes⏎analysis require longitudinal data spanning inpatient, outpatient, laboratory, public⏎health, and community-based care settings. Interoperability that supports consistent⏎use of standards-aligned data over time and across settings would fuel research and⏎strengthen real-world evidence generation.⏎4. Benchmarking, Evaluation, and Real-World Evidence Infrastructure⏎Enhanced interoperability is essential for shared benchmarking tools, reference⏎datasets, and evaluation frameworks. When AI inputs and outputs are grounded in⏎common standards, performance metrics can be compared meaningfully across⏎vendors and institutions, supporting post-deployment monitoring and adoption.⏎5. Supporting Evolution and Innovation in Standards⏎Interoperability frameworks should support responsible integration of emerging data⏎types and evolving standards while maintaining backward compatibility and alignment⏎with USCDI.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients⏎and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Comment: Research consistently shows that patients and caregivers support AI adoption⏎when it improves access, coordination, safety, and personalization of care while preserving⏎trust and the clinician–patient relationship. Patients value AI applications that improve⏎timeliness of care, reduce diagnostic delays, support care coordination for complex⏎conditions, reduce documentation burden, and enable more meaningful patient–clinician⏎interaction.⏎At the same time, patients express concerns related to loss of human connection, lack of⏎transparency or explainability, inconsistent performance across care settings, privacy and⏎secondary use of health data, and accountability when AI contributes to clinical decisions.⏎Evidence suggests patient trust increases when AI tools are transparent, grounded in high-⏎quality clinical data, evaluated in real-world settings, and deployed as decision support rather⏎than decision-makers.⏎4 loinc.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0409♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American College of Radiology (ACR), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American College of Radiology (ACR)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 19, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Subject: Docket ID: (HHS-ONC-2026-0001; 90 FR 60108) “HHS Health Sector AI RFI”—Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care; Comments of the American College of Radiology⏎The American College of Radiology (ACR)—professional association representing more than 40,000⏎physicians practicing diagnostic radiology, interventional radiology, radiation oncology, and nuclear⏎medicine, as well as medical physicists—appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Assistant⏎Secretary for Technology Policy’s (ASTP, or ASTP/ONC) request for information (RFI), “Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care” (HHS-ONC-2026-0001)⏎published Dec. 23, 2025.⏎The ACR established its Data Science Institute (DSI) in 2017 to advance safe, effective, and⏎clinically useful AI innovations for use by radiologists. DSI collaborates with radiology⏎professionals, industry, government, patients, and other stakeholders to develop programs and⏎tools in support of the implementation of AI applications that will help improve patient care.⏎Initiatives include:⏎• Defining clinically relevant use cases intended to guide the development of useful imaging⏎AI (ACR Define-AI).⏎• Establishing and making broadly available the first national recognition program for safe⏎and effective implementation of AI in imaging practices (ACR ARCH-AI), which is now used⏎in many different sites across the U.S.⏎• Creating opportunities to monitor the effectiveness of AI models in real-world clinical⏎practice, including the first large-scale quality registry for AI performance monitoring (ACR⏎Assess-AI).⏎• Participating in the Healthcare AI Challenge, a multi-institution collaborative effort⏎dedicated to crowdsourced evaluation of generative AI solutions.⏎• Sharing information about training and characteristics of radiology AI models (including⏎model cards) with radiologists to help them choose what works for their practices and⏎patients (ACR AI Central).⏎• Organizing thought leadership activities regarding regulatory, legal, and ethical issues⏎associated with radiology AI.⏎HHS General Questions⏎HHS seeks to establish a regulatory posture on AI that is well understood, predictable, and⏎proportionate to any risks presented to enable rapid innovation while protecting patients and⏎⏎the confidentiality of their identifiable health information. We seek feedback on how current⏎HHS regulations impact AI adoption and use for clinical care.⏎HHS regulation provides an essential foundation for the safe and effective clinical adoption of AI⏎technologies. For example, FDA oversight of AI enabled medical devices gives deployers and⏎end users added confidence in adopting these tools for radiology and other patient care⏎applications. However, FDA’s authority covers o-nly a subset of clinical AI, and many AI enabled⏎soft-ware functions used in care delivery are either entirely unregulated by HHS agen-cies or operate⏎as non device features embedded within voluntarily ASTP/ONC certified health IT syst-ems, such as⏎EHRs.⏎- -⏎HHS should enhance its regulations to address other aspects of AI adoption and use. There⏎remains a significant unmet need for federal support, incentives, or regulatory expectations that⏎promote good AI governance; standardize AI model transparency; ensure that primary clinical AI⏎end users are qualified to perform the patient care service at issue and empowered to mitigate risk;⏎and require sites to implement risk appropriate post deployment monitoring and related processes⏎to he-lp ensure patients are well served and pro-tected.⏎- -⏎Given the inherent flaws in legacy payment systems, we seek to ensure such payment⏎systems are modernized to meet the needs of a changing healthcare system. We seek⏎feedback on payment policy changes that ensure payers have the incentive and ability to⏎promote access to high value AI clinical interventions, foster competition among clinical care⏎AI tool builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI tools for clinical care.⏎While we appreciate HHS’s recognition that legacy payment systems must evolve, there is no clear⏎or consistent federal payment policy that supports the clinical use of AI tools. This gap limits⏎provider adoption, stifles innovation, and ultimately restricts patient access to high value AI-⏎enabled care. The ACR will continue working with HHS, CMS, industry partners, and other⏎stakeholders to develop a modern, robust payment framework that promotes access to clinically⏎valuable AI tools, encourages fair competition, and ensures sustainable, value-based integration of⏎AI into patient care. Under current rules, AI tools are typically treated as practice expense inputs or⏎bundled into existing CPT/HCPCS codes, regardless of whether they materially enhance diagnostic⏎accuracy, reduce variation, or support improved clinical outcomes. This approach effectively treats⏎AI the same as commodity software, even when the technology performs clinically meaningful⏎tasks and requires physicians, particularly radiologists, to invest additional time to interpret,⏎validate, and incorporate AI outputs into clinical decision-making.⏎Without a payment structure that recognizes this work, clinicians have limited incentives to use AI⏎tools, and health systems face a financial disincentive to adopt innovations that could otherwise⏎improve care delivery. A payment policy that ties reimbursement for AI-enabled services to⏎demonstrated improvements in quality, efficiency, or clinical outcomes would more effectively⏎incentivize best practices. Such an approach would allow Medicare to capture the true value of AI⏎tools, support responsible clinical adoption, and ensure that radiologists and other clinicians are⏎appropriately recognized for the work associated with integrating AI into patient care.⏎We seek input on ways in which HHS may invest in research & development (including public-⏎private partnerships and cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs)) to⏎2⏎⏎integrate AI in care delivery and create new, long-term market opportunities that improve the⏎health and wellbeing of all Americans.⏎ACR supports expanded federal investment in AI innovation, including targeted research and⏎development (R&D) activities that advance high value clinical use cases for radiologist end-users.⏎HHS agencies should work closely with professional associations, such as the ACR, to ensure that⏎federally funded R&D efforts are aligned with the- practical needs of clinicians and the care⏎environments in which AI will be deployed. Strategic public private partnerships and CRADAs⏎focused on clinically meaningful radiology applications would help accelerate development,⏎strengthen real world evaluation, and create durable marke-t opportunities that improve patient⏎care and long term health outcomes.⏎-⏎- HHS Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎Unsustainable Payment—Clinical AI adoption is not currently supported by sustainable and⏎appropriate Medicare payment policies that promote clinically useful innovation, reward safe and⏎effective deployment/governance practices, and advance appropriate/responsible use of AI by⏎qualified end-users.⏎Challenges Meeting Use Cases of Practical Value—Private sector innovators often pursue AI⏎tools optimized for regulatory success, academic recognition, or investor appeal rather than⏎solutions that meet high value, real world needs in current clinical environments. AI solutions that⏎deliver genuine clinical value are frequently obscured by a crowded landscape of tools that may not⏎meaningfully support rea-l world pra-ctice improvements and efficiencies.⏎Lack of AI Model Transpa-rency/Resources—There are few model transparency requirements and⏎trusted government or third-party resources available to support AI investment and adoption⏎decision-making. AI innovations must be financially and clinically justifiable to those making⏎adoption decisions, and deployers need information to ensure their investments are likely to yield⏎return on investment via patient care improvements and full cost recovery. This was a driver behind⏎ACR’s establishment of the AI Central repository of AI-enabled software medical devices; however,⏎this tool is limited to the radiology domain.⏎Interoperability/Integration Problems—Insufficient interoperability between AI functions and⏎disparate, fragmented systems within clinical settings create significant adoption and usability⏎barriers. For example, a lack of standardized API access poses a challenge for integrating AI⏎innovations into legacy platforms, including picture archiving and communication systems (PACS)⏎and EHR systems. This lack of interoperability between AI systems and EHR/PACS makes⏎performance monitoring cumbersome and costly.⏎Privacy/Security Compliance Uncertainty—Hesitancy about privacy/security compliance⏎requirements, such as requirements under the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act⏎(HIPAA), can also pose a barrier to AI adoption. Participation in clinical data registries, such as⏎Assess-AI, encourage AI adoption by providing real-world monitoring capabilities to qualified end-⏎users. However, due to unclear requirements or overly cautious compliance strategies regarding⏎3⏎⏎data sharing, clinical sites may be reluctant to share data with clinical data registries that they⏎perceive as requiring additional authorizations.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Through its agencies’ regulatory authorities and programmatic activities, HHS should promote or⏎reinforce appropriate and sustainable payment, AI model transparency, good governance practices⏎among deployers—such as post deployment monitoring via national registries and, where⏎appropriate, practice accreditation—and also ensure that primary clinical end users possess the⏎qualifications necessary to deliv-er the associated patient care services and effectively mitigate⏎risk. -⏎-⏎Performance Monitoring—Post-deployment performance monitoring is crucial for detecting⏎model drift, sub-population effectiveness gaps, and site-specific failures. ACR’s Assess-AI is a⏎national registry for radiology AI models that captures real-world data to provide qualified end-⏎users with technology for continuous automated monitoring of model inputs and outputs,⏎versioning, and concordance with radiologist reports. As a result, Assess-AI allows users to capture⏎data during clinical use to ensure safe and effective performance of AI medical devices in real world⏎settings. In addition, Assess-AI compares performance of local model-based inference with⏎aggregated national data (processed by same or different commercial models on identical clinical⏎use cases) to uncover performance variation and identify improvement opportunities.⏎To better enable AI performance monitoring via national registries and other mechanisms, HHS⏎regulations, guidance, and other initiatives should advance standardized exchange of AI outputs,⏎model/version identifiers, and device meta data to allow such comparisons on a broad scale. This⏎infrastructure could further serve as the basis for a structured “AI Product Facts” schema that⏎standardizes how model attributes and outputs are communicated and consistently interpreted⏎during performance monitoring. Additionally, HHS could address privacy/security concerns of⏎participation by clarifying via sub-regulatory guidance or other informational resources that AI⏎performance monitoring via national registries is a recognized “healthcare operations” activity⏎under HIPAA.⏎Good Governance—HHS’ regulatory framework should scale organizational readiness via⏎accreditation, attestation, and governance playbooks. Encouraging adoption of low-burden,⏎auditable building blocks, such as AI governance groups, algorithm inventories, workflow⏎documentation and training, and ongoing monitoring can help ensure organizations have capacity⏎for AI adoption. For example, ACR’s AI quality assurance program (ARCH-AI) supports⏎organizational readiness by recognizing adherence to best practices for AI governance.⏎Appropriate Payment—With respect to reimbursement, one of the central challenges in⏎establishing appropriate payment for AI tools lies in determining how these technologies should be⏎incorporated into the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule. AI applications span a wide range of⏎clinical workflows and are often developed for specific imaging modalities (e.g., CT, MRI,⏎ultrasound) and anatomical regions. As a result, creating distinct CPT codes for each AI solution⏎risks substantial fragmentation of the existing imaging code set. This fragmentation could⏎4⏎⏎undermine coding consistency, complicate billing, and create administrative burden for providers⏎and payers.⏎An alternative reimbursement approach may involve aligning payment for AI deployment and use⏎with the measurable value these tools contribute to clinical care. Although there are frequent⏎suggestions that AI tools could reduce radiologist workload, the current reality is quite different. In⏎practice, radiologists often invest additional time to review, validate, and interpret AI outputs,⏎particularly as they integrate these technologies into routine practice with appropriate clinical⏎caution. Radiologists continue to adopt AI because of its strong long-term value proposition, but⏎the short-term effect is often an increase, not a decrease, in cognitive and workflow demands.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎For non-device clinical AI—which is by definition “unregulated” by HHS agencies but may be in⏎voluntarily ASTP/ONC-certified health IT systems—there is a lack of clarity regarding where the⏎responsibility lies when errors occur. There are no established standards designating⏎responsibilities and expectations for handling model updates, data logging, and incidence⏎response sharing amongst HHS, the developer, the platform, and the clinical site.⏎ASTP/ONC should strive to help fill this gap via federal standards or recommendations for shared⏎safety, effectiveness, risk-appropriate monitoring, and privacy/security responsibilities for non-⏎device clinical AI use.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements,⏎and/or prize competitions)?⏎Evaluation methods should be applied in a consistent and risk-appropriate manner for all AI utilized⏎in patient care, including unregulated non-device AI. AI tools should undergo pre-deployment⏎testing as well as post-deployment monitoring.⏎Pre-deployment testing should include external validation, subgroup analyses to confirm that no⏎patient population is disproportionately impacted by differences in model performance,⏎stress/robustness testing, and usability/workflow impact testing.⏎Post-deployment monitoring should involve continuously gathering AI output in real-world clinical⏎settings to detect model drift and ensure safe and effective use. ACR’s Assess-AI national registry—⏎which is currently limited in applicability to AI medical devices used in radiology—exemplifies how⏎such post-deployment monitoring could be applied more broadly via tracking algorithm versions,⏎inputs, and concordance between AI outputs and radiology reports over time and across sub-⏎cohorts. Local clinical governance committees can use the resulting dashboards to benchmark⏎performance against national peer organizations, investigate outliers, and detect drift early.⏎5⏎⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS should encourage site readiness programs to standardize governance and monitoring⏎expectations across clinical sites, which could include future practice accreditation akin to⏎mammography and advanced diagnostic imaging provider accreditation programs at FDA and CMS.⏎ACR, as an experienced accreditor of various imaging and treatment modalities, is working toward⏎development of an AI practice accreditation program. In making progress toward that objective,⏎ACR established an attestation-based AI quality assurance program (ARCH-AI) that promotes and⏎recognizes adherence to best practices for AI governance in imaging interpretation while facilitating⏎gap analysis and quality and process improvement. Participants in that program also must promise⏎to participate if feasible in ACR’s Assess-AI registry. Additionally, ACR is using lessons learned from⏎these programs to help develop a “Practice Parameter”, which will form the basis for ACR’s future⏎accreditation program design, for radiology AI in 2026.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎Currently available AI tools in clinical care have demonstrated meaningful promise in several⏎targeted, task specific applications, particularly those that support clinician workflows rather than⏎replace clinical judgment. For example, in radiology, CAD detection and diagnostic devices⏎typically perfo-rm well when narrowly focused on a defined task and when integrated into clinical⏎workflows of a qualified end-user (i.e., an end-user qualified to perform the same patient care⏎service without the AI and therefore able to help mitigate safety/effectiveness risks).⏎In many other areas, however, AI tools have not yet met cost or performance expectations,⏎particularly where vendor pricing structures remain prohibitively high. In some cases, AI vendors⏎may capture a disproportionate share of the technical fee associated with a medical procedure,⏎creating substantial financial burdens for facilities and limiting broader adoption. As a result, while⏎the underlying technology may perform to expectation, real world deployment may be⏎unsustainable and impractical due to cost recovery problems.⏎-⏎Looking forward, several categories of newer, more advanced AI tools have significant potential to⏎improve outcomes and enhance value. Tools capable of real time, context aware integration of the⏎patient’s longitudinal medical record, powered by large language models (LLMs), could markedly⏎enhance diagnostic precision. These types of intelligent, inte-ractive system-s go beyond traditional⏎pattern-recognition algorithms. They have the potential to improve care quality, reduce diagnostic⏎error, and generate substantial efficiencies, especially if implemented at a price point⏎commensurate with the value delivered. For example, a radiologist may identify an indeterminate⏎liver lesion while interpreting a CT abdomen and pelvis study. A hypothetical AI system that⏎simultaneously reviews relevant prior imaging, pathology, clinical notes, and laboratory data, and⏎then proposes a contextually informed differential diagnosis with estimated likelihoods could⏎meaningfully improve diagnostic accuracy and clinical decision making.⏎-⏎6⏎⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Departmental governance, enterprise IT/security, legal and compliance teams, and procurement⏎and finance teams hold significant administrative influence over the adoption of AI within⏎healthcare organizations. These are generally important for handling quality assurance, legal, and⏎privacy/security compliance considerations.⏎However, administrators can encounter hurdles such as IT/infrastructure interoperability issues⏎and competition for limited resources within a given institution. Many AI tools designed for⏎healthcare settings are priced on a per patient/use basis, which significantly impacts financial⏎considerations and administrative decisions around acquisition, deployment, and use. Other⏎challenges include vendor contracts/agreements, security review complications, staffing/resource⏎limitations on quality assurance capabilities, and model/product transparency shortfalls.⏎There is currently a large, unmet need to organize and prioritize AI requests. There is a role for⏎professional/trade associations and others to help inform this type of administrative decision-⏎making. HHS could explore mechanisms for supporting this work through funding opportunities or⏎other collaborations.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would significantly broaden market opportunities, strengthen research,⏎and accelerate AI development for clinical care. Today, fragmentation in data types, standards, and⏎benchmarking across disparate systems creates barriers to both effective R&D and seamless⏎clinical integration. Improving interoperability would make it easier to aggregate datasets, evaluate⏎AI performance across diverse settings, and integrate AI outputs into existing IT infrastructure and⏎clinical workflows. To address these challenges, HHS should support and promote interoperability⏎of AI results and outputs across systems by advancing the use of criteria developed by appropriate⏎standards, professional, and trade organizations. This would empower R&D operations, reduce AI⏎integration burdens, improve consistency in evaluating model performance, and help ensure⏎clinically useful AI innovations can be adopted more readily and safely.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Radiology providers have observed patients’ frustrations with procedure scheduling and follow-up⏎delays, repeat/duplicative testing, and rising healthcare costs. Patients are generally interested in⏎ensuring that appropriate guardrails are in place to ensure safety, effectiveness, privacy, and⏎security (e.g., FDA’s reasonable assurance of safety and effectiveness is important to patients for⏎establishing trust in AI medical devices). Patients seem to be wary of an overly automated care⏎experience and may be more receptive to clinical AI when a physician with the appropriate⏎qualifications is managing AI-enhanced care. Establishing patient-facing disclosure standards⏎7⏎⏎(where risk-appropriate) and setting clear expectations for incident reporting can also help⏎establish or enhance patients’ trust in AI tool-empowered care by their physicians.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research into implementation science for real-world deployment of AI. These⏎efforts should include research into methods for detecting and addressing model drift and⏎implementing AI into clinical workflows. Monitoring and benchmarking of AI devices is critical for⏎safe and effective implementation; HHS should utilize public-private partnerships and cooperative⏎agreements to build shared infrastructure for developers and qualified users to monitor AI output.⏎Additionally, HHS should also encourage more research into AI for pediatrics and rare diseases—⏎with prioritized funding opportunities from NIH/ARPA-H/other research agencies and incentives for⏎developers to invest in these innovations (e.g., continued prioritization in FDA’s Breakthrough⏎Device Program)—to ensure AI has broad applicability and clinical impact. Pediatrics and rare⏎disease care are relatively small markets with limited data, which results in less commercial AI⏎development for these fields. A pediatric disease can have impacts that span the child’s entire life,⏎however.⏎The ACR welcomes further communication and collaboration with ASTP on AI safety and⏎effectiveness. For questions, please do not hesitate to contact Mike Peters, ACR Senior Director,⏎Government Affairs, at mpeters@acr.org, Lindsay Robbins, ACR Regulatory Policy Specialist, at⏎lmrobbins@acr.org, or Christina Berry, Manager, Economic Policy, at cberry@acr.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Dana H. Smetherman, MD, MPH, MBA, FACR⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎American College of Radiology⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔