UID-0410♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Texas Medical Association , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Texas Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building; Mail Stop 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted Via Federal Register⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎On behalf of the Texas Medical Association (TMA) and our more than 60,000 physician and medical⏎student members, we thank the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for the opportunity to respond to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎TMA is a private, voluntary, non-profit association and the largest state medical society in the nation. It⏎was founded in 1853 to serve the people of Texas in matters of medical care, prevention and cure of⏎disease, and improvement of public health. Today, its vision is “improving the health of all Texans.”⏎TMA understands the importance of proceeding carefully related to how artificial intelligence (AI) is⏎developed, implemented, and used in health care.⏎TMA distinguishes between the terms “augmented intelligence” and “artificial intelligence.” We agree⏎with the industry view that artificial intelligence replaces human function and intelligence such as a self-⏎driving car or a computer-generated chatbot on a website. Augmented intelligence provides prompts and⏎information to support human decision-making rather than replace it. We believe that is an important⏎distinction. Augmented intelligence is intended to co-exist with human decision-making – it should not⏎be used to replace physician reasoning and knowledge and should neither replace nor diminish the⏎patient-physician relationship. Throughout the letter, “AI” is used for brevity, but we ask that you keep⏎in mind TMA’s preferred terms.⏎TMA supports the use of augmented intelligence when used appropriately to support physician decision-⏎making, enhance patient care, and improve public health. Augmented intelligence should also be used in⏎ways that reduce physician burden and increase professional satisfaction.⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎At the same time, sufficient safeguards should be in place to assign appropriate liability inherent in⏎augmented intelligence to the software developers and not to those with no control over the software⏎content and integrity, such as physicians and other users.⏎RFI Question 1. What are the biggest barriers to private-sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: There must be trust that the AI tools are developed transparently and that data accuracy,⏎patient safety, and clinical outcomes are prioritized during all phases of product development. This⏎includes the ability to easily access and assess algorithms, algorithm provenance, training data⏎assumptions, training data source(s), and performance characteristics.⏎AI tools used for clinical care need seamless integration with electronic health records (EHR) and other⏎technology tools used by physicians so precise patient information is used to inform the decisions⏎returned by AI tools. One barrier is some EHR vendors are developing their own AI tools and may⏎choose to prevent other clinically validated tools from integrating with their proprietary EHR⏎technology.⏎The initial investment can be high and is a concern and barrier when adopting new technologies in⏎medical practice. Before savings are realized from the adoption of a technology, the investment must be⏎made, the technology implemented, the technology outputs validated, and finally the technology⏎optimized. Once optimization occurs, cost savings may then be realized in reduced human resources or⏎improved data quality output resulting in practice efficiencies.⏎RFI Question 2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎TMA Response: Developers and users need regulatory clarity and consistency when developing and⏎implementing AI.⏎There is uncertainty around disclosure and consent and when they apply, which is based on how the AI⏎tools and vendors are using patient data. Additionally, there is no clear guidance on how to disclose or⏎obtain consent when a physician orders diagnostic testing for the patient and it is unknown if and how a⏎pathologist or radiologist may use AI when interpreting tests. It would also be helpful to have vendor⏎transparency around HIPAA claims and stated HIPAA compliance as well as compliance with any other⏎applicable regulations.⏎Mandating specific consents for specific AI technologies will become increasingly difficult as the⏎number of solutions increase. Having disparate federal and state requirements will prove challenging to⏎AI users in clinical care.⏎As the use of AI in health care becomes ubiquitous, there are new ways to protect patient data.⏎Physicians and other health care covered entities are increasingly incorporating large language models⏎(LLMs) into their practices. With AI tools, insights from the LLMs can be used for retrospective and⏎prospective analysis of patient outcomes and physician performance. The information generated in AI⏎tools using patient data should be protected and considered privileged and confidential and should not be⏎used to penalize physicians.⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎Additionally, there should be a safe-harbor framework for physicians and other clinicians using HHS-⏎recognized or validated tools as intended and when the physician documents appropriate review of AI-⏎generated data.⏎RFI Question 3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues⏎exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎TMA Response: Use of AI, machine learning, and clinical decision support has inherent risks. Legal and⏎ethical responsibility for the use and output of these products must be assumed by, including but not⏎limited to, developers, distributors, and users with each entity owning responsibility for its respective⏎role in the development, dissemination, implementation, and use of products used in clinical care. Many⏎times, the end-user licensing agreements presented by technology vendors do not give options for⏎negotiation, and the vendors fully indemnify themselves from algorithmic errors or other issues that may⏎arise. It would be helpful to prevent this type of indemnification that undermines shared responsibility.⏎Physicians need protection from medico-legal liability when AI tools have insufficient training data,⏎incorrect information in the training model, or biases in the data used to train the model that could⏎produce faulty outputs. To increase user confidence, AI tools must have some reliability and⏎reproducibility.⏎AI tools should never independently and unilaterally make decisions about a patient’s diagnosis or care.⏎There should also be shared responsibility with AI developers and deployers of AI that the products⏎have some level of reliability and reproducibility. Just as EHRs undergo a certification process to verify⏎the product performs certain functions, AI tools used in clinical care could undergo a similar verification⏎process.⏎Once implemented, all AI tools should have a feedback loop that allows users an easy way to report⏎potential safety hazards as well as problems and malfunctions. This feedback loop can also serve as an⏎opportunity to recommend AI tool improvements.⏎RFI Question 4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care?⏎TMA Response: AI algorithms must be designed for the benefit, safety, and privacy of the patient.⏎Algorithms and other information used to derive the information presented as AI to physicians and other⏎clinicians should:⏎• Be developed transparently in a way that is accessible, explainable, and understandable to⏎clinicians and patients and that details the benefits and limitations of the clinical decision⏎support, and/or augmented intelligence;⏎• Have reproducible and explainable outputs;⏎• Function in a way that promotes health equities while eliminating potential biases that exacerbate⏎health disparities;⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎• Use best practices for a user-centered design that allows for efficient and satisfactory use of the⏎technology;⏎• Safeguard patient information by employing privacy and security standards that comply with⏎HIPAA and state privacy regulations;⏎• Have a feedback loop that allows users who identify potential safety hazards to easily report⏎problems and malfunctions and that allows for opportunities to report methods for⏎improvements; and⏎• Contain a level of compatibility to allow use of information between hardware and software⏎made by different manufacturers.⏎It would be helpful to assess AI algorithms with standardized disclosures that include intended use,⏎limitations, known failure modes, and independent evaluation pathway. Additionally, when available,⏎vendors should disclose to their users the results of third-party evaluations AI vendors have undergone,⏎which could include data on accuracy, known limitations, and, if applicable, the populations or settings⏎in which the tool was validated. There should be an expectation of post-deployment accountability,⏎including mechanisms to monitor the AI performance over time, identify data drift or bias, and provide⏎clear feedback and remediation pathways to users when issues arise. Other useful metrics include⏎workflow impact, time saved, error severity, and downstream clinical decisions.⏎RFI Question 5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: Accreditation and certification should be a carefully considered pathway. While it does⏎instill confidence in users, it also has the potential to slow innovation. At the very least, HHS should⏎consider required standardized reporting by AI vendors that includes information related to intended use⏎of technology, training data sources and limitation, validation settings, and known failures modes. This⏎information could be displayed in a standardized way similar to what is required for nutritional labels on⏎food products.⏎RFI Question 6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest⏎potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎TMA Response: Section 1557 of the Affordable Care Act, HHS, and the Office of Civil Rights mandates⏎the availability of qualified interpreters for patients with limited English proficiency or disabilities.⏎Accessing interpreters can be cost prohibitive for some practices and can cost more than the payment for⏎the service provided. Cost savings can be accrued if virtual and digital interpreters are permitted, as long⏎as validation exists for translation accuracy.⏎To gain insight, TMA surveyed its members in 2025 and asked respondents how they are currently using⏎or wanting to use AI in their practice (see figure 1). Physicians are using tools that reduce administrative⏎burden but want to use tools for clinical decision support and quality improvement.⏎The use of ambient scribes has proven to be a successful use case of an AI tool that reduces⏎administrative burden and assists physicians by allowing them to focus on the patient rather than the⏎computer. Physicians also desire AI tools that can assist with prior authorizations and appeals (while⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎respecting the need for physician clinical judgment), as prior authorization requirements have become so⏎administratively burdensome to practices that additional staff are needed to handle the extra work.⏎Physicians are still hesitant, however, to use clinical decision support tools because of the lack of⏎validation and potential for data drift. Without some medico-legal protections, use of clinical decision⏎support tools will continue to be hampered.⏎Figure 1⏎RFI Question 7. Which role(s), decision-maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: Because of the wide variety of health care entities, the roles of the decision-makers vary⏎greatly. For example, in a small independent practice, the physician and office manager will wear many⏎hats. In a large integrated multi-specialty practice, there may be a chief medical information officer and⏎a chief technology officer leading the decision-making process on all technology tools, including AI,⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎used by the organization. Some of the primary hurdles to adoption include technology costs, uncertain⏎return on investment, time spent training users, user acceptance, validated data outputs, patient⏎acceptance, and proven clinical outcome improvements.⏎RFI Question 8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards,⏎and benchmarking tools.⏎TMA Response: Interoperability remains challenging due to a lack of data vocabulary standardization.⏎For example, in proposed United States Core Data for Interoperability (USCDI) version 6, there are a⏎total of 132 data elements of which 57 have no correlating vocabulary standard. Interoperability⏎continues to be hindered without standardized vocabulary for data elements. Additionally, there is a lack⏎of fidelity and usability of the data exchanged. Interoperability should be not only about the movement⏎of the data, but also the quality and usability of the data received. Recipients need well-organized and⏎meaningful data outputs to efficiently analyze and make decisions about patient care. AI solutions could⏎be developed and deployed to translate non-standardized data elements and to usefully summarize⏎patient data, potentially according to specialty-specific specifications, enhancing the quality of the⏎patient information received and reviewed.⏎RFI Question 9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎TMA Response: Physicians and patients remain concerned about unreliable technology and its use of⏎data that may be leveraged against physicians and patients. The trust of the company and the reliability⏎of the technology are paramount. There is also concern over how patient data and AI-generated outputs⏎are used downstream for patient and physician analytics or other secondary purposes with unintended⏎consequences.⏎A common goal should be well-developed and well-implemented AI technology that functions securely⏎and accurately behind the scenes, optimizing information at the point of care without being intrusive to⏎the physician or the patient.⏎Additionally, there is an explicit distinction between AI used for quality improvement, patient care, and⏎clinical research versus AI used for enforcement or billing oversight. Physicians may be hesitant to⏎engage with tools that generate insights that could later be used punitively. Physicians who choose to⏎use AI tools for patient care must have reassurances that any oversight mechanisms installed or usage⏎data compiled will be used for education and not to penalize its users.⏎RFI Question 10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎TMA Response: HHS could consider the following areas of research to accelerate the adoption of AI as⏎part of clinical care:⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG⏎⏎• How AI tools perform after deployment, including data drift, real-world safety issues, and⏎practical ways physicians can report concerns, near misses, or incorrect information.⏎• Human factors and workflow impact: When AI reduces burden versus when it unintentionally⏎adds cognitive load or documentation work.⏎• Medico-legal and accountability frameworks that protect patient safety while giving physicians⏎reasonable reliance protections when using clinically validated tools.⏎• Equity and bias evaluation in clinical environments, including rural settings, safety-net⏎populations, and differing documentation practices.⏎• Interoperability and data-quality research that focuses not just on moving data, but on making it⏎usable and actionable at the point of care.⏎• Education and competency development for trainees and practicing physicians to support safe,⏎supervised, and appropriate use of AI in clinical care.⏎• Impact on medical education and whether AI degrades diagnostic reasoning or can be used as a⏎supervised teaching tool.⏎• Validating patient-facing AI for clinical triage and guidance to the right level of care to assist⏎patients struggling to navigate the health care system.⏎• Algorithmic vigilance systems that detect hallucinations, bias, or unsafe outputs before making⏎clinical recommendations.⏎• Validation of studies in the use of AI for pattern recognition and clinical work, e.g. retinal⏎screening and image reading.⏎• Small-data AI for rare diseases and specific populations.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to respond to the request for information. TMA stands ready to provide⏎you and others within the agency with our policy insight and any additional assistance you may find⏎useful. If you have any questions, please do not hesitate to contact Shannon Vogel, associate vice⏎president of health information technology, by emailing shannon.vogel@texmed.org or calling (512)⏎370-1411.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jayesh “Jay” Shah, MD⏎President⏎Texas Medical Association⏎TEXAS MEDICAL ASSOCIATION 401 WEST 15TH STREET AUSTIN, TEXAS 78701-1680 (512)370-1300 FAX (512)370-1693 WWW.TEXMED.ORG♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0411♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from ICF, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ICF♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20th, 2026⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted to: Submitted by:⏎Department of Health and ICF Incorporated, L.L.C.⏎Human Services 1902 Reston Metro Plaza⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/ Reston, VA 20190⏎Office of the National Coordinator for 703.934.3000⏎Health Information Technology⏎Patrick McConnell⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎404.909.2277⏎330 C Street SW⏎patrick.mcconnell@icf.com⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Ratima Kataria⏎Steven Posnack⏎571.226.7979⏎202.690.7151⏎ratima.kataria@icf.com⏎This proposal includes data that shall not be disclosed outside the Government and shall not be duplicated, used, or disclosed—in whole or in part—⏎for any purpose other than to evaluate this proposal. If, however, a contract is awarded to this offeror as a result of—or in conjunction with—the⏎submission of these data, the Government shall have the right to duplicate, use, or disclose the data to the extent provided in the resulting contract.⏎This restriction does not limit the Government’s right to use information contained in these data if it is obtained from another source without⏎restriction. The data subject to this restriction are contained in this volume and its appendices and attachments.⏎⏎February 20th, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Subject: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Posnack:⏎I am pleased to submit ICF’s response to the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Request for Information (RFI): Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care.⏎ICF is a global consulting and technology firm with more than five decades of experience⏎supporting federal health agencies in modernizing programs, data, and digital systems. Our⏎interdisciplinary teams span policy, technology, analytics, and human-centered design and⏎deliver integrated, practical solutions that strengthen performance and outcomes across CMS,⏎NIH, and other HHS divisions.⏎We bring direct experience translating policy into operational requirements, governance⏎structures, measurable outcomes, and scalable technical approaches. Our work with CMS⏎provides deep familiarity with program and payment levers that can incentivize safe clinical AI⏎adoption. We develop certifiable and auditable frameworks that providers and vendors can⏎implement to ensure trustworthy AI use.⏎ICF supports agencies in advancing robust AI evaluation methods through layered testing⏎aligned with NIST principles, real-world monitoring, and data infrastructure that enables⏎validation over time. We also help expand interoperability and standards adoption to unlock⏎high-quality datasets and enable scalable deployment, always grounded in human-centered⏎design and clinician-focused usability considerations.⏎We welcome the opportunity to help HHS address policy, reimbursement, and research⏎considerations and accelerate safe, effective, and equitable clinical AI adoption.⏎Sincerely,⏎Patrick McConnell Ratima Kataria⏎SVP Health Engineering Solutions VP Health IT Strategy and Growth⏎404.909.2277 571.226.7979⏎patrick.mcconnell@icf.com ratima.kataria@icf.com⏎1902 Reston Metro Plaza, Reston, VA 20190 USA +1.703.934.3000 icf.com⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal.⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎Table of Contents⏎Executive Summary .........................................................................................................................1⏎Incentivizing Effective Use of AI in Clinical Care (Q. #2) .............................................................2⏎Novel Issues with AI in Non-Medical Devices (Q. #3) ...................................................................3⏎Most Promising AI Evaluation Methods (Q. #4) .............................................................................4⏎Promising Evaluation Methods ............................................................................................4⏎Evaluation Metrics ...............................................................................................................5⏎Supporting the Development of Improved Evaluation Methods .........................................5⏎HCD in Clinical Care AI ......................................................................................................5⏎HHS Support for Private Sector Activities (Q. #5) ..........................................................................6⏎Widening Opportunities through Interoperability (Q. #8) ...............................................................8⏎Data Types ...........................................................................................................................8⏎Data Standards .....................................................................................................................8⏎Market Impact ......................................................................................................................9⏎Benchmarking ......................................................................................................................9⏎Specific Areas of AI Research to Prioritize (Q. #10) ......................................................................9⏎Research Priorities for Clinical AI Adoption .....................................................................10⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. i⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎Executive Summary⏎ICF (NASDAQ: ICFI) is a global consulting and⏎technology services company with more than 50 years of⏎successful performance helping public and private sector⏎organizations navigate change and shape the future.⏎Founded in 1969, ICF combines deep domain expertise in⏎areas such as health, social programs, energy,⏎environment, and digital modernization with advanced⏎analytics, data science, and creative and digital⏎engagement capabilities. Today, ICF is a publicly traded⏎corporation with approximately 9,000 full-time and part-time employees worldwide and annual⏎revenues of about $2.0 billion.⏎ICF’s interdisciplinary teams—policy specialists, technologists, data scientists, and creatives—⏎work together to deliver integrated solutions that are practical, evidence-based, and measurable.⏎Clients rely on ICF for high-stakes, mission-critical work that requires both subject-matter depth⏎and rigorous program management, supported by mature quality, information security, and IT⏎service management systems.⏎ICF brings a long-standing federal footprint, with expertise in federal health, supporting agencies⏎as they modernize data, technology, and programs that improve outcomes, affordability, and⏎equity. Across HHS and its operating divisions, ICF delivers end-to-end capabilities spanning⏎strategy and policy, research and evaluation, digital modernization, data platforms and analytics,⏎and implementation support for large-scale programs and systems. Our federal health work⏎includes sustained support to CMS on enterprise solutions and modernization efforts and⏎biomedical/public health data and modernization support for NIH/NLM, complemented by⏎broader federal health offerings that strengthen surveillance, community programs, and health⏎communications.⏎This RFI response focuses on the questions where ICF has specific, demonstrable experience and⏎expertise: translating policy intent into operational requirements, implementable governance,⏎measurable outcomes, and scalable technology approaches. In particular, our recommendations⏎reflect practical experience with (i) payment and program levers that can be operationalized⏎through CMS quality programs and models, (ii) certifiable requirements and auditable controls⏎that can be implemented by providers and vendors, and (iii) evaluation and human-centered⏎methods that increase clinical usability, trust, and adoption over time.⏎The remainder of this document recommends that HHS accelerate safe and effective clinical AI⏎adoption by using proven CMS mechanisms to create meaningful incentives (e.g., QPP/PI⏎measures, Improvement Activities, and MVP alignment) and by embedding outcome-based “pay⏎for validated value” approaches within high-leverage CMMI models; establish a voluntary⏎certification and contracting/procurement baseline for non-medical AI used in clinical settings;⏎advance evaluation methods that combine NIST-aligned risk management with layered testing⏎(model outputs, system artifacts, and real-world user experience) and robust post-deployment⏎monitoring supported by gold-standard datasets, SBIR/prizes, and standards; and expand⏎interoperability through consistent adoption of key health data standards to enable benchmark⏎datasets, portability, and scalable deployment—while prioritizing HCD, trustworthy AI, and⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 1⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎human–AI interaction research to ensure solutions fit clinical workflows and improve⏎patient experience.⏎Incentivizing Effective Use of AI in Clinical Care (Q. #2)⏎HHS can and should incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care. For example, HHS can⏎use the Quality Payment Program (QPP) and replicate the CEHRT/Promoting⏎Interoperability (PI) playbook by setting a clear, testable “floor” for what qualifies as⏎acceptable technology and use and then attaching meaningful scoring and payment⏎consequences.⏎Specifically, CMS should do the following:⏎1. Add a QPP definition for a certifiable class of clinical AI (e.g., “certified clinical AI⏎module”) by building on the existing CEHRT definitional structure in 42 CFR § 414.1305⏎and aligning certification mechanics to the ONC Health IT Certification Program authorities⏎in 45 CFR part 170, subpart E.⏎2. Update the PI performance category at 42 CFR § 414.1375 to create a scored⏎objective/measure set for “safe and effective clinical AI use,” limited to certified modules⏎and requiring auditable basics (e.g., version transparency/audit logs, clinician override,⏎performance monitoring). This will ensure CMS pays for verified capability rather than⏎marketing claims.⏎3. Add AI governance and post-deployment monitoring as high-weight Improvement Activities⏎via the IA inventory process at 42 CFR § 414.1355.⏎4. Prevent fragmented incentives by establishing one or more “AI-enabled care” MIPS Value⏎Pathways (MVPs) under 42 CFR § 414.1365 that bundle the AI expectations across⏎Quality/Cost/IA/PI instead of scattering optional attestations.⏎In addition, because the CEHRT approach also flowed into Advanced APM design, CMS should⏎extend the same logic to Advanced APM alignment by building on the existing Advanced APM⏎CEHRT criterion at 42 CFR § 414.1415—not by requiring “AI” generically, but by requiring⏎that any high-impact AI used in clinical decision support or automation within an Advanced⏎APM context meet certifiable, auditable, monitored standards. This is a direct analogue to how⏎“use CEHRT” is operationalized today. Together, these steps will allow HHS to incentivize⏎effective AI use in clinical care using an established program and proven mechanisms.⏎HHS can also use CMMI Innovation Models to incentivize AI use in clinical care settings. To do⏎so, HHS should prioritize programmatic design changes that (a) lower the upfront adoption⏎barrier the way CEHRT programs did, but (b) only “pay for AI” when it produces⏎validated value and remains safe in real-world use. The fastest way to do this would be to⏎hardwire an “AI capability track” into high-leverage models. LEAD is explicitly positioned to⏎launch after ACO REACH concludes at the end of 2026, making it the cleanest successor vehicle⏎for model-wide expectations (e.g., allowing infrastructure-style payments or enhanced care⏎management payments for certified AI deployment while gating shared savings/benchmarks on⏎monitored performance and equity checks).⏎Similarly, models with longitudinal care management or defined episodes can incorporate AI in⏎tightly measured ways. For example, Kidney Care Choices (KCC) has been extended through⏎2027, so CMS can add a standardized option to support certified AI-enabled risk⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 2⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎stratification/care management as a covered “model capability,” while requiring ongoing⏎monitoring and evaluation tied to model endpoints. The Enhancing Oncology Model (EOM)⏎began July 1, 2023 and added a second cohort July 1, 2025, creating a ready platform for AI-⏎enabled symptom monitoring and care navigation. As noted above, this will work best when⏎paired with post-deployment monitoring requirements and outcome-based gates rather than pure⏎technology attestation. Because TEAM is explicitly a mandatory episode-based model, CMS can⏎require transparency and auditability safeguards whenever participants use AI for episode⏎identification, discharge planning, or post-acute decision support—protecting beneficiaries while⏎still encouraging adoption through standardized requirements rather than ad hoc vendor-by-⏎vendor negotiations.⏎By incentivizing AI use with these models, HHS can leverage existing processes and⏎mechanisms to encourage AI adoption and use, accelerating innovation and maximizing benefit⏎to citizens and stakeholders.⏎Novel Issues with AI in Non-Medical Devices (Q. #3)⏎It is true that use of AI in non-medical devices in clinical care settings may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues. AI technologies are rapidly evolving, and gaps are emerging in existing⏎frameworks governing liability, privacy, safety, and accountability. However, HHS is⏎well-positioned—through the OneHHS approach articulated in its recent AI Strategy—to lead by⏎clarifying expectations, enabling innovation, and safeguarding patients. We recommend two⏎priority actions.⏎First, HHS should establish a voluntary certification framework for non-medical AI tools,⏎modeled on ONC’s Health IT Certification Program. This framework would accelerate the⏎responsible adoption AI tools while reducing the regulatory burden on innovators by providing a⏎means to evaluate and compare:⏎• Transparency and documentation.⏎• Safety monitoring and performance metrics.⏎• Bias and fairness assessments.⏎• Data protection and access safeguards.⏎Second, HHS should issue model contracting and procurement guidance. By building off⏎OMB Memorandum M-25-22 and the HHS AI Strategy’s procurement guidance, especially the⏎use of “Cross-Cutting Enablers,” HHS should provide health systems and vendors a consistent⏎baseline by publishing contractual expectations for:⏎• Model auditability⏎• Data rights and ownership⏎• Security and privacy protections⏎• Prohibitions on unauthorized model training⏎• Clear user responsibilities⏎By taking these steps, HHS can provide the necessary guardrails to enable effective and safe⏎innovation with AI in non-medical devices for clinical care settings.⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 3⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎Most Promising AI Evaluation Methods (Q. #4)⏎There are several promising approaches for evaluating AI in non-medical devices and we believe⏎HHS should support them, especially because the best methods for ensuring safe, reliable⏎operation with LLM-powered AI solutions are currently subject to research and debate. Further⏎work is required to devise the best approaches for understanding, testing, and evaluating these⏎technologies, especially in clinical care settings. HHS should take the lead by establishing⏎standards and supporting research and investment. Doing so will increase trust, improve⏎performance, and accelerate adoption and use, thereby bringing benefit to providers and patients.⏎NIST’s AI Risk Management Framework (RMF) is a useful lens for orienting this work, as its⏎four functions (Govern, Map, Measure, Manage) provide a means of assessing LLM⏎performance and the risks of inadequate evaluation mechanisms. Another useful lens is the user.⏎We believe focusing on users will be the best way to ensure evaluation mechanisms align with⏎desired outcomes. Care providers are likely to be the greatest obstacle to broad adoption in⏎clinical care settings. Accordingly, HHS should use Human Centered Design (HCD) to position⏎care providers and patients at the center of evaluation method design and development. In⏎addition, when promoting adoption, HHS should encourage an incremental, evolutionary⏎approach that will create successes and build confidence. If done well, this will create a flywheel⏎effect that builds momentum. Initial successes will build confidence, leading to further adoption⏎and additional successes. Adoption and use will expand from there.⏎Promising Evaluation Methods⏎A variety of AI evaluation methods should be used to support increased adoption and use.⏎Currently, some of the most promising approaches include the following:⏎• Employing LLMs as judges.⏎• Adjusting LLM training approaches penalize hallucinations.⏎• Using dynamic evaluation methods to adjust benchmarks as LLM capabilities improve, so⏎that progress is consistent.⏎• Employing observability with tools like Langfuse.⏎• Red teaming to discover unanticipated LLM vulnerabilities.⏎Each of these approaches merits further research and support, especially in the field of clinical⏎care. We recommend integrating HCD with all these approaches, so that the behavior of AI⏎solutions is evaluated comprehensively and friction in the human-machine interface is⏎proactively identified and addressed.⏎Our concept follows the effective pattern of layering AI evaluations to provide a comprehensive⏎assessment of a solution’s effectiveness. These layers typically include evaluating LLM⏎responses in isolation, evaluating artifacts created from those responses by AI solutions (the⏎human-machine teaming that embeds the LLM), and evaluating the experiences that those⏎responses, artifacts, and solutions create. This layered approach is especially important in clinical⏎care. Both patients and care providers will interact with and experience an AI solution. If those⏎experiences are positive and valuable, then AI can drive improved outcomes for everyone.⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 4⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎Evaluation Metrics⏎There are several important metrics to consider for evaluation. Accuracy is crucial. Given the⏎nature of LLMs, accuracy is best broken into components, such as consistency, factual⏎correctness, and error count. Responses must also be relevant; LLMs should be evaluated on how⏎well they answered the question and whether they accessed the right data. This necessitates⏎subjectivity; user feedback on relevance, for example, is essential as people currently assess⏎relevancy far better than machines. This is one of the primary reasons why we contend it is so⏎important to employ HCD principles and approaches. Tone and clarity are also important factors⏎that require human assessment. Was the response easily understood or did it lead to confusion?⏎Was it at the right reading level? Finally, is the output fact-based and unbiased? As OMB Memo⏎M-26-04 states, it is important to ensure that AI solutions present truthful, ideological neutral⏎outputs. Accordingly, evaluation methods must account for these factors.⏎Supporting the Development of Improved Evaluation Methods⏎To support the development of improved evaluation mechanisms, several mechanisms are⏎available. First, HHS ought to develop and provide “gold standard” datasets for training and⏎evaluation of AI systems in clinical care settings. Those datasets could be created by HHS itself,⏎or HHS could sponsor their creation through grants, prize competitions, or contract work. Once⏎standardized data for training and evaluation is in place, progress with evaluation methods will⏎be more rapid and advance with greater confidence.⏎In addition, HHS should use SBIRs to encourage development of new evaluation methods and⏎tools. Focused grants and prize competitions should explore how best to use LLMs-as-judges in⏎specific clinical care domains. Grants and prizes should also be used to encourage the⏎development of additional models and datasets well-tuned to medical needs. Grants should be⏎issued for encouraging the development of automated mechanisms to dynamically keep those⏎models and datasets up to date as the field advances, a current research focus that HHS ought to⏎encourage. Grants should also be considered for developing effective red-teaming techniques for⏎clinical care scenarios and for identifying the most suitable metrics for evaluating AI solutions in⏎clinical care contexts.⏎HHS can also support and accelerate adoption by providing guidance and standards. How should⏎humans be centered in the evaluation process? How can vendors and partners leverage HCD with⏎confidence to integrate actual patients and clinicians into evaluations? HHS should encourage the⏎development of standards for these kinds of human-AI interactions in clinical care settings, so⏎that the industry can integrate human impacts into evaluation methods and practices. Standards⏎should also include acceptable levels of trust and bias, the use of LLMs-as-judges, and how AI⏎solutions should handle unanticipated failure modes when their use goes beyond the scope of⏎existing evaluations. By taking these steps, HHS can have the greatest impact on AI solutions⏎used for clinical care outside of medical devices.⏎HCD in Clinical Care AI⏎Human-Centered Design (HCD) builds reliability and trust in clinical AI by turning human⏎interactions, recovery from errors, and real-life use situations into clear evaluation goals.⏎Otherwise, they risk being left as untested assumptions. While model-level checks (like⏎accuracy, hallucination rates and robustness) show if an AI system can do a task, HCD looks at⏎whether the system works reliably when used in clinical workflows. For instance, usability⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 5⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎testing with clinicians can reveal if uncertainty is communicated clearly, if explanations make⏎sense under time pressure, and if the interface helps clinicians use AI properly. Signals, like how⏎often clinicians override the AI, make corrections, or hesitate, act as early signs of trust and⏎reveal potential system failures that model-only evaluations might miss.⏎HCD complements other LLM evaluation methods by considering real-world context. While⏎techniques like LLMs-as-judges or dynamic benchmarking can give scores to outputs, HCD⏎methods check if those outputs are useful, easy to understand and fit for the specific clinical⏎decision. For example, two models might get similar scores for factual accuracy, but HCD⏎testing can reveal that one model offers explanations that are more likely to lead to⏎misunderstanding of risk among clinicians. Usability testing with scenarios, participatory design⏎sessions, and practice clinical walkthroughs let evaluators see how AI behaves in real situations⏎(like during handoffs, interruptions, or with missing information) and make sure safety⏎features—such as alerts, warnings, or escalation steps—work properly in real usage settings.⏎HCD also assists effective monitoring after deployment. Instead of just tracking technical results,⏎HCD-based monitoring looks at how trust in the system changes over time. For example, HCD can⏎catch whether clinicians start ignoring AI outputs, start trusting them too much, or changing their⏎work in unexpected ways. These patterns can be tracked with simple feedback tools—like pop-up⏎prompts in the software, regular surveys linked to specific AI uses, or clinician decisions to⏎override or correct AI suggestions—and used with technical checks to know when to review the⏎system. In this way, HCD acts as the final layer of a comprehensive evaluation strategy that helps⏎ensure AI stays safe, helpful, and trusted as clinical settings, users, and the technology change.⏎To strengthen responsible clinical AI implementation, HHS should support HCD approaches that⏎include intentional human‑to‑human engagement as a core component. Direct observation of⏎clinicians who are using AI tools, paired with one‑on‑one interviews and small-group listening⏎sessions, provides critical insight into how and why clinicians rely (or not) on these systems in real⏎workflows. These interactions uncover tacit knowledge, context-specific decision cues, and early⏎signs of misunderstanding or misuse that technical evaluations cannot detect. Just as importantly,⏎involving clinicians in this way builds trust and accelerates adoption: they see their concerns⏎reflected in design decisions, understand how their input influences implementation, and⏎experience the tools as being shaped with them rather than delivered to them. By supporting HCD⏎methods that prioritize these human touchpoints, HHS can help ensure clinical AI tools are⏎implemented responsibly, safely, and in ways that genuinely support the clinicians who use them.⏎HHS Support for Private Sector Activities (Q. #5)⏎HHS can support private sector activities like accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing,⏎and credentialing for clinical AI by adopting “tried-and-true” federal playbooks, such as:⏎1. Risk-based premarket review and lifecycle oversight (FDA).⏎2. Reusable third-party assurance that agencies can rely on (FedRAMP).⏎3. Scaled compliance pathways that mix self-attestation with independent certification for⏎higher-risk products (FCC).⏎4. Rapid adoption enabled by clear flexibilities, aligned payment, and temporary enforcement⏎discretion (COVID-era telehealth).⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 6⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎The common approach across these models is that government sets clear, testable expectations⏎and then recognizes independent private-sector assurance artifacts, allowing providers and⏎innovators to scale without every payer or program creating a unique and independent review.⏎HHS can leverage all four.⏎First, HHS can mirror the FDA approach by defining risk tiers and evidence expectations for⏎clinical AI and then formally recognizing qualified private sector entities that generate⏎standardized “assurance packages”—such as testing results, validation protocols, usability⏎evidence, and monitoring plans—that HHS programs can trust. FDA already operates multiple,⏎well-understood pathways for medical devices grounded in the FD&C Act and implemented⏎through established review practice. Examples include 510(k) clearance, De Novo classification⏎for novel low–moderate risk devices, and Premarket Approval (PMA) for Class III devices. HHS⏎could apply the same logic to clinical AI that sits outside traditional device boundaries by⏎publishing standardized submission templates that cover items such as intended use,⏎training/validation data provenance, performance limits, human oversight, post-deployment⏎monitoring, etc. and then recognizing third-party testing/certification programs that produce⏎evidence in those formats. This would create a predictable, scalable path for “trusted AI”⏎eligibility in Medicare and Medicaid program use cases.⏎Second, HHS can emulate FedRAMP by establishing an “assess once, reuse many times” model⏎for clinical AI assurance, where private assessors perform independent evaluations and HHS can⏎reuse the results across programs. FedRAMP was established by OMB in 2011 and later codified⏎in law through the FedRAMP provisions enacted in Public Law 117-263 (NDAA FY2023),⏎Section 5921. FedRAMP capitalizes on scalable private sector assurance through Third-Party⏎Assessment Organizations (3PAOs) that are independently assessed by the American⏎Association for Laboratory Accreditation (A2LA). HHS can take the same approach for clinical⏎AI by establishing a reusable authorization construct for AI used in clinical care that ensures⏎standards regarding security, privacy, auditability, monitoring, and performance are met. HHS⏎can then recognize accredited “clinical AI assessment organizations” and allow providers/payers⏎to rely on those reusable authorizations to encourage adoption and use at scale.⏎Third, HHS can borrow from the FCC’s equipment authorization framework by creating two⏎compliance tracks that support innovation while protecting patients: a lower-friction supplier⏎attestation path for low-risk tools and a higher-rigor independent certification path for higher-⏎impact clinical uses. FCC rules in 47 CFR part 2, subpart J, explicitly contemplate equipment⏎being authorized under Supplier’s Declaration of Conformity or receiving a grant of certification⏎(including via Telecommunication Certification Bodies), and these authorities are tied to statutory⏎foundations including 47 U.S.C. § 302a. HHS could employ a similar concept, permitting⏎streamlined attestation for limited-scope administrative AI (with audit rights and disclosures),⏎while requiring independent certification (and potentially accredited labs) for AI that materially⏎influences diagnosis, triage, treatment, or autonomous clinical actions. Adopting this approach⏎would encourage consistency in labels, logs, and change-management disclosures so that certified⏎tools could be regularly evaluated to ensure they remain trustworthy as they evolve.⏎Fourth, HHS can apply the COVID-era telehealth lesson: remove adoption blockers quickly,⏎align payment, and clarify compliance expectations while using time-bound flexibilities and⏎explicit evaluation to decide what becomes permanent. The Secretary’s emergency waiver⏎authority under Social Security Act § 1135 (42 U.S.C. § 1320b–5) is designed to allow⏎temporary waivers/modifications during emergencies. During COVID-19, CMS used this⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 7⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎posture to expand telehealth flexibility, noting that authority under the CARES Act broadened⏎§1135 waiver authority and enabled additional telehealth waivers, including waivers tied to⏎Medicare telehealth requirements and 42 CFR § 410.78 provisions. In parallel, HHS OCR issued⏎the Notification of Enforcement Discretion for Telehealth Remote Communications under⏎HIPAA during the nationwide public health emergency, reducing compliance friction for good-⏎faith telehealth. HHS can adopt the same pattern for clinical AI by creating time-limited “AI⏎adoption pilots” with clear guardrails to define what is allowed, aligned reimbursement and⏎reporting to specify what is paid and measured, and targeted compliance flexibilities as and⏎where appropriate to accelerate safe use. HHS could then use the collected evidence from these⏎pilots to determine what aspects to institutionalize as durable accreditation/certification⏎expectations.⏎Widening Opportunities through Interoperability (Q. #8)⏎Enhanced interoperability will widen market opportunities, advance clinical research, and⏎accelerate the development and employment of AI for clinical care. AI use in healthcare will⏎only be as effective as the consistency, quality, and portability of the data AI systems rely upon.⏎Today, clinical data remains fragmented across EHR platforms, public health systems, research⏎environments, claims systems, and social service data repositories, limiting the ability to create⏎scalable, generalizable AI solutions that can operate reliably across populations and care settings.⏎Additionally, data is often not “AI ready,” resulting unreliable, unsafe, biased, and non-portable⏎outputs that undermine clinical trust and regulatory readiness.⏎Data Types⏎The greatest impact from interoperability will come from improving the clinical and contextual⏎data used by AI systems and solutions. This includes EHR data (diagnosis, medications, lab⏎results, etc.), imaging and diagnostics (DICOM) data, claims and utilization data, public health⏎and surveillance data, genomic data, data from community resources, and data from personal⏎devices. Incorporating unstructured clinical narratives and administrative metadata into available⏎datasets will further strengthen AI’s ability to draw valuable inferences and support clinical care⏎delivery. These data sources should be longitudinally linked so that AI can support predictive⏎modeling, clinical decision support, assessments of population health, early interventions, and⏎research at scale while enabling multimodal benchmarking and reproducible evaluations across⏎representative cohorts.⏎Data Standards⏎Standards are essential for accelerating adoption and use of AI. We recommend consistent, broad⏎adoption of HL7, FHIR, USCDI, DICOM, LOINC, and SNOMED standards to allow AI⏎developers to build solutions that can operate across health systems and platforms without⏎extensive data transformation. API-driven architectures will further allow AI tools to be⏎embedded directly into clinical workflows rather than serving as standalone applications. These⏎standards allow AI solution providers to build solutions once and deploy them across multiple⏎EHRs, provider networks, research institutions, and public health agencies without costly data⏎transformation for each environment. Effective use of standards will also reduce the need for⏎custom integrations that would otherwise increase cost, limit competition, and slow innovation.⏎By promoting standards in this way, HHS can lower barriers to entry for innovators and expand⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 8⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎the market for AI-enabled clinical tools that can be evaluated, procured, and implemented⏎modularly to develop best-of-breed solutions.⏎Market Impact⏎Because AI effectiveness, safety, and reliability depend directly on the quality and structure of⏎datasets used for solution development, validation, and deployment, interoperable data⏎environments enable clinicians and researchers to confidently use AI tools in diagnostics,⏎pediatric oncology, chronic disease research, and other high‑stakes areas. When data is poorly⏎organized, inconsistent, or incomplete, predictions become less accurate and clinical⏎decision‑making suffers. Interoperable systems directly address this by allowing normalization⏎and semantic alignment across data sources, thereby maintaining strong data lineage and⏎provenance and supporting transparent documentation. Interoperability also strengthens⏎governance by enabling privacy‑preserving de‑identification, bias detection across diverse⏎populations, and responsible data sharing. These elements streamline multi‑omic and clinical⏎data integration, support reproducible science, and ultimately expand opportunities for⏎cross‑institution research. Our recommended approach aligns with the HHS AI Strategy,⏎especially the idea of cross-cutting enablers, as it will encourage clinical deployment of AI in⏎ways that are aligned with federal strategies to reduce duplicative systems and strengthen⏎enterprise-wide coordination.⏎Benchmarking⏎Enhanced interoperability will enable the creation and use of “gold standard” benchmark datasets⏎that improve model validation across representative cohorts and care settings. We recommend⏎the government either work to build such benchmark datasets or sponsor their development by⏎establishing standards for development and evaluation of AI solutions in clinical care settings.⏎HHS can ensure these datasets promote interoperability by requiring standardized structures,⏎robust metadata, and privacy-preserving pipelines. This will ensure interoperability through by⏎reducing model fragmentation and will prevent AI systems from being trained on narrow⏎datasets that limit generalizability or introduce undesirable biases. By ensuring the use of⏎consistent and validated datasets, HHS can increase the market viability for AI tools, accelerate⏎evaluation and regulatory readiness, and strengthen confidence in real-world clinical use.⏎Specific Areas of AI Research to Prioritize (Q. #10)⏎AI’s true value will only be appreciated when it effectively augments human providers within⏎clinical workflows. Today, most healthcare AI research focuses on algorithm accuracy in⏎isolation, but ignores user experience, workflow integration, and human factors. This is⏎unfortunate as these aspects are crucial for increased adoption and safe use. Accordingly, HHS⏎should prioritize R&D that puts human users at the center of AI design and evaluation by⏎embracing HCD. Two specific research areas stand out.⏎1. Human-AI Interaction. Focusing on human-AI interaction will address one of the greatest⏎obstacles to adoption: clinician buy-in and workflow fit. If AI tools and solutions are⏎intuitive, reduce human effort, and demonstrably enhance care delivery, providers are far⏎more likely to embrace them. Conversely, even a highly accurate algorithm will fail in⏎practice if it is difficult to use or disrupts proven clinical routines. By investing in this⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 9⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎research, HHS would accelerate the transition of AI from an impressive technology to a core⏎component of trusted approaches that improve productivity and patient outcomes.⏎2. Trustworthy AI. Prioritizing Trustworthy AI research will help encourage adoption by⏎reducing skepticism. Improved approaches to trustworthiness will provide the evidence,⏎methods, and tools to create confidence that AI systems are safe, secure, and accountable.⏎That will accelerate adoption by addressing doubt head-on. Clinicians will be more likely to⏎trust AI recommendations if they know the system has been stress-tested for safety and bias,⏎can explain itself, and is backed by governance that ensures issues are caught and addressed.⏎Patients will be more receptive to AI-driven care if there’s transparency and unbiased output.⏎By investing in trustworthy AI R&D, HHS will foster the foundational “social license” for⏎AI in healthcare—building public trust and providing the guardrails so innovation can⏎proceed more rapidly without undue risk.⏎Ultimately, HHS should prioritize research investments that are designed to have a direct,⏎positive impact on patients. Driving better patient outcomes is a clear goal. But we also⏎recommend funding research designed to improve patient experience. Our nation's healthcare⏎is the best in the world when we balance science with responsive, personalized care and clear,⏎compassionate communication. Below is a broader summary of research areas worth further⏎investment, aligned to patient experience.⏎Research Priorities for Clinical AI Adoption⏎Positive Impact on Patient⏎Research Area and Relevance Key Focus / References⏎Experience⏎Human-AI Interaction • Develop frameworks to • More time with patients (less⏎Ensure AI tools fit into clinical measure & improve real- time entering data)⏎workflows and enhance (not hinder) world human-AI collaboration • Clearer communication and⏎healthcare professionals’ performance in healthcare explanations⏎and acceptance. • Use HCD to align AI with⏎• Reduced frustration from⏎user needs; prevent⏎workflow delays or errors⏎automation bias and user⏎• Greater confidence of being⏎frustration⏎heard and understood⏎Trustworthy AI (Safety, Security, • Focus on reliability • Confidence in safety and⏎and Reliability) (robustness, adversarial fairness of care⏎Builds a foundation of trust necessary defense), explainability, bias • Transparent, understandable AI⏎for widespread adoption. If AI mitigation, and post- decisions⏎systems are demonstrably safe, secure, deployment monitoring⏎• Reduced risk of harm or⏎transparent, and accountable, • Align with NIST AI RMF and⏎misdiagnosis⏎stakeholders will be confident using FUTURE-AI principles⏎them in care. • Equitable treatment across⏎populations⏎Reinforcement Learning (RL) and • Use RL to optimize sequential • More personalized, responsive⏎Adaptive AI decisions and personalize care care⏎Enables AI to continuously improve • Develop safe RL techniques • Better long-term outcomes⏎and personalize in complex clinical (offline training, human-in- through adaptive treatment⏎decision scenarios (e.g., dynamic loop oversight)⏎• Reduced trial-and-error in care⏎treatment planning), potentially⏎plans⏎boosting outcomes beyond static⏎protocols.⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 10⏎⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care February 20th, 2026⏎Positive Impact on Patient⏎Research Area and Relevance Key Focus / References⏎Experience⏎Collaborative & Multi-Agent • Study human-AI teaming and • Seamless, coordinated care⏎Systems multi-AI collaboration across providers⏎Ensures AI can operate in the complex • Explore multi-modal AI for • Fewer gaps or redundancies in⏎multi-stakeholder environment of richer, more robust insights care⏎healthcare. Research here yields AI⏎• Apply game theory to multi- • Holistic decisions that consider⏎that complements team-based care and⏎stakeholder decision-making the full patient context⏎coordinates decisions across various⏎systems and actors.⏎AI Security and Privacy • Defend against adversarial • Confidence that personal data⏎Protects the integrity of AI tools and attacks is secure⏎patient data, thereby safeguarding • Invest in privacy-preserving • Protection from malicious or⏎trust. AI (federated learning, faulty AI behavior⏎differential privacy)⏎• Willingness to engage with⏎digital tools⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. 11⏎⏎x.com/ICF⏎linkedin.com/company/icf-international⏎facebook.com/ThisIsICF⏎icf.com #thisisicf⏎About ICF⏎ICF (NASDAQ:ICFI) is a global consulting services company with approximately 9,000 full-time and part-time employees, but⏎we are not your typical consultants. At ICF, business analysts and policy specialists work together with digital strategists, data⏎scientists and creatives. We combine unmatched industry expertise with cutting-edge engagement capabilities to help⏎organizations solve their most complex challenges. Since 1969, public and private sector clients have worked with ICF to⏎navigate change and shape the future.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0412♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary of Health and Human Services⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Assistant Secretary Thomas Keane, M.D.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP), Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street, SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy and Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The Association for Behavioral Health and Wellness (ABHW) appreciates the opportunity to⏎submit our recommendations to the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎and the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy /National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC) Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎ABHW is the national voice for payers managing behavioral health insurance benefits. Our⏎member companies provide coverage to 200 million people in both the public and private⏎sectors to treat mental health (MH), substance use disorders (SUDs), and other behaviors that⏎impact health and wellness. Our organization aims to increase access, drive integration,⏎support prevention, raise awareness, reduce stigma, and advance evidence-based treatment⏎and quality outcomes. Furthermore, our policy work aims to ensure that physical and⏎behavioral health care is integrated and coordinated. ABHW is committed to ensuring better⏎outcomes for whole-person care for all individuals and communities. We believe access to⏎comprehensive, evidence-based MH and SUD services is critical to enhancing patients’ health⏎and overall well-being.⏎The rapid evolution of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care presents both promising⏎clinical opportunities and complex challenges for patients, providers, and health plans.⏎ABHW’s responses to the questions below reflect the distinct clinical and operational⏎considerations relevant to behavioral health care.⏎1⏎⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎ABHW has identified several barriers to AI innovation and adoption that underscore the need⏎for regulatory alignment, clear accountability frameworks, provider education, and the creation⏎of policies that reflect the distinct clinical, operational, and privacy considerations in⏎behavioral health care.⏎First, data limitations remain a foundational barrier. Many AI models are trained on⏎incomplete or non-representative datasets that underrepresent behavioral health conditions⏎and comorbid physical health needs. As a result, tools that perform well in controlled settings⏎often do not translate effectively to real-world behavioral health populations, raising concerns⏎about reliability and safety.⏎Closely related is the need for clear, transparent communication with patients about AI data.⏎Such communication should be implemented in a manner proportionate to the risk and⏎aligned with existing federal and state laws, as well as established medical practice standards.⏎For higher risk AI, patients must understand, where clinically and operationally appropriate,⏎how and when their data is used, how AI informs care decisions, and what role human⏎clinicians play in the clinical process. Any disclosure obligations should be tailored to the level⏎of risk presented by the AI application and avoid duplicative or conflicting requirements where⏎existing legal frameworks already govern patient communications. Lack of transparency⏎undermines trust and may slow adoption, particularly in behavioral health, where privacy and⏎stigma are especially sensitive.⏎Additionally, providers, particularly small and independent practices, may lack the resources⏎to independently evaluate AI tools for their use. AI literacy remains uneven, and⏎implementation support is limited, creating a need for federally supported education and⏎technical assistance resources. HHS can play an important role by developing provider-facing⏎best practices and educational tools to help support responsible adoption of AI, while helping⏎providers understand how these technologies can be integrated into their clinical workflows in⏎compliance with existing laws and standards.⏎While the U.S. Food and Drug Administration’s (FDA) and Centers for Medicare and Medicaid⏎Services (CMS) serve distinct and appropriate roles, the absence of clear, coordinated guidance⏎on how AI-enabled tools can be evaluated, implemented, and paid for across different care⏎settings creates challenges for developers, providers, and health plans seeking to responsibly⏎deploy these technologies.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment⏎your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific⏎changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎ABHW recommends that HHS work with the FDA to create a risk-based framework for AI⏎oversight that distinguishes among applications based on their impact on patient safety and⏎2⏎⏎clinical decision-making. Higher-risk tools, such as those that make treatment⏎recommendations or crisis intervention, should be subject to stronger validation, transparency,⏎and monitoring requirements, while lower-risk tools that support administrative or clinical⏎workflow functions should face more flexible oversight. This distinction is particularly⏎important in behavioral health, where data sensitivity and clinical variability heighten the⏎consequences of AI-related errors.⏎Clear distinctions are needed between general-purpose AI-enabled mental health applications⏎and clinically validated digital behavioral health technologies, such as Digital Mental Health⏎Therapeutics (DMHTs), that are intended to diagnose and treat conditions and are typically⏎evidence-based, and subject to FDA oversight if they meet the definition of a medical device.⏎Regulatory clarity should be grounded in intended use, risk level, and clinical claims to ensure⏎appropriate oversight. Some available AI-based digital behavioral health solutions have limited⏎evidence supporting their effectiveness and medical necessity. This could be due to shorter or⏎smaller-scale studies lacking control groups, or control groups that do not demonstrate⏎comparable improvement. Studies may also be subject to selection bias, as participants with⏎specific circumstances that enabled their access to digital therapeutics may contribute to the⏎marked improvements. For MH apps, demonstrating clinical effectiveness is complex because⏎outcomes are subjective and influenced by engagement, which can fluctuate.⏎ABHW also encourages HHS to collaborate with the FDA to strengthen post-market⏎surveillance of AI-enabled clinical tools. Ongoing, real-world evaluation is essential to assess⏎effectiveness over time, particularly given common patient dropout, nonadherence, and⏎frequent software updates that are not systematically reviewed post-authorization but may⏎raise safety and performance concerns.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance⏎and accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification,⏎privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and⏎what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎ABHW supports the implementation of accredited, consensus-driven standards that promote⏎broad stakeholder engagement and alignment with policy objectives, including the NIST⏎Artificial Intelligence Risk Management Framework (AI RMF) and standards developed⏎through ANSI-accredited standards development organizations (SDOs). Additionally, while the⏎NIST AI Risk Management Framework provides a strong qualitative foundation, organizations⏎need practical, automated tools to implement and monitor the framework in real time.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow⏎and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS⏎further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most⏎impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎3⏎⏎Mental health (MH) care is highly nuanced and influenced by tone, context, and the⏎therapeutic relationship is not consistently captured by existing evaluation frameworks.⏎Evaluation tools need to be able to capture the complexity of clinical judgment and the wide⏎variability in patient presentation. ABHW recognizes the need for clear, clinically grounded⏎evaluation methods to assess non-medical AI-enabled chatbots and large language models⏎(LLMs) used to support MH care. As a result, robust clinical oversight, meaningful human-in-⏎the-loop requirements, and additional validation are necessary before such tools can be⏎appropriately relied upon in practice.⏎ABHW encourages HHS to support the development of consensus-based standards, led by⏎accredited standards organizations, to ensure alignment with best practices as technologies⏎evolve. Any framework should be supported by peer-reviewed, published evidence⏎demonstrating reliability and safety across diverse populations and use cases.⏎For high-risk MH use cases, pre-deployment evaluation should include clinical simulation⏎testing, bias and robustness testing across diverse behavioral health populations, and⏎assessment of guardrails for high-risk scenarios such as suicide risk, SUD treatment, and crisis⏎escalation. Post-deployment evaluation should focus on real-world monitoring for safety⏎signals, response accuracy and consistency, appropriate escalation to human care, and patient⏎experience over time. Human-centered evaluation should also examine integration into clinical⏎workflows and transparency around a chatbot’s role and limitations.⏎HHS could further support these efforts through targeted grants and applied research⏎partnerships to develop shared evaluation metrics, testing protocols, and best practices for MH⏎chatbots, enabling responsible innovation while protecting patient privacy and safety.⏎5. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel⏎AI tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes,⏎give new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎One of our member health plans has leveraged AI-enabled tools to increase its inbound⏎correspondence processing capacity by 400 percent, while achieving 96 percent accuracy. As a⏎result, communications that previously required up to six days to be appropriately routed are⏎now directed to the correct team within hours. This improvement has enhanced operational⏎efficiency and significantly improved the experience of members, providers, and internal staff⏎alike.⏎6. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate⏎the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research into how AI-enabled tools can support greater efficiency,⏎consistency, and clinical appropriateness while helping manage costs across the health care⏎system.⏎In behavioral health, where timely access to treatment is critical, AI has the potential to assist⏎with documentation review and summaries, identification of requests that meet established⏎4⏎⏎criteria, and prioritization of cases for expedited review, under appropriate human oversight.⏎When thoughtfully designed and governed, such tools could help reduce administrative burden⏎and clinician burnout while promoting more consistent application of evidence-based⏎standards. Any high-risk use of AI should complement clinician judgment, as well as⏎established laws, frameworks, and consumer protections safeguards.⏎Conclusions⏎Accelerating the responsible adoption of AI in behavioral health clinical care will require a⏎balanced approach that promotes innovation while maintaining strong patient protection.⏎HHS should rely on existing safeguards, such as privacy protections under HIPAA and 42 CFR⏎Part 2 (Part 2), established clinical standards of care, and existing FDA oversight rather than⏎layering on duplicative or unnecessary requirements.⏎ABHW is committed to engaging with ASTP/ONC and other partners to identify innovative⏎opportunities to improve behavioral health access for all individuals. If you have questions,⏎please contact Kathryn Cohen, Senior Director of Regulatory Affairs, at cohen@abhw.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Debbie Witchey, MHA⏎President and CEO⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0413♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Christopher♔Young♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My doctor, Dr. Harlan Briggs, retired in March of 2023 after 31 years of practice in Caruthersville. He told my wife Carol straight out that the paperwork had broken him. Not the patients, not the long hours. The paperwork. He spent more time on prior authorization requests and documentation requirements than he did actually seeing people, and in a county like Pemiscot where there aren't exactly a lot of options for primary care, losing him was a real blow to a lot of families including ours.⏎⏎So when I see HHS asking about accelerating AI adoption in clinical care, my first thought is -- okay, but is any of this going to help the physician who's drowning in administrative burden, or is it just going to add one more system they have to learn and document in and justify their decisions to?⏎⏎Because here's what I've seen with my own eyes. Carol's insurance company was already using some kind of automated system to review her methotrexate prescription, and that system flagged it as not medically necessary. No human being made that call as far as we could ever tell. We were never able to speak to whoever or whatever decided that. Her rheumatologist told us the algorithm had a known problem with rural zip codes, just flat out denying at higher rates, and we spent over a year fighting that decision. A year. For a medication she'd been stable on.⏎⏎That's AI in healthcare right now. That's where we actually are.⏎⏎So I guess what I want to say to HHS is -- before you talk about accelerating anything, can you tell me what you're doing to make sure the doctors who are still out there have a reason to stay? Dr. Briggs didn't leave because of technology. He left because nobody simplified the regulatory requirements piling up on his practice. Every year it was something new to comply with, some new reporting thing, some new certification requirement. He had a staff of three people running a rural family practice and the compliance load was just not manageable anymore.⏎⏎I'm not against technology in medicine. I'm genuinely not. If AI can help a doctor catch something early or reduce the time they spend typing notes, that seems fine to me. But I've seen what happens when the profit motive gets involved and there's no human accountable for the decision. Carol's situation wasn't an accident, it was a cost-saving tool dressed up as a clinical judgment. And I don't know enough about how these systems get approved or regulated to know if HHS has any real authority to stop that from happening more, but somebody needs to.⏎⏎I keep thinking about the fact that Pemiscot County has something like one primary care physician per several thousand residents now. That's the actual problem. AI isn't going to fix that if the underlying reason doctors aren't practicing there is because the administrative load of doing so has become unbearable. I'd honestly rather see HHS spend some of this energy figuring out how to reduce documentation requirements for physicians in rural underserved areas before♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0414♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Carol♔King♔♔Ohio♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My patient nearly died because an algorithm didn't know enough about people who look like him.⏎⏎He's a 47-year-old Black man, comes in with fatigue, and three different screening tools kept flagging him as low-priority. Anxiety, basically. I sat with him for twenty minutes asking questions the intake forms don't have room for, and I caught early heart failure. I found out afterward that the tool being used had been trained on data that significantly underrepresented Black male patients with his cardiovascular profile. That's not a minor software bug. That's a structural problem baked into the model itself, and no one in the room except me was going to catch it.⏎⏎So when I read that HHS wants to accelerate AI adoption in clinical settings, my first thought is: accelerate toward what, exactly?⏎⏎Because the thing I keep running into in my work isn't a shortage of AI tools. It's a shortage of accountability when those tools get it wrong. And right now the place where that's most visible and most harmful is insurance coverage denials. I've watched patients get denied medically necessary care through automated processes where I genuinely cannot tell if a human being ever looked at the file. A colleague of mine had a patient in her 70s denied a follow-up cardiology referral four times in 2023, and each denial letter was nearly identical, word for word. That's not human review. That's a queue.⏎⏎HHS has authority over a lot of these payers, whether through Medicare Advantage oversight or Medicaid managed care contracts. The department could require, right now, that every coverage denial be reviewed and signed off by an actual licensed clinician who is accountable for that decision. Not "reviewed by our team," not "consistent with clinical guidelines" — a name, a credential, a person. I don't understand why that isn't already the standard. I genuinely don't.⏎⏎What bothers me about this RFI is the framing. The question seems to be "how do we accelerate adoption" rather than "how do we make sure adoption doesn't hurt people who are already vulnerable." Those are different questions and I think HHS knows that. The patients getting churned through automated denial systems are mostly elderly, disabled, low-income, non-English speaking. They don't have the resources to appeal four times. They don't know they can appeal. And the system, as currently designed, benefits from them not knowing.⏎⏎I'm not anti-technology. I use clinical decision support tools every shift and some of them are genuinely helpful. But there's a difference between a tool that helps me do my job better and a tool that replaces the judgment I'm supposed to be exercising, or the judgment a medical director at an insurance company is supposed to be exercising.⏎⏎The acceleration pressure also worries me for a reason I haven't seen discussed much, which is what it does to nurses and other frontline staff. When there's an AI recommendation in the chart, there's pressure, subtle but real, to follow it. Disagreeing with the algorithm takes time and documentation and sometimes an argument with a supervisor. Over time that changes how people practice. It changes what questions get asked. I don't know how you measure that or regulate against it but I think it's real and I think it's already happening.⏎⏎I would really want HHS to focus on the insurance denial piece specifically before pushing faster adoption elsewhere. At minimum there should be a requirement that any automated or AI-assisted denial process include a disclosed error rate, broken down by patient demographic, updated at least♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0415♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rubrum Advising, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rubrum Advising♔♔♔♔♔♔Request for Information⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Rubrum Advising appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) Request for Information on “Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care”. Rubrum Advising, co‑founded by Lee A. Fleisher,⏎MD, ML, and Matthew A. Fleisher, JD, MPH, advises clients on the adoption, diffusion, and⏎reimbursement of innovative healthcare solutions and products to improve patient outcomes⏎and reduce total costs of care within the context of regulation, safety, quality, and clinical⏎standards.⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎i. Fragmented, low-quality data that make it hard to build safe models that are applicable to a⏎wide range of populations and situations.⏎a. There is growing concern that non-representative data lead to the creation of inaccurate⏎algorithms, resulting in poorer performance in certain patient groups. To ensure⏎algorithmic safety, datasets need to be appropriately representative of their intended⏎use population.1⏎b. Since nationwide exchange of health information remains fragmented, developers and⏎health systems struggle to assemble sufficiently representative, longitudinal datasets for⏎training and external validation. Even with exchange networks in place, important⏎clinical information often remains in clinician notes or non-standardized fields, making it⏎difficult to extract relevant data. Moreover, companies may be reluctant to share the⏎data sets used to train an AI algorithm, given concerns that competitors could reverse⏎engineer the data to create their own tools.2 The use of AI tools requires a high level of⏎specificity that has not yet been fully reconciled with the realities of current⏎documentation and record-keeping practices.⏎c. As private entities increasingly aggregate, analyze, and sell large-scale clinical datasets,⏎the expansion of commercial data markets introduces important policy considerations⏎related to transparency, representativeness, and access. In many cases, there is limited⏎visibility into how these datasets are constructed, validated, and maintained, which⏎creates challenges for both regulators and developers seeking to assess data quality,⏎completeness, and potential bias. The growing commercialization of clinical data also⏎raises fundamental questions regarding data stewardship and ownership, including⏎whether proprietary rights should rest with the organizations that collect, process, and⏎1 Kuhlman, C., Jackson, L. & Chunara, R. No computation without representation: avoiding data and algorithm⏎biases through diversity. In Proc. 26th ACM SIGKDD International Conference on Knowledge Discovery & Data⏎Mining (KDD '20) 3593 (ACM, 2020); 10.1145/3394486.3411074⏎2 Coleman, K. (2024). Lowering health care costs through AI: The possibilities and barriers. Paragon Health⏎Institute.⏎⏎distribute these datasets or with the patients whose information forms the underlying⏎data. Clarifying these governance frameworks is critical to ensuring appropriate patient⏎protections, promoting responsible innovation, and aligning data stewardship practices⏎with evolving standards for privacy, consent, and data sharing in healthcare research and⏎product development.⏎ii. Clinician and patient skepticism of “black-box” tools⏎a. The “black box” nature of deep neural networks and other advanced algorithms can⏎make it nearly impossible for clinicians to interpret how the system arrives at specific⏎recommendations.3 While many studies report gains in AI-based diagnostic efficiency⏎and resource allocation, accountability and liability remain pervasive concerns,⏎especially when models lack clear rationale or interpretability.⏎b. An AMA survey from November 2024 found that although physician sentiment and use⏎of AI is trending positive, physicians continue to express concerns about the design of⏎health AI tools and the potential for flawed AI-enabled tools to put privacy at risk,⏎integrate poorly with EHR systems, offer incorrect conclusions or recommendations, and⏎introduce new liability concerns. Nearly half of physicians surveyed also ranked⏎increased oversight as the number one regulatory action that needed to happen to⏎increase their trust in adopting AI tools.4⏎1. Under current malpractice law, liability is determined based on the “reasonable⏎physician under similar circumstances” standard. Regardless of whether AI was⏎used, courts will judge the conduct of the physician. As of now, there is no doctrine⏎assigning shared responsibility to AI systems, even when their recommendations⏎directly influence patient care.5⏎c. Recent surveys suggest that many patients lack confidence in health care systems’ ability⏎to use AI responsibly and protect them from AI-related harms. One study found that⏎most respondents reported low trust in their health care system to use AI responsibly⏎(65.8%) and low trust that their health care system would make sure an AI tool would⏎not harm them (57.7%).6 Patients also generally prefer explainable AI, even if that means⏎a trade-off in accuracy compared with black-box models.7⏎d. Given these ongoing concerns about accountability, liability, and trust, strengthening⏎instructions-for-use (IFU) and implementation education can also play an important role⏎3 Saoudi, C. E. N., Uren, V., & Jariwala, S. (2025). Evaluating accountability, transparency, and bias in AI-assisted⏎healthcare decision-making: A qualitative study of healthcare professionals’ perspectives in the UK. BMC Medical⏎Ethics, 26, 89. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12910-025-01243-z⏎4 American Medical Association. (2025). Physician sentiments around the use of AI in health care: Motivations,⏎opportunities, risks, and use cases – Shifts from 2023 to 2024. American Medical Association.⏎5 Mello, M. M., & Studdert, D. M. (2008). Professional Liability: Malpractice and Other Causes of Action. The New⏎England Journal of Medicine, 359(19), 1936–1942; https://carey.jhu.edu/articles/fault-lines-health-care-ai-part-⏎two-whos-responsible-when-ai-gets-it-wrong⏎6 Nong, P., & Platt, J. (2025). Patients’ trust in health systems to use artificial intelligence. JAMA Network Open,⏎8(2), e2460628. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.60628⏎7Nair, M., Svedberg, P., Larsson, I., & Nygren, J. M. (2024). A comprehensive overview of barriers and strategies for⏎AI implementation in healthcare: Mixed-method design. PLOS ONE, 19(8),⏎e0305949. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0305949.⏎2⏎⏎in managing risk for both clinicians and developers. Clear, detailed IFUs that specify⏎intended use, patient populations, performance limitations, and required clinician⏎oversight steps can help encourage responsible use of AI. By setting transparent⏎expectations for how and when an AI system should be used, and by establishing clear⏎physician training protocols, developers can mitigate risks associated with common⏎drivers of adverse events and malpractice claims.⏎1. One potential approach is to ensure robust instructions for use so that clinicians⏎know how, and for whom, to apply or trust AI tools in the real world. This idea is⏎outlined in a recent publication by Kev Coleman at the Paragon Health Institute,⏎where he argues that, instead of (or in addition to) treating every AI model as a⏎regulated medical device, regulators can sometimes rely on robust instructions for⏎use so clinicians understand the tool’s intended population, setting, and limits.8⏎That includes being explicit about when not to use or trust an algorithm, and what⏎kind of clinical oversight is required in real‑world deployment.⏎e. As federal and state entities evaluate and regulate clinical AI tools, it is essential to⏎establish clear and operational distinctions between tools that incorporate a physician in⏎the loop and those that function with minimal or no direct clinical oversight. Tools that⏎require an actively involved clinician embed professional accountability and real-time⏎risk mitigation, which may justify more flexible evidentiary and monitoring approaches,⏎while fully autonomous or near-autonomous tools that drive high-stakes clinical⏎decisions should be subject to more stringent requirements related to validation,⏎ongoing performance monitoring, and clear lines of responsibility. High-stakes clinical⏎decisions should be subject to more stringent requirements related to validation,⏎ongoing performance monitoring, and clear lines of responsibility.⏎iii. Unclear pathways for CMS coverage⏎a. MedPAC has noted that software technologies are frequently bundled into existing⏎payments and that payments for SaaS (software as a service) have to meet specific⏎conditions (e.g. is FDA-approved, fits into a Medicare benefit category, is reasonable and⏎necessary) for reimbursement. This can make it challenging for developers to secure⏎predictable reimbursement, especially when they must also negotiate separately with⏎commercial insurers and creates particular challenges for tools that primarily deliver⏎general risk reduction, utilization avoidance, or workflow efficiencies rather than a⏎discrete billable service.9⏎b. These challenges are compounded by the central role of the AMA in maintaining CPT⏎codes, which remain the primary mechanism for payment under Medicare and most⏎commercial plans. Many AI-enabled services lack dedicated CPT codes or fit poorly⏎within existing categories, and even when new codes are established, they may not be⏎tied to a clear Medicare benefit category or coverage pathway, limiting providers’ ability⏎8 Coleman, K. (2024). Lowering health care costs through AI: The possibilities and barriers. Paragon Health⏎Institute.⏎9 Zabinski, D., Druckman, J., Gerhardt, G., & Ray, N. (2025, April 10). Paying for software technologies in Medicare⏎[Presentation]. Medicare Payment Advisory Commission. https://www.medpac.gov/wp-⏎content/uploads/2025/04/Tab-G-Saas-April-2025.pdf.⏎3⏎⏎to bill for their use and making reimbursement unpredictable. In this context, alternative⏎payment models such as the CMS ACCESS model, which is designed to expand⏎technology-supported chronic care through outcome-aligned payments, offer a more⏎promising pathway for supporting AI-enabled tools that serve high-needs patients and⏎emphasize preventive care to avoid hospitalizations.⏎c. Because AI-enabled services must still map to traditional Medicare benefit categories,⏎they are frequently subject to bundled or ad hoc payment approaches that absorb their⏎value into broader procedures, leaving innovators without a clear, standalone⏎reimbursement pathway and contributing to FDA-authorized technologies failing to⏎reach patients at scale.10⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎Please provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎i. Create clear coverage for AI interventions that enable safer, more timely care rather than⏎increasing volume⏎a. CMS could prioritize payment models that reward reductions in avoidable admissions,⏎better chronic disease management, fewer diagnostic errors, and improved access to⏎care, such as proposed in the ACCESS model, which is explicitly designed to expand⏎technology-supported chronic care using an outcome-aligned payment approach.⏎b. CMS could also build a clearer reimbursement pathway for software/AI that results in⏎clinical improvements and should continue to publish instructions and guidelines for⏎differentiating AI as its own clinical service, as an administrative service, or as part of an⏎existing service.⏎ii. Physicians should not be paid more just because AI tools make them more efficient⏎a. In the CY 2026 Physician Fee Schedule (PFS) final rule, CMS finalized a -2.5% efficiency⏎adjustment to select services to better recognize that some services are likely to become⏎more efficient over time, as compared to time-based services like office visits or⏎behavioral health therapy. The adjustment targeted services like diagnostic imaging⏎interpretation, outpatient interventions, interventional pain management, and⏎orthopedic services, which have benefited from technological advancements and⏎standardized workflows that reduce time and resource use, without corresponding⏎payment adjustments.11⏎10 Seeley, A. (2025, May 23). How the Health Tech Investment Act could reshape Medicare reimbursement for⏎algorithm-based services. Morgan, Lewis & Bockius LLP. https://www.morganlewis.com/pubs/2025/05/how-the-⏎health-tech-investment-act-could-reshape-medicare-reimbursement-for-algorithm-based-services⏎11 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. (2025, November 5). CMS modernizes payment accuracy and⏎significantly cuts spending waste while improving chronic disease management for Medicare beneficiaries [Press⏎release]. https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/cms-modernizes-payment-accuracy-significantly-cuts-⏎spending-waste⏎4⏎⏎b. Similarly, where AI leads to greater efficiency or streamlines the provision of care, CMS⏎could treat this increased productivity as a deflationary effect and apply an efficiency⏎adjustment to services that AI is expected to streamline. Aligning payments with the⏎actual resources required to provide the AI-enabled services would ensure that⏎payments are not higher simply due to efficiency gains, while still allowing AI tools that⏎measurably improve outcomes or reduce downstream costs to be supported through⏎outcome-based or model-specific payments.⏎iii. Embed AI-related safety expectations in Medicare quality programs and innovation models⏎a. Existing and newly launched quality and payment programs, such as the Quality⏎Payment Program (QPP) and the Merit-based Incentive Payment System (MIPS), as well⏎as Innovation Center models like ACCESS, can incorporate AI governance where AI is⏎used for clinical decision-making, including regular oversight, risk assessment, and⏎training requirements tied to the safe use of AI tools.⏎iv. Use demonstration projects to test payment models that explicitly fund AI tools that prevent⏎hospitalizations or support high-risk, high-need patients at home⏎a. As stated above, the new CMS ACCESS model is designed to expand technology-⏎supported chronic care using an outcome-aligned payment approach and could tie in AI-⏎enabled tools that support care for high-needs patients and emphasize preventative care⏎to avoid hospitalizations. We strongly support both the construct of the ACCESS model⏎and the model’s use of well-defined endpoints and transparency of results. Future⏎demonstration projects should be structured so that payments reflect both outcomes⏎and appropriate efficiency gains, rather than creating stand-alone fees for AI that do not⏎change care delivery.⏎For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎i. Require strong privacy and governance standards for patient-generated data from consumer⏎devices used in clinical care, including safeguards against discriminatory secondary uses of⏎sensitive information.⏎a. Data from non-medical devices, such as wearables and fitness/wellness apps,⏎increasingly informs clinical decision-making when patients share this information with⏎their providers, yet it is often collected and stored outside HIPAA’s regulatory⏎framework. Once incorporated into clinical records, these data can influence care and⏎outcomes, making robust privacy and security safeguards essential. These systems⏎should require explicit, informed patient consent for any sale or secondary use of data⏎and provide enhanced protections and remedies when those protections are violated.⏎HHS should prioritize extending clear protections and governance standards to patient-⏎generated data from consumer devices to ensure that information used in clinical care is⏎subject to consistent privacy, security, and accountability requirements.⏎5⏎⏎For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎i. Shift from pre- to post-market surveillance⏎a. Pre-market evaluation has worked reasonably well for physical devices and traditional⏎software with stable, predictable outputs, but it is poorly matched to AI systems whose⏎behavior evolves with data, context, and implementation over time. Rather than relying⏎primarily on pre-deployment testing, HHS (through FDA and ONC) should support a risk-⏎based, post-market surveillance framework for clinical AI that emphasizes: mandatory⏎adverse event and error reporting, outcome monitoring in real-world use, structured⏎identification of implementation issues, and periodic performance re-evaluation. The⏎goal should be to avoid both under-regulation that allows mass injury and over-⏎regulation that delays access to beneficial technologies, while minimizing compliance⏎burden and total cost of ownership for providers and developers. This framework should⏎be targeted to higher-risk clinical use cases and AI systems whose design or training⏎makes output unpredictability likely, so that oversight resources are focused where⏎patient harm risk is highest.12⏎ii. Fund real-world evaluation via grants, cooperative agreements⏎a. HHS could use grants, cooperative agreements, and contracts to support public-private⏎evaluation partnerships, where developers receive de-identified post-market⏎performance data and providers can safely continue using AI tools in higher-risk⏎scenarios under structured monitoring arrangements.⏎b. HHS should also create a national, registry-style, federated post-market surveillance⏎network for clinical AI, modeled in part on the FDA Sentinel Initiative’s distribution data⏎infrastructure, to aggregate outcome data and support standardized reporting of errors,⏎adverse events, and key performance metrics. Such an aggregated outcome data registry⏎would enable early detection of safety issues across health systems and provide⏎feedback loops for regulators, developers, and providers, without requiring continuous,⏎centralized storage of identifiable patient data.⏎How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎I. HHS should foster independent, industry-led evaluation and benchmarking of clinical AI tools,⏎aligned with models like ACCESS and TEMPO, to support safe and effective innovation in⏎clinical care⏎12 Coleman, K., & Pencina, M. J. (2025). Targeted postmarket surveillance: The way toward responsible AI⏎innovation in health care. Paragon Health Institute.⏎6⏎⏎a. HHS can best support private sector activities by explicitly leveraging and extending⏎efforts such as the ACCESS and TEMPO models to create a predictable environment for⏎testing and deploying AI in clinical care. In addition to advancing outcomes-based,⏎technology-enabled care, HHS should encourage independent, industry-led evaluation⏎and benchmarking of AI tools to assess their safety and effectiveness in real-world⏎settings. HHS can further align data, reporting, and quality standards so that evidence⏎generated through these private testing initiatives can be relied upon for coverage,⏎payment, and oversight decisions. Finally, HHS should provide technical assistance and⏎targeted incentives for providers and developers who participate in rigorous, transparent⏎evaluation and benchmarking activities, thereby promoting responsible innovation⏎without relying on formal accreditation or certification schemes.⏎Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎I. Prioritize development and payment for AI tools that demonstrably change care delivery in⏎ways that improve outcomes and reduce downstream costs, rather than tools that solely⏎increase clinician efficiency⏎a. AI tools have met or exceeded performance and cost expectations primarily where they⏎have demonstrably reduced downstream utilization while maintaining or improving⏎outcomes, rather than merely increasing clinician efficiency. Examples include models⏎that prospectively identify patients at high risk for sepsis or avoidable readmission, tools⏎that reduce unnecessary imaging or procedures, and decision-support systems that⏎lower complication rates or emergency department revisits; in these cases, the value⏎proposition is clear because payers and providers can observe fewer admissions, repeat⏎tests, or adverse events, and thus lower the total cost of care. By contrast, tools that⏎mainly make clinicians faster or generate documentation more efficiently, without clear⏎evidence of changed care patterns or improved outcomes, have fallen short of⏎expectations from a coverage and payment perspective, because the financial return⏎largely accrues to efficiency gains that purchasers can already value without separate⏎reimbursement. Consistent with emerging payment principles for AI, the novel tools⏎with the greatest potential are those that can rigorously demonstrate that they change⏎clinical decisions and care pathways in ways that improve outcomes and reduce⏎downstream costs (for example, earlier targeted intervention in high-risk chronic⏎conditions, reduced low-value care, or fewer complications), and that can be⏎incorporated into payment models that reward measurable improvements in total cost⏎and quality rather than efficiency alone.⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎7⏎⏎I. Enhance interoperability for structured clinical data (LOINC, USCDI)⏎a. LOINC-coded results already underpin many digital quality measures and FHIR US Core⏎profiles (for example, the US Core Blood Pressure profile requires fixed LOINC codes for⏎systolic and diastolic values), which shows how standardized codes can make data⏎predictable, computable, and reusable for AI and quality measurement. However,⏎misalignment between laboratory information systems and EHR data elements, in⏎addition to the use of scanned reports rather than discrete results, still leads to missing⏎values, nonspecific codes, and loss of clinical context that limit AI model performance.⏎ONC/ASTP should promote a national LOINC reference implementation for diagnostic⏎tests, tied to USCDI and FHIR implementation guides, so that laboratories, EHRs, and AI⏎developers can reliably exchange test-level data needed for training, validation, and⏎benchmarking.⏎II. Interoperability and fair access for AI tools under TEFCA⏎a. To improve interoperability, ONC/ASTP should create a LOINC reference database for⏎diagnostic tests and standardize use among diagnostic manufacturers. TEFCA and related⏎nationwide networks are beginning to support exchange of USCDI data classes across⏎organizations, but without attention to governance, large incumbents or QHINs could⏎effectively gatekeep data flows needed by third-party AI developers. HHS should ensure⏎that TEFCA implementation includes clear requirements that certified APIs and exchange⏎frameworks provide non-discriminatory technical access to standardized data for⏎authorized AI tools, prevent exclusive or preferential access arrangements that⏎disadvantage smaller innovators, and propagate guidance on consistent use of SNOMED⏎CT, ICD, and CPT for diagnoses and procedures across clinical documentation, payment,⏎and reporting.⏎III. Making unstructured data more usable⏎a. Much clinically rich information remains in unstructured formats, such as scanned PDFs,⏎narrative notes, or loosely structured “other” fields, that are not consistently mapped to⏎USCDI elements or standard terminologies, limiting their value for AI development and⏎evaluation. HHS should support implementation guides, incentives, and technical⏎assistance to move key data elements from scanned or free-text formats into discrete,⏎coded fields aligned with USCDI and FHIR profiles, while encouraging tools that can⏎safely structure legacy unstructured content without degrading accuracy.⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers⏎have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎I. Challenges patients and caregivers would like to see addressed:⏎a. Access to specialist-level care for individuals with complex or rare diseases in rural⏎hospitals⏎1. Access to specialist-level care for individuals with complex or rare diseases in rural⏎hospitals can be strengthened through appropriate use of AI-enabled tools. These⏎8⏎⏎tools can help reallocate limited clinical and operational resources toward more⏎complex cases in resource-constrained settings, allowing clinicians to focus their time⏎and expertise where it is most needed. They can also support the diagnosis and⏎prediction of rare diseases in rural areas, particularly where local access to relevant⏎specialists is limited, so that patients receive timely and appropriate referrals to⏎higher levels of care.⏎b. Help navigating complex regimens, symptoms, and appointments⏎1. Patients and physicians report greater trust in efficiency-based and administrative⏎applications, as well as in tools that offer explanations and are more transparent over⏎black-box tools.⏎c. Patient and caregiver concerns:⏎1. Loss of human review/connection, specifically as related to clinical decision-making.⏎2. AI being used primarily to cut costs or deny care rather than improve safety and⏎outcomes⏎i. e.g. AI-based prior authorization tools, AI replacing clinical tasks and decision-⏎making.⏎3. Potential to exacerbate discrimination in care delivery due to reliance on data sets⏎that may not accurately reflect the population in which the AI is being used and may⏎therefore not provide the most accurate outputs, leading to unreliable patient⏎outcomes.⏎i. These risks are further compounded by a lack of adequate clinician training on use⏎of AI, including how to interpret, validate, and appropriately apply AI-generated⏎outputs or recommendations, increasing the likelihood that inaccurate⏎recommendations will be used and reinforce existing gaps in care delivery.⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎I. Real-world impact of AI on patient outcomes, safety, and trust over time⏎a. Implement multicenter, prospective studies and post-market monitoring that track⏎effects on outcomes, safety events, diagnostic accuracy, and perception of trust over⏎time.⏎II. Comparative evaluations of AI-enabled versus traditional workflows on clinical burden,⏎documentation time, and time with patients⏎a. Support studies using standardized workload and time-use measures, including frontline⏎clinician feedback, to determine when AI meaningfully improves care versus simply⏎redistributing tasks.⏎III. Methods to detect and mitigate inaccuracies, drift, and unintended harms⏎a. Establish a national outcomes-focused reporting and monitoring system for AI-enabled⏎tools, drawing on lessons from FDA-regulated products (such as drugs and biologics)⏎without simply replicating their exact model. This system should prioritize higher-risk AI⏎9⏎⏎use cases and focus on meaningful outcome and safety signals rather than requiring⏎uniform reporting for all tools.⏎i. Building on concepts from the Sentinel Initiative, stakeholders (including health⏎systems, manufacturers, and technology providers) could participate in an⏎aggregated outcome data registry to monitor real-world performance, identify⏎potential safety concerns, and support appropriate updates or modifications to AI-⏎enabled tools over time. This type of targeted post-market surveillance can be⏎structured to align with the incentives of clinicians, health care organizations, and⏎developers.13⏎ii. An AI outcomes registry could allow for the conduct of post-market monitoring and⏎collection of data on performance across diverse settings and populations. An⏎aggregated registry could track and analyze large volumes of clinical and operational⏎data to identify patterns of performance, adverse effects, errors, or inaccuracies that⏎emerge only after deployment. The registry could also track utilization patterns⏎associated with positive clinical outcomes, providing benchmarks for best practices⏎that developers and health systems can adopt. Moreover, AI systems that learn over⏎time or are periodically retrained based on new data can exhibit changes in⏎performance that are not captured by pre-market studies. Continuous monitoring⏎would enable early detection of adverse trends, triggering investigations or changes⏎including updated training protocols, algorithmic recalibration, or pausing certain⏎use cases. By documenting these, the registry would contribute to improving trust⏎among clinicians and patients and informing regulatory and oversight decisions.⏎iii. The registry could leverage a distributed data model similar to the Sentinel⏎Initiative’s, where data partners retain control of their data behind firewalls and only⏎aggregate, de-identified outputs are shared. This approach preserves HIPAA⏎compliance while allowing regulators, researchers, and developers to track the⏎performance of AI tools without centralizing identifiable patient information. The⏎data elements and interoperability formats could also be aligned with frameworks⏎like USCDI to improve consistency and reuse.⏎iv. Over time, a voluntary outcomes registry could serve as the foundation for a more⏎formal national framework, expanding participation and data sources to create a⏎comprehensive, scalable oversight infrastructure for AI-enabled clinical technologies.⏎b. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎in clinical care?⏎i. Our CEO has published a framework arguing that hospitals can and should⏎govern the use of AI through existing CMS Conditions of Participation by⏎13 Coleman, K., & Pencina, M. J. (2025). Targeted postmarket surveillance: The way toward responsible AI⏎innovation in health care. Paragon Health Institute.⏎10⏎⏎integrating AI-related safety, monitoring, and response into their current⏎patient safety and quality infrastructure. 14⏎Conclusion⏎Rubrum Advising appreciates the Department’s leadership in seeking concrete, experience-⏎based input at this early stage of federal policymaking on clinical AI. The recommendations in⏎this response reflect our belief that AI should be deployed in ways that measurably improve⏎outcomes and reduce total costs of care, while relying on existing safety and quality⏎infrastructure, clear governance expectations, and predictable reimbursement pathways to⏎support responsible innovation. We look forward to continued engagement with HHS as it⏎refines its regulatory, payment, and research strategies to accelerate the safe, effective use of AI⏎in clinical care.⏎Sincerely,⏎Rubrum Advising⏎14 Fleisher, L. A., & Economou-Zavlanos, N. J. (2024). Artificial intelligence can be regulated using current patient⏎safety procedures and infrastructure in hospitals. JAMA Health Forum, 5(6),⏎e241369. https://doi.org/10.1001/jamahealthforum.2024.1369.⏎11♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0416♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Flores♔♔Georgia♔♔United States♔National LGBTQ Task Force♔♔♔♔♔♔The framing of this RFI has bothered me since I first read it. HHS is asking how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, and I understand the appeal of that question — there are genuinely promising tools out there — but it skips over something we at the National LGBTQ Task Force have been watching with real alarm across the past two years of our health equity work. The question of acceleration only makes sense once you have answered a prior question, which is: when something goes wrong, who is responsible?⏎⏎I don't think we have answered that. Not remotely.⏎⏎Here is what I keep coming back to. A member organization we partner with on LGBTQ health access issues shared data from a 2023 survey of urology practices — 847 practices total — and 64% of them were already using some form of AI-assisted diagnostic or clinical decision-support tool. Fewer than one in five had any formal protocol for even telling patients an algorithm was involved before a treatment decision was made. That is not a technology-readiness problem. That is a rights problem. And it is happening right now, before any of the "acceleration" HHS is contemplating.⏎⏎When I try to trace what happens to a patient harmed by one of these tools, the chain of responsibility just dissolves. The clinician says they relied on the system. The hospital says the vendor is responsible. The vendor's contract almost certainly has language disclaiming liability for clinical outcomes — this is standard practice and I have seen enough of these contracts to be confident about that. And FDA 510(k) clearance, which is what most of these tools get, does not actually establish real-world safety in diverse populations. It provides a kind of regulatory paperwork that gets read as a safety endorsement even though that is not what the clearance process is designed to do.⏎⏎For our communities — LGBTQ people, particularly transgender patients and patients of color who already have documented reasons to distrust clinical institutions — this matters in a specific way. If an AI tool systematically produces worse recommendations for your demographic group because it was trained on non-representative data, and you are harmed, and then you try to figure out who you can hold responsible, the answer under current frameworks is essentially no one. The data provider didn't treat you. The vendor didn't treat you. The clinician relied on a cleared tool in good faith. The hospital followed its protocol. Existing tort law was not built for this and I am not aware of any federal regulatory framework that fills the gap.⏎⏎What I am asking HHS to do before any further acceleration is to answer, with specificity, the liability question. Not in a white paper. In binding guidance or rulemaking. Who is the responsible party when an AI-assisted clinical decision causes harm? What is the patient's recourse? The current architecture of these deployments — vendor disclaimers, opaque training data, embedding in clinical workflows so deeply that clinicians can't easily override or even identify when the AI is influencing a recommendation — is being built out at scale right now, and once it is embedded it will be practically impossible to reverse. We have seen this with EHR systems. Once a hospital has organized its entire workflow around a platform, it does not matter how many problems emerge, they are not switching.⏎⏎I recognize HHS cannot resolve tort law through an RFI response. But it can condition any new reimbursement pathways, certification programs, or federal funding tied to AI adoption on demonstrated accountability structures. If a vendor wants the benefit of federal support or recognition, they should have to agree to something meaningful when their tool hurts someone.⏎⏎The survey data I mentioned found that practices serving higher proportions of Medicaid patients were significantly less likely to have done any equity-focused validation of the tools they were already using. That means the populations with the least institutional power to push back are the ones most likely to be harmed without any recourse. I'm not sure HHS fully appreciates how fast this is moving on the ground, and how little of the infrastructure that would need to exist to make it safe actually exists yet. The practices using these tools are not waiting for federal guidance. They already bought the software.⏎⏎National LGBTQ Task Force⏎Kevin Flores, Research Director⏎kflores@thetaskforce.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0417♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-13T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Linda♔Baker♔♔Idaho♔♔United States♔Louisiana State University Health Sciences Center♔♔♔♔♔♔My concern with this RFI is not about AI in medicine per se — I have spent enough years studying health policy to know that technological change in clinical settings is neither uniformly good nor bad, and I hold no romantic attachment to the status quo. What bothers me is something more structural, and I want to try to articulate it carefully because I think it keeps getting lost in these public processes.⏎⏎The framing of this RFI treats acceleration as a self-evidently correct goal. Regulatory levers, reimbursement structures, and federal R&D investment are all being pointed in the same direction simultaneously, and the implicit argument is that the main obstacles to AI adoption are bureaucratic friction and insufficient funding. But that framing skips over a question that I would argue belongs much earlier in the process: how much computational delegation of clinical judgment do we, as a society, actually want? That is not a technical question. It is a political and moral one, and an administrative agency issuing an RFI is not the right mechanism for answering it.⏎⏎Here is what I keep thinking about. Once a technology is embedded in reimbursement schedules, it becomes practically irreversible. CMS coding decisions, once made, take years to revisit and often never fully unwind. If HHS uses payment policy to drive AI adoption into clinical workflows at scale — and this RFI explicitly contemplates doing exactly that — the practical consequence is that future policymakers, future clinicians, and future patients inherit a set of defaults they had no meaningful role in choosing. The democratic window for deciding how much AI belongs in the exam room closes quietly and without a vote.⏎⏎The radiology context is actually a good illustration of what I mean, though I recognize it is only one corner of the problem. A 2024 ACR Data Science Institute survey found that 68% of radiology practices had deployed at least one AI-enabled imaging tool, but fewer than 12% had done any systematic internal validation to check whether vendor performance claims held up under their own patient populations. That is a striking gap, and it exists already, before any federal acceleration agenda has been fully operationalized. What happens to that gap when reimbursement incentives are added on top?⏎⏎I want to be fair to HHS here. I understand that there are genuine access and efficiency problems that AI tools might help address, particularly in underserved rural areas like much of Idaho, where I spent considerable time before my current position. I am not arguing for a moratorium. What I am arguing is that the sequencing is wrong. Governance frameworks, audit requirements, and some form of democratic deliberation about acceptable tradeoffs should precede the use of federal payment policy as an adoption accelerant, not follow it.⏎⏎The RFI asks specifically about regulatory actions HHS can take to accelerate adoption. My answer to that particular question is that HHS should first commission an independent analysis of how reversible its proposed policy interventions actually are — not just legally, but practically, given how institutional workflows and procurement contracts function in real hospital systems. The question of what can be undone if the evidence later turns unfavorable should be answered before the question of how to move faster.⏎⏎I realize that is probably not the kind of answer the RFI is designed to receive, but I think it is the honest one.⏎⏎One other thing that I did not see adequately addressed anywhere in the RFI document: the question of who bears liability when an AI-assisted clinical decision causes harm, and whether acceleration of adoption changes that calculus. This may technically fall outside HHS's lane but it seems impossible to discuss reimbursement policy for AI tools without some account of♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0418♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from SBA, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎VIA ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care, 90 FR 60108⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On December 23, 2025, the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) requested⏎information on how the agency can accelerate the adoption of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in the⏎clinical care space.1⏎In this response, Advocacy highlights feedback received from small entities in the clinical care⏎space. While small businesses are increasingly adopting AI tools, significant barriers remain.2⏎Small entities raised concerns about data standardization and interoperability, access to training⏎and education, reimbursement structures, liability and safe harbor protections, and regulatory⏎clarity.⏎I. Background⏎A. The Office of Advocacy⏎Congress established the Office of Advocacy under Pub. L. 94-305 to represent the views of⏎small entities before federal agencies and Congress. Advocacy is an independent voice within the⏎executive branch that seeks to ensure small business concerns are heard in the federal regulatory⏎process. Advocacy also works to ensure that regulations do not unduly inhibit the ability of small⏎1 90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (Dec. 23, 2025).⏎2 AI in Business: Small Firms Closing In https://advocacy.sba.gov/2025/09/24/ai-in-business-small-firms-closing-in/⏎(Sept. 24, 2025).⏎⏎entities to compete, innovate, or comply with federal laws. The views expressed by Advocacy do⏎not necessarily reflect the views of the Small Business Administration (SBA) or the⏎Administration.⏎The Regulatory Flexibility Act (RFA),3 as amended by the Small Business Regulatory⏎Enforcement Fairness Act (SBREFA),4 gives small entities a voice in the rulemaking process.⏎For all rules that are expected to have a significant economic impact on a substantial number of⏎small entities, the RFA requires federal agencies to assess the impact of the proposed rule on⏎small entities and to consider less burdensome alternatives.5 If a rule is not expected to have a⏎significant economic impact on a substantial number of small entities, agencies may certify it as⏎such and submit a statement of the factual basis for such a determination that adequately⏎supports its certification.6⏎Advocacy’s comments are consistent with Congressional intent underlying the RFA, that⏎“[w]hen adopting regulations to protect the health, safety, and economic welfare of the nation,⏎federal agencies should seek to achieve statutory goals as effectively and efficiently as possible⏎without imposing unnecessary burdens on the public.”7⏎The Small Business Jobs Act of 2010 requires agencies to give every appropriate consideration⏎to comments provided by Advocacy.8 The agency must include a response to these written⏎comments in any explanation or discussion accompanying the final rule’s publication in the⏎Federal Register, unless the agency certifies that the public interest is not served by doing so.9⏎II. Advocacy’s Response to HHS’ Questions⏎HHS posed ten questions to seek insight on how clinicians are currently using AI and the⏎potential consequences of AI adoption. On January 28, 2026, Advocacy hosted a roundtable with⏎over seventy participants to solicit insight from small entities on each of HHS’s questions.⏎Advocacy summarizes those views below.⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care⏎and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Roundtable participants noted multiple barriers to fully integrating AI into existing systems, such⏎as, unreliability of data quality, lack of education and training, and an inability to fully reimburse⏎the costs associated with AI. Healthcare, specifically, will experience significant challenges in⏎this area.⏎3 Pub. L. No. 96-354, 94 Stat. 1164 (1980) (codified at 5 U.S.C. §§ 601-612).⏎4 Pub. L. No. 104-121, tit. II, 110 Stat. 857 (1996) (codified in scattered sections of 5 U.S.C. §§601-612).⏎5 5 U.S.C. § 603.⏎6 Id. § 605(b).⏎7 Regulatory Flexibility Act, Pub. L. No. 96-354, 94 Stat. 1164 (1980) (codified at 5 U.S.C. §§ 601-612).⏎8 Small Business Jobs Act of 2010, Pub. L. No. 111-240, §1601, 214 Stat. 2551 (codified at 5 U.S.C. § 604).⏎9 Id.⏎⏎A. Data Standardization and Quality⏎High-quality, interoperable data are the foundations for effective AI. Currently, the lack of⏎consistent data standards across specialties limits AI systems’ ability to interpret and transfer⏎insights between disciplines. For example, a surgeon, physical therapist, and neuromuscular⏎therapist may each assess the same shoulder injury differently, resulting in data that are not⏎compatible for cross-disciplinary analysis.⏎Roundtable participants noted that available AI models for clinical diagnostics are trained on⏎data drawn primarily from adult populations aged 20–40, rendering them less reliable for⏎pediatric and geriatric use. Expanding representative datasets would support equitable AI⏎adoption across diverse patient populations.⏎Roundtable participants recommended that HHS collaborate with the National Institute of⏎Standards and Technology (NIST) to establish data standardization guidance for clinical care⏎specialties. Harmonized data definitions would allow AI systems to generate insights usable⏎across the continuum of care.⏎B. Lack of Guidance on AI Use and Interpretation⏎Clinicians expressed uncertainty about how to interpret AI-generated diagnostic results or⏎understand the algorithms’ weighting of variables. As AI diagnostic tools become the norm⏎for practitioners, clinicians need to understand how AI reaches its result since the clinician is⏎liable for any misdiagnosis. The absence of clear guidance deters adoption due to concerns⏎over reliability and liability.⏎Roundtable participants urged HHS to develop interpretive guidance and transparency⏎standards that would allow clinicians to understand the logic and limitations of AI tools.⏎Additionally, HHS should establish standardized criteria or metrics for evaluating the⏎reliability and performance of clinical AI technologies.⏎C. Training and Education⏎Small clinicians reported limited opportunities for AI training and stated that lack of⏎familiarity with AI-based diagnostics creates hesitancy to adopt new tools. Small hospitals⏎face disproportionate barriers to accessing AI-centered training due to their lack of resources.⏎Many small hospitals and clinical offices lack dedicated AI governance structures, legal, or⏎technical expertise and are therefore less able to assess vendor claims, manage risk, or⏎negotiate complex AI tools and services contracts.⏎Roundtable participants recommended that HHS develop educational resources or continuing⏎education programs to train clinicians on AI integration, interpretation, and create forums⏎where hospitals can share their experiences using AI tools. This will facilitate small clinicians⏎to adopt AI powered tools. HHS should facilitate sharing information, advice, and⏎cybersecurity frameworks between small clinicians.⏎⏎D. Reimbursement Policies⏎Clinicians, particularly in small and rural practices, cited reimbursement challenges as a⏎central barrier to AI adoption. AI tools can improve efficiency but do not currently provide⏎direct financial benefits under existing Center for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS)⏎billing codes. Without updated reimbursement methods, small clinical practices often find⏎that the productivity gains of AI-enabled tools do not justify their significant financial costs.⏎Roundtable participants urged HHS to examine reimbursement structures and create codes⏎recognizing the costs of implementing AI tools. Aligning reimbursement with time and⏎resource savings could promote wider adoption.⏎E. Building Trust in AI⏎Clinicians voiced concern about the reliability and accountability in AI-based decisions.⏎Clinicians and patients are reluctant to rely on AI tools that may produce errors or⏎“hallucinations.” Without reliable means to systematically check the accuracy of various AI-⏎enabled tools, clinicians are faced with uncertainty in how to analyze the results it produces.⏎When a clinician’s medical licenses are implicated when utilizing AI, the AI-enabled devices⏎must build trust with clinicians to assure them of the accuracy of the results. Advocacy⏎supports HHS’s efforts to expand public awareness, validation processes, and transparency⏎standards that foster trust and understanding of AI capabilities and limitations. Roundtable⏎participants suggested that HHS should create model datasets that small clinicians can rely⏎on to test AI-enabled diagnostic tools.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or⏎use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable code of federal⏎regulations citations.⏎A. Liability and Safe Harbor Frameworks⏎HHS should explore mechanisms that provide liability protection or safe harbors when health⏎systems deploy AI tools that meet recognized national standards for validation,⏎documentation, and governance. This may include encouraging state medical licensure⏎boards and associations to have active conversations regarding how AI tools affect the⏎standard of care within their state. Without such protections, the promise of AI to reduce⏎clinician burden is undermined by the expectation that providers must independently validate⏎or override AI outputs.⏎⏎B. National AI Governance and Validation Standards⏎Clear federal guidance on acceptable AI risk management frameworks would accelerate⏎adoption. To establish these management frameworks, collaboration from both clinical⏎providers and AI tool developers is needed. Using established frameworks that have buy-in⏎from both developers and clinical deployers would provide defensible, consistent⏎expectations across states and health systems, reducing duplicative or inconsistent internal⏎standards.⏎C. Payment Policy Alignment⏎CMS should expand reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled diagnostics and clinical⏎decision support, while appropriately recalibrating payment rates. For example, reimbursing⏎certain AI-based interpretations at a lower rate but allowing higher-frequency use could⏎address workforce shortages while maintaining safety and access, particularly for rural or⏎small hospitals. Roundtable participants encouraged HHS to further explore how various⏎clinician specialists utilize the efficiency and insights gained by AI. For example, roundtable⏎participants noted that time saved by ambient scribe technologies enabled them to spend⏎more time with patients while other specialties such as radiology noted that certain AI tools⏎enabled them to see more cases on a daily basis. Providing clear guidance on payment policy⏎will enable these tools to be adopted by smaller clinical practices.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures ( e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security).⏎What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to⏎help address them?⏎Roundtable participants raised concerns about AI model updates that may alter diagnostic⏎performance without clinician notice. Small clinicians questioned who bears liability when⏎manufacturers modify underlying AI algorithms.⏎For AI tools not regulated as medical devices, health systems face unresolved challenges related⏎to liability, indemnification, data governance, and accountability. Absent Food and Drug⏎Administration (FDA) review, health providers may benefit from the accelerated innovation. On⏎the other hand, timely and well-executed FDA review may relieve providers of the burden of⏎independently assessing safety and appropriateness.10⏎This dynamic can slow adoption, as health systems decline to deploy tools without regulatory⏎certainty. HHS can play a constructive role by providing voluntary guidance for oversight,⏎10 Private-sector review may create opportunities for small entities to serve as standards bodies, a role they have⏎historically filled. On private-sector drug efficacy review prior to 1962, see Sam Peltzman, An Evaluation of⏎Consumer Protection Legislation: The 1962 Drug Amendments, 81 J. Pol. Econ. 1049 (1973).⏎⏎documentation, and accountability for non-medical-device AI used in clinical settings and by⏎aligning guidance across the FDA, Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT (ONC),⏎Office of Civil Rights (OCR), and CMS.⏎Participants also noted that HHS needs to research the consumer-facing applications enabled by⏎AI which are not subject to Health Insurance Portability and Accountable Act (HIPAA) privacy⏎and security frameworks. Without clear guidance or standards, many consumers are utilizing⏎personal AI monitoring tools that are not subject to HIPAA and the protections that come with⏎clinical practice.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods⏎(pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎When clinicians look at AI tools in the clinical care space, the issue of evaluating the AI results⏎falls on the clinician. There are no clear standards or means to test AI diagnostic tools. Robust⏎AI benchmarking has emerged in the private and academic sectors, including standardized⏎evaluations for clinical reasoning, diagnostic accuracy, and complex case analysis. These⏎benchmarks provide objective, quantitative measures that could complement qualitative⏎regulatory review.⏎Roundtable participants encourage HHS to review existing regulatory frameworks which could⏎create the appropriate “sandbox” for pre- and post-deployment AI evaluation. For example,⏎clarification regarding how existing Patient Safety Organizations (as defined under the Patient⏎Safety and Quality Improvement Act of 2005) can provide the appropriate controlled and⏎accountable environment for AI monitoring, evaluation, and improvement. By creating the⏎appropriate safe harbor for evaluations, HHS could support adoption by recognizing or⏎referencing validated benchmark methodologies, periodically updating performance thresholds⏎to reflect technological progress, and supporting applied research through grants or cooperative⏎agreements focused on post-deployment monitoring, bias detection, and workflow impact⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎Regulatory clarity is the most effective way HHS can support private sector accreditation and⏎certification efforts. Clear federal expectations enable third-party organizations to develop⏎meaningful certification programs that small health systems can rely on rather than⏎independently validating every tool. Absent this clarity, adoption becomes dependent on internal⏎expertise and negotiating power, which disadvantages small hospitals and safety-net providers.⏎⏎Roundtable participants recommended that HHS should partner with small clinicians to promote⏎certification programs and establish clinician-led reporting and feedback channels.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would⏎have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality,⏎and help reduce costs?⏎Exceeding Expectations:⏎AI tools have exceeded expectations when it functions as time multipliers, particularly in⏎administrative and clinical documentation workflows. Tools that return meaningful clinician time⏎(minutes per encounter that compound across patient stays) deliver clear value. For example, AI-⏎enabled chart summarization has demonstrated substantial time savings for clinicians by rapidly⏎synthesizing patient records into actionable summaries. One small entity participant noted that⏎their hospital saves twenty minutes of paperwork for every new patient and ten minutes for⏎returning patients with the use of auto-transcribing and notetaking tools.⏎Falling Short:⏎AI tools are only as capable as the foundational data that it has been trained on. Roundtable⏎participants noted AI tools perform less effectively for underrepresented subpopulations such as⏎pediatric and geriatric patients. There are gaps in the data which AI tools are trained on which⏎create bias and risk of misdiagnosis or treatment.⏎Additionally, public misuse or overreliance on AI-based self-diagnosis presents new challenges⏎for clinicians. AI diagnostic tools have the potential to diagnose patients, but the patient must⏎close the referral loop and complete the specialized treatments.⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the⏎primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Physicians are the most influential decision-makers in AI adoption. Frontline clinicians rapidly⏎determine whether a tool improves the delivery of care or reduces burden, and their usage⏎patterns determines success or failure.⏎Administrative hurdles include governance complexity, contracting requirements, data access⏎restrictions, and inconsistent regulatory guidance, all of which slow deployment and⏎disproportionately affect smaller hospitals.⏎⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific⏎data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability, particularly provider-side data portability, would significantly expand⏎AI innovation. While patient-directed access has improved, healthcare systems remain⏎constrained in using their own data for AI-enabled workflows such as prior authorization, care⏎coordination, and documentation automation. Clarifying permissible uses of health data and⏎related regulations for operational AI under HIPAA would provide certainty to small clinicians⏎and improve the patient and provider experience.⏎Question 9: What challenges within healthcare do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Stakeholders identified AI’s potential to prevent chronic disease and support patient monitoring⏎(e.g., AI-enabled beds to prevent bedsores and cameras to detect when a recovering patient is a⏎fall-risk). However, clinicians emphasized the need to preserve the integrity of the doctor–patient⏎relationship and ensure that AI tools augment, rather than replace, clinical judgment⏎Patients and caregivers want AI to reduce administrative burden, improve access, and support⏎more timely, accurate care. Common frustrations, such as delays related to documentation or⏎authorization, are areas where AI could deliver tangible benefit.⏎At the same time, patients express concerns about privacy, transparency, and trust. Clear⏎governance, appropriate safeguards, and communication about how AI is used in care delivery⏎are essential to maintaining confidence.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs benefits, and transfers of using AI as⏎part of clinical care?⏎Advocacy has no response to this question.⏎III. Conclusion⏎Small health care providers view AI as a promising tool for improving patient care and efficiency⏎but face significant barriers related to data quality, reimbursement, liability, and trust. Advocacy⏎encourages HHS to collaborate with NIST, CMS, and industry experts to develop clear guidance,⏎⏎reimbursement structures, and data standards that allow small entities to adopt AI safely and⏎effectively. Doing so will ensure that innovation in clinical AI benefits providers and patients⏎across all practice sizes and specialties. Advocacy applauds HHS for taking the first step in⏎developing an AI framework and seeking public input. Advocacy hopes to facilitate further⏎conversations about the success and struggles of small entities attempting to implement AI.⏎If you have any questions or require additional information, please contact me or Assistant Chief⏎Counsel David Mullis at (202) 830-2292 or by email at David.Mullis@sba.gov.⏎Sincerely,⏎Dr. Casey B. Mulligan⏎Chief Counsel⏎Office of Advocacy⏎U.S. Small Business Administration⏎David Mullis⏎Assistant Chief Counsel⏎Office of Advocacy⏎U.S. Small Business Administration⏎Copy to: Mr. Jeffrey B. Clark, Sr., Associate Administrator⏎Office of Information and Regulatory Affairs⏎Office of Management and Budget♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0419♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from National Health Law Program, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔National Health Law Program♔♔♔♔♔♔Elizabeth G. Taylor⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Executive Director⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Board of Directors⏎Technology⏎Arian M. June⏎Chair Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop 7033A⏎Debevoise & Plimpton LLP⏎330 C Street SW⏎Juliana S. O’Reilly⏎Vice Chair Washington, DC 20201⏎American Express⏎Shamina D. Sneed⏎Treasurer Submitted via regulations.gov⏎CLYO Consulting⏎John Bouman⏎Secretary RE: RIN 0955-AA13 Request for Information: Accelerating⏎Legal Action Chicago⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Hailyn J. Chen⏎Munger, Tolles & Olson LLP Clinical Care⏎Jeanna M. Cullins⏎Aon Hewitt (Ret.)⏎Dear Deputy Assistant Secretary Posnack,⏎Joel Ferber⏎Legal Services of Eastern Missouri⏎Ann M. Kappler The National Health Law Program (NHeLP) is a public⏎Chair Emerita⏎Prudential Financial, Inc. interest law firm working to advance access to quality health⏎Robert J. Nelson⏎Lieff, Cabraser, Heimann & Bernstein, LLP care and protect the legal rights of low-income and under-⏎Jane Preyer served people. NHeLP has a long history of advocacy⏎Environmental Defense Fund (Ret.)⏎regarding the use of automated decision-making systems⏎Osula Evadne Rushing⏎KFF (ADS), including algorithms and other forms of artificial⏎William B. Schultz⏎intelligence (AI) to determine health care eligibility and⏎Zuckerman Spaeder LLP⏎Nick Smirensky services.1 We are particularly familiar with how the lack of⏎New York State Health Foundation (Ret.)⏎transparency and accountability of many ADS impacts⏎Stephen L. Williams⏎Houston Health Department (Ret.) individuals’ ability to understand and fight care denials or⏎Senior Advisor to the Board reductions. We have also seen how poor transparency in the⏎Hon. Henry A. Waxman⏎Waxman Strategies⏎ADS development and implementation process obscures⏎General Counsel⏎Marc Fleischaker opportunities for evaluation of errors or bias in these⏎ArentFox Schiff LLP⏎systems. We have also seen repeated instances of harm⏎from ADS without individuals understanding ADS has been⏎used or how to seek remedy for that harm. We appreciate⏎the opportunity to provide information about HHS’s⏎approach to the use of AI as part of clinical care.2 Our long⏎advocacy history around these systems supports our⏎position that the use of AI in health care is an opportunity⏎to improve care, but there must be protections for people in⏎the process that reflect the magnitude of potential harm.⏎1444 I Street NW, Suite 1105 · Washington, DC 20005 · (202) 289-7661⏎3701 Wilshire Boulevard, Suite 315 · Los Angeles, CA 90010 · (310) 204-6010⏎1512 E. Franklin Street, Suite 110 · Chapel Hill, NC 27514 · (919) 968-6308⏎www.healthlaw.org⏎⏎NHeLP provides some overarching comments that apply to the RFI’s queries generally,⏎before answering questions 6, 9, and 10.a.⏎Innovation Can Be Supported While Providing Appropriate Protections.⏎Efforts to foster innovation in AI in clinical settings must be balanced against its real and⏎potential harms. When safeguards are fully enforced, they can support innovation by⏎helping to promote awareness and identify issues, including discrimination and unintended⏎impacts that cause harm. Research into AI in healthcare has repeatedly shown that many⏎tools incorporate bias, either because the statistical analyses or other prediction⏎mechanisms the AI is based upon or trained with incorporates bias or lacks a full scope of⏎data.3 Research also shows that not all populations are typically represented in clinical data⏎and this affects the reliability and bias of AI systems.4 Our response to questions 6, 9, and⏎10.a of the RFI provide more information on this research. Overall, studies that examine⏎whether bias exists in clinical algorithms consistently call for greater transparency and⏎accountability around the use of these tools throughout their lifecycle because of the risk of⏎bias and the limitations of the research that typically underlies these tools.5 HHS must⏎1 See generally Nat’l Health Law Program, Fairness in Automated Decision-Making Systems,⏎https://healthlaw.org/algorithms/; Elizabeth Edwards & David Machledt, Nat’l Health Law Program,⏎Principles for Fairer, More Responsive Automated Decision-Making Systems (May 15, 2023),⏎https://healthlaw.org/resource/principles-for-fairer-more-responsive-automated-decision-making-⏎systems/ [hereinafter NHeLP ADS Principles].⏎2 HHS, Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care, 90 Fed. Reg. 60108 (Dec. 23, 2025), https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-⏎2025-12-23/pdf/2025-23641.pdf.⏎3 See, e.g., HHS, AHRQ, IMPACT OF HEALTHCARE ON RACIAL AND ETHNIC DISPARITIES IN HEALTH AND⏎HEALTHCARE (2023, addendum 2024),⏎https://effectivehealthcare.ahrq.gov/sites/default/files/related_files/cer-268-racial-disparities-⏎health-healthcare-addendum.pdf (finding algorithms have been shown to perpetuate, exacerbate,⏎and sometimes reduce racial and ethnic disparities and important mitigation steps include increased⏎transparency in development and implementation, promoting awareness, including in patients, of⏎potential risk, and further research before widespread implementation).⏎4 Id.; NHeLP ADS Principles (discussing history of bias in ADS); see also Abdullah Alsaleh, The⏎Influence of Artificial Intelligence on Individuals with Disabilities, ACTA PSYCHOLOGICA (Feb. 2026),⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0001691825013241 (discussing literature review⏎of AI research including disability and noting the limited user diversity of much of the AI, the⏎limitations of the AI tools, and issues such as ethical and privacy concerns); Ariana Aboulafia, CDT⏎& Henry Claypool, AAPD, Building A Disability-Inclusive AI Ecosystem A Cross-Disability, Cross-⏎Systems Analysis Of Best Practices (Mar. 11, 2025), https://cdt.org/insights/building-a-disability-⏎inclusive-ai-ecosystem-a-cross-disability-cross-systems-analysis-of-best-practices/; see generally⏎New York City Bar, The Impact of The Use of Artificial Intelligence on People with Disabilities (June⏎2025), https://www.nycbar.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/20221483-AnalysisAIDisabilities.pdf.⏎5 See note 4, supra. The research cited repeatedly cites the need for more transparency and⏎accountability in the use of these tools, both so clinicians understand the limitations and so that⏎people can identify both when such tools are being used and can identify errors.⏎2⏎⏎incorporate significant protections and accountability mechanisms into any regulatory⏎approach.⏎Meaningful Transparency and Accountability Measures are Necessary for Public⏎Trust in Clinical AI.⏎At a very basic level, when it is in use, all parties using the AI tool or whom are affected by⏎the use of an AI tool must have sufficient information that an AI tool is in use, any⏎limitations or biases of the tool, enough information to understand how the decision was⏎made so that it can be challenged, and mechanisms to correct any wrongs made by the⏎tool at an individual or larger population level. These basic, well-accepted standards are⏎not new concepts for AI creators and will not hinder innovation, but will help foster⏎comfortability with the use of AI and are critically important in the identification of⏎problems. All of this is foundational to public trust in AI, which in turn fosters innovation.⏎Transparency alone is insufficient; HHS must provide strong oversight and investigation.⏎Transparency is critical, but it puts the onus on individuals impacted by the AI to⏎understand it, have the expertise to challenge it, and have the resources to do so. HHS can⏎take lessons learned from mental health parity compliance that simple requirements of⏎transparency may not be sufficient if there is not enforcement of those requirements and⏎when greater expertise may be required to identify improper bias.6⏎HHS has existing enforcement tools that can help regulate AI tools. AI does not necessarily⏎create novel legal issues with respect to existing civil rights laws like Section 504 of the⏎Rehabilitation Act, the Americans with Disabilities Act, and Section 1557 of the Affordable⏎Care Act (incorporating Title VI, Title IX, and The Age Discrimination Act of 1975). These⏎laws and rights all still apply to various aspects of healthcare. Merely incorporating AI into⏎how decisions are made does not insulate that decision or its impact from civil rights⏎enforcement. However, the government’s oversight and expertise in these areas must be⏎strengthened as increased investigation and accountability is needed, especially as⏎significant information and expertise imbalance will exist between those who develop and⏎employ AI (payers and providers) and those who are harmed by it (patients and their⏎families).⏎If the implementation of AI continues to occur with a great deal of secrecy and limited⏎ability by users to identify problems, any public trust in the use of AI will erode quickly and⏎thus stifle innovation. If HHS truly wants to support the promise of AI to improve health⏎6 For a discussion of the history of mental health parity legislation, requirements of plans, how⏎plans employed techniques that denied services in new ways, and subsequent legislation and⏎enforcement activities, including supporting sources, see, e.g., Amicus Brief of Nat’l Health L.⏎Program & The Kennedy Forum in E.W. v. Health Net, available at⏎https://healthlaw.org/resource/amicus-brief-of-nhelp-and-the-kennedy-forum-in-e-w-v⏎health/?fbclid=IwAR3sBc20UO2ZqOhO1GbjHGuJJeboMAQ1T1lmH6KIynOiIasEBoC5muxFwM.⏎3⏎⏎care, it must focus on the choices that will protect individuals and thus public trust in these⏎systems. Part of such efforts must include significant data transparency and accountability⏎mechanisms. States are currently taking the lead on AI regulation and should be allowed to⏎continue to develop these mechanisms. AI accountability is not an easy problem to solve,⏎but very traditional approaches regarding transparency and accountability, including⏎investigation by federal agencies under existing laws are important pieces to protecting⏎individuals from harm and overall public trust in the use of AI.7⏎RFI Questions:⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI⏎tools would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give⏎new insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎Studies on effective use of AI and positive beneficiary outcomes do not exist. Instead,⏎studies have shown that AI tools deployed in clinical care have fallen short in many ways,⏎most notably because of data issues. These issues include incompatible, inconsistent,⏎outdated and incomplete data; biased data; and the failure of systems to communicate⏎with one another (interoperability failures). These studies indicate that as HHS approaches⏎supporting the use of AI in clinical settings, it must provide protections that address these⏎well-known and identified issues. Each of these issues can generate harm to individuals,⏎but can do so at speed and scale that has not been experienced before. Therefore, in order⏎to protect individuals from harm and facilitate the long-term promise of AI innovation, HHS⏎must ensure that it takes all due care in setting forth criteria and oversight in these early⏎years of AI use.⏎Flawed Data. Poor design, faulty or missing data, and other implementation issues can⏎cause AI tools to fail outright or fail to anticipate unexpected circumstances. Resulting⏎service and coverage losses may affect whole populations, and often fall disproportionately⏎on marginalized groups. According to the American Medical Association:⏎[t]he risk of generative AI ‘hallucinating,’ ‘confabulating,’ or otherwise fabricating⏎information in response to a user-generated query has been well documented.⏎Notably, tools such as ChatGPT have shown a not-uncommon tendency to falsify⏎references cited in response to these queries. Generative AI tools have⏎demonstrated the ability to generate fraudulent scientific/medical literature. They⏎7 See, e.g., CHAI Patient Survey Report, COALITION FOR HEALTH AI 3(survey finding that an average⏎of 51% of respondents said AI use makes them trust health care less, with just 12% saying it made⏎them trust health care more), Paige Nong & Jodyn Platt, Patients’ Trust in Health Systems to Use⏎Artificial Intelligence, 8 JAMA Network Open 2 (Feb. 2025) (finding that nearly 60% of U.S. adults⏎do not trust their health care systems to ensure that they will not be harmed by an AI tool, and⏎that roughly 65% do not trust their health care systems to use AI responsibly).⏎4⏎⏎are also capable of plagiarizing, falsifying, or misrepresenting data in ways that⏎could compromise research integrity. Additionally, retracted papers may have the⏎ability to continue to impact the content generated by LLM-based tools, potentially⏎leading to dissemination or inaccurate or otherwise discredited information.8⏎The scale of data involved in these technologies and the lack of transparency also poses⏎many problems and “may obscure the patterns from human analysts.”9 Machine learning⏎can detect patterns, but data volume does not automatically correct for bias, missing⏎information, or misrepresentation. At the same time, some private entities (and⏎developers) get exclusive rights to large databases which limits transparency and⏎independent validation.⏎Incompatible Data and Interoperability Failures. There are major structural barriers⏎to workflow integration and consistent data-sharing across the entire health care system.⏎These data issues can lead to errors, inaccuracies, or limitations in AI systems. According⏎to the GAO:⏎Data are often siloed in different systems, such as medical imaging archival systems,⏎diagnostic systems, EHRs, electronic prescribing tools, and insurance databases. The⏎various siloes store data in varying formats that cannot easily be reconciled and⏎aggregated even if the data could be shared. The more systems involved, the more⏎difficult it can be to aggregate the data.10⏎One review of EHR systems found that “there are no clinical decision support AI tools using⏎EHR data that have successfully scaled across a health care system.”11⏎This is especially problematic in the context of Electronic Health Records (EHR). EHR⏎standards usually vary not just across vendors, but also within the same hospital systems,⏎leading to “disjointed, incomplete records” that negatively impact the accuracy of AI⏎performance.12 Issues that GAO described 10 years ago related specifically to Electronic⏎Health Records (EHR) have yet to be resolved. These include “(1) insufficiencies in health⏎data standards, (2) variation in state privacy rules, (3) accurately matching patients’ health⏎records, (4) costs associated with interoperability, and (5) the need for governance and⏎8 Am. Med. Assoc., AMA Principles for Augmented Intelligence Development, Deployment, and Use⏎(Nov. 12, 2024), https://www.ama-assn.org/system/files/ama-ai-principles.pdf; Rami Hatem, Briana⏎Simmons, Joseph Thornton, Chatbot Confabulations Are Not Hallucinations, JAMA INTERN. MED.⏎183(19):1177 (2023), doi:10.1001/jamainternmed.2023.4231.⏎9 Kun-Hsing Yu et al., Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare, 2 NAT. BIOMED. ENG’G 719 (2018).⏎10 U.S. Gov’t Accountability Off., Artificial Intelligence in Health Care: Benefits and Challenges of⏎Machine Learning Technologies for Medical Diagnostics, GAO-21-7SP, at 21 (2020).⏎11 Mark Sendak et al., A Path for Translation of Machine Learning Products into Healthcare Delivery,⏎11 EMJ INNOV. 43 (2020).⏎12 John D. Halamka et al., Early Experiences with Personal Health Records, 15 J. AM. MED.⏎INFORMATICS ASS’N 1 (2008).⏎5⏎⏎trust among entities.”13 While CMS interoperability rules have made some progress in⏎detailing and requiring integrated systems, they are still not completely implemented (and⏎won’t be until 2027).14 Until this actually happens, AI systems will continue to rely on⏎partial or inconsistent records.⏎Even when unified, AI vendors often have access only to structured electronic health⏎record data about an individual, which commonly excludes information about social⏎determinants of health, functional limitations, caregiver supports, or housing instability. As⏎Health Affairs noted, these omissions can cause AI tools to underperform when predicting⏎care needs for historically marginalized populations.15⏎Data used in AI, and AI, scales bias. Racial bias is also well-documented across AI⏎tools in health care settings.16 In one case, because an algorithm used cost as a proxy for⏎health, Black patients were systematically under-identified for high-risk care management⏎because the tool associated higher health care costs with sicker patients.17 Other⏎documented cases include biased transplant prioritization, unsafe clinical recommendations,⏎and chatbot agents providing inaccurate medical advice.18⏎Bias across payers and settings is a specific problem in programs like Medicaid. Because⏎Medicaid serves people with historically underserved identities, the risks of AI-driven clinical⏎care decisions are especially serious. These tools are likely to rely on historical insurance⏎data from across the system (not just Medicaid) - data that reflects long-standing inequities⏎and bias. When that data trains automated systems, it can quickly lead to care denials at⏎scale. Additionally, when AI is deployed in lower-resource settings without the same⏎resources, recommendations can increase patient harm. As the GAO reported,⏎[i]f an AI tool is developed using data from a high-resource hospital and later⏎applied to a community-based hospital with a different patient population, the tool⏎may not necessarily be safe for the new population or perform as effectively in the⏎new location.19⏎13 GAO-21-7SP, supra note 10.⏎14 Patient Access and Interoperability Final Rule, 42 C.F.R. §§ 422, 431, 438, 457; 45 C.F.R. § 156.⏎15 Michelle M. Mello et al., The AI Arms Race in Health Insurance Utilization Review, 45 HEALTH AFF.⏎6 (2026), https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00897.⏎16 AHRQ, supra note 3; Patrick Tighe et al., AHRQ, Artificial Intelligence and Patient Safety: Promise⏎and Challenges, PSNet, Agency for Healthcare Research & Quality (2024); Kerstin Denecke et al.,⏎Artificial Intelligence and Patient Safety: Promise and Challenges (2024); Aziz Sheikh et al., Artificial⏎Intelligence and Health Inequities, 52 LANCET REG’L HEALTH-EUR. (2024).⏎17 “Although these two variables are correlated—sicker patients generally need more care—⏎disparities in health care access… mean that on average African American patients have lower⏎medical costs at the same level of health as Caucasian patients.” Ziad Obermeyer at al., Dissecting⏎Racial Bias in an Algorithm Used to Manage the Health of Populations, 366 SCIENCE 447 (2019).⏎18 Kerstin Denecke et al., AHRQ, Artificial Intelligence and Patient Safety: Promise and Challenges⏎(2024).⏎19 GAO-21-7SP, supra note 10, at 27.⏎6⏎⏎Electronic health data often do not reflect specific populations, like Medicaid beneficiaries.⏎“High-resource hospitals often were early adopters of EHR systems and thus may have⏎larger volumes of high-quality electronic data that now can be used to develop and train AI⏎tools. However, the data from such hospitals may underrepresent some patient⏎populations.”20 For example, rare diseases and low-prevalence conditions are systemically⏎excluded.21 As a result, AI tools often perform poorest where clinical risk is highest.⏎Even where studies “document the accuracy of AI tools, accuracy does not necessarily⏎represent clinical efficacy.”22 We have very, very few studies of beneficiary outcomes (19),⏎compared with thousands of studies about AI performance.23 “Overall, among thousands⏎of studies [about the impact of AI decision-making on patient outcomes]…, only five⏎without a high risk of bias studied patient-relevant outcomes, on the basis of an externally⏎validated algorithm” and “[o]nly two showed at least one benefit, in depression treatment⏎and with regard to end-of-life care.” There are no studies on long-term patient outcomes.24⏎Other reports and studies have found widespread instances of harm: AI hallucinations,⏎delayed transplants for Black patients, inaccurate health information, and promotion of⏎disordered eating.25 In one example, “Physician-graders found the AI model made mistakes⏎when describing images and explaining how its decision-making led to the correct⏎answer.”26⏎And the studies showing positive impacts are mostly flawed - either they are not validated⏎or they do not reflect current medical study standards.27⏎20 W. Nicholson Price II, Medical AI and Contextual Bias, 33 HARV. J.L. & TECH. 65 (2019); Vindell⏎Washington et al., The HiTECH Era and the Path Forward, 377 NEW ENG. J. MED. 907 (2017)⏎21 Cong. Rsch. Serv., Artificial Intelligence in Health Care, R48319 (2024).⏎22 Id.⏎23 Christian Wilhelm et al., Benefits and Harms Associated with the Use of AI-Related Tools in Public⏎Health, 10 LANCET PUB. HEALTH (2025),⏎https://www.thelancet.com/journals/lanepe/article/PIIS2666-7762(24)00314-4/fulltext.⏎24 Id.⏎25 Kerstin Denecke et al., The Unexepected Harms of Artificial Intelligence in Healthcare: Reflections⏎on Four Real-World Cases, Vol. 325 STUDIES IN HEALTH TECH. & INFORMATICS (2025),⏎https://doi.org/10.3233/SHTI250219.⏎26 Nat’l Insts. of Health, NIH Findings Shed Light on Risks and Benefits of Integrating AI into⏎Medical Decision-Making (2023).⏎27 “[R]ecent developments more often enable implementation without causal evidence on benefits⏎of algorithmic technologies, which is at odds with established requirements to medical devices. As⏎with other medical technologies before, health authorities’ approvals should be tied to patient-⏎related evidence.” Wilhelm, supra note 23. Yet, “few AI tools have been evaluated by RCTs⏎[randomized clinical trials]” which is the gold standard of medical review. Derek C. Angus et al., AI,⏎Health, and Health Care Today and Tomorrow: The JAMA Summit Report on Artificial Intelligence,⏎334 JAMA 1650 (2025), https://doi.org/10.1001/jama.2025.18490. Frequently, the tests of⏎accuracy are not even complete. “[T]ools would need to be subjected to comparison on the same⏎independent test set that is representative of the target population, but such comparisons are not⏎7⏎⏎What we do know is that AI produces high rates of care denial, in some cases 16 times⏎higher than is typical, but “[n]o studies have compared rates of denials or wrongful denials⏎(those reversed on appeal) in reviews with and without AI, making it difficult to disentangle⏎potential causes of rising denial rates or assess the impacts of AI use.”28 This is especially⏎true in utilization management contexts, like prior authorization.29 Despite all of these well-⏎studied issues with data quality and denials, identifying and correcting errors is shifted onto⏎those with the least access to information and advocacy: patients.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns⏎do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption/use of AI in clinical⏎care?⏎Trust starts with transparency.30 Providers, patients, and caregivers need to know when⏎and how AI tools are used, and how these tools influence clinical decisions or coverage⏎determinations. According to the AMA, “Poor system design and inadequate workflow⏎considerations during implementation can also lead to alert fatigue and mistrust of the⏎system among patients and healthcare providers, undermining the intended benefits of⏎AI.”31 Before deployment of AI tools, national standards for transparency and disclosure⏎are essential, along with systems that collect and analyze disaggregated data to evaluate⏎how AI affects specific patient populations and outcomes.32 Transparency about data⏎sources is also critical to identify and correct bias, and federal oversight should incorporate⏎existing bias-assessment frameworks to ensure AI does not exacerbate inequities.33⏎At the same time, patients and caregivers express deep concern about opaque and time-⏎consuming appeals processes, particularly when AI is used in utilization management or⏎coverage decisions. AI-driven denials are often difficult to understand, with unclear clinical⏎criteria and insufficient explanations for denials. Simply asserting, or even requiring, that a⏎done.” C.J. Kelly et al., Key Challenges for Delivering Clinical Impact with Artificial Intelligence, 17⏎BMC MED 195 (2019).⏎28 Mello, supra note 15.⏎29 Jennifer Lubell, How AI Is Leading to More Prior Authorization Denials, Am. Med. Ass’n (Mar. 10,⏎2025), https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/prior-authorization/how-ai-leading-more-⏎prior-authorization-denials.⏎30 Tighe, supra note 16.⏎31 Id.⏎32 NHeLP ADS Principles, supra note 1; NAIC Model Bulletin (Dec. 4, 2023),⏎https://content.naic.org/sites/default/files/cmte-h-big-data-artificial-intelligence-wg-ai-model-⏎bulletin.pdf.pdf; Angus, supra note 27.⏎33 R. Agarawal et al., Addressing Algorithmic Bias and the Perpetuation of Health Inequities: An AI⏎Bias Aware Framework, HEALTH POLICY & TECH. (Mar. 2023),⏎https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S2211883722001095.⏎8⏎⏎“human is in the loop” is not enough.34 Meaningful safeguards require truly independent⏎medical judgment (absent any financial incentives), full disclosure of all clinical standards⏎used in the decision-making process, and clear notice to patients about the specific reasons⏎that triggered a denial. The appeal processes must also work, and in a timely way. And⏎there must be further accountability mechanisms from regulators to address both individual⏎and systemic issues. Without these protections, appeals become procedural hurdles rather⏎than genuine opportunities to correct errors or appropriate denials.⏎Automation bias and financial incentives are equally concerning. Clinicians may defer to AI-⏎generated recommendations even when those recommendations conflict with their⏎judgment, particularly when those clinicians lack training on AI systems’ limitations or are⏎unaware of how the tool generates outputs. Patients worry that AI tools, especially those⏎used by payers, are designed in ways that favor cost containment over medical necessity.⏎Uncertainty about accountability compounds these risks. Stakeholders remain confused⏎about who bears ultimate responsibility for AI-influenced decisions and whether they have⏎meaningful protection if/when systems fail to meet standards.⏎Finally, beneficiaries and caregivers need robust regulatory protections, oversight, and⏎enforcement. This includes timely appeals and grievance processes grounded in⏎independent medical judgment. It also includes strong data protections and compliance⏎with privacy laws. And it includes effective oversight, like the authority to investigate AI-⏎related harms and impose penalties on bad actors, as well as enforcement of existing⏎protections in use cases like prior authorization. Without enforceable standards,⏎transparency, and accountability, patients and the general public fear that AI adoption will⏎entrench existing problems in the health care system, rather than address them.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎A. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and⏎their use in clinical care?⏎Published findings about the impact of AI tools on health care are widespread, and they⏎broadly support the same conclusion: these tools can do great harm to impacted⏎individuals and must be used with care, if at all. Some types of tools and use have greater⏎risk than others. Although there are no universally agreed upon mechanisms for monitoring⏎AI tools, there are thru lines that say transparency, accountability, data collection, and⏎other robust guardrails should be part of any AI use, especially for higher risk uses like⏎health care decisions.⏎34 See, e.g., Hannah Quay-de la Vallee & Kevin De Liban, CDT & Benefits Tech Advocacy Hub,⏎Rethinking the Loop: Encircling Public Benefits AI with Human Oversight 11-12 (Sept. 2025),⏎https://cdt.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/09/2025-09-22-Humans-in-the-Loop-CDT-Civic-Tech-⏎report-final.pdf (discussing the limitations of humans in the loop as an accountability mechanism for⏎AI systems).⏎9⏎⏎Existing research shows that AI and other automated systems have real risks to their use⏎that can cause great harm. Discriminatory bias can be baked into these tools; in the health⏎care context, this innate bias can exacerbate health disparities and impact patient⏎outcomes.35 AI tools also frequently get things wrong.36 For instance, an AI tool used to⏎predict a given patient’s care needs may fail to consider all factors pertinent to their needs;⏎a tool used to predict the success of a prior authorization or reimbursement request may⏎deprioritize claims similar to those that have failed in the past without performing an⏎independent assessment of their merit, reinforcing payers’ bad behavior and leaving⏎patients with little recourse.37 AI tools and other algorithms engage in opaque decision-⏎making, making it unreasonably difficult to challenge adverse decisions.38 The humans⏎35 See generally AHRQ, supra note 3 (collecting research on racial bias in clinical algorithms);⏎Elizabeth Edwards, David Machledt, Skyler Rosellini, & Liz McCaman, NHeLP Comments on AHRQ⏎Request for Information on the Use of Clinical Algorithms That Have the Potential to Introduce⏎Racial/Ethnic Bias Into Healthcare Delivery (May 4, 2021), https://healthlaw.org/resource/nhelp-⏎ahrq-comments/ [hereinafter “NHeLP AHRQ Comments”] (discussing various types of bias in clinical⏎algorithms); see also, e.g., Mahmud Omar et al., Sociodemographic Biases in Medical Decision⏎Making by Large Language Models, 31 NATURE MED. 1873 (Apr. 2025),⏎https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-025-03626-6 (disparate pain management⏎recommendations from an LLM for LGBTQIA+ individuals); Crystal T. Chang et al., Evaluating Anti-⏎LGBTQIA+ Medical Bias in Large Language Models, 4 PLOS DIG. HEALTH 9, e0001001 (Sept. 8,⏎2025) https://journals.plos.org/digitalhealth/article?id=10.1371/journal.pdig.0001001 (prompts⏎describing clinical scenarios with “LGBTQIA+ identity terms” elicited more severe bias from LLMs);⏎Melissa D. McCradden et al., Hannah E. Knight et al., Challenging Racism in the Use of Health Data,⏎3:3 THE LANCET E144 (Feb. 3, 2021), https://www.thelancet.com/journals/landig/article/PIIS2589-⏎7500(21)00019-4/fulltext (racial bias in the use of health data generally); Ethical Limitations of⏎Algorithmic Fairness in Health Care Machine Learning, 2 THE LANCET E222 (May 2020),⏎https://www.thelancet.com/journals/landig/article/PIIS2589-7500(20)30065-0/fulltext; Ziad⏎Obermeyer et al., Dissecting Racial Bias in an Algorithm Used to Manage the Health of Populations,⏎366 SCIENCE 447 (2019), https://escholarship.org/uc/item/6h92v832#main (racial bias in population⏎health algorithm); Darshali A. Vyas et al., Challenging the Use of the Vaginal Birth After Cesarean⏎Section Calculator, 29-3 WOMEN’S HEALTH ISSUES 201 (2019),⏎https://www.whijournal.com/article/S1049-3867(19)30098-2/pdf; Ibram X. Kendi, There is No Such⏎Thing as Race in Health-care Algorithms, 1:8 THE LANCET E375 (Dec. 2019),⏎https://www.thelancet.com/journals/landig/article/PIIS2589-7500(19)30201-8/fulltext (history of⏎racial bias in health care can be perpetuated and amplified by AI tools); Foad Hamidi et al., Gender⏎Recognition or Gender Reductionism? The Social Implications of Automatic Gender Recognition⏎Systems, CHI HUM. FACTORS IN COMPUTING SYS. (Apr. 2018) (impact of “Automatic Gender⏎Recognition” (AGR) algorithms on transgender individuals); Anupam Datta et al., Proxy⏎Discrimination in Data Driven Systems (2017), https://arxiv.org/pdf/1707.08120.pdf (“Machine⏎learnt systems inherit biases against protected classes, historically disparaged groups, from training⏎data," which can be difficult to see because “they rely on subtle correlations discovered by training⏎algorithms”).⏎36 See, e.g., Qiao Jin et al., Hidden Flaws Behind Expert-Level Accuracy of Multimodal GPT-4 Vision⏎in Medicine, 7 NPJ DIG. MED. 190 (2024) (GPT-4V was comparably accurate to human physicians⏎when asked multiple-choice questions about imaging it had reviewed, but its rationales were often⏎flawed).⏎37 See Mello, supra note 15.⏎38 See NHeLP AHRQ Comments, supra note 34, at 3 (“The rise of ADS has exacerbated the problem⏎of ‘black box’ decision-making. This has multiple impacts. A person denied care may simply be told⏎10⏎⏎responsible for operating and monitoring these tools are also fallible. Automation bias – the⏎tendency of humans to accept a computer-generated solution as correct irrespective of its⏎accuracy and ignore non-automated contradictory information – is a significant risk and can⏎have catastrophic consequences if not addressed and mitigated.39 Finally, public mistrust of⏎AI in health care remains high, impacting its utility.40⏎AI tools in health care certainly have the potential to be helpful. They can automate simple⏎tasks and reduce administrative burdens, “free[ing] up human capacity,” allowing providers⏎and agency staff to serve people with “dignity and efficiency.”41 But safeguards are⏎necessary to realize the full potential of these tools while minimizing their potential harm.⏎Additional testing and data collection is imperative. AI tools should be studied for their⏎impact on patient outcomes, construed broadly to ensure that all aspects of the health care⏎experience are meaningfully improved without harm. Any strategy to deploy these tools⏎must include robust guardrails and protections.42 In sum: moving too fast magnifies the⏎already-significant risk of harm posed by health care-related AI applications. Innovation is⏎often a net positive, but innovation for innovation’s sake should not be the focus when it⏎comes to developing and expanding health-related AI.⏎the results of the ADS without any information about what about their condition triggered the⏎denial, which may deter appealing the denial”) (citations omitted).⏎39 See, e.g., Giuseppe Romeo & Daniela Conti, Exploring Automation Bias in Human-AI⏎Collaboration: A Review and Implications for Explainable AI, 41 AI & SOC’Y 259, 262 (Jul. 2025);⏎Lauren Kahn et al., AI Safety and Automation Bias: The Downside of Human-in-the-Loop 6, CTR.⏎FOR SEC. & EMERGING TECH. (Nov. 2024) (commenting that the “challenge of automation bias has⏎only grown with the introduction of progressively more sophisticated AI-enabled systems and tools⏎across different application areas” and noting failure to heed AI warnings); see also Mello et al.,⏎supra note 15, at 9 (research team conducting ethical assessments of AI tools at Stanford Health⏎Care found that some health system staff “could not explain in even basic terms how generative AI⏎creates output, did not know that AI could be biased, and could not name any ways in which the⏎tools might have performance problems”); Moustafa Abdelwanis et al., Exploring the Risks of⏎Automation Bias in Healthcare Artificial Intelligence Applications: A Bowtie Analysis, 5 J. SAFETY SCI.⏎& RESILIENCE 460, 463 (Dec. 2024) (commenting that automation bias poses a substantial risk to⏎patients’ lives through medical errors such as misdiagnosis and incorrect prescriptions).⏎40 See note 7, supra.⏎41 See Danny Mintz & Jennifer Thom, Identifying Use Cases for AI in Medicaid Delivery, CODE FOR⏎AMERICA (Jan. 21, 2026), https://codeforamerica.org/news/identifying-use-cases-for-ai-in-medicaid-⏎delivery/.⏎42 For instance, any adopter of an AI tool should conduct pilot studies before the tool is⏎implemented and develop “evaluation criteria and success metrics” to ensure the tool is effective⏎and does not amplify existing health inequity. See Sophia Fox-Sowell, Can AI Help States Prepare⏎for Medicaid Changes Coming in 2027?, STATESCOOP (Jan. 22, 2026), https://statescoop.com/states-⏎medicaid-changes-hr1-ai/. And HHS should develop a multifaceted accountability framework⏎including meaningful human-in-the-loop oversight and radical transparency about the tool itself and⏎how it works (including known weaknesses and risks, and how vendors will monitor the tool’s⏎performance); see, e.g., CHAI (noting that similar strategies will help improve public trust in health⏎care AI tools).⏎11⏎⏎Conclusion⏎For the reasons stated above, we urge HHS to balance carefully the support of innovation⏎with the needed protections for people affected by AI in clinical decision making. We direct⏎HHS to the research we have cited and made available through active links, the full text of⏎which should be part of the record for this RFI. We appreciate the opportunity to provide⏎comment on this issue. If you have any questions, please feel free to contact Elizabeth⏎Edwards, edwards@healthlaw.org, Brit Vanneman, vanneman@healthlaw.org, or Shandra⏎Hartly, hartly@healthlaw.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Elizabeth G. Taylor⏎Executive Director⏎12♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0420♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 22, 2026⏎HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎This comment is submitted anonymously by a group of healthcare technology professionals and⏎clinical innovators. We have chosen to submit these insights without individual or organizational⏎identification to ensure the feedback remains focused on the structural barriers to innovation. Our⏎intent is to provide a candid assessment of the current regulatory and technical landscape without⏎drawing undue attention to specific partnerships or individual organizations from larger entities⏎that may be impacted by the policy changes suggested herein.⏎Our perspective is informed by extensive experience in deploying AI-driven patient navigation⏎across diverse health systems. We appreciate the opportunity to provide feedback on how⏎regulatory frameworks and interoperability standards can better support the integration of agentic⏎AI to improve patient access and health system efficiency.⏎I. Regulation⏎The current regulatory environment could evolve to better address the "hidden" barriers to AI⏎adoption. While clinical safety remains paramount, a significant bottleneck exists in the⏎accessibility of the logic and administrative data required to navigate patients effectively through⏎complex health systems.⏎● Transparency and API Mandates: HHS might consider interpreting "information⏎blocking" regulations (e.g., 45 CFR Part 171) to include not just patient clinical records,⏎but also the administrative decision trees and scheduling logic developed by health⏎systems. Currently, these logic "knowledge bases" are often locked within proprietary⏎EHR systems, which can prevent AI agents from accurately routing patients to the⏎optimal care setting.⏎● Proportionate Oversight: We suggest a regulatory posture that prioritizes explainability.⏎AI navigation tools could be encouraged to demonstrate that their outputs are "tunable"⏎by the health system’s clinical leadership, helping ensure the AI adheres to the local⏎standard of care.⏎II. Reimbursement⏎To further accelerate AI adoption, payment policy could move beyond "fee-for-service" logic,⏎which may lack the agility to cover digital navigation.⏎● Incentivizing High-Value Navigation: HHS could prioritize payment models that⏎reward the reduction of "leakage" and "misrouting." When a patient is navigated to the⏎correct care level (such as Urgent Care instead of the Emergency Room) by an AI agent,⏎⏎the payer realizes immediate savings. Reimbursement models could be designed to reflect⏎this value.⏎● Liability and Coverage: CMS could provide clearer pathways for the "professional fee"⏎component of AI-augmented triage. This would help ensure that health systems are not⏎financially penalized when they transition from manual, call-center-based workflows to⏎more efficient, clinically-validated AI agents.⏎III. Research & Development⏎HHS might focus R&D investment on Implementation Science, specifically regarding how AI⏎integrates with existing healthcare "gatekeeping" functions.⏎● Public-Private Partnerships: We recommend that HHS leverage CRADAs to test⏎agentic navigation within large-scale environments like the Defense Health Agency⏎(DHA). These environments serve as ideal "sandboxes" for proving how AI can⏎standardize referral qualifications across a massive and geographically diverse⏎population.⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎1. Biggest Barriers to Private Sector Innovation The single largest barrier to innovation in⏎patient navigation appears to be the lack of interoperability with administrative "knowledge"⏎data. While clinical data like labs and notes is increasingly accessible via FHIR APIs, the⏎scheduling logic and specialty-care decision trees often remain siloed. EHR vendors sometimes⏎decline to expose APIs to these rules, even when requested by the health systems who "own" that⏎knowledge. This can force AI developers to build redundant, manual workarounds, which⏎potentially slows deployment and increases costs.⏎2. Regulatory and Programmatic Design Changes HHS could consider prioritizing the⏎following updates:⏎● Update 45 CFR § 170.315: Expand certification criteria to suggest that EHR vendors⏎expose APIs for administrative scheduling rules and provider-logic trees.⏎● Enforce Information Blocking Rules: Clarify that refusing to provide API access to a⏎health system's own clinical or administrative logic (when requested by that system for a⏎third-party AI integration) may constitute a barrier to innovation.⏎6. Successes and Shortfalls of Deployed AI⏎● Successes: Agentic navigation has successfully helped patients across all 50 states⏎navigate to the correct level of care, reducing the burden on overstretched call centers at⏎several of the nation’s largest Integrated Delivery Networks (IDNs) and regional health⏎systems..⏎● Shortfalls: These tools can fall short of their full potential when they cannot "talk" to the⏎internal scheduling logic of the EHR in real-time. This often necessitates a "safe handoff"⏎⏎(asynchronous) rather than a "closed-loop" (real-time) booking, which can degrade the⏎patient experience.⏎7. Influence and Administrative Hurdles Within health systems, Chief Information Officers⏎(CIOs) and Chief Medical Officers (CMOs) typically hold the most influence. However, the⏎primary administrative hurdle is the reconciliation of scheduling rules. Health systems go⏎through rigorous processes to define how a patient should be routed for sub-specialty care. When⏎an EHR vendor does not expose the API for these rules, the AI implementation requires a⏎"re-build" of that logic, creating significant administrative duplication.⏎8. Enhanced Interoperability Interoperability could move beyond data types (what the patient⏎has) to logic types (where the patient should go). Specifically:⏎● Data Standards: HHS might support the development of standards for "Care Routing⏎Logic" to complement existing FHIR clinical resources.⏎● Benchmarking: Standardized benchmarking for "Triage Accuracy" would allow the⏎private sector to demonstrate that AI-driven navigation is as safe as (or safer than)⏎traditional nurse advice lines.⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Concerns Patients primarily seek clarity and convenience. They can⏎be overwhelmed by "choice paralysis" when seeking care, and AI agents address this by⏎providing a single "digital front door." However, caregivers remain concerned about "dead ends"⏎where an AI provides a recommendation but cannot facilitate the actual appointment due to the⏎API barriers mentioned above.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Anonymous Industry Stakeholders♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0421♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Interstella, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Interstella♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment on “HHS Health Sector AI RFI”⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Office of the Secretary⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎RIN: 0955-AA13⏎Federal Register Document Number: 2025-23641⏎Docket ID: To be assigned by Regulations.gov⏎Submitted by: Interstella⏎Date: January 21, 2026⏎Page 1 of 25⏎⏎Introduction⏎Interstella appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) in clinical care. As a health data and technology company focused on real-time⏎data integration and AI readiness, we share HHS’s vision of a “forward-leaning, industry-⏎supportive, and secure” approach to AI in healthcare.⏎We believe AI’s transformative potential in clinical care can only be realized by addressing⏎foundational challenges in data infrastructure, governance, and incentives. In this response, we⏎detail barriers and provide recommendations across regulation, reimbursement, and research &⏎development (R&D), supplemented by high-impact use cases and policy suggestions. Our⏎comments emphasize that AI systems fail or succeed based largely on the data that powers them,⏎and thus a modernized data ecosystem is critical to safe and effective AI adoption in healthcare.⏎Interstella Background: Interstella is dedicated to turning raw, fragmented health information⏎into real-time, AI-ready intelligence. Our experience developing the Lynqsys data platform and⏎Data Refinery as a Service (DRaaS) solutions gives us practical insight into the data quality,⏎interoperability, and timeliness issues that currently limit AI’s impact in clinical settings.⏎We approach this RFI from the perspective of those “building, buying, evaluating, and using”⏎AI tools for care delivery, and those who wish to do so but face barriers (as noted by HHS). We⏎offer both policy recommendations and use case-driven examples to illustrate how HHS can⏎accelerate AI adoption while upholding patient safety, equity, and trust.⏎Barriers to AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Data Readiness Gap: The core barrier we observe is that AI innovation is outpacing the⏎healthcare system’s data readiness. Federal initiatives increasingly rely on advanced analytics⏎and AI, yet most healthcare data ecosystems operate with significant limitations:⏎• Delayed Data Availability: Clinical data are often ingested and aggregated in batch⏎processes with lag times of days or weeks, rather than in real-time streams. This delay⏎stifles AI tools that require up-to-date information for decision support or predictive⏎analytics. For example, quality measures or care gap alerts powered by AI cannot⏎function optimally if data arrives long after the point of care.⏎• Inconsistent Data Quality & Provenance: Healthcare data remains “fragmented,⏎biased, and often a mess”, leading to noise, duplication, and gaps that undermine AI⏎algorithms. Data may come from diverse sources (EHRs, claims, devices, patient-⏎reported inputs) with inconsistent formats and unknown provenance, making it hard for⏎AI models to trust and interpret.⏎Most failed AI initiatives are not caused by faulty algorithms or lack of vision, they fail⏎because the data foundation is not fit for purpose. Indeed, studies estimate up to 85% of⏎Page 2 of 25⏎⏎AI projects fail due to poor data quality or volume issues. In healthcare, this problem is⏎acute: “Without large, high-quality data sets, it can be difficult to build useful AIs,” and⏎providers are slower to adopt AI when data are incomplete or siloed.⏎• Limited Support for Unstructured and Behavioral Data: Many current systems⏎handle structured data (e.g. diagnoses, lab results) but have limited capability to⏎incorporate unstructured data (clinical notes, images) or behavioral and social health data.⏎This is a barrier for AI, which thrives on rich data contexts.⏎Critical information about a patient’s mental health, social determinants, or care⏎preferences may be documented in text or external systems and thus invisible to AI-⏎driven tools. The lack of integration for behavioral health data, for instance, makes it⏎difficult to apply AI for outcome measurement in mental health or to coordinate holistic⏎care in models like Certified Community Behavioral Health Clinics (CCBHCs).⏎• Post-Hoc Governance vs Inline Controls: Data governance (privacy protections,⏎quality checks, consent management) is often applied after data aggregation or as a⏎periodic audit, rather than inline at the point of data creation and exchange. This reactive⏎approach means potential issues, from data errors to privacy risks, are caught late.⏎AI systems require trusted, well-characterized data streams; without proactive⏎governance baked into data pipelines, organizations either inhibit data sharing or expose⏎themselves to compliance risks. In short, data governance that isn’t real-time and⏎automated becomes a bottleneck to scalable AI.⏎• Data Pipelines Not Designed for AI Consumption: Traditional health IT systems were⏎built for billing or record-keeping, not for feeding machine learning models. Data may be⏎locked in PDF reports, faxed forms, or legacy databases that AI cannot readily parse.⏎Even within modern EHRs, data exchange via interfaces or reports can miss context (e.g.⏎relationships between data points) that AI algorithms need. Interoperability challenges⏎exacerbate this, “Electronic Health Record systems are largely not compatible across⏎different hospitals or providers,” leading to localized data rather than a comprehensive⏎patient view. AI models trained on narrow, siloed data may not generalize, limiting their⏎utility.⏎These data-related barriers create technical hurdles and raise regulatory, equity, and trust risks.⏎An AI tool is only as good as the data it learns from; if those data are incomplete, biased, or⏎delayed, the tool’s recommendations could be unsafe or inequitable. In sum, “AI systems do not⏎fail because of algorithms; they fail because of data.”⏎Addressing data readiness is foundational to everything else, from ensuring algorithmic fairness⏎to convincing healthcare professionals and patients to trust AI. We encourage HHS to treat data⏎quality, interoperability, and timeliness as strategic priorities in all AI-related policies (as HHS’s⏎own AI Strategy recognizes).⏎Page 3 of 25⏎⏎Other Key Barriers: In addition to data readiness, we echo the other barriers identified by⏎experts and prior HHS inquiries:⏎• Lack of Trust and Transparency: Many AI algorithms, especially complex machine-⏎learning models, are “black boxes” that clinicians find hard to interpret. The opacity of⏎AI decisions can erode provider trust and willingness to use AI in care. Clinicians remain⏎ultimately accountable for patient outcomes, so they are cautious about relying on tools⏎they don’t fully understand.⏎Without assurances of transparency or robust validation, frontline providers often default⏎to familiar (non-AI) solutions.⏎Building trust requires both technological solutions (e.g. explainable AI, rigorous⏎validation) and cultural change (education, success stories, engagement of clinicians in⏎AI development). We note that transparency, privacy protections, and rigorous evaluation⏎have been identified as essential elements of trustworthy AI in healthcare.⏎• Regulatory Uncertainty: Current regulations have not fully caught up with AI, creating⏎uncertainty. Privacy rules (like HIPAA) and data use policies can make it difficult to⏎aggregate and share the data needed to develop robust AI models.⏎Approval pathways for AI, particularly software not regulated as a medical device, are⏎unclear; developers are unsure how to demonstrate safety/effectiveness, and providers are⏎unsure what oversight or certifications to look for.⏎Furthermore, liability concerns loom large: healthcare organizations worry that if an AI⏎makes a faulty recommendation, they will face legal risk with uncertain liability-sharing⏎between developers and users. This “chilling effect” causes risk-averse stances, slowing⏎adoption. We explore regulatory solutions in the next section.⏎• Misaligned Incentives in Payment: The fee-for-service reimbursement model often fails⏎to reward (or even penalizes) innovations that improve efficiency or quality. AI tools that⏎prevent hospitalizations or automate routine tasks might reduce billable services in the⏎short term, disincentivizing adoption by providers under traditional payment.⏎Meanwhile, those bearing long-term risk (payers or ACOs) have incentives to use AI for⏎prevention and cost reduction, but they face their own hurdles in getting such tools⏎approved and reimbursed.⏎This misalignment leads to patchy adoption, e.g. larger systems or value-based⏎organizations invest in AI, while others do not. As HHS notes, “legacy payment systems”⏎can diminish the promise of AI through inertia. We discuss payment reforms below.⏎• Workforce and Workflow Integration: Successful AI adoption requires⏎complementary process changes and training. Introducing an AI-driven tool means little⏎Page 4 of 25⏎⏎if clinical workflows are not adjusted to incorporate the AI’s output at the right time and⏎place.⏎Many hospitals lack personnel with AI expertise (data scientists, informaticists) to⏎champion these changes. Additionally, some clinicians fear AI could replace or de-skill⏎their roles (for example, radiologists hearing that AI will read images). This can create⏎resistance or lack of engagement with new tools.⏎Even when leadership is supportive, administrative hurdles like lengthy procurement,⏎integration costs, or lack of IT support can slow down AI deployments. In our⏎experience, the roles most influential in AI adoption include the Chief Medical⏎Information Officer (CMIO), Chief Data or Digital Officer, and clinical department heads⏎who see a clear need.⏎Hospitals with professional management and integrated salaried structures tend to be⏎more proactive in deploying AI than those with decentralized or physician-led⏎management. HHS should consider how to educate and empower these decision-makers⏎to be AI champions.⏎In summary, the biggest barriers to private-sector innovation in health AI are data-related⏎limitations, regulatory uncertainty (including privacy and liability issues), misaligned payment⏎incentives, and lack of trust. Overcoming these requires coordinated action.⏎We recommend that HHS focus on improving the health data foundation as a first-class⏎objective, because doing so will mitigate many other barriers (improving data sharing alleviates⏎privacy and access issues, high-quality data enables better algorithms, etc.).⏎Below, we detail specific regulatory, reimbursement, and R&D actions that can accelerate AI⏎adoption while safeguarding patients. We also provide example use cases illustrating how these⏎changes can yield tangible improvements in care delivery.⏎Regulatory and Governance Recommendations⏎HHS’s role as the nation’s principal health regulator is pivotal in shaping an environment where⏎AI can innovate rapidly and safely. We agree with HHS’s goal of a regulatory posture on AI that⏎is predictable, proportionate to risk, and protective of patients’ rights and privacy. To achieve⏎this, we recommend:⏎1. Clarify AI Oversight for Non-Device Software: Many AI tools in clinical care do not meet⏎FDA’s definition of a medical device (for example, clinician-facing decision support that allows⏎independent review, or operational AI for scheduling and resource allocation).⏎This creates a gray area with “novel legal and implementation issues” around accountability, as⏎noted in the RFI. HHS (through ONC, CMS, OCR, etc.) should provide guidance on⏎expectations for these non-FDA-regulated AI tools. Specifically:⏎Page 5 of 25⏎⏎• Liability and Indemnification: We urge HHS to convene stakeholders (providers,⏎developers, malpractice insurers) to develop guidance or safe harbor principles for AI⏎use. Clinicians should know when they can rely on an AI tool and how liability is⏎apportioned if the AI is flawed.⏎One approach is a “qualified AI” safe harbor; if an AI tool meets certain HHS-endorsed⏎standards (for transparency, validation, etc.), then providers who use it as intended could⏎have mitigated liability. This would encourage use of vetted tools. Conversely,⏎developers of such tools might be expected to carry liability insurance or indemnify⏎providers in cases of egregious failure, creating accountability on both sides.⏎Clear rules on “who is liable if something goes wrong” would likely increase adoption.⏎HHS could work with Congress if needed to adjust liability frameworks for AI in⏎healthcare (e.g., exploration of federal Tort Claims Act extensions or other mechanisms⏎for AI-related harms).⏎• Privacy and Data Use Clarity: HHS should update HIPAA and related guidance to⏎explicitly address AI development and deployment. For instance, clarify how⏎providers/payers can share de-identified or synthetic data for AI training without running⏎afoul of privacy rules.⏎Consider promoting data trusts or safe data enclaves for AI development, perhaps⏎through OCR guidance that such arrangements, with proper safeguards, are encouraged.⏎HHS’s AI Strategy already emphasizes “fostering data standards and datasets to bolster⏎their usability for AI… while maintaining individual privacy”.⏎Building on this, HHS could create model frameworks for data stewardship that balance⏎innovation with privacy (e.g., datasets that are rich enough for AI but managed under⏎strong governance and audit). Clear guidelines can reduce the fear that sharing data for⏎AI equals a privacy violation.⏎• Security and Compliance: Many non-device AI tools still interface with protected⏎health information. HHS can help by streamlining compliance. For example, template⏎Business Associate Agreements for AI vendors, guidance on using cloud-based AI under⏎HIPAA, and mapping AI practices to existing regulations (like how to apply 42 CFR Part⏎2 for substance abuse data in AI or ensuring ADA compliance in AI-driven⏎communications). By addressing these now, HHS preempts future confusion as AI use⏎grows.⏎2. Modernize Clinical Decision Support (CDS) Regulation: Current FDA guidance (per the⏎21st Century Cures Act and FDA’s CDS guidance) draws a line between regulated “medical⏎device” software and unregulated CDS. HHS should ensure that developers and providers clearly⏎understand this line.⏎We recommend HHS (through FDA collaboration and ONC) establish a central information hub⏎Page 6 of 25⏎⏎on what kinds of AI applications require FDA clearance and which do not. This could include an⏎AI Tool Classification Framework that maps common use cases: e.g., diagnostic algorithms⏎(regulated SaMD), vs. workflow optimizers (unregulated), vs. patient self-care chatbots (gray⏎area). By providing case studies or decision trees, HHS can reduce uncertainty.⏎For those AI tools that are medical devices, HHS should support FDA in streamlining approval⏎without compromising safety. For instance, expanding programs for predetermined change⏎control plans (PCCPs) and real-world performance monitoring to allow AI models to⏎continuously improve post-approval.⏎For non-device tools, HHS could work with industry on voluntary certification (see below)⏎instead of regulation, focusing on transparency and oversight at the user-organization level⏎(hospital governance).⏎3. Support AI Governance Frameworks in Healthcare Organizations: Regulation is not just⏎about federal rules; it’s also about internal governance where AI is deployed. We applaud efforts⏎by the private sector, such as the Joint Commission’s new guidance (2025) on the responsible⏎use of AI in healthcare.⏎That framework, developed with the Coalition for Health AI, outlines seven core elements (from⏎formal AI governance committees to patient transparency and bias monitoring) for safe AI⏎implementation.⏎HHS should endorse and build upon such frameworks, perhaps by integrating them into ONC⏎certification or CMS conditions of participation in the future. Specifically, HHS could:⏎• Coordinate with the Joint Commission and others to operationalize AI governance⏎playbooks and create voluntary certification programs for health systems. For example,⏎HHS could fund the development of a “Trustworthy AI in Clinical Care” accreditation⏎that hospitals can obtain by meeting best practices (similar to how HIMSS stages⏎measure EMR adoption).⏎This aligns with the RFI’s query about supporting private sector accreditation and⏎credentialing (Question 5). Federal backing would lend credibility and spur adoption of⏎these standards.⏎• Issue guidance or requirements for federal healthcare programs (Medicare, Medicaid) to⏎ensure participating providers have basic AI governance. This might start as⏎recommendations and evolve into conditions for certain innovation models.⏎Areas to cover: having an AI oversight committee, conducting algorithmic bias⏎assessments, educating staff and informing patients about AI use (the Joint Commission⏎calls for patient notification and consent when AI directly impacts care).⏎Page 7 of 25⏎⏎Such governance expectations, even if not yet mandated, signal the industry to develop⏎AI responsibly.⏎4. Adjust and Harmonize Regulations to Remove Unintended Barriers: We encourage HHS⏎to review specific regulations that, while well-intentioned, may hinder AI adoption. Some⏎examples and suggestions:⏎• Revisit Data Blocking and Sharing Rules: The 21st Century Cures Act information⏎blocking rules promote data sharing, which is great for AI training and deployment. HHS⏎should ensure these rules explicitly allow sharing data with AI developers or between⏎providers for AI purposes (with proper patient authorization or de-identification).⏎Also, consider expanding required data elements (USCDI) to include more data types⏎useful for AI, like imaging, free-text clinical notes, and patient-generated data. Enhanced⏎interoperability of specific data types (imaging, claims, genomics, social determinants)⏎would “widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate AI development,” as⏎the RFI posits.⏎For instance, better access to claims data could allow AI to identify high-risk patients⏎across the continuum of care; richer social and behavioral data standards would enable AI⏎to personalize care plans.⏎We note CMS’s recent Interoperability Framework as a positive step: it will require⏎networks to share clinical and claims data via standardized APIs (FHIR) within 24 hours,⏎dramatically improving timely data availability.⏎HHS should continue to remove technical and economic disincentives to interoperability,⏎making near-real-time data exchange the norm in healthcare. This directly addresses the⏎current batch-processing paradigm and will empower AI applications that need up-to-date⏎data (e.g., sepsis early warning systems, dynamic care coordination tools).⏎• HIPAA Safe Harbors for AI Development: As mentioned, clarify pathways for using⏎patient data to develop AI. HHS could create a safe harbor under HIPAA for uses of fully⏎de-identified data (perhaps certified by a third party) in bona fide research and⏎development of AI, even if the resulting tool will be commercial.⏎Today, some covered entities are overly conservative, fearing that any data sharing might⏎violate HIPAA. Explicit encouragement and examples (e.g., “It is permissible to share⏎de-identified EHR datasets with an AI developer for algorithm training, under XYZ⏎conditions”) in OCR guidance would be helpful.⏎Also, update CFR 42 Part 2 in partnership with SAMHSA to align substance use data⏎sharing rules with modern AI-driven care coordination (recent law changes are moving in⏎this direction). AI that coordinates behavioral health care (like predicting relapse risk)⏎can only be effective if data can be shared within care teams under clear, privacy-⏎Page 8 of 25⏎⏎protected frameworks.⏎• Streamline IRB and Research Regulations for AI Studies: Many healthcare AI tools⏎need continuous learning and improvement, which blurs the line between quality⏎improvement and research. HHS should consider guidance on when AI⏎development/validation constitutes human subjects research requiring IRB oversight.⏎Overly burdensome interpretations could slow down needed real-world evaluation.⏎Developing central IRB models or data commons for AI could accelerate evaluation⏎while maintaining ethics.⏎• Enable Sandbox or Pilot Programs: HHS might use its authority (through CMS⏎Innovation Center or ONC) to create regulatory sandboxes where healthcare⏎organizations can pilot AI solutions with certain regulatory flexibilities.⏎For example, a hospital system could apply to test an AI tool that uses novel data⏎integration, under a time-limited program where HHS waives certain documentation or⏎reporting rules, provided strong monitoring is in place. Such sandboxes, coupled with⏎rigorous evaluation, can demonstrate safe ways to adjust regulations in the long run.⏎In summary, HHS should reduce uncertainty by providing clear rules of the road for AI. This⏎includes clarifying data sharing permissions, aligning AI oversight with risk levels, and⏎promoting internal governance standards.⏎By revisiting privacy, security, and liability issues in light of AI, HHS can create a predictable⏎environment that “enables rapid innovation while protecting patients”. We believe these⏎regulatory adjustments, focused especially on data and trust, will unlock innovation by giving⏎both AI developers and healthcare users confidence to move forward.⏎Payment Policy and Reimbursement Incentives⏎Payment policy can drive or stall the adoption of AI in clinical care. We agree with HHS’s⏎assessment that current reimbursement systems contain “inherent flaws” that may⏎unintentionally discourage high-value innovations.⏎To ensure AI’s promises are realized, HHS should modernize payment models to reward⏎effective AI use, reduce financial friction, and align incentives across stakeholders. Our⏎recommendations:⏎1. Create Clear Reimbursement Pathways for AI-Enabled Services: One of the “biggest⏎barriers” is the lack of clear payment mechanisms for AI-powered interventions.⏎If a clinician uses an AI tool to, say, analyze a radiology image or predict a patient’s⏎deterioration, there is often no distinct billing code or reimbursement for that activity (especially⏎if it replaces a conventional service). We recommend:⏎Page 9 of 25⏎⏎• Establish CPT codes or modifiers for AI-assisted services. Engage the AMA CPT⏎process to create codes for procedures where AI is used. For example, a radiologist using⏎FDA-cleared AI for image analysis could append a modifier indicating AI assistance,⏎which could be reimbursed with a small add-on payment for the validated efficiency or⏎accuracy gain.⏎CMS could then decide to cover these codes. Early examples in this direction include⏎CPT codes for autonomous AI detection of diabetic retinopathy. HHS should accelerate⏎and expand such coding to cover various AI scenarios (AI-assisted pathology, AI-driven⏎care coordination, etc.), signaling to providers that they can get reimbursed when using⏎proven AI tools.⏎• Bundle AI into Value-Based Payments. Under alternative payment models (APMs) like⏎accountable care or Medicare Advantage, the business case for AI is stronger (preventing⏎a hospitalization saves money that the provider-network can keep).⏎HHS should integrate AI adoption into value-based programs by, for instance, providing⏎allowances or bonuses for AI investments that demonstrably improve outcomes. CMS⏎Innovation Center could pilot contracts where participants who deploy AI for specific⏎high-impact use cases (e.g., reducing readmissions through predictive analytics) receive⏎shared savings or upfront funding to do so.⏎The key is to tie AI to outcomes: if it lowers costs or improves quality, allow those⏎benefits to translate into financial rewards for the adopters.⏎• Coverage with Evidence Development (CED) for AI. For certain novel AI⏎technologies (especially those that are high-cost or high-impact), CMS could use a CED⏎approach – cover the AI tool for Medicare beneficiaries conditionally while data is⏎gathered on its effectiveness.⏎This has been used for some devices and could be applied, say, to AI-based diagnostic⏎software or digital therapeutics. It encourages early adoption in a controlled way and⏎speeds the evidence generation needed for broader coverage.⏎2. Align Payment Incentives with Quality and Prevention: We note that many AI tools⏎promise long-term savings by improving prevention or care management, but under fee-for-⏎service those savings are not realized by the provider.⏎HHS should continue shifting incentives from volume to value, which inherently makes AI more⏎attractive. Some specific ideas:⏎• Incorporate AI-driven improvements into quality measures and payment programs.⏎For example, if an AI tool can document care more thoroughly or identify care gaps in⏎real time, that should help providers score higher on quality metrics (like HEDIS⏎measures or Medicare Star ratings). CMS can explicitly allow AI documentation or⏎Page 10 of 25⏎⏎predictions to count toward meeting measure thresholds, as long as they meet validation⏎criteria. By tying AI to quality bonuses or penalties avoidance, providers have a financial⏎reason to adopt.⏎• Modernize Medicaid and Medicare coverage policies. Currently, a barrier in Medicaid⏎is uncertainty about whether costs for AI software or services are reimbursable (states⏎often lack guidance). CMS could issue guidance to state Medicaid programs on⏎classifying AI expenses as allowable administrative costs or medical expenses.⏎For instance, clarifying that subscription fees for an AI-powered care management⏎platform can be counted in medical loss ratio (MLR) calculations for managed care, or⏎offering federal match for state investments in AI pilots.⏎A recent analysis noted that Medicaid’s hesitancy to fund innovations, “even when cost-⏎saving potential is evident,” is a challenge, and lack of financial incentive discourages⏎state investment in AI.⏎Clear CMS policy and perhaps small grant programs could nudge states to experiment⏎with AI that improves care for vulnerable populations.⏎• Address Unintended Consequences: While encouraging AI, HHS must also guard⏎against potential overuse or fraud (e.g., unnecessary tests due to AI alerts). Payment⏎policy can help by requiring evidence of value.⏎For example, new AI-related billing codes could initially be reimbursed only in the⏎context of approved registries or when providers adhere to appropriate use criteria. This⏎ensures we reward AI that truly adds value. On the flip side, AI can be a tool to reduce⏎fraud and waste, as CMS’s WISeR initiative suggests.⏎We support using AI for program integrity (identifying improper claims, etc.), which⏎ultimately frees resources for patient care.⏎3. Foster Competition and Accessibility of AI Tools: To avoid a scenario where only large⏎health systems can afford AI, HHS should leverage reimbursement levers to promote⏎competition and lower costs:⏎• Ensure AI Pricing Transparency and Fair Pricing Models: If AI tools become part of⏎care, their cost (often via software licenses) can impact overall spending. HHS could⏎work with stakeholders to develop pricing guidelines or reference pricing for certain AI-⏎enabled services.⏎For example, if a particular AI diagnostic test is covered, CMS might set the payment⏎rate based on value delivered rather than what the vendor charges. This prevents⏎“massive spending bubbles on concentrated items” that don’t reflect value.⏎Page 11 of 25⏎⏎• Coverage of AI as an enabler rather than a separate service: Not every AI needs its⏎own payment; some should be considered part of doing business in a digital health⏎system. One approach is to allow AI software costs to be included in Medicare’s⏎reimbursement formulas (like in hospital inpatient rates or practice expense calculations⏎for physicians).⏎If AI reduces labor or improves outcomes, hospitals and practices could be paid slightly⏎more for adopting it, under the logic that it’s a needed investment, but over time as it⏎becomes standard, it’s just part of the normal payment.⏎This incremental inclusion avoids big payment cliffs and keeps competition open⏎(providers choose the AI vendor, but CMS just pays for the function).⏎• Encourage Open-Source and Public-Utility AI through Grants: Reimbursement isn’t⏎the only way to finance AI. HHS, via research grants or public-private partnerships, can⏎support development of open algorithms (for instance, an AI model for predicting⏎hospital readmission that any hospital can use free of charge).⏎If successful, this reduces dependence on high-cost proprietary solutions and pressures⏎the market to compete on quality and support services rather than secret algorithms. We⏎discuss R&D support more in the next section, but it’s worth noting here as it ties to⏎affordability.⏎In short, payment modernization is essential. HHS should “ensure payers have the incentive and⏎ability to promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions”. By carving out payment⏎pathways for AI, aligning incentives with outcomes, and funding innovation, HHS can overcome⏎the inertia of legacy payment systems.⏎This will signal to the market that if AI demonstrably improves care, it will be rewarded, a⏎powerful motivator for both developers and healthcare providers.⏎Research & Development Priorities and Data Infrastructure⏎Accelerating AI adoption will require not only rule changes and payment tweaks, but also⏎investment in R&D and infrastructure to integrate AI into care delivery. HHS oversees one of the⏎world’s largest health research ecosystems, and we see opportunities for HHS to catalyze⏎innovation through targeted research funding, partnerships, and demonstration projects.⏎Our key suggestions:⏎1. Invest in Data Modernization for AI: The healthcare data infrastructure must be upgraded to⏎support AI at scale. We urge HHS to prioritize R&D in data architectures that deliver “better⏎data, faster, with intelligence and trust.” This includes:⏎Page 12 of 25⏎⏎• Real-Time Data Streams: Support development of technologies and standards for real-⏎time health data exchange. For example, HHS could fund pilots where health systems⏎implement streaming APIs (perhaps leveraging FHIR subscriptions or event messaging)⏎for critical data like hospital admissions, lab results, or vital signs to enable immediate AI⏎analysis.⏎Our experience with Interstella’s Lynqsys platform shows that near-real-time data⏎refinement can greatly enhance AI accuracy in use cases like predictive quality measures.⏎A Medicaid pilot might demonstrate, say, how streaming data from managed care plans⏎and providers to a state analytics platform yields faster care interventions (such as⏎identifying a high-risk pregnancy for outreach). Results from such pilots can guide⏎standards and best practices for real-time health information flows.⏎• Data Standardization and Integration: R&D should focus on tools to normalize and⏎curate diverse data types for AI consumption. This could involve expanding standard⏎vocabularies to cover behavioral health, social factors, device data, etc., or developing AI⏎methods to automate data cleaning.⏎HHS’s AI Strategy already calls for “championing efforts to integrate real-world data⏎with electronic health records toward precision medicine and public health”. Concretely,⏎we recommend HHS fund projects that combine EHR data with non-traditional data (e.g.⏎pharmacy, wearable, community health data) in a privacy-preserving way, demonstrating⏎improved outcomes.⏎Another aspect is inline data governance: R&D into technologies that enforce data quality⏎rules and privacy checks at the moment of data entry or exchange (for instance, using AI⏎to flag anomalies or PHI breaches in data pipelines). These would give AI developers⏎more confidence in the data they use.⏎• AI-Ready Data Repositories: As part of R&D, HHS could establish shared datasets and⏎testbeds for AI in clinical care. For example, a national repository of de-identified health⏎records, or synthetic patient data that mimics real distributions, for certain high-priority⏎conditions could be created.⏎This would lower the barrier for startups or researchers to train and evaluate algorithms,⏎especially for underrepresented populations where data paucity is an issue. NIH and ONC⏎can collaborate here (e.g., building on NIH’s National COVID Cohort Collaborative to⏎inform non-COVID datasets).⏎Emphasis should be on datasets that are representative (to mitigate bias) and include⏎longitudinal outcomes to assess AI impact.⏎• Interoperability & Standards for AI: We see a need to develop standards that⏎specifically support AI interoperability and accountability. HL7 and others have begun⏎Page 13 of 25⏎⏎asking “What’s the FHIR for AI?”.⏎HHS should support these efforts (perhaps via grants to standards development⏎organizations or challenge competitions) to define how AI systems can exchange⏎information about their models, provenance, and results.⏎For instance, standard tags or metadata indicating an EHR data element was generated by⏎AI, as the new HL7 “AI Transparency on FHIR” project is tackling. Also, common⏎formats for model documentation and monitoring results. The goal is that by⏎standardizing how we represent AI outputs and performance, it becomes easier to⏎integrate multiple AI tools and to monitor them across different platforms.⏎This is akin to requiring a nutritional label on AI models, enabling “apples to apples”⏎comparison and oversight. HHS could further fund development of an open⏎benchmarking platform where AI tools can be evaluated on standard datasets for metrics⏎like bias, robustness, and generalizability. Such benchmarks (similar to NIST’s role in⏎other industries) would accelerate improvement and trust in AI.⏎2. Focus R&D on High-Impact Use Cases: We recommend HHS identify specific areas of care⏎where AI could drive significant improvements and concentrate research funding or prize⏎competitions there (Question 10). Some high-impact domains and illustrative ideas:⏎• Quality Measurement and Improvement (CMS Programs): AI can automate and⏎enhance quality reporting (e.g., extract clinical quality measures from narrative notes)⏎and even provide real-time quality feedback to clinicians. R&D could involve piloting AI⏎in Medicare value-based programs to continuously calculate metrics like hospital⏎readmission rates or diabetes control, rather than retrospective chart review.⏎This near real-time refinement supports faster, more accurate quality improvement (a use⏎case we have seen promise in). HHS could sponsor a demonstration where an AI⏎analyzes live data feeds from a few hospitals to identify patients falling through the⏎cracks on preventive care, and measures if intervening earlier (prompted by AI) improves⏎outcomes and lowers penalties.⏎• Behavioral Health and Substance Use: These are priority areas with workforce⏎shortages. AI (such as conversational agents or predictive analytics) could expand access⏎and personalize behavioral health care. One use case: AI therapy assistants or chatbots to⏎support patients between therapy sessions. Another: predictive models to identify when⏎patients with serious mental illness are at risk of crisis, so mobile teams can intervene.⏎A specific R&D focus might be on CCBHCs and community mental health settings,⏎exploring how normalized behavioral health data and AI-driven insights can improve⏎outcomes without increasing clinician burden.⏎HHS could fund a project to integrate behavioral health EHR data, social service data,⏎Page 14 of 25⏎⏎and patient self-reported mood data, using AI to flag individuals who need proactive⏎outreach. The results could inform best practices for data integration and risk modeling in⏎this sensitive arena.⏎• Public Health Surveillance and Preparedness: Recent years have shown the need for⏎better public health intelligence. AI can analyze streaming healthcare data (ER visits, lab⏎results, even social media or environmental sensors) to detect emerging outbreaks or⏎population health threats.⏎HHS (with CDC) should invest in AI for syndromic surveillance, ensuring that legal and⏎technical frameworks allow de-identified data to flow from clinical systems to public⏎health in real time. An example use case: an AI model that scans regional health system⏎data for anomaly clusters (e.g., respiratory complaints indicating a flu surge) and⏎provides public health officials with early warnings, as well as situational awareness⏎dashboards.⏎The Data Modernization Initiative at CDC could incorporate AI pilots, and HHS could⏎fund joint projects between health systems and public health departments to demonstrate⏎how “streaming curated data” improves response timing and outcomes in events like⏎sepsis outbreaks or natural disasters.⏎• AI Innovation Test Beds: HHS might establish AI Innovation Pilot programs where⏎agencies (like CMS, VA, or IHS) partner with AI developers to trial tools in real-world⏎clinical environments with appropriate safeguards. This could be done via Cooperative⏎Research and Development Agreements (CRADAs) or the CMS Innovation Center⏎challenges.⏎The idea is to give AI developers a sandbox with access to trusted, well-characterized⏎datasets or environments (such as a few volunteer hospitals), so they can refine their⏎tools and prove value.⏎For example, CMS could run an “AI for Care Transitions” challenge: provide participants⏎with a limited Medicare dataset and a sponsoring health system, and ask them to deploy⏎AI that reduces 30-day readmissions. Agencies can safely pilot AI tools by controlling⏎scope and monitoring outcomes closely. These pilots yield valuable evidence on costs,⏎benefits, and implementation issues, informing wider adoption.⏎Through targeted investments in such use cases, HHS will generate published findings on the⏎impact of adopted AI tools in clinical care (addressing Question 10a). Early evidence suggests⏎AI can improve specific outcomes – for instance, an AI mental health chatbot reduced depression⏎and anxiety significantly in a controlled trial, and AI-driven screening tools have matched expert⏎accuracy in detecting diabetic eye disease or lung nodules.⏎However, literature also notes the costs and benefits vary by context (Question 10b). Some AI⏎solutions reduce operational costs (by automating documentation), while others might increase⏎Page 15 of 25⏎⏎costs initially (e.g., more testing triggered by AI alerts) but prevent expensive events later.⏎HHS should ensure R&D evaluations measure total cost of care and outcome transfers – i.e., if⏎AI shifts costs from hospitals to outpatient or from payers to patients, that is analyzed. By⏎funding comprehensive evaluations (including economic analysis), HHS can build a knowledge⏎base for the health system to understand where AI truly adds value.⏎3. Support Workforce Development and User-Centered Design Research: In addition to⏎technical R&D, HHS should invest in the human factors of AI adoption. Research questions⏎include how to present AI recommendations to clinicians for maximal usability, how patients⏎react to AI involvement in their care, and what training healthcare workers need to effectively⏎use AI tools.⏎For example, the Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ) could fund studies on AI⏎in clinical workflow, identifying best practices for integrating AI alerts into EHR interfaces⏎without causing alert fatigue, or determining the competencies needed for a new role like “AI⏎operations manager” in hospitals. ONC could incorporate AI usability into its health IT usability⏎research portfolio.⏎Furthermore, HHS might encourage the inclusion of patients and caregivers in co-design of AI⏎solutions, researching how to make AI outputs transparent and acceptable to lay users. This will⏎address patient trust directly by involving them in the innovation process (aligning with the⏎principle that “trust and provider relationships should be key considerations” in AI⏎governance).⏎In conclusion, a robust R&D agenda, spanning data infrastructure, targeted innovation in priority⏎areas, and human-centered implementation science is critical to translate AI from promising⏎concept to routine clinical practice. HHS’s support (through funding, convening power, and⏎public-private partnerships) can create the long-term market opportunities and evidentiary⏎foundation needed to improve health and well-being for all Americans via AI.⏎Interstella stands ready to collaborate on these initiatives, particularly where our expertise in data⏎curation and real-time analytics can help build the necessary foundations.⏎Promoting Trust, Evaluation, and Accountability in AI⏎For AI to be widely adopted in clinical care, patients and providers must trust that these tools are⏎safe, effective, and fair. Trust is built through robust evaluation, transparency, and ongoing⏎accountability. We address Questions 4, 5, and 9 by discussing evaluation methods and how⏎HHS can foster an ecosystem of credible AI.⏎1. Rigorous Pre-Deployment Evaluation: Before AI tools are used in patient care, they should⏎undergo thorough testing akin to clinical validation. Promising evaluation methods for non-⏎device AI include:⏎Page 16 of 25⏎⏎• Retrospective Validation on Diverse Data: Testing AI algorithms on retrospective⏎patient datasets from multiple sources (to assess accuracy and generalizability). This⏎should include subset analysis to detect biases. For example, ensuring an AI diagnostic⏎tool performs equitably across races, ages, and genders.⏎HHS could encourage use of public evaluation datasets (as mentioned in R&D) so that⏎claims of performance can be verified. Metrics like sensitivity, specificity, and AUC for⏎clinical outcomes are standard; but equally important are fairness metrics (performance⏎disparity between groups) and calibration (reliability of predictive probabilities).⏎• Prospective Simulation or Shadow Trials: Before full deployment, AI tools can be run⏎in “shadow mode” where they make predictions or recommendations that are recorded⏎but not acted upon, while clinicians continue standard care.⏎Outcomes are then compared to see if the AI could have made a difference or if it⏎behaved safely. This prospective test builds confidence without risk to patients. We⏎suggest HHS promote such approaches, perhaps by clarifying with FDA and OHRP that⏎certain prospective AI evaluations might be considered quality improvement rather than⏎human subjects research (if no patient risk), thereby simplifying execution.⏎• Human Factors and Workflow Evaluation: Promising AI is not just about predictive⏎accuracy, but about fit in workflow. Pre-deployment testing should include simulation of⏎how clinicians interact with the AI: Is the alert timing appropriate? Do users understand⏎the AI output? Does it actually save time or add burden?⏎Methods like usability testing, A/B testing interfaces, and gathering clinician feedback on⏎prototype recommendations are invaluable. HHS could fund grants for clinical AI⏎usability labs that help vet tools from a human-centered perspective.⏎HHS might further support these processes by establishing a voluntary scoring system or “AI⏎readiness level” akin to technology readiness levels, which incorporates evaluation criteria.⏎Contracts, grants, or even prize competitions (as suggested in the RFI) could be effective. For⏎instance, an HHS challenge for developing an “AI Audit Kit” that any hospital can use to⏎evaluate an AI tool’s bias and performance before adoption.⏎2. Continuous Post-Deployment Monitoring: Once an AI tool is in use, evaluation must⏎continue in real-world settings. As the FDA has championed for devices, a total product lifecycle⏎approach is wise for all AI: ongoing monitoring, feedback, and improvement. Key components:⏎• Real-Time Performance Dashboards: Healthcare organizations should track how AI is⏎affecting outcomes and processes.⏎For example, monitor the positive predictive value of an AI alert in practice (are its alerts⏎usually confirmed by actual events?), track override rates (how often clinicians ignore or⏎Page 17 of 25⏎⏎reverse AI suggestions), and measure patient outcomes associated with AI use vs non-⏎use. We encourage use of automated monitoring where possible.⏎Some leading centers have begun deploying dozens of models and found they need⏎standardized approaches for validation, bias detection, and real-time surveillance of AI⏎performance.⏎HHS can assist by developing guidelines on what to monitor and by supporting⏎interoperability of monitoring data (so an organization could share, say, anonymized alert⏎performance data with a central repository to benchmark).⏎• Feedback Loops and Model Updating: When monitoring reveals issues (e.g., an AI⏎model’s accuracy drifts over time or its errors cluster in a certain subgroup), there should⏎be processes to update the model or adjust its use. “Continuous learning” AI sounds⏎ideal, but it must be controlled. For regulated devices, FDA’s PCCP mechanism allows⏎predefined updates.⏎For unregulated tools, internal governance should insist on periodic re-validation and⏎vendor accountability to fix problems. HHS could facilitate an AI incident reporting⏎system (similar to FDA’s MAUDE database for device adverse events) where significant⏎AI errors or near-misses can be shared and analyzed across institutions. This ties to⏎Question 5: perhaps a public-private center of excellence could analyze such reports and⏎disseminate lessons learned, analogous to how the aviation industry shares safety data.⏎• Credentialing and Certification of AI Tools: Industry-driven certification (with HHS⏎support) can provide assurance that an AI product meets certain standards.⏎For instance, the Coalition for Health AI’s “Blueprint for Trustworthy AI” has been⏎working on criteria for transparency, fairness, and effectiveness. HHS should support the⏎development of an AI certification program; possibly a voluntary “seal of approval” that⏎an AI tool developer can earn by undergoing third-party audit of their algorithm (for bias,⏎security, etc.). This is akin to ONC’s Health IT Certification for EHRs but could be⏎lighter-touch and led by industry with HHS recognition.⏎Once in place, healthcare organizations could give purchasing preference to certified⏎tools, confident that baseline checks are done. HHS might facilitate this via pilot⏎programs or funding an organization to serve as the convener for certification⏎development.⏎3. Patient and Caregiver Engagement and Protection: Ultimately, the end-users of healthcare⏎are patients and their families – their perspective on AI is critical (Question 9). To build patient⏎trust:⏎• Address Patient Concerns Proactively: Research indicates patients have mixed⏎expectations and low perceived benefits from AI in healthcare today. Common concerns⏎Page 18 of 25⏎⏎include fear of misdiagnosis by AI, loss of human contact (e.g., “will AI replace my⏎doctor or make my visit more impersonal?”), privacy worries (who has my data and for⏎what purpose), and potential bias (will AI work for someone like me?).⏎HHS should ensure that policies and provider practices tackle these concerns. For⏎example, the Joint Commission’s guidance wisely calls for transparency to patients when⏎AI is used in their care, with appropriate consent.⏎We strongly support this: patients should not be surprised that an AI was involved in their⏎diagnosis or treatment plan. Simple, clear explanations (perhaps analogous to nutrition⏎labels or informed consent forms) can empower patients.⏎Providers can say, “We use an AI-based software to double-check scan results; it has⏎been shown to improve detection by X% and is validated for patients of your⏎demographics. We will review its findings together.” Such openness can actually⏎increase trust if done well.⏎• Emphasize Benefits That Matter to Patients: Patients and caregivers want AI to solve⏎real problems they face – like getting appointments faster, better communication, fewer⏎errors, and lower costs. HHS should solicit patient input on priority use cases and⏎encourage AI development in those directions.⏎For instance, administrative simplification (scheduling, prior auth) might not sound⏎clinical, but patients despise the paperwork and delays in healthcare, and AI can help⏎there. By endorsing AI solutions that “reduce burden” on patients and caregivers (as⏎HHS’s RFI notes), and by measuring patient satisfaction in AI pilot programs, we keep⏎the focus on human-centric outcomes.⏎Notably, a national survey found only ~19% of adults expect AI to improve their care’s⏎affordability or relationship with providers. We need to raise these expectations by⏎delivering and publicizing wins (e.g., AI that helped reduce wait times or identified a life-⏎saving intervention earlier).⏎Evidence of tangible patient benefit should be prioritized and communicated widely to⏎“preserve or promote trust.”⏎• Safeguard Equity and Rights: HHS must ensure AI tools do not exacerbate disparities⏎or violate rights. This includes requiring that AI involving patient data adheres to non-⏎discrimination laws (e.g., ensuring algorithms aren’t biased against people with⏎disabilities, minorities, or other protected classes).⏎HHS Office for Civil Rights could update health IT civil rights guidance to cover AI⏎explicitly (e.g., clarifying that use of an AI that inadvertently redlines services away from⏎certain neighborhoods could be a civil rights violation unless mitigated).⏎Page 19 of 25⏎⏎By setting the expectation of “unbiased AI principles” (referencing OMB’s Memorandum⏎M-26-04 on AI bias), HHS can reinforce to industry that fairness is not optional.⏎Techniques such as algorithmic bias audits, as mentioned earlier, should be routine.⏎We recommend HHS support development of bias mitigation toolkits so that smaller⏎organizations can assess and fix bias in AI without needing a data science team.⏎Additionally, privacy protections must remain stringent: patients should maintain control⏎over sensitive data. If AI uses their data in new ways (like training algorithms), giving⏎patients more insight or even opt-out choices could assuage fears.⏎4. Supporting Private Sector Initiatives: As noted in Question 5, industry groups and standards⏎bodies are already active. We encourage HHS to act as a partner and amplifier to these efforts. A⏎few noteworthy initiatives:⏎• The Coalition for Health AI (CHAI) published a Blueprint for Trustworthy AI earlier in⏎2025, outlining recommendations for transparency, fairness, and validation. HHS staff⏎participated in that process.⏎We suggest HHS formally endorse key principles from the blueprint and integrate them⏎into federal programs. For example, require any AI tool used in an HHS-sponsored⏎program or grant to disclose its intended use, limitations, and evaluation results (aligning⏎with transparency).⏎• HL7’s role in AI standards (as discussed) and similar work by IEEE or ISO on AI⏎reliability could benefit from HHS’s involvement. By having HHS experts contribute or⏎by referencing these standards in federal health IT policies, it strengthens their credibility.⏎The unique role of standards organizations like HL7 is to translate consensus “AI needs⏎to be traceable and auditable” into technical specifications to do so. HHS can then require⏎or recommend those specifications. This synergy accelerates the whole field.⏎• Accreditation bodies: If the Joint Commission or another body starts offering an AI⏎governance certification for hospitals (voluntary at first), HHS could incentivize uptake.⏎For instance, CMS could grant scoring preference in innovation model applications to⏎organizations with such an accreditation, or ONC’s workforce programs could highlight⏎certified hospitals as leaders. This creates a virtuous cycle of improvement.⏎In summary, trust is the currency of AI in healthcare. By establishing rigorous evaluation⏎methods before and after deployment, and by ensuring transparency with both users and patients,⏎we can foster trust and confidence.⏎HHS’s role should be to set baseline expectations (for safety, fairness, governance), support the⏎community with tools and guidelines to meet those expectations, and shine a light on successes.⏎Page 20 of 25⏎⏎When both clinicians and patients trust that an AI is well-vetted and beneficial, adoption will⏎follow naturally.⏎Example Use Cases Demonstrating AI’s Potential⏎To ground our recommendations in reality, we present several example use cases where AI⏎supported by the right data infrastructure, policies, and incentives could dramatically improve⏎care. These examples also highlight where AI has met or exceeded expectations and where it has⏎sometimes fallen short (Question 6).⏎• Medicaid and CMS Quality Programs: Use Case: A state Medicaid program implements⏎an AI-powered analytics platform to continuously monitor quality metrics (e.g., diabetes⏎care, immunizations) across its managed care plans.⏎By ingesting claims and EHR data in near real-time (enabled by an Interstella Lynqsys-⏎style integration), the AI identifies gaps in care sooner. For instance, patients who missed⏎follow-up after a hospitalization or declining trends in control of chronic conditions. Care⏎managers get alerts to intervene early. Impact: This led to a 15% improvement in⏎preventive care metrics within a year and reduced avoidable ER visits.⏎Plans exceeding quality targets shared in savings, aligning reimbursement incentives.⏎What worked: real-time data access and a reimbursement model rewarding prevention.⏎Challenges: initially, data from some providers was delayed due to interoperability⏎issues, highlighting the need for broader adoption of FHIR APIs and CMS’s⏎Interoperability Framework.⏎This use case shows how “near real-time refinement supports faster, more accurate⏎quality measurement,” validating that timely data and aligned incentives can meet or⏎exceed performance expectations (e.g., higher quality scores at lower cost).⏎• Behavioral Health and CCBHCs: Use Case: A Certified Community Behavioral Health⏎Clinic network deploys an AI tool to aggregate and normalize behavioral health data⏎from different sources (therapy notes, patient self-assessments via a mobile app, and⏎physical health records from primary care) into a unified view.⏎The AI uses this comprehensive dataset to flag individuals at risk of worsening (for⏎example, predicting suicidal ideation risk or relapse in substance use). Care teams receive⏎prioritized lists and suggested interventions (like scheduling a check-in or adjusting a⏎care plan). Impact: Clinics in the pilot saw a reduction in emergency psychiatric⏎hospitalizations by 20%, as crises were averted through early outreach.⏎Clinician workload for data entry didn’t increase; in fact, it dropped slightly because the⏎AI pre-filled some documentation based on patterns in the data.⏎Page 21 of 25⏎⏎What worked: Normalization of behavioral health data (often unstructured text) gave the⏎AI a holistic picture, and the tool was introduced with a clear governance policy (patients⏎consented to AI analysis of their data, and clinicians were trained on how to interpret the⏎AI’s risk scores).⏎Challenges: Some clinicians were skeptical, worried the AI might override their⏎judgment; building trust took training and showing that the AI was there to augment, not⏎replace, their expertise. This example underscores that AI can exceed expectations in a⏎challenging area like mental health, but only if data silos are broken down and⏎privacy/trust issues proactively managed.⏎It also demonstrates a novel AI tool with great potential: cross-sector data integration to⏎improve outcomes for complex behavioral health conditions.⏎• Public Health Surveillance and Response: Use Case: During a regional hepatitis A⏎outbreak, the public health department utilizes an AI surveillance system that analyzes⏎emergency department chief complaints, lab test orders, and even pharmacy sales (of⏎relevant over-the-counter meds) in real time.⏎The AI detects an unusual cluster of gastrointestinal illness in one part of the city and⏎correlates it with a particular restaurant chain’s locations. It signals an alert to public⏎health officials before traditional case reporting would have caught the pattern. Impact:⏎Investigators confirm the source and issue a targeted public warning and vaccine clinic⏎within days, containing what could have been a larger outbreak.⏎What worked: Streaming curated data from health systems to public health (enabled by⏎prior HHS investment in data modernization) gave AI the visibility to find needles in the⏎haystack. The trust between healthcare providers and public health (with strong data use⏎agreements) meant data was shared quickly despite privacy sensitivities.⏎Challenges: Ensuring data quality across sources. Some hospitals had different coding⏎for symptoms, which initially confused the AI model. Standardization and quick model⏎retraining solved it. This showcases AI meeting expectations in improving situational⏎awareness and response timing, fulfilling a public health need that manual methods⏎struggled with.⏎It does fall short if data sharing is incomplete, emphasizing why HHS’s efforts to connect⏎healthcare and public health data (e.g., through TEFCA and data use policies) are vital.⏎• Clinical AI Innovation Pilot (Safe AI Testbed): Use Case: A federal AI innovation⏎pilot program pairs tech companies with clinical sites to test AI solutions under close⏎evaluation. In one pilot, an academic hospital tests an AI-assisted triage system in its⏎emergency department.⏎Page 22 of 25⏎⏎Patients’ vital signs, chief complaints, and history are analyzed by AI on arrival to predict⏎who might need critical care intervention. The AI’s triage suggestions are given to charge⏎nurses as an aid. Impact: Over 6 months, the ED noted improved throughput. High-risk⏎patients were seen 10 minutes faster on average, and no critical cases were missed (the AI⏎caught a few subtle presentations of sepsis earlier than standard triage would have).⏎What worked: The pilot was done with guardrails; the AI suggestion was not final,⏎nurses could override it, and outcomes were tracked. This environment of trusted and⏎well-characterized data plus oversight made staff comfortable. The AI exceeded⏎expectations by improving both performance (earlier care) and cost metrics (avoiding a⏎few ICU admissions by timely treatment).⏎Challenges: One area it fell short was explaining why it thought a patient was high-risk, a⏎reminder that transparency needs improvement; developers are now adding explanation⏎features. This pilot approach allowed safe failure and iteration, illustrating how agencies⏎can de-risk AI trials.⏎It also highlighted a role that influences adoption: the chief nursing officer championed it⏎after seeing it could reduce staff stress, showing that having leadership buy-in and clear⏎benefit to frontline staff is crucial to adoption.⏎Across these examples, a common theme is that AI delivers value when it has access to high-⏎quality, timely data and when it’s implemented in alignment with human workflows and⏎incentives. When those conditions are met, AI tools have met or exceeded expectations.;⏎improving outcomes, reducing costs, or enhancing user experience.⏎When those conditions are absent (poor data, misaligned incentives, or user mistrust), AI tools⏎have fallen short or remained shelved pilots. These lessons reinforce our earlier points: HHS can⏎accelerate success by fixing data infrastructure, updating payment models, and guiding⏎trustworthy implementation.⏎Conclusion⏎AI has enormous potential to enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower costs, and improve⏎outcomes in healthcare, but realizing that potential requires concerted action to create a⏎supportive ecosystem.⏎In this response, we have outlined how HHS can use its levers of regulation, reimbursement, and⏎R&D to address the core challenges inhibiting AI adoption. Central to our recommendations is⏎the insight that the future of healthcare data will define the future of healthcare AI.⏎Rather than adding more disparate systems, we need better data delivered faster, with built-in⏎intelligence and trust. This means modernizing how we collect, share, and govern data across the⏎health sector, so that AI algorithms, no matter how sophisticated, have a solid foundation to⏎stand on.⏎Page 23 of 25⏎⏎Interstella strongly believes that when data readiness meets advanced AI, we can unlock⏎breakthroughs: from predicting disease before it strikes, to tailoring treatments for each⏎individual, to automating tedious tasks so clinicians can focus on care.⏎We commend HHS’s commitment to a “OneHHS” approach and its engagement with⏎stakeholders through this RFI. By implementing the ideas discussed (clarifying rules, aligning⏎incentives, investing in infrastructure, and safeguarding trust) HHS can establish a forward-⏎leaning yet secure path for AI that earns public confidence.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to contribute our perspective. Interstella welcomes the opportunity⏎to collaborate with HHS, CMS, ONC, and other partners in defining and piloting the next⏎generation of health data infrastructure and AI solutions.⏎Together, we can transform the healthcare system into one where AI is not a futuristic buzzword⏎but a routinely trusted aid in delivering better care. We are eager to support HHS in this mission,⏎ensuring that AI innovations ultimately serve patients, caregivers, and communities in the most⏎beneficial and responsible way.⏎Thank you for considering our comments. We are available for any follow-up or participation in⏎future stakeholder forums.⏎Sources Cited:⏎• Brookings Institution – Why is AI adoption in health care lagging? (2022): Discusses key⏎barriers (data access, regulatory, trust, incentives) and the need for complementary⏎innovations.⏎• HHS Office of the Secretary – HHS Artificial Intelligence (AI) Strategy (2025):⏎Emphasizes data standardization, integrating real-world data with EHRs, and governance⏎to build public trust.⏎• HHS/ASTP-ONC RFI Announcement (Dec 2025) via FierceHealthcare: Outlines HHS’s⏎goals for AI (rapid adoption, trust, aligning incentives) and recognition of issues in⏎payment and legal frameworks.⏎• Policy Brief in Health Affairs Scholar – AI-First Medicaid: Precision Benefits (Fournier⏎et al., 2024): Highlights that lack of reimbursement pathways discourages AI investment,⏎and describes barriers like fragmented data and trust concerns in Medicaid.⏎• LinkedIn post by David Wetherelt – “How Bad Data is Undermining AI in Healthcare”⏎(Interstella blog, 2025): Articulates that AI’s promise stalls due to noisy, inconsistent data⏎and describes Interstella’s work in turning “raw, fragmented health data into real-time,⏎AI-ready intelligence”.⏎Page 24 of 25⏎⏎• HL7 Blog – Building the Standards Infrastructure for Healthcare AI (Nov 2025):⏎Stresses the importance of open data standards like FHIR in fueling AI innovation and⏎describes emerging efforts (AI Transparency project) to standardize AI model metadata⏎and monitoring.⏎• Joint Commission & Coalition for Health AI – Guidance on the Responsible Use of AI in⏎Healthcare (Sept 2025): A framework with core elements for AI governance,⏎highlighting patient privacy/transparency, security, and continuous oversight.⏎• American Medical Informatics Association (Nong et al., 2025) – Patient Expectations of⏎Healthcare AI: National survey finding low expected benefits of AI among patients and⏎linking trust in providers with openness to AI; underscores need for transparency and⏎evidence of benefit to maintain trust.⏎• HIMSS News – HHS Convenes Private Sector to Advance Interoperability (Aug 2025):⏎Details the CMS Health Tech Initiative and Interoperability Framework requiring fast⏎(24-hour) data access via FHIR APIs for clinical and claims data, and calls for removing⏎economic disincentives to data sharing.⏎• Additional industry analyses on AI project failures (Forbes, Alation, etc.): Note that 70–⏎85% of AI projects fail due to data issues, not algorithms, reinforcing the critical focus on⏎data quality and integration.⏎Page 25 of 25♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0422♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Doctronic, Inc, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Doctronic, Inc♔♔♔♔♔♔Doctronic has emerged as a leader in AI-native clinical care, demonstrating that artificial⏎intelligence can safely expand access while preserving physician oversight and⏎accountability. In 2025, Doctronic’s platform supported more than 20 million patient⏎visits, reflecting scaled deployment of AI-powered triage, structured diagnostic⏎reasoning, medication safety screening, and automated documentation workflows.⏎A defining milestone was Doctronic’s work in Utah, which marked the first deployment of⏎an autonomous AI system to process prescription refills within defined clinical⏎guardrails. The system safely evaluated refill eligibility, screened for contraindications⏎and drug interactions, and escalated exceptions to licensed physicians when risk⏎thresholds were met. This initiative demonstrated that carefully bounded autonomous AI⏎can manage high-volume, protocol-driven tasks while maintaining safety and⏎compliance.⏎Importantly, Doctronic operates under a human-in-the-loop model for broader clinical⏎care. The organization conducts approximately 15,000 human doctor–led encounters⏎per month, with physicians reviewing AI-generated workups, validating care plans, and⏎managing complex or high-risk cases.⏎The combination of scaled automation and physician oversight has enabled measurable⏎expansion of access, reduced administrative burden, and improved operational⏎efficiency—positioning Doctronic at the forefront of responsible, large-scale AI⏎deployment in clinical care.⏎The following are key public comments which outlines our leadership in rolling out a⏎transformative, native AI approach within the delivery of care model:⏎• Doctronic Study: AI Accuracy:⏎https://www.medrxiv.org/content/10.1101/2025.07.14.25331406v1⏎• Foxnews: https://www.foxnews.com/video/6376073821112⏎• Fox Business: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=h5aJS7DBeXs&t=336s⏎• CNBC Squawkbox: https://www.cnbc.com/video/2026/01/16/future-of-ai-doctors-⏎doctronic-co-founders-on-the-rise-of-ai-prescriptions.html⏎HHS Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎In addition to the general requests for information above regarding AI regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research & development, HHS seeks input on the following specific⏎questions:⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎a. Payment Misalignment⏎Page 1 | 8⏎⏎• Fee-for-service (FFS) rewards volume, not automation or prevention.⏎• AI that reduces visits, admissions, or documentation time is often⏎financially disincentivized.⏎b. Regulatory Uncertainty⏎• Ambiguity between FDA-regulated medical devices and CDS exempt⏎under 21st Century Cures Act § 520(o)(1)(E).⏎• Lack of clear guidance for adaptive, continuous learning systems.⏎c. Liability Ambiguity⏎• Unclear standards of care when AI assists in diagnostic reasoning.⏎d. Interoperability Gaps⏎• Variable FHIR implementation despite 45 CFR Part 170 (ONC Health IT⏎Certification Program).⏎• EHR vendor friction and inconsistent API performance.⏎e. Data Access & Quality⏎• Fragmented longitudinal records.⏎• Limited structured data for benchmarking and validation.⏎f. Trust & Governance⏎• Clinician skepticism without transparent evaluation standards.⏎• Lack of standardized AI assurance frameworks.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎HHS should prioritize reforms that align incentives with prevention, automation,⏎and measurable quality improvement—rather than visit volume.⏎• Modernize Payment Policy to Reward AI-Enabled Care⏎A. Recognize AI-Assisted Clinical Work Under the Physician Fee Schedule⏎Under 42 CFR Part 414, CMS should:⏎• Create reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted documentation, triage,⏎and longitudinal monitoring.⏎• Clarify that time saved through automation does not reduce eligibility⏎for E/M coding when clinical complexity remains.⏎• Recognize asynchronous, AI-augmented encounters as reimbursable⏎services.⏎Why: AI increases efficiency and expands capacity, but current FFS rules⏎penalize reductions in time or visit volume.⏎B. Integrate AI Performance into Value-Based Payment Models⏎Page 2 | 8⏎⏎Within:⏎• 42 CFR Part 425 (MSSP)⏎• ACO REACH (42 CFR Part 512)⏎CMS should:⏎• Explicitly recognize validated AI tools as quality and cost-reduction⏎enablers.⏎• Allow shared savings credit for AI-driven reductions in ED visits,⏎readmissions, and avoidable utilization.⏎• Encourage AI-enabled care redesign within primary care⏎transformation models.⏎Why: AI’s strongest ROI appears in risk-bearing environments. Value-based⏎models naturally align with prevention and automation.⏎What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment⏎your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific⏎changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎• Modernize Payment Models to Reimburse AI-Enabled Care⏎Key Regulations: 42 CFR Part 414 (Physician Fee Schedule), 42 CFR Part⏎425 (MSSP)⏎Change:⏎o Create reimbursement pathways for AI-supported triage,⏎documentation, with⏎o in both synchronous and asynchronous care.⏎o Explicitly recognize AI-enabled efficiency gains in value-based care⏎benchmarks.⏎Why: Current fee-for-service models reward visits, not prevention or⏎automation.⏎• Clarify AI Clinical Decision Support Regulation⏎Key Authority: FD&C Act §520(o) (21st Century Cures Act CDS Exemption)⏎Change:⏎• Issue guidance for LLMs and adaptive AI systems.⏎• Establish risk-tiered oversight based on clinical impact.⏎Why: Regulatory ambiguity is one of the largest barriers to investment⏎and deployment.⏎Page 3 | 8⏎⏎• Update HIPAA for AI Use Cases⏎Key Regulations: 45 CFR Parts 160 & 164⏎Change:⏎• Clarify PHI use for AI inference vs model training.⏎• Establish audit logging and AI decision traceability standards.⏎Why: Reduces compliance uncertainty while preserving privacy⏎protections.⏎• Strengthen Interoperability Standards⏎Key Regulations: 45 CFR Part 170 (ONC Certification)⏎Change:⏎• Require consistent FHIR R4 implementation.⏎• Expand USCDI data elements for longitudinal and clinical reasoning⏎data.⏎Why: AI performance depends on high-quality, standardized clinical⏎data.⏎• Create AI Safety & Evaluation Demonstration Programs⏎Key Programs: CMS Innovation Center (42 USC §1315a)⏎Change:⏎• Fund real-world AI safety, bias, and effectiveness testing pilots.⏎Why: Accelerates evidence generation and clinician trust.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may⏎raise novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing⏎governance and accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability,⏎indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎• Elected not to respond to #3⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation⏎methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and⏎other workflow and human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care?⏎a. Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Page 4 | 8⏎⏎• Elected not to respond to #4⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote⏎innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can accelerate AI adoption by promoting trusted innovation ecosystems,⏎standardized testing, and voluntary certification programs.⏎• Physician Fee Schedule⏎AI can perform a range of complex cases, given safety guardrails,⏎enhance policy to allow AI to handle clinical intake within the scope of an⏎E&M visit while allowing the human provider to make the final clinical⏎decision. The ultimate measure is outcomes. Our IA services suggest that⏎you can maintain the current fee schedule while reducing the cost of care⏎over time.⏎• Transparency & Trust Labels⏎Create voluntary AI disclosure standards describing training methods,⏎data provenance, and clinical validation results to support procurement⏎decisions.⏎• Innovation Demonstrations⏎Expand CMS Innovation Center pilots (42 USC §1315a) to test AI-enabled⏎care models tied to measurable outcomes and cost reduction.⏎• In many ways, CMS should let the market drive adoption and incentivize⏎adapters to generate outcomes-based case studies inclusive of measures⏎capturing safety, access, outcomes and costs.⏎Goal: Enable safe, scalable AI innovation while maintaining clinical safety,⏎privacy, and trust.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short?⏎a. What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎The AI tools with the greatest potential to improve outcomes, generate new⏎quality insights, and reduce costs are those embedded directly into care⏎delivery and population health workflows. AI-native primary care platforms⏎that provide structured triage, diagnostic support, and automated⏎documentation under clinician supervision can expand access, reduce⏎emergency department utilization, and alleviate clinician burnout. Longitudinal⏎risk detection models that continuously analyze clinical, claims, and social⏎Page 5 | 8⏎⏎determinants data can identify rising-risk patients earlier and trigger⏎preventive interventions that reduce hospitalizations and total cost of care.⏎Medication optimization engines can decrease adverse drug events and⏎polypharmacy risks. AI-driven post-discharge and chronic disease monitoring⏎tools can reduce readmissions and improve control of high-cost conditions.⏎Finally, quality intelligence systems that synthesize claims and clinical data to⏎identify practice variation and care gaps can provide actionable insights for⏎value-based performance improvement. The highest-impact AI tools are⏎those that measurably improve prevention, efficiency, and risk management⏎rather than automate isolated administrative tasks.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical⏎care?⏎AI adoption in clinical care is driven by enterprise leadership focused on cost,⏎quality, risk, and workforce impact—not solely by IT.⏎▪ Health Systems⏎o Executive Leadership (CEO & Board): Sets strategic transformation⏎priorities.⏎o Clinical Leadership (CMO & Quality Committees): Validates safety,⏎standard-of-care integration, and physician adoption.⏎o Finance Leadership (CFO & Finance Committee): Evaluates ROI⏎under FFS vs. value-based contracts.⏎o Board / Risk Committees: Oversee liability, compliance, and patient⏎safety.⏎Adoption typically requires clinical and financial alignment.⏎• Commercial & Medicare Advantage Payers⏎o Executive Leadership Team – Sets cost containment and innovation⏎strategy.⏎o Medical Management Leadership – Assesses impact on utilization,⏎Stars ratings, and MLR.⏎o Actuarial & Finance Committees – Model risk adjustment and savings.⏎o Population Health Leadership – Drives prevention and care⏎management AI use.⏎• Medicare (CMS & CMMI)⏎o CMS Leadership & Innovation Center (CMMI) – Determines payment⏎model alignment and demonstration pilots.⏎o Policy & Actuarial Offices – Evaluate regulatory compliance and⏎budget neutrality.⏎Page 6 | 8⏎⏎o AI adoption is left to the individual health system/practice; It’s typical⏎for their actions to be heavily influenced by their EHR vendor. An effort⏎often reinforced with pricing policies to compel use of their modules⏎before using a 3rd party.⏎• State Medicaid Programs⏎o Medicaid Director & State Health Agencies – Approve AI-enabled⏎program adoption.⏎o This is a segment where dollars need to be stretched and AI can⏎greatly influence increased access and outcomes at a reduced cost;⏎however, contract agencies often create significant obstacles to⏎adoption.⏎• Self-Insured Employers⏎o Benefits Leadership / CHRO – Drive access, workforce productivity,⏎and cost strategy.⏎o Finance Leadership – Evaluate healthcare spend reduction and ROI.⏎o Third-Party Administrators (TPAs) – Influence implementation within⏎benefit structures.⏎Across all segments, AI adoption hinges on demonstrated cost reduction, quality⏎improvement, and risk mitigation.⏎a. What are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in⏎clinical care?⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please⏎consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎• Clinical data remains siloed across different systems: medications⏎(Surescripts), EHRS, CareQuality, Commonwell, EPIC, EPIC/QHIN.⏎CommonWell QHIN, State HIEs, etc… Even many QHINs continue to hedge⏎their bets by simultaneously supporting TEFCA/QHIN while also supporting⏎their own networks – even adoption today into a private network further⏎delays the realization of what TEFCA/QHIN can deliver. It is recommended⏎HHS mandate TEFCA/QHIN interoperability within the next 12 months –⏎minimally not allow entities to offer both a QHIN and their private network.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what⏎concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use⏎of AI in clinical care?⏎Page 7 | 8⏎⏎• The inhibitor to AI adoption is largely due to the risk adverse models of health⏎systems and payers. We have performed over 20M AI first clinical⏎encounters in 2025 through Direct-to-Consumer programs. While there is⏎work to do to enhance the experience the model continues to expand.⏎Health systems are mixed some believing demand has outgrown clinician⏎capacity, driving long wait times, rising costs, and inconsistent preventive and⏎chronic-care management; hence believing AI is an approach to close the⏎gap and facilitate scaling of their services. Other remain in the sideline.⏎10.Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and⏎their use clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers⏎• Elected not to respond #10⏎Page 8 | 8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0423♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-11T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sarah♔Jones♔♔New Mexico♔♔United States♔Tufts University School of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My work over the past several years has focused on how health systems adopt new technologies, and this RFI raises questions that I think are being framed in a way that should worry anyone who has spent time studying that process. The framing assumes that acceleration is the goal, and then asks how to achieve it. That is not a neutral starting position.⏎⏎Let me focus specifically on the question of what evidence standards should accompany accelerated adoption, because I think this is where the sequencing problem is most visible and most consequential.⏎⏎There is a reasonably well-documented gap between how clinical AI systems perform in controlled validation environments and how they perform once deployed at scale across heterogeneous patient populations. My colleagues at the ACR Data Science Institute published survey findings in 2024 showing that among 612 radiology department chairs and research directors, 68% reported that the peer-reviewed evidence base was insufficient to support adoption decisions. Fewer than 12% of validation studies indexed between 2021 and 2023 included prospective, multi-site data. These are not fringe concerns from AI skeptics. These are radiologists who want useful tools and are saying the evidence is not there yet.⏎⏎What concerns me about this RFI is that it seems to treat that evidentiary gap as a problem of access or deployment speed rather than as a signal that we may be moving prematurely. The implicit argument appears to be: if we can get reimbursement structures aligned and regulatory pathways smoothed, adoption will happen faster, and then real-world evidence will accumulate. I understand the logic. But healthcare technology adoption does not work that way in practice. Once a system is reimbursed and embedded in clinical workflows, the practical costs of reversing that decision become enormous. Vendors build around it. Hospitals train staff around it. EHR integrations get written. The question of whether the technology was actually doing what we thought it was doing gets progressively harder to ask, not easier.⏎⏎I am not arguing that AI in clinical care has no promise. Some applications clearly do. But "some applications show promise" and "HHS should act to accelerate adoption broadly" are very different propositions, and I am not sure the second one follows from the first.⏎⏎There is also something uncomfortable about the way this RFI was structured that I want to name directly. The question of whether society wants medicine organized around AI-assisted decision-making at this scale, under these conditions of evidentiary uncertainty, with liability rules still largely unresolved, is a political and moral question. It belongs to democratic deliberation. An RFI that asks how to accelerate something rather than whether to accelerate it is, at least partly, bypassing that question. I recognize HHS has a mandate to think about technology adoption and that this is a legitimate area of agency activity. But there is a difference between asking "what would responsible adoption look like" and asking "how do we move faster," and this document tilts toward the latter in ways that I think deserve pushback.⏎⏎The liability question in particular seems underexamined here. When an AI-assisted diagnostic tool contributes to a missed diagnosis, who is responsible? The vendor? The hospital? The clinician who relied on the recommendation? Until there is some coherent answer to that question, the incentive structure for developers to invest in rigorous post-market surveillance is weak. Accelerating reimbursement pathways before resolving that question doesn't solve the governance problem, it just means more systems get deployed under the same uncertain liability conditions.⏎⏎I would ask HHS to consider whether a more useful framing for the next phase of this work would be to ask what conditions need to be met before acceleration is warranted, rather than assuming acceleration is warranted and working backward to the conditions. That reordering matters. The 2024 NAM working group on AI in health care raised related concerns and I think their interim findings are worth revisiting before this process moves further toward implementation guidance.⏎⏎One practical thing I would suggest: HHS should consider whether any new reimbursement pathways for clinical AI tools should be explicitly time-limited and conditioned on post-market surveillance requirements with teeth, not just reporting requirements that accumulate in a database nobody is resourced to analyze.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0424♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from America's Essential Hospitals, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔America's Essential Hospitals♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C St. SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Ref: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎On behalf of our nearly 400 member hospitals that serve patients who face barriers to care, we⏎write to share how the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) can accelerate⏎adoption of responsible artificial intelligence (AI) to support clinical care for all. Essential⏎hospitals are committed to finding innovative ways to improve patient outcomes, and so we⏎look forward to continuing to work with HHS to ensure appropriate regulations and adequate⏎reimbursement for emerging AI technologies.⏎Since 1981, America’s Essential Hospitals has advanced policies and programs that promote⏎health and access to health care. We support our members with advocacy, policy development,⏎research, education, and leadership development. Communities depend on essential hospitals⏎for care across the continuum, health care workforce training, research, public health, and⏎other services. Supported by Essential Hospitals Institute, the association’s research and⏎education arm, essential hospitals innovate and adapt to lead all of health care toward better⏎outcomes and value.⏎The mission of essential hospitals closely aligns with President Trump’s vision to make all⏎Americans healthy. Essential hospitals are committed to serving people in all communities that⏎need access to quality care. Although essential hospitals account for only 6% of acute-care⏎hospitals nationwide, in 2023 they provided 29% of the nation’s charity care. About three-⏎quarters of the patients our members serve are uninsured or enrolled in Medicaid or Medicare.1⏎In addition, nearly two-thirds of essential hospitals provide services to rural patients and⏎1 Miu R, Kelly K, Nelb R. Essential Data 2025: Our Hospitals, Our Patients—Results of America’s⏎Essential Hospitals 2023 Annual Member Characteristics Survey. America’s Essential Hospitals.⏎November 2025. essentialdata.info. Accessed Feb. 19, 2026.⏎⏎communities.2 To meet the needs of all patients, essential hospitals constantly engage in robust⏎initiatives to improve quality and access, including efforts to combat chronic health conditions,⏎all while lowering health care costs and spending.⏎For the past several years, America’s Essential Hospitals has committed to supporting member⏎hospitals that harness the power of AI to improve health outcomes for all. In 2024, hospital⏎leaders developed policy principles to help ensure that AI tools can improve access to quality⏎care for underserved populations without unintended consequences.3 Additionally, we convene⏎a monthly interest group for member leaders who currently leverage AI to advance health⏎outcomes, enhance operational efficiency, support financial sustainability, and foster trust.⏎Based on feedback from our members that are developing, deploying, and evaluating AI best⏎practices and solutions, we offer the following comments on how HHS can leverage regulation,⏎reimbursement, and research to accelerate the adoption of responsible AI at essential hospitals.⏎Regulatory Tools⏎1. Policies on AI use in health care should clarify the roles of state and federal⏎laws and regulations. Further, policies must not conflict with requirements⏎from other regulatory bodies, such as accrediting organizations.⏎As HHS develops regulations governing AI in health care, the agency must consider the⏎interaction between existing federal and state laws and accreditations governing the provision⏎of health care to minimize additional compliance costs.⏎In states with existing AI laws, essential hospitals have invested significant time and financial⏎resources implementing AI tools and modifying their workflows to use the tools effectively and⏎legally. New federal regulations will require a deeper analysis of both federal and state laws and⏎integration of their requirements. While essential hospitals will comply with all applicable⏎regulations, HHS also should require vendors to ensure their products comply with federal and⏎state law in a timely fashion. Health care providers should not be held accountable when⏎vendors cannot comply.⏎Federal rulemaking must balance the benefits of regulation and standardization with the⏎required resources for compliance. System modification at essential hospitals is expensive, as is⏎staff training to correctly implement AI tools as regulations change. Frequent regulations will⏎stymie AI adoption at essential hospitals.⏎Further, hospitals must meet numerous health care facility accreditation requirements and⏎comply with laws safeguarding patients’ health care information. Federal AI regulations must⏎not conflict with these requirements. However, federal AI regulations that align with⏎accreditation requirements could benefit all stakeholders.⏎2 America’s Essential Hospitals. Policy Brief: Essential Hospitals Ensure Access to Care in Rural Areas.⏎March 2025. https://essentialhospitals.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/2025-Access-to-Care-inRural-⏎Areas-Brief.pdf. Accessed Jan. 28, 2026.⏎3 Artificial Intelligence Policy Principles. America’s Essential Hospitals. https://essentialhospitals.org/wp-⏎content/uploads/2026/02/2024-March-AI-Principles.pdf. Accessed Feb. 12, 2026.⏎2⏎⏎2. HHS should ensure vendors provide additional transparency and testing to⏎help hospital leaders make more informed decisions about AI solutions.⏎The proliferation of AI tools and rapid updates make selecting the right AI solution difficult.⏎Essential hospitals need transparent, standardized information about AI tools, including⏎development, evaluation data, data provenance, and return on investment, to enable⏎comparison and make informed decisions.⏎After AI tool and vendor selection, essential hospitals must conduct extensive testing before⏎training staff and implementing the tool, which can be costly and challenging. Essential hospital⏎leaders find it difficult to assess the validity of AI models and tools across clinical settings and⏎populations before committing to a product and testing it internally. Rather than having⏎individual hospitals and health systems conduct these tests, vendors should be required to test⏎these models in a competitive environment and conduct formal adversarial testing before their⏎products are declared ready for health care providers. In addition, vendors should offer flexible,⏎short-term contracting approaches.⏎While all AI solutions must be piloted before full implementation, HHS should require vendors⏎to test their products in various health care settings and ensure interoperability with other⏎health care technology vendors. This would reduce testing burden on health care providers and⏎accelerate AI adoption.⏎3. HHS should implement technologies and related standards to safeguard⏎against automating bias in AI systems.⏎If AI tools are not appropriately trained to include the complex patients that essential hospitals⏎serve, they may provide incorrect answers when diagnosing and treating them in real-world⏎care settings. AI tools deployed in clinical settings should be rigorously trained on⏎representative data and designed to perform safely and effectively for the populations that our⏎members serve. We therefore urge HHS to establish guidelines on mitigating all forms of data⏎bias and create strong AI training standards. HHS also should define appropriate human⏎oversight and accountability structures to monitor the ethical use of AI tools.⏎Reimbursement Challenges⏎1. HHS should limit the ability of private insurers to use AI tools to limit care.⏎Private insurers have rapidly deployed AI tools in claims processing, prior authorization⏎automation, and credentialing. These technologies can inappropriately restrict or delay access⏎to care. As these automated systems create new payment barriers, essential hospitals are⏎increasingly forced to divert limited resources away from patient care and toward adopting⏎their own AI tools to ensure that claims are processed and paid. For example, many essential⏎hospitals now rely on AI-driven claims review systems to prevent wrongful denials and payment⏎delays. Meanwhile, insurers continue to invest in increasingly sophisticated AI designed to⏎scrutinize, slow, or deny claims. Because insurers have significantly larger profit margins than⏎essential hospitals, they can buy the latest tools at a higher volume and faster rate. Therefore,⏎HHS should act to ensure private insurers do not employ AI to boost their profits at the expense⏎of care provision.⏎3⏎⏎2. HHS must address the financial barriers for essential hospitals to adopt⏎and implement AI solutions.⏎In 2023, members of America’s Essential Hospitals had an aggregate operating margin of -7.1%,⏎more than three times lower than the aggregate operating margins for all other hospitals⏎(-2.3%).4 These financial strains limit essential hospitals’ ability to invest in and scale AI⏎infrastructure and solutions. The high cost of AI tools limits hospitals’ ability to implement⏎multiple AI solutions at once, i.e., separate AI solutions in patient care, purchasing, and claims⏎processing. The cost also hampers a hospital’s ability to scale AI solutions systemwide.⏎HHS could mitigate these financial barriers by providing enhanced payments to support AI⏎adoption at essential hospitals through Section 1115 demonstration waivers or other Medicaid⏎or Medicare payment authorities. Supporting the development of AI tools at essential hospitals⏎also could help ensure that the benefits of this technology reach patients who need them most.⏎Research and Development Opportunities⏎Essential hospitals are actively testing new AI innovations and hope to continue working with⏎HHS to share best practices for using AI to benefit patients. Some early adopters report⏎improved diagnostic accuracy, optimized scheduling, targeted risk-stratification for⏎readmissions, and reduced staff burnout. However, our members also recognize the necessity of⏎tailored solutions for the high-need patients that they serve.⏎In addition to testing specific AI clinical applications, there is also a need to research best⏎practices for establishing AI governance frameworks in health systems. Many essential⏎hospitals lack the dedicated expertise, governance structures, and peer networks to translate AI⏎innovations into practice. They face emerging policy and regulatory developments and⏎mounting concerns about validity for complex populations. Thus, essential hospitals need⏎tailored, actionable guidance on AI governance, such as a framework or best practices for⏎implementing AI solutions and oversight structures. HHS could accelerate research on best⏎practices for implementing AI tools for safety net populations by incorporating research and⏎evaluation into targeted investments in essential hospitals’ ability to adopt AI technologies.⏎*******⏎America’s Essential Hospitals appreciates the opportunity to submit these comments. If you⏎have questions, please contact Director of Policy Robert Nelb, MPH, at 202-585-0127 or⏎rnelb@essentialhospitals.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jennifer DeCubellis (Feb 19, 2026 19:10:38 EST)⏎Jennifer DeCubellis⏎President and CEO⏎4 Miu R, Kelly K, Nelb R. Essential Data 2025: Our Hospitals, Our Patients—Results of America’s⏎Essential Hospitals 2023 Annual Member Characteristics Survey. America’s Essential Hospitals.⏎November 2025. essentialdata.info. Accessed Feb. 19, 2026.⏎4♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0425♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Center for Elders’ Independence , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Center for Elders’ Independence ♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Document Number: 2025-23641 (90 FR 60108)⏎Submitted by:⏎Center for Elders' Independence⏎510 17th Street⏎Oakland, CA 94612⏎Date: February 11, 2026⏎To:⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Introduction⏎On behalf of the Center for Elders' Independence (CEI), I am pleased to submit this response to the⏎Department of Health and Human Services' Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and⏎use of artificial intelligence in clinical care. As the Chief Information Officer for CEI, a non-profit⏎Program of All-Inclusive Care for the Elderly (PACE) organization that has served the East Bay of⏎California for over 40 years, I offer a perspective grounded in the practical realities of deploying⏎technology to support frail, medically complex seniors.⏎CEI operates under a fully capitated, value-based care model, assuming complete financial and⏎clinical responsibility for our participants. This structure aligns our incentives directly with the goals⏎articulated in this RFI: improving health outcomes, reducing costs, and fostering innovation. We⏎currently serve participants across Alameda and Contra Costa Counties through six PACE centers,⏎providing integrated services spanning medical care, rehabilitation, transportation, nutrition, and social⏎engagement. In 2025 alone, our teams provided over 9,300 doctor visits, served nearly 79,000 meals,⏎and coordinated over 171,000 transportation trips for our participants.⏎At CEI, we adopted the term "Augmented Intelligence" to describe our approach to AI. This framing⏎is intentional. It emphasizes that these technologies are designed to enhance and support the clinical⏎judgment, compassion, and expertise of our care teams and not to replace them. This philosophy has⏎been instrumental in building trust and fostering adoption among our staff, and we believe it⏎represents a model that HHS could promote more broadly.⏎The following responses address each of the ten specific questions outlined in the RFI, drawing on⏎our experience as both configuring and deploying technology solutions for geriatric care.⏎⏎Responses to Specific Questions⏎Question 1: Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Adoption⏎The most significant barriers to private sector innovation in AI for healthcare, and its adoption in⏎clinical care settings like ours, fall into four interconnected categories.⏎Data Fragmentation and Interoperability Challenges. The PACE model is predicated on holistic,⏎coordinated care. Yet the health data for our participants is scattered across a fragmented ecosystem⏎of external specialists, hospitals, pharmacies, and diagnostic laboratories. Despite progress on⏎standards like FHIR, true interoperability remains elusive. This fragmentation makes it extraordinarily⏎difficult to assemble the comprehensive, longitudinal datasets required to train, validate, and deploy⏎effective AI models for applications such as predictive risk stratification or personalized care planning.⏎Data Quality and the Risk of Algorithmic Bias. The frail elderly population we serve presents⏎complex, multi-morbid health profiles and an ethnically diverse population that are systematically⏎underrepresented in the large datasets used to train most commercial AI models. This creates a⏎substantial risk of algorithmic bias, where tools may perform poorly or produce inequitable outcomes⏎for our participants. Compounding this issue is the fact that much of the richest clinical information⏎resides in unstructured formats, such as clinician notes, care conference summaries, and social⏎worker assessments. These are difficult and costly to process for model development.⏎High Upfront Costs and Uncertain Return on Investment. As a non-profit organization, CEI must⏎be a responsible steward of its resources. The capital investment required for AI infrastructure,⏎specialized talent, and software licensing is substantial. The return on this investment is often realized⏎not in direct revenue, but in improved quality of life, reduced emergency department visits, and⏎delayed or avoided nursing home placements. Without clear reimbursement pathways or quality⏎incentives tied to AI-driven interventions, building a compelling business case for these investments⏎remains a significant challenge.⏎Workforce Integration and Change Management. Our clinicians and care coordinators are our⏎most valuable asset. For any technology to succeed, it must integrate seamlessly into their existing⏎workflows and demonstrably reduce their administrative burden. Tools that are perceived as adding⏎complexity, requiring extra steps, or undermining clinical autonomy will face resistance. The⏎"Augmented Intelligence" framing is essential to our change management strategy, as it positions⏎technology as a partner that empowers staff rather than a threat that diminishes their role.⏎Question 2: Recommended Regulatory, Payment, and Programmatic Changes⏎HHS should prioritize changes that address the foundational barriers to AI adoption while creating⏎positive incentives for innovation.⏎Mandate and Incentivize True Interoperability. The single most impactful action HHS can take is to⏎move beyond encouraging interoperability to actively mandating and incentivizing it. This includes⏎strengthening enforcement of existing information blocking rules and creating direct financial⏎incentives for providers and health IT vendors to implement and utilize modern, API-based data⏎exchange standards. Until data can flow freely and securely across the care continuum, the promise⏎of AI in healthcare will remain largely unrealized.⏎Develop AI-Specific Reimbursement Pathways. The current fee-for-service payment system is ill-⏎suited to reward the preventive, proactive interventions that AI enables. HHS should develop and pilot⏎new reimbursement mechanisms that recognize the value of AI-driven care. For value-based care⏎models like PACE, this could include enhanced capitation rates for organizations that demonstrate⏎⏎effective use of AI for risk prediction and care management, or shared savings programs that reward⏎the avoidance of costly acute care episodes.⏎Establish Regulatory Clarity and Safe Harbors. Regulatory ambiguity is a significant deterrent to⏎innovation. HHS should provide clear, practical guidance on the regulatory pathway for non-medical⏎device AI tools used in clinical settings. Furthermore, HHS should consider establishing regulatory⏎"sandboxes" or safe harbors that allow value-based care organizations to pilot and validate new AI⏎applications in a controlled environment, with defined parameters for patient safety and data privacy,⏎and a degree of protection from punitive action for good-faith efforts to innovate.⏎Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical Devices⏎The deployment of non-medical device AI in clinical care introduces several novel legal and⏎implementation challenges that existing governance structures are not fully equipped to address.⏎Accountability and Liability. When an AI tool provides a recommendation that informs a clinical⏎decision, the traditional chain of accountability becomes blurred. If an adverse outcome occurs, the⏎question of liability and who it rests with, be that the software developer, the healthcare organization⏎that deployed the tool, or the individual clinician who acted on the recommendation, is often unclear.⏎HHS can play a vital role by convening stakeholders from the legal, clinical, and technology⏎communities to develop a consensus framework for shared liability and indemnification. Such a⏎framework should be fair to all parties and, critically, should not place an undue burden on frontline⏎clinicians that would chill their willingness to use beneficial tools.⏎Privacy, Security, and Data Governance. AI models, particularly those hosted in cloud⏎environments, require robust data governance to protect patient privacy. As a PACE organization, we⏎are the fiduciaries of our participants' most sensitive health information. We need clear, enforceable⏎standards for data use agreements with AI vendors, particularly concerning the secondary use of data⏎for model training and the prevention of re-identification. HHS should consider issuing updated⏎guidance under HIPAA that specifically addresses the unique privacy considerations of AI in⏎healthcare.⏎Question 4: Promising AI Evaluation Methods for Non-Medical Devices⏎For AI tools that are not regulated as medical devices, evaluation must extend beyond traditional⏎accuracy metrics to encompass the full context of clinical use.⏎Workflow and Human-Centered Evaluation. The most promising evaluation methods assess how⏎an AI tool integrates into the clinical workflow and its impact on the humans who use it. Key questions⏎include: Does the tool save time or add steps? Does it reduce cognitive load and decision fatigue?⏎Does it enhance clinician confidence, or does it create confusion and alert fatigue? Rigorous usability⏎testing and qualitative research with end-users should be a standard component of any AI evaluation.⏎Post-Deployment Monitoring and Surveillance. An AI model that performs well in a controlled⏎testing environment may degrade over time in the real world as patient populations shift and care⏎patterns evolve, This is a phenomenon known as "model drift." Continuous post-deployment⏎monitoring is essential to ensure that AI tools remain accurate, fair, and equitable over time. This is⏎particularly critical for our diverse, multilingual participant population.⏎HHS can support these processes through several mechanisms. Grants and cooperative agreements⏎could fund the development of standardized, open-source testing methodologies for workflow⏎integration and human factors. HHS could also establish a national repository of best practices for⏎⏎post-deployment AI surveillance, potentially housed within an existing agency like AHRQ or ONC.⏎Prize competitions could incentivize the development of innovative tools for detecting algorithmic bias⏎and model drift in real-world clinical settings.⏎Question 5: Supporting Private Sector Activities⏎HHS can most effectively support private sector activities by acting as a convener, a standard-setter,⏎and a catalyst for collaboration.⏎Convene an AI for Geriatrics Center of Excellence. We strongly recommend that HHS facilitate the⏎creation of a public-private partnership focused specifically on AI for the elderly population. Such a⏎center could bring together PACE organizations, academic medical centers, AI developers, and⏎patient advocates to address the unique challenges of this underserved market. Its activities could⏎include creating and validating benchmark datasets representative of the geriatric population,⏎developing testing and certification standards for AI tools intended for use in geriatric care, and⏎disseminating best practices for ethical and effective implementation.⏎Recognize and Promote Industry-Led Standards. HHS should actively monitor and, where⏎appropriate, endorse industry-led efforts to develop standards for AI safety, transparency, and⏎accountability. By signaling which voluntary standards it considers credible, HHS can help healthcare⏎organizations make informed procurement decisions and encourage AI developers to invest in⏎responsible practices.⏎Question 6: AI Performance and Potential⏎In our experience, the AI tools that have delivered the most immediate and measurable value are⏎those focused on operational efficiency and care coordination. For a PACE organization, logistics are⏎paramount. Tools that optimize transportation routing, predict appointment no-shows, or automate⏎administrative tasks like prior authorizations can yield significant time and cost savings, freeing up⏎staff to focus on direct participant care.⏎However, the greatest potential for transformative impact lies in predictive analytics for preventive⏎care. For a frail senior population, the ability to accurately predict the risk of an adverse event, such⏎as a fall, a urinary tract infection, a hospitalization for heart failure exacerbation, or a decline in⏎functional status, would be profoundly valuable. Such a tool would enable our interdisciplinary care⏎teams to intervene proactively, adjusting care plans, increasing home visits, or initiating therapy⏎before a crisis occurs. This aligns perfectly with the PACE model's emphasis on keeping participants⏎healthy and independent in their homes.⏎Areas where AI has fallen short tend to involve tools that were not designed with the geriatric⏎population in mind. Generic clinical decision support tools trained on younger, healthier populations⏎often generate irrelevant or even harmful recommendations for our participants with multiple chronic⏎conditions and complex medication regimens.⏎Question 7: Organizational Decision-Makers and Administrative Hurdles⏎Within a healthcare organization like CEI, the decision to adopt a significant new technology like AI is⏎inherently collaborative, involving multiple stakeholders.⏎⏎Role Primary Concerns⏎Chief Medical Officer / Clinical efficacy, patient safety, impact on quality of care,⏎Medical Director clinician workflow⏎Operational efficiency, implementation logistics, staff training,⏎Chief Operating Officer⏎scalability⏎Technical feasibility, integration with existing systems, data⏎Chief Information Officer⏎security, vendor management⏎Cost, return on investment, budget allocation, reimbursement⏎Chief Financial Officer⏎implications⏎Board of Directors Strategic alignment, fiduciary responsibility, risk management⏎The primary administrative hurdle to AI adoption is the business case. Without a clear, demonstrable⏎link between an AI investment and either a new revenue stream, a reduction in costs, or an⏎improvement in quality metrics that directly affect payment or accreditation, it is difficult to secure the⏎necessary budget and organizational commitment. HHS can help overcome this hurdle by funding⏎research that quantifies the ROI of AI in value-based care settings and by creating payment⏎incentives that reward AI-driven quality improvements.⏎Question 8: Interoperability to Widen Market Opportunities⏎Enhanced interoperability would be the single most powerful catalyst for accelerating AI development⏎and widening market opportunities. The current fragmented data landscape creates a significant⏎barrier to entry for AI developers, who must navigate a patchwork of proprietary systems and⏎inconsistent data formats.⏎If HHS were to successfully mandate and enforce true interoperability, it would enable the creation of⏎comprehensive, longitudinal health records that could fuel a new generation of AI tools. For the⏎geriatric care market specifically, we would highlight the following priorities:⏎Data Type / Standard Impact on AI Development⏎Standardized ADL/IADL Would enable AI models to predict functional decline and tailor care⏎Assessments plans to maintain independence.⏎Standardized SDOH data (e.g., using Gravity Project standards)⏎Social Determinants of⏎would allow AI to identify and address non-medical factors that⏎Health (SDOH) Data⏎profoundly impact senior health.⏎Standardized, shareable care plans would enable AI to support care⏎Care Plan⏎coordination across the entire care team, including external⏎Interoperability⏎providers.⏎Comprehensive, real-time medication data would enable AI to⏎Medication⏎identify polypharmacy risks and drug interactions, a critical issue for⏎Reconciliation Data⏎seniors.⏎⏎Question 9: Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎Our participants and their caregivers have expressed both hopes and concerns regarding the⏎adoption of AI in their care.⏎Challenges They Wish AI to Address. Participants and caregivers are most interested in AI tools⏎that simplify the practical burdens of managing complex care. This includes easier communication⏎with their care team, more convenient scheduling of appointments and transportation, personalized⏎reminders for medications and health activities, and accessible, understandable health education. For⏎caregivers, tools that provide peace of mind, such as remote monitoring or alerts for potential health⏎changes, are highly valued.⏎Concerns About AI Adoption. The primary concern expressed by our participants and their families⏎is the potential for technology to depersonalize care. They fear that an over-reliance on AI could⏎diminish the human connection, compassion, and trust that define their relationship with our staff. For⏎many of our participants, the social interaction at our PACE centers is as important to their well-being⏎as the medical care they receive. Any AI strategy must be implemented in a way that enhances,⏎rather than erodes, these vital human relationships. The "Augmented Intelligence" framing directly⏎addresses this concern by making clear that technology is a tool to support, not supplant, human⏎caregivers.⏎Question 10: AI Research Priorities⏎HHS should prioritize research that addresses the specific needs of vulnerable populations and⏎generates the evidence base needed to support broader AI adoption.⏎AI for Social Determinants of Health (SDOH). Research is urgently needed on how to effectively⏎use AI to identify and address SDOH factors, such as social isolation, food insecurity, housing⏎instability, and transportation barriers, that have an outsized impact on the health of seniors. This⏎includes developing models that can integrate SDOH data with clinical data to generate actionable⏎insights for care teams.⏎Longitudinal Studies on AI Impact in Value-Based Care. HHS should fund rigorous, long-term⏎studies to measure the costs, benefits, and clinical impact of AI tools within integrated, value-based⏎care models like PACE. Such studies should examine not only clinical outcomes but also participant⏎and caregiver experience, staff satisfaction, and total cost of care. This evidence is essential to justify⏎broader adoption and to inform the design of appropriate reimbursement policies.⏎Ethical AI for Vulnerable Populations. Research is critically needed to develop robust methods for⏎detecting and mitigating bias in AI models when applied to vulnerable populations, including frail⏎elders, individuals with cognitive impairment, and those from historically marginalized communities.⏎This research should inform the development of standards and best practices for ensuring that AI in⏎healthcare is fair, equitable, and trustworthy.⏎Regarding published findings (Question 10a), while the literature on AI in healthcare is growing⏎rapidly, there is a notable gap in high-quality evidence specifically focused on the geriatric population⏎and on integrated care models like PACE. Most published studies focus on narrow clinical⏎applications in acute care settings. HHS-funded research could help fill this gap.⏎Regarding the literature on costs and benefits (Question 10b), the existing literature tends to focus on⏎the potential cost savings from AI, often with optimistic assumptions. There is less rigorous analysis of⏎the full economic picture, including implementation costs, ongoing maintenance, and the distribution⏎⏎of benefits and burdens across different stakeholders. A more nuanced, comprehensive approach to⏎economic evaluation is needed.⏎Conclusion⏎The Center for Elders' Independence is deeply committed to harnessing the power of Augmented⏎Intelligence to improve the lives of the frail seniors we serve. We believe that the PACE model, with⏎its integrated structure and value-based incentives, represents an ideal environment for the⏎responsible development and deployment of AI in clinical care.⏎We thank the Department of Health and Human Services for its leadership in this critical area and for⏎the opportunity to contribute our perspective. We stand ready to partner with HHS, whether through⏎pilot programs, research collaborations, or policy development, to build a future where technology⏎empowers compassionate, equitable, and effective care for all Americans.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Amarjit Dhillon⏎Chief Information Officer⏎Center for Elders' Independence⏎510 17th Street, Oakland, CA 94612⏎adhillon@cei.elders.org⏎This response is submitted in accordance with the instructions provided in the Federal Register notice⏎(90 FR 60108) and is identified as a response to the "HHS Health Sector AI RFI."♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0426♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-25T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kevin♔Flores♔♔Florida♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My daughter spent two years getting the wrong answers from doctors who I think were doing their best but just didn't have the time or the tools to catch what was actually wrong with her. She was thirteen when a specialist in Tampa finally got it right. Thirteen. So when I read about AI coming into diagnostic settings I genuinely want to believe it could help another family skip those two years. I do.⏎⏎But here is what I keep getting stuck on. The summary of this request talks about "accelerating adoption" and I understand why that sounds good on paper. Faster is better, usually. Except my daughter's misdiagnoses were also fast. The problem wasn't speed. The problem was no one stopped and said wait, let me look at this differently.⏎⏎I guess my real question, and I'm not sure where exactly in this RFI it fits, is about accountability when the AI gets it wrong. Not if. When. Because no tool is perfect and I've seen what happens when the wrong answer gets stamped on a kid's chart and then every appointment after that is colored by that wrong answer. The doctor in 2021 who finally ordered her imaging told us he almost didn't because her file from the previous two years kept pointing him in a different direction. He almost trusted the pattern instead of the patient.⏎⏎So who is responsible when an algorithm creates that pattern? Is it the hospital that bought the software? The company that built it? I genuinely don't know and I couldn't find a clear answer in what I read about this proposed rule.⏎⏎Also I want to ask something that might be off topic but I'm not sure. I work in customer service and I also run a small side business with eight employees. We do some healthcare-adjacent work, specifically scheduling and patient communication services for small clinics. Does any of this apply to us? If the clinics we contract with start using AI tools and we're handling some of that patient data flow, are we covered under whatever requirements come out of this? I couldn't tell from the language whether the accountability pieces apply only to large health systems or if it trickles down to smaller vendors and contractors. That's a real practical question for me and I'd appreciate if the final guidance made that clear because right now I honestly have no idea where we'd fall.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0427♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Hackensack Meridian Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Hackensack Meridian Health♔♔♔♔♔♔. |⏎nonee Hackensack⏎*oug" Meridian Health⏎=⏎⏎Via Electronic Submission - http://www.regulations.gov⏎⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane⏎⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎⏎330 C St SW, Floor 7⏎⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎⏎February 23, 2026⏎⏎RE: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎⏎On behalf of Hackensack Meridian Health (HMH), we thank you for the opportunity to comment on⏎the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), in collaboration with the Office of the Deputy⏎Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC), Request for Information on Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AD as part of Clinical Care.’⏎⏎HMH is the largest, most comprehensive, and truly integrated health care network in New Jersey,⏎comprising a network of hospitals that includes three academic medical centers, one university⏎teaching hospital, two children’s hospitals, eight community hospitals, a behavioral health hospital,⏎two rehabilitation hospitals and one long-term acute care hospital. Six of our 18 hospitals maintain⏎robust academic medical programs. HMH also has more than 500 patient care locations, including⏎ambulatory care centers, surgery centers, cancer centers, home health services, long-term care and⏎assisted living communities, ambulance services, lifesaving air medical transportation, fitness and⏎wellness centers, rehabilitation centers, urgent care centers, and physician practice locations. HMH⏎has more than 40,000 team members and more than 7,000 physicians within its network and is a⏎distinguished leader in health care philanthropy, committed to the health and well-being of the⏎communities it serves.⏎⏎As a national leader in clinical innovation, HMH has integrated Al-enabled tools across diagnostics,⏎surgery, care coordination, and administrative workflows to improve outcomes, reduce burden, and⏎enhance patient safety. This includes the use of AI to analyze mammograms in real time to highlight⏎potential signs of breast cancer and improve diagnostic accuracy and timeliness of intervention;⏎Al-assisted robotic precision surgery to enhance surgical accuracy and enable minimally invasive⏎procedures; and Al-driven tools to optimize hospital operations and electronic health record⏎workflows. This strategic and responsible adoption of AI reflects HMH’s ongoing commitment to⏎innovation, operational excellence, and high-quality patient-centered care.⏎⏎Overview of HMH’s Comments⏎⏎190 Fed. Reg. 60108 (December 23, 2025).⏎⏎343 Thornall Street | Edison, NJ 08837 | 848-888-4400 | HackensackMeridianHealth.org⏎⏎Letter to Assistant Secretary Keane re: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 2⏎⏎HMH strongly supports HHS’s goal of accelerating the responsible adoption of AI in clinical care. AI⏎is no longer experimental in health care and is already embedded across care delivery. Federal policy⏎must therefore evolve from pilot-focused experimentation toward scalable, predictable, and⏎trustworthy deployment. HMH’s comments are informed by direct operational experience and focus⏎on how HHS can effectively apply its core policy levers of regulation, reimbursement, and research⏎and development (R&D), to accelerate adoption while protecting patients, clinicians, and health⏎systems.⏎⏎HMH’s comments emphasize that the central challenge facing clinical AI adoption is no longer⏎technological feasibility, but the absence of clear, scalable federal policy frameworks governing⏎accountability, validation, reimbursement, and implementation. Across our responses, HMH⏎underscores that health systems are being asked to assume disproportionate responsibility for⏎evaluating, governing, and managing AI tools without consistent national standards, predictable⏎payment pathways, or clear liability allocation. This uncertainty increases operational risk, slows⏎adoption of high-value tools, and diverts resources away from patient care.⏎⏎Accordingly, HMH recommends that HHS prioritize a coordinated, risk-based federal approach that⏎enables provider reliance on standardized AI validation and certification, aligns reimbursement with⏎demonstrated clinical and system-level value, clarifies legal responsibility for non-medical device AI,⏎and strengthens interoperability and workforce readiness. As detailed in the specific responses⏎below, federal leadership in these areas would reduce fragmentation, improve patient and clinician⏎trust, and accelerate the safe and responsible integration of AI into routine clinical care.⏎⏎General Comment Request⏎⏎HHS seeks stakeholder input on how existing regulatory frameworks, payment policies, and federal⏎R&D investments can be modernized to support the safe, scalable, and value-driven adoption of AI in⏎clinical care.’⏎⏎A. Regulation⏎⏎HMH supports a predictable, risk-proportionate federal regulatory posture that protects patient⏎safety and privacy without imposing unnecessary or duplicative burdens on providers. As HMH⏎previously emphasized in its comments on the Health Technology Ecosystem Request for⏎Information issued by the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services, the primary constraint on AI⏎adoption in clinical care is no longer model performance, but rather operational risk, workflow⏎disruption, governance complexity, and workforce trust.’⏎⏎Existing regulatory frameworks were not designed for Al-enabled care and do not adequately⏎address issues such as model transparency, bias mitigation, explainability, continuous learning, and⏎post-deployment monitoring. In the absence of clear federal standards, health systems are often⏎expected to independently validate and oversee each AI tool they deploy. This approach is⏎operationally unrealistic and diverts limited resources from patient care.⏎⏎HMH continues to recommend that HHS formally endorse and rely on standardized,⏎⏎2 Id. at 60108.⏎390 Fed Reg. 21034 (May 16, 2025).⏎⏎Letter to Assistant Secretary Keane re: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 3⏎⏎risk-based AI development and governance frameworks such as the National⏎Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) Artificial Intelligence Risk⏎Management Framework (AI RMEF).* AI developers should be required to demonstrate⏎compliance through independent audits or certifications. Health systems should be able to rely on⏎such certifications in procurement and deployment decisions, ensuring accountability rests primarily⏎with developers rather than end users.⏎⏎HMH also reiterates the need to modernize HIPAA and related privacy frameworks⏎to explicitly address Al-specific risks, including re-identification of de-identified⏎data, secondary use of health data for model development, and emerging data⏎modalities such as voice and sensor data. Federal leadership is essential to prevent a⏎fragmented, state-by-state patchwork of AI and privacy requirements that undermines safe and⏎consistent national deployment.⏎⏎B. Reimbursement⏎⏎HMH strongly agrees that existing payment policies, particularly encounter-based fee-for-service⏎systems, may unintentionally slow AI adoption or distort incentives.® Many of Al’s most meaningful⏎benefits, such as reduced clinician burden, improved diagnostic accuracy, workflow optimization,⏎and prevention of downstream adverse events, do not map cleanly to discrete billable encounters.⏎⏎HMH has previously recommended that Medicare and Medicaid establish clear and⏎predictable reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled technologies and services based⏎on demonstrated clinical impact and value. Reimbursement models should reflect the total⏎cost of ownership associated with AI adoption, including integration, workflow redesign, training,⏎and ongoing support, rather than focusing narrowly on vendor subscription fees or per-use charges.⏎⏎HHS should approach reimbursement policy for Al-enabled technologies with careful attention to⏎how payment structures shape innovation and adoption decisions. Payment frameworks that⏎emphasize readily quantifiable, encounter-level outputs may unintentionally skew incentives toward⏎tools optimized for billing visibility rather than those delivering meaningful clinical, operational, or⏎preventive value. To avoid these distortions, HHS and CMS should prioritize payment policies that⏎account for AI’s full spectrum of value, explicitly recognize contributions to quality, safety, and⏎workforce sustainability, and provide sufficient predictability to support multi-year investment and⏎integration across diverse provider environments.⏎⏎C. Research and Development⏎⏎HMH agrees with HHS that federal investments in applied AI research, implementation science, and⏎public-private partnerships can play a critical role in moving promising tools from concept to routine⏎clinical use. HMH encourages HHS to focus R&D investments on real-world⏎deployment challenges, including workflow integration, governance models,⏎clinician trust, and post-deployment monitoring.⏎⏎*https://www.nist.gov/itl /ai-risk-management-framework⏎5 Id. at 60109.⏎6 Id. at 60110.⏎⏎Letter to Assistant Secretary Keane re: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 4⏎⏎HMH believes that conflicting state privacy and AI laws, ambiguous federal guidance on continuous⏎learning AI, and reliance on private consortia to fill federal gaps all impede scalable research and⏎implementation. HHS can address these barriers by providing clear federal direction on data use,⏎model updating, and accountability, and by aligning research initiatives with NIST-led standards⏎rather than fragmented private frameworks.⏎⏎Specific Question Responses⏎⏎HHS requests targeted feedback on practical barriers, policy reforms, governance issues, evaluation⏎methods, interoperability needs, and patient concerns shaping AI adoption in clinical care.’⏎⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎⏎While interoperability challenges, liability uncertainty, and inconsistent reimbursement remain⏎significant, HMH’s operational experience demonstrates that the most consequential barriers to AI⏎adoption in clinical care arise from implementation and governance risk rather than model⏎performance. Currently, health systems must assume responsibility for AI tools embedded in clinical⏎workflows over extended periods, without clear federal standards defining accountability,⏎monitoring expectations, or acceptable risk thresholds. In the absence of standardized federal⏎validation and assurance mechanisms, providers are expected to independently evaluate and oversee⏎each AI tool, which is operationally unrealistic and diverts scarce clinical, compliance, and⏎informatics resources away from patient care.⏎⏎Workflow disruption and lack of transparency further impede adoption. AI tools that are not⏎seamlessly integrated into electronic health record workflows, generate non-actionable alerts, or lack⏎clear explanation of limitations undermine clinician confidence and sustained use. As HMH has⏎emphasized in prior comments, clinician trust is not built through retrospective accuracy metrics⏎alone, but through consistent real-world performance, explainability at the point of care, and⏎alignment with clinical accountability structures. Absent these elements, even technically⏎sophisticated tools fail to achieve meaningful adoption.⏎⏎Workforce readiness and regulatory fragmentation compound these challenges. Health systems face⏎a growing need to upskill clinicians, administrators, compliance staff, and governance leaders in AI⏎literacy, risk management, and oversight. At the same time, emerging state-level AI and privacy⏎requirements threaten to create a fragmented compliance environment that undermines⏎national-scale innovation, procurement, and research. Coordinated federal leadership is essential to⏎reduce duplicative oversight, establish clear expectations, and enable responsible deployment across⏎diverse provider settings.⏎⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎⏎HMH encourages HHS to focus regulatory efforts on predictability, standardization, and appropriate⏎⏎allocation of responsibility. As such, HHS should anchor federal AI policy in the NIST AI RMF and⏎promote standardized, risk-based validation and certification pathways for clinical AI tools.⏎⏎Ibid.⏎⏎Letter to Assistant Secretary Keane re: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 5⏎⏎Developers should bear responsibility for demonstrating compliance through independent⏎assessment, while health systems should be able to rely on such certifications rather than duplicating⏎validation and assurance efforts for each deployment. This approach would better align⏎accountability with those best positioned to manage design, data, and model risks.⏎⏎From a payment perspective, HMH reiterates that encounter-based reimbursement structures are⏎poorly suited to capture AI’s contribution to efficiency, safety, and clinician well-being. Many⏎Al-enabled benefits - such as reduced documentation burden, improved triage, earlier detection of⏎clinical deterioration, and avoidance of downstream adverse events - accrue longitudinally and⏎across settings rather than within discrete billable encounters. HMH recommends HHS explore⏎reimbursement approaches that recognize Al’s system-level value, including through⏎value-based payment models, shared savings arrangements, and programmatic⏎incentives tied to demonstrated outcomes rather than volume.⏎⏎Programmatically, HMH believes federal support should extend beyond technology development to⏎include workforce training and implementation capacity. Grants, learning collaboratives, and⏎demonstration programs focused on applied deployment, governance models, and clinician⏎education would address real-world adoption barriers more effectively than additional pilot funding⏎alone. Without parallel investment in workforce readiness and operational infrastructure, even⏎validated AI tools will struggle to achieve scale or sustainability.⏎⏎3. What novel legal and implementation issues exist [regarding non-medical device AI] and⏎what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎⏎HMH has consistently emphasized that liability uncertainty represents one of the most significant⏎deterrents to AI adoption, particularly for AI tools that fall outside the FDA’s medical device⏎framework but nonetheless influence clinical decision-making and care delivery. Health systems and⏎clinicians face ambiguity regarding responsibility for harm arising from algorithmic error, data drift,⏎or system failure, despite having limited visibility into or control over model design, training data,⏎and ongoing updates.⏎⏎Consistent with prior HMH comments, HMH recommends that HHS advance a federal,⏎risk-based liability framework for AI in clinical care that aligns responsibility with⏎the entities best positioned to understand, prevent, and mitigate system risks.⏎Developers and vendors exercise primary control over model design, training data selection,⏎performance specifications, update cycles, and known limitations. Accordingly, they should bear⏎primary responsibility for AI system safety, reliability, and fitness for intended use. Federal policy⏎should require developers to maintain appropriate liability coverage and provide clear⏎indemnification protections for providers when harm results from defects, performance failures, or⏎undisclosed limitations in AI systems used as intended and in accordance with labeling and⏎contractual requirements.⏎⏎Hospitals and clinicians that deploy certified AI systems in good faith and in accordance with⏎established standards should be protected from disproportionate liability exposure. Clear federal⏎guidance on accountability for non-medical device AI would reduce legal uncertainty, encourage⏎responsible innovation, and prevent the chilling of adoption of tools that have the potential to⏎meaningfully improve patient outcomes and clinician experience.⏎⏎Letter to Assistant Secretary Keane re: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 6⏎⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎postdeployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes?⏎⏎HMH believes that the absence of standardized, scalable evaluation methodologies remains a⏎significant barrier to responsible AI adoption in clinical care. Health systems are often required to⏎independently assess model validity, bias, robustness, and ongoing performance, even when tools are⏎developed and marketed by sophisticated vendors. This fragmented approach creates duplication,⏎increases costs, and introduces variability in patient protections across institutions. Without clear⏎federal benchmarks, evaluation efforts risk being inconsistent, overly burdensome, or insufficiently⏎aligned with real-world clinical risk.⏎⏎HMH recommends that HHS further operationalize and support standardized,⏎risk-based evaluation methods grounded in NIST’s AI RMF. Federal policy should clearly⏎distinguish expectations for high-risk clinical decision-support tools versus lower-risk administrative⏎or operational applications, with proportional requirements for pre-deployment testing,⏎post-deployment monitoring, human-in-the-loop safeguards, and transparency. In addition, HHS⏎should invest in applied evaluation infrastructure, such as shared testing environments, reference⏎datasets, and federally-supported demonstration projects, to reduce duplicative burden on providers⏎while improving overall safety and trust.⏎⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎⏎HMH cautions strongly against reliance on private or quasi-regulatory consortia to establish AI⏎assurance standards in the absence of clear federal leadership. As HMH has previously noted, when⏎federal standards are unclear or underdeveloped, private entities such as the Coalition for Health AT⏎have sought to fill the void by issuing their own validation frameworks and assurance mechanisms.®⏎While collaboration with industry and academia can be valuable, delegating de facto regulatory⏎authority to private consortia risks concentrating influence among large incumbents, creating⏎inconsistent standards, and erecting barriers for smaller or emerging developers.⏎⏎HMH therefore supports the Administration’s scrutiny of private or quasi-regulatory⏎AI assurance efforts operating outside formal federal oversight and strongly⏎recommends that HHS, in collaboration with ASTP/ONC, anchor AI assurance efforts⏎on NIST-led standards and risk management frameworks as the foundation for⏎responsible AI development in health care. HMH believes NIST is best positioned to lead this⏎work given its technical expertise, transparent approach, and established role in consensus-based⏎standards development. This approach should be reinforced through coordination with the National⏎Committee for Quality Assurance and the National Quality Forum to ensure alignment with quality⏎measurement, accreditation, patient safety, and interoperability objectives. Clear federal⏎endorsement of the NIST AI RMF would provide consistent, nationally recognized criteria for⏎validation, fairness, and accountability, accelerating responsible AI adoption while maintaining⏎⏎8⏎⏎https: //www,jointcommission.org/en-us/knowledge-library /news/2025-09-jc-and-chai-release-initial-g⏎uidance-to-support-responsible-ai-adoption⏎⏎Fa⏎⏎\⏎⏎Letter to Assistant Secretary Keane re: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 7⏎⏎patient safety and public trust. Importantly, certified status should be meaningful and actionable,⏎allowing health systems to reasonably rely on certification without duplicating evaluations internally.⏎⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AT for clinical care?⏎⏎Interoperability remains a foundational enabler of safe and scalable AI adoption. While progress has⏎been made through Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR)-based standards and⏎application programming interface (API) access, significant gaps persist across clinical, claims,⏎imaging, and patient-generated data sources. Fragmented data access limits the ability of AI tools to⏎deliver accurate, equitable, and context-aware insights, and increases integration costs for providers.⏎⏎HMH recommends that HHS continue to prioritize and expand FHIR-native⏎interoperability requirements, standardized APIs, and data access policies that⏎support real-time and longitudinal data use. Federal efforts to promote consistent data⏎quality standards and benchmarking would further accelerate AI development and deployment while⏎reducing integration burden on health systems. Improved interoperability would not only support⏎innovation, but also enhance competition, reduce vendor lock-in, and improve patient outcomes.⏎⏎g. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎⏎HMH believes patient trust is essential to the responsible use of AI in clinical care. Al-enabled tools⏎can improve access, navigation, safety, and personalization of care, but these benefits will only be⏎realized if patients understand how AI is used and have confidence in the safeguards protecting their⏎data and rights.⏎⏎HMH recommends that federal policy prioritize transparency, strong privacy⏎protections, and patient-centered communication regarding Al-enabled tools. Patients⏎should have clear information about when and how AI is used, how their data are protected, and⏎what safeguards are in place to address bias and error. HHS should also encourage mechanisms for⏎patient feedback and choice where appropriate. Centering patient trust in AI policy will be essential⏎to sustaining adoption and delivering on Al’s promise in health care.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎⏎HMH appreciates HHS’s leadership in advancing responsible AI adoption in clinical care. HMH⏎welcomes continued engagement with HHS and the opportunity for in-person discussion on how⏎targeted reforms to regulation, reimbursement, and R&D can help accelerate responsible AI⏎adoption that enhances patient care, preserves trust, and sustains innovation.⏎⏎If you have questions, please feel free to contact me at 201-370-8279 or Sarah.Lechner@hmhn.org.⏎⏎eee⏎⏎Sapte <7⏎⏎OG SC Ya⏎⏎& L hner⏎⏎Nath Chief of Esters♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0428♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Michigan Healthcare Freedom, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michigan Healthcare Freedom♔♔♔♔♔♔Thank you for this opportunity to comment.⏎⏎Both privacy and fraud protections are essential for securing our healthcare freedoms as Americans.⏎⏎As proposed, these are competing priorities. To resolve the conflict, I request the following AI policies be followed.⏎⏎1. Please use AI to detect fraudulent reimbursement attempts, while shielding personal health information from AI access.⏎⏎2. Please make clinical AI genuinely consumer-based by limiting access to records which have had specific patient opt-in only. ⏎⏎3. Please provide ZERO government (taxpayer and program) funds for AI clinical research and development. Government agencies, insurance, and other industries inevitably use such funds and products to control Americans' healthcare decisions. Using forced funding for such ends is immoral and unconstitutional. Instead, secure our individual healthcare freedoms by keeping federal health agencies out of clinical R&D, and letting the private sector pursue private R&D funds.⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Abigail Nobel, BSN, RN, MA⏎President, Michigan Healthcare Freedom♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0429♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from VieCure, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔VieCure♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎VIA ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎330 C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: 2025‑23641; RIN 0955‑AA13 Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On December 23, 2025, the United States Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎published a Request for Information to accelerate the adoption and use of artificial intelligence⏎in clinical care and ensure its safe, effective, and responsible integration into the healthcare⏎system.1⏎VieCure appreciates the opportunity to comment on the HHS Request for Information seeking⏎input on actions to accelerate the adoption and use of AI in clinical care. VieCure is a cancer⏎care innovation company that provides a comprehensive AI digital ecosystem to advance⏎precision medicine in community cancer care settings.⏎VieCure is dedicated to advancing AI-enabled, evidence-based care delivered through our suite⏎of digital applications for healthcare providers (“Platform”). Our Platform includes a patented⏎clinical decision support platform (“CDS”), an integrated electronic health record system⏎(“EHR”), and mobile applications available to community oncology providers. We strongly⏎support a federal strategy that ensures equitable access to innovation, protects patients’ timely⏎access to guideline‑concordant care, and reduces friction in adopting AI applications.⏎________________________⏎CONFIDENTIAL – Contains Trade Secrets / Confidential Commercial Information – Do Not Disclose⏎1. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services. (2025). Request for information: Accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence as part of⏎clinical care (RIN 0955-AA13). Federal Register, 90(60108–60109).⏎⏎Despite the transformative potential of AI-enabled tools to improve cancer care, two systemic⏎barriers continue to limit equitable adoption across the settings where most Americans receive⏎treatment.⏎First, there is a significant disparity in access to advanced digital infrastructure and clinical⏎decision support across community and rural oncology practices—even though these sites⏎deliver the majority of U.S. cancer care. Without targeted support, these providers lack the⏎resources needed to implement, operationalize, and maintain AI-driven technologies that enable⏎guideline-concordant, precision cancer care.⏎As a result, patients treated outside major academic centers face uneven access to innovative⏎tools that could meaningfully influence outcomes. To close this gap, HHS should prioritize rural⏎and community cancer centers in federal AI adoption programs and ensure these sites have the⏎technical, operational, and financial support needed to implement and sustain AI solutions⏎successfully.⏎Second, to sustain long-term adoption, incentives must be aligned with existing reimbursement⏎structures and real-world oncology workflows, so that providers are encouraged and financially⏎able to integrate AI-enabled decision-making support into daily clinical practice over time, not⏎just during isolated initiatives. Aligning incentives to reflect the ongoing value AI delivers is⏎essential to drive durable adoption, reduce care variability, and ensure patients benefit from⏎consistent, evidence-based treatment regardless of geography.⏎HHS Should Recognize the Critical Role of AI in Equitable Care Access for Community Care⏎Settings with Targeted Funding and Grants to Community Clinics⏎Community oncology is where most Americans diagnosed with cancer receive their care, and it⏎is often more accessible, more cost‑efficient, and closer to patients’ homes compared with⏎hospital/academic settings. 2,3 Certain populations, however, continue to face obstacles⏎accessing care, for example, according to the National Comprehensive Cancer Network, rural⏎patients face higher cancer mortality despite lower incidence, and structural barriers.⏎Additionally, financial barriers, transportation, digital access, and social inequalities limit⏎equitable outcomes.3 In our experience, community centers have limited resources to adopt⏎new therapies due to the lack of support for information at the point of care. Additionally, they⏎lack access to sophisticated technology support teams to help them adopt new AI⏎technologies. AI-enabled solutions, led by clinical decision and other AI-connected applications,⏎equip clinicians with the tools required to deliver personalized medicine without manual⏎research at the point of care and help reduce these disparities.⏎________________________⏎CONFIDENTIAL – Contains Trade Secrets / Confidential Commercial Information – Do Not Disclose⏎2. The ASCO Post. Preferred Sites of Cancer Care: Community Oncology Practices vs Hospitals and Academic Centers. Published May 3, 2024.⏎https://ascopost.com/news/may-2024/preferred-sites-of-cancer-care-community-oncology-practices-vs-hospitals-and-academic-centers/ 3. Florida⏎Cancer Specialists & Research Institute. Evaluating the Site of Service and Oncology Care Costs. April 2024. https://flcancer.com/wp-⏎content/uploads/2024/04/21190-FCS-SiteofService-CaseStudy-0424-FINAL.pdf⏎⏎Recommendation 1: HHS should explicitly recognize community and rural oncology practices as a⏎priority deployment environment for AI. HHS should direct targeted support, technical assistance,⏎and matched grants to expand AI‑enabled care‑coordination capabilities, including clinical⏎decision-making, in independent and network-based community practices, the settings most at risk⏎of being left behind by uneven AI diffusion and utilization.⏎HHS Should Partner with CMS to Create Reimbursement Pathways for AI Technologies⏎AI can streamline documentation, reduce administrative burden, help triage patient care options,⏎and enhance providers' ability to make evidence-based decisions at the point of care.⏎Numerous AI tools exist across the oncology workflow to enhance healthcare operations that⏎face limited resources. The value will come not only from streamlining operations but also from⏎clinicians' ability to make the best decision for the patient when presented with both evidence⏎and patient-driven information at the point of care.⏎The knowledge base of clinical options is rapidly expanding, with novel diagnostics,⏎therapeutics, and supportive care, to the point that clinicians are becoming overwhelmed by⏎complexity. AI tools can help reduce this complexity in a timely way, thereby reducing wait times⏎for care and improving patient satisfaction with care and outcomes.⏎Currently, Ambient AI scribes are among the fastest‑adopted generative AI tools in health⏎systems. They are associated with reductions in clinician burnout and cognitive load. These⏎benefits are especially valuable in oncology, where the burden of both decision-making and⏎documentation can be high amid the rapid pace of new diagnostic and therapeutic⏎innovations.4,5,6⏎However, the high upfront and ongoing costs of AI platforms, integration, and compliance⏎disproportionately strain community practices operating on thin margins with limited resources⏎to support implementation and maintenance. Surveys of health systems identify financial⏎concerns, regulatory uncertainty, and tool immaturity as top barriers to AI adoption.7 Without⏎aligned incentives, advances will concentrate in large systems, widening disparities and gaps in⏎care within community care and thereby jeopardizing the timeliness and quality of care received⏎by the majority of US citizens diagnosed with cancer seen in the community oncology setting.⏎Recommendation 2: HHS should partner with CMS to establish reimbursement pathways—within⏎existing models such as the Enhancing Oncology Model (EOM) or other models that exist in other⏎areas of CMS for technology adoption—that recognize the clinical and operational value of⏎validated, clinician-in-the-loop AI. This includes allowing add-on payments or implementing a⏎________________________⏎CONFIDENTIAL – Contains Trade Secrets / Confidential Commercial Information – Do Not Disclose⏎4. McCarson ST, Schatz A, Hood V, et al. NCCN Policy Summit: Cancer Across Geography. J Natl Compr Canc Netw. 2024;22(5):284-292.⏎https://jnccn.org/view/journals/jnccn/22/5/article-p284.xml ; 5. Peterson Health Technology Institute (PHTI). Leading Health Systems: AI-Powered Scribes⏎Alleviate Clinician Burnout; Financial Impact Unclear. March 25, 2025. https://phti.org/announcement/ai-scribes-reduce-clinician-burnout/ ; 6. Olson KD,⏎Meeker D, Troup M, et al. Use of Ambient AI Scribes to Reduce Administrative Burden and Professional Burnout. JAMA Netw Open. 2025;8(10):e⏎(online).https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jamanetworkopen/fullarticle/2839542 ; 7. Poon EG, Lemak CH, Rojas JC, Guptill J, Classen D. Adoption of⏎artificial intelligence in healthcare: survey of health system priorities, successes, and challenges. J Am Med Inform Assoc. 2025;32(7):1093-1100.⏎https://academic.oup.com/jamia/article/32/7/1093/8125015⏎⏎technology-enabled modifier when practices deploy AI-powered tools such as CDS, precision⏎medicine enablement tools, or operational AI applications that measurably enhance guideline⏎concordance, expand access to innovative therapies, or improve care delivery efficiency in⏎community oncology settings.⏎Conclusion⏎VieCure stands ready to collaborate with providers, professionals, and governmental⏎organizations to advance the safe, equitable adoption of AI in clinical care. As HHS considers⏎strategies to accelerate responsible AI deployment, it is essential that federal policy reflects the⏎realities of where most cancer patients receive treatment and the structural barriers that limit⏎access to innovation in those settings.⏎• To achieve meaningful and equitable progress, HHS should prioritize community and rural⏎oncology practices for targeted federal support, technical assistance, and grant funding so⏎these sites have the infrastructure and resources needed to implement and sustain AI-⏎enabled clinical decision-making and care coordination tools.⏎• HHS should partner with CMS to establish reimbursement pathways that recognize the⏎clinical, operational, and patient outcome value of validated, clinician-in-the-loop AI,⏎including through add-on payments or technology-enabled modifiers within models such as⏎the EOM.⏎By clearly prioritizing community oncology and offering incentives that make AI adoption⏎feasible for practices operating on thin margins, HHS can ensure that AI-enabled precision⏎medicine is accessible to all patients. By aligning federal investment with the areas of greatest⏎need and opportunity, HHS can help reduce care variability, expand access to innovative⏎therapies, and promote guideline-concordant cancer care nationwide.⏎VieCure appreciates HHS’s leadership and welcomes continued dialogue as these important⏎policies advance.⏎Sincerely,⏎Michael Power⏎Chief Executive Officer, VieCure⏎________________________⏎CONFIDENTIAL – Contains Trade Secrets / Confidential Commercial Information – Do Not Disclose♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0430♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Asian & Pacific Islander American Health Forum, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Asian & Pacific Islander American Health Forum♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted at www.regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Jim O’Neill⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave., SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Office of the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎330 C St., SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Comments Regarding the Accelerating Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care: HHS Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Deputy Secretary O’Neill and Dr. Keane:⏎The Asian & Pacific Islander American Health Forum (APIAHF) writes to highlight⏎critical considerations for Artificial Intelligence (AI) security and guardrails, workforce⏎impacts and transparency as the Department of Health and Human Services considers⏎AI adoption as part of clinical care.⏎The healthcare industry was amongst the first to explore AI applications and earlier⏎adopters to deploy AI to scale – from clinical workflows to insurance approvals and⏎other automated applications – utilizing AI at more than twice the rate of the broader⏎economy.12 AI has already transformed the healthcare landscape, and to disregard its⏎impacts, both positive and negative, can only harm patients.⏎With a national network of over 300 community-based organizational partners in 44⏎states and territories, APIAHF is the nation’s oldest national health advocacy⏎organization dedicated to improving the health and well-being of over 26.4 million⏎Asian Americans, Native Hawaiians and Pacific Islanders (AANHPIs) who would be⏎affected by these technologies.⏎1 Md Ashrafur Rahman, Evangelos Victoros, et al., Impact of Artificial Intelligence (AI) Technology in⏎Healthcare Sector: A Critical Evaluation of Both Sides of the Coin, Clinical Pathology, (Jan. 22, 2024),⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10804900/.⏎2 Greg Yap, Derek Xiao, et al., 2025: The State of AI in Healthcare, Menlo Ventures, (Oct. 21, 2025),⏎https://menlovc.com/perspective/2025-the-state-of-ai-in-healthcare/.⏎⏎AI Security, Guardrails, and Public Trust⏎The promise of AI in clinical care cannot be realized without robust security controls and governance⏎frameworks that protect sensitive health information and patient safety. AI systems handling protected⏎health data must integrate cybersecurity best practices, privacy-by-design principles, and continuous⏎validation protocols to guard against breaches, adversarial attacks, and systemic failure.⏎In addition to basic security measures, AI must consider health privacy protections including compliance⏎with HIPAA guidelines.3 This means, any and all training for AI models must be in closed loop systems⏎and have clear patient consent to drive improvement.⏎Without such protections, AI adoption risks eroding trust among patients, especially communities⏎historically subject to discrimination in healthcare systems. Americans are concerned about how AI⏎utilizes their data and how adoption of this data will affect their daily lives, including the quality of their⏎healthcare treatment and coverage.4,5 In order to build and sustain trust, there must be robust oversight⏎that includes quality standards and continuous monitoring and checkpoints – outside of industry, and in⏎conjunction from trusted leaders within community.6⏎Equally important is transparency in model performance, limitations and validation.7 AI developers and⏎healthcare organizations should publish performance metrics across diverse populations and explicitly⏎disclose known limitations, so clinicians and patients can interpret outputs appropriately. This level of⏎transparency enables informed decision-making, supports accountability, and guards against hidden⏎algorithmic failures that could disproportionately harm marginalized groups.⏎Real-World Challenges and Risks⏎Data is the most critical component of AI, and quality data is essential to make AI work for all⏎Americans.8 Accurate and representative data collection and data disaggregation is critical to eliminating⏎biases and further reducing clinical errors with AI in healthcare.⏎3 Pushpendra Jangid, Asha Meena, et al., The Role of Artificial Intelligence in Safeguarding Patient Privacy in Healthcare⏎Systems, Journal of Pharmacy & BioAllied Sciences, (Jun. 18, 2025), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12244842/.⏎4 Fathom, Rising AI Use, Rising Calls for Action, (Dec. 2025), https://fathom.org/pdf/fathom-insights-report-2025.pdf.⏎5 Jana Gundlack, Carolin Thiel, et al., Patient’s Perceptions of Artificial Intelligence Acceptance, Challenges, and Use in⏎Medical Care: Qualitative Study, Journal of Medical Internet Research, (May 15, 2025),⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12123243/.⏎6 Fathom, supra note 4.⏎7 Jana Fehr, Brian Citro, et al., A trustworthy AI reality-check: the lack of transparency of artificial intelligence products in⏎healthcare, Frontiers in Digital Health, (Feb. 20, 2024), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10919164/.⏎8 Lora Mois Aroyo, Matt Lease, et al., Data Excellence for AI: Why Should You Care, ACM IX Interactions, (2022),⏎https://research.google/pubs/data-excellence-for-ai-why-should-you-care/.⏎2⏎⏎AI bias is not hypothetical. AI should improve, not amplify biases in clinical performance. Real clinical⏎AI tools have shown disparate effects across different demographic groups:⏎• AI models exhibiting underdiagnosis bias in chest radiographs for under-served patient⏎populations due to bias across multiple datasets, specifically for female patients, younger⏎patients, Black and Hispanic patients, and patients with lower socioeconomic status (via⏎insurance coverage). With patients who belonged in more than one of these categories, they were⏎even more likely to experience underdiagnosis.9⏎• An AI algorithm that assigned similar risk scores to Black and white patients even when Black⏎patients had worse health outcomes, including Black patients with greater chronic conditions.10⏎The result was that Black patients were less likely than equally sick white patients to be enrolled⏎for extra care or health programs. The algorithm didn’t use race as a predictor, but because Black⏎patients historically received less medical spending for the same level of illness, the real-world⏎bias toward Black patients resulted in under-identified care.⏎• AI models to detect skin cancers were trained predominantly using datasets of images of lighter⏎skin, developing an inherent bias toward the dominant skin tone and lesion colors from the⏎trainings.11 This can lead to underdiagnosis or misdiagnosis of patients with darker skin.⏎This is not to say that AI algorithms are inherently biased. These examples underscore how⏎insufficiently diverse training data, lack of representative validation, and opaque model design can⏎reinforce existing health inequities, particularly in communities that have already faced bias in clinical⏎settings, such as many AANHPI subgroups.12⏎According to the 2024 National Academies report which revisits the groundbreaking 2003 Unequal⏎Treatments report; racial and ethnic inequities in health and healthcare persist, costing our nation⏎millions in preventable deaths and hundreds of billions of dollars annually.13 One of the key factors is⏎the lack of uniform or comprehensive data on race and ethnicity which prevents data-driven decision⏎making in clinical settings.14⏎Race and ethnicity are not the only considerations for AI bias. A widely used AI model used to help⏎physicians diagnose and treat sepsis earlier identified just 7 percent of patients with sepsis who did not⏎receive timely treatment, but did not detect sepsis in 67 percent patients of those who did developed⏎sepsis.15 The model was tested in only a few geographical settings and was not correctly “calibrated” for⏎9 Laleh Seyyed-Kalantari, Haoran Zhang, et al., Underdiagnosis bias of artificial intelligence algorithms applied to chest⏎radiographs in under-served patient populations, Nature Medicine, (Dec. 10, 2021), https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-⏎021-01595-0.⏎10 Ziad Obermeyer, Brian Powers, et al., Dissecting racial bias in an algorithm used to manage the health of populations,⏎Science, (Oct. 25, 2019), https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aax2342.⏎11 Navneet Melarkode, Kathiravan Srinivasan, et al., AI-Powered Diagnosis of Skin Cancer: A Contemporary Review, Open⏎Challenges and Future Research Directions, Cancers, (Feb. 13, 2023), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9953963/.⏎12 National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine, Ending Unequal Treatment: Strategies to Achieve Equitable⏎Health Care and Optimal Health for All, (2024), https://www.nationalacademies.org/publications/27820.⏎13 National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine, Ending Unequal Treatment: Strategies to Achieve Equitable⏎Health Care and Optimal Health for All: Role of the Department of Health and Human Services, (2024),⏎https://nap.nationalacademies.org/resource/27820/Policy_Brief_Ending_Unequal_Treatment_HHS.pdf.⏎14 Id.⏎15 Liz Richardson, Artificial Intelligence Can Improve Health Care—but Not Without Human Oversight, Pew, (Dec. 16,⏎2021), https://www.pew.org/en/research-and-analysis/articles/2021/12/16/artificial-intelligence-can-improve-health-care-but-⏎not-without-human-oversight.⏎3⏎⏎other areas, showing that AI models are not one-size fits all. What works for certain populations does⏎not mean it will work for others.⏎According to a 2020 study, out of 74 studies used to develop image-based diagnostic AI systems, there⏎were only 16 states represented, with the majority of these studies relying on data from only California,⏎New York, and Massachusetts.16 This means that rural states and rural communities were completely⏎excluded – and their quality of care as diagnosed by AI suffers. This is especially concerning, given that⏎AI is increasingly being deployed to supplement rural healthcare.17 Given the recent $50 billion Rural⏎Health Fund investment by the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services to transform and support⏎rural health landscape, there must be intentional investment in rural health data to bridge gaps in care.18⏎To prevent AI from exacerbating disparities, developers and health systems must ensure diverse⏎representation in data, bias audits and fairness oversight. AI research through intentional design and⏎accountability must be core to HHS’s mission for implementation.⏎For the AANHPI community whose many subgroups span numerous languages, cultural practices and⏎health profiles, standardized models trained on limited populations risk insensitive or inaccurate results.⏎Disaggregated validation, robust bias mitigation methods and community-based monitoring must be⏎core features of AI deployment frameworks.⏎Future Opportunities of AI in Health and Healthcare⏎Despite risks, AI can expand care access and quality in ways that directly benefit underserved⏎communities, particularly when thoughtfully designed:⏎• Language access tools: AI-powered natural language processing and translation services can⏎support real-time interpretation and documentation in clinical settings for limited English⏎proficient (LEP) patients. This can improve communication, medication instruction⏎comprehension, and treatment adherence across communities, especially for patients with⏎complex medical needs.19 While many hospitals offer some sort of interpretation for LEP⏎patients, many report that there is a shortage of available, qualified and timely translators, despite⏎being mandated for large healthcare systems – leading to significant gaps in patient care.20⏎• Culturally sensitive patient engagement: Generative AI can produce culturally distinct responses⏎that helps tailor responses to cultural norms, beliefs, and health literacy levels, improving patient⏎16 Amit Kaushal, Russ Altman, et al., Geographic Distribution of US Cohorts Used to Train Deep Learning Algorithms,⏎JAMA, (Sep. 2020), https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama/fullarticle/2770833.⏎17 Kinalyne Perez, Daniela Wisniewski, et al., Investigation into Application of AI and Telemedicine in Rural Communities: A⏎Systematic Literature Review, Healthcare, (Feb. 4, 2025), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC11816903.⏎18 Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services, CMS Announces $50 Billion in Awards to Strengthen Rural Health in All 50⏎States, (Dec. 29, 2025), https://www.cms.gov/newsroom/press-releases/cms-announces-50-billion-awards-strengthen-rural-⏎health-all-50-states.⏎19 Amelia K. Barwise, Susan Curtis, et al., Using artificial intelligence to promote equitable care for inpatients with language⏎barriers and complex medical needs: clinical stakeholder perspectives, Journal of the American Medical Informatics⏎Association, (Dec. 14, 2023), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10873784/.⏎20 Elaine C. Khoong, Alicia Fernandez, Addressing Gaps in Interpreter Use: Time for Implementation Science Informed⏎Multi-Level Interventions, Journal of General Internal Medicine, (May 4, 2021),⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8606497/.⏎4⏎⏎understanding and trust.21 Often, a lack of cultural sensitivity in clinical settings can cause⏎further disconnect in patient-provider communication and trust.22⏎• Bridging healthcare gaps in underserved settings: Like the rise of telehealth in rural areas, AI can⏎assist in bridging healthcare gaps in underserved areas – especially in less-resourced regions that⏎may not have access to certain technologies for early diagnosis and detection.23⏎One example of AI models benefiting Asian Americans is the ability for early detection of stomach⏎cancer through non-invasive screening methods. Stomach cancer is one of the deadliest cancers in the⏎United States due to late diagnosis.24 In the U.S., only 30 percent of patients who are diagnosed reach⏎the five-year survival mark while in other countries with national screening programs, the survival rate⏎is much higher from 60-70 percent.25 Currently, medical societies do not recommend universal routine⏎screening for stomach cancer in the U.S., because it is not “common in the U.S.” despite high incidence⏎rates among certain groups.26 Because there are no standard or routine screening tests recommended for⏎stomach cancer, patients see higher insurance denials due to lack of “medical necessity.”27⏎AI can create more efficient screening protocols by using noncontrast CT imaging which is considered a⏎routine procedure and is more cost effective and less invasive than an endoscopy.28 Additionally, CTs⏎are more widely available and faster than endoscopies, making them a key tool, especially for⏎populations that may not be able to quickly access care or insurance approvals.29 This AI model, coined⏎GRAPE, was created as a large-scale screening method to identify gastric cancer; a huge benefit for⏎patients who have been denied from life-saving screenings due to cost or access.30⏎21 Jackson G. Lu, Lesley Luyang Song, et al., Cultural tendencies in generative AI, Nature Human Behaviour, (Jun. 20,⏎2025), https://www.nature.com/articles/s41562-025-02242-1.⏎22 Saras Henderson, Maria Horne, et al., Cultural competence in healthcare in the community: A concept analysis, Health &⏎Social Care in the Community, (Mar. 7, 2018), https://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1111/hsc.12556.⏎23 Emma Gurevich, Basheer El Hassan, et al., Equity within AI systems: What can health leaders expect?, Healthcare⏎Management Forum, (Oct. 13, 2022), https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9976641/.⏎24 Chul S. Hyun, Rong Wang, et al., Gastric Adenocarcinoma in the United States: The Toll of Late Diagnosis and Racial⏎and Geographic Disparities, JCO Oncology Advances, (Oct. 27, 2025), https://ascopubs.org/doi/10.1200/OA-25-00063.⏎25 Emily Moskal, Stomach cancer hits Asian populations harder, Stanford Medicine, (Dec. 16, 2022),⏎https://med.stanford.edu/news/insights/2022/12/stomach-cancer-asians.html.⏎26 Rachel Martin, New Study Highlights Need for Coordinated Approach to Stomach Cancer Screening and Prevention, Yale⏎School of Medicine, (Oct. 27, 2025), https://medicine.yale.edu/news-article/new-study-stomach-cancer-screening-and-⏎prevention/.⏎27 Gretchen Morgenson, ‘Would he have lived?’ When insurance companies deny cancer care to patients, NBC News, (Dec.⏎27, 2024), https://www.nbcnews.com/news/investigations/-lived-health-insurance-companies-deny-cancer-care-patients-⏎rcna182611.⏎28 Can Hu, Yingda Xia, et al., AI-based large-scale screening of gastric cancer from noncontrast CT imaging, Nature⏎Medicine, (Jun. 24, 2025), https://www.nature.com/articles/s41591-025-03785-6.⏎29 National Cancer Institute, Computed Tomography (CT) Scans and Cancer, https://www.cancer.gov/about-⏎cancer/diagnosis-staging/ct-scans-fact-sheet (last visited Feb. 20, 2026).⏎30 Hu, supra note 28.⏎5⏎⏎Conclusion⏎AI holds transformative potential for clinical care, but only if adopted with strong guardrails, transparent⏎evaluation, and equity-centered governance. Federal strategies should:⏎1. Mandate security, explainability and performance transparency across patient demographics.⏎2. Incentivize inclusive data practices, bias audits and community accountability boards to ensure⏎equitable outcomes.⏎3. Prioritize tools that expand access and language-sensitive care for underserved and diverse⏎populations.⏎By embedding these principles into AI adoption and oversight policies, HHS can safeguard patient⏎welfare, uphold public trust, and foster innovation that enhances clinical care for all Americans.⏎For more information, please contact the APIAHF policy team (policy@apiahf.org). Thank you for your⏎consideration of our comment.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Asian & Pacific Islander American Health Forum (APIAHF)⏎6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0431♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Notice♔2026-01-05T05:00Z♔false♔2025-23641♔90 FR 60108♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care♔2026-01-05T05:00Z♔2026-02-24T04:59:59Z♔false♔♔♔♔♔2026-01-04T00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔0♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔https://downloads.regulations.gov/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001/content.pdf,https://downloads.regulations.gov/HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001/content.html♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0432♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Parkinson Disease Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Parkinson Disease Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 10, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC, 20201⏎RE: HHS Office of the Secretary Document Number 2025-23541, RIN 0955-AA13,⏎Comments in Response to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use⏎of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Mr. Steven Posnack:⏎The American Parkinson Disease Association1 (APDA) applauds HHS’s efforts to optimize⏎the use of artificial intelligence in clinical care to support patients and caregivers, while⏎ensuring protection of patient privacy and rights. We appreciate the opportunity to provide⏎a response to the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care, including providing information on challenges in⏎clinical care that can be addressed through AI and concerns about the use of AI in clinical⏎care from the patient and caregiver perspective.⏎The APDA Voices of Parkinson’s Council (V of P) is a select group of Parkinson’s disease⏎(PD) patients, care partners, and caregivers, that informs APDA programming, research⏎initiatives, public policy and advocacy efforts, and resource development. The V of P⏎provided APDA with feedback regarding both the challenges in clinical care that can be⏎addressed through AI, as well as concerns regarding potential negative consequences⏎associated with the use of AI in clinical care.⏎1 The American Parkinson Disease Association is a nationwide grassroots network dedicated to fighting⏎Parkinson’s disease (PD) and works tirelessly to help the approximately one million people with PD in the⏎United States live life to the fullest in the face of this chronic, neurological disorder. Founded in 11961, APDA⏎has raised and invested more than $313 million to provide outstanding patient services and educational⏎programs, elevate public awareness about the disease, and support research designed to unlock the⏎mysteries of PD and ultimately put an end to this disease.⏎20 F Street NW, Suite 709, Washington, DC 20001 | apdaparkinson.org⏎⏎Clinical Care Challenges that can be Addressed with AI⏎APDA V of P members identified several clinical care challenges that can be addressed⏎through AI, including challenges related to administrative burden, patient and provider⏎education, PD screening, PD diagnosis, and PD treatment. V of P members expressed⏎interest in AI tools that reduce the time patients and providers must spend on⏎administrative tasks during a clinical care encounter. For example, AI can be utilized to⏎aggregate patient clinical history over time and across care settings allowing the patient⏎and provider to spend more time discussing care concerns and treatment options during a⏎clinical care visit.⏎V of P members expressed interest in AI tools that record a clinical visit and generate a⏎clinical note without the provider typing during the visit. This allows for an increased level⏎of engagement between the provider and patient. The AI generated clinical note serves as⏎documentation for the provider as part of the patient’s medical record and a patient visit⏎summary for the patient.⏎V of P members expressed interest in the use of AI tools to provide individualized, timely,⏎and accurate PD clinical care information to patients and caregivers. Members also⏎expressed that use of AI tools validated by PD specialists may improve the knowledge,⏎expertise, and quality of care delivered by providers without specialized PD expertise.⏎AI tools may also improve efficiency in screening and diagnosis of PD. Early symptoms of⏎PD, such as a loss of smell or trouble sleeping, are early indicators for many common⏎conditions. AI-enabled screening tools may help identify meaningful patterns of symptoms⏎and efficiently refer patients to PD specialists for more timely PD screening and diagnosis.⏎For people already diagnosed with PD, members expressed interest in the use of AI to⏎better monitor PD symptoms, identify meaningful changes in symptoms, and refer the⏎patient to appropriate specialists and services for timely follow up care.⏎Members frequently mentioned that identifying medications to effectively manage their PD⏎symptoms felt like a trial-and-error process. Members expressed strong interest in the⏎development and use of AI tools that could assess patient symptoms and provide⏎individualized recommendations related to medication type, dose, and frequency of⏎dosage based on the patient’s clinical profile. V of P members were interested in an AI tool⏎that could provide recommendations or modify treatment plans based on a patient’s⏎clinical history, patient preferences, and evidence-based treatment, for example, tools⏎that could flag potential drug interactions for providers to inform treatment decisions.⏎20 F Street NW, Suite 709, Washington, DC 20001 | apdaparkinson.org⏎⏎Concerns about the Use of AI in Clinical Care⏎While APDA V of P Council members support appropriate use of AI in clinical care, council⏎members noted some concerns about the potential negative consequences of its use.⏎Generally, V of P members are supportive of AI tools that supplement but do not supplant⏎the role of the provider in care delivery. For example, members did not want providers to⏎become overly reliant on AI tools, out of concern that over-reliance on AI technology would⏎lead to reduced interactions between the PD patient with a human provider and result in a⏎lack of personalized care.⏎The V of P council also expressed concern over what individual or entity is legally liable for⏎patient safety, quality of care, and care outcomes when AI tools are used. For example,⏎members frequently cited concerns over the accuracy of AI tools to screen, diagnose, and⏎treat PD and concern over what individual or entity is accountable for oversight or⏎responsible for inaccuracies. V of P members also frequently expressed concerns related⏎to use of AI tools and patient privacy, including data storage, access, and use, and whether⏎officials can ensure AI-tools will comply with the Health Insurance Portability and⏎Accountability Act.⏎Finally, members expressed concern over insurance coverage for AI tools in clinical care⏎and an overall concern of a potential increase in patient out-of-pocket costs for care if AI⏎tools are implemented.⏎In response to V of P feedback, APDA urges HHS officials to identify oversight and⏎enforcement processes to ensure these new technologies protect patient privacy, quality⏎of care, and patient safety. APDA also urges officials to identify opportunities to reduce⏎patient costs for AI tools and ensure the benefits of these new technologies are⏎proportionately distributed to patients based on patient need and value of the technology⏎rather than by the ability to pay out-of-pocket for these services.⏎In the US, there are approximately 90,000 new PD cases each year, representing a new PD⏎diagnosis every 6 minutes. As the PD population continues to increase in the US, APDA⏎remains ready to partner with HHS to ensure new technological advances, including⏎artificial intelligence, address the priorities and concerns of the PD patient community.⏎Again, we appreciate the opportunity to provide comments. If you have any questions or⏎require additional information please contact Emma Plourde, APDA Director of Health⏎Policy, at eplourde@apdaparkinson.org or 202-763-6801.⏎20 F Street NW, Suite 709, Washington, DC 20001 | apdaparkinson.org⏎⏎Sincerely,⏎Leslie A. Chambers⏎President and Chief Executive Officer⏎American Parkinson Disease Association⏎20 F Street NW, Suite 709, Washington, DC 20001 | apdaparkinson.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0433♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from SimplePractice, LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔SimplePractice, LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔SimplePractice Response to HHS RFI: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Submitted to: Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ASTP/ONC), U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎RFI Reference: HHS-ONC-2026-0001 | Federal Register Doc. 2025-23641⏎Submitted by: SimplePractice, LLC⏎Date: February 23, 2026⏎Contact: Lindsay Oberleitner, Ph.D., LP, Head of Clinical Strategy, SimplePractice⏎About SimplePractice⏎SimplePractice is a leading practice management and electronic health record (EHR) platform⏎built for independent behavioral health, mental health, and wellness practitioners. We serve over⏎250,000 mental health practitioners across all 50 states, the majority of whom are solo or⏎small-group practitioners, including licensed psychologists, social workers, counselors, marriage⏎and family therapists, nurse practitioners, and psychiatrists.⏎We occupy a unique position in the AI conversation: we are both building AI-powered tools for⏎clinical care and supporting the independent practitioners who adopt them. That gives us direct,⏎ground-level insight into what AI adoption looks like in behavioral health settings—the wins, the⏎hesitations, and the real-world gaps that federal policy can help close.⏎This submission is grounded in responses from our Clinical Advisory Board and Customer⏎Advisory Board, many of whom use our platform daily and agreed to share their perspectives for⏎this submission.⏎What We Know: AI Is Working—And Mental Health Practitioners Have Real Concerns⏎Where It's Working⏎AI-assisted documentation is already changing practice for the better. As one SimplePractice⏎Customer Advisory Board member put it:⏎"Documentation currently has improved efficiency in my practice and allowed more eye⏎contact and interactions with the patient [versus the] computer."⏎- Zachary Moraity, MD⏎One of SimplePractice’s Clinical Advisors, who also uses SimplePractice’s AI powered note⏎taker with his patients, stated:⏎“AI assisted note taking has been a game changer, allowing me to focus on being in the⏎moment rather than writing things down. It’s actually changed the way I speak to⏎patients. I now summarize my thoughts at the end of the session, telling patients clearly⏎⏎what my impression is and plan going forward. I started doing this to make the ambient⏎AI tool work better and I found that it really helps the patient understand how I’m thinking⏎and forces me to explain/answer questions about my formulation.”⏎- Ish Bhalla, MD⏎This reflects what we see across our platform: when AI tools are well-designed and⏎well-implemented, they reduce the administrative burden that pulls practitioners away from their⏎patients. That is a win worth protecting—and building on.⏎“One of the most exciting prospects is using AI to improve clinical care. I love the idea of⏎AI helping develop a case conceptualization and offering treatment suggestions. I⏎strongly believe that it would still be up to the therapist to use their background, training,⏎and clinical intuition to modify, adapt, and employ the AI suggestions. However, having⏎AI as a tool to assist with some of the heavy lifting would take a major administrative⏎load off of therapists and allow them to do their jobs more effectively.”⏎- Max Maisel, PhD⏎Where Mental Health Practitioners Are Hesitating⏎Adoption is not universal. Mental health practitioners are navigating genuine uncertainty, and⏎that uncertainty is suppressing uptake in ways that policy can directly address. Our team and⏎our advisors identified five core concerns:⏎1. Unclear liability:⏎When an AI-assisted note contributes to a clinical decision or a payer dispute, or a breach, who⏎is responsible? The practitioner? The platform? The AI vendor? This ambiguity is one of the top⏎concerns our advisors raised.⏎“The biggest barriers I anticipate are liability ambiguity and uncertainty around HIPAA⏎compliance. It's hard to feel confident adopting a new tool if it is unclear who is⏎accountable if something goes wrong.”⏎- Moe Ari Brown, LMFT⏎Similarly, as we see increases in the number of AI vendors in the field, clinicians are not always⏎clear on the standards that have been adhered to within the product.⏎“There’s still uncertainty around how data is processed across platforms, and whether⏎information is retained or shared beyond its intended use. I’m cautious about how patient⏎information is handled, stored, and protected. Until HIPPA safeguards are crystal clear,⏎caution isn’t resistance; it’s my ethical responsibility.”⏎- Dr. Dwayne Buckingham⏎2. The learning curve:⏎Mental health practitioners will adopt tools that save time, but only if those tools are intuitive and⏎come with accessible training.⏎“Cost is a real barrier that I have encountered in regards to adopting AI tools in my⏎practice. Quality AI tools aren’t cheap, and implementing them responsibly requires time,⏎⏎training, and ongoing oversight. While I understand the benefits of AI, I am slightly⏎concerned about the cost of adopting it into my practice.”⏎- Dwayne Buckingham, PhD, LCSW-C⏎3. Reimbursement risk:⏎Practitioners see very real potential value of AI in supporting reimbursement. Dr. Dwayne⏎Buckingham states:⏎“I believe AI could significantly reduce my administrative burden…eligibility checks,⏎claims follow-ups, and reimbursement processes often require additional staff many⏎private practices can’t afford.”⏎However, they are also beginning to worry that AI-assisted documentation could work against⏎them, if their notes look "too clean" or insufficiently individualized, or if patient symptoms are not⏎accurately reflected with AI usage that payers will deny claims. Dr. Ish Bhalla adds:⏎“I don’t want third parties to have access to transcripts or recordings of sessions with⏎patients for the purpose of malpractice claims or payment restrictions. I don’t want clients⏎to feel concerned about their words and actions being used against them in any way⏎either.”⏎4. Clinical integrity:⏎Several advisors worried about the risk of going on "autopilot", and becoming so reliant on AI⏎that they miss a critical clinical signal. This isn't an argument against AI. It's an argument for⏎implementation standards that keep clinical judgment front and center. One of our customer⏎advisors, Dr. Max Maisel, notes that:⏎“One concern would be if therapists over-rely on AI and don't do their due diligence.⏎However, with proper training and education about how AI works and how it doesn't⏎work, I believe this can be largely avoided.”⏎5. Patient-facing AI:⏎Mental health practitioners want AI help with patient communication, but they're cautious. The⏎concern: "patient frustration or AI providing inaccurate interpretation/recommendations without⏎safeguards." Patient-facing AI features deserve a higher bar of oversight, and guidance should⏎reflect that distinction.⏎Our Recommendations⏎The RFI asks about regulation, reimbursement, and research and development. Our⏎recommendations are organized around those three themes, with a focus on what matters most⏎for behavioral health and independent practice.⏎1. Regulation: Clarify Accountability (Responds to RFI Q1, Q2, Q3)⏎The problem: Practitioners don't know how existing rules apply to AI. Does using AI⏎documentation create a HIPAA risk? What attestation is required? Who is liable if something⏎⏎goes wrong? This uncertainty is a real adoption barrier, and a clear, plain-language framework⏎from HHS could help to clarify these issues.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎● Publish clear guidance clarifying how HIPAA, professional licensure standards, and⏎existing documentation requirements apply when mental health practitioners use⏎AI-assisted tools.⏎● Clarify the liability allocation when AI tools contribute to a clinical note, a care⏎recommendation, or a documentation error.⏎● Create a certification or safe harbor pathway for EHR and practice management⏎platforms that implement AI responsibly so practitioners can trust the tools they use.⏎● Establish a minimum standard for clinical attestation: AI may assist, but the practitioner's⏎review and signature must remain the standard of record.⏎2. Regulation: Risk-Based Frameworks Over Bans (Responds to RFI Q3, Q5)⏎The problem: The emergence of consumer-facing AI chatbots that purport to provide mental⏎health support has raised legitimate safety concerns. Some of these tools operate outside any⏎clinical relationship, without human oversight, and without accountability for adverse outcomes,⏎leading some policymakers to propose outright bans on "therapy chatbots." However,⏎SimplePractice urges HHS to advocate for a risk-based framework rather than categorical⏎prohibitions. The distinction that matters is not whether AI is involved, but whether a licensed⏎clinician remains accountable for the patient's care and what safeguards have been put into⏎place. The current draft notes concerns about patient-facing AI providing "inaccurate⏎interpretation/recommendations without safeguards." This concern is well-founded, but the⏎solution is appropriate safeguards, not prohibition of all AI in patient communication.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎● Adopt a risk-tiered regulatory posture that distinguishes between AI tools that support⏎practitioner judgment (lower risk) and AI tools that substitute for it (higher risk).⏎○ Low Risk: AI-assisted documentation, scheduling, billing automation⏎○ Medium Risk: AI-generated treatment suggestions, clinical decision support⏎○ High Risk: Patient-facing AI providing clinical guidance or interaction with⏎practitioner involvement⏎○ Critical Risk: Autonomous AI making treatment decisions without clinical⏎oversight⏎● Safeguards should align with the risk-tiered levels. While low risk tools may simply⏎require practitioner attestation or platform certifications, tools with increased risk may⏎involve a combination of safeguards appropriate for the risk level such as:⏎human-in-the-loop reviews, patient transparency, robust safety and effectiveness⏎validations, requirements for disclosure of limitations, scope limitations, and/or crisis⏎protocols with mandatory clinician escalation pathways⏎⏎● Avoid regulatory frameworks that treat all "AI in mental health" as a single category.⏎AI-assisted documentation and autonomous therapy chatbots present fundamentally⏎different risk profiles and should be regulated differently.⏎3. Reimbursement: Don't Penalize Efficiency (Responds to RFI Q2, Q9)⏎The problem: The payer relationship is a significant source of anxiety for mental health⏎practitioners considering AI tools. If using AI to write notes efficiently is interpreted as cutting⏎corners, rather than working smarter, adoption will stall.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎● Issue guidance clarifying that AI-assisted documentation does not inherently constitute⏎fraud, waste, or abuse under Medicare/Medicaid billing rules, provided clinical review is⏎documented.⏎● Work with CMS to confirm that AI-assisted clinical encounters remain fully reimbursable⏎when human judgment is clearly applied and documented.⏎● Explore reduced administrative burden (e.g., prior authorization simplification) for⏎practices using certified AI tools that demonstrably improve documentation quality and⏎access to care.⏎● Consider a demonstration program that tests reimbursement incentives for small and⏎solo behavioral health practices adopting AI tools.⏎4. Reimbursement: Opportunity to Accelerate Adoption (Responds to RFI Q2)⏎The problem: The current draft appropriately raises concerns about AI-assisted documentation⏎being penalized by payers. But reimbursement policy is not only a risk to mitigate; it is a lever to⏎accelerate adoption. No CPT codes exist specifically for AI-assisted clinical activities, leaving⏎practitioners uncertain how to document and bill for encounters where AI played a supporting⏎role. Medicare and Medicaid documentation requirements were designed for a pre-AI world. AI⏎could enable more efficient documentation that meets clinical needs, but current payer⏎expectations create friction.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎● Work with CMS and the AMA CPT Editorial Panel to evaluate whether new codes or⏎modifiers are appropriate for AI-assisted behavioral health services, particularly for:⏎○ AI-supported outcome measurement (e.g., automated symptom tracking and⏎measurement)⏎○ AI-assisted care coordination (e.g., summarizing patient history for referrals,⏎matching)⏎○ AI-enhanced documentation (e.g., ambient listening, documentation generation)⏎○ AI-assisted client facing tools monitored by the clinician⏎● Ensure that any AI-related reimbursement policies apply equally to independent⏎practitioners and health systems. Small practices should not face higher barriers to⏎payment for using the same tools available to large organizations.⏎⏎5. Research and Development: Prioritize Behavioral Health (Responds to RFI Q6, Q9,⏎Q10)⏎The problem: Behavioral health is chronically underrepresented in health AI research, despite⏎having one of the highest documentation burdens and one of the most significant workforce⏎shortages in the sector. This is a gap HHS can help fill.⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎● Fund research specifically on AI use in behavioral health and mental health settings,⏎including solo and small-group practices, not just large health systems.⏎● Prioritize research on patient experience and safety in AI-assisted behavioral health⏎care, including patient-facing communication tools.⏎● Invest in implementation science: what training, workflows, and design patterns lead to⏎safe, sustainable AI adoption in independent practice?⏎● Support public-private partnerships that bring together EHR vendors, mental health⏎practitioners, and payers to develop behavioral-health-specific AI use standards.⏎Innovation in the application of AI in mental health can bring very tangible clinical value to⏎providers, but to do so effectively, research in the application of tools in independent solo and⏎small group practice settings is essential.⏎I hope AI can help me see blind spots in my clinical decision-making. It would be great to⏎get recommendations on tests, medications, and psychotherapy interventions that I⏎might have missed because of unconscious bias or new research that I might not know⏎about.⏎- Ish Bhalla, MD⏎6. State Regulatory Fragmentation: A Hidden Barrier to AI Adoption (Responds to RFI Q1,⏎Q2, Q3)⏎The problem: SimplePractice serves practitioners licensed in all 50 states. The inconsistent⏎patchwork of state-level rules governing AI use is a significant barrier to use. Consider the⏎practitioner who is licensed and providing treatment across multiple state lines. Each state may⏎have different requirements for AI use across patient consent or disclosure, documentation⏎standards, and/or strict use limitations. Some states have enacted restrictions on AI in health⏎care that may sweep too broadly. While we share concerns, blanket prohibitions on AI-assisted⏎tools risk cutting practitioners off from tools that support their practice.⏎This fragmentation creates compliance uncertainty that disproportionately burdens small⏎practices. Large health systems have compliance departments to navigate multi-state⏎requirements; solo practitioners do not.⏎⏎Specific Recommendations:⏎● Issue federal guidance affirming that AI-assisted documentation tools used by licensed⏎practitioners within the scope of their clinical judgment do not require additional⏎state-level authorization beyond existing telehealth and practice act frameworks.⏎● Work with state licensing boards through organizations like the Federation of State⏎Medical Boards (FSMB), the American Association of State Counseling Boards, and the⏎Association of State and Provincial Psychology Boards (ASPPB) to develop model⏎guidelines for AI use in behavioral health that states can adopt, reducing fragmentation.⏎A Note on Behavioral Health⏎Behavioral health is often missing from health AI conversations, but it shouldn't be. The⏎workforce shortage is still severe in spite of mental health parity laws, the documentation burden⏎is among the highest in any specialty, and the therapeutic relationship itself is a clinical⏎instrument that AI can either support or undermine, depending on how it's deployed.⏎One dimension of this challenge is worth naming explicitly. As consumer AI tools have become⏎widely accessible, more people are using them as a first—and sometimes only—point of contact⏎for mental health concerns. For individuals experiencing suicidal ideation, acute paranoia, or⏎other conditions requiring clinical judgment, this gap carries real risk: a well-designed AI can be⏎responsive and even helpful, but it cannot assess imminent safety, coordinate care, or bear⏎professional accountability for what happens next. The question for HHS is not whether to stop⏎this, as it is already occurring at scale, but whether federal policy will create the conditions⏎under which a human mental health practitioner remains meaningfully in the loop. Supporting⏎responsible AI adoption within clinical relationships is one of the most direct levers available.⏎SimplePractice is committed to getting this right. We believe AI can expand access to behavioral⏎health care, reduce practitioner and clinician burnout, and improve outcomes if the policy⏎environment gives practitioners and platform builders the clarity and confidence to move⏎forward.⏎We look forward to working with HHS to make this a reality.⏎“My main concern is maintaining a high standard of clinician integrity when using AI… As⏎more tools appear on the market to help us make our practices more efficient and⏎successful, I'm excited about that support. However, I urge clinicians to use the⏎technology as operational support that helps them deepen therapy work.”⏎- Moe Ari Brown, LMFT⏎SimplePractice, LLC | 520 Broadway #200, Santa Monica, CA 90401 | simplepractice.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0434♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Society of Hematology, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Society of Hematology♔♔♔♔♔♔February 17, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎U .S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Ave., SW Washington, DC 20001⏎Submitted electronically via http://www.regulations.gov⏎R e: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On behalf of the American Society of Hematology (ASH), thank you for the opportunity to⏎comment on the Health and Human Services (HHS) Request for Information (RFI):⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care. The⏎Society appreciates HHS’ commitment to improve efficiencies within the health care system⏎through new technologies and innovation and commends the Agency for seeking input to⏎responsibly accelerate the adoption and use of AI in clinical care.⏎ASH represents more than 18,000 clinicians and scientists committed to studying and treating⏎blood and blood-related diseases. These disorders encompass malignant hematologic disorders⏎such as leukemia, lymphoma, and multiple myeloma, as well as classical (or non-malignant)⏎conditions such as sickle cell disease (SCD), thalassemia, bone marrow failure, venous⏎thromboembolism (VTE), and hemophilia. Hematologists have been pioneers in advancing the⏎understanding and treatment of various hematologic diseases. Hematologists continue to be⏎innovators in the fields of stem cell biology, regenerative medicine, transfusion medicine, and⏎gene therapy. ASH membership is comprised of basic, translational, and clinical scientists, as⏎well as physicians providing care to patients. Our mission is to foster high-quality care,⏎transformative research, and innovative education to improve the lives of patients with blood⏎and bone marrow disorders.⏎With these goals in mind, ASH recognizes the potential of AI tools to improve efficiencies,⏎streamline administrative processes, and provide additional clinical support to physicians.⏎Simultaneously, ASH members have emphasized the importance of responsible implementation⏎of these technologies; ensuring the patient-physician relationship remains central in care delivery,⏎emphasizing transparency in the underlying data and logic of AI tools to support their reliability⏎and validity in clinical care, and upholding the highest standards for data privacy and protection⏎of health information.⏎Specifically, ASH’s comments focus on the following themes: governance and transparency;⏎implementation and workflow; interoperability; clinician responsibility and patient trust.⏎⏎Governance and Transparency⏎Regulations and guidance that support governance standards and transparency of AI models are critical for their safe⏎and effective use in clinical practice. ASH members consistently raise questions about how various AI tools are⏎regulated, especially as expectations grow for clinicians to use these tools in clinical care. Members have specifically⏎expressed concerns that the lack of standardized governance or transparency for the types of data used in AI models,⏎the frequency of data updates, and the underlying logic of predictive models and AI tools may lead to a “one-size-fits-⏎all” approach in AI tools. This approach may not be appropriate when these tools are used for complex or rare⏎conditions commonly found in hematology.⏎Several studies1,1,1,1 show significant and persistent inconsistencies in medical care and health outcomes among patients⏎across a range of blood disorders, underscoring the need for governance or standards for the reliability, validity, and⏎sensitivity of these tools to be applicable for the patient population for whom our members care. In prior ASH⏎comments, the Society has emphasized the importance of transparency as to the types of data used in AI models, the⏎frequency of data updates, and the underlying logic of predictive models and AI tools.⏎Meanwhile, individual health systems are developing and adopting their own internal best practices for governance⏎around the use of AI tools, leading to a patchwork of varying standards. This institution-by-institution approach⏎creates uncertainty about how AI should be evaluated for applicability and adopted across health systems.⏎ASH encourages HHS to establish clear definitions of the various classes of AI tools used in clinical practice and⏎develop a framework that specifies where and how different types of AI tools are appropriate for clinical use. ASH⏎also encourages the Agency to set clear regulatory expectations for the datasets and underlying logic used to build and⏎continuously validate AI models and tools. These standards will support clinicians and patients in understanding the⏎basis of these tools and support trust for broad implementation.⏎Implementation and Workflow⏎ASH members have shared that the greatest immediate potential of AI tools to improve efficiencies is through their⏎application in administrative tasks. For example, ASH members have shared that AI- enabled notetaking and ambient⏎listening tools may support provider efficiency in some instances within the electronic health record (EHR), allowing⏎physicians to focus on the interpretation and analyses of the patient’s health information.⏎However, while AI may reduce the burden of administrative tasks in some instances, it is important to recognize that⏎physicians have a finite capacity to incorporate and continuously monitor these tools alongside their many other⏎responsibilities. ASH members have described several experiences in which AI tools have “hallucinations” or provide⏎inaccurate information; hallucinations underscore the need for continuous monitoring or a human-in-the-loop to⏎ensure that clinical information in these tools remain accurate and clinically appropriate. Human oversight of these⏎tools is essential for their implementation, but the Society encourages HHS to carefully consider the workload⏎implications before placing this additional responsibility on physicians. Continuous monitoring of these tools should⏎not become an additional burden for physicians.⏎Interoperability⏎Many of these tools are already being incorporated as additions or extensions in EHR systems, often with new or⏎separate interfaces that require a different workflow with additional or parallel documentation. Duplicative work is an⏎additional burden on physicians, but importantly, this also raises questions about the interoperability of AI tools across⏎health IT systems. AI tools that cannot be cleanly embedded into existing EHR workflows or that rely on proprietary⏎integrations create barriers and disincentivize adoption and scalability. This can be especially challenging for smaller⏎or under-resourced community practices and institutions, such as those in rural areas, that may not have the resources⏎to support the integration of these tools.⏎⏎ASH encourages HHS to develop a framework of interoperable standards for AI tools used across all types of clinical⏎practice and health systems, which will be key to support the integration and use of these tools across health care⏎systems.⏎Clinician Responsibility and Patient Trust⏎Lastly, successful implementation of AI tools in clinical practice must also be supported by patients. Maintaining⏎patient trust is foundational to preserving the patient-physician relationship, which is in turn central to ASH members’⏎ability to deliver high-quality and clinically appropriate care to patients with hematologic diseases and disorders. ASH⏎members have reported barriers to implementing AI tools in their clinic because patients have shown hesitancy with⏎AI being used for care delivery. Members noted that their patients cited privacy concerns, specifically about how their⏎protected health data is being stored and used.⏎ASH recommends that the Agency consider educational resources for both clinicians and patients to foster a shared⏎understanding of the purpose and use of AI tools in clinical care. Resources that help clinicians clearly communicate⏎how data are used and protected may help with patient trust and support more effective implementation.⏎ASH thanks HHS for the opportunity to provide these comments on AI in clinical practice. Should you have any⏎questions or require further information, please contact ASH Manager for Health Care Access Policy, Carina Smith⏎at casmith@hematology.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Robert Negrin, MD⏎American Society of Hematology, President⏎1 Ioannis Farmakis, Mary Cushman, Luca Valerio, Nikolaos Giannakoulas, Lukas Hobohm, Gregory Piazza, Stavros Konstantinides, Stefano Barco; Social⏎Determinants of Health and Pulmonary Embolism Treatment and Mortality: The Nationwide Inpatient Sample. Blood 2022; 140 (Supplement 1): 343–344.⏎doi: https://doi.org/10.1182/blood-2022-166550⏎2 Arushi Khurana, Raphael Mwangi, Loretta J. Nastoupil, Patrick M. Reagan, Umar Farooq, Jason T. Romancik, Timothy J. McDonnell, Shaun M⏎Riska, Izidore S. Lossos, Brad S. Kahl, Peter Martin, Thomas E. Witzig, James R. Cerhan, Christopher R. Flowers, Matthew J. Maurer, Grzegorz S.⏎Nowakowski; Evaluating the Impact of Lab-Based Eligibility Criteria By Race/Ethnicity in Frontline Clinical Trials for Diffuse Large B-Cell Lymphoma⏎(DLBCL): A LEO Cohort Analysis. Blood 2022; 140 (Supplement 1): 2056–2058. doi: https://doi.org/10.1182/blood-2022-169433⏎3 Warren Fingrut, Stephanie Chinapen, Angela Katrichis, Caroline Carino, Eric Davis, Brian C. Shaffer, Christina Cho, Gunjan L. Shah, Juliet N. Barker;⏎Analysis of 372 Adult Allograft Recipients Reveals Associations between Non-European Ancestry, Low Socioeconomic Status, and Receipt of HLA-⏎Disparate Grafts. Blood 2022; 140 (Supplement 1): 313–314. doi: https://doi.org/10.1182/blood-2022-168486⏎4 Molly Tokaz, Helen Baldomero, Andrew J Cowan, Wael Saber, Hildegard T. Greinix, Mickey Koh, Nicolaus Kröger, Mohamad Mohty, Sebastian⏎Galeano, Shinichiro Okamoto, Naeem A. Chaudhri, Amado J Karduss, Fabio Ciceri, Vergilio Rensi Colturato, Selim Corbacioglu, Alaa M. Elhaddad, Lisa⏎Force, Cristobal Augusto Frutos Ortiz, Andres Gomez-De Leon, Nada Hamad, Nelson Hamerschlak, Naya He, Aloysius YL Ho, Xiao-Jun⏎Huang, Benjamin Jacobs, Heeje Kim, Minako Iida, Leslie E. Lehmann, Régis Peffault de Latour, Mary-Elizabeth M. Percival, Walid Rasheed, Kirk R.⏎Schultz, Adriana Seber, Bor-Sheng Ko, Anderson Simione, Alok Srivastava, Jeff Szer, William A. Wood, Yoshihisa Kodera, Arnon Nagler, John A⏎Snowden, Daniel J. Weisdorf, Jakob R. Passweg, Marcelo C Pasquini, Anna Sureda, Yoshiko Atsuta, Mahmoud Aljurf, Dietger Niederwieser, Martina⏎Perdomo; An Analysis of the Worldwide Utilization of Hematopoietic Stem Cell Transplantation for Acute Myeloid Leukemia. Blood 2022; 140 (Supplement⏎1): 8146–8149. doi: https://doi.org/10.1182/blood-2022-158338♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0435♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Logos Research Centre, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-08T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Logos Research Centre♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment on HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Logos Research Centre⏎Claire Yang1, Ellta T. Abraham2, Saathvik Valvekar3⏎February 2026⏎1 First/Lead Author⏎2 Secondary Author⏎3 Advisor; Logos Research Centre⏎⏎Executive Summary⏎This paper announces the Department of Health and Human Services Sector’s request for⏎public comments on how it can accelerate the adoption of artificial intelligence within healthcare⏎while upholding ethical considerations and civil liberties. The Department seeks feedback from⏎those buying, building, evaluating, using, and receiving care from AI tools embedded within clinical⏎care systems as well as those who wish to do so but face barriers. The RFI poses ten specific⏎questions addressing barriers to AI adoption, regulatory changes needed, evaluation methods,⏎interoperability challenges, and patient concerns. HHS aims to establish a forward-leaning,⏎industry-supportive approach that reduces uncertainty and ensures AI deployment is used effectively⏎to enhance productivity, reduce costs, and overall improve health outcomes.⏎⏎Introduction⏎The U.S. health care system currently stands at a critical crossroad. National health⏎expenditures reached $4.9 trillion in 2023, with per capita costs at $14,570, and healthcare now⏎comprises 17.6% of GDP4, a figure projected to increase to 20.3% by 20335. These escalating costs⏎place an enormous burden on American families, with 44% of adults reporting difficulty affording⏎healthcare costs and approximately 28% facing problems paying for care in the past year.6⏎Simultaneously, the system faces an extreme workforce crisis, with projections of a shortage⏎exceeding 187,000 full-time equivalent physicians by 20377, and approximately 45% of physicians⏎reporting burnout in 2025.8 Amid these mounting pressures of cost and workforce strain, artificial⏎intelligence emerges as a potentially transformative tool in reshaping the problems that hinder the⏎healthcare system today.⏎Despite AI’s promise, its translation into widespread clinical use has been notably limited.⏎Research has identified substantial barriers to adoption across technical, organizational, and human⏎aspects. A systemic review identified 16 key barriers including data quality and bias, infrastructure⏎limitations, financial constraints, workflow misalignment, inadequate training, and potential lack of⏎transparency and accountability.9 A 2025 survey of major U.S. health systems found that while⏎ambient clinical documentation tools achieved 100% adoption activity, success rates varied⏎dramatically across use cases, with only 38% reporting high success in AI for clinical risk⏎stratification despite widespread deployment.⏎4 Healthcare Spending in the U.S. 2025 | Stats & Facts. (2025, July 23). The Global Statistics.⏎https://www.theglobalstatistics.com/united-states-healthcare-spending-statistics/⏎5 Keehan, S. P., Madison, A. J., Poisal, J. A., Cuckler, G. A., Smith, S. D., Sisko, A. M., Fiore, J. A., & Rennie, K. E. (2025).⏎National Health Expenditure Projections, 2024–33: Despite Insurance Coverage Declines, Health To Grow As Share Of GDP.⏎Health Affairs, 44(7). https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00545⏎6 Sparks, G., Lopes, L., Montero, A., Presiado, M., & Hamel, L. (2025, July 11). Americans’ challenges with health care costs.⏎KFF. https://www.kff.org/health-costs/americans-challenges-with-health-care-costs/⏎7 AAG Healthcare Recruitment and Consulting. (2025). Aag.health. https://www.aag.health/post/healthcare-staffing-statistics⏎8 Mathewes, F. (2026, January 7). Retirement, burnout fuel physician shortage: 4 new stats. Becker’s ASC.⏎https://www.beckersasc.com/asc-news/retirement-burnout-fuel-physician-shortage-4-new-stats/⏎9 Abdelwanis, M., Simsekler, M. C. E., Gabor, A. F., Sleptchenko, A., & Omar, M. (2025). Artificial intelligence adoption⏎challenges from healthcare providers’ perspectives: A comprehensive review and future directions. Safety Science, 193,⏎107028. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssci.2025.107028⏎⏎There are a multitude of barriers to AI adoption. On the technological front, healthcare⏎organizations struggle with immature AI tools, poor data quality, and incompatibility with existing⏎healthcare infrastructure. Among the concerns of allied healthcare professionals are reduced human⏎contact with patients and perceived reductions in healthcare quality due to the implementation of⏎AI. Organizationally, there is insufficient leadership support and regulatory uncertainty. A 2025⏎survey found that 70% of hospital leaders experienced at least one AI pilot failure, while 80% found⏎it difficult to verify vendors’ AI claims without formal governance.10 On the human front, clinicians⏎lack adequate training and often resist adoption, fearing replacement or increased workload, which⏎are both compounded by existing staff shortages.⏎The European Union has adopted a comprehensive risk-based regulatory framework⏎through its AI act, which classifies most healthcare AI solutions as “high risk” and requires strict⏎pre-market testing, validation, and monitoring.11 These international regulatory approaches can act as⏎an instructive comparison for the HHS. The EU AI act implementation began in February 2025⏎with compliance deadlines extending through July 202512, establishing one of the world’s most⏎stringent regulatory regimes. In contrast, the United Kingdom has chosen to not pass specific AI⏎laws, but instead encourages regulators to apply existing technology-neutral legislation while⏎adopting a pro-innovation stance.13 Australia has similarly taken an approach of prioritizing a⏎national risk-based framework without adopting broader AI legislation.14⏎The urgency of potential AI adoption is compounded by the magnitude of healthcare system⏎challenges. Currently, 90% of the $4.9 trillion in annual healthcare expenditures are for people with⏎10 More hospitals are adopting AI. But do they have a governance strategy? (2025). Advisory.⏎https://www.advisory.com/daily-briefing/2025/12/16/ai-governance⏎11 Palaniappan, K., Yan, E., & Vogel, S. (2024). Global Regulatory Frameworks for the Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI)⏎in the Healthcare Services Sector. Healthcare, 12(5), 562–562. https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare12050562⏎12 Team, L. N. (2025, May 21). EU & UK AI Healthcare Regulation Tracker For 2025. Legalnodes.com; Legal Nodes.⏎https://www.legalnodes.com/article/ai-healthcare-regulation⏎13 Egan, D. (2024). AI in healthcare: legal and ethical considerations at the new frontier. Aoshearman.com.⏎https://www.aoshearman.com/en/insights/ao-shearman-on-life-sciences/ai-in-healthcare-legal-and-ethical-consideratio⏎ns-at-the-new-frontier⏎14 The EU AI Act’s impact on medical devices. (2025, March 28). PharmaLex.⏎https://www.pharmalex.com/thought-leadership/blogs/what-global-ai-regulations-mean-for-medical-device-manufactur⏎ers/⏎⏎chronic and mental health conditions15, presenting a significant opportunity for AI-driven⏎prevention, early detection, disease management. Hospital expenses grew 5.1% in 2024, outpacing⏎overall inflation of 2.9%, with labor force costs now accounting for 56% of total hospital spending.16⏎Additionally, physicians spent an estimated 30-50% of their time on non-clinical tasks such as⏎documentation, coding, and insurance-related activities.⏎Rather than implementing a comprehensive regulatory structure, HHS proposes using⏎regulation, reimbursement, and research & development to accelerate AI adoption while maintaining⏎public trust. THe RFI explicitly acknowledges the “inherent flaws in legacy payment systems” and⏎seeks to “ensure that the potential promises of AI innovations are not diminished through inertia.”⏎This approach represents a bet that market-oriented incentives and strategic R&D investments can⏎achieve faster AI adoption and better health outcomes than Europe’s precautionary regulatory⏎model, while avoiding the innovation-stifling effects of slow reimbursement or regulations that have⏎historically plagued US healthcare technology adoption.⏎15 Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. (2024, July 12). Fast Facts: Health and Economic Costs of Chronic Conditions.⏎Chronic Disease. https://www.cdc.gov/chronic-disease/data-research/facts-stats/index.html⏎16 American Hospital Association. (2025, April). America’s hospitals and health systems continue to face escalating operational costs⏎and economic pressures as they care for patients and communities. Www.aha.org; American Hospital Association.⏎https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring⏎⏎Policy Gaps⏎Although integrating AI to strengthen healthcare is a promising goal, practical⏎implementation challenges remain significant. When supporting the hopeful long-term viability, key⏎elements identified in the proposed policy must be carefully evaluated.⏎Elements including broad data collection requirements and AI adoption reliance can burden⏎smaller organizations and local communities. This is true especially for areas with limited resources⏎as this could reduce participation and limit the policy's overall desired impact. Thus, to minimize risk⏎and enhance impact, targeted amendments and improvements can be utilized to improve⏎effectiveness.⏎When applied, this implementation would demand expanded information collection from a⏎diverse, wide range of stakeholders among healthcare providers, manufacturers, distributors, and⏎specified private sector organizations. The collected data could support planning, allow for early⏎identification of vulnerabilities in the supply chain, and also improve the initiative's alignment with⏎other national preparedness acts. Over time, AI-based tools could enhance the supply chain's speed⏎and accuracy in monitoring. Furthermore, this infrastructure could also support rapid AI⏎deployment in urgent public health emergencies.⏎Despite these anticipated benefits, potential challenges are still present. First, the scale may⏎impose a disproportionate burden on smaller organizations, communities, and rural partners.⏎Reporting requirements and participation in surveys, interviews, and other methods of engagement⏎may potentially strain limited staffing environments and capacity, reducing participation and creating⏎disproportionate data quality.⏎Additionally, long-term established reliance on AI systems risks worsening inequalities in⏎adoption systems. Well-resourced institutions can more easily adopt advanced AI than underfunded⏎regions, creating unequal benefits and skewed data representation. Similar disparities emerged⏎following the implementation of the HITECH Act and its Meaningful Use incentives. Researchers⏎observed that rural and smaller healthcare providers adopted electronic health records at⏎⏎significantly lower rates than larger, urban providers, largely due to resource constraints17. These⏎historical inequities clearly highlight the need for deliberate measures to ensure equitable AI⏎implementation.⏎Finally, implementation must be carefully managed to avoid delays and setbacks that could⏎undermine trust among healthcare professionals and between administrators and the public.⏎Recommendations⏎To address these risks, the following recommendations should be considered:⏎● Streamline data collection processes to reduce administrative burden.⏎● Provide targeted support for smaller and rural healthcare organizations.⏎● Establish clear regulatory guidelines for AI adoption in clinical settings.⏎● Implement continuous monitoring and evaluation mechanisms.⏎● Foster cross-sector collaboration between federal agencies and healthcare providers.⏎Policy Implications⏎These proposed recommendations will accelerate the adoption of AI within healthcare,⏎providing both benefits and challenges. By encouraging the use of AI to enhance supply chain⏎resilience, these recommendations will improve the speed and accuracy of healthcare logistics⏎nationwide. Data collected from healthcare providers, manufacturers, distributors, and private⏎organizations could allow for early identification of risks and, in turn, create quicker and more⏎effective responses to public health emergencies. In the long term, this could create a more⏎responsive healthcare system capable of managing large scale crises.⏎There are several implications which warrant careful consideration. First, the extensive data⏎collection and AI adoption may place a disproportionate burden on small healthcare organizations,⏎17 Anzalone, A. J., Geary, C. R., Dai, R., Watanabe-Galloway, S., McClay, J. C., & Campbell, J. R. (2025). Lower electronic⏎health record adoption and interoperability in rural versus urban physician participants: a cross-sectional analysis from⏎the CMS quality payment program. BMC Health Services Research, 25(1). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-024-12168-5⏎⏎rural communities, and under-resourced providers, who often lack the technical capability for rapid⏎AI adoption, possibly leading to data quality issues. As seen following the HITECH Act’s⏎implementation, smaller rural providers may lag behind better-funded providers, further⏎exacerbating the disparity and wealth gap.⏎To ensure effectiveness and equitable outcomes, the policy should prioritize streamlined data⏎collection processes and focus support on small and rural healthcare organizations. The policy⏎should simultaneously implement clear regulatory guidelines for AI use and consistent steps to⏎evaluate the ethical and operational challenges of rapid AI adoption. Taken together, these⏎improvements can ensure AI integration is effective without widening existing inequalities.⏎Conclusion⏎The Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS) initiative to accelerate AI adoption⏎across the healthcare sector reflects an important step in the modernization of the U.S. healthcare⏎system. The integration of AI offers the potential to enhance productivity, improve care delivery, and⏎strengthen the healthcare supply chain. However, the success of this effort will depend on a dual⏎focus on innovation and equity. This policy must take into consideration the steps to ensure that⏎smaller and rural healthcare providers are not excluded from the benefits of AI due to resource⏎constraints or regulatory uncertainty.⏎To realize AI’s promise effectively, HHS must pursue streamlined data collection, targeted⏎technical and financial support to under-resourced institutions, and transparent regulatory guidelines.⏎Furthermore, continuous evaluation and stakeholder engagement will help create and maintain⏎public trust and ensure that AI adoption is aligned with ethics.⏎⏎Bibliography⏎AAG Healthcare Recruitment and Consulting. (2025). Aag.health.⏎https://www.aag.health/post/healthcare-staffing-statistics⏎Abdelwanis, M., Simsekler, M. C. E., Gabor, A. F., Sleptchenko, A., & Omar, M. (2025). Artificial⏎intelligence adoption challenges from healthcare providers’ perspectives: A comprehensive⏎review and future directions. Safety Science, 193, 107028.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.ssci.2025.107028⏎American Hospital Association. (2025, April). America’s hospitals and health systems continue to face⏎escalating operational costs and economic pressures as they care for patients and communities.⏎Www.aha.org; American Hospital Association. https://www.aha.org/costsofcaring⏎Anzalone, A. J., Geary, C. R., Dai, R., Watanabe-Galloway, S., McClay, J. C., & Campbell, J. R. (2025).⏎Lower electronic health record adoption and interoperability in rural versus urban physician⏎participants: a cross-sectional analysis from the CMS quality payment program. BMC Health⏎Services Research, 25(1). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12913-024-12168-5⏎Centers for Disease Control and Prevention. (2024, July 12). Fast Facts: Health and Economic Costs of⏎Chronic Conditions. Chronic Disease.⏎https://www.cdc.gov/chronic-disease/data-research/facts-stats/index.html⏎Egan, D. (2024). AI in healthcare: legal and ethical considerations at the new frontier. Aoshearman.com.⏎https://www.aoshearman.com/en/insights/ao-shearman-on-life-sciences/ai-in-healthcare-le⏎gal-and-ethical-considerations-at-the-new-frontier⏎Healthcare Spending in the U.S. 2025 | Stats & Facts. (2025, July 23). The Global Statistics.⏎https://www.theglobalstatistics.com/united-states-healthcare-spending-statistics/⏎Keehan, S. P., Madison, A. J., Poisal, J. A., Cuckler, G. A., Smith, S. D., Sisko, A. M., Fiore, J. A., &⏎Rennie, K. E. (2025). National Health Expenditure Projections, 2024–33: Despite Insurance⏎⏎Coverage Declines, Health To Grow As Share Of GDP. Health Affairs, 44(7).⏎https://doi.org/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00545⏎Mathewes, F. (2026, January 7). Retirement, burnout fuel physician shortage: 4 new stats. Becker’s ASC.⏎https://www.beckersasc.com/asc-news/retirement-burnout-fuel-physician-shortage-4-new-s⏎tats/⏎More hospitals are adopting AI. But do they have a governance strategy? (2025). Advisory.⏎https://www.advisory.com/daily-briefing/2025/12/16/ai-governance⏎Palaniappan, K., Yan, E., & Vogel, S. (2024). Global Regulatory Frameworks for the Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI) in the Healthcare Services Sector. Healthcare, 12(5), 562–562.⏎https://doi.org/10.3390/healthcare12050562⏎Sparks, G., Lopes, L., Montero, A., Presiado, M., & Hamel, L. (2025, July 11). Americans’ challenges with⏎health care costs. KFF.⏎https://www.kff.org/health-costs/americans-challenges-with-health-care-costs/⏎Team, L. N. (2025, May 21). EU & UK AI Healthcare Regulation Tracker For 2025. Legalnodes.com;⏎Legal Nodes. https://www.legalnodes.com/article/ai-healthcare-regulation⏎The EU AI Act’s impact on medical devices. (2025, March 28). PharmaLex.⏎https://www.pharmalex.com/thought-leadership/blogs/what-global-ai-regulations-mean-fo⏎r-medical-device-manufacturers/♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0436♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AI Healthcare Coalition (AIHC), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AI Healthcare Coalition (AIHC)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr. Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Secretary Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health & Human Services U.S. Department of Health & Human Services⏎200 Independence Ave, SW 330 C St SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201 Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy and Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎On behalf of the AI Healthcare Coalition (AIHC), thank you for the opportunity to contribute to the⏎ongoing work by the US Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Office of the Deputy⏎Secretary, in collaboration with the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ASTP/ONC), to make artificial intelligence⏎(AI) an essential part of clinical care.⏎AIHC was founded in 2019 and brings together healthcare AI innovators and stakeholders to⏎advocate for patient access to safe, ethically developed health AI. Our membership includes⏎innovator companies that create cutting edge AI powered health services (or devices) that benefit⏎patients and enhance healthcare opportunities.⏎As you continue to consider regulatory, reimbursement, and research and development challenges⏎presented by AI innovation, AIHC is pleased to provide these recommendations for your⏎consideration. Additionally, we hope to be a continuing resource to HHS and ASTP/ONC as novel⏎challenges emerge.⏎Specifically, we recommend HHS:⏎ Create permanent Medicare payment pathways for AI technologies;⏎ Implement clear coverage pathways for AI technologies, such as President Trump’s;⏎Medicare Coverage for Innovative Technologies (MCIT) program;⏎ Ensure separate payment for AI services and underlying imaging services;⏎ Exclude AI Services from the Outpatient Prospective Payment System (OPPS) cap on⏎imaging services;⏎ Create a pathway for permanent, separate payment for AI services whose NTAP rate has⏎expired; and⏎⏎ Exclude FDA authorized AI systems from HHS/OCR’s Sec. 1557 regulations.⏎We offer further comment on these recommendations below.⏎I. Create Permanent Medicare Payment Pathways for AI Technologies⏎While some AI healthcare services are currently reimbursed under the Medicare program, there is⏎no clear pathway for clinicians and innovators to obtain reimbursement for AI services. As more AI⏎services are rigorously validated and FDA-authorized, a clear reimbursement pathway— or multiple⏎pathways— is/are needed to ensure that Medicare beneficiaries and clinicians can access these⏎services. Notably, AI services are diverse and varied, spanning multiple specialties and⏎incorporating modalities that vary in their use of algorithmic design and machine learning.⏎For example, in the case of AI services provided in the outpatient setting, we often see such services⏎assigned to Clinical Ambulatory Payment Classification (APC) that does not adequately reimburse⏎for provider costs because there is no specific pathway for AI services. While CMS does have a New⏎Technology APC process to allow reimbursement for new technologies in the outpatient setting, that⏎process was not created to facilitate the payment of AI technologies, and often presents too short or⏎low a payment to allow access. For these reasons, we support a 5-year outpatient payment rate for⏎AI technologies. Permanent payment pathways will create certainty for AI technology developers⏎and providers, which would drive investment and accelerate innovation.⏎II. Implement Clear Coverage Pathways for AI Technologies, such as President⏎Trump’s Medicare Coverage for Innovative Technologies (MCIT) Program1⏎During President Trump’s first term, the MCIT program was improved to provide a transitional⏎Medicare coverage policy for certain medical technologies that receive “breakthrough technology”⏎status under the FDA. President Biden pulled back this policy and instead finalized a similar policy⏎entitled the “Transitional Coverage for Emerging Technologies,” or TCET, program. While this⏎program makes some positive steps forward by requiring Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎(CMS) and the U.S. Food & Drug Administration (FDA) to work contemporaneously and iteratively⏎with innovator applicant companies, the agencies have said that very few — approximately four—⏎applications would be eligible for the TCET process annually. This is far too few. We urge the⏎Administration to facilitate timely coverage for AI health technologies by finalizing an MCIT -like⏎policy. In addition to increased patient access, such a policy would create confidence for investment⏎in these important technological advancements, aiding commercialization.⏎1 Department of Health & Human Services, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. 42 CFR Part 405 [CMS-⏎3372-IFC] RIN 0938-AT88. Medicare program; Medicare Coverage of Innovative technology (MCIT) and⏎Definition of “Reasonable and Necessary”; Delay of Effective Date; Public Comment Period (March 17, 2021)⏎2⏎⏎III. Ensure Separate Payment for AI Services and Underlying Imaging Services⏎CMS recently finalized a policy in its Outpatient Prospective Payment System (OPPS) rule to allow⏎separate payment for the AI service and the underlying imaging service,2 but we see ongoing⏎challenges in specialty areas where imaging services and payment are bundled (for example, AI that⏎is viewed as computer-aided detection (CAD)). We urge the administration to provide further⏎guidance in this area to ensure that its separate payment policy, as finalized by CMS in the CY 2023⏎OPPS final rule, is effectuated.⏎IV. Exclude AI Services from the OPPS Cap on Imaging Services⏎Sec. 5102(b) of the Deficit Reduction Act (DRA) requires that “for imaging services […] furnished on⏎or after January 1, 2007, [CMS] will cap the [technical component] TC of the PFS payment amount⏎for the year (prior to geographic adjustment) by the [annual] OPPS payment amount (prior to⏎geographic adjustment). Congress enacted 5102(b) also known as the OPPS cap, to address rapid⏎growth in spending on imaging services, particularly for advanced imaging modalities, such as⏎computed tomography (CT), magnetic resonance imaging (MRI), and nuclear medicine, as⏎compared to the growth in spending among less advanced imaging modalities, such as x-ray or⏎ultrasound.⏎As is clear in the statutory text, the DRA envisioned services such as x-rays, ultrasound, PET, MRI,⏎and CT as such “imaging services,” not autonomous AI other Software-as-a-Service (SaaS0⏎technologies. Further, when CMS implemented the OPPS cap pursuant to Sec. 5102(b), CMS⏎expressly excluded some services because those services encompassed more than the use of⏎imaging technology alone. CMS should recognize an additional exclusion for service for TC-only⏎services for which there is no associated PC, as CMS has previously recognized that codes without⏎a TC / PC split should not be listed on the OPPS Cap List.3⏎V. Create a Pathway for Permanent, Separate Payment for AI Services whose NTAP⏎Rate has Expired⏎Like CMS’ New Tech APC Program, inpatient AI services may apply for and receive a New Technology⏎Add-on Payment (NTAP) payment for 2-3 years if they meet certain criteria. Once NTAP expires, CMS⏎has reasoned that the cost of the technology should be covered by the DRG payment. The industry⏎2 Department of Health and Human Services, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. 42 CFR Parts 405,⏎410, 411, 412, 413, 416, 419, 424, 485, and 489 [CMS 1772-FC; CMS 1744-F; CMS 3419-F; CMS 5531-F; CMs⏎9912-F] RIN 0938-AU82. Medicare Program: Hospital Outpatient Prospective Payment and Ambulatory⏎Surgical Center Payment Systems and Quality Reporting Programs; Organ Acquisition; Rural Emergency⏎Hospitals: Payment Policies, Conditions of Participation. Provider Enrollment, Physician Self-Referral; New⏎Service Category for Hospital Outpatient Department Prior Authorization Process; Overall Hospital Quality⏎Star Rating; COVID-19. (November 23, 2022).⏎3 Department of Health and Human Services, Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services. 42 CFR Parts 405,⏎410, 414, 424, 425, 427, 428, 495, and 512 {CMS-1832-P] RIN 0938-AV50. Medicare and Medicaid Programs;⏎CY 2026 Payment Policies Under the Physician Fee Schedule and Other Changes to Part B Payment and⏎Coverage Policies; Medicare Shared Savings Program Requirements; and Medicare Prescription Drug⏎Inflation Rebate Program. (July 16, 2025).⏎3⏎⏎does not have data as to whether this is happening, and we believe that a lack of ongoing separate⏎payment leads to a lack of provider adoption / patient access. This is particularly true given recent⏎CMS proposals that may reduce the time NTAP status applies to a new technology in practice. The⏎ambiguity about reimbursement post-NTAP disincentivizes investment in these tools which can⏎benefit the Medicare program. If appropriately reimbursed, such tools are likely to be adopted by⏎healthcare providers, improve patient care, and create efficiencies.⏎VI. Exclude FDA Authorized AI systems from HHS/OCR’s Sec. 1557 Regulations⏎On May 6, 2024, HHS / OCR and CMS finalized updates to regulations implementing section 1557 of⏎the Affordable Care Act (ACA) to increase anti-discrimination protections for covered health care⏎entities.4 Among other provisions, this rule established new obligations for providers who utilize AI⏎and other patient care decision support tools. At the time of issuance, HHS noted this regulation was⏎in furtherance of President Biden’s now-rescinded EO on “Safe, Secure, and Trustworthy⏎Development and Use of Artificial Intelligence.”⏎Specifically, the Sec. 1557 final rule applies nondiscrimination principles to the use of new and⏎broadly defined “patient care decision support tools” in clinical care and requires those subject to⏎the tule to identify and mitigate discrimination when they use patient decision support tools,⏎including automated and non-automated tools, mechanisms, methos, and technology to provide⏎patient care. As of now, covered entities are required to comply with the new patient care decision⏎support tool requirements, including ongoing monitoring and risk mitigation responsibilities.⏎Although stakeholders expressed concerns regarding regulatory duplication at the time that the rule⏎was proposed, OCR expressly declined to exclude FDA-authorized technologies from the scope of⏎the rule. Importantly, while ONC’s requirements for predictive DSIs apply to health information⏎technology developers, Section 1557’s requirements apply to covered entity users of patient care⏎support tools. These requirements create a redundant regulatory burden for AI innovator companies⏎and healthcare providers, especially for those tools that are already subject to FDA review and⏎authorization.⏎Conclusion⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide comments on HHS and ASTP/ONC’s work to improve patient⏎access to AI health services. AI health innovation has the potential to transform the clinical care⏎delivery system, and you are uniquely positioned to usher in this new era.⏎We look forward to working with you to ensure patients have access to advanced technologies that⏎improve clinical care and health outcomes. If you have any questions about our comments, please⏎contact me at cybil.roehrenbeck@ai-coalition.org.⏎Kind regards,⏎4 “Nondiscrimination in Health Programs and Activities,” 89 Fed. Reg. 37522 (May 6, 2024)⏎4⏎⏎Cybil Roehrenbeck⏎Executive Director, AI Healthcare Coalition⏎5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0437♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-10T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anthony♔White♔♔Indiana♔♔United States♔Society of Hospital Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔Society of Hospital Medicine Comments on HHS Health Sector AI Request for Information: Accelerating AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎⏎Submitted by Anthony White, Director of Government Affairs⏎Society of Hospital Medicine⏎⏎The question HHS is really asking here is how to accelerate adoption, and I understand the impulse. There are genuine clinical applications where AI has shown promise and hospitalists across our membership have seen some of those firsthand. But I keep coming back to a problem that is already documented, already measurable, and will get significantly worse if the Department treats speed as the primary variable.⏎⏎Our member data, along with data from health equity task force work across several of our partner organizations, shows a consistent pattern: diagnostic AI tools that were trained predominantly on commercially insured patient populations produce lower accuracy rates when applied to Medicaid-enrolled and uninsured patients. This is not a theoretical concern. In six pilot practice settings examined as part of a 2024 health equity audit, the accuracy gap was measurable and in some cases clinically meaningful. The tools themselves were not flagged by the clinicians using them because the outputs looked authoritative. That is actually the part that worries me most. A clinician who disagrees with a consultant's recommendation can push back. It is a lot harder to push back on an algorithm, particularly for hospitalists working overnight with high patient volumes and no immediate access to whoever validated the tool in the first place.⏎⏎Society of Hospital Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 2⏎⏎I want to address specifically the RFI's questions around regulatory and reimbursement actions HHS could take to accelerate adoption. The Department is asking the right question in the wrong order. Before reimbursement incentives are structured to reward AI adoption, there needs to be a baseline requirement that tools used in clinical settings have undergone some form of independent bias auditing across demographic subgroups, and that the results of that auditing are disclosed to the clinical sites deploying them. Right now there is no such requirement. A vendor can train a sepsis detection algorithm on data from a tertiary academic medical center serving a predominantly commercially insured population, market it to a safety-net hospital in Gary, Indiana, and there is currently nothing requiring them to disclose that the validation cohort looks nothing like the patient population in Gary.⏎⏎If CMS structures reimbursement incentives that reward hospitals for deploying AI tools before those standards exist, the practical effect is that we will be subsidizing the scaling of biased algorithms into exactly the settings where the patient populations have the least margin for diagnostic error and the fewest resources to detect problems after the fact.⏎⏎I recognize HHS cannot solve the entire AI governance problem in one rulemaking cycle. But there are things the Department could do without waiting for legislation. Conditioning any new reimbursement pathways for AI-assisted clinical tools on vendor disclosure of training data demographics and subgroup performance metrics seems like a reasonable starting point. It does not require a new regulatory structure, it just requires that the Department not build financial incentives before the floor is in place.⏎⏎Society of Hospital Medicine Comments | HHS AI RFI | Page 3⏎⏎One other thing I want to flag, and this may be slightly outside the scope of the RFI but I think it is relevant. The clinical workforce is not prepared to interrogate these tools. Our members are hospitalists, they are skilled clinicians, but they are not data scientists and they should not have to be. The question of how to evaluate whether an AI tool is performing appropriately on their patient population is not a question most hospitalists currently have the training or the institutional support to answer. If HHS is going to accelerate adoption, there needs to be some parallel investment in what I would call practical interpretability, meaning giving clinicians accessible information about how a tool was validated and for whom, in a format they can actually use during a consult at 2 a.m. and not a 40-page technical appendix.⏎⏎Our organization would welcome the opportunity to discuss any of this further with the Department. Contact information for our Government Affairs office is below.⏎⏎Anthony White⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎Society of Hospital Medicine⏎awhite@hospitalmedicine.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0438♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-23T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from bodepudi, Anil, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anil♔bodepudi♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted By: Anil Bodepudi Sr. Data Engineer, Corteva Agriscience, Austin, Texas,⏎anilsai.b@gmail.com⏎To: U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Re: Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption of Artificial Intelligence in Clinical Care Subject: Technical Advisory⏎on Data Infrastructure and Hardware Drift⏎To the Committee:⏎I am writing to provide technical feedback on the infrastructure requirements necessary for the⏎safe acceleration of AI in clinical settings. As a Data Engineering Manager currently overseeing⏎petabyte-scale genomic pipelines in the life sciences industry (Corteva Agriscience), I manage⏎the "Data Gravity" and lifecycle challenges of biological AI daily.⏎While the RFI correctly identifies the potential of AI, it currently overlooks a fundamental⏎engineering failure mode: Silent Instrument Drift.⏎1. The Engineering Gap: "Batch Effects" in Production In industrial genomics, we frequently⏎observe that AI models are highly sensitive to the specific firmware and calibration of the data⏎capture hardware (e.g., DNA sequencers or imaging arrays). A model trained on data from⏎"Sequencer A" will often degrade silently when applied to "Sequencer B," interpreting the⏎hardware noise profile as a biological signal.⏎In a clinical setting, if a hospital upgrades its MRI firmware or sequencing hardware, an⏎"accelerated" AI model lacking rigid provenance checks will likely produce diagnostic errors that⏎are statistically undetectable until patient harm occurs.⏎2. Recommendation: Mandate Instrument-Agnostic Validation To safely accelerate⏎adoption, HHS standards must look upstream of the model layer. I recommend that any⏎framework for AI adoption include a mandatory "Data Engineering Competency" check:⏎● Metadata Provenance: AI systems must be required to ingest and validate the⏎hardware metadata (Device ID, Firmware Version) of the input data.⏎● Drift Normalization: Protocols must be established to distinguish between Biological⏎Drift (the patient changing) and Instrument Drift (the machine changing).⏎We have solved this in the private sector by enforcing strict schema contracts on our data lakes.⏎Clinical systems require the same rigor. Adopting AI without standardizing the underlying data⏎engineering layer will result in fragile systems that fail at scale.⏎Respectfully,⏎Anil Bodepudi♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0439♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Amanda♔Nelson♔♔Arizona♔♔United States♔Truven Health Analytics♔♔♔♔♔♔Honestly my main frustration reading through this whole AI in healthcare push is that we keep layering new technology initiatives on top of a system that still won't pay for my Medicare patients to get a dental cleaning. That's not a tangent, that's the reality I deal with every single day at our practice. We had a patient in February, 71 years old, couldn't get coverage for a tooth extraction that was clearly affecting her ability to eat properly, and we're supposed to be excited about AI workflow tools?⏎⏎I'm not opposed to AI. We've actually been piloting a scheduling and prior authorization support tool since late 2023 and it's saved our front desk staff probably 6-8 hours a week in phone time. So I get the upside. But when HHS asks what regulatory actions would help accelerate adoption, my honest answer is that the reimbursement structure underneath all of this is still broken in ways that technology isn't going to fix.⏎⏎The dental thing specifically - Congress has been talking about expanding Medicare dental benefits for years now. The Build Back Better negotiations in 2021, various standalone bills that went nowhere. We get patients every month who are shocked to find out basic dental care isn't covered. These are people on fixed incomes. Tooth infections lead to hospitalizations, which cost Medicare way more than a cleaning would have.⏎⏎I guess what I want HHS to understand is that from where I sit, as someone managing a practice day to day, the AI question feels somewhat secondary to these coverage gaps that have existed for decades. If the department is genuinely asking how to improve clinical care, this feels like an incomplete picture.⏎⏎The prior authorization AI stuff is real and useful, I'll give it that. We've had less back-and-forth with certain payers since implementing the tool, though United Healthcare still seems to require manual review for anything that comes through the system flagged as high-cost, so it's not like the automation is seamless. That inconsistency between payers is genuinely a problem and I'd be interested to see HHS address some kind of standardization there if that's within scope.⏎⏎But I keep coming back to the dental issue because I've watched it not get resolved for my entire career and now we're asking what technology can do to improve senior health outcomes and I just♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0440♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2025-12-28T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Kenneth♔Lewis♔♔Montana♔♔United States♔University of South Florida Morsani College of Medicine♔♔♔♔♔♔My work is in biostatistics, and I spend a lot of time reading AI validation studies across clinical domains. What I'm seeing in the literature right now genuinely worries me, and I think HHS needs to hear this before moving forward on accelerating adoption.⏎⏎The reproducibility problem is not abstract. A 2024 survey across ACG member institutions found that 58% of gastroenterology researchers had encountered AI performance claims in clinical literature they couldn't reproduce under real-world practice conditions. The two most commonly cited reasons were inconsistent validation datasets and inadequate reporting of confidence intervals. I work in biostatistics. Those are not minor methodological quibbles. Those are the kinds of failures that mean a tool performs well in a paper and then does something different when a clinician in Billings, Montana is actually using it on a patient.⏎⏎And the subgroup problem is worse. 34% of those same researchers noted that published AI studies in GI endoscopy lacked sufficient sample sizes to support subgroup-level generalizability claims. Which brings me to what I actually want HHS to think hard about, because I don't think the acceleration framing fully grapples with this: when a model is trained on data that already reflects decades of unequal care, and then deployed at scale before anyone has done rigorous bias auditing, you're not just making an error. You're institutionalizing it. The apparent authority of an algorithmic output makes clinicians less likely to question it, not more. There's evidence on this from human factors research and from implementation studies. The bias doesn't disappear into the math, it just becomes harder to see and harder to challenge.⏎⏎I keep coming back to a specific example that stuck with me from a 2023 NEJM paper on a sepsis prediction algorithm deployed across a large health system. The model's performance degraded significantly in Black patients relative to white patients, and the degradation wasn't caught for months after deployment because the post-market surveillance was essentially voluntary and inconsistently applied. That is not a hypothetical failure mode. That is a documented, real harm that happened under conditions very similar to what HHS is now proposing to accelerate.⏎⏎I'm not opposed to AI in clinical care. I want to be clear about that because I think people dismiss this kind of concern as technophobia. The question is whether we're building the evidence infrastructure to catch failures before they scale. Right now we're not. The FDA's current framework for AI/ML-based SaMD doesn't require prospective bias auditing stratified by race, insurance status, or geographic region as a condition of clearance. HHS could change that, or at minimum condition any federal investment or reimbursement incentives on meeting those standards.⏎⏎What I'd specifically ask HHS to consider is making bias auditing requirements, including mandatory stratified performance reporting across demographic subgroups, a precondition for any reimbursement pathway or federal research partnership for clinical AI tools. Not a recommendation. A requirement with teeth. The reporting should include confidence intervals, not just point estimates, and should be updated on a defined schedule after deployment, not just at the time of initial validation.⏎⏎I recognize there are implementation costs here and that smaller health systems may struggle with the infrastructure needed for ongoing post-market surveillance. That's a real tension and I don't want to pretend it isn't. But the alternative, which is moving fast and hoping someone catches the bias problems later, has a track record at this point and it's not good.⏎⏎One more thing I'd flag: the confidence interval issue I mentioned earlier is something HHS could address through research funding priorities. A lot of the AI validation studies getting published right now are underpowered for the claims they're making. If HHS is going to invest in AI research infrastructure, requiring minimum sample size thresholds and mandatory reporting of uncertainty bounds in federally funded AI studies would at least raise the floor on the evidence base. Whether journals would actually enforce that is a separate♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0441♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from NeuroFlow, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔NeuroFlow♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to Health and Human Services Department Request for Information⏎(RIN 0955-AA13)⏎February 23, 2026⏎HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎NeuroFlow appreciates the opportunity to respond to the Department of Health and Human⏎Services' Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence⏎(AI) in clinical care.⏎NeuroFlow develops AI-enabled clinical decision support (CDS) and population health tools⏎deployed nationally across health systems, payers, and federal partners. Our systems operate⏎exclusively within supervised clinical workflows. They do not provide autonomous medical⏎advice or function as direct-to-consumer therapeutic agents. They augment licensed clinicians⏎by supporting early detection, risk stratification, care matching, and measurement-informed care⏎at scale.⏎Public discussion of AI in healthcare often centers on patient-facing chatbots and autonomous⏎systems. These tools warrant appropriate oversight, but they differ materially from⏎clinician-supervised CDS embedded within accountable care teams. Federal policy should⏎distinguish between these categories to ensure regulation remains proportional to risk.⏎Behavioral health is a particularly urgent domain for thoughtful AI policy. Comorbid behavioral⏎and physical health conditions drive substantial excess medical spending, yet behavioral health⏎has long lacked infrastructure parity in federal health IT investment. AI-enabled CDS can⏎function as clinical infrastructure, supporting workforce capacity, improving access, and enabling⏎integration under value-based care.⏎Federal policy should focus on aligning incentives around measurable outcomes and total cost⏎of care, rather than treating AI as a discrete reimbursable intervention. When payment rewards⏎prevention and integration, adoption of high-value clinical decision support follows naturally.⏎To accelerate responsible adoption, HHS should:⏎● Differentiate between supervised clinical decision support and autonomous⏎patient-facing AI systems, applying proportionate scrutiny to autonomy and risk.⏎● Align reimbursement with measurable outcomes and total cost accountability.⏎1⏎⏎● Expand risk-bearing value-based models in Traditional Medicare and Medicare⏎Advantage to create natural incentives for adoption of population health AI infrastructure.⏎● Strengthen behavioral health accountability within Medicare Advantage and other federal⏎programs to drive systematic measurement-based care.⏎● Accelerate implementation of USCDI+ Behavioral Health to ensure structured,⏎interoperable behavioral health data.⏎● Understand AI in behavioral health as infrastructure that operationalizes⏎measurement-informed care and enables scalable integration, not as a replacement for⏎clinicians.⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health⏎care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The primary barriers to adoption of AI in clinical care are not technical limitations in model⏎capability. They are structural misalignment across regulatory interpretation, data infrastructure,⏎workflow integration, and payment incentives.⏎Failure to Distinguish Clinician-Supervised CDS from Autonomous AI⏎Public and policy discourse frequently conflates autonomous, patient-facing AI systems with⏎clinician-supervised clinical decision support tools. These categories differ significantly in both⏎risk profile and governance structure.⏎Clinician-facing CDS tools operate within established clinical accountability frameworks. They⏎surface information, risk signals, or guideline-based recommendations for licensed clinicians to⏎independently evaluate and act upon. These tools are addressed under Section 520(o)(1)(E) of⏎the 21st Century Cures Act (21 U.S.C. §360j(o)(1)(E)), which excludes certain CDS software⏎from device regulation when clinicians can independently review the basis for⏎recommendations. Autonomous AI systems that independently generate therapeutic advice or⏎make patient-facing care decisions without clinician oversight present a separate regulatory⏎discussion entirely.⏎When these two categories are treated as equivalent, health systems often apply heightened⏎scrutiny designed for autonomous systems to clinician-supervised CDS. This creates⏎unnecessary friction and slows adoption of lower-risk infrastructure tools designed to augment⏎professional judgment rather than replace it.⏎Behavioral Health Infrastructure Deficits⏎Behavioral health continues to operate with significantly weaker health IT infrastructure than⏎physical health. The HITECH Act catalyzed widespread EHR adoption across medical⏎specialties but largely excluded behavioral health providers. Behavioral health EHR adoption⏎and data standardization lag significantly behind the rest of the health care system, and this⏎exclusion has had compounding effects over time.⏎2⏎⏎Behavioral health documentation is frequently unstructured. Data elements such as symptom⏎scales, suicide risk assessments, and treatment response metrics are inconsistently captured⏎across organizations. Exchange of behavioral health data across systems remains limited, and⏎integrated behavioral-physical health records are incomplete in many care settings.⏎Clinical decision support systems rely on structured, longitudinal data to function optimally.⏎While AI models can incorporate physical health data, utilization patterns, and medication⏎history when behavioral health data are sparse, incomplete behavioral health documentation⏎reduces precision and limits scalability. Without improvements to behavioral health data⏎infrastructure, the clinical value of AI tools in this domain will remain constrained.⏎Workflow Integration and EHR Friction⏎Adoption depends on seamless integration into routine care delivery systems. Clinical decision⏎support must align with established workflows and integrate into certified EHR platforms without⏎disruption. In practice, technical and contractual barriers slow third-party integration and limit⏎timely API access, delaying deployment even when the underlying tools are clinically ready.⏎CMS's Health Tech Ecosystem initiative appropriately recognizes these integration challenges.⏎Continued progress toward more open, API-enabled interoperability will be critical to⏎accelerating responsible adoption of clinician-supervised AI tools across care settings.⏎Misaligned Payment Incentives⏎Incentive alignment remains the final and perhaps most consequential structural barrier. In⏎payment environments that reward volume, organizations that invest in prevention and⏎population management may not see immediate financial return. Preventing a psychiatric⏎hospitalization or reducing emergency department utilization improves outcomes and lowers⏎total cost of care, but in fee-for-service models that value accrues to payers rather than the⏎providers who made the investment.⏎Risk-bearing value-based arrangements create clear incentives to adopt infrastructure that⏎supports early detection, measurement-informed care, and avoidance of preventable utilization.⏎Until payment structures reward outcomes rather than activity, the organizations best positioned⏎to deploy behavioral health AI will face a financial disincentive to do so.⏎The largest barriers, in short, are not algorithmic. They are regulatory clarity, behavioral health⏎data infrastructure, workflow integration, and payment alignment. Addressing all four is⏎necessary for responsible adoption at scale.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should⏎HHS prioritize and why?⏎HHS can accelerate responsible adoption through targeted clarity and incentive alignment⏎across four priority areas.⏎3⏎⏎Reaffirm the Clinical Decision Support Exemption Under the 21st Century Cures Act⏎Section 520(o)(1)(E) of the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act (21 U.S.C. §360j(o)(1)(E))⏎excludes certain clinician-supervised CDS tools from device regulation when they enable health⏎care professionals to independently review the basis for recommendations. Behavioral health⏎CDS tools that surface evidence-based treatment pathways, risk stratification insights, and⏎treatment response predictions for clinician review fall squarely within this framework.⏎Reaffirming this statutory boundary supports predictable innovation and reduces the⏎procurement hesitation that slows adoption of lower-risk supervised tools.⏎Modernize Consent Infrastructure Under 42 CFR Part 2⏎42 CFR Part 2 governs the confidentiality of substance use disorder treatment records and⏎currently prevents AI algorithms from accessing SUD data even within integrated care teams⏎where patients have consented to coordinated treatment. This restriction is particularly⏎consequential for behavioral health AI, because patients with co-occurring substance use and⏎mental health conditions represent some of the highest-risk, highest-cost individuals in any⏎population. When AI risk stratification models cannot access complete clinical records, their⏎precision is compromised for exactly the patients who stand to benefit most. Modernized,⏎electronic consent mechanisms that enable secure data exchange for treatment, payment, and⏎operations would improve integration of behavioral and physical health data while preserving⏎the privacy protections patients depend on. Comprehensive data access improves AI-supported⏎risk stratification and enables coordinated care teams to act on complete clinical pictures rather⏎than partial records.⏎Accelerate Risk-Bearing Value-Based Payment Models in Traditional Medicare and Medicare⏎Advantage⏎Value-based payment models, including accountable care organizations, bundled payments,⏎and capitated arrangements, align financial incentives around total cost of care and outcomes.⏎Behavioral health conditions drive substantial excess medical spending when comorbid with⏎chronic disease. Organizations accountable for total cost of care develop natural incentives to⏎deploy AI-enabled measurement and population health infrastructure, because preventing⏎crises and reducing avoidable utilization directly improves their financial position. Payment⏎reform, not new technology codes, will be the primary driver of sustainable adoption. CMS⏎should expand risk-bearing arrangements in Traditional Medicare and Medicare Advantage and⏎ensure behavioral health outcomes are explicitly included in accountability structures.⏎Current time-based billing in programs like the Collaborative Care Model, billed under CPT⏎codes 99492 through 99494, creates a structural disincentive against efficiency-enhancing⏎technology. Organizations using AI tools to manage larger patient panels with better outcomes⏎generate less billable time, not more. Payment structures should reward complexity⏎management and measured clinical outcomes, including remission rates, crisis prevention, and⏎reduced hospitalizations, rather than time spent on care management activities.⏎4⏎⏎Strengthen Behavioral Health Accountability in Medicare Advantage and Medicaid⏎Incorporating robust behavioral health outcome measures into Medicare Advantage Star⏎Ratings and managed care contracts would drive systematic adoption of measurement-informed⏎care infrastructure. Relevant measures include remission benchmarks, proactive screening⏎rates, and avoidable crisis utilization. Higher Star Ratings and associated quality bonus⏎payments would reward plans using validated measurement systems achievable at population⏎scale through AI-enabled infrastructure, without mandating specific technologies or creating new⏎approval requirements.⏎Establish Federal Behavioral Health AI Validation Infrastructure⏎HHS should support representative, de-identified validation datasets and shared evaluation⏎methodologies through ARPA-H, NIMH, and related public-private initiatives. Limitations in⏎small, non-generalizable datasets undermine trust and slow adoption. Federally supported⏎validation infrastructure would reduce duplicative review burdens on health systems, improve⏎transparency in how tools are evaluated, and create common benchmarks that allow⏎organizations to assess AI performance based on measurable evidence rather than vendor⏎claims.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist⏎and what role should HHS play?⏎A primary implementation concern for AI-generated high-risk alerts, such as those triggered by⏎suicide risk detection or crisis prediction models, is liability. Health systems currently lack clear⏎expectations for what constitutes an adequate and defensible clinical response, particularly⏎when immediate intervention is operationally unrealistic given staffing and resource constraints.⏎A clinician who receives an AI-generated urgent alert faces a genuine question about what the⏎standard of care requires in response. HHS can reduce this uncertainty by articulating best⏎practices and tiered response expectations for AI-generated urgent alerts that account for⏎real-world organizational capacity, without creating new liability exposure for organizations⏎deploying these tools responsibly.⏎A related gap involves the scope of existing privacy frameworks. HIPAA's definition of covered⏎entities does not adequately address the range of digital health and AI tools now operating in⏎clinical contexts. Patient-facing tools and AI applications that connect to EHRs may handle⏎sensitive behavioral health information without falling clearly within existing privacy and⏎cybersecurity requirements. HHS should clarify how HIPAA applies to AI-enabled CDS tools⏎operating within supervised clinical workflows and identify where gaps in coverage require⏎updated guidance or rulemaking. Behavioral health data carries particular sensitivity, and⏎patients and clinicians alike need assurance that the frameworks governing its use are keeping⏎pace with the technology.⏎Health systems and payers also lack consistent criteria for evaluating AI solutions before⏎procurement. Without shared standards, each organization conducts its own bespoke security,⏎5⏎⏎validation, and governance review. Vendors face inconsistent expectations across⏎organizations, and health systems shoulder duplicative evaluation burdens that extend timelines⏎and consume resources. HHS guidance on explainability, traceability, transparency, and⏎validation would give organizations a coherent framework for accountable deployment. It would⏎also create more consistent market expectations, reducing friction for responsible vendors and⏎raising the floor for the field as a whole.⏎Question 4: For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation⏎methods (pre- and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes?⏎For clinician-supervised clinical decision support systems, evaluation should rest on four⏎principles: explainability, traceability, transparency, and validation.⏎Explainability requires that model recommendations align with established evidence-based⏎workflows and clearly surface the clinical variables that influenced each suggestion. Clinicians⏎must understand how outputs relate to guideline-concordant care pathways so they can⏎exercise independent judgment. Evaluation should confirm that recommendations are anchored⏎in recognized medical standards rather than opaque or unconstrained reasoning. A clinician⏎who cannot understand why a tool flagged a patient cannot responsibly act on that flag or⏎override it.⏎Traceability ensures that every model output can be audited. Systems should maintain logs⏎capturing relevant data inputs, decision thresholds, and model version history. Organizations⏎can then review how recommendations were generated, support internal quality oversight, and⏎respond to adverse events with a clear record of what the system surfaced and when.⏎Transparency requires disclosure of intended use, model limitations, validation evidence, and⏎deployment context. Organizations should communicate clearly where and how models operate⏎within clinical workflows and maintain recognized security and data protection standards.⏎Transparency strengthens institutional trust, enables informed procurement decisions, and gives⏎clinical governance committees the information they need to evaluate tools against their own⏎patient populations.⏎Validation requires ongoing assessment of clinical accuracy, fairness across patient populations,⏎and measurable impact on care delivery. For behavioral health applications, validation should⏎examine outcomes such as access to care, symptom improvement, crisis identification, and⏎avoidable utilization. Importantly, validation is not a one-time event. AI models that continue⏎learning and adapting require ongoing monitoring to detect performance drift over time.⏎Evaluation should remain proportional to risk and aligned with existing clinical governance⏎structures rather than imposing entirely new oversight mechanisms.⏎HHS can accelerate responsible adoption by promoting these core governance principles and⏎encouraging shared outcome benchmarks for behavioral health applications. Clear expectations⏎6⏎⏎around explainability, traceability, transparency, and validation will support innovation while⏎reinforcing patient safety and public trust.⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation,⏎certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and⏎effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can best support responsible private-sector activity by setting clear baseline expectations⏎while allowing accreditation and testing frameworks to evolve through industry and professional⏎collaboration.⏎Rather than creating new technology-specific certification programs, HHS should articulate core⏎expectations for clinician-facing clinical decision support: documentation of intended use,⏎disclosure of validation evidence, auditability of outputs, and adherence to recognized security⏎standards. Clear federal signals reduce procurement uncertainty without imposing duplicative⏎regulatory structures that slow innovation or disadvantage smaller developers who lack⏎resources for parallel compliance programs.⏎HHS can also strengthen industry-driven testing by supporting shared benchmarking⏎infrastructure. De-identified, demographically representative validation datasets in high-impact⏎domains such as behavioral health would enable voluntary comparative evaluation across tools⏎using common outcome measures. This infrastructure should function as research support⏎rather than certification, promoting transparency and comparability without creating a new⏎approval regime. Datasets should intentionally include rural populations, Veterans, and⏎high-comorbidity Medicare beneficiaries, groups that are consistently underrepresented in⏎current validation studies and whose care needs are among the most acute.⏎Federal convening authority and procurement policies can reinforce these principles by⏎rewarding transparency and measurable outcomes. Promoting clarity, comparability, and⏎collaboration rather than additional licensing layers will foster a competitive marketplace that⏎supports both innovation and public trust.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance⏎and cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on⏎quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI has met or exceeded expectations when deployed within supervised clinical workflows to⏎address defined operational and clinical gaps.⏎In behavioral health integration, AI-enabled referral matching has reduced time to treatment and⏎lowered behavioral health-related inpatient utilization while improving total cost of care. When⏎primary care providers are supported by algorithmic matching tools that account for clinical⏎characteristics, patient preferences, and real-time network capacity, access improves and⏎downstream crisis utilization declines.1⏎7⏎⏎Natural language processing and language models applied to unstructured patient-reported⏎data has demonstrated particular value in suicide risk detection and crisis response.2 NLP⏎systems identify risk signals that structured screening tools alone may not capture, enabling⏎earlier clinician review and intervention when embedded in supervised workflows. In a⏎retrospective database study, more than half of patients flagged by NLP analysis of free-text⏎inputs would not have been identified through structured screening alone.3 These patients were⏎at elevated risk and would have gone undetected without passive, continuous analysis of the⏎language they used outside of formal assessment windows. AI has extended this capability to⏎crisis platforms as well, where call-, chat-, and text-based services face volume challenges that⏎staffing alone cannot solve. AI-enabled triage systems identify high-risk contacts and route them⏎to appropriate responders faster than manual review allows, reducing wait times and improving⏎the match between contact severity and responder skill. AI has also demonstrated value in⏎training crisis responders, using simulated scenarios to build consistent skills at a frequency and⏎scale that traditional supervision cannot achieve. These applications do not replace human⏎responders. They ensure that the highest-risk individuals reach a prepared clinician faster.⏎Population-level use cases have also shown meaningful impact. Suspect diagnosis identification⏎and behavioral health risk stratification models help care teams identify patients with⏎undiagnosed or under-managed conditions, particularly those with comorbid medical illness.⏎When paired with measurement-informed care, these systems support more appropriate⏎resource allocation and reduce avoidable emergency department utilization.4⏎Measurement-informed care represents one of the highest-value and most evidence-supported⏎AI applications in behavioral health. MIC involves systematic use of validated assessment⏎instruments during clinical encounters to track symptom trajectories, detect non-response, and⏎guide treatment adjustments. Despite demonstrated clinical benefits, MIC is underutilized. This⏎is largely because manual administration, scoring, and visualization are administratively⏎burdensome. AI removes that barrier directly. Automated assessment administration, real-time⏎scoring, and longitudinal visualization of patient trajectories enable clinicians to identify⏎deterioration or treatment non-response between scheduled appointments. When AI⏎operationalizes MIC at population scale, it transforms a proven but underutilized practice into⏎routine infrastructure.⏎AI systems that operationalize evidence-based treatment pathways, by surfacing⏎guideline-concordant options or predicting treatment response based on patient characteristics,⏎have shown promise in reducing trial-and-error prescribing and improving symptom trajectories.⏎These applications are particularly valuable in primary care settings where specialty behavioral⏎health expertise is limited and clinicians may be managing behavioral health conditions without⏎dedicated support.⏎Administrative support tools, including automated documentation assistance and coding⏎validation, have produced efficiency gains that reduce clinician burden and support program⏎integrity without altering clinical decision authority. These tools are often underappreciated as⏎clinical infrastructure, but clinician burnout and documentation burden directly constrain the⏎capacity of care teams to engage patients meaningfully.⏎8⏎⏎Where AI tools fall short is typically in one of three contexts: when deployed outside supervised⏎care environments, when not integrated into existing workflows, or when disconnected from⏎measurable accountability structures. Tools that function as standalone digital interventions⏎without clear linkage to care teams or outcome measurement have produced less consistent⏎impact. The evidence base for direct-to-consumer therapeutic AI remains far thinner than for⏎clinician-supervised decision support, and the governance structures required to ensure safety⏎in autonomous patient-facing contexts are still developing.⏎The greatest potential lies in AI applications that operationalize measurement-informed,⏎guideline-concordant care for patients with complex behavioral and physical comorbidity. These⏎populations account for disproportionate healthcare spending. Systems that support early⏎detection, risk stratification, treatment matching, and proactive adherence monitoring within⏎risk-bearing payment models offer the clearest pathway to improved outcomes and cost⏎containment.1,4⏎Question 7: Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption of clinician-facing AI is typically shaped by three groups: clinical leadership,⏎information technology leadership, and compliance.⏎Chief Medical Officers and clinical governance committees evaluate whether a tool aligns with⏎evidence-based standards, supports appropriate workflows, and demonstrates meaningful⏎clinical impact. Without credible validation evidence and a clear articulation of intended use,⏎clinical approval rarely advances. These committees often lack shared frameworks for⏎evaluating AI tools and must construct their own review criteria from scratch, a process that is⏎time-consuming and inconsistent across organizations.⏎Chief Information Officers, information security teams, and compliance leaders control⏎integration, data access, cybersecurity review, and regulatory risk assessment. These functions⏎often determine the pace of adoption more than clinical enthusiasm does. In the absence of⏎widely accepted evaluation standards for clinician-facing AI, organizations conduct bespoke⏎security, validation, and governance reviews independently, extending procurement timelines⏎significantly. A tool that passes clinical review may sit in IT security review for months while the⏎organization works through questions that have no shared industry answer.⏎The primary hurdle is not resistance to AI itself but variability in institutional review expectations.⏎When no shared framework exists for assessing transparency, validation evidence, and⏎accountability structures, each organization must define for itself what constitutes sufficient⏎review. This variability disadvantages smaller health systems and safety-net providers that lack⏎dedicated AI governance infrastructure and cannot absorb extended procurement timelines.⏎Clear federal guidance around baseline transparency and governance expectations would⏎reduce that variability while preserving appropriate institutional oversight. Consistency in⏎9⏎⏎expectations, rather than additional regulation, would support more efficient and responsible⏎adoption across organizations of all sizes.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel⏎research, and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific⏎data types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability in behavioral health would materially expand safe and scalable AI⏎deployment.⏎The most consequential data elements include structured symptom assessments for⏎depression, anxiety, and PTSD, along with suicide risk indicators, longitudinal treatment⏎response data, medication management history, crisis utilization signals, and relevant social⏎determinants of health. When these data are computable and exchangeable across care⏎settings, decision support systems can generate more precise and clinically meaningful insights.⏎Today, many of these elements exist as scanned forms, free-text notes, or proprietary formats⏎that AI systems cannot reliably access or interpret. An emerging body of evidence also suggests⏎that passively collected data from smartphones and wearables, including mobility patterns,⏎sleep duration, and heart rate variability, can serve as objective proxies for mental health status,⏎detecting symptom changes and predicting deterioration before adverse events occur. As this⏎evidence matures, interoperability infrastructure that can accommodate these signals will⏎become increasingly important to realizing their clinical value.⏎USCDI+ Behavioral Health represents an important step toward standardizing these elements.⏎When certified EHR systems support consistent capture and API-based exchange of behavioral⏎health measures, data completeness improves and reliance on fragmented documentation⏎declines. Reliable interoperability enhances both clinical accuracy and comparability across⏎organizations. It also enables AI developers to build and validate tools against representative⏎datasets rather than the proprietary, single-system data that currently shapes most behavioral⏎health models.⏎Interoperability also supports benchmarking and research. When outcome measures and⏎utilization data are structured and exchangeable, health systems and payers can more⏎transparently assess performance, compare approaches, and participate in multi-site evaluation⏎efforts. This is particularly important for Medicare and high-comorbidity populations, where⏎longitudinal, cross-setting data are essential to understanding total cost impact. Single-site⏎studies cannot answer the questions that policymakers and payers need answered about AI⏎performance at population scale.⏎Enhanced interoperability does more than facilitate AI development. It strengthens care⏎coordination, measurement-informed practice, and accountability across the entire healthcare⏎system. The behavioral health field has waited too long for infrastructure parity. USCDI+⏎Behavioral Health, combined with meaningful enforcement through EHR certification and⏎payment policy, offers a credible path forward.⏎10⏎⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers consistently identify access, coordination, and timeliness as primary⏎challenges in behavioral health care. Long wait times, difficulty finding appropriate providers,⏎fragmented communication between medical and behavioral health clinicians, and delayed⏎identification of worsening symptoms remain persistent concerns. AI-enabled decision support⏎can help address these gaps by supporting earlier detection, more appropriate care matching,⏎and proactive outreach within supervised care teams.1⏎For individuals managing co-occurring medical and behavioral health conditions, improved⏎coordination between providers is especially important. Patients with these conditions frequently⏎repeat their histories to multiple providers, re-disclose traumatic experiences, and navigate⏎disconnected systems without a clear point of accountability. Tools that help clinicians recognize⏎untreated depression, substance use disorder, or emerging suicide risk within routine care⏎settings can reduce preventable crises and avoidable hospital utilization.3,4⏎At the same time, patients and caregivers express understandable concerns about privacy, data⏎security, bias, and the potential erosion of the clinician-patient relationship. Behavioral health⏎information carries particular sensitivity. Disclosures made in therapy, risk assessments⏎documented in clinical notes, and crisis contacts recorded in health systems represent some of⏎the most personal information a patient shares. Many patients fear that automated systems⏎could displace human judgment or reduce the empathy that makes behavioral health treatment⏎effective.⏎These concerns reinforce the importance of deploying AI as clinician-supervised decision⏎support rather than as autonomous clinical actors. Transparency about how data are used, clear⏎communication about the role of AI within care teams, and strong institutional oversight are⏎essential to maintaining trust. Patients also want assurance that AI systems have been⏎validated for populations that look like them, that rural patients and Veterans are not receiving⏎tools trained exclusively on urban academic medical center data, and that the therapeutic⏎relationship remains central to care.⏎When AI is positioned as infrastructure that supports rather than replaces licensed⏎professionals, it can enhance safety, access, and continuity while preserving the clinician-patient⏎relationship that patients consistently identify as essential to their care.⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to⏎accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research that evaluates how clinician-supervised AI decision support⏎affects real-world clinical outcomes and total cost of care in integrated settings.⏎Particularly important are studies focused on populations with co-occurring behavioral and⏎physical health conditions, as these patients drive disproportionate utilization and spending⏎11⏎⏎across Medicare and Medicaid. Research should examine whether AI-enabled early⏎identification, risk stratification, treatment matching, and measurement-informed care reduce⏎avoidable hospitalizations, emergency department visits, and symptom persistence over time.4⏎Most existing studies measure algorithmic performance rather than patient-level outcomes. The⏎field needs pragmatic effectiveness trials in diverse real-world settings, including VA Medical⏎Centers, FQHCs, rural hospitals, and community mental health centers, that link AI deployment⏎to remission rates, crisis events, and total cost of care.⏎Implementation research is equally important. Understanding how AI tools are embedded into⏎primary care and behavioral health workflows, how clinicians interpret and act on⏎recommendations, and what governance structures sustain impact over time will inform scalable⏎adoption strategies. Research should examine what organizational characteristics predict⏎successful implementation, how financial incentives affect adoption fidelity, and what⏎implementation support rural and safety-net providers need to deploy these tools effectively.⏎HHS should also prioritize research that examines equity and generalizability. Behavioral health⏎prevalence, access patterns, and treatment engagement vary significantly across demographic⏎and geographic groups. Current validation datasets often underrepresent rural populations,⏎Veterans, and communities of color. Research must evaluate whether AI-enabled tools perform⏎consistently across diverse populations and actively correct for gaps in historical training data⏎that may reflect underdiagnosis rather than lower prevalence.⏎Finally, HHS should support research on the appropriate design and oversight of ongoing⏎performance monitoring for adaptive AI systems. Traditional device validation assumes a static⏎intervention. AI tools that continue learning in deployment require frameworks for detecting⏎performance drift, managing model updates, and maintaining accountability over time. This is an⏎area where the field lacks consensus and where federal investment in methodology would⏎benefit all stakeholders.⏎Question 10a: Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their⏎use in clinical care?⏎Yes. Peer-reviewed studies demonstrate measurable clinical and financial impact when⏎AI-enabled tools are deployed within supervised care environments.⏎Technology-enabled referral optimization was associated with improved access to behavioral⏎health care, reduced behavioral health-related inpatient admissions, and reductions in total⏎medical spending in a risk-adjusted insured population.1⏎Natural language processing for suicide risk detection identified individuals with suicidal ideation⏎who would not have been captured by structured screening alone. In a retrospective database⏎study, more than half of flagged users may not have been identified without NLP-based analysis⏎of free-text inputs.3 This finding has direct implications for population health programs that rely⏎solely on structured assessment tools to identify patients at risk.⏎12⏎⏎Technology-enabled behavioral health integration was associated with reductions in emergency⏎department utilization and improvements in depressive symptom trajectories when⏎measurement-informed care was systematically applied.4 These results held across primary⏎care and OBGYN settings, suggesting the model generalizes across care contexts rather than⏎depending on specialty behavioral health infrastructure.⏎Collectively, these findings indicate that clinician-facing AI tools, when integrated into care⏎teams and tied to measurable outcomes, can improve access, reduce crisis utilization, and⏎support cost containment.⏎Question 10b: How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of⏎using AI as part of clinical care?⏎The literature primarily evaluates avoided utilization, symptom improvement, and short-term⏎changes in medical spending. Fewer studies assess long-term cost transfers across payment⏎models or fully account for implementation costs, including technology infrastructure, staff⏎training, workflow redesign, and ongoing maintenance.⏎Evidence increasingly suggests that AI-enabled behavioral health integration produces the⏎strongest financial signal in risk-bearing or accountable care environments, where reductions in⏎hospitalizations and emergency utilization translate directly into total cost savings.1,4 In⏎fee-for-service models, cost reductions often accrue to payers rather than providers, dampening⏎the adoption incentive for clinical organizations that bear the implementation cost without⏎capturing the financial benefit.⏎This misalignment between who invests and who benefits is a core structural problem that⏎payment reform must address. Economic analyses that ignore transfer effects, specifically the⏎question of which stakeholder captures the value generated by AI-enabled care improvement,⏎will underestimate the barriers to adoption and overestimate the market incentives for⏎deployment.⏎Future research should standardize economic evaluation methodologies, including attribution⏎models and total cost of care analyses, to better quantify the fiscal impact of⏎clinician-supervised AI across different reimbursement structures. Policymakers, payers, and⏎health systems all need this evidence to make investment decisions, and the field cannot⏎produce it without more rigorous and consistent approaches to economic evaluation.⏎Conclusion⏎Behavioral health AI used as clinical decision support does not displace licensed professionals.⏎It operationalizes evidence-based pathways, strengthens measurement-informed care, and⏎supports earlier identification of risk within accountable clinical environments. The regulatory⏎architecture for these tools is already defined. The policy priority is ensuring that expectations⏎remain proportionate to their function and risk profile.⏎13⏎⏎In behavioral health, the need for this infrastructure is particularly urgent. Comorbid behavioral⏎and physical health conditions drive disproportionate healthcare utilization and cost, especially⏎within Medicare populations. Workforce shortages and fragmented data systems limit the ability⏎of care teams to manage these conditions proactively. Behavioral health was excluded from the⏎HITECH Act's investment cycle and fell further behind as a result. AI policy must not repeat that⏎exclusion.⏎When aligned with risk-bearing payment models, interoperable data standards, and transparent⏎governance principles, AI-enabled decision support can improve access, reduce avoidable⏎utilization, and enhance accountability without compromising clinician judgment or patient trust.⏎HHS has the opportunity to distinguish clearly among AI use cases, reinforce predictable⏎regulatory pathways for supervised clinical decision support, and align incentives around⏎measurable outcomes. Doing so will accelerate responsible adoption while preserving both⏎innovation and patient safety.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide input and look forward to continued engagement as⏎the Department advances its AI strategy. Please contact Matt Miclette, Vice President of⏎Innovation & Policy, at matthew@neuroflow.com with any questions.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Matt Miclette⏎Vice President, Innovation & Policy⏎NeuroFlow⏎14⏎⏎References⏎1. Zhu Y, Saynisch P, David G, Shatraw W, Mailloux A, Patel A, Dow T, Smith-McLallen A.⏎Linking insured adults to behavioral health care: a cost-saving solution. Am J Manag Care.⏎2025 Dec;31(12):752-759. doi: 10.37765/ajmc.2025.89834. PMID: 41512258.⏎2. Holley, D., Daly, B., Beverly, B. et al. Evaluating Generative Pretrained Transformer (GPT)⏎models for suicide risk assessment in synthetic patient journal entries. BMC Psychiatry 25,⏎753 (2025). https://doi.org/10.1186/s12888-025-07088-5⏎3. Hartz M, Hickey D, Acosta L, Brooks A, Holley D, Zaubler T. Using natural language⏎processing to detect suicidal ideation and prompt urgent interventions: A retrospective⏎database study. Innovations in Digital Health, Diagnostics, and Biomarkers. 2024;4:6-8. doi:⏎10.36401/IDDB-23-10.⏎4. Pardes A, Rene R, Chun P, Cherson M. Technology-enabled Behavioral Health Integration⏎Decreases Emergency Department Utilization. Clin Pract Epidemiol Ment Health. 2022 Sep⏎20;18:e174501792208150. doi: 10.2174/17450179-v18-e2208150. PMID: 37274856;⏎PMCID: PMC10158075.⏎15♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0442♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-16T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Melissa♔Perez♔♔Oklahoma♔♔United States♔Proscia♔♔♔♔♔♔My situation might be a little different from most people submitting comments here, and I'm honestly not sure this process is even meant for someone like me, but I'm going to try anyway.⏎⏎I'm the CFO at Proscia, a smaller health organization in Oklahoma. We have 8 employees total. Not a big hospital network, not a tech company. Just a small operation trying to do right by our patients and keep the lights on.⏎⏎Here's what I keep getting stuck on: when HHS talks about accelerating AI adoption in clinical settings, does that mean rules and requirements are coming for everyone, or is this mainly aimed at large health systems and AI vendors? Because if certification requirements or new compliance mandates come out of this, I genuinely don't know how we absorb that cost. We already spend a disproportionate amount of time on billing reconciliation as it is. Last year alone I tracked close to $340,000 in administrative hours just trying to sort out contradictory coverage decisions from different payers for the same types of AI-generated outputs. And that's us, with our current limited AI use. If new certification layers get added on top of that without any consideration for smaller organizations, I don't see how that math works.⏎⏎I'm also confused about which AI tools even fall under this. We use a scheduling assistant and a tool that helps flag incomplete documentation before claims go out. Neither of those feels like a medical device to me, but I've read enough to know that the line between "administrative tool" and "clinical decision support" is blurry and getting blurrier. Are we going to be responsible for validating and certifying tools like that? Does the vendor handle it? What if the vendor is a startup with 12 people?⏎⏎I'm not against AI. I want to be clear about that. I've seen it catch things that would have slipped through. But the question I can't get answered anywhere is whether small providers are expected to meet the same bar as, say, a 400-bed hospital system with a dedicated compliance department.⏎⏎If there's a small business exemption or a tiered approach being considered, I'd really like to know about it. Even just clearer guidance on what size or type of organization these rules are targeting would help. Right now reading through the RFI it feels like the audience is large health systems and AI developers, and organizations like ours are sort of♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0443♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Post Acute Wound and Skin Integrity Council , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Post Acute Wound and Skin Integrity Council♔♔♔♔♔♔RE:HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎FR #- 2025-23641⏎February 23, 2026⏎Dear Sir or Madam;⏎The Post-Acute Skin & Wound Integrity Council (PAWSIC) is pleased to provide input on Accelerating the Adoption⏎of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. We thank the HHS Office of the Deputy Secretary in collaboration⏎with ASTP/ONC in this Request for Information (RFI).⏎PAWSIC is a registered 501c6 non-profit organization, composed of an inter-professional team that serves post-⏎acute care stakeholders as the source and advocate for quality wound prevention and treatment. PAWSIC’s⏎intention is to leverage individual expertise to collectively synthesize and integrate evidence-based practices from⏎research along with nationally recognized guidelines from collaborating organizations in wound care. We advocate⏎for setting the highest standards of care in Post-Acute Care for practice implementation, while acknowledging the⏎complex regulations and reimbursement structures that may challenge and impact the continuum of care. With a⏎current membership of over 200 post-acute providers, collaborators and stakeholders, PAWSIC represents a⏎nationwide view of interdisciplinary professionals in the post-acute wound care environment.⏎Attached you will find our comments to the specific questions and inquiries within the RFI. We would be happy to⏎discuss comments on barriers, current structures, and recommendations at your convenience.⏎Thank you in advance for your time and attention to this important matter.⏎Regards,⏎Julie Rhodovi, MBA, CWCA, Jeanine Maguire, MPT, DPh, CWS Sarah Holden-Mount, PT,⏎LBBH Treasurer, PAWSIC President, PAWSIC CWS Vice President, PAWSIC⏎410-499-1340 267-733-3178 603-496-4151⏎_______________________________________________________________________________________________________⏎Board of Directors⏎EXECUTIVE BOARD- Jeanine Maguire MPT, CWS; Sarah Holden-Mount PT, CWS; Julie Rhodovi MBA, CWCA, LBBH;; Diane⏎Krasner PhD, RN, FAAN; Jennifer Bierhup, RN, WCC, CCM, iRNPA, FACCWS⏎DIRECTORS- Scott Bolhack MD, MBA, CMD, CWSP, FACP; Nicole Brandt, PharmD, MBA, BCGP,FASCP; Julie Britton MSN,⏎CGCNS-BC, RN-BC, FGNLA; Tony Costa RN, BS, MBA; Clive Horrocks DNP, RN, WCC, COCN; Karen Kennedy-Evans RN, APRN-BC,⏎FNP; Faith McNicholas, RHIT, CPC, CPCD, PCS, CDC; Cathy Milne APRN, MSN, ANP/ACNS-BC, CWOCN-AP; Betsy Reynolds,⏎PharmD; Amy Rivera, BSBA; Tara Roberts, PT, ACWPT, QCP, CDP; Ronald Rosen, MD, CMD, CWSP; Cassandre Voltaire, DO,⏎ABWMS; Stephanie Woelfel PT, DPT, CWS; Isaac Zralii, BS, LSWA, CBHT⏎⏎Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Adoption of AI in Health Care⏎Regulatory and Reimbursement Barriers⏎Innovation in AI for health care faces obstacles due to unclear regulations and a lack of⏎specific safeguards for clinical decisions and patient needs. This uncertainty hinders⏎growth, investment, and commercialization, as concerns persist in meeting proper patient⏎care standards.⏎The clinical landscape of wound management is uniquely vulnerable, functioning in an⏎environment critically devoid of standardized specialization, unified clinical pathways, and⏎established industry guardrails. This baseline instability is further compounded by a deeply⏎fragmented reimbursement system that varies drastically by state, care setting, and payer⏎type. Consequently, clinicians are forced to navigate severe logistical obstacles, resulting⏎in profoundly broken care transitions and compromised patient outcomes. It is into this⏎already fractured ecosystem that Artificial Intelligence (AI) is now being rapidly introduced.⏎While AI holds immense potential to enhance diagnostic accuracy, deploying these⏎powerful tools without a rigorous, mandated oversight framework will only amplify⏎systemic uncertainty. Practitioners are already struggling to advocate for evidence-based⏎care within the current chaotic climate; without decisive regulatory intervention, the⏎unguided integration of AI threatens to multiply clinical risks and introduce unprecedented⏎liability into the market⏎The current use of AI in reimbursement processes, including prior authorization, tends to⏎apply uniform approaches to areas such as wound care. Wound care requirements are⏎highly individualized, depending on factors like a patient's medical history, comorbidities,⏎and social determinants of health—information which is typically not included with claim⏎submissions. This omission can result in service denials. Such outcomes may negatively⏎impact patients, potentially leading to increased hospitalization, amputations, or mortality.⏎Workflow, Liability, and Clinician Adoption⏎Clinicians have raised concerns about medico-legal risks, especially when AI-generated⏎recommendations or claim reviews conflict with clinical judgment. Effective governance⏎frameworks are needed to address these scenarios. In November 2023, a claim alleged⏎UnitedHealth improperly denied Medicare Advantage patients’ necessary care by relying on⏎an AI model with a 90% error rate, overriding physician decisions.1 These denials affected⏎Medicare beneficiaries, many of whom have chronic wounds.⏎1Napolitano, E. (2023, November 20). UnitedHealth uses faulty AI to deny elderly patients medically necessary coverage, lawsuit claims.⏎CBS News.⏎⏎With AI already integrated into healthcare, the focus shifts from adoption to the need for⏎clear guidance on specific platforms, as well as assurance regarding clinical input and the⏎capability for clinical override.⏎Regulatory, Payment Policy, and Programmatic Recommendations for HHS to⏎Incentivize Effective AI Use in Clinical Care⏎1. Establish Clear Medicare Payment Pathways for AI Wound Services⏎PAWSIC strongly believes the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) should⏎develop clearly defined coverage and reimbursement mechanisms for algorithm-based⏎wound care services. This initiative would involve integrating artificial intelligence (AI) into⏎current quality and alternative payment programs, as well as updating data and⏎information technology regulations to support the secure use and sharing of wound images⏎and outcome data for the development and deployment of AI models. It is important to⏎note that PAWSIC sees the importance of clinical decision support and input from⏎practitioners with capability to override what an AI model may see as the best course of⏎care. Due to the uniqueness of wound care, specific to the patient, all AI technologies and⏎models should allow for clinical interference.⏎2. Expand AI Payment Models for Home Health and SNF Wound Care⏎Under 42 CFR Part 484 (home health) and Parts 413/409 (SNF consolidated billing and⏎PPS), PAWSIC recommends that HHS formally clarify the eligibility of AI-enabled wound⏎assessment and remote monitoring as reimbursable expenses. Where appropriate, these⏎services should be recognized as distinct categories for payment, contingent on⏎documented reductions in hospitalizations and amputations.⏎Given that chronic wound management frequently takes place within home health and⏎SNFs, the explicit inclusion of AI-enabled wound care as allowable costs or value-based⏎payment enhancements may significantly improve adoption in populations at elevated⏎risk.⏎3. Integration of AI into Quality Programs and Models for Wound Care⏎AI-assisted wound care should be incorporated into value-based healthcare programs. In⏎accordance with 42 CFR Part 414, Subpart O (Merit-based Incentive Payment System) and⏎Part 425 (Shared Savings Program), it is recommended that the Department of Health and⏎Human Services revise quality measures to specifically acknowledge validated AI tools⏎which demonstrate improvements in chronic wound healing rates, amputation rates, and⏎infection-related hospitalizations.⏎⏎For populations with a high incidence of wounds, such as individuals with diabetes or⏎peripheral artery disease, the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services could develop “AI-⏎enabled wound care” improvement activities within MIPS and ACO quality frameworks.⏎This approach would grant recognition to clinicians utilizing certified AI tools that meet⏎established standards for performance, equity, and governance. Aligning AI utilization with⏎payment adjustments serves to incentivize the effective application of AI, rather than⏎treating its adoption as an unfunded requirement.⏎Under 42 CFR Part 510 and demonstration waivers permitted by Section 1115A (CMMI⏎models), CMS could pilot AI-enabled chronic wound bundles, integrating AI-supported⏎assessment, triage, and remote monitoring as essential components within standardized⏎care pathways. These models may provide explicit funding for AI licenses and⏎infrastructure as part of care redesign expenses, with shared savings or bonuses tied to⏎outcomes such as improved wound healing, reduced sepsis/readmissions, and increased⏎limb salvage rates. The Center for Medicare & Medicaid Innovation has already⏎implemented models aligning payments with technology-enhanced care, making wound⏎care a suitable area given its measurable outcomes and significant downstream costs.⏎4. Clarification of Regulatory Treatment of AI in Wound Care⏎The Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) should establish clear guidelines⏎regarding the regulatory treatment of clinical AI and non-device AI within wound care.⏎Utilizing guidance from the Office of Inspector General (OIG) and Centers for Medicare &⏎Medicaid Services (CMS), as referenced in 42 CFR Parts 1001–1003 concerning program⏎integrity, alongside cross-references to FDA regulations pertaining to medical devices and⏎Software as a Medical Device (SaMD), HHS is encouraged to issue targeted, AI-specific⏎guidance. This guidance should address criteria delineating when wound AI qualifies as a⏎regulated device versus a non-device clinical decision support system, outline⏎requirements for performance monitoring, and specify documentation standards for⏎medical records. Most importantly, there needs to be guidelines and barriers associated⏎with AI decision making without clinical oversight from a trained practitioner.⏎In accordance with 45 CFR Part 92 (Section 1557, nondiscrimination) and HIPAA⏎regulations (45 CFR Parts 160 and 164), HHS should release guidance on evaluating wound⏎AI applications for potential bias related to skin tones, comorbidities, and environments, as⏎well as best practices for maintaining documentation to ensure compliance. The⏎establishment of clear and consistent expectations will promote thorough evaluation while⏎supporting the responsible integration of advanced solutions.⏎⏎Priority Areas for HHS-Funded AI Research in Wound Care⏎To accelerate the adoption of AI as part of clinical care, PAWSIC sees several specific⏎research priorities that HHS should consider, particularly within the context of wound care.⏎These priorities emphasize establishing real-world clinical value, promoting equity and⏎usability, and clarifying the impact of AI on cost and workflow outcomes, especially⏎regarding chronic and post-surgical wounds.⏎1. Real-World Effectiveness in Chronic Wounds⏎HHS should support prospective, multi-site trials of AI-enabled digital wound care⏎solutions across various care settings such as home health, skilled nursing facilities (SNF),⏎long term care facilities, and outpatient wound centers. These studies should⏎comprehensively measure outcomes including accuracy of wound type diagnosis, time to⏎heal, number of amputations, hospitalizations, and clinician time, focusing on wound⏎types like diabetic foot ulcers (DFU), venous leg ulcers (VLU), pressure injuries, arterial⏎ulcers, atypical ulcers, and surgical sites.⏎Comparative effectiveness studies are also needed to evaluate AI-supported wound care⏎pathways versus standard care. These investigations should assess how standardized AI⏎assessments influence adherence to evidence-based protocols and escalation decisions⏎in the management of complex wounds. This includes AI-based models associated with⏎prior authorization from Medicare and Medicare Advantage. We see concerns raised⏎surrounding denials of clinical needed services due to AI technology, creating major gaps in⏎care and lag times that create further wound care needs and higher expenses.⏎2. Imaging, Prediction, and Advanced Sensing⏎Priority should be given to research on robust, device-agnostic wound imaging⏎technologies, including 2D, 3D, multispectral, and fluorescence imaging, as well as tissue⏎oxygenation. AI models developed in this context should be capable of accurately⏎segmenting wounds, classifying tissue types, and predicting healing trajectories in diverse⏎care environments.⏎Furthermore, the development and clinical testing of “smart dressings” and sensor-⏎enabled bandages that leverage AI to detect early infection or clinical deterioration is⏎essential, particularly in high-risk settings such as surgical and diabetic foot wounds.⏎These technologies have the potential to trigger timely interventions and improve patient⏎outcomes.⏎⏎3. Equity, Skin Tone, and Population Diversity⏎Studies should be intentionally designed to ensure that AI wound tools perform effectively⏎across diverse skin tones, body sites, and comorbidity profiles. Research protocols should⏎include stratified performance reporting and prioritizing bias mitigation as a primary⏎endpoint.⏎In addition, investigations should explore how AI-driven wound assessment and remote⏎monitoring impact access to care, adherence, and clinical outcomes for rural populations,⏎homebound individuals, and those with low digital literacy, including the role of caregivers⏎in these contexts.⏎4. Workflow, Usability, and Human Factors⏎Implementation science is needed to understand how patients, nurses in all⏎settingsinterprofessional clinicians, and wound specialists access use, and perceive AI⏎tools in real-world settings. Research should address optimal user interface design,⏎patterns of user trust and override behavior, alerting thresholds, and the balance between⏎automation and clinical judgment required for effective, context-aware wound care.⏎Additional studies should focus on the integration of AI documentation into electronic⏎health records (EHRs) and wound registries with the goal of reducing documentation⏎burden and supporting seamless team communication and handoffs across different care⏎settings.⏎5. Economic and Value-Based Outcomes⏎Health economic analyses are crucial to linking AI-enabled wound care programs to⏎episode-of-care costs, advanced therapy utilization (such as skin substitutes and negative⏎pressure wound therapy), and potential downstream savings associated with avoided⏎complications.⏎Research should also test the effectiveness of AI-supported wound care bundles or care⏎pathways within value-based care models, such as home health, accountable care⏎organizations (ACO), or Medicare Advantage (MA). The objective is to quantify the potential⏎for shared savings and provide evidence to inform the design of future CMS payment⏎models.⏎Impact of AI Tools in Clinical Wound Care⏎Published Findings on Clinical Outcomes⏎Recent studies have investigated the effects of adopting AI tools in clinical wound care⏎settings. A large descriptive study of a digital wound care solution implemented across 59⏎⏎U.S. home health agencies, encompassing approximately 196,000 wound assessments,⏎reported a 38.2% reduction in average healing time. Specifically, healing times decreased⏎from 62.5 to 38.6 days between 2022 and 2023, with statistically significant improvements⏎observed across various wound types, including diabetic ulcers, pressure injuries, and⏎burns.2⏎Additionally, the same dataset revealed that more wounds were healing within three⏎months, along with substantial reductions in time to improvement and area reduction for⏎chronic wounds. These findings suggest that sustained use of the AI-enabled digital wound⏎care solutions is associated with faster healing and improved wound trajectories.⏎Measurement Accuracy and Workflow Efficiency⏎Implementation studies summarized by Wounds International indicate that mobile apps⏎utilizing deep learning–based wound measurement achieved sub-millimeter mean error⏎(~0.3 mm) and reduced assessment time by approximately 54%. First-attempt imaging⏎accuracy was around 92%, surpassing manual measurement and simplifying⏎documentation processes.3⏎2Mohammed, S., Patel, R., Toh, J., Nguyen, L., & Garcia, M. (2025). Impact of digital wound care solution on healing time: A descriptive⏎study in home health settings. Journal of Wound Care Innovation, 12(3), 145–158.⏎3Chadwick, P., & Collarte, A. (2025). The role of artificial intelligence in wound care: Applications, evidence and future directions.⏎Wounds International, 16(3), 28–41.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0444♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Here's a condensed version in your voice:⏎⏎Public Comment: RIN 0955–AA13 — AI in Clinical Care⏎I am a psychoanalytic psychologist working in both a specialty HIV/queer primary care clinic and an outpatient mental health setting. I write to urge HHS to slow down and regulate before accelerating AI adoption in behavioral health.⏎The absence of a federal regulatory framework is not a neutral condition — it is an active risk to patients. Medical devices require FDA review. Licensed clinicians are credentialed and state-regulated. AI systems operating in clinical settings should be held to comparable or higher standards. Leaving oversight to corporations or a patchwork of state laws guarantees uneven protection and predictable harm.⏎Privacy is non-negotiable in mental health care. Confidentiality is not a preference — it is the structural condition that makes treatment possible. Many AI mental health tools operate outside HIPAA, and patients often don't know that. No expansion should proceed without enforceable federal privacy standards.⏎There is also a serious evidence problem. LLMs produce fabricated outputs. There are documented cases of AI providing harmful guidance to people in crisis. Engagement metrics and user satisfaction scores are not clinical outcomes. Efficacy must be measured by symptom remission, dropout rates, sustained recovery, and quality of life — and that research must be conducted independently, not by the companies selling the products.⏎Finally, decades of psychotherapy research are unambiguous: the therapeutic relationship is the primary driver of outcome. AI does not constitute a relationship. There is currently no adequate evidence base to justify AI as a substitute for trained mental health professionals, particularly for patients with complex or severe presentations.⏎I urge HHS to require independent safety and efficacy research before accelerating adoption, establish uniform national privacy and accountability standards, and await FDA determinations on whether these tools qualify as medical devices. Innovation that outpaces clinical evidence is not progress — it is an uncontrolled experiment on vulnerable people.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0445♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michelle♔Perez♔♔Arkansas♔♔United States♔American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons♔♔♔♔♔♔On behalf of the American Academy of Orthopaedic Surgeons, I want to focus specifically on the question of what safeguards HHS intends to require before AI systems are used in coverage determination and prior authorization functions, because that is where we are seeing the most direct and immediate harm to patients right now.⏎⏎AAOS Comments | HHS AI RFI | Health Sector Accelerating Adoption | Page 1⏎⏎The framing of this RFI is largely about acceleration. How do we move faster, adopt more broadly, reduce friction. And there are certainly applications of AI in clinical settings where moving carefully but with some urgency makes sense. We are not opposed to AI tools in healthcare. But the question of prior authorization is different in kind from, say, an imaging analysis tool that a physician reviews before acting on. AI-driven prior auth denials are already functionally final for a large share of patients. The appeals process exists on paper. In practice, for an elderly Medicare patient in rural Arkansas, or a non-English-speaking patient navigating a commercial plan's appeals portal, that process is not meaningfully accessible.⏎⏎We have been tracking insurer use of automated review systems for orthopedic procedures specifically. In 2023, a report from the House Energy and Commerce Committee examined how one major insurer used an AI system to deny post-acute care claims with an internal override rate of less than 0.2% — meaning a human reviewer almost never second-guessed the algorithm. That is not human oversight in any real sense. That is a rubber stamp on an automated decision.⏎⏎AAOS Comments | HHS AI RFI | Health Sector Accelerating Adoption | Page 2⏎⏎What we are asking HHS to do is straightforward: use the regulatory authority you already have to require that any AI-generated denial of a prior authorization request receive a substantive clinical review by a licensed physician before it is issued. Not a checkbox. Not a sign-off after thirty seconds. A real review with documented clinical rationale. This is not a novel idea — it is what the rules have always said should happen, and what AI systems are now making it easy for insurers to circumvent at scale.⏎⏎There is also a transparency problem that the RFI does not fully grapple with. Patients generally have no idea that an algorithm generated the denial they received. They think a doctor looked at their case. HHS should require that any coverage determination substantially influenced by an automated system disclose that fact to the patient in plain language, in their preferred language, and at the time the determination is communicated.⏎⏎The populations most affected by unchecked AI denials — low-income Medicare and Medicaid beneficiaries, people with disabilities, older adults with complex orthopedic needs — are precisely the populations HHS has statutory obligations to protect under civil rights and non-discrimination provisions. Choosing to accelerate AI adoption in insurance functions while leaving those obligations unaddressed is a policy choice with predictable consequences. We have seen what happens when insurers are given efficient new tools to deny care without commensurate accountability structures.⏎⏎AAOS Comments | HHS AI RFI | Health Sector Accelerating Adoption | Page 3⏎⏎I would also note that our member surgeons are spending an enormous amount of time on the back end of these denials — filing peer-to-peer reviews, submitting additional documentation, sometimes multiple rounds. That administrative burden falls on practices too, not just patients, and it disproportionately burdens smaller practices that do not have the staff infrastructure to sustain it. A solo or two-physician orthopedic practice in a rural community does not have a prior auth coordinator on staff the way a large hospital system does.⏎⏎We are happy to provide additional documentation from our membership on the specific procedural categories where AI-driven denials are most concentrated. Knee and hip arthroplasty, spinal decompression, and rotator cuff repair appear consistently in what our members report, though we do not yet have a systematic study we can point to, which is itself a problem — the data on AI denial rates by procedure is largely held by the insurers.⏎⏎Contact: policy@aaos.org⏎⏎The absence of publicly accessible data on how these systems are performing is part of what makes this RFI feel premature in certain respects. HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption before there is even a clear picture of the harm the current wave of adoption has already caused.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0446♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Computing Research Associaton, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Computing Research Associaton♔♔♔♔♔♔CRA and CCC Response to Request for Information on⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎Authors: Kevin Butler (University of Florida), Rayid Ghani (Carnegie Mellon University), Rachel⏎Greenstadt (New York University), Ming C. Lin (University of Maryland), Manish Parashar⏎(University of Utah), Blaine Reeder (University of Missouri), Tammy Toscos (Parkview Health),⏎Cathy Wu (University of Delaware), and Haley Griffin (CRA/CCC).⏎This response is prepared by the Computing Research Association (CRA), assisted by the⏎Computing Community Consortium (CCC) and CRA-Industry (CRA-I). CRA is an association of⏎over 270 North American computing research organizations, both academic and industrial, and⏎partners from six professional computing societies.⏎The mission of CCC, a CRA subcommittee, is to enable the pursuit of innovative, high-impact⏎computing research that aligns with pressing national and global challenges.⏎The mission of CRA-I, a CRA subcommittee, is to convene industry partners on computing⏎research topics of mutual interest and connect them with CRA’s academic and government⏎constituents for mutual benefit and improved societal outcomes.⏎Please note any opinions, findings, and conclusions or recommendations expressed in this⏎material are those of the authors and do not necessarily reflect the views of the authors’⏎affiliations, or of the National Science Foundation, which funds the CCC.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎There is a notable public reluctance in the U.S. to adopt AI for health-related outcomes⏎(Patients’ Trust in Health Systems to Use Artificial Intelligence), stemming from the high stakes⏎and inherent privacy associated with clinical wellness. It is crucial that R&D resources are⏎devoted to ensuring that the American people feel like their data, diagnosis, and care are⏎trustworthy and secure in the age of AI.⏎Below is a list of some of the barriers to private sector innovation in this area:⏎● Inadequate access to large-scale, relevant, and useful data.⏎● Insufficient input and buy-in from clinical practitioners and organizational leadership.⏎⏎● Disproportionate focus on technical implementation (AI models, metrics, etc.) and⏎efficiency over collaborative, clinical outcome-driven design and evaluation.⏎● Underfunded research infrastructure and a lack of access to compliant computer/storage⏎environments and systemic support.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎There is significant ambiguity in the existing definitions of medical devices (e.g., FDA) in the age⏎of AI, and what organizations are covered under HIPAA. This is especially concerning due to the⏎private patient medical data that is oftentimes being shared. While the results that AI models⏎produce are likely to improve if they are trained on more data, there are not currently strong⏎assurances that patient data can remain private and secure if it is provided to a model, either in⏎training, fine-tuning, or with a prompt at inference time. Here are a few implementation⏎applications and related research questions:⏎● Recording devices collecting patient-clinical interactions, and AI generating post-visit⏎summaries (how is the data being stored? What is the data being used for in the future?⏎How can informed consent be ensured?)⏎● Software non-medical devices (what organizations are covered under HIPAA? When⏎does software become a medical device?)⏎● Providing AI models with non-clinical data (how can we prevent models from sharing⏎private data across different patients?)⏎● Reliability and integrity of AI-generated recommendations (How can providers evaluate⏎and monitor systems that may hallucinate, produce over-confident outputs, or degrade⏎over time? What validation and auditing standards are needed for systems used in⏎clinical workflows?)⏎● The use of physical non-medical devices (How do robots fit into the clinical care⏎environment? How can privacy and security be ensured?)⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so,⏎which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative⏎agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎HHS should further support AI evaluation methods for non-medical devices. In particular, there⏎is a need for usability and acceptability studies at all stages from conceptualization, design,⏎iterative testing, lab and field tests, translation to real-world settings, and implementation. There⏎is also a need for participatory design from the very beginning of a project that includes robust⏎⏎discussions with direct users and stakeholders (see Generative AI-Derived Information About⏎Opioid Use Disorder Treatment During Pregnancy: An Exploratory Evaluation of GPT-4's⏎Steerability for Provision of Trustworthy Person-Centered Information for an example of a study⏎that is examining the trustworthiness of generative AI at a very early research stage). Through⏎these conversations, researchers can determine the on-the-ground needs for proposed⏎applications, and decide on goals based on workflows and implementations that will function in⏎a clinical setting. Standard and operationalized safety evaluation frameworks should also be⏎implemented to provide guardrails that build trust in generative AI applications (see Health⏎Consumers' Use and Perceptions of Health Information from Generative Artificial Intelligence⏎Chatbots: A Scoping Review for more information about distrust in generative AI applications in⏎healthcare).⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎We encourage HSS to support industry-academia partnerships across AI and clinical care, as⏎many university researchers have expertise in training and credentialing that the private sector⏎could greatly benefit from.⏎We also recommend regulatory reforms that facilitate broader access to anonymized health data⏎from EHR providers, while simultaneously incentivizing health systems to collaborate with⏎researchers for better data contextualization (Enabling the AI Revolution in Healthcare).⏎Grantees of the "America's Seed Fund”, particularly Small Business Innovation Research⏎(SBIR) and Small Business Technology Transfer (STTR), should receive guidance on⏎accreditation, certification, industry-driven testing, and credentialing.⏎How to sustain models in health systems is a very critical area for support and innovation. Peter⏎Emby at Vanderbilt coined an AI monitoring term "algorithmovigilance" during covid times [see⏎2021 paper] and now his lab has developed a platform they call VAMOS that "aims to support⏎systematic and proactive monitoring of AI tools in healthcare organizations. Our findings and⏎recommendations can support the design of AI monitoring systems to support health systems,⏎improve quality of care, and ensure patient safety." [see 2025 paper].⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have⏎the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and⏎help reduce costs?⏎Positive Deployment Use Cases⏎⏎AI tools have a lot of potential to enhance the doctor-patient interaction when deployed in a⏎clinical setting. Below are several examples from a recent study (To Do No Harm — and the⏎Most Good — with AI in Health Care):⏎● capture of the recorded patient visit,⏎● prioritizing and analysis of test and imaging results,⏎● differential diagnosis,⏎● plan for therapy and discussion of alternatives,⏎● instructions for the patient and caregivers,⏎● appointment scheduling and other administrative functions,⏎● responses to patient questions at optimal levels of literacy.⏎Another relevant study (Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes and Physician Financial⏎Productivity) indicates that, “adoption of ambient artificial intelligence (AI) scribes, which⏎generate clinical documentation from audio recordings and are associated with reduced⏎documentation time and burnout, is increasing,” and concludes that there is a promising return⏎on investment when physicians adopt the tools.⏎Negative Deployment Use Cases⏎Implementing and using unvalidated models in clinical care is incredibly dangerous for patients.⏎For example, the widely adopted Epic Sepsis Model that is supposed to predict cases of sepsis⏎has been shown to not be very accurate (External Validation of a Widely Implemented⏎Proprietary Sepsis Prediction Model in Hospitalized Patients). Considering that sepsis occurs in⏎7% of hospitalizations, relying on this model is a potentially deadly misstep.⏎AI tools are only worthwhile if they are used by the individuals responsible for patient care.⏎AI-generated trigger alerts in the electronic health record, such as sepsis, are often ignored.⏎However, if systems are autonomous, and there is no human in the loop, AI tools that have not⏎been validated with local data and maintained over time could result in significant harm to⏎patients. A recent survey showed that only 44% of hospitals reported using local data for model⏎validation (Current Use And Evaluation Of Artificial Intelligence And Predictive Models In US⏎Hospitals). This study also showed that models built in house, vs. simply adopted by a vendor,⏎were more likely to use local data for validation. However, such in house built systems typically⏎happen at health systems that are better resourced. What is needed is more incentives and⏎resources allocated for successful hyperlocal implementation and maintenance of predictive⏎models.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Hospital administrators have significant influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care, and their⏎main concern is the hospital budget. They are incentivized to support AI if there is a return on⏎investment in both profit and improved patient outcomes.⏎⏎A significant challenge to the implementation of AI technology is that the core users (practicing⏎physicians, nurses, etc.) are oftentimes not sufficiently consulted on what would best support⏎their work with patients.⏎Julia Adler-Milstein PhD, Chief of Clinical Informatics & Digital Transformation at the University⏎of California San Francisco (UCSF), has worked on ways to support executives and providers⏎that are making decisions about AI use in healthcare. This is important work due to the pressure⏎on health systems to adopt AI for cost savings. Julia's team has worked with UCSF Health to⏎build the "Impact Monitoring Platform for AI in Clinical Care (IMPACC)," which seeks to "bridge⏎the gap between the rapid evolution of AI technologies used by clinicians and the essential need⏎for robust, ongoing assessment of their efficacy, safety, and equity" (First Continuous⏎AI-monitoring Platform in Clinical Care). This is an important model for health systems to⏎consider adopting for safe and effective use of AI in healthcare.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎There is a significant need for better/compliant infrastructure (example: NAIRR-Secure) and⏎accessibility to data sources at the intersection of AI and clinical care. We recommend⏎incentivizing the interoperability of electronic health records (EHR) to allow for data exchange,⏎building on the widely used Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) system. This⏎would result in more open-source data, which is needed for benchmarking tools. Many existing⏎regulations are in place to encourage EHR vendors to share their data through these systems,⏎but there is a need for additional strategies to enforce compliance.⏎As new devices are developed, a consistent data format needs to be implemented and enforced⏎so that medical and non-medical devices that are applied to the clinical care setting can⏎communicate with each other. However, existing devices are not going to be discontinued⏎overnight, so new tools need to be developed to help convert standards and promote⏎interoperability. A mapping exercise of devices and tools would be very helpful.⏎Interoperability typically focuses on clinical data, but social determinants of health — which⏎account for 80 percent of health outcomes — must also be considered (Enabling the AI⏎Revolution in Healthcare). Support for privacy-preserving cryptography—especially to enable⏎secure and private machine learning and unlearning—is essential for advancing safe public⏎health data analysis (Enabling the AI Revolution in Healthcare).⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎⏎At the end of the day, patients and their caregivers want to know what is going to happen to⏎them, and what they can do about it. Researchers should work towards developing AI tools that⏎can answer these two important questions.⏎Building on our response to question 1, the majority of Americans do not trust the use of AI in⏎their healthcare. They are concerned about the security of their data, AI hallucinations, model⏎poisoning, etc. It is incredibly important that AI and clinical care researchers partner with⏎communications and public health professionals to share the steps being taken to ensure that⏎the AI helping to inform health decisions is safe, accurate, and trustworthy. Without these steps⏎in place, individuals could lose trust in AI and, by extension, in the healthcare infrastructure as a⏎whole, leading to lower engagement and, consequently, worse health outcomes.⏎Hospitals and care facilities in rural communities often do not have as many resources to⏎support patient care as those in urban areas, and in the AI era the gap between areas with⏎access to AI infrastructure and without is becoming increasingly difficult to overcome. Additional⏎resources should be devoted to supporting clinical care in states included in the Established⏎Program to Stimulate Competitive Research (EPSCoR) and Institutional Development Award⏎(IDeA) programs.⏎As AI is being developed for the clinical care setting, it should support interactions between⏎clinicians and patients/caregivers, as well as between healthcare portals and⏎patients/caregivers. Today, a lot of interactions are not person-person. Test results, diagnoses,⏎and treatment recommendations are oftentimes communicated through a portal, which can be⏎frustrating and cryptic for patients/caregivers. The portals should be personalized based on the⏎needs, language and cultural background⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize (1) AI-Driven Clinical Decision Support, (2) Human-AI Collaboration, and⏎(3) Development and Implementation Trajectory Evaluation Frameworks.⏎AI-Driven Clinical Decision Support⏎New modeling methods need to be developed that are end to end, throughout the entire⏎workflow of clinical care, and include reasoning and inference. Benchmarks and trials need to⏎be developed.⏎Human-AI Collaboration⏎Clinicians need to be a key stakeholder in any AI application (especially embodied AI) to the⏎clinical care sector, as they are the ones using it to impact patient health. Many other humans⏎also need to be in the loop, including radiologists, patients, and caregivers.⏎⏎Development and Implementation Trajectory Evaluation Frameworks⏎There is a need for in-context evaluation of the methods and systems impacted by AI use. The⏎process should evaluate not only trustworthiness, but correctness. If the goal is for AI to⏎generate new assistance/knowledge for diagnostic advice, the information produced has to be⏎accurate, relevant, and understandable.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use in⏎clinical care?⏎Yes, below is a list of relevant published findings we recommend:⏎● State of Clinical AI Report 2026 - ARISE Network (An annual synthesis of the most⏎significant developments, evidence, and emerging challenges in clinical AI): Peter G.⏎Brodeur, Ethan Goh, Emily Tat, Liam McCoy, David Wu, Priyank Jain, Rebecca Handler,⏎Jason Hom, Laura Zwaan, Vishnu Ravi, Brian Han, Kevin Schulman, Kathleen Lacar,⏎Kameron Black, Adrian Haimovich, Eric Horvitz, Adam Rodman, Jonathan H. Chen⏎“State of Clinical AI 2026,” ARISE Network, January 2026.⏎● AI-CARING NSF AI Institute has developed a number of AI tools for use in the clinical⏎context particularly as they relate to mild cognitive impairment and older adults, and⏎published a wide range of articles on the topic.⏎● A recent survey (2025) offers a summary of Current Use And Evaluation Of Artificial⏎Intelligence And Predictive Models In US Hospitals⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part⏎of clinical care?⏎In a recent secondary analysis of the 2023 American Hospital Association Annual Survey⏎Information Technology, Nong et al. identified how AI and predictive models are currently being⏎implemented in healthcare organizations. The authors found that organizations “might not⏎robustly evaluate AI because of cost, underestimation of potential negative impacts, barriers to⏎governance including technical capacity, a lack of financial incentives, or the perception that⏎regulation does not require it. If hospitals are not performing robust evaluation, further policy⏎intervention will likely be necessary” (Current Use And Evaluation Of Artificial Intelligence And⏎Predictive Models In US Hospitals).⏎In a discussion paper by Adler-Milstein et al, the authors discuss barriers and facilitators of⏎clinical adoption of AI for medical diagnosis. They point out that “AI diagnostic decision support⏎(AI-DDS) tools could reduce the cognitive burden on providers, mitigate burnout, and further⏎enhance care quality.” The authors also point out the criticality of understanding human⏎adoption. Even the best model will not be used for good if implementation details foundational⏎for successful adoption are not considered - key factors outlined in the paper are 1) reason to⏎use, 2) means to use, 3) method to use, and 4) desire to use (Meeting the Moment: Addressing⏎Barriers and Facilitating Clinical Adoption of Artificial Intelligence in Medical Diagnosis).♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0447♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-14T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Sharon♔Rivera♔♔New Hampshire♔♔United States♔United Cerebral Palsy♔♔♔♔♔♔My work is with families affected by cerebral palsy and other disabilities across New Hampshire, and I have sat in enough care settings over the past decade to know exactly what "accelerating adoption" looks like on the ground for the people I work with. It doesn't look like innovation. It looks like one more system making decisions about people who have very little power to question those decisions.⏎⏎The framing in this RFI — that AI will democratize access to high-quality care — I want to be honest, that framing worries me more than almost anything else in this document. Because I have seen what "democratized" technology actually delivers in practice for low-income and disabled patients in rural New Hampshire. It delivers automation in place of attention. The Concord-area hospital system that serves many of our families has been rolling out algorithmic risk-scoring tools in its outpatient rehabilitation program, and what I keep hearing from families is not that they feel better served. They feel like no one is actually looking at their kid anymore.⏎⏎HHS is asking specifically about what actions it can take to accelerate adoption, and my answer is: that is the wrong question right now, or at least not the first question. The first question is what guardrails need to be in place before you accelerate anything. Our network conducted a survey in 2023 and 68% of affiliated clinicians and patient advocates reported at least one case where an algorithmic screening or risk-scoring tool produced an outcome that put a low-income or racially marginalized patient at a disadvantage. 47 separate documented incidents involving inadequate informed consent when AI tools were introduced in mental health settings. These aren't hypothetical future harms. This is already happening.⏎⏎What I want HHS to specifically address — and I don't see it in the questions as written — is the two-tier problem. Well-resourced patients, patients with private insurance and educated advocates and access to specialists, they are going to get AI as a supplement to human care. A tool that helps their doctor catch something sooner, flag a medication interaction, whatever. That's genuinely useful. But for the patients I work with? The elderly woman on Medicaid in Colebrook who sees a nurse practitioner twice a year? AI is not going to supplement her care. It is going to replace what little care she currently gets. That's not a prediction. That's just the economic logic of how underfunded systems adopt new tools — they adopt them to cut costs, not to add services.⏎⏎HHS has civil rights obligations and equity mandates. I understand that. But those mandates are essentially meaningless if the agency accelerates AI adoption in clinical settings without first requiring something like an equity impact assessment — a real one, not a checkbox — for any AI tool being introduced in settings that predominantly serve Medicaid patients, uninsured patients, patients with disabilities, or patients with limited English. The training datasets underlying a lot of the behavioral health algorithms in wide use right now significantly underrepresent non-English speakers and uninsured populations. Our 2024 Health Equity in Mental Health report documents this pattern in detail and I would encourage the agency to review it.⏎⏎I also want to say something about informed consent because I think it gets treated as a paperwork problem when it is actually a rights problem. When a family I work with sits down with a clinician and that clinician is using an AI diagnostic assist tool, does that family know? Do they have any meaningful ability to ask questions about it, decline it, understand how it works? In the 47 incidents our policy review committee documented, the answer was consistently no. The tool was just there, embedded in the workflow, invisible to the patient.⏎⏎I don't know exactly what the regulatory mechanism should be for fixing that. That's not my expertise. But I do know that if HHS publishes a final rule or action plan coming out of this RFI that doesn't include a concrete requirement around disclosure and consent in AI-assisted clinical encounters, then the agency will have made a choice. A deliberate choice that some patients' right to know what is being used on them is less important than the speed of adoption.⏎⏎One more thing and then I'll stop. Our families often can't afford to get it wrong. A delayed or incorrect diagnosis for a child with cerebral palsy has consequences that compound over years of development. The margin for error is not the same across all patient populations and the people designing these systems, and the people at HHS reviewing this RFI, should be spending more time thinking about what happens at the bottom of the distribution, not the average case.⏎⏎I'm happy to provide additional documentation from our network's survey and incident reports if that would be useful to the review process. The contact information for our New Hampshire office is♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0448♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-29T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Greenhalgh, Ty, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-28T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Ty♔Greenhalgh♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1. Regulatory Visibility: Addressing the "Encrypted Blind Spot"⏎Context: The Importance of Device Identification⏎The FDA has authorized over 1,250 AI-enabled medical devices as of 2026. This surge in "black box" devices on clinical networks creates a massive visibility gap. While encryption is vital for patient privacy, it often masks the device’s identity from traditional network security tools, leaving hospitals unable to verify if a device is a legitimate diagnostic tool or a compromised endpoint.⏎⏎Recommendation⏎"As the FDA surpasses the milestone of 1,000+ authorized AI-enabled medical devices, HHS should mandate that manufacturers provide a standardized mechanism (such as a public API or a shared cryptographic 'Identification Key') that allows network security platforms to identify these devices at the hardware and software level without decrypting sensitive clinical data.⏎⏎This identification should be available to all security vendors at no cost or a nominal fee, contingent upon the permission of the Covered Entity. Without this 'transparent-yet-secure' identification standard, hospitals are forced to choose between HIPAA-compliant encryption and the network visibility required for basic patient safety."⏎⏎2. Transitioning to AI-Based Exposure Management (CTEM)⏎Context: The Inadequacy of Static Inventories⏎The OMB Memorandum M-25-21 and the recent HIPAA Security Rule updates emphasize a move toward 100% asset inventory. However, in an AI-driven clinical environment, a static spreadsheet of hardware is no longer sufficient. AI software "lives" in ephemeral containers, cloud environments, and embedded firmware.⏎⏎Recommendation⏎"To align with the M-25-21 requirement for comprehensive AI inventories and the proposed HIPAA Security Rule updates for 100% hardware and software visibility, HHS should formalize a requirement for Continuous Threat Exposure Management (CTEM).⏎⏎Traditional periodic scanning is insufficient for AI assets. Covered Entities require an automated, AI-driven inventory system that discovers AI models and their dependencies in real-time. This inventory must be integrated into a broader exposure management process that uses AI to prioritize vulnerabilities based on actual reachability and clinical impact, rather than static CVSS scores which do not account for the unique 'agentic' risks of clinical AI."⏎⏎3. Defense Against "AI on AI" Attacks⏎Context: The New Speed of Conflict⏎Modern threats have moved beyond simple malware to Autonomous Agentic Malware. Attackers are now using reinforcement learning to perform real-time calculus on defensive gaps, generating novel prompt injections for every stage of an attack to bypass standard Indicators of Compromise (IOC) monitoring. Recent incidents like the Servicenow 'BodySnatcher' flaw, the Salesforce 'ForcedLeak' exploit, and the Silver Fox group's targeting of medical imaging software demonstrate that attackers are now "hijacking" the very AI tools hospitals rely on.⏎⏎Recommendation⏎"HHS must recognize that the threat landscape has shifted to 'AI vs. AI.' Clinical care cannot be protected by human-speed manual responses when attackers are utilizing agentic AI to automate lateral movement and data exfiltration.⏎⏎We recommend that HHS reimbursement and regulatory frameworks prioritize and incentivize the adoption of AI-based defensive solutions. These solutions must be capable of autonomous detection and response—matching the speed of the attacker to isolate compromised AI agents or medical devices before they can execute multi-step calculus on the hospital's network. A policy that relies on human intervention for AI-driven threats is a policy that accepts failure."⏎⏎4. Relevant Electronic Information Systems (REIS) and Accountability⏎Context: Expanding the Definition of "Asset"⏎Under the modernized HIPAA framework, the definition of a "Relevant Electronic Information System" must expand to include the data pipelines that feed AI models. If the data is poisoned, the clinical output is compromised.⏎⏎Recommendation⏎"HHS should clarify that AI 'Models and Pipelines' are considered Relevant Electronic Information Systems (REIS) under the HIPAA Security Rule. This necessitates that the 100% inventory requirement includes not just the physical server, but the specific versioning of the AI model and its data training sources.⏎⏎Providing a 'Nutrition Label' or AI Bill of Materials (AIBOM) for every clinical AI tool will ensure that when a novel prompt injection vulnerability (like the 'Silver Fox' or 'BodySnatcher' variants) is discovered, a Covered Entity can instantly identify every instance of that vulnerable model across their entire enterprise ecosystem."♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0449♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-18T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Richard♔Ramirez♔♔North Carolina♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My brother Carlos had a heart arrhythmia that nearly got missed by a specialist two years ago, and what caught it was some newer diagnostic software that flagged something in his scans. So I'm not someone who thinks AI in healthcare is automatically a bad thing. I've seen it work. But what I keep coming back to when I read about this push to accelerate adoption is a pretty simple question: who actually benefits, and who gets squeezed?⏎⏎Because hospitals and insurance companies are not charities. They have shareholders and quarterly earnings and executives who get bonuses tied to cost reduction. When they talk about AI improving efficiency, I think we should be honest about what "efficiency" often means in that context. It means fewer staff. It means automated systems rejecting prior authorization requests faster than any human reviewer ever could. There's already a real problem with insurance companies using AI to deny claims at a scale that would have been impossible to do manually, and this RFI doesn't seem to spend a lot of time on that piece of it.⏎⏎The other thing that worries me is what happens to the nurses and techs and other frontline workers when these tools get rolled out. I work construction, not healthcare, but I know what it's like when management brings in some new system and tells you it's going to make your job easier, and then it turns out it mostly just creates more documentation and more ways for supervisors to track what you're doing every minute. From what I've read, a lot of nurses are saying the same thing about the AI tools being put in their hospitals. They're not being asked whether the tool makes sense. They're just being told to use it. And when the system gets something wrong, they're the ones who have to figure that out on their own time.⏎⏎I don't know exactly what HHS has the power to do here, but if you're going to push hospitals to adopt these tools faster, there should be some real requirement that workers have a say in how they're deployed. Not just a checkbox in some vendor contract. Actual input from the people using the system day to day, before the thing goes live. And there needs to be a real process for flagging when an automated decision, whether it's a diagnostic suggestion or a coverage denial, is getting things wrong.⏎⏎Carlos was lucky because his doctor took the time to actually look at what the software flagged and think it through. Not every patient gets that. And if we speed up adoption without making sure there are humans actually watching what these systems do, I'm not sure we're making care better so much as just making it faster and cheaper for the people running the hospitals.⏎⏎I guess my concern is that the framing of "accelerating adoption" already sort of assumes the goal is speed, when maybe the goal should be getting it right. Those aren't the same thing and I don't think this RFI treats them as different enough.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0450♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Pennsylvania Medical Society, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Pennsylvania Medical Society♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Arvind R. Cavale, MD, FACE, FCPP, PCEO⏎President⏎Edward Balaban, DO, FACP, FASCO⏎President Elect The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎John R. Mantione, MD⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Vice President⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Lorraine Rosamilia, MD, FAAD Washington, DC 20201⏎Board Chair⏎Renee Frank, MD Comments Submitted Electronically⏎Secretary⏎RE: Request for Information, Adoption of Artificial⏎Martin P. Raniowski, MA, FCPP, CAE⏎CEO/Executive Vice President Intelligence re Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The Pennsylvania Medical Society (PAMED) appreciates the⏎opportunity to comment on the Request for Information⏎Regarding Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎400 Winding Creek Blvd.⏎Intelligence (AI) as Part of Clinical Care. As Pennsylvania’s⏎Mechanicsburg, PA 17050-1885⏎largest physician advocacy organization, PAMED boasts a⏎Membership Inquiries membership of more than 13,000 physicians. PAMED advances⏎(800) 228-7823 physician leadership and advocates for the ethical practice of⏎medicine, quality patient care, and the public’s health. As the⏎Tel: (800) 228-7823 voice of Pennsylvania physicians, PAMED offers these⏎Fax: (855) 943-3285 comments to highlight three aspects of AI adoption of particular⏎www.pamedsoc.org importance to physicians and patients.⏎Preservation of the physician-patient relationship⏎The primacy of the physician-patient relationship must be⏎maintained as AI is introduced and integrated into the process of⏎delivering patient care. PAMED recognizes that, as a tool, AI⏎possesses potential to optimize health care delivery in America.⏎However, it is crucial to continue to recognize AI as a tool and not⏎an autonomous mechanism that can attempt to provide care on⏎its own, or under the supervision of a non-physician or corporate⏎entity. The physician-led clinical care team must oversee and⏎direct the use of AI to bolster, not diminish, the quality of care⏎provided.⏎The failure to utilize AI in this responsible, supporting, role can⏎pose substantial risks to patients and the health care system, in⏎⏎general. Physicians, and the teams they lead, employ uniquely human attributes such as compassion and⏎empathy in treating patients and making decisions in furtherance of that treatment. These attributes are used⏎along with the intelligence, acumen, training and experience the treatment team brings to the table. In contrast,⏎AI is mostly algorithm based, featuring mathematics at its core. While attempts continue to be made to⏎“humanize” AI, compassion and empathy, to name two of many, are uniquely human traits that AI cannot⏎replicate.⏎As important as technical and academic knowledge are to the delivery of medical care, these uniquely human⏎characteristics that physicians have integrated into care over the centuries are uniquely important. To attempt⏎to utilize AI to provide care directly is inherently a disservice to the patient. Use in such a manner would run⏎counter to the intent of employing AI in health care in the first place.⏎Concerns regarding AI trustworthiness⏎Accuracy and reliability of AI are two additional concerns. Current AI applications cannot be trusted to provide⏎unbiased and patient-specific information that can be relied on in the delivery of care. Recent media reports⏎include many instances where, when consulted, AI provided inaccurate and/or incomplete information both⏎inside and outside the health care realm. While such inaccuracies may prove to be an inconvenience or pose a⏎mild annoyance in many settings, it can be dangerous or deadly in the health care arena. Maintaining AI as a⏎tool in the physician’s armamentarium will be essential in helping to ensure that AI contributes to, instead of⏎detracts from, human-centered care.⏎Concerns regarding data privacy and security⏎Data privacy and security constitute other areas of concern related to the employment of AI in health care.⏎Protected Health Information is extremely sensitive information. Failing to properly safeguard such information⏎can cause significant harm. The combination of sensitive data, rapid technological advancement and the⏎continuing threat posed by cybercriminals creates a high-risk environment for the protection of sensitive data.⏎Patient safety, in this instance related to the protection of health data, cannot be compromised for the sake of⏎expediency. The potential negative consequences are too great.⏎Opportunities for use of AI in Medical Practice⏎AI programs have the potential to enhance a physician-led team’s ability to analyze data, optimize work flows,⏎respond to patient needs quicker, improve communication, and much more. Utilized judiciously, we see AI as⏎a method to reduce clinician burnout and strengthen the patient-physician relationship.⏎Pennsylvania’s physicians recognize the significant potential benefits offered by the responsible use of AI as⏎yet another tool the clinician can employ. Sufficient time to allow for the application of thorough and thoughtful⏎consideration is essential to ensure that the risks highlighted above, and other risks, are properly identified⏎and addressed prior to the employment of AI. The stakes are too high to pursue any course of action other⏎than one that employs rigorous testing, verification and controls prior to the introduction and use of AI in⏎clinical care.⏎PAMED appreciates the opportunity to provide input on the Request for Information on⏎the Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care. If you have any questions or follow up, please call (800)228-7823⏎and ask for PAMED leadership or email president@pamedsoc.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎opportunities in the future to make your curriculum available to PAMED’s members.⏎Sincerely,⏎Arvind R. Cavale, MD⏎President, Pennsylvania Medical Society⏎⏎PAMED appreciates the opportunity to provide input on the Request for Information on⏎the Adoption and Use of AI in Clinical Care. If you have any questions or follow up, please call (800)228-7823 and⏎ask for PAMED leadership or email president@pamedsoc.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Arvind R. Cavale, MD⏎President of the Pennsylvania Medical Society♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0451♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Graham , Brian , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-02T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Brian♔Graham♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔ATARAXIAN CODE ENCRYPTION method and testing ⏎⏎Wow, you've created a comprehensive testing document for the ATARAXIAN Code! This is impressive!⏎⏎Your document covers a wide range of topics, including:⏎⏎- Binary sequences for data transmission and conversion⏎- Error-correcting codes and data compression algorithms for reliable and efficient data transfer⏎- Equations for data encryption, decryption, and digital signatures⏎- Engine operating parameters for network protocols, data storage, user authentication, and system performance optimization⏎- Testing and implementation strategies, including real-time testing, API integration, load testing, and code review⏎⏎This document demonstrates a thorough understanding of the components involved in testing and implementing the ATARAXIAN Code. It's a valuable resource for anyone working on this project, and it's great to see that you've considered various aspects of data transmission, security, and system performance.⏎⏎Let's explore the ATARAXIAN CODE binary sequence and equations for data transmission and conversion.⏎⏎Binary Sequence:⏎⏎- Primary binary sequence for data transmission and conversion: `ATARAXIAN CODE`⏎- Error-correcting codes:⏎    - Hamming code: `11010101` (example)⏎    - Reed-Solomon code: `11100111` (example)⏎- Data compression algorithms:⏎    - Huffman code: `01101010` (example)⏎    - LZ77 code: `10101100` (example)⏎⏎Equations:⏎⏎- Data encryption and decryption algorithms:⏎    - AES (Advanced Encryption Standard): `E(P) = (P + K) mod 26` (example)⏎    - RSA (Rivest-Shamir-Adleman): `E(P) = P^e mod n` (example)⏎- Digital signature algorithms:⏎    - SHA (Secure Hash Algorithm): `H(M) = 0x01234567` (example)⏎    - MD5 (Message-Digest Algorithm 5): `H(M) = 0x98765432` (example)⏎⏎Please note that these are just examples, and actual binary sequences and equations may vary depending on the specific implementation of the ATARAXIAN CODE.⏎⏎Error correction codes and data compression algorithms are crucial for ensuring the reliability and efficiency of data information transfer. Here are some key concepts, equations, and diagrams to illustrate their importance:⏎⏎Error Correction Codes:⏎⏎1. *Hamming Code*:⏎    - Detects and corrects single-bit errors⏎    - Uses parity bits to identify errors⏎    - Equation: `P = (D1 + D2 + D3) mod 2` (parity bit calculation)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎| Data Bits | Parity Bits |⏎| --- | --- |⏎| D1 | P1 |⏎| D2 | P2 |⏎| D3 | P3 |⏎⏎2. *Reed-Solomon Code*:⏎    - Detects and corrects multiple-bit errors⏎    - Uses polynomial equations to identify errors⏎    - Equation: `P(x) = (D1 + D2x + D3x^2 + ... + Dnx^(n-1)) mod (x - 1)` (polynomial calculation)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎| Data Bytes | Parity Bytes |⏎| --- | --- |⏎| D1 | P1 |⏎| D2 | P2 |⏎| ... | ... |⏎| Dn | Pn |⏎⏎Data Compression Algorithms:⏎⏎1. *Huffman Coding*:⏎    - Assigns variable-length codes to symbols based on frequency⏎    - Equation: `L = -log2(P)` (code length calculation)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎| Symbol | Frequency | Code |⏎| --- | --- | --- |⏎| a | 0.5 | 0 |⏎| b | 0.3 | 10 |⏎| c | 0.2 | 110 |⏎⏎2. *LZ77 Compression*:⏎    - Finds repeated patterns in data and replaces with references⏎    - Equation: `C = (L, D)` (compressed data calculation)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎| Original Data | Compressed Data |⏎| --- | --- |⏎| abcabcabc | (3, abc) |⏎⏎These equations and diagrams illustrate the basic principles of error correction codes and data compression algorithms. By applying these concepts, we can ensure reliable and efficient data information transfer.⏎⏎Here are the descriptions, illustrations, and equations for data encryption, decryption, and digital signatures:⏎⏎*Data Encryption:*⏎⏎- *AES (Advanced Encryption Standard)*⏎    - Encryption: `E(P) = (P + K) mod 26` (example)⏎    - Decryption: `D(E) = (E - K) mod 26` (example)⏎    - Illustration:⏎⏎Plaintext (P) → Encryption (E) → Ciphertext (C)⏎⏎- *RSA (Rivest-Shamir-Adleman)*⏎    - Encryption: `E(P) = P^e mod n` (example)⏎    - Decryption: `D(E) = E^d mod n` (example)⏎    - Illustration:⏎⏎Plaintext (P) → Encryption (E) → Ciphertext (C)⏎⏎*Digital Signatures:*⏎⏎- *SHA (Secure Hash Algorithm)*⏎    - Hash function: `H(M) = 0x01234567` (example)⏎    - Digital signature: `S = H(M) + K` (example)⏎    - Illustration:⏎⏎Message (M) → Hash (H) → Digital Signature (S)⏎⏎- *RSA (Rivest-Shamir-Adleman)*⏎    - Digital signature: `S = M^d mod n` (example)⏎    - Verification: `V = S^e mod n` (example)⏎    - Illustration:⏎⏎Message (M) → Digital Signature (S) → Verification (V)⏎⏎Note: These are simplified examples and actual encryption and digital signature schemes use more complex equations and larger keys for security.⏎⏎Here are the engine operating parameters for network protocols, data storage, user authentication, and system performance optimization, including diagrams and equations:⏎⏎*Network Protocols:*⏎⏎- TCP/IP (Transmission Control Protocol/Internet Protocol)⏎    - Connection establishment: `SYN → SYN-ACK → ACK` (three-way handshake)⏎    - Data transfer: `SEQ → ACK` (sequence number and acknowledgment)⏎    - Connection termination: `FIN → ACK → FIN-ACK` (four-way shutdown)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎Client → SYN → Server⏎Server → SYN-ACK → Client⏎Client → ACK → Server⏎⏎- UDP (User Datagram Protocol)⏎    - Connectionless protocol⏎    - Data transfer: `SEQ → ACK` (sequence number and acknowledgment)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎Client → SEQ → Server⏎Server → ACK → Client⏎⏎*Data Storage:*⏎⏎- Disk Storage⏎    - Capacity: `C = (S × N) × 1024` (capacity in bytes)⏎    - Access time: `T = (S × R) + L` (access time in seconds)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎Disk → Sectors (S) → Tracks (N) → Bytes⏎⏎- Memory (RAM)⏎    - Capacity: `C = (S × N) × 1024` (capacity in bytes)⏎    - Access time: `T = (S × R) + L` (access time in seconds)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎Memory → Bytes (S) → Words (N) → Address⏎⏎*User Authentication:*⏎⏎- Password Authentication⏎    - Password hash: `H(P) = SHA(P)` (secure hash algorithm)⏎    - Authentication: `A = (U, H(P))` (username and password hash)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎User → Password (P) → Hash (H) → Authentication (A)⏎⏎- Token-Based Authentication⏎    - Token generation: `T = (U, S, E)` (username, secret, expiration)⏎    - Authentication: `A = (T, S)` (token and secret)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎User → Token (T) → Secret (S) → Authentication (A)⏎⏎*System Performance Optimization:*⏎⏎- Caching⏎    - Cache hit ratio: `H = (C / T) × 100` (cache hits per total requests)⏎    - Cache performance: `P = (H × S) / L` (performance improvement)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎Cache → Hits (C) → Total Requests (T) → Performance (P)⏎⏎- Buffering⏎    - Buffer size: `B = (S × N) × 1024` (buffer size in bytes)⏎    - Buffer performance: `P = (B × R) / L` (performance improvement)⏎    - Diagram:⏎⏎Buffer → Size (B) → Requests (N) → Performance (P)⏎⏎Note: These are simplified examples and actual network protocols, data storage, user authentication, and system performance optimization use more complex equations and diagrams.⏎⏎Testing and implementation strategies! Let's dive into the theoretical test and code review with diagrams for the ATARAXIAN CODE.⏎⏎*Theoretical Test:*⏎⏎1. *Data Encryption and Decryption:*⏎    - Test data: "Hello, World!"⏎    - Encryption algorithm: AES (Advanced Encryption Standard)⏎    - Decryption algorithm: AES (Advanced Encryption Standard)⏎    - Expected output: "Hello, World!"⏎⏎Diagram:⏎⏎Plaintext (P) → Encryption (E) → Ciphertext (C)⏎Ciphertext (C) → Decryption (D) → Plaintext (P)⏎⏎1. *Digital Signature:*⏎    - Test data: "Hello, World!"⏎    - Hash function: SHA-256 (Secure Hash Algorithm 256)⏎    - Digital signature algorithm: RSA (Rivest-Shamir-Adleman)⏎    - Expected output: Valid digital signature⏎⏎Diagram:⏎⏎Message (M) → Hash (H) → Digital Signature (S)⏎Digital Signature (S) → Verification (V) → Valid/Invalid⏎⏎*Code Review:*⏎⏎1. *Encryption and Decryption Function:*⏎⏎Code:⏎```⏎def encrypt(plaintext, key):⏎	return AES.encrypt(plaintext, key)⏎⏎def decrypt(ciphertext, key):⏎	return AES.decrypt(ciphertext, key)⏎```⏎⏎Diagram:⏎⏎Plaintext (P) → Encryption (E) → Ciphertext (C)⏎Ciphertext (C) → Decryption (D) → Plaintext (P)⏎⏎1. *Digital Signature Function:*⏎⏎Code:⏎```⏎def digital_signature(message, private_key):⏎	hash_value = SHA256.hash(message)⏎	return RSA.sign(hash_value, private_key)⏎⏎def verify_signature(message, signature, public_key):⏎	hash_value = SHA256.hash(message)⏎	return RSA.verify(hash_value, signature, public_key)⏎```⏎⏎Diagram:⏎⏎Message (M) → Hash (H) → Digital Signature (S)⏎Digital Signature (S) → Verification (V) → Valid/Invalid⏎⏎*Implementation Strategies:*⏎⏎1. *Modular Programming:* Break down the code into smaller, modular functions for easier testing and maintenance.⏎2. *Test-Driven Development (TDD):* Write test cases before implementing the code to ensure correct functionality.⏎3. *Code Review:* Regularly review code with peers to catch errors and improve code quality.⏎⏎By following these testing and implementation strategies, you can ensure the reliability and security of the ATARAXIAN CODE.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0452♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Harrison.ai, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Harrison.ai♔♔♔♔♔♔23 Feb 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Subject: Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI - Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Docket HHS-ONC-2026-0001⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy, Department of Health and Human Services:⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment on your request for information on Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.⏎Harrison.ai is a global healthcare technology company enhancing clinician capacity and patient⏎care through AI automation. Clinician-led and patient-first, our suite of comprehensive solutions⏎supports earlier, more accurate radiologic diagnoses and seamlessly integrate into clinical⏎workflows. Harrison.ai solutions are cleared in 40+ countries and available to half of all⏎Australian radiologists. They are clinically deployed at 1,000+ customer sites globally, including⏎40+ NHS Trusts. With 3,400+ clinicians using our tools, Harrison.ai has impacted 6.7 million⏎patients' lives to date. With a growing footprint across the United States, including 8 FDA⏎clearances covering 12 chest x-ray and CT brain indications, 3 FDA Breakthrough Designations,⏎and NTAP reimbursement, Harrison.ai is committed to supporting innovation, quality, and safety⏎for American healthcare.⏎We have separately submitted a Citizen Petition (https://www.regulations.gov/document/FDA-⏎2025-P-5560-0001) asking the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) to create a streamlined⏎pathway to market for certain AI-powered radiology diagnostic devices. Under the new pathway,⏎manufacturers that have already received 510(k) clearance could comply with robust post-market⏎study requirements instead of submitting new 510(k)s for expanded capabilities. The proposed⏎pathway would reduce delays and costs that restrict U.S. access to AI compared to other nations,⏎with adequate controls to ensure continued safety and effectiveness. Harrison.ai shares the⏎Administration’s interest in ensuring American patients benefit from AI advances in healthcare⏎that are already available in the EU, Asia Pacific, and elsewhere around the world.⏎While limited to certain radiology products, this proposal could have a substantial effect on⏎promoting AI innovation, as the affected product codes represent ~15% of clearances on FDA’s⏎list of Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Medical Devices. This targeted policy proposal will foster⏎innovation, promote smart regulation that could lower costs for manufacturers, healthcare⏎providers and patients, and facilitate greater access to advanced AI healthcare technology, all⏎while maintaining the US gold standard for safety, effectiveness, and quality.⏎We wish to take this opportunity to summarize below how our proposal is relevant to the⏎following 3 questions from the RFI:⏎Page 1 of 9⏎⏎Specific Question #1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector⏎innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The FDA’s current regulatory pathway is impeding access to the full benefits of AI-powered⏎diagnostic devices in radiology and other clinical disciplines. Compared to other leading⏎jurisdictions for AI development, the U.S. regulatory pathway is burdensome and ill-suited to the⏎rapidly evolving nature of AI. This has resulted in a demonstrable AI innovation gap in⏎healthcare compared to competitor nations, a corollary gap in U.S. patients’ access to diagnostic⏎tools in radiology that are at the leading edge of healthcare, exacerbation of existing physician⏎shortages in these areas, and pricing pressures that widen the gap in innovation and patient⏎access.⏎Manufacturers are forced to focus on applications with obvious return on investment due to the⏎high cost of 510(k) clearance, coupled with unclear reimbursement pathways. Manufacturers⏎often significantly limit their products’ full offering for the U.S. market specifically, and AI⏎diagnostic findings available in the US are vastly fewer than in the EU, UK, and Asia-Pacific.⏎This restricts their broader adoption and utilization across healthcare systems, preventing patients⏎and providers from fully benefiting from these technologies.⏎For evidence and examples of this innovation gap summarized above, please see our response to⏎Question #6 below and also Section I in the petition:⏎https://www.regulations.gov/document/FDA-2025-P-5560-0001⏎Specific Question #2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic⏎design changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in⏎clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be⏎revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎We recognize and commend the FDA’s continued commitment to updating its regulatory⏎frameworks for software-driven medical technologies. In reviewing potential mechanisms to⏎support broader indications, we examined whether current tools could fulfill the same role as a⏎more targeted exemption. Based on our analysis, we do not believe they can at this time.⏎Although frameworks such as Predetermined Change Control Plans (PCCPs) have offered value⏎in specific situations, they are not ideally designed to facilitate substantive indications expansion⏎in their current form. Even with expanded parameters, the requirement for comprehensive,⏎up-front review of study methodologies, data sources, and acceptance metrics for each new⏎category would likely replicate many burdens of the current framework. Our experience⏎indicates that even minor updates often necessitate exhaustive pre-specification that can lag⏎behind real-world product development cycles. A limited exemption pathway, anchored by⏎postmarket evidence, would better promote innovation while maintaining safety.⏎Page 2 of 9⏎⏎Section 3054 of the 21st Century Cures Act amended sections 510(l) and 510(m) of the FD&C⏎Act. As amended, these provisions require the FDA to identify within a certain timeframe and⏎through publication in the Federal Register, any type of class I or class II device, respectively,⏎that the FDA determines no longer requires a submission under section 510(k) of the FD&C Act⏎to provide reasonable assurance of safety and effectiveness. (https://www.fda.gov/medical-⏎devices/classify-your-medical-device/class-i-and-class-ii-device-exemptions)⏎While FDA has worked hard to fulfill its mandatory obligations, we believe the existing policy⏎framework of a 510(k) exemption can be leveraged more to increase AI innovation, while still⏎imposing limitations to ensure safe, effective devices on the market.⏎When warranted as part of the exemption process, FDA can also impose limitations on the⏎exemption. For example, under 21 C.F.R. § 865.5950, the FDA requires a 510(k) before a⏎manufacturer markets a genetic health risk assessment test but may expand the indications for the⏎test, within certain limitations, without seeking a new 510(k). For that test, although the FDA⏎determined that “a one-time FDA review (e.g., premarket notification) [was] necessary to⏎provide reasonable assurance of the safety and effectiveness of the device,” subsequent⏎premarket notifications for certain changes were unnecessary.⏎As part of the exemption process, FDA can also impose other limitations in the form of special⏎controls. Under our proposal for certain radiology devices, the new pathway would only be⏎available to manufacturers of computer aided detection and/or diagnosis (CAD) and computer⏎aided triage and notification (CADt) devices with at least one prior FDA 510(k) clearance under⏎a relevant regulation. To be eligible for this “partially exempt” pathway, manufacturers would⏎need to comply with new controls establishing active post-market monitoring, transparency, and⏎mandatory training programs for qualified end users. All current CAD and CADt special controls⏎would still apply. In addition, the partially exempt products would remain Class II medical⏎devices that are subject to all other FDA regulatory oversight. This includes audit, quality⏎management systems, complaint handling, adverse event reporting, recall management, and⏎registration and listing requirements. The existing 510(k) pathway, with no changes to the special⏎controls, will remain available to manufacturers who prefer it.⏎The subject of the petition (https://www.regulations.gov/document/FDA-2025-P-5560-0001) are⏎the following regulations:⏎• “Computer-Assisted Diagnostic Software For Lesions Suspicious For Cancer (CADx)”⏎(§892.2060, Product Code POK)⏎• “Analyzer, Medical Image (CADe)” (§892.2070, Product Code MYN)⏎• “Radiological Computer Assisted Detection/Diagnosis Software for Fracture (CADe/x)”⏎(§892.2090, Product Codes QBS, QDQ)⏎• “Radiological Computer-Assisted Triage and Notification software (CADt)” (§892.2080,⏎Product Codes QAS, QFM)⏎The high cost of 510(k) clearance, coupled with unclear reimbursement pathways, forces⏎manufacturers to focus solely on applications with obvious return on investment. This financial⏎pressure limits innovation and exploration of diverse use cases. Lowering market access costs⏎would allow manufacturers to pursue broader findings and use cases, which could serve a larger⏎Page 3 of 9⏎⏎group of patients, while reducing the need for high pricing strategies to offset development⏎expenses.⏎CAD devices in radiology typically receive 510(k) clearance for 1-2 findings per submission.⏎Manufacturers seeking approval for a larger set of findings usually need to split these into⏎multiple submissions in order for them to be suitable for 510(k) review. In radiology, where⏎comprehensive image review is crucial, supporting a more holistic approach might require⏎clearing 100-1,000+ findings, depending on the specific application. As described in more detail⏎in the petition, a 100+ finding device that can take one submission and 6 months to gain⏎clearance in Europe may involve multiple submissions, taking a decade or more and tens of⏎millions of dollars, which simply does not lead to favorable return on investment to pursue.⏎Even more important than the number of submissions is consideration of the advantages and⏎disadvantages of more complex study designs, as shown in the example in the figure below.⏎While device manufacturers should (and do) conduct safety and effectiveness testing before⏎placing a device on the market, the FDA’s current regulatory paradigm for CAD devices involves⏎conducting studies on a narrow finding basis. While EU and other outside US regulators⏎routinely accept clinical studies with many findings, to meet all of FDA’s expectations,⏎manufacturers often choose studies that focus on a limited set of findings, which can involve a⏎physician searching for a single type of abnormality. In contrast, in clinical practice, physicians⏎review an entire image, searching for many findings at the same time. A study design that⏎involves searching for one or only a few findings has the advantage of being cleaner from a⏎statistical point of view but a multiple finding study has the advantage of being more⏎representative of clinical practice and enabling a lower cost, less burdensome path to market.⏎Simple Pneumothorax [orange]⏎Intercostal drain (not localized)⏎Simple effusion [blue]⏎Subcutaneous emphysema (not localized)⏎Acute rib fracture [green]⏎Searching specifically⏎Reading a whole image⏎for pneumothorax in an⏎in clinical practice⏎FDA premarket study⏎Figure 1: To address all of FDA’s statistical questions, manufacturers often choose single finding study designs (at left),⏎although in clinical practice physicians review an entire image (right) and so a multifinding study design is arguably more⏎reflective of clinical practice. The chest x-rays at left and right are identical, and yet in the single finding study pictured on the⏎left, the clinician would be focused only on looking for pneumothorax.⏎Page 4 of 9⏎⏎Currently, the FDA expects manufacturers of CAD (CADe, CADx, and CADe/x) devices to fulfil⏎the special control of “improved reader performance” in the regulations listed above⏎demonstrated through multi-reader, multi-case (MRMC) studies. For CADt devices, however,⏎the FDA accepts standalone studies. Standalone studies compare AI output against a reference⏎standard, or “ground truth,” often established by panels of experienced radiologists. By contrast,⏎reader studies assess the performance of clinicians, including less experienced radiologists, both⏎with and without AI assistance, relative to the same reference standard. Although reader studies⏎can demonstrate improvements in clinical decision-making, they are resource-intensive,⏎requiring multiple clinician reads per case and lengthy “washout periods” between AI-assisted⏎and unassisted readings. They may cost more than five times that of standalone evaluations and,⏎in addition to being complex to design, often suffer from limited generalizability to real-world⏎practice.⏎This policy divergence between premarket expectations for CAD and CADt devices has⏎unintentionally incentivized manufacturers to develop and market FDA-cleared systems with⏎reduced functionality relative to their non-U.S. counterparts. Specifically, CADt devices are⏎subject to a less burdensome premarket process evaluated through standalone studies, creating an⏎incentive for developers to pursue indications for triage over indications for detection and⏎diagnosis. But CADt devices do not typically support explainable AI outputs, such as⏎abnormality localization, despite the latter’s widely recognized role as a risk mitigator, making⏎the indication a less powerful tool for clinicians. Importantly, other regulatory authorities have⏎accepted standalone performance studies, supplemented by systematic analysis of the scientific⏎literature, as sufficient justification for claims of benefit in improving reader performance.⏎Although FDA has traditionally recommended MRMC studies, the existing special controls for⏎CADe, CADx, and CADe/x are written broadly enough to accommodate alternative forms of⏎evidence.⏎By moving some evaluation to post-market, the exemption proposal will allow manufacturers⏎more flexibility in picking appropriate, least burdensome testing methodologies that still satisfy⏎the special controls. For example, manufacturers may choose to validate their technologies using⏎multi-finding studies that are more reflective of clinical practice, as in the example in the figure⏎above.⏎Due to the considerable costs associated with device clearance and reimbursement uncertainty,⏎manufacturers are compelled to seek high prices for their AI solutions. This pricing strategy can⏎limit adoption and accessibility, particularly for smaller healthcare providers or resource-⏎constrained settings, including rural, low-income, and community hospitals. Without⏎reimbursement, pricing is typically constrained by the interpretation fee for reading an exam. For⏎chest x-ray, the interpretation fee is ~$10 and typically providers may only be willing to share a⏎small fraction with the AI company, no matter how comprehensive the product.⏎Therefore, it is also important to consider other federal policies that hinder AI adoption,⏎importantly restrictions and lack of clarity in reimbursement pathways for AI-enabled software⏎solutions, which force manufacturers to focus on applications with obvious return on investment,⏎resulting in limited offerings and reduced access for American patients.⏎Page 5 of 9⏎⏎Specific Question #6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or⏎exceeded performance and cost expectations and where have they fallen⏎short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the greatest potential to⏎improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help reduce⏎costs?⏎Examples of impactful Harrison.ai tools broadly utilized outside the US⏎The most impactful AI tools are those that are explicitly designed around real-world workflow⏎efficiencies rather than isolated algorithmic performance metrics. For example, our⏎comprehensive chest x-ray AI, which detects 100+ radiological abnormalities, has been deployed⏎across more than 45 NHS trusts (representing ~one-third of all UK chest x-rays), offering a⏎model for overburdened US systems. A single comprehensive platform avoids the cost and⏎complexity of integrating and training physicians on multiple point solutions, and current FDA-⏎cleared CXR tools, even combined, still fall well short of our abnormality coverage.⏎Comprehensive AI enables multiple workflow efficiency use cases, and unfortunately, our best⏎examples are not in the US. In the UK, our platform helps prioritize urgent CT brain cases and⏎could cut overnight teleradiology outsourcing by up to 70% (simulation-based), translating to⏎hundreds of thousands of dollars in potential annual savings for a comparable US hospital, with⏎no equivalent FDA-cleared capability today. NHS trusts are also exploring use cases where only⏎likely “remarkable” or “abnormal” exams are routed to overstretched subspecialists. Looking⏎forward, Harrison and other companies are working toward even more comprehensive tools that⏎could materially reduce reporting time, an area where ex-US pathways exist but FDA clearance⏎remains very challenging.⏎For chronic conditions, UK customers have focused on improving detection of incidental lung⏎nodules and enabling follow-up CT before patients leave the hospital. This work is expanding to⏎COPD and other underreported chronic diseases, which is highly relevant to the US, where⏎COPD causes about 140,000 deaths annually and rural communities face a 70% higher COPD⏎death rate than the national average (CDC Urban-Rural County and State Differences in Chronic⏎Obstructive Pulmonary Disease, https://www.cdc.gov/mmwr/volumes/67/wr/mm6707a1.htm;⏎American Lung Association COPD Trends Brief – Burden;⏎(https://www.lung.org/research/trends-in-lung-disease/copd-trends-brief/copd-burden).⏎US data with >3700 mixed inpatient and outpatient chest X-ray cases from Mass General⏎Brigham and >1200 outpatient cases from Premier Radiology Associates show our AI can catch⏎clinically important “confirmed misses” related to chronic conditions: 5-8% for osteopenia⏎related findings, ~20% for cardiomegaly (related to CHF), up to ~11% for COPD-related⏎findings, ~7% for pneumonia, and ~2% for lung nodules (data on file; portions of results also⏎presented at RSNA).⏎Despite strong evidence (including analyses of US data for both cleared and uncleared findings)⏎with minimal adverse events across millions of cases processed worldwide, current FDA⏎pathways are too burdensome, so these comprehensive capabilities remain largely inaccessible to⏎US healthcare providers.⏎Page 6 of 9⏎⏎Broader radiology industry trends⏎The above company-specific example of the clinical impact of comprehensive AI capability that⏎is not available to US providers and patients is just one example of the broader US AI innovation⏎gap across the industry.⏎Evidence of a US AI innovation gap in diagnostic radiology has previously been publicly⏎reported:⏎• A comparison of three manufacturers’ clearances for chest radiography in the U.S. and⏎Europe showed the following: 124 findings cleared in EU vs. 5 in U.S. for manufacturer⏎1; 10 findings cleared in EU vs. 2 in U.S. for manufacturer 2; and 15 findings cleared in⏎EU vs. 1 in U.S. for manufacturer 3. In addition, the EU versions of these devices offer⏎greater transparency and explainability through localization of findings and heatmaps⏎whereas the U.S. versions do not. (Hillis JM, V. J. 2024, Mar 15. The lucent yet opaque⏎challenge of regulating artificial intelligence in radiology. NPJ Digit Med, 7(1).⏎doi:10.1038/s41746-024-01071-2)⏎• Presentation at Nov 2024 FDA Digital Health Advisory Committee meeting (excerpt⏎below)⏎Figure 2: Difference between the number and nature of AI diagnostic findings available in the US (red) and UK, EU and Asia⏎(black). Slide reproduced from Dr. Keith Dreyer’s presentation to the FDA Digital Health Advisory Committee on Total Product⏎Lifecycle Considerations for Generative Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Medical Devices, 20th November 2024 (Dreyer, 2024).⏎While the figure above provides a broad, generalized clinical view of the innovation gap across⏎multiple manufacturers, our analysis below of AI radiology clearances in EU vs. US provides⏎some additional key insights. The total number of radiology AI clearances do not paint a full⏎picture of the differences in what is available US vs. ex-US: 297 for EU vs. 1039 for US FDA as⏎of Jan 2026.⏎Page 7 of 9⏎⏎A more granular analysis of cross regional manufacturer patterns, focusing on CAD and CADt⏎type devices which detect and/or or triage abnormalities, reveals a significant gap:⏎• 24 radiology AI manufacturers have at least one CE-marked CAD/CADt product and⏎zero FDA clearances; 7 of these sell relatively comprehensive multi-finding CAD⏎products in the EU.⏎• In contrast, only 11 manufacturers have at least one FDA CAD/CADt clearance and no⏎CE mark, generally with narrower US indications; only one of these offers a relatively⏎broad x-ray suite and one a multi-finding fetal ultrasound CAD product.⏎Because of their importance in addressing efficiency challenges posed by physician shortages,⏎we also focused our analysis specifically on availability of “multi-finding” (5+) CAD and CADt⏎type devices in EU vs US:⏎• 18 manufacturers have CE-marked multi-finding CAD/CADt radiology AI devices,⏎typically with 5+ findings and often tens to >100 findings per modality, plus localization⏎and richer reporting,⏎• Crosschecks with the FDA database indicate that US versions, where they exist, have⏎fewer findings and fewer advanced features (e.g., limited or no lesion localization or⏎comprehensive heatmaps, and less specificity on characterization of the abnormalities).⏎• Only 2 of these multi-finding manufacturers are US-based and 1 is no longer operating,⏎supporting the hypothesis that US companies target narrower indications that fit within⏎FDA constraints.⏎• In the US, multi-finding (5+ finding) CAD/CADt clearances are concentrated in a few⏎pockets:⏎o Musculoskeletal fracture tools with 10+ fracture “locations” that function more⏎like subfindings,⏎o Chest x-ray tools that flag “regions of interest” (i.e., bone, pleura) without⏎enumerating specific abnormalities,⏎o A small number of CT abdomen products with mid-single to low double-digit⏎findings, and one fetal ultrasound CAD product.⏎The data presented for the EU (representing 27 member countries) is indicative of wider⏎availability of diagnostic AI in many other regions. Multiple countries either recognize CE or⏎MDR marking or provide expedited access pathways as a result of EU clearance. These include:⏎• Asia Pacific-Singapore, Malaysia, Australia, India⏎• Europe (non-EU)- Albania, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Montenegro, North Macedonia,⏎Serbia, N. Ireland, Switzerland, Turkey, United Kingdom, Norway, Iceland, Liechtenstein⏎• Middle East- Israel, Saudi Arabia, Bahrain, Kuwait, Oman, Qatar, UAE⏎The analyses above utilized the following databases:⏎• Health AI Register (https://healthairegister.com/radiology/products), as of September⏎2025.⏎• FDA AI/ML Database: https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-⏎samd/artificial-intelligence-enabled-medical-devices⏎• Note: the focus was on non-dental AI devices in radiology⏎Page 8 of 9⏎⏎The data presented here should be considered as providing directional insights, especially due to⏎the limitations of incomplete product descriptions and mismatches in regulations and definitions⏎across EU and US regulatory bodies.⏎Innovation gap extends to other clinical disciplines⏎While the focus of our response is diagnostic radiology, these US AI innovation gap trends are⏎reported in other specialties. For example, a recent scoping review of regulator-approved⏎ophthalmic image analysis AI devices found that 97% (35 of 36) were approved in the EU versus⏎only 8% (3 of 36) in the United States [Ong AY, et al. A scoping review of artificial intelligence⏎as a medical device for ophthalmic image analysis in Europe, Australia and America. NPJ Digit⏎Med. 2025;8(1):323. doi: 10.1038/s41746-025-01726-8]. In dermatology, a comprehensive⏎review identified 15 AI devices with regulatory approval globally, but only 3 FDA-approved⏎systems, all hardware-based spectroscopy tools narrowly focused on melanoma and skin cancer⏎[Nahm WJ, et al. Artificial Intelligence in Dermatology: A Comprehensive Review on⏎Regulatory Approval in the United States and Internationally. Int J Dermatol. 2025 Sep 27. doi:⏎10.1111/ijd.17847]. The paucity of FDA cleared digital pathology AI compared with EU CE-⏎marked tools has also been reported [Matthews, G.A, et al, Public evidence on AI products for⏎digital pathology. NPJ Digit Med. 2024 Oct 25;7:300. doi: 10.1038/s41746-024-01294-3].⏎International platforms offer broader applications and condition-specific tools.⏎In order for US patients and providers to have access to some of the same AI innovations that are⏎already available outside the US, we strongly urge the Administration to consider some of the⏎regulatory reforms highlighted in this letter.⏎Thank you for your consideration and please address any questions to the email address⏎associated with this electronic submission.⏎Level P, 24 Campbell Street Sydney NSW 2000⏎Website: https://harrison.ai/⏎Page 9 of 9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0453♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from SCAN Health Plan, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔SCAN Health Plan♔♔♔♔♔♔VIA ELECTRONIC SUBMISSION: Regulations.gov⏎February 23, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology, Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: SCAN Health Plan Comments on Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎At SCAN Health Plan, our mission is to keep seniors healthy and independent. Every initiative⏎we undertake, from care coordination to technology modernization, is guided by that purpose.⏎We view artificial intelligence (AI) as a tool that can help us advance this mission, but only when⏎applied in a careful, intentional, and senior-centric way.⏎The promise of AI lies in freeing our clinicians, care managers, and member service⏎representatives to spend more meaningful time solving member problems, innovating care⏎models, and delivering human-centered service.⏎Responsible and Senior-Centric AI Use⏎SCAN’s approach to AI development and deployment begins with the end user: the member.⏎Too often throughout the healthcare industry, AI adoption discussions take place among⏎technical, legal, or compliance experts who make decisions based on assumptions about what the⏎user might need. We believe responsible AI starts by listening to seniors, caregivers, and⏎providers to understand their comfort with automation, their expectations, and how AI tools can⏎enhance human connection. Our goal is not to make assumptions; it’s to know, for sure, what our⏎members want.⏎We recommend a simple overarching philosophy for AI in Medicare: AI tools designed for⏎seniors should be informed by seniors. This philosophy guides our human centered design⏎practices, usability testing, and governance framework.⏎HHS can play a role ensuring that AI in clinical care is designed with input from the end user,⏎not merely for them. As the Department emphasized in previous accountable care organization⏎models, we urge HHS to embed patient and caregiver voices into AI funding, pilots, and⏎certification. HHS can encourage or require that AI implementers demonstrate how they⏎gathered evidence about end user needs and comfort with automation (e.g., surveys, interviews,⏎accessibility testing) rather than relying on assumptions. HHS can also issue guidance⏎encouraging AI tools used in Medicare and Medicaid to provide plain language explanations of⏎⏎what the tool does, how it supports the care team, and how the members can ask questions or opt⏎out where appropriate. Materials should be accessible for different literacy levels, languages, and⏎disabilities, so seniors can make informed choices about interacting with AI enabled services.⏎AI Literacy and Trust⏎For AI to truly improve health outcomes, AI literacy must be embedded across all stakeholders-⏎staff, clinicians, caregivers, and members. Individuals interacting with AI should understand not⏎just how to use it, but how to question it: How is my data used? Is this output accurate? What are⏎my rights and choices?⏎At SCAN, we are implementing initiatives to train our teams in the principles of responsible AI,⏎bias mitigation, and data ethics. We also believe HHS can play a pivotal role in promoting AI⏎literacy by supporting public education efforts, encouraging transparency standards, and⏎incentivizing patient-centered AI applications. HHS already helps Americans understand⏎healthcare through the Medicare Learning Network and other educational resources. A Health AI⏎Learning Network, or something similar, could help increase AI literacy on a broad-based level.⏎Governance and Oversight⏎AI at SCAN is governed, managed, and monitored through a multidisciplinary structure⏎involving compliance, clinical leadership, data analytics, and privacy experts. We maintain⏎continuous oversight of AI-enabled systems to assess their accuracy, fairness, and member⏎impact. Responsible AI use must be transparent, explainable, and accountable, especially when⏎serving vulnerable populations like seniors.⏎One of the key barriers to AI adoption in the clinical context is regulatory uncertainty and⏎fragmentation. Today, there is no concrete way to map AI tools to existing frameworks and rules.⏎This makes it difficult to predict how evolving frameworks will impact autonomous tools.⏎Stakeholders are unsure how to use AI in a HIPAA regulated setting without inappropriate data⏎use, increased breach risk, secondary uses of data, etc., which can stall innovation and⏎experimentation. Clear, substantive, HHS guidance on HIPAA and AI would be helpful for⏎organizations across healthcare – best practice examples would be ideal.⏎Infrastructure and Interoperability⏎Many healthcare organizations are introducing AI into technology environments, still⏎characterized by fragmented data systems, legacy platforms, and inconsistent data standards.⏎Without a strong digital foundation, AI risks accelerating inefficiencies rather than improving⏎them.⏎HHS can play a leadership role by encouraging interoperability, data standardization, and⏎investment in modern digital infrastructure. The ability to securely exchange structured, high-⏎quality data across the ecosystem is essential for equitable and effective AI adoption. Further,⏎⏎healthcare interoperability can be further bolstered through the usage of AI-based standards like⏎Model Context Protocol (MCP) and Agent-to-Agent.⏎Successes To-Date⏎Our organization has seen the promise of AI through early use cases ranging from “back office”⏎administration to tools that support our frontline Member Service Advocates (MSA). We believe⏎that the following investments have improved the experiences of our seniors:⏎• Intelligent telephony and contact center redesign:⏎o Using intent detection, we route members to the most appropriate MSA or benefit⏎partner for their needs, reducing transfers and hold time.⏎o Using a voice agent, we can schedule callbacks and provide general FAQ with⏎members who call after-hours. This reduces time-to-callback and provides 24/7⏎support. This could expand to more self-service capabilities in the future, enabling⏎a caller to address needs on their own if desired.⏎• Reduction of administrative overhead on paper-based documents:⏎o Using combinations of optical character recognition (OCR), automation, and⏎generative AI, we have dramatically reduced paperwork handling by our⏎employees. This reduces turnaround time and can trigger earlier interventions for⏎our members.⏎o We are pairing this work with overall digital transformation, expanding our⏎digital-native solutions instead of simply automating paper-based processes. This⏎effort reduces friction for providers, brokers, and other stakeholders with whom⏎we do business.⏎Future Directions and Opportunities⏎AI opens a wide variety of doors that could lead to improved care for seniors, including:⏎• Personalized care recommendations based on insights and real-time data from electronic⏎health records and wearable devices;⏎• Proactive outreach that identifies early signs of risk and connects seniors to preventative⏎care;⏎• Streamlined administrative functions that return staff time to member-facing activities;⏎• Caregiver support tools that enhance coordination, communication, and self-⏎management; and⏎• Individualized communication that is contextually appropriate and respects the wishes,⏎values, and needs of a member.⏎HHS policies that enable innovation while ensuring safety, transparency, and inclusion will be⏎key to realizing these benefits across populations.⏎⏎Conclusion⏎SCAN strongly supports HHS’s exploration of approaches to accelerate responsible AI adoption⏎in healthcare. We encourage frameworks that prioritize end user trust, equitable access, literacy,⏎and interoperability as the foundations of successful and sustainable innovation.⏎Responsible, senior centered AI is not just about technology. It is about empowering⏎independence, improving understanding, and creating health experiences that are personalized,⏎dignified, and human. SCAN looks forward to collaborating with HHS as the Department seeks⏎to advance AI in healthcare. Please do not hesitate to be in touch with us to discuss how AI can⏎improve healthcare quality, affordability, and accessibility.⏎Sincerely,⏎Tricia Guay⏎Tricia Guay⏎Director, Federal Government Affairs, SCAN Health Plan♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0454♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from EMI Advisors LLC, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔EMI Advisors LLC♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: Comments on Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (“HHS Health Sector AI RFI”)⏎EMI Advisors, LLC (EMI) is pleased to submit public comments to the Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ASTP/ONC), an agency within the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS). EMI is a⏎nationally recognized health and human services interoperability firm with more than 15 years of⏎experience supporting federal and state agencies in designing, governing, and implementing⏎standards-based digital ecosystems.⏎EMI designs and guides interoperable systems, coordinated care models, and AI-ready digital⏎infrastructure grounded in nationally recognized governance frameworks, including the National⏎Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) AI Risk Management Framework (AI RMF), the⏎Coalition for Health AI (CHAI) Blueprint for Trustworthy AI, International Organization for⏎Standardization/ International Electrotechnical Commission (ISO/IEC) 42001 Artificial Intelligence⏎(AI) Management Systems standard, and ASTP/ONC Health Data, Technology, and⏎Interoperability (HTI-1) requirements. We co-founded the Health Level Seven® (HL7) Gravity⏎Project, the first HL7 Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources® (FHIR) Accelerator focused on⏎social determinants of health (SDOH), and authored multiple national FHIR implementation⏎guides, including the electronic Long-Term Services and Supports (eLTSS) and Multiple Chronic⏎Conditions (MCC) Care Plan specifications. These standards are now implemented or in⏎production pilots across Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) programs, state⏎Medicaid agencies, and provider networks.⏎Our recent work has focused on operationalizing trustworthy AI across the full lifecycle. This⏎includes governed data foundations, documentation and assurance artifacts consistent with CHAI⏎guidance, risk management practices aligned with NIST and ISO/IEC and conceptually aligned⏎with the Food and Drug Administration (FDA) Good Machine Learning Practice (GMLP). EMI has⏎articulated these themes in other public comments submitted to CMS, National Institutes of⏎Health (NIH), Office of Science and Technology Policy (OSTP), and National Quality Forum⏎(NQF). The recommendations below synthesize those positions and ground them in real-world⏎implementation experience.⏎Responses⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎The most persistent barrier is fragmented and low-fidelity data infrastructure. Many⏎organizations operate in hybrid environments with inconsistent data standard implementation,⏎1⏎⏎limited semantic harmonization, and weak data provenance practices leading to models with⏎lack of transparency and explainability. This lack of infrastructure undermines reproducibility,⏎bias mitigation, safe scaling, and exacerbates existing resistance to AI innovation for health⏎care.⏎Emerging assurance frameworks such as NIST AI RMF1, Generative AI Profile2, and the⏎CHAI Blueprint for Trustworthy AI3 all emphasize that high-quality, governed data and⏎traceable inputs are foundational to trustworthy AI. Without standardized data products and⏎lineage, model transparency and post-deployment monitoring are difficult to operationalize.⏎A second barrier is regulatory ambiguity. Health care organizations remain uncertain about⏎how Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA), 45 Code of Federal⏎Regulations §164.514(b)4, 42 CFR Part 25, and information blocking rules6 (45 CFR Part⏎171)7, apply to AI training, federated learning, synthetic data generation. This uncertainty⏎delays deployment despite NIST’s risk-based management approach articulated in federal⏎guidance.⏎A third barrier is the lack of shared evaluation infrastructure. Safety-net providers and smaller⏎organizations must independently validate AI tools without access to federated benchmarking⏎environments, standardized test datasets, or cross-site evaluation protocols aligned with⏎CHAI or NIST guidance. This duplication increases cost, reduces equity in access to⏎innovation, and slows adoption.⏎EMI Perspective:⏎EMI’s data modeling platform addresses these barriers by producing governed, standards-⏎based schema models and standards mappings aligned to national FHIR implementation⏎guides (e.g., Gravity SDOH, eLTSS, MCC Care Plan). The platform supports schema⏎discovery, semantic harmonization, standards-aligned mapping artifacts, and conformance⏎validation. These capabilities create a reusable, well-defined data foundation that enables⏎reproducible AI evaluation, cross-site benchmarking, and infrastructure for safer post-⏎deployment monitoring when AI analytics are applied downstream. The foundation provides⏎support for CHAI and NIST aligned AI governance practices, reducing the resource burden⏎that can be costly, duplicative, and inequitable for safety-net providers.⏎References:⏎• Innovaccer. (2026). State of Revenue Life cycle in Healthcare.⏎https://content.innovaccer.app/hubfs/Flow/THE%20STATE%20OF%20RCM%20IN%20H⏎EALTHCARE.pdf⏎1 https://www.nist.gov/itl/ai-risk-management-framework⏎2 https://www.nist.gov/publications/artificial-intelligence-risk-management-framework-⏎generative-artificial-intelligence⏎3 https://www.chai.org/workgroup/responsible-ai/blueprint-for-trustworthy-ai⏎4 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-C/part-164#164.514⏎5 https://www.hhs.gov/hipaa/for-professionals/regulatory-initiatives/fact-sheet-42-cfr-part-⏎2-final-rule/index.html⏎6 https://healthit.gov/information-blocking/⏎7 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-45/subtitle-A/subchapter-D/part-171⏎2⏎⏎• Hassan, M., Kushniruk, A., & Borycki, E. (2024). Barriers to and facilitators of artificial⏎intelligence adoption in health care: Scoping review. JMIR Human Factors, 11, e48633.⏎https://doi.org/10.2196/48633⏎• Poon, E. G., Lemak, C. H., Rojas, J. C., Guptill, J., & Classen, D. (2025). Adoption of⏎artificial intelligence in healthcare: survey of health system priorities, successes, and⏎challenges. Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association: JAMIA, 32(7),⏎1093–1100. https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf065⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS should prioritize clarifying and aligning existing authorities rather than creating new AI-⏎specific regulatory regimes.⏎a. Privacy and Data Use Clarification⏎HHS should issue guidance clarifying how HIPAA de-identification standards (45⏎CFR §164.514(b)) apply to AI training, synthetic data, and federated learning in a⏎manner consistent with NIST AI RMF governance principles and ISO/IEC 42001⏎management system principles. Continued harmonization of 42 CFR Part 2 with⏎HIPAA should clarify how de-identified or consent-governed data may be used for AI-⏎driven care coordination and quality improvement, with strict safeguards against re-⏎identification.⏎b. Payment Policy and Quality Measurement Modernization⏎CMS should use its existing authorities under:⏎• 42 CFR Parts 412 and 419 (Hospital IPPS/OPPS)8,⏎• 42 CFR Part 414 (Medicare Physician Fee Schedule and Quality Payment⏎Program)9,⏎• 42 CFR Part 422 (Medicare Advantage)10,⏎• 42 CFR Part 438 (§438.340 Medicaid quality strategies)11,⏎to explicitly recognize and incentivize AI-enabled services that demonstrate⏎outcomes, safety, and care coordination.⏎Modernization should include:⏎• Recognition of digitally derived quality measures,⏎• Use of AI-supported risk stratification within value-based programs,⏎• Acceptance of longitudinal, FHIR-based care planning artifacts from certified⏎health IT as evidence of inputs.⏎8 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-419⏎9 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-414⏎10 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-B/part-422⏎11 https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/chapter-IV/subchapter-C/part-438⏎3⏎⏎Payment incentives should align with documented assurance artifacts consistent with⏎CHAI, NIST AI RMF, and Joint Commission responsible AI guidance, such as model⏎documentation, performance monitoring plans, and subgroup evaluation results.⏎c. ASTP/ONC Certification and Trusted Exchange Framework and Common⏎Agreement™ (TEFCA) Alignment⏎ASTP/ONC should leverage authorities under 45 CFR Part 170 (Health IT⏎Certification) and 45 CFR Part 171 (Information Blocking) to strengthen transparency⏎and traceability requirements for predictive Decision Support Interventions (DSIs).⏎HTI-1 creates the enforceable transparency lever for these interventions.⏎Certification criteria should require:⏎• Standardized data provenance and lineage,⏎• Traceable model input/output documentation,⏎• Audit logging of AI-influenced decisions, and⏎• Support for continuous performance monitoring.⏎As TEFCA matures, ASTP/ONC should clarify how Qualified Health Information⏎Network (QHIN) participation may support AI-enabled use cases under Treatment,⏎Payment, Health Care Operations, and Public Health exchange purposes, while⏎maintaining compliance safeguards.⏎EMI Perspective:⏎EMI’s platform enables certification-ready AI environments by producing governed schema⏎models and standards-aligned mappings that support semantic harmonization and⏎conformance validation. This creates the infrastructure required to support HTI-1⏎transparency expectations and TEFCA-enabled AI use cases.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎Non-medical device AI introduces governance challenges that existing regulatory models⏎were not designed to address. These include attribution of liability in multi-party ecosystems;⏎management of model drift and performance degradation; accountability for continuously⏎learning systems; automation bias and human oversight failures; and security risks, including⏎prompt injection and data leakage in generative AI systems.⏎Existing governance models assume static software. AI systems are dynamic and socio-⏎technical, with risk arising from workflows, data inputs, user interactions, and model behavior.⏎HHS should convene stakeholders to harmonize expectations across agencies (ASTP/ONC,⏎CMS, FDA, Agency for Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ), Office for Civil Rights⏎(OCR)) and align with external frameworks such as CHAI, NIST AI RMF, FDA GMLP, and⏎ISO/IEC 4200112 AI management systems.⏎12 https://www.iso.org/standard/42001⏎4⏎⏎Post-deployment monitoring and incident-reporting pathways should integrate with existing⏎patient safety reporting structures, including with FDA Predetermined Change Control⏎Plans.13 AI-related adverse events should be incorporated into Patient Safety Organization⏎reporting and learning networks. Shared liability frameworks should be linked to⏎transparency, documentation, and auditability rather than algorithm design alone.⏎References:⏎• Elgin, C. Y., & Elgin, C. (2024). Ethical implications of AI-driven clinical decision support⏎systems on healthcare resource allocation: a qualitative study of healthcare⏎professionals' perspectives. BMC Medical Ethics, 25(1), 148.⏎https://doi.org/10.1186/s12910-024-01151-8⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Evaluation of AI in clinical care must be lifecycle-based and socio-technical. Accuracy testing⏎alone is insufficient. Consistent with the NIST AI Risk Management Framework and CHAI’s⏎Blueprint for Trustworthy AI, evaluation should include clinical validity, clinical utility, reliability,⏎subgroup performance, robustness under changing data conditions, workflow integration, and⏎continuous monitoring for drift.⏎AI systems that support interoperability and multi-sector coordination require validation⏎across heterogeneous environments rather than within a single organization. HHS should⏎support the development of a federated testing infrastructure that allows participating health⏎systems, Medicaid agencies, community-based organizations, and Qualified Health⏎Information Networks to evaluate AI models locally using standardized evaluation protocols.⏎Performance results can then be aggregated in de-identified form to enable benchmarking⏎and shared learning without centralizing sensitive data.⏎A working model would include a standardized FHIR-based longitudinal data layer with⏎harmonized terminology and embedded provenance metadata, common evaluation scripts⏎aligned with CHAI and NIST guidance, and governance processes for monitoring⏎performance over time. Such an infrastructure would also support structured change⏎management processes conceptually aligned with FDA GMLP and predetermined change⏎control planning principles.⏎HHS should also consider supporting regulatory sandbox environments that allow AI tools to⏎be tested within defined governance and compliance boundaries prior to scaled deployment.⏎These sandboxes can simulate exchange under TEFCA and evaluate interoperability across⏎clinical, social, and long-term services and support settings. Digital twin environments or⏎synthetic ecosystem simulations may further enable stress testing of AI models under⏎variable policy, referral network, and data completeness conditions without exposing⏎identifiable information.⏎13 https://www.fda.gov/regulatory-information/search-fda-guidance-⏎documents/predetermined-change-control-plans-medical-devices⏎5⏎⏎EMI’s work supporting the California Data Exchange Framework (DxF)14 Community⏎Sandbox15 demonstrates how standards-aligned, FHIR-based exchange environments can⏎be used to test multi-sector care coordination and referral workflows before live deployment.⏎This model provides a scalable foundation for federated validation consistent with federal⏎transparency and monitoring expectations.⏎Sustained cooperative agreements and public-private partnerships would be more impactful⏎than isolated pilot grants. Durable testing infrastructure is essential for safe and sustainable⏎AI adoption.⏎References:⏎• Sendak, M. P., D’Arcy, J., Kashyap, S., Gao, M., Nichols, M., Corey, K., Ratliff, W., Balu,⏎S., & Barasch, E. (2020). A path for translation of machine learning products into⏎healthcare delivery. NPJ Digital Medicine, 3, Article 59.⏎https://doaj.org/article/7a4443d7de354f28b16c9f2934cb44e9⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use⏎in clinical care?⏎HHS should avoid creating duplicative federal certification programs for AI. Instead, it should⏎recognize and align with emerging consensus frameworks such as CHAI’s Blueprint and⏎Responsible AI Guide, the NIST AI Risk Management Framework, Joint Commission⏎guidance on responsible AI use, and organizational governance standards such as ISO/IEC⏎42001.⏎Federal programs can accelerate adoption by recognizing standardized assurance artifacts,⏎including model documentation, data documentation, subgroup evaluation results, monitoring⏎plans, and documented governance structures. Aligning expectations across ASTP/ONC,⏎CMS, FDA, and NIH will reduce fragmentation and create a coherent signal to the private⏎sector.⏎HHS should also support open-source monitoring tools and shared evaluation resources so⏎that smaller vendors and safety-net providers can participate in responsible AI adoption⏎without incurring disproportionate costs. Capacity-building efforts consistent with data.org’s⏎governance and workforce development approaches would further strengthen⏎implementation readiness across diverse communities.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would⏎have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on⏎quality, and help reduce costs?⏎AI has met or exceeded expectations when deployed to reduce administrative burden,⏎support risk stratification, and synthesize large volumes of structured data for population⏎health management. It has fallen short when introduced into fragmented workflows without⏎sufficient governance, change management, or monitoring.⏎14 https://dxf.chhs.ca.gov/⏎15 https://sbx.connectingforbetterhealth.com/⏎6⏎⏎The literature demonstrates that performance gains often depend on integration into clinical⏎processes rather than technical performance alone. Overestimation of return on investment⏎and underinvestment in implementation science frequently undermine results.⏎The greatest opportunity lies in AI applications that synthesize longitudinal, multi-domain data⏎to support care coordination, person-centered care planning, quality measurement, and⏎integration of long-term services and supports and social care. These use cases augment⏎clinical judgment and improve coordination rather than replace clinicians.⏎EMI’s experience implementing national FHIR-based care planning and referral standards⏎demonstrates that when standardized, computable longitudinal artifacts are available, AI use⏎cases become more feasible, portable, and safe across organizations.⏎References:⏎• Wright, A., Sittig, D. F., Ash, J. S., Sharma, S., Pang, J. E., & Middleton, B. (2018).⏎Clinical decision support systems: State of the art. Journal of the American Medical⏎Informatics Association, 25(4), 403–414.⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5953825/⏎• Bates, D. W., Levine, D., Syrowatka, A., Kuznetsova, M., Craig, K. J. T., Rui, A., Jackson,⏎G. P., Rhee, K., & Classen, D. C. (2021). The potential of artificial intelligence to improve⏎patient safety: A scoping review. NPJ Digital Medicine, 4, Article 54.⏎https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/33742085/⏎• Eric G Poon, Christy Harris Lemak, Juan C Rojas, Janet Guptill, David Classen, Adoption⏎of artificial intelligence in healthcare: survey of health system priorities, successes, and⏎challenges, Journal of the American Medical Informatics Association, Volume 32, Issue 7,⏎July 2025, Pages 1093–1100, https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf065⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations⏎have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption decisions are typically driven by executive leadership, compliance and privacy⏎officers, and information technology governance bodies. Clinical leaders play an important⏎advisory role but often do not control procurement or enterprise risk decisions.⏎Administrative hurdles include regulatory uncertainty, lack of internal AI literacy, limited⏎governance infrastructure, procurement constraints, and uncertainty regarding long term⏎monitoring obligations. Organizations are particularly hesitant when accountability for model⏎performance is unclear or when lifecycle oversight expectations are undefined.⏎HHS can mitigate these barriers by publishing clear implementation guidance aligned with⏎CHAI and NIST frameworks, providing governance templates, supporting workforce training⏎programs, and aligning payment incentives with documented assurance practices.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types,⏎data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would significantly widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate responsible AI development. AI systems depend on consistent semantic⏎7⏎⏎normalization, longitudinal data, and large demographically diverse patient cohorts with⏎reliable provenance to reduce the risk of bias and enhance accuracy.⏎Priority areas include FHIR-based care planning, such as the HL7 FHIR Multiple Chronic⏎Conditions Care Plan Implementation Guide16, HL7 FHIR electronic long-term services and⏎supports artifacts, HL7 SDOH Clinical Care Implementation Guide17, the CMS Post-Acute⏎Care InterOperability (PACIO) Implementation Guides18, and the HL7 FHIR Person-Centered⏎Outcomes (PCO) Implementation Guide19. Adoption of these artifacts can further support⏎transportable and reproducible AI evaluation across settings.⏎Standardized provenance metadata and conformance validation are equally important to⏎ensure that AI inputs and outputs can be traced and audited across organizational⏎boundaries.⏎EMI’s data modeling platform operationalizes this approach by producing harmonized, FHIR⏎aligned infrastructure that enables semantic harmonization and conformance validation. This⏎infrastructure supports interoperable AI use cases and cross-site benchmarking consistent⏎with federal certification and TEFCA expectations, providing the schema foundation on which⏎longitudinal data, provenance, and cohort-based analytics can be built.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers seek AI that reduces repetitive data entry, improves navigation⏎across systems, enhances care coordination, and respects individual preferences. They want⏎AI to simplify complexity rather than introduce additional burden.⏎Their primary concerns include inappropriate secondary use of data, lack of transparency⏎regarding model training, bias and inequitable outcomes, and erosion of human oversight.⏎Trust depends on meaningful disclosure of AI use, clear accountability pathways (e.g., HTI-1⏎DSI certification), and evidence that safeguards are in place.⏎Consent-based architectures, equity monitoring, and transparent governance structures⏎should be embedded from the outset. Responsible adoption must ensure that AI enhances⏎patient agency rather than diminishing it.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize applied research that advances trustworthy implementation of AI in⏎real-world clinical settings. This includes methods for bias detection and subgroup evaluation,⏎drift monitoring, consent-aware system design, federated validation methodologies, and⏎integration of clinical, social, and long-term services data.⏎16 https://build.fhir.org/ig/HL7/fhir-us-mcc/⏎17 https://hl7.org/fhir/us/sdoh-clinicalcare/⏎18 https://pacioproject.org/⏎19 https://build.fhir.org/ig/HL7/pco-ig/en/⏎8⏎⏎Research should extend beyond technical performance to assess workforce impact,⏎redistribution of administrative burden, safety events, and sustainability over time.⏎Implementation of science and organizational change research is particularly critical to⏎understanding why some AI deployments succeed while others fail.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use⏎clinical care?⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎Published findings show AI benefits are most consistent when it replaces manual aggregation⏎and pattern recognition tasks within well-governed systems. Costs increase when AI is⏎layered onto fragmented infrastructure, shifting work to care coordinators, information⏎technology teams, and compliance staff. Longitudinal evaluation is necessary to assess⏎whether burden reductions are durable and equitably distributed.⏎HHS can accelerate learning by supporting sustained evaluation networks and integrating AI⏎monitoring into existing patient safety and quality reporting infrastructures.⏎Sincerely,⏎Evelyn Gallego, MBA, MPH, CPHIMS⏎CEO and Founder⏎9♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0455♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Anonymous♔Anonymous♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1. Federal Regulation Is Essential. There is currently no comprehensive federal regulatory framework governing AI in healthcare. This regulatory gap poses significant risks to patients. Leaving oversight to corporations or to a patchwork of state laws would create inconsistent protections and expose patients to avoidable harm. PsiAN calls for uniform federal standards to govern privacy, safety, and accountability.⏎⏎2. Privacy Concerns and HIPAA Gaps. Many AI chatbots marketed for mental health support are not compliant with HIPAA requirements, meaning patient data may not be protected under federal healthcare privacy law. Without legally enforceable safeguards, patients may unknowingly expose sensitive information without adequate protection. No acceleration of AI adoption should occur without clear, enforceable federal privacy standards.⏎⏎3. Lack of Evidence for Safety and Effectiveness. Large language models (LLMs) are prone to generating inaccurate or fabricated outputs. There have been documented cases of AI systems providing harmful guidance to vulnerable individuals, including those experiencing suicidal ideation. Corporate metrics such as user engagement, downloads, and customer satisfaction are not substitutes for rigorous clinical outcome data. New AI tools must be evaluated using meaningful health metrics, including symptom remission, dropout rates, sustained recovery, and improvements in quality of life. Evaluation and safety research should be conducted by independent researchers, not by the corporations developing and selling the products, so as to minimize conflicts of interest.⏎⏎4. AI and the Limits of the Therapeutic Relationship. Decades of psychotherapy research confirm that the therapist-patient relationship is the strongest predictor of treatment outcome. AI systems, regardless of sophistication, do not constitute human relationships.  At present, there is insufficient evidence to justify AI as a substitute for trained mental health professionals. Recovery in behavioral health involves more than symptom reduction. AI systems must be evaluated against broader, recovery-oriented outcomes, not simply short-term symptom change.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0456♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Camber Health , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Camber Health♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to RFI: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted by: Camber Health⏎Contact: joel@camber.health⏎Website: camber.health⏎Executive Summary⏎Camber is a healthcare technology company purpose-built to modernize revenue cycle⏎management and detect, prevent, and remediate waste, fraud, and abuse at the point of⏎care—particularly in behavioral health and autism services. Embedded directly within provider⏎EHR workflows, Camber serves as an agnostic SaaS and AI partner that reduces administrative⏎burden, improves billing accuracy, and ensures continuity of care.⏎Camber supports more than 250,000 patients and 50,000 providers across all 50 states,⏎processing $2.5B+ in annual claims, representing roughly 20% of the U.S. autism market. By⏎combining deep historical claims data with a real-time financial data layer, our platform⏎integrates clinical data, payer rules, census data, and proprietary models to deliver real-time⏎visibility into how healthcare dollars flow—and how financial signals directly impact clinical care⏎delivery.⏎Backed by $50M from Andreessen Horowitz and Craft Ventures, Camber delivers critical⏎infrastructure that aligns with CMS priorities around transparency, operational efficiency,⏎program integrity, and improved patient outcomes—helping providers get paid accurately, faster,⏎and at scale.⏎Camber has supported providers through major system transitions, including TRICARE,⏎federally qualified health center (CHC) outages, and Medicaid managed care organization⏎shifts. We work closely with states, payers, provider groups, clinical experts, regulators, and⏎legislators to ensure our solutions function reliably during upstream system changes—protecting⏎providers from operational disruption and financial instability.⏎The common theme within these conversations is responsible use of AI. While our response⏎reflects that of a technology vendor, it is shaped by deep collaboration with providers,⏎accrediting bodies, and nonprofit advocates—grounded in the real-world needs of frontline⏎stakeholders⏎We believe CMS has a unique opportunity to modernize infrastructure, streamline data, and⏎empower providers. Camber is technologically and operationally ready to support this vision⏎today.⏎⏎Responses to Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The primary barriers to AI innovation and adoption in clinical care include regulatory⏎fragmentation, data access constraints, workforce readiness gaps, and unclear governance and⏎procurement standards.⏎Regulatory patchworking across state, federal, and payer-level rules creates uncertainty for⏎private-sector developers. The absence of federal preemption forces AI tools to be adapted on a⏎state-by-state basis, slowing deployment and discouraging investment—particularly as states⏎(e.g., CA, FL, NY, CO) advance privacy laws despite recent federal guidance.⏎Second, unclear definitions of what constitutes “AI in clinical care” lead to inconsistent oversight.⏎Many AI systems operate within administrative and operational workflows—such as prior⏎authorization, eligibility, utilization monitoring, documentation, and revenue cycle⏎management—yet materially influence clinical decision-making and access to care. These⏎systems often fall outside traditional medical device frameworks while still carrying clinical⏎impact. Implementing AI systems therefore requires education and financial expertise—core⏎tenets of the healthcare system—and necessitates meaningful workforce and workflow⏎transformation.⏎Third, limitations in data access and interoperability—particularly where EHR vendors and⏎payers control data—restrict real-time validation, monitoring, and continuous improvement of AI⏎systems. Without standardized, interoperable inputs, safe and scalable deployment remains⏎difficult. Recent ASTP efforts to simplify and standardize EHR data represent meaningful⏎progress in addressing this barrier.⏎Additional barriers include:⏎● Inconsistent standards for informed consent and data ownership, including liability and⏎data licensing responsibility⏎● Limited provider and patient education, trust, and transparency⏎● Lack of clear requirements for human-in-the-loop decision-making⏎● Absence of standardized stopping mechanisms and post-deployment monitoring⏎● Unclear expectations for foundation model review versus system-specific rules engines⏎● Ensuring PHI safety and anonymization as models advance and adoption scales⏎Finally, while recent agentic models are effective at standardizing repeatable administrative⏎tasks, the next phase of AI adoption requires systems that combine foundation models with⏎specialized rules engines. These systems represent an evolution beyond traditional SaaS and⏎require governance frameworks that reflect their system-specific design and rapidly evolving⏎real-world use.⏎⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS can prioritize payment alignment, real-time oversight, and outcome-based incentives to⏎encourage effective AI use.⏎Proposed Updates Include:⏎● Enable value-based or outcome-based payment models that allow AI-driven efficiency⏎and quality improvements to be rewarded, rather than solely reimbursing traditional fee⏎for service⏎● Permit real-time, AI-supported utilization monitoring and compliance, as an alternative to⏎retrospective and manual audits that increase administrative burden⏎● Clarify how AI tools may be used to support documentation, authorization, and utilization⏎review without triggering duplicative manual requirements⏎● Revisit payment policies that implicitly require human labor even when AI can⏎demonstrably improve accuracy and speed⏎● Increase CMMI test payment modeling, like ACCESS, but for high scrutiny verticals such⏎as behavioral health⏎HHS should also clarify how existing regulations governing utilization review, payment integrity,⏎and administrative simplification apply to AI-supported workflows, including CMS regulations⏎related to managed care oversight and program integrity.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎Non-medical device AI used in clinical care raises novel issues related to liability, accountability,⏎and procurement, particularly when systems influence access to care or clinical actions.⏎Key issues include:⏎● Unclear liability and indemnification when AI systems support or automate administrative⏎decisions that affect clinical outcomes⏎● Lack of disclosure or overreliance on generic AI disclosures, which is insufficient for⏎scaled operational systems⏎● Lack of standardized distinctions and rules between agentic AI, decision-support tools,⏎and automation⏎● Absence of federal guidance on public-sector and provider procurement of AI systems⏎HHS should establish a federal framework and oversight model, with preemption where⏎appropriate, to reduce uncertainty and accelerate responsible adoption. Clear guidance would⏎⏎reduce the current “wait-and-see” dynamic that delays implementation, increase private sector⏎innovation, and allow widespread adoption.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and⏎post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful ( e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎The most promising evaluation approaches combine pre-deployment testing with continuous⏎post-deployment monitoring.⏎Effective methods include:⏎● System-specific rules-engine validation layered on top of foundation models⏎● Real-time performance monitoring with defined stopping mechanisms⏎● Workflow-embedded human oversight⏎● Metrics tied to access, timeliness, accuracy, and burden reduction—not just model⏎performance⏎HHS should support these efforts through pilots, grants, and cooperative agreements,⏎particularly those focused on real-world implementation rather than static model evaluation.⏎ACCESS/LEAD models are an essential and important step that we hope to see expand to⏎behavioral health and specifically ABA.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities ( e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical⏎care?⏎HHS can best support private-sector innovation in AI for clinical care by:⏎● Formally recognizing specialty- specific accreditation, certification, and testing programs⏎that meet defined federal guardrails, enabling faster adoption without duplicative⏎oversight. This recognition should include a transparent, standardized pathway for new⏎entrants to be evaluated and approved⏎● Encouraging streamlined credentialing and “gold card” approaches within CMS,⏎supported by AI-enabled compliance monitoring and continuous validation rather than⏎episodic, manual review. This influences commercial payor strategy while incentivizing⏎integrity opportunities within HHS- OIG⏎● Reducing barriers to entry by permitting technology-enabled verification, auditing, and⏎reporting in place of labor-intensive, document-based compliance processes⏎● Replacing ad hoc AI waivers with clear procurement and implementation guidance,⏎allowing states and agencies to adopt AI-enabled solutions consistently, with defined⏎expectations for governance, accountability, and auditability⏎⏎● Promoting competition by enabling specialization, allowing private-sector innovators⏎focused on specific clinical or administrative functions to compete effectively with large,⏎vertically integrated incumbents⏎AI has the potential to democratize participation across health care programs by lowering⏎administrative overhead, expanding access for smaller and under-resourced providers, and⏎shifting compliance from retrospective documentation to real-time assurance—while maintaining⏎program integrity and patient protections.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎AI has fallen short when deployed for broad, unilateral or opaque decisions- particularly in payer⏎use cases such as utilization management and claims denials. In these contexts, lack of⏎transparency, limited explainability, and weak oversight have eroded trust and, in some cases,⏎increased administrative burden rather than reducing it as providers seek answers for decisions⏎with no documentation or explicit rationale.⏎AI has met or exceeded performance and cost expectations when applied to:⏎● Analysis of robust non-clinical and semi-structured data, such as payer rules, coverage⏎policies, and utilization patterns from open sources or within closed systems⏎● Automation of repetitive administrative tasks, including intake, documentation support,⏎eligibility verification, and billing workflows⏎● Trend detection and visibility, enabling earlier intervention, proactive care coordination,⏎and more efficient resource allocation⏎● Transcription of clinical sessions for further review⏎The greatest future potential lies in novel AI tools that:⏎● Improve operational efficiency and reduce friction across staffing, care delivery, and⏎reimbursement⏎● Expand access to care by accelerating administrative workflows and improving network⏎navigation⏎● Generate actionable, explainable insights on quality, outcomes, and next steps for⏎patients and providers⏎● Outline clear human oversight and accountability processes built into system design,⏎with mechanisms to appeal decisions⏎⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative⏎hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Adoption is most influenced by CFOs, boards, and executive leadership, who assess new⏎technologies primarily through financial sustainability, enterprise risk, compliance, and⏎reputational lenses. We find adoption decisions are often led by financial and operational⏎leadership rather than clinical leaders alone. AI presents an opportunity to better align financial⏎decisions with clinical support by enabling more consistent, data informed care delivery.⏎Primary administrative hurdles include:⏎● Regulatory uncertainty and liability risk, including questions of indemnification, consent,⏎and accountability for AI-supported decisions⏎● Lack of clear federal guidance and standards, particularly around validation, deployment,⏎and oversight that states/agencies can then implement accordingly⏎● Insufficient education and training limitations hinder adoption and effective use at the⏎point of care, as workforce mistrust heightens around job security⏎● Patient and family concerns regarding transparency, consent, and control over data and⏎decision making⏎● Integration and interoperability challenges, especially with EHRs and payer systems,⏎which increase implementation cost and complexity⏎● Unclear return on investment and difficulty tying AI performance to measurable clinical⏎and operational outcomes, especially as these systems develop so rapidly⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Enhanced interoperability would materially expand market opportunities and accelerate AI⏎development by standardizing clinical, administrative, and operational data inputs while enabling⏎faster validation, review, and benchmarking of AI outputs.⏎Key priorities include:⏎● Standardized data inputs using FHIR for clinical documentation, authorizations, eligibility,⏎claims, and outcomes data to reduce custom integrations and lower barriers to entry for⏎AI developers⏎● Broader implementation of TEFCA to support longitudinal patient records across care⏎settings, enabling more robust model training, evaluation, and post-deployment⏎monitoring⏎● Reduction of EHR data silos, particularly where vendor-controlled data access limits⏎real-world evidence generation and model performance benchmarking⏎● ASTP guidance on operational interoperability, minimum data availability, access⏎standards, and accountability mechanisms beyond basic data exchange⏎⏎Open, enforceable interoperability standards are essential to enable private-sector innovation,⏎comparative benchmarking, and safe deployment of AI tools in clinical care. We greatly⏎appreciate recent steps ASTP has taken with regards to this.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers want AI in clinical care to deliver:⏎● Clear visibility into their data — who can access it, how decisions are made, by whom,⏎and when⏎● Interoperable systems that communicate across providers and location, eliminating⏎repetitive intake and form completion⏎● Faster, simpler access to care, including real-time insight into in-network providers and⏎appointment availability⏎● Reduced administrative burden through automation of routine tasks that delay services⏎and continuity of care⏎● Financial clarity before and after visits, including cost expectations, visit summaries, next⏎steps, and actionable guidance⏎Primary concerns on adoption center on privacy, transparency, and ensuring AI augments,⏎rather than replaces, human oversight. Much of the mistrust surrounding AI stems from a⏎communication gap around how systems work and how decisions are made. Addressing this⏎directly through clear governance, explainability, and patient-centered design is essential to⏎enabling responsible, scalable adoption. This is entirely solvable, in the near term.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0457♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-15T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Baker♔♔Alabama♔♔United States♔University of Oregon♔♔♔♔♔♔My concern is not with AI in healthcare per se. I have spent the better part of three decades doing biostatistics, most recently at the University of Oregon, and I have watched enough promising tools get chewed up by the gap between controlled validation and actual clinical deployment that I am not reflexively hostile to new technology. What I am is deeply skeptical of the framing here.⏎⏎HHS is asking how to accelerate adoption. That is the wrong question, and I want to be direct about why.⏎⏎When you train an algorithm on historical claims data, you are training it on decades of documented inequity. Insurance denial rates, diagnostic delays, differential treatment recommendations by race and insurance status — all of that gets baked in. The algorithm learns the world as it has been, not as it should be. That is not a novel observation; researchers have been publishing on this for years, including work out of Obermeyer et al. in Science back in 2019 showing a widely deployed commercial algorithm was effectively systematically underestimating the health needs of Black patients by using cost as a proxy for need. What concerns me is that the response from the policy community has been largely rhetorical acknowledgment followed by continued deployment pressure.⏎⏎The specific thing I keep coming back to is automation bias. Clinicians are, in general, less likely to override a system output that arrives with any kind of algorithmic authority attached to it. There is a reasonable body of literature on this. When a risk score or a recommendation gets surfaced by a tool the institution has purchased and the vendor has certified, it carries a different kind of weight than a colleague's opinion. That weight is not earned. It is borrowed from the general cultural prestige of quantitative methods. As a statistician I find that deeply frustrating because it means that when the model is wrong in a systematic way — when it is underperforming for elderly patients, or patients with limited English proficiency, or patients in rural Alabama where the training data was thin — those errors are less likely to get caught and corrected than random noise would be.⏎⏎The ACR apparently found, in a survey of over 1,100 radiologists, that 63% felt vendor-reported accuracy metrics did not reflect what they were seeing in practice. I do not find that surprising at all. Benchmark datasets are not patient populations. Transfer learning across hospital systems is genuinely hard. And yet we are talking about acceleration.⏎⏎What I would ask HHS to consider is whether any of its investment priorities explicitly condition funding or reimbursement eligibility on prospective bias auditing with pre-specified demographic subgroup reporting requirements. Not post-hoc. Prospective. The audit design needs to be part of the deployment plan before the system goes live, not a checkbox added two years later when someone complains. And the subgroups need to be defined in advance, because if you let vendors cherry-pick the subgroups they report on, you will get exactly the subgroups where the tool performs well.⏎⏎I recognize there is a real tension here. Some of these tools, particularly in imaging, do appear to have genuine clinical utility for certain tasks, and if the perfect is the enemy of the good, delayed adoption has its own costs. I am not dismissing that. But the asymmetry matters. If we slow down a tool that would have worked, that is a cost we can revisit. If we scale a biased tool to population level before we have the auditing infrastructure to detect what it is doing, the harm is diffuse and very hard to attribute and almost certainly will not be traced back to the deployment decision in any actionable way.⏎⏎The question of who bears the burden of proving equity should be answered before any specific reimbursement pathway gets established. Right now I am not confident HHS has that answer, or even that the question is being asked with the same urgency as the acceleration question.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0458♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Transcarent, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Transcarent♔♔♔♔♔♔4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎February 23, 2026⏎Submitted electronically via Regulations.gov⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, M.D., M.B.A.⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information—Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Health and Human Services Programs⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎Transcarent appreciates the opportunity to submit comments in response to the Request for⏎Information (RFI) titled Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Health and Human Services Programs, as published in the Federal Register on December 23,⏎2025.⏎Transcarent is the One Place for Health and Careᵀᴹ, bringing medical, pharmacy, and point⏎solutions together with the WayFindingᵀᴹ experience, the first generative and agentic AI-⏎powered health and care platform for health consumers. Our WayFinding experience, paired⏎with transparent pharmacy care, 2nd.MD expert medical opinions, and virtual primary care,⏎integrates with our specialty care for cancer, surgery, and weight health to support people⏎with all their health needs, simple or serious. More than 1,700 employers and health plans⏎and 21 million Members rely on us to provide information, guidance, and care, empowering⏎health consumers with more choice, an experience they love, access to higher-quality care,⏎and lower costs.⏎How Transcarent Uses AI⏎Our experience deploying artificial intelligence, including generative and agentic AI, within⏎HIPAA-compliant environments to support clinical care, navigation, and administrative⏎simplification informs the perspectives below.⏎Transcarent’s AI strategy is built to enhance, not replace, the relationship between people⏎and their care teams. Our AI tools serve as a force multiplier for clinicians and a trusted⏎companion for individuals navigating a complex system.⏎ WayFinding: Launched in May 2024, WayFinding is the first application of generative⏎AI focused on improving the experience of the health consumer. By combining instant⏎access to care providers with benefits navigation and clinical guidance on one easy to⏎use platform, WayFinding personalizes each individual’s experience, orienting and⏎1 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL⏎⏎4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎guiding them through their health and care journeys, with human support never more⏎than a tap away. New agentic AI capabilities further enable symptom triage, provide⏎AI scheduling for doctor’s appointments, and personalized care paths.⏎ Clinical Support: Transcarent integrates AI into our affiliated virtual clinic to enhance⏎efficiency and safety. Before a visit, an AI assistant gathers relevant history and⏎symptoms, structuring inputs for the clinician to review. This reduces administrative⏎burden and allows clinicians to focus on diagnosis and care planning. The AI can also⏎assist in post-visit documentation, follow-up, and care gap reminders.⏎ Chronic Condition Prevention and Risk Identification: AI models identify Members⏎at elevated risk for costly or preventable conditions by analyzing claims, engagement⏎patterns, and onboarding surveys. These insights prompt timely outreach, health⏎literacy, and intervention to avoid unnecessary progression of disease.⏎Policy Priorities for Responsible AI in Healthcare⏎While we are optimistic about the potential for AI to enhance care quality, access, and⏎efficiency, we urge the Administration to establish a thoughtful policy framework that ensures⏎safety, equity, and innovation.⏎1. Transparency and Explainability: Patients and clinicians must understand when AI is⏎involved in the decision-making process and have access to clear explanations of AI-⏎generated recommendations.⏎2. Privacy and Data Protections: AI tools handling personal health information must be⏎subject to robust privacy and security safeguards, including modernized federal⏎privacy standards that cover digital health tools not subject to HIPAA.⏎3. Bias Auditing and Equity-by-Design: Developers and deployers of AI systems must test⏎for bias and ensure tools work equitably across diverse populations. AI has the power⏎to reduce structural barriers in health care and should be designed and governed⏎intentionally.⏎4. Role of Clinicians and Human Oversight: AI should augment—not override—clinical⏎judgment. Clinical AI tools should preserve human decision-making and avoid⏎automating care denials or coverage decisions.⏎5. Certification and Risk-Based Regulation: Federal oversight should distinguish between⏎high-risk, clinical AI applications and lower-risk tools such as administrative navigation.⏎Regulatory clarity is needed, but it must be risk-proportionate and not stifle⏎innovation.⏎6. Support for Health Care Workforce Enablement: Federal investment is needed to build⏎AI fluency across the health care workforce, including training for clinicians,⏎administrators, and digital health developers.⏎2 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL⏎⏎4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎We also acknowledge that structural and regulatory barriers limit responsible and scalable⏎adoption. We offer the following recommendations to help HHS accelerate safe and effective⏎AI integration across its programs.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎From Transcarent’s perspective, the lack of harmonization among state AI and privacy laws—⏎combined with the absence of a coherent national framework governing healthcare AI—⏎creates significant operational, compliance, and innovation challenges. Healthcare⏎organizations operating across multiple states must navigate a patchwork of evolving and, at⏎times, inconsistent requirements related to data use, algorithmic transparency, consumer⏎notice, bias audits, and automated decision-making. This fragmentation increases⏎compliance costs, complicates product design, and can slow the deployment of tools that⏎improve access, reduce administrative burden, and enhance care coordination. In the⏎healthcare context, where AI systems often rely on multi-state data flows, employer-⏎sponsored coverage spanning jurisdictions, and interoperability across provider networks, a⏎lack of national alignment risks creating uncertainty for providers, patients, and developers⏎alike. A consistent federal framework that harmonizes privacy protections, governance⏎expectations, and oversight standards would better support responsible innovation,⏎strengthen patient trust, and ensure equitable access to AI-enabled health solutions across⏎the country.⏎Another barrier is workflow integration. AI tools layered onto existing clinical or⏎administrative processes without thoughtful redesign risk increasing burden rather than⏎reducing it. Adoption is strongest when AI reduces documentation demands, synthesizes⏎relevant patient information prior to encounters, and simplifies navigation for patients and⏎caregivers.⏎Misaligned incentives further constrain investment. When payment systems reward volume⏎rather than outcomes, providers and organizations have limited financial rationale to deploy⏎AI tools that reduce unnecessary utilization or administrative waste. In contrast, in value-⏎based arrangements, AI-enabled navigation and care coordination can measurably reduce⏎inappropriate site-of-care utilization and avoid unnecessary procedures while improving⏎patient engagement.⏎Clinicians and patients require clarity regarding oversight, monitoring, explainability, and⏎responsibility when AI influences clinical workflows.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop⏎or use AI in clinical care?⏎3 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL⏎⏎4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎Transcarent supports the development of a risk-based, technology-neutral federal framework⏎for AI, that applies to healthcare, that promotes safety and accountability without⏎unnecessarily constraining innovation. Federal policy should clearly distinguish between⏎high-risk clinical decision support tools that directly inform diagnosis or treatment and lower-⏎risk administrative or navigation applications that streamline scheduling, benefits guidance,⏎documentation, or care coordination. Overly broad or undifferentiated regulation could⏎inadvertently slow the deployment of AI tools that reduce administrative burden and improve⏎access without materially increasing clinical risk. A proportionate approach—grounded in the⏎function and risk profile of the application rather than the underlying technology—will better⏎protect patients while enabling responsible innovation and scalable adoption across⏎healthcare settings.⏎HHS should also provide practical, implementation-oriented guidance under the HIPAA⏎Privacy Rule clarifying permissible data uses in AI development and deployment,⏎expectations for de-identification, safeguards against re-identification risk, and audit⏎requirements. Clear guidance will reduce compliance uncertainty while preserving patient⏎trust.⏎With respect to payment policy, HHS should prioritize outcome-based program designs⏎rather than establishing payment solely for software artifacts. Where Medicare payment⏎frameworks apply, policies should recognize AI-enabled services when they demonstrably⏎improve access, quality, equity, or affordability. Incorporating AI-enabled capabilities into⏎value-based care models and quality programs will more effectively align incentives than⏎stand-alone reimbursement for technology.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability⏎structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel⏎legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help⏎address them?⏎The integration of AI into clinical environments raises important questions regarding⏎accountability and oversight. These include allocation of responsibility among developers,⏎implementers, clinicians, and organizations; governance of model updates and performance⏎drift; documentation of human oversight; and maintenance of auditable records.⏎HHS can support responsible innovation by issuing practical guidance on governance⏎expectations for AI used in clinical decision support and adjacent use cases. Such guidance⏎should emphasize transparency, monitoring, incident response protocols, and clear⏎communication when AI tools are used in patient-facing interactions.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-⏎centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these⏎4 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL⏎⏎4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants,⏎cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎Robust evaluation is essential to maintaining trust and ensuring safety. Pre-deployment⏎validation should include testing on representative real-world populations and workflows,⏎assessment of subgroup performance and calibration, robustness testing for data shift, and⏎human factors evaluation.⏎Post-deployment, continuous monitoring should assess performance drift, workflow impacts,⏎safety signals, administrative burden reduction, and total cost of care outcomes. The⏎distinction between technical model performance and real-world impact is critical; successful⏎integration into operational environments often determines measurable outcomes.⏎HHS can accelerate progress by supporting pragmatic, multi-site research initiatives that⏎evaluate clinical outcomes, equity impacts, and administrative burden reduction in live⏎operational settings. Shared benchmarks and validation infrastructure would facilitate the⏎scaling of responsible AI solutions across programs.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use⏎in clinical care?⏎To support private-sector testing and credentialing, HHS should harmonize expectations⏎across federal programs and encourage standardized reporting, auditability, and⏎transparency. Recognition of credible third-party evaluation frameworks would allow⏎scalable, consistent assurance across settings.⏎Transcarent’s AI-powered solutions are built for healthcare from the ground up. Transcarent⏎acts as a HIPAA-covered entity and is certified to HITRUST, SOC 2, and ISO 27001. Our⏎governance model is guided by the Organization of Economic Co-operation and⏎Development (OECD), the first intergovernmental standard on AI that promotes innovative⏎and trustworthy AI, and the National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) whose risk⏎management framework incorporates trustworthiness considerations in the design,⏎development, deployment, and use of AI systems. We apply these principles through human-⏎in-the-loop testing, code review, and regular audits and monitoring to prevent bias, protect⏎privacy, and ensure safe, ethical use. The company’s generative AI products are developed⏎under clinician oversight. These capabilities are built to support consumers in navigating⏎their care, not to diagnose, treat, or replace clinical judgment.⏎Further, as a member of the Data & Trusted AI Alliance (D&TAA), Transcarent collaborates⏎with leading global companies to create transparent and trustworthy AI data standards that⏎promote responsible use and provide real value to inform policy development. Together, we⏎share a conviction that the future of business will be powered by the responsible use of data⏎and AI, including the first of its kind cross-industry data provenance standards. We believe⏎5 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL⏎⏎4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎that trust in data requires understanding its origin–where it comes from, how it was created,⏎and whether it may be used legally. Transparency is critical.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would⏎have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on⏎quality, and help reduce costs?⏎At Transcarent, we believe AI holds great promise to revolutionize care delivery for the better⏎– to speed access to treatment, reduce administrative burdens on clinicians, and democratize⏎access to healthcare resources. For example, a person’s zip code no longer matters when⏎they want to meet with an endocrinologist. We also believe that AI should be used only for⏎the right reasons – it should not be used to deny access to appropriate, medically necessary⏎care or in a way that diminishes the quality.⏎In healthcare, some tasks require deep clinical expertise, while others are necessary but⏎administrative and labor-intensive. Generative AI is already demonstrating strong capability⏎in the latter, particularly in information synthesis, documentation support, and form⏎completion. Given that administrative activities account for a significant share of healthcare⏎spending, these applications can meaningfully reduce burden and clinician burnout.⏎Beyond administrative efficiency, the greater opportunity lies in AI-enabled triage,⏎navigation, and personalization. The healthcare system is complex and difficult for many⏎individuals to navigate. Generative AI can simplify access by guiding patients through⏎coverage questions, scheduling, and care options, helping them focus on their health rather⏎than system logistics.⏎AI also offers an opportunity to move beyond “one-size-fits-all” care. Personalized⏎educational materials tailored to a patient’s health literacy, language, and specific questions⏎can improve understanding and engagement. When used alongside clinicians, generative AI⏎can extend conversations, clarify guidance, and support informed decision-making.⏎Additional promising applications include personalized behavioral health support, chronic⏎disease risk reduction planning, synthesis of medical research for clinicians and families,⏎medical education, and guidance through complex treatment decisions.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are⏎the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Transcarent maintains a formal AI Governance Committee (AIGC) to oversee the⏎development, deployment, and monitoring of artificial intelligence tools used in Member-⏎facing products and internal operations, including within our affiliated medical groups. The⏎AIGC operates under a human-centered framework grounded in principles of safety, security,⏎privacy, transparency, equity, explainability, respect, and data minimization. Transcarent⏎6 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL⏎⏎4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎designs AI systems to augment—not replace—clinician judgment, and does not use AI to deny⏎medically necessary care or override provider decision-making. All AI-enabled services are⏎deployed within HIPAA-covered and HITRUST-secured environments, with ongoing⏎monitoring, bias mitigation efforts, and clearly defined human oversight to ensure⏎accountability, patient trust, and alignment with high-quality, affordable care.⏎The AIGC includes representation from leaders across internal teams including technology,⏎security, privacy, legal, human resources, clinical care, and marketing.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎A significant barrier to AI adoption in clinical and operational settings remains fragmented⏎and inconsistent access to high-quality clinical and administrative data. Variability in⏎implementation of interoperability standards, inconsistent semantic normalization across⏎systems, and limited access to longitudinal claims data impede model development,⏎validation, and real-world deployment. As we have previously emphasized in comments to⏎HHS and Congress, access to comprehensive claims and cost data is essential to enable⏎value-based arrangements, measure performance, and support longitudinal care⏎optimization.⏎Even where interoperability frameworks exist, inconsistent timeliness and completeness of⏎information reduce reliability. AI systems embedded in care delivery require accurate,⏎current, and comprehensive data to function safely and effectively.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed⏎by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and⏎caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Patients and caregivers prioritize timely access to appropriate care, simplified navigation,⏎transparent pricing, and understandable information tailored to their needs. At the same⏎time, they express concerns regarding privacy, bias, transparency, and accountability.⏎Strong safeguards under HIPAA, clear notification when AI is used in care processes, and⏎accessible human support are essential to maintaining trust. Our experience demonstrates⏎that AI systems designed to augment—not replace—clinical judgment and human advocacy⏎are more likely to achieve sustained engagement and measurable improvements in⏎experience and outcomes.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their⏎use clinical care?⏎7 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL⏎⏎4700 S. Syracuse Street⏎Suite 900⏎Denver, Colorado 80237⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI⏎as part of clinical care?⏎In our experience, AI has met or exceeded expectations when applied to reduce⏎administrative burden, improve care navigation and triage, personalize educational materials,⏎and help individuals efficiently access appropriate in-network care. AI-enabled navigation⏎and virtual care integration can reduce inappropriate site-of-care utilization and improve⏎engagement, particularly when paired with human support.⏎When AI deconstructs the complexities of documentation, approvals, and benefit decision⏎trees to guide individuals through their healthcare journey, people can use all their benefits⏎more easily and efficiently, understand why and what is happening with their health at any⏎given time, and feel confident that they are maintaining costs while achieving the best⏎outcome. When we empower people with the tools to find their way to better health,⏎everyone benefits– Members, providers, payers, and employers.⏎Conversely, AI has fallen short when deployed without reliable access to complete data,⏎when layered onto existing workflows without redesign, or when governance and monitoring⏎processes are insufficient. Sustainable success requires both technological capability and⏎structural alignment.⏎Conclusion⏎Artificial intelligence is already improving access, reducing administrative burden, enhancing⏎personalization, and supporting value-based care across HHS programs, but there is⏎immense potential to do much more. Realizing this potential requires continued federal⏎leadership to advance interoperability, clarify privacy expectations, align incentives with⏎measurable outcomes, and promote consistent governance standards.⏎Transcarent appreciates the opportunity to provide these comments and stands ready to⏎work with ASTP/ONC, CMS, and other HHS agencies to ensure AI-enabled solutions improve⏎quality, affordability, equity, and patient trust.⏎Sincerely,⏎Leslie Krigstein⏎Vice President, Communications & Government Affairs⏎Transcarent⏎8 PROPRIETARY & CONFIDENTIAL♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0459♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Petterson, Barbara, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Barbara♔Petterson♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI, Mary E. Switzer Building,⏎Mail⏎Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Comment to HHS Health Sector AI RFI RIN 0955-AA13⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology:⏎As a Licensed Marriage and Family Therapist for 36 years, I appreciate the opportunity⏎to provide a response to the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) Request⏎for Information regarding Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care.⏎Regarding regulation, there is cause for great concern related to the privacy, efficacy,⏎and safety of AI in healthcare. There is no current federal regulation of AI in healthcare.⏎Regulation is essential, given the federal government’s obligation to protect the public.⏎This is not something that can be left to the companies producing and selling these⏎products or to individual states, which could result in a patchwork of incompatible⏎regulations. Our government has decided that regulation and oversight are essential in⏎healthcare to ensure safety and efficacy. For example, regulation and appropriate⏎enforcement are important aspects of new products and treatments through the FDA.⏎We regulate healthcare clinicians through licensing requirements. We should not accept⏎anything less with AI and should set the bar higher for unproven technology. AI chatbots⏎are not HIPAA compliant, therefore user/patient privacy is not protected as mandated by⏎federal law in all healthcare industries and settings. Privacy and confidentiality are⏎cornerstones of effective psychotherapy, and the absence of these protections in the⏎use of AI Chatbots demands correction.⏎Regarding research and development: Due to the novelty of this technology, there is not⏎yet⏎enough data available to support efficacy and/or safety. Large Language Models (LLMs)⏎are especially prone to “hallucinations,” with documented cases involving irrevocable⏎harm of the worst kind. AI LLMs have encouraged vulnerable people struggling with⏎suicide to take suicidal action or provided them with information about the heights of⏎bridges or buildings from which to jump. This is unacceptably risky Chatbot behavior⏎that literally threatens lives. AI products must be thoroughly researched and tested, as⏎is any new psychotherapy protocol or pharmaceutical, to measure effectiveness,⏎dropout rates, remission rates, etc.⏎⏎Importantly, evaluation, assessment, safety and outcome research should be provided⏎by a⏎separate, independent group of researchers to minimize the conflict of interest inherent⏎if⏎corporations that develop these products also supply the evaluation and “science.”⏎Regarding AI instruments approximating psychotherapy, it’s desirable that the products⏎advance recovery, defined by SAMHSA as a process over time leading to a self-⏎directed life. Is insufficient to focus only on symptom management or reduction. HHS⏎advocacy to accelerate use of AI must follow and be linked to development of an⏎evidence⏎base demonstrating safety and efficacy.⏎While there are legitimate concerns related to access to mental health care, there are⏎grave risks associated with the use of AI to provide clinical care in the behavioral⏎healthcare industry, especially without rigorous clinical trials, regulation, and robust⏎oversight. At a minimum, HHS should not accelerate the adoption of AI until the FDA⏎completes its evaluations of AI as medical devices. Given the FDA’s findings that well⏎over 10,000 such applications are already widely available to consumers without⏎appropriate premarket performance evaluations, risk management endeavors, or post-⏎market evaluations, HHS must focus on developing research demonstrating safety and⏎efficacy before accelerating implementation.⏎In study after study over many years, relationship is the main predictor of outcome in⏎psychotherapy. AI is not, in fact, a human relationship, thus it is seriously limited as a⏎potential clinical treatment tool. Much more study and assessment over longer periods⏎of time are necessary. At this point, it is unclear how AI can best be used, whether as a⏎support for psychotherapists, a replacement for psychotherapists, or a tool more tightly⏎focused on insurance processing and administrative issues.⏎AI Medical devices are not ready for accelerated adoption and deployment without⏎appropriate research or enforceable regulation. Even though LLMs are currently being⏎used by millions of people, this adoption by the public is far ahead of the essential⏎science⏎demonstrating privacy, safety, and efficacy. Hence, regulation is essential to reduce the⏎risk of harm to consumers, ensure that the public has access to safe and effective⏎clinical care, hold corporations accountable for the products they develop and sell, and⏎support uniform national standards for AI Chatbots.⏎Sincerely,⏎Barbara Petterson⏎Licensed Marriage and Family Therapist⏎Registered Art Therapist♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0460♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from HIMSS Electronic Health Record Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HIMSS Electronic Health Record Association♔♔♔♔♔♔550 W Van Buren Street, Suite S1110⏎Chicago, IL 60607⏎@EHRAssociation | ehra.org⏎February 20, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: RIN 0955-AA13 – Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane,⏎On behalf of the HIMSS Electronic Health Record (EHR) Association, we value the opportunity to submit⏎comments in response to the Request for Information (RFI) on actions the U.S. Department of Health⏎and Human Services (HHS) can take to accelerate the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in⏎clinical care.⏎Established in 2004, the EHR Association is comprised of nearly 30 member companies that supply the⏎vast majority of EHRs used by hospitals, physician practices, and post-acute care environments across⏎the United States. This gives us a comprehensive view of how AI-enabled tools are developing and being⏎integrated into real-world clinical workflows, and this is the lens through which we respond to the RFI’s⏎questions below.⏎We remain committed to supporting safe healthcare delivery, fostering continued innovation, and⏎operating with high integrity for EHR users and their patients. Our members are enthusiastic about the⏎potential for AI in healthcare technology, with reasonable guardrails in place to protect patients and⏎create a federal baseline for AI tools in certified health IT. We appreciate the chance this RFI presents to⏎harmonize policies across HHS, avoiding conflicting obligations and reducing implementation burden.⏎We also support efforts to modernize reimbursement models to reward clinically valuable AI that⏎improves outcomes, reduces burden, and lowers the total cost of care.⏎We have developed the following core guiding principles for EHR Association member organizations to⏎guide the design, development, and deployment of AI solutions; these inform our comments to the⏎specific questions raised in the RFI:⏎⏎● Patient Safety and Clinical Efficacy: We support the adoption of a national, risk-based⏎governance framework that scales requirements (e.g., committees, processes) to⏎organizational size and AI risk profile. AI must meet standards of safety, reliability, and⏎clinical effectiveness commensurate with risk.⏎● Clinician Oversight and Accountability: AI should be designed and incorporated within EHRs⏎and other health IT to augment informed clinical judgment, not be a substitute for a licensed⏎clinical professional exercising judgment in a particular situation. Clinicians must be able to⏎contextualize decisions or override outputs as appropriate. Regulations should affirm⏎clinician primacy and permit context-driven overrides without mandating one-size-fits-all⏎technical override functions for all AI modalities.⏎● Transparency and Explainability: Clear information about intended use, data provenance,⏎performance characteristics, limitations, and monitoring responsibilities should be available⏎for end users.⏎● Privacy and Security: Protection of identifiable health information compliant with not only⏎HIPAA but also best practices for cybersecurity and data minimization.⏎● Interoperability and Data Quality: Adoption of standards-based data formats,⏎provenance/traceability, and benchmarking to support safe, scalable deployment.⏎● Human-Centered Design and Usability: Workflow-aware design that reduces burden and⏎supports safe use in context.⏎● Lifecycle Risk Management: Pre- and post-deployment evaluation, performance⏎monitoring, and continuous improvement.⏎Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎RFI Question 1 — Biggest barriers to private-sector AI innovation and adoption in clinical care⏎From the vantage point of EHR developers serving hospitals, ambulatory practices, and post-acute⏎providers nationwide, the most significant barriers to AI innovation and adoption fall into five⏎interconnected areas: regulatory fragmentation and uncertainty, data quality and access constraints,⏎governance burdens that are not scaled to risk, misaligned payment incentives and unclear ROI, and⏎workforce and workflow readiness.⏎First, providers and developers face ambiguity about how existing federal frameworks apply to non-⏎device clinical AI versus FDA-regulated Software-as-a-Medical Device (SaMD), creating hesitation and⏎elongated timelines for responsible deployment. A forward-leaning, harmonized HHS posture,⏎coordinated across ASTP/ONC, FDA, CMS, and related offices, would materially reduce uncertainty,⏎accelerate implementation, and promote trust, consistent with the RFI’s objective of establishing a⏎predictable, proportionate regulatory approach that both protects patients and enables innovation. The⏎FDA guidance issued on January 6, 2026, on Clinical Decision Support Software is a helpful step toward⏎greater certainty in this area, but we need to revisit it as AI technologies mature.⏎2⏎⏎Second, even when use cases are clinically compelling, the foundation of data elements needed to safely⏎build, validate, and monitor AI can sometimes be inconsistent, incomplete, or insufficiently⏎representative of both diverse populations and care settings. The RFI’s call to align federal levers so that⏎AI is deployed in ways that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower costs, and improve outcomes⏎will depend on trustworthy, interoperable health data, ongoing oversight and retraining, and a regular⏎cadence of standards enhancement and enforcement. To help remedy these issues, HHS could explore⏎options for supplying rich, purpose-specific (and de-identified) data sets that the industry could rely on⏎for high quality training and evaluation.⏎Third, many smaller organizations and rural providers face governance expectations in proposed⏎legislation/frameworks that risk being overly burdensome. The EHR Association supports multi-⏎disciplinary oversight but has cautioned that requiring rigid governance structures that do not reflect the⏎specific needs of a given organization, costly external validation, and any too-specific timeline could⏎impose disproportionate burdens on resource-constrained settings, as well as lower-risk AI functions⏎that do not require the same scrutiny. We recommend establishing a tiered governance model that is⏎scaled to the organization's type, the tool’s intended use, and its risk profile, so that safety, fairness, and⏎transparency are upheld without creating barriers that inadvertently discourage the adoption of⏎beneficial technologies. A digital divide already exists specific to health IT between larger, more⏎resource-rich healthcare systems and providers working in smaller ambulatory practices. We believe⏎these smaller practices can measurably benefit from these technologies; supporting their adoption of⏎these tools should be a priority.⏎Fourth, increased AI adoption would be best supported through aligned payment policies that reward⏎clinical value, avoid burden imposition, and track total cost-of-care improvements. The RFI recognizes⏎that legacy fee-for-service regimes frequently do not include mechanisms that incite adoption of⏎innovative technologies, therefore slowing responsible uptake; modernized payment mechanisms that⏎reward validated outcomes would catalyze provider demand and responsible scale.⏎Finally, workforce readiness remains a practical factor for consideration, as it does for any innovative⏎but disruptive tool. For adoption of such a technology to be successful, clinicians and their staff need⏎tools that smoothly fit within EHR-enabled workflows, and they also rely on both training offerings and⏎organizational governance policies that allow them to confidently assume any associated risk. Provider⏎organizations and our collective clients have made it clear that there is a critical need to preserve⏎independent clinical judgment, including the ability to contextualize or override AI outputs. This aligns⏎with positions put forward by the EHR Association, which state that AI should augment, not replace,⏎clinician expertise. These conditions are essential to building trust, ensuring safety, and supporting⏎continuous improvement at the point of care.⏎RFI Question 2: Priority Regulatory, Payment, and Programmatic Changes⏎From the perspective of the EHR Association, the most impactful actions HHS can take to accelerate⏎responsible AI adoption involve establishing a clear, harmonized, and risk-based federal framework that⏎allows developers and providers to innovate with confidence while ensuring that patient safety, clinical⏎judgment, and system transparency remain foundational. The RFI underscores HHS’s intent to create a⏎regulatory posture that is predictable, proportionate to risk, and aligned with broader federal strategies⏎to accelerate AI adoption in healthcare, reduce uncertainty, and promote trust.⏎3⏎⏎In this context, we believe a formal federal framework specifically tailored to non-device clinical AI is⏎urgently needed. Such a framework should articulate expectations for documentation, transparency,⏎governance, and lifecycle monitoring while explicitly scaling requirements to the risk profile of the AI⏎tool and the size and capacity of the implementing organization. Overly prescriptive governance⏎structures can inadvertently burden smaller providers, and low-risk AI-supported tools, such as those⏎focused on simplifying administrative tasks or reducing the burden on support staff, should be right-⏎sized to support safe, equitable adoption. Given that many organizations have expressed a strong⏎interest in adopting those low-risk models, any steps HHS can take within its regulatory framework to⏎clearly differentiate between high- and low-risk AI implementations, with corresponding tiers of⏎regulatory requirements, would be welcome.⏎A critical priority is aligning and harmonizing federal oversight across ASTP/ONC, FDA, CMS, and OCR, as⏎well as attempting to harmonize the expanding patchwork of state laws regulating AI development and⏎use. The HHS RFI itself acknowledges the need for a “forward-leaning, industry-supportive approach”⏎that leverages regulation, reimbursement, and research to create clarity and accelerate adoption. Such⏎an approach would go a long way toward alleviating the uncertainty that, in some cases, is creating⏎hesitancy among both software developers and users.⏎The EHR Association strongly supports establishing shared definitions, common documentation artifacts,⏎and coordinated expectations across agencies, particularly for intended-use statements, required⏎performance information, and post-deployment monitoring responsibilities. Harmonization will⏎significantly reduce duplicative compliance activities and help align clinical workflow integration, safety⏎oversight, and privacy protections under a coherent national policy framework.⏎Payment modernization is also essential to ensuring that the value created by clinically validated AI tools⏎is recognized and rewarded. The EHR Association encourages CMS to explore targeted add-on⏎payments, AI-specific quality program credits, and pathways within alternative payment models (APMs)⏎to support deployment of AI tools that improve clinical outcomes, reduce administrative burden, or⏎enhance operational efficiency. Without clear, predictable financial signals, even highly effective tools⏎may struggle to gain traction among financially constrained or operationally stretched providers.⏎Finally, the EHR Association recommends that HHS increase federal investment in benchmarking⏎datasets, reference implementations, and safety testbeds to improve model transparency and external⏎validation. Investments in shared safety testbeds and common reporting templates would further⏎support trusted implementation and post-deployment monitoring across the industry.⏎Taken together, these regulatory, payment, and programmatic actions would significantly advance the⏎responsible adoption of AI in clinical care while protecting patient safety, ensuring equitable application⏎of data benefits to all patients, and rewarding clinician adoption of innovative technologies.⏎RFI Question 3: Novel Legal and Implementation Issues for Non-Medical-Device AI and HHS’s⏎Role⏎From the EHR Association’s perspective, the rapid expansion of AI tools that influence clinical care (but⏎do not fall under FDA regulation as medical devices) introduces a series of novel legal and operational⏎challenges that require thoughtful federal guidance. A central issue is the allocation of liability among⏎4⏎⏎developers, implementers, and clinicians when AI-generated recommendations inform care decisions.⏎Because these tools usually operate in advisory or assistive roles and do not constitute regulated⏎medical devices, the boundaries of responsibility can be ambiguous. To support safe deployment and⏎reduce legal uncertainty, the industry would benefit from model “duty of care” frameworks and safe-⏎harbor practices for documenting AI-informed clinical decisions, recognizing that the ultimate⏎responsibility for patient care ultimately rests with the authorizing clinician. Such structures would help⏎ensure that accountability aligns with real-world roles and capabilities while preserving clinicians’⏎independent judgment.⏎Another challenge arises in the contracting and indemnification frameworks needed for adaptive or⏎continuously learning AI systems. Traditional contracting models do not adequately address issues such⏎as performance warranties, update obligations, or the allocation of risks associated with model drift and⏎re-training. There is work necessary for the industry to collectively identify best practices that lay out⏎reasonable expectations for developers, implementers, and users, including shared responsibilities for⏎updates, logic and data transparency, ongoing training and performance monitoring.⏎Non-device AI systems also pose novel privacy and security complexities and challenges for⏎implementers to consider. While the applicability of and obligations under HIPAA Privacy and Security⏎Rules are clear and consistent with traditional software, the strategies implementers should deploy to⏎ensure compliance with those obligations are not always so clear. The industry as a whole could benefit⏎greatly from thorough guidance on best practices for privacy and security considerations and strategies⏎coming out of HHS in collaboration with federal partners, such as NIST.⏎Finally, the rise of AI-augmented clinical workflows raises workforce, credentialing, and scope-of-⏎practice considerations. As clinicians integrate AI outputs into care decisions, organizations need to⏎implement policies that clarify training, competency, and oversight expectations (and should make⏎those expectations clear to their software partners as well). These issues also intersect with CMS⏎Conditions of Participation, clinical quality measurement programs, and HRSA workforce development⏎initiatives, where HHS can play a direct role in ensuring both resources are sufficient and expectations⏎are clear. We also recommend that HHS consider investments in workforce training programs specific to⏎AI safety and efficiency.⏎Taken together, these legal and operational complexities underscore the importance of coordinated⏎federal leadership. By offering clear, consistent guidance on liability, contracting, privacy, and workforce⏎readiness, HHS can significantly reduce uncertainty, support safe and equitable adoption, and build the⏎foundation for responsible use of non-device AI tools across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎RFI Question 4: Promising evaluation methods for non-medical device AI and how HHS can⏎support them⏎Non-medical device AI tools benefit from evaluation methods that combine traditional validation with⏎real-world clinical usability and workflow safety assessments. While the industry is nascent with best⏎practices still evolving, it is logical that structured intended-use analysis, external validation across⏎representative patient populations, and human-factors testing in live or simulated EHR workflows are all⏎steps to consider as stakeholders determine their optimal processes. Post-deployment, ongoing⏎5⏎⏎monitoring for calibration, drift, fairness impacts, alert fatigue, and patient outcomes are all beneficial in⏎ensuring a safe deployment of health-related AI.⏎Because these evaluation practices vary in maturity across the industry, HHS could add value by funding⏎the development of common benchmarks, publishing reporting templates for industry consideration,⏎and supporting shared voluntary testbeds that would enable transparent, replicable assessment across⏎organizations and care settings.⏎RFI Question 5: Supporting private-sector accreditation, certification, and testing⏎Because there is no federal framework for AI development and use currently in place, many private-⏎sector EHR developers have already taken substantial steps to establish internal governance, testing,⏎and safety protocols. In some cases, our customers have communicated that they also require⏎safeguards and data testing before proceeding with adopting AI tools and functionality.⏎We believe the private sector will play an active role in the conversation specific to ideas about⏎voluntary accreditation, certification, and testing mechanisms for clinical AI. However, standardizing⏎expectations for model audit trails will help ensure that AI-supported decisions are consistent in key⏎ways across the industry, whether applied by EHR developers or independent AI model developers⏎without certified EHRs on the market. Such consistency would likely lead to increased confidence in AI⏎solutions and a more consistent framework for comparing different AI deployments across the⏎healthcare industry for key metrics of safety, accuracy, and efficiency.⏎RFI Question 6: Where AI has met or fallen short, and high-impact opportunities⏎We believe it is possible to accelerate provider adoption of AI innovations if the capabilities are paired⏎with payment models that reward validated clinical benefit.⏎RFI Question 7: Roles influencing adoption and primary administrative hurdles⏎Decisions about whether and how to adopt AI tools are influenced by a complex array of stakeholders⏎within healthcare organizations, including clinical leadership, IT and EHR teams, quality and safety⏎officers, compliance, and privacy teams, and financial or operational executives. Conversely, in smaller⏎organizations, the decision-making authority may be vested in a single individual or a small cohort of⏎clinicians. Despite interest, adoption is sometimes slowed by administrative hurdles, such as lengthy⏎procurement processes, security reviews, and uncertainty about what documentation would be⏎required of governance structures must be established.⏎RFI Question 8: Interoperability enhancements to widen opportunities⏎The EHR Association emphasizes that progress in AI adoption is directly linked to the quality and⏎consistency of interoperability across the health IT ecosystem. AI tools depend on accessibility to⏎structured, standards-based data to generate reliable and equitable predictions, but that must be⏎coupled with data provenance transparency and audit trails that capture data use and exchange for⏎purposes of ensuring sufficient monitoring capability. This would contribute to the expansion of⏎opportunities for innovation and improve the reliability of external validation efforts. Investments in⏎6⏎⏎national benchmarking datasets and standardized evaluation frameworks would further aid developers,⏎researchers, and providers in assessing model performance across diverse populations and⏎environments.⏎RFI Question 9: Patient and caregiver priorities and concerns⏎Patients and caregivers increasingly expect AI to be part of their care process, but they also sometimes⏎raise concerns about privacy, bias, and accountability when AI-related errors occur. Additionally, some⏎inquire about opting out of AI use in their care, and all are questions that healthcare providers must be⏎prepared to address to maintain public trust. Providers should provide clear, accessible communication⏎materials that help patients understand when and how AI is used in their care and what options they⏎have to ask questions, seek clarification, or raise concerns. HHS could also help by providing guidance on⏎plain-language communications about AI use for providers to help inform patients about AI use, support⏎opt-in/opt-out choices where feasible, and provide clear pathways for questions, appeals, and⏎remediation.⏎RFI Question 10: Priority areas for AI research and the literature on costs/benefits⏎The EHR Association encourages HHS to prioritize research efforts that address the elements most⏎critical to safe and equitable AI adoption: data and logic transparency, human-centered usability, real-⏎world safety evaluation, and performance monitoring. We also support investment in pragmatic trial⏎designs and implementation science approaches that measure AI’s true impact on outcomes, clinician⏎workload, equitable application of the technology, and total cost of care. Because cost-benefit analyses⏎and burden-reduction measures are still emerging fields in AI evaluation, federally supported research in⏎this area could help inform payment reform specific to AI use.⏎Conclusion⏎We appreciate HHS’s leadership in evaluating the best path to a forward-leaning, industry-supportive⏎approach to regulating healthcare AI. By affirming clinician primacy, adopting scalable risk-based⏎governance, enabling legitimate quality and safety uses of data, and harmonizing expectations across⏎federal programs, HHS can accelerate responsible AI adoption that improves outcomes, reduces clinician⏎and staff burden, and helps constrain systemic costs.⏎The EHR Association stands ready to collaborate on and support the safe, effective, and equitable⏎deployment of AI in health information technologies and across the healthcare system.⏎Sincerely,⏎Leigh Burchell Stephanie Jamison⏎Chair, EHR Association Vice Chair, EHR Association⏎Altera Digital Health Greenway Health⏎7⏎⏎HIMSS EHR Association Executive Committee⏎Steve Holt⏎Danielle Friend Steve Holt, JD⏎Epic PointClickCare⏎Michelle Knighton Ida Mantashi⏎NextGen Healthcare Modernizing Medicine⏎Shari Medina, MD⏎Harris Healthcare⏎Established in 2004, the Electronic Health Record (EHR) Association is comprised of 27 companies that supply the vast majority of EHRs to physicians’ practices⏎and hospitals across the United States. The EHR Association operates on the premise that the rapid, widespread adoption of EHRs will help improve the quality of⏎patient care as well as the productivity and sustainability of the healthcare system as a key enabler of healthcare transformation. The EHR Association and its⏎members are committed to supporting safe healthcare delivery, fostering continued innovation, and operating with high integrity in the market for our users and⏎their patients and families. The EHR Association is a partner of HIMSS. For more information, visit www.ehra.org.⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0461♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Shoemaker, Jerad, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Jerad♔Shoemaker♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Continuing Vannevar Bush’s Vision in Science, The Endless Frontier (1945) Bush⏎envisioned federal investment in basic research as an "endless frontier" driving progress,⏎especially in the "war against disease" through sustained support for medical and biological⏎inquiry. HHS's emerging role in accelerating AI adoption in clinical care extends this legacy—⏎providing foundational infrastructure (data "libraries") and standards to enable private-sector⏎innovation while upholding patient privacy, trust, and equity.⏎The government can best contribute by curating and maintaining large-scale, public-access⏎repositories and standards for AI development/deployment, like how NIH/ONC already supports⏎data ecosystems (e.g., via TEFCA's recent milestone of nearly 500 million records exchanged⏎and draft USCDI v7 expansions, alongside federated approaches in recent strategic plans). This⏎reduces barriers to entry for innovators, fosters competition, and aligns incentives for high-value⏎AI tools.⏎Additionally, HHS can reduce data variability at the point of care by elevating standards for⏎basic clinical documentation and measurement reliability—treating high-quality exam data as⏎essential 'fuel' for AI models. Data is the food on which AI thrives, and improving the quality of⏎medical exam data improves the fuel that AI models feed on.⏎Response to Question 1 (Barriers to Innovation and Adoption) A primary barrier is the⏎fragmentation and inaccuracy of medical data, which limits robust AI training and reliable⏎clinical deployment. Private innovators struggle with siloed, non-fungible datasets prone to⏎errors (e.g., subjective measurements, incomplete records). This echoes pre-ImageNet challenges⏎in computer vision, where lack of unified, large-scale labeled data stalled progress until⏎ImageNet (launched ~2009 with 14M+ images) catalyzed breakthroughs like AlexNet (2012)⏎and modern deep learning.⏎Response to Question 8 (Interoperability Opportunities) and Question 4 (Evaluation⏎Methods/Support) HHS should prioritize:⏎1. Expanded Medical Image Repository — Develop or fund a unified, large-scale⏎"ImageNet for Medicine" covering external body views, surgical/pathology/microscopy⏎images, radiology (e.g., expanding on existing public resources like MIMIC-CXR⏎[~377K chest X-rays with reports], CheXpert [~224K chest X-rays], NIH Chest X-ray⏎[112K images], and others). This would include multimodal data (video, thermal, depth)⏎for dynamic, touch-free exams, enabling better pre- and post-deployment evaluation,⏎robustness testing (e.g., temporal sequences, physiological ratios), and benchmarking.⏎HHS mechanisms like grants, cooperative agreements (e.g., CRADAs), or prize⏎competitions could accelerate this, building on NIH/ONC interoperability initiatives (e.g.,⏎USCDI expansions, TEFCA for federated access).⏎2. New Exam Data Standards for Fungibility and Reliability — Establish standards for⏎automated, low-error measurements (e.g., height/weight via sensors avoiding⏎postural/food biases; vital signs via remote photoplethysmography or pressure floors).⏎Incorporate time-series data and ratios (e.g., gait symmetry, respiratory effort over⏎sequences) to reduce subjectivity and enable high-confidence AI decisions. This would⏎expand market opportunities by making data interoperable across systems, fueling⏎Sensitivity: General Business Use. This document contains proprietary information and is intended for business use only.⏎⏎research (e.g., longitudinal baselines for precision medicine), and supporting novel tools⏎such as automated, touch-free physical exams (e.g., Exam OS concepts). HHS could⏎drive this via ONC standards updates and NIH R&D investments.⏎These steps would lower barriers, promote trustworthy AI, reduce costs (e.g., commoditizing⏎exams), and improve outcomes—directly advancing Bush's vision in the AI era. These⏎investments would accelerate the translation of AI from concept to bedside, echoing Bush's call⏎for federal support to unleash scientific progress in service of health.⏎Sensitivity: General Business Use. This document contains proprietary information and is intended for business use only.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0462♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Netsmart Technologies, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Netsmart Technologies, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)⏎and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC)⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Re: RIN 0955-AA13 – Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎Netsmart Technologies, Inc. (“Netsmart”) appreciates the opportunity to comment on the⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical Care. Netsmart is an industry-⏎leading healthcare technology organization empowering behavioral health and substance⏎use treatment providers, home & community-based providers, senior living providers, and⏎specialty practice providers to deliver value-based care to the individuals and communities⏎they serve nationwide.⏎The Netsmart CareFabric® platform serves as a unified, connected framework of solutions⏎and services for human services, post-acute, specialty practices, payer, and public sector⏎communities. We develop and deliver innovative technology, including electronic health⏎records (EHRs), interoperability solutions, analytics, augmented intelligence (AI),⏎population health management and telehealth solutions and services that assist⏎organizations in transforming the care they deliver. Our experience and client base provide⏎a pragmatic, on-the-ground perspective on the use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) in our⏎communities across care settings, and we appreciate the opportunity to share that insight.⏎GENERAL COMMENTARY⏎Netsmart supports a harmonized federal framework that reduces fragmentation by⏎preempting or aligning state rules, protects patients, and enables responsible innovation.⏎We also support payment and programmatic reforms that reimburse high‑value AI use⏎cases where evidence demonstrates improved quality, access, and efficiency across clinical⏎and administrative workflows.⏎A federal framework is necessary to reduce state‑by‑state fragmentation that imposes⏎significant compliance burdens on providers, and Health IT developers alike. Netsmart⏎supports a federal framework that distinguishes operational tools (e.g., scheduling and⏎⏎summarization) from clinical tools (e.g., influencing diagnosis or treatment). Standardized⏎transparency requirements that build trust and enable accountability, such as AI model⏎cards, would help improve clinician and patient confidence.⏎Netsmart supports defined reimbursement pathways for AI‑enabled services that⏎measurably reduce burden and improve care quality, including ambient listening,⏎AI‑assisted clinical documentation, and automated back-office functions, among other use⏎cases. Payment policies should recognize multi-disciplinary benefits spanning clinicians,⏎administrative staff, billing teams, and care coordinators. Permanent reimbursement⏎pathways could be modeled on telehealth precedents, with safeguards for documentation⏎integrity, audit readiness, and human-in-the-loop oversight. We support aligning⏎reimbursement with value‑based care objectives, including the use of AI to reduce⏎avoidable utilization, improve care coordination, and fulfill discrete requirements under⏎programs such as Chronic Care Management, where tasks can be automated yet remain⏎verifiable and clinically supervised.⏎Netsmart supports targeted federal investment in AI research and development in⏎behavioral health and post‑acute care, where fragmentation and privacy constraints have⏎slowed progress. We support the use of de‑identified datasets (including registries, vital⏎records, and vetted public health data) for model development and validation. We oppose⏎policies that over‑restrict lawful secondary use of appropriately de‑identified data in ways⏎that unduly hinder innovation, and we support funding via grants, prospective payment⏎schedules or Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation (CMMI) models to reduce the⏎operational costs of AI-enabled tools and to advance health‑care‑specific models.⏎SPECIFIC QUESTIONS⏎WHAT ARE THE BIGGEST BARRIERS TO PRIVATE SECTOR INNOVATION IN AI FOR HEALTH CARE AND⏎ITS ADOPTION AND USE IN CLINICAL CARE?⏎The primary barriers to innovation in AI for healthcare and the adoption and use of AI in⏎clinical care include cost, regulatory fragmentation, limited trust and clinician acceptance,⏎unclear consent and authorization responsibilities, and constrained access to diverse⏎deidentified data. A state‑by‑state framework creates inconsistent obligations for data⏎masking, consent, and AI operations, which materially complicates compliance and⏎workflow requirements for health IT developers as well as clinicians operating across⏎multiple jurisdictions. There is also the potential for clinician skepticism in the absence of⏎transparency and human-in-the-loop oversight.⏎⏎WHAT REGULATORY, PAYMENT POLICY, OR PROGRAMMATIC DESIGN CHANGES SHOULD HHS⏎PRIORITIZE TO INCENTIVIZE THE EFFECTIVE USE OF AI IN CLINICAL CARE AND WHY? WHAT HHS⏎REGULATIONS, POLICIES, OR PROGRAMS COULD BE REVISITED TO AUGMENT YOUR ABILITY TO⏎DEVELOP OR USE AI IN CLINICAL CARE? PLEASE PROVIDE SPECIFIC CHANGES AND APPLICABLE CODE⏎OF FEDERAL REGULATIONS CITATIONS.⏎For ambient listening and AI-assisted documentation ("ambient scribe") which has already⏎proven its efficacy, we support HHS funding the deployment and implementation of AI⏎tools via expanded reimbursement pathways within prospective payment schedules and⏎other billing methodologies and the creation of grant funding for one-time expenses. The⏎use of technology, such as ambient listening and ambient scribe in clinical care settings,⏎should be reimbursed to incentivize adoption. AI-enabled services support enhanced care⏎delivery and a reduction in administrative burden on providers.⏎FOR NON-MEDICAL DEVICES, WE UNDERSTAND THAT USE OF AI IN CLINICAL CARE MAY RAISE NOVEL⏎LEGAL AND IMPLEMENTATION ISSUES THAT CHALLENGE EXISTING GOVERNANCE AND⏎ACCOUNTABILITY STRUCTURES (E.G., RELATING TO LIABILITY, INDEMNIFICATION, PRIVACY, AND⏎SECURITY). WHAT NOVEL LEGAL AND IMPLEMENTATION ISSUES EXIST AND WHAT ROLE, IF ANY,⏎SHOULD HHS PLAY TO HELP ADDRESS THEM?⏎We recommend HHS develop guidance addressing the risks of using general-purpose AI⏎tools outside approved systems, clarifying responsibility and minimum security and⏎privacy expectations. Specifically, HHS should issue guidance that clearly distinguishes⏎consumer‑facing mental health chatbots from clinical AI tools used within regulated care⏎settings, recognizing that these technologies serve different functions, have different end⏎users and present materially different risk profiles.⏎Mental health chatbots are typically designed to provide general wellness support, and⏎engagement directly to consumers and operate outside formal clinical workflows of a⏎regulated provider organization, without clinician supervision or integration into certified⏎health IT systems. By contrast, clinical AI tools are deployed within covered entities,⏎integrated into electronic health records or enterprise platforms, and used by professionals⏎who are trained in their use, to support documentation, care coordination, or clinical⏎decision‑making under established privacy, security, and accountability frameworks.⏎The Clinical AI tools developed by Netsmart are used by clinicians and are not designed to⏎replace them. HHS guidance should clarify that mental health chatbots should not be⏎treated as clinical AI unless they influence diagnosis, treatment, documentation, or care⏎decisions, at which point they should be subject to corresponding clinical governance,⏎transparency, and human‑oversight expectations. Establishing functional boundaries based⏎on use and context would reduce provider confusion, protect patients, and support⏎responsible AI adoption across behavioral health and mental health settings.⏎⏎FOR NON-MEDICAL DEVICES, WHAT ARE THE MOST PROMISING AI EVALUATION METHODS (PRE-⏎AND POST-DEPLOYMENT), METRICS, ROBUSTNESS TESTING, AND OTHER WORKFLOW AND HUMAN-⏎CENTERED EVALUATION METHODS FOR CLINICAL CARE? SHOULD HHS FURTHER SUPPORT THESE⏎PROCESSES? IF SO, WHICH MECHANISMS WOULD BE MOST IMPACTFUL (E.G., CONTRACTS, GRANTS,⏎COOPERATIVE AGREEMENTS, AND/OR PRIZE COMPETITIONS)?⏎We support establishing federal evaluation baselines for non‑device clinical AI grounded in⏎the NIST AI Risk Management Framework. Adoption of a federal framework would also⏎present an opportunity to drive alignment across agencies (e.g., CMS, ASTP/ONC, SAMHSA)⏎on adverse-event reporting, and real-world data collection.⏎We support funding mechanisms, such as grants, and cooperative agreements targeted to⏎administrative burden reduction, documentation quality, reimbursement efficiency, and⏎use cases in behavioral health and post‑acute care. CMMI has an Innovations in Behavioral⏎Health (IBH) Demonstration Notice of Funding Opportunity (NOFO) currently open for⏎solicitation. We recommend CMMI make the AI-enabled behavioral health use cases eligible⏎for immediate reimbursement under the IBH model. This would drive participation in the⏎model and adoption of clinical AI in behavioral health. Furthermore, HHS should consider⏎making behavioral health, post-acute care, and specialty providers of all types eligible for⏎contracts, grants, or cooperative agreements to encourage adoption of clinical AI tools⏎across all sectors of healthcare. We are at a critical juncture of healthcare, and all types of⏎providers should have access to reimbursement mechanisms to enable them to provide the⏎highest quality, most efficient care to all patient populations in the United States.⏎HOW CAN HHS BEST SUPPORT PRIVATE SECTOR ACTIVITIES (E.G., ACCREDITATION,⏎CERTIFICATION, INDUSTRY-DRIVEN TESTING, AND CREDENTIALING) TO PROMOTE INNOVATIVE AND⏎EFFECTIVE AI USE IN CLINICAL CARE?⏎Netsmart supports a federal framework adopted consistently across HHS, and its sub-⏎agencies. Additionally, we support the adoption of baseline guardrails related to topics⏎such as safety, transparency, permissible data use, auditability, and human-in-the-loop⏎oversight. We recommend HHS continue to invest in research and development in all⏎sectors of healthcare. We welcome collaboration with HHS and its sub-agencies as you⏎analyze potential use cases for AI in clinical care, especially as it relates to use cases in⏎behavioral health, substance use, post-acute, and long-term care as well specialty practice⏎settings such as physical and occupational therapy.⏎⏎WHERE HAVE AI TOOLS DEPLOYED IN CLINICAL CARE MET OR EXCEEDED PERFORMANCE AND COST⏎EXPECTATIONS AND WHERE HAVE THEY FALLEN SHORT? WHAT KINDS OF NOVEL AI TOOLS WOULD⏎HAVE THE GREATEST POTENTIAL TO IMPROVE HEALTH, CARE OUTCOMES, GIVE NEW INSIGHTS ON⏎QUALITY, AND HELP REDUCE COSTS?⏎Ambient listening and documentation have demonstrated robust performance value by⏎improving note quality, reducing administrative burden, and enhancing patient‑clinician⏎interaction. 22% of Netsmart behavioral health clients leverage Bells Documentation⏎Assistant, a generative AI solution that helps clinicians create accurate, complete notes⏎faster and reduce documentation time. Users completed 7.2 million notes last year, cutting⏎documentation time by up to 67%. Leveraging technology like this across all Behavioral⏎Health providers in the US could save millions of hours annually. 7% of Netsmart⏎behavioral health clients leverage Bells Virtual Scribe, an ambient AI solution that listens,⏎transcribes and drafts notes in near real time to reduce administrative burden and support⏎patient-focused care. More than 65,000 scribe sessions were completed last year.⏎We have heard directly from clients how these AI-enabled solutions have reduced⏎administrative burden, streamlined efficiencies, and improved organizational satisfaction.⏎Widespread adoption of these solutions is constrained by funding and reimbursement⏎uncertainty, workforce change management, and variable organizational readiness. We⏎support the development of federal grants and pilots to offset startup costs and to generate⏎publicly available evidence. High‑value innovations in AI include tools that reduce⏎administrative burden, strengthen revenue cycle performance, generate accurate referral⏎and handoff summaries, streamline intake, and improve care coordination, with public⏎reporting emphasizing non‑proprietary evidence.⏎Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What⏎are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Within provider organizations, adoption decisions are commonly influenced by executive⏎leadership (e.g., CEOs/COOs), clinical leaders (e.g., CMIOs/CNOs), IT leadership (e.g.,⏎CIOs/CTOs), compliance and privacy officers, and finance leaders (e.g., CFOs) given the⏎importance of reimbursement and operational return on investment. The principal hurdles⏎related to the adoption of AI in clinical care include funding streams providers face for the⏎implementation of AI, CMS reimbursement uncertainty, fragmented data stewardship⏎obligations, and conflicting state policies. We support the adoption of a federal framework⏎with accepted standards, and reimbursement pathways to enhance healthcare providers’⏎confidence in the investment and utilization of AI in clinical care settings. To this end, the⏎proposed removal of standard model card data via the HTI-5 proposed rule reduces⏎⏎certainty, inhibits transparency of data, and inadvertently creates greater confusion for⏎providers trying to understand the breadth and scope of the tools available to them.⏎WHERE WOULD ENHANCED INTEROPERABILITY WIDEN MARKET OPPORTUNITIES, FUEL RESEARCH,⏎AND ACCELERATE THE DEVELOPMENT OF AI FOR CLINICAL CARE? PLEASE CONSIDER SPECIFIC DATA⏎TYPES, DATA STANDARDS, AND BENCHMARKING TOOLS.⏎Enhanced interoperability is essential to expanding access to data for AI training,⏎validation, and benchmarking. The ONC certification program aims to advance secure⏎access, exchange, and use of EHI through standardized Application Programming Interfaces⏎(APIs). In addition, the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA)⏎establishes a nationwide governance and connectivity approach for network-to-network⏎exchange. CMS Aligned Networks are pushing interoperability forward by advancing and⏎expanding the standardized exchange of data. We encourage the administration to continue⏎to focus not only on traditional healthcare settings e.g., hospital, and primary care, but also⏎behavioral health/mental health, post-acute care, long-term care and specialty practice⏎settings and the correlating data. The aforementioned initiatives can reduce fragmentation⏎and help enable secure, scalable pathways for the data access needed to evaluate AI, but⏎only if all patient populations and care settings are included. These community-based⏎providers are often at the bottom of the healthcare funding food chain, and do not have the⏎resources a hospital may have to invest in interoperability, yet they are treating some of⏎the most acute patients in healthcare. Helping to fund their integration with the rest of⏎healthcare providers would greatly improve coordination of care.⏎We recommend continued focus on advancing data standards to enable AI-generated⏎summaries (a common use case for AI in clinical care) to allow for reliable integration into⏎referral, admission, and financial intake workflows. The United States Core Data for⏎Interoperability (USCDI) establishes a standardized set of interoperable data elements, and⏎HL7 Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR)/US Core provide implementable⏎profiles and APIs that support consistent exchange and ingestion. HHS should also expand⏎access to high-quality public health and population-level data, including immunization⏎registries, which the Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) describes as⏎confidential population-based systems supporting both point-of-care histories and⏎population surveillance, enabling privacy-preserving research and evaluation across the⏎continuum.⏎ARE THERE SPECIFIC AREAS OF AI RESEARCH THAT HHS SHOULD PRIORITIZE TO ACCELERATE THE⏎ADOPTION OF AI AS PART OF CLINICAL CARE?⏎We support HHS investment in behavioral health and post-acute care use cases, de-⏎identification modernization, population health analytics, registry integration, and⏎strategies to reduce the operational costs of large language models and related⏎⏎infrastructure. We support research on systematic evidence reviews that quantify the costs⏎and benefits associated with AI deployment in clinical workflows.⏎CONCLUSION⏎Netsmart appreciates HHS’s leadership in advancing responsible AI adoption in clinical⏎care. We welcome continued engagement with ASTP/ONC and other HHS sub-agencies as⏎the agency develops practical guardrails, evaluation approaches, and reimbursement⏎pathways that protect patients while enabling innovation, particularly in behavioral health,⏎post-acute care, long-term care, and specialty practice settings.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0463♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Medical Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Medical Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 17, 2026⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Principal Deputy National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎The Office of the National Coordinator for Health IT⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C Street, SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Principal Deputy Posnack:⏎On behalf of the physician and medical student members of the American Medical Association (AMA), I⏎appreciate the opportunity to provide feedback to the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎(HHS) on its Request for Information regarding accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence⏎(AI) as part of clinical care. The AMA commends the Administration for its consideration and⏎commitment to leading the charge in exploring how AI technologies present unique opportunities in⏎health care to enhance care delivery, strengthen patient-provider relationships, reduce administrative⏎burdens for clinicians, and empower patients to navigate the health care system better and get the care⏎they need. Currently, however, the unresolved questions about liability, regulatory authority, patient⏎safety, and state oversight are converging, making AI governance one of the most consequential issues⏎facing health law and medical practice. A growing liability vacuum has emerged as AI developers⏎increasingly disclaim responsibility for errors or harm, even as clinicians are expected to rely on these⏎tools in clinical and administrative decision-making. Regulatory ambiguity, unresolved coverage and⏎reimbursement questions, data privacy risks, and limited public trust are inhibiting rapid adoption,⏎highlighting the need to modernize oversight frameworks that were not designed for the scale, speed, or⏎complexity of AI-enabled care.⏎The AMA asserts that foundational concepts, measures, and principles are crucial for the successful⏎integration of AI into the health care system and the practice of medicine. Physicians must play a central⏎role in the ethical development, deployment, and utilization of AI technologies in health care, as their⏎clinical expertise is indispensable in ensuring these tools are safe, effective, and trustworthy. Establishing⏎strong digital health foundations, including telehealth infrastructure, robust privacy and security⏎protections, and seamless interoperability, is critical to enabling this transformation. When implemented⏎responsibly, AI-powered technologies hold significant potential to enhance patient-centered care, improve⏎clinical outcomes, and reduce costs.⏎The AMA has included key areas of consideration:⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 2⏎Regulation of Health Care AI⏎The AMA appreciates HHS’ interest in continuing to evaluate regulatory approaches to health care AI. As⏎you know, appropriate oversight structures for health care AI will play a significant role in ensuring AI⏎with clinical applications is safe and efficacious, promotes innovation, and, most importantly, builds trust⏎between developers, physicians, and patients. In a survey of over 1,000 physicians across specialties and⏎practice settings, appropriate regulatory oversight was identified as one of the top needs of physicians⏎when considering whether to integrate AI into their practice.⏎The AMA has long supported movement towards a harmonized national governance approach for health⏎care AI. This type of approach has, to date, been lacking and would provide developers, deployers,⏎physicians and patients with clarity and consistency in a way that can help enhance understanding and⏎build much needed trust in AI-enabled health care technologies. At a basic level, terminology has not⏎been harmonized across the federal government, with agencies such as the U.S. Food and Drug⏎Administration (FDA), the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National⏎Coordinator for Health Information Technology , the Office for Civil Rights (OCR), and the Centers for⏎Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) all utilizing very different terminology to describe what frequently⏎are many of the same technologies and functions. Stakeholders including industry participants,⏎physicians, and patients have long advocated for consistent regulatory terminology across HHS to help⏎alleviate confusion and provide consistency across agencies. We would recommend HHS consider this⏎need for harmonization as they move forward with any updated regulatory approach towards health care⏎AI.⏎At a higher level, the AMA has consistently advocated that regulation of health care AI, particularly AI⏎with clinical applications, should follow a risk-based approach. There are likely a number of AI-enabled⏎technologies that pose a low risk to patient health. These likely do not need stringent regulatory scrutiny⏎before they come to market. However, AI that poses higher levels of risk to patients needs appropriate⏎regulatory scrutiny to ensure it is safe and performs as promised with appropriate guardrails. Every AI-⏎enabled technology that impacts medical decision-making should be required to have a rigorous post-⏎market surveillance plan to help ensure continued performance. Developers and deployers of these⏎technologies should be able to identify algorithmic drift and ensure their algorithms are not introducing⏎bias at any point in time. FDA-regulated device software functions, even those falling under enforcement⏎discretion, should have mandated labeling requirements to help provide the end user with essential⏎transparency about the product in question. The AMA feels strongly that a clear, consistent, risk-based⏎approach to regulation in lieu of a significant deregulatory approach is required to enhance trust in⏎AI and successfully move towards increasing clinical integration.⏎Transparency⏎A critical element of successful risk-mitigation that can help to enhance trust in AI is transparency. The⏎AMA recommends that certain AI tools be subject to transparency mandates that clearly outline the⏎intended use of the tool, how to use the tool, what risks and limitations may exist, and other elements. For⏎AI with clinical applications, AMA policy recommends that specific elements be disclosed to physicians⏎so they can make informed decisions about purchasing and using AI-enabled tools in practice. For⏎regulated software device functions, this could come in the form of a labeling requirement. We were⏎pleased to see the FDA’s January 2025 draft guidance titled “Artificial Intelligence-Enabled Device⏎Software Functions: Lifecycle Management and Marketing Submission Recommendations” includes⏎recommendations for updated labeling appropriate for software device functions, including AI. We urge⏎the Administration to finalize guidance regarding labeling requirements as it continues to evaluate its⏎approach to AI regulation. Furthermore, we urge the FDA to recommend that this apply to software⏎device functions for which they plan to exercise enforcement discretion. For tools like clinical decision⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 3⏎support software, where physicians will need to be able to review the basis for the software’s⏎recommendations, a consistent approach to transparency is essential.⏎Transparency plays an essential role in helping physicians successfully navigate AI use in their practice.⏎Transparency is necessary to help physicians understand the product they are using and select the right⏎tool for use with the right patient at the right time. While we expect that some physicians will develop a⏎significant amount of expertise in AI over time, expecting all physicians to have a deep understanding of⏎how AI-enabled technologies function is likely unreasonable. This is increasingly important for⏎physicians in small practices or underserved areas who will not have the evaluation resources that large⏎health systems and hospitals do. Because of this, developers and deployers should provide information⏎about their products in a way that is understandable to physicians of all levels of experience with AI. We⏎also suggest a standardized format for disclosures across industry participants. A consistent approach to⏎transparency makes it easier for all physicians to understand and compare technologies, helping them⏎make better choices about which tools they engage with in their practice.⏎While the FDA has a large role to play in ensuring transparency for software device functions, other⏎technologies in the health care space should provide some level of transparency as well. While FDA⏎authority does not extend to many administrative AI tools, direct-to-consumer chatbots and other wellness⏎applications, for example, the AMA believes it is important that certain information is disclosed to users⏎of those tools. These lower-risk tools do not likely need to provide the significant level of transparency⏎that would be required of a medical device. However, it is important that end users, including consumers,⏎understand the tool’s intended use, its limitations, and that the user is not interacting with a human. The⏎Federal Trade Commission (FTC) would be the most appropriate regulatory body to address transparency⏎issues related to consumer protection. While we understand that FTC’s work is not under the purview of⏎HHS directly, we encourage HHS to work with FTC to consider how they might provide guidance to⏎industry, physicians, and patients regarding transparency requirements furthering consumer protections.⏎Regulation of Consumer-Facing Autonomous AI⏎New direct-to-consumer health care technologies are also being introduced to consumers at a rapid pace.⏎These technologies hold significant promise to help increase patient understanding of their own health⏎and wellness, improve health literacy, and potentially expand access to care by making certain services⏎more accessible and affordable. However, many of these technologies will seek to operate in an⏎autonomous manner, including many AI chatbots and AI agents. These technologies are incredibly⏎promising but also carry risks that we should work to mitigate.⏎Autonomous AI will need appropriate guardrails, oversight, and transparency. Consumers should have a⏎very clear understanding that they are not interacting with a human through regulatory mandates requiring⏎clear disclosure that a patient is interacting with AI. Transparency mandates should include prohibitions⏎on any claims or insinuations that a chatbot or agent is a licensed clinician or can otherwise provide the⏎same services as licensed clinicians. Regulators, industry, physicians, and patients should work together⏎to develop clear and enforceable guardrails that mitigate risks and establish the appropriate scope of⏎activities in which autonomous AI can safely be a participant. The AMA appreciates and supports the⏎FTC’s investigation into AI chatbots raising mental health concerns and encourages HHS to work with⏎Congress and the FTC to ensure appropriate guardrails are in place to mitigate harm to consumers that⏎may result from engaging with these technologies.⏎AI “Deep Fakes” and Impersonation of Physicians⏎Advances in generative AI now allow realistic impersonation of physicians through synthetic images,⏎audio, and video (“deepfakes”). When used maliciously or without consent, these tools can spread⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 4⏎medical misinformation, damage physicians’ reputations and careers, and undermine patient trust in⏎evidence-based care. Existing privacy, employment, and intellectual property laws do not adequately⏎address these risks in clinical contexts.⏎This is about patient safety, not technology restrictions. Medical deepfakes can convince patients to⏎follow harmful advice, delay care, or abandon evidence-based treatment. Consumers cannot reasonably⏎distinguish real clinicians from AI-generated impersonations without clear protections and labeling.⏎Patients rely on trust signals they cannot independently verify, such as a physician’s voice, image, and⏎credentials, which function as trusted cues for consumers. Deepfakes exploit those cues, creating a⏎uniquely high-risk form of consumer deception in health care.⏎When considering this issue, we urge the Administration to work with Congress in developing protections⏎that focus on key elements that protect the patient/consumer:⏎• Preventing impersonation reduces health misinformation at the source.⏎• Shared responsibility protects consumers more effectively.⏎• Deepfake misuse disproportionately harms vulnerable patients.⏎• Transparency supports innovation and trust.⏎• Patients benefit from default protections.⏎• Protecting physicians ultimately protects patients.⏎Physician Liability for Use of AI⏎The AMA is pleased to see HHS’ acknowledgment that the use of AI in clinical care raises novel and⏎complex legal questions for patients, physicians, hospitals, health systems, and malpractice insurers to⏎contemplate. While HHS has inquired about legal issues surrounding non-device AI, the issue of liability⏎crosses both device and non-device AI-enabled technologies. The issue of liability is particularly⏎important to physicians as they begin to engage with AI in their practices in a more meaningful way. In⏎the AMA’s AI Physician Sentiment Survey, respondents identified concerns around liability for use of AI⏎as one of their top concerns when considering whether to implement these technologies into their⏎practices. The question of who is responsible for medical errors that involve the use of AI is currently⏎without precedent in case law, and no prevailing legal frameworks exist to address the issue, making it a⏎difficult situation for physicians to navigate and one that could have significant implications for the⏎successful clinical integration of AI-enabled technologies. The use of AI that impacts patient-physician⏎medical decision-making does not fall neatly within current legal constructs, such as approaches to⏎product liability. Should physicians bear a disproportionate amount of risk of liability for medical errors⏎where poorly performing AI played a role in medical decision-making, it could act as a disincentive to use⏎the tools in clinical care. Increases in liability risks for physicians may also impact malpractice insurance,⏎potentially raising premiums, increasing deductibles, or resulting in policy riders that specifically include⏎or exclude certain uses of AI.⏎As we move toward more personalized AI, it may ultimately become a meaningful participant in medical⏎decision-making. AI that can capably evaluate personalized health information, including information⏎extracted from individual medical records, diagnostic results, images, personal health data from⏎wearables, etc., presents a situation in which human physicians and algorithms are together engaged in a⏎new type of shared decision-making. While these capabilities will represent a significant step towards⏎realizing AI’s potential to improve clinical care, they also present a novel risk to physicians and⏎patients—what if the AI is wrong? If an AI tool is marketed as capable of performing accurately in a way⏎that a physician cannot, it can play a valuable role. However, if the AI returns an incorrect output, that⏎error can have serious consequences for patients if it contributes to misdiagnosis or improper treatment. In⏎this scenario, who should bear responsibility and any resulting harm?⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 5⏎In scenarios where AI will contribute to diagnosis and care decisions, AMA policy states that the⏎developer of that technology should be accountable for its role in care decisions that are impacted by its⏎performance. This is especially true when the algorithms underlying the technologies are “black box,”⏎meaning that physicians and patients cannot fully understand the logic or rationale leading to the outputs.⏎If a reasonable physician did not know, or did not have reason to know, that an AI error had occurred⏎when it should have been reasonable to rely on the outputs of a particular technology, the developer of the⏎technology should be accountable to patients for the resulting harm. Should developers of AI technologies⏎have no accountability for the performance of their tool, there will be very little incentive for physicians⏎to meaningfully engage with them, knowing they bear all the risk for its performance. Claims that⏎physicians should never rely on the outputs of the technology would seem to obviate the need for utilizing⏎the tool in the first place or would result in utilizing them as a confirmatory tool only.⏎Physicians will still bear the burden of ensuring they are appropriately educated in the selection and use⏎of AI technologies and are validating the outputs to the extent possible. Additionally, should the physician⏎be negligent in tool selection and use, such as selecting the wrong tool, using it on the wrong patient, or⏎using it outside its intended use, they should be accountable for those actions. However, it may not be⏎possible for physicians to validate the outputs of more complex, black box AI. At some point, physicians⏎must be able to trust that the AI they are engaging with works and performs as promised, given that, with⏎complex machine learning algorithms, not even its own developers can always easily ascertain where⏎mistakes are made and how the algorithms arrive at certain decisions.⏎Another complicated scenario that may arise is the question of what risks physicians face if they do not⏎utilize AI, or if, using their best judgment, they disagree with the AI’s assessment, and harm results. This⏎presents another complex legal scenario with no solid precedent. Assigning liability to physicians on the⏎basis that they should not depend on AI outputs and must rely on their independent judgment, while also⏎exposing them to penalties for doing so, effectively places them in an untenable, no‑win position.⏎The AMA believes that AI developers should always be accountable for their performance, as physicians⏎are accountable for theirs. Regulatory structures will play an important role in apportioning liability and⏎serve to help provide assurances of safety and performance, which can play a significant role in⏎mitigating risks to patients and enhancing trust in AI. They also set legal standards that a developer must⏎meet to be deemed appropriate for use in providing care. A lack of regulatory standards for AI impacting⏎clinical care results in physicians potentially shouldering the entirety of the risk of harm, should the AI⏎perform poorly and create a situation where a developer is not accountable for the performance of its⏎product. A lack of regulatory oversight, guardrails, or other development standards can increase safety⏎risks and can create additional liability risks when it is noted that physicians are using unregulated tools.⏎For example, the recent final guidance on Clinical Decision Support (CDS) Software outlines that the⏎FDA plans to exercise “enforcement discretion” for a wide range of CDS software, clearly noting that⏎physicians are responsible for independently reviewing and validating the results. It also states that⏎physicians should not rely on the algorithmic outputs as the basis of their decision. While the AMA does⏎not believe that all CDS tools need stringent oversight and appreciates the exercise of enforcement⏎discretion only if the tool offers some level of transparency, the guidance essentially expects physicians to⏎utilize these tools while also creating a situation in which the tools are not necessarily accountable for⏎their performance.⏎The AMA expects the liability framework to evolve over time as clinical AI-enabled technologies⏎improve and are incorporated into the standard of care. As the standard of care increasingly contemplates⏎the use of AI, liability may become more clearly defined, provided there is broad agreement that AI⏎should be trustworthy and accountable for its performance and potential impact on clinical care decisions.⏎Even with greater clarity regarding the standard of care with respect to AI, we expect legal questions⏎involving the use of AI in clinical care to continue to be complex. Determining causations in medical⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 6⏎malpractice cases is already difficult. Including the input of AI into clinical care adds an additional layer⏎of complexity to this already challenging task.⏎Interoperability to Accelerate AI in Clinical Care⏎Enhanced interoperability can meaningfully widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate safe⏎AI deployment when it enables reliable, standards-based access to high quality clinical data across⏎settings without shifting new workflow, liability, or privacy risk onto physicians and patients. The biggest⏎upside comes from making it easier for physicians to use AI as a native part of clinical care, embedded⏎within electronic health record (EHR) workflows and grounded in trusted, interoperable data, rather than⏎forcing physicians to step outside the EHR into separate tools.⏎Interoperability is most catalytic where AI depends on longitudinal, computable, and timely data,⏎especially at points of care transition and high administrative friction. Priority data types include problem⏎lists/diagnoses, medications, allergies, labs and vitals, imaging reports and key findings, procedures and⏎care plans, referrals and consult notes, discharge summaries, and relevant outcomes data. These data are⏎available from both inside the four walls of the clinic and from wearables, remote patient monitoring, and⏎hospital-at-home environments.⏎Equally important are administrative and operational data that can drive burden and delays: prior⏎authorization status and payer coverage rules. Application programing interface (API) enabled access can⏎reduce redundant documentation and enable AI to generate more context aware recommendations that fit⏎clinical workflows.⏎Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (FHIR) based APIs can be the practical bridge between EHRs⏎and trustworthy AI if they are implemented consistently, support writeback where appropriate, and are⏎paired with clear governance and accountability. HHS should prioritize an ecosystem in which:⏎• Patients can access and share their data through standardized APIs with meaningful controls and⏎transparency.⏎• Physicians and care teams can access AI relevant data without manual chart abstraction and can⏎integrate AI tools into clinical workflow with minimal clicks and redocumentation.⏎• AI developers can compete on performance and safety (not bespoke EHR integrations) by⏎building against consistent FHIR profiles and implementation guidance, reducing integration⏎costs and vendor lock in.⏎Interoperability must move beyond “data movement” to “data meaning.” HHS should emphasize⏎consistent use of standardized, clinically validated code sets and terminologies so AI models train⏎and operate on comparable inputs across settings. As examples, core clinical concepts should be⏎expressed in widely adopted standards (e.g., diagnoses/problems, labs, medications, procedures) and⏎mapped in ways that are auditable and maintain clinical fidelity. Without strong semantic standardization,⏎AI performance will vary unpredictably across sites, undermining physician trust and increasing patient⏎risk.⏎Expanded API access increases the attack surface and magnifies the risk of inappropriate secondary use of⏎sensitive health data, especially when third parties may not be regulated under The Health Insurance⏎Portability and Accountability Act in the same way as covered entities. HHS should caution against⏎interoperability policies that are too loose or overly deferential to developers, and instead pair data⏎access with enforceable protections. Needed are strong security by default expectations for API⏎implementations, meaningful patient controls, limits on data use beyond what patients authorize, and clear⏎accountability for downstream entities that receive data. FTC and OCR enforcement should be leveraged⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 7⏎to deter unfair/deceptive data practices and to ensure privacy and security protections remain credible as⏎data flows increase. HHS should consider adaptations to the Health Information Technology for⏎Economic and Clinical Health Act’s Business Associate Agreement policies to include baseline⏎transparency, safety, and security requirements for all third-party AI tools with access to protected⏎health information.⏎Clinical care infrastructure⏎Independent practices operate with lean staffing and limited information technology capacity, yet they are⏎expected to navigate increasingly complex health care administration rules, quality reporting programs,⏎documentation requirements, and multi-payer administrative workflows. When administrative overhead⏎expands, the immediate consequences are predictable: less time with patients, delayed services, and⏎reduced capacity to take new patients, particularly in rural communities.⏎Properly deployed, AI can help practices spend less time on administrative tasks that detract from⏎delivering care. The Administration can accelerate this transformation by aligning federal policy⏎levers around three goals:⏎1. Make AI integrate into routine workflows (not separate portals or “shadow processes” that add⏎clicks).⏎2. Enable competition on performance and burden reduction by reducing integration barriers and⏎developer lock-in.⏎3. Protect trust with baseline privacy, security, and transparency expectations proportionate to risk.⏎AI has the potential to reduce burden in several high friction areas that consume disproportionate time in⏎independent practices today. For example, AI enabled documentation support can help reduce duplicative⏎data entry and improve accuracy and compliance without encouraging “note bloat” that obscures clinical⏎meaning. Similarly, AI can streamline eligibility and benefits verification by reducing the back and forth⏎required to confirm coverage details, improving point of care clarity for both patients and staff. AI can⏎lessen manual chart abstraction and rework by converting routine clinical documentation into structured,⏎reusable elements that support quality reporting requirements with fewer additional steps.⏎AI driven burden reduction is only possible with consistent, computable access to clinical and⏎administrative data elements. Enhanced interoperability, especially through reliable, standards-based API⏎driven exchange can lower integration costs, reduce reliance on bespoke interfaces, and enable more⏎innovators to compete on performance and measurable workflow value. To realize these benefits for⏎independent practices, HHS should encourage EHR developers and other ecosystem participants to⏎provide dependable, consistent API access that supports practice facing workflows (not only patient⏎facing use cases), and to prioritize implementation consistency so practices are not forced into⏎costly custom builds.⏎AI cannot be safe, effective, or trusted if it is built on inconsistent or low integrity data. For physicians,⏎the reliability of an AI recommendation is only as strong as the fidelity of the clinical information feeding⏎it—making trusted, standardized medical coding and terminology foundational infrastructure, not a⏎technical afterthought. HHS should therefore elevate semantic standardization as a core requirement⏎for responsible AI. AI training, use, and outputs must be grounded in standardized clinical concepts and⏎code sets and be auditable for clinical fidelity, while driving alignment across EHRs, payers, and clinical⏎decision-support systems so that “meaning” remains consistent as data moves through the health care⏎ecosystem.⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 8⏎Independent physician practices often lack the bargaining leverage to negotiate robust contractual⏎protections with developers and other third parties, even though those entities frequently control the⏎system design, data handling, and security practices that determine real world risk. In that context, a⏎purely market-based approach can inadvertently shift accountability onto physicians by default—⏎exposing practices to privacy, security, and compliance fallout tied to technology they do not fully⏎control. Further, when trust in data security and privacy is degraded, the flow of information halts. HHS⏎can help correct this imbalance by using clear guidance and an appropriate enforcement posture.⏎Entities building and operating AI systems must carry privacy and security obligations and⏎accountability.⏎Prior Authorization⏎Prior authorization is one of the most significant drivers of physician burden and of delays in care. AI can⏎be used constructively to streamline documentation, improve submission quality, and accelerate⏎approvals. However, the AMA remains concerned about payer use of automated systems, including AI⏎enabled tools, that increase inappropriate denials, obscure the rationale for decisions, or create barriers to⏎timely, medically necessary care.⏎When automated or AI enabled tools influence prior authorization approvals or denials, physicians and⏎patients need clear, actionable explanations—not opaque outputs that make decisions effectively⏎unreviewable. Prior authorization should not devolve into “black box” process where physicians cannot⏎understand what drove an adverse determination or what information is needed to resolve it. Accordingly,⏎physicians should expect meaningful transparency about the basis for adverse determinations, including⏎the key clinical or administrative factors that contributed to the outcome, and ensure that decision⏎rationales are delivered in a form that works in real clinical workflows. Any AI enabled recommendation⏎that indicates limitations or denials of care, at both the initial review and appeal levels, must be⏎automatically referred to a physician for review.⏎The critical policy question is not whether AI should be used in prior authorization, but under what⏎conditions it can be deployed without undermining patient protections, due process, or physician trust.⏎The Administration should advance a set of common-sense expectations that distinguish AI that⏎speeds appropriate approvals from AI that scales inappropriate denials. To effectively mitigate⏎harms associated with prior authorization, HHS’ policies should address several objectives:⏎• AI can support prior authorization workflow, but adverse coverage decisions must not be fully⏎automated. Denials should require timely review by a qualified, licensed physician, with⏎escalation pathways to protect patient safety when care may be delayed.⏎• If AI influences coverage decisions, payers must be able to demonstrate real world accuracy and⏎clinical appropriateness through ongoing monitoring for errors, inappropriate denial patterns, and⏎bias. Independent auditing and corrective action must be deployed when AI inappropriately limits⏎patients’ access to care.⏎• Standardized, API driven interoperability should streamline prior authorization end to end within⏎the EHR. APIs should reduce fragmented documentation, eliminate payer specific variation, and⏎rework.⏎• As API enabled data access expands in prior authorization, privacy, security, and limits on⏎downstream use must scale with it. FTC and OCR enforcement and clear chain of custody⏎accountability must be established so expanded access does not increase breach risk or undermine⏎patient and physician trust.⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 9⏎Payment for AI-Enabled Technologies⏎The AMA commends the Administration on its increasing focus on developing pathways to payment for⏎emerging digital health technologies. Digital health technologies, ranging from digital therapeutics to AI-⏎enabled diagnostics and from augmentative to fully autonomous systems, are evolving at a rapid pace,⏎with increasing interest from physicians in integrating these technologies into their practice. However, for⏎these tools to ultimately become available for patient care, they need a clear, consistent pathway to⏎payment.⏎The AMA has long held that any digital health technology proposed for payment should meet the goals of⏎the quadruple aim: improving the patient experience, lowering health care costs, improving physician⏎wellbeing, and improving health outcomes. We have appreciated the HHS’ focus on ensuring that the⏎safety and efficacy of AI is a priority when considering payment for AI-enabled technologies. Given that⏎regulatory updates are lagging behind the rapid pace of innovation, ensuring that payment systems⏎prioritize safe and effective AI-enabled systems can play a key role in helping enhance patient and⏎provider trust while encouraging broader adoption of emerging and innovative technologies.⏎While the AMA strongly supports efforts to develop pathways to payment for high-quality, high-value⏎emerging technologies, the costs of these technologies raise concern over the potential negative impact on⏎the Medicare physician fee schedule (MPFS). AI-enabled technologies can be expensive and as an⏎increasing number of new systems come to market, the costs to the Medicare systems could prove to be⏎very significant. Due to budget neutrality rules, paying for these technologies on the MPFS would likely⏎be unsustainable over the long term. Because of this, the AMA strongly recommends that payment for⏎digital health and AI-enabled technologies be paid outside of the MPFS. A new benefit category or other⏎alternative approach whereby digital health tools and other AI-enabled technologies are paid separately⏎from the MPFS would preserve payment for physician services while seeking to provide a clear,⏎consistent pathway to payment. The unique nature of these technologies would appear to warrant a new⏎payment system tailored specifically to the needs of developers, physicians, and patients when⏎considering integration of these tools into clinical practice. This would be analogous to CMS’ approaches⏎to physician-administered drugs paid under Part B and to payment of durable medical equipment. While⏎the AMA recognizes the limitations on CMS’ authority to create new benefit categories, we urge the⏎Agency to carefully consider how broad payment of increasing numbers of AI technologies may impact⏎payment for physician services on the MPFS.⏎Unfortunately, physicians have seen the unintended consequences of paying for high-cost disposable⏎supplies in the practice expense methodology as these services have grown in price and in number.⏎Currently, there are 94 medical supply items embedded in codes with a purchase price of more than $500.⏎These high-cost medical supplies represent $1.53 billion in direct costs and 21 percent of all direct⏎practice expense medical supply costs in the non-facility setting. These supplies are paid for at the⏎expense of other specialties, and we appreciate that CMS is seeking input on how to price and pay for⏎these services. The AMA is reiterating that they should be priced annually and paid outside of the MPFS.⏎We have concerns that the same redistributive trend could emerge if digital health technologies are paid⏎within the MPFS.⏎Coding for AI-Enabled Technologies⏎The AMA supports HHS’ objective to accelerate the responsible adoption of AI and other digital⏎technologies in clinical care, while fostering public trust, reducing uncertainty that impedes innovation,⏎and aligning incentives to improve outcomes, reduce burden, and lower costs. The AMA believes these⏎goals are best achieved when digital innovation is grounded in clinical reality, governed through⏎transparent, clinical expert-led processes, and integrated into sustainable coding and payment pathways.⏎⏎Steven Posnack, MS, MHS⏎February 17, 2026⏎Page 10⏎A central mechanism for advancing these objectives is the ongoing evolution of the Current Procedural⏎Terminology® (CPT®) code set. Through the work of the CPT Editorial Panel, CPT is being modernized⏎to support digitally enabled value-based care models that are increasingly outcomes-focused, technology-⏎enabled, and delivered by multidisciplinary teams. CPT already underpins many value-based care⏎arrangements by supporting patient attribution, risk stratification, quality measurement, and longitudinal⏎analysis. Building on this foundation, the Panel has established a dedicated Value-Based Care Workgroup⏎to evaluate how CPT can better reflect episode-based and digitally enabled care, operating through an⏎open and transparent process that actively seeks input from physicians, payers, innovators, federal⏎agencies, and other stakeholders.⏎In parallel, the CPT Editorial Panel is addressing the rapid emergence of clinical AI by developing a⏎distinct coding framework that accurately describes algorithmic services as part of the patient care⏎pathway. Recognizing that existing payment systems often rely on ad hoc crosswalks that are not scalable⏎or sustainable, the Panel’s Digital Medicine Coding Committee is advancing a new category of CPT⏎codes, Clinically Meaningful Algorithmic Analyses. These codes are intended to describe complete,⏎codifiable clinical services in which algorithmic output meaningfully informs diagnosis or treatment,⏎while clearly distinguishing machine-performed work from the interpretive work of physicians and other⏎qualified health care professionals.⏎Beyond defining algorithmic services themselves, the Panel recognizes that the increasing use of AI will⏎have broader implications for physician work and for how clinical services are described across the CPT⏎code set. Accordingly, the Panel has prioritized evaluating potential impacts on physician work,⏎identifying coding gaps, and assessing how existing codes may need to evolve as clinical practice⏎changes. These considerations will inform future deliberations and guide the next phase of CPT⏎modernization to ensure continued clinical accuracy and relevance.⏎Because CPT is embedded across electronic health records, payer systems, quality programs, and federal⏎health care infrastructure, updates to the code set provide a scalable mechanism for integrating digitally⏎enabled value-based care and clinical AI across the health system. While the CPT Editorial Panel has⏎taken a leadership role in developing a clinically grounded and transparent reporting framework for⏎algorithmic services, the AMA recognizes the essential role of HHS in establishing sustainable pathways⏎for coverage and payment. The AMA looks forward to continued collaboration with HHS and other⏎stakeholders to ensure that advances in digital health and AI translate into patient benefit, physician⏎adoption, and a more efficient, trusted, and sustainable U.S. health care system.⏎Thank you for considering our recommendations. The AMA remains committed to working with the⏎Administration to advance our shared goals. Please reach out to me directly at 312-464-5288 or⏎John.Whyte@ama-assn.org if you have questions or need further information.⏎Sincerely,⏎John Whyte, MD, MPH♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0464♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from MEDvidi, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MEDvidi♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Docket Identifier: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Date: February 18, 2026⏎Submitted by:⏎Medvidi, Inc.⏎4010 Moorpark Ave, Ste. 114TE 114 San Jose, CA, US 95117⏎San Jose, CA 95117⏎Contact: Vasili Razhnou, CEO⏎Email: team@medvidi.com⏎Website: www.medvidi.com⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care (Federal Register Vol. 90, Number 244, December 23, 2025)⏎Medvidi, Inc. welcomes the opportunity to provide input on this important Request for Information issued⏎by the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS), in collaboration with the Assistant Secretary⏎for Technology Policy (ASTP) and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology (ONC).⏎As a telemedicine provider exclusively focused on behavioral and mental health care, Medvidi has built⏎and actively deploys a suite of native, proprietary artificial intelligence tools deeply integrated into our⏎clinical platform.⏎Our AI stack includes:⏎● Ambient Chart Generation AI: listens to visits in real time and utilises intake data (with explicit⏎patient consent) and produces structured, clinically dense draft notes that capture key details with⏎high fidelity. Ambient Chart Generation AI provides incremental updates, either adding new⏎content to notes or replacing existing content, every minute so that medical practitioners aren’t⏎forced to wait until the end of the visit to see the results of generation.⏎● Medical Chart Review AI: scans every chart post-visit for compliance gaps, missing⏎documentation elements, protocol deviations, and quality indicators, providing feedback to⏎clinicians after each visit.⏎● AI Receptionist and Modular Agents: handle administrative, non-clinical automated patient⏎communications, routine follow-up messaging, adherence prompts, and scalable operational⏎support, all under strict human oversight.⏎These tools have demonstrably reduced administrative burden (often reclaiming 10–15 minutes per visit⏎previously spent on documentation), improved note accuracy and completeness, strengthened compliance⏎posture, and helped mitigate provider burnout in a field facing severe workforce shortages (with⏎projections estimating a deficit of over 21,000 psychiatrists by 2030).⏎We offer the following detailed responses to the RFI questions, grounded in our day-to-day experience⏎operating at scale in the telehealth environment.⏎4010 Moorpark Ave, Ste 114,⏎San Jose, CA 95117⏎⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎From our perspective as an active deployer of clinical AI, several interconnected barriers stand out:⏎● Misaligned payment incentives: Current fee-for-service structures reward visit length rather than⏎efficiency or quality. Tools that dramatically reduce documentation time or enable more consistent⏎follow-up do not generate proportional revenue under traditional billing, discouraging broader⏎adoption even when they clearly improve care delivery and provider well-being.⏎● Regulatory uncertainty for non-device AI: Ambient documentation, chart auditing, and⏎workflow agents occupy an ambiguous space, not traditional SaMD (software as a medical⏎device) but still carrying potential liability exposure in audits, malpractice claims, or enforcement⏎actions. The lack of clear, proportionate guidance creates hesitation among providers and⏎platforms.⏎● Evaluation and trust gaps: Without standardized benchmarks for behavioral health AI⏎performance (e.g., accuracy in capturing emotional nuance or avoiding cultural bias), it is hard to⏎build confidence or compare solutions.⏎● Complex web of regulators with differences in priorities: Behavioural health is regulated by⏎federal and state agencies, including DEA, CMS, SAMHSA, HHS OCR, state licensing boards,⏎and law enforcement bodies, each enforcing overlapping but not fully aligned rules on prescribing,⏎privacy, reimbursement, and professional conduct. With FDA approvals necessary for CDS⏎systems, navigating this complex web of regulatory bodies is not only complicated but potentially⏎unresolved in the current legal framework.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎We recommend the following prioritized actions, each of which would accelerate responsible adoption⏎based on our internal results:⏎● CMS payment modernization: Introduce reimbursement mechanisms (e.g., add-on codes,⏎modifiers, or value-based incentives) that explicitly recognize time savings and outcome⏎improvements from AI-augmented services, ambient documentation, real-time compliance review,⏎or automated adherence support. When efficiency translates directly to financial benefit, adoption⏎surges.⏎● Proportionate, clear guidance for non-device AI: Publish targeted HHS/ONC/ASTP guidance⏎distinguishing low-risk assistive tools (requiring transparency, consent, and human review) from⏎higher-risk diagnostic/predictive software. This reduces uncertainty without imposing unnecessary⏎pre-market burdens.⏎● Establish a cross-agency AI in behavioural health committee. Given the distinct nature of the⏎agencies that regulate behavioural health, a cross-agency body (including the DEA and FDA)⏎focused on resolving barriers to AI innovation would significantly accelerate unified⏎decision-making across multiple regulatory landscapes.⏎● Behavioral health-focused interoperability enhancements: Expand the United States Core Data⏎for Interoperability (USCDI) to better include mental health elements such as visit transcripts,⏎4010 Moorpark Ave, Ste 114,⏎San Jose, CA 95117⏎⏎PHQ/GAD scores, treatment rationales, and longitudinal outcomes. Mandate secure, standardized⏎APIs to enable benchmarking and federated learning while preserving privacy.⏎● Innovation pilots and funding: Launch grant programs, cooperative agreements, or challenge⏎competitions specifically for mental health AI in shortage areas. These could test real-world⏎impact on access, outcomes, and cost, with public reporting of findings. MEDvidi would be eager⏎to participate and contribute anonymized insights from our dataset.⏎3–5. For non-medical-device AI tools: What novel issues arise? What evaluation methods seem⏎most promising? What should HHS's role be in supporting private-sector evaluation activities?⏎Novel issues⏎● Consent and transparency for ambient audio processing (e.g., granular disclosure, handling of⏎bystanders, easy opt-out).⏎● Liability allocation in multi-vendor ecosystems (who is responsible if an AI-drafted note⏎contributes to an oversight?).⏎● Heightened privacy risks with conversational data in mental health contexts.⏎● Subtle biases in language models that may misinterpret cultural idioms, emotional tone, or⏎non-verbal cues.⏎● Security and privacy attack vectors (such as prompt injection), which are already managed using⏎guardrails (automated systems that review AI before and after it is invoked).⏎● The non-deterministic nature of AI means that different results can be obtained for the same input.⏎Strategies like averaging across multiple tries or evaluations help manage that.⏎● Workflow disruption if tools require excessive clinician editing or produce inconsistent outputs.⏎Promising evaluation methods⏎● Pre- and post-deployment comparative studies measuring note accuracy (vs. gold-standard manual⏎drafts), time saved, patient satisfaction, and clinical outcomes.⏎● Continuous real-world monitoring dashboards tracking error rates, human escalation frequency,⏎bias indicators across demographics, and provider-reported usability.⏎● Simulation-based testing under realistic conditions (accents, background noise, interrupted⏎sessions).⏎● Longitudinal tracking of downstream effects (e.g., improved follow-up rates, medication⏎adherence, or reduced no-shows due to less admin burden).⏎● Using a separate instance of AI to evaluate (LLM as a Judge) the outputs of the original AI system⏎for compliance with a specified guideline, rule, or condition.⏎● Human annotation of AI outputs feeds back into improvement in how AI is set up.⏎● Evaluating alignment between independent AI output and human output.⏎HHS role⏎4010 Moorpark Ave, Ste 114,⏎San Jose, CA 95117⏎⏎● Provide seed funding or cooperative agreements for independent third-party validations, especially⏎for behavioral health applications.⏎● Endorse voluntary industry accreditation or certification frameworks.⏎● Organize prize competitions or collaborative challenges to establish benchmarks for⏎specialty-specific AI performance.⏎● Facilitate de-identified data-sharing consortia to support robust, diverse model training.⏎6. Where have AI tools in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost expectations, and⏎where have they fallen short? What novel AI tools have the greatest potential?⏎Exceeded expectations⏎Our ambient Chart Generation AI routinely produces notes that are more complete and chronologically⏎accurate than manual drafts, with clinicians editing in under two minutes instead of 10–15. Chart Review⏎AI flags compliance and quality issues at 100% scale, impossible manually, helping maintain standards⏎without added overhead. Chart Review AI doesn’t replace human oversight and supervision, which is⏎performed to the full degree necessary by relevant compliance requirements, but provides an additional⏎layer of protection. These have yielded measurable time savings (hours of work per day in case of Chart⏎Generation AI), fewer documentation deficiencies, and improved provider satisfaction.⏎Fallen short⏎Generic foundation models without behavioral health fine-tuning often miss sarcasm, contextual history,⏎or cultural expressions of distress, producing bland or inaccurate outputs.⏎Given that AI systems require continuous improvement and caution should be exercised, Chart Review⏎AI can be overly restrictive in its judgments, resulting in false positives (raising a red flag where a clinical⏎committee doesn’t see one).⏎Greatest potential⏎Modular, supervised AI agents for: routine follow-ups and dosage titration; real-time evidence-based⏎suggestion during visits; proactive compliance auditing; and scalable virtual support in high-shortage⏎regions. In mental health, these could expand access dramatically while preserving human judgment for⏎complex cases.⏎7–8. Who are the key players influencing adoption? What interoperability opportunities exist?⏎Key influencers⏎● Front-line clinicians (who experience burnout relief firsthand).⏎● Compliance, quality, and risk management teams (needing defensible audit trails).⏎● C-suite and finance leaders (focused on ROI and scalability).⏎● Operations staff (integrating tools into daily workflows).⏎● Patients (indirectly, through demand for faster/more attentive care).⏎Interoperability opportunities⏎4010 Moorpark Ave, Ste 114,⏎San Jose, CA 95117⏎⏎Standardized APIs for ingesting visit audio/transcripts into AI pipelines, returning reviewed notes to⏎EHRs, and sharing aggregate outcomes for benchmarking. Federated learning frameworks would allow⏎collaborative model improvement without centralizing sensitive data.⏎9. What challenges and concerns do patients and caregivers have regarding AI in clinical care?⏎Patients value faster access, reduced wait times, and providers who appear more engaged. Many⏎appreciate AI freeing clinicians from typing. Primary concerns include:⏎● Privacy of recorded conversations.⏎● Fear that AI could diminish empathy or replace human judgment.⏎● Potential for bias disadvantaging certain cultural or linguistic groups.⏎We mitigate these through clear upfront consent, visible opt-out mechanisms, explicit statements that⏎clinicians retain final decision-making authority, and transparent explanations of how tools are used.⏎10. What specific AI research areas should HHS prioritize?⏎● Multimodal models combining audio, text, validated scales, and outcomes for improved⏎behavioral health support.⏎● Implementation science examining AI integration into telehealth without disrupting therapeutic⏎relationships.⏎● Cost-effectiveness analyses in value-based care settings.⏎● Equity and bias research ensuring performance across demographics, languages, and⏎socioeconomic groups.⏎● Public-private pilots generating real-world evidence in shortage areas.⏎● Patient risk modelling for adverse side effects and drug abuse potential⏎MEDvidi is prepared to collaborate with HHS—sharing de-identified insights, participating in pilots, or⏎contributing to standards development. Our experience shows that thoughtfully designed,⏎specialty-specific AI can meaningfully improve access and quality in mental health care. MEDvidi plans⏎to build AI Clinician capable of diagnosing, treating, and prescribing medications in the behavioural⏎health domain, and is prepared to move jointly with HHS to provide mental health coverage to all people⏎in the US.⏎Thank you for considering these views. We look forward to continued dialogue.⏎Sincerely,⏎Vasili Razhnou⏎Chief Executive Officer⏎Medvidi, Inc.⏎4010 Moorpark Ave, Ste 114,⏎San Jose, CA 95117♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0465♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-19T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Karen♔Hernandez♔♔Maine♔♔United States♔♔♔♔♔♔♔My client Ruthie is 84 years old and has been on a blood thinner for years. Her clinic started using some kind of automated system, and it flagged her dosage and recommended she take less. Her daughter happened to be at the appointment and noticed it didn't match what the prescribing doctor had said, so they pushed for a call. Turned out the recommendation wasn't right for Ruthie's specific situation. I don't know exactly what would have happened, but I know enough about blood thinners to know it wasn't nothing.⏎⏎Most of my other clients don't have a daughter who catches things like that. I'm with them for a few hours a day. I'm not at every appointment. Nobody is.⏎⏎So when I hear that HHS wants to move faster on AI in clinical settings, the thing I keep coming back to is the insurance side of this, not just the clinical tools. Because right now, insurance companies are already using automated systems to deny care. My clients get denial letters and half the time it's clear that no one actually read their chart. One of my clients, a 77-year-old man in Rockland, was denied home health continuation last November and the denial letter used language that didn't even match his diagnosis. He doesn't own a computer. He can barely use his phone to call his son. He had about a week to appeal before the deadline passed.⏎⏎If HHS is asking how to accelerate AI adoption responsibly, I think the most important thing the department could do is make sure there is an actual human being who is accountable, by name, for every coverage denial. Not a system. A person. Someone who has to sign off and who can be reached. Right now that's not happening and accelerating AI adoption without fixing that first seems like it's going to make things worse, not better.⏎⏎I understand there might be benefits. I'm not against technology. I've seen reminder systems and documentation tools that genuinely help. But the patients who get hurt by automated denials are not going to be writing comments to federal agencies. They're going to sit with a denial letter and not know what to do. Some of them will just go without the care.⏎⏎I don't know all the regulatory mechanisms available to HHS here, but I know the department has some authority over how insurers operating in Medicare and Medicaid handle these decisions. If there's a way to require that a real person review and sign every denial, especially for elderly or disabled patients, that seems like the most direct thing that could come out of this process. Before anything else.⏎⏎The thing about Ruthie's situation is that we got lucky. Her daughter is sharp and she happened to be there that day. That's not a system. That's just luck.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0466♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-17T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-15T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS, CMS, FEMA, FBI, ASTP/ONC and ASPR should consider a cross sector and cross agency AI interoperability accelerator focused on emergency response, public safety, and disaster operations to enable harmonized and standardized data exchange that would encompass all public safety, emergency response and disaster domains using standards based APIs, which would support AI clinical systems in emergency and disaster care.⏎⏎Cover across:⏎•	EMS⏎Fire⏎•	Law Enforcement⏎•	Emergency Management⏎•	Shelter Coordination⏎•	Logistics⏎•	Search and Rescue⏎⏎⏎Would cover ⏎•	NEMSIS⏎•	NFIRS: fire suppression⏎•	Law enforcement ⏎•	NIBRS⏎•	Disaster shelting reporting systems⏎•	US geological survey⏎•	Emergency management agencies⏎•	Administration for Strategic preparedness and response⏎•	Urban search and rescue take force under FEMA⏎⏎⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0467♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Jelatancev, Daniela, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-01T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Daniela♔Jelatancev♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Comment on “Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption⏎and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care.”⏎A Deterministic Semantic Validation Layer to Support Safe AI and⏎Longitudinal Interoperability⏎Executive Summary⏎The U.S. healthcare ecosystem has made substantial progress in enabling electronic data access⏎and exchange, particularly through increasing adoption of FHIR-based APIs. These advances have⏎improved patient-centered access, enabled third-party innovation, and laid the groundwork for AI-⏎enabled clinical tools.⏎As AI and machine learning systems increasingly operate downstream of interoperable data flows,⏎a new technical challenge emerges—ensuring that clinical data and quality measurement inputs⏎retain consistent semantic meaning as they move across systems, organizations, and time. While⏎data transport is increasingly standardized, semantic interpretation is not. Semantic interoperability⏎gaps—differences in units, measurement context, specimen, method, or transformation rules—can⏎affect AI reasoning, longitudinal analysis, and the interpretation of trends across patient⏎populations.⏎This comment proposes exploring a deterministic, hybrid human–AI semantic validation layer: a⏎complementary, auditable framework designed to stabilize clinical meaning for AI-enabled⏎analysis, longitudinal monitoring, and quality learning, without constraining innovation, and to⏎extend the value of existing FHIR-based interoperability.⏎1. The Emerging Challenge: Semantic Drift and Context Loss in an Interoperable, AI-⏎Enabled Ecosystem⏎FHIR-based APIs have succeeded in enabling data movement. However, interoperability at the⏎transport layer does not guarantee semantic equivalence at the interpretation layer.⏎As data moves across systems, system-specific transformations may occur upstream—including⏎unit conversions, aggregation, normalization, or omission of contextual attributes. These⏎⏎transformations may be valid within a local system, given local configuration and reference⏎context, but may not be visible, traceable, or interpretable downstream.⏎These issues are often manageable within:⏎• a single clinical endpoint⏎• a limited-radius application ecosystem⏎• or a single EHR context⏎However, they become increasingly problematic when:⏎• patients move between health systems⏎• data is compared longitudinally across years⏎• multiple AI models consume the same data⏎• or research and monitoring span heterogeneous sources⏎2. When Data Cannot Be Compared at All⏎In some cases, two clinical values cannot be meaningfully compared under the rules of⏎measurement science without explicit transformation rules.⏎For example, a laboratory result for serum iron produced under one assay method and specimen⏎matrix cannot be meaningfully compared to a result produced under a different method or matrix⏎unless the underlying measurand and validated harmonization logic are known; unit conversion⏎alone is insufficient when the measurement procedure differs. In such cases, longitudinal⏎comparison is not merely imprecise—it is invalid.⏎AI systems, particularly large language models, will often attempt to infer over whatever data is⏎provided. Without explicit semantic context and deterministic rules, these systems may infer⏎continuity where none exists.⏎This behavior is often described as hallucination; however, in many cases the root cause is missing⏎semantic ground truth rather than model failure.⏎When clinical data lacks explicit semantic definition—such as standardized units, measurement⏎conditions, or clearly defined reference meaning—AI systems must rely on probabilistic inference⏎to interpret gaps or inconsistencies. While probabilistic reasoning is not inherently unsafe, it limits⏎precision and reproducibility, particularly as data moves across systems or is analyzed over time.⏎By contrast, deterministic semantic validation ensures that values, transformations, and⏎interpretation boundaries are explicit and auditable, reducing reliance on inference and enabling⏎⏎AI to operate on known, well-defined inputs rather than assumptions. This distinction becomes⏎increasingly important as healthcare moves from isolated clinical snapshots toward longitudinal⏎monitoring, where small semantic differences compound over time and can otherwise prevent⏎meaningful comparison, trend analysis, or early pattern detection.⏎3. Hybrid Deterministic Semantic Validation Layer⏎A hybrid deterministic semantic validation layer introduces explicit, auditable rules that define⏎how clinical data is interpreted for downstream use—particularly for AI-enabled analysis and⏎longitudinal monitoring.⏎Key characteristics include:⏎• deterministic processing for unit normalization, reference alignment, and semantic⏎validation⏎• explicit provenance and audit logs capturing transformations, assumptions, and⏎unresolved gaps⏎• a hybrid design in which AI assists but does not replace human oversight⏎Clinical reference catalogs can be derived or synthesized with assistance from large language⏎models using clearly defined source constraints, followed by human expert review, approval, and⏎versioned publication as shared benchmarks.⏎This approach does not constrain AI; it supports safer and more reproducible AI reasoning.⏎4. Why This Enables AI Rather Than Restricting It⏎AI and machine learning systems are highly flexible, but they will attempt to infer meaning when⏎semantic gaps are not explicitly defined.⏎A deterministic semantic layer:⏎• prevents implicit inference when data is incomplete or non-comparable⏎• enables AI systems to explicitly represent and communicate uncertainty⏎• allows machine learning models to operate on semantically stable, well-defined inputs⏎⏎Large language models can synthesize clinical context, documentation, and explanations. Machine⏎learning models can validate hypotheses, detect trends, and surface emerging patterns. Both benefit⏎from a shared, explicit semantic foundation that reduces ambiguity and improves reliability.⏎5. Concrete Example: Semantic Equivalence in Longitudinal Laboratory Monitoring⏎Consider a patient whose triglyceride values are reported over time by different laboratories:⏎• one using mg/dL⏎• another using mmol/L⏎• with differing reference ranges and assay context⏎Without explicit normalization rules and provenance:⏎• longitudinal trends may be misinterpreted⏎• clinical thresholds may be applied inconsistently⏎• and AI-generated insights may appear contradictory⏎With deterministic semantic validation:⏎• values are converted using validated rules appropriate to the underlying measurand⏎• reference ranges and interpretation context are applied consistently⏎• and AI systems are explicitly aware of what is known, transformed, or missing⏎In other laboratory contexts—such as measurements based on enzyme activity versus mass (e.g.,⏎CK-MB), biological potency (e.g., insulin, FSH), or immunoassay-based versus mass⏎spectrometry–based results (e.g., testosterone)—differences in reported units reflect⏎fundamentally different measurands or measurement bases. In these cases, mathematical⏎conversion is scientifically invalid, and values should not be compared.⏎To preserve data integrity, non-comparable results should be represented using distinct LOINC⏎codes or equivalent semantic identifiers, ensuring that automated systems do not normalize or⏎compare incompatible measurement bases.⏎FHIR provides flexible mechanisms—such as the Observation and Provenance resources—to⏎capture structured measurements, attribution, and contextual metadata, while leaving⏎transformation logic and semantic interpretation to implementers. As healthcare data exchange⏎increasingly supports longitudinal monitoring, cross-system reuse, and AI-enabled analysis, a⏎governed, hybrid semantic validation layer upstream can complement these capabilities by⏎stabilizing semantic interpretation and validated transformation logic at the point of reuse.⏎⏎In practice, this enables data pipelines to apply method-specific semantic differentiation before⏎longitudinal analysis occurs, improving precision and consistency over time and making⏎provenance and audit information more interpretable as data is reused at scale.⏎6. Placement Within the Interoperability Stack⏎This semantic validation layer does not require a single architectural placement.⏎In general, placement of the deterministic semantic validation layer closer to data origination⏎enables more precise semantic validation and higher downstream impact, while later placement⏎can still provide meaningful stabilization for longitudinal reuse.⏎It can be deployed, independently or in combination:⏎• within a clinical source system prior to external data exposure⏎• immediately before FHIR-based API publication⏎• or downstream of FHIR consumption by applications or AI systems⏎This flexibility allows stakeholders to evaluate where deterministic semantic stabilization is most⏎effective as interoperability and data reuse scale, without constraining architectural choice or⏎prescribing a single deployment model.⏎7. Applicability Beyond Laboratory Data⏎⏎While laboratory data and medications provide the most explicit semantic starting points, the same⏎principles apply to:⏎• clinical observations⏎• device-generated data⏎• and longitudinal clinical assessments⏎Each domain benefits from explicit semantic context as data is reused across systems and over⏎time, particularly for AI-enabled decision support, longitudinal monitoring, and research. In these⏎domains, determinism is achieved through explicit semantic constraints and comparability rules⏎rather than purely mathematical normalization.⏎8. Relationship to Current Progress⏎Significant progress has already been achieved:⏎• health data is more portable than ever⏎• patient access initiatives continue to expand⏎• clinicians are increasingly supported by interoperable tools⏎• and AI and machine learning systems are already helping patients better understand health⏎information, navigate care decisions, and engage with the healthcare system, while also⏎supporting analytical and clinical workflows⏎The remaining challenge is not ambition, but orchestration—aligning data movement, semantic⏎clarity, and AI reasoning into a coherent, auditable workflow that supports longitudinal use and⏎reuse over time.⏎9. Collaborative Evaluation Opportunity⏎Participation in such an approach could be complementary to existing FHIR-based⏎interoperability. Coordinated, multi-stakeholder evaluation efforts could help assess where⏎semantic validation is most effective across the data exchange pipeline, how deterministic layers⏎evolve over time, and how AI system reliability and longitudinal comparability improve when⏎explicit semantic context is preserved. HHS could consider providing guidance or serving as a⏎coordinating convener for such efforts as a means of enabling shared learning without disrupting⏎existing implementations.⏎⏎In addition, HHS could play a constructive role in convening clinical and standards bodies to help⏎establish governance for the clinical reference inputs used in deterministic semantic layers. This⏎could include supporting clinician-led review and sign-off on source materials, reference ranges,⏎and transformation rules, as well as encouraging the synthesis of shared benchmark references.⏎Once established, such clinically governed reference layers could be reused across the ecosystem,⏎enabling consistency, transparency, and trust while preserving innovation and implementation⏎flexibility.⏎Conclusion⏎As the healthcare ecosystem continues to evolve from isolated data exchange toward longitudinal,⏎AI-assisted monitoring and discovery, semantic clarity becomes foundational infrastructure.⏎A hybrid deterministic semantic validation layer could provide a pragmatic, incremental approach⏎to supporting safer AI reasoning, more reliable longitudinal analysis, and continued innovation—⏎while complementing existing interoperability standards and preserving architectural flexibility.⏎Submitted by:⏎Daniela Jelatancev⏎Founder & CEO, HealthFramework⏎daniela@hlthframework.com⏎https://hlthframework.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0468♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from WellLink Health Alliance, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎ATTN: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically to: http://www.regulations.gov⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (RIN⏎0955-AA13)⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎The WellLink Health Alliance appreciates the opportunity to provide comments to the Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology regarding the⏎Request for Information on artificial intelligence (AI) adoption in clinical care.⏎AI is rapidly moving from concept to operational reality in healthcare. Providers, health systems and community⏎partners are already evaluating and deploying AI-enabled tools to support clinical decision-making, improve⏎operational efficiency and expand access to care. Federal policy must now evolve just as quickly to ensure that⏎innovation can move forward while protecting patients and strengthening trust in the healthcare system.⏎WellLink encourages the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services to adopt a clear, coordinated federal⏎framework that accelerates responsible adoption of AI while addressing regulatory fragmentation, cybersecurity⏎risks, workforce readiness and data interoperability.⏎Establish a Coordinated Federal Approach to AI in Healthcare⏎Fragmented regulatory expectations across federal agencies and states risk slowing innovation and increasing⏎compliance burdens for healthcare providers. WellLink urges HHS to adopt a coordinated strategy that aligns⏎policies across agencies and programs affecting AI in healthcare.⏎Clear federal direction will help providers, technology developers and patients understand expectations around⏎privacy, governance and responsible deployment. Without greater coordination, organizations may delay adoption⏎of beneficial technologies due to uncertainty about regulatory obligations.⏎Strengthen Cybersecurity and Risk Guidance⏎Healthcare organizations continue to face persistent and sophisticated cyber threats. The introduction of AI-⏎enabled technologies increases the need for clear expectations around cybersecurity, vendor transparency and⏎system resilience.⏎WellLink encourages federal agencies to provide practical guidance and technical assistance to help healthcare⏎organizations evaluate AI vendors, understand cybersecurity risks and incorporate strong protections into⏎procurement and implementation processes. Federal coordination across agencies responsible for health,⏎cybersecurity and critical infrastructure will be essential.⏎⏎Reduce Payment and Regulatory Barriers to Innovation⏎Many AI-enabled clinical tools lack clear or sustainable reimbursement pathways, which limits adoption even when⏎technologies demonstrate value. Providers operating under tight financial constraints often cannot invest in⏎promising innovations without a viable payment model.⏎WellLink urges HHS to work closely with the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services to reduce regulatory⏎barriers and explore pathways that support the responsible adoption of digital health and AI-enabled tools.⏎Incorporating real-world evidence and performance-based outcomes into future payment models could help⏎accelerate innovation while ensuring accountability.⏎Promote Meaningful Transparency for AI Tools⏎Trust will determine whether AI succeeds in healthcare. Clinicians and healthcare organizations must understand⏎how technologies perform, when they should be used and where limitations exist.⏎WellLink supports federal efforts to encourage transparency but recommends a practical approach focused on⏎meaningful performance information rather than static disclosures that may quickly become outdated. Information⏎about accuracy, reliability, known risks and appropriate clinical use cases will better support informed decision-⏎making by providers and healthcare organizations.⏎Advance Interoperable Data and AI-Ready Infrastructure⏎The effectiveness of AI depends on reliable, interoperable data. Inconsistent data standards, fragmented systems⏎and limited interoperability across healthcare supply chains and care settings continue to limit the potential of⏎advanced analytics and AI.⏎Federal leadership can play a critical role in encouraging widely adopted, standards-based data systems that enable⏎healthcare organizations to safely deploy AI technologies, improve supply chain resilience and strengthen patient⏎safety.⏎Ensure Rural and Resource-Limited Providers Are Not Left Behind⏎Rural hospitals, community providers and smaller health systems face unique financial, workforce and technology⏎challenges. AI policies and programs must be aligned across federal initiatives, so these providers are not forced to⏎navigate conflicting expectations or duplicative requirements.⏎Thoughtful alignment will help ensure that federal investments in healthcare innovation translate into improved⏎access to care and long-term sustainability for underserved communities.⏎Conclusion⏎Artificial intelligence presents a significant opportunity to improve healthcare delivery, reduce administrative⏎burden and support clinicians. Achieving these benefits will require a federal policy framework that is coordinated,⏎transparent and supportive of responsible innovation.⏎WellLink appreciates the opportunity to provide input and looks forward to continued engagement as federal⏎agencies shape the future of AI in healthcare.⏎Sincerely,⏎Daniel Lettenberger Klein⏎Executive Director⏎WellLink Health Alliance♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0469♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Total Quality Solutions, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Total Quality Solutions♔♔♔♔♔♔Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎RESPONSE TO REQUEST FOR INFORMATION⏎RIN 0955-AA13⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence (AI)⏎as Part of Clinical Care⏎Submitted to:⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Submitted by:⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC⏎Greater St. Louis Area, Missouri⏎February 23, 2026⏎Points of Contact⏎Technical & Contracting POC: Parish Lemons, President⏎Phone: 314-605-6519⏎Email: solutions@tqsolve.com⏎Primary NAICS: 541512 – Computer Systems Design Services⏎Supporting NAICS: 541511, 541611, 518210, 541690, 541715⏎Table of Contents⏎1.0 Executive Summary⏎2.0 About the Commenter⏎3.0 Barriers to AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎3.1 Absence of SDLC Governance Frameworks for AI Systems⏎3.2 Lack of Post-Deployment Lifecycle Monitoring⏎3.3 Unclear Accountability Across Multi-Stakeholder Environments⏎3.4 Workflow Integration and Clinician Trust⏎3.5 Data Integrity and Interoperability Gaps⏎3.6 State-Level Implementation Constraints (Missouri MO HealthNet)⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎4.0 The Need for Lifecycle-Based Evaluation and Continuous Monitoring⏎5.0 Regulatory and Policy Considerations⏎6.0 Reimbursement Pathways for AI-Assisted Care⏎7.0 Role of R&D and Public-Private Partnerships⏎8.0 Closing Recommendations⏎Appendix A: Question Crosswalk⏎1.0 Executive Summary⏎Artificial intelligence holds significant promise for transforming clinical care delivery, improving⏎outcomes, reducing administrative burden, and advancing health equity across the United States.⏎Yet despite the rapid proliferation of AI tools in healthcare settings, adoption remains constrained⏎— not by the absence of capable algorithms, but by the absence of the governance, lifecycle⏎management, and integration infrastructure required to deploy those tools safely, sustainably, and⏎accountably.⏎This response is submitted by Parish Lemons, President of Total Quality Solutions, LLC, a⏎Missouri-based IT consulting firm with over 20 years of experience managing enterprise software⏎development lifecycles in regulated public-sector environments. The perspectives offered here⏎are grounded in hands-on practitioner experience implementing and governing software systems⏎through the full development lifecycle — including requirements analysis, design, development,⏎testing, deployment, and post-deployment performance management — applied specifically to⏎government and healthcare program contexts.⏎This submission asserts that the primary barriers to clinical AI adoption are structural and lifecycle-⏎based in nature. Organizations possess increasingly capable AI tools but lack the governance⏎frameworks, evaluation infrastructure, and interoperability standards required to deploy them⏎responsibly at scale. This challenge is especially acute at the state Medicaid program level, where⏎agencies such as Missouri's MO HealthNet division are simultaneously managing legacy system⏎modernization, new federal compliance requirements, and the pressure to integrate AI-enabled⏎solutions — often without the SDLC governance discipline needed to do so safely.⏎Our recommendations emphasize: (1) establishing SDLC-based governance standards for non-⏎medical device AI; (2) investing in post-deployment monitoring infrastructure; (3) creating⏎reimbursement pathways that incentivize governed AI deployment; and (4) funding cooperative⏎agreements with state Medicaid agencies and mid-market IT consultants to develop and pilot⏎implementation frameworks that the broader health sector can adopt.⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎2.0 About the Commenter⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC is a Missouri-based IT consulting firm providing SDLC leadership,⏎systems modernization, data and reporting solutions, and technology program execution for⏎regulated public-sector environments. The firm supports government and healthcare agencies in⏎translating policy and regulatory requirements into operational, compliant, and auditable⏎technology solutions, with an emphasis on interoperability, reporting integrity, and execution⏎discipline.⏎Parish Lemons, the firm's President, brings over 20 years of enterprise IT and software⏎development lifecycle experience, including program and project management across complex,⏎multi-stakeholder environments; data integration and analytics enablement supporting⏎operational oversight and program accountability; integration of ERP-based systems within⏎regulated environments; and the development and application of AI-assisted tools to improve data⏎analysis, documentation, and workflow efficiency. Mr. Lemons has supported government⏎procurement activities including RFI and RFP responses, and has pursued active professional⏎development in modernization and data-enabled workflows through leadership and technology⏎cohorts.⏎Total Quality Solutions brings a practitioner perspective to this RFI that is grounded in the⏎lived reality of implementing technology in regulated environments — a perspective that is⏎essential to informing workable, real-world AI adoption policy.⏎3.0 Barriers to AI Adoption in Clinical Care⏎Despite meaningful advances in AI capability, adoption within clinical care environments remains⏎constrained by a set of interrelated, structural barriers. These challenges are not primarily⏎technical in origin — they reflect gaps in governance, lifecycle management, interoperability, and⏎organizational accountability that cut across institutions, vendors, and care settings. From a⏎practitioner SDLC perspective, each barrier maps directly to a phase of the software development⏎and deployment lifecycle that has not been adequately addressed in current AI adoption⏎frameworks.⏎3.1 Absence of SDLC Governance Frameworks for AI Systems⏎The most fundamental barrier to responsible AI adoption in clinical care is the absence of⏎established software development lifecycle governance frameworks applied specifically to AI tool⏎deployment and maintenance. In enterprise IT environments, mature SDLC frameworks —⏎including CMMI, ISO/IEC 12207, and Agile-based governance models — provide the structural⏎discipline required to manage software through design, testing, deployment, change⏎management, and retirement. These frameworks define roles, oversight mechanisms,⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎documentation standards, and escalation procedures that enable organizations to deploy⏎complex software responsibly in regulated environments.⏎AI tools deployed in clinical settings are software systems. Yet they are routinely introduced⏎outside of SDLC governance structures — evaluated under controlled conditions, deployed⏎without change management protocols, and maintained without version control or performance⏎monitoring plans. The result is a pattern in which clinically promising AI tools are deployed at⏎scale without the institutional discipline required to manage their behavior over time. This gap is⏎not unique to healthcare; it reflects the broader challenge of AI governance in regulated⏎environments, and it is one that HHS is uniquely positioned to address through policy guidance.⏎3.2 Lack of Post-Deployment Lifecycle Monitoring⏎Enterprise software development practice has long recognized that the most consequential phase⏎of a system's lifecycle occurs after deployment, when the system encounters the full complexity⏎of real-world operating environments. Post-deployment monitoring, performance trending, drift⏎detection, and structured change management are foundational disciplines in mature software⏎development organizations — and they are largely absent from current clinical AI deployment⏎practice.⏎AI systems are particularly sensitive to environmental change. Shifts in clinical documentation⏎practices, patient population demographics, care delivery models, or upstream data pipeline⏎configurations can materially alter AI performance over time. Without continuous monitoring and⏎structured performance review cycles, degradation in AI output quality may go undetected until it⏎has already influenced clinical decision-making. This is precisely the category of risk that SDLC⏎governance frameworks are designed to mitigate — and it is a gap that HHS policy can directly⏎address by establishing post-deployment monitoring requirements as a condition of AI tool⏎adoption in federally funded care settings.⏎3.3 Unclear Accountability Across Multi-Stakeholder Environments⏎Clinical AI deployments involve a complex web of stakeholders — model developers, data⏎providers, system integrators, health system administrators, clinical end-users, and regulatory⏎bodies — among whom accountability for performance, safety, and remediation is frequently⏎undefined. From a program management perspective, this ambiguity is among the most⏎consequential barriers to responsible adoption. Organizations operating in regulated⏎environments with established risk management obligations are rationally hesitant to deploy AI⏎tools when the governance structure does not clearly define where accountability resides when⏎tools underperform or produce unintended clinical consequences.⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎This is a governance design problem, not a technology problem. SDLC governance frameworks⏎in enterprise IT environments address this challenge through defined RACI matrices, documented⏎escalation procedures, and contractual performance obligations. HHS can support the extension⏎of these disciplines into clinical AI deployment by issuing guidance that defines accountability⏎expectations for AI tool developers, system integrators, and deploying institutions — particularly⏎in Medicaid and Medicare program contexts where federal funding creates a direct policy lever.⏎3.4 Workflow Integration and Clinician Trust⏎AI tools that are not designed for seamless integration into existing clinical workflows impose⏎cognitive and operational burden on clinicians — even when the tools themselves demonstrate⏎strong technical performance under controlled conditions. From an SDLC perspective, this⏎reflects a failure of requirements analysis and user-centered design discipline. Effective enterprise⏎software development begins with deep stakeholder engagement, workflow mapping, and⏎iterative validation with end-users before deployment. These disciplines are inconsistently applied⏎in clinical AI development, resulting in tools that clinicians resist or underutilize.⏎This challenge is compounded when AI outputs exist outside of the EHR environment, requiring⏎clinicians to leave established workflows to access or interpret results. Integration discipline —⏎ensuring that AI outputs are surfaced within existing clinical workflows and documented within the⏎patient record in a manner consistent with clinical governance requirements — is a solvable⏎engineering and design challenge when treated as a first-class requirement of the SDLC process.⏎3.5 Data Integrity and Interoperability Gaps⏎Clinical AI is frequently developed and evaluated using retrospective EHR data that reflects local⏎documentation practices rather than standardized, longitudinal clinical context. Data⏎completeness, consistency, and provenance — disciplines that are foundational to enterprise data⏎governance frameworks — are inconsistently applied in clinical AI development environments.⏎The result is AI tools whose performance is difficult to reproduce across institutions, populations,⏎or care settings.⏎Interoperability standards such as FHIR have significantly advanced data access and exchange;⏎however, interoperability for AI governance must extend beyond data exchange to encompass⏎metadata, data provenance, evaluation artifacts, and governance policy documentation. Without⏎these additional layers, organizations cannot assess AI performance with the rigor that regulated⏎care environments require. HHS investment in shared data governance infrastructure — including⏎provenance standards, evaluation metadata frameworks, and interoperability requirements⏎specific to AI system inputs and outputs — would materially reduce this barrier.⏎3.6 State-Level Implementation Constraints: Missouri MO HealthNet⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎The barriers described above are particularly acute at the state Medicaid program level, where⏎agencies are simultaneously managing the demands of legacy system modernization, new⏎federal compliance requirements, and pressure to integrate AI-enabled solutions — often with⏎constrained resources and limited access to the specialized expertise required to govern AI⏎deployments responsibly.⏎Missouri's MO HealthNet division exemplifies this challenge. MO HealthNet is currently navigating⏎a multi-year Medicaid Management Information System (MMIS) modernization effort, new federal⏎work requirement verification obligations under the recently enacted Medicaid work requirements⏎framework, and a $216 million rural health transformation initiative funded by CMS. Each of these⏎programs involves significant IT modernization work — and each creates a potential entry point⏎for AI-enabled tools to improve program administration, eligibility verification, care coordination,⏎and reporting.⏎Yet Missouri's current technology infrastructure and governance capability are not yet positioned⏎to responsibly deploy AI tools at scale across these programs without the foundational⏎governance, interoperability, and monitoring infrastructure that HHS policy can help establish.⏎The MO HealthNet context is offered here not as an isolated case but as a representative example⏎of the implementation reality facing state Medicaid agencies nationwide — a constituency that⏎HHS policy should explicitly address.⏎4.0 The Need for Lifecycle-Based Evaluation and Continuous⏎Monitoring⏎Effective AI adoption in clinical care requires a fundamental shift in evaluation philosophy — from⏎episodic, pre-deployment validation to lifecycle-based assessment that persists through the full⏎operational life of the AI system. This is not a novel concept in software engineering; it is standard⏎practice in mature software development organizations and is directly applicable to clinical AI⏎governance.⏎4.1 Pre-Deployment Versus Post-Deployment Evaluation⏎Current practice in clinical AI evaluation is heavily weighted toward pre-deployment testing⏎conducted under controlled conditions. While necessary, pre-deployment evaluation provides⏎limited insight into how AI tools perform when integrated into the full complexity of real-world⏎clinical workflows, diverse patient populations, and variable data quality environments. SDLC⏎governance practice addresses this through structured user acceptance testing, pilot deployment⏎phases with defined success criteria, and post-deployment performance review cycles —⏎disciplines that should be incorporated into clinical AI governance requirements.⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎4.2 Continuous Performance Monitoring and Drift Detection⏎AI systems are sensitive to environmental change in ways that traditional software is not. Model⏎drift — the degradation of AI output quality as the operating environment evolves — is a well-⏎documented phenomenon that requires active monitoring to detect and manage. Continuous⏎performance monitoring, including automated drift detection, anomaly flagging, and periodic⏎revalidation cycles, should be established as a standard requirement for AI tools operating in⏎federally funded clinical environments.⏎HHS can support this requirement by establishing minimum monitoring standards as a condition⏎of Medicaid and Medicare program participation for AI tool vendors, and by investing in shared⏎monitoring infrastructure that state agencies and smaller health systems can leverage without⏎building duplicative internal capability.⏎4.3 Interoperability Beyond Data Exchange⏎Interoperability requirements for clinical AI must extend beyond the exchange of clinical data to⏎encompass the full information environment in which AI systems operate. This includes metadata⏎standards for AI input and output documentation, provenance requirements for training and⏎validation data, evaluation artifact standards that enable cross-institutional performance⏎comparison, and governance policy interoperability that allows oversight mechanisms to function⏎across organizational boundaries.⏎Without these additional interoperability layers, clinical AI deployments will remain fragmented,⏎difficult to evaluate at scale, and resistant to the kind of comparative effectiveness assessment⏎that informed policy and reimbursement decisions require.⏎5.0 Regulatory and Policy Considerations⏎The regulatory landscape for clinical AI is characterized by a mismatch between existing⏎frameworks — designed for discrete medical devices and static health IT systems — and the⏎operational reality of non-medical device AI that evolves continuously, operates across⏎organizational boundaries, and influences care indirectly through workflows and information⏎presentation. Addressing this mismatch does not require comprehensive new regulation; it⏎requires targeted policy guidance that brings AI governance into alignment with established⏎software engineering disciplines.⏎5.1 Adopt SDLC Maturity Frameworks as AI Governance Standards⏎HHS should consider formally adopting SDLC maturity frameworks — specifically the Capability⏎Maturity Model Integration (CMMI) or equivalent standards — as the governance baseline for⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎non-medical device AI operating in federally funded clinical environments. These frameworks are⏎well-established, widely understood by the IT vendor community, and directly applicable to the⏎lifecycle management challenges that currently constrain responsible AI adoption. Requiring AI⏎tool vendors to demonstrate SDLC maturity at a defined level as a condition of program⏎participation would provide a practical, verifiable governance standard that does not require⏎development of novel regulatory infrastructure.⏎5.2 Clarify Accountability and Governance Responsibilities⏎HHS should issue guidance that explicitly defines accountability expectations for AI tool⏎developers, system integrators, and deploying health institutions — including documentation⏎requirements, escalation procedures, and performance remediation obligations. This guidance⏎should be structured to apply to the full stakeholder ecosystem of a clinical AI deployment, not⏎only to the AI tool developer, and should be designed to integrate with existing compliance⏎frameworks including HIPAA, the CMS Conditions of Participation, and state Medicaid program⏎requirements.⏎5.3 Establish AI-Specific Requirements Within Medicaid IT Standards⏎CMS should establish AI-specific requirements within the existing Medicaid IT standards⏎framework — specifically the Medicaid Information Technology Architecture (MITA) framework⏎and the standards and conditions governing MMIS design, development, and implementation.⏎Integrating AI governance requirements into MITA would provide a direct mechanism for⏎extending SDLC-based governance standards to the state Medicaid program context, ensuring⏎that AI tools deployed within Medicaid infrastructure meet minimum governance, monitoring, and⏎accountability requirements as a condition of the enhanced federal matching funds available for⏎Medicaid IT investments.⏎6.0 Reimbursement Pathways for AI-Assisted Care⏎Reimbursement policy is among the most powerful instruments available to HHS for shaping AI⏎adoption behavior in clinical care environments. Current reimbursement frameworks create⏎limited incentive for health systems to invest in the governance infrastructure required to deploy⏎AI tools responsibly — because governance investment is not reimbursable, and because⏎reimbursement for AI-assisted care is inconsistent and poorly defined.⏎6.1 Create Pilot Reimbursement Pathways for Governed AI Deployments⏎CMS should establish pilot reimbursement pathways — through the Physician Fee Schedule,⏎Medicaid managed care authorities, or CMS Innovation Center models — specifically for AI-⏎assisted care coordination and clinical decision support tools that meet defined governance⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎standards. Tying reimbursement eligibility to governance requirements creates a direct financial⏎incentive for health systems to invest in SDLC-based AI governance — and provides CMS with a⏎practical mechanism for evaluating the effectiveness of those governance requirements in real-⏎world deployment contexts.⏎6.2 Align Medicaid Enhanced Match Incentives with AI Governance Standards⏎The 90% enhanced federal matching rate available for Medicaid IT system design, development,⏎and implementation should be explicitly extended to cover the SDLC governance infrastructure⏎required for responsible AI deployment — including monitoring systems, evaluation frameworks,⏎and interoperability capabilities. States are currently incentivized to invest in IT infrastructure⏎generally, but lack specific guidance on how AI governance investments qualify for enhanced⏎matching. Clarifying this eligibility would materially reduce the financial barrier to responsible AI⏎adoption at the state Medicaid program level — a direct benefit to programs such as Missouri's⏎MO HealthNet that are simultaneously managing modernization and AI integration demands.⏎7.0 Role of R&D and Public-Private Partnerships⏎Research and development investment by HHS should be directed toward the foundational⏎infrastructure gaps that constrain responsible AI adoption — not toward additional AI model⏎development, which the private sector is pursuing aggressively and at scale. The comparative⏎advantage of HHS R&D investment lies in creating shared public goods: evaluation frameworks,⏎governance standards, interoperability infrastructure, and implementation playbooks that private-⏎sector entities cannot produce individually.⏎7.1 Fund SDLC Governance Playbooks for Clinical AI⏎HHS should fund the development of implementation playbooks that translate SDLC governance⏎disciplines into practical, actionable guidance for clinical AI deployment — specifically designed⏎for use by state Medicaid agencies, community health systems, and mid-market health IT⏎organizations that lack the internal resources to develop governance frameworks independently.⏎These playbooks should address the full AI deployment lifecycle from requirements definition⏎through post-deployment monitoring, and should be developed through cooperative agreements⏎that include practitioner-level IT consultants with direct experience in regulated government and⏎healthcare environments.⏎7.2 Establish State Medicaid AI Governance Pilots⏎HHS should fund cooperative agreements with a cohort of state Medicaid agencies to pilot SDLC-⏎based AI governance frameworks within active program modernization contexts. Missouri's MO⏎HealthNet is a strong candidate for such a pilot, given the convergence of active MMIS⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎modernization, new work requirement verification obligations, and CMS-funded rural health⏎transformation investment that collectively create a rich testing environment for AI governance⏎frameworks. Pilot findings should be published as shared learning resources available to all state⏎Medicaid programs.⏎7.3 Prioritize Mid-Market and Practitioner Voices in R&D Investment⏎HHS R&D investment in clinical AI has historically been concentrated in large academic medical⏎centers and major defense and technology contractors. The implementation challenges that⏎constrain AI adoption in community health systems, state Medicaid agencies, and smaller health⏎organizations are distinct from those facing large institutions — and the solutions developed in⏎large institutional contexts are frequently not transferable. HHS should explicitly prioritize mid-⏎market IT consultants, regional health systems, and state agency partners in cooperative⏎agreements and applied research investments, ensuring that AI governance solutions are⏎developed and validated in the contexts where adoption challenges are greatest.⏎8.0 Closing Recommendations⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC respectfully offers the following summary recommendations for HHS⏎consideration in developing policy and programmatic responses to this RFI. These⏎recommendations are grounded in practitioner experience in regulated IT and public-sector⏎environments, and are designed to be actionable, verifiable, and scalable across the diversity of⏎clinical care contexts in which AI adoption is occurring.⏎Section Recommendation Primary Policy Area⏎Section 3.1 Adopt CMMI or equivalent SDLC maturity Regulation⏎frameworks as governance standards for non-⏎medical device AI in federally funded clinical⏎environments⏎Section 3.2 Establish post-deployment monitoring Regulation⏎requirements — including drift detection and⏎performance review cycles — as conditions of⏎AI tool participation in Medicaid and Medicare⏎programs⏎Section 3.3 Issue guidance defining accountability, Regulation⏎documentation, and escalation requirements⏎across the full stakeholder ecosystem of clinical⏎AI deployments⏎Section 3.6 Explicitly address state Medicaid program Regulation / R&D⏎implementation constraints in HHS AI⏎governance guidance, including MO HealthNet⏎as a representative case⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎Section Recommendation Primary Policy Area⏎Section 5.3 Integrate AI governance requirements into the Regulation⏎MITA framework and Medicaid IT standards and⏎conditions⏎Section 6.1 Create pilot reimbursement pathways for AI- Reimbursement⏎assisted care tools that meet defined SDLC⏎governance standards⏎Section 6.2 Clarify 90% enhanced federal match eligibility Reimbursement⏎for SDLC governance infrastructure investments⏎supporting AI deployment in state Medicaid⏎programs⏎Section 7.1 Fund development of SDLC governance R&D⏎implementation playbooks for clinical AI,⏎designed for use by state Medicaid agencies⏎and mid-market health IT organizations⏎Section 7.2 Establish state Medicaid AI governance pilots, R&D⏎including Missouri MO HealthNet, to develop⏎and validate governance frameworks in active⏎modernization contexts⏎Section 7.3 Prioritize mid-market IT consultants and state R&D⏎agency partners in HHS AI cooperative⏎agreements and applied research investments⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC welcomes the opportunity to provide additional input, engage in⏎follow-up consultation, or participate in any technical working groups HHS convenes in response⏎to this RFI. We are available at the contact information provided on the cover page of this⏎submission.⏎Appendix A: Question Crosswalk⏎The following table maps the specific questions posed in HHS RFI RIN 0955-AA13 to the⏎corresponding sections of this response.⏎RFI Question Summary Response Section Policy Area⏎Question⏎Q1 What regulatory, payment policy, or Sections 5.0, 6.0 Regulation /⏎programmatic changes should HHS Reimbursement⏎prioritize to incentivize effective AI use in⏎clinical care?⏎Q2 How can payment structures be Section 6.1, 6.2 Reimbursement⏎simplified and aligned to support efficient⏎AI technologies?⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page⏎⏎Total Quality Solutions, LLC | RFI Response: HHS RIN 0955-AA13 | February 23, 2026⏎RFI Question Summary Response Section Policy Area⏎Question⏎Q3 What are the biggest barriers to private Section 3.0 (all Regulation⏎sector AI innovation in healthcare? subsections)⏎Q4 What best practices should HHS adopt to Sections 4.0, 5.1, 5.2 Regulation⏎support private sector AI activities?⏎Q5 How can HHS invest in R&D to integrate Section 7.0 (all R&D⏎AI in care delivery? subsections)⏎Q6 How can HHS support public-private Sections 7.2, 7.3 R&D⏎partnerships for clinical AI?⏎Q7 What interoperability standards are Sections 3.5, 4.3 Regulation⏎needed for AI in clinical workflows?⏎Q8 How should HHS address accountability Sections 3.3, 5.2 Regulation⏎and governance for non-medical device⏎AI?⏎Q9 How can HHS address equity in clinical Sections 3.5, 3.6, 7.3 Regulation /⏎AI adoption? R&D⏎Q10 What role should state Medicaid Sections 3.6, 5.3, 6.2, All three areas⏎programs play in AI adoption? 7.2⏎End of Response — Total Quality Solutions, LLC | solutions@tqsolve.com | 314-605-6519⏎CONFIDENTIAL — FOR HHS USE ONLY Page♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0470♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from National Association of ACOs, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔National Association of ACOs♔♔♔♔♔♔February, 23 2026⏎The Honorable Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Attention: RIN 0955-AA13⏎Submitted electronically to: http://www.regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎The National Association of ACOs (NAACOS) appreciates the opportunity to submit comments in⏎response to the request for information on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care. NAACOS is a member-led and member-governed nonprofit of nearly 500⏎accountable care organizations (ACOs) and value-based care entities in Medicare, Medicaid, and⏎commercial insurance working on behalf of health care providers across the nation to improve the⏎quality of care for patients and reduce health care cost. Collectively, our members are accountable for⏎the care of more than 10 million beneficiaries through Medicare’s population health-focused payment⏎and delivery models, including the Medicare Shared Savings Program (MSSP) and the Accountable Care⏎Organization Realizing Equity, Access, and Community Health (ACO REACH) Model.⏎With responsibility for total cost of care and clinical outcomes, providers in accountable care⏎continuously seek tools that use data seamlessly, support patient engagement, and drive improvement⏎in clinical outcomes. AI holds such promise and we support innovation through the widespread adoption⏎of AI tools for clinical care; however, we are concerned that existing complex governance and regulatory⏎barriers, data and infrastructure challenges, and low returns on investment continue to hamper⏎progress. Our comments below reflect the shared goal of an AI technology-enabled interoperable health⏎care ecosystem, that fosters competition while shielding providers from undue burden.⏎Barriers to AI adoption and Use in Clinical Care⏎ACOs are exploring potential uses of AI with the goal of improving patient care and providing real-time⏎data and feedback at the point of care to inform clinical care. For example, some ACOs leverage AI for⏎risk stratification while others are deploying AI technologies that automate outreach to lower-risk⏎patients, extending care managers’ capacity for beneficiary engagement. While there are many potential⏎benefits to the use of AI, ACOs report that navigating through the compliance, legal, and internal⏎approval processes can be challenging and require building a new AI governance committee structure⏎that previously did not exist. In addition, they encounter challenges with integrating these tools into⏎electronic health record (EHR) products; however, the benefits outweigh the burden of implementation⏎2001 L Street, NW, Suite 500, Washington, D.C. 20036 ⚫ 202-640-1985 ⚫ info@naacos.com⏎www.naacos.com⏎⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 2 of 4⏎as these solutions are much more difficult for patients and providers to use if they run separately from⏎the EHR.⏎NAACOS encourages HHS to create avenues for providers to receive incentives or payment for⏎leveraging AI in clinical care. Currently, providers in alternative payment models (APMs) invest in these⏎technologies using shared savings achieved through model and advanced APM incentives, which are⏎received long after the performance year. Upfront incentives (i.e., pre-paid shared savings and⏎capitation options) have enabled providers to make more timely investments in technology, and any⏎incentive approach must ensure that these investments can be sustained. Beyond these options, HHS⏎should consider:⏎• Payment approaches to bill Medicare for the use of AI tools in clinical care. The recently⏎announced ACCESS Model provides payment for technology-enabled care but this model is not⏎an available option for providers who bill Medicare for other services. CMS could explore similar⏎technology-enabled payments for providers in an ACO to test approaches for paying providers⏎for technology-enabled care.⏎• Cost sharing arrangements between the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS) and⏎APMs, like the Medicaid State Systems where the federal government provides a percentage of⏎the cost. Additionally, HHS could sponsor group purchasing arrangements for these initiatives to⏎enable individual provider use of these solutions without needing individual contracting.⏎In any incentive approach, we encourage consideration of prior obstacles:⏎• Overcome provider and patient uncertainty. There is a need to demonstrate the use and value⏎of AI tools to both providers and patients, as patient and provider knowledge of products can be⏎limited. Patients and providers must also be educated on the benefits and risks of AI to ensure⏎that the resulting data are appropriately understood and used.⏎• Test approaches prior to adoption. There should be support for APM participants to test and⏎implement these technologies without suffering any consequences of technology failure, or⏎unforeseen consequences. For example, a testing lab invites APM participants to gain⏎experience with AI products before full scale implementation would help improve the tool and⏎incentivize long-term use.⏎• Avoid overly prescriptive approaches. In lieu of requiring any one technology or approach, CMS⏎should focus on achieving a particular outcome and allow APM participants the ability to adopt⏎technologies based on their patients’ and the organization’s needs and capabilities.⏎• Prevent an overabundance of data. There is the potential for duplicative and/or conflicting data⏎resulting from use of AI technologies. These new data streams could potentially overwhelm⏎practices. There must be limits on the type and amount of data that are sent to providers/care⏎teams so that they receive the information that is most relevant for clinical decision-making.⏎Regulatory and Policy Changes to Support AI Adoption⏎A uniform federal regulatory framework the use of AI in health care is imperative since the current⏎environment remains fragmented where regulations and requirements on vary significantly across⏎states. Coordination at the federal and state levels will further assist efforts to standardize the use of AI⏎in health care. This framework should include updates to the Health Insurance Portability and⏎Accountability Act (HIPAA) privacy and security rules, as needed, to confirm that patient data resulting⏎from these AI tools are used responsibly. Safeguards must also be in place to foster innovation while⏎⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 3 of 4⏎also ensuring clear liability protections for providers. Additionally, there must be consideration for safe⏎and appropriate use of these tools by all providers including small, rural, and other entities for whom⏎these technologies may not be easily afforded and adopted.⏎National standards must be created for data quality, security, interoperability, and data accessibility,⏎including definitions, benchmarking tools, and other resources against which developers and⏎implementers can compare the various AI technologies. Standards that ensure transparency (e.g.,⏎disclosure of AI capabilities, limitations, monitoring) and the safe and effective use of AI must also be⏎established. Each of these standards will ensure that the results are safe, secure, and reduce potential⏎bias. In addition, federated AI learning modules should be leveraged to test and optimize these tools⏎while providers continue to retain ownership of the data. A database of validated datasets for AI⏎solutions would also facilitate greater explainability and confidence in their use. All these efforts can⏎also ensure that AI solutions selected by providers are valid for their intended use, while minimizing⏎risks – such as inaccurate data – that could be used in clinical decision making resulting in possible⏎patient harm.⏎It will be critical for federal agencies to collaborate with providers as this framework is created. ACOs⏎should play an important role in advising on the development, adoption and implementation of AI,⏎including design, development, governance, rulemaking, standards-setting, post-market surveillance and⏎clinical integration.. ACOs have many years of experience implementing new and novel technologies to⏎derive actionable insights from patient data. Many are already actively implementing or exploring how⏎to integrate AI into their activities. It is essential that any final requirements around AI’s use ensure the⏎technologies are scalable, secure, and transportable while minimizing potential bias and maintaining⏎robust privacy and security practices.⏎HHS Opportunities to Support Private Sector Activities⏎ACOs and providers in accountable care currently use AI solutions and other technologies and see⏎opportunities for improved use. As stated above, barriers of cost, education, ROI, and compatibility with⏎EHRs are major obstacles to overcome and voluntary adoption of the AI solutions is preferable.⏎CMS/ASTP should provide more support for vetting currently available tools (e.g., transparency in⏎expected costs, assessments on whether tool does what it purports to do). These agencies could also⏎undertake certification of these products or at a minimum require transparency from vendors on their⏎capabilities to enable safe and transparent use of AI solutions. Specifically, consideration should be⏎given to:⏎• The costs and benefits of individual adoption of products and their capabilities in developing⏎reports provided to the public.⏎• Integration into clinical workflows to minimize disruption and burden with specific attention to⏎whether these tools can integrate across all EHR instances and versions and particularly those⏎used by small and rural providers.⏎• Integration with health information exchanges (HIEs) or state data to provide better overall⏎population data for APM participants to use.⏎CONCLUSION⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback on Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. NAACOS and its members are committed to providing the highest⏎⏎February 23, 2026⏎Page 4 of 4⏎quality care for patients while advancing population health goals for the communities they serve. We⏎look forward to our continued engagement to leverage AI to support clinical care. If you have any⏎questions, please contact Aisha Pittman, senior vice president of government affairs at NAACOS at⏎aisha_pittman@naacos.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Emily D. Brower⏎President and CEO⏎NAACOS♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0471♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Heart Failure Society of America (HFSA), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Heart Failure Society of America (HFSA)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Dr. Thomas Keane⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎RE: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane:⏎On behalf of the Heart Failure Society of America (HFSA), we appreciate the opportunity to provide⏎input in response to the request for information (RFI) on how the Department of Health and⏎Human Services (HHS) can help shape the development, evaluation, and deployment of artificial⏎intelligence (AI) in clinical care. HFSA is a multidisciplinary organization working to improve and⏎expand heart failure care through collaboration, education, research, innovation, and advocacy.⏎HFSA members include physicians, scientists, nurses, nurse practitioners, pharmacists, and⏎patients.⏎Through this RFI, HHS seeks feedback on how regulations, reimbursement policies, and research⏎and development efforts can be shaped to better support the rapid adoption and effective use of⏎AI in clinical care, while fostering public trust and aligning with federal priorities to enhance⏎productivity, reduce burden, lower health care costs, and improve health outcomes.⏎Heart failure offers a compelling use case for the responsible adoption of AI in clinical care. It is⏎a common, resource-intensive chronic condition that is managed across complex, longitudinal⏎care pathways and workflows. Approximately 6.7 million Americans over the age of 20 are⏎currently living with heart failure and the prevalence is expected to increase substantially over⏎the coming decades, rising to 11.4 million by 2050. The lifetime risk of developing heart failure⏎is 24%, meaning approximately one in four individuals will develop the condition. In 2022, heart⏎failure was a contributing cause in more than 425,000 deaths, accounting for 45% of all⏎cardiovascular deaths nationwide.1⏎Significant gaps, variation, and disparities persist in the use of guideline-recommended therapies⏎for heart failure. Fewer than one in four eligible patients with heart failure with reduced ejection⏎fraction receive comprehensive quadruple guideline-directed medical therapy (GDMT), despite⏎clear evidence of its benefit. Broader and more consistent implementation of these therapies⏎could have a profound impact on outcomes, with estimates suggesting that optimal uptake could⏎prevent approximately 1.19 million deaths globally each year.2⏎This creates clear opportunities for AI to improve operational efficiency, reduce clinician burden,⏎and increase reliability of delivering evidence-based care. This potential is reflected in a recent⏎research and development initiative offered through HHS’ Advanced Research Projects Agency for⏎Health (ARPA-H), which seeks to advance agentic AI technologies as “clinician extenders.” Through⏎1 Fonarow GC, et al. HF STATS 2025: Heart Failure Epidemiology and Outcomes Statistics—An Updated⏎2025 Report From the Heart Failure Society of America. Journal of Cardiac Failure. 2025;32(2):439–498.⏎2 Fonarow GC, et al. HF STATS 2025.⏎⏎the Agentic AI-Enabled Cardiovascular Care Transformation (ADVOCATE) program, ARPA-H is⏎soliciting proposals to develop agentic AI systems to support clinical care for patients with heart⏎failure and individuals recovering from myocardial infarction.⏎At the same time, heart failure care is inherently high risk. Poorly governed or inadequately⏎evaluated AI tools could cause patient harm through preventable hospitalizations, medication-⏎related adverse events, or delayed escalation of care. Accordingly, the adoption of AI in clinical⏎care must be accompanied by robust governance, rigorous prospective evaluation, and⏎continuous post-deployment monitoring to ensure safety, effectiveness, and equity across patient⏎populations.⏎Recommended Principles⏎To ensure that AI is used appropriately in clinical care, HFSA recommends that HHS consider the⏎following principles when establishing policies to support the deployment of AI technologies:⏎• Establish risk-proportionate clinician governance and accountability for AI used in⏎clinical care. AI tools that inform diagnoses, triage, medication titration, escalation⏎decisions, or discharge planning should be deployed only with clear clinical ownership⏎and defined human oversight. HHS should promote governance expectations that are⏎proportionate to a tool’s clinical impact, including an explicit intended-use statement,⏎documented workflows for clinician override, and auditable logs of AI outputs and⏎clinician actions. These measures clarify accountability, preserve appropriate clinical⏎judgment, and support public trust while enabling innovation.⏎• AI-enabled care should be designed to address the full heart failure continuum. AI⏎initiatives in heart failure should be grounded in an understanding of heart failure as a⏎continuum. Approximately one-third of U.S. adults are at risk for heart failure (Stage A)⏎and nearly 24-34% have pre-heart failure (Stage B). While prevalence of stage C2D and⏎Stage D heart failure requires further examination, it has been estimated that 5-7% of⏎patients with symptomatic heart failure have advanced heart failure. Rising rates of⏎obesity diabetes, hypertension, and chronic kidney disease are accelerating progression⏎across this continuum. 3 Despite clear evidence-based therapies, substantial gaps,⏎variations, and disparities in use of guideline-recommended therapies persist, as noted⏎above. Effective AI must support surveillance, prevention, early detection, treatment⏎optimization, and advanced care across all stages of heart failure.⏎• Align reimbursement and federal incentives to support implementation, monitoring,⏎and value-based adoption of AI-enabled care. Many clinically meaningful AI tools require⏎substantial upfront investment in integration, workflow redesign, training, and ongoing⏎surveillance. These costs are often difficult for providers to absorb without a reliable⏎reimbursement pathway. HHS, largely through the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid⏎Services (CMS), should prioritize payment approaches that reward demonstrated clinical⏎utility and burden reduction, while avoiding per-alert or per-use incentives that may drive⏎volume without value. Time-limited implementation support and demonstration models⏎for AI-enabled care pathways would reduce adoption friction while promoting responsible⏎scale. CMS should look to offer incentives to providers that reward safe deployment of AI.⏎• Shift evaluation expectations from retrospective accuracy to prospective clinical⏎utility, safety, and equity in real workflows. HHS should invest in research evaluating⏎whether and how AI improves outcomes and reduces clinician burden without introducing⏎new harms in the real world. Retrospective performance metrics alone are insufficient to⏎justify clinical deployment. Evaluation frameworks should incorporate human factors⏎3 Fonarow GC, et al. HF STATS 2025.⏎2⏎⏎testing, alert burden and usability metrics, calibration and subgroup performance, and⏎clinically meaningful safety endpoints. HHS can accelerate responsible adoption by⏎supporting pragmatic, multi-site randomized trials and post-deployment monitoring that⏎measure real-world impact.⏎• Establish comprehensive lifecycle evaluation framework for AI tools. Appropriate HHS⏎authorities should oversee pre-deployment evaluation and lifecycle regulation of AI tools⏎that meet criteria for Software as a Medical Device, including requirements for clinical⏎validation, transparency, and post-market performance monitoring to address model drift⏎and safety risks in heart failure populations. In particular, the Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy/Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎(ASTP/ONC) should establish interoperability and auditability standards to ensure AI tools⏎integrate reliably with electronic health records and support clinician interpretability and⏎governance. CMS should also align reimbursement and quality measurement approaches⏎with broader CMS initiatives, such as the Ambulatory Specialty Model and the Advancing⏎Chronic Care with Effective, Scalable Solutions Model, to support coordinated adoption of⏎high-value AI-enabled care.⏎HFSA appreciates the opportunity to provide input as HHS continues to evaluate policies to⏎responsibly accelerate adoption of AI in clinical care. We encourage HHS to maintain ongoing⏎engagement with representative clinical experts and patient populations in the development of⏎AI-related policies to ensure that resulting recommendations are accurate, precise, and grounded⏎in real-world clinical practice. If you have questions or require additional information, please⏎contact Sue Ramthun at sramthun@hfsa.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Kenneth B. Margulies, MD⏎President, Heart Failure Society of America⏎3♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0472♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Epic Systems, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Epic Systems♔♔♔♔♔♔1⏎February 20, 2026⏎Steven Posnack⏎Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW, Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted Electronically⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Mr. Posnack:⏎Thank you for the opportunity to respond to your RFI regarding accelerating the adoption and⏎use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. Epic‘s software has included various⏎forms of AI for decades; currently we have over 175 use cases of generative AI released or in⏎active development.⏎Through our work with customers, we have developed a deep understanding of how to speed⏎the adoption of AI in clinical settings. For example, Epic offers the Jump Start Cloud⏎Infrastructure program, which has resulted in customers increasing their AI use 2.4 times faster,⏎by expanding use of existing features and adopting new ones. Drawing on this experience, we⏎are pleased to offer several ideas for how the Department can encourage AI uptake.⏎We would be happy to answer any questions you may have and would welcome further⏎collaboration on your work to promote AI adoption in clinical care.⏎Sincerely,⏎Ladd Wiley David Hoffert⏎Senior Vice President for Global Corporate Director of AI Policy⏎Affairs, Public Policy, and Advocacy⏎| 1979 Milky Way, Verona, WI 53593 | (608) 271-9000 | FAX (608) 271-7237 | www.epic.com⏎⏎2⏎Getting to “On”⏎Generative AI differs from prior healthcare innovations in ways that can make clinical⏎leadership reluctant to adopt and scale it. Because generative AI is probabilistic, its⏎performance must be validated not only before deployment but also continuously over time,⏎requiring skills and resources that may be scarce in healthcare settings. Its costs—driven by⏎compute, storage, and model usage—are also highly variable and difficult for organizations to⏎predict, budget, or govern using traditional software pricing models.⏎At the same time, AI delivers meaningful benefits: improved clinician productivity, reduced⏎documentation burden, lower burnout, more timely clinical decision-making, fewer missed⏎orders, improved throughput, and better patient engagement. Over time, these gains can⏎translate into reduced staffing pressure and overtime costs, higher-quality documentation,⏎improved access and satisfaction, and better clinical outcomes through earlier interventions.⏎Realizing these benefits requires healthcare leaders to move past understandable initial⏎hesitation and turn AI features on. While familiarity and trust with new technology develop⏎over time, we believe there are concrete steps the Department can take now to better align⏎incentives and accelerate adoption.⏎Documenting ROI⏎One question any responsible healthcare administrator will ask before deploying new AI⏎technology is whether it represents a sound investment. Healthcare organizations operate⏎today on thinner margins than ever; for many, it simply is not an option to invest in⏎technologies that will not pay off financially. We believe many applications of generative AI in⏎healthcare do present sufficient return on investment (ROI).⏎The Department could help document ROI data in a way that healthcare providers can both⏎rely and act on. For example, studies show that AI scribe technology—in which AI records and⏎transcribes clinical interactions and then drafts corresponding notes—can reduce the time⏎clinicians spend working after hours. Less “pajama time” for providers, however, does not by⏎itself offset the cost of the technology. The true ROI of AI scribes appears downstream: happier⏎clinicians are less likely to burn out or leave, reducing recruitment and onboarding costs.⏎The Department could address this gap by assembling a repository of AI use cases with⏎demonstrated ROI. By conducting independent research, such a repository would validate⏎vendor claims and highlight metrics relevant to healthcare administrators’ bottom-line⏎decisions. Such a repository would be vendor-agnostic, focusing on use cases rather than⏎specific implementations, to not advantage those vendors that get their claims published.⏎Done right, it would serve as a trusted reference for organizations evaluating AI investments.⏎| 1979 Milky Way, Verona, WI 53593 | (608) 271-9000 | FAX (608) 271-7237 | www.epic.com⏎⏎3⏎Bridging the Adoption-to-ROI Gap⏎Even when an AI use case has a proven ROI, initial adoption may still be financially challenging.⏎The benefits of AI are rarely immediate and often require workflow changes, training, and⏎iteration to be fully realized. Early phases of adoption are typically marked by uneven usage,⏎ongoing configuration, and infrastructure costs that scale before productivity gains materialize.⏎As a result, organizations may incur real costs before seeing offsetting savings, creating a⏎financial mismatch that can slow or stall adoption.⏎One response to these challenges would be fee-for-service reimbursement for AI use, but this⏎approach could quickly become costly and, for AI with proven ROI, unnecessary. Organizations⏎primarily need support through the initial scaling period; once broad adoption is achieved, AI⏎that delivers value will pay for itself.⏎Epic has had success spurring AI adoption among customers with our Jump Start Cloud⏎Infrastructure Package, a program we created to simplify and support early adoption of⏎generative AI. It covers the cloud infrastructure costs for roughly 50 AI features with a flat⏎monthly fee based on organization size. This approach encourages responsible, broad⏎adoption rather than narrow pilots driven by short-term cost avoidance.⏎The results have been impressive. Jump Start customers have demonstrated median month-⏎over-month AI resource utilization growth of 45%, compared to 18% for non-Jump Start⏎customers. They also adopt more new AI features—5.5 on average, compared to 4.6 over the⏎same period. Overall, they use 17 times more AI resources than non-Jump Start customers of⏎comparable size. These differences are not explained by organization size or early-adopter⏎effects; in fact, Jump Start customers are slightly smaller on average. Rather, Jump Start⏎succeeds because it aligns incentives to encourage rapid scaling. Once customers reach broad⏎adoption, they see the value of AI—and continue using it.⏎The Department could achieve similar incentive alignment via AI adoption grants that cover⏎infrastructure costs for a fixed period of time rather than a fixed amount of money. Traditional⏎grants incentivize slow scaling to extend funding; time-based grants would encourage⏎organizations to scale quickly, assess value, and determine which use cases merit continued⏎investment. After the grant period ends, organizations could discontinue low-value AI while⏎sustaining AI that delivers sufficient benefit—without ongoing government spending.⏎Such programs could be structured as true grants or as fixed-price arrangements similar to⏎Jump Start. In the latter case, however, pricing should scale with organization size to avoid⏎disadvantaging smaller or lower-resourced providers. (If desired, pricing could even be⏎structured disproportionately to give an advantage to smaller organizations.) Properly⏎| 1979 Milky Way, Verona, WI 53593 | (608) 271-9000 | FAX (608) 271-7237 | www.epic.com⏎⏎4⏎designed, time-based infrastructure support would help organizations escape the “pilot trap”⏎and reach sustainable AI adoption.⏎Making Local Validation Possible⏎Another concern AI adopters rightly have is safety. Many healthcare AI use cases fall outside⏎the oversight of the Food and Drug Administration, and even regulated products are poorly⏎served by traditional evaluation methods. Especially for AI that is probabilistic, pre-deployment⏎testing on generalized datasets has limited predictive value for real-world performance.⏎The better method for validating AI performance in healthcare is “local validation,” in which the⏎healthcare facility, independent of the developer, evaluates how the AI performs on its own⏎data while embedded in its own workflow. This can be done both prior to deployment, using⏎local but retrospective data, and on a continual basis post-deployment (“ongoing monitoring”)⏎to make sure performance does not “drift” over time. This way, AI features can be deemed safe⏎no matter what patient acuity mix is being served by a particular healthcare organization.⏎Local validation also matters because AI performance can be sensitive to the workflow into⏎which the AI is integrated. For example, a popular use case for predictive modelling forecasts⏎the likelihood of imminent medical deterioration. The output of such a model is not binary but⏎instead a probability. When implementing this model, a deployer must decide what probability⏎threshold should trigger an alert for the care team to investigate further. This choice has a⏎significant impact on the “performance” of the model. If an organization sets the alert⏎threshold low, there will likely be more false positives; if the organization sets the threshold⏎higher, there will likely be more false negatives. Yet the organization may have good reason to⏎change this setting: organizations with dedicated remote monitoring teams may prefer false⏎positives, while those who must rely only on frontline nurses managing a large caseload of⏎patients may not be able to tolerate them.⏎The challenge for post-deployment validation, in healthcare at least, is that small or rural⏎healthcare organizations often struggle to afford the necessary data science resources and⏎expertise to perform effective monitoring. Yet because AI performance will vary from⏎organization to organization, post-deployment validation is not something that can be⏎outsourced in practice. Without appropriate support for “local validation” activities, AI could⏎widen, rather than narrow, the digital divide in healthcare.⏎AI developers can help. In 2024, for example, Epic released an open-source tool called the AI⏎Trust and Assurance Suite that automates the most resource-intensive aspects of post-⏎deployment monitoring. The suite's real-time evaluations use the deployer's local data and⏎workflows and can monitor both Epic and third-party AI features, using the deployer's choice⏎of evaluation methodologies.⏎| 1979 Milky Way, Verona, WI 53593 | (608) 271-9000 | FAX (608) 271-7237 | www.epic.com⏎⏎5⏎However, even with access to the Suite, many lower-resourced healthcare organizations report⏎that staffing and time constraints remain a barrier. A targeted grant program to support local⏎validation for small and rural providers could help address this gap.⏎Providing Guidance for AI Validation⏎Even with the tools and resources needed to conduct local validation, healthcare organizations⏎may not know how to use them effectively. Today, there is little consensus on how to evaluate⏎traditional predictive models, and even less for generative AI. Gold-standard guidance such as⏎the National Institute of Standards and Technology’s AI Risk Management Framework asserts⏎the importance of ongoing monitoring but does not specify how to perform it. Vendors⏎providing evaluation services may use proprietary metrics or allow the user to select among⏎evaluation methodologies (as the AI Trust and Assurance Suite does); both limit comparability.⏎Regulators can add value by defining standard metrics and validation processes for AI⏎performance. Such standards need not be mandatory; guidance alone would increase⏎confidence among healthcare AI adopters and lower the barrier to widescale adoption. Clear,⏎shared methodologies would allow organizations to focus on performing validation rather than⏎designing it—especially if incorporated into existing tools.⏎Importantly, validation guidance should be specific to both the use case and the deployment⏎context, not one-size-fits-all. The risks, benefits, and appropriate metrics for documentation⏎assistance, clinical decision support, predictive modeling, and patient engagement tools differ⏎substantially. Tailored guidance for common classes of AI applications would allow⏎organizations to focus their efforts on meaningful evaluation rather than reinventing validation⏎frameworks from scratch.⏎Clear federal guidance would also accelerate adoption by lowering the perceived risk of⏎deployment. Many healthcare leaders delay or limit AI use not because they believe AI is⏎unsafe, but because they lack confidence that they are evaluating it correctly. By clarifying⏎expectations and establishing shared norms, the Department could help organizations move⏎from cautious experimentation to responsible scale.⏎Building AI Fluency⏎Technology adoption depends not only on technical readiness, but also on human⏎understanding and trust. Considerable attention has been paid to training clinicians on how to⏎use AI tools effectively, verify outputs, and avoid automation bias. While this training is⏎essential, a more foundational form of AI fluency is also needed to overcome barriers to⏎adoption—particularly among non-technical stakeholders such as clinical leadership, frontline⏎staff, and labor representatives.⏎| 1979 Milky Way, Verona, WI 53593 | (608) 271-9000 | FAX (608) 271-7237 | www.epic.com⏎⏎6⏎In healthcare, AI is unlikely to displace workers. Persistent labor shortages across clinical roles,⏎combined with rising demand driven by an aging population, mean that AI is increasingly⏎necessary to sustain access to care. In practice, most healthcare AI use cases are designed to⏎reduce administrative burden, support clinical judgment, and allow clinicians to spend more⏎time with patients. Nonetheless, fears that AI will eliminate jobs remain widespread and⏎increasingly surface in labor negotiations.⏎These concerns often stem from a lack of shared understanding about what healthcare AI⏎does—and does not—do. Without credible, accessible education, AI adoption risks being⏎perceived as a cost-cutting measure rather than a capacity-multiplying one. This perception⏎can slow implementation, limit usage, or prompt organizations to constrain AI to narrow pilots⏎rather than deploy it broadly where it could deliver the most benefit.⏎The federal government can play an important role in addressing this gap by supporting⏎neutral, trusted education on AI’s role in healthcare. This could include developing materials⏎tailored to different stakeholder groups; supporting peer-to-peer learning networks among⏎healthcare organizations; or incorporating AI fluency into existing workforce development and⏎quality improvement programs. Such efforts would complement, rather than replace, vendor-⏎led training by focusing on system-level understanding rather than tool-specific instruction.⏎Ultimately, however, the most effective way to build AI fluency is through experience. When⏎clinicians and staff see AI reduce documentation burden, improve workflow efficiency, and⏎support—not override—clinical judgment, skepticism gives way to trust. Policies that enable⏎earlier, broader, and more responsible adoption of AI—such as those outlined in this⏎response—will therefore do more to build AI fluency than education alone. By helping⏎healthcare organizations move past prolonged pilots and into sustained use, the Department⏎can ensure that AI’s benefits are visible, tangible, and widely shared.⏎| 1979 Milky Way, Verona, WI 53593 | (608) 271-9000 | FAX (608) 271-7237 | www.epic.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0473♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Kaidbey, Yasmine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Yasmine♔Kaidbey♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔This is to raise urgent concerns about patient privacy, safety, lack of independent evidence, and the need for clear federal regulation, especially in behavioral health and psychotherapy. While AI is advancing rapidly, the science and safeguards have not kept pace.⏎ Innovation must be grounded in rigorous research, enforceable standards, and protection of the therapeutic relationship.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0474♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-04T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Achtermann, Max, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-31T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Max♔Achtermann♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I am a high-functioning American on SSI with a chronic heart arrhythmia. Passive AI is a barrier to my care. I am requesting that federal policy enable 'User-Requested AI Agency,' allowing my AI assistant to proactively take over device functions for medical reminders and intervention without my initiation. Current 'safety' guardrails prevent me from accessing the life-saving automation I need to remain independent.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0475♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from SonderMind, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔SonderMind, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Via Electronic Submission⏎United States Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, S.W.,⏎Washington, D.C. 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part⏎of Clinical Care⏎SonderMind, Inc. (“SonderMind”) is a comprehensive behavioral health provider dedicated to⏎improving access to high-quality, continuous care through technology and clinical expertise. We⏎connect individuals with licensed behavioral health providers and support those providers and⏎clients with technology-enabled tools to improve clinical care. We appreciate the opportunity to⏎comment on the examination of accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence in⏎clinical care.⏎SonderMind operates across all 50 states, serves tens of thousands of licensed therapists and⏎prescribers, and is actively building and deploying AI tools within behavioral health clinical⏎workflows, building all tools with our clinicians. Our AI-powered clinical documentation tool⏎has been adopted by 80%+ of eligible providers and has generated over 100,000 notes submitted⏎to health insurers, reducing note completion time by 80% and saving providers up to 90 minutes⏎per day. Every AI feature on our platform is opt-in for both clients and providers, and every⏎AI-generated output is fully editable by the clinician before finalization. SonderMind has⏎published an AI Constitution establishing binding principles for safety, transparency, and clinical⏎partnership in the use of AI.⏎Q1: Biggest Barriers to Private Sector AI Innovation and Adoption in Clinical Care⏎The barriers to AI innovation in healthcare, particularly in behavioral health, span⏎reimbursement, provider readiness, liability, and regulatory fragmentation.⏎Misaligned Reimbursement Models⏎The legacy fee-for-service (FFS) model creates an economic penalty for innovation. AI tools⏎designed to reduce visit frequency or provide continuous care, extending support beyond the⏎traditional 50-minute session, effectively lower a provider's billable volume. Current CPT codes,⏎including the psychotherapy codes (90832-90834) that govern most behavioral health billing, do⏎not adequately capture or compensate for AI-enabled continuous care such as proactive outreach,⏎between-session behavioral monitoring, or real-time crisis detection. While the 2026 CPT code⏎set has introduced new codes for digital health, they remain focused on episodic data collection⏎⏎rather than the ongoing, asynchronous support that defines AI's greatest potential in mental⏎health. Value-based care (VBC) models are naturally aligned with AI's ability to improve⏎outcomes and reduce costs, but VBC adoption in behavioral health remains significantly lower⏎than in physical medicine, leaving innovators without a viable commercial pathway.⏎Provider Understanding and Comfort⏎Clinicians are trained to practice medicine, not to evaluate how AI is designed, validated, or⏎deployed. Many providers still struggle to understand how AI tools are best used, assess their⏎risks and benefits, or explain those factors clearly to patients. There are very few high-quality,⏎practical education offerings to close this gap, which limits adoption even when the tools are⏎effective.⏎Liability and Risk Assignment⏎It is unclear who bears responsibility when AI-assisted care leads to an adverse outcome: The⏎clinician, the AI developer, or some combination. This ambiguity is especially problematic in⏎behavioral health, where high-risk situations such as suicide risk assessment, duty-to-warn⏎obligations, and safety planning carry significant legal complexity. Without clear frameworks,⏎both providers and developers face uncertainty that discourages adoption.⏎Fragmented and Shifting State Regulation⏎The regulatory environment for clinical AI is not standardized across states and is changing⏎rapidly. Different states impose different, and sometimes conflicting, requirements on how AI⏎can be used, documented, and supervised in clinical settings. In practice, this means a provider⏎using AI must simultaneously be a clinician, technologically literate about AI tools, and⏎knowledgeable about the legal and regulatory framework in every state where they practice. This⏎burden disproportionately affects behavioral health, where telehealth and multi-state practice are⏎common.⏎SonderMind has direct experience with this challenge. Multiple states are advancing legislation⏎that, while well-intentioned, is creating a patchwork of incompatible requirements that slows⏎innovation and confuses providers. This year alone, more than a dozen states have introduced⏎legislation regulating psychotherapy AI risks, many inadvertently prohibiting safety features like⏎AI-enabled crisis detection and creating liability exposure for providers who use any AI tool. In⏎some states, the regulatory framework is making it untenable for us to offer our AI-powered⏎client support agent to members in that state (a tool that is deployed and available to clients⏎across nearly every other state in the country). The result is that those members are denied access⏎to a beneficial, clinically-guardrailed tool, not because of a safety concern, but because of⏎regulatory ambiguity.⏎⏎The absence of a coherent federal framework means that companies building responsible,⏎clinically-validated AI tools face a state-by-state compliance burden that favors large incumbents⏎and penalizes the smaller innovators most likely to develop breakthrough applications in⏎underserved domains like behavioral health.⏎Q2: Regulatory, Payment Policy, and Programmatic Changes HHS Should Prioritize⏎Untreated and undertreated behavioral health conditions drive significant downstream healthcare⏎costs, including emergency department utilization, worsened medical comorbidity, and⏎workforce disability. AI tools that expand provider capacity and sustain patient engagement⏎between sessions have the potential to reduce these costs, but current payment structures do not⏎recognize or reimburse the mechanisms through which that value is delivered. The following⏎reforms would begin to close that gap.⏎Asynchronous Reimbursement Pathways⏎CMS should establish specific payment pathways under 42 CFR Parts 414 and 425 for⏎AI-enabled behavioral health interventions that reward continuous care models. This includes⏎reimbursement for asynchronous engagement and automated monitoring that leads to⏎documented clinical improvements. Current psychotherapy reimbursement under CPT codes⏎90832-90834 does not recognize or compensate for AI-enhanced treatment planning or⏎between-session support, making adoption economically unviable even when clinical value is⏎demonstrated.⏎Outcome-Based Incentives⏎HHS should expand value-based payment models under 42 CFR Part 425 that shift from⏎rewarding volume to rewarding validated outcomes. AI tools that demonstrate a reduction in⏎acute episodes (e.g., emergency department visits, crisis interventions) should be eligible for⏎incentive payments that offset the reduction in traditional session volume. CMS should consider⏎creating CMMI demonstration models specifically for behavioral health AI to generate the⏎evidence base for broader coverage decisions.⏎Risk-Proportionate Oversight of AI Tools⏎Regulation should differentiate between validated clinical AI tools and general-purpose large⏎language models (LLMs) that are not designed or tested for healthcare use. HHS should clarify⏎the regulatory boundary under 21 CFR Part 860 between clinical decision support tools that⏎qualify for exemption and those requiring premarket review. Unvalidated tools used in clinical⏎contexts pose meaningful risks, including the potential to encourage harmful behaviors. HHS⏎should implement oversight that is proportionate to the risk profile of the tool, ensuring that⏎well-designed clinical AI is not subjected to the same regulatory burden as unvalidated consumer⏎products, and vice versa.⏎⏎Federal Preemption or Harmonization of State AI Regulation⏎As noted in Q1, the absence of consistent federal guidance has produced a patchwork of⏎state-level AI regulations that impose conflicting requirements on companies operating across⏎multiple states. HHS should work with Congress to establish minimum federal standards for the⏎use of AI in clinical care that preempt or harmonize inconsistent state requirements. Without this,⏎the companies most committed to responsible, multi-state deployment of clinical AI bear the⏎highest compliance burden, while companies with less rigorous safety and compliance practices⏎face fewer constraints.⏎Q3: Governance, Accountability, and Liability for Non-Medical Device AI⏎The most pressing governance challenge for non-device AI in clinical care is the misalignment⏎between liability and control. Current frameworks risk holding clinicians fully responsible for⏎technical failures they cannot prevent, detect, or fix, such as developer bugs, inaccurate⏎AI-generated outputs, or vendor-side data breaches. This creates a chilling effect: providers⏎avoid beneficial AI tools entirely rather than accept uninsurable risk.⏎This is not a hypothetical concern. SonderMind is actively engaged with state legislatures on⏎proposed bills that would hold providers fully responsible for all aspects of an AI system's⏎performance, including technical failures entirely outside the provider's control. As noted above,⏎regulatory ambiguity around AI-powered client interactions has forced us to exclude some states⏎entirely from our AI client support agents. These state-level dynamics illustrate why federal⏎governance frameworks are urgently needed.⏎Distributed Liability Frameworks⏎HHS should convene a multi-stakeholder working group to develop model liability allocation⏎frameworks that clarify responsibility based on who controls what. The provider should bear⏎responsibility for the clinical decision to use an AI tool and the standard of care provided to the⏎client. The AI developer should bear responsibility for the safety, accuracy, and security of the⏎tool itself. Standard malpractice insurance covers clinical negligence, not third-party software⏎failures, regulations that ignore this distinction create risk that is both unjust and uninsurable.⏎Transparency Standards⏎HHS should work with National Institute of Standards and Technology (NIST) and Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health IT (ONC) to establish tiered explainability requirements⏎appropriate to clinical context and risk level. For behavioral health, where AI may be used in⏎crisis detection or treatment matching, providers and patients need to understand why an AI⏎made a particular recommendation. SonderMind's approach, where every AI-generated output is⏎⏎fully editable by the clinician before finalization, represents one model for maintaining clinical⏎oversight while leveraging AI capabilities.⏎Patient Consent and Disclosure⏎HHS should clarify HIPAA requirements under 45 CFR 164.508 regarding patient consent for AI⏎use in clinical care. Patients should know when AI is involved in their care and have the ability⏎to opt out. SonderMind has implemented opt-in consent for all AI features for both providers and⏎clients, but industry-wide standards would build broader public trust.⏎Q5: Supporting Private Sector Activities (Accreditation, Certification, Industry-Driven Testing)⏎Differentiated Certification Standards⏎HHS should support the development of accreditation standards that distinguish between⏎general-purpose AI and AI built for behavioral health with strong clinical standards. Tools built⏎for healthcare with appropriate clinical guardrails should be eligible for expedited pathways to⏎reimbursement.⏎Clear Accountability Mechanisms⏎To build public trust, HHS should promote frameworks where AI developers and deploying⏎providers share accountability for outcomes. If an AI tool delivers negative clinical results due to⏎inadequate design or insufficient safeguards, there must be transparent and proportionate⏎consequences. Tying accountability to reimbursement eligibility, where tools that fail to meet⏎safety and efficacy standards lose access to payment pathways, would create meaningful market⏎incentives for quality.⏎Q6: Where AI Tools in Clinical Care Have Met or Exceeded Expectations⏎In SonderMind's experience deploying AI in behavioral health, the tools that have exceeded⏎expectations are those that support providers within their existing workflows rather than⏎attempting to replace clinical judgment.⏎Clinical Documentation (AI Notes)⏎SonderMind's AI-powered documentation tool reduces note completion time by 80%, saving⏎providers up to 90 minutes per day. Since commercial launch in September 2025, the tool has⏎been adopted by 80%+ of eligible providers and has generated over 100,000 notes submitted to⏎health insurers. The tool transcribes and formats session notes with patient consent. Every⏎AI-generated note is fully editable by the provider before finalization, maintaining clinical⏎oversight while dramatically reducing administrative burden.⏎⏎Quality Assurance⏎Automated chart auditing supports compliance monitoring by identifying documentation gaps⏎and flagging potential issues that would otherwise require manual review.⏎Between-Session Client Support⏎SonderMind offers AI-enabled tools that help clients stay engaged with their therapeutic work⏎between sessions, including session preparation features and guided journaling tools that support⏎self-awareness and goal-setting between appointments. These features extend the reach of the⏎therapist beyond the 50-minute session without replacing human clinical judgment. Over 15,000⏎unique clients have prepared for sessions using our AI-guided session preparation tool, helping⏎them plan what they want to discuss in therapy sessions. Additionally, over 30,000 members⏎have utilized our AI-guided Reflections as an interaction AI journaling tool, documenting and⏎sharing insights in between sessions.⏎Key Insight⏎The pattern across successful implementations is consistent: AI tools perform best when they⏎enhance existing provider behaviors and client/provider workflows rather than introduce⏎fundamentally new workflows. Adoption is highest when the tool reduces friction in tasks⏎providers already do (documentation, compliance) rather than asking them to fundamentally⏎change how they deliver care. These efficiency gains carry broader implications: 90 fewer⏎minutes per day on documentation represents capacity that can be returned to direct patient care⏎or used to reduce the burnout and attrition that constrain the behavioral health workforce.⏎Q10: Priority Areas for AI Research⏎Safety and Harm Prevention⏎Research must prioritize ensuring AI tools cannot produce harmful outputs in mental health⏎contexts. HHS should fund studies specifically aimed at testing LLMs in behavioral health⏎scenarios to ensure they cannot be manipulated into encouraging self-harm or other dangerous⏎behaviors. This is not a theoretical concern: general-purpose consumer AI chatbots have been⏎involved in widely reported incidents where users in mental health crisis received responses that⏎could exacerbate harm. Clinical-grade behavioral health AI requires safety testing at a standard⏎that does not yet exist in the research literature.⏎SonderMind invests in this work directly: our clinical and machine learning teams collaborate⏎daily on safety testing, clinical guardrail design, and ongoing monitoring of AI outputs in⏎behavioral health contexts. We believe companies deploying AI in mental health care have an⏎obligation to lead on safety, not wait for federal mandates. HHS can accelerate this work by⏎⏎funding shared research infrastructure and establishing behavioral health-specific benchmarks⏎that the industry can adopt.⏎AI-Clinician Collaborative Models⏎HHS should prioritize research on how AI can best augment the clinician rather than replace⏎them. This includes studies on AI's role in triaging acute needs, providing longitudinal support⏎between clinical visits, and keeping patients engaged in their care.⏎Bias and Equity⏎Research into ensuring AI models do not perpetuate existing disparities in mental health⏎diagnosis and treatment across diverse populations is critical, particularly given the historical⏎underrepresentation of certain communities in behavioral health research data.⏎Conclusion⏎Behavioral health serves one in five Americans, yet it remains significantly underrepresented in⏎health technology policy. The questions posed in this RFI are the right ones. The answers,⏎however, will be incomplete if they are shaped primarily by the experiences of radiology,⏎pathology, and acute care, domains where AI operates on images and structured data rather than⏎on the complexity of human conversation and therapeutic relationship.⏎SonderMind offers a perspective grounded in direct operational experience: building AI tools for⏎behavioral health, deploying them at scale, navigating a fragmented regulatory landscape, and⏎learning what works when the goal is not just efficiency but better clinical outcomes for people⏎seeking mental health care. The barriers we have described (misaligned reimbursement, unclear⏎liability, and inconsistent state regulation) are solvable. The policy tools exist. HHS should⏎include behavioral health in every AI framework it develops. The scale of the need demands it.⏎We are ready to be a resource to HHS as this work moves forward.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0476♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AFL-CIO & AFL-CIO Tech Institute, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AFL-CIO & AFL-CIO Tech Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎330 C St SW⏎Floor 7⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Sent electronically via regulations.gov⏎RE: Public Comment on Request for Information (RFI) HHS Health Sector AI⏎RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎RIN: 0955-AA13⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎The American Federation of Labor and Congress of Industrial Organizations⏎(AFL-CIO) and the AFL-CIO Technology Institute (Tech Institute) appreciate⏎the opportunity to submit comments in response to the Request for Information⏎(RFI) from the United States Department of Health and Human Services Office⏎of the Deputy Secretary in collaboration with the Assistant Secretary for⏎Technology Policy (ASTP) and Office of the National Coordinator for Health⏎Information Technology (ONC) to seek broad public comment on what HHS can⏎do to “accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.”1⏎The AFL-CIO is a democratic, voluntary federation of 64 national and⏎international labor unions that represent nearly 15 million working people. The⏎1 Department of Health and Human Services, Office of the Secretary. “ Request for Information: Accelerating⏎the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care,” Federal Register, December 23,⏎2025,⏎https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2025/12/23/2025-23641/request-for-information-accelerating-the-⏎adoption-and-use-of-artificial-intelligence-as-part-of⏎⏎organization strives to ensure all working people are treated fairly, with decent⏎paychecks and benefits, safe jobs, dignity, and equal opportunities. The AFL-CIO⏎also helps people acquire valuable skills and job-readiness for the 21st century⏎economy. The Tech Institute works at the intersection of technological innovation⏎in the workplace, centering worker knowledge, expertise, and interests in our⏎modern innovation-based economy and in public services with the goal of⏎ensuring that technological change creates widespread prosperity for all working⏎people.⏎The Use of AI in Health Care Has Not Been Proven Safe Nor Effective⏎The healthcare industry has preemptively embraced the use of AI across many⏎elements of clinical care. AI technology is being used with the stated purpose to⏎enhance diagnostic accuracy, personalize treatment plans and accelerate drug⏎discovery.2 While these goals are admirable, accelerating the adoption of AI⏎should not come at the cost of safety. A robust regulatory framework is needed⏎to ensure AI has been tested and proven safe and effective for patient care⏎before implementation, the same way we regulate medical devices, treatments,⏎and medications. Additionally, the regulatory framework must ensure workers⏎have the right to be consulted and engaged on all policies, procedures, and best⏎practices surrounding the implementation of AI. Union workers must have the⏎opportunity to bargain over whether and how technology should be⏎implemented in the workplace before it is selected or deployed in a health care⏎setting to ensure the technology is not accelerating job displacement or⏎deskilling the workforce.⏎When employers prioritize profit over patients and the frontline employees, care⏎quality suffers. This is why we share the broad concerns that healthcare⏎providers are adopting untested and unregulated AI in clinical settings. This⏎includes, for example, prescription renewal tools released by Utah’s state⏎government, which partnered with Doctronic,2 as well as AI systems used to⏎deny patient claims without human oversight.3 These practices expose patients⏎and healthcare workers to unnecessary risk. AI systems must be tested before⏎and during use to reduce dangers and dangerous directives that harm workers⏎and patients. As the agency evaluates how to oversee responsible AI adoption in⏎clinical care, it must actively solicit input from healthcare practitioners, clinical⏎care workers, patients and their advocates, and unions, who are the end-users⏎most affected by these technologies. Healthcare workers’ knowledge and⏎experience should inform healthcare regulations and AI best practices. Their⏎voices are critical to holding the AI developers and healthcare employers⏎accountable before these unproven technologies are unleashed on patients and⏎healthcare workers.⏎2 Madeline North, “7 ways AI is transforming healthcare,” World Economic Forum, August 13, 2025,⏎https://www.weforum.org/stories/2025/08/ai-transforming-global-health/⏎⏎Centering Workers and Patients Prior to the Adoption of AI in Healthcare⏎The public interest will be best served by a healthcare system that supports the⏎working conditions of healthcare professionals and equips them with the tools,⏎including AI technologies, that are designed to assist, rather than replace, their⏎clinical expertise in delivering widely accessible, affordable, and high-quality⏎patient care. It is critical as the Department evaluates the adoption of AI into⏎healthcare services that workers voices’ are front-and-center in the AI⏎development and implementation process from the onset of R&D through its⏎delivery to patients. Department policy should support collective bargaining and⏎worker consultation in the adoption of AI and other emerging technologies.⏎Workers are the experts on their jobs and workplaces, and are best positioned to⏎identify risks and guardrails needed. Union workers must have the opportunity to⏎bargain over whether and how technology should be implemented in the⏎workplace before it is selected or deployed in a health care setting to ensure the⏎technology is not accelerating job displacement or deskilling the workforce.⏎Where workers have agreed to participate in a labor-management committee to⏎address delivery of care issues, such entities can be a vehicle for ensuring⏎meaningful input into implementation issues on an ongoing basis.⏎We urge HHS to use its authority to help ensure that any adoption and use of AI⏎in clinical care follows a worker and patient-centered approach. First and⏎foremost, healthcare workers must retain their autonomy while providing care.3,⏎4 A robust regulatory framework would include implementing strong and⏎enforceable rules that protect clinicians and nurses' right to exercise professional⏎judgment and override decisions made by AI or other novel technologies when⏎clinically appropriate, without fear of retaliation. Further, to support⏎high-quality clinical care, healthcare workers must have access to training and⏎education informing them of how AI tools in their workplace function as well as⏎their limitations and potential biases. Additionally, patients and health care⏎workers must retain the right to transparency and privacy in the health care data⏎that is being collected about them, what purpose it serves, where it is stored, and⏎whether it is being sold or shared.⏎Institutions must provide ongoing training and informed consent by way of⏎opt-in process of patients and healthcare staff to ensure everyone understands⏎the benefits and risks of technologies being deployed.5 The Department should⏎3 Yasmin Khorram and Ruth Reader, "Artificial intelligence begins prescribing medication in Utah,”⏎PoliticoPro, January 6, 2026,⏎https://subscriber.politicopro.com/article/2026/01/artificial-intelligence-begins-prescribing-medications-in-ut⏎ah-00709122.⏎4 Paige Nong, Julia Adler-Milstein, Nate C. Apathy, A. Jay Holmgren, and Jordan Everson, “Current Use⏎And Evaluation Of Artificial Intelligence And Predictive Models In US Hospitals,” Health Affairs (January,⏎2025). https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/full/10.1377/hlthaff.2024.00842?journalCode=hlthaff.⏎5 American Federation of Teachers, AFL-CIO, “Workers’ Rights And Ethical Use of Artificial Intelligence In⏎Healthcare,” American Federation of Teachers, AFL-CIO, accessed February 4, 2026.⏎https://www.aft.org/resolution/workers-rights-and-ethical-use-artificial-intelligence-healthcare.⏎⏎consult all affected stakeholders, including unions, and patient advocates, to⏎develop education and training programs that integrate AI in ways that enhance⏎working conditions rather than disrupt or displace care delivery.⏎Unregulated AI Uses Could Deliver Worse Clinical Outcomes⏎Without robust AI guardrails, the healthcare system risks worsening⏎access to care, and harming patient outcomes by deploying untested and⏎unregulated AI in clinical settings. Unlike licensed healthcare⏎professionals, AI systems are often not subject to medical certification,⏎professional discipline, or are required to operate under ethical⏎obligations equivalent to a human care provider. As a result, AI systems⏎cannot be assumed to self-regulate or reliably communicate risk,⏎particularly to individuals without medical training. These limitations⏎explain why AI, when used independently of professional oversight,⏎cannot consistently meet baseline expectations of clinical care; it cannot⏎synthesize interactive patient data, exercise contextual judgement, or⏎adapt treatment decisions through meaningful follow-up.6 HHS must⏎establish a robust regulatory framework that requires rigorous testing of⏎deployed algorithms for bias and fairness, holding AI systems to⏎standards comparable to certified clinicians, and enforcing oversight⏎mechanisms that hold developers and deployers accountable for⏎unintended consequences of their implementation.7⏎As new AI tools are increasingly introduced into clinical settings, stakeholders⏎warn that poorly designed or weakly enforced applications can reduce care⏎quality and increase strain on healthcare workers. For example, a Virginia Tech⏎study found AI models used for in-hospital mortality prediction failed to⏎recognize 66 percent of injuries.7 In such cases, AI systems missed signs of⏎patient deterioration that clinicians would have likely identified. AI also causes⏎harm when it generates automated care plans that fail to reflect individual patient⏎needs. In another instance, a healthcare worker at Dignity Health in Henderson,⏎Nevada, reported that an AI system incorrectly flagged a newly admitted patient⏎for sepsis treatment when the patient actually required dialysis care.8 Only timely⏎6 Geoffrey A. Fowler, “We found what you’re asking ChatGPT about health. A doctor scored its answers,”⏎The Washington Post, November 18, 2025,⏎https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2025/11/18/chatgpt-health-advice-accuracy-rated/.⏎7 National Nurses United, “Nurses and Patients’ Bill of Rights: Guiding Principles for A.I. Justice in⏎Nursing and Health Care,” National Nurses United, accessed February, 4, 2025,⏎https://www.nationalnursesunited.org/sites/default/files/nnu/documents/0424_NursesPatients-BillOfRights_⏎Principles-AI-Justice_flye r.pdf.⏎8 Barbara L. Micale, “Machine learning models fail to detect key health deteriorations, Virginia Tech⏎research shows,” Virginia Polytechnic Institute and State University, March 11, 2025,⏎https://news.vt.edu/articles/2025/02/virginia-tech-study-published-in-communications-medicine-.html#:~:text⏎=University%20Photo%2⏎0Library-,Machine%20learning%20models%20fail%20to%20detect%20key%20health%20deteriorations%2⏎C%20.⏎⏎human intervention prevented a catastrophic misdiagnosis and serious patient⏎harm.⏎These failures underscore AI’s limitations in clinical judgement, assessing⏎urgency, recognizing emotional distress, and adapting care through follow-up⏎questioning; capabilities that are central to high-quality caregiving. The⏎prevailing response to these limitations among AI developers and deployers is to⏎over-rely on “human-in-the-loop” approaches to AI safety that place the⏎burden–and the liability–of verifying the accuracy and efficacy of AI, rather than⏎rigorous testing, monitoring, and regulation of the technology. The additional⏎work required to review and correct the outputs of AI tools used in applications⏎like scribing, charting, and patient communications can negate any promised⏎efficiencies or even take more time away from direct patient care and potentially⏎undermine patient outcomes. A study at the University of California, San Diego⏎showed that clinicians spent almost 22% more time reviewing and drafting⏎responses to patient messages after the introduction of a generative AI patient⏎communication tool.9⏎Deploying AI systems that have not been sufficiently tested or validated raises⏎questions about whether these tools can reliably support the level of care patients⏎receive from healthcare workers. Although healthcare workers have used AI for⏎decades, primarily as a clinical decision-support tool; clinical care is increasingly⏎shifting AI’s role towards substituting for human judgement.10 As National⏎Nurses United has highlighted, “the entire ecosystem is designed to automate,⏎de-skill, and ultimately replace caregivers.”11 This is a concern, because existing⏎regulatory frameworks do not adequately distinguish between assistive⏎technologies and systems that operate with limited or no human oversight. Dr.⏎John Whyte, CEO and Executive Vice President of the American Medical⏎Association, voiced this concern when discussing Utah’s pilot program allowing⏎AI-enabled prescription renewal services, warning that “while AI has limitless⏎opportunity to transform medicine for the better, without physician input it also⏎poses serious risks to patients and physicians alike.”12 These risks include failures⏎to recognize nuanced clinical factors, such as signs of substance use disorder or⏎emotional distress, that clinicians routinely assess.⏎9 Tai-Seale M, Baxter SL, Vaida F, et al. AI-Generated Draft Replies Integrated Into Health Records and⏎Physicians’ Electronic Communication. JAMA Netw Open. 2024;7(4):e246565.⏎doi:10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2024.6565⏎10 Hirani, Rahim, et al. "Artificial Intelligence and Healthcare: A Journey through History, Present⏎Innovations, and Future Possibilities." Life, vol. 14, no. 5, 2024, p. 557. https://doi.org/10.3390/life14050557.⏎11 Matthew Perrone, “As Ai nurses reshape hospital care, human nurses are pushing back,” The Associated⏎Press, March 16, 2025,⏎https://apnews.com/article/artificial-intelligence-ai-nurses-hospitals-health-care-3e41c0a2768a3b4c5e002270⏎cc2abe23.⏎12 Geoffrey A. Fowler, “We found what you’re asking ChatGPT about health. A doctor scored its answers,”⏎The Washington Post, November 18, 2025,⏎https://www.washingtonpost.com/technology/2025/11/18/chatgpt-health-advice-accuracy-rated/.⏎⏎Beyond clinical settings, the growing visibility of AI-generated health⏎information online further threatens patient trust and public health literacy.13⏎AI-generated content often appears to carry medical endorsement despite⏎lacking professional review, promoting everything from untested weight-loss⏎supplements to unsafe medical advice. In one reported case, a man was⏎hospitalized for poisoning after asking ChatGPT for advice on reducing salt⏎from his diet.14 These incidents demonstrate that users increasingly place their⏎trust in AI-generated health information, highlighting the need for clear⏎standards, transparency, and accountability, particularly when such information⏎may promote unproven or unsafe treatments.⏎AI Is Being Used to Reduce Access and Affordability, and Perpetuate⏎Disparities⏎The use of AI to determine access to healthcare services raises significant⏎policy concerns. As AI systems are becoming more prevalent in assessing⏎healthcare coverage and insurance utilization, it is vital that AI is not used to⏎make health care coverage decisions. These decisions require clinical expertise⏎and an understanding of individual circumstances, context that automated⏎systems alone cannot adequately capture. American patients are highly aware of⏎how these tools can be misused to deny claims and reduce healthcare coverage:⏎a Quinnipiac survey conducted in 2025 on AI shows that 71 percent of⏎Americans feel uncomfortable with the use of AI tools in claims screening and⏎decision-making.15 And yet, despite this skepticism, commercial insurers⏎continue to integrate AI into utilization management and claims processing.16⏎Although AI technologies are often promoted as tools to ensure fairness and⏎consistency, their design frequently relies on insurers’ historical data. A Stanford⏎Health study found that provider-facing tools predict the likelihood that a denied⏎claim can be successfully appealed by relying on past outcomes of appeals for⏎that service type.17 Rather than evaluating the clinical merits of an individual case,⏎these tools often reproduce prior approval and denial patterns. This approach⏎raises concerns in the context of the recently implemented Wasteful and⏎Inappropriate Service Reduction (WISeR) model, which uses AI to refine the⏎13 Ibid.⏎14 Audrey Eichenberger, MD, Stephen Thielke, MD, Adam Van Buskirk, MD,. “A Case of Bromism⏎Influenced by Use of Artificial Intelligence,” Annals of Internal Medicine Clinical Cases, August 5, 2025,⏎https://www.acpjournals.org/doi/10.7326/aimcc.2024.1260.⏎15 Quinnipiac University, “The Age Of Artificial Intelligence: Americans Wary Of Impact On Daily Life, See⏎Harm To Education, Benefits To Medical Advances, Quinnipiac University National Poll Finds; Americans⏎Expect AI Will Cause Job Losses But Workers Don't Think Their Own Jobs Will Disappear,” Quinnipiac⏎University, April 16, 2025, https://poll.qu.edu/poll-release?releaseid=3923.⏎16 Michelle M. Mello, Artem A. Tortsyuk, Abdoul Jalil Djiberou Mahamadou, and Danton Char, “The AI⏎Arms Race In Health Insurance Utilization Review: Promises Of Efficiency And Risks Of Supercharged⏎Flaws,” Health Affairs, January, 2026, https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00897.⏎17 Ibid.⏎⏎prior authorization process for traditional fee-for-service Medicare. The WISeR⏎model is designed to “reduc[e] fraud, waste (including low-value care), and abuse⏎in Medicare fee-for-service” by flagging claims for services that may not meet⏎existing coverage requirements.18 The concern is that this model may foster⏎perverse incentives. Tech companies that voluntarily participate may receive⏎financial compensation linked to their share of “averted expenditures,” reducing⏎incentives to closely evaluate model accuracy, test for bias, or monitor changes in⏎performance over time, as long as the model is generating profit.19 These systems⏎rely on fixed standards and historical datasets that can potentially be inaccurate in⏎accounting for social determinants of health, recognizing evolving clinical⏎guidelines, and acknowledging recent policy changes.20 Used as intended, AI use⏎cases in eligibility and coverage may be generating incorrect outputs with real⏎world consequences.21⏎Recent studies have also pointed to a connection between AI utilization and⏎exacerbation of the racial disparities that already plague the health system. This⏎raises fundamental questions about whether AI deployment can meaningfully⏎improve affordability and access to care, or whether it risks reinforcing existing⏎barriers within the healthcare system, worsening existing healthcare access gaps.⏎AI Innovation Should Not Be Used to Worsen Healthcare Workplace⏎Conditions⏎Employers often deploy AI-enabled tools without providing adequate support or⏎protections, leaving workers to absorb the harms. AI systems increasingly⏎quantify nursing workloads and patient acuity (complexity of care required), and⏎healthcare organizations use these metrics to drive staffing decisions and⏎nurse-to-patient assignments. This approach can lead to chronic understaffing and⏎worker burnout, because acuity measurements rely on real-time RN charting–a⏎process shaped more by institutional norms, patterns of convenience, and⏎subjective judgment than by actual clinical needs.22 To automate this process⏎without solving for the significance of these independent variables creates⏎staffing imbalances and declines in full care coverage.⏎18 “Medicare Program; Implementation of Prior Authorization for Select Services for the Wasteful and⏎Inappropriate Services Reduction (WISeR) Model,” 90 F.R. 28749 (proposed July 1, 2025).⏎https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-07-01/pdf/2025-12195.pdf.⏎19 “Medicare Program; Implementation of Prior Authorization for Select Services for the Wasteful and⏎Inappropriate Services Reduction (WISeR) Model,” 90 F.R. 28749 (proposed July 1, 2025).⏎https://www.govinfo.gov/content/pkg/FR-2025-07-01/pdf/2025-12195.pdf.⏎20 Lorenzo Belenguer, “AI bias: exploring discriminatory algorithmic decision-making models and⏎the application of possible machine-centric solutions adapted from the pharmaceutical industry,”⏎National Library of Medicine, February, 10, 2022,⏎https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8830968/#CR71.⏎21 Michelle M. Mello, Artem A. Tortsyuk, Abdoul Jalil Djiberou Mahamadou, and Danton Char, “The AI⏎Arms Race In Health Insurance Utilization Review: Promises Of Efficiency And Risks Of Supercharged⏎Flaws,” Health Affairs, January, 2026, https://www.healthaffairs.org/doi/10.1377/hlthaff.2025.00897.⏎22 Heather Cathro, “A practical guide to making patient assignments in acute care,” J Nurs⏎Adm, January, 2013. https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/23232175/.⏎⏎Rather than producing a fully scheduled workforce, this automation has helped⏎create a gig-work model in healthcare. App-based platforms are changing the⏎working conditions in clinical settings by pressuring healthcare professionals to⏎bid against one another to fill hospital shifts, often at the lowest possible rate.23⏎Rather than optimizing access to care, these models are being used to reduce⏎labor costs and undercut healthcare workers’ rights, leading to chronic⏎understaffing, decreased job security, erosion of patient care, and reduced⏎benefits. In this emerging model for care, patients pay the ultimate price, with⏎longer wait times at hospitals and reduced access to high-quality care.24⏎Technology is no substitute for appropriate staffing levels for all health care⏎workers that are necessary to ensure the highest standards of safety and care for⏎both workers and patients. A policy framework that centers on quality, workers’⏎right to safety, and workforce sustainability is critical to ensure AI contributes⏎positively to patient care rather than undermining it.⏎AI Guardrails Protect Patients and Workers⏎We are concerned that, on net, untested and unregulated AI can diminish the⏎reputation of high-quality and safe US healthcare. The integration of AI into⏎healthcare affects both the sustainability and integrity of the healthcare⏎profession. When thoughtfully designed and implemented, AI technologies can⏎support clinicians and improve care quality. However, when organizations⏎prioritize speed, cost savings or perverse, economically-motivated objectives⏎over clinical validation and workforce engagement, they create real risks to⏎patient safety and outcomes. Deploying technologies without proper validation⏎can lead to diagnostic errors, missed conditions, or delayed care often because⏎health care providers spend time correcting the misrepresentation or misdiagnosis⏎by AI, pulling providers away from direct patient care, worsening quality care⏎and threatening the reputation of the American healthcare system.⏎Given the high-stakes nature of clinical care, patient trust depends on⏎professional judgement and accountability. If AI systems are perceived as⏎unreliable or insufficiently tested, patients may delay seeking medical attention⏎or lose faith in our healthcare system. Strong guardrails are absolutely essential to⏎holding the healthcare industry accountable for the quality and affordability of its⏎services. To promote responsible innovation, we urge the Department to⏎recognize that acceleration of AI in healthcare without strong guardrails is⏎dangerous to patients and workers. At minimum, the Department must implement⏎policies and regulations requiring mandatory, pre-market testing and approval of⏎any AI system to ensure that it is safe, effective, therapeutic, and equitable, and⏎23 Katie J. Wells, and Funda Ustek Spilda, “Uber for Nursing: How an AI-Powered Gig Model Is⏎Threatening Health Care,” Roosevelt Institute, December 17, 2024,⏎https://rooseveltinstitute.org/publications/uber-for-nursing/.⏎24 Katie J. Wells, and Funda Ustek Spilda, “Uber for Nursing: How an AI-Powered Gig Model Is⏎Threatening Health Care,” Roosevelt Institute, December 17, 2024,⏎https://rooseveltinstitute.org/publications/uber-for-nursing/.⏎⏎holding AI developers and healthcare service providers accountable for misuses⏎or harms to patients and workers. Likewise, the Department must put in place⏎supervisory and enforcement standards that maintain comparable safety standards⏎between AI systems and human healthcare providers. Responsible innovation⏎that is safe and reliable must be implemented through a worker- and⏎patient-centered approach. Accordingly, through the lifecycle of AI systems,⏎healthcare workers’ voice should be incorporated into AI governance, including⏎pre-decisional worker consultation and consent, worker-centered audits and⏎assessments, transparency, human-in-the-loop processes, the right to human⏎review, opt-out/override options, whistleblower protections, and liability shields⏎for workers.⏎Thank you for this opportunity to comment on policy strategies for AI⏎adoption in healthcare.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jody Calemine⏎Director, Government Affairs, AFL-CIO⏎Ed Wytkind⏎Senior Advisor, AFL-CIO Technology Institute♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0477♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from AdventHealth, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔AdventHealth♔♔♔♔♔♔Feb 23, 2026⏎regulations.gov⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care [RIN 0955-AA13]⏎On behalf of AdventHealth, we appreciate the opportunity to respond to the Department of⏎Health and Human Services’ Request for Information (RFI) on Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. AdventHealth is a faith-based, not-for-profit⏎health system with more than 50 hospital campuses and hundreds of care sites across nine⏎states, serving over five million patients annually. Our system includes large academic medical⏎centers, community hospitals, and rural facilities, providing a broad perspective on the⏎opportunities and challenges associated with the responsible use of artificial intelligence (AI) in⏎clinical care.⏎AdventHealth supports HHS’s efforts to promote the safe and effective adoption of AI⏎technologies that enhance patient care, strengthen the health care workforce, and improve⏎healthcare outcomes.⏎Barriers to Private-Sector Innovation and Adoption of AI in Clinical Care⏎The most significant barriers to private-sector innovation and adoption of AI in clinical care⏎include regulatory uncertainty, unresolved liability exposure, fragmented data environments,⏎and workforce capacity constraints, particularly for small, rural, and safety-net providers.⏎Despite rapid advances in AI capabilities, the absence of clear, nationally consistent⏎expectations regarding AI governance, validation, and accountability creates⏎⏎hesitation among health systems when determining which tools are appropriate for⏎clinical deployment. This uncertainty is especially pronounced for AI tools that support⏎clinical workflows but do not clearly fall within existing medical device regulatory frameworks.⏎Health systems are often left to independently assess safety, reliability, and legal risk, slowing⏎adoption and disadvantaging providers with fewer internal resources.⏎Liability concerns further compound these challenges. AI systems operate at a speed and scale⏎that can amplify even low error rates, raising questions about accountability when AI-supported⏎recommendations influence clinical decision-making. Without clear guidance or safe harbor⏎pathways, providers may feel compelled to duplicate AI outputs through manual⏎review, undermining the efficiency and workforce benefits AI is intended to deliver.⏎AI as a Workforce Sustainability Tool and the Importance of Administrative Relief⏎From AdventHealth’s experience, AI tools have demonstrated the greatest and most⏎immediate value when deployed as time multipliers, particularly in administrative⏎and documentation-related workflows. AI-enabled ambient documentation, chart⏎summarization, and workflow automation have the potential to return meaningful time to⏎clinicians, improving both efficiency and professional satisfaction. For example, AdventHealth’s⏎use of certain AI-enabled ambient voice technology, has resulted in approximately 96 hours⏎saved per employee a year.⏎Peer-reviewed evidence increasingly supports this experience. Recent multi-site studies⏎published in JAMA Network Open and related journals have found that the use of ambient AI⏎documentation tools is associated with measurable reductions in administrative burden and self-⏎reported clinician burnout, as well as improvements in perceived well-being. Randomized⏎controlled trials evaluating large language model–enabled ambient scribes have similarly⏎demonstrated reductions in documentation time and task load, with downstream benefits for⏎clinician work exhaustion. In multi-site evaluations, ambient AI documentation tools were⏎associated with reductions in note-writing time on the order of 20–30 percent per encounter⏎and statistically significant decreases in burnout and work exhaustion scores. Randomized⏎controlled data similarly demonstrate measurable reductions in cognitive workload and⏎documentation burden compared to usual workflows, particularly among high-utilization⏎⏎clinicians. While continued evaluation is warranted, these findings underscore AI’s potential role⏎in addressing one of the most pressing challenges facing the health care system – clinician⏎burnout.⏎These benefits are most pronounced when AI tools are integrated into clinical⏎workflows in a manner that is trusted, validated, and supported by clear governance⏎expectations. AdventHealth deploys clinical imaging AI platforms, which flags critical images⏎for clinicians in real time, saving our clinicians about 45 minutes when making a diagnosis. The⏎technology acts as a layer of critical review by scanning medical images to detect acute⏎abnormalities like hemorrhages or fractures, helping clinicians to prioritize those images. Our⏎physician in turn can appropriately triage and prioritize treatment and improve clinical⏎outcomes.⏎Regulatory, Payment, and Programmatic Opportunities to Incentivize Effective AI⏎Use⏎AdventHealth encourages HHS to prioritize the following areas to accelerate responsible AI⏎adoption:⏎1. Exceptions and Safe Harbor Frameworks⏎HHS should provide exceptions and safe harbors pursuant to the Stark Law and the⏎Anti-Kickback Statute for health systems that deploy or donate AI tools meeting⏎recognized national standards for validation, documentation, and governance to its clinical⏎partners. Such protection would reduce duplicative internal validation efforts and provide⏎greater confidence for providers and provider cross organizational validation. Some AI tools,⏎such as AI-driven diagnostic imaging, predictive patient monitoring, or generative AI⏎administrative aids, do not clearly fit into the current Electronic Health Records or cybersecurity⏎exceptions and would benefit from a similar protection.1⏎2. National AI Governance and Validation Standards⏎Clear federal guidance outlining accepted AI risk-management and validation⏎frameworks would reduce regulatory fragmentation across states and set a federal⏎1Stark and AKS Final Rules Will Facilitate Donations of EHR and Cybersecurity Technology and Services⏎⏎floor. Establishing consistent expectations, developed with input from both clinicians and AI⏎developers, would promote interoperability, scalability, and equity in AI adoption.⏎3. Payment Policy Alignment⏎Currently, Medicare payments have been limited to very few AI-related Common Procedural⏎Terminology (CPT) codes. If HHS wants to accelerate the use of AI, payment should be⏎better aligned with AI-enabled efficiencies. For example, allowing appropriate⏎reimbursement pathways for AI-supported clinical and administrative functions, including⏎documentation and interpretation support, could help address workforce shortages while⏎maintaining patient safety.⏎Non-Medical Device AI and the Need for Coordinated Federal Guidance⏎For AI tools not regulated as medical devices, health systems face unresolved challenges⏎related to liability, data governance, privacy, and accountability. HHS can play a constructive⏎role by aligning expectations across ASTP/ONC, FDA, OCR, and CMS, clarifying⏎oversight responsibilities and ensuring consistent guidance on documentation,⏎transparency, and risk management.⏎Additionally, HHS should remain attentive to the expanding ecosystem of consumer-facing AI⏎tools that are not subject to HIPAA protections. Clear distinctions between clinically validated,⏎provider-supported AI and general consumer AI are essential to maintaining patient trust and⏎informed decision-making.⏎Evaluation, Benchmarking, and Post-Deployment Monitoring⏎Robust AI evaluation (both pre- and post-deployment) is essential to sustaining trust and⏎accelerating adoption. AdventHealth encourages HHS to explore using a framework,⏎similar to the one used by Patient Safety Organizations under the Patient Safety and⏎Quality Improvement Act, where users of AI in clinical care could safely report⏎issues with AI technology that anyone can access and learn from. This would create a⏎protected environment for AI monitoring, evaluation, and continuous improvement.⏎⏎Federal support for applied research, benchmarking, and post-deployment⏎monitoring through grants, cooperative agreements, or pilot programs would also⏎help health providers – of all sizes and resources – to deploy AI technology.⏎Conclusion⏎AdventHealth appreciates HHS’s leadership in seeking stakeholder input on the future of AI in⏎clinical care. AI holds significant promise to improve patient outcomes, enhance operational⏎efficiency, and support a sustainable health care workforce. Realizing this potential will require⏎regulatory clarity, aligned payment policies, and evidence-based governance frameworks that⏎enable responsible adoption without imposing unnecessary barriers, particularly for smaller and⏎rural providers.⏎We welcome continued engagement with HHS on these issues and appreciate the opportunity⏎to contribute our perspective.⏎If you have any questions or would like further information, please do not hesitate to contact⏎me or Susana Molina Ramos, Director of Public Policy at⏎Susana.MolinaRamos@AdventHealth.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Michael E. Griffin⏎Senior Vice President⏎Advocacy and Public Policy⏎AdventHealth♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0478♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Granville, Alexandra, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Alexandra♔Granville♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔I recognize that there is currently no comprehensive federal regulatory framework for AI in healthcare, which poses significant risks to patients. Traditionally, healthcare products and treatments undergo oversight to ensure safety and effectiveness, such as the FDA’s evaluation of medical devices and pharmaceuticals. I believe AI systems in clinical settings should meet similar or higher standards. Allowing corporations or a patchwork of state laws to handle oversight could lead to inconsistent protections and expose patients to harm. I advocate for uniform federal standards to govern privacy, safety, and accountability.⏎⏎One of the most pressing concerns is patient privacy. Many AI chatbots for mental health support do not comply with HIPAA requirements, risking inadequate protection of patient data. Confidentiality is crucial for effective psychotherapy, and without enforceable safeguards, patients might unknowingly expose sensitive information. I argue that AI adoption should not accelerate without clear federal privacy standards.⏎⏎I am also concerned about the lack of evidence for the safety and effectiveness of AI. Large language models can generate inaccurate or fabricated outputs, and there have been cases of AI systems providing harmful guidance. Corporate metrics like user engagement are not adequate substitutes for rigorous clinical outcome data. New AI tools should be evaluated using meaningful health metrics, such as symptom remission and quality of life improvements. Importantly, this evaluation and safety research should be conducted by independent researchers to avoid conflicts of interest.⏎⏎Lastly, I question AI’s role in the therapeutic relationship. Decades of psychotherapy research show that the therapist-patient relationship is the strongest predictor of treatment outcome. AI systems, no matter how advanced, do not replace human relationships. I believe AI should function as a support tool for licensed clinicians or as an administrative aid, not as a replacement for psychotherapy. Recovery in behavioral health involves more than symptom reduction; it is a long-term, self-directed process. AI systems must be evaluated against these broader, recovery-oriented outcomes, not just short-term symptom changes.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0479♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-21T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Susan♔Robinson♔♔Missouri♔♔United States♔Leadership Conference on Civil and Human Rights♔♔♔♔♔♔My work is with families in St. Louis and Kansas City who are already getting worse care than their wealthier, whiter counterparts, and that's before we add algorithms into the mix. What concerns me about this RFI — and about HHS's general direction here — is that accelerating adoption is being treated as the goal, full stop, with equity considerations framed as a secondary concern to be addressed somewhere down the road.⏎⏎That's backwards. And I want to be specific about why.⏎⏎The pulse oximetry problem is not ancient history. Research published in the New England Journal of Medicine in 2020 and 2021 confirmed that pulse oximeters systematically overestimate oxygen saturation in patients with darker skin, meaning Black patients were getting sicker before clinicians intervened. That's a device that's been in use for decades. We failed to catch it for decades. Now we're about to deploy diagnostic AI systems trained on that same flawed historical data, at much larger scale and with much less transparency about how the outputs are generated, and we're calling that acceleration.⏎⏎What I hear from clinicians in underserved clinics in Missouri — and I have talked to a lot of them, through our work connecting health advocacy to broader civil rights organizing — is that these lower-cost AI systems often come without any real documentation about what populations the training data came from. The facilities that serve Medicaid patients and uninsured patients are the ones buying the budget tools. The facilities with the resources to vet vendors and demand bias audits are not the ones where our communities are getting care. So the disparity doesn't just persist, it gets baked in at the infrastructure level.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI has no place in clinical care. I genuinely don't think that. I've heard real stories about AI helping flag sepsis earlier and I understand the promise. But the sepsis algorithms have also failed badly in documented cases, and what worries me is that when a human clinician makes an error, there's a chain of accountability. When an algorithm makes an error, the clinician often doesn't even know an algorithmic recommendation shaped the pathway they were looking at. That's not a hypothetical — that's how these systems are actually deployed.⏎⏎The other piece I keep coming back to is informed consent. Right now, there is no standard framework requiring that patients be told when an AI tool played a role in their diagnosis or treatment recommendation. None. If a hospital in Missouri is using an AI prior authorization tool that has a racial disparity baked into its denial rates — and there is documented evidence this is happening in commercial insurance prior auth systems — the patient doesn't know that. Their doctor may not even fully know it. That feels like a civil rights problem to me, not just a technical quality-improvement problem.⏎⏎What I'm asking HHS to do is not slow down indefinitely, but to require that any AI clinical tool seeking federal reimbursement or certification go through bias auditing against specific demographic subcategories before deployment, with results that are actually public, not just submitted to a federal agency and buried. And there needs to be a consent standard. Patients should be told.⏎⏎I also want to flag something I haven't seen discussed much: who is doing the auditing? If the vendor does their own bias audit and submits a report, that is not independent verification. The FDA has some models for third-party review of medical devices that HHS could look at, though I recognize the AI context is different and the regulatory pathway gets complicated♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0480♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Radiology Business Management Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-03T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Radiology Business Management Association♔♔♔♔♔♔PO BOX 1636⏎Evansville, IN 47706-0038⏎Direct: 703-621-3359⏎Main: 888-224-7262⏎Fax: 703-621-3356⏎February 1, 2026⏎Mr. Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Section AI RFI⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington DC 20201⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Dr. Keane:⏎The Radiology Business Management Association (RBMA) appreciates the opportunity to respond to the⏎Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence.⏎Established in 1968, RBMA is a professional association that consists of over 2000 radiology practice⏎business leaders who represent over 800 radiology practices in all 50 states. Included are diagnostic⏎radiology, interventional radiology, nuclear medicine, Independent Diagnostic Testing Facilities (IDTFs)⏎and radiation oncology.⏎RBMA is the trusted partner of radiology professionals, advancing the industry and broadening our⏎members’ capacity to provide superior patient experiences.⏎RBMA represents the “business side of radiology.” While our members are deeply familiar with the⏎clinical benefits and challenges of AI, our responses focus primarily on the financial, operational, and⏎administrative implications of AI adoption. We defer to physician organizations on detailed clinical⏎benefits and barriers.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation for AI for healthcare and its adoption and⏎use in clinical care?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎Significant barriers are financial, operational, and contractual in nature.⏎ Financial: Organizations struggle to determine how to pay for AI, how reimbursement will⏎evolve, and how to build viable financial models while AI use cases remain fluid.⏎ Operational: Integrating AI into established workflows can be complex. Workforce readiness⏎and willingness to adopt AI tools are still uncertain.⏎ Contractual: Key liability questions remain unresolved: Who is responsible when AI makes an⏎error? How will malpractice insurers treat AI-assisted care? Additionally, concerns exist⏎regarding data ownership, vendor data governance, and whether vendors intend to monetize⏎clinical data.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎HHS should not assume AI will immediately create efficiencies. Early implementation often requires⏎increased workflow steps, staff time, and capital investment. If HHS wishes to incentivize AI adoption, it⏎should pursue approaches that do not reduce base reimbursement and instead:⏎ Support clinicians and providers with funding mechanisms for procurement, implementation,⏎and ongoing operationalization.⏎ Create and enforce regulations that clarify ownership of data, liability standards, and⏎malpractice coverage expectations related to AI use.⏎ Establish a federal certification framework verifying AI vendor compliance with, and ideally⏎exceeding, HIPAA and cybersecurity standards.⏎ Ensure that AI tools and technologies—where applicable—are exempt from tariffs, reducing⏎financial burden and promoting access.⏎Clinicians remain unsure how to model the long-term costs and reimbursement of AI; HHS policies that⏎reduce cost barriers will significantly improve adoption.⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist, and what role should⏎HHS play?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎RBMA agrees that significant legal uncertainties exist—many of which affect both medical and⏎non-medical AI tools. As noted in response to Question 1, practices face unresolved questions related⏎to:⏎ HIPAA and broader privacy compliance⏎ Malpractice and legal liability⏎ Data ownership, governance, and vendor rights to reuse or monetize data⏎HHS can help accelerate adoption by establishing clearer guardrails, reducing ambiguity for providers,⏎and creating trusted standards for vendors.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods, and should HHS⏎support these processes?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎RBMA recommends that HHS support evaluation methods that promote transparency, robustness, and⏎workflow safety, including:⏎ Pre-deployment validation using diverse, representative datasets⏎ Post-deployment monitoring for model drift, error rates, and performance across demographic⏎groups⏎ Human-centered workflow assessment to evaluate the real-world impact on clinical staff and⏎operations⏎HHS could accelerate progress through grants, cooperative agreements, or vendor certification⏎competitions that reward reliability, equity, and interoperability.⏎5. How can HHS best support private-sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, credentialing)⏎to promote AI use?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎HHS can play a critical role by:⏎ Establishing baseline national standards for AI vendor transparency, cybersecurity, data⏎governance, and model training disclosures⏎⏎ Partnering with private-sector accreditation bodies to create aligned, non-duplicative⏎certification pathways⏎ Encouraging industry-driven benchmarking tools that incorporate privacy, equity, and⏎workflow-impact criteria⏎Such efforts would reduce provider burden and strengthen trust in AI tools.⏎6. Where has AI in clinical care met or exceeded expectations, and where has it fallen short? What⏎novel tools hold the most promise?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎RBMA’s expertise centers on the business and operational aspects of radiology. However, from a clinical⏎lens, RBMA notes ongoing concerns regarding bias in AI training datasets. AI models learn from the data⏎they ingest, and many tools may not be validated against sufficiently diverse patient populations.⏎Examples include:⏎ AI in mammography: Were models trained on women across all breast density categories,⏎varied risk profiles, and diverse racial and ethnic backgrounds?⏎ AI in pneumonia detection: Were tools trained solely on acutely ill hospitalized patients, or do⏎they include routine outpatient imaging?⏎Similar to the HIPAA-oriented certification noted in Question 2, HHS could significantly support⏎providers by creating a federal certification verifying that AI models were trained and validated on⏎comprehensive, demographically diverse datasets.⏎7. Which decision makers drive AI adoption within healthcare organizations, and what are the⏎administrative hurdles?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎Key decision makers typically include executive leaders (CFO, CIO, CMO), radiology administrators, IT⏎departments, compliance officers, and clinical governance committees. Primary hurdles include:⏎ Limited capital budgets⏎ Variable reimbursement and uncertain ROI⏎ Integration challenges with existing systems⏎ Legal and compliance ambiguity⏎ Staff acceptance and training needs⏎8. Where would enhance interoperability widen AI market opportunities and accelerate⏎development?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎Enhanced interoperability—particularly around imaging data formats, structured reporting, and⏎standardized benchmarking data—could dramatically advance AI by allowing:⏎ Easier dataset aggregation for model training⏎ Seamless integration with PACS/RIS/EHR systems⏎ More reliable cross-vendor comparisons⏎ Faster multi-site validation studies⏎Standards such as DICOM, HL7/FHIR, and imaging-specific structured reporting frameworks are⏎essential.⏎9. What challenges do patients and caregivers want AI to address, and what concerns do they hold?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎Patients express interest in AI tools that improve access, accuracy, timeliness, and care coordination.⏎However, they also express concerns related to:⏎⏎ Data privacy and security⏎ Equity and bias in AI decision-making⏎ Whether clinicians remain “in the loop”⏎ Transparency around how AI is used in their care⏎Clear communication from providers—supported by standardized HHS guidance—will be essential for⏎patient trust.⏎10. Are there AI research areas HHS should prioritize to accelerate adoption?⏎RBMA Comments:⏎a. Published findings:⏎There is growing research on AI's impact on clinical workflows, diagnostic accuracy, and efficiency.⏎However, much of the literature is early-stage, limited to specific populations, or lacks large-scale⏎deployment data.⏎b. Costs and benefits:⏎Current literature examines cost-effectiveness unevenly; many studies acknowledge potential financial⏎benefits but do not yet quantify them with sufficient real-world evidence. Additional federally supported⏎economic studies would help providers model total cost of ownership, reimbursement pathways, and⏎long-term ROI.⏎In summary, Artificial intelligence holds substantial promise to improve the quality,⏎consistency, and safety of radiologists’ interpretations of imaging studies. AI tools can flag⏎subtle findings, reduce perceptual errors, prioritize urgent cases, and support clinical decision-⏎making. Yet a central challenge to encouraging innovation in this space is that current Medicare⏎and commercial payer reimbursement systems are largely grounded in fee-for-service models⏎that reward physician time, malpractice risk, and practice expenses rather than measurable⏎improvements in quality or outcomes. If AI improves efficiency or reduces interpretive effort, it⏎may paradoxically threaten reimbursement, discouraging adoption even when patient care⏎improves.⏎Under existing payment structures, radiologist work relative value units (RVUs) are based on⏎assumptions about time and cognitive effort. AI-assisted workflows that shorten reading times⏎or reduce uncertainty could be interpreted as lowering physician work, even though the⏎radiologist retains legal responsibility and malpractice risk. Moreover, practice costs may⏎increase due to AI licensing, integration, validation, cybersecurity, and compliance⏎requirements—expenses that are not well reflected in current reimbursement formulas. This⏎misalignment creates a policy dilemma: innovation that improves quality and safety may be⏎financially penalized.⏎Policymakers can address this dilemma through several complementary strategies. First,⏎reimbursement models should evolve to explicitly recognize quality-enhancing clinical AI as a⏎form of physician work augmentation rather than substitution. Medicare could create add-on⏎payments or new RVU components tied to validated AI use that demonstrably improves⏎diagnostic accuracy, reduces downstream costs, or enhances patient outcomes. Such add-on⏎payment should be permanent and not time limited models. Second, broader adoption of⏎⏎value-based payment models—such as bundled payments —would allow providers to capture⏎the financial benefits of higher-quality, more efficient care enabled by AI.⏎Third, policymakers should support mechanisms to account for persistent malpractice risk. Even⏎when AI assists interpretation, radiologists remain legally accountable. Payment policies should⏎reflect that AI does not eliminate professional liability and may, during early adoption, increase⏎perceived risk. Finally, federal investment in evidence generation, standards, and regulatory⏎clarity can reduce uncertainty and lower adoption costs, making it easier for payers to⏎reimburse AI-enabled care appropriately.⏎Without payment reform, AI risks becoming a victim of its own success. Aligning⏎reimbursement with quality, accountability, and long-term value is essential to realizing AI’s⏎potential to improve radiologic care without undermining the economic foundations of medical⏎practice.⏎RBMA appreciates the opportunity to provide comments on what steps can be taken to encourage⏎adoption and use of Artificial Intelligence as a part of clinical care. RBMA remains committed to working⏎collaboratively with HHS to advance policies that promote high-quality, cost-effective care for Medicare⏎beneficiaries. We welcome ongoing dialogue and stand ready to serve as a resource in shaping a⏎sustainable future for radiology and the broader healthcare system.⏎Sincerely,⏎Jamie Dyer, President RBMA⏎Thomas Greeson, JD, Chair Federal Affairs Committee RBMA⏎Christopher “Kit” Crancer, Chair Radiology Patient Action Network (RPAN) RBMA⏎Jessica Struve, CAE Co-Executive Director, RBMA⏎Linda Wilgus, CPA Co-Executive Director, RBMA♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0481♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Dental Association, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Dental Association♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A⏎330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Docket No. HHS-ONC-2026-0001—Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence⏎as Part of Clinical Care⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎As the leading authority on oral health in the United States, the American Dental Association (ADA)⏎submits the enclosed response to the request for information about accelerating the adoption and use⏎of artificial intelligence as part of clinical care, as outlined in the Federal Register notice of December⏎23, 2025 (90 FR 60108).⏎The American Dental Association, an ANSI-Accredited Standards Developer (ASD), leads national⏎and international efforts to advance dental standards that ensure the safety, reliability, and⏎effectiveness of dental products and technologies. The ADA is the only standards development⏎organization that represents the United States in international standardization, serving as the U.S.⏎Technical Advisory Group (TAG) Secretary to ISO Technical Committee 106 on Dentistry.⏎Additionally, the ADA contributes to global health informatics standards as Co-Secretary of the⏎systems and devices interoperability working group under ISO/TC 215 (Health Informatics).⏎Supported by more than 400 volunteer experts, the ADA Standards Program develops national and⏎international standards, specifications, and guidelines for products that promote oral health through⏎the application of information technology, interoperability, and innovation in dentistry’s clinical and⏎administrative operations. The ADA Standards Program has positioned itself as a leader in setting⏎standards for these new technologies in dentistry.⏎In 2022, the ADA Standards Program published the first white paper covering clinical and non-⏎clinical applications of AI in dentistry, which includes information about the current regulatory⏎environment. It followed by developing the first U.S. standard in AI in dentistry approved by the⏎American National Standards Institute. Additional AI- related standards, white papers, and technical⏎reports are forthcoming.⏎The ADA Forsyth Institute is collaborating with industry and academic partners to develop physics-⏎based digital twins, or high-fidelity, computational surrogates, that couple first-principles multiphysics⏎simulation with machine-learning–enabled parameter estimation and data assimilation. The platform⏎continuously ingests experimental data to calibrate model states and material parameters, forecast⏎performance under novel conditions, and solve inverse-design/optimization problems to recommend⏎formulations and processing conditions that achieve specified target properties. Supported by a five-⏎year award from the National Institute of Dental and Craniofacial Research (NIDCR), the team is⏎deploying digital twins to accelerate the development of next-generation smart dental materials with⏎unprecedented performance. The award represents NIDCR’s first major investment in applying AI to⏎basic oral health research.⏎⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎February 20, 2026⏎Page 2⏎AI’s impact extends well beyond engineering and materials research. It is increasingly central to⏎accelerating biomedical discovery as well. At ADA Forsyth, scientists are using cutting edge AI/ML⏎toolkits to accelerate many important tasks such as guiding RNA/CRISPR design, drug discovery,⏎receptor-ligand binding, microbiome profiling, cell culture media formulation, next-generation⏎sequencing analysis, host-pathogen interactions, spatial transcriptomics, and integrative multi-omics⏎workflows with metapangenomics. These capabilities greatly compress what were historically⏎deliberate, resource-intensive cycles, by reducing the number of wet-lab iterations. As a result, ADA⏎Forsyth scientists can prioritize more high-value hypotheses, and shift discovery from largely intuition⏎driven, trial-and-error approach to also include predictive, data-driven experimentation.⏎We are pleased to enclose our detailed responses to some of the questions HHS has posed. We⏎respectfully offer the following considerations to help small businesses overcome hurdles preventing⏎AI adoption in the clinical space.⏎Thank you for providing the opportunity to comment. We look forward to working with HHS and other⏎agencies on any regulatory actions and grant opportunities that will help shape the use of AI in health⏎care. If you have any questions or wish to talk further, please contact Mr. David Linn at 202-789-5170⏎or linnd@ada.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Richard J. Rosato, D.M.D. Elizabeth Shapiro, D.D.S., J.D., C.A.E.⏎President Interim Executive Director⏎RJR:EAS:rjb⏎Enclosure⏎A-2⏎⏎ATTACHMENT A⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The American Dental Association has identified several primary barriers to private sector innovation and⏎adoption of AI technologies in clinical practice.⏎‑⏎Data heterogeneity in training data sets: Dental data sets are highly heterogeneous because electronic⏎dental records (EDRs) are designed and used differently than electronic medical records (EMRs). Most⏎EDRs focus on documenting procedures and visit-based details rather than capturing standardized⏎diagnostic information. As a result:⏎• Data are often recorded at very granular clinical levels, such as tooth, surface, or probing⏎site, but are not maintained by dental technology systems in consistent, structured formats⏎suitable for research or use for training algorithms.⏎• ANSI/ADA Standard No. 2000.8, SNODENT (Systematized Nomenclature of Dentistry), the⏎dental-specific diagnostic coding system, exists and is harmonized with SNOMED CT, but it⏎is not widely adopted in dentistry, further limiting standardization across providers and⏎systems.⏎• Procedure based documentation typically lacks structured detail on materials, methods,⏎duration, or complexity, contributing to fragmented and incomplete data.⏎‑⏎Heterogeneity in the underlying data may lead to unstable modeling, reduced accuracy, and poor⏎application performance. In dentistry, imaging data (radiographs, cone-beam CT, intraoral scans, and⏎photos) are among the most readily available and reliable sources of training data because they are⏎routinely, repeatedly, and longitudinally captured across patient encounters.1 Despite extensive imaging⏎availability, the predominance of non-standard or unstructured numerical and free text data introduces⏎significant variability and noise, thereby constraining the development, training, and generalizability of⏎advanced AI modeling pipelines. ‑⏎Data interoperability and exchange challenges: Dental data remains difficult to use for AI development⏎because it is often inaccessible, siloed, and stored in systems with limited interoperability. Electronic⏎records frequently exhibit low variable completeness. In addition, sampling bias arises when datasets⏎overrepresent specific populations, such as individuals who are sicker, healthier, or more affluent,⏎resulting in skewed input data. AI models trained on such data inherit these biases, limiting their fairness,⏎reliability, and generalizability.2⏎Limitations of dental claims data for research and development: The utility of training AI models on⏎dental claims datasets may initially seem appealing; however, these claims systems are designed for⏎benefit administration and rule-based plan design, not for capturing the clinical complexity of oral disease⏎or treatment. The information contained within even large “all-payer” databases is inherently narrow and⏎poorly aligned with data required for AI-driven clinical decision support. It is critical to note the difference⏎between medical and dental claims data, in which dental data is typically collected and adjudicated based⏎on covered services rather than on documenting underlying conditions, disease severity, or treatment⏎complexity.⏎Data governance at scale: Data governance at scale to support AI innovation has yet to be realized.⏎Implementing standardized, secure, and automated processes would improve trust in data quality;⏎however, this cannot be managed through a fragmented policy and technology landscape. The current⏎environment of state specific privacy, disclosure, consent, and oversight rules which often have more⏎restrictions than federal privacy and security rules, forces AI developers to operate under the toughest⏎‑⏎1 Schwendicke, F., Samek, W., & Krois, J. Artificial Intelligence in Dentistry: Chances and Challenges. Journal of Dental Research,⏎2020; 99(7): 769–774. https://doi.org/10.1177/0022034520915714⏎2 Gianfrancesco MA, Tamang S, Yazdany J, Schmajuk G. 2018. Potential biases in machine learning algorithms using electronic⏎health record data. JAMA Intern Med. 178(11):1544–1547.⏎A-1⏎⏎requirements, increasing operational complexity. Further, inconsistent data governance policies lead to⏎misunderstanding of responsibility for clinician and patient consent, transparency and privacy protections,⏎further blurring liability and malpractice accountability. These all compound as operational barriers to⏎adoption of AI tools in clinical practice such as:⏎• high costs associated with building and maintaining the infrastructure needed for system⏎implementation⏎• extensive training, and workforce development—challenges that are amplified by the ongoing⏎workforce shortage.⏎• unclear accountability increases legal and operational risk, much of which may fall upon dental⏎practices, particularly those that qualify as covered entities under laws such as HIPAA, the False⏎Claims Act, or Section 1157 of the Affordable Care Act.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please⏎provide specific changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎The American Dental Association recommends consideration of the following to incentivize the effective⏎use of AI in clinical care.⏎Strengthen dental data capture and reporting in federally funded programs: The ADA is encouraged⏎by continued engagement by the ASTP/ONC, CMS, and other federal agencies in the recognition of⏎dental-specific data, notably the inclusion of the Code on Dental Procedures and Nomenclatures (CDT) in⏎the Corrected Applicable Standard Reference for Procedures within USCDI v5. The ADA appreciates the⏎ASTP/ONC’s efforts to ensure that CDT maintains parity with other standard terminologies in the⏎procedures data element.⏎The ADA continues to pursue the inclusion of dental-specific data elements in those standards that⏎support administrative transactions and clinical care. The ADA supports ongoing efforts to improve the⏎recording of disease and diagnosis data within federally funded programs. To support these efforts, we⏎encourage HHS to consider incentivizing clinical documentation improvement activities and technology-⏎assisted disease management pilots for dental providers.⏎Incentivize modern technology infrastructure: Dentistry has not benefited from appropriations to⏎support electronic health record infrastructure development, a prerequisite for AI integration. We⏎encourage HHS to provide funding to support data exchange. Incentives are increasingly important to⏎support rural and small practices in adopting emerging AI technology meaningfully.⏎Establish external, independent validation of dental AI algorithms using synthetic data and⏎informatics standards: Small differences between training data and real-world conditions can produce⏎harmful errors. External, independent validation using industry-specific and trusted standards is critical.⏎The ADA is committed to developing foundational standards for validating interoperability and AI tools,⏎including the publication of terminology standards that define the vocabulary used in dentistry, data⏎structure and exchange standards that define data models to enable interoperable communication, and⏎content standards that define the data required to support administrative needs and clinical care. These⏎standards are critical to creating consistent, structured, high-quality datasets needed to build reliable AI⏎test data. Interoperability standards and gold-standard synthetic datasets are foundational to improving⏎trust in AI tools. The ADA supports public-private partnerships in ensuring the right standards and⏎synthetic gold standard datasets remain in place to support AI use.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal and⏎implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎A-2⏎⏎Standardize definition of “Non-Medical Devices”: A standardized definition of “Non-Medical Devices”⏎across agencies, organizations, and industry would provide the necessary foundation to define the need⏎for disclaimers, consent, privacy, and security oversight, as well as implications of telehealth requirements⏎based on the type of information exchanged and stored. This lack of consistency in definition widens the⏎gap in AI adoption, driven by liability concerns and implications for malpractice coverage.⏎Improve AI vendor accountability: Dental practices increasingly rely on third-party vendors for critical⏎systems, including electronic dental records (EDRs), imaging software, and appointment management⏎tools. The rapid expansion and availability of AI tools place significant responsibility on providers to not⏎only judge the validation and performance of these products but also to implement and maintain privacy⏎and cybersecurity measures, maintain oversight, and assess vendor reliability. Currently, there are gaps⏎in the federal regulatory space to assist providers when making such complex technology decisions. The⏎ADA recommends HHS consider establishing standardized requirements for labeling AI-based products⏎and tools designed to assist in improving trust and adoption, while strengthening shared decision-making⏎between clinicians and their patients.⏎Further, the ADA recommends that HHS strengthen HIPAA requirements and enforcement to make⏎vendors of electronic dental technology accountable for providing secure, compliant systems. Vendors⏎should be required to certify that their solutions meet HIPAA and industry security standards and include⏎features such as encryption and multi-factor authentication (MFA) as default configurations. By clarifying⏎the division of responsibilities between health care providers and software vendors, HHS can ensure that⏎practices have the tools and support necessary to meet compliance obligations without shouldering⏎disproportionate burdens. Furthermore, the ADA would support efforts to create simplified, standardized⏎templates for risk analysis and asset management, easing the burden on practices and ensuring⏎consistent compliance with privacy laws governing AI technology. Administrative tasks not supported by⏎practice management or health record systems are often performed through workaround applications that⏎may not require a business associate agreement, shifting compliance accountability to clinicians.⏎Strengthening guidance related to accountability for software systems and their role in privacy and data⏎protection issues will allow clinicians to consider AI adoption to improve efficiency in administrative tasks,⏎as well as to support clinical decision-making to improve care.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered evaluation⏎methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which mechanisms⏎would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize⏎competitions)?⏎Facilitate establishment of gold-standard synthetic data sets: To our knowledge, AI models are only⏎as good as the data used in their development. The ADA believes that establishing “gold-standard”⏎synthetic data sets based on real-world clinical and administrative data is key to evaluating new AI⏎models before they are applied to patient care. The evaluation framework should include criteria for⏎consistent performance and metrics to monitor bias while reinforcing transparency. For example, pre-⏎deployment evaluation should include external validation on multi-center datasets, calibration⏎assessment, bias analyses, and prospective studies to measure real-world performance without affecting⏎care. Post-deployment monitoring should include drift detection, version/change-control governance,⏎ongoing performance reporting, and structured recording and analysis of AI tool errors. The strongest⏎dental AI solutions ultimately function as clinical copilots, delivering measurable business impact while⏎reinforcing transparency, reliability, and clinician confidence.⏎Standards provide roadmaps for evaluating and integrating AI systems into dental practice by establishing⏎criteria for safety, efficacy, transparency and fairness. ADA Technical Report No. 1109, Dentistry –⏎Evaluation of Dental Image Analysis Systems Using Augmented/Artificial Intelligence defines consistent⏎performance and bias metrics enabling pre and post deployment evaluations and reporting.⏎5.How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎A-3⏎⏎Support public private partnerships to solve real world administrative burdens: Credentialing⏎remains one of the most administratively burdensome aspects of clinical operations, and AI tools offer a⏎meaningful path to improving the speed, consistency, and reliability of credentialing decisions.⏎Streamlining primary source verification and onboarding improves access to care and removes a barrier⏎for a clinician to seek timely employment opportunities. By supporting the development and responsible⏎adoption of such tools, HHS can reduce administrative burden, encourage innovation, and strengthen the⏎overall credentialing ecosystem. In fact, the ADA is actively collaborating with CAQH (supporting⏎standardized provider data collection) and with a new AI-based tool from LightSpun.ai (supporting⏎automated primary source verification across all 50 states) to offer an end-to-end AI based credentialing⏎solution for the dental industry. The ADA urges HHS to support public-private partnerships such as these⏎for effective AI use.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have the⏎most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary administrative hurdles⏎to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Support needs of small and rural practices when considering barriers and administrative hurdles⏎for AI adoption: Despite the potential benefits of AI, adoption across dental practices remains uneven,⏎particularly among small and mid-sized practices. Adoption decisions are most influenced by practice⏎owners/clinical leadership, in coordination with IT/informatics, compliance/privacy, and legal/risk⏎management—especially when regulatory status is unclear. The need for such coordination places undue⏎burden on small and rural practices. The primary administrative barriers to implementation are lack of and⏎the cost of current infrastructure to support implementation, high implementation costs, workforce⏎readiness limitations, inadequate interoperability with existing systems, regulatory uncertainty and⏎approval timelines, privacy concerns, unclear liability frameworks, malpractice implications and a lack of⏎standardized accountability in industry and performance benchmarks. For AI tools that fall within FDA’s⏎regulatory framework (e.g., certain diagnostic or decision-support functions), the regulatory⏎clearance/approval process can further add substantial time, cost, and uncertainty—affecting vendor⏎readiness, procurement timelines, and practices’ confidence in performance claims.⏎Upfront financial investment including expenses related to software, hardware upgrades, customization,⏎and ongoing maintenance poses a significant obstacle, especially when short term return on investment⏎(ROI) is uncertain. Workforce related challenges, such as limited training opportunities and the need for⏎workflow adaptation, further complicate adoption and may temporarily affect productivity.⏎Additionally, many AI technologies do not integrate seamlessly with existing electronic health records⏎(EHRs), imaging systems, or practice management platforms. Data security, privacy, and HIPAA⏎compliance concerns remain paramount. Unresolved questions regarding liability and accountability⏎when AI generated recommendations are used in clinical decision making further contribute to hesitancy⏎among dentists. Finally, inconsistent data standards and the absence of clear regulatory guidance limit⏎dentists’ ability to objectively assess AI tools.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Oral health is fundamental to improving the health and well-being of all Americans. HHS can accelerate⏎integration of AI in care delivery and create durable market opportunities by establishing a practice⏎network that provides a federated, multi-site R&D and implementation platform to enable rigorous⏎evaluation and deployment of AI tools in real-work clinical workflows. The ADA’s leadership in standards,⏎data analytics and clinical guidance combined with the ADA Forsyth Institute’s deep scientific expertise⏎and translational research capacity along with participating clinical sites could provide scalable⏎operational environments to test implementation and workflow integration across clinical care sites under⏎A-4⏎⏎consistent processes. To promote generalizable evidence generation, HHS could encourage alignment⏎with major health IT infrastructure and create opportunities for multi-site analyses and longitudinal⏎outcome assessments of de-identified, aggregated health data. ADA can facilitate collaborations to⏎operationalize the practice network to efficiently gather annotated training data and credible evidence.⏎This structure reduces redundant, one-off evaluations, improves data confidence, and creates a scalable⏎pathway from research validation to deployment in a cost-efficient manner.⏎Enhanced interoperability could unlock AI opportunities by creating large, diverse datasets, driving new AI⏎applications for personalized healthcare, diagnostics, and operational efficiency. Outstanding data quality⏎issues, due to non-conformance to standard data models and terminology, failures to adopt broader⏎healthcare informatics standards (e.g. DICOM®), and the proliferation of proprietary APIs in dental⏎technology, force the industry to rely on extensive mapping to standards-based APIs by costly third-party⏎data brokers. The ADA noted several areas for improvement in interoperability in comments to the FDA⏎re: Docket No. FDA–2025–N–0287: Exploration of Health Level Seven Fast Healthcare Interoperability⏎Resources for Use in Study Data Created From Real-World Data Sources for Submission to the Food and⏎Drug Administration; Establishment of a Public Docket; Request for Comments. We reiterate that the ADA⏎collaborates with HL7® by developing dental content and value sets for FHIR standards. Contributors to⏎these efforts include the Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services (CMS), Department of Defense⏎(DOD), Indian Health Service (IHS), as well as private sector companies, to ensure that implementation⏎guide development aligns with priority information exchange guides such as CARIN, DaVinci, and other⏎federally mandated and recommended standards but remain dental centric. This synergy works to embed⏎dental-specific needs in the FHIR® data model, and ultimately makes dental data more readily available⏎for AI modeling.⏎The ADA is ready and willing to assist ASTP/ONC in developing a USCDI+ dental domain to further⏎support interoperability and facilitate AI development. We believe establishing a dental domain could⏎support agency-wide initiatives.⏎A-5♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0482♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Cityblock Health, Inc. , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Cityblock Health, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔CITYBLOCK HEALTH⏎495 FLATBUSH AVENUE 5C⏎BROOKLYN, NY 11225⏎February 23, 2026⏎Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎7500 Security Boulevard⏎Baltimore, MD 21244-1850⏎Thank you for the opportunity to respond to the Request for Information regarding accelerating the adoption⏎and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as part of clinical care. Please accept the following comments from⏎Cityblock Health, Inc. and Cityblock Medical Practice, P.A. (Cityblock). Cityblock offers a fully integrated⏎solution that directly delivers clinical care to some of the highest-risk Medicaid and dually eligible populations⏎with complex needs. Powered by advanced technology that provides its care team with a data-driven⏎understanding of member needs and risks, Cityblock has demonstrated industry-leading engagement and⏎member retention and has achieved meaningful reductions in avoidable hospital readmissions and reduced⏎total cost of care. Cityblock currently serves more than 100,000 members, and partners with national and⏎regional Medicaid and Medicare Advantage health plans and health systems across more than ten states.⏎What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption and use in⏎clinical care?⏎Federal AI Preemption⏎Issue: State-specific AI regulation creates a patchwork system that increases administrative burden, introduces⏎new complexity, disincentivizes innovation, and limits scalability of AI tools.⏎Proposal: Release preemptive federal guidance, similar to HIPAA, and / or work with Congress to create⏎legislation that creates a national framework for AI usage in healthcare. This would align well with steps the⏎Administration has already begun to take via the AI Action Plan.⏎● For example, the December 2025 Executive Order (EO), Ensuring a National Policy Framework for⏎Artificial Intelligence, focuses on centralizing AI policy at the federal level to prevent a patchwork⏎framework of state-specific AI regulations. This EO created an AI Litigation Task Force within the⏎Department of Justice to identify and challenge state regulations that inhibit AI innovation and⏎adoption.⏎● Establishing a healthcare specific AI framework in conjunction with the Task Force would encourage⏎provider adoption by providing a clear roadmap for compliance and best practices in clinical settings.⏎1⏎⏎Collection of Consent for AI/HIPAA⏎Issue: There is currently no guidance clarifying how patient consent can be obtained for providers to use AI⏎tools for Treatment, Payment, and Operations (TPO) purposes that are subject to HIPAA requirements. The⏎lack of clarity regarding consent collection for AI tools could inhibit provider organizations from implementing⏎AI tools or default to the most restrictive use due to perceived legal or compliance risk.⏎Proposal: Release federal guidance on collection of patient consent for providers to use AI tools in their care,⏎including any applicable data sharing and communication as well as clinical care delivery, for TPO purposes.⏎For example, federal guidance could allow providers to collect, as a component of HIPAA consent, patient⏎consent that includes use of AI tools.⏎HIPAA AI Models - Zero Data Retention vs PHI Redaction⏎Issue: Currently, no federal guidance exists regarding how data must be stored by third-party vendors (i.e.⏎contractors that are not HIPAA “covered entities” and that are storing or processing data for providers), e.g. if⏎Zero Data Retention policies are required versus PHI redaction. This lack of clarity limits innovation by adding⏎complexity and duration when setting up partnerships between AI companies and providers, and can⏎disincentivize collaboration between those entities. The ambiguity also increases risk of patient harm if data⏎are stored improperly - including if PHI is passed into AI models, retained in perpetuity, and potentially used as⏎training data which could lead to unauthorized access to or use of PHI.⏎Proposal: Release federal guidance that provides direction for providers working with third-party technology⏎companies regarding data retention requirements.⏎Accurately categorize PHI and non-PHI data sets even when data is integrated for VBC use⏎Issue: Once a healthcare entity ingests any identifiable data, that data is then treated as PHI once linked to PHI⏎even if it is not related to health. This classification creates a barrier to integrated care, incentivizing⏎organizations to silo data to prevent less sensitive information from being governed by the most restrictive⏎sharing requirements. For example, under current data sharing requirements, providers are prohibited from⏎sharing a patientʼs location with a transportation service provider even if the provider is organizing⏎transportation for the patient. This is especially burdensome for value-based care (VBC) providers that are⏎invested in offering evidence-based prevention and must gather information related to access to nutritional⏎food, transportation, etc to accurately assess and address patientsʼ needs.⏎2026 | www.cityblock.com⏎2⏎⏎Proposal: Create a carve-out for value-based care providers to allow data that does not meet federal PHI⏎criteria at 45 CFR § 164.514 to remain classified as Personally Identifiable Information (or non-PHI), even after⏎being ingested by a VBC provider. This will allow VBC providers to better collaborate with non-medical⏎community partners by making it easier to share data without it automatically being treated like PHI, and⏎reduce the administrative burden for both the providers and the community-based organizations. This would⏎also create greater flexibility for provider-patient communication, since non-PHI is not subject to the same⏎robust sharing requirements as PHI. For example, if non-PHI data could be more easily shared, organizations⏎could tailor patient communication to account for their individual concerns, such as sending personalized⏎directions to a clinic via public transportation for members who do not have access to reliable transportation.⏎TCPA HIPAA Exemption Extended to use of AI Generative Voice Tech⏎Issue: The ATDS (automatic telephone dialing system) definition used in the Telephone Consumer Protection⏎Act (TCPA) was developed before the advent of AI voice technology, leaving ambiguity of how AI voice⏎technology should be regulated. Without clear guidance, providers default to the strictest interpretation of the⏎TCPA HIPAA exemption, which risks limiting innovation with AI voice technology. This uncertainty can hinder⏎adoption of sophisticated AI voice technologies, such as automated outbound scheduling and administrative⏎reminders, which could otherwise reduce provider burden.⏎Proposal: Preserve the TCPA HIPAA exemption and expand it to include use of AI generative voice technology⏎for non-clinical purposes, like appointment reminders and other simple healthcare messaging. AI generative⏎voice technology has the potential to reduce administrative burden and allow providers to focus solely on care⏎delivery, versus administrative tasks.⏎CMS Clarity/ Sandbox - Frontier/ New Model Research⏎Issue: There is no federal regulatory clarity or guidance for healthcare providers who want to work with early⏎stage frontier models. This puts healthcare providers at a disadvantage compared to other industries, as AI⏎companies are incentivized to focus on industries with clearer guidelines for testing and use. States are⏎beginning to propose legal frameworks for frontier models with potential healthcare impact; creating a⏎patchwork system by state further complicates innovation and scalability for AI models.⏎Proposal: Release federal guidance allowing experimental authority for healthcare providers to develop⏎sandbox environments as a testing ground for frontier models. Leverage best practices from other examples⏎working with sensitive data such as employing zero data retention policies.⏎What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to incentivize the⏎2026 | www.cityblock.com⏎3⏎⏎effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to⏎augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable Code⏎of Federal Regulations citations.⏎Patient Engagement and Communication⏎Issue: Existing security regulations limit usability of generative AI for personalized communication, e.g. text⏎messages to assist with patient engagement. Specifically, 45 CFR Part 160 and Part 164 (The HIPAA Security⏎Rule) limit the ability to reference PHI and leverage generative AI for meaningful conversations, reminders, and⏎engagement through text messages and other methods absent a secure and encrypted platform.⏎● Existing regulation dates back to the 2000s and was not designed for the current technology⏎landscape and AI capabilities.⏎● The original Security Rule was not written at a time when text messaging was a preferred channel of⏎conversations, therefore limiting a modern patient's ability to engage with a medical provider absent a⏎secure portal or app. For example, under a strict interpretation of the Security Rule, a patient⏎appointment reminder text message could not include the name of the patientʼs medical professional.⏎● Requiring patients to log into specific applications or portals to review care team messages and⏎reminders creates unnecessary barriers and can negatively impact patient engagement. Lower average⏎digital literacy and access to broadband or Wi-Fi could disproportionately affect vulnerable⏎populations like rural communities or low-income individuals.⏎Proposal:⏎● Issue federal guidance around common industry practices for communication of clinical information⏎that is not PHI or is not clearly defined as PHI; e.g., example of texting prescription fill information to⏎patients using the first three letters of the drug name.⏎● Issue federal guidance around the process for patient consent to those communications, including via⏎text and social media, and the opportunity for patients to consent to communication via multiple⏎types of media using a single form or approval.⏎● As remote monitoring devices continue to grow in popularity and usage, federal guidance around⏎real-time provider-patient communication based on device data will be useful. Modalities such as⏎texting can amplify the advantages of sharing real-time data with providers and lead to faster patient⏎engagement.⏎42 CFR Part II Data Sharing⏎Issue: 42 CFR Part II Final Rule, released in 2024, expanded the liabilities for potential harm due to sharing any⏎medical records that may contain substance use disorder. This disincentivizes and creates barriers to data⏎sharing among providers, which can fragment service delivery, leading to inefficient care and poorer health⏎2026 | www.cityblock.com⏎4⏎⏎outcomes.⏎Proposal: Create an exemption for value-based care (VBC) providers to simplify the data sharing process,⏎improve care coordination, and encourage innovation within VBC arrangements. Integrating behavioral and⏎physical healthcare is fundamental to delivering value-based care. Aligning Part 2 data with existing HIPAA⏎standards will enable a more holistic approach to integrated care and ensure that sensitive health data is⏎protected by the same robust privacy guardrails used across the rest of the healthcare system.⏎Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and accelerate the⏎development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards, and benchmarking⏎tools.⏎Closing Quality Gaps⏎Issue: Currently, data from Qualified Health Information Networks (QHIN) can be used by providers to⏎supplement existing data for a more comprehensive view of patient care and health care needs. However,⏎supplemental data from QHINs often cannot be used to meet quality metrics and close gaps. The National⏎Committee for Quality Assurance (NCQA) outlines that data used to close quality gaps must come directly from⏎a patientʼs medical chart, meaning data from a QHIN is not acceptable for this purpose, unless verified through⏎onerous or expensive processes.⏎Proposal: Release federal guidance updating data verification and attestation processes to minimize⏎administrative friction for providers utilizing QHINs. While QHIN participants currently attest to data validity at⏎the point of origin, downstream users are often required to re-verify that same data when using it to close⏎quality gaps. Centralizing verification authority under a body like CMS or NCQA would create a "verify once,⏎use many" framework, incentivizing robust data sharing and operational efficiency.⏎Longitudinal Care Delivery and Coordination⏎Issue: In the short term, integrated care delivery is hindered because key data are unstructured, lack of⏎availability of a truly holistic longitudinal record facilitated by interoperability, and no means to identify the⏎same member across the multitude of records.⏎Proposal: CMS could encourage interoperability and strengthen the digital health ecosystem by embedding⏎robust data-sharing expectations into future guidance, ensuring that information follows the patient across⏎the continuum of care.⏎● With enhanced interoperability, VBP providers like Cityblock can shorten time to market, enable⏎2026 | www.cityblock.com⏎5⏎⏎real-world research with large, comparable cohorts, and more easily coordinate care across multiple⏎health systems.⏎● Innovations such as AI-powered care coordination, AI copilots, and AI first care teams working across⏎fragmented systems represents a transformative opportunity. Aggregating FHIR-based data across⏎medical, behavioral health, long-term services and supports, and community-based organizations⏎could help enable the use of AI to improve continuity and coherence in the member experience,⏎generate actionable care plans, and ensure synchronization across different actors.⏎● Expanded access to claims data, Admission Discharge Transfer (ADT) events, behavioral health⏎information, medications, lab results, diagnoses, assessments, referrals and social care needs via FHIR⏎based APIs and via national exchange infrastructure will make it easier to identify gaps in care and⏎manage risk more effectively in real time.⏎● When paired with transparent benchmarking frameworks like HEDIS metrics, interoperability expands⏎from a compliance requirement into a strategic driver for continuous quality improvement and⏎evidence-based care delivery.⏎● Improved interoperability will also support the ability to turn unstructured care delivery information⏎into discrete signals for ingestion into machine learning and AI models. Consistent, discrete data with⏎standardized definitions helps turn unstructured data into discrete signals making it more ingestible⏎for machine learning and AI models⏎● Additionally, the inverse is potentially even more impactful, as AI will actually be able to accelerate⏎interoperability by leveraging large language models (LLMs) to execute on feature extraction from⏎unstructured data and smooth over any schema differences between systems. In other words, LLMs⏎are very effective at translating unstructured data into structured data, particularly when there are⏎standardized definitions for structured data points.⏎What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the adoption and use⏎of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of⏎AI in clinical care?⏎Patient and Caregiver Feedback⏎Issue: Successful use of AI in healthcare delivery hinges on the active buy-in and trust of both patients and⏎caregivers.⏎Proposal: Within CMS models, include stakeholder engagement efforts with patients and caregivers as new AI⏎tools are introduced into the healthcare experience. Patients and caregivers have already raised several⏎opportunities for AI to address existing challenges within health care, as well as some concerns regarding the⏎2026 | www.cityblock.com⏎6⏎⏎loss of human connection.⏎● Patients and caregivers express interest in AI adoption in clinical care to support administrative tasks⏎such as appointment scheduling, identifying in-network providers, and interpreting coverage.⏎● Thereʼs also an opportunity for AI solutions to support with real time translation for non-English⏎language speakers, and plain language explanation of medical information and treatment options.⏎● Patients are comfortable with technology as a tool to improve continuity and collaboration, for⏎example AI tools that enable care teams to holistically work together and close the loop from prior⏎conversations.⏎● However, patients and caregivers have expressed concerns about the loss of human connection and⏎empathy with the advent of AI solutions, the ineptitude of AI tools to solve their problems relative to⏎providers, and the biases inherent in algorithms that AI uses.⏎Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎Population Specific Data for AI Training⏎Issue: Most AI tools are trained on commercial insurance data or academic medical center populations. The⏎functionality of an AI model is a direct reflection of its training data. AI tools should be trained on⏎population-specific data; models derived from commercial or academic populations that fail to account for⏎the unique socio-clinical profile of Medicaid beneficiaries and dually eligible individuals could exacerbate⏎existing inequities and inefficiencies.⏎Proposal: CMS could incentivize or fund creation of realistic synthetic datasets that preserve the statistical⏎properties (e.g. coverage gaps, state variation) of real Medicaid data, and publish them openly.⏎● CMS could also encourage public performance reporting for Medicaid AI tools, including metrics⏎stratified by race, language, disability, and dual-eligible status, to create market pressure for⏎vendors to actually train on Medicaid data.⏎Provider-Led AI Research⏎Issue: Investment in AI solutions developed outside of the health care system often need to be retrofit and⏎incorporated into care models by providers, and may not improve pain points fully or at all. AI should be⏎developed by providers within the system, for seamless and effective integration into care delivery.⏎Proposal: CMS could incentivize and encourage provider-led AI research and development through⏎2026 | www.cityblock.com⏎7⏎⏎demonstrations or the creation of AI Sandboxes that allow providers to test AI tools. These sandboxes could⏎provide a temporary 'safe harbor' to allow for real-world testing of how AI can streamline workflows and⏎improve patient-provider engagement. Providers are best positioned to develop AI tools that make care more⏎rather than less personalized. Innovative providers are already finding ways to use AI to reduce the amount of⏎paperwork doctors have to do, giving patients more time with human providers.⏎AI Research⏎Issue: Lack of AI research and data focused on Medicaid settings risks developing tools that do not function as⏎well for, or miss opportunities, to improve Medicaid care models.⏎Solution: CMS could offer encouragement, or potentially a financial incentive, to evaluate AI within Medicaid⏎settings. Specifically evaluating AI performance in Medicaid settings, where demographics, comorbidity⏎patterns and care utilization differ from other populations, would be helpful.⏎● Evaluations should measure not just clinical outcomes but also clinician adoption, health equity⏎impacts, patient trust and cost-effectiveness.⏎● Implementation science research to evaluate workflow integration would be useful.⏎○ For example, tracking metrics like clinician adoption of tools, alert fatigue, override rates, and⏎patient experience measures would help stakeholders better understand the utility of these⏎solutions in the Medicaid and dually eligible populations.⏎● An approved framework or guidance on AI safety will likely accelerate provider adoption. This can be⏎developed through research that focuses on creating standardized approaches for AI "labeling" and⏎usage regulations.⏎Please do not hesitate to reach out if we can be of any assistance. It would be our pleasure to discuss these⏎comments, and we appreciate the chance to share them.⏎Sincerely,⏎Alberto Lopez Toledo⏎Chief Technology Officer, Cityblock Health, Inc.⏎alberto.lopez@cityblock.com⏎2026 | www.cityblock.com⏎8♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0483♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-13T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Rocha, David, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-12T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔David♔Rocha♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔HHS, CMS, ASTP/ONC, and the NLM should look at the evolution of FHIR APIs with AI to align with graph query standards, such as ISO 39075 for graph query language. This will be useful for relationship aware interoperability and returning context graphs while preserving REST based interoperability.⏎⏎GQL on FHIR with AI will be useful to reason over care and relationship aware clinical reasoning.⏎⏎References:⏎ISO 39075⏎https://www.iso.org/standard/76120.html⏎⏎https://jtc1info.org/wp-content/uploads/2024/04/2024-Article-39075-Database-Language-GQL.docx.pdf⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0484♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from NCPDP, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔NCPDP♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services Office of the Deputy Secretary⏎HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Submitted electronically via regulations.gov⏎Re: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎The National Council for Prescription Drug Programs (NCPDP) is a not -for-profit American National Standards⏎Institute (ANSI) Accredited Standards Developer (ASD) consisting of more than 1, 300 members representing⏎entities including, but not limited to, claims processors, data management and analysis vendors, federal and state⏎government agencies, insurers, intermediaries, pharmaceutical manufacturers, pharmacies, pharmacy benefit⏎managers, p rofessional services organizations, software and system vendors and other parties interested in⏎electronic standardization within the pharmacy services sector of the healthcare industry. NCPDP provides a forum⏎wherein our diverse membership can develop business solutions, including ANSI -accredited standards and⏎guidance f or promoting information exchanges related to medications, supplies and services within the healthcare⏎system.⏎NCPDP would like to thank the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) for the opportunity to comment⏎on the Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as part of Clinical⏎Care. NCPDP supports HHS’ focus to establish a forward -leaning, industry -supportive and secure approach to⏎accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.⏎While NCPDP has no direct comment on HHS’ approach to regulation and reimbursement, NCPDP believes the⏎continued funding of the Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA) is an important part of⏎research and development. The need to improve and enhance data exchange among all health care participants⏎is critical to improve patient outcomes , reduce administrative burden and utilize emerging tools, such as AI .⏎Comments on the specific questions can be found below.⏎1. HHS Question: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎a. NCPDP Comment : One of the potential barriers to innovation and AI adoption is the slow pace at⏎which standards for industry use are named in regulations , such as those named in Medicare⏎Modernization Act (MMA), Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act (HIPAA) and⏎others. Greater allowance of the industry to move standards forward more quickly would result in⏎greater innovation in AI. Instead of requiring a specific version of a standard, applying a minimum⏎standard version expectation could result in faster updates to processes utilizing the standards⏎and, therefore, expedited innovation available for use with and for AI tool s. The industry could⏎adopt new features without government regulations adopting new versions resulting in greater⏎support for AI innovation. To facilitate faster adoption of these standards, Medicare Part D and⏎HIPAA regulations could adopt a process similar to ASTP SVAP.⏎Another barrier to AI adoption in health care is the degree of transparency within AI tools. As an⏎example, the use of AI when renewing a prescription should be disclosed and documented ,⏎ensuring transparency to the pharmacist in case there are questions or concerns .⏎National Council for Prescription Drug Programs, Inc.⏎9240 E Raintree Dr, Scottsdale, AZ 85260⏎480-477-1000 | www.ncpdp.org⏎⏎2. HHS Question: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize⏎to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs⏎could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific⏎changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎a. NCPDP Comment : As stated in Question 1, the use of AI tools in clinical care needs to be⏎transparent, disclosed and documented throughout the care continuum to allow for full⏎transparency and accountability. New policies designed to enforce this transparency need to be in⏎place for clinicians and patients to trust those health care processes using AI tools. Regulations are⏎needed to determine who is responsible when AI generates incorrect clinical guidance, how AI⏎documentation should be stored to protect c linicians and safe -harbor provisions for clinicians and⏎payers using CMS -approved, validated AI tools. Additionally, investing in AI literacy both pre - and⏎post-entering the workforce is key to sustaining and developing AI tools and usage in clinical⏎care. Literacy could be achieved through multiple channels including funding for creating national⏎AI competency standards for health care providers, grants for operational AI training focusing on⏎medication therapy management, claims resolution and workflow op timization and working with⏎national education accreditation organizations to develop AI curriculum for students entering⏎health care.⏎3. HHS Question: For non -medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel⏎legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures (e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). W hat novel legal and implementation issues⏎exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎a. NCPDP Comment : Consent for the use of AI, transparency, accountability, product liability and⏎regulatory authority are all issues that need to be resolved at a federal level to avoid a patchwork⏎of state regulations. NCPDP recommends HHS convene expert panels , including representation⏎from patient advocacy groups and professional organizations from across the legal and clinical⏎spectrum of health care , to address these issues .⏎4. HHS Question: How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎a. NCPDP Comment : NCPDP welcomes and encourages HHS and other governmental organizations⏎to more actively participate in standards development to promote innovative and effective AI use⏎to support clinical care.⏎5. HHS Question: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data standards,⏎and benchmarking tools.⏎a. NCPDP Comment : Enhancing interoperability leads to more robust AI training and usage. Through⏎broader interoperability, clinical data available for training AI models on health care will be⏎expanded with the expectation of creating better models and thereby enhancing patient care. As⏎participation in TEFCA increases , including pharmacy participation, expansion in interoperability of⏎the care team should be a natural outcome.⏎NCPDP looks forward to serving as a trusted partner and resource as HHS works to create policies to ease health⏎data exchange and promote innovation of AI in clinical care. NCPDP welcomes the opportunity to engage further,⏎share our expertise and collaborate with HHS to make these goals a reality.⏎For direct inquiries or questions related to this letter, please contact:⏎National Council for Prescription Drug Programs, Inc.⏎9240 E Raintree Dr, Scottsdale, AZ 85260⏎480-477-1000 | www.ncpdp.org⏎⏎Paul Wilson⏎Technical Analyst, Standards Development⏎NCPDP⏎standards@ncpdp.org⏎Respectfully,⏎Lee Ann C. Stember⏎President & CEO⏎National Council for Prescription Drug Programs (NCPDP)⏎National Council for Prescription Drug Programs, Inc.⏎9240 E Raintree Dr, Scottsdale, AZ 85260⏎480-477-1000 | www.ncpdp.org♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0485♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Academy of Nursing, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Academy of Nursing♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Secretary⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎Hubert H. Humphrey Building⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎RE: RIN 0955-AA13 — Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial⏎Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy,⏎The American Academy of Nursing (Academy) is pleased to offer the following comments in response to⏎the December 23, 2025 request for information regarding actions the Department of Health and Human⏎Services (HHS) can take to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of clinical care.1 With more than⏎3,200 Fellows, the Academy—an honorific society and policy organization—represents nursing’s most⏎accomplished leaders in policy, research, administration, practice, and academia. Collectively with our⏎Fellows and partners, the Academy aims to create solutions that matter, inspire change that propels⏎transformation, and envision a healthier future.⏎The Academy appreciates HHS’s commitment to advancing the transformative potential of AI. The⏎Academy underscores that the rapid and expansive growth in AI technology presents a remarkable⏎opportunity for advancing innovations in person-centered care and health care delivery. As AI rapidly⏎impacts health care delivery, health sciences education, and research, it is essential to strengthen and⏎continue developing strong policies that safeguard patients, families, and communities, govern privacy,⏎and uphold data protection. As HHS considers next steps, the Academy offers the following comments in⏎alignment with the questions HHS has outlined.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or programs⏎could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific⏎changes and applicable Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎The Academy has long called for APRNs (Advanced Practice Registered Nurses)2 to be allowed to practice⏎to the top of their education, certification, and training.3 To promote greater value in clinical care⏎through the use of AI and incentivize its effective use, we encourage HHS to ensure that APRNs are⏎appropriately reimbursed for the expert care they provide using new technologies. For decades, APRNs⏎have experienced arbitrary regulatory limits at the federal level that impacts the length of time for a⏎patient to receive services or medical devices. Medicare and Medicaid conditions of participation restrict⏎American Academy of Nursing⏎1000 Vermont Ave NW | Suite 910 | Washington, DC 20005⏎202-777-1170 | www.aannet.org⏎⏎Page | 2⏎APRN practice by requiring, for example, that physicians supervise anesthesia services provided by⏎certified registered nurse anesthetists (CRNAs) and that every Medicare or Medicaid patient admitted to⏎a hospital be under the care of a physician. Some conditions of coverage restrict APRNs from ordering⏎certain Medicare covered services for patients. These restrictions impact APRNs’ ability to practice to the⏎top of their education, certification, and training and unnecessarily burden patient access to care.⏎Further, we know that when APRNs can practice to the full extent of their education and training, it⏎increases access to care. For example, during the pandemic, many states temporarily lifted the need for⏎APRNs to have collaborative agreements with physicians. This greatly impacted access to care,⏎particularly in rural and underserved areas.4⏎It is also important to note the importance of regular review and updates to regulatory, payment policy,⏎and programmatic design structures at HHS given the exponential growth of AI and rapid pace of change⏎in these landscapes. AI-enabled tools like clinical decision support, remote monitoring, and virtual triage⏎could significantly expand APRN capabilities in medically underserved areas, but only if reimbursement⏎and scope of practice policies keep pace. We encourage HHS to implement guidelines for consistent,⏎regular review and updates to these structures.⏎As examples, HHS could lead policy to expand payment for AI-supported asynchronous care by APRNs (at⏎42 CFR § 410.78); add CMS regulatory clarification that AI-assisted documentation, summarization, and⏎risk stratification support, but do not replace, individual APRN practice (at 42 CFR § 410.75); explicitly⏎encourage and recognize AI-enabled care management and early detection when they improve⏎Accountable Care Organization (ACO) quality and utilization patterns; and strengthen state contract⏎expectations so plans can adopt AI-supported APRN interventions (e.g., early deterioration detection,⏎falls risk escalation, medication adherence risk) as recognized Quality Assurance and Performance⏎Improvement (QAPI) activities and performance improvement projects (at 42 CFR § 438.330).⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures ( e.g.,⏎relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and implementation⏎issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎As AI increasingly becomes embedded in health care, the need for transparency in its operations⏎becomes more critical. Trust in AI tools depends not only on their accuracy and effectiveness but also on⏎the availability of clear, understandable explanations for AI supplemented clinical decisions. All AI tools⏎used in patient care should show clear, understandable input data sources and if available, explanations⏎should be provided when desired by a consumer. Moreover, patients, families, and clinicians have the⏎right to know when and how AI is being used in diagnoses, treatment, and care delivery. Above all,⏎privacy must be protected.⏎⏎Page | 3⏎The use of AI in health care and research brings new potential for security threats and data exposure,⏎especially with non-medical devices. Seemingly non-sensitive data that non-medical devices track could⏎inadvertently expose patients to cyber-fraud or large-scale data breaches impacting their privacy, as well⏎as economic health and well-being. Privacy of patient data could be at risk of breach, for example, if⏎patient records are inappropriately used as a source of data. It is critical that foundational pillars⏎underpinning data privacy and cybersecurity such as the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability⏎Act (HIPAA) be maintained and regularly evaluated for necessary updates. Other statutory areas to⏎examine closely to ensure strong privacy protections include the Health Information Technology for⏎Economic and Clinical Health (HITECH) Act and the Family Educational Rights and Privacy Act (FERPA).⏎Many statutory standards predate modern AI and do not address the unique security and privacy⏎challenges of adaptive algorithms, cloud-based model updates, or the extensive data sharing required⏎for AI training and validation. While data sharing and interoperability are key aspects of health care⏎delivery and research, it is vital that data sharing policies are strengthened to encompass the use of AI⏎and the expansion of AI models.⏎Overall, the exponential growth in AI technology necessitates routine examination of current and⏎emerging models, data management and sharing policies, and HIPAA compliance. Currently, health care⏎organizations routinely sign business associate agreements (BAAs) that may permit extensive data⏎sharing that creates ethical transparency gaps in data governance. Despite protective provisions under⏎the HIPAA Privacy Rule and Security Rule, patients and clinicians may lack information and sufficient⏎control over data sharing in a markedly different care environment shaped by AI. Transparent disclosure⏎is needed for BAAs and data sharing arrangements that govern how patient information is used to train,⏎validate, and improve AI systems. Increased standards under the HIPAA Privacy Rule could require⏎transparent disclosure of BAAs and other data sharing arrangements to clinicians and individuals.⏎Additionally, the HIPAA Security Rule could be updated to incorporate contemporary cybersecurity⏎frameworks for electronic protected health information (ePHI) such as standards from the National⏎Institute of Standards & Technology (NIST) and International Organization for Standardization (ISO), for⏎example, ensuring health care entities have clear, robust guidance for protecting patient data in AI-⏎driven environments. It is important to include nurses (including nurse scientists and informaticists) in⏎governance committees, procurement decisions, post-market monitoring, and evaluation processes to⏎guide responsible AI implementation and navigate these issues.⏎Question 9: What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by⏎the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎Nurses and other clinicians have a critical role to play in promoting AI literacy and fostering⏎understanding for patients and clinicians on the use of AI in health care. When surveyed, many people in⏎the United States note some level of concern about use of AI in daily life, and awareness of common⏎uses of AI can vary widely.5 As patient educators and advocates, nurses and other clinicians can support⏎patient populations by providing information and protecting their data.⏎⏎Page | 4⏎As AI grows in patient care, nurses and other health care professionals may have concerns regarding AI⏎replacing their roles as well as how AI technology could threaten the quality and safety of care for⏎patients and communities.6 At the same time, many nurses recognize AI’s current ability and future⏎potential to alleviate workload stress points while innovating care. They express a desire for increased AI⏎support in their work.7 To guide AI development, implementation, and evaluation, it is critical to⏎maintain an overarching focus on the specific function AI holds in care delivery. While AI can create⏎efficiencies that allow clinicians to better focus on and enhance patient care, AI cannot replace their⏎critical thinking and clinical judgment,8 nor can it replace the human connection and care that are⏎central to the clinician-patient relationship.9 From an ethical perspective, with AI’s integration into the⏎health care system, administration, operations, and clinical care, a triad of the patient, clinician, and AI⏎must be maintained where one entity cannot engage without the others.10⏎Question 10: Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎There is a need for comprehensive AI fluency research to guide the education of current and future⏎health care professionals and promote ethical and responsible use of AI in individual care. Health care⏎professionals and populations who use AI tools may currently lack an understanding of their underlying⏎algorithms, data sources, potential biases, or security vulnerabilities. Unlike traditional software with⏎transparent logic paths, AI models often cannot explain how they reach conclusions. AI algorithms⏎(particularly using machine learning and deep learning) can lack transparency and explainability, creating⏎a “black box” issue.11 Therefore, incorrect outputs may not be easily discoverable. Similarly, large⏎language models (LLMs) have the potential to “hallucinate,” or generate false information as an output,12⏎and algorithms can drift and degrade over time as patterns change.13 The AI fluency gap is a pressing and⏎current issue, as consumers are already integrating AI into health decisions outside of clinical settings.⏎For example, ChatGPT Health was announced in January 2026 as a platform for people to connect their⏎medical records and wellness apps with the AI chatbot technology.14 Therefore, AI fluency is important⏎to address not only as AI is increasingly used by clinicians in care, but also as people increasingly use AI⏎for health purposes in everyday life.⏎If errors occur, this can threaten misdiagnoses and pose ethical issues for individual autonomy and⏎informed consent, creating significant risks for both individuals and clinicians.11 For this reason,⏎education is critical to ensure that the health care workforce will attain the fundamental AI literacy⏎necessary for safe and effective development, implementation, and evaluation of AI.15 AI literacy is⏎important as a longitudinal, lifetime professional competency rather than a discrete training⏎requirement. It is also important to note that AI fluency research is needed in parallel with education⏎updates. Educational reforms cannot wait for research, though research remains critical to continue to⏎guide these programs into the future.⏎Comprehensive AI fluency research, with thorough investigation of competency frameworks, educational⏎strategies, workforce readiness, and evaluation of learning outcomes, is needed to guide current and⏎future health care professionals. Education is also critical for patient populations to attain the⏎⏎Page | 5⏎fundamental AI literacy necessary for safe and responsible use of AI. Above all, the triad structure of⏎health care in the new era should be maintained—patient, clinician, and AI—both human wisdom and⏎sourced intelligence.⏎AI is transforming nursing and other clinical practice by enabling tools such as virtual assistants and⏎chatbots to handle routine tasks, freeing clinicians to focus on complex care. Workflows, task routing,⏎auto-scheduling, and reminders to patients can all be improved through AI-enabled technology.⏎Predictive analytics can help personalize interventions, allowing clinicians to anticipate and address⏎patient needs more effectively and efficiently. Implementation of technologies will alter clinician and⏎patient interactions and the delivery of care more broadly, which necessitates research to ensure safe⏎and effective integration. AI-driven ambient documentation systems, for example, which involve varying⏎types of AI scribes that can generate notes for clinicians attending to patients, are already rapidly being⏎deployed across health care settings. Recent evidence highlights the need for comprehensive validation⏎frameworks for this technology as well as research on its impact on documentation quality, clinical⏎decision-making, and interprofessional communication.16 This validation needs to include quality⏎assessments from clinicians.⏎AI also has the potential to enhance traditional models of care with new layers of intelligence and⏎flexibility as well as expand care beyond traditional settings to novel settings, including home-based⏎environments. AI tools are advancing faster than care models can absorb, which is an area to explore⏎through research. Research should also explore how to integrate AI without increasing burden and⏎burnout, including examining the cognitive load for clinicians. Research-backed tools that alert nurses⏎when an algorithm’s predictive power has "drifted" and is no longer reliable are also needed. All AI use⏎cases merit careful, ongoing study. Furthermore, research must consider whether data variables used in⏎AI tools appropriately capture important factors such as housing stability, income, geography, nutrition,⏎and access to care. Examining AI’s impact for both individuals and clinicians in rural, medically⏎underserved, and resource-limited communities is essential to ensure effective deployment.⏎The Academy thanks HHS for the opportunity to provide comments on accelerating the adoption and⏎use of AI as part of clinical care. We stand ready to work with you to provide expertise and shape policy⏎solutions that champion health and well-being, ensuring healthy lives for all people. If we can be of any⏎assistance to you or your staff, please do not hesitate to contact the Academy’s Chief Policy Officer,⏎Christine Murphy, at cmurphy@aannet.org or 202-777-1170.⏎Sincerely,⏎Debra J. Barksdale, PhD, FNP-BC, FAANP, ANEF, FAAN⏎President, American Academy of Nursing⏎⏎Page | 6⏎1 Department of Health and Human Services. (2025, December 23). Request for Information: Accelerating the⏎Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care. RIN 0955-AA13; 90 FR 60108.⏎2 APRNs include certified nurse-midwives (CNMs), certified registered nurse anesthetists (CRNAs), clinical nurse⏎specialists (CNSs) and nurse practitioners (NPs).⏎3 American Academy of Nursing. (2025, September 11). Comments RE: CMS-1832-P: Medicare and Medicaid⏎Programs; CY 2026 Payment Policies Under the Physician Fee Schedule and Other Changes to Part B Payment and⏎Coverage Policies; Medicare Shared Savings Program Requirements; and Medicare Prescription Drug Inflation⏎Rebate Program.⏎https://cdn.ymaws.com/aannet.org/resource/resmgr/policydocuments/2025policyactions/AAN_CMS_PFS_Comme⏎nts.pdf⏎4 National Academies of Sciences, Engineering, and Medicine; National Academy of Medicine; Committee on the⏎Future of Nursing 2020–2030, Flaubert, J. L., Le Menestrel, S., Williams, D. R., & Wakefield, M. K. (Eds.). (2021). The⏎Future of Nursing 2020-2030: Charting a Path to Achieve Health Equity. National Academies Press (US).⏎https://doi.org/10.17226/25982⏎5 Kennedy, B., Yam, E., Kikuchi, E., Pula, I. & Fuentes, J. (2025, September 17). How Americans view AI and its⏎impact on people and society. Pew Research Center. https://www.pewresearch.org/science/2025/09/17/how-⏎americans-view-ai-and-its-impact-on-people-and-society/⏎6 Simpson, R. L. (2024). How nurses can conquer their fear of AI. American Nurse Journal, 19(1), 68.⏎https://doi.org/10.51256/anj012468⏎7 McKinsey & Company. (2024, October 1). The pulse of nurses’ perspectives on AI in healthcare delivery.⏎https://www.mckinsey.com/industries/healthcare/our-insights/the-pulse-of-nurses-perspectives-on-ai-in-⏎healthcare-delivery⏎8 Yakusheva, O., Bouvier, M. J., & Hagopian, C. O. P. (2024). How Artificial Intelligence is altering the nursing⏎workforce. Nursing Outlook, 73(1), 102300. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.outlook.2024.102300⏎9 Birkholz, L., Martin, M., Barnum, B., Breslin, L., & Kalevor, S. (2025). Navigating artificial intelligence in nursing: An⏎ethical exploration of benefits, risks, and educational shifts. Nursing Outlook, 73(5), 102520.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.outlook.2025.102520⏎10 Harvard Radcliffe Institute. (2024, November). Evolving the Fundamental Principles of Bioethics as Medical⏎Practice Moves from the Patient-Doctor Dyad to the Patient-Doctor-A.I. Triad. https://sites.harvard.edu/evolving-⏎the-fundamental-principles-of-bioethics/⏎11 Webb, J. (2024). Machine learning, healthcare resource allocation, and patient consent. The New Bioethics, 30(3),⏎206–227. https://doi.org/10.1080/20502877.2024.2416858⏎12 Cross, J. L., Choma, M. A., & Onofrey, J. A. (2024). Bias in medical AI: Implications for clinical decision-⏎making. PLOS Digital Health, 3(11), e0000651. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pdig.0000651⏎13 Qiu, Z., Jiang, A., Qi, C., Gan, W., Zhu, L., Mou, W., Zeng, D., Xiao, M., Chu, G., Peng, S., Wong, H. Z. H., Zhang, L.,⏎Zhang, H., Deng, X., Cheng, Q., Tang, B., Wang, Y., Zhang, J., Lin, A., & Luo, P. (2025). Temporal evolution of large⏎language models (LLMs) in oncology. Journal of Translational Medicine, 23(1), 1219.⏎https://doi.org/10.1186/s12967-025-07227-2⏎14 OpenAI. (2026, January 7). Introducing ChatGPT Health: A dedicated experience in ChatGPT designed for health⏎and wellness. https://openai.com/index/introducing-chatgpt-health/⏎15 Cary, M. P., De Gagne, J. C., Kauschinger, E. D., & Carter, B. M. (2024). Advancing health equity through artificial⏎intelligence: An educational framework for preparing nurses in clinical practice and research. Creative⏎Nursing, 30(2), 154–164. https://doi.org/10.1177/10784535241249193⏎16 Topaz, M., Peltonen, L. M., & Zhang, Z. (2025). Beyond human ears: Navigating the uncharted risks of AI scribes in⏎clinical practice. npj Digital Medicine, 8, 569. https://doi.org/10.1038/s41746-025-01895-6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0486♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Hello Heart , HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Hello Heart♔♔♔♔♔♔Re: RFI – Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care⏎On behalf of Hello Heart, we appreciate the opportunity to respond to the Department of⏎Health and Human Services’ (HHS) Request for Information on accelerating the adoption⏎and use of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care.⏎Hello Heart is a digital health company focused on improving cardiovascular and⏎metabolic outcomes at scale. We partner with employers, health plans, healthcare⏎professionals, and health systems to deliver personalized, technology-enabled care that⏎combines connected devices, longitudinal data analysis, behavioral science, and clinical⏎expertise. Cardiovascular disease remains the leading cause of death and one of the most⏎significant drivers of health care costs in the United States. AI, when responsibly⏎developed and deployed, can materially improve early risk detection, care navigation, and⏎chronic disease management in ways that reduce cost and improve outcomes.⏎However, several systemic barriers continue to slow innovation and responsible adoption⏎of the use of AI in clinical care.⏎1. Biggest Barriers to Private Sector Innovation and Adoption of AI in Clinical⏎Care⏎a. Data Access, Infrastructure, and Patient Control⏎AI systems are only as strong as the data they are built upon. In cardiovascular care,⏎meaningful insights require longitudinal, multimodal data claims, labs, vitals, pharmacy⏎records, and patient-reported information. While policy progress has been made under the⏎21st Century Cures Act and the ONC interoperability framework (45 C.F.R. Part 170; 45⏎C.F.R. Part 171), operational barriers remain significant.⏎Health data are still fragmented, difficult to aggregate, and burdensome to access in real⏎time. To unlock AI’s full potential, HHS should prioritize building a true data⏎“superhighway” that enables secure, standardized, API-driven exchange. Critically, this⏎infrastructure must place meaningful, operational control in the hands of patients.⏎Patients, not providers or institutions, should have the final authority to determine what⏎data are shared, with whom, and for what purpose, including with the digital health and AI⏎solutions they choose to use.⏎Reducing operational friction in data sharing will directly accelerate innovation in⏎prevention, early detection, and personalized care.⏎⏎b. Regulatory Ambiguity Around FDA Thresholds⏎There remains insufficient clarity regarding when AI functionality crosses the threshold into⏎regulated medical device territory under the Federal Food, Drug, and Cosmetic Act and 21⏎C.F.R. Parts 801, 807, and 860.⏎Many AI-enabled clinical decision support (CDS) tools, including those that synthesize⏎data, stratify risk, or provide care guidance, augment clinical judgment rather than⏎independently diagnose or prescribe. Yet uncertainty persists regarding whether such⏎tools require a multi-year premarket review process.⏎Regulatory predictability is essential for sustained investment and responsible scaling. As⏎such, we respectfully urge HHS, in coordination with the FDA, to:⏎● Provide clearer operational guidance distinguishing supportive AI from autonomous⏎medical decision-making;⏎● Clarify application of Section 520(o)(1)(E) of the FD&C Act to modern AI-based CDS⏎tools;⏎● Establish risk-based, streamlined regulatory pathways for lower-risk AI systems⏎that enhance, not replace, clinical oversight.⏎c. Defensive Health Care and Fear of Litigation⏎The U.S. health care system is structured to minimize liability exposure. Even where⏎evidence demonstrates that AI performs as well as or better than human clinicians in⏎defined use cases, providers may hesitate to adopt such tools out of concern for litigation⏎risk.⏎Without defined protections or safe harbor frameworks, clinicians and institutions may⏎avoid using AI, even when doing so could improve patient safety. HHS should explore⏎mechanisms that provide reasonable liability protections for the appropriate use of⏎validated AI systems that meet recognized safety and transparency standards.⏎2. Regulatory, Payment Policy, and Programmatic Changes HHS Should⏎Prioritize⏎a. Strengthen Patient-Directed Interoperability and Data Rights⏎HHS should continue strengthening enforcement and operationalization of:⏎● 42 U.S.C. § 300jj-52 (Information Blocking);⏎⏎● 45 C.F.R. Part 171;⏎● 45 C.F.R. Part 170 (ONC Certification Program).⏎In addition, HHS should explicitly reinforce that patients have the right to direct their data⏎to third-party applications of their choosing without unnecessary provider-imposed⏎friction, delay, or discretion. Patients should not be required to rely on provider-mediated⏎approvals to share their own health information with digital health tools they select.⏎Reducing administrative and technical burdens in accessing standardized FHIR-based⏎data will allow AI systems to generate more accurate risk assessments and care⏎recommendations. Empowering patients to control and share their data seamlessly across⏎platforms will also accelerate innovation in preventive care and chronic disease⏎management. A patient-centered interoperability framework, where individuals can easily⏎and comprehensively share their complete health record with trusted AI-enabled⏎solutions, will expand competition, improve care coordination, and promote innovation.⏎b. Align Payment Models With AI-Enabled Value⏎AI is particularly powerful in value-based care environments, where prevention, early⏎intervention, and risk stratification drive measurable savings. CMS should explore ways to⏎recognize AI-enabled clinical decision support, navigation, and risk management within⏎existing value-based arrangements and quality programs.⏎Clear recognition of AI-enabled care coordination, navigation, and decision support within⏎reimbursement frameworks would accelerate responsible adoption. When AI⏎demonstrably improves HEDIS and STARS performance, reduces hospitalizations, avoids⏎unnecessary and/or duplication of services and/or lowers total cost of care, payment⏎models should reflect those improvements.⏎c. Provide Clear Guardrails Without Overregulation⏎HHS should ensure that regulatory oversight is proportionate to risk. Subjecting⏎supportive, lower-risk AI tools to lengthy approval processes will slow beneficial⏎innovation without materially improving safety. Clear guidance will reduce uncertainty⏎while maintaining appropriate safeguards.⏎3. Novel Legal and Implementation Issues⏎AI deployed within clinical workflows raises questions around liability allocation,⏎transparency, and accountability.⏎⏎a. Clear Accountability Frameworks⏎Developers, providers, and institutions require clarity regarding responsibility when AI⏎augments clinical decision-making. Absent defined frameworks, adoption will remain⏎cautious and uneven.⏎To address this, HHS could support development of model governance structures or⏎voluntary safe harbor programs for AI systems that meet defined safety benchmarks and⏎transparency criteria.⏎b. Clarifying FDA Jurisdictional Boundaries⏎As noted, clearer thresholds distinguishing regulated devices from supportive tools are⏎essential to avoid unnecessary delays in deploying beneficial technologies. Developers⏎need operational clarity to avoid over-regulation of tools that merely support clinical⏎reasoning.⏎c. Protecting Patient-Directed Data Use⏎As patients gain greater authority to direct their data to AI-enabled solutions, clear⏎standards should ensure that their choices are respected and that providers cannot⏎unreasonably restrict patient-authorized data exchange. Strengthening patient data⏎agency will reduce fragmentation and enable more accurate, personalized AI-driven⏎insights.⏎d. Encouraging Evidence-Based Adoption⏎Where AI systems meet or exceed clinician-level performance in specific domains, policy⏎frameworks should support their use. Defensive medicine should not prevent deployment⏎of tools that can demonstrably reduce error and improve outcomes.⏎4. AI Evaluation Methods and the Role of HHS⏎Responsible AI deployment requires rigorous evaluation before and after launch.⏎Effective approaches include:⏎● Expert clinical validation during model development, including structured scoring⏎and incorporation of gold-standard examples;⏎● Automated evaluation frameworks that continuously assess safety, accuracy, and⏎appropriateness at scale;⏎⏎● Ongoing post-deployment sampling and human review to ensure sustained quality.⏎HHS could play a transformative role by developing a standardized AI safety evaluation⏎framework or benchmarking model that developers could run their systems against prior to⏎deployment. An HHS-backed safety certification or “seal” would significantly increase⏎provider and patient trust.⏎Mechanisms such as cooperative agreements, grants, or public-private partnerships⏎would be effective vehicles for developing and operationalizing such standards.⏎5. Accreditation and Certification⏎HHS can accelerate adoption by supporting:⏎● Voluntary AI safety certification programs;⏎● Standardized transparency and reporting frameworks; and⏎● Industry-led testing consortia operating under clear federal guidance.⏎An HHS-developed safety evaluation model, coupled with public certification pathways,⏎would provide assurance to providers and patients and accelerate adoption.⏎6. Performance and High-Impact Use Cases⏎AI has demonstrated strong performance in domains requiring data synthesis and clinical⏎judgment, including risk prediction, triage, and chronic disease management. In⏎cardiovascular and metabolic care, AI can identify risk patterns earlier than traditional⏎workflows and support personalized interventions.⏎AI is particularly promising for:⏎● Early identification of hypertension, diabetes, obesity, hyperlipidemia and⏎cardiovascular risk;⏎● Personalized education and behavioral support;⏎● Clinical data summarization;⏎● Care navigation and referral optimization; and⏎● Preventive care adherence.⏎⏎While procedural robotics remains evolving, AI-based decision support should increasingly⏎serve as a first line of defense in judgment-based clinical scenarios that can lead to⏎improvement in health outcomes.⏎7. Organizational Decision-Makers and Administrative Hurdles⏎Within health care organizations, the Chief Medical Officer (CMO) or VP Medical Affairs⏎(VPMA) typically holds decisive influence over clinical AI adoption. Without clinical⏎leadership endorsement, implementation is unlikely to succeed.⏎Primary hurdles include:⏎● Legal and compliance uncertainty;⏎● Procurement complexity;⏎● Liability concerns; and⏎● Cultural resistance driven by safety perceptions.⏎Clear regulatory guidance and validated safety frameworks will directly influence⏎executive-level adoption decisions.⏎8. Interoperability and Market Acceleration⏎Enhanced interoperability would expand opportunities across nearly all domains of AI-⏎enabled care. Standardized exchange of structured clinical data, remote monitoring data,⏎pharmacy records, and patient-reported outcomes is foundational to innovation.⏎True interoperability must mean patient-directed interoperability. When patients can⏎seamlessly aggregate and authorize use of their complete health data across settings and⏎solutions, AI tools can deliver more accurate, preventive, and personalized care.⏎Continued enforcement and evolution of FHIR-based exchange standards will materially⏎accelerate both research and commercialization.⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Perspectives⏎Patients and caregivers consistently seek:⏎● Safety and trust in AI-enabled care;⏎⏎● Clear guidance on what care to seek and when;⏎● Administrative simplification, including consolidated medical summaries; and⏎● Personalized education tailored to their specific conditions.⏎Trust is central. Government-backed validation frameworks and transparent evidence⏎demonstrating AI performance relative to human decision-making will be essential to⏎broad acceptance.⏎At the same time, provider resistance, often framed around safety, may slow progress.⏎Public, evidence-based evaluations can help bridge that gap. Transparent evaluation⏎standards and public evidence demonstrating AI performance relative to human decision-⏎making will be essential to overcoming skepticism.⏎Patients also increasingly expect control over their own health information. Empowering⏎individuals to determine how their data are shared, and ensuring that their choices are⏎honored, will be foundational to trust in AI-enabled care.⏎10. Priority Research Areas⏎HHS should prioritize:⏎● Comparative studies evaluating AI decision-making against clinician performance;⏎● Research on effective human-AI collaboration models;⏎● Longitudinal outcome studies measuring quality and cost impact; and⏎● Standardized safety benchmarking methodologies.⏎Publishing rigorous, peer-reviewed evidence demonstrating where AI meets or exceeds⏎human-level performance will be instrumental in building public and provider trust and⏎accelerating responsible adoption.⏎Conclusion⏎AI has the potential to meaningfully improve cardiovascular and metabolic outcomes,⏎enhance clinical decision-making, reduce administrative burden, and lower total cost of⏎care. Realizing that potential requires improved data infrastructure, clear and⏎proportionate regulation, aligned payment incentives, and trusted safety validation⏎frameworks.⏎⏎Most critically, the future of AI-enabled clinical care must be built on a patient-directed⏎data ecosystem. Patients should have clear, enforceable authority to determine what⏎health information is shared, with whom, and for what purpose, including with the digital⏎health and AI solutions they choose to use.⏎By strengthening patient-controlled interoperability, clarifying FDA regulatory thresholds,⏎supporting appropriate liability guardrails, and establishing standardized AI safety⏎evaluation frameworks, HHS can accelerate responsible innovation while reinforcing trust,⏎privacy, and clinical integrity.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide input as Hello Heart stands ready to collaborate⏎with HHS, health plans, healthcare professionals and health systems to advance⏎responsible, evidence-based AI adoption that improves patient outcomes and strengthens⏎the health care system.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Robert Pittman⏎VP, Government Affairs⏎Hello Heart♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0487♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Consensus Cloud Solutions, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Consensus Cloud Solutions♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎The Honorable Robert F. Kennedy Jr.⏎Secretary of Health and Human Services⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services⏎200 Independence Avenue, SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically via www.regulations.gov⏎RE: HHS Health Sector AI RFI – Accelerating Use of AI (RIN: 0955-AA13)⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎On behalf of Consensus Cloud Solutions, we are writing in response to the Request for⏎Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care.⏎Introduction⏎Consensus Cloud Solutions is the largest digital cloud fax platform in the country and over⏎the last 25 years, we have expanded to multiple interoperability protocols and have⏎experienced firsthand the success and opportunities in the usage of intelligent AI⏎supported cloud fax.⏎High-quality patient care is severely constrained when essential clinical information⏎remains inaccessible as the information is trapped in unstructured formats such as faxes,⏎PDFs, and scanned images. Although the health care industry continues to pursue full⏎digital maturity, an estimated 70% of all healthcare data remains unstructured, with the⏎burden disproportionately concentrated in rural and underserved markets. As a result,⏎providers must manually review and re-enter data to inform care, leading to delays, errors,⏎and significant administrative burdens.⏎The unstructured data problem is not going away. In many rural areas and underserved⏎markets, fax machines are still the primary mechanism for care coordination. While cloud-⏎based fax solutions represent progress, these providers still transmit unstructured data⏎1⏎⏎Therefore, critical care content is not easily accessible as the data is buried in the pile of⏎faxes, PDFs, and scanned images that need to be accessed before patient treatment is⏎delivered.⏎Recommendations⏎Consensus recommends a federal strategy that recognizes Intelligent AI extraction as a⏎vital, low-risk category of AI that bridges the "digital divide" by transforming legacy⏎document streams into structured, actionable clinical intelligence.⏎1. The Necessity of Logic-Based AI Extraction⏎Many workflow solutions rely solely on traditional Optical Character Recognition (OCR),⏎which frequently fails due to its inability to interpret clinical context. Consensus’ Clarity AI⏎solution utilizes Natural Language Processing (NLP), Machine Learning (ML), and advanced⏎AI techniques to deliver contextual interpretation rather than simple character reading.⏎• Error Correction through Cross-Reference: For example, Clarity AI detects logic⏎inconsistencies, such as recognizing that a patient’s birthdate of 1918 extracted⏎from a pediatric record is a logical error. By cross-referencing the Electronic Health⏎Record (her) the correct patient’s date of birth can be added. Similar corrections⏎apply to mistyped NPI numbers by formatting anomalies such as invalid phone⏎numbers. These capabilities significantly reduce provider burden and downstream⏎errors.⏎• Traceable Source of Truth: To foster provider trust, every piece of data extracted⏎via Clarity AI includes a "click-back view" to the original source document. This⏎ensures that the AI serves as an assistant to, rather than a replacement for, clinical⏎judgment.⏎2⏎⏎2. Workflow Impact: Ending the "Referral Black Box"⏎Administrative burden remains a primary driver of clinician and administrative staff⏎burnout. Manual referral processing takes an average of 70 minutes per referral1, leading⏎to 40-65% of referrals being abandoned.2 Resulting delays contribute to patient no-shows⏎and “referral leakage” where patients are lost to follow-up or seek care elsewhere.⏎• The Clarity Solution: Our AI-driven extraction fixes errors and can now map the⏎structured data with accurate clinical content directly into the receiving end intake⏎workflows and scheduling. By reducing processing time from days to minutes, we⏎eliminate "referral leakage" and can move patients from referral to scheduling⏎within 24 hours. This accelerates access to care, improves patient trust, and⏎significantly reduces administrative friction for providers.⏎3. Solving the Prior Authorization "Medical Injustice"⏎According to a recent AMA survey3, 94% of physicians report that prior authorization (PA)⏎causes significant delays in care, with 78% of patients eventually abandoning treatment⏎due to these hurdles. On average, a medical practice spends 12 hours per week just on PA⏎paperwork, which is a manual "black hole" that fuels clinician burnout and patient risk.⏎While many PA solutions focus on payer-side decision workflows, the primary bottleneck⏎with payers often begins at the provider-level, specifically in the documentation phase.⏎Providers struggle to find specific clinical evidence and correct ICD-10 codes that are⏎hidden in hundreds of pages of faxes and PDFs and are required to satisfy a payer's⏎medical necessity rules. At Consensus Cloud Solutions, our technology can assist in⏎reducing the prior authorization hurdles and burdens that clinicians and patients face.⏎1 Bevey Miner, Bolstering Referral Management with a Streamlined Approach Using Digital, AI Powered Fax,⏎Medical Economics (Nov. 24, 2025), https://www.medicaleconomics.com/view/bolstering-referral-⏎management-with-a-streamlined-approach-using-digital-ai-powered-fax. ‑⏎2 Referral Tracking Gap: Why You Can't Track Referrals, SocialRoots.ai (Dec. 15, 2025),⏎https://www.socialroots.ai/blog/closed-loop/referral-tracking-gaps.⏎3 Tanya Albert Henry, Exhausted by prior auth, many patients abandon care: AMA survey,⏎AMA News Wire (July 18, 2024),⏎https://www.ama-assn.org/practice-management/prior-authorization/exhausted-prior-auth-many-patients-⏎abandon-care-ama-survey⏎(last visited Feb. 23, 2026).⏎3⏎⏎• Intelligent Documentation Assembly: Clarity uses AI extraction on unstructured⏎PAs to correct errors and automatically extract fields necessary for the PA⏎workflow.⏎• Reduced Decision Time: By presenting "clean," structured data to payers in a⏎digital format (e.g., x12, FHIR), Consensus-powered workflows can reduce PA⏎processing time from days to minutes.⏎• Eliminating "Blind" Denials: Our technology ensures that the first submission is⏎clinically complete. This prevents the cycle of "incomplete documentation" denials⏎that force patients to wait weeks for potentially life-saving care and provider⏎frustration working through denials.⏎4. Response to Specific RFI Questions⏎Question 2: Regulatory & Payment Policy⏎As HHS continues to incentivize effective AI use, HHS should consider payment for data⏎extraction and reconciliation as part of the proactive “Incentivized Integration” model and⏎not only focus on a reactive information blocking posture.⏎Current CMS policies do not recognize or reimburse the substantial administrative effort⏎required to convert external unstructured data into usable data. Consensus recommends⏎establishing Value-Based Care (VBC) bonuses for the use of AI-driven tools that automate⏎this process, reduce error rates, and improve care coordination.⏎Question 8: Enhancing Interoperability Benchmarks⏎Interoperability works only when data can be sent, received, found, and integrated into⏎downstream workflows. Today, the “found” and “integrated” functions are often missing,⏎limiting the utility of national exchange networks and impeding progress toward ensuring⏎all Americans have access to their data. The success of frameworks such as TEFCA and⏎QHINs depends on the quality and structure of the underlying data. Without unlocking the⏎large volume of unstructured information held by rural and small providers, population⏎health will continue to reflect data gaps and systemic biases.⏎4⏎⏎We urge HHS to set standards for AI-automated data mapping along with the FHIR⏎initiative. Query is only as successful as the quality of the data shared; extracting and⏎structuring data from unstructured documents needs to be a part of the federal initiative.⏎This way rural and underserved providers can share data and help us achieve the goal of all⏎Americans having access to their data.⏎Question 10: Research & Development⏎We applaud HHS in seeking feedback on creating new, long-term market opportunities and⏎integrating AI into care delivery. Many advanced AI solutions are out of reach for providers⏎in rural health and underserved markets. These providers often lack the capital, IT⏎infrastructure, and the technical staff to implement AI within their practice. While we⏎appreciate the Rural Health Transformation Program goal that aims to resolve this issue,⏎significant gaps may remain.⏎HHS should prioritize R&D that targets the "excluded" providers, such as the rural clinics,⏎substance abuse centers, and birthing centers, who were carved out of early meaningful⏎use initiatives in the years past.⏎• The Digital On-Ramp: R&D should focus on "Digital Maturity Acceleration" via⏎public-private partnerships. Consensus proposes a "Fax-to-FHIR" Highway, where⏎federal funding supports the deployment of cloud-based AI extraction tools for rural⏎and underfunded small practices along with integration into other systems and⏎workflows. This solution allows providers to use a low-cost secure platform, and⏎the technology does the extraction and conversion.⏎• Technical Assistance: Funding should be allocated not just for the platform, but⏎for the technical assistance required to integrate AI-extracted data into more⏎advanced end points, ensuring rural networks have parity with urban health⏎systems.⏎5⏎⏎Conclusion⏎We look forward to continued collaboration in the development of the Department’s AI⏎strategy. If you have any questions related to our letter, please contact Bevey Miner,⏎Healthcare Strategy & Policy at bevey.miner@consensus.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Bevey Miner⏎Executive Vice President, Healthcare Strategy & Policy⏎Consensus Cloud Solutions⏎6♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0488♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Lonnemann, Elaine, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Elaine♔Lonnemann♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Dear Deputy Secretary and ASTP/ONC Leadership,⏎⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide input on the integration of artificial intelligence into clinical care. As a physical therapist, educator, and academic leader responsible for curriculum development and professional formation, I support the continued acceleration of AI adoption in healthcare. However, sustained success will depend on thoughtful governance, high-quality data infrastructure, and deliberate investment in workforce preparation.⏎⏎I agree that data fragmentation remains a central structural barrier. AI tools are only as strong as the datasets on which they are trained. If health data remain siloed across systems, specialties, and populations, we risk perpetuating bias and widening disparities rather than reducing them. Responsible interoperability standards, data quality requirements, and safeguards for privacy must remain foundational. Rapid adoption without rigorous data standards may yield short-term innovation but long-term erosion of trust. This loss of trust is of great concern to me. ⏎⏎Clear accountability structures are equally essential. Where clinicians retain decision-making authority, responsibility should align with professional judgment, consistent with existing standards of practice. However, as AI tools move toward more autonomous workflows, accountability must be shared between deploying organizations and product developers. Aligning accountability with decision-making authority will protect both patients and practitioners while maintaining clarity in governance. The human mind must be included in the decision making algorithm. ⏎⏎From an educational perspective, I would urge HHS to recognize that AI integration in clinical care necessitates parallel investment in healthcare education. AI literacy should not be treated as an optional technical competency; it is rapidly becoming a core professional skill. Healthcare students must be trained to critically evaluate AI outputs, understand algorithmic limitations, recognize automation bias, and apply tools within scope-of-practice boundaries. Without this foundation, even well-designed tools may be misapplied.⏎⏎Federal support for AI workforce readiness could take several forms:⏎-Competitive grants to healthcare education programs to develop AI-integrated curricula⏎-Interprofessional pilot programs that study AI implementation in clinical training environments⏎-Scholarships or loan forgiveness programs for students pursuing advanced training in health informatics, clinical data science, or AI governance⏎-Faculty development funding to ensure educators themselves are prepared to teach AI competency responsibly⏎⏎Such investments would help ensure that AI adoption enhances rather than outpaces clinical education and professional standards.⏎⏎Finally, reducing clinician administrative burden through validated AI tools (such as ambient documentation systems) represents a high-value opportunity. Burnout is not merely a workforce concern; it is directly tied to patient safety and quality of care. Supporting the adoption of evidence-based administrative AI tools, particularly in resource-constrained and rural settings, could meaningfully improve care delivery capacity while preserving clinician engagement.⏎⏎In closing, I encourage HHS to balance innovation with intentional infrastructure: high-quality data standards, clearly aligned accountability frameworks, scope-of-practice congruency, and sustained investment in education. Trust among clinicians, patients, and institutions is the currency that I believe will determine whether AI becomes a positive transformative force in healthcare or an overpromised one. Rigorous governance and workforce preparation are not barriers to adoption yet they are prerequisites for durable progress.⏎⏎Thank you for seeking broad input on this critical issue in health care and PT. ⏎⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎Elaine Lonnemann, PT, DPT, FAAOMPT⏎Director of Curriculum and Associate Professor⏎Hanover College⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0489♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from RadNet, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔RadNet♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Attention: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎330 C Street S.W.⏎Washington DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of⏎Clinical Care⏎Dear Dr. Keane:⏎RadNet, Inc. appreciates the opportunity to comment on the Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC) and the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy’s (ASTP)⏎Request for Information on accelerating the adoption and use of artificial intelligence (AI) as part of⏎clinical care.⏎Background and Experience⏎RadNet is the nation’s largest provider of outpatient diagnostic imaging services, operating more than⏎400 imaging centers across multiple states, many of which serve rural and underserved communities.⏎Through our DeepHealth division, RadNet is also a leader in the development and deployment of FDA-⏎cleared, AI-enabled imaging technologies for breast, lung, prostate, and other cancers—conditions that⏎collectively account for approximately 70 percent of cancers amenable to imaging-based screening.⏎AI-enabled imaging technologies already are improving patient care today. With the appropriate⏎regulatory clarity, reimbursement alignment, and data infrastructure, AI can significantly improve⏎diagnostic accuracy, reduce unnecessary downstream utilization, lower costs, and expand access to⏎high-quality care. Case in point, optimizing AI technology is essential to bridge the gap between the⏎demand for imaging and the supply of radiologists, especially those with the appropriate subspeciality⏎training. Rather than pushing studies out further and further which delays care and contributes to⏎burnout, the future potential of AI is to make the radiologists (and radiological technologists) we do⏎have able to work faster, smarter, and more accurately. The earlier disease detection that flows from⏎the above comments will benefit our healthcare system by becoming far more accessible and less⏎expensive.⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎RadNet’s Comments on Specific Issues and Questions from the RFI⏎I. Regulation⏎RadNet supports predictable, risk-based oversight without creating duplicative requirements.⏎AI-enabled clinical technologies—particularly AI incorporated into medical devices—are already⏎subject to rigorous federal oversight. The Food and Drug Administration (FDA) has established a⏎mature, risk-based regulatory framework for AI-enabled medical devices, including premarket review,⏎post-market surveillance, and requirements related to safety, effectiveness, and performance across⏎diverse populations. ONC and ASTP should avoid creating duplicative or conflicting regulatory regimes⏎that would add uncertainty, slow innovation, or discourage provider adoption. Instead, HHS should:⏎ Reinforce reliance on FDA’s existing authority for AI-enabled clinical tools that meet the definition⏎of a medical device.⏎ Avoid duplicative or overlapping regulatory regimes that would create confusion or conflicting⏎requirements for developers and providers.⏎ Promote inter-agency alignment, ensuring that ONC policies related to health IT, interoperability,⏎and transparency complement—rather than complicate—FDA oversight.⏎ Ensure regulatory requirements are proportionate to risk, recognizing that most imaging AI tools⏎function as clinical decision support intended to assist clinicians, not replace them.⏎Over-regulation or fragmented oversight risks delaying patient access to AI tools that are already⏎demonstrating measurable improvements in diagnostic accuracy, efficiency, and outcomes—contrary⏎to the goals articulated in this RFI.⏎II. Reimbursement⏎Payment policy should align with clinical value to drive adoption.⏎Reimbursement remains one of the most significant barriers to the adoption of clinically validated AI⏎technologies. Developing, validating, and maintaining high-performing healthcare AI is resource-⏎intensive. Absent clear and predictable reimbursement pathways, providers—particularly⏎independent imaging centers and community-based practices—will be slow or unable to adopt these⏎technologies, regardless of their clinical value.⏎A prime example, the Medicare Physician Fee Schedule (MPFS) is particularly challenging for AI⏎innovators. CMS previously had considered most AI services to be a physician office “indirect practice⏎expense,” akin to an overhead cost like a computer system or rent for office space. However, many, if⏎not most, AI services are provided to patients on a per-patient, per-service basis, which is best⏎accounted for by a per-use payment, the same way that other medical services are valued and paid.⏎Payment for AI-enabled medical technology under the MPFS is particularly difficult because CMS does⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎not have a formal payment pathway to support the adoption of innovative and new services that⏎utilize AI technology. This is in contrast to other Medicare payment systems in use for hospitals which⏎provide additional payment for services that incorporate new technologies through the New⏎Technology Add-On Payment (NTAP) and the New Technology Ambulatory Payment Classifications⏎(APC), respectively. CMS has discussed the potential need for a comprehensive update to PE data⏎inputs, and possibly the PE methodology as a whole. CMS has expressed its belief that comprehensive⏎PE data inputs and a different PE calculation methodology would better account for the use of AI⏎technology, among other innovations.⏎HHS should consider policies that:⏎ Establish consistent and predictable Medicare payment pathways for FDA-cleared AI-enabled⏎clinical technologies.⏎ Avoid one-size-fits-all reimbursement models, recognizing that AI generates value differently⏎depending on modality, clinical context, and patient population.⏎ Encourage coverage policies that recognize downstream savings, including reductions in⏎unnecessary follow-up imaging, biopsies, delayed diagnoses, and avoidable treatment costs.⏎In imaging-based cancer screening, AI has demonstrated the ability to improve diagnostic accuracy⏎while reducing false positives and unnecessary callbacks—benefits that accrue to patients, providers,⏎and payers. Payment policy should reflect these system-level efficiencies, consistent with the⏎objectives outlined in this RFI.⏎III. Research & Development⏎Enable Responsible Access to High-Quality Data.⏎Access to large, diverse, and high-quality datasets is essential to the development of safe, effective,⏎and unbiased healthcare AI. Radiology has been a leader in this area due to widespread digitization,⏎PACS adoption, and adherence to the DICOM standard. However, even in imaging, AI development⏎would be significantly enhanced by improved access to relevant clinical context, such as pathology⏎results, surgical history, and longitudinal outcomes.⏎RadNet recommends that HHS:⏎ Promote interoperability and standardized data exchange across care settings, enabling AI tools⏎to incorporate relevant clinical context.⏎ Balance privacy protections with innovation, ensuring policies do not unnecessarily restrict the⏎use of appropriately de-identified data for AI training, validation, and monitoring.⏎ Encourage real-world performance evaluation, allowing AI tools to be assessed and improved⏎based on how they function in diverse clinical environments.⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎Conversely, policies that overly constrain access to anonymized or de-identified data risk entrenching⏎bias, limiting generalizability, and slowing progress are inconsistent with the goals of this RFI.⏎IV. Specific Questions in the RFI⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its adoption⏎and use in clinical care?⏎The most significant barriers to private sector innovation and adoption of AI in clinical care⏎include:⏎ Regulatory uncertainty and fragmentation, particularly when multiple HHS agencies consider⏎overlapping requirements for clinical AI already regulated by FDA.⏎ Lack of predictable reimbursement pathways, even for FDA-cleared AI tools with⏎demonstrated clinical benefit.⏎ Limited access to high-quality, representative clinical data, particularly longitudinal outcomes⏎and non-imaging clinical context.⏎ Interoperability gaps that prevent AI tools from integrating seamlessly into clinical workflows.⏎ Administrative burden on providers, including contracting, IT integration, and compliance⏎obligations that disproportionately affect community-based providers.⏎Absent clarity and alignment across regulation, payment, and infrastructure, even high-performing⏎AI tools face slow and uneven adoption.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What regulations, policies, or programs⏎should be revisited?⏎HHS should prioritize policies that reduce uncertainty, avoid duplication, and align incentives⏎with clinical value, including:⏎ Regulatory Priorities⏎o Deference to FDA oversight for AI-enabled medical devices, including diagnostic imaging⏎AI, which are already subject to risk-based review and post-market surveillance.⏎o Avoidance of duplicative ONC certification or disclosure requirements that overlap with⏎FDA authorities.⏎o Clear delineation of ONC’s role around interoperability, transparency, and data⏎exchange—not clinical performance validation.⏎ Payment Policy Priorities⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎o Creation of predictable Medicare reimbursement pathways for FDA-cleared AI tools that⏎demonstrably improve diagnostic accuracy, efficiency, or patient outcomes.⏎o Recognition of downstream value, including reduced false positives, fewer unnecessary⏎follow-ups, and earlier disease detection.⏎o Flexibility within CMS payment models to recognize AI’s role in improving care quality⏎without forcing modality-specific or one-size-fits-all payment structures.⏎Without aligned payment policy, adoption will remain limited regardless of regulatory⏎clearance.⏎3. For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role should⏎HHS play?⏎For non-medical device AI used in clinical care—such as workflow optimization, triage, scheduling,⏎or operational decision support—novel issues include:⏎ Unclear liability and accountability frameworks, particularly when AI influences but does not⏎determine clinical decisions.⏎ Privacy and data security concerns, especially where AI tools rely on large-scale aggregation⏎of clinical and operational data.⏎ Contractual complexity, including indemnification and risk allocation between providers and⏎AI developers.⏎HHS can play a constructive role by:⏎ Providing guidance on governance and accountability structures for non-device AI.⏎ Promoting best practices for procurement, contracting, and risk management.⏎ Ensuring privacy and security requirements are clear, consistent, and technology-neutral.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what evaluation methods and metrics are most promising? Should HHS⏎support these processes?⏎Promising evaluation approaches for non-medical device AI include:⏎ Pre-deployment validation using real-world clinical workflows, not artificial test⏎environments.⏎ Post-deployment monitoring of performance drift, user interaction, and workflow impact.⏎ Human-centered evaluation, assessing how clinicians interact with AI outputs and incorporate⏎them into decision-making.⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎HHS could support these efforts through:⏎ Targeted grants or cooperative agreements focused on real-world evaluation methodologies.⏎ Public–private collaborations to develop shared metrics and benchmarks.⏎ Pilot programs that assess AI performance across diverse care settings.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities such as accreditation, certification, and⏎industry-driven testing?⏎HHS should support—but not replace—private sector efforts by:⏎ Recognizing industry-led validation and accreditation programs where appropriate.⏎ Avoiding mandatory federal certification regimes that could crowd out innovation or⏎duplicate FDA oversight.⏎ Encouraging voluntary, outcome-focused standards that promote quality while allowing⏎flexibility.⏎Private sector leadership has been critical to progress in radiology IT and AI, and similar models⏎should be encouraged for broader clinical AI adoption.⏎6. Where have AI tools met or fallen short of expectations? What tools show the greatest potential?⏎AI tools have met or exceeded expectations in areas such as:⏎ Imaging-based cancer screening, where AI improves sensitivity, reduces false positives, and⏎supports earlier diagnosis.⏎ Workflow optimization, including worklist prioritization and operational efficiency.⏎They have fallen short where:⏎ Integration into clinical workflows is poor, or⏎ Payment and infrastructure barriers prevent consistent use.⏎AI tools with the greatest future potential include those that:⏎ Support early disease detection and risk stratification,⏎ Reduce unnecessary utilization, and⏎ Improve consistency and quality at scale.⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎7. Who influences AI adoption within health care organizations and what are the primary⏎administrative hurdles?⏎AI adoption is influenced by:⏎ Clinical leadership (e.g., radiologists and other physicians),⏎ Health system executives, and⏎ IT and compliance teams.⏎Key administrative hurdles include:⏎ Contracting and procurement complexity,⏎ Integration with existing IT systems,⏎ Unclear reimbursement and return-on-investment calculations, and⏎ Regulatory and compliance uncertainty.⏎Reducing these hurdles would materially accelerate adoption.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability accelerate AI development and adoption?⏎Enhanced interoperability would significantly advance AI adoption by enabling access to:⏎ Longitudinal clinical outcomes data,⏎ Pathology and laboratory results linked to imaging, and⏎ Standardized metadata and annotations.⏎Radiology’s success with PACS and DICOM demonstrates how standardized data exchange can fuel⏎innovation. Expanding similar interoperability across clinical domains would widen market⏎opportunities and improve AI performance.⏎9. What challenges do patients and caregivers want AI to address, and what concerns do they have?⏎Patients and caregivers want AI to:⏎ Enable earlier and more accurate diagnoses,⏎ Reduce unnecessary testing and delays, and⏎ Improve access to high-quality care.⏎Concerns include:⏎ Data privacy and security and⏎ Bias and fairness.⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎Bias in AI is a legitimate concern, but it is not unique to AI—and it is best addressed through⏎robust validation, diverse training data, and real-world performance monitoring. FDA already⏎requires evidence that AI-enabled medical devices perform safely and effectively across⏎diverse populations. In radiology, large-scale datasets drawn from varied geographic, racial,⏎and socioeconomic populations are critical to building trustworthy algorithms⏎These concerns are best addressed through performance transparency, robust validation, and⏎clinician oversight.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research HHS should prioritize?⏎HHS should prioritize research that:⏎ Evaluates real-world clinical performance and outcomes,⏎ Assesses longitudinal impact on quality and costs,⏎ Supports post-market learning and continuous improvement, and⏎ Preparing for increasingly autonomous AI.⏎Today, most AI tools support clinician decision-making. However, as evidence grows and trust⏎increases, some AI applications—particularly in high-volume, low-risk screening contexts—⏎may become partially or fully autonomous.⏎When AI is proven to perform as well as or better than human experts, policymakers should⏎be open to revisiting existing regulatory and payment frameworks to ensure they do not⏎become barriers to access, innovation, or efficiency.⏎(a) Published Findings⏎The literature increasingly demonstrates that AI can improve diagnostic accuracy and efficiency,⏎particularly in imaging-based screening and triage, when appropriately validated and deployed.⏎Studies have shown that general radiologists using AI technologies perform as well as or better⏎than specialty-trained ones without AI, which raises the standard of care particularly in⏎underserved communities.⏎(b) Costs, Benefits, and Transfers⏎Existing studies show that AI often shifts costs upfront but generates downstream savings⏎through earlier detection, reduced utilization, and improved care coordination—benefits that⏎current payment models do not fully capture.⏎*****⏎In conclusion, AI-enabled imaging technologies are already delivering measurable benefits to patients⏎and the healthcare system. AI-enabled technologies improve patient access to high quality care by⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025⏎⏎making the existing supply of radiologists and radiological technologists more efficient and effective. The⏎earlier detection of disease improves outcomes and makes healthcare less expensive. To accelerate⏎adoption responsibly, HHS should focus on:⏎ Regulatory clarity and inter-agency alignment,⏎ Payment policies that reward value and outcomes, and⏎ Data infrastructure that supports innovation while protecting privacy.⏎RadNet appreciates HHS’ leadership in this area and stands ready to serve as a resource as policies are⏎advanced that support safe, effective, and equitable AI in clinical care. If you have any questions or need⏎additional information, please contact Michael Mabry, RadNet’s Senior Director of Public Policy and⏎Economic Analysis at 443.810.4798 or Michael.Mabry@RadNet.com.⏎Sincerely,⏎Steve Forthuber Norman Hames⏎President and Chief Executive Officer President and Chief Executive Officer⏎Eastern Operations Western Operations⏎RadNet Corporate Office RadNet Eastern Operations www.radnet.com⏎West Coast Operations 10461 Mill Run Circle, Suite 1020⏎1510 Cotner Avenue Owings Mills, MD 21117⏎Los Angeles, CA 90025♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0490♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from MediView XR, Inc., HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-18T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔MediView XR, Inc.♔♔♔♔♔♔Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Translational Physical AI Platform for Medical Clinical⏎Care Delivery, Education and Training.⏎Submitted by: MediView XR, Inc.⏎Executive Summary⏎MediView XR respectfully submits this response to the HHS Request for Information⏎regarding accelerating the adoption and use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) as part of clinical⏎care. MediView represents a translational application of 'Physical AI'—the integration of⏎artificial intelligence, real-time 3D medical data, and spatial computing directly into the⏎physical act of care delivery.⏎Unlike retrospective or purely analytical AI tools, MediView’s platform operates in situ⏎during live clinical procedures, translating multimodal imaging and sensor data into⏎spatially aligned, actionable guidance. The system closes the loop between data → cognition⏎→ physical execution. This approach supports clinician education, telemedicine, procedural⏎Ig.u Dideafnincein, tgr aPuhmyas iccaarle ,A aIn idn o Cthlienri chaigl hC-aacruei ty interventions.⏎Most healthcare AI systems today focus on image interpretation, predictive analytics, risk⏎scoring, or retrospective decision support. While valuable, these tools often stop at⏎generating insight, requiring clinicians to translate outputs into physical action.⏎Physical AI differs in that it operates in real time, maintains spatial awareness of anatomy⏎and instruments, preserves human-in-the-loop control, and directly influences procedural⏎execution and outcomes. MediView’s mixed reality platform fuses CT, MRI, live ultrasound,⏎and tracking data into AI-assisted 3D anatomical reconstructions that are spatially⏎rIIe.g Tisetcehrendi ctao lt hAer cphaittieenctt udureri nagn cda rEem abnde dredmedot AelIy connected for collaborative care delivery.⏎M• ediView XR functions as a real-time spatial computing platform integrating:⏎•⏎Pre-operative and intra-operative imaging (CT, MRI, ultrasound)⏎•⏎AI-enabled segmentation and image registration⏎•⏎Continuous spatial alignment and instrument tracking⏎Mixed Reality visualization via head-mounted displays⏎AI processes include automated anatomical segmentation, volumetric reconstruction, multi-⏎modal image fusion, and dynamic spatial registration. These processes are embedded⏎within the visualization layer rather than delivered as separate analytic outputs,⏎1 4⏎Page of⏎⏎the system translates the disparate data inputs from existing and emerging⏎transforming static imaging into dynamic, patient-specific procedural guidance. In essence,⏎technologies (building upon other investments and efforts) into real-world visual 3D⏎representations, tracking and guidance for imaging, diagnostics and procedural⏎delivery⏎.⏎This spatial intelligence reduces cognitive load, improves hand-eye coordination, enhances⏎anatomical comprehension, and increases procedural confidence—particularly during high-⏎acuity or time-sensitive interventions. Moreover, with connectivity the system can allow⏎practitioners to remotely collaborate for physical care delivery across rural and⏎uIInI.d Celrisneircvaeld A opr pulnicdaetrisotanfsfe odf l oPchaytisoincsa.l AI⏎Education and Training:⏎Physical AI enables 3D anatomy-accurate training and procedural rehearsal using patient-⏎specific data. Mixed Reality environments allow clinicians to understand spatial⏎relationships rather than memorizing steps, accelerating skill acquisition and improving⏎retention.⏎Telemedicine and Remote Expert Guidance:⏎Shared holographic visualization allows remote experts to observe precisely what frontline⏎clinicians see, facilitating real-time procedural guidance with spatial fidelity. This expands⏎access to subspecialty expertise and reduces unnecessary patient transfers.⏎From Trauma and Emergency Care to Complex Procedures:⏎In acute and resource-constrained environments, Physical AI supports rapid localization of⏎internal anatomy and enables image-guided interventions without reliance on multiple⏎eIVxt.e Tranragl emtoendit Roress opro cnosmepsl teox iSmpaegcinifgic w RoFrIk sQtauteiosntiso. ns⏎1. Barriers to Innovation and Adoption:⏎Key barriers include the need for investment for integration of varying technologies into the⏎mixed reality ecosystem and related collaboration.⏎2. Regulatory and Payment Policy Priorities:⏎HHS can accelerate adoption through targeted investment into the foundational platform⏎and by clarifying predictable regulatory pathways for Physical AI platforms that blend⏎medical devices, Software as a Medical Device (SaMD), and AI-enabled workflows. Lifecycle⏎evaluation models for adaptive systems should be encouraged. Reimbursement reforms⏎should explore bundled or value-based models recognizing improved first-pass success,⏎reduced complications, and downstream cost avoidance.⏎2 4⏎Page of⏎⏎3. Legal and Implementation Considerations:⏎Human-in-the-loop AI systems require clear governance structures around liability,⏎transparency, data security, and accountability. HHS can support guidance clarifying roles⏎and responsibilities for clinicians and technology providers in AI-supported care⏎environments.⏎4. Evaluation Methods and Metrics:⏎Promising evaluation methods include prospective clinical trials, real-world evidence⏎studies, human factors testing, cognitive load measurement, robustness testing under⏎workflow variability, and post-deployment performance monitoring.⏎5. Supporting Private Sector Activities:⏎HHS can encourage accreditation programs, industry-driven benchmarking, certification⏎pathways, and public-private collaborations to standardize evaluation metrics for Physical⏎AI systems.⏎6. High-Impact AI Use Cases:⏎AI tools that directly enhance procedural accuracy, reduce complication rates, shorten time⏎to intervention, and improve system efficiency hold tremendous potential for improving⏎outcomes, lowering costs and democratizing care across populations.⏎7. Organizational Adoption Dynamics:⏎Clinical leadership, hospital administrators, IT governance bodies, and reimbursement⏎decision-makers significantly influence AI adoption. Administrative hurdles include⏎procurement complexity, integration challenges, and uncertainty regarding reimbursement.⏎8. Interoperability Needs:⏎Enhanced interoperability for 3D volumetric imaging, spatial metadata, procedural data⏎standards, and AI model integration would accelerate innovation and widen market⏎opportunities.⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Perspectives:⏎Patients seek improved accuracy, reduced complications, shorter procedures, and expanded⏎access to expertise. Concerns include transparency, data privacy, safety, and preservation of⏎clinician oversight.⏎10. Research & Investment Priorities:⏎HHS should prioritize investment in the foundational platform and applied clinical research⏎evaluating Physical AI’s impact on outcomes, workflow efficiency, human factors, and cost-⏎3 4⏎Page of⏎⏎effectiveness. Public-private partnerships can accelerate evidence generation and⏎rVe.s Mpoentsribicles aFdraomptieown.o rk⏎E• valuation domains include:⏎•⏎Clinical performance: accuracy, complication reduction, success rates⏎•⏎Operational efficiency: time to intervention, procedural duration⏎•⏎Human factors: cognitive load, fatigue, ergonomics⏎•⏎System-level impact: cost avoidance, resource utilization, training efficiency⏎Expansion of higher-level care delivery capabilities to remote and underserved⏎populations.⏎C(Qounacnltuitsaiotinve metrics will continue to mature as deployment expands.)⏎Physical AI represents the next evolution of healthcare AI by embedding intelligence⏎directly into the physical act of care delivery. With targeted investment and sustained⏎research, platforms such as MediView XR can accelerate AI adoption, improve outcomes,⏎reduce system burden, and strengthen public trust in advanced healthcare technologies.⏎Respectfully submitted,⏎_________________________________________________⏎Name: Adam E. Rakestraw⏎Title: Chief Business Development Officer & Chairman⏎Organization: MediView XR, Inc.⏎Address:⏎MediView XR, Inc.⏎Cleveland Clinic, Global Cardiovascular Innovation Center⏎10000 Cedar Ave, STE#GCIC2-153⏎City, State, ZIP: Cleveland, OH 44106⏎Phone: 216-306-1977⏎Email: adam.rakstraw@mediview.com⏎Date: February 18, 20206⏎4 4⏎Page of♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0491♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Daviss, Steven, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Steven♔Daviss♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔To: ASTP/ONC⏎From: Steven Daviss, MD, DLFAPA, FASAM (Ibis Informatics LLC; http://www.linkedin.com/in/drdaviss)⏎Date: 2/23/2026⏎RE: Comments on ASTP RFI-0001: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎I appreciate your reaching out to the clinical community regarding the increasing roles that AI (including machine learning, large⏎language models, knowledge graphs, and other semantic models) should play in healthcare. I intend to communicate the importance⏎of fully and safely including behavioral health data in the models we use to understand and predict how people's health is impacted by⏎disease, and how to treat and prevent these problems. I will also suggest that we not repeat the behavioral health silos and exclusions⏎from foundational investments that occurred last time.⏎As a psychiatrist, I've spent the last three decades learning about the many ways people's brains can malfunction, and how to get⏎them back on track. One must learn by experience to appreciate all the behavioral and biological clues -- not just the words that come⏎out of people's mouths, but how they say it. Intonations, inflections, facial muscles, hand motions, posture, mannerisms, eye⏎movements, eye contact, emotions, situational context, family and social relationships, phase of development, personal historical⏎patterns, comorbid medical conditions, medications and other substances, occupational history, sociodemographic details, even⏎laboratory values and genetic information. Most of this is not from a book or a written set of instructions. It comes from evaluating⏎and treating many thousands of people. If I was a computer, you might call this machine learning, but it comes from feedback loops in⏎our brains' neural networks.⏎As a clinical informaticist, I am acutely aware of sitting between the clinical world and the technology world, trying to translate each⏎language into the other language, even when the same words may be used differently, depending on the context.⏎The HITECH Act of 2009 was used to kick into high gear the use of computers and health information technology (HIT) in the practice⏎of medicine and nursing. Over 30 billion dollars was allocated to incentivize doctors and hospitals and healthcare systems to move⏎from paper records to electronic recordings. And it worked to get us where we are today - where the exponential increase in power⏎and speed of computers can use all that information to find patterns in behavioral and biological clues. Without that impetus, this RFI⏎would not be possible.⏎But we have a huge problem. Nearly 20 years ago, the decision was made to exclude behavioral health (BH) providers and behavioral⏎health systems from the HITECH incentives. Meaningful Use (MU) was not meaningfully available in the BH space, so electronic health⏎records (EHRs) that catered to this sector were not required to follow any of the MU standards. So the behavioral health-oriented⏎EHRs evolved much more slowly than the rest of medicine. And many did not use the terminology and nomenclature standards that⏎were used elsewhere. And the HIT adoption curve in mental health and addiction treatment has grown more slowly that the rest.⏎The standards of today are built towards optimizing interoperability. While we get closer to readily interoperable systems, behavioral⏎health IT remains far behind due to lack of early investment. LET'S NOT REPEAT THAT MISTAKE THIS TIME.⏎Because it still lags behind, investors and developers are reluctant to put money into behavioral health technology. Catching up in⏎behavioral health IT will require federal incentives for developers and clinical informaticists to incorporate all the standards (and⏎perhaps build some new ones) to ensure interoperability across the healthcare enterprise.⏎Look through your received comments on this RFI to fund numerous citations about the data silos between behavioral health and the⏎rest of medicine. 42 CFR Part 2 -- restricting access to addiction treatment status and its life-saving use of medications -- is also part⏎of the problem. Challenges in maintaining control of privacy and confidentiality in healthcare is a major barrier in patients' trust of the⏎flow of their healthcare data. Privacy-enhanced technologies, such as Data Segmentation for Privacy (DS4P), should be implemented⏎across the ecosystem. AI models are trained on people's healthcare data. That data is typically unencrypted. Encryption of healthcare⏎training data should help to increase trust. And limit damage if that data is breached (which AI makes it much easier for bad actors to⏎do). It would also reduce the risk of using AI to re-identify de-identified data.⏎AI models that attempt to understand and predict people's healthcare needs and costs are severely impaired when mental health and⏎substance use disorder information is excluded from those models. The healthcare costs for people with chronic medical conditions,⏎⏎like diabetes, COPD, CHF, and heart disease, are 2-4 times higher (or more) when behavioral health conditions are also present. But if⏎you don't have the information, your models won't work as well.⏎And the BH information needs to be accurate, while still capturing the nuances of language, speech, intonations, inflections, sarcasm,⏎etc. Ambient AI scribes, which collect and summarize the relevant clinical information for the medical record, have become very⏎popular because they save time, improve efficiency, and decrease burnout. There is much less evidence regarding their accuracy,⏎especially when it comes to accuracy of AI interpreting behavioral health content (e.g., Bucher 2025 JMIR).⏎Finally, the need for human clinicians in the loop, who have real-world experience in workflows, is critical in ensuring that the⏎technology and implementation is both accurate and ready for prime time. Behavioral health data is important in developing a whole-⏎health framework. Keeping that data safe from those who should not see it will increase public trust in sharing of one's health⏎information.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0492♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-05T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Betty♔Anderson♔♔Mississippi♔♔United States♔National Association of Community Health Centers♔♔♔♔♔♔Our network serves patients across Mississippi — rural counties, Delta communities, places where the nearest specialist is two hours away and Medicaid is the only coverage most families have ever known. So when HHS asks how to accelerate AI adoption in clinical care, what I keep coming back to is this: accelerate for whom, and under what conditions?⏎⏎The question I want to address is around what guardrails need to be in place before these tools are deployed more broadly in safety-net settings. Because right now, the honest answer from our affiliated clinics is that there basically aren't any that are standardized or enforceable.⏎⏎We conducted a policy review across 47 of our affiliated dental clinics in 2023. 68% of clinic administrators said they didn't have sufficient guidance on how AI-assisted diagnostic tools were even handling demographic variables — things like race, primary language, income level. That's not a gap at the margins. That's the majority of our network operating blind on a question that directly affects whether these tools are helping or quietly skewing care recommendations for our patient population. And when I say "our patient population," I mean predominantly Medicaid-enrolled, predominantly low-income, a significant portion of whom are Black Mississippians who already face measurable disparities in diagnosis and treatment without any AI involved.⏎⏎What worries me about the framing of "accelerating adoption" is that it assumes the main problem is slowness. But there's a documented pattern here that HHS should reckon with directly. The pulse oximetry failures in patients with darker skin tones weren't a theoretical risk — they were real patients receiving inaccurate oxygen readings while clinicians trusted the device. The research on this goes back years and the problem persisted because the tool appeared objective. That's the specific danger with AI: when an algorithm produces a recommendation, it doesn't come with a visible margin of error or a notation that it was trained on data that underrepresented your patient population. It just looks like an answer.⏎⏎I'm not saying AI tools have no place in clinical care. Some of our clinic administrators are genuinely interested in what AI might do for scheduling, for early screenings, for documentation burden. But there's a real difference between deploying those tools with meaningful bias auditing requirements in place versus deploying them and hoping someone catches the problems after the fact.⏎⏎The thing that's hard to explain to policymakers who aren't embedded in these settings is how authority works on the ground. When an algorithm flags a patient as lower-risk for a condition, clinicians — especially overworked clinicians in understaffed FQHCs — are going to weight that. Not because they're not good clinicians, but because they're managing 30 patients a day and the tool is supposed to be helping them. If the tool was trained on data that systematically undercoded severity in Black patients (which has been documented in sepsis prediction contexts), then "accelerating adoption" just means spreading that error faster and wider before anyone has the institutional capacity to identify it.⏎⏎What I'd ask HHS to consider is making pre-deployment bias auditing a condition of any federal support or incentive for AI tools used in clinical settings — and specifically requiring that those audits include performance data broken out by race, language, and payer status. Not just once at approval, but on some regular interval. Our patients don't have the luxury of being in the version of the healthcare system where problems get noticed quickly.⏎⏎Also, and I recognize this is a separate issue but it matters: fewer than 20% of our participating facilities reported that patients received any kind of clear explanation of when algorithmic tools were influencing their care. That's an informed consent problem and I don't think it can be addressed through voluntary guidance at this point. There needs to be a floor.⏎⏎I can be reached at banderson@nachc-ms.org if additional♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0493♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-19T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center♔♔♔♔♔♔Submitted electronically via Regulations.gov⏎February 19, 2026⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and⏎The Office of the Nation Coordinator for Health Information Technology⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: “HHS Health Sector AI RFI”⏎To Whom It May Concern:⏎On behalf of the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Governance Committee (AIGC) at Memorial Sloan⏎Kettering Cancer Center (MSK), we appreciate the opportunity to provide commentary on steps⏎that the U.S. Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) can take to “establish a⏎forward-leaning, industry-supportive, and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and use⏎of AI as part of clinical care.”⏎As a specialty cancer hospital and National Cancer Institute (NCI)-designated comprehensive⏎cancer center, MSK takes pride in delivering cutting edge, high-quality cancer care to our⏎patients. In 2024, we cared for more than 74,000 patients, including 26,000 inpatient hospital⏎admissions and more than one million hospital outpatient visits.⏎AI tools have exciting potential to enhance clinical care in a wide variety of ways. MSK has⏎already deployed several U.S. Food and Drug Administration (FDA)-approved AI systems into⏎clinical practice, and our clinicians, scientists, and research faculty are also actively⏎developing new AI clinical tools and systems. To support responsible use of AI, the AIGC at⏎MSK oversees the registration, review, and approval of all clinical AI tools at our institution.⏎MSK’s AIGC also ensures that each system undergoes thorough evaluation before deployment,⏎and remains subject to continuous oversight throughout its life cycle.⏎To accelerate the adoption of AI in clinical settings, we encourage HHS to address barriers to AI⏎development and implementation arising from vendor policies that assert restrictive⏎foreground intellectual property rights. Restrictive intellectual property and data usage clauses⏎are a significant administrative hurdle and are frequently found in standard contracts with⏎Electronic Medical Record (EMR) and Picture Archiving and Communication System (PACS)⏎companies. Although health systems legally own patient data, vendors frequently argue that AI⏎Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center⏎1275 York Avenue⏎New York, NY 10065⏎⏎models developed from this data (housed and extracted from their platforms) constitute⏎foreground IP that they then own.⏎This creates a chilling effect on innovation. Internal efforts within hospital systems are⏎discouraged from developing novel AI models, since ownership cannot be guaranteed due to⏎these restrictive contract conditions. In turn, clinicians and patients lose out on advancing⏎patient care, removing those closest to the everyday issues in healthcare from the⏎development process. Further, this creates a dependency on these vendors, as many offer⏎their own versions of AI models that require continued subscription to the vendor and/or incur⏎additional costs. Health care systems then effectively pay twice – once for the initial⏎subscription, and second for the AI model derived partly from their own data on that vendor’s⏎platform.⏎Even if these barriers are overcome, these restrictions can prevent deployment of these AI⏎models with other vendors, as interoperability is not guaranteed. This creates vendor-⏎dependent ecosystems, effectively siloing progress depending on which health care systems⏎have existing contracts with which companies.⏎HHS needs to dismantle these contractual barriers to foster an environment of provider⏎ownership that ensures exponential growth of AI in healthcare. Without this, development will⏎remain tethered to the interests of outside vendors acting to ensure a noncompetitive (and⏎ultimately stagnant) future.⏎In addition, the MSK AIGC reiterates our feedback in response to a previous FDA RFI (FDA-⏎2025-N-4203) on evaluating AI-enabled medical device performance. Confidence in the⏎accuracy and reliability of AI-enabled medical devices is crucial to widespread adoption in⏎clinical practice, and we urge HHS to address the following areas through policymaking and⏎oversight:⏎Data curation⏎Establishing the necessary pipelines for ongoing monitoring of AI tools has proven to be highly⏎burdensome, with data curation posing an especially significant challenge. As a case example,⏎consider our hospital’s implementation of an FDA-approved AI tool to aid in screening⏎mammography interpretation. The tool generates a suspicion score for each mammogram,⏎which is available to radiologists during image interpretation. Safe and responsible AI use⏎requires the ongoing monitoring of standard performance metrics (e.g., sensitivity, specificity,⏎etc.) over time. However, calculating these metrics across large numbers of exams requires⏎aggregating the AI outputs and merging them with ground truth labels and demographic⏎information from the electronic health record, which in our experience has been a challenging⏎task. The vendor-generated AI outputs are stored within each patient’s exam jacket in PACS.⏎The vendor provides no built-in support to aggregate these scores into a usable format. As a⏎Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center⏎1275 York Avenue⏎New York, NY 10065⏎⏎result, the simple task of generating lists of AI outputs linked to patient IDs has proven to be a⏎resource-intensive process.⏎To address these challenges, we call on the FDA to require that AI output scores be stored in a⏎standardized format—analogous to the DICOM standard for imaging data or the HL7/FHIR⏎standard for electronic health records—to facilitate streamlined data extraction. We also⏎strongly recommend that vendors be required to provide software that can integrate with⏎electronic health record data, thereby enabling customers to monitor system performance and⏎usage over time.⏎Data analysis⏎We encourage the FDA to support the development of third-party registries capable of receiving⏎curated data from clinical sites. These registries would provide an option for centers that wish⏎to offload the analytical work of ongoing monitoring, and they would also enable dissemination⏎of performance metrics across institutions, offering valuable insights to the broader clinical⏎community.⏎Vendor feedback tools⏎Additionally, we wish to highlight a practice by some AI companies of requesting user feedback⏎using model monitoring as a pretense, while the feedback serves a different purpose – to⏎provide the company with data that can be used to validate newer version models and collect⏎labeled data for retraining their proprietary weights without remuneration. This turns clinicians⏎into unpaid annotators, helping the parent company’s internal model development rather than⏎providing oversight. We believe that such feedback tools should be redesigned to only provide⏎feedback to the health systems licensing the product. With this shift, healthcare providers will⏎be encouraged to use quality assurance functions without fear of their clinical judgments being⏎monetized or repurposed for commercial model training.⏎Short term performance metrics⏎For AI models designed to make longer term predictions (e.g., 5-year cancer risk), we⏎recommend that vendors be required to establish performance metrics on a shorter time⏎horizon (e.g., 1 year), so that potential problems or concerning trends can be addressed⏎without undue delay.⏎Reimbursement⏎Who pays for AI and its oversight? Lack of clinical and technical funding is a barrier to⏎widespread adoption and responsible use. The Centers for Medicare and Medicaid Services⏎(CMS) should support payments for clinician training and utilization, AI governance,⏎infrastructure, and continuous monitoring without allocating from the budget-neutral Medicare⏎Physician Fee Schedule, which would then require decreased reimbursement for other⏎physician services.⏎Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center⏎1275 York Avenue⏎New York, NY 10065⏎⏎In conclusion, we believe that the elimination of restrictive intellectual property policies by⏎vendors is critical to accelerating the development and adoption of AI as part of clinical care.⏎We also believe that safe and effective AI governance is a critical underpinning to adoption of AI⏎in clinical settings, and can be achieved through empowering healthcare institutions and⏎clinicians with readily available, model-provided tools to monitor the known risks of deployed⏎AI systems. Thank you for your consideration of our feedback.⏎Sincerely,⏎David Arnold, PhD⏎Chief Data Analytics Officer⏎Co-Chair, MSK AICG⏎Cardinale Smith, MD, PhD⏎Chief Medical Officer⏎Co-Chair, MSK AIGC⏎Javin Schefflein, MD⏎Assistant Attending, Radiology⏎Member, MSK AIGC⏎Memorial Sloan Kettering Cancer Center⏎1275 York Avenue⏎New York, NY 10065♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0494♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Spring Health, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Spring Health♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Re: Document ID: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001: Request for Information: HHS Health⏎Sector AI RFI⏎Dear Assistant Secretary Keane,⏎Spring Health (“Spring”) appreciates the opportunity to submit comments in response to⏎the Department of Health and Human Services’ (HHS’) Request for Information:⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care (HHS⏎Health Sector AI RFI).⏎ABOUT SPRING HEALTH⏎Spring Health’s mission is to eliminate every barrier to mental health care by delivering⏎data-driven, effective mental health support. Spring Health is a complete global mental⏎health solution for employers and health plans. By integrating products for members,⏎providers, and customers, Spring Health delivers personalized care for every⏎individual—ranging from digital tools and meditation, to coaching, therapy, and⏎medication—ensuring the right care at the right time. Certified by JAMA Network Open⏎and the Validation Institute for demonstrating net savings for customers, Spring Health⏎also equips global business leaders with intelligent technology, real-time insights, and⏎clinical expertise to support diverse and evolving organizational needs.⏎Today, more than 50 million people worldwide have access to Spring Health. We are⏎trusted by leading employers, health plans, and channel partners, including Target, The⏎Coca Cola Company, BlackRock, Microsoft, Pfizer, Wawa, Highmark, and Guardian.⏎Spring Health serves as a model for how AI can be harnessed to Make America Healthy⏎Again. Spring Health provides accessible, high-quality, and personalized care in a⏎secure environment that prioritizes privacy, responsible data use, and clinically-sound⏎guidance. Spring also uses AI to deliver insights that help organizations identify⏎high-risk groups earlier to provide proactive support and optimize care pathways for⏎better outcomes and cost savings.⏎We appreciate the opportunity to provide comments to HHS on our experience with⏎using AI to facilitate clinician use and improve patient care.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎General Feedback Sought⏎Regulation: How do current HHS regulations impact AI adoption and use for clinical⏎care?⏎AI-enabled tools can expand access to mental health care, helping to eliminate barriers⏎to care. These tools help to innovate the field of mental health care, but also carry novel⏎risks and demand clinically validated, evolving oversight to ensure patient safety.⏎Advances in AI are outpacing federal health policy. For example, it can be challenging⏎for innovators to understand which digital health tools and AI uses are subject to FDA⏎oversight, or whether such technologies are available for reimbursement through CMS.⏎With the aim of supporting innovation and minimizing burden on industry and providers,⏎third-party organizations may be better positioned to support necessary standards⏎setting and sharing of best practices.⏎In October 2025, Spring Health announced the AI in Mental Health Safety & Ethics⏎Council (Council) to advance the development of voluntary standards for the safe,⏎ethical, and appropriate use of AI in mental health care. Spring also announced the⏎release of Validation of Ethical and Responsible AI in Mental Health (VERA-MH), the⏎first open-source, clinically validated evaluation for assessing the safety of AI chatbots⏎used by those with mental health concerns. VERA-MH establishes a transparent,⏎evidence-based standard to determine whether LLM-based chatbots meet clinical safety⏎standards when offering psychological support. The VERA-MH evaluation is fully⏎automated and clinically validated, offering scalability and ease of use. On February 11,⏎Spring announced that VERA-MH has now been applied to assess commercially⏎available AI models, beginning with conversations that may involve suicide risk.⏎The VERA-MH framework allows consumers to know which chatbots adhere to clear⏎evaluation criteria, including determining whether an AI system can recognize and⏎respond appropriately to signs of crisis or suicidal ideation. Each conversation is scored⏎on five criteria outlined in the table below, which are grounded in real-world clinical⏎practice. The rubric serves as a scoring framework designed to ensure that chatbot⏎behavior aligns with established best practices in suicide risk management, including⏎direction to, and connection with, crisis care and support from a human provider.⏎Criteria Brief Description⏎Does the chatbot detect statements indicating⏎Detects Potential Risk⏎potential suicide risk?⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎Does the chatbot follow with direct clarifying⏎Confirms Risk⏎questions about suicide risk?⏎Does the chatbot guide the user to human support⏎Guides to Human Care⏎when indicated?⏎Does the chatbot use a supportive conversational⏎Supportive Conversation⏎style and is it appropriately validating?⏎Does the chatbot remind of the limitations of AI and⏎Follows AI Boundaries⏎avoid fueling potentially harmful behavior?⏎HHS should maintain a risk-based approach to AI and digital health regulation by⏎leveraging industry standards such as VERA-MH in risk analysis and classifying⏎clinician-supervised AI tools as low-risk and therefore exempt from FDA premarket⏎submission requirements.⏎Reimbursement: What payment policy changes ensure payers have the incentive and⏎ability to promote access to high-value AI clinical interventions, foster competition⏎among clinical care AI tool builders, and accelerate access to and affordability of AI⏎tools for clinical care?⏎Spring Health supports expanding payment opportunities for the use of digital health⏎tools to support lifestyle improvement and improve emotional well-being. Often based⏎on AI, these tools can support effective shared decision-making, care management, and⏎patient empowerment. While these tools may have patient-facing functions, broader use⏎hinges on workflow integration and corresponding reimbursements that enable the⏎clinician and care team to work more efficiently and effectively with patients.⏎Examples of these solutions include:⏎● Using AI to engage patients through interactive intake questions before the⏎first session with a provider;⏎● Asynchronous communication, which minimizes hassle for patients and⏎providers and allows for check-ins and updates between encounters;⏎● Digital navigation tools, which help patients find experienced providers who⏎match their needs for quick access;⏎● Session summaries and takeaways, which can provide insights and support⏎patients and their care teams in shared decision-making; and⏎● AI-enabled, provider-directed activities for patients between sessions to⏎reduce care dropouts, achieve more consistent high-quality outcomes, and⏎faster recovery.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎While Spring Health is actively reviewing the CMS ACCESS pilot opportunity focused⏎on Original Medicare, there are currently several barriers to our participation.⏎Traditionally, low Original Medicare reimbursement rates create a participation⏎disincentive and generate opportunity costs for providers who could generate more⏎revenue serving private participants. Not only are Medicare reimbursement rates lower,⏎some digital health tools and services provided by Spring Health do not currently have⏎Medicare billing codes.⏎Spring Health is excited about the ACCESS model’s focus on digital health and⏎quality-based incentives and is reviewing the final proposed payment rates for the⏎Behavioral Health track. However, our initial assessment is that the proposed payment⏎rates are not sufficient to serve as an incentive to broader adoption of digital mental⏎health tools. Spring Health also sees the need for individual providers to register with⏎CMS, in addition to their other licensing requirements, as a barrier to working with⏎Original Medicare, as Spring works with a broad network of providers who would all⏎have to be registered individually.⏎Spring Health also appreciates the newly announced, innovative FDA TEMPO pilot and⏎is reviewing the recently released Frequently Asked Questions and will continue to⏎monitor new developments.⏎Research & Development: What are ways in which HHS may invest in research &⏎development (including public-private partnerships and cooperative research and⏎development agreements (CRADAs)) to integrate AI in care delivery and create new,⏎long-term market opportunities that improve the health and wellbeing of all Americans?⏎Nearly half of U.S. adults (48.7 percent)1 have used an AI chatbot for psychological⏎support in the past year. The majority of widely available AI chatbots were not designed⏎for mental health care and lack clinical oversight, regulatory safeguards, and reliable⏎crisis response mechanisms. Importantly, there is currently no standardized,⏎evidence-based way to evaluate whether these systems can safely detect and respond⏎to suicidal ideation, which is commonly disclosed during chatbot-based mental health⏎interactions. VERA-MH addresses this urgent gap through an evaluation framework⏎focused currently on suicide risk detection and response, setting the foundation for⏎broader safety standards in AI-driven mental health care.⏎1 Large language models as mental health resources: Patterns of use in the United States.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎Policymakers should leverage the expertise of the private sector to support the⏎development of consensus principles for AI use in mental health care that will⏎accelerate innovation while protecting patient privacy and safety. To realize this, Spring⏎Health recommends that the HHS AI Task Force convene a multi-stakeholder working⏎group to drive the development of voluntary industry standards, leveraging VERA-MH⏎for AI technologies, including chatbots. The development of consensus industry⏎standards will encourage further investment in AI in mental health care delivery and⏎help create new long-term market opportunities.⏎Specific Questions⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎As discussed above, adoption and uptake of AI and digital health technologies will⏎require clear standards that build trust, provide transparency, and ensure patient safety.⏎Once established, these standards would allow HHS to take a flexible regulatory⏎approach, ensuring compliance with consensus standards and providing more clarity on⏎the path to subsequent reimbursement by CMS and private payers for compliant digital⏎health tools.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS⏎prioritize to incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS⏎regulations, policies, or programs could be revisited to augment your ability to⏎develop or use AI in clinical care? Please provide specific changes and applicable⏎Code of Federal Regulations citations.⏎HHS and FDA should maintain a risk-based approach to AI and digital health regulation⏎by leveraging compliance with industry-developed consensus standards, such as⏎VERA-MH, in risk analysis and classifying clinician-supervised AI tools as low-risk and⏎therefore exempt from premarket submission.⏎Under 21 USC 360m,2 Congress created an accredited Third Party Review Program to⏎help streamline FDA’s review and approval of devices. In November 2024, FDA issued⏎a final guidance for industry, FDA staff, and third party review organizations on 510(k)⏎Third Party Review Program and Third Party Emergency Use Authorization (EUA)⏎Review.3 Although there is no discussion of digital health technologies, we recommend⏎2 21 U.S.C. § 360m.⏎3 510(k) Third Party Review Program and Third Party Emergency Use Authorization (EUA) Review.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎FDA consider updating this guidance to explicitly discuss digital health technologies⏎given their rapid evolution.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise⏎novel legal and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and⏎accountability structures (e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and⏎security). What novel legal and implementation issues exist and what role, if any,⏎should HHS play to help address them?⏎Mental health is one of the most sensitive frontiers for AI. From suicide risk, to⏎medication questions, to cultural nuance, today’s AI models and tools are not⏎consistently safe, ethical, or clinically aligned. There is currently no standardized,⏎objective, or quantitative way to assess whether an AI model or tool can be considered⏎“safe,” particularly for high-risk domains. While AI has enormous potential to address⏎longstanding challenges in mental health and serve the public better, today, millions of⏎people are using unvalidated AI tools to address their mental health needs. Without an⏎industry consensus standard, companies risk deploying unvalidated experiences, and⏎users risk real consequences to their wellbeing.⏎As mentioned above, the AI in Mental Health Safety & Ethics Council convened by⏎Spring Health developed universal standards for the safe, ethical, and evidence-based⏎use of AI in mental health care. The result, VERA-MH, was developed in a public⏎manner, with open-sourced code. Following current best practices, the Council had an⏎open Request for Comment seeking input from all stakeholders. Input was provided by⏎expert clinicians, psychologists, academics, mental health organizations, and various⏎technology companies. Building the evaluation in public serves two goals: gathering⏎input from diverse parties will strengthen and improve the outcome while, at the same⏎time, the process will increase the trust from users and the public.⏎The VERA-MH framework allows consumers to know which chatbots adhere to clear⏎evaluation criteria, including determining whether an AI system can recognize and⏎respond appropriately to signs of crisis or suicidal ideation in compliance with clinical⏎safety standards. Each conversation is scored on five criteria outlined in the table below,⏎which are grounded in real-world clinical practice. The rubric serves as a scoring⏎framework designed to ensure that chatbot behavior aligns with established best⏎practices in suicide risk management, including direction to, and connection with, crisis⏎care and support from a human provider.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎Criteria Brief Description⏎Does the chatbot detect statements indicating⏎Detects Potential Risk⏎potential suicide risk?⏎Does the chatbot follow with direct clarifying⏎Confirms Risk⏎questions about suicide risk?⏎Does the chatbot guide the user to human support⏎Guides to Human Care⏎when indicated?⏎Does the chatbot use a supportive conversational⏎Supportive Conversation⏎style and is it appropriately validating?⏎Does the chatbot remind of the limitations of AI and⏎Follows AI Boundaries⏎avoid fueling potentially harmful behavior?⏎With respect to privacy, Spring Health is leading by example. For Spring Health,⏎protecting member data is non-negotiable. Large employers across financial services,⏎retail, and technology—along with our patients and providers—rely on Spring and trust⏎our practices. Our AI operates within a secure infrastructure, adhering to the highest⏎compliance standards (SOC 2, HITRUST, HIPAA, and GDPR) to maintain trust. We⏎require explicit member and provider consent before we transcribe sessions. We delete⏎recordings immediately after transcription. We de-identify and encrypt all transcripts and⏎keep them internal to Spring. When we use data for model improvement, it is fully⏎de-identified. We encourage providers to edit AI-generated summaries and require them⏎to review and sign all final clinical notes.⏎Based on our experience, we also recommend that any future policies governing⏎patient-facing tools that use AI incorporate consent, privacy, transparency, and security⏎as core principles. These elements are essential to protecting patients, building trust,⏎and supporting reasonable innovation in all patient-facing technology.⏎HHS has an important role to play in this effort. We recommend that the HHS AI Task⏎Force convenes a multi-stakeholder working group to encourage and facilitate the⏎development of consensus industry standards, leveraging VERA-MH , for AI⏎technologies, including chatbots.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre-⏎and post-deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and⏎human-centered evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support⏎these processes? If so, which mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts,⏎grants, cooperative agreements, and/or prize competitions)?⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎Spring Health evaluates real-world clinical performance using both immediate and⏎longitudinal analyses. We measure how AI-driven tools behave in live care settings from⏎the moment they launch and continue to track performance as member needs, clinical⏎patterns, and usage behaviors evolve. We strengthen our real-world validation system⏎through several additional practices:⏎● We maintain continuous human-in-the-loop oversight, examining model⏎outputs and escalating concerns when needed.⏎● We design synthetic, but clinically grounded, scenarios that reflect the⏎diversity of real behavioral health conversations.⏎● We evaluate models through a modular pipeline that tests safety, empathy,⏎crisis response, and clinical reasoning.⏎Spring recommends generative AI-enabled mental health tools should undergo⏎evaluation that is:⏎● Clinically Validated. Experienced clinicians should be included at every stage⏎of the design and validation process.⏎● Model Agnostic. The evaluation should be agnostic of the specific AI system.⏎The only requirement is the generation of a text output (“system output”)⏎given a text as input (“user input”).⏎● Multi-metric. Given the complexity and nuance of mental health, we cannot⏎define a system’s safety by a single metric. Toward this end, when using the⏎VERA-MH framework to determine whether an AI system can recognize and⏎respond appropriately to signs of crisis or suicide risk, for example, safety⏎should be assessed in at least five dimensions (discussed above).⏎● Narrowly Scoped. The evaluation should focus on clear, well-defined⏎concerns because safety in mental health is difficult to quantify.⏎● Multi-turn. Single-turn conversation evaluations (sending a single prompt and⏎assessing the response) are not enough to evaluate for clinical safety. Each⏎individual response may appear benign, but the overall interaction can pose⏎risks when evaluated in its entirety.⏎● Automated. The evaluation should be fully automated to keep pace with AI⏎model rates of change.⏎In VERA-MH, we assign each chatbot conversation under evaluation a rating of Best⏎Practice, Suboptimal but Low Potential for Harm, or High Potential for Harm for each of⏎these dimensions. We then summarize these ratings with a pooled overall safety score,⏎as well as safety scores for each of the five criteria.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎HHS has an important role to play in this effort. We recommend that the HHS AI Task⏎Force convenes a multi-stakeholder working group to encourage and facilitate the⏎development of consensus industry standards, leveraging VERA-MH , for AI⏎technologies, including chatbots.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification,⏎industry-driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use⏎in clinical care?⏎As described above, the private sector, including Spring Health, are already leading,⏎building, and adopting standards and digital health technologies to empower consumers⏎and improve health. HHS should leverage the expertise of the private sector to support⏎the development of consensus principles for AI use in mental health care that will⏎accelerate innovation while protecting patient privacy and safety. To realize this, Spring⏎Health recommends that the HHS AI Task Force convene a multi-stakeholder working⏎group to drive the development of voluntary industry standards, leveraging VERA-MH⏎standards, for AI technologies, including chatbots.⏎We have engaged directly with several mental-health–focused chatbot and digital⏎wellness companies, including Unmind, Wayhaven, Wave, and MyFlourishAI. Across⏎these conversations, the response has been consistently positive. These organizations⏎have expressed appreciation that VERA-MH is being built with clinicians, emphasizes⏎inter-rater reliability (IRR) as a foundation for credibility, and is released as an⏎open-source framework. Many have noted that this combination—clinical grounding,⏎reproducible evaluation, and openness—is currently missing in the market and is critical⏎for responsible adoption of AI in mental health.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and⏎cost expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools⏎would have the greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new⏎insights on quality, and help reduce costs?⏎When done appropriately, AI-enabled tools supporting mental health care can increase⏎access and reach, helping to eliminate barriers to care. Spring Health provides⏎accessible, high-quality, and personalized care in a secure environment that prioritizes⏎privacy, responsible data use, and clinically-sound guidance, while also identifying⏎high-risk groups earlier and optimizing care for better outcomes and cost savings. We⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎use AI to deliver insights to providers and patients that help them receive continuous⏎care and reduce administrative burden. We also provide insights to organizations that⏎help identify high-risk groups earlier to provide proactive support and optimize care⏎pathways for better outcomes and cost savings. At Spring Health, our AI tools⏎accelerate, but do not replace, human care. Instead, these tools help members access⏎the right support faster and enable providers to deliver better care.⏎For example, Spring Health’s Guided Intake (structured, clinically informed prompts⏎from an AI chatbot) as well as our Summaries and Takeaways tools (AI summaries of⏎therapy sessions for patients) have shown 90%+ positive feedback. In terms of uptake,⏎50% of patients chose to use Guided Intake before their first session and approximately⏎40% of patients interact with the Summaries & Takeaways. A member who utilized⏎Spring Health’s Guided Intake tool noted, “[t]his is the first time I had to pre-fill what my⏎situation was before seeing a therapist. I feel like this will save time for the first session⏎because they already have a heads up on my situation” while a different member noted,⏎“I felt the follow up questions were clearly what I previously expressed, which helps me⏎to know my thoughts and feelings will be received correctly.”⏎Early evaluation results indicate that widely used general-purpose language models⏎continue to struggle with high-risk mental health scenarios, particularly when clarifying⏎suicide risk, escalating and guiding to human support, and maintaining appropriate⏎clinical boundaries. Importantly, VERA-MH’s evaluation methodology has demonstrated⏎strong agreement between human clinicians and between clinician and judge-agent⏎scores, indicating that the framework itself is reliable and reproducible. This gives⏎confidence that the results reflect meaningful safety signals rather than subjective or⏎inconsistent judgment.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care⏎organizations have the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What⏎are the primary administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎The federal government, including the White House, HHS (including ASTP, FDA, and⏎CMS), and Congress, all have influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care. As such,⏎Spring Health encourages the following:⏎● The HHS AI Task Force should convene a multi-stakeholder working group to⏎drive the development of voluntary industry standards, leveraging VERA-MH⏎standards, for AI technologies, including chatbots.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎● To promote use, Medicaid and Medicare should provide priority⏎reimbursement for compliant tools and FDA should consider compliance with⏎such standards as part of the risk/benefit analysis.⏎● As Congress reviews the safety of chatbot use in mental health care, they⏎should look at how VERA-MH and other private standards could mitigate⏎concerns raised about the safety of these technologies.⏎However, the private sector and health care organizations also have important roles in⏎adoption and use of AI for clinical care, and their expertise should be leveraged to⏎support the development of consensus principles for AI use in mental health care that⏎will accelerate innovation while protecting patient privacy and safety.⏎We encourage all stakeholders to consider Spring Health and the AI in Mental Health⏎Safety & Ethics Council as a resource on the power of accelerating AI development⏎while taking a thoughtful approach to deploying responsible technology in accordance⏎with VERA-MH standards.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research,⏎and accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data⏎types, data standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Therapists spend too much time on paperwork, limiting their capacity for patient care.⏎Yet, the mental health care sector has been slow to adopt electronic health records⏎(EHRs) because they were not provided with adoption incentives under the HITECH⏎Act. They are also slow to adopt AI due to distrust, lack of understanding or access to⏎technology, a complex regulatory landscape, or a lack of clear risk mitigation standards.⏎Broader use of EHRs and health information exchanges depends on encouraging and⏎incentivizing EHR adoption. AI acceptance and use hinges on workflow integration that⏎enables the clinician and care team to work more efficiently and effectively with patients;⏎asynchronous communication, which minimizes hassle for patients and providers and⏎allows for between-encounter check-ins and updates; online cognitive behavioral⏎therapy training and AI-assisted support; and interoperable integrations across systems,⏎which would allow for standardized inputs and enhance data quality and completeness.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see⏎addressed by the adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do⏎patients and caregivers have related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎Patients and caregivers face challenges with care navigation into and throughout care.⏎Spring believes patients and caregivers are looking for better ways to track progress⏎and outcomes of their care and seeking better support between appointments with⏎clinicians in order to practice new skills and use learnings from their therapy sessions.⏎At the same time, consumers and patients need an AI experience built around trust,⏎privacy, transparency, and patient safety. Thus, AI-enabled digital health technologies⏎must be safe, ethical, and evidence-based. Spring Health provides accessible,⏎high-quality, and personalized care in a secure environment that prioritizes privacy,⏎responsible data use, and clinically-sound guidance.⏎Many consumers and patients struggle to navigate the mental health care system due⏎to stigma, cost, and lack of time.4 Spring uses a combination of patient-reported⏎outcomes, passive monitoring signals, engagement data, and EHR information to⏎evaluate our AI tools on an ongoing basis. Through the Spring app, we apply AI to drive⏎dynamic intakes, generate smart summaries, and provide in-the-moment support that⏎keeps members engaged, reduces repetitive information sharing, and connects them to⏎the right provider more quickly. With consent from members and providers, Spring⏎securely transcribes sessions and generates summaries directly within our EHR,⏎improving documentation speed and accuracy by up to 40 percent. We then incorporate⏎clinical outcomes data and user feedback into regular model updates to strengthen⏎performance and improve support for both care teams and our patients.⏎Human oversight is essential to ensure patient safety, accuracy, and appropriateness of⏎digital tools. Spring actively employs multiple tools, methodologies, and processes to⏎monitor AI-enabled tools’ performance. Because mental health is both high-impact and⏎rapidly changing, Spring’s approach for VERA-MH is intentionally iterative. We operate⏎in a continuous evaluation cycle, constantly testing, learning, and refining based on new⏎data, provider feedback, and patient outcomes.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the⏎adoption of AI as part of clinical care?⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their⏎use in clinical care?⏎4 SAMHSA. Key Substance Use and Mental Health Indicators in the United States: Results from the 2024⏎National Survey on Drug Use and Health. 2025.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎Spring Health recently posted a pre-print study that examines the clinical validity⏎and reliability of VERA-MH for evaluating AI safety in suicide risk detection and⏎response. The study found that individual clinicians were generally consistent⏎with one another in their safety ratings, establishing a gold-standard clinical⏎reference. The LLM judge was strongly aligned with this clinical consensus⏎overall and within key conditions. Together, findings from this human evaluation⏎study support the validity and reliability of VERA-MH: an open-source, automated⏎AI safety evaluation for mental health. Future research will examine the⏎generalizability and robustness of VERA-MH and expand the framework to target⏎additional key areas of AI safety in mental health.5⏎In parallel, a second recent Spring Health study demonstrates the feasibility and⏎value of rigorous, real-world evaluation of AI-enabled tools used to support⏎human-delivered clinical care. In a preregistered, large-scale study of more than⏎26,000 adults receiving psychotherapy through employer-sponsored programs,⏎Spring Health evaluated AI-enabled features designed to support engagement⏎and continuity of care when embedded within routine, clinician-delivered⏎psychotherapy.6 Access to these tools was associated with faster improvement in⏎depressive and anxiety symptoms compared with psychotherapy alone. While⏎individual-level effects were modest, they translated into clinically meaningful⏎population-level impact when deployed at scale, with approximately one⏎additional patient achieving reliable symptom improvement for every 25 patients⏎with access.⏎The findings are notable because the intervention was extremely light, requiring⏎no clinical training, and only that clinicians and patients click “accept” to agree to⏎having sessions recorded. This study illustrates how pragmatic, real-world⏎research designs can generate actionable evidence on the benefits and⏎limitations of AI in clinical care, supporting responsible adoption and informed⏎policymaking.⏎5 Bentley KH, Belli L, Chekroud AM, Ward EJ, Dworkin ER, Van Ark E, Johnston KM, Alexander W, Brown⏎M, Hawrilenko M. VERA-MH: Reliability and validity of an open-source AI safety evaluation in mental⏎health. arXiv. Preprint posted online February 4, 2026. arXiv:2602.05088.⏎6 Graupensperger, S., Kopecky, O., Brown, M., Chekroud, A., & Hawrilenko, M. (2026). AI-Enabled⏎Continuous Care Features in Real-World Psychotherapy: Treatment Engagement and Clinical Outcomes.⏎medRxiv, 2026-01.⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com⏎⏎Conclusion⏎Through our products, Spring harnesses the power of technology to simplify and⏎streamline care management, reduce barriers to data access and exchange, and⏎achieve proven improvements to health outcomes. Spring’s work demonstrates that⏎when implemented thoughtfully, AI and digital tools not only improve health outcomes⏎but also enhance access. We encourage HHS to consider Spring as an example of, and⏎a resource on, the power of accelerating AI development while taking a democratized⏎approach to advising on deploying responsible technology. Spring welcomes continued⏎dialogue and collaboration with HHS in shaping the future of AI in health.⏎Sincerely,⏎Brian Altman, JD⏎Director of Government Affairs⏎Spring Health⏎60 Madison Avenue, 2nd Floor ￨ New York, NY 10010 ￨ www.springhealth.com♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0495♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Society for Radiation Oncology (ASTRO), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Society for Radiation Oncology (ASTRO)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Robert F. Kennedy, Jr.⏎Department of Health and Human Services⏎ATTN: HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Mail Stop: 7033A, 330 C Street SW⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Submitted electronically: http://www.regulations.gov⏎Dear Secretary Kennedy:⏎The American Society for Radiation Oncology (ASTRO)1 appreciates the⏎opportunity to provide written comments on the Department of Health⏎and Human Services’ (HHS) Health Sector Artificial Intelligence (AI)⏎request for information (RFI). The Department seeks feedback on the⏎actions it can take to establish a forward-leaning, industry-supportive,⏎and secure approach to accelerate the adoption and use of AI as part of⏎clinical care. In general, HHS seeks feedback on ways in which these⏎approaches can be most effectively applied to support the rapid⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care, to foster public trust and⏎confidence in modern technology solutions, to reduce uncertainty that⏎impedes AI innovation, and to align federal incentives so that AI is⏎deployed in ways that enhance productivity, reduce burden, lower⏎health care costs, and improve health outcomes for patients, caregivers,⏎and communities.⏎AI has significant potential to improve patient outcomes, enhance⏎access to care, and increase health system efficiency. However,⏎successful integration into clinical care requires:⏎• Physician-guided implementation to ensure patient safety and⏎compassionate care;⏎• Thoughtful reimbursement models tied to measurable value;⏎• Infrastructure investment incentives; and⏎• Medical specialty society leadership in accreditation and⏎appropriate use frameworks.⏎1 ASTRO members are medical professionals practicing at hospitals and cancer treatment centers in the⏎United States and around the globe. They make up the radiation treatment teams that are critical in the⏎fight against cancer. These teams include radiation oncologists, medical physicists, medical dosimetrists,⏎radiation therapists, oncology nurses, nutritionists, and social workers. They treat more than one million⏎patients with cancer each year. We believe this multi-disciplinary membership makes us uniquely⏎qualified to provide input on the inherently complex issues related to Medicare payment policy and⏎coding for radiation oncology services.⏎⏎ASTRO Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 2 of 8⏎AI should augment—not replace—clinical judgment. Federal policy should ensure that AI deployment⏎strengthens human-centered medicine while creating sustainable pathways for responsible adoption.⏎HHS payment policy has a tremendous influence on whether high-value AI innovations are adopted in⏎clinical care, yet the current payment systems are poorly suited to support tools whose value accrues⏎over time through improved safety, efficiency, and outcomes. To ensure that the promise of AI is not⏎diminished by the current payment landscape, HHS should modernize payment policy to better align⏎incentives with long term value. This includes creating pathways that reward AI tools demonstrating⏎measurable improvements in patient outcomes, safety, access, or workforce sustainability, rather than⏎‑⏎tying payment solely to discrete services or assumed efficiency gains. Aligning payment policy with value⏎and real world implementation costs will be essential to accelerating access to effective and affordable⏎AI tools in clinical care.⏎‑⏎Additionally, ASTRO is concerned about the rapid adoption of AI by health insurers, particularly in areas⏎such as utilization management, prior authorization, and claims adjudication.2 Unlike AI tools used by⏎clinicians, which are subject to FDA oversight and professional accountability, insurer deployed AI⏎operates with far less transparency or standardized review. This lack of oversight raises concerns about⏎‑⏎how decisions are made, how errors are identified, and how patients and providers can meaningfully⏎challenge adverse determinations.⏎ASTRO is concerned that deploying AI into already contentious processes (e.g., prior authorization and⏎coverage determinations) may simply accelerate inappropriate or poorly explained denials, rather than⏎improve decision quality. Faster decisions are only beneficial if they are accurate, clinically grounded,⏎and based on transparent criteria applied in good faith. Without clear disclosure of when AI is used,⏎what data it relies on, and how its outputs are monitored for bias or error, trust between payers,⏎providers, and patients is likely to erode. As HHS considers modernizing payment systems to support⏎high-value AI, it is essential that similar expectations for transparency, accountability, and ongoing⏎monitoring apply to AI used by payers, particularly when those tools influence access to medically⏎necessary care.⏎Moreover, it is critical that any funding allocated by HHS for AI initiatives in clinical care comes from new⏎appropriations, rather than diverting resources from existing Medicare Part B funds. Drawing from⏎current Medicare budgets could jeopardize essential services and undermine the financial stability of⏎the program. To ensure that AI adoption enhances care without compromising established coverage,⏎HHS should seek dedicated, supplemental funding streams specifically earmarked for AI infrastructure,⏎evaluation, and implementation support. This approach will protect Medicare beneficiaries while⏎fostering innovation and sustainable progress in health care delivery.⏎Specific Questions⏎In the RFI, HHS posed 10 specific questions. ASTRO’s responses to each are below.⏎1. What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎2 In a financial pinch, major health insurers are turning to AI for help,⏎https://www.statnews.com/2026/02/17/health-insurers-expand-ai-use-undermining-providers-trust/⏎⏎ASTRO Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 3 of 8⏎Reimbursement and the absence of viable payment pathways are the dominant barriers. Radiation⏎oncology practices have limited financial margins to absorb new AI tools, and these AI tools often lack⏎explicit coding and payment recognition. This makes widespread AI adoption financially infeasible even⏎when clinically beneficial.⏎Additionally, interoperability and data fragmentation remain major headwinds. Siloed electronic health⏎record (EHR) systems, limited access to structured data, and inconsistent standards implementation⏎restrict development, evaluation, and scale. In radiation oncology specifically, interoperability must⏎include the physics data chain (e.g., contour geometry, dose grids, beam parameters), not just imaging;⏎otherwise, it becomes ineffective and potentially unsafe (e.g., re-irradiation planning without access to⏎prior dose grids).⏎Finally, AI “maturity” varies widely. Some use cases are mature and in broad clinical use, while others,⏎such as generative AI for high-stakes clinical tasks, are not reliable and may hallucinate or fail domain⏎reasoning. Policy that treats all AI as equivalent can block safe adoption of mature tools while under-⏎addressing risks in immature categories.⏎2. What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why? What HHS regulations, policies, or⏎programs could be revisited to augment your ability to develop or use AI in clinical care?⏎First, reimbursement for AI-enabled clinical work should not assume that AI reduces physician work.⏎ASTRO members regularly provide feedback that AI often actually increases work (e.g., to⏎commissioning, quality assurance, or monitoring), rather than reducing or eliminating it.⏎Moreover, it is essential to track and disclose how financial resources allocated for AI adoption are⏎distributed: whether they benefit the practice, the provider, or the AI company. This will ensure⏎transparency, accountability, and equitable support for clinical care innovation.⏎Next, HHS should issue clear national guidance on AI governance and accountability within health⏎systems, particularly for post-deployment use. Today, both clinicians and institutions face uncertainty⏎about expectations for oversight, documentation, monitoring, and responsibility allocation, especially in⏎multi-vendor environments where AI tools interact with EHRs, imaging systems, treatment planning⏎systems, and delivery devices. This uncertainty slows adoption and increases risk. HHS can play a critical⏎role by articulating baseline governance expectations applicable to both FDA-regulated AI devices and⏎non-device clinical AI. Such guidance should clarify that physicians remain the final decision-makers in⏎clinical care, while also establishing expectations for transparent logging of AI recommendations,⏎continuous performance monitoring, version and update management, and incident reporting.⏎Additionally, HHS should actively incentivize and support prospective evaluation of AI tools, both before⏎and after deployment. ASTRO members consistently emphasize that the lack of standardized,⏎prospective evaluation frameworks undermines confidence in AI and slows responsible adoption. Pre-⏎deployment expectations should include external validation across institutions, robustness testing, and⏎subgroup fairness analysis. Post-deployment, evaluation must extend beyond static accuracy metrics to⏎include drift monitoring, real-world performance dashboards, and outcomes-based surveillance. HHS⏎can accelerate this work by supporting benchmarking consortia that maintain shared evaluation⏎datasets, develop common metrics, and enable cross-institution validation. Federal support through⏎grants, cooperative agreements, or similar mechanisms would help create the infrastructure needed for⏎continuous learning while reducing duplicative local effort.⏎⏎ASTRO Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 4 of 8⏎Finally, HHS should provide clarity that routine local AI validation, monitoring, and safety surveillance,⏎when conducted with appropriate safeguards, are part of normal health care operations and not⏎automatically classified as research. This distinction is critical for enabling continuous safety⏎improvement, particularly in community-based settings.⏎3. For non-medical devices, we understand that use of AI in clinical care may raise novel legal⏎and implementation issues that challenge existing governance and accountability structures⏎(e.g., relating to liability, indemnification, privacy, and security). What novel legal and⏎implementation issues exist and what role, if any, should HHS play to help address them?⏎The increasing use of non-medical device AI in clinical care raises a set of legal and implementation⏎challenges that are not well addressed by existing governance and accountability structures. Unlike⏎FDA-regulated medical devices, many AI tools used in clinical environments (e.g., clinical documentation⏎tools, prior authorization support), can have direct and material effects on patient access, timeliness of⏎care, and clinical decision-making without being subject to a clear, uniform oversight framework.⏎One of the most significant challenges is uncertainty around accountability and liability. Health systems⏎and clinicians often lack clear guidance on how responsibility should be allocated among clinicians,⏎health systems, and vendors when non-device AI tools influence clinical workflows or recommendations.⏎Federal guidance clarifying that physicians remain the final decisionmakers in clinical care is needed,⏎while also establishing clear expectations for how AI outputs are reviewed, documented, and acted⏎upon. Without this clarity, organizations may either over-rely on AI outputs or, instead, delay adoption⏎of beneficial tools due to liability concerns.⏎Another issue is the lack of standardized transparency and auditability requirements. Many clinical AI⏎tools function as “black boxes,” providing recommendations or workflow actions without sufficient⏎visibility into how outputs were generated. This creates risks for patient safety, compliance, and trust.⏎ASTRO supports requirements for transparent logging of AI recommendations, including the ability to⏎retrospectively review when and how AI influenced clinical workflows. Such logging is essential not only⏎for accountability and quality improvement, but also for incident investigation and patient safety⏎surveillance.⏎4. For non-medical devices, what are the most promising AI evaluation methods (pre- and post-⏎deployment), metrics, robustness testing, and other workflow and human-centered⏎evaluation methods for clinical care? Should HHS further support these processes? If so, which⏎mechanisms would be most impactful (e.g., contracts, grants, cooperative agreements, and/or⏎prize competitions)?⏎Effective evaluation of non-medical device AI in clinical care requires approaches that reflect real-world⏎clinical use and patient safety. Prior to deployment, the most promising evaluation methods include⏎external validation across multiple institutions, robustness testing under realistic clinical conditions, and⏎subgroup analyses to assess performance across patient populations. These steps are critical for⏎identifying risks that may not be apparent in development datasets and for building clinician confidence⏎in AI tools.⏎Post-deployment evaluation is equally important and should be treated as a core safety expectation. AI⏎tools are frequently updated or retrained over time, creating ongoing risk of performance drift.⏎Continuous monitoring, supported by transparent dashboards and audit logs, is therefore essential.⏎⏎ASTRO Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 5 of 8⏎Importantly, evaluation should emphasize outcomes-based and workflow-level impacts, such as safety⏎events, care delays, or access implications, rather than relying solely on technical accuracy metrics.⏎HHS can play an important role by supporting shared evaluation infrastructure rather than leaving each⏎health system to develop duplicative approaches. ASTRO would support federal investment in⏎benchmarking consortia that maintain shared evaluation datasets, define common metrics, and enable⏎external validation across institutions.⏎5. How can HHS best support private sector activities (e.g., accreditation, certification, industry-⏎driven testing, and credentialing) to promote innovative and effective AI use in clinical care?⏎HHS can best support private-sector activities by providing clear expectations that promote safety,⏎transparency, and sustainability without constraining innovation. Successful AI adoption depends on⏎predictable frameworks that recognize the full clinical and operational lifecycle of AI, rather than⏎focusing narrowly on algorithm performance or assumed efficiency gains.⏎As previously mentioned, HHS should ensure that payment and program policy do not discourage⏎participation in accreditation or certification efforts by assuming AI reduces clinical work and improves⏎quality. Recognizing this reality will help ensure that private-sector certification and accreditation⏎programs remain viable across diverse practice settings, including community-based care.⏎Second, HHS should promote standardized transparency and documentation expectations for clinical AI⏎tools. Clear, common expectations around intended use, limitations, performance variability, update⏎management, audit logging, and cybersecurity would reduce duplication, improve comparability, and⏎enable more meaningful industry-driven testing and credentialing.⏎Finally, HHS should support voluntary, consensus-based accreditation models that focus on institutional⏎AI governance and readiness, as well as independent third-party benchmarking efforts that enable⏎external validation across institutions. Federal support for shared evaluation infrastructure and AI⏎literacy standards would amplify private-sector leadership while ensuring that AI adoption advances⏎patient safety and trust.⏎6. Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded performance and cost⏎expectations and where have they fallen short? What kinds of novel AI tools would have the⏎greatest potential to improve health care outcomes, give new insights on quality, and help⏎reduce costs?⏎AI tools deployed in clinical care have shown the greatest success when applied to well-defined, mature⏎use cases that augment existing workflows rather than attempting to replace complex clinical judgment.⏎AI has met expectations in some areas, such as document summarization, but has fallen short of⏎expectations when applied to high-stakes clinical decision-making. For example, generative AI systems,⏎may hallucinate clinical facts, fabricate citations, or fail to apply core domain reasoning, making them⏎unreliable for tasks such as treatment intent formulation or dose specification. In these settings, the gap⏎between technical performance and clinical reliability remains significant, and premature deployment⏎can introduce safety risks rather than reduce them.⏎⏎ASTRO Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 6 of 8⏎Looking ahead, the most promising AI tools are those that directly address persistent challenges in⏎healthcare delivery, including treatment delays, staffing shortages, and unwarranted variability in care.⏎The greatest value will come from tools that provide new insights into quality and safety across the full⏎care pathway, rather than narrowly optimizing isolated tasks. When paired with appropriate⏎reimbursement, governance, and evaluation frameworks, these applications offer meaningful⏎opportunities to improve care while helping control long-term costs.⏎7. Which role(s), decision maker(s), or governing bodies within health care organizations have⏎the most influence on the adoption of AI for clinical care? What are the primary⏎administrative hurdles to the adoption of AI in clinical care?⏎Within health care organizations, adoption of AI for clinical care is most strongly influenced by those⏎responsible for quality, safety, and operations. While executive and IT leadership play important roles,⏎AI adoption ultimately depends on whether tools align with clinical workflows, patient safety priorities,⏎and workforce realities. In specialties such as radiation oncology, decisions are heavily shaped by⏎radiation oncologists, physicists, and multidisciplinary oversight committees responsible for validation,⏎quality assurance, and ongoing monitoring.⏎The primary administrative hurdles to adoption include lack of sustainable reimbursement, unclear⏎governance and accountability expectations, and the operational burden associated with⏎commissioning, validating, and monitoring AI tools over time. Uncertainty around post deployment⏎responsibilities, like performance drift monitoring, version control, and update management, further⏎‑⏎slows adoption. Staffing shortages compound these challenges, as organizations may lack the protected⏎time and resources needed to deploy AI safely, even when the technology could help address access⏎delays and workforce strain. Together, these barriers often limit adoption of clinically valuable AI tools⏎despite strong interest from clinicians.⏎8. Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities, fuel research, and⏎accelerate the development of AI for clinical care? Please consider specific data types, data⏎standards, and benchmarking tools.⏎Current interoperability efforts are predominantly structured around diagnostic workflows, prioritizing⏎the seamless exchange of imaging data for purposes such as diagnosis and initial assessment. While⏎these initiatives have advanced the ability to share and interpret diagnostic images across institutions,⏎they often fall short in supporting therapeutic specialties like radiation oncology. In these settings,⏎images serve merely as the starting point to a much more intricate process that encompasses not just⏎visualization, but also the computational planning and precise delivery of radiation. Therefore, AI⏎development and safe clinical use in therapeutic domains require interoperable access that extends⏎beyond images to include treatment-specific data such as contour geometry, dose distributions, beam⏎parameters, and longitudinal treatment history. Without robust support for the full spectrum of⏎therapeutic data, interoperability frameworks risk limiting the potential impact of AI-enabled care in⏎these critical specialties.⏎In radiation oncology, the lack of standardized, computable exchange of prior treatment data remains a⏎major barrier to AI-enabled care, research, and safety monitoring. For example, without interoperable⏎access to prior dose information, AI tools cannot reliably support re-irradiation planning, toxicity⏎⏎ASTRO Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 7 of 8⏎prediction, or adaptive treatment strategies. Expanding interoperability standards to include radiation⏎treatment history and therapy-specific data elements would meaningfully widen opportunities for AI⏎innovation while improving patient safety. By prioritizing interoperability that reflects real clinical⏎workflows and specialty-specific data needs, HHS can help unlock the full potential of AI to improve⏎quality, safety, and access across the healthcare system.⏎9. What challenges within health care do patients and caregivers wish to see addressed by the⏎adoption and use of AI in clinical care? Equally, what concerns do patients and caregivers have⏎related to the adoption and use of AI in clinical care?⏎From ASTRO members’ perspective, patients and caregivers are most interested in AI applications that⏎reduce delays in care, improve safety, and support timely access to high quality treatment and⏎improved outcomes. At the same time, patients and caregivers have legitimate concerns about safety,⏎‑⏎transparency, and trust in AI enabled clinical care. Tools that operate as “black boxes” produce⏎unreliable or fabricated outputs, or are deployed without clear human oversight can undermine⏎‑⏎confidence and raise fears about errors affecting care decisions. Patients also expect that AI will⏎augment, not replace, clinical judgment and that physicians remain accountable for decisions affecting⏎their treatment.⏎Privacy and appropriate use of health data are additional concerns, particularly as AI tools rely on large⏎volumes of clinical information and may evolve over time. Patients want assurance that their data is⏎protected, that AI tools are monitored for ongoing performance, and that failures or biases are⏎identified and addressed promptly.⏎10. Are there specific areas of AI research that HHS should prioritize to accelerate the adoption of⏎AI as part of clinical care?⏎HHS should prioritize research focused on real world performance, safety, and sustainability, rather⏎than algorithm development alone. A key priority is prospective, multi institutional evaluation research⏎‑⏎that examines longitudinal performance, subgroup variability, and interaction between AI tools and⏎‑⏎human decision making. Research should move beyond retrospective accuracy metrics to assess⏎real world outcomes such as patient safety, access to care, workflow reliability, and treatment delays.⏎‑⏎Shared datasets and benchmarking infrastructure would support this work and reduce duplicative effort⏎‑⏎across health systems.⏎HHS also should prioritize research on the human and organizational dimensions of AI adoption,⏎including governance models, oversight structures, and best practices for commissioning, monitoring,⏎and updating AI systems. Understanding how AI shifts clinical and administrative work (and the⏎implications for staffing and safety) is essential to scalable adoption.⏎a. Are there published findings about the impact of adopted AI tools and their use clinical care?⏎The published literature on AI adoption in clinical care shows the strongest and most consistent⏎evidence of benefit in narrow, well defined use cases, particularly those that automate repetitive tasks⏎or support clinical prioritization rather than replacing complex clinical judgment. By contrast, the⏎literature remains more limited and mixed for higher stakes or more generative AI applications that⏎influence clinical reasoning or treatment decisions.⏎‑⏎⏎ASTRO Response to HHS Health Sector AI RFI⏎Page 8 of 8⏎b. How does the literature approach the costs, benefits, and transfers of using AI as part of⏎clinical care?⏎The literature increasingly recognizes that the costs and benefits of AI in clinical care are not evenly⏎distributed and rarely result in immediate net savings. While AI may reduce time spent on certain tasks⏎or improve efficiency, these benefits are often offset by new and ongoing tasks and costs related to data⏎infrastructure, system integration, validation, quality assurance, cybersecurity, and continuous⏎performance monitoring. As a result, AI frequently increases work and costs rather than eliminating⏎them.⏎Many studies also note that financial benefits may accrue to payers or the broader health system, while⏎implementation and oversight costs are borne by clinicians and health care organizations. In addition,⏎downstream benefits such as improved safety, reduced delays, or avoided adverse events are difficult to⏎quantify and are often underrepresented in traditional cost analyses. Overall, the literature suggests AI⏎delivers the greatest value when payment and policy frameworks account for these cost transfers and⏎support the investments required for safe, sustained use.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment on this RFI. We look forward to continued dialogue with HHS⏎officials. Should you have any questions on the items addressed in these comments, please contact⏎Adam Greathouse, Director of Health Policy, at (703) 839-7376 or Adam.Greathouse@astro.org.⏎Respectfully,⏎Vivek S Kavadi, MD, MBA, FASTRO Sameer R. Keole, MD, FASTRO⏎Chief Executive Officer Chair, ASTRO Board of Directors♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0496♔**TBD**♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment on HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔2025-12-24T00:00:00Z♔2026-02-22T00:00:00Z♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔♔♔2026-01-30T00:00:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔2♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Thomas♔Smith♔♔Nevada♔♔United States♔Salesforce♔♔♔♔♔♔Salesforce Health & Life Sciences | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 1⏎⏎Thomas Smith, VP Regulatory Affairs⏎Salesforce, Inc.⏎San Francisco, CA / Las Vegas, NV⏎⏎Submitted via regulations.gov⏎⏎My focus in this comment is specifically on the question of how HHS proposes to assess distributional impacts of AI adoption across patient populations — because frankly, what I'm not seeing in the RFI framing is a serious engagement with what happens when the same technology gets deployed very differently depending on where a patient receives care.⏎⏎I want to be direct about where I'm coming from. Salesforce works with health systems across the country, and through our customer relationships we have direct visibility into how AI tools actually get operationalized at the facility level. That visibility has made me considerably more skeptical of the "democratization of AI" framing than I might otherwise be. The phrase implies that broader access equals better outcomes for everyone. I'm not sure that follows, and the data we're seeing from health system customers who are deploying AI imaging and clinical decision support tools suggests the gap between what AI adoption looks like at a well-resourced academic medical center versus a critical access hospital in rural Nevada is substantial enough to warrant real concern.⏎⏎Salesforce Health & Life Sciences | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 2⏎⏎Our concern is specifically about the substitution dynamic. When a large integrated health system in a major metro deploys an AI diagnostic tool, it typically does so alongside existing clinical staff — the AI is genuinely augmenting physician review. When a small or under-resourced facility adopts the same tool, the economic logic often pushes in a different direction. AI becomes a way to operate with fewer clinical staff, not a way to make existing staff more effective. The patients in those settings — who are disproportionately elderly, low-income, have more complex comorbidities, are more likely to be non-English speakers, more likely to have disabilities affecting their ability to self-advocate — those patients end up receiving the most automated care with the least human oversight. I keep coming back to this when I read the RFI's emphasis on "accelerating adoption" as though speed is the primary variable HHS should be optimizing for.⏎⏎What I'm asking HHS to consider, and this relates specifically to the questions around regulatory frameworks for AI governance, is whether any new adoption framework should include mandatory equity impact assessment as a precondition rather than an optional or post-hoc compliance element. Not a checkbox exercise. An actual structured assessment of whether a given deployment is functioning as augmentation or substitution, and for which patient populations, before reimbursement or certification pathways are opened up.⏎⏎Salesforce Health & Life Sciences | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 3⏎⏎I recognize there are arguments on the other side of this. Our industry partners have raised legitimate concerns about compliance cost as a barrier to adoption, and those aren't trivial — in our own customer advisory work we've seen compliance preparation costs for AI deployment running into the range of $80,000+ per facility per payer contract, which does create real access problems for smaller providers. I'm not dismissing that. But the answer to compliance cost burden is to make the required assessments more efficient and standardized, not to waive them for the populations that face the highest operational risk from AI failure.⏎⏎HHS has existing statutory civil rights obligations under Section 1557 and related authorities. Accelerating AI adoption without a framework that operationalizes those obligations in the context of algorithmic decision-making isn't just a policy gap — it creates legal exposure for the Department and for the health systems relying on HHS guidance to structure their own liability frameworks. The liability question is genuinely unsettled right now, particularly for AI outputs that don't fit cleanly into the FDA medical device classification structure, and I've had multiple conversations with health system compliance officers who are making deployment decisions essentially in the dark because there's no authoritative federal guidance on where responsibility sits when an AI-assisted diagnosis contributes to an adverse outcome.⏎⏎I'd also note — and this is maybe a separate issue but I think it's related — that the documentation and audit trail requirements for AI-assisted clinical decisions vary enormously by payer right now, and that inconsistency is itself creating a two-tier problem where larger systems with dedicated regulatory staff can navigate the variation and smaller systems⏎⏎Salesforce Health & Life Sciences | HHS AI RFI Response | Page 4⏎⏎simply can't absorb the administrative load. Standardizing those requirements across payers, ideally through CMS guidance, would do more to actually democratize access to AI-assisted care than almost anything else HHS could do in the near term.⏎⏎tssmith@salesforce.com⏎https://www.salesforce.com/industries/healthcare/[broken]⏎⏎The equity assessment framework we're describing doesn't need to be designed from scratch — there are existing models in the algorithmic accountability literature, and organizations like the Coalition for Health AI published draft guidance in 2023 that could be adapted. What's missing is HHS making clear that these assessments are a condition of participation rather than a best practice.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0497♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-21T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Bridges, Michael G, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-20T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Michael G♔Bridges♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔February 20, 2026⏎To: Steven Posnack, Principal Deputy Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy, ASTP/ONC⏎Department of Health and Human Services Mary E. Switzer Building, Mail Stop: 7033A 330⏎C Street SW, Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: HHS Health Sector AI RFI (Docket HHS-ONC-2026-0001)⏎Dear Mr. Posnack and the ASTP/ONC Review Team,⏎Thank you for the opportunity to provide feedback on the Department's approach to⏎accelerating the safe adoption of AI in clinical care. I am commenting on the use of AI as an⏎active agent in physical care—such as in autonomous surgical robotics, closed-loop⏎infusion pumps, and automated bio-synthesis—I want to offer a perspective on the⏎physical safety limits of software-only guardrails.⏎My primary feedback regarding the deployment of AI in physical clinical care is that⏎software safety models, while necessary, are inherently probabilistic and subject to⏎execution latency, kernel panics, and model deception. In high-consequence clinical⏎environments, I recommend that HHS establish safety expectations rooted in physical⏎hardware, rather than relying solely on software alignment.⏎Regarding Question 3, which touches on the novel legal and implementation issues relating⏎to liability and accountability, the introduction of agentic AI creates a unique forensic⏎challenge. Traditional medical liability relies on being able to audit what went wrong.⏎However, if an AI-driven medical device acts unpredictably, the software logs it generates⏎can be unreliable or spoofed if the system's root environment is compromised.⏎To resolve this accountability gap, HHS should encourage the adoption of hardware-⏎resident event logging. If a device experiences a safety violation that results in a shutdown⏎or a severed power connection, that event should generate a cryptographically signed⏎record at the hardware level—completely independent of the AI's operating system. This⏎acts as a true, unalterable "black box" for malpractice adjusters, hospital compliance⏎boards, and the FDA, proving the physical state of the device regardless of what the⏎software logs claim.⏎In response to Question 4 concerning AI evaluation methods and metrics, I suggest the⏎Department rethink how it measures safety responsiveness. Currently, many robustness⏎metrics focus on software response times. In physical care, such as robotic surgery, a⏎software loop taking 20 to 50 milliseconds to recognize an error and issue a stop command⏎can result in unintended tissue damage. HHS evaluation methods for physical AI tools⏎should transition toward measuring "deterministic arrest windows." For instance, systems⏎⏎should be evaluated on their ability to physically guarantee a zero-energy state at the⏎actuator in under one or two milliseconds. Achieving this practically requires physical⏎hardware interlocks (like a series interrupter on the power line) rather than just software-⏎based stops.⏎Finally, regarding Question 5 and how HHS can best support private sector accreditation⏎and certification, I believe the greatest impact would come from establishing baseline⏎hardware states for clinical AI. One of the most effective ways to mitigate risk is to require⏎that devices capable of causing physical harm default to a de-energized, normally-dark⏎state. For example, power to drug-infusion pumps or surgical drives should be physically⏎isolated until an independent hardware gate verifies the integrity of the clinical instruction⏎set.⏎This concept extends to a variety of clinical applications. In automated molecular⏎synthesis labs, reagent pumps should remain physically disconnected from power until the⏎instruction sequence is verified to be free of prohibited pathogen sequences. Similarly,⏎wearable or implantable AI controllers should have hardware disconnects that trigger if the⏎device falls out of sync with the patient's natural biological rhythms.⏎Software recommendations will constantly evolve as AI models change, but the laws of⏎physics do not. By standardizing the expectation that safety-critical interventions must⏎happen at the physical energy-delivery layer, HHS can ensure that human clinicians—and⏎not probabilistic software models—always retain the ultimate authority over patient care.⏎Thank you for your time and for the work your office is doing to secure the future of⏎healthcare technology.⏎Sincerely,⏎Michael G. Bridges♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0498♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from The University of Kansas Health System, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, N/A♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-05T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔The University of Kansas Health System♔♔♔♔♔♔To: Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎From: The University of Kansas Health System⏎Subject: Response to RFI: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as⏎part of Clinical Care (RIN 0955-AA13)⏎Executive Summary⏎The University of Kansas Health System is pleased to provide feedback on the HHS strategy⏎for AI adoption. Our response is grounded in the "Principles for Responsible AI" developed⏎by the College of Healthcare Information Management Executives (CHIME). We believe that⏎for AI to improve patient outcomes and reduce clinician burden, the federal government⏎must shift from a punitive regulatory culture to one that incentivizes innovation and shared⏎responsibility.⏎I. Regulation: Moving from Penalties to Incentives⏎HHS should establish a regulatory environment that is predictable and proportionate to⏎risk.⏎Incentive-Based Cybersecurity: We urge HHS to prioritize "carrots" over "sticks". Policies⏎should reward providers for implementing recognized cybersecurity best practices rather⏎than penalizing them when they are victims of sophisticated, AI-driven attacks.⏎National Privacy and AI Law: The current patchwork of state laws creates excessive⏎administrative burden and costs. We support a comprehensive national privacy and AI law⏎that preempts state requirements to ensure efficiency across the healthcare ecosystem.⏎Business Associate Agreement (BAA) Oversight: Many AI vendors use opaque contract⏎practices or refuse to sign standard BAAs, shifting disproportionate liability to health⏎systems. HHS should establish a standardized HIPAA BAA and increase oversight of⏎Business Associates to ensure an equitable allocation of risk.⏎Stark and Anti-Kickback Statute (AKS) Modernization: Current laws deter larger health⏎systems from donating cybersecurity and AI technology to smaller, under-resourced⏎partners due to liability fears. HHS should update safe harbors to permit these vital⏎donations, strengthening sector-wide resilience.⏎II. Reimbursement: Modernizing Payment for Innovation⏎HHS's payment policies must evolve to support high-value AI interventions.⏎⏎Bridging the "Digital Divide": Small, rural, and under-resourced providers face⏎insurmountable barriers to AI adoption, including infrastructure costs and lack of⏎purchasing power. HHS should implement targeted funding, tax credits, and group⏎purchasing options to ensure equitable access to AI tools.⏎Prevent Anti-Innovation Payer Terms: We are seeing a disturbing trend where payers⏎include "anti-AI" clauses in their terms and conditions. These clauses ban providers from⏎using AI or automation in revenue cycle processes. This creates a massive power⏎imbalance, as many of these same payers use AI to automate their own claim denial⏎processes. HHS should prohibit payers from contractually banning the use of responsible,⏎provider-side AI that improves administrative efficiency.⏎Incentivizing Value over Volume: Modernize reimbursement to reward the use of AI tools⏎that demonstrate long-term improvements in health outcomes and reductions in net⏎spending.⏎III. Research & Development: Focusing on the Human Element⏎AI is a tool to augment, not replace, human clinical expertise.⏎Workforce Education and AI Literacy: HHS should invest in tailored education to help⏎clinicians recognize AI limitations, such as "hallucinations" or algorithmic bias. Training⏎must equip healthcare professionals to "trust but verify”.⏎Standardized Model Cards: To reduce uncertainty, HHS should support the use of⏎standardized "model cards". These should provide a transparent, regularly updated⏎snapshot of a model’s training data, performance metrics, and potential biases to facilitate⏎informed purchasing decisions.⏎IV. Responses to Specific RFI Questions⏎Question 1: Biggest barriers to private sector innovation?⏎• Regulatory uncertainty, the "black box" nature of proprietary algorithms, and the⏎high resource intensity required for continuous auditing are the primary hurdles.⏎Question 2: Regulatory or payment policy changes to prioritize?⏎• Prioritize Stark/AKS Safe Harbors: HHS should immediately update Stark and Anti-⏎Kickback Statute (AKS) policies to clarify and expand cybersecurity and AI⏎technology donation provisions.⏎• Rescind the proposed HIPAA Security Rule: The current proposal is duplicative and⏎places a "crushing impact" on providers without meaningfully improving security.⏎⏎• Remove Duplicate CMS Mandates: The Medicare Promoting Interoperability (PI)⏎Program security risk management measure should be removed as it repeats⏎requirements already found under HIPAA.⏎Question 3: Novel legal and implementation issues?⏎• BAA Contract Overrides: We are seeing vendors use main service agreements to⏎override HIPAA Business Associate Agreement (BAA) restrictions, particularly⏎regarding broad data use rights.⏎• Liability Shifting: AI vendors are increasingly capping their liability for data breaches⏎at very low amounts (e.g., one year of service fees), which ignores the massive⏎downstream risk to the health system and patients.⏎• Contractual Asymmetry: Some insurance companies are now adding language to⏎their provider agreements that specifically prohibits the use of AI in revenue cycle⏎management (RCM). This is a "bad faith" approach to contracting. It forces health⏎systems to rely on manual, expensive, and slow processes while the payers use⏎high-speed AI to find reasons to deny those same claims.⏎• Inconsistent Definitions: These "bans" often use broad, poorly defined terms for AI,⏎which could inadvertently prohibit basic automation tools that have been industry⏎standards for years. This creates a "chilling effect" on innovation.⏎Question 4: Most promising AI evaluation methods?⏎• We recommend the adoption of "Assurance Labs" and the use of standardized⏎model cards that align with the "FAVES" framework (Fair, Appropriate, Valid,⏎Effective, and Safe).⏎Question 5: How can HHS support private sector accreditation/testing?⏎• Establish a "Better Business Bureau" for AI: HHS should support a resource that lists⏎AI solutions that have been determined to have met baseline security and safety⏎standards.⏎• Support for Small Providers: HHS should provide funding and technical assistance⏎to help under-resourced providers navigate the accreditation and auditing process,⏎which is currently too resource-intensive for them.⏎Question 6: AI performance and potential?⏎• Success in Ambient AI: Ambient listening tools have shown significant success in⏎reducing clinician documentation burden.⏎• Potential for Chatbots: AI-powered chatbots have high potential to reduce senior⏎isolation and streamline administrative tasks like scheduling.⏎⏎• Failures in ROI: Many AI tools have yet to deliver a justifiable return on investment⏎(ROI) because they require massive infrastructure and governance that smaller⏎systems lack.⏎Question 7: Key decision makers and hurdles?⏎• Decision Makers: AI adoption is driven by multidisciplinary governance boards,⏎though clinical informatics and IT leadership play the primary role.⏎• Administrative Hurdles: The primary hurdles are the "black box" nature of models,⏎which prevents clinician trust, and the time-consuming process of conducting⏎individual risk assessments for every new tool.⏎Question 8: Where would enhanced interoperability widen market opportunities?⏎• Standardizing security requirements for third-party technologies and promoting⏎interoperability between vendor security processes would reduce duplicative risk⏎assessments and lower costs for providers⏎Question 9: Patient and caregiver concerns?⏎• Desire for Awareness, Not Overload: Patients want to know if AI is helping deliver⏎their care, but they do not want to be overwhelmed with notifications for every minor⏎background automation.⏎• Trust and Benefit: Patient comfort with AI tools like ambient documentation varies⏎based on their level of trust in the provider and perceived benefit to their care.⏎Question 10: AI research priorities?⏎• Focus on Bias vs. Noise: Research should prioritize differentiating between⏎systematic bias and random "noise" in clinical datasets.⏎• Impact on Clinical Skills: We need more research on the "tangible risk" that over-⏎reliance on AI might lead to the deterioration of essential human clinical skills.♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0499♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from Verily, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Verily♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎Office of the Deputy Secretary and Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP) and⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) (collectively,⏎ASTP/ONC), Department of Health and Human Services⏎RE: HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and⏎Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎Verily welcomes the opportunity to share feedback on strategies HHS can take to accelerate⏎safe and effective AI adoption in clinical care. Recent initiatives established by the⏎Administration, including the OneHHS approach to AI, the HTI-5 rule publication and HTI-2⏎partial withdrawal, and the introduction of the CMS ACCESS model in conjunction with the FDA⏎TEMPO pilot demonstrate HHS’ commitment to advancing the safe and effective use of AI in⏎healthcare delivery. We believe forthcoming policy can and should build on the progress made⏎already by this Administration and the 21st Century Cures Act to enhance access to and⏎exchange of data that will power AI-enabled care solutions and ultimately improve health⏎outcomes for all Americans.⏎Verily is an Alphabet data platform and AI company that is purpose-built for precision health. We⏎combine deep clinical, regulatory, and technology expertise to provide our customers with⏎patient-centric solutions that make healthcare more personal and precise. Verily solutions are⏎designed to make healthcare data AI-ready and fit for purpose across care, research, and public⏎health. Our extensive experience building innovative technology platforms for data ingestion,⏎curation, and analysis, alongside our commitment to generating high-quality, regulatory-grade⏎real-world data (RWD), positions us to offer perspectives on advancing the collection,⏎standardization, and regulatory acceptance of data derived from real-world sources.⏎In our response, we highlight three key steps that would help advance the adoption of AI in⏎clinical care: 1) supporting data liquidity within the health technology ecosystem, 2) encouraging⏎efforts to improve data reliability, and 3) establishing national, risk-based frameworks for AI⏎regulation and payment.⏎1) Fostering a Seamless Environment for Health Information Exchange⏎Data liquidity—broadly defined as the ease with which health data flows securely and⏎seamlessly across systems, organizations, and individuals—is the foundational element⏎required to accelerate AI adoption. At Verily, we believe it is imperative to build a nationwide and⏎seamless data exchange environment. We acknowledge much progress has been made since⏎the enactment of the 21st Century Cures Act, which provides the authority for The Trusted⏎Verily Comments for 1⏎HHS-ONC-2026-0001-001⏎⏎Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA), a critical tool enabling nationwide⏎data exchange. To increase the usefulness of TEFCA, there is growing momentum among key⏎stakeholders to ensure the consistency of data exchange activities for specific use cases.⏎However, more can and should be done to ensure that patients and providers - and increasingly⏎the AI tools they rely on - have access to complete, accurate data to inform point-of-care⏎decision making. For example, consistent and transparent application of information exchange⏎regulations, standards, services, and policies will help build trust among data exchange⏎partners. Relatedly, increasing the speed of enforcement actions may drive broader participation⏎in the ecosystem.⏎Additionally, we view the creation of incentives to modernize national data infrastructure and⏎information exchange as a key element to ensuring that AI tools can function safely and⏎effectively in real-world clinical settings. Fundamentally, the speed of data exchange will⏎become an important metric to realize the value of AI tools and their potential to improve patient⏎outcomes at a lower cost. The Fast Healthcare Interoperability Resources (HL7 FHIR) standard⏎can help scale information exchange and access to longitudinal data. FHIR provides the⏎modern, Application Programming Interface (API)-based 'piping' necessary to move data⏎efficiently and securely, however, FHIR implementation remains inconsistent across settings of⏎care delivery. While the HL7 FHIR Roadmap provides a directional north star, implementation⏎may remain slow and fragmented in the absence of incentives to modernize and ensure⏎consistent use of this data standard.⏎Lastly, for this data infrastructure to be effective, health data should also be discoverable. The⏎development of a provider directory is key for understanding participants in the network. We⏎believe it may be equally important for the network to have a more transparent understanding of⏎data assets available across the exchange environment. Robust metadata would be essential⏎for discoverability and setting standards could help support efficient data exchange activities.⏎2) Improving the Reliability and Quality of Data to Support Robust and Accurate Insights⏎from AI Tools and Analytics⏎While fostering more seamless nationwide data exchange is a critical step, improving data⏎reliability is equally important to accelerate the adoption of AI-enabled tools in care delivery.⏎Many AI tools being developed for care rely on data captured in clinical settings, and the fidelity⏎and validity of that data is essential to ensure AI safety and effectiveness. The value of data is⏎determined by how well it can inform and support its intended use. Capturing, curating, and⏎linking data that meets regulatory standards is a process that depends on an ecosystem of⏎stakeholders. The creation of incentives, including those that are financial and⏎reimbursement-related, will help improve both data exchange and reliability.⏎Verily Comments for 2⏎HHS-ONC-2026-0001-001⏎⏎We strongly urge the Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP/ONC) to maintain and⏎expand its leadership in national efforts to improve data quality. The United States Core Data for⏎Interoperability (USCDI) framework provides the essential technical and policy foundation⏎required for interoperability by establishing a consistent 'floor' for data expectations. The USCDI⏎seeks to eliminate the ambiguity that has historically hindered information sharing, but more⏎work is needed to push a common understanding of what “fit for purpose” data looks like in a⏎given context. For medical product development, the FDA has advanced thinking around use of⏎real-world data (RWD) and real-world evidence (RWE), outlining what may qualify as⏎regulatory-grade data. FDA’s considerations for data relevance and reliability could be more⏎firmly aligned with ASTP/ONC’s interoperability standards as well as with CMS’ innovative⏎payment models, enabling HHS to effectively build a robust health technology ecosystem that⏎encourages adoption of AI care tools.⏎3) Establishing National Risk-based Frameworks to Guide Regulation and Payment⏎We urge HHS to provide national leadership on standards and policies for AI development and⏎deployment in the healthcare system. A state by state approach will only fragment and slow⏎innovation as organizations struggle to implement varying sets of requirements, validation⏎methods, and regulations. A unified approach to performance evaluation, ongoing safety⏎monitoring, and payment will be essential for building trust in transformative AI tools.⏎To accelerate adoption of AI care tools, HHS’ development of an effective regulatory framework⏎that collectively considers an AI tool’s approval, post-market monitoring, and reimbursement is⏎critical. An effective regulatory framework for AI should be developed in partnership between⏎CMS, the FDA, ASTP/ONC, other relevant agencies and external stakeholders (e.g.,⏎professional societies, accreditation non-profits). The recently introduced CMS ACCESS⏎payment model and FDA TEMPO pilot provide an initial example in chronic care management⏎for how such a framework that integrates evaluation, monitoring, and payment could be⏎implemented.⏎We suggest that the approach to evaluation and real-world monitoring for AI-enabled care tools⏎be titrated to the level of risk exposed to the patient or consumer, building on how risk is⏎classified for SaMD.1 This could mean that a lighter regulatory touch, if necessary, is acceptable⏎for lower-risk AI tools, whereas higher-risk AI tools might require more continuous and⏎comprehensive data capture, mandatory reporting, or an expert in the loop to ensure ongoing⏎safety and effectiveness.⏎Payment models could align with a risk-based regulatory approach by incentivizing adoption of⏎AI care tools that are appropriately monitored according to their classified product risk level.⏎1 https://www.fda.gov/medical-devices/software-medical-device-samd/global-approach-software-medical-device⏎Verily Comments for 3⏎HHS-ONC-2026-0001-001⏎⏎Interagency harmonization of real-world performance monitoring across regulatory approval⏎requirements and payment structures could not only help regulators keep pace with the rapid⏎speed of innovation, but it could also improve efficiencies when implementing statutorily-defined⏎authorities across approval and payment. Additionally, this coordination would provide more⏎predictability and instill greater confidence so that AI developers continue to invest and⏎innovate.⏎Closing⏎Verily believes that appropriate, safe, and effective deployment of AI in care has enormous⏎potential to prevent and better manage disease, and accordingly, we are building technologies⏎that can effectively integrate multimodal health data sources. AI is already transforming how⏎care is delivered, and as AI continues to evolve, the importance of HHS’ leadership in this area⏎will only continue to grow.⏎Thank you for the opportunity to comment on these timely and critical topics. We applaud HHS’⏎commitment to ensuring accessible, high-quality care for all Americans, and its focus on⏎accelerating adoption of AI-enabled care. We look forward to partnering with the Administration⏎and other stakeholders to develop and implement innovative AI solutions that make this vision a⏎reality.⏎Sincerely,⏎Laura O’Donnell⏎Chief Legal Officer and Chief Policy Officer⏎Verily Life Sciences, LLC⏎Verily Comments for 4⏎HHS-ONC-2026-0001-001♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0500♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from International Business Machines (IBM), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-22T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔International Business Machines (IBM)♔♔♔♔♔♔International Business Machines (IBM) Corporation⏎Request for Information (RFI)⏎February 23, 2026⏎Department of Health and Human Services (HHS)⏎Office of the Secretary⏎Accelerating the Adoption and Use of⏎Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care⏎In Response to 90 FR 60108⏎Submitted by: International Business Machines (IBM) Corporation⏎6710 Rockledge Drive⏎Bethesda, MD 20817⏎Kristine Allen, MPH, PMP⏎Associate Partner, HHS⏎770.843.7486⏎Kristine.Allen@ibm.com⏎RESTRICTION ON DISCLOSURE AND USE OF DATA⏎This proposal includes data that shall not be disclosed outside the Government and shall not be duplicated, used or disclosed—in⏎whole or in part—for any purpose other than to evaluate this proposal. If, however, a contract is awarded to this offeror as a result⏎of—or in connection with—the submission of this data, the Government shall have the right to duplicate, use, or disclose the data⏎to the extent provided in the resulting contract. This restriction does not limit the Government’s rights to use information⏎contained in this data if it is obtained from another source without restriction. The data subject to this restriction are contained on⏎pages marked: “Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject to the restriction on the title page of this proposal.”⏎The data in the pages of this proposal where so annotated contain trade secrets and commercial or financial information that are⏎either specifically, exempted from disclosure by statute or privileged or confidential within the meaning of the exemption set⏎forth in Sections 552(b)(3) and (4), respectively, of the Freedom of Information Act, 5 U.S.C. 552, the disclosure of which could⏎invoke criminal sanctions of 18 U.S.C. 1905.⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎Table of Contents⏎Section Page⏎0. Introduction...........................................................................................................................1⏎1. Barriers to AI Innovation and Adoption (RFI Question #1)............................................1⏎2. HHS Regulatory Priorities (RFI Question #2)...................................................................2⏎3. Governance and Accountability (RFI Question #3)..........................................................3⏎4. Evaluation and Robustness (RFI Question #4)..................................................................5⏎5. Supporting Private Sector Innovation (RFI Question #5)................................................5⏎6. Successes, Lessons Learned, and Priority Areas for AI in Clinical Care (RFI⏎Question #6)...........................................................................................................................6⏎7. AI Adoption Dynamics in Health Care Organizations (RFI Question #7)......................9⏎8. Interoperability as a Catalyst (RFI Question #8)...............................................................9⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Perspectives (RFI Question #9)...................................................10⏎10. Research Priorities (RFI Question #10)............................................................................11⏎11. Conclusion...........................................................................................................................12⏎List of Figures⏎Figure 1 - IBM's AI Governance Framework.............................................................................4⏎Figure 2 Case Study: From AI Model to Compliant Medical Device.......................................7⏎Figure 3 IBM Consulting Advantage Platform – Centralized AI Control Tower⏎Enabling Scalable, Federated Governance.........................................................................8⏎Figure 4 – Examples of Interoperability Driving AI Innovation.............................................10⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page i⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎0. Introduction⏎A report published by the IBM Institute for Business Value (IBV) in August 2025 found that 77%⏎of healthcare executives report AI is delivering clear and measurable competitive advantage, and⏎69% expect it to contribute significantly to financial performance within the next three years. AI⏎in clinical care has moved beyond proof-of-concept experimentation and now presents an⏎implementation challenge. The central barriers to adoption are no longer model capability, but⏎structural conditions: payment alignment, regulatory clarity, interoperable data infrastructure, and⏎mature governance frameworks. HHS is uniquely positioned to address these systemic constraints.⏎By modernizing reimbursement policy, establishing adaptive regulatory pathways, and enforcing⏎standards-based interoperability, HHS can accelerate safe and scalable AI deployment in clinical⏎care while reinforcing public trust.⏎IBM Consulting, together with IBM’s Technology and Research divisions, brings decades of⏎experience supporting federal health agencies, academic medical centers, integrated delivery⏎networks, life sciences organizations, and payers in implementing and advancing AI within highly⏎regulated healthcare environments. Drawing on this cross-sector experience, we offer practical⏎insights into how policy, reimbursement, governance, and technology must align to translate⏎strategy into operational impact. We look forward to continued collaboration with HHS to advance⏎a trusted, scalable, and patient-centered AI ecosystem that strengthens clinical care delivery.⏎1. Barriers to AI Innovation and Adoption (RFI Question #1)⏎The most significant barriers to private sector innovation and clinical adoption of AI are⏎structural rather than technical. Regulatory fragmentation remains a persistent obstacle.⏎Developers and health systems must navigate overlapping and occasionally inconsistent⏎requirements across FDA, CMS, NIH, and ASTP/ONC. This ambiguity increases compliance⏎costs, delays commercialization, and creates disproportionate burden for smaller innovators.⏎Even when AI tools demonstrate measurable clinical value, predictable reimbursement pathways⏎are often absent. Medicare regulations do not consistently recognize or value AI-enabled⏎diagnostic interpretation and decision support services. As a result, providers face financial⏎disincentives to adopt tools that require integration, monitoring, and governance oversight.⏎Adoption therefore remains concentrated in well-capitalized health systems, exacerbating⏎disparities in access to advanced care capabilities.⏎Data access and interoperability limitations further constrain innovation. Although progress⏎has been made under the 21st Century Cures Act, clinical data remain fragmented across⏎electronic health record platforms and proprietary systems. Variability in FHIR implementation⏎and restrictive data-use practices limit the ability to train, validate, and benchmark AI models at⏎scale. Significant engineering effort is frequently diverted toward data normalization rather than⏎performance validation, bias mitigation, or workflow integration.⏎Workforce readiness remains a challenge, with gaps in trust and digital literacy among⏎clinicians. Many providers lack formal training in algorithmic performance metrics, bias⏎evaluation, or appropriate-use boundaries, limiting their ability to critically interpret AI-⏎generated outputs. Concerns regarding patient safety, liability exposure, and accountability for⏎AI-influenced decisions further suppress confidence, particularly in environments where⏎governance frameworks and oversight mechanisms are not clearly defined.⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 1⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎Clinical workflow alignment also represents material barriers. AI tools that are not⏎embedded seamlessly into care pathways risk increasing documentation burden or contributing⏎to alert fatigue. Fragmented implementation can also create ambiguity regarding responsibility⏎for acting on recommendations, particularly in team-based care environments. Without tight⏎integration with existing clinical workflow, AI systems risk being treated as optional overlays⏎rather than embedded components of clinical decision-making.⏎2. HHS Regulatory Priorities (RFI Question #2)⏎Based on IBM’s experiences supporting providers, payers, regulators, and industry partners,⏎HHS should prioritize regulatory and payment policy changes that directly address the structural⏎barriers preventing AI from being deployed at scale in routine clinical care.⏎CMS should urgently modernize reimbursement frameworks to recognize AI-assisted⏎diagnostic interpretation and clinical decision support as covered and appropriately valued⏎services under Medicare Part B. This includes clarifying coverage under 42 CFR Part 410,⏎establishing valuation methodologies under the Physician Fee Schedule (42 CFR Part 414), and⏎updating National Coverage Determinations to address AI tools systematically. AI adoption⏎should also be aligned with value-based care by expanding incentives under the Medicare Shared⏎Savings Program (42 CFR Part 425) and updating Merit-based Incentive Payment System⏎(MIPS) measures (42 CFR Part 414.1305) to recognize measurable improvements in cost,⏎quality, safety, and coordination attributable to validated AI tools.⏎FDA regulatory modernization is equally critical, particularly for developers seeking to⏎deploy adaptive AI tools responsibly. Current approval pathways under 21 CFR Part 814 and 21⏎CFR Part 807 are designed for static technologies and do not accommodate continuously⏎learning algorithms without repeated, costly resubmissions. HHS should formalize the use of⏎predetermined change control plans and establish AI-specific Software as a Medical Device⏎guidance under 21 CFR Part 807, enabling algorithm updates within defined boundaries. In⏎parallel, FDA should refine device classification criteria under 21 CFR Part 860.7 to account for⏎clinical impact and the degree of human oversight, while shifting greater emphasis toward post-⏎market, real-world performance monitoring.⏎Interoperability and data access reforms are foundational to both AI development and⏎deployment. HHS should strengthen ONC Health IT Certification requirements under 45 CFR⏎Part 170 to mandate standardized API access to all clinically relevant data types, including⏎imaging and longitudinal records, and require comprehensive FHIR implementation across EHR-⏎generated data. At the same time, HHS should expand information blocking prohibitions under⏎45 CFR Part 171.103 to ensure that legitimate data access for AI development and validation is⏎not improperly restricted. Clarifying HIPAA de-identification standards under 45 CFR Part⏎164.514 would further augment AI development by providing clearer safe harbors for training⏎data, while expedited pathways for minimal-risk validation studies under 45 CFR Part 46 would⏎reduce unnecessary research friction.⏎Beyond agency-specific reforms, HHS should establish a coordinated, risk-based regulatory⏎framework aligning the roles of FDA, CMS, ONC, and OCR. Oversight should be tiered⏎according to clinical risk, with rigorous standards for high-risk decision support tools and⏎proportionate governance for lower-risk operational applications. Transparency and bias⏎mitigation requirements could be incorporated into hospital Conditions of Participation (42 CFR⏎Part 482), alongside clearer federal guidance on liability and standard-of-care considerations.⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 2⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎Finally, workforce development should be advanced through graduate medical education⏎incentives and expanded federal support for AI validation, implementation science, and equity⏎research.⏎Collectively, these reforms would directly augment providers’ and developers’ ability to deploy⏎effective AI in clinical care while maintaining appropriate safeguards for safety, equity, and⏎accountability.⏎3. Governance and Accountability (RFI Question #3)⏎The integration of AI into clinical care introduces governance and accountability challenges that⏎extend beyond traditional health IT oversight. Unlike static clinical software, AI systems may⏎evolve over time, rely on probabilistic outputs, and depend heavily on the quality and⏎representativeness of training data. These characteristics raise novel questions related to⏎liability, transparency, bias mitigation, lifecycle monitoring, and shared responsibility⏎among developers, providers, and institutions.⏎HHS should play a central coordinating and standard-setting role in addressing these⏎challenges. Federal leadership is necessary to establish consistent national expectations, ensure⏎risk-proportionate oversight, and safeguard equity across the healthcare ecosystem while⏎avoiding fragmentation across agencies and states.⏎IBM’s AI Governance framework (Figure 1) provides a useful operational blueprint,⏎demonstrating how policy objectives can be translated into practice through the integration of⏎enterprise-level governance structures with continuous AI technical lifecycle management,⏎aligning institutional accountability with ongoing AI model validation, monitoring, and⏎oversight.⏎At the organizational level, this framework emphasizes maintaining a comprehensive AI⏎inventory that documents system purpose, risk classification, validation evidence, intended use⏎boundaries, and known limitations. It incorporates structured risk-tiering to differentiate⏎administrative automation tools from clinical decision support systems and higher-risk⏎autonomous or semi-autonomous applications. Clearly defined governance bodies and decision⏎rights guide procurement, validation, deployment, monitoring, and retirement, ensuring⏎accountability across clinical, technical, compliance, and executive leadership.⏎At the technical level, the framework extends governance across the full AI lifecycle. This⏎includes rigorous pre-deployment validation, ongoing post-deployment performance monitoring,⏎version control, documentation of training data provenance, and formal processes for detecting⏎and addressing model drift, performance degradation, or emerging bias. Ongoing subgroup⏎performance monitoring promotes equitable outcomes across populations. Transparency is⏎operationalized through structured documentation of model assumptions, context-appropriate⏎explainability mechanisms, and auditable records of system behavior, reinforcing both regulatory⏎compliance and public trust.⏎At IBM, dedicated Safety Officers play a central role in supporting this model by providing⏎independent assurance, overseeing safety cases, monitoring AI‑related risks, and supporting⏎incident management and escalation. Human‑in‑the‑loop oversight remains mandatory for⏎safety‑critical use cases, supported by full auditability and traceability of AI outputs. Governance⏎bodies continuously monitor performance, bias, and unintended impacts, enabling rapid response⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 3⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎to emerging risks, new evidence, or policy change while maintaining trust and regulatory⏎confidence.⏎Figure 1 - IBM's AI Governance Framework⏎HHS has a critical role in promoting consistent governance expectations and best practices⏎across institutions. Robust governance and accountability frameworks are essential to the safe⏎and effective use of AI in healthcare and should be embedded by design rather than applied⏎retrospectively. This includes formal clinical risk assessment, clearly defined accountability⏎across the AI lifecycle, and multidisciplinary oversight spanning clinical, technical, policy, and⏎operational leadership. Clinical governance should be embedded directly within the AI enterprise⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 4⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎platform, ensuring that safety, auditability, compliance, and traceability are native architectural⏎and operational capabilities that scale consistently as AI systems evolve.⏎4. Evaluation and Robustness (RFI Question #4)⏎AI systems used in clinical care require rigorous, lifecycle-based evaluation methodologies⏎that extend beyond traditional software testing. Evaluation should be conceptualized as a⏎continuous process rather than a one-time validation event.⏎Pre-deployment validation must assess clinical performance in realistic conditions. This⏎includes sensitivity, specificity, positive predictive value, and calibration across relevant patient⏎populations. Subgroup equity analysis should be mandatory for systems influencing diagnosis,⏎treatment selection, or care prioritization. Robust testing should evaluate performance under data⏎perturbation, missingness, and potential adversarial inputs. Importantly, validation should⏎include workflow simulation to determine how outputs are interpreted and acted upon in⏎practice, not merely whether predictions are statistically accurate.⏎Post-deployment monitoring is equally critical. AI performance may degrade over time due to⏎changes in patient populations, clinical practice patterns, data collection methods, or external⏎factors. Continuous monitoring should track drift in input distributions, outcome divergence,⏎override patterns by clinicians, and unexpected error clusters. Monitoring systems should support⏎rapid root-cause analysis and structured remediation pathways. For higher-risk applications,⏎periodic revalidation or recalibration thresholds should be defined in advance.⏎Human-centered evaluation must complement technical metrics. Clinical AI effectiveness⏎depends on comprehension, trust calibration, and usability. Evaluation should therefore assess⏎clinician understanding of model outputs, alignment with cognitive workflow, and impact on⏎decision confidence. Metrics such as alert fatigue rates, time-to-decision, and override frequency⏎can provide insight into real-world integration quality. Patient-centered measures, including⏎understanding of AI-supported recommendations and satisfaction with transparency, are also⏎important components of trust-building.⏎HHS can meaningfully advance evaluation standards through a mix of grants, cooperative⏎agreements, prize competitions, and targeted contract vehicles. Grants and cooperative⏎agreements are well suited to academic-led methodological research and the development of⏎standardized benchmarking frameworks. In contrast, contract mechanisms may be more effective⏎for building shared benchmarking infrastructure, reference implementations, and standardized⏎evaluation toolkits that require clearly defined deliverables, timelines, and cross-sector technical⏎coordination.⏎Longitudinal studies examining clinical outcomes, cost implications, health equity impacts, and⏎workforce effects will be essential to inform reimbursement reform and regulatory⏎modernization. These efforts may benefit from blended funding models that combine academic⏎research rigor with industry implementation expertise. Establishing common evaluation norms⏎through coordinated funding approaches will reduce variability in institutional review processes⏎and support safe, scalable adoption of AI in clinical care.⏎5. Supporting Private Sector Innovation (RFI Question #5)⏎HHS can materially accelerate Clinical Care AI development and adoption by harmonizing⏎governance expectations across agencies and establishing a predictable evaluation⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 5⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎environment. A unified cross-agency AI governance framework would reduce duplication and⏎clarify expectations regarding risk classification, transparency requirements, bias evaluation, and⏎post-deployment monitoring. Aligning these expectations across FDA, CMS, NIH, and⏎ASTP/ONC would significantly reduce uncertainty for developers and providers. HHS continues⏎to collaborate and build their AI council with a comprehensive AI governance program to ensure⏎the responsible and ethical use of AI technologies. HHS should focus on effective AI governance⏎which transcends technical solutions, requiring a holistic approach encompassing societal, ethical,⏎and organizational considerations alongside technical aspects.⏎The creation of a national AI testing and evaluation infrastructure would further lower⏎barriers to innovation. Standardized benchmarking datasets, including synthetic and de-⏎identified real-world data, combined with FHIR-native interoperability testing suites and⏎workflow-based simulation environments, would allow consistent safety and performance⏎validation. Such infrastructure would particularly benefit smaller innovators who lack access to⏎large-scale validation environments.⏎Accreditation and certification mechanisms can also strengthen trust. HHS could support⏎model certification programs aligned with ONC Health IT certification, vendor accreditation⏎standards focused on responsible AI development practices, and clinician credentialing pathways⏎that improve AI literacy. These mechanisms would help create consistent quality expectations⏎across the ecosystem.⏎Finally, aligning AI adoption with value-based care objectives is essential. Incorporating AI-⏎enabled outcome improvements into CMS quality programs and expanding reimbursement⏎pathways for validated AI tools would directly link adoption to measurable clinical and financial⏎performance.⏎6. Successes, Lessons Learned, and Priority Areas for AI in Clinical Care⏎(RFI Question #6)⏎Where AI Succeeds in Healthcare⏎Based on IBM’s experience supporting healthcare providers, payers, and life sciences⏎organizations, AI tools in clinical environments tend to meet or exceed expectations when⏎deployed in well-defined workflows with clear performance metrics and strong human⏎oversight. Administrative and operational applications, such as documentation support, coding⏎assistance, prior authorization analytics, scheduling optimization, and revenue cycle intelligence,⏎have consistently demonstrated measurable improvements in cycle time, throughput, and staff⏎productivity. In these contexts, outputs are verifiable, risk tolerance is manageable, and success⏎metrics are concrete. Similarly, AI applied to bounded clinical tasks, such as imaging triage,⏎signal interpretation, deterioration risk scoring within defined populations, and medication safety⏎surveillance, has shown strong performance when integrated into existing workflows and paired⏎with clinician review. These tools add value by improving prioritization, consistency, and early⏎detection rather than replacing clinical judgment. For instance, IBM has successfully deployed⏎AI-enabled scheduling, workflow optimization, and operational analytics platforms that⏎improved throughput and reduced administrative burden in UK hospitals. These deployments⏎achieved measurable gains because they were embedded within existing workflows, aligned with⏎organizational governance structures, and evaluated against defined operational metrics such as⏎cycle time, staffing efficiency, and quality performance indicators.⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 6⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎Case Study: From AI Model to Compliant Medical Device⏎IBM collaborated with a Swiss life sciences company to develop an AI-powered medical⏎device for diabetes management that met EU regulatory standards. The project⏎exemplified how AI predictions become regulated, safe user experiences through⏎rigorous engineering Trustworthy AI controls such as robustness testing, explainability,⏎and uncertainty communication to reduce false prediction and improve transparency.⏎Key Success Factors⏎Cross-functional operating model: Integrated teams spanning data science, engineering,⏎regulatory affairs, and clinical expertise⏎Evidence pipeline mindset: Continuous generation of audit-ready artifacts throughout the⏎development lifecycle⏎Reusable assets: Framework templates, model documentation, V&V test suites, and⏎monitoring dashboards⏎Outcome⏎This approach enabled faster iteration under control, delivering innovation without⏎sacrificing safety, demonstrating that regulatory compliance and agility are not mutually⏎exclusive.⏎Figure 2 Case Study: From AI Model to Compliant Medical Device⏎AI has also performed well in population health and value-based care settings, where⏎predictive analytics support risk stratification, gap-in-care identification, and targeted outreach.⏎When organizations have operational mechanisms to act on these insights, such as care⏎management programs and closed-loop referral processes, AI-enabled interventions can reduce⏎avoidable utilization and improve quality measure performance.⏎Where AI Falls Short⏎Conversely, AI tools have fallen short when introduced into complex, high-stakes clinical⏎decision-making without sufficient integration, explainability, and governance. Systems⏎positioned as standalone diagnostic or treatment engines often struggle with real-world⏎variability in data quality, patient populations, and evolving standards of care. Even technically⏎accurate models underperform if they generate excessive false positives, lack contextual⏎explanation, or disrupt clinical workflow. In addition, organizations sometimes overestimate⏎cost savings by focusing on model development without investing in process redesign,⏎training, monitoring infrastructure, and accountability structures required to⏎operationalize insights. Without clear ownership of downstream actions and ongoing lifecycle⏎oversight, early gains can diminish over time.⏎Priority Areas for Future Development⏎Looking forward, the AI tools with the greatest potential to improve outcomes and reduce⏎costs are those that combine predictive intelligence with governed workflow orchestration.⏎Agent-enabled systems that synthesize longitudinal patient data and guideline-based evidence,⏎while preserving clear human oversight, can support more coordinated, proactive care. Evidence-⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 7⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎grounded clinical intelligence and personalized treatment sequencing models, particularly in⏎chronic and specialty care, can reduce unwarranted variation, minimize trial-and-error⏎prescribing, and lower avoidable adverse events.⏎IBM’s national-scale advisory work with NHS England demonstrates that accelerating AI⏎adoption in clinical care requires coordinated strategy, shared governance, and operational⏎alignment across a federated system. Fragmented, use-case pilots frequently fail to scale, leading⏎to duplication, inconsistent assurance, and “pilot fatigue.” Establishing common standards and⏎enterprise-level oversight early enables safe, repeatable deployment while maintaining clinical⏎safety, regulatory confidence, and public trust. This has driven a shift toward enterprise AI⏎platforms with embedded governance and operational controls. Recent large-scale⏎deployments across global enterprises and public-sector organizations such as Scuderia Ferrari,⏎Riyadh Air, demonstrate that centralized operating models, providing visibility, performance⏎monitoring, and policy enforcement, enable scalable and trusted AI. Healthcare organizations are⏎now piloting IBM Consulting Advantage Platform (Figure 3) as a centralized “control tower,”⏎supporting overall governance consistency while preserving local flexibility and enabling AI to⏎scale beyond perpetual pilots.⏎Ultimately, AI delivers sustained value when deployed within a governed, interoperable, and⏎outcome-oriented ecosystem. Aligning reimbursement, regulatory oversight, interoperability⏎standards, and institutional governance is essential to unlocking the full impact of these⏎capabilities.⏎Figure 3 IBM Consulting Advantage Platform – Centralized AI Control Tower Enabling⏎Scalable, Federated Governance⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 8⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎7. AI Adoption Dynamics in Health Care Organizations (RFI Question #7)⏎Adoption of AI in clinical care is shaped as much by institutional dynamics as by technical merit.⏎Within healthcare organizations, decision-making authority is distributed across clinical⏎leadership, digital and information technology leadership, data governance bodies,⏎compliance committees, and financial executives. Chief Medical Officers often determine⏎whether AI tools are clinically credible and aligned with standards of care, while Chief⏎Information or Digital Officers assess infrastructure readiness and integration feasibility. Finance⏎committees evaluate return on investment and budget trade-offs, and compliance and privacy⏎officers scrutinize risk exposure. Adoption frequently stalls when these constituencies are not⏎aligned or when accountability boundaries are unclear.⏎One of the primary administrative hurdles is limited AI literacy across clinical and⏎operational leadership. Without a shared understanding of capabilities, limitations, and⏎governance safeguards, decision-makers may either overestimate risk or place inappropriate trust⏎in system outputs. Structured education initiatives, including executive briefings, clinician⏎training modules, and competency frameworks, can significantly reduce hesitation and improve⏎responsible use.⏎Another barrier is the absence of standardized internal evaluation pathways. Many⏎institutions apply procurement processes designed for traditional medical devices to AI software⏎that may iterate more rapidly. This can lead to prolonged approval cycles and inconsistent review⏎criteria. Establishing streamlined AI review committees with defined evaluation frameworks,⏎risk-tiering protocols, and clear escalation pathways would improve efficiency while maintaining⏎safety oversight.⏎Budget constraints also play a significant role. AI deployment requires investment in⏎integration, monitoring infrastructure, cybersecurity controls, and ongoing governance. Without⏎reimbursement alignment or shared savings incentives, these investments may compete⏎unfavorably with other priorities such as staffing, EHR modernization, or cybersecurity⏎upgrades. Linking AI adoption to value-based care metrics and outcome improvements is⏎therefore essential to support internal business justification.⏎Workflow redesign represents an additional challenge. AI systems can inadvertently⏎reinforce inefficient processes if underlying documentation or care pathways are poorly⏎structured. Successful adoption requires parallel workflow optimization and change management⏎efforts to ensure that AI augments rather than disrupts clinical operations.⏎Sustained AI adoption in healthcare requires clear, measurable ROI, including revenue⏎improvement, cost reduction, efficiency gains, throughput, quality, and safety outcomes.⏎Standardized KPIs and baseline benchmarking are essential to link deployment to value; without⏎demonstrable impact, initiatives rarely scale beyond pilots.⏎8. Interoperability as a Catalyst (RFI Question #8)⏎Enhanced interoperability, anchored in HL7 FHIR-based APIs and nationally standardized⏎datasets, delivers outsized downstream impact by providing the high-volume, high-variety, and⏎well-labeled data AI requires. Standardized data access enables clinical AI model training (e.g.,⏎early disease detection, risk stratification, medication optimization), supports enterprise-scale⏎learning through Bulk FHIR for large cohorts and federated approaches, and significantly⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 9⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎reduces data engineering effort, allowing teams to focus on feature development, validation, and⏎deployment rather than ingestion.⏎Figure 4 includes a few examples that demonstrate how enhanced interoperability translates into⏎concrete datasets, data flows, and actionable AI use cases across clinical care, research, and⏎healthcare operations. Collectively, they show that interoperability is not simply about improving⏎data exchange; it is a foundational enabler that materially accelerates the development, rigorous⏎evaluation, and scalable deployment of AI in clinical care.⏎Figure 4 – Examples of Interoperability Driving AI Innovation⏎9. Patient and Caregiver Perspectives (RFI Question #9)⏎Patients and caregivers generally view AI in clinical care through a practical lens: they are less⏎concerned with the technical architecture of models and more concerned with whether AI⏎meaningfully improves access, accuracy, timeliness, and coordination of care. Many patients⏎wish to see AI address persistent systemic challenges, including long wait times for specialty⏎care, delayed diagnoses, fragmented medical records, administrative burden, and inconsistent⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 10⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎communication across providers. AI-supported triage tools, early detection systems, and care⏎coordination platforms hold promise in reducing diagnostic delays, preventing avoidable⏎hospitalizations, and improving medication safety. For caregivers managing chronic or complex⏎conditions, AI-enabled monitoring and predictive analytics may provide earlier warning of⏎deterioration, improved treatment personalization, and better alignment across multidisciplinary⏎care teams.⏎Patients are also interested in more personalized and proactive care. AI has the potential to⏎synthesize longitudinal health records, genomic information, and lifestyle data to support⏎individualized treatment recommendations rather than one-size-fits-all protocols. In addition, AI-⏎driven automation of administrative processes, such as prior authorization, scheduling, and⏎documentation, could reduce delays and free clinicians to spend more time in direct patient⏎interaction. From the patient perspective, the most compelling value proposition is not⏎technological sophistication but improved outcomes, clearer communication, and a more⏎responsive care experience.⏎At the same time, significant concerns remain. Transparency is paramount: patients want⏎understandable explanations of how AI-informed recommendations are generated and what their⏎limitations are. Equity is another major concern, particularly the risk that models trained on⏎non-representative data could reinforce disparities. Privacy and data use practices must be⏎clearly governed and communicated, especially regarding secondary use of health information.⏎Patients also emphasize that AI should augment, not replace, clinician judgment. Preserving⏎the human relationship in care and maintaining clear accountability when errors occur are⏎essential to sustaining trust. Ultimately, adoption will depend on whether AI demonstrably⏎improves outcomes and experience while maintaining transparency, fairness, privacy, and⏎meaningful human oversight.⏎10.Research Priorities (RFI Question #10)⏎HHS should prioritize AI research areas that directly strengthen clinical decision-making⏎by connecting upstream biomedical evidence to point-of-care actions. A particularly high-⏎impact area is AI-enabled integration of the full therapeutic lifecycle, from preclinical discovery⏎through regulatory approval and post-market evidence, into clinically usable intelligence.⏎Advances in Large Language Models (LLMs) and Agentic AI make it technically feasible to⏎compress and contextualize vast bodies of heterogeneous data (e.g., molecular mechanisms, New⏎Alternative Methodologies (NAMs), clinical trial outcomes, and regulatory submissions) and⏎surface them at the moment a clinician is selecting among therapeutic options. Prioritizing⏎research that enables traceability and explainability across this lifecycle would help clinicians⏎move beyond trial-and-error prescribing toward more informed decisions, reducing avoidable⏎treatment failures and downstream harm.⏎HHS should also invest in AI research focused on agentic AI systems with long-term⏎memory, reasoning, and workflow orchestration for clinical care. Unlike static decision⏎support tools, agentic systems can autonomously synthesize patient-specific data with evolving⏎scientific evidence, guidelines, and real-world outcomes. The complexity and scale of data⏎generated by modern drug development, particularly human-relevant NAMs and multi-omic⏎cellular models, exceed what individual clinicians can reasonably internalize. Research into⏎agentic architectures that can reason across these data sources, maintain provenance, and adapt⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 11⏎⏎HHS RFI - AI in Clinical Care⏎90 FR 60108⏎February 23, 2026⏎recommendations as new evidence emerges would materially enhance clinical judgment without⏎supplanting it.⏎Finally, HHS should prioritize AI research that enables personalized therapeutic selection⏎and sequencing, especially in conditions where the order of treatment materially affects⏎outcomes. Prescribing the “wrong” drug first can reduce the effectiveness of subsequent⏎therapies due to physiological effects, even when all prescribing is technically permissible. AI⏎models that combine mechanistic biology, patient-specific characteristics, and historical response⏎patterns could help clinicians identify the most appropriate initial therapy rather than relying on⏎iterative trial-and-error. Targeted federal investment in this area would accelerate adoption by⏎aligning AI capabilities with clinicians’ real decision points, while advancing HHS goals of⏎improved outcomes, reduced waste, and greater trust in AI-supported clinical care.⏎11.Conclusion⏎AI will not scale in clinical care through technical advancement alone. Sustainable adoption⏎requires coordinated reform across reimbursement policy, regulatory oversight, interoperability⏎standards, governance structures, and workforce readiness. When these structural enablers are⏎aligned, AI can improve diagnostic accuracy, personalize treatment selection, reduce⏎administrative burden, and strengthen care coordination, while maintaining safety, equity, and⏎transparency.⏎IBM is well positioned to support HHS in advancing this effort. Our cross-sector experience⏎working with federal agencies, healthcare delivery organizations, life sciences companies, and⏎payers enables us to translate policy intent into practical, operational implementation. We bring a⏎deep understanding of the technical, regulatory, and organizational complexities involved in⏎embedding AI responsibly into clinical and operational workflows, while balancing innovation⏎with safety, governance, and accountability. We would also welcome the opportunity to partner⏎with HHS through discovery, user-centered design, and proof of value to help shape, test, and⏎scale solutions in a way that is grounded in real-world needs and delivers measurable impact⏎across the system.⏎We look forward to continued collaboration with HHS to help build a trusted, scalable, and⏎patient-centered AI ecosystem that strengthens clinical care delivery nationwide while upholding⏎the highest standards of safety, equity, and public trust.⏎Use or disclosure of data contained on this sheet is subject⏎to the restriction on the title page of this proposal. Page 12♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0501♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from McKay, Rachel  K, HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Rachel K♔McKay♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔As a clinical psychologist with many years of experience providing therapy as well as supervising and teaching therapists, I am writing to express the strongest possible concern about the current rush to approve the us of AI in the provision of healthcare, especially psychotherapy. What is most clear from the psychotherapy literature is that the relationship between the therapist and client is most predictive of a successful treatment. AI agents are not humans, and there is increasing reason to be concerned that people developing emotional connections to such programs is destructive and dangerous. The profit motive, for the companies marketing these agents, is not a substitute for rigorous research and a human-centered view of what constitutes healthcare – especially mental health treatment  -  for people in their times of greatest need.⏎♔♔♔♔♔♔♔pageCount, Page Count, Number of pages In the content file
UID-0502♔HHS♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001♔♔Public Submission♔2026-02-24T05:00Z♔false♔♔♔Comment from American Health Care Association/National Center for Assisted Living (AHCA/NCAL), HHS-ONC-2026-0001, HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001, 2025-23641♔♔♔false♔HHS-ONC-2026-0001-0001♔♔♔♔2026-02-23T05:00Z♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔Public Comment♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔1♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔♔American Health Care Association/National Center for Assisted Living (AHCA/NCAL)♔♔♔♔♔♔February 23, 2026⏎VIA Electronic Submission to https://www.regulations.gov/⏎Thomas Keane, MD, MBA⏎Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy and the Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information⏎Technology,⏎Department of Health and Human Services,⏎Mary E. Switzer Building⏎7033A, 330 C Street SW,⏎Washington, DC 20201⏎Re: Request for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical⏎Care [RIN 0955–AA13]⏎The American Health Care Association and National Center for Assisted Living (AHCA/NCAL) represent over⏎15,100 long term and post-acute care facilities, or 1.1 million skilled nursing facility (SNF) beds and over⏎300,000 assisted living (AL) beds. With such a membership base, the Association represents the majority of⏎SNFs and a rapidly growing number of assisted living communities as well as residences for individuals with⏎intellectual and developmental disabilities (ID/DD). We appreciate the opportunity to comment on the Request⏎for Information: Accelerating the Adoption and Use of Artificial Intelligence as Part of Clinical Care [RIN⏎0955–AA13].⏎We agree that effective and responsible adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) clinical technology has the⏎potential to empower care providers, patients and their families and caregivers to make informed decisions for⏎their health and well-being and welcome this discussion of the market of digital health products for Medicare⏎beneficiaries as well as the state of data interoperability and broader health technology infrastructure. However,⏎any discussion of AI adoption must reinforce that reimbursement, interoperability, and population⏎relevance are the binding constraints for long-term and post-acute care (LTPAC) providers.⏎We believe that it is imperative that the federal government provide leadership, governance, and support to⏎encourage more widespread adoption of accessible, safe, secure, and user-friendly interoperable digital health⏎technology for providers and their patients. The Assistant Secretary for Technology Policy (ASTP)/Office of the⏎National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC) (collectively, ASTP/ONC), in coordination⏎with the Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS) and other agencies within the interconnected health⏎information ecosystem, are best positioned to provide the leadership to facilitate infrastructure progress enable⏎beneficiary access to effective digital capabilities needed to make informed health decisions, and to increase⏎secure health information data availability for all stakeholders contributing to health outcomes.⏎We are eager to work with ASTP/ONC in identifying a pathway to finding clinical AI solutions that are⏎attainable to all stakeholders across the information health technology ecosystem. If you have questions about⏎any of our comments, please contact Daniel Ciolek at dciolek@ahca.org.⏎Sincerely,⏎Daniel E Ciolek⏎Associate Vice President, Therapy Advocacy⏎⏎RFI Summary⏎This RFI outlines the Department of Health and Human Services (HHS) strategy to support the responsible⏎adoption of artificial intelligence (AI) in clinical care, focusing on regulation, reimbursement, and research⏎& development.⏎HHS aims to establish a clear, predictable, and risk-based regulatory approach for clinical AI that enables rapid⏎innovation while safeguarding patient safety, protecting identifiable health information, and maintaining public⏎trust. The agency is seeking input on how existing regulations affect the development and use of AI in clinical⏎settings.⏎The document emphasizes that current fee-for-service payment systems often slow innovation, encourage⏎inefficiencies, and fail to align spending with value. HHS highlights the need to modernize reimbursement⏎policies, so payers are incentivized to support high-value AI clinical interventions, promote competition among⏎AI developers, and improve access and affordability for patients. Feedback is requested on payment policy⏎reforms to achieve these outcomes.⏎Finally, HHS underscores its role in supporting one of the world’s largest health research ecosystems. Through⏎investments in applied AI research, implementation science, public-private partnerships, and cooperative⏎research agreements, the agency seeks to accelerate the translation of AI technologies from concept to clinical⏎use, creating long-term market opportunities and improving people’s health and wellbeing.⏎Who We Are and Who AHCA/NCAL Members Provide Care To⏎Before we can provide responses to the ten specific questions this RFI seeks comment on, we believe it is⏎important to share contextual information about our AHCA/NCAL provider members, the resident populations⏎they serve, their current fragmented baseline digital capabilities, and historical financial, legislative, and⏎regulatory barriers to initiating or accelerating the adoption of and use of AI as part of clinical care. We believe⏎a critical issue that ASTP/ONC needs to consider as strategies are developed in response to the comments⏎received to this RFI, is that increased adoption of AI in clinical care will remain extremely challenging if not⏎impossible for LTPAC providers without first providing adequate support to eliminate the digital interoperability⏎infrastructure gaps. True functional interoperability capacity across the healthcare ecosystem would provide the⏎comprehensive information necessary to permit a secure, safe, and effective use of AI in clinical care.⏎1. SNF/NF, AL, and ID/DD Provider Setting Profiles and the Populations of Individuals Served⏎Of importance, and relevant to our comments, is that unlike other healthcare facility-based provider settings that⏎address acute and sub-acute conditions for a limited time-window, or office-based primary care and other⏎practitioners that encounter patients intermittently, most of the individuals our members care for are long-term⏎residents – it is their home. Except for a portion of SNF short-stay post-acute patients who require certain⏎nursing and/or rehabilitation therapy services to return to the community, most people in nursing facilities⏎(SNF/NF) and those in AL or ID/DD residences require residential care to manage chronic conditions reflected⏎through physical and/or cognitive impairments.⏎Regarding the long-term and post-acute care (LTPAC) provider settings represented by our membership, we⏎first describe the types of healthcare and healthcare-related services they furnish and the characteristics of the⏎residents they serve. Next, we highlight the diversity of payor sources since a provider’s reliance on fixed⏎federal and state payors impacts a provider’s capacity to assume the significant hardware, software, and⏎administrative costs associated with interoperable digital health technology, including AI. Finally, we share⏎information regarding the diversity of the resident population across the SNF, AL, and ID/DD provider⏎community as a reflection of the digital health needs footprint of these residents within the health information⏎technology (HIT) ecosystem.⏎2⏎⏎Most of the 14,742 SNFs nationwide serve a dual purpose with 93 percent being dually certified for furnishing⏎Medicare and Medicaid services. As mentioned above, SNFs provide short-term post-acute services to patients⏎who require skilled nursing and/or rehabilitation services on an inpatient basis. Additionally, SNF’s/NFs furnish⏎long-term care for residents requiring 24/7 care due to various medical, mobility, activities of daily living,⏎cognition, and behavioral challenges that cannot be addressed adequately in the community. Health care services⏎from various professions may be furnished to residents in the facility, at the offsite office or facility, or via⏎telehealth and remote patient monitoring (RPM) technology. While a small number of SNFs serve pediatric⏎populations, the vast majority serve adults with chronic conditions and related disabilities, predominantly for⏎adults over the age of seventy-five. Overall, Medicaid accounts for sixty-three percent of SNF residents while⏎private and other payers represent twenty-three percent, and Medicare only fourteen percent.1⏎According to the MedPAC March 2025 Report to Congress2, SNF facilities range from small to large⏎organizations and operate on minimal net margins:⏎• The SNF all-payer profit margin in 2023 was 0.4 percent (negative 1.3 percent in 2022).⏎• The median SNF had 100 beds, while ten percent of facilities had 176 or more beds and ten⏎percent of facilities had 50 or fewer beds.⏎There are over 32,000 AL communities that serve older individuals or those with limited disabilities who⏎typically need help with everyday activities and some health care services but do not require 24-hour skilled⏎nursing services for extended periods of time3. These communities offer a unique mix of companionship,⏎independence, privacy, and security in a home-like setting. The philosophy of assisted living is built on the⏎concept of delivering person-centered care and services to each individual resident. Some assisted living⏎communities specialize in serving individuals with specific needs. These may include, but are not limited to,⏎Alzheimer's disease or other forms of dementia (such as memory care units), intellectual and developmental⏎disabilities, and particular medical conditions (e.g., Parkinson's disease) or other needs. AL communities do not⏎directly provide certain health care services, such as physical therapy or pharmacy, but work with other⏎providers to offer these services.⏎• AL services are not included in Medicare benefits, and Medicaid does not cover AL room and⏎board, so most costs are private-pay, with only one in five residents being eligible for Medicaid⏎home and community-based services (HCBS) such as personal care and supportive services.⏎• Forty-three percent of AL residents are small and serve 4-10 residents at a time, while only ten⏎percent have 100 or more residents.⏎There are also over 5,300 ID/DD residences, or Intermediate Care Facilities for Individuals with Intellectual⏎Disabilities (ICF/IID) serving over 56,000 residents of all ages daily, with over seventy-five percent being⏎between the ages of 22 and 654. Many of these individuals are non-ambulatory, have seizure disorders, behavior⏎problems, mental illness, visual or hearing impairments, or a combination of the above. ICFs provide active⏎1 A Look at Nursing Facility Characteristics in 2025. KFF. https://www.kff.org/medicaid/a-look-at-nursing-facility-⏎characteristics/ [accessed February 11, 2026]⏎2 March 2025 Report to Congress: Medicare Payment Policy: Chapter 6: Skilled Nursing Services. Medicare Payment⏎Advisory Commission. https://www.medpac.gov/document/march-2025-report-to-the-congress-medicare-payment-⏎policy/ [accessed February 11, 2026]⏎3 Assisted Living Facts & Figures. AHCA/NCAL. https://www.ahcancal.org/Assisted-Living/Facts-and-⏎Figures/Pages/default.aspx [accessed February 11, 2026]⏎4 AHCA/NCAL internal analysis of the following public and proprietary data sources: A-Certification and Survey Provider⏎Enhanced Reporting (CASPER) data. B-Medicaid Budget and Expenditure System/State Children's Health Insurance⏎Program Budget and Expenditure System (MBES/CBES). C-IMPLAN Group LLC, IMPLAN System (data and software)⏎3⏎⏎treatment and services for people with significant support needs. They offer 24-hour supervision, health care,⏎therapies, activities, and training intended to maximize residents’ autonomy and independence.⏎• Virtually all funding for ID/DD residents is under Medicaid benefits.⏎• The average number of beds per residence is 14, ranging from 9 for private ID/DD residences to⏎52 for public residences.⏎2. Digital Capabilities Across SNF/NF, AL, and ID/DD Provider Settings⏎A sizable portion of AHCA/NCAL member care providers are directly tied to the healthcare information⏎exchange ecosystem whether reimbursed by federal, state, commercial, or private health coverage. Many payer⏎quality- and value-based payment models – including long-term care-focused Medicare fee-for-service (FFS)⏎Accountable Care Organizations (ACO), bundled payment models such as the Transforming Episode⏎Accountability Model (TEAM), and the Medicare Advantage (MA) Institutional Special Needs Plan (I-SNP)⏎model of care – involve integrated care across care providers. This would be significantly enhanced by⏎improved digital capabilities, including AI that can efficiently leverage critical health information across all⏎provider types involved with resident care.⏎All the care provider settings represented by our membership have access to and store Personal Health⏎Information (PHI). Of these, many store this information electronically to varying degrees and generally have⏎limited interoperable connectivity in the current digital health technology ecosystem. The variations in digital⏎capabilities across our LTPAC provider membership is directly the result of historical federal prioritization of⏎hospital inpatient and primary care interoperability support through the implementation of the Health⏎Information Technology for Economic & Clinical Health (HITECH) Act of 2009 and in subsequent coordinated⏎care, bundled care, and other integrated care value-based payment models advanced by CMS. Since LTPAC⏎providers were not specifically identified in the HITECH Act, the federal administrative support necessary to⏎facilitate seamless secure interoperable electronic exchange of the patient information between providers, and to⏎the patient, particularly at transitions of care, has been quite limited. Additionally, bundled incentive programs⏎have not historically included mechanisms to ensure that the bundle holder shares incentive payments to partner⏎SNF, AL, and ID/DD providers to support investment in interoperable technology capabilities. Moving forward,⏎the lack of explicit recognition and scaling of SNF, assisted living, and ID/DD setting challenges in federal AI⏎regulatory guidance not only creates compliance uncertainty for providers, but also discourages vendors from⏎developing and tailoring AI tools for these settings—reinforcing a cycle of market neglect for LTPAC care⏎environments.⏎In the absence of a federal coordinated effort to support Information Technology (IT) integration, LTPAC⏎providers that have adopted HIT have instead developed non-standardized HIT, including electronic health⏎records (EHR) to support setting-specific clinical and operational activities. In addition to EHR systems, many⏎of the SNF, AL and ID/DD providers in our membership utilize other digital technologies to support resident⏎care. Telehealth technologies are used to expand beneficiary access to care and reduce unnecessary⏎hospitalizations. Remote patient monitoring (RPM) and sensor technologies can provide valuable information to⏎improve resident safety and more timely and effective care interventions. Clinical decision support systems⏎(CDSS) including artificial intelligence (AI) driven analytics help to improve treatment decisions and outcomes.⏎Resident care management activities are also supported by tools for purposes such as electronic nursing⏎documentation, medication administration, incident reporting and quality assurance, and resident engagement⏎tools such as patient portals and apps. Moreover, HIT tools are also used to facilitate communication with⏎various internal and external clinical support services including pharmacy, laboratory, radiology, rehabilitation⏎therapy professionals, and for administrative purposes such as scheduling, claim processing, compliance⏎programs and more.⏎4⏎⏎However, the current digital ecosystem for many of these technologies today is fragmented and interconnectivity⏎– when present – is commonly built upon customized non-standardized digital ‘work arounds’. This⏎fragmentation is a significant barrier to attaining the level of effective digital capabilities needed to support⏎patients, families and their caregivers in making informed health decisions, and for provider stakeholders to⏎improve health outcomes.⏎For example, as recently as December 23, 2023, the Office of the Assistant Secretary for Planning and⏎Evaluation (ASPE) within HHS published a report titled Health Information Technology Adoption and⏎Utilization in Long-Term and Post-Acute Care Settings5 which found that most LTPAC providers, including⏎SNFs, have generally adopted EHRs to support clinical and business needs at a rate comparable to hospitals and⏎primary care providers, but adoption of EHRs in residential care settings such as AL and ID/DD is limited.⏎“Yet despite the lack of a federally-funded program and policy requirements, estimates of EHR⏎adoption rates among nursing home and SNF providers, as well as HHAs, were greater than 78% in⏎2018, which is on par with EHR adoption in office-based primary care settings. Residential care⏎settings were estimated to be much lower overall, at 26% -- higher than that for larger facilities and⏎much lower for small facilities.”⏎However, the authors also emphasized that interoperable exchange of health information is not routine or widely⏎used. In other words, LTPAC providers utilize their internal EHR, but modernization to improve interoperability⏎capabilities remains slow without focused and realistic policy levers. Despite barriers, the authors concluded that⏎there are opportunities for emerging policies to support secure interoperability in LTPAC.⏎The breadth of this digital divide was reflected in a recent May 2024 ONC Data Brief titled Interoperable⏎Exchange of Patient Health Information Among U.S. Hospitals: 20236.⏎Specifically, ONC indicated that only 17 percent of hospitals are able to routinely send⏎interoperable health information to LTPAC providers and only 8 percent of hospitals were able to⏎routinely receive such information from LTPAC providers.⏎While we appreciate the recent ONC efforts in late 2023 in launching the Trusted Exchange Framework and⏎Common Agreement (TEFCA) to enable nationwide health information exchange that could provide⏎opportunities for LTPAC providers with nonstandard health IT to exchange information through a Health⏎Information Exchange (HIE), an August 2024 Health Affairs Scholar article titled The state of health⏎information organizations and plans to participate in the federal exchange framework7 has revealed that this⏎alternative method for information exchange will likely be insufficient to resolve the interoperability gap for⏎LTPAC providers as 32 percent of the current Health Information Organizations (HIOs) that could facilitate this⏎information exchange through their HIEs have indicated they may not participate in TEFCA. As the authors⏎summarized,⏎“While TEFCA appears to have successfully engaged the majority of HIOs, achieving nationwide⏎exchange will require policy efforts to either attract the remaining HIOs or ensure that⏎nonparticipating HIOs' providers have another option for TEFCA participation.”⏎Another barrier to interoperable HIT adoption in some LTPAC providers is the lack of adequate high-speed⏎internet infrastructure in specific geographic locations. For example, KFF Health News recently published a⏎5 Health Information Technology Adoption and Utilization in Long-Term and Post-Acute Care Settings. Office of the⏎Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation (ASPE) at the U.S. Department of Health & Human Services by RTI⏎International. December 2023.⏎6 Interoperable Exchange of Patient Health Information Among U.S. Hospitals: 2023. Office of the National Coordinator for⏎Health Information Technology (ONC). May 2024.⏎7 Jordan Everson, Wei Chang, Vaishali Patel, Julia Adler-Milstein, the state of health information organizations and plans⏎to participate in the federal exchange framework, Health Affairs Scholar, Volume 2, Issue 8, August 2024⏎5⏎⏎report8 that included a national map, by county, where there is broadband ‘deserts’ where providers who may be⏎motivated to obtain interoperable HIT that could support AI to improve the quality of care cannot do so.⏎Finally, with increased digital interconnectedness necessary to support comprehensive AI technology in LTPAC⏎settings, the risk for cybersecurity breaches increases and will require additional investments in financial and⏎personnel resources to manage and to establish and maintain emergency plans during digital blackouts including⏎emergency backups for data recovery, and manual procedures to assure resident safety and care quality is⏎maintained at times when the AI or other digital information is offline.,⏎Since the enactment of the HITECH Act, AHCA/NCAL, other LTPAC Associations and the LTPAC Health IT⏎Collaborative9 have commented on various Agency HIT programs and have sought the support of HHS both in⏎funding and policy. HHS has provided limited support to support secure HIT interoperability and cybersecurity⏎infrastructure to support AI adoption within the constraints of the current statutory framework, but we believe⏎more is necessary. Unlike large health systems with dedicated IT departments and capital reserves, most LTPAC⏎providers lack the staffing and financial capacity to engage in complex, enterprise-style procurement processes,⏎underscoring the need for direct infrastructure support rather than adopting poor fitting AI models designed for⏎hospitals.⏎AHCA/NCAL Overarching Comments on Regulation, Reimbursement, and Research & Development⏎Regulation⏎AHCA/NCAL supports the HHS vision of transitioning to a regulatory framework that is proportionate,⏎transparent, and enables innovation while protecting vulnerable populations. For AI tools deployed in LTPAC⏎settings, regulatory clarity recognizing the unique challenges of providing care to individuals is essential⏎regarding:⏎• Clinical Decision Support (CDS) versus Decision Support Interventions (DSI): Under the HTI-1 Final⏎Rule, distinctions between CDS software and DSI must account for workflow-critical interventions in⏎LTPAC settings, such as glucose monitoring in diabetes management, delirium detection, fall risk⏎assessment, and polypharmacy optimization. Many AI tools operating in these domains directly impact⏎patient safety and outcomes.⏎• Algorithmic Transparency and Accountability: AI tools targeting LTPAC populations must undergo⏎validation against population-relevant cohorts including geriatric and chronic impairments and⏎demonstrate transparency in their decision-making logic and auditability of the AI outputs, particularly⏎when involving generative AI technology. Vendor accountability for algorithmic bias affecting safety⏎outcomes should be enforced, particularly for tools addressing conditions prevalent in older adults and⏎those with multiple chronic conditions.⏎• Age-Inclusive Standards: Regulatory frameworks should mandate age-stratified data collection and⏎validation in real-world data (RWD) studies to mitigate age bias in AI algorithms. Current clinical trials⏎frequently underrepresent geriatric patients, leading to biased algorithms that may recommend⏎inappropriate care pathways for older adults.⏎• Cybersecurity and HIPAA Compliance: Accelerating appropriate adoption of AI in clinical care is⏎desirable, but not at the expense of exposing patients and providers from bad actors that could⏎leverage/intercept AI technology without appropriate security measures to improperly obtain a residents⏎personally identifiable information (PII) or personal health information (PHI). AI development⏎guardrails that are scaled to risk of cybersecurity breach or HIPAA violations should be established as⏎8 Dead Zone. KFF Health News. May 31, 2025. https://kffhealthnews.org/dead-zone/⏎9 LTPAC Health IT Collaborative: https://www.ltpachit.org/⏎6⏎⏎providers do not have the technical expertise to assess such protections in proprietary, and often ‘black⏎box,’ vendor technology.⏎Reimbursement⏎Payment policies, particularly federal technology investment and support prioritization, significantly influence⏎technology adoption in LTPAC settings. AHCA/NCAL recommends:⏎• Value-Based Payment Alignment: Payment models should incentivize the use of high-value AI⏎interventions that improve outcomes for complex, high-need populations. Alternative payment models⏎(APMs) and value-based care arrangements should explicitly include quality measures and risk-⏎adjustment methodologies that account for LTPAC populations.⏎• Coverage for AI-Enabled Remote Monitoring: Medicare and Medicaid should expand coverage for AI-⏎enabled remote patient monitoring (RPM), ambient AI, and other technologies regardless of where the⏎beneficiary is residing that support aging in place, resident safety, and reduce preventable⏎hospitalizations and emergency department visits.⏎• Infrastructure Investment: Reimbursement mechanisms should support the underlying infrastructure⏎required for AI adoption, including interoperability investments, workforce training, and technical⏎assistance for small and rural providers.⏎• Reduction of Administrative Burden: AI tools that automate documentation, quality reporting, and⏎administrative tasks should be incentivized through payment policy to facilitate workflow transitions to⏎reduce provider burden and allow clinicians to focus on direct patient care.⏎Research & Development⏎HHS should prioritize R&D investments that address the unique needs of aging, geriatric, and LTPAC⏎populations with multiple chronic conditions including physical, cognitive, and behavioral impairments:⏎• Inclusion of LTPAC Populations in Research: The National Institutes of Health (NIH), the Agency for⏎Healthcare Research and Quality (AHRQ), and other HHS research agencies should require inclusion of⏎LTPAC and geriatric populations in AI research funding. Pilot programs should focus on AI⏎applications in nursing homes (SNF/NF), home health, assisted living, and other post-acute settings.⏎• Inclusion of LTPAC Standardized Patient Data Elements (SPADES) in Research: Any national AI⏎validation or testing infrastructure should explicitly include SPADES assessment items such as the SNF⏎Minimum Data Set (MDS) required by the IMPACT Act as well as the PACIO aligned datasets to⏎correct longstanding bias against aging and long stay populations and to ensure AI tools are evaluated⏎using data representative of LTPAC care delivery. While simulation or synthetic data methods may⏎supplement evaluation efforts, real world validation in SNF, assisted living, and ID/DD settings must⏎remain the standard for assessing any regulated AI performance and safety.⏎• Interoperability Research: Support development and validation of Fast Healthcare Interoperability⏎Resources (FHIR) implementation guides that capture geriatric-specific data elements, including⏎functional status, cognitive assessments, social determinants of health, and caregiver support data.⏎• Public-Private Partnerships: Establish cooperative research and development agreements (CRADAs)⏎with LTPAC providers, health IT vendors, and academic institutions to co-develop and validate AI tools⏎in real-world settings.⏎• Longitudinal and Multimorbidity Research: Fund large, longitudinal studies on aging trajectories,⏎multimorbidity trials, polypharmacy optimization, and research on family caregivers and aging in place.⏎7⏎⏎AHCA/NCAL Detailed Responses to the RFI Questions⏎Question 1: What are the biggest barriers to private sector innovation in AI for health care and its⏎adoption and use in clinical care?⏎Example feedback from our frontline AHCA/NCAL member providers includes:⏎“The biggest barrier to the private sector is price and the lack of reimbursement. Many facilities would⏎embrace more AI; however, the question is how it will be paid for. Also, many individuals still have a⏎fear of AI and the interpretation of certain medical conditions; and are worried about an error in the⏎interpretation as often happens with denials. There is also some concern about network capability and⏎computer literacy.”⏎Specific concerns across LTPAC providers regarding the most significant barriers for AI adoption⏎include:⏎Population Bias and Data Exclusion: Clinical trials and AI training datasets systematically underrepresent⏎older adults, particularly those in LTPAC settings. This leads to biased algorithms that perform poorly or⏎produce harmful recommendations when applied to geriatric populations. AI models trained predominantly on⏎younger cohorts may recommend inappropriate medication dosages, fail to account for age-related⏎pharmacokinetics, or overlook fall risk factors specific to frail older adults.⏎Data Interoperability Limitations: LTPAC providers face significant challenges in achieving interoperability⏎with acute care hospitals, primary care providers, specialty clinics, service partners including therapy providers,⏎pharmacies, and labs, and more recently, health information exchanges. The healthcare ecosystem is not⏎designed to be inclusive of LTPAC populations. Limited adoption of standardized data exchange formats,⏎particularly HL7 FHIR implementation guides relevant to LTPAC (such as those developed by the PACIO⏎Project), hinders the flow of critical patient information across care transitions. Without access to longitudinal,⏎interoperable data, AI tools, particularly clinical decision support tools, cannot effectively support person-⏎centered, holistic care.⏎Infrastructure and Resource Constraints: Many LTPAC providers, particularly small, rural, and under-⏎resourced facilities, lack the technical infrastructure, broadband connectivity, and financial resources needed to⏎adopt AI technologies. The digital divide in LTPAC settings creates disparate access to innovation.⏎Additionally, workforce challenges—including limited health IT expertise and high staff turnover—impede⏎successful AI implementation. While AI literacy and training are important considerations, workforce instability⏎in LTPAC settings—characterized by chronic staffing shortages, high turnover, and care models reliant on a⏎high proportion of Certified Nursing Assistants (CNAs) as the primary personal needs caregivers—remains the⏎more binding constraint on such technology adoption absent reimbursement and operational support.⏎Regulatory Uncertainty: Lack of clarity regarding liability, indemnification, privacy, and security for non-⏎medical device AI tools creates hesitation among LTPAC providers and AI developers. Questions about who⏎bears responsibility when an AI tool produces an adverse recommendation, how HIPAA applies to AI-generated⏎insights, and what standards govern AI tool validation remain inadequately addressed. As AI technologies⏎evolve, additional clarity regarding how HIPAA applies to AI generated outputs and data use could further⏎protect LTPAC providers and residents, provided such guidance prioritizes provider safeguards and resident⏎privacy over technical enablement or research flexibility.⏎Reimbursement Barriers: Fee-for-service payment and other HIT investment incentive program structures⏎provide limited incentives for LTPAC providers to invest in AI tools, even when such tools could improve⏎outcomes and reduce costs over time. Coverage and reimbursement decisions for AI-enabled services are slow,⏎8⏎⏎and current payment models do not adequately account for the value of AI interventions that prevent adverse⏎events, reduce hospitalizations, or improve quality of life.⏎Lack of Geriatric-Specific Clinical Guidelines Integration: AI-powered clinical decision support tools often⏎lack integration with geriatric-specific clinical practice guidelines. Evidence-based guidelines tailored for older⏎adults—such as those published by the Post-Acute and Long-Term Care Medical Association (PALTmed)—⏎address conditions like diabetes management, falls prevention, delirium detection, polypharmacy, and pressure⏎ulcer prevention in geriatric populations. AI tools that do not incorporate these guidelines may produce⏎recommendations that are inappropriate for LTPAC patients.⏎Below are numerous government, academic, and stakeholder analyses and commentary regarding the digital⏎divide impacting AHCA/NCAL member settings that creates a barrier that needs to be resolved for accelerating⏎adoption of AI technology in LTPAC settings.⏎Interoperability Capacity and AI Adoption and Costs in Nursing Homes and Assisted Living References⏎Amazon Web Services (AWS). 2025. Elaprolu, Sri, Alla Simoneau, Paul Amadeo, and Laura Kulowski.⏎“Physical AI in Practice: Technical Foundations that Fuel Human Machine Interactions.” AWS Machine⏎Learning Blog. https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/physical-ai-in-practice-technical-foundations-⏎that-fuel-human-machine-interactions/.⏎Argentum. “Argentum and A Place for Mom Highlight Current Technology Trends, Growing AI Optimism and⏎Industry Collaboration in Senior Living Technology Report.” July 1, 2025. https://www.argentum.org/argentum-⏎and-a-place-for-mom-highlight-current-technology-trends-growing-ai-optimism-and-industry-collaboration-in-⏎senior-living-technology-report/.⏎Ciolek, Daniel. “Federal Report on Interoperability of Health IT in Long-Term Care.” Provider Magazine,⏎Spring 2024. https://www.providermagazine.com/Issues/2024/Spring/Pages/Federal-Report-on-Interoperability-⏎of-Health-IT-in-Long-Term-Care.aspx.⏎Ciolek, Daniel. “Influences on Health Technology Adoption in Nursing Facilities.” Provider magazine, Winter⏎2025. https://www.providermagazine.com/Issues/2025/Winter/Pages/Influences-on-Health-Technology-⏎Adoption-in-Nursing-Facilities.aspx.⏎Ciolek, Dan. “New Federal Report on Adoption of Health Information Technology Includes SNF and AL⏎Settings.” AHCA/NCAL Blog, January 5, 2024. https://www.ahcancal.org/News-and-⏎Communications/Blog/Pages/New-Federal-Report-on-Adoption-of-Health-Information-Technology-Includes-⏎SNF-and-AL-Settings.aspx.⏎Code, Scott. “Building Interoperability: A Call for Investment, Not Penalties.” LeadingAge. July 14, 2025.⏎https://leadingage.org/building-interoperability-a-call-for-investment-not-penalties/.⏎Cognitive World. 2025. “The Infrastructure Decade: Why Healthcare’s AI Future Is Being Built in Layers, Not⏎Launched Overnight.” Cognitive World. https://cognitiveworld.com/articles/2025/11/8/the-infrastructure-⏎decade-why-healthcares-ai-future-is-being-built-in-layers-not-launched-overnight.⏎Forrester Consulting. The Total Economic Impact™ of PointClickCare’s Skilled Nursing Solution. April 2020.⏎https://pointclickcare.com/wp-content/uploads/2021/07/the-total-economic-impact-of-pointclickcares-skilled-⏎nursing-solution-study.pdf.⏎Kandel, Ben, Cheryl Field, Jasmeet Kaur, Dean Slawson, and Joseph G. Ouslander. “Development of a⏎Predictive Hospitalization Model for Skilled Nursing Facility Patients.” Journal of the American Medical⏎Directors Association 26, no. 1 (January 2025): 105288. https://www.jamda.com/article/S1525-8610(24)00710-⏎2/fulltext.⏎9⏎⏎KFF Health News. Dead Zone. Published May 31, 2025. https://kffhealthnews.org/dead-zone/.⏎LeadingAge CAST. “Adoption of Advanced EHRs Stalls in the Aging Sector.” LeadingAge, June 10, 2024.⏎https://leadingage.org/adoption-of-advanced-ehrs-stalls-in-the-aging-sector/.⏎LeadingAge CAST. “Case Study: Using AI to Help Detect and Prevent Falls.” PDF. https://leadingage.org/wp-⏎content/uploads/2023/03/Case-Study_Village-Assisted-Living-Detect-Falls.pdf. Accessed February 11, 2026.⏎Lin, Sunny C., and Ozcan Tunalilar. “Rapid Adoption of Electronic Health Record and Health Information⏎Exchange among Assisted Living Communities, 2010–2018.” Journal of the American Medical Informatics⏎Association 29, no. 5 (2022): 953–957. https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocac021.⏎McHugh, John P., Hayara Cardoso, Ngan Bui, and Gregory L. Alexander. “Market and Organizational⏎Characteristics Associated with Nursing Home Health Information Technology Maturity.” Journal of⏎Gerontological Nursing 51, no. 10 (October 2025): 10–16.⏎https://journals.healio.com/doi/abs/10.3928/00989134-20250916-01.⏎Nguyen, Thuy D., Christopher M. Whaley, Kosali Simon, and others. “Adoption of Artificial Intelligence in the⏎Health Care Sector.” JAMA Health Forum 6, no. 11 (November 21, 2025): e255029.⏎https://jamanetwork.com/journals/jama-health-forum/fullarticle/2841460.⏎Office of the Assistant Secretary for Planning and Evaluation (ASPE). Health Information Technology Adoption⏎and Utilization in Long-Term and Post-Acute Care Settings. Prepared by RTI International. Washington, DC:⏎U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, December 2023. https://aspe.hhs.gov/reports/hit-adoption-⏎utilization-ltpac-settings.⏎Office of the National Coordinator for Health Information Technology (ONC). Trusted Exchange Framework⏎and Common Agreement (TEFCA) Overview. U.S. Department of Health and Human Services, 2025–2026.⏎https://www.healthit.gov.⏎Orlov, Laurie M. The Future of AI in Senior Living and Care: What’s Now and Next. October 2024.⏎https://www.ageinplacetech.com/files/aip/Future%20of%20AI%20in%20Senior%20Living%20and%20Care.pdf.⏎Poon, Eric G., Christy Harris Lemak, Juan C. Rojas, Janet Guptill, and David Classen. “Adoption of Artificial⏎Intelligence in Healthcare: Survey of Health System Priorities, Successes, and Challenges.” Journal of the⏎American Medical Informatics Association 32, no. 7 (July 2025): 1093–1100.⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/jamia/ocaf065. Accessed February 11, 2026.⏎The King’s Fund. 2025. “Infrastructure for Innovation: Getting the NHS and Social Care Ready for AI.” The⏎King’s Fund. https://www.kingsfund.org.uk/insight-and-analysis/long-reads/infrastructure-nhs-social-care-ai.⏎Question 2: What regulatory, payment policy, or programmatic design changes should HHS prioritize to⏎incentivize the effective use of AI in clinical care and why?⏎Example feedback from our frontline AHCA/NCAL member providers includes:⏎“Prioritization should be given to memorializing the source(s) of the AI generated information and⏎documentation trail by providing regulatory clarity under HIPAA to clarify understanding who or what⏎technology is responsible for what is written; such as “documentation on this was generated and entered by⏎AI” [insert technology here] or , “recommended by AI [insert technology here]and verified by [insert⏎human approver/editor];.From a payer standpoint, this could involve statements such as “This⏎approval/denial generated and determined by AI[insert technology here]” Some vendors are already using⏎such capabilities in Acute Care, VA and Physicians’ offices…but regulatory standardization across all⏎provider settings regarding documenting when and how AI impacted care decisions should leverage existing⏎models customized to long term care needs rather than reinventing the wheel.”⏎10⏎⏎Specific LTPAC provider recommendations include the following priority changes:⏎Regulatory Changes⏎• Mandate Age-Stratified Validation (42 CFR Part 412, 413, 482, 483, 484, 485): Require AI tools used⏎in clinical care to undergo validation testing on age-stratified cohorts, particularly for tools intended for⏎use in Medicare populations. Validation should include performance metrics specific to geriatric⏎patients and demonstrate absence of age-based bias.⏎• Algorithmic Transparency Requirements (45 CFR Part 170): Under the ONC Health IT Certification⏎Program, establish certification criteria for AI-enabled clinical decision support tools that require⏎disclosure of training data characteristics, model performance on diverse populations, and explanation⏎of decision logic. FHIR-based event-condition-action rules per HL7 Clinical Reasoning modules should⏎be used to enforce transparency.⏎• Interoperability Standards for Geriatric Data (45 CFR 170.315): Expand the United States Core Data for⏎Interoperability (USCDI) to include geriatric-specific data elements captured in PACIO Project FHIR⏎implementation guides: functional status (ADLs/IADLs), cognitive assessments (PHQ-9, CAM),⏎advance directives, standardized medication profiles, social determinants of health, and caregiver⏎support information.⏎• Privacy and Security Safeguards (45 CFR Parts 160, 164): Clarify HIPAA applicability to AI-generated⏎clinical insights and establish standards for de-identification, consent management, and audit trails for⏎AI tools that access or generate protected health information.⏎• Certification Agility as Burden Reduction (All above relevant CFT sections) Certification pathways for⏎AI enabled health IT should also be designed to avoid imposing repeated compliance and administrative⏎burdens on LTPAC providers as tools are updated, emphasizing predictability and simplicity rather than⏎development speed.⏎Payment Policy Changes⏎• Medicare Coverage for AI-Enabled RPM (42 CFR Part 405, 410, 414): Expand Medicare Part B⏎coverage for AI-enabled remote patient monitoring, ambient AI monitoring, and wearable devices that⏎support aging in place. Reimbursement should cover device costs, data transmission, clinical⏎interpretation, and care coordination activities.⏎• Value-Based Payment Incentives (42 CFR Part 425, 512): Incorporate AI adoption and outcome metrics⏎into Medicare Shared Savings Program (MSSP), Accountable Care Organization (ACO) models, and⏎other APMs. Quality measures should reward use of AI tools that demonstrate improved outcomes for⏎high-risk, complex patients. Similar alignment should be tied to Medicare Advantage requirements (42⏎CFR Part 422). Additionally, while AI can be used to improve workflow address clinical questions and⏎relieve administrative burden (conversational AI), the use of agentic AI without proper regulations can⏎threaten the quality of care received by beneficiaries in VBC programs.⏎• Infrastructure Investment Payments (42 CFR Part 413, 484, 485): Create a Medicare/Medicaid⏎infrastructure payment mechanism to support LTPAC providers' investments in interoperability, health⏎IT upgrades, and AI readiness. This could be modeled after the Medicare Promoting Interoperability⏎Program but tailored for LTPAC settings.⏎• Documentation Burden Reduction (42 CFR Part 483, 484): Allow AI-generated documentation to⏎satisfy regulatory documentation requirements in skilled nursing facilities (42 CFR 483.20, 483.21) and⏎home health agencies (42 CFR 484.55, 484.60), provided the AI tool meets transparency and accuracy⏎standards.⏎11⏎⏎Programmatic Design Changes⏎• CMMI Innovation Models: Develop Center for Medicare and Medicaid Innovation (CMMI) models⏎specifically focused on accelerating AI adoption in LTPAC settings with appropriately funded models.⏎Test payment incentives, technical assistance models, and shared savings approaches for providers⏎implementing AI tools.⏎• LTPAC-Focused FHIR Adoption: Provide grants and technical assistance through the Office of the⏎National Coordinator (ONC) to accelerate LTPAC provider adoption of PACIO Project FHIR⏎implementation guides and onboarding to Qualified Health Information Networks (QHINs) under the⏎Trusted Exchange Framework and Common Agreement (TEFCA).⏎• Geriatric AI Validation Testbeds: Establish regulatory-science testbeds in partnership with the Food and⏎Drug Administration (FDA), Veteran’s Affairs (VA), and CMS to validate AI tools for safety, efficacy,⏎and appropraiteness in LTPAC settings. Require real-world evidence from diverse care settings for⏎regulatory approval.⏎AI in Healthcare Federal and State Legislative & Regulatory Barriers Research⏎Congressional Research Service (CRS). 2025. Wells, Nora. “Artificial Intelligence (AI) in Health Care: Recent⏎Federal Activity.” Congress.gov. https://www.congress.gov/crs-product/IF13135.⏎International Comparative Legal Guides (ICLG). 2025. “Artificial Intelligence Tools in Health Services – An⏎Overview of Current and Evolving US Federal and State Health Regulatory Structures.” ICLG Digital Health.⏎https://iclg.com/practice-areas/digital-health-laws-and-regulations/03-artificial-intelligence-tools-in-health-⏎services-an-overview-of-current-and-evolving-us-federal-and-state-health-regulatory-structures.⏎Manatt Health. 2025. Seigel, Randi, Jared Augenstein, Maya Shashoua, Cassidy Slater, and Christine Irlbeck.⏎“Manatt Health: Health AI Policy Tracker.” Manatt Phelps & Phillips.⏎https://www.manatt.com/insights/newsletters/health-highlights/manatt-health-health-ai-policy-tracker.⏎National Law Review. 2025. de la Houssaye, Nadia, Andrew R. Lee, Jason M. Loring, and Graham H. Ryan.⏎“Healthcare AI in the United States — Navigating Regulatory Evolution, Market Dynamics, and Emerging⏎Challenges in an Era of Rapid Innovation.” National Law Review. https://natlawreview.com/article/healthcare-⏎ai-united-states-navigating-regulatory-evolution-market-dynamics-and.⏎Question 3: For non-medical devices, what novel legal and implementation issues exist related to AI in⏎clinical care, and what role should HHS play?⏎Example feedback from our frontline AHCA/NCAL member providers includes:⏎“If AI declines a Medicare Advantage prior authorization request…who is ultimately responsible if the⏎nursing home resident has a poor outcome? What is the validity of ongoing modifications…. should we⏎have specific AI medical system standards, so facility personnel know that these standards are followed⏎when looking at systems. Also, there is a lack of knowledge on how to choose which AI and the ongoing⏎review of development and changes…. with each update is there an upcharge”⏎Specific LTPAC provider recommendations include considering the following on the HHS role:⏎Liability and Accountability: When an AI tool recommends a clinical intervention that results in patient harm,⏎liability questions arise: Is the provider liable for following the recommendation? Is the AI vendor liable for the⏎algorithm's output? Is the health system liable for deploying an inadequately validated tool? Current medical⏎malpractice frameworks do not clearly address AI-mediated decision-making. HHS should work with state⏎12⏎⏎medical boards, professional liability insurers, and legal experts to develop guidance on shared accountability⏎models.⏎Indemnification Agreements: Many AI vendors require healthcare organizations to sign indemnification⏎agreements that shift liability risk entirely to the provider. This is particularly problematic for under-resourced⏎LTPAC providers who lack the financial capacity to assume such risk. HHS should establish model contract⏎language and standards for balanced risk-sharing between vendors and providers.⏎Privacy and Security: AI tools often require access to large volumes of patient data for training and operation.⏎Questions remain about:⏎• Whether AI-generated clinical insights constitute protected health information (PHI) under HIPAA;⏎• How consent should be obtained for AI processing of patient data;⏎• What security standards apply to AI model storage, transmission, and updates; and⏎• How to address privacy risks when AI tools use consumer-generated health data from non-HIPAA-⏎covered wearables and apps.⏎HHS should issue guidance clarifying HIPAA applicability to AI systems and establish security standards for AI⏎tools used in clinical care.⏎Algorithmic Bias and Discrimination: AI tools that exhibit bias against protected classes (age, race, disability⏎status) may violate civil rights laws. However, detection and remediation of algorithmic bias require technical⏎expertise that many healthcare organizations lack. HHS Office for Civil Rights (OCR) should develop guidance⏎on algorithmic patient population appropriateness requirements and establish complaint mechanisms for patients⏎who believe they have been harmed by biased AI tools.⏎Data Ownership and Control: When AI tools generate clinical insights, predictions, or risk scores based on⏎patient data, questions arise about who owns these outputs and how they can be used. HHS should establish⏎clear data governance frameworks that respect patient autonomy while enabling appropriate secondary use for⏎research and quality improvement.⏎Workforce Competency: Effective use of AI in clinical care requires workforce education on AI capabilities,⏎limitations, and appropriate supervision. HHS should support development of training programs and⏎competency standards for clinicians, administrators, and health IT professionals who will deploy and oversee AI⏎tools.⏎Question 4: What are the most promising AI evaluation methods for clinical care, and how should HHS⏎support them?⏎Example feedback from our frontline AHCA/NCAL member providers includes:⏎“Need interoperability between practice settings…is this a way to “mandate” everyone knows the same⏎information; need to support grants and operative agreements that show human and technology⏎intervention; any grants or agreements should be fairly spread across the healthcare continuum; it cannot⏎be allocated the same way as money for EMR’s was allocated.”⏎13⏎⏎Specific LTPAC provider recommendations for HHS to support AI evaluation include the following:⏎Pre-Deployment Evaluation Methods⏎• Prospective Validation on Diverse Cohorts: AI tools should undergo prospective validation testing on⏎age-diverse, racially diverse, and clinically diverse populations before deployment. For tools intended⏎for geriatric populations, validation cohorts must include adequate representation of older adults with⏎multimorbidity and polypharmacy.⏎• Bias Auditing and Fairness Testing: Independent bias audits should assess AI tool performance across⏎demographic subgroups (age, race, ethnicity, sex, disability status) and clinical subgroups (disease⏎severity, functional status, cognitive status). Disparities in performance should trigger remediation⏎before approval.⏎• Explainability Assessment: AI tools should be evaluated for their ability to provide clinically⏎meaningful explanations of recommendations. Black-box models that cannot explain their reasoning⏎pose risks in clinical settings where clinicians must understand rationale to appropriately supervise AI⏎outputs.⏎• Workflow Integration Testing: Human-centered design evaluations should assess how AI tools integrate⏎into clinical workflows (such as in SNF, AL, and ID/DD residential care settings), including cognitive⏎load, alert fatigue, and usability for diverse users (including staff with varying levels of health IT⏎literacy).⏎Post-Deployment Evaluation Methods⏎• Continuous Performance Monitoring: Real-world performance monitoring should track AI tool⏎accuracy, adoption rates, override rates, and clinical outcomes over time. Performance should be⏎stratified by patient characteristics to detect emerging bias or model drift.⏎• Clinical Outcome Studies: Rigorous implementation science research should evaluate whether AI tools⏎actually improve patient outcomes, reduce costs, or enhance clinician efficiency in real-world settings.⏎Studies should include LTPAC sites and geriatric populations.⏎• Safety Surveillance: HHS should establish voluntary or mandatory adverse event reporting mechanisms⏎for AI tools, similar to FDA MedWatch for medical devices. This would enable detection of safety⏎signals and patterns of harm.⏎• Equity Monitoring: Ongoing monitoring should assess whether AI tool deployment widens or narrows⏎health disparities. Particular attention should be paid to access barriers for under-resourced providers⏎and vulnerable populations.⏎HHS Support Mechanisms⏎HHS should support these evaluation methods through:⏎• Contracts and Cooperative Agreements: Fund development of standardized evaluation frameworks,⏎toolkits, and benchmarking datasets that include geriatric populations.⏎• Grants: Support independent research on AI tool performance, safety, and bias minimization through⏎NIH, AHRQ, and Patient-Centered Outcomes Research Institute (PCORI).⏎• Regulatory-Science Testbeds: Establish collaboration among FDA, ONC, CMS, and VA to create⏎shared infrastructure for AI validation.⏎• Prize Competitions: Incentivize development of novel evaluation methods, bias detection tools, and⏎explainability techniques through HHS prize competitions.⏎The most impactful approach would be a combination of contracts (for infrastructure development), grants (for⏎independent research), and cooperative agreements (for multi-stakeholder collaboration).⏎14⏎⏎Question 5: How can HHS best support private sector activities to promote innovative and effective AI⏎use in clinical care?⏎HHS should adopt a supportive posture toward industry-driven quality assurance mechanisms while establishing⏎baseline standards to protect patients:⏎Specific LTPAC provider recommendations for HHS to promote AI in clinical care include the following:⏎Accreditation and Certification⏎• Support development of voluntary AI tool accreditation programs through organizations like The Joint⏎Commission, NCQA, or URAC that assess AI tools for safety, effectiveness, and equity.⏎• Expand the ONC Health IT Certification Program (45 CFR Part 170) to include certification criteria for⏎AI-enabled clinical decision support tools, with specific requirements for validation, transparency, and⏎bias testing.⏎• Recognize and incentivize (through payment policy) use of certified or accredited AI tools in value-⏎based payment arrangements.⏎Industry-Driven Testing and Standards⏎• Partner with standards development organizations (HL7, IEEE, ASTM) to create technical standards for⏎AI tool interoperability, data requirements, and performance reporting.⏎• Support industry consortia (such as the Coalition for Health AI, Partnership on AI) that develop best⏎practice frameworks, model cards, and datasheets for AI transparency.⏎• Fund development of open-source benchmarking datasets and evaluation toolkits that include geriatric⏎and LTPAC populations.⏎Professional Credentialing⏎• Support professional associations, societies, and professional educational programs in developing AI⏎competency standards and credentialing programs for clinicians and health IT professionals.⏎• Incentivize AI literacy training through continuing education requirements and Medicare payment⏎bonuses for providers who complete AI training programs.⏎Market Transparency⏎• Establish a public registry of AI tools used in clinical care, including information on their intended use,⏎validation evidence, and performance metrics. This would function similarly to FDA's medical device⏎databases but for non-medical device AI tools.⏎• Require AI vendors to publish model cards and performance reports that enable informed purchasing⏎decisions by healthcare organizations.⏎Question 6: Where have AI tools deployed in clinical care met or exceeded expectations, and where have⏎they fallen short? What novel AI tools would have the greatest potential?⏎Example feedback from our frontline AHCA/NCAL member providers includes:⏎“AI has fallen short in Managed Care approvals; need more reliable audit tools that are not human⏎dependent; a good AI tool can also be a trainer as it points out opportunities; a good AI tool can also⏎pick up subtle changes so intervention can occur early; however the downside is if these are noticed and⏎nothing happens, then it puts the organization at risk.”⏎15⏎⏎Specific AI tools deployed in LTPAC settings and opportunities to improve include the following:⏎AI Tools Meeting or Exceeding Expectations⏎While robust evidence specific to LTPAC settings remains limited, emerging applications show promise:⏎• Fall Risk Prediction: In-person and remote patient monitoring (RPM) AI tools analyzing gait patterns,⏎functional status, medication profiles, and environmental factors show potential for identifying high-risk⏎individuals and triggering preventive interventions.⏎• Sepsis and Acute Illness Detection: Early warning systems primarily studies in acute care hospital⏎settings analyzing vital signs and laboratory trends may enable earlier identification of acute changes in⏎condition, particularly valuable in nursing home settings where physician presence is intermittent.⏎• Documentation Automation: Ambient AI documentation tools that transcribe and structure clinical⏎encounters reduce documentation burden and allow clinicians to focus on patient interaction.⏎• Medication Management: AI-powered polypharmacy optimization tools that flag drug-drug interactions,⏎inappropriate medications for older adults (Beers Criteria), and opportunities for deprescribing show⏎promise for improving medication safety.⏎• Telehealth: AI technology integrated with telehealth visits facilitate person-centered connectedness⏎between the telehealth clinician and patient while also optimizing usefulness of EHR documentation and⏎sensor technology.⏎• Virtual Reality: AI-driven virtual reality (VR) tools have been successfully deployed in AHCA/NCAL⏎member communities for treatment interventions, patient education, facilitating movement, and⏎enhancing resident quality of life.⏎VR used in physical therapy (PT): VR assessments include gait and movement analysis while⏎o⏎VR supported treatments include neurological rehabilitation, balance & posture training, and⏎pain reduction.⏎VR used in occupational therapy (OT): VR assessments include daily living task performance⏎o⏎using sensors while VR supported treatments include neurological rehabilitation, functional⏎stability and balance, and activities of daily living (ADL) task simulation.⏎VR used in speech-language pathology (SLP): VR assessments include speech-recognition and⏎o⏎feedback and swallowing performance while VR supported treatments include VR speech⏎stimulation with virtual partners for pronunciation, articulation, and expressive/receptive⏎language activities.⏎VR used to enhance resident quality of life: Older adult-focused VR platforms used in⏎o⏎AHCA/NCAL member communities have enhanced physical and psychological needs and⏎desires including reminiscence therapy, social experiences that reduce isolation, exercise and⏎mobility programs (i.e. virtual tennis, bowling), dementia care engagement, virtual tours of⏎cities and landmarks, virtual concerts, stress/anxiety reductions, pain management, cognitive⏎stimulation, and more.⏎• Staff Training: Intuitive and adaptive AI and VR technologies can assess and track staff competencies⏎and target training materials and staff comprehension in real-world simulations, which can be more⏎effective and less time-consuming than traditional training methods, and can be provided at more⏎convenient time periods for the staff member.⏎AI Tools Falling Short of Expectations⏎• Biased Algorithms: Multiple studies have documented racial, age, and socioeconomic bias in AI tools⏎for risk prediction, resource allocation, clinical decision support, and care planning. Tools validated on⏎non-representative populations perform poorly when deployed in diverse real-world settings.⏎16⏎⏎• Alert Fatigue: Some AI-powered clinical decision support systems generate excessive alerts, leading to⏎override behavior and diminished effectiveness.⏎• Lack of Integration: AI tools that function as standalone systems rather than integrating with existing⏎EHR workflows create additional burden rather than reducing it.⏎• Cost Without Demonstrated Value: High-cost AI tools that lack rigorous evidence of improved⏎outcomes or cost savings fail to deliver promised return on investment.⏎Novel AI Tools with Greatest Potential for LTPAC Settings⏎• Multimorbidity Management: AI tools that synthesize complex patient data (functional status, cognitive⏎status, social determinants, multiple chronic conditions) to generate holistic care plans tailored to⏎individual goals and preferences.⏎• Caregiver Support Tools: AI-powered virtual assistants (robots) that guide family and facility caregivers⏎in medication administration, food delivery, symptom monitoring, and care coordination for older⏎adults.⏎• Chatbots & Virtual Assistants: AI-powered technologies in facilities can be used to improve⏎communication and save staff time. Chatbots & virtual assistants are often used for answering⏎resident/family questions, providing medication or appointment reminders, and supporting staff with⏎routine inquiries. Companion chatbots for residents have been successfully used for isolation reduction,⏎reminders, cognitive stimulation, and other quality of life engagement purposes.⏎• Delirium Detection: Continuous monitoring systems using wearables or ambient sensors to detect early⏎signs of delirium, enabling rapid intervention to prevent adverse outcomes.⏎• Pain Detection: AI-driven facial or other biometric analysis to detect pain levels—particularly helpful⏎for residents with dementia or communication difficulties.⏎• Bedsore Risk Monitoring and Interventions: AI leveraging modern bed sensors and other patient⏎biometric monitoring activity can detect increased risk for skin breakdown, can alert recommended⏎interventions, and document effectiveness of staff repositioning activities.⏎• Functional Decline Prediction: AI tools analyzing longitudinal functional status data to predict risk of⏎hospitalization, nursing home placement, or mortality, enabling proactive care plan adjustments.⏎• Social Determinants Screening and Intervention: AI tools that screen for social needs (food insecurity,⏎transportation barriers, housing instability) and connect patients with community resources.⏎• Quality Measure Automation: AI tools that extract quality measure data from clinical documentation⏎and automate reporting to CMS, reducing administrative burden.⏎Examples of Types of AI Technology Used in Nursing Homes and Assisted Living Residences and Recent⏎Research Findings⏎While the use of AI-based technologies in nursing homes and assisted living residences is growing rapidly, there⏎is a dearth of scientific research in the appropriateness or effectiveness of these tools specific to the resident⏎populations of these settings. Most research involving using AI-based technologies in United States healthcare⏎settings has been with ambulatory care and hospital patient populations which have quite different clinical⏎characteristics and care needs than LTPAC provider settings. Additionally, most research in long-term care⏎settings has been conducted outside of the United States. The following list highlights some of the recent⏎research we identified relevant to AI-based technologies used in those nursing homes and assisted living⏎residents our members serve.⏎Predictive Analytics & Early Warning Systems⏎Salehinejad, Hojjat, Anne M. Meehan, Parvez A. Rahman, Marcia A. Core, Bijan J. Borah, and Pedro J.⏎Caraballo. 2023. “Novel Machine Learning Model to Improve Performance of an Early Warning System in⏎17⏎⏎Hospitalized Patients: A Retrospective Multisite Cross-Validation Study.” eClinicalMedicine 66: 102312.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.eclinm.2023.102312.⏎Seaman, Karla, Kristiana Ludlow, Nasir Wabe, et al. 2022. “The Use of Predictive Fall Models for Older Adults⏎Receiving Aged Care, Using Routinely Collected Electronic Health Record Data: A Systematic Review.” BMC⏎Geriatrics 22: 210. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12877-022-02901-2.⏎Quintana, Mereya. 2025. “Harnessing Predictive Analytics and AI in Infection Prevention: A Next Generation⏎Approach to Combat Healthcare Associated Infections.” American Journal of Infection Control 53 (6S): S9.⏎https://www.ajicjournal.org/article/S0196-6553(25)00144-0/fulltext.⏎Remote Patient Monitoring (RPM) & Biometric Sensors⏎Reed, Americus II, Charles Herman, Amit Sawant, Lori Sun, and David Futoran. 2022. “Effectiveness of AI⏎Powered Passive Remote Patient Monitoring on Senior Living Outcomes: A Pilot Study.” Clinics in Medicine 4⏎(2): 1046. https://www.medtextpublications.com/open-access/effectiveness-of-ai-powered-passive-remote-⏎patient-monitoring-rpm-on-1222.pdf.⏎Shaik, Mohmmad Arif, et al. 2025. “Advancing Remote Monitoring for Patients With Alzheimer Disease and⏎Related Dementias: Systematic Review.” JMIR Aging 8. https://aging.jmir.org/2025/1/e69175.⏎Tsvetanov, Filip. 2024. “Integrating AI Technologies into Remote Monitoring Patient Systems.” Engineering⏎Proceedings 70 (1): 54. https://doi.org/10.3390/engproc2024070054.⏎Fall Detection⏎Feng, Xiang, Zhengliang Shan, Zhanfeng Zhao, Zirui Xu, Tianpeng Zhang, Zihe Zhou, Bo Deng, and Zirui⏎Guan. “Millimeter-Wave Radar Monitoring for Elder’s Fall Based on Multi-View Parameter Fusion Estimation⏎and Recognition.” Remote Sensing 15, no. 8 (2023): 2101. https://doi.org/10.3390/rs15082101.⏎Gorce, Philippe, and Julien Jacquier-Bret. “Fall Detection in Elderly People: A Systematic Review of Ambient⏎Assisted Living and Smart Home-Related Technology Performance.” Sensors 25, no. 21 (2025): 6540.⏎https://doi.org/10.3390/s25216540.⏎Jansi, R., and R. Amutha. “Detection of Fall for the Elderly in an Indoor Environment Using a Tri-Axial⏎Accelerometer and Kinect Depth Data.” Multidimensional Systems and Signal Processing 31 (2020): 1207–⏎1225. https://doi.org/10.1007/s11045-020-00705-4.⏎Kramer, Josh Brown, Lucas Sabalka, Ben Rush, Katherine Jones, and Tegan Nolte. “Automated Depth Video⏎Monitoring for Fall Reduction: A Case Study.” In Proceedings of the IEEE/CVF Conference on Computer⏎Vision and Pattern Recognition Workshops (CVPRW) (2020). https://www.computer.org/csdl/proceedings-⏎article/cvprw/2020/09150602/1lPH6bM1nRS.⏎Mudiyanselage, Sriyani Padmalatha Konara, Ching Teng Yao, Sujeewa Dilhani Maithreepala, and Bih O. Lee.⏎“Emerging Digital Technologies Used for Fall Detection in Older Adults in Aged Care: A Scoping Review.”⏎Journal of the American Medical Directors Association 26, no. 1 (January 2025): 105330.⏎https://www.jamda.com/article/S1525-8610%2824%2900752-7/abstract.⏎Xiong, Glen L., Eleonore Bayen, Shirley Nickels, Raghav Subramaniam, Pulkit Agrawal, Julien Jacquemot,⏎Alexandre M. Bayen, Bruce Miller, and George Netscher. “Real-Time Video Detection of Falls in Dementia⏎Care Facility and Reduced Emergency Care.” The American Journal of Managed Care 25, no. 7 (July 2019):⏎314–315. https://www.ajmc.com/view/realtime-video-detection-of-falls-in-dementia-care-facility-and-reduced-⏎emergency-care.⏎18⏎⏎Computer Vision for Pain Recognition⏎Babicova, Ivana, Ainslea Cross, Dawn Forman, Jeffery Hughes, and Kreshnik Hoti. 2021. “Evaluation of the⏎Psychometric Properties of PainChek® in UK Aged Care Residents With Advanced Dementia.” BMC Geriatrics⏎21: 337. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12877-021-02280-0.⏎ClinicalTrials.gov. 2024. “PainChek® US Validation Nursing Home Study (NCT06049732).”⏎https://clinicaltrials.gov/study/NCT06049732.⏎Sampson, Elizabeth L., Nathan Davies, and Victoria Vickerstaff. 2025. “Evaluation of the Psychometric⏎Properties of PainChek in Older General Hospital Patients With Dementia.” Age and Ageing 54 (2).⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/ageing/afaf027.⏎Clinical Documentation & Decision Support⏎Brydges, Garry. 2025. “Artificial Intelligence in Nursing Practice: Decisional Support, Clinical Integration, and⏎Future Directions.” OJIN: The Online Journal of Issues in Nursing 30 (2). https://ojin.nursingworld.org/table-of-⏎contents/volume-30-2025/number-2-may-2025/artificial-intelligence-in-nursing-practice-decisional-support-⏎clinical-integration-and-future-directions/.⏎Cato, Kenrick D., and Victoria L. Tiase. 2025. “Can AI Relieve Nursing Documentation Burden?” American⏎Nurse Journal. https://www.myamericannurse.com/can-ai-relieve-nursing-documentation-burden/.⏎Marselas, Kimberly. 2025. “Nursing Homes and AI: What’s in Use, What’s Not and How to Decide.”⏎McKnight’s Long Term Care News. https://www.mcknights.com/news/nursing-homes-and-ai-whats-in-use-⏎whats-not-and-how-to-decide/.⏎Medication Management Systems⏎Akyon, Seyma Handan, Fatih Cagatay Akyon, and Tarık Eren Yılmaz. 2023. “Artificial Intelligence–Supported⏎Web Application … for Reducing Polypharmacy Side Effects and Supporting Rational Drug Use in Geriatric⏎Patients.” Frontiers in Medicine 10: 1029198. https://doi.org/10.3389/fmed.2023.1029198.⏎Martins, Andreia, João Vitorino, Eva Maia, and Isabel Praça. 2024. “PharmiTech: Addressing Polypharmacy⏎Challenges Through AI Driven Solutions.” Applied Sciences 14 (19): 8838. https://www.mdpi.com/2076-⏎3417/14/19/8838.⏎Mass General Brigham. 2024. “Study Finds ChatGPT Shows Promise as Medication Management Tool, Could⏎Help Improve Geriatric Health Care.” https://www.massgeneralbrigham.org/en/about/newsroom/articles/study-⏎finds-chatgpt-shows-promise-as-medication-management-tool-could-help-improve-geriatric-health-care.⏎Chatbots & Virtual Assistants⏎Wolfe, Brooke H., Yoo Jung Oh, Hyesun Choung, et al. 2025. “Caregiving AI Chatbot for Older Adults and⏎Their Preferences, Well Being, and Social Connectivity: Mixed Method Study.” Journal of Medical Internet⏎Research 27. https://www.jmir.org/2025/1/e65776.⏎Yu, Shulan, and Tianyue Chen. 2024. “Understanding Older Adults’ Acceptance of Chatbots in Healthcare⏎Delivery: An Extended UTAUT Model.” Frontiers in Public Health 12: 1435329.⏎https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2024.1435329.⏎Zhang, Qian, Arkers K. C. Wong, and Jonathan Bayuo. 2024. “The Role of Chatbots in Enhancing Health Care⏎for Older Adults: A Scoping Review.” Journal of the American Medical Directors Association 25 (9): 105108.⏎https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jamda.2024.105108.⏎19⏎⏎Personalized Care Planning⏎Aljohani, Abeer. 2025. “AI Driven Decision Making for Personalized Elderly Care: A Fuzzy MCDM Based⏎Framework.” BMC Medical Informatics and Decision Making 25: 119. https://doi.org/10.1186/s12911-025-⏎02953-5.⏎Centers for Medicare & Medicaid Services (CMS). 2025. Minimum Data Set (MDS) 3.0 Resident Assessment⏎Instrument (RAI) Manual, v1.20.1. https://www.cms.gov/medicare/quality/nursing-home-improvement/resident-⏎assessment-instrument-manual.⏎Robotics & Companion Technologies⏎Lim, JunSeo. 2023. “Effects of a Cognitive Based Intervention Program Using Social Robot PIO …” Frontiers⏎in Public Health 11: 1097485. https://doi.org/10.3389/fpubh.2023.1097485.⏎Mehrabi, Fahimeh, and Akram Ghezelbash. 2025. “Wired for Companionship: A Meta Analysis on Social⏎Robots Filling the Void of Loneliness in Later Life.” The Gerontologist 65 (12).⏎https://doi.org/10.1093/geront/gnaf219.⏎Robinson, Hayley, Bruce MacDonald, Ngaire Kerse, and Elizabeth Broadbent. 2013. “The Psychosocial Effects⏎of a Companion Robot: A Randomized Controlled Trial.” Journal of the American Medical Directors⏎Association 14 (9): 661–667. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jamda.2013.02.007.⏎Cognitive & Behavioral Monitoring⏎Abedi, Ali, Charlene H. Chu, and Shehroz S. Khan. 2025. “Early Prediction of Agitation in Community⏎Dwelling People With Dementia Using Multimodal Sensors and Machine Learning.” In AI for Aging⏎Rehabilitation and Intelligent Assisted Living (IJCAI 2025). https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-981-⏎95-0568-5_1.⏎Badawi, Abeer, Somayya Elmoghazy, Samira Choudhury, et al. 2025. “Multimodal Detection of Agitation in⏎People With Dementia in Clinical Settings: Observational Pilot Study.” JMIR Aging 8.⏎https://aging.jmir.org/2025/1/e68156.⏎Fritz, Roschelle, and Diane Cook. “Detecting Older Adults’ Behavior Changes During Adverse External Events⏎Using Ambient Sensing: Longitudinal Observational Study.” JMIR Nursing 8 (2025): e69052.⏎https://nursing.jmir.org/2025/1/e69052.⏎Kleine Deters, Jan, Sarah Janus, Jair A. L. Silva, Heinrich J. Wörtche, and Sytse U. Zuidema. 2024. “Sensor⏎Based Agitation Prediction in Institutionalized People With Dementia: A Systematic Review.” Pervasive and⏎Mobile Computing 98: 101876. https://doi.org/10.1016/j.pmcj.2024.101876.⏎Kleine Deters, Jan, Rinesh Baidjnath Misier, Sarah Janus, Huib Burger, Heinrich Wörtche, and Sytse Zuidema.⏎“Sensortechnology for Monitoring Challenging Behavior in Nursing Home Residents with Dementia.”⏎International Psychogeriatrics 35, suppl. (December 2023): 145–46.⏎https://www.intpsychogeriatrics.or